if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-37106"]="\"_Go then, my little Book, and show to all That entertain and bid thee welcome shall, What thou dost keep close shut up in thy breast; And wish what thou dost show them may be blest To them for good, may make them choose to be Pilgrims better, by far, than thee or me. Tell them of Mercy; she is one Who early hath her pilgrimage begun. Yea, let young damsels learn of her to prize The world which is to come, and so be wise; For little tripping maids may follow God Along the ways which saintly feet have trod._\"\n\nAdapted from JOHN BUNYAN.\n\n# XLVII. HARVEST TIME\n\n[The Illustrations, designed by FRANK T. MERRILL, drawn, engraved, and printed under the supervision of GEORGE T. ANDREW.]\n\nThey all drew to the fire, mother in the big chair, with Beth at her feet\n\nPreface\nContents\n\nTail-piece to Contents\n\nList of Illustrations\n\nTail-piece to Illustrations\n\nChristmas won't be Christmas without any presents\n\nBeth put a pair of slippers down to warm\n\nI used to be so frightened when it was my turn to sit in the big chair\n\nDo it this way, clasp your hands so\n\nIt was a cheerful, hopeful letter\n\nHow you used to play Pilgrim's Progress\n\nNo one but Beth could get much music out of the old piano\n\nAt nine they stopped work and sung as usual\n\nMerry Christmas\nThe procession set out\n\nOut came Meg with gray horse-hair hanging about her face\n\nA little figure in cloudy white\n\nThe lovers kneeling to receive Don Pedro's blessing\n\nWe talked over the fence\nTail-piece\n\nEating apples and crying over the \"Heir of Redclyffe\"\n\nJo undertook to pinch the papered locks\n\nMrs. Gardiner greeted them\n\nFace to face with the Laurence boy\n\nThey sat down on the stairs\n\nTell about the party\n\nThe kitten stuck like a burr just out of reach\n\nCurling herself up in the big chair\n\nReading that everlasting Belsham\n\nHe took her by the ear! by the ear!\n\nMr. Laurence hooked up a big fish\n\nTail-piece\n\nBeing neighborly\n\nLaurie opened the window\n\nPoll tweaked off his wig\n\nPutting his finger under her chin\n\nPlease give these to your mother\n\nTail-piece\n\nO sir, they do care very much\n\nMr. Laurence often opened his study door\n\nShe put both arms around his neck and kissed him\n\nThe Cyclops\n\nAmy bore without flinching several tingling blows\n\nYou do know her\n\nGirls, where are you going?\n\nI burnt it up\n\nHeld Amy up by his arms and hockey\n\nPacking the go abroady trunk\n\nMeg's partner appeared\n\nAsked to be introduced\n\nI wouldn't, Meg\n\nHolding a hand of each, Mrs. March said, &c.\n\nMr. Pickwick\n\nJo threw open the door of the closet\n\nJo spent the morning on the river\n\nAmy sat down to draw\nO Pip! O Pip!\n\nMiss Crocker made a wry face\n\nWe'll work like bees\n\nBeth was post-mistress\n\nAmy capped the climax by putting a clothes-pin on her nose 151\n\nMr. Laurence waving his hat\nNow, Miss Jo, I'll settle you\n\nA very merry lunch it was\n\nHe went prancing down a quiet street\n\n\"Oh, rise,\" she said\n\nA stunning blow from the big Greek lexicon\n\nHe sneezed\n\nThe Portuguese walked the plank\n\nWill you give me a rose?\n\nMiss Kate put up her glass\n\nEllen Tree\nTail-piece\n\nSwinging to and fro in his hammock\n\nIt was rather a pretty little picture\n\nWaved a brake before her face\n\nI see him bow and smile\n\nTail-piece\nJo was very busy\n\nHurrah for Miss March\n\nJo darted away\n\nJo laid herself on the sofa and affected to read\n\nNovember is the most disagreeable month in the year\n\nOne of them horrid telegraph things\n\nShe came suddenly upon Mr. Brooke\n\nThe man clipped\nTail-piece\n\nLetters\n\nShe rolled away\nI wind the clock\n\nYours Respectful, Hannah Mullet\n\nTail-piece\n\nIt didn't stir, and I knew it was dead\n\nHe sat down beside her\n\nWhat do you want now?\n\nBeth did have the fever\n\nGently stroking her head as her mother used to do\n\nAmy's Will\n\nPolish up the spoons and the fat silver teapot\n\nOn his back, with all his legs in the air\n\nI should choose this\n\nGravely promenaded to and fro\n\nAmy's Will\nTail-piece\n\nMrs. March would not leave Beth's side\n\nTail-piece\nLetters\n\nJo and her mother were reading the note\n\nGet up and don't be a goose\n\n\"Hold your tongue!\" cried Jo, covering her ears\n\nHe stood at the foot, like a lion in the path\n\nBeth was soon able to lie on the study sofa all day\n\nThe Jungfrau\n\nPopping in her head now and then\n\nHe sat in the big chair by Beth's sofa with the other three close by\n\nShall I tell you how?\n\nBless me, what's all this?\n\nFor Mrs. John Brooke\n\nHome of the Little Women\n\nThe Dove Cote\n\nA small watchman's rattle\n\nTail-piece\n\nThe First Wedding\n\nArtistic Attempts\n\nHer foot held fast in a panful of plaster\n\nPlease don't, it's mine\n\nTail-piece\n\nLiterary Lessons\n\nA check for one hundred dollars\n\nTail-piece\n\nDomestic Experiences\n\nBoth felt desperately uncomfortable\n\nA bargain, I assure you, ma'am\n\nLaurie heroically shut his eyes while something was put into his arms\n\nCalls\n\nShe took the saddle to the horse\n\nIt might have been worse\n\nThe call at Aunt March's\nTail-piece\n\nYou shall have another table\n\nBought up the bouquets\nTail-piece\n\nFlo and I ordered a hansom-cab\n\nEvery one was very kind, especially the officers\n\nI've seen the imperial family several times\n\nTrying to sketch the gray-stone lion's head on the wall\n\nShe leaned her head upon her hands\n\nNow, this is filling at the price\n\nUp with the Bonnets of Bonnie Dundee\n\nI amused myself by dropping gingerbread nuts over the seat\n\nThou shalt haf thy Bhaer\n\nHe waved his hand, sock and all\n\nDis is mine effalunt\n\nI sat down upon the floor and read and looked and ate\n\nTail-piece\n\nIn the presence of three gentlemen\n\nA select symposium\n\nHe doesn't prink at his glass before coming\n\nJo stuffed the whole bundle into the stove\n\nHe put the sisters into the carriage\n\nHe laid his head down on the mossy post\n\nO Jo, can't you?\nTail-piece\n\nWith her head in Jo's lap, while the wind blew healthfully over her\n\nTail-piece\n\nHe hurried forward to meet her\n\nHere are your flowers\n\nDemi and Daisy\n\nMornin' now\n\nMy dear man, it's a bonnet\n\nTail-piece\n\nSat piping on a stone while his goats skipped\n\nLaurie threw himself down on the turf\n\nA rough sketch of Laurie taming a horse\n\nThe Valley of the Shadow\n\nTail-piece\n\nSat staring up at the busts\n\nTurning the ring thoughtfully upon his finger\n\nO Laurie, Laurie, I knew you'd come\n\nHow well we pull together\n\nJo and her father\n\nJo laid her head on a comfortable rag-bag and cried\n\nA substantial lifelike ghost leaning over her\n\nThe tall uncle proceeded to toss and tousle the small nephew\n\nO Mr. Bhaer, I am so glad to see you\n\nMr. Bhaer sang heartily\n\nMrs. Laurence sitting in her mother's lap\n\nThey began to pace up and down\n\nTail-piece\n\nMe loves evvybody\n\nWhat makes my legs go, dranpa?\n\nDranpa, it's a We\nTail-piece\n\nMr. Bhaer and Jo were enjoying promenades\n\nLooking up she saw Mr. Bhaer\n\nDoes this suit you, Mr. Bhaer?\n\nUnder the umbrella\n\nTail-piece\n\nHarvest time\n\nTeddy bore a charmed life\n\nLeaving Mrs. March and her daughters under the festival tree\n\nTail-piece\n\n# PLAYING PILGRIMS.\n\n\"Christmas won't be Christmas without any presents,\" grumbled Jo, lying on the rug.\n\n\"It's so dreadful to be poor!\" sighed Meg, looking down at her old dress.\n\n\"I don't think it's fair for some girls to have plenty of pretty things, and other girls nothing at all,\" added little Amy, with an injured sniff.\n\n\"We've got father and mother and each other,\" said Beth contentedly, from her corner.\n\nThe four young faces on which the firelight shone brightened at the cheerful words, but darkened again as Jo said sadly,--\n\n\"We haven't got father, and shall not have him for a long time.\" She didn't say \"perhaps never,\" but each silently added it, thinking of father far away, where the fighting was.\n\nNobody spoke for a minute; then Meg said in an altered tone,--\n\n\"You know the reason mother proposed not having any presents this Christmas was because it is going to be a hard winter for every one; and she thinks we ought not to spend money for pleasure, when our men are suffering so in the army. We can't do much, but we can make our little sacrifices, and ought to do it gladly. But I am afraid I don't;\" and Meg shook her head, as she thought regretfully of all the pretty things she wanted.\n\n\"But I don't think the little we should spend would do any good. We've each got a dollar, and the army wouldn't be much helped by our giving that. I agree not to expect anything from mother or you, but I do want to buy Undine and Sintram for myself; I've wanted it _so_ long,\" said Jo, who was a bookworm.\n\n\"I planned to spend mine in new music,\" said Beth, with a little sigh, which no one heard but the hearth-brush and kettle-holder.\n\n\"I shall get a nice box of Faber's drawing-pencils; I really need them,\" said Amy decidedly.\n\n\"Mother didn't say anything about our money, and she won't wish us to give up everything. Let's each buy what we want, and have a little fun; I'm sure we work hard enough to earn it,\" cried Jo, examining the heels of her shoes in a gentlemanly manner.\n\n\"I know _I_ do,--teaching those tiresome children nearly all day, when I'm longing to enjoy myself at home,\" began Meg, in the complaining tone again.\n\n\"You don't have half such a hard time as I do,\" said Jo. \"How would you like to be shut up for hours with a nervous, fussy old lady, who keeps you trotting, is never satisfied, and worries you till you're ready to fly out of the window or cry?\"\n\n\"It's naughty to fret; but I do think washing dishes and keeping things tidy is the worst work in the world. It makes me cross; and my hands get so stiff, I can't practise well at all;\" and Beth looked at her rough hands with a sigh that any one could hear that time.\n\n\"I don't believe any of you suffer as I do,\" cried Amy; \"for you don't have to go to school with impertinent girls, who plague you if you don't know your lessons, and laugh at your dresses, and label your father if he isn't rich, and insult you when your nose isn't nice.\"\n\n\"If you mean _libel_, I'd say so, and not talk about _labels_, as if papa was a pickle-bottle,\" advised Jo, laughing.\n\n\"I know what I mean, and you needn't be _statirical_ about it. It's proper to use good words, and improve your _vocabilary_,\" returned Amy, with dignity.\n\n\"Don't peck at one another, children. Don't you wish we had the money papa lost when we were little, Jo? Dear me! how happy and good we'd be, if we had no worries!\" said Meg, who could remember better times.\n\n\"You said the other day, you thought we were a deal happier than the King children, for they were fighting and fretting all the time, in spite of their money.\"\n\n\"So I did, Beth. Well, I think we are; for, though we do have to work, we make fun for ourselves, and are a pretty jolly set, as Jo would say.\"\n\n\"Jo does use such slang words!\" observed Amy, with a reproving look at the long figure stretched on the rug. Jo immediately sat up, put her hands in her pockets, and began to whistle.\n\n\"Don't, Jo; it's so boyish!\"\n\n\"That's why I do it.\"\n\n\"I detest rude, unlady-like girls!\"\n\n\"I hate affected, niminy-piminy chits!\"\n\n\"'Birds in their little nests agree,'\" sang Beth, the peace-maker, with such a funny face that both sharp voices softened to a laugh, and the \"pecking\" ended for that time.\n\n\"Really, girls, you are both to be blamed,\" said Meg, beginning to lecture in her elder-sisterly fashion. \"You are old enough to leave off boyish tricks, and to behave better, Josephine. It didn't matter so much when you were a little girl; but now you are so tall, and turn up your hair, you should remember that you are a young lady.\"\n\n\"I'm not! and if turning up my hair makes me one, I'll wear it in two tails till I'm twenty,\" cried Jo, pulling off her net, and shaking down a chestnut mane. \"I hate to think I've got to grow up, and be Miss March, and wear long gowns, and look as prim as a China-aster! It's bad enough to be a girl, anyway, when I like boys' games and work and manners! I can't get over my disappointment in not being a boy; and it's worse than ever now, for I'm dying to go and fight with papa, and I can only stay at home and knit, like a poky old woman!\" And Jo shook the blue army-sock till the needles rattled like castanets, and her ball bounded across the room.\n\n\"Poor Jo! It's too bad, but it can't be helped; so you must try to be contented with making your name boyish, and playing brother to us girls,\" said Beth, stroking the rough head at her knee with a hand that all the dish-washing and dusting in the world could not make ungentle in its touch.\n\n\"As for you, Amy,\" continued Meg, \"you are altogether too particular and prim. Your airs are funny now; but you'll grow up an affected little goose, if you don't take care. I like your nice manners and refined ways of speaking, when you don't try to be elegant; but your absurd words are as bad as Jo's slang.\"\n\n\"If Jo is a tom-boy and Amy a goose, what am I, please?\" asked Beth, ready to share the lecture.\n\n\"You're a dear, and nothing else,\" answered Meg warmly; and no one contradicted her, for the \"Mouse\" was the pet of the family.\n\nAs young readers like to know \"how people look,\" we will take this moment to give them a little sketch of the four sisters, who sat knitting away in the twilight, while the December snow fell quietly without, and the fire crackled cheerfully within. It was a comfortable old room, though the carpet was faded and the furniture very plain; for a good picture or two hung on the walls, books filled the recesses, chrysanthemums and Christmas roses bloomed in the windows, and a pleasant atmosphere of home-peace pervaded it.\n\nMargaret, the eldest of the four, was sixteen, and very pretty, being plump and fair, with large eyes, plenty of soft, brown hair, a sweet mouth, and white hands, of which she was rather vain. Fifteen-year-old Jo was very tall, thin, and brown, and reminded one of a colt; for she never seemed to know what to do with her long limbs, which were very much in her way. She had a decided mouth, a comical nose, and sharp, gray eyes, which appeared to see everything, and were by turns fierce, funny, or thoughtful. Her long, thick hair was her one beauty; but it was usually bundled into a net, to be out of her way. Round shoulders had Jo, big hands and feet, a fly-away look to her clothes, and the uncomfortable appearance of a girl who was rapidly shooting up into a woman, and didn't like it. Elizabeth--or Beth, as every one called her--was a rosy, smooth-haired, bright-eyed girl of thirteen, with a shy manner, a timid voice, and a peaceful expression, which was seldom disturbed. Her father called her \"Little Tranquillity,\" and the name suited her excellently; for she seemed to live in a happy world of her own, only venturing out to meet the few whom she trusted and loved. Amy, though the youngest, was a most important person,--in her own opinion at least. A regular snow-maiden, with blue eyes, and yellow hair, curling on her shoulders, pale and slender, and always carrying herself like a young lady mindful of her manners. What the characters of the four sisters were we will leave to be found out.\n\nThe clock struck six; and, having swept up the hearth, Beth put a pair of slippers down to warm. Somehow the sight of the old shoes had a good effect upon the girls; for mother was coming, and every one brightened to welcome her. Meg stopped lecturing, and lighted the lamp, Amy got out of the easy-chair without being asked, and Jo forgot how tired she was as she sat up to hold the slippers nearer to the blaze.\n\n\"They are quite worn out; Marmee must have a new pair.\"\n\n\"I thought I'd get her some with my dollar,\" said Beth.\n\n\"No, I shall!\" cried Amy.\n\n\"I'm the oldest,\" began Meg, but Jo cut in with a decided--\n\n\"I'm the man of the family now papa is away, and _I_ shall provide the slippers, for he told me to take special care of mother while he was gone.\"\n\n\"I'll tell you what we'll do,\" said Beth; \"let's each get her something for Christmas, and not get anything for ourselves.\"\n\n\"That's like you, dear! What will we get?\" exclaimed Jo.\n\nEvery one thought soberly for a minute; then Meg announced, as if the idea was suggested by the sight of her own pretty hands, \"I shall give her a nice pair of gloves.\"\n\n\"Army shoes, best to be had,\" cried Jo.\n\n\"Some handkerchiefs, all hemmed,\" said Beth.\n\n\"I'll get a little bottle of cologne; she likes it, and it won't cost much, so I'll have some left to buy my pencils,\" added Amy.\n\n\"How will we give the things?\" asked Meg.\n\n\"Put them on the table, and bring her in and see her open the bundles. Don't you remember how we used to do on our birthdays?\" answered Jo.\n\n\"I used to be _so_ frightened when it was my turn to sit in the big chair with the crown on, and see you all come marching round to give the presents, with a kiss. I liked the things and the kisses, but it was dreadful to have you sit looking at me while I opened the bundles,\" said Beth, who was toasting her face and the bread for tea, at the same time.\n\n\"Let Marmee think we are getting things for ourselves, and then surprise her. We must go shopping to-morrow afternoon, Meg; there is so much to do about the play for Christmas night,\" said Jo, marching up and down, with her hands behind her back and her nose in the air.\n\n\"I don't mean to act any more after this time; I'm getting too old for such things,\" observed Meg, who was as much a child as ever about \"dressing-up\" frolics.\n\n\"You won't stop, I know, as long as you can trail round in a white gown with your hair down, and wear gold-paper jewelry. You are the best actress we've got, and there'll be an end of everything if you quit the boards,\" said Jo. \"We ought to rehearse to-night. Come here, Amy, and do the fainting scene, for you are as stiff as a poker in that.\"\n\n\"I can't help it; I never saw any one faint, and I don't choose to make myself all black and blue, tumbling flat as you do. If I can go down easily, I'll drop; if I can't, I shall fall into a chair and be graceful; I don't care if Hugo does come at me with a pistol,\" returned Amy, who was not gifted with dramatic power, but was chosen because she was small enough to be borne out shrieking by the villain of the piece.\n\n\"Do it this way; clasp your hands so, and stagger across the room, crying frantically, 'Roderigo! save me! save me!'\" and away went Jo, with a melodramatic scream which was truly thrilling.\n\nAmy followed, but she poked her hands out stiffly before her, and jerked herself along as if she went by machinery; and her \"Ow!\" was more suggestive of pins being run into her than of fear and anguish. Jo gave a despairing groan, and Meg laughed outright, while Beth let her bread burn as she watched the fun, with interest.\n\n\"It's no use! Do the best you can when the time comes, and if the audience laugh, don't blame me. Come on, Meg.\"\n\nThen things went smoothly, for Don Pedro defied the world in a speech of two pages without a single break; Hagar, the witch, chanted an awful incantation over her kettleful of simmering toads, with weird effect; Roderigo rent his chains asunder manfully, and Hugo died in agonies of remorse and arsenic, with a wild \"Ha! ha!\"\n\n\"It's the best we've had yet,\" said Meg, as the dead villain sat up and rubbed his elbows.\n\n\"I don't see how you can write and act such splendid things, Jo. You're a regular Shakespeare!\" exclaimed Beth, who firmly believed that her sisters were gifted with wonderful genius in all things.\n\n\"Not quite,\" replied Jo modestly. \"I do think 'The Witch's Curse, an Operatic Tragedy,' is rather a nice thing; but I'd like to try Macbeth, if we only had a trap-door for Banquo. I always wanted to do the killing part. 'Is that a dagger that I see before me?'\" muttered Jo, rolling her eyes and clutching at the air, as she had seen a famous tragedian do.\n\n\"No, it's the toasting fork, with mother's shoe on it instead of the bread. Beth's stage-struck!\" cried Meg, and the rehearsal ended in a general burst of laughter.\n\n\"Glad to find you so merry, my girls,\" said a cheery voice at the door, and actors and audience turned to welcome a tall, motherly lady, with a \"can-I-help-you\" look about her which was truly delightful. She was not elegantly dressed, but a noble-looking woman, and the girls thought the gray cloak and unfashionable bonnet covered the most splendid mother in the world.\n\n\"Well, dearies, how have you got on to-day? There was so much to do, getting the boxes ready to go to-morrow, that I didn't come home to dinner. Has any one called, Beth? How is your cold, Meg? Jo, you look tired to death. Come and kiss me, baby.\"\n\nWhile making these maternal inquiries Mrs. March got her wet things off, her warm slippers on, and sitting down in the easy-chair, drew Amy to her lap, preparing to enjoy the happiest hour of her busy day. The girls flew about, trying to make things comfortable, each in her own way. Meg arranged the tea-table; Jo brought wood and set chairs, dropping, overturning, and clattering everything she touched; Beth trotted to and fro between parlor and kitchen, quiet and busy; while Amy gave directions to every one, as she sat with her hands folded.\n\nAs they gathered about the table, Mrs. March said, with a particularly happy face, \"I've got a treat for you after supper.\"\n\nA quick, bright smile went round like a streak of sunshine. Beth clapped her hands, regardless of the biscuit she held, and Jo tossed up her napkin, crying, \"A letter! a letter! Three cheers for father!\"\n\n\"Yes, a nice long letter. He is well, and thinks he shall get through the cold season better than we feared. He sends all sorts of loving wishes for Christmas, and an especial message to you girls,\" said Mrs. March, patting her pocket as if she had got a treasure there.\n\n\"Hurry and get done! Don't stop to quirk your little finger, and simper over your plate, Amy,\" cried Jo, choking in her tea, and dropping her bread, butter side down, on the carpet, in her haste to get at the treat.\n\nBeth ate no more, but crept away, to sit in her shadowy corner and brood over the delight to come, till the others were ready.\n\n\"I think it was so splendid in father to go as a chaplain when he was too old to be drafted, and not strong enough for a soldier,\" said Meg warmly.\n\n\"Don't I wish I could go as a drummer, a _vivan_--what's its name? or a nurse, so I could be near him and help him,\" exclaimed Jo, with a groan.\n\n\"It must be very disagreeable to sleep in a tent, and eat all sorts of bad-tasting things, and drink out of a tin mug,\" sighed Amy.\n\n\"When will he come home, Marmee?\" asked Beth, with a little quiver in her voice.\n\n\"Not for many months, dear, unless he is sick. He will stay and do his work faithfully as long as he can, and we won't ask for him back a minute sooner than he can be spared. Now come and hear the letter.\"\n\nThey all drew to the fire, mother in the big chair with Beth at her feet, Meg and Amy perched on either arm of the chair, and Jo leaning on the back, where no one would see any sign of emotion if the letter should happen to be touching.\n\nVery few letters were written in those hard times that were not touching, especially those which fathers sent home. In this one little was said of the hardships endured, the dangers faced, or the homesickness conquered; it was a cheerful, hopeful letter, full of lively descriptions of camp life, marches, and military news; and only at the end did the writer's heart overflow with fatherly love and longing for the little girls at home.\n\n\"Give them all my dear love and a kiss. Tell them I think of them by day, pray for them by night, and find my best comfort in their affection at all times. A year seems very long to wait before I see them, but remind them that while we wait we may all work, so that these hard days need not be wasted. I know they will remember all I said to them, that they will be loving children to you, will do their duty faithfully, fight their bosom enemies bravely, and conquer themselves so beautifully, that when I come back to them I may be fonder and prouder than ever of my little women.\"\n\nEverybody sniffed when they came to that part; Jo wasn't ashamed of the great tear that dropped off the end of her nose, and Amy never minded the rumpling of her curls as she hid her face on her mother's shoulder and sobbed out, \"I _am_ a selfish girl! but I'll truly try to be better, so he mayn't be disappointed in me by and by.\"\n\n\"We all will!\" cried Meg. \"I think too much of my looks, and hate to work, but won't any more, if I can help it.\"\n\n\"I'll try and be what he loves to call me, 'a little woman,' and not be rough and wild; but do my duty here instead of wanting to be somewhere else,\" said Jo, thinking that keeping her temper at home was a much harder task than facing a rebel or two down South.\n\nBeth said nothing, but wiped away her tears with the blue army-sock, and began to knit with all her might, losing no time in doing the duty that lay nearest her, while she resolved in her quiet little soul to be all that father hoped to find her when the year brought round the happy coming home.\n\nMrs. March broke the silence that followed Jo's words, by saying in her cheery voice, \"Do you remember how you used to play Pilgrim's Progress when you were little things? Nothing delighted you more than to have me tie my piece-bags on your backs for burdens, give you hats and sticks and rolls of paper, and let you travel through the house from the cellar, which was the City of Destruction, up, up, to the house-top, where you had all the lovely things you could collect to make a Celestial City.\"\n\n\"What fun it was, especially going by the lions, fighting Apollyon, and passing through the Valley where the hobgoblins were!\" said Jo.\n\n\"I liked the place where the bundles fell off and tumbled down stairs,\" said Meg.\n\n\"My favorite part was when we came out on the flat roof where our flowers and arbors and pretty things were, and all stood and sung for joy up there in the sunshine,\" said Beth, smiling, as if that pleasant moment had come back to her.\n\n\"I don't remember much about it, except that I was afraid of the cellar and the dark entry, and always liked the cake and milk we had up at the top. If I wasn't too old for such things, I'd rather like to play it over again,\" said Amy, who began to talk of renouncing childish things at the mature age of twelve.\n\n\"We never are too old for this, my dear, because it is a play we are playing all the time in one way or another. Our burdens are here, our road is before us, and the longing for goodness and happiness is the guide that leads us through many troubles and mistakes to the peace which is a true Celestial City. Now, my little pilgrims, suppose you begin again, not in play, but in earnest, and see how far on you can get before father comes home.\"\n\n\"Really, mother? Where are our bundles?\" asked Amy, who was a very literal young lady.\n\n\"Each of you told what your burden was just now, except Beth; I rather think she hasn't got any,\" said her mother.\n\n\"Yes, I have; mine is dishes and dusters, and envying girls with nice pianos, and being afraid of people.\"\n\nBeth's bundle was such a funny one that everybody wanted to laugh; but nobody did, for it would have hurt her feelings very much.\n\n\"Let us do it,\" said Meg thoughtfully. \"It is only another name for trying to be good, and the story may help us; for though we do want to be good, it's hard work, and we forget, and don't do our best.\"\n\n\"We were in the Slough of Despond to-night, and mother came and pulled us out as Help did in the book. We ought to have our roll of directions, like Christian. What shall we do about that?\" asked Jo, delighted with the fancy which lent a little romance to the very dull task of doing her duty.\n\n\"Look under your pillows, Christmas morning, and you will find your guide-book,\" replied Mrs. March.\n\nThey talked over the new plan while old Hannah cleared the table; then out came the four little work-baskets, and the needles flew as the girls made sheets for Aunt March. It was uninteresting sewing, but to-night no one grumbled. They adopted Jo's plan of dividing the long seams into four parts, and calling the quarters Europe, Asia, Africa, and America, and in that way got on capitally, especially when they talked about the different countries as they stitched their way through them.\n\nAt nine they stopped work, and sung, as usual, before they went to bed. No one but Beth could get much music out of the old piano; but she had a way of softly touching the yellow keys, and making a pleasant accompaniment to the simple songs they sung. Meg had a voice like a flute, and she and her mother led the little choir. Amy chirped like a cricket, and Jo wandered through the airs at her own sweet will, always coming out at the wrong place with a croak or a quaver that spoilt the most pensive tune. They had always done this from the time they could lisp\n\n\"Crinkle, crinkle, 'ittle 'tar,\"\n\nand it had become a household custom, for the mother was a born singer. The first sound in the morning was her voice, as she went about the house singing like a lark; and the last sound at night was the same cheery sound, for the girls never grew too old for that familiar lullaby.\n\n# A MERRY CHRISTMAS.\n\nJo was the first to wake in the gray dawn of Christmas morning. No stockings hung at the fireplace, and for a moment she felt as much disappointed as she did long ago, when her little sock fell down because it was so crammed with goodies. Then she remembered her mother's promise, and, slipping her hand under her pillow, drew out a little crimson-covered book. She knew it very well, for it was that beautiful old story of the best life ever lived, and Jo felt that it was a true guide-book for any pilgrim going the long journey. She woke Meg with a \"Merry Christmas,\" and bade her see what was under her pillow. A green-covered book appeared, with the same picture inside, and a few words written by their mother, which made their one present very precious in their eyes. Presently Beth and Amy woke, to rummage and find their little books also,--one dove-colored, the other blue; and all sat looking at and talking about them, while the east grew rosy with the coming day.\n\nIn spite of her small vanities, Margaret had a sweet and pious nature, which unconsciously influenced her sisters, especially Jo, who loved her very tenderly, and obeyed her because her advice was so gently given.\n\n\"Girls,\" said Meg seriously, looking from the tumbled head beside her to the two little night-capped ones in the room beyond, \"mother wants us to read and love and mind these books, and we must begin at once. We used to be faithful about it; but since father went away, and all this war trouble unsettled us, we have neglected many things. You can do as you please; but _I_ shall keep my book on the table here, and read a little every morning as soon as I wake, for I know it will do me good, and help me through the day.\"\n\nThen she opened her new book and began to read. Jo put her arm round her, and, leaning cheek to cheek, read also, with the quiet expression so seldom seen on her restless face.\n\n\"How good Meg is! Come, Amy, let's do as they do. I'll help you with the hard words, and they'll explain things if we don't understand,\" whispered Beth, very much impressed by the pretty books and her sisters' example.\n\n\"I'm glad mine is blue,\" said Amy; and then the rooms were very still while the pages were softly turned, and the winter sunshine crept in to touch the bright heads and serious faces with a Christmas greeting.\n\n\"Where is mother?\" asked Meg, as she and Jo ran down to thank her for their gifts, half an hour later.\n\n\"Goodness only knows. Some poor creeter come a-beggin', and your ma went straight off to see what was needed. There never _was_ such a woman for givin' away vittles and drink, clothes and firin',\" replied Hannah, who had lived with the family since Meg was born, and was considered by them all more as a friend than a servant.\n\n\"She will be back soon, I think; so fry your cakes, and have everything ready,\" said Meg, looking over the presents which were collected in a basket and kept under the sofa, ready to be produced at the proper time. \"Why, where is Amy's bottle of cologne?\" she added, as the little flask did not appear.\n\n\"She took it out a minute ago, and went off with it to put a ribbon on it, or some such notion,\" replied Jo, dancing about the room to take the first stiffness off the new army-slippers.\n\n\"How nice my handkerchiefs look, don't they? Hannah washed and ironed them for me, and I marked them all myself,\" said Beth, looking proudly at the somewhat uneven letters which had cost her such labor.\n\n\"Bless the child! she's gone and put 'Mother' on them instead of 'M. March.' How funny!\" cried Jo, taking up one.\n\n\"Isn't it right? I thought it was better to do it so, because Meg's initials are 'M. M.,' and I don't want any one to use these but Marmee,\" said Beth, looking troubled.\n\n\"It's all right, dear, and a very pretty idea,--quite sensible, too, for no one can ever mistake now. It will please her very much, I know,\" said Meg, with a frown for Jo and a smile for Beth.\n\n\"There's mother. Hide the basket, quick!\" cried Jo, as a door slammed, and steps sounded in the hall.\n\nAmy came in hastily, and looked rather abashed when she saw her sisters all waiting for her.\n\n\"Where have you been, and what are you hiding behind you?\" asked Meg, surprised to see, by her hood and cloak, that lazy Amy had been out so early.\n\n\"Don't laugh at me, Jo! I didn't mean any one should know till the time came. I only meant to change the little bottle for a big one, and I gave _all_ my money to get it, and I'm truly trying not to be selfish any more.\"\n\nAs she spoke, Amy showed the handsome flask which replaced the cheap one; and looked so earnest and humble in her little effort to forget herself that Meg hugged her on the spot, and Jo pronounced her \"a trump,\" while Beth ran to the window, and picked her finest rose to ornament the stately bottle.\n\n\"You see I felt ashamed of my present, after reading and talking about being good this morning, so I ran round the corner and changed it the minute I was up: and I'm _so_ glad, for mine is the handsomest now.\"\n\nAnother bang of the street-door sent the basket under the sofa, and the girls to the table, eager for breakfast.\n\n\"Merry Christmas, Marmee! Many of them! Thank you for our books; we read some, and mean to every day,\" they cried, in chorus.\n\n\"Merry Christmas, little daughters! I'm glad you began at once, and hope you will keep on. But I want to say one word before we sit down. Not far away from here lies a poor woman with a little new-born baby. Six children are huddled into one bed to keep from freezing, for they have no fire. There is nothing to eat over there; and the oldest boy came to tell me they were suffering hunger and cold. My girls, will you give them your breakfast as a Christmas present?\"\n\nThey were all unusually hungry, having waited nearly an hour, and for a minute no one spoke; only a minute, for Jo exclaimed impetuously,--\n\n\"I'm so glad you came before we began!\"\n\n\"May I go and help carry the things to the poor little children?\" asked Beth, eagerly.\n\n\"_I_ shall take the cream and the muffins,\" added Amy, heroically giving up the articles she most liked.\n\nMeg was already covering the buckwheats, and piling the bread into one big plate.\n\n\"I thought you'd do it,\" said Mrs. March, smiling as if satisfied. \"You shall all go and help me, and when we come back we will have bread and milk for breakfast, and make it up at dinner-time.\"\n\nThey were soon ready, and the procession set out. Fortunately it was early, and they went through back streets, so few people saw them, and no one laughed at the queer party.\n\nA poor, bare, miserable room it was, with broken windows, no fire, ragged bed-clothes, a sick mother, wailing baby, and a group of pale, hungry children cuddled under one old quilt, trying to keep warm.\nHow the big eyes stared and the blue lips smiled as the girls went in!\n\n\"Ach, mein Gott! it is good angels come to us!\" said the poor woman, crying for joy.\n\n\"Funny angels in hoods and mittens,\" said Jo, and set them laughing.\n\nIn a few minutes it really did seem as if kind spirits had been at work there. Hannah, who had carried wood, made a fire, and stopped up the broken panes with old hats and her own cloak. Mrs. March gave the mother tea and gruel, and comforted her with promises of help, while she dressed the little baby as tenderly as if it had been her own. The girls, meantime, spread the table, set the children round the fire, and fed them like so many hungry birds,--laughing, talking, and trying to understand the funny broken English.\n\n\"Das ist gut!\" \"Die Engel-kinder!\" cried the poor things, as they ate, and warmed their purple hands at the comfortable blaze.\n\nThe girls had never been called angel children before, and thought it very agreeable, especially Jo, who had been considered a \"Sancho\" ever since she was born. That was a very happy breakfast, though they didn't get any of it; and when they went away, leaving comfort behind, I think there were not in all the city four merrier people than the hungry little girls who gave away their breakfasts and contented themselves with bread and milk on Christmas morning.\n\n\"That's loving our neighbor better than ourselves, and I like it,\" said Meg, as they set out their presents, while their mother was upstairs collecting clothes for the poor Hummels.\n\nNot a very splendid show, but there was a great deal of love done up in the few little bundles; and the tall vase of red roses, white chrysanthemums, and trailing vines, which stood in the middle, gave quite an elegant air to the table.\n\n\"She's coming! Strike up, Beth! Open the door, Amy! Three cheers for Marmee!\" cried Jo, prancing about, while Meg went to conduct mother to the seat of honor.\n\nBeth played her gayest march, Amy threw open the door, and Meg enacted escort with great dignity. Mrs. March was both surprised and touched; and smiled with her eyes full as she examined her presents, and read the little notes which accompanied them. The slippers went on at once, a new handkerchief was slipped into her pocket, well scented with Amy's cologne, the rose was fastened in her bosom, and the nice gloves were pronounced a \"perfect fit.\"\n\nThere was a good deal of laughing and kissing and explaining, in the simple, loving fashion which makes these home-festivals so pleasant at the time, so sweet to remember long afterward, and then all fell to work.\n\nThe morning charities and ceremonies took so much time that the rest of the day was devoted to preparations for the evening festivities. Being still too young to go often to the theatre, and not rich enough to afford any great outlay for private performances, the girls put their wits to work, and--necessity being the mother of invention,--made whatever they needed. Very clever were some of their productions,--pasteboard guitars, antique lamps made of old-fashioned butter-boats covered with silver paper, gorgeous robes of old cotton, glittering with tin spangles from a pickle factory, and armor covered with the same useful diamond-shaped bits, left in sheets when the lids of tin preserve-pots were cut out. The furniture was used to being turned topsy-turvy, and the big chamber was the scene of many innocent revels.\n\nNo gentlemen were admitted; so Jo played male parts to her heart's content, and took immense satisfaction in a pair of russet-leather boots given her by a friend, who knew a lady who knew an actor. These boots, an old foil, and a slashed doublet once used by an artist for some picture, were Jo's chief treasures, and appeared on all occasions. The smallness of the company made it necessary for the two principal actors to take several parts apiece; and they certainly deserved some credit for the hard work they did in learning three or four different parts, whisking in and out of various costumes, and managing the stage besides. It was excellent drill for their memories, a harmless amusement, and employed many hours which otherwise would have been idle, lonely, or spent in less profitable society.\n\nOn Christmas night, a dozen girls piled on to the bed which was the dress-circle, and sat before the blue and yellow chintz curtains in a most flattering state of expectancy. There was a good deal of rustling and whispering behind the curtain, a trifle of lamp-smoke, and an occasional giggle from Amy, who was apt to get hysterical in the excitement of the moment. Presently a bell sounded, the curtains flew apart, and the Operatic Tragedy began.\n\n\"A gloomy wood,\" according to the one play-bill, was represented by a few shrubs in pots, green baize on the floor, and a cave in the distance. This cave was made with a clothes-horse for a roof, bureaus for walls; and in it was a small furnace in full blast, with a black pot on it, and an old witch bending over it. The stage was dark, and the glow of the furnace had a fine effect, especially as real steam issued from the kettle when the witch took off the cover. A moment was allowed for the first thrill to subside; then Hugo, the villain, stalked in with a clanking sword at his side, a slouched hat, black beard, mysterious cloak, and the boots. After pacing to and fro in much agitation, he struck his forehead, and burst out in a wild strain, singing of his hatred to Roderigo, his love for Zara, and his pleasing resolution to kill the one and win the other. The gruff tones of Hugo's voice, with an occasional shout when his feelings overcame him, were very impressive, and the audience applauded the moment he paused for breath. Bowing with the air of one accustomed to public praise, he stole to the cavern, and ordered Hagar to come forth with a commanding \"What ho, minion! I need thee!\"\n\nOut came Meg, with gray horse-hair hanging about her face, a red and black robe, a staff, and cabalistic signs upon her cloak. Hugo demanded a potion to make Zara adore him, and one to destroy Roderigo. Hagar, in a fine dramatic melody, promised both, and proceeded to call up the spirit who would bring the love philter:--\n\n\"Hither, hither, from thy home, Airy sprite, I bid thee come! Born of roses, fed on dew, Charms and potions canst thou brew? Bring me here, with elfin speed, The fragrant philter which I need; Make it sweet and swift and strong, Spirit, answer now my song!\"\n\nA soft strain of music sounded, and then at the back of the cave appeared a little figure in cloudy white, with glittering wings, golden hair, and a garland of roses on its head. Waving a wand, it sang,--\n\n\"Hither I come, From my airy home, Afar in the silver moon. Take the magic spell, And use it well, Or its power will vanish soon!\"\n\nAnd, dropping a small, gilded bottle at the witch's feet, the spirit vanished. Another chant from Hagar produced another apparition,--not a lovely one; for, with a bang, an ugly black imp appeared, and, having croaked a reply, tossed a dark bottle at Hugo, and disappeared with a mocking laugh. Having warbled his thanks and put the potions in his boots, Hugo departed; and Hagar informed the audience that, as he had killed a few of her friends in times past, she has cursed him, and intends to thwart his plans, and be revenged on him. Then the curtain fell, and the audience reposed and ate candy while discussing the merits of the play.\n\nA good deal of hammering went on before the curtain rose again; but when it became evident what a masterpiece of stage-carpentering had been got up, no one murmured at the delay. It was truly superb! A tower rose to the ceiling; half-way up appeared a window, with a lamp burning at it, and behind the white curtain appeared Zara in a lovely blue and silver dress, waiting for Roderigo. He came in gorgeous array, with plumed cap, red cloak, chestnut love-locks, a guitar, and the boots, of course. Kneeling at the foot of the tower, he sang a serenade in melting tones. Zara replied, and, after a musical dialogue, consented to fly. Then came the grand effect of the play. Roderigo produced a rope-ladder, with five steps to it, threw up one end, and invited Zara to descend. Timidly she crept from her lattice, put her hand on Roderigo's shoulder, and was about to leap gracefully down, when, \"Alas! alas for Zara!\" she forgot her train,--it caught in the window; the tower tottered, leaned forward, fell with a crash, and buried the unhappy lovers in the ruins!\n\nA universal shriek arose as the russet boots waved wildly from the wreck, and a golden head emerged, exclaiming, \"I told you so! I told you so!\" With wonderful presence of mind, Don Pedro, the cruel sire, rushed in, dragged out his daughter, with a hasty aside,--\n\n\"Don't laugh! Act as if it was all right!\"--and, ordering Roderigo up, banished him from the kingdom with wrath and scorn. Though decidedly shaken by the fall of the tower upon him, Roderigo defied the old gentleman, and refused to stir. This dauntless example fired Zara: she also defied her sire, and he ordered them both to the deepest dungeons of the castle. A stout little retainer came in with chains, and led them away, looking very much frightened, and evidently forgetting the speech he ought to have made.\n\nAct third was the castle hall; and here Hagar appeared, having come to free the lovers and finish Hugo. She hears him coming, and hides; sees him put the potions into two cups of wine, and bid the timid little servant \"Bear them to the captives in their cells, and tell them I shall come anon.\" The servant takes Hugo aside to tell him something, and Hagar changes the cups for two others which are harmless. Ferdinando, the \"minion,\" carries them away, and Hagar puts back the cup which holds the poison meant for Roderigo. Hugo, getting thirsty after a long warble, drinks it, loses his wits, and, after a good deal of clutching and stamping, falls flat and dies; while Hagar informs him what she has done in a song of exquisite power and melody.\n\nThis was a truly thrilling scene, though some persons might have thought that the sudden tumbling down of a quantity of long hair rather marred the effect of the villain's death. He was called before the curtain, and with great propriety appeared, leading Hagar, whose singing was considered more wonderful than all the rest of the performance put together.\n\nAct fourth displayed the despairing Roderigo on the point of stabbing himself, because he has been told that Zara has deserted him. Just as the dagger is at his heart, a lovely song is sung under his window, informing him that Zara is true, but in danger, and he can save her, if he will. A key is thrown in, which unlocks the door, and in a spasm of rapture he tears off his chains, and rushes away to find and rescue his lady-love.\n\nAct fifth opened with a stormy scene between Zara and Don Pedro. He wishes her to go into a convent, but she won't hear of it; and, after a touching appeal, is about to faint, when Roderigo dashes in and demands her hand. Don Pedro refuses, because he is not rich. They shout and gesticulate tremendously, but cannot agree, and Roderigo is about to bear away the exhausted Zara, when the timid servant enters with a letter and a bag from Hagar, who has mysteriously disappeared. The latter informs the party that she bequeaths untold wealth to the young pair, and an awful doom to Don Pedro, if he doesn't make them happy. The bag is opened, and several quarts of tin money shower down upon the stage, till it is quite glorified with the glitter. This entirely softens the \"stern sire\": he consents without a murmur, all join in a joyful chorus, and the curtain falls upon the lovers kneeling to receive Don Pedro's blessing in attitudes of the most romantic grace.\n\nTumultuous applause followed, but received an unexpected check; for the cot-bed, on which the \"dress-circle\" was built, suddenly shut up, and extinguished the enthusiastic audience. Roderigo and Don Pedro flew to the rescue, and all were taken out unhurt, though many were speechless with laughter. The excitement had hardly subsided, when Hannah appeared, with \"Mrs. March's compliments, and would the ladies walk down to supper.\"\n\nThis was a surprise, even to the actors; and, when they saw the table, they looked at one another in rapturous amazement. It was like Marmee to get up a little treat for them; but anything so fine as this was unheard-of since the departed days of plenty. There was ice-cream,--actually two dishes of it, pink and white,--and cake and fruit and distracting French bonbons, and, in the middle of the table, four great bouquets of hot-house flowers!\n\nIt quite took their breath away; and they stared first at the table and then at their mother, who looked as if she enjoyed it immensely.\n\n\"Is it fairies?\" asked Amy,\n\n\"It's Santa Claus,\" said Beth.\n\n\"Mother did it\"; and Meg smiled her sweetest, in spite of her gray beard and white eyebrows.\n\n\"Aunt March had a good fit, and sent the supper,\" cried Jo, with a sudden inspiration.\n\n\"All wrong. Old Mr. Laurence sent it,\" replied Mrs. March.\n\n\"The Laurence boy's grandfather! What in the world put such a thing into his head? We don't know him!\" exclaimed Meg.\n\n\"Hannah told one of his servants about your breakfast party. He is an odd old gentleman, but that pleased him. He knew my father, years ago; and he sent me a polite note this afternoon, saying he hoped I would allow him to express his friendly feeling toward my children by sending them a few trifles in honor of the day. I could not refuse; and so you have a little feast at night to make up for the bread-and-milk breakfast.\"\n\n\"That boy put it into his head, I know he did! He's a capital fellow, and I wish we could get acquainted. He looks as if he'd like to know us; but he's bashful, and Meg is so prim she won't let me speak to him when we pass,\" said Jo, as the plates went round, and the ice began to melt out of sight, with \"Ohs!\" and \"Ahs!\" of satisfaction.\n\n\"You mean the people who live in the big house next door, don't you?\" asked one of the girls. \"My mother knows old Mr. Laurence; but says he's very proud, and doesn't like to mix with his neighbors. He keeps his grandson shut up, when he isn't riding or walking with his tutor, and makes him study very hard. We invited him to our party, but he didn't come. Mother says he's very nice, though he never speaks to us girls.\"\n\n\"Our cat ran away once, and he brought her back, and we talked over the fence, and were getting on capitally,--all about cricket, and so on,--when he saw Meg coming, and walked off. I mean to know him some day; for he needs fun, I'm sure he does,\" said Jo decidedly.\n\n\"I like his manners, and he looks like a little gentleman; so I've no objection to your knowing him, if a proper opportunity comes. He brought the flowers himself; and I should have asked him in, if I had been sure what was going on upstairs. He looked so wistful as he went away, hearing the frolic, and evidently having none of his own.\"\n\n\"It's a mercy you didn't, mother!\" laughed Jo, looking at her boots. \"But we'll have another play, some time, that he _can_ see. Perhaps he'll help act; wouldn't that be jolly?\"\n\n\"I never had such a fine bouquet before! How pretty it is!\" And Meg examined her flowers with great interest.\n\n\"They _are_ lovely! But Beth's roses are sweeter to me,\" said Mrs. March, smelling the half-dead posy in her belt.\n\nBeth nestled up to her, and whispered softly, \"I wish I could send my bunch to father. I'm afraid he isn't having such a merry Christmas as we are.\"\n\n# THE LAURENCE BOY.\n\n\"Jo! Jo! where are you?\" cried Meg, at the foot of the garret stairs.\n\n\"Here!\" answered a husky voice from above; and, running up, Meg found her sister eating apples and crying over the \"Heir of Redclyffe,\" wrapped up in a comforter on an old three-legged sofa by the sunny window. This was Jo's favorite refuge; and here she loved to retire with half a dozen russets and a nice book, to enjoy the quiet and the society of a pet rat who lived near by, and didn't mind her a particle. As Meg appeared, Scrabble whisked into his hole. Jo shook the tears off her cheeks, and waited to hear the news.\n\n\"Such fun! only see! a regular note of invitation from Mrs. Gardiner for to-morrow night!\" cried Meg, waving the precious paper, and then proceeding to read it, with girlish delight.\n\n\"'Mrs. Gardiner would be happy to see Miss March and Miss Josephine at a little dance on New-Year's Eve.' Marmee is willing we should go; now what _shall_ we wear?\"\n\n\"What's the use of asking that, when you know we shall wear our poplins, because we haven't got anything else?\" answered Jo, with her mouth full.\n\n\"If I only had a silk!\" sighed Meg. \"Mother says I may when I'm eighteen, perhaps; but two years is an everlasting time to wait.\"\n\n\"I'm sure our pops look like silk, and they are nice enough for us. Yours is as good as new, but I forgot the burn and the tear in mine. Whatever shall I do? the burn shows badly, and I can't take any out.\"\n\n\"You must sit still all you can, and keep your back out of sight; the front is all right. I shall have a new ribbon for my hair, and Marmee will lend me her little pearl pin, and my new slippers are lovely, and my gloves will do, though they aren't as nice as I'd like.\"\n\n\"Mine are spoilt with lemonade, and I can't get any new ones, so I shall have to go without,\" said Jo, who never troubled herself much about dress.\n\n\"You _must_ have gloves, or I won't go,\" cried Meg decidedly. \"Gloves are more important than anything else; you can't dance without them, and if you don't I should be _so_ mortified.\"\n\n\"Then I'll stay still. I don't care much for company dancing; it's no fun to go sailing round; I like to fly about and cut capers.\"\n\n\"You can't ask mother for new ones, they are so expensive, and you are so careless. She said, when you spoilt the others, that she shouldn't get you any more this winter. Can't you make them do?\" asked Meg anxiously.\n\n\"I can hold them crumpled up in my hand, so no one will know how stained they are; that's all I can do. No! I'll tell you how we can manage--each wear one good one and carry a bad one; don't you see?\"\n\n\"Your hands are bigger than mine, and you will stretch my glove dreadfully,\" began Meg, whose gloves were a tender point with her.\n\n\"Then I'll go without. I don't care what people say!\" cried Jo, taking up her book.\n\n\"You may have it, you may! only don't stain it, and do behave nicely. Don't put your hands behind you, or stare, or say 'Christopher Columbus!' will you?\"\n\n\"Don't worry about me; I'll be as prim as I can, and not get into any scrapes, if I can help it. Now go and answer your note, and let me finish this splendid story.\"\n\nSo Meg went away to \"accept with thanks,\" look over her dress, and sing blithely as she did up her one real lace frill; while Jo finished her story, her four apples, and had a game of romps with Scrabble.\n\nOn New-Year's Eve the parlor was deserted, for the two younger girls played dressing-maids, and the two elder were absorbed in the all-important business of \"getting ready for the party.\" Simple as the toilets were, there was a great deal of running up and down, laughing and talking, and at one time a strong smell of burnt hair pervaded the house. Meg wanted a few curls about her face, and Jo undertook to pinch the papered locks with a pair of hot tongs.\n\n\"Ought they to smoke like that?\" asked Beth, from her perch on the bed.\n\n\"It's the dampness drying,\" replied Jo.\n\n\"What a queer smell! it's like burnt feathers,\" observed Amy, smoothing her own pretty curls with a superior air.\n\n\"There, now I'll take off the papers and you'll see a cloud of little ringlets,\" said Jo, putting down the tongs.\n\nShe did take off the papers, but no cloud of ringlets appeared, for the hair came with the papers, and the horrified hair-dresser laid a row of little scorched bundles on the bureau before her victim.\n\n\"Oh, oh, oh! what _have_ you done? I'm spoilt! I can't go! My hair, oh, my hair!\" wailed Meg, looking with despair at the uneven frizzle on her forehead.\n\n\"Just my luck! you shouldn't have asked me to do it; I always spoil everything. I'm so sorry, but the tongs were too hot, and so I've made a mess,\" groaned poor Jo, regarding the black pancakes with tears of regret.\n\n\"It isn't spoilt; just frizzle it, and tie your ribbon so the ends come on your forehead a bit, and it will look like the last fashion. I've seen many girls do it so,\" said Amy consolingly.\n\n\"Serves me right for trying to be fine. I wish I'd let my hair alone,\" cried Meg petulantly.\n\n\"So do I, it was so smooth and pretty. But it will soon grow out again,\" said Beth, coming to kiss and comfort the shorn sheep.\n\nAfter various lesser mishaps, Meg was finished at last, and by the united exertions of the family Jo's hair was got up and her dress on. They looked very well in their simple suits,--Meg in silvery drab, with a blue velvet snood, lace frills, and the pearl pin; Jo in maroon, with a stiff, gentlemanly linen collar, and a white chrysanthemum or two for her only ornament. Each put on one nice light glove, and carried one soiled one, and all pronounced the effect \"quite easy and fine.\" Meg's high-heeled slippers were very tight, and hurt her, though she would not own it, and Jo's nineteen hair-pins all seemed stuck straight into her head, which was not exactly comfortable; but, dear me, let us be elegant or die!\n\n\"Have a good time, dearies!\" said Mrs. March, as the sisters went daintily down the walk. \"Don't eat much supper, and come away at eleven, when I send Hannah for you.\" As the gate clashed behind them, a voice cried from a window,--\n\n\"Girls, girls! _have_ you both got nice pocket-handkerchiefs?\"\n\n\"Yes, yes, spandy nice, and Meg has cologne on hers,\" cried Jo, adding, with a laugh, as they went on, \"I do believe Marmee would ask that if we were all running away from an earthquake.\"\n\n\"It is one of her aristocratic tastes, and quite proper, for a real lady is always known by neat boots, gloves, and handkerchief,\" replied Meg, who had a good many little \"aristocratic tastes\" of her own.\n\n\"Now don't forget to keep the bad breadth out of sight, Jo. Is my sash right? and does my hair look _very_ bad?\" said Meg, as she turned from the glass in Mrs. Gardiner's dressing-room, after a prolonged prink.\n\n\"I know I shall forget. If you see me doing anything wrong, just remind me by a wink, will you?\" returned Jo, giving her collar a twitch and her head a hasty brush.\n\n\"No, winking isn't lady-like; I'll lift my eyebrows if anything is wrong, and nod if you are all right. Now hold your shoulders straight, and take short steps, and don't shake hands if you are introduced to any one: it isn't the thing.\"\n\n\"How _do_ you learn all the proper ways? I never can. Isn't that music gay?\"\n\nDown they went, feeling a trifle timid, for they seldom went to parties, and, informal as this little gathering was, it was an event to them. Mrs. Gardiner, a stately old lady, greeted them kindly, and handed them over to the eldest of her six daughters. Meg knew Sallie, and was at her ease very soon; but Jo, who didn't care much for girls or girlish gossip, stood about, with her back carefully against the wall, and felt as much out of place as a colt in a flower-garden. Half a dozen jovial lads were talking about skates in another part of the room, and she longed to go and join them, for skating was one of the joys of her life. She telegraphed her wish to Meg, but the eyebrows went up so alarmingly that she dared not stir. No one came to talk to her, and one by one the group near her dwindled away, till she was left alone. She could not roam about and amuse herself, for the burnt breadth would show, so she stared at people rather forlornly till the dancing began. Meg was asked at once, and the tight slippers tripped about so briskly that none would have guessed the pain their wearer suffered smilingly. Jo saw a big red-headed youth approaching her corner, and fearing he meant to engage her, she slipped into a curtained recess, intending to peep and enjoy herself in peace. Unfortunately, another bashful person had chosen the same refuge; for, as the curtain fell behind her, she found herself face to face with the \"Laurence boy.\"\n\n\"Dear me, I didn't know any one was here!\" stammered Jo, preparing to back out as speedily as she had bounced in.\n\nBut the boy laughed, and said pleasantly, though he looked a little startled,--\n\n\"Don't mind me; stay, if you like.\"\n\n\"Sha'n't I disturb you?\"\n\n\"Not a bit; I only came here because I don't know many people, and felt rather strange at first, you know.\"\n\n\"So did I. Don't go away, please, unless you'd rather.\"\n\nThe boy sat down again and looked at his pumps, till Jo said, trying to be polite and easy,--\n\n\"I think I've had the pleasure of seeing you before; you live near us, don't you?\"\n\n\"Next door\"; and he looked up and laughed outright, for Jo's prim manner was rather funny when he remembered how they had chatted about cricket when he brought the cat home.\n\nThat put Jo at her ease; and she laughed too, as she said, in her heartiest way,--\n\n\"We did have such a good time over your nice Christmas present.\"\n\n\"Grandpa sent it.\"\n\n\"But you put it into his head, didn't you, now?\"\n\n\"How is your cat, Miss March?\" asked the boy, trying to look sober, while his black eyes shone with fun.\n\n\"Nicely, thank you, Mr. Laurence; but I am not Miss March, I'm only Jo,\" returned the young lady.\n\n\"I'm not Mr. Laurence, I'm only Laurie.\"\n\n\"Laurie Laurence,--what an odd name!\"\n\n\"My first name is Theodore, but I don't like it, for the fellows called me Dora, so I made them say Laurie instead.\"\n\n\"I hate my name, too--so sentimental! I wish every one would say Jo, instead of Josephine. How did you make the boys stop calling you Dora?\"\n\n\"I thrashed 'em.\"\n\n\"I can't thrash Aunt March, so I suppose I shall have to bear it\"; and Jo resigned herself with a sigh.\n\n\"Don't you like to dance, Miss Jo?\" asked Laurie, looking as if he thought the name suited her.\n\n\"I like it well enough if there is plenty of room, and every one is lively. In a place like this I'm sure to upset something, tread on people's toes, or do something dreadful, so I keep out of mischief, and let Meg sail about. Don't you dance?\"\n\n\"Sometimes; you see I've been abroad a good many years, and haven't been into company enough yet to know how you do things here.\"\n\n\"Abroad!\" cried Jo. \"Oh, tell me about it! I love dearly to hear people describe their travels.\"\n\nLaurie didn't seem to know where to begin; but Jo's eager questions soon set him going, and he told her how he had been at school in Vevay, where the boys never wore hats, and had a fleet of boats on the lake, and for holiday fun went walking trips about Switzerland with their teachers.\n\n\"Don't I wish I'd been there!\" cried Jo. \"Did you go to Paris?\"\n\n\"We spent last winter there.\"\n\n\"Can you talk French?\"\n\n\"We were not allowed to speak any thing else at Vevay.\"\n\n\"Do say some! I can read it, but can't pronounce.\"\n\n\"Quel nom a cette jeune demoiselle en les pantoufles jolis?\" said Laurie good-naturedly.\n\n\"How nicely you do it! Let me see,--you said, 'Who is the young lady in the pretty slippers,' didn't you?\"\n\n\"Oui, mademoiselle.\"\n\n\"It's my sister Margaret, and you knew it was! Do you think she is pretty?\"\n\n\"Yes; she makes me think of the German girls, she looks so fresh and quiet, and dances like a lady.\"\n\nJo quite glowed with pleasure at this boyish praise of her sister, and stored it up to repeat to Meg. Both peeped and criticised and chatted, till they felt like old acquaintances. Laurie's bashfulness soon wore off; for Jo's gentlemanly demeanor amused and set him at his ease, and Jo was her merry self again, because her dress was forgotten, and nobody lifted their eyebrows at her. She liked the \"Laurence boy\" better than ever, and took several good looks at him, so that she might describe him to the girls; for they had no brothers, very few male cousins, and boys were almost unknown creatures to them.\n\n\"Curly black hair; brown skin; big, black eyes; handsome nose; fine teeth; small hands and feet; taller than I am; very polite, for a boy, and altogether jolly. Wonder how old he is?\"\n\nIt was on the tip of Jo's tongue to ask; but she checked herself in time, and, with unusual tact, tried to find out in a roundabout way.\n\n\"I suppose you are going to college soon? I see you pegging away at your books,--no, I mean studying hard\"; and Jo blushed at the dreadful \"pegging\" which had escaped her.\n\nLaurie smiled, but didn't seem shocked, and answered, with a shrug,--\n\n\"Not for a year or two; I won't go before seventeen, anyway.\"\n\n\"Aren't you but fifteen?\" asked Jo, looking at the tall lad, whom she had imagined seventeen already.\n\n\"Sixteen, next month.\"\n\n\"How I wish I was going to college! You don't look as if you liked it.\"\n\n\"I hate it! Nothing but grinding or skylarking. And I don't like the way fellows do either, in this country.\"\n\n\"What do you like?\"\n\n\"To live in Italy, and to enjoy myself in my own way.\"\n\nJo wanted very much to ask what his own way was; but his black brows looked rather threatening as he knit them; so she changed the subject by saying, as her foot kept time, \"That's a splendid polka! Why don't you go and try it?\"\n\n\"If you will come too,\" he answered, with a gallant little bow.\n\n\"I can't; for I told Meg I wouldn't, because--\" There Jo stopped, and looked undecided whether to tell or to laugh.\n\n\"Because what?\" asked Laurie curiously.\n\n\"You won't tell?\"\n\n\"Well, I have a bad trick of standing before the fire, and so I burn my frocks, and I scorched this one; and, though it's nicely mended, it shows, and Meg told me to keep still, so no one would see it. You may laugh, if you want to; it is funny, I know.\"\n\nBut Laurie didn't laugh; he only looked down a minute, and the expression of his face puzzled Jo, when he said very gently,--\n\n\"Never mind that; I'll tell you how we can manage: there's a long hall out there, and we can dance grandly, and no one will see us. Please come?\"\n\nJo thanked him, and gladly went, wishing she had two neat gloves, when she saw the nice, pearl-colored ones her partner wore. The hall was empty, and they had a grand polka; for Laurie danced well, and taught her the German step, which delighted Jo, being full of swing and spring. When the music stopped, they sat down on the stairs to get their breath; and Laurie was in the midst of an account of a students' festival at Heidelberg, when Meg appeared in search of her sister. She beckoned, and Jo reluctantly followed her into a side-room, where she found her on a sofa, holding her foot, and looking pale.\n\n\"I've sprained my ankle. That stupid high heel turned, and gave me a sad wrench. It aches so, I can hardly stand, and I don't know how I'm ever going to get home,\" she said, rocking to and fro in pain.\n\n\"I knew you'd hurt your feet with those silly shoes. I'm sorry. But I don't see what you can do, except get a carriage, or stay here all night,\" answered Jo, softly rubbing the poor ankle as she spoke.\n\n\"I can't have a carriage, without its costing ever so much. I dare say I can't get one at all; for most people come in their own, and it's a long way to the stable, and no one to send.\"\n\n\"I'll go.\"\n\"No, indeed! It's past nine, and dark as Egypt. I can't stop here, for the house is full. Sallie has some girls staying with her. I'll rest till Hannah comes, and then do the best I can.\"\n\n\"I'll ask Laurie; he will go,\" said Jo, looking relieved as the idea occurred to her.\n\n\"Mercy, no! Don't ask or tell any one. Get me my rubbers, and put these slippers with our things. I can't dance any more; but as soon as supper is over, watch for Hannah, and tell me the minute she comes.\"\n\n\"They are going out to supper now. I'll stay with you; I'd rather.\"\n\n\"No, dear, run along, and bring me some coffee. I'm so tired, I can't stir!\"\n\nSo Meg reclined, with rubbers well hidden, and Jo went blundering away to the dining-room, which she found after going into a china-closet, and opening the door of a room where old Mr. Gardiner was taking a little private refreshment. Making a dart at the table, she secured the coffee, which she immediately spilt, thereby making the front of her dress as bad as the back.\n\n\"Oh, dear, what a blunderbuss I am!\" exclaimed Jo, finishing Meg's glove by scrubbing her gown with it.\n\n\"Can I help you?\" said a friendly voice; and there was Laurie, with a full cup in one hand and a plate of ice in the other.\n\n\"I was trying to get something for Meg, who is very tired, and some one shook me; and here I am, in a nice state,\" answered Jo, glancing dismally from the stained skirt to the coffee-colored glove.\n\n\"Too bad! I was looking for some one to give this to. May I take it to your sister?\"\n\n\"Oh, thank you! I'll show you where she is. I don't offer to take it myself, for I should only get into another scrape if I did.\"\n\nJo led the way; and, as if used to waiting on ladies, Laurie drew up a little table, brought a second instalment of coffee and ice for Jo, and was so obliging that even particular Meg pronounced him a \"nice boy.\" They had a merry time over the bonbons and mottoes, and were in the midst of a quiet game of \"Buzz,\" with two or three other young people who had strayed in, when Hannah appeared. Meg forgot her foot, and rose so quickly that she was forced to catch hold of Jo, with an exclamation of pain.\n\n\"Hush! Don't say anything,\" she whispered, adding aloud, \"It's nothing. I turned my foot a little, that's all\"; and limped up-stairs to put her things on.\n\nHannah scolded, Meg cried, and Jo was at her wits' end, till she decided to take things into her own hands. Slipping out, she ran down, and, finding a servant, asked if he could get her a carriage. It happened to be a hired waiter, who knew nothing about the neighborhood; and Jo was looking round for help, when Laurie, who had heard what she said, came up, and offered his grandfather's carriage, which had just come for him, he said.\n\n\"It's so early! You can't mean to go yet?\" began Jo, looking relieved, but hesitating to accept the offer.\n\n\"I always go early,--I do, truly! Please let me take you home? It's all on my way, you know, and it rains, they say.\"\n\nThat settled it; and, telling him of Meg's mishap, Jo gratefully accepted, and rushed up to bring down the rest of the party. Hannah hated rain as much as a cat does; so she made no trouble, and they rolled away in the luxurious close carriage, feeling very festive and elegant. Laurie went on the box; so Meg could keep her foot up, and the girls talked over their party in freedom.\n\n\"I had a capital time. Did you?\" asked Jo, rumpling up her hair, and making herself comfortable.\n\n\"Yes, till I hurt myself. Sallie's friend, Annie Moffat, took a fancy to me, and asked me to come and spend a week with her, when Sallie does. She is going in the spring, when the opera comes; and it will be perfectly splendid, if mother only lets me go,\" answered Meg, cheering up at the thought.\n\n\"I saw you dancing with the red-headed man I ran away from. Was he nice?\"\n\n\"Oh, very! His hair is auburn, not red; and he was very polite, and I had a delicious redowa with him.\"\n\n\"He looked like a grasshopper in a fit, when he did the new step. Laurie and I couldn't help laughing. Did you hear us?\"\n\n\"No; but it was very rude. What _were_ you about all that time, hidden away there?\"\n\nJo told her adventures, and, by the time she had finished, they were at home. With many thanks, they said \"Good night,\" and crept in, hoping to disturb no one; but the instant their door creaked, two little night-caps bobbed up, and two sleepy but eager voices cried out,--\n\n\"Tell about the party! tell about the party!\"\n\nWith what Meg called \"a great want of manners,\" Jo had saved some bonbons for the little girls; and they soon subsided, after hearing the most thrilling events of the evening.\n\n\"I declare, it really seems like being a fine young lady, to come home from the party in a carriage, and sit in my dressing-gown, with a maid to wait on me,\" said Meg, as Jo bound up her foot with arnica, and brushed her hair.\n\n\"I don't believe fine young ladies enjoy themselves a bit more than we do, in spite of our burnt hair, old gowns, one glove apiece, and tight slippers that sprain our ankles when we are silly enough to wear them.\" And I think Jo was quite right.\n\n\"Oh dear, how hard it does seem to take up our packs and go on,\" sighed Meg, the morning after the party; for, now the holidays were over, the week of merry-making did not fit her for going on easily with the task she never liked.\n\n\"I wish it was Christmas or New-Year all the time; wouldn't it be fun?\" answered Jo, yawning dismally.\n\n\"We shouldn't enjoy ourselves half so much as we do now. But it does seem so nice to have little suppers and bouquets, and go to parties, and drive home, and read and rest, and not work. It's like other people, you know, and I always envy girls who do such things; I'm so fond of luxury,\" said Meg, trying to decide which of two shabby gowns was the least shabby.\n\n\"Well, we can't have it, so don't let us grumble, but shoulder our bundles and trudge along as cheerfully as Marmee does. I'm sure Aunt March is a regular Old Man of the Sea to me, but I suppose when I've learned to carry her without complaining, she will tumble off, or get so light that I sha'n't mind her.\"\n\nThis idea tickled Jo's fancy, and put her in good spirits; but Meg didn't brighten, for her burden, consisting of four spoilt children, seemed heavier than ever. She hadn't heart enough even to make herself pretty, as usual, by putting on a blue neck-ribbon, and dressing her hair in the most becoming way.\n\n\"Where's the use of looking nice, when no one sees me but those cross midgets, and no one cares whether I'm pretty or not?\" she muttered, shutting her drawer with a jerk. \"I shall have to toil and moil all my days, with only little bits of fun now and then, and get old and ugly and sour, because I'm poor, and can't enjoy my life as other girls do. It's a shame!\"\n\nSo Meg went down, wearing an injured look, and wasn't at all agreeable at breakfast-time. Every one seemed rather out of sorts, and inclined to croak. Beth had a headache, and lay on the sofa, trying to comfort herself with the cat and three kittens; Amy was fretting because her lessons were not learned, and she couldn't find her rubbers; Jo _would_ whistle and make a great racket getting ready; Mrs. March was very busy trying to finish a letter, which must go at once; and Hannah had the grumps, for being up late didn't suit her.\n\n\"There never _was_ such a cross family!\" cried Jo, losing her temper when she had upset an inkstand, broken both boot-lacings, and sat down upon her hat.\n\n\"You're the crossest person in it!\" returned Amy, washing out the sum, that was all wrong, with the tears that had fallen on her slate.\n\n\"Beth, if you don't keep these horrid cats down cellar I'll have them drowned,\" exclaimed Meg angrily, as she tried to get rid of the kitten, which had scrambled up her back, and stuck like a burr just out of reach.\n\nJo laughed, Meg scolded, Beth implored, and Amy wailed, because she couldn't remember how much nine times twelve was.\n\n\"Girls, girls, do be quiet one minute! I _must_ get this off by the early mail, and you drive me distracted with your worry,\" cried Mrs. March, crossing out the third spoilt sentence in her letter.\n\nThere was a momentary lull, broken by Hannah, who stalked in, laid two hot turn-overs on the table, and stalked out again. These turn-overs were an institution; and the girls called them \"muffs,\" for they had no others, and found the hot pies very comforting to their hands on cold mornings. Hannah never forgot to make them, no matter how busy or grumpy she might be, for the walk was long and bleak; the poor things got no other lunch, and were seldom home before two.\n\n\"Cuddle your cats, and get over your headache, Bethy. Good-by, Marmee; we are a set of rascals this morning, but we'll come home regular angels. Now then, Meg!\" and Jo tramped away, feeling that the pilgrims were not setting out as they ought to do.\n\nThey always looked back before turning the corner, for their mother was always at the window, to nod and smile, and wave her hand to them. Somehow it seemed as if they couldn't have got through the day without that; for, whatever their mood might be, the last glimpse of that motherly face was sure to affect them like sunshine.\n\n\"If Marmee shook her fist instead of kissing her hand to us, it would serve us right, for more ungrateful wretches than we are were never seen,\" cried Jo, taking a remorseful satisfaction in the snowy walk and bitter wind.\n\n\"Don't use such dreadful expressions,\" said Meg, from the depths of the vail in which she had shrouded herself like a nun sick of the world.\n\n\"I like good strong words, that mean something,\" replied Jo, catching her hat as it took a leap off her head, preparatory to flying away altogether.\n\n\"Call yourself any names you like; but _I_ am neither a rascal nor a wretch, and I don't choose to be called so.\"\n\n\"You're a blighted being, and decidedly cross to-day because you can't sit in the lap of luxury all the time. Poor dear, just wait till I make my fortune, and you shall revel in carriages and ice-cream and high-heeled slippers and posies and red-headed boys to dance with.\"\n\n\"How ridiculous you are, Jo!\" but Meg laughed at the nonsense, and felt better in spite of herself.\n\n\"Lucky for you I am; for if I put on crushed airs, and tried to be dismal, as you do, we should be in a nice state. Thank goodness, I can always find something funny to keep me up. Don't croak any more, but come home jolly, there's a dear.\"\n\nJo gave her sister an encouraging pat on the shoulder as they parted for the day, each going a different way, each hugging her little warm turn-over, and each trying to be cheerful in spite of wintry weather, hard work, and the unsatisfied desires of pleasure-loving youth.\n\nWhen Mr. March lost his property in trying to help an unfortunate friend, the two oldest girls begged to be allowed to do something toward their own support, at least. Believing that they could not begin too early to cultivate energy, industry, and independence, their parents consented, and both fell to work with the hearty good-will which in spite of all obstacles, is sure to succeed at last. Margaret found a place as nursery governess, and felt rich with her small salary. As she said, she _was_ \"fond of luxury,\" and her chief trouble was poverty. She found it harder to bear than the others, because she could remember a time when home was beautiful, life full of ease and pleasure, and want of any kind unknown. She tried not to be envious or discontented, but it was very natural that the young girl should long for pretty things, gay friends, accomplishments, and a happy life. At the Kings' she daily saw all she wanted, for the children's older sisters were just out, and Meg caught frequent glimpses of dainty ball-dresses and bouquets, heard lively gossip about theatres, concerts, sleighing parties, and merry-makings of all kinds, and saw money lavished on trifles which would have been so precious to her. Poor Meg seldom complained, but a sense of injustice made her feel bitter toward every one sometimes, for she had not yet learned to know how rich she was in the blessings which alone can make life happy.\n\nJo happened to suit Aunt March, who was lame, and needed an active person to wait upon her. The childless old lady had offered to adopt one of the girls when the troubles came, and was much offended because her offer was declined. Other friends told the Marches that they had lost all chance of being remembered in the rich old lady's will; but the unworldly Marches only said,--\n\n\"We can't give up our girls for a dozen fortunes. Rich or poor, we will keep together and be happy in one another.\"\n\nThe old lady wouldn't speak to them for a time, but happening to meet Jo at a friend's, something in her comical face and blunt manners struck the old lady's fancy, and she proposed to take her for a companion. This did not suit Jo at all; but she accepted the place since nothing better appeared, and, to every one's surprise, got on remarkably well with her irascible relative. There was an occasional tempest, and once Jo had marched home, declaring she couldn't bear it any longer; but Aunt March always cleared up quickly, and sent for her back again with such urgency that she could not refuse, for in her heart she rather liked the peppery old lady.\n\nI suspect that the real attraction was a large library of fine books, which was left to dust and spiders since Uncle March died. Jo remembered the kind old gentleman, who used to let her build railroads and bridges with his big dictionaries, tell her stories about the queer pictures in his Latin books, and buy her cards of gingerbread whenever he met her in the street. The dim, dusty room, with the busts staring down from the tall book-cases, the cosy chairs, the globes, and, best of all, the wilderness of books, in which she could wander where she liked, made the library a region of bliss to her. The moment Aunt March took her nap, or was busy with company, Jo hurried to this quiet place, and, curling herself up in the easy-chair, devoured poetry, romance, history, travels, and pictures, like a regular book-worm. But, like all happiness, it did not last long; for as sure as she had just reached the heart of the story, the sweetest verse of the song, or the most perilous adventure of her traveller, a shrill voice called, \"Josy-phine! Josy-phine!\" and she had to leave her paradise to wind yarn, wash the poodle, or read Belsham's Essays by the hour together.\n\nJo's ambition was to do something very splendid; what it was she had no idea, as yet, but left it for time to tell her; and, meanwhile, found her greatest affliction in the fact that she couldn't read, run, and ride as much as she liked. A quick temper, sharp tongue, and restless spirit were always getting her into scrapes, and her life was a series of ups and downs, which were both comic and pathetic. But the training she received at Aunt March's was just what she needed; and the thought that she was doing something to support herself made her happy, in spite of the perpetual \"Josy-phine!\"\n\nBeth was too bashful to go to school; it had been tried, but she suffered so much that it was given up, and she did her lessons at home, with her father. Even when he went away, and her mother was called to devote her skill and energy to Soldiers' Aid Societies, Beth went faithfully on by herself, and did the best she could. She was a housewifely little creature, and helped Hannah keep home neat and comfortable for the workers, never thinking of any reward but to be loved. Long, quiet days she spent, not lonely nor idle, for her little world was peopled with imaginary friends, and she was by nature a busy bee. There were six dolls to be taken up and dressed every morning, for Beth was a child still, and loved her pets as well as ever. Not one whole or handsome one among them; all were outcasts till Beth took them in; for, when her sisters outgrew these idols, they passed to her, because Amy would have nothing old or ugly. Beth cherished them all the more tenderly for that very reason, and set up a hospital for infirm dolls. No pins were ever stuck into their cotton vitals; no harsh words or blows were ever given them; no neglect ever saddened the heart of the most repulsive: but all were fed and clothed, nursed and caressed, with an affection which never failed. One forlorn fragment of _dollanity_ had belonged to Jo; and, having led a tempestuous life, was left a wreck in the rag-bag, from which dreary poorhouse it was rescued by Beth, and taken to her refuge. Having no top to its head, she tied on a neat little cap, and, as both arms and legs were gone, she hid these deficiencies by folding it in a blanket, and devoting her best bed to this chronic invalid. If any one had known the care lavished on that dolly, I think it would have touched their hearts, even while they laughed. She brought it bits of bouquets; she read to it, took it out to breathe the air, hidden under her coat; she sung it lullabys, and never went to bed without kissing its dirty face, and whispering tenderly, \"I hope you'll have a good night, my poor dear.\"\n\nBeth had her troubles as well as the others; and not being an angel, but a very human little girl, she often \"wept a little weep,\" as Jo said, because she couldn't take music lessons and have a fine piano. She loved music so dearly, tried so hard to learn, and practised away so patiently at the jingling old instrument, that it did seem as if some one (not to hint Aunt March) ought to help her. Nobody did, however, and nobody saw Beth wipe the tears off the yellow keys, that wouldn't keep in tune, when she was all alone. She sang like a little lark about her work, never was too tired to play for Marmee and the girls, and day after day said hopefully to herself, \"I know I'll get my music some time, if I'm good.\"\n\nThere are many Beths in the world, shy and quiet, sitting in corners till needed, and living for others so cheerfully that no one sees the sacrifices till the little cricket on the hearth stops chirping, and the sweet, sunshiny presence vanishes, leaving silence and shadow behind.\n\nIf anybody had asked Amy what the greatest trial of her life was, she would have answered at once, \"My nose.\" When she was a baby, Jo had accidentally dropped her into the coal-hod, and Amy insisted that the fall had ruined her nose forever. It was not big, nor red, like poor \"Petrea's\"; it was only rather flat, and all the pinching in the world could not give it an aristocratic point. No one minded it but herself, and it was doing its best to grow, but Amy felt deeply the want of a Grecian nose, and drew whole sheets of handsome ones to console herself.\n\"Little Raphael,\" as her sisters called her, had a decided talent for drawing, and was never so happy as when copying flowers, designing fairies, or illustrating stories with queer specimens of art. Her teachers complained that, instead of doing her sums, she covered her slate with animals; the blank pages of her atlas were used to copy maps on; and caricatures of the most ludicrous description came fluttering out of all her books at unlucky moments. She got through her lessons as well as she could, and managed to escape reprimands by being a model of deportment. She was a great favorite with her mates, being good-tempered, and possessing the happy art of pleasing without effort. Her little airs and graces were much admired, so were her accomplishments; for beside her drawing, she could play twelve tunes, crochet, and read French without mispronouncing more than two thirds of the words. She had a plaintive way of saying, \"When papa was rich we did so-and-so,\" which was very touching; and her long words were considered \"perfectly elegant\" by the girls.\n\nAmy was in a fair way to be spoilt; for every one petted her, and her small vanities and selfishnesses were growing nicely. One thing, however, rather quenched the vanities; she had to wear her cousin's clothes. Now Florence's mamma hadn't a particle of taste, and Amy suffered deeply at having to wear a red instead of a blue bonnet, unbecoming gowns, and fussy aprons that did not fit. Everything was good, well made, and little worn; but Amy's artistic eyes were much afflicted, especially this winter, when her school dress was a dull purple, with yellow dots, and no trimming.\n\n\"My only comfort,\" she said to Meg, with tears in her eyes, \"is, that mother don't take tucks in my dresses whenever I'm naughty, as Maria Parks' mother does. My dear, it's really dreadful; for sometimes she is so bad, her frock is up to her knees, and she can't come to school. When I think of this _deggerredation_, I feel that I can bear even my flat nose and purple gown, with yellow sky-rockets on it.\"\n\nMeg was Amy's confidant and monitor, and, by some strange attraction of opposites, Jo was gentle Beth's. To Jo alone did the shy child tell her thoughts; and over her big, harum-scarum sister, Beth unconsciously exercised more influence than any one in the family. The two older girls were a great deal to one another, but each took one of the younger into her keeping, and watched over her in her own way; \"playing mother\" they called it, and put their sisters in the places of discarded dolls, with the maternal instinct of little women.\n\n\"Has anybody got anything to tell? It's been such a dismal day I'm really dying for some amusement,\" said Meg, as they sat sewing together that evening.\n\n\"I had a queer time with aunt to-day, and, as I got the best of it, I'll tell you about it,\" began Jo, who dearly loved to tell stories. \"I was reading that everlasting Belsham, and droning away as I always do, for aunt soon drops off, and then I take out some nice book, and read like fury till she wakes up. I actually made myself sleepy; and, before she began to nod, I gave such a gape that she asked me what I meant by opening my mouth wide enough to take the whole book in at once.\n\n\"'I wish I could, and be done with it,' said I, trying not to be saucy.\n\n\"Then she gave me a long lecture on my sins, and told me to sit and think them over while she just 'lost' herself for a moment. She never finds herself very soon; so the minute her cap began to bob, like a top-heavy dahlia, I whipped the 'Vicar of Wakefield' out of my pocket, and read away, with one eye on him, and one on aunt. I'd just got to where they all tumbled into the water, when I forgot, and laughed out loud. Aunt woke up; and, being more good-natured after her nap, told me to read a bit, and show what frivolous work I preferred to the worthy and instructive Belsham. I did my very best, and she liked it, though she only said,--\n\n\"'I don't understand what it's all about. Go back and begin it, child.'\n\n\"Back I went, and made the Primroses as interesting as ever I could. Once I was wicked enough to stop in a thrilling place, and say meekly, 'I'm afraid it tires you, ma'am; sha'n't I stop now?'\n\n\"She caught up her knitting, which had dropped out of her hands, gave me a sharp look through her specs, and said, in her short way,--\n\n\"'Finish the chapter, and don't be impertinent, miss.'\"\n\n\"Did she own she liked it?\" asked Meg.\n\n\"Oh, bless you, no! but she let old Belsham rest; and, when I ran back after my gloves this afternoon, there she was, so hard at the Vicar that she didn't hear me laugh as I danced a jig in the hall, because of the good time coming. What a pleasant life she might have, if she only chose. I don't envy her much, in spite of her money, for after all rich people have about as many worries as poor ones, I think,\" added Jo.\n\n\"That reminds me,\" said Meg, \"that I've got something to tell. It isn't funny, like Jo's story, but I thought about it a good deal as I came home. At the Kings to-day I found everybody in a flurry, and one of the children said that her oldest brother had done something dreadful, and papa had sent him away. I heard Mrs. King crying and Mr. King talking very loud, and Grace and Ellen turned away their faces when they passed me, so I shouldn't see how red their eyes were. I didn't ask any questions, of course; but I felt so sorry for them, and was rather glad I hadn't any wild brothers to do wicked things and disgrace the family.\"\n\n\"I think being disgraced in school is a great deal try_inger_ than anything bad boys can do,\" said Amy, shaking her head, as if her experience of life had been a deep one. \"Susie Perkins came to school to-day with a lovely red carnelian ring; I wanted it dreadfully, and wished I was her with all my might. Well, she drew a picture of Mr. Davis, with a monstrous nose and a hump, and the words, 'Young ladies, my eye is upon you!' coming out of his mouth in a balloon thing. We were laughing over it, when all of a sudden his eye _was_ on us, and he ordered Susie to bring up her slate. She was _parry_lized with fright, but she went, and oh, what _do_ you think he did? He took her by the ear, the ear! just fancy how horrid!--and led her to the recitation platform, and made her stand there half an hour, holding that slate so every one could see.\"\n\n\"Didn't the girls laugh at the picture?\" asked Jo, who relished the scrape.\n\n\"Laugh? Not one! They sat as still as mice; and Susie cried quarts, I know she did. I didn't envy her then; for I felt that millions of carnelian rings wouldn't have made me happy, after that. I never, never should have got over such a agonizing mortification.\" And Amy went on with her work, in the proud consciousness of virtue, and the successful utterance of two long words in a breath.\n\n\"I saw something that I liked this morning, and I meant to tell it at dinner, but I forgot,\" said Beth, putting Jo's topsy-turvy basket in order as she talked. \"When I went to get some oysters for Hannah, Mr. Laurence was in the fish-shop; but he didn't see me, for I kept behind a barrel, and he was busy with Mr. Cutter, the fish-man. A poor woman came in, with a pail and a mop, and asked Mr. Cutter if he would let her do some scrubbing for a bit of fish, because she hadn't any dinner for her children, and had been disappointed of a day's work. Mr. Cutter was in a hurry, and said 'No,' rather crossly; so she was going away, looking hungry and sorry, when Mr. Laurence hooked up a big fish with the crooked end of his cane, and held it out to her. She was so glad and surprised, she took it right in her arms, and thanked him over and over. He told her to 'go along and cook it,' and she hurried off, so happy! Wasn't it good of him? Oh, she did look so funny, hugging the big, slippery fish, and hoping Mr. Laurence's bed in heaven would be 'aisy.'\"\n\nWhen they had laughed at Beth's story, they asked their mother for one; and, after a moment's thought, she said soberly,--\n\n\"As I sat cutting out blue flannel jackets to-day, at the rooms, I felt very anxious about father, and thought how lonely and helpless we should be, if anything happened to him. It was not a wise thing to do; but I kept on worrying, till an old man came in, with an order for some clothes. He sat down near me, and I began to talk to him; for he looked poor and tired and anxious.\n\n\"'Have you sons in the army?' I asked; for the note he brought was not to me.\n\n\"'Yes, ma'am. I had four, but two were killed, one is a prisoner, and I'm going to the other, who is very sick in a Washington hospital,' he answered quietly.\n\n\"'You have done a great deal for your country, sir,' I said, feeling respect now, instead of pity.\n\n\"'Not a mite more than I ought, ma'am. I'd go myself, if I was any use; as I ain't, I give my boys, and give 'em free.'\n\n\"He spoke so cheerfully, looked so sincere, and seemed so glad to give his all, that I was ashamed of myself. I'd given one man, and thought it too much, while he gave four, without grudging them. I had all my girls to comfort me at home; and his last son was waiting, miles away, to say 'good by' to him, perhaps! I felt so rich, so happy, thinking of my blessings, that I made him a nice bundle, gave him some money, and thanked him heartily for the lesson he had taught me.\"\n\n\"Tell another story, mother,--one with a moral to it, like this. I like to think about them afterwards, if they are real, and not too preachy,\" said Jo, after a minute's silence.\n\nMrs. March smiled, and began at once; for she had told stories to this little audience for many years, and knew how to please them.\n\n\"Once upon a time, there were four girls, who had enough to eat and drink and wear, a good many comforts and pleasures, kind friends and parents, who loved them dearly, and yet they were not contented.\" (Here the listeners stole sly looks at one another, and began to sew diligently.) \"These girls were anxious to be good, and made many excellent resolutions; but they did not keep them very well, and were constantly saying, 'If we only had this,' or 'If we could only do that,' quite forgetting how much they already had, and how many pleasant things they actually could do. So they asked an old woman what spell they could use to make them happy, and she said, 'When you feel discontented, think over your blessings, and be grateful.'\" (Here Jo looked up quickly, as if about to speak, but changed her mind, seeing that the story was not done yet.)\n\n\"Being sensible girls, they decided to try her advice, and soon were surprised to see how well off they were. One discovered that money couldn't keep shame and sorrow out of rich people's houses; another that, though she was poor, she was a great deal happier, with her youth, health, and good spirits, than a certain fretful, feeble old lady, who couldn't enjoy her comforts; a third that, disagreeable as it was to help get dinner, it was harder still to have to go begging for it; and the fourth, that even carnelian rings were not so valuable as good behavior. So they agreed to stop complaining, to enjoy the blessings already possessed, and try to deserve them, lest they should be taken away entirely, instead of increased; and I believe they were never disappointed, or sorry that they took the old woman's advice.\"\n\n\"Now, Marmee, that is very cunning of you to turn our own stories against us, and give us a sermon instead of a romance!\" cried Meg.\n\n\"I like that kind of sermon. It's the sort father used to tell us,\" said Beth thoughtfully, putting the needles straight on Jo's cushion.\n\n\"I don't complain near as much as the others do, and I shall be more careful than ever now; for I've had warning from Susie's downfall,\" said Amy morally.\n\n\"We needed that lesson, and we won't forget it. If we do, you just say to us, as old Chloe did in 'Uncle Tom,' 'Tink ob yer marcies, chillen! tink ob yer marcies!'\" added Jo, who could not, for the life of her, help getting a morsel of fun out of the little sermon, though she took it to heart as much as any of them.\n\n# BEING NEIGHBORLY.\n\n\"What in the world are you going to do now, Jo?\" asked Meg, one snowy afternoon, as her sister came tramping through the hall, in rubber boots, old sack and hood, with a broom in one hand and a shovel in the other.\n\n\"Going out for exercise,\" answered Jo, with a mischievous twinkle in her eyes.\n\n\"I should think two long walks this morning would have been enough! It's cold and dull out; and I advise you to stay, warm and dry, by the fire, as I do,\" said Meg, with a shiver.\n\n\"Never take advice! Can't keep still all day, and, not being a pussycat, I don't like to doze by the fire. I like adventures, and I'm going to find some.\"\n\nMeg went back to toast her feet and read \"Ivanhoe\"; and Jo began to dig paths with great energy. The snow was light, and with her broom she soon swept a path all round the garden, for Beth to walk in when the sun came out; and the invalid dolls needed air. Now, the garden separated the Marches' house from that of Mr. Laurence. Both stood in a suburb of the city, which was still country-like, with groves and lawns, large gardens, and quiet streets. A low hedge parted the two estates. On one side was an old, brown house, looking rather bare and shabby, robbed of the vines that in summer covered its walls, and the flowers which then surrounded it. On the other side was a stately stone mansion, plainly betokening every sort of comfort and luxury, from the big coach-house and well-kept grounds to the conservatory and the glimpses of lovely things one caught between the rich curtains. Yet it seemed a lonely, lifeless sort of house; for no children frolicked on the lawn, no motherly face ever smiled at the windows, and few people went in and out, except the old gentleman and his grandson.\n\nTo Jo's lively fancy, this fine house seemed a kind of enchanted palace, full of splendors and delights, which no one enjoyed. She had long wanted to behold these hidden glories, and to know the \"Laurence boy,\" who looked as if he would like to be known, if he only knew how to begin. Since the party, she had been more eager than ever, and had planned many ways of making friends with him; but he had not been seen lately, and Jo began to think he had gone away, when she one day spied a brown face at an upper window, looking wistfully down into their garden, where Beth and Amy were snow-balling one another.\n\n\"That boy is suffering for society and fun,\" she said to herself. \"His grandpa does not know what's good for him, and keeps him shut up all alone. He needs a party of jolly boys to play with, or somebody young and lively. I've a great mind to go over and tell the old gentleman so!\"\n\nThe idea amused Jo, who liked to do daring things, and was always scandalizing Meg by her queer performances. The plan of \"going over\" was not forgotten; and when the snowy afternoon came, Jo resolved to try what could be done. She saw Mr. Laurence drive off, and then sallied out to dig her way down to the hedge, where she paused, and took a survey. All quiet,--curtains down at the lower windows; servants out of sight, and nothing human visible but a curly black head leaning on a thin hand at the upper window.\n\n\"There he is,\" thought Jo, \"poor boy! all alone and sick this dismal day. It's a shame! I'll toss up a snow-ball, and make him look out, and then say a kind word to him.\"\n\nUp went a handful of soft snow, and the head turned at once, showing a face which lost its listless look in a minute, as the big eyes brightened and the mouth began to smile. Jo nodded and laughed, and flourished her broom as she called out,--\n\n\"How do you do? Are you sick?\"\n\nLaurie opened the window, and croaked out as hoarsely as a raven,--\n\n\"Better, thank you. I've had a bad cold, and been shut up a week.\"\n\n\"I'm sorry. What do you amuse yourself with?\"\n\n\"Nothing; it's as dull as tombs up here.\"\n\n\"Don't you read?\"\n\n\"Not much; they won't let me.\"\n\n\"Can't somebody read to you?\"\n\n\"Grandpa does, sometimes; but my books don't interest him, and I hate to ask Brooke all the time.\"\n\n\"Have some one come and see you, then.\"\n\n\"There isn't any one I'd like to see. Boys make such a row, and my head is weak.\"\n\n\"Isn't there some nice girl who'd read and amuse you? Girls are quiet, and like to play nurse.\"\n\n\"Don't know any.\"\n\n\"You know us,\" began Jo, then laughed, and stopped.\n\n\"So I do! Will you come, please?\" cried Laurie.\n\n\"I'm not quiet and nice; but I'll come, if mother will let me. I'll go ask her. Shut that window, like a good boy, and wait till I come.\"\n\nWith that, Jo shouldered her broom and marched into the house, wondering what they would all say to her. Laurie was in a flutter of excitement at the idea of having company, and flew about to get ready; for, as Mrs. March said, he was \"a little gentleman,\" and did honor to the coming guest by brushing his curly pate, putting on a fresh collar, and trying to tidy up the room, which, in spite of half a dozen servants, was anything but neat. Presently there came a loud ring, then a decided voice, asking for \"Mr. Laurie,\" and a surprised-looking servant came running up to announce a young lady.\n\n\"All right, show her up, it's Miss Jo,\" said Laurie, going to the door of his little parlor to meet Jo, who appeared, looking rosy and kind and quite at her ease, with a covered dish in one hand and Beth's three kittens in the other.\n\n\"Here I am, bag and baggage,\" she said briskly. \"Mother sent her love, and was glad if I could do anything for you. Meg wanted me to bring some of her blanc-mange; she makes it very nicely, and Beth thought her cats would be comforting. I knew you'd laugh at them, but I couldn't refuse, she was so anxious to do something.\"\n\nIt so happened that Beth's funny loan was just the thing; for, in laughing over the kits, Laurie forgot his bashfulness, and grew sociable at once.\n\n\"That looks too pretty to eat,\" he said, smiling with pleasure, as Jo uncovered the dish, and showed the blanc-mange, surrounded by a garland of green leaves, and the scarlet flowers of Amy's pet geranium.\n\n\"It isn't anything, only they all felt kindly, and wanted to show it. Tell the girl to put it away for your tea: it's so simple, you can eat it; and, being soft, it will slip down without hurting your sore throat. What a cosy room this is!\"\n\n\"It might be if it was kept nice; but the maids are lazy, and I don't know how to make them mind. It worries me, though.\"\n\n\"I'll right it up in two minutes; for it only needs to have the hearth brushed, so,--and the things made straight on the mantel-piece so,--and the books put here, and the bottles there, and your sofa turned from the light, and the pillows plumped up a bit. Now, then, you're fixed.\"\n\nAnd so he was; for, as she laughed and talked, Jo had whisked things into place, and given quite a different air to the room. Laurie watched her in respectful silence; and when she beckoned him to his sofa, he sat down with a sigh of satisfaction, saying gratefully,--\n\n\"How kind you are! Yes, that's what it wanted. Now please take the big chair, and let me do something to amuse my company.\"\n\n\"No; I came to amuse you. Shall I read aloud?\" and Jo looked affectionately toward some inviting books near by.\n\n\"Thank you; I've read all those, and if you don't mind, I'd rather talk,\" answered Laurie.\n\n\"Not a bit; I'll talk all day if you'll only set me going. Beth says I never know when to stop.\"\n\n\"Is Beth the rosy one, who stays at home a good deal, and sometimes goes out with a little basket?\" asked Laurie, with interest.\n\n\"Yes, that's Beth; she's my girl, and a regular good one she is, too.\"\n\n\"The pretty one is Meg, and the curly-haired one is Amy, I believe?\"\n\n\"How did you find that out?\"\n\nLaurie colored up, but answered frankly, \"Why, you see, I often hear you calling to one another, and when I'm alone up here, I can't help looking over at your house, you always seem to be having such good times. I beg your pardon for being so rude, but sometimes you forget to put down the curtain at the window where the flowers are; and when the lamps are lighted, it's like looking at a picture to see the fire, and you all round the table with your mother; her face is right opposite, and it looks so sweet behind the flowers, I can't help watching it. I haven't got any mother, you know;\" and Laurie poked the fire to hide a little twitching of the lips that he could not control.\n\nThe solitary, hungry look in his eyes went straight to Jo's warm heart. She had been so simply taught that there was no nonsense in her head, and at fifteen she was as innocent and frank as any child. Laurie was sick and lonely; and, feeling how rich she was in home-love and happiness, she gladly tried to share it with him. Her face was very friendly and her sharp voice unusually gentle as she said,--\n\n\"We'll never draw that curtain any more, and I give you leave to look as much as you like. I just wish, though, instead of peeping, you'd come over and see us. Mother is so splendid, she'd do you heaps of good, and Beth would sing to you if _I_ begged her to, and Amy would dance; Meg and I would make you laugh over our funny stage properties, and we'd have jolly times. Wouldn't your grandpa let you?\"\n\n\"I think he would, if your mother asked him. He's very kind, though he does not look so; and he lets me do what I like, pretty much, only he's afraid I might be a bother to strangers,\" began Laurie, brightening more and more.\n\n\"We are not strangers, we are neighbors, and you needn't think you'd be a bother. We _want_ to know you, and I've been trying to do it this ever so long. We haven't been here a great while, you know, but we have got acquainted with all our neighbors but you.\"\n\n\"You see grandpa lives among his books, and doesn't mind much what happens outside. Mr. Brooke, my tutor, doesn't stay here, you know, and I have no one to go about with me, so I just stop at home and get on as I can.\"\n\n\"That's bad. You ought to make an effort, and go visiting everywhere you are asked; then you'll have plenty of friends, and pleasant places to go to. Never mind being bashful; it won't last long if you keep going.\"\n\nLaurie turned red again, but wasn't offended at being accused of bashfulness; for there was so much good-will in Jo, it was impossible not to take her blunt speeches as kindly as they were meant.\n\n\"Do you like your school?\" asked the boy, changing the subject, after a little pause, during which he stared at the fire, and Jo looked about her, well pleased.\n\n\"Don't go to school; I'm a business man--girl, I mean. I go to wait on my great-aunt, and a dear, cross old soul she is, too,\" answered Jo.\n\nLaurie opened his mouth to ask another question; but remembering just in time that it wasn't manners to make too many inquiries into people's affairs, he shut it again, and looked uncomfortable. Jo liked his good breeding, and didn't mind having a laugh at Aunt March, so she gave him a lively description of the fidgety old lady, her fat poodle, the parrot that talked Spanish, and the library where she revelled. Laurie enjoyed that immensely; and when she told about the prim old gentleman who came once to woo Aunt March, and, in the middle of a fine speech, how Poll had tweaked his wig off to his great dismay, the boy lay back and laughed till the tears ran down his cheeks, and a maid popped her head in to see what was the matter.\n\n\"Oh! that does me no end of good. Tell on, please,\" he said, taking his face out of the sofa-cushion, red and shining with merriment.\n\nMuch elated with her success, Jo did \"tell on,\" all about their plays and plans, their hopes and fears for father, and the most interesting events of the little world in which the sisters lived. Then they got to talking about books; and to Jo's delight, she found that Laurie loved them as well as she did, and had read even more than herself.\n\n\"If you like them so much, come down and see ours. Grandpa is out, so you needn't be afraid,\" said Laurie, getting up.\n\n\"I'm not afraid of anything,\" returned Jo, with a toss of the head.\n\n\"I don't believe you are!\" exclaimed the boy, looking at her with much admiration, though he privately thought she would have good reason to be a trifle afraid of the old gentleman, if she met him in some of his moods.\n\nThe atmosphere of the whole house being summer-like, Laurie led the way from room to room, letting Jo stop to examine whatever struck her fancy; and so at last they came to the library, where she clapped her hands, and pranced, as she always did when especially delighted. It was lined with books, and there were pictures and statues, and distracting little cabinets full of coins and curiosities, and sleepy-hollow chairs, and queer tables, and bronzes; and, best of all, a great open fireplace, with quaint tiles all round it.\n\n\"What richness!\" sighed Jo, sinking into the depth of a velvet chair, and gazing about her with an air of intense satisfaction. \"Theodore Laurence, you ought to be the happiest boy in the world,\" she added impressively.\n\n\"A fellow can't live on books,\" said Laurie, shaking his head, as he perched on a table opposite.\n\nBefore he could say more, a bell rung, and Jo flew up, exclaiming with alarm, \"Mercy me! it's your grandpa!\"\n\n\"Well, what if it is? You are not afraid of anything, you know,\" returned the boy, looking wicked.\n\n\"I think I am a little bit afraid of him, but I don't know why I should be. Marmee said I might come, and I don't think you're any the worse for it,\" said Jo, composing herself, though she kept her eyes on the door.\n\n\"I'm a great deal better for it, and ever so much obliged. I'm only afraid you are very tired talking to me; it was _so_ pleasant, I couldn't bear to stop,\" said Laurie gratefully.\n\n\"The doctor to see you, sir,\" and the maid beckoned as she spoke.\n\n\"Would you mind if I left you for a minute? I suppose I must see him,\" said Laurie.\n\n\"Don't mind me. I'm as happy as a cricket here,\" answered Jo.\n\nLaurie went away, and his guest amused herself in her own way. She was standing before a fine portrait of the old gentleman, when the door opened again, and, without turning, she said decidedly, \"I'm sure now that I shouldn't be afraid of him, for he's got kind eyes, though his mouth is grim, and he looks as if he had a tremendous will of his own. He isn't as handsome as _my_ grandfather, but I like him.\"\n\n\"Thank you, ma'am,\" said a gruff voice behind her; and there, to her great dismay, stood old Mr. Laurence.\n\nPoor Jo blushed till she couldn't blush any redder, and her heart began to beat uncomfortably fast as she thought what she had said. For a minute a wild desire to run away possessed her; but that was cowardly, and the girls would laugh at her: so she resolved to stay, and get out of the scrape as she could. A second look showed her that the living eyes, under the bushy gray eyebrows, were kinder even than the painted ones; and there was a sly twinkle in them, which lessened her fear a good deal. The gruff voice was gruffer than ever, as the old gentleman said abruptly, after that dreadful pause, \"So you're not afraid of me, hey?\"\n\n\"Not much, sir.\"\n\n\"And you don't think me as handsome as your grandfather?\"\n\n\"Not quite, sir.\"\n\n\"And I've got a tremendous will, have I?\"\n\n\"I only said I thought so.\"\n\n\"But you like me, in spite of it?\"\n\n\"Yes, I do, sir.\"\n\nThat answer pleased the old gentleman; he gave a short laugh, shook hands with her, and, putting his finger under her chin, turned up her face, examined it gravely, and let it go, saying, with a nod, \"You've got your grandfather's spirit, if you haven't his face. He _was_ a fine man, my dear; but, what is better, he was a brave and an honest one, and I was proud to be his friend.\"\n\n\"Thank you, sir;\" and Jo was quite comfortable after that, for it suited her exactly.\n\n\"What have you been doing to this boy of mine, hey?\" was the next question, sharply put.\n\n\"Only trying to be neighborly, sir;\" and Jo told how her visit came about.\n\n\"You think he needs cheering up a bit, do you?\"\n\n\"Yes, sir; he seems a little lonely, and young folks would do him good perhaps. We are only girls, but we should be glad to help if we could, for we don't forget the splendid Christmas present you sent us,\" said Jo eagerly.\n\n\"Tut, tut, tut! that was the boy's affair. How is the poor woman?\"\n\n\"Doing nicely, sir;\" and off went Jo, talking very fast, as she told all about the Hummels, in whom her mother had interested richer friends than they were.\n\n\"Just her father's way of doing good. I shall come and see your mother some fine day. Tell her so. There's the tea-bell; we have it early, on the boy's account. Come down, and go on being neighborly.\"\n\n\"If you'd like to have me, sir.\"\n\n\"Shouldn't ask you, if I didn't;\" and Mr. Laurence offered her his arm with old-fashioned courtesy.\n\n\"What _would_ Meg say to this?\" thought Jo, as she was marched away, while her eyes danced with fun as she imagined herself telling the story at home.\n\n\"Hey! Why, what the dickens has come to the fellow?\" said the old gentleman, as Laurie came running down stairs, and brought up with a start of surprise at the astonishing sight of Jo arm-in-arm with his redoubtable grandfather.\n\n\"I didn't know you'd come, sir,\" he began, as Jo gave him a triumphant little glance.\n\n\"That's evident, by the way you racket down stairs. Come to your tea, sir, and behave like a gentleman;\" and having pulled the boy's hair by way of a caress, Mr. Laurence walked on, while Laurie went through a series of comic evolutions behind their backs, which nearly produced an explosion of laughter from Jo.\n\nThe old gentleman did not say much as he drank his four cups of tea, but he watched the young people, who soon chatted away like old friends, and the change in his grandson did not escape him. There was color, light, and life in the boy's face now, vivacity in his manner, and genuine merriment in his laugh.\n\n\"She's right; the lad _is_ lonely. I'll see what these little girls can do for him,\" thought Mr. Laurence, as he looked and listened. He liked Jo, for her odd, blunt ways suited him; and she seemed to understand the boy almost as well as if she had been one herself.\n\nIf the Laurences had been what Jo called \"prim and poky,\" she would not have got on at all, for such people always made her shy and awkward; but finding them free and easy, she was so herself, and made a good impression. When they rose she proposed to go, but Laurie said he had something more to show her, and took her away to the conservatory, which had been lighted for her benefit. It seemed quite fairylike to Jo, as she went up and down the walks, enjoying the blooming walls on either side, the soft light, the damp sweet air, and the wonderful vines and trees that hung above her,--while her new friend cut the finest flowers till his hands were full; then he tied them up, saying, with the happy look Jo liked to see, \"Please give these to your mother, and tell her I like the medicine she sent me very much.\"\n\nThey found Mr. Laurence standing before the fire in the great drawing-room, but Jo's attention was entirely absorbed by a grand piano, which stood open.\n\n\"Do you play?\" she asked, turning to Laurie with a respectful expression.\n\n\"Sometimes,\" he answered modestly.\n\n\"Please do now. I want to hear it, so I can tell Beth.\"\n\n\"Won't you first?\"\n\n\"Don't know how; too stupid to learn, but I love music dearly.\"\n\nSo Laurie played, and Jo listened, with her nose luxuriously buried in heliotrope and tea-roses. Her respect and regard for the \"Laurence boy\" increased very much, for he played remarkably well, and didn't put on any airs. She wished Beth could hear him, but she did not say so; only praised him till he was quite abashed, and his grandfather came to the rescue. \"That will do, that will do, young lady. Too many sugar-plums are not good for him. His music isn't bad, but I hope he will do as well in more important things. Going? Well, I'm much obliged to you, and I hope you'll come again. My respects to your mother. Good-night, Doctor Jo.\"\n\nHe shook hands kindly, but looked as if something did not please him. When they got into the hall, Jo asked Laurie if she had said anything amiss. He shook his head.\n\n\"No, it was me; he doesn't like to hear me play.\"\n\n\"Why not?\"\n\n\"I'll tell you some day. John is going home with you, as I can't.\"\n\n\"No need of that; I am not a young lady, and it's only a step. Take care of yourself, won't you?\"\n\n\"Yes; but you will come again, I hope?\"\n\n\"If you promise to come and see us after you are well.\"\n\n\"I will.\"\n\n\"Good-night, Laurie!\"\n\n\"Good-night, Jo, good-night!\"\n\nWhen all the afternoon's adventures had been told, the family felt inclined to go visiting in a body, for each found something very attractive in the big house on the other side of the hedge. Mrs. March wanted to talk of her father with the old man who had not forgotten him; Meg longed to walk in the conservatory; Beth sighed for the grand piano; and Amy was eager to see the fine pictures and statues.\n\n\"Mother, why didn't Mr. Laurence like to have Laurie play?\" asked Jo, who was of an inquiring disposition.\n\n\"I am not sure, but I think it was because his son, Laurie's father, married an Italian lady, a musician, which displeased the old man, who is very proud. The lady was good and lovely and accomplished, but he did not like her, and never saw his son after he married. They both died when Laurie was a little child, and then his grandfather took him home. I fancy the boy, who was born in Italy, is not very strong, and the old man is afraid of losing him, which makes him so careful. Laurie comes naturally by his love of music, for he is like his mother, and I dare say his grandfather fears that he may want to be a musician; at any rate, his skill reminds him of the woman he did not like, and so he 'glowered,' as Jo said.\"\n\n\"Dear me, how romantic!\" exclaimed Meg.\n\n\"How silly!\" said Jo. \"Let him be a musician, if he wants to, and not plague his life out sending him to college, when he hates to go.\"\n\n\"That's why he has such handsome black eyes and pretty manners, I suppose. Italians are always nice,\" said Meg, who was a little sentimental.\n\n\"What do you know about his eyes and his manners? You never spoke to him, hardly,\" cried Jo, who was _not_ sentimental.\n\n\"I saw him at the party, and what you tell shows that he knows how to behave. That was a nice little speech about the medicine mother sent him.\"\n\n\"He meant the blanc-mange, I suppose.\"\n\n\"How stupid you are, child! He meant you, of course.\"\n\n\"Did he?\" and Jo opened her eyes as if it had never occurred to her before.\n\n\"I never saw such a girl! You don't know a compliment when you get it,\" said Meg, with the air of a young lady who knew all about the matter.\n\n\"I think they are great nonsense, and I'll thank you not to be silly, and spoil my fun. Laurie's a nice boy, and I like him, and I won't have any sentimental stuff about compliments and such rubbish. We'll all be good to him, because he hasn't got any mother, and he _may_ come over and see us, mayn't he, Marmee?\"\n\n\"Yes, Jo, your little friend is very welcome, and I hope Meg will remember that children should be children as long as they can.\"\n\n\"I don't call myself a child, and I'm not in my teens yet,\" observed Amy. \"What do you say, Beth?\"\n\n\"I was thinking about our 'Pilgrim's Progress,'\" answered Beth, who had not heard a word. \"How we got out of the Slough and through the Wicket Gate by resolving to be good, and up the steep hill by trying; and that maybe the house over there, full of splendid things, is going to be our Palace Beautiful.\"\n\n\"We have got to get by the lions, first,\" said Jo, as if she rather liked the prospect.\n\n# BETH FINDS THE PALACE BEAUTIFUL.\n\nThe big house did prove a Palace Beautiful, though it took some time for all to get in, and Beth found it very hard to pass the lions. Old Mr. Laurence was the biggest one; but after he had called, said something funny or kind to each one of the girls, and talked over old times with their mother, nobody felt much afraid of him, except timid Beth. The other lion was the fact that they were poor and Laurie rich; for this made them shy of accepting favors which they could not return. But, after a while, they found that he considered them the benefactors, and could not do enough to show how grateful he was for Mrs. March's motherly welcome, their cheerful society, and the comfort he took in that humble home of theirs. So they soon forgot their pride, and interchanged kindnesses without stopping to think which was the greater.\n\nAll sorts of pleasant things happened about that time; for the new friendship flourished like grass in spring. Every one liked Laurie, and he privately informed his tutor that \"the Marches were regularly splendid girls.\" With the delightful enthusiasm of youth, they took the solitary boy into their midst, and made much of him, and he found something very charming in the innocent companionship of these simple-hearted girls. Never having known mother or sisters, he was quick to feel the influences they brought about him; and their busy, lively ways made him ashamed of the indolent life he led. He was tired of books, and found people so interesting now that Mr. Brooke was obliged to make very unsatisfactory reports; for Laurie was always playing truant, and running over to the Marches.\n\n\"Never mind; let him take a holiday, and make it up afterwards,\" said the old gentleman. \"The good lady next door says he is studying too hard, and needs young society, amusement, and exercise. I suspect she is right, and that I've been coddling the fellow as if I'd been his grandmother. Let him do what he likes, as long as he is happy. He can't get into mischief in that little nunnery over there; and Mrs. March is doing more for him than we can.\"\n\nWhat good times they had, to be sure! Such plays and tableaux, such sleigh-rides and skating frolics, such pleasant evenings in the old parlor, and now and then such gay little parties at the great house. Meg could walk in the conservatory whenever she liked, and revel in bouquets; Jo browsed over the new library voraciously, and convulsed the old gentleman with her criticisms; Amy copied pictures, and enjoyed beauty to her heart's content; and Laurie played \"lord of the manor\" in the most delightful style.\n\nBut Beth, though yearning for the grand piano, could not pluck up courage to go to the \"Mansion of Bliss,\" as Meg called it. She went once with Jo; but the old gentleman, not being aware of her infirmity, stared at her so hard from under his heavy eyebrows, and said \"Hey!\" so loud, that he frightened her so much her \"feet chattered on the floor,\" she told her mother; and she ran away, declaring she would never go there any more, not even for the dear piano. No persuasions or enticements could overcome her fear, till, the fact coming to Mr. Laurence's ear in some mysterious way, he set about mending matters. During one of the brief calls he made, he artfully led the conversation to music, and talked away about great singers whom he had seen, fine organs he had heard, and told such charming anecdotes that Beth found it impossible to stay in her distant corner, but crept nearer and nearer, as if fascinated. At the back of his chair she stopped, and stood listening, with her great eyes wide open, and her cheeks red with the excitement of this unusual performance. Taking no more notice of her than if she had been a fly, Mr. Laurence talked on about Laurie's lessons and teachers; and presently, as if the idea had just occurred to him, he said to Mrs. March,--\n\n\"The boy neglects his music now, and I'm glad of it, for he was getting too fond of it. But the piano suffers for want of use. Wouldn't some of your girls like to run over, and practise on it now and then, just to keep it in tune, you know, ma'am?\"\n\nBeth took a step forward, and pressed her hands tightly together to keep from clapping them, for this was an irresistible temptation; and the thought of practising on that splendid instrument quite took her breath away. Before Mrs. March could reply, Mr. Laurence went on with an odd little nod and smile,--\n\n\"They needn't see or speak to any one, but run in at any time; for I'm shut up in my study at the other end of the house, Laurie is out a great deal, and the servants are never near the drawing-room after nine o'clock.\"\n\nHere he rose, as if going, and Beth made up her mind to speak, for that last arrangement left nothing to be desired. \"Please tell the young ladies what I say; and if they don't care to come, why, never mind.\" Here a little hand slipped into his, and Beth looked up at him with a face full of gratitude, as she said, in her earnest yet timid way,--\n\n\"O sir, they do care, very, very much!\"\n\n\"Are you the musical girl?\" he asked, without any startling \"Hey!\" as he looked down at her very kindly.\n\n\"I'm Beth. I love it dearly, and I'll come, if you are quite sure nobody will hear me--and be disturbed,\" she added, fearing to be rude, and trembling at her own boldness as she spoke.\n\n\"Not a soul, my dear. The house is empty half the day; so come, and drum away as much as you like, and I shall be obliged to you.\"\n\n\"How kind you are, sir!\"\n\nBeth blushed like a rose under the friendly look he wore; but she was not frightened now, and gave the big hand a grateful squeeze, because she had no words to thank him for the precious gift he had given her. The old gentleman softly stroked the hair off her forehead, and, stooping down, he kissed her, saying, in a tone few people ever heard,--\n\n\"I had a little girl once, with eyes like these. God bless you, my dear! Good day, madam;\" and away he went, in a great hurry.\n\nBeth had a rapture with her mother, and then rushed up to impart the glorious news to her family of invalids, as the girls were not at home. How blithely she sung that evening, and how they all laughed at her, because she woke Amy in the night by playing the piano on her face in her sleep. Next day, having seen both the old and young gentleman out of the house, Beth, after two or three retreats, fairly got in at the side-door, and made her way, as noiselessly as any mouse, to the drawing-room, where her idol stood. Quite by accident, of course, some pretty, easy music lay on the piano; and, with trembling fingers, and frequent stops to listen and look about, Beth at last touched the great instrument, and straightway forgot her fear, herself, and everything else but the unspeakable delight which the music gave her, for it was like the voice of a beloved friend.\n\nShe stayed till Hannah came to take her home to dinner; but she had no appetite, and could only sit and smile upon every one in a general state of beatitude.\n\nAfter that, the little brown hood slipped through the hedge nearly every day, and the great drawing-room was haunted by a tuneful spirit that came and went unseen. She never knew that Mr. Laurence often opened his study-door to hear the old-fashioned airs he liked; she never saw Laurie mount guard in the hall to warn the servants away; she never suspected that the exercise-books and new songs which she found in the rack were put there for her especial benefit; and when he talked to her about music at home, she only thought how kind he was to tell things that helped her so much. So she enjoyed herself heartily, and found, what isn't always the case, that her granted wish was all she had hoped. Perhaps it was because she was so grateful for this blessing that a greater was given her; at any rate, she deserved both.\n\n\"Mother, I'm going to work Mr. Laurence a pair of slippers. He is so kind to me, I must thank him, and I don't know any other way. Can I do it?\" asked Beth, a few weeks after that eventful call of his.\n\n\"Yes, dear. It will please him very much, and be a nice way of thanking him. The girls will help you about them, and I will pay for the making up,\" replied Mrs. March, who took peculiar pleasure in granting Beth's requests, because she so seldom asked anything for herself.\n\nAfter many serious discussions with Meg and Jo, the pattern was chosen, the materials bought, and the slippers begun. A cluster of grave yet cheerful pansies, on a deeper purple ground, was pronounced very appropriate and pretty; and Beth worked away early and late, with occasional lifts over hard parts. She was a nimble little needle-woman, and they were finished before any one got tired of them. Then she wrote a very short, simple note, and, with Laurie's help, got them smuggled on to the study-table one morning before the old gentleman was up.\n\nWhen this excitement was over, Beth waited to see what would happen. All that day passed, and a part of the next, before any acknowledgment arrived, and she was beginning to fear she had offended her crotchety friend. On the afternoon of the second day, she went out to do an errand, and give poor Joanna, the invalid doll, her daily exercise. As she came up the street, on her return, she saw three, yes, four, heads popping in and out of the parlor windows, and the moment they saw her, several hands were waved, and several joyful voices screamed,--\n\n\"Here's a letter from the old gentleman! Come quick, and read it!\"\n\n\"O Beth, he's sent you--\" began Amy, gesticulating with unseemly energy; but she got no further, for Jo quenched her by slamming down the window.\nBeth hurried on in a flutter of suspense. At the door, her sisters seized and bore her to the parlor in a triumphal procession, all pointing, and all saying at once, \"Look there! look there!\" Beth did look, and turned pale with delight and surprise; for there stood a little cabinet-piano, with a letter lying on the glossy lid, directed, like a sign-board, to \"Miss Elizabeth March.\"\n\n\"For me?\" gasped Beth, holding on to Jo, and feeling as if she should tumble down, it was such an overwhelming thing altogether.\n\n\"Yes; all for you, my precious! Isn't it splendid of him? Don't you think he's the dearest old man in the world? Here's the key in the letter. We didn't open it, but we are dying to know what he says,\" cried Jo, hugging her sister, and offering the note.\n\n\"You read it! I can't, I feel so queer! Oh, it is too lovely!\" and Beth hid her face in Jo's apron, quite upset by her present.\n\nJo opened the paper, and began to laugh, for the first words she saw were,--\n\n\"_Dear Madam_,--\"\n\n\"How nice it sounds! I wish some one would write to me so!\" said Amy, who thought the old-fashioned address very elegant.\n\n\"'I have had many pairs of slippers in my life, but I never had any that suited me so well as yours,'\" continued Jo. \"'Heart's-ease is my favorite flower, and these will always remind me of the gentle giver. I like to pay my debts; so I know you will allow \"the old gentleman\" to send you something which once belonged to the little granddaughter he lost. With hearty thanks and best wishes, I remain,\n\n\"'Your grateful friend and humble servant,\n\n\"There, Beth, that's an honor to be proud of, I'm sure! Laurie told me how fond Mr. Laurence used to be of the child who died, and how he kept all her little things carefully. Just think, he's given you her piano. That comes of having big blue eyes and loving music,\" said Jo, trying to soothe Beth, who trembled, and looked more excited than she had ever been before.\n\n\"See the cunning brackets to hold candles, and the nice green silk, puckered up, with a gold rose in the middle, and the pretty rack and stool, all complete,\" added Meg, opening the instrument and displaying its beauties.\n\n\"'Your humble servant, James Laurence'; only think of his writing that to you. I'll tell the girls. They'll think it's splendid,\" said Amy, much impressed by the note.\n\n\"Try it, honey. Let's hear the sound of the baby-pianny,\" said Hannah, who always took a share in the family joys and sorrows.\n\nSo Beth tried it; and every one pronounced it the most remarkable piano ever heard. It had evidently been newly tuned and put in apple-pie order; but, perfect as it was, I think the real charm of it lay in the happiest of all happy faces which leaned over it, as Beth lovingly touched the beautiful black and white keys and pressed the bright pedals.\n\n\"You'll have to go and thank him,\" said Jo, by way of a joke; for the idea of the child's really going never entered her head.\n\n\"Yes, I mean to. I guess I'll go now, before I get frightened thinking about it.\" And, to the utter amazement of the assembled family, Beth walked deliberately down the garden, through the hedge, and in at the Laurences' door.\n\n\"Well, I wish I may die if it ain't the queerest thing I ever see! The pianny has turned her head! She'd never have gone in her right mind,\" cried Hannah, staring after her, while the girls were rendered quite speechless by the miracle.\n\nThey would have been still more amazed if they had seen what Beth did afterward. If you will believe me, she went and knocked at the study-door before she gave herself time to think; and when a gruff voice called out, \"Come in!\" she did go in, right up to Mr. Laurence, who looked quite taken aback, and held out her hand, saying, with only a small quaver in her voice, \"I came to thank you, sir, for--\" But she didn't finish; for he looked so friendly that she forgot her speech, and, only remembering that he had lost the little girl he loved, she put both arms round his neck, and kissed him.\n\nIf the roof of the house had suddenly flown off, the old gentleman wouldn't have been more astonished; but he liked it,--oh, dear, yes, he liked it amazingly!--and was so touched and pleased by that confiding little kiss that all his crustiness vanished; and he just set her on his knee, and laid his wrinkled cheek against her rosy one, feeling as if he had got his own little granddaughter back again. Beth ceased to fear him from that moment, and sat there talking to him as cosily as if she had known him all her life; for love casts out fear, and gratitude can conquer pride. When she went home, he walked with her to her own gate, shook hands cordially, and touched his hat as he marched back again, looking very stately and erect, like a handsome, soldierly old gentleman, as he was.\n\nWhen the girls saw that performance, Jo began to dance a jig, by way of expressing her satisfaction; Amy nearly fell out of the window in her surprise; and Meg exclaimed, with uplifted hands, \"Well, I do believe the world is coming to an end!\"\n\n# AMY'S VALLEY OF HUMILIATION.\n\n\"That boy is a perfect Cyclops, isn't he?\" said Amy, one day, as Laurie clattered by on horseback, with a flourish of his whip as he passed.\n\n\"How dare you say so, when he's got both his eyes? and very handsome ones they are, too,\" cried Jo, who resented any slighting remarks about her friend.\n\n\"I didn't say anything about his eyes, and I don't see why you need fire up when I admire his riding.\"\n\n\"Oh, my goodness! that little goose means a centaur, and she called him a Cyclops,\" exclaimed Jo, with a burst of laughter.\n\n\"You needn't be so rude; it's only a 'lapse of lingy,' as Mr. Davis says,\" retorted Amy, finishing Jo with her Latin. \"I just wish I had a little of the money Laurie spends on that horse,\" she added, as if to herself, yet hoping her sisters would hear.\n\n\"Why?\" asked Meg kindly, for Jo had gone off in another laugh at Amy's second blunder.\n\n\"I need it so much; I'm dreadfully in debt, and it won't be my turn to have the rag-money for a month.\"\n\n\"In debt, Amy? What do you mean?\" and Meg looked sober.\n\n\"Why, I owe at least a dozen pickled limes, and I can't pay them, you know, till I have money, for Marmee forbade my having anything charged at the shop.\"\n\n\"Tell me all about it. Are limes the fashion now? It used to be pricking bits of rubber to make balls;\" and Meg tried to keep her countenance, Amy looked so grave and important.\n\n\"Why, you see, the girls are always buying them, and unless you want to be thought mean, you must do it, too. It's nothing but limes now, for every one is sucking them in their desks in school-time, and trading them off for pencils, bead-rings, paper dolls, or something else, at recess. If one girl likes another, she gives her a lime; if she's mad with her, she eats one before her face, and don't offer even a suck. They treat by turns; and I've had ever so many, but haven't returned them; and I ought, for they are debts of honor, you know.\"\n\n\"How much will pay them off, and restore your credit?\" asked Meg, taking out her purse.\n\n\"A quarter would more than do it, and leave a few cents over for a treat for you. Don't you like limes?\"\n\n\"Not much; you may have my share. Here's the money. Make it last as long as you can, for it isn't very plenty, you know.\"\n\n\"Oh, thank you! It must be so nice to have pocket-money! I'll have a grand feast, for I haven't tasted a lime this week. I felt delicate about taking any, as I couldn't return them, and I'm actually suffering for one.\"\n\nNext day Amy was rather late at school; but could not resist the temptation of displaying, with pardonable pride, a moist brown-paper parcel, before she consigned it to the inmost recesses of her desk. During the next few minutes the rumor that Amy March had got twenty-four delicious limes (she ate one on the way), and was going to treat, circulated through her \"set,\" and the attentions of her friends became quite overwhelming. Katy Brown invited her to her next party on the spot; Mary Kingsley insisted on lending her her watch till recess; and Jenny Snow, a satirical young lady, who had basely twitted Amy upon her limeless state, promptly buried the hatchet, and offered to furnish answers to certain appalling sums. But Amy had not forgotten Miss Snow's cutting remarks about \"some persons whose noses were not too flat to smell other people's limes, and stuck-up people, who were not too proud to ask for them;\" and she instantly crushed \"that Snow girl's\" hopes by the withering telegram, \"You needn't be so polite all of a sudden, for you won't get any.\"\n\nA distinguished personage happened to visit the school that morning, and Amy's beautifully drawn maps received praise, which honor to her foe rankled in the soul of Miss Snow, and caused Miss March to assume the airs of a studious young peacock. But, alas, alas! pride goes before a fall, and the revengeful Snow turned the tables with disastrous success. No sooner had the guest paid the usual stale compliments, and bowed himself out, than Jenny, under pretence of asking an important question, informed Mr. Davis, the teacher, that Amy March had pickled limes in her desk.\n\nNow Mr. Davis had declared limes a contraband article, and solemnly vowed to publicly ferrule the first person who was found breaking the law. This much-enduring man had succeeded in banishing chewing-gum after a long and stormy war, had made a bonfire of the confiscated novels and newspapers, had suppressed a private post-office, had forbidden distortions of the face, nicknames, and caricatures, and done all that one man could do to keep half a hundred rebellious girls in order. Boys are trying enough to human patience, goodness knows! but girls are infinitely more so, especially to nervous gentlemen, with tyrannical tempers, and no more talent for teaching than Dr. Blimber. Mr. Davis knew any quantity of Greek, Latin, Algebra, and ologies of all sorts, so he was called a fine teacher; and manners, morals, feelings, and examples were not considered of any particular importance. It was a most unfortunate moment for denouncing Amy, and Jenny knew it. Mr. Davis had evidently taken his coffee too strong that morning; there was an east wind, which always affected his neuralgia; and his pupils had not done him the credit which he felt he deserved: therefore, to use the expressive, if not elegant, language of a school-girl, \"he was as nervous as a witch and as cross as a bear.\" The word \"limes\" was like fire to powder; his yellow face flushed, and he rapped on his desk with an energy which made Jenny skip to her seat with unusual rapidity.\n\n\"Young ladies, attention, if you please!\"\n\nAt the stern order the buzz ceased, and fifty pairs of blue, black, gray, and brown eyes were obediently fixed upon his awful countenance.\n\n\"Miss March, come to the desk.\"\n\nAmy rose to comply with outward composure, but a secret fear oppressed her, for the limes weighed upon her conscience.\n\n\"Bring with you the limes you have in your desk,\" was the unexpected command which arrested her before she got out of her seat.\n\n\"Don't take all,\" whispered her neighbor, a young lady of great presence of mind.\n\nAmy hastily shook out half a dozen, and laid the rest down before Mr. Davis, feeling that any man possessing a human heart would relent when that delicious perfume met his nose. Unfortunately, Mr. Davis particularly detested the odor of the fashionable pickle, and disgust added to his wrath.\n\n\"Is that all?\"\n\n\"Not quite,\" stammered Amy.\n\n\"Bring the rest immediately.\"\n\nWith a despairing glance at her set, she obeyed.\n\n\"You are sure there are no more?\"\n\n\"I never lie, sir.\"\n\n\"So I see. Now take these disgusting things two by two, and throw them out of the window.\"\n\nThere was a simultaneous sigh, which created quite a little gust, as the last hope fled, and the treat was ravished from their longing lips. Scarlet with shame and anger, Amy went to and fro six dreadful times; and as each doomed couple--looking oh! so plump and juicy--fell from her reluctant hands, a shout from the street completed the anguish of the girls, for it told them that their feast was being exulted over by the little Irish children, who were their sworn foes. This--this was too much; all flashed indignant or appealing glances at the inexorable Davis, and one passionate lime-lover burst into tears.\n\nAs Amy returned from her last trip, Mr. Davis gave a portentous \"Hem!\" and said, in his most impressive manner,--\n\n\"Young ladies, you remember what I said to you a week ago. I am sorry this has happened, but I never allow my rules to be infringed, and I _never_ break my word. Miss March, hold out your hand.\"\n\nAmy started, and put both hands behind her, turning on him an imploring look which pleaded for her better than the words she could not utter. She was rather a favorite with \"old Davis,\" as, of course, he was called, and it's my private belief that he _would_ have broken his word if the indignation of one irrepressible young lady had not found vent in a hiss. That hiss, faint as it was, irritated the irascible gentleman, and sealed the culprit's fate.\n\n\"Your hand, Miss March!\" was the only answer her mute appeal received; and, too proud to cry or beseech, Amy set her teeth, threw back her head defiantly, and bore without flinching several tingling blows on her little palm. They were neither many nor heavy, but that made no difference to her. For the first time in her life she had been struck; and the disgrace, in her eyes, was as deep as if he had knocked her down.\n\n\"You will now stand on the platform till recess,\" said Mr. Davis, resolved to do the thing thoroughly, since he had begun.\n\nThat was dreadful. It would have been bad enough to go to her seat, and see the pitying faces of her friends, or the satisfied ones of her few enemies; but to face the whole school, with that shame fresh upon her, seemed impossible, and for a second she felt as if she could only drop down where she stood, and break her heart with crying. A bitter sense of wrong, and the thought of Jenny Snow, helped her to bear it; and, taking the ignominious place, she fixed her eyes on the stove-funnel above what now seemed a sea of faces, and stood there, so motionless and white that the girls found it very hard to study, with that pathetic figure before them.\n\nDuring the fifteen minutes that followed, the proud and sensitive little girl suffered a shame and pain which she never forgot. To others it might seem a ludicrous or trivial affair, but to her it was a hard experience; for during the twelve years of her life she had been governed by love alone, and a blow of that sort had never touched her before. The smart of her hand and the ache of her heart were forgotten in the sting of the thought,--\n\n\"I shall have to tell at home, and they will be so disappointed in me!\"\n\nThe fifteen minutes seemed an hour; but they came to an end at last, and the word \"Recess!\" had never seemed so welcome to her before.\n\n\"You can go, Miss March,\" said Mr. Davis, looking, as he felt, uncomfortable.\n\nHe did not soon forget the reproachful glance Amy gave him, as she went, without a word to any one, straight into the ante-room, snatched her things, and left the place \"forever,\" as she passionately declared to herself. She was in a sad state when she got home; and when the older girls arrived, some time later, an indignation meeting was held at once. Mrs. March did not say much, but looked disturbed, and comforted her afflicted little daughter in her tenderest manner. Meg bathed the insulted hand with glycerine and tears; Beth felt that even her beloved kittens would fail as a balm for griefs like this; Jo wrathfully proposed that Mr. Davis be arrested without delay; and Hannah shook her fist at the \"villain,\" and pounded potatoes for dinner as if she had him under her pestle.\n\nNo notice was taken of Amy's flight, except by her mates; but the sharp-eyed demoiselles discovered that Mr. Davis was quite benignant in the afternoon, also unusually nervous. Just before school closed, Jo appeared, wearing a grim expression, as she stalked up to the desk, and delivered a letter from her mother; then collected Amy's property, and departed, carefully scraping the mud from her boots on the door-mat, as if she shook the dust of the place off her feet.\n\n\"Yes, you can have a vacation from school, but I want you to study a little every day, with Beth,\" said Mrs. March, that evening. \"I don't approve of corporal punishment, especially for girls. I dislike Mr. Davis's manner of teaching, and don't think the girls you associate with are doing you any good, so I shall ask your father's advice before I send you anywhere else.\"\n\n\"That's good! I wish all the girls would leave, and spoil his old school. It's perfectly maddening to think of those lovely limes,\" sighed Amy, with the air of a martyr.\n\n\"I am not sorry you lost them, for you broke the rules, and deserved some punishment for disobedience,\" was the severe reply, which rather disappointed the young lady, who expected nothing but sympathy.\n\n\"Do you mean you are glad I was disgraced before the whole school?\" cried Amy.\n\n\"I should not have chosen that way of mending a fault,\" replied her mother; \"but I'm not sure that it won't do you more good than a milder method. You are getting to be rather conceited, my dear, and it is quite time you set about correcting it. You have a good many little gifts and virtues, but there is no need of parading them, for conceit spoils the finest genius. There is not much danger that real talent or goodness will be overlooked long; even if it is, the consciousness of possessing and using it well should satisfy one, and the great charm of all power is modesty.\"\n\n\"So it is!\" cried Laurie, who was playing chess in a corner with Jo. \"I knew a girl, once, who had a really remarkable talent for music, and she didn't know it; never guessed what sweet little things she composed when she was alone, and wouldn't have believed it if any one had told her.\"\n\n\"I wish I'd known that nice girl; maybe she would have helped me, I'm so stupid,\" said Beth, who stood beside him, listening eagerly.\n\n\"You do know her, and she helps you better than any one else could,\" answered Laurie, looking at her with such mischievous meaning in his merry black eyes, that Beth suddenly turned very red, and hid her face in the sofa-cushion, quite overcome by such an unexpected discovery.\n\nJo let Laurie win the game, to pay for that praise of her Beth, who could not be prevailed upon to play for them after her compliment. So Laurie did his best, and sung delightfully, being in a particularly lively humor, for to the Marches he seldom showed the moody side of his character. When he was gone, Amy, who had been pensive all the evening, said suddenly, as if busy over some new idea,--\n\n\"Is Laurie an accomplished boy?\"\n\n\"Yes; he has had an excellent education, and has much talent; he will make a fine man, if not spoilt by petting,\" replied her mother.\n\n\"And he isn't conceited, is he?\" asked Amy.\n\n\"Not in the least; that is why he is so charming, and we all like him so much.\"\n\n\"I see; it's nice to have accomplishments, and be elegant; but not to show off, or get perked up,\" said Amy thoughtfully.\n\n\"These things are always seen and felt in a person's manner and conversation, if modestly used; but it is not necessary to display them,\" said Mrs. March.\n\n\"Any more than it's proper to wear all your bonnets and gowns and ribbons at once, that folks may know you've got them,\" added Jo; and the lecture ended in a laugh.\n\n# JO MEETS APOLLYON.\n\n\"Girls, where are you going?\" asked Amy, coming into their room one Saturday afternoon, and finding them getting ready to go out, with an air of secrecy which excited her curiosity.\n\n\"Never mind; little girls shouldn't ask questions,\" returned Jo sharply.\n\nNow if there _is_ anything mortifying to our feelings, when we are young, it is to be told that; and to be bidden to \"run away, dear,\" is still more trying to us. Amy bridled up at this insult, and determined to find out the secret, if she teased for an hour. Turning to Meg, who never refused her anything very long, she said coaxingly, \"Do tell me! I should think you might let me go, too; for Beth is fussing over her piano, and I haven't got anything to do, and am _so_ lonely.\"\n\n\"I can't, dear, because you aren't invited,\" began Meg; but Jo broke in impatiently, \"Now, Meg, be quiet, or you will spoil it all. You can't go, Amy; so don't be a baby, and whine about it.\"\n\n\"You are going somewhere with Laurie, I know you are; you were whispering and laughing together, on the sofa, last night, and you stopped when I came in. Aren't you going with him?\"\n\n\"Yes, we are; now do be still, and stop bothering.\"\n\nAmy held her tongue, but used her eyes, and saw Meg slip a fan into her pocket.\n\n\"I know! I know! you're going to the theatre to see the 'Seven Castles!'\" she cried; adding resolutely, \"and I _shall_ go, for mother said I might see it; and I've got my rag-money, and it was mean not to tell me in time.\"\n\n\"Just listen to me a minute, and be a good child,\" said Meg soothingly. \"Mother doesn't wish you to go this week, because your eyes are not well enough yet to bear the light of this fairy piece. Next week you can go with Beth and Hannah, and have a nice time.\"\n\n\"I don't like that half as well as going with you and Laurie. Please let me; I've been sick with this cold so long, and shut up, I'm dying for some fun. Do, Meg! I'll be ever so good,\" pleaded Amy, looking as pathetic as she could.\n\n\"Suppose we take her. I don't believe mother would mind, if we bundle her up well,\" began Meg.\n\n\"If _she_ goes _I_ sha'n't; and if I don't, Laurie won't like it; and it will be very rude, after he invited only us, to go and drag in Amy. I should think she'd hate to poke herself where she isn't wanted,\" said Jo crossly, for she disliked the trouble of overseeing a fidgety child, when she wanted to enjoy herself.\n\nHer tone and manner angered Amy, who began to put her boots on, saying, in her most aggravating way, \"I _shall_ go; Meg says I may; and if I pay for myself, Laurie hasn't anything to do with it.\"\n\n\"You can't sit with us, for our seats are reserved, and you mustn't sit alone; so Laurie will give you his place, and that will spoil our pleasure; or he'll get another seat for you, and that isn't proper, when you weren't asked. You sha'n't stir a step; so you may just stay where you are,\" scolded Jo, crosser than ever, having just pricked her finger in her hurry.\n\nSitting on the floor, with one boot on, Amy began to cry, and Meg to reason with her, when Laurie called from below, and the two girls hurried down, leaving their sister wailing; for now and then she forgot her grown-up ways, and acted like a spoilt child. Just as the party was setting out, Amy called over the banisters, in a threatening tone, \"You'll be sorry for this, Jo March; see if you ain't.\"\n\n\"Fiddlesticks!\" returned Jo, slamming the door.\n\nThey had a charming time, for \"The Seven Castles of the Diamond Lake\" were as brilliant and wonderful as heart could wish. But, in spite of the comical red imps, sparkling elves, and gorgeous princes and princesses, Jo's pleasure had a drop of bitterness in it; the fairy queen's yellow curls reminded her of Amy; and between the acts she amused herself with wondering what her sister would do to make her \"sorry for it.\" She and Amy had had many lively skirmishes in the course of their lives, for both had quick tempers, and were apt to be violent when fairly roused. Amy teased Jo, and Jo irritated Amy, and semi-occasional explosions occurred, of which both were much ashamed afterward. Although the oldest, Jo had the least self-control, and had hard times trying to curb the fiery spirit which was continually getting her into trouble; her anger never lasted long, and, having humbly confessed her fault, she sincerely repented, and tried to do better. Her sisters used to say that they rather liked to get Jo into a fury, because she was such an angel afterward. Poor Jo tried desperately to be good, but her bosom enemy was always ready to flame up and defeat her; and it took years of patient effort to subdue it.\n\nWhen they got home, they found Amy reading in the parlor. She assumed an injured air as they came in; never lifted her eyes from her book, or asked a single question. Perhaps curiosity might have conquered resentment, if Beth had not been there to inquire, and receive a glowing description of the play. On going up to put away her best hat, Jo's first look was toward the bureau; for, in their last quarrel, Amy had soothed her feelings by turning Jo's top drawer upside down on the floor. Everything was in its place, however; and after a hasty glance into her various closets, bags, and boxes, Jo decided that Amy had forgiven and forgotten her wrongs.\n\nThere Jo was mistaken; for next day she made a discovery which produced a tempest. Meg, Beth, and Amy were sitting together, late in the afternoon, when Jo burst into the room, looking excited, and demanding breathlessly, \"Has any one taken my book?\"\n\nMeg and Beth said \"No,\" at once, and looked surprised; Amy poked the fire, and said nothing. Jo saw her color rise, and was down upon her in a minute.\n\n\"Amy, you've got it?\"\n\n\"No, I haven't.\"\n\n\"You know where it is, then?\"\n\n\"No, I don't.\"\n\n\"That's a fib!\" cried Jo, taking her by the shoulders, and looking fierce enough to frighten a much braver child than Amy.\n\n\"It isn't. I haven't got it, don't know where it is now, and don't care.\"\n\n\"You know something about it, and you'd better tell at once, or I'll make you,\" and Jo gave her a slight shake.\n\n\"Scold as much as you like, you'll never see your silly old book again,\" cried Amy, getting excited in her turn.\n\n\"Why not?\"\n\n\"I burnt it up.\"\n\n\"What! my little book I was so fond of, and worked over, and meant to finish before father got home? Have you really burnt it?\" said Jo, turning very pale, while her eyes kindled and her hands clutched Amy nervously.\n\n\"Yes, I did! I told you I'd make you pay for being so cross yesterday, and I have, so--\"\n\nAmy got no farther, for Jo's hot temper mastered her, and she shook Amy till her teeth chattered in her head; crying, in a passion of grief and anger,--\n\n\"You wicked, wicked girl! I never can write it again, and I'll never forgive you as long as I live.\"\n\nMeg flew to rescue Amy, and Beth to pacify Jo, but Jo was quite beside herself; and, with a parting box on her sister's ear, she rushed out of the room up to the old sofa in the garret, and finished her fight alone.\n\nThe storm cleared up below, for Mrs. March came home, and, having heard the story, soon brought Amy to a sense of the wrong she had done her sister. Jo's book was the pride of her heart, and was regarded by her family as a literary sprout of great promise. It was only half a dozen little fairy tales, but Jo had worked over them patiently, putting her whole heart into her work, hoping to make something good enough to print. She had just copied them with great care, and had destroyed the old manuscript, so that Amy's bonfire had consumed the loving work of several years. It seemed a small loss to others, but to Jo it was a dreadful calamity, and she felt that it never could be made up to her. Beth mourned as for a departed kitten, and Meg refused to defend her pet; Mrs. March looked grave and grieved, and Amy felt that no one would love her till she had asked pardon for the act which she now regretted more than any of them.\n\nWhen the tea-bell rung, Jo appeared, looking so grim and unapproachable that it took all Amy's courage to say meekly,--\n\n\"Please forgive me, Jo; I'm very, very sorry.\"\n\n\"I never shall forgive you,\" was Jo's stern answer; and, from that moment, she ignored Amy entirely.\n\nNo one spoke of the great trouble,--not even Mrs. March,--for all had learned by experience that when Jo was in that mood words were wasted; and the wisest course was to wait till some little accident, or her own generous nature, softened Jo's resentment, and healed the breach. It was not a happy evening; for, though they sewed as usual, while their mother read aloud from Bremer, Scott, or Edgeworth, something was wanting, and the sweet home-peace was disturbed. They felt this most when singing-time came; for Beth could only play, Jo stood dumb as a stone, and Amy broke down, so Meg and mother sung alone. But, in spite of their efforts to be as cheery as larks, the flute-like voices did not seem to chord as well as usual, and all felt out of tune.\n\nAs Jo received her good-night kiss, Mrs. March whispered gently,--\n\n\"My dear, don't let the sun go down upon your anger; forgive each other, help each other, and begin again to-morrow.\"\n\nJo wanted to lay her head down on that motherly bosom, and cry her grief and anger all away; but tears were an unmanly weakness, and she felt so deeply injured that she really _couldn't_ quite forgive yet. So she winked hard, shook her head, and said, gruffly because Amy was listening,--\n\n\"It was an abominable thing, and she don't deserve to be forgiven.\"\n\nWith that she marched off to bed, and there was no merry or confidential gossip that night.\n\nAmy was much offended that her overtures of peace had been repulsed, and began to wish she had not humbled herself, to feel more injured than ever, and to plume herself on her superior virtue in a way which was particularly exasperating. Jo still looked like a thunder-cloud, and nothing went well all day. It was bitter cold in the morning; she dropped her precious turn-over in the gutter, Aunt March had an attack of fidgets, Meg was pensive, Beth _would_ look grieved and wistful when she got home, and Amy kept making remarks about people who were always talking about being good, and yet wouldn't try, when other people set them a virtuous example.\n\n\"Everybody is so hateful, I'll ask Laurie to go skating. He is always kind and jolly, and will put me to rights, I know,\" said Jo to herself, and off she went.\n\nAmy heard the clash of skates, and looked out with an impatient exclamation,--\n\n\"There! she promised I should go next time, for this is the last ice we shall have. But it's no use to ask such a cross-patch to take me.\"\n\n\"Don't say that; you _were_ very naughty, and it _is_ hard to forgive the loss of her precious little book; but I think she might do it now, and I guess she will, if you try her at the right minute,\" said Meg. \"Go after them; don't say anything till Jo has got good-natured with Laurie, then take a quiet minute, and just kiss her, or do some kind thing, and I'm sure she'll be friends again, with all her heart.\"\n\n\"I'll try,\" said Amy, for the advice suited her; and, after a flurry to get ready, she ran after the friends, who were just disappearing over the hill.\n\nIt was not far to the river, but both were ready before Amy reached them. Jo saw her coming, and turned her back; Laurie did not see, for he was carefully skating along the shore, sounding the ice, for a warm spell had preceded the cold snap.\n\n\"I'll go on to the first bend, and see if it's all right, before we begin to race,\" Amy heard him say, as he shot away, looking like a young Russian, in his fur-trimmed coat and cap.\n\nJo heard Amy panting after her run, stamping her feet and blowing her fingers, as she tried to put her skates on; but Jo never turned, and went slowly zigzagging down the river, taking a bitter, unhappy sort of satisfaction in her sister's troubles. She had cherished her anger till it grew strong, and took possession of her, as evil thoughts and feelings always do, unless cast out at once. As Laurie turned the bend, he shouted back,--\n\n\"Keep near the shore; it isn't safe in the middle.\"\n\nJo heard, but Amy was just struggling to her feet, and did not catch a word. Jo glanced over her shoulder, and the little demon she was harboring said in her ear,--\n\n\"No matter whether she heard or not, let her take care of herself.\"\n\nLaurie had vanished round the bend; Jo was just at the turn, and Amy, far behind, striking out toward the smoother ice in the middle of the river. For a minute Jo stood still, with a strange feeling at her heart; then she resolved to go on, but something held and turned her round, just in time to see Amy throw up her hands and go down, with the sudden crash of rotten ice, the splash of water, and a cry that made Jo's heart stand still with fear. She tried to call Laurie, but her voice was gone; she tried to rush forward, but her feet seemed to have no strength in them; and, for a second, she could only stand motionless, staring, with a terror-stricken face, at the little blue hood above the black water. Something rushed swiftly by her, and Laurie's voice cried out,--\n\n\"Bring a rail; quick, quick!\"\n\nHow she did it, she never knew; but for the next few minutes she worked as if possessed, blindly obeying Laurie, who was quite self-possessed, and, lying flat, held Amy up by his arm and hockey till Jo dragged a rail from the fence, and together they got the child out, more frightened than hurt.\n\n\"Now then, we must walk her home as fast as we can; pile our things on her, while I get off these confounded skates,\" cried Laurie, wrapping his coat round Amy, and tugging away at the straps, which never seemed so intricate before.\n\nShivering, dripping, and crying, they got Amy home; and, after an exciting time of it, she fell asleep, rolled in blankets, before a hot fire. During the bustle Jo had scarcely spoken; but flown about, looking pale and wild, with her things half off, her dress torn, and her hands cut and bruised by ice and rails, and refractory buckles. When Amy was comfortably asleep, the house quiet, and Mrs. March sitting by the bed, she called Jo to her, and began to bind up the hurt hands.\n\n\"Are you sure she is safe?\" whispered Jo, looking remorsefully at the golden head, which might have been swept away from her sight forever under the treacherous ice.\n\n\"Quite safe, dear; she is not hurt, and won't even take cold, I think, you were so sensible in covering and getting her home quickly,\" replied her mother cheerfully.\n\n\"Laurie did it all; I only let her go. Mother, if she _should_ die, it would be my fault\"; and Jo dropped down beside the bed, in a passion of penitent tears, telling all that had happened, bitterly condemning her hardness of heart, and sobbing out her gratitude for being spared the heavy punishment which might have come upon her.\n\n\"It's my dreadful temper! I try to cure it; I think I have, and then it breaks out worse than ever. O mother, what shall I do? what shall I do?\" cried poor Jo, in despair.\n\n\"Watch and pray, dear; never get tired of trying; and never think it is impossible to conquer your fault,\" said Mrs. March, drawing the blowzy head to her shoulder, and kissing the wet cheek so tenderly that Jo cried harder than ever.\n\n\"You don't know, you can't guess how bad it is! It seems as if I could do anything when I'm in a passion; I get so savage, I could hurt any one, and enjoy it. I'm afraid I _shall_ do something dreadful some day, and spoil my life, and make everybody hate me. O mother, help me, do help me!\"\n\n\"I will, my child, I will. Don't cry so bitterly, but remember this day, and resolve, with all your soul, that you will never know another like it. Jo, dear, we all have our temptations, some far greater than yours, and it often takes us all our lives to conquer them. You think your temper is the worst in the world; but mine used to be just like it.\"\n\n\"Yours, mother? Why, you are never angry!\" and, for the moment, Jo forgot remorse in surprise.\n\n\"I've been trying to cure it for forty years, and have only succeeded in controlling it. I am angry nearly every day of my life, Jo; but I have learned not to show it; and I still hope to learn not to feel it, though it may take me another forty years to do so.\"\n\nThe patience and the humility of the face she loved so well was a better lesson to Jo than the wisest lecture, the sharpest reproof. She felt comforted at once by the sympathy and confidence given her; the knowledge that her mother had a fault like hers, and tried to mend it, made her own easier to bear and strengthened her resolution to cure it; though forty years seemed rather a long time to watch and pray, to a girl of fifteen.\n\n\"Mother, are you angry when you fold your lips tight together, and go out of the room sometimes, when Aunt March scolds, or people worry you?\" asked Jo, feeling nearer and dearer to her mother than ever before.\n\n\"Yes, I've learned to check the hasty words that rise to my lips; and when I feel that they mean to break out against my will, I just go away a minute, and give myself a little shake, for being so weak and wicked,\" answered Mrs. March, with a sigh and a smile, as she smoothed and fastened up Jo's dishevelled hair.\n\n\"How did you learn to keep still? That is what troubles me--for the sharp words fly out before I know what I'm about; and the more I say the worse I get, till it's a pleasure to hurt people's feelings, and say dreadful things. Tell me how you do it, Marmee dear.\"\n\n\"My good mother used to help me--\"\n\n\"As you do us--\" interrupted Jo, with a grateful kiss.\n\n\"But I lost her when I was a little older than you are, and for years had to struggle on alone, for I was too proud to confess my weakness to any one else. I had a hard time, Jo, and shed a good many bitter tears over my failures; for, in spite of my efforts, I never seemed to get on. Then your father came, and I was so happy that I found it easy to be good. But by and by, when I had four little daughters round me, and we were poor, then the old trouble began again; for I am not patient by nature, and it tried me very much to see my children wanting anything.\"\n\n\"Poor mother! what helped you then?\"\n\n\"Your father, Jo. He never loses patience,--never doubts or complains,--but always hopes, and works and waits so cheerfully, that one is ashamed to do otherwise before him. He helped and comforted me, and showed me that I must try to practise all the virtues I would have my little girls possess, for I was their example. It was easier to try for your sakes than for my own; a startled or surprised look from one of you, when I spoke sharply, rebuked me more than any words could have done; and the love, respect, and confidence of my children was the sweetest reward I could receive for my efforts to be the woman I would have them copy.\"\n\n\"O mother, if I'm ever half as good as you, I shall be satisfied,\" cried Jo, much touched.\n\n\"I hope you will be a great deal better, dear; but you must keep watch over your 'bosom enemy,' as father calls it, or it may sadden, if not spoil your life. You have had a warning; remember it, and try with heart and soul to master this quick temper, before it brings you greater sorrow and regret than you have known to-day.\"\n\n\"I will try, mother; I truly will. But you must help me, remind me, and keep me from flying out. I used to see father sometimes put his finger on his lips, and look at you with a very kind, but sober face, and you always folded your lips tight or went away: was he reminding you then?\" asked Jo softly.\n\n\"Yes; I asked him to help me so, and he never forgot it, but saved me from many a sharp word by that little gesture and kind look.\"\n\nJo saw that her mother's eyes filled and her lips trembled, as she spoke; and, fearing that she had said too much, she whispered anxiously, \"Was it wrong to watch you, and to speak of it? I didn't mean to be rude, but it's so comfortable to say all I think to you, and feel so safe and happy here.\"\n\n\"My Jo, you may say anything to your mother, for it is my greatest happiness and pride to feel that my girls confide in me, and know how much I love them.\"\n\n\"I thought I'd grieved you.\"\n\n\"No, dear; but speaking of father reminded me how much I miss him, how much I owe him, and how faithfully I should watch and work to keep his little daughters safe and good for him.\"\n\n\"Yet you told him to go, mother, and didn't cry when he went, and never complain now, or seem as if you needed any help,\" said Jo, wondering.\n\n\"I gave my best to the country I love, and kept my tears till he was gone. Why should I complain, when we both have merely done our duty and will surely be the happier for it in the end? If I don't seem to need help, it is because I have a better friend, even than father, to comfort and sustain me. My child, the troubles and temptations of your life are beginning, and may be many; but you can overcome and outlive them all if you learn to feel the strength and tenderness of your Heavenly Father as you do that of your earthly one. The more you love and trust Him, the nearer you will feel to Him, and the less you will depend on human power and wisdom. His love and care never tire or change, can never be taken from you, but may become the source of life-long peace, happiness, and strength. Believe this heartily, and go to God with all your little cares, and hopes, and sins, and sorrows, as freely and confidingly as you come to your mother.\"\n\nJo's only answer was to hold her mother close, and, in the silence which followed, the sincerest prayer she had ever prayed left her heart without words; for in that sad, yet happy hour, she had learned not only the bitterness of remorse and despair, but the sweetness of self-denial and self-control; and, led by her mother's hand, she had drawn nearer to the Friend who welcomes every child with a love stronger than that of any father, tenderer than that of any mother.\n\nAmy stirred, and sighed in her sleep; and, as if eager to begin at once to mend her fault, Jo looked up with an expression on her face which it had never worn before.\n\n\"I let the sun go down on my anger; I wouldn't forgive her, and to-day, if it hadn't been for Laurie, it might have been too late! How could I be so wicked?\" said Jo, half aloud, as she leaned over her sister, softly stroking the wet hair scattered on the pillow.\n\nAs if she heard, Amy opened her eyes, and held out her arms, with a smile that went straight to Jo's heart. Neither said a word, but they hugged one another close, in spite of the blankets, and everything was forgiven and forgotten in one hearty kiss.\n\n# MEG GOES TO VANITY FAIR.\n\n\"I do think it was the most fortunate thing in the world that those children should have the measles just now,\" said Meg, one April day, as she stood packing the \"go abroady\" trunk in her room, surrounded by her sisters.\n\n\"And so nice of Annie Moffat not to forget her promise. A whole fortnight of fun will be regularly splendid,\" replied Jo, looking like a windmill, as she folded skirts with her long arms.\n\n\"And such lovely weather; I'm so glad of that,\" added Beth, tidily sorting neck and hair ribbons in her best box, lent for the great occasion.\n\n\"I wish I was going to have a fine time, and wear all these nice things,\" said Amy, with her mouth full of pins, as she artistically replenished her sister's cushion.\n\n\"I wish you were all going; but, as you can't, I shall keep my adventures to tell you when I come back. I'm sure it's the least I can do, when you have been so kind, lending me things, and helping me get ready,\" said Meg, glancing round the room at the very simple outfit, which seemed nearly perfect in their eyes.\n\n\"What did mother give you out of the treasure-box?\" asked Amy, who had not been present at the opening of a certain cedar chest, in which Mrs. March kept a few relics of past splendor, as gifts for her girls when the proper time came.\n\n\"A pair of silk stockings, that pretty carved fan, and a lovely blue sash. I wanted the violet silk; but there isn't time to make it over, so I must be contented with my old tarlatan.\"\n\n\"It will look nicely over my new muslin skirt, and the sash will set it off beautifully. I wish I hadn't smashed my coral bracelet, for you might have had it,\" said Jo, who loved to give and lend, but whose possessions were usually too dilapidated to be of much use.\n\n\"There is a lovely old-fashioned pearl set in the treasure-box; but mother said real flowers were the prettiest ornament for a young girl, and Laurie promised to send me all I want,\" replied Meg. \"Now, let me see; there's my new gray walking-suit--just curl up the feather in my hat, Beth,--then my poplin, for Sunday, and the small party,--it looks heavy for spring, doesn't it? The violet silk would be so nice; oh, dear!\"\n\n\"Never mind; you've got the tarlatan for the big party, and you always look like an angel in white,\" said Amy, brooding over the little store of finery in which her soul delighted.\n\n\"It isn't low-necked, and it doesn't sweep enough, but it will have to do. My blue house-dress looks so well, turned and freshly trimmed, that I feel as if I'd got a new one. My silk sacque isn't a bit the fashion, and my bonnet doesn't look like Sallie's; I didn't like to say anything, but I was sadly disappointed in my umbrella. I told mother black, with a white handle, but she forgot, and bought a green one, with a yellowish handle. It's strong and neat, so I ought not to complain, but I know I shall feel ashamed of it beside Annie's silk one with a gold top,\" sighed Meg, surveying the little umbrella with great disfavor.\n\n\"Change it,\" advised Jo.\n\n\"I won't be so silly, or hurt Marmee's feelings, when she took so much pains to get my things. It's a nonsensical notion of mine, and I'm not going to give up to it. My silk stockings and two pairs of new gloves are my comfort. You are a dear, to lend me yours, Jo. I feel so rich, and sort of elegant, with two new pairs, and the old ones cleaned up for common;\" and Meg took a refreshing peep at her glove-box.\n\n\"Annie Moffat has blue and pink bows on her night-caps; would you put some on mine?\" she asked, as Beth brought up a pile of snowy muslins, fresh from Hannah's hands.\n\n\"No, I wouldn't; for the smart caps won't match the plain gowns, without any trimming on them. Poor folks shouldn't rig,\" said Jo decidedly.\n\n\"I wonder if I shall _ever_ be happy enough to have real lace on my clothes, and bows on my caps?\" said Meg impatiently.\n\n\"You said the other day that you'd be perfectly happy if you could only go to Annie Moffat's,\" observed Beth, in her quiet way.\n\n\"So I did! Well, I _am_ happy, and I _won't_ fret; but it does seem as if the more one gets the more one wants, doesn't it? There, now, the trays are ready, and everything in but my ball-dress, which I shall leave for mother to pack,\" said Meg, cheering up, as she glanced from the half-filled trunk to the many-times pressed and mended white tarlatan, which she called her \"ball-dress,\" with an important air.\n\nThe next day was fine, and Meg departed, in style, for a fortnight of novelty and pleasure. Mrs. March had consented to the visit rather reluctantly, fearing that Margaret would come back more discontented than she went. But she had begged so hard, and Sallie had promised to take good care of her, and a little pleasure seemed so delightful after a winter of irksome work, that the mother yielded, and the daughter went to take her first taste of fashionable life.\n\nThe Moffats _were_ very fashionable, and simple Meg was rather daunted, at first, by the splendor of the house and the elegance of its occupants. But they were kindly people, in spite of the frivolous life they led, and soon put their guest at her ease. Perhaps Meg felt, without understanding why, that they were not particularly cultivated or intelligent people, and that all their gilding could not quite conceal the ordinary material of which they were made. It certainly was agreeable to fare sumptuously, drive in a fine carriage, wear her best frock every day, and do nothing but enjoy herself. It suited her exactly; and soon she began to imitate the manners and conversation of those about her; to put on little airs and graces, use French phrases, crimp her hair, take in her dresses, and talk about the fashions as well as she could. The more she saw of Annie Moffat's pretty things, the more she envied her, and sighed to be rich. Home now looked bare and dismal as she thought of it, work grew harder than ever, and she felt that she was a very destitute and much-injured girl, in spite of the new gloves and silk stockings.\n\nShe had not much time for repining, however, for the three young girls were busily employed in \"having a good time.\" They shopped, walked, rode, and called all day; went to theatres and operas, or frolicked at home in the evening; for Annie had many friends, and knew how to entertain them. Her older sisters were very fine young ladies, and one was engaged, which was extremely interesting and romantic, Meg thought. Mr. Moffat was a fat, jolly old gentleman, who knew her father; and Mrs. Moffat, a fat, jolly old lady, who took as great a fancy to Meg as her daughter had done. Every one petted her; and \"Daisy,\" as they called her, was in a fair way to have her head turned.\n\nWhen the evening for the \"small party\" came, she found that the poplin wouldn't do at all, for the other girls were putting on thin dresses, and making themselves very fine indeed; so out came the tarlatan, looking older, limper, and shabbier than ever beside Sallie's crisp new one. Meg saw the girls glance at it and then at one another, and her cheeks began to burn, for, with all her gentleness, she was very proud. No one said a word about it, but Sallie offered to dress her hair, and Annie to tie her sash, and Belle, the engaged sister, praised her white arms; but in their kindness Meg saw only pity for her poverty, and her heart felt very heavy as she stood by herself, while the others laughed, chattered, and flew about like gauzy butterflies. The hard, bitter feeling was getting pretty bad, when the maid brought in a box of flowers. Before she could speak, Annie had the cover off, and all were exclaiming at the lovely roses, heath, and fern within.\n\n\"It's for Belle, of course; George always sends her some, but these are altogether ravishing,\" cried Annie, with a great sniff.\n\n\"They are for Miss March, the man said. And here's a note,\" put in the maid, holding it to Meg.\n\n\"What fun! Who are they from? Didn't know you had a lover,\" cried the girls, fluttering about Meg in a high state of curiosity and surprise.\n\n\"The note is from mother, and the flowers from Laurie,\" said Meg simply, yet much gratified that he had not forgotten her.\n\n\"Oh, indeed!\" said Annie, with a funny look, as Meg slipped the note into her pocket, as a sort of talisman against envy, vanity, and false pride; for the few loving words had done her good, and the flowers cheered her up by their beauty.\n\nFeeling almost happy again, she laid by a few ferns and roses for herself, and quickly made up the rest in dainty bouquets for the breasts, hair, or skirts of her friends, offering them so prettily that Clara, the elder sister, told her she was \"the sweetest little thing she ever saw;\" and they looked quite charmed with her small attention. Somehow the kind act finished her despondency; and when all the rest went to show themselves to Mrs. Moffat, she saw a happy, bright-eyed face in the mirror, as she laid her ferns against her rippling hair, and fastened the roses in the dress that didn't strike her as so _very_ shabby now.\n\nShe enjoyed herself very much that evening, for she danced to her heart's content; every one was very kind, and she had three compliments. Annie made her sing, and some one said she had a remarkably fine voice; Major Lincoln asked who \"the fresh little girl, with the beautiful eyes,\" was; and Mr. Moffat insisted on dancing with her, because she \"didn't dawdle, but had some spring in her,\" as he gracefully expressed it. So, altogether, she had a very nice time, till she overheard a bit of a conversation, which disturbed her extremely. She was sitting just inside the conservatory, waiting for her partner to bring her an ice, when she heard a voice ask, on the other side of the flowery wall,--\n\n\"How old is he?\"\n\n\"Sixteen or seventeen, I should say,\" replied another voice.\n\n\"It would be a grand thing for one of those girls, wouldn't it? Sallie says they are very intimate now, and the old man quite dotes on them.\"\n\n\"Mrs M. has made her plans, I dare say, and will play her cards well, early as it is. The girl evidently doesn't think of it yet,\" said Mrs. Moffat.\n\n\"She told that fib about her mamma, as if she did know, and colored up when the flowers came, quite prettily. Poor thing! she'd be so nice if she was only got up in style. Do you think she'd be offended if we offered to lend her a dress for Thursday?\" asked another voice.\n\n\"She's proud, but I don't believe she'd mind, for that dowdy tarlatan is all she has got. She may tear it to-night, and that will be a good excuse for offering a decent one.\"\n\n\"We'll see. I shall ask young Laurence, as a compliment to her, and we'll have fun about it afterward.\"\n\nHere Meg's partner appeared, to find her looking much flushed and rather agitated. She _was_ proud, and her pride was useful just then, for it helped her hide her mortification, anger, and disgust at what she had just heard; for, innocent and unsuspicious as she was, she could not help understanding the gossip of her friends. She tried to forget it, but could not, and kept repeating to herself, \"Mrs. M. has made her plans,\" \"that fib about her mamma,\" and \"dowdy tarlatan,\" till she was ready to cry, and rush home to tell her troubles and ask for advice. As that was impossible, she did her best to seem gay; and, being rather excited, she succeeded so well that no one dreamed what an effort she was making. She was very glad when it was all over, and she was quiet in her bed, where she could think and wonder and fume till her head ached and her hot cheeks were cooled by a few natural tears. Those foolish, yet well-meant words, had opened a new world to Meg, and much disturbed the peace of the old one, in which, till now, she had lived as happily as a child. Her innocent friendship with Laurie was spoilt by the silly speeches she had overheard; her faith in her mother was a little shaken by the worldly plans attributed to her by Mrs. Moffat, who judged others by herself; and the sensible resolution to be contented with the simple wardrobe which suited a poor man's daughter, was weakened by the unnecessary pity of girls who thought a shabby dress one of the greatest calamities under heaven.\n\nPoor Meg had a restless night, and got up heavy-eyed, unhappy, half resentful toward her friends, and half ashamed of herself for not speaking out frankly, and setting everything right. Everybody dawdled that morning, and it was noon before the girls found energy enough even to take up their worsted work. Something in the manner of her friends struck Meg at once; they treated her with more respect, she thought; took quite a tender interest in what she said, and looked at her with eyes that plainly betrayed curiosity. All this surprised and flattered her, though she did not understand it till Miss Belle looked up from her writing, and said, with a sentimental air,--\n\n\"Daisy, dear, I've sent an invitation to your friend, Mr. Laurence, for Thursday. We should like to know him, and it's only a proper compliment to you.\"\n\nMeg colored, but a mischievous fancy to tease the girls made her reply demurely,--\n\n\"You are very kind, but I'm afraid he won't come.\"\n\"Why not, _chérie_?\" asked Miss Belle.\n\n\"He's too old.\"\n\n\"My child, what do you mean? What is his age, I beg to know!\" cried Miss Clara.\n\n\"Nearly seventy, I believe,\" answered Meg, counting stitches, to hide the merriment in her eyes.\n\n\"You sly creature! Of course we meant the young man,\" exclaimed Miss Belle, laughing.\n\n\"There isn't any; Laurie is only a little boy,\" and Meg laughed also at the queer look which the sisters exchanged as she thus described her supposed lover.\n\n\"About your age,\" Nan said.\n\n\"Nearer my sister Jo's; _I_ am seventeen in August,\" returned Meg, tossing her head.\n\n\"It's very nice of him to send you flowers, isn't it?\" said Annie, looking wise about nothing.\n\n\"Yes, he often does, to all of us; for their house is full, and we are so fond of them. My mother and old Mr. Laurence are friends, you know, so it is quite natural that we children should play together;\" and Meg hoped they would say no more.\n\n\"It's evident Daisy isn't out yet,\" said Miss Clara to Belle, with a nod.\n\n\"Quite a pastoral state of innocence all round,\" returned Miss Belle, with a shrug.\n\n\"I'm going out to get some little matters for my girls; can I do anything for you, young ladies?\" asked Mrs. Moffat, lumbering in, like an elephant, in silk and lace.\n\n\"No, thank you, ma'am,\" replied Sallie. \"I've got my new pink silk for Thursday, and don't want a thing.\"\n\n\"Nor I,--\" began Meg, but stopped, because it occurred to her that she _did_ want several things, and could not have them.\n\n\"What shall you wear?\" asked Sallie.\n\n\"My old white one again, if I can mend it fit to be seen; it got sadly torn last night,\" said Meg, trying to speak quite easily, but feeling very uncomfortable.\n\n\"Why don't you send home for another?\" said Sallie, who was not an observing young lady.\n\n\"I haven't got any other.\" It cost Meg an effort to say that, but Sallie did not see it, and exclaimed, in amiable surprise,--\n\n\"Only that? How funny--\" She did not finish her speech, for Belle shook her head at her, and broke in, saying kindly,--\n\n\"Not at all; where is the use of having a lot of dresses when she isn't out? There's no need of sending home, Daisy, even if you had a dozen, for I've got a sweet blue silk laid away, which I've outgrown, and you shall wear it, to please me, won't you, dear?\"\n\n\"You are very kind, but I don't mind my old dress, if you don't; it does well enough for a little girl like me,\" said Meg.\n\n\"Now do let me please myself by dressing you up in style. I admire to do it, and you'd be a regular little beauty, with a touch here and there. I sha'n't let any one see you till you are done, and then we'll burst upon them like Cinderella and her godmother, going to the ball,\" said Belle, in her persuasive tone.\n\nMeg couldn't refuse the offer so kindly made, for a desire to see if she would be \"a little beauty\" after touching up, caused her to accept, and forget all her former uncomfortable feelings towards the Moffats.\n\nOn the Thursday evening, Belle shut herself up with her maid; and, between them, they turned Meg into a fine lady. They crimped and curled her hair, they polished her neck and arms with some fragrant powder, touched her lips with coralline salve, to make them redder, and Hortense would have added \"a _soupçon_ of rouge,\" if Meg had not rebelled. They laced her into a sky-blue dress, which was so tight she could hardly breathe, and so low in the neck that modest Meg blushed at herself in the mirror. A set of silver filagree was added, bracelets, necklace, brooch, and even ear-rings, for Hortense tied them on, with a bit of pink silk, which did not show. A cluster of tea-rosebuds at the bosom, and a _ruche_, reconciled Meg to the display of her pretty white shoulders, and a pair of high-heeled blue silk boots satisfied the last wish of her heart. A laced handkerchief, a plumy fan, and a bouquet in a silver holder finished her off; and Miss Belle surveyed her with the satisfaction of a little girl with a newly dressed doll.\n\n\"Mademoiselle is charmante, très jolie, is she not?\" cried Hortense, clasping her hands in an affected rapture.\n\n\"Come and show yourself,\" said Miss Belle, leading the way to the room where the others were waiting.\n\nAs Meg went rustling after, with her long skirts trailing, her ear-rings tinkling, her curls waving, and her heart beating, she felt as if her \"fun\" had really begun at last, for the mirror had plainly told her that she _was_ \"a little beauty.\" Her friends repeated the pleasing phrase enthusiastically; and, for several minutes, she stood, like the jackdaw in the fable, enjoying her borrowed plumes, while the rest chattered like a party of magpies.\n\n\"While I dress, do you drill her, Nan, in the management of her skirt, and those French heels, or she will trip herself up. Take your silver butterfly, and catch up that long curl on the left side of her head, Clara, and don't any of you disturb the charming work of my hands,\" said Belle, as she hurried away, looking well pleased with her success.\n\n\"I'm afraid to go down, I feel so queer and stiff and half-dressed,\" said Meg to Sallie, as the bell rang, and Mrs. Moffat sent to ask the young ladies to appear at once.\n\n\"You don't look a bit like yourself, but you are very nice. I'm nowhere beside you, for Belle has heaps of taste, and you're quite French, I assure you. Let your flowers hang; don't be so careful of them, and be sure you don't trip,\" returned Sallie, trying not to care that Meg was prettier than herself.\n\nKeeping that warning carefully in mind, Margaret got safely down stairs, and sailed into the drawing-rooms, where the Moffats and a few early guests were assembled. She very soon discovered that there is a charm about fine clothes which attracts a certain class of people, and secures their respect. Several young ladies, who had taken no notice of her before, were very affectionate all of a sudden; several young gentlemen, who had only stared at her at the other party, now not only stared, but asked to be introduced, and said all manner of foolish but agreeable things to her; and several old ladies, who sat on sofas, and criticised the rest of the party, inquired who she was, with an air of interest. She heard Mrs. Moffat reply to one of them,--\n\n\"Daisy March--father a colonel in the army--one of our first families, but reverses of fortune, you know; intimate friends of the Laurences; sweet creature, I assure you; my Ned is quite wild about her.\"\n\n\"Dear me!\" said the old lady, putting up her glass for another observation of Meg, who tried to look as if she had not heard, and been rather shocked at Mrs. Moffat's fibs.\n\nThe \"queer feeling\" did not pass away, but she imagined herself acting the new part of fine lady, and so got on pretty well, though the tight dress gave her a side-ache, the train kept getting under her feet, and she was in constant fear lest her ear-rings should fly off, and get lost or broken. She was flirting her fan and laughing at the feeble jokes of a young gentleman who tried to be witty, when she suddenly stopped laughing and looked confused; for, just opposite, she saw Laurie. He was staring at her with undisguised surprise, and disapproval also, she thought; for, though he bowed and smiled, yet something in his honest eyes made her blush, and wish she had her old dress on. To complete her confusion, she saw Belle nudge Annie, and both glance from her to Laurie, who, she was happy to see, looked unusually boyish and shy.\n\n\"Silly creatures, to put such thoughts into my head! I won't care for it, or let it change me a bit,\" thought Meg, and rustled across the room to shake hands with her friend.\n\n\"I'm glad you came, I was afraid you wouldn't,\" she said, with her most grown-up air.\n\n\"Jo wanted me to come, and tell her how you looked, so I did;\" answered Laurie, without turning his eyes upon her, though he half smiled at her maternal tone.\n\n\"What shall you tell her?\" asked Meg, full of curiosity to know his opinion of her, yet feeling ill at ease with him, for the first time.\n\n\"I shall say I didn't know you; for you look so grown-up, and unlike yourself, I'm quite afraid of you,\" he said, fumbling at his glove-button.\n\n\"How absurd of you! The girls dressed me up for fun, and I rather like it. Wouldn't Jo stare if she saw me?\" said Meg, bent on making him say whether he thought her improved or not.\n\n\"Yes, I think she would,\" returned Laurie gravely.\n\n\"Don't you like me so?\" asked Meg.\n\n\"No, I don't,\" was the blunt reply.\n\n\"Why not?\" in an anxious tone.\n\nHe glanced at her frizzled head, bare shoulders, and fantastically trimmed dress, with an expression that abashed her more than his answer, which had not a particle of his usual politeness about it.\n\n\"I don't like fuss and feathers.\"\n\nThat was altogether too much from a lad younger than herself; and Meg walked away, saying petulantly,--\n\n\"You are the rudest boy I ever saw.\"\n\nFeeling very much ruffled, she went and stood at a quiet window, to cool her cheeks, for the tight dress gave her an uncomfortably brilliant color. As she stood there, Major Lincoln passed by; and, a minute after, she heard him saying to his mother,--\n\n\"They are making a fool of that little girl; I wanted you to see her, but they have spoilt her entirely; she's nothing but a doll, to-night.\"\n\n\"Oh, dear!\" sighed Meg; \"I wish I'd been sensible, and worn my own things; then I should not have disgusted other people, or felt so uncomfortable and ashamed myself.\"\n\nShe leaned her forehead on the cool pane, and stood half hidden by the curtains, never minding that her favorite waltz had begun, till some one touched her; and, turning, she saw Laurie, looking penitent, as he said, with his very best bow, and his hand out,--\n\n\"Please forgive my rudeness, and come and dance with me.\"\n\n\"I'm afraid it will be too disagreeable to you,\" said Meg, trying to look offended, and failing entirely.\n\n\"Not a bit of it; I'm dying to do it. Come, I'll be good; I don't like your gown, but I do think you are--just splendid;\" and he waved his hands, as if words failed to express his admiration.\n\nMeg smiled and relented, and whispered, as they stood waiting to catch the time,--\n\n\"Take care my skirt don't trip you up; it's the plague of my life, and I was a goose to wear it.\"\n\n\"Pin it round your neck, and then it will be useful,\" said Laurie, looking down at the little blue boots, which he evidently approved of.\n\nAway they went, fleetly and gracefully; for, having practised at home, they were well matched, and the blithe young couple were a pleasant sight to see, as they twirled merrily round and round, feeling more friendly than ever after their small tiff.\n\n\"Laurie, I want you to do me a favor; will you?\" said Meg, as he stood fanning her, when her breath gave out, which it did very soon, though she would not own why.\n\n\"Won't I!\" said Laurie, with alacrity.\n\n\"Please don't tell them at home about my dress to-night. They won't understand the joke, and it will worry mother.\"\n\n\"Then why did you do it?\" said Laurie's eyes, so plainly that Meg hastily added,--\n\n\"I shall tell them, myself, all about it, and ''fess' to mother how silly I've been. But I'd rather do it myself; so you'll not tell, will you?\"\n\n\"I give you my word I won't; only what shall I say when they ask me?\"\n\n\"Just say I looked pretty well, and was having a good time.\"\n\n\"I'll say the first, with all my heart; but how about the other? You don't look as if you were having a good time; are you?\" and Laurie looked at her with an expression which made her answer, in a whisper,--\n\n\"No; not just now. Don't think I'm horrid; I only wanted a little fun, but this sort doesn't pay, I find, and I'm getting tired of it.\"\n\n\"Here comes Ned Moffat; what does he want?\" said Laurie, knitting his black brows, as if he did not regard his young host in the light of a pleasant addition to the party.\n\n\"He put his name down for three dances, and I suppose he's coming for them. What a bore!\" said Meg, assuming a languid air, which amused Laurie immensely.\n\nHe did not speak to her again till supper-time, when he saw her drinking champagne with Ned and his friend Fisher, who were behaving \"like a pair of fools,\" as Laurie said to himself, for he felt a brotherly sort of right to watch over the Marches, and fight their battles whenever a defender was needed.\n\n\"You'll have a splitting headache to-morrow, if you drink much of that. I wouldn't Meg; your mother doesn't like it, you know,\" he whispered, leaning over her chair, as Ned turned to refill her glass, and Fisher stooped to pick up her fan.\n\n\"I'm not Meg, to-night; I'm 'a doll,' who does all sorts of crazy things. To-morrow I shall put away my 'fuss and feathers,' and be desperately good again,\" she answered, with an affected little laugh.\n\n\"Wish to-morrow was here, then,\" muttered Laurie, walking off, ill-pleased at the change he saw in her.\n\nMeg danced and flirted, chattered and giggled, as the other girls did; after supper she undertook the German, and blundered through it, nearly upsetting her partner with her long skirt, and romping in a way that scandalized Laurie, who looked on and meditated a lecture. But he got no chance to deliver it, for Meg kept away from him till he came to say good-night.\n\n\"Remember!\" she said, trying to smile, for the splitting headache had already begun.\n\n\"Silence à la mort,\" replied Laurie, with a melodramatic flourish, as he went away.\n\nThis little bit of by-play excited Annie's curiosity; but Meg was too tired for gossip, and went to bed, feeling as if she had been to a masquerade, and hadn't enjoyed herself as much as she expected. She was sick all the next day, and on Saturday went home, quite used up with her fortnight's fun, and feeling that she had \"sat in the lap of luxury\" long enough.\n\n\"It does seem pleasant to be quiet, and not have company manners on all the time. Home _is_ a nice place, though it isn't splendid,\" said Meg, looking about her with a restful expression, as she sat with her mother and Jo on the Sunday evening.\n\n\"I'm glad to hear you say so, dear, for I was afraid home would seem dull and poor to you, after your fine quarters,\" replied her mother, who had given her many anxious looks that day; for motherly eyes are quick to see any change in children's faces.\n\nMeg had told her adventures gayly, and said over and over what a charming time she had had; but something still seemed to weigh upon her spirits, and, when the younger girls were gone to bed, she sat thoughtfully staring at the fire, saying little, and looking worried. As the clock struck nine, and Jo proposed bed, Meg suddenly left her chair, and, taking Beth's stool, leaned her elbows on her mother's knee, saying bravely,--\n\n\"Marmee, I want to ''fess.'\"\n\n\"I thought so; what is it, dear?\"\n\n\"Shall I go away?\" asked Jo discreetly.\n\n\"Of course not; don't I always tell you everything? I was ashamed to speak of it before the children, but I want you to know all the dreadful things I did at the Moffat's.\"\n\n\"We are prepared,\" said Mrs. March, smiling, but looking a little anxious.\n\n\"I told you they dressed me up, but I didn't tell you that they powdered and squeezed and frizzled, and made me look like a fashion-plate. Laurie thought I wasn't proper; I know he did, though he didn't say so, and one man called me 'a doll.' I knew it was silly, but they flattered me, and said I was a beauty, and quantities of nonsense, so I let them make a fool of me.\"\n\n\"Is that all?\" asked Jo, as Mrs. March looked silently at the downcast face of her pretty daughter, and could not find it in her heart to blame her little follies.\n\n\"No; I drank champagne and romped and tried to flirt, and was altogether abominable,\" said Meg self-reproachfully.\n\n\"There is something more, I think;\" and Mrs. March smoothed the soft cheek, which suddenly grew rosy, as Meg answered slowly,--\n\n\"Yes; it's very silly, but I want to tell it, because I hate to have people say and think such things about us and Laurie.\"\n\nThen she told the various bits of gossip she had heard at the Moffats; and, as she spoke, Jo saw her mother fold her lips tightly, as if ill pleased that such ideas should be put into Meg's innocent mind.\n\n\"Well, if that isn't the greatest rubbish I ever heard,\" cried Jo indignantly. \"Why didn't you pop out and tell them so, on the spot?\"\n\n\"I couldn't, it was so embarrassing for me. I couldn't help hearing, at first, and then I was so angry and ashamed, I didn't remember that I ought to go away.\"\n\n\"Just wait till _I_ see Annie Moffat, and I'll show you how to settle such ridiculous stuff. The idea of having 'plans,' and being kind to Laurie, because he's rich, and may marry us by and by! Won't he shout, when I tell him what those silly things say about us poor children?\" and Jo laughed, as if, on second thoughts, the thing struck her as a good joke.\n\n\"If you tell Laurie, I'll never forgive you! She mustn't, must she, mother?\" said Meg, looking distressed.\n\n\"No; never repeat that foolish gossip, and forget it as soon as you can,\" said Mrs. March gravely. \"I was very unwise to let you go among people of whom I know so little,--kind, I dare say, but worldly, ill-bred, and full of these vulgar ideas about young people. I am more sorry than I can express for the mischief this visit may have done you, Meg.\"\n\n\"Don't be sorry, I won't let it hurt me; I'll forget all the bad, and remember only the good; for I did enjoy a great deal, and thank you very much for letting me go. I'll not be sentimental or dissatisfied, mother; I know I'm a silly little girl, and I'll stay with you till I'm fit to take care of myself. But it _is_ nice to be praised and admired, and I can't help saying I like it,\" said Meg, looking half ashamed of the confession.\n\n\"That is perfectly natural, and quite harmless, if the liking does not become a passion, and lead one to do foolish or unmaidenly things. Learn to know and value the praise which is worth having, and to excite the admiration of excellent people by being modest as well as pretty, Meg.\"\n\nMargaret sat thinking a moment, while Jo stood with her hands behind her, looking both interested and a little perplexed; for it was a new thing to see Meg blushing and talking about admiration, lovers, and things of that sort; and Jo felt as if, during that fortnight, her sister had grown up amazingly, and was drifting away from her into a world where she could not follow.\n\n\"Mother, do you have 'plans,' as Mrs. Moffat said?\" asked Meg bashfully.\n\n\"Yes, my dear, I have a great many; all mothers do, but mine differ somewhat from Mrs. Moffat's, I suspect. I will tell you some of them, for the time has come when a word may set this romantic little head and heart of yours right, on a very serious subject. You are young, Meg, but not too young to understand me; and mothers' lips are the fittest to speak of such things to girls like you. Jo, your turn will come in time, perhaps, so listen to my 'plans,' and help me carry them out, if they are good.\"\n\nJo went and sat on one arm of the chair, looking as if she thought they were about to join in some very solemn affair. Holding a hand of each, and watching the two young faces wistfully, Mrs. March said, in her serious yet cheery way,--\n\n\"I want my daughters to be beautiful, accomplished, and good; to be admired, loved, and respected; to have a happy youth, to be well and wisely married, and to lead useful, pleasant lives, with as little care and sorrow to try them as God sees fit to send. To be loved and chosen by a good man is the best and sweetest thing which can happen to a woman; and I sincerely hope my girls may know this beautiful experience. It is natural to think of it, Meg; right to hope and wait for it, and wise to prepare for it; so that, when the happy time comes, you may feel ready for the duties and worthy of the joy. My dear girls, I _am_ ambitious for you, but not to have you make a dash in the world,--marry rich men merely because they are rich, or have splendid houses, which are not homes because love is wanting. Money is a needful and precious thing,--and, when well used, a noble thing,--but I never want you to think it is the first or only prize to strive for. I'd rather see you poor men's wives, if you were happy, beloved, contented, than queens on thrones, without self-respect and peace.\"\n\n\"Poor girls don't stand any chance, Belle says, unless they put themselves forward,\" sighed Meg.\n\n\"Then we'll be old maids,\" said Jo stoutly.\n\n\"Right, Jo; better be happy old maids than unhappy wives, or unmaidenly girls, running about to find husbands,\" said Mrs. March decidedly. \"Don't be troubled, Meg; poverty seldom daunts a sincere lover. Some of the best and most honored women I know were poor girls, but so love-worthy that they were not allowed to be old maids. Leave these things to time; make this home happy, so that you may be fit for homes of your own, if they are offered you, and contented here if they are not. One thing remember, my girls: mother is always ready to be your confidant, father to be your friend; and both of us trust and hope that our daughters, whether married or single, will be the pride and comfort of our lives.\"\n\n\"We will, Marmee, we will!\" cried both, with all their hearts, as she bade them good-night.\n\n# THE P. C. AND P. O.\n\nAs spring came on, a new set of amusements became the fashion, and the lengthening days gave long afternoons for work and play of all sorts. The garden had to be put in order, and each sister had a quarter of the little plot to do what she liked with. Hannah used to say, \"I'd know which each of them gardings belonged to, ef I see 'em in Chiny;\" and so she might, for the girls' tastes differed as much as their characters. Meg's had roses and heliotrope, myrtle, and a little orange-tree in it. Jo's bed was never alike two seasons, for she was always trying experiments; this year it was to be a plantation of sun-flowers, the seeds of which cheerful and aspiring plant were to feed \"Aunt Cockle-top\" and her family of chicks. Beth had old-fashioned, fragrant flowers in her garden,--sweet peas and mignonette, larkspur, pinks, pansies, and southernwood, with chickweed for the bird, and catnip for the pussies. Amy had a bower in hers,--rather small and earwiggy, but very pretty to look at,--with honeysuckles and morning-glories hanging their colored horns and bells in graceful wreaths all over it; tall, white lilies, delicate ferns, and as many brilliant, picturesque plants as would consent to blossom there.\n\nGardening, walks, rows on the river, and flower-hunts employed the fine days; and for rainy ones, they had house diversions,--some old, some new,--all more or less original. One of these was the \"P. C.\"; for, as secret societies were the fashion, it was thought proper to have one; and, as all of the girls admired Dickens, they called themselves the Pickwick Club. With a few interruptions, they had kept this up for a year, and met every Saturday evening in the big garret, on which occasions the ceremonies were as follows: Three chairs were arranged in a row before a table, on which was a lamp, also four white badges, with a big \"P. C.\" in different colors on each, and the weekly newspaper, called \"The Pickwick Portfolio,\" to which all contributed something; while Jo, who revelled in pens and ink, was the editor. At seven o'clock, the four members ascended to the club-room, tied their badges round their heads, and took their seats with great solemnity. Meg, as the eldest, was Samuel Pickwick; Jo, being of a literary turn, Augustus Snodgrass; Beth, because she was round and rosy, Tracy Tupman, and Amy, who was always trying to do what she couldn't, was Nathaniel Winkle. Pickwick, the president, read the paper, which was filled with original tales, poetry, local news, funny advertisements, and hints, in which they good-naturedly reminded each other of their faults and short-comings.\n\nOn one occasion, Mr. Pickwick put on a pair of spectacles without any glasses, rapped upon the table, hemmed, and, having stared hard at Mr. Snodgrass, who was tilting back in his chair, till he arranged himself properly, began to read:--\n\nThe Pickwick Portfolio.\n\nPoet's Corner.\n\n# ANNIVERSARY ODE.\n\nAgain we meet to celebrate With badge and solemn rite, Our fifty-second anniversary, In Pickwick Hall, to-night.\n\nWe all are here in perfect health, None gone from our small band; Again we see each well-known face, And press each friendly hand.\n\nOur Pickwick, always at his post, With reverence we greet, As, spectacles on nose, he reads Our well-filled weekly sheet.\n\nAlthough he suffers from a cold, We joy to hear him speak, For words of wisdom from him fall, In spite of croak or squeak.\n\nOld six-foot Snodgrass looms on high, With elephantine grace, And beams upon the company, With brown and jovial face.\n\nPoetic fire lights up his eye, He struggles 'gainst his lot. Behold ambition on his brow, And on his nose a blot!\n\nNext our peaceful Tupman comes, So rosy, plump, and sweet. Who chokes with laughter at the puns, And tumbles off his seat.\n\nPrim little Winkle too is here, With every hair in place, A model of propriety, Though he hates to wash his face.\n\nThe year is gone, we still unite To joke and laugh and read, And tread the path of literature That doth to glory lead.\n\nLong may our paper prosper well, Our club unbroken be, And coming years their blessings pour On the useful, gay \"P. C.\"\n\n# A TALE OF VENICE.\n\nGondola after gondola swept up to the marble steps, and left its lovely load to swell the brilliant throng that filled the stately halls of Count de Adelon. Knights and ladies, elves and pages, monks and flower-girls, all mingled gayly in the dance. Sweet voices and rich melody filled the air; and so with mirth and music the masquerade went on.\n\n\"Has your Highness seen the Lady Viola to-night?\" asked a gallant troubadour of the fairy queen who floated down the hall upon his arm.\n\n\"Yes; is she not lovely, though so sad! Her dress is well chosen, too, for in a week she weds Count Antonio, whom she passionately hates.\"\n\n\"By my faith, I envy him. Yonder he comes, arrayed like a bridegroom, except the black mask. When that is off we shall see how he regards the fair maid whose heart he cannot win, though her stern father bestows her hand,\" returned the troubadour.\n\n\"'Tis whispered that she loves the young English artist who haunts her steps, and is spurned by the old count,\" said the lady, as they joined the dance.\n\nThe revel was at its height when a priest appeared, and, withdrawing the young pair to an alcove hung with purple velvet, he motioned them to kneel. Instant silence fell upon the gay throng; and not a sound, but the dash of fountains or the rustle of orange-groves sleeping in the moonlight, broke the hush, as Count de Adelon spoke thus:--\n\n\"My lords and ladies, pardon the ruse by which I have gathered you here to witness the marriage of my daughter. Father, we wait your services.\"\n\nAll eyes turned toward the bridal party, and a low murmur of amazement went through the throng, for neither bride nor groom removed their masks. Curiosity and wonder possessed all hearts, but respect restrained all tongues till the holy rite was over. Then the eager spectators gathered round the count, demanding an explanation.\n\n\"Gladly would I give it if I could; but I only know that it was the whim of my timid Viola, and I yielded to it. Now, my children, let the play end. Unmask, and receive my blessing.\"\n\nBut neither bent the knee; for the young bridegroom replied, in a tone that startled all listeners, as the mask fell, disclosing the noble face of Ferdinand Devereux, the artist lover; and, leaning on the breast where now flashed the star of an English earl, was the lovely Viola, radiant with joy and beauty.\n\n\"My lord, you scornfully bade me claim your daughter when I could boast as high a name and vast a fortune as the Count Antonio. I can do more; for even your ambitious soul cannot refuse the Earl of Devereux and De Vere, when he gives his ancient name and boundless wealth in return for the beloved hand of this fair lady, now my wife.\"\n\nThe count stood like one changed to stone; and, turning to the bewildered crowd, Ferdinand added, with a gay smile of triumph, \"To you, my gallant friends, I can only wish that your wooing may prosper as mine has done; and that you may all win as fair a bride as I have, by this masked marriage.\"\n\n* * * * *\n\nWhy is the P. C. like the Tower of Babel? It is full of unruly members.\n\n* * * * *\n\n# THE HISTORY OF A SQUASH.\n\nOnce upon a time a farmer planted a little seed in his garden, and after a while it sprouted and became a vine, and bore many squashes. One day in October, when they were ripe, he picked one and took it to market. A grocer-man bought and put it in his shop. That same morning, a little girl, in a brown hat and blue dress, with a round face and snub nose, went and bought it for her mother. She lugged it home, cut it up, and boiled it in the big pot; mashed some of it, with salt and butter, for dinner; and to the rest she added a pint of milk, two eggs, four spoons of sugar, nutmeg, and some crackers; put it in a deep dish, and baked it till it was brown and nice; and next day it was eaten by a family named March. T. TUPMAN.\n\n* * * * *\n\nI address you upon the subject of sin the sinner I mean is a man named Winkle who makes trouble in his club by laughing and sometimes won't write his piece in this fine paper I hope you will pardon his badness and let him send a French fable because he can't write out of his head as he has so many lessons to do and no brains in future I will try to take time by the fetlock and prepare some work which will be all _commy la fo_ that means all right I am in haste as it is nearly school time\n\nYours respectably, N. WINKLE.\n\n[The above is a manly and handsome acknowledgment of past misdemeanors. If our young friend studied punctuation, it would be well.]\n\n# A SAD ACCIDENT.\n\nOn Friday last, we were startled by a violent shock in our basement, followed by cries of distress. On rushing, in a body, to the cellar, we discovered our beloved President prostrate upon the floor, having tripped and fallen while getting wood for domestic purposes. A perfect scene of ruin met our eyes; for in his fall Mr. Pickwick had plunged his head and shoulders into a tub of water, upset a keg of soft soap upon his manly form, and torn his garments badly. On being removed from this perilous situation, it was discovered that he had suffered no injury but several bruises; and, we are happy to add, is now doing well. ED.\n\n*************************************************************** * * * THE PUBLIC BEREAVEMENT. * * * * It is our painful duty to record the sudden and mysterious * * disappearance of our cherished friend, Mrs. Snowball Pat * * Paw. This lovely and beloved cat was the pet of a large * * circle of warm and admiring friends; for her beauty * * attracted all eyes, her graces and virtues endeared her to * * all hearts, and her loss is deeply felt by the whole * * community. * * * * When last seen, she was sitting at the gate, watching the * * butcher's cart; and it is feared that some villain, tempted * * by her charms, basely stole her. Weeks have passed, but no * * trace of her has been discovered; and we relinquish all * * hope, tie a black ribbon to her basket, set aside her dish, * * and weep for her as one lost to us forever. * * * ***************************************************************\n\nA sympathizing friend sends the following gem:--\n\n# FOR S. B. PAT PAW.\n\nWe mourn the loss of our little pet, And sigh o'er her hapless fate, For never more by the fire she'll sit, Nor play by the old green gate.\n\nThe little grave where her infant sleeps, Is 'neath the chestnut tree; But o'er _her_ grave we may not weep, We know not where it may be.\n\nHer empty bed, her idle ball, Will never see her more; No gentle tap, no loving purr Is heard at the parlor-door.\n\nAnother cat comes after her mice, A cat with a dirty face; But she does not hunt as our darling did, Nor play with her airy grace.\n\nHer stealthy paws tread the very hall Where Snowball used to play, But she only spits at the dogs our pet So gallantly drove away.\n\nShe is useful and mild, and does her best, But she is not fair to see; And we cannot give her your place, dear, Nor worship her as we worship thee. A. S.\n\nMISS ORANTHY BLUGGAGE, the accomplished Strong-Minded Lecturer, will deliver her famous Lecture on \"WOMAN AND HER POSITION,\" at Pickwick Hall, next Saturday Evening, after the usual performances.\n\nA WEEKLY MEETING will be held at Kitchen Place, to teach young ladies how to cook. Hannah Brown will preside; and all are invited to attend.\n\nTHE DUSTPAN SOCIETY will meet on Wednesday next, and parade in the upper story of the Club House. All members to appear in uniform and shoulder their brooms at nine precisely.\n\nMRS. BETH BOUNCER will open her new assortment of Doll's Millinery next week. The latest Paris Fashions have arrived, and orders are respectfully solicited.\n\nA NEW PLAY will appear at the Barnville Theatre, in the course of a few weeks, which will surpass anything ever seen on the American stage. \"THE GREEK SLAVE, or Constantine the Avenger,\" is the name of this thrilling drama!!!\n\nIf S. P. didn't use so much soap on his hands, he wouldn't always be late at breakfast. A. S. is requested not to whistle in the street. T. T. please don't forget Amy's napkin. N. W. must not fret because his dress has not nine tucks.\n\nMeg--Good. Jo--Bad. Beth--Very good. Amy--Middling.\n\nAs the President finished reading the paper (which I beg leave to assure my readers is a _bona fide_ copy of one written by _bona fide_ girls once upon a time), a round of applause followed, and then Mr. Snodgrass rose to make a proposition.\n\n\"Mr. President and gentlemen,\" he began, assuming a parliamentary attitude and tone, \"I wish to propose the admission of a new member,--one who highly deserves the honor, would be deeply grateful for it, and would add immensely to the spirit of the club, the literary value of the paper, and be no end jolly and nice. I propose Mr. Theodore Laurence as an honorary member of the P. C. Come now, do have him.\"\n\nJo's sudden change of tone made the girls laugh; but all looked rather anxious, and no one said a word, as Snodgrass took his seat.\n\n\"We'll put it to vote,\" said the President. \"All in favor of this motion please to manifest it by saying 'Ay.'\"\nA loud response from Snodgrass, followed, to everybody's surprise, by a timid one from Beth.\n\n\"Contrary minded say 'No.'\"\n\nMeg and Amy were contrary minded; and Mr. Winkle rose to say, with great elegance, \"We don't wish any boys; they only joke and bounce about. This is a ladies' club, and we wish to be private and proper.\"\n\n\"I'm afraid he'll laugh at our paper, and make fun of us afterward,\" observed Pickwick, pulling the little curl on her forehead, as she always did when doubtful.\n\nUp rose Snodgrass, very much in earnest. \"Sir, I give you my word as a gentleman, Laurie won't do anything of the sort. He likes to write, and he'll give a tone to our contributions, and keep us from being sentimental, don't you see? We can do so little for him, and he does so much for us, I think the least we can do is to offer him a place here, and make him welcome if he comes.\"\n\nThis artful allusion to benefits conferred brought Tupman to his feet, looking as if he had quite made up his mind.\n\n\"Yes, we ought to do it, even if we _are_ afraid. I say he _may_ come, and his grandpa, too, if he likes.\"\n\nThis spirited burst from Beth electrified the club, and Jo left her seat to shake hands approvingly. \"Now then, vote again. Everybody remember it's our Laurie, and say 'Ay!'\" cried Snodgrass excitedly.\n\"Ay! ay! ay!\" replied three voices at once.\n\n\"Good! Bless you! Now, as there's nothing like 'taking time by the _fetlock_,' as Winkle characteristically observes, allow me to present the new member;\" and, to the dismay of the rest of the club, Jo threw open the door of the closet, and displayed Laurie sitting on a rag-bag, flushed and twinkling with suppressed laughter.\n\n\"You rogue! you traitor! Jo, how could you?\" cried the three girls, as Snodgrass led her friend triumphantly forth; and, producing both a chair and a badge, installed him in a jiffy.\n\n\"The coolness of you two rascals is amazing,\" began Mr. Pickwick, trying to get up an awful frown, and only succeeding in producing an amiable smile. But the new member was equal to the occasion; and, rising, with a grateful salutation to the Chair, said, in the most engaging manner, \"Mr. President and ladies,--I beg pardon, gentlemen,--allow me to introduce myself as Sam Weller, the very humble servant of the club.\"\n\n\"Good! good!\" cried Jo, pounding with the handle of the old warming-pan on which she leaned.\n\n\"My faithful friend and noble patron,\" continued Laurie, with a wave of the hand, \"who has so flatteringly presented me, is not to be blamed for the base stratagem of to-night. I planned it, and she only gave in after lots of teasing.\"\n\n\"Come now, don't lay it all on yourself; you know I proposed the cupboard,\" broke in Snodgrass, who was enjoying the joke amazingly.\n\n\"Never you mind what she says. I'm the wretch that did it, sir,\" said the new member, with a Welleresque nod to Mr. Pickwick. \"But on my honor, I never will do so again, and henceforth _dewote_ myself to the interest of this immortal club.\"\n\"Hear! hear!\" cried Jo, clashing the lid of the warming-pan like a cymbal.\n\n\"Go on, go on!\" added Winkle and Tupman, while the President bowed benignly.\n\n\"I merely wish to say, that as a slight token of my gratitude for the honor done me, and as a means of promoting friendly relations between adjoining nations, I have set up a post-office in the hedge in the lower corner of the garden; a fine, spacious building, with padlocks on the doors, and every convenience for the mails,--also the females, if I may be allowed the expression. It's the old martin-house; but I've stopped up the door, and made the roof open, so it will hold all sorts of things, and save our valuable time. Letters, manuscripts, books, and bundles can be passed in there; and, as each nation has a key, it will be uncommonly nice, I fancy. Allow me to present the club key; and, with many thanks for your favor, take my seat.\"\n\nGreat applause as Mr. Weller deposited a little key on the table, and subsided; the warming-pan clashed and waved wildly, and it was some time before order could be restored. A long discussion followed, and every one came out surprising, for every one did her best; so it was an unusually lively meeting, and did not adjourn till a late hour, when it broke up with three shrill cheers for the new member.\n\nNo one ever regretted the admittance of Sam Weller, for a more devoted, well-behaved, and jovial member no club could have. He certainly did add \"spirit\" to the meetings, and \"a tone\" to the paper; for his orations convulsed his hearers, and his contributions were excellent, being patriotic, classical, comical, or dramatic, but never sentimental. Jo regarded them as worthy of Bacon, Milton, or Shakespeare; and remodelled her own works with good effect, she thought.\n\nThe P. O. was a capital little institution, and flourished wonderfully, for nearly as many queer things passed through it as through the real office. Tragedies and cravats, poetry and pickles, garden-seeds and long letters, music and gingerbread, rubbers, invitations, scoldings and puppies. The old gentleman liked the fun, and amused himself by sending odd bundles, mysterious messages, and funny telegrams; and his gardener, who was smitten with Hannah's charms, actually sent a love-letter to Jo's care. How they laughed when the secret came out, never dreaming how many love-letters that little post-office would hold in the years to come!\n\n\"The first of June! The Kings are off to the seashore to-morrow, and I'm free. Three months' vacation,--how I shall enjoy it!\" exclaimed Meg, coming home one warm day to find Jo laid upon the sofa in an unusual state of exhaustion, while Beth took off her dusty boots, and Amy made lemonade for the refreshment of the whole party.\n\n\"Aunt March went to-day, for which, oh, be joyful!\" said Jo. \"I was mortally afraid she'd ask me to go with her; if she had, I should have felt as if I ought to do it; but Plumfield is about as gay as a churchyard, you know, and I'd rather be excused. We had a flurry getting the old lady off, and I had a fright every time she spoke to me, for I was in such a hurry to be through that I was uncommonly helpful and sweet, and feared she'd find it impossible to part from me. I quaked till she was fairly in the carriage, and had a final fright, for, as it drove off, she popped out her head, saying, 'Josy-phine, won't you--?' I didn't hear any more, for I basely turned and fled; I did actually run, and whisked round the corner, where I felt safe.\"\n\n\"Poor old Jo! she came in looking as if bears were after her,\" said Beth, as she cuddled her sister's feet with a motherly air.\n\n\"Aunt March is a regular samphire, is she not?\" observed Amy, tasting her mixture critically.\n\n\"She means _vampire_, not sea-weed; but it doesn't matter; it's too warm to be particular about one's parts of speech,\" murmured Jo.\n\n\"What shall you do all your vacation?\" asked Amy, changing the subject, with tact.\n\n\"I shall lie abed late, and do nothing,\" replied Meg, from the depths of the rocking-chair. \"I've been routed up early all winter, and had to spend my days working for other people; so now I'm going to rest and revel to my heart's content.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Jo; \"that dozy way wouldn't suit me. I've laid in a heap of books, and I'm going to improve my shining hours reading on my perch in the old apple-tree, when I'm not having l------\"\n\n\"Don't say 'larks!'\" implored Amy, as a return snub for the \"samphire\" correction.\n\n\"I'll say 'nightingales,' then, with Laurie; that's proper and appropriate, since he's a warbler.\"\n\n\"Don't let us do any lessons, Beth, for a while, but play all the time, and rest, as the girls mean to,\" proposed Amy.\n\n\"Well, I will, if mother doesn't mind. I want to learn some new songs, and my children need fitting up for the summer; they are dreadfully out of order, and really suffering for clothes.\"\n\n\"May we, mother?\" asked Meg, turning to Mrs. March, who sat sewing, in what they called \"Marmee's corner.\"\n\n\"You may try your experiment for a week, and see how you like it. I think by Saturday night you will find that all play and no work is as bad as all work and no play.\"\n\n\"Oh, dear, no! it will be delicious, I'm sure,\" said Meg complacently.\n\n\"I now propose a toast, as my 'friend and pardner, Sairy Gamp,' says. Fun forever, and no grubbing!\" cried Jo, rising, glass in hand, as the lemonade went round.\n\nThey all drank it merrily, and began the experiment by lounging for the rest of the day. Next morning, Meg did not appear till ten o'clock; her solitary breakfast did not taste nice, and the room seemed lonely and untidy; for Jo had not filled the vases, Beth had not dusted, and Amy's books lay scattered about. Nothing was neat and pleasant but \"Marmee's corner,\" which looked as usual; and there Meg sat, to \"rest and read,\" which meant yawn, and imagine what pretty summer dresses she would get with her salary. Jo spent the morning on the river, with Laurie, and the afternoon reading and crying over \"The Wide, Wide World,\" up in the apple-tree. Beth began by rummaging everything out of the big closet, where her family resided; but, getting tired before half done, she left her establishment topsy-turvy, and went to her music, rejoicing that she had no dishes to wash. Amy arranged her bower, put on her best white frock, smoothed her curls, and sat down to draw, under the honeysuckles, hoping some one would see and inquire who the young artist was. As no one appeared but an inquisitive daddy-long-legs, who examined her work with interest, she went to walk, got caught in a shower, and came home dripping.\n\nAt tea-time they compared notes, and all agreed that it had been a delightful, though unusually long day. Meg, who went shopping in the afternoon, and got a \"sweet blue muslin,\" had discovered, after she had cut the breadths off, that it wouldn't wash, which mishap made her slightly cross. Jo had burnt the skin off her nose boating, and got a raging headache by reading too long. Beth was worried by the confusion of her closet, and the difficulty of learning three or four songs at once; and Amy deeply regretted the damage done her frock, for Katy Brown's party was to be the next day; and now, like Flora McFlimsey, she had \"nothing to wear.\" But these were mere trifles; and they assured their mother that the experiment was working finely. She smiled, said nothing, and, with Hannah's help, did their neglected work, keeping home pleasant, and the domestic machinery running smoothly. It was astonishing what a peculiar and uncomfortable state of things was produced by the \"resting and revelling\" process. The days kept getting longer and longer; the weather was unusually variable, and so were tempers; an unsettled feeling possessed every one, and Satan found plenty of mischief for the idle hands to do. As the height of luxury, Meg put out some of her sewing, and then found time hang so heavily that she fell to snipping and spoiling her clothes, in her attempts to furbish them up à la Moffat. Jo read till her eyes gave out, and she was sick of books; got so fidgety that even good-natured Laurie had a quarrel with her, and so reduced in spirits that she desperately wished she had gone with Aunt March. Beth got on pretty well, for she was constantly forgetting that it was to be _all play, and no work_, and fell back into her old ways now and then; but something in the air affected her, and, more than once, her tranquillity was much disturbed; so much so, that, on one occasion, she actually shook poor dear Joanna, and told her she was \"a fright.\" Amy fared worst of all, for her resources were small; and when her sisters left her to amuse and care for herself, she soon found that accomplished and important little self a great burden. She didn't like dolls, fairy-tales were childish, and one couldn't draw all the time; tea-parties didn't amount to much, neither did picnics, unless very well conducted. \"If one could have a fine house, full of nice girls, or go travelling, the summer would be delightful; but to stay at home with three selfish sisters and a grown-up boy was enough to try the patience of a Boaz,\" complained Miss Malaprop, after several days devoted to pleasure, fretting, and _ennui_.\n\nNo one would own that they were tired of the experiment; but, by Friday night, each acknowledged to herself that she was glad the week was nearly done. Hoping to impress the lesson more deeply, Mrs. March, who had a good deal of humor, resolved to finish off the trial in an appropriate manner; so she gave Hannah a holiday, and let the girls enjoy the full effect of the play system.\n\nWhen they got up on Saturday morning, there was no fire in the kitchen, no breakfast in the dining-room, and no mother anywhere to be seen.\n\n\"Mercy on us! what _has_ happened?\" cried Jo, staring about her in dismay.\n\nMeg ran upstairs, and soon came back again, looking relieved, but rather bewildered, and a little ashamed.\n\n\"Mother isn't sick, only very tired, and she says she is going to stay quietly in her room all day, and let us do the best we can. It's a very queer thing for her to do, she doesn't act a bit like herself; but she says it has been a hard week for her, so we mustn't grumble, but take care of ourselves.\"\n\n\"That's easy enough, and I like the idea; I'm aching for something to do--that is, some new amusement, you know,\" added Jo quickly.\n\nIn fact it _was_ an immense relief to them all to have a little work, and they took hold with a will, but soon realized the truth of Hannah's saying, \"Housekeeping ain't no joke.\" There was plenty of food in the larder, and, while Beth and Amy set the table, Meg and Jo got breakfast, wondering, as they did so, why servants ever talked about hard work.\n\n\"I shall take some up to mother, though she said we were not to think of her, for she'd take care of herself,\" said Meg, who presided, and felt quite matronly behind the teapot.\n\nSo a tray was fitted out before any one began, and taken up, with the cook's compliments. The boiled tea was very bitter, the omelette scorched, and the biscuits speckled with saleratus; but Mrs. March received her repast with thanks, and laughed heartily over it after Jo was gone.\n\n\"Poor little souls, they will have a hard time, I'm afraid; but they won't suffer, and it will do them good,\" she said, producing the more palatable viands with which she had provided herself, and disposing of the bad breakfast, so that their feelings might not be hurt,--a motherly little deception, for which they were grateful.\n\nMany were the complaints below, and great the chagrin of the head cook at her failures. \"Never mind, I'll get the dinner, and be servant; you be mistress, keep your hands nice, see company, and give orders,\" said Jo, who knew still less than Meg about culinary affairs.\n\nThis obliging offer was gladly accepted; and Margaret retired to the parlor, which she hastily put in order by whisking the litter under the sofa, and shutting the blinds, to save the trouble of dusting. Jo, with perfect faith in her own powers, and a friendly desire to make up the quarrel, immediately put a note in the office, inviting Laurie to dinner.\n\n\"You'd better see what you have got before you think of having company,\" said Meg, when informed of the hospitable but rash act.\n\n\"Oh, there's corned beef and plenty of potatoes; and I shall get some asparagus, and a lobster, 'for a relish,' as Hannah says. We'll have lettuce, and make a salad. I don't know how, but the book tells. I'll have blanc-mange and strawberries for dessert; and coffee, too, if you want to be elegant.\"\n\n\"Don't try too many messes, Jo, for you can't make anything but gingerbread and molasses candy, fit to eat. I wash my hands of the dinner-party; and, since you have asked Laurie on your own responsibility, you may just take care of him.\"\n\n\"I don't want you to do anything but be civil to him, and help to the pudding. You'll give me your advice if I get in a muddle, won't you?\" asked Jo, rather hurt.\n\n\"Yes; but I don't know much, except about bread, and a few trifles. You had better ask mother's leave before you order anything,\" returned Meg prudently.\n\n\"Of course I shall; I'm not a fool,\" and Jo went off in a huff at the doubts expressed of her powers.\n\n\"Get what you like, and don't disturb me; I'm going out to dinner, and can't worry about things at home,\" said Mrs. March, when Jo spoke to her. \"I never enjoyed housekeeping, and I'm going to take a vacation to-day, and read, write, go visiting, and amuse myself.\"\n\nThe unusual spectacle of her busy mother rocking comfortably, and reading, early in the morning, made Jo feel as if some natural phenomenon had occurred, for an eclipse, an earthquake, or a volcanic eruption would hardly have seemed stranger.\n\n\"Everything is out of sorts, somehow,\" she said to herself, going down stairs. \"There's Beth crying; that's a sure sign that something is wrong with this family. If Amy is bothering, I'll shake her.\"\n\nFeeling very much out of sorts herself, Jo hurried into the parlor to find Beth sobbing over Pip, the canary, who lay dead in the cage, with his little claws pathetically extended, as if imploring the food for want of which he had died.\n\n\"It's all my fault--I forgot him--there isn't a seed or a drop left. O Pip! O Pip! how could I be so cruel to you?\" cried Beth, taking the poor thing in her hands, and trying to restore him.\n\nJo peeped into his half-open eye, felt his little heart, and finding him stiff and cold, shook her head, and offered her domino-box for a coffin.\n\n\"Put him in the oven, and maybe he will get warm and revive,\" said Amy hopefully.\n\n\"He's been starved, and he sha'n't be baked, now he's dead. I'll make him a shroud, and he shall be buried in the garden; and I'll never have another bird, never, my Pip! for I am too bad to own one,\" murmured Beth, sitting on the floor with her pet folded in her hands.\n\n\"The funeral shall be this afternoon, and we will all go. Now, don't cry, Bethy; it's a pity, but nothing goes right this week, and Pip has had the worst of the experiment. Make the shroud, and lay him in my box; and, after the dinner-party, we'll have a nice little funeral,\" said Jo, beginning to feel as if she had undertaken a good deal.\n\nLeaving the others to console Beth, she departed to the kitchen, which was in a most discouraging state of confusion. Putting on a big apron, she fell to work, and got the dishes piled up ready for washing, when she discovered that the fire was out.\n\n\"Here's a sweet prospect!\" muttered Jo, slamming the stove-door open, and poking vigorously among the cinders.\n\nHaving rekindled the fire, she thought she would go to market while the water heated. The walk revived her spirits; and, flattering herself that she had made good bargains, she trudged home again, after buying a very young lobster, some very old asparagus, and two boxes of acid strawberries. By the time she got cleared up, the dinner arrived, and the stove was red-hot. Hannah had left a pan of bread to rise, Meg had worked it up early, set it on the hearth for a second rising, and forgotten it. Meg was entertaining Sallie Gardiner in the parlor, when the door flew open, and a floury, crocky, flushed, and dishevelled figure appeared, demanding tartly,--\n\n\"I say, isn't bread 'riz' enough when it runs over the pans?\"\n\nSallie began to laugh; but Meg nodded, and lifted her eyebrows as high as they would go, which caused the apparition to vanish, and put the sour bread into the oven without further delay. Mrs. March went out, after peeping here and there to see how matters went, also saying a word of comfort to Beth, who sat making a winding-sheet, while the dear departed lay in state in the domino-box. A strange sense of helplessness fell upon the girls as the gray bonnet vanished round the corner; and despair seized them, when, a few minutes later, Miss Crocker appeared, and said she'd come to dinner. Now, this lady was a thin, yellow spinster, with a sharp nose and inquisitive eyes, who saw everything, and gossiped about all she saw. They disliked her, but had been taught to be kind to her, simply because she was old and poor, and had few friends. So Meg gave her the easy-chair, and tried to entertain her, while she asked questions, criticised everything, and told stories of the people whom she knew.\n\nLanguage cannot describe the anxieties, experiences, and exertions which Jo underwent that morning; and the dinner she served up became a standing joke. Fearing to ask any more advice, she did her best alone, and discovered that something more than energy and good-will is necessary to make a cook. She boiled the asparagus for an hour, and was grieved to find the heads cooked off and the stalks harder than ever. The bread burnt black; for the salad-dressing so aggravated her, that she let everything else go till she had convinced herself that she could not make it fit to eat. The lobster was a scarlet mystery to her, but she hammered and poked, till it was unshelled, and its meagre proportions concealed in a grove of lettuce-leaves. The potatoes had to be hurried, not to keep the asparagus waiting, and were not done at last. The blanc-mange was lumpy, and the strawberries not as ripe as they looked, having been skilfully \"deaconed.\"\n\n\"Well, they can eat beef, and bread and butter, if they are hungry; only it's mortifying to have to spend your whole morning for nothing,\" thought Jo, as she rang the bell half an hour later than usual, and stood, hot, tired, and dispirited, surveying the feast spread for Laurie, accustomed to all sorts of elegance, and Miss Crocker, whose curious eyes would mark all failures, and whose tattling tongue would report them far and wide.\n\nPoor Jo would gladly have gone under the table, as one thing after another was tasted and left; while Amy giggled, Meg looked distressed, Miss Crocker pursed up her lips, and Laurie talked and laughed with all his might, to give a cheerful tone to the festive scene. Jo's one strong point was the fruit, for she had sugared it well, and had a pitcher of rich cream to eat with it. Her hot cheeks cooled a trifle, and she drew a long breath, as the pretty glass plates went round, and every one looked graciously at the little rosy islands floating in a sea of cream. Miss Crocker tasted first, made a wry face, and drank some water hastily. Jo, who had refused, thinking there might not be enough, for they dwindled sadly after the picking over, glanced at Laurie, but he was eating away manfully, though there was a slight pucker about his mouth, and he kept his eye fixed on his plate. Amy, who was fond of delicate fare, took a heaping spoonful, choked, hid her face in her napkin, and left the table precipitately.\n\n\"Oh, what is it?\" exclaimed Jo trembling.\n\n\"Salt instead of sugar, and the cream is sour,\" replied Meg, with a tragic gesture.\n\nJo uttered a groan, and fell back in her chair; remembering that she had given a last hasty powdering to the berries out of one of the two boxes on the kitchen table, and had neglected to put the milk in the refrigerator. She turned scarlet, and was on the verge of crying, when she met Laurie's eyes, which _would_ look merry in spite of his heroic efforts; the comical side of the affair suddenly struck her, and she laughed till the tears ran down her cheeks. So did every one else, even \"Croaker,\" as the girls called the old lady; and the unfortunate dinner ended gayly, with bread and butter, olives and fun.\n\n\"I haven't strength of mind enough to clear up now, so we will sober ourselves with a funeral,\" said Jo, as they rose; and Miss Crocker made ready to go, being eager to tell the new story at another friend's dinner-table.\n\nThey did sober themselves, for Beth's sake; Laurie dug a grave under the ferns in the grove, little Pip was laid in, with many tears, by his tender-hearted mistress, and covered with moss, while a wreath of violets and chickweed was hung on the stone which bore his epitaph, composed by Jo, while she struggled with the dinner:--\n\n\"Here lies Pip March, Who died the 7th of June; Loved and lamented sore, And not forgotten soon.\"\n\nAt the conclusion of the ceremonies, Beth retired to her room, overcome with emotion and lobster; but there was no place of repose, for the beds were not made, and she found her grief much assuaged by beating up pillows and putting things in order. Meg helped Jo clear away the remains of the feast, which took half the afternoon, and left them so tired that they agreed to be contented with tea and toast for supper. Laurie took Amy to drive, which was a deed of charity, for the sour cream seemed to have had a bad effect upon her temper. Mrs. March came home to find the three older girls hard at work in the middle of the afternoon; and a glance at the closet gave her an idea of the success of one part of the experiment.\n\nBefore the housewives could rest, several people called, and there was a scramble to get ready to see them; then tea must be got, errands done; and one or two necessary bits of sewing neglected till the last minute. As twilight fell, dewy and still, one by one they gathered in the porch where the June roses were budding beautifully, and each groaned or sighed as she sat down, as if tired or troubled.\n\n\"What a dreadful day this has been!\" begun Jo, usually the first to speak.\n\n\"It has seemed shorter than usual, but _so_ uncomfortable,\" said Meg.\n\n\"Not a bit like home,\" added Amy.\n\n\"It can't seem so without Marmee and little Pip,\" sighed Beth, glancing, with full eyes, at the empty cage above her head.\n\n\"Here's mother, dear, and you shall have another bird to-morrow, if you want it.\"\n\nAs she spoke, Mrs. March came and took her place among them, looking as if her holiday had not been much pleasanter than theirs.\n\n\"Are you satisfied with your experiment, girls, or do you want another week of it?\" she asked, as Beth nestled up to her, and the rest turned toward her with brightening faces, as flowers turn toward the sun.\n\n\"I don't!\" cried Jo decidedly.\n\n\"Nor I,\" echoed the others.\n\n\"You think, then, that it is better to have a few duties, and live a little for others, do you?\"\n\n\"Lounging and larking doesn't pay,\" observed Jo, shaking her head. \"I'm tired of it, and mean to go to work at something right off.\"\n\n\"Suppose you learn plain cooking; that's a useful accomplishment, which no woman should be without,\" said Mrs. March, laughing inaudibly at the recollection of Jo's dinner-party; for she had met Miss Crocker, and heard her account of it.\n\n\"Mother, did you go away and let everything be, just to see how we'd get on?\" cried Meg, who had had suspicions all day.\n\n\"Yes; I wanted you to see how the comfort of all depends on each doing her share faithfully. While Hannah and I did your work, you got on pretty well, though I don't think you were very happy or amiable; so I thought, as a little lesson, I would show you what happens when every one thinks only of herself. Don't you feel that it is pleasanter to help one another, to have daily duties which make leisure sweet when it comes, and to bear and forbear, that home may be comfortable and lovely to us all?\"\n\n\"We do, mother, we do!\" cried the girls.\n\n\"Then let me advise you to take up your little burdens again; for though they seem heavy sometimes, they are good for us, and lighten as we learn to carry them. Work is wholesome, and there is plenty for every one; it keeps us from _ennui_ and mischief, is good for health and spirits, and gives us a sense of power and independence better than money or fashion.\"\n\n\"We'll work like bees, and love it too; see if we don't!\" said Jo. \"I'll learn plain cooking for my holiday task; and the next dinner-party I have shall be a success.\"\n\n\"I'll make the set of shirts for father, instead of letting you do it, Marmee. I can and I will, though I'm not fond of sewing; that will be better than fussing over my own things, which are plenty nice enough as they are,\" said Meg.\n\n\"I'll do my lessons every day, and not spend so much time with my music and dolls. I am a stupid thing, and ought to be studying, not playing,\" was Beth's resolution; while Amy followed their example by heroically declaring, \"I shall learn to make button-holes, and attend to my parts of speech.\"\n\n\"Very good! then I am quite satisfied with the experiment, and fancy that we shall not have to repeat it; only don't go to the other extreme, and delve like slaves. Have regular hours for work and play; make each day both useful and pleasant, and prove that you understand the worth of time by employing it well. Then youth will be delightful, old age will bring few regrets, and life become a beautiful success, in spite of poverty.\"\n\n\"We'll remember, mother!\" and they did.\n\n# CAMP LAURENCE.\n\nBeth was post-mistress, for, being most at home, she could attend to it regularly, and dearly liked the daily task of unlocking the little door and distributing the mail. One July day she came in with her hands full, and went about the house leaving letters and parcels, like the penny post.\n\n\"Here's your posy, mother! Laurie never forgets that,\" she said, putting the fresh nosegay in the vase that stood in \"Marmee's corner,\" and was kept supplied by the affectionate boy.\n\n\"Miss Meg March, one letter and a glove,\" continued Beth, delivering the articles to her sister, who sat near her mother, stitching wristbands.\n\n\"Why, I left a pair over there, and here is only one,\" said Meg, looking at the gray cotton glove.\n\n\"Didn't you drop the other in the garden?\"\n\n\"No, I'm sure I didn't; for there was only one in the office.\"\n\n\"I hate to have odd gloves! Never mind, the other may be found. My letter is only a translation of the German song I wanted; I think Mr. Brooke did it, for this isn't Laurie's writing.\"\n\nMrs. March glanced at Meg, who was looking very pretty in her gingham morning-gown, with the little curls blowing about her forehead, and very womanly, as she sat sewing at her little work-table, full of tidy white rolls; so unconscious of the thought in her mother's mind as she sewed and sung, while her fingers flew, and her thoughts were busied with girlish fancies as innocent and fresh as the pansies in her belt, that Mrs. March smiled, and was satisfied.\n\n\"Two letters for Doctor Jo, a book, and a funny old hat, which covered the whole post-office, stuck outside,\" said Beth, laughing, as she went into the study, where Jo sat writing.\n\n\"What a sly fellow Laurie is! I said I wished bigger hats were the fashion, because I burn my face every hot day. He said, 'Why mind the fashion? Wear a big hat, and be comfortable!' I said I would if I had one, and he has sent me this, to try me. I'll wear it, for fun, and show him I _don't_ care for the fashion;\" and, hanging the antique broad-brim on a bust of Plato, Jo read her letters.\n\nOne from her mother made her cheeks glow and her eyes fill, for it said to her,--\n\n\"I write a little word to tell you with how much satisfaction I watch your efforts to control your temper. You say nothing about your trials, failures, or successes, and think, perhaps, that no one sees them but the Friend whose help you daily ask, if I may trust the well-worn cover of your guide-book. _I_, too, have seen them all, and heartily believe in the sincerity of your resolution, since it begins to bear fruit. Go on, dear, patiently and bravely, and always believe that no one sympathizes more tenderly with you than your loving\n\n\"That does me good! that's worth millions of money and pecks of praise. O Marmee, I do try! I will keep on trying, and not get tired, since I have you to help me.\"\n\nLaying her head on her arms, Jo wet her little romance with a few happy tears, for she _had_ thought that no one saw and appreciated her efforts to be good; and this assurance was doubly precious, doubly encouraging, because unexpected, and from the person whose commendation she most valued. Feeling stronger than ever to meet and subdue her Apollyon, she pinned the note inside her frock, as a shield and a reminder, lest she be taken unaware, and proceeded to open her other letter, quite ready for either good or bad news. In a big, dashing hand, Laurie wrote,--\n\n\"DEAR JO, What ho!\n\nSome English girls and boys are coming to see me to-morrow and I want to have a jolly time. If it's fine, I'm going to pitch my tent in Longmeadow, and row up the whole crew to lunch and croquet,--have a fire, make messes, gypsy fashion, and all sorts of larks. They are nice people, and like such things. Brooke will go, to keep us boys steady, and Kate Vaughn will play propriety for the girls. I want you all to come; can't let Beth off, at any price, and nobody shall worry her. Don't bother about rations,--I'll see to that, and everything else,--only do come, there's a good fellow!\n\n\"In a tearing hurry, Yours ever, LAURIE.\"\n\n\"Here's richness!\" cried Jo, flying in to tell the news to Meg.\n\n\"Of course we can go, mother? it will be such a help to Laurie, for I can row, and Meg see to the lunch, and the children be useful in some way.\"\n\n\"I hope the Vaughns are not fine, grown-up people. Do you know anything about them, Jo?\" asked Meg.\n\n\"Only that there are four of them. Kate is older than you, Fred and Frank (twins) about my age, and a little girl (Grace), who is nine or ten. Laurie knew them abroad, and liked the boys; I fancied, from the way he primmed up his mouth in speaking of her, that he didn't admire Kate much.\"\n\n\"I'm so glad my French print is clean; it's just the thing, and so becoming!\" observed Meg complacently. \"Have you anything decent, Jo?\"\n\n\"Scarlet and gray boating suit, good enough for me. I shall row and tramp about, so I don't want any starch to think of. You'll come, Bethy?\"\n\n\"If you won't let any of the boys talk to me.\"\n\n\"Not a boy!\"\n\n\"I like to please Laurie; and I'm not afraid of Mr. Brooke, he is so kind; but I don't want to play, or sing, or say anything. I'll work hard, and not trouble any one; and you'll take care of me, Jo, so I'll go.\"\n\n\"That's my good girl; you do try to fight off your shyness, and I love you for it. Fighting faults isn't easy, as I know; and a cheery word kind of gives a lift. Thank you, mother,\" and Jo gave the thin cheek a grateful kiss, more precious to Mrs. March than if it had given back the rosy roundness of her youth.\n\n\"I had a box of chocolate drops, and the picture I wanted to copy,\" said Amy, showing her mail.\n\n\"And I got a note from Mr. Laurence, asking me to come over and play to him to-night, before the lamps are lighted, and I shall go,\" added Beth, whose friendship with the old gentleman prospered finely.\n\n\"Now let's fly round, and do double duty to-day, so that we can play to-morrow with free minds,\" said Jo, preparing to replace her pen with a broom.\n\nWhen the sun peeped into the girls' room early next morning, to promise them a fine day, he saw a comical sight. Each had made such preparation for the fête as seemed necessary and proper. Meg had an extra row of little curl-papers across her forehead, Jo had copiously anointed her afflicted face with cold cream, Beth had taken Joanna to bed with her to atone for the approaching separation, and Amy had capped the climax by putting a clothes-pin on her nose, to uplift the offending feature. It was one of the kind artists use to hold the paper on their drawing-boards, therefore quite appropriate and effective for the purpose to which it was now put. This funny spectacle appeared to amuse the sun, for he burst out with such radiance that Jo woke up, and roused all her sisters by a hearty laugh at Amy's ornament.\n\nSunshine and laughter were good omens for a pleasure party, and soon a lively bustle began in both houses. Beth, who was ready first, kept reporting what went on next door, and enlivened her sisters' toilets by frequent telegrams from the window.\n\n\"There goes the man with the tent! I see Mrs. Barker doing up the lunch in a hamper and a great basket. Now Mr. Laurence is looking up at the sky, and the weathercock; I wish he would go, too. There's Laurie, looking like a sailor,--nice boy! Oh, mercy me! here's a carriage full of people--a tall lady, a little girl, and two dreadful boys. One is lame; poor thing, he's got a crutch. Laurie didn't tell us that. Be quick, girls! it's getting late. Why, there is Ned Moffat, I do declare. Look, Meg, isn't that the man who bowed to you one day, when we were shopping?\"\n\n\"So it is. How queer that he should come. I thought he was at the Mountains. There is Sallie; I'm glad she got back in time. Am I all right, Jo?\" cried Meg, in a flutter.\n\n\"A regular daisy. Hold up your dress and put your hat straight; it looks sentimental tipped that way, and will fly off at the first puff. Now, then, come on!\"\n\n\"O Jo, you are not going to wear that awful hat? It's too absurd! You shall _not_ make a guy of yourself,\" remonstrated Meg, as Jo tied down, with a red ribbon, the broad-brimmed, old-fashioned Leghorn Laurie had sent for a joke.\n\n\"I just will, though, for it's capital,--so shady, light, and big. It will make fun; and I don't mind being a guy if I'm comfortable.\" With that Jo marched straight away, and the rest followed,--a bright little band of sisters, all looking their best, in summer suits, with happy faces under the jaunty hat-brims.\n\nLaurie ran to meet, and present them to his friends, in the most cordial manner. The lawn was the reception-room, and for several minutes a lively scene was enacted there. Meg was grateful to see that Miss Kate, though twenty, was dressed with a simplicity which American girls would do well to imitate; and she was much flattered by Mr. Ned's assurances that he came especially to see her. Jo understood why Laurie \"primmed up his mouth\" when speaking of Kate, for that young lady had a stand-off-don't-touch-me air, which contrasted strongly with the free and easy demeanor of the other girls. Beth took an observation of the new boys, and decided that the lame one was not \"dreadful,\" but gentle and feeble, and she would be kind to him on that account. Amy found Grace a well-mannered, merry little person; and after staring dumbly at one another for a few minutes, they suddenly became very good friends.\n\nTents, lunch, and croquet utensils having been sent on beforehand, the party was soon embarked, and the two boats pushed off together, leaving Mr. Laurence waving his hat on the shore. Laurie and Jo rowed one boat; Mr. Brooke and Ned the other; while Fred Vaughn, the riotous twin, did his best to upset both by paddling about in a wherry like a disturbed water-bug. Jo's funny hat deserved a vote of thanks, for it was of general utility; it broke the ice in the beginning, by producing a laugh; it created quite a refreshing breeze, flapping to and fro, as she rowed, and would make an excellent umbrella for the whole party, if a shower came up, she said. Kate looked rather amazed at Jo's proceedings, especially as she exclaimed \"Christopher Columbus!\" when she lost her oar; and Laurie said, \"My dear fellow, did I hurt you?\" when he tripped over her feet in taking his place. But after putting up her glass to examine the queer girl several times, Miss Kate decided that she was \"odd, but rather clever,\" and smiled upon her from afar.\n\nMeg, in the other boat, was delightfully situated, face to face with the rowers, who both admired the prospect, and feathered their oars with uncommon \"skill and dexterity.\" Mr. Brooke was a grave, silent young man, with handsome brown eyes and a pleasant voice. Meg liked his quiet manners, and considered him a walking encyclopædia of useful knowledge. He never talked to her much; but he looked at her a good deal, and she felt sure that he did not regard her with aversion. Ned, being in college, of course put on all the airs which Freshmen think it their bounden duty to assume; he was not very wise, but very good-natured, and altogether an excellent person to carry on a picnic. Sallie Gardiner was absorbed in keeping her white piqué dress clean, and chattering with the ubiquitous Fred, who kept Beth in constant terror by his pranks.\n\nIt was not far to Longmeadow; but the tent was pitched and the wickets down by the time they arrived. A pleasant green field, with three wide-spreading oaks in the middle, and a smooth strip of turf for croquet.\n\n\"Welcome to Camp Laurence!\" said the young host, as they landed, with exclamations of delight.\n\n\"Brooke is commander-in-chief; I am commissary-general; the other fellows are staff-officers; and you, ladies, are company. The tent is for your especial benefit, and that oak is your drawing-room; this is the mess-room, and the third is the camp-kitchen. Now, let's have a game before it gets hot, and then we'll see about dinner.\"\n\nFrank, Beth, Amy, and Grace sat down to watch the game played by the other eight. Mr. Brooke chose Meg, Kate, and Fred; Laurie took Sallie, Jo, and Ned. The Englishers played well; but the Americans played better, and contested every inch of the ground as strongly as if the spirit of '76 inspired them. Jo and Fred had several skirmishes, and once narrowly escaped high words. Jo was through the last wicket, and had missed the stroke, which failure ruffled her a good deal. Fred was close behind her, and his turn came before hers; he gave a stroke, his ball hit the wicket, and stopped an inch on the wrong side. No one was very near; and running up to examine, he gave it a sly nudge with his toe, which put it just an inch on the right side.\n\n\"I'm through! Now, Miss Jo, I'll settle you, and get in first,\" cried the young gentleman, swinging his mallet for another blow.\n\n\"You pushed it; I saw you; it's my turn now,\" said Jo sharply.\n\n\"Upon my word, I didn't move it; it rolled a bit, perhaps, but that is allowed; so stand off, please, and let me have a go at the stake.\"\n\n\"We don't cheat in America, but you can, if you choose,\" said Jo angrily.\n\n\"Yankees are a deal the most tricky, everybody knows. There you go!\" returned Fred, croqueting her ball far away.\n\nJo opened her lips to say something rude, but checked herself in time, colored up to her forehead, and stood a minute, hammering down a wicket with all her might, while Fred hit the stake, and declared himself out with much exultation. She went off to get her ball, and was a long time finding it, among the bushes; but she came back, looking cool and quiet, and waited her turn patiently. It took several strokes to regain the place she had lost; and, when she got there, the other side had nearly won, for Kate's ball was the last but one, and lay near the stake.\n\n\"By George, it's all up with us! Good-by, Kate. Miss Jo owes me one, so you are finished,\" cried Fred excitedly, as they all drew near to see the finish.\n\n\"Yankees have a trick of being generous to their enemies,\" said Jo, with a look that made the lad redden, \"especially when they beat them,\" she added, as, leaving Kate's ball untouched, she won the game by a clever stroke.\n\nLaurie threw up his hat; then remembered that it wouldn't do to exult over the defeat of his guests, and stopped in the middle of a cheer to whisper to his friend,--\n\n\"Good for you, Jo! He did cheat, I saw him; we can't tell him so, but he won't do it again, take my word for it.\"\n\nMeg drew her aside, under pretence of pinning up a loose braid, and said approvingly,--\n\n\"It was dreadfully provoking; but you kept your temper, and I'm so glad, Jo.\"\n\n\"Don't praise me, Meg, for I could box his ears this minute. I should certainly have boiled over if I hadn't stayed among the nettles till I got my rage under enough to hold my tongue. It's simmering now, so I hope he'll keep out of my way,\" returned Jo, biting her lips, as she glowered at Fred from under her big hat.\n\n\"Time for lunch,\" said Mr. Brooke, looking at his watch. \"Commissary-general, will you make the fire and get water, while Miss March, Miss Sallie, and I spread the table? Who can make good coffee?\"\n\n\"Jo can,\" said Meg, glad to recommend her sister. So Jo, feeling that her late lessons in cookery were to do her honor, went to preside over the coffee-pot, while the children collected dry sticks, and the boys made a fire, and got water from a spring near by. Miss Kate sketched, and Frank talked to Beth, who was making little mats of braided rushes to serve as plates.\n\nThe commander-in-chief and his aids soon spread the table-cloth with an inviting array of eatables and drinkables, prettily decorated with green leaves. Jo announced that the coffee was ready, and every one settled themselves to a hearty meal; for youth is seldom dyspeptic, and exercise develops wholesome appetites. A very merry lunch it was; for everything seemed fresh and funny, and frequent peals of laughter startled a venerable horse who fed near by. There was a pleasing inequality in the table, which produced many mishaps to cups and plates; acorns dropped into the milk, little black ants partook of the refreshments without being invited, and fuzzy caterpillars swung down from the tree, to see what was going on. Three white-headed children peeped over the fence, and an objectionable dog barked at them from the other side of the river with all his might and main.\n\n\"There's salt here, if you prefer it,\" said Laurie, as he handed Jo a saucer of berries.\n\n\"Thank you, I prefer spiders,\" she replied, fishing up two unwary little ones who had gone to a creamy death. \"How dare you remind me of that horrid dinner-party, when yours is so nice in every way?\" added Jo, as they both laughed, and ate out of one plate, the china having run short.\n\n\"I had an uncommonly good time that day, and haven't got over it yet. This is no credit to me, you know; I don't do anything; it's you and Meg and Brooke who make it go, and I'm no end obliged to you. What shall we do when we can't eat any more?\" asked Laurie, feeling that his trump card had been played when lunch was over.\n\n\"Have games, till it's cooler. I brought 'Authors,' and I dare say Miss Kate knows something new and nice. Go and ask her; she's company, and you ought to stay with her more.\"\n\n\"Aren't you company too? I thought she'd suit Brooke; but he keeps talking to Meg, and Kate just stares at them through that ridiculous glass of hers. I'm going, so you needn't try to preach propriety, for you can't do it, Jo.\"\n\nMiss Kate did know several new games; and as the girls would not, and the boys could not, eat any more, they all adjourned to the drawing-room to play \"Rigmarole.\"\n\n\"One person begins a story, any nonsense you like, and tells as long as he pleases, only taking care to stop short at some exciting point, when the next takes it up and does the same. It's very funny when well done, and makes a perfect jumble of tragical comical stuff to laugh over. Please start it, Mr. Brooke,\" said Kate, with a commanding air, which surprised Meg, who treated the tutor with as much respect as any other gentleman.\n\nLying on the grass at the feet of the two young ladies, Mr. Brooke obediently began the story, with the handsome brown eyes steadily fixed upon the sunshiny river.\n\n\"Once on a time, a knight went out into the world to seek his fortune, for he had nothing but his sword and his shield. He travelled a long while, nearly eight-and-twenty years, and had a hard time of it, till he came to the palace of a good old king, who had offered a reward to any one who would tame and train a fine but unbroken colt, of which he was very fond. The knight agreed to try, and got on slowly but surely; for the colt was a gallant fellow, and soon learned to love his new master, though he was freakish and wild. Every day, when he gave his lessons to this pet of the king's, the knight rode him through the city; and, as he rode, he looked everywhere for a certain beautiful face, which he had seen many times in his dreams, but never found. One day, as he went prancing down a quiet street, he saw at the window of a ruinous castle the lovely face. He was delighted, inquired who lived in this old castle, and was told that several captive princesses were kept there by a spell, and spun all day to lay up money to buy their liberty. The knight wished intensely that he could free them; but he was poor, and could only go by each day, watching for the sweet face, and longing to see it out in the sunshine. At last, he resolved to get into the castle and ask how he could help them. He went and knocked; the great door flew open, and he beheld--\"\n\n\"A ravishingly lovely lady, who exclaimed, with a cry of rapture, 'At last! at last!'\" continued Kate, who had read French novels, and admired the style. \"''Tis she!' cried Count Gustave, and fell at her feet in an ecstasy of joy. 'Oh, rise!' she said, extending a hand of marble fairness. 'Never! till you tell me how I may rescue you,' swore the knight, still kneeling. 'Alas, my cruel fate condemns me to remain here till my tyrant is destroyed.' 'Where is the villain?' 'In the mauve salon. Go, brave heart, and save me from despair.' 'I obey, and return victorious or dead!' With these thrilling words he rushed away, and flinging open the door of the mauve salon, was about to enter, when he received--\"\n\n\"A stunning blow from the big Greek lexicon, which an old fellow in a black gown fired at him,\" said Ned. \"Instantly Sir What's-his-name recovered himself, pitched the tyrant out of the window, and turned to join the lady, victorious, but with a bump on his brow; found the door locked, tore up the curtains, made a rope ladder, got half-way down when the ladder broke, and he went head first into the moat, sixty feet below. Could swim like a duck, paddled round the castle till he came to a little door guarded by two stout fellows; knocked their heads together till they cracked like a couple of nuts, then, by a trifling exertion of his prodigious strength, he smashed in the door, went up a pair of stone steps covered with dust a foot thick, toads as big as your fist, and spiders that would frighten you into hysterics, Miss March. At the top of these steps he came plump upon a sight that took his breath away and chilled his blood--\"\n\n\"A tall figure, all in white with a veil over its face and a lamp in its wasted hand,\" went on Meg. \"It beckoned, gliding noiselessly before him down a corridor as dark and cold as any tomb. Shadowy effigies in armor stood on either side, a dead silence reigned, the lamp burned blue, and the ghostly figure ever and anon turned its face toward him, showing the glitter of awful eyes through its white veil. They reached a curtained door, behind which sounded lovely music; he sprang forward to enter, but the spectre plucked him back, and waved threateningly before him a--\"\n\n\"Snuff-box,\" said Jo, in a sepulchral tone, which convulsed the audience. \"'Thankee,' said the knight politely, as he took a pinch, and sneezed seven times so violently that his head fell off. 'Ha! ha!' laughed the ghost; and having peeped through the key-hole at the princesses spinning away for dear life, the evil spirit picked up her victim and put him in a large tin box, where there were eleven other knights packed together without their heads, like sardines, who all rose and began to--\"\n\n\"Dance a hornpipe,\" cut in Fred, as Jo paused for breath; \"and, as they danced, the rubbishy old castle turned to a man-of-war in full sail. 'Up with the jib, reef the tops'l halliards, helm hard a lee, and man the guns!' roared the captain, as a Portuguese pirate hove in sight, with a flag black as ink flying from her foremast. 'Go in and win, my hearties!' says the captain; and a tremendous fight begun. Of course the British beat; they always do.\"\n\n\"No, they don't!\" cried Jo, aside.\n\n\"Having taken the pirate captain prisoner, sailed slap over the schooner, whose decks were piled with dead, and whose lee-scuppers ran blood, for the order had been 'Cutlasses, and die hard!' 'Bosen's mate, take a bight of the flying-jib sheet, and start this villain if he don't confess his sins double quick,' said the British captain. The Portuguese held his tongue like a brick, and walked the plank, while the jolly tars cheered like mad. But the sly dog dived, came up under the man-of-war, scuttled her, and down she went, with all sail set, 'To the bottom of the sea, sea, sea,' where--\"\n\n\"Oh, gracious! what _shall_ I say?\" cried Sallie, as Fred ended his rigmarole, in which he had jumbled together, pell-mell, nautical phrases and facts, out of one of his favorite books. \"Well they went to the bottom, and a nice mermaid welcomed them, but was much grieved on finding the box of headless knights, and kindly pickled them in brine, hoping to discover the mystery about them; for, being a woman, she was curious. By and by a diver came down, and the mermaid said, 'I'll give you this box of pearls if you can take it up;' for she wanted to restore the poor things to life, and couldn't raise the heavy load herself. So the diver hoisted it up, and was much disappointed, on opening it, to find no pearls. He left it in a great lonely field, where it was found by a--\"\n\n\"Little goose-girl, who kept a hundred fat geese in the field,\" said Amy, when Sallie's invention gave out. \"The little girl was sorry for them, and asked an old woman what she should do to help them. 'Your geese will tell you, they know everything,' said the old woman. So she asked what she should use for new heads, since the old ones were lost, and all the geese opened their hundred mouths and screamed--\"\n\n\"'Cabbages!'\" continued Laurie promptly. \"'Just the thing,' said the girl, and ran to get twelve fine ones from her garden. She put them on, the knights revived at once, thanked her, and went on their way rejoicing, never knowing the difference, for there were so many other heads like them in the world that no one thought anything of it. The knight in whom I'm interested went back to find the pretty face, and learned that the princesses had spun themselves free, and all gone to be married, but one. He was in a great state of mind at that; and mounting the colt, who stood by him through thick and thin, rushed to the castle to see which was left. Peeping over the hedge, he saw the queen of his affections picking flowers in her garden. 'Will you give me a rose?' said he. 'You must come and get it. I can't come to you; it isn't proper,' said she, as sweet as honey. He tried to climb over the hedge, but it seemed to grow higher and higher; then he tried to push through, but it grew thicker and thicker, and he was in despair. So he patiently broke twig after twig, till he had made a little hole, through which he peeped, saying imploringly, 'Let me in! let me in!' But the pretty princess did not seem to understand, for she picked her roses quietly, and left him to fight his way in. Whether he did or not, Frank will tell you.\"\n\n\"I can't; I'm not playing, I never do,\" said Frank, dismayed at the sentimental predicament out of which he was to rescue the absurd couple. Beth had disappeared behind Jo, and Grace was asleep.\n\n\"So the poor knight is to be left sticking in the hedge, is he?\" asked Mr. Brooke, still watching the river, and playing with the wild rose in his button-hole.\n\n\"I guess the princess gave him a posy, and opened the gate, after a while,\" said Laurie, smiling to himself, as he threw acorns at his tutor.\n\n\"What a piece of nonsense we have made! With practice we might do something quite clever. Do you know 'Truth'?\" asked Sallie, after they had laughed over their story.\n\n\"I hope so,\" said Meg soberly.\n\n\"The game, I mean?\"\n\n\"What is it?\" said Fred.\n\n\"Why, you pile up your hands, choose a number, and draw out in turn, and the person who draws at the number has to answer truly any questions put by the rest. It's great fun.\"\n\n\"Let's try it,\" said Jo, who liked new experiments.\n\nMiss Kate and Mr. Brooke, Meg, and Ned declined, but Fred, Sallie, Jo, and Laurie piled and drew; and the lot fell to Laurie.\n\n\"Who are your heroes?\" asked Jo.\n\n\"Grandfather and Napoleon.\"\n\n\"Which lady here do you think prettiest?\" said Sallie.\n\n\"Margaret.\"\n\n\"Which do you like best?\" from Fred.\n\n\"Jo, of course.\"\n\n\"What silly questions you ask!\" and Jo gave a disdainful shrug as the rest laughed at Laurie's matter-of-fact tone.\n\n\"Try again; Truth isn't a bad game,\" said Fred.\n\n\"It's a very good one for you,\" retorted Jo, in a low voice.\n\nHer turn came next.\n\n\"What is your greatest fault?\" asked Fred, by way of testing in her the virtue he lacked himself.\n\n\"A quick temper.\"\n\n\"What do you most wish for?\" said Laurie.\n\n\"A pair of boot-lacings,\" returned Jo, guessing and defeating his purpose.\n\n\"Not a true answer; you must say what you really do want most.\"\n\n\"Genius; don't you wish you could give it to me, Laurie?\" and she slyly smiled in his disappointed face.\n\n\"What virtues do you most admire in a man?\" asked Sallie.\n\n\"Courage and honesty.\"\n\n\"Now my turn,\" said Fred, as his hand came last.\n\n\"Let's give it to him,\" whispered Laurie to Jo, who nodded, and asked at once,--\n\n\"Didn't you cheat at croquet?\"\n\n\"Well, yes, a little bit.\"\n\n\"Good! Didn't you take your story out of 'The Sea-Lion?'\" said Laurie.\n\"Rather.\"\n\n\"Don't you think the English nation perfect in every respect?\" asked Sallie.\n\n\"I should be ashamed of myself if I didn't.\"\n\n\"He's a true John Bull. Now, Miss Sallie, you shall have a chance without waiting to draw. I'll harrow up your feelings first, by asking if you don't think you are something of a flirt,\" said Laurie, as Jo nodded to Fred, as a sign that peace was declared.\n\n\"You impertinent boy! of course I'm not,\" exclaimed Sallie, with an air that proved the contrary.\n\n\"What do you hate most?\" asked Fred.\n\n\"Spiders and rice-pudding.\"\n\n\"What do you like best?\" asked Jo.\n\n\"Dancing and French gloves.\"\n\n\"Well, _I_ think Truth is a very silly play; let's have a sensible game of Authors, to refresh our minds,\" proposed Jo.\n\nNed, Frank, and the little girls joined in this, and, while it went on, the three elders sat apart, talking. Miss Kate took out her sketch again, and Margaret watched her, while Mr. Brooke lay on the grass, with a book, which he did not read.\n\n\"How beautifully you do it! I wish I could draw,\" said Meg, with mingled admiration and regret in her voice.\n\n\"Why don't you learn? I should think you had taste and talent for it,\" replied Miss Kate graciously.\n\n\"I haven't time.\"\n\n\"Your mamma prefers other accomplishments, I fancy. So did mine; but I proved to her that I had talent, by taking a few lessons privately, and then she was quite willing I should go on. Can't you do the same with your governess?\"\n\n\"I have none.\"\n\"I forgot; young ladies in America go to school more than with us. Very fine schools they are, too, papa says. You go to a private one, I suppose?\"\n\n\"I don't go at all; I am a governess myself.\"\n\n\"Oh, indeed!\" said Miss Kate; but she might as well have said, \"Dear me, how dreadful!\" for her tone implied it, and something in her face made Meg color, and wish she had not been so frank.\n\nMr. Brooke looked up, and said quickly, \"Young ladies in America love independence as much as their ancestors did, and are admired and respected for supporting themselves.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes; of course it's very nice and proper in them to do so. We have many most respectable and worthy young women, who do the same and are employed by the nobility, because, being the daughters of gentlemen, they are both well-bred and accomplished, you know,\" said Miss Kate, in a patronizing tone, that hurt Meg's pride, and made her work seem not only more distasteful, but degrading.\n\n\"Did the German song suit, Miss March?\" inquired Mr. Brooke, breaking an awkward pause.\n\n\"Oh, yes! it was very sweet, and I'm much obliged to whoever translated it for me;\" and Meg's downcast face brightened as she spoke.\n\n\"Don't you read German?\" asked Miss Kate, with a look of surprise.\n\n\"Not very well. My father, who taught me, is away, and I don't get on very fast alone, for I've no one to correct my pronunciation.\"\n\n\"Try a little now; here is Schiller's 'Mary Stuart,' and a tutor who loves to teach,\" and Mr. Brooke laid his book on her lap, with an inviting smile.\n\n\"It's so hard I'm afraid to try,\" said Meg, grateful, but bashful in the presence of the accomplished young lady beside her.\n\n\"I'll read a bit to encourage you;\" and Miss Kate read one of the most beautiful passages, in a perfectly correct but perfectly expressionless manner.\n\nMr. Brooke made no comment, as she returned the book to Meg, who said innocently,--\n\n\"I thought it was poetry.\"\n\n\"Some of it is. Try this passage.\"\n\nThere was a queer smile about Mr. Brooke's mouth as he opened at poor Mary's lament.\n\nMeg, obediently following the long grass-blade which her new tutor used to point with, read slowly and timidly, unconsciously making poetry of the hard words by the soft intonation of her musical voice. Down the page went the green guide, and presently, forgetting her listener in the beauty of the sad scene, Meg read as if alone, giving a little touch of tragedy to the words of the unhappy queen. If she had seen the brown eyes then, she would have stopped short; but she never looked up, and the lesson was not spoiled for her.\n\n\"Very well indeed!\" said Mr. Brooke, as she paused, quite ignoring her many mistakes, and looking as if he did, indeed, \"love to teach.\"\n\nMiss Kate put up her glass, and, having taken a survey of the little tableau before her, shut her sketch-book, saying, with condescension,--\n\n\"You've a nice accent, and, in time, will be a clever reader. I advise you to learn, for German is a valuable accomplishment to teachers. I must look after Grace, she is romping;\" and Miss Kate strolled away, adding to herself, with a shrug, \"I didn't come to chaperone a governess, though she _is_ young and pretty. What odd people these Yankees are; I'm afraid Laurie will be quite spoilt among them.\"\n\n\"I forgot that English people rather turn up their noses at governesses, and don't treat them as we do,\" said Meg, looking after the retreating figure with an annoyed expression.\n\n\"Tutors, also, have rather a hard time of it there, as I know to my sorrow. There's no place like America for us workers, Miss Margaret;\" and Mr. Brooke looked so contented and cheerful, that Meg was ashamed to lament her hard lot.\n\n\"I'm glad I live in it then. I don't like my work, but I get a good deal of satisfaction out of it after all, so I won't complain; I only wish I liked teaching as you do.\"\n\n\"I think you would if you had Laurie for a pupil. I shall be very sorry to lose him next year,\" said Mr. Brooke, busily punching holes in the turf.\n\n\"Going to college, I suppose?\" Meg's lips asked that question, but her eyes added, \"And what becomes of you?\"\n\n\"Yes; it's high time he went, for he is ready; and as soon as he is off, I shall turn soldier. I am needed.\"\n\n\"I am glad of that!\" exclaimed Meg. \"I should think every young man would want to go; though it is hard for the mothers and sisters who stay at home,\" she added sorrowfully.\n\n\"I have neither, and very few friends, to care whether I live or die,\" said Mr. Brooke, rather bitterly, as he absently put the dead rose in the hole he had made and covered it up, like a little grave.\n\n\"Laurie and his grandfather would care a great deal, and we should all be very sorry to have any harm happen to you,\" said Meg heartily.\n\n\"Thank you; that sounds pleasant,\" began Mr. Brooke, looking cheerful again; but before he could finish his speech, Ned, mounted on the old horse, came lumbering up to display his equestrian skill before the young ladies, and there was no more quiet that day.\n\n\"Don't you love to ride?\" asked Grace of Amy, as they stood resting, after a race round the field with the others, led by Ned.\n\n\"I dote upon it; my sister Meg used to ride when papa was rich, but we don't keep any horses now, except Ellen Tree,\" added Amy, laughing.\n\n\"Tell me about Ellen Tree; is it a donkey?\" asked Grace curiously.\n\n\"Why, you see, Jo is crazy about horses, and so am I, but we've only got an old side-saddle, and no horse. Out in our garden is an apple-tree, that has a nice low branch; so Jo put the saddle on it, fixed some reins on the part that turns up, and we bounce away on Ellen Tree whenever we like.\"\n\n\"How funny!\" laughed Grace. \"I have a pony at home, and ride nearly every day in the park, with Fred and Kate; it's very nice, for my friends go too, and the Row is full of ladies and gentlemen.\"\n\n\"Dear, how charming! I hope I shall go abroad some day; but I'd rather go to Rome than the Row,\" said Amy, who had not the remotest idea what the Row was, and wouldn't have asked for the world.\n\nFrank, sitting just behind the little girls, heard what they were saying, and pushed his crutch away from him with an impatient gesture as he watched the active lads going through all sorts of comical gymnastics. Beth, who was collecting the scattered Author-cards, looked up, and said, in her shy yet friendly way,--\n\n\"I'm afraid you are tired; can I do anything for you?\"\n\n\"Talk to me, please; it's dull, sitting by myself,\" answered Frank, who had evidently been used to being made much of at home.\n\nIf he had asked her to deliver a Latin oration, it would not have seemed a more impossible task to bashful Beth; but there was no place to run to, no Jo to hide behind now, and the poor boy looked so wistfully at her, that she bravely resolved to try.\n\n\"What do you like to talk about?\" she asked, fumbling over the cards, and dropping half as she tried to tie them up.\n\n\"Well, I like to hear about cricket and boating and hunting,\" said Frank, who had not yet learned to suit his amusements to his strength.\n\n\"My heart! what shall I do? I don't know anything about them,\" thought Beth; and, forgetting the boy's misfortune in her flurry, she said, hoping to make him talk, \"I never saw any hunting, but I suppose you know all about it.\"\n\n\"I did once; but I can never hunt again, for I got hurt leaping a confounded five-barred gate; so there are no more horses and hounds for me,\" said Frank, with a sigh that made Beth hate herself for her innocent blunder.\n\n\"Your deer are much prettier than our ugly buffaloes,\" she said, turning to the prairies for help, and feeling glad that she had read one of the boys' books in which Jo delighted.\n\nBuffaloes proved soothing and satisfactory; and, in her eagerness to amuse another, Beth forgot herself, and was quite unconscious of her sisters' surprise and delight at the unusual spectacle of Beth talking away to one of the dreadful boys, against whom she had begged protection.\n\n\"Bless her heart! She pities him, so she is good to him,\" said Jo, beaming at her from the croquet-ground.\n\n\"I always said she was a little saint,\" added Meg, as if there could be no further doubt of it.\n\n\"I haven't heard Frank laugh so much for ever so long,\" said Grace to Amy, as they sat discussing dolls, and making tea-sets out of the acorn-cups.\n\n\"My sister Beth is a very _fastidious_ girl, when she likes to be,\" said Amy, well pleased at Beth's success. She meant \"fascinating,\" but as Grace didn't know the exact meaning of either word, \"fastidious\" sounded well, and made a good impression.\n\nAn impromptu circus, fox and geese, and an amicable game of croquet, finished the afternoon. At sunset the tent was struck, hampers packed, wickets pulled up, boats loaded, and the whole party floated down the river, singing at the tops of their voices. Ned, getting sentimental, warbled a serenade with the pensive refrain,--\n\n\"Alone, alone, ah! woe, alone,\"\n\nand at the lines--\n\n\"We each are young, we each have a heart, Oh, why should we stand thus coldly apart?\"\n\nhe looked at Meg with such a lackadaisical expression that she laughed outright and spoilt his song.\n\n\"How can you be so cruel to me?\" he whispered, under cover of a lively chorus. \"You've kept close to that starched-up Englishwoman all day, and now you snub me.\"\n\n\"I didn't mean to; but you looked so funny I really couldn't help it,\" replied Meg, passing over the first part of his reproach; for it was quite true that she _had_ shunned him, remembering the Moffat party and the talk after it.\n\nNed was offended, and turned to Sallie for consolation, saying to her rather pettishly, \"There isn't a bit of flirt in that girl, is there?\"\n\n\"Not a particle; but she's a dear,\" returned Sallie, defending her friend even while confessing her short-comings.\n\n\"She's not a stricken deer, any way,\" said Ned, trying to be witty, and succeeding as well as very young gentlemen usually do.\n\nOn the lawn, where it had gathered, the little party separated with cordial good-nights and good-byes, for the Vaughns were going to Canada. As the four sisters went home through the garden, Miss Kate looked after them, saying, without the patronizing tone in her voice, \"In spite of their demonstrative manners, American girls are very nice when one knows them.\"\n\n\"I quite agree with you,\" said Mr. Brooke.\n\n# CASTLES IN THE AIR.\n\nLaurie lay luxuriously swinging to and fro in his hammock, one warm September afternoon, wondering what his neighbors were about, but too lazy to go and find out. He was in one of his moods; for the day had been both unprofitable and unsatisfactory, and he was wishing he could live it over again. The hot weather made him indolent, and he had shirked his studies, tried Mr. Brooke's patience to the utmost, displeased his grandfather by practising half the afternoon, frightened the maid-servants half out of their wits, by mischievously hinting that one of his dogs was going mad, and, after high words with the stable-man about some fancied neglect of his horse, he had flung himself into his hammock, to fume over the stupidity of the world in general, till the peace of the lovely day quieted him in spite of himself. Staring up into the green gloom of the horse-chestnut trees above him, he dreamed dreams of all sorts, and was just imagining himself tossing on the ocean, in a voyage round the world, when the sound of voices brought him ashore in a flash. Peeping through the meshes of the hammock, he saw the Marches coming out, as if bound on some expedition.\n\n\"What in the world are those girls about now?\" thought Laurie, opening his sleepy eyes to take a good look, for there was something rather peculiar in the appearance of his neighbors. Each wore a large, flapping hat, a brown linen pouch slung over one shoulder, and carried a long staff. Meg had a cushion, Jo a book, Beth a basket, and Amy a portfolio. All walked quietly through the garden, out at the little back gate, and began to climb the hill that lay between the house and river.\n\n\"Well, that's cool!\" said Laurie to himself, \"to have a picnic and never ask me. They can't be going in the boat, for they haven't got the key. Perhaps they forgot it; I'll take it to them, and see what's going on.\"\n\nThough possessed of half a dozen hats, it took him some time to find one; then there was a hunt for the key, which was at last discovered in his pocket; so that the girls were quite out of sight when he leaped the fence and ran after them. Taking the shortest way to the boat-house, he waited for them to appear: but no one came, and he went up the hill to take an observation. A grove of pines covered one part of it, and from the heart of this green spot came a clearer sound than the soft sigh of the pines or the drowsy chirp of the crickets.\n\n\"Here's a landscape!\" thought Laurie, peeping through the bushes, and looking wide-awake and good-natured already.\n\nIt _was_ rather a pretty little picture; for the sisters sat together in the shady nook, with sun and shadow flickering over them, the aromatic wind lifting their hair and cooling their hot cheeks, and all the little wood-people going on with their affairs as if these were no strangers, but old friends. Meg sat upon her cushion, sewing daintily with her white hands, and looking as fresh and sweet as a rose, in her pink dress, among the green. Beth was sorting the cones that lay thick under the hemlock near by, for she made pretty things of them. Amy was sketching a group of ferns, and Jo was knitting as she read aloud. A shadow passed over the boy's face as he watched them, feeling that he ought to go away, because uninvited; yet lingering, because home seemed very lonely, and this quiet party in the woods most attractive to his restless spirit. He stood so still that a squirrel, busy with its harvesting, ran down a pine close beside him, saw him suddenly and skipped back, scolding so shrilly that Beth looked up, espied the wistful face behind the birches, and beckoned with a reassuring smile.\n\n\"May I come in, please? or shall I be a bother?\" he asked, advancing slowly.\n\nMeg lifted her eyebrows, but Jo scowled at her defiantly, and said, at once, \"Of course you may. We should have asked you before, only we thought you wouldn't care for such a girl's game as this.\"\n\n\"I always liked your games; but if Meg doesn't want me, I'll go away.\"\n\n\"I've no objection, if you do something; it's against the rules to be idle here,\" replied Meg, gravely but graciously.\n\n\"Much obliged; I'll do anything if you'll let me stop a bit, for it's as dull as the Desert of Sahara down there. Shall I sew, read, cone, draw, or do all at once? Bring on your bears; I'm ready,\" and Laurie sat down, with a submissive expression delightful to behold.\n\n\"Finish this story while I set my heel,\" said Jo, handing him the book.\n\n\"Yes'm,\" was the meek answer, as he began, doing his best to prove his gratitude for the favor of an admission into the \"Busy Bee Society.\"\nThe story was not a long one, and, when it was finished, he ventured to ask a few questions as a reward of merit.\n\n\"Please, ma'am, could I inquire if this highly instructive and charming institution is a new one?\"\n\n\"Would you tell him?\" asked Meg of her sisters.\n\n\"He'll laugh,\" said Amy warningly.\n\n\"Who cares?\" said Jo.\n\n\"I guess he'll like it,\" added Beth.\n\n\"Of course I shall! I give you my word I won't laugh. Tell away, Jo, and don't be afraid.\"\n\n\"The idea of being afraid of you! Well, you see we used to play 'Pilgrim's Progress,' and we have been going on with it in earnest, all winter and summer.\"\n\n\"Yes, I know,\" said Laurie, nodding wisely.\n\n\"Who told you?\" demanded Jo.\n\n\"Spirits.\"\n\n\"No, I did; I wanted to amuse him one night when you were all away, and he was rather dismal. He did like it, so don't scold, Jo,\" said Beth meekly.\n\n\"You can't keep a secret. Never mind; it saves trouble now.\"\n\n\"Go on, please,\" said Laurie, as Jo became absorbed in her work, looking a trifle displeased.\n\n\"Oh, didn't she tell you about this new plan of ours? Well, we have tried not to waste our holiday, but each has had a task, and worked at it with a will. The vacation is nearly over, the stints are all done, and we are ever so glad that we didn't dawdle.\"\n\n\"Yes, I should think so;\" and Laurie thought regretfully of his own idle days.\n\n\"Mother likes to have us out of doors as much as possible; so we bring our work here, and have nice times. For the fun of it we bring our things in these bags, wear the old hats, use poles to climb the hill, and play pilgrims, as we used to do years ago. We call this hill the 'Delectable Mountain,' for we can look far away and see the country where we hope to live some time.\"\n\nJo pointed, and Laurie sat up to examine; for through an opening in the wood one could look across the wide, blue river, the meadows on the other side, far over the outskirts of the great city, to the green hills that rose to meet the sky. The sun was low, and the heavens plowed with the splendor of an autumn sunset. Gold and purple clouds lay on the hill-tops; and rising high into the ruddy light were silvery white peaks, that shone like the airy spires of some Celestial City.\n\n\"How beautiful that is!\" said Laurie softly, for he was quick to see and feel beauty of any kind.\n\n\"It's often so; and we like to watch it, for it is never the same, but always splendid,\" replied Amy, wishing she could paint it.\n\n\"Jo talks about the country where we hope to live some time,--the real country, she means, with pigs and chickens, and haymaking. It would be nice, but I wish the beautiful country up there was real, and we could ever go to it,\" said Beth musingly.\n\n\"There is a lovelier country even than that, where we _shall_ go, by and by, when we are good enough,\" answered Meg, with her sweet voice.\n\n\"It seems so long to wait, so hard to do; I want to fly away at once, as those swallows fly, and go in at that splendid gate.\"\n\n\"You'll get there, Beth, sooner or later; no fear of that,\" said Jo; \"I'm the one that will have to fight and work, and climb and wait, and maybe never get in after all.\"\n\n\"You'll have me for company, if that's any comfort. I shall have to do a deal of travelling before I come in sight of your Celestial City. If I arrive late, you'll say a good word for me, won't you, Beth?\"\n\nSomething in the boy's face troubled his little friend; but she said cheerfully, with her quiet eyes on the changing clouds, \"If people really want to go, and really try all their lives, I think they will get in; for I don't believe there are any locks on that door, or any guards at the gate. I always imagine it is as it is in the picture, where the shining ones stretch out their hands to welcome poor Christian as he comes up from the river.\"\n\n\"Wouldn't it be fun if all the castles in the air which we make could come true, and we could live in them?\" said Jo, after a little pause.\n\n\"I've made such quantities it would be hard to choose which I'd have,\" said Laurie, lying flat, and throwing cones at the squirrel who had betrayed him.\n\n\"You'd have to take your favorite one. What is it?\" asked Meg.\n\n\"If I tell mine, will you tell yours?\"\n\n\"Yes, if the girls will too.\"\n\n\"We will. Now, Laurie.\"\n\n\"After I'd seen as much of the world as I want to, I'd like to settle in Germany, and have just as much music as I choose. I'm to be a famous musician myself, and all creation is to rush to hear me; and I'm never to be bothered about money or business, but just enjoy myself, and live for what I like. That's my favorite castle. What's yours, Meg?\"\n\nMargaret seemed to find it a little hard to tell hers, and waved a brake before her face, as if to disperse imaginary gnats, while she said slowly, \"I should like a lovely house, full of all sorts of luxurious things,--nice food, pretty clothes, handsome furniture, pleasant people, and heaps of money. I am to be mistress of it, and manage it as I like, with plenty of servants, so I never need work a bit. How I should enjoy it! for I wouldn't be idle, but do good, and make every one love me dearly.\"\n\n\"Wouldn't you have a master for your castle in the air?\" asked Laurie slyly.\n\n\"I said 'pleasant people,' you know;\" and Meg carefully tied up her shoe as she spoke, so that no one saw her face.\n\n\"Why don't you say you'd have a splendid, wise, good husband, and some angelic little children? You know your castle wouldn't be perfect without,\" said blunt Jo, who had no tender fancies yet, and rather scorned romance, except in books.\n\n\"You'd have nothing but horses, inkstands, and novels in yours,\" answered Meg petulantly.\n\n\"Wouldn't I, though? I'd have a stable full of Arabian steeds, rooms piled with books, and I'd write out of a magic inkstand, so that my works should be as famous as Laurie's music. I want to do something splendid before I go into my castle,--something heroic or wonderful, that won't be forgotten after I'm dead. I don't know what, but I'm on the watch for it, and mean to astonish you all, some day. I think I shall write books, and get rich and famous: that would suit me, so that is _my_ favorite dream.\"\n\n\"Mine is to stay at home safe with father and mother, and help take care of the family,\" said Beth contentedly.\n\n\"Don't you wish for anything else?\" asked Laurie.\n\n\"Since I had my little piano, I am perfectly satisfied. I only wish we may all keep well and be together; nothing else.\"\n\n\"I have ever so many wishes; but the pet one is to be an artist, and go to Rome, and do fine pictures, and be the best artist in the whole world,\" was Amy's modest desire.\n\n\"We're an ambitious set, aren't we? Every one of us, but Beth, wants to be rich and famous, and gorgeous in every respect. I do wonder if any of us will ever get our wishes,\" said Laurie, chewing grass, like a meditative calf.\n\n\"I've got the key to my castle in the air; but whether I can unlock the door remains to be seen,\" observed Jo mysteriously.\n\n\"I've got the key to mine, but I'm not allowed to try it. Hang college!\" muttered Laurie, with an impatient sigh.\n\n\"Here's mine!\" and Amy waved her pencil.\n\n\"I haven't got any,\" said Meg forlornly.\n\n\"Yes, you have,\" said Laurie at once.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"In your face.\"\n\n\"Nonsense; that's of no use.\"\n\n\"Wait and see if it doesn't bring you something worth having,\" replied the boy, laughing at the thought of a charming little secret which he fancied he knew.\n\nMeg colored behind the brake, but asked no questions, and looked across the river with the same expectant expression which Mr. Brooke had worn when he told the story of the knight.\n\n\"If we are all alive ten years hence, let's meet, and see how many of us have got our wishes, or how much nearer we are then than now,\" said Jo, always ready with a plan.\n\n\"Bless me! how old I shall be,--twenty-seven!\" exclaimed Meg who felt grown up already, having just reached seventeen.\n\n\"You and I shall be twenty-six, Teddy, Beth twenty-four, and Amy twenty-two. What a venerable party!\" said Jo.\n\n\"I hope I shall have done something to be proud of by that time; but I'm such a lazy dog, I'm afraid I shall 'dawdle,' Jo.\"\n\n\"You need a motive, mother says; and when you get it, she is sure you'll work splendidly.\"\n\n\"Is she? By Jupiter I will, if I only get the chance!\" cried Laurie, sitting up with sudden energy. \"I ought to be satisfied to please grandfather, and I do try, but it's working against the grain, you see, and comes hard. He wants me to be an India merchant, as he was, and I'd rather be shot. I hate tea and silk and spices, and every sort of rubbish his old ships bring, and I don't care how soon they go to the bottom when I own them. Going to college ought to satisfy him, for if I give him four years he ought to let me off from the business; but he's set, and I 've got to do just as he did, unless I break away and please myself, as my father did. If there was any one left to stay with the old gentleman, I'd do it to-morrow.\"\n\nLaurie spoke excitedly, and looked ready to carry his threat into execution on the slightest provocation; for he was growing up very fast, and, in spite of his indolent ways, had a young man's hatred of subjection, a young man's restless longing to try the world for himself.\n\n\"I advise you to sail away in one of your ships, and never come home again till you have tried your own way,\" said Jo, whose imagination was fired by the thought of such a daring exploit, and whose sympathy was excited by what she called \"Teddy's wrongs.\"\n\n\"That's not right, Jo; you mustn't talk in that way, and Laurie mustn't take your bad advice. You should do just what your grandfather wishes, my dear boy,\" said Meg, in her most maternal tone. \"Do your best at college, and, when he sees that you try to please him, I'm sure he won't be hard or unjust to you. As you say, there is no one else to stay with and love him, and you'd never forgive yourself if you left him without his permission. Don't be dismal or fret, but do your duty; and you'll get your reward, as good Mr. Brooke has, by being respected and loved.\"\n\n\"What do you know about him?\" asked Laurie, grateful for the good advice, but objecting to the lecture, and glad to turn the conversation from himself, after his unusual outbreak.\n\n\"Only what your grandpa told us about him,--how he took good care of his own mother till she died, and wouldn't go abroad as tutor to some nice person, because he wouldn't leave her; and how he provides now for an old woman who nursed his mother; and never tells any one, but is just as generous and patient and good as he can be.\"\n\n\"So he is, dear old fellow!\" said Laurie heartily, as Meg paused, looking flushed and earnest with her story. \"It's like grandpa to find out all about him, without letting him know, and to tell all his goodness to others, so that they might like him. Brooke couldn't understand why your mother was so kind to him, asking him over with me, and treating him in her beautiful friendly way. He thought she was just perfect, and talked about it for days and days, and went on about you all in flaming style. If ever I do get my wish, you see what I'll do for Brooke.\"\n\n\"Begin to do something now, by not plaguing his life out,\" said Meg sharply.\n\n\"How do you know I do, miss?\"\n\n\"I can always tell by his face, when he goes away. If you have been good, he looks satisfied and walks briskly; if you have plagued him, he's sober and walks slowly, as if he wanted to go back and do his work better.\"\n\n\"Well, I like that! So you keep an account of my good and bad marks in Brooke's face, do you? I see him bow and smile as he passes your window, but I didn't know you'd got up a telegraph.\"\n\n\"We haven't; don't be angry, and oh, don't tell him I said anything! It was only to show that I cared how you get on, and what is said here is said in confidence, you know,\" cried Meg, much alarmed at the thought of what might follow from her careless speech.\n\n\"_I_ don't tell tales,\" replied Laurie, with his \"high and mighty\" air, as Jo called a certain expression which he occasionally wore. \"Only if Brooke is going to be a thermometer, I must mind and have fair weather for him to report.\"\n\n\"Please don't be offended. I didn't mean to preach or tell tales or be silly; I only thought Jo was encouraging you in a feeling which you'd be sorry for, by and by. You are so kind to us, we feel as if you were our brother, and say just what we think. Forgive me, I meant it kindly.\" And Meg offered her hand with a gesture both affectionate and timid.\n\nAshamed of his momentary pique, Laurie squeezed the kind little hand, and said frankly, \"I'm the one to be forgiven; I'm cross, and have been out of sorts all day. I like to have you tell me my faults and be sisterly, so don't mind if I am grumpy sometimes; I thank you all the same.\"\n\nBent on showing that he was not offended, he made himself as agreeable as possible,--wound cotton for Meg, recited poetry to please Jo, shook down cones for Beth, and helped Amy with her ferns, proving himself a fit person to belong to the \"Busy Bee Society.\" In the midst of an animated discussion on the domestic habits of turtles (one of those amiable creatures having strolled up from the river), the faint sound of a bell warned them that Hannah had put the tea \"to draw,\" and they would just have time to get home to supper.\n\n\"May I come again?\" asked Laurie.\n\n\"Yes, if you are good, and love your book, as the boys in the primer are told to do,\" said Meg smiling.\n\n\"I'll try.\"\n\n\"Then you may come, and I'll teach you to knit as the Scotchmen do; there's a demand for socks just now,\" added Jo, waving hers, like a big blue worsted banner, as they parted at the gate.\n\nThat night, when Beth played to Mr. Laurence in the twilight, Laurie, standing in the shadow of the curtain, listened to the little David, whose simple music always quieted his moody spirit, and watched the old man, who sat with his gray head on his hand, thinking tender thoughts of the dead child he had loved so much. Remembering the conversation of the afternoon, the boy said to himself, with the resolve to make the sacrifice cheerfully, \"I'll let my castle go, and stay with the dear old gentleman while he needs me, for I am all he has.\"\n\nJo was very busy in the garret, for the October days began to grow chilly, and the afternoons were short. For two or three hours the sun lay warmly in the high window, showing Jo seated on the old sofa, writing busily, with her papers spread out upon a trunk before her, while Scrabble, the pet rat, promenaded the beams overhead, accompanied by his oldest son, a fine young fellow, who was evidently very proud of his whiskers. Quite absorbed in her work, Jo scribbled away till the last page was filled, when she signed her name with a flourish, and threw down her pen, exclaiming,--\n\n\"There, I've done my best! If this won't suit I shall have to wait till I can do better.\"\n\nLying back on the sofa, she read the manuscript carefully through, making dashes here and there, and putting in many exclamation points, which looked like little balloons; then she tied it up with a smart red ribbon, and sat a minute looking at it with a sober, wistful expression, which plainly showed how earnest her work had been. Jo's desk up here was an old tin kitchen, which hung against the wall. In it she kept her papers and a few books, safely shut away from Scrabble, who, being likewise of a literary turn, was fond of making a circulating library of such books as were left in his way, by eating the leaves. From this tin receptacle Jo produced another manuscript; and, putting both in her pocket, crept quietly down stairs, leaving her friends to nibble her pens and taste her ink.\n\nShe put on her hat and jacket as noiselessly as possible, and, going to the back entry window, got out upon the roof of a low porch, swung herself down to the grassy bank, and took a roundabout way to the road. Once there, she composed herself, hailed a passing omnibus, and rolled away to town, looking very merry and mysterious.\n\nIf any one had been watching her, he would have thought her movements decidedly peculiar; for, on alighting, she went off at a great pace till she reached a certain number in a certain busy street; having found the place with some difficulty, she went into the door-way, looked up the dirty stairs, and, after standing stock still a minute, suddenly dived into the street, and walked away as rapidly as she came. This manœuvre she repeated several times, to the great amusement of a black-eyed young gentleman lounging in the window of a building opposite. On returning for the third time, Jo gave herself a shake, pulled her hat over her eyes, and walked up the stairs, looking as if she were going to have all her teeth out.\n\nThere was a dentist's sign, among others, which adorned the entrance, and, after staring a moment at the pair of artificial jaws which slowly opened and shut to draw attention to a fine set of teeth, the young gentleman put on his coat, took his hat, and went down to post himself in the opposite door-way, saying, with a smile and a shiver,--\n\n\"It's like her to come alone, but if she has a bad time she'll need some one to help her home.\"\n\nIn ten minutes Jo came running down stairs with a very red face, and the general appearance of a person who had just passed through a trying ordeal of some sort. When she saw the young gentleman she looked anything but pleased, and passed him with a nod; but he followed, asking with an air of sympathy,--\n\n\"Did you have a bad time?\"\n\n\"Not very.\"\n\n\"You got through quickly.\"\n\n\"Yes, thank goodness!\"\n\n\"Why did you go alone?\"\n\n\"Didn't want any one to know.\"\n\n\"You're the oddest fellow I ever saw. How many did you have out?\"\n\nJo looked at her friend as if she did not understand him; then began to laugh, as if mightily amused at something.\n\n\"There are two which I want to have come out, but I must wait a week.\"\n\n\"What are you laughing at? You are up to some mischief, Jo,\" said Laurie, looking mystified.\n\n\"So are you. What were you doing, sir, up in that billiard saloon?\"\n\n\"Begging your pardon, ma'am, it wasn't a billiard saloon, but a gymnasium, and I was taking a lesson in fencing.\"\n\n\"I'm glad of that.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"You can teach me, and then when we play Hamlet, you can be Laertes, and we'll make a fine thing of the fencing scene.\"\n\nLaurie burst out with a hearty boy's laugh, which made several passers-by smile in spite of themselves.\n\n\"I'll teach you whether we play Hamlet or not; it's grand fun, and will straighten you up capitally. But I don't believe that was your only reason for saying 'I'm glad,' in that decided way; was it, now?\"\n\n\"No, I was glad that you were not in the saloon, because I hope you never go to such places. Do you?\"\n\n\"Not often.\"\n\n\"I wish you wouldn't.\"\n\n\"It's no harm, Jo. I have billiards at home, but it's no fun unless you have good players; so, as I'm fond of it, I come sometimes and have a game with Ned Moffat or some of the other fellows.\"\n\n\"Oh dear, I'm so sorry, for you'll get to liking it better and better, and will waste time and money, and grow like those dreadful boys. I did hope you'd stay respectable, and be a satisfaction to your friends,\" said Jo, shaking her head.\n\n\"Can't a fellow take a little innocent amusement now and then without losing his respectability?\" asked Laurie, looking nettled.\n\n\"That depends upon how and where he takes it. I don't like Ned and his set, and wish you'd keep out of it. Mother won't let us have him at our house, though he wants to come; and if you grow like him she won't be willing to have us frolic together as we do now.\"\n\n\"Won't she?\" asked Laurie anxiously.\n\n\"No, she can't bear fashionable young men, and she'd shut us all up in bandboxes rather than have us associate with them.\"\n\n\"Well, she needn't get out her bandboxes yet; I'm not a fashionable party, and don't mean to be; but I do like harmless larks now and then, don't you?\"\n\n\"Yes, nobody minds them, so lark away, but don't get wild, will you? or there will be an end of all our good times.\"\n\n\"I'll be a double-distilled saint.\"\n\n\"I can't bear saints: just be a simple, honest, respectable boy, and we'll never desert you. I don't know what I _should_ do if you acted like Mr. King's son; he had plenty of money, but didn't know how to spend it, and got tipsy and gambled, and ran away, and forged his father's name, I believe, and was altogether horrid.\"\n\n\"You think I'm likely to do the same? Much obliged.\"\n\n\"No, I don't--oh, _dear_, no!--but I hear people talking about money being such a temptation, and I sometimes wish you were poor; I shouldn't worry then.\"\n\n\"Do you worry about me, Jo?\"\n\n\"A little, when you look moody or discontented, as you sometimes do; for you've got such a strong will, if you once get started wrong, I'm afraid it would be hard to stop you.\"\n\nLaurie walked in silence a few minutes, and Jo watched him, wishing she had held her tongue, for his eyes looked angry, though his lips still smiled as if at her warnings.\n\n\"Are you going to deliver lectures all the way home?\" he asked presently.\n\n\"Of course not; why?\"\n\n\"Because if you are, I'll take a 'bus; if you are not, I'd like to walk with you, and tell you something very interesting.\"\n\n\"I won't preach any more, and I'd like to hear the news immensely.\"\n\n\"Very well, then; come on. It's a secret, and if I tell you, you must tell me yours.\"\n\n\"I haven't got any,\" began Jo, but stopped suddenly, remembering that she had.\n\n\"You know you have,--you can't hide anything; so up and 'fess, or I won't tell,\" cried Laurie.\n\n\"Is your secret a nice one?\"\n\n\"Oh, isn't it! all about people you know, and such fun! You ought to hear it, and I've been aching to tell it this long time. Come, you begin.\"\n\n\"You'll not say anything about it at home, will you?\"\n\n\"Not a word.\"\n\"And you won't tease me in private?\"\n\n\"I never tease.\"\n\n\"Yes, you do; you get everything you want out of people. I don't know how you do it, but you are a born wheedler.\"\n\n\"Thank you; fire away.\"\n\n\"Well, I've left two stories with a newspaper man, and he's to give his answer next week,\" whispered Jo, in her confidant's ear.\n\n\"Hurrah for Miss March, the celebrated American authoress!\" cried Laurie, throwing up his hat and catching it again, to the great delight of two ducks, four cats, five hens, and half a dozen Irish children; for they were out of the city now.\n\n\"Hush! It won't come to anything, I dare say; but I couldn't rest till I had tried, and I said nothing about it, because I didn't want any one else to be disappointed.\"\n\n\"It won't fail. Why, Jo, your stories are works of Shakespeare, compared to half the rubbish that is published every day. Won't it be fun to see them in print; and sha'n't we feel proud of our authoress?\"\n\nJo's eyes sparkled, for it is always pleasant to be believed in; and a friend's praise is always sweeter than a dozen newspaper puffs.\n\n\"Where's _your_ secret? Play fair, Teddy, or I'll never believe you again,\" she said, trying to extinguish the brilliant hopes that blazed up at a word of encouragement.\n\n\"I may get into a scrape for telling; but I didn't promise not to, so I will, for I never feel easy in my mind till I've told you any plummy bit of news I get. I know where Meg's glove is.\"\n\n\"Is that all?\" said Jo, looking disappointed, as Laurie nodded and twinkled, with a face full of mysterious intelligence.\n\n\"It's quite enough for the present, as you'll agree when I tell you where it is.\"\n\n\"Tell, then.\"\n\nLaurie bent, and whispered three words in Jo's ear, which produced a comical change. She stood and stared at him for a minute, looking both surprised and displeased, then walked on, saying sharply, \"How do you know?\"\n\n\"Saw it.\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"Pocket.\"\n\n\"All this time?\"\n\n\"Yes; isn't that romantic?\"\n\n\"No, it's horrid.\"\n\n\"Don't you like it?\"\n\n\"Of course I don't. It's ridiculous; it won't be allowed. My patience! what would Meg say?\"\n\n\"You are not to tell any one; mind that.\"\n\n\"I didn't promise.\"\n\n\"That was understood, and I trusted you.\"\n\n\"Well, I won't for the present, any way; but I'm disgusted, and wish you hadn't told me.\"\n\n\"I thought you'd be pleased.\"\n\n\"At the idea of anybody coming to take Meg away? No, thank you.\"\n\n\"You'll feel better about it when somebody comes to take you away.\"\n\n\"I'd like to see any one try it,\" cried Jo fiercely.\n\n\"So should I!\" and Laurie chuckled at the idea.\n\n\"I don't think secrets agree with me; I feel rumpled up in my mind since you told me that,\" said Jo, rather ungratefully.\n\n\"Race down this hill with me, and you'll be all right,\" suggested Laurie.\n\nNo one was in sight; the smooth road sloped invitingly before her; and finding the temptation irresistible, Jo darted away, soon leaving hat and comb behind her, and scattering hair-pins as she ran. Laurie reached the goal first, and was quite satisfied with the success of his treatment; for his Atalanta came panting up, with flying hair, bright eyes, ruddy cheeks, and no signs of dissatisfaction in her face.\n\n\"I wish I was a horse; then I could run for miles in this splendid air, and not lose my breath. It was capital; but see what a guy it's made me. Go, pick up my things, like a cherub as you are,\" said Jo, dropping down under a maple-tree, which was carpeting the bank with crimson leaves.\n\nLaurie leisurely departed to recover the lost property, and Jo bundled up her braids, hoping no one would pass by till she was tidy again. But some one did pass, and who should it be but Meg, looking particularly ladylike in her state and festival suit, for she had been making calls.\n\n\"What in the world are you doing here?\" she asked, regarding her dishevelled sister with well-bred surprise.\n\n\"Getting leaves,\" meekly answered Jo, sorting the rosy handful she had just swept up.\n\n\"And hair-pins,\" added Laurie, throwing half a dozen into Jo's lap. \"They grow on this road, Meg; so do combs and brown straw hats.\"\n\n\"You have been running, Jo; how could you? When _will_ you stop such romping ways?\" said Meg reprovingly, as she settled her cuffs, and smoothed her hair, with which the wind had taken liberties.\n\n\"Never till I'm stiff and old, and have to use a crutch. Don't try to make me grow up before my time, Meg: it's hard enough to have you change all of a sudden; let me be a little girl as long as I can.\"\n\nAs she spoke, Jo bent over the leaves to hide the trembling of her lips; for lately she had felt that Margaret was fast getting to be a woman, and Laurie's secret made her dread the separation which must surely come some time, and now seemed very near. He saw the trouble in her face, and drew Meg's attention from it by asking quickly, \"Where have you been calling, all so fine?\"\n\n\"At the Gardiners', and Sallie has been telling me all about Belle Moffat's wedding. It was very splendid, and they have gone to spend the winter in Paris. Just think how delightful that must be!\"\n\n\"Do you envy her, Meg?\" said Laurie.\n\n\"I'm afraid I do.\"\n\n\"I'm glad of it!\" muttered Jo, tying on her hat with a jerk.\n\n\"Why?\" asked Meg, looking surprised.\n\n\"Because if you care much about riches, you will never go and marry a poor man,\" said Jo, frowning at Laurie, who was mutely warning her to mind what she said.\n\n\"I shall never '_go_ and marry' any one,\" observed Meg, walking on with great dignity, while the others followed, laughing, whispering, skipping stones, and \"behaving like children,\" as Meg said to herself, though she might have been tempted to join them if she had not had her best dress on.\n\nFor a week or two, Jo behaved so queerly that her sisters were quite bewildered. She rushed to the door when the postman rang; was rude to Mr. Brooke whenever they met; would sit looking at Meg with a woe-begone face, occasionally jumping up to shake, and then to kiss her, in a very mysterious manner; Laurie and she were always making signs to one another, and talking about \"Spread Eagles,\" till the girls declared they had both lost their wits. On the second Saturday after Jo got out of the window, Meg, as she sat sewing at her window, was scandalized by the sight of Laurie chasing Jo all over the garden, and finally capturing her in Amy's bower. What went on there, Meg could not see; but shrieks of laughter were heard, followed by the murmur of voices and a great flapping of newspapers.\n\n\"What shall we do with that girl? She never _will_ behave like a young lady,\" sighed Meg, as she watched the race with a disapproving face.\n\n\"I hope she won't; she is so funny and dear as she is,\" said Beth, who had never betrayed that she was a little hurt at Jo's having secrets with any one but her.\n\n\"It's very trying, but we never can make her _commy la fo_,\" added Amy, who sat making some new frills for herself, with her curls tied up in a very becoming way,--two agreeable things, which made her feel unusually elegant and ladylike.\n\nIn a few minutes Jo bounced in, laid herself on the sofa, and affected to read.\n\n\"Have you anything interesting there?\" asked Meg, with condescension.\n\n\"Nothing but a story; won't amount to much, I guess,\" returned Jo, carefully keeping the name of the paper out of sight.\n\n\"You'd better read it aloud; that will amuse us and keep you out of mischief,\" said Amy, in her most grown-up tone.\n\n\"What's the name?\" asked Beth, wondering why Jo kept her face behind the sheet.\n\n\"The Rival Painters.\"\n\n\"That sounds well; read it,\" said Meg.\n\nWith a loud \"Hem!\" and a long breath, Jo began to read very fast. The girls listened with interest, for the tale was romantic, and somewhat pathetic, as most of the characters died in the end.\n\n\"I like that about the splendid picture,\" was Amy's approving remark, as Jo paused.\n\n\"I prefer the lovering part. Viola and Angelo are two of our favorite names; isn't that queer?\" said Meg, wiping her eyes, for the \"lovering part\" was tragical.\n\n\"Who wrote it?\" asked Beth, who had caught a glimpse of Jo's face.\n\nThe reader suddenly sat up, cast away the paper, displaying a flushed countenance, and, with a funny mixture of solemnity and excitement, replied in a loud voice, \"Your sister.\"\n\n\"You?\" cried Meg, dropping her work.\n\n\"It's very good,\" said Amy critically.\n\n\"I knew it! I knew it! O my Jo, I _am_ so proud!\" and Beth ran to hug her sister, and exult over this splendid success.\n\nDear me, how delighted they all were, to be sure! how Meg wouldn't believe it till she saw the words, \"Miss Josephine March,\" actually printed in the paper; how graciously Amy criticised the artistic parts of the story, and offered hints for a sequel, which unfortunately couldn't be carried out, as the hero and heroine were dead; how Beth got excited, and skipped and sung with joy; how Hannah came in to exclaim \"Sakes alive, well I never!\" in great astonishment at \"that Jo's doin's;\" how proud Mrs. March was when she knew it; how Jo laughed, with tears in her eyes, as she declared she might as well be a peacock and done with it; and how the \"Spread Eagle\" might be said to flap his wings triumphantly over the House of March, as the paper passed from hand to hand.\n\n\"Tell us all about it.\" \"When did it come?\" \"How much did you get for it?\" \"What _will_ father say?\" \"Won't Laurie laugh?\" cried the family, all in one breath, as they clustered about Jo; for these foolish, affectionate people made a jubilee of every little household joy.\n\n\"Stop jabbering, girls, and I'll tell you everything,\" said Jo, wondering if Miss Burney felt any grander over her \"Evelina\" than she did over her \"Rival Painters.\" Having told how she disposed of her tales, Jo added, \"And when I went to get my answer, the man said he liked them both, but didn't pay beginners, only let them print in his paper, and noticed the stories. It was good practice, he said; and when the beginners improved, any one would pay. So I let him have the two stories, and to-day this was sent to me, and Laurie caught me with it, and insisted on seeing it, so I let him; and he said it was good, and I shall write more, and he's going to get the next paid for, and I _am_ so happy, for in time I may be able to support myself and help the girls.\"\n\nJo's breath gave out here; and, wrapping her head in the paper, she bedewed her little story with a few natural tears; for to be independent, and earn the praise of those she loved were the dearest wishes of her heart, and this seemed to be the first step toward that happy end.\n\n# A TELEGRAM.\n\n\"November is the most disagreeable month in the whole year,\" said Margaret, standing at the window one dull afternoon, looking out at the frost-bitten garden.\n\n\"That's the reason I was born in it,\" observed Jo pensively, quite unconscious of the blot on her nose.\n\n\"If something very pleasant should happen now, we should think it a delightful month,\" said Beth, who took a hopeful view of everything, even November.\n\n\"I dare say; but nothing pleasant ever _does_ happen in this family,\" said Meg, who was out of sorts. \"We go grubbing along day after day, without a bit of change, and very little fun. We might as well be in a treadmill.\"\n\n\"My patience, how blue we are!\" cried Jo. \"I don't much wonder, poor dear, for you see other girls having splendid times, while you grind, grind, year in and year out. Oh, don't I wish I could manage things for you as I do for my heroines! You're pretty enough and good enough already, so I'd have some rich relation leave you a fortune unexpectedly; then you'd dash out as an heiress, scorn every one who has slighted you, go abroad, and come home my Lady Something, in a blaze of splendor and elegance.\"\n\n\"People don't have fortunes left them in that style now-a-days; men have to work, and women to marry for money. It's a dreadfully unjust world,\" said Meg bitterly.\n\n\"Jo and I are going to make fortunes for you all; just wait ten years, and see if we don't,\" said Amy, who sat in a corner, making mud pies, as Hannah called her little clay models of birds, fruit, and faces.\n\n\"Can't wait, and I'm afraid I haven't much faith in ink and dirt, though I'm grateful for your good intentions.\"\n\nMeg sighed, and turned to the frost-bitten garden again; Jo groaned, and leaned both elbows on the table in a despondent attitude, but Amy spatted away energetically; and Beth, who sat at the other window, said, smiling, \"Two pleasant things are going to happen right away: Marmee is coming down the street, and Laurie is tramping through the garden as if he had something nice to tell.\"\n\nIn they both came, Mrs. March with her usual question, \"Any letter from father, girls?\" and Laurie to say in his persuasive way, \"Won't some of you come for a drive? I've been working away at mathematics till my head is in a muddle, and I'm going to freshen my wits by a brisk turn. It's a dull day, but the air isn't bad, and I'm going to take Brooke home, so it will be gay inside, if it isn't out. Come, Jo, you and Beth will go, won't you?\"\n\n\"Of course we will.\"\n\n\"Much obliged, but I'm busy;\" and Meg whisked out her work-basket, for she had agreed with her mother that it was best, for her at least, not to drive often with the young gentleman.\n\n\"We three will be ready in a minute,\" cried Amy, running away to wash her hands.\n\n\"Can I do anything for you, Madam Mother?\" asked Laurie, leaning over Mrs. March's chair, with the affectionate look and tone he always gave her.\n\n\"No, thank you, except call at the office, if you'll be so kind, dear. It's our day for a letter, and the postman hasn't been. Father is as regular as the sun, but there's some delay on the way, perhaps.\"\n\nA sharp ring interrupted her, and a minute after Hannah came in with a letter.\n\n\"It's one of them horrid telegraph things, mum,\" she said, handing it as if she was afraid it would explode and do some damage.\n\nAt the word \"telegraph,\" Mrs. March snatched it, read the two lines it contained, and dropped back into her chair as white as if the little paper had sent a bullet to her heart. Laurie dashed down stairs for water, while Meg and Hannah supported her, and Jo read aloud, in a frightened voice,--\n\n\"Your husband is very ill. Come at once. \"S. HALE, \"Blank Hospital, Washington\"\n\nHow still the room was as they listened breathlessly, how strangely the day darkened outside, and how suddenly the whole world seemed to change, as the girls gathered about their mother, feeling as if all the happiness and support of their lives was about to be taken from them. Mrs. March was herself again directly; read the message over, and stretched out her arms to her daughters, saying, in a tone they never forgot, \"I shall go at once, but it may be too late. O children, children, help me to bear it!\"\n\nFor several minutes there was nothing but the sound of sobbing in the room, mingled with broken words of comfort, tender assurances of help, and hopeful whispers that died away in tears. Poor Hannah was the first to recover, and with unconscious wisdom she set all the rest a good example; for, with her, work was the panacea for most afflictions.\n\n\"The Lord keep the dear man! I won't waste no time a cryin', but git your things ready right away, mum,\" she said, heartily, as she wiped her face on her apron, gave her mistress a warm shake of the hand with her own hard one, and went away, to work like three women in one.\n\n\"She's right; there's no time for tears now. Be calm, girls, and let me think.\"\n\nThey tried to be calm, poor things, as their mother sat up, looking pale, but steady, and put away her grief to think and plan for them.\n\n\"Where's Laurie?\" she asked presently, when she had collected her thoughts, and decided on the first duties to be done.\n\n\"Here, ma'am. Oh, let me do something!\" cried the boy, hurrying from the next room, whither he had withdrawn, feeling that their first sorrow was too sacred for even his friendly eyes to see.\n\n\"Send a telegram saying I will come at once. The next train goes early in the morning. I'll take that.\"\n\n\"What else? The horses are ready; I can go anywhere, do anything,\" he said, looking ready to fly to the ends of the earth.\n\n\"Leave a note at Aunt March's. Jo, give me that pen and paper.\"\n\nTearing off the blank side of one of her newly copied pages, Jo drew the table before her mother, well knowing that money for the long, sad journey must be borrowed, and feeling as if she could do anything to add a little to the sum for her father.\n\n\"Now go, dear; but don't kill yourself driving at a desperate pace; there is no need of that.\"\n\nMrs. March's warning was evidently thrown away; for five minutes later Laurie tore by the window on his own fleet horse, riding as if for his life.\n\n\"Jo, run to the rooms, and tell Mrs. King that I can't come. On the way get these things. I'll put them down; they'll be needed, and I must go prepared for nursing. Hospital stores are not always good. Beth, go and ask Mr. Laurence for a couple of bottles of old wine: I'm not too proud to beg for father; he shall have the best of everything. Amy, tell Hannah to get down the black trunk; and, Meg, come and help me find my things, for I'm half bewildered.\"\n\nWriting, thinking, and directing, all at once, might well bewilder the poor lady, and Meg begged her to sit quietly in her room for a little while, and let them work. Every one scattered like leaves before a gust of wind; and the quiet, happy household was broken up as suddenly as if the paper had been an evil spell.\n\nMr. Laurence came hurrying back with Beth, bringing every comfort the kind old gentleman could think of for the invalid, and friendliest promises of protection for the girls during the mother's absence, which comforted her very much. There was nothing he didn't offer, from his own dressing-gown to himself as escort. But that last was impossible. Mrs. March would not hear of the old gentleman's undertaking the long journey; yet an expression of relief was visible when he spoke of it, for anxiety ill fits one for travelling. He saw the look, knit his heavy eyebrows, rubbed his hands, and marched abruptly away, saying he'd be back directly. No one had time to think of him again till, as Meg ran through the entry, with a pair of rubbers in one hand and a cup of tea in the other, she came suddenly upon Mr. Brooke.\n\n\"I'm very sorry to hear of this, Miss March,\" he said, in the kind, quiet tone which sounded very pleasantly to her perturbed spirit. \"I came to offer myself as escort to your mother. Mr. Laurence has commissions for me in Washington, and it will give me real satisfaction to be of service to her there.\"\n\nDown dropped the rubbers, and the tea was very near following, as Meg put out her hand, with a face so full of gratitude, that Mr. Brooke would have felt repaid for a much greater sacrifice than the trifling one of time and comfort which he was about to make.\n\n\"How kind you all are! Mother will accept, I'm sure; and it will be such a relief to know that she has some one to take care of her. Thank you very, very much!\"\n\nMeg spoke earnestly, and forgot herself entirely till something in the brown eyes looking down at her made her remember the cooling tea, and lead the way into the parlor, saying she would call her mother.\n\nEverything was arranged by the time Laurie returned with a note from Aunt March, enclosing the desired sum, and a few lines repeating what she had often said before,--that she had always told them it was absurd for March to go into the army, always predicted that no good would come of it, and she hoped they would take her advice next time. Mrs. March put the note in the fire, the money in her purse, and went on with her preparations, with her lips folded tightly, in a way which Jo would have understood if she had been there.\n\nThe short afternoon wore away; all the other errands were done, and Meg and her mother busy at some necessary needle-work, while Beth and Amy got tea, and Hannah finished her ironing with what she called a \"slap and a bang,\" but still Jo did not come. They began to get anxious; and Laurie went off to find her, for no one ever knew what freak Jo might take into her head. He missed her, however, and she came walking in with a very queer expression of countenance, for there was a mixture of fun and fear, satisfaction and regret, in it, which puzzled the family as much as did the roll of bills she laid before her mother, saying, with a little choke in her voice, \"That's my contribution towards making father comfortable and bringing him home!\"\n\n\"My dear, where did you get it? Twenty-five dollars! Jo, I hope you haven't done anything rash?\n\n\"No, it's mine honestly; I didn't beg, borrow, or steal it. I earned it; and I don't think you'll blame me, for I only sold what was my own.\"\n\nAs she spoke, Jo took off her bonnet, and a general outcry arose, for all her abundant hair was cut short.\n\n\"Your hair! Your beautiful hair!\" \"O Jo, how could you? Your one beauty.\" \"My dear girl, there was no need of this.\" \"She doesn't look like my Jo any more, but I love her dearly for it!\"\n\nAs every one exclaimed, and Beth hugged the cropped head tenderly, Jo assumed an indifferent air, which did not deceive any one a particle, and said, rumpling up the brown bush, and trying to look as if she liked it, \"It doesn't affect the fate of the nation, so don't wail, Beth. It will be good for my vanity; I was getting too proud of my wig. It will do my brains good to have that mop taken off; my head feels deliciously light and cool, and the barber said I could soon have a curly crop, which will be boyish, becoming, and easy to keep in order. I'm satisfied; so please take the money, and let's have supper.\"\n\n\"Tell me all about it, Jo. _I_ am not quite satisfied, but I can't blame you, for I know how willingly you sacrificed your vanity, as you call it, to your love. But, my dear, it was not necessary, and I'm afraid you will regret it, one of these days,\" said Mrs. March.\n\n\"No, I won't!\" returned Jo stoutly, feeling much relieved that her prank was not entirely condemned.\n\n\"What made you do it?\" asked Amy, who would as soon have thought of cutting off her head as her pretty hair.\n\n\"Well, I was wild to do something for father,\" replied Jo, as they gathered about the table, for healthy young people can eat even in the midst of trouble. \"I hate to borrow as much as mother does, and I knew Aunt March would croak; she always does, if you ask for a ninepence. Meg gave all her quarterly salary toward the rent, and I only got some clothes with mine, so I felt wicked, and was bound to have some money, if I sold the nose off my face to get it.\"\n\n\"You needn't feel wicked, my child: you had no winter things, and got the simplest with your own hard earnings,\" said Mrs. March, with a look that warmed Jo's heart.\n\n\"I hadn't the least idea of selling my hair at first, but as I went along I kept thinking what I could do, and feeling as if I'd like to dive into some of the rich stores and help myself. In a barber's window I saw tails of hair with the prices marked; and one black tail, not so thick as mine, was forty dollars. It came over me all of a sudden that I had one thing to make money out of, and without stopping to think, I walked in, asked if they bought hair, and what they would give for mine.\"\n\n\"I don't see how you dared to do it,\" said Beth, in a tone of awe.\n\n\"Oh, he was a little man who looked as if he merely lived to oil his hair. He rather stared, at first, as if he wasn't used to having girls bounce into his shop and ask him to buy their hair. He said he didn't care about mine, it wasn't the fashionable color, and he never paid much for it in the first place; the work put into it made it dear, and so on. It was getting late, and I was afraid, if it wasn't done right away, that I shouldn't have it done at all, and you know when I start to do a thing, I hate to give it up; so I begged him to take it, and told him why I was in such a hurry. It was silly, I dare say, but it changed his mind, for I got rather excited, and told the story in my topsy-turvy way, and his wife heard, and said so kindly,--\n\n\"'Take it, Thomas, and oblige the young lady; I'd do as much for our Jimmy any day if I had a spire of hair worth selling.'\"\n\n\"Who was Jimmy?\" asked Amy, who liked to have things explained as they went along.\n\n\"Her son, she said, who was in the army. How friendly such things make strangers feel, don't they? She talked away all the time the man clipped, and diverted my mind nicely.\"\n\n\"Didn't you feel dreadfully when the first cut came?\" asked Meg, with a shiver.\n\n\"I took a last look at my hair while the man got his things, and that was the end of it. I never snivel over trifles like that; I will confess, though, I felt queer when I saw the dear old hair laid out on the table, and felt only the short, rough ends on my head. It almost seemed as if I'd an arm or a leg off. The woman saw me look at it, and picked out a long lock for me to keep. I'll give it to you, Marmee, just to remember past glories by; for a crop is so comfortable I don't think I shall ever have a mane again.\"\n\nMrs. March folded the wavy, chestnut lock, and laid it away with a short gray one in her desk. She only said \"Thank you, deary,\" but something in her face made the girls change the subject, and talk as cheerfully as they could about Mr. Brooke's kindness, the prospect of a fine day to-morrow, and the happy times they would have when father came home to be nursed.\n\nNo one wanted to go to bed, when, at ten o'clock, Mrs. March put by the last finished job, and said, \"Come, girls.\" Beth went to the piano and played the father's favorite hymn; all began bravely, but broke down one by one, till Beth was left alone, singing with all her heart, for to her music was always a sweet consoler.\n\n\"Go to bed and don't talk, for we must be up early, and shall need all the sleep we can get. Good-night, my darlings,\" said Mrs. March, as the hymn ended, for no one cared to try another.\n\nThey kissed her quietly, and went to bed as silently as if the dear invalid lay in the next room. Beth and Amy soon fell asleep in spite of the great trouble, but Meg lay awake, thinking the most serious thoughts she had ever known in her short life. Jo lay motionless, and her sister fancied that she was asleep, till a stifled sob made her exclaim, as she touched a wet cheek,--\n\n\"Jo, dear, what is it? Are you crying about father?\"\n\n\"No, not now.\"\n\n\"What then?\"\n\n\"My--my hair!\" burst out poor Jo, trying vainly to smother her emotion in the pillow.\n\nIt did not sound at all comical to Meg, who kissed and caressed the afflicted heroine in the tenderest manner.\n\n\"I'm not sorry,\" protested Jo, with a choke. \"I'd do it again to-morrow, if I could. It's only the vain, selfish part of me that goes and cries in this silly way. Don't tell any one, it's all over now. I thought you were asleep, so I just made a little private moan for my one beauty. How came you to be awake?\"\n\n\"I can't sleep, I'm so anxious,\" said Meg.\n\n\"Think about something pleasant, and you'll soon drop off.\"\n\n\"I tried it, but felt wider awake than ever.\"\n\n\"What did you think of?\"\n\n\"Handsome faces,--eyes particularly,\" answered Meg, smiling to herself, in the dark.\n\n\"What color do you like best?\"\n\n\"Brown--that is, sometimes; blue are lovely.\"\n\nJo laughed, and Meg sharply ordered her not to talk, then amiably promised to make her hair curl, and fell asleep to dream of living in her castle in the air.\n\nThe clocks were striking midnight, and the rooms were very still, as a figure glided quietly from bed to bed, smoothing a coverlid here, settling a pillow there, and pausing to look long and tenderly at each unconscious face, to kiss each with lips that mutely blessed, and to pray the fervent prayers which only mothers utter. As she lifted the curtain to look out into the dreary night, the moon broke suddenly from behind the clouds, and shone upon her like a bright, benignant face, which seemed to whisper in the silence, \"Be comforted, dear soul! There is always light behind the clouds.\"\n\nIn the cold gray dawn the sisters lit their lamp, and read their chapter with an earnestness never felt before; for now the shadow of a real trouble had come, the little books were full of help and comfort; and, as they dressed, they agreed to say good-by cheerfully and hopefully, and send their mother on her anxious journey unsaddened by tears or complaints from them. Everything seemed very strange when they went down,--so dim and still outside, so full of light and bustle within. Breakfast at that early hour seemed odd, and even Hannah's familiar face looked unnatural as she flew about her kitchen with her night-cap on. The big trunk stood ready in the hall, mother's cloak and bonnet lay on the sofa, and mother herself sat trying to eat, but looking so pale and worn with sleeplessness and anxiety that the girls found it very hard to keep their resolution. Meg's eyes kept filling in spite of herself; Jo was obliged to hide her face in the kitchen roller more than once; and the little girls' wore a grave, troubled expression, as if sorrow was a new experience to them.\n\nNobody talked much, but as the time drew very near, and they sat waiting for the carriage, Mrs. March said to the girls, who were all busied about her, one folding her shawl, another smoothing out the strings of her bonnet, a third putting on her overshoes, and a fourth fastening up her travelling bag,--\n\n\"Children, I leave you to Hannah's care and Mr. Laurence's protection. Hannah is faithfulness itself, and our good neighbor will guard you as if you were his own. I have no fears for you, yet I am anxious that you should take this trouble rightly. Don't grieve and fret when I am gone, or think that you can comfort yourselves by being idle and trying to forget. Go on with your work as usual, for work is a blessed solace. Hope and keep busy; and whatever happens, remember that you never can be fatherless.\"\n\n\"Yes, mother.\"\n\n\"Meg, dear, be prudent, watch over your sisters, consult Hannah, and, in any perplexity, go to Mr. Laurence. Be patient, Jo, don't get despondent or do rash things; write to me often, and be my brave girl, ready to help and cheer us all. Beth, comfort yourself with your music, and be faithful to the little home duties; and you, Amy, help all you can, be obedient, and keep happy safe at home.\"\n\n\"We will, mother! we will!\"\n\nThe rattle of an approaching carriage made them all start and listen. That was the hard minute, but the girls stood it well: no one cried, no one ran away or uttered a lamentation, though their hearts were very heavy as they sent loving messages to father, remembering, as they spoke, that it might be too late to deliver them. They kissed their mother quietly, clung about her tenderly, and tried to wave their hands cheerfully when she drove away.\n\nLaurie and his grandfather came over to see her off, and Mr. Brooke looked so strong and sensible and kind that the girls christened him \"Mr. Greatheart\" on the spot.\n\n\"Good-by, my darlings! God bless and keep us all!\" whispered Mrs. March, as she kissed one dear little face after the other, and hurried into the carriage.\n\nAs she rolled away, the sun came out, and, looking back, she saw it shining on the group at the gate, like a good omen. They saw it also, and smiled and waved their hands; and the last thing she beheld, as she turned the corner, was the four bright faces, and behind them, like a body-guard, old Mr. Laurence, faithful Hannah, and devoted Laurie.\n\n\"How kind every one is to us!\" she said, turning to find fresh proof of it in the respectful sympathy of the young man's face.\n\n\"I don't see how they can help it,\" returned Mr. Brooke, laughing so infectiously that Mrs. March could not help smiling; and so the long journey began with the good omens of sunshine, smiles, and cheerful words.\n\n\"I feel as if there had been an earthquake,\" said Jo, as their neighbors went home to breakfast, leaving them to rest and refresh themselves.\n\n\"It seems as if half the house was gone,\" added Meg forlornly.\n\nBeth opened her lips to say something, but could only point to the pile of nicely-mended hose which lay on mother's table, showing that even in her last hurried moments she had thought and worked for them. It was a little thing, but it went straight to their hearts; and, in spite of their brave resolutions, they all broke down, and cried bitterly.\n\nHannah wisely allowed them to relieve their feelings, and, when the shower showed signs of clearing up, she came to the rescue, armed with a coffee-pot.\n\n\"Now, my dear young ladies, remember what your ma said, and don't fret. Come and have a cup of coffee all round, and then let's fall to work, and be a credit to the family.\"\n\nCoffee was a treat, and Hannah showed great tact in making it that morning. No one could resist her persuasive nods, or the fragrant invitation issuing from the nose of the coffee-pot. They drew up to the table, exchanged their handkerchiefs for napkins, and in ten minutes were all right again.\n\n\"'Hope and keep busy;' that's the motto for us, so let's see who will remember it best. I shall go to Aunt March, as usual. Oh, won't she lecture though!\" said Jo, as she sipped with returning spirit.\n\n\"I shall go to my Kings, though I'd much rather stay at home and attend to things here,\" said Meg, wishing she hadn't made her eyes so red.\n\n\"No need of that; Beth and I can keep house perfectly well,\" put in Amy, with an important air.\n\n\"Hannah will tell us what to do; and we'll have everything nice when you come home,\" added Beth, getting out her mop and dish-tub without delay.\n\n\"I think anxiety is very interesting,\" observed Amy, eating sugar, pensively.\n\nThe girls couldn't help laughing, and felt better for it, though Meg shook her head at the young lady who could find consolation in a sugar-bowl.\n\nThe sight of the turn-overs made Jo sober again; and when the two went out to their daily tasks, they looked sorrowfully back at the window where they were accustomed to see their mother's face. It was gone; but Beth had remembered the little household ceremony, and there she was, nodding away at them like a rosy-faced mandarin.\n\n\"That's so like my Beth!\" said Jo, waving her hat, with a grateful face. \"Good-by, Meggy; I hope the Kings won't train to-day. Don't fret about father, dear,\" she added, as they parted.\n\n\"And I hope Aunt March won't croak. Your hair _is_ becoming, and it looks very boyish and nice,\" returned Meg, trying not to smile at the curly head, which looked comically small on her tall sister's shoulders.\n\n\"That's my only comfort;\" and, touching her hat _à la_ Laurie, away went Jo, feeling like a shorn sheep on a wintry day.\n\nNews from their father comforted the girls very much; for, though dangerously ill, the presence of the best and tenderest of nurses had already done him good. Mr. Brooke sent a bulletin every day, and, as the head of the family, Meg insisted on reading the despatches, which grew more and more cheering as the week passed. At first, every one was eager to write, and plump envelopes were carefully poked into the letter-box by one or other of the sisters, who felt rather important with their Washington correspondence. As one of these packets contained characteristic notes from the party, we will rob an imaginary mail, and read them:--\n\n\"It is impossible to tell you how happy your last letter made us, for the news was so good we couldn't help laughing and crying over it. How very kind Mr. Brooke is, and how fortunate that Mr. Laurence's business detains him near you so long, since he is so useful to you and father. The girls are all as good as gold. Jo helps me with the sewing, and insists on doing all sorts of hard jobs. I should be afraid she might overdo, if I didn't know that her 'moral fit' wouldn't last long. Beth is as regular about her tasks as a clock, and never forgets what you told her. She grieves about father, and looks sober except when she is at her little piano. Amy minds me nicely, and I take great care of her. She does her own hair, and I am teaching her to make button-holes and mend her stockings. She tries very hard, and I know you will be pleased with her improvement when you come. Mr. Laurence watches over us like a motherly old hen, as Jo says; and Laurie is very kind and neighborly. He and Jo keep us merry, for we get pretty blue sometimes, and feel like orphans, with you so far away. Hannah is a perfect saint; she does not scold at all, and always calls me Miss 'Margaret,' which is quite proper, you know, and treats me with respect. We are all well and busy; but we long, day and night, to have you back. Give my dearest love to father, and believe me, ever your own \"MEG.\"\n\nThis note, prettily written on scented paper, was a great contrast to the next, which was scribbled on a big sheet of thin foreign paper, ornamented with blots and all manner of flourishes and curly-tailed letters:--\n\n\"Three cheers for dear father! Brooke was a trump to telegraph right off, and let us know the minute he was better. I rushed up garret when the letter came, and tried to thank God for being so good to us; but I could only cry, and say, 'I'm glad! I'm glad!' Didn't that do as well as a regular prayer? for I felt a great many in my heart. We have such funny times; and now I can enjoy them, for every one is so desperately good, it's like living in a nest of turtle-doves. You'd laugh to see Meg head the table and try to be motherish. She gets prettier every day, and I'm in love with her sometimes. The children are regular archangels, and I--well, I'm Jo, and never shall be anything else. Oh, I must tell you that I came near having a quarrel with Laurie. I freed my mind about a silly little thing, and he was offended. I was right, but didn't speak as I ought, and he marched home, saying he wouldn't come again till I begged pardon. I declared I wouldn't, and got mad. It lasted all day; I felt bad, and wanted you very much. Laurie and I are both so proud, it's hard to beg pardon; but I thought he'd come to it, for I _was_ in the right. He didn't come; and just at night I remembered what you said when Amy fell into the river. I read my little book, felt better, resolved not to let the sun set on _my_ anger, and ran over to tell Laurie I was sorry. I met him at the gate, coming for the same thing. We both laughed, begged each other's pardon, and felt all good and comfortable again.\n\n\"I made a 'pome' yesterday, when I was helping Hannah wash; and, as father likes my silly little things, I put it in to amuse him. Give him the lovingest hug that ever was, and kiss yourself a dozen times for your \"TOPSY-TURVY JO.\"\n\n\"Queen of my tub, I merrily sing, While the white foam rises high; And sturdily wash and rinse and wring, And fasten the clothes to dry; Then out in the free fresh air they swing, Under the sunny sky.\n\n\"I wish we could wash from our hearts and souls The stains of the week away, And let water and air by their magic make Ourselves as pure as they; Then on the earth there would be indeed A glorious washing-day!\n\n\"Along the path of a useful life, Will heart's-ease ever bloom; The busy mind has no time to think Of sorrow or care or gloom; And anxious thoughts may be swept away, As we bravely wield a broom.\n\n\"I am glad a task to me is given, To labor at day by day; For it brings me health and strength and hope, And I cheerfully learn to say,-- 'Head, you may think, Heart, you may feel, But, Hand, you shall work alway!'\"\n\n\"There is only room for me to send my love, and some pressed pansies from the root I have been keeping safe in the house for father to see. I read every morning, try to be good all day, and sing myself to sleep with father's tune. I can't sing 'Land of the Leal' now; it makes me cry. Every one is very kind, and we are as happy as we can be without you. Amy wants the rest of the page, so I must stop. I didn't forget to cover the holders, and I wind the clock and air the rooms every day.\n\n\"Kiss dear father on the cheek he calls mine. Oh, do come soon to your loving \"LITTLE BETH.\"\n\n\"We are all well I do my lessons always and never corroberate the girls--Meg says I mean contradick so I put in both words and you can take the properest. Meg is a great comfort to me and lets me have jelly every night at tea its so good for me Jo says because it keeps me sweet tempered. Laurie is not as respeckful as he ought to be now I am almost in my teens, he calls me Chick and hurts my feelings by talking French to me very fast when I say Merci or Bon jour as Hattie King does. The sleeves of my blue dress were all worn out, and Meg put in new ones, but the full front came wrong and they are more blue than the dress. I felt bad but did not fret I bear my troubles well but I do wish Hannah would put more starch in my aprons and have buckwheats every day. Can't she? Didn't I make that interrigation point nice? Meg says my punchtuation and spelling are disgraceful and I am mortyfied but dear me I have so many things to do, I can't stop. Adieu, I send heaps of love to Papa. \"Your affectionate daughter, \"AMY CURTIS MARCH.\"\n\n\"I jes drop a line to say we git on fust rate. The girls is clever and fly round right smart. Miss Meg is going to make a proper good housekeeper; she hes the liking for it, and gits the hang of things surprisin quick. Jo doos beat all for goin ahead, but she don't stop to cal'k'late fust, and you never know where she's like to bring up. She done out a tub of clothes on Monday, but she starched 'em afore they was wrenched, and blued a pink calico dress till I thought I should a died a laughin. Beth is the best of little creeters, and a sight of help to me, bein so forehanded and dependable. She tries to learn everything, and really goes to market beyond her years; likewise keeps accounts, with my help, quite wonderful. We have got on very economical so fur; I don't let the girls hev coffee only once a week, accordin to your wish, and keep em on plain wholesome vittles. Amy does well about frettin, wearin her best clothes and eatin sweet stuff. Mr. Laurie is as full of didoes as usual, and turns the house upside down frequent; but he heartens up the girls, and so I let em hev full swing. The old gentleman sends heaps of things, and is rather wearin, but means wal, and it aint my place to say nothin. My bread is riz, so no more at this time. I send my duty to Mr. March, and hope he's seen the last of his Pewmonia. \"Yours Respectful, \"HANNAH MULLET.\"\n\n\"All serene on the Rappahannock, troops in fine condition, commissary department well conducted, the Home Guard under Colonel Teddy always on duty, Commander-in-chief General Laurence reviews the army daily, Quartermaster Mullett keeps order in camp, and Major Lion does picket duty at night. A salute of twenty-four guns was fired on receipt of good news from Washington, and a dress parade took place at head-quarters. Commander-in-chief sends best wishes, in which he is heartily joined by \"COLONEL TEDDY.\"\n\n\"The little girls are all well; Beth and my boy report daily; Hannah is a model servant, and guards pretty Meg like a dragon. Glad the fine weather holds; pray make Brooke useful, and draw on me for funds if expenses exceed your estimate. Don't let your husband want anything. Thank God he is mending. \"Your sincere friend and servant, \"JAMES LAURENCE.\"\n\n# LITTLE FAITHFUL.\n\nFor a week the amount of virtue in the old house would have supplied the neighborhood. It was really amazing, for every one seemed in a heavenly frame of mind, and self-denial was all the fashion. Relieved of their first anxiety about their father, the girls insensibly relaxed their praiseworthy efforts a little, and began to fall back into the old ways. They did not forget their motto, but hoping and keeping busy seemed to grow easier; and after such tremendous exertions, they felt that Endeavor deserved a holiday, and gave it a good many.\n\nJo caught a bad cold through neglect to cover the shorn head enough, and was ordered to stay at home till she was better, for Aunt March didn't like to hear people read with colds in their heads. Jo liked this, and after an energetic rummage from garret to cellar, subsided on the sofa to nurse her cold with arsenicum and books. Amy found that housework and art did not go well together, and returned to her mud pies. Meg went daily to her pupils, and sewed, or thought she did, at home, but much time was spent in writing long letters to her mother, or reading the Washington despatches over and over. Beth kept on, with only slight relapses into idleness or grieving. All the little duties were faithfully done each day, and many of her sisters' also, for they were forgetful, and the house seemed like a clock whose pendulum was gone a-visiting. When her heart got heavy with longings for mother or fears for father, she went away into a certain closet, hid her face in the folds of a certain dear old gown, and made her little moan and prayed her little prayer quietly by herself. Nobody knew what cheered her up after a sober fit, but every one felt how sweet and helpful Beth was, and fell into a way of going to her for comfort or advice in their small affairs.\n\nAll were unconscious that this experience was a test of character; and, when the first excitement was over, felt that they had done well, and deserved praise. So they did; but their mistake was in ceasing to do well, and they learned this lesson through much anxiety and regret.\n\n\"Meg, I wish you'd go and see the Hummels; you know mother told us not to forget them,\" said Beth, ten days after Mrs. March's departure.\n\n\"I'm too tired to go this afternoon,\" replied Meg, rocking comfortably as she sewed.\n\n\"Can't you, Jo?\" asked Beth.\n\n\"Too stormy for me with my cold.\"\n\n\"I thought it was almost well.\"\n\n\"It's well enough for me to go out with Laurie, but not well enough to go to the Hummels',\" said Jo, laughing, but looking a little ashamed of her inconsistency.\n\n\"Why don't you go yourself?\" asked Meg.\n\n\"I _have_ been every day, but the baby is sick, and I don't know what to do for it. Mrs. Hummel goes away to work, and Lottchen takes care of it; but it gets sicker and sicker, and I think you or Hannah ought to go.\"\n\nBeth spoke earnestly, and Meg promised she would go to-morrow.\n\n\"Ask Hannah for some nice little mess, and take it round, Beth; the air will do you good,\" said Jo, adding apologetically, \"I'd go, but I want to finish my writing.\"\n\n\"My head aches and I'm tired, so I thought may be some of you would go,\" said Beth.\n\n\"Amy will be in presently, and she will run down for us,\" suggested Meg.\n\n\"Well, I'll rest a little and wait for her.\"\n\nSo Beth lay down on the sofa, the others returned to their work, and the Hummels were forgotten. An hour passed: Amy did not come; Meg went to her room to try on a new dress; Jo was absorbed in her story, and Hannah was sound asleep before the kitchen fire, when Beth quietly put on her hood, filled her basket with odds and ends for the poor children, and went out into the chilly air, with a heavy head, and a grieved look in her patient eyes. It was late when she came back, and no one saw her creep upstairs and shut herself into her mother's room. Half an hour after Jo went to \"mother's closet\" for something, and there found Beth sitting on the medicine chest, looking very grave, with red eyes, and a camphor-bottle in her hand.\n\n\"Christopher Columbus! What's the matter?\" cried Jo, as Beth put out her hand as if to warn her off, and asked quickly,--\n\n\"You've had the scarlet fever, haven't you?\"\n\n\"Years ago, when Meg did. Why?\"\n\n\"Then I'll tell you. Oh, Jo, the baby's dead!\"\n\n\"What baby?\"\n\n\"Mrs. Hummel's; it died in my lap before she got home,\" cried Beth, with a sob.\n\n\"My poor dear, how dreadful for you! I ought to have gone,\" said Jo, taking her sister in her arms as she sat down in her mother's big chair, with a remorseful face.\n\n\"It wasn't dreadful, Jo, only so sad! I saw in a minute that it was sicker, but Lottchen said her mother had gone for a doctor, so I took baby and let Lotty rest. It seemed asleep, but all of a sudden it gave a little cry, and trembled, and then lay very still. I tried to warm its feet, and Lotty gave it some milk, but it didn't stir, and I knew it was dead.\"\n\n\"Don't cry, dear! What did you do?\"\n\n\"I just sat and held it softly till Mrs. Hummel came with the doctor. He said it was dead, and looked at Heinrich and Minna, who have got sore throats. 'Scarlet fever, ma'am. Ought to have called me before,' he said crossly. Mrs. Hummel told him she was poor, and had tried to cure baby herself, but now it was too late, and she could only ask him to help the others, and trust to charity for his pay. He smiled then, and was kinder; but it was very sad, and I cried with them till he turned round, all of a sudden, and told me to go home and take belladonna right away, or I'd have the fever.\"\n\n\"No, you won't!\" cried Jo, hugging her close, with a frightened look. \"O Beth, if you should be sick I never could forgive myself! What _shall_ we do?\"\n\n\"Don't be frightened, I guess I shan't have it badly. I looked in mother's book, and saw that it begins with headache, sore throat, and queer feelings like mine, so I did take some belladonna, and I feel better,\" said Beth, laying her cold hands on her hot forehead, and trying to look well.\n\n\"If mother was only at home!\" exclaimed Jo, seizing the book, and feeling that Washington was an immense way off. She read a page, looked at Beth, felt her head, peeped into her throat, and then said gravely; \"You've been over the baby every day for more than a week, and among the others who are going to have it; so I'm afraid _you_ are going to have it, Beth. I'll call Hannah, she knows all about sickness.\"\n\n\"Don't let Amy come; she never had it, and I should hate to give it to her. Can't you and Meg have it over again?\" asked Beth, anxiously.\n\n\"I guess not; don't care if I do; serve me right, selfish pig, to let you go, and stay writing rubbish myself!\" muttered Jo, as she went to consult Hannah.\n\nThe good soul was wide awake in a minute, and took the lead at once, assuring Jo that there was no need to worry; every one had scarlet fever, and, if rightly treated, nobody died,--all of which Jo believed, and felt much relieved as they went up to call Meg.\n\n\"Now I'll tell you what we'll do,\" said Hannah, when she had examined and questioned Beth; \"we will have Dr. Bangs, just to take a look at you, dear, and see that we start right; then we'll send Amy off to Aunt March's, for a spell, to keep her out of harm's way, and one of you girls can stay at home and amuse Beth for a day or two.\"\n\n\"I shall stay, of course; I'm oldest,\" began Meg, looking anxious and self-reproachful.\n\n\"_I_ shall, because it's my fault she is sick; I told mother I'd do the errands, and I haven't,\" said Jo decidedly.\n\n\"Which will you have, Beth? there ain't no need of but one,\" said Hannah.\n\n\"Jo, please;\" and Beth leaned her head against her sister, with a contented look, which effectually settled that point.\n\n\"I'll go and tell Amy,\" said Meg, feeling a little hurt, yet rather relieved, on the whole, for she did not like nursing, and Jo did.\n\nAmy rebelled outright, and passionately declared that she had rather have the fever than go to Aunt March. Meg reasoned, pleaded, and commanded: all in vain. Amy protested that she would _not_ go; and Meg left her in despair, to ask Hannah what should be done. Before she came back, Laurie walked into the parlor to find Amy sobbing, with her head in the sofa-cushions. She told her story, expecting to be consoled; but Laurie only put his hands in his pockets and walked about the room, whistling softly, as he knit his brows in deep thought. Presently he sat down beside her, and said, in his most wheedlesome tone, \"Now be a sensible little woman, and do as they say. No, don't cry, but hear what a jolly plan I've got. You go to Aunt March's, and I'll come and take you out every day, driving or walking, and we'll have capital times. Won't that be better than moping here?\"\n\n\"I don't wish to be sent off as if I was in the way,\" began Amy, in an injured voice.\n\n\"Bless your heart, child, it's to keep you well. You don't want to be sick, do you?\"\n\n\"No, I'm sure I don't; but I dare say I shall be, for I've been with Beth all the time.\"\n\n\"That's the very reason you ought to go away at once, so that you may escape it. Change of air and care will keep you well, I dare say; or, if it does not entirely, you will have the fever more lightly. I advise you to be off as soon as you can, for scarlet fever is no joke, miss.\"\n\n\"But it's dull at Aunt March's, and she is so cross,\" said Amy, looking rather frightened.\n\n\"It won't be dull with me popping in every day to tell you how Beth is, and take you out gallivanting. The old lady likes me, and I'll be as sweet as possible to her, so she won't peck at us, whatever we do.\"\n\n\"Will you take me out in the trotting wagon with Puck?\"\n\n\"On my honor as a gentleman.\"\n\n\"And come every single day?\"\n\n\"See if I don't.\"\n\n\"And bring me back the minute Beth is well?\"\n\n\"The identical minute.\"\n\n\"And go to the theatre, truly?\"\n\n\"A dozen theatres, if we may.\"\n\n\"Well--I guess--I will,\" said Amy slowly.\n\n\"Good girl! Call Meg, and tell her you'll give in,\" said Laurie, with an approving pat, which annoyed Amy more than the \"giving in.\"\n\nMeg and Jo came running down to behold the miracle which had been wrought; and Amy, feeling very precious and self-sacrificing, promised to go, if the doctor said Beth was going to be ill.\n\n\"How is the little dear?\" asked Laurie; for Beth was his especial pet, and he felt more anxious about her than he liked to show.\n\n\"She is lying down on mother's bed, and feels better. The baby's death troubled her, but I dare say she has only got cold. Hannah _says_ she thinks so; but she _looks_ worried, and that makes me fidgety,\" answered Meg.\n\n\"What a trying world it is!\" said Jo, rumpling up her hair in a fretful sort of way. \"No sooner do we get out of one trouble than down comes another. There doesn't seem to be anything to hold on to when mother's gone; so I'm all at sea.\"\n\n\"Well, don't make a porcupine of yourself, it isn't becoming. Settle your wig, Jo, and tell me if I shall telegraph to your mother, or do anything?\" asked Laurie, who never had been reconciled to the loss of his friend's one beauty.\n\n\"That is what troubles me,\" said Meg. \"I think we ought to tell her if Beth is really ill, but Hannah says we mustn't, for mother can't leave father, and it will only make them anxious. Beth won't be sick long, and Hannah knows just what to do, and mother said we were to mind her, so I suppose we must, but it doesn't seem quite right to me.\"\n\n\"Hum, well, I can't say; suppose you ask grandfather after the doctor has been.\"\n\n\"We will. Jo, go and get Dr. Bangs at once,\" commanded Meg; \"we can't decide anything till he has been.\"\n\n\"Stay where you are, Jo; I'm errand-boy to this establishment,\" said Laurie, taking up his cap.\n\n\"I'm afraid you are busy,\" began Meg.\n\n\"No, I've done my lessons for the day.\"\n\n\"Do you study in vacation time?\" asked Jo.\n\n\"I follow the good example my neighbors set me,\" was Laurie's answer, as he swung himself out of the room.\n\n\"I have great hopes of my boy,\" observed Jo, watching him fly over the fence with an approving smile.\n\n\"He does very well--for a boy,\" was Meg's somewhat ungracious answer, for the subject did not interest her.\n\nDr. Bangs came, said Beth had symptoms of the fever, but thought she would have it lightly, though he looked sober over the Hummel story. Amy was ordered off at once, and provided with something to ward off danger, she departed in great state, with Jo and Laurie as escort.\n\nAunt March received them with her usual hospitality.\n\n\"What do you want now?\" she asked, looking sharply over her spectacles, while the parrot, sitting on the back of her chair, called out,--\n\n\"Go away. No boys allowed here.\"\n\nLaurie retired to the window, and Jo told her story.\n\n\"No more than I expected, if you are allowed to go poking about among poor folks. Amy can stay and make herself useful if she isn't sick, which I've no doubt she will be,--looks like it now. Don't cry, child, it worries me to hear people sniff.\"\n\nAmy _was_ on the point of crying, but Laurie slyly pulled the parrot's tail, which caused Polly to utter an astonished croak, and call out,--\n\"Bless my boots!\" in such a funny way, that she laughed instead.\n\n\"What do you hear from your mother?\" asked the old lady gruffly.\n\n\"Father is much better,\" replied Jo, trying to keep sober.\n\n\"Oh, is he? Well, that won't last long, I fancy; March never had any stamina,\" was the cheerful reply.\n\n\"Ha, ha! never say die, take a pinch of snuff, good by, good by!\" squalled Polly, dancing on her perch, and clawing at the old lady's cap as Laurie tweaked him in the rear.\n\n\"Hold your tongue, you disrespectful old bird! and, Jo, you'd better go at once; it isn't proper to be gadding about so late with a rattle-pated boy like--\"\n\n\"Hold your tongue, you disrespectful old bird!\" cried Polly, tumbling off the chair with a bounce, and running to peck the \"rattle-pated\" boy, who was shaking with laughter at the last speech.\n\n\"I don't think I _can_ bear it, but I'll try,\" thought Amy, as she was left alone with Aunt March.\n\n\"Get along, you fright!\" screamed Polly; and at that rude speech Amy could not restrain a sniff.\n\n# DARK DAYS.\n\nBeth did have the fever, and was much sicker than any one but Hannah and the doctor suspected. The girls knew nothing about illness, and Mr. Laurence was not allowed to see her, so Hannah had everything all her own way, and busy Dr. Bangs did his best, but left a good deal to the excellent nurse. Meg stayed at home, lest she should infect the Kings, and kept house, feeling very anxious and a little guilty when she wrote letters in which no mention was made of Beth's illness. She could not think it right to deceive her mother, but she had been bidden to mind Hannah, and Hannah wouldn't hear of \"Mrs. March bein' told, and worried just for sech a trifle.\" Jo devoted herself to Beth day and night; not a hard task, for Beth was very patient, and bore her pain uncomplainingly as long as she could control herself. But there came a time when during the fever fits she began to talk in a hoarse, broken voice, to play on the coverlet, as if on her beloved little piano, and try to sing with a throat so swollen that there was no music left; a time when she did not know the familiar faces round her, but addressed them by wrong names, and called imploringly for her mother. Then Jo grew frightened, Meg begged to be allowed to write the truth, and even Hannah said she \"would think of it, though there was no danger _yet_.\" A letter from Washington added to their trouble, for Mr. March had had a relapse, and could not think of coming home for a long while.\n\nHow dark the days seemed now, how sad and lonely the house, and how heavy were the hearts of the sisters as they worked and waited, while the shadow of death hovered over the once happy home! Then it was that Margaret, sitting alone with tears dropping often on her work, felt how rich she had been in things more precious than any luxuries money could buy,--in love, protection, peace, and health, the real blessings of life. Then it was that Jo, living in the darkened room, with that suffering little sister always before her eyes, and that pathetic voice sounding in her ears, learned to see the beauty and the sweetness of Beth's nature, to feel how deep and tender a place she filled in all hearts, and to acknowledge the worth of Beth's unselfish ambition, to live for others, and make home happy by the exercise of those simple virtues which all may possess, and which all should love and value more than talent, wealth, or beauty. And Amy, in her exile, longed eagerly to be at home, that she might work for Beth, feeling now that no service would be hard or irksome, and remembering, with regretful grief, how many neglected tasks those willing hands had done for her. Laurie haunted the house like a restless ghost, and Mr. Laurence locked the grand piano, because he could not bear to be reminded of the young neighbor who used to make the twilight pleasant for him. Every one missed Beth. The milkman, baker, grocer, and butcher inquired how she did; poor Mrs. Hummel came to beg pardon for her thoughtlessness, and to get a shroud for Minna; the neighbors sent all sorts of comforts and good wishes, and even those who knew her best were surprised to find how many friends shy little Beth had made.\n\nMeanwhile she lay on her bed with old Joanna at her side, for even in her wanderings she did not forget her forlorn _protégé_. She longed for her cats, but would not have them brought, lest they should get sick; and, in her quiet hours, she was full of anxiety about Jo. She sent loving messages to Amy, bade them tell her mother that she would write soon; and often begged for pencil and paper to try to say a word, that father might not think she had neglected him. But soon even these intervals of consciousness ended, and she lay hour after hour, tossing to and fro, with incoherent words on her lips, or sank into a heavy sleep which brought her no refreshment. Dr. Bangs came twice a day, Hannah sat up at night, Meg kept a telegram in her desk all ready to send off at any minute, and Jo never stirred from Beth's side.\n\nThe first of December was a wintry day indeed to them, for a bitter wind blew, snow fell fast, and the year seemed getting ready for its death. When Dr. Bangs came that morning, he looked long at Beth, held the hot hand in both his own a minute, and laid it gently down, saying, in a low tone, to Hannah,--\n\n\"If Mrs. March _can_ leave her husband, she'd better be sent for.\"\n\nHannah nodded without speaking, for her lips twitched nervously; Meg dropped down into a chair as the strength seemed to go out of her limbs at the sound of those words; and Jo, after standing with a pale face for a minute, ran to the parlor, snatched up the telegram, and, throwing on her things, rushed out into the storm. She was soon back, and, while noiselessly taking off her cloak, Laurie came in with a letter, saying that Mr. March was mending again. Jo read it thankfully, but the heavy weight did not seem lifted off her heart, and her face was so full of misery that Laurie asked quickly,--\n\n\"What is it? is Beth worse?\"\n\n\"I've sent for mother,\" said Jo, tugging at her rubber boots with a tragical expression.\n\n\"Good for you, Jo! Did you do it on your own responsibility?\" asked Laurie, as he seated her in the hall chair, and took off the rebellious boots, seeing how her hands shook.\n\n\"No, the doctor told us to.\"\n\n\"O Jo, it's not so bad as that?\" cried Laurie, with a startled face.\n\n\"Yes, it is; she doesn't know us, she doesn't even talk about the flocks of green doves, as she calls the vine-leaves on the wall; she doesn't look like my Beth, and there's nobody to help us bear it; mother and father both gone, and God seems so far away I can't find Him.\"\n\nAs the tears streamed fast down poor Jo's cheeks, she stretched out her hand in a helpless sort of way, as if groping in the dark, and Laurie took it in his, whispering, as well as he could, with a lump in his throat,--\n\n\"I'm here. Hold on to me, Jo, dear!\"\n\nShe could not speak, but she did \"hold on,\" and the warm grasp of the friendly human hand comforted her sore heart, and seemed to lead her nearer to the Divine arm which alone could uphold her in her trouble. Laurie longed to say something tender and comfortable, but no fitting words came to him, so he stood silent, gently stroking her bent head as her mother used to do. It was the best thing he could have done; far more soothing than the most eloquent words, for Jo felt the unspoken sympathy, and, in the silence, learned the sweet solace which affection administers to sorrow. Soon she dried the tears which had relieved her, and looked up with a grateful face.\n\n\"Thank you, Teddy, I'm better now; I don't feel so forlorn, and will try to bear it if it comes.\"\n\n\"Keep hoping for the best; that will help you, Jo. Soon your mother will be here, and then everything will be right.\"\n\n\"I'm so glad father is better; now she won't feel so bad about leaving him. Oh, me! it does seem as if all the troubles came in a heap, and I got the heaviest part on my shoulders,\" sighed Jo, spreading her wet handkerchief over her knees to dry.\n\n\"Doesn't Meg pull fair?\" asked Laurie, looking indignant.\n\n\"Oh, yes; she tries to, but she can't love Bethy as I do; and she won't miss her as I shall. Beth is my conscience, and I _can't_ give her up. I can't! I can't!\"\n\nDown went Jo's face into the wet handkerchief, and she cried despairingly; for she had kept up bravely till now, and never shed a tear. Laurie drew his hand across his eyes, but could not speak till he had subdued the choky feeling in his throat and steadied his lips. It might be unmanly, but he couldn't help it, and I am glad of it. Presently, as Jo's sobs quieted, he said hopefully, \"I don't think she will die; she's so good, and we all love her so much, I don't believe God will take her away yet.\"\n\n\"The good and dear people always do die,\" groaned Jo, but she stopped crying, for her friend's words cheered her up, in spite of her own doubts and fears.\n\n\"Poor girl, you're worn out. It isn't like you to be forlorn. Stop a bit; I'll hearten you up in a jiffy.\"\n\nLaurie went off two stairs at a time, and Jo laid her wearied head down on Beth's little brown hood, which no one had thought of moving from the table where she left it. It must have possessed some magic, for the submissive spirit of its gentle owner seemed to enter into Jo; and, when Laurie came running down with a glass of wine, she took it with a smile, and said bravely, \"I drink--Health to my Beth! You are a good doctor, Teddy, and _such_ a comfortable friend; how can I ever pay you?\" she added, as the wine refreshed her body, as the kind words had done her troubled mind.\n\n\"I'll send in my bill, by and by; and to-night I'll give you something that will warm the cockles of your heart better than quarts of wine,\" said Laurie, beaming at her with a face of suppressed satisfaction at something.\n\n\"What is it?\" cried Jo, forgetting her woes for a minute, in her wonder.\n\n\"I telegraphed to your mother yesterday, and Brooke answered she'd come at once, and she'll be here to-night, and everything will be all right. Aren't you glad I did it?\"\n\nLaurie spoke very fast, and turned red and excited all in a minute, for he had kept his plot a secret, for fear of disappointing the girls or harming Beth. Jo grew quite white, flew out of her chair, and the moment he stopped speaking she electrified him by throwing her arms round his neck, and crying out, with a joyful cry, \"O Laurie! O mother! I _am_ so glad!\" She did not weep again, but laughed hysterically, and trembled and clung to her friend as if she was a little bewildered by the sudden news. Laurie, though decidedly amazed, behaved with great presence of mind; he patted her back soothingly, and, finding that she was recovering, followed it up by a bashful kiss or two, which brought Jo round at once. Holding on to the banisters, she put him gently away, saying breathlessly, \"Oh, don't! I didn't mean to; it was dreadful of me; but you were such a dear to go and do it in spite of Hannah that I couldn't help flying at you. Tell me all about it, and don't give me wine again; it makes me act so.\"\n\n\"I don't mind,\" laughed Laurie, as he settled his tie. \"Why, you see I got fidgety, and so did grandpa. We thought Hannah was overdoing the authority business, and your mother ought to know. She'd never forgive us if Beth--well, if anything happened, you know. So I got grandpa to say it was high time we did something, and off I pelted to the office yesterday, for the doctor looked sober, and Hannah most took my head off when I proposed a telegram. I never _can_ bear to be 'lorded over;' so that settled my mind, and I did it. Your mother will come, I know, and the late train is in at two, A.M. I shall go for her; and you've only got to bottle up your rapture, and keep Beth quiet, till that blessed lady gets here.\"\n\n\"Laurie, you're an angel! How shall I ever thank you?\"\n\n\"Fly at me again; I rather like it,\" said Laurie, looking mischievous,--a thing he had not done for a fortnight.\n\n\"No, thank you. I'll do it by proxy, when your grandpa comes. Don't tease, but go home and rest, for you'll be up half the night. Bless you, Teddy, bless you!\"\n\nJo had backed into a corner; and, as she finished her speech, she vanished precipitately into the kitchen, where she sat down upon a dresser, and told the assembled cats that she was \"happy, oh, _so_ happy!\" while Laurie departed, feeling that he had made rather a neat thing of it.\n\n\"That's the interferingest chap I ever see; but I forgive him, and do hope Mrs. March is coming on right away,\" said Hannah, with an air of relief, when Jo told the good news.\n\nMeg had a quiet rapture, and then brooded over the letter, while Jo set the sick-room in order, and Hannah \"knocked up a couple of pies in case of company unexpected.\" A breath of fresh air seemed to blow through the house, and something better than sunshine brightened the quiet rooms. Everything appeared to feel the hopeful change; Beth's bird began to chirp again, and a half-blown rose was discovered on Amy's bush in the window; the fires seemed to burn with unusual cheeriness; and every time the girls met, their pale faces broke into smiles as they hugged one another, whispering encouragingly, \"Mother's coming, dear! mother's coming!\" Every one rejoiced but Beth; she lay in that heavy stupor, alike unconscious of hope and joy, doubt and danger. It was a piteous sight,--the once rosy face so changed and vacant, the once busy hands so weak and wasted, the once smiling lips quite dumb, and the once pretty, well-kept hair scattered rough and tangled on the pillow. All day she lay so, only rousing now and then to mutter, \"Water!\" with lips so parched they could hardly shape the word; all day Jo and Meg hovered over her, watching, waiting, hoping, and trusting in God and mother; and all day the snow fell, the bitter wind raged, and the hours dragged slowly by. But night came at last; and every time the clock struck, the sisters, still sitting on either side the bed, looked at each other with brightening eyes, for each hour brought help nearer. The doctor had been in to say that some change, for better or worse, would probably take place about midnight, at which time he would return.\n\nHannah, quite worn out, lay down on the sofa at the bed's foot, and fell fast asleep; Mr. Laurence marched to and fro in the parlor, feeling that he would rather face a rebel battery than Mrs. March's anxious countenance as she entered; Laurie lay on the rug, pretending to rest, but staring into the fire with the thoughtful look which made his black eyes beautifully soft and clear.\n\nThe girls never forgot that night, for no sleep came to them as they kept their watch, with that dreadful sense of powerlessness which comes to us in hours like those.\n\n\"If God spares Beth I never will complain again,\" whispered Meg earnestly.\n\n\"If God spares Beth I'll try to love and serve Him all my life,\" answered Jo, with equal fervor.\n\n\"I wish I had no heart, it aches so,\" sighed Meg, after a pause.\n\n\"If life is often as hard as this, I don't see how we ever shall get through it,\" added her sister despondently.\n\nHere the clock struck twelve, and both forgot themselves in watching Beth, for they fancied a change passed over her wan face. The house was still as death, and nothing but the wailing of the wind broke the deep hush. Weary Hannah slept on, and no one but the sisters saw the pale shadow which seemed to fall upon the little bed. An hour went by, and nothing happened except Laurie's quiet departure for the station. Another hour,--still no one came; and anxious fears of delay in the storm, or accidents by the way, or, worst of all, a great grief at Washington, haunted the poor girls.\n\nIt was past two, when Jo, who stood at the window thinking how dreary the world looked in its winding-sheet of snow, heard a movement by the bed, and, turning quickly, saw Meg kneeling before their mother's easy-chair, with her face hidden. A dreadful fear passed coldly over Jo, as she thought, \"Beth is dead, and Meg is afraid to tell me.\"\n\nShe was back at her post in an instant, and to her excited eyes a great change seemed to have taken place. The fever flush and the look of pain were gone, and the beloved little face looked so pale and peaceful in its utter repose, that Jo felt no desire to weep or to lament. Leaning low over this dearest of her sisters, she kissed the damp forehead with her heart on her lips, and softly whispered, \"Good-by, my Beth; good-by!\"\n\nAs if waked by the stir, Hannah started out of her sleep, hurried to the bed, looked at Beth, felt her hands, listened at her lips, and then, throwing her apron over her head, sat down to rock to and fro, exclaiming, under her breath, \"The fever's turned; she's sleepin' nat'ral; her skin's damp, and she breathes easy. Praise be given! Oh, my goodness me!\"\n\nBefore the girls could believe the happy truth, the doctor came to confirm it. He was a homely man, but they thought his face quite heavenly when he smiled, and said, with a fatherly look at them, \"Yes, my dears, I think the little girl will pull through this time. Keep the house quiet; let her sleep, and when she wakes, give her--\"\n\nWhat they were to give, neither heard; for both crept into the dark hall, and, sitting on the stairs, held each other close, rejoicing with hearts too full for words. When they went back to be kissed and cuddled by faithful Hannah, they found Beth lying, as she used to do, with her cheek pillowed on her hand, the dreadful pallor gone, and breathing quietly, as if just fallen asleep.\n\n\"If mother would only come now!\" said Jo, as the winter night began to wane.\n\n\"See,\" said Meg, coming up with a white, half-opened rose, \"I thought this would hardly be ready to lay in Beth's hand to-morrow if she--went away from us. But it has blossomed in the night, and now I mean to put it in my vase here, so that when the darling wakes, the first thing she sees will be the little rose, and mother's face.\"\n\nNever had the sun risen so beautifully, and never had the world seemed so lovely, as it did to the heavy eyes of Meg and Jo, as they looked out in the early morning, when their long, sad vigil was done.\n\n\"It looks like a fairy world,\" said Meg, smiling to herself, as she stood behind the curtain, watching the dazzling sight.\n\n\"Hark!\" cried Jo, starting to her feet.\n\nYes, there was a sound of bells at the door below, a cry from Hannah, and then Laurie's voice saying, in a joyful whisper, \"Girls, she's come! she's come!\"\n\n# AMY'S WILL.\n\nWhile these things were happening at home, Amy was having hard times at Aunt March's. She felt her exile deeply, and, for the first time in her life, realized how much she was beloved and petted at home. Aunt March never petted any one; she did not approve of it; but she meant to be kind, for the well-behaved little girl pleased her very much, and Aunt March had a soft place in her old heart for her nephew's children, though she didn't think proper to confess it. She really did her best to make Amy happy, but, dear me, what mistakes she made! Some old people keep young at heart in spite of wrinkles and gray hairs, can sympathize with children's little cares and joys, make them feel at home, and can hide wise lessons under pleasant plays, giving and receiving friendship in the sweetest way. But Aunt March had not this gift, and she worried Amy very much with her rules and orders, her prim ways, and long, prosy talks. Finding the child more docile and amiable than her sister, the old lady felt it her duty to try and counteract, as far as possible, the bad effects of home freedom and indulgence. So she took Amy in hand, and taught her as she herself had been taught sixty years ago,--a process which carried dismay to Amy's soul, and made her feel like a fly in the web of a very strict spider.\n\nShe had to wash the cups every morning, and polish up the old-fashioned spoons, the fat silver teapot, and the glasses, till they shone. Then she must dust the room, and what a trying job that was! Not a speck escaped Aunt March's eye, and all the furniture had claw legs, and much carving, which was never dusted to suit. Then Polly must be fed, the lap-dog combed, and a dozen trips upstairs and down, to get things, or deliver orders, for the old lady was very lame, and seldom left her big chair. After these tiresome labors, she must do her lessons, which was a daily trial of every virtue she possessed. Then she was allowed one hour for exercise or play, and didn't she enjoy it? Laurie came every day, and wheedled Aunt March, till Amy was allowed to go out with him, when they walked and rode, and had capital times. After dinner, she had to read aloud, and sit still while the old lady slept, which she usually did for an hour, as she dropped off over the first page. Then patchwork or towels appeared, and Amy sewed with outward meekness and inward rebellion till dusk, when she was allowed to amuse herself as she liked till tea-time. The evenings were the worst of all, for Aunt March fell to telling long stories about her youth, which were so unutterably dull that Amy was always ready to go to bed, intending to cry over her hard fate, but usually going to sleep before she had squeezed out more than a tear or two.\n\nIf it had not been for Laurie, and old Esther, the maid, she felt that she never could have got through that dreadful time. The parrot alone was enough to drive her distracted, for he soon felt that she did not admire him, and revenged himself by being as mischievous as possible. He pulled her hair whenever she came near him, upset his bread and milk to plague her when she had newly cleaned his cage, made Mop bark by pecking at him while Madam dozed; called her names before company, and behaved in all respects like a reprehensible old bird. Then she could not endure the dog,--a fat, cross beast, who snarled and yelped at her when she made his toilet, and who lay on his back, with all his legs in the air and a most idiotic expression of countenance when he wanted something to eat, which was about a dozen times a day. The cook was bad-tempered, the old coachman deaf, and Esther the only one who ever took any notice of the young lady.\n\nEsther was a Frenchwoman, who had lived with \"Madame,\" as she called her mistress, for many years, and who rather tyrannized over the old lady, who could not get along without her. Her real name was Estelle, but Aunt March ordered her to change it, and she obeyed, on condition that she was never asked to change her religion. She took a fancy to Mademoiselle, and amused her very much, with odd stories of her life in France, when Amy sat with her while she got up Madame's laces. She also allowed her to roam about the great house, and examine the curious and pretty things stored away in the big wardrobes and the ancient chests; for Aunt March hoarded like a magpie. Amy's chief delight was an Indian cabinet, full of queer drawers, little pigeon-holes, and secret places, in which were kept all sorts of ornaments, some precious, some merely curious, all more or less antique. To examine and arrange these things gave Amy great satisfaction, especially the jewel-cases, in which, on velvet cushions, reposed the ornaments which had adorned a belle forty years ago. There was the garnet set which Aunt March wore when she came out, the pearls her father gave her on her wedding-day, her lover's diamonds, the jet mourning rings and pins, the queer lockets, with portraits of dead friends, and weeping willows made of hair inside; the baby bracelets her one little daughter had worn; Uncle March's big watch, with the red seal so many childish hands had played with, and in a box, all by itself, lay Aunt March's wedding-ring, too small now for her fat finger, but put carefully away, like the most precious jewel of them all.\n\n\"Which would Mademoiselle choose if she had her will?\" asked Esther, who always sat near to watch over and lock up the valuables.\n\n\"I like the diamonds best, but there is no necklace among them, and I'm fond of necklaces, they are so becoming. I should choose this if I might,\" replied Amy, looking with great admiration at a string of gold and ebony beads, from which hung a heavy cross of the same.\n\n\"I, too, covet that, but not as a necklace; ah, no! to me it is a rosary, and as such I should use it like a good Catholic,\" said Esther, eying the handsome thing wistfully.\n\n\"Is it meant to use as you use the string of good-smelling wooden beads hanging over your glass?\" asked Amy.\n\n\"Truly, yes, to pray with. It would be pleasing to the saints if one used so fine a rosary as this, instead of wearing it as a vain bijou.\"\n\n\"You seem to take a great deal of comfort in your prayers, Esther, and always come down looking quiet and satisfied. I wish I could.\"\n\n\"If Mademoiselle was a Catholic, she would find true comfort; but, as that is not to be, it would be well if you went apart each day, to meditate and pray, as did the good mistress whom I served before Madame. She had a little chapel, and in it found solacement for much trouble.\"\n\n\"Would it be right for me to do so too?\" asked Amy, who, in her loneliness, felt the need of help of some sort, and found that she was apt to forget her little book, now that Beth was not there to remind her of it.\n\n\"It would be excellent and charming; and I shall gladly arrange the little dressing-room for you if you like it. Say nothing to Madame, but when she sleeps go you and sit alone a while to think good thoughts, and pray the dear God to preserve your sister.\"\n\nEsther was truly pious, and quite sincere in her advice; for she had an affectionate heart, and felt much for the sisters in their anxiety. Amy liked the idea, and gave her leave to arrange the light closet next her room, hoping it would do her good.\n\n\"I wish I knew where all these pretty things would go when Aunt March dies,\" she said, as she slowly replaced the shining rosary, and shut the jewel-cases one by one.\n\n\"To you and your sisters. I know it; Madame confides in me; I witnessed her will, and it is to be so,\" whispered Esther, smiling.\n\n\"How nice! but I wish she'd let us have them now. Pro-cras-ti-nation is not agreeable,\" observed Amy, taking a last look at the diamonds.\n\n\"It is too soon yet for the young ladies to wear these things. The first one who is affianced will have the pearls--Madame has said it; and I have a fancy that the little turquoise ring will be given to you when you go, for Madame approves your good behavior and charming manners.\"\n\n\"Do you think so? Oh, I'll be a lamb, if I can only have that lovely ring! It's ever so much prettier than Kitty Bryant's. I do like Aunt March, after all;\" and Amy tried on the blue ring with a delighted face, and a firm resolve to earn it.\n\nFrom that day she was a model of obedience, and the old lady complacently admired the success of her training. Esther fitted up the closet with a little table, placed a footstool before it, and over it a picture taken from one of the shut-up rooms. She thought it was of no great value, but, being appropriate, she borrowed it, well knowing that Madame would never know it, nor care if she did. It was, however, a very valuable copy of one of the famous pictures of the world, and Amy's beauty-loving eyes were never tired of looking up at the sweet face of the divine mother, while tender thoughts of her own were busy at her heart. On the table she laid her little Testament and hymn-book, kept a vase always full of the best flowers Laurie brought her, and came every day to \"sit alone, thinking good thoughts, and praying the dear God to preserve her sister.\" Esther had given her a rosary of black beads, with a silver cross, but Amy hung it up and did not use it, feeling doubtful as to its fitness for Protestant prayers.\n\nThe little girl was very sincere in all this, for, being left alone outside the safe home-nest, she felt the need of some kind hand to hold by so sorely, that she instinctively turned to the strong and tender Friend, whose fatherly love most closely surrounds his little children. She missed her mother's help to understand and rule herself, but having been taught where to look, she did her best to find the way, and walk in it confidingly. But Amy was a young pilgrim, and just now her burden seemed very heavy. She tried to forget herself, to keep cheerful, and be satisfied with doing right, though no one saw or praised her for it. In her first effort at being very, very good, she decided to make her will, as Aunt March had done; so that if she _did_ fall ill and die, her possessions might be justly and generously divided. It cost her a pang even to think of giving up the little treasures which in her eyes were as precious as the old lady's jewels.\n\nDuring one of her play-hours she wrote out the important document as well as she could, with some help from Esther as to certain legal terms, and, when the good-natured Frenchwoman had signed her name, Amy felt relieved, and laid it by to show Laurie, whom she wanted as a second witness. As it was a rainy day, she went upstairs to amuse herself in one of the large chambers, and took Polly with her for company. In this room there was a wardrobe full of old-fashioned costumes, with which Esther allowed her to play, and it was her favorite amusement to array herself in the faded brocades, and parade up and down before the long mirror, making stately courtesies, and sweeping her train about, with a rustle which delighted her ears. So busy was she on this day that she did not hear Laurie's ring, nor see his face peeping in at her, as she gravely promenaded to and fro, flirting her fan and tossing her head, on which she wore a great pink turban, contrasting oddly with her blue brocade dress and yellow quilted petticoat. She was obliged to walk carefully, for she had on high-heeled shoes, and, as Laurie told Jo afterward, it was a comical sight to see her mince along in her gay suit, with Polly sidling and bridling just behind her, imitating her as well as he could, and occasionally stopping to laugh or exclaim, \"Ain't we fine? Get along, you fright! Hold your tongue! Kiss me, dear! Ha! ha!\"\n\nHaving with difficulty restrained an explosion of merriment, lest it should offend her majesty, Laurie tapped, and was graciously received.\n\n\"Sit down and rest while I put these things away; then I want to consult you about a very serious matter,\" said Amy, when she had shown her splendor, and driven Polly into a corner. \"That bird is the trial of my life,\" she continued, removing the pink mountain from her head, while Laurie seated himself astride of a chair. \"Yesterday, when aunt was asleep, and I was trying to be as still as a mouse, Polly began to squall and flap about in his cage; so I went to let him out, and found a big spider there. I poked it out, and it ran under the bookcase; Polly marched straight after it, stooped down and peeped under the bookcase, saying, in his funny way, with a cock of his eye, 'Come out and take a walk, my dear.' I _couldn't_ help laughing, which made Poll swear, and aunt woke up and scolded us both.\"\n\n\"Did the spider accept the old fellow's invitation?\" asked Laurie, yawning.\n\n\"Yes; out it came, and away ran Polly, frightened to death, and scrambled up on aunt's chair, calling out, 'Catch her! catch her! catch her!' as I chased the spider.\n\n\"That's a lie! Oh lor!\" cried the parrot, pecking at Laurie's toes.\n\n\"I'd wring your neck if you were mine, you old torment,\" cried Laurie, shaking his fist at the bird, who put his head on one side, and gravely croaked, \"Allyluyer! bless your buttons, dear!\"\n\n\"Now I'm ready,\" said Amy, shutting the wardrobe, and taking a paper out of her pocket. \"I want you to read that, please, and tell me if it is legal and right. I felt that I ought to do it, for life is uncertain and I don't want any ill-feeling over my tomb.\"\n\nLaurie bit his lips, and turning a little from the pensive speaker, read the following document, with praiseworthy gravity, considering the spelling:--\n\n\"I, Amy Curtis March, being in my sane mind, do give and bequeethe all my earthly property--viz. to wit:--namely\n\n\"To my father, my best pictures, sketches, maps, and works of art, including frames. Also my $100, to do what he likes with.\n\n\"To my mother, all my clothes, except the blue apron with pockets,--also my likeness, and my medal, with much love.\n\n\"To my dear sister Margaret, I give my turkquoise ring (if I get it), also my green box with the doves on it, also my piece of real lace for her neck, and my sketch of her as a memorial of her 'little girl.'\n\n\"To Jo I leave my breast-pin, the one mended with sealing wax, also my bronze inkstand--she lost the cover--and my most precious plaster rabbit, because I am sorry I burnt up her story.\n\n\"To Beth (if she lives after me) I give my dolls and the little bureau, my fan, my linen collars and my new slippers if she can wear them being thin when she gets well. And I herewith also leave her my regret that I ever made fun of old Joanna.\n\n\"To my friend and neighbor Theodore Laurence I bequeethe my paper marshay portfolio, my clay model of a horse though he did say it hadn't any neck. Also in return for his great kindness in the hour of affliction any one of my artistic works he likes, Noter Dame is the best.\n\n\"To our venerable benefactor Mr. Laurence I leave my purple box with a looking glass in the cover which will be nice for his pens and remind him of the departed girl who thanks him for his favors to her family, specially Beth.\n\n\"I wish my favorite playmate Kitty Bryant to have the blue silk apron and my gold-bead ring with a kiss.\n\n\"To Hannah I give the bandbox she wanted and all the patch work I leave hoping she 'will remember me, when it you see.'\n\n\"And now having disposed of my most valuable property I hope all will be satisfied and not blame the dead. I forgive every one, and trust we may all meet when the trump shall sound. Amen.\n\n\"To this will and testiment I set my hand and seal on this 20th day of Nov. Anni Domino 1861. \"AMY CURTIS MARCH.\n\nThe last name was written in pencil, and Amy explained that he was to rewrite it in ink, and seal it up for her properly.\n\n\"What put it into your head? Did any one tell you about Beth's giving away her things?\" asked Laurie soberly, as Amy laid a bit of red tape, with sealing-wax, a taper, and a standish before him.\n\nShe explained; and then asked anxiously, \"What about Beth?\"\n\n\"I'm sorry I spoke; but as I did, I'll tell you. She felt so ill one day that she told Jo she wanted to give her piano to Meg, her cats to you, and the poor old doll to Jo, who would love it for her sake. She was sorry she had so little to give, and left locks of hair to the rest of us, and her best love to grandpa. _She_ never thought of a will.\"\n\nLaurie was signing and sealing as he spoke, and did not look up till a great tear dropped on the paper. Amy's face was full of trouble; but she only said, \"Don't people put sort of postscripts to their wills, sometimes?\"\n\n\"Yes; 'codicils,' they call them.\"\n\n\"Put one in mine then--that I wish _all_ my curls cut off, and given round to my friends. I forgot it; but I want it done, though it will spoil my looks.\"\n\nLaurie added it, smiling at Amy's last and greatest sacrifice. Then he amused her for an hour, and was much interested in all her trials. But when he came to go, Amy held him back to whisper, with trembling lips, \"Is there really any danger about Beth?\"\n\n\"I'm afraid there is; but we must hope for the best, so don't cry, dear;\" and Laurie put his arm about her with a brotherly gesture which was very comforting.\n\nWhen he had gone, she went to her little chapel, and, sitting in the twilight, prayed for Beth, with streaming tears and an aching heart, feeling that a million turquoise rings would not console her for the loss of her gentle little sister.\n\nI don't think I have any words in which to tell the meeting of the mother and daughters; such hours are beautiful to live, but very hard to describe, so I will leave it to the imagination of my readers, merely saying that the house was full of genuine happiness, and that Meg's tender hope was realized; for when Beth woke from that long, healing sleep, the first objects on which her eyes fell _were_ the little rose and mother's face. Too weak to wonder at anything, she only smiled, and nestled close into the loving arms about her, feeling that the hungry longing was satisfied at last. Then she slept again, and the girls waited upon their mother, for she would not unclasp the thin hand which clung to hers even in sleep. Hannah had \"dished up\" an astonishing breakfast for the traveller, finding it impossible to vent her excitement in any other way; and Meg and Jo fed their mother like dutiful young storks, while they listened to her whispered account of father's state, Mr. Brooke's promise to stay and nurse him, the delays which the storm occasioned on the homeward journey, and the unspeakable comfort Laurie's hopeful face had given her when she arrived, worn out with fatigue, anxiety, and cold.\n\nWhat a strange, yet pleasant day that was! so brilliant and gay without, for all the world seemed abroad to welcome the first snow; so quiet and reposeful within, for every one slept, spent with watching, and a Sabbath stillness reigned through the house, while nodding Hannah mounted guard at the door. With a blissful sense of burdens lifted off, Meg and Jo closed their weary eyes, and lay at rest, like storm-beaten boats, safe at anchor in a quiet harbor. Mrs. March would not leave Beth's side, but rested in the big chair, waking often to look at, touch, and brood over her child, like a miser over some recovered treasure.\n\nLaurie, meanwhile, posted off to comfort Amy, and told his story so well that Aunt March actually \"sniffed\" herself, and never once said, \"I told you so.\" Amy came out so strong on this occasion that I think the good thoughts in the little chapel really began to bear fruit. She dried her tears quickly, restrained her impatience to see her mother, and never even thought of the turquoise ring, when the old lady heartily agreed in Laurie's opinion, that she behaved \"like a capital little woman.\" Even Polly seemed impressed, for he called her \"good girl,\" blessed her buttons, and begged her to \"come and take a walk, dear,\" in his most affable tone. She would very gladly have gone out to enjoy the bright wintry weather; but, discovering that Laurie was dropping with sleep in spite of manful efforts to conceal the fact, she persuaded him to rest on the sofa, while she wrote a note to her mother. She was a long time about it; and, when she returned, he was stretched out, with both arms under his head, sound asleep, while Aunt March had pulled down the curtains, and sat doing nothing in an unusual fit of benignity.\n\nAfter a while, they began to think he was not going to wake till night, and I'm not sure that he would, had he not been effectually roused by Amy's cry of joy at sight of her mother. There probably were a good many happy little girls in and about the city that day, but it is my private opinion that Amy was the happiest of all, when she sat in her mother's lap and told her trials, receiving consolation and compensation in the shape of approving smiles and fond caresses. They were alone together in the chapel, to which her mother did not object when its purpose was explained to her.\n\n\"On the contrary, I like it very much, dear,\" looking from the dusty rosary to the well-worn little book, and the lovely picture with its garland of evergreen. \"It is an excellent plan to have some place where we can go to be quiet, when things vex or grieve us. There are a good many hard times in this life of ours, but we can always bear them if we ask help in the right way. I think my little girl is learning this?\"\n\n\"Yes, mother; and when I go home I mean to have a corner in the big closet to put my books, and the copy of that picture which I've tried to make. The woman's face is not good,--it's too beautiful for me to draw,--but the baby is done better, and I love it very much. I like to think He was a little child once, for then I don't seem so far away, and that helps me.\"\n\nAs Amy pointed to the smiling Christ-child on his mother's knee, Mrs. March saw something on the lifted hand that made her smile. She said nothing, but Amy understood the look, and, after a minute's pause, she added gravely,--\n\n\"I wanted to speak to you about this, but I forgot it. Aunt gave me the ring to-day; she called me to her and kissed me, and put it on my finger, and said I was a credit to her, and she'd like to keep me always. She gave that funny guard to keep the turquoise on, as it's too big. I'd like to wear them, mother; can I?\"\n\n\"They are very pretty, but I think you're rather too young for such ornaments, Amy,\" said Mrs. March, looking at the plump little hand, with the band of sky-blue stones on the forefinger, and the quaint guard, formed of two tiny, golden hands clasped together.\n\n\"I'll try not to be vain,\" said Amy. \"I don't think I like it only because it's so pretty; but I want to wear it as the girl in the story wore her bracelet, to remind me of something.\"\n\n\"Do you mean Aunt March?\" asked her mother, laughing.\n\n\"No, to remind me not to be selfish.\" Amy looked so earnest and sincere about it, that her mother stopped laughing, and listened respectfully to the little plan.\n\n\"I've thought a great deal lately about my 'bundle of naughties,' and being selfish is the largest one in it; so I'm going to try hard to cure it, if I can. Beth isn't selfish, and that's the reason every one loves her and feels so bad at the thoughts of losing her. People wouldn't feel half so bad about me if I was sick, and I don't deserve to have them; but I'd like to be loved and missed by a great many friends, so I'm going to try and be like Beth all I can. I'm apt to forget my resolutions; but if I had something always about me to remind me, I guess I should do better. May I try this way?\"\n\n\"Yes; but I have more faith in the corner of the big closet. Wear your ring, dear, and do your best; I think you will prosper, for the sincere wish to be good is half the battle. Now I must go back to Beth. Keep up your heart, little daughter, and we will soon have you home again.\"\n\nThat evening, while Meg was writing to her father, to report the traveller's safe arrival, Jo slipped up stairs into Beth's room, and, finding her mother in her usual place, stood a minute twisting her fingers in her hair, with a worried gesture and an undecided look.\n\n\"What is it, deary?\" asked Mrs. March, holding out her hand, with a face which invited confidence.\n\n\"I want to tell you something, mother.\"\n\n\"About Meg?\"\n\"How quickly you guessed! Yes, it's about her, and though it's a little thing, it fidgets me.\"\n\n\"Beth is asleep; speak low, and tell me all about it. That Moffat hasn't been here, I hope?\" asked Mrs. March rather sharply.\n\n\"No, I should have shut the door in his face if he had,\" said Jo, settling herself on the floor at her mother's feet. \"Last summer Meg left a pair of gloves over at the Laurences', and only one was returned. We forgot all about it, till Teddy told me that Mr. Brooke had it. He kept it in his waistcoat pocket, and once it fell out, and Teddy joked him about it, and Mr. Brooke owned that he liked Meg, but didn't dare say so, she was so young and he so poor. Now, isn't it a _dread_ful state of things?\"\n\n\"Do you think Meg cares for him?\" asked Mrs. March, with an anxious look.\n\n\"Mercy me! I don't know anything about love and such nonsense!\" cried Jo, with a funny mixture of interest and contempt. \"In novels, the girls show it by starting and blushing, fainting away, growing thin, and acting like fools. Now Meg does not do anything of the sort: she eats and drinks and sleeps, like a sensible creature: she looks straight in my face when I talk about that man, and only blushes a little bit when Teddy jokes about lovers. I forbid him to do it, but he doesn't mind me as he ought.\"\n\n\"Then you fancy that Meg is _not_ interested in John?\"\n\n\"Who?\" cried Jo, staring.\n\n\"Mr. Brooke. I call him 'John' now; we fell into the way of doing so at the hospital, and he likes it.\"\n\n\"Oh, dear! I know you'll take his part: he's been good to father, and you won't send him away, but let Meg marry him, if she wants to. Mean thing! to go petting papa and helping you, just to wheedle you into liking him;\" and Jo pulled her hair again with a wrathful tweak.\n\n\"My dear, don't get angry about it, and I will tell you how it happened. John went with me at Mr. Laurence's request, and was so devoted to poor father that we couldn't help getting fond of him. He was perfectly open and honorable about Meg, for he told us he loved her, but would earn a comfortable home before he asked her to marry him. He only wanted our leave to love her and work for her, and the right to make her love him if he could. He is a truly excellent young man, and we could not refuse to listen to him; but I will not consent to Meg's engaging herself so young.\"\n\n\"Of course not; it would be idiotic! I knew there was mischief brewing; I felt it; and now it's worse than I imagined. I just wish I could marry Meg myself, and keep her safe in the family.\"\n\nThis odd arrangement made Mrs. March smile; but she said gravely, \"Jo, I confide in you, and don't wish you to say anything to Meg yet. When John comes back, and I see them together, I can judge better of her feelings toward him.\"\n\n\"She'll see his in those handsome eyes that she talks about, and then it will be all up with her. She's got such a soft heart, it will melt like butter in the sun if any one looks sentimentally at her. She read the short reports he sent more than she did your letters, and pinched me when I spoke of it, and likes brown eyes, and doesn't think John an ugly name, and she'll go and fall in love, and there's an end of peace and fun, and cosy times together. I see it all! they'll go lovering around the house, and we shall have to dodge; Meg will be absorbed, and no good to me any more; Brooke will scratch up a fortune somehow, carry her off, and make a hole in the family; and I shall break my heart, and everything will be abominably uncomfortable. Oh, dear me! why weren't we all boys, then there wouldn't be any bother.\"\n\nJo leaned her chin on her knees, in a disconsolate attitude, and shook her fist at the reprehensible John. Mrs. March sighed, and Jo looked up with an air of relief.\n\n\"You don't like it, mother? I'm glad of it. Let's send him about his business, and not tell Meg a word of it, but all be happy together as we always have been.\"\n\n\"I did wrong to sigh, Jo. It is natural and right you should all go to homes of your own, in time; but I do want to keep my girls as long as I can; and I am sorry that this happened so soon, for Meg is only seventeen, and it will be some years before John can make a home for her. Your father and I have agreed that she shall not bind herself in any way, nor be married, before twenty. If she and John love one another, they can wait, and test the love by doing so. She is conscientious, and I have no fear of her treating him unkindly. My pretty, tender-hearted girl! I hope things will go happily with her.\"\n\n\"Hadn't you rather have her marry a rich man?\" asked Jo, as her mother's voice faltered a little over the last words.\n\n\"Money is a good and useful thing, Jo; and I hope my girls will never feel the need of it too bitterly, nor be tempted by too much. I should like to know that John was firmly established in some good business, which gave him an income large enough to keep free from debt and make Meg comfortable. I'm not ambitious for a splendid fortune, a fashionable position, or a great name for my girls. If rank and money come with love and virtue, also, I should accept them gratefully, and enjoy your good fortune; but I know, by experience, how much genuine happiness can be had in a plain little house, where the daily bread is earned, and some privations give sweetness to the few pleasures. I am content to see Meg begin humbly, for, if I am not mistaken, she will be rich in the possession of a good man's heart, and that is better than a fortune.\"\n\n\"I understand, mother, and quite agree; but I'm disappointed about Meg, for I'd planned to have her marry Teddy by and by, and sit in the lap of luxury all her days. Wouldn't it be nice?\" asked Jo, looking up, with a brighter face.\n\n\"He is younger than she, you know,\" began Mrs. March; but Jo broke in,--\n\n\"Only a little; he's old for his age, and tall; and can be quite grown-up in his manners if he likes. Then he's rich and generous and good, and loves us all; and _I_ say it's a pity my plan is spoilt.\"\n\n\"I'm afraid Laurie is hardly grown up enough for Meg, and altogether too much of a weathercock, just now, for any one to depend on. Don't make plans, Jo; but let time and their own hearts mate your friends. We can't meddle safely in such matters, and had better not get 'romantic rubbish,' as you call it, into our heads, lest it spoil our friendship.\"\n\n\"Well, I won't; but I hate to see things going all criss-cross and getting snarled up, when a pull here and a snip there would straighten it out. I wish wearing flat-irons on our heads would keep us from growing up. But buds will be roses, and kittens, cats,--more's the pity!\"\n\n\"What's that about flat-irons and cats?\" asked Meg, as she crept into the room, with the finished letter in her hand.\n\n\"Only one of my stupid speeches. I'm going to bed; come, Peggy,\" said Jo, unfolding herself, like an animated puzzle.\n\n\"Quite right, and beautifully written. Please add that I send my love to John,\" said Mrs. March, as she glanced over the letter, and gave it back.\n\n\"Do you call him 'John'?\" asked Meg, smiling, with her innocent eyes looking down into her mother's.\n\n\"Yes; he has been like a son to us, and we are very fond of him,\" replied Mrs. March, returning the look with a keen one.\n\n\"I'm glad of that, he is so lonely. Good-night, mother, dear. It is so inexpressibly comfortable to have you here,\" was Meg's quiet answer.\n\nThe kiss her mother gave her was a very tender one; and, as she went away, Mrs. March said, with a mixture of satisfaction and regret, \"She does not love John yet, but will soon learn to.\"\n\n# LAURIE MAKES MISCHIEF, AND JO MAKES PEACE.\n\nJo's face was a study next day, for the secret rather weighed upon her, and she found it hard not to look mysterious and important. Meg observed it, but did not trouble herself to make inquiries, for she had learned that the best way to manage Jo was by the law of contraries, so she felt sure of being told everything if she did not ask. She was rather surprised, therefore, when the silence remained unbroken, and Jo assumed a patronizing air, which decidedly aggravated Meg, who in her turn assumed an air of dignified reserve, and devoted herself to her mother. This left Jo to her own devices; for Mrs. March had taken her place as nurse, and bade her rest, exercise, and amuse herself after her long confinement. Amy being gone, Laurie was her only refuge; and, much as she enjoyed his society, she rather dreaded him just then, for he was an incorrigible tease, and she feared he would coax her secret from her.\n\nShe was quite right, for the mischief-loving lad no sooner suspected a mystery than he set himself to find it out, and led Jo a trying life of it. He wheedled, bribed, ridiculed, threatened, and scolded; affected indifference, that he might surprise the truth from her; declared he knew, then that he didn't care; and, at last, by dint of perseverance, he satisfied himself that it concerned Meg and Mr. Brooke. Feeling indignant that he was not taken into his tutor's confidence, he set his wits to work to devise some proper retaliation for the slight.\n\nMeg meanwhile had apparently forgotten the matter, and was absorbed in preparations for her father's return; but all of a sudden a change seemed to come over her, and, for a day or two, she was quite unlike herself. She started when spoken to, blushed when looked at, was very quiet, and sat over her sewing, with a timid, troubled look on her face. To her mother's inquiries she answered that she was quite well, and Jo's she silenced by begging to be let alone.\n\n\"She feels it in the air--love, I mean--and she's going very fast. She's got most of the symptoms,--is twittery and cross, doesn't eat, lies awake, and mopes in corners. I caught her singing that song he gave her, and once she said 'John,' as you do, and then turned as red as a poppy. Whatever shall we do?\" said Jo, looking ready for any measures, however violent.\n\n\"Nothing but wait. Let her alone, be kind and patient, and father's coming will settle everything,\" replied her mother.\n\n\"Here's a note to you, Meg, all sealed up. How odd! Teddy never seals mine,\" said Jo, next day, as she distributed the contents of the little post-office.\n\nMrs. March and Jo were deep in their own affairs, when a sound from Meg made them look up to see her staring at her note, with a frightened face.\n\n\"My child, what is it?\" cried her mother, running to her, while Jo tried to take the paper which had done the mischief.\n\n\"It's all a mistake--he didn't send it. O Jo, how could you do it?\" and Meg hid her face in her hands, crying as if her heart was quite broken.\n\n\"Me! I've done nothing! What's she talking about?\" cried Jo, bewildered.\n\nMeg's mild eyes kindled with anger as she pulled a crumpled note from her pocket, and threw it at Jo, saying reproachfully,--\n\n\"You wrote it, and that bad boy helped you. How could you be so rude, so mean, and cruel to us both?\"\n\nJo hardly heard her, for she and her mother were reading the note, which was written in a peculiar hand.\n\n\"I can no longer restrain my passion, and must know my fate before I return. I dare not tell your parents yet, but I think they would consent if they knew that we adored one another. Mr. Laurence will help me to some good place, and then, my sweet girl, you will make me happy. I implore you to say nothing to your family yet, but to send one word of hope through Laurie to \"Your devoted JOHN.\"\n\n\"Oh, the little villain! that's the way he meant to pay me for keeping my word to mother. I'll give him a hearty scolding, and bring him over to beg pardon,\" cried Jo, burning to execute immediate justice. But her mother held her back, saying, with a look she seldom wore,--\n\n\"Stop, Jo, you must clear yourself first. You have played so many pranks, that I am afraid you have had a hand in this.\"\n\n\"On my word, mother, I haven't! I never saw that note before, and don't know anything about it, as true as I live!\" said Jo, so earnestly that they believed her. \"If I _had_ taken a part in it I'd have done it better than this, and have written a sensible note. I should think you'd have known Mr. Brooke wouldn't write such stuff as that,\" she added, scornfully tossing down the paper.\n\n\"It's like his writing,\" faltered Meg, comparing it with the note in her hand.\n\n\"O Meg, you didn't answer it?\" cried Mrs. March quickly.\n\n\"Yes, I did!\" and Meg hid her face again, overcome with shame.\n\n\"Here's a scrape! _Do_ let me bring that wicked boy over to explain, and be lectured. I can't rest till I get hold of him;\" and Jo made for the door again.\n\n\"Hush! let me manage this, for it is worse than I thought. Margaret, tell me the whole story,\" commanded Mrs. March, sitting down by Meg, yet keeping hold of Jo, lest she should fly off.\n\n\"I received the first letter from Laurie, who didn't look as if he knew anything about it,\" began Meg, without looking up. \"I was worried at first, and meant to tell you; then I remembered how you liked Mr. Brooke, so I thought you wouldn't mind if I kept my little secret for a few days. I'm so silly that I liked to think no one knew; and, while I was deciding what to say, I felt like the girls in books, who have such things to do. Forgive me, mother, I'm paid for my silliness now; I never can look him in the face again.\"\n\n\"What did you say to him?\" asked Mrs. March.\n\n\"I only said I was too young to do anything about it yet; that I didn't wish to have secrets from you, and he must speak to father. I was very grateful for his kindness, and would be his friend, but nothing more, for a long while.\"\n\nMrs. March smiled, as if well pleased, and Jo clapped her hands, exclaiming, with a laugh,--\n\n\"You are almost equal to Caroline Percy, who was a pattern of prudence! Tell on, Meg. What did he say to that?\"\n\n\"He writes in a different way entirely, telling me that he never sent any love-letter at all, and is very sorry that my roguish sister, Jo, should take such liberties with our names. It's very kind and respectful, but think how dreadful for me!\"\n\nMeg leaned against her mother, looking the image of despair, and Jo tramped about the room, calling Laurie names. All of a sudden she stopped, caught up the two notes, and, after looking at them closely, said decidedly, \"I don't believe Brooke ever saw either of these letters. Teddy wrote both, and keeps yours to crow over me with, because I wouldn't tell him my secret.\"\n\n\"Don't have any secrets, Jo; tell it to mother, and keep out of trouble, as I should have done,\" said Meg warningly.\n\n\"Bless you, child! Mother told me.\"\n\n\"That will do, Jo. I'll comfort Meg while you go and get Laurie. I shall sift the matter to the bottom, and put a stop to such pranks at once.\"\n\nAway ran Jo, and Mrs. March gently told Meg Mr. Brooke's real feelings. \"Now, dear, what are your own? Do you love him enough to wait till he can make a home for you, or will you keep yourself quite free for the present?\"\n\n\"I've been so scared and worried, I don't want to have anything to do with lovers for a long while,--perhaps never,\" answered Meg petulantly. \"If John _doesn't_ know anything about this nonsense, don't tell him, and make Jo and Laurie hold their tongues. I won't be deceived and plagued and made a fool of,--it's a shame!\"\n\nSeeing that Meg's usually gentle temper was roused and her pride hurt by this mischievous joke, Mrs. March soothed her by promises of entire silence, and great discretion for the future. The instant Laurie's step was heard in the hall, Meg fled into the study, and Mrs. March received the culprit alone. Jo had not told him why he was wanted, fearing he wouldn't come; but he knew the minute he saw Mrs. March's face, and stood twirling his hat, with a guilty air which convicted him at once. Jo was dismissed, but chose to march up and down the hall like a sentinel, having some fear that the prisoner might bolt. The sound of voices in the parlor rose and fell for half an hour; but what happened during that interview the girls never knew.\n\nWhen they were called in, Laurie was standing by their mother, with such a penitent face that Jo forgave him on the spot, but did not think it wise to betray the fact. Meg received his humble apology, and was much comforted by the assurance that Brooke knew nothing of the joke.\n\n\"I'll never tell him to my dying day,--wild horses sha'n't drag it out of me; so you'll forgive me, Meg, and I'll do anything to show how out-and-out sorry I am,\" he added, looking very much ashamed of himself.\n\n\"I'll try; but it was a very ungentlemanly thing to do. I didn't think you could be so sly and malicious, Laurie,\" replied Meg, trying to hide her maidenly confusion under a gravely reproachful air.\n\n\"It was altogether abominable, and I don't deserve to be spoken to for a month; but you will, though, won't you?\" and Laurie folded his hands together with such an imploring gesture, as he spoke in his irresistibly persuasive tone, that it was impossible to frown upon him, in spite of his scandalous behavior. Meg pardoned him, and Mrs. March's grave face relaxed, in spite of her efforts to keep sober, when she heard him declare that he would atone for his sins by all sorts of penances, and abase himself like a worm before the injured damsel.\n\nJo stood aloof, meanwhile, trying to harden her heart against him, and succeeding only in primming up her face into an expression of entire disapprobation. Laurie looked at her once or twice, but, as she showed no sign of relenting, he felt injured, and turned his back on her till the others were done with him, when he made her a low bow, and walked off without a word.\n\nAs soon as he had gone, she wished she had been more forgiving; and when Meg and her mother went upstairs, she felt lonely, and longed for Teddy. After resisting for some time, she yielded to the impulse, and, armed with a book to return, went over to the big house.\n\n\"Is Mr. Laurence in?\" asked Jo, of a housemaid, who was coming down stairs.\n\n\"Yes, miss; but I don't believe he's seeable just yet.\"\n\n\"Why not? is he ill?\"\n\n\"La, no, miss, but he's had a scene with Mr. Laurie, who is in one of his tantrums about something, which vexes the old gentleman, so I dursn't go nigh him.\"\n\n\"Where is Laurie?\"\n\n\"Shut up in his room, and he won't answer, though I've been a-tapping. I don't know what's to become of the dinner, for it's ready, and there's no one to eat it.\"\n\n\"I'll go and see what the matter is. I'm not afraid of either of them.\"\n\nUp went Jo, and knocked smartly on the door of Laurie's little study.\n\n\"Stop that, or I'll open the door and make you!\" called out the young gentleman, in a threatening tone.\n\nJo immediately knocked again; the door flew open, and in she bounced, before Laurie could recover from his surprise. Seeing that he really _was_ out of temper, Jo, who knew how to manage him, assumed a contrite expression, and going artistically down upon her knees, said meekly, \"Please forgive me for being so cross. I came to make it up, and can't go away till I have.\"\n\n\"It's all right. Get up, and don't be a goose, Jo,\" was the cavalier reply to her petition.\n\n\"Thank you; I will. Could I ask what's the matter? You don't look exactly easy in your mind.\"\n\n\"I've been shaken, and I won't bear it!\" growled Laurie indignantly.\n\n\"Who did it?\" demanded Jo.\n\n\"Grandfather; if it had been any one else I'd have--\" and the injured youth finished his sentence by an energetic gesture of the right arm.\n\n\"That's nothing; I often shake you, and you don't mind,\" said Jo soothingly.\n\n\"Pooh! you're a girl, and it's fun; but I'll allow no man to shake _me_.\"\n\n\"I don't think any one would care to try it, if you looked as much like a thunder-cloud as you do now. Why were you treated so?\"\n\n\"Just because I wouldn't say what your mother wanted me for. I'd promised not to tell, and of course I wasn't going to break my word.\"\n\n\"Couldn't you satisfy your grandpa in any other way?\"\n\n\"No; he _would_ have the truth, the whole truth, and nothing but the truth. I'd have told my part of the scrape, if I could without bringing Meg in. As I couldn't, I held my tongue, and bore the scolding till the old gentleman collared me. Then I got angry, and bolted, for fear I should forget myself.\"\n\n\"It wasn't nice, but he's sorry, I know; so go down and make up. I'll help you.\"\n\n\"Hanged if I do! I'm not going to be lectured and pummelled by every one, just for a bit of a frolic. I _was_ sorry about Meg, and begged pardon like a man; but I won't do it again, when I wasn't in the wrong.\"\n\n\"He didn't know that.\"\n\n\"He ought to trust me, and not act as if I was a baby. It's no use, Jo; he's got to learn that I'm able to take care of myself, and don't need any one's apron-string to hold on by.\"\n\n\"What pepper-pots you are!\" sighed Jo. \"How do you mean to settle this affair?\"\n\n\"Well, he ought to beg pardon, and believe me when I say I can't tell him what the fuss's about.\"\n\n\"Bless you! he won't do that.\"\n\n\"I won't go down till he does.\"\n\n\"Now, Teddy, be sensible; let it pass, and I'll explain what I can. You can't stay here, so what's the use of being melodramatic?\"\n\n\"I don't intend to stay here long, any way. I'll slip off and take a journey somewhere, and when grandpa misses me he'll come round fast enough.\"\n\n\"I dare say; but you ought not to go and worry him.\"\n\n\"Don't preach. I'll go to Washington and see Brooke; it's gay there, and I'll enjoy myself after the troubles.\"\n\n\"What fun you'd have! I wish I could run off too,\" said Jo, forgetting her part of Mentor in lively visions of martial life at the capital.\n\n\"Come on, then! Why not? You go and surprise your father, and I'll stir up old Brooke. It would be a glorious joke; let's do it, Jo. We'll leave a letter saying we are all right, and trot off at once. I've got money enough; it will do you good, and be no harm, as you go to your father.\"\n\nFor a moment Jo looked as if she would agree; for, wild as the plan was, it just suited her. She was tired of care and confinement, longed for change, and thoughts of her father blended temptingly with the novel charms of camps and hospitals, liberty and fun. Her eyes kindled as they turned wistfully toward the window, but they fell on the old house opposite, and she shook her head with sorrowful decision.\n\n\"If I was a boy, we'd run away together, and have a capital time; but as I'm a miserable girl, I must be proper, and stop at home. Don't tempt me, Teddy, it's a crazy plan.\"\n\n\"That's the fun of it,\" began Laurie, who had got a wilful fit on him, and was possessed to break out of bounds in some way.\n\n\"Hold your tongue!\" cried Jo, covering her ears. \"'Prunes and prisms' are my doom, and I may as well make up my mind to it. I came here to moralize, not to hear about things that make me skip to think of.\"\n\n\"I know Meg would wet-blanket such a proposal, but I thought you had more spirit,\" began Laurie insinuatingly.\n\n\"Bad boy, be quiet! Sit down and think of your own sins, don't go making me add to mine. If I get your grandpa to apologize for the shaking, will you give up running away?\" asked Jo seriously.\n\n\"Yes, but you won't do it,\" answered Laurie, who wished \"to make up,\" but felt that his outraged dignity must be appeased first.\n\n\"If I can manage the young one I can the old one,\" muttered Jo, as she walked away, leaving Laurie bent over a railroad map, with his head propped up on both hands.\n\n\"Come in!\" and Mr. Laurence's gruff voice sounded gruffer than ever, as Jo tapped at his door.\n\n\"It's only me, sir, come to return a book,\" she said blandly, as she entered.\n\n\"Want any more?\" asked the old gentleman, looking grim and vexed, but trying not to show it.\n\n\"Yes, please. I like old Sam so well, I think I'll try the second volume,\" returned Jo, hoping to propitiate him by accepting a second dose of Boswell's \"Johnson,\" as he had recommended that lively work.\n\nThe shaggy eyebrows unbent a little, as he rolled the steps toward the shelf where the Johnsonian literature was placed. Jo skipped up, and, sitting on the top step, affected to be searching for her book, but was really wondering how best to introduce the dangerous object of her visit. Mr. Laurence seemed to suspect that something was brewing in her mind; for, after taking several brisk turns about the room, he faced round on her, speaking so abruptly that \"Rasselas\" tumbled face downward on the floor.\n\n\"What has that boy been about? Don't try to shield him. I know he has been in mischief by the way he acted when he came home. I can't get a word from him; and when I threatened to shake the truth out of him he bolted upstairs, and locked himself into his room.\"\n\n\"He did do wrong, but we forgave him, and all promised not to say a word to any one,\" began Jo reluctantly.\n\n\"That won't do; he shall not shelter himself behind a promise from you soft-hearted girls. If he's done anything amiss, he shall confess, beg pardon, and be punished. Out with it, Jo, I won't be kept in the dark.\"\n\nMr. Laurence looked so alarming and spoke so sharply that Jo would have gladly run away, if she could, but she was perched aloft on the steps, and he stood at the foot, a lion in the path, so she had to stay and brave it out.\n\n\"Indeed, sir, I cannot tell; mother forbade it. Laurie has confessed, asked pardon, and been punished quite enough. We don't keep silence to shield him, but some one else, and it will make more trouble if you interfere. Please don't; it was partly my fault, but it's all right now; so let's forget it, and talk about the 'Rambler,' or something pleasant.\"\n\n\"Hang the 'Rambler!' come down and give me your word that this harum-scarum boy of mine hasn't done anything ungrateful or impertinent. If he has, after all your kindness to him, I'll thrash him with my own hands.\"\n\nThe threat sounded awful, but did not alarm Jo, for she knew the irascible old gentleman would never lift a finger against his grandson, whatever he might say to the contrary. She obediently descended, and made as light of the prank as she could without betraying Meg or forgetting the truth.\n\n\"Hum--ha--well, if the boy held his tongue because he promised, and not from obstinacy, I'll forgive him. He's a stubborn fellow, and hard to manage,\" said Mr. Laurence, rubbing up his hair till it looked as if he had been out in a gale, and smoothing the frown from his brow with an air of relief.\n\n\"So am I; but a kind word will govern me when all the king's horses and all the king's men couldn't,\" said Jo, trying to say a kind word for her friend, who seemed to get out of one scrape only to fall into another.\n\n\"You think I'm not kind to him, hey?\" was the sharp answer.\n\n\"Oh, dear, no, sir; you are rather too kind sometimes, and then just a trifle hasty when he tries your patience. Don't you think you are?\"\n\nJo was determined to have it out now, and tried to look quite placid, though she quaked a little after her bold speech. To her great relief and surprise, the old gentleman only threw his spectacles on to the table with a rattle, and exclaimed frankly,--\n\n\"You're right, girl, I am! I love the boy, but he tries my patience past bearing, and I don't know how it will end, if we go on so.\"\n\n\"I'll tell you, he'll run away.\" Jo was sorry for that speech the minute it was made; she meant to warn him that Laurie would not bear much restraint, and hoped he would be more forbearing with the lad.\n\nMr. Laurence's ruddy face changed suddenly, and he sat down, with a troubled glance at the picture of a handsome man, which hung over his table. It was Laurie's father, who _had_ run away in his youth, and married against the imperious old man's will. Jo fancied he remembered and regretted the past, and she wished she had held her tongue.\n\n\"He won't do it unless he is very much worried, and only threatens it sometimes, when he gets tired of studying. I often think I should like to, especially since my hair was cut; so, if you ever miss us, you may advertise for two boys, and look among the ships bound for India.\"\n\nShe laughed as she spoke, and Mr. Laurence looked relieved, evidently taking the whole as a joke.\n\n\"You hussy, how dare you talk in that way? Where's your respect for me, and your proper bringing up? Bless the boys and girls! What torments they are; yet we can't do without them,\" he said, pinching her cheeks good-humoredly. \"Go and bring that boy down to his dinner, tell him it's all right, and advise him not to put on tragedy airs with his grandfather. I won't bear it.\"\n\n\"He won't come, sir; he feels badly because you didn't believe him when he said he couldn't tell. I think the shaking hurt his feelings very much.\"\n\nJo tried to look pathetic, but must have failed, for Mr. Laurence began to laugh, and she knew the day was won.\n\n\"I'm sorry for that, and ought to thank him for not shaking _me_, I suppose. What the dickens does the fellow expect?\" and the old gentleman looked a trifle ashamed of his own testiness.\n\n\"If I were you, I'd write him an apology, sir. He says he won't come down till he has one, and talks about Washington, and goes on in an absurd way. A formal apology will make him see how foolish he is, and bring him down quite amiable. Try it; he likes fun, and this way is better than talking. I'll carry it up, and teach him his duty.\"\n\nMr. Laurence gave her a sharp look, and put on his spectacles, saying slowly, \"You're a sly puss, but I don't mind being managed by you and Beth. Here, give me a bit of paper, and let us have done with this nonsense.\"\n\nThe note was written in the terms which one gentleman would use to another after offering some deep insult. Jo dropped a kiss on the top of Mr. Laurence's bald head, and ran up to slip the apology under Laurie's door, advising him, through the key-hole, to be submissive, decorous, and a few other agreeable impossibilities. Finding the door locked again, she left the note to do its work, and was going quietly away, when the young gentleman slid down the banisters, and waited for her at the bottom, saying, with his most virtuous expression of countenance, \"What a good fellow you are, Jo! Did you get blown up?\" he added, laughing.\n\n\"No; he was pretty mild, on the whole.\"\n\n\"Ah! I got it all round; even you cast me off over there, and I felt just ready to go to the deuce,\" he began apologetically.\n\n\"Don't talk in that way; turn over a new leaf and begin again, Teddy, my son.\"\n\n\"I keep turning over new leaves, and spoiling them, as I used to spoil my copy-books; and I make so many beginnings there never will be an end,\" he said dolefully.\n\n\"Go and eat your dinner; you'll feel better after it. Men always croak when they are hungry,\" and Jo whisked out at the front door after that.\n\n\"That's a 'label' on my 'sect,'\" answered Laurie, quoting Amy, as he went to partake of humble-pie dutifully with his grandfather, who was quite saintly in temper and overwhelmingly respectful in manner all the rest of the day.\n\nEvery one thought the matter ended and the little cloud blown over; but the mischief was done, for, though others forgot it, Meg remembered. She never alluded to a certain person, but she thought of him a good deal, dreamed dreams more than ever; and once Jo, rummaging her sister's desk for stamps, found a bit of paper scribbled over with the words, \"Mrs. John Brooke;\" whereat she groaned tragically, and cast it into the fire, feeling that Laurie's prank had hastened the evil day for her.\n\n# PLEASANT MEADOWS.\n\nLike sunshine after storm were the peaceful weeks which followed. The invalids improved rapidly, and Mr. March began to talk of returning early in the new year. Beth was soon able to lie on the study sofa all day, amusing herself with the well-beloved cats, at first, and, in time, with doll's sewing, which had fallen sadly behindhand. Her once active limbs were so stiff and feeble that Jo took her a daily airing about the house in her strong arms. Meg cheerfully blackened and burnt her white hands cooking delicate messes for \"the dear;\" while Amy, a loyal slave of the ring, celebrated her return by giving away as many of her treasures as she could prevail on her sisters to accept.\n\nAs Christmas approached, the usual mysteries began to haunt the house, and Jo frequently convulsed the family by proposing utterly impossible or magnificently absurd ceremonies, in honor of this unusually merry Christmas. Laurie was equally impracticable, and would have had bonfires, sky-rockets, and triumphal arches, if he had had his own way. After many skirmishes and snubbings, the ambitious pair were considered effectually quenched, and went about with forlorn faces, which were rather belied by explosions of laughter when the two got together.\n\nSeveral days of unusually mild weather fitly ushered in a splendid Christmas Day. Hannah \"felt in her bones\" that it was going to be an unusually fine day, and she proved herself a true prophetess, for everybody and everything seemed bound to produce a grand success. To begin with, Mr. March wrote that he should soon be with them; then Beth felt uncommonly well that morning, and, being dressed in her mother's gift,--a soft crimson merino wrapper,--was borne in triumph to the window to behold the offering of Jo and Laurie. The Unquenchables had done their best to be worthy of the name, for, like elves, they had worked by night, and conjured up a comical surprise. Out in the garden stood a stately snow-maiden, crowned with holly, bearing a basket of fruit and flowers in one hand, a great roll of new music in the other, a perfect rainbow of an Afghan round her chilly shoulders, and a Christmas carol issuing from her lips, on a pink paper streamer:--\n\n\"God bless you, dear Queen Bess! May nothing you dismay, But health and peace and happiness Be yours, this Christmas Day.\n\n\"Here's fruit to feed our busy bee, And flowers for her nose; Here's music for her pianee, An Afghan for her toes.\n\n\"A portrait of Joanna, see, By Raphael No. 2, Who labored with great industry To make it fair and true.\n\n\"Accept a ribbon red, I beg, For Madam Purrer's tail; And ice-cream made by lovely Peg,-- A Mont Blanc in a pail.\n\n\"Their dearest love my makers laid Within my breast of snow: Accept it, and the Alpine maid, From Laurie and from Jo.\"\n\nHow Beth laughed when she saw it, how Laurie ran up and down to bring in the gifts, and what ridiculous speeches Jo made as she presented them!\n\n\"I'm so full of happiness, that, if father was only here, I couldn't hold one drop more,\" said Beth, quite sighing with contentment as Jo carried her off to the study to rest after the excitement, and to refresh herself with some of the delicious grapes the \"Jungfrau\" had sent her.\n\n\"So am I,\" added Jo, slapping the pocket wherein reposed the long-desired Undine and Sintram.\n\n\"I'm sure I am,\" echoed Amy, poring over the engraved copy of the Madonna and Child, which her mother had given her, in a pretty frame.\n\n\"Of course I am!\" cried Meg, smoothing the silvery folds of her first silk dress; for Mr. Laurence had insisted on giving it.\n\n\"How can _I_ be otherwise?\" said Mrs. March gratefully, as her eyes went from her husband's letter to Beth's smiling face, and her hand caressed the brooch made of gray and golden, chestnut and dark brown hair, which the girls had just fastened on her breast.\n\nNow and then, in this work-a-day world, things do happen in the delightful story-book fashion, and what a comfort that is. Half an hour after every one had said they were so happy they could only hold one drop more, the drop came. Laurie opened the parlor door, and popped his head in very quietly. He might just as well have turned a somersault and uttered an Indian war-whoop; for his face was so full of suppressed excitement and his voice so treacherously joyful, that every one jumped up, though he only said, in a queer, breathless voice, \"Here's another Christmas present for the March family.\"\n\nBefore the words were well out of his mouth, he was whisked away somehow, and in his place appeared a tall man, muffled up to the eyes, leaning on the arm of another tall man, who tried to say something and couldn't. Of course there was a general stampede; and for several minutes everybody seemed to lose their wits, for the strangest things were done, and no one said a word. Mr. March became invisible in the embrace of four pairs of loving arms; Jo disgraced herself by nearly fainting away, and had to be doctored by Laurie in the china-closet; Mr. Brooke kissed Meg entirely by mistake, as he somewhat incoherently explained; and Amy, the dignified, tumbled over a stool, and, never stopping to get up, hugged and cried over her father's boots in the most touching manner. Mrs. March was the first to recover herself, and held up her hand with a warning, \"Hush! remember Beth!\"\n\nBut it was too late; the study door flew open, the little red wrapper appeared on the threshold,--joy put strength into the feeble limbs,--and Beth ran straight into her father's arms. Never mind what happened just after that; for the full hearts overflowed, washing away the bitterness of the past, and leaving only the sweetness of the present.\n\nIt was not at all romantic, but a hearty laugh set everybody straight again, for Hannah was discovered behind the door, sobbing over the fat turkey, which she had forgotten to put down when she rushed up from the kitchen. As the laugh subsided, Mrs. March began to thank Mr. Brooke for his faithful care of her husband, at which Mr. Brooke suddenly remembered that Mr. March needed rest, and, seizing Laurie, he precipitately retired. Then the two invalids were ordered to repose, which they did, by both sitting in one big chair, and talking hard.\n\nMr. March told how he had longed to surprise them, and how, when the fine weather came, he had been allowed by his doctor to take advantage of it; how devoted Brooke had been, and how he was altogether a most estimable and upright young man. Why Mr. March paused a minute just there, and, after a glance at Meg, who was violently poking the fire, looked at his wife with an inquiring lift of the eyebrows, I leave you to imagine; also why Mrs. March gently nodded her head, and asked, rather abruptly, if he wouldn't have something to eat. Jo saw and understood the look; and she stalked grimly away to get wine and beef-tea, muttering to herself, as she slammed the door, \"I hate estimable young men with brown eyes!\"\n\nThere never _was_ such a Christmas dinner as they had that day. The fat turkey was a sight to behold, when Hannah sent him up, stuffed, browned, and decorated; so was the plum-pudding, which quite melted in one's mouth; likewise the jellies, in which Amy revelled like a fly in a honey-pot. Everything turned out well, which was a mercy, Hannah said, \"For my mind was that flustered, mum, that it's a merrycle I didn't roast the pudding, and stuff the turkey with raisins, let alone bilin' of it in a cloth.\"\n\nMr. Laurence and his grandson dined with them, also Mr. Brooke,--at whom Jo glowered darkly, to Laurie's infinite amusement. Two easy-chairs stood side by side at the head of the table, in which sat Beth and her father, feasting modestly on chicken and a little fruit. They drank healths, told stories, sung songs, \"reminisced,\" as the old folks say, and had a thoroughly good time. A sleigh-ride had been planned, but the girls would not leave their father; so the guests departed early, and, as twilight gathered, the happy family sat together round the fire.\n\n\"Just a year ago we were groaning over the dismal Christmas we expected to have. Do you remember?\" asked Jo, breaking a short pause which had followed a long conversation about many things.\n\n\"Rather a pleasant year on the whole!\" said Meg, smiling at the fire, and congratulating herself on having treated Mr. Brooke with dignity.\n\n\"I think it's been a pretty hard one,\" observed Amy, watching the light shine on her ring, with thoughtful eyes.\n\n\"I'm glad it's over, because we've got you back,\" whispered Beth, who sat on her father's knee.\n\n\"Rather a rough road for you to travel, my little pilgrims, especially the latter part of it. But you have got on bravely; and I think the burdens are in a fair way to tumble off very soon,\" said Mr. March, looking with fatherly satisfaction at the four young faces gathered round him.\n\n\"How do you know? Did mother tell you?\" asked Jo.\n\n\"Not much; straws show which way the wind blows, and I've made several discoveries to-day.\"\n\n\"Oh, tell us what they are!\" cried Meg, who sat beside him.\n\n\"Here is one;\" and taking up the hand which lay on the arm of his chair, he pointed to the roughened forefinger, a burn on the back, and two or three little hard spots on the palm. \"I remember a time when this hand was white and smooth, and your first care was to keep it so. It was very pretty then, but to me it is much prettier now,--for in these seeming blemishes I read a little history. A burnt-offering has been made of vanity; this hardened palm has earned something better than blisters; and I'm sure the sewing done by these pricked fingers will last a long time, so much good-will went into the stitches. Meg, my dear, I value the womanly skill which keeps home happy more than white hands or fashionable accomplishments. I'm proud to shake this good, industrious little hand, and hope I shall not soon be asked to give it away.\"\n\nIf Meg had wanted a reward for hours of patient labor, she received it in the hearty pressure of her father's hand and the approving smile he gave her.\n\n\"What about Jo? Please say something nice; for she has tried so hard, and been so very, very good to me,\" said Beth, in her father's ear.\n\nHe laughed, and looked across at the tall girl who sat opposite, with an unusually mild expression in her brown face.\n\n\"In spite of the curly crop, I don't see the 'son Jo' whom I left a year ago,\" said Mr. March. \"I see a young lady who pins her collar straight, laces her boots neatly, and neither whistles, talks slang, nor lies on the rug as she used to do. Her face is rather thin and pale, just now, with watching and anxiety; but I like to look at it, for it has grown gentler, and her voice is lower; she doesn't bounce, but moves quietly, and takes care of a certain little person in a motherly way which delights me. I rather miss my wild girl; but if I get a strong, helpful, tender-hearted woman in her place, I shall feel quite satisfied. I don't know whether the shearing sobered our black sheep, but I do know that in all Washington I couldn't find anything beautiful enough to be bought with the five-and-twenty dollars which my good girl sent me.\"\n\nJo's keen eyes were rather dim for a minute, and her thin face grew rosy in the firelight, as she received her father's praise, feeling that she did deserve a portion of it.\n\n\"Now Beth,\" said Amy, longing for her turn, but ready to wait.\n\n\"There's so little of her, I'm afraid to say much, for fear she will slip away altogether, though she is not so shy as she used to be,\" began their father cheerfully; but recollecting how nearly he _had_ lost her, he held her close, saying tenderly, with her cheek against his own, \"I've got you safe, my Beth, and I'll keep you so, please God.\"\n\nAfter a minute's silence, he looked down at Amy, who sat on the cricket at his feet, and said, with a caress of the shining hair,--\n\n\"I observed that Amy took drumsticks at dinner, ran errands for her mother all the afternoon, gave Meg her place to-night, and has waited on every one with patience and good-humor. I also observe that she does not fret much nor look in the glass, and has not even mentioned a very pretty ring which she wears; so I conclude that she has learned to think of other people more and of herself less, and has decided to try and mould her character as carefully as she moulds her little clay figures. I am glad of this; for though I should be very proud of a graceful statue made by her, I shall be infinitely prouder of a lovable daughter, with a talent for making life beautiful to herself and others.\"\n\n\"What are you thinking of, Beth?\" asked Jo, when Amy had thanked her father and told about her ring.\n\n\"I read in 'Pilgrim's Progress' to-day, how, after many troubles, Christian and Hopeful came to a pleasant green meadow, where lilies bloomed all the year round, and there they rested happily, as we do now, before they went on to their journey's end,\" answered Beth; adding, as she slipped out of her father's arms, and went slowly to the instrument, \"It's singing time now, and I want to be in my old place. I'll try to sing the song of the shepherd-boy which the Pilgrims heard. I made the music for father, because he likes the verses.\"\n\nSo, sitting at the dear little piano, Beth softly touched the keys, and, in the sweet voice they had never thought to hear again, sung to her own accompaniment the quaint hymn, which was a singularly fitting song for her:--\n\n\"He that is down need fear no fall, He that is low no pride; He that is humble ever shall Have God to be his guide.\n\n\"I am content with what I have, Little be it or much; And, Lord! contentment still I crave, Because Thou savest such.\n\n\"Fulness to them a burden is, That go on pilgrimage; Here little, and hereafter bliss, Is best from age to age!\"\n\n# AUNT MARCH SETTLES THE QUESTION.\n\nLike bees swarming after their queen, mother and daughters hovered about Mr. March the next day, neglecting everything to look at, wait upon, and listen to the new invalid, who was in a fair way to be killed by kindness. As he sat propped up in a big chair by Beth's sofa, with the other three close by, and Hannah popping in her head now and then, \"to peek at the dear man,\" nothing seemed needed to complete their happiness. But something _was_ needed, and the elder ones felt it, though none confessed the fact. Mr. and Mrs. March looked at one another with an anxious expression, as their eyes followed Meg. Jo had sudden fits of sobriety, and was seen to shake her fist at Mr. Brooke's umbrella, which had been left in the hall; Meg was absent-minded, shy, and silent, started when the bell rang, and colored when John's name was mentioned; Amy said \"Every one seemed waiting for something, and couldn't settle down, which was queer, since father was safe at home,\" and Beth innocently wondered why their neighbors didn't run over as usual.\n\nLaurie went by in the afternoon, and, seeing Meg at the window, seemed suddenly possessed with a melodramatic fit, for he fell down upon one knee in the snow, beat his breast, tore his hair, and clasped his hands imploringly, as if begging some boon; and when Meg told him to behave himself and go away, he wrung imaginary tears out of his handkerchief, and staggered round the corner as if in utter despair.\n\n\"What does the goose mean?\" said Meg, laughing, and trying to look unconscious.\n\n\"He's showing you how your John will go on by and by. Touching, isn't it?\" answered Jo scornfully.\n\n\"Don't say _my John_, it isn't proper or true;\" but Meg's voice lingered over the words as if they sounded pleasant to her. \"Please don't plague me, Jo; I've told you I don't care _much_ about him, and there isn't to be anything said, but we are all to be friendly, and go on as before.\"\n\n\"We can't, for something _has_ been said, and Laurie's mischief has spoilt you for me. I see it, and so does mother; you are not like your old self a bit, and seem ever so far away from me. I don't mean to plague you, and will bear it like a man, but I do wish it was all settled. I hate to wait; so if you mean ever to do it, make haste and have it over quickly,\" said Jo pettishly.\n\n\"_I_ can't say or do anything till he speaks, and he won't, because father said I was too young,\" began Meg, bending over her work, with a queer little smile, which suggested that she did not quite agree with her father on that point.\n\n\"If he did speak, you wouldn't know what to say, but would cry or blush, or let him have his own way, instead of giving a good, decided, No.\"\n\n\"I'm not so silly and weak as you think. I know just what I should say, for I've planned it all, so I needn't be taken unawares; there's no knowing what may happen, and I wished to be prepared.\"\n\nJo couldn't help smiling at the important air which Meg had unconsciously assumed, and which was as becoming as the pretty color varying in her cheeks.\n\n\"Would you mind telling me what you'd say?\" asked Jo more respectfully.\n\n\"Not at all; you are sixteen now, quite old enough to be my confidant, and my experience will be useful to you by and by, perhaps, in your own affairs of this sort.\"\n\n\"Don't mean to have any; it's fun to watch other people philander, but I should feel like a fool doing it myself,\" said Jo, looking alarmed at the thought.\n\n\"I think not, if you liked any one very much, and he liked you.\" Meg spoke as if to herself, and glanced out at the lane, where she had often seen lovers walking together in the summer twilight.\n\n\"I thought you were going to tell your speech to that man,\" said Jo, rudely shortening her sister's little reverie.\n\n\"Oh, I should merely say, quite calmly and decidedly, 'Thank you, Mr. Brooke, you are very kind, but I agree with father that I am too young to enter into any engagement at present; so please say no more, but let us be friends as we were.'\"\n\n\"Hum! that's stiff and cool enough. I don't believe you'll ever say it, and I know he won't be satisfied if you do. If he goes on like the rejected lovers in books, you'll give in, rather than hurt his feelings.\"\n\n\"No, I won't. I shall tell him I've made up my mind, and shall walk out of the room with dignity.\"\n\nMeg rose as she spoke, and was just going to rehearse the dignified exit, when a step in the hall made her fly into her seat, and begin to sew as if her life depended on finishing that particular seam in a given time. Jo smothered a laugh at the sudden change, and, when some one gave a modest tap, opened the door with a grim aspect, which was anything but hospitable.\n\n\"Good afternoon. I came to get my umbrella,--that is, to see how your father finds himself to-day,\" said Mr. Brooke, getting a trifle confused as his eye went from one tell-tale face to the other.\n\n\"It's very well, he's in the rack, I'll get him, and tell it you are here,\" and having jumbled her father and the umbrella well together in her reply, Jo slipped out of the room to give Meg a chance to make her speech and air her dignity. But the instant she vanished, Meg began to sidle towards the door, murmuring,--\n\n\"Mother will like to see you. Pray sit down, I'll call her.\"\n\n\"Don't go; are you afraid of me, Margaret?\" and Mr. Brooke looked so hurt that Meg thought she must have done something very rude. She blushed up to the little curls on her forehead, for he had never called her Margaret before, and she was surprised to find how natural and sweet it seemed to hear him say it. Anxious to appear friendly and at her ease, she put out her hand with a confiding gesture, and said gratefully,--\n\n\"How can I be afraid when you have been so kind to father? I only wish I could thank you for it.\"\n\n\"Shall I tell you how?\" asked Mr. Brooke, holding the small hand fast in both his own, and looking down at Meg with so much love in the brown eyes, that her heart began to flutter, and she both longed to run away and to stop and listen.\n\n\"Oh no, please don't--I'd rather not,\" she said, trying to withdraw her hand, and looking frightened in spite of her denial.\n\n\"I won't trouble you, I only want to know if you care for me a little, Meg. I love you so much, dear,\" added Mr. Brooke tenderly.\n\nThis was the moment for the calm, proper speech, but Meg didn't make it; she forgot every word of it, hung her head, and answered, \"I don't know,\" so softly, that John had to stoop down to catch the foolish little reply.\n\nHe seemed to think it was worth the trouble, for he smiled to himself as if quite satisfied, pressed the plump hand gratefully, and said, in his most persuasive tone, \"Will you try and find out? I want to know _so_ much; for I can't go to work with any heart until I learn whether I am to have my reward in the end or not.\"\n\n\"I'm too young,\" faltered Meg, wondering why she was so fluttered, yet rather enjoying it.\n\n\"I'll wait; and in the meantime, you could be learning to like me. Would it be a very hard lesson, dear?\"\n\n\"Not if I chose to learn it, but--\"\n\n\"Please choose to learn, Meg. I love to teach, and this is easier than German,\" broke in John, getting possession of the other hand, so that she had no way of hiding her face, as he bent to look into it.\n\nHis tone was properly beseeching; but, stealing a shy look at him, Meg saw that his eyes were merry as well as tender, and that he wore the satisfied smile of one who had no doubt of his success. This nettled her; Annie Moffat's foolish lessons in coquetry came into her mind, and the love of power, which sleeps in the bosoms of the best of little women, woke up all of a sudden and took possession of her. She felt excited and strange, and, not knowing what else to do, followed a capricious impulse, and, withdrawing her hands, said petulantly, \"I _don't_ choose. Please go away and let me be!\"\n\nPoor Mr. Brooke looked as if his lovely castle in the air was tumbling about his ears, for he had never seen Meg in such a mood before, and it rather bewildered him.\n\n\"Do you really mean that?\" he asked anxiously, following her as she walked away.\n\n\"Yes, I do; I don't want to be worried about such things. Father says I needn't; it's too soon and I'd rather not.\"\n\n\"Mayn't I hope you'll change your mind by and by? I'll wait, and say nothing till you have had more time. Don't play with me, Meg. I didn't think that of you.\"\n\n\"Don't think of me at all. I'd rather you wouldn't,\" said Meg, taking a naughty satisfaction in trying her lover's patience and her own power.\n\nHe was grave and pale now, and looked decidedly more like the novel heroes whom she admired; but he neither slapped his forehead nor tramped about the room, as they did; he just stood looking at her so wistfully, so tenderly, that she found her heart relenting in spite of her. What would have happened next I cannot say, if Aunt March had not come hobbling in at this interesting minute.\n\nThe old lady couldn't resist her longing to see her nephew; for she had met Laurie as she took her airing, and, hearing of Mr. March's arrival, drove straight out to see him. The family were all busy in the back part of the house, and she had made her way quietly in, hoping to surprise them. She did surprise two of them so much that Meg started as if she had seen a ghost, and Mr. Brooke vanished into the study.\n\n\"Bless me, what's all this?\" cried the old lady, with a rap of her cane, as she glanced from the pale young gentleman to the scarlet young lady.\n\n\"It's father's friend. I'm _so_ surprised to see you!\" stammered Meg, feeling that she was in for a lecture now.\n\n\"That's evident,\" returned Aunt March, sitting down. \"But what is father's friend saying to make you look like a peony? There's mischief going on, and I insist upon knowing what it is,\" with another rap.\n\n\"We were merely talking. Mr. Brooke came for his umbrella,\" began Meg, wishing that Mr. Brooke and the umbrella were safely out of the house.\n\n\"Brooke? That boy's tutor? Ah! I understand now. I know all about it. Jo blundered into a wrong message in one of your father's letters, and I made her tell me. You haven't gone and accepted him, child?\" cried Aunt March, looking scandalized.\n\n\"Hush! he'll hear. Sha'n't I call mother?\" said Meg, much troubled.\n\n\"Not yet. I've something to say to you, and I must free my mind at once. Tell me, do you mean to marry this Cook? If you do, not one penny of my money ever goes to you. Remember that, and be a sensible girl,\" said the old lady impressively.\n\nNow Aunt March possessed in perfection the art of rousing the spirit of opposition in the gentlest people, and enjoyed doing it. The best of us have a spice of perversity in us, especially when we are young and in love. If Aunt March had begged Meg to accept John Brooke, she would probably have declared she couldn't think of it; but as she was peremptorily ordered _not_ to like him, she immediately made up her mind that she would. Inclination as well as perversity made the decision easy, and, being already much excited, Meg opposed the old lady with unusual spirit.\n\n\"I shall marry whom I please, Aunt March, and you can leave your money to any one you like,\" she said, nodding her head with a resolute air.\n\n\"Highty tighty! Is that the way you take my advice, miss? You'll be sorry for it, by and by, when you've tried love in a cottage, and found it a failure.\"\n\n\"It can't be a worse one than some people find in big houses,\" retorted Meg.\n\nAunt March put on her glasses and took a look at the girl, for she did not know her in this new mood. Meg hardly knew herself, she felt so brave and independent,--so glad to defend John, and assert her right to love him, if she liked. Aunt March saw that she had begun wrong, and, after a little pause, made a fresh start, saying, as mildly as she could, \"Now, Meg, my dear, be reasonable, and take my advice. I mean it kindly, and don't want you to spoil your whole life by making a mistake at the beginning. You ought to marry well, and help your family; it's your duty to make a rich match, and it ought to be impressed upon you.\"\n\n\"Father and mother don't think so; they like John, though he _is_ poor.\"\n\n\"Your parents, my dear, have no more worldly wisdom than two babies.\"\n\n\"I'm glad of it,\" cried Meg stoutly.\n\nAunt March took no notice, but went on with her lecture. \"This Rook is poor, and hasn't got any rich relations, has he?\"\n\n\"No; but he has many warm friends.\"\n\n\"You can't live on friends; try it, and see how cool they'll grow. He hasn't any business, has he?\"\n\n\"Not yet; Mr. Laurence is going to help him.\"\n\n\"That won't last long. James Laurence is a crotchety old fellow, and not to be depended on. So you intend to marry a man without money, position, or business, and go on working harder than you do now, when you might be comfortable all your days by minding me and doing better? I thought you had more sense, Meg.\"\n\n\"I couldn't do better if I waited half my life! John is good and wise; he's got heaps of talent; he's willing to work, and sure to get on, he's so energetic and brave. Every one likes and respects him, and I'm proud to think he cares for me, though I'm so poor and young and silly,\" said Meg, looking prettier than ever in her earnestness.\n\n\"He knows _you_ have got rich relations, child; that's the secret of his liking, I suspect.\"\n\n\"Aunt March, how dare you say such a thing? John is above such meanness, and I won't listen to you a minute if you talk so,\" cried Meg indignantly, forgetting everything but the injustice of the old lady's suspicions. \"My John wouldn't marry for money, anymore than I would. We are willing to work, and we mean to wait. I'm not afraid of being poor, for I've been happy so far, and I know I shall be with him, because he loves me, and I--\"\n\nMeg stopped there, remembering all of a sudden that she hadn't made up her mind; that she had told \"her John\" to go away, and that he might be overhearing her inconsistent remarks.\n\nAunt March was very angry, for she had set her heart on having her pretty niece make a fine match, and something in the girl's happy young face made the lonely old woman feel both sad and sour.\n\n\"Well, I wash my hands of the whole affair! You are a wilful child, and you've lost more than you know by this piece of folly. No, I won't stop; I'm disappointed in you, and haven't spirits to see your father now. Don't expect anything from me when you are married; your Mr. Book's friends must take care of you. I'm done with you forever.\"\n\nAnd, slamming the door in Meg's face, Aunt March drove off in high dudgeon. She seemed to take all the girl's courage with her; for, when left alone, Meg stood a moment, undecided whether to laugh or cry. Before she could make up her mind, she was taken possession of by Mr. Brooke, who said, all in one breath, \"I couldn't help hearing, Meg. Thank you for defending me, and Aunt March for proving that you _do_ care for me a little bit.\"\n\n\"I didn't know how much, till she abused you,\" began Meg.\n\n\"And I needn't go away, but may stay and be happy, may I, dear?\"\n\nHere was another fine chance to make the crushing speech and the stately exit, but Meg never thought of doing either, and disgraced herself forever in Jo's eyes by meekly whispering, \"Yes, John,\" and hiding her face on Mr. Brooke's waistcoat.\n\nFifteen minutes after Aunt March's departure, Jo came softly down stairs, paused an instant at the parlor door, and, hearing no sound within, nodded and smiled, with a satisfied expression, saying to herself, \"She has sent him away as we planned, and that affair is settled. I'll go and hear the fun, and have a good laugh over it.\"\n\nBut poor Jo never got her laugh, for she was transfixed upon the threshold by a spectacle which held her there, staring with her mouth nearly as wide open as her eyes. Going in to exult over a fallen enemy, and to praise a strong-minded sister for the banishment of an objectionable lover, it certainly _was_ a shock to behold the aforesaid enemy serenely sitting on the sofa, with the strong-minded sister enthroned upon his knee, and wearing an expression of the most abject submission. Jo gave a sort of gasp, as if a cold shower-bath had suddenly fallen upon her,--for such an unexpected turning of the tables actually took her breath away. At the odd sound, the lovers turned and saw her. Meg jumped up, looking both proud and shy; but \"that man,\" as Jo called him, actually laughed, and said coolly, as he kissed the astonished new-comer, \"Sister Jo, congratulate us!\"\n\nThat was adding insult to injury,--it was altogether too much,--and, making some wild demonstration with her hands, Jo vanished without a word. Rushing upstairs, she startled the invalids by exclaiming tragically, as she burst into the room, \"Oh, _do_ somebody go down quick; John Brooke is acting dreadfully, and Meg likes it!\"\n\nMr. and Mrs. March left the room with speed; and, casting herself upon the bed, Jo cried and scolded tempestuously as she told the awful news to Beth and Amy. The little girls, however, considered it a most agreeable and interesting event, and Jo got little comfort from them; so she went up to her refuge in the garret, and confided her troubles to the rats.\n\nNobody ever knew what went on in the parlor that afternoon; but a great deal of talking was done, and quiet Mr. Brooke astonished his friends by the eloquence and spirit with which he pleaded his suit, told his plans, and persuaded them to arrange everything just as he wanted it.\n\nThe tea-bell rang before he had finished describing the paradise which he meant to earn for Meg, and he proudly took her in to supper, both looking so happy that Jo hadn't the heart to be jealous or dismal. Amy was very much impressed by John's devotion and Meg's dignity. Beth beamed at them from a distance, while Mr. and Mrs. March surveyed the young couple with such tender satisfaction that it was perfectly evident Aunt March was right in calling them as \"unworldly as a pair of babies.\" No one ate much, but every one looked very happy, and the old room seemed to brighten up amazingly when the first romance of the family began there.\n\n\"You can't say nothing pleasant ever happens now, can you, Meg?\" said Amy, trying to decide how she would group the lovers in the sketch she was planning to take.\n\n\"No, I'm sure I can't. How much has happened since I said that! It seems a year ago,\" answered Meg, who was in a blissful dream, lifted far above such common things as bread and butter.\n\n\"The joys come close upon the sorrows this time, and I rather think the changes have begun,\" said Mrs. March. \"In most families there comes, now and then, a year full of events; this has been such an one, but it ends well, after all.\"\n\n\"Hope the next will end better,\" muttered Jo, who found it very hard to see Meg absorbed in a stranger before her face; for Jo loved a few persons very dearly, and dreaded to have their affection lost or lessened in any way.\n\n\"I hope the third year from this _will_ end better; I mean it shall, if I live to work out my plans,\" said Mr. Brooke, smiling at Meg, as if everything had become possible to him now.\n\n\"Doesn't it seem very long to wait?\" asked Amy, who was in a hurry for the wedding.\n\n\"I've got so much to learn before I shall be ready, it seems a short time to me,\" answered Meg, with a sweet gravity in her face, never seen there before.\n\n\"You have only to wait; _I_ am to do the work,\" said John, beginning his labors by picking up Meg's napkin, with an expression which caused Jo to shake her head, and then say to herself, with an air of relief, as the front door banged, \"Here comes Laurie. Now we shall have a little sensible conversation.\"\n\nBut Jo was mistaken; for Laurie came prancing in, overflowing with spirits, bearing a great bridal-looking bouquet for \"Mrs. John Brooke,\" and evidently laboring under the delusion that the whole affair had been brought about by his excellent management.\n\n\"I knew Brooke would have it all his own way, he always does; for when he makes up his mind to accomplish anything, it's done, though the sky falls,\" said Laurie, when he had presented his offering and his congratulations.\n\n\"Much obliged for that recommendation. I take it as a good omen for the future, and invite you to my wedding on the spot,\" answered Mr. Brooke, who felt at peace with all mankind, even his mischievous pupil.\n\n\"I'll come if I'm at the ends of the earth; for the sight of Jo's face alone, on that occasion, would be worth a long journey. You don't look festive, ma'am; what's the matter?\" asked Laurie, following her into a corner of the parlor, whither all had adjourned to greet Mr. Laurence.\n\n\"I don't approve of the match, but I've made up my mind to bear it, and shall not say a word against it,\" said Jo solemnly. \"You can't know how hard it is for me to give up Meg,\" she continued, with a little quiver in her voice.\n\n\"You don't give her up. You only go halves,\" said Laurie consolingly.\n\n\"It never can be the same again. I've lost my dearest friend,\" sighed Jo.\n\n\"You've got me, anyhow. I'm not good for much, I know; but I'll stand by you, Jo, all the days of my life; upon my word I will!\" and Laurie meant what he said.\n\n\"I know you will, and I'm ever so much obliged; you are always a great comfort to me, Teddy,\" returned Jo, gratefully shaking hands.\n\n\"Well, now, don't be dismal, there's a good fellow. It's all right, you see. Meg is happy; Brooke will fly round and get settled immediately; grandpa will attend to him, and it will be very jolly to see Meg in her own little house. We'll have capital times after she is gone, for I shall be through college before long, and then we'll go abroad, or some nice trip or other. Wouldn't that console you?\"\n\n\"I rather think it would; but there's no knowing what may happen in three years,\" said Jo thoughtfully.\n\n\"That's true. Don't you wish you could take a look forward, and see where we shall all be then? I do,\" returned Laurie.\n\n\"I think not, for I might see something sad; and every one looks so happy now, I don't believe they could be much improved,\" and Jo's eyes went slowly round the room, brightening as they looked, for the prospect was a pleasant one.\n\nFather and mother sat together, quietly re-living the first chapter of the romance which for them began some twenty years ago. Amy was drawing the lovers, who sat apart in a beautiful world of their own, the light of which touched their faces with a grace the little artist could not copy. Beth lay on her sofa, talking cheerily with her old friend, who held her little hand as if he felt that it possessed the power to lead him along the peaceful way she walked. Jo lounged in her favorite low seat, with the grave, quiet look which best became her; and Laurie, leaning on the back of her chair, his chin on a level with her curly head, smiled with his friendliest aspect, and nodded at her in the long glass which reflected them both.\n* * * * *\n\nSo grouped, the curtain falls upon Meg, Jo, Beth, and Amy. Whether it ever rises again, depends upon the reception given to the first act of the domestic drama called \"LITTLE WOMEN.\"\n\nThe Second Part\n\nIn order that we may start afresh, and go to Meg's wedding with free minds, it will be well to begin with a little gossip about the Marches. And here let me premise, that if any of the elders think there is too much \"lovering\" in the story, as I fear they may (I'm not afraid the young folks will make that objection), I can only say with Mrs. March, \"What _can_ you expect when I have four gay girls in the house, and a dashing young neighbor over the way?\"\n\nThe three years that have passed have brought but few changes to the quiet family. The war is over, and Mr. March safely at home, busy with his books and the small parish which found in him a minister by nature as by grace,--a quiet, studious man, rich in the wisdom that is better than learning, the charity which calls all mankind \"brother,\" the piety that blossoms into character, making it august and lovely.\n\nThese attributes, in spite of poverty and the strict integrity which shut him out from the more worldly successes, attracted to him many admirable persons, as naturally as sweet herbs draw bees, and as naturally he gave them the honey into which fifty years of hard experience had distilled no bitter drop. Earnest young men found the gray-headed scholar as young at heart as they; thoughtful or troubled women instinctively brought their doubts and sorrows to him, sure of finding the gentlest sympathy, the wisest counsel; sinners told their sins to the pure-hearted old man, and were both rebuked and saved; gifted men found a companion in him; ambitious men caught glimpses of nobler ambitions than their own; and even worldlings confessed that his beliefs were beautiful and true, although \"they wouldn't pay.\"\n\nTo outsiders, the five energetic women seemed to rule the house, and so they did in many things; but the quiet scholar, sitting among his books, was still the head of the family, the household conscience, anchor, and comforter; for to him the busy, anxious women always turned in troublous times, finding him, in the truest sense of those sacred words, husband and father.\n\nThe girls gave their hearts into their mother's keeping, their souls into their father's; and to both parents, who lived and labored so faithfully for them, they gave a love that grew with their growth, and bound them tenderly together by the sweetest tie which blesses life and outlives death.\n\nMrs. March is as brisk and cheery, though rather grayer, than when we saw her last, and just now so absorbed in Meg's affairs that the hospitals and homes, still full of wounded \"boys\" and soldiers' widows, decidedly miss the motherly missionary's visits.\n\nJohn Brooke did his duty manfully for a year, got wounded, was sent home, and not allowed to return. He received no stars or bars, but he deserved them, for he cheerfully risked all he had; and life and love are very precious when both are in full bloom. Perfectly resigned to his discharge, he devoted himself to getting well, preparing for business, and earning a home for Meg. With the good sense and sturdy independence that characterized him, he refused Mr. Laurence's more generous offers, and accepted the place of book-keeper feeling better satisfied to begin with an honestly-earned salary, than by running any risks with borrowed money.\n\nMeg had spent the time in working as well as waiting, growing womanly in character, wise in housewifely arts, and prettier than ever; for love is a great beautifier. She had her girlish ambitions and hopes, and felt some disappointment at the humble way in which the new life must begin. Ned Moffat had just married Sallie Gardiner, and Meg couldn't help contrasting their fine house and carriage, many gifts, and splendid outfit, with her own, and secretly wishing she could have the same. But somehow envy and discontent soon vanished when she thought of all the patient love and labor John had put into the little home awaiting her; and when they sat together in the twilight, talking over their small plans, the future always grew so beautiful and bright that she forgot Sallie's splendor, and felt herself the richest, happiest girl in Christendom.\n\nJo never went back to Aunt March, for the old lady took such a fancy to Amy that she bribed her with the offer of drawing lessons from one of the best teachers going; and for the sake of this advantage, Amy would have served a far harder mistress. So she gave her mornings to duty, her afternoons to pleasure, and prospered finely. Jo, meantime, devoted herself to literature and Beth, who remained delicate long after the fever was a thing of the past. Not an invalid exactly, but never again the rosy, healthy creature she had been; yet always hopeful, happy, and serene, busy with the quiet duties she loved, every one's friend, and an angel in the house, long before those who loved her most had learned to know it.\n\nAs long as \"The Spread Eagle\" paid her a dollar a column for her \"rubbish,\" as she called it, Jo felt herself a woman of means, and spun her little romances diligently. But great plans fermented in her busy brain and ambitious mind, and the old tin kitchen in the garret held a slowly increasing pile of blotted manuscript, which was one day to place the name of March upon the roll of fame.\n\nLaurie, having dutifully gone to college to please his grandfather, was now getting through it in the easiest possible manner to please himself. A universal favorite, thanks to money, manners, much talent, and the kindest heart that ever got its owner into scrapes by trying to get other people out of them, he stood in great danger of being spoilt, and probably would have been, like many another promising boy, if he had not possessed a talisman against evil in the memory of the kind old man who was bound up in his success, the motherly friend who watched over him as if he were her son, and last, but not least by any means, the knowledge that four innocent girls loved, admired, and believed in him with all their hearts.\n\nBeing only \"a glorious human boy,\" of course he frolicked and flirted, grew dandified, aquatic, sentimental, or gymnastic, as college fashions ordained; hazed and was hazed, talked slang, and more than once came perilously near suspension and expulsion. But as high spirits and the love of fun were the causes of these pranks, he always managed to save himself by frank confession, honorable atonement, or the irresistible power of persuasion which he possessed in perfection. In fact, he rather prided himself on his narrow escapes, and liked to thrill the girls with graphic accounts of his triumphs over wrathful tutors, dignified professors, and vanquished enemies. The \"men of my class\" were heroes in the eyes of the girls, who never wearied of the exploits of \"our fellows,\" and were frequently allowed to bask in the smiles of these great creatures, when Laurie brought them home with him.\n\nAmy especially enjoyed this high honor, and became quite a belle among them; for her ladyship early felt and learned to use the gift of fascination with which she was endowed. Meg was too much absorbed in her private and particular John to care for any other lords of creation, and Beth too shy to do more than peep at them, and wonder how Amy dared to order them about so; but Jo felt quite in her element, and found it very difficult to refrain from imitating the gentlemanly attitudes, phrases, and feats, which seemed more natural to her than the decorums prescribed for young ladies. They all liked Jo immensely, but never fell in love with her, though very few escaped without paying the tribute of a sentimental sigh or two at Amy's shrine. And speaking of sentiment brings us very naturally to the \"Dove-cote.\"\n\nThat was the name of the little brown house which Mr. Brooke had prepared for Meg's first home. Laurie had christened it, saying it was highly appropriate to the gentle lovers, who \"went on together like a pair of turtle-doves, with first a bill and then a coo.\" It was a tiny house, with a little garden behind, and a lawn about as big as a pocket-handkerchief in front. Here Meg meant to have a fountain, shrubbery, and a profusion of lovely flowers; though just at present, the fountain was represented by a weather-beaten urn, very like a dilapidated slop-bowl; the shrubbery consisted of several young larches, undecided whether to live or die; and the profusion of flowers was merely hinted by regiments of sticks, to show where seeds were planted. But inside, it was altogether charming, and the happy bride saw no fault from garret to cellar. To be sure, the hall was so narrow, it was fortunate that they had no piano, for one never could have been got in whole; the dining-room was so small that six people were a tight fit; and the kitchen stairs seemed built for the express purpose of precipitating both servants and china pell-mell into the coal-bin. But once get used to these slight blemishes, and nothing could be more complete, for good sense and good taste had presided over the furnishing, and the result was highly satisfactory. There were no marble-topped tables, long mirrors, or lace curtains in the little parlor, but simple furniture, plenty of books, a fine picture or two, a stand of flowers in the bay-window, and, scattered all about, the pretty gifts which came from friendly hands, and were the fairer for the loving messages they brought.\n\nI don't think the Parian Psyche Laurie gave lost any of its beauty because John put up the bracket it stood upon; that any upholsterer could have draped the plain muslin curtains more gracefully than Amy's artistic hand; or that any store-room was ever better provided with good wishes, merry words, and happy hopes, than that in which Jo and her mother put away Meg's few boxes, barrels, and bundles; and I am morally certain that the spandy-new kitchen never _could_ have looked so cosey and neat if Hannah had not arranged every pot and pan a dozen times over, and laid the fire all ready for lighting, the minute \"Mis. Brooke came home.\" I also doubt if any young matron ever began life with so rich a supply of dusters, holders, and piece-bags; for Beth made enough to last till the silver wedding came round, and invented three different kinds of dishcloths for the express service of the bridal china.\n\nPeople who hire all these things done for them never know what they lose; for the homeliest tasks get beautified if loving hands do them, and Meg found so many proofs of this, that everything in her small nest, from the kitchen roller to the silver vase on her parlor table, was eloquent of home love and tender forethought.\n\nWhat happy times they had planning together, what solemn shopping excursions; what funny mistakes they made, and what shouts of laughter arose over Laurie's ridiculous bargains. In his love of jokes, this young gentleman, though nearly through college, was as much of a boy as ever. His last whim had been to bring with him, on his weekly visits, some new, useful, and ingenious article for the young housekeeper. Now a bag of remarkable clothes-pins; next, a wonderful nutmeg-grater, which fell to pieces at the first trial; a knife-cleaner that spoilt all the knives; or a sweeper that picked the nap neatly off the carpet, and left the dirt; labor-saving soap that took the skin off one's hands; infallible cements which stuck firmly to nothing but the fingers of the deluded buyer; and every kind of tin-ware, from a toy savings-bank for odd pennies, to a wonderful boiler which would wash articles in its own steam, with every prospect of exploding in the process.\n\nIn vain Meg begged him to stop. John laughed at him, and Jo called him \"Mr. Toodles.\" He was possessed with a mania for patronizing Yankee ingenuity, and seeing his friends fitly furnished forth. So each week beheld some fresh absurdity.\n\nEverything was done at last, even to Amy's arranging different colored soaps to match the different colored rooms, and Beth's setting the table for the first meal.\n\n\"Are you satisfied? Does it seem like home, and do you feel as if you should be happy here?\" asked Mrs. March, as she and her daughter went through the new kingdom, arm-in-arm; for just then they seemed to cling together more tenderly than ever.\n\n\"Yes, mother, perfectly satisfied, thanks to you all, and _so_ happy that I can't talk about it,\" answered Meg, with a look that was better than words.\n\n\"If she only had a servant or two it would be all right,\" said Amy, coming out of the parlor, where she had been trying to decide whether the bronze Mercury looked best on the whatnot or the mantle-piece.\n\n\"Mother and I have talked that over, and I have made up my mind to try her way first. There will be so little to do, that, with Lotty to run my errands and help me here and there, I shall only have enough work to keep me from getting lazy or homesick,\" answered Meg tranquilly.\n\n\"Sallie Moffat has four,\" began Amy.\n\n\"If Meg had four the house wouldn't hold them, and master and missis would have to camp in the garden,\" broke in Jo, who, enveloped in a big blue pinafore, was giving the last polish to the door-handles.\n\n\"Sallie isn't a poor man's wife, and many maids are in keeping with her fine establishment. Meg and John begin humbly, but I have a feeling that there will be quite as much happiness in the little house as in the big one. It's a great mistake for young girls like Meg to leave themselves nothing to do but dress, give orders, and gossip. When I was first married, I used to long for my new clothes to wear out or get torn, so that I might have the pleasure of mending them; for I got heartily sick of doing fancy work and tending my pocket handkerchief.\"\n\n\"Why didn't you go into the kitchen and make messes, as Sallie says she does, to amuse herself, though they never turn out well, and the servants laugh at her,\" said Meg.\n\n\"I did, after a while; not to 'mess,' but to learn of Hannah how things should be done, that my servants need _not_ laugh at me. It was play then; but there came a time when I was truly grateful that I not only possessed the will but the power to cook wholesome food for my little girls, and help myself when I could no longer afford to hire help. You begin at the other end, Meg, dear; but the lessons you learn now will be of use to you by and by, when John is a richer man, for the mistress of a house, however splendid, should know how work ought to be done, if she wishes to be well and honestly served.\"\n\n\"Yes, mother, I'm sure of that,\" said Meg, listening respectfully to the little lecture; for the best of women will hold forth upon the all-absorbing subject of housekeeping. \"Do you know I like this room most of all in my baby-house,\" added Meg, a minute after, as they went upstairs, and she looked into her well-stored linen-closet.\n\nBeth was there, laying the snowy piles smoothly on the shelves, and exulting over the goodly array. All three laughed as Meg spoke; for that linen-closet was a joke. You see, having said that if Meg married \"that Brooke\" she shouldn't have a cent of her money, Aunt March was rather in a quandary, when time had appeased her wrath and made her repent her vow. She never broke her word, and was much exercised in her mind how to get round it, and at last devised a plan whereby she could satisfy herself. Mrs. Carrol, Florence's mamma, was ordered to buy, have made, and marked, a generous supply of house and table linen, and send it as _her_ present, all of which was faithfully done; but the secret leaked out, and was greatly enjoyed by the family; for Aunt March tried to look utterly unconscious, and insisted that she could give nothing but the old-fashioned pearls, long promised to the first bride.\n\n\"That's a housewifely taste which I am glad to see. I had a young friend who set up housekeeping with six sheets, but she had finger bowls for company, and that satisfied her,\" said Mrs. March, patting the damask table-cloths, with a truly feminine appreciation of their fineness.\n\n\"I haven't a single finger-bowl, but this is a 'set out' that will last me all my days, Hannah says;\" and Meg looked quite contented, as well she might.\n\n\"Toodles is coming,\" cried Jo from below; and they all went down to meet Laurie, whose weekly visit was an important event in their quiet lives.\nA tall, broad-shouldered young fellow, with a cropped head, a felt-basin of a hat, and a fly-away coat, came tramping down the road at a great pace, walked over the low fence without stopping to open the gate, straight up to Mrs. March, with both hands out, and a hearty--\n\n\"Here I am, mother! Yes, it's all right.\"\n\nThe last words were in answer to the look the elder lady gave him; a kindly questioning look, which the handsome eyes met so frankly that the little ceremony closed, as usual, with a motherly kiss.\n\n\"For Mrs. John Brooke, with the maker's congratulations and compliments. Bless you, Beth! What a refreshing spectacle you are, Jo. Amy, you are getting altogether too handsome for a single lady.\"\n\nAs Laurie spoke, he delivered a brown paper parcel to Meg, pulled Beth's hair-ribbon, stared at Jo's big pinafore, and fell into an attitude of mock rapture before Amy, then shook hands all round, and every one began to talk.\n\n\"Where is John?\" asked Meg anxiously.\n\n\"Stopped to get the license for to-morrow, ma'am.\"\n\n\"Which side won the last match, Teddy?\" inquired Jo, who persisted in feeling an interest in manly sports, despite her nineteen years.\n\n\"Ours, of course. Wish you'd been there to see.\"\n\n\"How is the lovely Miss Randal?\" asked Amy, with a significant smile.\n\n\"More cruel than ever; don't you see how I'm pining away?\" and Laurie gave his broad chest a sounding slap and heaved a melodramatic sigh.\n\n\"What's the last joke? Undo the bundle and see, Meg,\" said Beth, eying the knobby parcel with curiosity.\n\n\"It's a useful thing to have in the house in case of fire or thieves,\" observed Laurie, as a watchman's rattle appeared, amid the laughter of the girls.\n\n\"Any time when John is away, and you get frightened, Mrs. Meg, just swing that out of the front window, and it will rouse the neighborhood in a jiffy. Nice thing, isn't it?\" and Laurie gave them a sample of its powers that made them cover up their ears.\n\n\"There's gratitude for you! and speaking of gratitude reminds me to mention that you may thank Hannah for saving your wedding-cake from destruction. I saw it going into your house as I came by, and if she hadn't defended it manfully I'd have had a pick at it, for it looked like a remarkably plummy one.\"\n\n\"I wonder if you will ever grow up, Laurie,\" said Meg, in a matronly tone.\n\n\"I'm doing my best, ma'am, but can't get much higher, I'm afraid, as six feet is about all men can do in these degenerate days,\" responded the young gentleman, whose head was about level with the little chandelier. \"I suppose it would be profanation to eat anything in this spick and span new bower, so, as I'm tremendously hungry, I propose an adjournment,\" he added presently.\n\n\"Mother and I are going to wait for John. There are some last things to settle,\" said Meg, bustling away.\n\n\"Beth and I are going over to Kitty Bryant's to get more flowers for to-morrow,\" added Amy, tying a picturesque hat over her picturesque curls, and enjoying the effect as much as anybody.\n\n\"Come, Jo, don't desert a fellow. I'm in such a state of exhaustion I can't get home without help. Don't take off your apron, whatever you do; it's peculiarly becoming,\" said Laurie, as Jo bestowed his especial aversion in her capacious pocket, and offered him her arm to support his feeble steps.\n\n\"Now, Teddy, I want to talk seriously to you about to-morrow,\" began Jo, as they strolled away together. \"You _must_ promise to behave well, and not cut up any pranks, and spoil our plans.\"\n\n\"Not a prank.\"\n\n\"And don't say funny things when we ought to be sober.\"\n\n\"I never do; you are the one for that.\"\n\n\"And I implore you not to look at me during the ceremony; I shall certainly laugh if you do.\"\n\n\"You won't see me; you'll be crying so hard that the thick fog round you will obscure the prospect.\"\n\n\"I never cry unless for some great affliction.\"\n\n\"Such as fellows going to college, hey?\" cut in Laurie, with a suggestive laugh.\n\n\"Don't be a peacock. I only moaned a trifle to keep the girls company.\"\n\n\"Exactly. I say, Jo, how is grandpa this week; pretty amiable?\"\n\n\"Very; why, have you got into a scrape, and want to know how he'll take it?\" asked Jo rather sharply.\n\n\"Now, Jo, do you think I'd look your mother in the face, and say 'All right,' if it wasn't?\" and Laurie stopped short, with an injured air.\n\n\"No, I don't.\"\n\n\"Then don't go and be suspicious; I only want some money,\" said Laurie, walking on again, appeased by her hearty tone.\n\n\"You spend a great deal, Teddy.\"\n\n\"Bless you, _I_ don't spend it; it spends itself, somehow, and is gone before I know it.\"\n\n\"You are so generous and kind-hearted that you let people borrow, and can't say 'No' to any one. We heard about Henshaw, and all you did for him. If you always spent money in that way, no one would blame you,\" said Jo warmly.\n\n\"Oh, he made a mountain out of a mole-hill. You wouldn't have me let that fine fellow work himself to death, just for the want of a little help, when he is worth a dozen of us lazy chaps, would you?\"\n\n\"Of course not; but I don't see the use of your having seventeen waistcoats, endless neckties, and a new hat every time you come home. I thought you'd got over the dandy period; but every now and then it breaks out in a new spot. Just now it's the fashion to be hideous,--to make your head look like a scrubbing-brush, wear a strait-jacket, orange gloves, and clumping, square-toed boots. If it was cheap ugliness, I'd say nothing; but it costs as much as the other, and I don't get any satisfaction out of it.\"\n\nLaurie threw back his head, and laughed so heartily at this attack, that the felt-basin fell off, and Jo walked on it, which insult only afforded him an opportunity for expatiating on the advantages of a rough-and-ready costume, as he folded up the maltreated hat, and stuffed it into his pocket.\n\n\"Don't lecture any more, there's a good soul! I have enough all through the week, and like to enjoy myself when I come home. I'll get myself up regardless of expense, to-morrow, and be a satisfaction to my friends.\"\n\n\"I'll leave you in peace if you'll _only_ let your hair grow. I'm not aristocratic, but I do object to being seen with a person who looks like a young prize-fighter,\" observed Jo severely.\n\n\"This unassuming style promotes study; that's why we adopt it,\" returned Laurie, who certainly could not be accused of vanity, having voluntarily sacrificed a handsome curly crop to the demand for quarter-of-an-inch-long stubble.\n\n\"By the way, Jo, I think that little Parker is really getting desperate about Amy. He talks of her constantly, writes poetry, and moons about in a most suspicious manner. He'd better nip his little passion in the bud, hadn't he?\" added Laurie, in a confidential, elder-brotherly tone, after a minute's silence.\n\n\"Of course he had; we don't want any more marrying in this family for years to come. Mercy on us, what _are_ the children thinking of?\" and Jo looked as much scandalized as if Amy and little Parker were not yet in their teens.\n\n\"It's a fast age, and I don't know what we are coming to, ma'am. You are a mere infant, but you'll go next, Jo, and we'll be left lamenting,\" said Laurie, shaking his head over the degeneracy of the times.\n\n\"Don't be alarmed; I'm not one of the agreeable sort. Nobody will want me, and it's a mercy, for there should always be one old maid in a family.\"\n\n\"You won't give any one a chance,\" said Laurie, with a sidelong glance, and a little more color than before in his sunburnt face. \"You won't show the soft side of your character; and if a fellow gets a peep at it by accident, and can't help showing that he likes it, you treat him as Mrs. Gummidge did her sweetheart,--throw cold water over him,--and get so thorny no one dares touch or look at you.\"\n\n\"I don't like that sort of thing; I'm too busy to be worried with nonsense, and I think it's dreadful to break up families so. Now don't say any more about it; Meg's wedding has turned all our heads, and we talk of nothing but lovers and such absurdities. I don't wish to get cross, so let's change the subject;\" and Jo looked quite ready to fling cold water on the slightest provocation.\n\nWhatever his feelings might have been, Laurie found a vent for them in a long low whistle, and the fearful prediction, as they parted at the gate, \"Mark my words, Jo, you'll go next.\"\n\n# THE FIRST WEDDING.\n\nThe June roses over the porch were awake bright and early on that morning, rejoicing with all their hearts in the cloudless sunshine, like friendly little neighbors, as they were. Quite flushed with excitement were their ruddy faces, as they swung in the wind, whispering to one another what they had seen; for some peeped in at the dining-room windows, where the feast was spread, some climbed up to nod and smile at the sisters as they dressed the bride, others waved a welcome to those who came and went on various errands in garden, porch, and hall, and all, from the rosiest full-blown flower to the palest baby-bud, offered their tribute of beauty and fragrance to the gentle mistress who had loved and tended them so long.\n\nMeg looked very like a rose herself; for all that was best and sweetest in heart and soul seemed to bloom into her face that day, making it fair and tender, with a charm more beautiful than beauty. Neither silk, lace, nor orange-flowers would she have. \"I don't want to look strange or fixed up to-day,\" she said. \"I don't want a fashionable wedding, but only those about me whom I love, and to them I wish to look and be my familiar self.\"\n\nSo she made her wedding gown herself, sewing into it the tender hopes and innocent romances of a girlish heart. Her sisters braided up her pretty hair, and the only ornaments she wore were the lilies of the valley, which \"her John\" liked best of all the flowers that grew.\n\n\"You _do_ look just like our own dear Meg, only so very sweet and lovely that I should hug you if it wouldn't crumple your dress,\" cried Amy, surveying her with delight, when all was done.\n\n\"Then I am satisfied. But please hug and kiss me, every one, and don't mind my dress; I want a great many crumples of this sort put into it to-day;\" and Meg opened her arms to her sisters, who clung about her with April faces for a minute, feeling that the new love had not changed the old.\n\n\"Now I'm going to tie John's cravat for him, and then to stay a few minutes with father quietly in the study;\" and Meg ran down to perform these little ceremonies, and then to follow her mother wherever she went, conscious that, in spite of the smiles on the motherly face, there was a secret sorrow hid in the motherly heart at the flight of the first bird from the nest.\n\nAs the younger girls stand together, giving the last touches to their simple toilet, it may be a good time to tell of a few changes which three years have wrought in their appearance; for all are looking their best just now.\n\nJo's angles are much softened; she has learned to carry herself with ease, if not grace. The curly crop has lengthened into a thick coil, more becoming to the small head atop of the tall figure. There is a fresh color in her brown cheeks, a soft shine in her eyes, and only gentle words fall from her sharp tongue to-day.\n\nBeth has grown slender, pale, and more quiet than ever; the beautiful, kind eyes are larger, and in them lies an expression that saddens one, although it is not sad itself. It is the shadow of pain which touches the young face with such pathetic patience; but Beth seldom complains, and always speaks hopefully of \"being better soon.\"\n\nAmy is with truth considered \"the flower of the family;\" for at sixteen she has the air and bearing of a full-grown woman--not beautiful, but possessed of that indescribable charm called grace. One saw it in the lines of her figure, the make and motion of her hands, the flow of her dress, the droop of her hair,--unconscious, yet harmonious, and as attractive to many as beauty itself. Amy's nose still afflicted her, for it never _would_ grow Grecian; so did her mouth, being too wide, and having a decided chin. These offending features gave character to her whole face, but she never could see it, and consoled herself with her wonderfully fair complexion, keen blue eyes, and curls, more golden and abundant than ever.\n\nAll three wore suits of thin silver gray (their best gowns for the summer), with blush-roses in hair and bosom; and all three looked just what they were,--fresh-faced, happy-hearted girls, pausing a moment in their busy lives to read with wistful eyes the sweetest chapter in the romance of womanhood.\n\nThere were to be no ceremonious performances, everything was to be as natural and homelike as possible; so when Aunt March arrived, she was scandalized to see the bride come running to welcome and lead her in, to find the bridegroom fastening up a garland that had fallen down, and to catch a glimpse of the paternal minister marching upstairs with a grave countenance, and a wine-bottle under each arm.\n\n\"Upon my word, here's a state of things!\" cried the old lady, taking the seat of honor prepared for her, and settling the folds of her lavender _moire_ with a great rustle. \"You oughtn't to be seen till the last minute, child.\"\n\n\"I'm not a show, aunty, and no one is coming to stare at me, to criticise my dress, or count the cost of my luncheon. I'm too happy to care what any one says or thinks, and I'm going to have my little wedding just as I like it. John, dear, here's your hammer;\" and away went Meg to help \"that man\" in his highly improper employment.\n\nMr. Brooke didn't even say \"Thank you,\" but as he stooped for the unromantic tool, he kissed his little bride behind the folding-door, with a look that made Aunt March whisk out her pocket-handkerchief, with a sudden dew in her sharp old eyes.\n\nA crash, a cry, and a laugh from Laurie, accompanied by the indecorous exclamation, \"Jupiter Ammon! Jo's upset the cake again!\" caused a momentary flurry, which was hardly over when a flock of cousins arrived, and \"the party came in,\" as Beth used to say when a child.\n\n\"Don't let that young giant come near me; he worries me worse than mosquitoes,\" whispered the old lady to Amy, as the rooms filled, and Laurie's black head towered above the rest.\n\n\"He has promised to be very good to-day, and he _can_ be perfectly elegant if he likes,\" returned Amy, gliding away to warn Hercules to beware of the dragon, which warning caused him to haunt the old lady with a devotion that nearly distracted her.\n\nThere was no bridal procession, but a sudden silence fell upon the room as Mr. March and the young pair took their places under the green arch. Mother and sisters gathered close, as if loath to give Meg up; the fatherly voice broke more than once, which only seemed to make the service more beautiful and solemn; the bridegroom's hand trembled visibly, and no one heard his replies; but Meg looked straight up in her husband's eyes, and said, \"I will!\" with such tender trust in her own face and voice that her mother's heart rejoiced, and Aunt March sniffed audibly.\n\nJo did _not_ cry, though she was very near it once, and was only saved from a demonstration by the consciousness that Laurie was staring fixedly at her, with a comical mixture of merriment and emotion in his wicked black eyes. Beth kept her face hidden on her mother's shoulder, but Amy stood like a graceful statue, with a most becoming ray of sunshine touching her white forehead and the flower in her hair.\n\nIt wasn't at all the thing, I'm afraid, but the minute she was fairly married, Meg cried, \"The first kiss for Marmee!\" and, turning, gave it with her heart on her lips. During the next fifteen minutes she looked more like a rose than ever, for every one availed themselves of their privileges to the fullest extent, from Mr. Laurence to old Hannah, who, adorned with a head-dress fearfully and wonderfully made, fell upon her in the hall, crying, with a sob and a chuckle, \"Bless you, deary, a hundred times! The cake ain't hurt a mite, and everything looks lovely.\"\n\nEverybody cleared up after that, and said something brilliant, or tried to, which did just as well, for laughter is ready when hearts are light. There was no display of gifts, for they were already in the little house, nor was there an elaborate breakfast, but a plentiful lunch of cake and fruit, dressed with flowers. Mr. Laurence and Aunt March shrugged and smiled at one another when water, lemonade, and coffee were found to be the only sorts of nectar which the three Hebes carried round. No one said anything, however, till Laurie, who insisted on serving the bride, appeared before her, with a loaded salver in his hand and a puzzled expression on his face.\n\n\"Has Jo smashed all the bottles by accident?\" he whispered, \"or am I merely laboring under a delusion that I saw some lying about loose this morning?\"\n\n\"No; your grandfather kindly offered us his best, and Aunt March actually sent some, but father put away a little for Beth, and despatched the rest to the Soldiers' Home. You know he thinks that wine should be used only in illness, and mother says that neither she nor her daughters will ever offer it to any young man under her roof.\"\n\nMeg spoke seriously, and expected to see Laurie frown or laugh; but he did neither, for after a quick look at her, he said, in his impetuous way, \"I like that! for I've seen enough harm done to wish other women would think as you do.\"\n\n\"You are not made wise by experience, I hope?\" and there was an anxious accent in Meg's voice.\n\n\"No; I give you my word for it. Don't think too well of me, either; this is not one of my temptations. Being brought up where wine is as common as water, and almost as harmless, I don't care for it; but when a pretty girl offers it, one doesn't like to refuse, you see.\"\n\n\"But you will, for the sake of others, if not for your own. Come, Laurie, promise, and give me one more reason to call this the happiest day of my life.\"\n\nA demand so sudden and so serious made the young man hesitate a moment, for ridicule is often harder to bear than self-denial. Meg knew that if he gave the promise he would keep it at all costs; and, feeling her power, used it as a woman may for her friend's good. She did not speak, but she looked up at him with a face made very eloquent by happiness, and a smile which said, \"No one can refuse me anything to-day.\" Laurie certainly could not; and, with an answering smile, he gave her his hand, saying heartily, \"I promise, Mrs. Brooke!\"\n\n\"I thank you, very, very much.\"\n\n\"And I drink 'long life to your resolution,' Teddy,\" cried Jo, baptizing him with a splash of lemonade, as she waved her glass, and beamed approvingly upon him.\n\nSo the toast was drunk, the pledge made, and loyally kept, in spite of many temptations; for, with instinctive wisdom, the girls had seized a happy moment to do their friend a service, for which he thanked them all his life.\n\nAfter lunch, people strolled about, by twos and threes, through house and garden, enjoying the sunshine without and within. Meg and John happened to be standing together in the middle of the grass-plot, when Laurie was seized with an inspiration which put the finishing touch to this unfashionable wedding.\n\n\"All the married people take hands and dance round the new-made husband and wife, as the Germans do, while we bachelors and spinsters prance in couples outside!\" cried Laurie, promenading down the path with Amy, with such infectious spirit and skill that every one else followed their example without a murmur. Mr. and Mrs. March, Aunt and Uncle Carrol, began it; others rapidly joined in; even Sallie Moffat, after a moment's hesitation, threw her train over her arm, and whisked Ned into the ring. But the crowning joke was Mr. Laurence and Aunt March; for when the stately old gentleman _chasséed_ solemnly up to the old lady, she just tucked her cane under her arm, and hopped briskly away to join hands with the rest, and dance about the bridal pair, while the young folks pervaded the garden, like butterflies on a midsummer day.\n\nWant of breath brought the impromptu ball to a close, and then people began to go.\n\n\"I wish you well, my dear, I heartily wish you well; but I think you'll be sorry for it,\" said Aunt March to Meg, adding to the bridegroom, as he led her to the carriage, \"You've got a treasure, young man, see that you deserve it.\"\n\n\"That is the prettiest wedding I've been to for an age, Ned, and I don't see why, for there wasn't a bit of style about it,\" observed Mrs. Moffat to her husband, as they drove away.\n\n\"Laurie, my lad, if you ever want to indulge in this sort of thing, get one of those little girls to help you, and I shall be perfectly satisfied,\" said Mr. Laurence, settling himself in his easy-chair to rest, after the excitement of the morning.\n\n\"I'll do my best to gratify you, sir,\" was Laurie's unusually dutiful reply, as he carefully unpinned the posy Jo had put in his button-hole.\n\nThe little house was not far away, and the only bridal journey Meg had was the quiet walk with John, from the old home to the new. When she came down, looking like a pretty Quakeress in her dove-colored suit and straw bonnet tied with white, they all gathered about her to say \"good-by,\" as tenderly as if she had been going to make the grand tour.\n\n\"Don't feel that I am separated from you, Marmee dear, or that I love you any the less for loving John so much,\" she said, clinging to her mother, with full eyes, for a moment. \"I shall come every day, father, and expect to keep my old place in all your hearts, though I _am_ married. Beth is going to be with me a great deal, and the other girls will drop in now and then to laugh at my housekeeping struggles. Thank you all for my happy wedding-day. Good-by, good-by!\"\n\nThey stood watching her, with faces full of love and hope and tender pride, as she walked away, leaning on her husband's arm, with her hands full of flowers, and the June sunshine brightening her happy face,--and so Meg's married life began.\n\n# ARTISTIC ATTEMPTS.\n\nIt takes people a long time to learn the difference between talent and genius, especially ambitious young men and women. Amy was learning this distinction through much tribulation; for, mistaking enthusiasm for inspiration, she attempted every branch of art with youthful audacity. For a long time there was a lull in the \"mud-pie\" business, and she devoted herself to the finest pen-and-ink drawing, in which she showed such taste and skill that her graceful handiwork proved both pleasant and profitable. But overstrained eyes soon caused pen and ink to be laid aside for a bold attempt at poker-sketching. While this attack lasted, the family lived in constant fear of a conflagration; for the odor of burning wood pervaded the house at all hours; smoke issued from attic and shed with alarming frequency, red-hot pokers lay about promiscuously, and Hannah never went to bed without a pail of water and the dinner-bell at her door, in case of fire. Raphael's face was found boldly executed on the under side of the moulding-board, and Bacchus on the head of a beer-barrel; a chanting cherub adorned the cover of the sugar-bucket, and attempts to portray Romeo and Juliet supplied kindlings for some time.\n\nFrom fire to oil was a natural transition for burnt fingers, and Amy fell to painting with undiminished ardor. An artist friend fitted her out with his cast-off palettes, brushes, and colors; and she daubed away, producing pastoral and marine views such as were never seen on land or sea. Her monstrosities in the way of cattle would have taken prizes at an agricultural fair; and the perilous pitching of her vessels would have produced sea-sickness in the most nautical observer, if the utter disregard to all known rules of shipbuilding and rigging had not convulsed him with laughter at the first glance. Swarthy boys and dark-eyed Madonnas, staring at you from one corner of the studio, suggested Murillo; oily-brown shadows of faces, with a lurid streak in the wrong place, meant Rembrandt; buxom ladies and dropsical infants, Rubens; and Turner appeared in tempests of blue thunder, orange lightning, brown rain, and purple clouds, with a tomato-colored splash in the middle, which might be the sun or a buoy, a sailor's shirt or a king's robe, as the spectator pleased.\n\nCharcoal portraits came next; and the entire family hung in a row, looking as wild and crocky as if just evoked from a coal-bin. Softened into crayon sketches, they did better; for the likenesses were good, and Amy's hair, Jo's nose, Meg's mouth, and Laurie's eyes were pronounced \"wonderfully fine.\" A return to clay and plaster followed, and ghostly casts of her acquaintances haunted corners of the house, or tumbled off closet-shelves on to people's heads. Children were enticed in as models, till their incoherent accounts of her mysterious doings caused Miss Amy to be regarded in the light of a young ogress. Her efforts in this line, however, were brought to an abrupt close by an untoward accident, which quenched her ardor. Other models failing her for a time, she undertook to cast her own pretty foot, and the family were one day alarmed by an unearthly bumping and screaming, and running to the rescue, found the young enthusiast hopping wildly about the shed, with her foot held fast in a pan-full of plaster, which had hardened with unexpected rapidity. With much difficulty and some danger she was dug out; for Jo was so overcome with laughter while she excavated, that her knife went too far, cut the poor foot, and left a lasting memorial of one artistic attempt, at least.\n\nAfter this Amy subsided, till a mania for sketching from nature set her to haunting river, field, and wood, for picturesque studies, and sighing for ruins to copy. She caught endless colds sitting on damp grass to book \"a delicious bit,\" composed of a stone, a stump, one mushroom, and a broken mullein-stalk, or \"a heavenly mass of clouds,\" that looked like a choice display of feather-beds when done. She sacrificed her complexion floating on the river in the midsummer sun, to study light and shade, and got a wrinkle over her nose, trying after \"points of sight,\" or whatever the squint-and-string performance is called.\n\nIf \"genius is eternal patience,\" as Michael Angelo affirms, Amy certainly had some claim to the divine attribute, for she persevered in spite of all obstacles, failures, and discouragements, firmly believing that in time she should do something worthy to be called \"high art.\"\n\nShe was learning, doing, and enjoying other things, meanwhile, for she had resolved to be an attractive and accomplished woman, even if she never became a great artist. Here she succeeded better; for she was one of those happily created beings who please without effort, make friends everywhere, and take life so gracefully and easily that less fortunate souls are tempted to believe that such are born under a lucky star. Everybody liked her, for among her good gifts was tact. She had an instinctive sense of what was pleasing and proper, always said the right thing to the right person, did just what suited the time and place, and was so self-possessed that her sisters used to say, \"If Amy went to court without any rehearsal beforehand, she'd know exactly what to do.\"\n\nOne of her weaknesses was a desire to move in \"our best society,\" without being quite sure what the _best_ really was. Money, position, fashionable accomplishments, and elegant manners were most desirable things in her eyes, and she liked to associate with those who possessed them, often mistaking the false for the true, and admiring what was not admirable. Never forgetting that by birth she was a gentlewoman, she cultivated her aristocratic tastes and feelings, so that when the opportunity came she might be ready to take the place from which poverty now excluded her.\n\n\"My lady,\" as her friends called her, sincerely desired to be a genuine lady, and was so at heart, but had yet to learn that money cannot buy refinement of nature, that rank does not always confer nobility, and that true breeding makes itself felt in spite of external drawbacks.\n\n\"I want to ask a favor of you, mamma,\" Amy said, coming in, with an important air, one day.\n\n\"Well, little girl, what is it?\" replied her mother, in whose eyes the stately young lady still remained \"the baby.\"\n\n\"Our drawing class breaks up next week, and before the girls separate for the summer, I want to ask them out here for a day. They are wild to see the river, sketch the broken bridge, and copy some of the things they admire in my book. They have been very kind to me in many ways, and I am grateful, for they are all rich, and know I am poor, yet they never made any difference.\"\n\n\"Why should they?\" and Mrs. March put the question with what the girls called her \"Maria Theresa air.\"\n\n\"You know as well as I that it _does_ make a difference with nearly every one, so don't ruffle up, like a dear, motherly hen, when your chickens get pecked by smarter birds; the ugly duckling turned out a swan, you know;\" and Amy smiled without bitterness, for she possessed a happy temper and hopeful spirit.\n\nMrs. March laughed, and smoothed down her maternal pride as she asked,--\n\n\"Well, my swan, what is your plan?\"\n\n\"I should like to ask the girls out to lunch next week, to take them a drive to the places they want to see, a row on the river, perhaps, and make a little artistic _fête_ for them.\"\n\n\"That looks feasible. What do you want for lunch? Cake, sandwiches, fruit, and coffee will be all that is necessary, I suppose?\"\n\n\"Oh dear, no! we must have cold tongue and chicken, French chocolate and ice-cream, besides. The girls are used to such things, and I want my lunch to be proper and elegant, though I _do_ work for my living.\"\n\n\"How many young ladies are there?\" asked her mother, beginning to look sober.\n\n\"Twelve or fourteen in the class, but I dare say they won't all come.\"\n\n\"Bless me, child, you will have to charter an omnibus to carry them about.\"\n\n\"Why, mother, how _can_ you think of such a thing? Not more than six or eight will probably come, so I shall hire a beach-wagon, and borrow Mr. Laurence's cherry-bounce.\" (Hannah's pronunciation of _char-à-banc_.)\n\n\"All this will be expensive, Amy.\"\n\n\"Not very; I've calculated the cost, and I'll pay for it myself.\"\n\n\"Don't you think, dear, that as these girls are used to such things, and the best we can do will be nothing new, that some simpler plan would be pleasanter to them, as a change, if nothing more, and much better for us than buying or borrowing what we don't need, and attempting a style not in keeping with our circumstances?\"\n\n\"If I can't have it as I like, I don't care to have it at all. I know that I can carry it out perfectly well, if you and the girls will help a little; and I don't see why I can't if I'm willing to pay for it,\" said Amy, with the decision which opposition was apt to change into obstinacy.\n\nMrs. March knew that experience was an excellent teacher, and when it was possible she left her children to learn alone the lessons which she would gladly have made easier, if they had not objected to taking advice as much as they did salts and senna.\n\n\"Very well, Amy; if your heart is set upon it, and you see your way through without too great an outlay of money, time, and temper, I'll say no more. Talk it over with the girls, and whichever way you decide, I'll do my best to help you.\"\n\n\"Thanks, mother; you are always _so_ kind;\" and away went Amy to lay her plan before her sisters.\n\nMeg agreed at once, and promised her aid, gladly offering anything she possessed, from her little house itself to her very best salt-spoons. But Jo frowned upon the whole project, and would have nothing to do with it at first.\n\n\"Why in the world should you spend your money, worry your family, and turn the house upside down for a parcel of girls who don't care a sixpence for you? I thought you had too much pride and sense to truckle to any mortal woman just because she wears French boots and rides in a _coupé_,\" said Jo, who, being called from the tragical climax of her novel, was not in the best mood for social enterprises.\n\n\"I _don't_ truckle, and I hate being patronized as much as you do!\" returned Amy indignantly, for the two still jangled when such questions arose. \"The girls do care for me, and I for them, and there's a great deal of kindness and sense and talent among them, in spite of what you call fashionable nonsense. You don't care to make people like you, to go into good society, and cultivate your manners and tastes. I do, and I mean to make the most of every chance that comes. _You_ can go through the world with your elbows out and your nose in the air, and call it independence, if you like. That's not my way.\"\n\nWhen Amy whetted her tongue and freed her mind she usually got the best of it, for she seldom failed to have common sense on her side, while Jo carried her love of liberty and hate of conventionalities to such an unlimited extent that she naturally found herself worsted in an argument. Amy's definition of Jo's idea of independence was such a good hit that both burst out laughing, and the discussion took a more amiable turn. Much against her will, Jo at length consented to sacrifice a day to Mrs. Grundy, and help her sister through what she regarded as \"a nonsensical business.\"\n\nThe invitations were sent, nearly all accepted, and the following Monday was set apart for the grand event. Hannah was out of humor because her week's work was deranged, and prophesied that \"ef the washin' and ironin' warn't done reg'lar nothin' would go well anywheres.\" This hitch in the mainspring of the domestic machinery had a bad effect upon the whole concern; but Amy's motto was \"Nil desperandum,\" and having made up her mind what to do, she proceeded to do it in spite of all obstacles. To begin with, Hannah's cooking didn't turn out well: the chicken was tough, the tongue too salt, and the chocolate wouldn't froth properly. Then the cake and ice cost more than Amy expected, so did the wagon; and various other expenses, which seemed trifling at the outset, counted up rather alarmingly afterward. Beth got cold and took to her bed, Meg had an unusual number of callers to keep her at home, and Jo was in such a divided state of mind that her breakages, accidents, and mistakes were uncommonly numerous, serious, and trying.\n\n\"If it hadn't been for mother I never should have got through,\" as Amy declared afterward, and gratefully remembered when \"the best joke of the season\" was entirely forgotten by everybody else.\n\nIf it was not fair on Monday, the young ladies were to come on Tuesday,--an arrangement which aggravated Jo and Hannah to the last degree. On Monday morning the weather was in that undecided state which is more exasperating than a steady pour. It drizzled a little, shone a little, blew a little, and didn't make up its mind till it was too late for any one else to make up theirs. Amy was up at dawn, hustling people out of their beds and through their breakfasts, that the house might be got in order. The parlor struck her as looking uncommonly shabby; but without stopping to sigh for what she had not, she skilfully made the best of what she had, arranging chairs over the worn places in the carpet, covering stains on the walls with pictures framed in ivy, and filling up empty corners with home-made statuary, which gave an artistic air to the room, as did the lovely vases of flowers Jo scattered about.\n\nThe lunch looked charmingly; and as she surveyed it, she sincerely hoped it would taste well, and that the borrowed glass, china, and silver would get safely home again. The carriages were promised, Meg and mother were all ready to do the honors, Beth was able to help Hannah behind the scenes, Jo had engaged to be as lively and amiable as an absent mind, an aching head, and a very decided disapproval of everybody and everything would allow, and, as she wearily dressed, Amy cheered herself with anticipations of the happy moment, when, lunch safely over, she should drive away with her friends for an afternoon of artistic delights; for the \"cherry-bounce\" and the broken bridge were her strong points.\n\nThen came two hours of suspense, during which she vibrated from parlor to porch, while public opinion varied like the weathercock. A smart shower at eleven had evidently quenched the enthusiasm of the young ladies who were to arrive at twelve, for nobody came; and at two the exhausted family sat down in a blaze of sunshine to consume the perishable portions of the feast, that nothing might be lost.\n\n\"No doubt about the weather to-day; they will certainly come, so we must fly round and be ready for them,\" said Amy, as the sun woke her next morning. She spoke briskly, but in her secret soul she wished she had said nothing about Tuesday, for her interest, like her cake, was getting a little stale.\n\n\"I can't get any lobsters, so you will have to do without salad to-day,\" said Mr. March, coming in half an hour later, with an expression of placid despair.\n\n\"Use the chicken, then; the toughness won't matter in a salad,\" advised his wife.\n\n\"Hannah left it on the kitchen-table a minute, and the kittens got at it. I'm very sorry, Amy,\" added Beth, who was still a patroness of cats.\n\n\"Then I _must_ have a lobster, for tongue alone won't do,\" said Amy decidedly.\n\n\"Shall I rush into town and demand one?\" asked Jo, with the magnanimity of a martyr.\n\n\"You'd come bringing it home under your arm, without any paper, just to try me. I'll go myself,\" answered Amy, whose temper was beginning to fail.\n\nShrouded in a thick veil and armed with a genteel travelling-basket, she departed, feeling that a cool drive would soothe her ruffled spirit, and fit her for the labors of the day. After some delay, the object of her desire was procured, likewise a bottle of dressing, to prevent further loss of time at home, and off she drove again, well pleased with her own forethought.\n\nAs the omnibus contained only one other passenger, a sleepy old lady, Amy pocketed her veil, and beguiled the tedium of the way by trying to find out where all her money had gone to. So busy was she with her card full of refractory figures that she did not observe a new-comer, who entered without stopping the vehicle, till a masculine voice said, \"Good-morning, Miss March,\" and, looking up, she beheld one of Laurie's most elegant college friends. Fervently hoping that he would get out before she did, Amy utterly ignored the basket at her feet, and, congratulating herself that she had on her new travelling dress, returned the young man's greeting with her usual suavity and spirit.\n\nThey got on excellently; for Amy's chief care was soon set at rest by learning that the gentleman would leave first, and she was chatting away in a peculiarly lofty strain, when the old lady got out. In stumbling to the door, she upset the basket, and--oh, horror!--the lobster, in all its vulgar size and brilliancy, was revealed to the highborn eyes of a Tudor.\n\n\"By Jove, she's forgotten her dinner!\" cried the unconscious youth, poking the scarlet monster into its place with his cane, and preparing to hand out the basket after the old lady.\n\n\"Please don't--it's--it's mine,\" murmured Amy, with a face nearly as red as her fish.\n\n\"Oh, really, I beg pardon; it's an uncommonly fine one, isn't it?\" said Tudor, with great presence of mind, and an air of sober interest that did credit to his breeding.\n\nAmy recovered herself in a breath, set her basket boldly on the seat, and said, laughing,--\n\n\"Don't you wish you were to have some of the salad he's to make, and to see the charming young ladies who are to eat it?\"\n\nNow that was tact, for two of the ruling foibles of the masculine mind were touched: the lobster was instantly surrounded by a halo of pleasing reminiscences, and curiosity about \"the charming young ladies\" diverted his mind from the comical mishap.\n\n\"I suppose he'll laugh and joke over it with Laurie, but I sha'n't see them; that's a comfort,\" thought Amy, as Tudor bowed and departed.\n\nShe did not mention this meeting at home (though she discovered that, thanks to the upset, her new dress was much damaged by the rivulets of dressing that meandered down the skirt), but went through with the preparations which now seemed more irksome than before; and at twelve o'clock all was ready again. Feeling that the neighbors were interested in her movements, she wished to efface the memory of yesterday's failure by a grand success to-day; so she ordered the \"cherry-bounce,\" and drove away in state to meet and escort her guests to the banquet.\n\n\"There's the rumble, they're coming! I'll go into the porch to meet them; it looks hospitable, and I want the poor child to have a good time after all her trouble,\" said Mrs. March, suiting the action to the word. But after one glance, she retired, with an indescribable expression, for, looking quite lost in the big carriage, sat Amy and one young lady.\n\n\"Run, Beth, and help Hannah clear half the things off the table; it will be too absurd to put a luncheon for twelve before a single girl,\" cried Jo, hurrying away to the lower regions, too excited to stop even for a laugh.\n\nIn came Amy, quite calm, and delightfully cordial to the one guest who had kept her promise; the rest of the family, being of a dramatic turn, played their parts equally well, and Miss Eliott found them a most hilarious set; for it was impossible to entirely control the merriment which possessed them. The remodelled lunch being gayly partaken of, the studio and garden visited, and art discussed with enthusiasm, Amy ordered a buggy (alas for the elegant cherry-bounce!) and drove her friend quietly about the neighborhood till sunset, when \"the party went out.\"\n\nAs she came walking in, looking very tired, but as composed as ever, she observed that every vestige of the unfortunate _fête_ had disappeared, except a suspicious pucker about the corners of Jo's mouth.\n\n\"You've had a lovely afternoon for your drive, dear,\" said her mother, as respectfully as if the whole twelve had come.\n\n\"Miss Eliott is a very sweet girl, and seemed to enjoy herself, I thought,\" observed Beth, with unusual warmth.\n\n\"Could you spare me some of your cake? I really need some, I have so much company, and I can't make such delicious stuff as yours,\" asked Meg soberly.\n\n\"Take it all; I'm the only one here who likes sweet things, and it will mould before I can dispose of it,\" answered Amy, thinking with a sigh of the generous store she had laid in for such an end as this.\n\n\"It's a pity Laurie isn't here to help us,\" began Jo, as they sat down to ice-cream and salad for the second time in two days.\n\nA warning look from her mother checked any further remarks, and the whole family ate in heroic silence, till Mr. March mildly observed, \"Salad was one of the favorite dishes of the ancients, and Evelyn\"--here a general explosion of laughter cut short the \"history of sallets,\" to the great surprise of the learned gentleman.\n\n\"Bundle everything into a basket and send it to the Hummels: Germans like messes. I'm sick of the sight of this; and there's no reason you should all die of a surfeit because I've been a fool,\" cried Amy, wiping her eyes.\n\n\"I thought I _should_ have died when I saw you two girls rattling about in the what-you-call-it, like two little kernels in a very big nutshell, and mother waiting in state to receive the throng,\" sighed Jo, quite spent with laughter.\n\n\"I'm very sorry you were disappointed, dear, but we all did our best to satisfy you,\" said Mrs. March, in a tone full of motherly regret.\n\n\"I _am_ satisfied; I've done what I undertook, and it's not my fault that it failed; I comfort myself with that,\" said Amy, with a little quiver in her voice. \"I thank you all very much for helping me, and I'll thank you still more if you won't allude to it for a month, at least.\"\n\nNo one did for several months; but the word \"_fête_\" always produced a general smile, and Laurie's birthday gift to Amy was a tiny coral lobster in the shape of a charm for her watch-guard.\n\n# LITERARY LESSONS.\n\nFortune suddenly smiled upon Jo, and dropped a good-luck penny in her path. Not a golden penny, exactly, but I doubt if half a million would have given more real happiness than did the little sum that came to her in this wise.\n\nEvery few weeks she would shut herself up in her room, put on her scribbling suit, and \"fall into a vortex,\" as she expressed it, writing away at her novel with all her heart and soul, for till that was finished she could find no peace. Her \"scribbling suit\" consisted of a black woollen pinafore on which she could wipe her pen at will, and a cap of the same material, adorned with a cheerful red bow, into which she bundled her hair when the decks were cleared for action. This cap was a beacon to the inquiring eyes of her family, who during these periods kept their distance, merely popping in their heads semi-occasionally, to ask, with interest, \"Does genius burn, Jo?\" They did not always venture even to ask this question, but took an observation of the cap, and judged accordingly. If this expressive article of dress was drawn low upon the forehead, it was a sign that hard work was going on; in exciting moments it was pushed rakishly askew; and when despair seized the author it was plucked wholly off, and cast upon the floor. At such times the intruder silently withdrew; and not until the red bow was seen gayly erect upon the gifted brow, did any one dare address Jo.\n\nShe did not think herself a genius by any means; but when the writing fit came on, she gave herself up to it with entire abandon, and led a blissful life, unconscious of want, care, or bad weather, while she sat safe and happy in an imaginary world, full of friends almost as real and dear to her as any in the flesh. Sleep forsook her eyes, meals stood untasted, day and night were all too short to enjoy the happiness which blessed her only at such times, and made these hours worth living, even if they bore no other fruit. The divine afflatus usually lasted a week or two, and then she emerged from her \"vortex,\" hungry, sleepy, cross, or despondent.\n\nShe was just recovering from one of these attacks when she was prevailed upon to escort Miss Crocker to a lecture, and in return for her virtue was rewarded with a new idea. It was a People's Course, the lecture on the Pyramids, and Jo rather wondered at the choice of such a subject for such an audience, but took it for granted that some great social evil would be remedied or some great want supplied by unfolding the glories of the Pharaohs to an audience whose thoughts were busy with the price of coal and flour, and whose lives were spent in trying to solve harder riddles than that of the Sphinx.\n\nThey were early; and while Miss Crocker set the heel of her stocking, Jo amused herself by examining the faces of the people who occupied the seat with them. On her left were two matrons, with massive foreheads, and bonnets to match, discussing Woman's Rights and making tatting. Beyond sat a pair of humble lovers, artlessly holding each other by the hand, a sombre spinster eating peppermints out of a paper bag, and an old gentleman taking his preparatory nap behind a yellow bandanna. On her right, her only neighbor was a studious-looking lad absorbed in a newspaper.\n\nIt was a pictorial sheet, and Jo examined the work of art nearest her, idly wondering what unfortuitous concatenation of circumstances needed the melodramatic illustration of an Indian in full war costume, tumbling over a precipice with a wolf at his throat, while two infuriated young gentlemen, with unnaturally small feet and big eyes, were stabbing each other close by, and a dishevelled female was flying away in the background with her mouth wide open. Pausing to turn a page, the lad saw her looking, and, with boyish good-nature, offered half his paper, saying bluntly, \"Want to read it? That's a first-rate story.\"\n\nJo accepted it with a smile, for she had never outgrown her liking for lads, and soon found herself involved in the usual labyrinth of love, mystery, and murder, for the story belonged to that class of light literature in which the passions have a holiday, and when the author's invention fails, a grand catastrophe clears the stage of one half the _dramatis personæ_, leaving the other half to exult over their downfall.\n\n\"Prime, isn't it?\" asked the boy, as her eye went down the last paragraph of her portion.\n\n\"I think you and I could do as well as that if we tried,\" returned Jo, amused at his admiration of the trash.\n\n\"I should think I was a pretty lucky chap if I could. She makes a good living out of such stories, they say;\" and he pointed to the name of Mrs. S. L. A. N. G. Northbury, under the title of the tale.\n\n\"Do you know her?\" asked Jo, with sudden interest.\n\n\"No; but I read all her pieces, and I know a fellow who works in the office where this paper is printed.\"\n\n\"Do you say she makes a good living out of stories like this?\" and Jo looked more respectfully at the agitated group and thickly-sprinkled exclamation-points that adorned the page.\n\n\"Guess she does! She knows just what folks like, and gets paid well for writing it.\"\n\nHere the lecture began, but Jo heard very little of it, for while Prof. Sands was prosing away about Belzoni, Cheops, scarabei, and hieroglyphics, she was covertly taking down the address of the paper, and boldly resolving to try for the hundred-dollar prize offered in its columns for a sensational story. By the time the lecture ended and the audience awoke, she had built up a splendid fortune for herself (not the first founded upon paper), and was already deep in the concoction of her story, being unable to decide whether the duel should come before the elopement or after the murder.\n\nShe said nothing of her plan at home, but fell to work next day, much to the disquiet of her mother, who always looked a little anxious when \"genius took to burning.\" Jo had never tried this style before, contenting herself with very mild romances for the \"Spread Eagle.\" Her theatrical experience and miscellaneous reading were of service now, for they gave her some idea of dramatic effect, and supplied plot, language, and costumes. Her story was as full of desperation and despair as her limited acquaintance with those uncomfortable emotions enabled her to make it, and, having located it in Lisbon, she wound up with an earthquake, as a striking and appropriate _dénouement_. The manuscript was privately despatched, accompanied by a note, modestly saying that if the tale didn't get the prize, which the writer hardly dared expect, she would be very glad to receive any sum it might be considered worth.\n\nSix weeks is a long time to wait, and a still longer time for a girl to keep a secret; but Jo did both, and was just beginning to give up all hope of ever seeing her manuscript again, when a letter arrived which almost took her breath away; for on opening it, a check for a hundred dollars fell into her lap. For a minute she stared at it as if it had been a snake, then she read her letter and began to cry. If the amiable gentleman who wrote that kindly note could have known what intense happiness he was giving a fellow-creature, I think he would devote his leisure hours, if he has any, to that amusement; for Jo valued the letter more than the money, because it was encouraging; and after years of effort it was _so_ pleasant to find that she had learned to do something, though it was only to write a sensation story.\n\nA prouder young woman was seldom seen than she, when, having composed herself, she electrified the family by appearing before them with the letter in one hand, the check in the other, announcing that she had won the prize. Of course there was a great jubilee, and when the story came every one read and praised it; though after her father had told her that the language was good, the romance fresh and hearty, and the tragedy quite thrilling, he shook his head, and said in his unworldly way,--\n\n\"You can do better than this, Jo. Aim at the highest, and never mind the money.\"\n\n\"_I_ think the money is the best part of it. What _will_ you do with such a fortune?\" asked Amy, regarding the magic slip of paper with a reverential eye.\n\n\"Send Beth and mother to the seaside for a month or two,\" answered Jo promptly.\n\n\"Oh, how splendid! No, I can't do it, dear, it would be so selfish,\" cried Beth, who had clapped her thin hands, and taken a long breath, as if pining for fresh ocean-breezes; then stopped herself, and motioned away the check which her sister waved before her.\n\n\"Ah, but you shall go, I've set my heart on it; that's what I tried for, and that's why I succeeded. I never get on when I think of myself alone, so it will help me to work for you, don't you see? Besides, Marmee needs the change, and she won't leave you, so you _must_ go. Won't it be fun to see you come home plump and rosy again? Hurrah for Dr. Jo, who always cures her patients!\"\n\nTo the sea side they went, after much discussion; and though Beth didn't come home as plump and rosy as could be desired, she was much better, while Mrs. March declared she felt ten years younger; so Jo was satisfied with the investment of her prize money, and fell to work with a cheery spirit, bent on earning more of those delightful checks. She did earn several that year, and began to feel herself a power in the house; for by the magic of a pen, her \"rubbish\" turned into comforts for them all. \"The Duke's Daughter\" paid the butcher's bill, \"A Phantom Hand\" put down a new carpet, and the \"Curse of the Coventrys\" proved the blessing of the Marches in the way of groceries and gowns.\n\nWealth is certainly a most desirable thing, but poverty has its sunny side, and one of the sweet uses of adversity is the genuine satisfaction which comes from hearty work of head or hand; and to the inspiration of necessity, we owe half the wise, beautiful, and useful blessings of the world. Jo enjoyed a taste of this satisfaction, and ceased to envy richer girls, taking great comfort in the knowledge that she could supply her own wants, and need ask no one for a penny.\n\nLittle notice was taken of her stories, but they found a market; and, encouraged by this fact, she resolved to make a bold stroke for fame and fortune. Having copied her novel for the fourth time, read it to all her confidential friends, and submitted it with fear and trembling to three publishers, she at last disposed of it, on condition that she would cut it down one third, and omit all the parts which she particularly admired.\n\n\"Now I must either bundle it back into my tin-kitchen to mould, pay for printing it myself, or chop it up to suit purchasers, and get what I can for it. Fame is a very good thing to have in the house, but cash is more convenient; so I wish to take the sense of the meeting on this important subject,\" said Jo, calling a family council.\n\n\"Don't spoil your book, my girl, for there is more in it than you know, and the idea is well worked out. Let it wait and ripen,\" was her father's advice; and he practised as he preached, having waited patiently thirty years for fruit of his own to ripen, and being in no haste to gather it, even now, when it was sweet and mellow.\n\n\"It seems to me that Jo will profit more by making the trial than by waiting,\" said Mrs. March. \"Criticism is the best test of such work, for it will show her both unsuspected merits and faults, and help her to do better next time. We are too partial; but the praise and blame of outsiders will prove useful, even if she gets but little money.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Jo, knitting her brows, \"that's just it; I've been fussing over the thing so long, I really don't know whether it's good, bad, or indifferent. It will be a great help to have cool, impartial persons take a look at it, and tell me what they think of it.\"\n\n\"I wouldn't leave out a word of it; you'll spoil it if you do, for the interest of the story is more in the minds than in the actions of the people, and it will be all a muddle if you don't explain as you go on,\" said Meg, who firmly believed that this book was the most remarkable novel ever written.\n\n\"But Mr. Allen says, 'Leave out the explanations, make it brief and dramatic, and let the characters tell the story,'\" interrupted Jo, turning to the publisher's note.\n\n\"Do as he tells you; he knows what will sell, and we don't. Make a good, popular book, and get as much money as you can. By and by, when, you've got a name, you can afford to digress, and have philosophical and metaphysical people in your novels,\" said Amy, who took a strictly practical view of the subject.\n\n\"Well,\" said Jo, laughing, \"if my people _are_ 'philosophical and metaphysical,' it isn't my fault, for I know nothing about such things, except what I hear father say, sometimes. If I've got some of his wise ideas jumbled up with my romance, so much the better for me. Now, Beth, what do you say?\"\n\n\"I should so like to see it printed _soon_,\" was all Beth said, and smiled in saying it; but there was an unconscious emphasis on the last word, and a wistful look in the eyes that never lost their childlike candor, which chilled Jo's heart, for a minute, with a foreboding fear, and decided her to make her little venture \"soon.\"\n\nSo, with Spartan firmness, the young authoress laid her first-born on her table, and chopped it up as ruthlessly as any ogre. In the hope of pleasing every one, she took every one's advice; and, like the old man and his donkey in the fable, suited nobody.\n\nHer father liked the metaphysical streak which had unconsciously got into it; so that was allowed to remain, though she had her doubts about it. Her mother thought that there _was_ a trifle too much description; out, therefore, it nearly all came, and with it many necessary links in the story. Meg admired the tragedy; so Jo piled up the agony to suit her, while Amy objected to the fun, and, with the best intentions in life, Jo quenched the sprightly scenes which relieved the sombre character of the story. Then, to complete the ruin, she cut it down one third, and confidingly sent the poor little romance, like a picked robin, out into the big, busy world, to try its fate.\n\nWell, it was printed, and she got three hundred dollars for it; likewise plenty of praise and blame, both so much greater than she expected that she was thrown into a state of bewilderment, from which it took her some time to recover.\n\n\"You said, mother, that criticism would help me; but how can it, when it's so contradictory that I don't know whether I've written a promising book or broken all the ten commandments?\" cried poor Jo, turning over a heap of notices, the perusal of which filled her with pride and joy one minute, wrath and dire dismay the next. \"This man says 'An exquisite book, full of truth, beauty, and earnestness; all is sweet, pure, and healthy,'\" continued the perplexed authoress. \"The next, 'The theory of the book is bad, full of morbid fancies, spiritualistic ideas, and unnatural characters.' Now, as I had no theory of any kind, don't believe in Spiritualism, and copied my characters from life, I don't see how this critic _can_ be right. Another says, 'It's one of the best American novels which has appeared for years' (I know better than that); and the next asserts that 'though it is original, and written with great force and feeling, it is a dangerous book.' 'Tisn't! Some make fun of it, some over-praise, and nearly all insist that I had a deep theory to expound, when I only wrote it for the pleasure and the money. I wish I'd printed it whole or not at all, for I do hate to be so misjudged.\"\n\nHer family and friends administered comfort and commendation liberally; yet it was a hard time for sensitive, high-spirited Jo, who meant so well, and had apparently done so ill. But it did her good, for those whose opinion had real value gave her the criticism which is an author's best education; and when the first soreness was over, she could laugh at her poor little book, yet believe in it still, and feel herself the wiser and stronger for the buffeting she had received.\n\n\"Not being a genius, like Keats, it won't kill me,\" she said stoutly; \"and I've got the joke on my side, after all; for the parts that were taken straight out of real life are denounced as impossible and absurd, and the scenes that I made up out of my own silly head are pronounced 'charmingly natural, tender, and true.' So I'll comfort myself with that; and when I'm ready, I'll up again and take another.\"\n\n# DOMESTIC EXPERIENCES.\n\nLike most other young matrons, Meg began her married life with the determination to be a model housekeeper. John should find home a paradise; he should always see a smiling face, should fare sumptuously every day, and never know the loss of a button. She brought so much love, energy, and cheerfulness to the work that she could not but succeed, in spite of some obstacles. Her paradise was not a tranquil one; for the little woman fussed, was over-anxious to please, and bustled about like a true Martha, cumbered with many cares. She was too tired, sometimes, even to smile; John grew dyspeptic after a course of dainty dishes, and ungratefully demanded plain fare. As for buttons, she soon learned to wonder where they went, to shake her head over the carelessness of men, and to threaten to make him sew them on himself, and then see if _his_ work would stand impatient tugs and clumsy fingers any better than hers.\n\nThey were very happy, even after they discovered that they couldn't live on love alone. John did not find Meg's beauty diminished, though she beamed at him from behind the familiar coffee-pot; nor did Meg miss any of the romance from the daily parting, when her husband followed up his kiss with the tender inquiry, \"Shall I send home veal or mutton for dinner, darling?\" The little house ceased to be a glorified bower, but it became a home, and the young couple soon felt that it was a change for the better. At first they played keep-house, and frolicked over it like children; then John took steadily to business, feeling the cares of the head of a family upon his shoulders; and Meg laid by her cambric wrappers, put on a big apron, and fell to work, as before said, with more energy than discretion.\n\nWhile the cooking mania lasted she went through Mrs. Cornelius's Receipt Book as if it were a mathematical exercise, working out the problems with patience and care. Sometimes her family were invited in to help eat up a too bounteous feast of successes, or Lotty would be privately despatched with a batch of failures, which were to be concealed from all eyes in the convenient stomachs of the little Hummels. An evening with John over the account-books usually produced a temporary lull in the culinary enthusiasm, and a frugal fit would ensue, during which the poor man was put through a course of bread-pudding, hash, and warmed-over coffee, which tried his soul, although he bore it with praiseworthy fortitude. Before the golden mean was found, however, Meg added to her domestic possessions what young couples seldom get on long without,--a family jar.\n\nFired with a housewifely wish to see her store-room stocked with home-made preserves, she undertook to put up her own currant jelly. John was requested to order home a dozen or so of little pots, and an extra quantity of sugar, for their own currants were ripe, and were to be attended to at once. As John firmly believed that \"my wife\" was equal to anything, and took a natural pride in her skill, he resolved that she should be gratified, and their only crop of fruit laid by in a most pleasing form for winter use. Home came four dozen delightful little pots, half a barrel of sugar, and a small boy to pick the currants for her. With her pretty hair tucked into a little cap, arms bared to the elbow, and a checked apron which had a coquettish look in spite of the bib, the young housewife fell to work, feeling no doubts about her success; for hadn't she seen Hannah do it hundreds of times? The array of pots rather amazed her at first, but John was so fond of jelly, and the nice little jars would look so well on the top shelf, that Meg resolved to fill them all, and spent a long day picking, boiling, straining, and fussing over her jelly. She did her best; she asked advice of Mrs. Cornelius; she racked her brain to remember what Hannah did that she had left undone; she reboiled, resugared, and restrained, but that dreadful stuff wouldn't \"_jell_.\"\n\nShe longed to run home, bib and all, and ask mother to lend a hand, but John and she had agreed that they would never annoy any one with their private worries, experiments, or quarrels. They had laughed over that last word as if the idea it suggested was a most preposterous one; but they had held to their resolve, and whenever they could get on without help they did so, and no one interfered, for Mrs. March had advised the plan. So Meg wrestled alone with the refractory sweetmeats all that hot summer day, and at five o'clock sat down in her topsy-turvy kitchen, wrung her bedaubed hands, lifted up her voice and wept.\n\nNow, in the first flush of the new life, she had often said,--\n\n\"My husband shall always feel free to bring a friend home whenever he likes. I shall always be prepared; there shall be no flurry, no scolding, no discomfort, but a neat house, a cheerful wife, and a good dinner. John, dear, never stop to ask my leave, invite whom you please, and be sure of a welcome from me.\"\n\nHow charming that was, to be sure! John quite glowed with pride to hear her say it, and felt what a blessed thing it was to have a superior wife. But, although they had had company from time to time, it never happened to be unexpected, and Meg had never had an opportunity to distinguish herself till now. It always happens so in this vale of tears; there is an inevitability about such things which we can only wonder at, deplore, and bear as we best can.\n\nIf John had not forgotten all about the jelly, it really would have been unpardonable in him to choose that day, of all the days in the year, to bring a friend home to dinner unexpectedly. Congratulating himself that a handsome repast had been ordered that morning, feeling sure that it would be ready to the minute, and indulging in pleasant anticipations of the charming effect it would produce, when his pretty wife came running out to meet him, he escorted his friend to his mansion, with the irrepressible satisfaction of a young host and husband.\n\nIt is a world of disappointments, as John discovered when he reached the Dove-cote. The front door usually stood hospitably open; now it was not only shut, but locked, and yesterday's mud still adorned the steps. The parlor-windows were closed and curtained, no picture of the pretty wife sewing on the piazza, in white, with a distracting little bow in her hair, or a bright-eyed hostess, smiling a shy welcome as she greeted her guest. Nothing of the sort, for not a soul appeared, but a sanguinary-looking boy asleep under the currant-bushes.\n\n\"I'm afraid something has happened. Step into the garden, Scott, while I look up Mrs. Brooke,\" said John, alarmed at the silence and solitude.\n\nRound the house he hurried, led by a pungent smell of burnt sugar, and Mr. Scott strolled after him, with a queer look on his face. He paused discreetly at a distance when Brooke disappeared; but he could both see and hear, and, being a bachelor, enjoyed the prospect mightily.\n\nIn the kitchen reigned confusion and despair; one edition of jelly was trickled from pot to pot, another lay upon the floor, and a third was burning gayly on the stove. Lotty, with Teutonic phlegm, was calmly eating bread and currant wine, for the jelly was still in a hopelessly liquid state, while Mrs. Brooke, with her apron over her head, sat sobbing dismally.\n\n\"My dearest girl, what is the matter?\" cried John, rushing in, with awful visions of scalded hands, sudden news of affliction, and secret consternation at the thought of the guest in the garden.\n\n\"O John, I _am_ so tired and hot and cross and worried! I've been at it till I'm all worn out. Do come and help me or I _shall_ die!\" and the exhausted housewife cast herself upon his breast, giving him a sweet welcome in every sense of the word, for her pinafore had been baptized at the same time as the floor.\n\n\"What worries you, dear? Has anything dreadful happened?\" asked the anxious John, tenderly kissing the crown of the little cap, which was all askew.\n\n\"Yes,\" sobbed Meg despairingly.\n\n\"Tell me quick, then. Don't cry, I can bear anything better than that. Out with it, love.\"\n\n\"The--the jelly won't jell and I don't know what to do!\"\n\nJohn Brooke laughed then as he never dared to laugh afterward; and the derisive Scott smiled involuntarily as he heard the hearty peal, which put the finishing stroke to poor Meg's woe.\n\n\"Is that all? Fling it out of window, and don't bother any more about it. I'll buy you quarts if you want it; but for heaven's sake don't have hysterics, for I've brought Jack Scott home to dinner, and--\"\n\nJohn got no further, for Meg cast him off, and clasped her hands with a tragic gesture as she fell into a chair, exclaiming in a tone of mingled indignation, reproach, and dismay,--\n\n\"A man to dinner, and everything in a mess! John Brooke, how _could_ you do such a thing?\"\n\n\"Hush, he's in the garden! I forgot the confounded jelly, but it can't be helped now,\" said John, surveying the prospect with an anxious eye.\n\n\"You ought to have sent word, or told me this morning, and you ought to have remembered how busy I was,\" continued Meg petulantly; for even turtle-doves will peck when ruffled.\n\n\"I didn't know it this morning, and there was no time to send word, for I met him on the way out. I never thought of asking leave, when you have always told me to do as I liked. I never tried it before, and hang me if I ever do again!\" added John, with an aggrieved air.\n\n\"I should hope not! Take him away at once; I can't see him, and there isn't any dinner.\"\n\n\"Well, I like that! Where's the beef and vegetables I sent home, and the pudding you promised?\" cried John, rushing to the larder.\n\n\"I hadn't time to cook anything; I meant to dine at mother's. I'm sorry, but I was _so_ busy;\" and Meg's tears began again.\n\nJohn was a mild man, but he was human; and after a long day's work, to come home tired, hungry, and hopeful, to find a chaotic house, an empty table, and a cross wife was not exactly conducive to repose of mind or manner. He restrained himself, however, and the little squall would have blown over, but for one unlucky word.\n\n\"It's a scrape, I acknowledge; but if you will lend a hand, we'll pull through, and have a good time yet. Don't cry, dear, but just exert yourself a bit, and knock us up something to eat. We're both as hungry as hunters, so we sha'n't mind what it is. Give us the cold meat, and bread and cheese; we won't ask for jelly.\"\n\nHe meant it for a good-natured joke; but that one word sealed his fate. Meg thought it was _too_ cruel to hint about her sad failure, and the last atom of patience vanished as he spoke.\n\n\"You must get yourself out of the scrape as you can; I'm too used up to 'exert' myself for any one. It's like a man to propose a bone and vulgar bread and cheese for company. I won't have anything of the sort in my house. Take that Scott up to mother's, and tell him I'm away, sick, dead,--anything. I won't see him, and you two can laugh at me and my jelly as much as you like: you won't have anything else here;\" and having delivered her defiance all in one breath, Meg cast away her pinafore, and precipitately left the field to bemoan herself in her own room.\n\nWhat those two creatures did in her absence, she never knew; but Mr. Scott was not taken \"up to mother's,\" and when Meg descended, after they had strolled away together, she found traces of a promiscuous lunch which filled her with horror. Lotty reported that they had eaten \"a much, and greatly laughed, and the master bid her throw away all the sweet stuff, and hide the pots.\"\n\nMeg longed to go and tell mother; but a sense of shame at her own short-comings, of loyalty to John, \"who might be cruel, but nobody should know it,\" restrained her; and after a summary clearing up, she dressed herself prettily, and sat down to wait for John to come and be forgiven.\n\nUnfortunately, John didn't come, not seeing the matter in that light. He had carried it off as a good joke with Scott, excused his little wife as well as he could, and played the host so hospitably that his friend enjoyed the impromptu dinner, and promised to come again. But John was angry, though he did not show it; he felt that Meg had got him into a scrape, and then deserted him in his hour of need. \"It wasn't fair to tell a man to bring folks home any time, with perfect freedom, and when he took you at your word, to flame up and blame him, and leave him in the lurch, to be laughed at or pitied. No, by George, it wasn't! and Meg must know it.\" He had fumed inwardly during the feast, but when the flurry was over, and he strolled home, after seeing Scott off, a milder mood came over him. \"Poor little thing! it was hard upon her when she tried so heartily to please me. She was wrong, of course, but then she was young. I must be patient and teach her.\" He hoped she had not gone home--he hated gossip and interference. For a minute he was ruffled again at the mere thought of it; and then the fear that Meg would cry herself sick softened his heart, and sent him on at a quicker pace, resolving to be calm and kind, but firm, quite firm, and show her where she had failed in her duty to her spouse.\n\nMeg likewise resolved to be \"calm and kind, but firm,\" and show _him_ his duty. She longed to run to meet him, and beg pardon, and be kissed and comforted, as she was sure of being; but, of course, she did nothing of the sort, and when she saw John coming, began to hum quite naturally, as she rocked and sewed, like a lady of leisure in her best parlor.\n\nJohn was a little disappointed not to find a tender Niobe; but, feeling that his dignity demanded the first apology, he made none, only came leisurely in, and laid himself upon the sofa, with the singularly relevant remark,--\n\n\"We are going to have a new moon, my dear.\"\n\n\"I've no objection,\" was Meg's equally soothing remark.\n\nA few other topics of general interest were introduced by Mr. Brooke, and wet-blanketed by Mrs. Brooke, and conversation languished. John went to one window, unfolded his paper, and wrapped himself in it, figuratively speaking. Meg went to the other window, and sewed as if new rosettes for her slippers were among the necessaries of life. Neither spoke; both looked quite \"calm and firm,\" and both felt desperately uncomfortable.\n\n\"Oh dear,\" thought Meg, \"married life is very trying, and does need infinite patience, as well as love, as mother says.\" The word \"mother\" suggested other maternal counsels, given long ago, and received with unbelieving protests.\n\n\"John is a good man, but he has his faults, and you must learn to see and bear with them, remembering your own. He is very decided, but never will be obstinate, if you reason kindly, not oppose impatiently. He is very accurate, and particular about the truth--a good trait, though you call him 'fussy.' Never deceive him by look or word, Meg, and he will give you the confidence you deserve, the support you need. He has a temper, not like ours,--one flash, and then all over,--but the white, still anger, that is seldom stirred, but once kindled, is hard to quench. Be careful, very careful, not to wake this anger against yourself, for peace and happiness depend on keeping his respect. Watch yourself, be the first to ask pardon if you both err, and guard against the little piques, misunderstandings, and hasty words that often pave the way for bitter sorrow and regret.\"\n\nThese words came back to Meg, as she sat sewing in the sunset, especially the last. This was the first serious disagreement; her own hasty speeches sounded both silly and unkind, as she recalled them, her own anger looked childish now, and thoughts of poor John coming home to such a scene quite melted her heart. She glanced at him with tears in her eyes, but he did not see them; she put down her work and got up, thinking, \"I _will_ be the first to say, 'Forgive me,'\" but he did not seem to hear her; she went very slowly across the room, for pride was hard to swallow, and stood by him, but he did not turn his head. For a minute she felt as if she really couldn't do it; then came the thought, \"This is the beginning, I'll do my part, and have nothing to reproach myself with,\" and stooping down, she softly kissed her husband on the forehead. Of course that settled it; the penitent kiss was better than a world of words, and John had her on his knee in a minute, saying tenderly,--\n\n\"It was too bad to laugh at the poor little jelly-pots. Forgive me, dear, I never will again!\"\n\nBut he did, oh bless you, yes, hundreds of times, and so did Meg, both declaring that it was the sweetest jelly they ever made; for family peace was preserved in that little family jar.\n\nAfter this, Meg had Mr. Scott to dinner by special invitation, and served him up a pleasant feast without a cooked wife for the first course; on which occasion she was so gay and gracious, and made everything go off so charmingly, that Mr. Scott told John he was a happy fellow, and shook his head over the hardships of bachelorhood all the way home.\n\nIn the autumn, new trials and experiences came to Meg. Sallie Moffat renewed her friendship, was always running out for a dish of gossip at the little house, or inviting \"that poor dear\" to come in and spend the day at the big house. It was pleasant, for in dull weather Meg often felt lonely; all were busy at home, John absent till night, and nothing to do but sew, or read, or potter about. So it naturally fell out that Meg got into the way of gadding and gossiping with her friend. Seeing Sallie's pretty things made her long for such, and pity herself because she had not got them. Sallie was very kind, and often offered her the coveted trifles; but Meg declined them, knowing that John wouldn't like it; and then this foolish little woman went and did what John disliked infinitely worse.\n\nShe knew her husband's income, and she loved to feel that he trusted her, not only with his happiness, but what some men seem to value more,--his money. She knew where it was, was free to take what she liked, and all he asked was that she should keep account of every penny, pay bills once a month, and remember that she was a poor man's wife. Till now, she had done well, been prudent and exact, kept her little account-books neatly, and showed them to him monthly without fear. But that autumn the serpent got into Meg's paradise, and tempted her, like many a modern Eve, not with apples, but with dress. Meg didn't like to be pitied and made to feel poor; it irritated her, but she was ashamed to confess it, and now and then she tried to console herself by buying something pretty, so that Sallie needn't think she had to economize. She always felt wicked after it, for the pretty things were seldom necessaries; but then they cost so little, it wasn't worth worrying about; so the trifles increased unconsciously, and in the shopping excursions she was no longer a passive looker-on.\n\nBut the trifles cost more than one would imagine; and when she cast up her accounts at the end of the month, the sum total rather scared her. John was busy that month, and left the bills to her; the next month he was absent; but the third he had a grand quarterly settling up, and Meg never forgot it. A few days before she had done a dreadful thing, and it weighed upon her conscience. Sallie had been buying silks, and Meg longed for a new one,--just a handsome light one for parties, her black silk was so common, and thin things for evening wear were only proper for girls. Aunt March usually gave the sisters a present of twenty-five dollars apiece at New Year; that was only a month to wait, and here was a lovely violet silk going at a bargain, and she had the money, if she only dared to take it. John always said what was his was hers; but would he think it right to spend not only the prospective five-and-twenty, but another five-and-twenty out of the household fund? That was the question. Sallie had urged her to do it, had offered to loan the money, and with the best intentions in life, had tempted Meg beyond her strength. In an evil moment the shopman held up the lovely, shimmering folds, and said, \"A bargain, I assure you, ma'am.\" She answered, \"I'll take it;\" and it was cut off and paid for, and Sallie had exulted, and she had laughed as if it were a thing of no consequence, and driven away, feeling as if she had stolen something, and the police were after her.\n\nWhen she got home, she tried to assuage the pangs of remorse by spreading forth the lovely silk; but it looked less silvery now, didn't become her, after all, and the words \"fifty dollars\" seemed stamped like a pattern down each breadth. She put it away; but it haunted her, not delightfully, as a new dress should, but dreadfully, like the ghost of a folly that was not easily laid. When John got out his books that night, Meg's heart sank, and for the first time in her married life, she was afraid of her husband. The kind, brown eyes looked as if they could be stern; and though he was unusually merry, she fancied he had found her out, but didn't mean to let her know it. The house-bills were all paid, the books all in order. John had praised her, and was undoing the old pocket-book which they called the \"bank,\" when Meg, knowing that it was quite empty, stopped his hand, saying nervously,--\n\n\"You haven't seen my private expense book yet.\"\n\nJohn never asked to see it; but she always insisted on his doing so, and used to enjoy his masculine amazement at the queer things women wanted, and made him guess what \"piping\" was, demand fiercely the meaning of a \"hug-me-tight,\" or wonder how a little thing composed of three rosebuds, a bit of velvet, and a pair of strings, could possibly be a bonnet, and cost five or six dollars. That night he looked as if he would like the fun of quizzing her figures and pretending to be horrified at her extravagance, as he often did, being particularly proud of his prudent wife.\n\nThe little book was brought slowly out, and laid down before him. Meg got behind his chair under pretence of smoothing the wrinkles out of his tired forehead, and standing there, she said, with her panic increasing with every word,--\n\n\"John, dear, I'm ashamed to show you my book, for I've really been dreadfully extravagant lately. I go about so much I must have things, you know, and Sallie advised my getting it, so I did; and my New-Year's money will partly pay for it: but I was sorry after I'd done it, for I knew you'd think it wrong in me.\"\n\nJohn laughed, and drew her round beside him, saying good-humoredly, \"Don't go and hide. I won't beat you if you _have_ got a pair of killing boots; I'm rather proud of my wife's feet, and don't mind if she does pay eight or nine dollars for her boots, if they are good ones.\"\n\nThat had been one of her last \"trifles,\" and John's eye had fallen on it as he spoke. \"Oh, what _will_ he say when he comes to that awful fifty dollars!\" thought Meg, with a shiver.\n\n\"It's worse than boots, it's a silk dress,\" she said, with the calmness of desperation, for she wanted the worst over.\n\n\"Well, dear, what is the 'dem'd total,' as Mr. Mantalini says?\"\n\nThat didn't sound like John, and she knew he was looking up at her with the straightforward look that she had always been ready to meet and answer with one as frank till now. She turned the page and her head at the same time, pointing to the sum which would have been bad enough without the fifty, but which was appalling to her with that added. For a minute the room was very still; then John said slowly,--but she could feel it cost him an effort to express no displeasure,--\n\n\"Well, I don't know that fifty is much for a dress, with all the furbelows and notions you have to have to finish it off these days.\"\n\n\"It isn't made or trimmed,\" sighed Meg faintly, for a sudden recollection of the cost still to be incurred quite overwhelmed her.\n\n\"Twenty-five yards of silk seems a good deal to cover one small woman, but I've no doubt my wife will look as fine as Ned Moffat's when she gets it on,\" said John dryly.\n\n\"I know you are angry, John, but I can't help it. I don't mean to waste your money, and I didn't think those little things would count up so. I can't resist them when I see Sallie buying all she wants, and pitying me because I don't. I try to be contented, but it is hard, and I'm tired of being poor.\"\n\nThe last words were spoken so low she thought he did not hear them, but he did, and they wounded him deeply, for he had denied himself many pleasures for Meg's sake. She could have bitten her tongue out the minute she had said it, for John pushed the books away, and got up, saying, with a little quiver in his voice, \"I was afraid of this; I do my best, Meg.\" If he had scolded her, or even shaken her, it would not have broken her heart like those few words. She ran to him and held him close, crying, with repentant tears, \"O John, my dear, kind, hard-working boy, I didn't mean it! It was so wicked, so untrue and ungrateful, how could I say it! Oh, how could I say it!\"\n\nHe was very kind, forgave her readily, and did not utter one reproach; but Meg knew that she had done and said a thing which would not be forgotten soon, although he might never allude to it again. She had promised to love him for better for worse; and then she, his wife, had reproached him with his poverty, after spending his earnings recklessly. It was dreadful; and the worst of it was John went on so quietly afterward, just as if nothing had happened, except that he stayed in town later, and worked at night when she had gone to cry herself to sleep. A week of remorse nearly made Meg sick; and the discovery that John had countermanded the order for his new great-coat reduced her to a state of despair which was pathetic to behold. He had simply said, in answer to her surprised inquiries as to the change, \"I can't afford it, my dear.\"\n\nMeg said no more, but a few minutes after he found her in the hall, with her face buried in the old great-coat, crying as if her heart would break.\n\nThey had a long talk that night, and Meg learned to love her husband better for his poverty, because it seemed to have made a man of him, given him the strength and courage to fight his own way, and taught him a tender patience with which to bear and comfort the natural longings and failures of those he loved.\n\nNext day she put her pride in her pocket, went to Sallie, told the truth, and asked her to buy the silk as a favor. The good-natured Mrs. Moffat willingly did so, and had the delicacy not to make her a present of it immediately afterward. Then Meg ordered home the great-coat, and, when John arrived, she put it on, and asked him how he liked her new silk gown. One can imagine what answer he made, how he received his present, and what a blissful state of things ensued. John came home early, Meg gadded no more; and that great-coat was put on in the morning by a very happy husband, and taken off at night by a most devoted little wife. So the year rolled round, and at midsummer there came to Meg a new experience,--the deepest and tenderest of a woman's life.\n\nLaurie came sneaking into the kitchen of the Dove-cote, one Saturday, with an excited face, and was received with the clash of cymbals; for Hannah clapped her hands with a saucepan in one and the cover in the other.\n\n\"How's the little mamma? Where is everybody? Why didn't you tell me before I came home?\" began Laurie, in a loud whisper.\n\n\"Happy as a queen, the dear! Every soul of 'em is upstairs a worshipin'; we didn't want no hurrycanes round. Now you go into the parlor, and I'll send 'em down to you,\" with which somewhat involved reply Hannah vanished, chuckling ecstatically.\n\nPresently Jo appeared, proudly bearing a flannel bundle laid forth upon a large pillow. Jo's face was very sober, but her eyes twinkled, and there was an odd sound in her voice of repressed emotion of some sort.\n\n\"Shut your eyes and hold out your arms,\" she said invitingly.\n\nLaurie backed precipitately into a corner, and put his hands behind him with an imploring gesture: \"No, thank you, I'd rather not. I shall drop it or smash it, as sure as fate.\"\n\n\"Then you sha'n't see your nevvy,\" said Jo decidedly, turning as if to go.\n\n\"I will, I will! only you must be responsible for damages;\" and, obeying orders, Laurie heroically shut his eyes while something was put into his arms. A peal of laughter from Jo, Amy, Mrs. March, Hannah, and John caused him to open them the next minute, to find himself invested with two babies instead of one.\n\nNo wonder they laughed, for the expression of his face was droll enough to convulse a Quaker, as he stood and stared wildly from the unconscious innocents to the hilarious spectators, with such dismay that Jo sat down on the floor and screamed.\n\n\"Twins, by Jupiter!\" was all he said for a minute; then, turning to the women with an appealing look that was comically piteous, he added, \"Take 'em quick, somebody! I'm going to laugh, and I shall drop 'em.\"\n\nJohn rescued his babies, and marched up and down, with one on each arm, as if already initiated into the mysteries of baby-tending, while Laurie laughed till the tears ran down his cheeks.\n\n\"It's the best joke of the season, isn't it? I wouldn't have you told, for I set my heart on surprising you, and I flatter myself I've done it,\" said Jo, when she got her breath.\n\n\"I never was more staggered in my life. Isn't it fun? Are they boys? What are you going to name them? Let's have another look. Hold me up, Jo; for upon my life it's one too many for me,\" returned Laurie, regarding the infants with the air of a big, benevolent Newfoundland looking at a pair of infantile kittens.\n\n\"Boy and girl. Aren't they beauties?\" said the proud papa, beaming upon the little, red squirmers as if they were unfledged angels.\n\n\"Most remarkable children I ever saw. Which is which?\" and Laurie bent like a well-sweep to examine the prodigies.\n\n\"Amy put a blue ribbon on the boy and a pink on the girl, French fashion, so you can always tell. Besides, one has blue eyes and one brown. Kiss them, Uncle Teddy,\" said wicked Jo.\n\n\"I'm afraid they mightn't like it,\" began Laurie, with unusual timidity in such matters.\n\n\"Of course they will; they are used to it now. Do it this minute, sir!\" commanded Jo, fearing he might propose a proxy.\n\nLaurie screwed up his face, and obeyed with a gingerly peck at each little cheek that produced another laugh, and made the babies squeal.\n\n\"There, I knew they didn't like it! That's the boy; see him kick; he hits out with his fists like a good one. Now then, young Brooke, pitch into a man of your own size, will you?\" cried Laurie, delighted with a poke in the face from a tiny fist, flapping aimlessly about.\n\n\"He's to be named John Laurence, and the girl Margaret, after mother and grandmother. We shall call her Daisy, so as not to have two Megs, and I suppose the mannie will be Jack, unless we find a better name,\" said Amy, with aunt-like interest.\n\n\"Name him Demijohn, and call him 'Demi' for short,\" said Laurie.\n\n\"Daisy and Demi,--just the thing! I _knew_ Teddy would do it,\" cried Jo, clapping her hands.\n\nTeddy certainly had done it that time, for the babies were \"Daisy\" and \"Demi\" to the end of the chapter.\n\n\"Come, Jo, it's time.\"\n\n\"For what?\"\n\n\"You don't mean to say you have forgotten that you promised to make half a dozen calls with me to-day?\"\n\n\"I've done a good many rash and foolish things in my life, but I don't think I ever was mad enough to say I'd make six calls in one day, when a single one upsets me for a week.\"\n\n\"Yes, you did; it was a bargain between us. I was to finish the crayon of Beth for you, and you were to go properly with me, and return our neighbors' visits.\"\n\n\"If it was fair--that was in the bond; and I stand to the letter of my bond, Shylock. There is a pile of clouds in the east; it's _not_ fair, and I don't go.\"\n\n\"Now, that's shirking. It's a lovely day, no prospect of rain, and you pride yourself on keeping promises; so be honorable; come and do your duty, and then be at peace for another six months.\"\n\nAt that minute Jo was particularly absorbed in dressmaking; for she was mantua-maker general to the family, and took especial credit to herself because she could use a needle as well as a pen. It was very provoking to be arrested in the act of a first trying-on, and ordered out to make calls in her best array, on a warm July day. She hated calls of the formal sort, and never made any till Amy compelled her with a bargain, bribe, or promise. In the present instance, there was no escape; and having clashed her scissors rebelliously, while protesting that she smelt thunder, she gave in, put away her work, and taking up her hat and gloves with an air of resignation, told Amy the victim was ready.\n\n\"Jo March, you are perverse enough to provoke a saint! You don't intend to make calls in that state, I hope,\" cried Amy, surveying her with amazement.\n\n\"Why not? I'm neat and cool and comfortable; quite proper for a dusty walk on a warm day. If people care more for my clothes than they do for me, I don't wish to see them. You can dress for both, and be as elegant as you please: it pays for you to be fine; it doesn't for me, and furbelows only worry me.\"\n\n\"Oh dear!\" sighed Amy; \"now she's in a contrary fit, and will drive me distracted before I can get her properly ready. I'm sure it's no pleasure to me to go to-day, but it's a debt we owe society, and there's no one to pay it but you and me. I'll do anything for you, Jo, if you'll only dress yourself nicely, and come and help me do the civil. You can talk so well, look so aristocratic in your best things, and behave so beautifully, if you try, that I'm proud of you. I'm afraid to go alone; do come and take care of me.\"\n\n\"You're an artful little puss to flatter and wheedle your cross old sister in that way. The idea of my being aristocratic and well-bred, and your being afraid to go anywhere alone! I don't know which is the most absurd. Well, I'll go if I must, and do my best. You shall be commander of the expedition, and I'll obey blindly; will that satisfy you?\" said Jo, with a sudden change from perversity to lamb-like submission.\n\n\"You're a perfect cherub! Now put on all your best things, and I'll tell you how to behave at each place, so that you will make a good impression. I want people to like you, and they would if you'd only try to be a little more agreeable. Do your hair the pretty way, and put the pink rose in your bonnet; it's becoming, and you look too sober in your plain suit. Take your light gloves and the embroidered handkerchief. We'll stop at Meg's, and borrow her white sunshade, and then you can have my dove-colored one.\"\n\nWhile Amy dressed, she issued her orders, and Jo obeyed them; not without entering her protest, however, for she sighed as she rustled into her new organdie, frowned darkly at herself as she tied her bonnet strings in an irreproachable bow, wrestled viciously with pins as she put on her collar, wrinkled up her features generally as she shook out the handkerchief, whose embroidery was as irritating to her nose as the present mission was to her feelings; and when she had squeezed her hands into tight gloves with three buttons and a tassel, as the last touch of elegance, she turned to Amy with an imbecile expression of countenance, saying meekly,--\n\n\"I'm perfectly miserable; but if you consider me presentable, I die happy.\"\n\n\"You are highly satisfactory; turn slowly round, and let me get a careful view.\" Jo revolved, and Amy gave a touch here and there, then fell back, with her head on one side, observing graciously, \"Yes, you'll do; your head is all I could ask, for that white bonnet _with_ the rose is quite ravishing. Hold back your shoulders, and carry your hands easily, no matter if your gloves do pinch. There's one thing you can do well, Jo, that is, wear a shawl--I can't; but it's very nice to see you, and I'm so glad Aunt March gave you that lovely one; it's simple, but handsome, and those folds over the arm are really artistic. Is the point of my mantle in the middle, and have I looped my dress evenly? I like to show my boots, for my feet _are_ pretty, though my nose isn't.\"\n\n\"You are a thing of beauty and a joy forever,\" said Jo, looking through her hand with the air of a connoisseur at the blue feather against the gold hair. \"Am I to drag my best dress through the dust, or loop it up, please, ma'am?\"\n\n\"Hold it up when you walk, but drop it in the house; the sweeping style suits you best, and you must learn to trail your skirts gracefully. You haven't half buttoned one cuff; do it at once. You'll never look finished if you are not careful about the little details, for they make up the pleasing whole.\"\n\nJo sighed, and proceeded to burst the buttons off her glove, in doing up her cuff; but at last both were ready, and sailed away, looking as \"pretty as picters,\" Hannah said, as she hung out of the upper window to watch them.\n\n\"Now, Jo dear, the Chesters consider themselves very elegant people, so I want you to put on your best deportment. Don't make any of your abrupt remarks, or do anything odd, will you? Just be calm, cool, and quiet,--that's safe and ladylike; and you can easily do it for fifteen minutes,\" said Amy, as they approached the first place, having borrowed the white parasol and been inspected by Meg, with a baby on each arm.\n\n\"Let me see. 'Calm, cool, and quiet,'--yes, I think I can promise that. I've played the part of a prim young lady on the stage, and I'll try it off. My powers are great, as you shall see; so be easy in your mind, my child.\"\n\nAmy looked relieved, but naughty Jo took her at her word; for, during the first call, she sat with every limb gracefully composed, every fold correctly draped, calm as a summer sea, cool as a snow-bank, and as silent as a sphinx. In vain Mrs. Chester alluded to her \"charming novel,\" and the Misses Chester introduced parties, picnics, the opera, and the fashions; each and all were answered by a smile, a bow, and a demure \"Yes\" or \"No,\" with the chill on. In vain Amy telegraphed the word \"Talk,\" tried to draw her out, and administered covert pokes with her foot. Jo sat as if blandly unconscious of it all, with deportment like Maud's face, \"icily regular, splendidly null.\"\n\n\"What a haughty, uninteresting creature that oldest Miss March is!\" was the unfortunately audible remark of one of the ladies, as the door closed upon their guests. Jo laughed noiselessly all through the hall, but Amy looked disgusted at the failure of her instructions, and very naturally laid the blame upon Jo.\n\n\"How could you mistake me so? I merely meant you to be properly dignified and composed, and you made yourself a perfect stock and stone. Try to be sociable at the Lambs', gossip as other girls do, and be interested in dress and flirtations and whatever nonsense comes up. They move in the best society, are valuable persons for us to know, and I wouldn't fail to make a good impression there for anything.\"\n\n\"I'll be agreeable; I'll gossip and giggle, and have horrors and raptures over any trifle you like. I rather enjoy this, and now I'll imitate what is called 'a charming girl;' I can do it, for I have May Chester as a model, and I'll improve upon her. See if the Lambs don't say, 'What a lively, nice creature that Jo March is!'\"\n\nAmy felt anxious, as well she might, for when Jo turned freakish there was no knowing where she would stop. Amy's face was a study when she saw her sister skim into the next drawing-room, kiss all the young ladies with effusion, beam graciously upon the young gentlemen, and join in the chat with a spirit which amazed the beholder. Amy was taken possession of by Mrs. Lamb, with whom she was a favorite, and forced to hear a long account of Lucretia's last attack, while three delightful young gentlemen hovered near, waiting for a pause when they might rush in and rescue her. So situated, she was powerless to check Jo, who seemed possessed by a spirit of mischief, and talked away as volubly as the old lady. A knot of heads gathered about her, and Amy strained her ears to hear what was going on; for broken sentences filled her with alarm, round eyes and uplifted hands tormented her with curiosity, and frequent peals of laughter made her wild to share the fun. One may imagine her suffering on overhearing fragments of this sort of conversation:--\n\n\"She rides splendidly,--who taught her?\"\n\n\"No one; she used to practise mounting, holding the reins, and sitting straight on an old saddle in a tree. Now she rides anything, for she doesn't know what fear is, and the stable-man lets her have horses cheap, because she trains them to carry ladies so well. She has such a passion for it, I often tell her if everything else fails she can be a horse-breaker, and get her living so.\"\n\nAt this awful speech Amy contained herself with difficulty, for the impression was being given that she was rather a fast young lady, which was her especial aversion. But what could she do? for the old lady was in the middle of her story, and long before it was done Jo was off again, making more droll revelations, and committing still more fearful blunders.\n\n\"Yes, Amy was in despair that day, for all the good beasts were gone, and of three left, one was lame, one blind, and the other so balky that you had to put dirt in his mouth before he would start. Nice animal for a pleasure party, wasn't it?\"\n\n\"Which did she choose?\" asked one of the laughing gentlemen, who enjoyed the subject.\n\n\"None of them; she heard of a young horse at the farmhouse over the river, and, though a lady had never ridden him, she resolved to try, because he was handsome and spirited. Her struggles were really pathetic; there was no one to bring the horse to the saddle, so she took the saddle to the horse. My dear creature, she actually rowed it over the river, put it on her head, and marched up to the barn to the utter amazement of the old man!\"\n\n\"Did she ride the horse?\"\n\n\"Of course she did, and had a capital time. I expected to see her brought home in fragments, but she managed him perfectly, and was the life of the party.\"\n\n\"Well, I call that plucky!\" and young Mr. Lamb turned an approving glance upon Amy, wondering what his mother could be saying to make the girl look so red and uncomfortable.\n\nShe was still redder and more uncomfortable a moment after, when a sudden turn in the conversation introduced the subject of dress. One of the young ladies asked Jo where she got the pretty drab hat she wore to the picnic; and stupid Jo, instead of mentioning the place where it was bought two years ago, must needs answer, with unnecessary frankness, \"Oh, Amy painted it; you can't buy those soft shades, so we paint ours any color we like. It's a great comfort to have an artistic sister.\"\n\n\"Isn't that an original idea?\" cried Miss Lamb, who found Jo great fun.\n\n\"That's nothing compared to some of her brilliant performances. There's nothing the child can't do. Why, she wanted a pair of blue boots for Sallie's party, so she just painted her soiled white ones the loveliest shade of sky-blue you ever saw, and they looked exactly like satin,\" added Jo, with an air of pride in her sister's accomplishments that exasperated Amy till she felt that it would be a relief to throw her card-case at her.\n\n\"We read a story of yours the other day, and enjoyed it very much,\" observed the elder Miss Lamb, wishing to compliment the literary lady, who did not look the character just then, it must be confessed.\n\nAny mention of her \"works\" always had a bad effect upon Jo, who either grew rigid and looked offended, or changed the subject with a _brusque_ remark, as now. \"Sorry you could find nothing better to read. I write that rubbish because it sells, and ordinary people like it. Are you going to New York this winter?\"\n\nAs Miss Lamb had \"enjoyed\" the story, this speech was not exactly grateful or complimentary. The minute it was made Jo saw her mistake; but, fearing to make the matter worse, suddenly remembered that it was for her to make the first move toward departure, and did so with an abruptness that left three people with half-finished sentences in their mouths.\n\n\"Amy, we _must_ go. _Good_-by, dear; _do_ come and see us; we are _pining_ for a visit. I don't dare to ask _you_, Mr. Lamb; but if you _should_ come, I don't think I shall have the heart to send you away.\"\nJo said this with such a droll imitation of May Chester's gushing style that Amy got out of the room as rapidly as possible, feeling a strong desire to laugh and cry at the same time.\n\n\"Didn't I do that well?\" asked Jo, with a satisfied air, as they walked away.\n\n\"Nothing could have been worse,\" was Amy's crushing reply. \"What possessed you to tell those stories about my saddle, and the hats and boots, and all the rest of it?\"\n\n\"Why, it's funny, and amuses people. They know we are poor, so it's no use pretending that we have grooms, buy three or four hats a season, and have things as easy and fine as they do.\"\n\n\"You needn't go and tell them all our little shifts, and expose our poverty in that perfectly unnecessary way. You haven't a bit of proper pride, and never will learn when to hold your tongue and when to speak,\" said Amy despairingly.\n\nPoor Jo looked abashed, and silently chafed the end of her nose with the stiff handkerchief, as if performing a penance for her misdemeanors.\n\n\"How shall I behave here?\" she asked, as they approached the third mansion.\n\n\"Just as you please; I wash my hands of you,\" was Amy's short answer.\n\n\"Then I'll enjoy myself. The boys are at home, and we'll have a comfortable time. Goodness knows I need a little change, for elegance has a bad effect upon my constitution,\" returned Jo gruffly, being disturbed by her failures to suit.\n\nAn enthusiastic welcome from three big boys and several pretty children speedily soothed her ruffled feelings; and, leaving Amy to entertain the hostess and Mr. Tudor, who happened to be calling likewise, Jo devoted herself to the young folks, and found the change refreshing. She listened to college stories with deep interest, caressed pointers and poodles without a murmur, agreed heartily that \"Tom Brown was a brick,\" regardless of the improper form of praise; and when one lad proposed a visit to his turtle-tank, she went with an alacrity which caused mamma to smile upon her, as that motherly lady settled the cap which was left in a ruinous condition by filial hugs, bear-like but affectionate, and dearer to her than the most faultless _coiffure_ from the hands of an inspired Frenchwoman.\n\nLeaving her sister to her own devices, Amy proceeded to enjoy herself to her heart's content. Mr. Tudor's uncle had married an English lady who was third cousin to a living lord, and Amy regarded the whole family with great respect; for, in spite of her American birth and breeding, she possessed that reverence for titles which haunts the best of us,--that unacknowledged loyalty to the early faith in kings which set the most democratic nation under the sun in a ferment at the coming of a royal yellow-haired laddie, some years ago, and which still has something to do with the love the young country bears the old, like that of a big son for an imperious little mother, who held him while she could, and let him go with a farewell scolding when he rebelled. But even the satisfaction of talking with a distant connection of the British nobility did not render Amy forgetful of time; and when the proper number of minutes had passed, she reluctantly tore herself from this aristocratic society, and looked about for Jo, fervently hoping that her incorrigible sister would not be found in any position which should bring disgrace upon the name of March.\n\nIt might have been worse, but Amy considered it bad; for Jo sat on the grass, with an encampment of boys about her, and a dirty-footed dog reposing on the skirt of her state and festival dress, as she related one of Laurie's pranks to her admiring audience. One small child was poking turtles with Amy's cherished parasol, a second was eating gingerbread over Jo's best bonnet, and a third playing ball with her gloves. But all were enjoying themselves; and when Jo collected her damaged property to go, her escort accompanied her, begging her to come again, \"it was such fun to hear about Laurie's larks.\"\n\n\"Capital boys, aren't they? I feel quite young and brisk again after that,\" said Jo, strolling along with her hands behind her, partly from habit, partly to conceal the bespattered parasol.\n\n\"Why do you always avoid Mr. Tudor?\" asked Amy, wisely refraining from any comment upon Jo's dilapidated appearance.\n\n\"Don't like him; he puts on airs, snubs his sisters, worries his father, and doesn't speak respectfully of his mother. Laurie says he is fast, and _I_ don't consider him a desirable acquaintance; so I let him alone.\"\n\n\"You might treat him civilly, at least. You gave him a cool nod; and just now you bowed and smiled in the politest way to Tommy Chamberlain, whose father keeps a grocery store. If you had just reversed the nod and the bow, it would have been right,\" said Amy reprovingly.\n\n\"No, it wouldn't,\" returned perverse Jo; \"I neither like, respect, nor admire Tudor, though his grandfather's uncle's nephew's niece _was_ third cousin to a lord. Tommy is poor and bashful and good and very clever; I think well of him, and like to show that I do, for he _is_ a gentleman in spite of the brown-paper parcels.\"\n\n\"It's no use trying to argue with you,\" began Amy.\n\n\"Not the least, my dear,\" interrupted Jo; \"so let us look amiable, and drop a card here, as the Kings are evidently out, for which I'm deeply grateful.\"\n\nThe family card-case having done its duty, the girls walked on, and Jo uttered another thanksgiving on reaching the fifth house, and being told that the young ladies were engaged.\n\n\"Now let us go home, and never mind Aunt March to-day. We can run down there any time, and it's really a pity to trail through the dust in our best bibs and tuckers, when we are tired and cross.\"\n\n\"Speak for yourself, if you please. Aunt likes to have us pay her the compliment of coming in style, and making a formal call; it's a little thing to do, but it gives her pleasure, and I don't believe it will hurt your things half so much as letting dirty dogs and clumping boys spoil them. Stoop down, and let me take the crumbs off of your bonnet.\"\n\n\"What a good girl you are, Amy!\" said Jo, with a repentant glance from her own damaged costume to that of her sister, which was fresh and spotless still. \"I wish it was as easy for me to do little things to please people as it is for you. I think of them, but it takes too much time to do them; so I wait for a chance to confer a great favor, and let the small ones slip; but they tell best in the end, I fancy.\"\n\nAmy smiled, and was mollified at once, saying with a maternal air,--\n\n\"Women should learn to be agreeable, particularly poor ones; for they have no other way of repaying the kindnesses they receive. If you'd remember that, and practise it, you'd be better liked than I am, because there is more of you.\"\n\n\"I'm a crotchety old thing, and always shall be, but I'm willing to own that you are right; only it's easier for me to risk my life for a person than to be pleasant to him when I don't feel like it. It's a great misfortune to have such strong likes and dislikes, isn't it?\"\n\n\"It's a greater not to be able to hide them. I don't mind saying that I don't approve of Tudor any more than you do; but I'm not called upon to tell him so; neither are you, and there is no use in making yourself disagreeable because he is.\"\n\n\"But I think girls ought to show when they disapprove of young men; and how can they do it except by their manners? Preaching does not do any good, as I know to my sorrow, since I've had Teddy to manage; but there are many little ways in which I can influence him without a word, and I say we _ought_ to do it to others if we can.\"\n\n\"Teddy is a remarkable boy, and can't be taken as a sample of other boys,\" said Amy, in a tone of solemn conviction, which would have convulsed the \"remarkable boy,\" if he had heard it. \"If we were belles, or women of wealth and position, we might do something, perhaps; but for us to frown at one set of young gentlemen because we don't approve of them, and smile upon another set because we do, wouldn't have a particle of effect, and we should only be considered odd and puritanical.\"\n\n\"So we are to countenance things and people which we detest, merely because we are not belles and millionaires, are we? That's a nice sort of morality.\"\n\n\"I can't argue about it, I only know that it's the way of the world; and people who set themselves against it only get laughed at for their pains. I don't like reformers, and I hope you will never try to be one.\"\n\n\"I do like them, and I shall be one if I can; for in spite of the laughing, the world would never get on without them. We can't agree about that, for you belong to the old set, and I to the new: you will get on the best, but I shall have the liveliest time of it. I should rather enjoy the brickbats and hooting, I think.\"\n\n\"Well, compose yourself now, and don't worry aunt with your new ideas.\"\n\n\"I'll try not to, but I'm always possessed to burst out with some particularly blunt speech or revolutionary sentiment before her; it's my doom, and I can't help it.\"\n\nThey found Aunt Carrol with the old lady, both absorbed in some very interesting subject; but they dropped it as the girls came in, with a conscious look which betrayed that they had been talking about their nieces. Jo was not in a good humor, and the perverse fit returned; but Amy, who had virtuously done her duty, kept her temper, and pleased everybody, was in a most angelic frame of mind. This amiable spirit was felt at once, and both the aunts \"my deared\" her affectionately, looking what they afterwards said emphatically,--\"That child improves every day.\"\n\n\"Are you going to help about the fair, dear?\" asked Mrs. Carrol, as Amy sat down beside her with the confiding air elderly people like so well in the young.\n\n\"Yes, aunt. Mrs. Chester asked me if I would, and I offered to tend a table, as I have nothing but my time to give.\"\n\n\"I'm not,\" put in Jo decidedly. \"I hate to be patronized, and the Chesters think it's a great favor to allow us to help with their highly connected fair. I wonder you consented, Amy: they only want you to work.\"\n\n\"I am willing to work: it's for the freedmen as well as the Chesters, and I think it very kind of them to let me share the labor and the fun. Patronage does not trouble me when it is well meant.\"\n\n\"Quite right and proper. I like your grateful spirit, my dear; it's a pleasure to help people who appreciate our efforts: some do not, and that is trying,\" observed Aunt March, looking over her spectacles at Jo, who sat apart, rocking herself, with a somewhat morose expression.\n\nIf Jo had only known what a great happiness was wavering in the balance for one of them, she would have turned dovelike in a minute; but, unfortunately, we don't have windows in our breasts, and cannot see what goes on in the minds of our friends; better for us that we cannot as a general thing, but now and then it would be such a comfort, such a saving of time and temper. By her next speech, Jo deprived herself of several years of pleasure, and received a timely lesson in the art of holding her tongue.\n\n\"I don't like favors; they oppress and make me feel like a slave. I'd rather do everything for myself, and be perfectly independent.\"\n\n\"Ahem!\" coughed Aunt Carrol softly, with a look at Aunt March.\n\n\"I told you so,\" said Aunt March, with a decided nod to Aunt Carrol.\n\nMercifully unconscious of what she had done, Jo sat with her nose in the air, and a revolutionary aspect which was anything but inviting.\n\n\"Do you speak French, dear?\" asked Mrs. Carrol, laying her hand on Amy's.\n\n\"Pretty well, thanks to Aunt March, who lets Esther talk to me as often as I like,\" replied Amy, with a grateful look, which caused the old lady to smile affably.\n\n\"How are you about languages?\" asked Mrs. Carrol of Jo.\n\n\"Don't know a word; I'm very stupid about studying anything; can't bear French, it's such a slippery, silly sort of language,\" was the _brusque_ reply.\n\nAnother look passed between the ladies, and Aunt March said to Amy, \"You are quite strong and well, now, dear, I believe? Eyes don't trouble you any more, do they?\"\n\n\"Not at all, thank you, ma'am. I'm very well, and mean to do great things next winter, so that I may be ready for Rome, whenever that joyful time arrives.\"\n\n\"Good girl! You deserve to go, and I'm sure you will some day,\" said Aunt March, with an approving pat on the head, as Amy picked up her ball for her.\n\n\"Cross-patch, draw the latch, Sit by the fire and spin,\"\n\nsqualled Polly, bending down from his perch on the back of her chair to peep into Jo's face, with such a comical air of impertinent inquiry that it was impossible to help laughing.\n\n\"Most observing bird,\" said the old lady.\n\n\"Come and take a walk, my dear?\" cried Polly, hopping toward the china-closet, with a look suggestive of lump-sugar.\n\n\"Thank you, I will. Come, Amy;\" and Jo brought the visit to an end, feeling more strongly than ever that calls did have a bad effect upon her constitution. She shook hands in a gentlemanly manner, but Amy kissed both the aunts, and the girls departed, leaving behind them the impression of shadow and sunshine; which impression caused Aunt March to say, as they vanished,--\n\n\"You'd better do it, Mary; I'll supply the money,\" and Aunt Carrol to reply decidedly, \"I certainly will, if her father and mother consent.\"\n\nMrs. Chester's fair was so very elegant and select that it was considered a great honor by the young ladies of the neighborhood to be invited to take a table, and every one was much interested in the matter. Amy was asked, but Jo was not, which was fortunate for all parties, as her elbows were decidedly akimbo at this period of her life, and it took a good many hard knocks to teach her how to get on easily. The \"haughty, uninteresting creature\" was let severely alone; but Amy's talent and taste were duly complimented by the offer of the art-table, and she exerted herself to prepare and secure appropriate and valuable contributions to it.\n\nEverything went on smoothly till the day before the fair opened; then there occurred one of the little skirmishes which it is almost impossible to avoid, when some five and twenty women, old and young, with all their private piques and prejudices, try to work together.\nMay Chester was rather jealous of Amy because the latter was a greater favorite than herself, and, just at this time, several trifling circumstances occurred to increase the feeling. Amy's dainty pen-and-ink work entirely eclipsed May's painted vases,--that was one thorn; then the all-conquering Tudor had danced four times with Amy, at a late party, and only once with May,--that was thorn number two; but the chief grievance that rankled in her soul, and gave her an excuse for her unfriendly conduct, was a rumor which some obliging gossip had whispered to her, that the March girls had made fun of her at the Lambs'. All the blame of this should have fallen upon Jo, for her naughty imitation had been too lifelike to escape detection, and the frolicsome Lambs had permitted the joke to escape. No hint of this had reached the culprits, however, and Amy's dismay can be imagined, when, the very evening before the fair, as she was putting the last touches to her pretty table, Mrs. Chester, who, of course, resented the supposed ridicule of her daughter, said, in a bland tone, but with a cold look,--\n\n\"I find, dear, that there is some feeling among the young ladies about my giving this table to any one but my girls. As this is the most prominent, and some say the most attractive table of all, and they are the chief getters-up of the fair, it is thought best for them to take this place. I'm sorry, but I know you are too sincerely interested in the cause to mind a little personal disappointment, and you shall have another table if you like.\"\n\nMrs. Chester had fancied beforehand that it would be easy to deliver this little speech; but when the time came, she found it rather difficult to utter it naturally, with Amy's unsuspicious eyes looking straight at her, full of surprise and trouble.\n\nAmy felt that there was something behind this, but could not guess what, and said quietly, feeling hurt, and showing that she did,--\n\n\"Perhaps you had rather I took no table at all?\"\n\n\"Now, my dear, don't have any ill feeling, I beg; it's merely a matter of expediency, you see; my girls will naturally take the lead, and this table is considered their proper place. _I_ think it very appropriate to you, and feel very grateful for your efforts to make it so pretty; but we must give up our private wishes, of course, and I will see that you have a good place elsewhere. Wouldn't you like the flower-table? The little girls undertook it, but they are discouraged. You could make a charming thing of it, and the flower-table is always attractive, you know.\"\n\n\"Especially to gentlemen,\" added May, with a look which enlightened Amy as to one cause of her sudden fall from favor. She colored angrily, but took no other notice of that girlish sarcasm, and answered, with unexpected amiability,--\n\n\"It shall be as you please, Mrs. Chester. I'll give up my place here at once, and attend to the flowers, if you like.\"\n\n\"You can put your own things on your own table, if you prefer,\" began May, feeling a little conscience-stricken, as she looked at the pretty racks, the painted shells, and quaint illuminations Amy had so carefully made and so gracefully arranged. She meant it kindly, but Amy mistook her meaning, and said quickly,--\n\n\"Oh, certainly, if they are in your way;\" and sweeping her contributions into her apron, pell-mell, she walked off, feeling that herself and her works of art had been insulted past forgiveness.\n\n\"Now she's mad. Oh, dear, I wish I hadn't asked you to speak, mamma,\" said May, looking disconsolately at the empty spaces on her table.\n\n\"Girls' quarrels are soon over,\" returned her mother, feeling a trifle ashamed of her own part in this one, as well she might.\n\nThe little girls hailed Amy and her treasures with delight, which cordial reception somewhat soothed her perturbed spirit, and she fell to work, determined to succeed florally, if she could not artistically. But everything seemed against her: it was late, and she was tired; every one was too busy with their own affairs to help her; and the little girls were only hindrances, for the dears fussed and chattered like so many magpies, making a great deal of confusion in their artless efforts to preserve the most perfect order. The evergreen arch wouldn't stay firm after she got it up, but wiggled and threatened to tumble down on her head when the hanging baskets were filled; her best tile got a splash of water, which left a sepia tear on the Cupid's cheek; she bruised her hands with hammering, and got cold working in a draught, which last affliction filled her with apprehensions for the morrow. Any girl-reader who has suffered like afflictions will sympathize with poor Amy, and wish her well through with her task.\n\nThere was great indignation at home when she told her story that evening. Her mother said it was a shame, but told her she had done right; Beth declared she wouldn't go to the fair at all; and Jo demanded why she didn't take all her pretty things and leave those mean people to get on without her.\n\n\"Because they are mean is no reason why I should be. I hate such things, and though I think I've a right to be hurt, I don't intend to show it. They will feel that more than angry speeches or huffy actions, won't they, Marmee?\"\n\n\"That's the right spirit, my dear; a kiss for a blow is always best, though it's not very easy to give it sometimes,\" said her mother, with the air of one who had learned the difference between preaching and practising.\n\nIn spite of various very natural temptations to resent and retaliate, Amy adhered to her resolution all the next day, bent on conquering her enemy by kindness. She began well, thanks to a silent reminder that came to her unexpectedly, but most opportunely. As she arranged her table that morning, while the little girls were in an ante-room filling the baskets, she took up her pet production,--a little book, the antique cover of which her father had found among his treasures, and in which, on leaves of vellum, she had beautifully illuminated different texts. As she turned the pages, rich in dainty devices, with very pardonable pride, her eye fell upon one verse that made her stop and think. Framed in a brilliant scroll-work of scarlet, blue, and gold, with little spirits of good-will helping one another up and down among the thorns and flowers, were the words, \"Thou shalt love thy neighbor as thyself.\"\n\n\"I ought, but I don't,\" thought Amy, as her eye went from the bright page to May's discontented face behind the big vases, that could not hide the vacancies her pretty work had once filled. Amy stood a minute, turning the leaves in her hand, reading on each some sweet rebuke for all heart-burnings and uncharitableness of spirit. Many wise and true sermons are preached us every day by unconscious ministers in street, school, office, or home; even a fair-table may become a pulpit, if it can offer the good and helpful words which are never out of season. Amy's conscience preached her a little sermon from that text, then and there; and she did what many of us do not always do,--took the sermon to heart, and straightway put it in practice.\n\nA group of girls were standing about May's table, admiring the pretty things, and talking over the change of saleswomen. They dropped their voices, but Amy knew they were speaking of her, hearing one side of the story, and judging accordingly. It was not pleasant, but a better spirit had come over her, and presently a chance offered for proving it. She heard May say sorrowfully,--\n\n\"It's too bad, for there is no time to make other things, and I don't want to fill up with odds and ends. The table was just complete then: now it's spoilt.\"\n\n\"I dare say she'd put them back if you asked her,\" suggested some one.\n\n\"How could I after all the fuss?\" began May, but she did not finish, for Amy's voice came across the hall, saying pleasantly,--\n\n\"You may have them, and welcome, without asking, if you want them. I was just thinking I'd offer to put them back, for they belong to your table rather than mine. Here they are; please take them, and forgive me if I was hasty in carrying them away last night.\"\n\nAs she spoke, Amy returned her contribution, with a nod and a smile, and hurried away again, feeling that it was easier to do a friendly thing than it was to stay and be thanked for it.\n\n\"Now, I call that lovely of her, don't you?\" cried one girl.\n\nMay's answer was inaudible; but another young lady, whose temper was evidently a little soured by making lemonade, added, with a disagreeable laugh, \"Very lovely; for she knew she wouldn't sell them at her own table.\"\n\nNow, that was hard; when we make little sacrifices we like to have them appreciated, at least; and for a minute Amy was sorry she had done it, feeling that virtue was not always its own reward. But it is,--as she presently discovered; for her spirits began to rise, and her table to blossom under her skilful hands; the girls were very kind, and that one little act seemed to have cleared the atmosphere amazingly.\n\nIt was a very long day, and a hard one to Amy, as she sat behind her table, often quite alone, for the little girls deserted very soon: few cared to buy flowers in summer, and her bouquets began to droop long before night.\n\nThe art-table _was_ the most attractive in the room; there was a crowd about it all day long, and the tenders were constantly flying to and fro with important faces and rattling money-boxes. Amy often looked wistfully across, longing to be there, where she felt at home and happy, instead of in a corner with nothing to do. It might seem no hardship to some of us; but to a pretty, blithe young girl, it was not only tedious, but very trying; and the thought of being found there in the evening by her family, and Laurie and his friends, made it a real martyrdom.\n\nShe did not go home till night, and then she looked so pale and quiet that they knew the day had been a hard one, though she made no complaint, and did not even tell what she had done. Her mother gave her an extra cordial cup of tea, Beth helped her dress, and made a charming little wreath for her hair, while Jo astonished her family by getting herself up with unusual care, and hinting darkly that the tables were about to be turned.\n\n\"Don't do anything rude, pray, Jo. I won't have any fuss made, so let it all pass, and behave yourself,\" begged Amy, as she departed early, hoping to find a reinforcement of flowers to refresh her poor little table.\n\n\"I merely intend to make myself entrancingly agreeable to every one I know, and to keep them in your corner as long as possible. Teddy and his boys will lend a hand, and we'll have a good time yet,\" returned Jo, leaning over the gate to watch for Laurie. Presently the familiar tramp was heard in the dusk, and she ran out to meet him.\n\n\"Is that my boy?\"\n\n\"As sure as this is my girl!\" and Laurie tucked her hand under his arm, with the air of a man whose every wish was gratified.\n\n\"O Teddy, such doings!\" and Jo told Amy's wrongs with sisterly zeal.\n\n\"A flock of our fellows are going to drive over by and by, and I'll be hanged if I don't make them buy every flower she's got, and camp down before her table afterward,\" said Laurie, espousing her cause with warmth.\n\n\"The flowers are not at all nice, Amy says, and the fresh ones may not arrive in time. I don't wish to be unjust or suspicious, but I shouldn't wonder if they never came at all. When people do one mean thing they are very likely to do another,\" observed Jo, in a disgusted tone.\n\n\"Didn't Hayes give you the best out of our gardens? I told him to.\"\n\n\"I didn't know that; he forgot, I suppose; and, as your grandpa was poorly, I didn't like to worry him by asking, though I did want some.\"\n\n\"Now, Jo, how could you think there was any need of asking! They are just as much yours as mine. Don't we always go halves in everything?\" began Laurie, in the tone that always made Jo turn thorny.\n\n\"Gracious, I hope not! half of some of your things wouldn't suit me at all. But we mustn't stand philandering here; I've got to help Amy, so you go and make yourself splendid; and if you'll be so very kind as to let Hayes take a few nice flowers up to the Hall, I'll bless you forever.\"\n\n\"Couldn't you do it now?\" asked Laurie, so suggestively that Jo shut the gate in his face with inhospitable haste, and called through the bars, \"Go away, Teddy; I'm busy.\"\n\nThanks to the conspirators, the tables _were_ turned that night; for Hayes sent up a wilderness of flowers, with a lovely basket, arranged in his best manner, for a centre-piece; then the March family turned out _en masse_, and Jo exerted herself to some purpose, for people not only came, but stayed, laughing at her nonsense, admiring Amy's taste, and apparently enjoying themselves very much. Laurie and his friends gallantly threw themselves into the breach, bought up the bouquets, encamped before the table, and made that corner the liveliest spot in the room. Amy was in her element now, and, out of gratitude, if nothing more, was as sprightly and gracious as possible,--coming to the conclusion, about that time, that virtue _was_ its own reward, after all.\n\nJo behaved herself with exemplary propriety; and when Amy was happily surrounded by her guard of honor, Jo circulated about the hall, picking up various bits of gossip, which enlightened her upon the subject of the Chester change of base. She reproached herself for her share of the ill-feeling, and resolved to exonerate Amy as soon as possible; she also discovered what Amy had done about the things in the morning, and considered her a model of magnanimity. As she passed the art-table, she glanced over it for her sister's things, but saw no signs of them. \"Tucked away out of sight, I dare say,\" thought Jo, who could forgive her own wrongs, but hotly resented any insult offered to her family.\n\n\"Good evening, Miss Jo. How does Amy get on?\" asked May, with a conciliatory air, for she wanted to show that she also could be generous.\n\n\"She has sold everything she had that was worth selling, and now she is enjoying herself. The flower-table is always attractive, you know, 'especially to gentlemen.'\"\n\nJo _couldn't_ resist giving that little slap, but May took it so meekly she regretted it a minute after, and fell to praising the great vases, which still remained unsold.\n\n\"Is Amy's illumination anywhere about? I took a fancy to buy that for father,\" said Jo, very anxious to learn the fate of her sister's work.\n\n\"Everything of Amy's sold long ago; I took care that the right people saw them, and they made a nice little sum of money for us,\" returned May, who had overcome sundry small temptations, as well as Amy, that day.\n\nMuch gratified, Jo rushed back to tell the good news; and Amy looked both touched and surprised by the report of May's words and manner.\n\n\"Now, gentlemen, I want you to go and do your duty by the other tables as generously as you have by mine--especially the art-table,\" she said, ordering out \"Teddy's Own,\" as the girls called the college friends.\n\n\"'Charge, Chester, charge!' is the motto for that table; but do your duty like men, and you'll get your money's worth of _art_ in every sense of the word,\" said the irrepressible Jo, as the devoted phalanx prepared to take the field.\n\n\"To hear is to obey, but March is fairer far than May,\" said little Parker, making a frantic effort to be both witty and tender, and getting promptly quenched by Laurie, who said, \"Very well, my son, for a small boy!\" and walked him off, with a paternal pat on the head.\n\n\"Buy the vases,\" whispered Amy to Laurie, as a final heaping of coals of fire on her enemy's head.\n\nTo May's great delight, Mr. Laurence not only bought the vases, but pervaded the hall with one under each arm. The other gentlemen speculated with equal rashness in all sorts of frail trifles, and wandered helplessly about afterward, burdened with wax flowers, painted fans, filigree portfolios, and other useful and appropriate purchases.\nAunt Carrol was there, heard the story, looked pleased, and said something to Mrs. March in a corner, which made the latter lady beam with satisfaction, and watch Amy with a face full of mingled pride and anxiety, though she did not betray the cause of her pleasure till several days later.\n\nThe fair was pronounced a success; and when May bade Amy good night, she did not \"gush\" as usual, but gave her an affectionate kiss, and a look which said, \"Forgive and forget.\" That satisfied Amy; and when she got home she found the vases paraded on the parlor chimney-piece, with a great bouquet in each. \"The reward of merit for a magnanimous March,\" as Laurie announced with a flourish.\n\n\"You've a deal more principle and generosity and nobleness of character than I ever gave you credit for, Amy. You've behaved sweetly, and I respect you with all my heart,\" said Jo warmly, as they brushed their hair together late that night.\n\n\"Yes, we all do, and love her for being so ready to forgive. It must have been dreadfully hard, after working so long, and setting your heart on selling your own pretty things. I don't believe I could have done it as kindly as you did,\" added Beth from her pillow.\n\n\"Why, girls, you needn't praise me so; I only did as I'd be done by. You laugh at me when I say I want to be a lady, but I mean a true gentlewoman in mind and manners, and I try to do it as far as I know how. I can't explain exactly, but I want to be above the little meannesses and follies and faults that spoil so many women. I'm far from it now, but I do my best, and hope in time to be what mother is.\"\n\nAmy spoke earnestly, and Jo said, with a cordial hug,--\n\n\"I understand now what you mean, and I'll never laugh at you again. You are getting on faster than you think, and I'll take lessons of you in true politeness, for you've learned the secret, I believe. Try away, deary; you'll get your reward some day, and no one will be more delighted than I shall.\"\n\nA week later Amy did get her reward, and poor Jo found it hard to be delighted. A letter came from Aunt Carrol, and Mrs. March's face was illuminated to such a degree, when she read it, that Jo and Beth, who were with her, demanded what the glad tidings were.\n\n\"Aunt Carrol is going abroad next month, and wants--\"\n\n\"Me to go with her!\" burst in Jo, flying out of her chair in an uncontrollable rapture.\n\n\"No, dear, not you; it's Amy.\"\n\n\"O mother! she's too young; it's my turn first. I've wanted it so long--it would do me so much good, and be so altogether splendid--I _must_ go.\"\n\n\"I'm afraid it's impossible, Jo. Aunt says Amy, decidedly, and it is not for us to dictate when she offers such a favor.\"\n\n\"It's always so. Amy has all the fun and I have all the work. It isn't fair, oh, it isn't fair!\" cried Jo passionately.\n\n\"I'm afraid it is partly your own fault, dear. When Aunt spoke to me the other day, she regretted your blunt manners and too independent spirit; and here she writes, as if quoting something you had said,--'I planned at first to ask Jo; but as \"favors burden her,\" and she \"hates French,\" I think I won't venture to invite her. Amy is more docile, will make a good companion for Flo, and receive gratefully any help the trip may give her.'\"\n\n\"Oh, my tongue, my abominable tongue! why can't I learn to keep it quiet?\" groaned Jo, remembering words which had been her undoing. When she had heard the explanation of the quoted phrases, Mrs. March said sorrowfully,--\n\n\"I wish you could have gone, but there is no hope of it this time; so try to bear it cheerfully, and don't sadden Amy's pleasure by reproaches or regrets.\"\n\n\"I'll try,\" said Jo, winking hard, as she knelt down to pick up the basket she had joyfully upset. \"I'll take a leaf out of her book, and try not only to seem glad, but to be so, and not grudge her one minute of happiness; but it won't be easy, for it is a dreadful disappointment;\" and poor Jo bedewed the little fat pincushion she held with several very bitter tears.\n\n\"Jo, dear, I'm very selfish, but I couldn't spare you, and I'm glad you are not going quite yet,\" whispered Beth, embracing her, basket and all, with such a clinging touch and loving face, that Jo felt comforted in spite of the sharp regret that made her want to box her own ears, and humbly beg Aunt Carrol to burden her with this favor, and see how gratefully she would bear it.\n\nBy the time Amy came in, Jo was able to take her part in the family jubilation; not quite as heartily as usual, perhaps, but without repinings at Amy's good fortune. The young lady herself received the news as tidings of great joy, went about in a solemn sort of rapture, and began to sort her colors and pack her pencils that evening, leaving such trifles as clothes, money, and passports to those less absorbed in visions of art than herself.\n\n\"It isn't a mere pleasure trip to me, girls,\" she said impressively, as she scraped her best palette. \"It will decide my career; for if I have any genius, I shall find it out in Rome, and will do something to prove it.\"\n\n\"Suppose you haven't?\" said Jo, sewing away, with red eyes, at the new collars which were to be handed over to Amy.\n\n\"Then I shall come home and teach drawing for my living,\" replied the aspirant for fame, with philosophic composure; but she made a wry face at the prospect, and scratched away at her palette as if bent on vigorous measures before she gave up her hopes.\n\n\"No, you won't; you hate hard work, and you'll marry some rich man, and come home to sit in the lap of luxury all your days,\" said Jo.\n\n\"Your predictions sometimes come to pass, but I don't believe that one will. I'm sure I wish it would, for if I can't be an artist myself, I should like to be able to help those who are,\" said Amy, smiling, as if the part of Lady Bountiful would suit her better than that of a poor drawing-teacher.\n\n\"Hum!\" said Jo, with a sigh; \"if you wish it you'll have it, for your wishes are always granted--mine never.\"\n\n\"Would you like to go?\" asked Amy, thoughtfully patting her nose with her knife.\n\n\"Rather!\"\n\n\"Well, in a year or two I'll send for you, and we'll dig in the Forum for relics, and carry out all the plans we've made so many times.\"\n\n\"Thank you; I'll remind you of your promise when that joyful day comes, if it ever does,\" returned Jo, accepting the vague but magnificent offer as gratefully as she could.\n\nThere was not much time for preparation, and the house was in a ferment till Amy was off. Jo bore up very well till the last flutter of blue ribbon vanished, when she retired to her refuge, the garret, and cried till she couldn't cry any more. Amy likewise bore up stoutly till the steamer sailed; then, just as the gangway was about to be withdrawn, it suddenly came over her that a whole ocean was soon to roll between her and those who loved her best, and she clung to Laurie, the last lingerer, saying with a sob,--\n\n\"Oh, take care of them for me; and if anything should happen--\"\n\n\"I will, dear, I will; and if anything happens, I'll come and comfort you,\" whispered Laurie, little dreaming that he would be called upon to keep his word.\n\nSo Amy sailed away to find the old world, which is always new and beautiful to young eyes, while her father and friend watched her from the shore, fervently hoping that none but gentle fortunes would befall the happy-hearted girl, who waved her hand to them till they could see nothing but the summer sunshine dazzling on the sea.\n\n\"Here I really sit at a front window of the Bath Hotel, Piccadilly. It's not a fashionable place, but uncle stopped here years ago, and won't go anywhere else; however, we don't mean to stay long, so it's no great matter. Oh, I can't begin to tell you how I enjoy it all! I never can, so I'll only give you bits out of my note-book, for I've done nothing but sketch and scribble since I started.\n\n\"I sent a line from Halifax, when I felt pretty miserable, but after that I got on delightfully, seldom ill, on deck all day, with plenty of pleasant people to amuse me. Every one was very kind to me, especially the officers. Don't laugh, Jo; gentlemen really are very necessary aboard ship, to hold on to, or to wait upon one; and as they have nothing to do, it's a mercy to make them useful, otherwise they would smoke themselves to death, I'm afraid.\n\n\"Aunt and Flo were poorly all the way, and liked to be let alone, so when I had done what I could for them, I went and enjoyed myself. Such walks on deck, such sunsets, such splendid air and waves! It was almost as exciting as riding a fast horse, when we went rushing on so grandly. I wish Beth could have come, it would have done her so much good; as for Jo, she would have gone up and sat on the main-top jib, or whatever the high thing is called, made friends with the engineers, and tooted on the captain's speaking-trumpet, she'd have been in such a state of rapture.\n\n\"It was all heavenly, but I was glad to see the Irish coast, and found it very lovely, so green and sunny, with brown cabins here and there, ruins on some of the hills, and gentlemen's country-seats in the valleys, with deer feeding in the parks. It was early in the morning, but I didn't regret getting up to see it, for the bay was full of little boats, the shore _so_ picturesque, and a rosy sky overhead. I never shall forget it.\n\n\"At Queenstown one of my new acquaintances left us,--Mr. Lennox,--and when I said something about the Lakes of Killarney, he sighed and sung, with a look at me,--\n\n'Oh, have you e'er heard of Kate Kearney? She lives on the banks of Killarney; From the glance of her eye, Shun danger and fly, For fatal's the glance of Kate Kearney.'\n\nWasn't that nonsensical?\n\n\"We only stopped at Liverpool a few hours. It's a dirty, noisy place, and I was glad to leave it. Uncle rushed out and bought a pair of dog-skin gloves, some ugly, thick shoes, and an umbrella, and got shaved _à la_ mutton-chop, the first thing. Then he flattered himself that he looked like a true Briton; but the first time he had the mud cleaned off his shoes, the little bootblack knew that an American stood in them, and said, with a grin, 'There yer har, sir. I've give 'em the latest Yankee shine.' It amused uncle immensely. Oh, I _must_ tell you what that absurd Lennox did! He got his friend Ward, who came on with us, to order a bouquet for me, and the first thing I saw in my room was a lovely one, with 'Robert Lennox's compliments,' on the card. Wasn't that fun, girls? I like travelling.\n\n\"I never _shall_ get to London if I don't hurry. The trip was like riding through a long picture-gallery, full of lovely landscapes. The farmhouses were my delight; with thatched roofs, ivy up to the eaves, latticed windows, and stout women with rosy children at the doors. The very cattle looked more tranquil than ours, as they stood knee-deep in clover, and the hens had a contented cluck, as if they never got nervous, like Yankee biddies. Such perfect color I never saw,--the grass so green, sky so blue, grain so yellow, woods so dark,--I was in a rapture all the way. So was Flo; and we kept bouncing from one side to the other, trying to see everything while we were whisking along at the rate of sixty miles an hour. Aunt was tired and went to sleep, but uncle read his guide-book, and wouldn't be astonished at anything. This is the way we went on: Amy, flying up,--'Oh, that must be Kenilworth, that gray place among the trees!' Flo, darting to my window,--'How sweet! We must go there some time, won't we, papa?' Uncle, calmly admiring his boots,--'No, my dear, not unless you want beer; that's a brewery.'\n\n\"A pause,--then Flo cried out, 'Bless me, there's a gallows and a man going up.' 'Where, where?' shrieks Amy, staring out at two tall posts with a cross-beam and some dangling chains. 'A colliery,' remarks uncle, with a twinkle of the eye. 'Here's a lovely flock of lambs all lying down,' says Amy. 'See, papa, aren't they pretty!' added Flo sentimentally. 'Geese, young ladies,' returns uncle, in a tone that keeps us quiet till Flo settles down to enjoy 'The Flirtations of Capt. Cavendish,' and I have the scenery all to myself.\n\n\"Of course it rained when we got to London, and there was nothing to be seen but fog and umbrellas. We rested, unpacked, and shopped a little between the showers. Aunt Mary got me some new things, for I came off in such a hurry I wasn't half ready. A white hat and blue feather, a muslin dress to match, and the loveliest mantle you ever saw. Shopping in Regent Street is perfectly splendid; things seem so cheap--nice ribbons only sixpence a yard. I laid in a stock, but shall get my gloves in Paris. Doesn't that sound sort of elegant and rich?\n\n\"Flo and I, for the fun of it, ordered a hansom cab, while aunt and uncle were out, and went for a drive, though we learned afterward that it wasn't the thing for young ladies to ride in them alone. It was so droll! for when we were shut in by the wooden apron, the man drove so fast that Flo was frightened, and told me to stop him. But he was up outside behind somewhere, and I couldn't get at him. He didn't hear me call, nor see me flap my parasol in front, and there we were, quite helpless, rattling away, and whirling around corners at a break-neck pace. At last, in my despair, I saw a little door in the roof, and on poking it open, a red eye appeared, and a beery voice said,--\n\n\"'Now then, mum?'\n\n\"I gave my order as soberly as I could, and slamming down the door, with an 'Aye, aye, mum,' the man made his horse walk, as if going to a funeral. I poked again, and said, 'A little faster;' then off he went, helter-skelter, as before, and we resigned ourselves to our fate.\n\n\"To-day was fair and we went to Hyde Park, close by, for we are more aristocratic than we look. The Duke of Devonshire lives near. I often see his footmen lounging at the back gate; and the Duke of Wellington's house is not far off. Such sights as I saw, my dear! It was as good as Punch, for there were fat dowagers rolling about in their red and yellow coaches, with gorgeous Jeameses in silk stockings and velvet coats, up behind, and powdered coachmen in front. Smart maids, with the rosiest children I ever saw; handsome girls, looking half asleep; dandies, in queer English hats and lavender kids, lounging about, and tall soldiers, in short red jackets and muffin caps stuck on one side, looking so funny I longed to sketch them.\n\n\"Rotten Row means '_Route de Roi_,' or the king's way; but now it's more like a riding-school than anything else. The horses are splendid, and the men, especially the grooms, ride well; but the women are stiff, and bounce, which isn't according to our rules. I longed to show them a tearing American gallop, for they trotted solemnly up and down, in their scant habits and high hats, looking like the women in a toy Noah's Ark. Every one rides,--old men, stout ladies, little children,--and the young folks do a deal of flirting here; I saw a pair exchange rosebuds, for it's the thing to wear one in the button-hole, and I thought it rather a nice little idea.\n\n\"In the P.M. to Westminster Abbey; but don't expect me to describe it, that's impossible--so I'll only say it was sublime! This evening we are going to see Fechter, which will be an appropriate end to the happiest day of my life.\n\n\"It's very late, but I can't let my letter go in the morning without telling you what happened last evening. Who do you think came in, as we were at tea? Laurie's English friends, Fred and Frank Vaughn! I was _so_ surprised, for I shouldn't have known them but for the cards. Both are tall fellows, with whiskers; Fred handsome in the English style, and Frank much better, for he only limps slightly, and uses no crutches. They had heard from Laurie where we were to be, and came to ask us to their house; but uncle won't go, so we shall return the call, and see them as we can. They went to the theatre with us, and we did have _such_ a good time, for Frank devoted himself to Flo, and Fred and I talked over past, present, and future fun as if we had known each other all our days. Tell Beth Frank asked for her, and was sorry to hear of her ill health. Fred laughed when I spoke of Jo, and sent his 'respectful compliments to the big hat.' Neither of them had forgotten Camp Laurence, or the fun we had there. What ages ago it seems, doesn't it?\n\n\"Aunt is tapping on the wall for the third time, so I _must_ stop. I really feel like a dissipated London fine lady, writing here so late, with my room full of pretty things, and my head a jumble of parks, theatres, new gowns, and gallant creatures who say 'Ah!' and twirl their blond mustaches with the true English lordliness. I long to see you all, and in spite of my nonsense am, as ever, your loving AMY.\"\n\n\"In my last I told you about our London visit,--how kind the Vaughns were, and what pleasant parties they made for us. I enjoyed the trips to Hampton Court and the Kensington Museum more than anything else,--for at Hampton I saw Raphael's cartoons, and, at the Museum, rooms full of pictures by Turner, Lawrence, Reynolds, Hogarth, and the other great creatures. The day in Richmond Park was charming, for we had a regular English picnic, and I had more splendid oaks and groups of deer than I could copy; also heard a nightingale, and saw larks go up. We 'did' London to our hearts' content, thanks to Fred and Frank, and were sorry to go away; for, though English people are slow to take you in, when they once make up their minds to do it they cannot be outdone in hospitality, _I_ think. The Vaughns hope to meet us in Rome next winter, and I shall be dreadfully disappointed if they don't, for Grace and I are great friends, and the boys very nice fellows,--especially Fred.\n\n\"Well, we were hardly settled here, when he turned up again, saying he had come for a holiday, and was going to Switzerland. Aunt looked sober at first, but he was so cool about it she couldn't say a word; and now we get on nicely, and are very glad he came, for he speaks French like a native, and I don't know what we should do without him. Uncle doesn't know ten words, and insists on talking English very loud, as if that would make people understand him. Aunt's pronunciation is old-fashioned, and Flo and I, though we flattered ourselves that we knew a good deal, find we don't, and are very grateful to have Fred do the '_parley vooing_,' as uncle calls it.\n\n\"Such delightful times as we are having! sight-seeing from morning till night, stopping for nice lunches in the gay _cafés_, and meeting with all sorts of droll adventures. Rainy days I spend in the Louvre, revelling in pictures. Jo would turn up her naughty nose at some of the finest, because she has no soul for art; but _I_ have, and I'm cultivating eye and taste as fast as I can. She would like the relics of great people better, for I've seen her Napoleon's cocked hat and gray coat, his baby's cradle and his old toothbrush; also Marie Antoinette's little shoe, the ring of Saint Denis, Charlemagne's sword, and many other interesting things. I'll talk for hours about them when I come, but haven't time to write.\n\n\"The Palais Royale is a heavenly place,--so full of _bijouterie_ and lovely things that I'm nearly distracted because I can't buy them. Fred wanted to get me some, but of course I didn't allow it. Then the Bois and the Champs Elysées are _très magnifique_. I've seen the imperial family several times,--the emperor an ugly, hard-looking man, the empress pale and pretty, but dressed in bad taste, _I_ thought,--purple dress, green hat, and yellow gloves. Little Nap. is a handsome boy, who sits chatting to his tutor, and kisses his hand to the people as he passes in his four-horse barouche, with postilions in red satin jackets, and a mounted guard before and behind.\n\n\"We often walk in the Tuileries Gardens, for they are lovely, though the antique Luxembourg Gardens suit me better. Père la Chaise is very curious, for many of the tombs are like small rooms, and, looking in, one sees a table, with images or pictures of the dead, and chairs for the mourners to sit in when they come to lament. That is so Frenchy.\n\n\"Our rooms are on the Rue de Rivoli, and, sitting in the balcony, we look up and down the long, brilliant street. It is so pleasant that we spend our evenings talking there, when too tired with our day's work to go out. Fred is very entertaining, and is altogether the most agreeable young man I ever knew,--except Laurie, whose manners are more charming. I wish Fred was dark, for I don't fancy light men; however, the Vaughns are very rich, and come of an excellent family, so I won't find fault with their yellow hair, as my own is yellower.\n\n\"Next week we are off to Germany and Switzerland; and, as we shall travel fast, I shall only be able to give you hasty letters. I keep my diary, and try to 'remember correctly and describe clearly all that I see and admire,' as father advised. It is good practice for me, and, with my sketch-book, will give you a better idea of my tour than these scribbles.\n\n\"Adieu; I embrace you tenderly. VOTRE AMIE.\"\n\n\"Having a quiet hour before we leave for Berne, I'll try to tell you what has happened, for some of it is very important, as you will see.\n\n\"The sail up the Rhine was perfect, and I just sat and enjoyed it with all my might. Get father's old guide-books, and read about it; I haven't words beautiful enough to describe it. At Coblentz we had a lovely time, for some students from Bonn, with whom Fred got acquainted on the boat, gave us a serenade. It was a moonlight night, and, about one o'clock, Flo and I were waked by the most delicious music under our windows. We flew up, and hid behind the curtains; but sly peeps showed us Fred and the students singing away down below. It was the most romantic thing I ever saw,--the river, the bridge of boats, the great fortress opposite, moonlight everywhere, and music fit to melt a heart of stone.\n\n\"When they were done we threw down some flowers, and saw them scramble for them, kiss their hands to the invisible ladies, and go laughing away,--to smoke and drink beer, I suppose. Next morning Fred showed me one of the crumpled flowers in his vest-pocket, and looked very sentimental. I laughed at him, and said I didn't throw it, but Flo, which seemed to disgust him, for he tossed it out of the window, and turned sensible again. I'm afraid I'm going to have trouble with that boy, it begins to look like it.\n\n\"The baths at Nassau were very gay, so was Baden-Baden, where Fred lost some money, and I scolded him. He needs some one to look after him when Frank is not with him. Kate said once she hoped he'd marry soon, and I quite agree with her that it would be well for him. Frankfort was delightful; I saw Goethe's house, Schiller's statue, and Dannecker's famous 'Ariadne.' It was very lovely, but I should have enjoyed it more if I had known the story better. I didn't like to ask, as every one knew it, or pretended they did. I wish Jo would tell me all about it; I ought to have read more, for I find I don't know anything, and it mortifies me.\n\n\"Now comes the serious part,--for it happened here, and Fred is just gone. He has been so kind and jolly that we all got quite fond of him; I never thought of anything but a travelling friendship, till the serenade night. Since then I've begun to feel that the moonlight walks, balcony talks, and daily adventures were something more to him than fun. I haven't flirted, mother, truly, but remembered what you said to me, and have done my very best. I can't help it if people like me; I don't try to make them, and it worries me if I don't care for them, though Jo says I haven't got any heart. Now I know mother will shake her head, and the girls say, 'Oh, the mercenary little wretch!' but I've made up my mind, and, if Fred asks me, I shall accept him, though I'm not madly in love. I like him, and we get on comfortably together. He is handsome, young, clever enough, and very rich,--ever so much richer than the Laurences. I don't think his family would object, and I should be very happy, for they are all kind, well-bred, generous people, and they like me. Fred, as the eldest twin, will have the estate, I suppose, and such a splendid one as it is! A city house in a fashionable street, not so showy as our big houses, but twice as comfortable, and full of solid luxury, such as English people believe in. I like it, for it's genuine. I've seen the plate, the family jewels, the old servants, and pictures of the country place, with its park, great house, lovely grounds, and fine horses. Oh, it would be all I should ask! and I'd rather have it than any title such as girls snap up so readily, and find nothing behind. I may be mercenary, but I hate poverty, and don't mean to bear it a minute longer than I can help. One of us _must_ marry well; Meg didn't, Jo won't, Beth can't yet, so I shall, and make everything cosey all round. I wouldn't marry a man I hated or despised. You may be sure of that; and, though Fred is not my model hero, he does very well, and, in time, I should get fond enough of him if he was very fond of me, and let me do just as I liked. So I've been turning the matter over in my mind the last week, for it was impossible to help seeing that Fred liked me. He said nothing, but little things showed it; he never goes with Flo, always gets on my side of the carriage, table, or promenade, looks sentimental when we are alone, and frowns at any one else who ventures to speak to me. Yesterday, at dinner, when an Austrian officer stared at us, and then said something to his friend,--a rakish-looking baron,--about '_ein wonderschönes Blöndchen_,' Fred looked as fierce as a lion, and cut his meat so savagely, it nearly flew off his plate. He isn't one of the cool, stiff Englishmen, but is rather peppery, for he has Scotch blood in him, as one might guess from his bonnie blue eyes.\n\n\"Well, last evening we went up to the castle about sunset,--at least all of us but Fred, who was to meet us there, after going to the Post Restante for letters. We had a charming time poking about the ruins, the vaults where the monster tun is, and the beautiful gardens made by the elector, long ago, for his English wife. I liked the great terrace best, for the view was divine; so, while the rest went to see the rooms inside, I sat there trying to sketch the gray stone lion's head on the wall, with scarlet woodbine sprays hanging round it. I felt as if I'd got into a romance, sitting there, watching the Neckar rolling through the valley, listening to the music of the Austrian band below, and waiting for my lover, like a real story-book girl. I had a feeling that something was going to happen, and I was ready for it. I didn't feel blushy or quakey, but quite cool, and only a little excited.\n\n\"By and by I heard Fred's voice, and then he came hurrying through the great arch to find me. He looked so troubled that I forgot all about myself, and asked what the matter was. He said he'd just got a letter begging him to come home, for Frank was very ill; so he was going at once, in the night train, and only had time to say good-by. I was very sorry for him, and disappointed for myself, but only for a minute, because he said, as he shook hands,--and said it in a way that I could not mistake,--'I shall soon come back; you won't forget me, Amy?'\n\n\"I didn't promise, but I looked at him, and he seemed satisfied, and there was no time for anything but messages and good-byes, for he was off in an hour, and we all miss him very much. I know he wanted to speak, but I think, from something he once hinted, that he had promised his father not to do anything of the sort yet awhile, for he is a rash boy, and the old gentleman dreads a foreign daughter-in-law. We shall soon meet in Rome; and then, if I don't change my mind, I'll say 'Yes, thank you,' when he says 'Will you, please?'\n\n\"Of course this is all _very private_, but I wished you to know what was going on. Don't be anxious about me; remember I am your 'prudent Amy,' and be sure I will do nothing rashly. Send me as much advice as you like; I'll use it if I can. I wish I could see you for a good talk, Marmee. Love and trust me.\n\n\"Ever your AMY.\"\n\n\"Jo, I'm anxious about Beth.\"\n\n\"Why, mother, she has seemed unusually well since the babies came.\"\n\n\"It's not her health that troubles me now; it's her spirits. I'm sure there is something on her mind, and I want you to discover what it is.\"\n\n\"What makes you think so, mother?\"\n\n\"She sits alone a good deal, and doesn't talk to her father as much as she used. I found her crying over the babies the other day. When she sings, the songs are always sad ones, and now and then I see a look in her face that I don't understand. This isn't like Beth, and it worries me.\"\n\n\"Have you asked her about it?\"\n\n\"I have tried once or twice; but she either evaded my questions, or looked so distressed that I stopped. I never force my children's confidence, and I seldom have to wait for it long.\"\n\nMrs. March glanced at Jo as she spoke, but the face opposite seemed quite unconscious of any secret disquietude but Beth's; and, after sewing thoughtfully for a minute, Jo said,--\n\n\"I think she is growing up, and so begins to dream dreams, and have hopes and fears and fidgets, without knowing why, or being able to explain them. Why, mother, Beth's eighteen, but we don't realize it, and treat her like a child, forgetting she's a woman.\"\n\n\"So she is. Dear heart, how fast you do grow up,\" returned her mother, with a sigh and a smile.\n\n\"Can't be helped, Marmee, so you must resign yourself to all sorts of worries, and let your birds hop out of the nest, one by one. I promise never to hop very far, if that is any comfort to you.\"\n\n\"It is a great comfort, Jo; I always feel strong when you are at home, now Meg is gone. Beth is too feeble and Amy too young to depend upon; but when the tug comes, you are always ready.\"\n\n\"Why, you know I don't mind hard jobs much, and there must always be one scrub in a family. Amy is splendid in fine works, and I'm not; but I feel in my element when all the carpets are to be taken up, or half the family fall sick at once. Amy is distinguishing herself abroad; but if anything is amiss at home, I'm your man.\"\n\n\"I leave Beth to your hands, then, for she will open her tender little heart to her Jo sooner than to any one else. Be very kind, and don't let her think any one watches or talks about her. If she only would get quite strong and cheerful again, I shouldn't have a wish in the world.\"\n\n\"Happy woman! I've got heaps.\"\n\n\"My dear, what are they?\"\n\n\"I'll settle Bethy's troubles, and then I'll tell you mine. They are not very wearing, so they'll keep;\" and Jo stitched away, with a wise nod which set her mother's heart at rest about her, for the present at least.\n\nWhile apparently absorbed in her own affairs, Jo watched Beth; and, after many conflicting conjectures, finally settled upon one which seemed to explain the change in her. A slight incident gave Jo the clue to the mystery, she thought, and lively fancy, loving heart did the rest. She was affecting to write busily one Saturday afternoon, when she and Beth were alone together; yet as she scribbled, she kept her eye on her sister, who seemed unusually quiet. Sitting at the window, Beth's work often dropped into her lap, and she leaned her head upon her hand, in a dejected attitude, while her eyes rested on the dull, autumnal landscape. Suddenly some one passed below, whistling like an operatic blackbird, and a voice called out,--\n\n\"All serene! Coming in to-night.\"\n\nBeth started, leaned forward, smiled and nodded, watched the passer-by till his quick tramp died away, then said softly, as if to herself,--\n\n\"How strong and well and happy that dear boy looks.\"\n\n\"Hum!\" said Jo, still intent upon her sister's face; for the bright color faded as quickly as it came, the smile vanished, and presently a tear lay shining on the window-ledge. Beth whisked it off, and glanced apprehensively at Jo; but she was scratching away at a tremendous rate, apparently engrossed in \"Olympia's Oath.\" The instant Beth turned, Jo began her watch again, saw Beth's hand go quietly to her eyes more than once, and, in her half-averted face, read a tender sorrow that made her own eyes fill. Fearing to betray herself, she slipped away, murmuring something about needing more paper.\n\n\"Mercy on me, Beth loves Laurie!\" she said, sitting down in her own room, pale with the shock of the discovery which she believed she had just made. \"I never dreamt of such a thing. What _will_ mother say? I wonder if he--\" there Jo stopped, and turned scarlet with a sudden thought. \"If he shouldn't love back again, how dreadful it would be. He must; I'll make him!\" and she shook her head threateningly at the picture of the mischievous-looking boy laughing at her from the wall. \"Oh dear, we _are_ growing up with a vengeance. Here's Meg married and a mamma, Amy flourishing away at Paris, and Beth in love. I'm the only one that has sense enough to keep out of mischief.\" Jo thought intently for a minute, with her eyes fixed on the picture; then she smoothed out her wrinkled forehead, and said, with a decided nod at the face opposite, \"No, thank you, sir; you're very charming, but you've no more stability than a weathercock; so you needn't write touching notes, and smile in that insinuating way, for it won't do a bit of good, and I won't have it.\"\n\nThen she sighed, and fell into a reverie, from which she did not wake till the early twilight sent her down to take new observations, which only confirmed her suspicion. Though Laurie flirted with Amy and joked with Jo, his manner to Beth had always been peculiarly kind and gentle, but so was everybody's; therefore, no one thought of imagining that he cared more for her than for the others. Indeed, a general impression had prevailed in the family, of late, that \"our boy\" was getting fonder than ever of Jo, who, however, wouldn't hear a word upon the subject, and scolded violently if any one dared to suggest it. If they had known the various tender passages of the past year, or rather attempts at tender passages which had been nipped in the bud, they would have had the immense satisfaction of saying, \"I told you so.\" But Jo hated \"philandering,\" and wouldn't allow it, always having a joke or a smile ready at the least sign of impending danger.\n\nWhen Laurie first went to college, he fell in love about once a month; but these small flames were as brief as ardent, did no damage, and much amused Jo, who took great interest in the alternations of hope, despair, and resignation, which were confided to her in their weekly conferences. But there came a time when Laurie ceased to worship at many shrines, hinted darkly at one all-absorbing passion, and indulged occasionally in Byronic fits of gloom. Then he avoided the tender subject altogether, wrote philosophical notes to Jo, turned studious, and gave out that he was going to \"dig,\" intending to graduate in a blaze of glory. This suited the young lady better than twilight confidences, tender pressures of the hand, and eloquent glances of the eye; for with Jo, brain developed earlier than heart, and she preferred imaginary heroes to real ones, because, when tired of them, the former could be shut up in the tin-kitchen till called for, and the latter were less manageable.\n\nThings were in this state when the grand discovery was made, and Jo watched Laurie that night as she had never done before. If she had not got the new idea into her head, she would have seen nothing unusual in the fact that Beth was very quiet, and Laurie very kind to her. But having given the rein to her lively fancy, it galloped away with her at a great pace; and common sense, being rather weakened by a long course of romance writing, did not come to the rescue. As usual, Beth lay on the sofa, and Laurie sat in a low chair close by, amusing her with all sorts of gossip; for she depended on her weekly \"spin,\" and he never disappointed her. But that evening, Jo fancied that Beth's eyes rested on the lively, dark face beside her with peculiar pleasure, and that she listened with intense interest to an account of some exciting cricket-match, though the phrases, \"caught off a tice,\" \"stumped off his ground,\" and \"the leg hit for three,\" were as intelligible to her as Sanscrit. She also fancied, having set her heart upon seeing it, that she saw a certain increase of gentleness in Laurie's manner, that he dropped his voice now and then, laughed less than usual, was a little absent-minded, and settled the afghan over Beth's feet with an assiduity that was really almost tender.\n\n\"Who knows? stranger things have happened,\" thought Jo, as she fussed about the room. \"She will make quite an angel of him, and he will make life delightfully easy and pleasant for the dear, if they only love each other. I don't see how he can help it; and I do believe he would if the rest of us were out of the way.\"\n\nAs every one _was_ out of the way but herself, Jo began to feel that she ought to dispose of herself with all speed. But where should she go? and burning to lay herself upon the shrine of sisterly devotion, she sat down to settle that point.\n\nNow, the old sofa was a regular patriarch of a sofa,--long, broad, well-cushioned, and low; a trifle shabby, as well it might be, for the girls had slept and sprawled on it as babies, fished over the back, rode on the arms, and had menageries under it as children, and rested tired heads, dreamed dreams, and listened to tender talk on it as young women. They all loved it, for it was a family refuge, and one corner had always been Jo's favorite lounging-place. Among the many pillows that adorned the venerable couch was one, hard, round, covered with prickly horsehair, and furnished with a knobby button at each end; this repulsive pillow was her especial property, being used as a weapon of defence, a barricade, or a stern preventive of too much slumber.\n\nLaurie knew this pillow well, and had cause to regard it with deep aversion, having been unmercifully pummelled with it in former days, when romping was allowed, and now frequently debarred by it from taking the seat he most coveted, next to Jo in the sofa corner. If \"the sausage\" as they called it, stood on end, it was a sign that he might approach and repose; but if it lay flat across the sofa, woe to the man, woman, or child who dared disturb it! That evening Jo forgot to barricade her corner, and had not been in her seat five minutes, before a massive form appeared beside her, and, with both arms spread over the sofa-back, both long legs stretched out before him, Laurie exclaimed, with a sigh of satisfaction,--\n\n\"Now, _this_ is filling at the price.\"\n\n\"No slang,\" snapped Jo, slamming down the pillow. But it was too late, there was no room for it; and, coasting on to the floor, it disappeared in a most mysterious manner.\n\n\"Come, Jo, don't be thorny. After studying himself to a skeleton all the week, a fellow deserves petting, and ought to get it.\"\n\n\"Beth will pet you; I'm busy.\"\n\n\"No, she's not to be bothered with me; but you like that sort of thing, unless you've suddenly lost your taste for it. Have you? Do you hate your boy, and want to fire pillows at him?\"\n\nAnything more wheedlesome than that touching appeal was seldom heard, but Jo quenched \"her boy\" by turning on him with the stern query,--\n\n\"How many bouquets have you sent Miss Randal this week?\"\n\n\"Not one, upon my word. She's engaged. Now then.\"\n\n\"I'm glad of it; that's one of your foolish extravagances,--sending flowers and things to girls for whom you don't care two pins,\" continued Jo reprovingly.\n\n\"Sensible girls, for whom I do care whole papers of pins, won't let me send them 'flowers and things,' so what can I do? My feelings must have a _went_.\"\n\n\"Mother doesn't approve of flirting, even in fun; and you do flirt desperately, Teddy.\"\n\n\"I'd give anything if I could answer, 'So do you.' As I can't, I'll merely say that I don't see any harm in that pleasant little game, if all parties understand that it's only play.\"\n\n\"Well, it does look pleasant, but I can't learn how it's done. I've tried, because one feels awkward in company, not to do as everybody else is doing; but I don't seem to get on,\" said Jo, forgetting to play Mentor.\n\n\"Take lessons of Amy; she has a regular talent for it.\"\n\n\"Yes, she does it very prettily, and never seems to go too far. I suppose it's natural to some people to please without trying, and others to always say and do the wrong thing in the wrong place.\"\n\n\"I'm glad you can't flirt; it's really refreshing to see a sensible, straightforward girl, who can be jolly and kind without making a fool of herself. Between ourselves, Jo, some of the girls I know really do go on at such a rate I'm ashamed of them. They don't mean any harm, I'm sure; but if they knew how we fellows talked about them afterward, they'd mend their ways, I fancy.\"\n\n\"They do the same; and, as their tongues are the sharpest, you fellows get the worst of it, for you are as silly as they, every bit. If you behaved properly, they would; but, knowing you like their nonsense, they keep it up, and then you blame them.\"\n\n\"Much you know about it, ma'am,\" said Laurie, in a superior tone. \"We don't like romps and flirts, though we may act as if we did sometimes. The pretty, modest girls are never talked about, except respectfully, among gentlemen. Bless your innocent soul! If you could be in my place for a month you'd see things that would astonish you a trifle. Upon my word, when I see one of those harum-scarum girls, I always want to say with our friend Cock Robin,--\n\n\"'Out upon you, fie upon you, Bold-faced jig!'\"\n\nIt was impossible to help laughing at the funny conflict between Laurie's chivalrous reluctance to speak ill of womankind, and his very natural dislike of the unfeminine folly of which fashionable society showed him many samples. Jo knew that \"young Laurence\" was regarded as a most eligible _parti_ by worldly mammas, was much smiled upon by their daughters, and flattered enough by ladies of all ages to make a coxcomb of him; so she watched him rather jealously, fearing he would be spoilt, and rejoiced more than she confessed to find that he still believed in modest girls. Returning suddenly to her admonitory tone, she said, dropping her voice, \"If you _must_ have a 'went,' Teddy, go and devote yourself to one of the 'pretty, modest girls' whom you do respect, and not waste your time with the silly ones.\"\n\n\"You really advise it?\" and Laurie looked at her with an odd mixture of anxiety and merriment in his face.\n\n\"Yes, I do; but you'd better wait till you are through college, on the whole, and be fitting yourself for the place meantime. You're not half good enough for--well, whoever the modest girl maybe,\" and Jo looked a little queer likewise, for a name had almost escaped her.\n\n\"That I'm not!\" acquiesced Laurie, with an expression of humility quite new to him, as he dropped his eyes, and absently wound Jo's apron-tassel round his finger.\n\n\"Mercy on us, this will never do,\" thought Jo; adding aloud, \"Go and sing to me. I'm dying for some music, and always like yours.\"\n\n\"I'd rather stay here, thank you.\"\n\n\"Well, you can't; there isn't room. Go and make yourself useful, since you are too big to be ornamental. I thought you hated to be tied to a woman's apron-string?\" retorted Jo, quoting certain rebellious words of his own.\n\n\"Ah, that depends on who wears the apron!\" and Laurie gave an audacious tweak at the tassel.\n\n\"Are you going?\" demanded Jo, diving for the pillow.\n\nHe fled at once, and the minute it was well \"Up with the bonnets of bonnie Dundee,\" she slipped away, to return no more till the young gentleman had departed in high dudgeon.\n\nJo lay long awake that night, and was just dropping off when the sound of a stifled sob made her fly to Beth's bedside, with the anxious inquiry, \"What is it, dear?\"\n\n\"I thought you were asleep,\" sobbed Beth.\n\n\"Is it the old pain, my precious?\"\n\n\"No; it's a new one; but I can bear it,\" and Beth tried to check her tears.\n\n\"Tell me all about it, and let me cure it as I often did the other.\"\n\n\"You can't; there is no cure.\" There Beth's voice gave way, and, clinging to her sister, she cried so despairingly that Jo was frightened.\n\n\"Where is it? Shall I call mother?\"\n\nBeth did not answer the first question; but in the dark one hand went involuntarily to her heart, as if the pain were there; with the other she held Jo fast, whispering eagerly, \"No, no, don't call her, don't tell her. I shall be better soon. Lie down here and 'poor' my head. I'll be quiet, and go to sleep; indeed I will.\"\n\nJo obeyed; but as her hand went softly to and fro across Beth's hot forehead and wet eyelids, her heart was very full, and she longed to speak. But young as she was, Jo had learned that hearts, like flowers, cannot be rudely handled, but must open naturally; so, though she believed she knew the cause of Beth's new pain, she only said, in her tenderest tone, \"Does anything trouble you, deary?\"\n\n\"Yes, Jo,\" after a long pause.\n\n\"Wouldn't it comfort you to tell me what it is?\"\n\n\"Not now, not yet.\"\n\n\"Then I won't ask; but remember, Bethy, that mother and Jo are always glad to hear and help you, if they can.\"\n\n\"I know it. I'll tell you by and by.\"\n\n\"Is the pain better now?\"\n\n\"Oh, yes, much better; you are so comfortable, Jo!\"\n\n\"Go to sleep, dear; I'll stay with you.\"\n\nSo cheek to cheek they fell asleep, and on the morrow Beth seemed quite herself again; for at eighteen, neither heads nor hearts ache long, and a loving word can medicine most ills.\n\nBut Jo had made up her mind, and, after pondering over a project for some days, she confided it to her mother.\n\n\"You asked me the other day what my wishes were. I'll tell you one of them, Marmee,\" she began, as they sat alone together. \"I want to go away somewhere this winter for a change.\"\n\n\"Why, Jo?\" and her mother looked up quickly, as if the words suggested a double meaning.\n\nWith her eyes on her work, Jo answered soberly, \"I want something new; I feel restless, and anxious to be seeing, doing, and learning more than I am. I brood too much over my own small affairs, and need stirring up, so, as I can be spared this winter, I'd like to hop a little way, and try my wings.\"\n\n\"Where will you hop?\"\n\n\"To New York. I had a bright idea yesterday, and this is it. You know Mrs. Kirke wrote to you for some respectable young person to teach her children and sew. It's rather hard to find just the thing, but I think I should suit if I tried.\"\n\n\"My dear, go out to service in that great boarding-house!\" and Mrs. March looked surprised, but not displeased.\n\n\"It's not exactly going out to service; for Mrs. Kirke is your friend,--the kindest soul that ever lived,--and would make things pleasant for me, I know. Her family is separate from the rest, and no one knows me there. Don't care if they do; it's honest work, and I'm not ashamed of it.\"\n\n\"Nor I; but your writing?\"\n\"All the better for the change. I shall see and hear new things, get new ideas, and, even if I haven't much time there, I shall bring home quantities of material for my rubbish.\"\n\n\"I have no doubt of it; but are these your only reasons for this sudden fancy?\"\n\n\"No, mother.\"\n\n\"May I know the others?\"\n\nJo looked up and Jo looked down, then said slowly, with sudden color in her cheeks, \"It may be vain and wrong to say it, but--I'm afraid--Laurie is getting too fond of me.\"\n\n\"Then you don't care for him in the way it is evident he begins to care for you?\" and Mrs. March looked anxious as she put the question.\n\n\"Mercy, no! I love the dear boy, as I always have, and am immensely proud of him; but as for anything more, it's out of the question.\"\n\n\"I'm glad of that, Jo.\"\n\n\"Why, please?\"\n\n\"Because, dear, I don't think you suited to one another. As friends you are very happy, and your frequent quarrels soon blow over; but I fear you would both rebel if you were mated for life. You are too much alike and too fond of freedom, not to mention hot tempers and strong wills, to get on happily together, in a relation which needs infinite patience and forbearance, as well as love.\"\n\n\"That's just the feeling I had, though I couldn't express it. I'm glad you think he is only beginning to care for me. It would trouble me sadly to make him unhappy; for I couldn't fall in love with the dear old fellow merely out of gratitude, could I?\"\n\n\"You are sure of his feeling for you?\"\n\nThe color deepened in Jo's cheeks, as she answered, with the look of mingled pleasure, pride, and pain which young girls wear when speaking of first lovers,--\n\n\"I'm afraid it is so, mother; he hasn't said anything, but he looks a great deal. I think I had better go away before it comes to anything.\"\n\n\"I agree with you, and if it can be managed you shall go.\"\n\nJo looked relieved, and, after a pause, said, smiling, \"How Mrs. Moffat would wonder at your want of management, if she knew; and how she will rejoice that Annie still may hope.\"\n\n\"Ah, Jo, mothers may differ in their management, but the hope is the same in all,--the desire to see their children happy. Meg is so, and I am content with her success. You I leave to enjoy your liberty till you tire of it; for only then will you find that there is something sweeter. Amy is my chief care now, but her good sense will help her. For Beth, I indulge no hopes except that she may be well. By the way, she seems brighter this last day or two. Have you spoken to her?\"\n\n\"Yes; she owned she had a trouble, and promised to tell me by and by. I said no more, for I think I know it;\" and Jo told her little story.\n\nMrs. March shook her head, and did not take so romantic a view of the case, but looked grave, and repeated her opinion that, for Laurie's sake, Jo should go away for a time.\n\n\"Let us say nothing about it to him till the plan is settled; then I'll run away before he can collect his wits and be tragical. Beth must think I'm going to please myself, as I am, for I can't talk about Laurie to her; but she can pet and comfort him after I'm gone, and so cure him of this romantic notion. He's been through so many little trials of the sort, he's used to it, and will soon get over his love-lornity.\"\n\nJo spoke hopefully, but could not rid herself of the foreboding fear that this \"little trial\" would be harder than the others, and that Laurie would not get over his \"love-lornity\" as easily as heretofore.\n\nThe plan was talked over in a family council, and agreed upon; for Mrs. Kirke gladly accepted Jo, and promised to make a pleasant home for her. The teaching would render her independent; and such leisure as she got might be made profitable by writing, while the new scenes and society would be both useful and agreeable. Jo liked the prospect and was eager to be gone, for the home-nest was growing too narrow for her restless nature and adventurous spirit. When all was settled, with fear and trembling she told Laurie; but to her surprise he took it very quietly. He had been graver than usual of late, but very pleasant; and, when jokingly accused of turning over a new leaf, he answered soberly, \"So I am; and I mean this one shall stay turned.\"\n\nJo was very much relieved that one of his virtuous fits should come on just then, and made her preparations with a lightened heart,--for Beth seemed more cheerful,--and hoped she was doing the best for all.\n\n\"One thing I leave to your especial care,\" she said, the night before she left.\n\n\"You mean your papers?\" asked Beth.\n\n\"No, my boy. Be very good to him, won't you?\"\n\n\"Of course I will; but I can't fill your place, and he'll miss you sadly.\"\n\n\"It won't hurt him; so remember, I leave him in your charge, to plague, pet, and keep in order.\"\n\n\"I'll do my best, for your sake,\" promised Beth, wondering why Jo looked at her so queerly.\n\nWhen Laurie said \"Good-by,\" he whispered significantly, \"It won't do a bit of good, Jo. My eye is on you; so mind what you do, or I'll come and bring you home.\"\n\n\"NEW YORK, November.\n\n\"I'm going to write you a regular volume, for I've got heaps to tell, though I'm not a fine young lady travelling on the continent. When I lost sight of father's dear old face, I felt a trifle blue, and might have shed a briny drop or two, if an Irish lady with four small children, all crying more or less, hadn't diverted my mind; for I amused myself by dropping gingerbread nuts over the seat every time they opened their mouths to roar.\n\n\"Soon the sun came out, and taking it as a good omen, I cleared up likewise, and enjoyed my journey with all my heart.\n\n\"Mrs. Kirke welcomed me so kindly I felt at home at once, even in that big house full of strangers. She gave me a funny little sky-parlor--all she had; but there is a stove in it, and a nice table in a sunny window, so I can sit here and write whenever I like. A fine view and a church-tower opposite atone for the many stairs, and I took a fancy to my den on the spot. The nursery, where I am to teach and sew, is a pleasant room next Mrs. Kirke's private parlor, and the two little girls are pretty children,--rather spoilt, I fancy, but they took to me after telling them 'The Seven Bad Pigs;' and I've no doubt I shall make a model governess.\n\n\"I am to have my meals with the children, if I prefer it to the great table, and for the present I do, for I _am_ bashful, though no one will believe it.\n\n\"'Now, my dear, make yourself at home,' said Mrs. K. in her motherly way; 'I'm on the drive from morning to night, as you may suppose with such a family; but a great anxiety will be off my mind if I know the children are safe with you. My rooms are always open to you, and your own shall be as comfortable as I can make it. There are some pleasant people in the house if you feel sociable, and your evenings are always free. Come to me if anything goes wrong, and be as happy as you can. There's the tea-bell; I must run and change my cap;' and off she bustled, leaving me to settle myself in my new nest.\n\n\"As I went downstairs, soon after, I saw something I liked. The flights are very long in this tall house, and as I stood waiting at the head of the third one for a little servant girl to lumber up, I saw a gentleman come along behind her, take the heavy hod of coal out of her hand, carry it all the way up, put it down at a door near by, and walk away, saying, with a kind nod and a foreign accent,--\n\n\"'It goes better so. The little back is too young to haf such heaviness.'\n\n\"Wasn't it good of him? I like such things, for, as father says, trifles show character. When I mentioned it to Mrs. K., that evening, she laughed, and said,--\n\n\"'That must have been Professor Bhaer; he's always doing things of that sort.'\n\n\"Mrs. K. told me he was from Berlin; very learned and good, but poor as a church-mouse, and gives lessons to support himself and two little orphan nephews whom he is educating here, according to the wishes of his sister, who married an American. Not a very romantic story, but it interested me; and I was glad to hear that Mrs. K. lends him her parlor for some of his scholars. There is a glass door between it and the nursery, and I mean to peep at him, and then I'll tell you how he looks. He's almost forty, so it's no harm, Marmee.\n\n\"After tea and a go-to-bed romp with the little girls, I attacked the big work-basket, and had a quiet evening chatting with my new friend. I shall keep a journal-letter, and send it once a week; so good-night, and more to-morrow.\"\n\n\"_Tuesday Eve._\n\n\"Had a lively time in my seminary, this morning, for the children acted like Sancho; and at one time I really thought I should shake them all round. Some good angel inspired me to try gymnastics, and I kept it up till they were glad to sit down and keep still. After luncheon, the girl took them out for a walk, and I went to my needle-work, like little Mabel, 'with a willing mind.' I was thanking my stars that I'd learned to make nice button-holes, when the parlor-door opened and shut, and some one began to hum,--\n\n'Kennst du das land,'\n\nlike a big bumble-bee. It was dreadfully improper, I know, but I couldn't resist the temptation; and lifting one end of the curtain before the glass door, I peeped in. Professor Bhaer was there; and while he arranged his books, I took a good look at him. A regular German,--rather stout, with brown hair tumbled all over his head, a bushy beard, good nose, the kindest eyes I ever saw, and a splendid big voice that does one's ears good, after our sharp or slipshod American gabble. His clothes were rusty, his hands were large, and he hadn't a really handsome feature in his face, except his beautiful teeth; yet I liked him, for he had a fine head; his linen was very nice, and he looked like a gentleman, though two buttons were off his coat, and there was a patch on one shoe. He looked sober in spite of his humming, till he went to the window to turn the hyacinth bulbs toward the sun, and stroke the cat, who received him like an old friend. Then he smiled; and when a tap came at the door, called out in a loud, brisk tone,--\n\n\"'Herein!'\n\n\"I was just going to run, when I caught sight of a morsel of a child carrying a big book, and stopped to see what was going on.\n\n\"'Me wants my Bhaer,' said the mite, slamming down her book, and running to meet him.\n\n\"'Thou shalt haf thy Bhaer; come, then, and take a goot hug from him, my Tina,' said the Professor, catching her up, with a laugh, and holding her so high over his head that she had to stoop her little face to kiss him.\n\n\"'Now me mus tuddy my lessin,' went on the funny little thing; so he put her up at the table, opened the great dictionary she had brought, and gave her a paper and pencil, and she scribbled away, turning a leaf now and then, and passing her little fat finger down the page, as if finding a word, so soberly that I nearly betrayed myself by a laugh, while Mr. Bhaer stood stroking her pretty hair, with a fatherly look, that made me think she must be his own, though she looked more French than German.\n\n\"Another knock and the appearance of two young ladies sent me back to my work, and there I virtuously remained through all the noise and gabbling that went on next door. One of the girls kept laughing affectedly, and saying 'Now Professor,' in a coquettish tone, and the other pronounced her German with an accent that must have made it hard for him to keep sober.\n\n\"Both seemed to try his patience sorely; for more than once I heard him say emphatically, 'No, no, it is _not_ so; you haf not attend to what I say;' and once there was a loud rap, as if he struck the table with his book, followed by the despairing exclamation, 'Prut! it all goes bad this day.'\n\n\"Poor man, I pitied him; and when the girls were gone, took just one more peep, to see if he survived it. He seemed to have thrown himself back in his chair, tired out, and sat there with his eyes shut till the clock struck two, when he jumped up, put his books in his pocket, as if ready for another lesson, and, taking little Tina, who had fallen asleep on the sofa, in his arms, he carried her quietly away. I fancy he has a hard life of it.\n\n\"Mrs. Kirke asked me if I wouldn't go down to the five o'clock dinner; and, feeling a little bit homesick, I thought I would, just to see what sort of people are under the same roof with me. So I made myself respectable, and tried to slip in behind Mrs. Kirke; but as she is short, and I'm tall, my efforts at concealment were rather a failure. She gave me a seat by her, and after my face cooled off, I plucked up courage, and looked about me. The long table was full, and every one intent on getting their dinner,--the gentlemen especially, who seemed to be eating on time, for they _bolted_ in every sense of the word, vanishing as soon as they were done. There was the usual assortment of young men absorbed in themselves; young couples absorbed in each other; married ladies in their babies, and old gentlemen in politics. I don't think I shall care to have much to do with any of them, except one sweet-faced maiden lady, who looks as if she had something in her.\n\n\"Cast away at the very bottom of the table was the Professor, shouting answers to the questions of a very inquisitive, deaf old gentleman on one side, and talking philosophy with a Frenchman on the other. If Amy had been here, she'd have turned her back on him forever, because, sad to relate, he had a great appetite, and shovelled in his dinner in a manner which would have horrified 'her ladyship.' I didn't mind, for I like 'to see folks eat with a relish,' as Hannah says, and the poor man must have needed a deal of food after teaching idiots all day.\n\n\"As I went upstairs after dinner, two of the young men were settling their hats before the hall-mirror, and I heard one say low to the other, 'Who's the new party?'\n\n\"'Governess, or something of that sort.'\n\n\"'What the deuce is she at our table for?'\n\n\"'Friend of the old lady's.'\n\n\"'Handsome head, but no style.'\n\n\"'Not a bit of it. Give us a light and come on.'\n\n\"I felt angry at first, and then I didn't care, for a governess is as good as a clerk, and I've got sense, if I haven't style, which is more than some people have, judging from the remarks of the elegant beings who clattered away, smoking like bad chimneys. I hate ordinary people!\"\n\n\"_Thursday._\n\n\"Yesterday was a quiet day, spent in teaching, sewing, and writing in my little room, which is very cosey, with a light and fire. I picked up a few bits of news, and was introduced to the Professor. It seems that Tina is the child of the Frenchwoman who does the fine ironing in the laundry here. The little thing has lost her heart to Mr. Bhaer, and follows him about the house like a dog whenever he is at home, which delights him, as he is very fond of children, though a 'bacheldore.' Kitty and Minnie Kirke likewise regard him with affection, and tell all sorts of stories about the plays he invents, the presents he brings, and the splendid tales he tells. The young men quiz him, it seems, call him Old Fritz, Lager Beer, Ursa Major, and make all manner of jokes on his name. But he enjoys it like a boy, Mrs. K. says, and takes it so good-naturedly that they all like him, in spite of his foreign ways.\n\n\"The maiden lady is a Miss Norton,--rich, cultivated, and kind. She spoke to me at dinner to-day (for I went to table again, it's such fun to watch people), and asked me to come and see her at her room. She has fine books and pictures, knows interesting persons, and seems friendly; so I shall make myself agreeable, for I _do_ want to get into good society, only it isn't the same sort that Amy likes.\n\n\"I was in our parlor last evening, when Mr. Bhaer came in with some newspapers for Mrs. Kirke. She wasn't there, but Minnie, who is a little old woman, introduced me very prettily: 'This is mamma's friend, Miss March.'\n\n\"'Yes; and she's jolly and we like her lots,' added Kitty, who is an _enfant terrible_.\n\n\"We both bowed, and then we laughed, for the prim introduction and the blunt addition were rather a comical contrast.\n\n\"'Ah, yes, I hear these naughty ones go to vex you, Mees Marsch. If so again, call at me and I come,' he said, with a threatening frown that delighted the little wretches.\n\n\"I promised I would, and he departed; but it seems as if I was doomed to see a good deal of him, for to-day, as I passed his door on my way out, by accident I knocked against it with my umbrella. It flew open, and there he stood in his dressing gown, with a big blue sock on one hand, and a darning-needle in the other; he didn't seem at all ashamed of it, for when I explained and hurried on, he waved his hand, sock and all, saying in his loud, cheerful way,--\n\n\"'You haf a fine day to make your walk. _Bon voyage, mademoiselle._'\n\n\"I laughed all the way downstairs; but it was a little pathetic, also, to think of the poor man having to mend his own clothes. The German gentlemen embroider, I know; but darning hose is another thing, and not so pretty.\"\n\n\"_Saturday._\n\n\"Nothing has happened to write about, except a call on Miss Norton, who has a room full of lovely things, and who was very charming, for she showed me all her treasures, and asked me if I would sometimes go with her to lectures and concerts, as her escort,--if I enjoyed them. She put it as a favor, but I'm sure Mrs. Kirke has told her about us, and she does it out of kindness to me. I'm as proud as Lucifer, but such favors from such people don't burden me, and I accepted gratefully.\n\n\"When I got back to the nursery there was such an uproar in the parlor that I looked in; and there was Mr. Bhaer down on his hands and knees, with Tina on his back, Kitty leading him with a jump-rope, and Minnie feeding two small boys with seed-cakes, as they roared and ramped in cages built of chairs.\n\n\"'We are playing _nargerie_,' explained Kitty.\n\n\"'Dis is mine effalunt!' added Tina, holding on by the Professor's hair.\n\n\"'Mamma always allows us to do what we like Saturday afternoon, when Franz and Emil come, doesn't she, Mr. Bhaer?' said Minnie.\n\n\"The 'effalunt' sat up, looking as much in earnest as any of them, and said soberly to me,--\n\n\"'I gif you my wort it is so. If we make too large a noise you shall say \"Hush!\" to us, and we go more softly.'\n\n\"I promised to do so, but left the door open, and enjoyed the fun as much as they did,--for a more glorious frolic I never witnessed. They played tag and soldiers, danced and sung, and when it began to grow dark they all piled on to the sofa about the Professor, while he told charming fairy stories of the storks on the chimney-tops, and the little 'kobolds,' who ride the snow-flakes as they fall. I wish Americans were as simple and natural as Germans, don't you?\n\n\"I'm so fond of writing, I should go spinning on forever if motives of economy didn't stop me, for though I've used thin paper and written fine, I tremble to think of the stamps this long letter will need. Pray forward Amy's as soon as you can spare them. My small news will sound very flat after her splendors, but you will like them, I know. Is Teddy studying so hard that he can't find time to write to his friends? Take good care of him for me, Beth, and tell me all about the babies, and give heaps of love to every one.\n\n\"From your faithful JO.\n\n\"P. S. On reading over my letter it strikes me as rather Bhaery; but I am always interested in odd people, and I really had nothing else to write about. Bless you!\"\n\n\"As this is to be a scribble-scrabble letter, I direct it to you, for it may amuse you, and give you some idea of my goings on; for, though quiet, they are rather amusing, for which, oh, be joyful! After what Amy would call Herculaneum efforts, in the way of mental and moral agriculture, my young ideas begin to shoot and my little twigs to bend as I could wish. They are not so interesting to me as Tina and the boys, but I do my duty by them, and they are fond of me. Franz and Emil are jolly little lads, quite after my own heart; for the mixture of German and American spirit in them produces a constant state of effervescence. Saturday afternoons are riotous times, whether spent in the house or out; for on pleasant days they all go to walk, like a seminary, with the Professor and myself to keep order; and then such fun!\n\n\"We are very good friends now, and I've begun to take lessons. I really couldn't help it, and it all came about in such a droll way that I must tell you. To begin at the beginning, Mrs. Kirke called to me, one day, as I passed Mr. Bhaer's room, where she was rummaging.\n\n\"'Did you ever see such a den, my dear? Just come and help me put these books to rights, for I've turned everything upside down, trying to discover what he has done with the six new handkerchiefs I gave him not long ago.'\n\n\"I went in, and while we worked I looked about me, for it was 'a den,' to be sure. Books and papers everywhere; a broken meerschaum, and an old flute over the mantel-piece as if done with; a ragged bird, without any tail, chirped on one window-seat, and a box of white mice adorned the other; half-finished boats and bits of string lay among the manuscripts; dirty little boots stood drying before the fire; and traces of the dearly beloved boys, for whom he makes a slave of himself, were to be seen all over the room. After a grand rummage three of the missing articles were found,--one over the bird-cage, one covered with ink, and a third burnt brown, having been used as a holder.\n\n\"'Such a man!' laughed good-natured Mrs. K., as she put the relics in the rag-bag. 'I suppose the others are torn up to rig ships, bandage cut fingers, or make kite-tails. It's dreadful, but I can't scold him: he's so absent-minded and good-natured, he lets those boys ride over him rough-shod. I agreed to do his washing and mending, but he forgets to give out his things and I forget to look them over, so he comes to a sad pass sometimes.'\n\n\"'Let me mend them,' said I. 'I don't mind it, and he needn't know. I'd like to,--he's so kind to me about bringing my letters and lending books.'\n\n\"So I have got his things in order, and knit heels into two pairs of the socks,--for they were boggled out of shape with his queer darns. Nothing was said, and I hoped he wouldn't find it out, but one day last week he caught me at it. Hearing the lessons he gives to others has interested and amused me so much that I took a fancy to learn; for Tina runs in and out, leaving the door open, and I can hear. I had been sitting near this door, finishing off the last sock, and trying to understand what he said to a new scholar, who is as stupid as I am. The girl had gone, and I thought he had also, it was so still, and I was busily gabbling over a verb, and rocking to and fro in a most absurd way, when a little crow made me look up, and there was Mr. Bhaer looking and laughing quietly, while he made signs to Tina not to betray him.\n\n\"'So!' he said, as I stopped and stared like a goose, 'you peep at me, I peep at you, and that is not bad; but see, I am not pleasanting when I say, haf you a wish for German?'\n\n\"'Yes; but you are too busy. I am too stupid to learn,' I blundered out, as red as a peony.\n\n\"'Prut! we will make the time, and we fail not to find the sense. At efening I shall gif a little lesson with much gladness; for, look you, Mees Marsch, I haf this debt to pay,' and he pointed to my work. 'Yes, they say to one another, these so kind ladies, \"he is a stupid old fellow; he will see not what we do; he will never opserve that his sock-heels go not in holes any more, he will think his buttons grow out new when they fall, and believe that strings make theirselves.\" Ah! but I haf an eye, and I see much. I haf a heart, and I feel the thanks for this. Come, a little lesson then and now, or no more good fairy works for me and mine.'\n\n\"Of course I couldn't say anything after that, and as it really is a splendid opportunity, I made the bargain, and we began. I took four lessons, and then I stuck fast in a grammatical bog. The Professor was very patient with me, but it must have been torment to him, and now and then he'd look at me with such an expression of mild despair that it was a toss-up with me whether to laugh or cry. I tried both ways; and when it came to a sniff of utter mortification and woe, he just threw the grammar on to the floor, and marched out of the room. I felt myself disgraced and deserted forever, but didn't blame him a particle, and was scrambling my papers together, meaning to rush upstairs and shake myself hard, when in he came, as brisk and beaming as if I'd covered myself with glory.\n\n\"'Now we shall try a new way. You and I will read these pleasant little Märchen together, and dig no more in that dry book, that goes in the corner for making us trouble.'\n\n\"He spoke so kindly, and opened Hans Andersen's fairy tales so invitingly before me, that I was more ashamed than ever, and went at my lesson in a neck-or-nothing style that seemed to amuse him immensely. I forgot my bashfulness, and pegged away (no other word will express it) with all my might, tumbling over long words, pronouncing according to the inspiration of the minute, and doing my very best. When I finished reading my first page, and stopped for breath, he clapped his hands and cried out, in his hearty way, 'Das ist gute! Now we go well! My turn. I do him in German; gif me your ear.' And away he went, rumbling out the words with his strong voice, and a relish which was good to see as well as hear. Fortunately the story was the 'Constant Tin Soldier,' which is droll, you know, so I could laugh,--and I did,--though I didn't understand half he read, for I couldn't help it, he was so earnest, I so excited, and the whole thing so comical.\n\n\"After that we got on better, and now I read my lessons pretty well; for this way of studying suits me, and I can see that the grammar gets tucked into the tales and poetry as one gives pills in jelly. I like it very much, and he doesn't seem tired of it yet,--which is very good of him, isn't it? I mean to give him something on Christmas, for I dare not offer money. Tell me something nice, Marmee.\n\n\"I'm glad Laurie seems so happy and busy, that he has given up smoking, and lets his hair grow. You see Beth manages him better than I did. I'm not jealous, dear; do your best, only don't make a saint of him. I'm afraid I couldn't like him without a spice of human naughtiness. Read him bits of my letters. I haven't time to write much, and that will do just as well. Thank Heaven Beth continues so comfortable.\"\n\n\"A Happy New Year to you all, my dearest family, which of course includes Mr. L. and a young man by the name of Teddy. I can't tell you how much I enjoyed your Christmas bundle, for I didn't get it till night, and had given up hoping. Your letter came in the morning, but you said nothing about a parcel, meaning it for a surprise; so I was disappointed, for I'd had a 'kind of a feeling' that you wouldn't forget me. I felt a little low in my mind, as I sat up in my room, after tea; and when the big, muddy, battered-looking bundle was brought to me, I just hugged it, and pranced. It was so _homey_ and refreshing, that I sat down on the floor and read and looked and ate and laughed and cried, in my usual absurd way. The things were just what I wanted, and all the better for being made instead of bought. Beth's new 'ink-bib' was capital; and Hannah's box of hard gingerbread will be a treasure. I'll be sure and wear the nice flannels you sent, Marmee, and read carefully the books father has marked. Thank you all, heaps and heaps!\n\n\"Speaking of books reminds me that I'm getting rich in that line for, on New Year's Day, Mr. Bhaer gave me a fine Shakespeare. It is one he values much, and I've often admired it, set up in the place of honor, with his German Bible, Plato, Homer, and Milton; so you may imagine how I felt when he brought it down, without its cover, and showed me my name in it, 'from my friend Friedrich Bhaer.'\n\n\"'You say often you wish a library: here I gif you one; for between these lids (he meant covers) is many books in one. Read him well, and he will help you much; for the study of character in this book will help you to read it in the world and paint it with your pen.'\n\n\"I thanked him as well as I could, and talk now about 'my library,' as if I had a hundred books. I never knew how much there was in Shakespeare before; but then I never had a Bhaer to explain it to me. Now _don't_ laugh at his horrid name; it isn't pronounced either Bear or Beer, as people _will_ say it, but something between the two, as only Germans can give it. I'm glad you both like what I tell you about him, and hope you will know him some day. Mother would admire his warm heart, father his wise head. I admire both, and feel rich in my new 'friend Friedrich Bhaer.'\n\n\"Not having much money, or knowing what he'd like, I got several little things, and put them about the room, where he would find them unexpectedly. They were useful, pretty, or funny,--a new standish on his table, a little vase for his flower,--he always has one, or a bit of green in a glass, to keep him fresh, he says,--and a holder for his blower, so that he needn't burn up what Amy calls 'mouchoirs.' I made it like those Beth invented,--a big butterfly with a fat body, and black and yellow wings, worsted feelers, and bead eyes. It took his fancy immensely, and he put it on his mantel-piece as an article of _vertu_; so it was rather a failure after all. Poor as he is, he didn't forget a servant or a child in the house; and not a soul here, from the French laundry-woman to Miss Norton, forgot him. I was so glad of that.\n\n\"They got up a masquerade, and had a gay time New Year's Eve. I didn't mean to go down, having no dress; but at the last minute, Mrs. Kirke remembered some old brocades, and Miss Norton lent me lace and feathers; so I dressed up as Mrs. Malaprop, and sailed in with a mask on. No one knew me, for I disguised my voice, and no one dreamed of the silent, haughty Miss March (for they think I am very stiff and cool, most of them; and so I am to whipper-snappers) could dance and dress, and burst out into a 'nice derangement of epitaphs, like an allegory on the banks of the Nile.' I enjoyed it very much; and when we unmasked, it was fun to see them stare at me. I heard one of the young men tell another that he knew I'd been an actress; in fact, he thought he remembered seeing me at one of the minor theatres. Meg will relish that joke. Mr. Bhaer was Nick Bottom, and Tina was Titania,--a perfect little fairy in his arms. To see them dance was 'quite a landscape,' to use a Teddyism.\n\n\"I had a very happy New Year, after all; and when I thought it over in my room, I felt as if I was getting on a little in spite of my many failures; for I'm cheerful all the time now, work with a will, and take more interest in other people than I used to, which is satisfactory. Bless you all! Ever your loving\n\nThough very happy in the social atmosphere about her, and very busy with the daily work that earned her bread, and made it sweeter for the effort, Jo still found time for literary labors. The purpose which now took possession of her was a natural one to a poor and ambitious girl; but the means she took to gain her end were not the best. She saw that money conferred power: money and power, therefore, she resolved to have; not to be used for herself alone, but for those whom she loved more than self.\n\nThe dream of filling home with comforts, giving Beth everything she wanted, from strawberries in winter to an organ in her bedroom; going abroad herself, and always having _more_ than enough, so that she might indulge in the luxury of charity, had been for years Jo's most cherished castle in the air.\n\nThe prize-story experience had seemed to open a way which might, after long travelling and much up-hill work lead to this delightful _château en Espagne_. But the novel disaster quenched her courage for a time, for public opinion is a giant which has frightened stouter-hearted Jacks on bigger bean-stalks than hers. Like that immortal hero, she reposed awhile after the first attempt, which resulted in a tumble, and the least lovely of the giant's treasures, if I remember rightly. But the \"up again and take another\" spirit was as strong in Jo as in Jack; so she scrambled up, on the shady side this time, and got more booty, but nearly left behind her what was far more precious than the money-bags.\n\nShe took to writing sensation stories; for in those dark ages, even all-perfect America read rubbish. She told no one, but concocted a \"thrilling tale,\" and boldly carried it herself to Mr. Dashwood, editor of the \"Weekly Volcano.\" She had never read \"Sartor Resartus,\" but she had a womanly instinct that clothes possess an influence more powerful over many than the worth of character or the magic of manners. So she dressed herself in her best, and, trying to persuade herself that she was neither excited nor nervous, bravely climbed two pairs of dark and dirty stairs to find herself in a disorderly room, a cloud of cigar-smoke, and the presence of three gentlemen, sitting with their heels rather higher than their hats, which articles of dress none of them took the trouble to remove on her appearance. Somewhat daunted by this reception, Jo hesitated on the threshold, murmuring in much embarrassment,--\n\n\"Excuse me, I was looking for the 'Weekly Volcano' office; I wished to see Mr. Dashwood.\"\n\nDown went the highest pair of heels, up rose the smokiest gentleman, and, carefully cherishing his cigar between his fingers, he advanced, with a nod, and a countenance expressive of nothing but sleep. Feeling that she must get through the matter somehow, Jo produced her manuscript, and, blushing redder and redder with each sentence, blundered out fragments of the little speech carefully prepared for the occasion.\n\n\"A friend of mine desired me to offer--a story--just as an experiment--would like your opinion--be glad to write more if this suits.\"\n\nWhile she blushed and blundered, Mr. Dashwood had taken the manuscript, and was turning over the leaves with a pair of rather dirty fingers, and casting critical glances up and down the neat pages.\n\n\"Not a first attempt, I take it?\" observing that the pages were numbered, covered only on one side, and not tied up with a ribbon,--sure sign of a novice.\n\n\"No, sir; she has had some experience, and got a prize for a tale in the 'Blarneystone Banner.'\"\n\n\"Oh, did she?\" and Mr. Dashwood gave Jo a quick look, which seemed to take note of everything she had on, from the bow in her bonnet to the buttons on her boots. \"Well, you can leave it, if you like. We've more of this sort of thing on hand than we know what to do with at present; but I'll run my eye over it, and give you an answer next week.\"\n\nNow, Jo did _not_ like to leave it, for Mr. Dashwood didn't suit her at all; but, under the circumstances, there was nothing for her to do but bow and walk away, looking particularly tall and dignified, as she was apt to do when nettled or abashed. Just then she was both; for it was perfectly evident, from the knowing glances exchanged among the gentlemen, that her little fiction of \"my friend\" was considered a good joke; and a laugh, produced by some inaudible remark of the editor, as he closed the door, completed her discomfiture. Half resolving never to return, she went home, and worked off her irritation by stitching pinafores vigorously; and in an hour or two was cool enough to laugh over the scene, and long for next week.\n\nWhen she went again, Mr. Dashwood was alone, whereat she rejoiced; Mr. Dashwood was much wider awake than before, which was agreeable; and Mr. Dashwood was not too deeply absorbed in a cigar to remember his manners: so the second interview was much more comfortable than the first.\n\n\"We'll take this\" (editors never say I), \"if you don't object to a few alterations. It's too long, but omitting the passages I've marked will make it just the right length,\" he said, in a business-like tone.\n\nJo hardly knew her own MS. again, so crumpled and underscored were its pages and paragraphs; but, feeling as a tender parent might on being asked to cut off her baby's legs in order that it might fit into a new cradle, she looked at the marked passages, and was surprised to find that all the moral reflections--which she had carefully put in as ballast for much romance--had been stricken out.\n\n\"But, sir, I thought every story should have some sort of a moral, so I took care to have a few of my sinners repent.\"\n\nMr. Dashwood's editorial gravity relaxed into a smile, for Jo had forgotten her \"friend,\" and spoken as only an author could.\n\n\"People want to be amused, not preached at, you know. Morals don't sell nowadays;\" which was not quite a correct statement, by the way.\n\n\"You think it would do with these alterations, then?\"\n\n\"Yes; it's a new plot, and pretty well worked up--language good, and so on,\" was Mr. Dashwood's affable reply.\n\n\"What do you--that is, what compensation--\" began Jo, not exactly knowing how to express herself.\n\n\"Oh, yes, well, we give from twenty-five to thirty for things of this sort. Pay when it comes out,\" returned Mr. Dashwood, as if that point had escaped him; such trifles often do escape the editorial mind, it is said.\n\n\"Very well; you can have it,\" said Jo, handing back the story, with a satisfied air; for, after the dollar-a-column work, even twenty-five seemed good pay.\n\n\"Shall I tell my friend you will take another if she has one better than this?\" asked Jo, unconscious of her little slip of the tongue, and emboldened by her success.\n\n\"Well, we'll look at it; can't promise to take it. Tell her to make it short and spicy, and never mind the moral. What name would your friend like to put to it?\" in a careless tone.\n\n\"None at all, if you please; she doesn't wish her name to appear, and has no _nom de plume_,\" said Jo, blushing in spite of herself.\n\n\"Just as she likes, of course. The tale will be out next week; will you call for the money, or shall I send it?\" asked Mr. Dashwood, who felt a natural desire to know who his new contributor might be.\n\n\"I'll call. Good morning, sir.\"\n\nAs she departed, Mr. Dashwood put up his feet, with the graceful remark, \"Poor and proud, as usual, but she'll do.\"\n\nFollowing Mr. Dashwood's directions, and making Mrs. Northbury her model, Jo rashly took a plunge into the frothy sea of sensational literature; but, thanks to the life-preserver thrown her by a friend, she came up again, not much the worse for her ducking.\n\nLike most young scribblers, she went abroad for her characters and scenery; and banditti, counts, gypsies, nuns, and duchesses appeared upon her stage, and played their parts with as much accuracy and spirit as could be expected. Her readers were not particular about such trifles as grammar, punctuation, and probability, and Mr. Dashwood graciously permitted her to fill his columns at the lowest prices, not thinking it necessary to tell her that the real cause of his hospitality was the fact that one of his hacks, on being offered higher wages, had basely left him in the lurch.\n\nShe soon became interested in her work, for her emaciated purse grew stout, and the little hoard she was making to take Beth to the mountains next summer grew slowly but surely as the weeks passed. One thing disturbed her satisfaction, and that was that she did not tell them at home. She had a feeling that father and mother would not approve, and preferred to have her own way first, and beg pardon afterward. It was easy to keep her secret, for no name appeared with her stories; Mr. Dashwood had, of course, found it out very soon, but promised to be dumb; and, for a wonder, kept his word.\n\nShe thought it would do her no harm, for she sincerely meant to write nothing of which she should be ashamed, and quieted all pricks of conscience by anticipations of the happy minute when she should show her earnings and laugh over her well-kept secret.\n\nBut Mr. Dashwood rejected any but thrilling tales; and, as thrills could not be produced except by harrowing up the souls of the readers, history and romance, land and sea, science and art, police records and lunatic asylums, had to be ransacked for the purpose. Jo soon found that her innocent experience had given her but few glimpses of the tragic world which underlies society; so, regarding it in a business light, she set about supplying her deficiencies with characteristic energy. Eager to find material for stories, and bent on making them original in plot, if not masterly in execution, she searched newspapers for accidents, incidents, and crimes; she excited the suspicions of public librarians by asking for works on poisons; she studied faces in the street, and characters, good, bad, and indifferent, all about her; she delved in the dust of ancient times for facts or fictions so old that they were as good as new, and introduced herself to folly, sin, and misery, as well as her limited opportunities allowed. She thought she was prospering finely; but, unconsciously, she was beginning to desecrate some of the womanliest attributes of a woman's character. She was living in bad society; and, imaginary though it was, its influence affected her, for she was feeding heart and fancy on dangerous and unsubstantial food, and was fast brushing the innocent bloom from her nature by a premature acquaintance with the darker side of life, which comes soon enough to all of us.\n\nShe was beginning to feel rather than see this, for much describing of other people's passions and feelings set her to studying and speculating about her own,--a morbid amusement, in which healthy young minds do not voluntarily indulge. Wrong-doing always brings its own punishment; and, when Jo most needed hers, she got it.\n\nI don't know whether the study of Shakespeare helped her to read character, or the natural instinct of a woman for what was honest, brave, and strong; but while endowing her imaginary heroes with every perfection under the sun, Jo was discovering a live hero, who interested her in spite of many human imperfections. Mr. Bhaer, in one of their conversations, had advised her to study simple, true, and lovely characters, wherever she found them, as good training for a writer. Jo took him at his word, for she coolly turned round and studied him,--a proceeding which would have much surprised him, had he known it, for the worthy Professor was very humble in his own conceit.\n\nWhy everybody liked him was what puzzled Jo, at first. He was neither rich nor great, young nor handsome; in no respect what is called fascinating, imposing, or brilliant; and yet he was as attractive as a genial fire, and people seemed to gather about him as naturally as about a warm hearth. He was poor, yet always appeared to be giving something away; a stranger, yet every one was his friend; no longer young, but as happy-hearted as a boy; plain and peculiar, yet his face looked beautiful to many, and his oddities were freely forgiven for his sake. Jo often watched him, trying to discover the charm, and, at last, decided that it was benevolence which worked the miracle. If he had any sorrow, \"it sat with its head under its wing,\" and he turned only his sunny side to the world. There were lines upon his forehead, but Time seemed to have touched him gently, remembering how kind he was to others. The pleasant curves about his mouth were the memorials of many friendly words and cheery laughs; his eyes were never cold or hard, and his big hand had a warm, strong grasp that was more expressive than words.\n\nHis very clothes seemed to partake of the hospitable nature of the wearer. They looked as if they were at ease, and liked to make him comfortable; his capacious waistcoat was suggestive of a large heart underneath; his rusty coat had a social air, and the baggy pockets plainly proved that little hands often went in empty and came out full; his very boots were benevolent, and his collars never stiff and raspy like other people's.\n\n\"That's it!\" said Jo to herself, when she at length discovered that genuine good-will towards one's fellow-men could beautify and dignify even a stout German teacher, who shovelled in his dinner, darned his own socks, and was burdened with the name of Bhaer.\n\nJo valued goodness highly, but she also possessed a most feminine respect for intellect, and a little discovery which she made about the Professor added much to her regard for him. He never spoke of himself, and no one ever knew that in his native city he had been a man much honored and esteemed for learning and integrity, till a countryman came to see him, and, in a conversation with Miss Norton, divulged the pleasing fact. From her Jo learned it, and liked it all the better because Mr. Bhaer had never told it. She felt proud to know that he was an honored Professor in Berlin, though only a poor language-master in America; and his homely, hard-working life was much beautified by the spice of romance which this discovery gave it.\n\nAnother and a better gift than intellect was shown her in a most unexpected manner. Miss Norton had the _entrée_ into literary society, which Jo would have had no chance of seeing but for her. The solitary woman felt an interest in the ambitious girl, and kindly conferred many favors of this sort both on Jo and the Professor. She took them with her, one night, to a select symposium, held in honor of several celebrities.\n\nJo went prepared to bow down and adore the mighty ones whom she had worshipped with youthful enthusiasm afar off. But her reverence for genius received a severe shock that night, and it took her some time to recover from the discovery that the great creatures were only men and women after all. Imagine her dismay, on stealing a glance of timid admiration at the poet whose lines suggested an ethereal being fed on \"spirit, fire, and dew,\" to behold him devouring his supper with an ardor which flushed his intellectual countenance. Turning as from a fallen idol, she made other discoveries which rapidly dispelled her romantic illusions. The great novelist vibrated between two decanters with the regularity of a pendulum; the famous divine flirted openly with one of the Madame de Staëls of the age, who looked daggers at another Corinne, who was amiably satirizing her, after out-manœuvring her in efforts to absorb the profound philosopher, who imbibed tea Johnsonianly and appeared to slumber, the loquacity of the lady rendering speech impossible. The scientific celebrities, forgetting their mollusks and glacial periods, gossiped about art, while devoting themselves to oysters and ices with characteristic energy; the young musician, who was charming the city like a second Orpheus, talked horses; and the specimen of the British nobility present happened to be the most ordinary man of the party.\n\nBefore the evening was half over, Jo felt so completely _désillusionée_, that she sat down in a corner to recover herself. Mr. Bhaer soon joined her, looking rather out of his element, and presently several of the philosophers, each mounted on his hobby, came ambling up to hold an intellectual tournament in the recess. The conversation was miles beyond Jo's comprehension, but she enjoyed it, though Kant and Hegel were unknown gods, the Subjective and Objective unintelligible terms; and the only thing \"evolved from her inner consciousness,\" was a bad headache after it was all over. It dawned upon her gradually that the world was being picked to pieces, and put together on new, and, according to the talkers, on infinitely better principles than before; that religion was in a fair way to be reasoned into nothingness, and intellect was to be the only God. Jo knew nothing about philosophy or metaphysics of any sort, but a curious excitement, half pleasurable, half painful, came over her, as she listened with a sense of being turned adrift into time and space, like a young balloon out on a holiday.\n\nShe looked round to see how the Professor liked it, and found him looking at her with the grimmest expression she had ever seen him wear. He shook his head, and beckoned her to come away; but she was fascinated, just then, by the freedom of Speculative Philosophy, and kept her seat, trying to find out what the wise gentlemen intended to rely upon after they had annihilated all the old beliefs.\n\nNow, Mr. Bhaer was a diffident man, and slow to offer his own opinions, not because they were unsettled, but too sincere and earnest to be lightly spoken. As he glanced from Jo to several other young people, attracted by the brilliancy of the philosophic pyrotechnics, he knit his brows, and longed to speak, fearing that some inflammable young soul would be led astray by the rockets, to find, when the display was over, that they had only an empty stick or a scorched hand.\n\nHe bore it as long as he could; but when he was appealed to for an opinion, he blazed up with honest indignation, and defended religion with all the eloquence of truth,--an eloquence which made his broken English musical, and his plain face beautiful. He had a hard fight, for the wise men argued well; but he didn't know when he was beaten, and stood to his colors like a man. Somehow, as he talked, the world got right again to Jo; the old beliefs, that had lasted so long, seemed better than the new; God was not a blind force, and immortality was not a pretty fable, but a blessed fact. She felt as if she had solid ground under her feet again; and when Mr. Bhaer paused, out-talked, but not one whit convinced, Jo wanted to clap her hands and thank him.\n\nShe did neither; but she remembered this scene, and gave the Professor her heartiest respect, for she knew it cost him an effort to speak out then and there, because his conscience would not let him be silent. She began to see that character is a better possession than money, rank, intellect, or beauty; and to feel that if greatness is what a wise man has defined it to be, \"truth, reverence, and good-will,\" then her friend Friedrich Bhaer was not only good, but great.\n\nThis belief strengthened daily. She valued his esteem, she coveted his respect, she wanted to be worthy of his friendship; and, just when the wish was sincerest, she came near losing everything. It all grew out of a cocked hat; for one evening the Professor came in to give Jo her lesson, with a paper soldier-cap on his head, which Tina had put there, and he had forgotten to take off.\n\n\"It's evident he doesn't look in his glass before coming down,\" thought Jo, with a smile, as he said \"Goot efening,\" and sat soberly down, quite unconscious of the ludicrous contrast between his subject and his head-gear, for he was going to read her the \"Death of Wallenstein.\"\n\nShe said nothing at first, for she liked to hear him laugh out his big, hearty laugh, when anything funny happened, so she left him to discover it for himself, and presently forgot all about it; for to hear a German read Schiller is rather an absorbing occupation. After the reading came the lesson, which was a lively one, for Jo was in a gay mood that night, and the cocked-hat kept her eyes dancing with merriment. The Professor didn't know what to make of her, and stopped at last, to ask, with an air of mild surprise that was irresistible,--\n\n\"Mees Marsch, for what do you laugh in your master's face? Haf you no respect for me, that you go on so bad?\"\n\n\"How can I be respectful, sir, when you forget to take your hat off?\" said Jo.\n\nLifting his hand to his head, the absent-minded Professor gravely felt and removed the little cocked-hat, looked at it a minute, and then threw back his head, and laughed like a merry bass-viol.\n\n\"Ah! I see him now; it is that imp Tina who makes me a fool with my cap. Well, it is nothing; but see you, if this lesson goes not well, you too shall wear him.\"\n\nBut the lesson did not go at all for a few minutes, because Mr. Bhaer caught sight of a picture on the hat, and, unfolding it, said, with an air of great disgust,--\n\n\"I wish these papers did not come in the house; they are not for children to see, nor young people to read. It is not well, and I haf no patience with those who make this harm.\"\n\nJo glanced at the sheet, and saw a pleasing illustration composed of a lunatic, a corpse, a villain, and a viper. She did not like it; but the impulse that made her turn it over was not one of displeasure, but fear, because, for a minute, she fancied the paper was the \"Volcano.\" It was not, however, and her panic subsided as she remembered that, even if it had been, and one of her own tales in it, there would have been no name to betray her. She had betrayed herself, however, by a look and a blush; for, though an absent man, the Professor saw a good deal more than people fancied. He knew that Jo wrote, and had met her down among the newspaper offices more than once; but as she never spoke of it, he asked no questions, in spite of a strong desire to see her work. Now it occurred to him that she was doing what she was ashamed to own, and it troubled him. He did not say to himself, \"It is none of my business; I've no right to say anything,\" as many people would have done; he only remembered that she was young and poor, a girl far away from mother's love and father's care; and he was moved to help her with an impulse as quick and natural as that which would prompt him to put out his hand to save a baby from a puddle. All this flashed through his mind in a minute, but not a trace of it appeared in his face; and by the time the paper was turned, and Jo's needle threaded, he was ready to say quite naturally, but very gravely,--\n\n\"Yes, you are right to put it from you. I do not like to think that good young girls should see such things. They are made pleasant to some, but I would more rather give my boys gunpowder to play with than this bad trash.\"\n\n\"All may not be bad, only silly, you know; and if there is a demand for it, I don't see any harm in supplying it. Many very respectable people make an honest living out of what are called sensation stories,\" said Jo, scratching gathers so energetically that a row of little slits followed her pin.\n\n\"There is a demand for whiskey, but I think you and I do not care to sell it. If the respectable people knew what harm they did, they would not feel that the living _was_ honest. They haf no right to put poison in the sugar-plum, and let the small ones eat it. No; they should think a little, and sweep mud in the street before they do this thing.\"\n\nMr. Bhaer spoke warmly, and walked to the fire, crumpling the paper in his hands. Jo sat still, looking as if the fire had come to her; for her cheeks burned long after the cocked hat had turned to smoke, and gone harmlessly up the chimney.\n\n\"I should like much to send all the rest after him,\" muttered the Professor, coming back with a relieved air.\n\nJo thought what a blaze her pile of papers upstairs would make, and her hard-earned money lay rather heavily on her conscience at that minute. Then she thought consolingly to herself, \"Mine are not like that; they are only silly, never bad, so I won't be worried;\" and taking up her book, she said, with a studious face,--\n\n\"Shall we go on, sir? I'll be very good and proper now.\"\n\n\"I shall hope so,\" was all he said, but he meant more than she imagined; and the grave, kind look he gave her made her feel as if the words \"Weekly Volcano\" were printed in large type on her forehead.\n\nAs soon as she went to her room, she got out her papers, and carefully re-read every one of her stories. Being a little short-sighted, Mr. Bhaer sometimes used eye-glasses, and Jo had tried them once, smiling to see how they magnified the fine print of her book; now she seemed to have got on the Professor's mental or moral spectacles also; for the faults of these poor stories glared at her dreadfully, and filled her with dismay.\n\n\"They _are_ trash, and will soon be worse than trash if I go on; for each is more sensational than the last. I've gone blindly on, hurting myself and other people, for the sake of money; I know it's so, for I can't read this stuff in sober earnest without being horribly ashamed of it; and what _should_ I do if they were seen at home, or Mr. Bhaer got hold of them?\"\n\nJo turned hot at the bare idea, and stuffed the whole bundle into her stove, nearly setting the chimney afire with the blaze.\n\n\"Yes, that's the best place for such inflammable nonsense; I'd better burn the house down, I suppose, than let other people blow themselves up with my gunpowder,\" she thought, as she watched the \"Demon of the Jura\" whisk away, a little black cinder with fiery eyes.\n\nBut when nothing remained of all her three months' work except a heap of ashes, and the money in her lap, Jo looked sober, as she sat on the floor, wondering what she ought to do about her wages.\n\n\"I think I haven't done much harm _yet_, and may keep this to pay for my time,\" she said, after a long meditation, adding impatiently, \"I almost wish I hadn't any conscience, it's so inconvenient. If I didn't care about doing right, and didn't feel uncomfortable when doing wrong, I should get on capitally. I can't help wishing sometimes, that father and mother hadn't been so particular about such things.\"\n\nAh, Jo, instead of wishing that, thank God that \"father and mother _were_ particular,\" and pity from your heart those who have no such guardians to hedge them round with principles which may seem like prison-walls to impatient youth, but which will prove sure foundations to build character upon in womanhood.\n\nJo wrote no more sensational stories, deciding that the money did not pay for her share of the sensation; but, going to the other extreme, as is the way with people of her stamp, she took a course of Mrs. Sherwood, Miss Edgeworth, and Hannah More; and then produced a tale which might have been more properly called an essay or a sermon, so intensely moral was it. She had her doubts about it from the beginning; for her lively fancy and girlish romance felt as ill at ease in the new style as she would have done masquerading in the stiff and cumbrous costume of the last century. She sent this didactic gem to several markets, but it found no purchaser; and she was inclined to agree with Mr. Dashwood, that morals didn't sell.\n\nThen she tried a child's story, which she could easily have disposed of if she had not been mercenary enough to demand filthy lucre for it. The only person who offered enough to make it worth her while to try juvenile literature was a worthy gentleman who felt it his mission to convert all the world to his particular belief. But much as she liked to write for children, Jo could not consent to depict all her naughty boys as being eaten by bears or tossed by mad bulls, because they did not go to a particular Sabbath-school, nor all the good infants, who did go, as rewarded by every kind of bliss, from gilded gingerbread to escorts of angels, when they departed this life with psalms or sermons on their lisping tongues. So nothing came of these trials; and Jo corked up her inkstand, and said, in a fit of very wholesome humility,--\n\n\"I don't know anything; I'll wait till I do before I try again, and, meantime, 'sweep mud in the street,' if I can't do better; that's honest, at least;\" which decision proved that her second tumble down the bean-stalk had done her some good.\n\nWhile these internal revolutions were going on, her external life had been as busy and uneventful as usual; and if she sometimes looked serious or a little sad no one observed it but Professor Bhaer. He did it so quietly that Jo never knew he was watching to see if she would accept and profit by his reproof; but she stood the test, and he was satisfied; for, though no words passed between them, he knew that she had given up writing. Not only did he guess it by the fact that the second finger of her right hand was no longer inky, but she spent her evenings downstairs now, was met no more among newspaper offices, and studied with a dogged patience, which assured him that she was bent on occupying her mind with something useful, if not pleasant.\n\nHe helped her in many ways, proving himself a true friend, and Jo was happy; for, while her pen lay idle, she was learning other lessons beside German, and laying a foundation for the sensation story of her own life.\n\nIt was a pleasant winter and a long one, for she did not leave Mrs. Kirke till June. Every one seemed sorry when the time came; the children were inconsolable, and Mr. Bhaer's hair stuck straight up all over his head, for he always rumpled it wildly when disturbed in mind.\n\n\"Going home? Ah, you are happy that you haf a home to go in,\" he said, when she told him, and sat silently pulling his beard, in the corner, while she held a little levee on that last evening.\n\nShe was going early, so she bade them all good-by over night; and when his turn came, she said warmly,--\n\n\"Now, sir, you won't forget to come and see us, if you ever travel our way, will you? I'll never forgive you if you do, for I want them all to know my friend.\"\n\n\"Do you? Shall I come?\" he asked, looking down at her with an eager expression which she did not see.\n\n\"Yes, come next month; Laurie graduates then, and you'd enjoy Commencement as something new.\"\n\n\"That is your best friend, of whom you speak?\" he said, in an altered tone.\n\n\"Yes, my boy Teddy; I'm very proud of him, and should like you to see him.\"\n\nJo looked up then, quite unconscious of anything but her own pleasure in the prospect of showing them to one another. Something in Mr. Bhaer's face suddenly recalled the fact that she might find Laurie more than a \"best friend,\" and, simply because she particularly wished not to look as if anything was the matter, she involuntarily began to blush; and the more she tried not to, the redder she grew. If it had not been for Tina on her knee, she didn't know what would have become of her. Fortunately, the child was moved to hug her; so she managed to hide her face an instant, hoping the Professor did not see it. But he did, and his own changed again from that momentary anxiety to its usual expression, as he said cordially,--\n\n\"I fear I shall not make the time for that, but I wish the friend much success, and you all happiness. Gott bless you!\" and with that, he shook hands warmly, shouldered Tina, and went away.\n\nBut after the boys were abed, he sat long before his fire, with the tired look on his face, and the \"_heimweh_,\" or homesickness, lying heavy at his heart. Once, when he remembered Jo, as she sat with the little child in her lap and that new softness in her face, he leaned his head on his hands a minute, and then roamed about the room, as if in search of something that he could not find.\n\n\"It is not for me; I must not hope it now,\" he said to himself, with a sigh that was almost a groan; then, as if reproaching himself for the longing that he could not repress, he went and kissed the two towzled heads upon the pillow, took down his seldom-used meerschaum, and opened his Plato.\n\nHe did his best, and did it manfully; but I don't think he found that a pair of rampant boys, a pipe, or even the divine Plato, were very satisfactory substitutes for wife and child and home.\n\nEarly as it was, he was at the station, next morning, to see Jo off; and, thanks to him, she began her solitary journey with the pleasant memory of a familiar face smiling its farewell, a bunch of violets to keep her company, and, best of all, the happy thought,--\n\n\"Well, the winter's gone, and I've written no books, earned no fortune; but I've made a friend worth having, and I'll try to keep him all my life.\"\n\nWhatever his motive might have been, Laurie studied to some purpose that year, for he graduated with honor, and gave the Latin oration with the grace of a Phillips and the eloquence of a Demosthenes, so his friends said. They were all there, his grandfather,--oh, so proud!--Mr. and Mrs. March, John and Meg, Jo and Beth, and all exulted over him with the sincere admiration which boys make light of at the time, but fail to win from the world by any after-triumphs.\n\n\"I've got to stay for this confounded supper, but I shall be home early to-morrow; you'll come and meet me as usual, girls?\" Laurie said, as he put the sisters into the carriage after the joys of the day were over. He said \"girls,\" but he meant Jo, for she was the only one who kept up the old custom; she had not the heart to refuse her splendid, successful boy anything, and answered warmly,--\n\n\"I'll come, Teddy, rain or shine, and march before you, playing '_Hail the conquering hero comes_,' on a jews-harp.\"\n\nLaurie thanked her with a look that made her think, in a sudden panic, \"Oh, deary me! I know he'll say something, and then what shall I do?\"\n\nEvening meditation and morning work somewhat allayed her fears, and having decided that she wouldn't be vain enough to think people were going to propose when she had given them every reason to know what her answer would be, she set forth at the appointed time, hoping Teddy wouldn't do anything to make her hurt his poor little feelings. A call at Meg's, and a refreshing sniff and sip at the Daisy and Demijohn, still further fortified her for the _tête-à-tête_, but when she saw a stalwart figure looming in the distance, she had a strong desire to turn about and run away.\n\n\"Where's the jews-harp, Jo?\" cried Laurie, as soon as he was within speaking distance.\n\n\"I forgot it;\" and Jo took heart again, for that salutation could not be called lover-like.\n\nShe always used to take his arm on these occasions; now she did not, and he made no complaint, which was a bad sign, but talked on rapidly about all sorts of far-away subjects, till they turned from the road into the little path that led homeward through the grove. Then he walked more slowly, suddenly lost his fine flow of language, and, now and then, a dreadful pause occurred. To rescue the conversation from one of the wells of silence into which it kept falling, Jo said hastily,--\n\n\"Now you must have a good long holiday!\"\n\n\"I intend to.\"\n\nSomething in his resolute tone made Jo look up quickly to find him looking down at her with an expression that assured her the dreaded moment had come, and made her put out her hand with an imploring,--\n\n\"No, Teddy, please don't!\"\n\n\"I will, and you _must_ hear me. It's no use, Jo; we've got to have it out, and the sooner the better for both of us,\" he answered, getting flushed and excited all at once.\n\n\"Say what you like, then; I'll listen,\" said Jo, with a desperate sort of patience.\n\nLaurie was a young lover, but he was in earnest, and meant to \"have it out,\" if he died in the attempt; so he plunged into the subject with characteristic impetuosity, saying in a voice that _would_ get choky now and then, in spite of manful efforts to keep it steady,--\n\n\"I've loved you ever since I've known you, Jo; couldn't help it, you've been so good to me. I've tried to show it, but you wouldn't let me; now I'm going to make you hear, and give me an answer, for I _can't_ go on so any longer.\"\n\n\"I wanted to save you this; I thought you'd understand--\" began Jo, finding it a great deal harder than she expected.\n\n\"I know you did; but girls are so queer you never know what they mean. They say No when they mean Yes, and drive a man out of his wits just for the fun of it,\" returned Laurie, entrenching himself behind an undeniable fact.\n\n\"_I_ don't. I never wanted to make you care for me so, and I went away to keep you from it if I could.\"\n\n\"I thought so; it was like you, but it was no use. I only loved you all the more, and I worked hard to please you, and I gave up billiards and everything you didn't like, and waited and never complained, for I hoped you'd love me, though I'm not half good enough--\" here there was a choke that couldn't be controlled, so he decapitated buttercups while he cleared his \"confounded throat.\"\n\n\"Yes, you are; you're a great deal too good for me, and I'm so grateful to you, and so proud and fond of you, I don't see why I can't love you as you want me to. I've tried, but I can't change the feeling, and it would be a lie to say I do when I don't.\"\n\n\"Really, truly, Jo?\"\n\nHe stopped short, and caught both her hands as he put his question with a look that she did not soon forget.\n\n\"Really, truly, dear.\"\n\nThey were in the grove now, close by the stile; and when the last words fell reluctantly from Jo's lips, Laurie dropped her hands and turned as if to go on, but for once in his life that fence was too much for him; so he just laid his head down on the mossy post, and stood so still that Jo was frightened.\n\n\"O Teddy, I'm so sorry, so desperately sorry, I could kill myself if it would do any good! I wish you wouldn't take it so hard. I can't help it; you know it's impossible for people to make themselves love other people if they don't,\" cried Jo inelegantly but remorsefully, as she softly patted his shoulder, remembering the time when he had comforted her so long ago.\n\n\"They do sometimes,\" said a muffled voice from the post.\n\n\"I don't believe it's the right sort of love, and I'd rather not try it,\" was the decided answer.\n\nThere was a long pause, while a blackbird sung blithely on the willow by the river, and the tall grass rustled in the wind. Presently Jo said very soberly, as she sat down on the step of the stile,--\n\n\"Laurie, I want to tell you something.\"\n\nHe started as if he had been shot, threw up his head, and cried out, in a fierce tone--\n\n\"_Don't_ tell me that, Jo; I can't bear it now!\"\n\n\"Tell what?\" she asked, wondering at his violence.\n\n\"That you love that old man.\"\n\n\"What old man?\" demanded Jo, thinking he must mean his grandfather.\n\n\"That devilish Professor you were always writing about. If you say you love him, I know I shall do something desperate;\" and he looked as if he would keep his word, as he clenched his hands, with a wrathful spark in his eyes.\n\nJo wanted to laugh, but restrained herself, and said warmly, for she, too, was getting excited with all this,--\n\n\"Don't swear, Teddy! He isn't old, nor anything bad, but good and kind, and the best friend I've got, next to you. Pray, don't fly into a passion; I want to be kind, but I know I shall get angry if you abuse my Professor. I haven't the least idea of loving him or anybody else.\"\n\n\"But you will after a while, and then what will become of me?\"\n\n\"You'll love some one else too, like a sensible boy, and forget all this trouble.\"\n\n\"I _can't_ love any one else; and I'll never forget you, Jo, never! never!\" with a stamp to emphasize his passionate words.\n\n\"What _shall_ I do with him?\" sighed Jo, finding that emotions were more unmanageable than she expected. \"You haven't heard what I wanted to tell you. Sit down and listen; for indeed I want to do right and make you happy,\" she said, hoping to soothe him with a little reason, which proved that she knew nothing about love.\n\nSeeing a ray of hope in that last speech, Laurie threw himself down on the grass at her feet, leaned his arm on the lower step of the stile, and looked up at her with an expectant face. Now that arrangement was not conducive to calm speech or clear thought on Jo's part; for how _could_ she say hard things to her boy while he watched her with eyes full of love and longing, and lashes still wet with the bitter drop or two her hardness of heart had wrung from him? She gently turned his head away, saying, as she stroked the wavy hair which had been allowed to grow for her sake,--how touching that was, to be sure!--\n\n\"I agree with mother that you and I are not suited to each other, because our quick tempers and strong wills would probably make us very miserable, if we were so foolish as to--\" Jo paused a little over the last word, but Laurie uttered it with a rapturous expression,--\n\n\"Marry,--no, we shouldn't! If you loved me, Jo, I should be a perfect saint, for you could make me anything you like.\"\n\n\"No, I can't. I've tried it and failed, and I won't risk our happiness by such a serious experiment. We don't agree and we never shall; so we'll be good friends all our lives, but we won't go and do anything rash.\"\n\n\"Yes, we will if we get the chance,\" muttered Laurie rebelliously.\n\n\"Now do be reasonable, and take a sensible view of the case,\" implored Jo, almost at her wit's end.\n\n\"I won't be reasonable; I don't want to take what you call 'a sensible view;' it won't help me, and it only makes you harder. I don't believe you've got any heart.\"\n\n\"I wish I hadn't!\"\n\nThere was a little quiver in Jo's voice, and, thinking it a good omen, Laurie turned round, bringing all his persuasive powers to bear as he said, in the wheedlesome tone that had never been so dangerously wheedlesome before,--\n\n\"Don't disappoint us, dear! Every one expects it. Grandpa has set his heart upon it, your people like it, and I can't get on without you. Say you will, and let's be happy. Do, do!\"\n\nNot until months afterward did Jo understand how she had the strength of mind to hold fast to the resolution she had made when she decided that she did not love her boy, and never could. It was very hard to do, but she did it, knowing that delay was both useless and cruel.\n\n\"I can't say 'Yes' truly, so I won't say it at all. You'll see that I'm right, by and by, and thank me for it\"--she began solemnly.\n\n\"I'll be hanged if I do!\" and Laurie bounced up off the grass, burning with indignation at the bare idea.\n\n\"Yes, you will!\" persisted Jo; \"you'll get over this after a while, and find some lovely, accomplished girl, who will adore you, and make a fine mistress for your fine house. I shouldn't. I'm homely and awkward and odd and old, and you'd be ashamed of me, and we should quarrel,--we can't help it even now, you see,--and I shouldn't like elegant society and you would, and you'd hate my scribbling, and I couldn't get on without it, and we should be unhappy, and wish we hadn't done it, and everything would be horrid!\"\n\n\"Anything more?\" asked Laurie, finding it hard to listen patiently to this prophetic burst.\n\n\"Nothing more, except that I don't believe I shall ever marry. I'm happy as I am, and love my liberty too well to be in any hurry to give it up for any mortal man.\"\n\n\"I know better!\" broke in Laurie. \"You think so now; but there'll come a time when you _will_ care for somebody, and you'll love him tremendously, and live and die for him. I know you will, it's your way, and I shall have to stand by and see it;\" and the despairing lover cast his hat upon the ground with a gesture that would have seemed comical, if his face had not been so tragical.\n\n\"Yes, I _will_ live and die for him, if he ever comes and makes me love him in spite of myself, and you must do the best you can!\" cried Jo, losing patience with poor Teddy. \"I've done my best, but you _won't_ be reasonable, and it's selfish of you to keep teasing for what I can't give. I shall always be fond of you, very fond indeed, as a friend, but I'll never marry you; and the sooner you believe it, the better for both of us,--so now!\"\n\nThat speech was like fire to gunpowder. Laurie looked at her a minute as if he did not quite know what to do with himself, then turned sharply away, saying, in a desperate sort of tone,--\n\n\"You'll be sorry some day, Jo.\"\n\n\"Oh, where are you going?\" she cried, for his face frightened her.\n\n\"To the devil!\" was the consoling answer.\n\nFor a minute Jo's heart stood still, as he swung himself down the bank, toward the river; but it takes much folly, sin, or misery to send a young man to a violent death, and Laurie was not one of the weak sort who are conquered by a single failure. He had no thought of a melodramatic plunge, but some blind instinct led him to fling hat and coat into his boat, and row away with all his might, making better time up the river than he had done in many a race. Jo drew a long breath and unclasped her hands as she watched the poor fellow trying to outstrip the trouble which he carried in his heart.\n\n\"That will do him good, and he'll come home in such a tender, penitent state of mind, that I sha'n't dare to see him,\" she said; adding, as she went slowly home, feeling as if she had murdered some innocent thing, and buried it under the leaves,--\n\n\"Now I must go and prepare Mr. Laurence to be very kind to my poor boy. I wish he'd love Beth; perhaps he may, in time, but I begin to think I was mistaken about her. Oh dear! how can girls like to have lovers and refuse them. I think it's dreadful.\"\n\nBeing sure that no one could do it so well as herself, she went straight to Mr. Laurence, told the hard story bravely through, and then broke down, crying so dismally over her own insensibility that the kind old gentleman, though sorely disappointed, did not utter a reproach. He found it difficult to understand how any girl could help loving Laurie, and hoped she would change her mind, but he knew even better than Jo that love cannot be forced, so he shook his head sadly, and resolved to carry his boy out of harm's way; for Young Impetuosity's parting words to Jo disturbed him more than he would confess.\n\nWhen Laurie came home, dead tired, but quite composed, his grandfather met him as if he knew nothing, and kept up the delusion very successfully for an hour or two. But when they sat together in the twilight, the time they used to enjoy so much, it was hard work for the old man to ramble on as usual, and harder still for the young one to listen to praises of the last year's success, which to him now seemed love's labor lost. He bore it as long as he could, then went to his piano, and began to play. The windows were open; and Jo, walking in the garden with Beth, for once understood music better than her sister, for he played the \"Sonata Pathétique,\" and played it as he never did before.\n\n\"That's very fine, I dare say, but it's sad enough to make one cry; give us something gayer, lad,\" said Mr. Laurence, whose kind old heart was full of sympathy, which he longed to show, but knew not how.\n\nLaurie dashed into a livelier strain, played stormily for several minutes, and would have got through bravely, if, in a momentary lull, Mrs. March's voice had not been heard calling,--\n\n\"Jo, dear, come in; I want you.\"\n\nJust what Laurie longed to say, with a different meaning! As he listened, he lost his place; the music ended with a broken chord, and the musician sat silent in the dark.\n\n\"I can't stand this,\" muttered the old gentleman. Up he got, groped his way to the piano, laid a kind hand on either of the broad shoulders, and said, as gently as a woman,--\n\n\"I know, my boy, I know.\"\n\nNo answer for an instant; then Laurie asked sharply,--\n\n\"Who told you?\"\n\n\"Jo herself.\"\n\n\"Then there's an end of it!\" and he shook off his grandfather's hands with an impatient motion; for, though grateful for the sympathy, his man's pride could not bear a man's pity.\n\n\"Not quite; I want to say one thing, and then there shall be an end of it,\" returned Mr. Laurence, with unusual mildness. \"You won't care to stay at home just now, perhaps?\"\n\n\"I don't intend to run away from a girl. Jo can't prevent my seeing her, and I shall stay and do it as long as I like,\" interrupted Laurie, in a defiant tone.\n\n\"Not if you are the gentleman I think you. I'm disappointed, but the girl can't help it; and the only thing left for you to do is to go away for a time. Where will you go?\"\n\n\"Anywhere. I don't care what becomes of me;\" and Laurie got up, with a reckless laugh, that grated on his grandfather's ear.\n\n\"Take it like a man, and don't do anything rash, for God's sake. Why not go abroad, as you planned, and forget it?\"\n\n\"I can't.\"\n\"But you've been wild to go, and I promised you should when you got through college.\"\n\n\"Ah, but I didn't mean to go alone!\" and Laurie walked fast through the room, with an expression which it was well his grandfather did not see.\n\n\"I don't ask you to go alone; there's some one ready and glad to go with you, anywhere in the world.\"\n\n\"Who, sir?\" stopping to listen.\n\"Myself.\"\n\nLaurie came back as quickly as he went, and put out his hand, saying huskily,--\n\n\"I'm a selfish brute; but--you know--grandfather--\"\n\n\"Lord help me, yes, I do know, for I've been through it all before, once in my own young days, and then with your father. Now, my dear boy, just sit quietly down, and hear my plan. It's all settled, and can be carried out at once,\" said Mr. Laurence, keeping hold of the young man, as if fearful that he would break away, as his father had done before him.\n\n\"Well, sir, what is it?\" and Laurie sat down, without a sign of interest in face or voice.\n\n\"There is business in London that needs looking after; I meant you should attend to it; but I can do it better myself, and things here will get on very well with Brooke to manage them. My partners do almost everything; I'm merely holding on till you take my place, and can be off at any time.\"\n\n\"But you hate travelling, sir; I can't ask it of you at your age,\" began Laurie, who was grateful for the sacrifice, but much preferred to go alone, if he went at all.\n\nThe old gentleman knew that perfectly well, and particularly desired to prevent it; for the mood in which he found his grandson assured him that it would not be wise to leave him to his own devices. So, stifling a natural regret at the thought of the home comforts he would leave behind him, he said stoutly,--\n\n\"Bless your soul, I'm not superannuated yet. I quite enjoy the idea; it will do me good, and my old bones won't suffer, for travelling nowadays is almost as easy as sitting in a chair.\"\n\nA restless movement from Laurie suggested that _his_ chair was not easy, or that he did not like the plan, and made the old man add hastily,--\n\n\"I don't mean to be a marplot or a burden; I go because I think you'd feel happier than if I was left behind. I don't intend to gad about with you, but leave you free to go where you like, while I amuse myself in my own way. I've friends in London and Paris, and should like to visit them; meantime you can go to Italy, Germany, Switzerland, where you will, and enjoy pictures, music, scenery, and adventures to your heart's content.\"\n\nNow, Laurie felt just then that his heart was entirely broken, and the world a howling wilderness; but at the sound of certain words which the old gentleman artfully introduced into his closing sentence, the broken heart gave an unexpected leap, and a green oasis or two suddenly appeared in the howling wilderness. He sighed, and then said, in a spiritless tone,--\n\n\"Just as you like, sir; it doesn't matter where I go or what I do.\"\n\n\"It does to me, remember that, my lad; I give you entire liberty, but I trust you to make an honest use of it. Promise me that, Laurie.\"\n\n\"Anything you like, sir.\"\n\n\"Good,\" thought the old gentleman. \"You don't care now, but there'll come a time when that promise will keep you out of mischief, or I'm much mistaken.\"\n\nBeing an energetic individual, Mr. Laurence struck while the iron was hot; and before the blighted being recovered spirit enough to rebel, they were off. During the time necessary for preparation, Laurie bore himself as young gentlemen usually do in such cases. He was moody, irritable, and pensive by turns; lost his appetite, neglected his dress, and devoted much time to playing tempestuously on his piano; avoided Jo, but consoled himself by staring at her from his window, with a tragical face that haunted her dreams by night, and oppressed her with a heavy sense of guilt by day. Unlike some sufferers, he never spoke of his unrequited passion, and would allow no one, not even Mrs. March, to attempt consolation or offer sympathy. On some accounts, this was a relief to his friends; but the weeks before his departure were very uncomfortable, and every one rejoiced that the \"poor, dear fellow was going away to forget his trouble, and come home happy.\" Of course, he smiled darkly at their delusion, but passed it by, with the sad superiority of one who knew that his fidelity, like his love, was unalterable.\n\nWhen the parting came he affected high spirits, to conceal certain inconvenient emotions which seemed inclined to assert themselves. This gayety did not impose upon anybody, but they tried to look as if it did, for his sake, and he got on very well till Mrs. March kissed him, with a whisper full of motherly solicitude; then, feeling that he was going very fast, he hastily embraced them all round, not forgetting the afflicted Hannah, and ran downstairs as if for his life. Jo followed a minute after to wave her hand to him if he looked round. He did look round, came back, put his arms about her, as she stood on the step above him, and looked up at her with a face that made his short appeal both eloquent and pathetic.\n\n\"O Jo, can't you?\"\n\n\"Teddy, dear, I wish I could!\"\n\nThat was all, except a little pause; then Laurie straightened himself up, said \"It's all right, never mind,\" and went away without another word. Ah, but it wasn't all right, and Jo _did_ mind; for while the curly head lay on her arm a minute after her hard answer, she felt as if she had stabbed her dearest friend; and when he left her without a look behind him, she knew that the boy Laurie never would come again.\n\n# BETH'S SECRET.\n\nWhen Jo came home that spring, she had been struck with the change in Beth. No one spoke of it or seemed aware of it, for it had come too gradually to startle those who saw her daily; but to eyes sharpened by absence, it was very plain; and a heavy weight fell on Jo's heart as she saw her sister's face. It was no paler and but little thinner than in the autumn; yet there was a strange, transparent look about it, as if the mortal was being slowly refined away, and the immortal shining through the frail flesh with an indescribably pathetic beauty. Jo saw and felt it, but said nothing at the time, and soon the first impression lost much of its power; for Beth seemed happy, no one appeared to doubt that she was better; and, presently, in other cares, Jo for a time forgot her fear.\n\nBut when Laurie was gone, and peace prevailed again, the vague anxiety returned and haunted her. She had confessed her sins and been forgiven; but when she showed her savings and proposed the mountain trip, Beth had thanked her heartily, but begged not to go so far away from home. Another little visit to the seashore would suit her better, and, as grandma could not be prevailed upon to leave the babies, Jo took Beth down to the quiet place, where she could live much in the open air, and let the fresh sea-breezes blow a little color into her pale cheeks.\n\nIt was not a fashionable place, but, even among the pleasant people there, the girls made few friends, preferring to live for one another. Beth was too shy to enjoy society, and Jo too wrapped up in her to care for any one else; so they were all in all to each other, and came and went, quite unconscious of the interest they excited in those about them, who watched with sympathetic eyes the strong sister and the feeble one, always together, as if they felt instinctively that a long separation was not far away.\n\nThey did feel it, yet neither spoke of it; for often between ourselves and those nearest and dearest to us there exists a reserve which it is very hard to overcome. Jo felt as if a veil had fallen between her heart and Beth's; but when she put out her hand to lift it up, there seemed something sacred in the silence, and she waited for Beth to speak. She wondered, and was thankful also, that her parents did not seem to see what she saw; and, during the quiet weeks, when the shadow grew so plain to her, she said nothing of it to those at home, believing that it would tell itself when Beth came back no better. She wondered still more if her sister really guessed the hard truth, and what thoughts were passing through her mind during the long hours when she lay on the warm rocks, with her head in Jo's lap, while the winds blew healthfully over her, and the sea made music at her feet.\n\nOne day Beth told her. Jo thought she was asleep, she lay so still; and, putting down her book, sat looking at her with wistful eyes, trying to see signs of hope in the faint color on Beth's cheeks. But she could not find enough to satisfy her, for the cheeks were very thin, and the hands seemed too feeble to hold even the rosy little shells they had been gathering. It came to her then more bitterly than ever that Beth was slowly drifting away from her, and her arms instinctively tightened their hold upon the dearest treasure she possessed. For a minute her eyes were too dim for seeing, and, when they cleared, Beth was looking up at her so tenderly that there was hardly any need for her to say,--\n\n\"Jo, dear, I'm glad you know it. I've tried to tell you, but I couldn't.\"\n\nThere was no answer except her sister's cheek against her own, not even tears; for when most deeply moved, Jo did not cry. She was the weaker, then, and Beth tried to comfort and sustain her, with her arms about her, and the soothing words she whispered in her ear.\n\n\"I've known it for a good while, dear, and, now I'm used to it, it isn't hard to think of or to bear. Try to see it so, and don't be troubled about me, because it's best; indeed it is.\"\n\n\"Is this what made you so unhappy in the autumn, Beth? You did not feel it then, and keep it to yourself so long, did you?\" asked Jo, refusing to see or say that it _was_ best, but glad to know that Laurie had no part in Beth's trouble.\n\n\"Yes, I gave up hoping then, but I didn't like to own it. I tried to think it was a sick fancy, and would not let it trouble any one. But when I saw you all so well and strong, and full of happy plans, it was hard to feel that I could never be like you, and then I was miserable, Jo.\"\n\n\"O Beth, and you didn't tell me, didn't let me comfort and help you! How could you shut me out, and bear it all alone?\"\n\nJo's voice was full of tender reproach, and her heart ached to think of the solitary struggle that must have gone on while Beth learned to say good-by to health, love, and life, and take up her cross so cheerfully.\n\n\"Perhaps it was wrong, but I tried to do right; I wasn't sure, no one said anything, and I hoped I was mistaken. It would have been selfish to frighten you all when Marmee was so anxious about Meg, and Amy away, and you so happy with Laurie,--at least, I thought so then.\"\n\n\"And I thought that you loved him, Beth, and I went away because I couldn't,\" cried Jo, glad to say all the truth.\n\nBeth looked so amazed at the idea that Jo smiled in spite of her pain, and added softly,--\n\n\"Then you didn't, deary? I was afraid it was so, and imagined your poor little heart full of love-lornity all that while.\"\n\n\"Why, Jo, how could I, when he was so fond of you?\" asked Beth, as innocently as a child. \"I do love him dearly; he is so good to me, how can I help it? But he never could be anything to me but my brother. I hope he truly will be, sometime.\"\n\n\"Not through me,\" said Jo decidedly. \"Amy is left for him, and they would suit excellently; but I have no heart for such things, now. I don't care what becomes of anybody but you, Beth. You _must_ get well.\"\n\n\"I want to, oh, so much! I try, but every day I lose a little, and feel more sure that I shall never gain it back. It's like the tide, Jo, when it turns, it goes slowly, but it can't be stopped.\"\n\n\"It _shall_ be stopped, your tide must not turn so soon, nineteen is too young. Beth, I can't let you go. I'll work and pray and fight against it. I'll keep you in spite of everything; there must be ways, it can't be too late. God won't be so cruel as to take you from me,\" cried poor Jo rebelliously, for her spirit was far less piously submissive than Beth's.\n\nSimple, sincere people seldom speak much of their piety; it shows itself in acts, rather than in words, and has more influence than homilies or protestations. Beth could not reason upon or explain the faith that gave her courage and patience to give up life, and cheerfully wait for death. Like a confiding child, she asked no questions, but left everything to God and nature, Father and mother of us all, feeling sure that they, and they only, could teach and strengthen heart and spirit for this life and the life to come. She did not rebuke Jo with saintly speeches, only loved her better for her passionate affection, and clung more closely to the dear human love, from which our Father never means us to be weaned, but through which He draws us closer to Himself. She could not say, \"I'm glad to go,\" for life was very sweet to her; she could only sob out, \"I try to be willing,\" while she held fast to Jo, as the first bitter wave of this great sorrow broke over them together.\n\nBy and by Beth said, with recovered serenity,--\n\n\"You'll tell them this when we go home?\"\n\n\"I think they will see it without words,\" sighed Jo; for now it seemed to her that Beth changed every day.\n\n\"Perhaps not; I've heard that the people who love best are often blindest to such things. If they don't see it, you will tell them for me. I don't want any secrets, and it's kinder to prepare them. Meg has John and the babies to comfort her, but you must stand by father and mother, won't you, Jo?\"\n\n\"If I can; but, Beth, I don't give up yet; I'm going to believe that it _is_ a sick fancy, and not let you think it's true,\" said Jo, trying to speak cheerfully.\n\nBeth lay a minute thinking, and then said in her quiet way,--\n\n\"I don't know how to express myself, and shouldn't try, to any one but you, because I can't speak out, except to my Jo. I only mean to say that I have a feeling that it never was intended I should live long. I'm not like the rest of you; I never made any plans about what I'd do when I grew up; I never thought of being married, as you all did. I couldn't seem to imagine myself anything but stupid little Beth, trotting about at home, of no use anywhere but there. I never wanted to go away, and the hard part now is the leaving you all. I'm not afraid, but it seems as if I should be homesick for you even in heaven.\"\n\nJo could not speak; and for several minutes there was no sound but the sigh of the wind and the lapping of the tide. A white-winged gull flew by, with the flash of sunshine on its silvery breast; Beth watched it till it vanished, and her eyes were full of sadness. A little gray-coated sand-bird came tripping over the beach, \"peeping\" softly to itself, as if enjoying the sun and sea; it came quite close to Beth, looked at her with a friendly eye, and sat upon a warm stone, dressing its wet feathers, quite at home. Beth smiled, and felt comforted, for the tiny thing seemed to offer its small friendship, and remind her that a pleasant world was still to be enjoyed.\n\n\"Dear little bird! See, Jo, how tame it is. I like peeps better than the gulls: they are not so wild and handsome, but they seem happy, confiding little things. I used to call them my birds, last summer; and mother said they reminded her of me,--busy, quaker-colored creatures, always near the shore, and always chirping that contented little song of theirs. You are the gull, Jo, strong and wild, fond of the storm and the wind, flying far out to sea, and happy all alone. Meg is the turtle-dove, and Amy is like the lark she writes about, trying to get up among the clouds, but always dropping down into its nest again. Dear little girl! she's so ambitious, but her heart is good and tender; and no matter how high she flies, she never will forget home. I hope I shall see her again, but she seems _so_ far away.\"\n\n\"She is coming in the spring, and I mean that you shall be all ready to see and enjoy her. I'm going to have you well and rosy by that time,\" began Jo, feeling that of all the changes in Beth, the talking change was the greatest, for it seemed to cost no effort now, and she thought aloud in a way quite unlike bashful Beth.\n\n\"Jo, dear, don't hope any more; it won't do any good, I'm sure of that. We won't be miserable, but enjoy being together while we wait. We'll have happy times, for I don't suffer much, and I think the tide will go out easily, if you help me.\"\n\nJo leaned down to kiss the tranquil face; and with that silent kiss, she dedicated herself soul and body to Beth.\n\nShe was right: there was no need of any words when they got home, for father and mother saw plainly, now, what they had prayed to be saved from seeing. Tired with her short journey, Beth went at once to bed, saying how glad she was to be at home; and when Jo went down, she found that she would be spared the hard task of telling Beth's secret. Her father stood leaning his head on the mantel-piece, and did not turn as she came in; but her mother stretched out her arms as if for help, and Jo went to comfort her without a word.\n\n# NEW IMPRESSIONS.\n\nAt three o'clock in the afternoon, all the fashionable world at Nice may be seen on the Promenade des Anglais,--a charming place; for the wide walk, bordered with palms, flowers, and tropical shrubs, is bounded on one side by the sea, on the other by the grand drive, lined with hotels and villas, while beyond lie orange-orchards and the hills. Many nations are represented, many languages spoken, many costumes worn; and, on a sunny day, the spectacle is as gay and brilliant as a carnival. Haughty English, lively French, sober Germans, handsome Spaniards, ugly Russians, meek Jews, free-and-easy Americans, all drive, sit, or saunter here, chatting over the news, and criticising the latest celebrity who has arrived,--Ristori or Dickens, Victor Emmanuel or the Queen of the Sandwich Islands. The equipages are as varied as the company, and attract as much attention, especially the low basket-barouches in which ladies drive themselves, with a pair of dashing ponies, gay nets to keep their voluminous flounces from overflowing the diminutive vehicles, and little grooms on the perch behind.\n\nAlong this walk, on Christmas Day, a tall young man walked slowly, with his hands behind him, and a somewhat absent expression of countenance. He looked like an Italian, was dressed like an Englishman, and had the independent air of an American,--a combination which caused sundry pairs of feminine eyes to look approvingly after him, and sundry dandies in black velvet suits, with rose-colored neckties, buff gloves, and orange-flowers in their button-holes, to shrug their shoulders, and then envy him his inches. There were plenty of pretty faces to admire, but the young man took little notice of them, except to glance, now and then, at some blonde girl, or lady in blue. Presently he strolled out of the promenade, and stood a moment at the crossing, as if undecided whether to go and listen to the band in the Jardin Publique, or to wander along the beach toward Castle Hill. The quick trot of ponies' feet made him look up, as one of the little carriages, containing a single lady, came rapidly down the street. The lady was young, blonde, and dressed in blue. He stared a minute, then his whole face woke up, and, waving his hat like a boy, he hurried forward to meet her.\n\n\"O Laurie, is it really you? I thought you'd never come!\" cried Amy, dropping the reins, and holding out both hands, to the great scandalization of a French mamma, who hastened her daughter's steps, lest she should be demoralized by beholding the free manners of these \"mad English.\"\n\n\"I was detained by the way, but I promised to spend Christmas with you, and here I am.\"\n\n\"How is your grandfather? When did you come? Where are you staying?\"\n\n\"Very well--last night--at the Chauvain. I called at your hotel, but you were all out.\"\n\n\"I have so much to say, I don't know where to begin! Get in, and we can talk at our ease; I was going for a drive, and longing for company. Flo's saving up for to-night.\"\n\n\"What happens then, a ball?\"\n\n\"A Christmas party at our hotel. There are many Americans there, and they give it in honor of the day. You'll go with us, of course? Aunt will be charmed.\"\n\n\"Thank you. Where now?\" asked Laurie, leaning back and folding his arms, a proceeding which suited Amy, who preferred to drive; for her parasol-whip and blue reins over the white ponies' backs, afforded her infinite satisfaction.\n\n\"I'm going to the banker's first, for letters, and then to Castle Hill; the view is so lovely, and I like to feed the peacocks. Have you ever been there?\"\n\n\"Often, years ago; but I don't mind having a look at it.\"\n\n\"Now tell me all about yourself. The last I heard of you, your grandfather wrote that he expected you from Berlin.\"\n\n\"Yes, I spent a month there, and then joined him in Paris, where he has settled for the winter. He has friends there, and finds plenty to amuse him; so I go and come, and we get on capitally.\"\n\n\"That's a sociable arrangement,\" said Amy, missing something in Laurie's manner, though she couldn't tell what.\n\n\"Why, you see he hates to travel, and I hate to keep still; so we each suit ourselves, and there is no trouble. I am often with him, and he enjoys my adventures, while I like to feel that some one is glad to see me when I get back from my wanderings. Dirty old hole, isn't it?\" he added, with a look of disgust, as they drove along the boulevard to the Place Napoleon, in the old city.\n\n\"The dirt is picturesque, so I don't mind. The river and the hills are delicious, and these glimpses of the narrow cross-streets are my delight. Now we shall have to wait for that procession to pass; it's going to the Church of St. John.\"\n\nWhile Laurie listlessly watched the procession of priests under their canopies, white-veiled nuns bearing lighted tapers, and some brotherhood in blue, chanting as they walked, Amy watched him, and felt a new sort of shyness steal over her; for he was changed, and she could not find the merry-faced boy she left in the moody-looking man beside her. He was handsomer than ever, and greatly improved, she thought; but now that the flush of pleasure at meeting her was over, he looked tired and spiritless,--not sick, nor exactly unhappy, but older and graver than a year or two of prosperous life should have made him. She couldn't understand it, and did not venture to ask questions; so she shook her head, and touched up her ponies, as the procession wound away across the arches of the Paglioni bridge, and vanished in the church.\n\n\"_Que pensez vous_?\" she said, airing her French, which had improved in quantity, if not in quality, since she came abroad.\n\n\"That mademoiselle has made good use of her time, and the result is charming,\" replied Laurie, bowing, with his hand on his heart, and an admiring look.\n\nShe blushed with pleasure, but somehow the compliment did not satisfy her like the blunt praises he used to give her at home, when he promenaded round her on festival occasions, and told her she was \"altogether jolly,\" with a hearty smile and an approving pat on the head. She didn't like the new tone; for, though not _blasé_, it sounded indifferent in spite of the look.\n\n\"If that's the way he's going to grow up, I wish he'd stay a boy,\" she thought, with a curious sense of disappointment and discomfort, trying meantime to seem quite easy and gay.\n\nAt Avigdor's she found the precious home-letters, and, giving the reins to Laurie, read them luxuriously as they wound up the shady road between green hedges, where tea-roses bloomed as freshly as in June.\n\n\"Beth is very poorly, mother says. I often think I ought to go home, but they all say 'stay;' so I do, for I shall never have another chance like this,\" said Amy, looking sober over one page.\n\n\"I think you are right, there; you could do nothing at home, and it is a great comfort to them to know that you are well and happy, and enjoying so much, my dear.\"\n\nHe drew a little nearer, and looked more like his old self, as he said that; and the fear that sometimes weighed on Amy's heart was lightened, for the look, the act, the brotherly \"my dear,\" seemed to assure her that if any trouble did come, she would not be alone in a strange land. Presently she laughed, and showed him a small sketch of Jo in her scribbling-suit, with the bow rampantly erect upon her cap, and issuing from her mouth the words, \"Genius burns!\"\n\nLaurie smiled, took it, put it in his vest-pocket, \"to keep it from blowing away,\" and listened with interest to the lively letter Amy read him.\n\n\"This will be a regularly merry Christmas to me, with presents in the morning, you and letters in the afternoon, and a party at night,\" said Amy, as they alighted among the ruins of the old fort, and a flock of splendid peacocks came trooping about them, tamely waiting to be fed. While Amy stood laughing on the bank above him as she scattered crumbs to the brilliant birds, Laurie looked at her as she had looked at him, with a natural curiosity to see what changes time and absence had wrought. He found nothing to perplex or disappoint, much to admire and approve; for, overlooking a few little affectations of speech and manner, she was as sprightly and graceful as ever, with the addition of that indescribable something in dress and bearing which we call elegance. Always mature for her age, she had gained a certain _aplomb_ in both carriage and conversation, which made her seem more of a woman of the world than she was; but her old petulance now and then showed itself, her strong will still held its own, and her native frankness was unspoiled by foreign polish.\n\nLaurie did not read all this while he watched her feed the peacocks, but he saw enough to satisfy and interest him, and carried away a pretty little picture of a bright-faced girl standing in the sunshine, which brought out the soft hue of her dress, the fresh color of her cheeks, the golden gloss of her hair, and made her a prominent figure in the pleasant scene.\n\nAs they came up on to the stone plateau that crowns the hill, Amy waved her hand as if welcoming him to her favorite haunt, and said, pointing here and there,--\n\n\"Do you remember the Cathedral and the Corso, the fishermen dragging their nets in the bay, and the lovely road to Villa Franca, Schubert's Tower, just below, and, best of all, that speck far out to sea which they say is Corsica?\"\n\n\"I remember; it's not much changed,\" he answered, without enthusiasm.\n\n\"What Jo would give for a sight of that famous speck!\" said Amy, feeling in good spirits, and anxious to see him so also.\n\n\"Yes,\" was all he said, but he turned and strained his eyes to see the island which a greater usurper than even Napoleon now made interesting in his sight.\n\n\"Take a good look at it for her sake, and then come and tell me what you have been doing with yourself all this while,\" said Amy, seating herself, ready for a good talk.\n\nBut she did not get it; for, though he joined her, and answered all her questions freely, she could only learn that he had roved about the continent and been to Greece. So, after idling away an hour, they drove home again; and, having paid his respects to Mrs. Carrol, Laurie left them, promising to return in the evening.\n\nIt must be recorded of Amy that she deliberately \"prinked\" that night. Time and absence had done its work on both the young people; she had seen her old friend in a new light, not as \"our boy,\" but as a handsome and agreeable man, and she was conscious of a very natural desire to find favor in his sight. Amy knew her good points, and made the most of them, with the taste and skill which is a fortune to a poor and pretty woman.\n\nTarlatan and tulle were cheap at Nice, so she enveloped herself in them on such occasions, and, following the sensible English fashion of simple dress for young girls, got up charming little toilettes with fresh flowers, a few trinkets, and all manner of dainty devices, which were both inexpensive and effective. It must be confessed that the artist sometimes got possession of the woman, and indulged in antique _coiffures_, statuesque attitudes, and classic draperies. But, dear heart, we all have our little weaknesses, and find it easy to pardon such in the young, who satisfy our eyes with their comeliness, and keep our hearts merry with their artless vanities.\n\n\"I do want him to think I look well, and tell them so at home,\" said Amy to herself, as she put on Flo's old white silk ball-dress, and covered it with a cloud of fresh illusion, out of which her white shoulders and golden head emerged with a most artistic effect. Her hair she had the sense to let alone, after gathering up the thick waves and curls into a Hebe-like knot at the back of her head.\n\n\"It's not the fashion, but it's becoming, and I can't afford to make a fright of myself,\" she used to say, when advised to frizzle, puff, or braid, as the latest style commanded.\n\nHaving no ornaments fine enough for this important occasion, Amy looped her fleecy skirts with rosy clusters of azalea, and framed the white shoulders in delicate green vines. Remembering the painted boots, she surveyed her white satin slippers with girlish satisfaction, and _chasséed_ down the room, admiring her aristocratic feet all by herself.\n\n\"My new fan just matches my flowers, my gloves fit to a charm, and the real lace on aunt's _mouchoir_ gives an air to my whole dress. If I only had a classical nose and mouth I should be perfectly happy,\" she said, surveying herself with a critical eye, and a candle in each hand.\n\nIn spite of this affliction, she looked unusually gay and graceful as she glided away; she seldom ran,--it did not suit her style, she thought, for, being tall, the stately and Junoesque was more appropriate than the sportive or piquante. She walked up and down the long saloon while waiting for Laurie, and once arranged herself under the chandelier, which had a good effect upon her hair; then she thought better of it, and went away to the other end of the room, as if ashamed of the girlish desire to have the first view a propitious one. It so happened that she could not have done a better thing, for Laurie came in so quietly she did not hear him; and, as she stood at the distant window, with her head half turned, and one hand gathering up her dress, the slender, white figure against the red curtains was as effective as a well-placed statue.\n\n\"Good evening, Diana!\" said Laurie, with the look of satisfaction she liked to see in his eyes when they rested on her.\n\n\"Good evening, Apollo!\" she answered, smiling back at him, for he, too, looked unusually _debonnaire_, and the thought of entering the ball-room on the arm of such a personable man caused Amy to pity the four plain Misses Davis from the bottom of her heart.\n\n\"Here are your flowers; I arranged them myself, remembering that you didn't like what Hannah calls a 'sot-bookay,'\" said Laurie, handing her a delicate nosegay, in a holder that she had long coveted as she daily passed it in Cardiglia's window.\n\n\"How kind you are!\" she exclaimed gratefully. \"If I'd known you were coming I'd have had something ready for you to-day, though not as pretty as this, I'm afraid.\"\n\n\"Thank you; it isn't what it should be, but you have improved it,\" he added, as she snapped the silver bracelet on her wrist.\n\n\"Please don't.\"\n\n\"I thought you liked that sort of thing?\"\n\n\"Not from you; it doesn't sound natural, and I like your old bluntness better.\"\n\n\"I'm glad of it,\" he answered, with a look of relief; then buttoned her gloves for her, and asked if his tie was straight, just as he used to do when they went to parties together, at home.\n\nThe company assembled in the long _salle à manger_, that evening, was such as one sees nowhere but on the Continent. The hospitable Americans had invited every acquaintance they had in Nice, and, having no prejudice against titles, secured a few to add lustre to their Christmas ball.\n\nA Russian prince condescended to sit in a corner for an hour, and talk with a massive lady, dressed like Hamlet's mother, in black velvet, with a pearl bridle under her chin. A Polish count, aged eighteen, devoted himself to the ladies, who pronounced him \"a fascinating dear,\" and a German Serene Something, having come for the supper alone, roamed vaguely about, seeking what he might devour. Baron Rothschild's private secretary, a large-nosed Jew, in tight boots, affably beamed upon the world, as if his master's name crowned him with a golden halo; a stout Frenchman, who knew the Emperor, came to indulge his mania for dancing, and Lady de Jones, a British matron, adorned the scene with her little family of eight. Of course, there were many light-footed, shrill-voiced American girls, handsome, lifeless-looking English ditto, and a few plain but piquante French demoiselles; likewise the usual set of travelling young gentlemen, who disported themselves gayly, while mammas of all nations lined the walls, and smiled upon them benignly when they danced with their daughters.\n\nAny young girl can imagine Amy's state of mind when she \"took the stage\" that night, leaning on Laurie's arm. She knew she looked well, she loved to dance, she felt that her foot was on her native heath in a ball-room, and enjoyed the delightful sense of power which comes when young girls first discover the new and lovely kingdom they are born to rule by virtue of beauty, youth, and womanhood. She did pity the Davis girls, who were awkward, plain, and destitute of escort, except a grim papa and three grimmer maiden aunts, and she bowed to them in her friendliest manner as she passed; which was good of her, as it permitted them to see her dress, and burn with curiosity to know who her distinguished-looking friend might be. With the first burst of the band, Amy's color rose, her eyes began to sparkle, and her feet to tap the floor impatiently; for she danced well, and wanted Laurie to know it: therefore the shock she received can better be imagined than described, when he said, in a perfectly tranquil tone,--\n\n\"Do you care to dance?\"\n\n\"One usually does at a ball.\"\n\nHer amazed look and quick answer caused Laurie to repair his error as fast as possible.\n\n\"I meant the first dance. May I have the honor?\"\n\n\"I can give you one if I put off the Count. He dances divinely; but he will excuse me, as you are an old friend,\" said Amy, hoping that the name would have a good effect, and show Laurie that she was not to be trifled with.\n\n\"Nice little boy, but rather a short Pole to support\n\"'A daughter of the gods, Divinely tall, and most divinely fair,'\"\n\nwas all the satisfaction she got, however.\n\nThe set in which they found themselves was composed of English, and Amy was compelled to walk decorously through a cotillon, feeling all the while as if she could dance the Tarantula with a relish. Laurie resigned her to the \"nice little boy,\" and went to do his duty to Flo, without securing Amy for the joys to come, which reprehensible want of forethought was properly punished, for she immediately engaged herself till supper, meaning to relent if he then gave any signs of penitence. She showed him her ball-book with demure satisfaction when he strolled, instead of rushing, up to claim her for the next, a glorious polka-redowa; but his polite regrets didn't impose upon her, and when she gallopaded away with the Count, she saw Laurie sit down by her aunt with an actual expression of relief.\n\nThat was unpardonable; and Amy took no more notice of him for a long while, except a word now and then, when she came to her chaperon, between the dances, for a necessary pin or a moment's rest. Her anger had a good effect, however, for she hid it under a smiling face, and seemed unusually blithe and brilliant. Laurie's eyes followed her with pleasure, for she neither romped nor sauntered, but danced with spirit and grace, making the delightsome pastime what it should be. He very naturally fell to studying her from this new point of view; and, before the evening was half over, had decided that \"little Amy was going to make a very charming woman.\"\n\nIt was a lively scene, for soon the spirit of the social season took possession of every one, and Christmas merriment made all faces shine, hearts happy, and heels light. The musicians fiddled, tooted, and banged as if they enjoyed it; everybody danced who could, and those who couldn't admired their neighbors with uncommon warmth. The air was dark with Davises, and many Joneses gambolled like a flock of young giraffes. The golden secretary darted through the room like a meteor, with a dashing Frenchwoman, who carpeted the floor with her pink satin train. The Serene Teuton found the supper-table, and was happy, eating steadily through the bill of fare, and dismayed the _garçons_ by the ravages he committed. But the Emperor's friend covered himself with glory, for he danced everything, whether he knew it or not, and introduced impromptu pirouettes when the figures bewildered him. The boyish abandon of that stout man was charming to behold; for, though he \"carried weight,\" he danced like an india-rubber ball. He ran, he flew, he pranced; his face glowed, his bald head shone; his coat-tails waved wildly, his pumps actually twinkled in the air, and when the music stopped, he wiped the drops from his brow, and beamed upon his fellow-men like a French Pickwick without glasses.\n\nAmy and her Pole distinguished themselves by equal enthusiasm, but more graceful agility; and Laurie found himself involuntarily keeping time to the rhythmic rise and fall of the white slippers as they flew by as indefatigably as if winged. When little Vladimir finally relinquished her, with assurances that he was \"desolated to leave so early,\" she was ready to rest, and see how her recreant knight had borne his punishment.\nIt had been successful; for, at three-and-twenty, blighted affections find a balm in friendly society, and young nerves will thrill, young blood dance, and healthy young spirits rise, when subjected to the enchantment of beauty, light, music, and motion. Laurie had a waked-up look as he rose to give her his seat; and when he hurried away to bring her some supper, she said to herself, with a satisfied smile,--\n\n\"Ah, I thought that would do him good!\"\n\n\"You look like Balzac's 'Femme peinte par elle-même,'\" he said, as he fanned her with one hand, and held her coffee-cup in the other.\n\n\"My rouge won't come off;\" and Amy rubbed her brilliant cheek, and showed him her white glove with a sober simplicity that made him laugh outright.\n\n\"What do you call this stuff?\" he asked, touching a fold of her dress that had blown over his knee.\n\n\"Illusion.\"\n\n\"Good name for it; it's very pretty--new thing, isn't it?\"\n\n\"It's as old as the hills; you have seen it on dozens of girls, and you never found out that it was pretty till now--_stupide_!\"\n\n\"I never saw it on you before, which accounts for the mistake, you see.\"\n\n\"None of that, it is forbidden; I'd rather take coffee than compliments just now. No, don't lounge, it makes me nervous.\"\n\nLaurie sat bolt upright, and meekly took her empty plate, feeling an odd sort of pleasure in having \"little Amy\" order him about; for she had lost her shyness now, and felt an irresistible desire to trample on him, as girls have a delightful way of doing when lords of creation show any signs of subjection.\n\n\"Where did you learn all this sort of thing?\" he asked, with a quizzical look.\n\n\"As 'this sort of thing' is rather a vague expression, would you kindly explain?\" returned Amy, knowing perfectly well what he meant, but wickedly leaving him to describe what is indescribable.\n\n\"Well--the general air, the style, the self-possession, the--the--illusion--you know,\" laughed Laurie, breaking down, and helping himself out of his quandary with the new word.\n\nAmy was gratified, but, of course, didn't show it, and demurely answered, \"Foreign life polishes one in spite of one's self; I study as well as play; and as for this\"--with a little gesture toward her dress--\"why, tulle is cheap, posies to be had for nothing, and I am used to making the most of my poor little things.\"\n\nAmy rather regretted that last sentence, fearing it wasn't in good taste; but Laurie liked her the better for it, and found himself both admiring and respecting the brave patience that made the most of opportunity, and the cheerful spirit that covered poverty with flowers. Amy did not know why he looked at her so kindly, nor why he filled up her book with his own name, and devoted himself to her for the rest of the evening, in the most delightful manner; but the impulse that wrought this agreeable change was the result of one of the new impressions which both of them were unconsciously giving and receiving.\n\n# ON THE SHELF.\n\nIn France the young girls have a dull time of it till they are married, when \"_Vive la liberté_\" becomes their motto. In America, as every one knows, girls early sign the declaration of independence, and enjoy their freedom with republican zest; but the young matrons usually abdicate with the first heir to the throne, and go into a seclusion almost as close as a French nunnery, though by no means as quiet. Whether they like it or not, they are virtually put upon the shelf as soon as the wedding excitement is over, and most of them might exclaim, as did a very pretty woman the other day, \"I'm as handsome as ever, but no one takes any notice of me because I'm married.\"\n\nNot being a belle or even a fashionable lady, Meg did not experience this affliction till her babies were a year old, for in her little world primitive customs prevailed, and she found herself more admired and beloved than ever.\n\nAs she was a womanly little woman, the maternal instinct was very strong, and she was entirely absorbed in her children, to the utter exclusion of everything and everybody else. Day and night she brooded over them with tireless devotion and anxiety, leaving John to the tender mercies of the help, for an Irish lady now presided over the kitchen department. Being a domestic man, John decidedly missed the wifely attentions he had been accustomed to receive; but, as he adored his babies, he cheerfully relinquished his comfort for a time, supposing, with masculine ignorance, that peace would soon be restored. But three months passed, and there was no return of repose; Meg looked worn and nervous, the babies absorbed every minute of her time, the house was neglected, and Kitty, the cook, who took life \"aisy,\" kept him on short commons. When he went out in the morning he was bewildered by small commissions for the captive mamma; if he came gayly in at night, eager to embrace his family, he was quenched by a \"Hush! they are just asleep after worrying all day.\" If he proposed a little amusement at home, \"No, it would disturb the babies.\" If he hinted at a lecture or concert, he was answered with a reproachful look, and a decided \"Leave my children for pleasure, never!\" His sleep was broken by infant wails and visions of a phantom figure pacing noiselessly to and fro in the watches of the night; his meals were interrupted by the frequent flight of the presiding genius, who deserted him, half-helped, if a muffled chirp sounded from the nest above; and when he read his paper of an evening, Demi's colic got into the shipping-list, and Daisy's fall affected the price of stocks, for Mrs. Brooke was only interested in domestic news.\nThe poor man was very uncomfortable, for the children had bereft him of his wife; home was merely a nursery, and the perpetual \"hushing\" made him feel like a brutal intruder whenever he entered the sacred precincts of Babyland. He bore it very patiently for six months, and, when no signs of amendment appeared, he did what other paternal exiles do,--tried to get a little comfort elsewhere. Scott had married and gone to housekeeping not far off, and John fell into the way of running over for an hour or two of an evening, when his own parlor was empty, and his own wife singing lullabies that seemed to have no end. Mrs. Scott was a lively, pretty girl, with nothing to do but be agreeable, and she performed her mission most successfully. The parlor was always bright and attractive, the chess-board ready, the piano in tune, plenty of gay gossip, and a nice little supper set forth in tempting style.\n\nJohn would have preferred his own fireside if it had not been so lonely; but as it was, he gratefully took the next best thing, and enjoyed his neighbor's society.\n\nMeg rather approved of the new arrangement at first, and found it a relief to know that John was having a good time instead of dozing in the parlor, or tramping about the house and waking the children. But by and by, when the teething worry was over, and the idols went to sleep at proper hours, leaving mamma time to rest, she began to miss John, and find her work-basket dull company, when he was not sitting opposite in his old dressing-gown, comfortably scorching his slippers on the fender. She would not ask him to stay at home, but felt injured because he did not know that she wanted him without being told, entirely forgetting the many evenings he had waited for her in vain. She was nervous and worn out with watching and worry, and in that unreasonable frame of mind which the best of mothers occasionally experience when domestic cares oppress them. Want of exercise robs them of cheerfulness, and too much devotion to that idol of American women, the teapot, makes them feel as if they were all nerve and no muscle.\n\n\"Yes,\" she would say, looking in the glass, \"I'm getting old and ugly; John doesn't find me interesting any longer, so he leaves his faded wife and goes to see his pretty neighbor, who has no incumbrances. Well, the babies love me; they don't care if I am thin and pale, and haven't time to crimp my hair; they are my comfort, and some day John will see what I've gladly sacrificed for them, won't he, my precious?\"\n\nTo which pathetic appeal Daisy would answer with a coo, or Demi with a crow, and Meg would put by her lamentations for a maternal revel, which soothed her solitude for the time being. But the pain increased as politics absorbed John, who was always running over to discuss interesting points with Scott, quite unconscious that Meg missed him. Not a word did she say, however, till her mother found her in tears one day, and insisted on knowing what the matter was, for Meg's drooping spirits had not escaped her observation.\n\n\"I wouldn't tell any one except you, mother; but I really do need advice, for, if John goes on so much longer I might as well be widowed,\" replied Mrs. Brooke, drying her tears on Daisy's bib, with an injured air.\n\n\"Goes on how, my dear?\" asked her mother anxiously.\n\n\"He's away all day, and at night, when I want to see him, he is continually going over to the Scotts'. It isn't fair that I should have the hardest work, and never any amusement. Men are very selfish, even the best of them.\"\n\n\"So are women; don't blame John till you see where you are wrong yourself.\"\n\n\"But it can't be right for him to neglect me.\"\n\n\"Don't you neglect him?\"\n\n\"Why, mother, I thought you'd take my part!\"\n\n\"So I do, as far as sympathizing goes; but I think the fault is yours, Meg.\"\n\n\"I don't see how.\"\n\n\"Let me show you. Did John ever neglect you, as you call it, while you made it a point to give him your society of an evening, his only leisure time?\"\n\n\"No; but I can't do it now, with two babies to tend.\"\n\n\"I think you could, dear; and I think you ought. May I speak quite freely, and will you remember that it's mother who blames as well as mother who sympathizes?\"\n\n\"Indeed I will! Speak to me as if I were little Meg again. I often feel as if I needed teaching more than ever since these babies look to me for everything.\"\n\nMeg drew her low chair beside her mother's, and, with a little interruption in either lap, the two women rocked and talked lovingly together, feeling that the tie of motherhood made them more one than ever.\n\n\"You have only made the mistake that most young wives make,--forgotten your duty to your husband in your love for your children. A very natural and forgivable mistake, Meg, but one that had better be remedied before you take to different ways; for children should draw you nearer than ever, not separate you, as if they were all yours, and John had nothing to do but support them. I've seen it for some weeks, but have not spoken, feeling sure it would come right in time.\"\n\n\"I'm afraid it won't. If I ask him to stay, he'll think I'm jealous; and I wouldn't insult him by such an idea. He doesn't see that I want him, and I don't know how to tell him without words.\"\n\n\"Make it so pleasant he won't want to go away. My dear, he's longing for his little home; but it isn't home without you, and you are always in the nursery.\"\n\n\"Oughtn't I to be there?\"\n\n\"Not all the time; too much confinement makes you nervous, and then you are unfitted for everything. Besides, you owe something to John as well as to the babies; don't neglect husband for children, don't shut him out of the nursery, but teach him how to help in it. His place is there as well as yours, and the children need him; let him feel that he has his part to do, and he will do it gladly and faithfully, and it will be better for you all.\"\n\n\"You really think so, mother?\"\n\n\"I know it, Meg, for I've tried it; and I seldom give advice unless I've proved its practicability. When you and Jo were little, I went on just as you are, feeling as if I didn't do my duty unless I devoted myself wholly to you. Poor father took to his books, after I had refused all offers of help, and left me to try my experiment alone. I struggled along as well as I could, but Jo was too much for me. I nearly spoilt her by indulgence. You were poorly, and I worried about you till I fell sick myself. Then father came to the rescue, quietly managed everything, and made himself so helpful that I saw my mistake, and never have been able to get on without him since. That is the secret of our home happiness: he does not let business wean him from the little cares and duties that affect us all, and I try not to let domestic worries destroy my interest in his pursuits. Each do our part alone in many things, but at home we work together, always.\"\n\n\"It is so, mother; and my great wish is to be to my husband and children what you have been to yours. Show me how; I'll do anything you say.\"\n\n\"You always were my docile daughter. Well, dear, if I were you, I'd let John have more to do with the management of Demi, for the boy needs training, and it's none too soon to begin. Then I'd do what I have often proposed, let Hannah come and help you; she is a capital nurse, and you may trust the precious babies to her while you do more housework. You need the exercise, Hannah would enjoy the rest, and John would find his wife again. Go out more; keep cheerful as well as busy, for you are the sunshine-maker of the family, and if you get dismal there is no fair weather. Then I'd try to take an interest in whatever John likes,--talk with him, let him read to you, exchange ideas, and help each other in that way. Don't shut yourself up in a bandbox because you are a woman, but understand what is going on, and educate yourself to take your part in the world's work, for it all affects you and yours.\"\n\n\"John is so sensible, I'm afraid he will think I'm stupid if I ask questions about politics and things.\"\n\n\"I don't believe he would; love covers a multitude of sins, and of whom could you ask more freely than of him? Try it, and see if he doesn't find your society far more agreeable than Mrs. Scott's suppers.\"\n\n\"I will. Poor John! I'm afraid I _have_ neglected him sadly, but I thought I was right, and he never said anything.\"\n\n\"He tried not to be selfish, but he _has_ felt rather forlorn, I fancy. This is just the time, Meg, when young married people are apt to grow apart, and the very time when they ought to be most together; for the first tenderness soon wears off, unless care is taken to preserve it; and no time is so beautiful and precious to parents as the first years of the little lives given them to train. Don't let John be a stranger to the babies, for they will do more to keep him safe and happy in this world of trial and temptation than anything else, and through them you will learn to know and love one another as you should. Now, dear, good-by; think over mother's preachment, act upon it if it seems good, and God bless you all!\"\n\nMeg did think it over, found it good, and acted upon it, though the first attempt was not made exactly as she planned to have it. Of course the children tyrannized over her, and ruled the house as soon as they found out that kicking and squalling brought them whatever they wanted. Mamma was an abject slave to their caprices, but papa was not so easily subjugated, and occasionally afflicted his tender spouse by an attempt at paternal discipline with his obstreperous son. For Demi inherited a trifle of his sire's firmness of character,--we won't call it obstinacy,--and when he made up his little mind to have or to do anything, all the king's horses and all the king's men could not change that pertinacious little mind. Mamma thought the dear too young to be taught to conquer his prejudices, but papa believed that it never was too soon to learn obedience; so Master Demi early discovered that when he undertook to \"wrastle\" with \"parpar,\" he always got the worst of it; yet, like the Englishman, Baby respected the man who conquered him, and loved the father whose grave \"No, no,\" was more impressive than all mamma's love-pats.\n\nA few days after the talk with her mother, Meg resolved to try a social evening with John; so she ordered a nice supper, set the parlor in order, dressed herself prettily, and put the children to bed early, that nothing should interfere with her experiment. But, unfortunately, Demi's most unconquerable prejudice was against going to bed, and that night he decided to go on a rampage; so poor Meg sung and rocked, told stories and tried every sleep-provoking wile she could devise, but all in vain, the big eyes wouldn't shut; and long after Daisy had gone to byelow, like the chubby little bunch of good-nature she was, naughty Demi lay staring at the light, with the most discouragingly wide-awake expression of countenance.\n\n\"Will Demi lie still like a good boy, while mamma runs down and gives poor papa his tea?\" asked Meg, as the hall-door softly closed, and the well-known step went tiptoeing into the dining-room.\n\n\"Me has tea!\" said Demi, preparing to join in the revel.\n\n\"No; but I'll save you some little cakies for breakfast, if you'll go bye-by like Daisy. Will you, lovey?\"\n\n\"Iss!\" and Demi shut his eyes tight, as if to catch sleep and hurry the desired day.\n\nTaking advantage of the propitious moment, Meg slipped away, and ran down to greet her husband with a smiling face, and the little blue bow in her hair which was his especial admiration. He saw it at once, and said, with pleased surprise,--\n\n\"Why, little mother, how gay we are to-night. Do you expect company?\"\n\n\"Only you, dear.\"\n\n\"Is it a birthday, anniversary, or anything?\"\n\n\"No; I'm tired of being a dowdy, so I dressed up as a change. You always make yourself nice for table, no matter how tired you are; so why shouldn't I when I have the time?\"\n\n\"I do it out of respect to you, my dear,\" said old-fashioned John.\n\n\"Ditto, ditto, Mr. Brooke,\" laughed Meg, looking young and pretty again, as she nodded to him over the teapot.\n\n\"Well, it's altogether delightful, and like old times. This tastes right. I drink your health, dear.\" And John sipped his tea with an air of reposeful rapture, which was of very short duration, however; for, as he put down his cup, the door-handle rattled mysteriously, and a little voice was heard, saying impatiently,--\n\n\"Opy doy; me's tummin!\"\n\n\"It's that naughty boy. I told him to go to sleep alone, and here he is, downstairs, getting his death a-cold pattering over that canvas,\" said Meg, answering the call.\n\n\"Mornin' now,\" announced Demi, in a joyful tone, as he entered, with his long night-gown gracefully festooned over his arm, and every curl bobbing gayly as he pranced about the table, eying the \"cakies\" with loving glances.\n\n\"No, it isn't morning yet. You must go to bed, and not trouble poor mamma; then you can have the little cake with sugar on it.\"\n\n\"Me loves parpar,\" said the artful one, preparing to climb the paternal knee, and revel in forbidden joys. But John shook his head, and said to Meg,--\n\n\"If you told him to stay up there, and go to sleep alone, make him do it, or he will never learn to mind you.\"\n\n\"Yes, of course. Come, Demi;\" and Meg led her son away, feeling a strong desire to spank the little marplot who hopped beside her, laboring under the delusion that the bribe was to be administered as soon as they reached the nursery.\n\nNor was he disappointed; for that short-sighted woman actually gave him a lump of sugar, tucked him into his bed, and forbade any more promenades till morning.\n\n\"Iss!\" said Demi the perjured, blissfully sucking his sugar, and regarding his first attempt as eminently successful.\n\nMeg returned to her place, and supper was progressing pleasantly, when the little ghost walked again, and exposed the maternal delinquencies by boldly demanding,--\n\n\"More sudar, marmar.\"\n\n\"Now this won't do,\" said John, hardening his heart against the engaging little sinner. \"We shall never know any peace till that child learns to go to bed properly. You have made a slave of yourself long enough; give him one lesson, and then there will be an end of it. Put him in his bed and leave him, Meg.\"\n\n\"He won't stay there; he never does, unless I sit by him.\"\n\n\"I'll manage him. Demi, go upstairs, and get into your bed, as mamma bids you.\"\n\n\"S'ant!\" replied the young rebel, helping himself to the coveted \"cakie,\" and beginning to eat the same with calm audacity.\n\n\"You must never say that to papa; I shall carry you if you don't go yourself.\"\n\n\"Go 'way; me don't love parpar;\" and Demi retired to his mother's skirts for protection.\n\nBut even that refuge proved unavailing, for he was delivered over to the enemy, with a \"Be gentle with him, John,\" which struck the culprit with dismay; for when mamma deserted him, then the judgment-day was at hand. Bereft of his cake, defrauded of his frolic, and borne away by a strong hand to that detested bed, poor Demi could not restrain his wrath, but openly defied papa, and kicked and screamed lustily all the way upstairs. The minute he was put into bed on one side, he rolled out on the other, and made for the door, only to be ignominiously caught up by the tail of his little toga, and put back again, which lively performance was kept up till the young man's strength gave out, when he devoted himself to roaring at the top of his voice. This vocal exercise usually conquered Meg; but John sat as unmoved as the post which is popularly believed to be deaf. No coaxing, no sugar, no lullaby, no story; even the light was put out, and only the red glow of the fire enlivened the \"big dark\" which Demi regarded with curiosity rather than fear. This new order of things disgusted him, and he howled dismally for \"marmar,\" as his angry passions subsided, and recollections of his tender bondwoman returned to the captive autocrat. The plaintive wail which succeeded the passionate roar went to Meg's heart, and she ran up to say beseechingly,--\n\n\"Let me stay with him; he'll be good, now, John.\"\n\n\"No, my dear, I've told him he must go to sleep, as you bid him; and he must, if I stay here all night.\"\n\n\"But he'll cry himself sick,\" pleaded Meg, reproaching herself for deserting her boy.\n\n\"No, he won't, he's so tired he will soon drop off, and then the matter is settled; for he will understand that he has got to mind. Don't interfere; I'll manage him.\"\n\n\"He's my child, and I can't have his spirit broken by harshness.\"\n\n\"He's my child, and I won't have his temper spoilt by indulgence. Go down, my dear, and leave the boy to me.\"\n\nWhen John spoke in that masterful tone, Meg always obeyed, and never regretted her docility.\n\n\"Please let me kiss him once, John?\"\n\n\"Certainly. Demi, say 'good-night' to mamma, and let her go and rest, for she is very tired with taking care of you all day.\"\n\nMeg always insisted upon it that the kiss won the victory; for after it was given, Demi sobbed more quietly, and lay quite still at the bottom of the bed, whither he had wriggled in his anguish of mind.\n\n\"Poor little man, he's worn out with sleep and crying. I'll cover him up, and then go and set Meg's heart at rest,\" thought John, creeping to the bedside, hoping to find his rebellious heir asleep.\n\nBut he wasn't; for the moment his father peeped at him, Demi's eyes opened, his little chin began to quiver, and he put up his arms, saying, with a penitent hiccough, \"Me's dood, now.\"\n\nSitting on the stairs, outside, Meg wondered at the long silence which followed the uproar; and, after imagining all sorts of impossible accidents, she slipped into the room, to set her fears at rest. Demi lay fast asleep; not in his usual spread-eagle attitude, but in a subdued bunch, cuddled close in the circle of his father's arm and holding his father's finger, as if he felt that justice was tempered with mercy, and had gone to sleep a sadder and a wiser baby. So held, John had waited with womanly patience till the little hand relaxed its hold; and, while waiting, had fallen asleep, more tired by that tussle with his son than with his whole day's work.\n\nAs Meg stood watching the two faces on the pillow, she smiled to herself, and then slipped away again, saying, in a satisfied tone,--\n\n\"I never need fear that John will be too harsh with my babies: he _does_ know how to manage them, and will be a great help, for Demi _is_ getting too much for me.\"\n\nWhen John came down at last, expecting to find a pensive or reproachful wife, he was agreeably surprised to find Meg placidly trimming a bonnet, and to be greeted with the request to read something about the election, if he was not too tired. John saw in a minute that a revolution of some kind was going on, but wisely asked no questions, knowing that Meg was such a transparent little person, she couldn't keep a secret to save her life, and therefore the clew would soon appear. He read a long debate with the most amiable readiness, and then explained it in his most lucid manner, while Meg tried to look deeply interested, to ask intelligent questions, and keep her thoughts from wandering from the state of the nation to the state of her bonnet. In her secret soul, however, she decided that politics were as bad as mathematics, and that the mission of politicians seemed to be calling each other names; but she kept these feminine ideas to herself, and when John paused, shook her head, and said with what she thought diplomatic ambiguity,--\n\n\"Well, I really don't see what we are coming to.\"\n\nJohn laughed, and watched her for a minute, as she poised a pretty little preparation of lace and flowers on her hand, and regarded it with the genuine interest which his harangue had failed to waken.\n\n\"She is trying to like politics for my sake, so I'll try and like millinery for hers, that's only fair,\" thought John the Just, adding aloud,--\n\n\"That's very pretty; is it what you call a breakfast-cap?\"\n\n\"My dear man, it's a bonnet! My very best go-to-concert-and-theatre bonnet.\"\n\n\"I beg your pardon; it was so small, I naturally mistook it for one of the fly-away things you sometimes wear. How do you keep it on?\"\n\n\"These bits of lace are fastened under the chin with a rosebud, so;\" and Meg illustrated by putting on the bonnet, and regarding him with an air of calm satisfaction that was irresistible.\n\n\"It's a love of a bonnet, but I prefer the face inside, for it looks young and happy again,\" and John kissed the smiling face, to the great detriment of the rosebud under the chin.\n\n\"I'm glad you like it, for I want you to take me to one of the new concerts some night; I really need some music to put me in tune. Will you, please?\"\n\n\"Of course I will, with all my heart, or anywhere else you like. You have been shut up so long, it will do you no end of good, and I shall enjoy it, of all things. What put it into your head, little mother?\"\n\n\"Well, I had a talk with Marmee the other day, and told her how nervous and cross and out of sorts I felt, and she said I needed change and less care; so Hannah is to help me with the children, and I'm to see to things about the house more, and now and then have a little fun, just to keep me from getting to be a fidgety, broken-down old woman before my time. It's only an experiment, John, and I want to try it for your sake as much as for mine, because I've neglected you shamefully lately, and I'm going to make home what it used to be, if I can. You don't object, I hope?\"\n\nNever mind what John said, or what a very narrow escape the little bonnet had from utter ruin; all that we have any business to know, is that John did _not_ appear to object, judging from the changes which gradually took place in the house and its inmates. It was not all Paradise by any means, but every one was better for the division of labor system; the children throve under the paternal rule, for accurate, steadfast John brought order and obedience into Babydom, while Meg recovered her spirits and composed her nerves by plenty of wholesome exercise, a little pleasure, and much confidential conversation with her sensible husband. Home grew home-like again, and John had no wish to leave it, unless he took Meg with him. The Scotts came to the Brookes' now, and every one found the little house a cheerful place, full of happiness, content, and family love. Even gay Sallie Moffatt liked to go there. \"It is always so quiet and pleasant here; it does me good, Meg,\" she used to say, looking about her with wistful eyes, as if trying to discover the charm, that she might use it in her great house, full of splendid loneliness; for there were no riotous, sunny-faced babies there, and Ned lived in a world of his own, where there was no place for her.\n\nThis household happiness did not come all at once, but John and Meg had found the key to it, and each year of married life taught them how to use it, unlocking the treasuries of real home-love and mutual helpfulness, which the poorest may possess, and the richest cannot buy. This is the sort of shelf on which young wives and mothers may consent to be laid, safe from the restless fret and fever of the world, finding loyal lovers in the little sons and daughters who cling to them, undaunted by sorrow, poverty, or age; walking side by side, through fair and stormy weather, with a faithful friend, who is, in the true sense of the good old Saxon word, the \"house-band,\" and learning, as Meg learned, that a woman's happiest kingdom is home, her highest honor the art of ruling it, not as a queen, but a wise wife and mother.\n\n# LAZY LAURENCE.\n\nLaurie went to Nice intending to stay a week, and remained a month. He was tired of wandering about alone, and Amy's familiar presence seemed to give a home-like charm to the foreign scenes in which she bore a part. He rather missed the \"petting\" he used to receive, and enjoyed a taste of it again; for no attentions, however flattering, from strangers, were half so pleasant as the sisterly adoration of the girls at home. Amy never would pet him like the others, but she was very glad to see him now, and quite clung to him, feeling that he was the representative of the dear family for whom she longed more than she would confess. They naturally took comfort in each other's society, and were much together, riding, walking, dancing, or dawdling, for, at Nice, no one can be very industrious during the gay season. But, while apparently amusing themselves in the most careless fashion, they were half-consciously making discoveries and forming opinions about each other. Amy rose daily in the estimation of her friend, but he sunk in hers, and each felt the truth before a word was spoken. Amy tried to please, and succeeded, for she was grateful for the many pleasures he gave her, and repaid him with the little services to which womanly women know how to lend an indescribable charm. Laurie made no effort of any kind, but just let himself drift along as comfortably as possible, trying to forget, and feeling that all women owed him a kind word because one had been cold to him. It cost him no effort to be generous, and he would have given Amy all the trinkets in Nice if she would have taken them; but, at the same time, he felt that he could not change the opinion she was forming of him, and he rather dreaded the keen blue eyes that seemed to watch him with such half-sorrowful, half-scornful surprise.\n\n\"All the rest have gone to Monaco for the day; I preferred to stay at home and write letters. They are done now, and I am going to Valrosa to sketch; will you come?\" said Amy, as she joined Laurie one lovely day when he lounged in as usual, about noon.\n\n\"Well, yes; but isn't it rather warm for such a long walk?\" he answered slowly, for the shaded _salon_ looked inviting, after the glare without.\n\n\"I'm going to have the little carriage, and Baptiste can drive, so you'll have nothing to do but hold your umbrella and keep your gloves nice,\" returned Amy, with a sarcastic glance at the immaculate kids, which were a weak point with Laurie.\n\n\"Then I'll go with pleasure;\" and he put out his hand for her sketch-book. But she tucked it under her arm with a sharp--\n\n\"Don't trouble yourself; it's no exertion to me, but _you_ don't look equal to it.\"\n\nLaurie lifted his eyebrows, and followed at a leisurely pace as she ran downstairs; but when they got into the carriage he took the reins himself, and left little Baptiste nothing to do but fold his arms and fall asleep on his perch.\n\nThe two never quarrelled,--Amy was too well-bred, and just now Laurie was too lazy; so, in a minute he peeped under her hat-brim with an inquiring air; she answered with a smile, and they went on together in the most amicable manner.\n\nIt was a lovely drive, along winding roads rich in the picturesque scenes that delight beauty-loving eyes. Here an ancient monastery, whence the solemn chanting of the monks came down to them. There a bare-legged shepherd, in wooden shoes, pointed hat, and rough jacket over one shoulder, sat piping on a stone, while his goats skipped among the rocks or lay at his feet. Meek, mouse-colored donkeys, laden with panniers of freshly-cut grass, passed by, with a pretty girl in a _capaline_ sitting between the green piles, or an old woman spinning with a distaff as she went. Brown, soft-eyed children ran out from the quaint stone hovels to offer nosegays, or bunches of oranges still on the bough. Gnarled olive-trees covered the hills with their dusky foliage, fruit hung golden in the orchard, and great scarlet anemones fringed the roadside; while beyond green slopes and craggy heights, the Maritime Alps rose sharp and white against the blue Italian sky.\n\nValrosa well deserved its name, for, in that climate of perpetual summer, roses blossomed everywhere. They overhung the archway, thrust themselves between the bars of the great gate with a sweet welcome to passers-by, and lined the avenue, winding through lemon-trees and feathery palms up to the villa on the hill. Every shadowy nook, where seats invited one to stop and rest, was a mass of bloom; every cool grotto had its marble nymph smiling from a veil of flowers, and every fountain reflected crimson, white, or pale pink roses, leaning down to smile at their own beauty. Roses covered the walls of the house, draped the cornices, climbed the pillars, and ran riot over the balustrade of the wide terrace, whence one looked down on the sunny Mediterranean, and the white-walled city on its shore.\n\n\"This is a regular honeymoon Paradise, isn't it? Did you ever see such roses?\" asked Amy, pausing on the terrace to enjoy the view, and a luxurious whiff of perfume that came wandering by.\n\n\"No, nor felt such thorns,\" returned Laurie, with his thumb in his mouth, after a vain attempt to capture a solitary scarlet flower that grew just beyond his reach.\n\n\"Try lower down, and pick those that have no thorns,\" said Amy, gathering three of the tiny cream-colored ones that starred the wall behind her. She put them in his button-hole, as a peace-offering, and he stood a minute looking down at them with a curious expression, for in the Italian part of his nature there was a touch of superstition, and he was just then in that state of half-sweet, half-bitter melancholy, when imaginative young men find significance in trifles, and food for romance everywhere. He had thought of Jo in reaching after the thorny red rose, for vivid flowers became her, and she had often worn ones like that from the greenhouse at home. The pale roses Amy gave him were the sort that the Italians lay in dead hands, never in bridal wreaths, and, for a moment, he wondered if the omen was for Jo or for himself; but the next instant his American common-sense got the better of sentimentality, and he laughed a heartier laugh than Amy had heard since he came.\n\n\"It's good advice; you'd better take it and save your fingers,\" she said, thinking her speech amused him.\n\n\"Thank you, I will,\" he answered in jest, and a few months later he did it in earnest.\n\n\"Laurie, when are you going to your grandfather?\" she asked presently, as she settled herself on a rustic seat.\n\n\"Very soon.\"\n\n\"You have said that a dozen times within the last three weeks.\"\n\n\"I dare say; short answers save trouble.\"\n\n\"He expects you, and you really ought to go.\"\n\n\"Hospitable creature! I know it.\"\n\n\"Then why don't you do it?\"\n\n\"Natural depravity, I suppose.\"\n\n\"Natural indolence, you mean. It's really dreadful!\" and Amy looked severe.\n\n\"Not so bad as it seems, for I should only plague him if I went, so I might as well stay, and plague you a little longer, you can bear it better; in fact, I think it agrees with you excellently;\" and Laurie composed himself for a lounge on the broad ledge of the balustrade.\n\nAmy shook her head, and opened her sketch-book with an air of resignation; but she had made up her mind to lecture \"that boy,\" and in a minute she began again.\n\n\"What are you doing just now?\"\n\n\"Watching lizards.\"\n\n\"No, no; I mean what do you intend and wish to do?\"\n\n\"Smoke a cigarette, if you'll allow me.\"\n\n\"How provoking you are! I don't approve of cigars, and I will only allow it on condition that you let me put you into my sketch; I need a figure.\"\n\n\"With all the pleasure in life. How will you have me,--full-length or three-quarters, on my head or my heels? I should respectfully suggest a recumbent posture, then put yourself in also, and call it '_Dolce far niente._'\"\n\n\"Stay as you are, and go to sleep if you like. _I_ intend to work hard,\" said Amy, in her most energetic tone.\n\n\"What delightful enthusiasm!\" and he leaned against a tall urn with an air of entire satisfaction.\n\n\"What would Jo say if she saw you now?\" asked Amy impatiently, hoping to stir him up by the mention of her still more energetic sister's name.\n\n\"As usual, 'Go away, Teddy, I'm busy!'\" He laughed as he spoke, but the laugh was not natural, and a shade passed over his face, for the utterance of the familiar name touched the wound that was not healed yet. Both tone and shadow struck Amy, for she had seen and heard them before, and now she looked up in time to catch a new expression on Laurie's face,--a hard, bitter look, full of pain, dissatisfaction, and regret. It was gone before she could study it, and the listless expression back again. She watched him for a moment with artistic pleasure, thinking how like an Italian he looked, as he lay basking in the sun with uncovered head, and eyes full of southern dreaminess; for he seemed to have forgotten her, and fallen into a reverie.\n\n\"You look like the effigy of a young knight asleep on his tomb,\" she said, carefully tracing the well-cut profile defined against the dark stone.\n\n\"Wish I was!\"\n\"That's a foolish wish, unless you have spoilt your life. You are so changed, I sometimes think--\" there Amy stopped, with a half-timid, half-wistful look, more significant than her unfinished speech.\n\nLaurie saw and understood the affectionate anxiety which she hesitated to express, and looking straight into her eyes, said, just as he used to say it to her mother,--\n\n\"It's all right, ma'am.\"\n\nThat satisfied her and set at rest the doubts that had begun to worry her lately. It also touched her, and she showed that it did, by the cordial tone in which she said,--\n\n\"I'm glad of that! I didn't think you'd been a very bad boy, but I fancied you might have wasted money at that wicked Baden-Baden, lost your heart to some charming Frenchwoman with a husband, or got into some of the scrapes that young men seem to consider a necessary part of a foreign tour. Don't stay out there in the sun; come and lie on the grass here, and 'let us be friendly,' as Jo used to say when we got in the sofa-corner and told secrets.\"\n\nLaurie obediently threw himself down on the turf, and began to amuse himself by sticking daisies into the ribbons of Amy's hat, that lay there.\n\n\"I'm all ready for the secrets;\" and he glanced up with a decided expression of interest in his eyes.\n\n\"I've none to tell; you may begin.\"\n\n\"Haven't one to bless myself with. I thought perhaps you'd had some news from home.\"\n\n\"You have heard all that has come lately. Don't you hear often? I fancied Jo would send you volumes.\"\n\n\"She's very busy; I'm roving about so, it's impossible to be regular, you know. When do you begin your great work of art, Raphaella?\" he asked, changing the subject abruptly after another pause, in which he had been wondering if Amy knew his secret, and wanted to talk about it.\n\n\"Never,\" she answered, with a despondent but decided air. \"Rome took all the vanity out of me; for after seeing the wonders there, I felt too insignificant to live, and gave up all my foolish hopes in despair.\"\n\n\"Why should you, with so much energy and talent?\"\n\n\"That's just why,--because talent isn't genius, and no amount of energy can make it so. I want to be great, or nothing. I won't be a common-place dauber, so I don't intend to try any more.\"\n\n\"And what are you going to do with yourself now, if I may ask?\"\n\n\"Polish up my other talents, and be an ornament to society, if I get the chance.\"\n\nIt was a characteristic speech, and sounded daring; but audacity becomes young people, and Amy's ambition had a good foundation. Laurie smiled, but he liked the spirit with which she took up a new purpose when a long-cherished one died, and spent no time lamenting.\n\n\"Good! and here is where Fred Vaughn comes in, I fancy.\"\n\nAmy preserved a discreet silence, but there was a conscious look in her downcast face, that made Laurie sit up and say gravely,--\n\n\"Now I'm going to play brother, and ask questions. May I?\"\n\n\"I don't promise to answer.\"\n\n\"Your face will, if your tongue won't. You aren't woman of the world enough yet to hide your feelings, my dear. I heard rumors about Fred and you last year, and it's my private opinion that, if he had not been called home so suddenly and detained so long, something would have come of it--hey?\"\n\n\"That's not for me to say,\" was Amy's prim reply; but her lips would smile, and there was a traitorous sparkle of the eye, which betrayed that she knew her power and enjoyed the knowledge.\n\n\"You are not engaged, I hope?\" and Laurie looked very elder-brotherly and grave all of a sudden.\n\n\"But you will be, if he comes back and goes properly down upon his knees, won't you?\"\n\n\"Very likely.\"\n\"Then you are fond of old Fred?\"\n\n\"I could be, if I tried.\"\n\n\"But you don't intend to try till the proper moment? Bless my soul, what unearthly prudence! He's a good fellow, Amy, but not the man I fancied you'd like.\"\n\n\"He is rich, a gentleman, and has delightful manners,\" began Amy, trying to be quite cool and dignified, but feeling a little ashamed of herself, in spite of the sincerity of her intentions.\n\n\"I understand; queens of society can't get on without money, so you mean to make a good match, and start in that way? Quite right and proper, as the world goes, but it sounds odd from the lips of one of your mother's girls.\"\n\n\"True, nevertheless.\"\n\nA short speech, but the quiet decision with which it was uttered contrasted curiously with the young speaker. Laurie felt this instinctively, and laid himself down again, with a sense of disappointment which he could not explain. His look and silence, as well as a certain inward self-disapproval, ruffled Amy, and made her resolve to deliver her lecture without delay.\n\n\"I wish you'd do me the favor to rouse yourself a little,\" she said sharply.\n\n\"Do it for me, there's a dear girl.\"\n\n\"I could, if I tried;\" and she looked as if she would like doing it in the most summary style.\n\n\"Try, then; I give you leave,\" returned Laurie, who enjoyed having some one to tease, after his long abstinence from his favorite pastime.\n\n\"You'd be angry in five minutes.\"\n\n\"I'm never angry with you. It takes two flints to make a fire: you are as cool and soft as snow.\"\n\n\"You don't know what I can do; snow produces a glow and a tingle, if applied rightly. Your indifference is half affectation, and a good stirring up would prove it.\"\n\n\"Stir away; it won't hurt me and it may amuse you, as the big man said when his little wife beat him. Regard me in the light of a husband or a carpet, and beat till you are tired, if that sort of exercise agrees with you.\"\n\nBeing decidedly nettled herself, and longing to see him shake off the apathy that so altered him, Amy sharpened both tongue and pencil, and began:--\n\n\"Flo and I have got a new name for you; it's 'Lazy Laurence.' How do you like it?\"\n\nShe thought it would annoy him; but he only folded his arms under his head, with an imperturbable \"That's not bad. Thank you, ladies.\"\n\n\"Do you want to know what I honestly think of you?\"\n\n\"Pining to be told.\"\n\n\"Well, I despise you.\"\n\nIf she had even said \"I hate you,\" in a petulant or coquettish tone, he would have laughed, and rather liked it; but the grave, almost sad, accent of her voice made him open his eyes, and ask quickly,--\n\n\"Why, if you please?\"\n\n\"Because, with every chance for being good, useful, and happy, you are faulty, lazy, and miserable.\"\n\n\"Strong language, mademoiselle.\"\n\n\"If you like it, I'll go on.\"\n\n\"Pray, do; it's quite interesting.\"\n\n\"I thought you'd find it so; selfish people always like to talk about themselves.\"\n\n\"Am _I_ selfish?\" The question slipped out involuntarily and in a tone of surprise, for the one virtue on which he prided himself was generosity.\n\n\"Yes, very selfish,\" continued Amy, in a calm, cool voice, twice as effective, just then, as an angry one. \"I'll show you how, for I've studied you while we have been frolicking, and I'm not at all satisfied with you. Here you have been abroad nearly six months, and done nothing but waste time and money and disappoint your friends.\"\n\n\"Isn't a fellow to have any pleasure after a four-years grind?\"\n\n\"You don't look as if you'd had much; at any rate, you are none the better for it, as far as I can see. I said, when we first met, that you had improved. Now I take it all back, for I don't think you half so nice as when I left you at home. You have grown abominably lazy; you like gossip, and waste time on frivolous things; you are contented to be petted and admired by silly people, instead of being loved and respected by wise ones. With money, talent, position, health, and beauty,--ah, you like that, Old Vanity! but it's the truth, so I can't help saying it,--with all these splendid things to use and enjoy, you can find nothing to do but dawdle; and, instead of being the man you might and ought to be, you are only--\" There she stopped, with a look that had both pain and pity in it.\n\n\"Saint Laurence on a gridiron,\" added Laurie, blandly finishing the sentence. But the lecture began to take effect, for there was a wide-awake sparkle in his eyes now, and a half-angry, half-injured expression replaced the former indifference.\n\n\"I supposed you'd take it so. You men tell us we are angels, and say we can make you what we will; but the instant we honestly try to do you good, you laugh at us, and won't listen, which proves how much your flattery is worth.\" Amy spoke bitterly, and turned her back on the exasperating martyr at her feet.\n\nIn a minute a hand came down over the page, so that she could not draw, and Laurie's voice said, with a droll imitation of a penitent child,--\n\n\"I will be good, oh, I will be good!\"\n\nBut Amy did not laugh, for she was in earnest; and, tapping on the outspread hand with her pencil, said soberly,--\n\n\"Aren't you ashamed of a hand like that? It's as soft and white as a woman's, and looks as if it never did anything but wear Jouvin's best gloves, and pick flowers for ladies. You are not a dandy, thank Heaven! so I'm glad to see there are no diamonds or big seal-rings on it, only the little old one Jo gave you so long ago. Dear soul, I wish she was here to help me!\"\n\n\"So do I!\"\n\nThe hand vanished as suddenly as it came, and there was energy enough in the echo of her wish to suit even Amy. She glanced down at him with a new thought in her mind; but he was lying with his hat half over his face, as if for shade, and his mustache hid his mouth. She only saw his chest rise and fall, with a long breath that might have been a sigh, and the hand that wore the ring nestled down into the grass, as if to hide something too precious or too tender to be spoken of. All in a minute various hints and trifles assumed shape and significance in Amy's mind, and told her what her sister never had confided to her. She remembered that Laurie never spoke voluntarily of Jo; she recalled the shadow on his face just now, the change in his character, and the wearing of the little old ring, which was no ornament to a handsome hand. Girls are quick to read such signs and feel their eloquence. Amy had fancied that perhaps a love trouble was at the bottom of the alteration, and now she was sure of it. Her keen eyes filled, and, when she spoke again, it was in a voice that could be beautifully soft and kind when she chose to make it so.\n\n\"I know I have no right to talk so to you, Laurie; and if you weren't the sweetest-tempered fellow in the world, you'd be very angry with me. But we are all so fond and proud of you, I couldn't bear to think they should be disappointed in you at home as I have been, though, perhaps, they would understand the change better than I do.\"\n\n\"I think they would,\" came from under the hat, in a grim tone, quite as touching as a broken one.\n\n\"They ought to have told me, and not let me go blundering and scolding, when I should have been more kind and patient than ever. I never did like that Miss Randal, and now I hate her!\" said artful Amy, wishing to be sure of her facts this time.\n\n\"Hang Miss Randal!\" and Laurie knocked the hat off his face with a look that left no doubt of his sentiments toward that young lady.\n\n\"I beg pardon; I thought--\" and there she paused diplomatically.\n\n\"No, you didn't; you knew perfectly well I never cared for any one but Jo.\" Laurie said that in his old, impetuous tone, and turned his face away as he spoke.\n\n\"I did think so; but as they never said anything about it, and you came away, I supposed I was mistaken. And Jo wouldn't be kind to you? Why, I was sure she loved you dearly.\"\n\n\"She _was_ kind, but not in the right way; and it's lucky for her she didn't love me, if I'm the good-for-nothing fellow you think me. It's her fault, though, and you may tell her so.\"\n\nThe hard, bitter look came back again as he said that, and it troubled Amy, for she did not know what balm to apply.\n\n\"I was wrong, I didn't know. I'm very sorry I was so cross, but I can't help wishing you'd bear it better, Teddy, dear.\"\n\n\"Don't, that's her name for me!\" and Laurie put up his hand with a quick gesture to stop the words spoken in Jo's half-kind, half-reproachful tone. \"Wait till you've tried it yourself,\" he added, in a low voice, as he pulled up the grass by the handful.\n\n\"I'd take it manfully, and be respected if I couldn't be loved,\" said Amy, with the decision of one who knew nothing about it.\n\nNow, Laurie flattered himself that he _had_ borne it remarkably well, making no moan, asking no sympathy, and taking his trouble away to live it down alone. Amy's lecture put the matter in a new light, and for the first time it did look weak and selfish to lose heart at the first failure, and shut himself up in moody indifference. He felt as if suddenly shaken out of a pensive dream, and found it impossible to go to sleep again. Presently he sat up, and asked slowly,--\n\n\"Do you think Jo would despise me as you do?\"\n\n\"Yes, if she saw you now. She hates lazy people. Why don't you do something splendid, and _make_ her love you?\"\n\n\"I did my best, but it was no use.\"\n\n\"Graduating well, you mean? That was no more than you ought to have done, for your grandfather's sake. It would have been shameful to fail after spending so much time and money, when every one knew you _could_ do well.\"\n\n\"I did fail, say what you will, for Jo wouldn't love me,\" began Laurie, leaning his head on his hand in a despondent attitude.\n\n\"No, you didn't, and you'll say so in the end, for it did you good, and proved that you could do something if you tried. If you'd only set about another task of some sort, you'd soon be your hearty, happy self again, and forget your trouble.\"\n\n\"That's impossible.\"\n\n\"Try it and see. You needn't shrug your shoulders, and think, 'Much she knows about such things.' I don't pretend to be wise, but I _am_ observing, and I see a great deal more than you'd imagine. I'm interested in other people's experiences and inconsistencies; and, though I can't explain, I remember and use them for my own benefit. Love Jo all your days, if you choose, but don't let it spoil you, for it's wicked to throw away so many good gifts because you can't have the one you want. There, I won't lecture any more, for I know you'll wake up and be a man in spite of that hardhearted girl.\"\n\nNeither spoke for several minutes. Laurie sat turning the little ring on his finger, and Amy put the last touches to the hasty sketch she had been working at while she talked. Presently she put it on his knee, merely saying,--\n\n\"How do you like that?\"\n\nHe looked and then he smiled, as he could not well help doing, for it was capitally done,--the long, lazy figure on the grass, with listless face, half-shut eyes, and one hand holding a cigar, from which came the little wreath of smoke that encircled the dreamer's head.\n\n\"How well you draw!\" he said, with genuine surprise and pleasure at her skill, adding, with a half-laugh,--\n\n\"Yes, that's me.\"\n\n\"As you are: this is as you were;\" and Amy laid another sketch beside the one he held.\n\nIt was not nearly so well done, but there was a life and spirit in it which atoned for many faults, and it recalled the past so vividly that a sudden change swept over the young man's face as he looked. Only a rough sketch of Laurie taming a horse; hat and coat were off, and every line of the active figure, resolute face, and commanding attitude, was full of energy and meaning. The handsome brute, just subdued, stood arching his neck under the tightly drawn rein, with one foot impatiently pawing the ground, and ears pricked up as if listening for the voice that had mastered him. In the ruffled mane, the rider's breezy hair and erect attitude, there was a suggestion of suddenly arrested motion, of strength, courage, and youthful buoyancy, that contrasted sharply with the supine grace of the \"_Dolce far niente_\" sketch. Laurie said nothing; but, as his eye went from one to the other, Amy saw him flush up and fold his lips together as if he read and accepted the little lesson she had given him. That satisfied her; and, without waiting for him to speak, she said, in her sprightly way,--\n\n\"Don't you remember the day you played Rarey with Puck, and we all looked on? Meg and Beth were frightened, but Jo clapped and pranced, and I sat on the fence and drew you. I found that sketch in my portfolio the other day, touched it up, and kept it to show you.\"\n\n\"Much obliged. You've improved immensely since then, and I congratulate you. May I venture to suggest in 'a honeymoon Paradise' that five o'clock is the dinner-hour at your hotel?\"\n\nLaurie rose as he spoke, returned the pictures with a smile and a bow, and looked at his watch, as if to remind her that even moral lectures should have an end. He tried to resume his former easy, indifferent air, but it _was_ an affectation now, for the rousing had been more efficacious than he would confess. Amy felt the shade of coldness in his manner, and said to herself,--\n\n\"Now I've offended him. Well, if it does him good, I'm glad; if it makes him hate me, I'm sorry; but it's true, and I can't take back a word of it.\"\n\nThey laughed and chatted all the way home; and little Baptiste, up behind, thought that monsieur and mademoiselle were in charming spirits. But both felt ill at ease; the friendly frankness was disturbed, the sunshine had a shadow over it, and despite their apparent gayety, there was a secret discontent in the heart of each.\n\n\"Shall we see you this evening, _mon frère_?\" asked Amy as they parted at her aunt's door.\n\n\"Unfortunately I have an engagement. _Au revoir, mademoiselle_,\" and Laurie bent as if to kiss her hand, in the foreign fashion, which became him better than many men. Something in his face made Amy say quickly and warmly,--\n\n\"No; be yourself with me, Laurie, and part in the good old way. I'd rather have a hearty English hand-shake than all the sentimental salutations in France.\"\n\n\"Good-by, dear,\" and with these words, uttered in the tone she liked, Laurie left her, after a hand-shake almost painful in its heartiness.\n\nNext morning, instead of the usual call, Amy received a note which made her smile at the beginning and sigh at the end:--\n\n\"Please make my adieux to your aunt, and exult within yourself, for 'Lazy Laurence' has gone to his grandpa, like the best of boys. A pleasant winter to you, and may the gods grant you a blissful honeymoon at Valrosa! I think Fred would be benefited by a rouser. Tell him so, with my congratulations.\n\n\"Yours gratefully, TELEMACHUS.\"\n\n\"Good boy! I'm glad he's gone,\" said Amy, with an approving smile; the next minute her face fell as she glanced about the empty room, adding, with an involuntary sigh,--\n\n\"Yes, I _am_ glad, but how I shall miss him!\"\n\n# THE VALLEY OF THE SHADOW.\n\nWhen the first bitterness was over, the family accepted the inevitable, and tried to bear it cheerfully, helping one another by the increased affection which comes to bind households tenderly together in times of trouble. They put away their grief, and each did his or her part toward making that last year a happy one.\n\nThe pleasantest room in the house was set apart for Beth, and in it was gathered everything that she most loved,--flowers, pictures, her piano, the little work-table, and the beloved pussies. Father's best books found their way there, mother's easy-chair, Jo's desk, Amy's finest sketches; and every day Meg brought her babies on a loving pilgrimage, to make sunshine for Aunty Beth. John quietly set apart a little sum, that he might enjoy the pleasure of keeping the invalid supplied with the fruit she loved and longed for; old Hannah never wearied of concocting dainty dishes to tempt a capricious appetite, dropping tears as she worked; and from across the sea came little gifts and cheerful letters, seeming to bring breaths of warmth and fragrance from lands that know no winter.\n\nHere, cherished like a household saint in its shrine, sat Beth, tranquil and busy as ever; for nothing could change the sweet, unselfish nature, and even while preparing to leave life, she tried to make it happier for those who should remain behind. The feeble fingers were never idle, and one of her pleasures was to make little things for the school-children daily passing to and fro,--to drop a pair of mittens from her window for a pair of purple hands, a needle-book for some small mother of many dolls, pen-wipers for young penmen toiling through forests of pot-hooks, scrap-books for picture-loving eyes, and all manner of pleasant devices, till the reluctant climbers up the ladder of learning found their way strewn with flowers, as it were, and came to regard the gentle giver as a sort of fairy godmother, who sat above there, and showered down gifts miraculously suited to their tastes and needs. If Beth had wanted any reward, she found it in the bright little faces always turned up to her window, with nods and smiles, and the droll little letters which came to her, full of blots and gratitude.\n\nThe first few months were very happy ones, and Beth often used to look round, and say \"How beautiful this is!\" as they all sat together in her sunny room, the babies kicking and crowing on the floor, mother and sisters working near, and father reading, in his pleasant voice, from the wise old books which seemed rich in good and comfortable words, as applicable now as when written centuries ago; a little chapel, where a paternal priest taught his flock the hard lessons all must learn, trying to show them that hope can comfort love, and faith make resignation possible. Simple sermons, that went straight to the souls of those who listened; for the father's heart was in the minister's religion, and the frequent falter in the voice gave a double eloquence to the words he spoke or read.\n\nIt was well for all that this peaceful time was given them as preparation for the sad hours to come; for, by and by, Beth said the needle was \"so heavy,\" and put it down forever; talking wearied her, faces troubled her, pain claimed her for its own, and her tranquil spirit was sorrowfully perturbed by the ills that vexed her feeble flesh. Ah me! such heavy days, such long, long nights, such aching hearts and imploring prayers, when those who loved her best were forced to see the thin hands stretched out to them beseechingly, to hear the bitter cry, \"Help me, help me!\" and to feel that there was no help. A sad eclipse of the serene soul, a sharp struggle of the young life with death; but both were mercifully brief, and then, the natural rebellion over, the old peace returned more beautiful than ever. With the wreck of her frail body, Beth's soul grew strong; and, though she said little, those about her felt that she was ready, saw that the first pilgrim called was likewise the fittest, and waited with her on the shore, trying to see the Shining Ones coming to receive her when she crossed the river.\n\nJo never left her for an hour since Beth had said, \"I feel stronger when you are here.\" She slept on a couch in the room, waking often to renew the fire, to feed, lift, or wait upon the patient creature who seldom asked for anything, and \"tried not to be a trouble.\" All day she haunted the room, jealous of any other nurse, and prouder of being chosen then than of any honor her life ever brought her. Precious and helpful hours to Jo, for now her heart received the teaching that it needed; lessons in patience were so sweetly taught her that she could not fail to learn them; charity for all, the lovely spirit that can forgive and truly forget unkindness, the loyalty to duty that makes the hardest easy, and the sincere faith that fears nothing, but trusts undoubtingly.\n\nOften, when she woke, Jo found Beth reading in her well-worn little book, heard her singing softly, to beguile the sleepless night, or saw her lean her face upon her hands, while slow tears dropped through the transparent fingers; and Jo would lie watching her, with thoughts too deep for tears, feeling that Beth, in her simple, unselfish way, was trying to wean herself from the dear old life, and fit herself for the life to come, by sacred words of comfort, quiet prayers, and the music she loved so well.\n\nSeeing this did more for Jo than the wisest sermons, the saintliest hymns, the most fervent prayers that any voice could utter; for, with eyes made clear by many tears, and a heart softened by the tenderest sorrow, she recognized the beauty of her sister's life,--uneventful, unambitious, yet full of the genuine virtues which \"smell sweet, and blossom in the dust,\" the self-forgetfulness that makes the humblest on earth remembered soonest in heaven, the true success which is possible to all.\n\nOne night, when Beth looked among the books upon her table, to find something to make her forget the mortal weariness that was almost as hard to bear as pain, as she turned the leaves of her old favorite Pilgrim's Progress, she found a little paper, scribbled over in Jo's hand. The name caught her eye, and the blurred look of the lines made her sure that tears had fallen on it.\n\n\"Poor Jo! she's fast asleep, so I won't wake her to ask leave; she shows me all her things, and I don't think she'll mind if I look at this,\" thought Beth, with a glance at her sister, who lay on the rug, with the tongs beside her, ready to wake up the minute the log fell apart.\n\n\"Sitting patient in the shadow Till the blessed light shall come, A serene and saintly presence Sanctifies our troubled home. Earthly joys and hopes and sorrows Break like ripples on the strand Of the deep and solemn river Where her willing feet now stand.\n\n\"O my sister, passing from me, Out of human care and strife, Leave me, as a gift, those virtues Which have beautified your life. Dear, bequeath me that great patience Which has power to sustain A cheerful, uncomplaining spirit In its prison-house of pain.\n\n\"Give me, for I need it sorely, Of that courage, wise and sweet, Which has made the path of duty Green beneath your willing feet. Give me that unselfish nature, That with charity divine Can pardon wrong for love's dear sake-- Meek heart, forgive me mine!\n\n\"Thus our parting daily loseth Something of its bitter pain, And while learning this hard lesson, My great loss becomes my gain. For the touch of grief will render My wild nature more serene, Give to life new aspirations, A new trust in the unseen.\n\n\"Henceforth, safe across the river, I shall see forevermore A beloved, household spirit Waiting for me on the shore. Hope and faith, born of my sorrow, Guardian angels shall become, And the sister gone before me By their hands shall lead me home.\"\n\nBlurred and blotted, faulty and feeble, as the lines were, they brought a look of inexpressible comfort to Beth's face, for her one regret had been that she had done so little; and this seemed to assure her that her life had not been useless, that her death would not bring the despair she feared. As she sat with the paper folded between her hands, the charred log fell asunder. Jo started up, revived the blaze, and crept to the bedside, hoping Beth slept.\n\n\"Not asleep, but so happy, dear. See, I found this and read it; I knew you wouldn't care. Have I been all that to you, Jo?\" she asked, with wistful, humble earnestness.\n\n\"O Beth, so much, so much!\" and Jo's head went down upon the pillow, beside her sister's.\n\n\"Then I don't feel as if I'd wasted my life. I'm not so good as you make me, but I _have_ tried to do right; and now, when it's too late to begin even to do better, it's such a comfort to know that some one loves me so much, and feels as if I'd helped them.\"\n\n\"More than any one in the world, Beth. I used to think I couldn't let you go; but I'm learning to feel that I don't lose you; that you'll be more to me than ever, and death can't part us, though it seems to.\"\n\n\"I know it cannot, and I don't fear it any longer, for I'm sure I shall be your Beth still, to love and help you more than ever. You must take my place, Jo, and be everything to father and mother when I'm gone. They will turn to you, don't fail them; and if it's hard to work alone, remember that I don't forget you, and that you'll be happier in doing that than writing splendid books or seeing all the world; for love is the only thing that we can carry with us when we go, and it makes the end so easy.\"\n\n\"I'll try, Beth;\" and then and there Jo renounced her old ambition, pledged herself to a new and better one, acknowledging the poverty of other desires, and feeling the blessed solace of a belief in the immortality of love.\n\nSo the spring days came and went, the sky grew clearer, the earth greener, the flowers were up fair and early, and the birds came back in time to say good-by to Beth, who, like a tired but trustful child, clung to the hands that had led her all her life, as father and mother guided her tenderly through the Valley of the Shadow, and gave her up to God.\n\nSeldom, except in books, do the dying utter memorable words, see visions, or depart with beatified countenances; and those who have sped many parting souls know that to most the end comes as naturally and simply as sleep. As Beth had hoped, the \"tide went out easily;\" and in the dark hour before the dawn, on the bosom where she had drawn her first breath, she quietly drew her last, with no farewell but one loving look, one little sigh.\n\nWith tears and prayers and tender hands, mother and sisters made her ready for the long sleep that pain would never mar again, seeing with grateful eyes the beautiful serenity that soon replaced the pathetic patience that had wrung their hearts so long, and feeling, with reverent joy, that to their darling death was a benignant angel, not a phantom full of dread.\n\nWhen morning came, for the first time in many months the fire was out, Jo's place was empty, and the room was very still. But a bird sang blithely on a budding bough, close by, the snow-drops blossomed freshly at the window, and the spring sunshine streamed in like a benediction over the placid face upon the pillow,--a face so full of painless peace that those who loved it best smiled through their tears, and thanked God that Beth was well at last.\n\n# LEARNING TO FORGET.\n\nAmy's lecture did Laurie good, though, of course, he did not own it till long afterward; men seldom do, for when women are the advisers, the lords of creation don't take the advice till they have persuaded themselves that it is just what they intended to do; then they act upon it, and, if it succeeds, they give the weaker vessel half the credit of it; if it fails, they generously give her the whole. Laurie went back to his grandfather, and was so dutifully devoted for several weeks that the old gentleman declared the climate of Nice had improved him wonderfully, and he had better try it again. There was nothing the young gentleman would have liked better, but elephants could not have dragged him back after the scolding he had received; pride forbid, and whenever the longing grew very strong, he fortified his resolution by repeating the words that had made the deepest impression, \"I despise you;\" \"Go and do something splendid that will _make_ her love you.\"\n\nLaurie turned the matter over in his mind so often that he soon brought himself to confess that he _had_ been selfish and lazy; but then when a man has a great sorrow, he should be indulged in all sorts of vagaries till he has lived it down. He felt that his blighted affections were quite dead now; and, though he should never cease to be a faithful mourner, there was no occasion to wear his weeds ostentatiously. Jo _wouldn't_ love him, but he might _make_ her respect and admire him by doing something which should prove that a girl's \"No\" had not spoilt his life. He had always meant to do something, and Amy's advice was quite unnecessary. He had only been waiting till the aforesaid blighted affections were decently interred; that being done, he felt that he was ready to \"hide his stricken heart, and still toil on.\"\n\nAs Goethe, when he had a joy or a grief, put it into a song, so Laurie resolved to embalm his love-sorrow in music, and compose a Requiem which should harrow up Jo's soul and melt the heart of every hearer. Therefore the next time the old gentleman found him getting restless and moody, and ordered him off, he went to Vienna, where he had musical friends, and fell to work with the firm determination to distinguish himself. But, whether the sorrow was too vast to be embodied in music, or music too ethereal to uplift a mortal woe, he soon discovered that the Requiem was beyond him, just at present. It was evident that his mind was not in working order yet, and his ideas needed clarifying; for often in the middle of a plaintive strain, he would find himself humming a dancing tune that vividly recalled the Christmas ball at Nice, especially the stout Frenchman, and put an effectual stop to tragic composition for the time being.\n\nThen he tried an Opera, for nothing seemed impossible in the beginning; but here, again, unforeseen difficulties beset him. He wanted Jo for his heroine, and called upon his memory to supply him with tender recollections and romantic visions of his love. But memory turned traitor; and, as if possessed by the perverse spirit of the girl, would only recall Jo's oddities, faults, and freaks, would only show her in the most unsentimental aspects,--beating mats with her head tied up in a bandanna, barricading herself with the sofa-pillow, or throwing cold water over his passion _à la_ Gummidge,--and an irresistible laugh spoilt the pensive picture he was endeavoring to paint. Jo wouldn't be put into the Opera at any price, and he had to give her up with a \"Bless that girl, what a torment she is!\" and a clutch at his hair, as became a distracted composer.\n\nWhen he looked about him for another and a less intractable damsel to immortalize in melody, memory produced one with the most obliging readiness. This phantom wore many faces, but it always had golden hair, was enveloped in a diaphanous cloud, and floated airily before his mind's eye in a pleasing chaos of roses, peacocks, white ponies, and blue ribbons. He did not give the complacent wraith any name, but he took her for his heroine, and grew quite fond of her, as well he might; for he gifted her with every gift and grace under the sun, and escorted her, unscathed, through trials which would have annihilated any mortal woman.\n\nThanks to this inspiration, he got on swimmingly for a time, but gradually the work lost its charm, and he forgot to compose, while he sat musing, pen in hand, or roamed about the gay city to get new ideas and refresh his mind, which seemed to be in a somewhat unsettled state that winter. He did not do much, but he thought a great deal and was conscious of a change of some sort going on in spite of himself. \"It's genius simmering, perhaps. I'll let it simmer, and see what comes of it,\" he said, with a secret suspicion, all the while, that it wasn't genius, but something far more common. Whatever it was, it simmered to some purpose, for he grew more and more discontented with his desultory life, began to long for some real and earnest work to go at, soul and body, and finally came to the wise conclusion that every one who loved music was not a composer. Returning from one of Mozart's grand operas, splendidly performed at the Royal Theatre, he looked over his own, played a few of the best parts, sat staring up at the busts of Mendelssohn, Beethoven, and Bach, who stared benignly back again; then suddenly he tore up his music-sheets, one by one, and, as the last fluttered out of his hand, he said soberly to himself,--\n\n\"She is right! Talent isn't genius, and you can't make it so. That music has taken the vanity out of me as Rome took it out of her, and I won't be a humbug any longer. Now what shall I do?\"\n\nThat seemed a hard question to answer, and Laurie began to wish he had to work for his daily bread. Now, if ever, occurred an eligible opportunity for \"going to the devil,\" as he once forcibly expressed it, for he had plenty of money and nothing to do, and Satan is proverbially fond of providing employment for full and idle hands. The poor fellow had temptations enough from without and from within, but he withstood them pretty well; for, much as he valued liberty, he valued good faith and confidence more, so his promise to his grandfather, and his desire to be able to look honestly into the eyes of the women who loved him, and say \"All's well,\" kept him safe and steady.\n\nVery likely some Mrs. Grundy will observe, \"I don't believe it; boys will be boys, young men must sow their wild oats, and women must not expect miracles.\" I dare say _you_ don't, Mrs. Grundy, but it's true nevertheless. Women work a good many miracles, and I have a persuasion that they may perform even that of raising the standard of manhood by refusing to echo such sayings. Let the boys be boys, the longer the better, and let the young men sow their wild oats if they must; but mothers, sisters, and friends may help to make the crop a small one, and keep many tares from spoiling the harvest, by believing, and showing that they believe, in the possibility of loyalty to the virtues which make men manliest in good women's eyes. If it _is_ a feminine delusion, leave us to enjoy it while we may, for without it half the beauty and the romance of life is lost, and sorrowful forebodings would embitter all our hopes of the brave, tender-hearted little lads, who still love their mothers better than themselves, and are not ashamed to own it.\n\nLaurie thought that the task of forgetting his love for Jo would absorb all his powers for years; but, to his great surprise, he discovered it grew easier every day. He refused to believe it at first, got angry with himself, and couldn't understand it; but these hearts of ours are curious and contrary things, and time and nature work their will in spite of us. Laurie's heart _wouldn't_ ache; the wound persisted in healing with a rapidity that astonished him, and, instead of trying to forget, he found himself trying to remember. He had not foreseen this turn of affairs, and was not prepared for it. He was disgusted with himself, surprised at his own fickleness, and full of a queer mixture of disappointment and relief that he could recover from such a tremendous blow so soon. He carefully stirred up the embers of his lost love, but they refused to burst into a blaze: there was only a comfortable glow that warmed and did him good without putting him into a fever, and he was reluctantly obliged to confess that the boyish passion was slowly subsiding into a more tranquil sentiment, very tender, a little sad and resentful still, but that was sure to pass away in time, leaving a brotherly affection which would last unbroken to the end.\n\nAs the word \"brotherly\" passed through his mind in one of these reveries, he smiled, and glanced up at the picture of Mozart that was before him:--\n\n\"Well, he was a great man; and when he couldn't have one sister he took the other, and was happy.\"\n\nLaurie did not utter the words, but he thought them; and the next instant kissed the little old ring, saying to himself,--\n\n\"No, I won't! I haven't forgotten, I never can. I'll try again, and if that fails, why, then--\"\n\nLeaving his sentence unfinished, he seized pen and paper and wrote to Jo, telling her that he could not settle to anything while there was the least hope of her changing her mind. Couldn't she, wouldn't she, and let him come home and be happy? While waiting for an answer he did nothing, but he did it energetically, for he was in a fever of impatience. It came at last, and settled his mind effectually on one point, for Jo decidedly couldn't and wouldn't. She was wrapped up in Beth, and never wished to hear the word \"love\" again. Then she begged him to be happy with somebody else, but always to keep a little corner of his heart for his loving sister Jo. In a postscript she desired him not to tell Amy that Beth was worse; she was coming home in the spring, and there was no need of saddening the remainder of her stay. That would be time enough, please God, but Laurie must write to her often, and not let her feel lonely, homesick, or anxious.\n\n\"So I will, at once. Poor little girl; it will be a sad going home for her, I'm afraid;\" and Laurie opened his desk, as if writing to Amy had been the proper conclusion of the sentence left unfinished some weeks before.\n\nBut he did not write the letter that day; for, as he rummaged out his best paper, he came across something which changed his purpose. Tumbling about in one part of the desk, among bills, passports, and business documents of various kinds, were several of Jo's letters, and in another compartment were three notes from Amy, carefully tied up with one of her blue ribbons, and sweetly suggestive of the little dead roses put away inside. With a half-repentant, half-amused expression, Laurie gathered up all Jo's letters, smoothed, folded, and put them neatly into a small drawer of the desk, stood a minute turning the ring thoughtfully on his finger, then slowly drew it off, laid it with the letters, locked the drawer, and went out to hear High Mass at Saint Stefan's, feeling as if there had been a funeral; and, though not overwhelmed with affliction, this seemed a more proper way to spend the rest of the day than in writing letters to charming young ladies.\n\nThe letter went very soon, however, and was promptly answered, for Amy _was_ homesick, and confessed it in the most delightfully confiding manner. The correspondence flourished famously, and letters flew to and fro, with unfailing regularity, all through the early spring. Laurie sold his busts, made allumettes of his opera, and went back to Paris, hoping somebody would arrive before long. He wanted desperately to go to Nice, but would not till he was asked; and Amy would not ask him, for just then she was having little experiences of her own, which made her rather wish to avoid the quizzical eyes of \"our boy.\"\n\nFred Vaughn had returned, and put the question to which she had once decided to answer \"Yes, thank you;\" but now she said, \"No, thank you,\" kindly but steadily; for, when the time came, her courage failed her, and she found that something more than money and position was needed to satisfy the new longing that filled her heart so full of tender hopes and fears. The words, \"Fred is a good fellow, but not at all the man I fancied you would ever like,\" and Laurie's face when he uttered them, kept returning to her as pertinaciously as her own did when she said in look, if not in words, \"I shall marry for money.\" It troubled her to remember that now, she wished she could take it back, it sounded so unwomanly. She didn't want Laurie to think her a heartless, worldly creature; she didn't care to be a queen of society now half so much as she did to be a lovable woman; she was so glad he didn't hate her for the dreadful things she said, but took them so beautifully, and was kinder than ever. His letters were such a comfort, for the home letters were very irregular, and were not half so satisfactory as his when they did come. It was not only a pleasure, but a duty to answer them, for the poor fellow was forlorn, and needed petting, since Jo persisted in being stony-hearted. She ought to have made an effort, and tried to love him; it couldn't be very hard, many people would be proud and glad to have such a dear boy care for them; but Jo never would act like other girls, so there was nothing to do but be very kind, and treat him like a brother.\n\nIf all brothers were treated as well as Laurie was at this period, they would be a much happier race of beings than they are. Amy never lectured now; she asked his opinion on all subjects; she was interested in everything he did, made charming little presents for him, and sent him two letters a week, full of lively gossip, sisterly confidences, and captivating sketches of the lovely scenes about her. As few brothers are complimented by having their letters carried about in their sisters' pockets, read and reread diligently, cried over when short, kissed when long, and treasured carefully, we will not hint that Amy did any of these fond and foolish things. But she certainly did grow a little pale and pensive that spring, lost much of her relish for society, and went out sketching alone a good deal. She never had much to show when she came home, but was studying nature, I dare say, while she sat for hours, with her hands folded, on the terrace at Valrosa, or absently sketched any fancy that occurred to her,--a stalwart knight carved on a tomb, a young man asleep in the grass, with his hat over his eyes, or a curly-haired girl in gorgeous array, promenading down a ball-room on the arm of a tall gentleman, both faces being left a blur according to the last fashion in art, which was safe, but not altogether satisfactory.\n\nHer aunt thought that she regretted her answer to Fred; and, finding denials useless and explanations impossible, Amy left her to think what she liked, taking care that Laurie should know that Fred had gone to Egypt. That was all, but he understood it, and looked relieved, as he said to himself, with a venerable air,--\n\n\"I was sure she would think better of it. Poor old fellow! I've been through it all, and I can sympathize.\"\n\nWith that he heaved a great sigh, and then, as if he had discharged his duty to the past, put his feet up on the sofa, and enjoyed Amy's letter luxuriously.\n\nWhile these changes were going on abroad, trouble had come at home; but the letter telling that Beth was failing never reached Amy, and when the next found her, the grass was green above her sister. The sad news met her at Vevay, for the heat had driven them from Nice in May, and they had travelled slowly to Switzerland, by way of Genoa and the Italian lakes. She bore it very well, and quietly submitted to the family decree that she should not shorten her visit, for, since it was too late to say good-by to Beth, she had better stay, and let absence soften her sorrow. But her heart was very heavy; she longed to be at home, and every day looked wistfully across the lake, waiting for Laurie to come and comfort her.\n\nHe did come very soon; for the same mail brought letters to them both, but he was in Germany, and it took some days to reach him. The moment he read it, he packed his knapsack, bade adieu to his fellow-pedestrians, and was off to keep his promise, with a heart full of joy and sorrow, hope and suspense.\n\nHe knew Vevay well; and as soon as the boat touched the little quay, he hurried along the shore to La Tour, where the Carrols were living _en pension_. The _garçon_ was in despair that the whole family had gone to take a promenade on the lake; but no, the blond mademoiselle might be in the chateau garden. If monsieur would give himself the pain of sitting down, a flash of time should present her. But monsieur could not wait even \"a flash of time,\" and, in the middle of the speech, departed to find mademoiselle himself.\n\nA pleasant old garden on the borders of the lovely lake, with chestnuts rustling overhead, ivy climbing everywhere, and the black shadow of the tower falling far across the sunny water. At one corner of the wide, low wall was a seat, and here Amy often came to read or work, or console herself with the beauty all about her. She was sitting here that day, leaning her head on her hand, with a homesick heart and heavy eyes, thinking of Beth, and wondering why Laurie did not come. She did not hear him cross the court-yard beyond, nor see him pause in the archway that led from the subterranean path into the garden. He stood a minute, looking at her with new eyes, seeing what no one had ever seen before,--the tender side of Amy's character. Everything about her mutely suggested love and sorrow,--the blotted letters in her lap, the black ribbon that tied up her hair, the womanly pain and patience in her face; even the little ebony cross at her throat seemed pathetic to Laurie, for he had given it to her, and she wore it as her only ornament. If he had any doubts about the reception she would give him, they were set at rest the minute she looked up and saw him; for, dropping everything, she ran to him, exclaiming, in a tone of unmistakable love and longing,--\n\n\"O Laurie, Laurie, I knew you'd come to me!\"\n\nI think everything was said and settled then; for, as they stood together quite silent for a moment, with the dark head bent down protectingly over the light one, Amy felt that no one could comfort and sustain her so well as Laurie, and Laurie decided that Amy was the only woman in the world who could fill Jo's place, and make him happy. He did not tell her so; but she was not disappointed, for both felt the truth, were satisfied, and gladly left the rest to silence.\n\nIn a minute Amy went back to her place; and, while she dried her tears, Laurie gathered up the scattered papers, finding in the sight of sundry well-worn letters and suggestive sketches good omens for the future. As he sat down beside her, Amy felt shy again, and turned rosy red at the recollection of her impulsive greeting.\n\n\"I couldn't help it; I felt so lonely and sad, and was so very glad to see you. It was such a surprise to look up and find you, just as I was beginning to fear you wouldn't come,\" she said, trying in vain to speak quite naturally.\n\n\"I came the minute I heard. I wish I could say something to comfort you for the loss of dear little Beth; but I can only feel, and--\" He could not get any further, for he, too, turned bashful all of a sudden, and did not quite know what to say. He longed to lay Amy's head down on his shoulder, and tell her to have a good cry, but he did not dare; so took her hand instead, and gave it a sympathetic squeeze that was better than words.\n\n\"You needn't say anything; this comforts me,\" she said softly. \"Beth is well and happy, and I mustn't wish her back; but I dread the going home, much as I long to see them all. We won't talk about it now, for it makes me cry, and I want to enjoy you while you stay. You needn't go right back, need you?\"\n\n\"Not if you want me, dear.\"\n\n\"I do, so much. Aunt and Flo are very kind; but you seem like one of the family, and it would be so comfortable to have you for a little while.\"\n\nAmy spoke and looked so like a homesick child, whose heart was full, that Laurie forgot his bashfulness all at once, and gave her just what she wanted,--the petting she was used to and the cheerful conversation she needed.\n\n\"Poor little soul, you look as if you'd grieved yourself half-sick! I'm going to take care of you, so don't cry any more, but come and walk about with me; the wind is too chilly for you to sit still,\" he said, in the half-caressing, half-commanding way that Amy liked, as he tied on her hat, drew her arm through his, and began to pace up and down the sunny walk, under the new-leaved chestnuts. He felt more at ease upon his legs; and Amy found it very pleasant to have a strong arm to lean upon, a familiar face to smile at her, and a kind voice to talk delightfully for her alone.\n\nThe quaint old garden had sheltered many pairs of lovers, and seemed expressly made for them, so sunny and secluded was it, with nothing but the tower to overlook them, and the wide lake to carry away the echo of their words, as it rippled by below. For an hour this new pair walked and talked, or rested on the wall, enjoying the sweet influences which gave such a charm to time and place; and when an unromantic dinner-bell warned them away, Amy felt as if she left her burden of loneliness and sorrow behind her in the chateau garden.\n\nThe moment Mrs. Carrol saw the girl's altered face, she was illuminated with a new idea, and exclaimed to herself, \"Now I understand it all,--the child has been pining for young Laurence. Bless my heart, I never thought of such a thing!\"\n\nWith praiseworthy discretion, the good lady said nothing, and betrayed no sign of enlightenment; but cordially urged Laurie to stay, and begged Amy to enjoy his society, for it would do her more good than so much solitude. Amy was a model of docility; and, as her aunt was a good deal occupied with Flo, she was left to entertain her friend, and did it with more than her usual success.\n\nAt Nice, Laurie had lounged and Amy had scolded; at Vevay, Laurie was never idle, but always walking, riding, boating, or studying, in the most energetic manner, while Amy admired everything he did, and followed his example as far and as fast as she could. He said the change was owing to the climate, and she did not contradict him, being glad of a like excuse for her own recovered health and spirits.\n\nThe invigorating air did them both good, and much exercise worked wholesome changes in minds as well as bodies. They seemed to get clearer views of life and duty up there among the everlasting hills; the fresh winds blew away desponding doubts, delusive fancies, and moody mists; the warm spring sunshine brought out all sorts of aspiring ideas, tender hopes, and happy thoughts; the lake seemed to wash away the troubles of the past, and the grand old mountains to look benignly down upon them, saying, \"Little children, love one another.\"\n\nIn spite of the new sorrow, it was a very happy time, so happy that Laurie could not bear to disturb it by a word. It took him a little while to recover from his surprise at the rapid cure of his first, and, as he had firmly believed, his last and only love. He consoled himself for the seeming disloyalty by the thought that Jo's sister was almost the same as Jo's self, and the conviction that it would have been impossible to love any other woman but Amy so soon and so well. His first wooing had been of the tempestuous order, and he looked back upon it as if through a long vista of years, with a feeling of compassion blended with regret. He was not ashamed of it, but put it away as one of the bitter-sweet experiences of his life, for which he could be grateful when the pain was over. His second wooing he resolved should be as calm and simple as possible; there was no need of having a scene, hardly any need of telling Amy that he loved her; she knew it without words, and had given him his answer long ago. It all came about so naturally that no one could complain, and he knew that everybody would be pleased, even Jo. But when our first little passion has been crushed, we are apt to be wary and slow in making a second trial; so Laurie let the days pass, enjoying every hour, and leaving to chance the utterance of the word that would put an end to the first and sweetest part of his new romance.\n\nHe had rather imagined that the _dénouement_ would take place in the chateau garden by moonlight, and in the most graceful and decorous manner; but it turned out exactly the reverse, for the matter was settled on the lake, at noonday, in a few blunt words. They had been floating about all the morning, from gloomy St. Gingolf to sunny Montreux, with the Alps of Savoy on one side, Mont St. Bernard and the Dent du Midi on the other, pretty Vevay in the valley, and Lausanne upon the hill beyond, a cloudless blue sky overhead, and the bluer lake below, dotted with the picturesque boats that look like white-winged gulls.\n\nThey had been talking of Bonnivard, as they glided past Chillon, and of Rousseau, as they looked up at Clarens, where he wrote his \"Héloise.\" Neither had read it, but they knew it was a love-story, and each privately wondered if it was half as interesting as their own. Amy had been dabbling her hand in the water during the little pause that fell between them, and, when she looked up, Laurie was leaning on his oars, with an expression in his eyes that made her say hastily, merely for the sake of saying something,--\n\n\"You must be tired; rest a little, and let me row; it will do me good; for, since you came, I have been altogether lazy and luxurious.\"\n\n\"I'm not tired; but you may take an oar, if you like. There's room enough, though I have to sit nearly in the middle, else the boat won't trim,\" returned Laurie, as if he rather liked the arrangement.\n\nFeeling that she had not mended matters much, Amy took the offered third of a seat, shook her hair over her face, and accepted an oar. She rowed as well as she did many other things; and, though she used both hands, and Laurie but one, the oars kept time, and the boat went smoothly through the water.\n\n\"How well we pull together, don't we?\" said Amy, who objected to silence just then.\n\n\"So well that I wish we might always pull in the same boat. Will you, Amy?\" very tenderly.\n\n\"Yes, Laurie,\" very low.\n\nThen they both stopped rowing, and unconsciously added a pretty little _tableau_ of human love and happiness to the dissolving views reflected in the lake.\n\n# ALL ALONE.\n\nIt was easy to promise self-abnegation when self was wrapped up in another, and heart and soul were purified by a sweet example; but when the helpful voice was silent, the daily lesson over, the beloved presence gone, and nothing remained but loneliness and grief, then Jo found her promise very hard to keep. How could she \"comfort father and mother,\" when her own heart ached with a ceaseless longing for her sister; how could she \"make the house cheerful,\" when all its light and warmth and beauty seemed to have deserted it when Beth left the old home for the new; and where in all the world could she \"find some useful, happy work to do,\" that would take the place of the loving service which had been its own reward? She tried in a blind, hopeless way to do her duty, secretly rebelling against it all the while, for it seemed unjust that her few joys should be lessened, her burdens made heavier, and life get harder and harder as she toiled along. Some people seemed to get all sunshine, and some all shadow; it was not fair, for she tried more than Amy to be good, but never got any reward, only disappointment, trouble, and hard work.\n\nPoor Jo, these were dark days to her, for something like despair came over her when she thought of spending all her life in that quiet house, devoted to humdrum cares, a few small pleasures, and the duty that never seemed to grow any easier. \"I can't do it. I wasn't meant for a life like this, and I know I shall break away and do something desperate if somebody don't come and help me,\" she said to herself, when her first efforts failed, and she fell into the moody, miserable state of mind which often comes when strong wills have to yield to the inevitable.\n\nBut some one did come and help her, though Jo did not recognize her good angels at once, because they wore familiar shapes, and used the simple spells best fitted to poor humanity. Often she started up at night, thinking Beth called her; and when the sight of the little empty bed made her cry with the bitter cry of an unsubmissive sorrow, \"O Beth, come back! come back!\" she did not stretch out her yearning arms in vain; for, as quick to hear her sobbing as she had been to hear her sister's faintest whisper, her mother came to comfort her, not with words only, but the patient tenderness that soothes by a touch, tears that were mute reminders of a greater grief than Jo's, and broken whispers, more eloquent than prayers, because hopeful resignation went hand-in-hand with natural sorrow. Sacred moments, when heart talked to heart in the silence of the night, turning affliction to a blessing, which chastened grief and strengthened love. Feeling this, Jo's burden seemed easier to bear, duty grew sweeter, and life looked more endurable, seen from the safe shelter of her mother's arms.\n\nWhen aching heart was a little comforted, troubled mind likewise found help; for one day she went to the study, and, leaning over the good gray head lifted to welcome her with a tranquil smile, she said, very humbly,--\n\n\"Father, talk to me as you did to Beth. I need it more than she did, for I'm all wrong.\"\n\n\"My dear, nothing can comfort me like this,\" he answered, with a falter in his voice, and both arms round her, as if he, too, needed help, and did not fear to ask it.\n\nThen, sitting in Beth's little chair close beside him, Jo told her troubles,--the resentful sorrow for her loss, the fruitless efforts that discouraged her, the want of faith that made life look so dark, and all the sad bewilderment which we call despair. She gave him entire confidence, he gave her the help she needed, and both found consolation in the act; for the time had come when they could talk together not only as father and daughter, but as man and woman, able and glad to serve each other with mutual sympathy as well as mutual love. Happy, thoughtful times there in the old study which Jo called \"the church of one member,\" and from which she came with fresh courage, recovered cheerfulness, and a more submissive spirit; for the parents who had taught one child to meet death without fear, were trying now to teach another to accept life without despondency or distrust, and to use its beautiful opportunities with gratitude and power.\n\nOther helps had Jo,--humble, wholesome duties and delights that would not be denied their part in serving her, and which she slowly learned to see and value. Brooms and dishcloths never could be as distasteful as they once had been, for Beth had presided over both; and something of her housewifely spirit seemed to linger round the little mop and the old brush, that was never thrown away. As she used them, Jo found herself humming the songs Beth used to hum, imitating Beth's orderly ways, and giving the little touches here and there that kept everything fresh and cosey, which was the first step toward making home happy, though she didn't know it, till Hannah said with an approving squeeze of the hand,--\n\n\"You thoughtful creter, you're determined we sha'n't miss that dear lamb ef you can help it. We don't say much, but we see it, and the Lord will bless you for't, see ef He don't.\"\n\nAs they sat sewing together, Jo discovered how much improved her sister Meg was; how well she could talk, how much she knew about good, womanly impulses, thoughts, and feelings, how happy she was in husband and children, and how much they were all doing for each other.\n\n\"Marriage is an excellent thing, after all. I wonder if I should blossom out half as well as you have, if I tried it?\" said Jo, as she constructed a kite for Demi, in the topsy-turvy nursery.\n\n\"It's just what you need to bring out the tender, womanly half of your nature, Jo. You are like a chestnut-burr, prickly outside, but silky-soft within, and a sweet kernel, if one can only get at it. Love will make you show your heart some day, and then the rough burr will fall off.\"\n\n\"Frost opens chestnut-burrs, ma'am, and it takes a good shake to bring them down. Boys go nutting, and I don't care to be bagged by them,\" returned Jo, pasting away at the kite which no wind that blows would ever carry up, for Daisy had tied herself on as a bob.\n\nMeg laughed, for she was glad to see a glimmer of Jo's old spirit, but she felt it her duty to enforce her opinion by every argument in her power; and the sisterly chats were not wasted, especially as two of Meg's most effective arguments were the babies, whom Jo loved tenderly. Grief is the best opener for some hearts, and Jo's was nearly ready for the bag: a little more sunshine to ripen the nut, then, not a boy's impatient shake, but a man's hand reached up to pick it gently from the burr, and find the kernel sound and sweet. If she had suspected this, she would have shut up tight, and been more prickly than ever; fortunately she wasn't thinking about herself, so, when the time came, down she dropped.\n\nNow, if she had been the heroine of a moral story-book, she ought at this period of her life to have become quite saintly, renounced the world, and gone about doing good in a mortified bonnet, with tracts in her pocket. But, you see, Jo wasn't a heroine; she was only a struggling human girl, like hundreds of others, and she just acted out her nature, being sad, cross, listless, or energetic, as the mood suggested. It's highly virtuous to say we'll be good, but we can't do it all at once, and it takes a long pull, a strong pull, and a pull all together, before some of us even get our feet set in the right way. Jo had got so far, she was learning to do her duty, and to feel unhappy if she did not; but to do it cheerfully--ah, that was another thing! She had often said she wanted to do something splendid, no matter how hard; and now she had her wish, for what could be more beautiful than to devote her life to father and mother, trying to make home as happy to them as they had to her? And, if difficulties were necessary to increase the splendor of the effort, what could be harder for a restless, ambitious girl than to give up her own hopes, plans, and desires, and cheerfully live for others?\n\nProvidence had taken her at her word; here was the task, not what she had expected, but better, because self had no part in it: now, could she do it? She decided that she would try; and, in her first attempt, she found the helps I have suggested. Still another was given her, and she took it, not as a reward, but as a comfort, as Christian took the refreshment afforded by the little arbor where he rested, as he climbed the hill called Difficulty.\n\n\"Why don't you write? That always used to make you happy,\" said her mother, once, when the desponding fit overshadowed Jo.\n\n\"I've no heart to write, and if I had, nobody cares for my things.\"\n\n\"We do; write something for us, and never mind the rest of the world. Try it, dear; I'm sure it would do you good, and please us very much.\"\n\n\"Don't believe I can;\" but Jo got out her desk, and began to overhaul her half-finished manuscripts.\n\nAn hour afterward her mother peeped in, and there she was, scratching away, with her black pinafore on, and an absorbed expression, which caused Mrs. March to smile, and slip away, well pleased with the success of her suggestion. Jo never knew how it happened, but something got into that story that went straight to the hearts of those who read it; for, when her family had laughed and cried over it, her father sent it, much against her will, to one of the popular magazines, and, to her utter surprise, it was not only paid for, but others requested. Letters from several persons, whose praise was honor, followed the appearance of the little story, newspapers copied it, and strangers as well as friends admired it. For a small thing it was a great success; and Jo was more astonished than when her novel was commended and condemned all at once.\n\n\"I don't understand it. What _can_ there be in a simple little story like that, to make people praise it so?\" she said, quite bewildered.\n\n\"There is truth in it, Jo, that's the secret; humor and pathos make it alive, and you have found your style at last. You wrote with no thought of fame or money, and put your heart into it, my daughter; you have had the bitter, now comes the sweet. Do your best, and grow as happy as we are in your success.\"\n\n\"If there _is_ anything good or true in what I write, it isn't mine; I owe it all to you and mother and to Beth,\" said Jo, more touched by her father's words than by any amount of praise from the world.\n\nSo, taught by love and sorrow, Jo wrote her little stories, and sent them away to make friends for themselves and her, finding it a very charitable world to such humble wanderers; for they were kindly welcomed, and sent home comfortable tokens to their mother, like dutiful children whom good fortune overtakes.\n\nWhen Amy and Laurie wrote of their engagement, Mrs. March feared that Jo would find it difficult to rejoice over it, but her fears were soon set at rest; for, though Jo looked grave at first, she took it very quietly, and was full of hopes and plans for \"the children\" before she read the letter twice. It was a sort of written duet, wherein each glorified the other in lover-like fashion, very pleasant to read and satisfactory to think of, for no one had any objection to make.\n\n\"You like it, mother?\" said Jo, as they laid down the closely written sheets, and looked at one another.\n\n\"Yes, I hoped it would be so, ever since Amy wrote that she had refused Fred. I felt sure then that something better than what you call the 'mercenary spirit' had come over her, and a hint here and there in her letters made me suspect that love and Laurie would win the day.\"\n\n\"How sharp you are, Marmee, and how silent! You never said a word to me.\"\n\n\"Mothers have need of sharp eyes and discreet tongues when they have girls to manage. I was half afraid to put the idea into your head, lest you should write and congratulate them before the thing was settled.\"\n\n\"I'm not the scatter-brain I was; you may trust me, I'm sober and sensible enough for any one's _confidante_ now.\"\n\n\"So you are, dear, and I should have made you mine, only I fancied it might pain you to learn that your Teddy loved any one else.\"\n\n\"Now, mother, did you really think I could be so silly and selfish, after I'd refused his love, when it was freshest, if not best?\"\n\n\"I knew you were sincere then, Jo, but lately I have thought that if he came back, and asked again, you might, perhaps, feel like giving another answer. Forgive me, dear, I can't help seeing that you are very lonely, and sometimes there is a hungry look in your eyes that goes to my heart; so I fancied that your boy might fill the empty place if he tried now.\"\n\n\"No, mother, it is better as it is, and I'm glad Amy has learned to love him. But you are right in one thing: I _am_ lonely, and perhaps if Teddy had tried again, I might have said 'Yes,' not because I love him any more, but because I care more to be loved than when he went away.\"\n\n\"I'm glad of that, Jo, for it shows that you are getting on. There are plenty to love you, so try to be satisfied with father and mother, sisters and brothers, friends and babies, till the best lover of all comes to give you your reward.\"\n\n\"Mothers are the _best_ lovers in the world; but I don't mind whispering to Marmee that I'd like to try all kinds. It's very curious, but the more I try to satisfy myself with all sorts of natural affections, the more I seem to want. I'd no idea hearts could take in so many; mine is so elastic, it never seems full now, and I used to be quite contented with my family. I don't understand it.\"\n\n\"I do;\" and Mrs. March smiled her wise smile, as Jo turned back the leaves to read what Amy said of Laurie.\n\n\"It is so beautiful to be loved as Laurie loves me; he isn't sentimental, doesn't say much about it, but I see and feel it in all he says and does, and it makes me so happy and so humble that I don't seem to be the same girl I was. I never knew how good and generous and tender he was till now, for he lets me read his heart, and I find it full of noble impulses and hopes and purposes, and am so proud to know it's mine. He says he feels as if he 'could make a prosperous voyage now with me aboard as mate, and lots of love for ballast.' I pray he may, and try to be all he believes me, for I love my gallant captain with all my heart and soul and might, and never will desert him, while God lets us be together. O mother, I never knew how much like heaven this world could be, when two people love and live for one another!\"\n\n\"And that's our cool, reserved, and worldly Amy! Truly, love does work miracles. How very, very happy they must be!\" And Jo laid the rustling sheets together with a careful hand, as one might shut the covers of a lovely romance, which holds the reader fast till the end comes, and he finds himself alone in the work-a-day world again.\n\nBy and by Jo roamed away upstairs, for it was rainy, and she could not walk. A restless spirit possessed her, and the old feeling came again, not bitter as it once was, but a sorrowfully patient wonder why one sister should have all she asked, the other nothing. It was not true; she knew that, and tried to put it away, but the natural craving for affection was strong, and Amy's happiness woke the hungry longing for some one to \"love with heart and soul, and cling to while God let them be together.\"\n\nUp in the garret, where Jo's unquiet wanderings ended, stood four little wooden chests in a row, each marked with its owner's name, and each filled with relics of the childhood and girlhood ended now for all. Jo glanced into them, and when she came to her own, leaned her chin on the edge, and stared absently at the chaotic collection, till a bundle of old exercise-books caught her eye. She drew them out, turned them over, and re-lived that pleasant winter at kind Mrs. Kirke's. She had smiled at first, then she looked thoughtful, next sad, and when she came to a little message written in the Professor's hand, her lips began to tremble, the books slid out of her lap, and she sat looking at the friendly words, as if they took a new meaning, and touched a tender spot in her heart.\n\n\"Wait for me, my friend. I may be a little late, but I shall surely come.\"\n\n\"Oh, if he only would! So kind, so good, so patient with me always; my dear old Fritz, I didn't value him half enough when I had him, but now how I should love to see him, for every one seems going away from me, and I'm all alone.\"\n\nAnd holding the little paper fast, as if it were a promise yet to be fulfilled, Jo laid her head down on a comfortable rag-bag, and cried, as if in opposition to the rain pattering on the roof.\n\nWas it all self-pity, loneliness, or low spirits? or was it the waking up of a sentiment which had bided its time as patiently as its inspirer? Who shall say?\n\nJo was alone in the twilight, lying on the old sofa, looking at the fire, and thinking. It was her favorite way of spending the hour of dusk; no one disturbed her, and she used to lie there on Beth's little red pillow, planning stories, dreaming dreams, or thinking tender thoughts of the sister who never seemed far away. Her face looked tired, grave, and rather sad; for to-morrow was her birthday, and she was thinking how fast the years went by, how old she was getting, and how little she seemed to have accomplished. Almost twenty-five, and nothing to show for it. Jo was mistaken in that; there was a good deal to show, and by and by she saw, and was grateful for it.\n\n\"An old maid, that's what I'm to be. A literary spinster, with a pen for a spouse, a family of stories for children, and twenty years hence a morsel of fame, perhaps; when, like poor Johnson, I'm old, and can't enjoy it, solitary, and can't share it, independent, and don't need it. Well, I needn't be a sour saint nor a selfish sinner; and, I dare say, old maids are very comfortable when they get used to it; but--\" and there Jo sighed, as if the prospect was not inviting.\n\nIt seldom is, at first, and thirty seems the end of all things to five-and-twenty; but it's not so bad as it looks, and one can get on quite happily if one has something in one's self to fall back upon. At twenty-five, girls begin to talk about being old maids, but secretly resolve that they never will be; at thirty they say nothing about it, but quietly accept the fact, and, if sensible, console themselves by remembering that they have twenty more useful, happy years, in which they may be learning to grow old gracefully. Don't laugh at the spinsters, dear girls, for often very tender, tragical romances are hidden away in the hearts that beat so quietly under the sober gowns, and many silent sacrifices of youth, health, ambition, love itself, make the faded faces beautiful in God's sight. Even the sad, sour sisters should be kindly dealt with, because they have missed the sweetest part of life, if for no other reason; and, looking at them with compassion, not contempt, girls in their bloom should remember that they too may miss the blossom time; that rosy cheeks don't last forever, that silver threads will come in the bonnie brown hair, and that, by and by, kindness and respect will be as sweet as love and admiration now.\nGentlemen, which means boys, be courteous to the old maids, no matter how poor and plain and prim, for the only chivalry worth having is that which is the readiest to pay deference to the old, protect the feeble, and serve womankind, regardless of rank, age, or color. Just recollect the good aunts who have not only lectured and fussed, but nursed and petted, too often without thanks; the scrapes they have helped you out of, the \"tips\" they have given you from their small store, the stitches the patient old fingers have set for you, the steps the willing old feet have taken, and gratefully pay the dear old ladies the little attentions that women love to receive as long as they live. The bright-eyed girls are quick to see such traits, and will like you all the better for them; and if death, almost the only power that can part mother and son, should rob you of yours, you will be sure to find a tender welcome and maternal cherishing from some Aunt Priscilla, who has kept the warmest corner of her lonely old heart for the \"the best nevvy in the world.\"\n\nJo must have fallen asleep (as I dare say my reader has during this little homily), for suddenly Laurie's ghost seemed to stand before her,--a substantial, lifelike ghost,--leaning over her, with the very look he used to wear when he felt a good deal and didn't like to show it. But, like Jenny in the ballad,--\n\n\"She could not think it he,\"\n\nand lay staring up at him in startled silence, till he stooped and kissed her. Then she knew him, and flew up, crying joyfully,--\n\n\"O my Teddy! O my Teddy!\"\n\n\"Dear Jo, you are glad to see me, then?\"\n\n\"Glad! My blessed boy, words can't express my gladness. Where's Amy?\"\n\n\"Your mother has got her down at Meg's. We stopped there by the way, and there was no getting my wife out of their clutches.\"\n\n\"Your what?\" cried Jo, for Laurie uttered those two words with an unconscious pride and satisfaction which betrayed him.\n\n\"Oh, the dickens! now I've done it;\" and he looked so guilty that Jo was down upon him like a flash.\n\n\"You've gone and got married!\"\n\n\"Yes, please, but I never will again;\" and he went down upon his knees, with a penitent clasping of hands, and a face full of mischief, mirth, and triumph.\n\n\"Actually married?\"\n\n\"Very much so, thank you.\"\n\n\"Mercy on us! What dreadful thing will you do next?\" and Jo fell into her seat, with a gasp.\n\n\"A characteristic, but not exactly complimentary, congratulation,\" returned Laurie, still in an abject attitude, but beaming with satisfaction.\n\n\"What can you expect, when you take one's breath away, creeping in like a burglar, and letting cats out of bags like that? Get up, you ridiculous boy, and tell me all about it.\"\n\n\"Not a word, unless you let me come in my old place, and promise not to barricade.\"\n\nJo laughed at that as she had not done for many a long day, and patted the sofa invitingly, as she said, in a cordial tone,--\n\n\"The old pillow is up garret, and we don't need it now; so, come and 'fess, Teddy.\"\n\n\"How good it sounds to hear you say 'Teddy'! No one ever calls me that but you;\" and Laurie sat down, with an air of great content.\n\n\"What does Amy call you?\"\n\n\"My lord.\"\n\n\"That's like her. Well, you look it;\" and Jo's eyes plainly betrayed that she found her boy comelier than ever.\n\nThe pillow was gone, but there _was_ a barricade, nevertheless,--a natural one, raised by time, absence, and change of heart. Both felt it, and for a minute looked at one another as if that invisible barrier cast a little shadow over them. It was gone directly, however, for Laurie said, with a vain attempt at dignity,--\n\n\"Don't I look like a married man and the head of a family?\"\n\n\"Not a bit, and you never will. You've grown bigger and bonnier, but you are the same scapegrace as ever.\"\n\n\"Now, really, Jo, you ought to treat me with more respect,\" began Laurie, who enjoyed it all immensely.\n\n\"How can I, when the mere idea of you, married and settled, is so irresistibly funny that I can't keep sober!\" answered Jo, smiling all over her face, so infectiously that they had another laugh, and then settled down for a good talk, quite in the pleasant old fashion.\n\n\"It's no use your going out in the cold to get Amy, for they are all coming up presently. I couldn't wait; I wanted to be the one to tell you the grand surprise, and have 'first skim,' as we used to say when we squabbled about the cream.\"\n\n\"Of course you did, and spoilt your story by beginning at the wrong end. Now, start right, and tell me how it all happened; I'm pining to know.\"\n\n\"Well, I did it to please Amy,\" began Laurie, with a twinkle that made Jo exclaim,--\n\n\"Fib number one; Amy did it to please you. Go on, and tell the truth, if you can, sir.\"\n\n\"Now she's beginning to marm it; isn't it jolly to hear her?\" said Laurie to the fire, and the fire glowed and sparkled as if it quite agreed. \"It's all the same, you know, she and I being one. We planned to come home with the Carrols, a month or more ago, but they suddenly changed their minds, and decided to pass another winter in Paris. But grandpa wanted to come home; he went to please me, and I couldn't let him go alone, neither could I leave Amy; and Mrs. Carrol had got English notions about chaperons and such nonsense, and wouldn't let Amy come with us. So I just settled the difficulty by saying, 'Let's be married, and then we can do as we like.'\"\n\n\"Of course you did; you always have things to suit you.\"\n\n\"Not always;\" and something in Laurie's voice made Jo say hastily,--\n\n\"How did you ever get aunt to agree?\"\n\n\"It was hard work; but, between us, we talked her over, for we had heaps of good reasons on our side. There wasn't time to write and ask leave, but you all liked it, had consented to it by and by, and it was only 'taking Time by the fetlock,' as my wife says.\"\n\n\"Aren't we proud of those two words, and don't we like to say them?\" interrupted Jo, addressing the fire in her turn, and watching with delight the happy light it seemed to kindle in the eyes that had been so tragically gloomy when she saw them last.\n\n\"A trifle, perhaps; she's such a captivating little woman I can't help being proud of her. Well, then, uncle and aunt were there to play propriety; we were so absorbed in one another we were of no mortal use apart, and that charming arrangement would make everything easy all round; so we did it.\"\n\n\"When, where, how?\" asked Jo, in a fever of feminine interest and curiosity, for she could not realize it a particle.\n\n\"Six weeks ago, at the American consul's, in Paris; a very quiet wedding, of course, for even in our happiness we didn't forget dear little Beth.\"\n\nJo put her hand in his as he said that, and Laurie gently smoothed the little red pillow, which he remembered well.\n\n\"Why didn't you let us know afterward?\" asked Jo, in a quieter tone, when they had sat quite still a minute.\n\n\"We wanted to surprise you; we thought we were coming directly home, at first; but the dear old gentleman, as soon as we were married, found he couldn't be ready under a month, at least, and sent us off to spend our honeymoon wherever we liked. Amy had once called Valrosa a regular honeymoon home, so we went there, and were as happy as people are but once in their lives. My faith! wasn't it love among the roses!\"\n\nLaurie seemed to forget Jo for a minute, and Jo was glad of it; for the fact that he told her these things so freely and naturally assured her that he had quite forgiven and forgotten. She tried to draw away her hand; but, as if he guessed the thought that prompted the half-involuntary impulse, Laurie held it fast, and said, with a manly gravity she had never seen in him before,--\n\n\"Jo, dear, I want to say one thing, and then we'll put it by forever. As I told you in my letter, when I wrote that Amy had been so kind to me, I never shall stop loving you; but the love is altered, and I have learned to see that it is better as it is. Amy and you change places in my heart, that's all. I think it was meant to be so, and would have come about naturally, if I had waited, as you tried to make me; but I never could be patient, and so I got a heartache. I was a boy then, headstrong and violent; and it took a hard lesson to show me my mistake. For it _was_ one, Jo, as you said, and I found it out, after making a fool of myself. Upon my word, I was so tumbled up in my mind, at one time, that I didn't know which I loved best, you or Amy, and tried to love both alike; but I couldn't, and when I saw her in Switzerland, everything seemed to clear up all at once. You both got into your right places, and I felt sure that it was well off with the old love before it was on with the new; that I could honestly share my heart between sister Jo and wife Amy, and love them both dearly. Will you believe it, and go back to the happy old times when we first knew one another?\"\n\n\"I'll believe it, with all my heart; but, Teddy, we never can be boy and girl again: the happy old times can't come back, and we mustn't expect it. We are man and woman now, with sober work to do, for playtime is over, and we must give up frolicking. I'm sure you feel this; I see the change in you, and you'll find it in me. I shall miss my boy, but I shall love the man as much, and admire him more, because he means to be what I hoped he would. We can't be little playmates any longer, but we will be brother and sister, to love and help one another all our lives, won't we, Laurie?\"\n\nHe did not say a word, but took the hand she offered him, and laid his face down on it for a minute, feeling that out of the grave of a boyish passion, there had risen a beautiful, strong friendship to bless them both. Presently Jo said cheerfully, for she didn't want the coming home to be a sad one,--\n\n\"I can't make it true that you children are really married, and going to set up housekeeping. Why, it seems only yesterday that I was buttoning Amy's pinafore, and pulling your hair when you teased. Mercy me, how time does fly!\"\n\n\"As one of the children is older than yourself, you needn't talk so like a grandma. I flatter myself I'm a 'gentleman growed,' as Peggotty said of David; and when you see Amy, you'll find her rather a precocious infant,\" said Laurie, looking amused at her maternal air.\n\n\"You may be a little older in years, but I'm ever so much older in feeling, Teddy. Women always are; and this last year has been such a hard one that I feel forty.\"\n\n\"Poor Jo! we left you to bear it alone, while we went pleasuring. You _are_ older; here's a line, and there's another; unless you smile, your eyes look sad, and when I touched the cushion, just now, I found a tear on it. You've had a great deal to bear, and had to bear it all alone. What a selfish beast I've been!\" and Laurie pulled his own hair, with a remorseful look.\n\nBut Jo only turned over the traitorous pillow, and answered, in a tone which she tried to make quite cheerful,--\n\n\"No, I had father and mother to help me, the dear babies to comfort me, and the thought that you and Amy were safe and happy, to make the troubles here easier to bear. I _am_ lonely, sometimes, but I dare say it's good for me, and--\"\n\n\"You never shall be again,\" broke in Laurie, putting his arm about her, as if to fence out every human ill. \"Amy and I can't get on without you, so you must come and teach 'the children' to keep house, and go halves in everything, just as we used to do, and let us pet you, and all be blissfully happy and friendly together.\"\n\n\"If I shouldn't be in the way, it would be very pleasant. I begin to feel quite young already; for, somehow, all my troubles seemed to fly away when you came. You always were a comfort, Teddy;\" and Jo leaned her head on his shoulder, just as she did years ago, when Beth lay ill, and Laurie told her to hold on to him.\n\nHe looked down at her, wondering if she remembered the time, but Jo was smiling to herself, as if, in truth, her troubles _had_ all vanished at his coming.\n\n\"You are the same Jo still, dropping tears about one minute, and laughing the next. You look a little wicked now; what is it, grandma?\"\n\n\"I was wondering how you and Amy get on together.\"\n\n\"Like angels!\"\n\n\"Yes, of course, at first; but which rules?\"\n\n\"I don't mind telling you that she does, now; at least I let her think so,--it pleases her, you know. By and by we shall take turns, for marriage, they say, halves one's rights and doubles one's duties.\"\n\n\"You'll go on as you begin, and Amy will rule you all the days of your life.\"\n\n\"Well, she does it so imperceptibly that I don't think I shall mind much. She is the sort of woman who knows how to rule well; in fact, I rather like it, for she winds one round her finger as softly and prettily as a skein of silk, and makes you feel as if she was doing you a favor all the while.\"\n\n\"That ever I should live to see you a henpecked husband and enjoying it!\" cried Jo, with uplifted hands.\n\nIt was good to see Laurie square his shoulders, and smile with masculine scorn at that insinuation, as he replied, with his \"high and mighty\" air,--\n\n\"Amy is too well-bred for that, and I am not the sort of man to submit to it. My wife and I respect ourselves and one another too much ever to tyrannize or quarrel.\"\n\nJo liked that, and thought the new dignity very becoming, but the boy seemed changing very fast into the man, and regret mingled with her pleasure.\n\n\"I am sure of that; Amy and you never did quarrel as we used to. She is the sun and I the wind, in the fable, and the sun managed the man best, you remember.\"\n\n\"She can blow him up as well as shine on him,\" laughed Laurie. \"Such a lecture as I got at Nice! I give you my word it was a deal worse than any of your scoldings,--a regular rouser. I'll tell you all about it sometime,--_she_ never will, because, after telling me that she despised and was ashamed of me, she lost her heart to the despicable party and married the good-for-nothing.\"\n\n\"What baseness! Well, if she abuses you, come to me, and I'll defend you.\"\n\n\"I look as if I needed it, don't I?\" said Laurie, getting up and striking an attitude which suddenly changed from the imposing to the rapturous, as Amy's voice was heard calling,--\n\n\"Where is she? Where's my dear old Jo?\"\n\nIn trooped the whole family, and every one was hugged and kissed all over again, and, after several vain attempts, the three wanderers were set down to be looked at and exulted over. Mr. Laurence, hale and hearty as ever, was quite as much improved as the others by his foreign tour, for the crustiness seemed to be nearly gone, and the old-fashioned courtliness had received a polish which made it kindlier than ever. It was good to see him beam at \"my children,\" as he called the young pair; it was better still to see Amy pay him the daughterly duty and affection which completely won his old heart; and best of all, to watch Laurie revolve about the two, as if never tired of enjoying the pretty picture they made.\n\nThe minute she put her eyes upon Amy, Meg became conscious that her own dress hadn't a Parisian air, that young Mrs. Moffat would be entirely eclipsed by young Mrs. Laurence, and that \"her ladyship\" was altogether a most elegant and graceful woman. Jo thought, as she watched the pair, \"How well they look together! I was right, and Laurie has found the beautiful, accomplished girl who will become his home better than clumsy old Jo, and be a pride, not a torment to him.\" Mrs. March and her husband smiled and nodded at each other with happy faces, for they saw that their youngest had done well, not only in worldly things, but the better wealth of love, confidence, and happiness.\n\nFor Amy's face was full of the soft brightness which betokens a peaceful heart, her voice had a new tenderness in it, and the cool, prim carriage was changed to a gentle dignity, both womanly and winning. No little affectations marred it, and the cordial sweetness of her manner was more charming than the new beauty or the old grace, for it stamped her at once with the unmistakable sign of the true gentlewoman she had hoped to become.\n\n\"Love has done much for our little girl,\" said her mother softly.\n\n\"She has had a good example before her all her life, my dear,\" Mr. March whispered back, with a loving look at the worn face and gray head beside him.\n\nDaisy found it impossible to keep her eyes off her \"pitty aunty,\" but attached herself like a lap-dog to the wonderful châtelaine full of delightful charms. Demi paused to consider the new relationship before he compromised himself by the rash acceptance of a bribe, which took the tempting form of a family of wooden bears from Berne. A flank movement produced an unconditional surrender, however, for Laurie knew where to have him.\n\n\"Young man, when I first had the honor of making your acquaintance you hit me in the face: now I demand the satisfaction of a gentleman;\" and with that the tall uncle proceeded to toss and tousle the small nephew in a way that damaged his philosophical dignity as much as it delighted his boyish soul.\n\n\"Blest if she ain't in silk from head to foot? Ain't it a relishin' sight to see her settin' there as fine as a fiddle, and hear folks calling little Amy, Mis. Laurence?\" muttered old Hannah, who could not resist frequent \"peeks\" through the slide as she set the table in a most decidedly promiscuous manner.\n\nMercy on us, how they did talk! first one, then the other, then all burst out together, trying to tell the history of three years in half an hour. It was fortunate that tea was at hand, to produce a lull and provide refreshment, for they would have been hoarse and faint if they had gone on much longer. Such a happy procession as filed away into the little dining-room! Mr. March proudly escorted \"Mrs. Laurence;\" Mrs. March as proudly leaned on the arm of \"my son;\" the old gentleman took Jo, with a whispered \"You must be my girl now,\" and a glance at the empty corner by the fire, that made Jo whisper back, with trembling lips, \"I'll try to fill her place, sir.\"\n\nThe twins pranced behind, feeling that the millennium was at hand, for every one was so busy with the new-comers that they were left to revel at their own sweet will, and you may be sure they made the most of the opportunity. Didn't they steal sips of tea, stuff gingerbread _ab libitum_, get a hot biscuit apiece, and, as a crowning trespass, didn't they each whisk a captivating little tart into their tiny pockets, there to stick and crumble treacherously, teaching them that both human nature and pastry are frail? Burdened with the guilty consciousness of the sequestered tarts, and fearing that Dodo's sharp eyes would pierce the thin disguise of cambric and merino which hid their booty, the little sinners attached themselves to \"Dranpa,\" who hadn't his spectacles on. Amy, who was handed about like refreshments, returned to the parlor on Father Laurence's arm; the others paired off as before, and this arrangement left Jo companionless. She did not mind it at the minute, for she lingered to answer Hannah's eager inquiry,--\n\n\"Will Miss Amy ride in her coop (_coupé_), and use all them lovely silver dishes that's stored away over yander?\"\n\n\"Shouldn't wonder if she drove six white horses, ate off gold plate, and wore diamonds and point-lace every day. Teddy thinks nothing too good for her,\" returned Jo with infinite satisfaction.\n\n\"No more there is! Will you have hash or fish-balls for breakfast?\" asked Hannah, who wisely mingled poetry and prose.\n\n\"I don't care;\" and Jo shut the door, feeling that food was an uncongenial topic just then. She stood a minute looking at the party vanishing above, and, as Demi's short plaid legs toiled up the last stair, a sudden sense of loneliness came over her so strongly that she looked about her with dim eyes, as if to find something to lean upon, for even Teddy had deserted her. If she had known what birthday gift was coming every minute nearer and nearer, she would not have said to herself, \"I'll weep a little weep when I go to bed; it won't do to be dismal now.\" Then she drew her hand over her eyes,--for one of her boyish habits was never to know where her handkerchief was,--and had just managed to call up a smile when there came a knock at the porch-door.\n\nShe opened it with hospitable haste, and started as if another ghost had come to surprise her; for there stood a tall, bearded gentleman, beaming on her from the darkness like a midnight sun.\n\n\"O Mr. Bhaer, I _am_ so glad to see you!\" cried Jo, with a clutch, as if she feared the night would swallow him up before she could get him in.\n\n\"And I to see Miss Marsch,--but no, you haf a party--\" and the Professor paused as the sound of voices and the tap of dancing feet came down to them.\n\n\"No, we haven't, only the family. My sister and friends have just come home, and we are all very happy. Come in, and make one of us.\"\n\nThough a very social man, I think Mr. Bhaer would have gone decorously away, and come again another day; but how could he, when Jo shut the door behind him, and bereft him of his hat? Perhaps her face had something to do with it, for she forgot to hide her joy at seeing him, and showed it with a frankness that proved irresistible to the solitary man, whose welcome far exceeded his boldest hopes.\n\n\"If I shall not be Monsieur de Trop, I will so gladly see them all. You haf been ill, my friend?\"\n\nHe put the question abruptly, for, as Jo hung up his coat, the light fell on her face, and he saw a change in it.\n\n\"Not ill, but tired and sorrowful. We have had trouble since I saw you last.\"\n\n\"Ah, yes, I know. My heart was sore for you when I heard that;\" and he shook hands again, with such a sympathetic face that Jo felt as if no comfort could equal the look of the kind eyes, the grasp of the big, warm hand.\n\n\"Father, mother, this is my friend, Professor Bhaer,\" she said, with a face and tone of such irrepressible pride and pleasure that she might as well have blown a trumpet and opened the door with a flourish.\n\nIf the stranger had had any doubts about his reception, they were set at rest in a minute by the cordial welcome he received. Every one greeted him kindly, for Jo's sake at first, but very soon they liked him for his own. They could not help it, for he carried the talisman that opens all hearts, and these simple people warmed to him at once, feeling even the more friendly because he was poor; for poverty enriches those who live above it, and is a sure passport to truly hospitable spirits. Mr. Bhaer sat looking about him with the air of a traveller who knocks at a strange door, and, when it opens, finds himself at home. The children went to him like bees to a honey-pot; and, establishing themselves on each knee, proceeded to captivate him by rifling his pockets, pulling his beard, and investigating his watch, with juvenile audacity. The women telegraphed their approval to one another, and Mr. March, feeling that he had got a kindred spirit, opened his choicest stores for his guest's benefit, while silent John listened and enjoyed the talk, but said not a word, and Mr. Laurence found it impossible to go to sleep.\n\nIf Jo had not been otherwise engaged, Laurie's behavior would have amused her; for a faint twinge, not of jealousy, but something like suspicion, caused that gentleman to stand aloof at first, and observe the new-comer with brotherly circumspection. But it did not last long. He got interested in spite of himself, and, before he knew it, was drawn into the circle; for Mr. Bhaer talked well in this genial atmosphere, and did himself justice. He seldom spoke to Laurie, but he looked at him often, and a shadow would pass across his face, as if regretting his own lost youth, as he watched the young man in his prime. Then his eye would turn to Jo so wistfully that she would have surely answered the mute inquiry if she had seen it; but Jo had her own eyes to take care of, and, feeling that they could not be trusted, she prudently kept them on the little sock she was knitting, like a model maiden aunt.\n\nA stealthy glance now and then refreshed her like sips of fresh water after a dusty walk, for the sidelong peeps showed her several propitious omens. Mr. Bhaer's face had lost the absent-minded expression, and looked all alive with interest in the present moment, actually young and handsome, she thought, forgetting to compare him with Laurie, as she usually did strange men, to their great detriment. Then he seemed quite inspired, though the burial customs of the ancients, to which the conversation had strayed, might not be considered an exhilarating topic. Jo quite glowed with triumph when Teddy got quenched in an argument, and thought to herself, as she watched her father's absorbed face, \"How he would enjoy having such a man as my Professor to talk with every day!\" Lastly, Mr. Bhaer was dressed in a new suit of black, which made him look more like a gentleman than ever. His bushy hair had been cut and smoothly brushed, but didn't stay in order long, for, in exciting moments, he rumpled it up in the droll way he used to do; and Jo liked it rampantly erect better than flat, because she thought it gave his fine forehead a Jove-like aspect. Poor Jo, how she did glorify that plain man, as she sat knitting away so quietly, yet letting nothing escape her, not even the fact that Mr. Bhaer actually had gold sleeve-buttons in his immaculate wristbands!\n\n\"Dear old fellow! He couldn't have got himself up with more care if he'd been going a-wooing,\" said Jo to herself; and then a sudden thought, born of the words, made her blush so dreadfully that she had to drop her ball, and go down after it to hide her face.\n\nThe manœuvre did not succeed as well as she expected, however; for, though just in the act of setting fire to a funeral-pile, the Professor dropped his torch, metaphorically speaking, and made a dive after the little blue ball. Of course they bumped their heads smartly together, saw stars, and both came up flushed and laughing, without the ball, to resume their seats, wishing they had not left them.\n\nNobody knew where the evening went to; for Hannah skilfully abstracted the babies at an early hour, nodding like two rosy poppies, and Mr. Laurence went home to rest. The others sat round the fire, talking away, utterly regardless of the lapse of time, till Meg, whose maternal mind was impressed with a firm conviction that Daisy had tumbled out of bed, and Demi set his night-gown afire studying the structure of matches, made a move to go.\n\n\"We must have our sing, in the good old way, for we are all together again once more,\" said Jo, feeling that a good shout would be a safe and pleasant vent for the jubilant emotions of her soul.\n\nThey were not _all_ there. But no one found the words thoughtless or untrue; for Beth still seemed among them, a peaceful presence, invisible, but dearer than ever, since death could not break the household league that love made indissoluble. The little chair stood in its old place; the tidy basket, with the bit of work she left unfinished when the needle grew \"so heavy,\" was still on its accustomed shelf; the beloved instrument, seldom touched now, had not been moved; and above it Beth's face, serene and smiling, as in the early days, looked down upon them, seeming to say, \"Be happy. I am here.\"\n\n\"Play something, Amy. Let them hear how much you have improved,\" said Laurie, with pardonable pride in his promising pupil.\n\nBut Amy whispered, with full eyes, as she twirled the faded stool,--\n\n\"Not to-night, dear. I can't show off to-night.\"\n\nBut she did show something better than brilliancy or skill; for she sung Beth's songs with a tender music in her voice which the best master could not have taught, and touched the listeners' hearts with a sweeter power than any other inspiration could have given her. The room was very still, when the clear voice failed suddenly at the last line of Beth's favorite hymn. It was hard to say,--\n\n\"Earth hath no sorrow that heaven cannot heal;\"\n\nand Amy leaned against her husband, who stood behind her, feeling that her welcome home was not quite perfect without Beth's kiss.\n\n\"Now, we must finish with Mignon's song; for Mr. Bhaer sings that,\" said Jo, before the pause grew painful. And Mr. Bhaer cleared his throat with a gratified \"Hem!\" as he stepped into the corner where Jo stood, saying,--\n\n\"You will sing with me? We go excellently well together.\"\n\nA pleasing fiction, by the way; for Jo had no more idea of music than a grasshopper. But she would have consented if he had proposed to sing a whole opera, and warbled away, blissfully regardless of time and tune. It didn't much matter; for Mr. Bhaer sang like a true German, heartily and well; and Jo soon subsided into a subdued hum, that she might listen to the mellow voice that seemed to sing for her alone.\n\n\"Know'st thou the land where the citron blooms,\"\nused to be the Professor's favorite line, for \"das land\" meant Germany to him; but now he seemed to dwell, with peculiar warmth and melody, upon the words,--\n\n\"There, oh there, might I with thee, O my beloved, go!\"\n\nand one listener was so thrilled by the tender invitation that she longed to say she did know the land, and would joyfully depart thither whenever he liked.\n\nThe song was considered a great success, and the singer retired covered with laurels. But a few minutes afterward, he forgot his manners entirely, and stared at Amy putting on her bonnet; for she had been introduced simply as \"my sister,\" and no one had called her by her new name since he came. He forgot himself still further when Laurie said, in his most gracious manner, at parting,--\n\n\"My wife and I are very glad to meet you, sir. Please remember that there is always a welcome waiting for you over the way.\"\n\nThen the Professor thanked him so heartily, and looked so suddenly illuminated with satisfaction, that Laurie thought him the most delightfully demonstrative old fellow he ever met.\n\n\"I too shall go; but I shall gladly come again, if you will gif me leave, dear madame, for a little business in the city will keep me here some days.\"\n\nHe spoke to Mrs. March, but he looked at Jo; and the mother's voice gave as cordial an assent as did the daughter's eyes; for Mrs. March was not so blind to her children's interest as Mrs. Moffat supposed.\n\n\"I suspect that is a wise man,\" remarked Mr. March, with placid satisfaction, from the hearth-rug, after the last guest had gone.\n\n\"I know he is a good one,\" added Mrs. March, with decided approval, as she wound up the clock.\n\n\"I thought you'd like him,\" was all Jo said, as she slipped away to her bed.\n\nShe wondered what the business was that brought Mr. Bhaer to the city, and finally decided that he had been appointed to some great honor, somewhere, but had been too modest to mention the fact. If she had seen his face when, safe in his own room, he looked at the picture of a severe and rigid young lady, with a good deal of hair, who appeared to be gazing darkly into futurity, it might have thrown some light upon the subject, especially when he turned off the gas, and kissed the picture in the dark.\n\n# MY LORD AND LADY.\n\n\"Please, Madam Mother, could you lend me my wife for half an hour? The luggage has come, and I've been making hay of Amy's Paris finery, trying to find some things I want,\" said Laurie, coming in the next day to find Mrs. Laurence sitting in her mother's lap, as if being made \"the baby\" again.\n\n\"Certainly. Go, dear; I forget that you have any home but this,\" and Mrs. March pressed the white hand that wore the wedding-ring, as if asking pardon for her maternal covetousness.\n\n\"I shouldn't have come over if I could have helped it; but I can't get on without my little woman any more than a--\"\n\n\"Weathercock can without wind,\" suggested Jo, as he paused for a simile; Jo had grown quite her own saucy self again since Teddy came home.\n\n\"Exactly; for Amy keeps me pointing due west most of the time, with only an occasional whiffle round to the south, and I haven't had an easterly spell since I was married; don't know anything about the north, but am altogether salubrious and balmy, hey, my lady?\"\n\n\"Lovely weather so far; I don't know how long it will last, but I'm not afraid of storms, for I'm learning how to sail my ship. Come home, dear, and I'll find your bootjack; I suppose that's what you are rummaging after among my things. Men are _so_ helpless, mother,\" said Amy, with a matronly air, which delighted her husband.\n\n\"What are you going to do with yourselves after you get settled?\" asked Jo, buttoning Amy's cloak as she used to button her pinafores.\n\n\"We have our plans; we don't mean to say much about them yet, because we are such very new brooms, but we don't intend to be idle. I'm going into business with a devotion that shall delight grandfather, and prove to him that I'm not spoilt. I need something of the sort to keep me steady. I'm tired of dawdling, and mean to work like a man.\"\n\n\"And Amy, what is she going to do?\" asked Mrs. March, well pleased at Laurie's decision, and the energy with which he spoke.\n\n\"After doing the civil all round, and airing our best bonnet, we shall astonish you by the elegant hospitalities of our mansion, the brilliant society we shall draw about us, and the beneficial influence we shall exert over the world at large. That's about it, isn't it, Madame Récamier?\" asked Laurie, with a quizzical look at Amy.\n\n\"Time will show. Come away, Impertinence, and don't shock my family by calling me names before their faces,\" answered Amy, resolving that there should be a home with a good wife in it before she set up a _salon_ as a queen of society.\n\n\"How happy those children seem together!\" observed Mr. March, finding it difficult to become absorbed in his Aristotle after the young couple had gone.\n\n\"Yes, and I think it will last,\" added Mrs. March, with the restful expression of a pilot who has brought a ship safely into port.\n\n\"I know it will. Happy Amy!\" and Jo sighed, then smiled brightly as Professor Bhaer opened the gate with an impatient push.\n\nLater in the evening, when his mind had been set at rest about the bootjack, Laurie said suddenly to his wife, who was flitting about, arranging her new art treasures,--\n\n\"Mrs. Laurence.\"\n\n\"My lord!\"\n\n\"That man intends to marry our Jo!\"\n\n\"I hope so; don't you, dear?\"\n\n\"Well, my love, I consider him a trump, in the fullest sense of that expressive word, but I do wish he was a little younger and a good deal richer.\"\n\n\"Now, Laurie, don't be too fastidious and worldly-minded. If they love one another it doesn't matter a particle how old they are nor how poor. Women _never_ should marry for money--\" Amy caught herself up short as the words escaped her, and looked at her husband, who replied, with malicious gravity,--\n\n\"Certainly not, though you do hear charming girls say that they intend to do it sometimes. If my memory serves me, you once thought it your duty to make a rich match; that accounts, perhaps, for your marrying a good-for-nothing like me.\"\n\n\"O my dearest boy, don't, don't say that! I forgot you were rich when I said 'Yes.' I'd have married you if you hadn't a penny, and I sometimes wish you _were_ poor that I might show how much I love you;\" and Amy, who was very dignified in public and very fond in private, gave convincing proofs of the truth of her words.\n\n\"You don't really think I am such a mercenary creature as I tried to be once, do you? It would break my heart if you didn't believe that I'd gladly pull in the same boat with you, even if you had to get your living by rowing on the lake.\"\n\n\"Am I an idiot and a brute? How could I think so, when you refused a richer man for me, and won't let me give you half I want to now, when I have the right? Girls do it every day, poor things, and are taught to think it is their only salvation; but you had better lessons, and, though I trembled for you at one time, I was not disappointed, for the daughter was true to the mother's teaching. I told mamma so yesterday, and she looked as glad and grateful as if I'd given her a check for a million, to be spent in charity. You are not listening to my moral remarks, Mrs. Laurence;\" and Laurie paused, for Amy's eyes had an absent look, though fixed upon his face.\n\n\"Yes, I am, and admiring the dimple in your chin at the same time. I don't wish to make you vain, but I must confess that I'm prouder of my handsome husband than of all his money. Don't laugh, but your nose is _such_ a comfort to me;\" and Amy softly caressed the well-cut feature with artistic satisfaction.\n\nLaurie had received many compliments in his life, but never one that suited him better, as he plainly showed, though he did laugh at his wife's peculiar taste, while she said slowly,--\n\n\"May I ask you a question, dear?\"\n\n\"Of course you may.\"\n\n\"Shall you care if Jo does marry Mr. Bhaer?\"\n\n\"Oh, that's the trouble, is it? I thought there was something in the dimple that didn't suit you. Not being a dog in the manger, but the happiest fellow alive, I assure you I can dance at Jo's wedding with a heart as light as my heels. Do you doubt it, my darling?\"\n\nAmy looked up at him, and was satisfied; her last little jealous fear vanished forever, and she thanked him, with a face full of love and confidence.\n\n\"I wish we could do something for that capital old Professor. Couldn't we invent a rich relation, who shall obligingly die out there in Germany, and leave him a tidy little fortune?\" said Laurie, when they began to pace up and down the long drawing-room, arm-in-arm, as they were fond of doing, in memory of the chateau garden.\n\n\"Jo would find us out, and spoil it all; she is very proud of him, just as he is, and said yesterday that she thought poverty was a beautiful thing.\"\n\n\"Bless her dear heart! she won't think so when she has a literary husband, and a dozen little professors and professorins to support. We won't interfere now, but watch our chance, and do them a good turn in spite of themselves. I owe Jo for a part of my education, and she believes in people's paying their honest debts, so I'll get round her in that way.\"\n\n\"How delightful it is to be able to help others, isn't it? That was always one of my dreams, to have the power of giving freely; and, thanks to you, the dream has come true.\"\n\n\"Ah! we'll do quantities of good, won't we? There's one sort of poverty that I particularly like to help. Out-and-out beggars get taken care of, but poor gentlefolks fare badly, because they won't ask, and people don't dare to offer charity; yet there are a thousand ways of helping them, if one only knows how to do it so delicately that it does not offend. I must say, I like to serve a decayed gentleman better than a blarneying beggar; I suppose it's wrong, but I do, though it is harder.\"\n\n\"Because it takes a gentleman to do it,\" added the other member of the domestic admiration society.\n\n\"Thank you, I'm afraid I don't deserve that pretty compliment. But I was going to say that while I was dawdling about abroad, I saw a good many talented young fellows making all sorts of sacrifices, and enduring real hardships, that they might realize their dreams. Splendid fellows, some of them, working like heroes, poor and friendless, but so full of courage, patience, and ambition, that I was ashamed of myself, and longed to give them a right good lift. Those are people whom it's a satisfaction to help, for if they've got genius, it's an honor to be allowed to serve them, and not let it be lost or delayed for want of fuel to keep the pot boiling; if they haven't, it's a pleasure to comfort the poor souls, and keep them from despair when they find it out.\"\n\n\"Yes, indeed; and there's another class who can't ask, and who suffer in silence. I know something of it, for I belonged to it before you made a princess of me, as the king does the beggar-maid in the old story. Ambitious girls have a hard time, Laurie, and often have to see youth, health, and precious opportunities go by, just for want of a little help at the right minute. People have been very kind to me; and whenever I see girls struggling along, as we used to do, I want to put out my hand and help them, as I was helped.\"\n\n\"And so you shall, like an angel as you are!\" cried Laurie, resolving, with a glow of philanthropic zeal, to found and endow an institution for the express benefit of young women with artistic tendencies. \"Rich people have no right to sit down and enjoy themselves, or let their money accumulate for others to waste. It's not half so sensible to leave legacies when one dies as it is to use the money wisely while alive, and enjoy making one's fellow-creatures happy with it. We'll have a good time ourselves, and add an extra relish to our own pleasure by giving other people a generous taste. Will you be a little Dorcas, going about emptying a big basket of comforts, and filling it up with good deeds?\"\n\n\"With all my heart, if you will be a brave St. Martin, stopping, as you ride gallantly through the world, to share your cloak with the beggar.\"\n\n\"It's a bargain, and we shall get the best of it!\"\n\nSo the young pair shook hands upon it, and then paced happily on again, feeling that their pleasant home was more home-like because they hoped to brighten other homes, believing that their own feet would walk more uprightly along the flowery path before them, if they smoothed rough ways for other feet, and feeling that their hearts were more closely knit together by a love which could tenderly remember those less blest than they.\n\n# DAISY AND DEMI.\n\nI cannot feel that I have done my duty as humble historian of the March family, without devoting at least one chapter to the two most precious and important members of it. Daisy and Demi had now arrived at years of discretion; for in this fast age babies of three or four assert their rights, and get them, too, which is more than many of their elders do. If there ever were a pair of twins in danger of being utterly spoilt by adoration, it was these prattling Brookes. Of course they were the most remarkable children ever born, as will be shown when I mention that they walked at eight months, talked fluently at twelve months, and at two years they took their places at table, and behaved with a propriety which charmed all beholders. At three, Daisy demanded a \"needler,\" and actually made a bag with four stitches in it; she likewise set up housekeeping in the sideboard, and managed a microscopic cooking-stove with a skill that brought tears of pride to Hannah's eyes, while Demi learned his letters with his grandfather, who invented a new mode of teaching the alphabet by forming the letters with his arms and legs, thus uniting gymnastics for head and heels. The boy early developed a mechanical genius which delighted his father and distracted his mother, for he tried to imitate every machine he saw, and kept the nursery in a chaotic condition, with his \"sewin-sheen,\"--a mysterious structure of string, chairs, clothes-pins, and spools, for wheels to go \"wound and wound;\" also a basket hung over the back of a big chair, in which he vainly tried to hoist his too confiding sister, who, with feminine devotion, allowed her little head to be bumped till rescued, when the young inventor indignantly remarked, \"Why, marmar, dat's my lellywaiter, and me's trying to pull her up.\"\n\nThough utterly unlike in character, the twins got on remarkably well together, and seldom quarrelled more than thrice a day. Of course, Demi tyrannized over Daisy, and gallantly defended her from every other aggressor; while Daisy made a galley-slave of herself, and adored her brother as the one perfect being in the world. A rosy, chubby, sunshiny little soul was Daisy, who found her way to everybody's heart, and nestled there. One of the captivating children, who seem made to be kissed and cuddled, adorned and adored like little goddesses, and produced for general approval on all festive occasions. Her small virtues were so sweet that she would have been quite angelic if a few small naughtinesses had not kept her delightfully human. It was all fair weather in her world, and every morning she scrambled up to the window in her little night-gown to look out, and say, no matter whether it rained or shone, \"Oh, pitty day, oh, pitty day!\" Every one was a friend, and she offered kisses to a stranger so confidingly that the most inveterate bachelor relented, and baby-lovers became faithful worshippers.\n\n\"Me loves evvybody,\" she once said, opening her arms, with her spoon in one hand, and her mug in the other, as if eager to embrace and nourish the whole world.\n\nAs she grew, her mother began to feel that the Dove-cote would be blest by the presence of an inmate as serene and loving as that which had helped to make the old house home, and to pray that she might be spared a loss like that which had lately taught them how long they had entertained an angel unawares. Her grandfather often called her \"Beth,\" and her grandmother watched over her with untiring devotion, as if trying to atone for some past mistake, which no eye but her own could see.\n\nDemi, like a true Yankee, was of an inquiring turn, wanting to know everything, and often getting much disturbed because he could not get satisfactory answers to his perpetual \"What for?\"\n\nHe also possessed a philosophic bent, to the great delight of his grandfather, who used to hold Socratic conversations with him, in which the precocious pupil occasionally posed his teacher, to the undisguised satisfaction of the womenfolk.\n\n\"What makes my legs go, dranpa?\" asked the young philosopher, surveying those active portions of his frame with a meditative air, while resting after a go-to-bed frolic one night.\n\n\"It's your little mind, Demi,\" replied the sage, stroking the yellow head respectfully.\n\n\"What is a little mine?\"\n\n\"It is something which makes your body move, as the spring made the wheels go in my watch when I showed it to you.\"\n\n\"Open me; I want to see it go wound.\"\n\n\"I can't do that any more than you could open the watch. God winds you up, and you go till He stops you.\"\n\n\"Does I?\" and Demi's brown eyes grew big and bright as he took in the new thought. \"Is I wounded up like the watch?\"\n\n\"Yes; but I can't show you how; for it is done when we don't see.\"\n\nDemi felt of his back, as if expecting to find it like that of the watch, and then gravely remarked,--\n\n\"I dess Dod does it when I's asleep.\"\n\nA careful explanation followed, to which he listened so attentively that his anxious grandmother said,--\n\n\"My dear, do you think it wise to talk about such things to that baby? He's getting great bumps over his eyes, and learning to ask the most unanswerable questions.\"\n\n\"If he is old enough to ask the questions he is old enough to receive true answers. I am not putting the thoughts into his head, but helping him unfold those already there. These children are wiser than we are, and I have no doubt the boy understands every word I have said to him. Now, Demi, tell me where you keep your mind?\"\n\nIf the boy had replied like Alcibiades, \"By the gods, Socrates, I cannot tell,\" his grandfather would not have been surprised; but when, after standing a moment on one leg, like a meditative young stork, he answered, in a tone of calm conviction, \"In my little belly,\" the old gentleman could only join in grandma's laugh, and dismiss the class in metaphysics.\n\nThere might have been cause for maternal anxiety, if Demi had not given convincing proofs that he was a true boy, as well as a budding philosopher; for, often, after a discussion which caused Hannah to prophesy, with ominous nods, \"That child ain't long for this world,\" he would turn about and set her fears at rest by some of the pranks with which dear, dirty, naughty little rascals distract and delight their parents' souls.\n\nMeg made many moral rules, and tried to keep them; but what mother was ever proof against the winning wiles, the ingenious evasions, or the tranquil audacity of the miniature men and women who so early show themselves accomplished Artful Dodgers?\n\n\"No more raisins, Demi, they'll make you sick,\" says mamma to the young person who offers his services in the kitchen with unfailing regularity on plum-pudding day.\n\n\"Me likes to be sick.\"\n\n\"I don't want to have you, so run away and help Daisy make patty-cakes.\"\n\nHe reluctantly departs, but his wrongs weigh upon his spirit; and, by and by, when an opportunity comes to redress them, he outwits mamma by a shrewd bargain.\n\n\"Now you have been good children, and I'll play anything you like,\" says Meg, as she leads her assistant cooks upstairs, when the pudding is safely bouncing in the pot.\n\n\"Truly, marmar?\" asks Demi, with a brilliant idea in his well-powdered head.\n\n\"Yes, truly; anything you say,\" replies the short-sighted parent, preparing herself to sing \"The Three Little Kittens\" half a dozen times over, or to take her family to \"Buy a penny bun,\" regardless of wind or limb. But Demi corners her by the cool reply,--\n\n\"Then we'll go and eat up all the raisins.\"\n\nAunt Dodo was chief playmate and _confidante_ of both children, and the trio turned the little house topsy-turvy. Aunt Amy was as yet only a name to them, Aunt Beth soon faded into a pleasantly vague memory, but Aunt Dodo was a living reality, and they made the most of her, for which compliment she was deeply grateful. But when Mr. Bhaer came, Jo neglected her playfellows, and dismay and desolation fell upon their little souls. Daisy, who was fond of going about peddling kisses, lost her best customer and became bankrupt; Demi, with infantile penetration, soon discovered that Dodo liked to play with \"the bear-man\" better than she did with him; but, though hurt, he concealed his anguish, for he hadn't the heart to insult a rival who kept a mine of chocolate-drops in his waistcoat-pocket, and a watch that could be taken out of its case and freely shaken by ardent admirers.\n\nSome persons might have considered these pleasing liberties as bribes; but Demi didn't see it in that light, and continued to patronize the \"bear-man\" with pensive affability, while Daisy bestowed her small affections upon him at the third call, and considered his shoulder her throne, his arm her refuge, his gifts treasures of surpassing worth.\nGentlemen are sometimes seized with sudden fits of admiration for the young relatives of ladies whom they honor with their regard; but this counterfeit philoprogenitiveness sits uneasily upon them, and does not deceive anybody a particle. Mr. Bhaer's devotion was sincere, however likewise effective,--for honesty is the best policy in love as in law; he was one of the men who are at home with children, and looked particularly well when little faces made a pleasant contrast with his manly one. His business, whatever it was, detained him from day to day, but evening seldom failed to bring him out to see--well, he always asked for Mr. March, so I suppose _he_ was the attraction. The excellent papa labored under the delusion that he was, and revelled in long discussions with the kindred spirit, till a chance remark of his more observing grandson suddenly enlightened him.\n\nMr. Bhaer came in one evening to pause on the threshold of the study, astonished by the spectacle that met his eye. Prone upon the floor lay Mr. March, with his respectable legs in the air, and beside him, likewise prone, was Demi, trying to imitate the attitude with his own short, scarlet-stockinged legs, both grovellers so seriously absorbed that they were unconscious of spectators, till Mr. Bhaer laughed his sonorous laugh, and Jo cried out, with a scandalized face,--\n\n\"Father, father, here's the Professor!\"\n\nDown went the black legs and up came the gray head, as the preceptor said, with undisturbed dignity,--\n\n\"Good evening, Mr. Bhaer. Excuse me for a moment; we are just finishing our lesson. Now, Demi, make the letter and tell its name.\"\n\n\"I knows him!\" and, after a few convulsive efforts, the red legs took the shape of a pair of compasses, and the intelligent pupil triumphantly shouted, \"It's a We, dranpa, it's a We!\"\n\n\"He's a born Weller,\" laughed Jo, as her parent gathered himself up, and her nephew tried to stand on his head, as the only mode of expressing his satisfaction that school was over.\n\n\"What have you been at to-day, bübchen?\" asked Mr. Bhaer, picking up the gymnast.\n\n\"Me went to see little Mary.\"\n\n\"And what did you there?\"\n\n\"I kissed her,\" began Demi, with artless frankness.\n\n\"Prut! thou beginnest early. What did the little Mary say to that?\" asked Mr. Bhaer, continuing to confess the young sinner, who stood upon his knee, exploring the waistcoat-pocket.\n\n\"Oh, she liked it, and she kissed me, and I liked it. _Don't_ little boys like little girls?\" added Demi, with his mouth full, and an air of bland satisfaction.\n\n\"You precocious chick! Who put that into your head?\" said Jo, enjoying the innocent revelations as much as the Professor.\n\n\"'Tisn't in mine head; it's in mine mouf,\" answered literal Demi, putting out his tongue, with a chocolate-drop on it, thinking she alluded to confectionery, not ideas.\n\n\"Thou shouldst save some for the little friend: sweets to the sweet, mannling;\" and Mr. Bhaer offered Jo some, with a look that made her wonder if chocolate was not the nectar drunk by the gods. Demi also saw the smile, was impressed by it, and artlessly inquired,--\n\"Do great boys like great girls, too, 'Fessor?\"\n\nLike young Washington, Mr. Bhaer \"couldn't tell a lie;\" so he gave the somewhat vague reply that he believed they did sometimes, in a tone that made Mr. March put down his clothes-brush, glance at Jo's retiring face, and then sink into his chair, looking as if the \"precocious chick\" had put an idea into _his_ head that was both sweet and sour.\nWhy Dodo, when she caught him in the china-closet half an hour afterward, nearly squeezed the breath out of his little body with a tender embrace, instead of shaking him for being there, and why she followed up this novel performance by the unexpected gift of a big slice of bread and jelly, remained one of the problems over which Demi puzzled his small wits, and was forced to leave unsolved forever.\n\n# UNDER THE UMBRELLA.\n\nWhile Laurie and Amy were taking conjugal strolls over velvet carpets, as they set their house in order, and planned a blissful future, Mr. Bhaer and Jo were enjoying promenades of a different sort, along muddy roads and sodden fields.\n\"I always do take a walk toward evening, and I don't know why I should give it up, just because I often happen to meet the Professor on his way out,\" said Jo to herself, after two or three encounters; for, though there were two paths to Meg's, whichever one she took she was sure to meet him, either going or returning. He was always walking rapidly, and never seemed to see her till quite close, when he would look as if his short-sighted eyes had failed to recognize the approaching lady till that moment. Then, if she was going to Meg's, he always had something for the babies; if her face was turned homeward, he had merely strolled down to see the river, and was just about returning, unless they were tired of his frequent calls.\n\nUnder the circumstances, what could Jo do but greet him civilly, and invite him in? If she _was_ tired of his visits, she concealed her weariness with perfect skill, and took care that there should be coffee for supper, \"as Friedrich--I mean Mr. Bhaer--doesn't like tea.\"\nBy the second week, every one knew perfectly well what was going on, yet every one tried to look as if they were stone-blind to the changes in Jo's face. They never asked why she sang about her work, did up her hair three times a day, and got so blooming with her evening exercise; and no one seemed to have the slightest suspicion that Professor Bhaer, while talking philosophy with the father, was giving the daughter lessons in love.\n\nJo couldn't even lose her heart in a decorous manner, but sternly tried to quench her feelings; and, failing to do so, led a somewhat agitated life. She was mortally afraid of being laughed at for surrendering, after her many and vehement declarations of independence. Laurie was her especial dread; but, thanks to the new manager, he behaved with praiseworthy propriety, never called Mr. Bhaer \"a capital old fellow\" in public, never alluded, in the remotest manner, to Jo's improved appearance, or expressed the least surprise at seeing the Professor's hat on the Marches' hall-table nearly every evening. But he exulted in private and longed for the time to come when he could give Jo a piece of plate, with a bear and a ragged staff on it as an appropriate coat-of-arms.\nFor a fortnight, the Professor came and went with lover-like regularity; then he stayed away for three whole days, and made no sign,--a proceeding which caused everybody to look sober, and Jo to become pensive, at first, and then--alas for romance!--very cross.\n\n\"Disgusted, I dare say, and gone home as suddenly as he came. It's nothing to me, of course; but I _should_ think he would have come and bid us good-by, like a gentleman,\" she said to herself, with a despairing look at the gate, as she put on her things for the customary walk, one dull afternoon.\n\"You'd better take the little umbrella, dear; it looks like rain,\" said her mother, observing that she had on her new bonnet, but not alluding to the fact.\n\n\"Yes, Marmee; do you want anything in town? I've got to run in and get some paper,\" returned Jo, pulling out the bow under her chin before the glass as an excuse for not looking at her mother.\n\"Yes; I want some twilled silesia, a paper of number nine needles, and two yards of narrow lavender ribbon. Have you got your thick boots on, and something warm under your cloak?\"\n\n\"If you happen to meet Mr. Bhaer, bring him home to tea. I quite long to see the dear man,\" added Mrs. March.\nJo heard _that_, but made no answer, except to kiss her mother, and walk rapidly away, thinking with a glow of gratitude, in spite of her heartache,--\n\n\"How good she is to me! What _do_ girls do who haven't any mothers to help them through their troubles?\"\nThe dry-goods stores were not down among the counting-houses, banks, and wholesale warerooms, where gentlemen most do congregate; but Jo found herself in that part of the city before she did a single errand, loitering along as if waiting for some one, examining engineering instruments in one window and samples of wool in another with most unfeminine interest; tumbling over barrels, being half-smothered by descending bales, and hustled unceremoniously by busy men who looked as if they wondered \"how the deuce she got there.\" A drop of rain on her cheek recalled her thoughts from baffled hopes to ruined ribbons; for the drops continued to fall, and, being a woman as well as a lover, she felt that, though it was too late to save her heart, she might her bonnet. Now she remembered the little umbrella, which she had forgotten to take in her hurry to be off; but regret was unavailing, and nothing could be done but borrow one or submit to a drenching. She looked up at the lowering sky, down at the crimson bow already flecked with black, forward along the muddy street, then one long, lingering look behind, at a certain grimy warehouse, with \"Hoffmann, Swartz, & Co.\" over the door, and said to herself, with a sternly reproachful air,--\n\n\"It serves me right! What business had I to put on all my best things and come philandering down here, hoping to see the Professor? Jo, I'm ashamed of you! No, you shall _not_ go there to borrow an umbrella, or find out where he is, from his friends. You shall trudge away, and do your errands in the rain; and if you catch your death and ruin your bonnet, it's no more than you deserve. Now then!\"\nWith that she rushed across the street so impetuously that she narrowly escaped annihilation from a passing truck, and precipitated herself into the arms of a stately old gentleman, who said, \"I beg pardon, ma'am,\" and looked mortally offended. Somewhat daunted, Jo righted herself, spread her handkerchief over the devoted ribbons, and, putting temptation behind her, hurried on, with increasing dampness about the ankles, and much clashing of umbrellas overhead. The fact that a somewhat dilapidated blue one remained stationary above the unprotected bonnet, attracted her attention; and, looking up, she saw Mr. Bhaer looking down.\n\n\"I feel to know the strong-minded lady who goes so bravely under many horse-noses, and so fast through much mud. What do you down here, my friend?\"\n\"I'm shopping.\"\n\nMr. Bhaer smiled, as he glanced from the pickle-factory on one side, to the wholesale hide and leather concern on the other; but he only said politely,--\n\"You haf no umbrella. May I go also, and take for you the bundles?\"\n\nJo's cheeks were as red as her ribbon, and she wondered what he thought of her; but she didn't care, for in a minute she found herself walking away arm-in-arm with her Professor, feeling as if the sun had suddenly burst out with uncommon brilliancy, that the world was all right again, and that one thoroughly happy woman was paddling through the wet that day.\n\"We thought you had gone,\" said Jo hastily, for she knew he was looking at her. Her bonnet wasn't big enough to hide her face, and she feared he might think the joy it betrayed unmaidenly.\n\n\"Did you believe that I should go with no farewell to those who haf been so heavenly kind to me?\" he asked so reproachfully that she felt as if she had insulted him by the suggestion, and answered heartily,--\n\"No, _I_ didn't; I knew you were busy about your own affairs, but we rather missed you,--father and mother especially.\"\n\n\"I'm always glad to see you, sir.\"\nIn her anxiety to keep her voice quite calm, Jo made it rather cool, and the frosty little monosyllable at the end seemed to chill the Professor, for his smile vanished, as he said gravely,--\n\n\"I haf no longer any business here; it is done.\"\n\n\"Successfully, I hope?\" said Jo, for the bitterness of disappointment was in that short reply of his.\n\"I ought to think so, for I haf a way opened to me by which I can make my bread and gif my Jünglings much help.\"\n\n\"Tell me, please! I like to know all about the--the boys,\" said Jo eagerly.\n\"That is so kind, I gladly tell you. My friends find for me a place in a college, where I teach as at home, and earn enough to make the way smooth for Franz and Emil. For this I should be grateful, should I not?\"\n\n\"Indeed you should. How splendid it will be to have you doing what you like, and be able to see you often, and the boys!\" cried Jo, clinging to the lads as an excuse for the satisfaction she could not help betraying.\n\"Ah! but we shall not meet often, I fear; this place is at the West.\"\n\n\"So far away!\" and Jo left her skirts to their fate, as if it didn't matter now what became of her clothes or herself.\nMr. Bhaer could read several languages, but he had not learned to read women yet. He flattered himself that he knew Jo pretty well, and was, therefore, much amazed by the contradictions of voice, face, and manner, which she showed him in rapid succession that day, for she was in half a dozen different moods in the course of half an hour. When she met him she looked surprised, though it was impossible to help suspecting that she had come for that express purpose. When he offered her his arm, she took it with a look that filled him with delight; but when he asked if she missed him, she gave such a chilly, formal reply that despair fell upon him. On learning his good fortune she almost clapped her hands: was the joy all for the boys? Then, on hearing his destination, she said, \"So far away!\" in a tone of despair that lifted him on to a pinnacle of hope; but the next minute she tumbled him down again by observing, like one entirely absorbed in the matter,--\n\n\"Here's the place for my errands; will you come in? It won't take long.\"\nJo rather prided herself upon her shopping capabilities, and particularly wished to impress her escort with the neatness and despatch with which she would accomplish the business. But, owing to the flutter she was in, everything went amiss; she upset the tray of needles, forgot the silesia was to be \"twilled\" till it was cut off, gave the wrong change, and covered herself with confusion by asking for lavender ribbon at the calico counter. Mr. Bhaer stood by, watching her blush and blunder; and, as he watched, his own bewilderment seemed to subside, for he was beginning to see that on some occasions women, like dreams, go by contraries.\n\nWhen they came out, he put the parcel under his arm with a more cheerful aspect, and splashed through the puddles as if he rather enjoyed it, on the whole.\n\"Should we not do a little what you call shopping for the babies, and haf a farewell feast to-night if I go for my last call at your so pleasant home?\" he asked, stopping before a window full of fruit and flowers.\n\n\"What will we buy?\" said Jo, ignoring the latter part of his speech, and sniffing the mingled odors with an affectation of delight as they went in.\n\"May they haf oranges and figs?\" asked Mr. Bhaer, with a paternal air.\n\n\"Hamburg grapes; yes, we shall surely drink to the Fatherland in those?\"\nJo frowned upon that piece of extravagance, and asked why he didn't buy a frail of dates, a cask of raisins, and a bag of almonds, and done with it? Whereat Mr. Bhaer confiscated her purse, produced his own, and finished the marketing by buying several pounds of grapes, a pot of rosy daisies, and a pretty jar of honey, to be regarded in the light of a demijohn. Then, distorting his pockets with the knobby bundles, and giving her the flowers to hold, he put up the old umbrella, and they travelled on again.\n\n\"Miss Marsch, I haf a great favor to ask of you,\" began the Professor, after a moist promenade of half a block.\n\n\"Yes, sir;\" and Jo's heart began to beat so hard she was afraid he would hear it.\n\"I am bold to say it in spite of the rain, because so short a time remains to me.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir;\" and Jo nearly crushed the small flower-pot with the sudden squeeze she gave it.\n\"I wish to get a little dress for my Tina, and I am too stupid to go alone. Will you kindly gif me a word of taste and help?\"\n\n\"Yes, sir;\" and Jo felt as calm and cool, all of a sudden, as if she had stepped into a refrigerator.\n\"Perhaps also a shawl for Tina's mother, she is so poor and sick, and the husband is such a care. Yes, yes, a thick, warm shawl would be a friendly thing to take the little mother.\"\n\n\"I'll do it with pleasure, Mr. Bhaer. I'm going very fast and he's getting dearer every minute,\" added Jo to herself; then, with a mental shake, she entered into the business with an energy which was pleasant to behold.\nMr. Bhaer left it all to her, so she chose a pretty gown for Tina, and then ordered out the shawls. The clerk, being a married man, condescended to take an interest in the couple, who appeared to be shopping for their family.\n\n\"Your lady may prefer this; it's a superior article, a most desirable color, quite chaste and genteel,\" he said, shaking out a comfortable gray shawl, and throwing it over Jo's shoulders.\n\n\"Does this suit you, Mr. Bhaer?\" she asked, turning her back to him, and feeling deeply grateful for the chance of hiding her face.\n\n\"Excellently well; we will haf it,\" answered the Professor, smiling to himself as he paid for it, while Jo continued to rummage the counters like a confirmed bargain-hunter.\n\"Now shall we go home?\" he asked, as if the words were very pleasant to him.\n\n\"Yes; it's late, and I'm _so_ tired.\" Jo's voice was more pathetic than she knew; for now the sun seemed to have gone in as suddenly as it came out, the world grew muddy and miserable again, and for the first time she discovered that her feet were cold, her head ached, and that her heart was colder than the former, fuller of pain than the latter. Mr. Bhaer was going away; he only cared for her as a friend; it was all a mistake, and the sooner it was over the better. With this idea in her head, she hailed an approaching omnibus with such a hasty gesture that the daisies flew out of the pot and were badly damaged.\n\"This is not our omniboos,\" said the Professor, waving the loaded vehicle away, and stopping to pick up the poor little flowers.\n\n\"I beg your pardon, I didn't see the name distinctly. Never mind, I can walk. I'm used to plodding in the mud,\" returned Jo, winking hard, because she would have died rather than openly wipe her eyes.\nMr. Bhaer saw the drops on her cheeks, though she turned her head away; the sight seemed to touch him very much, for, suddenly stooping down, he asked in a tone that meant a great deal,--\n\n\"Heart's dearest, why do you cry?\"\nNow, if Jo had not been new to this sort of thing she would have said she wasn't crying, had a cold in her head, or told any other feminine fib proper to the occasion; instead of which that undignified creature answered, with an irrepressible sob,--\n\n\"Because you are going away.\"\n\n\"Ach, mein Gott, that is _so_ good!\" cried Mr. Bhaer, managing to clasp his hands in spite of the umbrella and the bundles. \"Jo, I haf nothing but much love to gif you; I came to see if you could care for it, and I waited to be sure that I was something more than a friend. Am I? Can you make a little place in your heart for old Fritz?\" he added, all in one breath.\n\"Oh, yes!\" said Jo; and he was quite satisfied, for she folded both hands over his arm, and looked up at him with an expression that plainly showed how happy she would be to walk through life beside him, even though she had no better shelter than the old umbrella, if he carried it.\n\nIt was certainly proposing under difficulties, for, even if he had desired to do so, Mr. Bhaer could not go down upon his knees, on account of the mud; neither could he offer Jo his hand, except figuratively, for both were full; much less could he indulge in tender demonstrations in the open street, though he was near it: so the only way in which he could express his rapture was to look at her, with an expression which glorified his face to such a degree that there actually seemed to be little rainbows in the drops that sparkled on his beard. If he had not loved Jo very much, I don't think he could have done it _then_, for she looked far from lovely, with her skirts in a deplorable state, her rubber boots splashed to the ankle, and her bonnet a ruin. Fortunately, Mr. Bhaer considered her the most beautiful woman living, and she found him more \"Jove-like\" than ever, though his hat-brim was quite limp with the little rills trickling thence upon his shoulders (for he held the umbrella all over Jo), and every finger of his gloves needed mending.\nPassers-by probably thought them a pair of harmless lunatics, for they entirely forgot to hail a 'bus, and strolled leisurely along, oblivious of deepening dusk and fog. Little they cared what anybody thought, for they were enjoying the happy hour that seldom comes but once in any life, the magical moment which bestows youth on the old, beauty on the plain, wealth on the poor, and gives human hearts a foretaste of heaven. The Professor looked as if he had conquered a kingdom, and the world had nothing more to offer him in the way of bliss; while Jo trudged beside him, feeling as if her place had always been there, and wondering how she ever could have chosen any other lot. Of course, she was the first to speak--intelligibly, I mean, for the emotional remarks which followed her impetuous \"Oh, yes!\" were not of a coherent or reportable character.\n\n\"Friedrich, why didn't you--\"\n\"Ah, heaven, she gifs me the name that no one speaks since Minna died!\" cried the Professor, pausing in a puddle to regard her with grateful delight.\n\n\"I always call you so to myself--I forgot; but I won't, unless you like it.\"\n\"Like it? it is more sweet to me than I can tell. Say 'thou,' also, and I shall say your language is almost as beautiful as mine.\"\n\n\"Isn't 'thou' a little sentimental?\" asked Jo, privately thinking it a lovely monosyllable.\n\n\"Sentimental? Yes. Thank Gott, we Germans believe in sentiment, and keep ourselves young mit it. Your English 'you' is so cold, say 'thou,' heart's dearest, it means so much to me,\" pleaded Mr. Bhaer, more like a romantic student than a grave professor.\n\n\"Well, then, why didn't thou tell me all this sooner?\" asked Jo bashfully.\n\"Now I shall haf to show thee all my heart, and I so gladly will, because thou must take care of it hereafter. See, then, my Jo,--ah, the dear, funny little name!--I had a wish to tell something the day I said good-by, in New York; but I thought the handsome friend was betrothed to thee, and so I spoke not. Wouldst thou have said 'Yes,' then, if I _had_ spoken?\"\n\n\"I don't know; I'm afraid not, for I didn't have any heart just then.\"\n\n\"Prut! that I do not believe. It was asleep till the fairy prince came through the wood, and waked it up. Ah, well, 'Die erste Liebe ist die beste;' but that I should not expect.\"\n\"Yes, the first love _is_ the best; so be contented, for I never had another. Teddy was only a boy, and soon got over his little fancy,\" said Jo, anxious to correct the Professor's mistake.\n\n\"Good! then I shall rest happy, and be sure that thou givest me all. I haf waited so long, I am grown selfish, as thou wilt find, Professorin.\"\n\"I like that,\" cried Jo, delighted with her new name. \"Now tell me what brought you, at last, just when I most wanted you?\"\n\n\"This;\" and Mr. Bhaer took a little worn paper out of his waistcoat-pocket.\nJo unfolded it, and looked much abashed, for it was one of her own contributions to a paper that paid for poetry, which accounted for her sending it an occasional attempt.\n\n\"How could that bring you?\" she asked, wondering what he meant.\n\"I found it by chance; I knew it by the names and the initials, and in it there was one little verse that seemed to call me. Read and find him; I will see that you go not in the wet.\"\n\nJo obeyed, and hastily skimmed through the lines which she had christened--\n\n\"Four little chests all in a row, Dim with dust, and worn by time, All fashioned and filled, long ago, By children now in their prime. Four little keys hung side by side, With faded ribbons, brave and gay When fastened there, with childish pride, Long ago, on a rainy day. Four little names, one on each lid, Carved out by a boyish hand, And underneath there lieth hid Histories of the happy band Once playing here, and pausing oft To hear the sweet refrain, That came and went on the roof aloft, In the falling summer rain.\n\"'Meg' on the first lid, smooth and fair. I look in with loving eyes, For folded here, with well-known care, A goodly gathering lies, The record of a peaceful life,-- Gifts to gentle child and girl, A bridal gown, lines to a wife, A tiny shoe, a baby curl. No toys in this first chest remain, For all are carried away, In their old age, to join again In another small Meg's play. Ah, happy mother! well I know You hear, like a sweet refrain, Lullabies ever soft and low In the falling summer rain.\n\n\"'Jo' on the next lid, scratched and worn, And within a motley store Of headless dolls, of school-books torn, Birds and beasts that speak no more; Spoils brought home from the fairy ground Only trod by youthful feet, Dreams of a future never found, Memories of a past still sweet; Half-writ poems, stories wild, April letters, warm and cold, Diaries of a wilful child, Hints of a woman early old; A woman in a lonely home, Hearing, like a sad refrain,-- 'Be worthy love, and love will come,' In the falling summer rain.\n\"My Beth! the dust is always swept From the lid that bears your name, As if by loving eyes that wept, By careful hands that often came. Death canonized for us one saint, Ever less human than divine, And still we lay, with tender plaint, Relics in this household shrine.-- The silver bell, so seldom rung, The little cap which last she wore, The fair, dead Catherine that hung By angels borne above her door; The songs she sang, without lament, In her prison-house of pain, Forever are they sweetly blent With the falling summer rain.\n\n\"Upon the last lid's polished field-- Legend now both fair and true-- A gallant knight bears on his shield, 'Amy,' in letters gold and blue. Within lie snoods that bound her hair, Slippers that have danced their last, Faded flowers laid by with care, Fans whose airy toils are past; Gay valentines, all ardent flames, Trifles that have borne their part In girlish hopes and fears and shames,-- The record of a maiden heart Now learning fairer, truer spells, Hearing, like a blithe refrain, The silver sound of bridal bells In the falling summer rain.\n\"Four little chests all in a row, Dim with dust, and worn by time, Four women, taught by weal and woe To love and labor in their prime. Four sisters, parted for an hour, None lost, one only gone before, Made by love's immortal power, Nearest and dearest evermore. Oh, when these hidden stores of ours Lie open to the Father's sight, May they be rich in golden hours, Deeds that show fairer for the light, Lives whose brave music long shall ring, Like a spirit-stirring strain, Souls that shall gladly soar and sing In the long sunshine after rain.\n\n\"It's very bad poetry, but I felt it when I wrote it, one day when I was very lonely, and had a good cry on a rag-bag. I never thought it would go where it could tell tales,\" said Jo, tearing up the verses the Professor had treasured so long.\n\"Let it go, it has done its duty, and I will haf a fresh one when I read all the brown book in which she keeps her little secrets,\" said Mr. Bhaer, with a smile, as he watched the fragments fly away on the wind. \"Yes,\" he added earnestly, \"I read that, and I think to myself, 'She has a sorrow, she is lonely, she would find comfort in true love. I haf a heart full, full for her; shall I not go and say, 'If this is not too poor a thing to gif for what I shall hope to receive, take it in Gott's name?'\"\n\n\"And so you came to find that it was not too poor, but the one precious thing I needed,\" whispered Jo.\n\"I had no courage to think that at first, heavenly kind as was your welcome to me. But soon I began to hope, and then I said, 'I will haf her if I die for it,' and so I will!\" cried Mr. Bhaer, with a defiant nod, as if the walls of mist closing round them were barriers which he was to surmount or valiantly knock down.\n\nJo thought that was splendid, and resolved to be worthy of her knight, though he did not come prancing on a charger in gorgeous array.\n\"What made you stay away so long?\" she asked presently, finding it so pleasant to ask confidential questions and get delightful answers that she could not keep silent.\n\n\"It was not easy, but I could not find the heart to take you from that so happy home until I could haf a prospect of one to give you, after much time, perhaps, and hard work. How could I ask you to gif up so much for a poor old fellow, who has no fortune but a little learning?\"\n\"I'm glad you _are_ poor; I couldn't bear a rich husband,\" said Jo decidedly, adding, in a softer tone, \"Don't fear poverty; I've known it long enough to lose my dread, and be happy working for those I love; and don't call yourself old,--forty is the prime of life. I couldn't help loving you if you were seventy!\"\n\nThe Professor found that so touching that he would have been glad of his handkerchief, if he could have got at it; as he couldn't, Jo wiped his eyes for him, and said, laughing, as she took away a bundle or two,--\n\"I may be strong-minded, but no one can say I'm out of my sphere now, for woman's special mission is supposed to be drying tears and bearing burdens. I'm to carry my share, Friedrich, and help to earn the home. Make up your mind to that, or I'll never go,\" she added resolutely, as he tried to reclaim his load.\n\n\"We shall see. Haf you patience to wait a long time, Jo? I must go away and do my work alone. I must help my boys first, because, even for you, I may not break my word to Minna. Can you forgif that, and be happy while we hope and wait?\"\n\"Yes, I know I can; for we love one another, and that makes all the rest easy to bear. I have my duty, also, and my work. I couldn't enjoy myself if I neglected them even for you, so there's no need of hurry or impatience. You can do your part out West, I can do mine here, and both be happy hoping for the best, and leaving the future to be as God wills.\"\n\n\"Ah! thou gifest me such hope and courage, and I haf nothing to gif back but a full heart and these empty hands,\" cried the Professor, quite overcome.\n\n# HARVEST TIME.\n\nJo never, never would learn to be proper; for when he said that as they stood upon the steps, she just put both hands into his, whispering tenderly, \"Not empty now;\" and, stooping down, kissed her Friedrich under the umbrella. It was dreadful, but she would have done it if the flock of draggle-tailed sparrows on the hedge had been human beings, for she was very far gone indeed, and quite regardless of everything but her own happiness. Though it came in such a very simple guise, that was the crowning moment of both their lives, when, turning from the night and storm and loneliness to the household light and warmth and peace waiting to receive them, with a glad \"Welcome home!\" Jo led her lover in, and shut the door.\nFor a year Jo and her Professor worked and waited, hoped and loved, met occasionally, and wrote such voluminous letters that the rise in the price of paper was accounted for, Laurie said. The second year began rather soberly, for their prospects did not brighten, and Aunt March died suddenly. But when their first sorrow was over,--for they loved the old lady in spite of her sharp tongue,--they found they had cause for rejoicing, for she had left Plumfield to Jo, which made all sorts of joyful things possible.\n\n\"It's a fine old place, and will bring a handsome sum; for of course you intend to sell it,\" said Laurie, as they were all talking the matter over, some weeks later.\n\"No, I don't,\" was Jo's decided answer, as she petted the fat poodle, whom she had adopted, out of respect to his former mistress.\n\n\"Yes, I do.\"\n\"But, my dear girl, it's an immense house, and will take a power of money to keep it in order. The garden and orchard alone need two or three men, and farming isn't in Bhaer's line, I take it.\"\n\n\"And you expect to live on the produce of the place? Well, that sounds paradisiacal, but you'll find it desperate hard work.\"\n\"The crop we are going to raise is a profitable one;\" and Jo laughed.\n\n\"Of what is this fine crop to consist, ma'am?\"\n\"Boys. I want to open a school for little lads,--a good, happy, homelike school, with me to take care of them, and Fritz to teach them.\"\n\n\"There's a truly Joian plan for you! Isn't that just like her?\" cried Laurie, appealing to the family, who looked as much surprised as he.\n\n\"So do I,\" added her husband, who welcomed the thought of a chance for trying the Socratic method of education on modern youth.\n\n\"It will be an immense care for Jo,\" said Meg, stroking the head of her one all-absorbing son.\n\n\"Jo can do it, and be happy in it. It's a splendid idea. Tell us all about it,\" cried Mr. Laurence, who had been longing to lend the lovers a hand, but knew that they would refuse his help.\n\"I knew you'd stand by me, sir. Amy does too--I see it in her eyes, though she prudently waits to turn it over in her mind before she speaks. Now, my dear people,\" continued Jo earnestly, \"just understand that this isn't a new idea of mine, but a long-cherished plan. Before my Fritz came, I used to think how, when I'd made my fortune, and no one needed me at home, I'd hire a big house, and pick up some poor, forlorn little lads, who hadn't any mothers, and take care of them, and make life jolly for them before it was too late. I see so many going to ruin, for want of help at the right minute; I love so to do anything for them; I seem to feel their wants, and sympathize with their troubles, and, oh, I should _so_ like to be a mother to them!\"\n\nMrs. March held out her hand to Jo, who took it, smiling, with tears in her eyes, and went on in the old enthusiastic way, which they had not seen for a long while.\n\"I told my plan to Fritz once, and he said it was just what he would like, and agreed to try it when we got rich. Bless his dear heart, he's been doing it all his life,--helping poor boys, I mean, not getting rich; that he'll never be; money doesn't stay in his pocket long enough to lay up any. But now, thanks to my good old aunt, who loved me better than I ever deserved, _I'm_ rich, at least I feel so, and we can live at Plumfield perfectly well, if we have a flourishing school. It's just the place for boys, the house is big, and the furniture strong and plain. There's plenty of room for dozens inside, and splendid grounds outside. They could help in the garden and orchard: such work is healthy, isn't it, sir? Then Fritz can train and teach in his own way, and father will help him. I can feed and nurse and pet and scold them; and mother will be my stand-by. I've always longed for lots of boys, and never had enough; now I can fill the house full, and revel in the little dears to my heart's content. Think what luxury,--Plumfield my own, and a wilderness of boys to enjoy it with me!\"\n\nAs Jo waved her hands, and gave a sigh of rapture, the family went off into a gale of merriment, and Mr. Laurence laughed till they thought he'd have an apoplectic fit.\n\n\"I don't see anything funny,\" she said gravely, when she could be heard. \"Nothing could be more natural or proper than for my Professor to open a school, and for me to prefer to reside on my own estate.\"\n\n\"She is putting on airs already,\" said Laurie, who regarded the idea in the light of a capital joke. \"But may I inquire how you intend to support the establishment? If all the pupils are little ragamuffins, I'm afraid your crop won't be profitable in a worldly sense, Mrs. Bhaer.\"\n\n\"Now don't be a wet-blanket, Teddy. Of course I shall have rich pupils, also,--perhaps begin with such altogether; then, when I've got a start, I can take a ragamuffin or two, just for a relish. Rich people's children often need care and comfort, as well as poor. I've seen unfortunate little creatures left to servants, or backward ones pushed forward, when it's real cruelty. Some are naughty through mismanagement or neglect, and some lose their mothers. Besides, the best have to get through the hobbledehoy age, and that's the very time they need most patience and kindness. People laugh at them, and hustle them about, try to keep them out of sight, and expect them to turn, all at once, from pretty children into fine young men. They don't complain much,--plucky little souls,--but they feel it. I've been through something of it, and I know all about it. I've a special interest in such young bears, and like to show them that I see the warm, honest, well-meaning boys' hearts, in spite of the clumsy arms and legs and the topsy-turvy heads. I've had experience, too, for haven't I brought up one boy to be a pride and honor to his family?\"\n\"I'll testify that you tried to do it,\" said Laurie, with a grateful look.\n\n\"And I've succeeded beyond my hopes; for here you are, a steady, sensible business man, doing heaps of good with your money, and laying up the blessings of the poor, instead of dollars. But you are not merely a business man: you love good and beautiful things, enjoy them yourself, and let others go halves, as you always did in the old times. I _am_ proud of you, Teddy, for you get better every year, and every one feels it, though you won't let them say so. Yes, and when I have my flock, I'll just point to you, and say, 'There's your model, my lads.'\"\n\nPoor Laurie didn't know where to look; for, man though he was, something of the old bashfulness came over him as this burst of praise made all faces turn approvingly upon him.\n\n\"I say, Jo, that's rather too much,\" he began, just in his old boyish way. \"You have all done more for me than I can ever thank you for, except by doing my best not to disappoint you. You have rather cast me off lately, Jo, but I've had the best of help, nevertheless; so, if I've got on at all, you may thank these two for it;\" and he laid one hand gently on his grandfather's white head, the other on Amy's golden one, for the three were never far apart.\n\"I do think that families are the most beautiful things in all the world!\" burst out Jo, who was in an unusually uplifted frame of mind just then. \"When I have one of my own, I hope it will be as happy as the three I know and love the best. If John and my Fritz were only here, it would be quite a little heaven on earth,\" she added more quietly. And that night, when she went to her room, after a blissful evening of family counsels, hopes, and plans, her heart was so full of happiness that she could only calm it by kneeling beside the empty bed always near her own, and thinking tender thoughts of Beth.\n\nIt was a very astonishing year altogether, for things seemed to happen in an unusually rapid and delightful manner. Almost before she knew where she was, Jo found herself married and settled at Plumfield. Then a family of six or seven boys sprung up like mushrooms, and flourished surprisingly, poor boys as well as rich; for Mr. Laurence was continually finding some touching case of destitution, and begging the Bhaers to take pity on the child, and he would gladly pay a trifle for its support. In this way the sly old gentleman got round proud Jo, and furnished her with the style of boy in which she most delighted.\n\nOf course it was up-hill work at first, and Jo made queer mistakes; but the wise Professor steered her safely into calmer waters, and the most rampant ragamuffin was conquered in the end. How Jo did enjoy her \"wilderness of boys,\" and how poor, dear Aunt March would have lamented had she been there to see the sacred precincts of prim, well-ordered Plumfield overrun with Toms, Dicks, and Harrys! There was a sort of poetic justice about it, after all, for the old lady had been the terror of the boys for miles round; and now the exiles feasted freely on forbidden plums, kicked up the gravel with profane boots unreproved, and played cricket in the big field where the irritable \"cow with a crumpled horn\" used to invite rash youths to come and be tossed. It became a sort of boys' paradise, and Laurie suggested that it should be called the \"Bhaer-garten,\" as a compliment to its master and appropriate to its inhabitants.\nIt never was a fashionable school, and the Professor did not lay up a fortune; but it _was_ just what Jo intended it to be,--\"a happy, homelike place for boys, who needed teaching, care, and kindness.\" Every room in the big house was soon full; every little plot in the garden soon had its owner; a regular menagerie appeared in barn and shed, for pet animals were allowed; and, three times a day, Jo smiled at her Fritz from the head of a long table lined on either side with rows of happy young faces, which all turned to her with affectionate eyes, confiding words, and grateful hearts, full of love for \"Mother Bhaer.\" She had boys enough now, and did not tire of them, though they were not angels, by any means, and some of them caused both Professor and Professorin much trouble and anxiety. But her faith in the good spot which exists in the heart of the naughtiest, sauciest, most tantalizing little ragamuffin gave her patience, skill, and, in time, success; for no mortal boy could hold out long with Father Bhaer shining on him as benevolently as the sun, and Mother Bhaer forgiving him seventy times seven. Very precious to Jo was the friendship of the lads; their penitent sniffs and whispers after wrong-doing; their droll or touching little confidences; their pleasant enthusiasms, hopes, and plans; even their misfortunes, for they only endeared them to her all the more. There were slow boys and bashful boys; feeble boys and riotous boys; boys that lisped and boys that stuttered; one or two lame ones; and a merry little quadroon, who could not be taken in elsewhere, but who was welcome to the \"Bhaer-garten,\" though some people predicted that his admission would ruin the school.\n\nYes; Jo was a very happy woman there, in spite of hard work, much anxiety, and a perpetual racket. She enjoyed it heartily, and found the applause of her boys more satisfying than any praise of the world; for now she told no stories except to her flock of enthusiastic believers and admirers. As the years went on, two little lads of her own came to increase her happiness,--Rob, named for grandpa, and Teddy, a happy-go-lucky baby, who seemed to have inherited his papa's sunshiny temper as well as his mother's lively spirit. How they ever grew up alive in that whirlpool of boys was a mystery to their grandma and aunts; but they flourished like dandelions in spring, and their rough nurses loved and served them well.\nThere were a great many holidays at Plumfield, and one of the most delightful was the yearly apple-picking; for then the Marches, Laurences, Brookes, and Bhaers turned out in full force, and made a day of it. Five years after Jo's wedding, one of these fruitful festivals occurred,--a mellow October day, when the air was full of an exhilarating freshness which made the spirits rise, and the blood dance healthily in the veins. The old orchard wore its holiday attire; golden-rod and asters fringed the mossy walls; grasshoppers skipped briskly in the sere grass, and crickets chirped like fairy pipers at a feast; squirrels were busy with their small harvesting; birds twittered their adieux from the alders in the lane; and every tree stood ready to send down its shower of red or yellow apples at the first shake. Everybody was there; everybody laughed and sang, climbed up and tumbled down; everybody declared that there never had been such a perfect day or such a jolly set to enjoy it; and every one gave themselves up to the simple pleasures of the hour as freely as if there were no such things as care or sorrow in the world.\n\nMr. March strolled placidly about, quoting Tusser, Cowley, and Columella to Mr. Laurence, while enjoying--\n\n\"The gentle apple's winey juice.\"\nThe Professor charged up and down the green aisles like a stout Teutonic knight, with a pole for a lance, leading on the boys, who made a hook and ladder company of themselves, and performed wonders in the way of ground and lofty tumbling. Laurie devoted himself to the little ones, rode his small daughter in a bushel-basket, took Daisy up among the birds' nests, and kept adventurous Rob from breaking his neck. Mrs. March and Meg sat among the apple piles like a pair of Pomonas, sorting the contributions that kept pouring in; while Amy, with a beautiful motherly expression in her face, sketched the various groups, and watched over one pale lad, who sat adoring her with his little crutch beside him.\n\nJo was in her element that day, and rushed about, with her gown pinned up, her hat anywhere but on her head, and her baby tucked under her arm, ready for any lively adventure which might turn up. Little Teddy bore a charmed life, for nothing ever happened to him, and Jo never felt any anxiety when he was whisked up into a tree by one lad, galloped off on the back of another, or supplied with sour russets by his indulgent papa, who labored under the Germanic delusion that babies could digest anything, from pickled cabbage to buttons, nails, and their own small shoes. She knew that little Ted would turn up again in time, safe and rosy, dirty and serene, and she always received him back with a hearty welcome, for Jo loved her babies tenderly.\nAt four o'clock a lull took place, and baskets remained empty, while the apple-pickers rested, and compared rents and bruises. Then Jo and Meg, with a detachment of the bigger boys, set forth the supper on the grass, for an out-of-door tea was always the crowning joy of the day. The land literally flowed with milk and honey on such occasions, for the lads were not required to sit at table, but allowed to partake of refreshment as they liked,--freedom being the sauce best beloved by the boyish soul. They availed themselves of the rare privilege to the fullest extent, for some tried the pleasing experiment of drinking milk while standing on their heads, others lent a charm to leap-frog by eating pie in the pauses of the game, cookies were sown broadcast over the field, and apple-turnovers roosted in the trees like a new style of bird. The little girls had a private tea-party, and Ted roved among the edibles at his own sweet will.\n\nWhen no one could eat any more, the Professor proposed the first regular toast, which was always drunk at such times,--\"Aunt March, God bless her!\" A toast heartily given by the good man, who never forgot how much he owed her, and quietly drunk by the boys, who had been taught to keep her memory green.\n\n\"Now, grandma's sixtieth birthday! Long life to her, with three times three!\"\n\nThat was given with a will, as you may well believe; and the cheering once begun, it was hard to stop it. Everybody's health was proposed, from Mr. Laurence, who was considered their special patron, to the astonished guinea-pig, who had strayed from its proper sphere in search of its young master. Demi, as the oldest grandchild, then presented the queen of the day with various gifts, so numerous that they were transported to the festive scene in a wheelbarrow. Funny presents, some of them, but what would have been defects to other eyes were ornaments to grandma's,--for the children's gifts were all their own. Every stitch Daisy's patient little fingers had put into the handkerchiefs she hemmed was better than embroidery to Mrs. March; Demi's shoe-box was a miracle of mechanical skill, though the cover wouldn't shut; Rob's footstool had a wiggle in its uneven legs, that she declared was very soothing; and no page of the costly book Amy's child gave her was so fair as that on which appeared, in tipsy capitals, the words,--\"To dear Grandma, from her little Beth.\"\nDuring this ceremony the boys had mysteriously disappeared; and, when Mrs. March had tried to thank her children, and broken down, while Teddy wiped her eyes on his pinafore, the Professor suddenly began to sing. Then, from above him, voice after voice took up the words, and from tree to tree echoed the music of the unseen choir, as the boys sung, with all their hearts, the little song Jo had written, Laurie set to music, and the Professor trained his lads to give with the best effect. This was something altogether new, and it proved a grand success; for Mrs. March couldn't get over her surprise, and insisted on shaking hands with every one of the featherless birds, from tall Franz and Emil to the little quadroon, who had the sweetest voice of all.\n\nAfter this, the boys dispersed for a final lark, leaving Mrs. March and her daughters under the festival tree.\n\n\"I don't think I ever ought to call myself 'Unlucky Jo' again, when my greatest wish has been so beautifully gratified,\" said Mrs. Bhaer, taking Teddy's little fist out of the milk-pitcher, in which he was rapturously churning.\n\n\"And yet your life is very different from the one you pictured so long ago. Do you remember our castles in the air?\" asked Amy, smiling as she watched Laurie and John playing cricket with the boys.\n\n\"Dear fellows! It does my heart good to see them forget business, and frolic for a day,\" answered Jo, who now spoke in a maternal way of all mankind. \"Yes, I remember; but the life I wanted then seems selfish, lonely, and cold to me now. I haven't given up the hope that I may write a good book yet, but I can wait, and I'm sure it will be all the better for such experiences and illustrations as these;\" and Jo pointed from the lively lads in the distance to her father, leaning on the Professor's arm, as they walked to and fro in the sunshine, deep in one of the conversations which both enjoyed so much, and then to her mother, sitting enthroned among her daughters, with their children in her lap and at her feet, as if all found help and happiness in the face which never could grow old to them.\n\"My castle was the most nearly realized of all. I asked for splendid things, to be sure, but in my heart I knew I should be satisfied, if I had a little home, and John, and some dear children like these. I've got them all, thank God, and am the happiest woman in the world;\" and Meg laid her hand on her tall boy's head, with a face full of tender and devout content.\n\n\"My castle is very different from what I planned, but I would not alter it, though, like Jo, I don't relinquish all my artistic hopes, or confine myself to helping others fulfil their dreams of beauty. I've begun to model a figure of baby, and Laurie says it is the best thing I've ever done. I think so myself, and mean to do it in marble, so that, whatever happens, I may at least keep the image of my little angel.\"\nAs Amy spoke, a great tear dropped on the golden hair of the sleeping child in her arms; for her one well-beloved daughter was a frail little creature and the dread of losing her was the shadow over Amy's sunshine. This cross was doing much for both father and mother, for one love and sorrow bound them closely together. Amy's nature was growing sweeter, deeper, and more tender; Laurie was growing more serious, strong, and firm; and both were learning that beauty, youth, good fortune, even love itself, cannot keep care and pain, loss and sorrow, from the most blest; for--\n\n\"Into each life some rain must fall, Some days must be dark and sad and dreary.\"\n\n\"She is growing better, I am sure of it, my dear. Don't despond, but hope and keep happy,\" said Mrs. March, as tender-hearted Daisy stooped from her knee, to lay her rosy cheek against her little cousin's pale one.\n\n\"I never ought to, while I have you to cheer me up, Marmee, and Laurie to take more than half of every burden,\" replied Amy warmly. \"He never lets me see his anxiety, but is so sweet and patient with me, so devoted to Beth, and such a stay and comfort to me always, that I can't love him enough. So, in spite of my one cross, I can say with Meg, 'Thank God, I'm a happy woman.'\"\n\"There's no need for me to say it, for every one can see that I'm far happier than I deserve,\" added Jo, glancing from her good husband to her chubby children, tumbling on the grass beside her. \"Fritz is getting gray and stout; I'm growing as thin as a shadow, and am thirty; we never shall be rich, and Plumfield may burn up any night, for that incorrigible Tommy Bangs _will_ smoke sweet-fern cigars under the bed-clothes, though he's set himself afire three times already. But in spite of these unromantic facts, I have nothing to complain of, and never was so jolly in my life. Excuse the remark, but living among boys, I can't help using their expressions now and then.\"\n\n\"Yes, Jo, I think your harvest will be a good one,\" began Mrs. March, frightening away a big black cricket that was staring Teddy out of countenance.\n\"Not half so good as yours, mother. Here it is, and we never can thank you enough for the patient sowing and reaping you have done,\" cried Jo, with the loving impetuosity which she never could outgrow.\n\n\"I hope there will be more wheat and fewer tares every year,\" said Amy softly.\n\n\"A large sheaf, but I know there's room in your heart for it, Marmee dear,\" added Meg's tender voice.\n\nTouched to the heart, Mrs. March could only stretch out her arms, as if to gather children and grandchildren to herself, and say, with face and voice full of motherly love, gratitude, and humility,--\n\n\"O, my girls, however long you may live, I never can wish you a greater happiness than this!\"\n\n# THE LITTLE WOMEN SERIES.\n\nLouisa M. Alcott's Writings\n=Little Women=; or Meg, Jo, Beth, and Amy. Illustrated. 16mo. $1.50.\n\n=Little Men.= Life at Plumfield with Jo's Boys. Illustrated. 16mo. $1.50.\n=Jo's Boys and How They Turned Out.= A Sequel to \"Little Men.\" Portrait of Author. 16mo. $1.50.\n\n=Eight Cousins=; or, The Aunt-Hill. Illustrated. 16mo. $1.50.\n=Rose in Bloom.= A Sequel to \"Eight Cousins.\" Illustrated. 16mo. $1.50.\n\n=Jack and Jill.= A Village Story. Illustrated. 16mo. $1.50.\nThe above eight volumes, uniformly bound in cloth, gilt, in box, $12.00.\n\n# THE SPINNING-WHEEL SERIES\n\nTHE LITTLE WOMEN SERIES. _New Illustrated Edition._ Printed from new plates with new cover designs, and illustrated with 84 full-page plates from drawings especially made for this edition by Reginald B. Birch, Alice Barber Stephens, Jessie Willcox Smith, and Harriet Roosevelt Richards. 8 vols. Crown 8vo. Decorated cloth, gilt, in box. $16.00. Separately, $2.00.\n=Spinning-Wheel Stories.= =Silver Pitchers.= =Proverb Stories.= =A Garland for Girls.=\n\n# AUNT JO'S SCRAP-BAG\n\nTHE SPINNING-WHEEL SERIES. _New Illustrated Edition._ Uniform in size with the Illustrated Edition of the Little Women Series. With 36 full-page plates by well-known artists. 4 vols. Crown 8vo. Decorated cloth. In box, $6.00. Separately, $1.50.\n=My Boys.= =Shawl-Straps.= =Cupid and Chow-Chow.= =My Girls.= =Jimmy's Cruise in the Pinafore.= =An Old-Fashioned Thanksgiving.=\n\n# NOVELS, ETC.\n\n3 vols. Each, $1.00. The set uniformly bound in cloth, gilt, in box, $3.00.\n=Hospital Sketches.= =Work.= =Comic Tragedies.= =Moods.= =A Modern Mephistopheles.= =Life of Louisa May Alcott.=\n\nA simple story of the home life of four girls. A portrayal of child life, natural, wholesome, and inspiring. One of the best and most popular children's books ever written.\n2. LITTLE MEN: Life at Plumfield with Jo's Boys\n\nIllustrated. 16mo. $1.50.\nGives delightful pictures of boy life at old Plumfield, and is brimful of activity, merriment, health, and happiness.\n\nIllustrated. 16mo. $1.50.\nThis sequel to \"Little Men\" takes up the story and carries Jo's boys through the home struggles and adventures in the outside world until they are fairly launched on the sea of manhood.\n\nIllustrated. 16mo. $1.50.\nThe heroine of this book is shown as a possible improvement upon the girl of the period, who seems sadly ignorant or ashamed of the good old fashions which made women truly beautiful and honored.\n\n5. EIGHT COUSINS; or, the Aunt-Hill\nIllustrated. 16mo. $1.50.\n\nThe story of a pretty-faced and sunny-tempered little girl, obliged by the death of her parents to live with her uncle and her aunts, thereby coming in contact with seven cousins--all boys.\nIllustrated. 16mo. $1.50.\n\nThis sequel to \"Eight Cousins\" carries on the story of Rose and the cousins, and is full of vivacity, fresh and stirring incident, and brilliant character painting.\nIllustrated. 16mo. $1.50.\n\nBen and his dog Sancho run away from a circus and find a home with Bob and Betty in the old house under the lilacs. Told in Miss Alcott's best style.\n\nIllustrated. 16mo. $1.50.\nA vivid yet natural portrayal of home and school life in a New England village, full of the sympathetic quality which lends such a charm to Miss Alcott's writings. It is a lively and jolly narrative.\n\n# COMIC TRAGEDIES\n\n_Uniform with \"The Little Women Series.\"_\nWritten by \"Jo\" and \"Meg,\" and acted by the \"Little Women,\" with a Foreword by \"Meg.\" Portraits, etc. 16mo. $1.50.\n\n# LOUISA MAY ALCOTT\n\nHer Life, Letters, and Journals. Edited by Ednah D. Cheney. With photogravure portraits, etc. 16mo. $1.50.\nOther Stories by LOUISA M. ALCOTT\n\n# SPINNING-WHEEL STORIES\n\nFour volumes of healthy and hearty short stories so told as to fascinate the young people, while inculcating sturdy courage and kindness to the weak in the boys, and in the girls those virtues which fit them for filling a woman's place in the home.\n1. SPINNING-WHEEL STORIES With twelve initial illustrations. 16mo. $1.25.\n\n# AUNT JO'S SCRAP BAG\n\n4. A GARLAND FOR GIRLS With illustrations. 16mo. $1.25. The above four volumes, uniformly bound in cloth, gilt, in box, $5.00.\nSix books of jolly, readable stories told in Miss Alcott's best style and sure to please young people.\n\n2. SHAWL-STRAPS Illustrated. Story of a voyage abroad. 16mo. $1.00\n3. CUPID AND CHOW-CHOW Illustrated. 16mo. $1.00.\n\n5. JIMMY'S CRUISE IN THE PINAFORE, ETC. Illustrated. 16mo. $1.00.\n6. AN OLD-FASHIONED THANKSGIVING Illustrated. 16mo. $1.00.\n\n# LULU'S LIBRARY\n\nThe above six volumes, uniformly bound in cloth, gilt, in box, $6.00.\nDelightful short stories, many of them founded on incidents from Miss Alcott's life. Told so as to attract children, and all showing the spirit of cheerful accomplishment in the face of discouragements.\n\nThree volumes. Each, $1.00. The set, uniformly bound in cloth, gilt, in box, $3.00.\n\n# MISS ALCOTT'S NOVELS\n\nHOSPITAL SKETCHES and Camp and Fireside Stories. With illustrations. 16mo. $1.50.\nWORK A Story of Experience. Illustrated by Sol Eytinge. 16mo. $1.50.\n\nMOODS A Novel. 16mo. $1.50.\nA MODERN MEPHISTOPHELES and a Whisper in the Dark. 16mo. $1.50.\n\nOther Stories by LOUISA M. ALCOTT\nA HOLE IN THE WALL. Illustrated. 12mo. 50 cents.\n\nAn account of a poor boy's admiration for a beautiful garden to which he is invited by a little girl friend. (\"How They Camped Out\" in same volume.)\nMARJORIE'S THREE GIFTS. Illustrated. 12mo. 50 cents.\n\nA fairy tale told Marjorie comes true, and there enter into her life three good fairies: Industry, Cheerfulness, and Love. (\"Roses and Forget-me-nots\" in same volume.)\nMAY FLOWERS. Illustrated. 12mo. 50 cents.\n\nThe experiences of six earnest young girls who try to make the sad lives about them happier. Full of sensible hints as to wisest methods of charity.\n\nA CHRISTMAS DREAM. Illustrated. 12mo. 50 cents.\n\nA rather spoiled child gets her first real enjoyment of Christmas by making others happy. (\"Baa! Baa!\" in same volume.)\n\nTHE CANDY COUNTRY. Illustrated. 12mo. 50 cents.\n\nA quaint little fable in which the young heroine visits Candy-land and is finally contented to return to Bread-land. (\"How They Ran Away\" in same volume.)\nLITTLE BUTTON ROSE. Illustrated. 12mo. 50 cents.\n\nA bright, vivacious child visits her maiden aunts. Her influence on the somewhat narrow characters about her is delightfully described.\n\nTwo girls travel through Europe. The frivolous Ethel learns the advantages of culture and simple dignity from her companion.\n\nMOUNTAIN-LAUREL AND MAIDENHAIR. Illustrated. 12mo. 50 cents.\n\nThe story of a New Hampshire farmer's daughter who is fond of writing verses.\nPANSIES AND WATER-LILIES. Illustrated. 12mo. 50 cents.\n\n\"Pansies\" is a story of a girls' discussion of books; \"Water-Lilies\" is a romance by the sea-shore.\n\nA droll account of the travels of two dolls. (\"Shadow-Children\" and \"The Moss People\" in same volume.)\nMORNING GLORIES AND QUEEN ASTOR. Illustrated. 12mo. 50 cents.\n\n# THE LITTLE MEN PLAY. THE LITTLE WOMEN PLAY.\n\nAunt Wee changes Daisy from a petulant to a cheerful girl by interesting her in the wonderful world of nature which Daisy has never before learned to appreciate justly.\nAdapted from Miss Alcott's famous stories, \"Little Men\" and \"Little Women,\" by Elizabeth L. Gould. Illustrated. 12mo. Price 50 cents each.\n\nTwo forty-five minute plays of two acts each, for eight or ten little people. They will prove a source of limitless delight.\nLITTLE, BROWN, & COMPANY, Publishers\n\nProject Gutenberg has two versions of Little Women by Louisa May Alcott. Original Edition Illustrated Edition\nWelcome to the Project Gutenberg Illustrated Edition of Little Women by Louisa May Alcott, published by Little, Brown, and Company. Some versions of the book, such as this one, use the full title of the book from the title page, _Little Women; Or Meg, Jo, Beth, and Amy_.\n\nWe used the version of the book from Little, Brown, and Company: copyright 1896, for this transcription. A scanned copy of this book is available through the internet archive, courtesy of the New York Public Library.\n\nA copy of the first version of the novel, published in 1869, was consulted for emendations, the proper rendering of words hyphenated and split between two lines for spacing, and other issues in transcribing the novel. We are not trying to change this version of the novel back to the 1869 novel, but correct the errors that were made in re-transcribing and updating the text.\nPassages in italics were indicated by _underscores_.\n\nThroughout the dialogues, there were words used to mimic accents of the speakers. Those words were retained as-is.\nErrors in punctuations and hyphenation were not corrected unless otherwise noted below.\n\nA note about spacing of illustrations: If there are four lines above the illustration, then the illustration was at the top of a new chapter. If there are four lines below the illustration, then the illustration was at the end of a chapter. If there is two lines between a chapter heading and the illustration, then the illustration was an inline illustration (usually a drop-cap illustration).\nOn page vii, in the Table of Contents, change page 7 to 1 for the beginning of Chapter One.\n\nIn the List of Illustrations, for the illustration on page 147, \"postmistress\" was replaced with \"post-mistress\".\n\nIn the List of Illustrations, for the illustration on page 235, \"tea-pot\" was replaced with \"teapot\".\nOn page 30, the punctuation after 'stained they are' may be a colon, but on page 41 of the 1869 book, it is a semicolon. We used the semi-colon.\n\nOn page 34, transcribe red-headed with the hyphen. See page 44 of the 1869 novel.\nOn page 40, a period was added after \"room where old Mr\". See page 50 of the 1869 novel.\n\nOn page 41, the semicolon after \"Laurie went on the box\" was replaced with a comma. See page 52 of the 1869 novel.\nOn page 62, mantel-piece was transcribed with the hyphen. See Page 75 of the 1869 novel.\n\nOn page 63, checked the clause \"and I've been trying to do it this ever so long.\" It is written the same way on page 77 of the 1869 book. No change was made.\nOn page 64, add period after \"red and shining with merriment.\" See page 79 of the 1869 book.\n\nOn page 68, changed weet to sweet in 'the damp weet air.' See page 84 of the 1869 novel.\nOn page 79, add comma after I remain in the letter. See page 95 of the 1869 novel.\n\nOn page 84, ferrule was an adjustment from the 1869 book, which only used one r in spelling the word (see page 101). Webster's dictionary spells ferrule with two rs.\nOn page 109, a period was added after \"and the old man quite dotes on them\". See page 130 of the 1869 novel.\n\nOn page 109, a period was added after \"asked another voice\". See page 131 of the 1869 novel.\nOn page 112, change colon to semicolon after \"if you don't;\"--see page 134 of the 1869 novel.\n\nOn page 113, transcribe ear-rings with the hyphen. See page 135 of the 1869 novel.\nOn Page 123, \"One thing remember, my girls:\" is written as it appears in the 1896 novel. A comma instead of a colon was used after my girls in the 1869 novel (see page 146). \"One thing =to= remember,\" may work better today, or even \"Remember one thing,\" but we left this as Ms. Alcott wrote it.\n\nOn Page 124, the P. C. is the Pickwick Club from a novel by Charles Dickens. Samuel Pickwick, Tracy Tupman, Augustus Snodgrass, and Nathaniel Winkle were introdued by Charles Dickens in the first chapter of The Pickwick Papers. Samuel Weller makes his first appearance in Chapter Ten of that novel.\nOn page 128, in the Pickwick Portfolio, there is no period after \"it is nearly school time\" in N. Winkle's letter. This period was also missing on page 151 of the 1869 novel. The missing period was intentional.\n\nOn page 135, the ambiguous punctuation after \"Oh, dear, no!\" is an exclamation point. See page 160 of the 1869 novel.\nOn page 146, change buttonholes to button-holes. On page 173 of the 1869 novel, this word was hyphenated and split between two lines for spacing. There were seven other occurrences of button-hole or button-holes in the novel. We therefore used the hyphen.\n\nOn page 150, \"Betty\" was replaced with \"Bethy\". This error was also made in the 1869 novel--see page 177. The character addressed is Beth.\n\nOn page 158, a period was added after \"still kneeling\". See page 187 of the 1869 novel.\nOn page 160, \"hard a lee\" is spelled the same way in the 1869 novel (see page 189) and this novel. We left this as is.\n\nOn page 166, a comma was added after \"Meg\" in \"Meg obediently following\". See page 195 of the 1869 book.\nOn page 185, \"receptable\" was replaced with \"receptacle\". See page 217 of the 1869 novel.\n\nOn page 185, transcribe door-way with the hyphen. See page 217 of the 1869 novel. Also, change doorway to door-way a few lines down the same page. See page 218 of the 1869 novel.\nOn page 189, the comma after \"published every day\" was replaced with a period. See page 225 of 1869 book.\n\nOn page 198, the clause: \"Beth, go and ask Mr. Laurence for a couple of bottles of old wine:\" was written as it appeared in the 1896 novel. The clause ended in with a semi-colon in the 1869 book (see page 234).\nOn page 200, change needlework to needle-work. See page 236 of the 1869 novel.\n\nOn page 209, \"turnovers\" was replaced with \"turn-overs\". See page 246 of the 1869 novel.\nOn page 214, the single quotation mark before \"Head Nurse of Ward\" was replaced with a double quotation mark. See page 252 of the 1869 novel.\n\nOn page 218, \"Year's ago\" was replaced with \"Years ago\". See page 257 of the 1869 novel.\nOn page 219, \"ask him so help\" was replaced with \"ask him to help\". See page 257 of the 1869 novel.\n\nOn page 219, add period after \"give it to her.\" See page 258 of the 1869 novel.\nOn page 230, \"two, A.M.\" is spelled the same way, with the comma, in this book and in the 1869 novel (on page 272). The comma was retained.\n\nOn page 244, \"postscrips\" was replaced with \"postscripts\". See page 287 of the 1869 novel.\nOn page 279, place exclamation point after won't in 'No, I won't!' See page 329 in the 1869 novel.\n\nOn page 286, \"actingly\" was replaced with \"acting\". See page 337 of the 1869 novel.\nOn page 288, add comma after mankind in the clause \"who felt at peace with all mankind even his mischievous pupil.\" See page 339 of the 1869 novel.\n\nOn page 294, transcribe gray-headed with the hyphen. See page 5 of the 1869 novel.\nOn page 295, add a comma after salary in the phrase \"with an honestly-earned salary.\" See page 7 of the 1869 book.\n\nChecked the clause \"But once get used to these slight blemishes\" on page 297. The sentence appears the same way on page 10 of the 1869 novel.\nChecked the clause \"People who hire all these things done for them never know what they lose\" on page 298. The sentence has a comma after them, but is otherwise written the same way on page 11 of the 1869 novel.\n\nOn page 299, transcribe door-handles with the hyphen. See page 13 of the 1869 novel.\nOn page 339, \"shortcomings\" was replaced with \"short-comings\". See page 62 of the 1869 novel.\n\nOn page 345, \"furbelows and notions\" was written \"furbelows and quinny-dingles\" in the 1869 novel. See page 59 of the 1869 novel. We made no change, and only point this out because quinny-dingles is such a memorable word that those intimate with the novel may notice the change.\nOn page 353, change snowbank to snow-bank. See page 79 of the 1869 novel.\n\nOn page 363, a double quotation mark was added before \"Cross-patch, draw the latch\". See page 91 of the 1869 book.\nOn page 379, change period after Jo to a comma in the clause \"as for Jo. she would have gone up\". See page 109 of the 1869 book.\n\nOn page 380, a comma was added after \"all lying down\". See page 111 of the 1869 book.\nOn page 393, the punctuation after 'but so was everybody's' is difficult to read. It could be a colon or semicolon. In the 1869 novel, the mark is a semi-colon (see page 126). We used the semi-colon.\n\nOn page 396, the second line of the verse beginning with \"'Out upon you,\" is indented. In the 1869 version, the capital B of \"Bold-faced jig!'\" is lined-up under Out. We aligned the verse as the 1869 version of the novel--see page 131.\nOn page 404, add period after heaviness. See page 140 of the 1869 novel.\n\nOn page 405, transcribe needle-work with the hyphen. See page 141 of the 1869 book.\nOn Page 411, a letter is curiously addressed to Betsey, both here and on page 148 of the 1869 book.\n\nOn Page 413, removed double quotes around Yes in \"Yes,\" they say to one another, these so kind ladies. Instead, place a single quote in front of Yes, because Bhaer is resuming his quote. The resumed quote concludes with a single quote after me and mine. See page 151 of the 1869 novel. The double quote before 'he is a stupid old fellow' is actually a triple-nested quote, ending in make themselves.\nOn page 417, transcribe Teddy-ism as Teddyism. See page 155 of the 1869 novel.\n\nOn page 451, a period was added after \"I can't let you go\". See page 196 of the 1869 novel.\nOn page 463, \"Tarantula\" was used as the name of a dance, but the author might have meant \"Tarantella,\" which is the name of an Italian dance about tarantulas.\n\nOn page 468, transcribe chess-board with the hyphen. See page 218 of the 1869 novel.\nOn page 512, a period was added after \"she said softly\". If you see page 272 of the 1869 novel, you will also notice a comma in 'she said, softly.' We added the period but not the comma.\n\nOn page 514, the 1869 novel did not have a comma after oar in the sentence: \"I'm not tired; but you may take an oar, if you like. See page 525 of the 1869 novel. We did not remove the comma.\n\nOn page 527, \"the 'the best nevvy\" was replaced with \"the best nevvy\".\n\nOn page 527, change he to the in 'like Jenny and he ballad'. See page 291 of the 1869 novel.\n\nOn page 531, David and Peggotty refer to two characters from the novel \"David Copperfield\" by Charles Dickens.\n\nOn page 534, change of to off in the clause: Daisy found it impossible to keep her eyes of her \"pitty aunty,\" ... see page 300 of the 1869 novel.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-37106"]="작은 아씨들.\n작은 아씨들\n\n또는\n메그, 조, 베스, 그리고 에이미\n\n# XLVII. HARVEST TIME\n\n지은이\n루이자 메이 올컷\n\n「작은 신사들」, 「구식 소녀」,\n「물레 이야기」 등의 작가\n\n프랭크 T. 메릴의 200점이 넘는 삽화와\n\n에드먼드 H. 개럿이 그린\n\n작은 아씨들의 집 그림을 곁들여\n\n보스턴\n리틀, 브라운 앤드 컴퍼니\n\n1868년과 1869년 의회법에 따라 등록함, 등록자\n루이자 메이 올컷,\n\n매사추세츠 지방\n법원 서기과에서.\n\n판권, 1880년,\n루이자 메이 올컷.\n\n판권, 1896년,\n존 S. P. 올컷.\n\n보스턴\n앨프리드 머지 앤드 선 주식회사 인쇄\n\n\"자, 가거라, 나의 작은 책아, 너를 반기고\n\n기꺼이 맞아 줄 모든 이에게 보여 주어라,\n네 가슴속 깊이 닫아 둔 것이 무엇인지를.\n\n그리고 네가 보여 주는 것이 그들에게\n\n복이 되기를, 이로움이 되기를, 너나 나보다\n\n훨씬 더 나은 순례자가 되기를 그들이 택하게 되기를 바라거라.\n\n그들에게 머시의 이야기를 들려주어라. 그녀는\n\n일찍이 순례의 길을 떠난 사람이니.\n그래, 어린 처녀들이 그녀에게서 배우게 하여라,\n\n장차 올 세상을 귀히 여기고, 그리하여 지혜로워지도록.\n\n총총걸음 걷는 어린 소녀들도 하느님을 따를 수 있으니\n\n성인의 발길이 지나간 그 길을 따라서.\"\n\n존 버니언에서 각색함.\n장(章)\n\n1. 순례자 놀이\n2. 즐거운 크리스마스\n\n3. 로런스 가의 소년\n4. 짐\n\n5. 이웃이 되어\n\n6. 베스, 아름다운 궁전을 찾다\n7. 에이미가 겪은 굴욕의 골짜기\n\n8. 조, 아폴리온과 맞서다\n\n9. 메그, 허영의 시장에 가다\n\n10. 피크위크 클럽과 우편함\n11. 실험\n\n12. 로런스 캠프\n13. 공중누각\n\n14. 비밀\n\n15. 전보 한 통\n\n16. 편지\n17. 작고 충실한 아이\n\n18. 어두운 나날\n19. 에이미의 유언장\n\n20. 속마음\n21. 로리는 말썽을 부리고, 조는 화해를 시킨다\n\n22. 즐거운 초원\n23. 마치 대고모가 문제를 해결하다\n\n『작은 아씨들』 2부.\n\n24. 잡담\n25. 첫 번째 결혼식\n\n26. 예술적 시도\n27. 문학 수업\n\n28. 살림살이 경험\n29. 방문\n\n30. 결과\n\n31. 우리의 해외 통신원\n32. 마음 아픈 일들\n\n33. 조의 일기\n34. 친구\n\n35. 가슴앓이\n36. 베스의 비밀\n\n37. 새로운 인상\n\n38. 뒷전으로 밀려나\n39. 게으른 로런스\n\n40. 죽음의 그림자 골짜기\n41. 잊는 법을 배우며\n\n42. 외톨이\n43. 뜻밖의 일들\n\n44. 나의 영주와 귀부인\n45. 데이지와 데미\n\n46. 우산 아래에서\n47. 수확의 계절\n\n쪽수\n모두가 난롯가로 모여들었다, 큰 의자에 앉은 어머니와 그 발치에 앉은 베스\n\n차례\n차례 끝그림\n\n삽화 목록\n삽화 목록 끝그림\n\n선물 없는 크리스마스는 크리스마스가 아니야\n\n베스가 슬리퍼 한 켤레를 내려놓아 데웠다\n\n큰 의자에 앉을 차례가 되면 어찌나 무섭던지\n\n이렇게 해 봐, 두 손을 이렇게 마주 잡고\n\n명랑하고 희망에 찬 편지였다\n\n「천로역정」 놀이를 어떻게 하곤 했던지\n낡은 피아노에서 그럴듯한 음악을 끌어낼 수 있는 사람은 베스뿐이었다\n\n아홉 시가 되자 일손을 멈추고 늘 하던 대로 노래를 불렀다\n\n메리 크리스마스\n행렬이 출발했다\n\n회색 말털을 얼굴에 늘어뜨린 메그가 나왔다\n\n뽀얀 흰빛의 작은 형체\n\n돈 페드로의 축복을 받으려 무릎 꿇은 연인들\n우리는 울타리 너머로 이야기를 나누었다\n\n끝그림\n\n사과를 먹으면서 「레드클리프의 상속자」를 읽고 울었다\n\n조가 종이로 만 머리 마는 도구를 집어 들었다\n\n가드너 부인이 그들을 맞아 주었다\n\n로런스네 소년과 얼굴을 마주하고\n\n그들은 계단에 앉았다\n파티 이야기를 들려줘\n\n새끼 고양이가 도꼬마리처럼 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 딱 붙어 있었다\n\n큰 의자에 몸을 웅크리고\n\n그 지긋지긋한 벨샴을 읽으며\n\n그가 그녀의 귀를 잡아당겼다! 귀를!\n\n로런스 씨가 큰 물고기를 낚아 올렸다\n\n끝그림\n이웃답게\n\n로리가 창문을 열었다\n폴이 그의 가발을 홱 잡아챘다\n\n그녀의 턱밑에 손가락을 받쳐 들고\n이걸 어머니께 갖다 드리세요\n\n끝그림\n오, 어르신, 그들은 정말 많이 마음 쓰고 있어요\n\n로런스 씨는 종종 서재 문을 열어 두었다\n\n그녀는 두 팔로 그의 목을 감고 입을 맞췄다\n\n키클롭스\n\n에이미는 따끔하게 매를 몇 대 맞으면서도 움찔하지 않았다\n\n너는 정말 그녀를 알잖아\n얘들아, 너희 어디 가는 거니?\n\n내가 태워 버렸어\n\n에이미를 두 팔과 하키 채로 받쳐 올렸다\n외국으로 가는 짐을 꾸리며\n\n메그의 파트너가 나타났다\n\n소개해 달라고 청했다\n\n나라면 안 그럴 거야, 메그\n\n마치 부인이 두 사람의 손을 한쪽씩 잡고 말하기를, 운운\n\n피크위크 씨\n\n조가 벽장 문을 활짝 열어젖혔다\n조는 아침나절을 강에서 보냈다\n\n에이미가 그림을 그리려고 앉았다\n오, 핍! 오, 핍!\n\n크로커 양이 얼굴을 찡그렸다\n\n우리는 벌처럼 부지런히 일할 거야\n\n베스는 우체국장이었다\n\n에이미가 코에 빨래집게를 집으며 절정을 장식했다 151\n\n로런스 씨가 모자를 흔들었다\n\n자, 조 양, 내가 당신을 손봐 주지\n아주 즐거운 점심이었다\n\n그는 한적한 거리를 신나게 걸어 내려갔다\n\n\"아, 일어나세요.\" 그녀가 말했다\n큰 그리스어 사전으로 한 방 세게 얻어맞고\n\n그가 재채기를 했다\n포르투갈 사람이 판자 위를 걸었다\n\n장미 한 송이 주시겠어요?\n케이트 양이 안경을 들어 올렸다\n\n엘런 트리\n끝그림\n\n해먹에 누워 앞뒤로 흔들거리며\n제법 예쁘장한 작은 그림이었다\n\n고사리를 그녀의 얼굴 앞에서 흔들었다\n그가 고개 숙여 인사하고 미소 짓는 게 보인다\n\n끝그림\n조는 매우 바빴다\n\n마치 양 만세\n조가 쏜살같이 달려갔다\n\n조가 소파에 몸을 던지고는 읽는 척했다\n\n11월은 일 년 중 가장 마음에 안 드는 달이다\n\n그 끔찍한 전보라는 것 중 하나\n\n그녀는 갑작스레 브룩 씨와 마주쳤다\n남자가 머리를 잘랐다\n\n끝그림\n편지들\n\n그녀는 마차를 타고 떠났다\n\n내가 시계 태엽을 감습니다\n존경을 담아, 해나 멀릿\n\n끝그림\n미동도 하지 않기에, 나는 그것이 죽은 줄 알았다\n\n그가 그녀 곁에 앉았다\n\n이제 또 뭘 원하니?\n\n베스는 정말로 열병에 걸렸다\n\n어머니가 그러시던 것처럼 그녀의 머리를 부드럽게 쓸어 주며\n\n에이미의 유언장\n\n숟가락과 통통한 은 찻주전자를 닦아 윤을 내며\n\n다리를 모두 공중에 쳐든 채 등을 대고 누워\n나라면 이걸 고르겠어\n\n진지하게 이리저리 거닐며\n에이미의 유언장\n\n끝그림\n마치 부인은 베스의 곁을 떠나려 하지 않았다\n\n끝그림\n\n편지들\n조와 어머니가 쪽지를 읽고 있었다\n\n일어나, 바보같이 굴지 말고\n\n\"입 다물어!\" 조가 두 귀를 막으며 외쳤다\n\n그는 길목을 지키는 사자처럼 계단 아래 서 있었다\n\n베스는 곧 하루 종일 서재 소파에 누워 있을 수 있게 되었다\n\n융프라우\n이따금 머리를 쏙 들이밀며\n\n그는 베스의 소파 옆 큰 의자에 앉아 있었고 나머지 세 자매가 바싹 곁에 있었다\n\n어떻게 하는지 말해 줄까?\n이런 세상에, 이게 다 뭐냐?\n\n존 브룩 부인에게\n작은 아씨들의 집\n\n비둘기 둥지\n작은 야경꾼의 딸랑이\n\n끝그림\n첫 번째 결혼식\n\n예술적 시도\n석고가 든 대야에 발이 단단히 박혀\n\n제발 그러지 마, 그건 내 거야\n끝그림\n\n문학 수업\n백 달러짜리 수표\n\n끝그림\n살림살이 경험\n\n둘 다 견딜 수 없이 거북해했다\n\n장담하건대 거저나 다름없는 거래예요, 부인\n로리는 무언가가 자기 품에 안기는 동안 영웅처럼 눈을 감았다\n\n방문\n\n안장을 말에게 가져갔다\n더 나빴을 수도 있었다\n\n마치 대고모 댁 방문\n끝그림\n\n다른 탁자를 하나 더 갖게 될 거예요\n꽃다발을 몽땅 사들였다\n\n끝그림\n플로와 나는 이륜마차를 불렀다\n\n다들 무척 친절했고, 특히 장교들이 그랬다\n황실 가족을 여러 번 보았다\n\n벽에 새겨진 회색 돌사자 머리를 스케치하려 애쓰며\n그녀는 두 손에 머리를 기댔다\n\n자, 이건 값에 비해 든든하답니다\n\n보니 던디의 모자를 쓰고 일어서라\n좌석 너머로 생강 과자를 떨어뜨리며 혼자 즐거워했다\n\n당신은 바에르를 갖게 될 거예요\n\n그는 양말까지 손에 든 채 손을 흔들었다\n이건 내 코끼리야\n\n나는 바닥에 주저앉아 읽고, 바라보고, 먹었다\n\n끝그림\n세 신사 앞에서\n\n엄선된 좌담회\n\n그는 오기 전에 거울 앞에서 멋을 부리지 않는다\n조가 그 꾸러미를 통째로 난로에 쑤셔 넣었다\n\n그가 자매들을 마차에 태워 주었다\n\n그는 이끼 낀 기둥에 머리를 기댔다\n오, 조, 정말 안 되겠어?\n\n끝그림\n조의 무릎에 머리를 얹고, 바람이 그녀 위로 상쾌하게 불어오는 동안\n\n끝그림\n그는 서둘러 앞으로 나아가 그녀를 맞았다\n\n여기 당신의 꽃이에요\n데미와 데이지\n\n이제 아침이에요\n\n이보게, 그건 보닛이라네\n끝그림\n\n염소들이 뛰노는 동안 그는 돌 위에 앉아 피리를 불었다\n\n로리가 잔디밭에 벌렁 드러누웠다\n말을 길들이는 로리의 거친 스케치\n\n죽음의 그림자 골짜기\n끝그림\n\n흉상들을 올려다보며 앉아\n\n손가락의 반지를 생각에 잠겨 돌리며\n오, 로리, 로리, 네가 올 줄 알았어\n\n우리 정말 호흡이 잘 맞는구나\n조와 아버지\n\n조는 편안한 헝겊 자루에 머리를 묻고 울었다\n생생하게 살아 있는 듯한 든든한 유령이 그녀 위로 몸을 숙이고\n\n키 큰 삼촌이 어린 조카를 번쩍 들어 올려 마구 헝클어 놓았다\n\n오, 바에르 씨, 만나서 정말 반가워요\n\n바에르 씨가 진심으로 노래를 불렀다\n어머니의 무릎에 앉은 로런스 부인\n\n그들은 이리저리 거닐기 시작했다\n\n끝그림\n\n나는 모든 사람을 사랑해요\n\n내 다리는 왜 움직여요, 할아버지?\n\n할아버지, 이건 '우리'예요\n\n끝그림\n바에르 씨와 조는 함께 거니는 것을 즐겼다\n\n고개를 들어 보니 바에르 씨가 보였다\n\n이게 마음에 드세요, 바에르 씨?\n\n우산 아래에서\n\n끝그림\n\n수확의 계절\n\n테디는 신통하게도 무사했다\n\n축제의 나무 아래 마치 부인과 딸들을 남겨 두고\n\n끝그림\n1.\n\n# PLAYING PILGRIMS.\n\n순례자 놀이\n\"선물 없는 크리스마스는 크리스마스가 아니야.\" 조가 깔개에 드러누운 채 투덜거렸다.\n\n\"가난하다는 건 정말 끔찍해!\" 메그가 자기 낡은 옷을 내려다보며 한숨지었다.\n\n\"어떤 애들은 예쁜 걸 잔뜩 가졌는데 어떤 애들은 아무것도 못 갖는 건 공평하지 않은 것 같아.\" 막내 에이미가 서운한 듯 훌쩍이며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"우리한테는 아버지랑 어머니가 있고, 또 서로가 있잖아.\" 베스가 자기 구석에서 흐뭇하게 말했다.\n\n난롯불이 비치는 네 어린 얼굴이 그 다정한 말에 환해졌지만, 조가 슬프게 입을 열자 다시 어두워졌다.\n\n\"우리한테 아버지는 안 계셔, 그것도 오랫동안 못 뵐 거고.\" 조는 \"어쩌면 영영\"이라고는 말하지 않았지만, 모두가 전쟁이 벌어지는 먼 곳에 계신 아버지를 떠올리며 마음속으로 그 말을 덧붙였다.\n\n잠시 아무도 말이 없었다. 그러더니 메그가 달라진 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"엄마가 올 크리스마스에는 선물을 갖지 말자고 하신 건 모두에게 힘든 겨울이 될 테니까 그러신 거 알지. 우리 남자들이 군에서 그렇게 고생하는데 우리가 즐기자고 돈을 써서는 안 된다고 생각하시는 거야. 우리가 큰일을 할 순 없어도 작은 희생은 할 수 있고, 또 기꺼이 해야지. 그런데 난 그게 잘 안 되나 봐.\" 메그는 자기가 갖고 싶은 예쁜 물건들을 아쉬운 마음으로 떠올리며 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"하지만 우리가 쓸 그 푼돈이 무슨 도움이 될까 싶어. 우리는 각자 1달러씩 갖고 있는데, 그걸 내놓는다고 군이 크게 덕을 보지는 않을걸. 엄마나 언니한테 아무것도 기대하지 않는 건 좋아, 그래도 나는 「운디네와 진트람」을 나 주려고 사고 싶어. 그 책을 정말 오래 갖고 싶었단 말이야.\" 책벌레인 조가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 내 돈을 새 악보 사는 데 쓸 생각이었어.\" 베스가 작은 한숨을 내쉬었는데, 그 소리를 들은 건 난로 솔과 주전자 받침뿐이었다.\n\n\"나는 파버 그림 연필 한 상자를 좋은 걸로 살래. 정말 필요하거든.\" 에이미가 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"엄마가 우리 돈에 대해선 아무 말씀 안 하셨고, 우리가 모든 걸 포기하길 바라지도 않으실 거야. 각자 갖고 싶은 걸 사서 즐기자. 우리가 그 정도 누릴 만큼은 충분히 열심히 일하잖아.\" 조가 신사처럼 제 구두 뒤축을 살펴보며 외쳤다.\n\n\"나야 그렇지. 집에서 좀 즐기고 싶어 죽겠는데 하루 종일 그 성가신 애들을 가르치잖아.\" 메그가 다시 불평하는 어조로 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"언니는 나만큼 고생하는 것도 아니잖아.\" 조가 말했다. \"신경질적이고 까다로운 할머니랑 몇 시간씩 갇혀 지내 봐. 끊임없이 심부름시키지, 만족이라곤 없지, 창밖으로 뛰쳐나가거나 울고 싶어질 때까지 사람을 들들 볶는다니까.\"\n\n\"불평하는 건 나쁜 거지만, 그래도 설거지하고 집 안 치우는 게 세상에서 제일 힘든 일 같아. 그러면 짜증이 나고, 손이 너무 뻣뻣해져서 연습도 제대로 못 하거든.\" 베스가 자기 거친 손을 내려다보며 한숨을 쉬었는데, 이번엔 누구나 들을 수 있는 한숨이었다.\n\n\"너희 중 누구도 나만큼 고생하진 않을걸.\" 에이미가 외쳤다. \"너흰 버릇없는 애들이 있는 학교에 안 가도 되잖아. 그 애들은 수업을 모르면 괴롭히고, 옷을 비웃고, 아버지가 부자가 아니면 '아버지를 라벨'하고, 코가 예쁘지 않으면 모욕한단 말이야.\"\n\n\"'중상모략'을 말하려는 거면 그렇게 말해. '라벨'이 뭐니, 아빠가 무슨 절임 병이라도 되는 것처럼.\" 조가 웃으며 충고했다.\n\n\"내가 무슨 말 하는지 알거든, 그렇게 '풍지적'으로 굴 거 없어. 좋은 단어를 쓰고 '어회'를 늘리는 게 옳은 거야.\" 에이미가 점잔을 빼며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"서로 쪼아 대지 마, 얘들아. 우리 어렸을 때 아빠가 잃어버린 돈이 있으면 좋겠지, 조? 어머나! 걱정거리만 없다면 우리가 얼마나 행복하고 착해질까!\" 더 나은 시절을 기억하는 메그가 말했다.\n\n\"언니가 며칠 전에 그랬잖아, 우리가 킹네 애들보다 훨씬 행복한 것 같다고. 걔넨 돈이 많은데도 늘 싸우고 짜증을 내잖아.\"\n\n\"내가 그랬지, 베스. 음, 정말 그런 것 같아. 우리는 일을 해야 하지만 스스로 즐거움을 만들어 내고, 조 말마따나 꽤 유쾌한 패거리니까.\"\n\n\"조는 별 속어를 다 쓴다니까!\" 에이미가 깔개에 길게 뻗은 형체를 나무라는 눈길로 보며 말했다. 조는 곧장 일어나 앉아 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 휘파람을 불기 시작했다.\n\n\"그러지 마, 조. 너무 사내애 같아!\"\n\n\"그래서 하는 거야.\"\n\n\"난 거칠고 숙녀답지 못한 여자애가 정말 싫어!\"\n\n\"난 잘난 척하고 새침 떠는 계집애가 질색이야!\"\n\n\"'작은 둥지 속의 새들은 사이좋게 지낸다네.'\" 평화주의자 베스가 어찌나 우스운 얼굴로 노래를 불렀던지, 날 선 두 목소리가 웃음으로 누그러지면서 \"쪼아 대기\"는 그쯤에서 끝이 났다.\n\n\"정말이지, 얘들아, 둘 다 잘못이야.\" 메그가 큰언니다운 투로 훈계를 시작했다. \"이제 사내애 같은 장난은 그만두고 좀 더 얌전하게 굴 나이가 됐어, 조세핀. 어렸을 땐 별 상관 없었지만, 이제 키도 그렇게 크고 머리도 틀어 올렸으니 네가 숙녀라는 걸 기억해야지.\"\n\n\"난 숙녀 아니야! 머리 틀어 올리는 게 숙녀가 되는 거라면 스무 살까지 두 갈래로 땋고 다닐 거야.\" 조가 머리그물을 벗어 던지며 밤색 갈기 같은 머리를 풀어 헤치고 외쳤다. \"어른이 돼서 마치 양이 되고, 긴 가운을 입고, 과꽃처럼 새침하게 굴어야 한다니 생각만 해도 끔찍해! 어쨌든 여자로 태어난 것부터가 충분히 못마땅한데, 난 사내애들 놀이랑 일이랑 행동거지가 좋단 말이야! 남자로 못 태어난 게 두고두고 분해. 지금은 그게 더 심해, 아빠랑 같이 가서 싸우고 싶어 죽겠는데, 굼뜬 할머니처럼 집에 들어앉아 뜨개질이나 해야 하잖아!\" 조는 바늘이 캐스터네츠처럼 달그락거릴 때까지 파란 군용 양말을 흔들어 댔고, 실뭉치가 방 저편으로 튕겨 나갔다.\n\n\"가엾은 조! 정말 안됐지만 어쩔 수 없는걸. 그러니 이름이라도 사내애처럼 만들고, 우리 여자애들한테 오빠 노릇 하는 걸로 만족하도록 해 봐.\" 베스가 무릎에 기댄 텁수룩한 머리를 쓰다듬으며 말했는데, 세상의 그 모든 설거지와 먼지 떨이로도 그 손길의 다정함만은 거칠게 만들 수 없었다.\n\n\"너는 말이지, 에이미,\" 메그가 말을 이었다. \"넌 너무 까다롭고 새침해. 지금이야 네 으스대는 게 우습지만, 조심하지 않으면 자라서 잘난 척하는 멍청이가 될 거야. 네가 우아한 척하지 않을 때는 예쁜 행동거지랑 점잖은 말투가 마음에 들어. 하지만 그 터무니없는 단어들은 조의 속어만큼이나 나쁘다고.\"\n\n\"조가 말괄량이고 에이미가 멍청이라면, 그럼 나는 뭐야?\" 베스가 자기도 훈계를 같이 받겠다는 듯 물었다.\n\n\"넌 사랑스러운 아이지, 그것 말곤 없어.\" 메그가 따뜻하게 대답했고, 아무도 반박하지 않았다. \"생쥐\"는 온 가족의 귀염둥이였으니까.\n\n어린 독자들은 \"사람들이 어떻게 생겼는지\" 알고 싶어 하니, 이 대목에서 네 자매를 잠깐 스케치해 보기로 하자. 그들은 황혼 속에서 부지런히 뜨개질을 하고 있었고, 밖에서는 12월의 눈이 조용히 내렸으며, 안에서는 난롯불이 경쾌하게 타닥거렸다. 양탄자는 빛이 바랬고 가구는 무척 수수했지만, 아늑하고 오래된 방이었다. 벽에는 괜찮은 그림이 한두 점 걸려 있고, 벽감에는 책이 가득 꽂혀 있으며, 창가에는 국화와 크리스마스 장미가 피어 있고, 가정의 평화로운 분위기가 방 안에 가득 배어 있었으니까.\n\n네 자매 중 맏이인 마거릿은 열여섯 살이었고, 매우 예뻤다. 통통하고 살결이 희며, 큰 눈에 부드러운 갈색 머리가 풍성했고, 입매가 고왔으며, 손이 하얬는데 그 손을 은근히 자랑스러워했다. 열다섯 살 조는 키가 아주 크고 비쩍 말랐으며 가무잡잡해서 망아지를 떠올리게 했다. 자기한테 거추장스럽기만 한 긴 팔다리를 어떻게 해야 할지 도무지 모르는 것 같았으니까. 야무진 입매에 우스꽝스러운 코, 모든 걸 다 꿰뚫어 보는 듯한 날카로운 잿빛 눈을 가졌는데, 그 눈은 때로는 사납고 때로는 익살맞고 때로는 사색에 잠겨 있었다. 길고 숱 많은 머리카락이 그녀의 유일한 미인 요소였지만, 거추장스럽다고 보통 머리그물 속에 둘둘 말아 넣고 다녔다. 조는 어깨가 둥글고, 손발이 컸으며, 옷매무새가 늘 헐렁했고, 빠르게 여인으로 자라는 게 영 마뜩잖은 소녀 특유의 어색한 분위기를 풍겼다. 엘리자베스는 — 다들 베스라고 불렀다 — 발그레한 뺨에 매끄러운 머릿결, 초롱초롱한 눈을 지닌 열세 살 소녀로, 수줍은 태도와 소심한 목소리, 좀처럼 흐트러지지 않는 평온한 표정을 지녔다. 아버지는 그녀를 \"꼬마 평온이\"라고 불렀는데, 그 이름이 그녀에게 더없이 잘 어울렸다. 그녀는 자기만의 행복한 세계 속에서 사는 듯했고, 믿고 사랑하는 몇몇 사람을 만날 때만 그 세계 밖으로 조심스레 나왔으니까. 에이미는 막내였지만 가장 중요한 사람이었다 — 적어도 자기 생각으로는. 파란 눈에 어깨까지 곱슬거리는 노란 머리를 늘어뜨린 영락없는 눈의 요정 같았고, 창백하고 가냘팠으며, 늘 예의범절을 의식하는 숙녀처럼 처신했다. 네 자매의 성격이 어떤지는 차차 알아 가도록 남겨 두기로 하자.\n\n시계가 여섯 시를 쳤다. 베스는 난로 앞을 쓸고 나서 슬리퍼 한 켤레를 내려놓아 데웠다. 어쩐 일인지 그 낡은 신발을 보자 소녀들은 기분이 좋아졌다. 어머니가 오실 시간이라 다들 맞이할 채비로 환해졌으니까. 메그는 훈계를 멈추고 램프를 켰고, 에이미는 누가 시키지도 않았는데 안락의자에서 일어났으며, 조는 슬리퍼를 불길 가까이 대려고 일어나 앉으면서 자기가 얼마나 피곤한지도 잊어버렸다.\n\n\"많이 닳았네. 엄마한테 새 걸 사 드려야겠어.\"\n\n\"내 돈으로 한 켤레 사 드릴까 했는데.\" 베스가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 내가 할 거야!\" 에이미가 외쳤다.\n\n\"내가 제일 맏이잖아.\" 메그가 입을 떼자 조가 단호하게 끼어들었다.\n\n\"아빠가 안 계시니까 지금은 내가 이 집의 가장이야. 그러니까 슬리퍼는 내가 마련할 거야. 아빠가 떠나시면서 어머니를 각별히 잘 보살피라고 나한테 당부하셨거든.\"\n\n\"우리가 어떻게 할지 말해 줄게.\" 베스가 말했다. \"각자 크리스마스 선물을 엄마한테 하나씩 사 드리고, 우리 건 아무것도 사지 말자.\"\n\n\"역시 너답다, 얘! 뭘 사 드릴까?\" 조가 소리쳤다.\n\n다들 잠깐 진지하게 생각에 잠겼다. 그러더니 메그가, 자기 예쁜 손을 보고 떠올랐다는 듯이 말했다. \"난 좋은 장갑 한 켤레를 사 드려야지.\"\n\n\"군화로, 살 수 있는 제일 좋은 걸로!\" 조가 외쳤다.\n\n\"손수건 몇 장, 가장자리를 다 감친 걸로.\" 베스가 말했다.\n\n\"난 작은 오드콜로뉴 병을 살래. 엄마가 좋아하시고, 비싸지도 않으니까 연필 살 돈도 좀 남을 거야.\" 에이미가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"선물을 어떻게 드리지?\" 메그가 물었다.\n\n\"탁자에 올려놓고, 엄마를 모셔 와서 꾸러미 푸는 걸 지켜보자. 우리 생일 때 어떻게 했는지 기억 안 나?\" 조가 대답했다.\n\n\"내 차례가 돼서 왕관 쓰고 큰 의자에 앉아 있는 게 얼마나 무섭던지. 너희가 다들 빙 둘러서 행진하며 입을 맞추고 선물을 주는 거 말이야. 선물도 입맞춤도 좋았지만, 내가 꾸러미를 푸는 동안 너희가 앉아서 빤히 쳐다보는 건 정말 끔찍했어.\" 베스가 말했는데, 그러면서도 차에 곁들일 빵과 자기 얼굴을 동시에 불에 익히고 있었다.\n\n\"엄마한테는 우리가 각자 쓸 걸 사는 줄 알게 해 놓고, 깜짝 놀라게 해 드리자. 내일 오후엔 쇼핑하러 가야 해, 메그. 크리스마스 밤에 할 연극 준비할 게 잔뜩 있으니까.\" 조가 두 손을 등 뒤로 하고 코를 치켜든 채 이리저리 행진하며 말했다.\n\n\"난 이번을 끝으로 연극은 그만둘 생각이야. 그런 거 하기엔 나이가 너무 들었어.\" 메그가 말했는데, 그러면서도 \"변장\"하고 노는 일에 관해서라면 여전히 어린애 같았다.\n\n\"언니가 흰 가운을 끌고 다니면서 머리 풀어 헤치고 금종이 장신구를 걸칠 수 있는 한, 절대 그만두지 못할걸. 언니가 우리 중에 제일 연기를 잘하는데, 언니가 무대를 떠나면 다 끝장이라고.\" 조가 말했다. \"오늘 밤에 연습해야 해. 이리 와, 에이미, 기절하는 장면 좀 해 봐. 넌 그 장면에서 부지깽이처럼 뻣뻣하잖아.\"\n\n\"나도 어쩔 수 없어. 누가 기절하는 걸 본 적이 없는걸, 게다가 언니처럼 풀썩 넘어져서 온몸에 멍이 들고 싶지도 않아. 쉽게 쓰러질 수 있으면 그냥 쓰러지고, 안 되면 의자에 우아하게 주저앉을 거야. 휴고가 권총을 들고 나한테 달려들어도 난 상관없어.\" 에이미가 대꾸했다. 그녀는 연기에 재능을 타고나진 않았지만, 악당이 비명을 지르는 그녀를 번쩍 들어 안고 나갈 수 있을 만큼 몸집이 작아서 뽑힌 거였다.\n\n\"이렇게 해 봐. 두 손을 이렇게 마주 잡고, 방을 가로질러 비틀거리면서 미친 듯이 외치는 거야. '로드리고! 살려 줘요! 살려 줘요!'\" 그러더니 조가 정말로 소름 끼치는 멜로드라마풍 비명을 지르며 시범을 보였다.\n\n에이미가 따라 했지만, 두 손을 앞으로 뻣뻣하게 내밀고 기계처럼 덜컥덜컥 움직였다. 그녀의 \"아얏!\" 소리도 두려움과 고통이라기보다는 핀에 찔린 것 같은 느낌이었다. 조는 절망스러운 신음을 냈고, 메그는 큰 소리로 웃었으며, 베스는 그 재미난 광경에 정신이 팔린 나머지 빵이 타는 것도 내버려 두었다.\n\n\"안 되겠다! 때가 되면 그냥 최선을 다해 봐, 그래서 관객이 웃어도 날 탓하진 마. 자, 메그, 어서.\"\n\n그다음부터는 일이 순조롭게 풀렸다. 돈 페드로는 두 페이지짜리 대사를 단 한 번도 끊김 없이 읊으며 온 세상에 맞섰고, 마녀 하가르는 부글부글 끓는 두꺼비 가마솥 위에서 으스스한 효과를 내며 무시무시한 주문을 외웠으며, 로드리고는 사내답게 쇠사슬을 끊어 버렸고, 휴고는 회한과 비소의 고통 속에서 사납게 \"하! 하!\" 하며 죽어 갔다.\n\n\"지금까지 한 것 중에 제일 잘했어.\" 죽은 악당이 일어나 앉아 팔꿈치를 문지르며 메그가 말했다.\n\n\"어떻게 그렇게 멋진 걸 쓰고 또 연기까지 하는지 모르겠어, 조. 넌 정말 셰익스피어야!\" 베스가 외쳤는데, 그녀는 언니들이 모든 일에 놀라운 천재성을 타고났다고 굳게 믿고 있었다.\n\n\"그 정도는 아니지.\" 조가 겸손하게 대답했다. \"「마녀의 저주, 오페라 비극」이 제법 괜찮은 작품이라고 생각하긴 해. 그래도 밴쿠오가 떨어질 함정문만 있으면 「맥베스」에 도전해 보고 싶어. 난 늘 살인 장면을 해 보고 싶었거든. '내 앞에 보이는 저것은 단검인가?'\" 조가 눈을 부라리고 허공을 움켜쥐며 중얼거렸는데, 어느 유명한 비극 배우가 하는 걸 본 적이 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"아니, 그건 빵 대신 엄마 신발이 꽂힌 토스트용 포크야. 베스가 무대에 홀렸나 봐!\" 메그가 외쳤고, 연습은 다 같이 웃음을 터뜨리며 끝이 났다.\n\n\"우리 딸들이 이렇게 즐거워하니 반갑구나.\" 문 앞에서 명랑한 목소리가 들려왔고, 배우들과 관객들이 키 크고 인자한 부인을 맞으려 돌아보았다. 그 부인에게서는 \"내가 도와줄까?\" 하는 표정이 풍겨 나와 참으로 사랑스러웠다. 우아하게 차려입지는 않았지만 기품 있어 보이는 여인이었고, 소녀들은 그 회색 망토와 한물간 보닛이 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 어머니를 감싸고 있다고 여겼다.\n\n\"자, 얘들아, 오늘 하루 어떻게 보냈니? 내일 보낼 상자들을 챙기느라 할 일이 어찌나 많던지 점심때 집에 오지도 못했구나. 누구 다녀간 사람 있니, 베스? 감기는 좀 어떠니, 메그? 조, 너 죽도록 피곤해 보이는구나. 이리 와서 엄마한테 뽀뽀해 주렴, 아가.\"\n\n이렇게 엄마다운 질문을 던지는 동안 마치 부인은 젖은 옷을 벗고 따뜻한 슬리퍼를 신었으며, 안락의자에 앉아 에이미를 무릎으로 끌어당기고는 바쁜 하루 중 가장 행복한 시간을 즐길 채비를 했다. 소녀들은 저마다의 방식으로 분주하게 움직이며 이것저것 편하게 해 드리려 애썼다. 메그는 찻상을 차렸고, 조는 장작을 가져오고 의자를 놓다가 손대는 것마다 떨어뜨리고 엎고 달그락거렸으며, 베스는 거실과 부엌 사이를 조용히 부지런하게 오갔다. 한편 에이미는 두 손을 모은 채 앉아서 모두에게 이래라저래라 지시했다.\n\n다들 탁자에 둘러앉자 마치 부인이 유난히 행복한 얼굴로 말했다. \"저녁 먹고 나서 너희한테 줄 즐거운 게 있단다.\"\n\n햇살이 한 줄기 스치듯, 빠르고 환한 미소가 빙 둘러 번졌다. 베스는 들고 있던 비스킷도 아랑곳없이 손뼉을 쳤고, 조는 냅킨을 위로 던지며 외쳤다. \"편지다! 편지야! 아빠 만세!\"\n\n\"그래, 길고 좋은 편지란다. 아버지는 건강하시고, 추운 계절을 우리가 걱정했던 것보다 잘 넘기실 것 같다는구나. 크리스마스를 맞아 온갖 사랑의 인사를 보내셨고, 특별히 너희 딸들에게 전하는 말씀도 있단다.\" 마치 부인이 주머니에 무슨 보물이라도 든 것처럼 그곳을 토닥이며 말했다.\n\n\"어서 다 먹어! 새끼손가락 꼬부리고 접시 위에서 새침 떨고 있을 시간 없어, 에이미.\" 조가 외쳤는데, 즐거운 소식이 너무 듣고 싶은 나머지 차에 사레가 들리고 빵을 버터 바른 면이 아래로 가게 양탄자에 떨어뜨렸다.\n\n베스는 더 먹지 않고 살그머니 자기 그늘진 구석으로 가, 다른 식구들이 준비될 때까지 곧 다가올 즐거움을 곱씹으며 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"아버지가 징집되기엔 너무 나이가 많고 군인이 되기엔 건강도 충분치 않으신데 종군 목사로 가신 건 정말 훌륭한 일이야.\" 메그가 따뜻하게 말했다.\n\n\"나도 북 치는 병사나 종군… 뭐라더라? 아니면 간호사로라도 가서 아빠 곁에서 도울 수 있으면 좋겠어.\" 조가 신음하듯 외쳤다.\n\n\"천막에서 자고, 온갖 맛없는 걸 먹고, 양철 컵으로 물을 마셔야 한다니 정말 끔찍할 거야.\" 에이미가 한숨지었다.\n\n\"아빠는 언제 집에 오셔, 엄마?\" 베스가 목소리를 살짝 떨며 물었다.\n\n\"여러 달 동안은 못 오신단다, 얘야. 편찮으시지 않은 한은. 가능한 한 오래 머무르며 맡은 일을 충실히 하실 거야. 그러니 우리도 아버지가 빠지셔도 될 때보다 단 일 분이라도 일찍 돌아오시라고 조르지 말자. 자, 이제 이리 와서 편지를 듣자꾸나.\"\n\n다들 난롯가로 다가왔다. 어머니는 큰 의자에 앉고 베스는 그 발치에 앉았으며, 메그와 에이미는 의자 양쪽 팔걸이에 걸터앉았고, 조는 의자 등받이에 기댔다. 편지에 혹시 가슴 뭉클한 대목이 나오더라도 누구에게도 감정의 기색을 들키지 않을 자리였다.\n\n그 힘겨운 시절에 쓰인 편지 중 가슴 뭉클하지 않은 것은 거의 없었고, 특히 아버지들이 집으로 보내는 편지는 더욱 그러했다. 이 편지에는 견뎌 낸 고초나 맞닥뜨린 위험, 이겨 낸 향수병에 대해서는 별로 적혀 있지 않았다. 야영 생활과 행군, 군 소식에 대한 생생한 묘사로 가득한, 명랑하고 희망에 찬 편지였다. 그리고 마지막에 이르러서야 글쓴이의 마음이 집에 있는 어린 딸들을 향한 아버지의 사랑과 그리움으로 흘러넘쳤다.\n\n\"애들 모두에게 내 깊은 사랑과 입맞춤을 전해 주오. 낮이면 그 애들을 생각하고 밤이면 그 애들을 위해 기도하며, 그 애들의 사랑에서 언제나 가장 큰 위안을 얻는다고 전해 주오. 그 애들을 다시 보기까지 일 년은 무척 긴 기다림처럼 느껴지지만, 기다리는 동안 우리 모두 일할 수 있으니 이 힘든 나날을 헛되이 보낼 필요는 없다고 일러 주오. 그 애들은 내가 한 말을 다 기억하리라 믿소. 당신에게 다정한 자식이 되고, 제 본분을 충실히 다하며, 마음속 적과 용감히 싸우고, 스스로를 아름답게 이겨 내어, 내가 돌아갔을 때 그 어느 때보다 우리 작은 아씨들을 더 사랑하고 더 자랑스러워할 수 있게 되리라고 말이오.\"\n\n그 대목에 이르자 다들 코를 훌쩍였다. 조는 코끝에서 떨어지는 굵은 눈물방울을 부끄러워하지 않았고, 에이미는 엄마 어깨에 얼굴을 묻고 흐느끼느라 곱슬머리가 헝클어지는 것도 개의치 않았다. \"난 정말 이기적인 애야! 그래도 정말 더 나아지려고 애쓸 거야, 그래서 나중에 아빠가 나한테 실망하시지 않게.\"\n\n\"우리 모두 그럴 거야!\" 메그가 외쳤다. \"난 외모에 너무 신경 쓰고 일하는 걸 싫어하지만, 이제 어쩔 수 없는 경우가 아니면 안 그럴 거야.\"\n\n\"난 아빠가 즐겨 부르시는 그 이름, '작은 아씨'에 어울리는 사람이 되려고 애쓸래. 거칠고 사납게 굴지 않고, 어디 다른 데 가 있고 싶어 하는 대신 여기서 내 본분을 다할 거야.\" 조가 말했다. 그러면서 집에서 성질을 다스리는 게 남부에 가서 반란군 한두 명을 상대하는 것보다 훨씬 더 힘든 일이라고 생각했다.\n\n베스는 아무 말도 하지 않았지만 파란 군용 양말로 눈물을 훔치고는 온 힘을 다해 뜨개질을 하기 시작했다. 가장 가까이 놓인 본분을 하는 데 한순간도 허비하지 않으면서, 조용한 작은 마음속으로 다짐했다. 일 년이 돌고 돌아 행복한 귀향이 찾아올 때 아버지가 바라던 바로 그런 딸이 되어 있겠다고.\n\n마치 부인은 조의 말 뒤에 이어진 침묵을 깨며 명랑한 목소리로 말했다. \"너희 어렸을 때 「천로역정」 놀이를 어떻게 하곤 했는지 기억하니? 엄마가 헝겊 자루를 짐 삼아 너희 등에 묶어 주고, 모자랑 막대기랑 둘둘 만 종이를 주고, 멸망의 도시인 지하실에서 출발해 위로, 위로, 천상의 도시를 꾸미려고 모을 수 있는 온갖 예쁜 것들이 다 있는 옥상까지 집 안을 누비게 해 주는 것보다 너희를 더 즐겁게 한 건 없었지.\"\n\n\"정말 재밌었지, 특히 사자들 옆을 지나가고, 아폴리온이랑 싸우고, 도깨비들이 있는 골짜기를 통과하는 거 말이야!\" 조가 말했다.\n\n\"난 짐 보따리가 떨어져서 계단 아래로 굴러떨어지는 대목이 좋았어.\" 메그가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 제일 좋아하던 부분은 우리 꽃이랑 정자랑 예쁜 것들이 있는 평평한 옥상으로 나와서, 다 같이 서서 햇살 아래 기뻐서 노래 부르던 거야.\" 베스가, 그 즐거운 순간이 되살아난 듯 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"난 별로 기억 안 나. 지하실이랑 어두운 복도가 무서웠던 거랑, 늘 꼭대기에 올라가서 먹던 케이크랑 우유가 좋았던 것밖에. 이런 놀이 하기엔 너무 나이가 든 게 아니라면 다시 한번 해 보고 싶긴 해.\" 에이미가 말했는데, 그녀는 열두 살이라는 원숙한 나이에 어린애 같은 것들을 졸업하는 이야기를 막 시작하는 참이었다.\n\n\"우린 결코 이걸 하기에 나이가 든 게 아니란다, 얘야. 이건 우리가 늘 이런저런 방식으로 하고 있는 놀이니까. 우리의 짐은 여기 있고, 우리의 길은 우리 앞에 놓여 있으며, 선함과 행복을 향한 갈망이야말로 수많은 어려움과 실수를 헤치고 진정한 천상의 도시인 평화로 우리를 이끄는 안내자란다. 자, 나의 작은 순례자들아, 이번엔 놀이가 아니라 진심으로 다시 시작해서, 아버지가 집에 오시기 전까지 얼마나 멀리 나아갈 수 있는지 한번 보면 어떻겠니.\"\n\n\"정말요, 엄마? 우리 짐 보따리는 어디 있어요?\" 매우 곧이곧대로 생각하는 에이미가 물었다.\n\n\"베스만 빼고 방금 너희 각자 자기 짐이 뭔지 말했잖니. 베스는 짐이 없는 것 같구나.\" 어머니가 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 저도 있어요. 제 짐은 설거지랑 먼지 떨이, 그리고 좋은 피아노를 가진 애들을 부러워하는 거랑, 사람들을 무서워하는 거예요.\"\n\n베스의 짐 보따리가 어찌나 우스웠던지 다들 웃고 싶어 했다. 하지만 아무도 웃지 않았다. 그랬다간 베스의 마음을 몹시 아프게 할 테니까.\n\n\"우리 그걸 해 보자.\" 메그가 사려 깊게 말했다. \"그건 그저 착해지려고 애쓴다는 걸 달리 부르는 말일 뿐이고, 그 이야기가 우리한테 도움이 될지도 몰라. 우린 착해지고 싶어 하지만 그게 힘든 일이라 자꾸 잊어버리고 최선을 다하지 않으니까.\"\n\n\"우린 오늘 밤 절망의 늪에 빠져 있었는데, 책에서 '도움'이라는 인물이 그랬던 것처럼 엄마가 와서 우릴 끌어내 주신 거야. 크리스천처럼 우리한테도 두루마리 지침서가 있어야 해. 그건 어떻게 하지?\" 조가 물었는데, 본분을 다하는 그 따분하기 짝이 없는 일에 약간의 낭만을 입혀 주는 그 상상에 신이 나 있었다.\n\n\"크리스마스 아침에 베개 밑을 들춰 보렴, 그럼 너희 안내서를 찾게 될 거야.\" 마치 부인이 대답했다.\n\n늙은 해나가 식탁을 치우는 동안 그들은 새로운 계획에 대해 이야기를 나누었다. 그러고 나서 네 개의 작은 반짇고리가 나왔고, 소녀들이 마치 대고모에게 드릴 시트를 만드느라 바늘이 바삐 움직였다. 따분한 바느질이었지만 오늘 밤은 아무도 투덜대지 않았다. 그들은 긴 솔기를 네 부분으로 나누어 그 사분면을 각각 유럽, 아시아, 아프리카, 아메리카라고 부르는 조의 방식을 받아들였고, 그렇게 하니 일이 척척 잘 풀렸다. 특히 그 나라들을 한 땀 한 땀 누비며 여러 나라에 관해 이야기를 나눌 때 더욱 그랬다.\n\n아홉 시가 되자 그들은 일손을 멈추고 잠자리에 들기 전에 늘 하던 대로 노래를 불렀다. 낡은 피아노에서 그럴듯한 음악을 끌어낼 수 있는 사람은 베스뿐이었다. 그녀는 누런 건반을 부드럽게 짚어 그들이 부르는 소박한 노래에 듣기 좋은 반주를 넣는 재주가 있었다. 메그는 플루트 같은 목소리를 지녔고, 그녀와 어머니가 그 작은 합창단을 이끌었다. 에이미는 귀뚜라미처럼 재잘거렸고, 조는 제멋대로 곡조 사이를 헤매다가 늘 엉뚱한 데서 까악거리거나 떨리는 소리를 내어 더없이 애잔한 곡조도 망쳐 놓았다. 그들은 혀 짧은 소리로\n\n\"반짝, 반짝, 자근 별아,\"\n\n를 부르던 시절부터 늘 이렇게 노래해 왔고, 어머니가 타고난 가수였기에 그것은 집안의 관습이 되었다. 아침이면 가장 먼저 들리는 소리가 종달새처럼 노래하며 집 안을 오가는 어머니의 목소리였고, 밤이면 가장 마지막에 들리는 소리도 똑같이 그 명랑한 소리였다. 소녀들은 그 정겨운 자장가를 듣기에 나이가 너무 든 적이 결코 없었으니까.\n2.\n\n# A MERRY CHRISTMAS.\n\n즐거운 크리스마스\n크리스마스 아침, 잿빛 새벽에 제일 먼저 잠에서 깬 건 조였다. 난롯가에 양말은 걸려 있지 않았고, 잠시 동안 조는 오래전 양말에 맛난 것을 너무 가득 채운 나머지 작은 양말이 떨어졌을 때만큼이나 실망스러웠다. 그러다 어머니의 약속이 떠올라 베개 밑으로 손을 넣어 진홍색 표지의 작은 책을 한 권 꺼냈다. 조는 그 책을 아주 잘 알았다. 일찍이 살았던 가장 훌륭한 삶을 담은 그 아름답고 오래된 이야기였으니까. 조는 그 책이 먼 길을 떠나는 순례자라면 누구에게나 진정한 안내서가 되리라고 느꼈다. 조는 \"메리 크리스마스\"라고 외치며 메그를 깨우고는 베개 밑을 살펴보라고 했다. 똑같은 그림이 안에 들어 있는 초록색 표지의 책이 나왔고, 어머니가 적어 놓은 몇 마디 글귀가 있어 그들의 하나뿐인 선물을 더없이 소중하게 만들었다. 곧이어 베스와 에이미도 깨어나 뒤적여서 자기들의 작은 책을 찾았는데, 하나는 비둘기색이고 다른 하나는 파란색이었다. 다들 앉아서 그 책들을 들여다보고 이야기를 나누는 동안 동녘이 밝아 오는 햇살로 발그레해졌다.\n\n마거릿은 자잘한 허영심이 있긴 했지만 다정하고 경건한 성품을 지녔고, 그 성품이 자기도 모르게 동생들에게, 특히 조에게 영향을 미쳤다. 조는 마거릿을 무척이나 다정하게 사랑했고, 그녀의 충고가 워낙 부드럽게 주어졌기에 그 말을 잘 따랐다.\n\n\"얘들아,\" 메그가 옆에 헝클어진 머리를 한 동생에서 저쪽 방에 잠자리 모자를 쓴 두 동생에게로 눈길을 옮기며 진지하게 말했다. \"엄마는 우리가 이 책들을 읽고 사랑하고 마음에 새기길 바라셔. 그러니 우리 당장 시작해야 해. 예전엔 우리도 이걸 충실히 했지만, 아버지가 떠나시고 이 전쟁 때문에 다들 마음이 어수선해지면서 많은 걸 소홀히 했잖아. 너희야 너희 좋을 대로 해도 되지만, 나는 내 책을 여기 탁자에 놓아두고 매일 아침 깨자마자 조금씩 읽을 거야. 그게 나한테 도움이 되고 하루를 잘 보내게 해 줄 거란 걸 아니까.\"\n\n그러고 나서 메그는 새 책을 펴서 읽기 시작했다. 조는 메그에게 팔을 두르고 뺨을 맞댄 채, 그 안절부절못하는 얼굴에서는 좀처럼 보기 힘든 차분한 표정으로 함께 읽었다.\n\n\"메그 언니는 정말 착해! 자, 에이미, 우리도 언니들처럼 하자. 어려운 단어는 내가 도와주고, 이해 안 되는 건 언니들이 설명해 줄 거야.\" 베스가 그 예쁜 책들과 언니들의 본보기에 크게 감동받아 속삭였다.\n\n\"내 건 파란색이라 좋아.\" 에이미가 말했다. 그러고는 책장이 부드럽게 넘어가고 겨울 햇살이 살며시 스며들어 그 밝은 머리들과 진지한 얼굴들에 크리스마스 인사를 건네는 동안, 방 안은 아주 고요해졌다.\n\n\"엄마 어디 계셔?\" 삼십 분쯤 뒤에 메그가 선물에 대한 고마움을 전하려고 조와 함께 아래층으로 뛰어 내려오며 물었다.\n\n\"그건 하느님만 아시지. 어떤 가엾은 사람이 동냥하러 왔는데, 너희 엄마가 곧장 나가서 뭐가 필요한지 보러 가셨어. 먹을 거 마실 거 옷가지 땔감을 그렇게 퍼 주는 사람은 또 없을 거다.\" 해나가 대답했다. 그녀는 메그가 태어났을 때부터 이 집과 함께 살아온 사람으로, 식구들 모두 그녀를 하인이라기보다는 친구로 여겼다.\n\n\"엄마 곧 돌아오실 거야. 그러니까 케이크를 굽고 모든 걸 준비해 놓자.\" 메그가, 바구니에 모아 소파 밑에 넣어 두었다가 때가 되면 꺼낼 선물들을 살펴보며 말했다. \"그런데 에이미의 오드콜로뉴 병은 어디 있지?\" 그 작은 병이 보이지 않자 그녀가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"좀 전에 꺼내 가던데. 리본을 묶는다나 뭐라나 하면서.\" 조가 새 군용 슬리퍼의 뻣뻣함을 처음으로 풀어 보려고 방 안을 춤추듯 돌아다니며 대답했다.\n\n\"내 손수건들 예쁘지 않아? 해나가 빨아서 다려 줬고, 글자는 내가 다 직접 새겼어.\" 베스가 그토록 애써서 새긴, 다소 들쭉날쭉한 글자들을 자랑스럽게 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"어머나, 얘 좀 봐! 'M. 마치'가 아니라 '어머니'라고 새겨 놨네. 정말 우습다!\" 조가 손수건 하나를 집어 들며 외쳤다.\n\n\"이게 맞는 거 아니야? 이렇게 하는 게 더 좋을 것 같았어. 메그 언니 이니셜도 'M. M.'이잖아, 그리고 이건 엄마 말고 아무도 안 썼으면 좋겠거든.\" 베스가 곤란한 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"맞아, 얘야, 아주 예쁜 생각이야. 게다가 아주 분별 있기도 하고. 이제 누구도 헷갈리지 않을 테니까. 엄마가 무척 좋아하실 거야, 틀림없어.\" 메그가 조에게는 눈살을 찌푸리고 베스에게는 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"엄마 오신다. 바구니 어서 숨겨!\" 문이 쾅 닫히고 복도에서 발소리가 들리자 조가 외쳤다.\n\n에이미가 황급히 들어왔는데, 언니들이 모두 자기를 기다리고 있는 걸 보고는 다소 무안한 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"어디 갔다 왔어, 그리고 등 뒤에 감춘 건 뭐야?\" 메그가 물었다. 게으른 에이미가 두건과 망토 차림인 걸 보고 그렇게 일찍 밖에 나갔다 온 게 놀라웠던 것이다.\n\n\"비웃지 마, 조! 때가 될 때까진 아무도 몰랐으면 했어. 난 그저 작은 병을 큰 걸로 바꾸고 싶었던 것뿐이야, 그래서 내 돈을 다 털어서 샀어. 난 정말 더는 이기적으로 굴지 않으려고 애쓰는 중이야.\"\n\n에이미는 말하면서, 싸구려 병을 대신한 멋진 병을 내보였다. 자기를 잊으려는 그 작은 노력 속에 너무나 진지하고 겸손해 보여서 메그는 그 자리에서 동생을 껴안았고, 조는 그녀를 \"멋진 녀석\"이라고 추켜세웠으며, 베스는 창가로 달려가 그 근사한 병을 장식할 가장 고운 장미 한 송이를 꺾어 왔다.\n\n\"있잖아, 오늘 아침에 착해지자는 얘기를 읽고 나누고 나니까 내 선물이 부끄러웠어. 그래서 일어나자마자 모퉁이로 달려가서 바로 바꿔 왔지. 그래서 정말 기뻐, 이제 내 선물이 제일 멋지니까.\"\n\n다시 한번 현관문이 쾅 닫히는 소리에 바구니는 소파 밑으로 들어갔고, 소녀들은 아침을 먹고 싶어 안달하며 탁자로 모여들었다.\n\n\"메리 크리스마스, 엄마! 앞으로도 즐거운 크리스마스 많이 맞으세요! 책 고마워요. 좀 읽었고, 매일 읽을 거예요.\" 그들이 입을 모아 외쳤다.\n\n\"메리 크리스마스, 우리 딸들! 당장 시작했다니 기쁘구나, 앞으로도 계속하길 바란다. 그런데 앉기 전에 한마디 하고 싶구나. 여기서 멀지 않은 곳에 갓 태어난 아기를 둔 가엾은 여인이 누워 있단다. 불기운이 없어서 아이 여섯이 얼어 죽지 않으려고 한 침대에 옹기종기 모여 있어. 거기엔 먹을 게 하나도 없는데, 큰애가 와서 자기들이 추위와 굶주림에 시달리고 있다고 말해 주더구나. 우리 딸들아, 너희 아침을 그 사람들한테 크리스마스 선물로 주겠니?\"\n\n다들 거의 한 시간이나 기다린 터라 유난히 배가 고팠고, 잠깐 동안 아무도 말이 없었다. 정말 잠깐이었다. 조가 곧 충동적으로 외쳤으니까.\n\n\"우리가 먹기 전에 엄마가 와 줘서 정말 다행이에요!\"\n\n\"가서 그 가엾은 아이들한테 음식 나르는 걸 도와도 될까요?\" 베스가 간절하게 물었다.\n\n\"난 크림이랑 머핀을 가져갈래요.\" 에이미가, 자기가 제일 좋아하는 것들을 영웅처럼 내놓으며 덧붙였다.\n\n메그는 어느새 메밀 케이크를 덮고 빵을 큰 접시 하나에 차곡차곡 쌓고 있었다.\n\n\"너희라면 그럴 줄 알았다.\" 마치 부인이 흡족한 듯 미소 지으며 말했다. \"다 같이 가서 엄마를 도와주렴. 그리고 돌아와서는 빵하고 우유로 아침을 먹고, 점심때 보충하자꾸나.\"\n\n그들은 곧 채비를 마쳤고, 행렬이 출발했다. 다행히 이른 시각이었고 뒷골목을 지나갔기 때문에 본 사람이 거의 없었고, 그 이상한 무리를 비웃는 이도 없었다.\n\n그곳은 가난하고 휑하니 비참한 방이었다. 창문은 깨져 있고, 불기운도 없으며, 침구는 너덜너덜했고, 병든 어머니와 보채는 아기, 그리고 따뜻한 온기를 지키려고 낡은 누비이불 하나 밑에 옹기종기 모인 창백하고 굶주린 아이들 한 무리가 있었다. 소녀들이 들어서자 그 큰 눈들이 얼마나 휘둥그레지고 새파란 입술들이 얼마나 환하게 웃던지!\n\n\"아, 마인 고트! 우리한테 착한 천사들이 왔구나!\" 가엾은 여인이 기뻐서 울며 말했다.\n\n\"두건 쓰고 벙어리장갑 낀 우스운 천사들이죠.\" 조가 말하자 다들 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n몇 분 만에 정말로 그곳에서 친절한 정령들이 일을 한 것만 같았다. 장작을 날라 온 해나는 불을 피우고 깨진 유리창을 낡은 모자들과 자기 망토로 틀어막았다. 마치 부인은 어머니에게 차와 죽을 주고 도와주겠다는 약속으로 위로하면서, 마치 제 아기인 양 다정하게 갓난아기에게 옷을 입혔다. 그동안 소녀들은 식탁을 차리고 아이들을 불가에 둘러앉힌 다음, 굶주린 새 떼처럼 그들을 먹였다. 웃고 떠들고, 우스꽝스럽게 더듬거리는 영어를 알아들으려 애쓰면서.\n\n\"다스 이스트 구트!\" \"디 엥겔킨더!\" 가엾은 아이들이 먹으면서, 또 시퍼레진 손을 그 따뜻한 불길에 녹이면서 외쳤다.\n\n소녀들은 전에 천사 아이들이라고 불려 본 적이 없었기에 그게 무척 흐뭇했고, 특히 태어났을 때부터 \"산초\"로 여겨져 온 조가 그러했다. 비록 그들은 한 입도 먹지 못했지만 더없이 행복한 아침이었다. 그리고 그들이 온기를 남겨 두고 돌아왔을 때, 크리스마스 아침에 자기 아침을 내주고 빵과 우유로 만족한 그 배고픈 어린 소녀들보다 더 즐거운 사람은 온 도시를 통틀어 넷도 없었으리라.\n\n\"이게 바로 우리 자신보다 이웃을 더 사랑하는 거야, 난 이게 좋아.\" 메그가, 어머니가 위층에서 가엾은 후멜네 식구들에게 줄 옷가지를 챙기는 동안 선물들을 차려 놓으며 말했다.\n\n그리 대단한 구경거리는 아니었지만, 그 몇 안 되는 작은 꾸러미들 속에는 엄청난 사랑이 담겨 있었다. 그리고 한가운데 놓인, 빨간 장미와 흰 국화, 늘어진 덩굴이 어우러진 키 큰 꽃병이 탁자에 제법 우아한 분위기를 더해 주었다.\n\n\"엄마 오신다! 시작해, 베스! 문 열어, 에이미! 엄마 만세!\" 조가 깡충거리며 외쳤고, 메그는 어머니를 상석으로 모시러 갔다.\n\n베스는 가장 흥겨운 행진곡을 연주했고, 에이미는 문을 활짝 열었으며, 메그는 더없이 위엄 있게 호위 역할을 해냈다. 마치 부인은 놀라면서도 감동했다. 선물들을 살펴보고 거기 딸린 작은 쪽지들을 읽으며 눈에 가득 눈물이 고인 채 미소 지었다. 슬리퍼는 곧장 신었고, 에이미의 오드콜로뉴 향이 그윽한 새 손수건은 주머니에 넣었으며, 장미는 가슴에 꽂았고, 그 멋진 장갑은 \"꼭 맞는다\"는 평을 받았다.\n\n이런 가정 축제를 그 순간에는 그토록 즐겁게, 또 오랜 세월이 지난 뒤에도 그토록 달콤하게 떠올리게 하는 소박하고 다정한 방식으로, 웃고 입 맞추고 설명하는 일이 한참 이어졌다. 그러고 나서 다들 일에 매달렸다.\n\n아침의 자선과 의식이 너무 많은 시간을 잡아먹은 탓에, 그날 남은 시간은 저녁의 축제 준비에 바쳐졌다. 극장에 자주 가기엔 아직 너무 어렸고, 사적인 공연에 큰돈을 쓸 만큼 부유하지도 못했기에, 소녀들은 머리를 짜냈고 — 필요는 발명의 어머니라고 — 필요한 것은 무엇이든 손수 만들었다. 그들의 제작품 중에는 제법 기발한 것들이 있었다. 판지로 만든 기타, 은박지를 입힌 구식 버터 그릇으로 만든 골동품 램프, 절임 공장에서 나온 양철 장식 조각을 반짝반짝 붙인 낡은 무명천으로 만든 호화로운 의상, 그리고 양철 저장 단지 뚜껑을 도려낼 때 판으로 남는 똑같은 마름모꼴 조각들을 붙인 갑옷이 그러했다. 가구는 늘 위아래로 뒤집히곤 했고, 그 큰 방은 수많은 천진한 잔치의 무대가 되었다.\n\n남자는 출입 금지였다. 그래서 조는 마음껏 남자 역할을 맡았고, 어느 친구가 준 적갈색 가죽 부츠 한 켤레에 무한한 만족을 느꼈다. 그 친구는 어느 배우를 아는 어느 부인을 알았던 것이다. 이 부츠와 낡은 펜싱 검 한 자루, 그리고 예전에 어느 화가가 그림을 그릴 때 쓴 적이 있는 칼집 낸 더블릿이 조의 가장 큰 보물이었고, 모든 무대에 등장했다. 출연진이 적다 보니 두 주연 배우가 각각 여러 역할을 맡아야 했다. 그들은 서너 가지 다른 배역을 외우고, 갖가지 의상을 후딱후딱 갈아입고, 게다가 무대까지 관리하느라 무던히 애를 썼으니 정말이지 칭찬받을 만했다. 그건 그들의 기억력에 훌륭한 훈련이 되었고, 해롭지 않은 오락이었으며, 그러지 않았다면 한가하거나 외롭거나 덜 유익한 사람들과 어울리며 보냈을 많은 시간을 채워 주었다.\n\n크리스마스 밤, 특별석인 침대 위로 열두어 명의 소녀가 우르르 올라가 앉아, 파란색과 노란색 사라사 무명 커튼 앞에서 더없이 흐뭇한 기대에 잠겨 있었다. 커튼 뒤에서는 부스럭거리고 속닥거리는 소리, 약간의 램프 그을음, 그리고 그 순간의 흥분에 곧잘 발작적으로 들뜨곤 하는 에이미의 이따금씩 터지는 킥킥대는 웃음소리가 났다. 이윽고 종이 울리고, 커튼이 양쪽으로 휙 갈라지면서 오페라 비극이 시작되었다.\n\n한 장짜리 공연 안내문에 따른 \"음울한 숲\"은 화분 몇 개의 관목과 바닥에 깐 초록색 모직천, 그리고 저 멀리 동굴로 표현되었다. 이 동굴은 빨래 건조대를 지붕으로, 서랍장들을 벽으로 삼아 만들어졌다. 그 안에는 작은 화로가 활활 타오르며 위에 검은 솥이 얹혀 있었고, 늙은 마녀가 그 위로 몸을 굽히고 있었다. 무대는 어두웠고, 화로의 불빛은 멋진 효과를 냈는데, 특히 마녀가 뚜껑을 열자 솥에서 진짜 김이 피어오를 때 그러했다. 첫 전율이 가라앉을 잠깐의 틈이 주어졌다. 그러고는 악당 휴고가 옆구리에 절그럭거리는 검을 차고, 챙 늘어진 모자에 검은 수염, 신비로운 망토, 그리고 그 부츠를 신은 채 성큼성큼 들어왔다. 몹시 흥분한 채 이리저리 거닐던 그는 이마를 탁 치더니, 격렬한 가락으로 로드리고에 대한 증오와 자라에 대한 사랑, 그리고 하나는 죽이고 다른 하나는 차지하겠다는 흐뭇한 결심을 노래하며 터뜨렸다. 휴고의 굵직한 목소리는, 감정이 북받칠 때 이따금 터지는 고함과 더불어 무척 인상적이었고, 그가 숨을 고르려 멈추는 순간 관객들은 박수를 보냈다. 대중의 갈채에 익숙한 사람 특유의 분위기로 고개 숙여 인사한 그는 살금살금 동굴로 다가가, \"여봐라, 종복아! 네가 필요하다!\"라고 위엄 있게 명하며 하가르에게 나오라고 했다.\n회색 말털을 얼굴에 늘어뜨리고, 빨강과 검정이 어우러진 가운에 지팡이를 들고, 망토에 신비로운 부호들을 그려 넣은 메그가 나왔다. 휴고는 자라가 자기를 흠모하게 만들 묘약 하나와 로드리고를 없앨 묘약 하나를 요구했다. 하가르는 멋들어진 극적인 가락으로 둘 다 주겠노라 약속하고는, 사랑의 묘약을 가져올 정령을 불러내기 시작했다.\n\n\"이리로, 이리로, 네 집에서,\n바람의 정령이여, 너를 부르노라!\n\n장미에게서 태어나 이슬을 먹는 너,\n마법과 묘약을 빚을 줄 아느냐?\n\n요정의 빠르기로 내게 가져오라,\n내가 필요로 하는 향기로운 묘약을.\n\n달콤하고 빠르고 강하게 만들지니,\n정령이여, 이제 내 노래에 답하라!\"\n\n부드러운 음악 가락이 울리더니, 동굴 뒤편에 반짝이는 날개와 금빛 머리, 머리에 장미 화관을 두른, 뽀얀 흰빛의 작은 형체가 나타났다. 지팡이를 흔들며 그것이 노래했다.\n\"이리로 내가 왔노라,\n\n저 멀리 은빛 달에 있는\n바람의 내 집에서.\n\n이 마법의 주문을 받아\n잘 쓰도록 하라,\n\n안 그러면 그 힘이 곧 사라지리니!\"\n그러고는 작은 금빛 병을 마녀의 발치에 떨어뜨린 채 정령은 사라졌다. 하가르의 또 다른 주문이 또 다른 환영을 불러냈는데 — 이번엔 사랑스러운 게 아니었다. 쾅 하는 소리와 함께 흉측한 검은 도깨비가 나타나, 깍깍대며 대답하더니 검은 병 하나를 휴고에게 던지고는 조롱하는 웃음과 함께 사라졌으니까. 휴고는 노래하듯 고마움을 읊고 묘약들을 부츠 속에 넣고는 떠났다. 그리고 하가르는 그가 예전에 자기 친구 몇을 죽인 탓에 자기가 그에게 저주를 걸어 두었고, 그의 계획을 좌절시키고 복수할 작정이라고 관객에게 알렸다. 그런 다음 막이 내렸고, 관객들은 쉬면서 사탕을 먹으며 연극의 장점에 대해 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n막이 다시 오르기 전에 한참 망치질 소리가 났다. 하지만 얼마나 대단한 무대 목공 걸작이 마련되었는지가 드러나자 아무도 그 지체에 대해 투덜거리지 않았다. 정말이지 장관이었다! 탑 하나가 천장까지 솟아올랐고, 그 중간쯤에 램프가 타오르는 창이 나타났으며, 흰 커튼 뒤에는 사랑스러운 파란색과 은색 드레스를 입은 자라가 로드리고를 기다리고 있었다. 그가 깃털 장식 모자에 빨간 망토, 밤색 애교머리, 기타, 그리고 물론 그 부츠까지 갖춘 화려한 차림으로 들어왔다. 탑 아래에 무릎을 꿇은 그는 애간장 녹는 가락으로 세레나데를 불렀다. 자라가 화답했고, 음악 같은 대화 끝에 함께 도망치기로 했다. 그러고는 연극의 절정 효과가 펼쳐졌다. 로드리고가 다섯 단짜리 밧줄 사다리를 꺼내 한쪽 끝을 위로 던지고는 자라에게 내려오라고 청했다. 자라는 겁먹은 듯 격자창에서 기어 나와 로드리고의 어깨에 손을 얹고는 우아하게 뛰어내리려던 참이었는데 — \"아아! 가엾은 자라여!\" — 그녀가 그만 드레스 자락을 깜빡하고 말았다. 자락이 창에 걸린 것이다. 탑이 휘청거리고, 앞으로 기울더니, 와르르 무너져 불운한 연인들을 잔해 속에 파묻어 버렸다!\n적갈색 부츠가 잔해 속에서 마구 흔들리고 금빛 머리가 불쑥 튀어나와 \"내가 그랬잖아! 내가 그랬잖아!\" 하고 외치자, 사방에서 비명이 터져 나왔다. 놀라운 침착함을 발휘해 잔인한 아버지 돈 페드로가 달려들어 딸을 끌어내며 황급히 옆으로 속삭였다.\n\n\"웃지 마! 다 괜찮은 것처럼 연기해!\" 그러고는 로드리고에게 일어나라 명하고, 분노와 경멸로 그를 왕국에서 추방했다. 탑이 자기 위로 무너진 탓에 적잖이 흔들렸으면서도 로드리고는 노인에게 맞서며 꼼짝도 하지 않으려 했다. 이 담대한 본보기에 자라도 불이 붙었다. 그녀 역시 아버지에게 맞섰고, 그러자 아버지는 둘 다 성의 가장 깊은 지하 감옥으로 보내라고 명했다. 땅딸막한 가신이 쇠사슬을 들고 들어와 둘을 끌고 나갔는데, 잔뜩 겁먹은 표정에 자기가 했어야 할 대사는 까맣게 잊은 게 분명했다.\n\n3막은 성의 대전(大殿)이었다. 여기서 하가르가 등장했는데, 연인들을 풀어 주고 휴고를 끝장내려고 온 것이었다. 그녀는 그가 오는 소리를 듣고 몸을 숨긴다. 그가 두 잔의 포도주에 묘약을 타고는 겁 많은 작은 하인에게 \"이걸 감방의 죄수들한테 가져가서, 내가 곧 가겠다고 전해라\"라고 이르는 것을 본다. 하인이 휴고를 한쪽으로 데려가 무슨 말을 하는 사이에 하가르가 두 잔을 해롭지 않은 다른 두 잔으로 바꿔치기한다. \"종복\" 페르디난도가 그것들을 가지고 나가고, 하가르는 로드리고를 노린 독이 든 잔을 도로 가져다 놓는다. 한참 노래를 부른 뒤 목이 마른 휴고가 그것을 마시고는 정신을 잃더니, 한참을 움켜쥐고 발을 구르다가 풀썩 쓰러져 죽는다. 그러는 동안 하가르는 자기가 무슨 짓을 했는지를 더없이 강렬하고 아름다운 가락의 노래로 그에게 알린다.\n이것은 정말로 소름 끼치는 장면이었다. 비록 어떤 이들은 긴 머리카락이 갑자기 우수수 쏟아져 내린 것이 악당의 죽음이 자아내는 효과를 다소 망쳤다고 생각했을지도 모르지만. 그는 커튼 앞으로 불려 나왔고, 더없이 예의 바르게 하가르를 이끌고 나타났다. 하가르의 노래는 다른 모든 공연을 다 합친 것보다 더 훌륭하다는 평을 받았다.\n\n4막에서는 절망에 빠진 로드리고가 막 자기 몸을 찌르려는 참이었다. 자라가 자기를 버렸다는 말을 들었기 때문이다. 단검이 막 그의 심장에 닿으려는 찰나, 그의 창문 아래에서 사랑스러운 노래가 들려와, 자라는 변함없지만 위험에 처해 있으며 그가 마음만 먹으면 그녀를 구할 수 있다고 알린다. 열쇠 하나가 안으로 던져져 문을 열어 주고, 환희의 발작 속에서 그는 쇠사슬을 끊어 버리고는 사랑하는 여인을 찾아 구하러 달려 나간다.\n5막은 자라와 돈 페드로 사이의 격렬한 장면으로 열렸다. 그는 그녀가 수녀원에 들어가기를 바라지만, 그녀는 들으려고도 하지 않는다. 그리고 가슴 아픈 호소 끝에 막 기절하려는 찰나, 로드리고가 뛰어들어 그녀와의 결혼을 요구한다. 돈 페드로는 그가 부자가 아니라는 이유로 거절한다. 둘은 엄청나게 고함치고 손짓 발짓을 해 대지만 뜻을 모으지 못하고, 로드리고가 막 기진맥진한 자라를 데리고 나가려는데, 겁 많은 하인이 신비롭게 사라져 버린 하가르가 보낸 편지 한 통과 자루 하나를 들고 들어온다. 편지는 하가르가 젊은 두 사람에게는 헤아릴 수 없는 부를, 그리고 돈 페드로가 그들을 행복하게 해 주지 않으면 그에게는 끔찍한 파멸을 남긴다고 일행에게 알린다. 자루를 열자 양철 돈 몇 쿼트가 무대 위로 쏟아져 내려 반짝임으로 온 무대가 그야말로 찬란해진다. 이것이 그 \"냉혹한 아비\"를 완전히 누그러뜨린다. 그는 군말 없이 승낙하고, 모두가 기쁨에 찬 합창에 어우러지며, 더없이 낭만적이고 우아한 자세로 돈 페드로의 축복을 받으려 무릎 꿇은 연인들 위로 막이 내린다.\n\n우레와 같은 박수갈채가 뒤따랐지만, 뜻밖의 제동이 걸리고 말았다. \"특별석\"을 떠받치던 간이침대가 갑자기 접히면서 열광하던 관객들을 깔아뭉갠 것이다. 로드리고와 돈 페드로가 구하러 날아갔고, 다들 다친 데 없이 끌려 나왔지만 여럿이 웃느라 말도 못 했다. 그 소동이 채 가라앉기도 전에 해나가 나타나 \"마치 부인께서 안부 전하시며, 아가씨들 저녁 드시러 내려오시랍니다\"라고 했다.\n\n이것은 배우들에게조차 뜻밖의 일이었다. 그리고 그들은 식탁을 보고 황홀한 놀라움에 잠겨 서로를 쳐다보았다. 그들에게 작은 깜짝 선물을 마련하는 건 엄마다운 일이었지만, 이렇게 근사한 건 풍족했던 옛 시절이 지나간 이래로 듣도 보도 못한 일이었다. 아이스크림이 — 그것도 분홍색과 흰색, 두 접시나 — 있었고, 케이크와 과일, 그리고 정신이 아찔할 만큼 멋진 프랑스 봉봉이 있었으며, 식탁 한가운데에는 온실 꽃으로 만든 커다란 꽃다발 네 개가 놓여 있었다!\n\n다들 숨이 멎을 지경이었고, 식탁을 보았다가 다시 어머니를 보았다가 했는데, 어머니는 이걸 엄청나게 즐기는 듯한 표정이었다.\n\n\"요정들이 한 거예요?\" 에이미가 물었다.\n\n\"산타클로스야.\" 베스가 말했다.\n\n\"엄마가 하셨어.\" 메그가, 회색 수염과 흰 눈썹을 붙인 채로도 더없이 다정한 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"마치 대고모가 잠깐 마음이 동하셔서 저녁을 보내신 거야.\" 조가 문득 떠오른 생각에 외쳤다.\n\n\"다 틀렸어. 늙은 로런스 씨가 보내신 거란다.\" 마치 부인이 대답했다.\n\n\"로런스네 소년의 할아버지가요! 도대체 무슨 바람이 불어서 그런 생각을 하셨대요? 우린 그분을 알지도 못하는데!\" 메그가 외쳤다.\n\n\"해나가 그분 댁 하인 하나한테 너희 아침 잔치 얘기를 했단다. 별난 노신사이긴 한데, 그게 마음에 드셨나 봐. 그분은 오래전에 내 아버지를 아셨고, 오늘 오후에 정중한 쪽지를 보내셨더구나. 이날을 기념해 너희한테 자잘한 것 몇 가지를 보내 우리 아이들을 향한 다정한 마음을 표현하게 해 주면 좋겠다고 말이야. 거절할 수가 없었지. 그래서 빵과 우유로 때운 아침을 보충하라고 너희가 밤에 이런 작은 잔치를 누리게 된 거란다.\"\n\n\"그 애가 그 생각을 불어넣은 거예요, 분명해요! 정말 멋진 녀석인데, 우리가 알고 지낼 수 있으면 좋겠어요. 우리랑 알고 지내고 싶어 하는 눈치인데, 부끄럼을 타는 데다 메그 언니가 어찌나 새침한지 우리가 지나칠 때 내가 말도 못 걸게 한다니까요.\" 조가 말했는데, 그러는 동안 접시들이 돌고 아이스크림은 \"오!\"와 \"아!\" 하는 만족스러운 탄성과 함께 눈앞에서 녹아 사라지기 시작했다.\n\n\"옆집 큰 저택에 사는 사람들 말하는 거지?\" 소녀 중 하나가 물었다. \"우리 엄마가 늙은 로런스 씨를 아는데, 무척 거만한 양반이라 이웃들이랑 어울리는 걸 싫어하신대. 손자가 가정교사랑 말 타거나 산책할 때 빼고는 가둬 두고 공부를 아주 빡세게 시킨다나 봐. 우리 파티에 초대했는데 안 왔어. 우리 엄마 말로는 아주 좋은 애라는데, 우리 여자애들한텐 말도 안 걸어.\"\n\n\"한번은 우리 고양이가 달아났는데 그 애가 데려다줬어. 그래서 우리가 울타리 너머로 이야기를 나눴는데, 크리켓이며 뭐며 얘기하면서 척척 잘 통하던 참이었거든. 그러다 메그 언니가 오는 걸 보더니 가 버리더라. 난 언젠가 그 애랑 꼭 알고 지낼 거야. 그 앤 즐거움이 필요해, 분명 그래.\" 조가 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"그 애 행동거지가 마음에 들고, 어린 신사 같아 보이더구나. 그러니 적당한 기회만 온다면 너희가 그 애와 알고 지내는 데 반대하지 않으마. 꽃은 그 애가 직접 가져왔단다. 위층에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 확실히 알았더라면 안으로 들이자고 했을 텐데. 떠나면서 그 흥겨운 소리를 듣는데, 정작 자기는 그런 즐거움이 하나도 없는 듯 어찌나 아쉬운 표정이던지.\"\n\n\"안 들이시길 천만다행이에요, 엄마!\" 조가 자기 부츠를 내려다보며 웃었다. \"하지만 언젠가 그 애가 볼 수 있는 다른 연극을 하면 되죠. 어쩌면 그 애가 연기를 도와줄지도 몰라요. 그럼 신나지 않겠어요?\"\n\n\"이렇게 멋진 꽃다발은 처음 받아 봐! 정말 예쁘다!\" 메그가 자기 꽃들을 큰 관심을 갖고 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"정말 사랑스럽구나! 그래도 나한테는 베스의 장미가 더 향기롭단다.\" 마치 부인이 허리띠에 꽂힌, 반쯤 시든 작은 꽃다발의 냄새를 맡으며 말했다.\n\n베스가 어머니에게 바싹 다가가 가만히 속삭였다. \"제 꽃다발을 아빠한테 보낼 수 있으면 좋겠어요. 아빠는 우리만큼 즐거운 크리스마스를 보내고 계시지 못할 것 같아서요.\"\n3.\n\n# THE LAURENCE BOY.\n\n로런스네 소년\n\"조! 조! 어디 있어?\" 메그가 다락방 계단 밑에서 외쳤다.\n\n\"여기!\" 위에서 잠긴 목소리가 대답했다. 메그가 뛰어 올라가 보니, 동생은 양지바른 창가의 낡은 다리 셋 달린 소파에 누비이불을 두르고 앉아 사과를 먹으며 「레드클리프의 상속자」를 읽고 울고 있었다. 여기가 조가 가장 좋아하는 안식처였다. 조용한 분위기와, 근처에 살면서 자기를 조금도 신경 쓰지 않는 애완 쥐 한 마리의 벗 삼음을 즐기려고, 적갈색 사과 대여섯 개와 괜찮은 책 한 권을 들고 이리로 틀어박히는 걸 좋아했다. 메그가 나타나자 스크래블은 제 구멍으로 휙 달아났다. 조는 뺨에 흐른 눈물을 털어 내고 소식을 들으려고 기다렸다.\n\n\"정말 신나! 이것 좀 봐! 내일 밤 가드너 부인이 보낸 정식 초대장이야!\" 메그가 그 귀한 종이를 흔들며 외쳤고, 그러고는\n그것을 읽어 내려가는 그녀의 얼굴에 소녀다운 기쁨이 어렸다.\n\n\"'가드너 부인은 새해 전야의 작은 무도회에 메그 양과 조세핀 양을 모시게 되어 기쁘겠습니다.' 엄마가 가도 좋다고 하셨어. 그런데 우리 도대체 뭘 입지?\"\n\n\"그걸 왜 물어? 우리한텐 포플린 드레스밖에 없으니까 그거 입을 게 뻔한데.\" 조가 입에 음식을 가득 문 채 대답했다.\n\n\"나한테 실크 드레스 하나만 있다면!\" 메그가 한숨을 쉬었다. \"엄마가 그러시는데, 아마 내가 열여덟 살이 되면 하나 사 주실 거래. 그런데 두 해를 기다리려면 끝도 없이 멀게만 느껴져.\"\n\n\"우리 포플린도 실크처럼 보이는걸. 그리고 우리한텐 충분히 괜찮잖아. 언니 건 거의 새것이나 다름없는데, 난 그만 내 옷에 탄 자국이랑 찢어진 데를 깜빡했지 뭐야. 어쩌면 좋아? 탄 자국이 너무 눈에 띄는데, 도려낼 수도 없고.\"\n\n\"넌 되도록 가만히 앉아서 등을 안 보이게 하면 돼. 앞쪽은 멀쩡하잖아. 난 머리에 맬 새 리본을 살 거고, 엄마가 작은 진주 핀을 빌려주신다고 하셨어. 내 새 슬리퍼는 정말 예쁘고, 장갑도 그럭저럭 괜찮아. 내가 바라는 만큼 고운 건 아니지만.\"\n\n\"내 건 레모네이드 때문에 다 망가졌고, 새 걸 살 수도 없으니 그냥 끼지 말고 가야겠어.\" 조가 말했다. 옷차림 따위로는 좀처럼 골머리를 앓지 않는 그녀였다.\n\n\"넌 반드시 장갑을 껴야 해. 안 그러면 나도 안 갈 거야.\" 메그가 단호하게 외쳤다. \"장갑은 다른 어떤 것보다 중요해. 장갑이 없으면 춤도 못 추는걸. 네가 장갑을 안 끼면 난 정말 창피해서 견딜 수가 없을 거야.\"\n\n\"그럼 난 가만히 앉아 있을게. 사교 춤 같은 건 별로 좋아하지도 않아. 미끄러지듯 빙빙 도는 게 무슨 재미야. 난 이리저리 날아다니면서 깡충깡충 뛰는 게 좋단 말이야.\"\n\n\"엄마한테 새 걸 사 달라고 할 순 없어. 너무 비싸고, 넌 또 워낙 덤벙대잖아. 지난번에 네가 장갑을 못 쓰게 만들었을 때, 엄마가 이번 겨울엔 더 안 사 주신다고 하셨어. 그거 어떻게 좀 끼고 갈 순 없니?\" 메그가 근심스럽게 물었다.\n\n\"손안에 꼬깃꼬깃 쥐고 있으면 돼. 그럼 아무도 얼마나 얼룩졌는지 모를 거야. 내가 할 수 있는 건 그게 다야. 아니지! 어떻게 하면 되는지 내가 알려 줄게. 각자 멀쩡한 거 한 짝씩 끼고, 망가진 건 한 짝씩 손에 쥐고 가는 거야. 알겠어?\"\n\n\"네 손이 내 손보다 큰데, 그러면 내 장갑이 끔찍하게 늘어날 거야.\" 메그가 입을 열었다. 그녀에게 장갑은 예민한 문제였다.\n\n\"그럼 난 그냥 안 끼고 갈래. 사람들이 뭐라든 상관없어!\" 조가 책을 집어 들며 외쳤다.\n\n\"껴도 돼, 껴! 그 대신 얼룩만 묻히지 말고, 얌전히 굴어야 해. 손을 뒤로 돌리지도 말고, 빤히 쳐다보지도 말고, '크리스토퍼 콜럼버스!' 같은 말도 하지 마. 알겠지?\"\n\n\"내 걱정은 마. 최대한 점잖게 굴면서, 어떻게든 사고는 안 칠게. 자, 이제 가서 답장이나 써. 난 이 멋진 이야기를 마저 읽게.\"\n\n그래서 메그는 자리를 떠나 \"감사히 참석하겠다\"는 답장을 쓰고, 자기 옷을 살펴보고, 단 하나뿐인 진짜 레이스 주름 장식을 손질하면서 즐겁게 노래를 흥얼거렸다. 그동안 조는 이야기를 끝까지 읽고, 사과 네 알을 다 먹어 치우고, 스크래블과 한바탕 뛰어놀았다.\n\n새해 전야, 거실은 텅 비어 있었다. 두 동생은 옷 시중을 드느라, 두 언니는 \"무도회 갈 채비\"라는 더없이 중대한 일에 푹 빠져 있었기 때문이다. 단장이라야 소박하기 그지없었지만, 위층 아래층을 오르내리고, 웃고 떠드는 소동이 한바탕 벌어졌고, 한때는 머리카락 타는 냄새가 집 안에 진동했다. 메그가 얼굴 둘레에 곱슬머리를 좀 만들고 싶어 했고, 조가 종이로 만 머리 타래를 뜨거운 인두로 집어 보겠다고 나섰다.\n\n\"머리카락이 저렇게 연기가 나도 괜찮은 거야?\" 침대에 걸터앉은 베스가 물었다.\n\n\"습기가 마르는 거야.\" 조가 대답했다.\n\n\"무슨 냄새가 이렇게 이상해! 깃털 타는 냄새 같아.\" 에이미가 자기 예쁜 곱슬머리를 우쭐한 태도로 매만지며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 이제 종이를 떼어 내면 작은 고수머리가 구름처럼 뭉게뭉게 나타날 거야.\" 조가 인두를 내려놓으며 말했다.\n\n조는 정말로 종이를 떼어 냈지만, 고수머리 구름은 나타나지 않았다. 머리카락이 종이에 딸려 나오는 바람에, 경악한 미용사는 희생자 앞 화장대 위에 자그마한 그을린 다발들을 한 줄로 늘어놓고 말았다.\n\n\"어머, 어머, 어머! 너 도대체 무슨 짓을 한 거야? 나 망했어! 이러고 어떻게 가! 내 머리, 아, 내 머리!\" 메그가 이마 위로 들쭉날쭉 곱슬거리는 머리를 절망스레 바라보며 울부짖었다.\n\n\"내가 그렇지 뭐! 언니가 나한테 시키지 말았어야 했어. 난 늘 모든 걸 망쳐 버린다니까. 정말 미안해. 인두가 너무 뜨거워서 이렇게 엉망으로 만들어 버렸어.\" 가엾은 조가 후회의 눈물을 머금고 그 까만 팬케이크들을 바라보며 끙끙댔다.\n\n\"망친 게 아니야. 그냥 곱슬곱슬하게 손질하고, 리본을 매서 끝자락이 이마 위로 살짝 내려오게 하면, 최신 유행처럼 보일 거야. 그렇게 하는 여자애들 많이 봤어.\" 에이미가 위로하듯 말했다.\n\n\"멋 좀 내 보겠다고 까불었으니 싸지. 머리를 그냥 내버려 둘걸.\" 메그가 짜증스레 외쳤다.\n\n\"나도 그래. 정말 매끄럽고 예뻤는데. 하지만 금방 다시 자랄 거야.\" 베스가 다가와 털 깎인 양에게 입을 맞추고 다독여 주며 말했다.\n\n크고 작은 사고를 여러 차례 겪은 끝에, 마침내 메그의 단장이 끝났고, 온 식구가 힘을 합친 덕분에 조의 머리도 올려 묶이고 드레스도 입혀졌다. 소박한 차림이었지만 두 사람은 무척 보기 좋았다. 메그는 은빛 도는 회갈색 드레스에 파란 벨벳 머리띠와 레이스 주름 장식, 그리고 진주 핀을 달았고, 조는 적갈색 드레스에 빳빳하고 신사다운 리넨 깃을 세우고 흰 국화 한두 송이를 유일한 장식으로 꽂았다. 둘은 각자 멀쩡한 밝은색 장갑 한 짝씩을 끼고, 얼룩진 한 짝씩을 손에 쥐었으며, 그 모양새가 \"꽤 편안하고 멋지다\"고 입을 모았다. 메그의 굽 높은 슬리퍼는 너무 꽉 끼어 발이 아팠지만 그녀는 내색하지 않았고, 조의 머리에 꽂은 열아홉 개의 머리핀은 죄다 머리통에 곧장 꽂혀 들어가는 것만 같아 그리 편치는 않았다. 하지만 어쩌랴, 우아해지든가 죽든가 둘 중 하나인 것을!\n\n\"즐거운 시간 보내렴, 얘들아!\" 자매가 산뜻한 걸음으로 길을 내려가자 마치 부인이 말했다. \"저녁은 많이 먹지 말고, 열한 시에 한나를 보낼 테니 그때 나오너라.\" 등 뒤로 대문이 철컹 닫히는 순간, 창문에서 목소리가 들려왔다.\n\n\"얘들아, 얘들아! 둘 다 깨끗한 손수건은 챙겼니?\"\n\n\"네, 네, 아주 말끔한 걸로요. 메그 언니 건 향수까지 뿌렸어요.\" 조가 외치고는, 걸어가면서 웃으며 덧붙였다. \"지진이 나서 우리가 다 도망치는 중이라도 엄마는 분명 저걸 물어보실 거야.\"\n\n\"그건 엄마의 우아한 취향 가운데 하나고, 아주 옳은 거야. 진짜 숙녀는 깔끔한 부츠와 장갑, 손수건으로 알아보는 거니까.\" 메그가 대답했다. 그녀 역시 자기만의 자잘한 \"우아한 취향\"이 제법 많았다.\n\n\"자, 조, 망가진 자락 안 보이게 하는 거 잊지 마. 내 허리띠 괜찮아? 그리고 내 머리 많이 흉해 보여?\" 가드너 부인의 화장실 거울 앞에서 한참을 매만지던 메그가 거울에서 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"난 분명 깜빡할 거야. 내가 뭔가 잘못하는 게 보이면, 그냥 눈짓으로 알려 줘. 알겠지?\" 조가 깃을 홱 잡아당기고 머리를 허둥지둥 쓸어 넘기며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"안 돼, 눈짓은 숙녀답지 못해. 뭔가 잘못되면 내가 눈썹을 치켜올릴게. 괜찮으면 고개를 끄덕이고. 자, 어깨 똑바로 펴고, 보폭은 작게 하고, 누구한테 소개받더라도 악수는 하지 마. 그건 예의가 아니거든.\"\n\n\"언니는 그 많은 예법을 다 어떻게 익혔어? 난 도저히 못 하겠어. 저 음악 참 경쾌하지 않니?\"\n\n두 사람은 아래층으로 내려갔는데, 좀처럼 무도회에 가 본 일이 없는 터라 약간 주눅이 들었고, 이 작은 모임이 비격식이라고는 해도 그들에게는 하나의 사건이었다. 위풍당당한 노부인인 가드너 부인이 다정하게 그들을 맞이하더니, 여섯 딸 가운데 맏이에게 넘겨주었다. 메그는 샐리와 아는 사이여서 금세 마음이 편안해졌다. 그러나 여자애들이나 여자애들의 수다 따위엔 별 관심이 없는 조는, 등을 조심스레 벽에 기댄 채 우두커니 서서, 꽃밭에 들어선 망아지처럼 어색하기 짝이 없는 기분이었다. 방 한쪽에서는 쾌활한 청년 대여섯이 스케이트 이야기를 나누고 있었는데, 스케이트는 그녀가 삶에서 누리는 즐거움 가운데 하나였기에 그쪽으로 가서 어울리고 싶어 좀이 쑤셨다. 그녀는 메그에게 그 바람을 눈짓으로 알렸지만, 눈썹이 어찌나 무섭게 치켜올라가는지 감히 꼼짝도 할 수 없었다. 아무도 말을 걸어오지 않았고, 가까이 있던 무리도 하나둘 흩어지더니 마침내 그녀 혼자만 덩그러니 남았다. 탄 자국이 드러날까 봐 이리저리 돌아다니며 혼자 즐길 수도 없었던 그녀는, 춤이 시작될 때까지 다소 처량하게 사람들을 바라보고만 있었다. 메그는 곧바로 춤 신청을 받았고, 꽉 끼는 슬리퍼를 신고도 어찌나 경쾌하게 발을 놀리는지, 그것을 신은 이가 웃으며 견디는 고통을 아무도 짐작하지 못했을 것이다. 조는 빨강머리의 덩치 큰 청년이 자기가 있는 구석으로 다가오는 것을 보고, 그가 춤을 청할까 봐 슬그머니 커튼이 드리워진 후미진 곳으로 숨어들었다. 거기서 조용히 내다보며 마음 편히 즐길 작정이었다. 그런데 공교롭게도 다른 수줍은 사람 하나가 똑같은 피난처를 골라 둔 터라, 커튼이 뒤로 내려오는 순간 그녀는 \"로런스 도련님\"과 정면으로 마주치고 말았다.\n\n\"어머, 누가 여기 있는 줄 몰랐어요!\" 조가 더듬거리며, 뛰어든 만큼이나 잽싸게 물러서려 했다.\n\n그러나 소년은 약간 놀란 기색이면서도 유쾌하게 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마세요. 괜찮으면 그냥 계세요.\"\n\n\"제가 방해되지 않을까요?\"\n\n\"전혀요. 저도 아는 사람이 별로 없어서 여기 온 거예요. 처음엔 좀 어색했거든요.\"\n\n\"저도 그랬어요. 더 좋으시다면 모를까, 부디 가지 마세요.\"\n\n소년은 다시 자리에 앉아 자기 무도화만 내려다보았고, 마침내 조가 예의 바르고 편안해 보이려 애쓰며 입을 열었다.\n\n\"전에 한 번 뵌 적이 있는 것 같은데요. 우리 옆에 사시죠?\"\n\n\"바로 옆집이에요.\" 그러면서 그가 고개를 들고 큰 소리로 웃었다. 고양이를 데려다주었을 때 둘이 크리켓 이야기를 나누던 일이 떠오르자, 조의 점잔 빼는 태도가 어지간히 우스웠던 것이다.\n\n그 웃음에 조도 마음이 편해져서 함께 웃으며, 더없이 진심 어린 투로 말했다.\n\n\"그 멋진 크리스마스 선물 덕분에 우리 정말 즐거웠어요.\"\n\n\"할아버지가 보내신 거예요.\"\n\n\"하지만 도련님이 할아버지께 그 생각을 심어 드린 거잖아요, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"고양이는 잘 있나요, 마치 양?\" 소년이 짐짓 점잖은 표정을 지으려 했지만, 그의 까만 눈동자는 장난기로 반짝였다.\n\n\"잘 있어요, 고마워요, 로런스 씨. 그런데 전 마치 양이 아니에요. 그냥 조예요.\" 아가씨가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"전 로런스 씨가 아니에요. 그냥 로리예요.\"\n\n\"로리 로런스라니, 참 이상한 이름이네요!\"\n\n\"원래 이름은 시어도어인데, 그게 싫어요. 애들이 절 도라라고 불렀거든요. 그래서 대신 로리라고 부르게 만들었죠.\"\n\n\"저도 제 이름이 싫어요. 너무 감상적이잖아요! 다들 조세핀 말고 조라고 불러 주면 좋겠어요. 애들이 도라라고 부르는 걸 어떻게 그만두게 했어요?\"\n\n\"두들겨 팼죠.\"\n\n\"난 마치 고모를 두들겨 팰 순 없으니, 그냥 참는 수밖에 없겠네요.\" 조가 한숨을 쉬며 체념했다.\n\n\"춤 추는 거 안 좋아해요, 조 양?\" 로리가, 그 이름이 그녀에게 잘 어울린다고 여기는 듯한 표정으로 물었다.\n\n\"공간이 넉넉하고 다들 활기차면 꽤 좋아해요. 그런데 이런 데서는 영락없이 뭔가를 엎거나, 남의 발을 밟거나, 끔찍한 사고를 치게 돼요. 그래서 말썽을 안 부리려고 가만히 있고, 메그 언니나 빙빙 돌게 두는 거죠. 도련님은 춤 안 춰요?\"\n\n\"가끔요. 저는 외국에서 여러 해 살아서, 여기 사람들이 어떻게 하는지 알 만큼 사교 모임에 자주 나가 본 적이 없거든요.\"\n\n\"외국에서요!\" 조가 외쳤다. \"어머, 얘기 좀 해 줘요! 난 사람들이 여행담 들려주는 거 정말 좋아해요.\"\n\n로리는 어디서부터 시작해야 할지 모르는 듯했지만, 조의 열띤 질문에 곧 입을 열었다. 그는 브베의 학교에 다녔던 이야기를 들려주었다. 그곳에서는 남학생들이 모자를 쓰는 법이 없었고, 호수에 보트 선단을 띄워 놓았으며, 휴일이면 선생님들과 함께 스위스 곳곳을 걸어서 여행하는 즐거움을 누렸다고 했다.\n\n\"나도 거기 가 봤더라면 얼마나 좋았을까!\" 조가 외쳤다. \"파리에도 갔어요?\"\n\n\"지난겨울을 거기서 보냈어요.\"\n\n\"프랑스어 할 줄 알아요?\"\n\n\"브베에서는 다른 말은 한마디도 못 쓰게 했는걸요.\"\n\n\"좀 해 봐요! 난 읽을 줄은 알아도 발음은 못 하거든요.\"\n\n\"Quel nom a cette jeune demoiselle en les pantoufles jolis?\" 로리가 너그럽게 말했다.\n\n\"발음 정말 잘하네요! 어디 보자, '저 예쁜 슬리퍼를 신은 아가씨는 누구인가요'라고 했죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"Oui, mademoiselle.\"\n\n\"우리 언니 마거릿이에요. 알면서 물어본 거죠! 언니가 예쁜 것 같아요?\"\n\n\"네. 정말 산뜻하고 차분해 보이고, 숙녀답게 춤추는 게 독일 아가씨들이 떠오르네요.\"\n\n언니를 향한 이 소년다운 칭찬에 조는 기쁨으로 환하게 달아올라, 메그에게 그대로 전해 줄 요량으로 그 말을 마음속에 차곡차곡 담아 두었다. 둘은 함께 내다보고, 품평하고, 수다를 떨다가 어느새 오랜 친구라도 된 듯한 기분이 들었다. 로리의 수줍음도 금세 가셨다. 조의 신사다운 태도가 그를 즐겁게 하고 마음을 편하게 해 주었기 때문이다. 조 역시 다시 명랑한 본래 모습으로 돌아왔는데, 드레스 걱정도 잊었고 아무도 그녀에게 눈썹을 치켜올리지 않았기 때문이다. 그녀는 \"로런스 도련님\"이 그 어느 때보다 마음에 들었고, 여동생들에게 그를 묘사해 줄 수 있도록 몇 번이나 찬찬히 뜯어보았다. 그들에겐 남자 형제도 없었고, 사내 사촌도 거의 없어서, 소년이란 거의 미지의 생물이나 다름없었던 것이다.\n\n'곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리에 까무잡잡한 피부, 크고 까만 눈, 잘생긴 코, 가지런한 치아, 작은 손발, 키는 나보다 크고, 소년치고는 무척 예의 바르고, 무엇보다 유쾌해. 나이가 몇 살일까?'\n\n조는 그 말이 입 밖으로 튀어나오려는 걸 가까스로 참고, 평소답지 않은 재치를 발휘해 빙 둘러 알아내려 했다.\n\n\"머지않아 대학에 가시겠죠? 한창 책에 코를 박고 끙끙대시는 거 봤어요. 아니, 열심히 공부하신다는 뜻이에요.\" 그러고는 자기도 모르게 튀어나온 끔찍한 \"끙끙대다\"라는 말에 조는 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n로리는 미소만 지을 뿐 별로 충격받은 기색 없이, 어깨를 으쓱하며 대답했다.\n\n\"앞으로 한두 해는 안 가요. 어쨌든 열일곱 살 전엔 안 갈 거예요.\"\n\n\"열다섯밖에 안 됐어요?\" 조가, 이미 열일곱쯤 됐으리라 짐작했던 그 키 큰 소년을 바라보며 물었다.\n\n\"다음 달이면 열여섯이에요.\"\n\n\"나도 대학에 갈 수 있다면 얼마나 좋을까! 도련님은 가는 게 별로 안 좋은가 봐요.\"\n\n\"질색이에요! 죽도록 공부만 하거나 멍청하게 노닥거리는 것뿐이거든요. 게다가 이 나라 애들이 노는 방식도 마음에 안 들어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 뭘 좋아해요?\"\n\n\"이탈리아에서 살면서, 내 방식대로 즐기는 거요.\"\n\n조는 그 '내 방식'이 뭔지 몹시 묻고 싶었지만, 그가 검은 눈썹을 찌푸리는 모습이 어쩐지 위협적으로 보여, 발로 박자를 맞추며 화제를 돌렸다. \"저거 정말 멋진 폴카네요! 가서 한번 춰 보지 그래요?\"\n\n\"같이 추신다면요.\" 그가 정중하게 살짝 고개를 숙이며 대답했다.\n\n\"난 못 춰요. 메그 언니한테 안 추겠다고 했거든요. 왜냐하면…….\" 거기서 조는 말을 멈추고, 털어놓을지 웃어넘길지 망설이는 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"왜냐하면 뭔데요?\" 로리가 궁금해하며 물었다.\n\n\"말 안 할 거죠?\"\n\n\"절대 안 해요!\"\n\"음, 난 난롯불 앞에 서 있는 나쁜 버릇이 있어서, 드레스를 자주 태워 먹어요. 이것도 그렇게 그을려 버렸죠. 그래서, 잘 꿰매긴 했어도 자국이 보여요. 메그 언니가 아무도 못 보게 가만히 있으라고 했고요. 웃고 싶으면 웃어도 돼요. 우습다는 거 나도 알아요.\"\n\n그러나 로리는 웃지 않았다. 그저 잠깐 고개를 숙였는데, 그 표정이 조로서는 알 수 없었다. 이윽고 그가 아주 다정하게 말했다.\n\n\"그런 건 신경 쓰지 마세요. 어떻게 하면 되는지 알려 줄게요. 저쪽에 긴 복도가 있는데, 거기서 신나게 춤추면 아무도 우리를 못 볼 거예요. 같이 가요, 네?\"\n\n조는 고맙다고 하고는 기꺼이 따라나섰는데, 짝꿍이 끼고 있는 고운 진주색 장갑을 보자 자기도 멀쩡한 장갑 두 짝이 있었으면 싶었다. 복도는 텅 비어 있었고, 둘은 멋진 폴카를 추었다. 로리는 춤 솜씨가 좋았고, 조에게 독일식 스텝을 가르쳐 주었는데, 휘돌고 튕기는 동작이 가득해서 조는 무척 신이 났다. 음악이 멈추자 둘은 계단에 앉아 숨을 골랐고, 로리가 하이델베르크의 학생 축제 이야기를 한창 들려주고 있을 때, 메그가 동생을 찾아 나타났다. 그녀가 손짓하자 조는 마지못해 따라 옆방으로 갔는데, 메그는 소파에 앉아 발을 부여잡은 채 핼쑥한 얼굴을 하고 있었다.\n\n\"발목을 삐었어. 그 멍청한 굽이 꺾이는 바람에 발이 심하게 접질렸어. 너무 아파서 거의 서 있질 못하겠어. 이러고 어떻게 집에 가야 할지 모르겠다.\" 그녀가 아파서 몸을 앞뒤로 흔들며 말했다.\n\n\"그 바보 같은 신발 때문에 언니 발 다칠 줄 알았어. 안됐다. 그렇지만 마차를 부르든가, 여기서 밤을 새우든가 하는 것 말고는 무슨 수가 있을지 모르겠어.\" 조가 그렇게 말하며 가엾은 발목을 살살 문질러 주었다.\n\n\"마차는 돈이 엄청나게 들어서 안 돼. 사실 부를 수나 있을지 모르겠어. 다들 자기 마차를 타고 왔을 테고, 마구간까지는 멀고, 심부름 보낼 사람도 없잖아.\"\n\"내가 갔다 올게.\"\n\n\"안 돼, 절대로! 아홉 시가 넘어서 밖이 칠흑같이 어두운걸. 그렇다고 여기 머물 수도 없어. 집이 손님으로 꽉 찼거든. 샐리네 묵는 여자애들도 있고. 한나가 올 때까지 쉬다가, 어떻게든 해 봐야지.\"\n\n\"로리한테 부탁할게. 걔가 갔다 올 거야.\" 그 생각이 떠오르자 조가 마음이 놓인 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"맙소사, 안 돼! 아무한테도 묻지도 말고 말하지도 마. 내 덧신이나 갖다주고, 이 슬리퍼는 우리 물건이랑 같이 챙겨 둬. 이제 춤은 더 못 추겠어. 그래도 저녁 식사가 끝나는 대로 한나가 오는지 잘 봐 뒀다가, 오는 즉시 나한테 알려 줘.\"\n\n\"지금 다들 저녁 먹으러 나가는걸. 난 언니랑 같이 있을래. 그게 더 좋아.\"\n\n\"아니야, 얘, 어서 가서 커피나 좀 갖다줘. 너무 지쳐서 꼼짝도 못 하겠어!\"\n\n그래서 메그는 덧신을 잘 감추고 비스듬히 기대앉았고, 조는 우당탕거리며 식당으로 향했다. 도자기 찬장으로 잘못 들어가 보고, 가드너 노인이 혼자 가벼운 요기를 하고 있는 방문을 열어 보고 나서야 겨우 식당을 찾아냈다. 그녀는 식탁으로 잽싸게 달려들어 커피를 손에 넣었지만, 곧바로 쏟아 버리는 바람에 드레스 앞쪽도 뒤쪽만큼이나 엉망으로 만들고 말았다.\n\n\"어머나, 난 어쩜 이렇게 덤벙거릴까!\" 조가 외치며, 메그의 장갑으로 드레스를 박박 문질러 그 한 짝마저 못 쓰게 만들었다.\n\n\"제가 도와드릴까요?\" 다정한 목소리가 들려왔다. 한 손엔 가득 찬 잔을, 다른 손엔 얼음 접시를 든 로리였다.\n\n\"메그 언니한테 뭐 좀 갖다주려던 참이었어요. 언니가 많이 지쳐서요. 그런데 누가 절 밀치는 바람에, 이 꼴이 됐지 뭐예요.\" 조가 얼룩진 치마에서 커피색으로 물든 장갑으로 침울하게 눈길을 옮기며 대답했다.\n\n\"저런! 마침 이걸 줄 사람을 찾고 있었어요. 제가 언니한테 갖다드려도 될까요?\"\n\n\"어머, 고마워요! 어디 있는지 알려 드릴게요. 제가 직접 갖다주겠다고는 못 하겠어요. 그랬다간 또 사고나 칠 게 뻔하거든요.\"\n\n조가 앞장섰다. 그리고 로리는 마치 숙녀 시중에 익숙한 사람처럼 작은 탁자를 끌어다 놓고, 조에게도 커피와 얼음을 두 번째로 가져다주며 어찌나 살뜰히 굴던지, 까다로운 메그조차 그를 \"착한 소년\"이라고 인정했다. 셋은 봉봉과 사탕 포장지에 적힌 글귀를 두고 즐거운 시간을 보냈고, 어슬렁어슬렁 끼어든 다른 젊은이 두세 명과 함께 \"버즈\" 놀이를 조용히 한창 하고 있을 때 한나가 나타났다. 메그는 발 아픈 것도 잊고 너무 급히 일어서는 바람에, 아파서 짧은 소리를 내며 조를 붙잡고 말았다.\n\n\"쉿! 아무 말도 하지 마.\" 그녀가 속삭이고는, 큰 소리로 덧붙였다. \"아무것도 아니야. 발을 살짝 접질렸을 뿐이야.\" 그러고는 절뚝이며 위층으로 올라가 채비를 했다.\n\n한나는 잔소리를 했고, 메그는 울었으며, 조는 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다. 결국 그녀는 일을 직접 처리하기로 마음먹었다. 슬그머니 빠져나가 아래층으로 달려간 그녀는, 하인을 발견하고 마차를 좀 구해 줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었다. 그런데 하필 그는 고용된 임시 웨이터라서 이 동네에 대해선 아무것도 몰랐다. 조가 도움을 청할 사람이 없나 두리번거리고 있는데, 그녀의 말을 들은 로리가 다가오더니 자기 할아버지의 마차를 내주겠다고 했다. 마침 자기를 데리러 막 도착한 참이라는 것이었다.\n\n\"아직 너무 이르잖아요! 설마 벌써 가려는 건 아니죠?\" 조가 마음이 놓이면서도 그 제안을 선뜻 받아들이기를 망설이며 입을 열었다.\n\n\"전 늘 일찍 가요. 정말이에요! 집까지 모셔다드리게 해 주세요. 어차피 가는 길인 데다, 비도 온다잖아요.\"\n\n그 말에 결정이 났다. 조는 메그가 다친 일을 그에게 알리고는 고맙게 그 제안을 받아들였고, 위층으로 달려가 나머지 일행을 데리고 내려왔다. 한나는 고양이만큼이나 비를 싫어하는 터라 군말 없이 따랐고, 일행은 호화로운 밀폐 마차에 올라 무척 들뜨고 우아한 기분으로 굴러갔다. 로리는 마부석에 올라탔고, 덕분에 메그는 발을 올려놓을 수 있었으며, 자매는 마음 편히 무도회 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n\"난 정말 재미있었어. 언니는?\" 조가 머리를 헝클어뜨리며 편안하게 자세를 고쳐 앉고 물었다.\n\n\"응, 다치기 전까지는. 샐리의 친구 애니 모팻이 나한테 마음에 들었나 봐. 샐리가 갈 때 자기 집에 와서 일주일 묵으라고 했어. 봄에 오페라가 올라올 때 간다는데, 엄마가 가게만 해 주신다면 정말 끝내주게 멋질 거야.\" 메그가 그 생각에 기운을 차리며 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 도망쳐 나온 그 빨강머리 남자랑 언니가 춤추는 거 봤어. 괜찮은 사람이었어?\"\n\n\"응, 아주! 빨강이 아니라 적갈색 머리야. 그리고 무척 예의 바르더라. 그 사람이랑 레도바를 황홀하게 췄어.\"\n\n\"새 스텝을 밟을 때 보니까 발작 일으킨 메뚜기 같던데. 로리랑 나랑 웃음을 참을 수가 없었어. 우리 웃는 소리 들었어?\"\n\n\"아니. 그래도 그건 무척 무례한 짓이야. 그렇게 거기 숨어서 그동안 도대체 뭘 한 거니?\"\n\n조가 자기 모험담을 들려주었고, 이야기를 마칠 즈음 마차는 집에 다다랐다. 둘은 거듭 고맙다고 인사하며 \"안녕히 가세요\"라고 한 뒤, 아무도 깨우지 않으려고 살금살금 안으로 들어갔다. 그러나 문이 삐걱대는 순간, 작은 잠자리 모자 두 개가 불쑥 솟아오르더니, 졸음에 겨우면서도 들뜬 목소리 둘이 외쳤다.\n\n\"무도회 얘기 해 줘! 무도회 얘기 해 달라고!\"\n\n메그가 \"예의범절이 영 모자란다\"고 한 행동이었지만, 조는 동생들 주려고 봉봉을 좀 챙겨 두었었다. 그래서 두 아이는 그날 저녁의 가장 짜릿한 사건들을 듣고 나서 곧 잠잠해졌다.\n\n\"정말이지, 무도회에서 마차 타고 집에 돌아와서, 시중드는 하녀까지 두고 실내복 차림으로 앉아 있으니, 진짜 우아한 아가씨가 된 기분이야.\" 조가 아르니카로 발을 싸매고 머리를 빗겨 주는 동안 메그가 말했다.\n\"우아한 아가씨들이라고 해서 우리보다 조금이라도 더 즐겁진 않을걸. 우리 머리가 그을리고, 드레스가 낡고, 장갑이 한 짝뿐이고, 멍청하게 신은 굽 높은 슬리퍼 때문에 발목을 삐긴 했어도 말이야.\"\n\n그리고 내 생각엔, 조의 말이 전적으로 옳았다.\n4.\n\n짐.\n\"아휴, 다시 짐을 짊어지고 길을 나서기란 어쩜 이렇게도 힘이 들까.\" 무도회 다음 날 아침, 메그가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. 이제 연휴가 끝났는데, 한 주 내내 즐기며 보낸 탓에 평소에도 좋아한 적 없는 일을 다시 수월하게 해 나갈 마음가짐이 도무지 되지 않았던 것이다.\n\n\"일 년 내내 크리스마스나 새해였으면 좋겠다. 그럼 얼마나 신날까?\" 조가 침울하게 하품하며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그럼 지금만큼 즐겁지도 않을걸. 그래도 자그마한 저녁 만찬에 꽃다발도 받고, 무도회에 가고, 마차 타고 돌아오고, 책 읽고 쉬면서 일은 안 한다는 게 참 좋긴 해. 다른 사람들처럼 사는 거잖아. 난 그런 걸 하는 여자애들이 늘 부럽더라. 호사스러운 게 어찌나 좋은지.\" 메그가 후줄근한 드레스 두 벌 가운데 어느 쪽이 덜 후줄근한지 가려내려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"뭐, 그렇게는 못 사니까 투덜대지 말고, 엄마처럼 짐을 둘러메고 명랑하게 터덜터덜 걸어가자고. 나한테 마치 고모는 영락없는 '바다의 노인'이야. 그래도 불평 없이 짊어지는 법을 익히고 나면, 알아서 떨어져 나가든가, 아니면 너무 가벼워져서 신경도 안 쓰이게 되겠지.\"\n\n이 생각이 조의 상상력을 간질여 기분을 띄워 주었다. 하지만 메그는 도무지 밝아지지 않았다. 버릇없는 아이 넷으로 이루어진 그녀의 짐이 그 어느 때보다 무겁게만 느껴졌던 것이다. 그녀는 평소처럼 파란 목 리본을 매고 머리를 가장 어울리게 매만져 예쁘게 꾸밀 마음조차 들지 않았다.\n\n\"심술쟁이 꼬맹이들 말고는 봐 줄 사람도 없고, 내가 예쁘든 말든 아무도 신경 안 쓰는데, 곱게 차려입은들 무슨 소용이야?\" 그녀가 서랍을 홱 닫으며 중얼거렸다. \"난 평생 죽도록 힘들게 일하면서, 어쩌다 한 번씩 자그마한 즐거움이나 누리다가, 늙고 못생기고 심술궂게 변해 가겠지. 가난한 데다, 다른 여자애들처럼 인생을 즐길 수도 없으니까. 정말 너무해!\"\n\n그래서 메그는 잔뜩 상처 입은 표정으로 아래층에 내려왔고, 아침 식사 시간 내내 영 기분이 좋지 않았다. 다들 어쩐지 짜증이 나서 투덜대고 싶은 기색이었다. 베스는 머리가 아파 소파에 누운 채 고양이와 새끼 고양이 세 마리로 마음을 달래려 했고, 에이미는 숙제도 못 했는데 덧신마저 못 찾아 안달이었으며, 조는 휘파람을 불어 대며 채비하느라 한바탕 요란을 떨었다. 마치 부인은 당장 부쳐야 할 편지를 마무리하느라 무척 바빴고, 한나는 늦게까지 깨어 있던 게 영 맞지 않아 잔뜩 부어 있었다.\n\n\"이렇게 심술궂은 집은 정말 어디에도 없을 거야!\" 조가 잉크병을 엎고, 부츠 끈을 양쪽 다 끊어 먹고, 자기 모자 위에 주저앉고 나서 발끈하며 외쳤다.\n\n\"이 집에서 제일 심술궂은 사람은 언니거든!\" 에이미가 죄다 틀린 계산을, 석판 위로 떨어진 눈물로 지워 가며 받아쳤다.\n\n\"베스, 이 끔찍한 고양이들 지하실에 안 가둬 두면 다 물에 빠뜨려 버릴 거야.\" 메그가 화를 내며 외쳤다. 새끼 고양이 한 마리가 등을 타고 기어 올라와 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 들러붙어 있는 걸 떼어 내려던 참이었다.\n\n조는 웃고, 메그는 잔소리하고, 베스는 애원하고, 에이미는 9 곱하기 12가 얼마였는지 기억나지 않아 울먹였다.\n\n\"얘들아, 얘들아, 잠깐만 좀 조용히 해 다오! 이걸 첫 우편에 꼭 부쳐야 하는데, 너희가 떠들어 대니 정신이 하나도 없구나.\" 마치 부인이 편지에서 세 번째로 망친 문장을 그어 지우며 외쳤다.\n\n잠시 잠잠해졌다가, 한나가 성큼성큼 들어와 따끈한 턴오버 두 개를 식탁에 올려놓고는 다시 성큼성큼 나가면서 그 정적이 깨졌다. 이 턴오버는 하나의 관례였다. 자매는 다른 게 없었으므로 그것을 \"토시\"라고 불렀는데, 추운 아침이면 따뜻한 파이가 손을 녹여 주어 큰 위안이 되었다. 한나는 아무리 바쁘거나 심사가 뒤틀려도 한 번도 그것 만드는 걸 잊지 않았다. 길은 멀고 황량한 데다, 가엾은 자매는 다른 점심거리도 없었고, 두 시 전에 집에 돌아오는 일이 좀처럼 없었기 때문이다.\n* 턴오버: 반죽을 반으로 접어 속을 넣고 구운 파이 : 옮긴이.\n\n\"고양이들 꼭 끌어안고, 두통도 나으렴, 베스. 안녕히 계세요, 엄마. 오늘 아침 우리가 영 못된 망나니들이었지만, 집에 올 때는 영락없는 천사가 되어 올게요. 자, 가자, 메그!\" 그렇게 조는 성큼성큼 걸어 나가면서, 순례자들이 마땅히 그래야 할 모습으로 길을 나서지는 못했다고 느꼈다.\n\n자매는 모퉁이를 돌기 전에 늘 뒤를 돌아보았다. 엄마가 언제나 창가에 서서 고개를 끄덕이고 미소 지으며 손을 흔들어 주었기 때문이다. 어쩐지 그것 없이는 하루를 견뎌 낼 수 없을 것만 같았다. 기분이 어떻든 간에, 마지막으로 흘끗 본 그 자애로운 얼굴은 어김없이 햇살처럼 그들의 마음에 와닿았다.\n\n\"엄마가 우리한테 손에 입맞춰 흔들어 주는 대신 주먹을 휘두른다 해도 우린 싸. 우리보다 더 배은망덕한 못난이들은 세상에 없을 테니까.\" 조가 외치며, 눈 덮인 길과 매서운 바람 속에서 회한 섞인 만족을 느꼈다.\n\n\"그런 끔찍한 말 좀 쓰지 마.\" 세상에 진력난 수녀처럼 자기 몸을 휘감은 베일 속 깊은 곳에서 메그가 말했다.\n\n\"난 뭔가 뜻이 담긴, 야무지고 힘찬 말이 좋단 말이야.\" 조가, 모자가 머리에서 폴짝 뛰어올라 아예 날아가 버릴 채비를 하는 걸 붙잡으며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"넌 너 좋을 대로 무슨 이름으로든 자처해. 그렇지만 나는 망나니도 아니고 못난이도 아니니까, 그렇게 불리고 싶지 않아.\"\n\n\"언니는 시들어 버린 가엾은 존재라서, 늘 호사의 무릎에만 앉아 있을 수 없다는 이유로 오늘 영 심술이 났구나. 가엾은 우리 언니, 내가 한밑천 잡을 때까지 기다리기만 해. 그럼 마차에, 아이스크림에, 굽 높은 슬리퍼에, 꽃다발에, 같이 춤출 빨강머리 소년들까지 실컷 누리게 해 줄게.\"\n\n\"넌 어쩜 그렇게 우스꽝스럽니, 조!\" 그러면서도 메그는 그 허튼소리에 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 자기도 모르게 기분이 한결 나아졌다.\n\n\"내가 그런 게 언니한텐 다행이지. 만일 나까지 언니처럼 풀 죽은 척, 침울한 척하고 다니면 우리 꼴이 어떻게 되겠어. 다행히도 난 늘 기운 낼 우스운 거리를 찾아낼 줄 안다고. 더는 투덜대지 말고, 집에 올 땐 명랑하게 와. 알겠지, 우리 언니.\"\n\n조는 헤어지며 언니의 어깨를 격려하듯 토닥였다. 그렇게 둘은 각자 다른 길로 들어서서, 저마다 따뜻한 턴오버를 꼭 끌어안고, 겨울 날씨와 고된 일과 즐거움을 갈망하는 젊은 날의 채워지지 않는 욕구에도 불구하고 명랑해지려 애썼다.\n\n마치 씨가 불운한 친구를 도우려다 재산을 잃었을 때, 두 맏딸은 적어도 제 앞가림은 스스로 하게 해 달라고 졸랐다. 활력과 근면, 자립심을 기르는 데에는 아무리 일찍 시작해도 이르지 않다고 믿었기에, 부모는 이를 허락했고, 둘은 온갖 장애에도 끝내 성공하고야 마는 진심 어린 의지를 품고 일에 뛰어들었다. 마거릿은 보모 겸 가정교사 자리를 얻었고, 박봉이나마 그것으로 부자가 된 기분이었다. 그녀가 말했듯 그녀는 \"호사스러운 걸 좋아\"했고, 그녀의 가장 큰 괴로움은 가난이었다. 그녀는 다른 자매들보다 그것을 견디기가 더 힘들었다. 집이 아름답고, 삶이 안락과 즐거움으로 가득하며, 그 어떤 결핍도 모르던 시절을 기억하고 있었기 때문이다. 시샘하거나 불만스러워하지 않으려 애썼지만, 어린 소녀가 예쁜 물건들과 화사한 친구들, 갖가지 교양과 행복한 삶을 갈망하는 것은 지극히 당연한 일이었다. 킹 씨네 집에서 그녀는 자기가 원하는 모든 것을 날마다 보았다. 그 집 아이들의 손위 언니들이 막 사교계에 나온 참이라, 메그는 우아한 무도회 드레스와 꽃다발을 심심찮게 흘끗거렸고, 극장이며 음악회며 썰매 모임이며 온갖 잔치판에 관한 활기찬 수다를 들었으며, 자기한테라면 그토록 귀했을 자질구레한 것들에 돈을 펑펑 쓰는 모습을 지켜보았다. 가엾은 메그는 좀처럼 불평하지 않았지만, 부당하다는 느낌이 때로 그녀를 모든 사람에게 모질게 만들기도 했다. 삶을 행복하게 해 주는 유일한 것, 곧 자신이 누리는 축복 안에서 그녀가 얼마나 부유한지를 아직 깨우치지 못했던 것이다.\n\n조는 마침 마치 고모와 잘 맞았다. 다리가 불편한 고모에게는 시중들어 줄 활동적인 사람이 필요했던 것이다. 자식이 없는 이 노부인은 집안에 어려움이 닥쳤을 때 딸 가운데 하나를 입양하겠다고 제의했었는데, 그 제의가 거절당하자 몹시 언짢아했다. 다른 친구들은 마치네 집안이 이 부유한 노부인의 유언장에 이름을 올릴 기회를 모조리 날려 버렸다고 했지만, 세속에 초연한 마치 가족은 이렇게만 말했다.\n\n\"열두 사람 몫의 재산을 준다 해도 우리 딸들을 내줄 순 없어요. 부유하든 가난하든, 우린 함께 모여 서로를 의지하며 행복할 겁니다.\"\n\n노부인은 한동안 그들에게 말을 걸지 않았지만, 어느 친구 집에서 우연히 조를 마주쳤을 때 그 우스꽝스러운 얼굴과 무뚝뚝한 태도의 무언가가 노부인의 마음에 들어, 조를 말동무로 데려가겠다고 제안했다. 조로서는 영 내키지 않았지만, 달리 더 나은 자리가 나타나지 않았으므로 받아들였고, 모두의 놀라움 속에 그 다혈질 친척과 놀랄 만큼 잘 지냈다. 이따금 폭풍이 일긴 했고, 한번은 조가 더는 못 견디겠다며 집으로 돌아와 버린 적도 있었다. 그러나 마치 고모는 늘 금세 마음이 개어, 거절할 수 없을 만큼 간곡히 사람을 보내 그녀를 다시 불러들였다. 사실 조는 마음속으로 그 깐깐한 노부인을 제법 좋아하는 편이었던 것이다.\n\n내가 짐작하기로 진짜 매력은 좋은 책들로 가득한 커다란 서재였다. 마치 큰아버지가 돌아가신 뒤로 먼지와 거미가 차지하도록 방치된 곳이었다. 조는 그 인자한 노신사를 기억하고 있었다. 큰아버지는 그녀가 커다란 사전들로 철도와 다리를 쌓게 해 주었고, 라틴어 책에 실린 기묘한 그림들에 얽힌 이야기를 들려주었으며, 길에서 마주칠 때마다 진저브레드를 사 주곤 했다. 키 큰 책장 위에서 흉상들이 내려다보는, 어둑하고 먼지 낀 그 방, 아늑한 의자들과 지구본들, 그리고 무엇보다도 마음껏 거닐 수 있는 책의 황야가, 조에게는 그 서재를 더없는 행복의 영토로 만들어 주었다. 마치 고모가 낮잠을 자거나 손님 접대로 바쁜 틈만 나면 조는 이 조용한 곳으로 달려가, 안락의자에 몸을 웅크리고는 영락없는 책벌레처럼 시며 소설이며 역사며 여행기며 그림책을 게걸스레 탐독했다. 그러나 모든 행복이 그렇듯, 그것도 오래가지는 못했다. 이야기의 한복판이나, 노래의 가장 감미로운 구절, 혹은 여행자의 가장 아슬아슬한 모험에 막 다다랐다 싶으면, 어김없이 \"조세-핀! 조세-핀!\" 하고 날카로운 목소리가 불러 댔고, 그녀는 자기 낙원을 떠나 실을 감거나, 푸들을 씻기거나, 벨샴의 수필을 몇 시간씩 내리 읽어 주어야 했다.\n\n조의 야망은 무언가 아주 멋진 일을 해내는 것이었다. 그게 무엇인지는 아직 떠오르지 않아 시간이 일러 줄 때까지 미뤄 두고 있었다. 그러는 동안 그녀의 가장 큰 고통은 마음껏 책을 읽고, 뛰어다니고, 말을 탈 수 없다는 사실이었다. 욱하는 성미와 날카로운 혀, 가만있지 못하는 기질 탓에 늘 사고에 휘말렸고, 그녀의 삶은 우스우면서도 애처로운 기복의 연속이었다. 하지만 마치 고모 집에서 받은 단련이야말로 그녀에게 꼭 필요한 것이었다. 그리고 스스로 생계를 위해 무언가 하고 있다는 생각이, 끊임없이 들려오는 \"조세-핀!\" 소리에도 불구하고 그녀를 행복하게 했다.\n\n베스는 너무 수줍어서 학교에 다니지 못했다. 시도는 해 보았지만 어찌나 괴로워하는지 결국 그만두게 했고, 베스는 아버지와 함께 집에서 공부했다. 아버지가 떠나고, 어머니마저 군인 구호회에 그 솜씨와 기력을 바치라는 부름을 받았을 때에도, 베스는 혼자서 성실히 공부를 이어 가며 할 수 있는 데까지 최선을 다했다. 베스는 살림꾼다운 자그마한 아이여서, 한나를 도와 일하는 사람들을 위해 집 안을 깔끔하고 안락하게 가꾸었으며, 사랑받는 것 말고는 어떤 보답도 바라지 않았다. 길고 조용한 나날을 보냈지만 외롭거나 빈둥거리지는 않았다. 그녀의 작은 세계는 상상 속의 친구들로 북적였고, 그녀는 천성이 바지런한 꿀벌이었으니까. 매일 아침 일으켜 옷을 입혀 주어야 할 인형이 여섯이나 되었는데, 베스는 여전히 어린아이였고 자기 애완물을 변함없이 아꼈던 것이다. 그 가운데 멀쩡하거나 예쁜 것은 하나도 없었다. 모두 베스가 거두어들이기 전까지는 버림받은 신세였다. 언니들은 이 우상들을 다 가지고 놀고 나면 베스에게 물려주었는데, 에이미가 낡거나 못생긴 것이라면 질색했기 때문이다. 바로 그 이유로 베스는 그것들을 더욱 살뜰히 보살폈고, 병든 인형들을 위한 병원까지 차렸다. 그 솜뭉치 같은 몸에 핀이 꽂히는 일도, 모진 말이나 매질이 가해지는 일도 결코 없었고, 아무리 흉측한 인형도 무관심에 마음 상하는 일이 없었다. 모두가 한결같은 애정 속에 먹이고 입혀지고, 보살핌받고 어루만져졌다. 인형 신세의 그 처량한 한 조각은 본래 조의 것이었는데, 파란만장한 삶을 살다가 넝마 자루에 폐물로 처박혀 있던 것을, 베스가 그 음울한 구빈원에서 구해 내어 자기 피난처로 데려왔다. 머리 윗부분이 없어서 베스는 깔끔한 작은 모자를 씌워 주었고, 두 팔과 두 다리도 다 떨어져 나가서 담요로 둘둘 감싸 그 결함을 가리고는, 가장 좋은 침대를 이 만성 환자에게 내주었다. 만일 누군가 그 인형에게 쏟는 정성을 알았다면, 비록 웃음을 터뜨리면서도 마음이 뭉클해졌으리라. 베스는 그 인형에게 꽃다발 조각을 가져다주고, 책을 읽어 주고, 외투 안에 숨겨 바깥바람을 쐬어 주었다. 자장가를 불러 주었고, 그 더러운 얼굴에 입맞추고 다정하게 속삭이지 않고는 결코 잠자리에 들지 않았다. \"잘 자렴, 가엾은 우리 아가.\"\n\n베스에게도 다른 자매들 못지않게 나름의 괴로움이 있었다. 그녀는 천사가 아니라 지극히 인간적인 어린 소녀였기에, 조의 말마따나 \"한바탕 작은 울음\"을 우는 일이 잦았다. 음악 수업을 받을 수도, 좋은 피아노를 가질 수도 없었기 때문이다. 음악을 그토록 애틋이 사랑하고, 그토록 열심히 배우려 했으며, 그 삐걱대는 낡은 악기로 그토록 끈기 있게 연습하니, 정말이지 누군가(마치 고모를 두고 하는 말은 아니다) 그녀를 도와주어야 마땅할 것 같았다. 그러나 아무도 돕지 않았고, 혼자 있을 때 음정도 안 맞는 누런 건반 위로 베스가 눈물을 훔치는 것을 본 사람도 없었다. 그녀는 일하면서 작은 종달새처럼 노래했고, 엄마와 자매들을 위해 연주하는 일에 결코 지치는 법이 없었으며, 날마다 스스로에게 희망에 차서 말했다. \"착하게 지내다 보면 언젠가 내 음악을 갖게 될 거야.\"\n\n세상에는 베스 같은 사람이 많다. 수줍고 조용하게, 필요해질 때까지 구석에 앉아, 남을 위해 어찌나 명랑하게 살아가는지, 난롯가의 작은 귀뚜라미가 우는 소리를 그치고, 그 다정하고 햇살 같은 존재가 사라져 뒤에 정적과 그늘만 남기기 전까지는 아무도 그 희생을 알아채지 못한다.\n\n누가 에이미에게 인생의 가장 큰 시련이 무엇이냐고 물었다면, 그녀는 곧장 이렇게 대답했을 것이다. \"제 코예요.\" 에이미가 갓난아기였을 때, 조가 실수로 그녀를 석탄 통에 떨어뜨린 적이 있었는데, 에이미는 그 낙상 때문에 코가 영영 망가졌다고 우겼다. 가엾은 \"페트레아\"의 코처럼 크거나 빨갛지는 않았다. 그저 좀 납작할 뿐이었고, 아무리 꼬집어 봐도 도무지 귀족적인 오뚝함을 얻을 수가 없었다. 본인 말고는 아무도 신경 쓰지 않았고, 코도 나름대로 자라느라 애쓰는 중이었지만, 에이미는 그리스식 코가 없다는 것을 뼈저리게 안타까워하며, 스스로를 위로하려고 잘생긴 코를 종이가 닳도록 그려 댔다. 언니들이 \"꼬마 라파엘로\"라 부르던 그녀는 그림에 확실한 재능이 있어서, 꽃을 베껴 그리거나 요정을 도안하거나 기묘한 솜씨로 이야기에 삽화를 그릴 때만큼 행복한 적이 없었다. 선생님들은 그녀가 셈은 안 하고 석판을 온통 동물로 뒤덮어 놓는다고 불평했다. 지도책의 빈 면은 지도를 베껴 그리는 데 쓰였고, 더없이 우스꽝스러운 풍자화들이 하필이면 곤란한 순간에 그녀의 책마다 팔랑팔랑 떨어져 나왔다. 그녀는 할 수 있는 데까지 수업을 따라갔고, 품행의 모범이 됨으로써 꾸지람을 피해 갔다. 성격이 좋고, 힘들이지 않고도 남을 즐겁게 하는 행복한 재주를 지닌 덕분에 그녀는 친구들에게 무척 인기가 많았다. 그녀의 자그마한 거드름과 우아한 태도는 큰 찬사를 받았고, 그녀의 교양도 그러했다. 그림 말고도 그녀는 곡을 열두 가지나 칠 줄 알았고, 코바늘뜨기를 할 줄 알았으며, 단어의 삼분의 이 넘게는 잘못 발음하지 않고 프랑스어를 읽을 줄 알았다. 그녀에게는 \"아빠가 부자였을 땐 우리도 이러저러했는데\"라고 구슬프게 말하는 버릇이 있었는데, 그게 무척 애처로웠다. 그리고 그녀가 쓰는 긴 단어들은 여자애들 사이에서 \"완벽하게 우아하다\"는 평을 들었다.\n\n에이미는 한창 버릇없어지기 좋은 길로 들어서고 있었다. 다들 그녀를 떠받들었고, 자그마한 허영과 이기심도 무럭무럭 자라났다. 다만 한 가지가 그 허영을 어지간히 꺾어 놓았으니, 바로 사촌의 옷을 물려 입어야 한다는 것이었다. 그런데 플로런스의 엄마는 안목이라곤 눈곱만큼도 없어서, 에이미는 파란 모자 대신 빨간 모자를 쓰고, 어울리지도 않는 드레스에, 몸에 맞지도 않는 요란한 앞치마를 둘러야 하는 게 여간 괴롭지 않았다. 죄다 질 좋고, 바느질도 잘됐고, 거의 입지도 않은 새것이었지만, 에이미의 예술가다운 눈에는 큰 고역이었다. 특히 이번 겨울이 그랬으니, 학교에 입고 갈 드레스가 노란 점이 박힌 칙칙한 보라색에, 장식이라곤 하나도 없는 것이었기 때문이다.\n\n\"내 유일한 위안은,\" 그녀가 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며 메그에게 말했다. \"엄마가 마리아 파크스 엄마처럼 내가 못되게 굴 때마다 드레스 단을 줄여 버리진 않는다는 거야. 언니, 그건 정말 끔찍해. 가끔 걔가 어찌나 못되게 구는지, 드레스가 무릎까지 올라가서 학교에도 못 와. 이런 '굴요글'을 생각하면, 난 납작한 코도, 노란 폭죽이 박힌 보라색 드레스도 견딜 만하다는 생각이 들어.\"\n\n메그는 에이미의 속내를 털어놓는 상대이자 잔소리꾼이었고, 정반대 것들이 끌리는 어떤 묘한 이끌림 때문인지, 조는 온화한 베스의 그런 상대였다. 수줍음 많은 그 아이는 오직 조에게만 속마음을 털어놓았다. 그리고 베스는, 덜렁대고 정신없는 그 언니에게, 자기도 모르는 새 집안의 그 누구보다 큰 영향을 미치고 있었다. 두 언니는 서로에게 무척 큰 존재였지만, 각자 동생 하나씩을 맡아 제 나름의 방식으로 보살폈다. 그들은 그것을 \"엄마 놀이\"라 불렀고, 작은 아씨들다운 모성 본능으로, 내다 버린 인형 대신 동생들을 그 자리에 앉혀 놓았다.\n\n\"누구 뭐 들려줄 얘기 없어? 오늘 하루가 어찌나 따분했는지, 무슨 재밋거리라도 없으면 죽을 것 같아.\" 그날 저녁 다 함께 바느질을 하며 앉아 있을 때 메그가 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 고모랑 좀 별난 일이 있었는데, 내가 한판 이겼거든. 그 얘기 해 줄게.\" 이야기 들려주는 걸 무척 좋아하는 조가 입을 열었다. \"내가 그 지긋지긋한 벨샴을 읽고 있었어. 늘 그렇듯이 단조롭게 웅얼웅얼 읽었지. 그래야 고모가 금방 졸거든. 그럼 난 좋은 책을 꺼내 들고, 고모가 깰 때까지 미친 듯이 읽는 거야. 그런데 진짜로 내가 졸려 버렸지 뭐야. 고모가 졸기도 전에 내가 어찌나 하품을 크게 했던지, 책을 통째로 한입에 삼킬 만큼 입을 그렇게 떡 벌리는 게 무슨 짓이냐고 물으시더라.\n\n\"'그럴 수 있다면 좋겠어요. 그래서 다 끝내 버리게요.' 내가 건방지지 않게 굴려고 애쓰면서 대답했어.\n\n\"그랬더니 고모가 내 죄목을 두고 한참 잔소리를 늘어놓고는, 잠깐 '깜빡 잠드실' 동안 앉아서 그걸 곰곰이 반성하라고 하시더라. 고모는 절대 금방 안 깨거든. 그래서 고모 모자가 머리 무거운 달리아처럼 까딱거리기 시작하자마자, 난 주머니에서 '웨이크필드의 목사'를 잽싸게 꺼내 들고, 한 눈은 책에, 한 눈은 고모에게 두고 정신없이 읽었어. 다들 물에 풍덩 빠지는 데까지 막 다다랐는데, 그만 깜빡하고 소리 내서 웃어 버렸지 뭐야. 고모가 깨더라. 그런데 한숨 자고 나니 한결 마음이 너그러워졌는지, 좀 읽어 보라면서, 훌륭하고 유익한 벨샴보다 어떤 시시한 글이 그렇게 좋다는 건지 보여 달라고 하셨어. 난 정성껏 읽어 드렸고, 고모도 마음에 들어 했어. 비록 이렇게밖에 말씀 안 하셨지만.\n\n\"'대체 무슨 얘긴지 하나도 모르겠구나. 도로 가서 처음부터 시작해라, 얘야.'\n\n\"그래서 처음으로 돌아가서, 프림로즈 가족을 최대한 재미나게 들려드렸지. 한번은 짓궂은 마음이 들어서, 한창 짜릿한 대목에서 멈추고는 얌전하게 여쭸어. '아무래도 고모님이 피곤하실 것 같아요. 이제 그만 읽을까요?'\n\n\"그러자 고모는 손에서 떨어뜨린 뜨개질감을 도로 집어 들고, 안경 너머로 날 매섭게 흘겨보더니, 그 무뚝뚝한 투로 말씀하셨어.\n\n\"'그 장이나 마저 읽고, 건방지게 굴지 마라, 아가씨.'\"\n\n\"좋다고 인정은 하셨어?\" 메그가 물었다.\n\n\"어머, 천만에! 그래도 그 낡은 벨샴은 좀 쉬게 두셨지. 그리고 오늘 오후에 내가 장갑 가지러 다시 들어갔더니, 고모가 그 '목사'에 어찌나 푹 빠져 계신지, 내가 곧 올 좋은 시간 생각에 신나서 복도에서 지그 춤을 추며 웃는 소리도 못 들으시더라. 마음만 먹으면 얼마나 즐겁게 사실 수 있을까. 돈이 많은데도 난 고모가 별로 안 부러워. 따지고 보면 부자들도 가난한 사람들 못지않게 근심이 많은 것 같거든.\" 조가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그러고 보니,\" 메그가 말했다. \"나도 들려줄 얘기가 있어. 조 얘기처럼 우습진 않지만, 집에 오면서 한참 생각한 거야. 오늘 킹 씨네 집에 갔더니 다들 허둥지둥 난리가 났더라. 아이 하나가 그러는데, 자기 맏오빠가 끔찍한 짓을 저질러서 아빠가 멀리 쫓아 보냈다는 거야. 킹 부인이 우는 소리하고 킹 씨가 큰 소리로 야단치는 소리가 들렸어. 그레이스랑 엘런은 내 앞을 지나갈 때, 눈이 얼마나 빨갛게 부었는지 내가 못 보게 얼굴을 돌리더라. 물론 난 아무것도 안 물었지. 그런데 걔네가 어찌나 안됐던지, 못된 짓을 저질러 집안을 망신시킬 거친 오빠가 나한텐 없는 게 차라리 다행이다 싶었어.\"\n\n\"학교에서 망신당하는 게, 나쁜 남자애들이 저지를 수 있는 그 어떤 짓보다 훨씬 더 견디기 힘든 것 같아.\" 에이미가 마치 인생의 깊은 경험이라도 한 사람처럼 고개를 절레절레 저으며 말했다. \"오늘 수지 퍼킨스가 예쁜 빨간 홍옥수 반지를 끼고 학교에 왔어. 난 그게 끔찍이도 갖고 싶어서, 온 마음을 다해 내가 걔였으면 했지 뭐야. 그런데 글쎄, 걔가 데이비스 선생님 그림을 그렸어. 어마어마하게 큰 코에다 곱사등으로, 입에서 '아가씨들, 내 눈은 너희를 지켜보고 있다!'라는 말이 풍선 같은 데 담겨 나오게 말이야. 다들 그걸 보며 웃고 있는데, 갑자기 선생님 눈이 정말로 우리한테 향한 거야. 선생님이 수지더러 석판을 가지고 나오라고 하셨어. 걔는 무서워서 굳어 버렸는데, 그래도 나갔지. 그런데, 선생님이 어떻게 하셨는지 알아? 걔 귀를, 귀를 잡고서, 어휴, 얼마나 끔찍한지 상상이나 해 봐! 낭독대로 끌고 가서, 다들 볼 수 있게 그 석판을 든 채로 거기 삼십 분이나 서 있게 했다니까.\"\n\n\"여자애들이 그 그림 보고 안 웃었어?\" 그 소동을 흥미진진하게 듣던 조가 물었다.\n\n\"웃긴! 한 명도 안 웃었어. 다들 쥐 죽은 듯이 가만히 앉아 있었어. 그리고 수지는 진짜 양동이로 들이부을 만큼 울었어. 분명히 그랬다니까. 그때는 걔가 하나도 안 부럽더라. 그런 일을 겪고 나니, 홍옥수 반지가 수백만 개 있어도 내가 행복해지진 않을 것 같았거든. 난 그렇게 괴롭게 망신을 당하면, 절대로, 절대로 다시는 일어서지 못했을 거야.\" 그러고서 에이미는, 미덕을 지녔다는 뿌듯한 자부심과 긴 단어 두 개를 한숨에 무사히 내뱉었다는 성취감 속에 다시 바느질을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"난 오늘 아침에 마음에 드는 걸 봤어. 점심때 얘기하려고 했는데 깜빡했지 뭐야.\" 베스가 이야기하면서 조의 뒤죽박죽 바구니를 정리하며 말했다. \"한나 줄 굴을 사러 갔는데, 로런스 씨가 생선 가게에 계셨어. 그런데 날 못 보셨어. 내가 통 뒤에 숨어 있었고, 그분은 생선 장수 커터 씨하고 한창 얘기 중이셨거든. 가난한 아주머니 한 분이 양동이랑 대걸레를 들고 들어와서는, 생선 한 토막만 주면 청소를 좀 해 드리겠다고 커터 씨한테 물었어. 아이들 먹일 저녁거리가 없는데, 하루 일거리도 못 구했다면서. 커터 씨는 바빠서 좀 퉁명스럽게 '안 돼요'라고 했지. 그래서 아주머니가 배고프고 속상한 얼굴로 돌아서 가려는데, 로런스 씨가 지팡이 굽은 끝으로 큼직한 생선 한 마리를 걸어 올리시더니 그걸 아주머니한테 내미셨어. 아주머니는 어찌나 기쁘고 놀랐는지, 그걸 덥석 품에 안고 몇 번이나 고맙다고 인사했어. 그분이 '어서 가서 요리하세요'라고 하셨고, 아주머니는 그렇게 좋아하며 서둘러 가셨어! 그분, 참 마음씨 곱지 않아? 아휴, 큼직하고 미끌거리는 생선을 끌어안고서, 로런스 씨의 천국 잠자리가 '펜안'하기를 바란다고 하는데, 정말 웃기더라.\"\n\n베스의 이야기에 한바탕 웃고 나서, 자매는 엄마에게도 하나 들려달라고 청했다. 마치 부인은 잠시 생각하더니 차분히 말문을 열었다.\n\n\"오늘 회관에서 파란 플란넬 재킷을 마름질하며 앉아 있는데, 아버지가 무척 걱정되더구나. 그이한테 무슨 일이라도 생긴다면 우리가 얼마나 외롭고 막막할까 하는 생각이 들었어. 현명한 짓은 아니었지. 그래도 자꾸 근심하고 있는데, 옷을 맞추러 한 노인이 들어왔단다. 그 사람이 내 옆에 앉기에 말을 걸었지. 가난하고 지치고 근심에 잠긴 얼굴이었거든.\n\n\"'아드님이 군대에 있나요?' 내가 물었어. 그이가 가져온 주문서는 내 앞으로 온 게 아니었으니까.\n\n\"'네, 부인. 넷이나 있었는데, 둘은 전사하고, 하나는 포로가 됐고, 지금은 워싱턴 병원에서 위독하게 앓고 있는 다른 아들한테 가는 길입니다.' 그이가 조용히 대답했어.\n\n\"'나라를 위해 정말 많은 일을 하셨군요, 선생님.' 내가 말했지. 이제 연민이 아니라 존경심이 들었거든.\n\n\"'제 도리만큼도 못 했는걸요, 부인. 쓸모만 있다면 저라도 갔겠지요. 그런데 그렇질 못하니, 아들들을 보냈습니다. 아낌없이 보냈지요.'\n\n\"그이가 어찌나 쾌활하게 말하고, 어찌나 진실해 보이고, 자기가 가진 전부를 내준 걸 그토록 기뻐하는 듯하던지, 나 자신이 부끄러워지더구나. 난 한 사람을 보내 놓고도 너무 많이 바쳤다고 여겼는데, 그이는 넷을 내주면서도 조금도 아까워하지 않았으니까. 내겐 위로해 줄 너희가 다 집에 있는데, 그이의 마지막 남은 아들은 어쩌면 작별 인사를 하려고 몇십 리 밖에서 기다리고 있을지도 몰랐지! 내가 누리는 축복들을 헤아리니 어찌나 부유하고 행복한 기분이 들던지, 그이에게 옷을 단단히 싸 주고, 돈도 조금 쥐여 주고, 깨우침을 준 것에 진심으로 고마워했단다.\"\n\n\"엄마, 이런 거 말고 또 다른 얘기 해 줘요. 이렇게 교훈이 담긴 걸로요. 진짜 있었던 일이고, 너무 설교 같지만 않으면, 나중에 두고두고 곱씹어 보는 게 좋거든요.\" 잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤 조가 말했다.\n\n마치 부인은 미소를 짓더니 곧바로 이야기를 시작했다. 여러 해 동안 이 작은 청중에게 이야기를 들려준 터라, 그들을 어떻게 즐겁게 해야 하는지 잘 알고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"옛날 옛적에, 네 자매가 있었단다. 먹고 마시고 입을 것이 넉넉했고, 갖가지 안락과 즐거움도 누렸으며, 다정한 친구들과 자기들을 끔찍이 사랑하는 부모도 있었지. 그런데도 그 애들은 만족할 줄 몰랐어.\" (여기서 듣고 있던 자매들은 슬그머니 서로를 흘끔거리고는 부지런히 바느질하기 시작했다.) \"이 자매들은 착해지고 싶어 했고, 훌륭한 결심도 숱하게 했단다. 하지만 그 결심을 잘 지키지 못했고, 자기들이 이미 얼마나 많은 걸 가졌는지, 또 실제로 얼마나 많은 즐거운 일을 할 수 있는지는 까맣게 잊은 채, 늘 '이것만 있으면 좋을 텐데'라거나 '저것만 할 수 있으면 좋을 텐데'라고 입버릇처럼 말했지. 그래서 어떻게 하면 행복해질 수 있을지, 무슨 주문을 외면 되겠는지 한 노파에게 물었더니, 노파가 이렇게 일러 주었단다. '불만스러울 때면, 네가 받은 축복들을 곰곰이 헤아려 보고, 감사하렴.'\" (여기서 조가 무슨 말이라도 하려는 듯 얼른 고개를 들었지만, 이야기가 아직 끝나지 않은 것을 보고 마음을 고쳐먹었다.)\n\n\"분별 있는 아이들이었던지라, 그 충고를 따라 보기로 했고, 곧 자기들이 얼마나 풍족한지 깨닫고는 놀랐단다. 한 아이는 돈이 부잣집에서 부끄러움과 슬픔을 막아 주지 못한다는 걸 알게 됐어. 또 한 아이는, 자기가 비록 가난해도, 안락을 누리지도 못하는 어떤 안달복달하고 병약한 노부인보다, 젊음과 건강과 쾌활함을 지닌 자기가 훨씬 더 행복하다는 걸 깨달았지. 또 한 아이는, 저녁 차리는 일을 거드는 게 아무리 싫어도, 그걸 구걸하러 다녀야 하는 건 그보다 더 힘들다는 걸 알았어. 그리고 넷째 아이는, 홍옥수 반지조차 바른 행실만큼 값지지는 않다는 걸 깨달았단다. 그래서 그 애들은 불평을 그치고, 이미 가진 축복들을 누리며, 그것이 더 늘어나기는커녕 아예 빼앗겨 버리는 일이 없도록 그 축복에 어울리는 사람이 되려고 애쓰기로 뜻을 모았지. 그리고 내가 믿기로, 그 애들은 노파의 충고를 따른 것을 결코 후회하거나 아쉬워하지 않았단다.\"\n\n\"엄마, 우리 이야기를 우리한테 되돌려서 로맨스 대신 설교를 들려주다니, 참 약으시네요!\" 메그가 외쳤다.\n\n\"난 그런 설교가 좋아. 아빠가 들려주시던 그런 거잖아.\" 베스가 조의 바늘방석에 바늘들을 가지런히 꽂으며 사려 깊게 말했다.\n\n\"난 다른 애들만큼 불평하지도 않거니와, 이제부터는 그 어느 때보다 더 조심할 거야. 수지가 망신당한 걸 보고 미리 경고를 받은 셈이니까.\" 에이미가 점잔을 빼며 말했다.\n\n\"우린 그 교훈이 필요했고, 잊지 말아야 해. 혹시 잊거든, '톰 아저씨'에 나오는 늙은 클로이가 그랬던 것처럼 우리한테 이렇게 말해 줘. '받은 은혜를 생각혀, 얘들아! 받은 은혜를 생각혀!'\" 조가 덧붙였다. 그 작은 설교를 다른 누구 못지않게 마음 깊이 새기면서도, 거기서 우스갯거리 한 조각을 끄집어내지 않고는 도저히 못 배기는 그녀였다.\n5.\n\n# BEING NEIGHBORLY.\n\n이웃과 친해지기.\n\"대체 또 뭘 하려는 거야, 조?\" 어느 눈 내리는 오후, 한 손엔 빗자루를, 다른 손엔 삽을 들고 고무장화에 낡은 외투와 두건 차림으로 복도를 쿵쿵 걸어오는 동생을 보고 메그가 물었다.\n\n\"운동하러 나가는 거야.\" 조가 눈을 짓궂게 반짝이며 대답했다.\n\n\"오늘 아침에 긴 산책을 두 번이나 했으면 충분할 텐데! 밖은 춥고 음산해. 나처럼 난롯가에서 따뜻하고 보송보송하게 있는 게 좋을 것 같은데.\" 메그가 몸을 부르르 떨며 말했다.\n\n\"난 충고 같은 거 안 들어! 하루 종일 가만있을 순 없고, 난 고양이가 아니니까 난롯가에서 꾸벅꾸벅 조는 것도 싫어. 난 모험이 좋고, 그래서 좀 찾으러 가는 거야.\"\n\n메그는 다시 발을 쬐며 '아이반호'를 읽으러 갔고, 조는 기운차게 길을 내기 시작했다. 눈이 가벼워서, 빗자루로 정원을 빙 둘러 길을 금세 쓸어 놓았다. 해가 나면 베스가 거기를 걸을 수 있도록, 또 병든 인형들에게도 바람을 쐬어 줘야 했으니까. 그런데 그 정원이 마치네 집과 로런스 씨네 집을 갈라놓고 있었다. 두 집 다 도시의 외곽에 있었는데, 그곳은 아직 시골 분위기가 남아 있어 작은 숲과 잔디밭, 너른 정원과 한적한 거리가 있었다. 낮은 산울타리가 두 저택을 나누었다. 한쪽에는 낡고 갈색을 띤 집이 서 있었는데, 여름이면 담을 뒤덮던 덩굴과 둘레를 에워싸던 꽃들을 빼앗긴 채 다소 휑하고 초라해 보였다. 다른 쪽에는 위풍당당한 석조 저택이 서 있어, 커다란 마차고와 잘 가꾼 부지에서부터 온실과 화려한 커튼 사이로 언뜻언뜻 비치는 어여쁜 것들에 이르기까지, 온갖 안락과 호사를 한눈에 드러냈다. 그런데도 어쩐지 외롭고 생기 없는 집 같았다. 잔디밭에서 뛰노는 아이도 없었고, 창가에 미소 짓는 자애로운 얼굴도 없었으며, 노신사와 그 손자 말고는 드나드는 사람도 거의 없었으니까.\n\n조의 발랄한 상상 속에서, 이 멋진 집은 아무도 즐기지 못하는 온갖 화려함과 즐거움으로 가득 찬 일종의 마법에 걸린 궁전 같았다. 그녀는 오래전부터 이 감추어진 영화를 두 눈으로 보고 싶었고, 시작할 방법만 안다면 알고 지내고 싶어 하는 듯한 그 \"로런스 도련님\"과 사귀고 싶었다. 무도회 이후로 그 마음은 더욱 간절해져서, 그와 친구가 될 온갖 방법을 궁리했다. 그런데 요사이 그가 통 보이지 않아, 멀리 떠난 게 아닐까 생각하던 차에, 어느 날 위층 창가에서 까무잡잡한 얼굴 하나가 베스와 에이미가 눈싸움을 하고 있는 자기네 정원을 그리운 듯 내려다보는 것을 발견했다.\n\n'저 애는 어울릴 사람과 재밋거리가 간절한 거야.' 그녀는 속으로 생각했다. '저 애 할아버지는 뭐가 저 애한테 좋은지도 모르고, 혼자 가둬만 두시는 거지. 같이 놀 유쾌한 남자애들이 한 무리 있거나, 아니면 누구든 젊고 활기찬 사람이 곁에 있어야 해. 정말이지 건너가서 그 노신사한테 그렇게 말씀드리고 싶다!'\n\n대담한 일을 즐기고, 그 별난 행동으로 늘 메그를 기겁하게 만드는 조에게 그 생각은 흥미로웠다. \"건너간다\"는 계획은 잊히지 않았고, 눈 내리는 오후가 찾아오자 조는 어디 한번 해 보기로 마음먹었다. 그녀는 로런스 씨가 마차를 타고 떠나는 것을 본 뒤 밖으로 나서서 산울타리까지 길을 냈고, 거기서 걸음을 멈추고 사방을 살폈다. 사방이 고요했다. 아래층 창문엔 커튼이 내려져 있고, 하인들도 보이지 않았으며, 사람이라곤 위층 창가에서 여윈 손에 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리를 괴고 있는 모습 말고는 보이지 않았다.\n\n'저기 있구나.' 조는 생각했다. '가엾어라! 이 음산한 날에 혼자 아파서 저러고 있다니. 너무하잖아! 눈덩이를 던져서 내다보게 한 다음, 다정하게 한마디 건네야겠어.'\n\n부드러운 눈 한 줌이 날아오르자 그 머리가 곧장 돌아섰고, 큰 눈이 환해지며 입가에 미소가 번지자, 그 얼굴에서 무기력한 기색이 순식간에 사라졌다. 조는 고개를 끄덕이며 웃고는 빗자루를 흔들며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"안녕하세요? 아파요?\"\n\n로리가 창문을 열더니 까마귀처럼 쉰 목소리로 깍깍거리듯 말했다.\n\n\"좀 나아졌어요, 고마워요. 독한 감기에 걸려서 일주일째 갇혀 있었어요.\"\n\n\"안됐네요. 뭐 하면서 시간 보내요?\"\n\n\"아무것도 안 해요. 여기 위는 무덤처럼 따분하다니까요.\"\n\n\"책은 안 읽어요?\"\n\n\"별로요. 못 읽게 해요.\"\n\n\"누가 읽어 줄 사람도 없어요?\"\n\n\"할아버지가 가끔 읽어 주세요. 그런데 제 책엔 통 흥미가 없으시고, 브룩 선생님한테 매번 부탁하기는 싫고요.\"\n\n\"그럼 누구라도 보러 오라고 해요.\"\n\n\"보고 싶은 사람이 없어요. 남자애들은 어찌나 시끄러운지, 제 머리가 약해서요.\"\n\n\"읽어 주고 즐겁게 해 줄 마음씨 좋은 여자애는 없어요? 여자애들은 조용하고, 간병 놀이도 좋아하는데.\"\n\n\"아는 애가 없어요.\"\n\n\"우리를 알잖아요.\" 조가 입을 열다가, 웃음을 터뜨리며 멈추었다.\n\n\"아, 그렇네요! 와 주실래요, 부탁이에요?\" 로리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"난 조용하지도, 마음씨 좋지도 않지만, 엄마가 허락하시면 갈게요. 가서 여쭤볼게요. 착하게 그 창문 닫고, 내가 올 때까지 기다려요.\"\n\n그렇게 말하고 조는 빗자루를 둘러메고 집 안으로 성큼성큼 들어가며, 식구들이 다들 뭐라고 할까 궁금해했다. 손님이 온다는 생각에 잔뜩 들뜬 로리는 채비를 하느라 부산을 떨었다. 마치 부인의 말마따나 그는 \"어린 신사\"였기에, 곱슬머리를 빗어 넘기고 깨끗한 깃을 갈아 달고, 방을 좀 치우려 애쓰며 다가올 손님에게 예를 갖추었다. 하인이 대여섯이나 있는데도 방은 깔끔함과는 거리가 멀었다. 이윽고 요란하게 초인종이 울리고, 이어 또렷한 목소리가 \"로리 도련님\"을 찾았으며, 어리둥절한 표정의 하인이 젊은 아가씨가 왔다고 알리러 뛰어 올라왔다.\n\n\"좋아, 올려보내요. 조 양이에요.\" 로리가 자기 작은 응접실 문 앞으로 가서 조를 맞이하며 말했다. 조는 한 손엔 뚜껑 덮인 접시를, 다른 손엔 베스의 새끼 고양이 세 마리를 안고 나타났는데, 발그레한 얼굴에 다정하고 무척 편안해 보였다.\n\n\"짐 보따리까지 다 챙겨서 왔어요.\" 그녀가 활기차게 말했다. \"엄마가 안부 전하시면서, 내가 뭐라도 도움이 되면 좋겠다고 하셨어요. 메그 언니는 자기가 만든 블랑망제를 좀 가져가라고 했고요. 솜씨가 아주 좋거든요. 그리고 베스는 자기 고양이들이 위로가 될 거라고 했어요. 도련님이 보고 웃을 줄 알았지만, 베스가 뭐라도 하고 싶어서 어찌나 안달인지 거절할 수가 없었어요.\"\n\n공교롭게도 베스의 우스운 빌림이 안성맞춤이었다. 새끼 고양이들을 보고 웃다 보니 로리는 수줍음을 잊고 단박에 붙임성 있게 굴기 시작했으니까.\n\n\"먹기 아까울 만큼 예쁜데요.\" 조가 접시 뚜껑을 열어, 푸른 잎사귀 화환과 에이미가 애지중지하는 제라늄의 진홍색 꽃에 둘러싸인 블랑망제를 보여 주자, 그가 기쁜 듯 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"별것 아니에요. 그저 다들 마음을 써서, 그걸 표현하고 싶어 했을 뿐이죠. 하녀더러 차 마실 때 드시게 치워 두라고 하세요. 워낙 부드러워서 그냥 먹어도 되고, 말랑말랑하니까 아픈 목을 안 다치고 술술 넘어갈 거예요. 방이 참 아늑하네요!\"\n\n\"잘 가꾸기만 하면 그럴 텐데, 하녀들이 게을러서요. 게다가 전 걔네를 어떻게 부려야 할지도 모르고요. 그래도 마음이 쓰이긴 해요.\"\n\n\"내가 이 분 안에 말끔히 정리해 줄게요. 난로만 이렇게 쓸어 주고, 벽난로 선반 위 물건들을 이렇게 가지런히 놓고, 책은 여기, 병은 저기 두고, 소파는 빛을 등지게 돌려놓고, 쿠션은 좀 통통하게 부풀려 주면 돼요. 자, 이제 다 됐어요.\"\n\n정말 그러했다. 조는 웃고 떠들면서 물건들을 휙휙 제자리에 놓아, 방에 사뭇 다른 분위기를 입혀 놓았던 것이다. 로리는 공손한 침묵 속에 그녀를 지켜보다가, 그녀가 소파로 오라고 손짓하자 만족스러운 한숨을 내쉬며 앉아 고맙게 말했다.\n\n\"정말 친절하시네요! 맞아요, 바로 이렇게 됐으면 했어요. 자, 이제 그 큰 의자에 앉으세요. 제가 손님을 즐겁게 해 드릴 수 있게요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 난 도련님을 즐겁게 해 주러 왔는걸요. 책 읽어 줄까요?\" 그러면서 조는 근처에 놓인 마음을 끄는 책들을 정겹게 바라보았다.\n\n\"고마워요. 그건 다 읽었어요. 괜찮으시면 그냥 얘기나 했으면 좋겠어요.\" 로리가 대답했다.\n\n\"그래요. 말문만 터 주면 난 하루 종일도 떠들 수 있어요. 베스 말로는, 난 도무지 멈출 줄을 모른대요.\"\n\n\"베스가 그 발그레한 얼굴에 집에 오래 있고, 가끔 작은 바구니를 들고 나가는 아이지?\" 로리가 관심을 보이며 물었다.\n\n\"맞아, 그 애가 베스야. 내 동생인데, 정말 착하기도 하지.\"\n\n\"예쁜 애가 메그고, 곱슬머리가 에이미인 것 같은데?\"\n\n\"그걸 어떻게 알았어?\"\n\n로리는 얼굴이 발그레해졌지만 솔직하게 대답했다. \"저, 그게 말이야, 너희가 서로 부르는 소리를 자주 듣거든. 그리고 여기 위에 혼자 있을 때면 너희 집을 자꾸 건너다보게 돼. 너희는 늘 그렇게 즐겁게 지내는 것 같아서. 이렇게 무례하게 군 거 미안해. 그런데 너희가 가끔 꽃이 놓인 창문에 커튼 내리는 걸 잊거든. 등불이 켜지면 난롯불이며, 엄마를 둘러싸고 식탁에 앉은 너희 모습이 꼭 그림을 보는 것 같아. 어머니 얼굴이 바로 맞은편에 있는데, 꽃들 뒤로 보이는 그 모습이 어찌나 다정한지 안 볼 수가 없어. 나는 엄마가 없잖아.\" 로리는 어쩔 수 없이 떨리는 입술을 감추려고 난로를 들쑤셨다.\n\n그 눈에 어린 외롭고 허기진 빛이 조의 따뜻한 마음을 곧장 파고들었다. 조는 워낙 단순하게 자라서 머릿속에 잔꾀라곤 없었고, 열다섯 살이지만 여느 아이처럼 천진하고 솔직했다. 로리는 병약하고 외로웠다. 조는 자신이 가정의 사랑과 행복을 얼마나 풍족하게 누리고 있는지 새삼 느끼며, 기꺼이 그것을 그와 나누고 싶었다. 조는 무척 다정한 얼굴로, 평소의 날카로운 목소리와는 사뭇 다른 부드러운 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"앞으로 그 커튼은 절대 안 내릴게. 그러니까 마음껏 봐도 좋아. 그런데 말이야, 그렇게 훔쳐보지 말고 우리 집에 놀러 오면 좋겠어. 우리 엄마는 정말 멋진 분이라서 너한테 큰 도움이 될 거야. 베스는 내가 부탁하면 너한테 노래도 불러 줄 거고, 에이미는 춤을 출 거야. 메그하고 나는 우리 우스꽝스러운 연극 소품들로 너를 웃겨 줄게. 우리 정말 재밌게 지낼 수 있어. 너희 할아버지가 허락 안 해 주실까?\"\n\n\"너희 어머니가 부탁하시면 허락하실 것 같아. 겉보기엔 안 그래 보여도 무척 인자하신 분이거든. 그리고 내가 하고 싶은 대로 거의 다 하게 해 주셔. 다만 내가 낯선 사람들한테 폐가 될까 봐 걱정하시는 거야.\" 로리가 점점 더 밝아진 얼굴로 말문을 열었다.\n\n\"우린 낯선 사람들이 아니라 이웃이야. 그리고 네가 폐가 될 거라고 생각할 필요도 없어. 우리는 너랑 친해지고 싶어. 사실 나도 오래전부터 그러려고 애썼는걸. 우리가 여기 온 지 그리 오래되진 않았지만, 너만 빼고 이웃들하곤 다 알고 지내거든.\"\n\n\"할아버지는 책 속에 파묻혀 사셔서 바깥일엔 별로 관심이 없으셔. 우리 가정교사인 브룩 선생님도 여기 안 머무시고. 그래서 같이 다닐 사람이 아무도 없어. 그냥 집에 있으면서 되는대로 지내는 거지.\"\n\n\"그건 안 좋아. 너도 좀 노력해서 초대받는 곳마다 다녀야 해. 그러면 친구도 많아지고 갈 만한 즐거운 곳도 생길 거야. 수줍어하는 건 걱정하지 마. 자꾸 다니다 보면 금세 사라질 테니까.\"\n\n로리는 다시 얼굴이 빨개졌지만, 수줍어한다는 말을 들었다고 기분 나빠하지는 않았다. 조에겐 호의가 가득해서, 그 무뚝뚝한 말들도 그 본뜻 그대로 다정하게 받아들이지 않을 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n\"학교는 마음에 들어?\" 잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 난로를 응시하던 소년이 화제를 바꾸며 물었다. 그동안 조는 흐뭇하게 주위를 둘러보고 있었다.\n\n\"난 학교 안 다녀. 직장인이거든. 아니, 직장 다니는 여자애지. 큰할머니 시중을 들어 드리러 다녀. 그런데 정말 다정하면서도 까칠한 할머니야.\" 조가 대답했다.\n\n로리는 또 다른 질문을 하려고 입을 열었지만, 남의 사정을 너무 꼬치꼬치 캐묻는 건 예의가 아니라는 게 마침 떠올라 다시 입을 다물고는 어색한 표정을 지었다. 조는 그의 예의 바른 태도가 마음에 들었고, 또 큰할머니 흉을 보는 것쯤은 개의치 않았기에, 그 안절부절못하는 할머니며 살찐 푸들이며 스페인어를 지껄이는 앵무새며, 자기가 마음껏 누비는 서재까지 신나게 들려주었다. 로리는 그 이야기를 무척 재미있어했다. 그리고 조가 큰할머니에게 청혼하러 한번 찾아온 점잖빼는 노신사 이야기며, 그가 멋들어진 연설을 하던 도중에 앵무새 폴이 그의 가발을 홱 잡아채 버려 그를 기겁하게 만들었다는 대목을 들려주자, 소년은 뒤로 벌렁 누워 눈물이 뺨을 타고 흘러내릴 만큼 웃어 댔고, 하녀가 무슨 일인가 싶어 빼꼼 고개를 들이밀 정도였다.\n\n\"아! 정말 속이 다 후련해진다. 더 해 줘, 부탁이야.\" 그는 소파 쿠션에서 얼굴을 떼며 말했는데, 그 얼굴은 유쾌함으로 발갛게 상기되어 반짝거렸다.\n\n자기 이야기의 성공에 한껏 우쭐해진 조는 정말로 \"이야기를 이어 나가\" 자매들의 연극이며 계획, 아버지에 대한 희망과 걱정, 그리고 자매들이 사는 작은 세계에서 일어나는 가장 흥미로운 사건들까지 죄다 들려주었다. 그러다 둘은 책 이야기로 접어들었는데, 조는 로리가 자기만큼이나 책을 좋아하고, 게다가 자기보다 더 많이 읽었다는 사실을 알고 기뻐했다.\n\n\"책을 그렇게 좋아하면, 내려와서 우리 책들 좀 봐. 할아버지 안 계시니까 무서워할 거 없어.\" 로리가 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"난 무서운 거 하나도 없어.\" 조가 고개를 홱 젖히며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"정말 그럴 것 같아!\" 소년이 그녀를 무척 감탄하는 눈길로 바라보며 소리쳤다. 비록 속으로는, 할아버지가 어떤 기분일 때 마주친다면 조도 좀 무서워할 만한 이유가 충분히 있을 거라고 생각했지만.\n\n집 안 전체가 여름처럼 따스했으므로, 로리는 이 방 저 방 앞장서서 안내하며 조가 마음 끌리는 것은 무엇이든 멈춰 서서 살펴보게 했다. 그렇게 해서 마침내 둘은 서재에 이르렀고, 조는 특별히 기쁠 때면 늘 그러듯 손뼉을 치며 깡충깡충 뛰었다. 서재는 책들로 빼곡했고, 그림과 조각상이 있었으며, 동전과 진기한 물건이 가득 든 정신 사나운 작은 진열장들과 푹 파묻히는 안락의자, 기묘한 탁자, 청동상들이 있었다. 무엇보다도 사방에 고풍스러운 타일이 둘러진 커다란 벽난로가 있었다.\n\n\"이렇게 풍족할 수가!\" 조는 벨벳 의자 깊숙이 파묻히며, 더없이 만족스러운 표정으로 주위를 둘러보면서 탄성을 질렀다. \"시어도어 로런스, 넌 세상에서 제일 행복한 소년일 거야.\" 그녀가 힘주어 덧붙였다.\n\n\"사람이 책만으로 살 순 없잖아.\" 로리가 맞은편 탁자에 걸터앉으며 고개를 저었다.\n\n그가 더 말을 잇기도 전에 종소리가 울렸고, 조는 놀라서 펄쩍 뛰며 외쳤다. \"어머나! 너희 할아버지셔!\"\n\n\"그러면 어때서? 넌 무서운 거 하나도 없다며.\" 소년이 짓궂은 표정으로 대꾸했다.\n\n\"좀 무서운 것 같긴 해. 그런데 왜 그래야 하는지 모르겠어. 엄마가 와도 된다고 하셨고, 너한테 해될 것도 없잖아.\" 조는 문에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 마음을 가다듬으며 말했다.\n\n\"오히려 나한텐 큰 도움이 됐는걸. 정말 고마워. 다만 나랑 얘기하느라 너무 지친 건 아닌지 걱정이야. 너무 즐거워서 도무지 멈출 수가 없었거든.\" 로리가 고마운 마음을 담아 말했다.\n\n\"의사 선생님이 오셨어요, 도련님.\" 하녀가 말하면서 손짓을 했다.\n\n\"잠깐 자리를 비워도 괜찮을까? 선생님을 봬야 할 것 같아서.\" 로리가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 신경 쓰지 마. 여기서 더없이 행복하니까.\" 조가 대답했다.\n\n로리가 나가자, 손님은 자기 나름대로 시간을 즐겼다. 그녀가 노신사의 멋진 초상화 앞에 서 있을 때 문이 다시 열렸고, 조는 돌아보지도 않은 채 단호하게 말했다. \"이제 확실해. 이분이 무섭지 않을 것 같아. 입매는 험상궂고 자기 고집이 어마어마하게 셀 것 같긴 하지만, 눈이 인자하시거든. 우리 할아버지만큼 잘생기진 않으셨어도, 난 이분이 마음에 들어.\"\n\n\"고맙구나, 아가씨.\" 등 뒤에서 굵직한 목소리가 들려왔다. 그리고 그녀가 기겁할 만하게도 거기, 늙은 로런스 씨가 서 있었다.\n\n가엾은 조는 더 이상 빨개질 수 없을 만큼 얼굴이 빨개졌고, 자기가 한 말을 떠올리자 심장이 불편할 만큼 빠르게 뛰기 시작했다. 잠시 도망치고 싶은 마음이 미친 듯이 솟구쳤다. 하지만 그건 비겁한 짓이고, 자매들이 비웃을 터였다. 그래서 그녀는 남아서 이 곤경을 어떻게든 헤쳐 나가기로 마음먹었다. 다시 보니 텁수룩한 잿빛 눈썹 아래 살아 있는 눈은 초상화 속 눈보다도 더 인자했다. 게다가 그 눈에는 장난기 어린 반짝임이 깃들어 있어 그녀의 두려움을 꽤나 누그러뜨렸다. 그 끔찍한 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 노신사가 어느 때보다 더 굵직한 목소리로 불쑥 말했다. \"그래, 내가 무섭지 않다고, 응?\"\n\n\"별로요, 할아버지.\"\n\n\"그리고 내가 네 할아버지만큼 잘생기지 않았다고?\"\n\n\"꼭 그렇진 않아요, 할아버지.\"\n\n\"내 고집이 어마어마하게 세다고, 그래?\"\n\n\"그럴 것 같다고만 했어요.\"\n\n\"그래도 그런데도 내가 마음에 든다고?\"\n\n\"네, 그래요, 할아버지.\"\n\n그 대답이 노신사를 흡족하게 했다. 그는 짤막하게 웃고는 그녀와 악수를 한 뒤, 손가락을 그녀의 턱밑에 대고 얼굴을 들어 올려 진지하게 살펴보더니 놓아주며 고개를 끄덕였다. \"얼굴은 안 닮았어도 네 할아버지의 기개는 물려받았구나. 네 할아버지는 정말 훌륭한 분이었단다, 얘야. 하지만 그보다 더 훌륭한 건, 용감하고 정직한 분이었다는 거지. 나는 그분의 친구라는 게 자랑스러웠단다.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 할아버지.\" 그 말을 듣고 나서 조는 마음이 아주 편안해졌다. 자기에게 딱 들어맞는 말이었기 때문이다.\n\n\"내 손자한테는 무슨 짓을 한 게냐, 응?\" 다음 질문이 날카롭게 날아왔다.\n\n\"그저 이웃답게 지내려고 한 것뿐이에요, 할아버지.\" 그러고서 조는 어떻게 해서 찾아오게 됐는지 들려주었다.\n\n\"그 애한테 기운을 좀 북돋워 줘야 한다고 생각하는 게냐, 그래?\"\n\n\"네, 할아버지. 그 애가 좀 외로워 보여서요. 또래 친구들이 도움이 될지도 모르잖아요. 우린 그저 여자애들일 뿐이지만, 도울 수 있다면 기꺼이 돕고 싶어요. 할아버지가 보내 주신 그 멋진 크리스마스 선물도 잊지 않고 있거든요.\" 조가 열심히 말했다.\n\n\"쯧, 쯧, 쯧! 그건 그 녀석이 한 일이었지. 그 가엾은 부인은 어떻게 지내느냐?\"\n\n\"잘 지내고 있어요, 할아버지.\" 그러고서 조는 후멜 가족 이야기를 신나게 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그녀의 어머니가 자기들보다 형편이 넉넉한 친구들을 그 가족에게 관심 갖도록 끌어들였던 것이다.\n\n\"꼭 제 애비가 선행을 베풀던 방식이로구나. 언제 날 좋은 날에 너희 어머니를 뵈러 가마. 그렇게 전해 드려라. 마침 차 마실 시간을 알리는 종이 울리는구나. 그 녀석 때문에 우린 차를 일찍 마신단다. 내려가서, 계속 이웃답게 지내려무나.\"\n\n\"저를 원하신다면요, 할아버지.\"\n\n\"원하지 않으면 청하지도 않았지.\" 그러고서 로런스 씨는 옛 방식의 정중함으로 그녀에게 팔을 내밀었다.\n\n'메그가 이걸 보면 뭐라고 할까?' 끌려가다시피 걸음을 옮기면서 조는 생각했다. 집에서 이 이야기를 들려주는 자기 모습을 그려 보자 그녀의 두 눈이 장난기로 반짝였다.\n\n\"이런! 아니, 저 녀석한테 도대체 무슨 일이 생긴 게냐?\" 로리가 계단을 뛰어 내려오다가, 자기가 그토록 어려워하는 할아버지와 조가 팔짱을 끼고 있는 놀라운 광경에 흠칫 멈춰 서자 노신사가 말했다.\n\n\"오신 줄 몰랐어요, 할아버지.\" 조가 그에게 의기양양한 눈길을 살짝 던지는 가운데 그가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"계단을 그렇게 요란하게 내려오는 걸 보면 알 만하다. 차 마시러 가자꾸나, 도련님. 그리고 신사답게 굴어라.\" 로런스 씨는 애정의 표시로 소년의 머리를 한번 헝클어뜨리고는 앞장서 걸어갔고, 그 사이 로리는 두 사람 등 뒤에서 우스꽝스러운 몸짓을 잇달아 해 보여 조가 하마터면 웃음을 터뜨릴 뻔하게 만들었다.\n\n노신사는 차를 넉 잔이나 마시는 동안 별말이 없었지만, 이내 오랜 친구처럼 재잘대는 두 젊은이를 지켜보았고, 손자에게 일어난 변화를 놓치지 않았다. 이제 소년의 얼굴에는 혈색과 빛과 생기가 돌았고, 몸짓에는 활기가, 웃음에는 진심에서 우러난 즐거움이 어려 있었다.\n\n'저 애 말이 맞아. 우리 손자가 외로웠던 게야. 이 어린 아가씨들이 그 녀석한테 뭘 해 줄 수 있을지 지켜봐야겠다.' 로런스 씨는 두 사람을 보고 들으며 생각했다. 그는 조가 마음에 들었다. 그 별나고 무뚝뚝한 태도가 자기와 잘 맞았던 것이다. 게다가 조는 마치 자기가 소년인 양 로리를 거의 완벽하게 이해하는 것 같았다.\n\n만약 로런스 집안이 조가 말하는 \"점잔 떨고 답답한\" 부류였다면 조는 영 어울리지 못했을 것이다. 그런 사람들 앞에선 늘 수줍고 어색해졌으니까. 하지만 그들이 격의 없고 편안하다는 걸 알자 조도 그렇게 굴었고, 좋은 인상을 남겼다. 모두 자리에서 일어났을 때 조는 가 봐야겠다고 했지만, 로리는 보여 줄 게 더 있다며 그녀를 데리고 온실로 갔다. 온실은 그녀를 위해 환히 밝혀져 있었다. 조에게는 그곳이 마치 동화 속처럼 보였다. 길을 오르락내리락하며 양옆으로 활짝 핀 꽃 벽이며 부드러운 빛, 촉촉하고 향긋한 공기, 머리 위에 드리운 신기한 덩굴과 나무들을 즐기는 동안, 새 친구는 두 손 가득 찰 때까지 가장 고운 꽃들을 잘랐다. 그러고는 그것들을 한데 묶으며, 조가 보기 좋아하는 행복한 표정으로 말했다. \"이거 너희 어머니께 드려. 그리고 보내 주신 약이 정말 마음에 든다고 전해 줘.\"\n\n두 사람은 로런스 씨가 커다란 응접실에서 난로 앞에 서 있는 걸 보았지만, 조의 시선은 온통 뚜껑이 열린 채 놓인 그랜드 피아노에 쏠려 있었다.\n\n\"너 칠 줄 알아?\" 조가 로리를 돌아보며 존경 어린 표정으로 물었다.\n\n\"가끔.\" 그가 겸손하게 대답했다.\n\n\"지금 좀 쳐 봐. 듣고 싶어. 그래야 베스한테 얘기해 줄 수 있거든.\"\n\n\"네가 먼저 치지 않을래?\"\n\n\"칠 줄 몰라. 배우기엔 너무 둔하거든. 그래도 음악은 정말 좋아해.\"\n\n그래서 로리가 연주했고, 조는 헬리오트로프와 티로즈에 코를 호사스럽게 파묻은 채 귀를 기울였다. \"로런스네 소년\"에 대한 그녀의 존경과 호감은 부쩍 커졌다. 그가 놀랄 만큼 연주를 잘하면서도 전혀 거드름을 피우지 않았기 때문이다. 베스도 이걸 들을 수 있다면 좋겠다 싶었지만, 그 말은 하지 않았다. 그저 그가 무척 멋쩍어할 만큼 칭찬을 늘어놓는 통에 할아버지가 구해 주러 나섰다. \"그만, 그만하면 됐다, 아가씨. 사탕을 너무 많이 주면 그 녀석한테 좋지 않아. 음악 솜씨가 나쁘진 않다만, 더 중요한 일들도 그만큼 잘해 줬으면 좋겠구나. 가려고? 그래, 정말 고마웠다. 또 와 주려무나. 너희 어머니께 안부 전해 다오. 잘 가거라, 의사 선생 조.\"\n\n그는 다정하게 악수했지만, 무언가 마음에 들지 않는 듯한 표정이었다. 현관에 이르자 조는 로리에게 자기가 무슨 잘못된 말을 했느냐고 물었다. 로리는 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"아니야, 나 때문이야. 할아버지는 내가 피아노 치는 걸 듣기 싫어하셔.\"\n\n\"왜?\"\n\n\"언젠가 얘기해 줄게. 내가 못 데려다주니까 존이 너랑 같이 갈 거야.\"\n\n\"그럴 필요 없어. 나는 아가씨도 아니고, 코앞이잖아. 몸조심해, 알았지?\"\n\n\"응. 그런데 또 와 줄 거지?\"\n\n\"네가 다 나은 뒤에 우리 집에 놀러 오겠다고 약속하면.\"\n\n\"갈게.\"\n\n\"잘 자, 로리!\"\n\n\"잘 자, 조, 잘 자!\"\n\n그날 오후의 모험담을 다 전해 듣고 나자, 식구들은 다 함께 그 집에 놀러 가고 싶은 마음이 일었다. 산울타리 건너편 그 큰 집에서 저마다 무척 끌리는 무언가를 찾아냈기 때문이다. 마치 부인은 자기 아버지를 잊지 않고 있는 그 노인과 아버지 이야기를 나누고 싶었고, 메그는 온실을 거닐고 싶어 안달이었으며, 베스는 그랜드 피아노를 생각하며 한숨을 쉬었고, 에이미는 멋진 그림과 조각상을 보고 싶어 했다.\n\n\"엄마, 로런스 씨는 왜 로리가 피아노 치는 걸 싫어하셨을까요?\" 캐묻기 좋아하는 성미의 조가 물었다.\n\n\"확실하진 않다만, 아마 그분 아들, 그러니까 로리의 아버지가 이탈리아 출신 음악가 여인과 결혼한 게 마음에 안 드셨던 모양이다. 워낙 자존심이 강한 분이시니까. 그 부인은 선하고 사랑스럽고 재능도 많았지만, 그분은 그 며느리가 마음에 들지 않으셨고, 아들이 결혼한 뒤로는 다시는 아들을 보지 않으셨단다. 로리가 어린아이였을 때 두 분 다 세상을 떠났고, 그래서 할아버지가 그 애를 데려오신 거야. 이탈리아에서 태어난 그 애가 그리 튼튼하진 못한 모양인데, 노인은 그 애마저 잃을까 두려워하시니 그렇게 애지중지하시는 거지. 로리가 음악을 좋아하는 건 어머니를 닮아 타고난 거란다. 짐작건대 할아버지는 그 애가 음악가가 되고 싶어 할까 봐 걱정하시는 것 같아. 어쨌든 그 애의 솜씨가 마음에 들지 않던 그 여인을 떠올리게 하니까, 조 말마따나 '얼굴을 찌푸리신' 거지.\"\n\n\"어머, 어쩜 그렇게 낭만적일까!\" 메그가 탄성을 질렀다.\n\n\"어쩜 그렇게 어리석을까!\" 조가 말했다. \"음악가가 되고 싶다면 되게 해 주면 되잖아. 가기도 싫어하는 애를 대학에 보내려고 들들 볶을 게 아니라.\"\n\n\"그래서 그 애가 그렇게 멋진 검은 눈에 고운 매너를 지녔나 봐. 이탈리아 사람들은 늘 멋지잖아.\" 약간 감상적인 데가 있는 메그가 말했다.\n\n\"언니가 그 애 눈하고 매너에 대해 뭘 안다고 그래? 말도 거의 안 해 봤으면서.\" 감상적이지 않은 조가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"파티에서 봤는걸. 게다가 네가 들려준 얘기를 봐도 그 애가 처신할 줄 안다는 게 드러나잖아. 엄마가 보낸 약 얘기, 그거 참 정겨운 인사말이었어.\"\n\n\"블랑망제를 두고 한 말이겠지, 뭐.\"\n\n\"어쩜 그렇게 둔하니, 얘야! 당연히 너를 두고 한 말이지.\"\n\n\"그랬어?\" 조는 그런 생각은 한 번도 해 본 적 없다는 듯 눈을 동그랗게 떴다.\n\n\"너 같은 애는 처음 본다! 칭찬을 받고도 그게 칭찬인 줄 모르다니.\" 메그가 그런 일이라면 죄다 안다는 듯 다 큰 아가씨 같은 태도로 말했다.\n\n\"난 그런 거 다 쓸데없는 소리라고 봐. 그러니까 언니도 괜히 호들갑 떨면서 내 재미를 망치지 말아 줬으면 좋겠어. 로리는 좋은 애고, 나도 그 애가 마음에 들어. 그렇지만 칭찬이니 뭐니 하는 그런 감상적인 소리는 사양이야. 우리 다 그 애한테 잘해 주자. 엄마가 없는 애잖아. 그리고 그 애가 우리 집에 놀러 와도 되죠, 그렇죠, 엄마?\"\n\n\"그럼, 조, 네 친구를 두 팔 벌려 환영하마. 그리고 메그도 아이들은 그럴 수 있는 동안엔 아이답게 지내야 한다는 걸 기억했으면 좋겠구나.\"\n\n\"전 제가 어린애라고 생각 안 해요. 아직 십 대도 안 됐고요.\" 에이미가 한마디 했다. \"넌 어떻게 생각해, 베스?\"\n\n\"난 우리 '천로역정' 생각을 하고 있었어.\" 한마디도 듣지 못한 베스가 대답했다. \"착해지기로 결심해서 우리가 어떻게 늪에서 빠져나와 좁은 문을 지났는지, 또 애를 써서 어떻게 가파른 언덕을 올라갔는지 말이야. 그리고 어쩌면 멋진 것들로 가득한 저 건너편 집이 우리의 아름다운 궁전이 될지도 모른다고.\"\n\n\"먼저 사자들부터 지나가야 해.\" 조가 그 일을 은근히 반기는 듯한 투로 말했다.\n\n# BETH FINDS THE PALACE BEAUTIFUL.\n\n큰 집은 정말 아름다운 궁전임이 드러났다. 모두가 그 안에 들어가기까지는 얼마간 시간이 걸렸고, 특히 베스는 사자들을 지나가기가 무척 힘겨웠지만 말이다. 늙은 로런스 씨가 가장 큰 사자였다. 하지만 그가 한번 찾아와 소녀들 하나하나에게 재미있거나 다정한 말을 건네고, 그들의 어머니와 옛이야기를 나눈 뒤로는, 겁 많은 베스를 빼고는 아무도 그를 그리 무서워하지 않게 되었다. 또 다른 사자는 자기들은 가난하고 로리는 부자라는 사실이었다. 이 때문에 보답할 수 없는 호의를 받기가 꺼려졌던 것이다. 하지만 얼마 지나지 않아, 그들은 오히려 로리가 자기들을 은인으로 여기고 있으며, 마치 부인의 어머니 같은 환대와 그들의 명랑한 어울림, 그리고 그가 그 소박한 집에서 느낀 위안에 얼마나 고마워하는지를 보여 주려고 무엇이든 다 해 주려 한다는 걸 알게 되었다. 그래서 그들은 곧 자존심을 잊고, 어느 쪽이 더 큰지 따지지도 않은 채 서로 친절을 주고받았다.\n\n그 무렵엔 온갖 즐거운 일이 일어났다. 새로 싹튼 우정이 봄날의 풀처럼 무성하게 자라났기 때문이다. 다들 로리를 좋아했고, 로리는 가정교사에게 \"마치 자매들은 정말이지 더할 나위 없이 멋진 아가씨들\"이라고 슬쩍 일러바쳤다. 젊은이다운 흐뭇한 열정으로 그들은 외로운 소년을 자기들 한가운데로 받아들여 무척 아껴 주었고, 로리는 그 순진한 소녀들과의 천진한 어울림에서 더없이 매력적인 무언가를 찾아냈다. 어머니도 누이도 가져 본 적이 없던 그는 그들이 자기 주위에 가져다주는 영향을 금세 느꼈다. 게다가 그들의 분주하고 활기찬 생활은 자기가 보내 온 게으른 삶을 부끄럽게 만들었다. 그는 책에 싫증이 났고, 이제는 사람들이 너무 흥미로워서 브룩 선생은 영 시원찮은 보고서를 올릴 수밖에 없었다. 로리가 늘 수업을 빼먹고 마치 집으로 달려갔기 때문이다.\n\n\"괜찮네. 휴일을 좀 갖게 두고, 나중에 만회하게 하지.\" 노신사가 말했다. \"옆집 그 착한 부인 말이, 그 녀석이 공부를 너무 무리하게 해서 또래들과 어울리고, 놀고, 운동을 할 필요가 있다는군. 내 생각에도 그 말이 맞는 것 같아. 그동안 내가 마치 그 애 할미라도 되는 양 그 녀석을 너무 싸고돌았던 게지. 행복하기만 하다면 하고 싶은 대로 하게 두세. 저 건너편 작은 수녀원 같은 집에서야 못된 짓에 빠질 리 없고, 또 마치 부인이 우리가 해 줄 수 있는 것보다 더 많은 걸 그 애한테 해 주고 있으니 말일세.\"\n\n정말이지 얼마나 즐거운 시절이었던가! 그런 연극과 활인화, 그런 썰매 타기와 신나는 스케이트 놀이, 낡은 응접실에서의 그토록 즐거운 저녁 시간, 그리고 이따금 큰 집에서 열리는 그런 흥겨운 작은 모임들. 메그는 마음 내킬 때면 언제든 온실을 거닐며 꽃다발 속에서 호사를 누렸고, 조는 새 서재를 게걸스럽게 누비며 자기 평론으로 노신사를 배꼽 잡게 했으며, 에이미는 그림을 모사하며 마음껏 아름다움을 즐겼고, 로리는 더없이 즐거운 모습으로 \"영주 노릇\"을 했다.\n\n하지만 베스는 그랜드 피아노를 그토록 그리워하면서도, 메그가 \"행복의 저택\"이라 부르는 그곳에 갈 용기를 좀처럼 내지 못했다. 한번은 조와 함께 갔지만, 노신사가 그녀의 약한 성정을 모른 채 짙은 눈썹 아래로 어찌나 빤히 쳐다보며 어찌나 큰 소리로 \"응!\" 하고 외쳤던지, 베스가 그 바람에 너무 겁에 질려 어머니에게 한 말로는 \"발이 마룻바닥에서 달가닥거렸다\"고 했다. 그녀는 달아나며 다시는 그곳에 가지 않겠다고, 그 사랑하는 피아노를 위해서라도 안 가겠다고 선언했다. 어떤 설득이나 꾐도 그녀의 두려움을 이기지 못했는데, 이 사실이 무슨 영문인지 로런스 씨의 귀에 들어가자 그가 일을 바로잡으려고 나섰다. 그는 짧게 들른 어느 날, 교묘하게 화제를 음악으로 끌고 가, 자기가 본 위대한 가수들이며 들어 본 훌륭한 오르간 이야기를 늘어놓고, 어찌나 매력적인 일화들을 들려주었던지, 베스는 도저히 저 멀리 구석에 머물러 있을 수가 없어 마치 홀린 듯 점점 더 가까이 다가왔다. 그러고는 그의 의자 등받이 뒤에서 멈춰 서서, 커다란 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 이 색다른 이야기의 흥분으로 뺨이 발그레해진 채 귀를 기울였다. 로런스 씨는 그녀가 마치 파리라도 되는 양 더 이상 아랑곳하지 않고, 로리의 수업과 선생들에 대해 이야기를 이어 갔다. 그러다 이제 막 떠오른 생각인 양 마치 부인에게 말했다.\n\n\"그 녀석이 요즘 음악을 소홀히 하는데, 나야 다행이다 싶소. 너무 빠져들고 있었으니 말이오. 그런데 피아노가 안 쓰여서 영 망가지고 있단 말이지. 댁의 따님들 가운데 누가 가끔 건너와서 한 번씩 쳐 주면 어떻겠소? 그저 음을 맞춰 두려고 말이오, 부인.\"\n\n베스는 한 걸음 앞으로 나서며 손뼉을 치지 않으려고 두 손을 꼭 맞잡았다. 도저히 뿌리칠 수 없는 유혹이었기 때문이다. 그 훌륭한 악기로 연습한다는 생각만으로도 숨이 멎을 것 같았다. 마치 부인이 미처 대답하기도 전에, 로런스 씨가 묘하게 고개를 끄덕이고 미소 지으며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"누구를 보거나 말을 걸 필요도 없소. 그저 아무 때나 들어와 치면 되오. 나는 집 저쪽 끝 서재에 틀어박혀 있고, 로리는 밖에 나가 있는 때가 많고, 하인들은 아홉 시가 지나면 응접실 근처엔 얼씬도 안 하니까.\"\n\n여기서 그가 가려는 듯 자리에서 일어나자, 베스는 말을 하기로 마음먹었다. 그 마지막 조건이 더할 나위 없었기 때문이다. \"내가 한 말을 따님들한테 전해 주시오. 만약 오고 싶지 않다면야, 뭐, 어쩔 수 없고.\"\n이때 작은 손 하나가 그의 손 안으로 슬며시 미끄러져 들어왔고, 베스는 고마움이 가득한 얼굴로 그를 올려다보며 진지하면서도 수줍은 투로 말했다.\n\n\"오, 할아버지, 따님들 정말, 정말 많이 가고 싶어 해요!\"\n\n\"네가 그 음악 좋아하는 아이냐?\" 그가 깜짝 놀랄 만한 \"응!\" 소리도 없이, 무척 인자하게 그녀를 내려다보며 물었다.\n\n\"전 베스예요. 음악을 정말 좋아해요. 아무도 제 소리를 안 듣고… 방해받지 않을 거라고 확실히 약속해 주신다면, 갈게요.\" 그녀는 무례할까 봐 걱정스러운 마음에 그렇게 덧붙였고, 말을 하면서 자신의 대담함에 스스로 떨었다.\n\n\"한 사람도 안 듣는단다, 얘야. 하루의 절반은 집이 텅 비어 있으니까. 그러니 와서 마음껏 쳐 다오. 그러면 내가 오히려 고맙겠구나.\"\n\n\"정말 친절하세요, 할아버지!\"\n\n베스는 그가 짓는 다정한 표정에 장미처럼 얼굴을 붉혔다. 하지만 이제는 무섭지 않았고, 그 큰 손을 고마운 마음으로 꼭 쥐었다. 그가 자기에게 준 그 소중한 선물에 고마움을 표할 말을 도무지 찾을 수 없었기 때문이다. 노신사는 그녀의 이마에서 머리카락을 부드럽게 쓸어 올리고는, 몸을 굽혀 입을 맞추며, 좀처럼 사람들이 들어 본 적 없는 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"내게도 한때 이런 눈을 가진 어린 딸이 있었단다. 하느님의 축복이 함께하기를, 얘야! 그럼 안녕히 계시오, 부인.\" 그러고서 그는 몹시 서두르며 떠났다.\n\n베스는 어머니와 함께 한껏 기쁨에 젖었다가, 자매들이 집에 없었기에 자기의 병약한 식구들에게 그 영광스러운 소식을 알리러 위층으로 달려갔다. 그날 저녁 그녀는 어찌나 명랑하게 노래를 불렀던지. 그리고 한밤중에 잠결에 에이미의 얼굴 위에서 피아노 치는 시늉을 하다가 에이미를 깨우는 바람에 모두가 그녀를 두고 한바탕 웃었다. 이튿날, 노신사와 젊은 신사가 둘 다 집을 나서는 걸 확인한 베스는, 두세 번 물러섰다 하더니, 마침내 옆문으로 들어가, 생쥐처럼 소리 없이 자기 우상이 서 있는 응접실로 향했다. 물론 순전히 우연이겠지만, 피아노 위에는 예쁘고 쉬운 악보가 놓여 있었다. 떨리는 손가락으로, 귀를 기울이고 주위를 살피느라 자꾸만 멈추면서, 베스는 마침내 그 훌륭한 악기에 손을 댔고, 그 순간 두려움도, 자기 자신도, 그 음악이 안겨 주는 형언할 수 없는 기쁨 말고는 모든 것을 잊어버렸다. 그것은 사랑하는 친구의 목소리 같았으니까.\n\n베스는 해나가 점심을 먹으러 데리러 올 때까지 거기 머물렀다. 하지만 입맛이 없어, 그저 앉아서 더없는 행복에 잠긴 채 모두를 향해 미소만 지을 뿐이었다.\n\n그 뒤로 그 작은 갈색 모자는 거의 날마다 산울타리 사이를 빠져나갔고, 커다란 응접실에는 보이지 않게 왔다 가는 가락의 정령이 깃들었다. 베스는 로런스 씨가 자기가 좋아하는 옛 가락을 들으려고 종종 서재 문을 열어 두는 줄 몰랐고, 로리가 하인들을 물리려고 현관에서 보초를 서는 것도 보지 못했으며, 악보꽂이에서 발견한 연습 교본과 새 노래책이 오로지 자기를 위해 거기 놓였다는 것도 짐작조차 못 했다. 그리고 그가 자기 집에서 음악 이야기를 들려줄 때면, 그저 자기에게 그토록 도움이 되는 이야기를 들려주시다니 참 친절하시다고만 생각했다. 그래서 베스는 진심으로 즐거워했고, 늘 그런 것만은 아닌 일, 곧 이루어진 소원이 바라던 그대로였다는 사실을 깨달았다. 어쩌면 이 축복에 그토록 감사했기에 더 큰 축복이 주어졌는지도 모른다. 어쨌든 그녀는 둘 다 받을 자격이 있었다.\n\n\"엄마, 저 로런스 할아버지께 슬리퍼 한 켤레를 떠 드리려고요. 저한테 너무 친절하셔서 고마운 마음을 전해야 하는데, 다른 방법을 모르겠어요. 그래도 될까요?\" 그 뜻깊은 방문이 있고 몇 주 뒤, 베스가 물었다.\n\n\"그럼, 얘야. 무척 기뻐하실 거고, 고마움을 전하는 좋은 방법이지. 언니들이 도와줄 거고, 마무리하는 데 드는 값은 내가 내주마.\" 마치 부인이 대답했다. 베스가 자기 자신을 위해선 좀처럼 무얼 부탁하는 법이 없었기에, 부인은 베스의 부탁을 들어주는 일에서 각별한 기쁨을 느꼈다.\n\n메그, 조와 여러 번 진지하게 의논한 끝에 무늬가 정해졌고, 재료를 사 왔으며, 슬리퍼 뜨기가 시작되었다. 좀 더 짙은 보라색 바탕에 진지하면서도 명랑한 팬지꽃을 모아 놓은 무늬가 무척 어울리고 예쁘다는 평을 받았다. 베스는 어려운 부분에서 가끔 도움을 받아 가며 아침저녁으로 부지런히 떴다. 손놀림이 야무진 작은 바느질꾼이라, 누구도 싫증이 나기 전에 슬리퍼가 완성되었다. 그런 다음 그녀는 아주 짧고 소박한 편지를 써서, 로리의 도움을 받아 어느 날 아침 노신사가 일어나기 전에 서재 탁자 위로 몰래 갖다 놓았다.\n\n이 흥분이 가라앉자, 베스는 무슨 일이 일어날지 기다렸다. 그날 하루가 다 가고 이튿날도 한나절이 지나도록 아무런 답례가 오지 않자, 그녀는 자기가 그 별난 친구의 기분을 상하게 한 건 아닌지 걱정되기 시작했다. 이튿날 오후, 그녀는 심부름도 하고 병약한 인형 조애나에게 매일의 산책도 시켜 줄 겸 밖으로 나갔다. 돌아오는 길에 거리를 올라오던 그녀는 응접실 창문으로 머리 세 개가, 아니 네 개가 들락날락하는 것을 보았다. 그들이 그녀를 보자마자 여러 손이 흔들리고 여러 명랑한 목소리가 외쳐 댔다.\n\n\"할아버지한테서 편지가 왔어! 빨리 와서 읽어 봐!\"\n\n\"오, 베스, 할아버지가 너한테 보내신 게—\" 에이미가 점잖지 못할 만큼 격하게 손짓하며 입을 열었다. 하지만 더 잇지 못했으니, 조가 창문을 쾅 닫아 그녀를 입막음했기 때문이다. 베스는 마음 졸이며 두근거리는 가슴으로 서둘러 들어갔다. 문 앞에서 언니들이 그녀를 붙잡아 의기양양한 행렬을 이루며 응접실로 데려갔는데, 다들 손가락질을 해 대며 동시에 외쳤다. \"저기 봐! 저기 봐!\" 베스는 정말로 보았고, 기쁨과 놀라움에 새파랗게 질렸다. 거기 작은 카비닛 피아노가 놓여 있었고, 그 윤기 나는 뚜껑 위에는 마치 간판처럼 \"엘리자베스 마치 양에게\"라고 받는 이가 적힌 편지 한 통이 놓여 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"내 거예요?\" 베스가 조에게 매달린 채 헐떡이며 말했다. 도무지 압도되는 일이라 금방이라도 쓰러질 것만 같았다.\n\n\"그럼, 다 네 거야, 우리 보물! 할아버지 정말 멋지시지 않니? 세상에서 제일 다정한 할아버지 같지 않아? 열쇠는 편지 안에 있어. 우린 안 뜯었어. 그래도 할아버지가 뭐라고 쓰셨는지 궁금해 죽겠어.\" 조가 동생을 끌어안으며 편지를 내밀었다.\n\n\"언니가 읽어 줘! 난 못 읽겠어, 너무 이상한 기분이야! 아, 너무 사랑스러워!\" 베스는 선물에 완전히 마음이 흔들려 조의 앞치마에 얼굴을 파묻었다.\n\n조가 종이를 펼치고는 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 맨 먼저 눈에 들어온 글귀가 이러했기 때문이다.\n\"마치 양에게:\n\n친애하는 부인께,—\"\n\n\"어쩜 그렇게 멋지게 들리니! 누가 나한테도 저렇게 편지를 써 줬으면!\" 그 옛날식 호칭이 무척 우아하다고 생각한 에이미가 말했다.\n\n\"'나는 평생 슬리퍼를 여러 켤레 가져 보았지만, 양의 것만큼 내게 잘 맞는 것은 없었소.'\" 조가 계속 읽어 나갔다.\n\"'팬지꽃은 내가 가장 좋아하는 꽃이라오. 이 슬리퍼는 언제나 다정한 선물의 임자를 떠올리게 해 줄 것이오. 나는 빚 갚기를 좋아하니, \"이 노인\"이 잃은 어린 손녀에게 한때 속해 있던 물건을 양에게 보내는 것을 허락해 주리라 믿소. 진심 어린 감사와 더없는 축복을 담아, 이만 줄이겠소.\n\n\"'양의 고마운 친구이자 보잘것없는 종,\n\n\"'제임스 로런스.'\"\n\"자, 베스, 이건 정말 자랑스러워할 만한 영광이야! 로런스 할아버지가 돌아가신 그 아이를 얼마나 아끼셨는지, 그 아이 물건을 하나하나 얼마나 소중히 간직해 오셨는지 로리가 얘기해 줬거든. 생각해 봐, 그 아이 피아노를 너한테 주신 거야. 큰 파란 눈을 가지고 음악을 사랑한 덕분이지.\" 조가 베스를 달래려고 말했다. 베스는 떨고 있었고, 이제껏 본 적 없을 만큼 흥분한 표정이었다.\n\n\"양초 꽂는 앙증맞은 받침대 좀 봐. 그리고 가운데 금빛 장미가 달린 채 자글자글 주름 잡힌 고운 초록색 비단이며, 예쁜 악보꽂이에 의자까지, 죄다 갖춰져 있어.\" 메그가 악기를 열어 그 아름다움을 펼쳐 보이며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"'양의 보잘것없는 종, 제임스 로런스'라니. 너한테 이렇게 써 주셨다고 생각해 봐. 학교 애들한테 얘기해야지. 다들 정말 멋지다고 할 거야.\" 그 편지에 무척 감동한 에이미가 말했다.\n\n\"한번 쳐 보렴, 아가. 우리 아기 피아노 소리 좀 들어 보자꾸나.\" 늘 식구들의 기쁨과 슬픔을 함께 나누는 해나가 말했다.\n\n그래서 베스가 쳐 보았고, 모두 입을 모아 이제껏 들어 본 것 중 가장 놀라운 피아노라고 했다. 새로 조율해서 더할 나위 없이 말끔하게 손봐 둔 게 분명했다. 하지만 그토록 완벽하긴 했어도, 그 진짜 매력은 베스가 사랑스럽게 그 아름다운 흑백 건반을 어루만지고 반짝이는 페달을 밟을 때, 그 위로 몸을 숙인 더없이 행복한 얼굴에 깃들어 있었다고 나는 생각한다.\n\n\"가서 고맙다고 인사드려야겠다.\" 조가 농담 삼아 말했다. 그 애가 정말로 갈 거라는 생각은 머릿속에 떠오르지도 않았던 것이다.\n\n\"응, 그럴 생각이야. 생각하다가 겁먹기 전에 지금 가는 게 좋겠어.\" 그러고는 모여 있던 식구들이 깜짝 놀라게도, 베스는 차분하게 정원을 가로질러 산울타리를 지나 로런스네 문 안으로 걸어 들어갔다.\n\n\"세상에, 내 평생 본 것 중에 제일 별난 일이 아니면 내가 죽어도 좋아! 피아노가 저 애 정신을 쏙 빼놨네! 제정신이라면 저렇게 들어갈 리가 없지.\" 해나가 그 뒷모습을 멍하니 바라보며 소리쳤고, 소녀들은 그 기적에 그만 말문이 막혔다.\n\n베스가 그 뒤에 한 일을 보았더라면 그들은 한층 더 놀랐을 것이다. 정말이지, 베스는 미처 생각할 틈도 주지 않고 서재 문을 두드렸다. 그리고 굵직한 목소리가 \"들어와요!\" 하고 외치자, 정말로 들어가, 사뭇 당황한 표정의 로런스 씨에게 곧장 다가가 손을 내밀며, 목소리에 약간의 떨림만 담아 말했다. \"고맙다는 인사를 드리러 왔어요, 할아버지, 그—\" 하지만 끝맺지 못했다. 그가 너무도 다정한 표정을 짓는 바람에 인사말을 잊어버렸고, 그저 그가 사랑하던 어린 딸을 잃었다는 사실만 떠올라, 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안고 입을 맞추었던 것이다.\n\n집 지붕이 갑자기 날아가 버렸다 해도 노신사가 이보다 더 놀라지는 못했을 것이다. 하지만 그는 그게 좋았다. 오, 정말이지 그렇고말고, 더없이 좋았다! 그 미더운 작은 입맞춤에 어찌나 감동하고 기뻤던지, 그의 무뚝뚝함이 죄다 사라져 버렸다. 그는 그녀를 무릎에 앉히고 주름진 뺨을 그녀의 발그레한 뺨에 갖다 댔는데, 마치 자기 어린 손녀를 다시 되찾은 듯한 기분이었다. 그 순간부터 베스는 그가 더 이상 무섭지 않았고, 마치 평생 알고 지낸 사이처럼 그와 정답게 이야기를 나누며 거기 앉아 있었다. 사랑은 두려움을 몰아내고, 감사는 자존심을 이길 수 있는 법이니까. 베스가 집으로 돌아갈 때, 그는 그녀의 집 대문까지 같이 걸어가 따뜻하게 악수하고는 모자에 손을 얹어 인사한 뒤 다시 돌아갔다. 무척이나 위엄 있고 꼿꼿한 모습이, 본래 그러했듯 잘생기고 군인 같은 노신사다웠다.\n\n소녀들이 그 광경을 보자, 조는 만족감을 표하느라 지그춤을 추기 시작했고, 에이미는 너무 놀라 하마터면 창밖으로 떨어질 뻔했으며, 메그는 두 손을 치켜들며 외쳤다. \"어머, 정말이지 세상이 끝나려나 봐!\"\n\n# AMY'S VALLEY OF HUMILIATION.\n\n\"저 애는 완전 키클롭스 같지 않아?\" 어느 날 로리가 말을 타고 채찍을 한번 휘두르며 요란하게 지나가자 에이미가 말했다.\n\n\"어떻게 그런 말을 하니, 두 눈이 멀쩡한데? 게다가 아주 잘생긴 눈이라고.\" 자기 친구를 깔보는 말이라면 발끈하는 조가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"난 그 애 눈에 대해선 아무 말도 안 했어. 내가 그 애 말 타는 모습에 감탄하는데 왜 언니가 발끈하는지 모르겠네.\"\n\n\"어머, 맙소사! 이 멍청한 애가 켄타우로스를 두고 키클롭스라고 했네.\" 조가 웃음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇게 무례하게 굴 거 없잖아. 데이비스 선생님 말마따나 그냥 '말 실순'일 뿐인데.\" 에이미가 라틴어를 들먹이며 조의 말문을 막고는 받아쳤다. \"로리가 저 말한테 쓰는 돈의 조금만이라도 나한테 있었으면 좋겠어.\" 그녀가 혼잣말처럼 덧붙였지만, 실은 언니들이 듣기를 바라는 눈치였다.\n\n\"왜?\" 메그가 다정하게 물었다. 조는 에이미의 두 번째 실수에 또 한바탕 웃음을 터뜨리고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"그게 너무 필요하거든. 빚이 어마어마한데, 헝겊 모은 돈 받을 차례가 돌아오려면 한 달이나 남았단 말이야.\"\n\n\"빚이라니, 에이미? 그게 무슨 소리니?\" 메그가 정색했다.\n\n\"있잖아, 절인 라임을 적어도 열두 개나 빚졌는데 갚을 수가 없어. 돈이 생기기 전엔 안 되거든. 엄마가 가게에서 외상으로 사는 건 절대 안 된다고 하셨잖아.\"\n\n\"전부 다 얘기해 봐. 요즘은 라임이 유행이니? 예전엔 고무 조각을 콕콕 찔러서 공 만드는 거였잖아.\" 메그는 에이미가 너무도 진지하고 거창한 표정을 짓는 통에 웃음을 참으려고 애썼다.\n\n\"있잖아, 애들이 늘 그걸 사거든. 그러니 인색하다는 소리 듣기 싫으면 너도 사야 해. 요즘은 죄다 라임뿐이야. 다들 수업 시간에 책상 밑에서 라임을 빨아 먹고, 쉬는 시간엔 연필이며 구슬 반지, 종이 인형 같은 걸로 바꾸거든. 어떤 애가 다른 애를 좋아하면 라임을 주고, 그 애한테 화가 났으면 그 애 앞에서 라임을 먹으면서 한 번 빨아 보라는 말도 안 해. 돌아가면서 대접하는데, 나도 그동안 엄청 많이 얻어먹고선 한 번도 갚질 못했어. 그런데 갚아야만 해. 그게 명예가 걸린 빚이거든.\"\n\n\"얼마면 그걸 다 갚고 네 신용을 되찾을 수 있겠니?\" 메그가 지갑을 꺼내며 물었다.\n\n\"25센트면 갚고도 남아서 언니 줄 거 사 먹을 돈도 몇 센트 남을 거야. 언니 라임 안 좋아해?\"\n\n\"별로. 내 몫은 네가 가지렴. 여기 돈 있다. 넉넉지 않으니까 최대한 아껴 써.\"\n\n\"어머, 고마워! 용돈이 있다는 건 정말 좋은 일인가 봐! 거하게 잔치를 벌여야지. 이번 주엔 라임 맛도 못 봤거든. 갚지를 못하니까 얻어먹기가 영 미안했어. 그래서 정말이지 하나가 간절했단 말이야.\"\n\n이튿날 에이미는 학교에 좀 늦었다. 하지만 그 축축한 갈색 종이 꾸러미를 책상 깊숙한 구석에 넣어 두기 전에, 봐줄 만한 자부심을 담아 한번 자랑하고 싶은 유혹을 떨치지 못했다. 이후 몇 분 사이에 에이미 마치가 맛있는 라임을 스물네 개나 손에 넣었고(오는 길에 하나 먹어 치웠다) 한턱낼 거라는 소문이 그녀의 \"패거리\" 사이에 쫙 퍼졌고, 친구들의 관심은 그야말로 쏟아져 들어왔다. 케이티 브라운은 그 자리에서 자기 다음 파티에 에이미를 초대했고, 메리 킹슬리는 쉬는 시간까지 자기 시계를 빌려주겠다고 우겼으며, 라임 없는 에이미의 처지를 비열하게 비아냥대던 빈정거리기 좋아하는 아가씨 제니 스노는 얼른 화해의 손길을 내밀며 끔찍한 산수 문제 몇 개의 답을 알려 주겠다고 했다. 하지만 에이미는 \"남의 라임 냄새를 맡을 만큼 콧대가 낮지 않은 사람들, 또 그걸 달라고 할 만큼 콧대가 높지 않은 잘난 척하는 사람들\"에 대한 스노 양의 가시 돋친 말을 잊지 않았다. 그래서 그녀는 \"갑자기 그렇게 친절한 척할 거 없어. 너한텐 하나도 안 줄 테니까\"라는 매서운 통첩으로 \"저 스노라는 애\"의 기대를 단번에 짓밟아 버렸다.\n\n그날 아침 마침 어떤 귀빈이 학교를 방문했는데, 에이미가 아름답게 그린 지도가 칭찬을 받았다. 자기 원수에게 돌아간 이 영광이 스노 양의 가슴에 사무쳤고, 마치 양은 공부 잘하는 어린 공작새 같은 거드름을 피우게 만들었다. 하지만, 아아, 아아! 교만은 패망의 선봉이라, 복수심에 불탄 스노가 처참한 성공을 거두며 형세를 뒤집어 놓았다. 손님이 으레 하는 진부한 인사치레를 마치고 절을 하며 물러가기가 무섭게, 제니는 중요한 질문을 하는 척하며 교사인 데이비스 선생에게 에이미 마치가 책상 속에 절인 라임을 가지고 있다고 일러바쳤다.\n\n당시 데이비스 선생은 라임을 금지 품목으로 선언하고, 그 규칙을 어기다 들킨 첫 번째 사람은 공개적으로 회초리를 치겠다고 엄숙히 맹세한 터였다. 이 인내심 많은 사내는 길고 격렬한 전쟁 끝에 껌을 몰아내는 데 성공했고, 압수한 소설과 신문을 모닥불에 태웠으며, 비밀 우체국을 폐쇄했고, 얼굴 찌푸리기, 별명 부르기, 풍자화 그리기를 금지했으며, 쉰 명에 달하는 반항적인 소녀들을 질서 잡으려고 한 사람이 할 수 있는 일은 죄다 했다. 사내아이들도 사람의 인내심을 시험하기에 충분하지만, 천만에! 여자아이들은 그보다 한없이 더하다. 특히 폭군 같은 성미에다, 블림버 박사만큼이나 가르치는 재능이라곤 없는 신경질적인 신사에게는. 데이비스 선생은 그리스어, 라틴어, 대수학, 온갖 학문에 막대한 지식을 갖춘지라 훌륭한 교사로 불렸다. 매너며 도덕, 감정, 모범 따위는 그다지 중요하게 여겨지지 않았다. 에이미를 고발하기에 그날은 더없이 불운한 순간이었고, 제니도 그걸 알고 있었다. 데이비스 선생은 그날 아침 분명 커피를 너무 진하게 마신 것 같았고, 늘 그의 신경통을 건드리는 동풍이 불었으며, 게다가 학생들은 그가 받아 마땅하다고 여기는 만큼의 명예를 안겨 주지 못했던 것이다. 그러니 어느 여학생의 표현, 그다지 우아하지는 않지만 실감 나는 말을 빌리자면, \"그는 마녀처럼 신경이 곤두서 있고 곰처럼 심술이 났다.\" \"라임\"이라는 말은 화약에 불을 댄 격이었다. 그의 누런 얼굴이 벌게졌고, 어찌나 세게 책상을 두드렸던지 제니가 평소답지 않은 잽싼 속도로 제자리로 뛰어가 앉을 정도였다.\n\n\"아가씨들, 주목하세요!\"\n\n그 엄한 명령에 웅성거림이 멎었고, 파랗고 검고 잿빛이고 갈색인 쉰 쌍의 눈이 순순히 그의 무시무시한 얼굴에 고정되었다.\n\n\"마치 양, 교탁으로 나오세요.\"\n\n에이미는 겉으로는 침착하게 따랐지만, 속으로는 두려움에 짓눌렸다. 라임이 양심을 무겁게 짓누르고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"책상 속에 있는 라임을 가지고 나오세요.\" 자리에서 미처 일어서기도 전에 그녀를 멈춰 세운 뜻밖의 명령이었다.\n\n\"전부 다 가져가진 마.\" 침착함이 대단한 아가씨인 옆자리 짝꿍이 속삭였다.\n\n에이미는 황급히 여섯 개를 털어 내고는 나머지를 데이비스 선생 앞에 내려놓았다. 사람의 마음을 가진 이라면 누구든 그 맛있는 향기가 코끝에 닿으면 누그러지리라 생각하면서. 그러나 안타깝게도 데이비스 선생은 그 유행하는 절임의 냄새를 유독 질색했고, 혐오감이 그의 분노에 더해졌다.\n\n\"그게 다인가요?\"\n\n\"꼭 다는 아니에요.\" 에이미가 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"당장 나머지를 가져오세요.\"\n\n자기 패거리를 절망적인 눈빛으로 한번 바라본 뒤, 그녀는 시키는 대로 했다.\n\n\"이제 정말 더 없는 게 확실하죠?\"\n\n\"전 거짓말 안 해요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"그래 보이는군요. 자, 이 역겨운 것들을 두 개씩 들고 창밖으로 던져 버리세요.\"\n\n마지막 희망이 사라지고 한턱이 그 간절한 입술에서 빼앗기는 순간, 일제히 새어 나온 한숨이 자그마한 돌풍을 일으킬 정도였다. 수치심과 분노로 새빨개진 에이미는 끔찍하게도 여섯 번을 오갔다. 그리고 통통하고 즙이 가득해 보이는 그 한 쌍 한 쌍의 가엾은 라임이 차마 놓고 싶지 않은 그녀의 손에서 떨어질 때마다, 거리에서 들려오는 환호성이 소녀들의 고통을 극에 달하게 했다. 그것은 자기들의 철천지원수인 동네 아일랜드 꼬마들이 자기들의 잔칫거리를 두고 환호하고 있다는 걸 알려 주었기 때문이다. 이건—이건 너무 심했다. 모두가 그 냉정한 데이비스 선생에게 분노 어린, 혹은 애원하는 눈빛을 번득였고, 라임을 끔찍이 좋아하는 한 아이는 그만 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n에이미가 마지막 걸음을 마치고 돌아오자, 데이비스 선생은 불길하게 \"흠!\" 하더니, 더없이 위엄 있는 태도로 말했다.\n\n\"아가씨들, 일주일 전에 내가 한 말을 기억할 겁니다. 이런 일이 벌어져 유감이지만, 나는 내 규칙이 어겨지는 걸 결코 용납하지 않으며, 결코 내 말을 어기지 않습니다. 마치 양, 손을 내미세요.\"\n\n에이미는 움찔하며 두 손을 등 뒤로 감추고는, 차마 입 밖에 내지 못한 말을 대신해 자기를 변호해 주는 애원의 눈빛을 그에게 보냈다. 그녀는 물론 다들 그렇게 부르듯 \"데이비스 영감\"에게 제법 귀여움을 받는 편이었다. 그래서 내 짐작으로는, 어쩔 줄 모르는 한 아가씨의 분노가 쉿 소리로 터져 나오지만 않았더라면 그가 정말로 자기 말을 어겼을지도 모른다. 그 쉿 소리는 비록 희미했지만, 성마른 신사의 신경을 거슬렀고, 죄인의 운명을 결정지어 버렸다.\n\n\"손을 내미세요, 마치 양!\" 그녀의 말 없는 호소에 돌아온 대답은 그것뿐이었다. 그러자 에이미는 울거나 빌기엔 너무 자존심이 강해서, 이를 악물고 고개를 도전적으로 젖힌 채, 자그마한 손바닥에 떨어지는 따끔한 매를 몇 대 움찔하지도 않고 견뎌 냈다. 매는 많지도 무겁지도 않았지만, 그녀에겐 그게 아무 상관 없었다. 태어나서 처음으로 매를 맞은 것이었고, 그 치욕은 그녀가 보기엔 그가 자기를 때려눕힌 것이나 다름없을 만큼 깊었다.\n\n\"쉬는 시간까지 교단 위에 서 있도록 하세요.\" 데이비스 선생이 말했다. 일단 시작한 김에 철저히 해치우기로 작정한 것이다.\n\n그건 끔찍한 일이었다. 자기 자리로 돌아가 친구들의 가엾어하는 얼굴이나 몇 안 되는 적들의 고소해하는 얼굴을 보는 것만으로도 충분히 괴로웠겠지만, 그 갓 당한 수치심을 안은 채 학교 전체와 마주한다는 건 도무지 불가능해 보였다. 한순간 그녀는 선 자리에 그대로 주저앉아 엉엉 울며 가슴이 미어질 것만 같았다. 부당함에 대한 쓰라린 감정과 제니 스노에 대한 생각이 그걸 견디게 해 주었다. 그녀는 그 치욕스러운 자리에 서서, 이제 얼굴들의 바다처럼 보이는 그 위로 난로 연통에 눈을 고정한 채, 어찌나 미동도 없이 새하얗게 서 있었던지, 소녀들은 그 애처로운 모습을 눈앞에 두고 공부하기가 무척 힘들었다.\n\n뒤이은 십오 분 동안, 자존심 세고 예민한 그 소녀는 결코 잊지 못할 수치와 고통을 겪었다. 남들에겐 우습거나 사소한 일로 보였을지 모르나, 그녀에겐 혹독한 경험이었다. 열두 해를 사는 동안 그녀는 오로지 사랑으로만 다스려졌고, 그런 매질은 한 번도 닿아 본 적이 없었기 때문이다. 손바닥의 따가움도 가슴의 아픔도, 한 가지 생각이 안기는 쓰라림 앞에선 잊혔다.\n\n'집에 가서 얘기해야 할 텐데, 다들 나한테 너무 실망하실 거야!'\n\n십오 분이 한 시간 같았다. 하지만 마침내 끝이 났고, \"쉬는 시간!\"이라는 말이 그녀에게 그토록 반갑게 들린 적은 없었다.\n\n\"가도 좋아요, 마치 양.\" 데이비스 선생이 스스로 느끼기에도 거북한 표정으로 말했다.\n\n그가 두고두고 잊지 못한 것은, 에이미가 누구에게도 한마디 없이 곧장 대기실로 들어가 자기 물건을 챙긴 뒤 스스로에게 격하게 선언하듯 \"영영\" 그곳을 떠나며 그에게 던진 그 원망 어린 눈빛이었다. 집에 도착했을 때 그녀는 처량한 상태였다. 그리고 얼마 뒤 언니들이 돌아오자 곧바로 성토 대회가 열렸다. 마치 부인은 별말은 하지 않았지만 심란한 표정으로, 상처받은 어린 딸을 더없이 다정하게 위로해 주었다. 메그는 모욕당한 손을 글리세린과 눈물로 씻겨 주었고, 베스는 이런 슬픔에는 자기가 그토록 사랑하는 새끼 고양이들조차 위안이 못 되리라 느꼈으며, 조는 분개해서 데이비스 선생을 당장 잡아들여야 한다고 했고, 해나는 그 \"악당\"을 향해 주먹을 흔들며 마치 그를 절구통 아래 넣어 둔 양 저녁 식사에 쓸 감자를 짓이겼다.\n\n에이미가 도망쳐 나온 걸 알아챈 사람은 동무들밖에 없었다. 하지만 눈썰미 좋은 아가씨들은 오후 내내 데이비스 선생이 사뭇 인자해진 데다 평소답지 않게 신경이 곤두서 있다는 걸 알아챘다. 학교가 파하기 직전, 조가 험상궂은 표정을 한 채 나타나 성큼성큼 교탁으로 걸어가더니 어머니의 편지를 건넸다. 그러고는 에이미의 물건을 챙겨, 마치 그곳의 먼지를 발에서 털어 내듯 문 앞 매트에 부츠의 진흙을 정성껏 닦아 내고는 떠났다.\n\n\"그래, 학교는 좀 쉬어도 된다. 하지만 베스랑 날마다 조금씩 공부는 해야 해.\" 그날 저녁 마치 부인이 말했다. \"나는 체벌에 찬성하지 않아. 특히 여자아이한테는 말이다. 데이비스 선생의 가르치는 방식도 마음에 안 들고, 네가 어울리는 아이들도 너한테 별 도움이 되는 것 같지 않구나. 그러니 다른 데 보내기 전에 네 아버지의 조언을 구해 봐야겠다.\"\n\n\"잘됐어요! 애들이 다 그만둬서 그 영감 학교를 망쳐 버렸으면 좋겠어요. 그 사랑스러운 라임들을 생각하면 정말 미칠 것 같아요.\" 에이미가 순교자 같은 표정으로 한숨을 쉬었다.\n\n\"네가 그걸 잃은 건 안됐다고 생각 안 한다. 규칙을 어겼으니, 불복종에 대한 벌을 좀 받아 마땅했지.\" 엄한 대답이 돌아오자, 동정밖에 기대하지 않았던 어린 아가씨는 적잖이 실망했다.\n\n\"그럼 제가 학교 전체 앞에서 망신당한 게 기쁘시다는 거예요?\" 에이미가 외쳤다.\n\n\"잘못을 고치는 데 나라면 그런 방법을 택하진 않았을 게다.\" 어머니가 대답했다. \"하지만 그게 더 부드러운 방법보다 너한테 더 큰 도움이 안 되리라고 장담은 못 하겠구나. 너는 점점 좀 우쭐대는 데가 생기고 있어, 얘야. 이제 그걸 바로잡기 시작할 때가 됐단다. 너한테는 자그마한 재능과 미덕이 여럿 있지만, 그걸 굳이 드러낼 필요는 없어. 자만은 아무리 빼어난 재능도 망쳐 버리거든. 진짜 재능이나 선함이 오래 묻혀 있을 위험은 별로 없단다. 설령 그렇다 해도, 그걸 지니고 잘 쓰고 있다는 자각만으로 만족할 수 있어야 하지. 모든 힘의 가장 큰 매력은 겸손에 있는 거란다.\"\n\n\"정말 그래요!\" 한구석에서 조와 체스를 두던 로리가 외쳤다. \"전에 음악에 정말 놀라운 재능을 지닌 여자애를 한 명 알았는데요, 그 애는 자기 재능을 몰랐어요. 혼자 있을 때 자기가 얼마나 사랑스러운 곡들을 짓는지 짐작도 못 했고, 누가 말해 줘도 믿지 않았을걸요.\"\n\n\"나도 그 좋은 애를 알았으면 좋았을 텐데. 나처럼 멍청한 애를 도와줬을지도 모르잖아.\" 로리 곁에 서서 열심히 듣고 있던 베스가 말했다.\n\n\"넌 이미 그 애를 알아. 게다가 그 애는 누구보다도 너를 잘 도와주고 있는걸.\" 로리가 명랑한 검은 눈에 어찌나 장난기 어린 의미를 담아 그녀를 바라보았던지, 베스는 갑자기 새빨개져서, 그 뜻밖의 깨달음에 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 소파 쿠션에 얼굴을 파묻었다.\n\n조는 자기 베스를 그렇게 칭찬해 준 보답으로 로리가 게임을 이기게 해 주었다. 칭찬을 받은 뒤로 베스는 아무리 졸라도 그들에게 연주를 들려주려 하지 않았기 때문이다. 그래서 로리는 자기 솜씨를 한껏 발휘해 멋들어지게 노래를 불렀다. 유독 명랑한 기분이었던 것이다. 마치 자매들 앞에서 그는 자기 성격의 침울한 면을 좀처럼 드러내지 않았다. 그가 가고 나자, 저녁 내내 골똘하던 에이미가 무슨 새로운 생각에 골몰한 듯 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"로리는 교양 있는 애인가요?\"\n\n\"그럼. 훌륭한 교육을 받았고, 재능도 많지. 응석받이로 망치지만 않는다면 멋진 사내가 될 게다.\" 어머니가 대답했다.\n\n\"그리고 우쭐대지도 않죠, 그렇죠?\" 에이미가 물었다.\n\n\"전혀 그렇지 않지. 그래서 그렇게 매력적이고, 우리 모두가 그 애를 그토록 좋아하는 거란다.\"\n\n\"알겠어요. 교양 있고 우아한 건 좋지만, 으스대거나 잘난 척하면 안 된다는 거죠.\" 에이미가 생각에 잠겨 말했다.\n\n\"겸손하게 지니고만 있으면 그런 것들은 그 사람의 태도와 대화에서 언제나 드러나고 느껴지는 법이란다. 굳이 과시할 필요가 없어.\" 마치 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그건 마치 자기가 가진 걸 남들이 다 알게 하려고 모자며 드레스며 리본을 한꺼번에 죄다 걸치고 다니는 게 점잖지 못한 거랑 똑같네.\" 조가 덧붙였고, 그 설교는 한바탕 웃음으로 끝이 났다.\n\n# JO MEETS APOLLYON.\n\n\"언니들, 어디 가?\" 어느 토요일 오후, 에이미가 언니들의 방에 들어왔다가 그들이 외출 채비를 하고 있는 걸 보고 물었다. 무언가 비밀스러운 낌새가 그녀의 호기심을 자극했다.\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마. 어린애는 그런 거 묻는 거 아니야.\" 조가 쌀쌀맞게 대꾸했다.\n\n그런데 어릴 적에 우리 감정을 가장 자존심 상하게 하는 게 있다면, 바로 그런 말을 듣는 것이다. 게다가 \"저리 가렴, 얘야\" 하는 말은 한층 더 견디기 힘든 법이다. 에이미는 이 모욕에 발끈해서, 한 시간을 졸라서라도 그 비밀을 알아내고 말겠다고 결심했다. 자기 부탁이라면 오래 거절하는 법이 없는 메그를 돌아보며 그녀는 어르듯 말했다. \"제발 좀 말해 줘! 나도 같이 가게 해 줄 수 있잖아. 베스는 피아노에 매달려 있고, 난 할 일도 없어서 너무 외롭단 말이야.\"\n\n\"안 돼, 얘야. 넌 초대받지 않았거든.\" 메그가 입을 열었다. 하지만 조가 짜증스럽게 끼어들었다. \"메그 언니, 가만히 좀 있어. 안 그러면 다 망쳐 놓겠어. 넌 못 가, 에이미. 그러니까 아기처럼 굴면서 칭얼대지 마.\"\n\n\"언니들 로리랑 어디 가는 거지, 분명해. 어젯밤 소파에서 둘이 속닥거리면서 웃다가, 내가 들어가니까 뚝 그쳤잖아. 로리랑 가는 거 아니야?\"\n\n\"맞아, 로리랑 가. 그러니 이제 좀 가만히 있고, 그만 좀 귀찮게 해.\"\n\n에이미는 입을 다물었지만 눈을 굴렸고, 메그가 부채를 주머니에 슬쩍 넣는 걸 보았다.\n\n\"알았다! 알았어! 언니들 '일곱 성' 보러 극장에 가는 거지!\" 그녀가 외치고는 단호하게 덧붙였다. \"그럼 나도 갈 거야. 엄마가 봐도 된다고 하셨고, 나도 헝겊 모은 돈 있단 말이야. 진작 말 안 해 준 건 너무했어.\"\n\n\"잠깐만 내 말 좀 들어 봐, 착하게 굴고.\" 메그가 달래듯 말했다. \"엄마는 이번 주에 네가 가는 걸 원치 않으셔. 네 눈이 아직 이 동화극의 환한 빛을 견딜 만큼 다 낫지 않았거든. 다음 주에 베스랑 해나랑 같이 가서 즐겁게 보면 돼.\"\n\n\"그건 언니들이랑 로리랑 가는 거의 절반만큼도 재미없잖아. 제발 가게 해 줘. 이 감기로 이렇게 오래 앓으면서 집에만 갇혀 있었더니, 재미있는 게 너무 그리워 죽겠어. 그래 줘, 메그 언니! 정말 착하게 굴게.\" 에이미가 최대한 애처로운 표정을 지으며 애원했다.\n\n\"데려가는 게 어떨까. 잘 싸매면 엄마도 괜찮다고 하실 것 같은데.\" 메그가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"쟤가 가면 난 안 가. 그리고 내가 안 가면 로리도 싫어할 거야. 우리만 초대해 놨는데 에이미를 끌고 가면 너무 무례하잖아. 자기가 환영받지 못하는 데에 끼어들기 싫어하는 게 당연하다고 봐.\" 조가 짜증스럽게 말했다. 모처럼 즐기고 싶은데 안절부절못하는 어린애를 챙겨야 하는 수고가 싫었던 것이다.\n\n그 말투와 태도가 에이미를 화나게 했고, 그녀는 더없이 약 올리는 투로 부츠를 신으며 말했다. \"난 갈 거야. 메그가 가도 된댔어. 그리고 내 표 값은 내가 내면 로리랑은 아무 상관 없잖아.\"\n\n\"넌 우리랑 같이 못 앉아. 우리 자리는 예약돼 있거든. 그렇다고 너 혼자 앉을 수도 없으니, 로리가 자기 자리를 너한테 양보할 테고, 그럼 우리 즐거움을 다 망쳐 놓는 거야. 아니면 로리가 네 자리를 따로 구해 줘야 하는데, 초대받지도 않았는데 그건 점잖지 못한 일이지. 넌 한 발짝도 못 움직여. 그러니 그냥 있던 자리에 있어.\" 조가 어느 때보다 더 짜증을 내며 야단쳤다. 서두르다가 막 손가락을 바늘에 찔린 참이었다.\n\n부츠 한 짝만 신은 채 바닥에 앉아 에이미가 울음을 터뜨렸고, 메그가 그녀를 타이르고 있을 때 아래에서 로리가 불렀다. 두 소녀는 울고 있는 동생을 남겨 둔 채 서둘러 내려갔다. 에이미는 이따금 다 큰 척하는 태도를 잊고 응석받이 어린애처럼 굴곤 했던 것이다. 일행이 막 나서려는 참에, 에이미가 난간 너머로 위협하는 투로 외쳤다.\n\"이 일 후회하게 될 거야, 조 마치. 두고 봐.\"\n\n\"쓸데없는 소리!\" 조가 문을 쾅 닫으며 대꾸했다.\n\n그들은 즐거운 시간을 보냈다. \"다이아몬드 호수의 일곱 성\"은 더 바랄 게 없을 만큼 화려하고 멋졌으니까. 하지만 우스꽝스러운 빨간 도깨비들과 반짝이는 요정들, 으리으리한 왕자와 공주들에도 불구하고, 조의 즐거움에는 한 방울의 씁쓸함이 섞여 있었다. 요정 여왕의 노란 곱슬머리가 에이미를 떠올리게 했던 것이다. 막간마다 그녀는 동생이 자기를 \"후회하게\" 만들려고 무슨 짓을 할지 궁리하며 마음을 달랬다. 그녀와 에이미는 살아오는 동안 여러 차례 한바탕씩 다투었다. 둘 다 성미가 급한 데다, 일단 제대로 발끈하면 격해지기 쉬웠기 때문이다. 에이미가 조를 약 올리면 조가 에이미를 자극했고, 그렇게 이따금 폭발이 일어났는데, 나중에는 둘 다 무척 부끄러워했다. 맏이긴 했어도 조는 자제력이 가장 부족했고, 늘 자기를 곤경에 빠뜨리는 그 불같은 성미를 다스리려 애쓰느라 고생이 많았다. 그녀의 화는 오래가지 않았고, 자기 잘못을 겸손하게 인정한 뒤엔 진심으로 뉘우치며 더 잘하려고 애썼다. 자매들은 조를 화나게 만드는 게 은근히 재미있다고들 했다. 화내고 난 뒤엔 어찌나 천사 같았던지. 가엾은 조는 착해지려고 필사적으로 애썼지만, 그녀의 마음속 원수는 늘 활활 타올라 그녀를 무너뜨릴 채비가 되어 있었다. 그걸 잠재우는 데는 여러 해에 걸친 끈기 있는 노력이 필요했다.\n\n집에 돌아오니 에이미가 응접실에서 책을 읽고 있었다. 그들이 들어와도 그녀는 부루퉁한 태도를 지으며, 책에서 눈을 떼지도, 한마디 묻지도 않았다. 어쩌면 호기심이 원망을 이겼을지도 모르나, 마침 베스가 거기 있어 이것저것 물어보고는 연극에 대한 신나는 묘사를 들었다. 외출용 모자를 치우러 위층으로 올라간 조가 맨 먼저 눈길을 준 곳은 서랍장이었다. 지난번 다툼 때 에이미가 조의 맨 위 서랍을 바닥에 거꾸로 쏟아 분풀이를 했던 것이다. 하지만 모든 게 제자리에 있었다. 여러 벽장과 가방, 상자들을 후딱 살펴본 뒤, 조는 에이미가 자기에게 당한 억울함을 용서하고 잊었다고 결론지었다.\n\n하지만 조의 생각은 틀렸다. 이튿날 그녀는 폭풍을 불러일으킬 사실을 발견하게 되었으니까. 늦은 오후, 메그와 베스, 에이미가 함께 앉아 있을 때 조가 흥분한 얼굴로 방에 뛰어들며 숨도 못 쉬고 다그쳤다. \"누가 내 책 가져갔어?\"\n\n메그와 베스는 곧장 \"아니\"라고 하며 놀란 표정을 지었지만, 에이미는 난로만 들쑤시며 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 조는 그녀의 얼굴이 붉어지는 걸 보고 단숨에 덮쳐들었다.\n\n\"에이미, 네가 가져갔지?\"\n\n\"아니야, 안 가져갔어.\"\n\n\"그럼 어디 있는지 알지?\"\n\n\"아니, 몰라.\"\n\n\"거짓말!\" 조가 에이미의 어깨를 붙잡고, 에이미보다 훨씬 더 용감한 아이라도 겁먹게 할 만큼 사나운 표정으로 소리쳤다.\n\n\"거짓말 아니야. 난 안 가졌고, 지금 어디 있는지도 모르고, 알고 싶지도 않아.\"\n\n\"넌 뭔가 알고 있어. 당장 말하는 게 좋을 거야. 안 그러면 말하게 만들 테니까.\" 조가 그녀를 살짝 흔들었다.\n\n\"마음껏 야단쳐 봐. 언니의 그 한심한 낡은 책은 두 번 다시 못 볼 테니까.\" 에이미도 덩달아 흥분하며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"왜 못 봐?\"\n\n\"내가 태워 버렸거든.\"\n\n\"뭐! 내가 그렇게 아끼고, 그토록 공들였고, 아버지 돌아오시기 전에 끝내려던 내 그 작은 책을? 정말 그걸 태웠다고?\" 조가 새파랗게 질리며 말했다. 두 눈에는 불이 일었고, 두 손은 에이미를 신경질적으로 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"그래, 태웠어! 어제 그렇게 심술부린 대가를 치르게 하겠다고 했잖아. 그래서 치르게 한 거야, 그러니까—\"\n\n에이미는 더 잇지 못했다. 조의 불같은 성미가 그녀를 사로잡아, 이가 딱딱 부딪칠 만큼 에이미를 마구 흔들며, 슬픔과 분노로 격해져 외쳤다.\n\n\"이 못된, 못된 것! 난 그걸 다시는 못 써. 그리고 평생 너를 용서하지 않을 거야.\"\n\n메그가 에이미를 구하러, 베스가 조를 달래러 달려들었지만, 조는 완전히 제정신이 아니었다. 마지막으로 동생의 따귀를 한 대 올려붙이고는 방을 뛰쳐나가 다락방의 낡은 소파로 올라가, 혼자서 분을 삭였다.\n\n아래층의 폭풍은 가라앉았다. 마치 부인이 집에 돌아와 사연을 듣고는, 얼마 안 가 에이미에게 언니에게 저지른 잘못을 깨닫게 해 주었기 때문이다. 조의 책은 그녀의 자랑이요 마음의 보배였고, 식구들은 그것을 큰 가능성을 지닌 문학의 새싹으로 여겼다. 고작 자그마한 동화 여섯 편에 지나지 않았지만, 조는 인쇄해도 좋을 만큼 훌륭한 무언가를 만들어 내기를 바라며, 온 마음을 쏟아 끈기 있게 매달려 왔다. 그녀는 막 그것들을 무척 정성스레 옮겨 적고 낡은 원고를 없애 버린 터라, 에이미가 지른 불은 몇 해에 걸쳐 정성껏 쓴 작품을 모조리 태워 버린 셈이었다. 남들에게는 사소한 손실로 보였겠지만, 조에게는 끔찍한 재앙이었고, 무엇으로도 결코 보상받을 수 없는 일이라는 느낌이 들었다. 베스는 새끼 고양이가 죽기라도 한 것처럼 슬퍼했고, 메그는 자기 동생을 두둔하려 들지 않았다. 마치 부인은 침통하고 슬픈 표정을 지었으며, 에이미는 자기가 한 짓을 그 누구보다 더 후회하면서도, 용서를 빌기 전에는 아무도 자기를 사랑해 주지 않으리라는 생각이 들었다.\n\n차 마실 시간을 알리는 종이 울리자, 조가 나타났다. 너무도 험악하고 다가갈 수 없는 표정이어서, 에이미는 온 용기를 다 끌어모은 뒤에야 겨우 고분고분하게 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"미안해, 조 언니. 정말, 정말 잘못했어.\"\n\n\"절대 용서 안 해.\" 조는 매몰차게 대답했다. 그리고 그 순간부터 에이미를 완전히 못 본 척했다.\n\n아무도 그 큰일에 대해 말을 꺼내지 않았다. 마치 부인조차도. 조가 그런 기분일 때는 말을 해 봤자 소용없다는 걸 다들 경험으로 알고 있었기 때문이다. 가장 현명한 방법은 사소한 일이 생기거나, 조 자신의 너그러운 성품이 발동하여 노여움이 누그러지고 갈라진 틈이 아물 때까지 기다리는 것이었다. 그날 저녁은 즐겁지 못했다. 평소처럼 어머니가 브레머나 스콧, 에지워스의 책을 소리 내어 읽어 주는 동안 다들 바느질을 했지만, 무언가가 빠진 듯했고 정겨운 가정의 평화가 깨어져 있었다. 노래할 시간이 되자 그 사실이 가장 뚜렷이 느껴졌다. 베스는 그저 연주만 했고, 조는 돌처럼 입을 다물고 서 있었으며, 에이미는 그만 목이 메어 버려서, 결국 메그와 어머니만 둘이서 노래했다. 하지만 종달새처럼 명랑하게 굴어 보려고 애를 써도, 피리 같은 목소리들이 평소처럼 잘 어우러지지 않았고, 다들 어딘가 어긋난 기분이었다.\n\n조가 잘 자라는 입맞춤을 받을 때, 마치 부인이 다정하게 속삭였다.\n\n\"얘야, 노여움을 품은 채 해가 지게 하지 말거라. 서로 용서하고, 서로 돕고, 내일 다시 시작하렴.\"\n\n조는 그 어머니의 품에 머리를 묻고, 슬픔과 노여움을 죄다 울어서 씻어 내고 싶었다. 하지만 눈물은 사내답지 못한 나약함이었고, 마음에 너무 깊은 상처를 입은 터라 아직은 정말 도저히 용서할 수가 없었다. 그래서 조는 눈을 꾹 깜빡이고 고개를 저으며, 에이미가 듣고 있었던 탓에 퉁명스럽게 말했다.\n\n\"정말 못된 짓이었어요. 그 앤 용서받을 자격이 없어요.\"\n\n그 말을 끝으로 조는 성큼성큼 자러 가 버렸고, 그날 밤에는 즐겁거나 다정한 수다가 오가지 않았다.\n\n에이미는 화해의 손길이 거절당한 것이 무척 서운했고, 괜히 자존심을 굽혔다는 생각이 들기 시작했다. 그러자 어느 때보다 더 상처받은 기분이 들었고, 자기가 더 도덕적으로 우월하다며 으스대기 시작했는데, 그 태도가 유독 사람 속을 긁었다. 조는 여전히 먹구름 같은 얼굴이었고, 하루 종일 되는 일이 하나도 없었다. 아침에는 살을 에는 듯이 추웠다. 조는 아끼던 파이를 도랑에 떨어뜨렸고, 마치 대고모는 안절부절못하며 신경질을 부렸으며, 메그는 시무룩했고, 베스는 집에 돌아오면 어김없이 슬프고 안타까운 표정을 지었다. 게다가 에이미는, 남들이 착하게 사는 모범을 보여 줘도 늘 착해지겠다는 말만 입에 달고 살면서 정작 노력은 안 하는 사람들에 대해 계속 한마디씩 던졌다.\n\n\"다들 어찌나 밉살스러운지, 로리한테 스케이트 타러 가자고 해야겠어. 걘 늘 친절하고 명랑하니까, 분명히 내 기분을 풀어 줄 거야.\" 조는 혼잣말을 하더니 휙 나가 버렸다.\n\n에이미는 스케이트 부딪치는 소리를 듣고, 답답하다는 듯 소리치며 밖을 내다보았다.\n\n\"저것 봐! 다음번엔 날 데려가겠다고 약속해 놓고선. 이번이 올해 마지막으로 얼음을 탈 기회란 말이야. 하지만 저렇게 심술이 난 애한테 데려가 달라고 해 봤자 소용없겠지.\"\n\n\"그렇게 말하지 마. 네가 정말 못되게 굴긴 했잖아. 조 언니가 그 소중한 작은 책을 잃은 걸 용서하기란 쉽지 않은 일이야. 그래도 이제는 용서할 만하다고 봐. 적당한 순간을 잘 골라서 다가가면, 분명 용서해 줄 거야.\" 메그가 말했다. \"언니랑 로리 뒤를 따라가. 조 언니가 로리랑 어울리면서 기분이 좋아질 때까지는 아무 말도 하지 말고. 그러다 조용한 틈을 봐서 그냥 입맞춤을 하든지, 뭐든 다정한 일을 해 봐. 그럼 진심으로 다시 사이좋게 지내려 할 거야, 틀림없이.\"\n\n\"해 볼게.\" 에이미가 말했다. 그 조언이 마음에 들었기 때문이다. 부랴부랴 채비를 마친 뒤, 에이미는 마침 언덕 너머로 사라지려는 두 사람을 뒤쫓아 달려갔다.\n\n강까지는 멀지 않았지만, 에이미가 따라잡기도 전에 둘은 채비를 다 마친 상태였다. 조는 에이미가 오는 것을 보고 등을 돌려 버렸다. 로리는 보지 못했는데, 추위가 닥치기 전에 따뜻한 날씨가 며칠 이어졌던 탓에, 강가를 따라 조심조심 스케이트를 지치며 얼음 상태를 살피고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"경주를 시작하기 전에, 첫 번째 굽이까지 가서 괜찮은지 봐 둘게.\" 로리가 모피로 가장자리를 두른 외투와 모자 차림으로 어린 러시아 소년처럼 쏜살같이 달려 나가며 하는 말이 에이미의 귀에 들렸다.\n\n조는 에이미가 달려온 탓에 숨을 헐떡이고, 발을 동동 구르며 손가락에 입김을 불면서 스케이트를 신으려고 애쓰는 소리를 들었다. 하지만 조는 돌아보지 않고, 강을 따라 천천히 지그재그로 미끄러져 내려갔다. 동생이 곤란해하는 모습에서 씁쓸하고 불행한 만족감을 느끼면서. 조는 노여움을 품고 또 품어서 그것이 점점 강해지도록 내버려 두었고, 결국 노여움이 조를 사로잡고 말았다. 사악한 생각과 감정이란 곧바로 내쫓지 않으면 늘 그렇게 마음을 차지해 버리는 법이다. 로리가 굽이를 돌면서 뒤돌아 외쳤다.\n\n\"강가 가까이 붙어! 한가운데는 위험해!\"\n\n조에게는 들렸지만, 에이미는 마침 안간힘을 쓰며 일어서던 참이라 한마디도 알아듣지 못했다. 조가 어깨 너머로 흘끗 보았더니, 마음속에 품고 있던 작은 악마가 귓가에 속삭였다.\n\n\"걔가 들었든 안 들었든 무슨 상관이야. 자기 일은 자기가 알아서 챙기라지.\"\n\n로리는 굽이를 돌아 사라진 뒤였다. 조는 막 굽이에 다다른 참이었고, 한참 뒤처져 있던 에이미는 강 한가운데의 더 매끄러운 얼음을 향해 나아가고 있었다. 잠시 조는 가슴에 이상한 느낌이 든 채 가만히 서 있었다. 그러다 계속 가기로 마음먹었지만, 무언가가 조를 붙들어 돌려세웠다. 마침 그때, 에이미가 두 팔을 번쩍 치켜들며 가라앉는 모습이 보였다. 썩은 얼음이 갑작스레 쩍 갈라지는 소리, 물이 튀는 소리, 그리고 조의 심장을 두려움으로 멎게 하는 비명. 조는 로리를 부르려 했지만 목소리가 나오지 않았고, 앞으로 달려가려 했지만 발에 힘이 하나도 없는 것 같았다. 한순간, 조는 그저 꼼짝 못 한 채, 공포에 질린 얼굴로 검은 물 위에 떠 있는 작고 파란 모자만 멍하니 바라볼 수밖에 없었다.\n무언가가 조의 곁을 휙 스쳐 지나갔고, 로리의 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"가로대 가져와! 빨리, 빨리!\"\n\n어떻게 해냈는지 조 자신도 알지 못했다. 하지만 그 뒤 몇 분 동안 조는 무언가에 홀린 듯이, 로리가 시키는 대로 정신없이 움직였다. 로리는 무척 침착하게, 몸을 납작 엎드린 채 팔과 하키 채로 에이미를 받쳐 들고 있었고, 그사이 조가 울타리에서 가로대를 뽑아 왔다. 둘이 힘을 합쳐 아이를 끌어냈는데, 다친 것보다는 겁에 질린 정도였다.\n\n\"자, 이제 최대한 빨리 데리고 걸어가야 해. 우리 옷가지를 죄다 이 애한테 덮어 줘. 그동안 나는 이 빌어먹을 스케이트를 벗을 테니까.\" 로리가 외쳤다. 그러면서 외투로 에이미를 감싸고는, 어느 때보다 더 풀기 어렵게 느껴지는 끈을 잡아당겼다.\n\n부들부들 떨고, 물을 뚝뚝 흘리고, 울먹이면서 그들은 에이미를 집으로 데려왔다. 한바탕 소동을 치른 뒤, 에이미는 활활 타오르는 난롯불 앞에서 담요에 둘둘 말린 채 잠이 들었다. 그 북새통 내내 조는 거의 입을 열지 않았다. 그저 창백하고 넋이 나간 얼굴로 이리저리 뛰어다녔는데, 옷은 반쯤 벗겨졌고, 드레스는 찢어졌으며, 손은 얼음과 가로대와 말 안 듣는 버클에 베이고 멍이 들어 있었다. 에이미가 편안히 잠들고 집 안이 조용해지자, 침대 곁에 앉아 있던 마치 부인이 조를 불러서는 상처 난 손을 싸매 주기 시작했다.\n\n\"정말 괜찮은 거예요?\" 조는 황금빛 머리를 후회 어린 눈길로 바라보며 속삭였다. 그 머리는 음흉한 얼음 밑으로 영영 자기 눈앞에서 휩쓸려 사라질 뻔하지 않았던가.\n\n\"아주 괜찮단다, 얘야. 다친 데도 없고, 감기조차 안 걸릴 것 같구나. 네가 그렇게 분별 있게 잘 덮어서 얼른 집으로 데려왔으니 말이다.\" 어머니가 밝게 대답했다.\n\n\"전부 로리가 한 거예요. 저는 그냥 에이미를 내버려 뒀고요. 엄마, 만약에 에이미가 죽기라도 했으면, 그건 다 제 잘못이었을 거예요.\" 조는 침대 곁에 털썩 주저앉아, 뉘우치는 눈물을 쏟으며 그동안 있었던 일을 전부 털어놓았다. 자기 마음이 모질었던 것을 호되게 자책하면서, 자기에게 닥쳤을지도 모르는 무거운 벌을 면하게 된 데 대한 고마움을 흐느끼며 토해 냈다.\n\n\"제 이 끔찍한 성질 때문이에요! 고치려고 애쓰는데, 고쳤다 싶으면 또 전보다 더 심하게 터져 나와요. 아, 엄마, 어떡하면 좋아요? 어떡하면 좋죠?\" 가엾은 조가 절망에 빠져 외쳤다.\n\n\"깨어 있고 기도하렴, 얘야. 노력하는 일에 결코 지치지 말고, 네 결점을 이겨 내는 일이 불가능하다고는 절대 생각하지 말거라.\" 마치 부인이 헝클어진 머리를 자기 어깨로 끌어당기며 말했다. 그러고는 젖은 뺨에 어찌나 다정하게 입을 맞추는지, 조는 그만 더 서럽게 울고 말았다.\n\n\"엄마는 모르세요, 이게 얼마나 나쁜지 짐작도 못 하실 거예요! 욱하면 무슨 짓이든 할 수 있을 것 같아요. 어찌나 사나워지는지, 누굴 해치고도 그걸 즐길 수 있을 것 같다고요. 언젠가는 끔찍한 짓을 저질러서 제 인생을 망치고, 다들 절 미워하게 만들까 봐 무서워요. 아, 엄마, 도와주세요, 제발 좀 도와주세요!\"\n\n\"도와주마, 얘야, 도와주고말고. 그렇게 서럽게 울지는 말고, 오늘을 기억하렴. 그리고 두 번 다시 이런 날을 겪지 않겠다고, 온 마음을 다해 결심하렴. 조야, 우리 모두에게는 유혹이 있단다. 어떤 건 네 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 것도 있지. 그리고 그걸 이겨 내는 데 평생이 걸리는 경우도 많아. 너는 네 성질이 세상에서 제일 못됐다고 여기겠지만, 내 성질도 꼭 그랬단다.\"\n\n\"엄마가요? 아니, 엄마는 화내는 법이 없잖아요!\" 그 순간 조는 놀란 나머지 후회마저 잊어버렸다.\n\n\"마흔 해 동안 그걸 고치려고 애써 왔지만, 겨우 다스리는 데까지밖에 못 왔단다. 나는 거의 매일같이 화가 난단다, 조야. 다만 그걸 드러내지 않는 법을 배웠을 뿐이지. 그리고 언젠가는 화를 느끼지 않는 법까지 배우길 여전히 바라고 있어. 비록 그러기까지 또 마흔 해가 걸릴지도 모르지만 말이다.\"\n\n조가 그토록 사랑하는 그 얼굴에 어린 인내와 겸손은, 그 어떤 현명한 훈계나 따끔한 꾸지람보다 더 좋은 가르침이 되었다. 자기에게 베풀어진 공감과 신뢰에 조는 곧바로 위로를 받았다. 어머니에게도 자기와 같은 결점이 있고, 그걸 고치려고 애써 왔다는 사실을 아니, 자신의 결점도 한결 견디기 쉬워졌고 그걸 고치겠다는 결심도 더 단단해졌다. 비록 깨어 있고 기도하는 데 마흔 해라니, 열다섯 살 소녀에게는 꽤 긴 세월처럼 느껴지긴 했지만 말이다.\n\n\"엄마, 마치 대고모님이 야단치시거나 사람들이 엄마를 성가시게 할 때, 가끔 입술을 꾹 다물고 방을 나가시잖아요. 그게 화가 나신 거예요?\" 조는 그 어느 때보다 어머니가 가깝고 소중하게 느껴져서 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 입가로 치밀어 오르는 성급한 말들을 억누르는 법을 배웠단다. 그 말들이 내 뜻과는 상관없이 터져 나오려 한다 싶으면, 잠깐 자리를 피해서, 그렇게 나약하고 못된 나 자신을 몸을 살짝 흔들며 다잡는 거지.\" 마치 부인이 한숨 섞인 미소를 지으며 대답했다. 그러면서 헝클어진 조의 머리를 매만지고 단정히 묶어 주었다.\n\n\"가만히 참는 법을 어떻게 배우셨어요? 바로 그게 제 고민이거든요. 무슨 말을 하는지 미처 깨닫기도 전에 날카로운 말들이 튀어나오니까요. 게다가 말을 하면 할수록 더 심해져서, 나중엔 남의 마음을 다치게 하고 끔찍한 말을 하는 게 즐거워질 지경이에요. 어떻게 하시는 건지 가르쳐 주세요, 엄마.\"\n\n\"우리 착한 어머니께서 나를 도와주시곤 했단다.\"\n\n\"지금 엄마가 우릴 도와주시는 것처럼요.\" 조가 고마운 마음에 입을 맞추며 끼어들었다.\n\n\"하지만 지금 네 나이보다 조금 더 먹었을 무렵에 그분을 여의었고, 그 뒤로 여러 해 동안 홀로 싸워 나가야 했단다. 다른 누구에게 내 약점을 털어놓기엔 자존심이 너무 셌거든. 힘든 시절이었어, 조야. 내 실패 때문에 쓰디쓴 눈물도 숱하게 흘렸지. 아무리 애를 써도 도무지 나아지는 것 같지 않았으니까. 그러다 네 아버지를 만났고, 어찌나 행복했던지 착해지기가 한결 쉬워지더구나. 그런데 얼마 안 가서, 어린 딸 넷을 곁에 두고 살림은 가난하니, 옛날의 그 골칫거리가 또 시작됐단다. 나는 천성이 참을성 있는 사람이 아니거든. 내 아이들이 뭔가를 갖고 싶어 하면서도 갖지 못하는 걸 보는 게 너무도 괴로웠어.\"\n\n\"가엾은 엄마! 그땐 뭐가 도움이 됐어요?\"\n\n\"네 아버지란다, 조야. 그분은 결코 참을성을 잃는 법이 없고, 의심하거나 불평하는 법도 없단다. 늘 희망을 품고, 어찌나 명랑하게 일하며 기다리시는지, 그분 앞에서는 차마 다르게 굴기가 부끄러워지지. 그분은 나를 돕고 위로해 주셨고, 내 딸들이 갖추기를 바라는 모든 미덕을 내가 먼저 실천하려고 애써야 한다는 걸 일깨워 주셨단다. 내가 너희의 본보기였으니까. 나 자신을 위해서보다 너희를 위해서 노력하는 게 더 쉬웠어. 내가 날카롭게 말할 때 너희 중 누군가가 깜짝 놀라거나 어리둥절해하던 그 표정이, 그 어떤 말보다도 나를 더 호되게 꾸짖었단다. 그리고 내 아이들의 사랑과 존경과 신뢰야말로, 너희가 본받았으면 하는 그런 여자가 되려고 애쓴 내 노력에 대한 가장 달콤한 보상이었지.\"\n\n\"아, 엄마, 제가 언젠가 엄마의 반만큼이라도 착해진다면, 그걸로 만족할 거예요.\" 조가 크게 감동하여 외쳤다.\n\n\"네가 훨씬 더 나은 사람이 되길 바란단다, 얘야. 하지만 네 아버지가 '가슴속의 적'이라고 부르는 그것을 늘 경계해야 해. 안 그러면 네 인생을 망치진 않더라도 슬프게 만들 수 있으니까. 너는 한 번 경고를 받은 셈이야. 그걸 기억하고, 오늘 겪은 것보다 더 큰 슬픔과 후회를 불러오기 전에, 온 마음과 영혼을 다해 그 욱하는 성질을 다스리려고 애쓰렴.\"\n\n\"노력할게요, 엄마. 정말로 그럴게요. 하지만 엄마가 도와주셔야 해요. 일깨워 주시고, 제가 욱하지 않게 막아 주세요. 예전에 아버지가 가끔 손가락을 입술에 갖다 대고, 무척 다정하지만 진지한 얼굴로 엄마를 바라보시는 걸 봤어요. 그러면 엄마는 늘 입술을 꼭 다물거나 자리를 피하셨고요. 그게 그때 아버지가 엄마를 일깨워 주시던 거였어요?\" 조가 부드럽게 물었다.\n\n\"그래. 내가 그렇게 도와 달라고 부탁했단다. 그분은 결코 그걸 잊지 않으셨고, 그 작은 몸짓과 다정한 눈길로 내가 날카로운 말을 숱하게 내뱉지 않도록 막아 주셨지.\"\n\n조는 어머니가 그렇게 말하면서 눈에 눈물이 차오르고 입술이 떨리는 것을 보았다. 자기가 말을 너무 많이 한 게 아닌가 싶어, 조는 걱정스레 속삭였다. \"제가 엄마를 지켜보고, 또 그 얘길 꺼낸 게 잘못이었어요? 무례하게 굴려던 건 아니었는데, 엄마한테는 생각하는 걸 죄다 말하는 게 너무 편하고, 여기 있으면 마음이 너무 든든하고 행복해서요.\"\n\n\"우리 조, 엄마한테는 무슨 말이든 해도 된단다. 내 딸들이 나를 믿어 주고, 내가 자기들을 얼마나 사랑하는지 알아준다고 느끼는 게, 나에게는 가장 큰 행복이자 자랑이거든.\"\n\n\"엄마를 슬프게 해 드린 줄 알았어요.\"\n\n\"아니란다, 얘야. 다만 네 아버지 얘기를 하다 보니, 그분이 얼마나 그리운지, 그분께 얼마나 큰 빚을 졌는지, 그리고 그분의 어린 딸들을 안전하고 착하게 지켜 드리려고 내가 얼마나 한결같이 깨어 있고 애써야 하는지가 새삼 떠올랐을 뿐이야.\"\n\n\"그래도 엄마는 아버지더러 가라고 하셨고, 떠나실 때도 울지 않으셨고, 지금도 불평 한마디 없고, 도움이 필요한 기색도 전혀 없잖아요.\" 조가 의아해하며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 사랑하는 나라에 내가 가진 가장 소중한 것을 바쳤고, 그분이 떠나실 때까지 눈물을 참았단다. 우리 둘 다 그저 우리의 본분을 다했을 뿐이고, 끝내는 분명 그 덕분에 더 행복해질 텐데, 내가 왜 불평하겠니? 내가 도움이 필요 없어 보인다면, 그건 나에게 네 아버지보다도 더 좋은 친구가 있어서, 그분이 나를 위로하고 지탱해 주시기 때문이란다. 얘야, 네 인생의 시련과 유혹은 이제 막 시작됐고, 앞으로 숱하게 많을지도 몰라. 하지만 이 땅의 아버지에게서 느끼는 것처럼 하늘에 계신 아버지의 힘과 다정함을 느끼는 법을 배운다면, 너는 그 모든 걸 이겨 내고 끝까지 살아남을 수 있단다. 그분을 사랑하고 믿을수록, 너는 그분을 더 가까이 느끼게 되고, 인간의 힘과 지혜에 덜 기대게 될 거야. 그분의 사랑과 보살핌은 결코 지치거나 변하지 않고, 결코 네게서 빼앗길 수도 없으며, 도리어 평생토록 이어지는 평화와 행복과 힘의 원천이 될 수 있단다. 이걸 진심으로 믿고, 네 사소한 걱정과 희망과 죄와 슬픔을 모두 안고서, 엄마에게 오듯 그렇게 거리낌 없이 믿음을 갖고 하느님께 가렴.\"\n\n조의 유일한 대답은 어머니를 꼭 끌어안는 것이었다. 그리고 뒤이은 침묵 속에서, 조가 일찍이 드려 본 적 없는 가장 진실한 기도가, 말없이 그 마음에서 흘러나왔다. 슬프지만 행복한 그 시간에, 조는 후회와 절망의 쓰라림뿐만 아니라 자기를 누르고 다스리는 일의 달콤함도 배웠으니까. 그리고 어머니의 손에 이끌려, 그 어떤 아버지의 사랑보다 강하고 그 어떤 어머니의 사랑보다 다정한 마음으로 모든 아이를 맞아 주시는 그 친구에게 한 걸음 더 다가갔던 것이다.\n\n에이미가 몸을 뒤척이며 잠결에 한숨을 쉬었다. 그러자 마치 당장이라도 자기 잘못을 바로잡고 싶다는 듯, 조가 고개를 들었는데, 그 얼굴에는 일찍이 한 번도 떠오른 적 없는 표정이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"노여움을 품은 채 해가 지게 내버려 뒀어. 끝내 용서하지 않았고. 오늘 로리가 아니었으면, 영영 너무 늦어 버렸을지도 몰라! 내가 어쩜 그렇게 못됐을까?\" 조는 동생 위로 몸을 기울여, 베개 위에 흩어진 젖은 머리카락을 부드럽게 쓰다듬으며 반쯤 소리 내어 말했다.\n\n그 말이 들리기라도 한 듯, 에이미가 눈을 떴다. 그러고는 조의 가슴에 곧장 와닿는 미소를 지으며 두 팔을 내밀었다. 둘 다 아무 말도 하지 않았지만, 담요에 싸인 채로 서로를 꼭 끌어안았고, 진심 어린 입맞춤 한 번으로 모든 것이 용서되고 잊혔다.\n9장\n\n# MEG GOES TO VANITY FAIR.\n\n메그, 허영의 시장에 가다\n\"바로 이맘때 그 애들이 홍역에 걸렸다는 게, 세상에 이렇게 운 좋은 일이 또 있을까 싶어.\" 어느 4월의 어느 날, 메그가 자기 방에서 동생들에게 둘러싸인 채 \"나들이용\" 트렁크를 꾸리며 서서 말했다.\n\n\"게다가 애니 모펏이 약속을 잊지 않다니 정말 고마운 일이야. 꼬박 두 주 동안 즐겁게 놀 텐데, 더없이 근사할 거야.\" 조가 대답했다. 긴 팔로 치마를 개키는 모습이 꼭 풍차 같았다.\n\n\"날씨도 어쩜 이렇게 좋을까. 그게 너무 다행이야.\" 베스가 거들었다. 이 큰일을 위해 빌려 온 자기의 가장 좋은 상자에, 목에 두르는 리본과 머리 리본을 정성껏 정리하면서.\n\n\"나도 멋지게 놀면서 이 예쁜 것들을 죄다 입어 봤으면 좋겠다.\" 에이미가 핀을 입에 한가득 문 채로 말하며, 언니의 바늘꽂이를 솜씨 좋게 다시 채워 넣었다.\n\n\"너희도 다 같이 가면 좋겠는데. 그럴 수가 없으니, 돌아와서 내 모험담을 들려줄게. 너희가 이렇게 친절하게 물건도 빌려주고 채비도 도와줬으니, 그 정도는 해 줘야지.\" 메그가 방을 둘러보며 말했다. 무척 소박한 옷가지였지만, 동생들 눈에는 거의 완벽해 보였다.\n\n\"엄마가 보물 상자에서 뭘 주셨어?\" 에이미가 물었다. 에이미는 마치 부인이 삼나무 궤를 열 때 그 자리에 없었던 것이다. 그 궤에는 지난날의 화려했던 시절의 유품 몇 가지가 들어 있었는데, 부인은 때가 되면 딸들에게 선물로 주려고 그것들을 간직해 두고 있었다.\n\n\"비단 양말 한 켤레랑, 저 예쁜 조각 부채, 그리고 사랑스러운 파란 띠. 난 보라색 비단이 갖고 싶었는데, 그걸 고쳐 입을 시간이 없으니, 낡은 모슬린으로 만족해야지.\"\n\n\"내 새 모슬린 치마 위에 받쳐 입으면 잘 어울릴 거야. 게다가 그 띠를 두르면 아주 돋보일걸. 내 산호 팔찌를 깨뜨리지만 않았어도 언니한테 줬을 텐데, 아쉽다.\" 조가 말했다. 조는 무엇이든 주거나 빌려주기를 좋아했지만, 가진 물건들이 대개 너무 낡아서 별 쓸모가 없었다.\n\n\"보물 상자에 사랑스러운 옛날식 진주 장신구 한 벌이 있긴 한데, 엄마가 그러시더라, 어린 처녀한테는 생화가 가장 예쁜 장식이라고. 게다가 로리가 내가 원하는 만큼 다 보내 주겠다고 약속했어.\" 메그가 대답했다. \"자, 어디 보자. 새로 산 회색 외출복이 있고. 베스, 내 모자에 달린 깃털 좀 동그랗게 말아 줄래? 그리고 일요일이랑 작은 파티에 입을 포플린이 있는데, 봄옷치고는 좀 무거워 보이지 않니? 보라색 비단이면 참 좋았을 텐데. 아유, 정말!\"\n\n\"괜찮아. 큰 파티에는 모슬린 드레스가 있잖아. 게다가 언니는 흰옷만 입으면 늘 천사 같아 보여.\" 에이미가 말했다. 자기가 마음속으로 좋아해 마지않는 그 자그마한 고운 옷가지들을 골똘히 들여다보면서.\n\n\"목이 깊이 파인 것도 아니고, 자락도 충분히 끌리지 않지만, 그걸로 버텨야지. 내 파란 평상복은 안팎을 뒤집어서 새로 단을 손봤더니 어찌나 멀쩡해 보이는지, 마치 새 옷이라도 생긴 기분이야. 내 비단 재킷은 도무지 유행에 안 맞고, 내 보닛도 샐리 것 같지가 않아. 뭐라고 말은 안 했지만, 우산은 정말 실망스러웠어. 엄마한테 흰 손잡이가 달린 검정으로 해 달라고 했는데, 깜박하시고는 누르스름한 손잡이가 달린 초록색을 사 오셨지 뭐야. 튼튼하고 깔끔하니까 불평하면 안 되겠지만, 애니의 금색 꼭지가 달린 비단 우산 옆에 두면 분명히 부끄러울 거야.\" 메그가 한숨을 쉬며 그 작은 우산을 무척 못마땅하게 훑어보았다.\n\n\"바꿔.\" 조가 권했다.\n\n\"그렇게 어리석게 굴진 않을 거야. 엄마가 그렇게 애써서 내 물건들을 마련해 주셨는데, 엄마 마음을 상하게 할 순 없잖아. 다 내 어처구니없는 생각일 뿐이니까, 거기에 휘둘리진 않을 거야. 비단 양말이랑 새 장갑 두 켤레가 내 위안거리지. 조, 네 걸 빌려주다니 정말 고마워. 새 거 두 켤레에다, 낡은 건 깨끗이 손질해서 평소용으로 쓰면 되니까, 어쩐지 부자가 된 것 같고 우아해진 기분이야.\" 메그가 장갑 상자를 흐뭇하게 들여다보았다.\n\n\"애니 모펏은 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자에 파란색이랑 분홍색 매듭을 달았던데, 내 것에도 좀 달아 줄래?\" 메그가 물었다. 마침 베스가 해나의 손을 갓 거친 새하얀 모슬린을 한 무더기 들고 왔다.\n\n\"아니, 난 안 달겠어. 그 멋진 모자는 아무 장식 없는 수수한 잠옷이랑 안 어울리니까. 가난한 사람은 함부로 치장하면 안 돼.\" 조가 딱 잘라 말했다.\n\n\"나도 언젠가는 옷에 진짜 레이스를 달고, 모자에 매듭을 달 만큼 형편이 좋아지는 날이 올까?\" 메그가 안달하며 말했다.\n\n\"언닌 며칠 전에 애니 모펏 집에 가기만 하면 더 바랄 게 없을 만큼 행복하겠다고 했잖아.\" 베스가 특유의 조용한 말투로 일러 주었다.\n\n\"그랬지! 그래, 난 행복해. 그러니까 투정 안 부릴 거야. 하지만 사람이란 가지면 가질수록 더 갖고 싶어지는 것 같지 않니? 자, 이제 칸막이 칸은 다 채웠고, 무도회 드레스만 빼고 전부 넣었어. 그건 엄마한테 꾸려 달라고 남겨 둘래.\" 메그가 기운을 차리며 말했다. 그러고는 반쯤 채운 트렁크에서, 여러 번 다리미질하고 기워 입은 그 흰 모슬린 드레스로 눈길을 옮겼다. 메그는 그 드레스를 자못 거창하게 자기의 \"무도회 드레스\"라고 불렀다.\n\n이튿날은 날씨가 좋았고, 메그는 두 주간의 새롭고 즐거운 나날을 향해 한껏 차려입고 떠났다. 마치 부인은 이번 방문을 다소 마지못해 허락했다. 마거릿이 떠날 때보다 더 불만에 가득 차서 돌아올까 봐 걱정스러웠던 것이다. 하지만 메그가 어찌나 간곡히 졸랐는지, 샐리도 잘 돌봐 주겠다고 약속한 데다, 지긋지긋한 일에 시달린 겨울 끝에 잠깐의 즐거움이 무척 달콤해 보이기도 해서, 어머니는 결국 뜻을 굽혔고, 딸은 처음으로 사교계 생활을 맛보러 떠났다.\n\n모펏 집안 사람들은 정말이지 무척 사교계다웠고, 소박한 메그는 처음에는 그 집의 으리으리함과 식구들의 우아함에 다소 주눅이 들었다. 그러나 그들은 경박한 삶을 살기는 해도 친절한 사람들이어서, 이내 손님을 편안하게 해 주었다. 어쩌면 메그는, 왜 그런지는 몰라도, 그들이 특별히 교양 있거나 똑똑한 사람들은 아니며, 그 모든 겉치레로도 그들의 평범한 본바탕을 다 가리지는 못한다는 것을 느꼈는지도 모른다. 호화롭게 먹고, 멋진 마차를 타고, 매일 가장 좋은 옷을 입고, 그저 즐기기만 하는 것은 분명 즐거운 일이었다. 그건 메그에게 딱 들어맞았다. 그래서 이내 메그는 주위 사람들의 몸가짐과 말투를 흉내 내기 시작했다. 거드름을 피우며 우아한 척하고, 프랑스어 표현을 쓰고, 머리를 곱슬곱슬하게 지지고, 드레스를 몸에 꼭 맞게 줄이고, 할 수 있는 한 유행 이야기를 늘어놓았다. 애니 모펏의 예쁜 물건들을 보면 볼수록 메그는 애니가 부러워졌고, 부자였으면 하고 한숨을 쉬었다. 이제 집을 떠올리면 휑하고 음울해 보였고, 일은 어느 때보다 고되게 느껴졌으며, 새 장갑과 비단 양말이 있는데도 자기가 무척 가난하고 박복한 처녀라는 생각이 들었다.\n\n그러나 한탄할 틈은 그리 많지 않았다. 세 소녀가 \"신나게 노는\" 데 한창 바빴기 때문이다. 그들은 하루 종일 쇼핑을 하고, 산책하고, 마차를 타고, 여기저기 방문을 다녔다. 극장과 오페라에 가거나, 저녁이면 집에서 떠들썩하게 놀았다. 애니에게는 친구가 많았고, 그들을 대접하는 법도 잘 알았다. 애니의 언니들은 무척 세련된 아가씨들이었는데, 그중 하나는 약혼한 사이여서, 메그가 보기에 그게 더없이 흥미롭고 낭만적이었다. 모펏 씨는 메그의 아버지를 아는, 뚱뚱하고 유쾌한 노신사였다. 모펏 부인도 뚱뚱하고 유쾌한 노부인이었는데, 자기 딸 못지않게 메그를 마음에 들어 했다. 다들 메그를 떠받들었고, 그들이 \"데이지\"라고 부르는 메그는 자칫 우쭐해질 판이었다.\n\n\"작은 파티\" 열리는 저녁이 되자, 포플린은 영 어울리지 않는다는 걸 알게 되었다. 다른 소녀들은 얇은 드레스를 차려입고 한껏 멋을 부렸기 때문이다. 그래서 모슬린 드레스를 꺼냈는데, 샐리의 빳빳한 새 드레스 옆에 두니 어느 때보다 낡고 후줄근하고 초라해 보였다. 메그는 소녀들이 그 드레스를 흘끔거리다가 서로 눈짓을 주고받는 것을 보았고, 두 뺨이 화끈거리기 시작했다. 메그는 그토록 온순한데도 자존심만은 무척 셌던 것이다. 누구도 그것에 대해 한마디도 하지 않았다. 샐리는 머리를 만져 주겠다고 했고, 애니는 띠를 매 주겠다고 했으며, 약혼한 언니인 벨은 메그의 흰 팔을 칭찬했다. 하지만 메그는 그 친절 속에서 자기의 가난에 대한 동정밖에 보지 못했고, 다른 이들이 웃고 떠들며 얇은 천 나비처럼 이리저리 날아다니는 동안, 혼자 우두커니 서 있자니 마음이 무척 무거웠다. 모질고 씁쓸한 감정이 꽤나 심해지려던 참에, 하녀가 꽃 상자를 하나 들고 들어왔다. 메그가 미처 입을 떼기도 전에 애니가 뚜껑을 열어젖혔고, 다들 그 안에 든 사랑스러운 장미와 히스, 고사리를 보고 탄성을 질렀다.\n\n\"물론 벨 언니 거겠지. 조지가 늘 언니한테 꽃을 보내잖아. 근데 이건 정말이지 황홀한걸.\" 애니가 한껏 향기를 들이마시며 외쳤다.\n\n\"마치 양에게 온 거래요, 배달 온 사람이 그러던데요. 여기 쪽지도 있고요.\" 하녀가 끼어들며 메그에게 쪽지를 내밀었다.\n\n\"어머나, 재미있다! 누가 보낸 거야? 너한테 연인이 있는 줄은 몰랐는걸.\" 소녀들이 호기심과 놀라움에 잔뜩 들떠서 메그 주위로 몰려들며 외쳤다.\n\n\"쪽지는 엄마한테서, 꽃은 로리한테서 온 거야.\" 메그가 담담하게 말했지만, 로리가 자기를 잊지 않았다는 사실에 무척 기뻐했다.\n\n\"어머나, 그래?\" 애니가 묘한 표정을 지으며 말했다. 메그는 그 쪽지를 호주머니에 슬쩍 넣었다. 시기와 허영과 그릇된 자존심을 막아 주는 일종의 부적처럼. 그 몇 마디 다정한 말이 메그에게 큰 힘이 되었고, 꽃은 그 아름다움으로 메그의 기운을 북돋아 주었던 것이다.\n\n거의 다시 행복해진 메그는 자기 몫으로 고사리와 장미 몇 송이를 따로 챙겨 두고, 나머지로는 친구들의 가슴이며 머리며 치마에 꽂을 앙증맞은 꽃다발을 재빨리 만들었다. 어찌나 예쁘게 건네는지, 맏언니인 클라라는 메그더러 \"여태껏 본 중에 가장 사랑스러운 아이\"라고 했고, 다들 그 작은 정성에 무척 흐뭇해하는 듯했다. 어쩐 일인지 그 친절한 행동이 메그의 침울함을 말끔히 씻어 주었다. 나머지가 모펏 부인에게 차림새를 보여 주러 간 사이, 메그는 거울 속에서 행복하고 눈이 반짝이는 얼굴을 보았다. 물결치는 머리에 고사리를 갖다 대고, 이제는 그다지 초라해 보이지 않는 드레스에 장미를 꽂으면서.\n\n그날 저녁 메그는 무척 즐거웠다. 마음껏 춤을 추었으니까. 다들 친절했고, 칭찬도 세 번이나 들었다. 애니가 노래를 시켰는데, 누군가 메그의 목소리가 유난히 곱다고 했다. 링컨 소령은 \"눈이 아름다운 저 풋풋한 어린 아가씨\"가 누구냐고 물었다. 모펏 씨는 메그가 \"꾸물대지 않고 활기가 넘친다\"며 한사코 메그와 춤을 추자고 했다. 그가 점잖게 표현한 말이었다. 그래서 메그는 더없이 즐거운 시간을 보냈는데, 어쩌다 대화 한 토막을 엿듣고는 몹시 마음이 어지러워졌다. 메그가 마침 온실 바로 안쪽에 앉아 춤 상대가 아이스크림을 가져다주기를 기다리고 있을 때, 꽃이 우거진 벽 너머에서 누군가가 묻는 소리가 들려왔다.\n\n\"그 애 몇 살이래요?\"\n\n\"열여섯이나 열일곱쯤 됐을 거예요.\" 다른 목소리가 대답했다.\n\n\"저 자매 중 누구한테든 대단한 행운이겠죠, 안 그래요? 샐리 말로는 지금 둘이 무척 가깝다던데, 그 노인네가 아주 그 애들한테 푹 빠졌다잖아요.\"\n\n\"마치 부인이 다 계획을 세워 뒀겠죠, 아무렴. 아직 이르긴 해도 패를 잘 굴릴 거예요. 정작 그 애는 아직 그런 생각은 못 하는 게 분명하지만요.\" 모펏 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"엄마 얘길 하면서 그렇게 거짓말을 하는 걸 보면, 다 알고 있는 것 같던데요. 게다가 꽃이 왔을 때 얼굴까지 발그레해지더라니까요, 어찌나 예쁘던지. 가엾기도 하지! 제대로 차려입히기만 하면 얼마나 곱겠어요. 목요일에 우리가 드레스를 빌려주겠다고 하면 기분 나빠할까요?\" 또 다른 목소리가 물었다.\n\n\"자존심이 세긴 해도, 싫다고는 안 할걸요. 가진 거라곤 그 촌스러운 모슬린 드레스뿐이니까요. 오늘 밤 그게 찢어지기라도 하면, 그게 마침 멀쩡한 걸 빌려줄 좋은 핑계가 되겠죠.\"\n\n\"두고 보자고요. 그 애 체면을 세워 주는 뜻에서 어린 로런스를 초대해야겠어요. 그러고 나서 그 일로 한바탕 재미나게 굴어 보자고요.\"\n\n이때 메그의 춤 상대가 나타났다가, 메그가 얼굴이 잔뜩 상기되고 다소 동요한 모습인 것을 발견했다. 메그는 정말 자존심이 셌고, 바로 그 순간만은 그 자존심이 도움이 되었다. 방금 들은 말에 느낀 굴욕과 분노와 역겨움을 감추는 데 보탬이 됐으니까. 메그는 순진하고 의심 없는 사람이었지만, 그래도 친구들의 험담을 알아듣지 않을 수 없었다. 메그는 그것을 잊으려 애썼지만 그럴 수 없었고, \"마치 부인이 다 계획을 세워 뒀다\", \"엄마 얘기로 거짓말을 한다\", \"촌스러운 모슬린 드레스\"라는 말을 속으로 되뇌고 또 되뇌다가, 그만 울음을 터뜨리고 집으로 달려가 고민을 털어놓고 조언을 구하고 싶어졌다. 그럴 수 없는 노릇이었기에, 메그는 명랑해 보이려고 최선을 다했다. 다소 흥분한 상태였던 덕에 어찌나 잘해 냈던지, 메그가 얼마나 애를 쓰고 있는지는 아무도 짐작조차 하지 못했다. 모든 것이 끝나고 침대에 조용히 누워, 머리가 지끈거리고 달아오른 두 뺨이 자연스레 흐른 몇 방울 눈물에 식혀질 때까지 마음껏 생각하고 의아해하고 속을 끓일 수 있게 되자, 메그는 무척 기뻤다. 그 어리석지만 악의는 없는 말들이 메그에게 새로운 세계를 열어 보였고, 지금껏 어린아이처럼 행복하게 살아온 옛 세계의 평화를 크게 흩뜨려 놓았다. 로리와의 순진한 우정은 엿들은 그 시시한 말 때문에 망가졌다. 자기 잣대로 남을 재는 모펏 부인이 갖다 붙인 그 세속적인 계획 때문에 어머니에 대한 믿음도 조금 흔들렸다. 그리고 가난한 사람의 딸에게 어울리는 소박한 옷차림에 만족하겠다던 그 현명한 결심도, 초라한 드레스를 천하에 가장 큰 재앙쯤으로 여기는 소녀들의 쓸데없는 동정에 약해지고 말았다.\n\n가엾은 메그는 잠 못 이루는 밤을 보내고, 눈이 퉁퉁 부은 채 일어났다. 마음이 울적했고, 친구들에게 반쯤은 화가 났으며, 솔직하게 털어놓고 모든 걸 바로잡지 못한 자신이 반쯤은 부끄러웠다. 그날 아침엔 다들 늑장을 부려서, 정오가 다 되어서야 소녀들은 털실 자수라도 손에 들 만큼 기운을 차렸다. 친구들의 태도에서 무언가가 메그의 눈에 대뜸 들어왔다. 메그가 보기에 다들 자기를 한층 정중하게 대했다. 메그가 하는 말에 자못 다정한 관심을 보였고, 호기심이 빤히 드러나는 눈으로 메그를 바라보았다. 이 모든 것이 메그를 놀라게 하면서도 우쭐하게 했지만, 벨 양이 편지를 쓰다 고개를 들고는 감상적인 투로 말하기 전까지는 도무지 영문을 알 수 없었다.\n\n\"데이지, 얘. 네 친구 로런스 씨한테 목요일 초대장을 보냈단다. 우리도 그분을 알고 싶고, 그게 너한테도 합당한 예우니까.\"\n메그는 얼굴을 붉혔지만, 소녀들을 골려 주고 싶은 장난기가 발동해서 짐짓 새침하게 대답했다.\n\n\"정말 친절하시네요. 하지만 그분은 오지 않으실 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"왜 안 오신다는 거니, 셰리(chérie : 프랑스어로 '귀여운 아가씨'라는 뜻 : 옮긴이)?\" 벨 양이 물었다.\n\n\"너무 연세가 많으셔서요.\"\n\n\"얘, 그게 무슨 소리니? 그분 연세가 대체 어떻게 되는데?\" 클라라 양이 외쳤다.\n\n\"아마 일흔 가까이 되셨을걸요.\" 메그가 눈에 어린 웃음기를 감추려고 뜨개코를 세며 대답했다.\n\n\"이 능청맞은 것 같으니! 당연히 우리가 말한 건 그 젊은 분이지.\" 벨 양이 웃음을 터뜨리며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"그런 사람은 없는데요. 로리는 그저 어린 남자애일 뿐인걸요.\" 메그도 따라 웃었다. 자기가 사귄다는 그 연인을 이렇게 묘사하자 자매가 주고받는 묘한 눈길을 보고서.\n\n\"네 또래쯤 되는?\" 낸이 말했다.\n\n\"우리 언니 조에 더 가까워요. 저는 8월이면 열일곱 살이 되거든요.\" 메그가 고개를 까딱하며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그분이 너한테 꽃을 보내다니, 참 다정하지 않니?\" 애니가 아무것도 아닌 일에 다 안다는 듯한 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"네, 종종 보내 주세요, 우리 모두한테요. 그 집은 사람이 북적이고, 우리도 그 집 식구들을 무척 좋아하거든요. 우리 엄마랑 로런스 할아버지가 친구 사이시니까, 우리 같은 애들이 함께 어울려 노는 게 지극히 자연스러운 일이죠.\" 메그는 그들이 더는 말을 꺼내지 않기를 바랐다.\n\n\"데이지가 아직 사교계에 나오지 않은 게 분명하네.\" 클라라 양이 벨에게 고갯짓을 하며 말했다.\n\n\"하나같이 목가적이리만치 순진한 상태로구나.\" 벨 양이 어깨를 으쓱하며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"내 딸들한테 자질구레한 것 좀 사다 주러 나가는데, 아가씨들, 뭐 필요한 거라도 있나요?\" 모펏 부인이 비단과 레이스를 두른 채 코끼리처럼 육중하게 들어오며 물었다.\n\n\"아뇨, 괜찮아요, 부인.\" 샐리가 대답했다. \"목요일에 입을 새 분홍 비단 드레스를 마련해 둬서, 필요한 게 하나도 없어요.\"\n\n\"저도 그래—\" 메그가 입을 떼다가 멈췄다. 자기에게는 필요한 게 여러 가지 있지만, 그걸 가질 수 없다는 생각이 퍼뜩 들었기 때문이다.\n\n\"넌 뭘 입을 거니?\" 샐리가 물었다.\n\n\"그 낡은 흰 드레스를 다시 입을래. 봐 줄 만하게 손볼 수만 있다면. 어젯밤에 어쩌다 심하게 찢어졌거든.\" 메그가 아주 태연하게 말하려 했지만, 속으로는 무척 불편했다.\n\n\"왜 집에 사람을 보내서 다른 걸 가져오라고 안 하니?\" 샐리가 말했다. 눈치가 빠른 아가씨는 못 되었던 것이다.\n\n\"다른 게 없거든.\" 그 말을 하기까지 메그는 한참 애를 먹었지만, 샐리는 그걸 알아채지 못하고 천연덕스럽게 놀라며 외쳤다.\n\n\"그게 다라고? 어머, 이상하기도 하지—\" 샐리는 말을 끝맺지 못했다. 벨이 고개를 저어 막고는, 다정하게 끼어들었기 때문이다.\n\n\"전혀 이상할 거 없어. 아직 사교계에 나오지도 않았는데 드레스가 많아서 뭐 하겠어? 데이지, 설령 네게 드레스가 열두 벌이 있다 해도 집에 가지러 보낼 것까진 없어. 나한테 사랑스러운 파란 비단 드레스가 한 벌 따로 넣어 둔 게 있거든. 내가 입기엔 작아져서 말이야. 네가 그걸 입어 주면 내 기분이 좋겠어, 그래 줄 거지, 응?\"\n\n\"정말 친절하시네요. 하지만 언니가 괜찮으시다면 전 제 낡은 드레스도 상관없어요. 저 같은 어린애한테는 그걸로 충분하니까요.\" 메그가 말했다.\n\n\"자, 너를 멋지게 차려입혀서 내 기분 좀 좋게 해 줘. 난 그런 걸 좋아하거든. 여기저기 조금만 손보면 넌 영락없는 작은 미인이 될 거야. 다 끝날 때까지 아무한테도 안 보여 주다가, 무도회에 가는 신데렐라와 그 대모처럼 짠 하고 나타나는 거지.\" 벨이 구슬리는 투로 말했다.\n\n이토록 친절하게 건넨 제안을 메그는 거절할 수 없었다. 조금만 손보면 자기가 정말 \"작은 미인\"이 되는지 보고 싶은 마음에 그 제안을 받아들였고, 모펏 집안 사람들에게 품었던 거북한 감정도 죄다 잊어버렸다.\n\n목요일 저녁, 벨은 하녀와 함께 방에 틀어박혔다. 둘이서 메그를 어엿한 숙녀로 탈바꿈시켰다. 메그의 머리를 지지고 곱슬곱슬하게 말았고, 향기로운 분으로 목과 팔을 매끄럽게 다듬었으며, 입술을 더 붉게 하려고 산호빛 연지를 발랐다. 메그가 한사코 마다하지 않았더라면, 오르탕스는 \"볼연지도 조금\" 더했을 것이다. 둘은 하늘빛 드레스 끈을 졸라매 메그를 그 안에 욱여넣었는데, 어찌나 꼭 끼는지 숨도 제대로 못 쉴 지경이었고, 목이 어찌나 깊이 파였는지 정숙한 메그는 거울 속 자기 모습에 얼굴을 붉혔다. 거기에 은 세공 장신구 한 벌을 더했다. 팔찌, 목걸이, 브로치, 심지어 귀걸이까지. 오르탕스가 분홍 비단실 한 가닥으로 귀걸이를 매어 주었는데, 그건 겉으로 드러나지 않았다. 가슴께에 단 찻장미 꽃봉오리 한 다발과 주름 장식 덕에, 메그도 그 예쁜 흰 어깨를 드러내는 걸 그럭저럭 받아들이게 되었고, 굽 높은 파란 비단 부츠 한 켤레가 메그의 마지막 소원까지 채워 주었다. 레이스 손수건과 깃털 부채, 은제 꽃대에 꽂은 꽃다발로 마무리하자, 벨 양은 새로 옷을 입힌 인형을 든 어린 소녀처럼 흐뭇하게 메그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"마드무아젤은 정말 매력적이고, 무척 아름다워요, 안 그래요?\" 오르탕스가 짐짓 황홀한 척 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤다.\n\n\"가서 네 모습을 보여 줘.\" 벨 양이 다른 사람들이 기다리고 있는 방으로 앞장서며 말했다.\n\n긴 치맛자락을 끌고, 귀걸이를 짤랑이고, 곱슬머리를 출렁이고, 가슴을 콩닥거리며 메그가 사각사각 소리를 내며 뒤따라 들어갈 때, 마침내 자기의 \"즐거운 시간\"이 정말로 시작되었다는 기분이 들었다. 거울이 분명히 일러 주었으니까, 자기가 정말로 \"작은 미인\"이라고. 친구들이 그 듣기 좋은 말을 열렬히 되풀이했고, 메그는 한동안 우화 속의 갈까마귀처럼, 빌려 온 깃털을 뽐내며 서 있었다. 그동안 나머지는 한 무리의 까치 떼처럼 재잘거렸다.\n\n\"내가 옷 입는 동안, 낸, 네가 데이지한테 치마 다루는 법이랑 저 프랑스식 굽을 어떻게 다뤄야 하는지 좀 일러 줘. 안 그러면 제 발에 걸려 넘어질 테니까. 클라라, 너는 네 은나비를 가져다가 머리 왼쪽에 늘어진 저 긴 곱슬머리를 추켜올려 줘. 그리고 누구든 내가 정성껏 매만진 작품을 흐트러뜨리면 안 돼.\" 벨이 자기 솜씨에 무척 흡족한 얼굴로 서둘러 나가며 말했다.\n\n\"내려가기가 무서워. 어쩐지 어색하고 뻣뻣하고, 옷도 반밖에 안 걸친 것 같아서.\" 종이 울리고 모펏 부인이 아가씨들더러 어서 모습을 보이라고 사람을 보내자, 메그가 샐리에게 말했다.\n\n\"넌 너 같지가 않아. 하지만 정말 근사한걸. 네 옆에 서면 난 명함도 못 내밀겠어. 벨 언니는 안목이 대단하고, 너는 영락없이 프랑스풍이야, 정말이라니까. 꽃은 그냥 늘어뜨려 둬. 그렇게 신경 쓰지 말고, 절대 넘어지지 않게 조심해.\" 샐리가 대답했다. 메그가 자기보다 더 예쁘다는 게 신경 쓰이지 않는 척하면서.\n\n그 주의를 마음에 단단히 새긴 채, 마거릿은 무사히 아래층으로 내려가, 모펏 집안 사람들과 일찍 온 손님 몇몇이 모여 있는 응접실로 미끄러져 들어갔다. 메그는 곧 멋진 옷에는 어떤 매력이 있어서, 특정 부류의 사람들을 끌어당기고 그들의 존경을 얻어 낸다는 것을 깨달았다. 전에는 메그에게 눈길조차 주지 않던 아가씨 여럿이 별안간 다정하게 굴었다. 다른 파티에서 메그를 빤히 쳐다보기만 하던 청년 여럿이 이제는 쳐다볼 뿐 아니라 소개해 달라고 청하고는, 온갖 시답잖지만 듣기 좋은 말을 늘어놓았다. 그리고 소파에 앉아 나머지 손님들을 품평하던 노부인 여럿이 관심 어린 표정으로 메그가 누구냐고 물었다. 메그는 모펏 부인이 그중 한 사람에게 대답하는 소리를 들었다.\n\n\"데이지 마치예요. 아버지가 군대 대령인데, 우리 동네에서 손꼽히는 집안이었답니다. 하지만 가세가 기울었지요, 아시잖아요. 로런스 집안과는 막역한 사이고요. 정말 사랑스러운 아이예요. 우리 네드가 아주 그 애한테 푹 빠졌답니다.\"\n\n\"어머나!\" 노부인이 메그를 다시 한번 살펴보려고 안경을 추켜올리며 말했다. 메그는 못 들은 척하려 애썼지만, 모펏 부인의 거짓말에 자못 충격을 받았다.\n\n그 \"어색한 기분\"은 가시지 않았지만, 메그는 자기가 어엿한 숙녀라는 새 역할을 연기하고 있다고 상상하며 그럭저럭 잘 해 나갔다. 비록 꽉 끼는 드레스 탓에 옆구리가 결리고, 치맛자락이 자꾸 발에 걸렸으며, 귀걸이가 튕겨 나가 잃어버리거나 부서질까 봐 줄곧 마음을 졸였지만 말이다. 메그가 부채를 살랑이며, 재치 있는 척 애쓰는 한 청년의 시시한 농담에 웃고 있을 때, 갑자기 웃음을 멈추고 당황한 표정을 지었다. 바로 맞은편에 로리가 보였기 때문이다. 로리는 노골적으로 놀란, 그리고 메그가 보기엔 못마땅해하는 표정으로 메그를 쳐다보고 있었다. 고개를 숙여 인사하고 미소를 짓긴 했지만, 그 정직한 눈빛 속의 무언가가 메그를 얼굴 붉히게 했고, 차라리 낡은 옷을 입고 있었으면 싶게 만들었다. 메그의 당혹감을 완성하려는 듯, 벨이 애니를 쿡 찌르는 게 보였고, 둘 다 메그에게서 로리에게로 눈길을 옮겼다. 로리는 평소와 달리 어린애같이 수줍어 보였는데, 메그는 그 모습을 보고 다행이라 여겼다.\n\n\"바보 같은 것들, 내 머릿속에 그런 생각을 집어넣다니! 신경 안 쓸 거야. 그런 것 때문에 내가 조금이라도 달라지진 않을 거야.\" 메그는 그렇게 생각하며, 사각사각 소리를 내며 방을 가로질러 가서 친구와 악수를 했다.\n\n\"네가 와 줘서 기뻐. 안 올까 봐 걱정했거든.\" 메그가 더없이 어른스러운 태도로 말했다.\n\n\"조가 가서 네가 어떻게 차려입었는지 보고 와서 얘기해 달라고 해서 왔어.\" 로리가 메그에게서 눈길을 돌린 채 대답했다. 메그의 어머니 같은 말투에 반쯤 웃긴 했지만.\n\n\"조한테 뭐라고 할 건데?\" 메그가 자기에 대한 로리의 생각이 궁금해서 물었다. 그러면서도 난생처음으로 로리가 거북하게 느껴졌다.\n\n\"널 몰라봤다고 할 거야. 너무 어른스러워지고 너답지가 않아서, 어쩐지 네가 무섭기까지 하다고.\" 로리가 장갑 단추를 만지작거리며 말했다.\n\n\"별소릴 다 하네! 애들이 재미 삼아 날 꾸며 준 건데, 난 꽤 마음에 들어. 조가 날 보면 눈이 휘둥그레지지 않을까?\" 메그가 말했다. 자기가 더 나아 보이는지 아닌지 로리 입에서 기어이 듣고야 말겠다는 심산으로.\n\n\"응, 그럴 것 같아.\" 로리가 진지하게 대답했다.\n\n\"이런 내가 마음에 안 들어?\" 메그가 물었다.\n\n\"응, 안 들어.\" 무뚝뚝한 대답이었다.\n\n\"왜?\" 불안한 목소리로.\n\n로리는 지진 머리며 드러난 어깨며 별나게 치장한 드레스를 흘끔 보았다. 그 표정은 평소의 예의라곤 눈곱만큼도 없는 그 대답보다도 메그를 더 무안하게 했다.\n\n\"난 호들갑이랑 깃털 장식이 싫어.\"\n\n자기보다 어린 녀석한테서 나온 말치고는 너무 지나쳤다. 메그는 발끈해서 한마디 내뱉으며 자리를 떴다.\n\n\"넌 내가 본 중에 제일 무례한 애야.\"\n\n잔뜩 기분이 상한 메그는 조용한 창가로 가서 섰다. 꽉 끼는 드레스 탓에 거북할 만큼 얼굴이 발갛게 달아올라서, 두 뺨을 식히려는 것이었다. 메그가 거기 서 있는데 링컨 소령이 지나갔고, 잠시 후 그가 자기 어머니에게 하는 말이 들려왔다.\n\n\"저 사람들이 저 어린 아가씨를 우스운 꼴로 만들어 놨네요. 어머니께 보여 드리고 싶었는데, 아주 망쳐 놨어요. 오늘 밤 저 애는 인형이나 다름없어요.\"\n\n\"어머, 어떡해!\" 메그가 한숨을 쉬었다. \"분별 있게 굴어서 내 옷을 입을걸. 그랬으면 남들 눈에 거슬리지도 않았을 테고, 나도 이렇게 거북하고 부끄럽지 않았을 텐데.\"\n\n메그는 서늘한 유리창에 이마를 기대고, 커튼에 반쯤 가려진 채 서 있었다. 가장 좋아하는 왈츠가 시작된 것도 아랑곳하지 않고서. 그러다 누군가 메그를 건드렸고, 돌아보니 뉘우치는 표정의 로리가 더없이 정중하게 고개를 숙이고 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"무례하게 굴어서 미안해. 나랑 춤추자.\"\n\n\"너한텐 너무 거슬릴 것 같은데.\" 메그가 화난 척하려 했지만, 영 그러지 못한 채 말했다.\n\n\"전혀 안 그래. 정말 추고 싶어 죽겠는걸. 자, 얌전히 굴게. 네 드레스는 마음에 안 들지만, 넌 정말이지—그냥 눈부셔.\" 로리가 두 손을 휘저었다. 말로는 자기의 감탄을 다 표현할 수 없다는 듯이.\n\n메그는 미소 지으며 마음을 풀었고, 박자를 맞추려고 둘이 서서 기다리는 사이에 속삭였다.\n\n\"내 치마에 걸려 넘어지지 않게 조심해. 정말 골칫거리야. 이런 걸 입다니 내가 바보였어.\"\n\n\"치마를 목에 둘러 매. 그럼 쓸모라도 있겠지.\" 로리가 작은 파란 부츠를 내려다보며 말했다. 그 부츠만은 마음에 드는 게 분명했다.\n\n둘은 날렵하고 우아하게 춤을 추기 시작했다. 집에서 연습한 적이 있어 호흡이 잘 맞았고, 사소한 다툼 끝에 어느 때보다 다정해진 이 명랑한 한 쌍이 즐겁게 빙글빙글 도는 모습은 보기에 흐뭇했다.\n\n\"로리, 부탁 하나만 들어줄래?\" 메그가 말했다. 숨이 차자 로리가 곁에 서서 부채질을 해 줄 때였는데, 메그는 이유를 밝히려 들지 않았지만 숨은 금세 차올랐던 것이다.\n\n\"안 들어줄 리가!\" 로리가 선뜻 대답했다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 내 드레스 얘기, 집에 가서 하지 말아 줘. 식구들은 이 장난을 이해 못 할 거고, 엄마는 걱정하실 테니까.\"\n\n\"그럼 왜 그런 짓을 했는데?\" 로리의 눈이 어찌나 빤히 그렇게 말하는지, 메그가 황급히 덧붙였다.\n\n\"내가 직접 식구들한테 다 얘기하고, 엄마한테 내가 얼마나 바보 같았는지 '고백'할 거야. 그래도 내가 직접 하는 게 낫겠어. 그러니까 너는 말 안 할 거지?\"\n\n\"맹세코 안 할게. 그런데 식구들이 물어보면 난 뭐라고 해?\"\n\n\"그냥 내가 꽤 예뻐 보였고, 즐겁게 놀았다고 해 줘.\"\n\n\"앞엣것은 진심으로 그렇게 말할게. 그런데 뒤엣것은 어쩌지? 넌 즐겁게 노는 것 같지가 않은데, 정말 그래?\" 로리가 어떤 표정으로 메그를 바라보았는데, 그 바람에 메그는 속삭이듯 대답하고 말았다.\n\n\"아니, 지금은 아냐. 날 못됐다고 생각하진 마. 그냥 좀 즐겁게 놀고 싶었을 뿐인데, 이런 식의 즐거움은 별 보람이 없다는 걸 알겠어. 이젠 슬슬 지겨워지고 있고.\"\n\n\"네드 모펏이 오네. 쟤가 뭘 바라는 거야?\" 로리가 검은 눈썹을 찌푸리며 말했다. 자기네 젊은 주인을 파티에 반가운 손님으로는 여기지 않는다는 듯이.\n\n\"춤을 세 번 추겠다고 이름을 적어 뒀거든. 그걸 추러 오나 봐. 정말 따분해!\" 메그가 나른한 척하며 말했고, 로리는 그 모습이 무척 우스웠다.\n\n로리는 저녁 식사 시간이 될 때까지 메그에게 다시 말을 걸지 않았다. 그러다 메그가 네드와 그의 친구 피셔와 함께 샴페인을 마시는 것을 보았다. 로리가 보기에 그 둘은 \"한 쌍의 바보처럼\" 굴고 있었다. 로리는 마치 집안에 대해 오빠 같은 권리를 느끼고 있어서, 보호자가 필요할 때면 언제든 그들 편에 서서 싸워 주려 했던 것이다.\n\n\"그렇게 많이 마시면 내일 머리가 깨질 듯이 아플 거야. 나라면 안 그러겠어, 메그. 너희 어머니가 싫어하시는 거 너도 알잖아.\" 네드가 메그의 잔을 다시 채우려고 돌아서고 피셔가 부채를 주우려고 몸을 굽힌 사이, 로리가 메그의 의자 위로 몸을 기울이며 속삭였다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 난 메그가 아니야. 난 온갖 미친 짓을 다 하는 '인형'이거든. 내일이면 내 '호들갑이랑 깃털 장식'은 치워 버리고, 다시 끔찍하게 착해질 거야.\" 메그가 짐짓 가볍게 웃으며 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 차라리 내일이 와 버렸으면 좋겠네.\" 로리가 중얼거리며 자리를 떴다. 메그에게서 보이는 변화가 영 못마땅했던 것이다.\n\n메그는 다른 소녀들이 하는 대로 춤추고 시시덕거리고 재잘대며 키득거렸다. 저녁 식사 뒤에는 독일식 춤에 나섰다가 우왕좌왕하며 헤맸고, 긴 치마로 춤 상대를 하마터면 넘어뜨릴 뻔했으며, 어찌나 분방하게 까불었던지 곁에서 지켜보던 로리가 한마디 해야겠다고 마음먹을 정도였다. 하지만 그럴 기회는 오지 않았다. 메그가 작별 인사를 하러 올 때까지 로리를 피해 다녔기 때문이다.\n\n\"잊지 마!\" 메그가 미소 지으려 애쓰며 말했다. 머리가 깨질 듯한 두통이 벌써 시작된 터였다.\n\n\"죽음처럼 침묵하리(Silence à la mort : 프랑스어 : 옮긴이).\" 로리가 멜로드라마처럼 과장된 몸짓으로 대답하고는 자리를 떴다.\n\n이 자그마한 곁다리 연극이 애니의 호기심을 자극했지만, 메그는 너무 지쳐서 수다를 떨 기운도 없이 잠자리에 들었다. 마치 가장무도회에라도 다녀온 듯한 기분이었고, 기대했던 만큼 즐겁지도 않았다. 이튿날 메그는 종일 앓았고, 토요일에 집으로 돌아왔다. 두 주간의 즐거움에 완전히 녹초가 된 채, \"호사의 무릎에 앉아\" 지낼 만큼 지냈다는 기분으로.\n\n\"조용히 지내면서 늘 손님 노릇 하지 않아도 되니, 정말 좋다. 집은 으리으리하진 않아도, 역시 좋은 곳이야.\" 일요일 저녁, 어머니와 조와 함께 앉아 있던 메그가 편안한 표정으로 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n\"네 입에서 그런 말을 들으니 기쁘구나, 얘야. 그렇게 좋은 데서 지내다 오면 집이 칙칙하고 초라해 보일까 봐 걱정했거든.\" 어머니가 대답했다. 어머니는 그날 메그에게 여러 번 근심 어린 눈길을 보냈다. 어머니의 눈은 아이들 얼굴에 나타난 사소한 변화도 재빨리 알아채는 법이니까.\n\n메그는 자기 모험담을 신나게 들려주었고, 얼마나 즐거웠는지 거듭 이야기했다. 하지만 무언가가 여전히 그 마음을 짓누르는 듯했고, 어린 동생들이 잠자리에 든 뒤에는 골똘히 난롯불을 바라보며 말수가 적어지고 근심스러운 표정을 지었다. 시계가 아홉 시를 치고 조가 잘 시간이라고 하자, 메그가 별안간 의자에서 일어나 베스의 발판을 가져다가 어머니 무릎에 팔꿈치를 괴고는 용기를 내어 말했다.\n\n\"엄마, 저 '고백'하고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"그럴 줄 알았다. 무슨 일이니, 얘야?\"\n\n\"난 나가 있을까?\" 조가 눈치껏 물었다.\n\n\"무슨 소리야, 당연히 아니지. 내가 너한테 뭐든 다 얘기하잖아? 동생들 앞에서 말하기는 부끄러웠지만, 내가 모펏 집에서 저지른 끔찍한 일들을 네가 다 알았으면 좋겠어.\"\n\n\"각오는 돼 있단다.\" 마치 부인이 미소를 지으면서도 조금 걱정스러운 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"그 사람들이 날 차려입혀 줬다고는 말씀드렸지만, 분을 바르고 몸을 졸라매고 머리를 지져서, 절 패션 화보 속 사람처럼 만들어 놨다는 건 말 안 했어요. 로리는 제가 단정치 못하다고 생각했어요. 입 밖에 내진 않았지만 분명 그랬어요. 게다가 어떤 남자는 절 '인형'이라고 불렀고요. 어리석은 짓인 줄 알면서도, 그 사람들이 절 추켜세우며 미인이라느니 온갖 허튼소리를 늘어놓는 통에, 그만 절 우스운 꼴로 만들도록 내버려 뒀지 뭐예요.\"\n\n\"그게 다야?\" 조가 물었다. 마치 부인은 예쁜 딸의 풀 죽은 얼굴을 말없이 바라보며, 차마 그 작은 어리석음들을 나무랄 마음이 들지 않았던 것이다.\n\n\"아뇨. 샴페인을 마시고, 까불고, 시시덕거리려 들기까지 했어요. 정말이지 형편없었죠.\" 메그가 자책하며 말했다.\n\n\"더 있는 것 같은데.\" 마치 부인이 부드러운 뺨을 쓰다듬자, 메그가 천천히 대답하면서 그 뺨이 갑자기 발그레해졌다.\n\n\"네. 정말 어리석은 일이지만, 그래도 말씀드리고 싶어요. 사람들이 우리랑 로리에 대해 그런 말을 하고 그런 생각을 하는 게 너무 싫거든요.\"\n\n그러고 나서 메그는 모펏 집에서 들은 갖가지 험담을 들려주었다. 메그가 말하는 동안, 조는 어머니가 입술을 꼭 다무는 것을 보았다. 그런 생각이 메그의 순진한 마음에 심겼다는 게 못마땅하다는 듯이.\n\n\"세상에, 그렇게 터무니없는 헛소리는 처음 들어 봐.\" 조가 분개하여 외쳤다. \"왜 그 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 그렇다고 쏘아붙이지 않았어?\"\n\n\"그럴 수가 없었어. 너무 난처했거든. 처음엔 듣지 않을 수가 없었고, 그다음엔 어찌나 화가 나고 부끄럽던지, 자리를 떠야 한다는 생각조차 못 했어.\"\n\n\"애니 모펏을 만나기만 해 봐. 그런 어처구니없는 소리를 어떻게 잠재우는지 보여 줄 테니까. '계획'이라니, 그리고 로리가 부자라서, 나중에 우리 중 누구랑 결혼할지도 모른다고 걔한테 잘해 준다니! 그 바보 같은 것들이 우리 가난한 애들에 대해 떠드는 소릴 로리한테 일러 주면, 걔가 얼마나 깔깔거릴까?\" 조가 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 다시 생각해 보니 그게 그럴듯한 농담으로 여겨졌던 모양이다.\n\n\"로리한테 말하면, 너 평생 용서 안 해! 그러면 안 되죠, 엄마?\" 메그가 괴로운 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"안 되고말고. 그런 어리석은 험담은 두 번 다시 입에 올리지 말고, 되도록 빨리 잊어버리렴.\" 마치 부인이 진지하게 말했다. \"내가 잘 알지도 못하는 사람들 틈에 너를 보낸 게 무척 경솔했구나. 친절한 사람들이긴 하겠지만, 세속적이고, 교양 없고, 젊은이들에 대해 그런 천박한 생각으로 가득 차 있으니. 이번 방문이 너한테 끼쳤을지 모를 해악이, 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 안타깝구나, 메그.\"\n\"안타까워하지 마세요. 그것 때문에 상처받지 않을 거예요. 나쁜 건 다 잊어버리고 좋은 것만 기억할래요. 정말 즐거운 일도 많았으니까, 보내 주셔서 정말 고마워요. 감상에 젖거나 불만스러워하지 않을 거예요, 엄마. 제가 어리석은 어린애라는 거 잘 알아요. 그러니 혼자 앞가림할 수 있을 만큼 자랄 때까지 엄마 곁에 있을래요. 하지만 칭찬받고 우러름받는 건 역시 기분 좋은 일이라, 솔직히 그게 좋다는 말은 안 할 수가 없네요.\" 메그가 그렇게 고백하면서 반쯤 부끄러운 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"그건 지극히 자연스러운 일이고, 그 좋아하는 마음이 집착이 되어 어리석거나 처녀답지 못한 짓을 하게 만들지만 않는다면 전혀 해로울 게 없단다. 가질 만한 가치가 있는 칭찬이 무엇인지 알아보고 귀히 여기는 법을 배우렴. 그리고 예쁠 뿐 아니라 겸손해서 훌륭한 사람들의 감탄을 자아내는 사람이 되거라, 메그.\"\n마거릿이 잠시 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있는 동안, 조는 두 손을 등 뒤로 한 채 흥미로우면서도 약간 어리둥절한 표정으로 서 있었다. 메그가 얼굴을 붉히며 감탄이니 연인이니 그런 것들에 대해 이야기하는 모습은 처음 보았으니까. 조는 지난 두 주 동안 언니가 놀랄 만큼 어른이 되어, 자기는 따라갈 수 없는 세계로 멀어져 가고 있는 듯한 기분이 들었다.\n\n\"엄마, 엄마도 모펏 부인이 말한 그런 '계획'이 있으세요?\" 메그가 수줍게 물었다.\n\"그럼, 얘야, 아주 많단다. 어느 어머니나 그렇지. 다만 내 계획은 모펏 부인의 것과는 좀 다를 거다. 그중 몇 가지를 너희한테 말해 주마. 매우 중대한 문제에 대해, 너의 그 낭만적인 작은 머리와 마음을 바로잡아 줄 말 한마디를 건넬 때가 왔으니 말이다. 너는 아직 어리지만, 내 말을 못 알아들을 만큼 어리지는 않아. 게다가 너 같은 여자아이한테 그런 이야기를 들려주기에는 어머니의 입이 가장 알맞은 법이지. 조, 네 차례도 언젠가 올 테니, 내 '계획'을 잘 들어 두렴. 그리고 그게 좋은 계획이라면, 그걸 이루도록 나를 도와주고.\"\n\n조는 의자 팔걸이에 가서 앉았다. 자기들이 무슨 무척 엄숙한 일에라도 동참하려는 거라고 여기는 표정으로. 마치 부인은 두 딸의 손을 하나씩 잡고, 두 어린 얼굴을 애틋하게 바라보며, 진지하면서도 명랑한 특유의 말투로 말했다.\n\"나는 내 딸들이 아름답고, 교양 있고, 착하기를 바란단다. 사랑받고, 존경받기를 바라지. 행복한 젊은 시절을 보내고, 좋은 사람과 현명하게 짝을 이루며, 하느님이 보시기에 합당한 만큼의 근심과 슬픔만 겪으면서, 쓸모 있고 즐거운 삶을 살기를 바라. 좋은 남자에게 사랑받고 선택받는 것은 여자에게 일어날 수 있는 가장 좋고 가장 달콤한 일이란다. 그러니 내 딸들도 이 아름다운 경험을 알게 되길 진심으로 바라지. 그걸 생각하는 건 자연스러운 일이야, 메그. 그걸 바라며 기다리는 건 옳은 일이고, 그걸 위해 준비하는 건 현명한 일이지. 그래야 행복한 때가 왔을 때, 그 책임을 감당할 준비가 되어 있고 그 기쁨을 누릴 자격이 있다고 느낄 수 있을 테니까. 얘들아, 나는 너희를 위해 야심을 품고 있단다. 하지만 너희가 세상에서 돋보이게 하려는 야심은 아니야. 단지 부자라는 이유로 부자와 결혼하거나, 사랑이 없어 집이라고 할 수도 없는 으리으리한 저택을 차지하려는 게 아니란다. 돈은 꼭 필요하고 귀한 것이며, 잘 쓰면 고귀한 것이기도 해. 하지만 나는 너희가 돈을 좇아야 할 첫째가는, 또는 유일한 보상이라고 여기는 건 결코 바라지 않아. 자존심도 평온함도 없이 왕좌에 앉은 여왕이 되느니, 차라리 행복하고 사랑받고 만족하며 사는 가난한 사람의 아내가 되는 너희를 보는 편이 더 좋겠다.\"\n\n\"가난한 여자들은 자기를 내세우지 않으면 가망이 없다고, 벨 언니가 그러던데요.\" 메그가 한숨을 쉬었다.\n\"그럼 우린 노처녀가 되면 되지.\" 조가 씩씩하게 말했다.\n\n\"옳은 말이다, 조. 불행한 아내가 되거나, 남편감을 찾아 이리저리 뛰어다니는 처녀답지 못한 여자가 되느니, 행복한 노처녀가 되는 편이 낫지.\" 마치 부인이 단호하게 말했다. \"걱정하지 마라, 메그. 가난이 진실한 연인을 물러서게 하는 일은 드무니까. 내가 아는 가장 훌륭하고 가장 존경받는 여자들 중에는 가난한 처녀였던 이들도 있었지만, 사랑받을 만한 사람들이라 노처녀로 남도록 내버려 두질 않았단다. 이런 일들은 시간에 맡겨 두렴. 이 집을 행복하게 만들어서, 너희만의 가정을 꾸릴 기회가 온다면 그럴 자격을 갖추고, 그렇지 않더라도 여기서 만족하며 지낼 수 있도록 하렴. 한 가지만 기억해 다오, 얘들아. 어머니는 언제나 너희의 비밀을 들어 줄 준비가 되어 있고, 아버지는 너희의 친구가 되어 줄 준비가 되어 있단다. 그리고 우리 두 사람은, 결혼을 하든 안 하든, 우리 딸들이 우리 삶의 자랑이자 위안이 되어 주리라 믿고 또 바란단다.\"\n\"그럴게요, 엄마, 꼭 그럴게요!\" 어머니가 잘 자라고 인사하자, 두 딸이 진심을 다해 외쳤다.\n\n10장\nP. C.와 P. O.\n\n봄이 다가오자 새로운 놀이들이 유행하기 시작했고, 길어진 낮 덕분에 온갖 일과 놀이를 즐길 긴 오후가 생겼다. 정원을 손봐야 했는데, 자매들은 저마다 그 작은 땅의 한 구획씩을 맡아 마음대로 가꾸기로 했다. 해나는 늘 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"저 정원들이 중국에 있어도, 어느 게 누구 건지 단박에 알아볼 거예요.\" 정말 그럴 만했다. 소녀들의 취향이 각자의 성격만큼이나 제각각이었으니까. 메그의 정원에는 장미와 헬리오트로프, 도금양, 그리고 작은 오렌지나무가 있었다. 조의 화단은 두 해 연속으로 같은 적이 없었다. 늘 이런저런 시도를 했기 때문이다. 올해는 해바라기밭으로 꾸밀 작정이었는데, 그 명랑하고 위로 쭉쭉 뻗는 식물의 씨앗으로 \"코클탑 아주머니\"와 그 병아리 식구들을 먹일 참이었다. 베스의 정원에는 옛날식의 향기로운 꽃들이 있었다. 스위트피와 물푸레향, 참제비고깔, 패랭이꽃, 팬지, 그리고 사철쑥. 거기에 새를 위한 별꽃과 고양이를 위한 개박하도 있었다. 에이미의 정원에는 정자가 하나 있었는데, 다소 작고 집게벌레가 들끓긴 해도 보기에는 무척 예뻤다. 인동덩굴과 나팔꽃이 색색의 뿔과 종 모양 꽃을 우아한 화환처럼 온통 드리우고 있었고, 키 큰 흰 백합과 섬세한 고사리, 그리고 거기서 기꺼이 꽃피우려는 화사하고 그림 같은 식물들도 잔뜩 있었다.\n날씨 좋은 날에는 정원 가꾸기와 산책, 강에서 배 젓기, 꽃 찾기로 시간을 보냈다. 비 오는 날에는 집 안에서 즐기는 놀이가 있었는데, 어떤 건 오래된 것이고 어떤 건 새것이었지만, 하나같이 어느 정도 독창적이었다. 그중 하나가 \"P. C.\"였다. 비밀 결사가 유행이었던 터라, 하나쯤 만드는 게 마땅하다고 여겨졌고, 소녀들이 모두 디킨스를 흠모했기에, 자기들을 피크위크 클럽이라고 불렀다. 몇 번 거른 적은 있지만, 그들은 이 모임을 일 년이나 이어 왔고, 매주 토요일 저녁이면 커다란 다락방에 모였다. 그때의 의식은 다음과 같았다. 탁자 앞에 의자 세 개를 한 줄로 놓고, 탁자 위에는 등불 하나와 더불어 저마다 색깔이 다른 큼직한 \"P. C.\" 글자가 박힌 흰 배지 네 개, 그리고 다들 무언가 한 편씩 기고하는 주간 신문 \"피크위크 문집\"을 올려 두었다. 펜과 잉크에 흠뻑 빠져 사는 조가 편집장이었다. 일곱 시가 되면 회원 네 명이 클럽 방으로 올라가, 배지를 머리에 둘러 묶고, 무척 엄숙하게 자리에 앉았다. 메그는 맏이라서 새뮤얼 피크위크였고, 조는 문학적인 기질이 있어서 오거스터스 스노드그래스였으며, 베스는 통통하고 발그레해서 트레이시 텁먼, 그리고 늘 안 되는 일을 해 보려고 애쓰는 에이미는 너새니얼 윙클이었다. 회장인 피크위크가 신문을 낭독했는데, 거기에는 창작 이야기와 시, 동네 소식, 우스운 광고, 그리고 서로의 결점과 부족한 점을 너그럽게 일깨워 주는 충고가 가득했다.\n\n한번은 피크위크 씨가 알이 없는 안경을 쓰고, 탁자를 톡톡 두드리고, 헛기침을 한 다음, 의자를 뒤로 젖히고 앉아 있던 스노드그래스 씨를 똑바로 노려보았다. 그가 자세를 바로 할 때까지 그러고 있다가, 낭독을 시작했다.\n피크위크 문집\n\n18××년 5월 20일\n시인의 모퉁이\n\n기념일 송시\n오늘 밤 우리 다시 모여 기리네\n\n배지와 엄숙한 의식으로,\n우리의 쉰두 번째 기념일을,\n\n# THE P. C. AND P. O.\n\n이 피크위크 홀에서.\n우리 모두 더없이 건강하게 모였고,\n\n우리 작은 무리에서 빠진 이 하나 없네.\n다시금 낯익은 얼굴들을 마주하고,\n\n정다운 손을 마주 잡네.\n언제나 제자리를 지키는 우리 피크위크를,\n\n우리는 경의를 담아 맞이하네.\n코에 안경을 걸친 채, 그가\n\n알차게 채운 우리 주간지를 읽어 내릴 때.\n\n# ANNIVERSARY ODE.\n\n비록 그가 감기로 고생하고 있어도,\n우리는 그의 말을 들어 기쁘니,\n\n그의 입에서 지혜로운 말씀이 떨어지는 까닭에,\n곤돌라가 끽끽거리며 삐걱대도.\n\n키 큰 육 척의 스노드그래스가 우뚝 솟아오르네,\n코끼리처럼 우아한 자태로,\n\n갈색의 유쾌한 얼굴로\n좌중을 환히 비추네.\n\n그의 눈에는 시의 불꽃이 타오르고,\n제 운명과 맞서 싸우는도다.\n\n보라, 그 이마에 서린 야망을,\n그리고 코에 묻은 얼룩을!\n\n다음으로 우리의 온화한 터프먼이 오네,\n장밋빛으로 통통하고 사랑스러운 그가.\n\n익살에 웃음을 참지 못해 컥컥대다가\n의자에서 굴러떨어지고 마는도다.\n\n깔끔한 꼬마 윙클도 여기 있네,\n머리카락 한 올 흐트러짐 없이,\n\n예의범절의 본보기 같지만,\n세수하기는 끔찍이도 싫어하지.\n\n# A TALE OF VENICE.\n\n한 해가 지나도, 우리는 여전히 모여\n농담하고 웃고 글을 읽으며,\n\n영광으로 이끄는\n문학의 길을 걸어가네.\n\n우리의 신문이여 오래도록 번창하라,\n우리의 클럽이 깨지지 않기를,\n\n그리고 다가오는 세월이 그 축복을\n유익하고 흥겨운 \"피클럽\"에 쏟아붓기를.\n\nA. 스노드그래스.\n가면을 쓴 결혼식.\n\n베네치아 이야기.\n곤돌라가 줄지어 대리석 계단으로 미끄러져 들어와, 그 사랑스러운 짐을 부려 놓으니, 데 아델롱 백작의 위풍당당한 홀을 가득 메운 화려한 무리는 더욱 불어났다. 기사와 귀부인, 요정과 시동, 수도사와 꽃 파는 소녀가 모두 흥겹게 어우러져 춤을 추었다. 감미로운 목소리와 풍성한 선율이 공중에 가득했고, 그렇게 환희와 음악 속에서 가장무도회는 계속되었다.\n\n\"전하, 오늘 밤 비올라 아가씨를 보셨습니까?\" 늠름한 음유시인이 자기 팔에 기대어 홀을 흘러내리듯 거니는 요정 여왕에게 물었다.\n\"보았네. 그토록 슬픈데도 어찌 그리 사랑스러운지! 옷차림도 잘 골랐구나. 일주일 후면 안토니오 백작과 혼인하니까. 그녀가 그토록 열렬히 미워하는 사람이건만.\"\n\n\"맹세코, 그가 부럽습니다. 저기 그가 오는군요, 검은 가면만 빼면 신랑처럼 차려입었습니다. 저 가면을 벗으면 그가 그 아리따운 처녀를 어떻게 바라보는지 알게 되겠지요. 그녀의 매정한 아버지가 손을 내어 준다 해도, 그는 그녀의 마음을 얻지 못하니까요.\" 음유시인이 대답했다.\n\"소문에는 그녀가 자기 발길을 좇아다니는 젊은 영국 화가를 사랑한다는군요. 늙은 백작이 그를 내쳤다고 합니다.\" 귀부인이 말하며, 두 사람은 춤판에 끼어들었다.\n\n흥겨움이 절정에 이르렀을 때 한 사제가 나타나, 젊은 한 쌍을 자줏빛 벨벳이 드리워진 골방으로 데려가더니, 무릎을 꿇으라고 손짓했다. 흥겹던 무리에 순식간에 정적이 내려앉았다. 분수가 부서지는 소리와 달빛 속에 잠든 오렌지 숲이 사각이는 소리 외에는 그 어떤 소리도 정적을 깨지 못했으니, 데 아델롱 백작이 이렇게 말하는 동안이었다.\n\n\"신사 숙녀 여러분, 제 딸의 혼례를 지켜봐 주십사 이리로 여러분을 모은 술책을 용서하시오. 신부님, 의식을 기다리고 있습니다.\"\n모든 눈이 신혼부부 쪽으로 쏠렸고, 무리 사이로 나직한 경탄의 웅성거림이 번졌다. 신부도 신랑도 가면을 벗지 않았기 때문이다. 호기심과 의아함이 모든 가슴을 사로잡았으나, 신성한 의식이 끝날 때까지 경의가 모든 입을 억눌렀다. 그러다 의식이 끝나자 애가 단 구경꾼들이 백작 주위로 모여들어 해명을 요구했다.\n\n\"내가 알기만 한다면 기꺼이 말해 주겠소. 허나 나는 그저 그것이 수줍은 내 딸 비올라의 변덕이었고, 내가 거기에 따라 주었다는 것밖에 모르오. 자, 얘들아, 이제 연극을 끝내자꾸나. 가면을 벗고 내 축복을 받으렴.\"\n그러나 둘 중 누구도 무릎을 꿇지 않았다. 젊은 신랑이, 가면이 떨어져 화가 연인 페르디난드 디브로의 고결한 얼굴이 드러나는 가운데, 듣는 이들 모두를 놀라게 하는 어조로 대답했기 때문이다. 그리고 이제 영국 백작의 별 모양 훈장이 번쩍이는 그 가슴에 기댄 것은, 기쁨과 아름다움으로 빛나는 사랑스러운 비올라였다.\n\n\"백작이시여, 당신은 제가 안토니오 백작만큼 높은 가문과 막대한 재산을 자랑할 수 있을 때 당신의 딸을 데려가라고 비웃으며 말씀하셨지요. 저는 그 이상을 할 수 있습니다. 디브로 앤드 디비어 백작이 자신의 유서 깊은 가문과 무한한 재산을 내어 주고 이 아리따운 여인, 이제는 제 아내가 된 사람의 사랑하는 손을 얻겠다는데, 당신의 야심 찬 영혼인들 어찌 거절할 수 있겠습니까.\"\n백작은 돌로 변한 사람처럼 우뚝 서 있었다. 페르디난드는 어리둥절한 무리 쪽으로 돌아서서, 승리의 흥겨운 미소를 지으며 덧붙였다. \"늠름한 벗들이여, 그대들에게 바라노니, 그대들의 구애도 나의 것처럼 무르익기를. 그리고 이 가면을 쓴 결혼식으로 내가 그러했듯이, 그대들 모두가 이토록 아리따운 신부를 얻게 되기를.\"\n\nS. 픽윅.\n\n피클럽은 어째서 바벨탑과 같은가? 제멋대로 구는 회원들로 가득하기 때문이다.\n\n호박 한 통의 역사.\n\n# THE HISTORY OF A SQUASH.\n\n옛날 옛적에 한 농부가 자기 밭에 작은 씨앗 하나를 심었는데, 얼마 지나자 그것이 싹을 틔워 덩굴이 되었고 호박을 잔뜩 맺었다. 시월 어느 날, 호박이 다 익자 농부는 하나를 따서 장에 가져갔다. 어느 식료품 장수가 그것을 사다 가게에 놓아두었다. 바로 그날 아침, 갈색 모자에 파란 원피스를 입고, 둥근 얼굴에 들창코를 한 어린 소녀가 와서 자기 엄마를 위해 그것을 샀다. 소녀는 호박을 낑낑대며 집으로 날라다 잘라서 큰 솥에 삶았다. 그중 일부는 소금과 버터를 넣고 으깨어 저녁상에 올렸고, 나머지에는 우유 한 파인트와 달걀 두 개, 설탕 네 숟갈, 육두구, 그리고 크래커를 좀 넣어, 깊은 접시에 담아 노릇노릇 먹음직스러워질 때까지 구웠다. 그리고 이튿날 그것은 마치라는 가족이 먹어 치웠다.\nT. 터프먼.\n\n픽윅 씨, 귀하께,\n\n저는 죄에 관한 문제로 귀하께 말씀드립니다 제가 말하는 죄인은 윙클이라는 사람으로 웃어서 클럽에 말썽을 일으키고 가끔은 이 훌륭한 신문에 제 글을 안 쓰려 합니다 부디 그의 나쁜 짓을 용서하시고 프랑스 우화를 보내도록 허락해 주시기 바랍니다 왜냐하면 그는 할 공부가 너무 많고 머리에 든 것이 없어 제 머리로 글을 못 짓기 때문입니다 앞으로는 시간의 발목을 잡아 일을 좀 준비해 보겠습니다 그러면 다 콤미 라 포 할 것인데 그것은 다 괜찮다는 뜻입니다 거의 등교 시간이라 바쁩니다\n존경을 담아 올림, N. 윙클.\n\n(위의 글은 지난날의 비행을 사내답고 보기 좋게 인정한 것이다. 우리 젊은 벗이 구두점을 공부한다면 더욱 좋겠다.)\n슬픈 사고.\n\n지난 금요일, 우리는 지하실에서 일어난 격렬한 충격에 깜짝 놀랐고, 뒤이어 비탄에 잠긴 비명이 들려왔다. 한 무리가 되어 지하실로 달려간 우리는, 사랑하는 우리 회장님이 집안일에 쓸 장작을 가지러 갔다가 발이 걸려 넘어져 바닥에 엎어진 것을 발견했다. 완전한 폐허의 장면이 우리 눈앞에 펼쳐졌으니, 픽윅 씨는 넘어지면서 머리와 어깨를 물통에 처박았고, 무른 비누 한 통을 사내다운 몸 위에 엎어 버렸으며, 옷을 심하게 찢어 놓았기 때문이다. 이 위태로운 처지에서 끌어내어진 후, 그가 여러 군데 멍든 것 말고는 다친 데가 없음이 밝혀졌고, 덧붙여 기쁘게 알리노니, 지금은 잘 회복하고 있다.\n편집장.\n\n# A SAD ACCIDENT.\n\n공공의 사별.\n우리는 소중한 벗, 스노볼 팻 포 부인의 갑작스럽고 불가사의한 실종을 기록해야 하는 가슴 아픈 의무를 진다. 이 사랑스럽고 정 깊은 고양이는 따뜻하고 흠모하는 벗들로 이루어진 커다란 모임의 귀염둥이였다. 그 미모는 모든 눈길을 사로잡았고, 그 우아함과 미덕은 모든 가슴에 사랑받았으며, 그 상실은 온 공동체가 깊이 느끼는 바이다.\n\n마지막으로 목격되었을 때, 그녀는 대문가에 앉아 푸줏간 수레를 지켜보고 있었다. 그 매력에 홀린 어느 악당이 비열하게 그녀를 훔쳐 간 것이 아닌가 우려된다. 몇 주가 지났으나 그녀의 흔적은 발견되지 않았으니, 우리는 모든 희망을 접고, 그녀의 바구니에 검은 리본을 매고, 밥그릇을 치워 두고, 영영 잃어버린 이를 위하듯 그녀를 위해 운다.\n마음을 함께하는 벗이 다음의 보석 같은 시를 보내왔다.\n\nS. B. 팻 포를 위한\n애가.\n\n# FOR S. B. PAT PAW.\n\n우리는 우리의 작은 귀염둥이를 잃고 슬퍼하며,\n그 가엾은 운명을 두고 한숨짓네,\n\n다시는 난롯가에 앉지 못할 테고,\n낡은 푸른 대문가에서 놀지 못할 테니.\n\n그 새끼가 잠든 작은 무덤은\n밤나무 아래 있건만,\n\n그녀의 무덤 위에서는 울 수가 없네,\n그것이 어디 있는지 알 수 없으니.\n\n텅 빈 잠자리, 버려진 공은\n다시는 그녀를 보지 못하리,\n\n응접실 문가에서는\n상냥한 발톱질도, 사랑스러운 가르랑 소리도 들리지 않네.\n\n다른 고양이가 그녀의 쥐를 쫓아 들어왔으니,\n얼굴이 더러운 고양이라네.\n\n허나 그것은 우리 귀염둥이처럼 사냥하지 못하고,\n그 사뿐한 우아함으로 놀지도 못하네.\n\n그 살금대는 발이 바로 그 홀을 밟고 다니네,\n스노볼이 놀곤 하던 그곳을.\n\n허나 그것은 우리 귀염둥이가 그토록\n늠름하게 쫓아 버린 개들에게 침이나 뱉을 뿐.\n\n쓸모 있고 온순하며 제 딴엔 애를 쓰지만,\n보기에 아름답지는 못하네.\n\n그러니 우리는 그것에게 네 자리를 줄 수 없단다, 얘야,\n우리가 너를 떠받들 듯 그것을 떠받들 수도 없단다.\n\nA. S.\n광고.\n\n박식한 강성 여성 강연자, 오랜시 블러기지 양이 그 유명한 강연 \"여성과 그 지위\"를, 다음 토요일 저녁, 평소의 공연이 끝난 후, 픽윅 홀에서 행할 예정입니다.\n부엌터에서 매주 모임이 열려 젊은 숙녀들에게 요리하는 법을 가르칠 것입니다. 해나 브라운이 사회를 맡을 것이며, 누구나 참석을 환영합니다.\n\n쓰레받기 협회가 다음 수요일에 모여, 클럽 회관 위층에서 행진을 합니다. 모든 회원은 제복 차림으로 나와 아홉 시 정각에 빗자루를 어깨에 멜 것.\n베스 바운서 부인이 다음 주에 인형 모자 신상품을 선보입니다. 최신 파리 유행품이 도착했으며, 주문을 정중히 받습니다.\n\n새 연극이 몇 주 안에 반빌 극장에서 막을 올릴 것이니, 미국 무대에서 일찍이 본 그 무엇도 능가할 것입니다. \"그리스인 노예, 또는 복수자 콘스탄틴\"이 이 짜릿한 드라마의 제목입니다!!!\n귀띔.\n\nS. P.가 손에 비누를 그렇게 많이 쓰지 않는다면, 늘 아침 식사에 늦지는 않을 것이다. A. S.는 길에서 휘파람을 불지 말 것을 부탁받는다. T. T.는 에이미의 냅킨을 잊지 말기를 바란다. N. W.는 자기 옷에 주름이 아홉 개 안 잡혔다고 안달하면 안 된다.\n\n주간 보고.\n메그 — 양호.\n\n조 — 불량.\n베스 — 매우 양호.\n\n에이미 — 보통.\n회장이 신문 낭독을 마치자(독자 여러분께 굳이 알려 드리거니와, 이것은 옛날 옛적 실제 소녀들이 쓴 진짜 신문을 충실히 옮긴 것이다), 한바탕 박수가 뒤따랐고, 이어 스노드그래스 씨가 일어나 한 가지 제안을 했다.\n\n\"회장님, 그리고 신사 여러분.\" 그녀는 의회식 자세와 어조를 취하며 운을 뗐다. \"저는 새 회원의 가입을 제안하고자 합니다. 그 영예를 받을 자격이 충분하고, 그 영예에 깊이 감사할 것이며, 클럽의 활기와 신문의 문학적 가치에 엄청나게 보탬이 될 데다, 한없이 유쾌하고 좋은 사람입니다. 저는 시어도어 로런스 씨를 피클럽의 명예 회원으로 추천합니다. 자, 어서요, 받아들여 주세요.\"\n조의 갑작스러운 어조 변화에 소녀들은 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그러나 다들 다소 불안한 기색이었고, 스노드그래스가 자리에 앉을 때까지 아무도 한마디 하지 않았다.\n\n\"표결에 부치겠습니다.\" 회장이 말했다. \"이 동의에 찬성하시는 분은 '찬성'이라고 말하여 의사를 표시해 주세요.\"\n\n스노드그래스가 큰 소리로 응답했고, 뒤이어 모두를 놀라게 하며 베스가 머뭇머뭇 응답했다.\n\"반대하시는 분은 '반대'라고 하세요.\"\n\n메그와 에이미가 반대 의견이었다. 윙클 씨가 일어나 매우 우아하게 말했다. \"우리는 사내아이는 누구도 원치 않아요. 걔들은 농담이나 하고 이리저리 까불기만 하잖아요. 이건 숙녀들의 클럽이고, 우리는 사적이고 품위 있게 지내고 싶다고요.\"\n\n\"걔가 우리 신문을 비웃고, 나중에 우리를 놀려 댈까 봐 걱정이에요.\" 픽윅이 말하며, 미심쩍을 때면 늘 그러듯 이마에 드리운 작은 곱슬머리를 잡아당겼다.\n스노드그래스가 매우 진지하게 일어섰다. \"회장님, 신사로서 맹세하건대, 로리는 절대 그런 짓은 안 할 겁니다. 걔는 글쓰기를 좋아하고, 우리 기고문에 격조를 더해 줄 거고, 우리가 감상적으로 흐르지 않게 막아 줄 거예요, 아시겠어요? 우리는 걔를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 게 거의 없는데, 걔는 우리를 위해 그토록 많은 걸 해 주잖아요. 그러니 최소한 여기 자리 하나 내주고, 걔가 오면 반갑게 맞아 주는 게 우리가 할 수 있는 일이라고 생각해요.\"\n\n베푼 은혜를 이렇게 교묘히 들먹이자 터프먼이 자리에서 일어났는데, 마음을 단단히 정한 듯한 표정이었다.\n\"그래요, 우리가 비록 겁이 난다 해도, 그렇게 해야 해요. 걔가 와도 좋다고 말하겠어요. 걔가 원하면 그 할아버지도요.\"\n\n베스의 이 활기찬 외침은 클럽을 짜릿하게 했고, 조는 찬성의 뜻으로 악수하려고 자리에서 일어났다. \"자 그럼, 다시 표결합시다. 다들 우리 로리라는 거 기억하고, '찬성!'이라고 외쳐요!\" 스노드그래스가 흥분하여 소리쳤다.\n\n\"찬성! 찬성! 찬성!\" 세 목소리가 한꺼번에 대답했다.\n\n\"좋아요! 고마워요! 자, 윙클이 그 특유의 말투로 짚었듯 '시간의 발목을 잡는 것'만 한 게 없으니, 새 회원을 소개하게 해 주세요.\" 그러더니 나머지 클럽 회원들이 경악하는 가운데, 조가 벽장 문을 활짝 열어젖히자, 로리가 헝겊 자루 위에 앉아 웃음을 꾹 참느라 얼굴이 발그레해진 채 눈을 반짝이고 있는 것이 드러났다.\n\n\"이 장난꾸러기! 이 배신자! 조, 어떻게 그럴 수 있어?\" 세 소녀가 외쳤고, 그러는 사이 스노드그래스는 의기양양하게 자기 벗을 앞으로 데리고 나와, 의자와 배지를 둘 다 마련해 순식간에 그를 자리에 앉혔다.\n\n\"이 두 악당의 태연함이란 놀랍기 짝이 없군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 무섭게 인상을 쓰려 애쓰며 운을 뗐지만, 결국 인자한 미소만 짓고 말았다. 그러나 새 회원은 그 상황에 능히 대처했다. 그는 일어나 의장석에 감사의 인사를 올리며, 더없이 매력적인 태도로 말했다. \"회장님, 그리고 숙녀 여러분 — 아, 죄송합니다, 신사 여러분 — 클럽의 더없이 미천한 종, 샘 웰러로 제 소개를 하게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"좋아! 좋아!\" 조가 기대고 있던 낡은 침대 데우개 손잡이로 쾅쾅 두드리며 외쳤다.\n\n\"저를 이토록 과분하게 소개해 준 제 충실한 벗이자 고귀한 후원자는,\" 로리가 손을 한 번 휘저으며 말을 이었다. \"오늘 밤의 이 비열한 계략을 두고 탓할 사람이 아닙니다. 제가 그것을 꾸몄고, 그녀는 한참 졸라 댄 끝에야 넘어왔을 뿐입니다.\"\n\"이봐, 다 자기 탓으로만 돌리지 마. 벽장은 내가 제안한 거 너도 알잖아.\" 스노드그래스가 끼어들었는데, 이 농담을 엄청나게 즐기고 있었다.\n\n\"그녀가 하는 말은 신경 쓰지 마십시오. 그 짓을 한 비열한 놈은 접니다, 회장님.\" 새 회원이 픽윅 씨에게 웰러식으로 고개를 까딱하며 말했다. \"허나 명예를 걸고, 다시는 그러지 않을 것이며, 이제부터 이 불멸의 클럽의 이익에 저 자신을 바치겠습니다.\"\n\"옳소! 옳소!\" 조가 침대 데우개 뚜껑을 심벌즈처럼 쨍그랑 부딪치며 외쳤다.\n\n\"계속해, 계속해!\" 윙클과 터프먼이 덧붙였고, 그러는 동안 회장은 자애롭게 고개를 숙였다.\n\"제게 베풀어 주신 영예에 대한 감사의 작은 표시로서, 그리고 이웃한 두 나라 사이의 우호 관계를 증진하는 수단으로서, 한 가지만 말씀드리고자 합니다. 저는 정원 아래쪽 모퉁이의 산울타리에 우체국을 세웠습니다. 멋지고 널찍한 건물로, 문에는 자물쇠가 달려 있고, 우편물을 위한 온갖 편의가 갖춰져 있습니다 — 또한 여성용 우편물을 위해서도요, 이런 표현을 써도 된다면 말입니다. 그건 낡은 제비집인데, 제가 입구를 막고 지붕을 열리게 만들어 놓아서, 온갖 것을 담을 수 있고 우리의 귀중한 시간도 아껴 줄 겁니다. 편지, 원고, 책, 꾸러미를 거기로 주고받을 수 있고, 각 나라가 열쇠를 하나씩 가지고 있으니, 참으로 멋질 것이라 생각합니다. 클럽 열쇠를 드리겠습니다. 그리고 여러분의 호의에 거듭 감사드리며, 제 자리로 돌아가겠습니다.\"\n\n웰러 씨가 작은 열쇠 하나를 탁자 위에 올려놓고 자리에 앉자 큰 박수가 터졌다. 침대 데우개가 쨍그랑거리며 미친 듯이 흔들렸고, 질서가 회복되기까지는 한참이 걸렸다. 긴 토론이 이어졌고, 저마다 최선을 다한 덕에 모두가 놀라운 모습을 보였다. 그리하여 그 모임은 유난히 활기찼고, 늦은 시각이 되어서야 새 회원을 위한 세 차례의 날카로운 환호와 함께 산회했다.\n샘 웰러를 받아들인 것을 후회한 사람은 아무도 없었으니, 그보다 더 헌신적이고 예의 바르며 유쾌한 회원은 어느 클럽에도 없었을 것이기 때문이다. 그는 확실히 모임에 \"활기\"를 더했고 신문에 \"격조\"를 더했다. 그의 연설은 듣는 이들을 자지러지게 했고, 그의 기고문은 애국적이거나 고전적이거나 우스꽝스럽거나 극적이긴 해도 결코 감상적이지 않아 일품이었다. 조는 그것을 베이컨이나 밀턴, 혹은 셰익스피어에 견줄 만하다고 여겼고, 자기 작품도 거기에 맞춰 고쳐 쓰니 효과가 좋다고 생각했다.\n\n우체국은 굉장히 훌륭한 작은 기관으로 놀랍도록 번창했으니, 진짜 우체국 못지않게 별의별 희한한 것들이 그곳을 거쳐 갔기 때문이다. 비극과 넥타이, 시와 피클, 정원 씨앗과 긴 편지, 악보와 생강빵, 덧신, 초대장, 잔소리, 그리고 강아지까지. 노신사는 그 재미를 좋아해서, 별난 꾸러미와 알쏭달쏭한 전갈과 우스운 전보를 보내며 즐거워했다. 해나의 매력에 푹 빠진 그의 정원사는 실제로 연애편지를 조 앞으로 보내기도 했다. 비밀이 드러났을 때 다들 얼마나 웃었던지, 그 작은 우체국이 다가올 세월에 얼마나 많은 연애편지를 품게 될지는 꿈에도 모른 채 말이다!\n\n\"유월 첫날이야! 킹 씨네가 내일 바닷가로 떠나니까 나는 자유야. 석 달의 방학이라니 — 얼마나 즐길 작정인지!\" 어느 무더운 날 집에 돌아온 메그가 외쳤다. 조는 보기 드물게 기진맥진한 모습으로 소파에 누워 있었고, 베스는 언니의 먼지투성이 부츠를 벗겨 주고 있었으며, 에이미는 일행 모두의 원기 회복을 위해 레모네이드를 만들고 있었다.\n\"마치 대고모님이 오늘 떠나셨어. 그러니, 아, 기뻐하자고!\" 조가 말했다. \"고모님이 같이 가자고 하실까 봐 죽도록 겁났거든. 그러셨다면 나도 가야 할 것만 같았을 거야. 그렇지만 플럼필드는 알다시피 교회 묘지만큼이나 흥겨운 곳이잖아, 그래서 난 빠지는 편이 나아. 늙으신 고모님을 떠나보내느라 한바탕 부산을 떨었는데, 고모님이 내게 말을 거실 때마다 가슴이 철렁했어. 빨리 일을 마치고 싶은 마음이 어찌나 굴뚝같던지 유난히 살갑고 곰살맞게 굴었거든. 그래서 고모님이 나랑 못 헤어지겠다고 하실까 봐 겁났던 거야. 고모님이 마차에 완전히 올라타실 때까지 벌벌 떨었는데, 마지막에 또 한 번 기겁했어. 마차가 떠나는데 고모님이 고개를 쑥 내밀고 이러시지 뭐야. '조지핀, 너 ─ 안 하련?' 그 뒤는 못 들었어, 비열하게도 돌아서서 줄행랑쳤거든. 정말로 달렸다니까, 모퉁이를 휙 돌고 나서야 안심이 됐어.\"\n\n\"불쌍한 우리 조! 곰한테 쫓기기라도 한 것 같은 꼴로 들어왔다니까.\" 베스가 엄마 같은 손길로 언니의 발을 감싸며 말했다.\n\n\"마치 대고모님은 영락없는 함초 같지 않니?\" 에이미가 자기가 만든 음료를 꼼꼼히 맛보며 말했다.\n\n\"걔가 말하려는 건 흡혈귀지, 해초가 아니야. 그래도 상관없어. 자기가 쓰는 단어 따위에 까다롭게 굴기엔 너무 더우니까.\" 조가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"넌 방학 내내 뭐 할 거야?\" 에이미가 눈치껏 화제를 돌리며 물었다.\n\n\"난 늦잠 자고 아무것도 안 할래.\" 흔들의자 깊숙이 파묻힌 메그가 대답했다. \"겨우내 일찍 깨워지는 통에 종일 남을 위해 일해야 했으니까, 이젠 실컷 쉬면서 마음껏 즐길 거야.\"\n\n\"아니야.\" 조가 말했다. \"그렇게 빈둥대는 건 나랑 안 맞아. 책을 잔뜩 쌓아 뒀거든, 나는 늙은 사과나무 위 내 자리에 올라앉아 책을 읽으면서 이 빛나는 시간을 알차게 쓸 거야. 종달새 흉내를 ─\"\n\n\"'종달새 흉내'라고 하지 마!\" 에이미가 '함초'를 고쳐 준 데 대한 앙갚음으로 애원했다.\n\n\"그럼 로리랑 같이 '나이팅게일'이라고 하지 뭐. 걔는 지저귀는 새니까, 그게 적절하고 알맞겠네.\"\n\n\"우리도 한동안 공부는 하지 말자, 베스. 언니들이 그러려는 것처럼 내내 놀면서 쉬자고.\" 에이미가 제안했다.\n\n\"음, 엄마가 괜찮다고 하시면 그럴래. 새 노래 몇 곡을 배우고 싶고, 내 아이들도 여름맞이로 단장해 줘야 하거든. 걔들 꼴이 말이 아니게 엉망이라 정말이지 옷이 없어 고생하고 있어.\"\n\n\"그래도 돼요, 엄마?\" 메그가 그들이 \"엄마의 구석\"이라고 부르는 곳에 앉아 바느질하던 마치 부인을 돌아보며 물었다.\n\n\"일주일 동안 시험 삼아 해 보고, 마음에 드는지 보렴. 토요일 밤쯤이면 놀기만 하고 일을 안 하는 것이 일만 하고 놀지 않는 것만큼이나 나쁘다는 걸 알게 될 것 같구나.\"\n\n\"어머, 그럴 리가요! 분명 근사할 거예요.\" 메그가 흡족하게 말했다.\n\n\"이제 내 '벗이자 동무인 새리 갬프'가 말하듯 건배를 제안할게. 영원한 즐거움이여, 그리고 고된 일은 없기를!\" 조가 잔을 손에 들고 일어서며 외쳤고, 레모네이드 잔이 한 바퀴 돌았다.\n\n다들 흥겹게 잔을 비웠고, 그날 남은 시간을 빈둥대며 보내는 것으로 그 시험을 시작했다. 이튿날 아침, 메그는 열 시가 되어서야 모습을 드러냈다. 혼자 먹는 아침 식사는 맛이 없었고, 방은 외롭고 어수선해 보였다. 조가 꽃병을 채워 두지 않았고, 베스는 먼지를 닦지 않았으며, 에이미의 책들은 여기저기 흩어져 있었던 탓이다. 평소와 다름없어 보이는 \"엄마의 구석\"만 빼고는 무엇 하나 깔끔하고 산뜻한 데가 없었다. 메그는 거기 앉아 \"쉬면서 책을 읽었\"는데, 그건 곧 하품을 하며 봉급으로 어떤 예쁜 여름 원피스를 살까 상상하는 것이었다. 조는 오전을 로리와 강에서 보냈고, 오후에는 사과나무 위에 올라가 『넓고 넓은 세상』을 읽으며 울었다. 베스는 자기 식구들이 사는 큰 벽장에서 모든 걸 끄집어내는 것으로 시작했지만, 절반도 하기 전에 지쳐서 살림살이를 뒤죽박죽인 채 내버려 두고, 설거지할 그릇이 없는 것을 기뻐하며 음악으로 향했다. 에이미는 자기 정자를 꾸미고, 제일 좋은 흰 원피스를 입고, 곱슬머리를 매만진 다음, 인동덩굴 아래 앉아 그림을 그렸다. 누군가가 보고서 저 젊은 화가가 누구냐고 물어봐 주기를 바라면서. 그러나 호기심 많은 갈처지거미 한 마리만 나타나 흥미롭게 그녀의 그림을 살펴볼 뿐 아무도 오지 않자, 에이미는 산책을 나갔다가 소나기를 만나 흠뻑 젖은 채 집에 돌아왔다.\n\n차 마실 시간에 다들 그날 있었던 일을 나누었고, 비록 유난히 긴 하루였지만 즐거웠다는 데 모두 동의했다. 오후에 장을 보러 가서 \"고운 파란 모슬린\"을 산 메그는 천을 마름질해 잘라 낸 뒤에야 그 천이 물빨래가 안 된다는 걸 알게 되어, 그 낭패에 조금 짜증이 나 있었다. 조는 뱃놀이를 하다가 콧등 살갗이 다 타 버렸고, 너무 오래 책을 읽은 탓에 머리가 깨질 듯 아팠다. 베스는 어수선한 벽장과 한꺼번에 서너 곡을 익히는 어려움에 골머리를 앓았다. 그리고 에이미는 원피스가 망가진 것을 몹시 안타까워했으니, 다음 날이 케이티 브라운의 파티인데 이제 플로라 맥플림지처럼 \"입을 옷이 없었\"기 때문이다. 그러나 이런 건 사소한 일들이었고, 그들은 엄마에게 이 시험이 아주 잘되어 가고 있다고 장담했다. 엄마는 미소만 지을 뿐 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 해나의 도움을 받아 그들이 소홀히 한 일을 해내며, 집안을 안락하게 하고 살림이라는 기계가 매끄럽게 돌아가게 했다. 그렇게 별나고 불편한 상황이 점점 더 길게 이어지니 참으로 놀라웠다. 날씨가 유난히 변덕스러웠고, 성미들도 그러했다. 모두가 안절부절못하는 기분에 사로잡혔고, 사탄은 한가한 손에게 시킬 못된 장난을 잔뜩 찾아 주었다. 호사의 극치로, 메그는 자기 바느질감을 일부 남에게 맡겼는데, 그러고 나니 시간이 어찌나 안 가던지, 결국 모팻 풍으로 옷을 손보겠답시고 옷을 자르고 망쳐 놓기에 이르렀다. 조는 눈이 빠지도록 책을 읽다가 책이라면 진절머리가 났고, 어찌나 안달복달했던지 그 사람 좋은 로리마저도 그녀와 다투었으며, 기운이 어찌나 빠졌던지 마치 대고모님과 함께 갈걸 그랬다고 간절히 바랄 지경이었다. 베스는 그럭저럭 잘 지냈으니, 일을 안 하고 내내 놀기로 했다는 걸 자꾸만 잊고 이따금 옛 버릇으로 되돌아갔기 때문이다. 그러나 무언가 공기 중에 떠도는 것이 그녀에게 영향을 미쳐, 한두 번이 아니게 그 마음의 평온이 크게 흐트러졌다. 어찌나 심했던지, 한번은 가엾고 사랑스러운 조애나를 실제로 흔들어 대며 \"꼴이 흉측하다\"고 말하기까지 했다. 에이미는 누구보다 형편이 나빴으니, 가진 밑천이 적었기 때문이다. 언니들이 그녀를 내버려 두어 혼자 놀고 알아서 지내야 하게 되자, 그녀는 곧 그 잘난 척하는 중요한 자기 자신이 큰 짐이라는 걸 깨달았다. 인형은 싫었고, 동화는 유치했으며, 누구라도 종일 그림만 그릴 수는 없는 노릇이었다. 다과회도 별것 아니었고, 소풍 역시 아주 잘 꾸려지지 않는 한 마찬가지였다. \"근사한 집에 좋은 여자애들이 가득하다거나 여행이라도 갈 수 있다면 여름이 즐거울 텐데, 이기적인 언니 셋이랑 다 큰 사내애 하나랑 집에만 있으려니 보아스의 인내심도 시험에 들 지경이지.\" 며칠을 즐거움과 짜증과 권태에 바친 끝에 맬러프롭 양이 투덜거렸다.\n\n시험에 싫증이 났다고 인정하려는 사람은 아무도 없었다. 그러나 금요일 밤이 되자, 저마다 일주일이 거의 끝나 가는 게 다행이라고 속으로 인정했다. 마치 부인은 익살기가 다분한 사람이라, 이 교훈을 더 깊이 새겨 주고자 그 시험을 적절한 방식으로 마무리하기로 마음먹었다. 그래서 해나에게 하루 휴가를 주고, 소녀들이 노는 제도의 효과를 톡톡히 누리도록 내버려 두었다.\n\n토요일 아침에 일어났을 때, 부엌에는 불이 없었고, 식당에는 아침 식사가 없었으며, 엄마는 어디에도 보이지 않았다.\n\n\"맙소사! 대체 무슨 일이 일어난 거야?\" 조가 어쩔 줄 몰라 사방을 두리번거리며 외쳤다.\n\n메그는 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔다가 이내 다시 내려왔는데, 안도한 표정이면서도 어딘지 어리둥절하고 약간 부끄러워하는 기색이었다.\n\n\"엄마는 편찮으신 건 아니고, 그냥 몹시 피곤하시대. 그래서 오늘은 종일 방에서 조용히 쉬면서 우리가 알아서 하게 두시겠대. 엄마가 이러시는 건 정말 별일이야, 평소 엄마 같지가 않아. 그래도 이번 주가 힘드셨다니까, 우린 투덜대지 말고 알아서 챙기자.\"\n\n\"그쯤이야 식은 죽 먹기지, 게다가 그 생각 마음에 들어. 난 뭐든 할 게 없나 근질근질하던 참이거든 — 그러니까, 뭔가 새로운 재밋거리 말이야.\" 조가 얼른 덧붙였다.\n\n사실 그들 모두에게 약간의 일거리가 생긴 것은 엄청난 위안이었고, 그래서 의욕을 가지고 달려들었지만, 곧 \"살림은 장난이 아니다\"라는 해나의 말이 진실임을 깨달았다. 식료품 저장실에는 먹을 것이 잔뜩 있었고, 베스와 에이미가 상을 차리는 동안 메그와 조는 아침 식사를 준비했다. 그러면서 두 사람은 어째서 하인들이 힘든 일이니 어쩌니 떠드는지 의아해했다.\n\n\"엄마 신경 쓰지 말라고, 알아서 챙기시겠다고 하셨지만, 그래도 좀 가져다드려야겠어.\" 메그가 말했다. 메그는 좌장 노릇을 하며 찻주전자 뒤에 앉아 자못 안주인 같은 기분이 들었다.\n\n그래서 누가 먹기 시작하기도 전에 쟁반이 차려졌고, 요리사의 인사와 함께 위로 올려졌다. 끓인 차는 무척 썼고, 오믈렛은 탔으며, 비스킷에는 베이킹소다가 알록달록 박혀 있었다. 그러나 마치 부인은 고맙다며 그 끼니를 받아 들었고, 조가 가고 난 뒤에는 한바탕 호탕하게 웃었다.\n\n\"가엾은 것들, 한동안 고생깨나 하겠구나. 그래도 크게 탈 날 일은 없을 테고, 이게 다 약이 될 거야.\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하며, 미리 챙겨 둔 좀 더 입에 맞는 음식을 꺼내고, 아이들의 마음이 상하지 않도록 형편없는 아침상을 처리했다. 엄마다운 작은 속임수였고, 아이들은 그것을 고마워했다.\n\n아래층에서는 불평이 많았고, 수석 요리사는 자기 실패작에 크게 풀이 죽었다. \"괜찮아, 저녁은 내가 차리고 하인 노릇을 할게. 언니는 안주인이 되어 손을 곱게 간수하고, 손님을 맞고, 명령이나 내려.\" 메그보다 요리에 대해 아는 게 더 없는 조가 말했다.\n\n이 고마운 제안은 기꺼이 받아들여졌고, 마거릿은 응접실로 물러나, 먼지 닦는 수고를 덜려고 어질러진 것들을 소파 밑으로 휙 쓸어 넣고 덧문을 닫아 부랴부랴 정돈했다. 조는 자기 능력에 대한 완벽한 믿음과, 다툼을 풀려는 다정한 마음에서, 곧장 우체국에 로리를 저녁 식사에 초대하는 쪽지를 넣었다.\n\n\"손님 부를 생각을 하기 전에 가진 게 뭐가 있는지부터 봐 두는 게 좋을걸.\" 그 후하지만 경솔한 행동을 전해 들은 메그가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 코드비프랑 감자가 잔뜩 있고, 아스파라거스도 좀 사 올 거고, 해나 말마따나 '입맛 돋우게' 바닷가재도 사 올 거야. 상추도 사다가 샐러드를 만들 거고. 어떻게 하는진 모르지만 책에 다 나와 있어. 후식으로는 블랑망제랑 딸기를 낼 거야. 그리고 우아하게 차리고 싶으면 커피도 내고.\"\n\n\"너무 여러 가지 음식 만들려고 하지 마, 조. 넌 생강빵이랑 당밀 사탕 말고는 먹을 만한 걸 만들 줄 모르잖아. 난 이 만찬에서 손 뗄래. 그리고 네 책임으로 로리를 불렀으니까, 걔는 네가 알아서 챙겨.\"\n\n\"언니는 걔한테 그냥 예의 바르게 굴고, 푸딩 담는 거나 거들어 주면 돼. 내가 갈팡질팡하면 조언 정도는 해 줄 거지?\" 조가 다소 서운해하며 물었다.\n\n\"그래. 그래도 난 빵이랑 자잘한 것 몇 가지밖에 잘 몰라. 뭐든 주문하기 전에 엄마한테 먼저 허락받는 게 좋을 거야.\" 메그가 신중하게 대답했다.\n\n\"당연히 그래야지. 나도 바보는 아니라고.\" 그러고는 조가 자기 능력을 의심받은 데 골이 나서 휙 가 버렸다.\n\n\"먹고 싶은 거 사고, 날 귀찮게 하지 말아라. 난 저녁 먹으러 나갈 거고, 집안일은 신경 쓸 수가 없구나.\" 조가 말을 건네자 마치 부인이 말했다. \"난 살림을 즐겨 본 적이 없어서, 오늘은 휴가를 좀 내서 책 읽고, 글 쓰고, 마실 다니고, 즐기련다.\"\n\n부산하던 엄마가 이른 아침에 흔들의자에 편안히 앉아 책을 읽는 진귀한 광경에 조는 무슨 자연 현상이라도 일어난 듯한 기분이 들었으니, 일식이나 지진, 화산 폭발이라 한들 이보다 더 이상해 보이지는 않았을 터였다.\n\n\"어쩐지 죄다 뒤숭숭해.\" 조는 아래층으로 내려가며 혼잣말을 했다. \"베스가 울고 있잖아. 저건 이 집에 뭔가 잘못됐다는 확실한 신호야. 에이미가 속을 썩이면, 흔들어 줘야지.\"\n\n스스로도 영 마음이 뒤숭숭한 조가 응접실로 서둘러 들어가 보니, 베스가 새장 안에서 죽어 있는 카나리아 핍을 두고 흐느끼고 있었다. 핍은 먹을 것이 없어 굶어 죽었음을 애원하기라도 하듯, 작은 발톱을 애처롭게 뻗은 채였다.\n\n\"다 내 잘못이야 — 내가 얘를 잊어버렸어 — 씨앗 한 톨, 물 한 방울 안 남았잖아. 오, 핍! 오, 핍! 내가 어쩌면 너한테 이렇게 모질 수 있었을까?\" 베스가 가엾은 것을 두 손에 받쳐 들고 되살려 보려 애쓰며 외쳤다.\n\n조는 반쯤 뜬 핍의 눈을 들여다보고, 작은 심장을 만져 보더니, 뻣뻣하고 차가운 것을 알고는 고개를 가로젓고, 관으로 쓰라며 자기 도미노 상자를 내주었다.\n\n\"오븐에 넣어 보자, 어쩌면 따뜻해져서 되살아날지도 몰라.\" 에이미가 희망 어린 말을 했다.\n\n\"얜 굶어 죽은 거야, 이제 죽었는데 굽기까지 할 순 없어. 내가 수의를 만들어서 정원에 묻어 줄 거야. 그리고 다시는 새를 키우지 않을 거야, 절대로, 내 핍! 난 새를 키울 자격도 없을 만큼 못된 애니까.\" 베스가 자기 귀염둥이를 두 손에 감싸 든 채 바닥에 앉아 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"장례식은 오늘 오후에 치르자, 우리 다 같이 가는 거야. 자, 울지 마, 베스. 안된 일이지만, 이번 주엔 되는 일이 하나도 없네. 핍이 이 시험의 가장 큰 화를 입은 거야. 수의를 만들어서 내 상자에 뉘어 둬. 그리고 저녁 식사가 끝나면 멋진 작은 장례식을 치르자.\" 조가 말했지만, 일을 너무 많이 떠맡은 것 같은 기분이 들기 시작했다.\n\n다른 식구들에게 베스를 위로하는 일을 맡기고, 조는 부엌으로 향했는데, 그곳은 더없이 의기소침해질 만큼 난장판이었다. 큰 앞치마를 두르고 일에 달려들어, 그릇을 설거지할 수 있게 차곡차곡 쌓아 놓고 보니, 불이 꺼져 있었다.\n\n\"이거 참 근사한 앞날이군!\" 조가 화덕 문을 쾅 열어젖히고 잿더미 속을 힘차게 쑤셔 대며 투덜거렸다.\n\n불을 다시 지핀 다음, 그녀는 물이 데워지는 동안 장을 보러 가야겠다고 생각했다. 걷고 나니 기운이 살아났고, 흥정을 잘했다고 스스로 우쭐해하며, 아주 어린 바닷가재 한 마리와 아주 늙은 아스파라거스 약간, 그리고 신 딸기 두 상자를 산 뒤 터덜터덜 집으로 돌아왔다. 다 정리하고 나니 장 봐 온 것이 도착했고, 화덕은 시뻘겋게 달아올라 있었다. 해나가 부풀라고 빵 반죽 한 판을 놓아두었는데, 메그가 그것을 일찌감치 치대어 두 번째로 부풀라고 화덕가에 올려놓고는 잊어버린 것이었다. 메그가 응접실에서 샐리 가드너를 대접하고 있는데, 문이 벌컥 열리더니, 밀가루투성이에 그을음투성이로 얼굴이 벌게지고 머리가 헝클어진 형체가 나타나, 톡 쏘듯 캐물었다.\n\n\"있잖아, 빵이 판에서 넘쳐 나오면 충분히 '부푼' 거 아니야?\"\n\n샐리가 웃기 시작했지만, 메그는 고개를 끄덕이며 눈썹을 한껏 치켜올렸다. 그러자 그 유령 같은 형체는 사라졌고, 더는 지체 없이 시큼해진 빵을 화덕에 넣었다. 마치 부인은 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지 여기저기 들여다본 다음, 도미노 상자 안에 고이 안치된 망자를 곁에 두고 수의를 짓고 있는 베스에게 위로의 한마디를 건네고는 집을 나섰다. 회색 보닛이 모퉁이를 돌아 사라지자 소녀들에게는 야릇한 무력감이 엄습했다. 그리고 몇 분 후, 크로커 양이 나타나 저녁을 먹으러 왔다고 하자, 그들은 절망에 사로잡혔다. 그런데 이 부인은 뾰족한 코에 캐묻기 좋아하는 눈을 가진, 비쩍 마른 누런 노처녀로, 무엇이든 다 보고는 본 것을 죄다 떠벌리고 다니는 사람이었다. 그들은 그녀를 싫어했지만, 단지 그녀가 늙고 가난하며 벗이 거의 없다는 이유로 그녀에게 친절하라고 배워 온 터였다. 그래서 메그는 그녀에게 안락의자를 내주고 즐겁게 해 주려 애썼고, 그러는 동안 그녀는 이것저것 캐묻고, 무엇이든 트집을 잡고, 자기가 아는 사람들 이야기를 늘어놓았다.\n\n그날 아침 조가 겪은 근심과 경험과 노고는 말로 다 할 수 없으며, 그녀가 차려 낸 저녁 식사는 두고두고 우스갯거리가 되었다. 더는 조언을 구하기가 겁이 나서, 그녀는 혼자 최선을 다했고, 요리사가 되려면 의욕과 선의 이상의 무언가가 필요하다는 걸 깨달았다. 아스파라거스를 한 시간이나 삶고 보니, 머리 부분은 푹 삶겨 떨어져 나가고 줄기는 어느 때보다 질겨져, 마음이 상했다. 빵은 시커멓게 탔다. 샐러드 드레싱이 어찌나 약을 올리는지, 그것을 먹을 만하게 만들 수 없다는 걸 스스로 확인할 때까지 다른 모든 걸 내팽개쳤기 때문이다. 바닷가재는 그녀에게 새빨간 수수께끼였지만, 두드리고 쑤신 끝에 껍데기를 벗겨, 그 빈약한 살점을 상추 잎 덤불 속에 감추었다. 감자는 아스파라거스를 기다리게 하지 않으려고 서둘러야 했는데, 결국 다 익지 못했다. 블랑망제는 멍울이 졌고, 딸기는 보기와 달리 그리 익지 않은 데다 솜씨 좋게 \"윗물만 좋아 보이게\" 골라 담긴 것이었다.\n\n\"뭐, 배가 고프면 소고기랑 버터 바른 빵을 먹으면 되지. 다만 아침 내내 헛고생한 게 분할 뿐이야.\" 조는 평소보다 삼십 분이나 늦게 종을 울리며 이렇게 생각했고, 덥고 지치고 풀이 죽은 채로, 온갖 우아한 것에 익숙한 로리와, 그 호기심 어린 눈으로 실패작이란 실패작은 죄다 짚어 내고 그 수다스러운 혀로 사방에 퍼뜨릴 크로커 양을 위해 차려진 잔칫상을 둘러보고 서 있었다.\n\n가엾은 조는 음식이 하나하나 맛만 보고 남겨지는 동안 차라리 식탁 밑으로 기어들어 가고 싶은 심정이었다. 그러는 동안 에이미는 킥킥거렸고, 메그는 괴로운 표정을 지었으며, 크로커 양은 입술을 오므렸고, 로리는 이 잔치 자리에 흥겨운 분위기를 돋우려고 온 힘을 다해 이야기하고 웃어 댔다. 조의 한 가지 강점은 과일이었으니, 설탕을 충분히 뿌려 두었고 함께 먹을 진한 크림을 한 주전자 마련해 두었기 때문이다. 예쁜 유리 접시가 한 바퀴 돌고, 다들 크림의 바다에 떠 있는 작고 발그레한 섬들을 흐뭇하게 바라보자, 달아올랐던 조의 두 뺨이 조금 식었고, 그녀는 긴 숨을 내쉬었다. 크로커 양이 먼저 맛을 보더니, 얼굴을 찡그리고는 황급히 물을 들이켰다. 골라낸 뒤에 딸기 양이 안타깝게 줄어든 터라 모자랄까 봐 사양했던 조는 로리를 흘끗 보았는데, 그는 입가가 약간 일그러지고 시선을 줄곧 접시에 고정한 채이긴 했어도 사내답게 꿋꿋이 먹고 있었다. 입맛이 까다로운 에이미는 한 숟갈 수북이 떠 넣더니, 컥컥거리며 냅킨에 얼굴을 묻고는 황급히 식탁에서 일어났다.\n\n\"어머, 왜 그래?\" 조가 떨며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"설탕 대신 소금이 들어갔고, 크림은 쉬었어.\" 메그가 비극적인 몸짓으로 대답했다.\n\n조는 신음을 토하며 의자에 털썩 기댔다. 부엌 탁자 위 두 상자 중 하나에서 마지막으로 딸기에 황급히 가루를 뿌렸던 것과, 우유를 냉장고에 넣어 두는 걸 잊었던 것이 떠올랐기 때문이다. 그녀는 얼굴이 새빨개져서 막 울음을 터뜨리려던 참에 로리의 눈과 마주쳤는데, 그 눈은 그가 그토록 영웅적으로 애를 쓰는데도 기어이 즐거운 빛을 띠고 있었다. 문득 이 일의 우스운 면이 그녀의 마음을 때렸고, 그녀는 두 뺨에 눈물이 흐를 만큼 웃어 댔다. 다른 사람들도 모두, 소녀들이 \"투덜이\"라 부르는 그 노부인까지도 그랬다. 그리하여 불운한 저녁 식사는 버터 바른 빵과 올리브와 웃음과 더불어 흥겹게 끝이 났다.\n\n\"지금은 치울 마음의 여력이 없으니, 장례식으로 우리 마음을 좀 가라앉히자.\" 일어서며 조가 말했다. 그리고 크로커 양은 다른 벗의 저녁 식탁에서 이 새로운 이야기를 들려주고 싶어 안달이 나서, 떠날 채비를 했다.\n\n그들은 베스를 위해 정말로 마음을 가라앉혔다. 로리가 숲속 고사리 아래 무덤을 팠고, 마음씨 고운 주인 베스가 숱한 눈물과 함께 어린 핍을 뉘었으며, 이끼로 덮어 주었다. 한편 핍의 비문이 새겨진 돌에는 제비꽃과 별꽃으로 엮은 화환이 걸렸는데, 그 비문은 조가 저녁 식사와 씨름하는 동안 지은 것이었다.\n\"여기 핍 마치 잠들다,\n\n유월 칠일에 죽다.\n사랑받고 깊이 애도되며,\n\n이내 잊히지 않으리.\"\n의식이 끝나자, 베스는 북받치는 감정과 바닷가재 탓에 기진하여 자기 방으로 물러났다. 그러나 침대가 정돈되어 있지 않아 쉴 자리가 없었고, 베개를 두드려 부풀리고 물건을 제자리에 두다 보니 슬픔이 훨씬 누그러지는 걸 느꼈다. 메그는 잔치의 잔해를 치우는 조를 도왔는데, 그 일에 오후의 절반이 걸려 둘 다 어찌나 지쳤던지, 저녁은 차와 토스트로 만족하기로 했다. 로리는 에이미를 마차에 태워 드라이브를 시켜 주었는데, 그것은 자선 행위였으니, 쉰 크림이 그녀의 성미에 나쁜 영향을 미친 듯했기 때문이다. 마치 부인이 집에 돌아와 보니 위의 세 딸이 오후의 한복판에 부지런히 일하고 있었고, 벽장을 한 번 흘끗 본 것만으로도 시험의 한 부분이 얼마나 성공했는지를 짐작할 수 있었다.\n\n이 살림꾼들이 미처 쉬기도 전에 몇 사람이 찾아왔고, 그들을 맞이할 채비를 하느라 한바탕 부산을 떨었다. 그러고는 차를 준비해야 했고, 심부름도 해야 했으며, 꼭 해야 할 바느질 한두 가지는 마지막 순간까지 미뤄졌다. 이슬이 내려 고요한 황혼이 깔리자, 그들은 유월의 장미가 아름답게 봉오리를 맺고 있는 현관에 하나둘 모여들었고, 저마다 지쳤거나 시름에 잠긴 듯 자리에 앉으며 끙끙대거나 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"오늘 정말 끔찍한 하루였어!\" 늘 제일 먼저 입을 떼는 조가 운을 뗐다.\n\n\"평소보다 짧게 느껴지긴 했는데, 정말이지 불편한 하루였어.\" 메그가 말했다.\n\n\"우리 집 같지가 않았어.\" 에이미가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"엄마랑 어린 핍이 없으니 그럴 수밖에.\" 베스가 한숨지으며, 눈물 그렁그렁한 눈으로 머리 위 텅 빈 새장을 올려다보았다.\n\n\"여기 엄마 왔다, 얘야. 그리고 원한다면 내일 새를 한 마리 더 사 줄게.\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 마치 부인이 다가와 아이들 사이에 자리를 잡았는데, 그녀의 휴일도 그들의 하루보다 그리 즐겁지는 않았던 듯한 표정이었다.\n\n\"얘들아, 너희 시험에 만족하니, 아니면 일주일 더 해 보고 싶으니?\" 그녀가 묻자, 베스는 엄마 품에 파고들었고, 나머지 아이들은 꽃이 해를 향하듯 환해진 얼굴로 그녀를 돌아보았다.\n\n\"전 싫어요!\" 조가 단호하게 외쳤다.\n\n\"저도요.\" 다른 아이들도 따라 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 해야 할 일이 조금은 있고, 남을 위해 조금은 살아가는 편이 더 낫다고 생각하는 거니?\"\n\n\"빈둥대고 놀기만 하는 건 남는 게 없어요.\" 조가 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"이젠 진절머리가 나요, 당장 뭔가 일에 매달릴 거예요.\"\n\n\"평범한 요리를 배우는 게 어떻겠니. 그건 유익한 재주라서, 어떤 여자도 모르고 지내선 안 되는 거야.\" 마치 부인이 조의 만찬을 떠올리며 소리 없이 웃으며 말했다. 그녀는 크로커 양과 마주쳐 그 일에 대한 이야기를 들었던 것이다.\n\n\"엄마, 그럼 우리가 어떻게 헤쳐 나가는지 보려고 일부러 나가서 다 내버려 두신 거예요?\" 종일 미심쩍어하던 메그가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래. 너희가 저마다 자기 몫을 충실히 해내는 데 모두의 안락이 달려 있다는 걸 알았으면 했단다. 해나와 내가 너희 일을 하는 동안에는 너희가 제법 잘 지냈지. 그렇게 행복하거나 상냥하진 않았던 것 같지만 말이야. 그래서 작은 교훈으로, 다들 자기 생각만 할 때 어떤 일이 벌어지는지 보여 주려 했던 거야. 서로 돕고, 여가가 왔을 때 그것을 달콤하게 해 주는 나날의 의무를 갖고, 집이 우리 모두에게 안락하고 사랑스러운 곳이 되도록 참고 양보하는 게 더 즐겁다는 걸 이제 알겠지?\"\n\n\"알아요, 엄마, 정말 알아요!\" 소녀들이 외쳤다.\n\n\"그럼 너희에게 권하건대, 너희의 작은 짐을 다시 짊어지렴. 그것이 때로는 무거워 보여도 우리에게 이로운 것이고, 짊어지는 법을 배워 갈수록 가벼워지는 법이란다. 일은 몸에 좋은 것이고, 누구에게나 할 일은 넘치도록 많아. 일은 우리를 권태와 못된 장난에서 지켜 주고, 건강과 기분에 이로우며, 돈이나 유행보다 나은 힘과 독립의 감각을 안겨 주지.\"\n\n\"우린 꿀벌처럼 일할 거예요, 그리고 그걸 좋아하기까지 할 거예요. 두고 보세요!\" 조가 말했다. \"전 방학 숙제로 평범한 요리를 배울 거예요. 그래서 다음에 만찬을 열 땐 꼭 성공할 거예요.\"\n\n\"엄마가 하시게 두는 대신, 제가 아빠 셔츠 한 벌을 지을게요, 엄마. 바느질을 좋아하진 않지만 할 수 있고, 또 할 거예요. 그게 지금 이대로도 충분히 좋은 내 옷가지나 만지작거리는 것보다 나을 거예요.\" 메그가 말했다.\n\n\"전 매일 공부를 할 거고, 음악이랑 인형에 시간을 그렇게 많이 쏟지 않을래요. 전 멍청한 애라서, 놀 게 아니라 공부를 해야 해요.\" 이것이 베스의 결심이었다. 한편 에이미도 그들을 본받아 영웅적으로 선언했다. \"전 단춧구멍 만드는 법을 배우고, 제 품사에 신경 쓸 거예요.\"\n\n\"아주 좋아! 그럼 난 이 시험에 아주 만족하고, 다시 되풀이할 필요는 없을 것 같구나. 다만 다른 극단으로 치달아 노예처럼 죽도록 일하지는 마라. 일하는 시간과 노는 시간을 정해 두고, 하루하루를 유익하면서도 즐겁게 보내며, 시간을 잘 써서 그 가치를 안다는 걸 증명하렴. 그러면 젊음은 즐거울 것이고, 노년에는 후회가 적을 것이며, 가난 속에서도 인생은 아름다운 성공이 될 거야.\"\n\n\"명심할게요, 엄마!\" 그리고 그들은 정말로 그렇게 했다.\n\n# CAMP LAURENCE.\n\n베스가 우체국장이었으니, 가장 집에 많이 있는 데다 그 일을 꼬박꼬박 챙길 수 있었고, 매일 그 작은 문을 열어 우편물을 나눠 주는 일을 무척 좋아했기 때문이다. 칠월의 어느 날, 그녀는 두 손 가득 우편물을 안고 들어와, 동전 우편배달부처럼 집안을 돌며 편지와 소포를 놓아두었다.\n\n\"엄마, 꽃다발 왔어요! 로리는 이걸 절대 잊는 법이 없다니까.\" 그녀가 \"엄마의 구석\"에 놓인 꽃병에 싱싱한 꽃다발을 꽂으며 말했다. 그 꽃병은 다정한 그 소년 덕에 늘 꽃이 채워져 있었다.\n\n\"메그 마치 양, 편지 한 통과 장갑 한 짝.\" 베스가 어머니 곁에 앉아 소맷부리를 깁고 있는 언니에게 물건을 건네며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"어머, 난 저기 한 켤레를 두고 왔는데, 여긴 한 짝뿐이네.\" 메그가 회색 무명 장갑을 보며 말했다.\n\n\"나머지 한 짝은 정원에 떨어뜨린 거 아니야?\"\n\n\"아니야, 그러지 않은 게 분명해. 우체국엔 한 짝밖에 없었거든.\"\n\n\"짝 안 맞는 장갑은 정말 질색인데! 괜찮아, 나머지 한 짝도 나오겠지. 내 편지는 그냥 내가 갖고 싶었던 독일 노래 번역일 뿐이야. 브룩 씨가 해 준 것 같아, 로리 글씨가 아니거든.\"\n\n마치 부인은 메그를 흘끗 보았다. 깅엄 아침 가운 차림에 이마 위로 작은 곱슬머리가 나풀거리는 메그는 무척 예뻤고, 정갈하게 둘둘 만 흰 천이 가득한 작은 반짇고리 곁에 앉아 바느질하는 모습이 무척 여인다웠다. 손가락은 부지런히 움직이고, 생각은 허리띠에 꽂힌 팬지꽃처럼 순수하고 싱그러운 소녀다운 공상에 잠긴 채, 바느질하며 노래하는 메그는 어머니의 마음속 생각을 까맣게 모르고 있었다. 그래서 마치 부인은 미소 지으며 흐뭇해했다.\n\n\"조 박사님께 편지 두 통, 책 한 권, 그리고 우체국 전체를 뒤덮을 만큼 우스꽝스러운 낡은 모자가 바깥에 박혀 있어.\" 베스가 웃으며 말하고는, 조가 앉아 글을 쓰고 있는 서재로 들어갔다.\n\n\"로리는 정말 능청맞은 녀석이야! 더운 날마다 얼굴이 다 타니까 더 큰 모자가 유행이면 좋겠다고 했더니, 걔가 '유행 따위 신경 쓸 게 뭐람? 큰 모자를 쓰고 편하게 지내!'라고 하잖아. 나도 모자가 있으면 그러겠다고 했더니, 나를 시험하려고 이걸 보낸 거지. 난 재미 삼아 쓰고서, 내가 유행 따위 신경 안 쓴다는 걸 걔한테 보여 줄 거야.\" 그러고는 그 고풍스러운 챙 넓은 모자를 플라톤 흉상에 걸어 두고, 조는 편지를 읽었다.\n\n어머니가 보낸 한 통은 그녀의 두 뺨을 달아오르게 하고 눈가를 적셨으니, 거기엔 이렇게 쓰여 있었다.\n\n\"사랑하는 딸에게,\n네가 성미를 다스리려고 애쓰는 모습을 얼마나 흐뭇하게 지켜보고 있는지 알려 주려고 짧은 글을 적는다. 너는 네 시련이나 실패, 성공에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않고, 어쩌면 매일 도움을 구하는 그 벗 말고는 아무도 그것을 보지 못한다고 생각하는지도 모르겠구나. 네 안내서의 닳고 닳은 표지를 믿어도 된다면 말이야. 나 역시 그 모든 것을 보았고, 네 결심이 진심임을 진심으로 믿는단다. 이제 그것이 열매를 맺기 시작했으니까. 계속하렴, 얘야, 참을성 있게 그리고 용감하게. 그리고 너를 사랑하는 이 엄마보다 더 다정하게 너와 마음을 함께하는 사람은 없다는 걸 언제나 믿으렴.\n\n엄마가.\"\n\"이게 큰 힘이 돼! 이건 수백만 금이나 산더미 같은 칭찬보다 값진 거야. 오 엄마, 저 정말 노력하고 있어요! 저를 도와주실 엄마가 계시니, 계속 노력하고 지치지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n조는 두 팔에 머리를 묻고, 자신의 작은 소설을 행복한 눈물 몇 방울로 적셨다. 자신이 착해지려고 애쓰는 것을 아무도 보지도 알아주지도 않는다고 여겨 왔기 때문이다. 게다가 이 다짐은 뜻밖이었고, 그것도 자신이 가장 소중히 여기는 사람의 칭찬이었기에, 갑절로 귀하고 갑절로 힘이 났다. 자신의 아폴리온과 맞서 싸워 무찌를 힘이 그 어느 때보다 강해진 것을 느끼며, 그녀는 불시에 당하지 않도록 방패이자 일깨움으로 삼고자 그 쪽지를 옷 안쪽에 핀으로 꽂았다. 그러고는 좋은 소식이든 나쁜 소식이든 받아들일 채비를 갖추고서 다른 편지를 펼쳤다. 큼직하고 활달한 글씨로 로리는 이렇게 썼다.\n\n\"조에게,\n이게 웬일이냐!\n\n내일 영국 남자애들이랑 여자애들 몇이 나를 보러 오는데, 신나게 놀고 싶어. 날이 좋으면 롱메도에 천막을 치고, 다 함께 노 저어 올라가 점심도 먹고 크로케도 할 거야. 불도 피우고, 집시처럼 음식도 만들고, 별의별 신나는 놀이를 다 할 거야. 다들 좋은 애들이고, 이런 걸 좋아해. 브룩 선생님도 우리 사내애들을 점잖게 단속하러 갈 거고, 케이트 본이 여자애들 인솔자 노릇을 할 거야. 너희 모두 왔으면 해. 베스도 무슨 일이 있어도 빠지면 안 돼, 아무도 걜 괴롭히지 못하게 할게. 먹을 것 걱정은 하지 마 ─ 그건 내가 알아서 할게, 다른 것도 다. 그저 꼭 와 줘, 착한 친구니까!\n\n정신없이 바쁜 와중에,\n언제나 너의, 로리.\"\n\n\"이거 횡재네!\" 조가 메그에게 소식을 전하러 날듯이 뛰어 들어가며 외쳤다.\n\n\"우리 당연히 가도 되죠, 엄마? 로리한테 큰 도움이 될 거예요, 제가 노를 저을 수 있고, 메그 언니가 점심을 챙기고, 동생들도 어떻게든 쓸모가 있을 거잖아요.\"\n\n\"본 씨네가 점잖은 어른들이 아니었으면 좋겠는데. 조, 너 그 사람들에 대해 뭐 좀 아니?\" 메그가 물었다.\n\n\"넷이 있다는 것밖에 몰라. 케이트는 언니보다 나이가 많고, 프레드랑 프랭크(쌍둥이야)는 나랑 비슷한 또래고, 어린 여자애(그레이스)는 아홉이나 열 살쯤이래. 로리가 외국에서 알게 됐는데, 남자애들은 좋아했어. 케이트 얘기를 할 때 입을 새침하게 오므리는 걸 보니, 케이트는 그다지 마음에 안 드는 것 같더라.\"\n\n\"내 프랑스 사라사 옷이 깨끗해서 정말 다행이야. 그게 딱 좋고, 또 얼마나 잘 어울리는데!\" 메그가 흡족하게 말했다. \"조, 넌 입을 만한 게 좀 있니?\"\n\n\"빨강이랑 회색 뱃놀이옷, 나한테는 그거면 충분해. 난 노 젓고 여기저기 쏘다닐 거니까, 풀 먹인 옷 따위는 신경 쓰고 싶지 않거든. 너도 올 거지, 베스?\"\n\n\"남자애들 누구도 나한테 말 안 걸게 해 준다면.\"\n\n\"한 명도!\"\n\n\"난 로리를 기쁘게 해 주고 싶어. 그리고 브룩 선생님은 워낙 친절하셔서 무섭지 않아. 그렇지만 난 놀고 싶지도, 노래하고 싶지도, 무슨 말을 하고 싶지도 않아. 열심히 일하면서 아무도 성가시게 안 할게. 그리고 언니가 날 챙겨 줄 테니까, 갈래.\"\n\n\"착하기도 하지. 넌 정말로 그 부끄럼을 떨쳐 내려고 애쓰는구나, 그래서 난 네가 좋아. 잘못과 싸우는 게 쉽지 않다는 걸 나도 알거든. 그럴 때 다정한 말 한마디가 힘이 되어 주잖니. 고마워요, 엄마.\" 그러고는 조가 그 야윈 뺨에 고마운 입맞춤을 했는데, 마치 부인에게는 그것이 젊은 날의 발그레하고 통통하던 뺨을 되찾는 것보다 더 값진 것이었다.\n\n\"난 초콜릿 드롭스 한 상자랑, 내가 베껴 그리고 싶었던 그림을 받았어.\" 에이미가 자기 우편물을 보이며 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 난 로런스 할아버지한테 쪽지를 받았어. 오늘 밤 등불을 켜기 전에 건너와서 연주해 달라고 하셔서, 갈 거야.\" 노신사와의 우정이 무르익어 가던 베스가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"자, 이제 부지런히 움직여서 오늘은 두 배로 일하자. 그래야 내일은 홀가분한 마음으로 놀 수 있으니까.\" 조가 펜을 빗자루로 바꿔 들 채비를 하며 말했다.\n\n이튿날 이른 아침, 좋은 날씨를 약속하려고 햇살이 소녀들의 방을 빼꼼 들여다보았을 때, 그 눈에 우스꽝스러운 광경이 들어왔다. 저마다 이 잔치를 위해 필요하고 마땅해 보이는 채비를 해 둔 것이었다. 메그는 이마에 작은 종이 머리 마는 것을 한 줄 더 둘렀고, 조는 화상 입은 얼굴에 콜드크림을 듬뿍 발랐으며, 베스는 다가올 이별을 보상하려고 조애나를 데리고 잤고, 에이미는 그 코를 추켜올려 주려고 거슬리는 코에 빨래집게를 집어 화룡점정을 찍었다. 그것은 화가들이 그림판에 종이를 고정하는 데 쓰는 종류라, 이제 쓰인 용도에 아주 적절하고 효과적이었다. 이 우스운 광경이 해를 즐겁게 했는지, 그가 어찌나 환하게 터져 나왔던지 조가 잠에서 깼고, 에이미의 장식을 보고 한바탕 호탕하게 웃어 동생들을 모두 깨웠다.\n\n햇살과 웃음은 놀이 모임에 좋은 징조였고, 곧 양쪽 집에서 활기찬 부산함이 시작되었다. 제일 먼저 채비를 마친 베스는 옆집에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 줄곧 알렸고, 창가에서 수시로 전보를 띄워 언니 동생의 단장에 활기를 불어넣었다.\n\n\"천막 든 사람이 간다! 바커 부인이 광주리랑 큼지막한 바구니에 점심을 담는 게 보여. 이제 로런스 할아버지가 하늘이랑 바람개비를 올려다보셔. 할아버지도 같이 가시면 좋겠다. 저기 로리가 있는데, 꼭 선원 같아 ─ 멋진 녀석! 어머, 맙소사! 여기 사람들로 가득 찬 마차가 왔어 ─ 키 큰 부인이랑, 어린 여자애랑, 끔찍한 남자애 둘. 한 명은 다리를 절어. 가엾어라, 목발을 짚었네. 로리가 그 얘긴 안 해 줬잖아. 서둘러, 얘들아! 늦겠어. 어머, 저기 네드 모팻이잖아, 정말이야. 봐 봐, 메그 언니, 저 사람 우리가 장 보러 갔을 때 언니한테 인사했던 그 사람 아니야?\"\n\n\"맞네. 저 사람이 오다니 참 묘하다. 산에 가 있는 줄 알았는데. 저기 샐리도 있어. 제때 돌아와서 다행이야. 나 괜찮아 보여, 조?\" 메그가 들떠서 외쳤다.\n\n\"영락없는 데이지꽃이야. 옷자락 잡고 모자 똑바로 써. 그렇게 비스듬히 쓰니까 감상적으로 보이고, 바람 한 번 불면 날아가겠어. 자 그럼, 가자!\"\n\n\"오 조, 너 그 끔찍한 모자 쓰고 가려는 건 아니지? 너무 우스꽝스러워! 너 스스로를 웃음거리로 만들면 안 돼.\" 조가 로리가 장난삼아 보낸 그 챙 넓은 구식 밀짚모자를 빨간 리본으로 동여매자, 메그가 나무랐다.\n\n\"아니, 난 쓸 거야, 이게 일품이거든 ─ 그늘도 지고, 가볍고, 큼직하잖아. 재미도 있을 거고. 편하기만 하면 웃음거리가 되는 것쯤은 상관없어.\" 그 말과 함께 조는 곧장 성큼성큼 걸어 나갔고, 나머지가 뒤따랐다 ─ 챙 끝 멋스럽게 늘어진 모자 아래로 행복한 얼굴을 하고, 여름옷을 차려입은 채 저마다 가장 멋진 모습을 한 한 무리의 밝은 자매들이었다.\n\n로리가 달려 나와 그들을 맞이하고는, 더없이 다정한 태도로 자기 친구들에게 소개했다. 잔디밭이 응접실이 되어, 한동안 그곳에서 활기찬 장면이 펼쳐졌다. 메그는 케이트 양이 스무 살인데도 미국 소녀들이 본받으면 좋을 만큼 소박하게 차려입은 것을 보고 흐뭇했고, 자기를 보러 특별히 왔다는 네드 씨의 장담에 으쓱했다. 조는 로리가 케이트 이야기를 할 때 어째서 \"입을 새침하게 오므렸\"는지 알 수 있었으니, 그 아가씨는 가까이 오지 마 손대지 마 하는 분위기를 풍겨, 다른 소녀들의 자유롭고 편안한 태도와 뚜렷이 대조되었기 때문이다. 베스는 새로 온 남자애들을 살피더니, 다리 저는 아이는 \"끔찍하\"기는커녕 온순하고 가냘프다고 판단하고, 그 점 때문에 그에게 친절하게 대해 주리라 마음먹었다. 에이미는 그레이스가 예의 바르고 명랑한 어린 아가씨임을 알게 되었고, 몇 분간 말없이 서로를 빤히 쳐다보던 끝에 두 사람은 어느새 아주 좋은 친구가 되었다.\n\n천막과 점심과 크로케 용구가 미리 보내진 터라, 일행은 곧 배에 올랐고, 로런스 할아버지가 강가에서 모자를 흔드는 가운데 두 배가 함께 출발했다. 로리와 조가 한 배의 노를 저었고, 브룩 씨와 네드가 다른 배를 저었다. 한편 떠들썩한 쌍둥이 프레드 본은 성가신 물벌레처럼 거룻배를 타고 이리저리 휘젓고 다니며 두 배를 다 뒤집어 놓으려고 기를 썼다. 조의 우스운 모자는 감사의 표결을 받을 만했으니, 두루 쓸모가 있었기 때문이다. 그것은 시작부터 웃음을 자아내어 어색함을 깨뜨렸고, 조가 노를 저을 때 앞뒤로 펄럭이며 제법 시원한 바람을 일으켰으며, 소나기가 쏟아지면 일행 전체를 위한 훌륭한 우산이 될 거라고 그녀는 말했다. 케이트는 조의 행동에 다소 어리둥절한 표정이었는데, 특히 노를 놓쳤을 때 \"콜럼버스 만세!\"라고 외치고, 로리가 자기 자리에 앉다가 그녀의 발에 걸려 넘어지며 \"이런, 친구, 내가 아프게 했나?\"라고 했을 때 더욱 그러했다. 그러나 안경을 추켜올려 그 별난 소녀를 여러 번 살펴본 끝에, 케이트 양은 그녀가 \"별나긴 해도 제법 영리하다\"고 판단하고, 멀찍이서 그녀에게 미소를 지어 보였다.\n\n다른 배에 탄 메그는 노 젓는 두 사람과 마주 보는, 더없이 흐뭇한 자리에 앉아 있었는데, 두 사람은 경치도 감탄하며 보고, 보기 드문 \"솜씨와 능란함\"으로 노를 깃처럼 눕혀 저었다. 브룩 씨는 진중하고 과묵한 청년으로, 잘생긴 갈색 눈에 듣기 좋은 목소리를 지녔다. 메그는 그의 차분한 태도가 마음에 들었고, 그를 유익한 지식이 가득한 걸어다니는 백과사전이라 여겼다. 그는 그녀에게 말을 많이 거는 법이 없었지만, 그녀를 꽤 자주 바라보았고, 메그는 그가 자기를 싫어하는 눈빛으로 보지는 않는다고 확신했다. 네드는 대학생인지라, 물론 신입생이라면 으레 떠맡아야 할 의무로 여기는 온갖 거드름을 다 피웠다. 그는 그리 똑똑하지는 않았지만 매우 서글서글했고, 두루 보아 소풍을 이끌어 가기에 더없이 좋은 사람이었다. 샐리 가드너는 흰 피케 원피스를 깨끗이 간수하는 데 골몰하면서, 어디에나 끼어드는 프레드와 수다를 떨었는데, 프레드는 그 장난질로 베스를 줄곧 공포에 떨게 했다.\n\n롱메도까지는 멀지 않았지만, 그들이 도착했을 때는 이미 천막이 쳐지고 크로케 골문이 세워져 있었다. 가운데에 넓게 가지를 드리운 떡갈나무 세 그루가 선, 기분 좋은 푸른 들판이었고, 크로케를 할 매끈한 잔디밭이 펼쳐져 있었다.\n\n\"로런스 캠프에 온 걸 환영해!\" 그들이 환호성을 지르며 배에서 내리자, 젊은 주인이 말했다.\n\n\"브룩 선생님이 총사령관이고, 난 병참감, 나머지 녀석들은 참모 장교, 그리고 숙녀 여러분, 당신들은 부대원입니다. 천막은 특별히 여러분을 위한 거고, 저 떡갈나무는 여러분의 응접실, 이쪽은 식당, 그리고 세 번째 나무는 야영지 부엌입니다. 자, 더워지기 전에 한 게임 하고, 그다음에 저녁을 어떻게 할지 봅시다.\"\n\n프랭크와 베스, 에이미, 그레이스는 앉아서 나머지 여덟 명이 하는 경기를 구경했다. 브룩 씨는 메그와 케이트, 프레드를 골랐고, 로리는 샐리와 조, 네드를 데려갔다. 영국 아이들은 잘했지만, 미국 아이들은\n더 잘했다. 마치 76년의 정신(미국 독립 정신을 가리킴 : 옮긴이)이 들린 사람들처럼 한 치의 땅도 끝까지 다투었다. 조와 프레드는 몇 차례 가벼운 충돌을 빚었고, 한 번은 험한 말이 오갈 뻔한 것을 간신히 피했다. 조는 마지막 위켓을 통과했다가 타구를 놓쳤는데, 그 실수에 적잖이 부아가 치밀었다. 프레드는 바로 그녀 뒤에 있었고, 차례가 그녀보다 먼저 돌아왔다. 그가 공을 쳤더니 공이 위켓에 맞고 엉뚱한 쪽으로 한 치쯤 빗나가 멈췄다. 가까이에 아무도 없자, 그는 살피러 달려가는 척하며 발끝으로 슬그머니 공을 툭 밀어 정확히 한 치 옳은 쪽으로 옮겨 놓았다.\n\n\"통과! 자, 조 양, 내가 당신을 처리하고 먼저 들어가겠어.\" 젊은 신사는 다시 한 번 치려고 나무망치를 휘두르며 외쳤다.\n\n\"당신이 공을 밀었잖아. 내가 봤어. 이제 내 차례야.\" 조가 날카롭게 말했다.\n\n\"맹세컨대 난 건드리지 않았어. 약간 굴러갔을지는 몰라도 그건 허용되는 거야. 그러니 비켜 줘, 말뚝을 한 번 칠 테니.\"\n\n\"미국에서는 속임수를 쓰지 않아. 당신이 원하면 쓰겠지만.\" 조가 화를 내며 말했다.\n\n\"양키들이야말로 제일 잔꾀를 잘 부린다는 건 누구나 다 아는 사실이지. 자, 받아라!\" 프레드는 이렇게 받아치며 그녀의 공을 저 멀리 쳐 날려 버렸다.\n\n조는 무슨 무례한 말을 하려고 입을 열었지만, 때맞춰 자신을 다잡고 이마까지 새빨개진 채로 잠시 서서 위켓 하나를 있는 힘껏 땅에 박았다. 그러는 동안 프레드는 말뚝을 맞히고 의기양양하게 자기가 끝났다고 선언했다. 그녀는 공을 가지러 갔고, 덤불 사이에서 한참을 찾았다. 그러나 돌아왔을 때는 차분하고 침착한 얼굴이었고, 참을성 있게 자기 차례를 기다렸다. 잃어버린 자리를 되찾는 데 몇 번의 타구가 필요했다. 거기에 이르렀을 때는 상대편이 거의 이긴 상태였으니, 케이트의 공이 마지막에서 두 번째로 말뚝 가까이에 놓여 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"이런, 우린 다 틀렸어! 잘 가, 케이트. 조 양이 나한테 한 방 빚졌으니, 넌 끝난 거야.\" 모두가 결말을 보려고 가까이 다가서자 프레드가 들뜬 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"양키들은 적에게 너그러운 버릇이 있지.\" 조가 소년의 얼굴을 붉히게 만드는 눈빛으로 말했다. \"특히 그들을 이겼을 때는.\" 그녀는 이렇게 덧붙이며 케이트의 공은 건드리지 않은 채 멋진 한 타로 게임을 이겼다.\n\n로리는 모자를 공중으로 던졌다. 그러다 손님들이 진 것을 두고 우쭐대서는 안 된다는 데 생각이 미쳐, 환호하다 말고 멈추어 친구에게 속삭였다.\n\n\"잘했어, 조! 그 녀석이 속임수를 쓴 거 맞아. 내가 봤어. 대놓고 말할 순 없지만, 다시는 못 그럴 거야. 내 말 믿어.\"\n\n메그는 풀린 머리 가닥을 매만져 주는 척 그녀를 옆으로 끌고 가서 칭찬하듯 말했다.\n\n\"정말 약 오르는 일이었는데, 그래도 화를 참았구나. 정말 다행이야, 조.\"\n\n\"칭찬하지 마, 메그. 난 지금 당장이라도 그 녀석 따귀를 갈길 수 있을 것 같으니까. 입을 다물 만큼 화가 가라앉을 때까지 쐐기풀 사이에 있지 않았다면 분명 끓어 넘쳤을 거야. 지금도 부글부글 끓고 있으니, 그 녀석이 내 앞에 얼씬거리지 않으면 좋겠어.\" 조는 커다란 모자 아래로 프레드를 노려보며 입술을 깨물고 대꾸했다.\n\n\"점심 먹을 시간이군요.\" 브룩 씨가 회중시계를 보며 말했다. \"병참 장교 양반, 메그 양과 샐리 양과 내가 식탁을 차리는 동안 불을 피우고 물을 길어 오겠소? 커피는 누가 잘 끓이지요?\"\n\n\"조가 잘 끓여요.\" 메그가 동생을 추천하게 되어 기뻐하며 말했다. 그래서 조는 최근에 배운 요리 솜씨가 빛을 발하게 되었다는 생각에 커피 주전자를 맡으러 갔고, 그동안 아이들은 마른 나뭇가지를 모았으며 소년들은 불을 피우고 근처 샘에서 물을 길어 왔다. 케이트 양은 그림을 그렸고, 프랭크는 골풀을 엮어 접시 대신 쓸 작은 깔개를 만들고 있던 베스에게 말을 걸었다.\n\n총사령관과 부관들은 곧 식탁보를 펴고 그 위에 먹을거리와 마실 거리를 먹음직스럽게 차려 놓은 다음 푸른 잎사귀로 예쁘게 장식했다. 조가 커피가 다 됐다고 알리자, 모두가 자리를 잡고 푸짐한 식사에 들어갔다. 젊은이들에게는 좀처럼 소화 불량이 없는 데다, 운동은 건강한 식욕을 돋우는 법이니까. 무척 유쾌한 점심이었다. 모든 것이 신선하고 우스워 보였고, 잦은 웃음소리가 근처에서 풀을 뜯던 늙은 말을 놀라게 했다. 식탁이 기분 좋게 울퉁불퉁한 탓에 컵과 접시에 갖은 사고가 일어났다. 도토리가 우유 속으로 떨어졌고, 초대받지 않은 작고 까만 개미들이 음식을 나누어 먹었으며, 보송보송한 애벌레들이 무슨 일인가 구경하려고 나무에서 대롱대롱 내려왔다. 머리가 하얀 아이 셋이 울타리 너머로 빼꼼 들여다보았고, 강 건너편에서는 못마땅한 개 한 마리가 죽을힘을 다해 그들을 향해 짖어 댔다.\n\n\"소금이 여기 있어, 그게 더 좋다면.\" 로리가 조에게 산딸기 한 접시를 건네며 말했다.\n\n\"고마워, 난 거미가 더 좋은데.\" 그녀는 크림 같은 죽음을 맞으러 들어간 부주의한 작은 거미 두 마리를 건져 올리며 대꾸했다. \"당신네 식사는 어느 모로 보나 이렇게 멋진데, 어떻게 그 끔찍한 만찬 파티를 떠올리게 하는 거야?\" 조는 이렇게 덧붙였고, 두 사람은 함께 웃으며 한 접시에서 먹었다. 도자기 그릇이 모자랐기 때문이다.\n\n\"그날 난 정말 좀처럼 없을 만큼 즐거웠고, 아직도 그 기분에서 못 벗어났어. 알다시피 그건 내 공이 아니야. 난 아무것도 안 했거든. 잘 돌아가게 만든 건 너랑 메그랑 브룩 선생님이지. 정말 너희한테 고마워. 더는 먹을 수 없게 되면 우린 뭘 하지?\" 점심이 끝나면 자신의 으뜸패를 다 썼다는 기분이 들어 로리가 물었다.\n\n\"좀 시원해질 때까지 놀이를 하자. 내가 '작가들' 카드를 가져왔고, 케이트 양도 분명히 뭔가 새롭고 재미있는 걸 알 거야. 가서 물어봐. 손님이잖아. 너도 그분이랑 더 어울려야 해.\"\n\n\"너도 손님 아니야? 케이트가 브룩 선생님이랑 잘 맞을 줄 알았는데, 선생님은 줄곧 메그랑만 이야기하고, 케이트는 그 우스꽝스러운 안경 너머로 둘을 빤히 쳐다보기만 하잖아. 나 갈게, 그러니 예의범절 설교 따위는 하지 마. 넌 그런 거 못 하니까, 조.\"\n\n케이트 양은 정말 새로운 놀이를 여럿 알고 있었다. 소녀들은 더 먹으려 들지 않았고 소년들은 더 먹을 수가 없었으므로, 모두 응접실로 자리를 옮겨 '횡설수설'을 하기로 했다.\n\n\"한 사람이 이야기를 시작하는데, 아무 엉터리 같은 내용이라도 좋아요. 원하는 만큼 길게 풀어 나가다가 흥미진진한 대목에서 딱 멈추면 다음 사람이 이어받아 똑같이 하는 거예요. 잘만 하면 무척 재미있어서, 비극과 희극이 완전히 뒤죽박죽 섞인 우스운 이야기가 만들어지지요. 시작해 주세요, 브룩 씨.\" 케이트가 분부하는 듯한 태도로 말하자, 가정 교사를 다른 어느 신사 못지않게 깍듯이 대하던 메그는 깜짝 놀랐다.\n\n두 젊은 숙녀의 발치 풀밭에 누운 브룩 씨는 순순히 이야기를 시작했다. 잘생긴 갈색 눈은 햇살 반짝이는 강물에 줄곧 고정한 채로.\n\n\"옛날 옛적에, 한 기사가 운명을 찾아 세상으로 나섰습니다. 가진 것이라곤 칼과 방패뿐이었으니까요. 그는 오랫동안, 거의 스물여덟 해를 떠돌며 갖은 고생을 하다가 마침내 어느 인자한 노왕의 궁전에 이르렀습니다. 왕은 자신이 무척 아끼는, 훌륭하지만 길들지 않은 망아지 한 마리를 길들여 훈련시키는 자에게 상을 내리겠노라 약속한 터였지요. 기사는 해 보겠다고 했고, 더디지만 착실하게 일을 진척시켰습니다. 망아지는 기개가 넘치는 녀석이라, 변덕스럽고 사납기는 해도 곧 새 주인을 사랑하게 되었거든요. 날마다 왕이 아끼는 이 녀석을 가르칠 때면 기사는 그 말을 타고 시내를 누볐는데, 말을 달리면서 어떤 아름다운 얼굴을 찾아 사방을 살폈습니다. 꿈에서 여러 번 보았으나 한 번도 찾지 못한 얼굴이었지요. 그러던 어느 날, 그가 한적한 거리를 의기양양하게 지나가는데, 다 허물어져 가는 성의 창문에 그 사랑스러운 얼굴이 보이는 게 아니겠습니까. 그는 뛸 듯이 기뻐하며 이 낡은 성에 누가 사는지 물었고, 마법에 걸린 갇힌 공주 여럿이 그곳에 갇혀, 자유를 살 돈을 모으려고 온종일 실을 잣는다는 이야기를 들었습니다. 기사는 그들을 풀어 주고 싶은 마음이 간절했지만, 가난한 처지라 날마다 그 앞을 지나며 그 고운 얼굴을 살피고, 그 얼굴이 햇살 속으로 나오기를 갈망하는 것밖에 할 수 없었지요. 마침내 그는 성안으로 들어가 어떻게 그들을 도울 수 있을지 물어보기로 결심했습니다. 그가 가서 문을 두드리자, 커다란 문이 활짝 열렸고, 그의 눈앞에 나타난 것은—\"\n\n\"황홀하리만치 아름다운 귀부인이었으니, 그녀는 환희에 찬 목소리로 외쳤어요. '드디어! 드디어!'\" 케이트가 이야기를 이어받았다. 프랑스 소설을 읽어 그 문체를 흠모하던 터였다. \"'바로 그녀로구나!' 구스타브 백작은 이렇게 외치며 기쁨에 겨워 그녀의 발치에 쓰러졌습니다. '오, 일어나세요!' 그녀는 대리석처럼 흰 손을 내밀며 말했지요. '안 됩니다! 어떻게 하면 당신을 구할 수 있는지 말씀해 주시기 전에는!' 기사는 여전히 무릎을 꿇은 채 맹세했어요. '아아, 잔인한 운명이 저를 이곳에 묶어 두는군요, 저를 핍박하는 자가 사라질 때까지는.' '그 악당은 어디 있습니까?' '연보랏빛 응접실에요. 가세요, 용감한 분이여, 저를 절망에서 구해 주세요.' '분부 받들겠습니다. 승리하지 못하면 주검이 되어 돌아오겠소!' 이 가슴 떨리는 말을 남기고 그는 달려갔고, 연보랏빛 응접실 문을 벌컥 열어젖히며 막 들어서려는 순간, 그가 맞은 것은—\"\n\n\"검은 가운을 입은 늙은이가 던진 커다란 그리스어 사전에 호되게 한 방 맞은 거였죠.\" 네드가 말했다. \"곧바로 정신을 차린 아무개 경은 그 폭군을 창밖으로 내던지고, 승리한 몸으로 숙녀에게 가려고 돌아섰지만, 이마에 혹이 하나 났답니다. 문이 잠긴 걸 발견하자 커튼을 찢어 밧줄 사다리를 만들고는 절반쯤 내려갔는데, 사다리가 끊어지는 바람에 머리부터 곤두박질쳐 18미터 아래 해자로 떨어졌어요. 오리처럼 헤엄칠 줄 알았던 그는 성 둘레를 첨벙첨벙 헤엄쳐 다니다가 건장한 사내 둘이 지키는 작은 문에 다다랐고, 두 놈의 머리를 마주 부딪쳐 호두 두 알처럼 쪼개 버렸어요. 그러고는 어마어마한 힘을 살짝만 써서 문을 부수고 들어가, 먼지가 30센티미터나 쌓인 돌계단을 올랐는데, 거기엔 주먹만 한 두꺼비들이며 보기만 해도 발작을 일으킬 만한 거미들이 우글거렸답니다, 메그 양. 그 계단 꼭대기에서 그는 숨이 턱 막히고 피가 얼어붙는 광경과 정면으로 맞닥뜨렸으니—\"\n\n\"키 큰 형체가 온통 흰옷을 두르고, 얼굴에는 베일을 드리우고, 앙상한 손에는 등불을 들고 있었어.\" 메그가 이야기를 이어 갔다. \"그것은 손짓하며 소리 없이 미끄러지듯 그의 앞에서 무덤처럼 어둡고 차가운 복도를 따라 나아갔지. 양옆에는 갑옷을 두른 그림자 같은 조각상들이 서 있었고, 죽은 듯한 정적이 감돌았으며, 등불은 푸르게 타올랐어. 유령 같은 형체는 이따금 그를 향해 고개를 돌렸고, 그때마다 흰 베일 너머로 무시무시한 두 눈이 번뜩였지. 그들은 커튼이 드리운 문에 이르렀는데, 그 뒤에서 아름다운 음악 소리가 들려왔어. 그가 들어가려고 앞으로 뛰어들었지만, 망령이 그를 홱 잡아당기더니 그의 눈앞에서 위협하듯 흔들어 댄 것은—\"\n\n\"코담뱃갑이었어.\" 조가 무덤 속에서 울리는 듯한 목소리로 말하자, 듣던 사람들이 자지러졌다. \"'고맙소.' 기사는 정중히 말하며 담배를 한 줌 집어 들었고, 어찌나 세게 일곱 번이나 재채기를 했는지 머리가 뚝 떨어져 버렸어. '하! 하!' 유령이 웃었지. 그러고는 열쇠 구멍으로 죽을힘을 다해 실을 잣는 공주들을 들여다본 다음, 그 악령은 제 희생자를 집어 들어 커다란 양철 상자에 넣었는데, 그 안에는 머리 없는 다른 기사가 열한 명이나 정어리처럼 차곡차곡 들어 있었어. 그들은 모두 일어나서—\"\n\n\"호른파이프 춤을 추기 시작했지.\" 조가 숨을 돌리려 멈추자 프레드가 끼어들었다. \"그들이 춤을 추는 동안, 그 다 쓰러져 가는 낡은 성은 돛을 활짝 편 군함으로 변했어. '삼각돛을 올려라, 윗돛 활대를 줄여라, 키를 바람 아래로 바짝 돌리고, 대포 앞에 붙어라!' 선장이 고함쳤지. 포르투갈 해적선이 시야에 들어왔는데, 앞돛대에 잉크처럼 새까만 깃발을 휘날리고 있었거든. '돌격해서 이겨라, 용맹한 친구들아!' 선장이 말했고, 어마어마한 전투가 시작됐어. 물론 영국이 이겼지. 영국은 늘 이기니까.\"\n\n\"아니, 안 이겨!\" 조가 옆에서 외쳤다.\n\n\"해적 선장을 포로로 잡은 다음, 단숨에 그 스쿠너선을 들이받았는데, 그 갑판에는 시체가 산더미처럼 쌓이고 배수구로는 피가 흘렀어. '단검을 들고 끝까지 싸워라!'는 명령이 떨어졌었거든. '갑판장 조수, 삼각돛 밧줄을 한 가닥 잡아채서 이 악당이 당장 죄를 자백하지 않거든 따끔하게 손봐 줘라.' 영국 선장이 말했지. 포르투갈 놈은 벽돌처럼 입을 꾹 다물고 널빤지 위를 걸어 바다로 떨어졌고, 흥겨운 선원들은 미친 듯이 환호했어. 그런데 그 교활한 놈이 물속으로 잠수해서는 군함 밑으로 올라와 배 밑창에 구멍을 뚫었고, 배는 돛을 활짝 편 채로 '바다 밑바닥으로, 밑바닥으로, 밑바닥으로' 가라앉았는데, 거기에서—\"\n\n\"어머, 세상에! 난 대체 뭐라고 해야 해?\" 프레드가 횡설수설을 끝내자 샐리가 외쳤다. 그는 자기가 좋아하는 책에서 따온 항해 용어와 사실들을 마구잡이로 뒤섞어 놓은 참이었다. \"어, 그래서 그들은 바다 밑바닥으로 가라앉았고, 어여쁜 인어가 그들을 맞이했어. 그런데 머리 없는 기사들이 든 상자를 발견하고는 무척 슬퍼하면서, 그들에 얽힌 수수께끼를 밝혀 보려고 친절하게 소금물에 절여 두었지. 인어도 여자인지라 호기심이 많았거든. 이윽고 잠수부 하나가 내려오자 인어가 말했어. '이 진주 상자를 끌어 올려 주면 당신께 드릴게요.' 인어는 그 가엾은 이들을 되살리고 싶었지만 그 무거운 짐을 혼자서는 들어 올릴 수 없었거든. 그래서 잠수부가 상자를 끌어 올렸는데, 열어 보니 진주는 없어서 무척 실망했지. 그는 상자를 외딴 넓은 들판에 버려두었고, 그것을 발견한 건—\"\n\n\"들판에서 살진 거위 백 마리를 치던 어린 거위지기 소녀였어.\" 샐리의 상상력이 바닥나자 에이미가 말했다. \"그 어린 소녀는 그들이 가여워서 늙은 할멈에게 어떻게 하면 도울 수 있느냐고 물었어. '네 거위들이 일러 줄 게다, 그것들은 뭐든 다 아니까.' 할멈이 말했지. 그래서 소녀는 옛 머리들은 잃어버렸으니 새 머리로 무얼 쓰면 되겠느냐고 물었고, 거위 백 마리가 일제히 백 개의 입을 벌리고 꽥꽥 외쳤어—\"\n\n\"'양배추!'\" 로리가 냉큼 이어받았다. \"'그거 딱 좋겠다.' 소녀가 말하며 텃밭으로 달려가 좋은 놈으로 열두 개를 따 왔어. 그걸 얹어 주자 기사들은 곧바로 되살아나 소녀에게 고맙다고 인사하고는 기뻐하며 길을 떠났는데, 자기들이 달라진 줄은 전혀 몰랐지. 세상에는 그들과 똑같은 머리가 하도 많아서 아무도 대수롭게 여기지 않았거든. 내가 관심 있는 그 기사는 그 고운 얼굴을 찾으러 돌아갔다가, 공주들이 실을 자아 스스로 자유로워졌고 한 사람만 빼고 다들 시집가 버렸다는 걸 알게 됐어. 그는 그 사실에 마음이 무척 어지러워졌지. 그래서 험한 일 궂은일 마다 않고 곁을 지켜 준 그 망아지에 올라타고, 누가 남았는지 보려고 성으로 달려갔어. 울타리 너머로 빼꼼 들여다보니, 마음속 여왕이 정원에서 꽃을 따고 있는 게 보였어. '제게 장미 한 송이를 주시겠습니까?' 그가 말했지. '와서 가져가셔야 해요. 제가 당신께 갈 수는 없답니다, 그건 예의가 아니니까요.' 그녀가 꿀처럼 달콤하게 말했어. 그는 울타리를 넘어가려 했지만 울타리는 점점 더 높아지는 듯했고, 헤치고 들어가려 했더니 점점 더 빽빽해져서 그는 절망에 빠졌어. 그래서 그는 참을성 있게 잔가지를 하나하나 꺾어 마침내 작은 구멍을 냈고, 그 사이로 들여다보며 애원하듯 말했지. '들여보내 주세요! 들여보내 주세요!' 그런데 그 어여쁜 공주는 알아듣지 못하는 모양이었어. 잠자코 장미를 따면서 그가 알아서 길을 뚫고 들어오게 내버려 두었거든. 그가 들어갔는지 못 들어갔는지는 프랭크가 말해 줄 거야.\"\n\n\"난 못 해. 나 안 하잖아, 한 번도 안 했어.\" 프랭크가 말했다. 자기더러 그 우스꽝스러운 한 쌍을 구해 내라며 떠넘긴 그 감상적인 곤경에 당황한 것이다. 베스는 조의 등 뒤로 사라져 버렸고, 그레이스는 잠들어 있었다.\n\n\"그래서 그 가엾은 기사는 울타리에 끼인 채로 남겨지게 되는 건가?\" 브룩 씨가 여전히 강물을 바라보며, 단춧구멍에 꽂은 들장미를 만지작거리며 물었다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 공주가 한참 있다가 그에게 꽃다발을 건네주고 문을 열어 줬을 것 같은데요.\" 로리가 혼자 빙그레 웃으며 가정 교사에게 도토리를 던지며 말했다.\n\n\"이런 엉터리 이야기를 만들어 냈군요! 연습하면 꽤 그럴듯한 걸 만들 수 있겠어요. '진실 게임' 아세요?\" 다들 자기들 이야기를 두고 한바탕 웃은 뒤 샐리가 물었다.\n\n\"그랬으면 좋겠어요.\" 메그가 진지하게 말했다.\n\n\"게임 말이에요.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔데?\" 프레드가 물었다.\n\n\"있잖아, 손을 차곡차곡 포갠 다음 숫자를 하나 정하고, 차례대로 손을 빼는 거야. 그 숫자에서 빠지는 사람이 나머지 사람들이 묻는 어떤 질문에든 솔직하게 대답해야 해. 굉장히 재미있어.\"\n\n\"한번 해 보자.\" 새로운 시도를 좋아하는 조가 말했다.\n\n케이트 양과 브룩 씨, 메그, 네드는 빠지겠다고 했지만, 프레드와 샐리, 조, 로리는 손을 포개고 차례로 뺐다. 그리고 제비가 로리에게 떨어졌다.\n\n\"네 영웅은 누구야?\" 조가 물었다.\n\n\"할아버지하고 나폴레옹.\"\n\n\"여기 있는 숙녀 중에 누가 제일 예쁜 것 같아?\" 샐리가 말했다.\n\n\"마거릿.\"\n\n\"누가 제일 좋아?\" 프레드가 던졌다.\n\n\"당연히 조지.\"\n\n\"무슨 그런 시시한 질문을 하니!\" 나머지 사람들이 로리의 무덤덤한 말투에 웃음을 터뜨리자 조는 멸시하듯 어깨를 으쓱했다.\n\n\"다시 해 봐. 진실 게임도 나쁘진 않네.\" 프레드가 말했다.\n\n\"너한텐 아주 좋은 게임이지.\" 조가 낮은 목소리로 받아쳤다.\n\n다음은 그녀의 차례였다.\n\n\"네 가장 큰 결점이 뭐야?\" 프레드가 정작 자기에게 없는 미덕을 그녀에게서 시험해 보려는 듯 물었다.\n\n\"불같은 성질.\"\n\n\"가장 갖고 싶은 게 뭐야?\" 로리가 물었다.\n\n\"구두끈 한 켤레.\" 조는 그의 속셈을 알아채고 김을 빼며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"솔직한 대답이 아니잖아. 정말로 가장 원하는 걸 말해야지.\"\n\n\"천재성. 너 그거 나한테 줄 수 있으면 좋겠지, 로리?\" 그녀는 실망한 그의 얼굴을 보며 짓궂게 웃었다.\n\n\"남자에게서 가장 우러러보는 미덕은 뭐야?\" 샐리가 물었다.\n\n\"용기하고 정직함.\"\n\n\"이제 내 차례군.\" 프레드가 말했다. 그의 손이 마지막으로 남았던 것이다.\n\n\"이 녀석한테 한 방 먹이자.\" 로리가 조에게 속삭였고, 조는 고개를 끄덕이고는 곧바로 물었다.\n\n\"크로케에서 속임수 쓴 거 아니야?\"\n\n\"뭐, 그래, 좀 썼지.\"\n\n\"좋았어! 네 이야기, 『바다사자』에서 베껴 온 거 아니야?\" 로리가 말했다.\n\n\"뭐, 그런 셈이지.\"\n\"영국 민족이 모든 면에서 완벽하다고 생각하지 않니?\" 샐리가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇게 생각하지 않는다면 내 자신이 부끄러울 거야.\"\n\n\"역시 진짜 존 불(전형적인 영국인을 뜻하는 별칭 : 옮긴이)이군. 자, 샐리 양, 이번엔 손을 뽑지 않아도 기회를 드리지. 우선 당신 마음을 들쑤셔 놓겠어. 당신, 자기가 좀 바람기 있다고 생각하지 않나요?\" 로리가 조에게 고개를 끄덕여 화해가 성립됐다는 신호를 보내며 말했다.\n\n\"이 버릇없는 녀석! 당연히 아니지.\" 샐리가 그 반대임을 증명하는 듯한 태도로 외쳤다.\n\n\"제일 싫은 게 뭐예요?\" 프레드가 물었다.\n\n\"거미하고 쌀 푸딩.\"\n\n\"제일 좋아하는 건요?\" 조가 물었다.\n\n\"춤하고 프랑스제 장갑.\"\n\n\"음, 내 생각엔 진실 게임은 정말 시시한 놀이야. 머리도 식힐 겸 '작가들'이나 점잖게 한판 하자.\" 조가 제안했다.\n\n네드와 프랭크와 어린 소녀들이 이 놀이에 끼었고, 놀이가 이어지는 동안 손위 셋은 따로 앉아 이야기를 나누었다. 케이트 양은 다시 스케치북을 꺼냈고, 마거릿은 그녀를 지켜보았으며, 브룩 씨는 풀밭에 누워 책을 들고 있었으나 읽지는 않았다.\n\n\"정말 잘 그리시네요! 저도 그림을 그릴 수 있으면 좋겠어요.\" 메그가 감탄과 아쉬움이 뒤섞인 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"왜 배우지 않으세요? 보아하니 당신은 그림에 안목도 재능도 있을 것 같은데요.\" 케이트 양이 너그러이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"시간이 없어서요.\"\n\n\"어머님이 다른 재능을 더 좋아하시는 모양이군요. 우리 어머니도 그러셨는데, 제가 몰래 몇 차례 수업을 받아서 재능이 있다는 걸 증명해 보였더니 그제야 계속해도 좋다고 흔쾌히 허락하셨답니다. 당신도 가정 교사를 두고 그렇게 해 보면 어때요?\"\n\"저한텐 가정 교사가 없어요.\"\n\n\"아, 깜박했네요. 미국 아가씨들은 우리보다 학교를 더 많이 다니지요. 게다가 아주 훌륭한 학교들이라고 아빠가 그러시더군요. 당신도 사립 학교에 다니나 보죠?\"\n\n\"전 학교에 아예 안 다녀요. 제가 바로 가정 교사거든요.\"\n\n\"어머, 그러세요!\" 케이트 양은 이렇게 말했지만, 그 어조에는 \"저런, 어쩌면 그리 끔찍할까!\" 하는 뜻이 담겨 있었고, 표정에 어린 무언가에 메그는 얼굴이 붉어지며 그렇게까지 솔직하게 말하지 말걸 하고 후회했다.\n\n브룩 씨가 고개를 들어 얼른 말했다. \"미국 아가씨들은 자기네 선조들 못지않게 독립을 사랑하지요. 그래서 자기 힘으로 살아가는 모습이 칭송받고 존경받습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그럼요. 물론 그렇게 하는 건 아주 보기 좋고 마땅한 일이지요. 우리에게도 그렇게 하는 점잖고 훌륭한 아가씨들이 많은데, 신사의 딸들인 만큼 교양 있고 재주도 뛰어나서 귀족들이 고용한답니다.\" 케이트 양이 짐짓 윗사람 행세하는 어조로 말했다. 그 말은 메그의 자존심에 상처를 주었고, 자기 일이 한층 더 달갑지 않을 뿐 아니라 천하게까지 여겨지게 만들었다.\n\n\"그 독일어 노래는 마음에 드셨나요, 메그 양?\" 브룩 씨가 어색한 침묵을 깨며 물었다.\n\n\"아, 네! 정말 감미로웠어요. 그걸 번역해 주신 분이 누구든 무척 고맙고요.\" 그렇게 말하는 메그의 풀 죽었던 얼굴이 환해졌다.\n\n\"독일어 못 읽으세요?\" 케이트 양이 놀란 표정으로 물었다.\n\n\"잘은 못 읽어요. 저를 가르쳐 주시던 아버지가 멀리 계셔서, 혼자서는 잘 늘지 않더라고요. 발음을 바로잡아 줄 사람이 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"지금 조금 해 보세요. 여기 실러의 『마리아 슈투아르트』가 있고, 가르치기 좋아하는 선생도 있으니.\" 브룩 씨가 권하는 듯한 미소를 지으며 책을 그녀의 무릎 위에 놓았다.\n\n\"너무 어려워서 엄두가 안 나요.\" 메그는 고마우면서도 옆에 앉은 재주 많은 아가씨 앞에서 부끄러워하며 말했다.\n\n\"용기를 북돋아 드리려고 제가 조금 읽어 볼게요.\" 케이트 양은 가장 아름다운 대목 가운데 하나를 흠잡을 데 없이 정확하지만 더없이 무미건조하게 읽었다.\n\n브룩 씨는 케이트 양이 책을 다시 메그에게 돌려줄 때 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 메그는 천진하게 말했다.\n\n\"전 그게 시인 줄 알았어요.\"\n\n\"일부는 시랍니다. 이 대목을 한번 해 보세요.\"\n\n브룩 씨가 가엾은 마리아의 비탄이 담긴 대목을 펼칠 때 그의 입가에는 묘한 미소가 어렸다.\n\n메그는 새 선생이 짚어 주는 기다란 풀잎을 고분고분 따라가며 더듬더듬 수줍게 읽어 나갔는데, 음악적인 목소리의 부드러운 억양 덕에 어려운 단어들이 저도 모르게 시가 되었다. 푸른 길잡이가 책장을 따라 내려갔고, 이윽고 슬픈 장면의 아름다움에 빠져 듣는 이를 잊어버린 메그는 마치 혼자 있는 듯이 읽으며, 불행한 여왕의 대사에 비극의 빛깔을 살짝 입혔다. 만약 그때 그 갈색 눈을 보았다면 그녀는 읽다 말고 멈추었을 테지만, 한 번도 고개를 들지 않은 덕에 그녀의 수업은 망쳐지지 않았다.\n\n\"정말 훌륭해요!\" 메그가 멈추자 브룩 씨가 말했다. 그녀의 숱한 실수는 모르는 체하면서, 정말로 \"가르치기를 좋아하는\" 사람 같은 표정으로.\n\n케이트 양은 안경을 들어 눈앞의 자그마한 정경을 한 차례 살피고는 스케치북을 덮으며 거들먹거리는 투로 말했다.\n\n\"발음이 좋으시네요. 시간이 지나면 글 읽는 솜씨가 제법 늘겠어요. 배워 두시라고 권하고 싶어요. 독일어는 가정 교사에게 값진 재주거든요. 전 그레이스를 좀 돌봐야겠어요, 너무 뛰어다녀서요.\" 케이트 양은 이렇게 말하고 어슬렁어슬렁 자리를 떴고, 어깨를 으쓱하며 속으로 덧붙였다. '난 가정 교사 시중을 들러 온 게 아니야, 어리고 예쁘긴 하지만. 이 양키들은 어쩜 이리 별난 사람들일까. 로리가 이런 사람들 틈에서 완전히 망가지지나 않을까 걱정이네.'\n\n\"영국 사람들이 가정 교사를 좀 깔보고, 우리처럼 대해 주지 않는다는 걸 깜박했어요.\" 메그가 멀어져 가는 뒷모습을 짜증스러운 표정으로 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"가정 교사들도 거기서는 꽤 고생을 한답니다. 저도 뼈저리게 겪어서 알지요. 우리처럼 일하는 사람에게 미국만 한 곳이 없어요, 마거릿 양.\" 그렇게 말하는 브룩 씨의 표정이 어찌나 흡족하고 명랑한지, 메그는 자기 처지를 한탄한 것이 부끄러워졌다.\n\n\"그럼 제가 미국에 사는 게 다행이네요. 제 일이 좋지는 않지만, 그래도 결국엔 거기서 적잖은 보람을 얻으니 불평하지 않을래요. 다만 선생님처럼 저도 가르치는 일을 좋아할 수 있으면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"로리 같은 학생이 있으면 좋아하게 될 거예요. 내년에 그 아이를 떠나보내야 한다니 무척 섭섭하군요.\" 브룩 씨가 잔디밭에 부지런히 구멍을 뚫으며 말했다.\n\n\"대학에 가나 보죠?\" 메그의 입은 그렇게 물었지만, 그녀의 눈은 \"그럼 선생님은 어떻게 되나요?\" 하고 덧붙이고 있었다.\n\n\"네. 갈 때가 됐어요, 준비도 됐고요. 그 아이가 떠나는 대로 저는 군인이 되려고 합니다. 제가 필요하니까요.\"\n\n\"그거 잘됐네요!\" 메그가 외쳤다. \"젊은 남자라면 누구나 가고 싶어 할 것 같아요. 집에 남는 어머니와 누이들에게는 힘든 일이지만요.\" 그녀가 서글프게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"저는 어머니도 누이도 없고, 제가 살든 죽든 마음 써 줄 친구도 거의 없답니다.\" 브룩 씨가 다소 씁쓸하게 말하며, 무심코 시든 장미를 자기가 파 놓은 구멍에 넣고는 작은 무덤처럼 흙으로 덮었다.\n\n\"로리하고 그 애 할아버지는 무척 마음 쓰실 거예요. 그리고 선생님께 무슨 나쁜 일이라도 생긴다면 저희 모두 정말 슬플 거예요.\" 메그가 진심으로 말했다.\n\n\"고마워요. 듣기 좋은 말이군요.\" 브룩 씨가 다시 밝은 얼굴로 입을 떼었지만, 미처 말을 맺기도 전에 네드가 늙은 말에 올라타고 숙녀들 앞에서 승마 솜씨를 뽐내려고 터덜터덜 다가왔고, 그날은 더 이상 조용할 틈이 없었다.\n\n\"넌 말 타는 거 좋아하지 않니?\" 네드를 따라 다른 사람들과 들판을 한 바퀴 돌며 달린 뒤 쉬고 있을 때 그레이스가 에이미에게 물었다.\n\n\"무척 좋아해. 우리 언니 메그도 아빠가 부자였을 땐 말을 탔는데, 지금은 엘런 트리 말고는 말이 없어.\" 에이미가 웃으며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"엘런 트리에 대해 얘기해 줘. 당나귀야?\" 그레이스가 궁금해하며 물었다.\n\n\"있잖아, 조 언니가 말이라면 사족을 못 쓰는데, 나도 그렇거든. 그런데 우리한텐 낡은 곁안장 하나만 있고 말은 없어. 우리 정원에 사과나무가 있는데, 낮게 뻗은 가지가 마침 알맞아. 그래서 조 언니가 거기에 안장을 얹고, 위로 휘어진 부분에 고삐를 매어서, 우린 내키면 언제든 엘런 트리를 타고 깡충깡충 달린단다.\"\n\n\"정말 우습다!\" 그레이스가 웃었다. \"난 집에 조랑말이 있어서, 프레드랑 케이트랑 거의 매일 공원에서 말을 타. 정말 좋아, 친구들도 같이 가거든. 로(승마용 산책로 : 옮긴이)에는 신사 숙녀들이 가득하지.\"\n\n\"어머, 얼마나 멋질까! 나도 언젠가 외국에 가 봤으면 좋겠어. 그래도 로보다는 로마에 가고 싶어.\" 에이미가 말했다. 로가 무엇인지 전혀 몰랐지만 죽어도 묻지는 않을 작정이었다.\n\n어린 소녀들 바로 뒤에 앉아 있던 프랭크는 그들의 이야기를 듣고는, 날렵한 소년들이 온갖 우스꽝스러운 체조 동작을 펼치는 것을 지켜보다가 답답하다는 듯 목발을 멀찍이 밀쳐 냈다. 흩어진 '작가들' 카드를 모으고 있던 베스가 고개를 들어, 수줍지만 다정한 그녀 특유의 말투로 말했다.\n\n\"피곤하신가 봐요. 제가 뭐 도와드릴까요?\"\n\n\"제발 말 좀 걸어 줘요. 혼자 앉아 있으니 따분해서요.\" 집에서 늘 떠받들어진 게 분명한 프랭크가 대답했다.\n\n그가 라틴어로 연설을 해 달라고 부탁했더라도 수줍음 많은 베스에게는 그보다 더 불가능한 일로 느껴지지 않았을 것이다. 하지만 지금은 달아날 곳도 없고, 뒤에 숨을 조도 없었으며, 그 가엾은 소년이 어찌나 간절하게 그녀를 바라보던지, 그녀는 용감하게 해 보기로 마음먹었다.\n\n\"무슨 얘기 하는 거 좋아하세요?\" 베스가 카드를 만지작거리다가, 묶으려던 카드를 절반이나 떨어뜨리며 물었다.\n\n\"음, 난 크리켓이나 보트 타기, 사냥 얘기 듣는 걸 좋아해요.\" 아직 자기 형편에 맞게 취미를 고를 줄 모르는 프랭크가 말했다.\n\n'어쩌나! 어떡하지? 난 그런 거 하나도 모르는데.' 베스는 생각했다. 그러고는 허둥대는 통에 소년의 불행한 처지를 깜박 잊고는, 그가 말을 하게 만들 작정으로 말했다. \"전 사냥하는 걸 한 번도 못 봤지만, 그쪽은 다 잘 아시겠죠.\"\n\n\"한때는 알았죠. 하지만 이젠 다시는 사냥을 못 해요. 빌어먹을 다섯 막대기 울타리를 뛰어넘다가 다쳤거든요. 그래서 저한텐 이제 말도 사냥개도 없답니다.\" 프랭크가 한숨을 내쉬며 말했고, 그 한숨에 베스는 무심코 저지른 실수 때문에 자신이 미워졌다.\n\n\"그쪽 나라 사슴이 우리 못생긴 들소보다 훨씬 더 예뻐요.\" 그녀는 도움을 청하듯 대초원으로 화제를 돌리며 말했다. 조가 즐겨 읽던 소년용 책 가운데 한 권을 읽어 둔 게 다행이라는 생각이 들었다.\n\n들소 이야기는 마음을 누그러뜨리고 흡족하게 만드는 것으로 드러났다. 다른 사람을 즐겁게 해 주려는 열의에 빠져 베스는 자신을 잊었고, 보호해 달라고 애원하던 그 무서운 소년들 가운데 하나와 신나게 떠들고 있는 보기 드문 광경에 언니들이 놀라며 기뻐하는 것도 전혀 알아채지 못했다.\n\n\"어쩜 저렇게 착할까! 그 애가 가여워서 잘해 주는 거야.\" 조가 크로케 경기장에서 베스를 향해 함박웃음을 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 늘 말했잖아, 쟤는 자그마한 성녀라고.\" 메그가 이제 그 점에 더는 의심의 여지가 없다는 듯이 덧붙였다.\n\n\"프랭크가 저렇게 많이 웃는 건 정말 오랜만이야.\" 그레이스가 도토리 깍지로 찻잔 세트를 만들며 인형 이야기를 나누다가 에이미에게 말했다.\n\n\"우리 베스 언니는 마음만 먹으면 아주 까다로운 애야.\" 베스의 성공에 흐뭇해진 에이미가 말했다. 그녀가 하려던 말은 \"매력적인\"이었지만, 그레이스가 두 단어의 정확한 뜻을 몰랐던 덕에 \"까다로운\"도 그럴듯하게 들렸고 좋은 인상을 남겼다.\n\n즉석에서 벌인 곡예와 여우와 거위 놀이, 그리고 사이좋게 한판 벌인 크로케로 오후가 마무리되었다. 해 질 녘에 천막을 걷고, 바구니를 꾸리고, 위켓을 뽑고, 보트에 짐을 실은 다음, 일행은 모두 목청껏 노래를 부르며 강을 따라 떠내려갔다. 네드는 감상에 젖어 구슬픈 후렴이 담긴 세레나데를 흥얼거렸다.\n\n\"외로이, 외로이, 아아! 슬프도다, 외로이,\"\n\n그리고 이런 구절에 이르러—\n\n\"우리는 저마다 젊고, 우리는 저마다 가슴을 지녔거늘,\n아, 어찌하여 우리는 이리 차갑게 떨어져 있어야 하나?\"\n\n그가 어찌나 나른한 표정으로 메그를 바라보던지, 그녀는 그만 웃음을 터뜨려 그의 노래를 망쳐 버렸다.\n\n\"어떻게 나한테 그렇게 모질 수가 있어요?\" 그가 흥겨운 합창 소리에 묻어 속삭였다. \"온종일 그 빳빳하게 풀 먹인 영국 여자한테 딱 붙어 있더니, 이제는 날 쌀쌀맞게 대하는군요.\"\n\n\"그럴 생각은 아니었어요. 하지만 표정이 어찌나 우습던지 정말 어쩔 수가 없었어요.\" 메그는 그의 책망 가운데 앞부분만 슬쩍 넘기며 대답했다. 사실 그녀가 모팻 파티와 그 뒤에 오간 말을 떠올리고 그를 피한 것은 분명했으니까.\n\n기분이 상한 네드는 위로를 받으러 샐리에게로 돌아서며 다소 뾰로통하게 말했다. \"저 여자한텐 바람기라곤 눈곱만큼도 없어, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"손톱만큼도 없지. 하지만 사랑스러운 애야.\" 샐리가 친구의 흠을 인정하면서도 그녀를 두둔하며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"어쨌든 상심한 사슴은 아니로군.\" 네드가 재치를 부려 보려고 한 말이었는데, 그 솜씨는 여느 젊은 신사들과 별반 다르지 않았다.\n\n일행이 모여 든 잔디밭에서 사람들은 정겹게 인사를 나누고 작별을 고하며 흩어졌다. 본 씨네 식구들이 캐나다로 떠날 참이었기 때문이다. 네 자매가 정원을 가로질러 집으로 가는데, 케이트 양이 그들의 뒷모습을 바라보며 거들먹거리는 기색 없는 목소리로 말했다. \"감정을 드러내는 태도가 좀 그렇긴 해도, 미국 아가씨들은 알고 보면 무척 괜찮아요.\"\n\n\"전적으로 같은 생각입니다.\" 브룩 씨가 말했다.\n\n# CASTLES IN THE AIR.\n\n공중누각\n9월의 어느 따스한 오후, 로리는 해먹에 누워 한가로이 앞뒤로 흔들거리며, 이웃집 식구들이 뭘 하고 있을까 궁금해했지만 가서 알아볼 만큼 부지런하지는 못했다. 그날따라 기분이 좋지 않았다. 하루가 보람도 없고 마음에도 차지 않아, 다시 한번 살 수 있다면 좋겠다는 생각이 들 정도였다. 무더운 날씨에 나른해진 그는 공부를 게을리하고, 브룩 씨의 인내심을 한계까지 시험했으며, 오후 내내 피아노를 친답시고 할아버지를 언짢게 했고, 자기 개 한 마리가 미치려 한다고 짓궂게 흘려 하녀들의 혼을 쏙 빼놓았다. 게다가 자기 말을 소홀히 했다는 괜한 트집을 잡아 마부와 험한 말을 주고받은 뒤, 해먹에 몸을 던지고 누워 세상이 온통 멍청하다며 씩씩거렸다. 그러다 아름다운 하루의 평온함이 본인의 뜻과 상관없이 그를 달래 주었다. 머리 위 마로니에의 푸른 그늘을 올려다보며 그는 온갖 꿈을 꾸었고, 마침 세계 일주 항해를 하며 대양 위에서 출렁이는 자신을 상상하던 참인데, 말소리가 들려와 순식간에 그를 뭍으로 끌어내렸다. 해먹의 그물코 사이로 내다보니 마치 집 식구들이 무슨 나들이라도 가는 듯이 밖으로 나오고 있었다.\n\n'대체 저 애들이 지금 뭘 하려는 거지?' 로리는 졸린 눈을 뜨고 자세히 살피려 했다. 이웃집 식구들의 행색에 뭔가 좀 별난 데가 있었던 것이다. 저마다 챙이 넓어 펄럭이는 모자를 쓰고, 갈색 리넨 자루를 한쪽 어깨에 둘러메고, 긴 막대기를 들고 있었다. 메그는 방석을, 조는 책을, 베스는 바구니를, 에이미는 화첩을 들고 있었다. 다들 정원을 조용히 가로질러 작은 뒷문으로 나가더니, 집과 강 사이에 있는 언덕을 오르기 시작했다.\n\n'야, 너무하네!' 로리는 속으로 중얼거렸다. '소풍을 가면서 나한테는 한마디도 안 하다니. 보트를 타려는 건 아닐 거야, 열쇠가 없으니까. 어쩌면 깜박했을지도 몰라. 내가 갖다주면서 무슨 일인지 봐야겠다.'\n\n모자가 여섯 개나 있었는데도 하나 찾는 데 시간이 좀 걸렸다. 그다음엔 열쇠를 뒤졌는데, 결국 자기 주머니에서 나왔다. 그래서 그가 울타리를 뛰어넘어 뒤쫓아 갔을 때는 소녀들이 이미 시야에서 사라진 뒤였다. 보트 창고로 가는 지름길을 택해 그들이 나타나기를 기다렸지만 아무도 오지 않자, 그는 언덕에 올라가 살펴보기로 했다. 언덕 한쪽은 소나무 숲으로 덮여 있었는데, 그 푸른 곳 한복판에서 솔잎의 나직한 한숨이나 귀뚜라미의 졸린 울음보다 또렷한 소리가 들려왔다.\n\n'이거 멋진 풍경인데!' 로리는 덤불 사이로 들여다보며 생각했다. 어느새 잠기운이 가시고 기분도 좋아져 있었다.\n\n정말이지 꽤 어여쁜 그림 같은 정경이었다. 자매들은 그늘진 후미진 곳에 함께 앉아 있었고, 그들 위로 햇살과 그림자가 어른거렸으며, 향긋한 바람이 머리카락을 들어 올려 달아오른 뺨을 식혀 주었고, 작은 숲속 식구들은 그들을 낯선 이가 아니라 오랜 친구라도 되는 양 제 할 일을 계속하고 있었다. 메그는 방석에 앉아 하얀 손으로 곱게 바느질을 하며, 분홍 드레스 차림으로 초록빛 속에서 장미처럼 싱그럽고 어여뻤다. 베스는 근처 솔송나무 아래 수북이 떨어진 솔방울을 골라 모으고 있었으니, 그것으로 예쁜 물건을 만들곤 했기 때문이다. 에이미는 한 무리의 고사리를 스케치하고 있었고, 조는 책을 소리 내어 읽으면서 뜨개질을 했다. 그들을 지켜보는 소년의 얼굴에 그늘이 스쳤다. 초대받지 않았으니 가야 한다는 생각이 들면서도, 집은 너무나 쓸쓸하고 숲속의 이 조용한 모임은 들떠 있는 그의 마음에 더없이 끌렸기에 차마 발길이 떨어지지 않았다. 그가 어찌나 가만히 서 있었던지, 먹이를 거두느라 분주하던 다람쥐 한 마리가 바로 옆 소나무를 타고 내려오다가 그를 보고는 화들짝 되돌아 올라가며 어찌나 날카롭게 꾸짖어 대던지, 베스가 고개를 들었다가 자작나무 뒤의 간절한 얼굴을 발견하고는 안심시키는 미소를 지으며 손짓했다.\n\n\"저 들어가도 될까요? 아니면 방해가 될까요?\" 그가 천천히 다가서며 물었다.\n\n메그가 눈썹을 치켜올렸지만, 조는 도전하듯 언니를 노려보며 곧바로 말했다. \"당연히 되지. 진작 부르려고 했는데, 이런 여자애들 놀이를 네가 좋아하지 않을 줄 알았거든.\"\n\n\"난 너희 놀이라면 늘 좋았어. 그래도 메그가 싫다면 갈게.\"\n\n\"네가 뭐라도 한다면 나는 반대하지 않아. 여기서는 빈둥거리는 게 규칙 위반이거든.\" 메그가 점잖지만 상냥하게 대답했다.\n\n\"고마워. 잠깐만 있게 해 주면 뭐든 할게. 저 아래는 사하라 사막처럼 따분하거든. 바느질을 할까, 책을 읽을까, 솔방울을 주울까, 그림을 그릴까, 아니면 한꺼번에 다 할까? 어떤 곰이든 데려와, 난 준비됐어.\" 로리가 보기에도 흐뭇할 만큼 고분고분한 표정으로 자리에 앉으며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 발뒤꿈치 뜨는 동안 이 이야기 마저 읽어 줘.\" 조가 그에게 책을 건네며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 알겠습니다.\" 그는 고분고분 대답하며 읽기 시작했고, '부지런한 꿀벌 모임'에 끼워 준 호의에 대한 고마움을 증명하려고 최선을 다했다. 이야기는 길지 않았고, 다 읽고 나서 그는 잘한 데 대한 상으로 몇 가지를 물어봐도 되느냐고 조심스레 청했다.\n\n\"저, 여쭤봐도 될까요, 이 대단히 유익하고 멋진 모임이 새로 생긴 건가요?\"\n\n\"얘기해 줘도 될까?\" 메그가 동생들에게 물었다.\n\n\"비웃을걸.\" 에이미가 주의를 주듯 말했다.\n\n\"비웃으면 어때?\" 조가 말했다.\n\n\"좋아할 것 같은데.\" 베스가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"당연히 좋아하지! 절대 안 웃겠다고 약속할게. 얘기해 줘, 조, 겁내지 말고.\"\n\n\"너를 겁낸다니 말도 안 돼! 그러니까 우린 예전에 '천로역정' 놀이를 하곤 했는데, 겨울 내내 여름 내내 그걸 진지하게 계속해 온 거야.\"\n\n\"응, 알아.\" 로리가 아는 체하며 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"누가 말해 줬어?\" 조가 따져 물었다.\n\n\"영혼들이.\"\n\n\"아니야, 내가 그랬어. 언니들이 다 나가고 없던 어느 날 밤에, 이 애가 좀 시무룩하길래 즐겁게 해 주고 싶었거든. 정말 좋아했으니까 나무라지 마, 조.\" 베스가 풀 죽은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"넌 비밀을 못 지킨다니까. 괜찮아, 덕분에 수고를 덜었으니까.\"\n\n\"계속해 줘.\" 조가 살짝 못마땅한 얼굴로 자기 뜨개질에 몰두하자 로리가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 베스가 우리 이 새 계획 얘기는 안 해 줬구나? 그게, 우린 방학을 헛되이 보내지 않으려고 저마다 할 일을 하나씩 정해서 마음을 다해 해 왔어. 방학이 거의 끝나가는데 정해 둔 몫을 다 끝냈으니, 빈둥거리지 않은 게 정말 기뻐.\"\n\n\"응, 그렇겠다.\" 로리는 자신이 빈둥거린 날들을 떠올리며 후회스러워했다.\n\n\"엄마는 우리가 되도록 밖에 많이 나가 있길 바라셔. 그래서 우린 일거리를 여기로 가져와서 즐겁게 지내. 재미 삼아서 이 자루에 물건을 담고, 낡은 모자를 쓰고, 막대기로 언덕을 오르면서, 몇 년 전에 하던 것처럼 순례자 놀이를 하는 거야. 우린 이 언덕을 '기쁨의 산'이라고 부르는데, 여기서 멀리까지 내다보면 우리가 언젠가 살았으면 하는 고장이 보이거든.\"\n\n조가 손가락으로 가리키자, 로리가 몸을 일으켜 살펴보았다. 숲 사이로 트인 곳을 통해, 넓고 푸른 강 너머 건너편 초원, 그리고 저 멀리 대도시의 변두리 너머로, 하늘에 맞닿을 듯 솟아오른 푸른 언덕들이 한눈에 들어왔다. 해는 나직이 걸렸고, 하늘은 가을 노을의 찬란함으로 일구어진 듯했다. 황금빛과 보랏빛 구름이 산마루에 걸려 있었고, 붉은 빛 속으로 높이 솟은 은백색 봉우리들은 어느 천상의 도시의 가뭇한 첨탑처럼 반짝였다.\n\n\"정말 아름답다!\" 로리가 나직이 말했다. 그는 어떤 종류든 아름다움을 재빨리 알아보고 느끼는 사람이었으니까.\n\n\"자주 저래. 우린 저걸 보는 게 좋아, 한 번도 같은 적이 없으면서 늘 멋지거든.\" 에이미가 저것을 그림으로 옮길 수 있으면 좋겠다고 바라며 대답했다.\n\n\"조 언니는 우리가 언젠가 살았으면 하는 고장 얘기를 하는데, 언니가 말하는 건 진짜 시골이야. 돼지랑 닭이 있고, 건초를 만드는 그런 곳 말이야. 그것도 좋겠지만, 난 저 위에 있는 저 아름다운 고장이 진짜라서 우리가 언젠가 갈 수 있으면 좋겠어.\" 베스가 생각에 잠겨 말했다.\n\n\"저것보다 훨씬 더 사랑스러운 고장이 있단다. 우리가 충분히 착해지면 머지않아 그곳으로 가게 될 거야.\" 메그가 고운 목소리로 대답했다.\n\n\"기다리려니 너무 오래 걸리고, 하기도 너무 어려워. 난 저 제비들이 날아가듯 당장 날아가서 저 찬란한 문으로 들어가고 싶어.\"\n\n\"넌 가게 될 거야, 베스, 머지않아. 그건 염려 마.\" 조가 말했다. \"싸우고 일하고, 오르고 기다려야 하는 건 나야. 그러고도 결국 못 들어갈지도 모르고.\"\n\n\"위안이 될지 모르겠지만, 내가 너랑 동무해 줄게. 너의 그 천상의 도시가 눈에 들어오기까지 나는 엄청나게 먼 길을 가야 할 테니까. 내가 늦게 도착하거든 나 좀 잘 말해 줄 거지, 베스?\"\n\n소년의 얼굴에 어린 무언가가 그의 어린 친구를 마음 쓰이게 했다. 하지만 베스는 변해 가는 구름에 잔잔한 눈길을 둔 채 밝게 말했다. \"정말로 가고 싶어 하고, 평생토록 정말로 애쓴다면, 누구든 들어가게 될 거라고 난 믿어. 그 문에는 자물쇠도 없고, 문지기도 없을 것 같거든. 난 늘 그곳이 그림 속에서랑 똑같을 거라고 상상해. 빛나는 이들이 강에서 올라오는 가엾은 크리스천을 맞이하려고 손을 내미는 그 그림 말이야.\"\n\n\"우리가 짓는 공중누각이 다 진짜가 돼서 그 안에서 살 수 있다면 얼마나 재미있을까?\" 잠시 사이를 두었다가 조가 말했다.\n\n\"난 하도 많이 지어 놔서 어느 걸 가질지 고르기도 힘들겠어.\" 로리가 벌렁 누워, 자기를 들킨 그 다람쥐에게 솔방울을 던지며 말했다.\n\n\"제일 마음에 드는 걸 가져야지. 그게 뭔데?\" 메그가 물었다.\n\n\"내 걸 말하면 네 것도 말해 줄래?\"\n\n\"응, 다른 애들도 말한다면.\"\n\n\"우리도 말할게. 자, 로리.\"\n\n\"내가 보고 싶은 만큼 세상을 다 본 다음엔, 독일에 자리를 잡고 마음껏 음악을 누리고 싶어. 나는 유명한 음악가가 될 거고, 온 세상이 내 연주를 들으러 몰려드는 거야. 그리고 돈이나 사업 따위로는 골치 썩일 일 없이, 그저 즐기면서 내가 좋아하는 걸 위해 사는 거지. 그게 내가 제일 좋아하는 누각이야. 네 건 뭐야, 메그?\"\n\n마거릿은 자기 것을 말하기가 좀 어려운 모양이었다. 상상 속 모기라도 쫓듯 양치류 잎을 얼굴 앞에서 흔들며 천천히 말했다. \"난 온갖 호사스러운 것들로 가득한 사랑스러운 집을 갖고 싶어. 맛있는 음식, 예쁜 옷, 멋진 가구, 유쾌한 사람들, 그리고 돈도 산더미처럼. 내가 그 집의 안주인이 되어서 마음대로 꾸려 나가는 거야. 하인도 많아서 내가 손끝 하나 까딱할 필요가 없는 거지. 얼마나 즐거울까! 난 빈둥거리지 않고 좋은 일을 하면서 모두가 나를 진심으로 사랑하게 만들 거야.\"\n\n\"네 공중누각엔 주인 양반은 없는 거야?\" 로리가 짓궂게 물었다.\n\n\"내가 '유쾌한 사람들'이라고 했잖아.\" 메그는 이렇게 말하며 아무도 자기 얼굴을 못 보도록 조심스레 신발 끈을 묶었다.\n\n\"근사하고 지혜롭고 착한 남편에다 천사 같은 아이들도 몇 두고 싶다고 왜 말하지 않아? 그게 없으면 네 누각도 완벽하지 않을 거라는 거 너도 알잖아.\" 조가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. 아직 다정한 공상이라곤 없었고, 책 속에서가 아니면 연애담을 다소 깔보는 그녀였으니까.\n\n\"네 누각엔 말이랑 잉크병이랑 소설책밖에 없겠지.\" 메그가 뾰로통하게 받아쳤다.\n\n\"왜 아니겠어? 난 마구간 가득 아라비아산 준마를 채우고, 방마다 책을 산더미처럼 쌓아 둘 거야. 그리고 마법의 잉크병으로 글을 써서, 내 작품이 로리의 음악만큼 유명해지게 만들 거야. 난 내 누각으로 들어가기 전에 뭔가 근사한 일을 하고 싶어. 영웅적이거나 놀라운 일, 내가 죽고 나서도 잊히지 않을 그런 일 말이야. 그게 뭔지는 모르겠지만, 늘 눈을 부릅뜨고 찾고 있어. 언젠가 너희 모두를 깜짝 놀라게 할 작정이야. 난 책을 써서 부자가 되고 유명해질 것 같아. 그게 내 적성에 맞으니까, 그게 바로 내가 제일 좋아하는 꿈이야.\"\n\n\"내 꿈은 아버지 어머니랑 안전하게 집에 머물면서 식구들 보살피는 걸 돕는 거야.\" 베스가 흡족하게 말했다.\n\n\"넌 다른 건 바라지 않아?\" 로리가 물었다.\n\n\"내 작은 피아노가 생긴 뒤로는 완전히 만족스러워. 그저 우리 모두 건강하게 함께 지낼 수 있기만 바랄 뿐, 다른 건 없어.\"\n\n\"난 바라는 게 엄청 많은데, 그중 으뜸은 화가가 돼서 로마에 가는 거야. 멋진 그림을 그려서 온 세상에서 제일가는 화가가 되는 거지.\" 에이미가 조심스레 소망을 밝혔다.\n\n\"우린 야심 찬 무리네, 그렇지? 베스만 빼고 다들 부자에 유명인이 되고, 모든 면에서 으리으리해지고 싶어 하니. 우리 중에 누구라도 정말 소원을 이루게 될지 궁금해.\" 로리가 생각에 잠긴 송아지처럼 풀잎을 씹으며 말했다.\n\n\"난 내 공중누각 열쇠를 손에 넣었어. 다만 그 문을 열 수 있을지는 두고 봐야겠지.\" 조가 의미심장하게 말했다.\n\n\"나도 내 거 열쇠는 있는데, 써 볼 수가 없어. 대학이고 뭐고!\" 로리가 답답한 듯 한숨을 내쉬며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"내 건 여기 있어!\" 에이미가 연필을 흔들어 보였다.\n\n\"난 아무것도 없어.\" 메그가 풀이 죽어 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 있어.\" 로리가 곧바로 말했다.\n\n\"어디에?\"\n\n\"네 얼굴에.\"\n\n\"말도 안 돼. 그런 건 아무 쓸모도 없어.\"\n\n\"두고 봐, 그게 너한테 가질 만한 뭔가를 가져다주는지 안 가져다주는지.\" 자기가 안다고 여기는 어떤 사랑스러운 작은 비밀을 떠올리며 소년이 웃으며 대답했다.\n\n메그는 양치류 잎 뒤에서 얼굴을 붉혔지만 아무것도 묻지 않았고, 브룩 씨가 그 기사 이야기를 들려줄 때 짓던 것과 똑같은 기대 어린 표정으로 강 건너편을 바라보았다.\n\n\"우리 모두 지금부터 십 년 뒤에도 살아 있거든, 만나서 우리 중 몇이나 소원을 이루었는지, 아니면 그때는 지금보다 얼마나 더 가까워졌는지 보자.\" 언제나 계획을 척척 내놓는 조가 말했다.\n\n\"맙소사! 그럼 난 얼마나 나이를 먹는 거야, 스물일곱이라니!\" 이제 막 열일곱이 되었으면서 벌써 다 큰 어른 같은 기분이던 메그가 외쳤다.\n\n\"너랑 나는 스물여섯, 테디, 베스는 스물넷, 에이미는 스물둘이겠네. 정말 점잖은 모임이겠다!\" 조가 말했다.\n\n\"그때쯤이면 자랑스러워할 만한 일을 뭐라도 해 놨으면 좋겠어. 하지만 난 워낙 게으른 놈이라, '빈둥거리고' 있을까 봐 걱정이야, 조.\"\n\n\"엄마가 그러시는데, 넌 동기가 필요한 거래. 그걸 손에 넣으면 분명히 멋지게 해낼 거라고 확신하셔.\"\n\n\"그러셔? 맹세코 해낼 거야, 기회만 잡는다면!\" 로리가 갑자기 기운이 솟아 벌떡 일어나 앉으며 외쳤다. \"할아버지를 기쁘게 해 드리는 것만으로 만족해야 마땅하고, 나도 애를 쓰긴 해. 그런데 그게 영 마음과는 어긋나는 일이라 힘이 들어. 할아버지는 당신이 그러셨던 것처럼 나를 인도 무역상으로 만들고 싶어 하시는데, 난 차라리 총 맞는 게 낫겠어. 차나 비단, 향신료 따위, 할아버지의 그 낡은 배들이 실어 오는 온갖 잡동사니가 다 싫어. 그게 내 거가 되면 죄다 바다 밑으로 가라앉아 버리든 말든 상관없어. 대학에 가는 걸로 만족하셔야 할 텐데. 내가 사 년을 바치면 사업에서는 놓아주셔야 마땅하잖아. 그런데도 할아버지는 고집을 부리셔. 내가 우리 아버지처럼 뛰쳐나가 내 멋대로 살지 않는 한, 할아버지가 하신 그대로 따라야만 하는 거지. 할아버지 곁을 지켜 줄 사람이 누구라도 남아 있다면, 난 당장 내일이라도 그렇게 할 거야.\"\n\n로리는 흥분해서 말했고, 자그마한 빌미만 생겨도 그 협박을 실행에 옮길 듯한 얼굴이었다. 그는 무척 빠르게 자라고 있었고, 게으른 태도와는 달리 속박을 싫어하는 젊은이의 마음과, 제 힘으로 세상에 부딪쳐 보고 싶어 하는 젊은이의 들뜬 갈망을 품고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"네 배 가운데 하나에 올라타고 멀리 떠나서, 네 방식대로 해 보기 전엔 절대 집에 돌아오지 마.\" 조가 말했다. 그런 대담한 모험을 떠올리자 상상력에 불이 붙었고, 그녀가 \"테디가 당하는 부당한 일\"이라고 부르는 것 때문에 동정심이 솟았던 것이다.\n\n\"그건 옳지 않아, 조. 너도 그렇게 말하면 안 되고, 로리도 네 나쁜 충고를 따르면 안 돼. 넌 할아버지가 바라시는 대로 해야 해, 얘야.\" 메그가 더없이 어머니다운 목소리로 말했다. \"대학에서 최선을 다하렴. 네가 당신을 기쁘게 하려고 애쓰는 걸 보시면, 분명 너한테 모질거나 부당하게 굴지 않으실 거야. 네 말대로 곁에 머물며 할아버지를 사랑해 드릴 사람이 달리 없잖니. 허락도 없이 떠나 버린다면 넌 절대 자신을 용서하지 못할 거야. 우울해하거나 안달하지 말고 네 도리를 다해. 그러면 착한 브룩 선생님처럼 존경받고 사랑받는 보답을 얻게 될 거야.\"\n\n\"넌 그 사람에 대해 뭘 안다고 그래?\" 로리가 물었다. 좋은 충고는 고마웠지만 설교는 마뜩잖았고, 평소답지 않게 속을 터뜨린 뒤라 화제를 자기에게서 돌릴 수 있어 다행이었다.\n\n\"네 할아버지가 그분에 대해 우리한테 들려주신 것밖에 몰라. 그분이 어머니가 돌아가실 때까지 정성껏 보살펴 드렸고, 어머니를 두고 떠날 수 없다며 어느 훌륭한 분의 가정 교사로 외국에 가는 것도 거절했다는 거랑, 지금은 자기 어머니를 돌봐 준 노부인을 부양하면서도 누구한테도 그 얘기를 안 하고, 그저 더없이 너그럽고 참을성 있고 착하게 지낸다는 거 말이야.\"\n\n\"그래, 정말 그래, 그 좋은 양반!\" 메그가 자기 이야기로 얼굴이 상기되고 진지해진 채 말을 멈추자 로리가 진심으로 말했다. \"그분 모르게 그분에 대해 다 알아내고, 그분의 착한 마음씨를 남들한테 죄다 얘기해서 사람들이 그분을 좋아하게 만드는 건 우리 할아버지다워. 브룩 선생님은 너희 어머니가 왜 자기한테 그렇게 잘해 주시는지, 나랑 함께 초대하고 그 아름답고 다정한 태도로 대해 주시는지 영문을 몰라 하셨어. 너희 어머니가 더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 분이라고 여기시면서 며칠이고 그 얘기를 하셨고, 너희 모두에 대해서도 입에 침이 마르도록 칭찬하셨어. 내가 소원을 이루게 되면, 내가 브룩 선생님을 위해 뭘 할지 두고 봐.\"\n\n\"이제부터 뭐라도 시작해, 그분 속을 그만 썩이는 것부터.\" 메그가 날카롭게 말했다.\n\n\"내가 그런다는 걸 어떻게 알아, 아가씨?\"\n\n\"난 그분이 돌아가실 때 얼굴만 보면 늘 알 수 있어. 네가 착하게 굴었으면 만족스러운 표정으로 활기차게 걸어가시고, 네가 속을 썩였으면 침울한 얼굴로 천천히 걸어가시거든, 마치 되돌아가서 자기 일을 더 잘하고 싶다는 듯이.\"\n\n\"이거 참 어이없네! 그러니까 네가 브룩 선생님 얼굴에서 내 잘잘못 점수를 매겨 둔다, 이거지? 그분이 너희 집 창문 앞을 지나며 인사하고 미소 짓는 건 봤지만, 너희가 그런 전신기를 차려 놓은 줄은 몰랐는걸.\"\n\n\"그런 건 없어. 화내지 말고, 아 제발, 내가 무슨 말 했다고 그분한테 이르진 마! 그저 네가 어떻게 지내는지 내가 마음 쓴다는 걸 보이려던 것뿐이야. 그리고 여기서 한 말은 비밀로 한 거라는 거 알잖아.\" 메그가 자기 경솔한 말에서 뭐가 비롯될지 생각하며 몹시 당황해 외쳤다.\n\n\"난 고자질 같은 거 안 해.\" 로리가 조가 이따금 그가 짓는 어떤 표정을 두고 \"거만하고 도도하다\"라고 부르던 바로 그 태도로 대답했다. \"다만 브룩 선생님이 온도계 노릇을 할 작정이라면, 나도 정신 차리고 그분이 좋은 날씨를 보고할 수 있게 해 드려야겠군.\"\n\n\"제발 기분 상해하지 마. 난 설교를 하거나 고자질을 하거나 어리석게 굴 생각은 없었어. 그저 조가 네가 나중에 후회할 마음을 부추기는 것 같아서 그랬어. 넌 우리한테 어찌나 잘해 주는지, 우린 네가 우리 오빠 같아서 생각하는 대로 다 말하는 거야. 용서해 줘, 좋은 뜻으로 한 말이었어.\" 메그가 다정하면서도 머뭇거리는 몸짓으로 손을 내밀었다.\n\n순간의 짜증이 부끄러워진 로리는 그 친절한 작은 손을 꼭 쥐고 솔직하게 말했다. \"용서받아야 할 사람은 나야. 내가 까칠하게 굴었고, 하루 종일 심사가 뒤틀려 있었거든. 난 네가 내 결점을 말해 주고 누나처럼 대해 주는 게 좋아. 그러니 내가 가끔 투덜대더라도 신경 쓰지 마, 어쨌든 고마워.\"\n\n기분이 상하지 않았다는 걸 보여 주려고 단단히 마음먹은 그는 더없이 싹싹하게 굴었다. 메그를 위해 무명실을 감아 주고, 조를 즐겁게 하려고 시를 읊고, 베스를 위해 솔방울을 흔들어 떨어뜨리고, 에이미의 고사리 작업을 거들면서, 자신이 '부지런한 꿀벌 모임'에 들어올 만한 사람임을 증명했다. 거북의 생활 습성에 대해 한창 활기차게 토론하던 중에(그 온순한 녀석 한 마리가 강에서 어슬렁어슬렁 올라온 참이었다), 어렴풋한 종소리가 들려와 해나가 차를 \"우려\" 놓았으니 저녁을 먹으러 집에 갈 시간이 빠듯하다는 걸 일러 주었다.\n\n\"또 와도 돼?\" 로리가 물었다.\n\n\"응, 입문서 속 소년들이 들은 대로, 네가 착하게 굴고 네 책을 사랑한다면.\" 메그가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"노력할게.\"\n\n\"그럼 와도 좋아. 그리고 내가 스코틀랜드 사람들처럼 뜨개질하는 법을 가르쳐 줄게. 요즘 양말이 잘 나가거든.\" 조가 헤어지는 문간에서, 자기 뜨개질감을 커다란 파란 털실 깃발처럼 흔들며 덧붙였다.\n\n그날 밤, 베스가 황혼 녘에 로렌스 씨에게 피아노를 연주해 드릴 때, 로리는 커튼 그늘에 서서 그 어린 다윗의 연주에 귀를 기울였다. 그 소박한 음악은 언제나 그의 침울한 마음을 가라앉혀 주었으니까. 그리고 그는 백발의 머리를 한 손에 괴고 앉아, 그토록 사랑했던 죽은 아이를 다정하게 떠올리는 노인을 바라보았다. 오후의 대화를 떠올리며, 그 희생을 기꺼이 치르겠다는 다짐을 품고 소년은 속으로 말했다. '내 누각은 포기하자. 그리고 저 다정한 노인이 나를 필요로 하시는 한 곁을 지키자. 내가 할아버지에게는 전부니까.'\n비밀\n\n조는 다락방에서 무척 바빴다. 10월의 날들이 쌀쌀해지기 시작했고 오후가 짧아졌기 때문이다. 두세 시간 동안 높다란 창으로 햇살이 따스하게 들어와, 낡은 소파에 앉아 부지런히 글을 쓰는 조를 비추었다. 그녀 앞 트렁크 위에는 원고가 펼쳐져 있었고, 머리 위 들보에서는 애완 쥐 스크래블이 산책을 했는데, 자기 수염을 무척 자랑스러워하는 게 분명한 훤칠한 맏아들을 거느리고 있었다. 일에 푹 빠진 조는 마지막 장이 다 채워질 때까지 끄적거리다가, 자기 이름을 멋들어지게 휘갈겨 서명하고는 펜을 내던지며 외쳤다.\n\n\"자, 최선을 다했어! 이걸로 안 되면 더 잘 쓸 수 있을 때까지 기다려야겠지.\"\n\n소파에 등을 기대고 누워, 그녀는 원고를 처음부터 끝까지 꼼꼼히 읽으며 여기저기 줄을 긋고, 작은 풍선처럼 보이는 느낌표를 잔뜩 집어넣었다. 그러고는 산뜻한 빨간 리본으로 원고를 묶고는, 잠시 진지하고 간절한 표정으로 그것을 바라보며 앉아 있었는데, 그 표정에는 그녀의 작업이 얼마나 진심이었는지가 고스란히 드러나 있었다. 이 위쪽에 있는 조의 책상은 벽에 걸린 낡은 양철 부엌 살림이었다. 그녀는 그 안에 원고와 책 몇 권을 넣어, 스크래블에게서 안전하게 닫아 두었다. 그 녀석도 문학적인 취향이 있는 터라, 손닿는 곳에 있는 책이라면 그 책장을 갉아 먹어 일종의 순회 도서관으로 삼기를 즐겼던 것이다. 조는 이 양철 보관함에서 또 다른 원고를 꺼냈고, 둘 다 주머니에 넣은 다음, 자기 친구들이 펜을 갉고 잉크를 맛보도록 내버려 둔 채 조용히 아래층으로 살금살금 내려갔다.\n\n그녀는 되도록 소리를 내지 않고 모자와 재킷을 걸친 다음, 뒤쪽 통로 창으로 가서 낮은 현관 지붕 위로 빠져나가, 풀이 우거진 둑으로 몸을 흔들어 내려서서는 빙 둘러 가는 길로 큰길에 이르렀다. 일단 거기 이르자 그녀는 마음을 가다듬고 지나가는 승합 마차를 불러 세워 시내로 굴러갔는데, 무척 즐겁고도 알 수 없는 표정이었다.\n\n누가 그녀를 지켜보았다면 그 행동이 사뭇 별나다고 여겼을 것이다. 마차에서 내린 그녀는 어느 번잡한 거리의 어떤 번지에 이를 때까지 빠른 걸음으로 내달았다. 어렵사리 그곳을 찾아낸 그녀는 출입구로 들어가 지저분한 계단을 올려다보더니, 잠시 꼼짝 않고 서 있다가 별안간 거리로 뛰쳐나와 왔던 것만큼이나 빠르게 걸어가 버렸다. 이런 동작을 그녀는 몇 번이나 되풀이했고, 맞은편 건물 창가에서 빈둥거리던 검은 눈의 젊은 신사가 그것을 무척 재미있어했다. 세 번째로 되돌아왔을 때, 조는 몸을 한 번 부르르 떨고는 모자를 눈 위로 푹 눌러쓰고서, 마치 이를 죄다 뽑으러 가는 사람 같은 얼굴로 계단을 올라갔다.\n\n출입구를 장식한 여러 간판 가운데 치과 간판이 하나 있었다. 멋진 치아 한 벌에 눈길을 끌려고 천천히 벌어졌다 닫혔다 하는 인공 턱 한 쌍을 잠시 빤히 쳐다본 뒤, 그 젊은 신사는 외투를 걸치고 모자를 집어 들더니 아래로 내려가 맞은편 출입구에 자리를 잡았다. 그러고는 미소를 지으면서도 몸서리를 치며 말했다.\n\n\"혼자 오는 게 그 애답지. 하지만 힘든 일을 겪고 나면 집까지 데려다줄 사람이 필요할 거야.\"\n\n십 분 뒤, 조가 얼굴이 새빨개져서는, 무슨 고된 시련이라도 막 치르고 나온 사람 같은 행색으로 계단을 뛰어 내려왔다. 젊은 신사를 보자 그녀는 도무지 반갑지 않은 표정으로 고개만 까딱하고 지나쳤지만, 그는 동정하는 기색으로 따라오며 물었다.\n\n\"많이 힘들었어?\"\n\n\"별로.\"\n\n\"빨리 끝났네.\"\n\n\"응, 다행히도!\"\n\n\"왜 혼자 갔어?\"\n\n\"아무도 몰랐으면 해서.\"\n\n\"넌 내가 본 사람 중에 제일 별난 애야. 몇 개나 뽑았어?\"\n\n조는 무슨 말인지 모르겠다는 듯이 친구를 바라보다가, 무언가가 어지간히 우습다는 듯 웃기 시작했다.\n\n\"뽑아냈으면 하는 게 두 개 있긴 한데, 일주일은 기다려야 해.\"\n\n\"뭐가 그렇게 우스워? 너 무슨 꿍꿍이가 있구나, 조.\" 로리가 어리둥절한 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"너야말로 그렇지. 거기 위 당구장에서 뭐 하고 있었어, 도련님?\"\n\n\"실례지만, 아가씨, 거긴 당구장이 아니라 체육관이었고, 난 펜싱 수업을 받고 있었답니다.\"\n\n\"그거 다행이네.\"\n\n\"왜?\"\n\n\"네가 나한테 가르쳐 주면, 우리가 햄릿을 공연할 때 네가 레어티스를 맡아서 펜싱 장면을 멋지게 만들 수 있잖아.\"\n\n로리가 소년다운 호탕한 웃음을 터뜨리자, 지나가던 사람 몇몇이 자기도 모르게 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"햄릿을 공연하든 안 하든 내가 가르쳐 줄게. 정말 신나는 데다 네 자세도 멋지게 곧게 펴 줄 거야. 그런데 네가 그렇게 단호하게 '다행이네'라고 한 게 그 이유만은 아닌 것 같은데, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"응, 난 네가 당구장에 없었던 게 다행이었어. 네가 그런 데는 절대 가지 않길 바라거든. 가니?\"\n\n\"자주는 안 가.\"\n\n\"안 갔으면 좋겠어.\"\n\n\"별일 아니야, 조. 우리 집에도 당구대가 있지만, 잘 치는 상대가 없으면 재미가 없거든. 그래서 난 당구를 좋아하니까, 가끔 가서 네드 모팻이나 다른 친구들이랑 한판 치는 거야.\"\n\n\"어머, 어쩌나, 정말 안타까워. 그러다 점점 더 좋아하게 돼서 시간이랑 돈을 낭비하고, 그 끔찍한 애들처럼 돼 버릴 거 아냐. 난 정말 네가 점잖게 지내면서 친구들한테 기쁨이 돼 주길 바랐는데.\" 조가 고개를 저으며 말했다.\n\n\"가끔 죄 없는 즐길 거리 좀 즐긴다고 사내가 점잖음을 잃기야 하겠어?\" 로리가 발끈한 표정으로 물었다.\n\n\"그건 어떻게, 어디서 즐기느냐에 달렸지. 난 네드랑 그 패거리가 싫어. 그러니 너도 거기 끼지 않았으면 좋겠어. 엄마는 네드가 우리 집에 오고 싶어 해도 못 오게 하셔. 그리고 네가 그 애처럼 되면, 엄마는 우리가 지금처럼 너랑 어울려 노는 걸 더는 허락하지 않으실 거야.\"\n\n\"안 하실까?\" 로리가 걱정스레 물었다.\n\n\"응, 엄마는 멋만 부리는 젊은 남자들을 못 견뎌 하셔. 우리가 그런 애들이랑 어울리게 하느니 우리를 모자 상자 속에 죄다 가둬 버리실걸.\"\n\n\"그래도 아직 모자 상자를 꺼내실 필요는 없어. 난 멋만 부리는 부류도 아니고, 그럴 생각도 없으니까. 다만 가끔 해롭지 않은 장난은 좋아해, 너도 그렇잖아?\"\n\n\"응, 그건 아무도 뭐라 안 하니까 실컷 장난쳐도 돼. 하지만 막 나가진 않을 거지? 안 그러면 우리 좋은 시간도 다 끝나 버릴 테니까.\"\n\n\"난 두 번 정제한 성인군자가 될게.\"\n\n\"난 성인군자는 질색이야. 그저 소박하고 정직하고 점잖은 남자애가 돼 줘. 그럼 우린 절대 널 버리지 않을 테니까. 네가 킹 씨네 아들처럼 군다면 난 어떻게 해야 할지 정말 모르겠어. 그 애는 돈은 넘쳐 났지만 쓸 줄을 몰라서, 술에 절고 노름을 하고 도망쳤다가, 아버지 이름을 위조하기까지 했다나 봐. 어쨌든 아주 형편없었대.\"\n\n\"넌 내가 그렇게 될 것 같아? 참 고맙기도 하다.\"\n\n\"아니, 그렇진 않아. 어머, 세상에, 절대 아니지! 다만 사람들이 돈이 그렇게 큰 유혹이라고 떠드는 걸 들으면, 난 가끔 네가 차라리 가난했으면 싶어. 그럼 걱정 안 할 텐데.\"\n\n\"넌 내 걱정을 하는 거야, 조?\"\n\n『난 가끔 네가 침울하거나 불만스러운 표정을 지을 때면 조금 걱정이 돼. 넌 의지가 워낙 강해서, 한번 잘못된 길로 들어서면 멈추게 하기가 어려울 것 같거든.』\n\n로리는 잠시 말없이 걸었고, 조는 괜히 입을 놀렸다 싶어 후회하면서 그를 지켜보았다. 그의 입가에는 그녀의 충고를 비웃기라도 하듯 미소가 걸려 있었지만, 눈빛은 화가 나 보였다.\n\n『집까지 가는 내내 설교만 늘어놓을 작정이야?』 이윽고 그가 물었다.\n\n『그럴 리가. 왜?』\n\n『그럴 거면 난 합승마차를 탈 거고, 아니라면 너랑 같이 걸으면서 아주 재미있는 얘길 하나 들려줄게.』\n\n『더는 설교 안 할 테니까, 그 얘기 무지하게 듣고 싶다.』\n\n『좋아, 그럼. 가자. 비밀인데, 내가 말해 주면 너도 네 비밀을 말해 줘야 해.』\n\n『난 비밀 같은 거 없는데.』 조가 입을 떼다 말고 뚝 멈췄다. 자기에게도 비밀이 있다는 사실이 떠올랐던 것이다.\n\n『있으면서. 넌 아무것도 못 숨겨. 그러니까 어서 털어놔, 안 그러면 나도 말 안 할 테니까.』 로리가 소리쳤다.\n\n『네 비밀은 괜찮은 거야?』\n\n『그럼, 끝내주지! 네가 아는 사람들 얘기라 얼마나 재밌다고! 넌 꼭 들어야 해. 나도 이걸 말하고 싶어서 한참을 좀이 쑤셨거든. 자, 네가 먼저.』\n\n『집에 가서 아무 말도 안 할 거지?』\n『한마디도 안 해.』\n\n『그리고 둘만 있을 때 날 놀려먹지도 않을 거지?』\n\n『난 놀려먹은 적 없어.』\n\n『있거든. 넌 사람들한테서 원하는 건 죄다 끄집어내잖아. 어떻게 하는지는 모르겠지만, 넌 타고난 살살이야.』\n\n『고맙군. 어서 말해 봐.』\n\n『그게, 내가 신문사 사람한테 소설을 두 편 맡겼는데, 다음 주에 답을 준대.』 조가 비밀을 털어놓는 상대의 귀에 대고 속삭였다.\n\n『미국의 저명한 여류 작가, 마치 양 만세!』 로리가 모자를 하늘로 던졌다가 다시 받으며 외쳤다. 그 바람에 오리 두 마리, 고양이 네 마리, 암탉 다섯 마리, 그리고 아일랜드 아이들 여섯 명이 신이 났다. 두 사람은 어느새 도시를 벗어나 있었던 것이다.\n\n『쉿! 아마 아무 일도 안 될 거야. 그래도 한번 해 보지 않고는 견딜 수가 없었어. 누구한테도 말 안 한 건, 괜히 다른 사람까지 실망하는 게 싫어서야.』\n\n『실패할 리 없어. 조, 매일같이 쏟아지는 그 쓰레기 같은 글들 절반에 비하면, 네 소설은 셰익스피어 작품이라고. 활자로 박혀 나온 걸 보면 얼마나 재밌을까. 우리 여류 작가가 얼마나 자랑스러울까!』\n\n조의 눈이 반짝였다. 누군가 자기를 믿어 준다는 건 언제나 기분 좋은 일이고, 친구의 칭찬은 신문에 실린 수십 마디 찬사보다 늘 더 달콤한 법이다.\n\n『그래서 네 비밀은? 공평하게 해, 테디. 안 그러면 다신 널 안 믿을 거야.』 조는 격려의 말 한마디에 활활 타오른 눈부신 희망을 애써 꺼뜨리며 말했다.\n\n『이걸 말하면 나 곤란해질지도 몰라. 그래도 말 안 하겠다고 약속한 적은 없으니까 할게. 솔깃한 소식을 들으면 너한테 털어놓기 전엔 영 마음이 편치 않거든. 나 메그 누나 장갑이 어디 있는지 알아.』\n\n『그게 다야?』 로리가 알 듯 말 듯 신비로운 표정을 잔뜩 짓고서 고개를 끄덕이며 눈을 반짝이자, 조는 실망한 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n『지금으로선 그걸로 충분해. 장갑이 어디 있는지 들으면 너도 그렇게 생각할걸.』\n\n『그럼 말해 봐.』\n\n로리는 몸을 숙여 조의 귀에 세 마디를 속삭였고, 그러자 우스꽝스러운 변화가 일어났다. 조는 잠시 그 자리에 멈춰 서서 놀라움과 못마땅함이 뒤섞인 얼굴로 그를 빤히 쳐다보다가, 다시 걸음을 옮기며 날카롭게 물었다. 『어떻게 알았어?』\n\n『봤거든.』\n\n『어디서?』\n\n『주머니에서.』\n\n『그동안 내내?』\n\n『응. 낭만적이지 않아?』\n\n『아니, 끔찍해.』\n\n『맘에 안 들어?』\n\n『당연하지. 말도 안 돼. 그런 건 허락할 수 없어. 세상에! 메그 언니가 알면 뭐라고 하겠어?』\n\n『아무한테도 말하면 안 돼. 명심해.』\n\n『나 약속 안 했어.』\n\n『그건 당연한 거고, 난 너를 믿었어.』\n\n『뭐, 일단 지금은 말 안 할게. 그래도 정말 역겨워. 차라리 말해 주지 말걸 그랬어.』\n\n『난 네가 좋아할 줄 알았지.』\n\n『누가 메그 언니를 데려가려 한다는 생각에? 사양하겠어.』\n\n『누군가 널 데려가려 하면 그땐 기분이 좀 나아질걸.』\n\n『누가 한번 그래 보라지.』 조가 사납게 외쳤다.\n\n『나도 그게 보고 싶군!』 로리가 그 생각에 킥킥거렸다.\n\n『난 비밀이랑 영 안 맞나 봐. 네가 그 얘기 한 뒤로 마음이 다 구겨진 기분이야.』 조가 다소 고마운 줄도 모르고 말했다.\n\n『나랑 이 언덕 아래까지 달리기 시합하자. 그럼 괜찮아질 거야.』 로리가 제안했다.\n\n주위엔 아무도 없었다. 매끄러운 길이 눈앞으로 비스듬히 뻗어 있어 절로 마음을 끌었다. 도저히 뿌리칠 수 없는 유혹에 조는 쏜살같이 내달렸고, 금세 모자와 빗을 뒤에 떨어뜨린 채 머리핀까지 흩뿌리며 달렸다. 로리가 먼저 결승점에 닿았고, 자기 처방이 성공한 데 무척 흡족해했다. 그의 아탈란타가 헝클어진 머리를 날리며, 눈을 빛내고 두 뺨이 발그레해진 채로, 얼굴에 불만이라곤 조금도 없이 헐떡이며 다가왔으니 말이다.\n\n『내가 말이었으면 좋겠어. 그럼 이 멋진 공기 속을 몇 마일이고 달려도 숨이 안 찰 텐데. 정말 끝내줬어. 그런데 이것 봐, 내 꼴이 영 우습게 됐잖아. 가서 내 물건들 좀 주워 와, 천사 같은 녀석.』 조는 단풍나무 아래 털썩 주저앉으며 말했다. 그 나무는 진홍빛 잎으로 둑을 카펫처럼 뒤덮고 있었다.\n\n로리는 잃어버린 물건들을 찾으러 느긋하게 떠났고, 조는 다시 단정해질 때까지 아무도 지나가지 않기를 바라며 땋은 머리를 둘둘 말아 올렸다. 그런데 정말로 누군가 지나갔으니, 다름 아닌 메그였다. 외출했다 오는 길이라, 나들이옷에 외출복까지 차려입은 메그는 유난히 숙녀다워 보였다.\n\n『대체 여기서 뭐 하는 거야?』 메그가 헝클어진 동생을 보고 점잖게 놀라며 물었다.\n\n『나뭇잎 줍고 있어.』 조가 방금 쓸어 모은 발그레한 한 줌을 추리며 얌전히 대답했다.\n\n『머리핀도요.』 로리가 머리핀 여섯 개쯤을 조의 무릎에 던지며 덧붙였다. 『이 길에선 이런 게 자라나거든요, 메그 누나. 빗이랑 갈색 밀짚모자도요.』\n\n『너 또 달렸구나, 조. 어떻게 그럴 수 있어? 그 우당탕거리는 버릇은 대체 언제 그만둘 거야?』 메그가 나무라듯 말하며, 바람에 흐트러진 머리를 매만지고 소맷부리를 단정히 했다.\n\n『뻣뻣하게 늙어서 지팡이 짚을 때까지는 안 그만둘 거야. 날 제 나이보다 빨리 어른으로 만들려고 하지 마, 언니. 언니가 이렇게 갑자기 변하는 것만으로도 충분히 힘들단 말이야. 그러니까 나는 할 수 있는 한 오래오래 어린애로 있게 해 줘.』\n\n말하면서 조는 떨리는 입술을 감추려고 나뭇잎 위로 몸을 숙였다. 요즘 들어 마거릿이 빠르게 어엿한 여인이 되어 가는 걸 느끼던 차에, 로리의 비밀까지 듣고 나니 언젠가 반드시 닥칠, 그리고 이제는 무척 가까워 보이는 이별이 두려워졌던 것이다. 로리는 그녀의 얼굴에 어린 근심을 알아채고, 얼른 화제를 돌리려 메그에게 물었다. 『그렇게 곱게 차려입고 어디 다녀오는 거예요?』\n\n『가드너 댁에. 샐리가 벨 모펏 결혼식 얘기를 죄다 들려줬어. 정말 굉장했대. 그 부부는 파리에서 겨울을 나러 떠났다지 뭐야. 그게 얼마나 멋질지 한번 생각해 봐!』\n\n『부러워요, 메그 누나?』 로리가 물었다.\n\n『그런 것 같아.』\n\n『그거 잘됐네!』 조가 모자를 홱 잡아당겨 묶으며 중얼거렸다.\n\n『왜?』 메그가 놀란 얼굴로 물었다.\n\n『언니가 재물을 그렇게 좋아한다면, 가난한 남자한텐 시집갈 일이 없을 테니까.』 조는 자기 말조심하라고 말없이 눈치를 주는 로리를 향해 인상을 찌푸리며 말했다.\n\n『난 누구한테든 \"시집가는\" 일 같은 건 없을 거야.』 메그가 자못 위엄 있게 걸음을 옮기며 말했고, 나머지 둘은 그 뒤를 따르며 웃고 속닥이고 물수제비를 떴다. 메그는 속으로 \"꼭 어린애들 같다니까\" 하고 생각했지만, 제일 좋은 옷만 입지 않았더라면 자기도 끼고 싶었을지 모른다.\n\n한두 주 동안 조의 행동이 어찌나 별나던지 자매들은 어리둥절하기 짝이 없었다. 우체부가 종을 울리면 문으로 달려나갔고, 브룩 씨를 만날 때마다 무례하게 굴었으며, 비탄에 잠긴 얼굴로 메그를 멍하니 바라보다가 이따금 벌떡 일어나 흔들어 대더니 또 입을 맞추곤 했는데, 그 모양이 영 알쏭달쏭했다. 게다가 로리와 조는 늘 서로에게 신호를 주고받으며 \"독수리 날개를 활짝\"이 어쩌고 떠들어 댔으니, 자매들은 둘 다 제정신이 아니라고 단언할 지경이었다. 조가 창문으로 빠져나간 그날로부터 두 번째 토요일, 창가에 앉아 바느질을 하던 메그는 로리가 정원을 가로지르며 조를 쫓아다니다 마침내 에이미의 정자에서 붙잡는 광경에 기겁했다. 그 안에서 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 메그는 볼 수 없었지만, 까르르 터지는 웃음소리에 이어 두런두런 말소리와 신문지 퍼덕이는 소리가 요란하게 들려왔다.\n\n『저 애를 대체 어쩌면 좋아? 끝까지 숙녀답게 굴 줄을 모른다니까.』 메그가 못마땅한 얼굴로 그 추격전을 지켜보며 한숨지었다.\n\n『난 안 그랬으면 좋겠어. 언니는 지금 그대로가 너무 재밌고 사랑스럽잖아.』 베스가 말했다. 베스는 조가 자기 말고 다른 누군가와 비밀을 나눈다는 사실에 조금 서운했지만, 한 번도 그런 내색을 한 적이 없었다.\n\n『정말 골치 아파. 하지만 우린 언니를 도무지 \"콩미 라 포(품위 있게 : 옮긴이)\"로 만들 수가 없나 봐.』 에이미가 덧붙였다. 그녀는 한쪽에 앉아 자기가 쓸 새 주름 장식을 만드는 중이었는데, 곱슬머리를 아주 예쁘게 묶어 올린 데다 그 두 가지 흐뭇한 일 덕분에 평소보다 우아하고 숙녀다운 기분에 젖어 있었다.\n\n잠시 후 조가 통통 튀어 들어오더니 소파에 벌렁 드러누워 책 읽는 시늉을 했다.\n\n『거기 뭐 재밌는 거라도 있어?』 메그가 짐짓 너그러운 척 물었다.\n\n『그냥 소설일 뿐인데, 별거 아닌 것 같아.』 조가 신문 제목을 들키지 않으려고 조심하며 대꾸했다.\n\n『소리 내서 읽어 봐. 그럼 우리도 재밌고, 너도 딴짓 안 하고 좋잖아.』 에이미가 어른스러운 척 잔뜩 말투를 꾸며 말했다.\n\n『제목이 뭐야?』 베스가 조가 왜 자꾸 신문 뒤로 얼굴을 가리는지 의아해하며 물었다.\n\n『경쟁하는 화가들.』\n\n『재밌겠다. 읽어 봐.』 메그가 말했다.\n\n『흠!』 조는 큰 소리로 헛기침을 하고 길게 숨을 들이쉰 뒤 아주 빠르게 읽어 나갔다. 자매들은 흥미진진하게 귀를 기울였다. 이야기는 낭만적이었고, 끝에 가서 등장인물 대부분이 죽는 바람에 다소 애처롭기도 했다.\n\n『그 멋진 그림 얘기가 마음에 들어.』 조가 잠시 멈추자 에이미가 흡족하다는 듯 한마디 했다.\n\n『난 사랑하는 대목이 더 좋아. 비올라랑 안젤로는 우리가 좋아하는 이름이잖아. 신기하지 않니?』 메그가 눈물을 훔치며 말했다. \"사랑하는 대목\"이 비극적이었던 것이다.\n\n『이거 누가 쓴 거야?』 조의 얼굴을 슬쩍 엿본 베스가 물었다.\n\n읽던 사람이 별안간 벌떡 일어나 신문을 내던지자, 발갛게 상기된 얼굴이 드러났다. 그러더니 엄숙함과 흥분이 우습게 뒤섞인 표정으로 큰 소리로 대답했다. 『너희 언니지.』\n\n『언니가?』 메그가 바느질감을 떨어뜨리며 소리쳤다.\n\n『정말 잘 썼다.』 에이미가 비평가라도 된 듯 말했다.\n\n『그럴 줄 알았어! 그럴 줄 알았다고! 아, 우리 조 언니, 나 정말 자랑스러워!』 베스가 언니에게 달려가 끌어안으며 이 눈부신 성공을 함께 기뻐했다.\n\n세상에, 다들 어찌나 좋아하던지! 메그는 신문에 박힌 \"조세핀 마치 양\"이라는 글자를 두 눈으로 확인하고서야 믿었고, 에이미는 이야기의 예술적인 부분을 점잖게 비평하며 후속편에 쓸 만한 조언까지 내놓았지만, 안타깝게도 주인공 남녀가 죽어 버린 탓에 실현될 수는 없었다. 베스는 신이 나서 깡충깡충 뛰며 노래를 불렀고, 해나는 \"어머나 세상에, 내 살다 살다 이런 일은 처음일세!\" 하고 들어와 \"저 조 아씨가 한 일\"에 입을 떡 벌리며 감탄했다. 마치 부인은 소식을 듣고 무척이나 자랑스러워했다. 조는 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채로 웃으며, 이제 아주 공작새가 되어 버려도 그만이라고 너스레를 떨었다. 그리고 신문이 이 손에서 저 손으로 건네지는 동안, \"활짝 펼친 독수리\"가 마치 가문 위로 의기양양하게 날갯짓을 한다고 해도 좋을 만했다.\n\n『다 얘기해 봐.』 『언제 온 거야?』 『얼마 받았어?』 『아버지가 뭐라고 하실까?』 『로리가 웃겠지?』 식구들이 조를 빙 둘러싸고서 한꺼번에 외쳐 댔다. 이 어리석고도 다정한 사람들은 집안의 자그마한 기쁨 하나하나를 죄다 축제로 만들었던 것이다.\n\n『재잘대지 좀 말아 봐. 그럼 죄다 얘기해 줄 테니까.』 조가 말했다. 그러면서 버니 양은 「에벨리나」를 두고 자기가 「경쟁하는 화가들」을 두고 느끼는 것보다 조금이라도 더 우쭐한 기분이 들었을까 궁금해했다. 조는 자기가 소설을 어떻게 처리했는지 들려준 뒤 이렇게 덧붙였다. 『답을 들으러 갔더니, 그 사람이 둘 다 마음에 든다면서도 초보자한테는 원고료를 안 주고 신문에 실어 주면서 소설을 봐주는 거래. 좋은 연습이 될 거고, 초보자가 실력이 늘면 누구든 돈을 줄 거라더라. 그래서 두 편을 그냥 줬어. 그랬더니 오늘 이게 온 거야. 로리가 이걸 들고 있는 날 붙잡고는 기어이 보겠다고 우겨서 보여 줬지. 그랬더니 잘 썼다면서, 나더러 더 쓰라고, 다음 건 자기가 원고료를 받게 해 주겠대. 나 정말 행복해. 머잖아 내 힘으로 먹고살면서 다들 도울 수 있을지도 모르잖아.』\n\n여기서 조는 숨이 차올랐다. 그러더니 신문지에 얼굴을 파묻고는 자기의 자그마한 소설 위로 자연스레 솟구친 눈물 몇 방울을 떨구었다. 자립해서, 사랑하는 사람들에게 칭찬받는 것이야말로 그녀가 마음속 깊이 가장 간절히 바라던 소망이었고, 이 일이 바로 그 행복한 끝을 향한 첫걸음처럼 여겨졌던 것이다.\n\n# A TELEGRAM.\n\n15장 전보\n『11월은 한 해를 통틀어 가장 고약한 달이야.』 어느 흐린 오후, 마거릿이 창가에 서서 서리 맞은 정원을 내다보며 말했다.\n\n『그러니까 내가 11월에 태어났지.』 조가 코에 묻은 잉크 얼룩도 모른 채 시무룩하게 말했다.\n\n『지금 아주 즐거운 일이 하나라도 생기면, 우린 11월을 근사한 달이라고 여길걸.』 11월조차 희망적으로 바라보는 베스가 말했다. 베스는 무엇이든 좋게 보는 아이였다.\n\n『그렇겠지. 하지만 이 집안엔 즐거운 일이라곤 도통 안 생기잖아.』 심사가 뒤틀린 메그가 말했다. 『변화라곤 눈곱만큼도 없이, 재미라곤 거의 없이, 날이면 날마다 죽어라 일만 하고. 차라리 쳇바퀴 도는 다람쥐 신세가 낫겠다.』\n\n『세상에, 우리 왜 이렇게 우울해!』 조가 외쳤다. 『하긴, 우리 가엾은 언니를 탓할 수도 없지. 다른 애들은 신나게 놀러 다니는데 언니는 일 년 내내 갈고 또 갈아 대니까. 아, 내가 소설 속 주인공들한테 하듯 언니 일도 척척 처리해 줄 수 있다면! 언니는 이미 충분히 예쁘고 충분히 착하니까, 부유한 친척이 느닷없이 언니한테 큰 재산을 물려주게 하는 거야. 그럼 언니는 상속녀가 되어 화려하게 등장해서, 언니를 무시했던 사람들을 죄다 비웃어 주고, 외국에 나갔다가 눈부시게 우아하고 화려한 모습으로 무슨무슨 귀부인이 되어 돌아오는 거지.』\n\n『요즘 세상엔 그런 식으로 재산을 물려받는 사람 없어. 남자는 일을 해야 하고, 여자는 돈 보고 시집을 가야 하는걸. 정말이지 끔찍하게 불공평한 세상이야.』 메그가 씁쓸하게 말했다.\n\n『조 언니랑 내가 다들 부자로 만들어 줄 테니까, 딱 십 년만 기다려 봐. 우리가 안 그러나.』 한쪽 구석에 앉아 진흙 파이를 만들던 에이미가 말했다. 해나는 에이미가 빚는 그 작은 점토 새며 과일이며 얼굴 모형을 그렇게 불렀다.\n\n『못 기다려. 그리고 잉크랑 진흙은 영 미덥지가 않은걸. 그래도 너희 마음 씀씀이는 고맙다.』\n\n메그는 한숨을 쉬며 다시 서리 맞은 정원으로 고개를 돌렸다. 조는 끙 소리를 내며 두 팔꿈치를 식탁에 괸 채 풀이 죽었지만, 에이미는 기운차게 점토를 두드려 댔다. 그리고 맞은편 창가에 앉아 있던 베스가 미소 지으며 말했다. 『지금 당장 즐거운 일이 두 가지나 생길 거야. 엄마가 길을 따라 오고 계시고, 로리가 무슨 좋은 소식이라도 있는 양 정원을 성큼성큼 가로질러 오고 있어.』\n\n두 사람이 함께 들어섰다. 마치 부인은 늘 하던 대로 『얘들아, 아버지한테서 편지 왔니?』 하고 물었고, 로리는 특유의 설득하는 말투로 말했다. 『누구 마차 드라이브하러 갈 사람 없어요? 머리가 뒤죽박죽될 때까지 수학을 붙잡고 씨름했더니, 신나게 한 바퀴 돌면서 머리 좀 식히려고요. 날이 흐리긴 해도 공기는 나쁘지 않아요. 브룩 선생님도 댁까지 모셔다드릴 거라, 밖은 어떨지 몰라도 안은 즐거울 거예요. 가요, 조. 조랑 베스는 갈 거지?』\n\n『당연히 가야지.』\n\n『고맙지만 난 바빠.』 메그가 바느질 바구니를 홱 꺼내며 말했다. 적어도 자기만큼은 그 젊은 신사와 너무 자주 마차를 타고 다니지 않는 게 좋겠다고 어머니와 합의해 둔 터였다.\n\n『우리 셋은 금방 준비할게요.』 에이미가 손을 씻으러 후다닥 달려가며 외쳤다.\n\n『제가 뭐 도와드릴 일 없을까요, 어머님?』 로리가 마치 부인의 의자에 몸을 기대며 물었다. 그는 늘 부인에게 다정한 눈빛과 말투를 건넸다.\n\n『고맙지만 없단다. 그래도 괜찮다면 우체국에 좀 들러 주겠니, 얘야. 오늘이 편지 오는 날인데 우체부가 아직 안 왔구나. 아버지는 해처럼 어김없는 분인데, 아마 오는 길에 무슨 사정이 생긴 모양이야.』\n\n날카로운 초인종 소리가 그녀의 말을 끊었고, 잠시 후 해나가 편지 한 통을 들고 들어왔다.\n\n『그 고약한 전보라는 거예요, 마님.』 해나가 마치 그게 터져서 무슨 사달이라도 낼까 봐 겁내듯 건네며 말했다.\n\n\"전보\"라는 말에 마치 부인은 편지를 낚아채 두 줄짜리 내용을 읽더니, 그 작은 종잇장이 심장에 총알이라도 박은 듯 새하얗게 질려 의자에 털썩 기댔다. 로리가 물을 가지러 아래층으로 뛰어 내려가는 동안, 메그와 해나가 부인을 부축했고, 조는 겁에 질린 목소리로 소리 내어 읽었다.\n『마치 부인께,\n\n남편께서 위독하십니다. 즉시 와 주십시오.\nS. 헤일, 워싱턴 ◯◯ 병원』\n\n자매들이 숨죽여 귀 기울이던 그 순간, 방 안은 어찌나 고요했던지. 바깥은 어찌나 기이하게 어두워졌는지. 그리고 온 세상이 어찌나 별안간 뒤바뀐 듯했는지. 자매들은 어머니를 빙 둘러쌌고, 삶의 모든 행복과 버팀목이 곧 빼앗길 것만 같았다. 마치 부인은 이내 정신을 차렸다. 전보를 다시 한번 읽고는 딸들에게 두 팔을 벌리며, 두고두고 잊지 못할 목소리로 말했다. 『지금 당장 떠나마. 하지만 너무 늦었을지도 몰라. 아, 얘들아, 얘들아, 이걸 견디게 도와주렴!』\n\n몇 분 동안 방 안에는 흐느낌 소리뿐이었다. 거기에 더듬거리는 위로의 말, 돕겠다는 다정한 다짐, 그리고 눈물 속에 사그라드는 희망 어린 속삭임이 뒤섞였다. 가엾은 해나가 가장 먼저 정신을 차리고는, 자기도 모르게 지혜를 발휘해 나머지 사람들에게 좋은 본보기가 되었다. 그녀에게 일이란 거의 모든 괴로움을 다스리는 만병통치약이었던 것이다.\n\n『하느님, 우리 주인어른을 지켜 주소서! 저는 울면서 시간 낭비 안 하고, 당장 마님 짐부터 꾸리겠습니다.』 해나는 진심을 담아 말하고는, 앞치마로 얼굴을 닦고 거친 손으로 안주인의 손을 따뜻하게 꼭 쥔 뒤, 혼자서 세 사람 몫의 일을 하러 나갔다.\n\n『해나 말이 맞아. 지금은 눈물 흘릴 때가 아니야. 얘들아, 진정하렴. 어미가 생각 좀 하게.』\n\n가엾은 자매들은 진정하려 애썼다. 어머니가 몸을 일으켜 앉았다. 창백하지만 굳건한 얼굴로, 슬픔은 한쪽으로 밀어 두고 아이들을 위해 궁리하고 계획을 세웠다.\n\n『로리는 어디 있니?』 생각을 추스르고 가장 먼저 해야 할 일들을 정한 뒤, 부인이 이내 물었다.\n\n『여기 있어요, 부인. 아, 저도 뭐든 하게 해 주세요!』 소년이 옆방에서 황급히 달려 나오며 외쳤다. 그는 이들의 첫 슬픔이 너무도 신성해 자기처럼 다정한 눈길조차 들여다봐선 안 될 것 같아 그 방으로 물러나 있던 참이었다.\n\n『전보를 쳐서 즉시 가겠다고 전해 다오. 다음 기차가 내일 아침 일찍 떠나니, 그걸 타마.』\n\n『그 밖에는요? 말은 준비됐어요. 어디든 가고, 뭐든 할게요.』 그는 당장이라도 땅끝까지 날아갈 듯한 기세로 말했다.\n\n『마치 고모님 댁에 쪽지를 좀 전해 주렴. 조, 펜이랑 종이 좀 다오.』\n\n조는 방금 정서한 원고 가운데 한 장을 골라 뒷면 빈 곳을 찢어 내고, 어머니 앞으로 탁자를 끌어다 놓았다. 길고 서글픈 여정에 쓸 돈을 빌려야 한다는 걸 잘 알았기에, 아버지를 위해서라면 그 액수에 한 푼이라도 보탤 수만 있다면 무엇이든 할 수 있을 것 같았다.\n\n『이제 가 보렴, 얘야. 하지만 무작정 내달리다 몸 상하게 하진 말고. 그럴 필요 없으니까.』\n\n마치 부인의 당부는 보나 마나 허사였다. 오 분 뒤, 로리가 자기의 날쌘 말을 타고 목숨이라도 건 듯 창문 앞을 쏜살같이 지나갔으니 말이다.\n\n『조, 킹 부인 댁으로 달려가서 내가 못 간다고 전하렴. 가는 길에 이것들 좀 사 오고. 적어 줄게. 다 필요한 것들이야. 간병할 채비를 단단히 하고 가야 하거든. 병원 물품이 늘 좋은 건 아니니까. 베스, 너는 로런스 어른께 가서 오래된 포도주를 두어 병만 부탁드려 봐. 아버지를 위해서라면 구걸하는 것도 부끄럽지 않아. 아버지껜 뭐든 가장 좋은 걸 드려야지. 에이미, 해나한테 검은 트렁크를 내려 달라고 전하렴. 그리고 메그, 너는 와서 내 짐 찾는 것 좀 도와줘. 정신이 반쯤 나간 것 같구나.』\n\n쓰고, 생각하고, 지시하는 일을 한꺼번에 하려니 가엾은 부인은 머리가 어지러울 만도 했다. 메그는 부인에게 잠시 방에서 조용히 앉아 쉬시라고, 나머지는 자기들이 하겠다고 졸랐다. 모두가 돌풍 앞의 나뭇잎처럼 흩어졌다. 그리고 그 종잇장이 무슨 사악한 주문이라도 되는 양, 조용하고 행복하던 집안은 순식간에 산산이 흐트러졌다.\n\n로런스 씨가 베스와 함께 부랴부랴 돌아왔다. 친절한 노신사는 환자를 위해 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 위안거리를 챙겨 왔고, 어머니가 없는 동안 딸들을 지켜 주겠다는 더없이 다정한 약속까지 건네어 부인의 마음을 크게 위로했다. 그는 자기 실내복부터 길동무가 되어 드리겠다는 제안까지, 못 내놓을 것이 없었다. 하지만 마지막 것만큼은 불가능했다. 마치 부인은 노신사가 그 먼 길을 함께하겠다는 말을 도무지 받아들이려 하지 않았다. 그래도 그가 그 얘길 꺼냈을 때 부인의 얼굴에 안도하는 기색이 비쳤으니, 근심에 싸인 사람에게 여행길 동행이란 영 거추장스러운 법이었던 것이다. 노신사는 그 표정을 알아채고는 짙은 눈썹을 찌푸리고 두 손을 비비더니, 금방 돌아오겠다는 말을 남기고 휙 가 버렸다. 아무도 그를 다시 떠올릴 겨를이 없었는데, 메그가 한 손에 덧신을, 다른 한 손에 찻잔을 들고 현관을 가로질러 달려가다가 그만 브룩 씨와 딱 마주쳤다.\n\n『이런 일을 듣게 되어 정말 유감입니다, 마치 양.』 그가 친절하고 차분한 목소리로 말했는데, 그 음성은 심란한 그녀의 마음에 무척 다정하게 들렸다. 『어머님의 길동무가 되어 드리고자 찾아왔습니다. 로런스 어른께서 워싱턴에 처리할 일을 맡기셨거든요. 거기서 어머님께 도움을 드릴 수 있다면 저로서도 정말 기쁜 일이고요.』\n\n덧신이 툭 떨어졌고, 찻잔도 하마터면 따라 떨어질 뻔했다. 메그가 손을 내미는데, 그 얼굴에 어찌나 고마움이 가득하던지, 브룩 씨로서는 이제부터 치를 사소한 시간과 수고쯤은 물론이고 그보다 훨씬 큰 희생을 했더라도 충분히 보상받았다고 느꼈을 것이다.\n\n『다들 어쩜 이렇게 친절하실까요! 엄마도 분명 받아들이실 거예요. 곁에서 엄마를 돌봐 줄 분이 있다는 걸 알면 얼마나 마음이 놓일지 몰라요. 정말, 정말 고맙습니다!』\n\n메그는 진심으로 말했고, 자기 자신은 까맣게 잊고 있었는데, 자기를 내려다보는 갈색 눈동자에 담긴 무언가에 식어 가는 차가 퍼뜩 떠올랐다. 그녀는 어머니를 불러오겠다고 말하며 그를 응접실로 안내했다.\n\n로리가 마치 고모의 쪽지를 들고 돌아왔을 무렵엔 모든 준비가 끝나 있었다. 쪽지에는 부탁한 돈과 함께, 전에도 여러 번 한 말을 되풀이하는 몇 줄이 적혀 있었다. 마치가 군대에 들어가는 건 어처구니없는 짓이라고 늘 일러두지 않았느냐, 그래 봐야 좋을 게 하나도 없을 거라고 늘 예언하지 않았느냐, 다음번엔 부디 자기 충고를 새겨들으면 좋겠다는 내용이었다. 마치 부인은 쪽지를 난롯불에 던지고 돈은 지갑에 넣은 뒤, 입술을 꾹 다문 채로 채비를 이어 갔다. 만약 조가 그 자리에 있었다면 그 표정의 뜻을 알아챘을 것이다.\n\n짧은 오후가 저물어 갔다. 다른 심부름은 죄다 끝났고, 메그와 어머니는 꼭 필요한 바느질에 매달렸으며, 베스와 에이미는 차를 준비했고, 해나는 자기 말마따나 \"탁탁 쾅쾅\" 하며 다림질을 마쳤지만, 조는 여태 돌아오지 않았다. 다들 슬슬 걱정이 되기 시작했고, 로리가 그녀를 찾으러 나섰다. 조가 무슨 엉뚱한 생각을 머릿속에 떠올릴지는 아무도 몰랐기 때문이다. 그런데 로리는 그녀와 엇갈렸고, 조는 아주 묘한 표정을 지은 채 걸어 들어왔다. 그 얼굴엔 재미와 두려움, 만족과 후회가 뒤섞여 있어 식구들을 어리둥절하게 했는데, 그녀가 어머니 앞에 내려놓은 지폐 뭉치도 그에 못지않게 식구들을 어리둥절하게 했다. 조가 조금 목멘 소리로 말했다. 『이건 아버지를 편안하게 해 드리고 집으로 모셔 오는 데 보태는 내 몫이야!』\n\n『얘야, 이걸 어디서 났니? 이십오 달러나! 조, 설마 무슨 경솔한 짓을 한 건 아니겠지?』\n\n『아니야, 떳떳하게 번 내 돈이야. 구걸한 것도, 빌린 것도, 훔친 것도 아니야. 내가 벌었어. 엄마도 날 나무라진 못할걸. 내 것을 팔았을 뿐이니까.』\n\n말하면서 조가 모자를 벗자, 일제히 탄성이 터져 나왔다. 그 풍성하던 머리카락이 죄다 짧게 잘려 있었던 것이다.\n\n『네 머리카락이! 그 아름다운 머리카락이!』 『아, 조, 어떻게 그럴 수가? 네 유일한 자랑이었는데.』 『얘야, 그럴 것까진 없었는데.』 『이제 내 조 같지가 않구나. 그래도 그렇게 해 준 게 너무 사랑스러워!』\n\n저마다 탄성을 쏟아 내고 베스가 짧아진 머리를 다정하게 끌어안는 동안, 조는 짐짓 아무렇지 않은 척했지만 그 누구도 손톱만큼도 속아 넘어가지 않았다. 조는 갈색 더부룩한 머리를 마구 헝클며 마음에 든다는 표정을 지어 보이려 애쓰며 말했다. 『이게 나라의 운명을 바꾸는 것도 아닌데, 그만 울어, 베스. 내 허영심에도 약이 될 거야. 내 가발을 너무 자랑스러워하던 참이었거든. 이 덥수룩한 걸 떼어 내니 머리도 맑아지겠지. 머리가 기분 좋게 가볍고 시원해. 이발사 말이 곧 곱슬곱슬하게 자랄 거래. 그럼 소년처럼 보이고, 잘 어울리고, 손질하기도 쉬울 거라더라. 난 만족해. 그러니까 그 돈 받아 줘, 그리고 저녁 먹자.』\n\n『다 얘기해 봐, 조. 난 영 만족스럽진 않다만, 그렇다고 널 나무랄 순 없구나. 네가 사랑하는 사람을 위해 네 말마따나 허영심을 얼마나 기꺼이 희생했는지 아니까. 하지만 얘야, 그럴 것까진 없었어. 머잖아 네가 후회하게 될까 봐 걱정이구나.』 마치 부인이 말했다.\n\n『아니야, 절대 안 그럴 거야!』 조가 야무지게 대꾸하며, 자기 장난이 아주 호되게 야단맞지는 않은 데 한결 마음을 놓았다.\n\n『대체 왜 그랬어?』 자기 머리를 자르느니 차라리 목을 자르는 게 낫다고 여길 에이미가 물었다.\n\n『그게, 아버지를 위해 뭐라도 하고 싶어서 안달이 났거든.』 식탁에 둘러앉으며 조가 대답했다. 건강한 젊은이들은 근심 한가운데서도 밥은 먹는 법이니까. 『난 엄마만큼이나 돈 빌리는 게 싫어. 그리고 마치 고모님은 분명 잔소리를 늘어놓으실 거야. 단돈 구 펜스만 달라고 해도 늘 그러시잖아. 메그 언니는 분기 봉급을 죄다 집세에 보탰는데, 난 내 몫으로 옷 몇 벌만 산 거잖아. 그래서 영 마음이 찔리던 차에, 내 코를 떼어 팔아서라도 돈을 좀 마련해야겠다 싶었어.』\n\n『찔릴 것 없단다, 얘야. 너는 겨울옷이 하나도 없었고, 네가 힘들게 번 돈으로 제일 검소한 것만 샀잖니.』 마치 부인이 조의 마음을 따뜻하게 데우는 눈빛으로 말했다.\n\n『처음엔 머리카락을 팔 생각은 눈곱만큼도 없었어. 그런데 길을 걷다 보니 자꾸 내가 뭘 할 수 있을까 궁리하게 되더라고. 그 으리으리한 가게들에 뛰어들어 막 집어 오고 싶은 심정이었지. 그러다 어느 이발소 진열창에 값을 매겨 놓은 머리타래들이 보이는 거야. 그중 내 것만큼 숱이 많지도 않은 검은 머리타래가 사십 달러더라고. 그 순간 문득, 나한테도 돈 될 게 하나 있구나 싶었어. 그래서 생각할 겨를도 없이 안으로 들어가서, 머리카락을 사느냐, 내 걸 사면 얼마나 주겠느냐고 물어봤지.』\n\n『어떻게 그럴 엄두를 냈는지 모르겠어.』 베스가 경외하는 듯한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n『아, 머리에 기름 바르는 게 사는 낙인 것처럼 보이는 자그마한 남자였어. 처음엔 빤히 쳐다보더라고. 여자애가 가게에 불쑥 들어와서 머리카락을 사라고 하는 게 영 익숙지 않은 모양이었어. 자기는 내 머리카락엔 관심 없다나. 유행하는 색도 아니고, 애초에 그런 머리는 값을 별로 안 쳐준다는 거야. 손이 많이 가서 비싸지는 거라나 어쩌나. 시간은 점점 늦어 가는데, 지금 당장 안 해 버리면 영영 못 할 것만 같았어. 그리고 엄마도 알다시피 난 한번 하기로 마음먹은 일은 도중에 포기하는 게 죽기보다 싫잖아. 그래서 제발 사 달라고 사정하면서, 왜 이렇게 급한지 사연을 다 털어놨지. 어리석은 짓이었는지도 모르지만, 그 덕에 그 사람 마음이 바뀌었어. 내가 좀 흥분해서 두서없이 사연을 늘어놓는 걸 그 사람 아내가 듣고는, 어찌나 다정하게 말하던지.』\n\n『여보, 토머스, 그냥 사 줘요, 이 아가씨 좀 도와줘요. 우리 지미한테라면 나도 언제든 그렇게 할 거예요, 팔 만한 머리카락이 한 올이라도 있다면요.』\n\n『지미가 누구야?』 이야기가 진행되는 대로 그때그때 설명 듣기를 좋아하는 에이미가 물었다.\n\n『군대에 간 아들이래. 그런 얘길 하니까 낯선 사람끼리도 금세 친근해지지 않니? 그 아주머니는 남자가 머리를 자르는 내내 쉴 새 없이 이야기를 해서 내 마음을 잘 달래 줬어.』\n\n『처음 가위가 들어갈 때 끔찍하지 않았어?』 메그가 몸을 떨며 물었다.\n\n『남자가 도구를 챙기는 동안 내 머리카락을 마지막으로 한번 봤어. 그게 끝이었지. 난 그런 사소한 일로 훌쩍거리진 않아. 그래도 솔직히, 그 정든 머리카락이 탁자 위에 늘어진 걸 보고 머리엔 짧고 까칠한 끄트머리만 남은 걸 느끼니까 기분이 묘하긴 하더라. 팔이나 다리 한쪽이 떨어져 나간 것만 같았어. 그 아주머니가 내가 머리카락 쳐다보는 걸 보고는, 간직하라고 긴 머리타래를 하나 골라 줬어. 그걸 엄마한테 줄게, 마미. 옛 영광을 추억하시라고. 이렇게 짧은 머리가 어찌나 편한지, 다신 갈기 같은 머리를 기를 일은 없을 것 같아.』\n\n마치 부인은 물결치는 밤색 머리타래를 접어, 짧은 잿빛 머리타래 하나와 함께 책상 안에 넣어 두었다. 부인은 그저 『고맙다, 우리 딸』이라고만 했지만, 그 얼굴에 어린 무언가에 자매들은 화제를 바꾸어, 브룩 씨의 친절이며, 내일 날이 갤 것 같다는 이야기며, 아버지가 집에 돌아와 간호받을 때 누릴 행복한 시간에 대해 애써 명랑하게 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n열 시가 되어 마치 부인이 마지막 일감을 마저 끝내고 『자, 얘들아』라고 했을 때, 아무도 잠자리에 들고 싶어 하지 않았다. 베스가 피아노 앞으로 가서 아버지가 가장 좋아하는 찬송가를 연주했다. 다들 씩씩하게 부르기 시작했지만 하나둘 목이 메었고, 결국 베스만 홀로 남아 온 마음을 다해 노래했다. 베스에게 음악은 언제나 다정한 위로였던 것이다.\n\n『가서 자거라, 더는 떠들지 말고. 우린 일찍 일어나야 하니까 잘 수 있을 때 푹 자 둬야 해. 잘 자렴, 내 사랑하는 아이들아.』 찬송가가 끝나자 마치 부인이 말했다. 아무도 다른 곡을 더 불러 보려 하지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n자매들은 조용히 어머니에게 입을 맞추고는, 마치 사랑하는 환자가 옆방에 누워 있기라도 한 듯 소리 없이 잠자리에 들었다. 베스와 에이미는 큰 근심에도 이내 잠들었지만, 메그는 짧은 생애에 처음 겪는 더없이 심각한 생각에 잠겨 말똥말똥 누워 있었다. 조는 미동도 없이 누워 있어 언니는 동생이 잠든 줄로만 알았는데, 억눌린 흐느낌이 새어 나오자 젖은 뺨에 손을 대며 외쳤다.\n\n『조, 얘, 왜 그래? 아버지 때문에 우는 거야?』\n\n『아니, 지금은 아니야.』\n\n『그럼 뭔데?』\n\n『내, 내 머리카락!』 가엾은 조가 베개에 감정을 묻어 보려 했지만 헛되이 터뜨리고 말았다.\n\n메그에게는 그 말이 조금도 우습게 들리지 않았다. 메그는 더없이 다정하게 슬픔에 잠긴 동생에게 입 맞추고 어루만졌다.\n\n『후회하는 건 아니야.』 조가 목멘 소리로 항변했다. 『할 수만 있다면 내일 또 그럴 거야. 그저 내 안의 허영심 많고 이기적인 부분이 이렇게 바보같이 우는 것뿐이야. 아무한테도 말하지 마, 이젠 다 괜찮으니까. 언니가 잠든 줄 알고, 내 유일한 자랑을 두고 혼자 조금 끙끙댄 거야. 언닌 왜 안 자고 있었어?』\n\n『잠이 안 와, 너무 걱정돼서.』 메그가 말했다.\n\n『뭔가 즐거운 걸 생각해 봐. 그럼 금방 잠들 거야.』\n\n『해 봤는데, 오히려 정신이 더 말똥말똥해지는걸.』\n\n『뭘 생각했는데?』\n\n『잘생긴 얼굴들. 특히 눈을 생각했어.』 메그가 어둠 속에서 혼자 미소 지으며 대답했다.\n\n『무슨 색이 제일 좋아?』\n\n『갈색. 그러니까 가끔은. 파란색도 예쁘고.』\n\n조가 웃자 메그는 그만 떠들라고 매몰차게 명했다가, 곧 다정하게 머리를 곱슬곱슬하게 만들어 주겠다고 약속하고는, 자기만의 공중누각에 사는 꿈을 꾸며 잠들었다.\n\n자정을 알리는 시계 소리가 울리고 방마다 고요한데, 한 사람이 침대에서 침대로 가만가만 옮겨 다녔다. 여기선 이불을 매만지고 저기선 베개를 바로 놓으며, 잠든 줄도 모르는 얼굴 하나하나를 오래도록 다정하게 들여다보려 멈춰 서고, 말없이 축복하는 입술로 하나하나에 입을 맞추고, 오직 어머니만이 올릴 수 있는 간절한 기도를 드렸다. 그녀가 적막한 밤을 내다보려 커튼을 걷자, 구름 뒤에서 달이 불쑥 솟아올라 밝고 인자한 얼굴인 양 그녀를 비추었다. 그 달이 고요 속에서 이렇게 속삭이는 듯했다. 『위안을 얻으렴, 가여운 영혼아! 구름 뒤에는 언제나 빛이 있단다.』\n16장 편지\n\n차가운 잿빛 새벽에 자매들은 등불을 켜고, 일찍이 느껴 본 적 없는 간절함으로 성경을 읽었다. 이제 진짜 시련의 그림자가 드리우자, 그 작은 책들은 도움과 위로로 가득했던 것이다. 그리고 옷을 입으며, 그들은 명랑하고 희망차게 작별 인사를 건네어, 근심 어린 길을 떠나는 어머니를 자기들의 눈물이나 푸념으로 슬프게 하지 말자고 다짐했다. 아래층으로 내려가니 모든 게 무척 낯설게 느껴졌다. 바깥은 어둑하고 고요한데, 안은 불빛과 부산함으로 가득했다. 그렇게 이른 시각의 아침 식사는 어색하기만 했고, 잠자리 모자를 쓴 채 부엌을 분주히 오가는 해나의 낯익은 얼굴마저 어쩐지 낯설어 보였다. 커다란 트렁크가 현관에 채비를 갖추고 놓였고, 어머니의 망토와 보닛은 소파 위에 놓여 있었다. 어머니는 억지로 뭐라도 먹어 보려 앉아 있었지만, 못 잔 데다 근심에 시달려 어찌나 창백하고 수척해 보이는지, 자매들은 다짐을 지키기가 무척이나 힘들었다. 메그는 저도 모르게 자꾸 눈물이 차올랐고, 조는 부엌 수건에 얼굴을 파묻기를 한두 번이 아니었으며, 어린 두 자매는 슬픔이 처음 겪는 일이기라도 한 듯 심각하고 근심 어린 표정을 짓고 있었다.\n\n다들 말이 없었지만, 떠날 시간이 코앞에 다가와 마차를 기다리며 앉아 있을 때, 마치 부인이 자기 주위에서 부산을 떨던 딸들에게 말했다. 한 아이는 숄을 개고, 또 한 아이는 보닛 끈을 매만지고, 또 다른 아이는 덧신을 신겨 주고, 마지막 아이는 여행 가방을 잠그고 있었다.\n\n『얘들아, 너희를 해나의 보살핌과 로런스 어른의 보호에 맡기고 가마. 해나는 그야말로 성실함 그 자체고, 우리 좋은 이웃은 너희를 친자식처럼 지켜 주실 거야. 너희 걱정은 안 한다만, 너희가 이 시련을 올바르게 받아들였으면 싶구나. 내가 떠난 뒤에 슬퍼하거나 안달하지 말고, 빈둥거리며 잊어 보려는 걸로 위안을 삼을 수 있다고도 여기지 말렴. 평소처럼 너희 할 일을 해 나가거라. 일이야말로 더없이 복된 위안이니까. 희망을 품고 부지런히 지내렴. 그리고 무슨 일이 있어도, 너희에게 아버지가 안 계신 일은 결코 없으리란 걸 기억하렴.』\n\n『네, 엄마.』\n\n『메그, 얘야, 신중하게 행동하고, 동생들을 보살피고, 해나와 의논하고, 곤란한 일이 생기면 로런스 어른을 찾아가렴. 조, 인내심을 갖고, 낙담하거나 경솔한 짓을 하지 마라. 자주 편지하고, 우리 모두를 돕고 기운 나게 해 줄 씩씩한 우리 딸이 되어 다오. 베스, 너는 음악으로 마음을 달래고, 소소한 집안일에 충실하렴. 그리고 에이미, 너는 할 수 있는 한 다 거들고, 말 잘 듣고, 집에서 즐겁고 안전하게 잘 지내거라.』\n\n『그럴게요, 엄마! 그럴게요!』\n\n다가오는 마차 소리가 덜컹덜컹 들려오자 다들 흠칫하며 귀를 기울였다. 그것이 가장 힘든 순간이었지만, 자매들은 잘 견뎌 냈다. 아무도 울지 않았고, 달아나거나 한탄을 내뱉는 사람도 없었다. 비록 아버지에게 사랑의 안부를 전하며 가슴이 한없이 무거웠지만, 그리고 말을 하면서도 그것을 전하기엔 너무 늦었을지도 모른다는 생각이 들었지만 말이다. 자매들은 조용히 어머니에게 입을 맞추고, 다정하게 매달렸으며, 어머니가 마차를 타고 떠날 때 애써 명랑하게 손을 흔들었다.\n\n로리와 그의 할아버지가 배웅하러 건너왔고, 브룩 씨가 어찌나 듬직하고 분별 있고 다정해 보이던지, 자매들은 그 자리에서 그를 \"그레이트하트 씨\"라고 이름 붙였다.\n\n『잘 있으렴, 내 사랑하는 아이들아! 하느님께서 우리 모두를 축복하시고 지켜 주시기를!』 마치 부인이 사랑스러운 작은 얼굴들에 차례로 입을 맞추며 속삭이고는, 서둘러 마차에 올랐다.\n\n마차가 굴러가기 시작하자 해가 났고, 부인이 뒤돌아보니 그 햇살이 대문 앞에 모인 무리 위로 비치고 있어 마치 좋은 징조 같았다. 자매들도 그것을 보고 미소 지으며 손을 흔들었다. 그리고 모퉁이를 돌며 부인이 마지막으로 본 것은 환한 얼굴 넷이었고, 그 뒤로는 호위병처럼 늙은 로런스 씨와 충직한 해나, 헌신적인 로리가 서 있었다.\n\n『다들 우리한테 어쩜 이리도 친절할까!』 부인이 그 새로운 증거를 찾으려는 듯 고개를 돌리니, 젊은이의 얼굴에 어린 공손한 연민이 보였다.\n\n『안 그러고는 못 배기는걸요.』 브룩 씨가 어찌나 옮아 오게 웃던지 마치 부인도 따라 웃지 않을 수 없었다. 그렇게 그 긴 여정은 햇살과 미소와 다정한 말이라는 좋은 징조 속에 시작되었다.\n\n『꼭 지진이라도 난 것 같아.』 이웃들이 자매들을 쉬며 기운 차리도록 두고 아침을 먹으러 집으로 돌아가자 조가 말했다.\n\n『집 절반이 사라진 것 같아.』 메그가 쓸쓸하게 덧붙였다.\n\n베스가 무슨 말을 하려고 입을 떼었지만, 그저 어머니의 탁자 위에 놓인, 정성껏 기운 양말 더미를 가리킬 수밖에 없었다. 그것은 어머니가 그 마지막 황망한 순간에도 자기들을 생각하며 손을 놀렸다는 증거였다. 자그마한 일이었지만, 그것이 곧장 자매들의 가슴을 파고들었다. 그리고 씩씩한 다짐에도 불구하고, 다들 그만 무너져 서럽게 울고 말았다.\n\n해나는 슬기롭게도 그들이 감정을 풀어내도록 내버려 두었다가, 그 소나기가 그칠 기미를 보이자 커피 주전자로 무장한 채 구원에 나섰다.\n\n『자, 우리 아가씨들, 어머님이 하신 말씀 잊지 말고 안달하지 마세요. 다 같이 커피 한 잔씩 마시고, 그런 다음 일에 매달려서 이 집안의 자랑이 되자고요.』\n\n커피는 별미였고, 그날 아침 해나는 그것을 별미로 만드는 데 대단한 솜씨를 발휘했다. 권하듯 끄덕이는 그녀의 고갯짓이나, 주전자 주둥이에서 풍겨 나오는 향긋한 초대를 누구도 뿌리칠 수 없었다. 자매들은 식탁에 둘러앉아 손수건을 냅킨으로 바꿔 들었고, 십 분 만에 다들 다시 멀쩡해졌다.\n\n『\"희망을 품고 부지런히 지내자.\" 그게 우리 좌우명이니까, 누가 제일 잘 기억하나 보자고. 난 평소처럼 마치 고모님 댁에 갈 거야. 아휴, 또 얼마나 잔소릴 늘어놓으실까!』 조가 기운을 되찾아 커피를 홀짝이며 말했다.\n\n『난 킹 댁에 가야지. 정말이지 집에 남아 여기 일을 돌보고 싶긴 하지만.』 메그가 눈이 너무 빨개지지 않았으면 좋겠다고 바라며 말했다.\n\n『그럴 필요 없어. 베스랑 나랑 둘이서 집안일 거뜬히 해낼 수 있어.』 에이미가 자못 으스대는 투로 끼어들었다.\n\n『해나가 우리한테 뭘 할지 일러 줄 거야. 언니들이 돌아올 때쯤엔 죄다 말끔하게 해 놓을게.』 베스가 지체 없이 대걸레와 설거지통을 꺼내며 덧붙였다.\n\n『난 걱정하는 게 무척 흥미로운 일 같아.』 에이미가 시무룩하게 설탕을 집어 먹으며 한마디 했다.\n\n자매들은 웃지 않을 수 없었고, 그 덕에 한결 기분이 나아졌다. 비록 메그는 설탕 그릇에서 위안을 찾는 이 아가씨를 향해 고개를 절레절레 흔들었지만 말이다.\n\n빵 만두를 보자 조는 다시 진지해졌다. 그리고 두 사람이 매일 할 일을 하러 나설 때, 그들은 늘 어머니의 얼굴을 보곤 하던 창문을 서글피 돌아보았다. 그 얼굴은 없었다. 하지만 베스가 그 자그마한 집안 의식을 잊지 않고 거기 서서, 발그레한 얼굴의 만다린 인형처럼 그들을 향해 고개를 끄덕이고 있었다.\n\n『꼭 우리 베스답네!』 조가 고마운 얼굴로 모자를 흔들며 말했다. 『잘 가, 메기 언니. 킹 댁 아이들이 오늘은 진을 빼지 말아야 할 텐데. 아버지 걱정은 말고, 언니.』 헤어지면서 조가 덧붙였다.\n\n『그리고 마치 고모님이 잔소리 안 하셔야 할 텐데. 네 머리 잘 어울려. 소년 같고 아주 보기 좋다.』 메그가 대꾸하며, 키 큰 동생의 어깨 위에 우스꽝스러울 만큼 작아 보이는 곱슬머리를 보고 웃음을 참았다.\n\n『그게 내 유일한 위안이지.』 조는 로리처럼 모자에 손을 살짝 갖다 대며 인사하고는, 추운 겨울날 털 깎인 양이라도 된 기분으로 떠나갔다.\n\n아버지의 소식은 자매들에게 큰 위안이 되었다. 비록 위독하긴 했지만, 더없이 훌륭하고 다정한 간호사가 곁에 있다는 것만으로도 벌써 차도가 있었던 것이다. 브룩 씨가 날마다 병세를 알려 왔고, 집안의 가장으로서 메그가 그 소식을 자기가 읽겠다고 우겼는데, 한 주가 지날수록 소식은 점점 더 반가워졌다. 처음엔 다들 앞다투어 편지를 쓰려 했고, 자매들 중 누군가가 두툼한 봉투를 우체통에 정성껏 밀어 넣곤 했다. 워싱턴과 편지를 주고받는다는 게 어쩐지 자못 대단하게 느껴졌던 것이다. 그 묶음 중 하나에 식구들 저마다의 개성이 담긴 편지들이 들어 있었으니, 우리는 상상 속 우편물을 슬쩍 가로채 그것들을 읽어 보기로 하자.\n『더없이 사랑하는 엄마께,\n\n지난번 엄마 편지가 우릴 얼마나 행복하게 했는지 이루 다 말할 수가 없어요. 소식이 어찌나 좋던지 웃다가 울다가 했답니다. 브룩 씨는 정말 친절하시고, 로런스 어른의 볼일이 그렇게 오래 엄마 가까이 그분을 붙잡아 두다니 또 얼마나 다행인지요. 그분이 엄마와 아버지께 큰 도움이 되어 드리니까요. 동생들은 다들 더없이 착하게 지내요. 조는 제 바느질을 돕고, 온갖 힘든 일을 죄다 자기가 하겠다고 우긴답니다. 조의 \"도덕적 발작\"이 오래가지 않으리란 걸 몰랐다면, 무리하다 탈이라도 날까 걱정했을 거예요. 베스는 시계처럼 어김없이 제 할 일을 하고, 엄마가 일러 준 걸 절대 잊는 법이 없어요. 아버지 일로 슬퍼하고, 작은 피아노 앞에 앉아 있을 때 말고는 표정이 시무룩해요. 에이미는 제 말을 잘 듣고, 저도 그 애를 정성껏 돌보고 있어요. 머리도 혼자 손질하고, 제가 단춧구멍 내는 법이랑 양말 깁는 법을 가르치는 중이에요. 무척 열심히 하니까, 엄마가 돌아오시면 그 애가 부쩍 나아진 걸 보고 분명 기뻐하실 거예요. 로런스 어른은, 조 말마따나, 어미 닭처럼 우릴 보살펴 주세요. 그리고 로리는 무척 친절하고 이웃다워요. 그 애랑 조가 우릴 즐겁게 해 준답니다. 엄마가 그렇게 멀리 계시니 우리도 가끔은 꽤나 울적해져서 고아가 된 기분이 들거든요. 해나는 그야말로 성인이에요. 야단이라곤 도통 안 치고, 늘 저를 \"마거릿 아씨\"라고 부르는데, 그게 아주 격에 맞잖아요, 그렇죠? 저를 깍듯이 대해 준답니다. 우린 다들 건강하고 바쁘게 지내요. 하지만 밤낮으로 엄마가 돌아오시기만을 손꼽아 기다린답니다. 아버지께 제 더없는 사랑을 전해 주세요. 그리고 언제까지나 엄마의 딸임을 믿어 주세요.\n메그 올림.』\n\n향기 나는 종이에 곱게 쓴 이 편지는 다음 편지와 큰 대조를 이루었다. 그 편지는 커다란 얇은 외제 종이에 갈겨썼는데, 잉크 얼룩에다 온갖 멋 부린 장식과 끄트머리가 동그랗게 말린 글자들로 치장되어 있었다.\n『소중한 우리 마미께,\n\n사랑하는 아버지를 위해 만세 삼창! 브룩 씨는 정말 멋진 분이에요. 아버지가 차도를 보이자마자 그 순간 바로 전보를 쳐서 알려 줬거든요. 편지가 왔을 때 저는 다락방으로 뛰어 올라가서, 우리한테 이리도 잘해 주신 하느님께 감사드리려 했어요. 그런데 그저 울면서 \"기뻐요! 기뻐요!\" 하는 말밖에 못 했지 뭐예요. 그래도 정식 기도만큼은 됐겠죠? 제 마음속엔 수없이 많은 기도가 있었으니까요. 우린 정말 재밌게 지내요. 그리고 이젠 그걸 즐길 수 있어요. 다들 어찌나 끔찍하게 착한지, 꼭 멧비둘기 둥지 속에 사는 것 같거든요. 메그 언니가 식탁 상석에 앉아 엄마 노릇을 하려고 애쓰는 걸 보면 엄마도 웃으실 거예요. 언니는 날이 갈수록 예뻐져서, 가끔은 제가 다 반할 지경이에요. 동생들은 영락없는 천사들이고, 저는, 뭐, 저는 조죠. 앞으로도 영영 다른 사람은 못 될 거고요. 아 참, 로리랑 하마터면 다툴 뻔한 얘길 해 드려야겠네요. 제가 사소하고 어리석은 일을 두고 속에 있는 말을 다 해 버렸더니, 그 애가 기분 나빠 했어요. 제 말이 옳긴 했지만, 말투가 영 마땅찮았던 거예요. 그 애는 제가 사과할 때까진 다신 안 오겠다면서 집으로 가 버렸어요. 저도 절대 사과 안 하겠다며 화를 냈고요. 하루 종일 그러고 있으려니 마음이 안 좋아서, 엄마가 무척 보고 싶었어요. 로리랑 저는 둘 다 자존심이 어찌나 센지 사과하기가 참 어렵거든요. 그래도 제가 옳았으니까, 그 애가 먼저 굽히고 들어올 줄 알았어요. 그런데 안 오더라고요. 그러다 밤이 되어서야, 에이미가 강에 빠졌을 때 엄마가 하신 말씀이 떠올랐어요. 제 작은 책을 읽으니 마음이 좀 나아졌고, 제 노여움을 품은 채로 해를 넘기지 말자고 마음먹고는, 로리한테 미안하다고 말하려고 한달음에 달려갔어요. 그런데 대문에서 그 애를 만났지 뭐예요. 그 애도 똑같은 마음으로 오던 길이었던 거예요. 우린 둘 다 웃음을 터뜨리며 서로에게 사과했고, 다시 다 풀려서 편안해졌답니다.\n어제 해나가 빨래하는 걸 돕다가 \"시\"를 한 편 지었어요. 아버지가 제 어설픈 끄적임을 좋아하시니까, 아버지를 즐겁게 해 드리려고 같이 넣어요. 아버지를 세상에서 제일 사랑스럽게 꼭 안아 드리고, 엄마도 엄마 자신한테 열두 번쯤 입맞춰 주세요.\n\n뒤죽박죽 조 올림.』\n『비눗물에서 부르는 노래\n\n내 빨래통의 여왕, 나는 즐거이 노래하네, 하얀 거품이 높이 솟아오를 때. 씩씩하게 빨고 헹구고 비틀어 짜고, 빨랫줄에 옷가지를 널어 말리지. 그러면 시원하고 상쾌한 바깥 공기 속에 옷들이 나부끼네, 햇살 비치는 하늘 아래에서.\n우리 마음과 영혼에서도 빨아 낼 수 있다면, 한 주 동안 묻은 얼룩을 말끔히. 물과 공기가 마법을 부려 우리 자신도 저 옷들처럼 깨끗하게 해 준다면, 그땐 이 땅에도 정녕 찬란한 빨랫날이 찾아오리라!\n\n쓸모 있는 삶의 오솔길을 따라서는 마음의 평안이 늘 꽃피우리. 바쁜 마음에는 슬픔이나 근심이나 우울을 생각할 겨를이 없으니. 근심스러운 생각들도 쓸어 낼 수 있으리, 우리가 씩씩하게 빗자루를 휘두를 때.\n내게 할 일이 주어진 것이 기쁘다네, 날마다 매달려 애쓸 일이. 그것은 내게 건강과 힘과 희망을 가져다주니, 나는 기꺼이 이렇게 말하기를 배운다네. \"머리야, 너는 생각해도 좋아, 마음아, 너는 느껴도 좋아, 하지만 손아, 너는 언제나 일하여라!\"』\n\n『사랑하는 엄마께,\n제 사랑과, 아버지께 보여 드리려고 집 안에 고이 간직해 둔 그 뿌리에서 딴 팬지꽃을 눌러 말린 것 몇 송이를 보낼 자리밖에 없네요. 저는 아침마다 책을 읽고, 하루 종일 착하게 지내려 애쓰고, 아버지가 즐겨 부르던 곡조를 부르며 잠들어요. 이제 \"충직한 자들의 땅\"은 못 부르겠어요. 그 노랜 자꾸 눈물이 나거든요. 다들 무척 친절하고, 우린 엄마가 안 계셔도 더없이 행복하게 지내요. 에이미가 남은 자리를 쓰고 싶다니까 저는 이만 줄여야겠어요. 냄비 받침에 천 씌우는 거 잊지 않았고, 매일 시계태엽을 감고 방마다 환기도 시켜요.\n\n아버지께서 제 것이라고 하시는 그 뺨에 입맞춤해 주세요. 아, 부디 어서 돌아오세요, 엄마를 사랑하는\n어린 베스가.』\n\n『사랑하는 엄마께,\n우리는 모두 잘 지내요 저는 늘 공부를 하고 절대로 언니들을 협동하지 않아요—메그 언니가 반박이라는 뜻이랬는데 그래서 두 단어를 다 적으니 엄마가 더 알맞은 걸 고르세요. 메그 언니는 저한테 큰 위로가 돼 주고 매일 차 마실 때 젤리를 먹게 해 줘요 그게 저한테 그렇게 좋대요 조 언니 말로는 그래야 제 성질이 곱게 유지된다나 봐요. 로리는 이제 제가 거의 십 대인데도 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 공손하지가 않아요, 저를 병아리라고 부르고 제가 해티 킹처럼 메르시나 봉주르라고 하면 프랑스어를 아주 빠르게 지껄여 대서 제 기분을 상하게 해요. 제 파란 원피스 소매가 죄다 해져서 메그 언니가 새 소매를 달아 줬는데 앞판 전체가 잘못 나와서 원피스보다 더 파래요. 속상했지만 안달하진 않았어요 저는 제 어려움을 잘 견디거든요 하지만 해나가 제 앞치마에 풀을 좀 더 먹여 주고 매일 메밀 빵을 만들어 주면 정말 좋겠어요. 그래 줄 수 없을까요? 저 그 물음표 잘 만들지 않았어요? 메그 언니가 제 구두점이랑 철자가 형편없다고 해서 부끄러워요 하지만 어머 저는 할 일이 너무 많아서 멈출 수가 없어요. 안녕히 계세요, 아빠께 사랑을 가득가득 보내요.\n\n엄마를 사랑하는 딸, 에이미 커티스 마치 올림.』\n* 협동·반박: 에이미가 \"반박하다(contradict)\"를 \"협동하다(corroborate)\"로 잘못 쓴 것을 그대로 옮긴 것이다 : 옮긴이.\n\n『친애하는 마치 마님께,\n저희가 아주 잘 지낸다는 말씀 한 줄 적습니다요. 아씨들은 영리하고 야무지게 잘들 돌아댕깁니다요. 메그 아씨는 어엿한 살림꾼이 되실 겁니다요. 그쪽에 취미가 있으셔서 일머리를 놀랍도록 빨리 익히세요. 조 아씨는 척척 해치우는 데는 당할 사람이 없는데, 먼저 따져 보질 않으셔서 어디서 일을 끝맺으실지 도통 알 수가 없습죠. 월요일에 빨래 한 통을 다 해 놓으셨는데, 헹구기도 전에 풀을 먹이질 않나, 분홍 옥양목 원피스를 파랗게 물들여 놓으셔서 제가 웃다가 죽는 줄 알았습니다요. 베스 아씨는 더없이 착한 아이로, 손이 야무지고 미더워서 저한테 큰 도움이 됩죠. 뭐든 배우려 하고, 정말이지 제 나이답지 않게 장도 잘 봐 와요. 또 제 도움을 받아 가며 장부도 어찌나 잘 적는지 놀랍습니다요. 여태 아주 알뜰하게 살림을 꾸려 왔습니다요. 마님 뜻대로 아씨들한텐 커피는 일주일에 한 번만 드리고, 늘 소박하고 몸에 좋은 음식만 먹입죠. 에이미 아씨는 제일 좋은 옷을 입고 단것을 먹으며 안달하지 않고 잘 지냅니다요. 로리 도련님은 여전히 장난기가 넘쳐서 집을 뒤집어 놓기 일쑤지만, 아씨들 기운을 북돋아 주니 저도 마음껏 놀게 둡니다요. 노어르신은 온갖 것들을 잔뜩 보내 주시는데 좀 성가시긴 해도 뜻은 좋으시니, 제가 뭐라 할 처지는 아닙죠. 빵 반죽이 부풀었으니 이만 줄입니다요. 마치 어른께 안부 여쭙고, 부디 그 폐렴이라는 것과는 영영 작별하셨기를 바랍니다요.\n\n삼가 올림, 해나 멀릿.』\n* 폐렴: 해나가 \"폐렴(Pneumonia)\"을 \"퓨모니아(Pewmonia)\"로 잘못 쓴 것을 옮긴 것이다 : 옮긴이.\n\n『제2병동 수간호사께,\n래퍼해녹 강가는 모두 평온하고, 부대 상태 양호하며, 보급부는 잘 운영되고 있음. 테디 대령 휘하의 향토방위대는 늘 근무 중이고, 총사령관 로런스 장군이 매일 군대를 사열하며, 병참장교 멀릿이 야영지 질서를 유지하고, 라이언 소령이 야간 보초를 섬. 워싱턴에서 온 희소식을 받고 스물네 발의 예포를 발사했으며, 사령부에서 사열식이 거행됨. 총사령관이 안부를 전하며, 여기에 진심으로 뜻을 같이하는 사람,\n\n테디 대령.』\n『친애하는 부인께,\n\n# LITTLE FAITHFUL.\n\n어린 아가씨들은 모두 잘 있습니다. 베스와 우리 아이가 매일 소식을 전합니다. 해나는 모범적인 일꾼으로, 어여쁜 메그를 용처럼 지키고 있습니다. 좋은 날씨가 이어져 다행입니다. 부디 브룩을 요긴하게 부리시고, 비용이 예상을 넘으면 제게 청구하십시오. 남편분께서 부족한 것이 없도록 해 주십시오. 차도가 있다니 하느님께 감사드립니다.\n당신의 진실한 벗이자 종, 제임스 로런스.』\n\n17장 충직한 어린 사람\n한 주 동안 이 오래된 집에 쌓인 미덕의 양은 온 동네에 나누어 줘도 될 만했다. 정말이지 놀라울 지경이었으니, 다들 천국에라도 있는 듯한 마음가짐이었고, 극기야말로 최고의 유행이었다. 아버지에 대한 첫 근심을 덜자 자매들은 자기도 모르게 그 갸륵한 노력을 조금씩 늦추기 시작했고, 다시 옛 버릇으로 돌아갔다. 좌우명을 잊은 건 아니었지만, 희망을 품고 부지런히 지내는 일이 점점 수월해지는 듯했다. 그렇게 어마어마하게 애를 쓰고 나니, '노력'에도 휴일이 마땅하다 싶어 휴일을 꽤 여러 날 주었다.\n\n조는 짧아진 머리를 충분히 덮지 않아 독한 감기에 걸렸고, 나을 때까지 집에 있으라는 명을 받았다. 마치 고모가 머릿속에 감기 든 사람이 책 읽는 소리를 듣기 싫어했던 것이다. 조는 이게 마음에 들어서, 다락방부터 지하실까지 기운차게 뒤지고 다닌 뒤 소파에 자리 잡고 비소제와 책으로 감기를 다스렸다. 에이미는 집안일과 예술이 영 어울리지 않는다는 걸 깨닫고 다시 진흙 파이로 돌아갔다. 메그는 날마다 가르치는 아이들에게 갔고, 집에서는 바느질을 하거나 한다고 여겼지만, 사실은 어머니에게 긴 편지를 쓰거나 워싱턴에서 온 소식을 읽고 또 읽느라 많은 시간을 보냈다. 베스는 이따금 게으름을 피우거나 슬픔에 잠기는 일이 잠깐 있긴 했어도 꾸준히 제 할 일을 해 나갔다. 소소한 집안일은 날마다 빠짐없이 충실히 해냈고, 언니들 몫까지 도맡아 했다. 언니들은 잘 잊어버렸고, 집은 마치 추가 마실이라도 나간 시계 같았던 것이다. 어머니가 그립거나 아버지가 걱정되어 마음이 무거워지면, 베스는 어떤 벽장 안으로 들어가 어떤 정든 낡은 가운 자락에 얼굴을 묻고, 혼자 조용히 조그맣게 흐느끼며 조그맣게 기도를 올렸다. 베스가 침울한 발작 뒤에 어떻게 기운을 차렸는지는 아무도 몰랐지만, 다들 베스가 얼마나 사랑스럽고 의지가 되는지를 느꼈고, 사소한 일에 위로나 조언을 구할 때면 으레 그 애를 찾게 되었다.\n이 경험이 인격의 시험이라는 걸 다들 알아채지 못했다. 그리고 처음의 흥분이 가시자, 자기들이 잘해 냈으니 칭찬받아 마땅하다고 여겼다. 정말 그러긴 했다. 하지만 그들의 잘못은 잘하던 것을 그만둔 데 있었으니, 그들은 숱한 근심과 후회를 치르고 나서야 이 교훈을 배웠다.\n\n『메그 언니, 언니가 험멜네 좀 다녀와 줬으면 좋겠어. 알잖아, 엄마가 그 집 사람들 잊지 말라고 하셨잖아.』 마치 부인이 떠난 지 열흘째 되던 날, 베스가 말했다.\n\n『오늘 오후엔 너무 피곤해서 못 가겠어.』 메그가 편안히 흔들의자에 앉아 바느질하며 대꾸했다.\n\n『조 언니는 못 가?』 베스가 물었다.\n\n『감기 든 데다 날씨가 너무 사나워서 안 되겠어.』\n\n『거의 다 나은 줄 알았는데.』\n\n『로리랑 나갈 만큼은 나았지만, 험멜네까지 갈 만큼은 아니야.』 조가 웃으며 말했지만, 자기 모순에 좀 멋쩍어하는 기색이었다.\n\n『네가 직접 가면 되잖아.』 메그가 물었다.\n\n『난 매일 다녀왔단 말이야. 그런데 그 집 아기가 아픈데 어떻게 해 줘야 할지 모르겠어. 험멜 부인은 일하러 나가고 로트헨이 아기를 돌보는데, 점점 더 심해지기만 해. 언니나 해나가 가 봐야 할 것 같아.』\n\n베스가 간곡하게 말하자, 메그는 내일 다녀오겠다고 약속했다.\n\n『해나한테 맛있는 음식 좀 얻어서 가져다주렴, 베스. 바람 쐬면 너한테도 좋을 거야.』 조가 말하고는 미안한 듯 덧붙였다. 『나도 가고 싶지만, 글을 마저 끝내고 싶어서.』\n\n『머리도 아프고 피곤해서, 너희 중 누가 가 주려나 했지.』 베스가 말했다.\n\n『에이미가 곧 들어올 테니까, 그 애가 우리 대신 달려가 줄 거야.』 메그가 제안했다.\n\n『그래, 그럼 좀 쉬면서 그 애를 기다릴게.』\n\n그렇게 베스는 소파에 누웠고, 나머지는 다시 하던 일로 돌아갔으며, 험멜네는 잊히고 말았다. 한 시간이 지났다. 에이미는 오지 않았다. 메그는 새 원피스를 입어 보러 자기 방으로 갔고, 조는 이야기에 푹 빠져 있었으며, 해나는 부엌 난롯불 앞에서 곤히 잠들어 있었다. 그때 베스가 조용히 두건을 쓰고, 바구니에 가난한 아이들에게 줄 자질구레한 것들을 채워, 무거운 머리와 인내심 어린 두 눈에 서글픈 빛을 띤 채 쌀쌀한 바깥으로 나갔다. 베스가 돌아온 건 늦은 시각이었고, 그 애가 살그머니 위층으로 올라가 어머니 방에 들어가 문을 닫는 걸 본 사람은 아무도 없었다. 삼십 분 뒤 조가 뭘 가지러 \"엄마 벽장\"에 갔다가, 베스가 약상자 위에 앉아 있는 걸 발견했다. 눈이 빨갛고 손에는 장뇌 병을 든 채, 무척이나 심각한 얼굴을 하고 있었다.\n\n『맙소사! 무슨 일이야?』 베스가 가까이 오지 말라는 듯 손을 내밀며 다급히 묻자 조가 외쳤다.\n\n『언니 성홍열 앓은 적 있지?』\n\n『옛날에 메그 언니랑 같이 앓았지. 왜?』\n\n『그럼 말할게. 아, 조 언니, 그 아기가 죽었어!』\n\n『무슨 아기?』\n\n『험멜 부인네 아기. 부인이 집에 오기도 전에 내 무릎에서 죽었어.』 베스가 흐느끼며 외쳤다.\n\n『가엾은 우리 베스, 얼마나 끔찍했을까! 내가 갔어야 했는데.』 조가 후회 가득한 얼굴로 어머니의 커다란 의자에 앉으며 동생을 두 팔로 끌어안았다.\n\n『끔찍하진 않았어, 조 언니. 그냥 너무 슬펐어! 아기가 더 안 좋아진 게 한눈에 보였는데, 로트헨이 자기 엄마가 의사를 부르러 갔다길래, 내가 아기를 안고 로티를 좀 쉬게 했어. 잠든 것 같았는데, 갑자기 가냘프게 한번 울더니, 부르르 떨고는, 아주 가만히 있는 거야. 발을 따뜻하게 해 주려고 했고, 로티가 우유도 좀 먹였는데, 꿈쩍도 안 해서, 죽은 걸 알았어.』\n\n『울지 마, 얘. 그래서 어떻게 했어?』\n\n『그냥 앉아서 살며시 안고 있었어, 험멜 부인이 의사 선생님이랑 올 때까지. 선생님이 아기가 죽었다고 하고는, 목이 부어오른 하인리히랑 미나를 보더라. \"성홍열입니다, 부인. 진작 절 불렀어야죠.\" 하고 퉁명스럽게 말했어. 험멜 부인이 자긴 가난해서 직접 아기를 고쳐 보려 했다고, 그런데 이젠 너무 늦었으니 다른 아이들이라도 살려 달라고, 진료비는 자비를 베풀어 달라고 부탁했어. 그제야 선생님이 미소를 짓고 좀 더 다정해졌어. 하지만 너무 슬퍼서 나도 그 사람들이랑 같이 울었는데, 선생님이 별안간 돌아서더니 나더러 당장 집에 가서 벨라도나를 먹으라고, 안 그러면 나도 열병에 걸린다고 했어.』\n\n『아니, 언닌 안 걸릴 거야!』 조가 겁에 질린 얼굴로 동생을 꼭 끌어안으며 외쳤다. 『아, 베스, 언니가 아프기라도 하면 난 나 자신을 영영 용서 못 할 거야! 우리 어떡하지?』\n\n『겁먹지 마. 심하게 걸리진 않을 것 같아. 엄마 책을 봤더니, 두통이랑 인후통이랑 나처럼 이상한 느낌으로 시작한다고 적혀 있더라고. 그래서 벨라도나를 좀 먹었더니 한결 나아.』 베스가 차가운 손을 뜨거운 이마에 갖다 대며, 멀쩡해 보이려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n『엄마가 집에 계시기만 했어도!』 조가 책을 움켜쥐며 외쳤다. 워싱턴이 까마득히 멀게만 느껴졌다. 조는 한 쪽을 읽고, 베스를 보고, 이마를 짚어 보고, 목구멍을 들여다본 뒤, 심각하게 말했다. 『언니, 일주일 넘게 매일 그 아기 곁에 있었던 데다, 앞으로 그 병에 걸릴 다른 애들 사이에도 있었잖아. 그러니까 아무래도 언니도 걸릴 것 같아, 베스. 해나를 부를게. 그 사람은 병이라면 훤히 아니까.』\n\n『에이미는 못 오게 해. 그 앤 한 번도 안 걸렸으니까, 옮기면 정말 싫을 거야. 언니랑 메그 언니는 다시 걸리진 않을까?』 베스가 근심스레 물었다.\n\n『안 걸릴 것 같아. 걸려도 상관없어. 언니를 보내 놓고 나 혼자 앉아 시답잖은 글이나 쓰고 있었으니, 이기적인 돼지 같으니, 그래도 싸지!』 조는 중얼거리며 해나에게 의논하러 갔다.\n\n마음씨 고운 해나는 순식간에 잠이 확 깨어 곧장 일을 떠맡았다. 걱정할 것 없다고, 성홍열은 누구나 앓는 병이며 제대로 치료만 하면 죽는 사람은 없다고 조를 안심시켰다. 조는 그 말을 다 믿고는 한결 마음이 놓인 채, 두 사람은 메그를 부르러 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n『자, 우리가 어떻게 할지 일러 드릴게요.』 베스를 살펴보고 이것저것 물어본 뒤 해나가 말했다. 『뱅스 선생님을 모셔다가, 그저 아가씨를 한번 봐 주십사 하는 거예요, 우리가 제대로 시작하나 보게요. 그런 다음 에이미 아가씨는 마치\n마치네 고모님 댁으로 한동안 보내서 위험을 피하게 하고, 너희 중 하나가 집에 남아 하루 이틀 베스를 돌봐 주면 되겠다.\"\n\n\"내가 남을게요, 당연히. 내가 맏이잖아요.\" 메그가 걱정스럽고 자책하는 얼굴로 입을 열었다.\n\n\"내가 남을래요. 베스가 아픈 게 내 잘못이니까. 엄마한테 심부름은 내가 한다고 해 놓고 안 했잖아요.\" 조가 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"누가 좋겠니, 베스? 한 사람만 있으면 되니까.\" 해나가 물었다.\n\n\"조, 언니가 좋아요.\" 베스가 만족스러운 표정으로 언니 어깨에 머리를 기댔고, 그 한마디로 문제는 깔끔하게 정리되었다.\n\n\"내가 가서 에이미한테 말할게.\" 메그는 조금 서운하면서도 전체적으로는 오히려 마음이 놓였다. 그녀는 간병이 싫었고, 조는 좋아했기 때문이다.\n\n에이미는 대놓고 반항하며, 마치 고모님 댁에 가느니 차라리 자기가 열병을 앓는 게 낫겠다고 격하게 선언했다. 메그가 타이르고, 사정하고, 명령까지 해 봤지만 다 소용없었다. 에이미는 절대 안 가겠다고 버텼고, 메그는 절망한 채 해나에게 어떻게 해야 할지 물어보러 갔다. 메그가 돌아오기 전에 로리가 거실로 들어왔다가, 소파 쿠션에 머리를 파묻고 흐느끼고 있는 에이미를 발견했다. 에이미는 위로받기를 기대하며 사정을 털어놓았다. 그러나 로리는 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 방 안을 거닐며, 미간을 잔뜩 찌푸리고 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯 나직이 휘파람만 불 뿐이었다. 이윽고 그는 에이미 곁에 앉아 더없이 살살 구슬리는 어조로 말했다. \"자, 이제 분별 있는 작은 숙녀답게 굴고, 어른들이 시키는 대로 해. 아니, 울지 말고 내가 짜낸 끝내주는 계획을 좀 들어 봐. 너는 마치네 고모님 댁에 가고, 내가 매일 가서 너를 데리고 나오는 거야. 마차도 타고 산책도 하고, 신나게 노는 거지. 여기서 시무룩하게 있는 것보다 그게 낫지 않겠어?\"\n\n\"내가 거치적거린다고 쫓겨나는 건 싫어.\" 에이미가 서운한 목소리로 말문을 열었다.\n\n\"이런, 얘야, 그건 네가 건강하라고 그러는 거야. 너도 아프기는 싫잖아, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"응, 정말 싫어. 하지만 난 분명히 걸리고 말 거야. 베스랑 줄곧 같이 있었으니까.\"\n\n\"바로 그래서 당장 떠나야 한다는 거야. 그래야 병을 피할 수 있으니까. 공기를 바꾸고 몸을 잘 챙기면 건강을 지킬 수 있을 거야. 설령 완전히 막지는 못한다 해도 훨씬 가볍게 앓고 넘어갈 수 있어. 되도록 빨리 떠나는 게 좋겠어. 성홍열은 우습게 볼 게 아니거든, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"하지만 고모님 댁은 따분하고, 고모님은 너무 까다로우신걸.\" 에이미가 겁먹은 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n\"내가 매일 들러서 베스가 어떤지 알려 주고 너를 데리고 놀러 다닐 텐데 따분할 리가 있나. 그 어르신은 나를 좋아하시니까, 내가 최대한 싹싹하게 굴게. 그러면 우리가 뭘 하든 고모님이 잔소리하지 않으실 거야.\"\n\n\"퍽이 끄는 경마차에 태워 줄 거지?\"\n\n\"신사의 명예를 걸고.\"\n\n\"매일매일 빠짐없이 오고?\"\n\n\"내가 안 오나 두고 봐.\"\n\n\"베스가 낫는 순간 곧장 집에 데려다주고?\"\n\n\"바로 그 순간에.\"\n\n\"극장에도 데려가 줄 거야, 정말?\"\n\n\"가능하면 극장을 열두 군데라도.\"\n\n\"음… 그럼… 갈래.\" 에이미가 천천히 말했다.\n\n\"착하다! 메그를 불러서 항복하겠다고 말하렴.\" 로리가 칭찬하듯 토닥였는데, 그 행동이 '항복'이라는 말보다 에이미를 더 짜증 나게 했다.\n\n메그와 조가 기적이 일어난 광경을 보려고 뛰어 내려왔다. 에이미는 자기가 무척 귀하고 갸륵한 희생을 한다는 기분에 젖어, 의사가 베스가 아플 거라고 진단하면 가겠노라고 약속했다.\n\n\"우리 꼬마는 좀 어때?\" 로리가 물었다. 베스는 그가 특별히 아끼는 동생이었고, 그는 겉으로 드러내고 싶은 것보다 훨씬 더 그녀를 걱정하고 있었다.\n\n\"엄마 침대에 누워 있는데, 좀 나아진 것 같아. 아기가 죽은 일로 충격을 받긴 했지만, 내 생각엔 그냥 감기에 걸린 것 같아. 해나도 그렇게 생각한다고 말은 하는데, 표정이 영 걱정스러워 보여서 나도 안절부절못하겠어.\" 메그가 대답했다.\n\n\"세상은 어쩜 이렇게 사람을 시험에 들게 할까!\" 조가 짜증 섞인 손길로 머리를 헝클어뜨리며 말했다. \"한 가지 걱정에서 빠져나오기가 무섭게 또 다른 걱정이 닥치잖아. 엄마가 안 계시니까 도무지 붙잡을 게 없는 것 같아. 그래서 나는 갈피를 못 잡겠어.\"\n\n\"이런, 고슴도치처럼 굴지 마. 어울리지도 않아. 그 가발 같은 머리나 좀 다듬으렴, 조. 그리고 내가 너희 어머니한테 전보를 칠까, 아니면 뭐든 해 줄까?\" 로리가 물었다. 그는 친구의 단 하나뿐인 자랑거리를 잃은 일을 영영 받아들이지 못하고 있었다.\n\n\"바로 그게 걱정이야.\" 메그가 말했다. \"베스가 정말 아픈 거라면 엄마한테 알려야 한다고 생각하는데, 해나는 안 된대. 엄마는 아빠 곁을 떠날 수 없는 데다, 괜히 두 분만 더 걱정하게 만들 뿐이라는 거야. 베스가 오래 앓지는 않을 거고, 해나는 어떻게 해야 하는지 잘 알고, 엄마도 해나 말을 잘 들으라고 하셨으니까 그래야겠지. 그런데 나한테는 영 옳은 일 같지가 않아.\"\n\n\"흠, 글쎄, 나도 뭐라 말은 못 하겠다. 의사 선생님이 다녀가신 다음에 할아버지한테 한번 여쭤보는 게 어때?\"\n\n\"그러자. 조, 당장 가서 뱅스 선생님 좀 모셔 와.\" 메그가 지시했다. \"선생님이 다녀가시기 전에는 아무것도 결정할 수 없으니까.\"\n\n\"그냥 있어, 조. 이 집 심부름꾼은 나니까.\" 로리가 모자를 집어 들며 말했다.\n\n\"넌 바쁠 텐데.\" 메그가 말문을 열었다.\n\n\"아니, 오늘 공부는 다 끝냈어.\"\n\n\"방학에도 공부를 해?\" 조가 물었다.\n\n\"이웃이 보여 준 훌륭한 본보기를 따르는 거지.\" 로리는 그렇게 대답하며 몸을 휙 날려 방을 나섰다.\n\n\"내 친구한테 거는 기대가 크다니까.\" 그가 울타리를 훌쩍 뛰어넘는 모습을 흐뭇한 미소로 바라보며 조가 말했다.\n\n\"꽤 잘하는 편이지, 남자애치고는.\" 메그가 다소 무뚝뚝하게 대꾸했다. 그녀에게는 이 화제가 별 흥미를 끌지 못했던 것이다.\n\n뱅스 선생이 다녀갔다. 그는 베스에게 열병 증상이 있다고 했지만, 가볍게 앓고 넘어갈 것 같다고 보았다. 다만 후멜네 이야기를 들었을 때는 표정이 굳어졌다. 에이미는 당장 떠나라는 명령을 받았고, 위험을 막아 줄 약을 챙긴 채, 조와 로리를 호위로 거느리고 거창하게 길을 나섰다.\n\n마치 고모는 평소처럼 환대하며 그들을 맞았다.\n\n\"이번엔 또 뭐냐?\" 고모가 안경 너머로 날카롭게 쳐다보며 물었다. 그러는 동안 의자 등받이에 앉은 앵무새가 소리를 질렀다.\n\n\"저리 가. 여긴 남자애 출입 금지.\"\n\n로리는 창가로 물러났고, 조가 사정을 이야기했다.\n\n\"가난뱅이들 틈에 함부로 들락거리게 내버려 뒀으니 이쯤은 예상한 바다. 에이미는 아프지만 않으면 남아서 제 몫을 하면 되겠지. 하긴 분명 앓게 될 게다. 지금도 그래 보이는구나. 울지 마라, 얘야. 사람이 훌쩍거리는 소리는 듣기만 해도 신경 쓰이니까.\"\n\n에이미는 막 울음을 터뜨리려던 참이었는데, 로리가 슬쩍 앵무새 꼬리를 잡아당겼다. 그 바람에 폴리가 깜짝 놀라 꽥 소리를 지르더니 너무나 우스꽝스러운 목소리로 \"어이쿠 깜짝이야!\" 하고 외치는 바람에, 에이미는 울음 대신 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"네 어미한테서는 무슨 소식이 있더냐?\" 고모가 퉁명스럽게 물었다.\n\n\"아버지는 많이 좋아지셨어요.\" 조가 웃음을 참으며 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 그래? 글쎄, 오래는 못 가지 싶다만. 마치 집안은 원래 끈기라곤 없으니까.\" 고모가 쾌활하게 대꾸했다.\n\n\"하, 하! 죽는소리 말고 코담배나 한 줌 들이켜라, 잘 가, 잘 가!\" 폴리가 횃대 위에서 춤추듯 까불며 꽥꽥거렸다. 로리가 뒤에서 꼬리를 잡아당기자, 폴리는 고모의 모자를 발톱으로 할퀴어 댔다.\n\n\"입 다물지 못해, 이 버르장머리 없는 늙은 새야! 그리고 조, 너는 어서 가는 게 좋겠다. 저렇게 머리에 든 것 없는 사내애랑 이 늦은 시각에 쏘다니는 건 점잖지 못한 일이니—\"\n\n\"입 다물지 못해, 이 버르장머리 없는 늙은 새야!\" 폴리가 의자에서 폴짝 뛰어내리더니, 방금 한 말에 웃음을 참지 못해 들썩거리던 그 '머리에 든 것 없는' 사내애를 쪼아 대려고 달려가며 외쳤다.\n\n'도저히 못 견딜 것 같지만, 그래도 한번 견뎌 봐야지.' 에이미는 고모와 단둘이 남게 되자 그렇게 생각했다.\n\n\"썩 꺼지지 못해, 이 흉측한 것아!\" 폴리가 빽 소리를 질렀고, 그 무례한 말에 에이미는 끝내 훌쩍이고 말았다.\n\n# DARK DAYS.\n\n암울한 나날들.\n베스는 정말로 열병에 걸렸고, 해나와 의사 말고는 누구도 짐작하지 못할 만큼 심하게 앓았다. 자매들은 병에 대해 아는 게 없었고, 로런스 씨도 베스를 보지 못하게 한 터라, 모든 일은 해나가 도맡아 처리했다. 바쁜 뱅스 선생은 최선을 다했지만 많은 부분을 그 훌륭한 간병인에게 맡겼다. 메그는 킹네 아이들에게 병을 옮길까 봐 집에 머물며 살림을 꾸렸는데, 베스의 병에 대해서는 한마디도 적지 않은 편지를 쓸 때면 몹시 불안하고 조금은 죄스러운 마음이 들었다. 메그는 어머니를 속이는 게 옳다고는 도무지 생각할 수 없었지만, 해나의 말을 따르라는 당부를 받은 데다, 해나는 \"이만한 일로 마치 부인한테 알려서 걱정을 끼친다\"는 건 들으려고도 하지 않았다. 조는 밤낮으로 베스에게 매달렸다. 베스가 무척 잘 참아 내고, 스스로를 다스릴 수 있는 한 불평 없이 고통을 견뎌 냈으므로 그리 힘든 일은 아니었다. 그러나 열이 발작처럼 치솟을 때면 베스가 쉰 듯 끊어지는 목소리로 헛소리를 늘어놓기 시작하는 때가 찾아왔다. 그녀는 마치 사랑하는 작은 피아노를 치듯 이불을 두드렸고, 음악이라곤 남아 있지 않을 만큼 퉁퉁 부은 목으로 노래를 부르려 했다. 둘러선 낯익은 얼굴들을 알아보지 못하고 엉뚱한 이름으로 부르며, 애원하듯 어머니를 찾는 때가 온 것이다. 그제야 조는 겁에 질렸고, 메그는 사실대로 편지를 쓰게 해 달라고 애원했다. 해나조차도 \"아직 위험한 건 아니지만, 한번 생각해 보겠다\"고 말했다. 게다가 워싱턴에서 온 편지가 그들의 근심을 더했다. 마치 씨가 다시 병세가 도져서, 한동안 집에 돌아올 엄두를 내지 못한다는 것이었다.\n\n이제 그 나날들은 얼마나 어둡게 느껴졌으며, 집안은 얼마나 슬프고 적막했던가. 한때 행복했던 보금자리에 죽음의 그림자가 어른거리는 동안, 일을 하며 기다리는 자매들의 마음은 또 얼마나 무거웠던가! 바로 그때, 혼자 앉아 바느질감에 자꾸만 눈물을 떨구던 마거릿은, 자신이 돈으로 살 수 있는 그 어떤 사치보다 훨씬 더 소중한 것들—사랑과 보호, 평화와 건강이라는, 삶의 진정한 축복들—을 얼마나 넉넉히 누려 왔는지를 깨달았다. 바로 그때, 어두운 방에 틀어박혀 고통받는 어린 동생을 늘 눈앞에 두고, 그 애처로운 목소리를 줄곧 귀에 담고 지내던 조는, 베스의 천성에 깃든 아름다움과 다정함을 보는 법을 배웠다. 베스가 모든 이의 마음속에서 얼마나 깊고 따뜻한 자리를 채우고 있는지를 느꼈고, 남을 위해 살고, 누구나 지닐 수 있으며 누구나 재능이나 부나 미모보다 더 사랑하고 귀히 여겨야 할 그 소박한 미덕을 실천함으로써 가정을 행복하게 만들겠다는 베스의 욕심 없는 포부가 얼마나 값진 것인지를 인정하게 되었다. 그리고 유배지에 있던 에이미는 집으로 돌아가 베스를 위해 일하기를 간절히 바랐다. 이제는 어떤 수고도 힘들거나 성가시지 않을 것 같았고, 그 기꺼운 손길이 자신을 위해 해 준, 미처 고마워할 줄 몰랐던 그 많은 일들을 떠올리며 회한에 잠겼다. 로리는 안절부절못하는 유령처럼 그 집 주위를 맴돌았고, 로런스 씨는 그랜드피아노에 자물쇠를 채워 버렸다. 황혼녘을 즐겁게 해 주던 어린 이웃이 떠오르는 것을 차마 견딜 수 없었기 때문이다. 모두가 베스를 그리워했다. 우유 배달부도, 빵집 주인도, 식료품점 주인도, 정육점 주인도 베스의 안부를 물었다. 가엾은 후멜 부인이 자기 경솔함을 사죄하고 미나에게 입힐 수의를 얻으러 찾아왔으며, 이웃들은 온갖 위로의 음식과 안부를 보내왔다. 그녀를 가장 잘 안다고 여겼던 사람들조차, 수줍은 꼬마 베스가 그토록 많은 친구를 사귀어 두었다는 사실에 놀랐다.\n\n그러는 동안 베스는 침대에 누워 있었고, 곁에는 낡은 조애나가 함께 있었다. 헛소리를 하는 와중에도 그녀는 그 가엾고 버림받은 인형을 잊지 않았던 것이다. 베스는 고양이들을 보고 싶어 했지만, 행여 병이 옮을까 봐 데려오게 하지는 않았다. 그리고 정신이 맑은 시간이면 조를 걱정하는 마음으로 가득했다. 에이미에게는 사랑을 담은 말을 전했고, 어머니에게는 곧 편지를 쓰겠다고 전해 달라고 했다. 또 아버지가 자신이 무심했다고 여기지 않도록 한마디라도 적어 보려고 자주 연필과 종이를 찾았다. 그러나 곧 그렇게 정신이 드는 순간들마저 끝나 버렸고, 베스는 입가에 두서없는 말을 흘리며 몇 시간이고 이리저리 뒤척이거나, 아무런 기운도 되찾지 못하는 깊은 잠 속으로 가라앉았다. 뱅스 선생은 하루에 두 번씩 다녀갔고, 해나는 밤을 새워 곁을 지켰으며, 메그는 언제든 즉시 부칠 수 있도록 전보를 책상에 준비해 두었고, 조는 베스 곁을 한시도 떠나지 않았다.\n\n12월 초하루는 그들에게 그야말로 겨울다운 하루였다. 매서운 바람이 불고, 눈이 펑펑 쏟아졌으며, 한 해가 죽음을 맞을 채비를 하는 듯했다. 그날 아침 뱅스 선생은 베스를 한참 들여다보더니, 그 뜨거운 손을 자기 두 손으로 잠시 감싸 쥐었다가 가만히 내려놓고는, 해나에게 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"마치 부인이 남편 곁을 떠나올 수 있다면, 오시라고 기별하는 게 좋겠소.\"\n\n해나는 아무 말 없이 고개만 끄덕였다. 입술이 불안하게 떨렸기 때문이다. 메그는 그 말을 듣는 순간 사지에서 힘이 빠져나가는 듯 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다. 조는 잠시 핏기 없는 얼굴로 서 있다가 거실로 달려가 전보를 낚아채더니, 옷을 걸치고 폭풍 속으로 뛰쳐나갔다. 조는 금세 돌아왔고, 소리 없이 망토를 벗고 있을 때 로리가 편지 한 통을 들고 들어와, 마치 씨가 다시 회복되고 있다는 소식을 전했다. 조는 고마운 마음으로 편지를 읽었지만, 가슴을 짓누르던 무거운 짐은 도무지 가시지 않는 듯했다. 그 얼굴에 비참함이 가득해서, 로리가 다급히 물었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이야? 베스가 더 나빠졌어?\"\n\n\"엄마한테 기별했어.\" 조가 비극적인 표정으로 고무장화를 잡아당기며 말했다.\n\n\"잘했어, 조! 네가 알아서 한 거야?\" 로리가 물으며, 조의 손이 떨리는 것을 보고 그녀를 현관 의자에 앉히고 말 안 듣는 장화를 벗겨 주었다.\n\n\"아니, 의사 선생님이 그러라고 하셨어.\"\n\n\"오, 조, 그렇게까지 안 좋은 거야?\" 로리가 깜짝 놀란 얼굴로 외쳤다.\n\n\"응, 그래. 베스가 우리를 못 알아봐. 벽에 붙은 덩굴잎을 보고 '초록 비둘기 떼'라고 부르곤 했는데, 이젠 그 얘기조차 안 해. 내가 알던 베스 같지가 않아. 그런데 이걸 견디게 도와줄 사람이 아무도 없어. 엄마도 아빠도 다 떠나 계시고, 하느님은 너무 멀리 계신 것 같아서 도무지 찾을 수가 없어.\"\n\n가엾은 조의 뺨으로 눈물이 줄줄 흘러내리는 가운데, 그녀는 어둠 속을 더듬듯 막막하게 손을 내밀었다. 로리는 그 손을 자기 손으로 감싸 쥐고, 목이 멘 채로나마 애써 속삭였다.\n\n\"내가 여기 있잖아. 나를 꼭 붙잡아, 조.\"\n\n조는 말은 할 수 없었지만 정말로 그를 \"꼭 붙잡았다.\" 다정한 사람의 따스한 손길이 그녀의 쓰라린 마음을 위로해 주었고, 그 고난 속에서 오직 자신을 떠받쳐 줄 수 있는 신성한 팔에 한 걸음 더 다가서게 이끌어 주는 듯했다. 로리는 뭔가 다정하고 위안이 될 말을 건네고 싶었지만, 마땅한 말이 떠오르지 않았다. 그래서 그는 말없이 서서, 예전에 조의 어머니가 그러던 것처럼 숙인 그녀의 머리를 가만히 쓰다듬었다. 그것은 그가 할 수 있는 가장 좋은 일이었다. 그 어떤 유려한 말보다 훨씬 위로가 되었으니, 조는 그 무언의 공감을 느꼈고, 그 침묵 속에서 애정이 슬픔에 베푸는 달콤한 위안을 깨달았다. 이윽고 조는 마음을 가볍게 해 준 눈물을 닦고, 고마운 얼굴로 고개를 들었다.\n\n\"고마워, 테디. 이제 좀 나아졌어. 그렇게 막막하지가 않아. 그런 일이 닥치더라도 견뎌 보도록 애써 볼게.\"\n\n\"늘 좋은 쪽으로 희망을 품어. 그게 너한테 힘이 될 거야, 조. 곧 너희 어머니가 오실 테고, 그러면 다 잘될 거야.\"\n\n\"아빠가 좋아지셔서 정말 다행이야. 이제 엄마도 아빠를 두고 오는 걸 그렇게 마음 아파하지 않으셔도 되니까. 아, 정말! 모든 근심거리가 한꺼번에 들이닥쳐서, 그중에서도 제일 무거운 짐이 내 어깨에 얹힌 것만 같아.\" 조가 한숨을 쉬며, 젖은 손수건을 무릎 위에 펼쳐 말렸다.\n\n\"메그가 제 몫을 안 하는 거야?\" 로리가 발끈한 표정으로 물었다.\n\n\"아, 아니야. 메그도 애쓰긴 해. 하지만 나만큼 베스를 사랑하진 못해. 또 나처럼 베스를 그리워하지도 않을 거야. 베스는 내 양심 같은 존재라서, 도저히 그 애를 놓아줄 수가 없어. 못 해! 절대 못 해!\"\n\n조의 얼굴이 다시 젖은 손수건 속에 파묻혔고, 그녀는 절망적으로 흐느꼈다. 지금까지 용감하게 버티며 눈물 한 방울 보이지 않았던 그녀였다. 로리는 손등으로 눈가를 훔쳤지만, 목구멍에 치미는 먹먹한 느낌을 가라앉히고 입술을 다잡고서야 겨우 입을 뗄 수 있었다. 사내답지 못한 일인지도 모르지만, 그로서는 어쩔 수 없었다. 그리고 나는 그 점이 다행스럽다. 이윽고 조의 흐느낌이 잦아들자, 그는 희망을 담아 말했다. \"베스는 죽지 않을 거야. 그렇게 착하고, 우리 모두가 그렇게 사랑하는데, 하느님이 그 애를 아직 데려가실 리 없어.\"\n\n\"착하고 사랑스러운 사람들이 늘 먼저 죽는걸.\" 조가 신음하듯 말했다. 그러나 자신의 의심과 두려움에도 불구하고 친구의 말이 힘을 북돋아 준 덕에, 그녀는 울음을 그쳤다.\n\n\"가엾은 것, 너 완전히 지쳤구나. 막막해하는 건 너답지 않아. 잠깐만 기다려. 금방 기운 나게 해 줄게.\"\n\n로리는 한 번에 계단 두 개씩 뛰어 내려갔고, 조는 베스가 식탁에 두고 간 작은 갈색 모자에 지친 머리를 기댔다. 아무도 그것을 치울 생각을 못 했던 것이다. 그 모자에는 무슨 마력이라도 깃들어 있었던 모양이다. 그 온화한 주인의 순한 마음씨가 조에게로 스며드는 듯했기 때문이다. 로리가 포도주 한 잔을 들고 뛰어 내려오자, 조는 미소를 지으며 잔을 받아 들고 씩씩하게 말했다. \"우리 베스의 건강을 위하여, 한잔할게! 넌 정말 좋은 의사야, 테디. 게다가 이렇게나 든든한 친구라니. 내가 어떻게 다 갚지?\" 친절한 말이 어지러운 마음을 달래 주었듯, 포도주가 몸에 생기를 불어넣자 조가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"청구서는 나중에 보낼게. 그리고 오늘 밤엔 포도주 몇 잔보다 훨씬 더 네 마음속 깊은 곳까지 따뜻하게 데워 줄 걸 안겨 줄게.\" 로리가 뭔가 흡족함을 애써 누른 얼굴로 그녀를 향해 환하게 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"그게 뭔데?\" 조가 잠시 슬픔도 잊은 채 궁금해하며 외쳤다.\n\n\"어제 너희 어머니한테 전보를 쳤거든. 그랬더니 브룩 선생님이 곧 오시겠다고 답을 보내왔어. 오늘 밤 도착하실 거야. 그러면 다 잘될 거라고. 내가 그렇게 해서 기쁘지 않아?\"\n\n로리는 무척 빠르게 말했고, 순식간에 얼굴이 빨개지며 들떴다. 자매들을 실망시키거나 베스에게 해가 될까 봐 자기 계획을 비밀에 부쳐 왔던 것이다. 조는 새파랗게 질려 의자에서 벌떡 일어나더니, 그가 말을 마치는 순간 두 팔로 그의 목을 와락 끌어안으며 기쁨에 겨운 소리로 외쳐 그를 깜짝 놀라게 했다. \"오, 로리! 오, 엄마! 정말, 정말 기뻐!\" 조는 다시 울지는 않았지만, 격하게 웃음을 터뜨리며 부들부들 떨었고, 갑작스러운 소식에 잠시 정신이 얼떨떨한 듯 친구에게 매달렸다. 로리는 분명 놀랐으면서도 침착하게 행동했다. 그는 조의 등을 다독여 달래 주었고, 그녀가 진정하는 것을 보고는 내친김에 수줍게 입맞춤을 한두 번 했는데, 그 바람에 조는 단번에 정신이 들었다. 그녀는 난간을 붙든 채 그를 가만히 밀어내며 숨 가쁘게 말했다. \"아, 이러지 마! 일부러 그런 건 아니야. 내가 너무 경솔했어. 그래도 네가 해나의 반대를 무릅쓰고 그 일을 해 줄 만큼 정말 고마운 친구라서, 너한테 와락 안기는 걸 참을 수가 없었어. 어떻게 된 건지 다 얘기해 줘. 그리고 다시는 나한테 포도주 주지 마. 마시면 내가 이렇게 행동하잖아.\"\n\n\"난 괜찮은데.\" 로리가 넥타이를 바로잡으며 웃었다. \"그게 말이야, 내가 안절부절못했거든. 할아버지도 그러셨고. 우리는 해나가 권한을 너무 휘두른다고, 너희 어머니가 사실을 아셔야 한다고 생각했어. 만에 하나 베스가… 그러니까, 무슨 일이라도 생기면 너희 어머니는 우리를 결코 용서하지 않으실 거 아니야. 그래서 할아버지더러 이제 우리가 뭔가 할 때가 됐다고 말씀해 달라고 했고, 어제 전보국으로 달려갔지. 의사 선생님 표정이 심상치 않았던 데다, 내가 전보 얘기를 꺼냈을 때 해나가 하마터면 내 머리를 물어뜯을 뻔했거든. 난 누가 나한테 '윗사람 행세'하는 건 도무지 못 참아. 그래서 마음을 굳혔고, 일을 저질러 버린 거야. 너희 어머니는 분명 오실 거고, 막차가 새벽 두 시에 도착해. 내가 모시러 갈게. 너는 그저 그 복된 분이 도착하실 때까지 기쁨을 꾹 눌러 담고, 베스를 조용히 돌보고 있기만 하면 돼.\"\n\n\"로리, 넌 천사야! 내가 어떻게 고마움을 다 갚지?\"\n\n\"또 나한테 와락 안겨 봐. 난 그게 꽤 좋던데.\" 로리가 장난기 어린 표정을 지으며 말했다. 그가 그런 표정을 지은 건 두 주 만이었다.\n\n\"아니, 사양할게. 그건 너희 할아버지가 오시면 대신 그분께 할 거야. 짓궂게 굴지 말고 어서 집에 가서 쉬어. 밤을 거의 새워야 할 테니까. 고마워, 테디, 정말 고마워!\"\n\n조는 어느새 구석으로 물러나 있었고, 말을 끝마치기가 무섭게 부엌으로 후다닥 사라졌다. 그녀는 찬장에 걸터앉아, 모여 있는 고양이들에게 자기가 \"행복하다고, 오, 정말 행복하다고!\" 떠들어 댔다. 그러는 동안 로리는, 자기가 꽤 깔끔하게 일을 해냈다는 뿌듯함을 느끼며 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"저렇게 참견 잘하는 녀석은 내 평생 처음 본다니까. 그래도 용서해 줘야지. 마치 부인이 곧장 오시면 정말 좋겠구나.\" 조가 기쁜 소식을 전하자, 해나가 안도하는 표정으로 말했다.\n\n메그는 조용히 기쁨에 젖었다가 편지를 곰곰이 들여다보았다. 그동안 조는 병실을 정돈했고, 해나는 \"갑자기 손님이 올지도 모르니\" 파이 두어 개를 뚝딱 만들어 냈다. 집 안으로 신선한 공기 한 줄기가 불어 드는 듯했고, 햇살보다 더 좋은 무언가가 고요한 방들을 환하게 밝혔다. 모든 것이 그 희망찬 변화를 느끼는 듯했다. 베스의 새가 다시 지저귀기 시작했고, 창가에 놓인 에이미의 화분에서 반쯤 핀 장미 한 송이가 발견되었다. 난롯불도 유난히 명랑하게 타오르는 듯했다. 자매들은 마주칠 때마다 창백한 얼굴에 웃음꽃을 피우며 서로 끌어안고는, 힘을 북돋우듯 속삭였다. \"엄마가 오신대! 엄마가 오신대!\" 베스만 빼고 모두가 기뻐했다. 베스는 그 깊은 혼수상태에 빠져, 희망도 기쁨도, 의심도 위험도 한결같이 알지 못한 채 누워 있었다. 그것은 가엾은 광경이었다. 한때 발그레했던 얼굴은 그토록 변하여 멍하니 비어 있었고, 한때 부지런하던 손은 그토록 힘없이 야위었으며, 한때 미소 짓던 입술은 아무 말도 못 한 채 굳어 있었고, 한때 곱고 단정하던 머리칼은 베개 위에 거칠게 헝클어진 채 흩어져 있었다. 베스는 온종일 그렇게 누워, 이따금 정신이 들 때만 바싹 마른 입술로 겨우 단어 모양을 만들며 \"물!\" 하고 중얼거릴 뿐이었다. 조와 메그는 온종일 그 곁을 맴돌며 지켜보고, 기다리고, 희망을 품고, 하느님과 어머니를 믿었다. 그리고 온종일 눈이 내리고, 매서운 바람이 사납게 몰아쳤으며, 시간은 더디게 흘러갔다. 그러나 마침내 밤이 찾아왔다. 시계가 울릴 때마다, 여전히 침대 양옆에 앉아 있던 자매들은 환해지는 눈빛으로 서로를 바라보았다. 한 시간 한 시간이 도움을 더 가까이 데려다주었기 때문이다. 의사는 다녀가며, 자정 무렵에 좋든 나쁘든 어떤 변화가 나타날 듯하다고, 그 시각에 다시 오겠다고 말했다.\n\n녹초가 된 해나는 침대 발치 소파에 누워 이내 깊이 잠들었다. 로런스 씨는 거실을 오락가락하며, 마치 부인이 들어설 때 그 근심 어린 얼굴을 마주하느니 차라리 적의 포대를 마주하는 편이 낫겠다고 느꼈다. 로리는 깔개 위에 누워 쉬는 척했지만, 사색에 잠긴 표정으로 난롯불을 들여다보고 있었다. 그 표정이 그의 검은 눈동자를 더없이 부드럽고 맑게 만들어 주었다.\n\n자매들은 그날 밤을 결코 잊지 못했다. 밤을 새워 곁을 지키는 동안 그들에게는 잠이 오지 않았고, 그런 시각이면 우리에게 찾아오기 마련인 그 끔찍한 무력감이 짓눌렀던 것이다.\n\n\"하느님이 베스를 살려 주신다면, 다시는 절대 불평하지 않을 거야.\" 메그가 간절히 속삭였다.\n\n\"하느님이 베스를 살려 주신다면, 평생토록 그분을 사랑하고 섬기려고 애쓸 거야.\" 조도 그에 못지않은 열의로 답했다.\n\n\"마음이라는 게 없었으면 좋겠어. 너무 아파서.\" 메그가 잠시 침묵하다 한숨지었다.\n\n\"인생이 늘 이만큼 힘들다면, 우리가 어떻게 이걸 다 헤쳐 나갈 수 있을지 모르겠어.\" 동생이 낙담한 듯 덧붙였다.\n\n그때 시계가 열두 시를 알렸고, 두 사람은 자기들을 잊은 채 베스를 지켜보았다. 그녀의 핼쑥한 얼굴 위로 어떤 변화가 스치는 것 같았기 때문이다. 집 안은 죽은 듯이 고요했고, 흐느끼는 바람 소리 말고는 그 깊은 정적을 깨뜨리는 것이 아무것도 없었다. 지친 해나는 계속 잠들어 있었고, 그 작은 침대 위에 드리우는 듯한 창백한 그림자를 본 사람은 두 자매뿐이었다. 한 시간이 지났지만, 로리가 역으로 조용히 떠난 것 말고는 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다. 또 한 시간이 지났다. 여전히 아무도 오지 않았고, 폭풍 속에서 지체되거나, 도중에 사고가 났거나, 무엇보다도 워싱턴에 큰 슬픔이 닥친 게 아닐까 하는 불안한 두려움이 가엾은 자매들을 사로잡았다.\n\n두 시가 넘은 무렵이었다. 창가에 서서 눈으로 뒤덮인 수의 같은 세상이 얼마나 황량해 보이는지 생각하던 조가 침대 쪽에서 어떤 움직임을 듣고 재빨리 돌아보니, 메그가 어머니의 안락의자 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 얼굴을 파묻고 있었다. 끔찍한 두려움이 싸늘하게 조를 스치고 지나갔다. '베스가 죽었구나. 그래서 메그가 차마 나한테 말을 못 하는 거야.'\n\n조는 순식간에 제자리로 돌아왔고, 흥분한 그녀의 눈에는 엄청난 변화가 일어난 듯 보였다. 열에 들뜬 홍조와 고통스러운 기색이 사라지고, 그 사랑스러운 작은 얼굴이 완전한 안식 속에서 너무도 창백하고 평온해 보여서, 조는 울거나 슬퍼할 마음조차 들지 않았다. 가장 사랑하는 동생 위로 몸을 깊이 숙인 채, 그녀는 온 마음을 입술에 담아 그 축축한 이마에 입을 맞추고 가만히 속삭였다. \"잘 가, 베스야. 잘 가!\"\n\n그 기척에 깬 듯 해나가 잠에서 흠칫 깨어나 침대로 황급히 다가가더니, 베스를 들여다보고, 손을 만져 보고, 입가에 귀를 대고 숨소리를 듣고는, 앞치마를 머리 위로 뒤집어쓰고 주저앉아 몸을 앞뒤로 흔들며 낮은 소리로 외쳤다. \"열이 내렸어. 자연스럽게 잠든 거라고. 살갗이 촉촉하고 숨도 편안해졌어. 하느님 감사합니다! 아이고, 세상에나!\"\n\n자매들이 그 기쁜 사실을 채 믿기도 전에, 의사가 와서 그것을 확인해 주었다. 그는 못생긴 사람이었지만, 그가 미소 지으며 그들을 아버지처럼 바라보고 말했을 때, 그 얼굴은 더없이 천사 같아 보였다. \"그래요, 얘들아, 이번에는 이 어린 아가씨가 잘 이겨 낼 것 같구나. 집 안을 조용히 하고, 푹 자게 두렴. 그리고 깨어나면 이걸 먹이고—\"\n\n무엇을 먹이라는 것이었는지, 둘 중 누구도 듣지 못했다. 두 사람 다 어두운 복도로 살그머니 빠져나가, 계단에 앉아 서로를 꼭 끌어안고는, 말로 다 못 할 만큼 벅찬 마음으로 기뻐했기 때문이다. 충실한 해나에게 입맞춤과 포옹을 받으러 다시 들어갔을 때, 베스는 예전처럼 한쪽 뺨을 손에 베고 누워 있었고, 그 무서운 창백함은 사라지고 없었으며, 막 잠든 사람처럼 고요하게 숨 쉬고 있었다.\n\n\"이제 엄마만 오시면 좋으련만!\" 겨울밤이 차츰 물러가기 시작할 무렵 조가 말했다.\n\n\"이것 봐.\" 메그가 반쯤 핀 하얀 장미 한 송이를 들고 다가오며 말했다. \"혹시 베스가… 우리 곁을 떠난다면, 내일 그 애 손에 쥐여 주기에 이게 좀처럼 피지 못할 줄 알았는데. 그런데 밤새 꽃을 피웠지 뭐야. 이제 여기 내 꽃병에 꽂아 둘래. 그러면 그 애가 깨어났을 때 제일 먼저 보는 게 이 작은 장미랑 엄마 얼굴일 테니까.\"\n\n길고 슬픈 밤샘이 끝난 이른 아침, 밖을 내다보는 메그와 조의 무거운 눈에 비친 것만큼, 해가 그토록 아름답게 떠오른 적도, 세상이 그토록 사랑스러워 보인 적도 없었다.\n\n\"꼭 요정 나라 같아.\" 메그가 커튼 뒤에 서서 눈부신 광경을 바라보며 혼자 미소 지었다.\n\n\"쉿!\" 조가 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다.\n\n정말이었다. 아래쪽 현관에서 방울 소리가 났고, 해나의 외침이 들리더니, 이어 로리의 목소리가 기쁨에 찬 속삭임으로 말했다. \"얘들아, 오셨어! 오셨어!\"\n에이미의 유언장.\n\n# AMY'S WILL.\n\n집에서 이런 일들이 벌어지는 동안, 에이미는 마치 고모 댁에서 힘든 시간을 보내고 있었다. 그녀는 유배 생활을 절절히 느꼈고, 난생처음 자신이 집에서 얼마나 많은 사랑과 귀여움을 받아 왔는지를 깨달았다. 마치 고모는 누구도 어하지 않았다. 그런 것을 탐탁지 않게 여겼던 것이다. 하지만 고모도 다정하게 대하려는 마음은 있었으니, 얌전한 그 꼬마가 무척 마음에 들었기 때문이다. 게다가 마치 고모의 늙은 가슴 한구석에는, 입 밖에 내는 것을 점잖지 못하다 여겼을 뿐, 조카의 아이들을 향한 살뜰한 정이 자리하고 있었다. 고모는 정말 에이미를 행복하게 해 주려고 애를 썼지만, 아이고 저런, 어찌나 실수를 많이 했던지! 어떤 노인들은 주름과 백발에도 불구하고 마음만은 젊어서, 아이들의 자잘한 근심과 기쁨에 공감하고, 아이들을 편안하게 해 주며, 즐거운 놀이 속에 지혜로운 가르침을 슬쩍 감추고, 더없이 다정한 방식으로 우정을 주고받는다. 그러나 마치 고모에게는 그런 재주가 없었고, 자신의 규칙과 명령, 새침한 태도, 길고 따분한 설교로 에이미를 몹시 들볶았다. 고모는 그 아이가 언니보다 한결 고분고분하고 상냥한 것을 알고는, 집에서의 자유와 응석이 끼친 나쁜 영향을 되도록 바로잡는 것이 자기 의무라고 여겼다. 그리하여 고모는 에이미를 손에 쥐고, 자기가 육십 년 전에 배운 그대로 가르쳤다. 그 과정은 에이미의 영혼에 절망을 가져다주었고, 그녀를 마치 무척이나 엄격한 거미의 거미줄에 걸린 파리 신세처럼 느끼게 했다.\n\n에이미는 아침마다 찻잔을 씻고, 구식 숟가락이며 통통한 은제 찻주전자며 유리잔을 반들반들 윤이 나도록 닦아야 했다. 그런 다음에는 방의 먼지를 털어야 했는데, 그게 얼마나 진을 빼는 일이었던지! 티끌 하나도 마치 고모의 눈을 벗어나지 못했고, 가구마다 갈고리 모양 다리에 정교한 조각이 가득해서 아무리 닦아도 고모 마음에 차게 할 수가 없었다. 그러고 나면 폴리에게 모이를 주고, 무릎에 앉히는 강아지를 빗질해 주고, 물건을 가져오거나 분부를 전하느라 위아래층을 열두 번씩 오르내려야 했다. 고모는 다리가 몹시 불편해서 좀처럼 그 큰 의자를 떠나지 않았던 것이다. 이 진 빠지는 노동을 마치고 나면 공부를 해야 했는데, 이는 에이미가 지닌 온갖 미덕을 날마다 시험하는 일이었다. 그런 다음에야 운동이나 놀이를 위한 한 시간이 주어졌는데, 그 시간을 어찌나 만끽했던지! 로리가 날마다 찾아와 마치 고모를 살살 구슬려, 에이미가 그와 함께 외출할 수 있게 해 주었고, 그러면 둘은 걷기도 하고 마차도 타며 신나게 놀았다. 저녁을 먹고 나면 에이미는 책을 소리 내어 읽어야 했고, 고모가 잠든 동안 가만히 앉아 있어야 했다. 고모는 대개 첫 장을 넘기다 말고 곯아떨어져서는 한 시간씩 잤다. 그다음에는 조각보나 수건이 나타났고, 에이미는 겉으로는 고분고분, 속으로는 반항하며 땅거미가 질 때까지 바느질을 했다. 그러고 나면 차 마실 시간까지 마음대로 놀아도 좋다는 허락이 떨어졌다. 그중에서도 저녁 시간이 가장 끔찍했다. 마치 고모가 자기 젊은 시절 이야기를 길게 늘어놓기 시작했는데, 어찌나 이루 말할 수 없이 따분한지, 에이미는 늘 자기 가혹한 신세를 한탄하며 울 작정으로 잠자리에 들 채비를 했다. 하지만 대개는 눈물 한두 방울을 채 짜내기도 전에 잠들어 버리곤 했다.\n\n로리와 늙은 하녀 에스터가 아니었다면, 에이미는 그 끔찍한 시간을 도저히 견뎌 내지 못했을 것이라고 느꼈다. 앵무새 한 마리만으로도 그녀를 정신없게 만들기에 충분했다. 폴리는 에이미가 자기를 좋아하지 않는다는 걸 금세 알아채고는, 할 수 있는 한 못되게 굴어 앙갚음을 했다. 에이미가 가까이 다가가기만 하면 머리칼을 잡아당겼고, 새장을 갓 청소해 놓으면 빵과 우유를 엎질러 골탕을 먹였으며, 마님이 졸고 있을 때 모프를 쪼아 짖게 만들었다. 손님 앞에서 에이미에게 욕설을 퍼붓는 등, 모든 면에서 괘씸하기 짝이 없는 늙은 새처럼 굴었다. 게다가 에이미는 그 개도 견딜 수가 없었다. 살이 뒤룩뒤룩 찐 심술궂은 짐승으로, 단장을 해 주려고만 하면 으르렁대고 깽깽거렸으며, 먹을 게 탐날 때면—하루에 열두 번쯤은 그랬다—발랑 누워서 네 다리를 죄다 공중에 쳐든 채 더없이 멍청한 표정을 지었다. 요리사는 성미가 고약했고, 늙은 마부는 귀가 먹었으며, 그 어린 아가씨에게 조금이라도 관심을 보이는 사람은 에스터뿐이었다.\n\n에스터는 프랑스 여자로, 자기가 \"마님\"이라 부르는 안주인과 여러 해를 함께 살아온 사람이었다. 그녀는 자기 없이는 지낼 수 없는 그 노부인에게 오히려 군림하다시피 했다. 본명은 에스텔이었지만, 마치 고모가 이름을 바꾸라고 명령했고, 그녀는 다시는 종교를 바꾸라는 말을 듣지 않는다는 조건으로 그 명령에 따랐다. 에스터는 마드무아젤을 무척 마음에 들어 했고, 에이미가 곁에 앉아 자기가 마님의 레이스를 손질하는 동안, 프랑스에서 살던 시절의 별난 이야기들을 들려주며 즐겁게 해 주었다. 또 그 큰 집 안을 마음껏 돌아다니며, 커다란 옷장과 오래된 궤짝 속에 간직된 진기하고 예쁜 물건들을 구경하게 해 주었다. 마치 고모는 까치처럼 물건을 잔뜩 쟁여 두는 사람이었던 것이다. 에이미가 가장 좋아한 것은 인도풍 장식장이었는데, 별난 서랍과 작은 칸들, 비밀 공간들로 가득했고, 그 안에는 온갖 장신구가 들어 있었다. 어떤 것은 값진 것이었고, 어떤 것은 그저 진기한 것이었으며, 하나같이 다소간 골동품이었다. 이 물건들을 살펴보고 정리하는 일은 에이미에게 큰 만족을 주었는데, 특히 보석함이 그러했다. 벨벳 방석 위에는 사십 년 전 한 미인을 빛내 주던 장신구들이 놓여 있었다. 마치 고모가 사교계에 처음 나설 때 걸쳤던 석류석 세트, 결혼식 날 아버지가 준 진주, 연인이 준 다이아몬드, 흑옥으로 만든 상복용 반지와 핀, 죽은 친구들의 초상이 든 별난 로켓에는 안쪽에 머리카락으로 만든 수양버들 무늬가 들어 있었다. 하나뿐인 어린 딸이 차던 아기 팔찌, 그토록 많은 아이 손이 가지고 놀던 붉은 인장이 달린 마치 고모부의 커다란 회중시계도 있었다. 그리고 상자 하나에는 마치 고모의 결혼반지가 따로 들어 있었는데, 이제는 통통해진 손가락에 끼우기엔 너무 작았지만, 그 모든 보석 중에서도 가장 귀한 것인 양 고이 간직되어 있었다.\n\n\"마드무아젤은 마음대로 고를 수 있다면 어느 걸 고르시겠어요?\" 에스터가 물었다. 그녀는 늘 가까이 앉아 귀중품을 살피고 잠가 두곤 했다.\n\n\"난 다이아몬드가 제일 좋은데, 그중엔 목걸이가 없네. 난 목걸이를 좋아하거든. 잘 어울리니까. 고를 수 있다면 이걸 고를래.\" 에이미가 대답하며, 금과 흑단 구슬을 꿴 줄에 똑같은 재질의 묵직한 십자가가 매달린 것을 한껏 감탄 어린 눈으로 바라보았다.\n\n\"저도 그게 탐나요. 하지만 목걸이로서는 아니에요. 아, 그럼요! 제게 그건 묵주랍니다. 그러니 저라면 착실한 가톨릭 신자답게 그 용도로 쓰겠어요.\" 에스터가 그 아름다운 물건을 아쉬운 듯 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"그게, 아주머니가 거울 위에 걸어 둔 좋은 향이 나는 나무 구슬 줄처럼 쓰는 거예요?\" 에이미가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 정말 그래요. 기도할 때 쓰는 거죠. 이렇게 고운 묵주를 헛된 장신구로 걸치는 대신 기도에 쓴다면, 성인들께서도 기뻐하실 거예요.\"\n\n\"아주머니는 기도하면서 큰 위안을 얻나 봐요, 에스터. 늘 차분하고 흡족한 얼굴로 내려오시잖아요. 나도 그럴 수 있으면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"마드무아젤이 가톨릭 신자라면 참된 위안을 얻으실 텐데요. 하지만 그럴 수 없으니, 제가 마님 이전에 모셨던 좋은 안주인처럼, 날마다 따로 한적한 데로 가서 사색하고 기도하시면 좋을 거예요. 그분께는 작은 예배실이 있었는데, 그 안에서 숱한 시름을 달래셨답니다.\"\n\n\"나도 그렇게 하는 게 옳을까요?\" 에이미가 물었다. 외로움 속에서 그녀는 무언가 도움이 필요하다고 느끼던 참이었고, 베스가 곁에서 일러 주지 않으니 자기 작은 책을 잊어버리기 일쑤라는 걸 깨닫고 있었다.\n\n\"아주 훌륭하고 멋진 일이죠. 마드무아젤이 좋으시다면 제가 기꺼이 작은 화장방을 꾸며 드릴게요. 마님께는 아무 말씀 마시고, 마님이 주무실 때 거기로 가서 잠시 혼자 앉아 좋은 생각을 하고, 동생을 지켜 달라고 하느님께 기도하세요.\"\n\n에스터는 진심으로 신앙심이 깊었고, 그 조언도 무척 진실한 것이었다. 다정한 마음씨를 지닌 그녀는 근심에 잠긴 자매들을 깊이 가엾게 여겼던 것이다. 에이미는 그 생각이 마음에 들어, 자기 방 옆의 환한 벽장을 꾸며도 좋다고 허락하며, 그것이 자기에게 도움이 되기를 바랐다.\n\n\"마치 고모님이 돌아가시면 이 예쁜 물건들이 다 어디로 갈지 알았으면 좋겠어요.\" 에이미가 반짝이는 묵주를 천천히 제자리에 놓고 보석함을 하나하나 닫으며 말했다.\n\n\"아가씨하고 언니들한테 가요. 제가 알아요. 마님이 저한테 털어놓으셨거든요. 제가 유언장 증인을 섰으니까요. 그렇게 되어 있어요.\" 에스터가 미소 지으며 속삭였다.\n\n\"잘됐다! 그래도 지금 우리한테 주시면 좋을 텐데. 미-루-는 건 별로거든요.\" 에이미가 다이아몬드를 마지막으로 한 번 더 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"아가씨들이 이런 걸 차기엔 아직 너무 일러요. 제일 먼저 약혼하는 분이 진주를 갖게 될 거예요. 마님이 그렇게 말씀하셨거든요. 그리고 제 짐작에는, 아가씨가 떠날 때 저 작은 터키석 반지를 주실 것 같아요. 마님이 아가씨의 얌전한 행실과 사랑스러운 몸가짐을 흡족해하시니까요.\"\n\n\"정말요? 아, 저 예쁜 반지만 가질 수 있다면 양처럼 얌전해질래요! 키티 브라이언트 것보다 훨씬 예쁜걸. 역시 마치 고모님이 난 좋아요.\" 에이미는 기뻐하는 얼굴로 그 파란 반지를 끼워 보며, 꼭 그것을 얻어 내고 말겠다고 굳게 마음먹었다.\n\n그날부터 에이미는 순종의 본보기가 되었고, 노부인은 자기 교육이 거둔 성과에 흐뭇하게 감탄했다. 에스터는 벽장에 작은 탁자를 들이고, 그 앞에 발받침을 놓고, 위에는 잠긴 방들 중 한 곳에서 가져온 그림을 걸어 주었다. 에스터는 그 그림이 그리 값진 것은 아니라고 여겼지만, 마침맞은 그림이라 빌려 온 것이었다. 마님이 결코 알아채지 못할 것이며, 안다 한들 개의치 않으리라는 걸 잘 알았던 것이다. 그러나 그것은 사실 세계의 유명한 그림 중 하나를 매우 값지게 모사한 것이었고, 아름다움을 사랑하는 에이미의 눈은 거룩한 성모의 다정한 얼굴을 올려다보는 데 도무지 싫증을 내지 않았다. 그러는 동안 그녀의 마음속에서는 자신만의 애틋한 생각들이 분주히 일었다. 탁자 위에는 작은 신약성서와 찬송가책을 올려 두었고, 로리가 가져다주는 가장 좋은 꽃들로 꽃병을 늘 가득 채워 두었으며, 날마다 그곳에 와서 \"혼자 앉아 좋은 생각을 하고, 동생을 지켜 달라고 하느님께 기도\"했다. 에스터는 에이미에게 은 십자가가 달린 검은 구슬 묵주를 주었지만, 에이미는 그것을 걸어 두기만 하고 쓰지는 않았다. 개신교 기도에 어울리는 것인지 미심쩍었기 때문이다.\n\n이 모든 일에서 그 어린 소녀는 무척 진실했다. 안전한 가정의 둥지 밖에 홀로 남겨지고 보니, 매달릴 다정한 손길이 너무도 절실하여, 그녀는 본능적으로 그 강하고 다정한 친구께로 돌아섰다. 아버지의 사랑으로 자신의 어린 자녀들을 가장 가까이서 감싸 안아 주시는 분께로. 에이미는 자신을 이해하고 다스리는 데 어머니의 도움이 그리웠지만, 어디를 바라봐야 하는지를 배워 둔 터라, 그 길을 찾아 미덥게 그 위를 걸어가려고 최선을 다했다. 그러나 에이미는 어린 순례자였고, 지금으로서는 그 짐이 무척 무겁게 느껴졌다. 그녀는 자신을 잊고, 명랑한 마음을 유지하고, 아무도 보아 주거나 칭찬해 주지 않더라도 옳은 일을 하는 것으로 만족하려고 애썼다. 아주, 아주 착해지려는 첫 시도로, 에이미는 마치 고모가 그랬듯 자기 유언장을 작성하기로 마음먹었다. 정말로 자기가 병에 걸려 죽기라도 하면, 자기 소유물이 공정하고 너그럽게 나누어지도록 하기 위해서였다. 자기 눈에는 고모의 보석들만큼이나 소중한 그 작은 보물들을 내어 줄 생각만 해도 가슴이 아렸다.\n\n놀이 시간 중 하루를 골라, 에이미는 그 중요한 문서를 나름대로 정성껏 써 내려갔는데, 몇몇 법률 용어는 에스터의 도움을 받았다. 그 마음씨 고운 프랑스 여자가 자기 이름을 서명해 주자, 에이미는 안도하며 그것을 잘 챙겨 두었다. 두 번째 증인으로 세우고 싶은 로리에게 보여 줄 작정이었다. 그날은 비가 와서, 에이미는 큰 침실 중 한 곳에서 혼자 놀려고 위층으로 올라가며, 말동무 삼아 폴리를 데려갔다. 그 방에는 구식 의상으로 가득 찬 옷장이 있었는데, 에스터가 그 옷들을 가지고 놀게 해 주었던 것이다. 에이미가 가장 좋아하는 놀이는, 빛바랜 비단옷으로 차려입고, 긴 거울 앞을 오르락내리락 거닐며, 점잔 빼며 인사를 하고, 옷자락을 사르락사르락 끌고 다니는 것이었다. 그 소리가 귀를 즐겁게 했던 것이다. 이날도 어찌나 그 놀이에 빠져 있었던지, 로리가 초인종을 누르는 소리도 듣지 못했고, 자기를 들여다보는 그의 얼굴도 보지 못했다. 그녀는 자못 점잖게 이리저리 거닐며, 부채를 살랑살랑 흔들고 고개를 까닥거렸다. 머리에는 커다란 분홍색 터번을 쓰고 있었는데, 파란 비단 드레스에 노란 누비 속치마와 묘하게 어우러졌다. 굽 높은 구두를 신은 탓에 조심조심 걸어야 했는데, 나중에 로리가 조에게 말했듯이, 그 화사한 차림으로 종종걸음 치는 모습은 우스꽝스러운 광경이었다. 게다가 폴리가 바로 뒤에서 옆으로 뒤뚱거리며 거드름을 피우면서, 할 수 있는 한 에이미를 흉내 냈고, 이따금 멈춰 서서 웃거나 이렇게 외치곤 했다. \"우리 멋지지? 썩 꺼져, 이 흉측한 것아! 입 다물어! 입 맞춰 줘, 얘야! 하! 하!\"\n\n여왕 폐하의 심기를 거스를까 봐 터져 나오려는 웃음을 가까스로 참고서, 로리는 문을 두드렸고, 정중한 환대를 받았다.\n\n\"앉아서 쉬고 있어. 내가 이것들 좀 치울게. 그러고 나서 아주 진지한 문제로 너랑 상의하고 싶거든.\" 에이미가 자기 화려한 차림을 다 보여 주고, 폴리를 구석으로 몰아넣은 뒤 말했다. \"저 새는 내 인생의 골칫거리야.\" 그녀가 머리에서 분홍색 산더미를 벗어 내리며 말을 이었다. 로리는 의자에 거꾸로 걸터앉았다. \"어제 고모님이 주무시고, 내가 생쥐처럼 쥐 죽은 듯 있으려고 애쓰는데, 폴리가 새장 안에서 빽빽거리며 푸드덕거리는 거야. 그래서 꺼내 주려고 갔더니, 글쎄 거기 커다란 거미가 있지 뭐야. 내가 거미를 쿡 찔러서 내쫓으니까, 그게 책장 밑으로 도망쳤어. 폴리가 곧장 그 뒤를 졸졸 따라가더니, 몸을 굽히고 책장 밑을 들여다보면서, 그 우스운 말투로 한쪽 눈을 까닥하며 이러는 거야. '나와서 산책이나 하지, 얘야.' 나도 모르게 웃음이 터졌고, 그 바람에 폴리가 욕을 해 대니까, 고모님이 깨셔서 우리 둘 다 야단치셨지 뭐야.\"\n\n\"그래서 거미가 그 영감님 초대에 응하던?\" 로리가 하품하며 물었다.\n\n\"응. 거미가 기어 나오니까, 폴리는 기겁을 하고 도망쳐서 고모님 의자 위로 기어 올라가더니, 내가 거미를 쫓는 동안 '저거 잡아! 잡아! 잡아!' 하고 외쳐 댔어.\"\n\n\"거짓말! 어이쿠 맙소사!\" 앵무새가 로리의 발가락을 쪼며 외쳤다.\n\n\"네가 내 거였으면 목을 비틀어 버렸을 텐데, 이 늙은 골칫덩어리야.\" 로리가 새를 향해 주먹을 흔들며 외치자, 폴리는 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃하고는 점잖게 깍깍거렸다. \"할렐루야! 복 받으세요, 얘야!\"\n\n\"이제 됐어.\" 에이미가 옷장을 닫고 주머니에서 종이 한 장을 꺼내며 말했다. \"이거 좀 읽어 봐 줘. 그리고 이게 법적으로 맞고 제대로 된 건지 말해 줘. 난 이걸 꼭 해 둬야 할 것 같았어. 인생이란 알 수 없는 거고, 내 무덤을 두고 서로 감정 상하는 일은 없었으면 하니까.\"\n\n로리는 입술을 깨물고, 수심에 잠긴 그 작은 연설가에게서 살짝 몸을 돌린 채, 다음 문서를 읽어 내려갔다. 철자법을 감안하면 칭찬할 만큼 진지한 태도였다.\n\"나의 마지막 유언장과 유언.\"\n\n\"나, 에이미 커티스 마치는, 멀쩡한 정신으로, 나의 모든 현세 재산을 다음과 같이 주고 물려준다. 즉, 곧, 말하자면—\"\n\n\"아버지께는, 나의 가장 좋은 그림들과 스케치, 지도, 그리고 액자를 포함한 미술 작품들을. 또 나의 100달러를, 마음대로 쓰시도록.\"\n\n\"어머니께는, 주머니 달린 파란 앞치마만 빼고 나의 모든 옷을. 또 나의 초상화와 메달을, 깊은 사랑과 함께.\"\n\n\"사랑하는 언니 마거릿에게는, 나의 터키석 반지를 (내가 갖게 된다면) 준다. 또 비둘기 무늬가 그려진 초록 상자도, 그리고 목에 두를 진짜 레이스 한 조각과, 언니의 '어린 동생'을 기리는 뜻으로 내가 그린 언니 스케치도.\"\n\n\"조 언니에게는 나의 브로치를 남긴다. 봉랍으로 고친 그거 말이야. 또 청동 잉크스탠드도—뚜껑은 언니가 잃어버렸지만—그리고 내가 가장 아끼는 석고 토끼도. 언니 이야기를 태워 버린 게 미안하니까.\"\n\n\"베스에게는 (나보다 오래 산다면) 나의 인형들과 작은 서랍장, 부채, 아마포 깃, 그리고 새 슬리퍼를 준다. 다 나으면 몸이 야월 테니 신을 수 있다면. 그리고 늙은 조애나를 놀려 댄 것에 대한 후회도 이로써 함께 남긴다.\"\n\n\"나의 친구이자 이웃인 시어도어 로런스에게는 나의 종이 죽 포트폴리오를 물려준다. 또 내가 만든 점토 말 모형도. 비록 그게 목이 없다고 말하긴 했지만. 또 시련의 시간에 베풀어 준 그 큰 친절에 대한 보답으로, 내 미술 작품 중 그가 좋아하는 것 무엇이든 하나를. 노트르담이 제일 잘된 거야.\"\n\n\"우리의 존경하는 은인 로런스 씨께는, 뚜껑에 거울이 달린 자주색 상자를 남긴다. 그분 펜을 넣어 두기에 좋을 것이고, 자기 가족에게, 특히 베스에게 베풀어 주신 호의에 감사하는 떠난 소녀를 떠올리시게 할 것이다.\"\n\n\"내가 가장 좋아하는 놀이 동무 키티 브라이언트에게는 파란 비단 앞치마와 금구슬 반지를, 입맞춤과 함께 주고 싶다.\"\n\n\"해나에게는 갖고 싶어 하던 모자 상자와, 내가 남기는 조각보를 다 준다. '이걸 볼 때마다 나를 기억해' 주기를 바라면서.\"\n\n\"이제 나의 가장 값진 재산을 다 처분했으니, 모두가 만족하고 죽은 자를 탓하지 않기를 바란다. 나는 모든 이를 용서하며, 나팔 소리가 울릴 때 우리 모두 다시 만나기를 믿는다. 아멘.\"\n\n\"이 유언장과 유언에, 나는 1861년 주님의 해 11월 20일에 나의 손으로 서명하고 봉인한다.\"\n\"에이미 커티스 마치.\n\n증인: 에스텔 발노르, 시어도어 로런스.\"\n마지막 이름은 연필로 적혀 있었고, 에이미는 그가 그것을 잉크로 다시 써서 자기를 위해 제대로 봉해 주어야 한다고 설명했다.\n\n\"이런 생각을 어떻게 한 거야? 누가 베스가 자기 물건들을 나눠 주려 한다는 얘길 해 줬어?\" 로리가 진지하게 물었다. 에이미가 봉랍이 묻은 붉은 끈 한 조각과 가느다란 양초, 잉크 받침대를 그의 앞에 놓아 주는 동안이었다.\n\n에이미는 자초지종을 설명하더니, 불안하게 물었다. \"베스는 어때?\"\n\n\"괜히 말을 꺼냈네. 하지만 이미 했으니까 얘기해 줄게. 어느 날 베스가 너무 아파서, 조한테 피아노는 메그한테, 고양이들은 너한테, 그리고 그 가엾고 낡은 인형은 조한테 주고 싶다고 했대. 자기를 봐서라도 조가 인형을 아껴 줄 테니까. 베스는 줄 게 너무 적어서 미안하다며, 나머지 우리한테는 머리카락 한 줌씩을 남기고, 할아버지께는 가장 큰 사랑을 전했어. 베스는 유언장 같은 건 생각도 못 했지.\"\n\n로리는 말을 하면서 서명하고 봉인하느라, 종이 위로 굵은 눈물 한 방울이 떨어질 때까지 고개를 들지 않았다. 에이미의 얼굴은 시름으로 가득했지만, 그녀는 이렇게만 말했다. \"사람들이 유언장에 가끔 추신 같은 걸 덧붙이기도 하지 않아?\"\n\n\"응. '추가 조항'이라고들 하지.\"\n\n\"그럼 내 것에도 하나 넣어 줘. 내 곱슬머리를 전부 잘라서 친구들한테 나눠 주고 싶다고. 깜빡했어. 그렇게 하고 싶어. 내 외모가 망가지겠지만.\"\n\n로리는 에이미의 마지막이자 가장 큰 희생에 미소 지으며 그것을 덧붙여 주었다. 그러고 나서 한 시간 동안 그녀를 즐겁게 해 주었고, 그녀가 겪은 온갖 시련을 무척 흥미롭게 들어 주었다. 그러나 돌아가려고 일어서자, 에이미가 그를 붙잡고 떨리는 입술로 속삭였다. \"베스가 정말로 위험해?\"\n\n\"그런 것 같아. 하지만 우린 좋은 쪽으로 희망을 품어야 해. 그러니 울지 마, 얘야.\" 로리는 형제처럼 다정한 몸짓으로 그녀를 한 팔로 감쌌고, 그것은 큰 위로가 되었다.\n\n그가 떠나자, 에이미는 자기 작은 예배실로 가서, 황혼 속에 앉아 베스를 위해 기도했다. 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 가슴이 미어지는 채로, 터키석 반지가 백만 개 있어도 그 다정한 어린 동생을 잃은 슬픔은 위로받을 수 없으리라는 것을 느끼면서.\n은밀한 이야기.\n\n어머니와 딸들의 재회를 묘사할 말을 나는 도무지 찾지 못하겠다. 그런 시간은 살아 내기에는 더없이 아름답지만 글로 옮기기는 무척 어려운 법이니, 그저 독자 여러분의 상상에 맡기고, 다만 집 안이 진정한 행복으로 가득했으며 메그의 애틋한 희망이 이루어졌다는 것만 말해 두겠다. 베스가 그 길고 치유의 잠에서 깨어났을 때, 그 눈에 맨 처음 들어온 것이 정말로 작은 장미와 어머니의 얼굴이었던 것이다. 너무 기운이 없어 무엇에도 놀랄 수 없었던 베스는 그저 미소만 지으며, 자기를 감싸는 사랑의 품속으로 바싹 파고들었다. 마침내 그 굶주린 갈망이 채워졌다고 느끼면서. 그러고는 다시 잠들었고, 자매들은 어머니의 시중을 들었다. 어머니가 잠결에도 자기 손을 꼭 쥔 그 야윈 손을 차마 풀지 못했기 때문이다. 해나는 흥분을 달리 풀 길이 없자, 먼 길을 온 분을 위해 놀라운 아침상을 \"차려 냈다.\" 메그와 조는 효성스러운 아기 황새들처럼 어머니에게 음식을 떠먹이며, 어머니가 속삭이듯 들려주는 이야기에 귀를 기울였다. 아버지의 상태며, 남아서 아버지를 간병하겠다는 브룩 씨의 약속이며, 돌아오는 길에 폭풍이 빚은 지체며, 피로와 근심과 추위에 녹초가 된 채 도착했을 때 로리의 희망찬 얼굴이 안겨 준 이루 말할 수 없는 위안에 대해서.\n\n얼마나 기이하면서도 즐거운 하루였던가! 밖은 그토록 눈부시고 화사했으니, 마치 온 세상이 첫눈을 맞이하러 나온 듯했다. 안은 그토록 고요하고 평온했으니, 모두가 밤샘에 지쳐 잠들었고, 졸린 해나가 문간에서 보초를 서는 동안 집 안에는 안식일 같은 정적이 감돌았다. 짐을 벗어 던진 듯한 더없는 안도감 속에서, 메그와 조는 지친 눈을 감고 누워 쉬었다. 마치 폭풍에 시달리다 고요한 항구에 무사히 닻을 내린 배들처럼. 마치 부인은 베스 곁을 떠나지 않고 큰 의자에서 쉬면서도, 마치 되찾은 보물을 지켜보는 구두쇠처럼, 자주 깨어나 아이를 바라보고, 만져 보고, 골똘히 들여다보았다.\n\n한편 로리는 에이미를 위로하러 부리나케 달려가서, 어찌나 이야기를 잘했던지 마치 고모마저 자기도 모르게 \"훌쩍\"였고, 단 한 번도 \"내가 그럴 줄 알았다\"는 말을 하지 않았다. 이 자리에서 에이미가 어찌나 의젓하게 처신했던지, 나는 그 작은 예배실에서의 좋은 생각들이 정말로 열매를 맺기 시작했다고 본다. 에이미는 얼른 눈물을 닦았고, 어머니를 보고 싶은 조바심을 억눌렀으며, 노부인이 에이미가 \"훌륭한 작은 숙녀답게 처신했다\"는 로리의 의견에 진심으로 동의했을 때, 터키석 반지 생각조차 하지 않았다. 폴리마저 감명을 받은 듯, 에이미를 \"착한 아이\"라 부르고, 복을 빌어 주며, 더없이 상냥한 어조로 \"이리 와서 산책하지, 얘야\" 하고 졸랐다. 에이미는 화창한 겨울 날씨를 즐기러 기꺼이 밖으로 나가고 싶었다. 하지만 로리가 사내답게 애써 감추려는데도 졸음에 겨워 꾸벅거리는 것을 알아채고는, 그를 구슬려 소파에서 쉬게 하고, 자신은 어머니에게 보낼 편지를 썼다. 한참 걸려 편지를 다 쓰고 돌아와 보니, 로리는 두 팔을 머리 밑에 받친 채 길게 뻗어 깊이 잠들어 있었고, 마치 고모는 커튼을 내리고서 보기 드물게 너그러운 마음이 들었는지 아무것도 하지 않고 앉아 있었다.\n\n얼마쯤 지나자, 그들은 로리가 밤이 될 때까지 깨지 않을 거라고 생각하기 시작했다. 어머니를 본 에이미의 기쁨에 찬 외침이 그를 확실하게 깨우지 않았더라면, 정말 그랬을지도 모를 일이다. 그날 그 도시 안팎에는 행복한 어린 소녀가 꽤 많았을 테지만, 어머니의 무릎에 앉아 자기가 겪은 시련을 이야기하며, 흐뭇한 미소와 다정한 손길의 형태로 위로와 보상을 받던 그 순간의 에이미가 그중 가장 행복했으리라는 것이 나의 사사로운 견해다. 두 사람은 예배실에 단둘이 있었는데, 그 용도를 설명해 드리자 어머니도 반대하지 않았다.\n\n\"오히려 난 그게 무척 마음에 드는구나, 얘야.\" 어머니는 먼지 쌓인 묵주에서 손때 묻은 작은 책으로, 그리고 상록수 화환을 두른 사랑스러운 그림으로 눈길을 옮기며 말했다. \"일이 우리를 짜증 나게 하거나 슬프게 할 때 조용히 가 있을 수 있는 곳을 마련해 두는 건 아주 좋은 방법이야. 이 인생에는 힘든 때가 참 많지만, 올바른 방식으로 도움을 청한다면 우린 언제든 그것들을 견뎌 낼 수 있단다. 우리 딸이 이걸 배우고 있는 모양이지?\"\n\n\"네, 엄마. 그리고 집에 가면, 큰 벽장 한구석에 제 책들이랑, 제가 따라 그려 본 저 그림 모사본을 둘 거예요. 여자분 얼굴은 잘 못 그렸어요. 제가 그리기엔 너무 아름다워서요. 하지만 아기는 더 잘 그렸고, 전 그게 무척 좋아요. 그분도 한때는 어린아이였다는 걸 생각하는 게 좋아요. 그러면 그분이 그렇게 멀게 느껴지지 않거든요. 그게 제게 힘이 돼요.\"\n\n에이미가 어머니 무릎에 앉아 미소 짓는 아기 예수를 가리켰을 때, 마치 부인은 그 치켜든 손에 있는 무언가를 보고 미소를 지었다. 어머니는 아무 말도 하지 않았지만, 에이미는 그 눈길을 알아채고, 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 진지하게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"이것에 대해 엄마한테 말씀드리려고 했는데, 깜빡했어요. 고모님이 오늘 이 반지를 주셨어요. 저를 부르시더니 입을 맞춰 주시고, 제 손가락에 끼워 주시면서, 제가 당신께 자랑거리라고, 저를 늘 곁에 두고 싶다고 하셨어요. 터키석이 너무 커서 빠지지 않게 잡아 주는 이 우스운 덧반지도 주셨고요. 이걸 끼고 싶어요, 엄마. 그래도 돼요?\"\n\n\"아주 예쁘긴 하구나. 하지만 넌 그런 장신구를 끼기엔 좀 어린 것 같은데, 에이미.\" 마치 부인이 그 통통한 작은 손을 바라보며 말했다. 집게손가락에는 하늘빛 보석이 박힌 반지가, 그리고 작고 앙증맞은 두 황금 손이 마주 잡은 모양으로 된 별난 덧반지가 끼워져 있었다.\n\n\"허영을 부리지 않으려고 애쓸게요.\" 에이미가 말했다. \"이게 그냥 예뻐서 좋은 건 아닌 것 같아요. 이야기 속 소녀가 무언가를 떠올리려고 팔찌를 찼던 것처럼, 저도 그렇게 끼고 싶은 거예요.\"\n\n\"마치 고모님을 떠올리려고?\" 어머니가 웃으며 물었다.\n\n\"아니요. 이기적으로 굴지 말자고 떠올리려고요.\" 에이미가 어찌나 진지하고 진실하게 그 말을 했던지, 어머니는 웃음을 멈추고 그 작은 계획을 정중히 귀담아들었다.\n\n\"전 요즘 제 '못된 점 보따리'에 대해 많이 생각해 봤는데, 이기적인 게 그중에서 제일 큰 거예요. 그러니 할 수 있다면 그걸 고치려고 열심히 애쓸 거예요. 베스는 이기적이지 않잖아요. 그래서 다들 그 애를 사랑하고, 그 애를 잃을 생각에 그렇게 마음 아파하는 거고요. 만약 제가 아팠다면 사람들이 그 절반만큼도 마음 아파하지 않을 거예요. 저는 그럴 자격도 없고요. 하지만 저도 많은 친구한테 사랑받고, 또 그리워하는 사람이 되고 싶어요. 그래서 할 수 있는 한 베스처럼 되려고 애쓸 거예요. 전 결심을 잘 잊어버리는데, 늘 곁에 뭔가 떠올리게 해 줄 게 있으면 더 잘할 수 있을 것 같아요. 이런 식으로 해 봐도 될까요?\"\n\n\"그래. 하지만 난 큰 벽장 한구석을 더 믿는단다. 반지를 끼렴, 얘야. 그리고 최선을 다하렴. 넌 잘해 낼 거야. 착해지고자 하는 진실한 소망이야말로 절반의 승리니까. 자, 이제 난 베스한테 가 봐야겠다. 기운 내렴, 우리 막내딸. 곧 너도 집으로 데려올 테니까.\"\n\n그날 저녁, 메그가 아버지에게 길손이 무사히 도착했음을 알리는 편지를 쓰는 동안, 조는 살그머니 위층 베스의 방으로 올라갔다. 어머니가 늘 있던 자리에 있는 것을 보고, 조는 잠시 서서 근심스러운 몸짓으로 손가락에 머리칼을 배배 꼬며, 머뭇거리는 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이니, 얘야?\" 마치 부인이 속내를 털어놓으라는 듯한 얼굴로 손을 내밀며 물었다.\n\n\"엄마한테 할 얘기가 있어요.\"\n\"메그에 관한 거니?\"\n\n\"어떻게 그렇게 금방 알아채셨어요! 네, 언니에 관한 건데, 별것 아닌 일이긴 하지만 제 마음이 영 편치 않아서요.\"\n\n\"베스가 자고 있으니, 목소리를 낮추고 다 얘기해 보렴. 그 모팻이라는 작자가 여기 왔던 건 아니겠지?\" 마치 부인이 다소 날카롭게 물었다.\n\n\"아뇨, 만약 왔다면 제가 코앞에서 문을 닫아 버렸을걸요.\" 조가 어머니 발치 바닥에 자리를 잡고 앉으며 말했다. \"지난여름에 메그 언니가 로런스 댁에 장갑 한 켤레를 두고 왔는데, 한 짝만 돌아왔어요. 우린 그 일을 까맣게 잊고 있었는데, 테디가 브룩 선생님이 그걸 갖고 있다고 알려 줬어요. 조끼 주머니에 넣고 다녔는데, 한번은 그게 떨어지니까 테디가 그걸 가지고 놀려 댔대요. 그러자 브룩 선생님이 메그 언니를 좋아한다고 털어놓으면서, 언니가 너무 어리고 자기는 너무 가난해서 차마 말은 못 하겠다고 했대요. 자, 이거 정말 끔찍한 상황 아니에요?\"\n\n\"넌 메그가 그 사람한테 마음이 있는 것 같니?\" 마치 부인이 근심 어린 표정으로 물었다.\n\n\"아유, 참! 전 사랑이니 그런 시시한 것에 대해선 아무것도 몰라요!\" 조가 흥미와 경멸이 우습게 뒤섞인 어조로 외쳤다. \"소설에서는 여자애들이 깜짝 놀라고 얼굴을 붉히고, 기절하고, 비쩍 마르고, 바보처럼 굴면서 그 마음을 드러내잖아요. 그런데 메그 언니는 그런 짓을 전혀 안 해요. 멀쩡한 사람처럼 잘 먹고, 잘 마시고, 잘 자요. 제가 그 사람 얘길 꺼내도 제 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보고, 테디가 연인 어쩌고 놀려 댈 때만 살짝 얼굴을 붉힐 뿐이에요. 제가 테디한테 그러지 말라고 했는데, 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 제 말을 듣질 않네요.\"\n\n\"그럼 넌 메그가 존한테 관심이 없다고 보는 거니?\"\n\n\"누구요?\" 조가 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 외쳤다.\n\n\"브룩 선생님 말이다. 난 이제 그분을 '존'이라고 부른단다. 병원에서 그렇게 부르게 됐는데, 그분도 좋아하시더구나.\"\n\n\"아이고, 이런! 엄마가 그분 편을 드실 줄 알았어요. 아빠한테 잘해 드렸으니, 엄마는 그분을 내치지 않고, 메그 언니가 원하면 결혼하게 두실 거잖아요. 비겁하긴! 아빠한테 살갑게 굴고 엄마를 도운 게 다 엄마 마음을 사려는 수작이었어.\" 조가 다시 화난 듯 머리칼을 홱 잡아당겼다.\n\n\"얘야, 그 일로 화내지 마. 어떻게 된 일인지 말해 줄 테니. 존은 로런스 씨의 부탁으로 나와 함께 갔는데, 가엾은 네 아버지께 어찌나 헌신적이던지 우리도 그분을 좋아하지 않을 수가 없었단다. 그분은 메그에 대해 더없이 솔직하고 떳떳했어. 메그를 사랑한다고 말하면서도, 청혼하기 전에 먼저 안락한 보금자리를 마련하겠다고 했으니까. 그분은 그저 메그를 사랑하고 메그를 위해 일할 수 있도록 우리 허락을 구한 것뿐이고, 할 수만 있다면 메그도 자기를 사랑하게 만들 권리를 바란 것뿐이었어. 그분은 정말 훌륭한 청년이라, 우리도 그 말을 들어주지 않을 수 없었단다. 하지만 메그가 이렇게 어린 나이에 약혼하는 것에는 동의하지 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"당연하죠. 그건 바보 같은 짓이에요! 뭔가 수상한 일이 벌어지고 있다는 걸 알았어요. 느낌이 왔거든요. 그런데 이젠 제 상상보다 더 나빠졌네요. 차라리 제가 메그 언니랑 결혼해서, 언니를 우리 식구로 안전하게 붙잡아 둘 수 있으면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n이 엉뚱한 발상에 마치 부인은 미소를 지었지만, 진지하게 말했다. \"조, 난 너를 믿는단다. 그러니 아직은 메그한테 아무 말도 하지 말아 다오. 존이 돌아오고, 두 사람이 함께 있는 걸 보면, 메그가 그분을 어떻게 생각하는지 더 잘 판단할 수 있을 테니.\"\n\n\"언니는 늘 입에 올리는 그 잘생긴 눈동자 속에서 그 사람 마음을 읽어 낼 거고, 그럼 언니는 끝장이에요. 언니는 마음이 어찌나 여린지, 누가 다정한 눈길로 쳐다보기만 해도 햇볕에 놓인 버터처럼 녹아 버릴걸요. 언니는 그 사람이 보낸 짧은 소식을 엄마 편지보다 더 열심히 읽었고, 제가 그 얘길 하니까 저를 꼬집었어요. 그리고 갈색 눈을 좋아하고, 존이라는 이름이 못생긴 이름은 아니라고 생각하죠. 그러니 언니는 그냥 사랑에 빠져 버릴 거고, 그러면 우리의 평화와 즐거움, 함께 보내던 아늑한 시간도 다 끝장이에요. 다 보여요! 두 사람은 온 집 안을 사랑에 빠져 돌아다닐 거고, 우린 그걸 피해 다녀야 할걸요. 메그 언니는 그 사람한테 푹 빠져서 더는 저한테 아무 쓸모도 없어질 거예요. 브룩 선생님은 어떻게든 재산을 그러모아서 언니를 데려가 버릴 거고, 우리 식구에 구멍을 낼 거예요. 그럼 전 가슴이 찢어질 거고, 모든 게 끔찍하게 불편해질 거예요. 아유, 정말! 우리가 다 남자로 태어났더라면 이런 골칫거리는 없었을 텐데.\"\n\n조는 낙담한 자세로 무릎에 턱을 괴고, 그 괘씸한 존을 향해 주먹을 흔들었다. 마치 부인이 한숨을 쉬자, 조가 안도하는 표정으로 고개를 들었다.\n\n\"엄마도 이게 싫으신 거죠? 정말 다행이에요. 그 사람한테 제 갈 길 가라고 하고, 메그 언니한테는 한마디도 하지 말고, 우리끼리 늘 그래 왔듯이 다 같이 행복하게 지내요.\"\n\n\"내가 한숨을 쉰 건 잘못이구나, 조. 때가 되면 너희가 모두 저마다의 가정을 꾸려 떠나는 건 자연스럽고 옳은 일이야. 하지만 난 내 딸들을 되도록 오래 곁에 두고 싶구나—\"\n있단다. 그리고 이 일이 너무 일찍 일어나서 안타깝구나. 메그는 이제 겨우 열일곱 살이고, 존이 메그에게 가정을 꾸려 줄 수 있으려면 몇 해는 더 있어야 할 테니까. 네 아버지와 나는 메그가 어떤 식으로든 자신을 매어 두지 않기로, 또 스무 살 전에는 결혼하지 않기로 합의했단다. 메그와 존이 서로 사랑한다면 기다릴 수 있고, 그렇게 함으로써 그 사랑을 시험해 볼 수 있겠지. 그 애는 양심적인 아이라, 존을 매정하게 대할까 봐 걱정하지는 않아. 예쁘고 마음씨 고운 우리 딸! 그 애에게 모든 일이 행복하게 풀리기를 바란단다.\"\n\n\"엄마는 메그가 부자랑 결혼하는 편이 더 낫다고 생각하시는 거 아니에요?\" 어머니의 목소리가 마지막 말에서 살짝 떨리는 것을 보고 조가 물었다.\n\n\"돈은 좋고 쓸모 있는 거란다, 조. 우리 딸들이 돈이 없어서 너무 쓰라리게 아쉬워하는 일도 없고, 또 너무 많은 돈에 휘둘리는 일도 없기를 바라지. 나는 존이 빚 없이 지내고 메그를 편안하게 해 줄 만큼 충분한 수입이 나오는 어떤 좋은 일자리에 든든히 자리 잡았다는 걸 알게 되면 좋겠어. 나는 우리 딸들이 굉장한 재산이나 화려한 지위, 대단한 명성을 누리기를 욕심내지는 않는단다. 만약 사랑과 미덕에 더해 지위와 돈까지 따라온다면 고맙게 받아들이고 너희의 행운을 함께 기뻐하겠지. 하지만 나는 경험으로 안단다. 날마다 양식을 벌어야 하고 약간의 궁핍이 몇 안 되는 즐거움에 더 큰 단맛을 주는, 그런 소박하고 작은 집에서도 얼마나 진정한 행복을 누릴 수 있는지를. 나는 메그가 조촐하게 시작하는 걸 봐도 만족스럽단다. 내 짐작이 틀리지 않다면 그 애는 좋은 사람의 마음을 차지하는 부자가 될 테고, 그건 재산보다 더 나은 것이니까.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀인지 알겠어요, 엄마. 저도 전적으로 동의해요. 하지만 메그 일은 좀 실망스러워요. 저는 나중에 메그를 테디랑 결혼시켜서 평생 호강하며 살게 하려고 계획을 세워 뒀거든요. 그러면 멋지지 않을까요?\" 조가 한결 밝아진 얼굴로 고개를 들며 물었다.\n\n\"걔는 메그보다 어리잖니.\" 마치 부인이 입을 열었지만, 조가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"조금밖에 안 어려요. 테디는 나이에 비해 어른스럽고 키도 크고, 마음만 먹으면 행동거지가 꽤 어른스러워질 수 있다고요. 게다가 부자에다 너그럽고 착하고, 우리 모두를 좋아하잖아요. 제 계획이 틀어진 게 정말 아쉽다니까요.\"\n\n\"로리는 메그한테는 아직 그다지 어른스럽지 못한 데다, 지금으로선 누가 기댈 만한 사람이라기엔 너무 변덕쟁이란다. 계획 같은 건 세우지 말렴, 조. 시간과 그 애들 자신의 마음이 네 친구들의 짝을 맺어 주게 두자꾸나. 그런 일에는 우리가 함부로 끼어들 수 없으니, 네가 말하는 '낭만 어쩌고 하는 헛것' 같은 걸 머릿속에 넣어 두지 않는 게 좋겠다. 자칫하면 우정을 망칠 수도 있으니까.\"\n\n\"네, 안 그럴게요. 하지만 여기 한 번 잡아당기고 저기 한 번 싹둑 자르면 곧게 펼 수 있는데, 일이 죄다 비뚤어지고 엉켜 가는 걸 보는 건 정말 싫어요. 우리가 머리에 다리미라도 이고 있으면 자라지 않을 텐데. 하지만 꽃봉오리는 장미가 되고 새끼 고양이는 고양이가 되겠죠. 더더욱 안타까운 일이라니까요!\"\n\n\"다리미랑 고양이가 어쨌다는 거야?\" 다 쓴 편지를 손에 들고 방으로 슬며시 들어오며 메그가 물었다.\n\n\"그냥 내가 한 멍청한 소리야. 난 이제 자러 갈래. 가자, 페기.\" 살아 움직이는 수수께끼 인형처럼 몸을 펴며 조가 말했다.\n\n\"아주 잘 썼구나, 글도 곱고. 내가 존에게 사랑을 전한다고 한 줄 덧붙여 주렴.\" 편지를 훑어보고 돌려주며 마치 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"엄마는 그분을 '존'이라고 부르세요?\" 천진한 눈으로 어머니의 눈을 내려다보며 메그가 미소 띤 채 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 그 사람은 우리에게 아들이나 다름없었고, 우리 모두 그를 무척 아낀단다.\" 마치 부인이 날카로운 눈빛으로 그 시선을 받으며 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다니 기뻐요, 그분은 너무 외로우니까요. 안녕히 주무세요, 사랑하는 엄마. 엄마가 여기 계시니 말로 다 못 할 만큼 마음이 놓여요.\" 메그가 조용히 대답했다.\n\n어머니가 메그에게 해 준 입맞춤은 더없이 다정했다. 그리고 메그가 방을 나간 뒤, 마치 부인은 만족과 아쉬움이 뒤섞인 목소리로 말했다. \"저 애는 아직 존을 사랑하진 않지만, 머지않아 사랑하는 법을 배우게 되겠지.\"\n\n# LAURIE MAKES MISCHIEF, AND JO MAKES PEACE.\n\n로리가 말썽을 부리고, 조가 화해를 시키다\n이튿날 조의 얼굴은 그야말로 볼만했다. 비밀이 어지간히 마음을 짓눌러서, 신비롭고 거창한 표정을 짓지 않으려고 무던히 애를 써야 했던 것이다. 메그도 그걸 눈치챘지만 굳이 캐물으려 들지는 않았다. 조를 다루는 가장 좋은 방법은 거꾸로 하는 것임을 익히 알고 있었기에, 묻지만 않으면 다 털어놓으리라고 확신했던 것이다. 그래서 침묵이 깨지지 않은 채로 이어지고, 조가 사뭇 잘난 체하는 태도를 취하자 메그는 적잖이 놀랐다. 그 태도에 약이 오른 메그는 이번에는 자기대로 위엄 있게 새침한 태도를 취하며 어머니 곁에만 붙어 있었다. 그 바람에 조는 제멋대로 지낼 수밖에 없었다. 마치 부인이 간병인 자리를 대신 맡으면서, 오랫동안 갇혀 지냈으니 이제 쉬고 운동도 하고 즐겁게 보내라고 일렀기 때문이다. 에이미가 떠나고 없으니 조의 유일한 피난처는 로리뿐이었다. 그런데 그와 어울리는 게 즐겁기는 해도, 그 무렵 조는 오히려 로리가 두려웠다. 도무지 고쳐지지 않는 짓궂은 장난꾼인 그가 비밀을 슬쩍 꾀어내려 들 것 같았기 때문이다.\n\n조의 짐작은 그대로 들어맞았다. 장난을 좋아하는 그 녀석은 뭔가 비밀이 있다는 낌새를 챘다 하면 곧장 그것을 캐내려 들었고, 그래서 조를 한껏 시달리게 했다. 그는 살살 꾀기도 하고, 매수도 하고, 비웃기도 하고, 으르기도 하고, 나무라기도 했다. 진실을 불쑥 끌어내려고 짐짓 관심 없는 척도 했다. 다 안다고 큰소리쳤다가, 다음엔 알 게 뭐냐는 듯 굴기도 했다. 그러다 끝내 끈질긴 노력 덕분에, 그게 메그와 브룩 씨에 관한 일이라는 걸 스스로 확신하게 되었다. 자기 가정교사가 자신을 믿고 털어놓지 않았다는 데에 분이 난 로리는, 그 무시당한 일에 대해 그럴듯하게 앙갚음할 방법을 짜내려고 머리를 굴렸다.\n\n그동안 메그는 그 일을 까맣게 잊은 듯, 아버지가 돌아올 준비에 푹 빠져 있었다. 그런데 별안간 무언가 변화가 찾아온 듯, 하루 이틀 동안 메그는 영 평소답지 않았다. 누가 말을 걸면 흠칫 놀라고, 누가 쳐다보면 얼굴을 붉혔으며, 무척 말이 없어지고, 겁먹은 듯 근심스러운 표정으로 바느질에만 매달려 있었다. 어머니가 어디 아프냐고 물으면 아주 괜찮다고 대답했고, 조에게는 제발 좀 내버려 두라고 사정하며 입을 다물게 했다.\n\n\"메그는 그 기운을 공기로 느끼는 거예요. 사랑 말이에요. 그것도 아주 빠르게 빠져들고 있어요. 증상이란 증상은 거의 다 나타났다고요. 안절부절못하고 까칠해지고, 잘 먹지도 않고, 잠도 못 자고, 구석에서 시무룩하게 있고요. 그 사람이 준 노래를 흥얼거리는 걸 잡아낸 적도 있어요. 한번은 엄마처럼 '존'이라고 부르더니, 양귀비처럼 새빨개졌다니까요. 대체 어쩌면 좋아요?\" 조가 아무리 과격한 수단이라도 마다하지 않을 기세로 말했다.\n\n\"기다리는 것밖에 없단다. 그냥 내버려 두고, 다정하고 끈기 있게 대하렴. 아버지가 오시면 모든 일이 정리될 거야.\" 어머니가 대답했다.\n\n\"메그 언니, 언니한테 온 쪽지야. 봉인까지 다 돼 있어. 이상하다! 테디는 내 건 한 번도 봉한 적이 없는데.\" 이튿날, 그 작은 우체국의 내용물을 나눠 주며 조가 말했다.\n\n마치 부인과 조가 저마다 제 일에 푹 빠져 있는데, 메그 쪽에서 무슨 소리가 나서 고개를 들어 보니, 메그가 겁에 질린 얼굴로 쪽지를 뚫어져라 보고 있었다.\n\n\"얘야, 무슨 일이니?\" 어머니가 메그에게 달려가며 외쳤고, 그사이 조는 일을 저지른 그 종이를 빼앗으려 했다.\n\n\"전부 오해였어요. 그 사람은 이걸 보낸 적이 없대요. 아, 조, 어떻게 이럴 수가 있어?\" 메그는 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고, 가슴이 완전히 찢어진 듯 울었다.\n\n\"나라고! 난 아무것도 안 했어! 대체 무슨 소릴 하는 거야?\" 영문을 모른 조가 외쳤다.\n\n메그의 순한 두 눈에 노여움이 타오르더니, 호주머니에서 구겨진 쪽지를 꺼내 조에게 던지며 원망스럽게 말했다.\n\n\"네가 쓰고, 그 못된 녀석이 거든 거잖아. 어쩜 우리 둘한테 이렇게 무례하고, 못되고, 잔인할 수가 있어?\"\n\n조는 그 말이 거의 귀에 들어오지 않았다. 어머니와 함께 그 쪽지를 읽고 있었기 때문인데, 거기엔 묘한 필체로 이렇게 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"내 가장 사랑하는 마거릿,\n더는 내 마음을 억누를 수가 없어, 돌아가기 전에 내 운명을 알아야겠소. 아직 당신 부모님께 말씀드릴 엄두는 나지 않지만, 우리가 서로 사모하고 있다는 걸 아시면 허락해 주시리라 생각하오. 로런스 씨가 내게 좋은 자리를 마련해 주실 테니, 그러면, 사랑하는 그대여, 당신이 나를 행복하게 해 주오. 부디 아직은 가족에게 아무 말도 말고, 로리를 통해 희망의 한 마디만 전해 주기를 간청하오.\n\n당신에게 헌신하는 존이.\"\n\"아, 그 쪼끄만 악당! 엄마와 한 약속을 지킨 대가를 이런 식으로 갚으려고 했군. 내가 단단히 혼쭐을 내서, 이리로 끌고 와 용서를 빌게 만들 거야.\" 당장이라도 응징에 나설 기세로 조가 외쳤다. 그러나 어머니가 좀처럼 짓지 않는 표정으로 조를 붙들며 말했다.\n\n\"멈추렴, 조. 먼저 네 결백부터 밝혀야지. 넌 워낙 장난을 많이 쳐 왔으니, 이번 일에도 네가 한몫 거든 게 아닐까 걱정스럽구나.\"\n\n\"맹세코, 엄마, 전 아니에요! 그 쪽지는 처음 봤고, 이 일에 대해 아무것도 몰라요, 정말이라니까요!\" 조가 어찌나 진지하게 말하던지 두 사람은 그 말을 믿었다. \"내가 정말 끼어들었다면 이것보다는 훨씬 잘 썼을 거고, 말이 되는 쪽지를 썼겠지. 브룩 씨가 이런 시답잖은 글을 쓸 사람이 아니라는 건 진작 알았어야지.\" 그러면서 조는 그 종이를 경멸하듯 내던졌다.\n\n\"그분 글씨랑 비슷한데.\" 메그가 손에 든 쪽지와 견주어 보며 떨리는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"아, 메그, 답장한 건 아니지?\" 마치 부인이 다급히 외쳤다.\n\n\"했어요!\" 메그는 부끄러움에 못 이겨 다시 얼굴을 가렸다.\n\n\"이거 큰일 났네! 제발 그 못된 녀석을 데려와 설명하게 하고 따끔하게 혼내게 해 줘요. 그 녀석을 붙잡기 전엔 가만히 못 있겠어.\" 조가 다시 문 쪽으로 향했다.\n\n\"쉿! 이 일은 내가 처리하마. 생각보다 더 고약하구나. 마거릿, 자초지종을 다 말해 보렴.\" 마치 부인이 메그 곁에 앉으며 분부했다. 그러면서도 조가 날아가 버리기라도 할까 봐 조를 붙든 채였다.\n\n\"첫 번째 편지는 로리한테서 받았어요. 걔는 이 일에 대해 아무것도 모르는 것 같았고요.\" 메그가 고개도 들지 못한 채 입을 열었다. \"처음엔 걱정스러워서 엄마한테 말씀드리려고 했어요. 그러다 엄마가 브룩 씨를 좋게 보시는 게 떠올라서, 며칠쯤 제 작은 비밀로 간직해도 괜찮으시겠거니 했어요. 제가 어리석어서, 아무도 모른다고 생각하는 게 좋았던 거예요. 뭐라고 답할지 정하는 동안, 이런 일을 겪는 책 속 소녀들이 된 기분이었어요. 용서해 주세요, 엄마, 어리석은 짓의 대가를 지금 치르고 있어요. 이제 다시는 그분 얼굴을 못 볼 거예요.\"\n\n\"그 사람에게 뭐라고 했니?\" 마치 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"그냥 저는 아직 그런 일을 하기엔 너무 어리고, 엄마한테 비밀을 두고 싶지 않으니 아버지께 말씀드려야 한다고만 했어요. 친절히 대해 주셔서 정말 감사하고, 한동안은 친구로만 지내고 싶을 뿐 그 이상은 아니라고요.\"\n\n마치 부인은 흡족하다는 듯 미소 지었고, 조는 손뼉을 치며 웃음 섞인 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"언니 거의 신중함의 본보기였던 캐럴라인 퍼시급이네! 더 말해 봐, 메그 언니. 그랬더니 그 사람이 뭐래?\"\n\n\"완전히 다른 투로 답장이 왔어. 자긴 연애편지 같은 건 절대 보낸 적이 없고, 장난기 많은 우리 동생 조가 우리 이름을 그렇게 함부로 가져다 쓴 게 너무 안타깝다는 거야. 정말 친절하고 정중하긴 한데, 나로선 이게 얼마나 끔찍한 일인지 생각해 봐!\"\n\n메그는 절망 그 자체인 얼굴로 어머니에게 기댔고, 조는 로리를 욕하며 방 안을 쿵쿵 걸어 다녔다. 그러다 별안간 걸음을 멈추고는 두 쪽지를 집어 들어 찬찬히 살펴보더니 단호하게 말했다. \"브룩 씨는 이 두 편지를 다 본 적도 없을걸. 테디가 둘 다 쓴 거야. 그리고 내가 비밀을 안 알려 줬다고, 나한테 으스대려고 언니 답장을 가지고 있는 거라고.\"\n\n\"비밀 같은 건 갖지 마, 조. 엄마한테 털어놓고 괜한 곤란에 빠지지 마. 나도 그랬어야 했어.\" 메그가 타이르듯 말했다.\n\n\"걱정 마, 언니! 엄마가 나한테 다 말씀해 주셨거든.\"\n\n\"그만하렴, 조. 네가 가서 로리를 데려오는 동안 내가 메그를 다독이마. 이 일을 바닥까지 샅샅이 가려내서, 이런 장난을 당장 끝낼 거야.\"\n\n조가 달려 나가자, 마치 부인은 브룩 씨의 진짜 마음을 메그에게 살며시 일러 주었다. \"자, 얘야, 네 마음은 어떠니? 그가 너를 위해 가정을 꾸릴 수 있을 때까지 기다릴 만큼 그를 사랑하니, 아니면 지금으로선 아무 데도 매이지 않고 자유롭게 지내고 싶니?\"\n\n\"너무 놀라고 마음을 졸여서, 한동안은, 아니 어쩌면 영영, 연인이니 뭐니 하는 것과는 엮이고 싶지 않아요.\" 메그가 토라진 듯 대답했다. \"존이 이 어처구니없는 일에 대해 정말 아무것도 모른다면, 그분께는 말하지 마세요. 그리고 조랑 로리도 입 다물게 해 주세요. 속고, 시달리고, 바보 취급당하는 건 싫어요. 정말 너무해요!\"\n\n평소 온화하던 메그의 성미가 들끓고, 이 짓궂은 장난에 자존심이 상했음을 알아챈 마치 부인은, 철저히 비밀을 지키고 앞으로 매사 신중히 하겠노라 약속하며 메그를 달랬다. 현관에서 로리의 발소리가 들리는 순간, 메그는 서재로 도망쳐 버렸고, 마치 부인은 혼자서 그 죄인을 맞이했다. 조는 로리가 안 올까 봐 왜 부르는지 말하지 않았지만, 로리는 마치 부인의 얼굴을 보는 순간 곧바로 알아챘고, 모자를 빙빙 돌리며 죄지은 기색으로 서 있었는데, 그 모습만으로도 이미 유죄였다. 조는 물러가라는 말을 들었지만, 죄수가 달아나기라도 할까 봐 다소 걱정스러워, 보초처럼 현관을 왔다 갔다 하기로 했다. 응접실에서 들려오는 목소리가 반 시간 동안 높아졌다 낮아졌다 했지만, 그 면담 중에 무슨 일이 오갔는지는 자매들도 끝내 알지 못했다.\n\n자매들이 안으로 불려 들어갔을 때, 로리는 어머니 곁에 어찌나 뉘우치는 얼굴로 서 있었던지, 조는 그 자리에서 그를 용서해 버렸다. 다만 그 사실을 드러내는 게 현명하다고는 여기지 않았다. 메그는 그의 겸허한 사과를 받아들였고, 브룩이 그 장난에 대해 전혀 모른다는 다짐을 듣고 크게 마음이 놓였다.\n\n\"죽는 날까지 절대 그한테 말 안 할게. 사나운 말들이 끌어당겨도 입 밖에 안 낼 거야. 그러니 날 용서해 줘, 메그. 내가 얼마나 철저히 미안해하는지 보여 줄 수만 있다면 뭐든 할게.\" 로리가 잔뜩 부끄러워하는 기색으로 덧붙였다.\n\n\"노력해 볼게. 하지만 정말이지 신사답지 못한 짓이었어. 네가 그렇게 음흉하고 심술궂을 줄은 몰랐어, 로리.\" 메그가 처녀다운 당혹감을 짐짓 엄숙하게 나무라는 태도 뒤에 감추려 애쓰며 대답했다.\n\n\"정말이지 끔찍한 짓이었어. 한 달쯤은 나한테 말도 걸지 말아야 마땅하지만, 그래도 말은 걸어 줄 거지, 응?\" 로리가 거부할 수 없을 만큼 사람을 홀리는 어조로 말하며 두 손을 모아 어찌나 애원하는 시늉을 하던지, 그 망측한 짓에도 불구하고 도저히 그를 향해 눈살을 찌푸릴 수 없었다. 메그는 그를 용서했고, 로리가 온갖 고행으로 자신의 죄를 속죄하고 상처 입은 아가씨 앞에서 벌레처럼 납작 엎드리겠노라고 선언하는 것을 듣자, 마치 부인도 짐짓 근엄함을 지키려 애썼지만 굳었던 표정이 그만 풀어지고 말았다.\n\n그러는 동안 조는 한 발 물러나, 그에게 마음을 모질게 먹으려 애썼지만, 결국 얼굴을 새침하게 굳혀 완전한 못마땅함의 표정을 짓는 데만 성공할 뿐이었다. 로리가 한두 번 그쪽을 쳐다봤지만, 조가 누그러질 기미를 도통 보이지 않자 서운한 마음이 들었던지, 다른 사람들과 이야기가 끝날 때까지 조에게 등을 돌리고 있다가, 마지막에 정중히 허리를 굽혀 인사하고는 한마디도 없이 가 버렸다.\n\n그가 가 버리자마자 조는 좀 더 너그럽게 굴걸 그랬다고 후회했다. 그리고 메그와 어머니가 위층으로 올라가자, 조는 외로워졌고 테디가 그리워졌다. 한참 마음을 다잡아 보다가 결국 충동에 굴복한 조는, 돌려줄 책 한 권을 들고 큰 집으로 건너갔다.\n\n\"로런스 씨 계세요?\" 계단을 내려오는 하녀에게 조가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 아가씨. 하지만 지금은 뵙기 어려우실 것 같은데요.\"\n\n\"왜요? 편찮으세요?\"\n\n\"아유, 아니에요, 아가씨. 그런데 로리 도련님이 무슨 일로 잔뜩 심통이 나서 한바탕 소동을 벌이는 바람에 어르신이 단단히 언짢으셔서요. 그래서 저는 곁에 얼씬도 못 하고 있어요.\"\n\n\"로리는 어디 있는데요?\"\n\n\"방에 틀어박혀서는, 제가 아무리 두드려도 대답을 안 하세요. 저녁이 다 차려졌는데 먹을 사람이 없으니, 이걸 어쩌면 좋을지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"제가 가서 무슨 일인지 볼게요. 저는 두 분 다 안 무서우니까요.\"\n\n조는 위층으로 올라가 로리의 작은 서재 문을 똑똑 세게 두드렸다.\n\n\"그만두지 못해, 안 그러면 문을 열고 가만 안 둘 테니까!\" 그 도련님이 으름장 놓는 투로 소리쳤다.\n\n조는 곧바로 다시 두드렸다. 문이 벌컥 열렸고, 로리가 놀라 정신을 차리기도 전에 조가 안으로 뛰어들었다. 그가 정말로 화가 나 있다는 걸 본 조는, 그를 다루는 법을 잘 아는지라, 뉘우치는 표정을 지으며 멋들어지게 무릎을 꿇고는 다소곳이 말했다. \"내가 너무 못되게 군 거 용서해 줘. 화해하러 왔는데, 화해 전엔 못 가.\"\n\n\"됐어. 일어나, 바보같이 굴지 마, 조.\" 그녀의 청에 돌아온 건 무뚝뚝한 대답이었다.\n\n\"고마워, 일어날게. 무슨 일인지 물어봐도 돼? 마음이 영 편치 않아 보여서.\"\n\n\"누가 날 마구 흔들어 댔어, 난 그런 거 못 참아!\" 로리가 분에 차 으르렁거렸다.\n\n\"누가?\" 조가 다그쳤다.\n\n\"할아버지가. 다른 사람이었으면 내가 가만……\" 분이 난 그 청년은 오른팔을 힘차게 휘두르는 동작으로 말을 맺었다.\n\n\"그게 뭐 대수라고. 나도 너 자주 흔들어 대는데, 넌 신경도 안 쓰잖아.\" 조가 달래듯 말했다.\n\n\"피! 넌 여자애니까 그건 장난이지. 하지만 남자가 날 흔드는 건 절대 못 참아.\"\n\n\"지금처럼 먹구름 같은 얼굴을 하고 있으면 아무도 너 흔들 엄두는 안 낼 것 같은데. 왜 그런 대접을 받은 거야?\"\n\n\"그냥 너희 어머니가 날 왜 부르셨는지 말하지 않았다고 그런 거야. 말 안 하겠다고 약속했으니, 당연히 약속을 깰 순 없었지.\"\n\n\"다른 방법으로 할아버지를 납득시킬 순 없었어?\"\n\n\"없었어. 할아버지는 진실을, 오로지 진실만을, 남김없이 다 들으려 하셨거든. 메그를 끌어들이지 않고 말할 수 있었다면, 내 쪽 사정은 얘기했을 거야. 근데 그럴 수가 없으니 입을 다물고 야단을 견뎠지. 그러다 할아버지가 내 멱살을 잡으시는 거야. 그때 화가 치밀어서, 나도 모르게 무슨 짓을 할까 봐 뛰쳐나와 버렸어.\"\n\n\"잘한 일은 아니지만, 할아버지도 후회하고 계실 거야, 분명히. 그러니까 내려가서 화해해. 내가 도와줄게.\"\n\n\"내가 그러면 사람이 아니지! 그깟 장난 좀 쳤다고 아무한테나 잔소리 듣고 두들겨 맞을 순 없어. 메그 일은 정말 미안해서 사내답게 용서를 빌었어. 하지만 잘못한 것도 없는데 또 그러진 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"할아버지는 그걸 모르셨잖아.\"\n\n\"날 믿어 주셔야지, 아기 다루듯 하면 안 되는 거잖아. 소용없어, 조. 할아버지는 내가 내 한 몸 건사할 수 있고, 누구 치맛자락 붙잡고 매달릴 필요 없다는 걸 아셔야 해.\"\n\n\"너희 정말 둘 다 고추처럼 맵구나!\" 조가 한숨을 쉬었다. \"이 일을 어떻게 마무리할 생각인데?\"\n\n\"음, 할아버지가 먼저 사과하셔야지. 그리고 이 소동이 뭣 때문인지 말씀드릴 수 없다고 할 때 그 말을 믿어 주셔야 하고.\"\n\n\"세상에! 할아버지가 그러실 리 없잖아.\"\n\n\"그럼 그러실 때까진 안 내려가.\"\n\n\"이봐, 테디, 좀 분별 있게 굴어. 그냥 넘겨, 내가 말할 수 있는 데까진 설명해 줄게. 넌 여기 계속 있을 순 없잖아, 그러니 신파극처럼 굴어 봐야 무슨 소용이야?\"\n\n\"어차피 여기 오래 있을 생각도 없어. 슬쩍 빠져나가 어디로든 여행이나 떠날 거야. 그럼 할아버지가 날 찾다가 금방 누그러지시겠지.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지. 하지만 떠나서 할아버지 속을 태우면 안 돼.\"\n\n\"설교는 그만해. 워싱턴에 가서 브룩을 만날 거야. 거긴 신나잖아. 이 골치 아픈 일들도 다 겪었으니, 좀 즐겨야지.\"\n\n\"얼마나 재미있을까! 나도 같이 달아날 수 있으면 좋겠다.\" 조가 멘토 역할은 까맣게 잊은 채, 수도의 군대 생활을 생생히 그려 보며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 같이 가자! 왜 안 돼? 넌 가서 아버지를 깜짝 놀라게 해 드리고, 난 브룩 영감을 흔들어 깨우는 거지. 정말 멋진 장난이 될 거야. 하자, 조. 우린 잘 있다는 편지 한 통 남기고 당장 떠나는 거야. 돈은 내가 충분히 있어. 너한테도 좋을 거고, 아버지한테 가는 거니까 나쁠 것도 없잖아.\"\n\n한순간 조는 그러자고 할 듯한 표정이었다. 무모한 계획이긴 해도, 딱 조의 마음에 들었던 것이다. 조는 보살핌과 갇힌 생활에 진력이 났고 변화를 갈망하던 차였다. 게다가 아버지 생각이, 야영지와 병원, 자유와 즐거움이라는 새로운 매력과 솔깃하게 뒤섞였다. 창문 쪽으로 아련히 향하던 조의 두 눈이 반짝였지만, 그 시선이 맞은편 낡은 집에 가닿자, 조는 서글픈 결심으로 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"내가 남자애였다면 같이 달아나서 신나게 보냈을 거야. 하지만 난 처량한 여자애니까, 얌전히 굴고 집에 붙어 있어야지. 날 부추기지 마, 테디, 그건 미친 계획이야.\"\n\n\"그게 바로 재미있는 거지.\" 고집이 발동한 데다 어떻게든 울타리를 박차고 나가고 싶어 안달이 난 로리가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"입 다물어!\" 조가 두 귀를 막으며 외쳤다. \"'얌전하고 새침하게' 사는 게 내 운명이야, 그러니 그냥 마음을 정하는 편이 낫겠지. 난 여기 교훈을 늘어놓으러 온 거지, 생각만 해도 가슴이 뛰는 그런 얘기나 들으러 온 게 아니라고.\"\n\n\"메그라면 그런 제안에 찬물을 끼얹을 줄 알았지만, 넌 좀 더 배짱이 있는 줄 알았는데.\" 로리가 슬쩍 부추기듯 운을 뗐다.\n\n\"못된 녀석, 조용히 좀 해! 앉아서 네 죄나 반성해, 내 죄까지 보태게 만들지 말고. 내가 너희 할아버지한테 흔든 일 사과하시게 만들면, 달아나려는 거 그만둘래?\" 조가 진지하게 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 하지만 넌 못 할걸.\" 로리가 대답했다. 사실 그도 '화해하고' 싶었지만, 짓밟힌 자존심부터 달래야 한다고 느꼈던 것이다.\n\n\"어린 녀석을 다룰 수 있으면, 늙은 어른도 다룰 수 있지.\" 조는 중얼거리며 걸어 나갔고, 로리는 두 손으로 머리를 괸 채 철도 지도 위로 몸을 숙이고 있었다.\n\n\"들어오게!\" 조가 문을 똑똑 두드리자, 로런스 씨의 퉁명스러운 목소리가 어느 때보다 퉁명스럽게 울렸다.\n\n\"저예요, 어르신. 책 돌려드리러 왔어요.\" 조가 들어서며 상냥하게 말했다.\n\n\"더 볼 텐가?\" 어르신이 험상궂고 언짢은 얼굴로, 그러나 그걸 안 드러내려 애쓰며 물었다.\n\n\"네, 그러고 싶어요. 새뮤얼 영감 이야기가 어찌나 마음에 들던지, 두 번째 권도 읽어 보려고요.\" 조가 대답했다. 로런스 씨가 권한 그 활기찬 책, 보즈웰이 쓴 『존슨전』을 두 번째로 받아 들임으로써 그의 기분을 풀어 주려는 속셈이었다.\n\n덥수룩한 눈썹이 조금 펴지면서, 그가 존슨 관련 서적이 꽂힌 서가 쪽으로 사다리를 굴려 갔다. 조는 폴짝 올라가 맨 위 계단에 걸터앉아, 제 책을 찾는 척하면서도, 사실은 이 방문의 위험천만한 목적을 어떻게 꺼내야 가장 좋을지 궁리하고 있었다. 로런스 씨는 조의 머릿속에서 뭔가 꿍꿍이가 무르익고 있음을 눈치챈 듯했다. 방 안을 몇 차례 성큼성큼 거닐더니, 조를 홱 돌아보며 어찌나 불쑥 말을 꺼내던지, 『라셀라스』가 바닥에 엎어지며 나뒹굴 정도였다.\n\n\"그 녀석이 대체 무슨 짓을 한 거냐? 감쌀 생각 말고. 집에 돌아왔을 때 행동거지를 보니, 무슨 못된 짓을 저지른 게 분명해. 그 녀석한테선 한마디도 들을 수가 없어. 진실을 흔들어서라도 끄집어내겠다고 으름장을 놨더니, 위층으로 뛰어 올라가 제 방에 틀어박혀 버렸다.\"\n\n\"잘못을 하긴 했어요. 하지만 저희가 용서했고, 다들 누구에게도 한마디 안 하기로 약속했거든요.\" 조가 마지못해 입을 뗐다.\n\n\"그건 안 될 말이지. 그 녀석이 너희 마음 여린 처녀들이 한 약속 뒤에 숨게 둘 순 없어. 잘못한 게 있다면, 고백하고, 용서를 빌고, 벌을 받아야지. 어서 말해 보거라, 조. 난 어둠 속에 갇혀 있진 않을 테니.\"\n\n로런스 씨가 어찌나 무섭게 보이고 또 날카롭게 말하던지, 조는 할 수만 있다면 기꺼이 달아났을 것이다. 하지만 조는 사다리 높은 곳에 올라앉아 있었고, 그는 길목을 지키는 사자처럼 사다리 발치에 버티고 서 있었으니, 조는 남아서 정면으로 맞서는 수밖에 없었다.\n\n\"정말이지, 어르신, 말씀드릴 수가 없어요. 엄마가 그러지 말라고 하셨거든요. 로리는 고백도 하고, 용서도 빌었고, 벌도 충분히 받았어요. 저희가 입을 다무는 건 로리를 감싸려는 게 아니라 다른 사람 때문이에요. 어르신이 끼어드시면 일이 더 커질 거예요. 부디 그러지 마세요. 일부는 제 잘못이기도 했지만, 이제 다 잘 풀렸으니까요. 그러니 이 일은 잊고, 『램블러』 같은 거나, 뭔가 즐거운 얘기나 해요.\"\n\n\"『램블러』는 집어치워! 내려와서 내게 약속하거라. 그 덜렁대는 내 손자 녀석이 배은망덕하거나 무례한 짓은 하지 않았다고 말이다. 그 모든 친절을 베풀어 줬는데도 그런 짓을 했다면, 내 이 손으로 직접 그놈을 매질할 테니.\"\n\n그 으름장은 무시무시하게 들렸지만 조를 겁먹게 하진 못했다. 성마른 이 어르신이 말로는 무어라 하든, 결코 손자에게 손가락 하나 까딱하지 않으리라는 걸 조는 알고 있었으니까. 조는 순순히 내려와, 메그를 들춰내지도 진실을 저버리지도 않는 선에서 최대한 그 장난을 대수롭지 않은 일로 둘러댔다.\n\n\"흠……허……그래, 그 녀석이 고집 때문이 아니라 약속을 지키느라 입을 다문 거라면, 용서하마. 워낙 고집불통이라 다루기 힘든 녀석이지.\" 로런스 씨가 머리카락을 어찌나 비벼 대던지 폭풍 속에라도 다녀온 듯했고, 안도하는 기색으로 이마의 주름을 펴며 말했다.\n\n\"저도 그래요. 하지만 다정한 말 한마디면 임금님의 말과 임금님의 신하가 다 나서도 못 하는 걸 해낼 수 있죠.\" 조가 친구를 위해 좋은 말 한마디 거들려고 애쓰며 말했다. 하나의 곤경에서 빠져나오나 싶으면 또 다른 곤경에 빠지는 친구였다.\n\n\"내가 그 녀석한테 다정하지 않다고 생각하는 게냐, 응?\" 날카로운 대꾸가 돌아왔다.\n\n\"아유, 아니에요, 어르신. 어르신은 가끔 너무 다정하실 때도 있는걸요. 그러다 그 애가 인내심을 시험하면, 아주 조금 성급해지시는 거죠. 안 그러세요?\"\n\n조는 이번엔 끝장을 보겠다고 작정하고, 그 대담한 말을 내뱉고서 속으로는 조금 떨면서도 짐짓 아주 태연한 척했다. 그런데 어르신은, 조의 크나큰 안도와 놀라움 속에, 안경을 탁자 위에 달그락 내던지며 솔직하게 외칠 뿐이었다.\n\n\"네 말이 맞다, 얘야, 그래! 난 그 녀석을 사랑하지만, 그놈은 참다 참다 못 견딜 만큼 내 인내심을 시험하지. 이대로 계속 가다간 어떻게 끝날지 나도 모르겠구나.\"\n\n\"제가 말씀드릴게요. 로리는 달아날 거예요.\" 조는 그 말을 내뱉는 순간 후회했다. 로리가 지나친 속박은 견디지 못한다는 걸 경고해서, 어르신이 그 애에게 좀 더 너그러워지기를 바랐을 뿐이었는데.\n\n로런스 씨의 불그레한 얼굴이 갑자기 변하더니, 그는 자리에 앉아 탁자 위에 걸린 잘생긴 남자의 초상화를 근심스러운 눈길로 바라보았다. 그건 로리의 아버지였는데, 그는 젊은 시절 정말로 집을 뛰쳐나가, 위압적인 노인의 뜻을 거슬러 결혼했던 것이다. 조는 어르신이 지난 일을 떠올리며 후회하는 거라고 짐작했고, 괜한 말을 했다고 후회했다.\n\n\"로리는 어지간히 속이 상하지 않는 한 그러진 않을 거예요. 공부에 지칠 때나 가끔 으름장처럼 그러는 거죠. 저도 그러고 싶다는 생각을 자주 해요, 특히 머리를 자른 뒤로요. 그러니 저희가 사라지면, 사내아이 둘을 찾는다고 광고를 내시고, 인도로 가는 배들 사이를 뒤져 보시면 될 거예요.\"\n\n조는 말하면서 웃었고, 로런스 씨도 안도하는 기색이었다. 이 모든 걸 농담으로 받아들인 게 분명했다.\n\n\"이런 발칙한 것, 어디서 감히 그런 식으로 말하느냐? 나에 대한 존경심이며, 제대로 받은 교육은 다 어디 갔어? 사내아이들이고 계집아이들이고 다 어쩌면 좋을꼬! 어찌나 사람 애를 먹이는지. 그래도 너희가 없으면 우린 못 살지.\" 어르신이 다정하게 조의 두 볼을 꼬집으며 말했다. \"가서 그 녀석을 저녁 먹으러 데려오너라. 다 괜찮다고 전하고, 제 할아비 앞에서 비극 배우처럼 처량한 척하지 말라고 일러라. 그런 꼴은 못 봐주겠으니.\"\n\n\"안 올 거예요, 어르신. 자기가 말할 수 없다고 했을 때 어르신이 안 믿어 주셔서 속상해하고 있거든요. 흔드신 게 그 애 마음을 단단히 상하게 한 것 같아요.\"\n\n조가 애처로운 표정을 지어 보이려 했지만 실패한 게 분명했다. 로런스 씨가 웃기 시작했고, 조는 오늘의 승리를 거뒀음을 알았다.\n\n\"그거 안됐구나. 그 녀석이 날 안 흔들어 준 걸 고마워해야 할 판이로군. 대체 그놈은 뭘 바라는 게야?\" 어르신은 자신의 성마름이 조금은 부끄러운 기색이었다.\n\n\"제가 어르신이라면, 사과 편지를 써 보내겠어요. 그 애는 사과를 받기 전엔 안 내려온다고 하고, 워싱턴이 어쩌고 하면서 어처구니없게 굴고 있거든요. 격식 차린 사과 편지를 받으면 자기가 얼마나 어리석은지 깨닫고, 아주 고분고분하게 내려올 거예요. 한번 해 보세요. 그 앤 장난을 좋아하니까, 이 방법이 말로 하는 것보다 나아요. 제가 위층으로 가져가서 그 애한테 도리를 가르치고 올게요.\"\n\n로런스 씨는 조를 날카롭게 한번 쳐다보고는 안경을 쓰며 천천히 말했다. \"넌 참 약은 고양이로구나. 하지만 너랑 베스한테라면 휘둘려도 싫지 않지. 자, 종이 한 장 다오, 이 어처구니없는 일을 끝내자꾸나.\"\n\n그 편지는 한 신사가 다른 신사에게 깊은 모욕을 준 뒤에나 쓸 법한 격식 있는 말투로 쓰였다. 조는 로런스 씨의 벗어진 정수리에 입맞춤을 떨구고는, 위층으로 달려가 그 사과 편지를 로리의 문틈으로 밀어 넣었다. 그러면서 열쇠 구멍 너머로, 고분고분하고 예의 바르게 굴라고, 그리고 그 밖에 몇 가지 듣기 좋되 도무지 될 성싶지 않은 것들을 당부했다. 문이 또 잠겨 있는 걸 알고는, 편지가 알아서 제 몫을 하도록 두고 조용히 떠나려는데, 그 도련님이 난간을 타고 미끄러져 내려와 계단 밑에서 기다리고 있다가, 더없이 점잖은 표정으로 말했다. \"넌 정말 좋은 녀석이야, 조! 너도 한바탕 야단맞았어?\" 그가 웃으며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"아니. 어르신은 전체적으로 꽤 부드러우셨어.\"\n\n\"아! 난 사방에서 다 당했어. 너까지 저쪽에서 날 내쳤으니, 그냥 지옥에라도 갈 기분이었다고.\" 그가 변명하듯 운을 뗐다.\n\n\"그런 식으로 말하지 마. 새 마음으로 다시 시작하는 거야, 테디, 내 아들아.\"\n\n\"새 마음으로 계속 다시 시작하긴 하는데, 자꾸 망쳐 버려. 옛날에 공책 망치던 것처럼 말이야. 시작은 어찌나 많이 하는지, 끝은 영영 안 날 거야.\" 그가 풀이 죽어 말했다.\n\n\"가서 저녁이나 먹어. 먹고 나면 기분이 나아질 거야. 남자들은 배가 고프면 늘 투덜대거든.\" 그 말을 남기고 조는 휙 정문 밖으로 나가 버렸다.\n\n\"내 '종족'에게 붙은 '딱지'로군.\" 로리는 에이미의 말을 흉내 내어 대답하고는, 할아버지와 함께 얌전히 굴욕의 만찬을 들러 갔다. 할아버지는 그날 남은 시간 내내 성인군자처럼 너그러운 성미에, 더할 나위 없이 깍듯한 태도였다.\n\n다들 일이 마무리되고 작은 먹구름도 걷혔다고 여겼다. 하지만 화근은 이미 뿌려진 뒤였다. 다른 사람들은 다 잊었어도 메그는 잊지 않았던 것이다. 그녀는 어떤 사람에 대해 한마디도 입에 올리지 않았지만, 속으로는 그를 꽤나 자주 떠올렸고, 그 어느 때보다 더 많은 꿈을 꾸었다. 한번은 조가 우표를 찾으려고 언니의 책상을 뒤지다가 \"존 브룩 부인\"이라는 글자가 잔뜩 끄적여진 종이쪽지를 발견하고는, 비극 배우처럼 신음을 내뱉으며 그것을 불 속에 던져 버렸다. 로리의 장난이 자신에게는 불행한 날을 앞당겨 버렸다는 기분이 들었던 것이다.\n즐거운 초원\n\n# PLEASANT MEADOWS.\n\n폭풍 뒤의 햇살 같은 것이 바로 그 뒤로 이어진 평온한 몇 주였다. 환자들은 빠르게 회복했고, 마치 씨는 새해 초에 돌아올 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다. 머지않아 베스는 하루 종일 서재 소파에 누워 지낼 수 있게 되었는데, 처음엔 그토록 아끼는 고양이들과 놀며 시간을 보내다가, 차츰 한참 밀려 있던 인형 옷 바느질로 마음을 달랬다. 한때 그토록 활발했던 팔다리가 어찌나 뻣뻣하고 힘이 없던지, 조는 날마다 베스를 튼튼한 두 팔로 안아 집 안을 돌며 바람을 쐬어 주었다. 메그는 \"우리 귀염둥이\"를 위해 정성스러운 음식을 만드느라 새하얀 손을 그을리고 데어 가면서도 기꺼이 그렇게 했다. 한편 그 반지의 충직한 종, 에이미는 자기 보물들을 언니 동생들이 받아 주는 대로 한껏 나눠 주는 것으로 자신의 귀환을 자축했다.\n\n크리스마스가 다가오면서, 으레 그렇듯 비밀스러운 분위기가 집 안을 감돌기 시작했다. 조는 이 유난히 즐거운 크리스마스를 기리겠다며 도무지 말도 안 되거나 거창하게 터무니없는 의식들을 제안해 식구들을 곧잘 웃음바다로 만들었다. 로리도 못지않게 비현실적이어서, 제멋대로 하게 뒀다면 모닥불에 폭죽에 개선문까지 동원했을 것이다. 여러 번의 옥신각신과 핀잔 끝에, 이 야심만만한 두 사람은 완전히 진압된 것으로 여겨졌고, 풀 죽은 얼굴로 돌아다녔다. 하지만 둘이 함께 있을 때면 터져 나오는 폭소가 그 시무룩한 얼굴이 거짓임을 여실히 드러냈다.\n\n유난히 포근한 날씨가 며칠 이어지더니, 마침 멋진 크리스마스 날을 열어 주었다. 해나는 유난히 좋은 날이 되리라고 \"뼛속으로 느꼈는데\", 과연 그녀는 진짜 예언자임이 드러났다. 모든 사람과 모든 일이 큰 성공을 거두기로 작정한 듯했으니 말이다. 우선 마치 씨가 곧 식구들과 함께하게 될 거라는 편지를 보내왔다. 그리고 베스가 그날 아침 유난히 몸 상태가 좋아서, 어머니가 준 선물인 부드러운 진홍색 메리노 모직 실내복을 입고는, 의기양양하게 창가로 옮겨져 조와 로리의 선물을 보게 되었다. '진압 불가능한 두 사람'은 그 이름값을 하려고 최선을 다했으니, 요정들처럼 밤새 작업해서 우스꽝스러운 깜짝 선물을 마련해 둔 것이다. 정원에는 호랑가시나무로 관을 쓴 위풍당당한 눈 처녀가 서 있었는데, 한 손에는 과일과 꽃이 담긴 바구니를, 다른 손에는 새 악보 한 다발을 들고, 시린 어깨엔 완벽한 무지개 같은 아프간 숄을 두르고, 입가에선 분홍색 종이 띠에 적힌 크리스마스 캐럴이 흘러나오고 있었다.\n\"베스에게 보내는 융프라우.\n\n신의 가호가 함께하기를, 사랑하는 베스 여왕이여! 어떤 일에도 마음 졸이지 말기를, 오직 건강과 평화와 행복만이 이 크리스마스 날 그대 것이 되기를.\n\n여기 우리 부지런한 꿀벌을 먹일 과일이 있고, 코끝에 향기 더할 꽃이 있어요. 여기 그대 피아노를 위한 악보가 있고, 발끝을 덮을 아프간 숄이 있지요.\n\n조애나의 초상화도 보세요, 라파엘로 2세가 그렸답니다. 참되고 곱게 그려 내려고 무던히도 애를 썼지요.\n\n부디 빨간 리본 하나 받아 주세요, 야옹이 마님의 꼬리에 매시라고요. 그리고 사랑스러운 페그가 만든 아이스크림은 양동이에 담긴 몽블랑이랍니다.\n\n저를 만든 이들이 가장 깊은 사랑을 이 눈의 가슴속에 담아 두었어요. 그 사랑을, 그리고 이 알프스 처녀를 받아 주세요, 로리와 조가 보냅니다.\"\n\n그것을 보았을 때 베스가 얼마나 웃었는지, 선물들을 들이느라 로리가 얼마나 오르락내리락 뛰어다녔는지, 그리고 그것들을 건네며 조가 얼마나 우스꽝스러운 말들을 늘어놓았는지!\n\n\"행복이 너무 가득 차서, 아버지만 여기 계셨다면 단 한 방울도 더는 못 담을 것 같아.\" 조가 흥분을 가라앉히고 쉬게 하려고 베스를 서재로 데려가, '융프라우'가 보내 준 그 맛있는 포도로 기운을 돋게 해 주는 동안 베스가 더없이 흡족한 한숨을 내쉬며 말했다.\n\n\"나도 그래.\" 그토록 갖고 싶었던 『운디네와 진트람』이 들어 있는 호주머니를 툭툭 치며 조가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"난 정말 그렇고말고.\" 어머니가 예쁜 액자에 넣어 준 성모자상 판화를 들여다보며 에이미가 맞장구쳤다.\n\n\"당연히 나도지!\" 자기 생애 첫 비단 드레스의 은빛 주름을 매만지며 메그가 외쳤다. 로런스 씨가 한사코 선물한 드레스였다.\n\n\"내가 어찌 안 그럴 수 있겠니?\" 마치 부인이 고마운 마음으로 말했다. 그녀의 두 눈은 남편의 편지에서 베스의 미소 띤 얼굴로 옮겨 갔고, 한 손으로는 회색과 금색, 밤색과 짙은 갈색 머리카락으로 만든, 딸들이 방금 가슴에 달아 준 브로치를 어루만지고 있었다.\n\n이 일에 매인 세상살이에서도, 이따금은 이렇게 즐거운 동화책 같은 일이 정말로 일어나곤 하니, 그 얼마나 위안이 되는지. 모두가 너무 행복해서 단 한 방울밖에는 더 담을 수 없겠다고 말한 지 반 시간 만에, 그 한 방울이 찾아왔다. 로리가 응접실 문을 열고 아주 조용히 고개만 쏙 들이밀었다. 그가 차라리 공중제비를 돌며 인디언의 함성이라도 질렀더라면 좋았을 것이다. 그의 얼굴엔 억눌린 흥분이 가득하고 목소리는 어찌나 들뜬 기쁨을 감추지 못하던지, 그저 묘하게 숨 가쁜 목소리로 \"마치 가족에게 줄 크리스마스 선물이 하나 더 있습니다\"라고만 했는데도 모두가 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n그 말이 채 입 밖으로 다 나오기도 전에, 로리는 어떻게 된 영문인지 휙 옆으로 비켜났고, 그 자리에 키 큰 남자가 나타났다. 눈만 빼고 온통 동여맨 채, 무어라 말하려 했지만 끝내 말을 잇지 못하는 또 다른 키 큰 남자의 팔에 기대 있었다. 당연히 한바탕 난리가 났다. 몇 분 동안 다들 넋을 놓은 듯했으니, 더없이 기묘한 일들이 벌어졌고 아무도 한마디 하지 않았다. 마치 씨는 사랑하는 네 쌍의 팔에 둘러싸여 모습이 보이지 않게 되었다. 조는 거의 까무러칠 뻔하는 추태를 부려, 도자기 보관실에서 로리에게 응급조치를 받아야 했다. 브룩 씨는, 본인이 어쩐지 횡설수설 해명한 바로는, 완전히 실수로 메그에게 입을 맞추고 말았다. 그리고 그 점잖던 에이미는 발판에 걸려 나뒹굴고는, 일어날 새도 없이 그대로 아버지의 부츠를 끌어안고 더없이 가슴 뭉클하게 울었다. 가장 먼저 정신을 차린 사람은 마치 부인이었다. 그녀가 손을 들어 올리며 주의를 주었다. \"쉿! 베스를 생각해야지!\"\n\n하지만 이미 늦었다. 서재 문이 벌컥 열리고, 작고 빨간 실내복이 문턱에 나타났다. 기쁨이 그 가냘픈 팔다리에 힘을 불어넣었던 것이다. 베스는 곧장 아버지의 품으로 달려들었다. 그 직후에 무슨 일이 일어났는지는 신경 쓰지 않아도 된다. 벅찬 가슴들이 넘쳐흘러 지난날의 쓰라림을 다 씻어 내고, 오직 현재의 달콤함만을 남겨 두었으니까.\n\n도무지 낭만적이라곤 할 수 없었지만, 진심 어린 웃음 한바탕이 모두를 다시 제정신으로 돌려놓았다. 해나가 부엌에서 뛰어 올라오면서 미처 내려놓지 못한 살진 칠면조를 끌어안고 문 뒤에서 흐느끼고 있는 게 발각된 것이다. 웃음이 잦아들자, 마치 부인은 남편을 충실히 돌봐 준 브룩 씨에게 고마움을 전하기 시작했다. 그러자 브룩 씨는 마치 씨에게 휴식이 필요하다는 게 별안간 떠오른 듯, 로리를 붙들고는 황급히 물러갔다. 그러고 나서 두 환자에게 안정을 취하라는 명이 떨어졌는데, 두 사람은 하나의 큰 의자에 함께 앉아 쉴 새 없이 이야기를 나누는 것으로 그 명을 따랐다.\n\n마치 씨는 식구들을 깜짝 놀라게 해 주고 싶어 얼마나 애가 탔는지, 그리고 날씨가 좋아지자 의사가 그 기회를 잡도록 허락해 준 일이며, 브룩이 얼마나 헌신적이었는지, 또 그가 모든 면에서 더없이 존경스럽고 올곧은 청년인지를 들려주었다. 마치 씨가 바로 그 대목에서 잠시 말을 멈추고는, 난롯불을 사납게 쑤석거리고 있는 메그를 한번 흘끗 보더니, 눈썹을 캐묻듯 치켜올리며 아내를 바라본 까닭은, 여러분의 상상에 맡기겠다. 또한 마치 부인이 가만히 고개를 끄덕이고는, 다소 불쑥, 뭐 좀 드시지 않겠느냐고 물은 까닭도. 조는 그 눈빛을 보았고 그 뜻을 알아챘다. 그래서 포도주와 쇠고기 수프를 가지러 험상궂은 표정으로 성큼성큼 걸어 나가며, 문을 쾅 닫고는 혼자 중얼거렸다. \"갈색 눈을 한 존경스러운 청년들이라면 딱 질색이야!\"\n\n그날 그들이 보낸 것 같은 크리스마스 만찬은 일찍이 없었다. 해나가 속을 채우고 노릇하게 굽고 장식까지 해서 올려 보낸 그 살진 칠면조는 그야말로 볼만한 광경이었다. 입안에서 사르르 녹는 플럼 푸딩도 그랬고, 에이미가 꿀단지에 빠진 파리처럼 흠뻑 즐긴 젤리도 마찬가지였다. 모든 게 잘되었으니, 해나의 말마따나 천만다행이었다. \"제 정신이 어찌나 얼떨떨하던지요, 마님, 푸딩을 오븐에 굽고 칠면조 속에 건포도를 채우지 않은 게, 아니, 천에 싸서 삶지 않은 게 기적이라니까요.\"\n\n로런스 씨와 그의 손자도 함께 만찬을 들었고, 브룩 씨도 그랬다. 조가 그를 향해 험상궂게 노려보는 통에 로리는 한없이 즐거워했다. 식탁 상석에는 안락의자 두 개가 나란히 놓였고, 그 자리에 베스와 그녀의 아버지가 앉아 닭고기와 약간의 과일로 조촐하게 식사를 즐겼다. 그들은 서로의 건강을 빌며 잔을 들고, 이야기를 나누고, 노래를 부르고, 노인네들 말마따나 \"옛일을 떠올리며\", 더없이 즐거운 시간을 보냈다. 썰매를 타러 가기로 했었지만 딸들이 아버지 곁을 떠나려 하지 않아서, 손님들은 일찌감치 돌아갔다. 그리고 땅거미가 내려앉을 무렵, 행복한 가족은 난롯가에 둘러앉았다.\n\n\"꼭 일 년 전에 우린 음울한 크리스마스를 보내게 됐다고 끙끙 앓았었잖아. 다들 기억해?\" 여러 가지 이야기를 길게 나눈 뒤 찾아온 짧은 침묵을 깨며 조가 물었다.\n\n\"전체적으로 보면 꽤 즐거운 한 해였어!\" 메그가 난롯불을 향해 미소 지으며, 브룩 씨를 위엄 있게 대했노라 스스로를 대견해하며 말했다.\n\n\"난 꽤 힘든 한 해였던 것 같아.\" 자기 반지에 비치는 불빛을 사려 깊은 눈으로 바라보며 에이미가 말했다.\n\n\"난 이 해가 끝나서 기뻐, 아버지가 우리 곁으로 돌아오셨으니까.\" 아버지 무릎에 앉은 베스가 속삭였다.\n\n\"너희가 걸어온 길이 꽤나 험했지, 나의 어린 순례자들아, 특히 그 끝자락이 말이다. 하지만 너희는 용감하게 헤쳐 왔어. 그리고 그 짐들도 머지않아 훌훌 벗겨질 것 같구나.\" 자기 주위에 둘러앉은 네 앳된 얼굴을 아버지다운 흐뭇함으로 바라보며 마치 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"어떻게 아세요? 엄마가 말씀해 주셨어요?\" 조가 물었다.\n\n\"많이는 아니란다. 지푸라기 하나로도 바람이 어느 쪽으로 부는지 알 수 있는 법이지. 오늘 몇 가지를 발견했단다.\"\n\n\"아, 그게 뭔지 말씀해 주세요!\" 아버지 곁에 앉은 메그가 외쳤다.\n\n\"여기 하나 있구나.\" 그러면서 의자 팔걸이에 놓인 손을 들어, 거칠어진 집게손가락과 손등의 화상 자국, 그리고 손바닥의 두어 군데 굳은살을 가리켰다. \"이 손이 희고 매끄럽던 시절, 그것을 그대로 지키는 게 네 첫째 관심사였던 때가 기억나는구나. 그때도 무척 예뻤지만, 내겐 지금이 훨씬 더 예쁘단다. 흠집처럼 보이는 이것들 속에서 작은 역사를 읽을 수 있으니까. 허영이 번제물로 바쳐졌고, 이 굳어진 손바닥은 물집보다 더 나은 무언가를 벌어들였지. 그리고 이 바늘에 찔린 손가락들이 해낸 바느질은 오래도록 갈 거라고 확신한다. 그만큼 많은 정성이 그 한 땀 한 땀에 담겼으니까. 메그, 얘야, 나는 가정을 행복하게 지키는 여인다운 솜씨를, 흰 손이나 세련된 교양보다 더 귀히 여긴단다. 이 착하고 부지런한 작은 손을 잡게 되어 자랑스럽고, 머지않아 이 손을 누군가에게 내어 달라는 부탁을 받는 일은 없기를 바란다.\"\n\n여러 시간 묵묵히 일한 데 대한 보상을 메그가 바랐다면, 그녀는 아버지의 진심 어린 손길과 그가 보내 준 흐뭇한 미소 속에서 그것을 받은 셈이었다.\n\n\"조는요? 좋은 말씀 좀 해 주세요. 조는 정말 애를 많이 썼고, 저한테도 너무너무 잘해 줬거든요.\" 베스가 아버지의 귀에 대고 말했다.\n\n그는 웃으며 맞은편에 앉은 키 큰 딸을 건너다보았다. 그 갈색 얼굴엔 유난히 온화한 빛이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"곱슬곱슬한 짧은 머리에도 불구하고, 일 년 전에 내가 두고 떠난 '아들 조'는 보이지 않는구나.\" 마치 씨가 말했다. \"옷깃을 반듯이 여미고, 부츠 끈을 단정히 매고, 예전처럼 휘파람을 불거나 속어를 쓰거나 양탄자 위에 드러눕지도 않는 한 아가씨가 보이는걸. 지금은 간병과 근심으로 얼굴이 좀 야위고 창백하지만, 난 그 얼굴을 보는 게 좋단다. 더 부드러워졌고, 목소리도 낮아졌으니까. 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰지 않고 조용히 움직이며, 어떤 작은 사람을 어머니처럼 보살피는데, 그 모습이 날 기쁘게 하는구나. 내 거친 딸이 좀 그립기도 하지만, 그 자리에 강하고, 든든하고, 마음씨 다정한 여인을 얻게 된다면, 나는 더없이 만족할 거야. 머리를 자른 일이 우리 검은 양을 차분하게 만들었는지는 모르겠다만, 워싱턴을 다 뒤져도 내 착한 딸이 보내 준 이십오 달러로 살 만한 아름다운 물건을 도무지 찾을 수 없었다는 것만은 분명히 안단다.\"\n\n조의 날카로운 두 눈이 잠시 흐릿해졌고, 그 야윈 얼굴이 난롯불빛 속에 발그레해졌다. 아버지의 칭찬을 받으며, 자신이 그 칭찬의 얼마쯤은 받을 자격이 있다고 느꼈던 것이다.\n\n\"이제 베스 차례예요.\" 에이미가 자기 차례를 고대하면서도 기꺼이 기다릴 준비를 한 채 말했다.\n\n\"이 아이는 너무 작아서, 자칫 아주 미끄러져 사라져 버릴까 봐 많은 말은 못 하겠구나. 예전만큼 수줍어하진 않게 됐다만.\" 아버지가 명랑하게 입을 떼다가, 이 아이를 하마터면 잃을 뻔했던 일을 떠올리고는, 자기 뺨에 베스의 뺨을 맞댄 채 다정하게 말하며 아이를 꼭 끌어안았다. \"이제 너를 안전하게 데려왔으니, 신의 가호로 너를 이대로 지켜 줄 거야, 나의 베스.\"\n\n잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 그는 자기 발치의 작은 의자에 앉은 에이미를 내려다보며, 그 반짝이는 머리카락을 어루만지면서 말했다.\n\n\"보니 에이미는 저녁 식사 때 닭다리를 집어 들고, 오후 내내 어머니 심부름을 다니고, 오늘 밤엔 메그에게 제 자리를 양보하고, 모두를 참을성 있고 상냥하게 시중들더구나. 또 보니 별로 보채지도 않고, 거울을 들여다보지도 않으며, 자기가 낀 무척 예쁜 반지에 대해서도 일언반구 없더구나. 그러니 이 아이가 다른 사람을 더 많이, 자기 자신은 덜 생각하는 법을 배웠고, 그 작은 진흙 인형들을 정성껏 빚듯이 제 인격도 정성껏 빚어 보기로 마음먹었구나 짐작하게 된단다. 나는 이것이 기쁘구나. 이 아이가 만든 우아한 조각상이라면 무척 자랑스럽겠지만, 자기 자신과 다른 이들에게 삶을 아름답게 만드는 재능을 지닌, 사랑스러운 딸이라면 한없이 더 자랑스러울 테니까.\"\n\n\"무슨 생각하니, 베스?\" 에이미가 아버지에게 고마움을 전하고 자기 반지 이야기를 한 뒤, 조가 물었다.\n\n\"오늘 『천로역정』에서 읽었어. 크리스천과 호프풀이 숱한 고난 끝에, 일 년 내내 백합이 피어나는 어느 아름답고 푸른 초원에 다다랐대. 거기서 둘은 여정의 끝으로 나아가기 전에, 지금 우리처럼 행복하게 쉬었다는 거야.\" 베스가 대답했다. 그러고는 아버지의 품에서 미끄러져 나와 천천히 피아노 쪽으로 걸어가며 덧붙였다. \"이제 노래할 시간이에요, 내 옛 자리에 앉고 싶어요. 순례자들이 들었던 그 양치기 소년의 노래를 불러 볼게요. 그 시를 아버지가 좋아하셔서, 제가 곡을 붙였거든요.\"\n\n그렇게, 사랑스러운 작은 피아노 앞에 앉아, 베스는 건반을 가만히 어루만지며, 다시는 듣지 못하리라 여겼던 그 고운 목소리로, 제 반주에 맞추어 그 예스러운 찬가를 불렀다. 그것은 그녀에게 유난히도 잘 어울리는 노래였다.\n\n\"낮은 곳에 있는 이는 떨어질 일을 두려워 말지니, 아래에 있는 이에겐 교만이 없도다. 겸손한 이는 언제까지나 하느님을 길잡이로 모시리라.\n\n내게 있는 것에 나는 만족하노라, 그것이 적든 많든. 그리고, 주여! 나는 늘 만족을 구하나니, 주께서 그런 이들을 구원하시기 때문이라.\n\n순례의 길을 가는 이들에게는 넘침이란 도리어 짐이로다. 이승에선 적게, 저승에선 더없는 복을 누림이 대대로 가장 좋은 것이로다!\"\n\n# AUNT MARCH SETTLES THE QUESTION.\n\n마치 대고모가 매듭을 짓다\n여왕벌을 좇아 떼 지어 모여드는 벌들처럼, 이튿날 어머니와 딸들은 마치 씨 주위를 맴돌며, 모든 걸 제쳐 두고 이 새로운 환자를 보살피고, 시중들고, 그의 말에 귀 기울였다. 그 바람에 마치 씨는 친절에 치여 죽을 지경이었다. 그가 베스의 소파 옆 큰 의자에 등을 기대고 앉고, 나머지 셋이 바짝 곁에 붙고, 해나가 이따금 \"그 귀하신 양반을 한 번씩 들여다보러\" 고개를 들이미는 것까지 더하니, 그들의 행복을 완성하는 데 더 필요한 것은 없어 보였다. 하지만 무언가가 정말로 필요했고, 어른들은 그걸 느끼고 있었다. 아무도 입 밖에 내지는 않았지만 말이다. 마치 부부는 두 눈으로 메그를 좇으며 근심 어린 표정으로 서로를 바라보았다. 조는 별안간 진지해지는 순간들이 있었고, 현관에 놓인 브룩 씨의 우산을 향해 주먹을 휘두르는 모습이 목격되기도 했다. 메그는 정신이 딴 데 가 있고, 수줍어하고, 말이 없었으며, 초인종이 울리면 흠칫 놀라고 존의 이름이 나오면 얼굴을 붉혔다. 에이미는 \"다들 뭔가를 기다리는 것 같은데, 아버지가 무사히 집에 돌아오셨으니 이상하게도 마음을 가라앉히질 못한다\"고 했고, 베스는 이웃들이 왜 평소처럼 놀러 오지 않는지 천진하게 궁금해했다.\n\n오후에 로리가 지나가다가 창가에 있는 메그를 보고는, 별안간 신파극에 들린 듯, 눈 위에 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 제 가슴을 치고 머리를 쥐어뜯으며 무슨 은혜라도 구걸하듯 두 손을 모아 애원하는 시늉을 했다. 메그가 점잖게 굴고 가 버리라고 하자, 로리는 손수건으로 있지도 않은 눈물을 짜내고는, 완전히 절망에 빠진 듯 비틀거리며 모퉁이를 돌아 사라졌다.\n\n\"저 바보가 무슨 짓이야?\" 메그가 웃으면서, 짐짓 아무것도 모르는 척하려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"언니의 존이 머지않아 어떻게 굴지 보여 주는 거지. 감동적이지 않아?\" 조가 경멸하듯 대꾸했다.\n\n\"내 존이라고 하지 마, 그건 적절하지도, 사실도 아니야.\" 그러면서도 메그의 목소리는 그 말이 듣기 좋기라도 한 듯 그 말 위에 머뭇거렸다. \"제발 날 좀 괴롭히지 마, 조. 그 사람한테 별 관심 없다고 했잖아. 아무 말도 오갈 일 없고, 우린 다 그냥 사이좋게, 전처럼 지내면 돼.\"\n\n\"그럴 순 없어, 이미 무슨 말이 오갔고, 로리의 장난이 언니를 망쳐서 내 언니가 아니게 만들어 버렸으니까. 나도 알고 엄마도 아셔. 언니는 예전의 언니랑은 조금도 같지 않고, 나한테서 한없이 멀어진 것 같아. 언니를 괴롭히려는 게 아니라 사내답게 견뎌 보려는 거야. 그래도 이 일이 다 매듭지어졌으면 좋겠어. 난 기다리는 게 싫거든. 그러니까 언젠가 할 거라면, 서둘러서 후딱 끝내 버려.\" 조가 토라진 듯 말했다.\n\n\"그분이 먼저 말을 꺼내기 전엔 나도 아무 말, 아무 일도 못 해. 그런데 그분은 말을 안 꺼낼 거야. 아버지가 내가 너무 어리다고 하셨으니까.\" 메그가 일감 위로 고개를 숙인 채, 그 점에 대해서는 아버지 말씀에 썩 동의하지 않는다는 듯한 묘한 미소를 띠며 입을 뗐다.\n\n\"그 사람이 정말 말을 꺼내도, 언니는 뭐라고 해야 할지 몰라서 울거나 얼굴을 붉히거나, 아니면 그 사람 뜻대로 하게 둘걸. 야무지고 단호하게 '싫어요' 한마디 하는 대신에 말이야.\"\n\n\"네 생각만큼 내가 그렇게 바보 같고 마음 약하진 않거든. 무슨 말을 할지 다 정해 뒀어. 다 계획해 뒀으니까 불시에 당할 일은 없어. 무슨 일이 생길지 모르는 거니까, 미리 대비해 두고 싶었어.\"\n\n조는 메그가 자기도 모르게 짐짓 짓고 있는 그 거창한 태도에 웃음을 참을 수 없었다. 그 태도는 두 뺨에 오르내리는 고운 홍조만큼이나 잘 어울렸다.\n\n\"무슨 말을 할 건지 나한테 말해 줄 수 있어?\" 조가 한결 공손하게 물었다.\n\n\"못 할 것도 없지. 너도 이제 열여섯이니, 내 비밀을 털어놓을 만큼은 충분히 컸고, 내 경험이 언젠가 너도 이런 종류의 일을 겪을 때 도움이 될지도 모르니까.\"\n\n\"난 그런 거 안 겪을 거야. 다른 사람들이 사랑 놀음하는 걸 구경하는 건 재밌지만, 내가 직접 하면 바보가 된 기분일 것 같아.\" 그 생각만으로도 질겁한 표정을 지으며 조가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지 않을걸. 네가 누군가를 무척 좋아하고, 그 사람도 너를 좋아한다면 말이야.\" 메그가 혼잣말하듯 말하며, 여름 땅거미 속에서 연인들이 함께 거니는 모습을 자주 보았던 그 오솔길 쪽을 흘끗 내다보았다.\n\n\"그 사람한테 할 말을 나한테 들려준다며.\" 조가 언니의 작은 몽상을 무례하게 끊으며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 난 그냥 아주 차분하고 단호하게 이렇게만 말할 거야. '고마워요, 브룩 씨, 정말 친절하시지만, 저는 아직 어떤 약혼을 하기엔 너무 어리다는 아버지 말씀에 동의해요. 그러니 더는 말씀하지 마시고, 우리 예전처럼 친구로 지내요.'\"\n\n\"흠! 충분히 딱딱하고 쌀쌀맞긴 하네. 하지만 난 언니가 그 말을 끝내 못 할 거라고 봐. 그리고 설령 한다 해도 그 사람이 그걸로 만족 안 할 거란 것도 알아. 그 사람이 책 속에서 퇴짜 맞은 연인들처럼 군다면, 언니는 그 사람 마음 상하게 하느니 차라리 굽히고 말걸.\"\n\n\"안 그래. 마음을 굳혔다고 말하고, 위엄 있게 방에서 걸어 나올 거야.\"\n\n메그는 그렇게 말하면서 일어나, 그 위엄 있게 나가는 모습을 막 연습해 보려던 참이었다. 그런데 현관에서 발소리가 나자 메그는 후다닥 제자리로 날아가 앉아, 정해진 시간 안에 그 솔기 하나를 다 꿰매지 못하면 큰일이라도 나는 양 바느질을 시작했다. 조는 그 갑작스러운 변화에 웃음을 삼켰고, 누군가 조심스레 똑똑 두드리자, 도무지 손님맞이라곤 할 수 없는 험상궂은 얼굴로 문을 열었다.\n\n\"안녕하세요. 제 우산을 가지러 왔어요. 아니, 그러니까, 오늘 아버님께서 좀 어떠신지 보러 왔습니다.\" 브룩 씨가, 시선이 한쪽의 속내 드러난 얼굴에서 다른 쪽 얼굴로 옮겨 가며 조금 당황한 채 말했다.\n\n\"아주 잘 계세요, 그건 우산꽂이에 있고요, 제가 가져올게요, 그리고 아저씨 오셨다고 전할게요.\" 그렇게 대답 속에 아버지와 우산을 한데 뒤죽박죽 섞어 버린 조는, 메그가 그 할 말을 하고 위엄을 뽐낼 기회를 주려고 방에서 슬쩍 빠져나갔다. 그러나 조가 사라지기가 무섭게, 메그는 문 쪽으로 슬금슬금 다가가며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"엄마가 보면 반가워하실 거예요. 부디 앉으세요, 제가 엄마를 불러올게요.\"\n\n\"가지 마세요. 내가 무섭습니까, 마거릿?\" 브룩 씨가 어찌나 상처받은 표정을 짓던지, 메그는 자신이 뭔가 아주 무례한 짓을 한 게 틀림없다고 생각했다. 메그는 이마의 잔 곱슬머리까지 새빨개졌다. 그가 그녀를 마거릿이라고 부른 건 처음이었고, 그가 그렇게 부르는 걸 듣는 게 얼마나 자연스럽고 달콤한지 깨닫고는 스스로도 놀랐던 것이다. 다정하고 편안해 보이고 싶은 마음에, 메그는 미덥다는 듯한 몸짓으로 손을 내밀며 고마운 마음으로 말했다.\n\n\"아버지께 그토록 친절히 대해 주셨는데 제가 어떻게 무서워하겠어요? 그저 그 일에 대해 감사를 드릴 수만 있다면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 하면 되는지 말씀드릴까요?\" 브룩 씨가 그 작은 손을 자기 두 손으로 꼭 잡고, 그 갈색 눈에 어찌나 사랑을 가득 담아 메그를 내려다보던지, 그녀의 가슴이 두근거리기 시작했다. 도망쳐 버리고 싶으면서도 한편으론 멈춰 서서 듣고 싶었다.\n\n\"아, 아니에요, 제발 그러지 마세요. 안 듣는 게 낫겠어요.\" 손을 빼내려 애쓰며, 아니라고 하면서도 겁먹은 얼굴로 메그가 말했다.\n\n\"성가시게 하진 않을게요. 그저 당신이 나를 조금이라도 마음에 두는지 알고 싶을 뿐이에요, 메그. 난 당신을 너무나 사랑해요, 그대.\" 브룩 씨가 다정하게 덧붙였다.\n\n지금이야말로 그 차분하고 격식 있는 대사를 할 순간이었지만, 메그는 그러지 못했다. 그 말을 한 마디도 빠짐없이 다 잊어버린 채 고개를 떨구고는, \"모르겠어요\"라고 어찌나 작게 대답하던지, 존은 그 어리석은 작은 대답을 들으려고 몸을 굽혀야 했다.\n\n그는 그게 그럴 만한 값어치가 있다고 여긴 듯, 더없이 흡족한 양 혼자 미소 지으며, 그 통통한 손을 고맙다는 듯 꼭 쥐고는, 더없이 설득력 있는 어조로 말했다. \"한번 알아봐 주겠어요? 난 너무나 알고 싶어요. 내가 끝내 보상을 받게 될지 아닐지를 알기 전엔, 도무지 일에 마음을 붙일 수가 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"전 너무 어려요.\" 메그가 더듬더듬 말했다. 왜 이렇게 가슴이 두근거리는지 의아하면서도, 한편으론 그게 제법 즐겁기도 했다.\n\n\"기다릴게요. 그동안 당신은 나를 좋아하는 법을 배워 갈 수 있을 테고요. 그게 그렇게 어려운 공부일까요, 그대?\"\n\n\"제가 배우기로 마음먹는다면 어렵진 않겠지만, 하지만……\"\n\n\"부디 배우기로 마음먹어 줘요, 메그. 난 가르치는 걸 좋아하는데, 이건 독일어보다 쉬우니까요.\" 존이 끼어들며 다른 한 손마저 잡아 버려서, 그가 얼굴을 들여다보려고 몸을 굽혔을 때 메그는 얼굴을 가릴 길이 없었다.\n\n그의 어조는 더없이 간곡했다. 하지만 수줍게 그를 훔쳐보던 메그는, 그의 눈이 다정할 뿐 아니라 장난기로도 반짝이고, 자기 성공을 추호도 의심하지 않는 사람의 흡족한 미소를 띠고 있는 걸 보았다. 이것이 메그의 화를 돋웠다. 애니 모팻이 가르쳐 준 그 어리석은 교태의 수법이 머릿속에 떠올랐고, 더없이 착한 작은 아씨들의 가슴속에도 잠들어 있는 그 군림하고 싶은 욕망이 별안간 깨어나 그녀를 사로잡았다. 메그는 흥분되고 묘한 기분이 들어, 달리 어쩔 줄을 몰라 변덕스러운 충동을 따라 두 손을 빼내고는 토라진 듯 말했다. \"전 마음먹지 않을 거예요. 제발 가 주시고 절 좀 내버려 두세요!\"\n\n가엾은 브룩 씨는 공중에 지어 올린 자신의 아름다운 성이 귓전에서 와르르 무너져 내리는 듯한 표정이었다. 메그가 이런 기분에 빠진 모습을 한 번도 본 적이 없었던 그는, 적잖이 어리둥절했다.\n\n\"정말 진심으로 하는 말입니까?\" 그가 걸어가는 메그를 따라가며 근심스럽게 물었다.\n\n\"네, 그래요. 이런 일로 시달리고 싶지 않아요. 아버지도 그럴 필요 없다고 하셨고요. 너무 이르고, 전 그러고 싶지 않아요.\"\n\n\"머지않아 마음이 바뀌기를 바라도 될까요? 기다릴게요, 그리고 당신이 좀 더 시간을 가질 때까지 아무 말 안 할게요. 날 가지고 놀지 말아요, 메그. 당신이 그럴 줄은 몰랐어요.\"\n\n\"제 생각은 아예 하지 마세요. 그러지 않으셨으면 좋겠어요.\" 연인의 인내심과 자기 자신의 힘을 시험해 보는 데서 짓궂은 만족을 느끼며 메그가 말했다.\n\n이제 그는 진지하고 창백해져서, 그녀가 흠모하던 소설 속 영웅들을 한결 더 닮아 보였다. 그러나 그는 그들처럼 제 이마를 치지도, 방 안을 쿵쿵 거닐지도 않았다. 그저 서서 메그를 어찌나 애틋하게, 어찌나 다정하게 바라보던지, 메그는 자기도 모르게 마음이 누그러지는 걸 느꼈다. 그다음에 무슨 일이 일어났을지는 나도 모르겠다. 바로 이 흥미로운 순간에 마치 대고모가 절뚝거리며 들어오지 않았더라면 말이다.\n\n노부인은 조카를 보고 싶은 마음을 도무지 억누를 수가 없었다. 바람을 쐬러 나갔다가 로리를 만나, 마치 씨가 돌아왔다는 소식을 듣고는 곧장 그를 보러 마차를 몰아온 것이다. 식구들은 모두 집 뒤편에서 분주했고, 노부인은 그들을 깜짝 놀라게 해 줄 셈으로 조용히 안으로 들어섰다. 그런데 그 둘을 어찌나 깜짝 놀라게 했던지, 메그는 귀신이라도 본 듯 흠칫했고 브룩 씨는 서재로 사라져 버렸다.\n\n\"세상에, 이게 다 무슨 일이냐?\" 노부인이 지팡이를 탁 치며, 창백한 청년에게서 새빨개진 아가씨에게로 시선을 옮기며 외쳤다.\n\n\"아버지 친구분이세요. 대고모님을 뵈니 너무 놀랐어요!\" 이제 잔소리를 듣게 생겼다고 느끼며 메그가 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"그건 한눈에 보이는구나.\" 마치 대고모가 자리에 앉으며 대꾸했다. \"그런데 아버지 친구가 무슨 말을 했길래 네가 작약처럼 빨개진 게냐? 무슨 수상한 일이 벌어지고 있어, 그게 뭔지 내 꼭 알아야겠다.\" 그러면서 또 한 번 지팡이를 탁 쳤다.\n\n\"그냥 이야기만 나누고 있었어요. 브룩 씨는 우산을 가지러 오셨고요.\" 브룩 씨와 그 우산이 어서 무사히 집 밖으로 나가 있기를 바라며 메그가 입을 뗐다.\n\n\"브룩? 그 도련님의 가정교사 말이냐? 아! 이제 알겠다. 다 알겠어. 조가 네 아버지 편지에 엉뚱한 전갈을 잘못 끼워 넣었길래, 내가 그 애한테 다 말하게 했지. 설마 그 사람을 받아들인 건 아니겠지, 얘야?\" 마치 대고모가 기겁한 얼굴로 외쳤다.\n\n\"쉿! 들으시겠어요. 엄마를 불러올까요?\" 메그가 몹시 난처해하며 말했다.\n\n\"아직은 됐다. 너한테 할 말이 있어, 당장 속 시원히 털어놔야겠다. 말해 보거라, 너 이 쿡인지 뭔지 하는 사람과 결혼할 셈이냐? 만약 그런다면, 내 돈은 한 푼도 너한테 안 갈 줄 알아라. 그걸 명심하고, 분별 있는 아이가 되거라.\" 노부인이 위엄 있게 말했다.\n\n그런데 마치 대고모는 더없이 온순한 사람의 가슴속에서도 반항심을 부추기는 재주를 완벽하게 지니고 있었고, 그렇게 하는 걸 즐겼다. 우리 중에 가장 착한 사람도 청개구리 같은 구석이 조금은 있기 마련인데, 젊고 사랑에 빠졌을 때는 더더욱 그렇다. 만약 마치 대고모가 존 브룩을 받아들이라고 사정했더라면, 메그는 아마 그건 생각조차 할 수 없다고 잘라 말했을 것이다. 그러나 그를 좋아하지 말라고 단호하게 명령받자, 메그는 곧장 그러기로 마음을 굳혀 버렸다. 마음이 그쪽으로 기운 데다 반항심까지 더해져 결심은 쉬웠고, 이미 한껏 흥분해 있던 메그는 유난한 기세로 노부인에게 맞섰다.\n\n\"전 제가 원하는 사람과 결혼할 거예요, 대고모님. 그리고 대고모님 돈은 마음에 드는 누구에게든 물려주시면 돼요.\" 메그가 단호한 기색으로 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n\"흥, 별꼴이야! 그게 내 충고를 받아들이는 태도냐, 아가씨? 두고 보거라, 오두막에서의 사랑이란 걸 겪어 보고 그게 실패라는 걸 알게 되면, 머지않아 후회할 테니.\"\n\n\"큰 집에서 어떤 사람들이 겪는 것보다 더 나쁘진 않을 거예요.\" 메그가 받아쳤다.\n\n마치 대고모는 안경을 쓰고 아가씨를 찬찬히 들여다보았다. 이런 새로운 모습의 메그는 도무지 알아볼 수가 없었던 것이다. 메그 자신도 자신이 낯설게 느껴졌다. 그만큼\n용감하고 당당하게 — 마음만 먹으면 존을 변호하고, 그를 사랑할 권리를 주장하게 되어 차라리 기뻤다. 마치 대고모는 시작을 잘못했다는 걸 깨닫고는, 잠시 뜸을 들인 후 다시 말을 꺼냈다. 가능한 한 부드러운 목소리로. \"메그, 얘야, 이성적으로 생각하고 내 충고를 받아들이렴. 다 너 잘되라고 하는 말이야. 첫 단추를 잘못 끼워서 네 인생 전체를 망치는 꼴은 보고 싶지 않아. 너는 좋은 곳으로 시집가서 가족을 도와야 해. 부잣집에 시집가는 게 네 의무이고, 그 사실을 분명히 새겨 두어야 한다.\"\n\n\"엄마 아빠는 그렇게 생각하지 않으세요. 두 분은 존을 좋아하시는걸요. 그가 가난하긴 해도요.\"\n\n\"네 부모님은, 얘야, 갓난아기 둘만큼도 세상 물정을 모르는 분들이란다.\"\n\n\"그래서 다행이에요.\" 메그가 단호하게 외쳤다.\n\n마치 대고모는 들은 척도 않고 잔소리를 계속했다. \"이 루크라는 남자는 가난한 데다 부자 친척도 하나 없지, 안 그러냐?\"\n\n\"네. 하지만 그에게는 따뜻한 친구가 많아요.\"\n\n\"친구를 먹고 살 순 없어. 어디 한번 그래 보렴. 친구들이 얼마나 쌀쌀맞게 식어 가는지 보게 될 테니. 그 사람, 사업도 없지?\"\n\n\"아직은요. 로런스 씨가 도와주실 거예요.\"\n\n\"그것도 오래 못 갈 거다. 제임스 로런스는 괴팍한 늙은이라 믿을 만한 사람이 못 돼. 그러니까 너는 돈도, 지위도, 일자리도 없는 남자와 결혼해서, 지금보다 더 고되게 일하면서 살겠다는 거구나. 이 늙은이 말 잘 들어서 더 나은 길을 택하면 평생 편하게 살 수 있는데 말이야. 나는 네가 좀 더 분별 있는 줄 알았다, 메그.\"\n\n\"제 인생의 절반을 기다린대도 더 나은 사람은 못 만나요! 존은 착하고 현명해요. 재능도 넘치고, 기꺼이 일하려 하고, 분명히 성공할 거예요. 그렇게 정력적이고 용감하니까요. 다들 그를 좋아하고 존경해요. 저는 이렇게 가난하고 어리고 어리석은데도 그가 저를 마음에 두어 준다는 게 자랑스러워요.\" 메그가 말했다. 진심을 담아 말하는 그 모습이 그 어느 때보다 예뻐 보였다.\n\n\"그 사람은 네게 부자 친척이 있다는 걸 알고 있어, 얘야. 그게 그 사람이 너를 좋아하는 속셈인 게지, 내 짐작엔.\"\n\n\"마치 대고모, 어떻게 그런 말씀을 하실 수 있어요? 존은 그런 비열한 짓을 할 사람이 아니에요. 그런 식으로 말씀하시면 한순간도 듣고 있지 않겠어요.\" 메그가 분개하며 외쳤다. 노부인의 부당한 의심 말고는 모든 것을 잊어버린 채였다. \"내 존은 저처럼 돈을 보고 결혼할 사람이 아니에요. 우리는 기꺼이 일할 거고, 기다릴 작정이에요. 저는 가난을 두려워하지 않아요. 지금까지도 행복했고, 그 사람과 함께라면 앞으로도 행복할 거라는 걸 아니까요. 그는 저를 사랑하고, 저도—\"\n\n메그는 거기서 말을 멈췄다. 아직 마음을 정하지 못했다는 사실이, '자기 존'에게 떠나라고 말했다는 사실이, 그리고 그가 이 앞뒤 안 맞는 말을 엿듣고 있을지도 모른다는 사실이 문득 떠올랐기 때문이다.\n\n마치 대고모는 몹시 화가 났다. 예쁜 조카가 번듯한 혼처에 시집가기를 마음속으로 바라고 있었던 탓이었다. 게다가 그 아이의 행복에 찬 젊은 얼굴에 어린 무언가가 외로운 노부인을 서글프고도 심술궂은 기분에 빠지게 했다.\n\n\"좋아, 이 일에서 난 손을 떼마! 너는 고집불통 아이고, 이 어리석은 짓으로 네가 아는 것보다 훨씬 많은 걸 잃은 거야. 아니, 안 있어. 너한테 실망했으니, 지금은 네 아버지를 볼 기분도 아니다. 결혼하고 나서 나한테는 아무것도 기대하지 마라. 네 그 부크 씨인지 하는 사람의 친구들이 너를 돌봐 줘야겠지. 너랑은 영영 끝이다.\"\n\n그러더니 마치 대고모는 메그의 면전에서 문을 쾅 닫고 잔뜩 화가 나서 마차를 몰고 떠나 버렸다. 노부인이 그 아이의 용기를 죄다 가져가 버린 것 같았다. 혼자 남겨진 메그는 웃어야 할지 울어야 할지 결정하지 못한 채 한순간 우두커니 서 있었다. 그러나 미처 마음을 정하기도 전에 브룩 씨에게 사로잡히고 말았다. 그가 단숨에 말했다. \"안 들으려 해도 들리는 걸 어떡해요, 메그. 나를 변호해 줘서 고마워요. 그리고 마치 대고모님께도 고맙군요. 당신이 나를 조금은 마음에 두고 있다는 걸 증명해 주셨으니.\"\n\n\"얼마나 마음에 두고 있었는지는 저도 몰랐어요. 대고모님이 당신을 헐뜯기 전까지는요.\" 메그가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"그럼 나는 떠나지 않아도 되고, 여기 남아 행복해도 되는 거죠, 그래도 되죠, 여보?\"\n\n여기서 또 한 번 단호한 말을 내뱉고 위엄 있게 퇴장할 좋은 기회가 있었지만, 메그는 둘 중 어느 쪽도 할 생각을 하지 못했다. 그러고는 조의 눈에 영원히 체면을 잃을 짓을 하고 말았으니, 순순히 \"네, 존\" 하고 속삭이며 브룩 씨의 조끼에 얼굴을 묻어 버린 것이다.\n\n마치 대고모가 떠난 지 십오 분 후, 조가 살그머니 아래층으로 내려와 거실 문 앞에서 잠시 멈춰 섰다. 안에서 아무 소리도 들리지 않자, 만족스러운 표정으로 고개를 끄덕이며 미소 지었다. 그러고는 혼잣말을 했다. \"우리가 계획한 대로 언니가 그 사람을 돌려보냈구나. 그 일은 마무리됐어. 가서 그 재미난 얘기나 들으면서 실컷 웃어야지.\"\n\n하지만 가엾은 조는 끝내 웃지 못했다. 문지방 위에서 어떤 광경에 못 박힌 듯 굳어 버렸기 때문이다. 그 광경에 붙들린 채, 입을 눈만큼이나 떡 벌리고 멍하니 바라볼 수밖에 없었다. 패배한 적을 두고 기뻐하고, 못마땅한 연인을 내쫓은 의지 굳은 언니를 칭찬하려고 들어선 참이었다. 그러니 그 적이라는 자가 소파에 태평하게 앉아 있고, 의지 굳은 언니가 그의 무릎 위에 옥좌에 오른 듯 앉아, 더없이 비굴하게 복종하는 표정을 짓고 있는 모습을 본 것은 정말이지 충격이었다. 조는 마치 갑자기 찬물 세례라도 뒤집어쓴 듯 숨이 턱 막혔다. 이렇게 예상치 못하게 형세가 뒤바뀌니 실제로 숨이 멎을 지경이었던 것이다. 그 묘한 소리에 두 연인이 돌아보고 그녀를 알아차렸다. 메그가 벌떡 일어났다. 자랑스러우면서도 수줍은 표정이었다. 그러나 조가 '저 남자'라고 부르는 그자는 정말로 웃음을 터뜨리고는, 어안이 벙벙한 새 식구에게 입을 맞추며 태연하게 말했다. \"처제, 우리를 축하해 줘요!\"\n\n이건 상처에 모욕을 더하는 격이었다. 도무지 견딜 수 없는 노릇이었다. 조는 두 손으로 알 수 없는 격한 몸짓을 하더니 한마디 말도 없이 사라져 버렸다. 위층으로 달려 올라간 그녀는 방으로 뛰어들며 비극적으로 외쳐 환자들을 놀라게 했다. \"오, 누가 좀 얼른 내려가 봐요. 존 브룩이 끔찍한 짓을 하고 있는데, 메그 언니가 좋아한다고요!\"\n\n마치 부부가 서둘러 방을 나섰다. 조는 침대에 몸을 던지고는 베스와 에이미에게 그 끔찍한 소식을 전하면서 폭풍처럼 울고 나무라기를 거듭했다. 그러나 어린 두 동생은 그것을 더없이 즐겁고 흥미로운 사건으로 여겼다. 조는 동생들에게서 별 위안을 얻지 못하자, 다락방의 은신처로 올라가 쥐들에게 자신의 고민을 털어놓았다.\n\n그날 오후 거실에서 무슨 일이 있었는지는 아무도 알지 못했다. 그러나 무척 많은 이야기가 오갔고, 과묵한 브룩 씨는 청혼을 호소하고 자신의 계획을 밝히며, 모든 일을 자기가 원하는 대로 정하도록 설득하는 그 웅변과 패기로 친구들을 놀라게 했다.\n\n그가 메그를 위해 일구겠다는 낙원을 미처 다 그려 보이기도 전에 차 마실 시간을 알리는 종이 울렸다. 그는 자랑스럽게 그녀를 데리고 저녁 식탁으로 갔다. 두 사람이 어찌나 행복해 보이던지 조도 차마 질투하거나 침울해할 마음이 들지 않았다. 에이미는 존의 헌신과 메그의 기품에 깊은 감명을 받았다. 베스는 멀찍이서 두 사람을 향해 환히 웃었고, 마치 부부는 그 젊은 한 쌍을 어찌나 다정하고 흐뭇하게 바라보았던지, 마치 대고모가 두 사람을 '갓난아기 한 쌍처럼 세상 물정 모른다'고 한 말이 옳았음이 확연히 드러났다. 다들 많이 먹지는 못했지만 모두가 무척 행복해 보였고, 이 집안 최초의 로맨스가 그곳에서 시작되니 그 오래된 방이 놀라우리만치 환해지는 것 같았다.\n\n\"이제 좋은 일이라곤 도통 일어나지 않는다는 말은 못 하겠지, 언니?\" 에이미가 말했다. 그녀는 그리려고 마음먹은 스케치 속에 두 연인을 어떻게 배치할지 궁리하는 중이었다.\n\n\"그래, 정말 못 하겠다. 내가 그 말을 한 뒤로 얼마나 많은 일이 일어났는지! 일 년 전 일처럼 느껴지는걸.\" 메그가 대답했다. 그녀는 더없이 행복한 꿈에 잠겨, 빵이니 버터니 하는 그런 평범한 것들 따위는 까마득히 초월한 상태였다.\n\n\"이번에는 기쁜 일이 슬픈 일에 바짝 잇따라 찾아오는구나. 변화가 시작된 것 같아.\" 마치 부인이 말했다. \"대부분의 가정에는 이따금 사건으로 가득한 한 해가 찾아온단다. 올해가 바로 그런 해였지. 하지만 결국 좋게 끝나니 다행이야.\"\n\n\"내년은 더 좋게 끝났으면 좋겠네요.\" 조가 중얼거렸다. 메그가 눈앞에서 낯선 남자에게 빠져 있는 모습을 지켜보기가 무척 괴로웠던 것이다. 조는 몇몇 사람을 무척 애틋하게 사랑했고, 그 애정이 어떤 식으로든 사라지거나 옅어지는 것을 두려워했다.\n\n\"지금부터 삼 년째 되는 해는 분명 더 좋게 끝날 겁니다. 그렇게 만들 거예요. 제가 살아서 계획을 이뤄 낸다면요.\" 브룩 씨가 메그를 보며 미소 지으며 말했다. 마치 이제 자기에게는 모든 일이 가능해진 듯한 표정이었다.\n\n\"기다리기엔 너무 긴 시간 아니에요?\" 에이미가 물었다. 그녀는 결혼식을 보고 싶어 안달이 나 있었다.\n\n\"준비가 되기까지 배워야 할 게 너무 많아서, 나한텐 짧은 시간으로 느껴지는걸.\" 메그가 대답했다. 그 얼굴에는 전에는 본 적 없는 다정한 진지함이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"당신은 기다리기만 하면 돼요. 일은 내가 할 테니까요.\" 존이 말하며, 그 노고의 첫걸음으로 메그의 냅킨을 주워 올렸다. 그 표정에 조는 고개를 절레절레 흔들었다. 그러고는 현관문이 쾅 닫히는 소리에 마음이 놓인다는 듯 혼잣말을 했다. \"로리가 오는군. 이제야 분별 있는 대화를 좀 나눌 수 있겠어.\"\n\n하지만 조의 생각은 빗나갔다. 로리가 의기양양하게 활기를 뿜으며 깡충깡충 들어왔으니까. '존 브룩 부인'에게 줄 신부에게나 어울릴 법한 커다란 꽃다발을 들고서, 이 모든 일이 자신의 훌륭한 솜씨로 성사되었다는 착각에 단단히 빠져 있는 게 분명했다.\n\n\"브룩이 자기 뜻대로 다 해낼 줄 알았어. 그 친구는 늘 그렇거든. 무슨 일이든 이루겠다고 마음먹으면, 하늘이 무너져도 해내고 마니까.\" 로리가 선물과 축하 인사를 건넨 뒤 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 추천해 줘서 정말 고맙군. 미래에 대한 좋은 징조로 받아들이고, 그 자리에서 자네를 내 결혼식에 초대하겠네.\" 브룩 씨가 대답했다. 그는 온 인류와, 심지어 자신의 장난꾸러기 제자와도 평화로운 기분이었다.\n\n\"제가 땅끝에 있더라도 가겠습니다. 그날 조의 얼굴만 봐도 먼 길 갈 가치가 충분할 테니까요. 표정이 영 즐거워 보이지 않네, 아가씨. 무슨 일이야?\" 로리가 물으며 그녀를 따라 거실 한구석으로 갔다. 다들 로런스 씨를 맞이하러 자리를 옮긴 참이었다.\n\n\"난 이 결혼이 마음에 안 들지만, 참기로 마음먹었으니 반대하는 말은 한마디도 안 할 거야.\" 조가 엄숙하게 말했다. \"메그 언니를 떠나보내는 게 나한테 얼마나 힘든 일인지 넌 모를 거야.\" 그녀가 목소리를 가늘게 떨며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"언니를 떠나보내는 게 아니야. 그냥 반으로 나누는 것뿐이지.\" 로리가 위로하듯 말했다.\n\n\"다시는 예전 같을 수 없어. 가장 소중한 친구를 잃은 거라고.\" 조가 한숨을 쉬었다.\n\n\"어쨌든 나한테는 네가 있잖아. 내가 변변치 못한 거 나도 알아. 하지만 나는 평생토록 네 곁을 지킬 거야, 조. 맹세코 그럴게!\" 로리는 진심으로 한 말이었다.\n\n\"네가 그러리라는 거 알아. 정말 고마워. 너는 늘 나한테 큰 위안이 돼, 테디.\" 조가 고마운 마음으로 그의 손을 잡으며 대답했다.\n\n\"자, 그러니까 침울해하지 마, 착하지. 다 잘된 일이잖아. 봐. 메그는 행복하고, 브룩은 부지런히 움직여서 금방 자리 잡을 거야. 할아버지가 그를 챙겨 주실 거고, 메그가 자기만의 작은 집에 사는 모습을 보면 무척 흐뭇할 거라고. 언니가 떠나도 우린 신나게 지낼 수 있어. 나도 머지않아 대학을 마칠 테니, 그러면 우리 외국에 가거나 어디 근사한 여행이라도 떠나자. 그러면 위로가 되지 않겠어?\"\n\n\"그럴 것도 같네. 하지만 삼 년 안에 무슨 일이 일어날지 누가 알겠어.\" 조가 생각에 잠겨 말했다.\n\n\"그건 그래. 너는 앞일을 미리 내다보고서 우리 모두가 그때 어디에 있을지 보고 싶지 않아? 나는 그래.\" 로리가 대답했다.\n\n\"난 안 보고 싶어. 슬픈 걸 보게 될지도 모르니까. 그리고 지금 다들 너무 행복해 보여서, 이보다 더 나아질 수는 없을 것 같거든.\" 조의 눈길이 천천히 방 안을 돌았다. 보는 대로 눈빛이 환해졌으니, 눈앞에 펼쳐진 광경이 흐뭇했기 때문이다.\n\n아버지와 어머니가 나란히 앉아, 한 이십 년 전 두 사람에게 시작된 로맨스의 첫 장을 조용히 다시 살아 보고 있었다. 에이미는 두 연인을 그리고 있었다. 그들은 자기들만의 아름다운 세계에 떨어져 앉아 있었고, 그 세계의 빛이 어린 화가로서는 도저히 옮겨 그릴 수 없는 우아함으로 두 사람의 얼굴을 어루만지고 있었다. 베스는 소파에 누워 오랜 벗과 즐겁게 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 벗은 그녀의 작은 손을, 마치 그 손에 그녀가 걷는 평화로운 길로 자신을 이끌어 줄 힘이 있다고 느끼는 듯 꼭 쥐고 있었다. 조는 자신이 가장 좋아하는 낮은 자리에 비스듬히 기대어 앉아 있었다. 그녀에게 가장 잘 어울리는 진지하고 차분한 표정으로. 그리고 로리는 그녀의 의자 등받이에 기대어, 턱을 그녀의 곱슬머리와 같은 높이에 둔 채, 더없이 다정한 표정으로 미소 지으며, 두 사람을 함께 비추고 있는 기다란 거울 속 그녀를 향해 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n이렇게 한자리에 모인 메그, 조, 베스, 그리고 에이미 위로 막이 내린다. 그 막이 다시 오를지 어떨지는, 「작은 아씨들」이라 불리는 가정극의 제1막이 어떤 반응을 얻느냐에 달려 있다.\n스물넷\n\n이런저런 이야기\n\n다시 새롭게 시작하여 홀가분한 마음으로 메그의 결혼식에 가려면, 마치 가족에 관한 잡담을 좀 늘어놓는 것으로 시작하는 게 좋겠다. 그리고 여기서 미리 밝혀 두자면, 혹시 어른들 가운데 누군가 이 이야기에 '연애'가 너무 많다고 여긴다면(아무래도 그럴 것 같아 걱정이다. 젊은 독자들이 그런 트집을 잡으리라고는 걱정하지 않지만), 나는 마치 부인의 말로 답할 수밖에 없다. \"집 안에 발랄한 처녀가 넷이나 있고, 길 건너에 활기찬 젊은 이웃까지 있는데, 그럼 무얼 기대하겠어요?\"\n\n지난 삼 년은 이 조용한 가족에게 변화를 거의 가져오지 않았다. 전쟁은 끝났고 마치 씨는 무사히 집에 돌아와, 책과 작은 교구 일에 분주했다. 그 교구는 그에게서, 은총으로뿐 아니라 천성으로도 목사인 사람을 발견한 터였다. 그는 조용하고 학구적인 사람으로, 학식보다 나은 지혜와, 온 인류를 '형제'라 부르는 자비심과, 인격으로 피어나 그 인격을 위엄 있고 사랑스럽게 만드는 경건함이 풍부한 사람이었다.\n\n이러한 자질은, 가난과, 더 세속적인 성공으로부터 그를 멀어지게 한 엄격한 정직함에도 불구하고, 향긋한 풀이 벌을 끌어들이듯 자연스럽게 수많은 훌륭한 사람들을 그에게로 이끌었다. 그리고 그 역시 자연스럽게 그들에게 꿀을 내주었으니, 오십 년에 걸친 고된 경험이 빚어 낸 그 꿀에는 쓴 방울이 한 점도 섞여 있지 않았다. 진지한 젊은이들은 그 백발의 학자가 자기들 못지않게 젊은 마음을 지녔음을 알아보았다. 사려 깊거나 괴로움에 빠진 여인들은 본능적으로 그에게 자신의 의혹과 슬픔을 가져왔다. 더없이 다정한 공감과 더없이 현명한 조언을 분명 얻으리라 믿으면서. 죄인들은 이 순결한 마음의 노인에게 자신의 죄를 고백했고, 꾸짖음과 구원을 함께 받았다. 재능 있는 사람들은 그에게서 벗을 찾았다. 야심 있는 사람들은 자신의 것보다 고귀한 야망을 어렴풋이 엿보았다. 그리고 세속적인 사람들조차 그의 신념이 '돈은 안 될지언정' 아름답고 참되다는 것을 인정했다.\n\n바깥 사람들에게는 그 활기찬 다섯 여인이 집안을 다스리는 것처럼 보였고, 실제로 많은 일에서 그러했다. 그러나 책들 사이에 앉아 있는 그 조용한 학자가 여전히 한 집안의 가장이요, 집안의 양심이며, 닻이고, 위로하는 이였다. 분주하고 근심 많은 여인들은 곤란한 때면 언제나 그에게로 향했고, 그 거룩한 말의 가장 참된 의미에서 남편이자 아버지인 그를 발견했으니까.\n\n딸들은 마음은 어머니의 보살핌에, 영혼은 아버지의 보살핌에 맡겼다. 그리고 자신들을 위해 그토록 충실히 살고 애쓰는 두 부모에게, 딸들은 자라남과 더불어 함께 자라난 사랑을 바쳤다. 그 사랑은 삶을 축복하고 죽음보다도 오래 살아남는 더없이 달콤한 끈으로 그들을 다정하게 한데 묶어 주었다.\n\n마치 부인은 우리가 마지막으로 보았을 때보다 머리가 좀 더 세긴 했지만 여전히 활기차고 명랑했다. 다만 지금은 메그의 일에 어찌나 골몰해 있는지, 여전히 부상당한 '병사들'과 군인의 미망인들로 가득한 병원과 보호소에서는 어머니 같은 그 선교사의 방문이 끊겨 확연히 아쉬워하고 있었다.\n\n존 브룩은 일 년 동안 사내답게 의무를 다하다가 부상을 입어 집으로 돌려보내졌고, 복귀를 허락받지 못했다. 그는 훈장도 계급장도 받지 못했지만 받아 마땅한 사람이었다. 자신이 가진 모든 것을 기꺼이 걸었으니까. 그리고 삶과 사랑이 둘 다 한창 피어날 때면 그 둘은 무척 소중한 법이다. 그는 제대를 흔쾌히 받아들이고는, 건강을 되찾고, 일을 준비하고, 메그를 위해 보금자리를 마련하는 데 몰두했다. 그를 특징짓는 분별력과 굳건한 독립심으로, 그는 로런스 씨의 더 후한 제안을 거절하고 경리 자리를 받아들였다. 빌린 돈으로 위험을 무릅쓰느니, 정직하게 번 봉급으로 시작하는 편이 더 만족스러웠던 것이다.\n\n메그는 그동안 기다리면서도 부지런히 일하며 시간을 보냈다. 인격은 여인다워졌고, 살림 솜씨에 현명해졌으며, 그 어느 때보다 예뻐졌다. 사랑은 대단한 미용사니까. 그녀에게도 소녀다운 야망과 희망이 있었기에, 새 삶을 이토록 초라하게 시작해야 한다는 데 다소 실망스럽기도 했다. 네드 모팻이 막 샐리 가드너와 결혼한 참이라, 메그는 그들의 멋진 집과 마차, 숱한 선물과 화려한 혼수를 자기 것과 견주어 보지 않을 수 없었고, 자기도 그런 걸 가질 수 있다면 하고 남몰래 바랐다. 그러나 어찌 된 일인지, 존이 자기를 기다리는 그 작은 집에 쏟아부은 그 모든 끈기 있는 사랑과 수고를 떠올리면 부러움도 불만도 이내 사라졌다. 그리고 둘이 황혼 녘에 나란히 앉아 자기들의 소박한 계획을 이야기하다 보면, 미래는 언제나 어찌나 아름답고 환하게 펼쳐지던지, 메그는 샐리의 화려함 따위는 잊어버리고 자신이 온 기독교 세계에서 가장 부유하고 행복한 처녀라고 느끼는 것이었다.\n\n조는 마치 대고모에게 다시 가지 않았다. 노부인이 에이미를 어찌나 마음에 들어 했던지, 당대 최고의 선생들 중 한 명에게 그림 수업을 받게 해 주겠다는 제안으로 에이미를 구슬렸기 때문이다. 그 혜택을 위해서라면 에이미는 훨씬 더 까다로운 주인이라도 모셨을 것이다. 그래서 에이미는 오전은 의무에, 오후는 즐거움에 바치며 훌륭하게 잘 지냈다. 한편 조는 문학에, 그리고 베스에게 헌신했다. 베스는 열병이 다 지나간 후로도 오래도록 허약한 채였다. 딱히 환자라고는 할 수 없었지만, 예전의 그 발그레하고 건강한 아이로 다시는 돌아가지 못했다. 그래도 늘 희망에 차 행복하고 평온했으며, 자기가 좋아하는 조용한 일들로 분주했고, 모두의 벗이었으며, 그녀를 가장 사랑하는 이들이 그 사실을 채 알아차리기도 훨씬 전부터 이미 그 집안의 천사였다.\n\n「독수리 깃발」이 그녀가 '쓰레기'라 부르는 글에 한 단 당 1달러를 쳐주는 한, 조는 자신을 재력가로 여기며 부지런히 자잘한 로맨스를 지어냈다. 그러나 분주한 머리와 야심 찬 마음속에서는 원대한 계획이 끓어오르고 있었고, 다락방의 낡은 양철 부엌에는 잉크 얼룩진 원고 더미가 조금씩 쌓여 가고 있었다. 그것은 언젠가 마치라는 이름을 명성의 명부에 올려놓게 될 것이었다.\n\n로리는 할아버지를 기쁘게 해 드리려고 의무적으로 대학에 들어가긴 했지만, 이제는 자기 자신을 기쁘게 하려고 가능한 한 가장 수월한 방식으로 학업을 헤쳐 나가고 있었다. 돈과 매너와 넘치는 재능, 그리고 남을 곤경에서 빼내려다 정작 자기가 곤경에 빠지곤 하는 더없이 친절한 마음 덕분에 그는 누구에게나 인기 있었다. 그런 만큼 버릇이 잘못 들 위험이 컸고, 여느 장래가 촉망되는 소년들이 그러하듯 실제로 그렇게 되었을지도 모른다. 만일 그에게 악을 막아 줄 부적이 없었더라면 말이다. 그 부적이란, 그의 성공에 자기 인생을 건 친절한 노인에 대한 기억과, 자기 친아들인 양 그를 지켜봐 주는 어머니 같은 벗과, 그리고 무엇보다도 결코 사소하지 않은, 순진한 네 소녀가 진심을 다해 자신을 사랑하고 우러르며 믿어 준다는 사실이었다.\n\n그저 '근사한 인간 소년'에 지나지 않았으니, 그는 당연히 까불고 시시덕거렸고, 대학의 유행이 명하는 대로 멋쟁이가 되었다가, 보트를 탔다가, 감상에 젖었다가, 운동에 빠졌다가 했다. 신입생을 괴롭히기도 하고 자기도 괴롭힘을 당했으며, 속어를 써 대고, 한두 번이 아니게 정학과 퇴학의 위태로운 문턱까지 갔다. 그러나 이런 장난들의 원인이 넘치는 혈기와 재미를 좋아하는 마음이었던 만큼, 그는 늘 솔직한 고백이나 떳떳한 속죄, 또는 그가 완벽하게 지닌 거부할 수 없는 설득력으로 곤경을 모면해 냈다. 사실 그는 자신이 아슬아슬하게 위기를 넘긴 일들을 은근히 자랑스러워했고, 노발대발하는 조교들과 점잖은 교수들, 무릎 꿇린 적들을 상대로 거둔 승리를 생생하게 들려주며 소녀들을 짜릿하게 해 주기를 즐겼다. '우리 학년 친구들'은 소녀들 눈에 영웅이었고, 소녀들은 '우리 패거리'의 무용담에 결코 싫증 내는 법이 없었으며, 로리가 그들을 집에 데려오면 종종 이 대단한 인물들의 미소를 쬐는 영광을 누리곤 했다.\n\n특히 에이미가 이 대단한 영광을 즐겨, 그들 사이에서 제법 인기 있는 아가씨가 되었다. 우리 아가씨께서는 자신에게 타고난 매혹의 재능을 일찍부터 알아차리고 부려 먹는 법을 익혔으니까. 메그는 자신만의 특별한 존에게 너무 푹 빠져 있어서 다른 어떤 만물의 영장에게도 관심이 없었고, 베스는 너무 수줍어서 그들을 슬쩍 엿보며 에이미가 어떻게 그들을 저렇게 마음대로 부릴 수 있는지 신기해하는 것 말고는 더 하지 못했다. 그러나 조는 완전히 제 세상을 만난 기분이었고, 신사다운 자세며 말투며 재주를 따라 하고 싶은 마음을 억누르기가 무척 힘들었다. 그녀에게는 그런 것들이 젊은 숙녀에게 정해진 예법보다 더 자연스러웠다. 다들 조를 무척 좋아했지만 사랑에 빠지지는 않았다. 반면에 에이미라는 제단 앞에서 감상적인 한숨 한두 번을 바치는 공물 없이 빠져나가는 이는 거의 없었다. 감상 이야기가 나온 김에, 우리는 아주 자연스럽게 '비둘기 둥지'로 넘어가게 된다.\n\n그것은 브룩 씨가 메그의 첫 보금자리로 마련한 작은 갈색 집의 이름이었다. 로리가 그렇게 이름 붙였는데, 그 다정한 연인들에게 더없이 잘 어울린다고 했다. \"한 쌍의 멧비둘기처럼 부리부리 입을 맞추다가 구구 우는\" 사이라나. 그것은 자그마한 집이었다. 뒤에는 작은 정원이 있고, 앞에는 손수건만 한 잔디밭이 있었다. 메그는 여기에 분수와 관목, 그리고 사랑스러운 꽃들을 흐드러지게 가꿀 작정이었다. 다만 지금 당장은 그 분수란 게 풍상에 시달린 항아리 하나로 대신되어 있었는데, 다 부서진 개숫물 그릇과 영락없이 닮은 꼴이었다. 관목이라야 어린 낙엽송 몇 그루로, 살아야 할지 죽어야 할지 마음을 못 정한 모양새였다. 그리고 흐드러진 꽃이란 것은, 씨를 심어 둔 자리를 표시하느라 줄지어 꽂아 둔 막대들로 그저 암시되어 있을 뿐이었다.\n그러나 안으로 들어가면 그곳은 정말이지 매력적이었고, 행복한 신부는 다락에서 지하실까지 흠 하나 발견하지 못했다. 하기야 현관 복도가 어찌나 좁던지, 피아노가 없는 게 다행이었다. 한 대를 통째로 들여놓을 수가 없었을 테니까. 식당은 어찌나 작은지 여섯 명이면 빠듯하게 들어찼고, 부엌 계단은 하인이며 그릇이며 죄다 석탄 통 속으로 곤두박질치게 하려고 일부러 지은 것만 같았다. 그러나 이런 자잘한 흠들에 한번 익숙해지고 나면, 이보다 더 완벽할 수 없었다. 분별과 안목이 가구를 들이는 일을 도맡았고, 그 결과는 무척 만족스러웠으니까. 그 작은 거실에는 대리석 상판 탁자도, 기다란 거울도, 레이스 커튼도 없었지만, 소박한 가구와 넉넉한 책들, 멋진 그림 한두 점, 퇴창에 놓인 화분대가 있었고, 여기저기 흩어진 채로 다정한 손길에서 온 예쁜 선물들이 놓여 있었다. 그 선물들은 함께 실려 온 애정 어린 인사말 덕분에 한결 더 아름다웠다.\n\n로리가 준 패리언 도자기 프시케 상이 그 받침대를 존이 직접 달았다 해서 그 아름다움을 조금이라도 잃었다고는 생각지 않는다. 어떤 실내장식가가 단다 한들 에이미의 예술적인 솜씨보다 그 수수한 모슬린 커튼을 더 우아하게 드리울 수 있었으리라고도, 또 조와 어머니가 메그의 몇 안 되는 상자와 통과 꾸러미를 정리해 넣은 그 곳간보다 좋은 소망과 즐거운 말과 행복한 희망이 더 잘 갖춰진 곳간이 일찍이 있었으리라고도 생각지 않는다. 그리고 나는 양심을 걸고 단언하건대, 한나가 모든 냄비와 팬을 열두 번씩 거듭 정돈하고, '브룩 마님이 집에 오시는' 그 순간 바로 불을 붙일 수 있게 아궁이를 다 준비해 두지 않았더라면, 그 산뜻한 새 부엌이 결코 그토록 아늑하고 깔끔해 보일 수 없었을 것이다. 또 나는 의심스럽다. 일찍이 어떤 젊은 새댁이 그토록 풍족하게 갖춰진 먼지떨이와 냄비 받침과 헝겊 주머니를 가지고 살림을 시작했을지. 베스가 은혼식이 돌아올 때까지 쓰고도 남을 만큼 만들어 주었고, 신혼 식기를 닦는 일에만 쓰라고 세 가지 종류의 행주까지 고안해 냈으니 말이다.\n\n이런 일들을 죄다 남에게 시키며 사는 사람들은 자기가 무엇을 잃는지 결코 알지 못한다. 아무리 보잘것없는 일도 사랑하는 손길이 하면 아름다워지는 법이니까. 메그는 이를 증명하는 숱한 증거들을 찾아냈으니, 부엌의 밀방망이에서 거실 탁자 위의 은화병에 이르기까지, 그 작은 보금자리의 모든 것이 가정의 사랑과 다정한 배려를 웅변하고 있었다.\n\n함께 계획을 짜며 보낸 그 행복한 시간들이라니, 그 엄숙한 장보기 나들이라니. 또 얼마나 우스운 실수들을 저질렀던지, 그리고 로리가 사 온 어이없는 물건들을 두고 얼마나 큰 웃음이 터져 나왔던지. 농담을 좋아하는 이 젊은 신사는 대학을 거의 마쳐 가는데도 여전히 영락없는 소년이었다. 그의 최근 변덕은, 매주 찾아올 때마다 젊은 살림꾼을 위해 무언가 새롭고 쓸모 있고 기발한 물건을 가져오는 것이었다. 어느 주에는 유별난 빨래집게 한 봉지를, 그다음에는 첫 시험에 산산조각 나 버린 놀라운 육두구 강판을, 또 칼을 죄다 망쳐 놓는 칼 가는 기구를, 아니면 카펫에서 보풀만 말끔히 떼어 가고 먼지는 그대로 남기는 청소기를, 손 살갗을 벗겨 놓는 일손 더는 비누를, 속아 산 사람의 손가락 말고는 어디에도 단단히 붙지 않는 절대 접착제를, 그리고 자투리 동전을 넣는 장난감 저금통에서부터 김을 내며 물건을 빠는, 그러나 그 과정에서 폭발할 가능성이 다분한 놀라운 보일러에 이르기까지 온갖 양철 제품을 가져왔다.\n\n메그가 제발 그만하라고 사정해도 소용없었다. 존은 그를 보고 웃었고, 조는 그를 '투들스 씨'라고 불렀다. 그는 양키의 창의력에 후원자 노릇 하면서 자기 친구들이 알맞게 살림을 갖추는 걸 보고 싶다는 열병에 단단히 사로잡혀 있었다. 그래서 매주 무슨 새로운 우스꽝스러운 물건이 나타나는 것이었다.\n\n마침내 모든 일이 끝났다. 에이미가 방마다 다른 색깔에 맞춰 색색의 비누를 배치하는 일까지, 그리고 베스가 첫 식사를 위해 식탁을 차리는 일까지.\n\n\"만족하니? 집 같은 느낌이 들어? 여기서 행복할 것 같아?\" 마치 부인이 물었다. 그녀와 딸은 팔짱을 끼고 새 왕국을 둘러보는 중이었다. 바로 그때 두 사람은 그 어느 때보다 다정하게 서로에게 매달려 있는 듯했다.\n\n\"네, 엄마, 더없이 만족해요. 다 여러분 덕분이에요. 그리고 너무너무 행복해서 말로는 다 못 하겠어요.\" 메그가 대답했다. 그 표정이 어떤 말보다도 많은 것을 담고 있었다.\n\n\"하인이 한둘만 있으면 다 좋을 텐데.\" 에이미가 거실에서 나오며 말했다. 그녀는 그 안에서 청동 머큐리 상을 장식장 위에 두는 게 나을지 벽난로 선반 위에 두는 게 나을지 결정하려 애쓰던 참이었다.\n\n\"엄마하고 그 얘기를 나눠 봤는데, 우선은 엄마 방식대로 해 보기로 마음먹었어. 할 일이 워낙 적을 테니까, 로티가 심부름을 다녀 주고 여기저기 거들어 주면, 게으름 피우거나 향수병에 걸리지 않을 만큼만 일하게 되는 거지.\" 메그가 차분하게 대답했다.\n\n\"샐리 모팻은 넷이나 두는데.\" 에이미가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"메그 언니가 넷이나 두면 집이 못 버틸걸. 주인 양반하고 마님은 정원에서 노숙해야 할 거야.\" 조가 끼어들었다. 그녀는 커다란 파란 앞치마를 두른 채 문손잡이에 마지막 광을 내고 있었다.\n\n\"샐리는 가난한 사람의 아내가 아니고, 그 화려한 살림에는 하녀가 많은 게 어울리지. 메그하고 존은 소박하게 시작하지만, 나는 그 작은 집에도 큰 집 못지않은 행복이 가득하리라는 느낌이 들어. 메그 같은 젊은 처녀들이 차려입고, 지시 내리고, 잡담이나 하는 것 말고는 할 일이 없게 되는 건 큰 잘못이란다. 나도 처음 결혼했을 때는 새 옷이 닳거나 해어지기를 간절히 바라곤 했어. 그걸 깁는 즐거움이라도 누리고 싶어서 말이야. 수예나 하고 손수건이나 만지작거리는 일에 진절머리가 났거든.\"\n\n\"엄마는 왜 샐리처럼 부엌에 들어가서 음식이나 만들면서 시간을 보내지 않으셨어요? 샐리는 그렇게 한다잖아요, 재미 삼아서요. 그래 봤자 제대로 되는 법이 없어서 하인들이 비웃는다지만요.\" 메그가 말했다.\n\n\"나도 얼마 지나서는 그랬단다. '음식 장난'을 치려던 게 아니라, 한나에게 일을 어떻게 해야 하는지 배우려고 말이야. 내 하인들이 나를 비웃지 못하게 하려고. 그때는 놀이였지. 하지만 내가 어린 딸들에게 몸에 좋은 음식을 해 먹일 의지뿐 아니라 능력까지 갖추고 있다는 사실에, 그리고 더는 일손을 살 형편이 못 될 때 스스로 해낼 수 있다는 사실에 진심으로 감사하게 되는 날이 왔단다. 너는 정반대 쪽 끝에서 시작하는구나, 메그. 하지만 지금 배우는 것들이 나중에, 존이 더 부유해졌을 때 네게 쓸모가 있을 거야. 한 집안의 안주인이라면, 아무리 화려한 집이라도, 일이 어떻게 돌아가야 하는지를 알아야 하니까. 제대로 정직한 시중을 받고 싶다면 말이야.\"\n\n\"네, 엄마, 그건 분명해요.\" 메그가 그 짤막한 가르침에 공손히 귀를 기울이며 말했다. 아무리 훌륭한 여인이라도 살림이라는 더없이 마음을 사로잡는 주제에 대해서는 일장 연설을 늘어놓기 마련이니까. \"있잖아요, 저는 우리 인형의 집에서 이 방이 제일 좋아요.\" 잠시 후 메그가 덧붙였다. 두 사람이 위층으로 올라가던 참이었고, 그녀가 그득하게 채워진 리넨 벽장 안을 들여다보던 참이었다.\n\n베스가 거기 있었다. 눈처럼 흰 무더기를 선반 위에 가지런히 올려놓으며, 그 푸짐한 진열에 흐뭇해하고 있었다. 메그가 말하자 셋 다 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그 리넨 벽장은 하나의 우스개였기 때문이다. 사연인즉슨 이러했다. 메그가 '그 브룩이라는 자'와 결혼한다면 자기 돈은 한 푼도 못 받을 거라고 말해 놓은 터라, 마치 대고모는 시간이 흘러 노여움이 가라앉고 그 맹세를 후회하게 되자 다소 난처한 처지가 되었다. 노부인은 결코 자기 말을 어기는 법이 없는지라, 어떻게 그 말을 비껴갈지 몹시 머리를 썩였고, 마침내 스스로 흡족할 만한 묘책을 짜냈다. 플로렌스의 엄마인 캐럴 부인에게 집안 리넨과 식탁보를 넉넉하게 사서, 만들게 하고, 이름까지 새겨, 그것을 자기 선물로 보내라고 시킨 것이다. 그 일은 모두 충실하게 이행되었다. 그러나 비밀이 새어 나갔고, 온 가족이 그것을 무척 즐거워했다. 마치 대고모가 짐짓 아무것도 모르는 척하면서, 첫 신부에게 오래전에 약속해 둔 그 구식 진주 말고는 아무것도 줄 수 없다고 우겨 댔으니 말이다.\n\n\"그게 바로 살림꾼다운 안목인데, 나는 그게 보기 좋더라. 내게 시트 여섯 장으로 살림을 차린 젊은 친구가 하나 있었는데, 그러면서도 손님 접대용으로 핑거볼은 갖추고 있었고, 그걸로 흡족해하더구나.\" 마치 부인이 말했다. 다마스크 식탁보를 토닥이며, 그 고운 천을 진정 여성다운 안목으로 음미하면서.\n\n\"저는 핑거볼은 하나도 없지만, 이건 한나 말로는 제가 평생 쓸 수 있는 '혼수'래요.\" 메그가 무척 흐뭇한 표정으로 말했다. 그럴 만도 했다.\n\n\"투들스가 온다!\" 아래층에서 조가 외쳤다. 다들 로리를 맞이하러 내려갔다. 그의 매주 방문은 이들의 조용한 삶에서 중요한 사건이었다. 키 크고 어깨 넓은 젊은 사내가, 짧게 깎은 머리에 대야처럼 생긴 펠트 모자를 쓰고 펄럭이는 외투를 걸친 채, 성큼성큼 길을 따라 내려왔다. 그러더니 대문을 열려고 멈추지도 않고 낮은 울타리를 넘어, 곧장 마치 부인에게로 두 손을 내밀며 다가와 진심 어린 인사를 건넸다.\n\n\"저 왔어요, 어머니! 네, 다 잘됐어요.\"\n\n마지막 말은 노부인이 그에게 보낸 눈길에 대한 대답이었다. 다정하게 묻는 그 눈길을, 그 잘생긴 두 눈이 어찌나 솔직하게 마주했던지, 그 자그마한 의식은 여느 때처럼 어머니다운 입맞춤으로 마무리되었다.\n\n\"존 브룩 부인께, 만든 사람의 축하와 찬사를 담아서요. 잘 있었니, 베스! 너는 어쩜 이리 보기만 해도 상쾌하니, 조. 에이미, 너는 미혼 숙녀치고는 너무 예뻐지고 있어.\"\n\n로리는 이렇게 말하면서 갈색 종이 꾸러미를 메그에게 건네주고, 베스의 머리 리본을 잡아당기고, 조의 커다란 앞치마를 빤히 쳐다보고, 에이미 앞에서는 짐짓 황홀해하는 자세를 취하더니, 죽 돌아가며 모두와 악수를 했다. 그러자 모두가 입을 열기 시작했다.\n\n\"존은 어디 있어요?\" 메그가 걱정스레 물었다.\n\n\"내일 쓸 혼인 허가증을 받느라 들렀어요, 부인.\"\n\n\"마지막 경기는 어느 편이 이겼어, 테디?\" 조가 물었다. 그녀는 열아홉 나이에도 사내들의 운동 경기에 한사코 관심을 두었다.\n\n\"당연히 우리 편이지. 네가 와서 봤으면 좋았을 텐데.\"\n\n\"그 어여쁜 랜들 양은 어떻게 지내?\" 에이미가 의미심장한 미소를 띠며 물었다.\n\n\"그 어느 때보다 매정하지. 내가 어떻게 애를 태우고 있는지 안 보여?\" 로리는 자기 넓은 가슴을 철썩 소리 나게 치고는 연극조로 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"이번엔 무슨 우스운 물건이야? 꾸러미를 풀어 봐, 메그.\" 베스가 울퉁불퉁한 꾸러미를 궁금한 눈으로 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"불이 나거나 도둑이 들었을 때 집에 두면 쓸모 있는 물건이지.\" 로리가 말했다. 소녀들의 웃음 속에서 야경꾼의 딸랑이가 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"존이 없을 때 언제든 겁이 나면, 메그 부인, 그냥 이걸 앞 창문 밖으로 휘두르기만 해요. 그럼 눈 깜짝할 새에 온 동네가 다 깨어날 테니까. 괜찮은 물건이죠?\" 로리는 그것의 위력을 한 차례 시범으로 보여 주었고, 모두 두 귀를 틀어막아야 했다.\n\n\"그래, 고맙기도 하지! 고맙다는 말이 나온 김에 한마디 하자면, 너희 웨딩 케이크가 박살 나는 걸 막아 준 한나에게 고마워해야 할 거야. 오는 길에 그게 너희 집으로 들어가는 걸 봤는데, 한나가 사내처럼 그걸 지켜 내지 않았더라면 내가 한 입 떼어 갔을 거야. 자두가 어찌나 듬뿍 든 것 같던지.\"\n\n\"넌 도대체 언제쯤 철이 들려나, 로리.\" 메그가 부인다운 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"최선을 다하고 있어요, 부인. 하지만 더는 못 클까 봐 걱정이네요. 이 타락한 시대에 사내가 클 수 있는 키라야 6피트가 고작이니 말이에요.\" 젊은 신사가 대답했다. 그의 머리는 작은 샹들리에와 거의 같은 높이에 있었다. \"이 말끔하고 새것 티 나는 보금자리에서 뭘 먹는 건 신성모독일 테니, 내가 지독하게 배가 고파서 하는 말인데, 자리를 옮길 것을 제안하는 바입니다.\" 그가 곧이어 덧붙였다.\n\n\"엄마하고 나는 존을 기다릴 거야. 마지막으로 정할 일이 좀 남아 있거든.\" 메그가 부산히 자리를 뜨며 말했다.\n\n\"베스하고 나는 키티 브라이언트네 집에 가서 내일 쓸 꽃을 더 가져올 거야.\" 에이미가 덧붙였다. 그림 같은 곱슬머리 위에 그림 같은 모자를 동여매며, 그 효과를 누구 못지않게 즐기면서.\n\n\"가자, 조, 친구를 저버리지 말고. 어찌나 기진맥진한지 도와주지 않으면 집까지 못 가겠다. 앞치마는 절대 벗지 마. 그게 유난히 잘 어울리거든.\" 로리가 말했다. 조는 그가 유독 질색하는 그 물건을 자신의 널찍한 주머니에 챙겨 넣고는, 비틀거리는 그의 발걸음을 부축하라고 팔을 내밀었다.\n\n\"자, 테디, 내일 일에 대해 진지하게 얘기 좀 하고 싶어.\" 둘이 함께 거닐며 조가 입을 열었다. \"넌 반드시 얌전히 굴겠다고, 무슨 장난도 치지 않겠다고, 우리 계획을 망치지 않겠다고 약속해야 해.\"\n\n\"장난 안 칠게.\"\n\n\"그리고 우리가 진지해야 할 때 우스운 소리도 하지 말고.\"\n\n\"난 그런 적 없어. 그건 네가 잘하는 거지.\"\n\n\"그리고 제발 부탁인데, 예식 중에 나를 쳐다보지 마. 네가 쳐다보면 난 분명히 웃음이 터질 테니까.\"\n\n\"넌 날 못 볼걸. 어찌나 펑펑 우는지 네 주위에 자욱한 안개가 시야를 가려 버릴 테니까.\"\n\n\"난 큰 슬픔이 아니고서는 절대 안 울어.\"\n\n\"이를테면 친구들이 대학에 가는 거 같은 거 말이야, 응?\" 로리가 의미심장하게 웃으며 끼어들었다.\n\n\"잘난 척 좀 작작 해. 난 그저 여자애들 장단 맞춰 주느라 조금 훌쩍였을 뿐이야.\"\n\n\"바로 그거지. 그건 그렇고, 조, 이번 주 할아버지는 좀 어때? 꽤 너그러우셔?\"\n\n\"아주 그러셔. 왜, 너 무슨 사고라도 쳐 놓고 할아버지가 어떻게 받아들이실지 알고 싶은 거야?\" 조가 다소 날카롭게 물었다.\n\n\"이봐, 조, 너 정말 내가 네 어머니 얼굴을 똑바로 보면서 '다 잘됐어요' 하고 말할 사람으로 보여? 그게 사실이 아니라면 말이야.\" 로리가 발걸음을 뚝 멈추고는 억울하다는 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"아니, 안 그래.\"\n\n\"그럼 의심하지 마. 난 그저 돈이 좀 필요할 뿐이야.\" 로리가 다시 걸음을 옮기며 말했다. 그녀의 진심 어린 어조에 마음이 풀린 것이다.\n\n\"넌 돈을 너무 많이 써, 테디.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리, 나는 돈을 안 써. 그게 어떻게든 알아서 써지고, 내가 알아차리기도 전에 사라져 버린다니까.\"\n\n\"넌 너무 후하고 마음이 여려서 남들이 빌려 가게 내버려 두고, 누구한테도 '안 돼'라는 말을 못 하잖아. 우리도 헨쇼 얘기랑, 네가 그를 위해 해 준 일들을 다 들었어. 네가 늘 그런 식으로 돈을 쓴다면 아무도 너를 탓하지 않을 거야.\" 조가 따뜻하게 말했다.\n\n\"아, 걔가 별것 아닌 일을 침소봉대한 거야. 그렇게 훌륭한 친구가, 우리 게으른 녀석들 열 명을 합친 것보다 나은 친구가, 그저 약간의 도움이 없어서 죽도록 일하다 쓰러지게 내버려 두라는 거야, 설마?\"\n\n\"당연히 아니지. 하지만 네가 조끼를 열일곱 벌이나 두고, 끝도 없이 넥타이를 갖고, 집에 올 때마다 새 모자를 쓰는 건 무슨 소용인지 모르겠어. 난 네가 멋쟁이 시기는 지난 줄 알았는데, 그게 이따금 새로운 데서 또 터져 나오더라. 요즘은 흉하게 꾸미는 게 유행이라며. 머리를 솔처럼 보이게 하고, 구속복 같은 옷을 입고, 주황색 장갑을 끼고, 투박하고 코가 네모난 부츠를 신고. 그게 싸게 먹히는 추함이라면 나도 아무 말 안 하겠어. 하지만 멀쩡한 것만큼이나 돈이 들면서, 난 거기서 아무 만족도 못 얻으니 하는 말이야.\"\n\n로리는 고개를 뒤로 젖히고 이 공격에 어찌나 호탕하게 웃어 댔던지, 대야 같은 펠트 모자가 떨어졌고, 조가 그 위를 밟고 지나가 버렸다. 그러나 이 모욕은 그에게 도리어 수수하고 실용적인 차림새의 장점을 늘어놓을 기회만 주었을 뿐이다. 그는 짓밟힌 모자를 접어 주머니에 쑤셔 넣으며 그렇게 했다.\n\n\"잔소리 좀 그만해, 착하지! 일주일 내내 잔소리는 실컷 듣고, 집에 오면 좀 즐기고 싶단 말이야. 내일은 돈 아끼지 않고 쫙 빼입어서 내 친구들을 흡족하게 해 줄게.\"\n\n\"네가 머리만 좀 기른다면 너를 가만 내버려 두겠어. 내가 무슨 귀족 취향은 아니지만, 풋내기 권투 선수처럼 생긴 사람하고 같이 다니는 모습을 남들한테 보이는 건 정말 싫거든.\" 조가 매섭게 한마디 했다.\n\n\"이 수수한 스타일이 공부를 북돋아 주거든. 그래서 우리가 이걸 택하는 거야.\" 로리가 대꾸했다. 그는 분명 허영을 부린다고는 할 수 없는 사람이었다. 멋진 곱슬머리를 자진해서 희생하고 4분의 1인치짜리 짧은 까까머리로 만든 사람이었으니.\n\n\"그건 그렇고, 조, 내 생각에 그 파커라는 꼬마가 에이미한테 진짜 푹 빠진 것 같아. 노상 걔 얘기를 하고, 시를 쓰고, 영 수상쩍게 넋을 놓고 다니더라고. 그 풋사랑은 싹부터 잘라 버리는 게 낫지 않겠어?\" 잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 로리가 은밀히 형 같은 어조로 덧붙였다.\n\n\"당연히 그래야지. 우린 앞으로 몇 년 동안은 이 집에서 더 이상 결혼하는 사람이 안 나왔으면 좋겠어. 맙소사, 그 어린것들이 대체 무슨 생각이람?\" 조는 에이미와 꼬마 파커가 아직 십 대도 안 된 양 어처구니없어하는 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"속도가 빠른 시대잖아. 우리가 대체 어찌 되려는 건지 모르겠다니까, 부인. 너는 한낱 갓난아기지만, 다음 차례는 너야, 조. 그러면 우린 슬피 한탄하는 신세로 남겨지겠지.\" 로리가 시대의 타락을 두고 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다.\n\n\"걱정 마. 난 그런 호감 사는 부류가 아니거든. 아무도 날 원하지 않을 테고, 그게 다행이야. 집안에는 으레 노처녀가 하나쯤 있어야 하는 법이니까.\"\n\n\"넌 누구한테도 기회를 안 주잖아.\" 로리가 곁눈질하며 말했다. 그의 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴에 아까보다 조금 더 붉은 기가 돌았다. \"넌 네 성격의 부드러운 면을 안 보여 줘. 그러다 어쩌다 어떤 녀석이 그걸 우연히 엿보고, 그게 마음에 든다는 걸 차마 감추지 못하면, 넌 그를 거미지 부인이 제 연인을 대하듯 다뤄 버리지. 찬물을 끼얹어 버리고는, 어찌나 가시를 곤두세우는지 아무도 감히 너를 건드리거나 쳐다보지도 못하게 만든다고.\"\n\n거미지 부인: 찰스 디킨스의 「데이비드 코퍼필드」에 나오는, 늘 신세를 한탄하는 인물.\n\"난 그런 거 싫어. 난 너무 바빠서 그런 시답잖은 일로 골치 썩일 틈이 없고, 그렇게 가족을 갈라놓는 건 끔찍한 일이라고 생각해. 자, 이제 그 얘기는 그만하자. 메그 언니 결혼식 때문에 우리 모두 머리가 어떻게 됐나 봐. 노상 연인이니 뭐니 그런 어처구니없는 얘기만 하고 있잖아. 난 화내고 싶지 않으니까, 화제를 바꾸자.\" 조는 조금만 자극받아도 당장 찬물이라도 끼얹을 듯한 표정이었다.\n\n그의 속마음이 어떠했든, 로리는 길고 낮은 휘파람으로 그것을 풀어냈다. 그리고 대문 앞에서 헤어지며 무시무시한 예언을 남겼다. \"내 말 새겨들어, 조, 다음 차례는 너야.\"\n스물다섯\n\n# THE FIRST WEDDING.\n\n첫 번째 결혼식\n그날 아침 현관 위 6월의 장미들은 밝고 이른 시각에 깨어나, 구름 한 점 없는 햇살 속에서 온 마음으로 기뻐했다. 정다운 작은 이웃들답게 말이다. 바람에 흔들리며 자기들이 본 것을 서로에게 속삭이느라 그 발그레한 얼굴들이 흥분으로 한껏 달아올라 있었다. 몇몇은 잔치가 차려진 식당 창문 안을 들여다보았고, 몇몇은 기어올라가 신부를 단장시키는 자매들에게 고개를 끄덕이며 미소 지었으며, 또 몇몇은 정원과 현관과 복도에서 이런저런 심부름으로 오가는 이들에게 환영의 손짓을 보냈다. 그리고 모두가, 가장 발그레하게 활짝 핀 꽃부터 가장 창백한 어린 봉오리까지, 그토록 오래 자기들을 사랑하고 돌봐 준 다정한 안주인에게 아름다움과 향기의 공물을 바쳤다.\n\n메그 자신도 한 송이 장미처럼 보였다. 그날 그녀의 마음과 영혼에서 가장 선하고 가장 다정한 모든 것이 얼굴에 활짝 피어난 것 같았고, 그리하여 그 얼굴은 곱고도 부드러웠으며, 미모보다 더 아름다운 매력을 띠고 있었다. 그녀는 비단도, 레이스도, 오렌지꽃도 마다했다. \"오늘은 낯설어 보이거나 잔뜩 꾸민 것처럼 보이고 싶지 않아.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"유행을 따른 결혼식이 아니라, 그저 내가 사랑하는 사람들만 곁에 있었으면 좋겠어. 그리고 그 사람들에게는 평소 그대로의 내 모습으로 보이고, 또 그렇게 있고 싶어.\"\n\n그래서 그녀는 웨딩드레스를 직접 만들었다. 소녀다운 마음의 다정한 희망과 순수한 로맨스를 한 땀 한 땀 그 안에 꿰매어 넣으면서. 자매들이 그녀의 고운 머리를 땋아 올려 주었고, 그녀가 단 유일한 장신구는 은방울꽃이었다. '자기 존'이 온갖 꽃들 중에서 가장 좋아하는 꽃이었다.\n\n\"정말이지 우리 사랑하는 메그 언니 그대로네. 다만 너무너무 곱고 사랑스러워서, 드레스가 구겨지지만 않는다면 와락 껴안아 주고 싶을 정도야.\" 모든 단장이 끝나자 에이미가 흐뭇하게 그녀를 바라보며 외쳤다.\n\n\"그럼 됐어. 하지만 다들 나를 껴안고 입맞춰 줘, 드레스 따위는 신경 쓰지 말고. 오늘만큼은 이런 구김살이 잔뜩 들어갔으면 좋겠으니까.\" 메그가 자매들에게 두 팔을 벌렸다. 자매들은 4월처럼 환한 얼굴로 잠시 그녀에게 매달렸다. 새로운 사랑이 옛 사랑을 바꾸어 놓지 않았음을 느끼면서.\n\n\"이제 가서 존의 넥타이를 매 주고, 그다음엔 서재에서 아버지와 조용히 잠깐 함께 있을 거야.\" 메그는 이 자그마한 의식들을 치르러 아래층으로 달려 내려갔고, 그러고는 어머니가 가는 곳마다 따라다녔다. 그 어머니다운 얼굴에 떠오른 미소에도 불구하고, 어머니의 가슴속에는 둥지를 떠나는 첫 새를 두고 남모를 슬픔이 감추어져 있다는 것을 의식하면서.\n\n동생들이 나란히 서서 소박한 몸단장에 마지막 손질을 하고 있는 지금이, 삼 년이라는 세월이 그들의 모습에 가져온 몇 가지 변화를 이야기하기에 좋은 때일 듯하다. 다들 지금 한창 가장 좋은 모습을 하고 있으니 말이다.\n\n조의 모난 데가 한결 부드러워졌다. 우아하지는 않을지언정 몸가짐에 여유가 생겼다. 짧은 곱슬머리는 길어져 굵은 쪽으로 틀어 올려졌는데, 큰 키 위에 얹힌 작은 머리에 한결 잘 어울렸다. 갈색 뺨에는 생기 있는 혈색이 돌고, 눈에는 부드러운 빛이 어렸으며, 오늘만큼은 그 매서운 혀에서도 다정한 말만 흘러나왔다.\n\n베스는 야위고 창백해졌으며, 그 어느 때보다 말수가 줄었다. 아름답고 다정한 두 눈은 더 커졌고, 그 안에는 보는 이를 서글프게 하는 어떤 표정이 어려 있었다. 정작 그 표정 자체가 슬픈 것은 아니었지만. 그것은 그 어린 얼굴을 그토록 애처로운 인내로 어루만지는 고통의 그림자였다. 그러나 베스는 좀처럼 불평하는 법이 없었고, 늘 \"곧 나아질 거야\"라며 희망에 차서 말하곤 했다.\n\n에이미는 사실 그대로 '집안의 꽃'으로 여겨졌다. 열여섯에 그녀는 어엿한 성인 여성의 분위기와 자태를 갖추었으니까. 미인은 아니었지만, 우아함이라 불리는 그 형언할 수 없는 매력을 지니고 있었다. 그것은 그녀의 몸매 선에서, 손의 생김새와 움직임에서, 드레스가 흘러내리는 모양에서, 머리카락이 드리워진 모양에서 드러났다. 의식하지 않은 것이면서도 조화로웠고, 많은 이들에게 미모 그 자체만큼이나 매력적이었다. 에이미의 코는 여전히 그녀의 골칫거리였으니, 도무지 그리스풍으로 자라 주지 않았던 것이다. 입도 마찬가지여서 너무 넓었고, 턱은 또렷이 도드라졌다. 이 거슬리는 이목구비가 그녀의 얼굴 전체에 개성을 부여했지만, 정작 그녀는 그것을 알아보지 못했고, 놀랍도록 흰 살결과 또렷한 푸른 눈, 그리고 그 어느 때보다 더 금빛으로 풍성해진 곱슬머리로 스스로를 위로했다.\n\n세 사람 모두 얇은 은회색 정장(여름철 가장 좋은 드레스)을 입고, 머리와 가슴에는 연분홍 장미를 달았다. 그리고 세 사람 모두 꼭 있는 그대로의 모습으로 보였다. 싱그러운 얼굴에 행복한 마음을 지닌 처녀들이, 분주한 삶 속에서 잠시 멈추어 서서 여성의 로맨스 가운데 가장 달콤한 장을 동경 어린 눈으로 읽고 있는 모습이었다.\n\n격식을 차린 절차는 일절 없을 것이었고, 모든 것이 가능한 한 자연스럽고 가정적으로 치러질 것이었다. 그래서 마치 대고모가 도착했을 때, 그녀는 그 광경에 어처구니가 없었다. 신부가 자기를 맞이하러 달려 나와 안으로 이끄는가 하면, 신랑이 떨어진 꽃 장식을 다시 매다는 모습이 눈에 들어왔고, 부친이자 목사인 이가 양팔에 포도주병을 하나씩 낀 채 근엄한 얼굴로 위층으로 올라가는 모습이 언뜻 보였으니 말이다.\n\n\"세상에, 이게 무슨 꼴이람!\" 노부인이 외쳤다. 자기를 위해 마련된 상석에 앉으며, 라벤더색 모아레 옷자락을 요란하게 바스락거리며 여미면서. \"신부는 마지막 순간까지 모습을 보여선 안 되는 거야, 얘야.\"\n\n\"전 구경거리가 아니에요, 대고모님. 게다가 누가 절 빤히 쳐다보거나, 제 드레스를 흠잡거나, 점심값을 따져 보러 오는 것도 아닌걸요. 전 누가 무슨 말을 하든 무슨 생각을 하든 신경 쓰지 않을 만큼 행복하고, 제 작은 결혼식을 제가 원하는 그대로 치를 거예요. 존, 여기 망치 있어요.\" 그러고서 메그는 '저 남자'가 하던 그 지극히 부적절한 일을 도우러 가 버렸다.\n\n브룩 씨는 \"고마워요\"라는 말조차 하지 않았지만, 그 낭만이라곤 없는 연장을 집으려 몸을 굽히면서, 접이문 뒤에서 자신의 어린 신부에게 입을 맞추었다. 그 눈빛에 마치 대고모는 손수건을 홱 꺼내 들었고, 매서운 노안에 불현듯 이슬이 맺혔다.\n\n쨍그랑 소리, 외마디 비명, 그리고 로리의 웃음소리가, \"주피터 암몬이시여! 조가 또 케이크를 엎었어요!\" 하는 무례한 외침과 함께 터져 나와 한바탕 소동이 일었다. 그 소동이 채 가라앉기도 전에 사촌들이 떼 지어 도착했고, 베스가 어렸을 때 곧잘 말하던 대로 \"손님들이 들이닥쳤다.\"\n\n\"저 젊은 거인이 내 근처엔 얼씬도 못 하게 해라. 모기보다 더 성가신 녀석이니.\" 노부인이 에이미에게 속삭였다. 방마다 사람들로 들어차고, 로리의 검은 머리가 다른 이들 위로 우뚝 솟아 있을 때였다.\n\n\"오늘은 아주 얌전히 굴겠다고 약속했고, 마음만 먹으면 더없이 우아하게 굴 수 있는 애예요.\" 에이미가 대답하며, 헤라클레스에게 그 용을 조심하라 일러 주러 슬며시 자리를 떴다. 그 경고에 로리는 도리어 노부인 곁을 떠나지 않고 어찌나 극진하게 시중을 들었던지, 노부인은 거의 정신이 다 나갈 지경이었다.\n\n신부 행렬 같은 건 없었지만, 마치 씨와 젊은 한 쌍이 푸른 아치 아래 자리를 잡자 방 안에 문득 정적이 내려앉았다. 어머니와 자매들이 메그를 떠나보내기 아쉬운 듯 바짝 모여들었다. 아버지의 목소리가 한 번 이상 갈라졌는데, 그것은 오히려 그 예식을 한층 더 아름답고 엄숙하게 만드는 것 같았다. 신랑의 손은 눈에 띄게 떨렸고, 그의 대답을 들은 사람은 아무도 없었다. 그러나 메그는 남편의 두 눈을 똑바로 올려다보며 \"네, 그러겠습니다!\"라고 말했다. 그 얼굴과 목소리에 어찌나 다정한 믿음이 담겨 있던지, 어머니의 가슴은 기쁨으로 벅찼고, 마치 대고모는 들릴 만큼 코를 훌쩍였다.\n\n조는 울지 않았다. 한 번은 울 뻔했지만 말이다. 다만 로리가 그 짓궂은 검은 눈에 익살과 감정이 우스꽝스럽게 뒤섞인 표정으로 자기를 빤히 쳐다보고 있다는 것을 의식한 덕분에 가까스로 눈물을 보이지 않을 수 있었다. 베스는 어머니의 어깨에 얼굴을 묻고 있었지만, 에이미는 우아한 조각상처럼 서 있었다. 더없이 잘 어울리는 한 줄기 햇살이 그녀의 흰 이마와 머리에 꽂은 꽃을 어루만지고 있었다.\n\n이건 도무지 격식에 맞는 일이 아니었지 싶지만, 결혼이 완전히 성립된 그 순간, 메그가 외쳤다. \"첫 입맞춤은 엄마에게!\" 그러고는 돌아서서 마음을 입술에 담아 그 입맞춤을 했다. 이어진 십오 분 동안 그녀는 그 어느 때보다 한 송이 장미처럼 보였다. 로런스 씨부터 늙은 한나에 이르기까지 모두가 저마다의 권리를 한껏 누렸기 때문이다. 한나는 무시무시하고도 신묘하게 만들어진 머리 장식으로 치장한 채, 복도에서 그녀에게 와락 달려들어 흐느낌과 너털웃음을 섞어 가며 외쳤다. \"복 받으세요, 아가씨, 백 번이라도요! 케이크는 조금도 안 상했고, 모든 게 다 사랑스럽기만 하답니다.\"\n\n그 후로 모두가 마음을 가다듬고는, 무언가 재치 있는 말을 하거나, 적어도 하려고 애썼는데, 그것만으로도 충분했다. 마음이 가벼울 때면 웃음은 언제든 터져 나올 채비가 되어 있는 법이니까. 선물을 늘어놓는 일은 없었다. 이미 다 그 작은 집에 가 있었으니까. 거창한 아침 식사도 없었고, 다만 꽃으로 장식한 케이크와 과일을 푸짐하게 차린 점심이 있었다. 물과 레모네이드와 커피가 세 명의 헤베가 들고 도는 유일한 종류의 음료임이 드러나자, 로런스 씨와 마치 대고모는 서로를 보며 어깨를 으쓱하고 미소 지었다. 그러나 아무도 아무 말 하지 않았다. 신부 시중을 한사코 들겠다던 로리가 잔뜩 채운 쟁반을 손에 들고 어리둥절한 표정으로 그녀 앞에 나타나기 전까지는.\n헤베: 그리스 신화에서 신들에게 음료를 따라 주던 청춘의 여신.\n\n\"조가 실수로 병을 죄다 깨뜨린 거예요?\" 그가 속삭였다. \"아니면 오늘 아침 여기저기 병이 굴러다니는 걸 봤다고 제가 그냥 착각하는 건가요?\"\n\n\"아니야. 너희 할아버지께서 친절하게도 가장 좋은 걸 내주셨고, 마치 대고모님도 실제로 좀 보내 주셨어. 하지만 아버지가 베스를 위해 조금 따로 챙겨 두시고는, 나머지는 상이용사 요양소로 보내셨지. 알다시피 아버지는 포도주란 병이 났을 때만 써야 한다고 생각하시거든. 그리고 엄마는, 당신도 당신 딸들도 이 지붕 아래에서는 어떤 젊은이에게도 결코 술을 권하지 않을 거라고 하셨어.\"\n\n메그는 진지하게 말하며, 로리가 인상을 찌푸리거나 웃음을 터뜨리리라 예상했다. 그러나 그는 둘 중 어느 것도 하지 않았다. 그녀를 흘끗 보더니, 그 특유의 충동적인 투로 말했다. \"그거 마음에 드네요! 저도 그 폐해를 충분히 봐 와서, 다른 여자들도 부인처럼 생각해 주었으면 싶거든요.\"\n\n\"너는 경험으로 그걸 깨우친 건 아니길 바라.\" 메그의 목소리에 근심 어린 가락이 묻어났다.\n\n\"아니에요. 맹세코 그건 아니에요. 그렇다고 저를 너무 좋게 보지도 마세요. 이건 제 유혹거리 축에도 못 끼니까요. 포도주가 물만큼이나 흔하고, 또 물만큼이나 해롭지 않은 곳에서 자라다 보니, 저는 거기에 별 관심이 없어요. 다만 예쁜 아가씨가 권할 때는, 보다시피 거절하기가 좀 그렇잖아요.\"\n\n\"하지만 너는 거절할 거야. 네 자신을 위해서가 아니라면 다른 사람들을 위해서라도. 자, 로리, 약속해. 그래서 오늘을 내 인생에서 가장 행복한 날이라 부를 이유를 하나 더 만들어 줘.\"\n\n이토록 갑작스럽고도 진지한 요구에 젊은이는 잠시 망설였다. 조롱은 종종 극기보다 견디기가 더 힘든 법이니까. 메그는 만일 그가 약속한다면 무슨 일이 있어도 그것을 지키리라는 것을 알았기에, 자신의 힘을 느끼며 그것을 한 여인이 벗의 이로움을 위해 쓸 법한 방식으로 썼다. 그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 다만 행복으로 한껏 웅변적이 된 얼굴로 그를 올려다보았고, \"오늘은 누구도 내 청을 거절할 수 없어요\"라고 말하는 듯한 미소를 지었다. 로리는 분명 거절할 수 없었다. 화답하는 미소와 함께 그는 그녀에게 손을 내밀며 진심으로 말했다. \"약속할게요, 브룩 부인!\"\n\n\"정말, 정말 고마워.\"\n\n\"그럼 나는 '네 결심이여 만수무강하라'를 위해 건배하겠어, 테디.\" 조가 외치며, 잔을 흔들어 레모네이드를 한 줄기 끼얹어 그에게 세례를 베풀고는, 흐뭇하게 그를 향해 환히 웃었다.\n\n그리하여 건배가 이루어졌고, 맹세가 세워졌으며, 숱한 유혹에도 불구하고 충실히 지켜졌다. 본능적인 지혜로 소녀들이 행복한 한순간을 붙들어 벗에게 봉사를 베푼 것이었고, 그는 평생토록 그들에게 감사했다.\n\n점심 식사 후 사람들은 둘씩 셋씩 짝지어 집과 정원을 거닐며, 안팎의 햇살을 만끽했다. 메그와 존이 마침 잔디밭 한가운데 함께 서 있을 때, 로리에게 영감이 떠올랐고, 그것이 이 격식 없는 결혼식에 마지막 화룡점정을 찍었다.\n\n\"기혼자들은 모두 손을 잡고 갓 부부가 된 신랑 신부를 둘러싸고 빙빙 돌아요, 독일 사람들이 하듯이요. 그동안 우리 미혼 총각 처녀들은 바깥에서 짝지어 깡충깡충 추는 거예요!\" 로리가 외치며 에이미와 함께 길을 따라 거니는데, 그 흥과 솜씨가 어찌나 사람을 끌어들이던지 다른 이들도 군말 없이 그들을 따라 했다. 마치 부부와 캐럴 이모 내외가 먼저 시작했고, 다른 이들이 빠르게 합류했다. 샐리 모팻조차 잠시 머뭇거리더니 옷자락을 팔에 척 걸치고는 네드를 휙 끌어들여 원 안으로 들어왔다. 그러나 가장 압권은 로런스 씨와 마치 대고모였다. 그 위엄 있는 노신사가 근엄하게 미끄러지듯 스텝을 밟으며 노부인에게 다가가자, 그녀가 지팡이를 팔 밑에 척 끼우고는 경쾌하게 깡충깡충 뛰어가 다른 이들과 손을 맞잡고 신랑 신부를 둘러싸고 춤을 추었으니. 그러는 동안 젊은이들은 한여름의 나비처럼 정원을 누비고 다녔다.\n\n숨이 차오르자 이 즉흥 무도회는 막을 내렸고, 그러고 나서 사람들이 하나둘 돌아가기 시작했다.\n\n\"잘 살길 바라, 얘야, 진심으로 잘 살길 바란다. 하지만 내 생각엔 네가 후회하게 될 것 같구나.\" 마치 대고모가 메그에게 말했다. 그러고는 신랑이 자기를 마차로 안내하자 그에게 덧붙였다. \"자네는 보물을 얻은 거네, 젊은이. 그 보물에 걸맞은 사람이 되도록 하게.\"\n\n\"이렇게 사랑스러운 결혼식은 정말 오랜만에 가 보네요, 네드. 왜 그런지는 모르겠어요. 격식이라곤 눈곱만치도 없었는데 말이에요.\" 모팻 부인이 마차를 몰고 떠나며 남편에게 말했다.\n\n\"로리, 얘야, 네가 언젠가 이런 걸 한번 누려 보고 싶거든, 저 어린 아가씨들 중 하나에게 도와 달라고 하렴. 그러면 나는 더없이 흡족할 거다.\" 로런스 씨가 말했다. 아침나절의 흥분이 지나고 안락의자에 몸을 묻고 쉬면서.\n\n\"최선을 다해 할아버지를 흡족하게 해 드리지요.\" 로리가 평소답지 않게 고분고분 대답했다. 그러면서 조가 자기 단춧구멍에 꽂아 준 꽃을 조심스레 빼냈다.\n\n그 작은 집은 멀지 않았고, 메그에게 주어진 유일한 신혼여행이라곤 옛집에서 새집까지 존과 함께 걷는 조용한 산책뿐이었다. 그녀가 비둘기색 정장 차림에 흰 끈으로 묶은 밀짚 보닛을 쓰고, 어여쁜 퀘이커 교도처럼 보이는 모습으로 내려오자, 모두가 그녀 주위로 모여들어 \"잘 가\"라는 인사를 건넸다. 마치 그녀가 세계 일주 대여행이라도 떠나는 양 다정하게.\n\n\"제가 엄마에게서 떨어져 나간다고 느끼지 마세요, 사랑하는 엄마. 존을 이렇게 많이 사랑한다고 해서 엄마를 조금이라도 덜 사랑하는 거라고도 생각하지 마시고요.\" 그녀가 눈물 그렁한 눈으로 어머니에게 잠시 매달리며 말했다. \"매일 올 거예요, 아버지. 그리고 비록 결혼은 했지만, 여러분 모두의 마음속에서 제 옛 자리를 그대로 지킬 작정이에요. 베스가 저랑 자주 함께 지낼 거고, 다른 동생들도 이따금 들러서 제 살림 분투기를 보며 웃어 주겠지요. 다들 제게 행복한 결혼식을 만들어 줘서 고마워요. 잘 있어요, 잘 있어요!\"\n\n그들은 사랑과 희망과 다정한 자랑스러움이 가득한 얼굴로 그녀를 지켜보며 서 있었다. 그녀가 남편의 팔에 기대어, 두 손 가득 꽃을 들고, 6월의 햇살이 그 행복한 얼굴을 환히 비추는 가운데 걸어가는 모습을. 그리하여 메그의 결혼 생활이 시작되었다.\n스물여섯\n\n# ARTISTIC ATTEMPTS.\n\n예술적 시도\n사람들이 재능과 천재의 차이를 깨닫는 데는 오랜 시간이 걸린다. 특히 야심 찬 젊은 남녀가 그러하다. 에이미는 숱한 시련을 겪으며 이 차이를 배워 나가고 있었다. 열정을 영감으로 착각한 나머지, 그녀는 젊은이다운 대담함으로 미술의 온갖 분야에 손을 댔던 것이다. 한동안은 '진흙 파이' 사업이 잠잠했고, 그녀는 더없이 섬세한 펜화에 몰두했다. 거기서 그녀는 어찌나 안목과 솜씨를 발휘했던지, 그 우아한 작품들은 즐거움이자 돈벌이가 되어 주었다. 그러나 눈을 너무 혹사한 탓에 펜과 잉크는 이내 밀쳐 두고, 인두 그림이라는 대담한 시도에 나서게 되었다. 이 발작이 이어지는 동안 온 가족은 불이 날까 봐 끊임없이 마음을 졸였다. 나무 타는 냄새가 시도 때도 없이 집 안에 진동했고, 다락방과 헛간에서 연기가 놀랍도록 자주 피어올랐으며, 시뻘겋게 달군 인두가 여기저기 어지러이 놓여 있었고, 한나는 불이 날까 봐 물 한 동이와 저녁 식사용 종을 문가에 두지 않고서는 결코 잠자리에 들지 않았다. 라파엘로의 얼굴이 반죽판 밑면에 대담하게 새겨진 채 발견되었고, 맥주 통 머리에는 바쿠스가, 설탕 통 뚜껑에는 노래하는 천사가 그려져 있었으며, 로미오와 줄리엣을 그리려던 시도들은 한동안 불쏘시개 노릇을 했다.\n\n불에 덴 손가락으로서는 불에서 기름으로 옮겨 가는 게 자연스러운 수순이었고, 에이미는 식지 않은 열정으로 유화에 빠져들었다. 화가인 친구 하나가 자신이 쓰던 팔레트와 붓과 물감을 그녀에게 마련해 주었고, 그녀는 마구 칠해 댔다. 그렇게 해서 땅에서도 바다에서도 결코 본 적 없는 목가화와 바다 그림을 만들어 냈다. 그녀가 그린 소 따위의 괴물들은 농업 박람회에서 상을 탔을 법했고, 위태롭게 출렁이는 그녀의 배들은 아무리 뱃멀미에 이골이 난 관객이라도 멀미를 일으켰을 법했다. 다만 알려진 모든 조선과 삭구의 법칙을 깡그리 무시한 탓에 첫눈에 보는 이가 웃음을 터뜨리지만 않았다면 말이다. 화실 한 귀퉁이에서 당신을 빤히 쳐다보는 가무잡잡한 소년들과 검은 눈의 성모들은 무리요를 떠올리게 했고, 엉뚱한 자리에 섬뜩한 줄무늬가 들어간 기름진 갈색의 얼굴 그림자들은 렘브란트를 뜻했으며, 풍만한 여인들과 부종 걸린 아기들은 루벤스를, 그리고 터너는 푸른 천둥과 주황색 번개와 갈색 비와 보랏빛 구름의 폭풍 속에 등장했는데, 그 한가운데 토마토색 얼룩이 하나 있어서, 보는 사람 마음대로 그것은 태양일 수도 부표일 수도, 선원의 셔츠일 수도 왕의 예복일 수도 있었다.\n\n다음은 목탄 초상화 차례였다. 온 가족이 한 줄로 죽 걸렸는데, 방금 석탄 통에서 불려 나온 것처럼 거칠고 검댕투성이로 보였다. 크레용 스케치로 부드럽게 하니 한결 나았다. 닮은 구석이 제법 있어서, 에이미의 머리카락, 조의 코, 메그의 입, 로리의 눈은 \"놀랍도록 잘 그렸다\"는 평을 받았다. 이어서 점토와 석고로 돌아왔고, 지인들의 으스스한 본뜬 상이 집 안 구석구석에 출몰하거나, 벽장 선반에서 굴러떨어져 사람들 머리 위로 떨어지곤 했다. 아이들이 모델로 꾀어 들여졌는데, 급기야 그녀가 벌이는 알 수 없는 짓에 대한 아이들의 횡설수설한 설명 탓에 에이미 양은 어린 식인귀 취급을 받기에 이르렀다. 그러나 이 방면에서의 그녀의 노력은 한 가지 불운한 사고로 별안간 막을 내렸고, 그 사고가 그녀의 열정을 꺼뜨렸다. 한동안 다른 모델들이 구해지지 않자, 그녀는 자기 자신의 예쁜 발을 본뜨는 일에 나섰다. 그러던 어느 날, 가족은 섬뜩한 쿵쿵 소리와 비명에 깜짝 놀랐고, 구하러 달려가 보니,\n헛간 안을 정신없이 폴짝거리는 어린 예술가의 발이, 예상보다 훨씬 빨리 굳어 버린 석고가 가득한 냄비 속에 꼼짝없이 박혀 있었다.\n\n상당한 어려움과 약간의 위험을 무릅쓰고 나서야 에이미는 그 속에서 파내어졌다. 조가 발을 파내는 동안 웃음을 참지 못한 탓에 칼이 너무 깊이 들어가, 가엾은 발을 베어 적어도 한 번의 예술적 시도에 길이 남을 기념물을 새겨 놓고 만 것이었다.\n그 일이 있은 뒤로 에이미는 한동안 잠잠했다. 그러다 자연을 사생하고 싶다는 열병에 사로잡혀, 그림 같은 풍경을 찾아 강과 들판과 숲을 헤매고, 베껴 그릴 폐허가 없다며 한숨짓기 시작했다. 돌멩이 하나, 그루터기 하나, 버섯 하나, 부러진 우단담배풀 줄기로 이루어진 \"기막히게 멋진 한 폭\"을 화첩에 담겠다고 축축한 풀밭에 앉아 있다가 끝없이 감기에 걸렸고, 다 그려 놓고 보면 깃털 이불을 죽 늘어놓은 것처럼 보이는 \"천상의 구름 떼\"를 그리겠다고도 했다. 빛과 그림자를 연구한답시고 한여름 뙤약볕 아래 강에 떠 있느라 고운 피부를 망쳤고, \"시점(視點)\"인지 뭔지, 실눈을 뜨고 실로 재는 그 일을 하느라 콧잔등에 주름이 잡혔다.\n\n미켈란젤로의 말마따나 \"천재란 영원한 인내\"라면, 에이미에게도 그 신성한 자질에 대한 권리가 어느 정도는 있었다. 온갖 장애와 실패, 좌절에도 굴하지 않고 끈질기게 매달리며, 언젠가는 \"고매한 예술\"이라 불릴 만한 무언가를 해내리라 굳게 믿었으니 말이다.\n\n그러는 동안에도 에이미는 다른 여러 가지를 배우고, 해내고, 즐겼다. 위대한 예술가가 되지 못하더라도 매력 있고 교양 있는 여인이 되겠다고 마음먹은 까닭이었다. 이 점에서는 더 잘 해냈다. 에이미는 애쓰지 않고도 사람들을 기쁘게 하고, 어디서든 친구를 사귀며, 인생을 더없이 우아하고 손쉽게 헤쳐 나가는, 행복하게 태어난 부류 가운데 하나여서, 덜 운 좋은 이들은 그런 사람은 행운의 별 아래 태어난 게 분명하다고 믿고 싶어질 정도였다.\n모두가 에이미를 좋아했다. 그녀가 지닌 여러 장점 가운데 하나가 바로 눈치였기 때문이다. 무엇이 사람을 기쁘게 하고 무엇이 알맞은지를 본능적으로 알아챘고, 늘 알맞은 사람에게 알맞은 말을 했으며, 때와 장소에 꼭 맞게 행동했고, 어찌나 침착한지 언니들이 이런 말을 할 정도였다. \"에이미는 미리 연습 한 번 안 하고 궁궐에 가도 어떻게 처신해야 할지 정확히 알 거야.\"\n\n에이미의 약점 가운데 하나는 \"우리네 상류 사회\"에서 어울리고 싶어 한다는 것이었는데, 정작 진정한 상류가 무엇인지는 잘 알지 못했다. 돈과 지위, 세련된 교양과 우아한 몸가짐이 그녀의 눈에는 가장 탐나는 것들이었고, 그런 것을 지닌 이들과 어울리기를 좋아한 나머지, 가짜를 진짜로 착각하고 감탄할 만하지 않은 것에 감탄하는 일도 잦았다. 태생이 귀부인이라는 사실을 결코 잊지 않았기에, 귀족다운 취향과 감성을 가다듬어, 기회가 오면 지금은 가난이 가로막고 있는 그 자리를 차지할 준비가 되어 있고자 했다.\n\n친구들이 부르듯 \"우리 마님\"은 진심으로 진정한 숙녀가 되기를 바랐고, 마음속으로는 이미 그러했지만, 돈으로 천성의 우아함을 살 수 없다는 것, 지위가 늘 고귀함을 부여하지는 않는다는 것, 그리고 참된 교양은 외형적 결점에도 불구하고 저절로 드러난다는 것은 아직 배워야 할 숙제였다.\n\n\"엄마, 부탁드릴 일이 있어요.\" 어느 날 에이미가 자못 진지한 태도로 들어서며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 우리 막내, 뭔데?\" 어머니가 대답했다. 어머니의 눈에는 이 의젓한 아가씨가 여전히 \"갓난아기\"로 남아 있었다.\n\n\"우리 그림 수업이 다음 주에 끝나는데요, 여자애들이 여름 동안 흩어지기 전에 하루 여기로 초대하고 싶어요. 다들 강을 보고, 부서진 다리를 사생하고, 제 화첩에서 마음에 드는 걸 베껴 그리고 싶어 안달이거든요. 그 애들이 여러모로 저한테 참 잘해 줬어요. 다들 부자고 제가 가난한 걸 알면서도 전혀 차별하지 않았으니, 고마운 마음이에요.\"\n\n\"왜 차별을 하겠니?\" 마치 부인은 딸들이 \"마리아 테레지아 같은 위엄\"이라 부르는 태도로 그렇게 물었다.\n\n\"거의 누구한테나 차별이라는 게 정말 있다는 건 엄마도 저만큼 잘 아시잖아요. 그러니까 새끼 닭들이 더 영악한 새들한테 쪼였다고 다정한 어미 닭처럼 깃털을 곤두세우진 마세요. 미운 오리 새끼도 백조가 됐잖아요.\" 에이미는 모진 데 없이 웃었다. 천성이 밝고 희망에 찬 영혼이었던 것이다.\n\n마치 부인은 웃으며 모성의 자부심을 다독이고는 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 우리 백조님, 계획이 뭐지?\"\n\n\"다음 주에 그 애들을 점심에 초대하고, 보고 싶어 하는 곳들로 마차 산책도 시켜 주고, 어쩌면 강에서 뱃놀이도 하면서 작은 예술 축제를 열어 주고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"그럴듯하구나. 점심은 뭘 차릴 거니? 케이크에 샌드위치, 과일하고 커피면 충분하지 않을까?\"\n\n\"어머, 안 돼요! 차가운 소 혀 요리에 닭고기, 거기다 프랑스식 초콜릿에 아이스크림까지 있어야죠. 그 애들은 그런 것에 익숙하단 말이에요. 비록 제가 생계를 위해 일하는 처지지만, 점심만큼은 격식 있고 우아하게 차리고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"아가씨가 몇이나 되는데?\" 어머니가 차츰 표정이 굳어지며 물었다.\n\n\"수업엔 열둘이나 열네 명쯤 있는데, 다 오진 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"세상에, 얘야, 그 애들을 다 실어 나르려면 합승마차라도 한 대 빌려야겠다.\"\n\n\"엄마도 참, 어쩜 그런 생각을 다 하세요? 많아야 예닐곱 명쯤 올 테니까, 전 바닷가용 마차를 한 대 빌리고, 로렌스 씨네 '체리바운스'를 빌릴 거예요.\" (해나가 '샤라방(char-à-banc)'을 그렇게 발음했다.)\n\n\"그러면 다 돈이 들 텐데, 에이미.\"\n\n\"그렇게 많이 들진 않아요. 비용은 다 계산해 뒀고, 제가 직접 낼 거예요.\"\n\n\"얘야, 그 애들은 그런 것에 익숙하니까 우리가 아무리 잘 차려 봐야 새로울 게 없을 텐데, 그럴 바엔 좀 더 소박한 계획이 그 애들한테도 색다른 즐거움이 되지 않겠니? 필요도 없는 걸 사거나 빌리고 우리 형편에 맞지도 않는 격식을 흉내 내는 것보다, 우리한테도 훨씬 나을 테고 말이야.\"\n\n\"제 뜻대로 못 할 거면 차라리 안 하는 게 나아요. 엄마랑 언니들이 조금만 도와주면 완벽하게 해낼 수 있어요. 제가 돈을 내겠다는데 못 할 게 뭐 있어요.\" 에이미가 단호하게 말했다. 반대에 부딪히면 그 단호함이 곧잘 고집으로 바뀌곤 했다.\n\n마치 부인은 경험이 더없이 훌륭한 스승임을 알고 있었기에, 가능한 경우에는 아이들이 스스로 교훈을 깨치게 내버려 두었다. 기꺼이 그 길을 쉽게 만들어 주고 싶었지만, 아이들은 마치 설사약이나 센나 차를 거부하듯 충고 듣기를 마다했던 것이다.\n\n\"좋아, 에이미. 정 마음을 굳혔고, 돈과 시간과 기분을 너무 많이 쏟지 않고 해낼 길이 보인다면 더는 말리지 않으마. 친구들이랑 의논해 보렴. 어느 쪽으로 결정하든 내가 힘껏 도와줄게.\"\n\n\"고마워요, 엄마. 엄마는 언제나 너무너무 다정해요.\" 그러고는 에이미가 언니들에게 계획을 알리러 쪼르르 달려갔다.\n\n메그는 곧장 찬성하며 도와주겠다고 약속했고, 자기 작은 집부터 가장 좋은 소금 숟가락까지 가진 것이라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 내놓겠다고 했다. 하지만 조는 그 계획 전체를 못마땅해하며 처음에는 거들떠보지도 않으려 했다.\n\n\"도대체 왜 너한테 동전 한 닢어치도 관심 없는 계집애들 무리를 위해서 돈을 쓰고, 식구들을 들볶고, 집 안을 발칵 뒤집어 놓겠다는 거야? 난 네가 자존심도 분별도 넘쳐서, 프랑스제 부츠나 신고 사륜마차나 타고 다닌다고 어떤 여자한테든 굽실거릴 애는 아니라고 생각했는데.\" 소설의 비극적 절정에서 불려 나온 조는 사교 행사 같은 데 가장 어울리지 않는 기분이었다.\n\n\"난 굽실거리지 않아. 그리고 언니만큼이나 나도 동정받는 거 싫어!\" 에이미가 발끈하며 받아쳤다. 이런 문제가 나올 때면 둘은 여전히 티격태격했다. \"그 애들은 정말 날 좋아하고 나도 그 애들을 좋아해. 언니가 유행 좇는 헛짓이라고 부르는 거 말고도, 그 애들 사이엔 친절과 분별과 재능이 가득하다고. 언니는 사람들이 자기를 좋아하게 만들거나, 좋은 사교계에 드나들거나, 몸가짐과 취향을 가다듬는 일엔 관심이 없잖아. 난 관심 있고, 다가오는 기회를 하나도 빠짐없이 살릴 거야. 언니야 팔꿈치를 내밀고 코를 치켜든 채 세상을 헤쳐 나가면서 그걸 독립이라고 부르고 싶으면 그러든가. 그건 내 방식이 아니야.\"\n\n에이미가 혀를 벼리고 속마음을 거침없이 쏟아 낼 때면 대개 그녀가 이겼다. 상식이 그녀 편에 서 있는 일이 좀처럼 빠지지 않았던 반면, 조는 자유에 대한 사랑과 인습에 대한 미움을 워낙 끝없이 밀어붙이는 통에 자연히 말다툼에서 밀리곤 했다. 조의 독립관에 대한 에이미의 정의가 어찌나 정곡을 찔렀던지, 둘은 그만 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 논쟁은 한결 누그러진 방향으로 흘러갔다. 내키지 않았지만 조도 마침내 하루쯤 세상의 눈치(그런디 부인)에 양보하여, 자기가 \"말도 안 되는 일\"이라 여기던 그 일을 동생이 해내도록 돕기로 했다.\n* 그런디 부인 : 영국에서 '세상의 평판·체면'을 빗대는 가공의 인물명.\n\n초대장이 보내졌고, 거의 모두가 수락했으며, 다음 월요일이 그 성대한 행사를 위한 날로 정해졌다. 해나는 한 주의 일과가 뒤죽박죽이 되어 심기가 불편했고, \"빨래와 다림질을 제때 안 하면 어디서든 되는 일이 하나도 없을 거\"라고 예언했다. 가사라는 기계의 태엽에 생긴 이 차질은 일 전체에 나쁜 영향을 미쳤다. 하지만 에이미의 좌우명은 \"절대 절망하지 말라\"였고, 무엇을 할지 마음을 정한 이상 온갖 장애를 무릅쓰고 밀고 나갔다.\n우선 해나의 요리부터 잘되지 않았다. 닭고기는 질겼고, 소 혀는 너무 짰으며, 초콜릿은 거품이 제대로 일지 않았다. 게다가 케이크와 얼음이 에이미가 예상한 것보다 비쌌고, 마차도 마찬가지였으며, 처음엔 사소해 보이던 이런저런 비용이 나중에 합쳐 보니 꽤 아찔할 만큼 불어났다. 베스는 감기에 걸려 자리에 누웠고, 메그는 유난히 손님이 많이 찾아와 집을 비울 수 없었으며, 조는 마음이 너무 갈팡질팡한 나머지 깨뜨리고 사고 치고 실수하는 일이 유난히 잦고 심각하고 골치 아팠다.\n\n\"엄마가 아니었으면 난 절대 못 해냈을 거야.\" 에이미는 나중에 이렇게 단언했고, 다른 식구들 모두가 \"그해 최고의 우스개\"를 까맣게 잊은 뒤에도 고마운 마음으로 그 일을 기억했다.\n\n월요일에 날이 좋지 않으면 아가씨들은 화요일에 오기로 했는데, 이 약속은 조와 해나를 더할 나위 없이 짜증 나게 했다. 월요일 아침, 날씨는 줄기차게 쏟아붓는 비보다 더 사람 속을 뒤집어 놓는 그 어정쩡한 상태였다. 조금 부슬거리다 조금 갰다가 조금 바람이 불더니, 누구든 마음을 정하기엔 이미 너무 늦은 뒤에야 겨우 마음을 정했다. 에이미는 동틀 녘에 일어나, 집을 정돈하려고 식구들을 침대에서 끌어내고 아침을 후딱 먹게 다그쳤다. 거실이 유난히 초라해 보였지만, 없는 것을 한탄하느라 멈춰 서지 않고, 있는 것을 솜씨껏 최대한 활용했다. 카펫에서 닳은 자리에는 의자를 놓아 가리고, 벽의 얼룩은 담쟁이덩굴로 액자 두른 그림으로 덮었으며, 빈 구석은 손수 만든 조각상으로 채워 방에 예술적인 분위기를 더했다. 조가 여기저기 흩어 놓은 사랑스러운 꽃병들도 한몫했다.\n\n점심상은 보기에 멋졌다. 에이미는 상을 둘러보며 맛도 좋기를, 그리고 빌려 온 유리잔과 도자기와 은식기가 무사히 제집으로 돌아가기를 진심으로 빌었다. 마차도 약속받았고, 메그와 어머니는 손님 맞을 채비를 마쳤으며, 베스는 무대 뒤에서 해나를 거들 수 있을 만했고, 조는 정신이 다른 데 가 있고 머리가 지끈거리고 모든 사람과 모든 일이 영 못마땅한 와중에도 허락하는 한껏 활기차고 상냥하게 굴겠다고 약속해 둔 터였다. 지친 몸으로 옷을 차려입으면서 에이미는, 점심이 무사히 끝나고 친구들과 마차를 몰고 나가 예술적 기쁨으로 가득한 오후를 보낼 그 행복한 순간을 그리며 스스로를 다독였다. \"체리바운스\"와 부서진 다리야말로 그녀가 자신 있게 내세우는 부분이었으니 말이다.\n\n이어 두 시간 동안의 초조한 기다림이 찾아왔고, 그동안 에이미는 거실과 현관을 오가며 마음을 졸였으며, 여론은 바람개비처럼 변덕을 부렸다. 열한 시에 한차례 세차게 퍼부은 소나기가, 열두 시에 오기로 한 아가씨들의 열정을 분명히 식혀 버린 듯, 아무도 오지 않았다. 그리고 두 시가 되자 기진맥진한 식구들은 쨍쨍 내리쬐는 햇살 속에 둘러앉아, 무엇 하나 버려지지 않도록 잔치 음식 가운데 상하기 쉬운 것들을 먹어 치웠다.\n\n\"오늘은 날씨가 의심할 여지 없잖아. 그 애들은 틀림없이 올 테니까, 우리 서둘러서 맞을 준비를 해야 해.\" 이튿날 아침 햇살에 잠을 깬 에이미가 말했다. 말투는 씩씩했지만, 속으로는 화요일 얘기를 꺼내지 말걸 그랬다고 후회하고 있었다. 케이크처럼 그녀의 흥미도 조금씩 김이 빠지고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"바닷가재를 도무지 못 구하겠구나. 그러니 오늘은 샐러드 없이 지내야겠다.\" 삼십 분쯤 뒤에 들어온 마치 씨가 차분한 체념의 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 닭고기를 쓰면 되죠. 샐러드에 넣으면 질긴 건 상관없으니까요.\" 부인이 일러 주었다.\n\n\"해나가 잠깐 부엌 식탁에 올려놨더니 새끼 고양이들이 손을 댔지 뭐야. 정말 미안해, 에이미.\" 여전히 고양이들의 후원자인 베스가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그럼 꼭 바닷가재를 구해야 해. 소 혀만으론 안 되니까.\" 에이미가 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"내가 읍내로 달려가서 한 마리 구해 올까?\" 조가 순교자 같은 도량을 보이며 물었다.\n\n\"언니는 날 약 올리려고 종이에 싸지도 않은 채 그냥 팔에 끼고 들고 올걸. 내가 직접 갈래.\" 에이미가 대답했다. 그녀의 인내심도 슬슬 바닥나기 시작했다.\n\n두꺼운 베일을 둘러쓰고 점잖은 여행용 바구니로 무장한 에이미는 길을 나섰다. 시원한 마차 산책이 헝클어진 마음을 달래 주고 그날의 고된 일을 견딜 채비를 해 주리라 여기면서. 다소 지체된 끝에 원하던 물건을 손에 넣었고, 집에서 시간을 더 허비하지 않도록 드레싱 한 병도 함께 사서, 자기 선견지명에 흐뭇해하며 다시 마차를 몰고 떠났다.\n\n합승마차에는 졸고 있는 노부인 한 사람뿐이었으므로, 에이미는 베일을 주머니에 넣고, 그 많은 돈이 다 어디로 갔는지 따져 보며 길의 지루함을 달랬다. 좀처럼 말을 안 듣는 숫자가 빼곡한 카드에 정신이 팔린 나머지, 마차를 세우지도 않고 올라탄 새 승객을 알아채지 못했는데, 남자 목소리가 \"안녕하세요, 마치 양\"이라고 하기에 고개를 들어 보니, 로리의 대학 친구 가운데 가장 우아한 축에 드는 사람이었다. 그가 자기보다 먼저 내리기를 간절히 빌면서, 에이미는 발치의 바구니를 철저히 못 본 척했고, 새 여행용 드레스를 입고 있다는 데 스스로를 다행스럽게 여기며, 평소의 상냥함과 활기로 청년의 인사에 답했다.\n\n둘은 더없이 잘 어울려 이야기를 나누었다. 그 신사가 먼저 내릴 것임을 알게 되자 에이미의 가장 큰 걱정거리가 이내 가라앉았고, 그녀가 유난히 도도한 어조로 한창 수다를 떨고 있을 때 노부인이 내렸다. 문 쪽으로 비틀거리며 가던 노부인이 그만 바구니를 엎고 말았으니 — 아, 끔찍해라! — 그 천박할 만큼 크고 번지르르한 바닷가재가 튜더라는 귀하신 분의 눈앞에 고스란히 드러나고 말았다.\n\n\"저런, 저분이 저녁거리를 두고 내리셨네요!\" 영문을 모르는 청년이 외치며, 지팡이로 그 새빨간 괴물을 제자리에 쑤셔 넣고는, 노부인을 뒤따라 바구니를 건네주려 했다.\n\n\"제발 그러지 마세요. 그건… 그건 제 거예요.\" 에이미가 제 생선만큼이나 새빨개진 얼굴로 우물거렸다.\n\n\"아, 이런, 실례했습니다. 아주 굉장한 놈이네요, 그렇죠?\" 튜더가 침착하게, 그리고 그의 교양을 빛내 주는 진지한 관심의 표정으로 말했다.\n\n에이미는 한 호흡 만에 평정을 되찾고, 보란 듯이 바구니를 좌석에 올려놓더니 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"이놈으로 만들 샐러드를 좀 맛보고, 그걸 먹을 매력적인 아가씨들도 보고 싶지 않으세요?\"\n\n이거야말로 눈치였다. 남자 마음을 좌우하는 두 가지 약점을 건드렸으니 말이다. 바닷가재는 순식간에 흐뭇한 추억의 후광에 둘러싸였고, \"매력적인 아가씨들\"에 대한 호기심이 그의 마음을 그 우스꽝스러운 사고에서 딴 데로 돌려놓았다.\n\n'저 사람이 로리한테 이 얘길 하면서 웃고 농담을 하겠지. 하지만 난 그 자리에 없을 테니, 그나마 다행이야.' 튜더가 인사하고 떠나는 동안 에이미는 그렇게 생각했다.\n\n집에서는 이 만남을 입에 올리지 않았다(다만 바구니가 엎어진 탓에 드레싱이 도랑처럼 치맛자락을 타고 흘러내려 새 드레스가 몹시 망가졌다는 건 알아챘다). 그저 이제는 어제보다 더 성가시게 느껴지는 준비를 마저 해 나갔고, 열두 시가 되자 모든 게 다시 채비되었다. 이웃들이 자기 거동에 관심을 두고 있다고 느낀 에이미는 어제의 실패를 오늘의 대성공으로 지워 버리고 싶었다. 그래서 \"체리바운스\"를 시켜 위풍당당하게 손님들을 마중하러, 잔치에 모셔 오려고 마차를 몰고 떠났다.\n\n\"덜컹거리는 소리야, 오나 봐! 내가 현관에 나가서 맞아야겠다. 그래야 손님 대접하는 티가 나지. 그 가엾은 애가 그 고생을 했으니 부디 즐거운 시간을 보냈으면 좋겠구나.\" 마치 부인이 말하며 곧장 그렇게 행동에 옮겼다. 그런데 한 번 흘긋 보고는 무어라 형용할 수 없는 표정으로 도로 들어왔으니, 그 커다란 마차 안에 영 어색해 보이게 앉아 있는 건 에이미와 아가씨 단 한 명뿐이었다.\n\n\"베스, 어서 가서 해나가 식탁에서 음식 절반을 치우는 거 좀 도와! 계집애 하나 앞에 열둘이 먹을 점심을 떡하니 차려 놓는 것만큼 우스운 일도 없잖아.\" 조가 외치며 웃음 한 번 참지 못할 만큼 들떠 아래층으로 후다닥 내려갔다.\n\n에이미가 들어왔다. 아주 침착했고, 약속을 지킨 단 한 명의 손님에게 더없이 다정했다. 극적인 기질을 타고난 나머지 식구들도 저마다 제 역할을 똑같이 잘 해냈고, 엘리엇 양은 이 집 사람들이 무척이나 유쾌한 무리라고 여겼다. 그들을 사로잡은 웃음기를 도무지 완전히 다스릴 수가 없었던 것이다. 새로 차린 점심을 즐겁게 나눠 먹고, 작업실과 정원을 둘러보고, 예술을 열정적으로 논한 뒤, 에이미는 이륜마차를 부르고는(아아, 우아한 체리바운스는 어디로!) 해 질 무렵까지 친구를 태우고 동네를 조용히 돌았다. 그러고 나서 \"손님이 돌아갔다.\"\n\n몹시 지쳐 보였지만 여느 때처럼 침착한 모습으로 걸어 들어온 에이미는, 그 불운한 축제의 흔적이 죄다 사라지고 없다는 걸 알아챘다. 다만 조의 입가에 수상쩍게 잡혀 있는 주름만은 빼고.\n\n\"오늘 마차 산책하기 참 좋은 오후였겠구나, 얘야.\" 어머니가 마치 열둘이 다 온 것처럼 정중하게 말했다.\n\n\"엘리엇 양은 참 상냥한 아가씨더라. 보니까 꽤 즐거워하는 것 같던데.\" 베스가 평소답지 않게 다정한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"네 케이크 좀 나눠 줄 수 있겠니? 사실 좀 필요해. 손님이 워낙 많은데, 난 네 것처럼 맛있는 걸 못 만들거든.\" 메그가 정색하며 청했다.\n\n\"다 가져가. 단걸 좋아하는 건 여기서 나뿐인데, 내가 처치하기도 전에 곰팡이가 슬 테니까.\" 에이미가 대답하며, 이런 결말을 위해 넉넉히 마련해 둔 음식들을 떠올리고는 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"로리가 거들러 와 줬으면 좋았을 텐데.\" 이틀 만에 두 번째로 아이스크림과 샐러드를 앞에 두고 둘러앉으며 조가 입을 뗐다.\n\n어머니의 경고하는 눈짓에 더 이상의 말은 끊겼고, 온 식구가 비장한 침묵 속에 음식을 먹었다. 그러다 마치 씨가 점잖게 한마디 했다. \"샐러드는 고대인들이 즐겨 먹던 요리 가운데 하나였지. 그리고 이블린은—\" 여기서 일제히 터져 나온 웃음이 \"샐러드의 역사\"를 뚝 끊어 놓았고, 박식한 신사는 크게 놀랐다.\n\n\"전부 바구니에 담아서 후멜네 집에 보내. 독일 사람들은 잡탕을 좋아하니까. 난 이걸 보는 것만으로도 진절머리가 나. 내가 바보짓을 했다고 다들 과식해서 죽을 까닭은 없잖아.\" 에이미가 눈가를 훔치며 외쳤다.\n\n\"네가 그 뭐라더라 그 마차에 둘이 덜컹덜컹 타고 오는 걸 봤을 때 난 정말 죽는 줄 알았어. 어찌나 커다란 호두 껍데기에 든 알맹이 두 알 같던지. 게다가 엄마는 손님 무리를 맞겠다고 위풍당당하게 기다리고 계셨잖아.\" 조가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. 웃다 지쳐 기진맥진해 있었다.\n\n\"네가 실망해서 참 안됐구나, 얘야. 그래도 우린 다들 널 만족시키려고 최선을 다했단다.\" 마치 부인이 어머니로서의 안타까움이 가득 밴 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"전 만족해요. 제가 맡은 일을 해냈고, 그게 실패한 건 제 잘못이 아니잖아요. 그렇게 위안 삼을래요.\" 에이미가 목소리를 살짝 떨며 말했다. \"도와주셔서 다들 정말 고마워요. 그리고 적어도 한 달 동안은 이 일을 입에 올리지 말아 주시면 더 고맙겠어요.\"\n\n여러 달 동안 아무도 입에 올리지 않았다. 하지만 \"축제\"라는 말만 나오면 모두 빙그레 웃었고, 로리가 에이미에게 준 생일 선물은 시곗줄에 다는 장식용으로 만든 자그마한 산호 바닷가재였다.\n\n# LITERARY LESSONS.\n\n조에게 갑자기 행운이 미소를 지으며, 그녀의 앞길에 운수 좋은 동전 한 닢을 떨어뜨렸다. 꼭 황금 동전이라 할 수는 없었지만, 이런 식으로 그녀에게 들어온 그 작은 돈만큼의 진정한 행복을 오십만 달러가 줄 수 있었을지는 의문이다.\n\n조는 몇 주에 한 번씩 방에 틀어박혀, 끄적이는 작업복을 차려입고, 본인 말마따나 \"소용돌이에 빠져들어\", 온 마음과 영혼을 쏟아 소설을 써 내려갔다. 그것을 끝마치기 전에는 도무지 마음이 편치 않았기 때문이다. 그녀의 \"끄적이는 작업복\"이란, 펜을 마음대로 닦을 수 있는 검은 모직 앞치마와, 그것과 같은 천에 명랑한 빨간 리본을 단 모자였는데, 작업 채비를 마치면 그 안에 머리를 둘둘 말아 넣었다. 이 모자는 식구들의 궁금한 눈길을 위한 봉화였다. 그런 시기에 식구들은 거리를 두면서, 이따금 고개만 빼꼼 들이밀고는 흥미롭게 물었다. \"조, 천재성이 불타오르고 있니?\" 식구들은 늘 감히 이 질문을 던지지도 못한 채, 모자를 관찰하고 그에 따라 판단하곤 했다. 이 표정 풍부한 옷가지가 이마 위로 푹 눌러쓰여 있으면 한창 힘든 작업이 진행 중이라는 표시였고, 흥이 오른 순간에는 멋들어지게 비스듬히 젖혀졌으며, 절망이 작가를 사로잡으면 모자는 통째로 벗겨져 바닥에 내동댕이쳐졌다. 그럴 때면 침입자는 말없이 물러갔고, 그 빨간 리본이 재능 있는 이마 위에 명랑하게 곧추선 모습이 보이기 전에는 아무도 감히 조에게 말을 걸지 못했다.\n\n조는 결코 스스로를 천재라 여기지 않았다. 하지만 글 쓰고 싶은 발작이 도지면 그것에 온전히 몸을 내맡긴 채 더없이 행복한 나날을 보냈다. 결핍도, 근심도, 궂은 날씨도 잊은 채, 살아 있는 누구 못지않게 진짜 같고 사랑스러운 친구들로 가득한 상상의 세계 속에서 안전하고 행복하게 앉아 있었다. 잠이 두 눈에서 달아났고, 끼니는 손도 안 댄 채 놓여 있었으며, 오로지 그런 때에만 그녀에게 깃들어, 다른 열매를 맺지 못하더라도 그 시간만큼은 살 만한 가치가 있게 해 주는 그 행복을 누리기엔 밤낮이 모두 너무 짧았다. 이 신성한 영감은 보통 한두 주 이어졌고, 그러고 나면 조는 배고프고 졸리고 짜증 나고 풀이 죽은 채 \"소용돌이\"에서 빠져나왔다.\n\n이런 발작에서 막 회복되어 갈 무렵, 조는 크로커 양을 강연에 데려다주는 임무를 떠맡게 되었는데, 그 선행의 대가로 새로운 발상이라는 보상을 받았다. '민중을 위한 강좌'였고, 강연 주제는 피라미드였다. 조는 그런 청중에게 그런 주제를 골랐다는 게 다소 의아했지만, 석탄과 밀가루 값에 골몰하고 스핑크스의 수수께끼보다 더 어려운 수수께끼를 풀려 애쓰며 살아가는 청중에게 파라오의 영광을 펼쳐 보임으로써 어떤 거대한 사회악이 바로잡히거나 어떤 거대한 필요가 채워지려나 보다고 여기며 넘겼다.\n\n그들은 일찌감치 도착했고, 크로커 양이 양말 뒤꿈치를 뜨는 동안, 조는 같은 좌석에 앉은 사람들의 얼굴을 살피며 시간을 보냈다. 왼쪽에는 떡 벌어진 이마에 그에 어울리는 보닛을 쓴 부인 둘이 여성의 권리를 논하며 태팅 레이스를 뜨고 있었다. 그 너머에는 천진하게 손을 맞잡은 수수한 연인 한 쌍, 종이봉투에서 박하사탕을 꺼내 먹는 침울한 노처녀, 노란 손수건 뒤에서 미리 한숨 자 두는 노신사가 있었다. 오른쪽 유일한 옆자리에는 신문에 푹 빠진 공부깨나 한 듯한 소년이 있었다.\n\n그것은 그림이 실린 신문이었고, 조는 자기 가까이에 있는 그 예술 작품을 들여다보며, 늑대에게 목을 물린 채 정장 차림으로 절벽에서 굴러떨어지는 인디언, 그 옆에서 부자연스럽게 작은 발과 커다란 눈을 한 두 청년이 서로를 칼로 찌르는 모습, 그리고 배경에서 입을 헤벌린 채 산발한 여자가 달아나는 장면을 담은 이 통속적인 삽화를 어떤 기막힌 사건의 우연한 얽힘이 필요로 했을까 하고 멍하니 궁금해했다. 한 면을 넘기려다 소년은 그녀가 보고 있다는 걸 알아챘고, 소년다운 호의로 신문의 절반을 내밀며 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"읽으실래요? 끝내주는 이야기예요.\"\n\n조는 미소 지으며 받아 들었다. 사내아이들을 좋아하는 마음만큼은 나이가 들어도 여전했던 것이다. 그러고는 이내 사랑과 비밀과 살인이 뒤엉킨 여느 미궁 속으로 빠져들었다. 그 이야기는 격정이 휴일을 만끽하는 부류의 가벼운 읽을거리에 속했고, 작가의 창의력이 바닥나면 거대한 참사가 등장인물 절반을 무대에서 싹 치워 버리고, 남은 절반에게 그들의 몰락을 두고 의기양양해할 거리를 남겨 주는 식이었다.\n\n\"끝내주죠, 그렇죠?\" 그녀의 눈이 자기 몫의 마지막 단락을 훑어 내려가자 소년이 물었다.\n\n\"우리 둘이 해 봐도 이만큼은 쓸 수 있을 것 같은데.\" 조가 그 시시한 글에 대한 소년의 감탄이 우스워 대꾸했다.\n\n\"제가 그럴 수 있다면 꽤나 운 좋은 녀석이라고 생각할걸요. 듣자 하니 이런 이야기로 잘 먹고산다던데요.\" 그러면서 소년이 이야기 제목 아래에 적힌 '슬랭 노스버리 부인(Mrs. S. L. A. N. G. Northbury)'이라는 이름을 가리켰다.\n\n\"그 작가를 아니?\" 조가 갑자기 흥미를 보이며 물었다.\n\n\"아뇨. 근데 그 사람 글은 다 읽어요. 그리고 이 신문 찍는 사무실에서 일하는 친구를 하나 알거든요.\"\n\n\"이런 이야기로 잘 먹고산다고?\" 조는 그 면을 장식한 흥분한 인물 무리와 빽빽이 찍힌 느낌표들을 한층 존경스러운 눈길로 바라보았다.\n\n\"그럼요! 그 작가는 사람들이 뭘 좋아하는지 딱 알고, 그걸 써서 돈도 두둑이 받는걸요.\"\n\n이때 강연이 시작되었지만 조는 거의 귀에 들어오지 않았다. 샌즈 교수가 벨초니와 케오프스, 풍뎅이 부적과 상형문자에 관해 지루하게 늘어놓는 동안, 그녀는 슬그머니 그 신문의 주소를 받아 적으며, 거기 지면에 내건 선정 소설 백 달러 상금에 한번 도전해 보겠다고 대담하게 결심했다. 강연이 끝나고 청중이 잠에서 깰 무렵, 그녀는 이미 스스로를 위해 굉장한 부를 쌓아 올렸고(종이 위에 세운 첫 번째 재산은 아니었지만), 결투를 사랑의 도피 앞에 둘지 살인 뒤에 둘지 정하지 못한 채, 벌써 이야기 짜내기에 깊이 빠져 있었다.\n\n조는 집에서는 이 계획에 대해 한마디도 하지 않았지만, 이튿날 작업에 착수했고, 이는 \"천재성이 불타오를\" 때면 늘 조금 불안한 표정을 짓던 어머니를 적잖이 불안하게 했다. 조는 전에 이런 문체를 시도해 본 적이 없었고, \"스프레드 이글\"지에 아주 점잖은 연애담이나 실으며 만족해 왔다. 이제는 연극 경험과 잡다한 독서가 도움이 되었다. 그것들이 극적 효과에 대한 어느 정도의 감각을 주었고, 줄거리와 언어와 의상을 마련해 주었으니 말이다. 그녀의 이야기는, 그 불편한 감정들을 겪어 본 일이 빈약한 자기 처지에서 빚어낼 수 있는 한껏, 발악과 절망으로 가득했다. 그리고 무대를 리스본으로 잡은 그녀는, 이야기를 인상적이고 적절한 결말이랍시고 지진으로 마무리 지었다. 원고는 은밀히 부쳐졌고, 만약 이 이야기가 상을 받지 못한다면 — 작가로서는 감히 기대하기 어려운 일이지만 — 그 가치만큼 어떤 금액이든 받게 된다면 무척 기쁘겠다고 겸손하게 적은 쪽지가 함께 따라갔다.\n\n육 주는 기다리기에 긴 시간이고, 소녀가 비밀을 지키기엔 더더욱 긴 시간이다. 하지만 조는 그 둘 다를 해냈고, 다시는 제 원고를 볼 수 없으리라는 희망을 막 접으려던 참에, 거의 숨이 멎을 만한 편지 한 통이 도착했다. 봉투를 뜯자 백 달러짜리 수표가 무릎 위로 툭 떨어졌던 것이다. 잠시 그녀는 그것을 뱀이라도 되는 양 빤히 쳐다보더니, 편지를 읽고는 울기 시작했다. 그 다정한 쪽지를 써 보낸 친절한 신사가, 자기가 한 사람에게 얼마나 강렬한 행복을 안겨 주었는지 알 수 있었더라면, 여가 시간이 있다면 그 시간을 죄다 이 즐거움에 바쳤으리라 싶다. 조는 그 편지를 돈보다 더 귀하게 여겼으니, 그것이 격려가 되었기 때문이다. 그리고 여러 해 애쓴 끝에, 비록 선정 소설 한 편을 쓴 것에 지나지 않더라도, 자기가 무언가를 해낼 줄 알게 되었다는 사실을 알게 된 것은 너무도 기뻤다.\n\n마음을 가다듬은 뒤, 한 손에는 편지를, 다른 손에는 수표를 들고 식구들 앞에 나타나 상을 탔다고 알렸을 때의 조만큼 의기양양한 젊은 여인도 좀처럼 없었으리라. 물론 대단한 축하 잔치가 벌어졌고, 이야기가 도착하자 모두가 읽고 칭찬했다. 다만 아버지는 문장이 좋고 이야기가 신선하고 진솔하며 비극이 꽤 손에 땀을 쥐게 한다고 말한 뒤, 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 세속에 물들지 않은 특유의 말투로 말했다.\n\n\"넌 이것보다 더 잘 쓸 수 있단다, 조. 가장 높은 데를 겨냥하고, 돈일랑 신경 쓰지 마라.\"\n\n\"전 돈이 제일 좋은 부분 같은데요. 그렇게 큰돈으로 뭘 하실 거예요?\" 에이미가 그 마법 같은 종잇조각을 경건한 눈길로 바라보며 물었다.\n\n\"베스랑 엄마를 한두 달 바닷가에 보내 드릴 거야.\" 조가 선뜻 대답했다.\n\n\"어머, 정말 멋지다! 아니야, 그럴 순 없어, 언니. 그건 너무 이기적인걸.\" 베스가 외쳤다. 그녀는 신선한 바닷바람을 못내 그리워하듯 야윈 두 손을 마주치고 길게 숨을 들이쉬었다가, 곧 스스로를 멈추고는 언니가 눈앞에 흔드는 수표를 손짓으로 물렸다.\n\n\"아니야, 넌 꼭 가야 해. 난 이걸 작정했단 말이야. 이게 바로 내가 노린 거고, 그래서 내가 성공한 거라고. 난 나 혼자만 생각하면 영 일이 안 풀리거든. 그러니까 널 위해서 일하면 도움이 될 거야, 알겠지? 게다가 엄마도 기분 전환이 필요한데, 엄마는 널 두고는 안 가실 테니까, 네가 꼭 가야 해. 네가 통통하고 발그레해져서 돌아오는 걸 보면 얼마나 즐겁겠어? 늘 환자를 낫게 하는 조 박사 만세!\"\n\n여러 번 의논한 끝에 두 사람은 바닷가로 떠났다. 베스가 바라는 만큼 통통하고 발그레해져서 돌아오지는 못했지만 한결 좋아졌고, 마치 부인은 열 살은 젊어진 기분이라고 단언했다. 그래서 조는 상금을 그렇게 투자한 데 만족했고, 그 기분 좋은 수표를 더 많이 벌어들이겠다고 마음먹은 채 명랑한 기운으로 작업에 매달렸다. 그해에 조는 정말로 수표를 여러 장 벌었고, 집안에서 스스로를 하나의 힘으로 느끼기 시작했다. 펜의 마법으로 그녀의 \"쓰레기\"가 식구 모두를 위한 안락으로 바뀌었으니 말이다. \"공작의 딸\"은 정육점 외상값을 갚아 주었고, \"유령의 손\"은 새 카펫을 깔아 주었으며, \"코번트리가의 저주\"는 식료품과 옷가지의 형태로 마치가의 축복임이 드러났다.\n\n부유함이란 분명 더없이 탐나는 것이지만, 가난에도 양지바른 구석은 있는 법이다. 역경의 달콤한 쓰임새 가운데 하나는 머리나 손을 부지런히 놀려 일한 데서 오는 진정한 만족이며, 세상의 지혜롭고 아름답고 유익한 축복의 절반은 필요라는 영감 덕분이다. 조는 이런 만족을 한 자락 맛보았고, 더 부유한 처녀들을 부러워하기를 그만두었다. 제 필요는 제 손으로 채울 수 있고, 누구에게도 동전 한 닢 손 벌릴 일이 없다는 사실에서 큰 위안을 얻었던 것이다.\n\n그녀의 이야기들은 별다른 주목을 받지 못했지만, 그래도 팔릴 곳을 찾았다. 이 사실에 고무된 조는 명성과 부를 노린 대담한 한 수를 두기로 마음먹었다. 소설을 네 번째로 베껴 쓰고, 가까운 친구들에게 죄다 읽어 주고, 두렵고 떨리는 마음으로 출판업자 셋에게 들이민 끝에, 마침내 분량을 삼분의 일로 줄이고 자기가 특히 아끼는 부분을 죄다 빼낸다는 조건으로 그것을 넘기게 되었다.\n\n\"이제 이걸 도로 양철 부엌에 처박아 곰팡이가 슬게 두든가, 내 돈으로 출판하든가, 아니면 사겠다는 사람들 입맛에 맞게 잘게 썰어서 받을 수 있는 만큼 받든가 해야 해. 명성이라는 게 집에 두면 참 좋긴 한데, 현금이 더 편리하단 말이지. 그러니 이 중대한 사안에 대해 회의의 의견을 듣고 싶어.\" 조가 가족회의를 소집하며 말했다.\n\n\"네 책을 망치지 마라, 얘야. 네가 아는 것보다 그 안에는 더 많은 게 담겨 있고, 발상도 잘 풀어냈으니까. 묵혀 두고 무르익게 두렴.\" 이것이 아버지의 충고였다. 그리고 아버지는 설교한 대로 실천하는 사람이었다. 자신의 결실이 무르익기를 삼십 년이나 참을성 있게 기다려 왔고, 이제 그것이 달고 농익은 지금조차 서둘러 거두려 하지 않았으니 말이다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 조가 기다리는 것보다 한번 시도해 보는 데서 더 많은 걸 얻을 것 같아요.\" 마치 부인이 말했다. \"이런 작업에는 비평이 가장 좋은 시금석이거든요. 미처 몰랐던 장점과 결점을 둘 다 보여 줘서, 다음번엔 더 잘하게 도와줄 테니까요. 우린 너무 편들기 마련이지만, 바깥사람들의 칭찬과 비난은, 설령 돈은 얼마 못 벌더라도 유익할 거예요.\"\n\n\"맞아요.\" 조가 눈썹을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"바로 그거예요. 이걸 하도 오래 붙들고 끙끙댔더니, 이게 좋은지 나쁜지 그저 그런지 정말 모르겠어요. 냉정하고 공정한 사람들이 한번 봐 주고 어떻게 생각하는지 말해 주면 큰 도움이 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"난 한 마디도 빼지 않겠어. 그러면 책을 망친다고. 이 이야기의 묘미는 인물들의 행동보다 마음속에 더 있는데, 진행하면서 설명을 안 하면 죄다 뒤죽박죽이 될 거야.\" 메그가 말했다. 이 책이야말로 일찍이 쓰인 어떤 소설보다 뛰어난 작품이라고 그녀는 굳게 믿고 있었다.\n\n\"하지만 앨런 씨는 '설명은 빼고, 짧고 극적으로 만들어서, 인물들이 이야기를 끌어가게 하라'고 하시던데.\" 조가 출판업자의 쪽지로 화제를 돌리며 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그분 말씀대로 해. 그분은 뭐가 팔릴지 알고, 우린 모르니까. 좋고 인기 있는 책을 만들어서 돈을 최대한 많이 벌어. 그러다 이름이 알려지면, 그때 가서 여담도 늘어놓고 소설에 철학적이고 형이상학적인 인물도 집어넣을 여유가 생길 거야.\" 이 문제를 철저히 실용적인 관점에서 본 에이미가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄.\" 조가 웃으며 말했다. \"내 인물들이 '철학적이고 형이상학적'이라면 그건 내 잘못이 아니야. 난 그런 것에 대해선 아버지가 가끔 하시는 말씀 말고는 아무것도 모르니까. 아버지의 지혜로운 생각이 내 연애담에 섞여 들어갔다면, 나한테야 잘된 일이지. 자, 베스, 넌 어떻게 생각해?\"\n\n\"난 이게 어서 출판되는 걸 봤으면 좋겠어.\" 베스가 한 말은 이게 전부였고, 그렇게 말하며 미소를 지었다. 하지만 마지막 한마디에 자기도 모르게 힘이 실려 있었고, 어린아이 같은 천진함을 결코 잃은 적 없는 그 두 눈에 어린 애틋한 빛이, 한순간 조의 마음을 불길한 두려움으로 서늘하게 만들었다. 그리하여 조는 자신의 작은 모험을 \"어서\" 감행하기로 마음을 정했다.\n\n그리하여 스파르타 사람 같은 결연함으로, 젊은 여류 작가는 자신의 첫 소생을 책상에 뉘어 놓고, 여느 식인귀 못지않게 무자비하게 잘게 잘라 냈다. 모두를 기쁘게 하려는 바람으로 모두의 충고를 따랐고, 우화에 나오는 노인과 당나귀처럼, 결국 아무도 흡족하게 하지 못했다.\n\n아버지는 저도 모르게 스며든 형이상학적 색채를 마음에 들어 했고, 그래서 그 부분은 — 조 자신은 미심쩍어했지만 — 그대로 남겨 두었다. 어머니는 묘사가 조금 지나치게 많다고 여겼고, 그래서 그것은 거의 다 빠졌으며, 그와 함께 이야기에 꼭 필요한 연결 고리도 여럿 사라졌다. 메그는 비극을 좋아했고, 그래서 조는 메그의 입맛에 맞게 비통함을 잔뜩 쌓아 올렸다. 반면 에이미는 익살스러운 부분을 못마땅해했고, 그래서 더없이 좋은 의도로, 조는 이야기의 침울한 성격을 누그러뜨려 주던 발랄한 장면들을 꺼 버렸다. 그러고는 파국을 완성하듯 분량을 삼분의 일로 줄여, 깃털 다 뽑힌 울새 같은 그 가엾은 작은 연애담을, 운명을 시험하라며 크고 분주한 세상으로 천연덕스럽게 내보냈다.\n\n자, 책은 출판되었고, 조는 그 대가로 삼백 달러를 받았다. 그와 함께 칭찬과 비난도 잔뜩 받았는데, 둘 다 기대보다 훨씬 커서 그녀는 한동안 헤어나오는 데 시간이 걸린 어리둥절한 상태에 빠지고 말았다.\n\n\"엄마는 비평이 절 도와줄 거라고 하셨잖아요. 그런데 어떻게 도움이 돼요? 어찌나 모순투성이인지, 제가 가망 있는 책을 쓴 건지 십계명을 죄다 어긴 건지 모르겠다고요!\" 가엾은 조가 외치며 한 무더기의 평을 뒤적였는데, 그것들을 읽노라면 한순간 자부심과 기쁨이 차올랐다가 다음 순간 분노와 끔찍한 낙담이 밀려왔다. \"이 사람은 '진실과 아름다움과 진지함으로 가득한 더없이 훌륭한 책. 모든 것이 달콤하고 순수하고 건강하다'고 했어요.\" 갈피를 못 잡은 여류 작가가 말을 이었다. \"그다음 사람은 '이 책의 주제 의식은 형편없고, 병적인 공상과 심령주의 사상과 부자연스러운 인물로 가득하다'고 했고요. 그런데 전 무슨 주제 의식 같은 건 아예 없었고, 심령주의도 안 믿고, 인물도 실제 인생에서 베껴 왔으니, 이 비평가가 어떻게 맞을 수가 있겠어요. 또 누구는 '수년 만에 나온 최고의 미국 소설 가운데 하나'라고 했는데(저도 그 정도는 아니란 거 알아요), 바로 다음 사람은 '독창적이고 대단한 힘과 감정으로 쓰였지만, 위험한 책'이라고 단언하네요. 위험하긴요! 누구는 비웃고, 누구는 지나치게 떠받들고, 거의 다들 제가 무슨 심오한 주제를 펼치려 했다고 우기는데, 전 그저 즐거움과 돈을 위해 썼을 뿐이라고요. 차라리 통째로 내든가 아예 안 낼걸 그랬어요. 이렇게 오해받는 건 정말 싫으니까요.\"\n\n식구들과 친구들이 위로와 칭찬을 아낌없이 베풀었다. 그래도 그토록 좋은 뜻으로 했건만 겉보기엔 그토록 형편없이 해낸 듯한, 예민하고 기개 넘치는 조에게는 힘든 시절이었다. 하지만 그것은 그녀에게 약이 되었다. 정말로 가치 있는 의견을 지닌 이들이 작가에게 최고의 교육이 되는 비평을 들려주었기 때문이다. 그리고 처음의 쓰라림이 가시고 나자, 조는 자신의 가엾은 작은 책을 두고 웃을 수 있게 되었으면서도 여전히 그 책을 믿었고, 자기가 받은 매질 덕분에 한층 지혜로워지고 단단해졌음을 느꼈다.\n\n\"난 키츠 같은 천재가 아니니까, 이런 일로 죽진 않아.\" 조가 꿋꿋하게 말했다. \"게다가 어쨌든 우스개는 내 편이야. 실제 인생에서 곧이곧대로 가져온 부분은 말도 안 되고 터무니없다고 욕먹는데, 내 어리석은 머릿속에서 지어낸 장면은 '매력적이고 자연스럽고 다정하고 진실하다'고 평가받으니 말이야. 그러니 그걸로 위안 삼을래. 그리고 준비가 되면 다시 일어나서 또 한 편 써 볼 거야.\"\n\n# DOMESTIC EXPERIENCES.\n\n여느 젊은 새색시들이 대개 그렇듯, 메그도 모범적인 살림꾼이 되겠다는 다짐과 함께 결혼 생활을 시작했다. 존에게 집은 낙원이어야 했다. 그는 언제나 미소 띤 얼굴을 보아야 하고, 날마다 진수성찬을 누려야 하며, 단추가 떨어진 일 따위는 결코 알아서는 안 되었다. 메그는 그 일에 어찌나 많은 사랑과 활력과 명랑함을 쏟아부었던지, 몇몇 장애가 있긴 했어도 성공하지 않을 수 없었다. 다만 그 낙원은 그리 평온하지만은 않았다. 이 작은 부인은 안달복달했고, 남편을 기쁘게 하려고 지나치게 마음을 졸였으며, 갖은 근심에 시달리는 진짜 마르다처럼 부산을 떨었던 것이다. 어떤 때는 너무 지쳐 미소조차 짓지 못했다. 존은 진귀한 요리가 연달아 나오자 소화불량에 걸려, 고마운 줄도 모르고 소박한 음식을 달라고 했다. 단추로 말하자면, 메그는 이내 그것들이 다 어디로 가는지 의아해하고, 남자들의 칠칠치 못함에 고개를 절레절레 흔들며, 직접 달아 보라고, 그래서 당신 솜씨가 성마른 잡아당김과 서툰 손가락을 내 솜씨보다 더 잘 견뎌 내는지 두고 보라고 으름장을 놓는 법을 배우게 되었다.\n\n* 마르다 : 성경에서 손님 접대로 분주했던 여인.\n두 사람은 사랑만으로는 살 수 없다는 걸 깨달은 뒤에도 무척 행복했다. 메그가 낯익은 커피 주전자 너머에서 환하게 미소 지어 보여도 존은 그녀의 아름다움이 줄었다고 여기지 않았고, 메그 역시 날마다의 작별에서 낭만이 사라졌다고 느끼지 않았다. 남편이 입맞춤 뒤에 다정하게 \"여보, 저녁에 송아지 고기를 보낼까, 양고기를 보낼까?\" 하고 물을 때조차 말이다. 작은 집은 더 이상 찬란한 신혼의 보금자리가 아니었지만 하나의 가정이 되었고, 젊은 부부는 곧 그것이 더 나은 방향으로의 변화임을 느꼈다. 처음에 둘은 소꿉놀이하듯 살림을 꾸리며 아이들처럼 그것을 즐겼다. 그러다 존은 한 가정의 가장이라는 책임이 어깨에 얹힌 것을 느끼며 착실히 사업에 매진했고, 메그는 고운 무명 실내복을 치워 두고 커다란 앞치마를 두른 채, 앞서 말했듯 분별보다는 의욕을 앞세워 일에 매달렸다.\n\n요리에 대한 열병이 이어지는 동안 메그는 코닐리어스 부인의 요리책을\n자, 모든 일을 수학 문제 풀듯 정성껏 인내심을 갖고 차근차근 해 나갔다. 어떤 날은 성공작이 너무 풍성하게 쏟아져 식구들을 불러다 함께 먹어 치우게 하기도 했고, 어떤 날은 실패작을 한 무더기 로티 편에 몰래 들려 보내, 휴멜네 어린 것들의 만만한 뱃속에 감쪽같이 감춰 버리기도 했다. 존과 함께 가계부를 들여다보며 저녁을 보낸 뒤에는 으레 요리에 대한 열정이 잠시 가라앉으면서 절약하는 시기가 찾아왔고, 그동안 가엾은 남편은 빵 푸딩과 다진 고기 요리, 그리고 데운 커피를 줄줄이 견뎌야 했는데, 그것은 그의 영혼을 시험하는 일이었지만 그는 칭찬받아 마땅할 만큼 꿋꿋이 참아 냈다. 그러나 황금률을 찾기도 전에 메그는 젊은 부부가 오래 지내다 보면 으레 갖게 마련인 것 하나를 살림살이에 보태고 말았다. 바로 집안싸움이었다.\n\n저장실을 직접 만든 잼으로 가득 채우고 싶다는 주부다운 소망에 불타올라, 메그는 손수 까치밥나무 젤리를 만들어 보기로 했다. 존에게는 작은 단지를 열두 개쯤 주문해 오고, 설탕도 넉넉히 더 사 오라고 부탁했다. 자기 집 까치밥나무 열매가 다 익어 당장 손을 봐야 했기 때문이다. 존은 \"내 아내\"가 무엇이든 척척 해낸다고 굳게 믿었고, 아내의 솜씨를 당연한 듯 자랑스러워했기에, 아내를 흐뭇하게 해 주고, 단 한 번뿐인 열매 수확을 더없이 보기 좋은 모양으로 갈무리해 겨우내 먹기로 마음먹었다. 그리하여 집으로 사랑스러운 작은 단지 네 다스와 설탕 반 통, 그리고 까치밥나무 열매를 따 줄 사내아이 하나가 들어왔다. 예쁜 머리카락을 작은 모자 속에 밀어 넣고, 팔을 팔꿈치까지 걷어붙인 채, 가슴받이가 달렸는데도 어딘가 멋스러워 보이는 체크무늬 앞치마를 두른 젊은 주부는 일에 달려들었다. 성공을 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 한나가 이 일을 수백 번이나 하는 것을 보지 않았던가? 늘어선 단지들이 처음에는 좀 놀라웠지만, 존이 젤리를 워낙 좋아하는 데다, 그 예쁜 작은 병들이 맨 위 선반에 놓이면 얼마나 보기 좋을까 싶어, 메그는 그것을 죄다 채우기로 마음먹고는, 열매를 따고 끓이고 거르고 젤리를 만드느라 부산을 떨며 긴 하루를 보냈다. 그녀는 최선을 다했다. 코닐리어스 부인에게 조언도 구했다. 한나가 했던 일 가운데 자기가 빠뜨린 게 무엇인지 머리를 쥐어짜며 떠올려 보았다. 다시 끓이고, 설탕을 더 넣고, 다시 걸렀다. 그래도 그 지긋지긋한 것은 도무지 \"굳질\" 않았다.\n\n가슴받이 앞치마를 두른 채로라도 친정으로 달려가 엄마에게 손을 좀 빌려 달라고 하고 싶었지만, 존과 메그는 자기들끼리의 걱정거리나 시행착오, 다툼으로 결코 남을 성가시게 하지 않기로 약속했었다. 두 사람은 그 마지막 단어를 두고, 그게 가당치도 않은 생각이라는 듯 함께 웃었었다. 그래도 그들은 그 결심을 지켰고, 도움 없이 해낼 수 있을 때면 언제나 그렇게 했으며, 아무도 끼어들지 않았다. 마치 부인이 그렇게 하라고 일러 준 방식이었기 때문이다. 그래서 메그는 그 무더운 여름날 내내 말 안 듣는 단것과 홀로 씨름하다가, 다섯 시가 되자 엉망진창이 된 부엌에 주저앉아, 끈적끈적해진 두 손을 비틀어 짜며 목 놓아 울었다.\n\n자, 새로운 삶의 첫 설렘에 젖어 있을 적에 메그는 곧잘 이렇게 말하곤 했다.\n\n\"우리 남편은 언제든 마음 내킬 때 친구를 집에 데려와도 괜찮다고 늘 느낄 수 있어야 해. 난 언제나 준비가 되어 있을 거야. 허둥대거나 잔소리하거나 불편해하는 일 없이, 깔끔한 집과 명랑한 아내, 그리고 맛있는 저녁이 있는 거지. 존, 여보, 절대 내 허락을 구하느라 멈추지 말고, 좋아하는 사람을 마음껏 초대해요. 내가 반갑게 맞아 줄 테니 안심해도 돼요.\"\n\n정말이지 얼마나 매력적인 말인가! 존은 그 말을 듣고 자랑스러움에 한껏 들떴고, 자기보다 나은 아내를 둔다는 게 얼마나 큰 축복인지 실감했다. 그러나 이따금 손님을 맞기는 했어도, 어쩌다 예고 없이 닥친 적은 한 번도 없었기에, 메그는 지금까지 자신의 솜씨를 뽐낼 기회가 없었다. 이 눈물의 골짜기에서는 늘 이런 식이다. 그런 일들에는 우리가 그저 놀라워하고, 한탄하고, 되도록 잘 견뎌 내는 수밖에 없는 어떤 필연이 깃들어 있다.\n\n존이 젤리에 관한 일을 까맣게 잊지만 않았더라도, 일 년 중 하필 그날을, 친구를 예고 없이 저녁 식사에 데려올 날로 고른 것은 정말 용서받기 힘든 일이었다. 그날 아침 푸짐한 식사를 주문해 두었다고 흐뭇해하며, 그것이 어김없이 제시간에 준비되어 있으리라 확신하면서, 예쁜 아내가 자기를 맞으러 달려 나올 때 그 식사가 빚어낼 근사한 효과를 즐겁게 그리며, 존은 젊은 주인이자 남편으로서의 억누를 수 없는 만족감을 안고 친구를 자기 저택으로 안내했다.\n\n이 세상은 실망의 연속이라는 것을, 존은 비둘기 둥지에 다다랐을 때 깨달았다. 현관문은 늘 손님을 반기듯 활짝 열려 있었는데, 지금은 닫혀 있을 뿐 아니라 잠겨 있었고, 어제 묻은 진흙이 아직도 계단을 장식하고 있었다. 응접실 창문은 닫힌 채 커튼이 드리워져 있었고, 흰옷을 입고 머리에 정신을 쏙 빼놓는 작은 리본을 단 예쁜 아내가 베란다에서 바느질하는 그림 같은 모습도, 손님을 맞으며 수줍게 미소 짓는 눈동자 반짝이는 안주인의 모습도 없었다. 그런 것은 전혀 없었다. 까치밥나무 덤불 아래 핏빛으로 물든 듯한 꼴을 한 사내아이가 잠들어 있을 뿐, 사람이라곤 그림자도 보이지 않았다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 생긴 것 같군. 스콧, 잠깐 정원에 들어가 있게. 그동안 내가 브룩 부인을 찾아볼 테니.\" 존이 그 적막과 고요에 놀라 말했다.\n\n그는 설탕 타는 매캐한 냄새에 이끌려 집을 빙 돌아 서둘러 갔고, 스콧 씨는 야릇한 표정을 지으며 그 뒤를 어슬렁어슬렁 따라갔다. 브룩이 사라지자 그는 눈치껏 멀찍이 떨어져 멈춰 섰다. 하지만 보이고 들리는 것은 다 알 수 있었고, 총각이었던 터라 그 광경을 무척이나 즐겼다.\n\n부엌은 혼란과 절망이 지배하고 있었다. 한쪽 젤리는 이 단지에서 저 단지로 줄줄 흘러내렸고, 또 한쪽은 바닥에 널브러져 있었으며, 세 번째 것은 난로 위에서 신나게 타고 있었다. 로티는 게르만 사람 특유의 무신경함으로 빵과 까치밥나무주를 태연히 먹고 있었다. 젤리가 아직도 가망 없이 액체 상태였기 때문이다. 한편 브룩 부인은 앞치마를 머리에 뒤집어쓴 채 앉아 처량하게 흐느끼고 있었다.\n\n\"여보, 무슨 일이오?\" 존이 데인 손이며, 갑작스러운 비보(悲報)며, 정원에 있는 손님 생각에 속으로 아찔해하며 끔찍한 상상에 사로잡혀 뛰어 들어오며 외쳤다.\n\n\"오, 존, 나 정말 너무 지치고 덥고 짜증 나고 속상해! 이러다 완전히 녹초가 될 때까지 매달렸다고. 와서 좀 도와줘요, 안 그럼 나 죽을 것 같아!\" 기진맥진한 주부는 남편의 가슴에 몸을 던졌고, 말 그대로 온갖 의미로 달콤한 환영을 안겨 주었다. 그녀의 앞치마가 바닥과 동시에 세례를 받았던 까닭이다.\n\n\"뭐가 그리 속상하오, 여보? 무슨 끔찍한 일이라도 났소?\" 걱정스러운 존이 비뚜름하게 돌아간 작은 모자의 꼭대기에 다정하게 입을 맞추며 물었다.\n\n\"응.\" 메그가 절망스럽게 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"그럼 어서 말해 봐요. 울지 말고. 그것 말고는 뭐든 견딜 수 있으니까. 말해 봐요, 여보.\"\n\n\"그…… 그 젤리가 안 굳어, 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어!\"\n\n존 브룩은 그때 이후로는 감히 다시 웃어 보지 못할 만큼 호탕하게 웃어 댔다. 그리고 빈정대던 스콧도 그 호탕한 웃음소리를 듣고는 저도 모르게 빙긋 웃었는데, 그 웃음이 가엾은 메그의 슬픔에 마지막 일격을 가하고 말았다.\n\n\"그게 다요? 창밖으로 내던져 버리고 더는 신경 쓰지 마요. 원한다면 몇 쿼트씩이라도 사다 줄 테니까. 하지만 제발 신경질은 부리지 말아요. 잭 스콧을 저녁 식사에 데려왔으니까, 그리고……\"\n\n존은 더 말을 잇지 못했다. 메그가 그를 떼밀어 내고는 비극적인 몸짓으로 두 손을 맞잡으며 의자에 털썩 주저앉아, 분노와 책망과 낙담이 뒤섞인 어조로 외쳤기 때문이다.\n\n\"손님이 저녁을 먹으러 오는데, 죄다 엉망진창이라고! 존 브룩, 어쩜 그럴 수가 있어요?\"\n\n\"쉿, 그 사람 정원에 있어요! 내가 그 망할 젤리를 깜빡했지만, 이젠 어쩔 수 없잖소.\" 존이 근심 어린 눈으로 형세를 살피며 말했다.\n\n\"미리 기별을 하든가, 아니면 오늘 아침에 말을 했어야죠. 그리고 내가 얼마나 바빴는지 생각이라도 했어야 하고요.\" 메그가 토라져서 계속 쏘아붙였다. 산비둘기도 깃털이 헝클어지면 쪼아 대는 법이니까.\n\n\"오늘 아침엔 나도 몰랐고, 나오는 길에 그 친구를 만났으니 기별을 보낼 새도 없었소. 당신이 늘 좋을 대로 하라고 했으니, 허락을 구할 생각은 한 번도 안 했지. 전엔 이런 적이 없었고, 다시 또 그러면 내가 사람이 아니지!\" 존이 억울하다는 듯 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그러길 바라요! 당장 그 사람 데리고 나가요. 난 그 사람 못 봐요, 게다가 저녁거리도 없다고요.\"\n\n\"이거 참 기가 막히는군! 내가 보내 준 쇠고기랑 채소는 어디 가고, 당신이 만들겠다던 푸딩은 또 어디 갔소?\" 존이 식료품 저장실로 달려가며 외쳤다.\n\n\"아무것도 요리할 시간이 없었어요. 친정에서 저녁을 먹을 생각이었다고요. 미안해요, 하지만 내가 너무 바빴어요.\" 그러더니 메그의 눈물이 다시 터지기 시작했다.\n\n존은 온순한 사람이었지만, 그도 사람이었다. 하루 종일 일하고 지치고 배고프고 기대에 부풀어 집에 돌아왔는데, 난장판이 된 집과 텅 빈 식탁, 그리고 골이 난 아내를 마주하게 된 것은 마음의 평온이나 점잖은 태도를 지키는 데 그리 보탬이 되는 일은 아니었다. 그래도 그는 자제했고, 그 작은 풍파도 한마디 불운한 말만 아니었다면 그냥 지나갔을 터였다.\n\n\"낭패스러운 일인 건 인정해요. 하지만 당신이 거들어만 준다면 어떻게든 헤쳐 나가서 그래도 즐거운 시간을 보낼 수 있을 거요. 울지 말고, 여보, 조금만 힘을 내서 뭐든 먹을 걸 좀 차려 주구려. 우리 둘 다 사냥꾼처럼 배가 고프니, 뭐든 상관없소. 찬 고기랑 빵이랑 치즈를 내주구려. 젤리까진 바라지도 않을 테니.\"\n\n그는 좋은 뜻으로 농담을 한 것이었지만, 그 한마디가 그의 운명을 결정짓고 말았다. 메그는 자신의 서글픈 실패를 들먹이는 것이 너무도 잔인하다고 여겼고, 그가 말하는 순간 마지막 남은 인내심의 한 조각마저 사라져 버렸다.\n\n\"이 낭패는 당신 알아서 헤쳐 나가요. 난 누구를 위해서 '힘을 낼' 만큼 기운이 남아 있질 않으니까. 손님한테 뼈다귀에다 천박하게 빵이랑 치즈나 내놓자는 게 딱 남자다운 발상이지. 우리 집에서 그런 일은 절대 못 해요. 그 스콧이라는 사람 친정으로 데려가서, 내가 집에 없다고, 아프다고, 죽었다고, 뭐라고든 말해요. 난 그 사람 안 만날 거고, 당신네 둘이서 나랑 내 젤리를 실컷 비웃어요. 여기선 그것 말고 다른 건 아무것도 못 얻을 테니까요.\" 그렇게 단숨에 도전장을 내던진 메그는 앞치마를 벗어 던지고는, 자기 방으로 가서 신세 한탄이나 하려고 황급히 전장을 떠나 버렸다.\n\n그 두 사람이 메그가 없는 동안 무엇을 했는지, 그녀는 끝내 알지 못했다. 다만 스콧 씨가 \"친정으로\" 끌려가지 않은 것만은 분명했다. 두 사람이 함께 어슬렁어슬렁 나가 버린 뒤 메그가 아래층으로 내려와 보니, 닥치는 대로 차려 먹은 점심의 흔적이 있어 그녀를 소름 끼치게 했다. 로티의 전언에 따르면, 두 사람은 \"엄청 많이 먹고 크게 웃었으며, 주인어른이 그 단것은 죄다 버리고 단지는 숨겨 두라고 일렀다\"고 했다.\n\n메그는 친정으로 달려가 엄마에게 죄다 털어놓고 싶었다. 하지만 자신의 부족함에 대한 부끄러움과, \"잔인할지언정 아무에게도 알려서는 안 될\" 존에 대한 의리가 그녀를 붙잡았다. 그래서 후딱 뒷정리를 마친 뒤, 그녀는 예쁘게 차려입고 앉아, 존이 돌아와 용서받기를 기다렸다.\n\n안타깝게도, 존은 그 일을 그런 식으로 보지 않았던 터라 돌아오지 않았다. 그는 스콧과 함께 그 일을 좋은 농담거리로 넘겨 버렸고, 어린 아내를 되도록 잘 감싸 주었으며, 손님 대접을 어찌나 극진히 했던지 친구는 그 즉석 저녁을 흡족하게 즐기고는 또 오겠노라 약속했다. 그러나 존은 내색은 하지 않았어도 화가 나 있었다. 메그가 자기를 곤경에 빠뜨려 놓고는, 정작 도움이 절실한 순간에 자기를 저버렸다는 생각이 들었다. \"언제든 마음껏 자유롭게 사람을 데려오라고 해 놓고, 막상 그 말대로 했더니 발끈해서 비난을 퍼붓고, 비웃음거리나 동정거리가 되도록 사람을 곤경에 내팽개치다니, 그건 공정하지 않아. 천만에, 절대 공정하지 않지! 메그도 그걸 알아야 해.\" 그는 식사 내내 속으로 부글부글 끓었다. 하지만 소동이 끝나고 스콧을 배웅한 뒤 어슬렁어슬렁 집으로 걸어오자, 한결 누그러진 마음이 찾아들었다. \"가엾은 사람! 그렇게 진심으로 나를 기쁘게 해 주려 했는데, 그게 너무 힘에 부쳤던 거지. 물론 잘못이야 했지만, 아직 젊으니까. 내가 참고 가르쳐 줘야지.\" 그는 아내가 친정에 가지 않았기를 바랐다. 남의 입에 오르내리거나 누가 끼어드는 게 질색이었던 것이다. 그 생각만으로도 그는 잠시 또 마음이 거슬렸다. 하지만 메그가 울다 못해 병이라도 나면 어쩌나 하는 두려움에 마음이 누그러져, 침착하고 다정하되 단호하게, 아주 단호하게 굴면서 아내가 남편에 대한 도리에서 어디를 잘못했는지 일러 주리라 마음먹고는 걸음을 재촉했다.\n\n메그 역시 \"침착하고 다정하되 단호하게\" 굴면서 남편에게 그의 도리를 일깨워 주리라 마음먹었다. 그녀는 그를 맞으러 달려 나가 용서를 빌고, 틀림없이 그러리라 확신하는 대로 입맞춤과 위로를 받고 싶었다. 그러나 물론 그녀는 그런 짓은 전혀 하지 않았고, 존이 오는 것을 보자 한가로운 안주인이 자기 집 가장 좋은 응접실에 앉아 있기라도 한 듯, 흔들의자에 앉아 바느질을 하며 아주 자연스럽게 콧노래를 흥얼거리기 시작했다.\n\n존은 다정한 니오베를 만나지 못해 조금 실망했다. 하지만 자신의 체면상 먼저 사과를 받아야 마땅하다고 느꼈기에, 그는 아무 사과도 하지 않고, 그저 느긋이 들어와 소파에 몸을 누이고는, 묘하게 들어맞는 말을 했다.\n\n\"여보, 곧 초승달이 뜨겠구려.\"\n\n\"난 별 이의 없어요.\" 메그도 그에 못지않게 누그러뜨리는 대꾸를 했다.\n\n브룩 씨가 두루 관심 가질 만한 화제를 몇 가지 더 꺼냈으나 브룩 부인이 찬물을 끼얹어, 대화는 시들해졌다. 존은 한쪽 창가로 가서 신문을 펼쳐 들고는, 말하자면 그 속에 자신을 파묻어 버렸다. 메그는 다른 쪽 창가로 가서, 마치 슬리퍼에 달 새 장미 매듭 장식이 살아가는 데 꼭 필요한 물건이라도 되는 양 바느질을 했다. 둘 다 입을 열지 않았다. 둘 다 자못 \"침착하고 단호해\" 보였고, 둘 다 못 견디게 불편한 심정이었다.\n\n\"아이참.\" 메그는 생각했다. \"결혼 생활이란 참 고된 거구나. 엄마 말씀처럼 사랑뿐 아니라 한없는 인내심도 필요한 거야.\" '엄마'라는 단어가 오래전에 들려주셨던, 그때는 믿지 못하겠다며 항변하면서 받아들였던 다른 모성의 충고들을 떠올리게 했다.\n\n\"존은 좋은 사람이지만, 그에게도 결점은 있단다. 너는 네 자신의 결점도 떠올리면서, 그 결점들을 보아 내고 견디는 법을 배워야 해. 그 사람은 무척 단호하지만, 네가 다정하게 이치를 따져 설득하고 성급하게 맞서지만 않는다면 결코 고집을 부리지는 않을 거야. 그 사람은 아주 꼼꼼하고 진실에 까다로운데, 그건 좋은 점이란다, 네가 '유난스럽다'고 하더라도 말이야. 메그야, 절대로 눈빛으로든 말로든 그를 속이지 마라. 그러면 그 사람은 네가 받아 마땅한 신뢰와 네게 필요한 든든한 버팀목이 되어 줄 거야. 그 사람에게도 성미가 있는데, 우리 같은 성미는 아니란다. 우리 것은 한 번 번쩍하고는 곧 사그라지지만, 그 사람 것은 좀처럼 일지 않는 희고 고요한 분노여서, 한번 불붙으면 좀처럼 꺼뜨리기 어렵단다. 조심해라, 아주 조심해서, 너 자신에게 그 분노가 일지 않게 하렴. 평화와 행복이란 그 사람의 존중을 잃지 않는 데 달려 있으니까. 너 자신을 잘 살피고, 둘 다 잘못했을 땐 먼저 용서를 구하렴. 그리고 흔히 쓰디쓴 슬픔과 후회로 가는 길을 닦곤 하는 사소한 토라짐과 오해, 성급한 말들을 경계하렴.\"\n\n이 말들이 노을 속에서 바느질을 하며 앉아 있는 메그의 마음속에 되살아났다. 특히 그 마지막 대목이. 이번이 처음으로 겪는 심각한 불화였다. 자신이 성급하게 내뱉었던 말들을 떠올려 보니 어리석고도 매정하게 들렸고, 자신의 분노가 이제는 유치해 보였으며, 가엾은 존이 그런 광경이 펼쳐진 집에 돌아온다는 생각에 그녀의 마음이 완전히 녹아내렸다. 그녀는 눈물이 그렁그렁한 눈으로 그를 흘끔 보았지만, 그는 그것을 보지 못했다. 그녀는 바느질감을 내려놓고 일어서며 \"내가 먼저 '용서해 줘요'라고 말할 거야\"라고 생각했지만, 그는 그 소리를 못 들은 듯했다. 자존심을 삼키기가 힘들어, 그녀는 아주 천천히 방을 가로질러 가서 그의 곁에 섰지만, 그는 고개를 돌리지 않았다. 잠시 그녀는 정말이지 도저히 못 하겠다는 심정이 들었다. 그때 이런 생각이 떠올랐다. \"이게 시작이야. 내 몫을 다하면 스스로 책망할 일이 없을 거야.\" 그러고는 몸을 숙여 남편의 이마에 살며시 입을 맞추었다. 물론 그것으로 모든 게 해결되었다. 뉘우치는 입맞춤은 그 어떤 말보다도 나았고, 존은 이내 그녀를 무릎에 앉히고는 다정하게 말했다.\n\n\"가엾은 젤리 단지를 두고 웃다니 정말 못됐어. 용서해 줘요, 여보, 다시는 안 그럴 테니!\"\n\n하지만 그는 다시 그랬다. 오, 어쩌랴, 그것도 수백 번이나. 메그도 마찬가지였다. 둘 다 그게 자기들이 만든 가장 달콤한 젤리였다고 입을 모았다. 그 작은 집안싸움 속에서 가정의 평화가 지켜졌으니 말이다.\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤, 메그는 특별히 초대해 스콧 씨를 저녁 식사에 모셨고, 이번에는 첫 코스로 익어 버린 아내를 내놓는 일 없이 즐거운 잔칫상을 차려 냈다. 그날 메그가 어찌나 명랑하고 우아하게, 모든 일을 어찌나 근사하게 진행했던지, 스콧 씨는 존에게 자네는 참 행복한 사람이라고 말하고는, 집으로 돌아가는 내내 총각 신세의 고달픔에 고개를 절레절레 흔들었다.\n\n가을이 되자 메그에게 새로운 시련과 경험이 찾아왔다. 샐리 모펏이 다시 우정을 다지면서, 수다를 떨려고 작은 집으로 줄곧 달려오거나, 큰 집으로 와서 하루를 보내라고 \"그 가엾은 것\"을 초대하곤 했다. 그것은 즐거운 일이었다. 흐린 날이면 메그는 종종 외로움을 느꼈으니까. 친정 식구들은 다들 바빴고, 존은 밤이 되어야 돌아왔으며, 할 일이라곤 바느질하거나 책을 읽거나 빈둥거리는 것뿐이었다. 그래서 메그가 친구와 어울려 쏘다니며 수다 떠는 데 빠져든 것도 자연스러운 일이었다. 샐리의 예쁜 물건들을 보고 있으면 그런 것들이 갖고 싶어졌고, 그것들을 가지지 못한 자신이 가여워졌다. 샐리는 무척 친절해서 탐나는 그 자잘한 물건들을 곧잘 주려 했지만, 메그는 존이 싫어하리란 걸 알기에 사양했다. 그러고는 이 어리석은 작은 여인은 존이 그보다 한없이 더 싫어할 일을 저지르고 말았다.\n\n메그는 남편의 수입을 알고 있었고, 그가 자신의 행복뿐 아니라 어떤 남자들은 그보다 더 귀히 여기는 듯한 것, 곧 돈까지 맡겨 준다는 사실을 느끼는 것이 좋았다. 그녀는 돈이 어디 있는지 알았고, 마음에 드는 것은 마음껏 가져다 쓸 수 있었으며, 그가 바라는 거라곤 그저 한 푼까지 장부에 적고, 한 달에 한 번 청구서를 치르며, 자신이 가난한 사람의 아내임을 잊지 말라는 것뿐이었다. 지금까지는 잘해 왔다. 알뜰하고 정확하게, 작은 가계부를 깔끔하게 적어 두었다가 매달 거리낌 없이 그에게 보여 주었다. 그러나 그 가을, 뱀이 메그의 낙원으로 기어들어, 현대의 수많은 이브가 그러하듯, 사과가 아니라 옷으로 그녀를 꾀었다. 메그는 동정받으며 가난뱅이 취급당하는 것이 싫었다. 그것이 그녀를 못 견디게 했지만, 그녀는 그렇다고 털어놓기가 부끄러웠다. 그래서 이따금 예쁜 물건을 사며 스스로를 달래려 했으니, 샐리가 자기를 절약해야 하는 처지로 여기지 않게끔 하려는 것이었다. 그러고 나면 늘 죄책감이 들었다. 그 예쁜 물건들이란 좀처럼 꼭 필요한 것이 아니었으니까. 하지만 워낙 값이 싸니 걱정할 거리도 못 된다 싶었고, 그렇게 자잘한 물건들이 자기도 모르게 늘어 갔으며, 쇼핑을 다닐 때 그녀는 더는 가만히 구경만 하는 사람이 아니었다.\n\n하지만 그 자잘한 물건들은 생각보다 값이 더 나갔다. 그래서 월말에 장부를 결산해 보니, 총액이 그녀를 적잖이 놀라게 했다. 그달에 존은 바빠서 청구서를 그녀에게 맡겨 두었다. 다음 달에는 그가 집을 비웠다. 하지만 셋째 달에는 분기별로 거창하게 결산을 하게 되었고, 메그는 그날을 영영 잊지 못했다. 며칠 전 그녀는 끔찍한 짓을 저질렀고, 그것이 양심을 짓눌렀다. 샐리가 비단을 여러 필 사들였던 터라, 메그도 새 비단옷이 하나 갖고 싶었다. 그저 파티에 입고 갈 고운 연한 빛깔의 옷이면 됐다. 검은 비단옷은 너무 흔했고, 저녁 모임에 입을 얇은 옷이란 처녀들에게나 어울리는 것이었으니까. 마치 대고모는 새해마다 자매들에게 으레 한 사람당 이십오 달러씩을 선물로 주었다. 한 달만 기다리면 될 일이었고, 여기 사랑스러운 보랏빛 비단이 헐값에 나와 있었으며, 그녀에게는 그 돈이 있었다. 차마 손을 댈 용기만 낸다면 말이다. 존은 늘 자기 것이 곧 그녀 것이라고 말했다. 하지만 앞으로 받을 이십오 달러뿐 아니라 살림 비용에서 또 이십오 달러까지 쓰는 것을 그가 옳다고 여길까? 그것이 문제였다. 샐리는 그렇게 하라고 부추겼고, 돈을 빌려주겠다고까지 했으며, 더없이 좋은 뜻에서 메그를 그녀의 분수에 넘치도록 유혹했다. 불행한 순간에 점원이 사랑스럽게 어른거리는 비단 주름을 펼쳐 들며 말했다. \"거저나 다름없어요, 부인, 정말입니다.\" 그녀는 대답했다. \"그걸로 할게요.\" 그러자 비단이 잘려 나가고 값이 치러졌으며, 샐리는 의기양양해했고, 그녀는 아무것도 아닌 일인 양 웃었다. 그러고는 마치 무언가를 훔쳐서 경찰이 뒤쫓기라도 하는 듯한 기분으로 마차를 몰아 떠났다.\n\n집에 돌아온 그녀는 사랑스러운 비단을 펼쳐 놓으며 후회의 가책을 달래 보려 했다. 하지만 이제 그것은 덜 은빛으로 보였고, 알고 보니 자신에게 어울리지도 않았으며, \"오십 달러\"라는 글자가 폭마다 무늬처럼 찍혀 있는 것만 같았다. 그녀는 그것을 치워 두었다. 하지만 그것은 그녀를 따라다니며 괴롭혔다. 새 옷이 으레 그러하듯 즐겁게가 아니라, 쉽사리 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 어리석은 짓의 유령처럼 끔찍하게. 그날 밤 존이 장부를 꺼내 들자 메그는 가슴이 철렁 내려앉았고, 결혼 생활을 통틀어 처음으로 남편이 두려워졌다. 그 다정한 갈색 눈이 엄해질 수도 있을 것처럼 보였다. 그리고 그가 여느 때와 달리 즐거워하긴 했지만, 그녀는 그가 자신의 잘못을 다 알아채고도 모른 척 넘어가려는 것이라 짐작했다. 살림 청구서는 모두 치러졌고, 장부도 다 정돈되어 있었다. 존은 그녀를 칭찬하고는, 그들이 \"은행\"이라 부르던 낡은 지갑을 풀고 있었다. 그것이 텅 비어 있다는 걸 아는 메그가 초조하게 그의 손을 막으며 말했다.\n\n\"내 개인 지출 장부는 아직 안 봤잖아요.\"\n\n존은 한 번도 그것을 보자고 한 적이 없었지만, 그녀가 늘 그걸 보라고 우겼다. 여자들이 원하는 별난 물건들에 그가 남자답게 어리둥절해하는 모습을 그녀는 즐겼던 것이다. \"파이핑\"이 뭐냐고 알아맞혀 보라고 하거나, \"허그미타이트\"가 무슨 뜻이냐고 짐짓 다그쳐 묻게 하거나, 장미 봉오리 세 개와 벨벳 한 조각, 끈 한 쌍으로 이루어진 자그마한 물건이 어떻게 보닛이라는 것이며 어떻게 대여섯 달러나 할 수 있는지 의아해하게 만들었던 것이다. 그날 밤 그는 평소처럼, 그녀가 적어 놓은 숫자를 가지고 놀려 대고 그녀의 낭비에 질겁한 척하는 재미를 즐기고 싶어 하는 듯 보였다. 알뜰한 아내를 유난히 자랑스러워하던 그였으니까.\n\n작은 장부가 천천히 꺼내져 그의 앞에 놓였다. 메그는 지친 그의 이마에 잡힌 주름을 펴 준다는 핑계로 의자 뒤로 가서 섰고, 거기 선 채 말마다 점점 더해 가는 공포를 안고 입을 열었다.\n\n\"존, 여보, 내 장부를 보여 주기가 부끄러워요. 사실 요즘 끔찍하게 낭비를 했거든요. 워낙 여기저기 다니다 보니 이것저것 필요해서 그래요, 알잖아요. 샐리가 사라고 권해서 사긴 했어요. 새해에 받을 돈으로 얼마쯤은 치를 수 있을 거예요. 하지만 하고 나서 후회했어요. 당신이 내가 잘못했다고 여길 줄 알았으니까요.\"\n\n존은 웃으며 그녀를 끌어다 곁에 앉히고는 다정하게 말했다. \"가서 숨고 그러지 말아요. 당신이 사람 죽이게 멋진 부츠를 한 켤레 샀다 해도 때리진 않을 테니까. 난 아내 발이 예쁜 게 좀 자랑스럽거든. 좋은 거라면 부츠에 팔구 달러쯤 치른다 해도 상관없소.\"\n\n그것이 바로 그녀가 최근에 산 \"자잘한 물건들\" 가운데 하나였고, 그가 그 말을 하는 동안 그의 눈길이 마침 거기에 떨어진 것이었다. '오, 저 끔찍한 오십 달러에 이르면 뭐라고 할까!' 메그는 몸을 떨며 생각했다.\n\n\"부츠보다 더한 거예요. 비단 드레스예요.\" 그녀는 자포자기한 침착함으로 말했다. 최악의 고비를 어서 넘기고 싶었던 것이다.\n\n\"음, 여보, 그래서 그 '망할 놈의 총액'이 얼마요, 만탈리니 씨 말마따나?\"\n\n그건 존답지 않은 말이었다. 그가 지금까지는 늘 그녀가 거리낌 없이 마주하고 거리낌 없이 답해 오던 그 솔직한 눈빛으로 자신을 올려다보고 있다는 걸 그녀는 알았다. 그녀는 페이지를 넘기면서 동시에 고개를 돌려, 오십 달러가 없어도 충분히 고약했을, 그러나 그 돈이 더해지니 그녀에게는 소름 끼치는 그 액수를 가리켰다. 잠시 방 안이 아주 고요했다. 이윽고 존이 천천히 말했다. 불쾌함을 드러내지 않으려고 애쓰는 것이 그녀에게도 느껴졌다.\n\n\"음, 요즘은 옷 하나를 마무리하는 데 온갖 주름 장식이며 자질구레한 것들이 다 있어야 하니, 드레스 하나에 오십 달러가 그리 많은 건진 모르겠구려.\"\n\n\"아직 짓지도, 장식을 달지도 않은 거예요.\" 메그가 가냘프게 한숨지었다. 앞으로 더 들어갈 비용이 문득 떠올라 그녀를 완전히 짓눌렀던 것이다.\n\n\"비단 스물다섯 마(碼)면 자그마한 여자 하나 감싸기엔 꽤 많은 것 같긴 한데, 내 아내가 그걸 입으면 네드 모펏 부인 못지않게 근사해 보이겠지.\" 존이 무덤덤하게 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 화났다는 거 알아요, 존. 하지만 어쩔 수가 없었어요. 당신 돈을 헤프게 쓸 생각은 없었고, 그 자잘한 것들이 그렇게나 쌓일 줄도 몰랐어요. 샐리가 원하는 건 다 사 대면서, 못 사는 나를 가엾어하는 걸 보면 도무지 못 참겠어요. 만족하려고 애써 보지만, 너무 힘들어요. 이젠 가난한 게 지긋지긋해요.\"\n\n마지막 말은 어찌나 나직이 했던지 그녀는 그가 듣지 못했으리라 여겼다. 하지만 그는 들었고, 그 말은 그에게 깊은 상처를 주었다. 메그를 위해 그는 숱한 즐거움을 마다해 왔으니까. 그 말을 내뱉은 순간 그녀는 혀라도 깨물고 싶은 심정이었다. 존이 장부를 밀쳐 내고 일어서며, 목소리에 가벼운 떨림을 담아 말했기 때문이다. \"이렇게 될까 봐 걱정했소. 난 최선을 다하고 있어요, 메그.\" 그가 그녀를 꾸짖었거나, 심지어 마구 흔들어 댔더라도, 그 몇 마디만큼 그녀의 가슴을 무너뜨리지는 못했을 것이다. 그녀는 그에게 달려가 꼭 끌어안고는, 뉘우치는 눈물을 쏟으며 울부짖었다. \"오, 존, 다정하고 착하고 부지런히 일하는 내 사람, 진심이 아니었어요! 그건 너무 못되고, 너무 사실이 아니고, 너무 배은망덕한 말이었어요, 내가 어떻게 그런 말을 했을까! 오, 어떻게 그런 말을!\"\n\n그는 무척 다정하게 선뜻 용서해 주었고, 단 한마디 책망도 하지 않았다. 하지만 메그는 자신이, 비록 그가 다시는 입에 올리지 않을지라도, 좀처럼 잊히지 않을 짓과 말을 저질렀음을 알았다. 그녀는 좋을 때나 궂을 때나 그를 사랑하겠노라 맹세했었다. 그런데 그의 아내인 그녀가, 그가 벌어 온 돈을 무모하게 써 놓고는, 그의 가난을 들어 그를 책망한 것이었다. 끔찍한 일이었다. 그중에서도 가장 견디기 힘든 것은, 그 뒤로 존이 너무도 조용히 지냈다는 점이었다. 마치 아무 일도 없었던 것처럼. 다만 시내에 더 늦게까지 머물렀고, 그녀가 울다 잠든 밤이면 밤늦도록 일을 했다. 일주일 동안의 가책에 메그는 거의 앓아누울 지경이었다. 그리고 존이 새 외투 주문을 취소했다는 사실을 알게 되자, 그녀는 차마 보기 딱할 만큼 절망에 빠지고 말았다. 무엇이 달라졌느냐는 놀란 물음에 그는 그저 이렇게 대답했을 뿐이었다. \"그럴 형편이 못 돼서요, 여보.\"\n\n메그는 더 말하지 않았다. 하지만 몇 분 뒤 그는 현관에서 그녀를 발견했다. 낡은 외투에 얼굴을 파묻은 채, 가슴이 미어지도록 울고 있는 그녀를.\n\n그날 밤 두 사람은 오래도록 이야기를 나누었고, 메그는 남편의 가난 때문에 그를 더욱 사랑하게 되었다. 그 가난이야말로 그를 한 사람의 어른으로 만들어, 제힘으로 길을 헤쳐 나갈 힘과 용기를 주었고, 사랑하는 이들의 어쩔 수 없는 갈망과 실패를 너그러이 견디고 다독여 줄 다정한 인내심을 가르쳐 준 듯했기 때문이다.\n\n이튿날 그녀는 자존심을 주머니에 넣어 두고 샐리를 찾아가 사실대로 털어놓고는, 부탁이니 그 비단을 사 달라고 청했다. 마음씨 고운 모펏 부인은 기꺼이 그렇게 해 주었고, 곧바로 그것을 메그에게 선물로 주는 무례를 범하지 않을 만큼 세심한 배려를 보였다. 그런 다음 메그는 외투를 집으로 주문해 두었다가, 존이 돌아오자 그것을 입혀 주고는 자기 새 비단 드레스가 어떠냐고 물었다. 그가 어떤 대답을 했을지, 자신의 선물을 어떻게 받았을지, 그리고 얼마나 행복한 상황이 뒤따랐을지는 능히 짐작할 수 있으리라. 존은 일찍 집에 돌아왔고, 메그는 더는 쏘다니지 않았다. 그리고 그 외투는 아침이면 더없이 행복한 남편이 걸쳤고, 밤이면 더없이 헌신적인 작은 아내가 벗겨 주었다. 그렇게 한 해가 흘러갔고, 한여름에 이르러 메그에게 새로운 경험이 찾아왔으니, 여자의 삶에서 가장 깊고 가장 다정한 경험이었다.\n\n어느 토요일, 로리가 들뜬 얼굴로 비둘기 둥지의 부엌으로 살그머니 들어왔다가 심벌즈가 쩌렁쩌렁 울리는 환영을 받았다. 한나가 한 손엔 냄비를, 다른 손엔 뚜껑을 든 채 손뼉을 쳤던 것이다.\n\n\"꼬마 엄마는 좀 어때요? 다들 어디 있고요? 내가 집에 돌아오기 전에 왜 말 안 해 주셨어요?\" 로리가 큰 소리로 속삭이듯 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"여왕님처럼 행복하지요, 그 귀한 양반! 식구들 죄다 위층에서 떠받들고 있다우. 우린 그 근처에서 무슨 회오리바람 같은 건 일으키고 싶지 않았으니까. 자, 응접실로 들어가 있으면 내가 다들 내려보내 드리리다.\" 그렇게 다소 뒤죽박죽인 대답을 남기고 한나는 기뻐서 낄낄대며 사라졌다.\n\n이윽고 조가 큼직한 베개 위에 펼쳐 올린 플란넬 꾸러미를 자랑스레 받쳐 들고 나타났다. 조의 얼굴은 무척 진지했지만, 두 눈은 반짝였고, 목소리에는 무언가 억눌린 감정의 묘한 울림이 배어 있었다.\n\n\"눈 감고 두 팔 내밀어 봐.\" 조가 부추기듯 말했다.\n\n로리는 황급히 구석으로 물러서며 두 손을 뒤로 돌리고는 사정하는 몸짓을 했다. \"아니, 됐어. 그냥 안 할래. 떨어뜨리거나 짓뭉개거나, 틀림없이 그럴 테니까.\"\n\n\"그럼 네 조카는 못 보지.\" 조가 단호하게 말하며 돌아서서 가려는 듯한 시늉을 했다.\n\n\"볼게, 볼게! 대신 무슨 일 생기면 네가 책임져야 해.\" 그리고 명령에 따라 로리가 비장하게 눈을 감자 무언가가 그의 두 팔에 안겨졌다. 조와 에이미, 마치 부인, 한나, 그리고 존이 한꺼번에 터뜨린 웃음소리에 그는 다음 순간 눈을 떴고, 자신이 아기 하나가 아니라 둘을 떠안고 있다는 걸 알아챘다.\n\n그들이 웃은 것도 무리는 아니었다. 영문도 모르는 천진한 것들에서 깔깔대는 구경꾼들에게로 그가 눈을 휘둥그렇게 뜬 채 어찌나 낭패스러운 표정으로 번갈아 쳐다보던지, 그 표정이 퀘이커교도라도 자지러지게 할 만큼 우스꽝스러웠고, 조는 바닥에 주저앉아 비명을 질러 댔다.\n\n\"쌍둥이라니, 맙소사!\" 그가 잠시 동안 할 수 있는 말은 그게 다였다. 그러더니 우습도록 가련하게 애원하는 표정으로 여자들 쪽을 돌아보며 덧붙였다. \"누가 좀 얼른 받아 줘요! 웃음이 터질 것 같은데, 이러다 떨어뜨리겠어요.\"\n\n존이 자기 아기들을 구해 냈고, 한쪽 팔에 하나씩 안은 채 마치 이미 아기 돌보기의 비법을 다 터득한 사람처럼 왔다 갔다 거닐었다. 그동안 로리는 두 뺨에 눈물이 흐를 만큼 웃어 댔다.\n\n\"올 들어 최고의 농담이지, 안 그래요? 미리 알려 주고 싶지 않았어요. 깜짝 놀라게 해 줄 작정이었거든요. 제대로 해낸 것 같아 뿌듯하네요.\" 숨을 돌린 조가 말했다.\n\n\"내 평생 이렇게 얼떨떨해 본 적은 처음이야. 정말 재미있지 않아요? 둘 다 사내아인가요? 이름은 뭐라고 지을 거예요? 한 번 더 보게 해 줘요. 좀 붙잡아 줘, 조. 정말이지 나한테는 하나로도 벅차니까.\" 로리가 마치 덩치 크고 인자한 뉴펀들랜드견이 새끼 고양이 한 쌍을 바라보듯 아기들을 들여다보며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"사내아이 하나, 계집아이 하나야. 예쁘지 않아요?\" 자랑스러운 아빠가 마치 갓 깃털도 나지 않은 천사라도 되는 양 발갛게 꼬물거리는 것들을 향해 환히 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"내 평생 본 아이들 중에 가장 비범한 아이들이에요. 어느 쪽이 어느 쪽인가요?\" 그러면서 로리는 우물의 두레박 장대처럼 허리를 숙여 그 신동들을 살펴보았다.\n\n\"에이미가 프랑스식으로 사내아이한텐 파란 리본을, 계집아이한텐 분홍 리본을 매 줬으니까 늘 구별할 수 있어요. 게다가 하나는 눈이 파랗고 하나는 갈색이고요. 입 맞춰 줘요, 테디 삼촌.\" 짓궂은 조가 말했다.\n\n\"얘들이 싫어할까 봐 걱정인데.\" 로리가 그런 일에는 영 어울리지 않게 머뭇거리며 운을 뗐다.\n\n\"당연히 좋아하지요. 이젠 익숙하니까요. 당장 해요, 어서!\" 조가 명령했다. 그가 대신 시킬 사람이라도 내세울까 봐 걱정스러웠던 것이다.\n\n로리는 얼굴을 잔뜩 찡그리고는 명령에 따라 두 뺨에 조심조심 입을 맞추었는데, 그 바람에 또 한바탕 웃음이 터졌고 아기들은 꽥꽥 울어 댔다.\n\n\"거봐요, 싫어할 줄 알았어요! 이쪽이 사내아이네요. 발버둥 치는 것 좀 봐요. 주먹을 제법 잘도 휘두르네. 자, 어린 브룩, 네 덩치에 맞는 상대한테나 덤벼 보시지?\" 자그마한 주먹이 허공을 아무렇게나 휘젓다가 자기 얼굴을 콕 찌르자 로리가 기뻐서 외쳤다.\n\n\"이 애 이름은 엄마랑 할머니 이름을 따서 존 로렌스로 짓고, 계집애는 마거릿으로 지을 거예요. 메그가 둘이 되지 않게 얘는 데이지라고 부를 거고요. 사내 녀석은 더 나은 이름을 찾지 못하면 잭이 되겠죠.\" 에이미가 이모다운 관심을 보이며 말했다.\n\n\"데미존이라고 짓고, 줄여서 '데미'라고 불러.\" 로리가 말했다.\n\n\"데이지랑 데미라니, 딱 좋네! 테디가 해낼 줄 알았다니까.\" 조가 손뼉을 치며 외쳤다.\n\n테디는 이번만큼은 정말로 해낸 셈이었다. 그 아기들은 끝까지 \"데이지\"와 \"데미\"로 불렸으니 말이다.\n방문\n\n\"조, 어서, 시간 됐어.\"\n\n\"무슨 시간?\"\n\n\"설마 오늘 나랑 여섯 군데 방문하기로 약속한 걸 잊었다는 말은 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"내 평생 경솔하고 어리석은 짓을 숱하게 해 왔지만, 한 군데만 다녀와도 일주일을 시달리는 내가 하루에 여섯 군데를 방문하겠다고 할 만큼 제정신이 아니었던 적은 없는 것 같은데.\"\n\n\"아니, 그랬어. 우리 둘이 약속한 거잖아. 난 언니 대신 베스의 크레용 그림을 마무리해 주고, 언니는 제대로 차려입고 나랑 같이 가서 이웃들 방문 답례를 하기로.\"\n\n\"날이 맑으면, 그게 약속의 조건이었어. 난 약속의 문구 그대로 지킬 거야, 샤일록 양반. 동쪽에 구름이 잔뜩 끼어 있잖아. 날이 안 맑으니까, 난 안 가.\"\n\n\"그건 발뺌이야. 이렇게 화창한 날에 비 올 기미도 없고, 언니는 약속 잘 지키는 걸 자랑으로 여기잖아. 그러니 신의를 지켜. 가서 도리를 다하고 나면, 그다음 여섯 달은 마음 편히 지낼 수 있을 거 아냐.\"\n\n그 순간 조는 옷 짓는 일에 유난히 푹 빠져 있었다. 집안의 재봉 총책임자였던 그녀는 펜만큼이나 바늘도 잘 다룬다는 것을 스스로 대견히 여겼던 것이다. 처음 가봉을 해 보는 와중에 붙들려, 무더운 7월의 어느 날 가장 좋은 옷으로 차려입고 방문을 나가라는 명령을 받는 것은 여간 짜증 나는 일이 아니었다. 그녀는 격식 차린 방문이라면 질색이어서, 에이미가 거래나 미끼, 약속으로 억지로 끌어내지 않는 한 결코 그런 데를 가는 법이 없었다. 이번 경우에는 빠져나갈 길이 없었다. 그래서 천둥 냄새가 난다고 투덜대며 가위를 반항적으로 쩔그럭거리다가, 끝내 손을 들고는 일감을 치워 두고, 체념한 기색으로 모자와 장갑을 집어 들며 제물이 준비됐다고 에이미에게 말했다.\n\n\"조 마치, 언니는 성인군자도 화나게 할 만큼 심술궂어! 설마 그 꼴로 방문을 나갈 셈은 아니겠지.\" 에이미가 어이없다는 듯 언니를 훑어보며 외쳤다.\n\n\"왜 안 돼? 난 단정하고 시원하고 편한걸. 더운 날 먼지 길을 걷기엔 딱 알맞지. 사람들이 나라는 사람보다 내 옷에 더 관심을 둔다면, 난 그런 사람들 보고 싶지도 않아. 넌 두 사람 몫으로 차려입고 마음껏 우아하게 굴면 되잖아. 너야 곱게 꾸미면 본전을 뽑지만, 난 안 그래. 게다가 주름 장식은 나만 성가시게 할 뿐이라고.\"\n\n\"아이참!\" 에이미가 한숨지었다. \"이제 또 청개구리 심보가 도졌으니, 제대로 채비를 시키기도 전에 날 미치게 만들겠네. 오늘 나가는 게 나라고 뭐 즐거워서 그러는 건 아니야. 하지만 이건 우리가 사교계에 진 빚이고, 그걸 갚을 사람이라곤 언니랑 나밖에 없잖아. 뭐든 다 해 줄게, 조, 제발 곱게만 차려입고 와서 예의 차리는 거 좀 거들어 줘. 언니는 마음만 먹으면 말도 어찌나 잘하고, 좋은 옷을 입으면 어찌나 기품 있어 보이고, 행동도 어찌나 멋스러운지, 내가 다 자랑스럽다니까. 혼자 가긴 무서워. 제발 같이 가서 날 좀 지켜 줘.\"\n\n\"심술궂은 늙은 언니를 그런 식으로 살살 구슬려 꾀어내다니, 넌 참 교활한 새끼 고양이로구나. 내가 기품 있고 예의 바른 사람이라느니, 네가 어디 혼자 가기 무섭다느니, 어느 쪽이 더 터무니없는지 모르겠다. 좋아, 꼭 가야 한다면 가서 최선을 다하지. 네가 이 원정대의 대장이 되면, 난 무조건 따를게. 그럼 됐니?\" 조가 청개구리 심보에서 어린양 같은 순종으로 돌변하며 말했다.\n\n\"언니는 완벽한 천사야! 자, 가장 좋은 옷을 다 갖춰 입어. 그리고 가는 곳마다 어떻게 처신해야 좋은 인상을 줄지 내가 일러 줄게. 사람들이 언니를 좋아해 줬으면 해. 언니가 조금만 더 상냥하게 굴려고 애쓰면 다들 좋아할 거야. 머리는 예쁘게 손질하고, 보닛에는 분홍 장미를 꽂아. 그게 잘 어울려. 그 밋밋한 옷차림으론 너무 칙칙해 보이거든. 연한 빛깔 장갑이랑 수놓은 손수건도 챙기고. 메그 언니네 들러서 흰 양산을 빌리면, 언닌 내 비둘기빛 양산을 쓰면 돼.\"\n\n에이미는 채비를 하면서 이런저런 지시를 내렸고, 조는 그대로 따랐다. 물론 군말 없이는 아니었다. 새 오건디 드레스를 사각거리며 입을 때는 한숨을 쉬었고, 보닛 끈을 나무랄 데 없는 매듭으로 맬 때는 자기 자신을 향해 어둡게 눈살을 찌푸렸으며, 깃을 달 때는 핀과 사납게 씨름했고, 손수건을 털어 펼칠 때는 얼굴을 온통 잔뜩 찌푸렸다. 그 손수건의 자수는 이번 임무가 그녀의 심사를 긁는 만큼이나 그녀의 코를 간질였다. 그러고는 우아함의 마지막 손질로, 단추 세 개에 술이 달린 꽉 끼는 장갑 속에 두 손을 우겨 넣고는, 얼빠진 표정으로 에이미를 돌아보며 고분고분 말했다.\n\n\"완전히 비참한 기분이지만, 네가 보기에 봐줄 만하다면, 난 행복하게 죽을게.\"\n\n\"아주 흡족해. 천천히 한 바퀴 돌아 봐. 내가 꼼꼼히 살펴보게.\" 조가 빙글 돌자, 에이미가 여기저기 손을 대 매만지더니, 한 발짝 물러서서 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃하며 흐뭇하게 평했다. \"그래, 그만하면 됐어. 머리는 더 바랄 게 없네. 장미 꽂은 그 흰 보닛은 정말이지 황홀하니까. 어깨는 쫙 펴고, 손은 편하게 놀려. 장갑이 좀 낀다고 해도 상관없어. 언니가 잘하는 게 하나 있는데, 바로 숄을 두르는 거야. 난 못 하는데, 언니가 두른 모습은 정말 보기 좋아. 마치 대고모가 그 사랑스러운 걸 언니한테 줘서 어찌나 다행인지. 수수하지만 근사하고, 팔에 드리워진 그 주름이 정말 멋스럽거든. 내 망토 끝자락이 가운데로 잘 와 있어? 드레스 자락은 고르게 걷어 올렸고? 난 부츠를 좀 내보이고 싶어. 코는 안 예뻐도 발은 예쁘니까.\"\n\n\"넌 영원토록 변치 않을 아름다움이요 기쁨이로다.\" 조가 금빛 머리카락에 어우러진 파란 깃털을 감식가라도 된 양 손으로 가린 채 들여다보며 말했다. \"내 가장 좋은 드레스를 먼지 속에 질질 끌고 다니랍니까, 아니면 걷어 올릴까요, 마님?\"\n\n\"걸을 땐 들어 올리고, 집 안에선 늘어뜨려. 끌리는 차림새가 언니한텐 제일 잘 어울리니까, 치맛자락을 우아하게 끄는 법을 익혀야 해. 한쪽 소맷부리 단추를 반밖에 안 채웠잖아. 당장 채워. 자잘한 부분에 신경 쓰지 않으면 절대 마무리가 깔끔해 보이지 않아. 그런 것들이 모여서 보기 좋은 전체를 이루는 거니까.\"\n\n조는 한숨을 쉬며 소맷부리를 채우다가 장갑 단추를 죄다 뜯어 버리고 말았다. 하지만 마침내 둘 다 채비를 마치고 미끄러지듯 길을 나섰고, 한나가 위층 창밖으로 몸을 내밀어 그들을 지켜보며 말한 대로 \"그림처럼 예쁜\" 모습이었다.\n\n\"자, 조 언니, 체스터네 사람들은 자기들이 무척 우아한 사람들이라고 여기니까, 최대한 점잖게 굴어 줘. 불쑥불쑥 내뱉는 말도 하지 말고, 별난 짓도 하지 마, 알았지? 그냥 차분하고 침착하고 조용하게만 있어. 그게 안전하고 숙녀다운 거야. 십오 분쯤이야 언니도 쉽게 할 수 있잖아.\" 흰 양산을 빌려 들고, 양팔에 아기를 하나씩 안은 메그의 점검까지 받고 나서 첫 번째 집으로 다가가며 에이미가 말했다.\n\n\"어디 보자. '차분하고 침착하고 조용하게'라. 그래, 그건 약속할 수 있을 것 같아. 무대에서 새침한 아가씨 역도 해 봤으니, 무대 밖에서도 한번 해 보지. 내 연기력이 대단하다는 걸 곧 알게 될 거야. 그러니 마음 푹 놓아라, 우리 아가.\"\n\n에이미는 마음이 놓이는 듯했지만, 짓궂은 조는 그 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들였다. 첫 번째 방문 내내 조는 사지를 우아하게 가지런히 모으고, 주름 하나하나를 똑바로 드리운 채, 여름 바다처럼 차분하고 눈더미처럼 냉랭하며 스핑크스처럼 말이 없었다. 체스터 부인이 그녀의 \"멋진 소설\"을 들먹여도, 체스터 가의 아가씨들이 파티며 소풍이며 오페라며 유행 이야기를 꺼내도 헛수고였다. 그 모든 말에 돌아오는 것이라곤 미소와 목례, 그리고 냉기를 잔뜩 머금은 새침한 \"네\"나 \"아니요\"뿐이었다. 에이미가 \"말 좀 해\"라고 눈짓을 보내고, 말을 끌어내 보려 애쓰고, 몰래 발로 슬쩍슬쩍 찔러 봐도 다 헛일이었다. 조는 그 모든 걸 천연덕스럽게 모르는 척 앉아 있었고, 그 처신은 모드의 얼굴처럼 \"차갑도록 반듯하고, 훌륭하도록 무미건조\"했다.\n\n\"저 큰언니 마치 양은 어쩌면 저렇게 거만하고 재미없는 사람일까!\" 손님들 등 뒤로 문이 닫히자 한 부인이 안타깝게도 다 들리도록 한 말이었다. 조는 복도를 지나는 내내 소리 없이 웃었지만, 에이미는 자기 지시가 실패로 돌아간 데 진저리를 치며 아주 자연스럽게 그 탓을 조에게 돌렸다.\n\n\"어쩜 날 그렇게 오해할 수가 있어? 난 그저 언니가 점잖고 차분하게 처신하라는 뜻이었는데, 언니는 완전히 나무토막이나 돌덩이가 돼 버렸잖아. 램네 집에선 좀 싹싹하게 굴어 봐. 다른 여자애들처럼 수다도 떨고, 옷이며 연애 이야기며 뭐든 시답잖은 화제에도 관심을 보이라고. 그 집은 최고의 사교계에서 노는 사람들이라 우리가 알아 두면 요긴한 분들이니까, 거기서만은 무슨 일이 있어도 좋은 인상을 남기고 싶어.\"\n\n\"상냥하게 굴게. 수다도 떨고 까르르 웃고, 네가 원하는 무슨 시시한 일에든 질겁도 하고 황홀해하기도 할게. 이건 좀 재미있는데. 자, 이제 소위 '매력적인 아가씨'라는 걸 흉내 내 볼게. 메이 체스터를 본으로 삼으면 되니 할 수 있어. 게다가 더 멋지게 해낼 거야. 램네 사람들이 '저 조 마치라는 아가씨, 어쩜 저렇게 발랄하고 사랑스러울까!' 하지 않나 봐.\"\n\n에이미는 불안해졌는데, 그럴 만도 했다. 조가 일단 변덕을 부리기 시작하면 어디까지 갈지 알 수 없었으니까. 언니가 다음 응접실로 사뿐히 들어가서는, 젊은 아가씨들 모두에게 호들갑스럽게 입을 맞추고, 젊은 신사들에게 환하게 미소를 지으며, 구경하던 사람이 다 놀랄 만큼 활기차게 잡담에 끼어드는 모습을 본 에이미의 표정은 가관이었다. 에이미는 자기를 아끼는 램 부인에게 붙들려, 루크리셔가 지난번에 또 발작을 일으킨 이야기를 길게 들어 주어야 했다. 그동안 멋진 젊은 신사 셋이 곁을 맴돌며, 그녀를 구해 낼 틈이 나기만을 기다렸다. 그런 처지라 에이미는 조를 막을 도리가 없었는데, 조는 마치 장난기 어린 무언가에 씐 듯 그 노부인 못지않게 거침없이 떠들어 댔다. 사람들 머리가 그녀 주위로 한 무리 모여들자, 에이미는 무슨 이야기가 오가는지 들으려고 귀를 곤두세웠다. 토막토막 끊긴 말들이 그녀를 불안하게 했고, 동그래진 눈과 치켜든 손들이 궁금증으로 그녀를 애태웠으며, 잦은 웃음보가 터질 때마다 그녀는 그 재미에 끼어들지 못해 안달이 났다. 이런 식의 대화 조각들을 엿들으며 그녀가 얼마나 괴로웠을지 능히 짐작이 가리라.\n\n\"말을 정말 잘 타던데요. 누가 가르쳤어요?\"\n\n\"아무도요. 나무에 얹어 둔 헌 안장에 올라타고, 고삐를 잡고, 똑바로 앉는 연습을 하곤 했죠. 이젠 무슨 말이든 다 타요. 두려움이 뭔지를 모르거든요. 마구간지기가 말을 싸게 빌려주기까지 한답니다. 동생이 말을 길들여서 숙녀들도 잘 태우게 해 주니까요. 어찌나 좋아하는지, 제가 종종 그래요. 다른 일이 다 안 풀리면 말 조련사가 돼서 먹고살면 되겠다고요.\"\n\n이 끔찍한 말에 에이미는 가까스로 마음을 추슬렀다. 자신이 꽤나 말괄량이 아가씨라는 인상이 심어지고 있었으니까. 그건 그녀가 무엇보다도 질색하는 것이었다. 하지만 어쩌겠는가? 노부인은 한창 이야기를 늘어놓는 중이었고, 그 이야기가 채 끝나기도 한참 전에 조는 또다시 입을 놀려, 더 익살스러운 폭로를 하고, 더더욱 끔찍한 실수를 저지르고 있었다.\n\n\"네, 에이미가 그날 절망에 빠졌었죠. 쓸 만한 짐승들은 죄다 나가 버리고, 남은 세 마리 중에 하나는 절름발이, 하나는 장님, 나머지 하나는 어찌나 고집이 센지 입에 흙을 물려야만 겨우 움직이는 녀석이었거든요. 나들이 가기엔 참 근사한 짐승이었죠, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"그래서 어느 걸 골랐나요?\" 그 화제를 즐기던 웃음 띤 신사 중 하나가 물었다.\n\n\"하나도 안 골랐어요. 강 건너 농가에 어린 말이 한 마리 있다는 소릴 듣고는, 숙녀가 타 본 적이 없는 말인데도 한번 타 보기로 작정했죠. 잘생기고 기운이 넘쳤거든요. 동생이 어찌나 애를 썼는지 정말 눈물겨웠어요. 말을 안장 있는 데로 데려올 사람이 없으니까, 글쎄 안장을 말한테 가져갔지 뭐예요. 세상에, 그걸 손수 배에 실어 강을 건너서는, 머리에 이고 헛간까지 걸어가는 통에 그 영감님이 입을 떡 벌리고 말았죠!\"\n\n\"그래서 그 말을 탔나요?\"\n\n\"그럼요, 탔고말고요. 아주 신나게 탔는걸요. 전 동생이 산산조각으로 실려 올 줄 알았는데, 어찌나 능숙하게 다루던지, 그 나들이의 활력소가 됐지 뭐예요.\"\n\n\"이야, 그거 참 대단한 배짱이군요!\" 젊은 램 씨가 에이미에게 흐뭇한 눈길을 던지며, 어머니가 무슨 말을 했기에 저 아가씨가 저토록 얼굴을 붉히며 불편해하는지 의아해했다.\n\n잠시 뒤 대화가 갑자기 옷 이야기로 흘러가자, 에이미는 더더욱 얼굴을 붉히며 더욱 불편해했다. 젊은 아가씨 하나가 조에게 소풍 때 쓴 그 예쁜 담갈색 모자를 어디서 샀느냐고 물었다. 그러자 멍청한 조는 그게 이 년 전에 산 곳을 말하는 대신, 굳이 쓸데없이 솔직하게 이렇게 대답하고 말았다. \"아, 그거 에이미가 칠한 거예요. 그렇게 은은한 색조는 사고 싶어도 못 사거든요. 그래서 우린 직접 원하는 색을 칠하죠. 예술가 동생을 둔다는 건 참 큰 위안이에요.\"\n\n\"그거 참 기발한 생각이네요!\" 조가 무척 재미있다고 여긴 램 양이 외쳤다.\n\n\"그건 동생의 빛나는 솜씨에 비하면 아무것도 아니에요. 저 애가 못 하는 게 없거든요. 글쎄, 샐리네 파티에 신을 파란 부츠가 필요하니까, 더러워진 흰 부츠를 여태 본 적 없는 사랑스러운 하늘빛으로 칠해 버렸는데, 영락없이 새틴처럼 보였다니까요.\" 조가 동생의 재주를 자랑스러워하는 기색을 띠며 덧붙였는데, 그 바람에 에이미는 명함 갑이라도 던지면 속이 후련하겠다 싶을 만큼 약이 올랐다.\n\n\"요전 날 댁이 쓰신 소설을 읽었는데, 아주 재미있게 봤어요.\" 손위 램 양이 그 여류 작가를 추어올리고 싶어 한마디 했다. 하기야 그 순간만큼은 작가다운 구석이라곤 도무지 보이지 않았다는 점은 인정해야겠지만.\n\n자기 \"작품\"을 입에 올리는 건 무엇이든 조에게 안 좋은 영향을 끼쳤다. 조는 뻣뻣하게 굳어 기분 상한 표정을 짓거나, 아니면 지금처럼 무뚝뚝한 말로 화제를 돌려 버리곤 했다. \"더 나은 읽을거리를 못 찾으셨다니 안됐네요. 전 그런 쓰레기를 팔리니까 쓰는 거예요. 평범한 사람들이 좋아하거든요. 이번 겨울에 뉴욕에 가세요?\"\n\n램 양은 그 소설을 \"재미있게\" 봤던 터라, 이 말은 딱히 고맙다거나 칭찬으로 들릴 만한 게 아니었다. 말을 내뱉은 순간 조는 자기 실수를 깨달았다. 하지만 일을 더 그르칠까 두려워, 문득 자리를 뜨자고 먼저 나서야 하는 사람이 자기라는 걸 떠올리고는, 어찌나 불쑥 일어섰던지 세 사람이 하던 말을 입 안에 반토막씩 문 채로 남겨 두고 말았다.\n\n\"에이미, 우리 가야 해. 안녕히 계세요, 여러분. 꼭 놀러 오세요. 다들 와 주시기만을 애타게 기다릴 테니까요. 램 씨, 당신을 차마 청하진 못하겠지만, 혹시라도 와 주신다면 차마 돌려보낼 엄두가 안 날 것 같아요.\" 조가 메이 체스터의 호들갑스러운 말투를 어찌나 익살스럽게 흉내 냈던지, 에이미는 웃고 싶으면서도 울고 싶은 심정에 사로잡혀 가능한 한 잽싸게 방을 빠져나갔다.\n\n\"내가 그거 잘하지 않았니?\" 조가 흐뭇한 기색으로 물으며 걸어 나갔다.\n\n\"그보다 더 못할 순 없었을걸.\" 에이미의 대답이 비수처럼 꽂혔다. \"대체 뭐에 씌어서 내 안장 얘기며, 모자며 부츠며, 그 나머지 죄다 떠벌린 거야?\"\n\n\"왜, 재미있잖아. 사람들도 즐거워하고. 우리가 가난한 거야 다들 아는데, 마부를 둔 척, 철마다 모자를 서너 개씩 사는 척, 자기들처럼 만사가 수월하고 호사스러운 척해 봤자 무슨 소용이야.\"\n\n\"우리 살림 꾸리는 그 온갖 군색한 수단을 굳이 죄다 일러바치고, 그렇게 쓸데없이 우리 가난을 까발릴 건 없잖아. 언니는 변변한 자존심이라곤 눈곱만큼도 없고, 언제 입을 다물고 언제 입을 열어야 할지 영영 못 배울 거야.\" 에이미가 절망스럽게 말했다.\n\n가엾은 조는 무안한 얼굴로, 마치 자기 잘못을 속죄라도 하듯 빳빳한 손수건으로 말없이 코끝을 문질렀다.\n\n\"여기선 어떻게 처신할까?\" 세 번째 저택으로 다가가며 조가 물었다.\n\n\"맘대로 해. 난 언니한테서 손 뗐으니까.\" 에이미가 짤막하게 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그럼 난 마음껏 즐겨야지. 사내아이들이 집에 있을 테니, 우린 편하게 어울려 놀 거야. 정말이지 나도 기분 전환이 좀 필요했어. 우아하게 구는 건 내 체질에 안 맞거든.\" 자기가 영 맘에 안 차게 처신한 일에 심사가 뒤틀린 조가 무뚝뚝하게 대꾸했다.\n\n덩치 큰 사내아이 셋과 예쁘장한 아이들 몇몇이 열렬히 반겨 주자 헝클어졌던 그녀의 마음도 금세 누그러졌다. 에이미에게 안주인과, 마침 함께 방문 와 있던 튜더 씨를 상대하도록 맡기고, 조는 젊은 패거리에게 푹 빠져들었으니, 그 기분 전환이 더없이 상쾌했다. 그녀는 대학 시절 이야기에 깊은 관심을 보이며 귀를 기울였고, 군말 없이 포인터며 푸들을 쓰다듬었으며, \"톰 브라운은 정말 멋진 녀석\"이라는, 칭찬치고는 점잖지 못한 표현에도 진심으로 맞장구를 쳤다. 그러다 한 사내아이가 자기 거북이 수조를 보러 가자고 하자, 그녀는 냉큼 따라나섰고, 그 모습에 그 집 안주인은 흐뭇하게 미소 지었다. 그 인자한 부인은 자식들의 곰처럼 거칠지만 다정한 포옹에 엉망이 된 모자를 매만지면서도, 그 모자가 영감받은 프랑스 여인의 손길로 빚어낸 흠잡을 데 없는 머리 모양보다 더 소중하다고 여겼던 것이다.\n\n언니를 제멋대로 하도록 내버려 두고, 에이미는 마음껏 즐기기 시작했다. 튜더 씨의 삼촌은 생존해 있는 어느 귀족의 육촌뻘 되는 영국 부인과 결혼한 사이였고, 에이미는 그 집안 전체를 깍듯이 떠받들었다. 미국에서 나고 자랐으면서도 그녀에게는 작위에 대한 그 경외심이 깃들어 있었으니, 그것은 우리 가운데 가장 훌륭한 이들조차 떨쳐 내지 못하는 마음이요, 왕에 대한 옛 믿음을 향한 인정하기 싫은 충심이었다. 바로 몇 해 전, 노란 머리의 어린 왕족 소년이 찾아왔을 때 이 땅에서 가장 민주적인 나라마저 들끓게 만들었던 그 마음 말이다. 그리고 그것은 지금도, 이 젊은 나라가 늙은 나라에 품는 애정과 얼마간 관련이 있는데, 그 애정이란 마치 위압적인 작은 어머니를 향한 덩치 큰 아들의 마음과도 같다. 그 어머니는 붙잡을 수 있는 한 그를 곁에 붙들어 두다가, 그가 반항하자 작별의 꾸지람과 함께 그를 떠나보냈던 것이다. 하지만 영국 귀족의 먼 친척과 이야기를 나누는 그 만족감조차 에이미가 시간 가는 줄을 잊게 하지는 못했다. 정해진 분수의 시간이 흐르자, 그녀는 아쉬운 마음으로 이 귀족적인 모임에서 몸을 떼어 내고는, 그 구제 불능 언니가 마치 가문에 망신을 끼칠 만한 꼴로 있지나 않기를 간절히 바라며 조를 찾아 두리번거렸다.\n\n더 고약할 수도 있었겠지만, 에이미가 보기엔 그것만으로도 충분히 고약했다. 조는 사내아이들에게 진을 친 듯 빙 둘러싸여 잔디밭에 앉아, 발이 더러운 개 한 마리를 자신의 나들이용 정장 치맛자락에 떡하니 눕혀 둔 채, 로리가 저지른 장난 하나를 감탄하는 청중에게 들려주고 있었다. 어린아이 하나는 에이미가 애지중지하는 양산으로 거북이들을 쿡쿡 찔러 대고 있었고, 둘째 녀석은 조의 가장 좋은 보닛 위에서 생강과자를 먹고 있었으며, 셋째 녀석은 조의 장갑으로 공놀이를 하고 있었다. 하지만 다들 신나게 놀고 있었다. 조가 망가진 자기 물건들을 챙겨 들고 떠나려 하자, 호위대가 그녀를 배웅하며 또 와 달라고 졸랐다. \"로리의 장난 이야기를 듣는 게 어찌나 재미있던지\"라면서.\n\n\"멋진 녀석들이야, 안 그래? 저러고 나니 다시 젊고 생기가 나는 기분인걸.\" 조가 두 손을 등 뒤로 돌린 채 걸으며 말했다. 반쯤은 버릇이었고, 반쯤은 흙탕이 튄 양산을 감추려는 속셈이었다.\n\n\"언닌 왜 늘 튜더 씨를 피해?\" 에이미가 조의 추레해진 행색에 대해서는 슬기롭게 한마디도 하지 않으면서 물었다.\n\n\"그 사람이 싫으니까. 거들먹거리고, 누이들을 면박 주고, 아버지를 속 썩이고, 어머니 얘긴 함부로 하잖아. 로리 말로는 행실이 방탕하다던데, 난 그런 사람이랑 알고 지내고 싶지 않아. 그래서 상대 안 하는 거야.\"\n\n\"적어도 예의는 갖춰야지. 언닌 그 사람한테 쌀쌀맞게 목례만 하고선, 방금 전엔 토미 체임벌린한테는 더없이 깍듯하게 허리 굽혀 인사하고 미소까지 지었잖아. 토미 아버진 식료품 가게나 하는데. 그 목례랑 인사를 서로 바꿔서 했다면 딱 맞았을 거야.\" 에이미가 나무라듯 말했다.\n\n\"천만에, 안 그래.\" 청개구리 같은 조가 대꾸했다. \"난 튜더가 싫고, 존경하지도, 우러르지도 않아. 비록 그 사람 할아버지의 삼촌의 조카의 조카딸이 어느 귀족의 육촌이었다 해도 말이야. 토미는 가난하고 숫기 없고 착하고 무척 똑똑해. 난 그 애를 좋게 보고, 그걸 보여 주는 게 좋아. 갈색 종이 꾸러미를 들고 다닐지언정 그 앤 신사니까.\"\n\n\"언니랑은 따져 봤자 소용이 없어.\" 에이미가 입을 떼었다.\n\n\"눈곱만큼도 소용없지, 우리 아가.\" 조가 가로막았다. \"그러니 우린 상냥한 얼굴이나 하고, 여긴 명함이나 한 장 두고 가자. 킹네 사람들은 보아하니 외출 중인 모양이니까. 정말 다행이다 싶어.\"\n\n가족 명함 갑이 제 소임을 다하자, 자매는 다시 걸음을 옮겼고, 다섯 번째 집에 이르러 그 집 아가씨들이 약속이 있다는 말을 듣자 조는 또 한 번 감사 기도를 올렸다.\n\n\"이제 집에 가자. 오늘 마치 대고모는 그냥 넘어가도 되잖아. 거긴 아무 때나 들를 수 있고, 지치고 짜증 난 마당에 제일 좋은 옷차림으로 먼지 속을 끌고 다니긴 정말 아깝다고.\"\n\n\"그건 언니 사정이고. 대고모는 우리가 격식을 차려 와서 정식으로 문안 인사를 올리는 걸 좋아하셔. 별것 아닌 일이지만 그분을 기쁘게 해 드리는 일이고, 더러운 개들이며 우당탕대는 사내아이들이 언니 옷을 망치게 내버려 두는 것에 비하면 옷이 절반도 안 상할 거야. 허리 좀 숙여 봐. 언니 보닛에 묻은 부스러기 좀 떼어 줄게.\"\n\n\"넌 참 착한 아이야, 에이미!\" 조가 망가진 자기 차림새와, 여전히 산뜻하고 티 한 점 없는 동생의 차림새를 견주어 보며 뉘우치는 눈길로 말했다. \"나도 너처럼 사람들 기쁘게 하는 자잘한 일을 손쉽게 할 수 있으면 좋겠어. 생각이야 하지만, 그걸 하는 데 너무 품이 들어서 말이야. 그래서 큰 호의를 베풀 기회가 오기를 기다리느라 작은 것들은 흘려보내곤 하지. 하지만 결국엔 그 작은 것들이 제일 빛을 발하는 것 같아.\"\n\n에이미는 미소를 지으며 금세 누그러져, 어머니 같은 태도로 말했다.\n\n\"여자라면, 특히 가난한 여자라면 상냥해지는 법을 익혀야 해. 받은 친절을 갚을 길이 그것밖에 없으니까. 언니가 그걸 명심하고 몸에 익힌다면, 나보다 더 사랑받을 거야. 언닌 속이 더 깊으니까.\"\n\n\"난 별난 늙은이고, 앞으로도 늘 그럴 테지만, 네 말이 옳다는 건 기꺼이 인정해. 다만 난 누구한테 마음이 안 내킬 때 상냥하게 구는 것보다, 차라리 그 사람을 위해 목숨을 거는 게 더 쉬워. 호불호가 이렇게 분명하다는 건 참 큰 불행이야, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"그걸 감추지 못하는 게 더 큰 불행이지. 나도 언니 못지않게 튜더가 마뜩잖다는 거 숨길 생각 없어. 하지만 굳이 그 사람한테 그렇다고 말할 까닭은 없잖아. 언니도 그렇고. 그 사람이 마음에 안 든다고 굳이 언니가 까칠하게 굴어 봤자 아무 소용 없어.\"\n\n\"하지만 난 여자들이 젊은 남자를 못마땅해할 땐 그걸 드러내야 한다고 봐. 처신 말고 무슨 수로 그걸 보여 주겠어? 설교는 아무 소용이 없어. 테디를 다뤄 보고 뼈저리게 깨달았지. 하지만 말 한마디 없이도 그 애한테 영향을 줄 자잘한 방법이 얼마든지 있어. 그러니 우리도 할 수만 있다면 다른 사람들한테도 그래야 한다는 거야.\"\n\n\"테디는 유별난 애라 다른 남자들의 본보기로 삼을 순 없어.\" 에이미가 자못 단호한 확신의 어조로 말했는데, 그 \"유별난 애\"가 이 말을 들었더라면 자지러졌을 것이다. \"우리가 미녀이거나 재산과 지위를 갖춘 여자라면 어쩌면 뭔가 할 수 있을지도 몰라. 하지만 우리 같은 처지에 어떤 젊은 신사들이 마음에 안 든다고 인상을 쓰고, 어떤 이들은 마음에 든다고 미소를 보낸다 한들 눈곱만큼도 효과가 없을걸. 그저 별나고 청교도처럼 깐깐하다는 소리나 듣겠지.\"\n\n\"그럼 우린 미녀도 백만장자도 아니라는 이유만으로, 우리가 질색하는 일이며 사람들을 그저 두둔해 줘야 한다 이거지? 그것참 훌륭한 도덕이로구나.\"\n\n\"난 그거에 대해선 따질 줄 모르겠고, 다만 그게 세상 이치라는 것만은 알아. 거기에 맞서는 사람들은 애만 쓰고 비웃음이나 사는 거야. 난 개혁가들이 싫어. 언니도 부디 그런 사람이 되려고 하지 않았으면 좋겠어.\"\n\n\"난 그 사람들이 좋아. 그리고 할 수만 있다면 나도 그런 사람이 될 거야. 비웃음을 사긴 해도, 그런 사람들이 없으면 세상은 결코 나아가지 못할 테니까. 이건 우리 둘이 영영 합의 못 볼 문제야. 넌 낡은 패에 속하고, 난 새 패에 속하니까. 넌 잘 살아 나가겠지만, 난 누구보다 활기찬 삶을 살 거야. 돌팔매질이나 야유쯤은 차라리 즐길 수 있을 것 같아.\"\n\n\"자, 이제 그만 마음 좀 가라앉히고, 대고모 앞에서 그 새로운 생각으로 속 썩이지 마.\"\n\n\"안 그러려고 애써 보겠지만, 난 늘 대고모 앞에만 가면 유난히 무뚝뚝한 말이나 혁명 같은 소리를 불쑥 내뱉고 싶어 안달이 나. 그게 내 팔자인걸, 어쩔 수가 없어.\"\n\n가 보니 캐럴 고모가 노부인과 함께 있었는데, 둘 다 무척 흥미로운 어떤 화제에 푹 빠져 있었다. 하지만 자매가 들어서자 그들은 그 이야기를 뚝 그쳤고, 자기네 조카딸들 얘기를 하고 있었음을 들켜 버린 멋쩍은 기색이 역력했다. 조는 기분이 좋지 않았던 데다 그 청개구리 심보가 다시 도졌다. 하지만 갸륵하게도 제 도리를 다하고 성미를 잘 다스려 모두를 흐뭇하게 한 에이미는 더없이 천사 같은 마음 상태였다. 이 사랑스러운 기색은 대번에 전해져, 두 고모할머니는 그녀를 다정하게 \"아가, 아가\" 하고 불렀고, 나중에 힘주어 말한 대로 이런 눈빛을 보냈다. \"저 애는 날마다 더 나아진다니까.\"\n\n\"바자회는 거들 거니, 얘야?\" 에이미가 곁에 앉자 캐럴 부인이 물었다. 어른들이 젊은이에게서 그토록 좋아하는, 그 미덥게 따르는 태도를 보이며 앉은 에이미에게.\n\n\"네, 고모할머니. 체스터 부인이 도와주겠느냐고 물으셔서, 제가 가판대 하나를 맡겠다고 했어요. 저야 내드릴 거라곤 시간밖에 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"난 안 해요.\" 조가 단호하게 끼어들었다. \"전 누가 베푸는 척하는 게 질색이거든요. 게다가 체스터네 사람들은 자기네 그 으리으리한 바자회 일을 우리한테 거들게 해 주는 걸 무슨 큰 은혜라도 베푸는 줄 알아요. 에이미가 어떻게 승낙했는지 모르겠네요. 그 사람들은 그저 너를 부려 먹고 싶은 것뿐이야.\"\n\n\"난 기꺼이 일하겠어. 그건 체스터네 사람들만이 아니라 해방 노예들을 위한 일이기도 하고, 내가 그 수고와 즐거움을 함께 나누게 해 주는 게 난 무척 고마운 일이라고 봐. 좋은 뜻에서 베푸는 거라면 난 그런 호의가 거슬리지 않거든.\"\n\n\"아주 옳고 마땅한 말이다. 난 네 그 고마워하는 마음씨가 좋구나, 얘야. 우리 정성을 알아주는 사람들을 돕는 건 즐거운 일이지. 더러 그러지 않는 이들도 있는데, 그게 참 사람을 힘 빠지게 하거든.\" 마치 대고모가 안경 너머로, 다소 시무룩한 표정으로 한쪽에 떨어져 몸을 흔들고 있는 조를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n만약 둘 중 하나에게 얼마나 큰 행복이 저울 위에서 위태롭게 흔들리고 있는지를 조가 알기만 했더라도, 그녀는 당장 비둘기처럼 온순해졌으련만.\n안타깝게도 우리 가슴에는 창문이 달려 있지 않아서, 친구의 마음속에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 들여다볼 수가 없다. 대개는 그러는 편이 우리에게 이롭지만, 가끔은 그것이 얼마나 위안이 될지, 또 얼마나 시간과 감정을 아껴 줄지 모른다. 조는 다음에 내뱉은 말 한마디로 몇 해에 걸친 즐거움을 스스로 날려 버렸고, 입을 다무는 법에 관한 때맞은 교훈을 얻었다.\n\n\"전 호의를 받는 게 싫어요. 부담스럽기도 하고, 꼭 노예가 된 기분이 들거든요. 차라리 뭐든 제 손으로 하고, 완전히 독립적으로 사는 게 나아요.\"\n\n\"에헴!\" 캐럴 고모할머니가 마치 고모할머니를 흘낏 쳐다보며 나직이 헛기침을 했다.\n\n\"내가 그랬잖니.\" 마치 고모할머니가 캐럴 고모할머니에게 단호히 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n자신이 무슨 짓을 저질렀는지 다행히도 까맣게 모른 채, 조는 코를 치켜들고 앉아 있었는데, 그 반항적인 표정이라곤 도무지 호감을 살 만한 것이 못 되었다.\n\n\"얘야, 너는 프랑스어를 할 줄 아니?\" 캐럴 부인이 에이미의 손 위에 자기 손을 얹으며 물었다.\n\n\"꽤 잘해요. 다 마치 고모할머니 덕분이죠. 제가 원할 때마다 에스더하고 얘기하게 해 주셨거든요.\" 에이미가 감사 어린 눈빛으로 대답하자, 노부인은 흐뭇하게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"너는 외국어에 어떠냐?\" 캐럴 부인이 조에게 물었다.\n\n\"한마디도 못해요. 전 뭘 공부하는 데는 영 머리가 나빠서요. 프랑스어라면 질색이에요. 그렇게 미끈거리고 어리석은 언어가 또 없거든요.\" 퉁명스러운 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n두 부인 사이에 또 한 차례 눈길이 오갔고, 마치 고모할머니가 에이미에게 말했다. \"얘야, 이제 너는 아주 튼튼하고 건강하지, 아마? 눈도 더는 말썽을 부리지 않고, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"전혀요, 고맙습니다, 고모할머니. 아주 건강해요. 그래서 다음 겨울에는 큰 공부를 할 작정이에요. 그 즐거운 날이 언제 오든 로마에 갈 채비를 해 두려고요.\"\n\n\"착하기도 하지! 갈 자격이 있고말고. 언젠가 꼭 가게 될 거란다.\" 에이미가 떨어진 털실 뭉치를 주워 주자, 마치 고모할머니가 머리를 쓰다듬으며 흐뭇하게 말했다.\n\n\"심술쟁이야, 빗장을 걸고 난롯가에 앉아 실을 잣거라.\"\n\n폴리가 의자 등받이 위 횃대에서 몸을 숙여 조의 얼굴을 들여다보며 꽥꽥거렸는데, 그 무례하게 캐묻는 듯한 우스꽝스러운 태도가 어찌나 익살맞은지 웃지 않고는 배길 수가 없었다.\n\n\"여간 눈썰미 있는 새가 아니구나.\" 노부인이 말했다.\n\n\"같이 산책하러 갈까, 얘야?\" 폴리가 각설탕을 떠올리게 하는 표정으로 도자기장 쪽으로 폴짝폴짝 뛰어가며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"고마워, 그러자. 가자, 에이미.\" 조는 이렇게 방문을 끝맺으면서, 남의 집을 찾아가는 일이 자기 체질에는 영 안 맞는다는 사실을 그 어느 때보다 강하게 느꼈다. 조는 사내답게 악수를 했지만, 에이미는 두 고모할머니에게 입을 맞추었다. 두 소녀가 떠난 자리에는 그늘과 햇살 같은 인상이 남았고, 그 인상 때문에 둘이 사라지자 마치 고모할머니가 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 하는 게 좋겠다, 메리. 돈은 내가 대마.\" 그러자 캐럴 고모할머니가 단호하게 대답했다. \"애 부모만 동의한다면야 당연히 그래야지요.\"\n\n체스터 부인의 자선 바자회는 어찌나 우아하고 격조 높은 행사인지, 동네 젊은 아가씨들 사이에서는 판매대를 하나 맡아 달라는 초대를 받는 것이 대단한 영광으로 여겨졌고, 다들 그 일에 큰 관심을 쏟았다. 에이미는 초대를 받았지만 조는 받지 못했는데, 이는 모두에게 다행스러운 일이었다. 이 무렵 조는 양 팔꿈치를 잔뜩 치켜든 채로 살았고, 세상을 무던하게 헤쳐 나가는 법을 깨우치기까지 호된 부딪침을 숱하게 겪어야 했기 때문이다. \"거만하고 재미라곤 없는 인간\"은 매정하게 따돌림을 당했지만, 에이미의 재능과 안목은 미술 판매대를 맡아 달라는 제안으로 마땅한 인정을 받았고, 에이미는 그 판매대에 어울리는 값진 출품작을 준비하고 확보하느라 온 힘을 쏟았다.\n\n바자회가 열리기 전날까지는 모든 일이 순조롭게 흘러갔다. 그러다 작은 다툼 하나가 벌어졌는데, 노소를 막론한 스물다섯 명 남짓한 여자들이 저마다의 사사로운 앙심과 편견을 안고 함께 일하려 들 때면 거의 피할 수 없는 종류의 충돌이었다. 메이 체스터는 에이미를 다소 질투했다. 에이미가 자기보다 더 많은 사랑을 받았기 때문인데, 마침 이 무렵 그 감정을 부추기는 사소한 일이 몇 가지 겹쳐 일어났다. 에이미의 정교한 펜화가 메이가 그린 꽃병 그림을 완전히 무색하게 만든 것이 첫 번째 가시였다. 그다음으로 모든 이를 사로잡는 튜더가 얼마 전 어느 파티에서 에이미와는 네 번 춤을 추고 메이와는 단 한 번밖에 추지 않은 것이 두 번째 가시였다. 그러나 그 영혼 속에서 가장 사무치게 곪아 들며 비우호적인 행동의 빌미가 되어 준 가장 큰 불만은, 어떤 친절한 수다쟁이가 귀띔해 준 소문이었다. 즉, 마치 집안 딸들이 램네 집에서 자기를 흉봤다는 것이었다. 이 일의 책임은 마땅히 조에게 돌아갔어야 했다. 조의 짓궂은 흉내가 너무도 실감 나서 들키지 않을 수가 없었던 데다, 장난기 많은 램네 사람들이 그 농담이 새어 나가도록 내버려 두었기 때문이다. 그러나 정작 죄를 지은 당사자들에게는 이런 낌새가 조금도 전해지지 않았으니, 바자회 바로 전날 저녁, 에이미가 자신의 예쁜 판매대에 마지막 손질을 하고 있을 때 자기 딸이 비웃음을 당했다고 여겨 당연히 분개한 체스터 부인이 부드러운 말투에 차가운 눈빛으로 이렇게 말했을 때, 에이미가 얼마나 당혹스러웠을지는 충분히 짐작할 수 있을 것이다.\n\n\"얘야, 내가 알아보니 이 판매대를 우리 애들이 아닌 다른 사람에게 맡긴 것을 두고 젊은 아가씨들 사이에 말이 좀 있더구나. 이게 가장 눈에 띄는 데다, 어떤 이들은 모든 판매대 중에서 제일 매력적이라고도 하고, 또 우리 애들이 이 바자회를 주도해서 꾸린 만큼, 그 애들이 이 자리를 맡는 편이 가장 좋다는 의견이거든. 미안하구나. 하지만 너는 진심으로 이 일에 마음을 쏟고 있으니 사소한 개인적 실망쯤은 개의치 않으리라 믿는다. 원한다면 다른 판매대를 하나 줄게.\"\n\n체스터 부인은 이 짧은 말을 늘어놓기가 쉬울 거라고 미리 짐작했었다. 그러나 막상 그 순간이 닥치자, 놀라움과 곤혹스러움이 가득한 에이미의 의심 없는 두 눈이 자기를 똑바로 바라보고 있으니, 그 말을 자연스럽게 내뱉기가 여간 어려운 것이 아니었다.\n\n에이미는 이 말 뒤에 뭔가가 숨어 있다는 것을 느꼈지만 그것이 무엇인지는 짐작하지 못했고, 상처받은 기색을 그대로 드러내며 조용히 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 차라리 제가 판매대를 아예 안 맡는 편이 나으시겠어요?\"\n\n\"얘야, 제발 나쁘게 받아들이지 말아 다오. 그저 편의를 따진 것뿐이란다, 알겠니? 우리 애들이 당연히 앞장을 서야 하고, 이 판매대는 그 애들에게 어울리는 자리로 여겨지니까. 나는 너에게 아주 잘 맞는다고 생각한단다. 이렇게 예쁘게 꾸미려고 애써 줘서 정말 고맙고. 하지만 우리 모두 사사로운 바람은 접어야 하지 않겠니. 다른 좋은 자리를 꼭 마련해 주마. 꽃 판매대는 어떻겠니? 어린 여자애들이 맡았는데, 그 애들이 기가 좀 꺾여 있단다. 너라면 아주 멋지게 꾸밀 수 있을 거야. 알다시피 꽃 판매대는 언제나 인기가 많잖니.\"\n\n\"특히 신사분들한테요.\" 메이가 한마디 덧붙였는데, 그 눈빛이 에이미에게 자신이 갑작스레 총애를 잃게 된 한 가지 까닭을 알려 주었다. 에이미는 화가 나서 얼굴이 붉어졌지만, 그 어린애 같은 빈정거림에는 더는 아랑곳하지 않고 뜻밖의 상냥함으로 대답했다.\n\n\"부인 뜻대로 하세요, 체스터 부인. 원하신다면 지금 당장 이 자리를 내드리고 꽃을 맡을게요.\"\n\n\"네 물건은 네 판매대에 그대로 놓아도 돼. 그러고 싶다면 말이야.\" 메이가 운을 떼었는데, 에이미가 그토록 정성스레 만들어 우아하게 배치해 둔 예쁜 받침대며 채색한 조개껍데기며 고풍스러운 채식화를 바라보고 있자니 양심에 조금 찔리는 모양이었다. 좋은 뜻으로 한 말이었으나, 에이미는 그 뜻을 오해하고 얼른 대꾸했다.\n\n\"아, 물론이죠, 거치적거리신다면요.\" 그러고는 자기 출품작을 앞치마에 마구잡이로 쓸어 담더니, 자기 자신과 자기 예술 작품이 도저히 용서 못 할 만큼 모욕당했다고 느끼며 걸어 나갔다.\n\n\"이제 쟤 단단히 화났네. 아이참, 엄마한테 말해 달라고 부탁하지 말걸 그랬어.\" 메이가 자기 판매대에 휑하니 빈자리를 쓸쓸하게 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"여자애들 싸움은 금세 가라앉는 법이란다.\" 어머니가 대답했지만, 자신도 이 다툼에 한몫했다는 사실에 조금은 부끄러움을 느꼈는데, 그럴 만도 했다.\n\n어린 여자애들은 에이미와 그 보물 같은 물건들을 반갑게 맞이했고, 그 따뜻한 환대 덕분에 에이미의 뒤숭숭했던 마음이 다소 가라앉았다. 에이미는 예술적으로는 못 해도 꽃으로는 기어이 성공하리라 다짐하며 일에 매달렸다. 그러나 모든 것이 그녀를 가로막는 듯했다. 시간은 늦었고, 그녀는 지쳐 있었으며, 다들 제 일에 너무 바빠 도와줄 사람이 없었고, 어린 여자애들은 그저 방해만 될 뿐이었다. 그 귀여운 것들이 까치 떼처럼 부산을 떨고 재잘대면서, 완벽한 질서를 지키겠다는 천진한 노력으로 오히려 큰 혼란만 일으켰던 것이다. 상록수 아치는 세워 놓고 나니 좀처럼 단단히 서 있질 못하고 흔들거렸고, 매다는 바구니에 꽃을 채우자 그녀 머리 위로 와르르 무너질 듯했다. 그녀가 가장 아끼는 타일에는 물이 튀어 큐피드의 뺨에 갈색 눈물 자국이 남았고, 망치질을 하다 손에 멍이 들었으며, 외풍 속에서 일하다 감기에 걸렸는데, 마지막 이 시련은 그녀에게 이튿날에 대한 걱정을 한가득 안겨 주었다. 이와 같은 고초를 겪어 본 여자 독자라면 가엾은 에이미에게 공감하며, 그녀가 그 일을 무사히 끝마치기를 빌어 줄 것이다.\n\n그날 저녁 에이미가 자초지종을 털어놓자 집 안에는 큰 분노가 일었다. 어머니는 너무하다고 하면서도, 에이미가 옳게 처신했다고 말해 주었다. 베스는 자기는 바자회에 아예 가지 않겠다고 잘라 말했고, 조는 어째서 예쁜 물건들을 죄다 챙겨 와서 그 못된 사람들끼리 알아서 하게 내버려 두지 않았느냐고 따져 물었다.\n\n\"그 사람들이 못됐다고 해서 나까지 못되게 굴어야 할 이유는 없잖아. 난 그런 게 싫어. 상처받을 만하다고 생각은 하지만, 그걸 내색할 마음은 없어. 그러는 편이 화난 말이나 토라진 행동보다 그 사람들한테 더 사무칠 거야, 그렇지 않아요, 엄마?\"\n\n\"바로 그 마음이 옳단다, 얘야. 때리는 손에 입맞춤으로 갚는 게 언제나 최선이지, 비록 가끔은 그러기가 그리 쉽지 않더라도 말이야.\" 어머니가 설교와 실천의 차이를 깨우친 사람다운 태도로 말했다.\n\n분개하고 보복하고 싶은 지극히 자연스러운 유혹이 여러 차례 일었지만, 에이미는 친절로 적을 이기겠다고 마음먹은 채 이튿날 내내 그 결심을 지켰다. 뜻밖에, 그러나 더없이 때맞춰 찾아온 어떤 말없는 깨우침 덕분에 에이미는 좋은 출발을 끊었다. 그날 아침, 어린 여자애들이 곁방에서 바구니를 채우는 동안 판매대를 정리하다가, 에이미는 자기가 가장 아끼는 작품을 집어 들었다. 작은 책이었는데, 아버지가 자신의 소장품 가운데서 찾아낸 고풍스러운 표지를 단 것으로, 양피지 책장마다 여러 성구를 아름답게 채식해 둔 것이었다. 정교한 도안으로 가득한 책장을 꽤나 봐줄 만한 자부심을 느끼며 넘기다가, 에이미의 눈길이 한 구절에 멈춰 그녀를 잠시 생각에 잠기게 했다. 진홍과 파랑과 금빛의 화려한 당초무늬 테두리 안에, 가시와 꽃 사이를 오르내리며 서로를 돕는 작은 선의의 정령들과 함께, 이런 말이 적혀 있었다. \"네 이웃을 네 몸과 같이 사랑하라.\"\n\n'나도 그래야 하는데, 그러질 못하잖아.' 에이미는 그렇게 생각하며, 밝은 책장에서 큰 꽃병들 뒤편 메이의 불만 어린 얼굴로 눈길을 옮겼다. 그 꽃병들도 에이미의 예쁜 작품들이 한때 채웠던 빈자리를 가리지는 못했다. 에이미는 잠시 그 자리에 서서 손안의 책장을 넘기며, 가슴속의 온갖 앙금과 매정한 마음을 향한 다정한 꾸짖음을 한 장 한 장에서 읽어 나갔다. 거리에서, 학교에서, 일터나 가정에서, 그것을 의식하지 못하는 설교자들이 날마다 우리에게 지혜롭고 참된 설교를 숱하게 들려준다. 자선 바자회 판매대조차도, 철 지나는 법 없는 선하고 이로운 말을 건넬 수만 있다면 하나의 강단이 될 수 있는 것이다. 바로 그때 그 자리에서, 에이미의 양심은 그 성구로 짤막한 설교를 베풀었다. 그리고 에이미는 우리 대다수가 늘 하지는 못하는 일을 했다. 그 설교를 가슴에 새기고는, 곧바로 실천에 옮긴 것이다.\n\n여자애들 한 무리가 메이의 판매대 주위에 둘러서서 예쁜 물건들에 감탄하며 판매원이 바뀐 일을 두고 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 그들은 목소리를 낮추었지만, 에이미는 그들이 자기 이야기를 하고 있다는 걸 알았다. 그들은 이야기의 한쪽 면만 듣고서 거기에 맞춰 판단하고 있었다. 유쾌한 일은 아니었지만, 에이미에게는 더 나은 마음이 깃들어 있었고, 마침 그것을 증명할 기회가 찾아왔다. 메이가 서글프게 말하는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"너무 안타까워. 다른 걸 만들 시간도 없고, 자질구레한 것들로 채우긴 싫은데. 그때는 판매대가 딱 완벽했잖아. 이제는 망쳐 버렸어.\"\n\n\"네가 부탁하면 도로 갖다 놓아 줄 것 같은데.\" 누군가가 넌지시 말했다.\n\n\"그런 소동을 다 벌이고 나서 내가 어떻게 그래?\" 메이가 운을 떼다가 말을 끝맺지 못했으니, 에이미의 목소리가 홀을 가로질러 상냥하게 들려왔기 때문이다.\n\n\"부탁할 것 없이 그냥 가져도 돼. 필요하다면 얼마든지. 마침 도로 갖다 놓겠다고 할까 생각하던 참이었거든. 내 판매대보다는 네 판매대에 더 어울리는 물건들이니까. 여기 있어. 부디 받아 줘. 그리고 어젯밤에 성급하게 들고 가 버린 거 용서해 줘.\"\n\n말을 하면서 에이미는 고갯짓과 미소와 함께 자기 출품작을 돌려주고는 다시 서둘러 자리를 떴다. 친절을 베푸는 것이, 거기 남아서 고맙다는 인사를 받는 것보다 차라리 쉽다고 느끼면서.\n\n\"이거야말로 정말 멋지지 않니?\" 한 여자애가 외쳤다.\n\n메이의 대답은 들리지 않았다. 그러나 레모네이드를 만드느라 심사가 좀 뒤틀린 게 분명한 또 다른 아가씨가 못마땅한 웃음을 지으며 덧붙였다. \"정말 멋지지. 어차피 자기 판매대에서는 못 팔 거란 걸 알았으니까.\"\n\n이건 좀 야박한 말이었다. 작은 희생을 치를 때면 우리는 적어도 알아주기라도 바라는 법이니까. 그래서 에이미는 잠시, 미덕이 늘 그 자체로 보상이 되는 건 아니라고 느끼며, 괜히 그랬다고 후회했다. 그러나 미덕은 분명 그 자체로 보상이 되었으니, 곧 에이미가 깨닫게 되었듯이 그녀의 기분이 차츰 살아나기 시작했고, 솜씨 좋은 손길 아래 판매대가 꽃처럼 피어났던 것이다. 여자애들은 무척 친절했고, 그 작은 행동 하나가 분위기를 놀라울 만큼 말끔하게 갈아 놓은 듯했다.\n\n에이미에게는 무척 긴 하루, 또 고된 하루였다. 어린 여자애들이 금세 자리를 떠 버린 탓에 에이미는 판매대 뒤에 혼자 앉아 있을 때가 많았다. 여름철에 꽃을 사려는 사람은 드물었고, 그녀의 꽃다발은 밤이 되기 한참 전부터 시들기 시작했다.\n\n미술 판매대는 그 방에서 정말로 가장 인기가 많았다. 온종일 그 주위로 사람들이 북적였고, 판매를 맡은 이들은 자못 중요해 보이는 얼굴로 돈 상자를 절그럭거리며 쉴 새 없이 이리저리 분주히 오갔다. 에이미는 자기 마음이 편하고 행복하다고 느껴지는 그곳에 가 있고 싶어, 할 일 없이 구석에 처박혀 있는 대신 거기 있고 싶어, 종종 사무치는 눈길로 건너편을 바라보았다. 우리 가운데 누군가에게는 이것이 별로 대수롭지 않은 고생처럼 보일지도 모른다. 그러나 예쁘고 쾌활한 어린 아가씨에게는 지루할 뿐 아니라 무척 견디기 힘든 일이었다. 게다가 저녁에 가족과 로리와 그 친구들에게 이런 데 있는 모습을 들킬 거라는 생각은 그것을 진짜 순교나 다름없게 만들었다.\n\n에이미는 밤이 되어서야 집에 돌아왔는데, 얼굴이 어찌나 핼쑥하고 말이 없는지, 식구들은 불평 한마디 없고 자기가 한 일을 입 밖에 내지도 않았지만 그 하루가 고됐다는 걸 알 수 있었다. 어머니는 그녀에게 평소보다 진하게 우려낸 차 한 잔을 따라 주었고, 베스는 옷 입는 것을 도우며 머리에 꽂을 어여쁜 작은 화관을 만들어 주었다. 한편 조는 평소답지 않게 공들여 차려입어 식구들을 놀라게 하고는, 곧 형세가 뒤집힐 거라고 은근히 의미심장하게 언질을 주었다.\n\n\"제발 무례한 짓은 하지 마, 조 언니. 난 소란 떠는 거 싫으니까, 다 그냥 넘어가고 얌전히 굴어.\" 에이미가 가엾은 자기 판매대를 새롭게 꾸며 줄 꽃 지원군을 구할 수 있기를 바라며 일찍 나서면서 당부했다.\n\n\"난 그저 내가 아는 모든 사람한테 정신을 못 차릴 만큼 상냥하게 굴어서, 그 사람들을 되도록 오래 네 구석에 붙잡아 둘 작정일 뿐이야. 테디하고 걔네 친구들도 거들어 줄 테니, 우리 아주 신나게 놀아 보자고.\" 조가 대문에 기대 로리를 기다리며 대답했다. 이윽고 땅거미 속에서 귀에 익은 발소리가 들려오자, 조는 달려 나가 그를 맞이했다.\n\n\"우리 도련님인가?\"\n\n\"여기 있는 게 우리 아가씨인 게 분명하듯이!\" 로리는 모든 소원이 다 이루어진 사내 같은 태도로 조의 손을 자기 팔짱 안에 끼웠다.\n\n\"아, 테디, 글쎄 이런 일이 있었어!\" 조는 누이다운 열의로 에이미가 당한 부당한 일을 들려주었다.\n\n\"우리 패거리가 좀 있다가 마차 몰고 우르르 갈 거야. 걔들을 시켜서 에이미가 가진 꽃을 한 송이도 빠짐없이 사게 하고, 그러고 나서 걔 판매대 앞에 진을 치고 앉지 않으면 내 손에 장을 지지지.\" 로리가 열을 내며 조의 편을 들었다.\n\n\"꽃이 영 신통치 않다고 에이미가 그러더라. 게다가 새 꽃은 제때 도착하지 않을지도 몰라. 부당하게 굴거나 의심하고 싶진 않지만, 그 꽃이 아예 안 와도 난 놀라지 않을 것 같아. 사람이 한 가지 못된 짓을 하면 또 한 가지를 하기 십상이거든.\" 조가 진저리 난다는 투로 말했다.\n\n\"헤이스가 우리 정원에서 제일 좋은 걸 골라 주지 않았어? 내가 그러라고 했는데.\"\n\n\"그건 몰랐는걸. 그 사람이 깜빡한 모양이야. 그리고 너희 할아버지가 편찮으셔서, 꽃이 좀 필요하긴 했지만 여쭤봐서 성가시게 해 드리고 싶지가 않았어.\"\n\n\"이런, 조, 어떻게 굳이 부탁할 필요가 있다고 생각할 수가 있어! 그건 내 것이나 마찬가지로 네 것이기도 한걸. 우리 늘 뭐든 반씩 나누지 않아?\" 로리가 늘 조를 가시 돋게 만드는 그 말투로 말문을 열었다.\n\n\"세상에, 제발 그러지 말아 줘! 네 물건 중에는 반쪽 가져도 나한테 영 안 어울리는 게 있단 말이야. 그나저나 여기서 이렇게 시시덕거리고 있을 때가 아니지. 난 에이미를 도와야 하니까, 너는 가서 멋지게 차려입고 와. 그리고 헤이스 시켜서 회관에 좋은 꽃 몇 송이만 갖다 놓도록 그렇게 친절을 베풀어 주면, 내가 평생 너를 축복할게.\"\n\n\"지금 그러면 안 될까?\" 로리가 어찌나 의미심장하게 묻는지, 조는 야박할 만큼 다급히 그의 면전에서 대문을 닫아 버리고는 창살 사이로 외쳤다. \"저리 가, 테디. 나 바쁘거든.\"\n\n음모자들 덕분에, 그날 밤 형세는 정말로 뒤집혔다. 헤이스가 꽃을 산더미처럼 보내왔는데, 한가운데 놓을 장식으로 솜씨를 한껏 부려 꾸민 어여쁜 바구니까지 곁들였던 것이다. 그러자 마치 집안 식구들이 총출동했고, 조가 톡톡히 한몫을 해낸 덕에 사람들이 모여들기만 한 게 아니라 머물러 있었으니, 그녀의 익살에 웃고 에이미의 안목에 감탄하면서 다들 무척 즐거워하는 눈치였다. 로리와 그 친구들은 의기롭게 그 빈틈에 뛰어들어 꽃다발을 죄다 사들이고는, 판매대 앞에 진을 친 채 그 구석을 방 안에서 가장 활기찬 자리로 만들어 놓았다. 이제 에이미는 물 만난 고기 같았고, 다른 건 몰라도 고마운 마음에서라도 더없이 발랄하고 우아하게 굴었다. 그러면서 그 무렵 미덕은 결국 그 자체로 보상이 되더라는 결론에 이르렀다.\n\n조는 더없이 바르게 처신했다. 그리고 에이미가 명예 호위병들에게 흐뭇하게 둘러싸여 있는 동안, 조는 홀을 이리저리 돌아다니며 갖가지 소문 조각을 주워 모았는데, 그것들이 체스터 집안의 자리 변경 건에 관해 그녀를 깨우쳐 주었다. 조는 그 반감에 자기가 한몫했다는 사실을 자책하며, 되도록 빨리 에이미의 누명을 벗겨 주리라 마음먹었다. 또한 에이미가 그날 아침 물건들을 두고 어떻게 처신했는지도 알게 되어, 동생을 너그러움의 본보기로 여기게 되었다. 미술 판매대를 지나며 조는 동생의 물건이 있나 훑어보았지만, 그 흔적조차 보이지 않았다.\n'눈에 안 띄게 어디 치워 버렸나 보지.' 조는 그렇게 생각했다. 자기가 당한 부당함은 용서할 수 있어도 가족에게 가해진 모욕만큼은 발끈 분개하는 그녀였다.\n\n\"안녕하세요, 조 양. 에이미는 잘하고 있나요?\" 메이가 화해하려는 듯한 태도로 물었으니, 자기 역시 너그러울 수 있다는 걸 보여 주고 싶었던 것이다.\n\n\"팔 만한 건 죄다 팔아 치우고, 이제는 즐기는 중이에요. 알다시피 꽃 판매대는 언제나 인기가 많잖아요, '특히 신사분들한테' 말이죠.\"\n\n조는 그 작은 한 방을 날리고 싶은 마음을 참을 수가 없었다. 그러나 메이가 너무도 순순히 받아들이는 바람에 잠시 후 조는 그 말을 후회했고, 아직 팔리지 않은 채 남아 있는 큰 꽃병들을 칭찬하기 시작했다.\n\n\"에이미가 채식한 책은 어디 없나요? 그건 아버지께 사다 드리고 싶은데.\" 조가 동생 작품의 운명을 몹시 궁금해하며 말했다.\n\n\"에이미 물건은 진작에 다 팔렸어요. 제가 알맞은 사람들 눈에 띄게 신경을 좀 썼더니, 우리한테 짭짤한 돈을 벌어 주었지요.\" 메이가 대답했는데, 그날 그녀 역시 에이미 못지않게 자잘한 유혹들을 여럿 이겨 낸 터였다.\n\n무척 흐뭇해진 조는 그 좋은 소식을 전하러 달려갔고, 에이미는 메이의 말과 태도에 관한 이야기를 듣고는 감동한 동시에 놀란 기색이었다.\n\n\"자, 여러분, 제 판매대에 베풀어 주신 만큼 다른 판매대에도 너그럽게 의무를 다하러 가 주세요. 특히 미술 판매대로요.\" 에이미가 여자애들이 \"테디 부대\"라고 부르는 대학생 친구들을 내보내며 말했다.\n\n\"'돌격하라, 체스터, 돌격하라!'가 저 판매대의 표어로다. 다만 사내답게 의무를 다하시라, 그러면 온갖 의미에서 '예술'에 걸맞은 본전을 뽑게 되리니.\" 헌신적인 방진(方陣)이 출정을 준비하는 가운데, 못 말리는 조가 말했다.\n* 방진: 병사들을 네모꼴로 빽빽이 세운 진형. 옮긴이.\n\n\"명을 들으면 곧 따르리, 허나 마치는 메이보다 훨씬 어여뻐라.\" 어린 파커가 재치 있는 동시에 다정하려고 필사적으로 애를 쓰며 말했다가, 곧장 로리한테 진압당하고 말았다. 로리가 \"어린 녀석치곤 제법이로구나, 얘야!\" 하고는 아버지처럼 그 머리를 토닥이며 데리고 가 버린 것이다.\n\n\"꽃병들을 사 줘.\" 에이미가 적의 머리에 마지막으로 숯불을 쌓아 올리는 셈치고 로리에게 속삭였다.\n\n메이가 크게 기뻐하게도, 로런스 씨는 그 꽃병들을 사 주었을 뿐 아니라 양쪽 겨드랑이에 하나씩 끼고 홀을 누비고 다녔다. 다른 신사들도 그에 못지않게 무모하게 온갖 부서지기 쉬운 자질구레한 물건들에 투자했고, 그 뒤로는 밀랍 꽃이며 채색 부채며 가는 세공 무늬 문서철이며 그 밖의 쓸모 있고 적절한 구매품들을 잔뜩 짊어진 채 어쩔 줄 모르고 헤매고 다녔다. 캐럴 고모할머니도 그 자리에 있다가 자초지종을 듣고는 흐뭇해하며, 한쪽 구석에서 마치 부인에게 뭐라고 말을 건넸다. 그 말에 마치 부인은 만족스레 얼굴이 환해져서는 자랑스러움과 걱정스러움이 뒤섞인 표정으로 에이미를 지켜보았으나, 그 기쁨의 까닭을 털어놓은 것은 며칠이 더 지나서였다.\n\n바자회는 성공작으로 평가받았다. 그리고 메이는 에이미에게 작별 인사를 할 때, 여느 때처럼 호들갑을 떠는 대신 다정하게 입을 맞추고는 \"용서하고 잊자\"라고 말하는 듯한 눈빛을 보냈다. 에이미는 그것으로 흡족했다. 집에 돌아오니 그 꽃병들이 응접실 벽난로 선반 위에 나란히 전시되어 있었고, 저마다 커다란 꽃다발이 꽂혀 있었다. \"너그러운 마치 양에게 내리는 미덕의 포상이라네.\" 로리가 거창한 손짓과 함께 선언했다.\n\n\"넌 내가 인정해 준 것보다 훨씬 더 지조 있고 너그럽고 고결한 성품을 지녔구나, 에이미. 정말 곱게 처신했어. 진심으로 널 존경한다.\" 그날 밤늦게 함께 머리를 빗으며 조가 따뜻하게 말했다.\n\n\"맞아, 우리 모두 그래. 그렇게 선뜻 용서해 줘서 더 사랑스럽고. 그토록 오래 공들이고 네 예쁜 물건들을 꼭 팔겠다고 마음을 단단히 먹었는데, 얼마나 견디기 힘들었겠니. 나라면 너처럼 그렇게 다정하게 해내진 못했을 거야.\" 베스가 베개에 머리를 묻은 채 덧붙였다.\n\n\"아유, 언니들, 그렇게 칭찬할 거 없어. 난 그저 남한테 대접받고 싶은 대로 했을 뿐인걸. 내가 숙녀가 되고 싶다고 하면 다들 비웃지만, 내 말은 마음씨와 몸가짐이 다 참된 숙녀가 되고 싶다는 거야. 그래서 내가 아는 한 그렇게 되려고 애쓰는 거고. 정확히 설명은 못 하겠지만, 난 그토록 많은 여자들을 망쳐 놓는 그 자잘한 야비함이며 어리석음이며 결점들을 넘어서고 싶어. 지금이야 한참 멀었지만, 그래도 최선을 다하고 있고, 언젠가는 엄마 같은 사람이 되고 싶어.\"\n\n에이미가 진지하게 말하자, 조가 다정하게 끌어안으며 말했다.\n\n\"이제 네 말뜻을 알겠어. 다시는 널 비웃지 않을게. 넌 네 생각보다 빨리 자라고 있어. 참된 예의범절은 내가 너한테 배워야겠다. 넌 그 비결을 터득한 것 같으니까. 부지런히 애써 보렴, 얘야. 언젠가 그 보답을 받게 될 거야. 그러면 나보다 더 기뻐할 사람은 없을 거고.\"\n\n일주일 뒤 에이미는 정말로 그 보답을 받았는데, 가엾은 조는 그것을 기뻐하기가 여간 힘든 게 아니었다. 캐럴 고모할머니에게서 편지가 한 통 왔는데, 그것을 읽는 마치 부인의 얼굴이 어찌나 환해지던지, 곁에 있던 조와 베스가 무슨 반가운 소식이냐고 다그쳐 물었다.\n\n\"캐럴 고모님이 다음 달에 외국에 나가시는데, 글쎄……\"\n\n\"저더러 같이 가자고요!\" 조가 걷잡을 수 없는 환희에 의자에서 벌떡 일어나며 말을 가로챘다.\n\n\"아니란다, 얘야. 너 말고 에이미야.\"\n\n\"아, 엄마! 쟤는 너무 어려요. 순서로 따지면 제가 먼저잖아요. 전 정말 오랫동안 그걸 바랐어요. 저한테 얼마나 도움이 되겠어요. 그리고 더없이 멋진 일이 될 텐데. 저, 꼭 가야 해요.\"\n\n\"안타깝지만 그건 불가능하단다, 조. 고모님께서 분명히 에이미라고 하셨고, 그런 호의를 베풀어 주시는데 우리가 이래라저래라 할 처지는 못 되잖니.\"\n\n\"늘 이런 식이라니까. 재미있는 건 죄다 에이미 차지고, 일은 죄다 제 몫이고요. 불공평해요, 아, 정말 불공평하다고요!\" 조가 격하게 소리쳤다.\n\n\"안타깝지만 그건 어느 정도 네 탓이기도 하단다, 얘야. 며칠 전 고모님이 나한테 말씀하실 때, 네 무뚝뚝한 태도와 지나치게 독립적인 기질을 아쉬워하시더구나. 여기 보렴, 네가 했던 말을 인용하시기라도 한 듯이 이렇게 쓰셨어. '처음엔 조를 청할 생각이었지만, 그 애는 「호의를 부담스러워하고」 「프랑스어라면 질색」이니, 선뜻 초대하지는 못하겠구나. 에이미가 더 고분고분하고, 플로에게 좋은 길동무가 되어 줄 테고, 이번 여행이 주는 도움이라면 무엇이든 고맙게 받아들이겠지.'\"\n\n\"아, 내 혀, 이 끔찍한 내 혀! 어째서 난 입을 다물 줄을 모를까?\" 조가 자신을 망쳐 놓은 그 말들을 떠올리며 신음했다. 인용된 구절들에 대한 설명을 듣고 나자, 마치 부인이 서글프게 말했다.\n\n\"네가 갈 수 있었으면 좋았을 텐데, 이번에는 가망이 없구나. 그러니 의젓하게 견뎌 보렴. 원망이나 후회로 에이미의 기쁨에 그늘을 드리우지 말고.\"\n\n\"그래 볼게요.\" 조가 눈을 꾹꾹 깜박이며 말하고는, 좀 전에 기뻐서 엎어 버린 바구니를 주우려고 무릎을 꿇었다. \"쟤한테서 한 수 배워서, 기쁜 척만 하는 게 아니라 정말로 기뻐하고, 쟤 행복을 단 한순간도 시샘하지 않도록 애써 볼게요. 하지만 쉽진 않을 거예요. 너무도 끔찍한 실망이니까요.\" 그러면서 가엾은 조는 손에 쥔 통통한 작은 바늘꽂이를, 무척이나 쓰라린 눈물 몇 방울로 적셨다.\n\n\"조 언니, 난 정말 이기적이지만, 난 언니를 보낼 수가 없어. 언니가 아직 안 가게 돼서 기뻐.\" 베스가 바구니까지 통째로 끌어안듯 매달리는 손길과 사랑스러운 얼굴로 속삭이자, 조는 날카로운 후회를 무릅쓰고도 마음이 누그러지는 것을 느꼈다. 그 후회 때문에 조는 자기 귀라도 한 대 쥐어박고 싶었고, 캐럴 고모할머니께 이 호의의 짐을 부디 자기에게 지워 주십사, 그러면 얼마나 고맙게 견뎌 내는지 보시라며 겸손히 빌고 싶었던 것이다.\n\n에이미가 들어올 무렵에는, 조도 식구들의 환희에 한몫 거들 수 있게 되었다. 아마 평소만큼 진심에서 우러나진 못했겠지만, 그래도 에이미의 행운을 두고 투덜대지는 않았다. 정작 본인은 그 소식을 더없이 기쁜 낭보로 받아들여, 자못 엄숙한 환희에 젖어 돌아다녔다. 그러고는 그날 저녁부터 물감을 분류하고 연필을 챙기기 시작하면서, 옷이며 돈이며 여권 같은 자질구레한 것들은 자기처럼 예술의 환상에 푹 빠지지 않은 사람들에게 맡겨 버렸다.\n\n\"이건 나한테 그저 놀러 가는 여행이 아니야, 언니들.\" 에이미가 가장 아끼는 팔레트를 긁어내며 의미심장하게 말했다. \"내 진로를 결정짓는 일이거든. 나한테 조금이라도 천재성이 있다면 로마에서 그걸 알아낼 테고, 그러면 그걸 증명할 뭔가를 해낼 테니까.\"\n\n\"천재성이 없으면 어쩌고?\" 조가 에이미에게 건네줄 새 칼라를 충혈된 눈으로 꿰매며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 집에 돌아와서 그림 가르치며 먹고살아야지.\" 명성을 꿈꾸는 그녀가 철학자다운 침착함으로 대답했다. 그러나 그 앞날을 떠올리자 얼굴을 찡그렸고, 희망을 접기 전에 한바탕 격렬한 수단이라도 강구하려는 듯 팔레트를 박박 긁어 댔다.\n\n\"아니, 넌 안 그럴걸. 넌 고된 일이라면 질색이니까, 어디 부자랑 결혼해서 평생 호강하며 살려고 집에 돌아올 거야.\" 조가 말했다.\n\n\"언니 예언이 가끔 들어맞긴 하지만, 그 예언은 안 맞을 것 같아. 그렇게 됐으면 좋겠다는 생각은 들지만 말이야. 내가 직접 예술가가 못 된다면, 예술가들을 도와줄 수 있는 사람이라도 됐으면 하거든.\" 에이미가 미소 지으며 말했는데, 마치 가난한 그림 선생 노릇보다는 자선 부인 노릇이 자기에게 더 어울린다는 듯한 투였다.\n\n\"흥!\" 조가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"네가 바라면 다 이루어질 거야. 네 소원은 늘 들어주시니까. 내 건 한 번도 안 들어주시고.\"\n\n\"언니도 가고 싶어?\" 에이미가 칼로 자기 코를 톡톡 두드리며 생각에 잠긴 듯 물었다.\n\n\"가고말고!\"\n\n\"그럼, 일이 년 뒤에 내가 언니를 부를게. 우리 둘이 포룸에서 유물도 캐내고, 그동안 숱하게 세워 온 계획들을 죄다 실행에 옮기자.\"\n\n\"고마워. 그 즐거운 날이 오면, 만약 오기나 한다면, 네 약속을 일깨워 줄게.\" 조가 막연하지만 근사한 그 제안을 되도록 고맙게 받아들이며 대답했다.\n\n채비할 시간이 넉넉지 않아, 에이미가 떠날 때까지 집 안은 온통 들썩였다. 조는 마지막으로 펄럭이던 파란 리본 자락이 사라질 때까지는 잘 버텼지만, 그 뒤로는 자신의 도피처인 다락방으로 물러가 더는 울 수 없을 때까지 울었다. 에이미도 증기선이 출항할 때까지는 꿋꿋이 버텼다. 그러다 막 트랩이 거두어지려는 순간, 자기와 자기를 가장 사랑하는 사람들 사이로 곧 대양 하나가 통째로 출렁이게 되리라는 사실이 불현듯 사무쳐 와, 마지막까지 머뭇거리던 로리에게 매달리며 흐느끼는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"아, 나 대신 식구들을 잘 보살펴 줘. 그리고 혹시라도 무슨 일이 생기면……\"\n\n\"그럴게, 에이미, 그럴게. 무슨 일이 생기면 내가 가서 널 위로해 줄게.\" 로리가 속삭였다. 자기가 그 약속을 지켜야 할 날이 오리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못한 채.\n\n그리하여 에이미는 구세계를 찾아 항해를 떠났으니, 그곳은 젊은 눈에는 늘 새롭고 아름다운 법이다. 한편 그녀의 아버지와 친구는 물가에 서서 그녀를 지켜보며, 마음씨 고운 이 소녀에게 부디 다정한 운명 외에는 아무것도 닥치지 않기를 간절히 빌었다. 소녀는 바다 위에 눈부시게 반짝이는 여름 햇살 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않게 될 때까지 그들에게 손을 흔들었다.\n\"런던.\n\n\"더없이 사랑하는 식구들에게,\n\"저는 지금 정말로 피커딜리에 있는 배스 호텔 앞쪽 창가에 앉아 있어요. 멋들어진 곳은 아니지만, 외삼촌이 몇 해 전에 여기 묵으신 뒤로 다른 데는 통 안 가려고 하시거든요. 그래도 오래 머물 생각은 아니니 별 상관없어요. 아, 이 모든 게 얼마나 즐거운지 도무지 말로 다 못 하겠어요. 영영 못 할 것 같으니, 그냥 제 수첩에서 토막토막 골라 들려드릴게요. 출발한 뒤로 스케치하고 끄적이는 것 말고는 아무것도 안 했거든요.\n\n\"핼리팩스에서 한 줄 부쳤을 땐 꽤 비참한 기분이었는데, 그 뒤로는 즐겁게 잘 지냈어요. 좀처럼 멀미도 안 하고, 온종일 갑판에 나와 있었고, 저를 즐겁게 해 줄 유쾌한 사람들도 많았거든요. 다들 저한테 무척 친절했는데, 특히 장교들이 그랬어요. 웃지 마, 조 언니. 배에서는 신사들이 정말 꼭 필요해. 붙잡고 의지할 사람으로든, 시중들어 줄 사람으로든 말이야. 게다가 그 사람들은 할 일도 없으니, 쓸모 있게 부려 먹는 게 자비를 베푸는 셈이지. 안 그러면 줄담배만 피우다 제 명에 못 죽을 게 분명하거든.\n\n\"고모할머니랑 플로는 오는 내내 몸이 안 좋아서 가만히 내버려 두는 걸 좋아하셨어요. 그래서 두 분께 해 드릴 수 있는 걸 다 하고 나면, 저는 가서 마음껏 즐겼어요. 갑판 위를 거니는 그 산책이며, 그 노을이며, 그 멋진 공기와 파도라니! 우리가 그토록 웅장하게 물살을 가르며 나아갈 때면, 빠른 말을 타는 것만큼이나 가슴 벅찼어요. 베스 언니도 왔으면 좋았을 텐데. 언니한테 정말 좋았을 거야. 조 언니로 말하자면, 메인 톱 지브인지 그 높다란 게 뭐라 불리든 그 위에 기어올라 앉아서, 기관사들하고 친구가 되고, 선장의 메가폰을 빵빵 불어 댔겠지. 그렇게나 황홀해했을 거야.\n\n\"다 천국 같았지만, 그래도 아일랜드 해안을 보니 반가웠고, 무척 아름답더군요. 어찌나 푸르고 햇살이 가득하던지, 군데군데 갈색 오두막이 있고, 언덕마다 폐허가 서 있고, 골짜기에는 신사들의 시골 저택이 있고, 사슴들이 사유지에서 풀을 뜯고 있었어요. 이른 아침이었지만 그걸 보려고 일어난 걸 후회하지 않았어요. 만에는 작은 배들이 가득했고, 해안은 어찌나 그림 같던지, 머리 위로는 장밋빛 하늘이 펼쳐져 있었거든요. 절대 못 잊을 거예요.\n\n\"퀸스타운에서 새로 사귄 사람 중 하나가 우리 곁을 떠났어요. 레녹스 씨였는데, 제가 킬라니 호수 이야기를 좀 꺼냈더니, 절 바라보며 한숨을 쉬고는 이렇게 노래하더군요.\n\n'아, 케이트 컨이를 들어 본 적 있나요? 킬라니 호숫가에 사는 그녀를. 그녀의 눈빛을 보거든 위험을 피해 달아나요, 케이트 컨이의 눈길은 치명적이니.'\n\n그거 참 시답잖지 않아요?\n\n\"리버풀에는 몇 시간밖에 안 머물렀어요. 지저분하고 시끄러운 곳이라 떠나게 돼서 다행이었죠. 외삼촌은 부리나케 나가서 제일 먼저 개가죽 장갑 한 켤레랑 보기 흉한 두툼한 신발이며 우산을 사고, 양고기 갈빗살 모양으로 구레나룻을 다듬었어요. 그러고는 자기가 진짜 영국 신사처럼 보인다고 흐뭇해하셨죠. 그런데 처음으로 신발에 묻은 진흙을 닦으니, 그 어린 구두닦이가 그 안에 미국인이 들어 있다는 걸 알아채고는 씩 웃으며 그러더래요. '다 됐습니다, 손님. 최신 양키식으로 광을 내 드렸지요.' 외삼촌은 그게 어찌나 우스웠는지 몰라요. 아, 그 엉뚱한 레녹스가 무슨 짓을 했는지 꼭 들려드려야겠어요! 우리랑 같이 온 친구 워드를 시켜서 저한테 꽃다발을 주문하게 한 거예요. 제 방에 들어가서 제일 먼저 본 게 어여쁜 꽃다발이었는데, 카드에 '로버트 레녹스 드림'이라고 적혀 있었어요. 재미있지 않니, 언니들? 난 여행이 참 좋아.\n\n\"이러다가 서두르지 않으면 도무지 런던에 닿질 못하겠네요. 그 여정은 사랑스러운 풍경으로 가득한 기다란 화랑을 지나는 것 같았어요. 농가들이 제일 마음에 들었어요. 초가지붕에 처마까지 담쟁이가 타고 올랐고, 격자창이 달렸고, 문간에는 혈색 좋은 아이들을 데리고 선 튼실한 아낙들이 있었죠. 토끼풀 속에 무릎까지 잠겨 선 가축들조차 우리 쪽 가축보다 더 평온해 보였고, 암탉들은 양키 닭들처럼 신경질을 부리는 법이 없는 듯, 만족스럽게 꼬꼬댁거렸어요. 그렇게 완벽한 색은 난생처음 봤어요. 풀은 어찌나 푸르고, 하늘은 어찌나 파랗고, 곡식은 어찌나 노랗고, 숲은 어찌나 짙던지, 가는 내내 황홀했어요. 플로도 그랬고요. 우리는 시속 100킬로미터로 쌩쌩 달리는 와중에 뭐든 다 보려고 이쪽저쪽으로 통통 옮겨 다녔어요. 고모할머니는 지치셔서 주무셨고, 외삼촌은 안내서를 읽으며 뭘 봐도 놀라는 법이 없었죠. 우리는 이런 식으로 갔어요. 에이미가 벌떡 일어나서 '아, 저게 케닐워스인가 봐, 나무들 사이에 저 회색 건물!' 플로가 제 창가로 쏜살같이 와서, '어쩜 이렇게 멋질까! 우리 언제 저기 가야 해요, 그렇죠, 아빠?' 외삼촌은 태연히 자기 신발에 감탄하며, '아니란다, 얘야. 맥주가 마시고 싶다면 모를까. 저건 양조장이거든.'\n\n\"잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 플로가 외쳤어요. '어머나, 저기 교수대가 있고 사람이 올라가고 있어요.' '어디, 어디?' 에이미가 가로대가 달린 두 개의 높다란 기둥이며 거기 매달려 흔들리는 쇠사슬들을 멍하니 내다보며 비명을 질렀죠. '탄광이로구나.' 외삼촌이 눈을 반짝이며 말했어요. '여기 사랑스러운 양 떼가 죄다 누워 있어요.' 에이미가 말하자, '봐요, 아빠, 예쁘죠!' 플로가 감상에 젖어 덧붙였어요. '거위란다, 아가씨들.' 외삼촌이 우리를 잠잠하게 만드는 말투로 대꾸하자, 플로가 얌전히 「캐번디시 대위의 연애 행각」을 즐기는 데 정착했고, 저는 풍경을 온전히 혼자 차지하게 되었죠.\n\n\"런던에 닿으니 당연하다는 듯 비가 왔고, 안개와 우산 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았어요. 우리는 쉬고, 짐을 풀고, 비가 잠깐씩 멎는 사이에 쇼핑을 좀 했어요. 메리 고모할머니가 새 물건을 몇 가지 사 주셨어요. 워낙 급하게 떠나오느라 채비가 반도 안 됐었거든요. 하얀 모자에 파란 깃털, 거기 어울리는 모슬린 드레스, 그리고 여태 본 것 중 가장 어여쁜 망토예요. 리젠트 거리에서 쇼핑하는 건 정말 근사해요. 물건이 어찌나 싼지, 고운 리본이 1야드에 겨우 6펜스예요. 잔뜩 사 두었지만, 장갑은 파리에서 살 거예요. 어쩐지 우아하고 부티 나는 소리지 않아요?\n\n\"플로랑 저는 재미 삼아, 고모할머니랑 외삼촌이 나가신 사이에 핸섬 마차를 한 대 불러서 드라이브를 갔어요. 나중에야 젊은 아가씨들이 그런 마차를 단둘이 타는 건 점잖지 못한 일이라는 걸 알았지만요. 어찌나 우습던지! 나무 덮개에 갇혀 있는데 마부가 어찌나 빨리 모는지, 플로가 겁을 먹고 저더러 마부를 세우라고 했거든요. 그런데 그 사람은 어딘가 바깥 뒤쪽 높은 데 올라타 있어서 도무지 닿을 수가 없었어요. 제가 불러도 못 듣고, 앞에서 양산을 펄럭여도 못 보고, 우리는 그저 속수무책으로 덜컹덜컹 내달리며 목이 부러질 듯한 속도로 모퉁이를 휙휙 돌았죠. 마침내 절망에 빠진 저는 지붕에 난 작은 문 하나를 발견했고, 그걸 콕콕 찔러 열자 벌건 눈 하나가 나타나더니, 술 취한 목소리가 그러더군요.\n\n\"'뭡니까, 아씨?'\n\n\"저는 되도록 진지하게 분부를 내렸고, 그 사람은 '예이, 예이, 아씨' 하며 문을 쾅 닫더니, 꼭 장례 행렬에라도 가듯 말을 걷게 했어요. 제가 다시 콕콕 찌르고 '좀 더 빨리요' 하자, 그제야 아까처럼 정신없이 내달렸고, 우리는 운명에 우리를 내맡겼죠.\n\n\"오늘은 날이 개어서 바로 옆에 있는 하이드 파크에 갔어요. 우리가 겉보기보다 더 귀족적이거든요. 데번셔 공작이 근처에 사는데, 그 댁 종복들이 뒷문에서 빈둥거리는 걸 자주 봐요. 웰링턴 공작 저택도 멀지 않고요. 글쎄, 거기서 본 광경이라니! 펀치 인형극 못지않았어요. 뚱뚱한 미망인 부인들이 빨갛고 노란 마차를 타고 굴러다녔는데, 뒤에는 비단 양말에 벨벳 외투를 걸친 화려한 하인들이 매달려 있고, 앞에는 분을 바른 마부들이 앉아 있었거든요. 산뜻한 하녀들이 여태 본 것 중 가장 혈색 좋은 아이들을 데리고 있고, 어여쁜 아가씨들은 반쯤 졸린 듯한 얼굴이고, 멋쟁이들은 괴상한 영국식 모자에 연보랏빛 새끼염소 가죽 장갑을 끼고 어슬렁거리고, 키 큰 병사들은 짧은 빨간 재킷에 머핀처럼 생긴 모자를 한쪽으로 삐딱하게 얹은 채로 어찌나 우스워 보이던지, 그려 보고 싶어 안달이 났어요.\n\n\"로튼 로는 '왕의 길'이라는 뜻이래요. 그렇지만 지금은 뭐랄까 승마 학교에 더 가까워요. 말들은 훌륭하고, 남자들은, 특히 마부들은 말을 잘 타지만, 여자들은 뻣뻣하게 굴면서 통통 튀는데 그건 우리 식 규칙엔 안 맞아요. 저는 미국식으로 질주하는 멋진 모습을 보여 주고 싶어 안달이 났어요. 그 사람들은 짧은 승마복에 높은 모자를 쓰고 점잔을 빼며 위아래로 천천히 다녔는데, 꼭 장난감 노아의 방주에 나오는 여자들 같았거든요. 다들 말을 타요. 노인들도, 뚱뚱한 부인들도, 어린 아이들도요. 그리고 젊은이들은 여기서 한껏 시시덕거리고요. 한 쌍이 장미 봉오리를 주고받는 걸 봤는데, 여기선 그걸 단춧구멍에 꽂는 게 유행이거든요. 제법 괜찮은 발상이다 싶었어요.\n\n\"오후에는 웨스트민스터 사원에 갔어요. 그렇지만 그걸 묘사해 달라는 기대는 마세요. 그건 불가능하니까요. 그냥 숭고했다고만 할게요! 오늘 저녁엔 페히터를 보러 가요. 제 생애 가장 행복한 하루의 알맞은 마무리가 되겠죠.\n\"자정.\n\n\"무척 늦었지만, 어젯밤에 무슨 일이 있었는지 들려드리지 않고서는 아침에 편지를 부칠 수가 없네요. 우리가 차를 마시고 있는데 누가 들어왔게요? 로리의 영국 친구들, 프레드랑 프랭크 본이었어요! 어찌나 놀랐는지, 카드가 없었으면 못 알아봤을 거예요. 둘 다 구레나룻을 기른 키 큰 청년들인데, 프레드는 영국식으로 잘생겼고, 프랭크는 훨씬 더 나아요. 이젠 약간 절뚝거릴 뿐 목발도 안 짚거든요. 둘은 로리한테서 우리가 어디 있을지 듣고서, 자기네 집에 초대하려고 찾아왔어요. 그런데 외삼촌이 안 가려고 하셔서, 우리가 답방을 가서 되도록 자주 보기로 했어요. 둘이 우리랑 같이 극장에 갔는데, 정말 재밌게 놀았어요. 프랭크는 플로한테 푹 빠져 있었고, 프레드랑 저는 평생 알고 지낸 사이처럼 지난날, 오늘, 그리고 앞으로 누릴 재미에 관해 이야기를 나눴거든요. 베스 언니한테 프랭크가 안부를 묻더라고, 언니가 아프다는 말에 안타까워하더라고 전해 줘. 프레드는 제가 조 언니 얘기를 꺼내자 웃으면서 '그 큰 모자한테 정중한 안부를 전한다'고 했어요. 둘 다 로런스 캠프며 거기서 누렸던 재미를 잊지 않고 있었어요. 그게 얼마나 까마득한 옛날 같은지, 그렇지 않아요?\n\n\"고모할머니가 벌써 세 번째 벽을 두드리시니, 이만 멈춰야겠어요. 이렇게 밤늦도록 예쁜 물건으로 가득한 방에 앉아 편지를 쓰고 있자니, 머릿속은 공원이며 극장이며 새 드레스며 '아!' 하고 감탄사를 내뱉으며 진짜 영국 귀족다운 거드름으로 금빛 콧수염을 배배 꼬는 멋쟁이들로 뒤죽박죽이고, 정말이지 방탕한 런던 귀부인이 된 기분이에요. 다들 너무 보고 싶어요. 이런 실없는 소리를 늘어놓아도, 저는 언제까지나 식구들을 사랑하는\n에이미.\"\n\n\"사랑하는 언니들에게, \"파리.\n\"지난 편지에서 런던 방문 이야기를 들려드렸죠. 본 집안 사람들이 얼마나 친절했는지, 우리를 위해 얼마나 즐거운 모임들을 마련해 줬는지요. 그중에서도 햄프턴 코트랑 켄싱턴 박물관에 간 게 제일 즐거웠어요. 햄프턴에서는 라파엘로의 밑그림을 봤고, 박물관에서는 터너, 로런스, 레이놀즈, 호가스, 그 밖의 위대한 거장들의 그림으로 가득한 방들을 봤거든요. 리치먼드 공원에서 보낸 하루는 매혹적이었어요. 우리가 제대로 된 영국식 소풍을 즐겼는데, 도저히 다 옮겨 그릴 수 없을 만큼 멋진 떡갈나무며 사슴 무리가 있었거든요. 나이팅게일 소리도 듣고, 종달새가 날아오르는 것도 봤어요. 프레드랑 프랭크 덕분에 우리는 런던을 마음껏 '구경'했고, 떠나기가 아쉬웠어요. 영국 사람들은 마음을 여는 데 더디지만, 일단 받아들이기로 마음을 정하면 그 환대만큼은 누구한테도 뒤지지 않는다고 저는 생각해요. 본 집안 사람들은 다음 겨울에 로마에서 우리랑 만나기를 바라는데, 그러지 못하면 저는 끔찍하게 실망할 거예요. 그레이스랑 저는 둘도 없는 친구가 됐고, 그 남자애들도 아주 좋은 친구들이거든요. 특히 프레드가요.\n\n\"그런데 여기 자리를 잡기가 무섭게 그 애가 또 나타난 거예요. 휴가를 보내러 왔다면서, 스위스로 갈 거래요. 고모할머니가 처음엔 떨떠름한 표정이셨지만, 그 애가 어찌나 태연한지 한마디도 못 하시더라고요. 이젠 우리도 잘 지내고, 그 애가 와 줘서 무척 다행이에요. 프레드는 프랑스어를 토박이처럼 하는데, 그 애가 없으면 어쩌나 싶거든요. 외삼촌은 열 마디도 못 하면서, 그러면 사람들이 알아듣기라도 하는 양 영어로 아주 크게 말하겠다고 고집을 부리세요. 고모할머니 발음은 구식이고, 플로랑 저는 우리가 꽤 안다고 으스댔지만 알고 보니 모르는 터라, 외삼촌이 '파를리부' 라고 부르는 그 프랑스어 노릇을 프레드가 해 주니 무척 고맙죠.\n\n\"우리는 얼마나 즐거운 시간을 보내는지 몰라요! 아침부터 밤까지 구경을 다니고, 흥겨운 카페에서 멈춰 맛있는 점심을 먹고, 온갖 우스꽝스러운 모험을 만나기도 해요. 비 오는 날엔 루브르에서 그림에 흠뻑 빠져 지내요. 조 언니라면 가장 빼어난 그림 몇 점을 보고도 그 못된 코를 치켜들 거예요. 예술을 느끼는 영혼이 없으니까요. 그렇지만 저는 있어서, 눈과 안목을 되도록 빨리 갈고닦는 중이에요. 언니는 차라리 위인들의 유물을 더 좋아할 거예요. 제가 나폴레옹의 삼각모랑 회색 외투, 그 아들의 요람과 낡은 칫솔을 봤거든요. 마리 앙투아네트의 작은 신발이며, 생드니의 반지, 샤를마뉴의 검, 그 밖에 흥미로운 것들도 많이 봤고요. 가서 몇 시간이고 그 이야기를 들려줄게요. 지금은 쓸 시간이 없어서요.\n\n\"팔레 루아얄은 천국 같은 곳이에요. 보석 세공품이며 사랑스러운 물건들로 가득한데, 살 수가 없으니 미칠 지경이에요. 프레드가 좀 사 주려고 했지만, 당연히 못 하게 했죠. 그다음에 불로뉴 숲이랑 샹젤리제는 정말이지 장엄해요. 황실 가족도 여러 번 봤어요. 황제는 못생기고 험상궂게 생긴 남자였고, 황후는 창백하고 예뻤지만 차림새가 영 촌스러웠어요. 보랏빛 드레스에 초록 모자, 노란 장갑이라니. 어린 나폴레옹은 잘생긴 소년인데, 가정교사랑 재잘재잘 이야기하며 앉아 있다가, 사두마차를 타고 지나가면서 사람들에게 손에 입을 맞춰 보내요. 마차에는 빨간 새틴 재킷을 입은 마부들이 타고, 앞뒤로는 기마 호위병들이 따르고요.\n\n\"우리는 종종 튀일리 정원을 거닐어요. 거기가 사랑스럽거든요. 그래도 저는 고풍스러운 뤽상부르 정원이 더 마음에 들어요. 페르 라셰즈는 무척 신기해요. 무덤 가운데 상당수가 작은 방 같아서, 들여다보면 탁자가 있고, 그 위에 고인의 조각상이나 초상화가 있고, 애도하러 온 사람들이 앉아서 슬퍼할 수 있게 의자도 있거든요. 그게 참 프랑스답죠.\n\n\"우리 방은 리볼리 거리에 있어서, 발코니에 앉으면 길고 화려한 거리를 위아래로 내다볼 수 있어요. 어찌나 기분 좋은지, 낮 동안 너무 지쳐서 밖에 못 나갈 때면 저녁을 거기서 이야기를 나누며 보내요. 프레드는 무척 재미있는 사람이라, 제가 여태 알던 청년 중에 통틀어 가장 유쾌해요. 로리만 빼고요. 그 애 매너가 더 매력적이거든요. 프레드가 머리색이 짙었으면 좋았을 텐데. 저는 머리 밝은 남자는 별로거든요. 그래도 본 집안은 무척 부자고 훌륭한 가문 출신이니, 그 노란 머리를 탓하진 않을래요. 제 머리가 더 노라니까요.\n\n\"다음 주에는 독일이랑 스위스로 떠나요. 빠르게 이동할 테니, 급하게 쓴 편지밖에 못 보내 드릴 거예요. 저는 일기를 쓰면서, 아버지가 일러 주신 대로 '내가 보고 감탄하는 모든 것을 정확히 기억하고 똑똑히 묘사'하려고 애쓰고 있어요. 저한테 좋은 연습이 되고, 스케치북이랑 같이 보면 이런 끄적임보다 제 여행을 훨씬 잘 그려 보실 수 있을 거예요.\n\"안녕히 계세요. 다정하게 안아 드려요. 당신들의 친구.\"\n\n\"사랑하는 엄마에게, \"하이델베르크.\n\n\"베른으로 떠나기 전에 조용한 시간이 좀 나서, 그동안 무슨 일이 있었는지 들려드릴게요. 보시면 아시겠지만, 그중엔 무척 중요한 일도 있거든요.\n\n\"라인강을 거슬러 오르는 뱃길은 완벽해서, 저는 그저 앉아서 온 힘을 다해 즐겼어요. 아버지의 낡은 안내서들을 꺼내서 그 강에 관해 읽어 보세요. 저는 그걸 묘사할 만큼 아름다운 말을 갖고 있지 못하거든요. 코블렌츠에서는 즐거운 시간을 보냈는데, 프레드가 배에서 사귄 본 출신 대학생 몇 명이 우리한테 세레나데를 불러 줬거든요. 달밤이었는데, 새벽 한 시쯤 플로랑 저는 창문 아래서 들려오는 더없이 황홀한 음악에 잠을 깼어요. 우리는 벌떡 일어나 커튼 뒤에 숨었지만, 슬쩍 엿보니 프레드랑 대학생들이 저 아래서 노래하고 있더군요. 여태 본 것 중 가장 낭만적인 광경이었어요. 강이며, 배로 이은 다리며, 맞은편의 거대한 요새며, 사방의 달빛이며, 돌 같은 마음도 녹일 만한 음악이라니.\n\n\"그들이 노래를 마치자 우리는 꽃을 몇 송이 던져 주었고, 그들이 그걸 주우려고 다투다가, 보이지 않는 숙녀들에게 손에 입을 맞춰 보내고는 웃으며 사라지는 걸 봤어요. 담배 피우고 맥주 마시러 갔겠죠, 아마. 이튿날 아침 프레드가 조끼 주머니에 든 그 구겨진 꽃 한 송이를 보여 주며 무척 감상에 젖은 표정을 짓더군요. 저는 그를 비웃으며 그건 제가 던진 게 아니라 플로가 던진 거라고 했어요. 그러자 그가 질색하는 눈치였는지 그걸 창밖으로 던져 버리고 다시 멀쩡해지더라고요. 아무래도 저 애 때문에 골치 좀 썩을 것 같아요. 그런 낌새가 보이기 시작했거든요.\n\n\"나사우의 온천은 무척 흥겨웠고, 바덴바덴도 그랬어요. 거기서 프레드가 돈을 좀 잃기에 제가 야단을 쳤어요. 프랭크가 곁에 없을 땐 누군가 그 애를 돌봐 줘야 해요. 케이트가 언젠가 그 애가 어서 결혼했으면 좋겠다고 했는데, 그게 그 애한테 좋을 거라는 말엔 저도 전적으로 동감이에요. 프랑크푸르트는 아주 즐거웠어요. 괴테의 집이랑 실러의 동상, 그리고 다네커의 유명한 '아리아드네'를 봤거든요. 무척 사랑스러웠지만, 그 이야기를 더 잘 알았더라면 훨씬 더 즐겼을 거예요. 다들 알거나 아는 척하는 통에 물어보기가 싫었거든요. 조 언니가 그 이야기를 죄다 들려줬으면 좋겠어요. 책을 더 많이 읽었어야 했는데, 제가 아는 게 아무것도 없다는 걸 깨닫고 나니 창피해요.\n\n\"이제 심각한 부분이 나와요. 그게 여기서 일어났고, 프레드는 방금 떠났거든요. 그 애가 어찌나 친절하고 유쾌한지 우리 모두 그 애를 무척 좋아하게 됐어요. 세레나데가 있던 밤 전까지만 해도, 저는 여행 중에 나누는 우정 말고는 아무 생각도 안 했거든요. 그런데 그 뒤로, 그 달빛 산책이며 발코니에서 나눈 이야기며 날마다 겪는 모험들이 그 애한테는 단순한 재미 이상이라는 걸 느끼기 시작했어요. 전 시시덕거리지 않았어요, 엄마, 정말이에요. 엄마가 저한테 해 주신 말씀을 기억하면서 정말 최선을 다했거든요. 사람들이 절 좋아하는 걸 제가 어쩌겠어요. 일부러 그러게 만들지도 않고, 제가 그 사람들한테 마음이 없으면 그게 또 마음에 걸리는걸요. 조 언니는 제가 마음이라곤 없다고 하지만요. 자, 엄마가 고개를 저으실 거고, 언니들은 '아, 저 돈만 밝히는 못된 것!' 하고 말할 줄 알아요. 그래도 저는 마음을 정했어요. 프레드가 청혼하면 저는 받아들일 거예요. 미친 듯이 사랑하는 건 아니지만요. 저는 그 애가 좋고, 우리는 함께 있으면 편안하게 잘 지내거든요. 그 애는 잘생기고, 젊고, 머리도 제법 좋고, 무척 부자예요. 로런스 집안보다 훨씬 더 부유하죠. 그 애 가족도 반대하지 않을 것 같고, 저는 무척 행복할 거예요. 다들 친절하고 예의 바르고 너그러운 사람들이고, 절 좋아하니까요. 프레드가 쌍둥이 형이니까 영지를 물려받겠죠, 아마. 그게 어찌나 훌륭한 영지인지! 멋들어진 거리에 있는 시내 저택은 우리네 큰 집들만큼 화려하진 않아도 두 배는 더 안락하고, 영국 사람들이 신봉하는 그런 든든한 호사로 가득해요. 그게 가짜가 아니라 진짜라 저는 마음에 들어요. 은식기며 가문의 보석이며 늙은 하인들이며, 사유지와 그곳의 정원과 큰 저택, 사랑스러운 부지와 멋진 말들을 담은 그림도 봤어요. 아, 그거면 제가 바랄 게 더 없겠어요! 여자애들이 그토록 덥석 채 가지만 막상 뒤에는 아무것도 없는 그런 작위 따위보다 저는 그게 더 좋아요. 제가 돈을 밝히는 건지도 모르지만, 저는 가난이 싫고, 어쩔 수 없는 경우가 아니라면 단 한순간도 더 견딜 마음이 없어요. 우리 중 누군가는 잘 결혼해야 해요. 메그 언니는 그러지 못했고, 조 언니는 안 할 거고, 베스 언니는 아직 못 하니, 제가 해서 두루두루 다 편안하게 만들어야죠. 제가 미워하거나 경멸하는 남자랑은 결혼하지 않을 거예요. 그건 확실해요. 그리고 프레드가 제 이상형은 아니지만, 그 애 정도면 무척 괜찮아요. 그 애가 저를 무척 사랑해서 제가 하고 싶은 대로 다 하게 내버려 둔다면, 시간이 지나면서 저도 그 애를 충분히 좋아하게 될 거예요. 그래서 지난 한 주 동안 이 일을 곰곰이 곱씹어 봤어요. 프레드가 절 좋아한다는 걸 모를 수가 없었거든요. 말로는 아무 내색도 안 했지만, 자잘한 것들이 그걸 드러냈어요. 그 앤 절대 플로랑 같이 안 가고 늘 마차나 식탁이나 산책길에서 제 옆자리를 차지하고, 우리 둘만 있으면 감상에 젖은 표정을 짓고, 다른 누가 저한테 감히 말이라도 걸려고 하면 인상을 찡그려요. 어제 저녁 식사 때, 어느 오스트리아 장교가 우리를 빤히 쳐다보더니 방탕해 보이는 남작인 자기 친구한테 '아인 분더쇠네스 블뢴트헨' 어쩌고 하니까, 프레드가 사자처럼 사납게 노려보면서 고기를 어찌나 험악하게 써는지 접시에서 튀어나갈 뻔했어요. 그 앤 차갑고 뻣뻣한 영국 남자가 아니라, 오히려 다혈질이에요. 그 어여쁜 파란 눈만 봐도 짐작할 수 있듯이, 스코틀랜드 피가 흐르거든요.\n\n* 아인 분더쇠네스 블뢴트헨: 독일어로 '몹시 아름다운 금발 아가씨'라는 뜻. 옮긴이.\n\"그래서 어젯밤 우리는 해 질 무렵 성에 올라갔어요. 적어도 프레드만 빼고 다요. 그 앤 우체국에 편지를 찾으러 갔다가 거기서 우리랑 만나기로 했거든요. 우리는 폐허며, 그 괴물 같은 거대한 술통이 있는 지하실이며, 오래전에 선제후가 영국인 아내를 위해 만든 아름다운 정원을 둘러보며 매혹적인 시간을 보냈어요. 저는 그 커다란 테라스가 제일 좋았어요. 경치가 황홀했거든요. 그래서 다들 안쪽 방들을 보러 간 사이, 저는 거기 앉아 벽에 새겨진 회색 돌 사자 머리를 그려 보려고 했어요. 그 둘레로는 진홍빛 인동덩굴 가지들이 늘어져 있었죠. 거기 앉아 골짜기를 굽이쳐 흐르는 네카어강을 바라보고, 아래쪽 오스트리아 악단의 음악을 들으며, 진짜 이야기책 속 소녀처럼 제 연인을 기다리고 있자니, 마치 소설 속에라도 들어온 기분이었어요. 뭔가 일어날 것 같은 예감이 들었고, 저는 그럴 채비가 되어 있었죠. 얼굴이 붉어지거나 떨리지도 않고, 아주 침착하게, 그저 조금 들떠 있을 뿐이었어요.\n\n\"이윽고 프레드의 목소리가 들리더니, 그 애가 절 찾아 그 큰 아치를 지나 부랴부랴 다가왔어요. 어찌나 근심스러운 얼굴이던지, 저는 제 일 따위는 까맣게 잊고 무슨 일이냐고 물었어요. 방금 편지를 받았는데 어서 집으로 오라는 내용이래요. 프랭크가 위독하다고요. 그래서 그 앤 그 길로 밤 기차를 타고 떠나야 한다며, 작별 인사를 할 시간밖에 없었어요. 저는 그 애가 무척 안됐고, 저 스스로도 실망스러웠지만, 그것도 잠시뿐이었어요. 그 애가 제 손을 잡으며, 그것도 오해할 수 없는 어조로 이렇게 말했거든요. '곧 돌아올게요. 저를 잊지 않을 거죠, 에이미?'\n\n\"저는 약속하진 않았지만, 그 애를 바라보았고, 그 앤 흡족해 하는 눈치였어요. 그러고는 전갈을 남기고 작별 인사를 나누는 것 말곤 다른 걸 할 틈이 없었어요. 한 시간 만에 떠나 버렸거든요. 우리 모두 그 애를 무척 그리워해요. 그 애가 말을 하고 싶어 했다는 건 알지만, 언젠가 그 애가 넌지시 비친 말로 미루어 짐작건대, 한동안은 그런 일을 하지 않겠다고 아버지께 약속한 것 같아요. 그 애가 워낙 무모한 데다, 그 노신사가 외국인 며느리를 몹시 꺼리거든요. 우리는 곧 로마에서 만날 거예요. 그때 가서 제 마음이 변하지 않는다면, 그 애가 '제발 저랑 결혼해 줄래요?' 하고 물을 때 저는 '네, 고마워요' 하고 답할 거예요.\n\n\"물론 이건 전부 극비예요. 그래도 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 엄마는 알고 계셨으면 했어요. 저 때문에 걱정 마세요. 제가 엄마의 '신중한 에이미'라는 거 기억하시고, 절대 경솔하게 굴지 않을 테니 안심하세요. 충고는 얼마든지 보내 주세요. 할 수 있으면 따를게요. 엄마랑 마주 앉아 실컷 이야기 나눌 수 있다면 좋겠어요. 절 사랑하고 믿어 주세요.\n\n\"언제까지나 엄마의 에이미.\"\n\n\"조, 베스가 걱정이구나.\"\n\n\"왜요, 엄마? 아기들이 태어난 뒤로 베스는 유난히 좋아 보이던데요.\"\n\n\"지금 내가 걱정하는 건 그 애 건강이 아니라 마음이란다. 무언가 마음에 걸리는 게 있는 게 분명한데, 네가 그게 뭔지 알아내 줬으면 싶구나.\"\n\n\"왜 그렇게 생각하세요, 엄마?\"\n\n\"혼자 앉아 있을 때가 많고, 예전만큼 아버지하고 이야기도 안 하더구나. 며칠 전엔 아기들을 보며 우는 걸 봤단다. 노래를 부를 때면 늘 슬픈 곡들이고, 이따금 그 애 얼굴에서 도무지 알 수 없는 표정이 스치곤 해. 이건 베스답지 않아서, 마음이 쓰인단다.\"\n\n\"엄마가 그 애한테 물어보셨어요?\"\n\n\"한두 번 해 봤지. 그런데 내 물음을 슬쩍 피하거나, 너무 괴로운 표정을 짓기에 그만뒀단다. 나는 내 아이들이 속내를 털어놓도록 억지로 다그치는 법이 없고, 그걸 오래 기다려야 했던 적도 드물거든.\"\n\n마치 부인이 말하면서 조를 흘낏 보았지만, 맞은편 얼굴은 베스의 일 말고는 그 어떤 은밀한 근심도 알지 못하는 듯했다. 잠시 생각에 잠겨 바느질하던 조가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"제 생각엔 그 애가 자라고 있어서, 그래서 까닭도 모르고 설명도 못 하면서 이런저런 꿈을 꾸고, 희망이며 두려움이며 안절부절못하는 마음을 품기 시작한 것 같아요. 글쎄, 엄마, 베스가 열여덟이잖아요. 그런데 우린 그걸 실감하지 못하고 그 애가 어엿한 여자라는 걸 잊은 채 어린애처럼 대하고 있어요.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇구나. 아이고, 너희가 어쩜 이리도 빨리 자라는지.\" 어머니가 한숨과 미소를 함께 지으며 대답했다.\n\n\"어쩔 수 없잖아요, 엄마. 그러니 온갖 걱정거리에 단단히 마음을 다잡으시고, 새끼 새들이 둥지 밖으로 한 마리씩 폴짝폴짝 날아가게 내버려 두셔야죠. 저는 절대 멀리는 안 날아가겠다고 약속할게요. 그게 조금이나마 위안이 되신다면요.\"\n\n\"큰 위안이 되고말고, 조. 메그가 떠난 지금, 네가 집에 있으면 나는 늘 든든하단다. 베스는 너무 약하고 에이미는 너무 어려서 기댈 수가 없는데, 막상 힘든 고비가 닥치면 너는 언제나 채비가 되어 있으니까.\"\n\n\"아, 제가 고된 일은 별로 마다하지 않잖아요. 게다가 집안에는 늘 궂은일 도맡을 사람이 하나쯤 있어야 하고요. 에이미는 섬세한 일에는 빼어나지만 저는 아니에요. 그래도 온 집 안 양탄자를 다 걷어 내야 한다거나, 식구 절반이 한꺼번에 앓아눕거나 할 때면 저는 물 만난 기분이 들거든요. 에이미는 외국에서 두각을 나타내고 있지만, 집에 무슨 일이 생기면 제가 적임자죠.\"\n\n\"그럼 베스는 네 손에 맡기마. 그 애는 다른 누구보다도 너 조한테 그 여린 마음을 더 빨리 열 테니까. 아주 다정하게 대해 주고, 누가 자기를 지켜보거나 자기 얘기를 한다는 생각이 들지 않게 해 주렴. 그 애가 다시 아주 튼튼하고 명랑해지기만 한다면, 나는 이 세상에 더 바랄 게 없겠다.\"\n\n\"행복한 분이시네요! 저는 바라는 게 수두룩한데.\"\n\n\"얘야, 그게 뭔데?\"\n\n\"베스 일부터 해결하고 나서 제 걸 말씀드릴게요. 그렇게 닳아 없어질 것들은 아니니까, 좀 묵혀 둬도 괜찮아요.\" 조가 슬기로운 고갯짓과 함께 바느질을 계속하자, 어머니의 마음은 적어도 당분간은 조에 대한 걱정을 내려놓고 놓였다.\n\n겉으로는 제 일에 푹 빠진 듯하면서도, 조는 베스를 지켜보았다. 그리고 서로 엇갈리는 온갖 추측 끝에, 마침내 동생의 변화를 설명해 줄 듯한 한 가지에 생각이 닿았다. 사소한 사건 하나가 조에게 그 수수께끼의 실마리를 던져 주었다고 그녀는 여겼고, 발랄한 상상력과 사랑 가득한 마음이 나머지를 채웠다. 어느 토요일 오후, 조는 베스와 단둘이 있을 때 바삐 글을 쓰는 척하고 있었다. 그러나 끄적이는 와중에도 유난히 조용한 동생에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다. 창가에 앉은 베스는 일감을 자꾸 무릎에 떨어뜨리고는, 한 손에 머리를 괴고 풀 죽은 자세로, 칙칙한 가을 풍경에 눈길을 머문 채였다. 갑자기 누군가가 오페라에 나오는 검은지빠귀처럼 휘파람을 불며 아래쪽을 지나갔고, 한 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"별일 없지! 오늘 밤에 들를게.\"\n\n베스는 깜짝 놀라 몸을 앞으로 기울이더니, 미소 지으며 고개를 끄덕이고는, 지나가는 그를 가만히 바라보았다. 그의 빠른 발소리가 멀어져 사라질 때까지 지켜보다가, 혼잣말처럼 나직이 말했다.\n\n\"저 사랑스러운 애는 어쩜 저렇게 튼튼하고 건강하고 행복해 보일까.\"\n\n\"흠!\" 조는 여전히 언니의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 말했다. 밝게 떠올랐던 홍조가 떠오를 때만큼이나 빠르게 사라지고, 미소도 가시더니, 이내 눈물 한 방울이 창틀 위에서 반짝이며 맺혔다. 베스는 얼른 그것을 훔쳐 내고는 불안한 듯 조를 흘끔 보았다. 하지만 조는 무서운 속도로 펜을 놀리며 「올림피아의 맹세」에 푹 빠져 있는 듯했다. 베스가 고개를 돌리는 순간, 조는 다시 언니를 살피기 시작했고, 베스의 손이 한 번 이상 조용히 눈가로 올라가는 것을, 그리고 반쯤 돌린 얼굴에서 자신의 눈마저 젖게 만드는 애틋한 슬픔을 읽어 냈다. 속마음을 들킬까 두려워, 조는 종이가 더 필요하다는 둥 뭐라 중얼거리며 슬그머니 자리를 떴다.\n\n\"세상에, 베스가 로리를 사랑하는 거야!\" 조는 자기 방으로 들어가 앉으며 말했다. 방금 알아냈다고 믿은 그 사실의 충격으로 얼굴이 새하얗게 질려 있었다. \"그런 건 꿈에도 생각 못 했어. 엄마는 뭐라고 하실까? 혹시 그 애도——\" 거기서 조는 말을 멈추더니, 문득 떠오른 생각에 얼굴이 새빨개졌다. \"만약 그 애가 같은 마음이 아니라면, 얼마나 끔찍할까. 그 애도 사랑해야 해. 내가 그렇게 만들 거야!\" 그러면서 조는 벽에서 자신을 보고 웃고 있는 장난기 가득한 소년의 사진을 향해 위협하듯 고개를 흔들었다. \"어휴, 우리 정말 무섭게도 자라 버렸네. 메그는 결혼해서 엄마가 됐고, 에이미는 파리에서 잘나가고 있고, 베스는 사랑에 빠졌어. 사고나 안 치고 정신 똑바로 차린 건 나뿐이잖아.\" 조는 사진에 눈을 고정한 채 잠시 골똘히 생각하더니, 찌푸렸던 이마를 펴고, 맞은편 얼굴을 향해 단호하게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"아뇨, 됐어요, 선생님. 당신은 무척 매력적이지만, 바람개비만큼이나 줏대가 없거든요. 그러니 가슴 뭉클한 쪽지를 써 보내거나, 그런 식으로 은근슬쩍 미소 지을 필요 없어요. 아무 소용 없을 테고, 난 그런 거 안 받을 테니까.\"\n\n그러고 나서 조는 한숨을 쉬며 깊은 상념에 잠겼다. 이른 황혼이 그녀를 아래층으로 내려보내 새로운 관찰을 하게 만들 때까지 그 상념에서 깨어나지 못했고, 그 관찰은 그녀의 의심을 더욱 굳혀 줄 뿐이었다. 로리가 에이미와 시시덕거리고 조와 농담을 주고받기는 했지만, 베스를 대하는 태도만큼은 늘 유난히 다정하고 부드러웠다. 그러나 사람들 모두가 그랬으므로, 그가 베스를 다른 자매들보다 더 마음에 두고 있으리라고는 아무도 상상하지 못했다. 사실 요즘 들어 가족들 사이에는 \"우리 애\"가 조를 그 어느 때보다 좋아하고 있다는 인상이 널리 퍼져 있었다. 다만 조는 그 이야기를 한마디도 들으려 하지 않았고, 누가 감히 그런 말을 꺼내기라도 하면 사납게 쏘아붙였다. 만약 지난 한 해 동안 오갔던 갖가지 애틋한 장면들을, 아니, 싹도 트기 전에 잘려 나간 애틋한 장면들의 시도를 가족들이 알았더라면, \"그것 봐, 내가 뭐랬어\" 하고 말하는 어마어마한 만족감을 누렸을 것이다. 그러나 조는 \"연애질\"을 질색했고 그런 걸 용납하지 않았으며, 위험이 닥칠 기미가 조금이라도 보이면 늘 농담이나 미소를 준비해 두었다.\n\n로리는 대학에 처음 들어갔을 무렵 한 달에 한 번꼴로 사랑에 빠졌다. 하지만 이런 작은 불꽃들은 뜨거운 만큼이나 짧았고, 아무런 피해도 입히지 않았으며, 조를 무척 즐겁게 해 주었다. 조는 매주 둘이 나누는 대화에서 그가 털어놓는 희망과 절망과 체념의 교차를 흥미진진하게 들었다. 그런데 어느 시점에 이르러 로리는 여러 신전에 경배하기를 그만두고, 무언가 온통 마음을 사로잡는 열정에 대해 어둡게 암시하기 시작하더니, 이따금 바이런풍의 우울에 빠져들곤 했다. 그러고는 애틋한 화제를 아예 피하고, 조에게 철학적인 쪽지를 써 보내고, 면학에 매달렸으며, 화려한 영광 속에 졸업할 작정으로 \"파고들\" 거라고 공언했다. 이것은 황혼녘의 속삭임이나 다정하게 손을 맞잡는 일이나 그윽한 눈길보다 조에게 훨씬 잘 맞았다. 조는 가슴보다 머리가 먼저 발달한 데다, 진짜 영웅보다 상상 속 영웅을 더 좋아했기 때문이다. 상상 속 영웅이란 싫증이 나면 부를 때까지 양철 부엌에 가둬 둘 수 있는데, 진짜 영웅은 그렇게 다루기가 만만치 않았다.\n\n상황이 이러할 때 그 대단한 발견이 이루어졌고, 조는 그날 밤 일찍이 본 적 없는 눈으로 로리를 지켜보았다. 만약 그 새로운 생각이 머릿속에 박혀 있지 않았더라면, 베스가 무척 조용하고 로리가 베스에게 무척 친절하다는 사실에서 아무런 이상함도 느끼지 못했을 것이다. 그러나 발랄한 공상에 일단 고삐를 풀어 주자, 그 공상은 엄청난 속도로 조를 태우고 내달렸다. 그리고 오랫동안 연애 소설을 써 온 탓에 다소 약해진 상식은 그녀를 구하러 와 주지 않았다. 여느 때처럼 베스는 소파에 누워 있었고, 로리는 그 곁의 낮은 의자에 앉아 온갖 잡담으로 그녀를 즐겁게 해 주고 있었다. 베스는 매주 그의 \"수다\"에 기대를 걸었고, 그는 한 번도 그녀를 실망시키지 않았다. 그런데 그날 저녁, 조는 베스의 눈길이 곁에 있는 발랄하고 거무스레한 얼굴에 유난한 기쁨으로 머물러 있는 것처럼, 그리고 베스가 어느 흥미진진한 크리켓 경기 이야기를 강렬한 관심을 가지고 듣고 있는 것처럼 느꼈다. \"타이스에 걸려 아웃\", \"공이 빠져 스텀프 아웃\", \"레그 사이드로 쳐서 3점\" 같은 말들은 베스에게 산스크리트어만큼이나 알아들을 수 없는 것이었는데도 말이다. 또한 조는 그것을 꼭 보고야 말겠다고 마음먹은 터라, 로리의 태도에서 어떤 다정함이 더해진 것을, 그가 이따금 목소리를 낮추고, 평소보다 덜 웃고, 약간 넋이 나가 있고, 거의 다정하다 할 만큼 정성스레 베스의 발 위에 무릎담요를 덮어 주는 것을 본 것 같았다.\n\n\"누가 알겠어? 더 별난 일도 일어나는걸.\" 조는 방 안을 부산스레 오가며 생각했다. \"베스라면 저 애를 완전한 천사로 만들 거고, 저 애는 우리 사랑스러운 베스를 위해 인생을 즐겁고 편안하게 만들어 줄 거야. 둘이 서로 사랑하기만 한다면 말이지. 저 애가 안 그럴 리가 없잖아. 우리 나머지가 방해만 안 된다면 분명 그럴 거야.\"\n\n자신을 빼고는 모두가 방해되지 않는 처지였으므로, 조는 자기 자신을 한시바삐 치워 버려야겠다고 느끼기 시작했다. 그런데 어디로 가야 하나? 자매애라는 신전에 자기 몸을 바치고 싶어 안달이 난 조는, 그 문제를 정하려고 자리에 앉았다.\n\n그런데 그 낡은 소파는 소파 중에서도 어엿한 가장(家長)이었다. 길고, 넓고, 푹신하고, 낮았다. 다소 낡기는 했지만, 그럴 만도 한 것이, 아이들은 갓난아기였을 때 그 위에서 잠자고 뒹굴었고, 어린아이였을 때는 등받이 너머로 낚시질을 하고 팔걸이에 올라타고 그 밑에 동물원을 차렸으며, 처녀가 되어서는 그 위에서 지친 머리를 쉬고 꿈을 꾸고 다정한 이야기에 귀를 기울였다. 자매들은 모두 그 소파를 사랑했다. 그것은 가족의 안식처였고, 한쪽 구석은 늘 조가 가장 좋아하는 자리였다. 이 유서 깊은 소파를 장식한 여러 베개 가운데, 딱딱하고 둥글며 까슬까슬한 말총으로 덮여 있고 양 끝에 울퉁불퉁한 단추가 달린 것이 하나 있었다. 이 흉측한 베개야말로 조의 특별한 소유물이었다. 방어용 무기로, 바리케이드로, 혹은 지나친 졸음을 막는 엄중한 예방책으로 쓰였다.\n\n로리는 이 베개를 잘 알았고, 그것을 깊이 혐오할 만한 이유가 있었다. 한바탕 뒹구는 놀이가 허용되던 옛날에는 그 베개로 무자비하게 두들겨 맞았고, 지금은 소파 구석 조의 옆자리, 그가 가장 탐내는 그 자리에 앉지 못하도록 번번이 그 베개에 가로막혔기 때문이다. 그들이 \"소시지\"라고 부르던 그 베개가 세워져 있으면 그가 다가와 쉬어도 좋다는 신호였지만, 소파를 가로질러 납작하게 누워 있으면 감히 그것을 건드리는 남자든 여자든 아이든 화를 면치 못했다! 그날 저녁 조는 자기 구석에 바리케이드 치는 것을 깜빡 잊었고, 자리에 앉은 지 채 오 분도 되지 않아, 옆에 떡 벌어진 체구가 나타나더니, 두 팔을 소파 등받이에 쫙 펴고 두 다리를 앞으로 길게 뻗으며 로리가 만족스러운 한숨과 함께 외쳤다.\n\n\"자, 이거야말로 값진 호사로군.\"\n\n\"속어 쓰지 마.\" 조가 쏘아붙이며 베개를 탁 내리쳤다. 하지만 이미 늦었다. 베개를 둘 자리가 없었던 것이다. 베개는 바닥으로 미끄러져 내려가더니 도무지 알 수 없는 방식으로 사라져 버렸다.\n\n\"왜 그래, 조, 그렇게 가시 돋칠 거 없잖아. 일주일 내내 공부하느라 뼈만 남은 녀석은 떠받들어 줄 자격이 있고, 마땅히 그래야 하는 거라고.\"\n\n\"베스가 떠받들어 줄 거야. 난 바빠.\"\n\n\"아냐, 베스를 나 때문에 귀찮게 하면 안 되지. 그리고 넌 그런 거 좋아하잖아. 갑자기 취향이 바뀐 게 아니라면 말이야. 그래? 넌 네 녀석이 미워서 베개나 던지고 싶은 거야?\"\n\n그 가슴 뭉클한 호소보다 더 알랑거리는 말은 좀처럼 듣기 힘들었지만, 조는 엄격한 질문으로 돌아서며 \"자기 녀석\"의 기를 꺾어 버렸다.\n\n\"이번 주에 랜들 양한테 꽃다발 몇 개나 보냈어?\"\n\n\"맹세코 하나도 안 보냈어. 그 여자 약혼했거든. 자, 됐지.\"\n\n\"잘됐네. 그게 바로 네 어리석은 사치 중 하나야. 눈곱만큼도 마음에 없는 여자애들한테 꽃이며 뭐며 보내는 거.\" 조가 나무라듯 말을 이었다.\n\n\"마음에 쏙 드는 분별 있는 여자애들은 나한테 '꽃이며 뭐며' 보내게 해 주질 않으니, 내가 어쩌겠어? 내 감정도 배출구가 있어야 할 거 아냐.\"\n\n\"엄마는 장난이라도 시시덕거리는 걸 좋게 안 보셔. 그런데 넌 정말 필사적으로 시시덕거리잖아, 테디.\"\n\n\"'너도 그러잖아'라고 받아칠 수만 있다면 뭐든 내놓겠어. 그럴 수 없으니, 그냥 이 말만 할게. 관련된 모든 사람이 그게 그냥 놀이일 뿐이라는 걸 안다면, 그 즐거운 작은 놀이에 무슨 해가 있는지 모르겠다고.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 즐거워 보이긴 하는데, 난 그걸 어떻게 하는 건지 못 배우겠더라. 사람들 틈에서 다들 하는 걸 안 하면 어색하니까 해 보긴 했는데, 영 늘질 않는 거 있지.\" 조가 멘토 노릇 하는 걸 깜빡한 채 말했다.\n\n\"에이미한테 배워. 걔는 그 방면에 아주 타고난 재능이 있으니까.\"\n\n\"맞아, 걔는 그걸 아주 예쁘게 하고, 절대 도를 넘는 법이 없지. 어떤 사람들은 애쓰지 않아도 남을 즐겁게 하는 게 타고나고, 또 어떤 사람들은 늘 엉뚱한 자리에서 엉뚱한 말과 행동을 하는 게 타고나나 봐.\"\n\n\"난 네가 시시덕거리지 못하는 게 좋아. 분별 있고 솔직한 여자애가 자기 망신살 안 뻗치면서도 유쾌하고 다정할 수 있는 걸 보면 정말 신선하다니까. 우리끼리 얘긴데, 조, 내가 아는 여자애들 중엔 정말 어찌나 심하게 구는지 내가 다 부끄러울 정도야. 나쁜 뜻이 없는 건 분명한데, 우리 남자들이 나중에 자기들 얘기를 어떻게 떠드는지 알면 행실을 고칠 거야, 아마.\"\n\n\"여자애들도 똑같아. 게다가 여자들 혀가 더 매서우니까, 너희 남자들이 더 호되게 당하는 거지. 너희도 여자애들 못지않게 어리석거든, 하나부터 열까지. 너희가 제대로 처신하면 여자애들도 그럴 거야. 그런데 너희가 자기들 허튼짓을 좋아하는 걸 아니까 계속하는 거고, 그래 놓고 너흰 여자애들을 탓하잖아.\"\n\n\"네가 그쪽 일을 잘도 알겠다, 아가씨.\" 로리가 잘난 체하는 어투로 말했다. \"우린 가끔 그런 척할 때도 있지만, 까불대고 시시덕거리는 여자애들을 좋아하진 않아. 예쁘고 얌전한 여자애들은 신사들 사이에서 정중하게가 아니면 입에 오르내리지도 않는다고. 그 순진한 마음에 복이 있기를! 네가 한 달만 내 입장이 되어 보면 좀 놀랄 만한 것들을 보게 될걸. 정말이지, 그 덜렁대는 여자애들 중 하나를 볼 때마다, 난 늘 우리 친구 콕 로빈처럼 말하고 싶어진다니까——\n\n'썩 꺼져라, 망측해라, 뻔뻔스러운 깡충이야!'\"\n\n여자라는 족속을 험담하기 꺼리는 로리의 기사도적 망설임과, 사교계가 숱하게 보여 준 여자답지 못한 어리석음에 대한 그의 지극히 자연스러운 혐오, 그 우스꽝스러운 충돌에 웃지 않을 도리가 없었다. 조는 \"젊은 로런스\"가 세속적인 어머니들에게 더없이 좋은 신랑감으로 여겨지고, 그 딸들에게서 숱한 미소를 받으며, 온갖 나이대의 여성들에게서 멋쟁이 허풍선이로 만들 만큼 충분히 떠받들어진다는 걸 알고 있었다. 그래서 그가 버릇이 잘못 들까 두려워 다소 질투 어린 눈으로 그를 지켜보았고, 그가 여전히 얌전한 여자애들을 믿고 있다는 걸 알고는 입 밖으로 낸 것보다 훨씬 더 기뻤다. 갑자기 타이르는 어조로 돌아가, 조는 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. \"테디, 정 '배출구'가 있어야겠다면, 네가 정말 존경하는 그 '예쁘고 얌전한 여자애' 중 하나에게 가서 정성을 쏟아. 어리석은 애들한테 시간 낭비하지 말고.\"\n\n\"정말 그러라고 권하는 거야?\" 로리는 얼굴에 불안과 즐거움이 묘하게 뒤섞인 표정으로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"응, 그래. 하지만 전체적으로 보면 대학을 마칠 때까지 기다리는 게 낫겠어. 그동안 그 자리에 어울리는 사람이 되도록 갈고닦으면서 말이야. 넌 그——그러니까, 그 얌전한 여자애가 누구든 간에, 그 절반만큼도 못한 사람이거든.\" 조 역시 좀 묘한 표정이 되었는데, 하마터면 이름 하나가 입 밖으로 새어 나올 뻔했기 때문이다.\n\n\"맞아, 난 그래!\" 로리는 그에게는 영 낯선 겸허한 표정으로 수긍하며, 눈을 내리깔고는 무심히 조의 앞치마 술을 손가락에 감았다.\n\n\"세상에, 이러면 안 되는데.\" 조는 속으로 생각하며, 소리 내어 덧붙였다. \"가서 노래나 불러 줘. 난 음악이 너무 듣고 싶고, 네 노래는 늘 좋으니까.\"\n\n\"여기 있는 게 더 좋은데, 사양할게.\"\n\n\"안 돼, 그럴 수 없어. 자리가 없잖아. 가서 쓸모 있는 일이나 해. 장식품으로 두기엔 넌 너무 크니까. 넌 여자 앞치마 끈에 매여 있는 거 싫어한다며?\" 조가 예전에 그가 했던 반항적인 말을 인용하며 받아쳤다.\n\n\"아, 그건 누가 앞치마를 두르고 있느냐에 달렸지!\" 로리가 대담하게 술을 홱 잡아당겼다.\n\n\"갈 거야, 말 거야?\" 조가 베개를 향해 몸을 던지며 다그쳤다.\n\n그는 단숨에 달아났고, \"어여쁜 던디의 보닛을 높이 들어라\"가 한창 울려 퍼지는 사이에 조는 슬그머니 자리를 떴다. 그 젊은 신사가 잔뜩 골이 난 채 떠나 버릴 때까지 다시는 돌아오지 않았다.\n\n조는 그날 밤 오랫동안 잠들지 못하고 깨어 있다가, 막 잠이 들려는 참에 억눌린 흐느낌 소리가 들려와 베스의 침대맡으로 날아갔다. \"왜 그래, 베스?\" 그녀는 걱정스레 물었다.\n\n\"언니 자는 줄 알았어.\" 베스가 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"또 그 통증이니, 우리 귀한 동생?\"\n\n\"아니, 다른 통증이야. 그래도 견딜 수 있어.\" 베스는 눈물을 참으려 애썼다.\n\n\"전부 다 얘기해 봐. 예전 그 통증 자주 낫게 해 줬듯이, 내가 낫게 해 줄게.\"\n\n\"못 해. 이건 약이 없는 거야.\" 거기서 베스의 목소리가 무너졌고, 언니에게 매달려 어찌나 절망적으로 우는지 조는 겁이 났다.\n\n\"어디가 아픈데? 엄마 불러올까?\"\n\n베스는 첫 번째 질문에는 답하지 않았다. 하지만 어둠 속에서 한 손이 마치 거기에 통증이 있는 듯 저도 모르게 가슴으로 갔다. 다른 손으로는 조를 꼭 붙든 채 다급히 속삭였다. \"아니, 아니, 엄마 부르지 마, 엄마한텐 말하지 마. 나 곧 괜찮아질 거야. 여기 누워서 내 머리 좀 '쓰다듬어' 줘. 나 조용히 있다가 잘게, 정말이야.\"\n\n조는 그 말을 따랐다. 하지만 그녀의 손이 베스의 뜨거운 이마와 젖은 눈꺼풀 위를 부드럽게 오가는 동안, 그녀의 가슴은 벅차올랐고, 무어라 말하고 싶어 견딜 수 없었다. 그러나 조는 아직 어렸어도, 마음이란 꽃과 같아서 거칠게 다룰 수 없으며 저절로 피어나야 한다는 걸 배운 터였다. 그래서 베스의 새로운 통증의 원인을 안다고 믿으면서도, 더없이 다정한 어조로 이렇게만 말했다. \"뭔가 널 괴롭히는 게 있니, 동생아?\"\n\n\"응, 언니.\" 한참 뜸을 들인 뒤였다.\n\n\"그게 뭔지 나한테 말하면 위로가 되지 않을까?\"\n\n\"지금은 아니야, 아직은.\"\n\n\"그럼 안 물어볼게. 하지만 베스, 엄마랑 나는 할 수만 있다면 언제든 네 얘기를 들어 주고 도와주고 싶어 한다는 거, 잊지 마.\"\n\n\"알아. 조만간 말할게.\"\n\n\"이제 통증은 좀 나아졌어?\"\n\n\"응, 훨씬 나아. 언니가 옆에 있으니 너무 편안해, 조!\"\n\n\"자, 자거라, 동생아. 내가 곁에 있어 줄게.\"\n\n그렇게 둘은 뺨을 맞대고 잠이 들었고, 이튿날 베스는 완전히 제 모습으로 돌아온 듯했다. 열여덟 살에는 머리도 마음도 오래 아프지 않는 법이고, 사랑이 담긴 한마디면 웬만한 병은 다 나으니까.\n\n하지만 조는 마음을 굳혔고, 며칠 동안 한 가지 계획을 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에 엄마에게 털어놓았다.\n\n\"엄마, 며칠 전에 제 바람이 뭐냐고 물으셨죠. 그중 하나를 말씀드릴게요.\" 둘이 단둘이 앉아 있을 때 조가 입을 열었다. \"올겨울에 기분 전환 삼아 어디론가 떠나고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"왜, 조?\" 엄마는 그 말이 두 가지 의미를 담고 있기라도 한 듯 재빨리 고개를 들었다.\n\n조는 일감에 눈을 둔 채 차분히 대답했다. \"뭔가 새로운 게 필요해요. 안절부절못하겠고, 지금보다 더 많이 보고 행동하고 배우고 싶어 안달이 나요. 제 자잘한 일에 너무 골몰하니까, 좀 자극이 필요해요. 그러니까 올겨울엔 제가 없어도 괜찮으니, 잠깐 멀리 폴짝 뛰어나가 날개를 한번 시험해 보고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"어디로 폴짝 뛰어나갈 건데?\"\n\n\"뉴욕이요. 어제 좋은 생각이 떠올랐는데, 이거예요. 커크 부인이 엄마한테 편지로 자기 아이들 가르치고 바느질해 줄 점잖은 젊은 사람을 구한다고 했잖아요. 딱 맞는 사람을 찾기가 꽤 어렵긴 한데, 제가 해 보면 적임일 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"얘야, 그 큰 하숙집에 가서 일을 하겠다고!\" 마치 부인은 놀란 표정이었지만, 못마땅해하지는 않았다.\n\n\"꼭 일하러 가는 건 아니에요. 커크 부인은 엄마 친구이고——세상에 둘도 없이 마음씨 고운 분이고——제가 편하게 지내도록 해 주실 거예요, 전 알아요. 그 가족은 다른 사람들이랑 따로 지내고, 거긴 절 아는 사람도 없어요. 안다 해도 상관없고요. 떳떳한 일인데 부끄러울 게 뭐 있어요.\"\n\"나도 부끄럽다고 생각 안 한다. 그런데 네 글쓰기는?\"\n\n\"기분 전환하면 오히려 더 잘될 거예요. 새로운 걸 보고 듣고, 새로운 아이디어도 얻을 거고요. 거기서 시간이 많지 않더라도, 제 글거리가 될 재료를 잔뜩 가지고 돌아올 거예요.\"\n\n\"그건 의심하지 않는다. 그런데 이 갑작스러운 생각의 이유가 그게 다니?\"\n\n\"아니에요, 엄마.\"\n\n\"다른 이유도 알 수 있을까?\"\n\n조는 고개를 들었다가 다시 숙이더니, 두 뺨에 갑자기 홍조를 띤 채 천천히 말했다. \"이런 말 하는 게 잘난 척 같고 잘못된 건지도 모르겠지만——제 생각엔——로리가 절 너무 좋아하게 되는 것 같아서 겁이 나요.\"\n\n\"그럼 넌, 그 애가 너에 대해 분명히 품기 시작한 그런 감정을 똑같이 그 애한테 품고 있진 않다는 거니?\" 마치 부인이 그 질문을 던지며 걱정스러운 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"세상에, 아니에요! 그 사랑스러운 애를 늘 그랬듯이 아끼고, 그 애가 무척 자랑스럽긴 해요. 하지만 그 이상은, 어림도 없어요.\"\n\n\"그렇다니 다행이구나, 조.\"\n\n\"왜요, 엄마?\"\n\n\"얘야, 너희 둘은 서로 어울리지 않는다고 생각하거든. 친구로서는 너희가 무척 행복하고, 자주 다퉈도 금세 풀리지. 하지만 만약 평생을 짝으로 맺어진다면 둘 다 반발할 것 같아 걱정이야. 너희는 너무 닮았고 자유를 너무 좋아하는 데다, 다혈질에 고집까지 센데, 사랑은 물론이고 무한한 인내와 너그러움이 필요한 관계에서 행복하게 지내기란 어려운 일이거든.\"\n\n\"제가 느낀 게 바로 그거예요, 표현은 못 했지만. 엄마가 그 애가 이제 막 절 좋아하기 시작한 거라고 생각하셔서 기뻐요. 그 애를 불행하게 만든다면 너무 마음이 아플 거예요. 단지 고마워서 그 사랑스러운 옛 친구랑 사랑에 빠질 수는 없잖아요, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"그 애가 너한테 품은 감정을 확신하니?\"\n\n조의 두 뺨에 홍조가 짙어졌고, 첫사랑을 이야기하는 어린 처녀들이 짓는, 기쁨과 자긍심과 아픔이 뒤섞인 표정으로 대답했다.\n\n\"그런 것 같아요, 엄마. 그 애가 뭐라고 말한 건 아니지만, 눈빛이 많은 걸 말하거든요. 일이 어떻게 되기 전에 멀리 떠나는 게 나을 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"엄마도 같은 생각이다. 그러니 일이 잘 풀린다면 너는 떠나는 거다.\"\n\n조는 마음이 놓이는 표정이었고, 잠시 뒤 미소 지으며 말했다. \"모펏 부인이 이 일을 알면 엄마의 처세가 서투르다고 얼마나 놀랄까요. 그리고 애니한테 아직 희망이 있다고 얼마나 기뻐할까요.\"\n\n\"아, 조, 엄마들마다 처세는 다를지언정 바라는 건 다 똑같단다——제 자식이 행복한 걸 보고 싶은 마음이지. 메그는 행복하고, 엄마는 그 애가 잘된 걸로 만족한다. 너는 자유를 실컷 누리도록 내버려 두마, 네가 그것에 싫증이 날 때까지. 그래야만 비로소 그보다 더 달콤한 게 있다는 걸 깨닫게 될 테니까. 지금 엄마가 가장 마음 쓰는 건 에이미인데, 그 애는 분별이 있으니 스스로 잘 헤쳐 나갈 거야. 베스에 대해선, 그저 건강하기만을 바랄 뿐 다른 바람은 품지 않는다. 그러고 보니 그 애가 요 며칠 좀 밝아 보이더구나. 네가 그 애랑 이야기를 나눴니?\"\n\n\"네. 베스가 고민이 있다고 인정했고, 조만간 말해 주겠다고 약속했어요. 전 더 묻지 않았어요, 그게 뭔지 알 것 같아서요.\" 그러고는 조가 자신의 짤막한 이야기를 들려주었다.\n\n마치 부인은 고개를 가로저으며 그 일을 그리 낭만적으로 보지 않았지만, 진지한 표정으로, 로리를 위해서라도 조가 한동안 떠나 있는 게 좋겠다는 의견을 거듭 밝혔다.\n\n\"계획이 정해지기 전까진 그 애한테 아무 말도 하지 말기로 해요. 그런 다음 그 애가 정신 차리고 비극을 연출하기 전에 제가 달아나 버릴게요. 베스는 제가 그저 제 좋자고 가는 줄 알 거예요, 실제로도 그렇고요. 베스한테 로리 얘긴 할 수 없으니까요. 하지만 제가 떠나고 나면 베스가 그 애를 다독이고 위로해 줄 수 있을 테고, 그렇게 해서 그 애의 이 낭만적인 생각을 고쳐 줄 수 있겠죠. 그 애는 이런 사소한 시련을 워낙 많이 겪어 봐서 익숙하니까, 금세 이 상사병을 떨쳐 낼 거예요.\"\n\n조는 희망 섞인 어조로 말했지만, 이 \"사소한 시련\"이 다른 시련들보다 더 힘겨우리라는, 그리고 로리가 예전처럼 쉽게 \"상사병\"을 떨쳐 내지는 못하리라는 불길한 예감을 떨쳐 버릴 수 없었다.\n\n이 계획은 가족회의에서 논의되어 합의에 이르렀다. 커크 부인이 조를 기꺼이 받아들였고, 편안한 보금자리를 마련해 주겠다고 약속했기 때문이다. 가르치는 일은 조를 자립하게 해 줄 테고, 틈틈이 생기는 여가는 글쓰기로 알차게 쓸 수 있을 터였다. 게다가 새로운 풍경과 사람들은 유익하면서도 즐거운 경험이 될 것이었다. 조는 이 전망이 마음에 들었고 어서 떠나고 싶어 안달이었다. 집이라는 둥지가 안절부절못하는 그녀의 천성과 모험심에는 너무 비좁아지고 있었던 것이다. 모든 게 정해지자, 조는 두려움에 떨며 로리에게 그 소식을 전했다. 그런데 뜻밖에도 그는 무척 담담하게 받아들였다. 그는 요즘 들어 평소보다 진지했지만 무척 유쾌했고, 새사람이 되려는 거냐고 농담 삼아 추궁하자 차분하게 대답했다. \"맞아. 그리고 이번엔 새사람이 된 채로 쭉 갈 작정이야.\"\n\n조는 마침 그때 그의 갸륵한 마음이 발동한 것에 무척 안도하며 한결 가벼워진 마음으로 떠날 채비를 했다. 베스도 더 명랑해 보였고, 자신이 모두를 위해 최선을 다하고 있다고 믿어졌다.\n\n\"한 가지를 특별히 너한테 부탁하고 갈게.\" 떠나기 전날 밤 조가 말했다.\n\n\"언니 원고들 말이야?\" 베스가 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 내 녀석 말이야. 그 애한테 잘해 줘, 알았지?\"\n\n\"당연히 그럴게. 하지만 내가 언니 자리를 대신할 순 없고, 그 앤 언니를 무척 그리워할 거야.\"\n\n\"그래도 그 앤 괜찮을 거야. 그러니까 기억해, 내가 그 애를 너한테 맡기는 거야. 골려 주고, 다독여 주고, 단속해 주라고.\"\n\n\"언니를 위해서 최선을 다할게.\" 베스가 약속하며, 조가 왜 자기를 그렇게 묘하게 쳐다보는지 의아해했다.\n\n로리는 \"잘 가\"라고 말하면서, 의미심장하게 속삭였다. \"아무 소용 없을걸, 조. 내 눈이 널 지켜보고 있으니까, 행동 조심해. 안 그러면 내가 가서 널 집으로 데려올 거야.\"\n\"뉴욕, 11월.\n\n사랑하는 엄마와 베스에게,\n\n제가 대륙을 여행하는 우아한 아가씨는 아니지만, 들려드릴 이야기가 산더미라 책 한 권은 너끈히 쓸 작정이에요. 아빠의 사랑스러운 옛 얼굴이 시야에서 사라졌을 때 좀 울적해져서 짭짤한 눈물 한두 방울 흘릴 뻔했어요. 그런데 어린아이 넷을 데리고 탄 어떤 아일랜드 부인이 제 마음을 딴 데로 돌려놓지 않았다면 정말 그랬을 거예요. 그 애들이 너나없이 울어 댔거든요. 그 애들이 울려고 입을 벌릴 때마다 좌석 너머로 생강과자를 떨어뜨려 주면서 혼자 즐거워했어요.\n\n곧 해가 나왔는데, 좋은 징조로 여기고 저도 마음이 환해져서 여행을 진심으로 즐겼어요.\n\n커크 부인이 어찌나 친절히 맞아 주시는지, 낯선 사람들로 가득한 그 큰 집에서도 단번에 마음이 편안해졌어요. 부인은 저한테 우습고 자그마한 다락방을 내주셨어요. 가진 게 그것뿐이라면서요. 그래도 난로가 있고, 볕이 드는 창가에 멋진 탁자가 있어서, 마음 내킬 때마다 여기 앉아 글을 쓸 수 있어요. 전망도 좋고 맞은편엔 교회 탑도 있어서 그 많은 계단을 오르내리는 수고가 보상이 돼요. 전 단번에 제 방이 마음에 들었어요. 제가 아이들을 가르치고 바느질할 육아실은 커크 부인의 개인 응접실 옆에 있는 쾌적한 방이고, 두 어린 여자아이는 예쁜 아이들이에요. 좀 버릇없긴 한 것 같지만, '못된 일곱 마리 돼지' 이야기를 들려줬더니 저를 따르더라고요. 전 분명히 모범적인 가정교사가 될 거예요.\n\n제가 큰 식탁보다 아이들과 함께 먹는 게 좋으면 그래도 된대요. 당분간은 그러려고요. 아무도 안 믿겠지만, 전 정말 수줍음을 타거든요.\n\n'자, 얘야, 편하게 지내렴.' 커크 부인이 어머니 같은 어투로 말씀하셨어요. '보다시피 이렇게 식구가 많으니 난 아침부터 밤까지 동분서주한단다. 그래도 아이들이 너와 함께 있어 안전하다는 걸 알면 큰 걱정 하나는 덜겠구나. 내 방은 언제든 너한테 열려 있고, 네 방도 내가 할 수 있는 한 편안하게 해 주마. 사람들과 어울리고 싶으면, 이 집에 좋은 사람들이 더러 있단다. 저녁 시간은 언제나 자유고. 뭔가 잘 안 풀리면 나한테 오렴, 그리고 되도록 즐겁게 지내거라. 어머나, 차 마실 시간 종이 울리네. 어서 가서 모자를 바꿔 써야겠다.' 그러고는 부산스레 가 버리셔서, 저는 새 둥지에 혼자 자리를 잡게 됐어요.\n\n조금 뒤에 아래층으로 내려가다가 마음에 드는 광경을 봤어요. 이 높다란 집은 계단참이 무척 긴데, 제가 세 번째 계단참 꼭대기에서 어린 하녀 아이가 무겁게 올라오기를 기다리고 서 있는데, 어떤 신사가 그 애 뒤로 따라오더니, 그 애 손에서 무거운 석탄 통을 받아 들고, 위까지 죽 날라다가, 가까운 어느 문 앞에 내려놓고는, 친절히 고개를 끄덕이며 외국식 억양으로 이렇게 말하고 걸어가는 거예요.\n\n'이렇게 하는 게 낫지요. 어린 등이 그런 무거운 걸 들기엔 너무 어려요.'\n\n참 갸륵하지 않아요? 전 그런 게 좋아요. 아빠 말씀처럼 사소한 일이 사람 됨됨이를 보여 주잖아요. 그날 저녁 커크 부인한테 그 얘기를 했더니 웃으면서 이러시더라고요.\n\n'분명 베어 교수일 거야. 그분은 늘 그런 일을 하거든.'\n\n커크 부인 말로는 그분은 베를린에서 왔고, 무척 박식하고 선량하지만, 교회 쥐만큼이나 가난해서, 자기 입에 풀칠하고 여기서 가르치고 있는 어린 고아 조카 둘을 교육시키느라 강습을 한대요. 미국 사람과 결혼한 누이의 뜻에 따라서 그 조카들을 맡았다나 봐요. 그리 낭만적인 이야기는 아니지만 저는 흥미로웠어요. 그리고 커크 부인이 그분 학생 몇몇을 위해 응접실을 빌려준다는 얘길 듣고 반가웠어요. 응접실과 육아실 사이에 유리문이 있거든요. 그래서 그분을 슬쩍 엿보고, 어떻게 생긴 분인지 말씀드릴게요. 그분은 마흔 가까이 되었으니, 흠 잡힐 일은 아니죠, 엄마.\n\n차를 마시고 어린 여자아이들과 잠자리 들기 전 한바탕 뒹굴고 난 뒤, 저는 큰 반짇고리를 공략했고, 새 친구와 도란도란 이야기 나누며 조용한 저녁을 보냈어요. 일기 형식으로 편지를 써서 일주일에 한 번씩 부칠게요. 그럼 안녕히 주무세요. 내일 더 쓸게요.\"\n\n\"화요일 저녁.\n\n오늘 아침엔 제 학당에서 한바탕 소동이 벌어졌어요. 아이들이 산초처럼 굴어서, 한순간엔 정말 전부 붙잡고 흔들어 줄까 싶더라니까요. 어느 착한 천사가 영감을 줘서 체조를 시켜 봤는데, 아이들이 기진맥진해서 기꺼이 자리에 앉아 얌전히 있을 때까지 계속했어요. 점심을 먹고 나서 하녀 아이가 애들을 데리고 산책을 나갔고, 저는 작은 메이블처럼 '기꺼운 마음으로' 바느질에 매달렸어요. 단춧구멍을 깔끔하게 만드는 법을 배워 둔 걸 다행으로 여기고 있는데, 응접실 문이 열렸다 닫히더니 누군가 콧노래를 흥얼거리기 시작하는 거예요——\n\n'그대는 아는가, 저 나라를'\n\n마치 커다란 호박벌처럼요. 무례한 짓인 줄 알지만, 유혹을 떨칠 수가 없었어요. 그래서 유리문 앞에 드리운 커튼 한쪽 끝을 들추고 안을 들여다봤죠. 베어 교수가 거기 있었어요. 그분이 책을 정리하는 동안 저는 그분을 찬찬히 뜯어봤어요. 영락없는 독일 사람이에요. 좀 통통하고, 갈색 머리는 온통 헝클어져 있고, 텁수룩한 턱수염에, 코는 잘생겼고, 제가 본 중에 가장 다정한 눈을 가졌고, 우리 미국 사람들의 새된 수다나 어설픈 말투 끝에 듣자니 귀가 다 시원해지는 멋지고 우렁찬 목소리를 지녔어요. 옷은 후줄근하고 손은 컸고, 얼굴엔 정말 잘생긴 데라곤 한 군데도 없었어요. 아름다운 치아만 빼고요. 그래도 저는 그분이 마음에 들었어요. 멋진 머리를 가졌거든요. 셔츠는 무척 깔끔했고, 코트 단추 두 개가 떨어지고 한쪽 신발엔 기운 자국이 있었지만, 신사처럼 보였어요. 흥얼거리는 와중에도 진지해 보이더니, 창가로 가서 히아신스 구근을 햇빛 쪽으로 돌려놓고 고양이를 쓰다듬는데, 그 고양이가 오랜 친구처럼 그분을 반기더라고요. 그제야 그분이 미소를 지었어요. 그리고 문을 똑똑 두드리는 소리가 나자, 우렁차고 활기찬 목소리로 외쳤어요——\n\n'들어와요!'\n\n막 달아나려는데, 큰 책을 든 자그마한 아이가 눈에 들어와서, 무슨 일인가 보려고 멈춰 섰어요.\n\n'베어 아저씨가 필요해요.' 그 꼬마가 책을 쾅 내려놓고 그분에게 달려가며 말했어요.\n\n'네 베어 아저씨를 주마. 자, 이리 와서 아저씨한테 꼭 안기렴, 우리 티나.' 교수가 웃으며 그 애를 번쩍 들어 올려 머리 위로 한껏 치켜드는 바람에, 아이는 입을 맞추려고 자그마한 얼굴을 숙여야 했어요.\n\n'이제 공부를 해야 해요.' 그 우스운 꼬마가 말을 이었어요. 그러자 그분은 아이를 탁자 위에 앉히고, 아이가 가져온 큰 사전을 펼쳐 종이와 연필을 주었고, 아이는 이따금 책장을 넘기며 글씨를 끄적였어요. 마치 단어를 찾는 듯 통통한 작은 손가락으로 책장을 죽 짚어 내려가는데, 어찌나 진지한지 하마터면 웃음을 터뜨려 들킬 뻔했어요. 그동안 베어 씨는 그 아이의 예쁜 머리칼을 쓰다듬으며 아버지 같은 눈길로 바라보아서, 그 애가 독일 아이라기보다 프랑스 아이처럼 보였는데도 분명 그분 친자식일 거라는 생각이 들었어요.\n\n또 누군가 노크를 하고 젊은 아가씨 둘이 나타나는 바람에 저는 일감으로 돌아갔고, 옆방에서 벌어지는 온갖 시끄러운 소리와 떠드는 소리에도 꿋꿋이 자리를 지켰어요. 한 아가씨는 계속 꾸민 듯이 웃으면서 교태 어린 어조로 '어머, 교수님' 하고 그랬고, 다른 아가씨는 독일어를 어찌나 이상한 억양으로 발음하는지 그분이 진지함을 유지하기 어려웠을 거예요.\n\n둘 다 그분의 인내심을 호되게 시험하는 것 같았어요. 그분이 한 번 이상 단호하게 이렇게 말하는 게 들렸거든요. '아니요, 아니에요, 그게 아니지요. 내가 한 말을 안 들으셨군요.' 그리고 한 번은 책으로 탁자를 내리치기라도 한 듯 쾅 하는 소리가 나더니, 절망적인 탄식이 뒤따랐어요. '프루트! 오늘은 죄다 엉망진창이군.'\n\n가엾은 분, 정말 안쓰러웠어요. 그래서 아가씨들이 가고 난 뒤, 그분이 무사한지 보려고 딱 한 번 더 들여다봤어요. 그분은 기진맥진해서 의자에 몸을 뒤로 던지듯 기댄 채, 눈을 감고 앉아 있다가, 시계가 두 시를 치자 벌떡 일어나 다음 강습이라도 준비하듯 책들을 주머니에 넣고는, 소파에서 잠든 어린 티나를 안아 들어 조용히 데리고 나갔어요. 아무래도 그분은 고된 삶을 사는 것 같아요.\n\n커크 부인이 다섯 시 만찬에 내려오지 않겠냐고 물으셨어요. 저는 좀 향수에 젖어 있던 터라, 저랑 한 지붕 아래 사는 사람들이 어떤 부류인지나 볼까 해서 그러겠다고 했어요. 그래서 단정하게 차려입고 커크 부인 뒤에 슬쩍 묻어 들어가려 했는데, 부인은 키가 작고 저는 키가 커서, 몸을 숨기려는 노력이 영 실패로 돌아갔어요. 부인이 자기 옆자리를 내주셨고, 저는 얼굴 화끈거리는 게 가라앉은 뒤에 용기를 내어 주위를 둘러봤어요. 긴 식탁은 사람들로 꽉 찼고, 다들 식사에 여념이 없었어요. 특히 신사들은 시간 맞춰 먹어야 하는 듯, 말 그대로 후딱 해치우고는 다 먹기가 무섭게 사라져 버렸어요. 거기엔 으레 있는 부류가 다 모여 있었어요. 자기 자신에게 몰두한 젊은 남자들, 서로에게 몰두한 젊은 연인들, 아기에게 몰두한 결혼한 부인들, 정치에 몰두한 노신사들. 한 사람만 빼고는 누구와도 깊이 엮이고 싶지 않을 것 같아요. 그 한 사람은 다정한 얼굴의 노처녀인데, 속에 뭔가 든 사람처럼 보이거든요.\n\n식탁 맨 끝자리에 내쳐지듯 앉아 있는 분이 바로 그 교수였어요. 한쪽에선 무척 꼬치꼬치 캐묻는 귀먹은 노신사의 질문에 큰 소리로 답하고, 다른 한쪽에선 어느 프랑스 사람과 철학을 논하면서요. 만약 에이미가 여기 있었다면 그분에게서 영영 등을 돌렸을 거예요. 말하기 안됐지만, 그분은 식욕이 어마어마해서 '귀하신 그 아가씨'가 기겁할 만한 방식으로 음식을 퍼 넣었거든요. 저는 개의치 않았어요. 해나 말마따나 '사람이 맛나게 먹는 걸 보는' 게 좋고, 게다가 하루 종일 멍청이들을 가르친 가엾은 분이라면 음식이 무척 많이 필요했을 테니까요.\n\n만찬을 마치고 위층으로 올라가는데, 젊은 남자 둘이 현관 거울 앞에서 모자를 매만지고 있었고, 한 사람이 다른 사람에게 나직이 하는 말이 들렸어요. '저 새로 온 여자는 누구야?'\n\n'가정교사나 뭐 그런 거겠지.'\n\n'대체 왜 우리 식탁에 앉아 있는 거야?'\n\n'노부인 친구래.'\n\n'머리는 그럴듯한데, 세련된 맛이 없군.'\n\n'전혀 없지. 불 좀 빌려줘, 가자.'\n\n처음엔 화가 났는데, 그다음엔 상관없어졌어요. 가정교사도 점원만큼이나 떳떳한 일이고, 저는 세련된 맛은 없어도 분별은 있으니까요. 그건 어떤 사람들보다는 나은 거잖아요. 굴뚝처럼 담배 연기를 뿜으며 또각또각 걸어가던 그 우아하신 분들의 말투로 미루어 보면요. 저는 속물 같은 인간들이 정말 싫어요!\"\n\n\"목요일.\n\n어제는 조용한 하루였어요. 불 켜고 난로 지핀, 무척 아늑한 제 작은 방에서 가르치고 바느질하고 글 쓰며 보냈죠. 자잘한 소식을 몇 가지 주워들었고, 교수에게 소개도 받았어요. 알고 보니 티나는 여기 세탁실에서 고급 다림질을 맡고 있는 프랑스 여자의 아이래요. 그 꼬마는 베어 씨에게 홀딱 반해서, 그분이 집에 있을 때면 강아지처럼 집 안 곳곳을 졸졸 따라다녀요. 그분은 '총각'이지만 아이들을 무척 좋아해서 그걸 흐뭇해하시고요. 키티와 미니 커크도 그분을 무척 따르고, 그분이 만들어 내는 놀이며, 가져다주는 선물이며, 들려주는 멋진 이야기에 대해 온갖 얘기를 늘어놓아요. 젊은 남자들은 그분을 놀려 대는 것 같아요. '늙은 프리츠', '라거 맥주', '큰곰자리'라고 부르면서 그분 이름을 가지고 온갖 농담을 한대요. 그래도 그분은 소년처럼 그걸 즐기고, 어찌나 너그럽게 받아넘기는지, 그분의 외국식 행동거지에도 불구하고 다들 그분을 좋아한대요. 커크 부인이 그러시더라고요.\n\n그 노처녀는 노턴 양이라는 분이에요. 부유하고, 교양 있고, 친절하죠. 오늘 만찬에서 저한테 말을 거셨어요. (제가 또 식탁에 갔거든요. 사람 구경하는 게 어찌나 재밌는지요.) 그러고는 자기 방에 한번 놀러 오라고 하셨어요. 그분은 좋은 책과 그림을 가지고 있고, 흥미로운 사람들도 알고, 친근해 보여요. 그러니 저도 싹싹하게 굴어야겠어요. 정말이지 좋은 사람들과 어울리고 싶거든요. 다만 에이미가 좋아하는 부류와는 다른 부류지만요.\n\n어젯밤 우리 응접실에 있는데 베어 씨가 커크 부인께 드릴 신문 몇 부를 들고 들어오셨어요. 부인은 거기 없었지만, 작은 노부인 같은 미니가 저를 아주 예쁘게 소개했어요. '이분은 엄마 친구인 마치 양이에요.'\n\n'네, 그리고 유쾌하고, 우린 이 언니를 엄청 좋아해요.' 천방지축인 키티가 덧붙였어요.\n\n우리 둘은 인사를 나누고 나서 웃음을 터뜨렸어요. 깍듯한 소개와 무뚝뚝한 첨언이 꽤 우스꽝스러운 대조를 이뤘거든요.\n\n'아, 그렇군요, 이 말썽꾸러기들이 마치 양을 성가시게 하는 모양이지요. 또 그러거든 나를 부르세요, 그럼 내가 가지요.' 그분이 위협하듯 인상을 찌푸리며 말했고, 그 작은 악동들은 그 모습에 신이 났어요.\n\n저는 그러겠다고 했고, 그분은 가셨어요. 그런데 아무래도 저는 그분을 자주 마주칠 운명인가 봐요. 오늘 외출하다가 그분 방 앞을 지나는데, 그만 우산으로 문을 툭 치고 말았거든요. 문이 벌컥 열리더니, 그 안에 그분이 실내복 차림으로 서 있었어요. 한 손엔 커다란 파란 양말을 끼고, 다른 손엔 깁는 바늘을 들고서요. 그분은 전혀 부끄러워하는 기색이 없었어요. 제가 사정을 설명하고 서둘러 가려는데, 양말까지 낀 손을 흔들면서 특유의 우렁차고 명랑한 어투로 말씀하셨거든요——\n\n'산책하기 좋은 날이군요. 잘 다녀오세요, 마드무아젤.'\n\n저는 아래층까지 내려가는 내내 웃었어요. 하지만 가엾은 분이 직접 옷을 기워야 한다고 생각하니 좀 애처롭기도 했어요. 독일 신사들이 자수는 놓는다지만, 양말 깁기는 또 다른 얘기고, 그리 보기 좋은 일도 아니잖아요.\"\n\n\"토요일.\n\n쓸 만한 일이라곤 노턴 양을 방문한 것밖에 없네요. 그분 방은 사랑스러운 물건들로 가득했고, 그분은 무척 매력적이었어요. 자기 보물을 죄다 보여 주고는, 제가 강연이나 음악회를 좋아한다면 가끔 자기와 함께 동행자로 가지 않겠냐고 물으셨거든요. 호의처럼 말씀하셨지만, 분명 커크 부인이 우리 사정을 얘기해서 저를 배려해 주시려는 거예요. 저는 루시퍼처럼 자존심이 세지만, 그런 분들의 그런 호의는 부담스럽지 않아서, 감사히 받아들였어요.\n\n육아실로 돌아오니 응접실에서 어찌나 시끌벅적한지 들여다봤어요. 베어 씨가 손과 무릎을 바닥에 짚고 엎드려 있는데 티나가 그 등에 올라타 있고, 키티는 줄넘기 줄로 그분을 끌고 다니고, 미니는 의자로 만든 우리 안에서 으르렁대며 날뛰는 사내아이 둘에게 씨앗 케이크를 먹이고 있었어요.\n\n'우리 동물원 놀이 하는 거예요.' 키티가 설명했어요.\n\n'이건 내 코끼리예요!' 티나가 교수의 머리칼을 붙들고 덧붙였어요.\n\n'엄마는 토요일 오후에 프란츠랑 에밀이 오면 우리가 하고 싶은 대로 하게 늘 허락해 주세요, 그렇죠, 베어 씨?' 미니가 말했어요.\n\n그 '코끼리'가 몸을 일으켜 앉더니, 누구 못지않게 진지한 표정으로 저한테 차분히 말했어요——\n\n'정말입니다, 내 약속하지요. 우리가 너무 시끄럽게 굴거든 \"쉿!\" 하고 말해 주세요, 그럼 우린 더 조용히 할 테니까요.'\n\n저는 그러겠다고 약속했지만, 문을 열어 둔 채 아이들 못지않게 그 놀이를 즐겼어요. 그렇게 신나는 한바탕 놀이는 본 적이 없거든요. 술래잡기와 병정놀이를 하고, 춤추고 노래하다가, 날이 어둑해지자 모두 교수를 둘러싸고 소파에 우르르 올라앉았고, 그분은 굴뚝 꼭대기에 사는 황새들과, 눈송이가 떨어질 때 그걸 타고 내려오는 작은 '코볼트들' 이야기 같은 멋진 동화를 들려줬어요. 미국 사람들도 독일 사람들처럼 소박하고 꾸밈없으면 좋겠어요, 그렇지 않아요?\n\n전 글쓰기를 워낙 좋아해서, 절약을 생각하지 않았다면 한도 끝도 없이 써 내려갔을 거예요. 얇은 종이에 잔글씨로 썼는데도, 이 긴 편지에 우표가 얼마나 들지 생각하면 아찔하거든요. 에이미 편지는 여유 되는 대로 어서 부쳐 주세요. 제 자잘한 소식은 그 애의 화려한 소식 뒤에 들으면 무척 시시하겠지만, 두 분은 좋아하실 거 알아요. 테디는 친구들한테 편지 쓸 틈도 없을 만큼 그렇게 열심히 공부하는 거예요? 저 대신 그 애 잘 챙겨 줘, 베스. 그리고 아기들 얘기도 다 들려주고, 모두에게 사랑을 듬뿍 전해 줘.\n\n언제나 변함없는 너희의 조가.\n\n추신. 편지를 다시 읽어 보니 좀 베어 교수 얘기투성이 같네요. 하지만 저는 별난 사람들한테 늘 관심이 가고, 정말 달리 쓸 거리가 없었거든요. 모두 잘 지내요!\"\n\"12월.\n\n사랑하는 우리 베치에게,\n이건 갈겨쓰는 두서없는 편지가 될 테니까 너한테 보낸다. 네가 재밌어할 테고, 내가 어떻게 지내는지 짐작도 될 거야. 조용히 지내긴 해도 꽤 재밌게 지내거든. 그러니 자, 기뻐하렴! 에이미라면 '헤르쿨라네움 같은 노력'이라고 부를 만한 정신적·도덕적 농사를 지은 끝에, 내 어린 새싹들이 움트기 시작하고, 어린 잔가지들이 내가 바라는 대로 휘어지고 있어. 그 애들은 티나나 사내아이들만큼 흥미롭진 않지만, 난 그 애들한테 내 본분을 다하고, 그 애들도 날 좋아해. 프란츠랑 에밀은 유쾌한 꼬맹이들인데, 딱 내 마음에 들어. 그 애들 안에 독일과 미국의 기질이 뒤섞여 있어서 늘 부글부글 끓어오르는 상태거든. 토요일 오후는 집 안에서 보내든 밖에서 보내든 시끌벅적한 시간이야. 날이 좋으면 다들 학당처럼 줄지어 산책을 나가는데, 교수와 내가 질서를 잡는 역할을 맡아. 그러면 얼마나 재밌는지!\n\n우린 이제 무척 좋은 친구가 됐고, 난 강습도 받기 시작했어. 정말 안 받을 수가 없었는데, 그 사연이 어찌나 우스꽝스러운지 꼭 들려줘야겠어. 처음부터 얘기하자면, 어느 날 내가 베어 씨 방 앞을 지나는데 커크 부인이 그 안에서 뭔가를 뒤지다가 날 부르셨어.\n\n'얘야, 이런 굴 본 적 있니? 어서 와서 이 책들 정리하는 것 좀 도와주렴. 얼마 전에 내가 준 새 손수건 여섯 장을 이분이 어떻게 했는지 찾아내려고 죄다 뒤집어엎어 놨거든.'\n\n들어가서 정리하면서 둘러봤는데, 정말이지 '굴'이 따로 없더라. 책이랑 종이가 사방에 널려 있고, 벽난로 선반 위엔 깨진 해포석 파이프랑 다 쓴 듯한 낡은 플루트가 놓여 있고, 꽁지 빠진 너덜너덜한 새 한 마리가 한쪽 창가에서 짹짹거리고, 다른 쪽 창가는 흰 쥐가 든 상자가 장식하고 있었어. 만들다 만 배들이랑 끈 토막이 원고들 사이에 널려 있고, 더러운 작은 장화가 난롯불 앞에서 마르고 있고, 그분이 종처럼 헌신하는 그 사랑스러운 사내아이들의 흔적이 온 방에 가득했어. 한바탕 뒤진 끝에 잃어버린 손수건 중 세 장을 찾았는데, 하나는 새장 위에 있고, 하나는 잉크범벅이 됐고, 또 하나는 냄비 받침으로 쓰여서 갈색으로 그을려 있었어.\n\n'사람도 참!' 마음씨 좋은 커크 부인이 그 잔해들을 헝겊 자루에 넣으며 웃으셨어. '나머지는 배 돛 만드느라, 베인 손가락 싸매느라, 아니면 연 꼬리 만드느라 찢어 버렸겠지. 한심하긴 하지만 그분을 나무랄 수가 없어. 어찌나 정신이 없고 너그러운지, 저 사내애들이 자길 마구잡이로 부려 먹게 내버려 두거든. 내가 그분 빨래랑 수선을 맡아 주기로 했는데, 그분은 내놓을 걸 내놓는 걸 잊고, 나는 또 그걸 살피는 걸 잊어버려서, 그분이 가끔 딱한 처지가 된단다.'\n\n'제가 수선할게요.' 내가 말했어. '저는 괜찮고, 그분이 몰라도 되잖아요. 저도 하고 싶어요. 제 편지 가져다주고 책 빌려주고, 그분이 저한테 어찌나 친절하신데요.'\n\n그래서 그분 물건을 정리해 두고, 양말 두 켤레엔 뒤꿈치를 떠 넣었어. 그분의 별난 깁기 솜씨 탓에 모양이 다 뒤틀려 있었거든. 아무 말도 안 했고 그분이 알아채지 못하길 바랐는데, 지난주 어느 날 나를 딱 붙잡고 마셨지 뭐야. 그분이 남들한테 해 주는 강습을 듣다 보니 어찌나 흥미롭고 재밌던지 나도 배우고 싶어졌거든. 티나가 들락날락하면서 문을 열어 둬서 들을 수 있었어. 나는 이 문 가까이 앉아 마지막 양말을 마무리하면서, 그분이 나만큼이나 아둔한 새 학생에게 하는 말을 알아들어 보려 애쓰고 있었어. 그 여학생은 가고, 너무 조용해서 그분도 갔거니 했는데, 나는 동사 하나를 부지런히 중얼거리면서 우스꽝스럽기 짝이 없게 몸을 앞뒤로 흔들고 있었어. 그런데 작은 웃음소리에 고개를 들어 보니, 베어 씨가 나를 보며 조용히 웃고 있고, 티나한테는 자기를 들키지 말라고 손짓을 하고 있는 거야.\n\n'그것 보세요!' 내가 멈추고 거위처럼 멍하니 쳐다보자 그분이 말했어. '당신은 나를 엿보고, 나는 당신을 엿보고, 그것도 나쁘지 않지요. 하지만 보세요, 농담이 아니고 진지하게 묻는 겁니다. 독일어를 배우고 싶은 마음이 있나요?'\n\n'네, 하지만 너무 바쁘시잖아요. 저는 너무 아둔해서 못 배워요.' 나는 작약처럼 얼굴이 새빨개진 채 더듬더듬 말했어.\n\n'프루트! 시간이야 만들면 되고, 우리는 뜻을 찾아내는 데 실패하지 않을 겁니다. 저녁에 내 아주 기쁜 마음으로 짧은 강습을 해 드리지요. 그게 말입니다, 마치 양, 나한텐 갚아야 할 빚이 있으니까요.' 그러면서 내 일감을 가리켰어. '그래요, 그 친절한 부인들은 자기들끼리 이렇게 말하지요. \"저이는 아둔한 늙은이라, 우리가 뭘 하는지 못 볼 거야. 자기 양말 뒤꿈치에 더는 구멍이 안 난다는 걸 결코 알아채지 못할 거고, 떨어진 단추가 새로 돋아난 줄 알 거고, 끈이 저절로 생기는 줄 믿겠지.\" 아! 하지만 나한텐 눈이 있고, 보는 게 많아요. 나한텐 마음이 있고, 이에 대한 고마움을 느끼지요. 자, 그러니 지금 당장 짧은 강습을 시작합시다. 안 그러면 나와 내 식구를 위한 착한 요정의 일도 더는 없을 테니까요.'\n\n그러니 그 뒤로 무슨 말을 할 수 있었겠어. 게다가 정말 멋진 기회라, 약속을 맺고 시작했어. 네 번 강습을 받았는데, 그러다 문법의 수렁에 단단히 빠지고 말았어. 교수는 나한테 무척 인내심을 발휘했지만, 그분한텐 분명 고역이었을 거야. 이따금 나를 그렇게 온화한 절망의 표정으로 바라보는데, 웃어야 할지 울어야 할지 갈팡질팡할 정도였어. 둘 다 해 봤지. 그러다 도저히 못 참겠다는 굴욕과 비탄의 콧소리가 나오는 지경에 이르자, 그분은 문법책을 바닥에 휙 던져 버리고 방을 나가 버렸어. 나는 영영 망신당하고 버림받은 기분이 들었지만, 그분을 조금도 탓하진 않았어. 위층으로 달려가 나 자신을 호되게 흔들어 줄 작정으로 종이들을 주섬주섬 챙기고 있는데, 마치 내가 영광에 휩싸이기라도 한 듯 활기차고 환한 얼굴로 그분이 들어오는 거야.\n\n'이제 새로운 방법을 써 봅시다. 당신과 내가 이 즐겁고 짤막한 메르헨을 함께 읽기로 해요. 우리를 괴롭히는 저 메마른 책은 더 파지 말고, 구석으로 치워 버리는 겁니다.'\n\n어찌나 다정하게 말씀하면서, 한스 안데르센의 동화책을 내 앞에 어찌나 솔깃하게 펼쳐 놓는지, 나는 어느 때보다 부끄러워져서, 죽기 살기 식으로 강습에 덤벼들었어. 그게 그분을 무척 즐겁게 한 것 같더라. 나는 수줍음도 잊고 (이보다 더 적확한 표현은 없을 거야) 죽어라 매달려서, 긴 단어에서 자빠지고, 그때그때 영감 가는 대로 발음하면서, 정말 온 힘을 다했어. 첫 쪽을 다 읽고 숨을 돌리려 멈추자, 그분은 손뼉을 치며 특유의 진심 어린 어투로 외쳤어. '잘했어요! 이제 잘되는군요! 내 차례입니다. 독일어로 읽어 줄 테니, 귀를 기울여 보세요.' 그러고는 우렁찬 목소리로 단어들을 우르릉우르릉 굴려 가며 읽어 내려갔는데, 듣기에도 보기에도 좋은 그 흥겨움이라니. 다행히 그 이야기가 '꿋꿋한 양철 병정'이었거든. 너도 알다시피 우스운 이야기라 나도 웃을 수 있었어. 정말 웃었지. 그분이 읽는 걸 절반도 못 알아들었지만, 어쩔 수가 없었어. 그분은 너무 진지하고, 나는 너무 들떴고, 모든 게 너무 우스꽝스러웠거든.\n\n그 뒤로 우리는 더 잘 진도를 나갔고, 이제 난 강습 내용을 꽤 잘 읽어. 이런 공부 방식이 나한테 딱 맞거든. 보니까 문법이 이야기랑 시 속에 쏙쏙 박혀 들어오더라, 마치 알약을 젤리에 싸서 먹이듯이. 난 그게 무척 좋고, 그분은 아직 싫증을 안 내는 것 같아. 정말 갸륵하지 않니? 크리스마스 때 그분께 뭔가 드리고 싶어. 돈은 차마 못 드리겠으니까. 뭐 좋은 게 없을까, 엄마, 좀 알려 줘요.\n\n로리가 그렇게 행복하고 바쁘게 지내고, 담배도 끊고, 머리도 기르고 있다니 기뻐. 봐, 베스가 나보다 그 앨 더 잘 다루잖아. 질투하는 거 아니야, 동생아. 네 최선을 다해, 다만 그 애를 성인군자로 만들진 마. 사람다운 장난기가 좀 없으면 그 앨 좋아하기 어려울 것 같거든. 그 애한테 내 편지 몇 토막 읽어 줘. 나는 길게 쓸 시간이 없으니, 그걸로도 충분할 거야. 베스가 계속 이렇게 편안하다니, 하느님 감사합니다.\"\n\"1월.\n\n새해 복 많이 받으세요, 사랑하는 우리 식구들 모두. 물론 여기엔 L. 어르신과 테디라는 이름의 청년도 포함이에요. 크리스마스 꾸러미가 얼마나 기뻤는지 이루 다 말할 수가 없어요. 밤이 돼서야 받아서, 이젠 틀렸구나 단념하고 있었거든요. 편지는 아침에 왔는데, 두 분이 소포 얘긴 일언반구도 안 하셔서요. 놀라게 해 주려고 그러셨겠지만, 그래서 좀 서운했어요. 두 분이 절 잊지 않으리라는 '느낌 비슷한 게' 있었거든요. 차를 마신 뒤 방에 앉아 있는데 마음이 좀 가라앉았어요. 그런데 크고, 진흙 묻고, 너덜너덜해진 그 꾸러미가 제게 전해지자, 저는 그냥 그걸 끌어안고 깡충깡충 뛰었어요. 어찌나 정겹고 기운이 나던지, 바닥에 주저앉아 읽고 들여다보고 먹고 웃고 울고, 늘 그러는 우스꽝스러운 식으로 그랬어요. 물건들은 죄다 제가 원하던 거였고, 산 게 아니라 손수 만든 거라 더더욱 좋았어요. 베스의 새 '잉크 받이'는 일품이고, 해나가 준 딱딱한 생강과자 상자는 보물이 될 거예요. 엄마가 보내 주신 멋진 플란넬 옷은 꼭 챙겨 입고, 아빠가 표시해 두신 책들은 꼼꼼히 읽을게요. 모두 정말정말 고마워요!\n\n책 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데, 그 방면으로 제가 부자가 되어 가고 있어요. 새해 첫날에 베어 씨가 멋진 셰익스피어 전집을 주셨거든요. 그분이 무척 아끼는 책이라, 저도 자주 감탄하며 봤어요. 그분의 독일어 성경, 플라톤, 호메로스, 밀턴과 함께 가장 귀한 자리에 모셔져 있던 책이거든요. 그러니 그분이 그걸 내려서, 표지도 떼고, 제 이름이 적힌 걸 보여 줬을 때 제 심정이 어땠을지 상상이 가실 거예요. '내 벗 프리드리히 베어가.'\n\n'당신은 서재를 갖고 싶다는 말을 자주 했지요. 자, 여기 하나 드립니다. 이 표지 사이에는 (그분은 책장을 뜻했어요) 여러 책이 한 권에 담겨 있으니까요. 잘 읽으세요, 그러면 당신에게 큰 도움이 될 겁니다. 이 책에서 사람 됨됨이를 공부하면, 세상 속에서 그것을 읽어 내고 당신의 펜으로 그려 내는 데 도움이 될 테니까요.'\n\n저는 할 수 있는 한 정성껏 감사를 표했고, 이제 책을 백 권이나 가진 양 '제 서재' 얘기를 하고 다녀요. 셰익스피어 안에 그렇게 많은 게 들어 있는 줄 예전엔 미처 몰랐어요. 하긴 그걸 설명해 줄 베어 씨가 없었으니까요. 자, 그분 이름이 끔찍하다고 비웃지 마요. 그건 사람들이 자꾸 그러듯이 '베어(곰)'도 '비어(맥주)'도 아니고, 그 중간쯤 되는, 독일 사람만 낼 수 있는 발음이거든요. 두 분 다 제가 그분에 대해 들려드린 얘기를 마음에 들어 하셔서 기뻐요. 언젠가 그분을 알게 되시면 좋겠어요. 엄마는 그분의 따뜻한 마음을, 아빠는 그분의 지혜로운 머리를 존경하실 거예요. 저는 둘 다 존경하고, 제 새로운 '벗 프리드리히 베어' 덕분에 부자가 된 기분이에요.\n\n돈도 많지 않고 그분이 뭘 좋아할지도 몰라서, 자잘한 것들을 몇 개 마련해서 방 여기저기에 놓아 두었어요. 그분이 뜻밖에 발견하도록요. 쓸모 있거나, 예쁘거나, 우스운 것들이었어요. 탁자에 놓을 새 잉크 스탠드, 꽃을 꽂을 작은 꽃병——그분은 늘 꽃 한 송이를, 아니면 유리잔에 푸른 잎사귀 한 가지를 꽂아 둬요. 그래야 기운이 난다나요——그리고 입김기에 씌울 받침도요. 에이미가 '무슈아르'라고 부르는 것(손수건)을 태워 먹지 않도록요. 그건 베스가 고안한 것처럼 만들었어요. 통통한 몸통에 검고 노란 날개가 달리고, 털실 더듬이에 구슬 눈이 박힌 커다란 나비로요. 그분이 그걸 어찌나 마음에 들어 하던지, 골동품처럼 벽난로 선반에 올려놓으시는 거예요. 그러니 결국 좀 실패한 셈이죠. 그분은 그렇게 가난하면서도, 이 집의 하인이며 아이며 한 사람도 빠뜨리지 않았어요. 그리고 여기 있는 누구 하나, 프랑스 세탁부에서 노턴 양에 이르기까지, 그분을 잊지 않았고요. 저는 그게 무척 기뻤어요.\n\n다들 가장무도회를 열어서 섣달그믐을 흥겹게 보냈어요. 저는 입을 옷이 없어서 내려가지 않으려 했는데, 막판에 커크 부인이 낡은 양단 옷 몇 벌을 떠올리셨고, 노턴 양이 레이스와 깃털을 빌려줘서, 저는 맬러프롭 부인으로 차려입고 가면을 쓴 채 들어섰어요. 목소리를 바꿔서 아무도 절 못 알아봤어요. 말없고 도도한 마치 양이 (대다수가 절 무척 뻣뻣하고 차갑다고 여기거든요. 사실 풋내기들한텐 제가 그렇긴 해요) 춤추고 차려입고 '나일 강둑의 우의(寓意)처럼 묘비명을 멋들어지게 헝클어뜨리며' 신나게 떠들 줄은 아무도 꿈에도 생각 못 했죠. 정말 즐거웠어요. 가면을 벗었을 때 다들 절 빤히 쳐다보는 걸 보는 게 재밌었어요. 젊은 남자 하나가 다른 남자한테 제가 배우였던 게 틀림없다고 하는 걸 들었어요. 실은 어느 군소 극장에서 절 본 기억이 난다나요. 메그가 그 농담을 좋아할 거예요. 베어 씨는 닉 보텀으로 분장했고, 티나는 티타니아였어요. 그분 품에 안긴 완벽한 작은 요정이었죠. 둘이 춤추는 걸 보고 있자니, 테디식 표현을 빌리자면 '한 폭의 풍경화' 같았어요.\n\n이러니저러니 해도 저는 무척 행복한 새해를 맞았어요. 방에서 곰곰이 돌이켜 보니, 숱한 실패에도 불구하고 제가 조금씩 나아가고 있는 것 같은 기분이 들었어요. 요즘은 줄곧 명랑하고, 의욕을 가지고 일하고, 예전보다 다른 사람들한테 더 관심을 갖게 됐거든요. 그게 흐뭇해요. 모두에게 축복이 있기를! 언제나 너희를 사랑하는\n조가.\"\n\n자기를 둘러싼 사교적인 분위기 속에서 무척 행복하고, 자기 밥벌이가 되어 주는, 그리고 그 수고 덕분에 더 달콤해지는 일과로 무척 바빴지만, 조는 그래도 문학 작업을 위한 시간을 냈다. 지금 그녀를 사로잡은 목적은 가난하고 야심 찬 처녀에게는 자연스러운 것이었다. 다만 그 목적을 이루기 위해 택한 수단이 최선은 아니었다. 돈이 권력을 가져다준다는 것을 그녀는 알았다. 그러니 돈과 권력, 그것을 손에 넣기로 그녀는 결심했다. 자기 혼자만을 위해서가 아니라, 자기 자신보다 더 사랑하는 이들을 위해서였다.\n\n집을 안락함으로 채우고, 베스에게 그 애가 바라는 건 무엇이든, 겨울철 딸기부터 침실에 둘 오르간까지 다 마련해 주고, 자신도 외국에 나가 보고, 늘 차고 넘칠 만큼 가져서 자선이라는 호사를 마음껏 누리는 꿈. 그것은 수년간 조가 가장 소중히 품어 온 공중누각이었다.\n\n현상 공모 입선의 경험은, 오랜 여정과 숱한 고된 노력 끝에 이 즐거운 공중누각으로 이어질지도 모를 길 하나를 열어 준 것 같았다. 그러나 그 소설의 참사가 한동안 그녀의 용기를 꺾어 놓았다. 여론이란 그녀의 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 콩나무 줄기에 올라탄, 그녀보다 더 담대한 잭들마저 겁먹게 한 거인이니까. 그 불멸의 영웅처럼, 그녀도 굴러떨어지는 것으로, 그리고 내 기억이 맞다면 거인의 보물 중 가장 보잘것없는 것을 얻는 것으로 끝난 첫 번째 시도 뒤에 한동안 쉬었다. 하지만 \"다시 일어나 또 한 번\"이라는 정신은 잭에게나 조에게나 똑같이 강했다. 그래서 그녀는 다시 기어 올라갔다. 이번엔 그늘진 쪽으로. 그리고 더 많은 노획물을 손에 넣었지만, 돈 자루보다 훨씬 더 소중한 것을 하마터면 뒤에 두고 올 뻔했다.\n\n조는 선정적인 이야기를 쓰기 시작했다. 그 어두운 시대에는 완벽하기 그지없다는 미국조차도 쓰레기 같은 글을 읽었으니까. 그녀는 아무에게도 말하지 않고 \"오싹한 이야기\" 하나를 지어내, 대담하게도 그것을 직접 「위클리 볼케이노」의 편집장인 대시우드 씨에게 들고 갔다. 그녀는 「의상 철학」을 읽어 본 적은 없었지만, 옷차림이 많은 사람들에게 인격의 가치나 매너의 마력보다 더 강력한 영향을 미친다는 여자다운 직감은 있었다. 그래서 가장 좋은 옷으로 차려입고, 흥분하지도 긴장하지도 않았다고 스스로를 설득하려 애쓰면서, 용감하게 어둡고 더러운 계단 두 칸을 올라갔다. 그러자 어수선한 방과, 자욱한 시가 연기와, 모자보다 발뒤꿈치를 한참 더 높이 올린 채 앉아 있는 신사 셋이 나타났다. 그들 중 누구도 그녀가 나타났다고 해서 그 모자라는 의복을 굳이 벗으려 들지 않았다. 이런 환대에 다소 기가 죽어, 조는 문턱에서 머뭇거리며 무척 당황한 채 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 「위클리 볼케이노」 사무실을 찾고 있었는데요. 대시우드 씨를 뵙고 싶어서요.\"\n\n가장 높이 올라가 있던 발뒤꿈치가 내려가고, 가장 연기에 휩싸인 신사가 일어나더니, 시가를 손가락 사이에 조심스레 끼운 채, 고개를 까딱하며 졸음 말고는 아무것도 드러나지 않는 표정으로 다가왔다. 어떻게든 이 일을 해치워야 한다고 느낀 조는 원고를 꺼냈고, 한 문장 한 문장 점점 더 빨갛게 달아오르면서, 이 자리를 위해 공들여 준비한 짤막한 말을 토막토막 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"제 친구가 한 편 제출해 보라고 해서요——이야기 한 편이요——그냥 시험 삼아서요——의견을 듣고 싶다고요——이게 괜찮으면 더 쓰고 싶다고요.\"\n\n그녀가 얼굴을 붉히며 더듬대는 동안, 대시우드 씨는 원고를 받아 들고, 다소 더러운 손가락으로 책장을 넘기면서, 깔끔한 지면을 위아래로 비평하듯 훑어보았다.\n\n\"첫 시도는 아닌 것 같은데요?\" 책장에 번호가 매겨져 있고, 한쪽 면에만 글이 적혀 있으며, 리본으로 묶여 있지 않은 것을 보고 한 말이었다. 그것은 풋내기가 아니라는 확실한 표시였으니까.\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다. 그 친구는 경험이 좀 있고, 「블라니스톤 배너」에 실린 이야기로 상도 탔어요.\"\n\n\"오, 그랬군요.\" 대시우드 씨는 조를 재빨리 한 번 훑어보았다. 보닛에 단 리본부터 장화의 단추까지, 그녀가 걸친 모든 것을 눈여겨보는 듯한 눈길이었다. \"음, 원하시면 두고 가셔도 됩니다. 이런 종류는 지금 우리가 감당 못 할 만큼 쌓여 있긴 한데, 그래도 한번 훑어보고 다음 주에 답을 드리지요.\"\n\n이제 조는 그것을 두고 가고 싶지가 않았다. 대시우드 씨가 영 마음에 들지 않았기 때문이다. 하지만 사정이 그러하니, 고개를 숙여 인사하고 걸어 나오는 것 말고는 달리 할 수 있는 게 없었다. 그녀는 발끈하거나 무안해질 때면 으레 그러듯, 유난히 키가 크고 위엄 있어 보이는 모습으로 그렇게 했다. 마침 그 순간 그녀는 발끈하기도 무안하기도 했다. 신사들이 주고받는 그 알 만하다는 눈길로 보아, \"제 친구\"라는 그녀의 작은 허구가 멋진 농담거리로 여겨졌다는 게 분명했기 때문이다. 그리고 편집장이 들리지 않게 무어라 한 말에 터져 나온 웃음소리가,\n그가 문을 닫는 순간, 그녀의 낭패감은 절정에 달했다. 조는 다시는 돌아오지 않겠다고 반쯤 다짐하며 집으로 돌아갔고, 앞치마를 힘차게 꿰매면서 짜증을 풀어 냈다. 그러다 한두 시간 뒤에는 마음이 충분히 가라앉아 그 장면을 떠올리며 웃을 수 있었고, 다음 주가 기다려지기까지 했다.\n\n다시 찾아갔을 때 대시우드 씨는 혼자였고, 조는 그게 반가웠다. 대시우드 씨는 전보다 훨씬 또렷하게 깨어 있었고, 그것도 기분 좋은 일이었다. 게다가 대시우드 씨는 시가에 너무 깊이 빠져 예의를 잊을 만큼은 아니었다. 그래서 두 번째 만남은 첫 번째보다 훨씬 편안했다.\n\n\"이걸 채택하겠습니다.\" (편집자는 결코 '제가'라고 말하지 않는다.) \"몇 군데 손보는 데 이의가 없으시다면요. 너무 길지만, 제가 표시한 부분을 빼면 딱 적당한 길이가 될 겁니다.\" 그가 사무적인 어조로 말했다.\n\n조는 자기 원고를 거의 알아보지 못할 지경이었다. 종이마다, 문단마다 어찌나 구겨지고 줄이 그어져 있던지. 그러나 새 요람에 맞추기 위해 아기의 다리를 잘라 내라는 요구를 받은 자상한 부모의 심정이 되어 표시된 부분들을 살펴보던 조는, 많은 낭만에 무게중심을 잡아 주려고 정성껏 끼워 넣었던 도덕적 성찰들이 죄다 삭제되어 있는 것을 발견하고 깜짝 놀랐다.\n\n\"하지만 선생님, 저는 모든 이야기에는 어떤 식으로든 교훈이 있어야 한다고 생각해서, 일부러 죄인들 몇몇을 뉘우치게 만들었는데요.\"\n\n대시우드 씨의 편집자다운 근엄함이 누그러지며 미소로 바뀌었다. 조가 자기 \"친구\" 이야기를 깜빡 잊고 오직 작가만이 할 법한 말을 했기 때문이다.\n\n\"사람들은 즐거움을 원하지, 설교를 듣고 싶어 하지 않거든요. 요즘 교훈은 안 팔립니다.\" 덧붙이자면, 이건 그다지 정확한 말은 아니었다.\n\n\"그럼 이렇게 손보면 괜찮겠다는 말씀이신가요?\"\n\n\"네, 새로운 줄거리에다 꽤 잘 다듬어졌습니다. 문장도 좋고요.\" 대시우드 씨의 상냥한 대답이었다.\n\n\"그러면 얼마나, 그러니까 보수는 얼마나—\" 조는 어떻게 말을 꺼내야 할지 정확히 알지 못한 채 입을 열었다.\n\n\"아, 네, 그렇죠. 이런 종류의 글에는 이십오 달러에서 삼십 달러까지 드립니다. 게재될 때 지급하고요.\" 마치 그 부분을 깜빡했다는 듯이 대시우드 씨가 대답했다. 그런 사소한 일은 편집자의 머릿속에서 곧잘 빠져나가곤 한다지 않는가.\n\n\"좋아요, 이걸 가지셔도 돼요.\" 조는 만족스러운 표정으로 원고를 다시 건네며 말했다. 한 단(段)에 일 달러짜리 일을 해 온 처지로서는 이십오 달러도 후한 보수처럼 느껴졌던 것이다.\n\n\"제 친구가 이것보다 더 나은 글을 가지고 있으면, 또 받아 주시겠다고 전해도 될까요?\" 조는 자기도 모르게 말이 헛나온 것을 의식하지 못한 채, 성공에 용기를 얻어 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 보긴 보겠습니다만 채택을 약속할 순 없습니다. 짧고 자극적으로 쓰라고, 교훈은 신경 쓰지 말라고 전하세요. 친구분은 어떤 이름을 붙이고 싶어 하시나요?\" 무심한 어조였다.\n\n\"이름은 아예 안 넣었으면 해요. 제 친구는 자기 이름이 드러나는 걸 원치 않고, 필명도 없거든요.\" 조는 자기도 모르게 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다.\n\n\"물론 본인 뜻대로 하셔야죠. 이 이야기는 다음 주에 나올 겁니다. 돈은 찾으러 오시겠습니까, 아니면 제가 보내 드릴까요?\" 대시우드 씨가 물었다. 새 기고자가 누구일지 알고 싶은 자연스러운 호기심이 일었던 것이다.\n\n\"찾으러 오겠어요. 안녕히 계세요, 선생님.\"\n\n조가 떠나자 대시우드 씨는 발을 책상에 올려놓으며 우아하게 한마디 했다. \"여느 때처럼 가난하고 자존심 세군. 하지만 쓸 만하겠어.\"\n\n대시우드 씨의 지시를 따르고 노스베리 부인을 본보기 삼아, 조는 무모하게도 선정적인 문학의 거품 이는 바다로 풍덩 뛰어들었다. 그러나 한 친구가 던져 준 구명대 덕분에 그녀는 다시 떠올랐고, 물에 빠진 것치고는 그리 큰 해를 입지 않았다.\n\n여느 젊은 글쟁이들처럼 조도 인물과 배경을 외국에서 가져왔다. 그리하여 산적과 백작, 집시와 수녀, 공작 부인이 그녀의 무대에 등장해, 기대할 수 있는 만큼의 정확함과 활기로 제 역할을 해냈다. 그녀의 독자들은 문법이나 구두점, 개연성 따위의 사소한 것에는 까다롭지 않았고, 대시우드 씨는 가장 낮은 값으로 자기 지면을 채우도록 너그러이 허락했다. 그가 이렇게 후한 진짜 이유가, 글품팔이꾼 하나가 더 높은 보수를 제안받고 비열하게도 자기를 곤경에 빠뜨린 채 떠나 버렸기 때문이라는 사실은 굳이 그녀에게 말할 필요가 없다고 여겼다.\n\n조는 곧 자기 일에 흥미를 붙였다. 비쩍 마른 지갑이 두둑해졌고, 다음 여름에 베스를 산으로 데려가려고 모으던 작은 돈도 몇 주가 지나면서 느리지만 확실하게 불어났기 때문이다. 한 가지가 그녀의 만족에 그늘을 드리웠는데, 바로 집에 이 일을 말하지 않았다는 점이었다. 아버지와 어머니가 찬성하지 않으리라는 느낌이 있어서, 일단 제멋대로 한 다음 나중에 용서를 빌고 싶었던 것이다. 비밀을 지키기는 쉬웠다. 글에는 이름이 실리지 않았으니까. 대시우드 씨야 물론 곧 알아챘지만 입을 다물겠다고 약속했고, 놀랍게도 그 약속을 지켰다.\n\n조는 이 일이 자기에게 해가 되지 않으리라 생각했다. 부끄러워할 만한 글은 결코 쓰지 않을 작정이었으니까. 그리고 언젠가 자기가 번 돈을 보여 주며 잘 지켜 온 비밀을 두고 함께 웃을 행복한 순간을 떠올리는 것으로 양심의 가책을 모두 가라앉혔다.\n\n그러나 대시우드 씨는 짜릿한 이야기 외에는 무엇도 받아 주지 않았다. 짜릿함이란 독자의 영혼을 갈가리 헤집어 놓는 것 말고는 만들어 낼 수 없는 것이어서, 역사와 로맨스, 육지와 바다, 과학과 예술, 경찰 기록과 정신병원까지 그 목적을 위해 샅샅이 뒤져야 했다. 조는 곧 자신의 순진한 경험으로는 사회의 밑바닥에 깔린 비극적 세계를 그저 몇 자락밖에 들여다보지 못했음을 깨달았다. 그래서 이를 사업적인 관점으로 바라보며, 특유의 활력으로 부족한 부분을 채우는 일에 착수했다. 이야기 소재를 찾는 데 열심이었고, 솜씨는 빼어나지 못해도 줄거리만은 독창적으로 만들겠다는 일념으로, 그녀는 신문에서 사고와 사건과 범죄를 뒤졌다. 독극물에 관한 책을 찾는 바람에 공공 도서관 사서들의 의심을 사기도 했다. 거리에서 사람들의 얼굴을 살피고, 주변의 선하고 악하고 그저 그런 온갖 인물들을 연구했다. 너무 오래되어 도리어 새것이나 다름없는 사실이나 허구를 찾아 옛 시대의 먼지를 파헤쳤고, 제한된 기회가 허락하는 한 어리석음과 죄악과 비참함에 스스로를 들이밀었다. 그녀는 자기가 잘나가고 있다고 생각했지만, 자기도 모르게 여자의 성품 가운데 가장 여성스러운 자질들을 더럽히기 시작하고 있었다. 그녀는 나쁜 사회 속에서 살고 있었고, 비록 상상 속의 것일망정 그 영향은 그녀에게 미쳤다. 위험하고 실체 없는 양식으로 마음과 공상을 살찌우고 있었으며, 누구에게나 머지않아 찾아오게 마련인 삶의 어두운 면을 너무 일찍 접한 탓에 천성의 순결한 빛을 빠르게 지워 가고 있었던 것이다.\n\n조는 이것을 눈으로 보기보다는 차츰 느끼기 시작했다. 다른 사람들의 격정과 감정을 숱하게 묘사하다 보니, 자기 자신의 그것을 들여다보고 곱씹게 되었던 것이다. 건강한 젊은 정신이라면 자발적으로 빠져들지 않을 병적인 유희였다. 잘못은 언제나 제 나름의 벌을 데려오는 법이고, 조에게 그 벌이 가장 필요했을 때 그것은 어김없이 찾아왔다.\n\n셰익스피어 연구가 인물을 읽어 내는 데 도움을 주었는지, 아니면 정직하고 용감하고 강한 것을 알아보는 여자의 본능적 직감이 그랬는지 나로서는 모르겠다. 다만 상상 속 영웅들에게 세상의 온갖 완벽함을 갖춰 주는 동안, 조는 인간적인 결점이 많은데도 자기 마음을 끄는 살아 있는 영웅 하나를 발견하고 있었다. 바에르 씨는 언젠가 대화 중에, 어디서 마주치든 소박하고 진실하며 사랑스러운 인물들을 연구하라고, 그것이 작가에게 좋은 훈련이 된다고 조에게 조언한 적이 있었다. 조는 그 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들였다. 천연덕스럽게 돌아앉아 그를 연구했던 것이다. 그가 이 사실을 알았다면 무척 놀랐을 행동이었으니, 그 훌륭한 교수는 자기 자신에 대해 매우 겸손했기 때문이다.\n\n처음에 조를 어리둥절하게 한 것은 어째서 모두가 그를 좋아하는가 하는 점이었다. 그는 부자도 거물도 아니었고, 젊지도 잘생기지도 않았다. 흔히 말하는 매혹적이거나 위풍당당하거나 화려한 구석이라곤 어느 모로도 없었다. 그런데도 그는 따뜻한 불처럼 사람을 끌어당겼고, 사람들은 마치 온기 도는 화롯가에 모이듯 자연스레 그의 곁에 모여드는 듯했다. 그는 가난했지만 늘 무언가를 베푸는 사람처럼 보였다. 이방인이었지만 누구나 그의 벗이었다. 더는 젊지 않았으나 소년처럼 마음이 즐거웠다. 수수하고 별난 사람이었지만 많은 이들에게 그 얼굴은 아름다워 보였고, 그의 괴팍함은 그를 봐서 기꺼이 용서받았다. 조는 그 매력의 정체를 알아내려고 종종 그를 지켜보았고, 마침내 그 기적을 일으킨 것은 인자함이라고 결론 내렸다. 그에게 슬픔이 있다 한들 그것은 \"날개 아래 머리를 묻고 잠들어 있었\"고, 그는 오직 햇살 비치는 쪽만을 세상에 내보였다. 그의 이마에는 주름이 잡혀 있었지만, 세월마저도 그가 남들에게 얼마나 친절했는지를 기억하고 부드럽게 어루만지고 지나간 듯했다. 입가의 정다운 곡선들은 숱한 다정한 말과 유쾌한 웃음의 흔적이었다. 그의 눈은 결코 차갑거나 매섭지 않았고, 그 커다란 손은 따뜻하고 힘찬 악수를 건넸으니, 그것은 말보다 더 많은 것을 전했다.\n\n심지어 그의 옷마저 입은 사람의 후덕한 성품을 닮은 듯했다. 옷들은 편안해 보였고, 그를 편하게 해 주는 것을 좋아하는 것 같았다. 넉넉한 조끼는 그 안에 큰 마음이 들어 있음을 짐작게 했고, 빛바랜 코트에는 사람 좋은 분위기가 어려 있었으며, 불룩한 주머니들은 작은 손들이 종종 빈 채로 들어갔다가 가득 채워 나왔음을 분명히 보여 주었다. 그의 장화조차 인자해 보였고, 깃은 남들 것처럼 빳빳하고 까칠하지가 않았다.\n\n\"바로 그거야!\" 마침내 조는 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. 동료 인간을 향한 진심 어린 선의가, 저녁밥을 퍼먹고 양말을 손수 꿰매고 바에르라는 이름까지 짊어진 뚱뚱한 독일인 선생마저 아름답고 기품 있게 만들 수 있다는 사실을 깨닫고서였다.\n\n조는 선함을 무척 귀하게 여겼지만, 지성에 대한 지극히 여성스러운 존경심도 지니고 있었다. 그리고 교수에 관해 알게 된 작은 사실 하나가 그에 대한 존경을 한층 더했다. 그는 결코 자기 이야기를 하지 않았고, 그가 고향 도시에서 학식과 지조로 크게 존경받고 추앙받던 인물이었다는 것을 아무도 몰랐다. 그러던 차에 한 동향 사람이 그를 찾아왔고, 노턴 양과 나눈 대화에서 그 흐뭇한 사실을 털어놓았다. 조는 그녀에게서 그 이야기를 전해 듣고, 바에르 씨가 한 번도 입에 올리지 않았던 까닭에 더욱 좋게 여겼다. 비록 미국에서는 가난한 어학 선생에 불과하지만 베를린에서는 존경받는 교수였다는 사실을 알게 되자 조는 자랑스러웠다. 이 발견이 더해 준 한 줄기 낭만이, 그의 소박하고 고된 삶을 한결 아름답게 빛내 주었다.\n\n지성보다 더 나은 또 다른 선물이 뜻밖의 방식으로 조에게 모습을 드러냈다. 노턴 양은 문학계에 출입할 자격이 있었는데, 그녀가 아니었다면 조로서는 그 세계를 구경할 기회조차 없었을 터였다. 외로운 그 여인은 야심 찬 소녀에게 관심을 가져, 조와 교수 두 사람 모두에게 이런 종류의 호의를 친절히 여러 차례 베풀어 주었다. 어느 날 밤, 그녀는 몇몇 명사들을 기리는 자리로 마련된 특별한 모임에 두 사람을 데려갔다.\n\n조는 멀리서 젊은 열정으로 우러러보던 위대한 인물들 앞에 머리를 조아리고 흠모할 채비를 하고 갔다. 그러나 천재를 향한 그녀의 경외심은 그날 밤 심한 충격을 받았고, 그 위대한 존재들도 결국은 한낱 남자와 여자에 지나지 않더라는 깨달음에서 헤어나기까지는 적잖은 시간이 걸렸다. \"정령과 불과 이슬\"을 먹고 사는 천상의 존재를 떠올리게 하는 시를 짓는 시인에게 수줍은 흠모의 눈길을 슬쩍 던졌다가, 그가 지적인 얼굴을 벌겋게 물들일 만큼 열렬한 기세로 저녁을 게걸스레 먹어 치우는 모습을 보았을 때 그녀가 느낀 낭패감을 상상해 보라. 우상이 무너지기라도 한 듯 그쪽에서 눈을 돌린 조는, 자신의 낭만적 환상을 순식간에 깨뜨리는 다른 사실들도 발견했다. 위대한 소설가는 추(錘)처럼 규칙적으로 두 개의 디캔터 사이를 오갔다. 유명한 신학자는 그 시대의 마담 드 스탈 가운데 하나와 대놓고 시시덕거리고 있었는데, 그 여인은 또 다른 코린을 노려보고 있었다. 그 코린은 상냥하게 그녀를 풍자하던 참이었으니, 심오한 철학자를 차지하려는 책략에서 그녀를 따돌린 직후였다. 정작 그 철학자는 존슨식으로 차를 들이켜며 조는 듯 보였는데, 부인의 수다 때문에 도무지 말을 끼워 넣을 수가 없었던 것이다. 과학계의 명사들은 자기네 연체동물과 빙하기는 잊은 채 예술을 두고 잡담을 나누면서, 특유의 활력으로 굴과 아이스크림에 몰두했다. 제2의 오르페우스처럼 온 도시를 매료시키던 젊은 음악가는 말[馬] 이야기를 늘어놓았고, 그 자리에 있던 영국 귀족의 표본은 공교롭게도 일행 가운데 가장 평범한 인물이었다.\n\n저녁 모임이 절반도 지나기 전에 조는 어찌나 완전히 환멸을 느꼈던지, 마음을 추스르려고 구석에 가 앉았다. 곧 바에르 씨가 자기도 영 어색한 듯한 얼굴로 그녀 곁에 와 앉았고, 이내 철학자 몇몇이 저마다 자기 신조라는 말[馬]에 올라타 그 후미진 자리로 어슬렁어슬렁 다가와 지적인 한판 시합을 벌였다. 대화는 조의 이해를 까마득히 넘어서는 것이었지만, 그녀는 그것을 즐겼다. 칸트와 헤겔이 알 수 없는 신들이고 주관이니 객관이니 하는 것이 알아들을 수 없는 용어이긴 했어도 말이다. 그 모든 것이 끝난 뒤에 \"그녀의 내적 의식에서 진화해 나온\" 유일한 것이라곤 지독한 두통뿐이었다. 세상이 갈가리 해체되었다가, 새롭게, 그리고 떠드는 자들 말로는 전보다 무한히 더 나은 원리에 따라 다시 짜 맞춰지고 있다는 것이 조에게 차츰 분명해졌다. 종교는 따져 묻기로 따져 묻다가 무(無)로 사그라들 판이었고, 지성만이 유일한 신이 될 참이었다. 조는 철학이니 형이상학이니 하는 것에 대해서는 아무것도 몰랐지만, 절반은 즐겁고 절반은 괴로운 묘한 흥분이 그녀를 사로잡았다. 마치 휴일을 맞아 떠오른 어린 풍선처럼, 시간과 공간 속으로 떠밀려 표류하는 듯한 기분으로 귀를 기울이면서였다.\n\n교수가 이 자리를 어떻게 여기는지 보려고 둘러보던 조는, 그가 일찍이 본 적 없는 가장 험상궂은 표정으로 자기를 바라보고 있는 것을 발견했다. 그는 고개를 가로젓고 어서 나가자고 손짓했다. 하지만 그녀는 그때 사변 철학의 거침없음에 매료되어 자리를 지킨 채, 이 현명한 신사들이 옛 신념을 죄다 무너뜨린 다음에는 대체 무엇에 기대려는 것인지 알아내려 애썼다.\n\n그런데 바에르 씨는 수줍은 사람이라 자기 의견을 좀처럼 꺼내 놓지 않았다. 의견이 확고하지 않아서가 아니라, 너무 진실하고 진지해서 가벼이 입에 올릴 수 없었기 때문이다. 철학적 불꽃놀이의 현란함에 홀린 조와 다른 몇몇 젊은이를 번갈아 바라보던 그는 미간을 찌푸렸고, 말을 하고 싶어 안달이 났다. 불붙기 쉬운 어린 영혼 하나가 그 폭죽에 이끌려 길을 잃었다가, 막상 불꽃놀이가 끝나고 나면 손에 남은 거라곤 텅 빈 막대기나 그을린 손바닥뿐임을 알게 될까 봐 두려웠던 것이다.\n\n그는 참을 수 있는 데까지 참았다. 그러나 의견을 묻는 말이 그에게 던져지자, 그는 정직한 분노로 활활 타올라 진리만이 지닌 온갖 웅변으로 종교를 변호했다. 그 웅변은 그의 서툰 영어를 음악처럼 들리게 하고, 그 수수한 얼굴을 아름답게 만들었다. 현명한 자들이 논변에 능했으므로 그것은 힘겨운 싸움이었다. 하지만 그는 자기가 졌다는 것을 알지 못한 채 사내답게 제 깃발을 지켰다. 어찌 된 영문인지, 그가 말하는 동안 세상은 조에게 다시 제자리를 찾았다. 그토록 오래 이어져 온 옛 신념들이 새것보다 나아 보였다. 신은 눈먼 힘이 아니었고, 불멸은 그럴듯한 우화가 아니라 복된 사실이었다. 그녀는 발밑에 다시 단단한 땅이 디뎌지는 듯했다. 바에르 씨가 말문이 막혀 멈추었으나 조금도 설득당하지는 않은 채 말을 그치자, 조는 손뼉을 치며 그에게 고마워하고 싶었다.\n\n그녀는 둘 다 하지 않았다. 그러나 이 장면을 마음에 새겼고, 교수에게 진심 어린 존경을 바쳤다. 그때 그 자리에서 소신을 밝히는 데 그가 적잖은 용기를 냈음을 알았기 때문이다. 양심이 그를 침묵하도록 내버려 두지 않았던 것이다. 그녀는 성품이 돈이나 지위, 지성, 미모보다 더 나은 재산임을 깨닫기 시작했다. 그리고 어느 현자가 정의한 대로 위대함이 \"진리와 경외와 선의\"라면, 자기 벗 프리드리히 바에르는 선할 뿐만 아니라 위대하기도 한 사람이라고 느끼게 되었다.\n\n이 믿음은 나날이 굳어졌다. 그녀는 그의 호감을 소중히 여기고, 그의 존경을 갈망하고, 그의 우정에 걸맞은 사람이 되고 싶었다. 그런데 그 바람이 가장 진실했던 바로 그때, 그녀는 모든 것을 잃을 뻔했다. 모든 일은 종이 모자 하나에서 비롯되었다. 어느 날 저녁 교수가 조에게 수업을 해 주러 왔는데, 머리에 종이로 만든 군인 모자를 쓰고 있었던 것이다. 티나가 씌워 준 것을 그가 벗는 것을 깜빡한 채였다.\n\n'분명 내려오기 전에 거울을 안 보신 거야.' 그가 \"안녕하시오\" 하고 의젓하게 자리에 앉는 것을 보며 조는 미소 띤 채 생각했다. 그는 자기가 읽으려는 주제와 머리에 쓴 것 사이의 우스꽝스러운 대비를 전혀 의식하지 못하고 있었다. 그가 그녀에게 읽어 줄 것은 바로 \"발렌슈타인의 죽음\"이었으니까.\n\n조는 처음에는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 우스운 일이 생기면 터져 나오는 그의 크고 호탕한 웃음소리를 듣는 것을 좋아했던 터라, 스스로 알아채도록 내버려 두었던 것이다. 그러다 이내 그 일을 까맣게 잊었다. 독일인이 실러를 낭독하는 것을 듣는 일이란 꽤나 사람을 몰입시키는 일이었으니까. 낭독이 끝나자 수업이 이어졌고, 그날따라 조의 기분이 들떠 있었던 데다 종이 모자 때문에 눈에 자꾸 장난기 어린 웃음이 어른거려, 수업은 활기가 넘쳤다. 교수는 그녀를 어떻게 이해해야 할지 몰라 결국 멈추고는, 도무지 거부할 수 없는 잔잔한 놀라움이 어린 표정으로 물었다.\n\n\"마치 양, 어찌 선생 면전에서 그리 웃는 거요? 그렇게 못되게 구는 걸 보니 나에 대한 존경심이 없는 게요?\"\n\n\"선생님이 모자 벗는 걸 깜빡하셨는데 어떻게 존경할 수 있겠어요?\" 조가 말했다.\n\n건망증 심한 교수는 손을 머리로 가져가 진지하게 더듬더니, 그 작은 종이 모자를 벗어 잠깐 들여다보고는, 고개를 뒤로 젖히고 흥겨운 베이스 비올처럼 껄껄 웃었다.\n\n\"아! 이제 알겠소. 그 말썽꾸러기 티나가 모자로 나를 바보로 만들었군. 뭐, 별일 아니오. 하지만 두고 보시오, 이 수업이 잘 안 되면 양도 이걸 써야 할 게요.\"\n\n그러나 수업은 몇 분 동안 영 진행되지 못했다. 바에르 씨가 모자에 그려진 그림을 발견하고 그것을 펼치더니, 몹시 역겹다는 듯이 말했기 때문이다.\n\n\"이런 신문이 집 안에 들어오지 않으면 좋겠소. 아이들이 볼 것도, 젊은이들이 읽을 것도 아니오. 좋지 않아요. 이런 해악을 만드는 자들은 도무지 참아 줄 수가 없소.\"\n\n조가 그 종이를 흘끗 보니, 미치광이와 시체, 악당과 독사로 이루어진 보기 좋은 삽화가 그려져 있었다. 그녀는 그것이 마음에 들지 않았다. 하지만 그것을 뒤집게 만든 충동은 불쾌함이 아니라 두려움이었다. 한순간 그 신문이 \"볼케이노\"인 줄 알았던 것이다. 그러나 그것은 아니었고, 설령 그렇다 한들, 그리고 거기에 자기 이야기가 실려 있다 한들, 정체를 드러낼 이름은 적혀 있지 않으리라는 데 생각이 미치자 놀란 마음은 가라앉았다. 그러나 그녀는 표정과 홍조로 이미 스스로 정체를 드러내고 만 셈이었다. 건망증 있는 사람이긴 해도, 교수는 사람들이 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 많은 것을 보고 있었으니까. 그는 조가 글을 쓴다는 것을 알았고, 신문사들이 모여 있는 거리에서 그녀와 한두 번 마주친 적도 있었다. 하지만 그녀가 한 번도 그 이야기를 하지 않았으므로, 그녀의 작품을 보고 싶은 마음이 간절했어도 아무것도 묻지 않았다. 그런데 지금, 그녀가 스스로 인정하기 부끄러워하는 일을 하고 있다는 생각이 들자 그는 마음이 편치 않았다. 그는 많은 사람들이 그러듯 '내가 알 바 아니지, 내겐 뭐라 말할 권리가 없어'라고 자기에게 말하지 않았다. 다만 그녀가 어머니의 사랑과 아버지의 보살핌에서 멀리 떨어진, 젊고 가난한 소녀임을 떠올렸을 뿐이다. 그리하여 그는, 웅덩이에 빠지려는 아기를 구하려고 손을 내밀 때만큼이나 빠르고 자연스러운 충동에 이끌려 그녀를 돕고 싶어졌다. 이 모든 것이 순식간에 그의 머릿속을 스쳤지만, 그 흔적은 얼굴에 조금도 드러나지 않았다. 신문이 뒤집히고 조의 바늘에 실이 꿰일 무렵, 그는 아주 자연스럽게, 그러나 매우 진지하게 말할 채비가 되어 있었다.\n\n\"그래요, 그걸 멀리하는 게 옳소. 착한 처녀들이 그런 걸 봐야 한다고 생각하면 영 마음이 안 좋소. 그런 게 어떤 이들에게는 즐거움이 되겠지만, 나는 이런 못된 쓰레기를 주느니 차라리 내 아이들에게 화약을 쥐여 주고 놀게 하겠소.\"\n\n\"다 나쁜 건 아니고, 그저 시시한 것뿐이에요, 아시겠지만. 게다가 수요가 있는데 그걸 공급하는 게 무슨 해가 되는지 모르겠어요. 점잖은 사람들 중에도 이른바 선정 소설로 정직하게 먹고사는 이가 많거든요.\" 조는 어찌나 힘차게 주름을 잡는지, 핀이 지나간 자리마다 작은 구멍이 줄지어 났다.\n\n\"위스키에도 수요가 있지만, 양이나 나나 그걸 팔고 싶지는 않잖소. 점잖은 사람들이 자기가 끼치는 해악을 안다면, 그걸 정직한 벌이라 여기지 못할 거요. 사탕에 독을 넣어 어린것들이 먹게 할 권리는 없소. 안 되지. 그런 짓을 하느니 잠시 생각을 좀 하고, 차라리 길에서 진흙이나 쓸어야 하오.\"\n\n바에르 씨는 열을 띠고 말하며 신문을 손에 구겨 쥔 채 난로 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 조는 가만히 앉아 있었는데, 마치 불이 자기에게 옮겨붙은 듯한 모습이었다. 종이 모자가 연기로 변해 굴뚝으로 무해하게 사라진 뒤에도 한참 동안 그녀의 두 뺨은 화끈거렸으니까.\n\n\"나머지도 죄다 저놈을 따라 보내 버리면 좋겠소.\" 교수가 후련한 얼굴로 돌아오며 중얼거렸다.\n\n조는 위층에 쌓아 둔 자기 신문 더미가 얼마나 큰 불길을 일으킬까 생각했고, 그 순간 힘겹게 번 돈이 양심을 꽤 무겁게 짓눌렀다. 그러다 스스로를 위로하듯 생각했다. '내 건 저런 게 아냐. 그냥 시시할 뿐이지, 나쁘진 않으니까 걱정할 거 없어.' 그러고는 책을 집어 들고 열심히 공부하려는 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n\"계속할까요, 선생님? 이제 아주 얌전하고 바르게 굴게요.\"\n\n\"그러길 바라오.\" 그가 한 말은 이것뿐이었지만, 그 속에는 그녀가 짐작하는 것보다 더 많은 뜻이 담겨 있었다. 그가 그녀에게 던진 진지하고 다정한 눈빛에, 그녀는 마치 자기 이마에 \"위클리 볼케이노\"라는 글자가 큼직하게 박혀 있는 듯한 기분이 들었다.\n\n방으로 돌아오자마자 그녀는 자기 신문들을 꺼내, 자기가 쓴 이야기를 하나하나 꼼꼼히 다시 읽었다. 약간 근시였던 바에르 씨는 가끔 안경을 썼는데, 조는 한 번 그것을 써 보고는 자기 책의 작은 활자가 어찌나 크게 보이던지 웃은 적이 있었다. 그런데 지금은 교수의 정신적, 혹은 도덕적 안경까지 쓴 것만 같았다. 이 한심한 이야기들의 결점이 끔찍하리만치 또렷이 눈에 들어와 그녀를 낭패감으로 가득 채웠던 것이다.\n\n'이건 정말 쓰레기야. 이대로 계속하다간 곧 쓰레기보다 못한 게 될 거야. 한 편 한 편이 갈수록 더 선정적이잖아. 나는 돈 때문에 눈먼 채로 달려와, 나 자신과 다른 사람들을 해치고 있었어. 그래, 그게 맞아. 이런 걸 멀쩡한 정신으로는 끔찍하게 부끄럽지 않고서는 못 읽겠으니까. 그런데 만약 이게 집에서 발각되거나, 바에르 씨 손에 들어가기라도 하면 난 대체 어떡하지?'\n\n생각만으로도 조는 얼굴이 화끈 달아올랐고, 그 뭉치를 통째로 난로에 쑤셔 넣었다. 불길이 어찌나 거센지 하마터면 굴뚝에 불이 붙을 뻔했다.\n\n'그래, 이런 불붙기 쉬운 헛소리에는 저기가 제격이야. 다른 사람들이 내 화약으로 자폭하게 두느니, 차라리 집을 태워 버리는 게 낫겠지.' 조는 \"쥐라산맥의 악마\"가 이글거리는 눈을 한 작은 검정 잿덩이가 되어 휙 사라지는 것을 지켜보며 생각했다.\n\n그러나 석 달간의 작업이 한 줌의 재 더미와 무릎 위에 놓인 돈 말고는 아무것도 남기지 않았을 때, 조는 바닥에 앉아 자기가 받은 보수를 어찌해야 할지 고민하며 심각한 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"아직은 큰 해를 끼친 것 같진 않으니, 이건 내가 들인 시간값으로 받아 둬도 되겠지.\" 한참을 골똘히 생각한 끝에 그녀는 말하고는, 짜증스럽게 덧붙였다. \"차라리 양심이란 게 없었으면 좋겠어. 어찌나 거추장스러운지. 옳은 일을 하는 데 마음 쓰지 않고, 그른 일을 할 때 불편하지만 않으면, 정말 잘나갈 텐데. 가끔은 아버지하고 어머니가 그런 일에 그렇게까지 까다롭지 않으셨더라면 하는 바람을 떨칠 수가 없다니까.\"\n\n아, 조야, 그런 바람을 품을 게 아니라 \"아버지와 어머니가 까다로우셨던\" 것을 하느님께 감사하렴. 그리고 너를 원칙으로 빙 둘러싸 줄 그런 보호자가 없는 이들을 진심으로 가엾이 여기렴. 그 원칙이 참을성 없는 젊은이에게는 감옥의 벽처럼 느껴질지 몰라도, 여인이 되었을 때 성품을 쌓아 올릴 든든한 주춧돌이 되어 줄 테니 말이다.\n\n조는 더 이상 선정 소설을 쓰지 않았다. 그 돈이 자기가 떠안은 선정성의 몫을 보상해 주지 못한다는 결론을 내렸던 것이다. 그러나 그녀 같은 부류의 사람들이 으레 그렇듯 정반대 극단으로 치달아, 셔우드 부인과 에지워스 양, 해나 모어를 한차례 섭렵했다. 그러고는 차라리 수필이나 설교라고 부르는 편이 더 알맞을 법한 이야기를 한 편 써냈다. 그만큼 지독하게 교훈적이었다. 그녀는 처음부터 그것에 의구심을 품고 있었다. 발랄한 공상과 소녀다운 낭만이 이 새로운 문체 속에서 영 불편해했던 것이다. 마치 그녀 자신이 지난 세기의 뻣뻣하고 거추장스러운 의상을 걸치고 가장무도회에 나선 듯한 기분이었다. 그녀는 이 교훈적인 보석을 여러 곳에 보냈지만 사 줄 사람을 찾지 못했고, 교훈은 안 팔린다던 대시우드 씨의 말에 수긍하는 쪽으로 기울었다.\n\n그다음으로 조는 아동물을 써 보았는데, 그 대가로 더러운 돈을 요구할 만큼 잇속을 따지지만 않았더라면 쉽게 팔 수 있었을 것이다. 아동 문학에 도전해 볼 만한 값을 제시한 유일한 사람은, 온 세상을 자기 특정 신앙으로 개종시키는 것을 사명으로 여기는 어느 점잖은 신사였다. 그러나 아무리 아이들을 위한 글을 쓰고 싶어도, 조는 자기 이야기 속 말썽꾸러기 소년들이 특정 주일학교에 다니지 않는다는 이유로 죄다 곰에게 잡아먹히거나 미친 황소에게 들이받히게 그리고, 거기에 다니는 착한 아이들은 죄다, 혀 짧은 입으로 찬송가나 설교를 읊으며 이 세상을 떠날 때 금박 입힌 생강 과자에서 천사의 호위에 이르기까지 온갖 복락으로 보상받는 식으로 묘사하는 데는 동의할 수 없었다. 그리하여 이 시도들에서도 아무 성과가 없었고, 조는 잉크병 마개를 닫고 아주 건전한 겸손에 사로잡혀 말했다.\n\n\"난 아무것도 몰라. 제대로 알게 될 때까지 기다렸다가 다시 시도하겠어. 그동안에는 더 나은 걸 할 수 없다면 '길에서 진흙이나 쓸겠어.' 그건 적어도 정직한 일이니까.\" 이 결심은 콩나무에서 두 번째로 굴러떨어진 것이 그녀에게 어느 정도 약이 되었음을 보여 주었다.\n\n이런 내적인 변혁이 일어나는 동안, 그녀의 겉으로 드러난 삶은 여느 때처럼 바쁘면서도 별일 없이 흘러갔다. 가끔 그녀가 심각해 보이거나 조금 슬퍼 보일 때도, 그것을 알아챈 사람은 바에르 교수밖에 없었다. 그가 어찌나 조용히 살폈던지, 조는 그가 자기 충고를 받아들여 도움이 될지 지켜보고 있다는 것을 전혀 몰랐다. 하지만 그녀는 그 시험을 견뎌 냈고, 그는 만족했다. 두 사람 사이에 오간 말은 없었어도, 그녀가 글쓰기를 그만두었음을 그는 알았기 때문이다. 그녀의 오른손 둘째손가락에 더 이상 잉크가 묻어 있지 않다는 사실로도 짐작했지만, 그뿐이 아니었다. 그녀는 이제 저녁 시간을 아래층에서 보냈고, 신문사들 사이에서 더는 마주치지 않았으며, 끈질긴 인내로 공부에 매달렸다. 그것은 그녀가, 비록 즐겁지는 않더라도 무언가 유익한 일에 마음을 쏟기로 작정했음을 그에게 확신시켜 주었다.\n\n그는 여러 모로 그녀를 도우며 자신이 참된 벗임을 증명했고, 조는 행복했다. 펜을 놀리지 않는 동안 그녀는 독일어 말고도 다른 가르침을 배우고 있었고, 자기 인생이라는 한 편의 선정 소설의 토대를 놓고 있었으니까.\n\n즐겁고도 긴 겨울이었다. 그녀가 커크 부인의 집을 떠난 것은 6월이 되어서였다. 떠날 때가 오자 모두가 서운해하는 듯했다. 아이들은 위로받을 길이 없었고, 바에르 씨의 머리카락은 온통 곤두서 있었다. 그는 마음이 심란할 때면 늘 머리를 마구 헝클어뜨렸기 때문이다.\n\n\"집에 가신다고요? 아, 양은 돌아갈 집이 있으니 행복한 사람이오.\" 그녀가 떠난다고 말하자 그가 한 말이었다. 그러고는 마지막 날 저녁 그녀가 작은 송별 모임을 여는 동안, 그는 구석에 말없이 앉아 수염만 잡아당겼다.\n\n그녀는 아침 일찍 떠날 예정이라 밤사이에 모두에게 작별 인사를 했다. 그의 차례가 되자 그녀는 따뜻하게 말했다.\n\n\"자, 선생님, 혹시라도 저희 동네 쪽으로 여행하시게 되면 꼭 들러 주실 거죠? 안 그러시면 절대 용서하지 않을 거예요. 우리 식구들 모두에게 제 친구를 소개하고 싶거든요.\"\n\n\"그러고 싶소? 내가 가도 되겠소?\" 그가 그녀로서는 미처 보지 못한 간절한 표정으로 그녀를 내려다보며 물었다.\n\n\"네, 다음 달에 오세요. 그때 로리가 졸업하는데, 졸업식이 새로운 구경거리가 돼서 즐거우실 거예요.\"\n\n\"양이 말하는 그 사람이 가장 친한 친구요?\" 그가 달라진 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"네, 제 친구 테디예요. 저는 그 애가 무척 자랑스럽고, 선생님께도 보여 드리고 싶어요.\"\n\n그제야 조는 고개를 들었는데, 두 사람을 서로에게 소개할 생각에 들뜬 것 말고는 아무것도 의식하지 못한 채였다. 그런데 바에르 씨의 얼굴에 어린 무언가가 문득, 자기가 로리를 한낱 \"가장 친한 친구\" 이상으로 여기게 될지도 모른다는 사실을 떠올리게 했다. 그러자 단지 무슨 일이라도 있는 것처럼 보이고 싶지 않다는 그 마음 때문에, 그녀는 자기도 모르게 얼굴을 붉히기 시작했다. 그리고 붉히지 않으려 애쓸수록 더욱 빨개졌다. 무릎 위에 티나가 없었더라면 어떻게 됐을지 그녀 자신도 알 수 없었다. 다행히 아이가 그녀를 와락 끌어안았고, 덕분에 그녀는 잠깐 얼굴을 숨기며 교수가 못 봤기를 바랄 수 있었다. 하지만 그는 보았고, 그의 얼굴은 그 한순간의 불안에서 다시 평소의 표정으로 돌아왔다. 그러고는 다정하게 말했다.\n\n\"그때 시간을 낼 수 있을 것 같진 않소만, 그 친구가 크게 성공하고 양들 모두가 행복하기를 바라오. 신의 가호가 있기를!\" 그렇게 말하며 그는 따뜻하게 악수를 하고 티나를 어깨에 들쳐 업은 채 떠났다.\n\n그러나 아이들이 잠자리에 든 뒤, 그는 얼굴에 지친 빛을 띤 채 오래도록 난롯불 앞에 앉아 있었고, 가슴속에는 \"하임베\", 곧 향수(鄕愁)가 무겁게 자리하고 있었다. 한 번은 어린아이를 무릎에 앉히고 얼굴에 그 새로운 부드러움을 띠고 있던 조를 떠올리며 잠시 두 손에 머리를 묻었다가, 무언가 찾을 수 없는 것을 찾기라도 하듯 방 안을 서성거렸다.\n\n\"이건 내 것이 아니야. 지금은 바라서는 안 돼.\" 그는 거의 신음에 가까운 한숨을 내쉬며 혼잣말을 했다. 그러고는 억누를 수 없는 그 갈망을 자책이라도 하듯, 베개 위 헝클어진 두 머리에 입을 맞추고, 좀처럼 쓰지 않던 해포석 파이프를 내려 들고는 플라톤을 펼쳤다.\n\n그는 최선을 다했고, 또 사내답게 해냈다. 하지만 펄펄 뛰는 두 사내아이와 파이프 한 대, 심지어 신성한 플라톤조차 아내와 자식과 가정을 대신해 줄 만족스러운 대용품이 되지는 못했으리라.\n\n이른 시각이었지만 이튿날 아침 그는 조를 배웅하러 역에 나와 있었다. 그 덕분에 그녀는 작별의 미소를 띤 낯익은 얼굴의 흐뭇한 기억과, 길동무가 되어 줄 제비꽃 한 다발과, 무엇보다도 행복한 생각 하나를 품은 채 홀로 길을 떠날 수 있었다.\n\n\"그래, 겨울은 지나갔어. 책을 쓰지도, 재물을 벌지도 못했지. 하지만 사귈 만한 가치가 있는 벗을 하나 얻었으니, 평생 그를 잃지 않도록 애써야지.\"\n35장\n\n가슴앓이\n동기가 무엇이었든 간에, 로리는 그해 어느 정도 보람 있게 공부했다. 우등으로 졸업하면서 라틴어 연설을 했는데, 친구들 말로는 필립스 같은 우아함과 데모스테네스 같은 웅변을 갖추었다고 했다. 식구들 모두가 그 자리에 있었다. 할아버지는 — 아, 어찌나 자랑스러워하시던지! — 그리고 마치 부부, 존과 메그, 조와 베스가 다 함께 그를 진심으로 흠모하며 기뻐했다. 그것은 소년들이 그 당시에는 대수롭지 않게 여기지만, 훗날 어떤 승리로도 세상에서 얻어 내지 못하는 그런 흠모였다.\n\n\"이 빌어먹을 만찬 때문에 남아 있어야 하지만, 내일은 일찍 집에 갈 거야. 너희들, 여느 때처럼 마중 나와 줄 거지?\" 그날의 즐거움이 끝난 뒤 자매들을 마차에 태우며 로리가 말했다.\n그는 \"너희들\"이라고 했지만, 사실은 조를 두고 한 말이었다. 옛 관습을 지키는 사람은 조밖에 없었으니까. 조는 자기의 멋지고 성공한 소년에게 무엇 하나 거절할 마음이 없어서, 따뜻하게 대답했다.\n\n\"갈게, 테디. 비가 오나 해가 나나 갈 거야. 그리고 입금(口琴)으로 '보아라, 정복의 용사 오신다'를 연주하면서 네 앞에서 행진해 줄게.\"\n\n로리는 어떤 눈빛으로 그녀에게 고마움을 표했는데, 그 눈빛에 조는 문득 겁이 나서 이런 생각이 들었다. '아, 어떡하지! 분명 무슨 말을 할 텐데, 그러면 난 어쩌지?'\n\n저녁의 사색과 아침의 일이 그녀의 두려움을 어느 정도 가라앉혀 주었다. 자기가 어떤 대답을 할지 충분히 짐작할 만한 빌미를 다 주어 놓고도 상대가 청혼할 거라고 여길 만큼 자만하지는 않겠다고 마음먹은 그녀는, 약속한 시간에 길을 나섰다. 테디가 그 가엾고 여린 마음에 상처를 줄 만한 짓을 하지 않기를 바라면서였다. 메그의 집에 들러 데이지와 데미존을 보며 기운을 차리고 한숨 돌리자, 그 단둘의 만남을 앞두고 한층 더 마음이 다잡아졌다. 하지만 저 멀리 건장한 형체가 불쑥 나타나는 것을 보자, 돌아서서 달아나고 싶은 강한 충동이 일었다.\n\n\"입금은 어디 있어, 조?\" 말소리가 닿을 만한 거리에 이르자마자 로리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"깜빡했어.\" 조는 다시 마음을 놓았다. 그런 인사라면 연인다운 것이라고는 할 수 없었으니까.\n\n평소 이럴 때면 그녀는 늘 그의 팔짱을 끼곤 했다. 그런데 이번에는 그러지 않았고, 그는 불평하지 않았다. 그것이 나쁜 징조였다. 그는 집으로 향하는 작은 오솔길로 들어서서 작은 숲을 지날 때까지, 갖가지 동떨어진 화제를 빠르게 늘어놓기만 했다. 그러다 숲에 접어들자 그는 걸음이 느려지더니, 갑자기 그 유창하던 말솜씨를 잃어버렸고, 이따금 끔찍한 침묵이 흘렀다. 자꾸만 빠져드는 그 침묵의 우물 가운데 하나에서 대화를 건져 올리려고 조가 황급히 말했다.\n\n\"이제 너 푹 쉬는 긴 휴가를 보내야겠다!\"\n\n\"그럴 생각이야.\"\n\n그의 단호한 어조에서 무언가가 느껴져 조는 얼른 고개를 들었고, 그가 두려워하던 순간이 왔음을 알려 주는 표정으로 자기를 내려다보고 있는 것을 발견했다. 그녀는 애원하듯 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"안 돼, 테디, 제발 그러지 마!\"\n\n\"할 거야, 너는 내 말을 들어야 해. 소용없어, 조. 우린 이 문제를 매듭지어야 하고, 빠를수록 우리 둘 다에게 좋아.\" 그가 갑자기 얼굴이 붉어지고 흥분해서 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 하고 싶은 말을 해. 들을게.\" 조가 자포자기한 듯한 인내심으로 말했다.\n\n로리는 젊은 연인이었지만 진심이었고, 그러다 죽는 한이 있어도 \"매듭짓겠다\"고 작정했다. 그래서 그는 특유의 격정으로 본론에 뛰어들어, 침착하게 유지하려는 사내다운 노력에도 불구하고 자꾸만 목이 메는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"너를 안 뒤로 줄곧 사랑해 왔어, 조. 어쩔 수가 없었어. 네가 나한테 너무 잘해 줬으니까. 그걸 보여 주려고 애썼지만 넌 받아 주지 않았지. 이제 난 너한테 기어이 듣게 하고, 대답을 받아 낼 거야. 더는 이렇게 못 견디겠으니까.\"\n\n\"난 너한테 이런 일을 겪게 하고 싶지 않았어. 네가 알아주리라 생각했는데—\" 조가 입을 열었지만, 생각보다 훨씬 더 힘든 일이었다.\n\n\"알아, 그랬다는 거. 하지만 여자들은 어찌나 알 수 없는지 무슨 뜻인지 도무지 모르겠어. '아니'라고 해 놓고 '응'이라는 뜻이고, 그냥 재미로 남자를 미치게 하잖아.\" 로리가 부정할 수 없는 사실 뒤에 몸을 숨기며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"난 안 그래. 난 네가 나를 그렇게 좋아하게 만들고 싶었던 적이 없어. 그래서 할 수 있다면 막아 보려고 멀리 떠났던 거야.\"\n\n\"나도 그렇게 생각했어. 너다운 일이었지. 하지만 소용없었어. 난 오히려 너를 더 사랑하게 됐을 뿐이야. 네 마음에 들려고 무던히 애쓰고, 당구도 끊고, 네가 싫어하는 건 다 그만뒀어. 그리고 기다리면서 한 번도 불평하지 않았어. 내가 절반도 좋은 사람은 못 되지만, 그래도 네가 날 사랑해 주기를 바랐으니까—\" 여기서 도저히 억누를 수 없는 울먹임이 새어 나오자, 그는 \"빌어먹을 목\"을 가다듬는 척하며 미나리아재비 꽃 모가지를 똑똑 꺾었다.\n\n\"넌 충분히 좋은 사람이야. 오히려 나한테는 과분하지. 난 너한테 정말 고맙고, 또 네가 너무 자랑스럽고 좋아. 그런데 왜 네가 바라는 대로 너를 사랑할 수 없는지 모르겠어. 애써 봤지만, 이 감정을 바꿀 수가 없어. 사랑하지 않으면서 사랑한다고 말하는 건 거짓말일 거야.\"\n\n\"정말, 진심으로 그래, 조?\"\n\n그는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 그녀의 두 손을 붙잡고, 그녀가 좀처럼 잊지 못할 눈빛으로 물었다.\n\n\"정말, 진심으로 그래.\"\n\n이제 두 사람은 작은 숲속, 울타리 층계 바로 옆에 있었다. 마지막 말이 조의 입술에서 마지못해 떨어지자, 로리는 그녀의 손을 놓고 계속 걸어가려는 듯 돌아섰다. 하지만 평생 처음으로 그 울타리가 그에게는 너무 버거웠다. 그래서 그는 이끼 낀 기둥에 그저 머리를 기댄 채, 조가 겁이 날 만큼 꼼짝도 않고 서 있었다.\n\n\"아, 테디, 정말 미안해. 너무너무 미안해서, 그게 도움이 된다면 차라리 나를 죽이고 싶을 정도야! 네가 이걸 그렇게 힘들어하지 않으면 좋겠어. 나도 어쩔 수가 없어. 사람이 사랑하지 않는 사람을 억지로 사랑하게 만드는 건 불가능하다는 거, 너도 알잖아.\" 조가 격식도 없이, 하지만 가책에 차서 외쳤다. 그러면서 오래전 그가 자기를 그토록 위로해 주던 때를 떠올리며 그의 어깨를 가만히 토닥였다.\n\n\"가끔은 그렇게 되기도 해.\" 기둥 쪽에서 먹먹한 목소리가 들려왔다.\n\n\"그건 올바른 종류의 사랑이 아닐 거고, 난 그런 건 시도하고 싶지 않아.\" 단호한 대답이었다.\n\n긴 침묵이 흘렀다. 강가 버드나무 위에서 검은새 한 마리가 흥겹게 노래했고, 키 큰 풀이 바람에 사각거렸다. 이윽고 조가 울타리 층계에 앉으며 아주 차분하게 말했다.\n\n\"로리, 너한테 할 말이 있어.\"\n\n그는 총에라도 맞은 듯 흠칫하더니 고개를 번쩍 들고 사나운 어조로 외쳤다.\n\n\"그 말은 하지 마, 조. 지금은 도저히 못 견뎌!\"\n\n\"무슨 말?\" 그의 격렬함에 의아해하며 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"네가 그 늙은이를 사랑한다는 말.\"\n\n\"무슨 늙은이?\" 조가 따져 물었다. 그가 분명 자기 할아버지를 두고 하는 말이라고 생각하면서.\n\n\"네가 늘 편지에 쓰던 그 빌어먹을 교수 말이야. 네가 그자를 사랑한다고 하면, 난 분명 무슨 끔찍한 짓을 저지를 거야.\" 그가 두 손을 불끈 쥐고 눈에 노여운 불꽃을 튀기며, 정말 그 말대로 할 것 같은 표정을 지었다.\n\n조는 웃음이 터질 뻔했지만 꾹 참고, 자기도 이 모든 일에 흥분이 일던 터라 따뜻하게 말했다.\n\n\"욕하지 마, 테디! 그분은 늙지도 않았고, 나쁜 구석이라곤 하나도 없어. 선하고 친절하시고, 너 다음으로 내가 가진 가장 좋은 친구야. 제발, 이렇게 발끈하지 마. 난 너한테 다정하게 굴고 싶은데, 네가 우리 교수님을 모욕하면 분명 화가 날 것 같아. 난 그분이든 다른 누구든 사랑할 생각이 눈곱만큼도 없어.\"\n\n\"하지만 언젠가는 그렇게 될 거고, 그럼 난 어떻게 되는 거야?\"\n\n\"넌 분별 있는 소년답게 다른 누군가를 사랑하게 될 거고, 이 모든 괴로움은 다 잊어버릴 거야.\"\n\n\"난 다른 누구도 사랑할 수 없어. 그리고 너를 절대 잊지 않을 거야, 조, 절대로! 절대로!\" 그가 격정에 찬 말에 힘을 싣느라 발을 쾅 굴렀다.\n\n'얘를 어쩌면 좋아?' 조는 감정이라는 게 생각보다 훨씬 다루기 힘든 것임을 깨달으며 한숨지었다. \"넌 내가 하려던 말을 아직 안 들었잖아. 앉아서 들어 봐. 난 정말로 옳은 일을 하고 너를 행복하게 해 주고 싶거든.\" 그녀는 약간의 이치로 그를 달래 보려 했지만, 그것은 그녀가 사랑에 대해 아무것도 모른다는 증거였다.\n\n그 마지막 말에서 한 줄기 희망의 빛을 본 로리는 그녀의 발치 풀밭에 털썩 몸을 던지고, 울타리 아래 층계에 팔을 기댄 채 기대에 찬 얼굴로 그녀를 올려다보았다. 그런데 그런 자세는 조가 차분하게 말하거나 또렷하게 생각하는 데 전혀 도움이 되지 않았다. 사랑과 갈망으로 가득한 눈으로, 그녀의 모진 마음이 짜낸 쓰디쓴 눈물 한두 방울로 아직 속눈썹이 젖은 채 자기를 바라보는 소년에게, 어떻게 매정한 말을 할 수 있단 말인가? 그녀는 그를 위해 기르도록 내버려 둔 그 물결치는 머리카락을 쓰다듬으며 — 그게 얼마나 가슴 뭉클한 일이었던가! — 그의 고개를 부드럽게 돌려놓고 말했다.\n\n\"엄마 말씀이 맞아. 너랑 나는 서로 어울리지 않아. 우리 둘 다 성미가 급하고 고집이 세서, 만약 어리석게도 그런 짓을 했다간 아마 아주 비참해질 테니까—\" 조는 마지막 말 앞에서 잠시 머뭇거렸지만, 로리가 황홀한 표정으로 그 말을 내뱉었다.\n\n\"결혼 말이지. 아니, 우린 비참해지지 않을 거야! 네가 날 사랑한다면, 난 완벽한 성자가 될 거야. 넌 날 네가 원하는 대로 뭐든 만들 수 있으니까.\"\n\n\"아니, 난 못 해. 해 봤지만 실패했어. 그런 중대한 실험으로 우리 행복을 걸 순 없어. 우린 서로 안 맞고, 앞으로도 절대 그럴 거야. 그러니 평생 좋은 친구로 지내되, 경솔한 짓은 하지 말자.\"\n\n\"기회만 생기면 우린 할 거야.\" 로리가 반항하듯 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"제발 이성적으로 생각하고, 이 일을 분별 있게 봐 줘.\" 조가 거의 어찌할 바를 모르며 간청했다.\n\n\"난 이성적으로 안 굴 거야. 네가 말하는 그 '분별 있는 관점'이란 것도 갖고 싶지 않아. 그건 나한테 아무 도움도 안 되고, 너만 더 모질게 만들 뿐이야. 난 너한테 마음이란 게 있긴 한지 모르겠어.\"\n\n\"나도 없었으면 좋겠어!\"\n\n조의 목소리가 살짝 떨렸고, 그것을 좋은 징조로 여긴 로리는 돌아앉아 온갖 설득력을 동원해, 일찍이 그토록 위험할 만큼 달콤했던 적 없는 달래는 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"우리를 실망시키지 마, 조! 다들 그렇게 되길 바라고 있어. 할아버지도 그 일에 마음을 두고 계시고, 네 식구들도 좋아하고, 난 너 없이는 살 수가 없어. 그러겠다고 말해 줘, 우리 행복해지자. 제발, 제발!\"\n\n자기는 자기 소년을 사랑하지 않으며 앞으로도 결코 사랑할 수 없다고 결론 내렸을 때 다진 그 결심을, 어떻게 그토록 굳건히 지킬 정신력이 있었는지 조는 여러 달이 지나서야 비로소 이해할 수 있었다. 무척 힘든 일이었지만 그녀는 해냈다. 머뭇거려 봤자 소용도 없고 잔인하기만 하다는 것을 알았기 때문이다.\n\n\"난 진심으로 '응'이라고 말할 수 없으니까, 아예 말하지 않을래. 머잖아 내가 옳았다는 걸 알게 될 거고, 오히려 나한테 고마워하게 될 거야—\" 그녀가 엄숙하게 입을 열었다.\n\n\"내가 그러면 목을 매달겠어!\" 로리가 생각만으로도 분에 차 풀밭에서 벌떡 튀어 일어났다.\n\n\"아니, 그렇게 될 거야!\" 조가 고집스레 말을 이었다. \"넌 머잖아 이걸 다 극복하고, 사랑스럽고 교양 있는 아가씨를 만날 거야. 너를 흠모하고, 네 멋진 집의 훌륭한 안주인이 되어 줄 그런 사람을. 난 그렇게 못 해. 난 못생기고 어설프고 별나고 나이 들었어. 너는 나를 부끄러워하게 될 거고, 우린 싸우게 될 거야. 봐, 지금도 어쩔 수 없잖아. 게다가 난 우아한 사교계를 싫어하지만 넌 좋아하고, 넌 내 끄적거리는 글을 질색할 텐데 난 그게 없으면 못 살아. 그러니 우린 불행해지고, 괜히 그런 짓을 했다고 후회하게 될 거고, 모든 게 끔찍해질 거야!\"\n\n\"더 할 말 있어?\" 로리가 이 예언 같은 쏟아냄을 참을성 있게 듣기 힘들어하며 물었다.\n\n\"없어. 다만, 난 평생 결혼하지 않을 것 같아. 난 지금 이대로 행복하고, 내 자유를 너무 사랑해서, 어떤 남자를 위해서든 그걸 서둘러 포기할 마음이 없거든.\"\n\n\"난 잘 알아!\" 로리가 끼어들었다. \"지금은 그렇게 생각하지. 하지만 언젠가 네가 누군가를 정말로 마음에 두게 될 때가 올 거고, 그땐 그 사람을 엄청나게 사랑하고, 그 사람을 위해 살고 죽으려 할 거야. 분명 그럴 거야, 그게 너다운 거니까. 그리고 난 옆에 서서 그걸 지켜봐야겠지.\" 절망에 빠진 연인은 모자를 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳤는데, 그 얼굴이 그토록 비극적이지만 않았다면 우스꽝스러워 보였을 몸짓이었다.\n\n\"그래, 난 그 사람을 위해 살고 죽을 거야. 만약 그런 사람이 나타나서, 나 자신도 어쩔 수 없이 그를 사랑하게 만든다면 말이야. 그러니 넌 네가 할 수 있는 최선을 다해야 해!\" 조가 가엾은 테디에게 인내심을 잃고 외쳤다. \"난 최선을 다했는데도 넌 도무지 이성적으로 굴지를 않아. 내가 줄 수 없는 걸 자꾸 졸라 대는 건 이기적인 짓이야. 난 친구로서 너를 늘 좋아할 거야, 정말 무척이나. 하지만 너랑은 절대 결혼하지 않아. 그러니 빨리 그걸 믿을수록 우리 둘 다에게 좋아. 자, 됐지!\"\n\n그 말은 화약에 불을 댕긴 것과 같았다. 로리는 잠시 어쩔 줄 모르겠다는 듯이 그녀를 바라보다가, 홱 돌아서며 자포자기한 듯한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"언젠간 후회할 거야, 조.\"\n\n\"아니, 어디 가는 거야?\" 그의 얼굴이 그녀를 겁먹게 했기에 그녀가 외쳤다.\n\n\"악마한테나!\" 그것이 위로가 되는 대답이었다.\n\n순간 조의 심장이 멎는 듯했다. 그가 강 쪽으로 둑을 미끄러져 내려갔던 것이다. 하지만 젊은이를 끔찍한 죽음으로 몰아넣으려면 어지간한 어리석음이나 죄악, 혹은 비참함이 있어야 하는 법이고, 로리는 한 번의 실패에 무너질 만큼 나약한 부류가 아니었다. 그에게는 극적으로 강물에 뛰어들겠다는 생각 따위는 없었다. 다만 어떤 맹목적인 본능에 이끌려 모자와 코트를 자기 보트에 내던지고, 있는 힘껏 노를 저어 멀어져 갔다. 어느 시합 때보다도 빠른 속도로 강을 거슬러 올라가면서. 조는 긴 숨을 내쉬고 맞잡았던 두 손을 풀었다. 그 가엾은 친구가 가슴에 품은 괴로움을 떼어 놓으려 애쓰는 모습을 지켜보면서였다.\n\n\"저렇게 하면 좀 나아질 거야. 그리고 집에 돌아올 때쯤이면 어찌나 다정하고 뉘우치는 마음일지, 난 차마 그 애를 못 볼 거야.\" 그녀는 그렇게 말하고는, 마치 죄 없는 무언가를 죽여 나뭇잎 아래 묻어 버린 듯한 기분으로 천천히 집을 향해 걸으며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"이제 가서 로렌스 할아버지께, 우리 가엾은 소년한테 아주 다정하게 대해 달라고 미리 말씀드려야겠어. 할아버지가 베스를 사랑해 주시면 좋겠는데. 어쩌면 시간이 지나면 그러실지도 몰라. 하지만 내가 그 애에 대해 잘못 생각했던 것 같아. 아, 정말! 여자애들은 어떻게 연인을 두고서 또 거절하기를 좋아할 수가 있을까. 정말 끔찍한 일이야.\"\n\n이 일은 자기만큼 잘해 낼 사람이 없으리라 확신한 조는 곧장 로렌스 씨에게 가서, 그 괴로운 이야기를 용감하게 끝까지 전했다. 그러고는 무너져 내려, 자신의 무정함을 두고 어찌나 서럽게 울던지, 그 인자한 노신사는 몹시 실망스러우면서도 책망 한마디 입에 올리지 않았다. 그는 어떤 처녀가 로리를 사랑하지 않을 수 있는지 도무지 이해할 수가 없었고, 그녀가 마음을 바꾸기를 바랐다. 하지만 사랑이란 억지로 되는 것이 아님을 그는 조보다도 더 잘 알았기에, 슬프게 고개를 가로저으며 자기 소년을 위험에서 멀리 데리고 가기로 결심했다. 그 혈기 왕성한 젊은이가 조에게 남긴 작별의 말이, 그가 인정하려는 것 이상으로 그를 불안하게 했던 것이다.\n\n로리가 녹초가 되었지만 꽤 차분해진 모습으로 집에 돌아오자, 할아버지는 아무것도 모르는 척 그를 맞이했고, 한두 시간 동안 그 시치미를 아주 그럴듯하게 유지했다. 그러나 두 사람이 그토록 즐기곤 하던 황혼 녘에 함께 앉아 있자니, 늙은이로서는 여느 때처럼 이런저런 이야기를 늘어놓기가 힘들었고, 젊은이로서는 지난해의 성공에 대한 칭찬을 듣기가 더더욱 괴로웠다. 그에게 그것은 이제 사랑의 헛수고처럼 여겨졌으니까. 그는 참을 수 있는 데까지 참다가, 피아노로 가서 연주를 시작했다. 창문은 열려 있었다. 베스와 함께 정원을 거닐던 조는 이번만은 동생보다 음악을 더 잘 이해했다. 그가 \"비창 소나타\"를, 그것도 일찍이 친 적 없는 연주로 치고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"썩 훌륭하구나. 그런데 사람을 울리고도 남을 만큼 구슬프네. 좀 더 흥겨운 걸 들려주렴, 얘야.\" 로렌스 씨가 말했다. 그 인자한 노인의 마음은 보여 주고 싶지만 어떻게 표현해야 할지 모르는 연민으로 가득했다.\n\n로리는 좀 더 활기찬 가락으로 옮겨 가, 몇 분간 거세게 연주했다. 잠시 소리가 잦아드는 틈에 마치 부인의 목소리가 들려오지만 않았더라면, 그는 용케 끝까지 잘 쳐 냈을 것이다.\n\n\"조야, 들어오너라. 너한테 볼일이 있구나.\"\n\n로리가 다른 의미로 그토록 하고 싶었던 바로 그 말이었다! 그 소리를 듣다가 그는 박자를 놓쳤다. 음악은 깨진 화음으로 끝났고, 연주자는 어둠 속에 말없이 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"이건 못 견디겠구나.\" 노신사가 중얼거렸다. 그는 자리에서 일어나 더듬더듬 피아노로 다가가, 그 넓은 두 어깨에 다정한 손을 얹고는 여인처럼 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"안다, 얘야. 다 안다.\"\n\n한순간 아무 대답이 없었다. 그러다 로리가 날카롭게 물었다.\n\n\"누가 그러셨어요?\"\n\n\"조가 직접 그러더구나.\"\n\n\"그럼 다 끝난 거네요!\" 그는 짜증스러운 몸짓으로 할아버지의 손을 뿌리쳤다. 그 연민이 고맙긴 했어도, 사내의 자존심으로는 사내의 동정을 견딜 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n\"아주 끝난 건 아니다. 한마디만 하마. 그러고 나면 이걸로 끝내자꾸나.\" 로렌스 씨가 보기 드물게 부드러운 어조로 대답했다. \"지금은 집에 있고 싶지 않을 테지, 아마?\"\n\n\"전 계집애한테서 달아날 생각은 없어요. 조가 제가 그 애를 만나는 걸 막을 순 없고, 전 여기 남아서 제가 원하는 만큼 그 애를 볼 거예요.\" 로리가 반항적인 어조로 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"네가 내가 생각하는 그런 신사라면 그러지 않겠지. 나도 실망스럽다만, 그 애도 어쩔 수 없는 일이야. 네가 할 수 있는 유일한 일은 한동안 멀리 떠나는 것뿐이다. 어디로 가겠느냐?\"\n\n\"아무 데나요. 제가 어떻게 되든 상관없어요.\" 로리가 자리에서 일어나며, 할아버지의 귀에 거슬리는 자포자기한 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"사내답게 받아들이고, 제발 경솔한 짓일랑 하지 마라. 계획했던 대로 외국에 나가서 잊어버리는 게 어떻겠느냐?\"\n\"그럴 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"하지만 넌 그렇게 가고 싶어 안달하지 않았느냐. 대학을 마치면 보내 주겠다고 내가 약속도 했고.\"\n\n\"아, 하지만 전 혼자 갈 생각은 아니었어요!\" 로리는 방 안을 빠르게 서성거렸다. 할아버지가 못 본 것이 다행인 표정을 지은 채.\n\n\"혼자 가라는 게 아니다. 너와 함께 세상 어디든 기꺼이 갈 채비가 된 사람이 하나 있단다.\"\n\n\"그게 누군데요?\" 그가 걸음을 멈추고 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"바로 나다.\"\n로리는 나갈 때처럼 잽싸게 돌아와 손을 내밀며 잠긴 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"전 이기적인 짐승이에요. 하지만 — 아시잖아요 — 할아버지 —\"\n\n\"오, 알다마다. 안다, 나도 다 겪어 봤으니까. 한 번은 내 젊은 시절에, 또 한 번은 네 아버지 일로 말이다. 자, 얘야, 그저 가만히 앉아서 내 계획을 들어 보렴. 다 정해진 일이고, 당장 실행에 옮길 수 있단다.\" 로렌스 씨는 젊은이를 붙잡은 채로 말했다. 마치 예전에 그 아버지가 그랬듯 그가 뿌리치고 달아날까 두려워하는 듯이.\n\n\"그래서, 그게 뭔데요?\" 로리는 얼굴에도 목소리에도 아무 관심을 내비치지 않은 채 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"런던에 살펴봐야 할 사업이 있다. 원래는 네가 처리하기를 바랐다만, 내가 직접 하는 편이 낫겠고, 이곳 일은 브룩이 맡아 관리하면 아주 잘 굴러갈 거야. 동업자들이 거의 다 해 주고 있으니, 나야 네가 내 자리를 물려받을 때까지 그저 붙들고 있는 것뿐이라 언제든 떠날 수 있어.\"\n\n\"하지만 할아버지는 여행을 싫어하시잖아요. 연세도 있으신데 그걸 부탁드릴 순 없어요.\" 로리가 입을 열었다. 그 희생이 고맙긴 했지만, 정작 가게 된다면 혼자 가는 편이 훨씬 좋았던 것이다.\n\n노신사는 그 점을 완벽히 알았고, 특히 그것을 막고 싶어 했다. 손자가 처한 그 심정으로 보아, 제멋대로 하도록 내버려 두는 것은 현명하지 못하다는 확신이 들었기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 뒤에 두고 갈 가정의 안락을 떠올리며 절로 솟는 아쉬움을 억누르고, 씩씩하게 말했다.\n\n\"걱정 마라, 난 아직 노망난 늙은이가 아니다. 그 생각을 하니 꽤 즐거워지는구나. 내게도 좋은 일이 될 거고, 이 늙은 뼈도 끄떡없을 게다. 요즘 여행이란 의자에 앉아 있는 것만큼이나 편하니까 말이다.\"\n\n로리가 안절부절못하는 몸짓을 하는 것을 보니 그의 의자가 편치 않거나, 아니면 그 계획이 마음에 들지 않는다는 뜻이었다. 그래서 노인은 황급히 덧붙였다.\n\n\"훼방꾼이나 짐이 될 생각은 없다. 내가 가는 건, 내가 뒤에 남는 것보다 네가 더 마음 편할 것 같아서야. 너와 졸졸 붙어 다닐 생각은 없고, 네가 가고 싶은 곳을 마음껏 다니게 두마. 나는 내 식대로 즐길 테니. 런던과 파리에 친구들이 있으니 그들을 찾아보고 싶구나. 그동안 너는 이탈리아든 독일이든 스위스든 가고 싶은 데로 가서, 그림이며 음악이며 풍경이며 모험을 실컷 즐기렴.\"\n\n그때 로리는 자기 마음이 완전히 부서졌고 세상이 울부짖는 황야 같다고 느끼고 있었다. 그러나 노신사가 마지막 문장에 슬쩍 끼워 넣은 어떤 말들이 귀에 들어오자, 부서진 마음이 뜻밖에 한 번 펄쩍 뛰었고, 울부짖는 황야 속에 푸른 오아시스 한두 개가 불쑥 나타났다. 그는 한숨을 쉬고는 맥없는 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"좋으실 대로 하세요, 할아버지. 제가 어디로 가든 무얼 하든 상관없으니까요.\"\n\n\"내게는 상관있단다, 그걸 명심해라, 얘야. 너한테 완전한 자유를 주마. 다만 그 자유를 정직하게 쓰리라 믿는다. 그러겠다고 약속하렴, 로리.\"\n\n\"뭐든 원하시는 대로요, 할아버지.\"\n\n'됐다.' 노신사는 생각했다. '지금은 아무래도 좋다지만, 언젠가 그 약속이 너를 말썽에서 지켜 줄 때가 올 게다. 내 생각이 크게 틀리지 않았다면 말이야.'\n\n기력이 넘치는 사람답게 로렌스 씨는 쇠가 달았을 때 두드렸다. 그리하여 풀이 죽은 그 사람이 반항할 만큼 기운을 차리기도 전에 두 사람은 떠났다. 출발 준비에 필요한 기간 동안, 로리는 이런 경우 젊은 신사들이 으레 그러는 식으로 처신했다. 그는 침울했다가, 짜증을 냈다가, 수심에 잠겼다가 했다. 입맛을 잃고 옷차림을 소홀히 했으며, 피아노를 격정적으로 치는 데 많은 시간을 쏟았다. 조를 피하면서도, 창문에서 그녀를 빤히 바라보는 것으로 스스로를 위로했다. 그 비극적인 얼굴은 밤이면 그녀의 꿈자리를 어지럽혔고, 낮이면 그녀를 무거운 죄책감으로 짓눌렀다. 어떤 고통받는 이들과 달리, 그는 보답받지 못한 사랑을 결코 입에 올리지 않았고, 마치 부인을 비롯한 그 누구도 위로하거나 동정을 건네지 못하게 했다. 어떤 면에서 이것은 친구들에게 다행이었다. 하지만 그가 떠나기 전 몇 주는 무척 불편했고, 모두가 \"그 가엾고 다정한 친구가 멀리 떠나서 괴로움을 잊고 행복하게 돌아오기를\" 바라며 안도했다. 물론 그는 그들의 그런 헛된 기대에 어두운 미소를 지었지만, 자기의 신의가 자기의 사랑처럼 변치 않으리라는 것을 아는 자의 슬픈 우월감으로 그것을 흘려보냈다.\n\n이별의 순간이 오자, 그는 자꾸만 고개를 들려는 거추장스러운 감정들을 감추려고 한껏 들뜬 척했다. 그 명랑함에 속는 사람은 아무도 없었지만, 모두 그를 위해 속는 척했다. 그리하여 그는 마치 부인이 어머니다운 염려가 가득 담긴 속삭임과 함께 입을 맞출 때까지는 꽤 잘 버텼다. 그러나 자기가 무너지는 게 빨라지는 것을 느끼자, 그는 황급히 모두를 한 바퀴 끌어안았다. 슬픔에 잠긴 해나도 빠뜨리지 않고서. 그러고는 목숨이라도 걸린 듯 아래층으로 뛰어 내려갔다. 잠시 후 조가 뒤따라 나갔다. 그가 돌아본다면 손을 흔들어 주려고. 그는 정말로 돌아보았고, 되돌아와 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안았다. 그녀가 그보다 한 단 위 계단에 서 있는 채로. 그러고는 그녀를 올려다보았는데, 그 얼굴이 그의 짧은 호소를 웅변적이면서도 애처롭게 만들었다.\n\n\"아, 조, 정말 안 되겠니?\"\n\n\"테디, 나도 그럴 수 있으면 좋겠어!\"\n\n그게 다였다. 잠깐의 침묵 말고는. 이윽고 로리는 몸을 똑바로 펴고 \"괜찮아, 신경 쓰지 마\"라고 말한 뒤, 한마디도 더 없이 떠나갔다. 아, 하지만 괜찮지 않았고, 조도 정말로 마음이 쓰였다. 그 매정한 대답 직후 그 곱슬머리가 잠시 자기 팔에 기대어 있는 동안, 그녀는 자기가 가장 소중한 벗을 칼로 찌른 듯한 기분이었다. 그리고 그가 한 번도 뒤돌아보지 않고 그녀를 떠났을 때, 그녀는 소년 로리가 결코 다시는 돌아오지 않으리라는 것을 알았다.\n36장\n\n# BETH'S SECRET.\n\n베스의 비밀\n그해 봄 집에 돌아왔을 때 조는 베스의 변화에 충격을 받았다. 아무도 그것을 입에 올리지 않았고, 알아채는 것 같지도 않았다. 변화가 너무 서서히 찾아온 탓에 날마다 보는 사람들은 놀라지 않았던 것이다. 하지만 떨어져 지내면서 예민해진 눈에는 그것이 너무도 또렷했고, 동생의 얼굴을 본 조의 가슴에는 무거운 짐이 내려앉았다. 베스의 얼굴은 가을에 비해 더 창백하지도, 별로 더 야위지도 않았다. 그런데도 묘하게 투명한 기운이 어려 있었다. 마치 죽어야 할 부분이 천천히 정제되어 깎여 나가고, 죽지 않는 부분이 그 연약한 살을 뚫고 말로 표현할 수 없이 애잔한 아름다움으로 빛나는 듯했다. 조는 그것을 보았고 느꼈지만, 그때는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그리고 곧 그 첫인상은 힘을 많이 잃었다. 베스가 행복해 보였고, 아무도 그녀가 나아지고 있다는 것을 의심하지 않는 듯했으며, 이내 다른 근심거리들 속에서 조는 한동안 그 두려움을 잊었다.\n\n그러나 로리가 떠나고 다시 평온이 깃들자, 그 막연한 불안이 되돌아와 그녀를 떠나지 않았다. 그녀는 자기 죄를 고백하고 용서받았다. 하지만 모아 둔 돈을 내보이며 산으로 가자고 제안했을 때, 베스는 진심으로 고마워하면서도 집에서 그렇게 멀리 떠나지는 말자고 청했다. 바닷가로 한 번 더 짧게 다녀오는 편이 자기에게 더 맞으리라는 것이었다. 할머니는 아기들을 떼어 놓고 떠나도록 설득할 수가 없었으므로, 조는 베스를 데리고 그 조용한 곳으로 내려갔다. 거기서라면 동생이 바깥 공기를 마음껏 쐬며 지내고, 상쾌한 바닷바람이 그 창백한 두 뺨에 약간의 혈색을 불어넣어 줄 수 있을 터였다.\n\n세련된 곳은 아니었지만, 그곳의 유쾌한 사람들 틈에서도 두 자매는 친구를 거의 사귀지 않았다. 서로만을 위해 지내는 편을 좋아했던 것이다. 베스는 너무 수줍어서 사교를 즐기지 못했고, 조는 동생에게 너무 푹 빠져 있어 다른 누구에게도 마음을 쓸 수 없었다. 그리하여 둘은 서로에게 전부였고, 주위 사람들이 자기들에게 품는 관심도 전혀 의식하지 못한 채 오가곤 했다. 사람들은 동정 어린 눈으로 그 강한 언니와 연약한 동생을 지켜보았다. 늘 함께 붙어 있는 두 사람을, 마치 머지않아 긴 이별이 다가오리라는 것을 본능적으로 느끼기라도 한 듯이.\n\n두 사람도 그것을 느끼고 있었다. 그러나 누구도 그 이야기를 입에 올리지 않았다. 우리 자신과, 우리에게 가장 가깝고 소중한 이들 사이에는 좀처럼 넘어서기 힘든 어떤 조심스러움이 자리하는 법이니까. 조는 마치 자기 마음과 베스의 마음 사이에 베일이 드리워진 것 같았다. 하지만 그것을 걷어 올리려고 손을 내밀면, 그 침묵 속에 무언가 신성한 것이 깃든 듯해서 베스가 먼저 입을 열기를 기다렸다. 그녀는 부모님이 자기가 보는 것을 보지 못하는 듯한 게 의아하면서도 감사했다. 그리고 그 그늘이 그토록 또렷이 보이던 그 고요한 몇 주 동안, 집에 있는 식구들에게는 그 일에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 베스가 나아지지 않은 채 돌아가면 저절로 드러나게 되리라 여겼던 것이다. 그녀는 더더욱 의아했다. 동생이 정말로 그 가혹한 진실을 짐작하고 있는지, 그리고 따뜻한 바위에 누워 조의 무릎에 머리를 얹은 채, 건강을 부르는 바람이 위로 불어오고 발치에서 바다가 음악을 연주하는 그 긴 시간 동안 동생의 마음속에는 어떤 생각들이 스쳐 가는지.\n\n어느 날 베스가 그것을 털어놓았다. 동생이 어찌나 미동도 없이 누워 있던지 조는 잠든 줄로만 알았다. 그래서 책을 내려놓고 동생을 애틋한 눈길로 바라보며, 베스의 두 뺨에 어린 희미한 혈색에서 희망의 징표를 찾아보려 했다. 하지만 그녀를 안심시킬 만큼은 찾을 수 없었다. 두 뺨은 너무 야위었고, 두 손은 그동안 주워 모으던 그 발그레한 작은 조개껍데기조차 쥐기 버거워 보일 만큼 가냘팠으니까. 그 순간, 베스가 자기에게서 천천히 멀어져 가고 있다는 사실이 그 어느 때보다도 쓰라리게 다가왔고, 그녀의 두 팔은 본능적으로 동생을 더 꼭 끌어안았다.\n그녀가 지닌 가장 소중한 보물을 붙잡고 있던 손길이었다. 잠시 두 눈이 흐려져 아무것도 보이지 않았고, 다시 맑아졌을 때 베스가 너무도 다정하게 그녀를 올려다보고 있어, 굳이 말을 보탤 필요도 없을 정도였다.\n\n\"조 언니, 언니가 알아서 다행이야. 말하려고 애써 봤는데, 도무지 못 하겠더라.\"\n\n대답이라곤 자기 뺨에 와 닿은 동생의 뺨뿐, 눈물조차 없었다. 가장 깊이 마음이 흔들릴 때면 조는 울지 않았다. 그럴 때 더 약한 쪽은 조였다. 베스가 두 팔로 언니를 감싸 안은 채 위로하고 다독이며 귓가에 부드러운 말을 속삭였다.\n\n\"꽤 오래전부터 알고 있었어, 언니. 이제 익숙해져서, 생각하거나 견디는 게 그렇게 힘들지 않아. 언니도 그렇게 받아들이려고 해 봐. 그리고 내 걱정은 하지 마. 이게 제일 좋은 거니까. 정말 그래.\"\n\n\"그래서 가을에 그렇게 마음이 무거웠던 거니, 베스? 그때 이미 느꼈으면서, 그렇게 오래 혼자만 묻어 두고 있었던 거야?\" 조가 물었다. 그게 제일 좋은 일이라는 걸 보지도, 입에 담지도 않으려 했지만, 베스의 괴로움에 로리가 아무런 관련이 없다는 걸 알게 되어 마음이 놓였다.\n\n\"응, 그때 희망을 접었어. 그래도 인정하기는 싫었거든. 그냥 아파서 떠오른 헛생각일 거라고 여기려 했고, 누구한테도 짐이 되게 하고 싶지 않았어. 그런데 다들 그렇게 건강하고 기운 넘치고, 행복한 계획으로 가득 찬 모습을 보니까, 나는 절대 언니들처럼 될 수 없다는 게 너무 힘들더라. 그래서 비참했어, 언니.\"\n\n\"아, 베스, 그런데도 나한테 말 안 하고, 내가 위로하고 도와주지도 못하게 했단 말이야! 어떻게 나를 밀어내고 그걸 혼자서 다 견딜 수 있었니?\"\n\n조의 목소리에는 애틋한 책망이 가득했다. 베스가 건강과 사랑과 삶에게 작별을 고하는 법을 익히고, 그토록 밝게 자기 십자가를 짊어지는 동안 홀로 치렀을 그 싸움을 생각하니 가슴이 아렸다.\n\n\"잘못한 건지도 몰라. 그래도 옳게 하려고 애썼어. 확신도 없었고, 아무도 무슨 말을 해 주지 않았고, 내가 잘못 안 거였으면 좋겠다 싶었거든. 엄마는 메그 언니 일로 그렇게 마음 졸이고, 에이미는 멀리 가 있고, 언니는 로리랑 그렇게 행복한데, 다들 겁먹게 하는 건 이기적인 일이잖아. 적어도 그때는 그렇게 생각했어.\"\n\n\"난 또 네가 그 애를 좋아하는 줄 알았어, 베스. 그래서 난 그럴 수가 없어서 떠나 버린 거고.\" 조가 외쳤다. 마침내 진실을 다 털어놓을 수 있어 후련했다.\n\n베스가 그 말에 어찌나 놀란 표정을 짓던지, 조는 가슴 아픈 와중에도 웃음이 나서 부드럽게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그럼 넌 아니었던 거니, 얘? 난 그런 줄 알고 걱정했단다. 네 가엾고 작은 가슴이 그동안 내내 짝사랑으로 가득 차 있는 줄 알고 말이야.\"\n\n\"어머, 언니, 그 사람이 언니를 그렇게 좋아하는데 내가 어떻게 그래?\" 베스가 어린아이처럼 천진하게 물었다. \"난 그 사람을 정말 좋아해. 나한테 얼마나 잘해 주는데, 어떻게 안 좋아할 수가 있겠어? 그래도 그 사람은 나한테 오빠 말고는 아무것도 될 수 없어. 언젠가 정말 오빠가 되면 좋겠다.\"\n\n\"나를 통해서는 아니야.\" 조가 단호하게 말했다. \"그 애한테는 에이미가 남아 있잖아. 둘이 아주 잘 어울릴 거야. 하지만 난 지금 그런 일에 마음 쓸 여유가 없어. 너 말고는 누가 어떻게 되든 신경 안 써, 베스. 넌 꼭 나아야 해.\"\n\n\"나도 그러고 싶어, 아, 얼마나 그러고 싶은데! 애써 봐. 그런데 날마다 조금씩 잃어 가고, 다시는 되찾지 못하리란 게 점점 더 확실해져. 꼭 밀물 같아, 언니. 한번 방향이 바뀌면 천천히 가긴 해도, 멈출 수는 없는 거잖아.\"\n\n\"멈추게 할 거야. 네 물때는 그렇게 일찍 바뀌면 안 돼. 열아홉은 너무 어려. 베스, 난 널 보낼 수 없어. 일하고 기도하고, 그것과 맞서 싸울 거야. 무슨 일이 있어도 널 붙잡아 둘 거야. 분명 방법이 있을 거야. 너무 늦었을 리 없어. 하느님이 너를 내게서 데려가실 만큼 잔인하실 리 없어.\" 가엾은 조가 반항하듯 외쳤다. 베스만큼 경건하게 순응하는 영혼과는 거리가 멀었기 때문이다.\n\n소박하고 진실한 사람들은 자신의 신앙을 좀처럼 입에 올리지 않는다. 신앙은 말보다 행동으로 드러나며, 설교나 다짐보다 더 큰 힘을 지닌다. 베스는 삶을 내려놓고 기꺼이 죽음을 기다릴 용기와 인내를 주는 그 믿음을 따져 묻거나 설명할 수 없었다. 그저 모든 것을 믿고 의지하는 아이처럼, 아무것도 묻지 않고, 우리 모두의 아버지요 어머니인 하느님과 자연에 모든 것을 맡겼다. 그분들만이, 오직 그분들만이 이 삶과 다가올 삶을 위해 마음과 영혼을 가르치고 굳세게 해 주실 수 있다고 믿어 의심치 않았다. 베스는 성인 같은 말로 조를 나무라지 않았다. 다만 그 격렬한 애정 때문에 언니를 더욱 사랑했고, 우리 아버지께서 결코 우리를 떼어 놓으려 하지 않으시며 오히려 그것을 통해 우리를 당신 곁으로 더 가까이 이끄시는, 그 소중한 인간의 사랑에 더욱 매달렸다. \"기꺼이 가겠어\"라고는 말할 수 없었다. 베스에게 삶은 너무도 달콤했으니까. 그저 \"기꺼이 받아들이려고 애써\"라고 흐느낄 뿐이었다. 그 커다란 슬픔의 첫 쓰디쓴 물결이 두 사람 위로 함께 부서져 내리는 동안, 베스는 조를 꼭 붙들었다.\n\n이윽고 베스가 다시 평온을 되찾은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"우리 집에 돌아가면 언니가 식구들한테 이 얘기를 해 줄 거지?\"\n\n\"말하지 않아도 다들 알 것 같은데.\" 조가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. 이제는 베스가 하루가 다르게 변해 가는 것만 같았다.\n\n\"아마 모를 거야. 가장 사랑하는 사람들이 이런 일에는 가장 눈멀어 있는 경우가 많다고 들었거든. 식구들이 알아채지 못하면, 언니가 날 대신해서 말해 줘. 난 비밀을 두고 싶지 않고, 미리 마음의 준비를 시켜 드리는 게 더 다정한 거잖아. 메그 언니한테는 존 형부랑 아기들이 위로가 되어 주겠지만, 언니는 아빠 엄마 곁을 지켜 줘야 해. 그렇게 해 줄 거지, 언니?\"\n\n\"할 수 있다면 그럴게. 그래도, 베스, 난 아직 포기 안 해. 이게 아파서 떠오른 헛생각이라고 믿을 거고, 네가 그걸 사실이라고 여기게 두지 않을 거야.\" 조가 애써 밝게 말하려 했다.\n\n베스는 잠시 누운 채 생각에 잠겼다가, 특유의 차분한 말투로 입을 열었다.\n\n\"어떻게 말해야 할지 모르겠어. 언니가 아닌 다른 사람한테는 아예 해 보려고도 안 할 거야. 우리 조 언니가 아니면 속을 털어놓을 수가 없거든. 그냥 내가 하고 싶은 말은, 난 애초에 오래 살게 되어 있던 게 아니라는 느낌이 든다는 거야. 난 다른 식구들이랑은 달라. 자라서 뭘 할지 같은 계획을 세워 본 적도 없고, 언니들처럼 결혼을 생각해 본 적도 없어. 난 도무지 나 자신을, 집 안에서 종종거리며 돌아다니고 거기 말고는 어디서도 쓸모없는 멍청하고 작은 베스 말고 다른 무엇으로 상상할 수가 없었어. 어디로도 떠나고 싶었던 적이 없어. 지금 힘든 건 식구들을 두고 떠나는 거야. 무섭진 않은데, 천국에서도 식구들이 그리워서 향수병에 걸릴 것만 같아.\"\n\n조는 말을 잇지 못했다. 몇 분 동안 바람의 한숨 소리와 밀물이 찰싹거리는 소리 말고는 아무 소리도 없었다. 하얀 날개의 갈매기 한 마리가 은빛 가슴에 햇살을 반짝이며 날아갔다. 베스는 그것이 사라질 때까지 지켜보았고, 두 눈에는 슬픔이 가득했다. 회색 옷을 입은 작은 물떼새 한 마리가 해변을 종종걸음으로 가로지르며, 햇살과 바다를 즐기듯 혼자서 가만가만 \"삐악거렸다.\" 새는 베스에게 아주 가까이 다가와 다정한 눈으로 그녀를 바라보더니, 따뜻한 돌 위에 앉아 젖은 깃털을 다듬으며 제집처럼 편안히 굴었다. 베스는 미소 지으며 위로를 받았다. 그 자그마한 것이 작은 우정을 건네고, 아직 즐길 만한 즐거운 세상이 남아 있음을 일깨워 주는 것 같았다.\n\n\"귀여운 작은 새! 언니, 봐, 얼마나 길이 잘 들었는지. 난 갈매기보다 물떼새가 더 좋아. 갈매기처럼 거칠고 멋지진 않지만, 행복하고 다정한 작은 것들 같거든. 작년 여름에 난 얘네를 내 새들이라고 불렀어. 엄마는 얘네를 보면 내가 생각난다고 했지. 부지런하고 퀘이커교도처럼 수수한 빛깔에, 늘 물가 가까이 머물면서, 늘 자기들만의 만족스러운 작은 노래를 지저귀는 것들이라고. 언니는 갈매기야, 강하고 거칠고, 폭풍과 바람을 좋아하고, 멀리 바다로 날아가서 혼자서도 행복한. 메그 언니는 멧비둘기고, 에이미는 자기가 쓴 시 속의 종달새 같아. 구름 사이로 솟아오르려 애쓰지만, 늘 다시 둥지로 내려앉는. 사랑스러운 애! 야망이 정말 크지만, 마음은 곱고 다정하잖아. 아무리 높이 날아도 집은 절대 잊지 않을 거야. 그 애를 다시 볼 수 있으면 좋겠는데, 너무나 멀리 있는 것 같아.\"\n\n\"봄에 올 거야. 그때 네가 그 애를 맞이하고 함께 즐길 수 있게 다 준비시켜 둘 거야. 그때까지 널 건강하고 발그레하게 만들어 놓을 거야.\" 조가 입을 뗐다. 베스의 여러 변화 중에서도 말이 많아진 변화가 가장 크다는 생각이 들었다. 이제는 아무런 힘도 들이지 않는 듯했고, 수줍던 예전의 베스답지 않게 생각을 소리 내어 말했다.\n\n\"조 언니, 더는 희망 갖지 마. 아무 소용없을 거야. 난 그걸 확실히 알아. 우리 비참해하지는 말고, 기다리는 동안 함께 있는 걸 즐기자. 행복한 시간을 보내자. 난 별로 고통스럽지 않고, 언니가 도와주면 물때가 쉽게 빠져나갈 것 같거든.\"\n\n조는 몸을 숙여 그 평온한 얼굴에 입을 맞추었다. 그 말 없는 입맞춤으로, 조는 영혼과 몸을 다 바쳐 베스에게 헌신하기로 했다.\n\n베스의 말이 옳았다. 집에 돌아왔을 때 굳이 말이 필요 없었다. 아버지와 어머니는 이제, 보지 않게 해 달라고 기도해 온 그것을 또렷이 알아보았다. 짧은 여정에도 지친 베스는 집에 와서 얼마나 기쁜지 모르겠다며 곧장 잠자리에 들었다. 조가 아래층으로 내려갔을 때, 베스의 비밀을 털어놓아야 하는 힘든 일을 덜게 되었음을 알았다. 아버지는 벽난로 선반에 머리를 기댄 채 서 있었고, 그녀가 들어와도 돌아보지 않았다. 다만 어머니가 도움을 청하듯 두 팔을 벌렸고, 조는 말없이 다가가 어머니를 위로했다.\n새로운 인상\n\n# NEW IMPRESSIONS.\n\n오후 세 시면 니스의 멋쟁이들이 모두 프로므나드 데 장글레로 나온다. 매혹적인 곳이다. 야자수와 꽃, 열대 관목이 늘어선 넓은 산책로는 한쪽으로는 바다와 맞닿아 있고, 다른 쪽으로는 호텔과 별장이 즐비한 대로변과 이어지며, 그 너머로는 오렌지 과수원과 언덕이 펼쳐져 있다. 온갖 나라 사람들이 모여 온갖 언어를 쓰고 온갖 옷차림을 하고 있어, 화창한 날이면 그 광경이 카니발만큼이나 흥겹고 화려하다. 거만한 영국인, 활기찬 프랑스인, 진중한 독일인, 잘생긴 스페인인, 못생긴 러시아인, 온순한 유대인, 격식 없는 미국인이 모두 마차를 몰거나 앉아 있거나 거닐면서, 새 소식을 이야기하고, 최근 도착한 유명 인사를 입에 올린다. 리스토리든 디킨스든, 빅토르 에마누엘이든 샌드위치 제도의 여왕이든 말이다. 마차도 사람들만큼이나 가지각색이라 못지않게 눈길을 끄는데, 특히 숙녀들이 직접 모는 낮은 바구니 모양의 사륜마차가 그렇다. 멋진 조랑말 한 쌍에, 풍성한 주름 장식이 자그마한 마차 밖으로 흘러넘치지 않도록 화려한 망을 두르고, 뒤편 발판에는 어린 마부들이 올라타 있다.\n\n크리스마스 날, 이 산책로를 따라 키 큰 청년 하나가 뒷짐을 진 채, 어딘가 멍한 표정으로 천천히 걷고 있었다. 그는 이탈리아 사람처럼 보였고, 영국 사람처럼 차려입었으며, 미국 사람 특유의 거리낌 없는 분위기를 풍겼다. 그 조합 덕분에 여러 여인의 눈길이 흐뭇하게 그의 뒤를 좇았고, 검은 벨벳 정장에 장밋빛 넥타이를 매고 황갈색 장갑을 끼고 단춧구멍에 오렌지 꽃을 꽂은 멋쟁이들은 어깨를 으쓱하고는 그의 큰 키를 부러워했다. 감탄할 만한 예쁜 얼굴이 많았지만, 청년은 그들에게 별 관심을 두지 않았다. 그저 이따금 금발 아가씨나 파란 옷을 입은 숙녀에게 눈길을 던질 뿐이었다. 이윽고 그는 산책로를 벗어나 횡단로 어귀에서 잠시 멈춰 섰다. 자르댕 퓌블리크에서 악단의 연주를 들으러 갈지, 아니면 해변을 따라 캐슬 힐 쪽으로 거닐지 마음을 정하지 못한 듯했다. 조랑말의 빠른 발굽 소리에 그가 고개를 들었다. 숙녀 한 명만 탄 작은 마차 한 대가 거리를 빠르게 내려오고 있었다. 그 숙녀는 젊고 금발이었으며, 파란 옷을 입고 있었다. 그는 잠시 멍하니 바라보다가 얼굴이 온통 환해지더니, 소년처럼 모자를 흔들며 서둘러 그녀를 맞으러 다가갔다.\n\n\"어머, 로리, 정말 너야? 영영 안 올 줄 알았는데!\" 에이미가 고삐를 떨구고 두 손을 내밀며 소리쳤다. 그 바람에 곁에 있던 프랑스 부인이 크게 놀라, 이 \"정신 나간 영국 사람들\"의 격식 없는 태도를 보고 딸이 물들까 봐 걸음을 재촉했다.\n\n\"오는 길에 일이 좀 있어서 늦었어. 그래도 너랑 크리스마스를 보내겠다고 약속했잖아. 그래서 이렇게 왔지.\"\n\n\"할아버지는 어떠셔? 언제 왔어? 어디 묵고 있어?\"\n\n\"아주 잘 지내셔. 어젯밤에 왔고, 쇼뱅 호텔에 묵고 있어. 너희 호텔에 들렀는데, 다들 나가고 없더라.\"\n\n\"할 말이 너무 많아서 어디서부터 시작해야 할지 모르겠어! 어서 타. 편하게 얘기하자. 마차 산책을 나가려던 참이었는데, 마침 같이 갈 사람이 있었으면 했거든. 플로는 오늘 밤을 위해 기운을 아끼는 중이야.\"\n\n\"오늘 밤에 무슨 일 있어? 무도회야?\"\n\n\"우리 호텔에서 크리스마스 파티를 열어. 미국 사람들이 많이 묵고 있는데, 이날을 기념해서 여는 거야. 너도 우리랑 같이 갈 거지? 이모님이 무척 좋아하실 거야.\"\n\n\"고마워. 그럼 지금은 어디로 가?\" 로리가 등을 기대고 팔짱을 끼며 물었다. 직접 모는 걸 더 좋아하는 에이미에게는 잘된 일이었다. 양산 채찍과 흰 조랑말 등 위로 늘어뜨린 파란 고삐가 그녀에게 더없는 만족을 안겨 주었으니까.\n\n\"먼저 은행에 가서 편지를 받고, 그다음에 캐슬 힐로 갈 거야. 경치가 정말 사랑스럽고, 난 공작새한테 먹이 주는 게 좋거든. 거기 가 본 적 있어?\"\n\n\"여러 번, 몇 년 전에. 그래도 한 번 더 둘러보는 것도 괜찮지.\"\n\n\"이제 네 얘기 좀 다 해 봐. 네 소식을 마지막으로 들은 게, 할아버지가 네가 베를린에서 올 거라고 편지에 쓰셨을 때야.\"\n\n\"응, 거기서 한 달 지내고, 파리에서 할아버지랑 합류했어. 겨울 동안 파리에 자리 잡으셨거든. 거기 친구분들이 계셔서 즐길 거리가 많으셔. 그래서 난 오가고, 우리 둘 다 잘 지내.\"\n\n\"사이좋게 지내시는구나.\" 에이미가 말했다. 로리의 태도에서 뭔가 빠진 듯한 느낌이 들었지만, 그게 뭔지는 알 수 없었다.\n\n\"그게, 할아버지는 여행을 질색하시고, 난 가만있는 걸 질색하잖아. 그러니까 각자 좋을 대로 하면 아무 문제가 없어. 자주 곁에 있어 드리고, 할아버지는 내 모험담을 즐기시지. 나도 떠돌다 돌아오면 누군가 날 반겨 준다는 게 좋고. 지저분하고 낡은 동네지?\" 그가 옛 시가지의 나폴레옹 광장으로 가는 큰길을 달리며, 질색하는 표정으로 덧붙였다.\n\n\"지저분한 게 그림 같아서 난 괜찮아. 강이랑 언덕은 정말 멋지고, 좁은 골목길이 언뜻언뜻 보이는 게 난 너무 좋거든. 자, 이제 저 행렬이 지나갈 때까지 기다려야겠다. 생장 교회로 가는 행렬이야.\"\n\n로리가 닫집 아래의 사제들과 불 켠 가는 초를 든 흰 너울의 수녀들, 그리고 걸으면서 성가를 부르는 파란 옷의 어느 수도회 사람들을 무심히 바라보는 동안, 에이미는 로리를 바라보았다. 그러자 새로운 종류의 수줍음이 그녀에게 스며들었다. 그가 변했기 때문이다. 자기가 두고 떠났던 명랑한 얼굴의 소년을, 곁에 있는 침울해 보이는 남자에게서 찾을 수가 없었다. 에이미가 보기에 그는 그 어느 때보다 잘생겼고, 훨씬 멋있어졌다. 하지만 그녀를 만난 기쁨의 홍조가 가시고 나니, 그는 지치고 풀이 죽어 보였다. 아프거나 딱히 불행해 보이지는 않았지만, 한두 해의 풍요로운 삶이 만들었을 법한 것보다 더 늙고 더 진중해 보였다. 에이미는 그 까닭을 알 수 없었고, 감히 캐묻지도 못했다. 그래서 행렬이 팔리오니 다리의 아치를 가로질러 굽이굽이 멀어지다가 교회로 사라지자, 고개를 한 번 젓고는 조랑말을 가볍게 채찍질했다.\n\n\"크 팡세 부?\" 그녀가 프랑스어를 뽐내며 말했다. 외국에 나온 뒤로 질은 몰라도 양은 늘어난 프랑스어였다.\n\n\"마드무아젤이 시간을 잘 보냈고, 그 결과가 매혹적이라는 거지.\" 로리가 가슴에 손을 얹고 고개를 숙이며, 감탄 어린 눈빛으로 대답했다.\n\n에이미는 기뻐서 얼굴을 붉혔다. 하지만 어쩐지 그 찬사는, 예전에 집에서 그가 해 주던 무뚝뚝한 칭찬만큼 마음에 차지 않았다. 그때는 잔칫날이면 그녀 주위를 빙 돌면서, 진심 어린 미소로 머리를 흔쾌히 토닥이며 \"아주 근사하다\"고 말해 주곤 했었다. 에이미는 이 새로운 말투가 마음에 들지 않았다. 권태에 찌든 건 아니었지만, 그 눈빛에도 불구하고 어딘가 무심하게 들렸다.\n\n'저렇게 어른이 될 거라면, 차라리 소년인 채로 있는 게 낫겠어.' 에이미는 묘한 실망감과 불편함을 느끼며 그렇게 생각했다. 그러면서도 자기는 아주 편안하고 즐거워 보이려 애썼다.\n\n아비그도르 은행에서 에이미는 그토록 소중한 집에서 온 편지들을 받았고, 고삐를 로리에게 넘긴 채 그것을 한껏 즐기며 읽었다. 푸른 산울타리 사이로 그늘진 길을 굽이굽이 올라가는데, 거기엔 차나무 장미가 유월처럼 싱그럽게 피어 있었다.\n\n\"엄마가 그러는데, 베스가 많이 안 좋대. 난 자꾸 집에 가야 하는 게 아닌가 싶은데, 다들 '그냥 있으라'고 해. 그래서 여기 있는 거야. 이런 기회는 다시는 없을 테니까.\" 에이미가 한 장을 읽으며 침울한 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 네 말이 맞아. 집에 가도 네가 할 수 있는 일이 없을 거고, 네가 잘 지내고 행복하고 이렇게 많은 걸 누리고 있다는 걸 아는 게 식구들에게는 큰 위안이 될 거야.\"\n\n그가 말하면서 조금 더 가까이 다가왔고, 한결 예전의 그다워 보였다. 그러자 이따금 에이미의 마음을 짓누르던 두려움이 가벼워졌다. 그 눈빛과 몸짓, 오빠 같은 \"이런\" 한마디가, 혹시라도 무슨 어려움이 닥치더라도 낯선 땅에서 혼자가 아니리라는 걸 그녀에게 확인시켜 주는 것 같았다. 이윽고 그녀가 웃으며, 글을 끄적이는 차림새의 조를 그린 작은 그림을 보여 주었다. 모자 위에는 리본이 사납게 곤두서 있고, 입에서는 \"천재의 불꽃이 타오른다!\"라는 말이 흘러나오고 있었다.\n\n로리는 미소 지으며 그것을 받아, \"날아가 버리지 않게\" 조끼 주머니에 넣고는, 에이미가 읽어 주는 생기 넘치는 편지에 흥미롭게 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"이번엔 정말 즐거운 크리스마스가 되겠다. 아침엔 선물, 오후엔 너랑 편지, 밤엔 파티.\" 에이미가 말했다. 두 사람은 옛 요새의 폐허 속에 내려섰고, 화려한 공작새 떼가 길든 모습으로 먹이를 기다리며 그들 주위로 우르르 몰려들었다.\n에이미가 그의 위쪽 둑에 서서 화려한 새들에게 빵 부스러기를 뿌리며 웃고 있는 동안, 로리는 그녀가 그를 보았던 것처럼 그녀를 바라보았다. 세월과 떨어져 있던 시간이 어떤 변화를 만들어 냈는지 보려는 자연스러운 호기심이었다. 그는 당혹스럽거나 실망스러운 점은 찾지 못했고, 감탄하고 흐뭇해할 점은 많이 찾아냈다. 말투와 태도에 밴 자그마한 가식 몇 가지만 눈감아 준다면, 그녀는 여전히 발랄하고 우아했으며, 거기에 우리가 기품이라 부르는, 옷차림과 몸가짐에 어린 그 무어라 형용할 수 없는 무언가까지 더해져 있었다. 나이에 비해 늘 어른스러웠던 그녀는, 몸가짐과 대화 양쪽에서 일종의 침착함을 얻어, 실제보다 더 세상물정에 밝은 여인처럼 보였다. 하지만 옛날의 발끈하는 성미가 이따금 비쳤고, 강한 의지는 여전히 제 몫을 지켰으며, 타고난 솔직함은 외국식 세련됨에도 망가지지 않고 그대로였다.\n\n로리가 그녀의 공작새 먹이 주는 모습을 지켜보는 동안 이 모든 걸 다 읽어 낸 건 아니었다. 하지만 흐뭇하고 흥미를 느낄 만큼은 보았고, 햇살 속에 서 있는 환한 얼굴의 아가씨라는 어여쁜 작은 그림 하나를 마음에 담아 갔다. 햇살이 그녀의 옷의 부드러운 빛깔과 뺨의 싱그러운 혈색, 머리카락의 금빛 윤기를 도드라지게 했고, 그 즐거운 풍경 속에서 그녀를 단연 돋보이는 인물로 만들어 주었다.\n\n언덕 꼭대기를 이루는 돌 고원에 올라서자, 에이미는 그를 자기가 좋아하는 곳으로 맞아들이듯 손을 흔들며, 여기저기를 가리키면서 말했다.\n\n\"대성당이랑 코르소 거리, 만에서 그물을 끌어올리는 어부들, 빌라프랑카로 가는 사랑스러운 길, 바로 저 아래 슈베르트 탑, 그리고 무엇보다, 저 멀리 바다에 코르시카라고들 하는 저 점, 기억나?\"\n\n\"기억나. 별로 변한 게 없네.\" 그가 별다른 감흥 없이 대답했다.\n\n\"조 언니가 저 유명한 점을 한 번 볼 수 있다면 뭐든 다 내놓을 텐데!\" 에이미가 말했다. 기분이 좋았고, 그도 그렇기를 바랐다.\n\n\"그래.\" 그가 한 말이라곤 그게 다였지만, 몸을 돌려 눈을 가늘게 뜨고 그 섬을 바라보았다. 이제는 나폴레옹보다 더 큰 찬탈자가 그의 눈에 그 섬을 흥미롭게 만들어 놓은 것이었다.\n\n\"언니를 생각해서 잘 봐 둬. 그리고 와서 그동안 뭐 하고 지냈는지 다 얘기해 줘.\" 에이미가 진득하게 이야기할 채비를 하고 자리에 앉으며 말했다.\n\n하지만 그러지는 못했다. 그가 그녀 곁에 앉아 모든 질문에 거리낌 없이 답하긴 했어도, 알아낼 수 있는 거라곤 그가 대륙 여기저기를 떠돌았고 그리스에 다녀왔다는 것뿐이었다. 그렇게 한 시간을 빈둥거리다가, 두 사람은 다시 마차를 몰아 집으로 돌아왔다. 로리는 캐롤 부인에게 인사를 드린 뒤, 저녁에 다시 오겠다고 약속하며 자리를 떴다.\n\n그날 밤 에이미가 작정하고 \"치장에 공을 들였다\"는 사실은 기록해 두어야겠다. 세월과 떨어져 있던 시간은 두 젊은이 모두에게 제 일을 해 놓았다. 그녀는 옛 친구를 새로운 눈으로 보게 되었다. \"우리 꼬마\"가 아니라, 잘생기고 호감 가는 한 남자로 말이다. 그리고 그의 눈에 들고 싶다는, 지극히 자연스러운 마음을 의식하고 있었다. 에이미는 자기 장점을 알았고, 가난하고 예쁜 여자에게 큰 재산이나 다름없는 안목과 솜씨로 그것을 한껏 살려 냈다.\n\n니스에서는 탈라탕과 튈이 쌌으므로, 그녀는 그런 자리가 있을 때면 그 천으로 몸을 감쌌다. 그리고 어린 아가씨에게는 수수하게 입히는 분별 있는 영국식 유행을 따라, 싱싱한 꽃 몇 송이와 자그마한 장신구 몇 개, 온갖 앙증맞은 장치들로 매혹적인 작은 차림새를 꾸며 냈는데, 돈도 적게 들면서 효과는 그만이었다. 다만 고백하건대, 이따금 그녀 안의 예술가가 여자를 사로잡아, 고풍스러운 머리 모양과 조각상 같은 자세, 고전적인 드레이프 차림에 빠져들기도 했다. 하지만 어쩌랴, 우리 모두에겐 저마다 작은 약점이 있는 법, 그리고 그런 약점은 젊은이들에게서라면 너그러이 봐주기 쉬운 법이다. 젊은이들은 그 아리따움으로 우리 눈을 즐겁게 하고, 그 천진한 허영심으로 우리 마음을 흥겹게 해 주니까.\n\n\"내가 예뻐 보인다고 그가 생각해 줬으면 좋겠어. 그래서 집에 그렇게 전해 줬으면 좋겠어.\" 에이미가 플로의 낡은 흰 실크 무도회 드레스를 입고, 그 위를 싱그러운 일루전 천의 구름으로 덮으면서 혼잣말을 했다. 그 사이로 하얀 어깨와 금빛 머리가 더없이 예술적인 효과를 내며 드러났다. 머리는 굵은 물결과 곱슬을 한데 모아 뒤통수에 헤베 여신처럼 매듭 지은 다음, 더 손대지 않을 만큼의 분별은 있었다.\n\n\"이게 유행은 아니지만 잘 어울려. 그리고 난 나 자신을 흉하게 만들 형편이 못 되거든.\" 그녀는 최신 유행에 따라 머리를 지지거나 부풀리거나 땋으라는 권유를 받을 때면 그렇게 말하곤 했다.\n\n이 중요한 자리에 걸맞은 고급 장신구가 없었던 에이미는, 가볍고 보드라운 치맛자락을 장밋빛 진달래 송이로 잡아 묶고, 하얀 어깨를 가녀린 초록 덩굴로 둘러 감쌌다. 그림이 그려진 부츠의 기억을 떠올리며, 흰 새틴 슬리퍼를 소녀다운 만족감으로 살펴보고는, 방 안에서 혼자 샤세 스텝을 밟으며 자기의 귀족적인 발을 흐뭇하게 감상했다.\n\n\"새 부채는 내 꽃이랑 딱 어울리고, 장갑은 신통하게도 꼭 맞고, 이모님 무슈아르에 달린 진짜 레이스는 드레스 전체에 운치를 더해 주네. 코랑 입만 좀 고전적이면 더 바랄 게 없을 텐데.\" 그녀가 양손에 촛불을 하나씩 든 채, 비평하는 눈으로 자기 모습을 살피며 말했다.\n\n그런 고민에도 불구하고, 그녀는 미끄러지듯 걸어 나갈 때 유난히 흥겹고 우아해 보였다. 그녀는 좀처럼 뛰지 않았다. 자기 스타일에 어울리지 않는다고 여겼기 때문이다. 키가 큰 만큼, 까불거리거나 발랄한 것보다 위엄 있고 유노 여신 같은 분위기가 더 어울린다고 생각했다. 그녀는 로리를 기다리며 긴 응접실을 오갔고, 한번은 샹들리에 아래에 자리를 잡았는데, 그게 머리에 좋은 효과를 냈다. 그러다 다시 생각을 고쳐먹고는, 첫인상을 좋게 남기고 싶어 하는 소녀다운 마음이 부끄러운 듯, 방 반대편 끝으로 가 버렸다. 그런데 공교롭게도 그보다 더 잘한 일이 없었다. 로리가 어찌나 조용히 들어왔는지 그녀가 그 소리를 듣지 못한 것이다. 그녀가 멀찍한 창가에 고개를 반쯤 돌리고 한 손으로 드레스 자락을 그러쥔 채 서 있는 모습은, 붉은 커튼을 배경으로 한 그 가녀린 흰 자태가 자리를 잘 잡은 조각상만큼이나 인상적이었다.\n\n\"안녕, 디아나!\" 로리가 말했다. 그가 그녀에게 눈길을 둘 때면 그녀가 보고 싶어 하던 바로 그 흐뭇한 표정이었다.\n\n\"안녕, 아폴로!\" 그녀도 미소로 화답했다. 그 역시 유난히 멋스러워 보였다. 이렇게 풍채 좋은 남자의 팔짱을 끼고 무도회장에 들어선다는 생각에, 에이미는 그 수수한 데이비스 자매 네 사람이 진심으로 가엾게 느껴질 정도였다.\n\n\"네 꽃 여기 있어. 너는 해나가 '꽃다발 잡탕'이라고 부르는 그런 걸 싫어하던 걸 기억하고 내가 직접 만들었어.\" 로리가 가녀린 꽃다발을 건네며 말했다. 그것을 꽂은 받침은 그녀가 카르딜리아 가게 진열장을 날마다 지나치면서 오랫동안 탐내던 것이었다.\n\n\"어쩜 이렇게 다정하니!\" 그녀가 고마워하며 외쳤다. \"네가 오는 줄 알았으면 오늘 너한테 줄 걸 미리 준비해 뒀을 텐데. 이것만큼 예쁘진 못했겠지만.\"\n\n\"고마워. 제대로 된 모양새는 아니지만, 네가 더 보기 좋게 만들었네.\" 그녀가 손목에 은팔찌를 채우는 걸 보며 그가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"제발 그러지 마.\"\n\n\"넌 그런 말 좋아하는 줄 알았는데?\"\n\n\"너한테 듣는 건 아니야. 너답지가 않아서, 차라리 예전의 그 무뚝뚝함이 더 좋아.\"\n\n\"그렇다니 다행이네.\" 그가 안도하는 표정으로 대답했다. 그러고는 예전에 함께 집에서 파티에 가던 때처럼, 그녀의 장갑 단추를 채워 주고 자기 넥타이가 똑바른지 물었다.\n\n그날 밤 길쭉한 식당에 모인 사람들은, 유럽 대륙이 아니고서는 어디서도 볼 수 없는 부류였다. 손님 대접을 좋아하는 미국인들이 니스에서 아는 사람이란 사람은 죄다 초대했고, 작위에 대한 거부감이 없었던 터라, 크리스마스 무도회에 광채를 더하려고 작위 있는 인사 몇 명도 확보해 두었다.\n\n러시아 공작 한 명이 황송하게도 한 시간 동안 구석에 앉아, 햄릿의 어머니처럼 검은 벨벳을 두르고 턱 밑에 진주 끈을 늘어뜨린 풍채 좋은 부인과 이야기를 나누었다. 열여덟 살 난 폴란드 백작은 숙녀들에게 헌신적으로 굴어, 숙녀들로부터 \"매력 넘치는 사람\"이라는 평을 받았고, 독일의 무슨 전하라는 인사는 오로지 저녁 식사 때문에 와서, 뭘 먹어 치울까 두리번거리며 정처 없이 돌아다녔다. 로스차일드 남작의 개인 비서인, 꽉 끼는 부츠를 신은 코 큰 유대인은, 마치 주인의 이름이 자기 머리에 황금빛 후광을 씌워 주기라도 한 양 세상을 향해 상냥하게 미소를 흘렸다. 황제와 안면이 있다는 뚱뚱한 프랑스인은 춤에 미친 자기 취미를 마음껏 풀러 왔고, 영국 부인인 더 존스 부인은 여덟 식구나 되는 단란한 가족으로 그 자리를 빛냈다. 물론 발놀림 가볍고 목소리 새된 미국 아가씨들도 많았고, 잘생겼지만 생기 없어 보이는 영국 아가씨들도 그만큼 있었으며, 수수해도 톡톡 튀는 프랑스 아가씨 몇몇도 있었다. 또 여느 때처럼 여행 중인 젊은 신사들 무리가 흥겹게 즐겼고, 온갖 나라의 어머니들이 벽가에 늘어서서 제 딸들과 춤추는 청년들을 인자하게 바라보며 미소 지었다.\n\n그날 밤 로리의 팔에 기대어 \"무대에 오른\" 순간 에이미가 어떤 심정이었을지는, 어떤 젊은 아가씨든 능히 상상할 수 있으리라. 그녀는 자기가 예뻐 보인다는 걸 알았고, 춤추기를 좋아했으며, 무도회장에서는 제 세상에 있는 듯한 기분이 들었다. 그리고 젊은 아가씨가 미모와 젊음, 여성다움이라는 권능으로 다스리도록 태어난 그 새롭고 사랑스러운 왕국을 처음 발견했을 때 찾아오는, 그 짜릿한 권력의 감각을 만끽했다. 어색하고 수수한 데다, 무뚝뚝한 아버지와 그보다 더 무뚝뚝한 노처녀 이모 셋 말고는 함께해 줄 사람도 없는 데이비스네 아가씨들이 그녀는 정말 가여웠다. 그래서 지나치면서 더없이 다정한 태도로 그들에게 인사를 건넸다. 친절한 일이었다. 덕분에 그들은 그녀의 드레스를 구경할 수 있었고, 저 기품 있어 보이는 친구가 대체 누구인지 궁금해서 애가 탔으니까. 악단이 첫 곡을 터뜨리자 에이미의 얼굴에는 혈색이 돌고, 두 눈은 반짝이기 시작했으며, 두 발은 조바심을 내며 바닥을 톡톡 두드렸다. 그녀는 춤을 잘 추었고, 로리에게 그걸 알려 주고 싶었다. 그러니 그가 더없이 태평한 어조로 이렇게 물었을 때 그녀가 받은 충격은, 말로 설명하기보다 그저 상상하는 편이 나을 것이다.\n\n\"춤추고 싶어?\"\n\n\"무도회에서야 보통 그렇지.\"\n\n그녀의 놀란 표정과 톡 쏘는 대답에, 로리는 서둘러 자기 실수를 만회했다.\n\n\"첫 춤 말이야. 영광을 베풀어 주겠어?\"\n\n\"백작님과의 약속을 미루면 한 곡 줄 수는 있어. 그분은 춤을 기막히게 잘 추거든. 그래도 네가 오랜 친구니까 양해해 주실 거야.\" 에이미가 말했다. 그 이름이 좋은 효과를 내서, 자기를 함부로 대해선 안 된다는 걸 로리가 알아주기를 바라면서.\n\n\"착한 꼬마긴 한데, '여신의 따님, 신처럼 키 크고, 더없이 신처럼 어여쁜' 이를 받쳐 주기엔 키가 좀 짧은 폴란드 사람이로군.\"\n\n하지만 그녀가 얻은 만족이라곤 그게 다였다.\n\n두 사람이 끼게 된 조는 영국 사람들로 짜여 있어, 에이미는 코티용을 점잖게 추며 걸어 나갈 수밖에 없었다. 그러면서도 내내, 신이 나서 타란텔라라도 출 수 있을 것 같은 기분이었다. 로리는 그녀를 \"착한 꼬마\"에게 넘기고, 앞으로의 즐거움을 위해 에이미를 미리 잡아 두지도 않은 채, 플로에 대한 의무를 다하러 가 버렸다. 이 괘씸하리만치 앞을 못 내다본 처사는 마땅한 벌을 받았다. 에이미가 그 즉시 저녁 식사 때까지 춤 상대를 다 정해 버렸기 때문이다. 단, 그가 그때 가서 뉘우치는 기색이라도 보이면 마음을 누그러뜨릴 셈이었다. 그가 다음 곡, 그 멋진 폴카 르도바를 추자며 달려오기는커녕 어슬렁어슬렁 다가왔을 때, 그녀는 새침한 만족감으로 자기 무도회 수첩을 보여 주었다. 하지만 그의 정중한 사과는 그녀를 속이지 못했고, 그녀가 백작과 함께 미끄러져 가는데, 로리가 실제로 안도하는 표정을 지으며 이모 곁에 앉는 게 보였다.\n\n그건 용서할 수 없는 일이었다. 그래서 에이미는 한참 동안 그에게 더는 눈길을 주지 않았다. 다만 춤과 춤 사이에, 핀이 필요하거나 잠깐 쉬려고 보호자인 이모에게 올 때 이따금 한마디씩 건넬 뿐이었다. 그래도 그녀의 분노는 좋은 효과를 냈다. 미소 띤 얼굴 뒤에 그것을 감추니, 유난히 명랑하고 눈부셔 보였던 것이다. 로리의 눈길이 흐뭇하게 그녀를 좇았다. 그녀는 까불지도 어슬렁대지도 않고, 활기와 기품을 담아 춤추며, 그 즐거운 놀이를 본디 그래야 할 모습으로 만들어 냈다. 그는 아주 자연스럽게 이 새로운 시각에서 그녀를 살펴보기 시작했고, 저녁이 반도 지나기 전에 \"어린 에이미가 아주 매력적인 여인이 되겠다\"고 단정 짓고 말았다.\n\n활기 넘치는 광경이었다. 이내 사교철의 흥취가 모두를 사로잡았고, 크리스마스의 흥겨움이 모든 얼굴을 빛내고 마음을 즐겁게 하고 발걸음을 가볍게 했다. 악사들은 바이올린을 켜고 나팔을 불고 북을 두드리며 신이 난 듯했다. 출 수 있는 사람은 다 춤을 췄고, 못 추는 사람은 보기 드문 열의로 옆 사람을 찬탄했다. 데이비스 사람들로 공기가 어두컴컴할 지경이었고, 수많은 존스 사람들은 어린 기린 떼처럼 깡충거렸다. 금빛 비서는 분홍 새틴 자락으로 바닥을 쓸고 다니는 화려한 프랑스 여인과 함께 유성처럼 방 안을 누볐다. 점잖은 독일 사람은 만찬 식탁을 찾아내어 행복해했는데, 차림표를 처음부터 끝까지 꾸준히 먹어 치우며, 그 약탈상에 급사들을 아연실색하게 만들었다. 그러나 황제의 친구는 영광을 한 몸에 뒤집어썼다. 그는 아는 춤이든 모르는 춤이든 가리지 않고 죄다 췄고, 스텝이 헷갈릴 때면 즉흥적으로 피루엣을 끼워 넣었던 것이다. 그 뚱뚱한 남자의 소년 같은 거침없음은 보고 있기에 흐뭇했다. \"몸집은 묵직해도\" 고무공처럼 춤을 췄으니까. 그는 달리고, 날고, 껑충거렸다. 얼굴은 달아오르고 벗어진 머리는 번들거렸으며, 연미복 자락은 사납게 펄럭이고 무도화는 정말이지 공중에서 반짝거렸다. 음악이 멈추자 그는 이마의 땀방울을 훔치고는, 안경 없는 프랑스판 피크위크처럼 동료 인간들을 향해 환하게 웃었다.\n\n에이미와 그녀의 폴란드 사람은 똑같은 열의에, 더 우아한 날렵함으로 단연 돋보였다. 로리는 자기도 모르게, 날개라도 단 듯 지칠 줄 모르고 휙휙 스쳐 지나가는 흰 슬리퍼가 율동적으로 오르락내리락하는 데 맞춰 박자를 타고 있었다. 마침내 어린 블라디미르가 \"이렇게 일찍 떠나야 하다니 비통하다\"는 인사를 남기고 그녀를 놓아주자, 에이미는 쉬면서, 자기를 배신했던 기사가 벌을 어떻게 견뎌 냈는지 살펴볼 채비가 되어 있었다. 벌은 효과가 있었다. 스물세 살 나이에는 시든 애정도 다정한 사교 모임에서 위안을 얻는 법이고, 젊은 신경은 짜릿하게 떨리고 젊은 피는 춤추며 건강한 젊은 기운은 솟구치게 마련이니, 미모와 빛과 음악과 율동의 마법에 걸리면 더더욱 그렇다. 로리는 자리를 내주려고 일어서는데 어딘가 활기를 되찾은 얼굴이었다. 그가 저녁거리를 가지러 서둘러 떠나자, 그녀는 만족스러운 미소를 지으며 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n'아, 저게 도움이 될 줄 알았어!'\n\n\"넌 발자크의 '자화상을 그린 여인' 같아.\" 그가 한 손으로는 그녀에게 부채질을 하고 다른 손으로는 그녀의 커피잔을 든 채 말했다.\n\n\"내 볼연지는 안 지워져.\" 에이미가 발그레한 뺨을 문지르고는, 진지하고 천연덕스러운 태도로 흰 장갑을 그에게 보여 주었다. 그 바람에 그는 그만 소리 내어 웃고 말았다.\n\n\"이 천은 뭐라고 부르는 거야?\" 그가 자기 무릎 위로 흘러내린 그녀의 드레스 주름을 만지며 물었다.\n\n\"일루전.\"\n\n\"이름 한번 잘 지었네. 아주 예쁜데, 새로 나온 거야?\"\n\n\"산만큼 오래된 거야. 수많은 여자애들이 입은 걸 봤으면서, 이제껏 그게 예쁜지도 몰랐던 거잖아. 멍청이!\"\n\n\"네가 입은 건 처음 봤거든. 그래서 못 알아본 거지, 알겠어?\"\n\n\"그런 말 마, 금지야. 지금은 칭찬보다 커피가 낫겠어. 아니, 그렇게 늘어져 있지 마, 신경 쓰이니까.\"\n\n로리는 등을 꼿꼿이 세우고 얌전히 그녀의 빈 접시를 받아 들었다. \"어린 에이미\"에게 이래라저래라 부림을 당하는 게 묘하게 즐거웠다. 그녀는 이제 수줍음을 떨쳐 냈고, 그를 짓밟고 싶은 거부할 수 없는 욕구를 느끼고 있었다. 만물의 주인이라는 남자들이 조금이라도 굴복하는 기색을 보이면, 여자들이 흐뭇하게 그렇게 굴게 마련인 것처럼.\n\n\"이런 건 다 어디서 배운 거야?\" 그가 짓궂은 표정으로 물었다.\n\n\"'이런 거'라는 게 좀 애매한 표현인데, 친절하게 설명 좀 해 줄래?\" 에이미가 받아쳤다. 그가 무슨 뜻인지 뻔히 알면서도, 짓궂게도 형용할 수 없는 것을 그더러 설명해 보라고 떠넘긴 것이다.\n\n\"그게, 전체적인 분위기랄까, 스타일이랄까, 침착함이랄까, 그, 그, 일루전 같은 거, 알잖아.\" 로리가 더듬거리며 웃다가, 그 새 단어로 곤경에서 빠져나왔다.\n\n에이미는 흐뭇했지만, 당연히 그 내색은 하지 않고 새침하게 대꾸했다. \"외국 생활은 본인 뜻과 상관없이 사람을 세련되게 만들거든. 난 노는 만큼 공부도 해. 그리고 이건,\" 드레스 쪽으로 가볍게 손짓하며, \"글쎄, 튈은 싸고, 꽃은 거저 얻을 수 있고, 난 가진 게 변변찮으니 그걸 최대한 살리는 데 이골이 났거든.\"\n\n에이미는 마지막 문장이 좀 후회스러웠다. 품위 없게 들렸을까 봐 걱정스러웠던 것이다. 하지만 로리는 그 말 때문에 그녀가 더 좋아졌다. 기회를 최대한 살려 내는 그 씩씩한 인내심과, 가난을 꽃으로 덮어 가리는 그 명랑한 기상에 감탄하는 동시에 존경하는 마음이 일었다. 에이미는 그가 왜 그렇게 다정하게 자기를 바라보는지, 왜 자기 무도회 수첩을 자기 이름으로 다 채우는지, 왜 그날 밤 남은 시간 동안 더없이 즐거운 태도로 자기에게만 매달리는지 알지 못했다. 하지만 이 유쾌한 변화를 빚어낸 충동은, 두 사람이 알지 못하는 사이에 서로 주고받고 있던 그 새로운 인상들 중 하나가 빚어낸 결과였다.\n선반 위에 얹혀\n\n# ON THE SHELF.\n\n프랑스에서 젊은 아가씨들은 결혼하기 전까지 따분한 시절을 보내다가, 결혼하고 나서야 \"자유 만세!\"가 그들의 좌우명이 된다. 미국에서는, 누구나 알다시피, 아가씨들이 일찌감치 독립 선언서에 서명하고 공화주의자의 열의로 자유를 누린다. 하지만 젊은 새댁들은 대개 왕위를 이을 첫 후계자가 생기면 자리에서 물러나, 프랑스 수녀원만큼이나 폐쇄적인—물론 결코 그만큼 조용하지는 않은—은둔 생활로 들어간다. 좋든 싫든 그들은 결혼식의 들뜬 기분이 가시기가 무섭게 사실상 선반 위에 얹히고 마는데, 그들 대부분은 얼마 전 어느 무척 예쁜 여인이 한 말처럼 외칠 만하다. \"난 여전히 예쁜데, 결혼했다고 아무도 날 거들떠보지 않아.\"\n\n미인도, 멋쟁이 부인도 아니었던 메그는 아기들이 한 살이 될 때까지는 이런 시련을 겪지 않았다. 그녀의 작은 세상에서는 소박한 관습이 통했고, 그녀는 자기가 그 어느 때보다 더 칭송받고 사랑받는다는 걸 알게 되었기 때문이다.\n\n여성스러운 작은 여인이었던 메그는 모성 본능이 무척 강했고, 다른 모든 것과 모든 이를 완전히 제쳐 둔 채 아이들에게만 온통 빠져 있었다. 밤낮으로 지칠 줄 모르는 헌신과 노심초사로 아이들 위에 마음을 졸였고, 존은 도우미의 손길에 내맡겨 두었다. 이제 부엌은 아일랜드 여인이 맡아 꾸리고 있었으니 말이다. 가정적인 남자였던 존은 그동안 으레 받아 오던 아내의 보살핌이 못내 아쉬웠다. 하지만 아기들을 끔찍이 사랑했으므로, 머잖아 평온이 되돌아오리라는 남자다운 무지로, 한동안은 기꺼이 자기 안락을 양보했다. 그러나 석 달이 지나도록 평온은 돌아오지 않았다. 메그는 지치고 신경이 곤두선 얼굴이었고, 아기들이 그녀의 일분일초를 다 앗아 갔으며, 집안은 방치되었고, \"느긋하게\" 사는 요리사 키티는 그를 변변찮은 식사로 굶주리게 했다. 아침에 나갈 때면 그는 집에 갇힌 엄마가 시키는 자질구레한 심부름들로 어리둥절했고, 밤에 식구들을 안고 싶어 신이 나서 들어오면 \"쉿! 온종일 보채다가 이제 막 잠들었어요\"라는 말에 김이 샜다. 집에서 가볍게 즐길 거리를 제안하면 \"안 돼요, 아기들이 깨요\"였고, 강연이나 음악회 얘기를 슬쩍 꺼내면 책망 어린 눈빛과 함께 \"재미 보겠다고 내 아이들을 두고 가라고요? 절대 안 돼요!\"라는 단호한 대답이 돌아왔다. 그의 잠은 아기들의 울음소리와, 밤의 정적 속에서 소리 없이 이리저리 거니는 유령 같은 형상의 환영에 깨지기 일쑤였다. 식사는, 위층 둥지에서 희미한 칭얼거림이라도 들려오면 음식을 반쯤 덜다 말고 그를 버려두고 달려가는 살림의 여신 때문에 번번이 끊겼다. 저녁에 신문을 읽으려 들면, 데미의 배앓이가 선적 명세란에 끼어들고 데이지의 넘어진 일이 주가에 영향을 미쳤다. 브룩 부인의 관심사라곤 오로지 집안 소식뿐이었으니까. 가엾은 남자는 무척 불편했다. 아이들이 그에게서 아내를 앗아 가 버린 것이다. 집은 한낱 육아실이 되어 버렸고, 끊임없는 \"쉿\" 소리에 그는 베이비랜드라는 그 신성한 영역에 발을 들일 때마다 자기가 무지막지한 침입자가 된 기분이었다. 여섯 달 동안 그는 무척이나 참을성 있게 견뎠다. 그러나 나아질 기미가 보이지 않자, 다른 아버지 유배자들이 하는 대로 했다. 다른 데서나마 약간의 위안을 얻으려 한 것이다. 스콧이 결혼해서 멀지 않은 곳에 살림을 차렸던 터라, 존은 자기 집 거실이 텅 비고 아내가 끝도 없을 듯한 자장가나 부르고 있는 저녁이면, 한두 시간씩 그 집에 건너가 지내는 버릇이 들었다. 스콧 부인은 발랄하고 예쁜 여인으로, 상냥하게 구는 것 말고는 할 일이 없었고, 그 소임을 더없이 멋지게 해냈다. 거실은 늘 환하고 매력적이었으며, 체스판이 준비되어 있고, 피아노는 잘 조율되어 있었으며, 흥겨운 잡담이 넘쳐 났고, 군침 도는 차림새의 아담한 저녁 식사가 차려졌다.\n\n자기 집 난롯가가 그토록 외롭지만 않았다면 존은 그쪽을 더 좋아했을 것이다. 하지만 사정이 사정인지라, 그는 차선의 것을 고맙게 받아들이며 이웃의 사교를 즐겼다.\n\n메그도 처음에는 이 새로운 형편을 제법 반겼다. 존이 거실에서 꾸벅꾸벅 졸거나, 집 안을 쿵쿵 돌아다니며 아이들을 깨우는 대신 즐거운 시간을 보낸다고 생각하니 마음이 놓였던 것이다. 그러나 이윽고 이앓이로 인한 걱정이 끝나고, 그 작은 우상들이 적당한 시간에 잠들어 엄마에게 쉴 틈이 생기자, 메그는 존이 그리워지기 시작했다. 그가 낡은 실내복 차림으로 맞은편에 앉아, 난로 망에 슬리퍼를 편안하게 그슬리고 있지 않으니, 바느질 바구니가 따분한 벗으로 느껴졌다. 그렇다고 그에게 집에 있어 달라고 청하지는 않았다. 다만 말하지 않아도 자기가 그를 원하는 걸 그가 알아주지 못하니 서운했다. 그가 헛되이 자기를 기다린 그 숱한 저녁들은 까맣게 잊은 채로 말이다. 그녀는 아이를 지켜보고 노심초사하느라 신경이 곤두서고 진이 빠진 상태였고, 집안일에 짓눌릴 때면 더없이 훌륭한 어머니들조차 이따금 빠지는 그 불합리한 심정에 사로잡혀 있었다. 운동 부족은 그들에게서 명랑함을 앗아 가고, 미국 여인들의 우상인 찻주전자에 지나치게 빠지다 보면 마치 온몸이 신경 덩어리일 뿐 근육이라곤 없는 것처럼 느껴지게 만든다.\n\n\"그래.\" 그녀는 거울을 들여다보며 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"난 늙고 추해지고 있어. 존은 더 이상 날 흥미로워하지 않으니까, 빛바랜 아내를 두고 거치적거릴 게 없는 예쁜 이웃을 보러 가는 거야. 그래도 아기들은 날 사랑하지. 내가 마르고 창백하든, 머리 지질 틈이 없든 신경 안 쓰니까. 얘들은 내 위안이야. 그리고 언젠가는 존도 내가 얘들을 위해 무얼 기꺼이 희생했는지 알게 되겠지, 그렇지, 우리 아가?\"\n\n그 애처로운 하소연에 데이지는 옹알이로, 데미는 까르르 소리로 답해 주었고, 그러면 메그는 한탄을 접어 두고 모성의 향연에 빠져들었다. 그것이 그때만큼은 그녀의 외로움을 달래 주었다. 하지만 정치가 존을 사로잡으면서 고통은 깊어 갔다. 그는 흥미로운 화젯거리를 의논하러 늘 스콧네로 건너갔고, 메그가 자기를 그리워하는 줄은 전혀 알지 못했다. 그래도 그녀는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 어느 날 어머니가 그녀가 눈물짓는 걸 발견하고는, 대체 무슨 일이냐며 한사코 알려 들기 전까지는. 메그의 풀 죽은 기색이 어머니의 눈을 비껴가지는 못했던 것이다.\n\n\"엄마 말고는 아무한테도 말 안 할 거예요. 하지만 정말 조언이 필요해서요. 존이 이런 식으로 계속 나가다간, 저는 차라리 과부가 되는 편이 낫겠어요.\" 브룩 부인이 서러운 기색으로, 데이지의 턱받이로 눈물을 닦으며 대답했다.\n\n\"어떤 식으로 나가는데, 얘야?\" 어머니가 걱정스레 물었다.\n\n\"온종일 나가 있고, 밤에 제가 보고 싶을 때는 끊임없이 스콧네로 건너가요. 가장 힘든 일은 제가 다 떠맡고, 즐길 거리라곤 하나도 없다니 너무 불공평해요. 남자들은 정말 이기적이에요. 아무리 좋은 남자라도요.\"\n\n\"여자들도 마찬가지란다. 네가 어디서 잘못하고 있는지 살펴보기 전에는 존을 탓하지 마라.\"\n\n\"하지만 저를 소홀히 하는 건 옳을 리 없잖아요.\"\n\n\"넌 그이를 소홀히 하지 않았고?\"\n\n\"어머, 엄마, 전 엄마가 제 편을 들어 주실 줄 알았어요!\"\n\n\"그래, 마음으로 헤아려 주는 거라면 네 편이야. 하지만 잘못은 너한테 있는 것 같구나, 메그.\"\n\n\"어째서 그런지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"내가 짚어 주마. 네가 저녁마다—그이한테는 유일한 여가 시간이지—꼬박꼬박 곁을 지켜 주던 동안에는, 네 말마따나 존이 너를 소홀히 한 적이 있었니?\"\n\n\"아니요. 하지만 이제는 돌볼 아기가 둘이나 되는데 그럴 수가 없잖아요.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔 할 수 있을 것 같구나, 얘야. 그리고 마땅히 그래야 한다고 생각해. 아주 솔직하게 말해도 되겠니? 그리고 마음으로 헤아려 주는 것도 엄마지만, 나무라는 것도 엄마라는 걸 기억해 주겠니?\"\n\n\"그럼요! 제가 다시 어린 메그가 된 것처럼 말씀해 주세요. 이 아기들이 모든 걸 저한테 기대게 되면서, 저는 그 어느 때보다 더 가르침이 필요한 것 같은 기분이 자주 들거든요.\"\n\n메그는 낮은 의자를 어머니 곁으로 끌어다 놓았고, 두 여인은 각자 무릎에 아기를 하나씩 안은 채 이따금 방해를 받아 가며, 다정하게 흔들고 이야기를 나누었다. 모성이라는 끈이 그 어느 때보다 둘을 하나로 묶어 주는 것을 느끼면서.\n\n\"넌 그저 젊은 아내들 대부분이 저지르는 실수를 했을 뿐이야. 아이들을 향한 사랑에 빠져 남편에 대한 도리를 잊은 거지. 아주 자연스럽고 용서받을 만한 실수란다, 메그. 하지만 네가 영영 다른 길로 들어서기 전에 바로잡는 편이 좋겠어. 아이들이란 마치 다 네 것이고 존은 그저 먹여 살리기만 하면 된다는 듯이 너희를 갈라놓는 게 아니라, 그 어느 때보다 너희를 가까이 끌어당겨 줘야 하는 거니까. 몇 주 전부터 지켜봤지만, 때가 되면 바로잡히려니 싶어 아무 말 안 했단다.\"\n\n\"바로잡힐 것 같지가 않아요. 제가 집에 있어 달라고 하면 그이는 제가 질투한다고 여길 거예요. 그런 생각으로 그이를 모욕하고 싶진 않아요. 그이는 제가 자길 원하는 걸 모르고, 저는 말하지 않고 어떻게 전해야 할지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"집을 너무 즐거운 곳으로 만들어서, 그이가 나가고 싶지 않게 하렴. 얘야, 그이는 자기의 작은 집을 그리워하고 있단다. 하지만 네가 없으면 집이 아니고, 너는 늘 육아실에만 있잖니.\"\n\n\"제가 거기 있으면 안 되나요?\"\n\n\"내내 있어선 안 되지. 너무 갇혀 지내면 신경이 곤두서고, 그러면 무엇 하나 제대로 할 수 없게 돼. 게다가 너는 아기들한테 못지않게 존한테도 갚아야 할 게 있단다. 아이들 때문에 남편을 소홀히 하지 말고, 그이를 육아실에서 밀어내지 말고, 거기서 거들 줄 알도록 가르치렴. 그이의 자리도 네 자리만큼이나 거기 있고, 아이들에게도 아빠가 필요해. 그이도 자기 몫이 있다는 걸 느끼게 해 줘. 그러면 기꺼이, 성실하게 해낼 거고, 너희 모두에게 더 나을 거야.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇게 생각하세요, 엄마?\"\n\n\"난 안단다, 메그. 직접 해 봤으니까. 그리고 엄마는 효과를 입증해 보지 않은 조언은 좀처럼 하지 않아. 너랑 조가 어렸을 때, 나도 꼭 지금 너처럼 했단다. 너희한테 온전히 매달리지 않으면 도리를 다하지 못하는 것 같았거든. 가엾은 네 아빠는, 내가 도와주겠다는 손길을 다 마다하자 책으로 파고들었고, 나는 혼자서 그 시도를 떠안게 됐지. 나름대로 안간힘을 썼지만, 조는 내게 너무 벅찼어. 응석을 받아 주다 그 애를 망칠 뻔했지. 너는 몸이 약했고, 나는 너를 걱정하다 그만 내가 앓아눕고 말았어. 그제야 네 아빠가 나서서, 모든 걸 조용히 꾸려 나가며 어찌나 큰 힘이 되어 주던지, 나는 내 잘못을 깨달았고, 그 후로는 그이 없이 살아갈 수 없게 되었단다. 그게 우리 집 행복의 비결이야. 그이는 일 때문에 우리 모두에게 영향을 미치는 자질구레한 보살핌과 의무에서 멀어지는 법이 없고, 나도 집안 걱정 때문에 그이의 일에 대한 관심을 저버리지 않으려 애쓰지. 많은 일들을 각자 따로 해내지만, 집에서는 늘 함께 일한단다.\"\n\n\"맞아요, 엄마. 제 큰 소원은 엄마가 아빠랑 우리에게 그래 주신 것처럼, 저도 제 남편과 아이들에게 그런 사람이 되는 거예요. 어떻게 하면 되는지 알려 주세요. 엄마가 말씀하시는 건 뭐든 할게요.\"\n\n\"넌 늘 엄마 말 잘 듣는 딸이었지. 그럼, 얘야, 내가 너라면, 존이 데미를 돌보는 일에 더 관여하게 하겠어. 그 애한텐 가르침이 필요한데, 지금 시작해도 결코 이른 게 아니거든. 그리고 내가 자주 권하던 대로, 해나를 불러 너를 돕게 하겠어. 해나는 일등 유모니까, 네가 집안일을 더 하는 동안 그 소중한 아기들을 믿고 맡길 수 있어. 너한텐 운동이 필요하고, 해나는 그 쉬는 일을 즐거워할 거고, 존은 잃어버린 아내를 되찾게 되겠지. 바깥출입을 더 하렴. 바쁘게 지내되 명랑하게도 지내야 해. 너는 이 집의 햇살을 만드는 사람이고, 네가 우울해지면 맑은 날이 없는 셈이니까. 그리고 존이 좋아하는 건 뭐든 관심을 가지려고 해 봐. 그이와 이야기를 나누고, 그이가 너한테 책을 읽어 주게 하고, 생각을 주고받고, 그렇게 서로 도우렴. 여자라는 이유로 작은 상자 속에 너를 가두지 말고, 세상이 어떻게 돌아가는지 알고, 세상일에서 네 몫을 해낼 수 있도록 너 자신을 갈고닦으렴. 그게 다 너와 네 식구에게 영향을 미치는 일이니까.\"\n\n\"존은 워낙 분별이 있어서, 제가 정치 같은 걸 물으면 절 멍청하다고 여길까 봐 걱정이에요.\"\n\n\"난 그러지 않을 거라고 믿어. 사랑은 숱한 허물을 덮어 주는 법이고, 그이만큼 네가 거리낌 없이 물을 수 있는 사람이 또 어디 있겠니? 한번 해 보렴. 그이가 스콧 부인의 저녁 식사보다 네 곁을 훨씬 더 좋아하게 되는지 어떤지 두고 보자.\"\n\n\"그럴게요. 가엾은 존! 제가 그이를 너무 슬프게 소홀히 했나 봐요. 그래도 전 제가 옳은 줄 알았고, 그이는 한 번도 아무 말 안 했거든요.\"\n\n\"그이는 이기적이지 않으려고 애썼지만, 꽤 쓸쓸했을 거야, 아마. 메그, 지금이 바로 그때란다. 젊은 부부가 서로 멀어지기 쉬운 때이자, 그래서 가장 함께해야 할 때이지. 첫 다정함은 정성껏 지켜 내지 않으면 금세 닳아 없어지고 마니까. 그리고 부모에게는 길러 내라고 주어진 그 작은 생명들의 첫 몇 해만큼 아름답고 소중한 때가 없단다. 존이 아기들한테 낯선 사람이 되게 하지 마. 이 시험과 유혹의 세상에서 아이들만큼 그이를 안전하고 행복하게 지켜 줄 건 또 없거든. 그리고 아이들을 통해 너희 둘은 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 서로를 알고 사랑하는 법을 배우게 될 거야. 자, 얘야, 이제 가 봐야겠다. 엄마의 잔소리를 잘 곱씹어 보고, 좋을 것 같으면 그대로 해 보렴. 그리고 너희 모두에게 하느님의 축복이 함께하기를!\"\n\n메그는 그 말을 곱씹어 보았고, 좋은 조언이라 여겨 실행에 옮겼다. 비록 첫 시도가 그녀가 계획한 그대로 이루어지지는 않았지만 말이다. 물론 아이들은 그녀에게 횡포를 부렸고, 발버둥 치고 악을 쓰면 원하는 건 뭐든 얻어 낼 수 있다는 걸 알게 되자마자 온 집안을 쥐락펴락했다. 엄마는 그들의 변덕에 굽실거리는 노예였지만, 아빠는 그리 쉽게 굴복하지 않았다. 그래서 이따금 떼쓰는 아들을 아버지답게 다잡으려 들어, 다정한 아내를 괴롭게 했다. 데미는 제 아비의 굳은 성품을—고집이라고는 부르지 말자—조금 물려받았던 터라, 일단 작은 마음을 정하고 무얼 가지거나 하겠다고 들면, 임금님의 말도, 임금님의 신하들도 그 옹고집의 작은 마음을 바꿀 수 없었다. 엄마는 우리 귀한 애가 편견을 이겨 내도록 가르치기엔 아직 너무 어리다고 여겼지만, 아빠는 순종을 배우는 데 이른 때란 결코 없다고 믿었다. 그리하여 데미 도련님은, \"아빠\"와 \"씨름\"을 하려 들 때면 늘 자기가 지고 만다는 걸 일찍이 깨달았다. 그러면서도 영국 사람처럼, 우리 아기는 자기를 이긴 사람을 존경했고, 엄마의 그 어떤 사랑의 토닥임보다도 그 진중한 \"안 돼, 안 돼\"가 더 인상적인 아빠를 사랑했다.\n\n어머니와 이야기를 나눈 며칠 뒤, 메그는 존과 함께 단란한 저녁 한때를 보내 보기로 마음먹었다. 그래서 맛깔난 저녁을 차리고, 거실을 정돈하고, 예쁘게 차려입고, 아이들을 일찍 재워, 자기 시도에 아무것도 끼어들지 못하게 했다. 하지만 공교롭게도 데미의 가장 꺾기 힘든 고집은 잠자리에 드는 것에 대한 거부였고, 그날 밤 그 애는 한바탕 난리를 치기로 작정했다. 그래서 가엾은 메그는 노래를 부르고 흔들어 주고, 이야기를 들려주고, 잠을 부를 만한 온갖 꾀를 다 부려 보았지만, 죄다 허사였다. 그 큰 눈은 도무지 감기지 않았다. 그리고 천성이 좋은, 통통하고 자그마한 덩어리인 데이지가 곤히 잠든 지 한참이 지나도록, 짓궂은 데미는 더없이 맥 빠지게 말짱한 표정으로 불빛을 빤히 쳐다보며 누워 있었다.\n\n\"데미가 착한 아이답게 가만히 누워 있을래? 엄마가 잠깐 내려가서 가엾은 아빠한테 차를 좀 갖다 드리는 동안에 말이야.\" 현관문이 살며시 닫히고, 귀에 익은 발소리가 발끝으로 식당으로 들어가자 메그가 물었다.\n\n\"나도 차!\" 데미가 그 잔치에 끼어들 채비를 하며 말했다.\n\n\"안 돼. 그래도 데이지처럼 코 자면, 아침에 먹게 작은 과자 좀 남겨 둘게. 그렇게 할래, 우리 아가?\"\n\n\"응!\" 데미가 두 눈을 질끈 감았다. 마치 잠을 붙들어 그 바라는 아침을 어서 오게 하려는 듯이.\n\n이 좋은 틈을 타, 메그는 살그머니 빠져나와 아래층으로 내려가, 미소 띤 얼굴로 남편을 맞았다. 머리에는 그가 유독 좋아하는 그 작은 파란 리본을 매고서. 그는 그것을 단번에 알아보고, 반갑고 놀란 기색으로 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 우리 작은 엄마, 오늘 밤 우리 참 화사한데. 손님이라도 오시나?\"\n\n\"당신뿐이에요, 여보.\"\n\n\"생일이야, 기념일이야, 아니면 무슨 날이야?\"\n\n\"아니요. 칠칠치 못한 모습이 지겨워서, 기분 전환 삼아 차려입어 봤어요. 당신은 아무리 피곤해도 식사 자리에선 늘 단정하게 차려입잖아요. 그러니 시간 있을 때 저라고 그러면 안 될 것 없죠.\"\n\n\"난 당신에 대한 예의로 그러는 거요, 여보.\" 구식인 존이 말했다.\n\n\"저도 마찬가지예요, 똑같아요, 브룩 씨.\" 메그가 웃으며, 찻주전자 너머로 그에게 고개를 끄덕였다. 다시 젊고 예뻐 보였다.\n\n\"음, 더없이 즐겁군. 꼭 옛날 같아. 이거 맛이 제대로네. 당신을 위해 건배하리다, 여보.\" 그러고 존은 차분하고 황홀한 기색으로 차를 한 모금 들이켰다. 하지만 그 황홀함은 무척 짧았다. 그가 잔을 내려놓는 순간, 문손잡이가 알 수 없이 덜그럭거리더니, 작은 목소리가 조바심을 내며 들려왔던 것이다.\n\n\"무이 여어, 내가 갈 꼬야!\"\n\n\"저 짓궂은 녀석. 혼자 자라고 일러뒀더니, 글쎄 저렇게 아래층까지 내려와서, 저 천 깔개 위를 발발거리다 감기로 죽을 작정인가 봐요.\" 메그가 그 부름에 답하며 말했다.\n\n\"이제 아침이야.\" 데미가 즐거운 목소리로 알리며 들어왔다. 긴 잠옷을 팔에 우아하게 늘어뜨리고, 식탁 주위를 깡충거리며 곱슬머리가 죄다 흥겹게 까딱이는 채로, \"과자\"를 애정 어린 눈길로 곁눈질하면서.\n\n\"아니야, 아직 아침 아니야. 잠자리에 가야지, 가엾은 엄마 괴롭히지 말고. 그러면 위에 설탕 얹은 작은 케이크를 줄게.\"\n\n\"나 아빠 조아.\" 영악한 녀석이 아버지 무릎에 기어올라 금지된 즐거움에 빠질 채비를 하며 말했다. 하지만 존은 고개를 저으며 메그에게 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 저 애한테 거기 올라가서 혼자 자라고 일렀으면, 그렇게 하게 만들어요. 안 그러면 영영 당신 말을 듣는 법을 못 배울 테니까.\"\n\n\"그래요, 맞아요. 자, 데미.\" 메그가 아들을 데리고 가는데, 곁에서 깡충거리는 그 훼방꾼 꼬마를 한 대 찰싹 때려 주고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았다. 데미는 육아실에 닿기만 하면 약속한 미끼를 받게 되리라는 착각에 부풀어 있었다.\n\n그리고 그 기대는 빗나가지 않았다. 그 앞 못 보는 여인이 정말로 그에게 설탕 한 덩이를 주고는, 이불을 덮어 재우며 아침까지는 더 이상 나돌아 다니지 말라고 일렀으니까.\n\n\"응!\" 위증한 데미가 황홀하게 설탕을 빨며, 자기의 첫 시도를 대단한 성공으로 여기며 대답했다.\n\n메그는 제자리로 돌아왔고, 저녁 식사가 기분 좋게 이어지고 있었다. 그런데 그 작은 유령이 다시 나타나, 대담하게 요구하며 엄마의 잘못을 까발렸다.\n\n\"엄마, 어얼탕 더.\"\n\n\"이건 안 되겠어.\" 존이 그 귀여운 작은 죄인에게 마음을 모질게 먹으며 말했다. \"저 애가 제대로 잠자리에 드는 법을 배우기 전엔 우리에게 평화란 없을 거요. 당신은 충분히 오래 자기 자신을 노예로 만들었어. 한 번만 가르치면, 그걸로 끝날 거요. 침대에 눕히고 그냥 두고 와요, 메그.\"\n\n\"저 애는 거기 안 있을 거예요. 제가 곁에 앉아 있지 않으면 절대 안 그러는걸요.\"\n\n\"내가 다잡지. 데미, 위층으로 올라가서, 엄마가 시키는 대로 침대에 들어가.\"\n\n\"시여!\" 어린 반항아가 그 탐내던 \"과자\"를 집어 들고는, 태연하고 뻔뻔하게 그것을 먹기 시작하며 대답했다.\n\n\"아빠한테는 절대 그렇게 말하면 안 돼. 네가 스스로 안 가면 내가 안고 가겠다.\"\n\n\"저리 가. 나 아빠 안 조아.\" 데미가 보호받으려고 엄마 치맛자락 뒤로 물러났다.\n\n하지만 그 피난처마저 소용없었다. \"살살 다뤄요, 존\"이라는 말과 함께 그가 적에게 넘겨졌으니까. 그 말은 어린 죄인을 경악에 빠뜨렸다. 엄마가 자기를 버린 마당이니, 이제 심판의 날이 코앞이었던 것이다. 케이크를 빼앗기고, 장난도 빼앗기고, 억센 손에 안겨 그 끔찍한 침대로 실려 가게 되자, 가엾은 데미는 분을 참지 못하고 드러내 놓고 아빠에게 대들며, 위층으로 올라가는 내내 발버둥 치고 악을 써 댔다. 한쪽으로 침대에 눕혀지기가 무섭게 반대쪽으로 굴러 나와 문으로 내달았다가, 작은 잠옷 자락을 잡혀 보기 좋게 붙들려 다시 눕혀졌다. 이 활기찬 공연은 도련님의 기운이 다할 때까지 이어졌고, 그러자 그는 목청껏 울부짖는 데 온 힘을 쏟았다. 이 성악 연습은 으레 메그를 굴복시키곤 했다. 하지만 존은 흔히들 귀머거리라고 여기는 기둥처럼 꿈쩍도 하지 않고 앉아 있었다. 어르는 말도, 설탕도, 자장가도, 이야기도 없었다. 심지어 불까지 꺼지고, 난롯불의 붉은 빛만이 그 \"커다란 어둠\"을 밝힐 뿐이었는데, 데미는 그것을 두려움보다는 호기심으로 바라보았다. 이 새로운 질서가 그는 못마땅해서, 분에 찬 격정이 가라앉고 자기의 그 다정한 시녀에 대한 기억이 붙들린 폭군에게 되돌아오자, \"엄마\"를 부르며 처량하게 울부짖었다. 그 격렬한 울부짖음에 뒤이은 애처로운 흐느낌이 메그의 가슴을 찔렀고, 그녀는 위층으로 달려 올라가 사정하듯 말했다.\n\n\"저 애 곁에 있게 해 줘요. 이제 착하게 굴 거예요, 존.\"\n\n\"안 돼, 여보. 난 저 애한테 당신이 시키는 대로 자야 한다고 일렀고, 내가 밤새도록 여기 있는 한이 있어도 그렇게 해야 해.\"\n\n\"하지만 울다가 병이라도 나면 어떡해요.\" 메그가 아들을 버려둔 자신을 책망하며 애원했다.\n\n\"아니, 안 그래. 너무 지쳐서 곧 곯아떨어질 거고, 그러면 일이 매듭지어지는 거지. 자기가 말을 들어야 한다는 걸 깨닫게 될 테니까. 끼어들지 마요. 내가 다잡을 테니.\"\n\n\"저 앤 제 아이고, 전 저 애의 기개가 모진 매질에 꺾이는 걸 두고 볼 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"저 앤 내 아이고, 난 저 애의 성품이 응석으로 망가지는 걸 두고 보지 않겠소. 내려가요, 여보. 저 앤 나한테 맡기고.\"\n\n존이 그 위엄 있는 어조로 말할 때면 메그는 늘 따랐고, 자기가 고분고분했던 걸 후회한 적이 없었다.\n\n\"한 번만 입 맞추게 해 줘요, 존?\"\n\n\"물론이지. 데미, 엄마한테 '잘 자' 인사하고, 가서 쉬게 해 드려. 엄마는 온종일 너희 보살피느라 무척 지쳤으니까.\"\n\n메그는 늘, 그 입맞춤이 승리를 가져왔다고 우겼다. 입맞춤을 해 주고 나니, 데미가 한결 잠잠하게 흐느끼며, 마음의 괴로움에 몸부림치다 기어든 침대 발치에 가만히 누웠으니까.\n\n'가엾은 녀석, 잠이랑 울음에 진이 다 빠졌군. 이불 덮어 주고, 가서 메그 마음을 놓이게 해 줘야지.' 존은 그 반항하던 후계자가 잠들었기를 바라며, 살그머니 침대 곁으로 다가가며 생각했다.\n\n하지만 그는 자고 있지 않았다. 아버지가 들여다보는 순간 데미의 두 눈이 떠졌고, 작은 턱이 떨리기 시작하더니, 두 팔을 들어 올리며 뉘우치는 딸꾹질과 함께 말했다. \"나 이제 차캐.\"\n\n밖에서 계단에 앉아 있던 메그는, 그 소란 뒤에 이어진 긴 침묵에 의아해했다. 그러다 온갖 있을 법하지 않은 사고들을 다 상상하고는, 두려움을 가라앉히려 방 안으로 살며시 들어갔다. 데미는 곤히 잠들어 있었다. 평소처럼 팔다리를 활짝 벌린 모양새가 아니라, 얌전히 웅크린 채, 아버지의 팔이 두른 원 안에 바싹 파고들어 아버지의 손가락을 꼭 쥐고 있었다. 마치 정의에 자비가 섞여 있음을 느끼고, 한결 슬프지만 한결 지혜로워진 아기가 되어 잠든 듯했다. 그렇게 손가락을 붙들린 채, 존은 그 작은 손이 쥔 힘을 풀 때까지 여인 같은 인내심으로 기다리다가, 기다리던 끝에 그만 잠들고 말았다. 아들과의 그 씨름이 온종일의 일보다 더 그를 지치게 한 것이었다.\n\n베개 위의 두 얼굴을 지켜보며 서 있던 메그는 혼자 미소 지었다. 그러고는 만족스러운 어조로 이렇게 말하며 다시 살그머니 빠져나갔다.\n\n\"존이 우리 아기들한테 너무 모질까 봐 걱정할 일은 없겠어. 정말 다잡을 줄 아는구나. 큰 도움이 되겠어. 데미가 나한테는 너무 벅차지고 있으니까.\"\n\n마침내 존이 내려왔을 때, 그는 시무룩하거나 원망 어린 아내를 보게 되리라 짐작했지만, 메그가 차분하게 보닛을 손질하고 있는 모습을 보고 기분 좋게 놀랐다. 게다가 너무 피곤하지 않다면 선거에 관한 기사를 좀 읽어 달라는 부탁까지 받았다. 존은 어떤 종류의 혁명 같은 것이 일어나고 있음을 단번에 알아차렸지만, 메그가 워낙 속이 훤히 들여다보이는 사람이라 목숨이 걸려도 비밀을 못 지킨다는 걸 알았기에, 머지않아 단서가 드러나리라 여기고 현명하게 아무것도 묻지 않았다. 그는 더없이 상냥하고 선뜻한 태도로 긴 토론 기사를 읽어 준 다음, 자기 딴에는 가장 명료한 방식으로 그것을 설명했다. 그러는 동안 메그는 깊은 관심이 있는 척하려고, 똑똑한 질문을 던지려고, 그리고 생각이 나라의 형편에서 자기 보닛의 형편으로 새어 나가지 않도록 애썼다. 하지만 마음속 깊은 곳에서는 정치가 수학만큼이나 고약하고, 정치인들의 사명이란 서로에게 욕을 퍼붓는 일인 듯하다고 결론지었다. 그래도 메그는 이런 여자다운 생각을 혼자만 간직했고, 존이 잠시 멈추자 고개를 저으며 스스로 외교적인 애매함이라 여긴 말투로 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"음, 정말이지 우리가 어떻게 되려는 건지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n존은 웃음을 터뜨리고는, 그녀가 레이스와 꽃으로 곱게 꾸민 작은 물건을 손 위에 올려놓고서 자기의 일장 연설로는 끝내 불러일으키지 못했던 진정한 관심을 담아 그것을 바라보는 모습을 잠시 지켜보았다.\n\n\"이 사람은 나를 위해서 정치를 좋아해 보려고 애쓰는구나. 그러니 나도 이 사람을 위해서 모자 만드는 일을 좋아해 보려고 애써야지, 그게 공평하니까.\" 공정한 존은 이렇게 생각하고는 소리 내어 덧붙였다.\n\n\"아주 예쁜데, 이게 당신이 말하는 아침 모자라는 거요?\"\n\n\"여보, 이건 보닛이에요! 음악회랑 극장 갈 때 쓰는 제일 좋은 보닛이라고요.\"\n\n\"미안하오. 워낙 작아서 당신이 가끔 쓰는 그 하늘하늘한 것들 가운데 하나인 줄 알았지. 이건 어떻게 쓰고 있는 거요?\"\n\n\"이 레이스 자락을 턱 밑에서 장미 봉오리로 여미는 거예요, 이렇게요.\" 그러면서 메그는 보닛을 직접 써 보이고는, 거역할 수 없을 만큼 차분히 만족스러운 표정으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"사랑스러운 보닛이군. 하지만 난 그 안에 든 얼굴이 더 좋아. 다시 젊고 행복해 보이니까.\" 그러면서 존은 미소 짓는 그 얼굴에 입을 맞추었고, 그 바람에 턱 밑의 장미 봉오리는 큰 손상을 입고 말았다.\n\n\"마음에 드신다니 기뻐요. 언제 밤에 새로 하는 음악회 가운데 하나에 저를 데려가 주셨으면 해서요. 정말이지 마음을 가다듬으려면 음악이 좀 필요하거든요. 그래 주실 거죠, 네?\"\n\n\"물론이지, 기꺼이. 음악회든 당신이 가고 싶은 어디든. 너무 오래 집 안에만 갇혀 있었으니, 당신한테 더없이 좋을 거요. 나도 무엇보다 즐거울 테고. 그런데 어쩌다 그런 생각을 하게 됐소, 작은 엄마?\"\n\n\"음, 며칠 전에 엄마하고 이야기를 나눴어요. 제가 얼마나 신경이 곤두서고 짜증 나고 기분이 엉망인지 말씀드렸더니, 변화하고 부담을 좀 덜라고 하시더라고요. 그래서 해나가 아이들 돌보는 걸 도와주기로 했고, 저는 집안일을 좀 더 챙기고, 이따금 소소하게 즐기기도 하면서, 제 나이보다 일찍 안절부절못하고 시들어 버린 노파가 되지 않도록 하려는 거예요. 그저 시험 삼아 해 보는 거예요, 존. 제 자신을 위해서이기도 하지만 당신을 위해서이기도 해요. 요즘 제가 부끄러울 만큼 당신을 소홀히 했으니까요. 그래서 할 수만 있다면 우리 집을 예전의 그 모습으로 되돌려 놓을 작정이에요. 반대하지 않으시죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n존이 무어라 말했는지, 그 작은 보닛이 완전히 망가질 위기를 얼마나 아슬아슬하게 모면했는지는 굳이 따질 것 없다. 우리가 알아 둘 만한 것이라곤, 집과 그 식구들에게 차츰 일어난 변화로 미루어 볼 때 존이 반대하는 기색을 전혀 보이지 않았다는 사실뿐이다. 결코 모든 것이 낙원 같지는 않았지만, 분업 체계 덕분에 식구마다 한결 나아졌다. 정확하고 한결같은 존이 아기 나라에 질서와 순종을 가져온 덕에 아이들은 아버지의 다스림 아래 무럭무럭 자랐고, 메그는 건강에 좋은 운동을 충분히 하고 소소한 즐거움을 누리고 분별 있는 남편과 흉허물 없는 대화를 많이 나누면서 활기를 되찾고 신경을 가라앉혔다. 집은 다시 집다워졌고, 존은 메그를 데려가는 경우가 아니면 집을 떠나고 싶어 하지 않았다. 이제 스콧 부부가 브룩 부부의 집을 찾아왔고, 누구나 이 작은 집을 행복과 만족과 가족애가 가득한 즐거운 곳으로 여겼다. 명랑한 샐리 모팻조차 그곳에 가기를 좋아했다. \"여긴 늘 어쩌면 이렇게 조용하고 기분이 좋은지 몰라. 나한테 참 좋아, 메그.\" 그녀는 마치 화려한 외로움으로 가득한 자기 큰 집에서도 써먹을 수 있도록 그 매력의 정체를 찾아내려는 듯, 동경 어린 눈으로 주위를 둘러보며 그렇게 말하곤 했다. 그녀의 집에는 떠들썩하게 뛰노는 햇살 같은 아기들이 없었고, 네드는 그녀가 들어설 자리 없는 자기만의 세계 속에서 살았기 때문이다.\n\n이 가정의 행복은 한꺼번에 찾아온 것이 아니었다. 다만 존과 메그는 그 열쇠를 찾아냈고, 결혼 생활의 한 해 한 해가 그것을 어떻게 쓰는지를 두 사람에게 가르쳐 주었다. 그리하여 가장 가난한 이도 가질 수 있고 가장 부유한 이도 살 수 없는, 참된 가정의 사랑과 서로 돕는 마음이라는 보물 창고를 열어 갔다. 이것이야말로 젊은 아내와 어머니들이 기꺼이 몸을 누일 만한 선반이다. 세상의 끊임없는 근심과 열병으로부터 안전하고, 자기에게 매달리는 어린 아들딸 속에서 슬픔과 가난과 나이에도 굴하지 않는 충실한 연인을 찾으며, 맑은 날에도 폭풍우 치는 날에도 충직한 벗과 나란히 걸어가는 것이다. 그 벗이야말로 옛 색슨어의 참뜻 그대로 \"집을 묶어 주는 띠\"이니, 메그가 배웠듯 여자의 가장 행복한 왕국은 가정이며 그 가장 높은 명예는 그곳을 다스리는 기술임을, 여왕으로서가 아니라 지혜로운 아내이자 어머니로서 다스리는 기술임을 배워 가는 것이다.\n\n# LAZY LAURENCE.\n\n* 집을 묶어 주는 띠 : 영어 husband(남편)의 옛 어원은 '집(house)'과 '묶다(band)'에서 왔다는 뜻 : 옮긴이.\n로리는 일주일만 머물 생각으로 니스에 갔다가 한 달을 머물렀다. 혼자 떠도는 데 지쳐 있던 그에게, 에이미라는 낯익은 존재는 그녀가 한몫을 맡고 있는 이국의 풍경에 집 같은 매력을 더해 주는 듯했다. 그는 예전에 받던 \"응석 받아 주기\"가 못내 그리웠고, 다시 그 맛을 보는 것이 즐거웠다. 아무리 우쭐하게 만드는 것이라 해도 낯선 사람들의 호의는, 집에 있는 소녀들의 누이다운 흠모에 견주면 절반도 즐겁지 않았기 때문이다. 에이미는 다른 자매들처럼 그를 떠받들어 주려 하지 않았지만, 지금은 그를 보게 되어 무척 기뻐했고, 그가 자기가 인정하는 것보다 더 그리워하던 그리운 가족의 대표라는 생각에 그에게 거의 매달리다시피 했다. 두 사람은 자연스럽게 서로의 벗으로 위안을 얻었고, 함께 말을 타고 걷고 춤추고 빈둥대며 많은 시간을 같이 보냈다. 니스에서는 화려한 사교철에 그 누구도 그리 부지런할 수 없는 법이니까. 하지만 겉으로는 더없이 무심한 척 즐기는 동안에도, 두 사람은 반쯤은 의식하면서 서로에 대해 이런저런 사실을 발견하고 견해를 세워 가고 있었다. 에이미는 날마다 친구의 평가 속에서 올라갔지만 그는 그녀의 평가 속에서 내려갔고, 두 사람 다 한마디도 나누기 전에 그 진실을 느끼고 있었다. 에이미는 그를 기쁘게 하려 애썼고 또 성공했다. 그가 안겨 준 수많은 즐거움이 고마웠기에, 여자다운 여자만이 형언할 수 없는 매력을 불어넣을 줄 아는 그 소소한 시중으로 보답한 것이다. 로리는 어떤 종류의 노력도 하지 않았다. 그저 되도록 편안하게 흘러가는 대로 자신을 내맡긴 채, 잊으려 애쓰면서, 한 여자가 자기에게 냉랭했으니 모든 여자가 자기에게 다정한 말 한마디쯤은 빚지고 있다고 느꼈다. 그에게 너그럽게 구는 것은 아무런 수고도 들지 않는 일이라, 에이미가 받기만 한다면 니스의 장신구를 죄다 사 줄 수도 있었으리라. 하지만 동시에 그는 그녀가 자기에 대해 세워 가는 평가를 바꿀 수 없음을 느꼈고, 반쯤은 슬픈, 반쯤은 비웃는 듯한 놀라움으로 자기를 지켜보는 듯한 그 날카로운 파란 눈이 다소 두렵기도 했다.\n\n\"다른 사람들은 다 모나코로 하루 나들이를 갔어요. 난 집에 남아서 편지나 쓰는 게 좋았고요. 이제 다 썼으니까 발로사에 그림 그리러 가려는데, 같이 갈래요?\" 어느 화창한 날, 평소처럼 정오 무렵 어슬렁어슬렁 들어선 로리에게 에이미가 다가가며 말했다.\n\n\"음, 그래요. 그런데 이렇게 긴 산책을 하기엔 좀 더운 거 아닌가?\" 그는 느릿느릿 대답했다. 밖의 눈부신 햇살을 보고 나니 그늘진 응접실이 더 끌렸기 때문이다.\n\n\"작은 마차를 쓸 거예요. 바티스트가 몰아 줄 거고요. 그러니 당신은 양산이나 받쳐 들고 장갑이나 깨끗하게 간수하는 것 말고는 할 일이 없을 거예요.\" 에이미가 흠 하나 없는 새끼 양가죽 장갑을 비꼬듯 흘끗 보며 받아쳤다. 그 장갑은 로리의 약점이었다.\n\n\"그렇다면 기꺼이 가지요.\" 그러면서 그는 그녀의 스케치북을 받으려고 손을 내밀었다. 하지만 그녀는 그것을 팔 밑에 잽싸게 끼워 넣으며 톡 쏘았다.\n\n\"수고하실 것 없어요. 저한테는 힘든 일도 아니고, 당신은 그럴 기운도 없어 보이는걸요.\"\n\n로리는 눈썹을 치켜올린 채, 그녀가 계단을 뛰어 내려가자 느긋한 걸음으로 뒤따랐다. 하지만 마차에 올라타자 그는 직접 고삐를 쥐었고, 어린 바티스트는 팔짱을 끼고 마부석에서 잠드는 것 말고는 할 일이 없게 되었다.\n\n두 사람은 결코 다투지 않았다. 에이미는 너무나 교양 있었고, 지금의 로리는 너무나 게을렀으니까. 그래서 잠시 후 그가 살피는 표정으로 그녀의 모자챙 아래를 들여다보자, 그녀는 미소로 답했고, 두 사람은 더없이 다정한 분위기 속에 함께 길을 이어 갔다.\n\n아름다움을 사랑하는 눈을 즐겁게 하는 그림 같은 풍경이 가득한, 구불구불한 길을 따라가는 사랑스러운 마차 길이었다. 한쪽에는 오래된 수도원이 있어, 수도사들의 엄숙한 성가가 그들에게까지 흘러 내려왔다. 다른 한쪽에는 맨다리에 나막신을 신고 뾰족한 모자를 쓰고 한쪽 어깨에 거친 윗옷을 걸친 양치기가 돌 위에 앉아 피리를 불었고, 그의 염소들은 바위 사이를 폴짝거리거나 발치에 누워 있었다. 갓 베어 낸 풀을 광주리에 가득 실은 온순하고 쥐색을 띤 당나귀들이, 초록빛 풀더미 사이에 카팔린을 쓴 예쁜 소녀를 태우거나, 가면서 물레가락으로 실을 잣는 노파를 태운 채 지나갔다. 갈색 피부에 부드러운 눈을 한 아이들이 고풍스러운 돌 오두막에서 뛰어나와 꽃다발이나 아직 가지에 달린 오렌지 송이를 내밀었다. 옹이진 올리브나무들이 거무스름한 잎으로 언덕을 뒤덮었고, 과수원에는 황금빛 열매가 매달렸으며, 길가에는 새빨간 아네모네가 가장자리를 수놓았다. 그리고 푸른 비탈과 바위투성이 산봉우리 너머로, 마리팀 알프스가 이탈리아의 푸른 하늘을 배경으로 또렷하고 새하얗게 솟아 있었다.\n\n발로사는 그 이름값을 톡톡히 했다. 영원한 여름이 이어지는 그 기후 속에서 장미가 어디에나 피어 있었기 때문이다. 장미는 아치 위로 늘어졌고, 큰 대문 창살 사이로 고개를 내밀어 지나가는 이들에게 향긋한 인사를 건넸으며, 레몬나무와 깃털 같은 야자수 사이로 구불구불 이어져 언덕 위 별장까지 닿는 길가를 따라 줄지어 있었다. 발길을 멈추고 쉬어 가라며 의자가 놓인 그늘진 구석마다 꽃이 무더기로 피었고, 서늘한 동굴마다 대리석 요정이 꽃의 너울 사이로 미소 짓고 있었으며, 분수마다 진홍빛, 흰빛, 옅은 분홍빛 장미가 비쳐, 제 아름다움에 미소 지으려는 듯 고개를 숙이고 있었다. 장미는 집의 벽을 뒤덮었고, 처마 장식을 휘감았고, 기둥을 타고 올랐으며, 넓은 테라스의 난간 위로 제멋대로 흐드러졌다. 그 테라스에서는 햇살 가득한 지중해와 그 기슭에 자리한 흰 성벽의 도시가 내려다보였다.\n\n\"여긴 영락없이 신혼여행 낙원이네요, 안 그래요? 이런 장미를 본 적 있어요?\" 에이미가 테라스에 멈춰 서서 경치와, 흘러오는 풍성한 향기 한 줄기를 즐기며 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 이런 가시도 느껴 본 적 없는걸.\" 로리가 엄지손가락을 입에 문 채 대꾸했다. 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 핀 진홍빛 꽃 한 송이를 꺾으려다 헛수고를 한 뒤였다.\n\n\"더 아래쪽으로 손을 뻗어서 가시 없는 걸 따요.\" 에이미가 자기 뒤편 벽을 별처럼 수놓은 자그마한 크림색 장미 세 송이를 꺾으며 말했다. 그녀는 화해의 표시로 그것을 그의 단춧구멍에 꽂아 주었고, 그는 묘한 표정으로 잠시 그것을 내려다보며 서 있었다. 그의 이탈리아인다운 천성 속에는 미신적인 데가 조금 있었던 데다, 마침 그는 상상력이 풍부한 젊은이들이 하찮은 것에서 의미를 찾고 어디서나 낭만의 양식을 발견하게 되는, 반쯤은 달콤하고 반쯤은 쓰라린 우수에 잠겨 있었기 때문이다. 그는 가시 돋친 붉은 장미를 향해 손을 뻗으면서 조를 떠올렸다. 선명한 꽃이 그녀에게 잘 어울렸고, 집의 온실에서 꺾은 그런 꽃을 그녀가 종종 달고 다녔으니까. 에이미가 준 옅은 빛깔의 장미는 이탈리아 사람들이 죽은 이의 손에 놓아 줄 뿐 신부의 화관에는 결코 쓰지 않는 종류였다. 그래서 그는 한순간, 그 불길한 징조가 조를 향한 것인지 자기를 향한 것인지 의아해했다. 하지만 다음 순간 그의 미국인다운 상식이 감상을 누르고 이겨 냈고, 그는 그가 온 뒤로 에이미가 들어 보지 못한, 한층 더 활기찬 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"좋은 충고예요. 그 말 듣고 손가락이나 아끼는 게 낫겠어요.\" 에이미가 자기 말이 그를 웃긴 줄 알고 말했다.\n\n\"고마워요, 그럴게요.\" 그는 농담조로 대답했고, 몇 달 뒤 진심으로 그렇게 했다.\n\n\"로리, 할아버지께는 언제 갈 거예요?\" 에이미가 곧 시골풍 의자에 자리를 잡으며 물었다.\n\n\"곧.\"\n\n\"지난 삼 주 동안 그 말만 열두 번은 했어요.\"\n\n\"그럴 만도 하지. 짧게 대답하면 성가신 일이 줄어드니까.\"\n\n\"할아버지가 기다리시잖아요. 정말 가야 해요.\"\n\n\"손님 대접 좋아하시는 분이지. 나도 알아요.\"\n\n\"그럼 왜 안 가요?\"\n\n\"타고난 못된 성미 때문이겠죠, 아마.\"\n\n\"타고난 게으름이겠죠. 정말 너무하네요!\" 에이미가 엄한 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"보기만큼 나쁘진 않아요. 내가 가 봤자 할아버지를 성가시게 할 뿐이니, 차라리 여기 머물면서 당신을 좀 더 성가시게 하는 게 낫지. 당신은 그걸 더 잘 견디니까. 사실 당신한테는 아주 잘 맞는 것 같던데.\" 그러면서 로리는 난간의 넓은 턱에 비스듬히 기대 빈둥거릴 자세를 잡았다.\n\n에이미는 고개를 저으며 체념한 듯한 표정으로 스케치북을 펼쳤다. 하지만 그녀는 \"저 녀석\"에게 한바탕 훈계를 하기로 마음먹은 터라, 곧 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"지금 뭐 해요?\"\n\n\"도마뱀 구경.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니요. 앞으로 뭘 할 작정이고 뭘 하고 싶으냐는 거예요.\"\n\n\"허락해 주면 담배나 한 대 피우고 싶군.\"\n\n\"정말 사람 약 올리네요! 난 시가 같은 거 안 좋아해요. 하지만 당신을 내 그림에 넣게 해 준다면 봐줄게요. 인물이 하나 필요하거든요.\"\n\n\"세상에 더없이 기쁜 일이지. 어떻게 그릴 거요? 전신상으로, 아니면 사분의 삼만? 머리로 설지, 발로 설지? 정중하게 제안하건대, 비스듬히 누운 자세가 좋겠소. 그리고 당신도 함께 그려 넣어서 '돌체 파르 니엔테'라고 이름 붙이면 되겠군.\"\n\n\"그 자세 그대로 있어요. 졸리면 자도 좋고요. 나는 열심히 그릴 작정이니까.\" 에이미가 더없이 의욕에 찬 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"이 무슨 즐거운 열정이람!\" 그는 더없이 흡족한 표정으로 키 큰 항아리에 몸을 기댔다.\n\n\"지금 당신 모습을 조가 본다면 뭐라고 할까요?\" 에이미가 안달하며 물었다. 자기보다 한층 더 의욕 넘치는 언니의 이름을 들먹여 그를 자극해 보려는 속셈이었다.\n\n\"늘 하던 대로 '저리 가, 테디, 나 바빠!' 하겠지.\" 그는 말하면서 웃었지만, 그 웃음은 자연스럽지 않았고 그의 얼굴 위로 그늘이 스쳤다. 그 익숙한 이름을 입에 올리자, 아직 아물지 않은 상처가 건드려졌기 때문이다. 그 어조와 그늘이 에이미의 마음을 쳤다. 전에도 보고 들은 적이 있었기 때문이다. 그리고 이번에는 마침 그녀가 고개를 들어, 로리의 얼굴에 떠오른 새로운 표정을 포착했다. 고통과 불만과 후회로 가득한, 단단하고 쓰라린 표정이었다. 그녀가 그것을 찬찬히 살피기도 전에 그 표정은 사라졌고, 다시 무기력한 표정이 돌아왔다. 그녀는 예술가다운 즐거움을 느끼며 잠시 그를 지켜보았다. 모자도 쓰지 않고 햇살을 쬐며 누운 채 남국의 몽상으로 가득한 눈을 한 그의 모습이 어쩌면 이리도 이탈리아 사람 같은지 생각하면서. 그는 그녀를 잊은 듯 깊은 몽상에 빠져 있었다.\n\n\"꼭 무덤 위에 잠든 젊은 기사의 조각상 같아요.\" 에이미가 어두운 돌을 배경으로 또렷이 드러난 그의 잘생긴 옆모습을 조심스레 따라 그리며 말했다.\n\n\"인생을 망쳐 버린 게 아니라면, 그건 어리석은 소망이에요. 당신은 너무 변했어요. 가끔 이런 생각이 들어요—\" 거기서 에이미는 말을 멈추었다. 반쯤은 머뭇거리고 반쯤은 안타까워하는 그 눈빛이, 끝맺지 못한 말보다 더 많은 것을 담고 있었다.\n\n로리는 그녀가 차마 입에 담지 못하는 애정 어린 걱정을 보았고 또 이해했다. 그래서 그녀의 어머니에게 그러던 그대로, 그녀의 눈을 똑바로 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"다 괜찮아요, 부인.\"\n\n그 말이 그녀를 안심시켰고, 요즘 그녀를 괴롭히기 시작한 의심을 잠재웠다. 또한 그 말은 그녀의 마음을 건드렸고, 그녀는 다정한 어조로 그 마음을 드러냈다.\n\n\"그렇다니 다행이에요! 당신이 아주 못된 짓을 하고 다녔으리라곤 생각하지 않았지만, 혹시 그 못된 바덴바덴에서 돈을 탕진했거나, 남편 있는 매력적인 프랑스 여자한테 마음을 빼앗겼거나, 아니면 젊은 남자들이 외국 여행에 으레 따라붙는 거라고 여기는 그런 사고를 친 건 아닐까 싶었거든요. 거기 햇볕 아래 그러고 있지 말고, 이리 와서 풀밭에 누워요. 그리고 조가 우리가 소파 구석에 모여 비밀 이야기를 나눌 때 하던 말처럼, '우리 사이좋게 지내자고요.'\"\n\n로리는 순순히 잔디밭에 몸을 던지고는, 거기 놓여 있던 에이미의 모자 리본에 데이지를 꽂으며 장난을 치기 시작했다.\n\n\"비밀 이야기, 들을 준비 다 됐어요.\" 그가 눈에 분명한 관심을 띠며 고개를 들었다.\n\n\"난 할 얘기 없어요. 당신부터 시작해요.\"\n\n\"나도 내세울 비밀 하나 없는걸. 혹시 집에서 무슨 소식이라도 들었나 했지.\"\n\n\"최근에 온 소식은 당신도 다 들었잖아요. 자주 소식 못 받아요? 난 조가 당신한테 책 몇 권 분량은 써 보낼 줄 알았는데.\"\n\n\"걔는 무척 바쁘니까. 게다가 난 이렇게 떠돌아다니니 꼬박꼬박 받기가 불가능하지, 알잖아요. 그런데 당신의 위대한 예술 작품은 언제 시작할 거요, 라파엘라 양?\" 그가 또 한 차례 침묵 끝에 불쑥 화제를 바꾸며 물었다. 그 침묵 동안 그는 에이미가 자기 비밀을 알고서 그 이야기를 하고 싶어 하는 것은 아닐까 생각하던 참이었다.\n\n\"영영 안 해요.\" 에이미가 풀이 죽었으면서도 단호한 태도로 대답했다. \"로마가 내 허영심을 죄다 앗아 갔거든요. 거기서 그 경이로운 것들을 보고 나니, 내가 너무 보잘것없어서 살아갈 가치도 없는 듯 느껴졌어요. 그래서 절망 속에 어리석은 희망을 다 버렸죠.\"\n\n\"그렇게 기운도 재능도 넘치는 사람이 왜 그래요?\"\n\n\"바로 그래서예요. 재능은 천재성이 아니고, 아무리 기운이 넘쳐도 그걸 천재성으로 만들 순 없으니까요. 난 위대해지거나, 아니면 아무것도 안 되거나 둘 중 하나예요. 평범하게 물감이나 처바르는 사람은 되지 않을 거예요. 그러니 더는 시도할 생각이 없어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 이제 자신을 어떻게 할 작정인지 물어봐도 될까요?\"\n\n\"내 다른 재능들을 갈고닦아서, 기회가 닿으면 사교계의 꽃이 될 거예요.\"\n\n그것은 그녀다운 말이었고 당돌하게 들렸다. 하지만 대담함은 젊은이들에게 잘 어울리는 법이고, 에이미의 야망에는 탄탄한 근거가 있었다. 로리는 미소를 지었지만, 오래 품어 온 목표 하나가 죽자 한탄으로 시간을 허비하지 않고 새 목표를 받아드는 그 기개가 마음에 들었다.\n\n\"좋아요! 그리고 여기서 프레드 본이 등장하는 거겠죠, 내 짐작에는.\"\n\n에이미는 신중하게 침묵을 지켰지만, 고개 숙인 그녀의 얼굴에는 무언가 의식하는 기색이 떠올라 있었다. 그 기색에 로리는 몸을 일으켜 앉으며 진지하게 말했다.\n\n\"이제 내가 오빠 노릇을 하면서 질문 좀 할게요. 그래도 될까요?\"\n\n\"대답하겠다고 약속은 못 해요.\"\n\n\"입은 말을 안 해도 얼굴이 말해 줄 거요. 당신은 아직 감정을 숨길 만큼 세상 물정에 닳은 여자가 아니거든, 에이미. 작년에 당신하고 프레드에 관한 소문을 들었는데, 내 개인적인 생각으로는 만약 그가 그렇게 갑작스레 집으로 불려 가서 그토록 오래 발이 묶이지 않았다면 무슨 일이 벌어졌을 것 같단 말이지. 어때요?\"\n\n\"그건 내가 할 말이 아니죠.\" 에이미는 새침하게 대답했다. 하지만 입가에는 자꾸 미소가 번졌고, 눈에는 배신처럼 반짝이는 빛이 어려, 자기 힘을 잘 알고 그 앎을 즐기고 있음을 드러냈다.\n\n\"약혼한 건 아니죠, 설마?\" 로리가 별안간 무척 오빠다운 진지한 표정을 지었다.\n\"아니요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그가 돌아와서 제대로 무릎을 꿇으면 약혼하겠죠, 안 그래요?\"\n\"그럴 가능성이 크죠.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 프레드 녀석을 좋아하긴 하는 거요?\"\n\n\"좋아하려고 마음먹으면 그럴 수 있어요.\"\n\n\"하지만 적당한 때가 오기 전엔 마음먹을 생각이 없다는 거군? 맙소사, 이 무슨 세상에 다시없을 신중함이람! 그는 좋은 친구예요, 에이미. 하지만 내가 생각하기에 당신이 좋아할 만한 사람은 아닌데.\"\n\n\"그는 부자고, 신사고, 매너가 아주 훌륭해요.\" 에이미는 자못 차분하고 위엄 있게 보이려 애쓰며 입을 뗐지만, 그 진심 어린 의도에도 불구하고 어쩐지 스스로가 조금 부끄러웠다.\n\n\"알겠어요. 사교계의 여왕은 돈이 없으면 굴러갈 수 없으니, 당신은 좋은 혼처를 잡아서 그렇게 출발할 작정이라는 거군요. 세상 돌아가는 이치로 보면 지극히 옳고 마땅한 일이지만, 당신 어머니 딸의 입에서 나오니 어쩐지 어색하게 들리는걸.\"\n\n\"그래도 사실인걸요.\"\n\n짧은 말이었지만, 그것을 내뱉는 차분한 단호함이 그 젊은 화자와는 묘하게 어울리지 않았다. 로리는 본능적으로 그것을 느끼고는, 까닭을 알 수 없는 실망감과 함께 다시 몸을 뉘었다. 그의 표정과 침묵이, 그리고 어떤 속마음의 자책감이 에이미를 언짢게 했고, 미루지 말고 당장 훈계를 하리라 마음먹게 했다.\n\n\"제발 부탁이니 좀 분발해 줬으면 좋겠어요.\" 그녀가 톡 쏘듯 말했다.\n\n\"나 대신 분발해 줘요, 착한 아가씨.\"\n\n\"마음만 먹으면 할 수 있어요.\" 그녀는 더없이 간단명료한 방식으로 그러고 싶다는 듯한 표정이었다.\n\n\"그럼 해 봐요. 허락할 테니.\" 로리가 받아쳤다. 가장 좋아하는 소일거리를 오래 끊고 지낸 터라, 약 올릴 상대가 생긴 것이 즐거웠다.\n\n\"오 분도 안 돼서 화낼걸요.\"\n\n\"난 당신한테는 절대 화 안 내요. 불을 일으키려면 부싯돌이 두 개 있어야 하는데, 당신은 눈처럼 차갑고 부드럽거든.\"\n\n\"내가 무얼 할 수 있는지 모르는군요. 눈도 제대로 쓰면 화끈거리고 따끔거리는 법이에요. 당신의 무관심은 절반은 꾸민 거고, 한번 제대로 휘저어 놓으면 그게 드러날걸요.\"\n\n\"실컷 휘저어 봐요. 난 아프지도 않을 거고, 당신은 재미있을지도 모르니. 덩치 큰 사내가 자그마한 아내한테 매를 맞으면서 한 말처럼 말이오. 날 남편이나 양탄자쯤으로 여기고, 그런 운동이 당신한테 맞는다면 지칠 때까지 두드려 봐요.\"\n\n자기도 단단히 약이 올라, 그를 그토록 딴사람으로 만든 그 무기력을 그가 떨쳐 내는 모습을 보고 싶어 애가 탄 에이미는, 혀끝도 연필 끝도 날카롭게 벼리고는 입을 열었다.\n\n\"플로하고 내가 당신한테 새 별명을 붙였어요. '게으른 로런스'예요. 어때요, 마음에 들어요?\"\n\n그를 약 올릴 줄 알았는데, 그는 머리 밑에 팔을 괴며 태연하게 말할 뿐이었다. \"나쁘지 않은데. 고마워요, 아가씨들.\"\n\n\"내가 당신을 정말 어떻게 생각하는지 알고 싶어요?\"\n\n\"못 들어서 안달이 났는걸.\"\n\n\"음, 난 당신을 경멸해요.\"\n\n만약 그녀가 토라진 척, 혹은 새침한 어조로 \"당신이 미워요\"라고만 했다면 그는 웃어넘기고 오히려 좋아했을 것이다. 하지만 진지하다 못해 거의 슬픔에 잠긴 그 목소리에 그는 눈을 번쩍 뜨고 다급히 물었다.\n\n\"왜요, 어디 말해 봐요?\"\n\n\"착하고 쓸모 있고 행복할 기회가 얼마든지 있는데도, 당신은 결점투성이에 게으르고 비참하니까요.\"\n\n\"센 표현이군요, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"마음에 들면 계속할게요.\"\n\n\"제발 그래 줘요. 아주 흥미진진하니까.\"\n\n\"그렇게 느낄 줄 알았어요. 이기적인 사람들은 늘 자기 얘기 하는 걸 좋아하거든요.\"\n\n\"내가 이기적이라고?\" 그 질문은 본의 아니게, 놀란 어조로 불쑥 새어 나왔다. 그가 스스로 자부하던 단 하나의 미덕이 너그러움이었기 때문이다.\n\n\"네, 아주 이기적이죠.\" 에이미가 차분하고 냉정한 목소리로 말을 이었다. 그 목소리는 마침 화난 목소리보다 곱절은 더 효과적이었다. \"어떻게 그런지 보여 줄게요. 우리가 신나게 노는 동안 내가 당신을 쭉 살펴봤는데, 영 마음에 안 들거든요. 외국에 나온 지 여섯 달이 다 돼 가는데, 당신이 한 일이라곤 시간과 돈을 허비하고 친구들을 실망시킨 것뿐이잖아요.\"\n\n\"사 년이나 죽어라 공부했으면 좀 즐길 수도 있는 거 아닌가?\"\n\n\"그리 즐긴 것 같아 보이지도 않는걸요. 어쨌든 내가 보기엔 그걸로 조금도 나아진 게 없어요. 우리가 처음 만났을 때 난 당신이 한결 나아졌다고 했죠. 이제 그 말 다 취소할래요. 내가 집을 떠나올 때보다 절반도 괜찮아 보이지 않거든요. 끔찍하게 게을러졌고, 남 얘기나 좋아하고, 하찮은 일에 시간을 허비하고, 지혜로운 사람들한테 사랑받고 존경받는 대신 어리석은 사람들한테 떠받들리고 떠받들어지는 데 만족하고 있잖아요. 돈에 재능에 지위에 건강에 외모까지—아, 그 말은 좋은가 보군요, 이 허영덩어리 양반! 하지만 사실인걸요, 그러니 안 할 수가 없네요—이 멋진 것들을 다 쓰고 누릴 수 있으면서도, 당신은 빈둥대는 것 말고는 할 일을 못 찾고 있어요. 그리고 될 수도 있고 되어야 마땅한 사람이 되는 대신, 당신은 그저—\" 거기서 그녀는 말을 멈추었다. 그 눈빛에는 고통과 연민이 함께 담겨 있었다.\n\n\"석쇠 위에 놓인 성 라우렌티우스란 말이지.\" 로리가 부드럽게 그 문장을 마무리 지어 주었다. 하지만 훈계는 효과를 내기 시작했다. 이제 그의 눈에는 또렷이 깨어난 빛이 반짝였고, 반쯤은 화나고 반쯤은 상처받은 표정이 아까의 무관심을 밀어내고 들어섰다.\n* 성 라우렌티우스 : 석쇠 위에서 화형당한 순교 성인. 에이미의 '게으른 로런스'라는 별명을 받아 자신을 순교자에 빗댄 말장난 : 옮긴이.\n\n\"그렇게 받아들일 줄 알았어요. 당신네 남자들은 우리더러 천사라고, 우리가 마음먹은 대로 당신들을 만들 수 있다고 하죠. 하지만 우리가 진심으로 당신들을 위해 좋게 해 주려고 하는 순간, 비웃기나 하고 귀담아듣질 않잖아요. 그게 바로 당신들의 입에 발린 칭찬이 얼마나 값어치 없는지 증명하는 거예요.\" 에이미가 쓰라리게 내뱉고는, 발치의 그 약 올리는 순교자에게서 등을 돌렸다.\n\n잠시 후 손 하나가 그림 위로 내려와 그녀가 그림을 그릴 수 없게 했고, 로리의 목소리가 참회하는 어린아이를 우스꽝스럽게 흉내 내며 들려왔다.\n\n\"착하게 굴게요, 아, 착하게 굴게요!\"\n\n하지만 에이미는 웃지 않았다. 진지했기 때문이다. 그녀는 펼쳐진 그 손을 연필로 톡톡 두드리며 차분히 말했다.\n\n\"이런 손이 부끄럽지도 않아요? 여자 손처럼 보드랍고 하얀데, 평생 주뱅이 최고급 장갑이나 끼고 숙녀들한테 꽃이나 꺾어 주는 것 말고는 아무 일도 안 해 본 것 같잖아요. 하늘에 감사하게도 당신이 멋이나 부리는 한량은 아니죠. 그러니 여기 다이아몬드나 커다란 인장 반지 같은 게 없고, 오래전에 조가 준 그 작고 낡은 반지 하나만 끼워져 있는 게 다행이에요. 그 착한 애가 여기 있어서 날 도와줬으면 좋겠네요!\"\n\n\"나도 그래요!\"\n\n손은 나타날 때만큼이나 갑작스레 사라졌고, 그 소망에 맞장구치는 울림에는 에이미조차 흡족할 만큼 충분한 기운이 실려 있었다. 그녀는 새로운 생각을 품은 채 그를 내려다보았다. 하지만 그는 햇볕을 가리려는 듯 모자로 얼굴을 반쯤 덮은 채 누워 있었고, 콧수염이 입을 가리고 있었다. 그녀에게 보이는 것이라곤 한숨이었을지도 모를 긴 숨에 가슴이 오르내리는 것과, 입에 담기엔 너무 소중하거나 너무 여린 무언가를 감추려는 듯 반지 낀 손이 풀밭 속으로 파고드는 모습뿐이었다. 순식간에 갖가지 암시와 사소한 일들이 에이미의 머릿속에서 형체와 의미를 갖추며, 언니가 결코 털어놓은 적 없는 사실을 그녀에게 일러 주었다. 그녀는 로리가 한 번도 제 입으로 조 이야기를 꺼낸 적이 없음을 떠올렸고, 방금 그의 얼굴에 스친 그늘과, 그의 성격에 일어난 변화와, 잘생긴 손에 어울리는 장신구라곤 할 수 없는 그 작고 낡은 반지를 끼고 다니는 것을 곱씹어 보았다. 소녀들은 이런 징표를 재빨리 읽어 내고 그 웅변을 느끼는 법이다. 에이미는 어쩌면 사랑의 아픔이 그 변화의 밑바닥에 깔려 있는 게 아닐까 짐작해 왔는데, 이제 확신이 섰다. 그녀의 날카로운 눈에 물기가 차올랐고, 다시 입을 열었을 때 그 목소리는, 마음먹기에 따라 그토록 아름답게 부드럽고 다정해질 수 있는 그런 목소리였다.\n\n\"내가 당신한테 이렇게 말할 자격이 없다는 거 알아요, 로리. 만약 당신이 세상에서 제일 성품이 너그러운 사람이 아니었다면 나한테 무척 화를 냈겠죠. 하지만 우린 모두 당신을 무척 아끼고 자랑스러워하니까, 나처럼 집에서도 다들 당신한테 실망하게 된다고 생각하면 견딜 수가 없었어요. 어쩌면 그들은 나보다 이 변화를 더 잘 이해할지도 모르지만요.\"\n\n\"그럴 거예요.\" 모자 밑에서 무뚝뚝한 어조로 대답이 흘러나왔다. 그 무뚝뚝함은 떨리는 목소리만큼이나 가슴을 울렸다.\n\n\"진작 나한테 말해 줬어야죠. 그래서 내가 그렇게 더듬대며 잔소리나 늘어놓게 두지 말고요. 난 그 어느 때보다 다정하고 인내심 있게 굴었어야 했는데. 난 그 랜들 양인지 뭔지 하는 여자가 처음부터 마음에 안 들었는데, 이제 그 여자가 미워요!\" 교활한 에이미가 말했다. 이번에는 사실을 확실히 해 두고 싶었던 것이다.\n\n\"랜들 양이라니, 무슨!\" 그러면서 로리는 손으로 얼굴에서 모자를 쳐 냈다. 그 표정에는 그 젊은 아가씨에 대한 자기 감정이 어떠한지 의심의 여지가 없었다.\n\n\"미안해요. 난 또—\" 거기서 그녀는 능숙하게 말끝을 흐렸다.\n\n\"아니, 그렇게 생각한 거 아니잖아요. 내가 조 말고는 그 누구한테도 마음을 둔 적 없다는 걸 당신은 똑똑히 알고 있으면서.\" 로리는 예전의 그 격한 어조로 그렇게 말하고는, 말하는 동안 얼굴을 돌렸다.\n\n\"그렇게 생각하긴 했어요. 하지만 다들 그 얘기는 한마디도 안 했고, 당신은 떠나 버렸으니, 내가 잘못 안 줄 알았죠. 그래, 조가 당신한테 다정하게 굴지 않았다는 거예요? 어머, 난 조가 당신을 끔찍이 사랑하는 줄로만 알았는데.\"\n\n\"다정하긴 했지. 하지만 옳은 방식은 아니었어요. 그리고 내가 당신 생각대로 그렇게 못난 녀석이라면, 걔가 날 사랑하지 않은 게 걔한텐 다행이지. 그래도 그건 걔 잘못이에요. 걔한테 그렇게 전해도 좋아요.\"\n\n그 말을 하면서 단단하고 쓰라린 표정이 다시 돌아왔고, 그것이 에이미의 마음을 아프게 했다. 어떤 위로의 약을 발라 주어야 할지 알 수 없었기 때문이다.\n\n\"내가 잘못했어요, 몰랐던 거예요. 그렇게 매몰차게 굴어서 정말 미안해요. 하지만 좀 더 의젓하게 견뎌 줬으면 하는 바람을 어쩔 수가 없네요, 테디.\"\n\n\"하지 말아요, 그건 조가 날 부르던 이름이니까!\" 로리는 조의 반쯤은 다정하고 반쯤은 나무라는 듯한 그 어조로 나온 말을 가로막으려는 듯, 잽싼 몸짓으로 손을 들어 올렸다. \"당신도 직접 겪어 보고 나서 말해요.\" 그는 한 움큼씩 풀을 쥐어뜯으며 낮은 목소리로 덧붙였다.\n\n\"나라면 사내답게 견뎌 내겠어요. 사랑받지 못한다면 존경이라도 받겠어요.\" 에이미가 그 일에 대해 아무것도 모르는 사람 특유의 단호함으로 말했다.\n\n그런데 로리는, 자기가 그것을 놀랄 만큼 잘 견뎌 왔다고, 신음 한 번 흘리지 않고 동정 한 번 구하지 않은 채 홀로 아픔을 이겨 내려고 짊어지고 다녔다고 자부하던 터였다. 에이미의 훈계는 그 일을 새로운 빛 아래 비추어 주었고, 처음으로 그는 첫 실패에 낙심해 음울한 무관심 속에 스스로를 가두는 것이 정말 나약하고 이기적으로 보였다. 그는 마치 시름에 잠긴 꿈에서 갑작스레 흔들려 깨어난 듯했고, 다시 잠들기란 불가능하다는 것을 깨달았다. 이윽고 그는 몸을 일으켜 앉으며 천천히 물었다.\n\n\"조도 당신처럼 날 경멸할까요?\"\n\n\"네, 지금 당신을 본다면요. 그 애는 게으른 사람을 싫어해요. 뭔가 멋진 일을 해서 그 애가 당신을 사랑하게 만들면 되잖아요?\"\n\n\"최선을 다했지만 소용없었어요.\"\n\n\"우수한 성적으로 졸업한 걸 말하는 거예요? 그건 할아버지를 봐서라도 당연히 했어야 할 일 그 이상도 이하도 아니에요. 다들 당신이 잘할 수 있다는 걸 아는데, 그 많은 시간과 돈을 들이고도 실패했다면 부끄러운 일이었겠죠.\"\n\n\"뭐라 해도 난 실패한 거예요. 조가 날 사랑하지 않았으니까.\" 로리가 풀이 죽은 자세로 손에 머리를 괴며 입을 뗐다.\n\n\"아니요, 당신은 실패하지 않았어요. 결국엔 당신도 그렇게 말하게 될걸요. 그건 당신한테 좋은 일이었고, 당신이 마음만 먹으면 뭔가 해낼 수 있다는 걸 증명해 줬으니까요. 어떤 종류든 또 다른 일에 착수하기만 하면, 당신은 곧 예전처럼 활기차고 행복한 사람으로 돌아가서 아픔을 잊게 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"그건 불가능해요.\"\n\n\"해 보면 알아요. 어깨를 으쓱하면서 '저 애가 그런 걸 뭘 알아' 하고 생각할 것 없어요. 내가 지혜로운 척하는 건 아니지만, 잘 살피긴 하거든요. 당신이 생각하는 것보다 훨씬 많은 걸 본다고요. 난 다른 사람들의 경험과 모순에 관심이 많아요. 그리고 설명은 못 해도, 그걸 기억해 두었다가 내게 도움이 되게 쓰죠. 원한다면 평생 조를 사랑해도 좋아요. 하지만 그것 때문에 자신을 망치진 말아요. 가질 수 없는 하나 때문에 그 많은 좋은 선물을 내던지는 건 못된 짓이니까요. 자, 더는 잔소리 안 할게요. 그 모진 여자애가 어쨌든 간에, 당신이 정신을 차리고 어엿한 남자가 되리란 걸 아니까요.\"\n\n두 사람 다 한동안 말이 없었다. 로리는 손가락에 낀 작은 반지를 빙빙 돌리며 앉아 있었고, 에이미는 이야기하는 동안 그리던 즉석 스케치에 마지막 손질을 했다. 이윽고 그녀는 그것을 그의 무릎에 올려놓으며 한마디만 했다.\n\n\"이거 어때요?\"\n\n그는 그림을 보고는 미소 지을 수밖에 없었다. 솜씨가 빼어났기 때문이다. 풀밭 위에 길게 늘어진 게으른 형상, 무기력한 얼굴, 반쯤 감긴 눈, 그리고 한 손에 쥔 시가에서 피어오른 작은 연기 고리가 몽상가의 머리를 감싸고 있었다.\n\n\"정말 잘 그리는군!\" 그는 그녀의 솜씨에 진심으로 놀라고 기뻐하며 말하고는, 반쯤 웃으며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그래요, 이게 나죠.\"\n\n\"지금의 당신이죠. 이건 예전의 당신이고요.\" 그러면서 에이미는 그가 들고 있는 그림 옆에 또 다른 스케치 한 장을 내려놓았다.\n\n그것은 솜씨가 그만 못했지만, 여러 흠을 메워 줄 만큼 생기와 기백이 넘쳤고, 과거를 어찌나 생생하게 떠올려 주는지, 그것을 바라보는 젊은이의 얼굴 위로 별안간 변화가 스쳐 갔다. 그것은 로리가 말을 길들이는 모습을 거칠게 그린 스케치에 지나지 않았다. 모자도 외투도 벗어 던졌고, 활동적인 형상과 결연한 얼굴과 위풍당당한 자세의 선 하나하나에 기운과 의미가 가득했다. 막 굴복당한 잘생긴 짐승은 팽팽히 당겨진 고삐 아래 목을 둥글게 휜 채, 한쪽 발로 안달하듯 땅을 차고, 자기를 제압한 그 목소리에 귀 기울이기라도 하듯 두 귀를 쫑긋 세우고 서 있었다. 흐트러진 갈기와, 바람에 날리는 기수의 머리칼과 꼿꼿한 자세에는, 갑작스레 멈춰 선 동작과 힘과 용기와 젊음의 활기가 어려, '돌체 파르 니엔테' 스케치의 나른한 우아함과 날카롭게 대비되었다. 로리는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 하지만 그의 눈이 한 그림에서 다른 그림으로 옮겨 가는 동안, 에이미는 그가 얼굴을 붉히며 입을 꾹 다무는 것을 보았다. 마치 그녀가 일러 준 그 작은 교훈을 읽고 받아들이기라도 하듯이. 그것으로 그녀는 만족했고, 그가 입을 열기를 기다리지 않고 특유의 발랄한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 퍽을 데리고 레어리 흉내를 내던 날 기억 안 나요? 우리가 다 같이 구경했잖아요. 메그하고 베스는 무서워했지만, 조는 손뼉을 치며 깡충깡충 뛰었고, 난 울타리에 앉아서 당신을 그렸죠. 며칠 전에 화첩에서 그 스케치를 발견하고는, 손을 좀 보고 당신한테 보여 주려고 간직해 뒀어요.\"\n\n* 레어리 : 당대에 유명했던 미국의 조련사 존 레어리. 말을 부드럽게 길들이는 기법으로 이름을 날렸다 : 옮긴이.\n\"고마워요. 그때 이후로 솜씨가 엄청나게 늘었군요. 축하해요. 그런데 '신혼여행 낙원'에서 외람되지만 한 말씀 드려도 될까요? 당신네 호텔 저녁 식사 시간이 다섯 시던데요.\"\n\n로리는 말하면서 일어나, 미소와 함께 정중히 고개 숙여 그림들을 돌려주고는, 도덕적인 훈계에도 끝이 있어야 한다는 것을 일깨우려는 듯 시계를 들여다보았다. 그는 예전의 편안하고 무심한 태도를 되찾으려 애썼지만, 이제 그것은 꾸민 태도에 불과했다. 그를 흔들어 깨운 것이 그가 인정하려는 것보다 훨씬 큰 효과를 냈기 때문이다. 에이미는 그의 태도에 드리운 차가운 그늘을 느끼고 속으로 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"이제 내가 저이 기분을 상하게 했네. 음, 그게 저이한테 도움이 된다면 기쁘고, 저이가 날 미워하게 된다면 슬프지만, 사실인 건 사실이니 한마디도 도로 거둘 순 없어.\"\n\n두 사람은 집으로 오는 내내 웃고 떠들었고, 뒤편에 앉은 어린 바티스트는 무슈와 마드무아젤이 기분이 아주 좋은 모양이라고 생각했다. 하지만 둘 다 마음이 편치 않았다. 다정한 솔직함이 흐트러졌고, 햇살 위에 그늘이 드리웠으며, 겉으로는 명랑해 보였어도 각자의 마음속에는 남모를 불만이 자리 잡고 있었다.\n\n\"오늘 저녁에 만날까요, 몽 프레르?\" 에이미가 숙모의 집 앞에서 헤어지며 물었다.\n\n\"안타깝게도 선약이 있어요. 오 르부아르, 마드무아젤.\" 그러면서 로리는 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추려는 듯, 다른 많은 남자들보다 그에게 더 잘 어울리는 그 이국적인 방식으로 몸을 숙였다. 그의 얼굴에 어린 무언가가 에이미로 하여금 다급하고 다정하게 말하게 했다.\n\n\"안 돼요. 나한테는 본래의 당신으로 있어 줘요, 로리. 그리고 그 좋은 옛날 방식으로 헤어지자고요. 난 프랑스식의 그 모든 감상적인 인사보다 영국식의 힘찬 악수가 더 좋아요.\"\n\n\"잘 있어요, 그럼.\" 그녀가 좋아하는 그 어조로 이 말을 건네고는, 거의 아플 만큼 힘찬 악수를 끝으로 로리는 그녀 곁을 떠났다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 평소의 방문 대신 에이미는 한 통의 쪽지를 받았는데, 그 글은 시작 부분에서는 그녀를 미소 짓게 했고 끝부분에서는 한숨짓게 했다.\n\n\"나의 멘토에게,\n숙모님께 작별 인사를 전해 주시고, 마음속으로 의기양양해하셔도 좋습니다. '게으른 로런스'가 더없이 착한 소년처럼 할아버지께로 갔으니까요. 즐거운 겨울 보내시길, 그리고 신들께서 발로사에서의 더없이 행복한 신혼여행을 당신께 허락하시기를! 프레드에게는 한바탕 흔들어 깨우는 게 약이 될 것 같군요. 내 축하의 말과 함께 그렇게 전해 주세요.\n\n감사를 담아, 텔레마코스.\"\n\n* 멘토·텔레마코스 : 호메로스의 서사시에서 멘토는 텔레마코스를 이끈 스승. 에이미가 자신을 가르쳤다는 뜻에서 로리가 스스로를 텔레마코스에, 에이미를 멘토에 빗댄 것이다 : 옮긴이.\n\"착하기도 하지! 떠났다니 다행이야.\" 에이미가 흐뭇한 미소를 지으며 말했다. 그러나 다음 순간, 텅 빈 방을 둘러보던 그녀의 얼굴은 어두워졌고, 그녀는 저도 모르게 한숨을 쉬며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그래, 다행이긴 한데, 그이가 얼마나 그리울까!\"\n\n# THE VALLEY OF THE SHADOW.\n\n처음의 쓰라림이 가시자, 가족은 피할 수 없는 일을 받아들이고 그것을 의연하게 견디려 애썼다. 어려운 시절에 한 가정을 다정하게 묶어 주는, 한층 깊어진 애정으로 서로를 도우면서. 그들은 슬픔을 한쪽으로 밀어 두고, 그 마지막 한 해를 행복한 한 해로 만들기 위해 저마다 제 몫을 다했다.\n\n집에서 가장 쾌적한 방을 베스에게 따로 마련해 주었고, 그 방에는 베스가 가장 사랑하는 모든 것을 모아 두었다. 꽃, 그림, 그녀의 피아노, 작은 반짇고리, 그리고 사랑하는 고양이들. 아버지의 가장 아끼는 책들이 그리로 옮겨졌고, 어머니의 안락의자며, 조의 책상이며, 에이미의 가장 멋진 스케치들도 그리로 왔다. 그리고 날마다 메그는 베스 이모에게 햇살을 비춰 주려고 사랑스러운 순례 길에 아기들을 데려왔다. 존은 그 환자에게 그녀가 좋아하고 그리워하는 과일을 끊이지 않게 대 주는 기쁨을 누리려고 조용히 약간의 돈을 따로 떼어 두었고, 늙은 해나는 변덕스러운 입맛을 돋우려 맛깔스러운 음식을 만드는 일에 도무지 지치는 법이 없었으니, 일하는 내내 눈물을 떨구었다. 그리고 바다 건너편에서는 자그마한 선물과 명랑한 편지들이 와서, 겨울을 모르는 땅에서 따스함과 향기의 숨결을 실어 오는 듯했다.\n\n여기, 신전 안에 모셔진 가정의 성인처럼, 베스는 여느 때나 다름없이 평온하고 부지런하게 앉아 있었다. 그 무엇도 그 곱고 헌신적인 천성을 바꾸지 못했으니, 삶을 떠날 채비를 하는 와중에도 그녀는 뒤에 남을 이들을 위해 삶을 더 행복하게 만들려 애썼다. 가냘픈 손가락은 한시도 놀지 않았고, 그녀의 즐거움 가운데 하나는 날마다 오가는 학교 아이들을 위해 자그마한 것들을 만드는 일이었다. 보랏빛으로 곱은 손을 위해 창문으로 벙어리장갑 한 켤레를 떨어뜨려 주고, 인형을 잔뜩 거느린 어린 엄마에게는 바늘집을, 갈고리 모양 글자의 숲을 헤쳐 나가는 어린 글공부꾼에게는 펜 닦개를, 그림 좋아하는 눈을 위해서는 스크랩북을, 그 밖에도 온갖 즐거운 물건을 만들어 주었다. 그리하여 배움의 사다리를 마지못해 오르던 아이들은 제 갈 길에 꽃이 뿌려진 것을 발견하게 되었고, 저 위에 앉아 자기들의 취향과 필요에 신통하게도 꼭 맞는 선물을 비처럼 뿌려 주는 그 다정한 분을, 일종의 요정 대모로 여기게 되었다. 베스가 어떤 보답을 바랐다면, 그녀는 늘 자기 창문을 올려다보며 고개를 끄덕이고 미소 짓는 그 환한 작은 얼굴들에서, 그리고 잉크 얼룩과 고마움으로 가득한 우스꽝스러운 작은 편지들 속에서 그 보답을 찾았다.\n\n처음 몇 달은 무척 행복했다. 베스는 자주 주위를 둘러보며 \"정말 아름다워!\" 하고 말하곤 했다. 모두가 그녀의 볕 잘 드는 방에 함께 모여 앉아, 아기들이 바닥에서 발길질하며 옹알이하고, 어머니와 자매들이 곁에서 바느질하며, 아버지가 그 다정한 목소리로 지혜로운 옛 책들을 읽어 주던 때 말이다. 그 책들은 좋고 위로가 되는 말씀이 풍성하여, 수백 년 전에 쓰였을 때나 지금이나 한결같이 들어맞는 듯했다. 그곳은 작은 예배당이었고, 아버지라는 사제가 자기 양 떼에게 누구나 배워야 할 그 어려운 가르침을 일러 주며, 희망이 사랑을 위로할 수 있고 믿음이 체념을 가능하게 한다는 것을 보여 주려 애썼다. 듣는 이의 영혼에 곧장 가닿는 소박한 설교였다. 그 목사의 신앙 속에 아버지의 마음이 깃들어 있었기에, 자주 떨리던 그 목소리가 그가 말하거나 읽는 말씀에 곱절의 웅변을 더해 주었던 것이다.\n\n다가올 슬픈 시간을 위한 준비로 이 평화로운 시간이 그들에게 주어진 것은 모두에게 다행한 일이었다. 머지않아 베스가 바늘이 \"너무 무겁다\"고 말하며 그것을 영영 내려놓았기 때문이다. 말하는 것이 그녀를 지치게 했고, 사람들의 얼굴이 그녀를 괴롭혔으며, 고통이 그녀를 제 것이라 주장했고, 그 평온한 영혼은 가냘픈 육신을 괴롭히는 병고에 슬프도록 흔들렸다. 아, 그 얼마나 무거운 나날이었던가, 그 얼마나 길고 긴 밤이었던가, 그 얼마나 미어지는 가슴과 애원하는 기도였던가. 그녀를 가장 사랑하는 이들이, 그 야윈 손이 자기들을 향해 애원하듯 뻗쳐 오는 것을 보아야 했고, \"도와줘, 도와줘!\" 하는 쓰라린 울부짖음을 들어야 했으며, 그러면서도 도울 길이 없음을 느껴야 했으니. 평온하던 영혼의 슬픈 일식이요, 젊은 생명이 죽음과 벌이는 모진 싸움이었다. 하지만 둘 다 자비롭게도 짧았고, 이윽고 그 자연스러운 저항이 끝나자 옛 평화가 그 어느 때보다 아름답게 되돌아왔다. 가녀린 육신이 무너져 가는 가운데, 베스의 영혼은 강해졌다. 그녀는 말은 거의 없었지만, 곁에 있는 이들은 그녀가 채비를 마쳤음을 느꼈고, 가장 먼저 부름받은 순례자가 또한 가장 합당한 자임을 보았으며, 그녀와 함께 강기슭에서 기다렸다. 그녀가 강을 건널 때 그녀를 맞으러 오는 빛나는 이들을 보려 애쓰면서.\n\n조는 베스가 \"네가 곁에 있으면 더 힘이 나\"라고 말한 뒤로, 단 한 시간도 그녀 곁을 떠나지 않았다. 그녀는 방 안의 긴 의자에서 자다가 자주 깨어 불을 다시 지피고, 좀처럼 무엇도 청하는 법 없이 \"폐가 되지 않으려\" 애쓰는 그 가여운 환자에게 먹을 것을 떠먹이고, 일으켜 앉히고, 시중을 들었다. 온종일 그녀는 그 방을 떠나지 못하고 다른 간병인이 끼어드는 것을 질투했으며, 그때 간병할 이로 뽑힌 것을, 제 인생이 가져다준 그 어떤 영예보다 더 자랑스러워했다. 조에게는 더없이 소중하고 보탬이 되는 시간이었다. 이제 그녀의 마음이 꼭 필요한 가르침을 받았으니까. 인내의 교훈이 어찌나 곱게 가르쳐졌던지 그녀는 그것을 배우지 않을 수 없었다. 모두를 향한 자애, 모진 처사를 용서하고 참되이 잊을 줄 아는 그 사랑스러운 마음, 가장 힘든 일도 수월하게 만드는 의무에 대한 충실함, 그리고 아무것도 두려워하지 않고 의심 없이 믿는 진실한 신앙을.\n\n조는 잠에서 깰 때면 베스가 손때 묻은 작은 책을 읽고 있는 것을 자주 보았고, 잠 못 드는 밤을 달래려 가만히 노래 부르는 것을 듣기도 했으며, 투명한 손가락 사이로 느린 눈물이 떨어지는 동안 두 손에 얼굴을 묻는 것을 보기도 했다. 그러면 조는 누운 채로 그녀를 지켜보며, 눈물로도 다 담을 수 없을 만큼 깊은 생각에 잠기곤 했다. 베스가 그 소박하고 헌신적인 방식으로, 위로의 거룩한 말씀과 조용한 기도와 그토록 사랑하던 음악을 통해, 정든 옛 삶에서 자신을 떼어 내고 다가올 삶에 자신을 맞추려 애쓰고 있음을 느끼면서.\n\n이것을 지켜보는 일은, 그 어떤 목소리로 읊어진 가장 지혜로운 설교나, 가장 성스러운 찬송가나, 가장 간절한 기도보다도 조에게 더 큰 것을 해 주었다. 숱한 눈물로 맑아진 눈과, 더없이 다정한 슬픔으로 보드라워진 마음으로, 그녀는 언니의 삶이 지닌 아름다움을 알아보게 되었으니까. 굵직한 사건도 없고 야망도 없었으나, \"흙먼지 속에서도 향기를 풍기며 피어나는\" 참된 미덕으로 가득했던 삶을. 땅 위에서 가장 비천한 이를 하늘에서 가장 먼저 기억하게 하는 그 자기 망각을, 누구에게나 가능한 그 진정한 성공을.\n\n어느 밤, 베스가 고통만큼이나 견디기 힘든 그 죽을 것 같은 피로를 잊게 해 줄 무언가를 찾으려고 탁자 위의 책들을 뒤지다가, 가장 좋아하던 낡은 『천로역정』의 책장을 넘기던 중, 조의 필체로 휘갈겨 쓴 작은 종이 한 장을 발견했다. 제 이름이 눈에 들어왔고, 흐릿하게 번진 글줄들로 미루어 그 위에 눈물이 떨어졌었음을 확신했다.\n\n\"가엾은 조! 곤히 잠들어 있으니 깨워서 허락을 구하진 않을래. 언닌 나한테 뭐든 다 보여 주잖아. 그러니 이걸 봐도 마음 쓰지 않을 거야.\" 베스는 양탄자 위에 누운 언니를 흘끗 보며 생각했다. 조는 장작이 무너지는 순간 곧장 깰 채비로, 부젓가락을 곁에 둔 채 누워 있었다.\n\n\"나의 베스에게.\n축복의 빛이 찾아올 때까지 그늘 속에 묵묵히 앉아, 고요하고 성스러운 그 존재가 근심 어린 우리 집을 거룩하게 하네. 땅 위의 기쁨도 희망도 슬픔도 깊고 엄숙한 강의 기슭에서 잔물결처럼 부서지나니, 이제 그녀의 발길이 기꺼이 그곳에 서 있네.\n\n오 나의 언니, 내게서 떠나 인간의 근심과 다툼에서 벗어나는 이여, 당신의 삶을 아름답게 한 그 미덕들을 선물로 내게 남겨 주오. 사랑하는 언니, 그 위대한 인내를 물려주오, 고통의 감옥 속에서도 밝고 불평 없는 정신을 지탱할 힘을 지닌 그 인내를.\n\n내게 주오, 절실히 필요하니, 지혜롭고 다정한 그 용기를, 당신의 기꺼운 발길 아래 의무의 길을 푸르게 만든 그 용기를. 그 사심 없는 천성을 내게 주오, 거룩한 자애로 사랑을 위해 잘못을 용서할 줄 아는 그 마음을— 온유한 마음이여, 나의 잘못을 용서해 주오!\n\n그리하여 우리의 이별은 날마다 그 쓰라린 아픔을 조금씩 덜어 가고, 이 모진 가르침을 배우는 동안 나의 큰 상실은 나의 얻음이 되네. 슬픔의 손길이 나의 거친 천성을 한결 평온하게 하고, 삶에 새로운 열망을, 보이지 않는 것에 대한 새 믿음을 줄 테니.\n\n이제부터 강을 무사히 건너, 나는 영원히 보게 되리, 강기슭에서 나를 기다리는 사랑하는, 가정의 영혼을. 나의 슬픔에서 태어난 희망과 믿음이 수호천사가 되어, 나보다 먼저 떠난 언니가 그들의 손을 빌려 나를 집으로 이끌어 주리.\"\n\n글줄들은 흐릿하게 번지고 얼룩지고, 서툴고 힘이 없었지만, 베스의 얼굴에 이루 말할 수 없는 위안의 빛을 가져다주었다. 그녀의 한 가지 회한은 자기가 한 일이 너무 적었다는 것이었는데, 이 글이 그녀에게, 그녀의 삶이 헛되지 않았음을, 그녀의 죽음이 그녀가 두려워하던 절망을 불러오지 않으리란 것을 확신시켜 주는 듯했기 때문이다. 그녀가 두 손 사이에 종이를 접어 쥔 채 앉아 있는데, 까맣게 탄 장작이 두 동강 났다. 조가 벌떡 일어나 불길을 되살리고는 베스가 잠들었기를 바라며 침대 곁으로 살금살금 다가왔다.\n\n\"잠든 거 아냐, 오히려 무척 행복해, 조. 이것 봐, 내가 이걸 발견해서 읽었어. 언니가 괜찮아할 줄 알았거든. 내가 너한테 정말 이런 사람이었니, 조?\" 베스가 그립고도 겸허한 진심을 담아 물었다.\n\n\"오 베스, 정말 그래, 정말 그렇고말고!\" 그러면서 조의 머리가 언니 곁의 베개 위로 떨어졌다.\n\n\"그렇다면 내 인생을 허비한 게 아닌 것 같아. 난 네가 그려 준 만큼 착하진 않지만, 옳게 살려고 애쓰긴 했거든. 그리고 이제 더 잘 살아 보기를 시작하기에도 너무 늦은 마당에, 누군가 날 이렇게나 사랑하고, 내가 그들에게 도움이 됐다고 느낀다는 걸 아는 게 이렇게 위안이 되네.\"\n\n\"세상 그 누구보다도 그래, 베스. 난 언닐 보낼 수 없을 것만 같았어. 하지만 이젠 내가 언닐 잃는 게 아니라는 걸, 언니가 그 어느 때보다 내게 더 큰 존재가 되리란 걸, 죽음이 우릴 갈라놓는 것 같아도 결코 갈라놓을 수 없다는 걸 느끼는 법을 배워 가고 있어.\"\n\n\"그럴 수 없다는 거, 나도 알아. 그래서 더는 두렵지 않아. 난 분명 여전히 너의 베스로 남아서, 그 어느 때보다 더 너를 사랑하고 돕게 될 테니까. 네가 내 자리를 대신해 줘야 해, 조. 내가 떠나고 나면 아버지 어머니께 모든 것이 되어 드려. 두 분은 너한테 기대실 테니, 두 분을 저버리지 마. 그리고 혼자 애쓰는 게 힘들거든, 내가 너를 잊지 않는다는 걸, 그리고 그렇게 하는 것이 멋진 책을 쓰거나 온 세상을 구경하는 것보다 너를 더 행복하게 하리란 걸 기억해. 사랑이야말로 우리가 떠날 때 함께 가져갈 수 있는 유일한 것이고, 그것이 마지막을 그토록 수월하게 해 주니까.\"\n\n\"그렇게 할게, 베스.\" 그리고 바로 그때 그 자리에서, 조는 옛 야망을 버리고 더 새롭고 더 나은 야망에 자신을 바치기로 다짐했다. 다른 욕망들의 빈곤함을 인정하며, 사랑의 불멸에 대한 믿음이 주는 그 복된 위안을 느끼면서.\n\n그렇게 봄날은 오고 갔고, 하늘은 더 맑아지고, 땅은 더 푸르러지고, 꽃들은 곱고 일찍 피어났으며, 새들은 베스에게 작별을 고하러 때맞춰 돌아왔다. 베스는 지치되 미더운 아이처럼, 평생 자기를 이끌어 준 그 손들을 꼭 붙들었고, 아버지와 어머니는 죽음의 골짜기를 지나는 그녀를 다정하게 인도하여 하느님께 그녀를 내드렸다.\n\n책 속에서가 아니고서는, 죽어 가는 이가 기억에 남을 말을 하거나, 환영을 보거나, 거룩한 빛이 어린 얼굴로 떠나는 일은 좀처럼 없다. 그리고 떠나는 영혼을 숱하게 배웅해 본 이들은, 대다수에게 끝이란 잠처럼 자연스럽고 단순하게 찾아온다는 것을 안다. 베스가 바라던 대로 \"물결은 수월하게 빠져나갔다.\" 그리고 동트기 전 어두운 시각, 처음 숨을 들이쉬던 그 품 안에서, 그녀는 단 한 번의 사랑스러운 눈길과 단 한 번의 작은 한숨 말고는 작별 인사도 없이, 조용히 마지막 숨을 거두었다.\n\n눈물과 기도와 다정한 손길로, 어머니와 자매들은 고통이 다시는 그르칠 수 없는 그 긴 잠을 위해 그녀를 단장해 주었다. 그토록 오래 그들의 가슴을 미어지게 했던 가련한 인내가 이내 물러나고 그 자리에 깃든 아름다운 평온을 고마운 눈으로 바라보면서, 그리고 사랑하는 이에게 죽음이란 두려움 가득한 유령이 아니라 자애로운 천사임을 경건한 기쁨으로 느끼면서.\n\n아침이 밝았을 때, 여러 달 만에 처음으로 난롯불은 꺼져 있었고, 조의 자리는 비어 있었으며, 방은 무척 고요했다. 하지만 바로 곁의 움트는 나뭇가지 위에서 새 한 마리가 명랑하게 노래했고, 창가에는 스노드롭이 싱그럽게 피어났으며, 봄 햇살이 축복처럼 흘러들어 베개 위의 그 평온한 얼굴을 비추었다. 고통 없는 평화로 그토록 가득한 얼굴이라, 그녀를 가장 사랑하던 이들은 눈물 너머로 미소 지으며, 베스가 마침내 평안해졌음을 하느님께 감사드렸다.\n\n# LEARNING TO FORGET.\n\n에이미의 훈계는 로리에게 도움이 되었다. 물론 그는 한참 뒤에야 그것을 인정했지만 말이다. 남자들이란 좀처럼 그러지 않는 법이다. 여자가 충고를 하면, 만물의 영장이라는 그들은 그것이 마침 자기가 하려던 바로 그것이라고 스스로를 설득하기 전까지는 충고를 받아들이지 않으니까. 그렇게 설득하고 나서야 그대로 행동하고, 만약 그것이 성공하면 그 공의 절반을 약한 그릇인 여자에게 돌리며, 만약 실패하면 너그럽게도 그 책임 전부를 여자에게 떠넘긴다. 로리는 할아버지에게로 돌아갔고, 몇 주 동안 어찌나 효성스럽게 헌신했던지, 노신사는 니스의 기후가 손자를 놀랍게 좋아지게 했으니 다시 한번 가 보는 게 좋겠다고 말했다. 이 젊은 신사로서야 그보다 더 반가운 일이 없었겠지만, 그렇게 한바탕 꾸중을 들은 마당에 코끼리로 끌어도 그를 다시 그리로 데려갈 수는 없었을 것이다. 자존심이 그것을 허락하지 않았으니, 그리움이 몹시 강해질 때마다 그는 가장 깊은 인상을 남긴 그 말들을 되뇌며 결심을 다졌다. \"난 당신을 경멸해요.\" \"멋진 일을 해서 그 애가 당신을 사랑하게 만들어요.\"\n\n로리는 그 문제를 마음속으로 하도 여러 번 곱씹은 끝에, 자기가 이기적이고 게을렀음을 인정하기에 이르렀다. 하지만 한 남자에게 큰 슬픔이 닥쳤을 때는, 그것을 이겨 낼 때까지 온갖 변덕을 너그러이 봐줘야 마땅한 법이다. 그는 이제 자기의 짓밟힌 애정이 완전히 죽었다고 느꼈다. 그리고 비록 자기가 영영 충실한 애도자이기를 그치지 않으리라 해도, 그 상복을 굳이 요란하게 떨치고 다닐 까닭은 없었다. 조는 그를 사랑하지 않겠지만, 소녀의 \"싫어요\" 한마디가 자기 인생을 망쳐 놓지 못했음을 증명할 무언가를 해냄으로써, 그녀가 자기를 존경하고 흠모하게 만들 수는 있으리라. 그는 늘 무언가를 해낼 작정이었으니, 에이미의 충고는 사실 필요 없는 것이었다. 그저 앞서 말한 그 짓밟힌 애정이 점잖게 묻힐 때까지 기다리고 있었을 뿐이었다. 그 장례가 끝나자, 그는 이제 \"상처받은 가슴을 감추고 묵묵히 힘써 나갈\" 준비가 되었다고 느꼈다.\n\n괴테가 기쁨이나 슬픔이 생기면 그것을 노래로 지었던 것처럼, 로리도 자기의 사랑의 슬픔을 음악 속에 방부 처리해 두기로 마음먹고, 조의 영혼을 뒤흔들고 듣는 이마다 가슴을 녹일 진혼곡을 작곡하기로 했다. 그리하여 다음번에 노신사가 그가 다시 안절부절못하며 침울해하는 것을 보고 어디든 가라고 명하자, 그는 음악을 하는 친구들이 있는 빈으로 가서, 반드시 두각을 나타내고 말겠다는 굳은 결심으로 작업에 매달렸다. 하지만 그 슬픔이 음악에 담기에 너무 거대했던 탓인지, 아니면 음악이 너무 영묘하여 인간의 비애를 떠받치기에는 역부족이었던 탓인지, 그는 적어도 지금으로서는 그 진혼곡이 제 능력 밖임을 곧 깨달았다. 그의 정신이 아직 제대로 돌아갈 상태가 아니고, 그의 착상이 정리될 필요가 있다는 것이 분명했다. 처량한 가락을 짓는 도중에, 그는 어느새 자기가 니스의 크리스마스 무도회를, 특히 그 뚱뚱한 프랑스 남자를 생생히 떠올리게 하는 흥겨운 춤곡을 흥얼거리고 있음을 발견하기 일쑤였고, 그러면 비극적인 작곡은 그것으로 영락없이 중단되곤 했으니 말이다.\n\n그래서 그는 오페라를 시도해 보았다. 처음에는 무엇 하나 불가능해 보이지 않았으니까. 하지만 여기서도 또다시 뜻밖의 난관이 그를 가로막았다. 그는 조를 자기 작품의 여주인공으로 삼고 싶어, 그 사랑에 대한 다정한 추억과 낭만적인 환상을 떠올려 달라고 기억에 청했다. 그런데 기억은 배신자로 돌변했다. 마치 그 소녀의 짓궂은 혼령에라도 씐 듯, 기억은 오로지 조의 별난 점과 결점과 변덕만을 떠올려 주었고, 그녀를 더없이 비낭만적인 모습으로만 보여 주었다. 반다나로 머리를 동여맨 채 깔개를 두드려 먼지를 털어 내거나, 소파 쿠션으로 자기를 둘러싸 바리케이드를 치거나, 거미지 식으로 그의 열정에 찬물을 끼얹는 모습 따위로 말이다. 그러면 도무지 참을 수 없는 웃음이 그가 그리려 애쓰던 시름 어린 그림을 망쳐 놓았다. 조는 어떤 값을 치러도 오페라 속에 들어가려 하지 않았고, 그는 \"저 애 좀 보게, 정말 골칫덩어리라니까!\" 하면서, 심란한 작곡가답게 머리칼을 움켜쥔 채 그녀를 포기해야 했다.\n\n* 거미지 : 찰스 디킨스의 소설 『데이비드 코퍼필드』에 나오는 인물로, 늘 신세를 한탄하며 우는소리를 하는 과부 : 옮긴이.\n그래서 그가 가락 속에 영원히 남길, 좀 더 다루기 쉬운 다른 처녀를 찾아 주위를 둘러보자, 기억은 더없이 고분고분한 태도로 하나를 내놓았다. 이 환영은 여러 얼굴을 하고 있었지만, 언제나 금발이었고, 비치는 듯한 구름에 감싸여, 장미와 공작과 흰 조랑말과 푸른 리본이 어우러진 기분 좋은 혼돈 속에서 그의 마음의 눈앞에 사뿐히 떠다녔다. 그는 이 흡족한 환영에 아무 이름도 붙이지 않았지만, 그녀를 자기 작품의 여주인공으로 삼고는 꽤 좋아하게 되었다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그는 그녀에게 하늘 아래 온갖 재능과 우아함을 부여하고, 어떤 인간 여자라도 산산조각 냈을 시련들을 그녀가 흠집 하나 없이 헤쳐 나가도록 호위해 주었으니까.\n\n이 영감 덕분에 그는 한동안 일이 순조롭게 풀렸지만, 차츰 그 작업은 매력을 잃어 갔고, 펜을 손에 쥔 채 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있거나, 새 착상을 얻고 정신을 새롭게 하려고 그 화려한 도시를 이리저리 쏘다니는 동안 작곡하는 것을 잊어버렸다. 그해 겨울 그의 마음은 어쩐지 어수선한 상태인 듯했다. 그는 한 일이 별로 없었지만 생각은 무척 많이 했고, 자기도 모르게 어떤 변화가 일어나고 있음을 의식했다. \"어쩌면 천재성이 무르익는 중인지도 몰라. 푹 익도록 내버려 두고 무엇이 나오는지 봐야지.\" 그는 그렇게 말했지만, 그 내내 마음 한구석에는 그것이 천재성이 아니라 훨씬 더 흔한 무언가라는 은밀한 의심이 자리하고 있었다. 그것이 무엇이었든 간에 어떤 결실을 향해 무르익어 갔으니, 그는 그 종잡을 수 없는 생활에 점점 더 불만을 느끼게 되었고, 영혼과 육신을 다 바쳐 매달릴 진실하고 진지한 일을 갈망하기 시작했으며, 마침내 음악을 사랑하는 사람이라고 다 작곡가는 아니라는 현명한 결론에 이르렀다. 왕립 극장에서 훌륭하게 공연된 모차르트의 웅장한 오페라 한 편을 보고 돌아온 그는, 자기가 쓴 것을 훑어보고 가장 잘된 대목 몇을 연주해 본 다음, 멘델스존과 베토벤과 바흐의 흉상을 올려다보며 앉아 있었다. 그들도 인자하게 그를 마주 내려다보았다. 그러다 그는 별안간 악보를 한 장 한 장 찢어 버렸고, 마지막 한 장이 손에서 팔랑팔랑 떨어져 나가자 차분히 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n\"에이미 말이 옳아! 재능은 천재성이 아니고, 그걸 천재성으로 만들 수도 없어. 로마가 그 애한테서 허영심을 앗아 갔듯, 이 음악이 나한테서 허영심을 앗아 갔군. 더는 사기꾼 노릇 안 할 테야. 자, 이제 뭘 한다?\"\n\n그것은 답하기 어려운 질문인 듯했고, 로리는 자기가 날마다 먹을 빵을 벌어야 하는 처지였더라면 좋았겠다는 생각마저 들기 시작했다. 지금이야말로, 언젠가 그가 거칠게 표현한 대로 \"타락의 길로 빠지기\"에 더없이 알맞은 기회였다. 돈은 넘쳐 나는데 할 일은 없었고, 사탄은 속담대로 손이 차고 한가한 자에게 일거리를 마련해 주기를 즐기는 법이니까. 가엾은 친구에게는 밖에서도 안에서도 유혹이 차고 넘쳤지만, 그는 그것을 꽤나 잘 견뎌 냈다. 그가 자유를 소중히 여겼던 만큼이나, 아니 그 이상으로 신의와 신뢰를 소중히 여겼기 때문이다. 그래서 할아버지에게 한 약속과, 자기를 사랑하는 여인들의 눈을 떳떳이 들여다보며 \"다 잘 지내고 있어요\"라고 말할 수 있기를 바라는 마음이 그를 안전하고 흔들림 없이 지켜 주었다.\n\n아마 어떤 그런디 부인은 이렇게 말하리라. \"난 그 말 못 믿겠어. 사내아이는 사내아이일 뿐이고, 젊은 남자는 한 번쯤 방탕을 부려 봐야 하는 법이며, 여자들이 기적을 바라선 안 되지.\" 그래요, 그런디 부인, 당신이야 못 믿겠지요. 하지만 그래도 그것은 사실입니다. 여자들은 숱한 기적을 일으키니까요. 그리고 나는, 여자들이 그런 말에 맞장구치기를 거부함으로써 남자다움의 기준을 끌어올리는 기적마저도 일으킬 수 있으리라 믿습니다. 사내아이는 사내아이답게, 되도록 오래 그렇게 두어요. 젊은 남자가 굳이 방탕을 부려야겠다면 그러게 두고요. 하지만 어머니와 누이와 벗들은, 선량한 여자들의 눈에 남자를 가장 남자답게 만드는 그 미덕에 대한 충실함이 가능함을 믿고 또 믿는다는 것을 보여 줌으로써, 그 수확을 적게 만들고 숱한 잡초가 추수를 망치지 못하게 도울 수 있습니다. 만약 그것이 여자들의 망상이라면, 망상일망정 누릴 수 있을 때 누리게 두어요. 그것이 없다면 삶의 아름다움과 낭만의 절반은 사라질 테고, 슬픈 예감이 우리의 모든 희망을 쓰디쓰게 할 테니까요. 아직도 자기 자신보다 어머니를 더 사랑하고, 그것을 부끄러워하지 않고 인정하는, 용감하고 마음 여린 어린 사내아이들에 대한 희망 말입니다.\n\n로리는 조에 대한 사랑을 잊는 일이 앞으로 몇 년 동안 자기의 모든 기력을 빨아들이리라 여겼다. 하지만 그를 무척 놀라게 한 것은, 그 일이 날마다 점점 더 수월해진다는 사실이었다. 그는 처음에는 그것을 믿으려 하지 않았고, 스스로에게 화를 냈으며, 도무지 영문을 알 수 없었다. 하지만 우리네 마음이란 묘하고 어긋나는 물건이라, 시간과 자연은 우리야 어쨌든 제 뜻대로 일을 해치우는 법이다. 로리의 마음은 좀처럼 아파 주려 하지 않았다. 상처는 그를 어리둥절하게 할 만큼 빠르게 줄곧 아물어 갔고, 잊으려 애쓰는 대신 그는 어느새 자기가 기억해 내려 애쓰고 있음을 발견했다. 그는 일이 이렇게 돌아가리라곤 예상하지 못했기에 미처 마음의 준비가 되어 있지 않았다. 그는 자기 자신에게 정나미가 떨어졌고, 자기의 변덕스러움에 놀랐으며, 그토록 엄청난 타격에서 이렇게나 빨리 회복될 수 있다는 데에 실망과 안도가 묘하게 뒤섞인 감정으로 가득했다. 그는 꺼져 가는 사랑의 잿불을 조심스레 들쑤셔 보았지만, 그것은 좀처럼 불길로 타오르려 하지 않았다. 그저 아늑한 온기만이 남아 있을 뿐이었다.\n그것은 그를 열에 들뜨게 하지 않으면서도 따뜻하게 데워 주고 기운을 북돋워 주는 무엇이었다. 그리하여 그는 마지못해 인정할 수밖에 없었다. 소년다운 격정이 서서히 가라앉으면서 한결 차분한 감정으로 바뀌어 가고 있다는 것을. 그 감정은 무척 부드럽고, 아직 조금은 슬프고 원망스럽기도 했지만, 시간이 지나면 틀림없이 사라지고, 끝까지 변치 않을 형제 같은 애정만 남을 터였다.\n\n그렇게 이런저런 몽상에 잠겨 있던 어느 순간, \"형제 같은\"이라는 말이 머릿속을 스치자 그는 빙긋 웃으며 눈앞에 걸린 모차르트의 초상화를 올려다보았다.\n\n\"그래, 그분은 위대한 사람이었지. 한 자매를 얻을 수 없게 되자 다른 자매를 택했고, 그래서 행복했으니까.\"\n\n로리는 그 말을 입 밖에 내지는 않았으나 마음속으로 그렇게 생각했다. 그러고는 이내 손가락에 낀 작고 낡은 반지에 입을 맞추며 스스로에게 말했다.\n\n\"아니야, 그러지 않을 거야! 난 잊지 않았어, 결코 잊을 수 없어. 다시 한번 부딪쳐 볼 거야. 그래도 안 된다면, 그땐, 그때는—\"\n\n말을 끝맺지 못한 채 그는 펜과 종이를 집어 들고 조에게 편지를 썼다. 조가 마음을 바꿀 가능성이 조금이라도 남아 있는 한 자기는 무슨 일에도 도무지 마음을 붙일 수 없다고. 그러니 마음을 바꿔 줄 수는 없겠느냐고, 그래서 자기가 집으로 돌아가 행복해질 수 있게 해 줄 수는 없겠느냐고. 답장을 기다리는 동안 그는 아무 일도 하지 못했지만, 그 아무것도 하지 않는 일만큼은 열정적으로 해냈다. 조바심에 온몸이 달아올랐기 때문이다. 마침내 답장이 왔고, 적어도 한 가지만은 그의 마음을 확실하게 정리해 주었다. 조는 단호하게 마음을 바꿀 수도, 바꾸려 하지도 않았던 것이다. 조는 베스에게 온통 마음을 쏟고 있었고, \"사랑\"이라는 말은 두 번 다시 듣고 싶지 않다고 했다. 그러면서 부디 다른 사람과 행복해지라고, 다만 늘 자기를 사랑하는 누이 조를 위해 마음 한구석은 조금 비워 두라고 당부했다. 추신에서는 베스가 더 나빠졌다는 사실을 에이미에게는 알리지 말아 달라고 했다. 에이미는 봄에 집으로 돌아올 텐데, 남은 여행을 굳이 슬프게 만들 필요는 없으니까. 하느님이 허락하신다면 그때 알려도 늦지 않으리라고. 다만 로리가 자주 편지를 써서 에이미가 외롭거나, 향수에 젖거나, 불안해하지 않도록 해 주기를 바란다고 했다.\n\n\"그래, 당장 써야지. 가엾은 것, 집으로 돌아오는 길이 슬프기만 하겠구나.\" 그러고는 로리는 책상을 열었다. 마치 몇 주 전에 끝맺지 못한 그 문장의 마땅한 결말이 에이미에게 편지를 쓰는 일이라는 듯이.\n\n그러나 그날 편지를 쓰지는 못했다. 가장 좋은 편지지를 뒤지다가 마음을 바꿔 놓는 무언가와 마주쳤기 때문이다. 책상 한쪽, 청구서와 여권과 온갖 사무 서류들이 뒤엉킨 틈에 조의 편지 몇 통이 굴러다니고 있었다. 그리고 다른 칸에는 에이미가 보낸 쪽지 세 장이 그녀의 파란 리본 하나로 정성스레 묶여 있었는데, 그 안에 곱게 끼워 둔 빛바랜 장미 꽃잎이 은은하게 떠오를 만큼 향긋했다. 반쯤은 뉘우치고 반쯤은 재미있어하는 표정으로, 로리는 조의 편지를 모두 그러모아 반듯이 펴고 접어서 책상의 작은 서랍에 가지런히 넣었다. 그러고는 한참을 서서 손가락에 낀 반지를 생각에 잠겨 돌리다가, 천천히 빼서 편지와 함께 넣고 서랍을 잠갔다. 그런 다음 마치 장례를 치른 듯한 기분으로 성 슈테판 성당의 대미사를 들으러 나갔다. 슬픔에 짓눌린 것은 아니었지만, 사랑스러운 아가씨에게 편지를 쓰는 것보다는 그렇게 하루의 나머지를 보내는 편이 더 어울릴 것만 같았다.\n\n그렇지만 편지는 곧 부쳐졌고, 답장도 즉시 돌아왔다. 에이미는 정말로 향수병에 걸려 있었고, 더없이 사랑스럽고 솔직한 태도로 그것을 털어놓았기 때문이다. 편지 왕래는 보란 듯이 무르익었고, 편지는 이른 봄 내내 어김없이 규칙적으로 오갔다. 로리는 자기가 만든 흉상들을 팔아 치우고, 오페라 악보는 불쏘시개로 만들어 버린 뒤 파리로 돌아갔다. 머지않아 누군가 도착하기를 바라면서. 그는 니스로 가고 싶어 견딜 수가 없었지만, 청을 받기 전에는 가지 않을 작정이었다. 그리고 에이미는 그에게 와 달라고 청하지 않았다. 그 무렵 그녀는 그녀 나름의 작은 일들을 겪고 있었고, 그래서 \"우리 애\"의 짓궂은 눈길을 되도록 피하고 싶었기 때문이다.\n\n프레드 본이 돌아와, 한때 에이미가 \"네, 고마워요\" 하고 대답하기로 마음먹었던 바로 그 질문을 던졌다. 하지만 이제 그녀는 \"아니요, 고마워요\"라고, 다정하지만 단호하게 말했다. 정작 그때가 오자 용기가 나지 않았고, 부드러운 희망과 두려움으로 가득 차오른 가슴속의 새로운 갈망을 채우기에는 돈과 지위만으로는 부족하다는 것을 깨달았기 때문이다. \"프레드는 좋은 친구지만, 누나가 좋아할 만한 사람과는 전혀 거리가 멀어\"라던 말과, 그 말을 할 때 로리의 표정이 자꾸만 그녀에게 되돌아왔다. 마치 그녀 자신이 말로는 아니어도 눈빛으로 \"난 돈을 보고 결혼할 거야\"라고 했을 때의 그 표정이 끈질기게 되살아나듯이. 이제 그 일을 떠올리는 것이 그녀를 괴롭혔다. 도로 주워 담을 수 있었으면 싶었다. 그 말이 너무도 여자답지 못하게 들렸으니까. 그녀는 로리가 자기를 매정하고 속물적인 인간으로 여기는 게 싫었다. 이제는 사교계의 여왕이 되는 일보다 사랑스러운 여자가 되는 일이 곱절은 더 간절했다. 그녀가 했던 그 끔찍한 말들 때문에 로리가 자기를 미워하기는커녕 오히려 그것을 그토록 너그럽게 받아 주고 전보다 더 다정하게 굴어 주어 얼마나 다행인지 몰랐다. 그의 편지는 큰 위안이 되었다. 집에서 오는 편지는 들쭉날쭉한 데다, 어쩌다 와도 그의 편지만큼 흡족하지가 않았던 것이다. 그의 편지에 답장을 쓰는 것은 즐거움일 뿐 아니라 의무이기도 했다. 조가 한사코 매정하게 굴고 있으니, 그 가엾은 사람은 외로워하며 누군가의 다정함이 필요했으니까. 조도 좀 애를 써서 그를 사랑해 보려 했어야 했다. 그리 어려운 일도 아닐 텐데. 그렇게 다정한 청년이 마음을 써 준다면 많은 사람이 자랑스럽고 기쁠 텐데. 하지만 조는 결코 다른 여자들처럼 행동하지 않았다. 그러니 할 수 있는 일이라곤 그저 그에게 더없이 다정하게 굴면서 오빠 대하듯 하는 것뿐이었다.\n\n만약 세상의 모든 오빠들이 이 무렵 로리가 받은 만큼 잘 대접받는다면, 그들은 지금보다 훨씬 더 행복한 족속이 되었으리라. 에이미는 더 이상 잔소리를 하지 않았다. 무슨 일이든 그의 의견을 물었고, 그가 하는 모든 일에 관심을 보였으며, 사랑스러운 선물을 손수 만들어 주고, 일주일에 두 통씩 편지를 보냈다. 발랄한 이야기와 누이다운 속마음, 그리고 주위의 아름다운 풍경을 매혹적으로 그린 스케치로 가득한 편지를. 누이의 주머니 속에 편지가 담겨 다니고, 부지런히 읽고 또 읽히고, 짧으면 그것이 아쉬워 눈물 짓고, 길면 거기에 입을 맞추며, 고이고이 간직되는 영광을 누리는 오빠는 흔치 않으니, 에이미가 그런 애틋하고도 어리석은 짓들을 했다고 굳이 귀띔하지는 않으련다. 다만 그해 봄 그녀가 조금 창백해지고 수심에 잠긴 것만은 분명했다. 사교계의 흥취를 부쩍 잃고서, 혼자 스케치를 하러 나가는 일이 잦아졌다. 집에 돌아와도 내보일 만한 것은 거의 없었지만, 아마 자연을 공부하고 있었으리라. 발로사 테라스에 두 손을 모은 채 몇 시간이고 앉아 있거나, 문득 떠오르는 무엇이든 무심히 그려 보면서. 무덤에 새겨진 늠름한 기사라든가, 모자로 눈을 가린 채 풀밭에 잠든 청년이라든가, 화려하게 차려입은 곱슬머리 소녀가 키 큰 신사의 팔을 잡고 무도회장을 거니는 모습이라든가. 두 사람의 얼굴은 요즘 미술의 유행을 따라 흐릿하게 남겨 두었는데, 안전하기는 했지만 영 만족스럽지는 못했다.\n\n이모는 에이미가 프레드에게 한 대답을 후회한다고 여겼다. 아니라고 해 봤자 소용없고 설명하기도 불가능했으므로, 에이미는 이모가 좋을 대로 생각하게 내버려 두었다. 다만 프레드가 이집트로 떠났다는 사실만큼은 로리가 알도록 신경 썼다. 그게 다였지만 로리는 그 뜻을 알아차렸고, 안도한 표정으로 짐짓 어른스러운 태도를 지으며 혼자 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"역시 다시 생각할 줄 알았어. 가엾은 친구! 나도 다 겪어 본 일이니, 그 마음 알지.\"\n\n그러고는 크게 한숨을 내쉬더니, 마치 지난날에 대한 도리를 다했다는 듯 두 발을 소파에 올려놓고 에이미의 편지를 호사스럽게 음미했다.\n\n이렇게 외국에서 이런 변화들이 일어나는 동안, 집에는 불행이 닥쳐왔다. 그러나 베스가 위중하다는 편지는 끝내 에이미에게 닿지 못했고, 다음 편지가 그녀를 찾았을 때는 이미 동생의 무덤 위로 풀이 푸르게 돋아 있었다. 슬픈 소식은 브베에서 그녀를 맞았다. 5월의 더위에 쫓겨 니스를 떠난 일행은 제노바와 이탈리아 호수들을 거쳐 천천히 스위스로 옮겨 가 있던 참이었다. 에이미는 잘 견뎌 냈고, 여행을 앞당기지 말라는 가족의 결정에 조용히 따랐다. 이제 와 베스에게 작별 인사를 할 수도 없게 된 마당이니, 차라리 남아 있으면서 떨어져 지내는 동안 슬픔을 누그러뜨리는 편이 나았던 것이다. 그래도 그녀의 마음은 무겁기만 했다. 집에 가고 싶어 못 견뎌 하며, 날마다 호수 건너편을 애타게 바라보았다. 로리가 와서 자기를 위로해 주기를 기다리며.\n\n로리는 정말 곧 찾아왔다. 같은 우편으로 두 사람 모두에게 편지가 왔지만, 로리는 독일에 있었던 터라 닿기까지 며칠이 걸렸다. 편지를 읽은 그 순간 그는 배낭을 꾸리고 함께 걷던 도보 여행 동료들에게 작별을 고한 뒤, 약속을 지키러 길을 나섰다. 기쁨과 슬픔, 희망과 초조함으로 가슴이 가득한 채로.\n\n그는 브베를 잘 알았다. 배가 작은 부두에 닿자마자 호숫가를 따라 라투르로 서둘러 갔다. 캐럴 일가가 하숙하고 있는 곳이었다. 종업원은 절망스럽다는 듯, 온 식구가 호수로 산책을 나갔다고 했다. 하지만, 아니, 금발의 아가씨라면 성 정원에 계실지도 모른다고. 무슈가 잠시 앉아 수고로움을 감내해 주신다면 눈 깜짝할 사이에 모셔 오겠노라고. 그러나 무슈는 \"눈 깜짝할 사이\"조차 기다릴 수가 없어, 그 말이 채 끝나기도 전에 직접 아가씨를 찾으러 자리를 떴다.\n\n아름다운 호숫가에 자리한 정겹고 오래된 정원이었다. 머리 위로는 밤나무가 바스락거리고, 사방에는 담쟁이가 기어오르고, 탑의 검은 그림자가 햇살 부서지는 물 위로 길게 드리워져 있었다. 나지막하고 너른 담장 한 모서리에 의자가 하나 놓여 있었는데, 에이미는 곧잘 이곳에 와서 책을 읽거나 바느질을 하거나, 주위의 아름다움에 마음을 달래곤 했다. 그날도 그녀는 거기 앉아 한 손으로 머리를 괴고, 향수에 젖은 가슴과 무거운 눈으로 베스를 생각하며 로리가 왜 오지 않는지 궁금해하고 있었다. 그녀는 그가 저 너머 안뜰을 가로질러 오는 소리도 듣지 못했고, 지하 통로에서 정원으로 이어지는 아치문 앞에 그가 멈춰 서는 것도 보지 못했다. 그는 잠시 그 자리에 서서, 새로운 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다. 일찍이 아무도 본 적 없는 것—에이미라는 사람의 부드러운 면을 보았다. 그녀를 둘러싼 모든 것이 말없이 사랑과 슬픔을 일깨웠다. 무릎 위의 눈물 자국 번진 편지, 머리를 묶은 검은 리본, 얼굴에 어린 여인다운 아픔과 인내. 목에 걸린 작은 흑단 십자가조차 로리에게는 애처롭게 보였다. 그가 준 것이었고, 그녀가 유일한 장신구로 걸고 다니는 것이었기 때문이다. 그녀가 자기를 어떻게 맞아 줄지 일말의 의심이라도 있었다면, 그녀가 고개를 들어 그를 보는 순간 그 의심은 말끔히 사라졌으리라. 그녀는 모든 것을 내던지고 그에게로 달려오며, 더없이 분명한 사랑과 그리움이 담긴 목소리로 외쳤으니까.\n\n\"오, 로리, 로리, 와 줄 줄 알았어요!\"\n\n그때 모든 말이 오갔고, 모든 것이 정해졌다고 나는 생각한다. 두 사람이 잠시 말없이 마주 선 채, 검은 머리가 환한 머리 위로 감싸듯 숙여졌을 때, 에이미는 로리만큼 자기를 잘 위로하고 지탱해 줄 사람은 없다는 것을 느꼈고, 로리는 조의 자리를 채워 자기를 행복하게 해 줄 여자는 이 세상에 에이미뿐이라고 마음을 정했다. 그는 그렇게 말로 옮기지는 않았다. 그래도 에이미는 실망하지 않았다. 두 사람 모두 그 진실을 느꼈고, 만족했으며, 나머지는 기꺼이 침묵에 맡겼으니까.\n\n이윽고 에이미는 제자리로 돌아갔다. 그녀가 눈물을 닦는 동안 로리는 흩어진 종이들을 그러모았다. 닳고 닳은 편지 몇 장과 의미심장한 스케치들을 보면서 앞날에 대한 좋은 징조를 읽어 냈다. 그가 곁에 앉자, 에이미는 다시 수줍어져서, 조금 전 충동적으로 인사하던 일을 떠올리며 발그레해졌다.\n\n\"어쩔 수가 없었어요. 너무 외롭고 슬펐는데, 당신을 보니 너무 반가워서. 이제 안 오시려나 보다 막 걱정하기 시작하던 참에 고개를 드니 당신이 있어서, 어찌나 놀랐는지.\" 그녀는 애써 아무렇지 않게 말하려 했지만 잘되지 않았다.\n\n\"소식을 듣자마자 왔어요. 사랑스러운 베스를 잃은 슬픔을 위로할 무슨 말이라도 할 수 있으면 좋으련만. 그저 마음으로 느낄 수밖에 없네요, 그리고—\" 그도 더는 말을 잇지 못했다. 그 역시 별안간 부끄러워져서, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었던 것이다. 그는 에이미의 머리를 자기 어깨에 기대게 하고 실컷 울라고 말해 주고 싶었지만, 차마 그럴 용기가 나지 않았다. 그래서 그 대신 그녀의 손을 잡고, 말보다 나은 위로의 손길로 꼭 쥐어 주었다.\n\n\"아무 말 안 해도 돼요. 이것만으로도 위로가 되니까요.\" 그녀가 나직이 말했다. \"베스는 편안하고 행복할 테니, 돌아오라고 빌어선 안 되겠죠. 그래도 집에 가는 게 두려워요. 다들 보고 싶어 미칠 것 같은데도요. 지금은 그 얘기 그만해요. 자꾸 눈물이 나니까. 당신이 머무는 동안만큼은 함께 있는 걸 누리고 싶어요. 바로 돌아가실 건 아니죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"네가 원한다면 안 가지.\"\n\n\"원해요, 무척이나. 이모도 플로도 정말 다정하지만, 당신은 꼭 우리 식구 같으니까요. 잠시라도 곁에 있어 주면 얼마나 든든할까요.\"\n\n에이미의 말과 표정이 마음 가득 향수에 젖은 어린아이 같아서, 로리는 단번에 수줍음을 잊고 그녀가 바라는 것을 그대로 주었다. 익숙한 다정함과, 그녀에게 필요한 명랑한 대화를.\n\n\"가엾은 것, 슬픔에 잠겨 반쯤 병이라도 든 사람 같구나! 이제 내가 돌볼 테니 그만 울고, 같이 좀 걷자. 바람이 차서 가만히 앉아 있으면 안 되겠어.\" 그가 에이미가 좋아하는, 반쯤은 어르고 반쯤은 명령하는 투로 말하며 그녀의 모자를 매 주고, 자기 팔에 그녀의 팔을 끼우더니, 새잎 돋은 밤나무 아래 햇살 비치는 산책로를 천천히 거닐기 시작했다. 그는 두 발로 걸으니 한결 마음이 놓였다. 그리고 에이미는 기댈 든든한 팔과 마주 보고 웃어 줄 익숙한 얼굴, 오직 자기만을 위해 즐겁게 이야기해 주는 다정한 목소리가 있다는 것이 무척이나 좋았다.\n\n이 예스럽고 정겨운 정원은 수많은 연인을 품어 왔으며, 그들을 위해 일부러 만들어진 곳 같았다. 햇볕이 따스하고 호젓한 데다, 그들을 내려다보는 것이라곤 탑뿐이었고, 발치에서 잔물결을 일으키며 흘러가는 너른 호수가 그들의 말의 메아리를 멀리 실어 갔으니까. 한 시간 동안 이 새로운 한 쌍은 걷고 이야기를 나누거나, 담장에 기대어 쉬면서, 그 시간과 장소에 그토록 매혹을 더해 주는 달콤한 기운을 만끽했다. 그리고 낭만이라곤 없는 저녁 식사 종이 그들을 불러들였을 때, 에이미는 외로움과 슬픔의 짐을 성 정원에 두고 오는 듯한 기분이었다.\n\n캐럴 부인은 달라진 에이미의 얼굴을 본 순간, 새로운 생각이 번뜩 떠올라 속으로 외쳤다. \"이제 다 알겠구나. 이 아이가 그동안 젊은 로런스를 그리워하며 애를 태운 거야. 세상에, 내가 그 생각을 못 했다니!\"\n\n이 마음씨 좋은 부인은 칭찬받을 만큼 신중하게 아무 말도 하지 않고 눈치챈 기색조차 내비치지 않았다. 다만 로리에게 더 머물라고 진심으로 권하고, 에이미에게는 그와 함께 시간을 즐기라고 했다. 혼자 외롭게 지내는 것보다 그편이 훨씬 이로울 테니까. 에이미는 더없이 고분고분했다. 이모가 플로를 챙기느라 정신이 없어, 그녀는 친구를 상대하는 몫을 맡게 되었고, 그 일을 여느 때보다 훨씬 훌륭하게 해냈다.\n\n니스에서는 로리가 빈둥거렸고 에이미가 잔소리를 했지만, 브베에서는 로리가 한시도 가만있지 않았다. 늘 걷고, 말을 타고, 배를 젓고, 공부를 했는데, 그것도 더없이 정력적으로였다. 그러는 동안 에이미는 그가 하는 모든 일에 감탄하며, 할 수 있는 한 멀리, 빠르게 그를 따라 했다. 그는 그 변화가 기후 덕분이라고 했고, 그녀는 굳이 반박하지 않았다. 되찾은 건강과 활기에 자기도 같은 핑계를 댈 수 있어 다행이었던 것이다.\n\n기운을 북돋워 주는 공기는 두 사람 모두에게 이로웠고, 많은 운동은 몸뿐 아니라 마음에도 건강한 변화를 가져왔다. 영원히 변치 않는 산봉우리들 가운데서 두 사람은 삶과 의무를 한결 또렷하게 바라보게 된 듯했다. 신선한 바람은 낙심하게 하는 의심과 그릇된 환상, 우울한 안개를 날려 버렸다. 따스한 봄 햇살은 온갖 높은 뜻과 부드러운 희망, 행복한 생각을 끌어냈다. 호수는 지난날의 시름을 씻어 내는 듯했고, 웅장하고 오래된 산들은 자애로이 그들을 굽어보며 이렇게 말하는 것 같았다. \"어린아이들아, 서로 사랑하여라.\"\n\n새로운 슬픔에도 불구하고, 그것은 무척 행복한 시간이었다. 로리가 한마디 말로도 그 분위기를 깨뜨릴 수 없을 만큼 행복했다. 그가 자신의 첫사랑이자, 굳게 믿어 온 마지막이자 유일한 사랑이 그토록 빠르게 치유된 데 대한 놀라움에서 벗어나기까지는 얼마간 시간이 걸렸다. 그는 조의 동생은 조 자신과 거의 다름없다는 생각과, 에이미가 아니고서는 어떤 여자도 이토록 빨리, 이토록 깊이 사랑하기란 불가능했으리라는 확신으로, 겉보기에 변심처럼 보이는 그 일에 스스로 위안을 얻었다. 그의 첫사랑은 폭풍 같은 종류였고, 이제 그는 그것을 아득한 세월 너머의 일처럼 돌아보며 후회와 뒤섞인 연민을 느꼈다. 그는 그 사랑을 부끄러워하지 않았다. 다만 아픔이 지나가고 나면 고마워할 수 있는, 삶의 쓰면서도 단 경험 가운데 하나로 마음 한구석에 갈무리해 두었다. 두 번째 구애만큼은 되도록 차분하고 단순하게 하리라 마음먹었다. 장면을 만들 필요도 없고, 에이미에게 사랑한다고 굳이 말할 필요조차 거의 없었다. 그녀는 말 없이도 그것을 알았고, 이미 오래전에 그에게 답을 주었으니까. 모든 것이 너무도 자연스럽게 이루어져 누구도 탓할 수 없었고, 그는 조차 포함해 모두가 기뻐하리라는 것을 알았다. 그러나 첫 풋사랑이 한번 짓밟히고 나면 두 번째 시도에는 조심스럽고 더디어지는 법이라, 로리는 매 순간을 누리며 날들을 흘려보냈고, 새로운 사랑 이야기의 가장 처음이자 가장 달콤한 부분에 종지부를 찍을 그 한마디를 언제 입에 올릴지는 우연에 맡겨 두었다.\n\n그는 결말이 달빛 비치는 성 정원에서, 더없이 우아하고 점잖은 방식으로 이루어지리라 막연히 상상했었다. 그러나 정작은 정반대로 흘러갔다. 그 일은 한낮의 호수 위에서, 무뚝뚝한 몇 마디로 정해졌으니까. 그들은 오전 내내 배를 타고 떠다녔다. 음울한 생젱골프에서 햇살 가득한 몽트뢰까지, 한쪽으로는 사부아 알프스, 다른 쪽으로는 생베르나르 산과 당뒤미디를 두고서. 골짜기에는 어여쁜 브베가, 그 너머 언덕에는 로잔이 있었고, 머리 위에는 구름 한 점 없는 푸른 하늘이, 발아래에는 그보다 더 푸른 호수가 펼쳐졌다. 호수 위에는 흰 날개 갈매기 같은 그림 같은 배들이 점점이 떠 있었다.\n\n그들은 시용을 미끄러지듯 지나며 보니바르 이야기를 했고, 루소가 「엘로이즈」를 쓴 클라랑을 올려다보며 루소 이야기를 했다. 둘 다 그 작품을 읽어 본 적은 없었지만 사랑 이야기라는 것은 알았고, 저마다 속으로 그것이 자기들 이야기의 절반만큼이라도 흥미로울까 궁금해했다. 두 사람 사이에 잠시 침묵이 내려앉은 동안 에이미는 손을 물에 담그고 찰랑이고 있었는데, 고개를 들어 보니 로리가 노에 기댄 채 어떤 눈빛으로 그녀를 바라보고 있었다. 그 눈빛에 그녀는 그저 무슨 말이라도 해야겠다는 생각에 황급히 입을 열었다.\n\n\"피곤하겠어요. 좀 쉬어요, 제가 저을게요. 저한테도 좋을 거예요. 당신이 온 뒤로 저는 줄곧 게으름만 피우며 호사를 누렸으니까요.\"\n\n\"피곤하지 않아. 그래도 원하면 노 하나 잡아도 돼. 자리는 넉넉하니까. 다만 균형이 안 맞아서 내가 거의 한가운데 앉아야겠지만.\" 로리는 그 자리 배치가 꽤 마음에 든다는 투로 대답했다.\n\n별로 사정을 나아지게 하지 못했다는 것을 느끼며, 에이미는 권하는 대로 자리 삼분의 일에 걸터앉아 머리를 얼굴 위로 늘어뜨리고 노를 받아 들었다. 그녀는 다른 여러 일을 잘하듯 노 젓는 것도 잘했다. 그녀는 두 손을 쓰고 로리는 한 손만 썼는데도 노는 박자를 맞추었고, 배는 물 위를 매끄럽게 나아갔다.\n\n\"우리 정말 손발이 잘 맞죠?\" 그 순간만큼은 침묵이 싫었던 에이미가 말했다.\n\n\"너무 잘 맞아서, 언제까지나 같은 배를 함께 저으면 좋겠어. 그래 줄래, 에이미?\" 무척 다정하게.\n\n\"네, 로리.\" 아주 나직하게.\n\n그러고는 두 사람 다 노 젓기를 멈추었고, 자기들도 모르는 사이에 호수에 비쳐 어른거리는 풍경 위로, 인간의 사랑과 행복이 빚어내는 어여쁜 한 폭의 그림을 보태고 있었다.\n\n# ALL ALONE.\n\n자기 자신이 다른 사람에게 온통 쏠려 있고, 마음과 영혼이 어떤 다정한 본보기로 정화되어 있을 때는 자기를 버리겠노라 약속하기가 쉬웠다. 그러나 그 힘이 되어 주던 목소리가 잠잠해지고, 날마다의 가르침이 끝나고, 사랑하던 이의 존재가 사라지고, 남은 것이라곤 외로움과 슬픔뿐일 때, 조는 그 약속을 지키기가 무척이나 어렵다는 것을 깨달았다. 동생에 대한 끝없는 그리움으로 제 가슴이 미어지는데 어떻게 \"아버지와 어머니를 위로\"할 수 있단 말인가. 베스가 옛집을 떠나 새집으로 가 버리면서 집 안의 빛과 온기와 아름다움이 죄다 떠나 버린 것만 같은데 어떻게 \"집을 명랑하게\" 만들 수 있단 말인가. 그리고 이 너른 세상 어디에서, 그 자체로 보답이던 사랑의 봉사를 대신할 \"쓸모 있고 행복한 일거리\"를 찾을 수 있단 말인가? 그녀는 앞이 막막한 채로 절망스럽게 제 의무를 다하려 애썼다. 그러면서도 내내 속으로는 그 모든 것에 반항했다. 제 몇 안 되는 기쁨은 줄어들고 짐은 더 무거워지고, 발버둥 칠수록 삶이 점점 더 고되어지는 것이 부당하게만 느껴졌으니까. 어떤 사람들은 온통 햇살만 받고 어떤 사람들은 온통 그늘만 받는 듯했다. 그것은 공평하지 않았다. 자기는 에이미보다 더 착하게 살려고 애썼는데도 보답은커녕 실망과 시름, 고된 일밖에 돌아오는 게 없었으니까.\n\n가엾은 조, 그녀에게는 어두운 나날이었다. 이 조용한 집에서 평생토록 따분한 살림살이와 몇 가지 자잘한 즐거움, 그리고 도무지 쉬워질 줄 모르는 의무에 매여 살 생각을 하면 절망 비슷한 것이 밀려왔던 것이다. \"난 못 해. 난 이런 삶에 맞는 사람이 아니야. 누군가 와서 날 도와주지 않으면 분명 뛰쳐나가 무슨 무모한 짓이라도 저지르고 말 거야.\" 처음의 노력이 실패로 돌아가고, 강한 의지가 피할 수 없는 운명에 굴복해야 할 때 흔히 찾아오는 음울하고 비참한 심정에 빠져들었을 때, 그녀는 그렇게 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n그러나 누군가 와서 그녀를 도와주었다. 조는 자신의 수호천사들을 단번에 알아보지 못했다. 그들이 낯익은 모습을 하고서, 가엾은 인간에게 가장 알맞은 소박한 주문을 썼기 때문이다. 그녀는 밤마다 베스가 부르는 것만 같아 화들짝 깨곤 했다. 작고 텅 빈 침대를 보고 순순히 받아들이지 못한 슬픔의 쓰라린 울음을 토할 때면—\"오, 베스, 돌아와! 돌아오라고!\"—그리워 뻗은 두 팔이 헛되지 않았다. 동생의 가장 희미한 속삭임에도 귀를 곤두세웠던 그 어머니가 이제는 조의 흐느낌을 누구보다 빨리 듣고 위로하러 왔으니까. 말로써만이 아니라, 손길 하나로 달래 주는 끈기 있는 다정함과, 조의 것보다 더 큰 슬픔을 말없이 일깨우는 눈물과, 기도보다 더 절절한 끊긴 속삭임으로. 희망에 찬 체념이 자연스러운 슬픔과 손을 맞잡고 있었기에 그러했다. 밤의 정적 속에서 마음과 마음이 이야기를 나누며 고통을 축복으로 바꾸어, 슬픔을 다듬고 사랑을 굳건히 해 주던 거룩한 순간들. 이것을 느끼자 조의 짐은 한결 가벼워진 듯했고, 의무는 더 달콤해졌으며, 어머니의 품이라는 안전한 안식처에서 바라본 삶은 더 견딜 만해 보였다.\n\n쓰라리던 마음이 조금 위로받자 어지럽던 머릿속에도 도움이 찾아왔다. 어느 날 그녀는 서재로 가서, 차분한 미소로 자기를 맞으려 들린 그 고운 잿빛 머리 위로 몸을 숙이며 무척 겸손하게 말했다.\n\n\"아버지, 베스한테 하시던 것처럼 저한테도 말씀해 주세요. 저한테는 그게 그 애보다 더 필요해요. 전 다 엉망진창이거든요.\"\n\n\"얘야, 이보다 더 큰 위로는 없단다.\" 그가 떨리는 목소리로 대답하며 두 팔로 그녀를 감쌌다. 마치 그 역시 도움이 필요했고, 그것을 청하기를 두려워하지 않는다는 듯이.\n\n그리하여 베스가 쓰던 작은 의자에 아버지 곁에 바싹 붙어 앉아, 조는 제 시름을 털어놓았다. 동생을 잃은 원망스러운 슬픔, 자기를 낙담시키던 헛된 노력들, 삶을 그토록 어둡게만 보이게 한 믿음의 결핍, 그리고 우리가 절망이라 부르는 그 모든 서글픈 막막함을. 그녀가 온 마음을 다 털어놓자 아버지는 그녀에게 필요한 도움을 주었고, 그 일로 두 사람 다 위안을 얻었다. 이제 부녀로서만이 아니라 한 사람의 남자와 여자로서, 사랑뿐 아니라 서로에 대한 공감으로 기꺼이 서로를 도울 수 있는 때가 온 것이다. 조가 \"신도가 한 사람뿐인 교회\"라 부르던 그 오래된 서재에서 보낸 행복하고 사색에 잠긴 시간들. 그녀는 그곳에서 새로운 용기와 되찾은 명랑함, 그리고 한결 순순해진 마음을 안고 나왔다. 한 아이에게 두려움 없이 죽음을 맞도록 가르쳤던 부모가, 이제 또 다른 아이에게 낙심이나 불신 없이 삶을 받아들이고, 삶이 주는 아름다운 기회들을 감사함과 힘으로 쓰도록 가르치려 애쓰고 있었으니까.\n\n조에게는 또 다른 도움들도 있었다. 그녀를 섬기는 제 몫을 외면당하지 않으려는, 소박하고 건강한 의무와 즐거움들. 조는 그것들을 보고 소중히 여기는 법을 차츰 배워 갔다. 빗자루와 행주는 더 이상 예전처럼 진저리 나는 물건일 수 없었다. 베스가 둘 다 맡아 쓰던 것이었으니까. 결코 버리지 않은 그 작은 걸레와 낡은 솔 언저리에는 동생의 살림꾼다운 정신이 어려 있는 듯했다. 그것들을 쓰면서 조는 어느새 베스가 흥얼거리던 노래를 흥얼거리고, 베스의 단정한 살림 솜씨를 흉내 내고, 여기저기 작은 손길을 더해 모든 것을 산뜻하고 아늑하게 가꾸고 있는 자신을 발견했다. 그것이 집을 행복하게 만드는 첫걸음이었지만, 그녀는 미처 알지 못했다. 해나가 손을 흐뭇하게 꼭 쥐며 이렇게 말하기 전까지는.\n\n\"참 속 깊은 아가씨야. 할 수만 있으면 그 사랑스러운 양을 우리가 그리워하지 않게 하려고 단단히 마음먹었구먼. 우리야 말은 많이 안 해도 다 보고 있다우. 하느님께서 그 일로 아가씨한테 복을 내리실 거예요, 안 그러시나 두고 보라고요.\"\n\n함께 앉아 바느질을 하다가, 조는 언니 메그가 얼마나 좋게 변했는지 알게 되었다. 얼마나 말을 조리 있게 하는지, 착하고 여자다운 마음과 생각과 감정에 대해 얼마나 많은 것을 아는지, 남편과 아이들 곁에서 얼마나 행복한지, 그리고 그들이 서로를 위해 얼마나 많은 것을 하고 있는지를.\n\n\"결혼이란 게 결국 참 좋은 거구나. 나도 한번 해 보면 언니만큼이나 활짝 피어날 수 있을까?\" 어수선한 아이 방에서 데미에게 줄 연을 만들며 조가 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그게 네 안에 있는 부드럽고 여자다운 반쪽을 끌어내 줄 거야, 조. 너는 밤송이 같은 사람이야. 겉은 까칠해도 속은 비단처럼 부드럽고, 일단 닿기만 하면 알맹이가 달콤하지. 언젠가 사랑이 네 마음을 열어 보이게 할 거고, 그럼 그 거친 껍질도 떨어져 나갈 거야.\"\n\n\"밤송이는 서리가 내려야 벌어지는 법이고, 떨어뜨리려면 한참을 흔들어 대야 하잖아. 사내애들이 밤 주우러 다니지만, 난 그 애들 자루에 담기긴 싫은걸.\" 조는 그렇게 받아치며 연에 풀칠을 했다. 데이지가 꼬리 추 노릇을 하겠다며 제 몸을 매달아 놓은 통에, 어떤 바람이 불어도 결코 하늘로 떠오를 리 없는 연이었다.\n\n메그는 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 조의 옛 기개가 살짝 비치는 것이 반가웠던 것이다. 그러면서도 자기가 할 수 있는 온갖 논리를 동원해 제 생각을 밀어붙이는 것이 도리라고 여겼다. 그리고 그 자매간의 수다는 헛되지 않았다. 특히 메그의 가장 효과적인 두 가지 논거가 바로 아기들이었으니, 조가 그 아이들을 끔찍이 사랑했기 때문이다. 어떤 마음에는 슬픔이 가장 좋은 여는 도구가 된다. 조의 마음도 거의 자루에 담길 채비가 되어 있었다. 밤알을 여물게 할 햇살이 조금만 더 있으면, 그다음엔 사내애의 조급한 흔듦이 아니라 어른의 손이 뻗어 와 부드럽게 껍질에서 그것을 따내어, 알맹이가 온전하고 달콤하다는 것을 알아보면 될 일이었다. 만약 조가 이를 눈치챘다면 단단히 입을 다물고 어느 때보다 까칠하게 굴었으리라. 다행히도 그녀는 자기 생각에 골몰해 있지 않았기에, 때가 오자 톡 떨어졌다.\n\n자, 만약 조가 교훈적인 이야기책의 여주인공이었다면, 인생의 이 무렵에 이르러 마땅히 무척 성스러운 사람이 되어 세상을 등지고, 수수한 보닛을 쓴 채 주머니에 전도지를 넣고 다니며 선행을 베풀었어야 했으리라. 하지만 보시다시피 조는 여주인공이 아니었다. 그녀는 그저 수백 명의 다른 소녀들과 마찬가지로 발버둥 치는 한 인간 소녀였을 뿐이고, 그저 제 본성대로 행동했다. 기분에 따라 슬퍼하기도, 짜증을 내기도, 무기력해지기도, 활기에 차기도 하면서. 착하게 살겠다고 말하기야 더없이 갸륵하지만, 그것을 단번에 해낼 수는 없는 법이다. 우리 중 누군가가 두 발을 제대로 된 길에 디디기까지에도 오래 끌어당기고, 힘껏 끌어당기고, 다 함께 끌어당겨야 한다. 조는 거기까지는 와 있었다. 제 의무를 다하는 법을 배워 가고 있었고, 그러지 못하면 마음이 편치 않았다. 그러나 그것을 기꺼이 즐겁게 해내는 일—아, 그건 또 다른 문제였다! 그녀는 곧잘 무언가 멋진 일을, 아무리 힘들어도 좋으니 해 보고 싶다고 말하곤 했다. 그리고 이제 그 소원이 이루어진 셈이었다. 아버지와 어머니를 위해 제 삶을 바쳐, 그분들이 자기에게 그래 주었듯 그분들에게 집을 행복하게 만들어 드리는 것보다 더 아름다운 일이 어디 있겠는가? 그리고 그 노력의 빛이 더 환해지려면 어려움이 필요하다면, 들떠 있고 야심 찬 소녀에게 자기 자신의 희망과 계획과 욕망을 단념하고 기꺼이 남을 위해 사는 것보다 더 힘든 일이 어디 있겠는가?\n\n섭리는 그녀의 말 그대로를 받아들였다. 여기 그 과업이 있었다. 그녀가 기대했던 것은 아니었지만, 거기엔 자기 자신이 끼어들 자리가 없었으므로 오히려 더 나은 것이었다. 자, 그녀가 그것을 해낼 수 있을까? 그녀는 한번 해 보기로 마음먹었고, 그 첫 시도에서 앞서 이야기한 도움들을 만났다. 또 하나의 도움이 그녀에게 주어졌고, 그녀는 그것을 보상이 아니라 위안으로 받아들였다. \"곤경\"이라 불리는 언덕을 오르던 크리스천이 작은 정자에서 누렸던 쉼을 받아들이듯이.\n\n\"넌 왜 글을 안 쓰니? 그게 늘 너를 행복하게 해 주었잖아.\" 한번은 낙심한 기운이 조를 짓누를 때, 어머니가 그렇게 말했다.\n\n\"글 쓸 마음이 안 나요. 설령 쓴다 해도 제 글에 신경 쓰는 사람은 아무도 없어요.\"\n\n\"우리가 신경 쓰잖니. 우리를 위해 뭐든 써 보렴, 세상 사람들이야 어떻든 신경 쓰지 말고. 한번 해 봐, 얘야. 분명 너한테도 좋을 거고, 우리도 무척 기쁠 거야.\"\n\n\"할 수 있을 것 같지 않아요.\" 그래도 조는 책상을 꺼내, 쓰다 만 원고들을 뒤적이기 시작했다.\n\n한 시간쯤 뒤 어머니가 살짝 들여다보니, 조는 검은 앞치마를 두른 채 무언가에 온통 빠져든 표정으로 사각사각 글을 써 내려가고 있었다. 그 모습에 마치 부인은 빙긋 웃으며 슬며시 물러났다. 자신의 제안이 성공한 것에 흐뭇해하면서. 어찌 된 일인지 조 자신도 알 수 없었지만, 그 이야기에는 읽는 이의 마음을 곧장 파고드는 무언가가 깃들어 있었다. 식구들이 그 글을 읽고 웃다가 울고 나자, 아버지는 그녀가 한사코 마다하는데도 그것을 어느 인기 잡지사로 보냈는데, 놀랍게도 원고료를 받았을 뿐 아니라 다른 글도 청탁받았던 것이다. 그 짧은 이야기가 실린 뒤로, 그 칭찬이 곧 영예인 여러 사람의 편지가 잇따랐고, 신문들이 그것을 옮겨 실었으며, 친구뿐 아니라 낯선 이들까지 그 글에 감탄했다. 작은 것치고는 큰 성공이었다. 조는 자기 소설이 칭찬과 비난을 한꺼번에 받았을 때보다 더 어리둥절했다.\n\n\"이해가 안 돼요. 그렇게 단순하고 자그마한 이야기에 도대체 무엇이 들었기에 사람들이 그토록 칭찬하는 걸까요?\" 그녀가 어리둥절해하며 말했다.\n\n\"거기엔 진실이 담겨 있단다, 조. 그게 비결이야. 해학과 애잔함이 그것을 살아 있게 하지. 너는 마침내 네 문체를 찾은 거란다. 너는 명성이나 돈은 생각지도 않고, 거기에 네 마음을 쏟아부었으니까, 얘야. 쓴 것을 맛보았으니, 이제 단 것이 올 차례구나. 최선을 다하렴. 그리고 네 성공을 보며 우리만큼 행복해지렴.\"\n\n\"제 글에 무언가 좋거나 참된 것이 있다면, 그건 제 것이 아니에요. 다 아버지와 어머니, 그리고 베스 덕분이지요.\" 조는 세상의 그 어떤 칭찬보다 아버지의 말에 더 깊이 감동하며 말했다.\n\n그리하여 사랑과 슬픔으로 단련된 조는 자그마한 이야기들을 써서, 그 글들이 스스로와 그녀를 위해 친구를 사귀도록 세상으로 내보냈다. 그녀는 세상이 이런 보잘것없는 떠돌이들에게 무척 너그러운 곳임을 알게 되었다. 글들은 따뜻하게 환영받았고, 행운을 만난 효성스러운 자식들처럼 어머니에게 흐뭇한 선물을 보내왔으니까.\n\n에이미와 로리가 약혼 소식을 전해 왔을 때, 마치 부인은 조가 그 일을 기뻐하기 어려워하지 않을까 걱정했다. 그러나 그 걱정은 곧 사라졌다. 조는 처음엔 표정이 굳었지만 무척 차분하게 받아들였고, 편지를 두 번 채 읽기도 전에 \"그 아이들\"에 대한 희망과 계획으로 가득해졌으니까. 그것은 일종의 글로 쓴 이중창 같았다. 연인답게 서로를 한껏 추켜세우고 있어서, 읽기에도 즐겁고 떠올리기에도 흐뭇했다. 누구도 토를 달 데가 없었다.\n\n\"마음에 드세요, 어머니?\" 빽빽이 쓰인 편지지를 내려놓고 서로 바라보며 조가 말했다.\n\n\"그래. 에이미가 프레드를 거절했다고 써 보낸 뒤로 줄곧 이렇게 되기를 바랐단다. 그때 난 그 애한테 네가 말하던 그 '돈만 밝히는 마음'보다 더 나은 무언가가 찾아왔다고 확신했지. 그리고 편지 여기저기에 비치는 기색을 보고, 사랑과 로리가 이기리라는 걸 짐작했어.\"\n\n\"마미도 참 눈치가 빠르셔라, 그러고도 어쩜 그리 입이 무거우셨어요! 저한테는 한마디도 안 하셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"딸들을 건사하려면 어미는 눈이 밝고 입은 신중해야 하는 법이란다. 그 생각을 네 머릿속에 넣어 줄까 봐 적이 두려웠어. 일이 정해지기도 전에 네가 편지를 써서 그 애들을 축하해 버릴까 봐.\"\n\n\"전 이제 예전처럼 덜렁이가 아니에요. 절 믿으셔도 돼요. 이젠 누구의 속내 친구가 되어도 좋을 만큼 진중하고 분별 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고, 얘야. 진작 너를 내 속내 친구로 삼았어야 했는데, 다만 너의 테디가 다른 사람을 사랑하게 됐다는 걸 알면 네가 아파할까 봐 그랬단다.\"\n\n\"어머니, 정말로 제가 그렇게 어리석고 이기적일 거라고 생각하셨어요? 그 사랑이 가장 좋을 때는 아니었어도 가장 싱싱할 때 제가 거절했는데도요?\"\n\n\"그땐 네가 진심이라는 걸 알았다, 조. 다만 요즘 들어 이런 생각이 들더구나. 만약 그 애가 돌아와 다시 청한다면, 어쩌면 너는 이번엔 다른 대답을 하고 싶어질지도 모르겠다고. 용서하렴, 얘야. 네가 무척 외로워하는 걸 어미가 모를 수 없잖니. 가끔 네 눈에 어린 굶주린 듯한 빛이 내 가슴을 찌른단다. 그래서 만약 너의 그 애가 이제라도 다시 부딪쳐 본다면, 그 빈자리를 채워 줄 수 있지 않을까 싶었어.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 어머니. 지금 이대로가 나아요. 그리고 에이미가 그를 사랑하게 돼서 기뻐요. 하지만 한 가지는 어머니 말씀이 맞아요. 전 외로워요. 그러니 만약 테디가 다시 부딪쳐 왔다면, 어쩌면 '네'라고 했을지도 몰라요. 그를 더 사랑해서가 아니라, 그가 떠났을 때보다 지금이 사랑받는 일에 더 마음이 쓰이니까요.\"\n\n\"그 말을 들으니 기쁘구나, 조. 네가 나아지고 있다는 뜻이니까. 너를 사랑할 사람은 많으니, 가장 좋은 연인이 와서 네게 보답을 줄 때까지 아버지와 어머니, 언니와 동생들, 친구들과 아기들로 만족하려무나.\"\n\n\"세상에서 가장 좋은 연인은 어머니들인걸요. 그래도 마미한테는 살짝 귀띔할게요. 전 온갖 종류의 사랑을 다 해 보고 싶어요. 참 이상해요. 온갖 자연스러운 애정으로 제 자신을 채워 보려 할수록, 더 많은 걸 바라게 되는 것 같아요. 마음이 그렇게 많은 걸 담을 수 있을 줄은 몰랐어요. 제 마음은 어찌나 늘어나는지 이젠 도무지 가득 차는 법이 없어요. 예전엔 식구들만으로도 충분히 만족했는데. 이해가 안 가요.\"\n\n\"난 이해한단다.\" 마치 부인은 지혜로운 미소를 지었고, 조는 에이미가 로리에 대해 쓴 대목을 다시 읽으려 편지를 뒤로 넘겼다.\n\n\"로리가 날 사랑해 주는 만큼 사랑받는다는 건 너무도 아름다운 일이에요. 그이는 감상적이지 않고, 그 일에 대해 말을 많이 하지도 않아요. 하지만 그이가 하는 모든 말과 행동에서 저는 그것을 보고 느껴요. 그래서 너무나 행복하고 또 너무나 겸허해져서, 제가 예전의 그 아이가 아닌 것만 같아요. 저는 그이가 얼마나 착하고 너그럽고 다정한지 이제껏 몰랐어요. 그이가 제게 마음을 활짝 열어 보여 주는데, 그 안이 고귀한 충동과 희망과 다짐으로 가득해서, 그게 제 것이라는 게 너무 자랑스러워요. 그이는 '이제 너를 항해사로 태우고, 사랑을 잔뜩 바닥짐으로 실으면 순조로운 항해를 할 수 있을 것 같다'고 말해요. 부디 그렇게 되기를 빌고, 그이가 믿어 주는 그런 사람이 되려고 애쓰고 있어요. 저는 제 늠름한 선장을 온 마음과 영혼과 힘을 다해 사랑하고, 하느님이 우리를 함께 있게 해 주시는 한 결코 그이를 떠나지 않을 테니까요. 오, 어머니, 두 사람이 서로를 사랑하고 서로를 위해 살아갈 때, 이 세상이 천국과 이토록 비슷해질 수 있는지 전 미처 몰랐어요!\"\n\n\"이게 우리의 그 차분하고 새침하고 속물적이던 에이미라니! 사랑이란 정말 기적을 일으키는구나. 그 애들이 얼마나, 얼마나 행복할까!\" 그러고는 조는 사각거리는 편지지를 조심스러운 손길로 한데 모았다. 끝이 올 때까지 독자를 단단히 붙잡아 두다가, 마지막 장을 덮는 순간 다시 자기 혼자 분주한 일상의 세상에 남겨졌음을 깨닫게 하는, 어느 아름다운 사랑 이야기의 표지를 덮듯이.\n\n이윽고 조는 위층으로 어슬렁어슬렁 올라갔다. 비가 와서 산책을 할 수 없었던 것이다. 어딘가 들뜬 기운이 그녀를 사로잡았고, 옛 감정이 다시 찾아왔다. 한때처럼 쓰라리지는 않았지만, 어찌하여 한 자매는 바라는 것을 다 갖고 다른 자매는 아무것도 갖지 못하는가, 슬프지만 참을성 있게 묻는 그런 의문이었다. 그건 사실이 아니었다. 그녀도 알았기에 그 생각을 떨치려 애썼지만, 애정을 향한 자연스러운 갈망은 강했고, 에이미의 행복은 \"온 마음과 영혼으로 사랑하며, 하느님이 함께 있게 해 주시는 동안 매달릴\" 누군가를 향한 굶주린 그리움을 일깨웠다.\n\n조의 불안한 발걸음이 멎은 다락방에는 작은 나무 궤짝 네 개가 나란히 놓여 있었다. 저마다 주인의 이름이 적혀 있었고, 이제는 모두에게 끝나 버린 어린 시절과 소녀 시절의 유물들로 가득했다. 조는 궤짝들을 들여다보다가 자기 것에 이르자 가장자리에 턱을 괴고, 뒤죽박죽 모여 있는 물건들을 멍하니 바라보았다. 그러다 낡은 연습장 한 묶음에 눈길이 갔다. 그것을 꺼내 이리저리 뒤적이며, 그녀는 친절한 커크 부인 댁에서 보낸 그 즐거운 겨울을 다시 살았다. 처음엔 미소 짓다가, 이내 생각에 잠긴 표정이 되더니, 그다음엔 슬퍼졌다. 그리고 교수의 글씨로 적힌 짧은 글귀에 이르자, 입술이 떨리기 시작하고 책들이 무릎에서 미끄러져 내렸다. 그녀는 그 다정한 글귀를 가만히 바라보았다. 마치 그것이 새로운 의미를 띠며 가슴속 어떤 여린 자리를 건드리기라도 한 듯이.\n\n\"날 기다려 주오, 나의 벗이여. 조금 늦을지는 몰라도, 반드시 가리다.\"\n\n\"오, 정말 와 주신다면! 늘 그토록 다정하고, 그토록 착하고, 내게 그토록 참을성 있던 분. 사랑하는 나의 프리츠. 곁에 계실 땐 그분의 진가를 절반도 알아보지 못했는데, 이제는 어찌나 뵙고 싶은지. 다들 내게서 떠나가 버리고, 난 이렇게 혼자가 됐으니까.\"\n\n그러고는 마치 아직 이루어지지 않은 약속이라도 되는 양 그 작은 종이를 꼭 쥔 채, 조는 푹신한 헝겊 자루에 머리를 묻고 울었다. 지붕을 두드리는 빗소리에 맞서기라도 하듯이.\n\n그것은 모두 자기연민이었을까, 외로움이었을까, 아니면 가라앉은 기분이었을까? 그도 아니면, 그것을 일깨운 이가 그러했듯 끈기 있게 제때를 기다려 온 어떤 감정의 깨어남이었을까? 그 누가 말할 수 있으랴?\n\n조는 황혼 무렵 혼자 낡은 소파에 누워, 난롯불을 바라보며 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 땅거미 지는 시간을 보내는 그녀의 가장 좋아하는 방식이었다. 아무도 그녀를 방해하지 않았고, 그녀는 베스가 쓰던 작고 붉은 베개에 누워 이야기를 구상하고, 꿈을 꾸고, 결코 멀리 있는 것 같지 않은 동생을 향한 애틋한 생각에 잠기곤 했다. 그녀의 얼굴은 지치고, 무겁고, 다소 슬퍼 보였다. 내일이 그녀의 생일이었던 것이다. 세월이 얼마나 빠르게 흘러가는지, 자기가 얼마나 나이를 먹어 가는지, 그러고도 이뤄 놓은 것은 얼마나 보잘것없는지를 생각하고 있었다. 곧 스물다섯인데 내세울 것이 아무것도 없었다. 그 점에서 조는 잘못 생각하고 있었다. 내세울 것은 꽤 많았으니까. 머지않아 그녀도 그것을 알아보고 고마워하게 된다.\n\n\"노처녀, 내가 될 건 그거지. 펜을 신랑 삼고, 이야기들을 자식 삼은 문학하는 노처녀. 그러다 한 이십 년 뒤엔 어쩌면 한 줌의 명성이라도 얻겠지. 그땐 가엾은 존슨처럼 늙어서 그걸 누리지도 못하고, 외로워서 함께 나누지도 못하고, 자립해서 그게 필요하지도 않을 때겠지만. 뭐, 그래도 시큰둥한 성자도, 이기적인 죄인도 될 건 없잖아. 노처녀도 익숙해지고 나면 꽤 편안하다잖아. 하지만—\" 그러고는 조는 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그 앞날이 그리 달갑지만은 않다는 듯이.\n\n처음에는 좀처럼 달갑지 않은 법이고, 스물다섯에게 서른은 모든 것의 끝처럼 보인다. 그러나 보기만큼 나쁘지는 않아서, 스스로 기댈 무언가만 있다면 얼마든지 행복하게 살아갈 수 있다. 스물다섯에 소녀들은 노처녀가 된다는 이야기를 꺼내기 시작하지만, 속으로는 결코 그렇게 되지 않으리라 다짐한다. 서른이 되면 그 이야기는 입에 올리지 않은 채 조용히 그 사실을 받아들이고, 분별이 있다면 앞으로도 스무 해는 더 쓸모 있고 행복한 세월이 남아 그동안 우아하게 늙어 가는 법을 배울 수 있다고 떠올리며 스스로를 위로한다. 노처녀들을 비웃지 마라, 사랑스러운 소녀들이여. 점잖은 옷자락 아래 그토록 잔잔히 뛰는 가슴속에는 더없이 애틋하고 비극적인 사랑 이야기가 숨어 있는 일이 많고, 젊음과 건강과 야심, 그리고 사랑 자체를 말없이 바친 수많은 희생이 그 빛바랜 얼굴들을 하느님 보시기에 아름답게 만드니까. 슬프고 시큰둥한 자매들조차 따뜻하게 대해 주어야 한다. 다른 까닭이 없더라도, 그들이 인생에서 가장 달콤한 부분을 놓쳤다는 그 한 가지 이유만으로. 그들을 경멸이 아니라 연민으로 바라보면서, 한창 피어나는 소녀들은 자기들 역시 꽃 피는 시절을 놓칠 수 있다는 것을, 발그레한 뺨이 영원하지 않다는 것을, 고운 갈색 머리에도 은실이 섞일 것이며, 머지않아 친절과 존경이 지금의 사랑과 찬사만큼 달콤해지리라는 것을 기억해야 한다. 신사 여러분, 그러니까 소년들이여, 노처녀들에게 예의를 갖추어라. 아무리 가난하고 못나고 깐깐하더라도. 지닐 만한 가치가 있는 단 하나의 기사도란 신분이나 나이, 피부색을 가리지 않고 노인을 가장 기꺼이 받들고, 약한 이를 보호하며, 여인들을 섬기는 것이니까. 그저 떠올려 보라. 잔소리를 하고 부산을 떨 뿐 아니라 간호해 주고 다독여 주기도 한, 그러고도 너무 자주 고맙다는 말 한마디 듣지 못한 그 좋은 아주머니들을. 그분들이 너희를 끌어내 준 곤경들을, 그 빠듯한 살림에서 쥐여 준 \"용돈\"을, 참을성 있는 늙은 손가락이 너희를 위해 떠 준 바늘땀들을, 기꺼이 움직여 준 늙은 발걸음들을. 그러니 고마운 마음으로, 그 다정한 노부인들에게 여자들이 평생토록 받고 싶어 하는 그 작은 정성들을 갚아 드려라. 눈 맑은 소녀들은 그런 됨됨이를 금세 알아보고, 그 때문에 너희를 더욱 좋아할 것이다. 그리고 어머니와 아들을 갈라놓을 수 있는 거의 유일한 힘인 죽음이 너희에게서 어머니를 앗아 가더라도, 너희는 \"세상에서 둘도 없는 조카 녀석\"을 위해 외로운 늙은 가슴의 가장 따뜻한 한구석을 비워 둔 어느 프리실라 아주머니에게서 다정한 환대와 어머니 같은 보살핌을 분명 얻게 될 것이다.\n\n조는 (이 짤막한 설교를 듣는 동안 나의 독자가 그러했으리라 짐작되듯) 잠이 들었던 게 틀림없다. 별안간 로리의 유령이 그녀 앞에 서 있는 것만 같았으니까. 살아 있는 듯 생생한, 실체 있는 유령이었다. 그 유령은 그녀 위로 몸을 숙이고 있었는데, 무언가를 깊이 느끼면서도 내색하기 싫어할 때 짓던 바로 그 표정 그대로였다. 그러나 옛 노래 속의 제니처럼,\n\n\"그것이 그이라고는 차마 믿을 수 없어\"\n\n그녀는 깜짝 놀라 말없이 그를 올려다보기만 했다. 그가 몸을 숙여 입을 맞출 때까지. 그제야 그를 알아본 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 기쁨에 차 외쳤다.\n\n\"오, 내 테디! 오, 내 테디!\"\n\n\"조 누나, 그래도 날 보고 반가운 거지?\"\n\n\"반갑다마다! 내 귀한 녀석아, 이 반가움은 말로는 다 못 해. 에이미는 어디 있어?\"\n\n\"어머니가 메그 누나네로 데려가셨어. 오는 길에 거기 들렀는데, 다들 내 아내를 붙잡고 놓아주질 않잖아.\"\n\n\"네 뭐라고?\" 조가 소리쳤다. 로리가 그 두 마디를 자기도 모르게 우쭐대며 흐뭇하게 내뱉어 속내를 들켜 버렸기 때문이다.\n\n\"이런, 제기랄! 그만 일을 저질러 버렸네.\" 그가 어찌나 죄지은 표정을 짓던지, 조는 번개같이 그를 몰아세웠다.\n\n\"기어이 결혼을 해 버렸구나!\"\n\n\"응, 미안. 하지만 두 번 다시는 안 할게.\" 그러고는 그는 뉘우치듯 두 손을 모으고 무릎을 꿇었다. 짓궂음과 즐거움과 의기양양함이 가득한 얼굴로.\n\n\"정말로 결혼한 거야?\"\n\n\"아주 확실하게, 고마워.\"\n\n\"세상에! 다음엔 또 무슨 끔찍한 짓을 저지를 작정이니?\" 그러고는 조는 숨을 헐떡이며 자리에 털썩 주저앉았다.\n\n\"누나다운 축하긴 한데, 그리 듣기 좋은 축하는 아니네.\" 로리가 여전히 비굴한 자세 그대로, 그러면서도 만족감에 환히 빛나며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"도둑처럼 살금살금 기어들어 와서 사람 숨을 다 막아 놓고, 그렇게 비밀을 툭 흘려 놓고선 무슨 축하를 바라니? 일어나, 이 우스꽝스러운 녀석아. 그리고 자초지종을 다 얘기해.\"\n\n\"한마디도 못 해. 내 옛 자리에 앉게 해 주고, 막아 세우지 않겠다고 약속하지 않으면.\"\n\n조는 그 말에 오랜만에 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그러고는 다정한 목소리로 소파를 토닥이며 권했다.\n\n\"그 베개는 다락방에 있어. 이제 필요도 없고. 그러니 와서 다 털어놔, 테디.\"\n\n\"누나가 '테디'라고 불러 주는 게 어찌나 듣기 좋은지! 날 그렇게 부르는 사람은 누나밖에 없어.\" 그러고는 로리는 더없이 만족스러운 태도로 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"에이미는 널 뭐라고 불러?\"\n\n\"내 주인님.\"\n\n\"그 애답네. 하긴, 너도 그래 보이긴 해.\" 조의 눈은 제 녀석이 어느 때보다 잘생겨 보인다고 분명하게 말하고 있었다.\n\n베개는 사라졌지만, 그래도 막아선 무언가가 있긴 했다. 세월과 떨어져 지낸 시간, 그리고 변해 버린 마음이 세워 놓은 자연스러운 장벽이었다. 두 사람 다 그것을 느꼈고, 잠시 서로를 바라보았다. 그 보이지 않는 장벽이 둘 위로 옅은 그늘을 드리우기라도 한 듯이. 그러나 그 그늘은 곧 사라졌다. 로리가 짐짓 위엄을 부려 보려 헛되이 애쓰며 이렇게 말했으니까.\n\n\"내가 결혼한 남자에다 한 집안의 가장으로 안 보여?\"\n\n\"눈곱만큼도. 그리고 앞으로도 영 안 그럴걸. 덩치는 커지고 더 잘생겨졌지만, 넌 여전히 옛날 그대로 말썽꾸러기인걸.\"\n\n\"이거 참, 조 누나, 날 좀 더 존중해 줘야 하는 거 아니야.\" 로리가 이 모든 게 더없이 즐겁다는 듯 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"어떻게 그래? 네가 결혼해서 자리 잡았다는 생각만 해도 우스워 죽겠는데 정색을 어떻게 하니!\" 조가 온 얼굴에 웃음을 띠며 대꾸했는데, 어찌나 옮는지 두 사람은 또 한바탕 웃고 나서, 옛 정겨운 방식 그대로 도란도란 이야기를 시작했다.\n\n\"에이미 데리러 추운 데 나갈 것 없어. 다들 곧 올라올 테니까. 난 도무지 못 기다리겠더라고. 그 깜짝 소식을 누나한테 맨 처음 전하는 사람이 되고 싶었거든. 옛날에 크림 가지고 다툴 때 말마따나 '첫 국물'을 떠먹고 싶었어.\"\n\n\"그래, 너답구나. 그러다 거꾸로 시작해서 이야기를 다 망쳐 버렸잖니. 자, 이번엔 제대로 처음부터, 어떻게 다 된 건지 얘기해 봐. 궁금해 미치겠으니까.\"\n\n\"음, 난 에이미를 기쁘게 해 주려고 한 거야.\" 로리가 눈을 반짝이며 입을 떼자, 조가 외쳤다.\n\n\"거짓말 첫 번째. 에이미가 널 기쁘게 해 주려고 한 거겠지. 자, 계속해 봐. 할 수 있으면 진실만 말해, 이 양반아.\"\n\n\"이제 슬슬 훈계 선생님 노릇을 하려 드는걸. 저 말투 듣는 게 어찌나 재미난지 몰라.\" 로리가 난롯불을 향해 말하자, 난롯불도 전적으로 동의한다는 듯 환히 일렁이며 탁탁 튀었다. \"어차피 다 같은 얘기야. 그 애랑 나는 하나니까. 한 달 남짓 전에 우린 캐럴 댁 식구들과 함께 집으로 돌아오기로 했었어. 그런데 그분들이 갑자기 마음을 바꿔서 파리에서 겨울을 한 번 더 나기로 한 거야. 하지만 할아버지는 집에 오고 싶어 하셨어. 날 위해 가셨던 거니까, 혼자 보내 드릴 순 없었지. 그렇다고 에이미를 두고 올 수도 없고. 게다가 캐럴 부인은 보호자니 뭐니 하는 영국식 관념이 머릿속에 박혀 있어서, 에이미가 우리랑 같이 오는 걸 허락하지 않으시는 거야. 그래서 내가 그냥 이렇게 말해서 문제를 정리해 버렸어. '우리 결혼하자, 그럼 마음대로 할 수 있잖아'라고.\"\n\n\"그래, 너답네. 넌 늘 일을 네 좋을 대로 맞추는 녀석이지.\"\n\n\"늘 그런 건 아니야.\" 로리의 목소리에 어린 무언가에 조는 황급히 말을 돌렸다.\n\n\"그래, 도대체 이모님은 어떻게 설득한 거야?\"\n\n\"쉽지 않았어. 그래도 둘이 같이 이모님을 구슬렸지. 우리 쪽에 그럴듯한 이유가 잔뜩 있었거든. 편지로 허락을 구할 시간이야 없었지만, 어차피 다들 좋아할 일이고 머지않아 동의했을 일이잖아. 그러니 그저 내 아내 말마따나 '시간의 발목을 잡아챈' 것뿐이었어.\"\n\n\"'내 아내'라는 그 두 마디가 어지간히도 자랑스럽고, 입에 올리고 싶어 죽겠나 보지?\" 조가 이번엔 자기가 난롯불을 향해 끼어들며, 마지막으로 보았을 때 그토록 비극적으로 어둡던 그 눈에 이제 행복의 빛이 일렁이는 것을 흐뭇하게 지켜보며 말했다.\n\n\"아주 조금은, 그럴지도. 그 앤 어찌나 매혹적인 여자인지 자랑스럽지 않을 수가 없는걸. 어쨌든, 그때 외삼촌과 이모님이 거기 계셔서 체면치레를 해 주셨고, 우린 서로한테 푹 빠져 있어서 떨어져 있으면 아무짝에도 쓸모가 없었어. 그러니 그 멋진 방법이면 모든 게 두루 수월해질 거였지. 그래서 그렇게 한 거야.\"\n\n\"언제, 어디서, 어떻게?\" 조가 여자다운 관심과 호기심에 달떠 물었다. 도무지 한 톨도 실감이 나지 않았던 것이다.\n\n\"여섯 주 전에, 파리의 미국 영사관에서. 물론 아주 조용한 결혼식이었어. 그렇게 행복한 와중에도 사랑스러운 베스를 잊지는 않았으니까.\"\n\n조가 그 말에 그의 손에 제 손을 포갰고, 로리는 자기가 잘 기억하는 그 작고 붉은 베개를 가만히 쓸어 주었다.\n\n\"그런데 왜 나중에라도 우리한테 안 알린 거야?\" 두 사람이 잠시 가만히 앉아 있다가, 조가 한결 차분해진 목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"누나를 놀래 주고 싶었거든. 처음엔 곧장 집으로 올 줄 알았어. 그런데 그 다정하신 할아버지가 결혼하시자마자 적어도 한 달은 채비할 시간이 필요하다는 걸 아시고는, 우리더러 어디든 좋은 데서 신혼여행을 보내고 오라며 떠나보내셨지 뭐야. 에이미가 언젠가 발로사를 두고 그야말로 신혼여행을 보낼 만한 곳이라고 했었거든. 그래서 거기로 갔지. 사람이 평생에 단 한 번 누릴 만큼 행복했어. 맙소사! 장미꽃 속의 사랑이라니, 그야말로!\"\n\n로리는 잠시 조의 존재를 잊은 듯했고, 조는 그게 반가웠다. 그가 이런 이야기를 그토록 거리낌 없이 자연스럽게 한다는 것은, 그가 완전히 용서하고 잊었다는 증거였으니까. 그녀는 손을 빼려 했지만, 반쯤 무심결에 그런 충동이 인 까닭을 알아채기라도 한 듯 로리가 손을 꼭 붙들었다. 그러고는 일찍이 그에게서 본 적 없는 사내다운 진중함으로 말했다.\n\n\"조 누나, 한 가지만 말하고, 그다음엔 영영 덮어 두자. 편지에 썼던 것처럼, 에이미가 내게 그토록 다정하게 대해 줬다고 쓰면서 말했듯이, 난 결코 누나를 사랑하는 걸 멈추지 않을 거야. 하지만 그 사랑은 달라졌고, 지금 이대로가 더 낫다는 걸 알게 됐어. 그저 내 마음속에서 에이미와 누나가 자리를 바꾼 것뿐이야. 난 그게 본래 그렇게 될 일이었다고 생각해. 누나가 그러라고 했던 것처럼 내가 기다렸다면 자연스레 그렇게 됐을 거야. 그런데 난 도무지 참을성이 없어서 가슴앓이를 했던 거지. 그땐 난 소년이었어. 고집 세고 격하기만 한. 그래서 내 잘못을 깨닫는 데 호된 가르침이 필요했지. 그건 잘못이었으니까, 조 누나. 누나 말대로. 그리고 난 한참 바보짓을 하고 나서야 그걸 알았어. 정말이지 한때는 마음이 어찌나 뒤죽박죽이던지, 누나가 좋은지 에이미가 좋은지도 모르겠어서 둘을 똑같이 사랑하려고 해 봤어. 그런데 그게 안 되더라고. 그러다 스위스에서 그 애를 본 순간, 모든 게 단번에 또렷해졌어. 누나도 그 애도 제자리를 찾았고, 새 사랑을 시작하기 전에 옛 사랑을 잘 매듭지었다는 걸 확실히 느꼈어. 난 누나 조와 아내 에이미에게 내 마음을 떳떳하게 나누어, 둘 다 깊이 사랑할 수 있어. 누나도 그걸 믿고, 우리가 처음 알게 됐던 그 행복하던 옛 시절로 돌아가 주겠어?\"\n\n\"진심으로 믿을게. 하지만 테디, 우린 두 번 다시 소년 소녀로 돌아갈 순 없어. 그 행복하던 옛 시절은 돌아오지 않고, 우리도 그걸 바라선 안 돼. 이제 우린 어른이 됐고, 진중한 할 일이 있잖아. 놀이 시간은 끝났으니, 까불며 노는 건 그만둬야지. 너도 분명 느낄 거야. 난 네게서 그 변화를 봐. 너도 내게서 보게 될 거고. 내 녀석이 그리울 테지만, 어른이 된 너도 그만큼 사랑할 거고, 더 우러러볼 거야. 네가 내가 바라던 사람이 되려고 하니까. 우린 더 이상 어린 놀이 동무가 될 순 없지만, 오누이가 되어 평생토록 서로를 사랑하고 도울 거야. 그렇지, 로리?\"\n\n그는 한마디도 하지 않고, 그녀가 내미는 손을 잡아 잠시 그 위에 얼굴을 묻었다. 소년다운 격정의 무덤에서 두 사람 모두를 축복할 아름답고 굳건한 우정이 피어났음을 느끼면서. 이윽고 조가 명랑하게 말했다. 돌아온 이 만남을 슬프게 만들고 싶지 않았던 것이다.\n\n\"너희가 정말 결혼해서 살림을 차린다는 게 도무지 실감이 안 나. 어제만 해도 에이미 앞치마를 채워 주고, 네가 놀리면 머리채를 잡아당기곤 했던 것 같은데. 어머나, 세월 참 빠르기도 하지!\"\n\n\"그 아이들 중 하나는 누나보다 나이가 많은데, 무슨 할머니처럼 말씀하시네. 난 페거티가 데이비드를 두고 한 말마따나 '다 자란 신사'라고 자부하는걸. 그리고 에이미를 보면, 그 애가 꽤나 조숙한 갓난쟁이라는 걸 알게 될 거야.\" 로리가 그녀의 어머니다운 태도를 재미있어하며 말했다.\n* 페거티가 데이비드를 두고 한 말 : 찰스 디킨스의 소설 「데이비드 코퍼필드」에서 유모 페거티가 한 표현(옮긴이).\n\n\"나이야 네가 조금 많을지 몰라도, 마음만큼은 내가 훨씬 늙었어, 테디. 여자들은 늘 그렇거든. 게다가 지난 한 해가 어찌나 고됐던지, 난 마흔은 된 기분이야.\"\n\n\"가엾은 조 누나! 우리가 놀러 다니는 동안 누나를 혼자 두고 그걸 다 견디게 했구나. 정말 나이가 들었네. 여기 주름 한 줄, 저기 또 한 줄. 누나는 웃지 않으면 눈이 슬퍼 보여. 방금 쿠션을 만져 보니 눈물이 떨어져 있더라. 누나는 견뎌야 할 게 너무 많았는데, 그걸 다 혼자 견뎌야 했구나. 난 얼마나 이기적인 짐승이었던 거야!\" 로리는 뉘우치는 표정으로 제 머리카락을 쥐어뜯었다.\n\n그러나 조는 그저 그 속내를 들켜 버린 베개를 뒤집어 놓고는, 애써 명랑하게 들리도록 한 목소리로 대답했다.\n\n\"아니야. 난 도와주는 아버지와 어머니가 계셨고, 위로가 돼 주는 사랑스러운 아기들이 있었어. 게다가 너랑 에이미가 무사히 행복하게 지낸다는 생각이 여기서의 시름을 더 견디기 쉽게 해 줬는걸. 가끔 외롭긴 하지만, 그것도 나한테 좋은 거겠지, 그리고—\"\n\n\"이제 두 번 다시 외롭게 두지 않을게.\" 로리가 마치 온갖 불행을 막아 주려는 듯 그녀를 팔로 감싸며 말을 가로챘다. \"에이미랑 나는 누나 없인 못 살아. 그러니 누나가 와서 '그 아이들'한테 살림하는 법을 가르쳐 주고, 옛날처럼 뭐든 반씩 나누고, 우리한테 응석도 부려 줘. 다 같이 더없이 행복하고 다정하게 지내자.\"\n\n\"내가 방해만 안 된다면, 그것도 참 즐겁겠다. 벌써 한결 젊어지는 기분인걸. 어쩐 일인지 네가 오니까 내 시름이 죄다 날아가 버린 것 같아. 넌 늘 위안이 됐어, 테디.\" 그러고는 조는 그의 어깨에 머리를 기댔다. 몇 해 전 베스가 앓아누웠을 때 로리가 자기한테 기대라고 했던 그때처럼.\n\n그는 그녀가 그때를 기억하는지 궁금해하며 내려다보았지만, 조는 혼자 미소 짓고 있었다. 정말로 그가 와 준 것만으로 시름이 죄다 사라져 버리기라도 한 듯이.\n\n\"누나는 여전히 그대로네. 한순간 눈물을 뚝뚝 흘리다가 다음 순간 깔깔 웃으니. 지금은 좀 짓궂은 표정인데, 무슨 일이야, 할머니?\"\n\n\"너랑 에이미가 어떻게 지낼지 궁금해서.\"\n\n\"천사들처럼!\"\n\n\"그래, 그야 물론, 처음엔 그렇겠지. 그런데 누가 휘어잡는데?\"\n\n\"지금은 그 애가 휘어잡는다고 솔직히 말해 줄게. 적어도 그 애가 그렇게 여기게 해 준다고나 할까. 그게 그 애를 기쁘게 하거든. 머지않아 번갈아 가게 될 거야. 결혼이란 게 권리는 반으로 줄이고 의무는 곱으로 늘린다잖아.\"\n\n\"넌 시작한 대로 쭉 갈 거고, 에이미는 평생토록 널 휘어잡을걸.\"\n\n\"뭐, 그 애가 어찌나 티 안 나게 하는지 난 별로 개의치 않을 것 같아. 그 앤 잘 다스릴 줄 아는 부류의 여자거든. 솔직히 난 오히려 그게 좋아. 명주실 한 타래처럼 부드럽고 어여쁘게 사람을 제 손가락에 감아쥐면서도, 내내 마치 자기가 내게 호의를 베푸는 것처럼 느끼게 만드니까.\"\n\n\"내가 살다 살다 네가 공처가가 돼서, 그걸 즐기는 꼴까지 보게 될 줄이야!\" 조가 두 손을 번쩍 치켜들며 외쳤다.\n\n로리가 어깨를 펴고, 그런 빈정거림에 사내다운 경멸의 미소를 지어 보이는 것은 보기에 좋았다. 그는 \"도도하고 위풍당당한\" 태도로 대꾸했다.\n\n\"에이미는 그러기엔 너무 교양 있는 여자고, 나도 그런 데 굴복할 사내는 아니야. 내 아내와 나는 우리 자신과 서로를 너무 존중해서, 군림하거나 다투는 일은 없을 거야.\"\n\n조는 그 말이 마음에 들었고, 그 새로운 위엄이 무척 어울린다고 생각했다. 그러나 소년은 어른으로 빠르게 변해 가는 듯했고, 그 기쁨에 아쉬움이 뒤섞였다.\n\n\"분명 그럴 거야. 에이미랑 너는 우리처럼 다툰 적이 없었으니까. 우화에서처럼 그 애는 해이고 난 바람이지. 너도 기억하지, 사람을 다룬 건 해가 더 솜씨 있었잖아.\"\n\n\"그 애는 사람한테 햇볕만 쬐어 주는 게 아니라 한바탕 호되게 몰아붙일 줄도 알아.\" 로리가 웃었다. \"니스에서 내가 받은 그 설교라니! 정말이지 누나의 어떤 잔소리보다 한참 더 매서웠어. 그야말로 사람을 깨우는 호통이었지. 언젠가 다 얘기해 줄게. 그 앤 절대 안 할 테니까. 날 경멸하고 부끄럽다고 해 놓고선, 정작 그 경멸하던 작자한테 마음을 빼앗겨 그 변변찮은 녀석이랑 결혼해 버렸으니 말이야.\"\n\n\"이런 못된 것! 흠, 그 애가 널 함부로 대하거든 나한테 와. 내가 편들어 줄 테니까.\"\n\n\"내가 그런 게 필요해 보여, 안 그래?\" 로리가 일어서며 한 자세를 취했는데, 에이미의 목소리가 들려오자 그 자세는 위풍당당하던 데서 별안간 황홀한 것으로 바뀌었다.\n\n\"어디 있어요? 나의 사랑하는 조 언니 어디 있어요?\"\n\n온 식구가 우르르 몰려 들어왔고, 모두가 다시 한번 끌어안고 입을 맞추었다. 그리고 몇 번의 헛수고 끝에야 세 떠돌이를 자리에 앉혀 놓고 바라보며 한껏 기뻐할 수 있었다. 여느 때처럼 정정하고 기운찬 로런스 씨는 외국 여행으로 다른 이들 못지않게 좋아져 있었다. 무뚝뚝함은 거의 가시고, 예스러운 품위에는 윤기가 더해져 어느 때보다 따뜻해 보였다. 그가 \"내 아이들\"이라 부르는 젊은 부부를 향해 환히 웃는 모습은 보기에 좋았다. 에이미가 그 노인의 마음을 송두리째 사로잡는 딸다운 도리와 애정을 그에게 바치는 모습은 더더욱 보기 좋았다. 그리고 무엇보다 좋은 것은, 로리가 두 사람이 빚어내는 어여쁜 그림을 아무리 보아도 질리지 않는다는 듯 그 둘 주위를 맴도는 모습이었다.\n\n에이미를 보는 순간, 메그는 제 옷차림에 파리풍이라곤 없다는 것을, 젊은 모팻 부인이 젊은 로런스 부인 앞에서는 완전히 빛을 잃으리라는 것을, 그리고 \"그 귀부인\"이 더없이 우아하고 기품 있는 여인이라는 것을 의식하게 되었다. 조는 그 한 쌍을 지켜보며 생각했다. '둘이 정말 잘 어울리는구나! 내가 옳았어. 로리는 그 집을 투박한 옛 조보다 더 잘 가꿔 줄, 그래서 그에게 괴로움이 아니라 자랑이 될 아름답고 교양 있는 여자를 찾았어.' 마치 부인과 그 남편은 행복한 얼굴로 서로에게 미소를 지으며 고개를 끄덕였다. 막내딸이 세속적인 면에서뿐 아니라, 사랑과 신뢰와 행복이라는 더 값진 재산에서도 잘해 냈음을 보았기 때문이다.\n\n에이미의 얼굴에는 평화로운 마음을 드러내는 부드러운 광채가 가득했고, 목소리에는 전에 없던 다정함이 깃들어 있었으며, 차갑고 새침하던 몸가짐은 여성스럽고도 사람을 끌어당기는 우아한 기품으로 바뀌어 있었다. 가식이라곤 조금도 그 모습을 해치지 않았고, 진심에서 우러난 다정한 태도는 새로 얻은 아름다움이나 예전의 우아함보다도 더 매력적이었다. 그것이야말로 에이미가 되고자 했던 진정한 숙녀임을 분명하게 새겨 주는 표시였기 때문이다.\n\n\"사랑이 우리 어린 딸을 많이 변하게 했군요.\" 어머니가 나직이 말했다.\n\n\"평생 좋은 본보기를 곁에 두고 자랐으니까요, 여보.\" 마치 씨가 옆에 앉은 늙고 지친 얼굴과 희끗한 머리를 애정 어린 눈길로 바라보며 속삭이듯 대답했다.\n\n데이지는 \"예쁜 이모\"에게서 도무지 눈을 뗄 수 없었지만, 무릎 강아지처럼 매달린 곳은 사랑스러운 장신구들이 잔뜩 달린 그 멋진 허리 장식이었다. 데미는 베른에서 온 나무 곰 가족 인형이라는 솔깃한 형태의 뇌물을 덥석 받아들여 자신을 굽히기 전에, 잠시 멈추어 이 새로운 관계를 곰곰이 따져 보았다. 그러나 측면 공격이 무조건 항복을 이끌어 냈으니, 로리는 데미를 어떻게 다루어야 하는지 잘 알고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"이봐, 젊은이, 내가 처음 자네와 인사를 나누는 영광을 누렸을 때 자네는 내 얼굴을 한 대 쳤지. 이제 신사로서 그 결투의 사죄를 요구하네.\" 그렇게 말하더니 키 큰 삼촌은 작은 조카를 번쩍 들어 올려 헝클어뜨리기 시작했고, 그 바람에 데미의 철학자 같은 위엄은 무너졌지만 사내아이다운 마음은 그만큼 신이 났다.\n\n\"머리부터 발끝까지 비단을 두르고 있으니 이거 원, 정말 대단하지 않아? 저렇게 앉아 있는 모습이 바이올린처럼 곱고, 사람들이 우리 꼬맹이 에이미를 보고 로런스 부인이라고 부르는 걸 듣자니 어찌나 흐뭇한지!\" 늙은 해나가 중얼거렸다. 그녀는 도무지 종잡을 수 없는 방식으로 식탁을 차리면서도 문틈으로 자꾸만 \"엿보고\" 싶은 마음을 참을 수가 없었다.\n\n세상에, 어찌나 이야기들을 해 대던지! 처음엔 한 사람이, 그다음엔 또 한 사람이, 그러다 결국 모두가 한꺼번에 터져 나오며 삼 년 세월을 반 시간 안에 풀어놓으려 했다. 마침 차 마실 시간이 다가온 게 다행이었으니, 덕분에 잠시 숨을 돌리고 기운을 차릴 수 있었다. 그렇게라도 멈추지 않았다면 다들 목이 쉬고 기진맥진했을 터였다. 작은 식당으로 줄지어 들어가는 그 행렬은 어찌나 행복해 보이던지! 마치 씨는 자랑스럽게 \"로런스 부인\"을 모셨고, 마치 부인은 그 못지않게 자랑스럽게 \"내 아들\"의 팔에 기댔다. 노신사는 조의 팔을 잡으며 \"이제 자네가 내 딸이 되어 주어야 하네\" 하고 속삭이고는, 난롯가의 텅 빈 구석을 흘끗 바라보았다. 그 눈길에 조도 입술을 떨며 속삭이듯 대답했다. \"그 자리를 채우도록 애써 볼게요.\"\n\n쌍둥이는 천년왕국이 코앞에 온 듯한 기분으로 뒤에서 깡충거렸다. 다들 새로 온 사람들에게 정신이 팔려 둘을 마음껏 뛰놀게 내버려 두었으니, 두 아이가 그 기회를 놓치지 않고 한껏 누렸으리라는 건 두말할 나위가 없다. 차를 몰래 홀짝거리고, 진저브레드를 마음껏 입에 쑤셔 넣고, 따끈한 비스킷을 하나씩 차지하지 않았겠는가. 게다가 가장 큰 죄로, 둘 다 먹음직스러운 작은 타르트를 하나씩 조그만 주머니에 슬쩍 집어넣어 그 안에서 음흉하게 들러붙고 부스러지게 만들지 않았겠는가. 인간 본성도 페이스트리도 다 약하기 짝이 없다는 사실을 그렇게 배운 셈이다. 슬쩍 빼돌린 타르트 때문에 죄책감을 짊어진 데다, 도도의 날카로운 눈이 노획물을 가린 얇은 옥양목과 메리노 천을 꿰뚫어 볼까 봐 겁이 난 두 어린 죄인은, 안경을 쓰지 않은 \"할아버지\"에게 찰싹 달라붙었다.\n다과처럼 이 사람 저 사람에게 떠밀려 다니던 에이미는 로런스 할아버지의 팔에 기대어 응접실로 돌아갔다. 나머지는 아까처럼 짝을 지었고, 그렇게 짝짓다 보니 조만 짝이 없게 되었다. 조는 그 순간엔 별로 개의치 않았으니, 해나의 다급한 물음에 대답하느라 뒤에 처져 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"에이미 아가씨는 그 마차(쿠페)를 타고 다니고, 저쪽에 잔뜩 쟁여 둔 그 예쁜 은그릇들을 다 쓰게 되나요?\"\n\n\"여섯 마리 백마가 끄는 마차를 몰고, 금 접시에 음식을 담아 먹고, 다이아몬드에 레이스 장식을 날마다 두르고 다닌대도 난 놀라지 않을 거예요. 테디는 에이미한테는 아무리 좋은 것도 부족하다고 생각하니까요.\" 조가 더없이 흡족한 얼굴로 대답했다.\n\n\"암, 그렇고말고요! 아침으로는 해시를 드릴까요, 생선완자를 드릴까요?\" 시(詩)와 산문을 슬기롭게 섞어 쓰는 해나가 물었다.\n\n\"아무거나 상관없어요.\" 조는 그렇게 말하고 문을 닫았다. 지금 음식 얘기는 영 마음에 맞지 않는 화제라는 생각이 들었기 때문이다. 조는 잠시 서서 위층으로 사라지는 식구들을 바라보았다. 데미의 짧은 격자무늬 다리가 마지막 계단을 힘겹게 오르는 모습을 보고 있자니, 별안간 외로움이 어찌나 사무치게 밀려오던지, 조는 무언가 기댈 것을 찾으려는 듯 흐릿해진 눈으로 주위를 둘러보았다. 테디마저 자기를 버리고 가 버렸으니 말이다. 만약 어떤 생일 선물이 매 순간 점점 더 가까이 다가오고 있는지 알았더라면, 조는 속으로 \"잠자리에 들면 조금 울어야지. 지금 우울해 봤자 소용없으니까\" 하고 중얼거리지는 않았을 것이다. 그러고는 손등으로 눈을 훔쳤다. 사내아이 같은 버릇 중 하나로, 손수건이 어디 있는지 도무지 모르는 게 그녀였으니까. 그렇게 간신히 미소를 지어 올렸을 때, 현관문을 두드리는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n조는 손님을 맞이하듯 서둘러 문을 열었다가, 또 다른 유령이라도 나타나 자기를 놀래기라도 한 양 흠칫 놀랐다. 거기 키 크고 수염을 기른 신사가 서서, 어둠 속에서 한밤중의 태양처럼 그녀를 향해 환하게 미소 짓고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"오, 베어 선생님, 정말 정말 반가워요!\" 조는 그를 와락 붙잡으며 소리쳤다. 안으로 들이기도 전에 밤이 그를 삼켜 버릴까 두려워하기라도 하듯이.\n\n\"저도 마치 양을 뵈어서—아니, 손님이 와 계시는군요.\" 안에서 두런거리는 목소리와 춤추는 발소리가 들려오자 베어 교수는 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"아니에요, 손님은 없어요. 식구들뿐이에요. 언니랑 친구들이 막 돌아와서 다들 무척 기뻐하고 있어요. 들어와서 함께해요.\"\n\n베어 씨는 무척 사교적인 사람이긴 했지만, 내 생각에는 점잖게 물러갔다가 다른 날 다시 찾아오려 했을 것이다. 그러나 조가 그의 등 뒤로 문을 닫고 모자까지 빼앗아 버렸으니 어쩔 도리가 없지 않았겠는가? 어쩌면 조의 얼굴 때문이었는지도 모른다. 그를 보고 반가운 마음을 감추는 걸 깜빡 잊고, 거부할 수 없을 만큼 솔직하게 그 기쁨을 드러냈으니까. 그 환대는 외로운 그가 감히 품었던 가장 대담한 기대마저 훌쩍 뛰어넘는 것이었다.\n\n\"제가 거추장스러운 사람이 아니라면, 다들 기꺼이 뵙고 싶군요. 그동안 편찮으셨소, 나의 친구?\"\n\n그는 불쑥 그렇게 물었다. 조가 그의 외투를 걸어 두는 동안 불빛이 그녀의 얼굴에 비쳤고, 그는 거기서 어떤 변화를 보았던 것이다.\n\n\"아픈 건 아니고요, 지치고 슬펐어요. 지난번에 뵌 뒤로 힘든 일이 있었거든요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇군요, 알고 있소. 그 소식을 들었을 때 제 마음도 아렸지요.\" 그는 다시 손을 맞잡았는데, 그 얼굴이 어찌나 다정하던지, 조는 그 따뜻한 눈빛과 크고 따스한 손길만 한 위로가 세상에 또 있을까 싶었다.\n\n\"아버지, 어머니, 이분이 제 친구 베어 교수님이세요.\" 조는 도무지 억누를 수 없는 자부심과 기쁨이 어린 얼굴과 목소리로 말했다. 차라리 나팔을 불고 문을 활짝 열어젖히며 요란하게 소개했다 해도 그보다 더하지는 않았을 것이다.\n\n이방인이 자기를 어떻게 맞이할지 조금이라도 의심을 품었다면, 그 따뜻한 환대에 단숨에 마음이 놓였을 것이다. 다들 처음에는 조를 봐서 그를 다정히 맞았지만, 이내 그 자체로 그를 좋아하게 되었다. 그럴 수밖에 없었으니, 그는 모든 마음의 문을 여는 부적을 지니고 있었던 것이다. 이 소박한 사람들은 단번에 그에게 마음이 따뜻해졌고, 그가 가난하다는 사실 때문에 오히려 더 친근하게 느껴졌다. 가난은 그것을 넘어서 사는 이들을 풍요롭게 하고, 진정 후한 마음을 지닌 이들에게 가는 확실한 통행증이 되기 때문이다. 베어 씨는 낯선 문을 두드렸다가 문이 열리자 제집에 온 듯한 기분이 든 나그네 같은 표정으로 주위를 둘러보며 앉아 있었다. 아이들은 꿀단지에 모여드는 벌처럼 그에게로 달려갔다. 양쪽 무릎에 자리를 잡고 앉더니, 어린아이다운 대담함으로 그의 주머니를 뒤지고, 수염을 잡아당기고, 회중시계를 살펴보며 그를 사로잡기 시작했다. 부인들은 흡족한 마음을 서로 눈짓으로 주고받았고, 마치 씨는 마음이 통하는 벗을 얻었다는 기분에 자신의 가장 귀한 이야기 곳간을 손님을 위해 열었다. 말없는 존은 그 대화를 듣고 즐기면서도 한마디도 하지 않았고, 로런스 씨는 도무지 잠들 수가 없었다.\n\n만약 조가 다른 일에 정신이 팔려 있지 않았더라면 로리의 태도가 그녀에게 꽤나 재미있었을 것이다. 질투까지는 아니어도 의심에 가까운 어떤 미묘한 거슬림 때문에, 이 신사는 처음엔 한발 물러나 형제다운 신중함으로 새로 온 사람을 살폈던 것이다. 하지만 그건 오래가지 않았다. 그는 자기도 모르게 흥미를 느꼈고, 어느새 그 무리 속으로 끌려들었다. 베어 씨가 이 화기애애한 분위기 속에서 말솜씨를 잘 발휘하며 자기 진면목을 드러냈기 때문이다. 그는 좀처럼 로리에게 직접 말을 걸지 않았지만, 자주 그를 바라보았고, 그럴 때마다 얼굴에 그늘이 스쳤다. 한창때의 젊은이를 지켜보면서, 마치 자기가 잃어버린 청춘을 아쉬워하는 듯이. 그러다 그의 눈길이 조에게로 향할 때면 어찌나 애틋하던지, 만약 조가 그 눈길을 보았더라면 그 말 없는 물음에 분명 대답했을 것이다. 하지만 조는 자기 눈을 단속하느라 바빴고, 그 눈을 믿을 수 없다고 느낀 터라, 본보기가 될 만한 노처녀 이모처럼 조심스레 시선을 자기가 뜨개질하던 작은 양말에 붙박아 두었다.\n\n이따금 슬쩍 던지는 눈길은 먼지투성이 길을 걷고 난 뒤 마시는 시원한 물 한 모금처럼 그녀를 새롭게 했다. 곁눈질로 살핀 그 모습에 여러 가지 좋은 조짐이 보였기 때문이다. 베어 씨의 얼굴에서는 멍한 표정이 사라지고, 지금 이 순간에 대한 관심으로 온통 생기가 돌았다. 정말이지 젊고 잘생겨 보였다고 조는 생각했다. 평소 낯선 남자들을 로리와 견주어 한참 깎아내리던 버릇도 깜빡 잊은 채로. 게다가 그는 꽤 영감에 차 보였는데, 대화가 옛사람들의 장례 풍습으로 흘러가 있었으니 그게 딱히 신나는 화제라고는 할 수 없는데도 그랬다. 테디가 논쟁에서 입을 다물게 되자 조는 승리의 기쁨에 환히 빛났고, 무언가에 몰두한 아버지의 얼굴을 바라보며 속으로 생각했다. \"우리 교수님 같은 분과 날마다 이야기를 나눌 수 있다면 아버지가 얼마나 좋아하실까!\" 마지막으로, 베어 씨는 새 검은색 정장을 차려입고 있어서 그 어느 때보다 신사다워 보였다. 텁수룩하던 머리는 잘라서 매끈하게 빗어 넘겼지만 오래 정돈된 채로 있지는 않았다. 흥분하는 순간이면 늘 하던 그 우스꽝스러운 버릇대로 머리카락을 헝클어뜨렸기 때문이다. 조는 그게 납작하게 붙어 있는 것보다 제멋대로 곤두선 모습이 더 좋았으니, 그래야 그의 멋진 이마가 주피터 같은 위풍을 띤다고 여겼기 때문이다. 가엾은 조, 그 수수한 남자를 어찌나 떠받들던지. 그토록 조용히 앉아 뜨개질을 하면서도 무엇 하나 놓치지 않았으니, 베어 씨가 티 하나 없는 소맷부리에 금 단추를 달고 있다는 사실까지도 말이다!\n\n\"다정한 양반 같으니! 청혼하러 가는 길이었대도 이보다 더 정성껏 차려입지는 못했을 거야.\" 조는 속으로 그렇게 중얼거렸다. 그러다 그 말에서 비롯된 갑작스러운 생각에 얼굴이 어찌나 끔찍하게 달아오르던지, 실뭉치를 떨어뜨리고는 그걸 줍는다는 핑계로 몸을 숙여 얼굴을 감춰야 했다.\n\n하지만 그 작전은 그녀가 기대한 만큼 잘 먹히지 않았다. 비유하자면 막 화장용 장작더미에 불을 붙이려던 참이었던 교수가 횃불을 떨어뜨리고 그 작은 파란 실뭉치를 향해 쑥 몸을 숙였기 때문이다. 당연히 둘은 머리를 세게 부딪쳤고, 눈앞에 별이 번쩍였으며, 둘 다 실뭉치는 못 집은 채 얼굴이 빨개져 웃으며 일어나서는 자리로 돌아갔다. 차라리 자리를 뜨지 말걸 하고 후회하면서.\n\n저녁이 어떻게 지나갔는지는 아무도 몰랐다. 해나가 발그레한 양귀비 두 송이처럼 꾸벅거리던 아기들을 이른 시각에 솜씨 좋게 빼돌렸고, 로런스 씨는 쉬러 집으로 돌아갔다. 나머지는 난롯가에 둘러앉아 시간 가는 줄도 모른 채 이야기를 나누었다. 그러다 어머니다운 마음에 데이지가 침대에서 굴러떨어졌고 데미가 성냥의 구조를 연구하다 잠옷에 불을 붙였으리라는 확고한 믿음에 사로잡힌 메그가 일어설 채비를 했다.\n\n\"옛날 그 좋던 방식대로 다 함께 노래를 불러야 해. 다시 한 번 모두 한자리에 모였으니까.\" 조가 말했다. 한바탕 목청껏 노래하는 것이 가슴 가득 차오른 기쁨을 풀어놓을 안전하고 즐거운 출구라는 생각이 들었던 것이다.\n\n모두가 그 자리에 있는 것은 아니었다. 하지만 누구도 그 말을 경솔하다거나 사실이 아니라고 여기지 않았다. 베스는 여전히 그들 가운데 있는 것 같았으니까. 평온한 존재로, 보이지는 않아도 그 어느 때보다 소중하게. 죽음도 사랑이 풀 수 없도록 단단히 맺어 놓은 그 가족의 결속을 끊지는 못했기 때문이다. 작은 의자는 제자리에 그대로 놓여 있었다. 바늘이 \"너무 무거워졌을\" 때 미처 끝내지 못하고 남긴 일감이 담긴 단정한 바구니도 늘 두던 선반 위에 그대로 있었다. 이제는 좀처럼 만지지 않는 그 사랑하던 악기도 옮기지 않은 채였고, 그 위에서는 옛날처럼 평온하게 미소 짓는 베스의 얼굴이 그들을 내려다보며 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"행복하세요. 저는 여기 있어요.\"\n\n\"뭐든 한번 쳐 봐, 에이미. 네가 얼마나 늘었는지 다들 들려주렴.\" 로리가 장래가 촉망되는 제자를 향해 봐줄 만한 자부심을 담아 말했다.\n\n그러나 에이미는 색이 바랜 의자를 빙그르르 돌리며 눈에 눈물을 가득 머금고 속삭였다.\n\n\"오늘 밤은 아니에요, 여보. 오늘은 솜씨를 뽐낼 수가 없어요.\"\n\n하지만 에이미는 화려함이나 기교보다 더 나은 무언가를 보여 주었다. 베스가 부르던 노래들을 부드러운 음색에 실어 불렀는데, 그건 제아무리 뛰어난 스승도 가르칠 수 없는 것이었고, 그 어떤 영감으로도 얻을 수 없었을 더 감미로운 힘으로 듣는 이들의 마음을 어루만졌다. 베스가 가장 좋아하던 찬송가의 마지막 구절에서 그 맑은 목소리가 별안간 끊겼을 때, 방 안은 아주 고요했다. 그 구절은 차마 부르기가 힘들었다.\n\n\"하늘이 고치지 못할 슬픔이란 이 땅에 없으리니.\"\n\n그러자 에이미는 뒤에 서 있던 남편에게 몸을 기댔다. 베스의 입맞춤 없는 귀향은 어쩐지 온전하지 못하다는 느낌이 들었던 것이다.\n\n\"자, 이제 미뇽의 노래로 마무리하자. 그건 베어 선생님이 부르시니까.\" 침묵이 괴로워지기 전에 조가 말했다. 그러자 베어 씨는 흡족한 듯 \"흠!\" 하고 헛기침을 하고는 조가 서 있는 구석으로 걸어가며 말했다.\n\n\"저와 함께 부르시겠소? 우리 둘이 아주 잘 어울리니까.\"\n\n그건 그런데 듣기 좋으라고 하는 말이었다. 조는 음악에 관해서라면 메뚜기만큼도 아는 게 없었으니까. 하지만 그가 오페라 한 편을 통째로 부르자고 했어도 그녀는 응했을 것이다. 그녀는 박자도 음정도 더없이 행복하게 무시한 채 흥얼거렸다. 그래도 큰 문제는 없었다. 베어 씨가 진짜 독일 사람답게 진심을 담아 멋지게 불렀으니까. 그리고 조는 이내 나직한 흥얼거림으로 잦아들었다. 오직 자기만을 위해 부르는 듯한 그 부드러운 목소리에 귀 기울이려고.\n\n\"레몬꽃 피어나는 그 땅을 그대 아는가\" 하는 구절은 교수가 늘 가장 좋아하던 대목이었다. 그에게 \"그 땅\"이란 곧 독일을 뜻했으니까. 하지만 지금 그는 유난히 따뜻하고 선율 가득한 목소리로 이 구절에 머무는 듯했다.\n\n\"그곳, 오 그곳으로, 그대와 함께,\n오 내 사랑이여, 갈 수 있다면!\"\n\n그러자 듣고 있던 한 사람은 그 다정한 초대에 어찌나 가슴이 떨리던지, 자기는 그 땅을 안다고, 그가 원한다면 언제든 기꺼이 그곳으로 떠나겠다고 말하고 싶어졌다.\n\n그 노래는 대성공으로 여겨졌고, 가수는 월계관을 쓰고 물러났다. 하지만 몇 분 뒤 그는 예의를 완전히 잊은 채, 보닛을 쓰는 에이미를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 에이미가 그저 \"제 여동생\"으로만 소개되었고, 그가 온 뒤로 아무도 그녀를 새 이름으로 부르지 않았던 것이다. 헤어질 때 로리가 더없이 정중한 태도로 이렇게 말하자, 그는 더더욱 정신을 놓고 말았다.\n\n\"제 아내와 저는 선생님을 뵙게 되어 무척 기쁩니다. 길 건너 저희 집에는 언제든 환영이 기다리고 있다는 걸 기억해 주십시오.\"\n\n그러자 교수가 어찌나 진심으로 고마워하며 별안간 흡족함으로 환하게 빛나던지, 로리는 그를 여태껏 만나 본 사람 중 가장 유쾌하게 속내를 잘 드러내는 양반이라고 여겼다.\n\n\"저도 그만 가 보아야겠지만, 허락해 주신다면 다시 기꺼이 찾아오겠소, 부인. 시내에 볼일이 좀 있어서 며칠은 여기 더 머물게 될 테니.\"\n\n그는 마치 부인에게 말했지만 시선은 조를 향하고 있었다. 그리고 어머니의 목소리는 딸의 눈빛 못지않게 다정하게 승낙의 뜻을 전했다. 마치 부인은 모팻 부인이 짐작한 것처럼 자기 아이들의 마음에 어두운 사람이 아니었으니까.\n\n\"내 보기에 저 사람은 현명한 사람이오.\" 마지막 손님이 돌아간 뒤, 마치 씨가 난롯가 깔개 위에서 잔잔한 흡족함을 담아 말했다.\n\n\"좋은 사람인 건 분명해요.\" 마치 부인이 시계태엽을 감으며 단호한 찬성의 뜻을 보탰다.\n\n\"두 분이 그분을 좋아하실 줄 알았어요.\" 조는 그 말만 남기고 슬그머니 잠자리로 빠져나갔다.\n\n조는 무슨 볼일이 베어 씨를 시내로 데려왔을까 궁금해하다가, 결국 그가 어딘가에서 큰 명예로운 자리에 임명되었는데 너무 겸손해서 그 사실을 입에 올리지 않은 거라고 결론지었다. 만약 그가 제 방으로 돌아가 안전해지자 한 그림을 바라보던 모습을 조가 보았더라면, 어쩌면 이 문제에 약간의 빛이 비쳤을지도 모른다. 그 그림 속에는 숱 많은 머리를 한, 엄격하고 꼿꼿한 젊은 여인이 어두운 눈길로 미래를 응시하는 듯 그려져 있었다. 더욱이 그가 가스등을 끄고 어둠 속에서 그 그림에 입을 맞추는 모습을 보았더라면 말이다.\n\n# MY LORD AND LADY.\n\n\"어머니, 제 아내를 반 시간만 빌려주시겠어요? 짐이 도착해서 제가 찾는 물건들을 찾느라 에이미의 파리 옷가지들을 죄다 뒤집어 놓는 중이거든요.\" 이튿날 들어선 로리가 말했다. 로런스 부인이 어머니 무릎에 앉아 다시 \"아기\"가 된 듯이 있는 모습을 보고 한 말이었다.\n\n\"그럼요. 가 보렴, 얘야. 네게 이 집 말고 다른 집이 있다는 걸 깜빡했구나.\" 그렇게 말하며 마치 부인은 결혼반지를 낀 그 흰 손을 꼭 쥐었다. 어머니다운 욕심을 용서해 달라는 듯이.\n\n\"제가 어떻게든 안 와도 됐으면 안 왔을 텐데, 우리 작은 아내가 없으면 도무지 안 되거든요. 마치—\"\n\n\"바람 없는 풍향계 신세지.\" 로리가 비유할 말을 찾느라 멈칫하자 조가 거들었다. 테디가 돌아온 뒤로 조는 다시 예전의 발랄하고 당돌한 자기 모습으로 돌아와 있었다.\n\n\"바로 그거야. 에이미는 나를 대부분 정서쪽으로만 향하게 하고, 가끔 남쪽으로 살짝 돌게 할 뿐이거든. 결혼한 뒤로 동풍이 분 적은 한 번도 없고, 북쪽은 어떤지 전혀 모르겠지만, 어쨌든 아주 쾌적하고 온화하지. 안 그래, 마님?\"\n\n\"여태까진 날씨가 더없이 좋네요. 얼마나 갈지는 모르겠지만, 폭풍이 와도 무섭지 않아요. 제 배를 어떻게 몰아야 하는지 배우는 중이거든요. 집에 가요, 여보. 부츠 벗기는 걸 찾아 줄게요. 내 물건들을 뒤지는 게 아마 그것 때문이겠죠. 남자들은 어쩜 이렇게 손이 많이 가는지, 어머니.\" 에이미가 안주인다운 태도로 말하자 남편은 그 모습에 즐거워했다.\n\n\"자리 잡고 나면 둘이 뭘 할 작정이니?\" 조가 에이미의 망토 단추를 채워 주며 물었다. 예전에 앞치마 단추를 채워 주던 것처럼.\n\n\"우리도 계획이 있어. 아직은 우리가 워낙 새내기다 보니 그 얘긴 별로 안 하려고. 그래도 빈둥거릴 생각은 없어. 난 할아버지가 기뻐하실 만큼 헌신적으로 사업에 뛰어들어서, 내가 버릇없이 망가지지 않았다는 걸 증명할 거야. 나를 다잡으려면 그런 게 좀 필요하거든. 빈둥대는 데도 이젠 질렸으니, 남자답게 일할 작정이야.\"\n\n\"그럼 에이미는 뭘 할 거니?\" 로리의 결심과 그 말에 담긴 패기에 흡족해진 마치 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"두루두루 예의를 차리고 우리의 가장 좋은 보닛을 한껏 뽐낸 다음에는, 우리 저택의 우아한 환대로, 우리가 끌어모을 화려한 사교계로, 그리고 우리가 온 세상에 끼칠 유익한 영향력으로 두 분을 놀라게 해 드릴 거예요. 대충 그런 거지, 안 그래, 레카미에 부인?\" 로리가 에이미를 짓궂게 바라보며 물었다.\n\n\"두고 보면 알겠죠. 이리 와요, 버르장머리 없는 양반. 식구들 앞에서 별명으로 부르며 우리 집안을 놀라게 하지 말고요.\" 에이미가 대답했다. 사교계의 여왕으로 살롱을 차리기 전에, 우선 좋은 아내가 있는 가정을 꾸리겠다고 마음먹으면서.\n\n\"저 아이들, 함께 있는 모습이 어찌나 행복해 보이는지!\" 마치 씨가 말했다. 젊은 부부가 돌아간 뒤로 아리스토텔레스에 좀처럼 몰두하지 못하면서.\n\n\"그래요, 그리고 그게 오래갈 것 같아요.\" 마치 부인이 배를 무사히 항구로 데려온 도선사처럼 편안한 표정으로 덧붙였다.\n\n\"분명히 그럴 거예요. 행복한 에이미!\" 조는 한숨을 내쉬었다가, 베어 교수가 조바심을 내며 대문을 밀치고 들어서자 환하게 미소 지었다.\n\n그날 저녁 늦게, 부츠 벗기개 일로 마음이 놓인 로리가 새로 장만한 미술품들을 정리하느라 이리저리 분주히 다니는 아내에게 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"로런스 부인.\"\n\n\"네, 나리!\"\n\n\"저 양반, 우리 조랑 결혼할 작정이야!\"\n\n\"그랬으면 좋겠어요. 당신도 그렇지 않아요, 여보?\"\n\n\"음, 여보, 난 그 사람을 그 말의 가장 넓은 뜻에서 정말 멋진 사람이라고 생각해. 다만 좀 더 젊고 훨씬 더 부자였으면 좋겠다 싶긴 하지.\"\n\n\"이런, 로리, 너무 까다롭고 속물스럽게 굴지 말아요. 두 사람이 서로 사랑한다면 나이가 몇이든 얼마나 가난하든 조금도 상관없어요. 여자는 절대로 돈 때문에 결혼하면 안—\" 에이미는 말이 입 밖으로 새어 나가자 화들짝 말을 끊고 남편을 바라보았고, 남편은 짓궂게 짐짓 진지한 얼굴로 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그럼, 절대 안 되고말고. 가끔 매력적인 아가씨들이 그럴 작정이라고 말하는 걸 듣긴 하지만 말이야. 내 기억이 맞다면, 당신도 한때는 부유한 결혼을 하는 게 도리라고 여겼었지. 그러니 어쩌면 그래서 나 같은 쓸모없는 인간이랑 결혼한 건지도 모르겠군.\"\n\n\"오, 내 사랑, 제발, 제발 그런 말 말아요! '네'라고 대답했을 때 난 당신이 부자라는 걸 잊고 있었어요. 당신한테 한 푼도 없었어도 결혼했을 거예요. 가끔은 차라리 당신이 가난했으면 좋겠어요. 내가 당신을 얼마나 사랑하는지 보여 줄 수 있게요.\" 사람들 앞에서는 무척 점잖고 둘만 있을 때는 무척 다정한 에이미는, 자기 말이 진심임을 분명히 증명해 보였다.\n\n\"내가 한때 되려고 애썼던 것처럼 그렇게 돈만 밝히는 인간이라고 정말로 생각하는 건 아니죠? 당신이 호수에서 노를 저어 먹고살아야 하는 처지였대도 기꺼이 당신과 한 배를 탔으리라는 걸 믿어 주지 않는다면, 내 가슴이 찢어질 거예요.\"\n\n\"내가 멍청이에 짐승이라도 되는 줄 알아? 어떻게 그렇게 생각하겠어. 당신은 나를 위해 더 부유한 남자를 마다했고, 이제 내가 줄 권리가 생겼는데도 주고 싶은 것의 절반도 받으려 하질 않는데. 가엾게도 여자들은 날마다 그렇게들 하고, 그게 자기들의 유일한 구원이라고 배우지. 하지만 당신은 더 나은 가르침을 받았어. 한때는 당신 때문에 마음 졸이기도 했지만 실망하진 않았어. 딸이 어머니의 가르침에 충실했으니까. 어제 장모님께도 그렇게 말씀드렸더니, 자선에 쓰라고 백만 달러짜리 수표라도 드린 것처럼 기뻐하고 고마워하셨어. 듣고 있지 않군, 로런스 부인. 내 도덕적인 이야기를.\" 로리는 말을 멈추었다. 에이미의 눈이 그의 얼굴에 붙박여 있으면서도 어딘가 딴 데 가 있는 듯했기 때문이다.\n\n\"듣고 있어요. 동시에 당신 턱의 보조개를 감탄하며 보고 있었고요. 당신을 우쭐하게 만들고 싶진 않지만, 솔직히 고백하자면 난 당신 돈 전부보다 잘생긴 내 남편이 더 자랑스러워요. 웃지 마요, 당신 코는 정말이지 나한테 큰 위안이거든요.\" 그렇게 말하며 에이미는 그 잘 빠진 콧날을 예술가다운 흡족함으로 부드럽게 어루만졌다.\n\n로리는 살면서 숱한 찬사를 들었지만, 이보다 더 마음에 드는 건 한 번도 없었다. 그건 표정에 고스란히 드러났다. 아내의 별난 취향에 웃기는 했지만 말이다. 그러는 동안 에이미가 천천히 말했다.\n\n\"하나 물어봐도 돼요, 여보?\"\n\n\"물론이지.\"\n\n\"조가 정말 베어 씨랑 결혼하면 당신은 신경 쓰일까요?\"\n\n\"오, 그게 걱정이었군? 그 보조개 속에 당신 마음에 안 드는 뭔가가 있다 싶었어. 난 구유 위의 개* 같은 사람이 아니라 세상에서 가장 행복한 사내야. 장담하는데, 난 발만큼이나 가벼운 마음으로 조의 결혼식에서 춤출 수 있어. 못 믿겠어, 내 사랑?\"\n* 구유 위의 개: 자기는 못 먹으면서 남도 못 먹게 막는 심술쟁이를 뜻하는 옛 우화 : 옮긴이.\n\n에이미는 그를 올려다보고는 마음이 놓였다. 마지막 남은 작은 질투의 두려움이 영영 사라졌고, 그녀는 사랑과 신뢰가 가득한 얼굴로 그에게 고마움을 전했다.\n\n\"저 멋진 노교수를 위해 뭐든 해 드릴 수 있으면 좋겠어. 독일 어딘가에서 친절하게 돌아가시면서 그분께 제법 짭짤한 재산을 남겨 줄 부유한 친척이라도 하나 지어낼 수 없을까?\" 로리가 말했다. 두 사람이 긴 응접실을 팔짱을 끼고 오르락내리락 거닐기 시작할 무렵이었다. 그건 성(城)의 정원을 추억하며 둘이 즐겨 하던 일이었다.\n\n\"조가 알아채고 다 망쳐 버릴 거예요. 조는 지금 그대로의 그분을 무척 자랑스러워하거든요. 어제는 가난이야말로 아름다운 거라고 생각한다고 했어요.\"\n\n\"그 착한 마음씨 좀 봐! 글 쓰는 남편에다 부양해야 할 꼬마 교수 나리와 교수 아씨를 한 다스나 거느리게 되면 그렇게 생각하진 않을걸. 지금은 우리가 나서지 말고 기회를 엿보다가, 두 사람이 모르는 새에 좋은 일을 해 주자고. 난 조한테 교육의 일부를 빚졌고, 조는 사람이 정직한 빚은 갚아야 한다고 믿는 사람이니, 그런 식으로 둘러서 조를 구슬려 보지 뭐.\"\n\n\"남을 도울 수 있다는 게 얼마나 기쁜 일인지 몰라요. 그렇죠? 거리낌 없이 베풀 수 있는 힘을 갖는 것, 그게 늘 제 꿈 중 하나였어요. 그런데 당신 덕분에 그 꿈이 이루어졌네요.\"\n\n\"아! 우린 좋은 일을 잔뜩 할 거야. 안 그래? 내가 특히 돕고 싶은 가난이 한 종류 있어. 노골적인 거지들은 보살핌을 받지만, 가난한 양반들은 형편이 딱하거든. 손을 내밀 줄도 모르고, 사람들도 감히 자선을 베풀 엄두를 못 내니까. 그래도 그들을 돕는 방법은 천 가지나 있어. 기분 상하지 않게 아주 섬세하게 하는 법만 안다면 말이야. 솔직히 말해서, 난 입에 발린 소리나 늘어놓는 거지보다 몰락한 양반을 돕는 게 더 좋아. 그게 잘못된 건지도 모르지만, 정말 그래. 더 어려운 일인데도 말이야.\"\n\n\"그건 양반이라야 할 수 있는 일이니까요.\" 가정 내 상호 찬미회의 또 다른 회원이 거들었다.\n\n\"고마워. 그 멋진 칭찬을 받을 자격이 내겐 없는 것 같지만 말이야. 그건 그렇고, 내가 외국에서 빈둥거리며 지낼 때, 꿈을 이루려고 온갖 희생을 치르고 진짜 고생을 견뎌 내는 재능 있는 젊은이들을 많이 봤어. 그중엔 정말 훌륭한 친구들도 있었지. 영웅처럼 일하고, 가난하고 의지가지없으면서도 어찌나 용기와 인내와 야망이 넘치던지, 난 내 자신이 부끄러워졌고 그들을 제대로 끌어 주고 싶어 안달이 났어. 그런 사람들은 돕는 보람이 있어. 재능을 타고났다면 그들을 섬길 수 있다는 게 영광이고, 솥을 계속 끓일 땔감이 없어서 그 재능이 사그라지거나 늦춰지지 않게 해 주는 거니까. 재능이 없다 해도, 그 가엾은 영혼들을 위로하고 그 사실을 깨달았을 때 절망하지 않게 막아 주는 건 기쁜 일이지.\"\n\n\"그래요, 정말로요. 그리고 손 내밀 줄 모르고 말없이 괴로워하는 또 다른 부류가 있어요. 저도 그게 어떤 건지 좀 알아요. 옛이야기에서 임금님이 거지 소녀를 공주로 만들어 주듯 당신이 나를 공주로 만들어 주기 전까지, 저도 그 부류에 속했으니까요. 야망 있는 여자들은 힘든 시절을 보내요, 로리. 그리고 딱 알맞은 순간에 약간의 도움이 없어서 청춘과 건강과 소중한 기회를 그냥 흘려보내는 일이 많죠. 사람들은 제게 무척 친절했어요. 그러니 예전의 우리처럼 힘겹게 헤쳐 나가는 여자아이들을 볼 때마다, 제가 도움받았던 것처럼 손을 내밀어 그들을 돕고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"그래, 당신은 그렇게 할 거야. 천사 같은 당신이니까!\" 로리가 외쳤다. 그러고는 박애심에 불타올라, 예술적 재능을 지닌 젊은 여성들을 위한 기관을 세우고 기금을 대기로 마음먹었다. \"부유한 사람들은 가만히 앉아 자기들만 즐기거나, 남들이 탕진하라고 돈을 쌓아 둘 권리가 없어. 죽을 때 유산을 남기는 건, 살아 있는 동안 그 돈을 현명하게 쓰고 그것으로 이웃을 행복하게 만들며 즐거워하는 것에 비하면 절반도 분별 있는 일이 못 돼. 우린 우리대로 즐겁게 지내면서, 남들에게도 후하게 맛보게 해 줌으로써 우리 즐거움에 특별한 풍미를 더할 거야. 당신은 작은 도르가*가 되어 줄래? 위안거리를 잔뜩 담은 큰 바구니를 비우고 다니면서 그 자리에 선행을 채워 넣는?\"\n* 도르가: 가난한 이들을 위해 옷을 지어 베푼 성경 속 자비로운 여인 : 옮긴이.\n\n\"기꺼이요. 당신이 용감한 성 마르티노*가 되어 준다면요. 세상을 늠름하게 말 달려 지나가다 멈춰 서서, 거지에게 당신 망토를 나눠 주는 그런 사람이요.\"\n\n* 성 마르티노: 추위에 떠는 거지에게 자기 망토를 잘라 나눠 준 일화로 유명한 성인 : 옮긴이.\n\n\"그럼 약속한 거다. 그리고 그 약속에서 가장 큰 덕을 보는 건 우리겠지!\"\n그렇게 젊은 부부는 그 약속의 표시로 손을 맞잡고, 다시 행복하게 거닐기 시작했다. 다른 가정들을 환하게 밝혀 주기를 바라기에 자기들의 정겨운 집이 한층 더 집답게 느껴졌고, 다른 발들이 디딜 거친 길을 평탄히 골라 준다면 눈앞에 펼쳐진 꽃길을 따라 자기들의 발걸음도 한결 더 바르게 나아가리라 믿었으며, 자기들보다 덜 축복받은 이들을 다정히 기억할 줄 아는 사랑으로 두 사람의 마음이 한층 더 단단히 맺어진 것을 느끼면서.\n\n# DAISY AND DEMI.\n\n마치 집안의 보잘것없는 기록자로서 그 집안의 가장 소중하고 중요한 두 식구에게 적어도 한 장(章)을 바치지 않고서는, 내 도리를 다했다고 도무지 느낄 수가 없다. 데이지와 데미는 이제 분별이 생길 나이에 이르렀다. 이 빠른 시대에는 서너 살짜리 아기들도 제 권리를 주장하고, 또 그걸 얻어 내기까지 하니, 어른들 가운데 많은 이들도 못 하는 일이다. 만약 떠받들림에 흠뻑 젖어 완전히 버릇이 망가질 위험에 처한 쌍둥이가 세상에 있었다면, 바로 이 재잘대는 브룩 집안 쌍둥이였을 것이다. 물론 둘은 여태껏 태어난 아이들 중 가장 비범했으니, 여덟 달에 걸음마를 했고, 열두 달에 청산유수로 말을 했으며, 두 살에는 식탁에 제자리를 차지하고 앉아 보는 이들을 모두 매료시킬 만큼 의젓하게 굴었다는 사실만 말해도 알 만하다. 세 살에 데이지는 \"바늘\"을 달라고 졸라 정말로 네 땀을 떠서 주머니를 만들었고, 찬장에 살림을 차려 놓고 손톱만 한 요리용 화덕을 어찌나 솜씨 좋게 다루던지 해나의 눈에 자랑스러운 눈물이 맺힐 정도였다. 한편 데미는 할아버지에게서 글자를 배웠는데, 할아버지는 팔다리로 글자 모양을 만들어 알파벳을 가르치는 새로운 방법을 고안해 냈으니, 머리와 발의 체조를 한데 결합한 셈이었다. 사내아이는 일찌감치 기계에 대한 재능을 드러내어 아버지를 기쁘게 하고 어머니를 골치 아프게 했다. 보이는 기계마다 흉내 내려 들어 육아실을 온통 난장판으로 만들었던 것이다. 그의 \"재봉틀\"은 끈과 의자와 빨래집게와 실패로 이루어진 알 수 없는 구조물로, 바퀴가 \"빙글빙글\" 돌게 만든 것이었다. 또 큰 의자 등받이에 바구니를 매달아 놓고는 너무 잘 믿는 누이를 거기 끌어올리려 헛되이 애썼는데, 누이는 여성스러운 헌신으로 작은 머리가 쿵쿵 부딪치도록 가만히 있다가 구조되곤 했다. 그러면 어린 발명가는 분개하여 이렇게 말하는 것이었다. \"엄마, 이건 내 승강기란 말이야. 누나를 위로 끌어올리려는 거라고.\"\n\n성격은 영판 딴판이었지만 쌍둥이는 놀랄 만큼 사이좋게 지냈고, 하루에 세 번 넘게 다투는 일은 좀처럼 없었다. 물론 데미는 데이지에게 군림하면서도 다른 어떤 침입자로부터든 늠름하게 누이를 지켜 주었고, 데이지는 갤리선 노예가 되어 오빠를 세상에서 둘도 없는 완벽한 존재로 떠받들었다. 데이지는 발그레하고 통통하며 햇살처럼 밝은 작은 영혼이라, 누구의 마음에든 파고들어 그 안에 둥지를 틀었다. 입맞춤을 받고 품에 안기고, 어린 여신처럼 꾸며지고 떠받들리며, 잔칫날마다 사람들의 칭찬을 받으려고 태어난 듯한, 그런 사랑스러운 아이들 가운데 하나였다. 자그마한 미덕들이 어찌나 사랑스럽던지, 자그마한 말썽이 몇 가지 끼어 사랑스럽게 인간다움을 지켜 주지 않았다면 거의 천사 같았을 것이다. 그 아이의 세상은 늘 맑은 날씨여서, 매일 아침 작은 잠옷 차림으로 창가로 기어올라 밖을 내다보며, 비가 오든 해가 나든 상관없이 이렇게 말했다. \"오, 예쁜 날, 오, 예쁜 날!\" 누구나 다 친구였고, 낯선 사람에게도 어찌나 미덥게 입맞춤을 건네던지, 아무리 골수 독신주의자라도 마음이 누그러졌고, 아기를 좋아하는 사람들은 충실한 숭배자가 되었다.\n\n\"나는 모든 사람을 사랑해.\" 언젠가 데이지는 한 손에 숟가락을, 다른 손에 머그잔을 든 채 두 팔을 활짝 벌리며 그렇게 말했다. 마치 온 세상을 끌어안고 먹여 주고 싶어 안달이 난 듯이.\n\n데이지가 자라면서, 어머니는 비둘기집*이 그 옛집을 집답게 만들어 주었던 그 존재만큼이나 평온하고 사랑 가득한 식구의 손길로 축복받게 되리라는 느낌이 들기 시작했다. 그리고 얼마나 오랫동안 자기들도 모르게 천사를 품어 왔는지를 최근에야 가르쳐 준 그런 상실을 데이지만은 면하게 해 달라고 기도하게 되었다. 할아버지는 데이지를 자주 \"베스\"라고 불렀고, 할머니는 자기 눈에만 보이는 어떤 지난 잘못을 속죄하려는 듯이 지칠 줄 모르는 정성으로 그 아이를 지켜보았다.\n\n* 비둘기집: 메그와 존이 사는 작은 집의 별칭 : 옮긴이.\n데미는 진짜 양키답게 캐묻기를 좋아하는 성미라 무엇이든 알고 싶어 했고, 끊임없는 \"왜요?\"에 시원한 답을 얻지 못해 자주 몹시 안달했다.\n\n또한 그는 철학적인 기질도 지니고 있어서 할아버지를 크게 기쁘게 했다. 할아버지는 데미와 소크라테스식 대화를 나누곤 했는데, 그 조숙한 제자가 이따금 스승을 쩔쩔매게 만들어 집안 여인들을 대놓고 흡족하게 했다.\n\n\"할아버지, 내 다리는 뭐 때문에 움직여요?\" 어린 철학자가 어느 날 밤 자기 전 한바탕 뛰놀고 나서 쉬던 중에, 그 부지런히 움직이는 신체 부위를 명상하듯 살피며 물었다.\n\n\"그건 네 작은 마음이란다, 데미.\" 현자가 그 노란 머리를 정중히 쓰다듬으며 대답했다.\n\n\"작은 마음이 뭔데요?\"\n\n\"네 몸을 움직이게 하는 무언가지. 전에 내가 보여 준 회중시계에서 태엽이 바퀴를 돌게 하는 것처럼 말이다.\"\n\n\"날 열어 봐요. 그게 빙글빙글 도는 걸 보고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"그건 네가 시계를 열 수 없는 것만큼이나 나도 못 한단다. 하느님이 너한테 태엽을 감아 주시면, 너는 그분이 멈추실 때까지 움직이는 거야.\"\n\n\"내가요?\" 데미의 갈색 눈이 새로운 생각을 받아들이며 크고 반짝반짝 빛났다. \"내가 시계처럼 태엽이 감겼어요?\"\n\n\"그렇단다. 하지만 어떻게 그러는지는 보여 줄 수가 없어. 우리가 보지 않을 때 이루어지는 일이거든.\"\n\n데미는 시계처럼 되어 있는지 알아보려는 듯 제 등을 만져 보더니, 진지하게 말했다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 하느님이 내가 자고 있을 때 하시는 것 같아요.\"\n\n이어서 정성스러운 설명이 뒤따랐고, 데미가 어찌나 골똘히 듣던지 걱정스러워진 할머니가 말했다.\n\n\"여보, 저 아기한테 그런 얘기를 하는 게 현명한 일일까요? 눈 위로 혹이 점점 커지고, 도무지 답할 수 없는 질문들만 배우고 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"질문을 할 만큼 나이를 먹었으면 참된 답을 받을 만큼 나이를 먹은 거요. 난 저 아이 머릿속에 생각을 집어넣는 게 아니라, 이미 거기 있는 생각을 펼치도록 돕는 거요. 이 아이들은 우리보다 더 지혜로워서, 저 녀석이 내가 한 말을 한마디도 빠짐없이 알아듣는다는 걸 난 의심치 않소. 자, 데미, 네 마음을 어디에 두고 있는지 말해 보겠니?\"\n\n만약 그 아이가 알키비아데스처럼 \"신들께 맹세코, 소크라테스여, 저는 모르겠습니다\"라고 대답했더라도 할아버지는 놀라지 않았을 것이다. 하지만 데미가 명상에 잠긴 어린 황새처럼 한쪽 다리로 잠시 서 있다가 차분한 확신의 어조로 \"내 작은 배 속에요\"라고 대답하자, 노신사는 그저 할머니의 웃음에 함께 끼어들며 형이상학 수업을 마치는 수밖에 없었다.\n\n만약 데미가 새싹 같은 철학자일 뿐 아니라 진짜 사내아이라는 사실을 미덥게 증명해 보이지 않았더라면, 어머니로서 걱정할 만도 했을 것이다. 해나가 불길하게 고개를 끄덕이며 \"저 애는 이 세상 사람 같지가 않아\"라고 예언하게 만드는 토론을 벌이고 나면, 데미는 곧잘 돌변하여, 사랑스럽고 꾀죄죄하고 말썽꾸러기인 어린 개구쟁이들이 부모의 영혼을 애태우고 또 즐겁게 하는 그런 장난들로 그녀의 두려움을 잠재워 놓곤 했기 때문이다.\n\n메그는 도덕 규칙을 많이 정해 놓고 그것들을 지키려 애썼다. 하지만 그토록 일찍부터 노련한 솜씨꾼 사기꾼*의 면모를 드러내는 이 꼬마 남녀의 사람 홀리는 꾀와 기발한 둘러대기, 혹은 천연덕스러운 대담함 앞에서 끄떡없었던 어머니가 세상에 어디 있었던가?\n\n* 솜씨꾼 사기꾼: 찰스 디킨스 소설 「올리버 트위스트」에 나오는 소매치기 소년의 별명 : 옮긴이.\n\"건포도는 그만, 데미. 그러다 배탈 난다.\" 자두 푸딩 만드는 날이면 어김없이 부엌에 와서 거들겠다고 나서는 어린 양반에게 엄마가 말한다.\n\n\"나는 배탈 나는 거 좋아.\"\n\n\"엄마는 싫어. 그러니 저리 가서 데이지가 패티케이크 만드는 거나 도와주렴.\"\n\n데미는 마지못해 물러나지만, 자기가 당한 부당함이 마음을 짓누른다. 그러다 이윽고 그것을 바로잡을 기회가 오자, 그는 약삭빠른 흥정으로 엄마를 한 수 위에서 따돌린다.\n\n\"자, 너희 착하게 굴었으니, 너희가 원하는 건 뭐든 놀아 줄게.\" 푸딩이 냄비 속에서 무사히 통통 끓고 있을 때, 메그가 조수 요리사들을 위층으로 데리고 가며 말한다.\n\n\"정말이지, 엄마?\" 데미가 하얗게 분칠된 머릿속에 멋진 생각 하나를 떠올리며 묻는다.\n\n\"그럼, 정말이지. 네가 말하는 건 뭐든.\" 근시안적인 엄마가, 바람이 불든 다리가 빠지든 아랑곳없이 \"세 마리 아기 고양이\"를 대여섯 번이나 불러 주거나, \"1페니짜리 빵을 사러\" 식구들을 데려갈 채비를 하며 대답한다. 그러나 데미는 시치미를 뚝 떼고 이렇게 대답하여 엄마를 궁지로 몰아넣는다.\n\n\"그럼 우리 가서 건포도 다 먹어 버리자.\"\n\n도도 이모는 두 아이의 으뜸가는 놀이 동무이자 비밀 털어놓는 상대였고, 셋이서 작은 집을 발칵 뒤집어 놓았다. 에이미 이모는 아직 이름뿐인 존재였고, 베스 이모는 이내 어렴풋하고 정겨운 기억으로 희미해졌지만, 도도 이모는 살아 있는 실재였다. 두 아이는 도도 이모를 한껏 활용했고, 그 점에 대해 도도는 깊이 고마워했다. 그런데 베어 씨가 오자 조는 놀이 동무들을 등한시했고, 두 어린 영혼에는 절망과 황량함이 내려앉았다. 입맞춤을 팔러 다니기 좋아하던 데이지는 가장 큰 단골을 잃고 파산했다. 데미는 어린아이다운 통찰력으로, 도도가 자기보다 \"곰 아저씨\"와 노는 걸 더 좋아한다는 사실을 곧 알아차렸다. 하지만 마음이 아프면서도 그 괴로움을 감추었으니, 조끼 주머니에 초콜릿 사탕 광맥을 품고 다니고, 케이스에서 꺼내 열렬한 숭배자들이 마음껏 흔들어 볼 수 있는 회중시계를 가진 연적을 차마 모욕할 마음이 들지 않았던 것이다.\n\n어떤 사람들은 이런 기분 좋은 호의를 뇌물로 여겼을지도 모른다. 하지만 데미는 그렇게 보지 않았고, 우수에 잠긴 듯 너그러운 태도로 \"곰 아저씨\"를 계속 후원했다. 한편 데이지는 세 번째 부름 만에 그 작은 애정을 그에게 바쳤고, 그의 어깨를 자기 옥좌로, 그의 팔을 자기 피난처로, 그의 선물을 더없이 값진 보물로 여겼다. 신사들은 더러 자기가 마음을 둔 여인의 어린 친척들에게 별안간 발작적인 애정을 쏟기도 한다. 하지만 그런 거짓 자식 사랑은 그들에게 어색하게 들러붙어 있을 뿐, 조금도 누구를 속이지 못한다. 그러나 베어 씨의 애정은 진심이었고, 더욱이 효과적이었다. 사랑에서도 법에서와 마찬가지로 정직이 최선의 방책이니까. 그는 아이들과 편안하게 어울리는 부류의 사람이었고, 어린 얼굴들이 그의 사내다운 얼굴과 보기 좋은 대비를 이룰 때면 유난히 멋져 보였다. 그의 볼일은, 그게 무엇이든, 날이면 날마다 그를 붙들어 두었지만, 저녁이면 그는 어김없이 길을 나서 와서—음, 그는 늘 마치 씨를 찾았으니, 짐작건대 그분이 그를 끌어당기는 사람이었나 보다. 인자한 아빠는 자기가 그 사람이라는 착각에 빠져, 마음이 통하는 벗과 긴 토론을 나누며 즐거워했다. 그러던 어느 날, 좀 더 눈치 빠른 손자의 무심한 한마디가 별안간 그의 눈을 뜨게 해 주기 전까지는.\n\n어느 날 저녁 베어 씨가 들어서다가 서재 문턱에서 멈춰 서서는, 눈앞에 펼쳐진 광경에 깜짝 놀랐다. 마치 씨가 그 점잖은 다리를 허공에 치켜든 채 바닥에 엎드려 있었고, 그 옆에는 역시나 엎드린 데미가 자기의 짧고 새빨간 양말을 신은 다리로 그 자세를 흉내 내려 애쓰고 있었다. 두 엎드린 자는 어찌나 진지하게 몰두해 있던지 구경꾼이 있는 줄도 몰랐는데, 베어 씨가 그 우렁찬 웃음을 터뜨리고 조가 어처구니없다는 얼굴로 소리치고 나서야 알아차렸다.\n\n\"아버지, 아버지, 교수님 오셨어요!\"\n\n검은 다리가 내려가고 희끗한 머리가 올라오는 동안, 스승은 흐트러짐 없는 위엄으로 말했다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 베어 선생. 잠시만 실례하겠소. 지금 막 수업을 끝내는 참이라. 자, 데미, 글자를 만들고 그 이름을 말해 보렴.\"\n\n\"나 이거 알아요!\" 그렇게 외치고는 몇 차례 안간힘을 쓴 끝에 빨간 다리가 컴퍼스 한 쌍 모양이 되더니, 영리한 제자가 의기양양하게 소리쳤다. \"이건 더블유예요, 할아버지, 더블유라고요!\"\n\n\"타고난 웰러로구나.\" 아버지가 몸을 추스르고 조카가 학교가 끝난 기쁨을 표현할 유일한 방법이라는 듯 물구나무서기를 시도하자, 조가 웃으며 말했다.\n* 웰러: 「픽윅 클럽 여행기」의 등장인물 새뮤얼 웰러를 빗댄 말장난. 그는 자기 이름을 'V'로 발음했다 : 옮긴이.\n\n\"오늘은 무얼 했니, 꼬마야?\" 베어 씨가 그 작은 체조 선수를 안아 올리며 물었다.\n\n\"꼬마 메리 보러 갔어요.\"\n\n\"가서는 무얼 했고?\"\n\n\"그 애한테 뽀뽀했어요.\" 데미가 천진하고 솔직하게 입을 떼었다.\n\n\"저런! 일찌감치 시작하는구나. 그 꼬마 메리는 거기에 뭐라고 하더냐?\" 베어 씨가 물으며, 자기 무릎에 서서 조끼 주머니를 탐색하던 어린 죄인의 고백을 계속 들었다.\n\n\"아, 메리도 좋아했어요. 메리도 나한테 뽀뽀했고, 나도 좋았어요. 사내아이들은 여자아이들 좋아하잖아요?\" 데미가 입을 가득 채운 채, 흐뭇하고 부드러운 표정으로 덧붙였다.\n\n\"이 조숙한 병아리 같으니! 누가 그런 생각을 머릿속에 넣어 줬니?\" 조가 교수만큼이나 그 천진한 고백을 즐기며 물었다.\n\n\"그건 머릿속에 없어요. 내 입속에 있어요.\" 곧이곧대로 받아들이는 데미가, 그녀가 생각이 아니라 사탕을 말하는 줄 알고 초콜릿 사탕을 올려놓은 혀를 쑥 내밀며 대답했다.\n\n\"꼬마 친구한테 좀 남겨 줘야지. 달콤한 건 달콤한 이에게, 꼬마 양반.\" 그렇게 말하며 베어 씨가 조에게도 좀 권했는데, 그 눈빛에 조는 초콜릿이야말로 신들이 마시던 신주(神酒)가 아닐까 싶을 정도였다. 데미도 그 미소를 보았고, 거기에 마음이 움직여 천연덕스럽게 물었다.\n\n\"교수님, 큰 남자아이들도 큰 여자아이들 좋아해요?\"\n\n# UNDER THE UMBRELLA.\n\n어린 워싱턴처럼 베어 씨는 \"거짓말을 할 줄 몰랐다.\" 그래서 가끔은 그러는 것 같다고 다소 모호하게 대답했는데, 그 어조 때문에 마치 씨는 옷솔을 내려놓고, 물러나는 조의 얼굴을 흘끗 보더니 의자에 푹 주저앉았다. 마치 \"조숙한 병아리\"가 달콤하면서도 시큼한 어떤 생각을 자기 머릿속에 집어넣기라도 한 듯한 표정으로.\n\n반 시간쯤 뒤에 도도가 도자기 찬장에서 데미를 붙잡았을 때, 거기 있었다고 흔들어 대는 대신 다정한 포옹으로 그 작은 몸의 숨이 거의 막힐 만큼 꼭 끌어안은 까닭은 무엇이었을까. 또 그 색다른 행동에 이어 큼직한 잼 바른 빵 한 조각을 뜻밖에 선물한 까닭은 무엇이었을까. 그건 데미가 그 작은 머리를 굴리며 풀어 보려 애쓰다가, 끝내 영영 풀지 못한 채 남겨 둘 수밖에 없었던 수수께끼 가운데 하나로 남았다.\n\n로리와 에이미가 보드라운 양탄자 위를 부부답게 거닐며 집을 정돈하고 더없이 행복한 미래를 그려 가는 동안, 베어 씨와 조는 진흙길과 질척한 들판을 따라 그와는 사뭇 다른 산책을 즐기고 있었다.\n\n\"난 늘 저녁 무렵에 산책을 하는데, 마침 교수님이 나오시는 길에 자주 마주친다고 해서 그걸 그만둘 이유는 없잖아.\" 두세 차례 마주친 뒤 조가 속으로 중얼거렸다. 메그네로 가는 길이 두 갈래 있었지만, 어느 쪽으로 가든 그녀는 오가는 길에 영락없이 그와 마주쳤던 것이다. 그는 늘 빠르게 걷다가, 아주 가까이 와서야 그녀를 알아보는 것 같았다. 그제야 근시인 눈이 다가오는 숙녀를 그 순간까지 못 알아본 듯한 표정을 짓는 것이었다. 그러고 나서 그녀가 메그네로 가는 길이면 그는 늘 아기들 줄 것을 가지고 있었고, 그녀가 집 쪽으로 가는 길이면 그는 그저 강 구경을 하러 내려왔다가 막 돌아가려던 참이라고 했다. 자기가 자주 들르는 게 식구들이 지겹지 않다면 말이다.\n\n이런 상황에서 조가 그에게 정중히 인사하고 안으로 들이는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가? 설령 그의 방문이 지겨웠다 해도 조는 그 싫증을 완벽한 솜씨로 감추었고, 저녁 식사에는 커피가 빠지지 않도록 신경 썼다. \"프리드리히가—아니, 베어 선생님이 차를 안 좋아하시니까.\"\n\n둘째 주가 되자 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 다들 훤히 알게 되었지만, 그래도 다들 조의 얼굴에 일어나는 변화에 까막눈인 척하려 애썼다. 조가 왜 일하면서 노래를 흥얼거리는지, 왜 하루에 세 번이나 머리를 매만지는지, 저녁 운동을 하고 와서 왜 그토록 발그레하게 피어나는지, 아무도 묻지 않았다. 그리고 베어 교수가 아버지와 철학을 논하는 사이에 딸에게는 사랑을 가르치고 있다는 걸 조금이라도 눈치챈 사람은 아무도 없는 듯했다.\n\n조는 점잖게 마음을 빼앗기지조차 못하고, 그 감정을 엄하게 억누르려 애쓰다가, 그러지 못한 끝에 다소 마음이 들뜬 나날을 보냈다. 그토록 여러 차례 열렬히 독립을 선언해 놓고 항복했다고 비웃음당할까 봐 죽도록 겁이 났던 것이다. 그중에서도 로리가 가장 두려웠다. 하지만 새 감독* 덕분에 로리는 칭찬받아 마땅할 만큼 점잖게 처신했다. 사람들 앞에서 베어 씨를 \"멋진 노인장\"이라고 부르지 않았고, 조의 달라진 외모를 조금도 입에 올리지 않았으며, 거의 매일 저녁 마치 집안 현관 탁자에 놓인 교수의 모자를 보고도 털끝만큼의 놀라움도 내비치지 않았다. 다만 속으로는 의기양양해하며, 곰과 울퉁불퉁한 지팡이를 어울리는 문장(紋章)으로 새긴 은쟁반을 조에게 선물할 날이 오기를 고대했다.\n\n* 새 감독: 로리의 아내가 된 에이미를 가리킴 : 옮긴이.\n\n두 주 동안 교수는 연인다운 규칙성으로 오갔다. 그러다 사흘을 내리 발길을 끊고 아무런 기별도 없었다. 그 일로 다들 침울한 표정이 되었고, 조는 처음엔 수심에 잠기더니, 그다음엔—아아, 가엾은 낭만이여!—몹시 심술궂어졌다.\n\n\"질려서 왔을 때처럼 갑자기 집으로 가 버린 거겠지. 물론 나야 아무 상관도 없지만, 그래도 신사답게 와서 작별 인사쯤은 하고 갈 줄 알았는데.\" 어느 음울한 오후, 조가 으레 하는 산책에 나서려고 옷을 차려입으며, 대문을 절망스럽게 바라보며 속으로 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"작은 우산을 가져가는 게 좋겠다, 얘야. 비가 올 것 같구나.\" 어머니가 말했다. 조가 새 보닛을 쓴 것을 알아챘지만 그 점은 입에 올리지 않은 채.\n\n\"네, 엄마. 시내에서 뭐 필요한 거 있어요? 종이를 좀 사러 들어가야 하거든요.\" 조가 대답하며, 어머니를 보지 않으려는 핑계로 거울 앞에서 턱 밑의 리본을 잡아당겼다.\n\n\"그래. 능직 무명천이랑, 9호 바늘 한 봉지랑, 가느다란 라벤더색 리본 2야드만 사다 주렴. 두꺼운 부츠는 신었고, 망토 안에 따뜻한 거 입었니?\"\n\n\"그런 것 같아요.\" 조가 건성으로 대답했다.\n\n\"혹시 베어 선생님을 만나거든, 차 마시러 집으로 모시고 오렴. 그 다정한 양반이 정말 보고 싶구나.\" 마치 부인이 덧붙였다.\n\n조는 그 말을 들었지만 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고, 그저 어머니에게 입을 맞추고는 빠르게 걸어 나갔다. 가슴이 아픈 와중에도 고마움에 가슴이 뜨거워지며 이렇게 생각하면서.\n\n\"엄마는 나한테 어쩌면 이렇게 잘해 주실까! 힘든 일을 겪을 때 곁에서 도와줄 엄마가 없는 여자아이들은 대체 어떻게 살아갈까?\"\n\n포목점들은 신사들이 가장 많이 모여드는 회계 사무소며 은행이며 도매 창고들 사이에 있지 않았다. 그런데도 조는 심부름 한 가지 보기도 전에 시내의 바로 그 구역에 와 있었다. 누군가를 기다리기라도 하듯 어슬렁대면서, 한쪽 진열창에서는 측량 기구를, 다른 진열창에서는 양모 견본을 여자답지 않은 관심으로 들여다보았다. 통에 걸려 비틀거리고, 내려오는 짐짝에 깔려 숨이 막힐 뻔하고, \"도대체 어쩌다 여기 온 거야\" 하고 의아해하는 듯한 바쁜 남자들에게 거리낌 없이 떠밀리면서. 뺨에 떨어진 빗방울 한 줄기가 그녀의 생각을 어그러진 희망에서 망가진 리본으로 되돌려 놓았다. 빗방울이 계속 떨어졌고, 그녀는 사랑에 빠진 여자이기도 했으므로, 마음을 구하기엔 너무 늦었어도 보닛만은 구할 수 있을지 모른다고 느꼈던 것이다. 그제야 조는 작은 우산을 떠올렸다. 서둘러 나오느라 챙기는 걸 깜빡한 것이었다. 하지만 후회해 봐야 소용없었고, 우산을 빌리거나 흠뻑 젖는 걸 감수하는 수밖에 도리가 없었다. 그녀는 잔뜩 찌푸린 하늘을 올려다보고, 이미 검은 얼룩이 점점이 진 진홍색 리본을 내려다보고, 진창인 거리를 앞으로 내다보더니, 마지막으로 어떤 거뭇한 창고를 한참 동안 미련스레 뒤돌아보았다. 문 위에 \"호프만, 스워츠 상회\"라고 적힌 그 창고를. 그러고는 자기를 엄하게 나무라는 투로 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"꼴좋다! 가장 좋은 옷을 다 차려입고 교수님을 볼까 하는 마음에 여기까지 어슬렁대며 내려온 게 잘한 짓이람? 조, 너 정말 부끄러운 줄 알아! 안 돼, 우산을 빌리거나 그분 친구들한테서 그분이 어디 있는지 알아내려고 저기 들어가면 절대 안 돼. 빗속을 터덜터덜 걸어가서 심부름이나 하는 거야. 그러다 죽을병에 걸리고 보닛을 망친대도, 그건 네가 당해도 싼 일이야. 자, 그럼!\"\n\n그렇게 말하고는 어찌나 거세게 길을 가로질러 내달렸던지, 지나가던 짐마차에 치여 박살 날 뻔한 걸 가까스로 피하면서 어느 위엄 있는 노신사의 품으로 곤두박질치고 말았다. 노신사는 \"실례하오, 부인\" 하고는 몹시 기분이 상한 표정을 지었다. 다소 기가 죽은 조는 몸을 바로 세우고, 그 가엾은 리본 위에 손수건을 펼쳐 덮고는, 유혹을 뒤로한 채 서둘러 걸음을 옮겼다. 발목께가 점점 더 축축해지고 머리 위로는 우산들이 요란하게 부딪히는 가운데. 보호받지 못하는 보닛 위로 어딘가 낡아빠진 파란 우산 하나가 가만히 머물러 있다는 사실이 그녀의 주의를 끌었다. 올려다보니 베어 씨가 내려다보고 있었다.\n\n\"이렇게 숱한 말 코앞을 용감히 헤치고, 이렇게 많은 진창을 빠르게 지나가는 심지 굳은 숙녀를 알아보겠소. 여기서 무얼 하시오, 나의 친구?\"\n\n\"쇼핑하는 중이에요.\"\n\n베어 씨는 한쪽의 피클 공장에서 다른 쪽의 가죽 도매상으로 눈길을 옮기며 미소 지었지만, 그저 정중하게 말할 뿐이었다.\n\n\"우산이 없구려. 저도 같이 가서 짐을 좀 들어드려도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"네, 고마워요.\"\n\n조의 뺨은 리본만큼이나 빨개졌고, 그가 자기를 어떻게 생각할까 궁금했다. 하지만 상관없었다. 다음 순간 그녀는 교수와 팔짱을 끼고 걷고 있었으니까. 별안간 해가 유난히 눈부시게 터져 나와 세상이 다시 온전해진 듯했고, 더없이 행복한 여자 하나가 그날 빗속을 첨벙첨벙 걸어가고 있는 듯한 기분이 들었다.\n\n\"우린 선생님이 가 버리신 줄 알았어요.\" 조가 황급히 말했다. 그가 자기를 보고 있다는 걸 알았기 때문이다. 보닛이 얼굴을 가릴 만큼 크지 않아서, 거기 드러난 기쁨을 그가 처녀답지 못하다고 여길까 봐 두려웠다.\n\n\"그토록 천국같이 친절히 대해 준 분들께 작별 인사도 없이 떠나리라 여기셨소?\" 그가 어찌나 나무라는 투로 묻던지, 조는 그런 말로 그를 모욕한 듯한 기분이 들어 진심으로 대답했다.\n\n\"아니요, 그렇게 생각 안 했어요. 선생님이 선생님 일로 바쁘신 줄 알았어요. 그래도 우린 선생님이 좀 그리웠어요. 특히 아버지랑 어머니가요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그대는?\"\n\n\"전 늘 선생님을 뵈면 반가워요.\"\n\n목소리를 아주 침착하게 유지하려고 애쓰다 보니, 조의 말투는 오히려 다소 쌀쌀맞아졌고, 끝에 붙은 그 차가운 짧은 한마디가 교수의 마음을 얼어붙게 한 듯했다. 그의 미소가 사라졌고, 그는 진지하게 말했다.\n\n\"고맙소. 떠나기 전에 한 번 더 들르겠소.\"\n\n\"그럼 정말 가시는 거예요?\"\n\n\"여기엔 이제 볼일이 없소. 다 끝났으니.\"\n\n\"잘되셨기를 바라요.\" 조가 말했다. 그의 그 짧은 대답에 실망의 씁쓸함이 배어 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"그래야겠지요. 빵벌이를 하며 내 어린 학생들에게 큰 도움을 줄 수 있는 길이 열렸으니. 이 일을 감사히 여겨야겠지요, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"그럼요, 그러셔야죠. 선생님이 좋아하시는 일을 하시게 되다니 얼마나 멋진 일이에요. 그리고 선생님과 그 아이들을 자주 볼 수 있을 테고요!\" 조가 외쳤다. 도무지 감출 수 없는 기쁨을 그 아이들을 핑계 삼아 둘러대면서.\n\n\"아! 하지만 우린 자주 못 만날 것 같소. 그곳이 서부라서.\"\n\n\"그렇게 멀리요!\" 조는 이제 옷이든 자기 몸이든 어찌 되든 상관없다는 듯, 치맛자락을 운명에 내맡겨 버렸다.\n\n베어 씨는 몇 개 국어를 읽을 줄 알았지만, 아직 여자를 읽는 법은 익히지 못했다. 그는 자기가 조를 꽤 잘 안다고 자부했고, 그래서 그날 조가 잇따라 보여 준 목소리와 표정과 태도의 모순에 무척이나 어리둥절했다. 반 시간 사이에 조의 기분이 대여섯 번이나 바뀌었던 것이다. 그를 만났을 때 조는 놀란 표정이었지만, 바로 그 목적으로 거기 왔으리라는 의심을 떨치기란 불가능했다. 그가 팔을 내밀자 조는 그를 기쁨으로 가득 채우는 눈빛으로 그 팔을 잡았다. 하지만 자기가 그리웠느냐고 묻자 어찌나 쌀쌀맞고 격식 차린 대답을 하던지 그는 절망에 빠졌다. 그의 행운을 듣고는 손뼉이라도 칠 듯했지만—그 기쁨이 다 아이들 때문이었을까? 그러다 그의 행선지를 듣고는 절망스러운 어조로 \"그렇게 멀리요!\"라고 말해 그를 희망의 꼭대기로 들어 올렸다. 하지만 다음 순간 마치 그 일에만 온통 몰두한 사람처럼 이렇게 말해 그를 다시 굴러떨어뜨렸다.\n\n\"여기가 제 심부름할 가게예요. 들어가실래요? 오래 안 걸릴 거예요.\"\n\n조는 자기 쇼핑 솜씨를 제법 자랑스러워했고, 특히 깔끔하고 신속하게 일을 해치우는 모습으로 동행에게 깊은 인상을 주고 싶었다. 하지만 마음이 들떠 있던 탓에 모든 게 어긋났다. 바늘 쟁반을 엎질렀고, 무명천을 잘라 낸 다음에야 \"능직\"으로 해야 했다는 걸 깜빡 떠올렸고, 거스름돈을 잘못 내주었으며, 옥양목 판매대에서 라벤더색 리본을 달라고 해서 스스로를 당황스럽게 만들었다. 베어 씨는 곁에 서서 그녀가 얼굴을 붉히고 실수하는 모습을 지켜보았다. 그렇게 지켜보는 사이, 그의 당혹감도 가라앉는 듯했다. 여자란 어떤 경우에는 꿈과 마찬가지로 거꾸로 풀이된다는 걸 알아차리기 시작했던 것이다.\n\n가게를 나서며 그는 한결 밝아진 표정으로 꾸러미를 옆구리에 끼었고, 대체로 보아 오히려 즐거워하는 듯 웅덩이를 첨벙첨벙 밟으며 걸었다.\n\n\"이른바 쇼핑이라는 걸 아기들을 위해 좀 하고, 내가 그토록 즐거운 그대의 집에 마지막으로 들르게 된다면, 오늘 밤 작별 잔치를 벌이는 게 어떻겠소?\" 그가 과일과 꽃이 가득한 진열창 앞에 멈춰 서며 물었다.\n\n\"뭘 살까요?\" 조가 그의 말 뒷부분은 못 들은 척하며, 가게 안으로 들어서면서 짐짓 즐거운 듯이 뒤섞인 향기를 들이마시며 물었다.\n\n\"오렌지랑 무화과 먹어도 되겠소?\" 베어 씨가 아버지다운 태도로 물었다.\n\n\"생기면 잘 먹어요.\"\n\n\"견과류는 좋아하오?\"\n\n\"다람쥐처럼요.\"\n\n\"함부르크 포도는, 그렇지, 이걸로 꼭 조국을 위해 건배해야겠소?\"\n\n조는 그 사치스러움에 눈살을 찌푸리며, 차라리 대추야자 한 바구니에 건포도 한 통, 아몬드 한 자루를 사서 끝내 버리지 그러냐고 물었다. 그러자 베어 씨는 그녀의 지갑을 압수하고 자기 것을 꺼내더니, 포도 몇 파운드와 발그레한 데이지 화분 하나, 그리고 큰 술병처럼 여길 만한 예쁜 꿀단지 하나를 사며 장보기를 끝냈다. 그러고는 울퉁불퉁한 꾸러미들로 주머니를 잔뜩 불룩하게 만들고, 그녀에게는 꽃을 들게 한 다음, 낡은 우산을 펼쳐 들었고, 둘은 다시 길을 걸었다.\n\n\"마치 양, 한 가지 큰 부탁이 있소.\" 반 블록쯤 축축한 산책을 한 끝에 교수가 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"네, 말씀하세요.\" 조의 심장이 어찌나 세차게 뛰던지, 그가 그 소리를 들을까 봐 겁이 났다.\n\n\"비가 오는데도 감히 말하는 건, 내게 남은 시간이 너무 짧아서요.\"\n\n\"네, 말씀하세요.\" 조는 별안간 손에 힘이 들어가 그 작은 화분을 으스러뜨릴 뻔했다.\n\n\"내 티나에게 줄 작은 옷을 한 벌 사고 싶은데, 나 혼자 가기엔 너무 어리석어서 말이오. 안목 있는 한마디와 도움을 좀 베풀어 주시겠소?\"\n\n# HARVEST TIME.\n\n\"네, 그러죠.\" 조는 별안간 냉장고 속으로 발을 들여놓기라도 한 듯 차분하고 싸늘해진 기분이 들었다.\n\n\"어쩌면 티나 어머니에게 줄 숄도 하나. 그 부인이 워낙 가난하고 병약한 데다 남편 때문에 얼마나 고생을 하는지. 그래요, 그래, 두툼하고 따뜻한 숄이라면 그 가엾은 어머니에게 가져다주기에 정다운 선물이 되겠지.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 도와드릴게요, 베어 선생님. 난 아주 빠르게 가고 있는데, 그분은 매 순간 점점 더 사랑스러워지고 있어.\" 조가 속으로 덧붙였다. 그러고는 마음을 한 번 다잡고서, 보기 좋을 만큼의 활기를 띠고 그 일에 뛰어들었다.\n\n베어 씨가 모든 걸 그녀에게 맡겼으므로, 조는 티나에게 줄 예쁜 옷을 고르고 나서 숄을 가져오라고 했다. 결혼한 몸인 점원은 가족을 위해 쇼핑하는 듯 보이는 이 두 사람에게 짐짓 관심을 보이며 거들었다.\n\n\"부인께서는 이걸 더 마음에 들어 하실 겁니다. 고급품인 데다 더없이 좋은 색이고, 아주 정숙하고 품위 있지요.\" 그는 그렇게 말하며 포근한 회색 숄을 펼쳐 조의 어깨에 둘러 주었다.\n\n\"이게 마음에 드세요, 베어 선생님?\" 조가 그에게 등을 돌리며 물었다. 얼굴을 감출 기회가 생긴 것에 깊이 감사하면서.\n\n\"아주 마음에 드오. 이걸로 하지요.\" 교수가 대답하며 속으로 미소 지은 채 값을 치렀고, 조는 영락없는 알뜰 흥정꾼처럼 계속 판매대를 뒤졌다.\n\n\"이제 집에 갈까요?\" 그가 그 말이 무척이나 기쁘다는 듯 물었다.\n\n\"네, 늦었고, 나 정말 너무 지쳤어요.\" 조의 목소리는 자기가 아는 것보다 더 애처로웠다. 이제 해가 떠올랐을 때만큼이나 갑작스레 다시 숨어 버린 듯했고, 세상은 다시 진창에다 비참해졌으며, 그녀는 처음으로 자기 발이 시리고 머리가 아프며, 가슴이 발보다 더 차갑고 머리보다 더 고통으로 가득하다는 걸 깨달았다. 베어 씨는 떠나간다. 그는 그저 친구로서 자기를 아낄 뿐이다. 다 착각이었으니, 빨리 끝날수록 좋다. 그런 생각에 사로잡혀, 조는 다가오는 합승마차를 어찌나 황급한 손짓으로 불러 세웠던지 화분에서 데이지꽃들이 튀어나와 잔뜩 망가지고 말았다.\n\n\"이건 우리 마차가 아니오.\" 교수가 짐을 가득 실은 그 마차를 손짓으로 보내고는, 그 가엾은 작은 꽃들을 주우려고 멈춰 서며 말했다.\n\n\"죄송해요. 이름을 똑똑히 못 봤어요. 괜찮아요, 걸어가면 돼요. 진창을 터덜터덜 걷는 데는 이골이 났거든요.\" 조가 대답하며 눈을 질끈질끈 깜빡였다. 차라리 죽을지언정 대놓고 눈물을 닦지는 않으려 했던 것이다.\n\n바에르 선생은 조가 고개를 돌렸는데도 그녀의 뺨에 맺힌 물방울을 보았다. 그 모습이 그의 마음을 몹시 흔들었는지, 그는 갑자기 몸을 숙이며 깊은 뜻이 담긴 목소리로 물었다.\n\"내 마음속 가장 소중한 사람, 왜 우는 거요?\"\n\n이런 일에 익숙한 사람이었다면 조는 우는 게 아니라고, 감기에 걸렸다고, 아니면 이런 상황에 어울리는 여자다운 거짓말이라도 했을 것이다. 그런데 이 체면머리 없는 사람은 참을 수 없는 흐느낌과 함께 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n\"당신이 떠나니까요.\"\n\n\"아, 마인 고트, 그것 참 다행이오!\" 바에르 선생이 외쳤다. 우산과 보따리를 든 채로도 어떻게든 두 손을 마주 잡으려 애쓰면서. \"조, 내가 줄 수 있는 건 가득한 사랑뿐이오. 당신이 그것을 받아 줄 수 있을지 보려고 왔소. 그리고 내가 친구 이상의 무언가가 맞는지 확인하려고 기다렸소. 내가 그렇소? 이 늙은 프리츠를 위해 당신 마음속에 자그마한 자리 하나 마련해 줄 수 있겠소?\" 그는 단숨에 덧붙였다.\n\n\"오, 그럼요!\" 조가 말했다. 그는 더없이 만족스러웠다. 조가 두 손을 그의 팔에 포개고는, 그가 우산을 들어 주기만 한다면 이 낡은 우산밖에 비를 피할 데가 없다 해도 그의 곁에서 평생을 걸어가는 일이 얼마나 행복할지 환히 드러내는 표정으로 그를 올려다보았기 때문이다.\n\n그것은 분명 여러 가지로 곤란한 처지에서 하는 청혼이었다. 마음이야 굴뚝같았겠지만 바에르 선생은 진창 때문에 무릎을 꿇을 수도 없었다. 두 손이 다 차 있으니 비유적으로가 아니라면 조에게 손을 내밀 수도 없었다. 더구나 곁에 한길이 있는데 길거리에서 다정한 표현을 마음껏 할 수도 없는 노릇이었다. 그래서 그가 환희를 표현할 수 있는 유일한 방법은 그녀를 바라보는 것뿐이었는데, 그 표정이 그의 얼굴을 어찌나 환하게 빛나게 했던지 수염에 반짝이는 물방울 속에 정말로 작은 무지개가 떠 있는 것만 같았다. 만약 그가 조를 무척 사랑하지 않았더라면 그때 그렇게 할 수는 없었으리라. 치맛자락은 한심한 꼴이었고, 고무장화는 발목까지 흙탕물이 튀었으며, 보닛은 엉망이 되어, 조의 모습은 사랑스러움과는 거리가 멀었으니까. 다행히도 바에르 선생은 그녀를 세상에서 가장 아름다운 여인이라 여겼고, 모자챙이 흘러내리는 물줄기에 축 늘어져 그의 어깨로 빗물이 떨어지고(우산을 온통 조에게 씌워 주고 있었으니) 장갑 손가락마다 죄다 기워야 할 형편인데도, 조는 그가 어느 때보다 더 \"주피터 같다\"고 느꼈다.\n\n지나가는 사람들은 아마 두 사람을 별 해 없는 미치광이 한 쌍으로 여겼을 것이다. 둘은 마차를 불러 세우는 것조차 까맣게 잊은 채, 짙어 가는 땅거미와 안개도 아랑곳없이 한가로이 거닐었으니까. 누가 어떻게 생각하든 조금도 개의치 않았다. 두 사람은 평생에 좀처럼 한 번밖에 오지 않는 행복한 시간을, 늙은이에게 젊음을, 평범한 이에게 아름다움을, 가난한 이에게 부를 안겨 주고, 인간의 마음에 천국을 미리 맛보게 해 주는 그 마법 같은 순간을 누리고 있었다. 교수는 마치 왕국 하나를 정복한 듯, 세상이 더는 그에게 베풀 행복이 없다는 듯한 표정이었다. 한편 조는 그의 곁을 터벅터벅 걸으며, 자기 자리가 늘 거기였던 것만 같은 기분에 잠겨, 어떻게 다른 인생을 택할 수도 있었을까 의아해했다. 물론 먼저 입을 연 건 조였다. 알아들을 만한 말로 입을 연 건 말이다. 그녀의 격정적인 \"오, 그럼요!\"에 뒤이은 감정 어린 말들은 조리 있지도, 옮겨 적을 만하지도 않았으니까.\n\n\"프리드리히, 왜 당신은……\"\n\n\"아, 맙소사, 미나가 죽은 뒤로 아무도 부르지 않던 이름을 이 사람이 불러 주는구려!\" 교수가 외치며 물웅덩이 한가운데 멈춰 서서 고마움 어린 기쁨으로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"속으로는 늘 그렇게 불렀어요…… 깜박했네요. 하지만 당신이 싫다면 안 부를게요.\"\n\n\"싫다니? 말로 다 못 할 만큼 달콤한걸. '그대'라고도 불러 주오. 그러면 당신 나라 말이 우리 말만큼이나 아름답다고 말하겠소.\"\n\n\"'그대'는 좀 감상적이지 않아요?\" 조가 물었다. 속으로는 사랑스러운 한 음절이라고 생각하면서.\n\n\"감상적이라고? 그렇소. 고트께 감사하게도, 우리 독일 사람들은 감상을 믿고, 그걸로 젊음을 지킨다오. 당신네 영어의 '유'는 너무 차갑소. '그대'라고 해 주오, 내 마음속 가장 소중한 사람, 나에게는 정말 큰 의미라오.\" 바에르 선생이 근엄한 교수라기보다 낭만적인 학생처럼 졸랐다.\n\n\"그럼, 왜 그대는 이 모든 걸 진작 말하지 않았어요?\" 조가 수줍게 물었다.\n\n\"이제 그대에게 내 마음을 모두 보여 줘야겠구려. 기꺼이 그러겠소. 앞으로 그대가 그 마음을 돌봐 주어야 하니까. 자, 그럼 나의 조여 — 아, 사랑스럽고 우스운 그 작은 이름! — 뉴욕에서 작별 인사를 하던 날, 나는 무언가 말하고 싶었소. 하지만 그 잘생긴 친구가 그대와 약혼한 줄로 알았기에 입을 다물었소. 만약 그때 내가 말했더라면, 그대는 '네'라고 했겠소?\"\n\n\"모르겠어요. 그러지 않았을 것 같아요. 그때는 마음이라는 게 없었거든요.\"\n\n\"풋! 그건 못 믿겠소. 그건 그저 잠들어 있었을 뿐, 요정 왕자가 숲을 지나 와서 깨워 줄 때까지 말이오. 아, 뭐, '첫사랑이 가장 좋은 사랑'이라지만, 그것까진 바라지 않겠소.\"\n\n\"맞아요, 첫사랑이 가장 좋은 사랑이에요. 그러니 만족하세요. 나에겐 다른 사랑이 없었으니까요. 테디는 그저 소년이었고, 잠깐의 변덕은 금세 떨쳐 버렸어요.\" 교수의 오해를 바로잡고 싶은 마음에 조가 말했다.\n\n\"잘됐소! 그럼 나는 마음 놓고 행복할 수 있겠구려. 그대가 모든 걸 나에게 준다고 확신하면서 말이오. 너무 오래 기다린 탓에 나는 이기적인 사람이 됐다오, 당신도 곧 알게 되겠지만, 나의 교수 부인.\"\n\n\"그거 마음에 들어요.\" 조가 새 호칭에 기뻐하며 외쳤다. \"이제 말해 봐요. 무엇 때문에 내가 가장 당신을 필요로 한 바로 그때 마침내 찾아온 거예요?\"\n\n\"이것 때문이오.\" 바에르 선생이 조끼 주머니에서 낡고 작은 종잇조각을 꺼냈다.\n\n조는 그것을 펼쳐 보고는 몹시 무안해했다. 그것은 시에 원고료를 주는 어느 신문에 자기가 보낸 글 가운데 하나였기 때문이다. 그래서 그녀가 가끔 한 편씩 시도해 보낸 것이었다.\n\n\"이게 어떻게 당신을 오게 했다는 거예요?\" 무슨 말인지 몰라 조가 물었다.\n\n\"우연히 발견했소. 이름과 머리글자를 보고 알아봤지. 그 안에 나를 부르는 듯한 작은 시 한 구절이 있었소. 읽어 보고 그를 찾아보오. 당신이 빗물에 젖지 않게 내가 우산을 봐 주리다.\"\n\n조는 시키는 대로, 자기가 이렇게 제목 붙인 시구를 서둘러 훑어보았다.\n\"다락방에서.\n\n작은 궤짝 넷이 나란히 한 줄로, 먼지에 흐려지고 세월에 닳았네, 오래전에 모두 짜이고 채워졌지, 이제는 한창때를 맞은 아이들 손에.\n\n작은 열쇠 넷이 나란히 걸려 있네, 빛바랜 리본 달고, 씩씩하고 화사했지 어린아이의 자랑으로 거기 매어 두던 그때, 오래전, 어느 비 오던 날에.\n\n작은 이름 넷, 뚜껑마다 하나씩, 소년다운 손길로 새겨 두었고, 그 아래엔 숨어 있네, 행복한 무리의 이야기들이.\n\n# THE LITTLE WOMEN SERIES.\n\n한때 여기서 놀다가, 곧잘 멈추어 달콤한 후렴에 귀 기울이던, 저 높은 지붕 위로 오갔던 그 가락, 여름 비 내리던 그 소리에.\n\n'메그' 첫 번째 뚜껑에, 매끈하고 고운. 나는 사랑 어린 눈으로 들여다보네, 여기 낯익은 정성으로 고이 접혀, 어여쁜 것들이 한가득 모여 있으니, 평온한 한 생애의 기록이라네 — 상냥한 아이와 소녀에게 준 선물들, 웨딩드레스, 아내에게 바친 시구, 조그마한 신발 하나, 아기 머리카락 한 올.\n\n이 첫 궤짝엔 장난감이 남지 않았네, 모두 어딘가로 옮겨 갔으니, 세월이 흘러, 또 다른 작은 메그의 놀이에 다시 함께하려고.\n\n아, 행복한 어머니! 잘 알고 있네 그대가 달콤한 후렴처럼 듣고 있음을, 한없이 부드럽고 나직한 자장가를, 여름 비 내리는 그 소리 속에서.\n\n# THE SPINNING-WHEEL SERIES\n\n'조' 다음 뚜껑에, 긁히고 닳은, 그 안엔 잡동사니가 가득하네, 머리 없는 인형들, 찢어진 교과서, 이제는 말 없는 새들과 짐승들, 젊은 발길만이 밟던 요정 나라에서 가져온 전리품들, 끝내 찾지 못한 미래의 꿈들, 여전히 달콤한 지난날의 추억들, 쓰다 만 시들, 거친 이야기들, 사월의 편지들, 따뜻하고 차가운, 고집 센 아이의 일기장, 일찍 늙어 버린 여인의 자취들, 외로운 집 안의 한 여인, 구슬픈 후렴처럼 듣고 있네 — '사랑받을 만하면, 사랑은 찾아오리라.' 여름 비 내리는 그 소리 속에서.\n\n# AUNT JO'S SCRAP-BAG\n\n나의 베스! 네 이름이 적힌 뚜껑에는 먼지가 늘 말끔히 닦여 있네, 눈물 흘리던 사랑의 눈길이, 자주 찾던 정성스러운 손길이 그러했듯. 죽음이 우리에게 성인 하나를 거룩히 했으니, 인간이라기보다 늘 신성에 가까웠던, 그래도 우리는 다정한 탄식과 함께 이 가정의 성소에 유물을 놓아두네 — 좀처럼 울리지 않던 은방울, 그 애가 마지막으로 쓰던 작은 모자, 천사들이 받쳐 들어 문 위에 걸어 두었던 곱고 죽어 버린 캐서린, 고통의 감옥 안에서도 한탄 없이 부르던 그 애의 노래들, 영원토록 그것들은 달콤하게 어우러지네, 여름 비 내리는 그 소리와 함께.\n\n# NOVELS, ETC.\n\n마지막 뚜껑의 윤기 흐르는 면 위에 — 이제는 곱고도 참된 전설이라 — 한 늠름한 기사가 방패에 새겨 지녔네, '에이미'라고, 금빛과 푸른빛 글자로. 그 안엔 그녀의 머리를 묶던 댕기, 마지막 춤을 끝낸 무도화, 정성스레 놓아둔 시든 꽃들, 가벼운 수고를 다한 부채들, 화사한 밸런타인 카드들, 온통 뜨거운 불꽃들, 소녀다운 희망과 두려움과 부끄러움에서 한몫을 했던 자질구레한 것들 — 처녀의 마음이 남긴 기록이라네, 이제는 더 곱고 참된 주문을 배우며, 경쾌한 후렴처럼 듣고 있네, 혼례의 은빛 종소리를, 여름 비 내리는 그 소리 속에서.\n\n작은 궤짝 넷이 나란히 한 줄로, 먼지에 흐려지고 세월에 닳았네, 네 여인이 기쁨과 슬픔에 배워 한창때에 사랑하고 수고하였네. 네 자매, 한때 잠시 헤어졌으나, 하나도 잃지 않고, 한 사람만 먼저 떠났을 뿐, 사랑의 불멸한 힘으로 맺어져 영원토록 가장 가깝고 가장 소중하리. 오, 우리의 이 숨겨 둔 곳간이 아버지의 눈앞에 활짝 펼쳐질 때, 황금 같은 시간들로, 빛 속에서 더 고와지는 행실들로 풍성하기를, 씩씩한 가락이 오래도록 울려 퍼질, 영혼을 일깨우는 선율 같은 삶들이기를, 비 갠 뒤 길게 비치는 햇살 속에서 기꺼이 솟아올라 노래할 영혼들이기를.\n\n제이 엠.\"\n\n\"형편없는 시예요. 하지만 쓸 때는 정말 그렇게 느꼈어요. 무척 외로웠던 어느 날, 헝겊 자루에 기대 실컷 울고 나서요. 이게 비밀을 까발릴 수 있는 데까지 가게 될 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐어요.\" 조가 교수가 그토록 오래 간직해 온 시구를 북북 찢으며 말했다.\n\n\"그냥 보내 주오. 제 할 일은 다 했으니. 그리고 그녀가 작은 비밀들을 적어 둔 갈색 책을 다 읽고 나면 새것을 하나 얻으면 되지.\" 바에르 선생이 미소 지으며 말했다. 조각들이 바람에 날아가는 것을 지켜보면서. \"그렇소.\" 그가 진지하게 덧붙였다. \"나는 그것을 읽고 속으로 생각했소. '이 사람은 슬픔을 안고 있구나, 외롭구나, 참된 사랑에서 위안을 얻을 수 있겠구나. 내겐 그녀를 향한 마음이 가득, 가득 차 있으니, 가서 이렇게 말하면 어떨까. 내가 받기를 바라는 것에 비해 이것이 너무 보잘것없는 게 아니라면, 신의 이름으로 받아 주오, 라고.'\"\n\n\"그래서 와 보니 그게 보잘것없기는커녕 내게 꼭 필요한 단 하나의 소중한 것이었군요.\" 조가 속삭였다.\n\n\"처음엔 그렇게 생각할 용기가 없었소. 당신이 천사처럼 다정하게 나를 맞아 주었는데도 말이오. 하지만 곧 희망을 품기 시작했고, 그러고는 말했소. '죽는 한이 있어도 이 사람을 얻으리라.' 그리고 그렇게 할 것이오!\" 바에르 선생이 단호히 고개를 끄덕이며 외쳤다. 마치 그들을 에워싸며 닫혀 오는 안개의 벽이 자기가 넘거나 용감히 무너뜨려야 할 장벽이라는 듯이.\n\n조는 그 모습이 멋지다고 생각했고, 화려한 차림으로 군마를 타고 달려오지는 않았어도, 자기 기사에게 어울리는 사람이 되겠다고 다짐했다.\n\n\"왜 그렇게 오래 안 왔어요?\" 조가 곧 물었다. 은밀한 질문을 던지고 기쁜 대답을 듣는 일이 어찌나 즐거운지 도무지 잠자코 있을 수가 없었다.\n\n\"쉽지 않았소. 하지만 당신에게 줄 보금자리를 마련할 가망이 생기기 전까지는, 그 행복한 집에서 당신을 데려올 엄두가 나지 않았소. 아마 오랜 시간과 고된 일을 거쳐야 했겠지. 가진 거라곤 약간의 학식뿐인 가난하고 늙은 사내를 위해, 어떻게 당신더러 그토록 많은 걸 포기하라고 할 수 있었겠소?\"\n\n# COMIC TRAGEDIES\n\n\"당신이 가난한 게 오히려 기뻐요. 부유한 남편은 견디지 못했을 거예요.\" 조가 단호하게 말하더니, 한결 부드러운 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"가난을 두려워하지 마세요. 나는 가난을 충분히 겪어서 더는 무섭지 않고, 사랑하는 사람들을 위해 일하는 게 행복하답니다. 그리고 자신을 늙었다고 하지 마세요. 마흔은 인생의 한창때예요. 당신이 일흔이라 해도 나는 사랑하지 않을 수 없었을 거예요!\"\n\n# LOUISA MAY ALCOTT\n\n교수는 그 말이 어찌나 가슴에 사무쳤던지 손수건을 꺼낼 수만 있었다면 좋아했을 텐데, 꺼낼 수가 없으니 조가 대신 그의 눈물을 닦아 주었다. 그러고는 보따리 한두 개를 받아 들며 웃으면서 말했다.\n\n# SPINNING-WHEEL STORIES\n\n\"내가 주관이 세긴 해도, 이젠 누구도 내가 분수를 벗어났다곤 못할걸요. 여자의 특별한 사명이란 눈물을 닦아 주고 짐을 나눠 지는 거라고들 하니까요. 내 몫은 내가 지겠어요, 프리드리히, 그리고 보금자리 마련하는 걸 도울 거예요. 그렇게 마음먹지 않으면 나는 안 갈래요.\" 그가 짐을 도로 가져가려 하자 조가 단호하게 덧붙였다.\n\n# AUNT JO'S SCRAP BAG\n\n\"두고 봅시다. 오래 기다릴 인내심이 있겠소, 조? 나는 떠나서 혼자 내 일을 해야 하오. 내 아이들부터 도와야 하니까. 당신을 위해서라도 미나와의 약속을 저버릴 순 없기 때문이오. 그것을 용서하고, 우리가 희망을 품고 기다리는 동안 행복해 줄 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"네, 그럴 수 있어요. 우리는 서로 사랑하니까요. 그러면 나머지는 다 견디기 쉬워져요. 나에게도 내 의무가 있고, 내 일이 있어요. 당신을 위해서라도 그것들을 소홀히 하면 나는 즐거울 수 없을 거예요. 그러니 서두르거나 조급해할 필요가 없어요. 당신은 서부에서 당신 몫을 하고, 나는 여기서 내 몫을 하고, 둘 다 가장 좋은 결과를 바라며 행복하게, 미래는 하느님 뜻에 맡기면 돼요.\"\n\n\"아! 당신은 나에게 이토록 큰 희망과 용기를 주는데, 나는 가득한 마음과 이 빈손밖에 돌려줄 게 없구려.\" 교수가 완전히 감격해 외쳤다.\n\n# LULU'S LIBRARY\n\n조는 끝끝내 얌전해지는 법을 배우지 못할 사람이었다. 그가 계단에 서서 그렇게 말하자, 조는 두 손을 그의 손에 포개 넣으며 다정하게 속삭였다. \"이젠 빈손이 아니에요.\" 그러고는 몸을 숙여 우산 아래에서 자기 프리드리히에게 입을 맞췄다. 끔찍한 짓이었지만, 울타리에 늘어앉은 꼬질꼬질한 참새 떼가 사람이었다 해도 조는 그렇게 했을 것이다. 정말이지 깊이 빠져 버려, 자기 행복 말고는 아무것도 아랑곳하지 않았으니까. 비록 그토록 소박한 모습으로 찾아왔지만, 그 순간은 두 사람 인생의 절정이었다. 밤과 폭풍과 외로움에서 돌아서서, 그들을 맞으려 기다리는 가정의 불빛과 온기와 평화를 향해, \"잘 왔어요!\" 하는 반가운 인사와 함께 조가 연인을 안으로 이끌고 문을 닫던 그 순간이.\n\n추수기\n\n# MISS ALCOTT'S NOVELS\n\n조와 그녀의 교수는 일 년 동안 일하며 기다렸고, 희망을 품으며 사랑했고, 이따금 만났으며, 어찌나 방대한 편지를 주고받았던지 종잇값이 오른 게 다 그 탓이라고 로리가 말할 정도였다. 둘째 해는 다소 우울하게 시작됐다. 형편은 좀처럼 나아지지 않았고, 마치 대고모님이 갑자기 세상을 떠났기 때문이다. 하지만 첫 슬픔이 가시고 나자 — 입은 매서웠어도 다들 그 노부인을 사랑했으니까 — 그들에게는 기뻐할 까닭이 생겼다. 대고모님이 플럼필드를 조에게 남겼고, 그것이 온갖 즐거운 일을 가능케 했던 것이다.\n\n\"근사한 옛집이라 꽤 괜찮은 값을 받을 거야. 물론 팔 생각이겠지.\" 몇 주 뒤 다들 그 일을 의논하던 자리에서 로리가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 안 팔아.\" 조의 단호한 대답이었다. 옛 주인을 생각해 거둬들인 살찐 푸들을 쓰다듬으면서.\n\n\"거기서 살겠다는 건 아니지?\"\n\n\"맞아, 살 거야.\"\n\n\"하지만 조, 그 집은 어마어마하게 커서 관리하는 데만도 큰돈이 들어. 정원과 과수원만 해도 사람 두세 명은 있어야 하고, 농사는 바에르 선생 분야가 아닐 텐데.\"\n\n\"내가 권하면 그이도 거기서 한번 해 볼 거야.\"\n\n\"그래서 그 땅에서 나는 걸로 먹고살겠다고? 글쎄, 듣기엔 무릉도원 같지만, 막상 해 보면 죽도록 힘든 일이라는 걸 알게 될걸.\"\n\n\"우리가 거둘 작물은 수지맞는 거야.\" 조가 웃었다.\n\n\"그 근사한 작물이라는 게 대체 뭡니까, 부인?\"\n\n\"사내아이들이지. 어린 사내아이들을 위한 학교를 열고 싶어. 좋고, 행복하고, 가정 같은 학교를. 내가 그 애들을 돌보고, 프리츠가 가르치는 거야.\"\n\n\"이거야말로 진짜 조다운 계획이네! 딱 조답지 않아?\" 로리가 식구들에게 호소하듯 외쳤지만, 식구들도 그만큼이나 놀란 표정이었다.\n\n\"난 마음에 드는구나.\" 마치 부인이 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"나도 그렇다.\" 남편이 덧붙였다. 그는 현대의 젊은이들에게 소크라테스식 교육법을 시험해 볼 기회가 생긴다는 생각을 반겼다.\n\n\"조한테는 엄청난 짐이 될 거예요.\" 메그가 자기의 온 마음을 사로잡은 외아들의 머리를 쓰다듬으며 말했다.\n\n\"조라면 해낼 수 있고, 그 안에서 행복할 거야. 멋진 생각이야. 어디 자세히 말해 보렴.\" 로런스 씨가 외쳤다. 그는 두 연인에게 손을 보태 주고 싶어 안달이었지만, 그들이 자기 도움을 마다하리라는 걸 알고 있었다.\n\n# THE LITTLE MEN PLAY. THE LITTLE WOMEN PLAY.\n\n\"어르신은 제 편이 되어 주실 줄 알았어요. 에이미도 그렇고요 — 눈을 보면 알아요. 신중해서 입을 떼기 전에 곰곰이 생각하느라 기다리고 있지만요. 자, 사랑하는 여러분.\" 조가 진지하게 말을 이었다. \"이게 갑자기 떠오른 생각이 아니라 오래 품어 온 계획이라는 걸 알아주세요. 프리츠가 오기 전부터 저는 늘 생각했어요. 돈을 모으고 집에서 더는 나를 필요로 하지 않게 되면, 큰 집을 빌려서 어머니도 없는 가엾고 쓸쓸한 사내아이들을 거둬들여 돌보고, 너무 늦기 전에 그 애들의 삶을 즐겁게 해 주겠다고요. 제때 도움을 받지 못해 망가져 가는 아이들이 정말 많아요. 저는 그 애들을 위해 뭐든 해 주는 게 너무 좋고, 그 애들이 무엇을 원하는지 느껴지고, 그 애들의 고민에 공감이 가요. 아, 저는 정말이지 그 애들의 어머니가 되고 싶어요!\"\n\n마치 부인이 조에게 손을 내밀자, 조는 눈물 어린 눈으로 미소 지으며 그 손을 잡았다. 그러고는 오래도록 보지 못했던 옛날의 그 열정적인 투로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"언젠가 프리츠에게 제 계획을 얘기했더니, 그이도 꼭 그러고 싶었다면서 우리가 부자가 되면 한번 해 보자고 했어요. 그이의 다정한 마음에 축복이 있기를. 그이는 평생 그 일을 해 왔어요 — 가난한 아이들을 돕는 일 말이에요, 부자가 되는 게 아니라요. 부자는 절대 못 될 거예요. 돈이 주머니에 모일 만큼 오래 머무는 법이 없으니까요. 하지만 이제, 제가 받을 자격도 없을 만큼 저를 아껴 주신 좋은 대고모님 덕분에 저는 부자예요. 적어도 그런 기분이에요. 학교만 잘되면 우리는 플럼필드에서 아주 잘 살 수 있어요. 사내아이들에겐 더없이 맞춤한 곳이에요. 집은 크고, 가구는 튼튼하고 수수해요. 안에는 수십 명이 들어갈 자리가 넉넉하고, 바깥에는 멋진 마당이 있어요. 그 애들이 정원과 과수원 일을 거들 수 있어요. 그런 일이 건강에 좋잖아요, 어르신? 그러면 프리츠는 자기 방식대로 아이들을 길들이고 가르치고, 아버지가 그이를 도와주실 거예요. 저는 그 애들을 먹이고 보살피고 어르고 야단치고, 어머니가 제 든든한 버팀목이 되어 주실 거예요. 저는 늘 사내아이들이 많았으면 했는데, 한 번도 충분한 적이 없었어요. 이제 집을 가득 채우고, 그 작은 녀석들과 마음껏 즐길 수 있어요. 얼마나 호사스러운지 생각해 보세요 — 플럼필드가 제 것이고, 그 안을 함께 누빌 사내아이들이 우글우글하다니!\"\n\n조가 두 손을 흔들며 황홀한 한숨을 내쉬자, 식구들은 까르르 웃음보를 터뜨렸고, 로런스 씨는 졸도라도 할 줄 알 만큼 웃어 댔다.\n\n\"뭐가 우습다는 건지 모르겠네요.\" 다시 말소리가 들릴 만해지자 조가 진지하게 말했다. \"제 교수님이 학교를 여는 것, 그리고 제가 제 소유의 땅에서 사는 걸 더 좋아하는 것보다 더 자연스럽고 마땅한 일이 어디 있어요.\"\n\n\"벌써 거드름을 피우는데.\" 이 생각을 그저 기막힌 농담으로 여기는 로리가 말했다. \"그런데 그 학교를 어떻게 꾸려 갈 생각인지 여쭤봐도 될까? 학생이 죄다 꼬마 부랑아라면, 세속적인 의미에서는 작물이 수지가 안 맞을까 걱정인데요, 바에르 부인.\"\n\n\"찬물 끼얹지 마, 테디. 물론 부유한 학생도 받을 거야 — 어쩌면 그런 애들로만 시작할지도 모르고. 그러다 자리가 잡히면 별미 삼아 부랑아 한둘쯤 받는 거지. 부잣집 아이들도 가난한 아이들 못지않게 보살핌과 위안이 필요할 때가 많아. 하인들 손에 내맡겨진 불쌍한 어린것들이나, 정말 잔인하게도 뒤처진다고 억지로 떠밀리는 아이들을 봤거든. 어떤 애들은 잘못된 양육이나 방치 탓에 못된 아이가 되고, 어떤 애들은 어머니를 잃지. 게다가 제일 착한 애들도 어설픈 사춘기를 거쳐야 하는데, 바로 그때야말로 가장 큰 인내와 다정함이 필요한 시기야. 사람들은 그 애들을 비웃고, 떠밀고, 눈에 안 띄게 두려 하면서, 예쁜 아이가 어느 날 갑자기 의젓한 청년으로 변하기를 바라지. 그 애들은 별로 불평도 안 해 — 야무진 어린 영혼들이거든 — 하지만 다 느낀단 말이야. 나도 비슷한 걸 겪어 봐서 잘 알아. 나는 그런 어린 곰 같은 녀석들에게 각별한 관심이 가고, 서툰 팔다리와 뒤죽박죽인 머릿속에도 불구하고 그 따뜻하고 정직하고 선량한 소년의 마음이 내 눈엔 보인다는 걸 알려 주고 싶어. 게다가 나는 경험도 있어. 한 소년을 길러서 집안의 자랑이자 영예가 되게 했잖아?\"\n\n\"네가 그러려고 애쓴 건 내가 증언하지.\" 로리가 고마운 눈길로 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 나는 기대 이상으로 성공했어. 봐, 너는 이렇게 듬직하고 분별 있는 사업가가 됐고, 돈으로 숱한 좋은 일을 하면서, 달러 대신 가난한 이들의 축복을 쌓고 있잖아. 하지만 너는 단순한 사업가가 아니야. 너는 선하고 아름다운 것을 사랑하고, 그것을 스스로 즐기면서도 옛날처럼 늘 남들과 절반씩 나누지. 나는 네가 정말 자랑스러워, 테디. 너는 해마다 더 나아지고, 누구나 그걸 느껴, 네가 입 밖에 내지 못하게 하는데도 말이야. 그래, 내 아이들을 거두게 되면 나는 너를 가리키며 이렇게 말할 거야. '얘들아, 저게 너희가 본받을 본보기란다.'\"\n\n가엾은 로리는 시선을 둘 데를 몰랐다. 어엿한 어른이 됐는데도, 이렇게 쏟아진 칭찬에 모든 얼굴이 흐뭇하게 그를 향하자 옛날의 수줍음이 어느새 되살아났던 것이다.\n\n\"이봐, 조, 그건 좀 과한데.\" 그가 옛날 소년 시절 그대로의 투로 입을 열었다. \"너희는 내가 평생 다 갚을 수 없을 만큼 많은 걸 해 줬어. 너희를 실망시키지 않으려고 최선을 다하는 것 말고는 갚을 길이 없을 정도야. 요즘 들어 너는 나를 좀 멀리했지만, 그래도 나는 더없이 좋은 도움을 받았어. 그러니 내가 조금이라도 잘됐다면, 그건 이 두 사람 덕으로 돌리렴.\" 그러고는 한 손을 할아버지의 흰머리에, 다른 손을 에이미의 금빛 머리에 다정하게 얹었다. 세 사람은 좀처럼 멀리 떨어지는 법이 없었다.\n\n\"나는 정말이지 가족이야말로 이 세상에서 가장 아름다운 거라고 생각해!\" 그때 유난히 마음이 벅차오른 조가 불쑥 외쳤다. \"언젠가 나도 내 가정을 갖게 되면, 내가 알고 가장 사랑하는 이 세 가정처럼 행복하길 바라. 존이랑 우리 프리츠까지 여기 있다면, 정말이지 이 땅의 작은 천국일 텐데.\" 그녀가 한결 차분하게 덧붙였다. 그리고 그날 밤, 가족과 의논하고 희망하고 계획하던 더없이 행복한 저녁을 보낸 뒤 자기 방으로 올라간 조는, 마음이 행복으로 가득 차 도무지 가라앉질 않아, 늘 자기 침대 곁에 놓인 빈 침대 옆에 무릎 꿇고 베스를 다정하게 떠올리고 나서야 겨우 마음을 가라앉힐 수 있었다.\n\n그해는 모든 면에서 참으로 놀라운 한 해였다. 일들이 유난히 빠르고 기쁘게 일어나는 것만 같았으니까. 조는 정신을 차릴 새도 없이 어느새 결혼해 플럼필드에 자리를 잡았다. 그러자 예닐곱 명의 사내아이들이 버섯처럼 쑥쑥 솟아나 놀랍도록 무성하게 자랐다. 부잣집 아이도, 가난한 집 아이도 함께였다. 로런스 씨가 끊임없이 가슴 아픈 빈곤 사례를 찾아내, 바에르 부부에게 그 아이를 가엾이 여겨 거둬 달라며, 양육비로 약간이나마 기꺼이 내겠다고 부탁했기 때문이다. 이런 식으로 그 능청스러운 노신사는 자존심 센 조를 구슬려, 그녀가 가장 좋아하는 유형의 사내아이들을 대 주었다.\n\n물론 처음엔 고된 오르막길이었고, 조는 엉뚱한 실수도 저질렀다. 하지만 현명한 교수가 그녀를 잔잔한 물길로 무사히 이끌어 주었고, 아무리 막무가내인 부랑아도 끝내는 손에 길들여졌다. 조는 그 \"우글거리는 사내아이들\"을 얼마나 즐거워했던가. 그리고 가엾고 사랑스러운 마치 대고모님이 살아서, 단정하고 질서 정연하던 플럼필드의 신성한 영역이 톰이며 딕이며 해리 같은 녀석들로 우글거리는 걸 봤다면 얼마나 한탄했을까! 그래도 따지고 보면 일종의 인과응보 같은 데가 있었다. 그 노부인은 한때 몇 마일 안의 사내아이들에게 공포의 대상이었으니까. 이제 그 추방당했던 녀석들이 금지된 자두를 마음껏 따 먹고, 신성모독 같은 신발로 자갈을 야단도 안 맞고 걷어차며, 성마른 \"뿔 굽은 암소\"가 겁 없는 젊은이더러 들이받혀 보라며 부르곤 하던 그 너른 들판에서 크리켓을 했다. 그곳은 일종의 사내아이들의 낙원이 되었고, 로리는 그곳 주인에게 바치는 찬사이자 그곳 주민들에게도 어울리게, \"바에르 정원\"이라고 부르자고 제안했다.\n\n그곳은 결코 유행하는 학교가 아니었고, 교수는 재산을 모으지도 못했다. 하지만 그곳은 조가 의도한 바로 그것이었다 — \"가르침과 보살핌과 다정함이 필요한 사내아이들을 위한, 행복하고 가정 같은 곳.\" 그 큰 집의 방마다 이내 가득 찼고, 정원의 작은 텃밭마다 이내 임자가 생겼다. 애완동물을 기르게 허락했기에 헛간과 광에는 어엿한 동물원이 생겨났다. 그리고 하루 세 번, 조는 양옆으로 행복한 어린 얼굴들이 죽 늘어앉은 긴 식탁의 상석에서 자기 프리츠에게 미소를 보냈다. 그 얼굴들은 하나같이 다정한 눈길과 믿음 어린 말과 고마워하는 마음으로, \"바에르 어머니\"를 향한 사랑을 가득 담아 그녀를 돌아보았다.\n\n이제 조에게는 사내아이들이 넘쳐 났지만, 결코 싫증 나지 않았다. 그 애들이 천사는 어림도 없었고, 더러는 교수와 교수 부인에게 적잖은 골칫거리와 근심을 안겨 주었는데도 말이다. 하지만 아무리 못되고 건방지고 약 올리는 부랑아라도 그 마음 한구석에 있는 선한 자리를 향한 그녀의 믿음이 그녀에게 인내와 솜씨를, 그리고 마침내 성공을 안겨 주었다. 햇살처럼 자애롭게 비춰 주는 바에르 아버지와, 일흔 번씩 일곱 번이라도 용서해 주는 바에르 어머니 앞에서, 오래 버틸 수 있는 사내아이란 세상에 없었으니까. 조에게는 그 녀석들과의 우정이 무척이나 소중했다. 잘못을 저지른 뒤의 뉘우치는 훌쩍임과 속삭임, 우습거나 가슴 뭉클한 자그마한 속내, 흐뭇한 열정과 희망과 계획, 심지어 그 애들의 불운까지도. 그런 것들은 그저 그 애들을 더욱더 사랑스럽게 만들 뿐이었다. 굼뜬 아이도 있고 수줍은 아이도 있었다. 허약한 아이도 있고 시끌벅적한 아이도 있었다. 혀짤배기 아이도 있고 말 더듬는 아이도 있었다. 다리 저는 아이도 한둘 있었다. 그리고 다른 데서는 받아 주지 않았지만 \"바에르 정원\"에서는 환영받은, 명랑한 어린 혼혈아도 있었다. 몇몇 사람은 그 아이를 받으면 학교가 망할 거라고 점쳤지만 말이다.\n\n그렇다, 조는 그곳에서 무척 행복한 여인이었다. 고된 일과 숱한 근심, 끊이지 않는 소동에도 불구하고. 그녀는 그것을 진심으로 즐겼고, 사내아이들의 갈채가 세상의 그 어떤 칭찬보다 더 흡족하다는 걸 알았다. 이제 그녀는 오로지 자기를 열렬히 믿고 따르는 무리에게만 이야기를 들려주었으니까. 세월이 흐르면서 자기 자식인 어린 사내아이 둘이 생겨나 그녀의 행복을 더해 주었다 — 할아버지의 이름을 딴 롭, 그리고 천하태평한 아기 테디였는데, 어머니의 발랄한 기질뿐 아니라 아빠의 햇살 같은 성정까지 물려받은 듯했다. 사내아이들이 소용돌이치는 그 한복판에서 어떻게 두 아이가 무사히 자랐는지는 할머니와 이모들에게 수수께끼였지만, 두 아이는 봄날의 민들레처럼 무럭무럭 자랐고, 그 거친 보모들은 두 아이를 사랑하며 알뜰히 보살폈다.\n\n플럼필드에는 휴일이 무척 많았는데, 그중에서도 가장 즐거운 것 하나가 해마다 열리는 사과 따기였다. 그때면 마치네, 로런스네, 브룩네, 바에르네 식구가 총출동해 하루를 즐겼던 것이다. 조가 결혼한 지 오 년째 되던 해, 이 풍요로운 잔치 가운데 하나가 열렸다. 무르익은 시월의 어느 날로, 공기에는 기운을 북돋우는 상쾌함이 가득해 마음이 들뜨고 혈관 속 피가 건강하게 춤추는 것 같았다. 오래된 과수원은 잔칫날 차림새를 하고 있었다. 미역취와 과꽃이 이끼 낀 돌담을 수놓았고, 메뚜기가 시든 풀밭에서 활기차게 뛰어다녔으며, 귀뚜라미가 잔칫상의 요정 피리 부는 악사처럼 울어 댔다. 다람쥐들은 저들의 자그마한 추수에 분주했고, 새들은 골목길 오리나무 위에서 작별 인사를 지저귀었으며, 나무마다 처음 한 번 흔들기만 하면 빨갛고 노란 사과를 우수수 떨어뜨릴 채비를 하고 있었다. 모두가 거기 모였다. 모두가 웃고 노래하며, 기어 올라갔다가 굴러떨어졌다. 모두가 이렇게 완벽한 날도, 그날을 즐기는 이렇게 유쾌한 무리도 일찍이 없었다고 입을 모았다. 그리고 저마다, 마치 이 세상에 근심이나 슬픔 따위는 존재하지도 않는다는 듯, 그 시간의 소박한 즐거움에 거리낌 없이 자신을 내맡겼다.\n\n마치 씨는 평온하게 거닐며 로런스 씨에게 터서와 카울리와 콜루멜라를 인용해 주었다. 그러면서—\n\n\"순한 사과의 포도주 같은 즙\"\n\n을 음미했다.\n\n교수는 창 대신 장대를 들고 푸른 통로를 오르내리며, 마치 건장한 게르만 기사처럼 사내아이들을 이끌었다. 아이들은 자기들끼리 갈고리 사다리 소방대를 꾸려, 땅에서고 공중에서고 온갖 재주넘기로 놀라운 묘기를 펼쳤다. 로리는 어린 꼬마들에게 헌신했다. 어린 딸을 한 말들이 광주리에 태워 주고, 데이지를 새 둥지가 있는 데까지 들어 올려 주고, 모험심 많은 롭이 목이 부러지지 않게 지켰다. 마치 부인과 메그는 한 쌍의 포모나처럼 사과 더미 사이에 앉아, 끊임없이 쏟아져 들어오는 수확물을 골라냈다. 한편 에이미는 얼굴에 아름다운 어머니다운 표정을 띠고서, 여러 무리를 스케치하며, 곁에 작은 목발을 둔 채 그녀를 흠모하듯 바라보고 앉은 한 창백한 사내아이를 지켜보았다.\n\n그날 조는 물 만난 고기 같았다. 치맛자락을 걷어 핀으로 꽂고, 모자는 머리 아닌 아무 데나 걸쳐 둔 채, 아기를 옆구리에 낀 채로 이리저리 뛰어다니며, 어떤 신나는 모험이 벌어지든 뛰어들 채비를 하고 있었다. 어린 테디는 마치 마법에 걸린 듯 무탈했다. 도무지 무슨 일도 생기는 법이 없어서, 한 아이가 그 애를 번쩍 들어 나무 위로 올려놓든, 다른 아이가 등에 업고 달리든, 아기란 절인 양배추부터 단추, 못, 제 신발까지 뭐든 소화한다는 게르만식 미신에 빠진 너그러운 아빠가 시큼한 사과를 먹이든, 조는 조금도 걱정하지 않았다. 어린 테드가 때가 되면 다시 나타나리라는 걸, 무사하고 발그레하게, 꼬질꼬질하면서도 태평하게 돌아오리라는 걸 알았으니까. 그리고 그녀는 늘 진심 어린 환영으로 그 애를 다시 맞았다. 조는 자기 아기들을 더없이 다정하게 사랑했던 것이다.\n\n네 시가 되자 잠시 한가한 틈이 찾아왔고, 광주리는 빈 채로 놓여 있었다. 사과 따던 이들이 쉬면서 옷이 찢긴 데와 멍든 데를 견주어 보는 동안에. 그러고 나서 조와 메그는 큰 사내아이들 한 무리를 데리고 풀밭에 저녁상을 차렸다. 야외에서 마시는 차야말로 늘 그날의 절정의 기쁨이었으니까. 그런 날이면 땅에는 그야말로 젖과 꿀이 흘러넘쳤다. 사내아이들에게 식탁에 앉으라고 요구하지 않고, 좋을 대로 음식을 들게 허락했던 것이다. 자유야말로 소년의 영혼이 가장 사랑하는 양념이니까. 아이들은 이 드문 특권을 마음껏 누렸다. 누구는 물구나무를 선 채 우유 마시기라는 흥미로운 실험을 해 보았고, 누구는 등 짚고 넘기 놀이 중간중간 파이를 먹어 가며 그 놀이에 멋을 더했으며, 쿠키는 들판에 온통 흩뿌려졌고, 사과 턴오버는 새로운 종류의 새처럼 나무에 올라앉았다. 어린 여자아이들은 자기들끼리 따로 다과회를 열었고, 테드는 제 마음 내키는 대로 먹을거리 사이를 누볐다.\n\n아무도 더는 먹을 수 없게 되자, 교수가 첫 번째 정식 건배를 제의했는데, 이런 자리에서면 늘 빠지지 않던 것이었다 — \"마치 대고모님, 하느님의 축복이 함께하기를!\" 그녀에게 얼마나 큰 신세를 졌는지 결코 잊지 않는 그 선한 사내가 진심으로 외친 건배였고, 그녀를 기억하도록 배워 온 사내아이들이 조용히 잔을 비웠다.\n\n\"이제 할머니의 예순 번째 생신을 위하여! 만수무강을 빌며, 만세 삼창 세 번!\"\n\n그것은 능히 짐작할 수 있듯이 우렁차게 외쳐졌고, 한번 시작된 환호는 좀처럼 멈추기 어려웠다. 그들의 특별한 후원자로 여겨지던 로런스 씨부터, 어린 주인을 찾아 제 본분의 영역을 벗어나 헤매던 어리둥절한 기니피그에 이르기까지, 모두의 건강을 위한 건배가 제의됐다. 그러고 나서 맏손주인 데미가 그날의 여왕에게 갖가지 선물을 바쳤는데, 어찌나 많은지 잔치 자리까지 손수레로 실어 날라야 할 정도였다. 더러는 우스꽝스러운 선물들이었지만, 다른 사람의 눈에는 흠으로 보일 것들이 할머니의 눈에는 장식이었다 — 아이들의 선물은 전부 손수 만든 것이었으니까. 데이지의 참을성 있는 작은 손가락이 손수건에 떠 넣은 한 땀 한 땀이, 마치 부인에게는 자수보다 더 나았다. 데미의 신발 상자는 뚜껑이 닫히지 않았어도 기계 솜씨의 기적이었다. 롭의 발받침은 비뚤배뚤한 다리가 흔들거렸는데, 부인은 그게 아주 마음을 편안하게 해 준다고 했다. 그리고 에이미의 아이가 드린 값비싼 책의 그 어떤 면도, 비뚤배뚤한 대문자로 \"사랑하는 할머니께, 어린 베스가\"라고 적힌 그 면만큼 곱지 않았다.\n\n이 의식이 치러지는 동안 사내아이들은 슬그머니 사라지고 없었다. 그리고 마치 부인이 아이들에게 고마움을 전하려다 그만 목이 메어 버리고, 테디가 자기 앞치마로 부인의 눈물을 닦아 주던 그때, 교수가 불쑥 노래를 부르기 시작했다. 그러자 그의 머리 위에서 한 목소리 또 한 목소리가 그 노랫말을 이어받았고, 나무에서 나무로 보이지 않는 합창단의 가락이 메아리쳤다. 사내아이들이 온 마음을 다해, 조가 짓고 로리가 곡을 붙이고 교수가 가장 멋지게 부르도록 가르친 그 작은 노래를 부른 것이다. 이것은 완전히 새로운 것이었고, 대성공이었다. 마치 부인은 놀라움을 가라앉히지 못하고, 키 큰 프란츠와 에밀부터 모두 중에 가장 고운 목소리를 지닌 그 어린 혼혈아에 이르기까지, 깃털 없는 새들 한 마리 한 마리와 일일이 악수를 나누겠다고 고집했다.\n\n이 일이 끝나고 사내아이들은 마지막으로 한바탕 놀려고 흩어졌고, 마치 부인과 딸들만 잔치의 나무 아래 남았다.\n\n\"이렇게 내 가장 큰 소원이 이토록 곱게 이루어졌으니, 이제 다시는 나 자신을 '운 없는 조'라고 불러서는 안 되겠어요.\" 바에르 부인이 어린 테디의 작은 주먹을 우유 단지에서 꺼내며 말했다. 아이가 그 안을 신나게 휘젓고 있던 참이었다.\n\n\"그런데도 언니 인생은 언니가 옛날에 그리던 거랑은 아주 다르네. 우리의 공중누각 기억나?\" 에이미가 사내아이들과 크리켓을 하는 로리와 존을 지켜보며 미소 띤 얼굴로 물었다.\n\n\"착한 사람들! 저렇게 일을 잊고 하루 신나게 노는 걸 보니 내 마음이 다 흐뭇하구나.\" 조가 대답했다. 그녀는 이제 온 인류를 두고 어머니 같은 투로 말했다. \"그래, 기억나. 하지만 그때 내가 바라던 인생은 지금의 내겐 이기적이고 외롭고 차갑게만 보여. 아직 좋은 책을 한 권 쓸 수 있으리란 희망은 버리지 않았지만, 기다릴 수 있어. 그리고 이런 경험과 본보기 덕분에 분명 더 나은 책이 될 거라고 확신해.\" 그러면서 조는 저 멀리 활기찬 사내아이들에게서, 교수의 팔에 기댄 채 햇살 속을 오가며 둘 다 그토록 좋아하는 대화에 푹 빠진 자기 아버지에게로, 그리고 다시 어머니에게로 손짓했다. 어머니는 딸들 한가운데 보좌에 앉은 듯, 손주들을 무릎에 올리고 발치에 둔 채로 있었는데, 그 얼굴은 그들에게 결코 늙는 법이 없었고, 다들 그 얼굴에서 위로와 행복을 얻는 것 같았다.\n\n\"내 누각이 모두 중에서 가장 가깝게 이루어졌어. 물론 나는 근사한 것들을 바라긴 했지만, 마음속으로는 알고 있었어. 작은 집과 존, 그리고 이런 사랑스러운 아이들만 몇 있으면 만족하리라는 걸. 나는 그걸 다 가졌고, 하느님께 감사하게도 세상에서 가장 행복한 여자야.\" 메그가 자기의 키 큰 사내아이 머리에 손을 얹으며 말했다. 다정하고 경건한 만족이 가득한 얼굴로.\n\n\"내 누각은 내가 계획했던 거랑은 아주 다르지만, 그렇다고 바꾸진 않을래. 비록 언니처럼 나도 예술가의 꿈을 다 접거나, 남들이 아름다움의 꿈을 이루도록 돕는 일에만 나를 가두진 않을 거지만. 나는 아기 조각상을 만들기 시작했는데, 로리는 그게 내가 만든 것 중에 최고래. 나도 그렇게 생각하고, 대리석으로 만들 작정이야. 무슨 일이 생기든 적어도 내 어린 천사의 모습만은 간직할 수 있게.\" 에이미가 말하는 사이, 그녀의 품에서 잠든 아이의 금빛 머리 위로 굵은 눈물 한 방울이 떨어졌다. 그녀가 너무도 사랑하는 하나뿐인 딸은 연약한 어린것이었고, 그 아이를 잃을지 모른다는 두려움이 에이미의 햇살에 드리운 그늘이었던 것이다. 이 십자가는 아버지에게도 어머니에게도 많은 것을 해 주고 있었다. 하나의 사랑과 하나의 슬픔이 두 사람을 가깝게 묶어 주었으니까. 에이미의 천성은 더 다정하고 더 깊고 더 부드럽게 자라 갔다. 로리는 더 진중하고 강하고 굳건해져 갔다. 그리고 두 사람은 배워 가고 있었다. 아름다움도, 젊음도, 행운도, 심지어 사랑 그 자체도, 가장 복받은 사람에게서조차 근심과 고통, 상실과 슬픔을 막아 주지는 못한다는 것을. 왜냐하면—\n\n\"누구의 삶에든 얼마쯤 비는 내리는 법, 어떤 날은 어둡고 슬프고 음울할 수밖에.\"\n\n\"저 애는 점점 나아지고 있어, 나는 확신한단다, 얘야. 낙심하지 말고, 희망을 품고 행복하게 지내렴.\" 마치 부인이 말했다. 마음씨 고운 데이지가 할머니 무릎에서 몸을 숙여, 발그레한 뺨을 어린 사촌의 창백한 뺨에 갖다 댈 때였다.\n\n\"마미가 절 북돋워 주시고, 로리가 모든 짐의 절반 넘게 짊어져 주는데, 제가 낙심해선 안 되죠.\" 에이미가 따뜻하게 대답했다. \"그이는 절대 제게 자기 근심을 내비치는 법이 없고, 제게 너무도 다정하고 참을성 있고, 베스에게 그토록 헌신적이고, 언제나 제게 든든한 버팀목이자 위안이 되어 주어서, 저는 아무리 사랑해도 모자라요. 그러니 제 십자가 하나에도 불구하고, 저도 메그 언니처럼 말할 수 있어요. '하느님께 감사하게도, 나는 행복한 여자야.'\"\n\n\"나야 그런 말을 할 필요도 없지. 내가 누릴 자격보다 훨씬 더 행복하다는 건 누가 봐도 뻔하니까.\" 조가 덧붙였다. 착한 남편에게서, 곁의 풀밭에서 뒹구는 토실토실한 자기 아이들에게로 눈길을 옮기면서. \"프리츠는 점점 머리가 세고 살이 붙어 가고, 나는 그림자처럼 비쩍 말라 가는 데다 서른이야. 우리는 결코 부자가 못 될 테고, 그 못 말리는 토미 뱅스가 벌써 세 번이나 제 몸에 불을 냈는데도 기어이 이불 속에서 양치류 시가를 피워 대는 통에, 플럼필드는 어느 날 밤에 홀랑 타 버릴지도 몰라. 하지만 이런 낭만 없는 사실들에도 불구하고, 나는 불평할 게 하나도 없고, 평생 이렇게 즐거운 적이 없었어. 이런 말 써서 미안하지만, 사내아이들 틈에서 살다 보니 가끔 그 애들 말투를 안 쓸 수가 없네.\"\n\n\"그래, 조, 네 추수는 풍성할 거다.\" 마치 부인이 말을 꺼내며, 테디를 빤히 노려보아 무안하게 만들던 커다란 검은 귀뚜라미 한 마리를 쫓아 주었다.\n\n\"엄마 것의 절반에도 못 미칠걸요. 자, 여기 있잖아요, 엄마가 그토록 참을성 있게 씨 뿌리고 거두신 걸 우리는 아무리 감사해도 모자라요.\" 조가 외쳤다. 끝내 못 버린 그 사랑 넘치는 격정으로.\n\n\"해마다 밀은 더 많아지고 가라지는 더 적어지면 좋겠어요.\" 에이미가 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"큰 볏단이지만, 마미의 마음속엔 그걸 담을 자리가 있다는 걸 알아요.\" 메그의 다정한 목소리가 덧붙였다.\n\n마음 깊이 감동한 마치 부인은 그저 두 팔을 활짝 벌릴 뿐이었다. 마치 자식들과 손주들을 죄다 품에 끌어안기라도 하려는 듯이. 그러고는 어머니다운 사랑과 감사와 겸손이 가득한 얼굴과 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"오, 내 딸들아, 너희가 아무리 오래 산다 해도, 나는 결코 이보다 더 큰 행복을 빌어 줄 수가 없겠구나!\"";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-37106"]=[[["\"_Go then, my little Book, and show to all That entertain and bid thee welcome shall, What thou dost keep close shut up in thy breast; And wish what thou dost show them may be blest To them for good, may make them choose to be Pilgrims better, by far, than thee or me.", "작은 아씨들."], ["Tell them of Mercy; she is one Who early hath her pilgrimage begun. Yea, let young damsels learn of her to prize The world which is to come, and so be wise; For little tripping maids may follow God Along the ways which saintly feet have trod._\"", "작은 아씨들"]], [["Adapted from JOHN BUNYAN.", "또는\n메그, 조, 베스, 그리고 에이미"]], [["# XLVII. HARVEST TIME", "# XLVII. HARVEST TIME"]], [["[The Illustrations, designed by FRANK T. MERRILL, drawn, engraved, and printed under the supervision of GEORGE T. ANDREW.]", "지은이\n루이자 메이 올컷"]], [["They all drew to the fire, mother in the big chair, with Beth at her feet", "「작은 신사들」, 「구식 소녀」,\n「물레 이야기」 등의 작가"]], [["Preface\nContents", "프랭크 T. 메릴의 200점이 넘는 삽화와"]], [["Tail-piece to Contents", "에드먼드 H. 개럿이 그린"]], [["List of Illustrations", "작은 아씨들의 집 그림을 곁들여"]], [["Tail-piece to Illustrations", "보스턴\n리틀, 브라운 앤드 컴퍼니"]], [["Christmas won't be Christmas without any presents", "1868년과 1869년 의회법에 따라 등록함, 등록자\n루이자 메이 올컷,"]], [["Beth put a pair of slippers down to warm", "매사추세츠 지방\n법원 서기과에서."]], [["I used to be so frightened when it was my turn to sit in the big chair", "판권, 1880년,\n루이자 메이 올컷."]], [["Do it this way, clasp your hands so", "판권, 1896년,\n존 S. P. 올컷."]], [["It was a cheerful, hopeful letter", "보스턴\n앨프리드 머지 앤드 선 주식회사 인쇄"]], [["How you used to play Pilgrim's Progress", "\"자, 가거라, 나의 작은 책아, 너를 반기고"]], [["No one but Beth could get much music out of the old piano", "기꺼이 맞아 줄 모든 이에게 보여 주어라,\n네 가슴속 깊이 닫아 둔 것이 무엇인지를."]], [["At nine they stopped work and sung as usual", "그리고 네가 보여 주는 것이 그들에게"]], [["Merry Christmas\nThe procession set out", "복이 되기를, 이로움이 되기를, 너나 나보다"]], [["Out came Meg with gray horse-hair hanging about her face", "훨씬 더 나은 순례자가 되기를 그들이 택하게 되기를 바라거라."]], [["A little figure in cloudy white", "그들에게 머시의 이야기를 들려주어라. 그녀는"]], [["The lovers kneeling to receive Don Pedro's blessing", "일찍이 순례의 길을 떠난 사람이니. 그래, 어린 처녀들이 그녀에게서 배우게 하여라,"]], [["We talked over the fence\nTail-piece", "장차 올 세상을 귀히 여기고, 그리하여 지혜로워지도록."]], [["Eating apples and crying over the \"Heir of Redclyffe\"", "총총걸음 걷는 어린 소녀들도 하느님을 따를 수 있으니"]], [["Jo undertook to pinch the papered locks", "성인의 발길이 지나간 그 길을 따라서.\""]], [["Mrs.", "존 버니언에서 각색함."], ["Gardiner greeted them", "장(章)"]], [["Face to face with the Laurence boy", "1. 순례자 놀이\n2. 즐거운 크리스마스"]], [["They sat down on the stairs", "3. 로런스 가의 소년\n4. 짐"]], [["Tell about the party", "5. 이웃이 되어"]], [["The kitten stuck like a burr just out of reach", "6. 베스, 아름다운 궁전을 찾다\n7. 에이미가 겪은 굴욕의 골짜기"]], [["Curling herself up in the big chair", "8. 조, 아폴리온과 맞서다"]], [["Reading that everlasting Belsham", "9. 메그, 허영의 시장에 가다"]], [["He took her by the ear!", "10. 피크위크 클럽과 우편함\n11."], ["by the ear!", "실험"]], [["Mr.", "12."], ["Laurence hooked up a big fish", "로런스 캠프\n13. 공중누각"]], [["Tail-piece", "14. 비밀"]], [["Being neighborly", "15. 전보 한 통"]], [["Laurie opened the window", "16. 편지\n17. 작고 충실한 아이"]], [["Poll tweaked off his wig", "18. 어두운 나날\n19. 에이미의 유언장"]], [["Putting his finger under her chin", "20. 속마음\n21. 로리는 말썽을 부리고, 조는 화해를 시킨다"]], [["Please give these to your mother", "22. 즐거운 초원\n23. 마치 대고모가 문제를 해결하다"]], [["Tail-piece", "『작은 아씨들』 2부."]], [["O sir, they do care very much", "24. 잡담\n25. 첫 번째 결혼식"]], [["Mr.", "26."], ["Laurence often opened his study door", "예술적 시도\n27. 문학 수업"]], [["She put both arms around his neck and kissed him", "28. 살림살이 경험\n29. 방문"]], [["The Cyclops", "30. 결과"]], [["Amy bore without flinching several tingling blows", "31. 우리의 해외 통신원\n32. 마음 아픈 일들"]], [["You do know her", "33. 조의 일기\n34. 친구"]], [["Girls, where are you going?", "35. 가슴앓이\n36. 베스의 비밀"]], [["I burnt it up", "37. 새로운 인상"]], [["Held Amy up by his arms and hockey", "38. 뒷전으로 밀려나\n39. 게으른 로런스"]], [["Packing the go abroady trunk", "40. 죽음의 그림자 골짜기\n41. 잊는 법을 배우며"]], [["Meg's partner appeared", "42. 외톨이\n43. 뜻밖의 일들"]], [["Asked to be introduced", "44. 나의 영주와 귀부인\n45. 데이지와 데미"]], [["I wouldn't, Meg", "46. 우산 아래에서\n47. 수확의 계절"]], [["Holding a hand of each, Mrs. March said, &c.", "쪽수\n모두가 난롯가로 모여들었다, 큰 의자에 앉은 어머니와 그 발치에 앉은 베스"]], [["Mr. Pickwick", "차례\n차례 끝그림"]], [["Jo threw open the door of the closet", "삽화 목록\n삽화 목록 끝그림"]], [["Jo spent the morning on the river", "선물 없는 크리스마스는 크리스마스가 아니야"]], [["Amy sat down to draw\nO Pip! O Pip!", "베스가 슬리퍼 한 켤레를 내려놓아 데웠다"]], [["Miss Crocker made a wry face", "큰 의자에 앉을 차례가 되면 어찌나 무섭던지"]], [["We'll work like bees", "이렇게 해 봐, 두 손을 이렇게 마주 잡고"]], [["Beth was post-mistress", "명랑하고 희망에 찬 편지였다"]], [["Amy capped the climax by putting a clothes-pin on her nose 151", "「천로역정」 놀이를 어떻게 하곤 했던지\n낡은 피아노에서 그럴듯한 음악을 끌어낼 수 있는 사람은 베스뿐이었다"]], [["Mr. Laurence waving his hat\nNow, Miss Jo, I'll settle you", "아홉 시가 되자 일손을 멈추고 늘 하던 대로 노래를 불렀다"]], [["A very merry lunch it was", "메리 크리스마스\n행렬이 출발했다"]], [["He went prancing down a quiet street", "회색 말털을 얼굴에 늘어뜨린 메그가 나왔다"]], [["\"Oh, rise,\" she said", "뽀얀 흰빛의 작은 형체"]], [["A stunning blow from the big Greek lexicon", "돈 페드로의 축복을 받으려 무릎 꿇은 연인들\n우리는 울타리 너머로 이야기를 나누었다"]], [["He sneezed", "끝그림"]], [["The Portuguese walked the plank", "사과를 먹으면서 「레드클리프의 상속자」를 읽고 울었다"]], [["Will you give me a rose?", "조가 종이로 만 머리 마는 도구를 집어 들었다"]], [["Miss Kate put up her glass", "가드너 부인이 그들을 맞아 주었다"]], [["Ellen Tree\nTail-piece", "로런스네 소년과 얼굴을 마주하고"]], [["Swinging to and fro in his hammock", "그들은 계단에 앉았다\n파티 이야기를 들려줘"]], [["It was rather a pretty little picture", "새끼 고양이가 도꼬마리처럼 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 딱 붙어 있었다"]], [["Waved a brake before her face", "큰 의자에 몸을 웅크리고"]], [["I see him bow and smile", "그 지긋지긋한 벨샴을 읽으며"]], [["Tail-piece\nJo was very busy", "그가 그녀의 귀를 잡아당겼다! 귀를!"]], [["Hurrah for Miss March", "로런스 씨가 큰 물고기를 낚아 올렸다"]], [["Jo darted away", "끝그림\n이웃답게"]], [["Jo laid herself on the sofa and affected to read", "로리가 창문을 열었다\n폴이 그의 가발을 홱 잡아챘다"]], [["November is the most disagreeable month in the year", "그녀의 턱밑에 손가락을 받쳐 들고\n이걸 어머니께 갖다 드리세요"]], [["One of them horrid telegraph things", "끝그림\n오, 어르신, 그들은 정말 많이 마음 쓰고 있어요"]], [["She came suddenly upon Mr. Brooke", "로런스 씨는 종종 서재 문을 열어 두었다"]], [["The man clipped\nTail-piece", "그녀는 두 팔로 그의 목을 감고 입을 맞췄다"]], [["Letters", "키클롭스"]], [["She rolled away\nI wind the clock", "에이미는 따끔하게 매를 몇 대 맞으면서도 움찔하지 않았다"]], [["Yours Respectful, Hannah Mullet", "너는 정말 그녀를 알잖아\n얘들아, 너희 어디 가는 거니?"]], [["Tail-piece", "내가 태워 버렸어"]], [["It didn't stir, and I knew it was dead", "에이미를 두 팔과 하키 채로 받쳐 올렸다\n외국으로 가는 짐을 꾸리며"]], [["He sat down beside her", "메그의 파트너가 나타났다"]], [["What do you want now?", "소개해 달라고 청했다"]], [["Beth did have the fever", "나라면 안 그럴 거야, 메그"]], [["Gently stroking her head as her mother used to do", "마치 부인이 두 사람의 손을 한쪽씩 잡고 말하기를, 운운"]], [["Amy's Will", "피크위크 씨"]], [["Polish up the spoons and the fat silver teapot", "조가 벽장 문을 활짝 열어젖혔다\n조는 아침나절을 강에서 보냈다"]], [["On his back, with all his legs in the air", "에이미가 그림을 그리려고 앉았다\n오, 핍! 오, 핍!"]], [["I should choose this", "크로커 양이 얼굴을 찡그렸다"]], [["Gravely promenaded to and fro", "우리는 벌처럼 부지런히 일할 거야"]], [["Amy's Will\nTail-piece", "베스는 우체국장이었다"]], [["Mrs. March would not leave Beth's side", "에이미가 코에 빨래집게를 집으며 절정을 장식했다 151"]], [["Tail-piece\nLetters", "로런스 씨가 모자를 흔들었다"]], [["Jo and her mother were reading the note", "자, 조 양, 내가 당신을 손봐 주지\n아주 즐거운 점심이었다"]], [["Get up and don't be a goose", "그는 한적한 거리를 신나게 걸어 내려갔다"]], [["\"Hold your tongue!\"", "\"아, 일어나세요.\""], ["cried Jo, covering her ears", "그녀가 말했다\n큰 그리스어 사전으로 한 방 세게 얻어맞고"]], [["He stood at the foot, like a lion in the path", "그가 재채기를 했다\n포르투갈 사람이 판자 위를 걸었다"]], [["Beth was soon able to lie on the study sofa all day", "장미 한 송이 주시겠어요? 케이트 양이 안경을 들어 올렸다"]], [["The Jungfrau", "엘런 트리\n끝그림"]], [["Popping in her head now and then", "해먹에 누워 앞뒤로 흔들거리며\n제법 예쁘장한 작은 그림이었다"]], [["He sat in the big chair by Beth's sofa with the other three close by", "고사리를 그녀의 얼굴 앞에서 흔들었다\n그가 고개 숙여 인사하고 미소 짓는 게 보인다"]], [["Shall I tell you how?", "끝그림\n조는 매우 바빴다"]], [["Bless me, what's all this?", "마치 양 만세\n조가 쏜살같이 달려갔다"]], [["For Mrs. John Brooke", "조가 소파에 몸을 던지고는 읽는 척했다"]], [["Home of the Little Women", "11월은 일 년 중 가장 마음에 안 드는 달이다"]], [["The Dove Cote", "그 끔찍한 전보라는 것 중 하나"]], [["A small watchman's rattle", "그녀는 갑작스레 브룩 씨와 마주쳤다\n남자가 머리를 잘랐다"]], [["Tail-piece", "끝그림\n편지들"]], [["The First Wedding", "그녀는 마차를 타고 떠났다"]], [["Artistic Attempts", "내가 시계 태엽을 감습니다\n존경을 담아, 해나 멀릿"]], [["Her foot held fast in a panful of plaster", "끝그림\n미동도 하지 않기에, 나는 그것이 죽은 줄 알았다"]], [["Please don't, it's mine", "그가 그녀 곁에 앉았다"]], [["Tail-piece", "이제 또 뭘 원하니?"]], [["Literary Lessons", "베스는 정말로 열병에 걸렸다"]], [["A check for one hundred dollars", "어머니가 그러시던 것처럼 그녀의 머리를 부드럽게 쓸어 주며"]], [["Tail-piece", "에이미의 유언장"]], [["Domestic Experiences", "숟가락과 통통한 은 찻주전자를 닦아 윤을 내며"]], [["Both felt desperately uncomfortable", "다리를 모두 공중에 쳐든 채 등을 대고 누워\n나라면 이걸 고르겠어"]], [["A bargain, I assure you, ma'am", "진지하게 이리저리 거닐며\n에이미의 유언장"]], [["Laurie heroically shut his eyes while something was put into his arms", "끝그림\n마치 부인은 베스의 곁을 떠나려 하지 않았다"]], [["Calls", "끝그림"]], [["She took the saddle to the horse", "편지들\n조와 어머니가 쪽지를 읽고 있었다"]], [["It might have been worse", "일어나, 바보같이 굴지 말고"]], [["The call at Aunt March's\nTail-piece", "\"입 다물어!\" 조가 두 귀를 막으며 외쳤다"]], [["You shall have another table", "그는 길목을 지키는 사자처럼 계단 아래 서 있었다"]], [["Bought up the bouquets\nTail-piece", "베스는 곧 하루 종일 서재 소파에 누워 있을 수 있게 되었다"]], [["Flo and I ordered a hansom-cab", "융프라우\n이따금 머리를 쏙 들이밀며"]], [["Every one was very kind, especially the officers", "그는 베스의 소파 옆 큰 의자에 앉아 있었고 나머지 세 자매가 바싹 곁에 있었다"]], [["I've seen the imperial family several times", "어떻게 하는지 말해 줄까? 이런 세상에, 이게 다 뭐냐?"]], [["Trying to sketch the gray-stone lion's head on the wall", "존 브룩 부인에게\n작은 아씨들의 집"]], [["She leaned her head upon her hands", "비둘기 둥지\n작은 야경꾼의 딸랑이"]], [["Now, this is filling at the price", "끝그림\n첫 번째 결혼식"]], [["Up with the Bonnets of Bonnie Dundee", "예술적 시도\n석고가 든 대야에 발이 단단히 박혀"]], [["I amused myself by dropping gingerbread nuts over the seat", "제발 그러지 마, 그건 내 거야\n끝그림"]], [["Thou shalt haf thy Bhaer", "문학 수업\n백 달러짜리 수표"]], [["He waved his hand, sock and all", "끝그림\n살림살이 경험"]], [["Dis is mine effalunt", "둘 다 견딜 수 없이 거북해했다"]], [["I sat down upon the floor and read and looked and ate", "장담하건대 거저나 다름없는 거래예요, 부인\n로리는 무언가가 자기 품에 안기는 동안 영웅처럼 눈을 감았다"]], [["Tail-piece", "방문"]], [["In the presence of three gentlemen", "안장을 말에게 가져갔다\n더 나빴을 수도 있었다"]], [["A select symposium", "마치 대고모 댁 방문\n끝그림"]], [["He doesn't prink at his glass before coming", "다른 탁자를 하나 더 갖게 될 거예요\n꽃다발을 몽땅 사들였다"]], [["Jo stuffed the whole bundle into the stove", "끝그림\n플로와 나는 이륜마차를 불렀다"]], [["He put the sisters into the carriage", "다들 무척 친절했고, 특히 장교들이 그랬다\n황실 가족을 여러 번 보았다"]], [["He laid his head down on the mossy post", "벽에 새겨진 회색 돌사자 머리를 스케치하려 애쓰며\n그녀는 두 손에 머리를 기댔다"]], [["O Jo, can't you? Tail-piece", "자, 이건 값에 비해 든든하답니다"]], [["With her head in Jo's lap, while the wind blew healthfully over her", "보니 던디의 모자를 쓰고 일어서라\n좌석 너머로 생강 과자를 떨어뜨리며 혼자 즐거워했다"]], [["Tail-piece", "당신은 바에르를 갖게 될 거예요"]], [["He hurried forward to meet her", "그는 양말까지 손에 든 채 손을 흔들었다\n이건 내 코끼리야"]], [["Here are your flowers", "나는 바닥에 주저앉아 읽고, 바라보고, 먹었다"]], [["Demi and Daisy", "끝그림\n세 신사 앞에서"]], [["Mornin' now", "엄선된 좌담회"]], [["My dear man, it's a bonnet", "그는 오기 전에 거울 앞에서 멋을 부리지 않는다\n조가 그 꾸러미를 통째로 난로에 쑤셔 넣었다"]], [["Tail-piece", "그가 자매들을 마차에 태워 주었다"]], [["Sat piping on a stone while his goats skipped", "그는 이끼 낀 기둥에 머리를 기댔다\n오, 조, 정말 안 되겠어?"]], [["Laurie threw himself down on the turf", "끝그림\n조의 무릎에 머리를 얹고, 바람이 그녀 위로 상쾌하게 불어오는 동안"]], [["A rough sketch of Laurie taming a horse", "끝그림\n그는 서둘러 앞으로 나아가 그녀를 맞았다"]], [["The Valley of the Shadow", "여기 당신의 꽃이에요\n데미와 데이지"]], [["Tail-piece", "이제 아침이에요"]], [["Sat staring up at the busts", "이보게, 그건 보닛이라네\n끝그림"]], [["Turning the ring thoughtfully upon his finger", "염소들이 뛰노는 동안 그는 돌 위에 앉아 피리를 불었다"]], [["O Laurie, Laurie, I knew you'd come", "로리가 잔디밭에 벌렁 드러누웠다\n말을 길들이는 로리의 거친 스케치"]], [["How well we pull together", "죽음의 그림자 골짜기\n끝그림"]], [["Jo and her father", "흉상들을 올려다보며 앉아"]], [["Jo laid her head on a comfortable rag-bag and cried", "손가락의 반지를 생각에 잠겨 돌리며\n오, 로리, 로리, 네가 올 줄 알았어"]], [["A substantial lifelike ghost leaning over her", "우리 정말 호흡이 잘 맞는구나\n조와 아버지"]], [["The tall uncle proceeded to toss and tousle the small nephew", "조는 편안한 헝겊 자루에 머리를 묻고 울었다\n생생하게 살아 있는 듯한 든든한 유령이 그녀 위로 몸을 숙이고"]], [["O Mr. Bhaer, I am so glad to see you", "키 큰 삼촌이 어린 조카를 번쩍 들어 올려 마구 헝클어 놓았다"]], [["Mr. Bhaer sang heartily", "오, 바에르 씨, 만나서 정말 반가워요"]], [["Mrs. Laurence sitting in her mother's lap", "바에르 씨가 진심으로 노래를 불렀다\n어머니의 무릎에 앉은 로런스 부인"]], [["They began to pace up and down", "그들은 이리저리 거닐기 시작했다"]], [["Tail-piece", "끝그림"]], [["Me loves evvybody", "나는 모든 사람을 사랑해요"]], [["What makes my legs go, dranpa?", "내 다리는 왜 움직여요, 할아버지?"]], [["Dranpa, it's a We\nTail-piece", "할아버지, 이건 '우리'예요"]], [["Mr. Bhaer and Jo were enjoying promenades", "끝그림\n바에르 씨와 조는 함께 거니는 것을 즐겼다"]], [["Looking up she saw Mr. Bhaer", "고개를 들어 보니 바에르 씨가 보였다"]], [["Does this suit you, Mr. Bhaer?", "이게 마음에 드세요, 바에르 씨?"]], [["Under the umbrella", "우산 아래에서"]], [["Tail-piece", "끝그림"]], [["Harvest time", "수확의 계절"]], [["Teddy bore a charmed life", "테디는 신통하게도 무사했다"]], [["Leaving Mrs. March and her daughters under the festival tree", "축제의 나무 아래 마치 부인과 딸들을 남겨 두고"]], [["Tail-piece", "끝그림\n1."]], [["# PLAYING PILGRIMS.", "# PLAYING PILGRIMS."]], [["\"Christmas won't be Christmas without any presents,\"", "순례자 놀이\n\"선물 없는 크리스마스는 크리스마스가 아니야.\""], ["grumbled Jo, lying on the rug.", "조가 깔개에 드러누운 채 투덜거렸다."]], [["\"It's so dreadful to be poor!\"", "\"가난하다는 건 정말 끔찍해!\""], ["sighed Meg, looking down at her old dress.", "메그가 자기 낡은 옷을 내려다보며 한숨지었다."]], [["\"I don't think it's fair for some girls to have plenty of pretty things, and other girls nothing at all,\"", "\"어떤 애들은 예쁜 걸 잔뜩 가졌는데 어떤 애들은 아무것도 못 갖는 건 공평하지 않은 것 같아.\""], ["added little Amy, with an injured sniff.", "막내 에이미가 서운한 듯 훌쩍이며 덧붙였다."]], [["\"We've got father and mother and each other,\"", "\"우리한테는 아버지랑 어머니가 있고, 또 서로가 있잖아.\""], ["said Beth contentedly, from her corner.", "베스가 자기 구석에서 흐뭇하게 말했다."]], [["The four young faces on which the firelight shone brightened at the cheerful words, but darkened again as Jo said sadly,--", "난롯불이 비치는 네 어린 얼굴이 그 다정한 말에 환해졌지만, 조가 슬프게 입을 열자 다시 어두워졌다."]], [["\"We haven't got father, and shall not have him for a long time.\"", "\"우리한테 아버지는 안 계셔, 그것도 오랫동안 못 뵐 거고.\""], ["She didn't say \"perhaps never,\" but each silently added it, thinking of father far away, where the fighting was.", "조는 \"어쩌면 영영\"이라고는 말하지 않았지만, 모두가 전쟁이 벌어지는 먼 곳에 계신 아버지를 떠올리며 마음속으로 그 말을 덧붙였다."]], [["Nobody spoke for a minute; then Meg said in an altered tone,--", "잠시 아무도 말이 없었다. 그러더니 메그가 달라진 어조로 말했다."]], [["\"You know the reason mother proposed not having any presents this Christmas was because it is going to be a hard winter for every one; and she thinks we ought not to spend money for pleasure, when our men are suffering so in the army.", "\"엄마가 올 크리스마스에는 선물을 갖지 말자고 하신 건 모두에게 힘든 겨울이 될 테니까 그러신 거 알지. 우리 남자들이 군에서 그렇게 고생하는데 우리가 즐기자고 돈을 써서는 안 된다고 생각하시는 거야."], ["We can't do much, but we can make our little sacrifices, and ought to do it gladly.", "우리가 큰일을 할 순 없어도 작은 희생은 할 수 있고, 또 기꺼이 해야지."], ["But I am afraid I don't;\"", "그런데 난 그게 잘 안 되나 봐.\""], ["and Meg shook her head, as she thought regretfully of all the pretty things she wanted.", "메그는 자기가 갖고 싶은 예쁜 물건들을 아쉬운 마음으로 떠올리며 고개를 저었다."]], [["\"But I don't think the little we should spend would do any good.", "\"하지만 우리가 쓸 그 푼돈이 무슨 도움이 될까 싶어."], ["We've each got a dollar, and the army wouldn't be much helped by our giving that.", "우리는 각자 1달러씩 갖고 있는데, 그걸 내놓는다고 군이 크게 덕을 보지는 않을걸."], ["I agree not to expect anything from mother or you, but I do want to buy Undine and Sintram for myself; I've wanted it _so_ long,\"", "엄마나 언니한테 아무것도 기대하지 않는 건 좋아, 그래도 나는 「운디네와 진트람」을 나 주려고 사고 싶어. 그 책을 정말 오래 갖고 싶었단 말이야.\""], ["said Jo, who was a bookworm.", "책벌레인 조가 말했다."]], [["\"I planned to spend mine in new music,\"", "\"나는 내 돈을 새 악보 사는 데 쓸 생각이었어.\""], ["said Beth, with a little sigh, which no one heard but the hearth-brush and kettle-holder.", "베스가 작은 한숨을 내쉬었는데, 그 소리를 들은 건 난로 솔과 주전자 받침뿐이었다."]], [["\"I shall get a nice box of Faber's drawing-pencils; I really need them,\"", "\"나는 파버 그림 연필 한 상자를 좋은 걸로 살래. 정말 필요하거든.\""], ["said Amy decidedly.", "에이미가 단호하게 말했다."]], [["\"Mother didn't say anything about our money, and she won't wish us to give up everything.", "\"엄마가 우리 돈에 대해선 아무 말씀 안 하셨고, 우리가 모든 걸 포기하길 바라지도 않으실 거야."], ["Let's each buy what we want, and have a little fun; I'm sure we work hard enough to earn it,\"", "각자 갖고 싶은 걸 사서 즐기자. 우리가 그 정도 누릴 만큼은 충분히 열심히 일하잖아.\""], ["cried Jo, examining the heels of her shoes in a gentlemanly manner.", "조가 신사처럼 제 구두 뒤축을 살펴보며 외쳤다."]], [["\"I know _I_ do,--teaching those tiresome children nearly all day, when I'm longing to enjoy myself at home,\"", "\"나야 그렇지. 집에서 좀 즐기고 싶어 죽겠는데 하루 종일 그 성가신 애들을 가르치잖아.\""], ["began Meg, in the complaining tone again.", "메그가 다시 불평하는 어조로 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["\"You don't have half such a hard time as I do,\"", "\"언니는 나만큼 고생하는 것도 아니잖아.\""], ["said Jo.", "조가 말했다."], ["\"How would you like to be shut up for hours with a nervous, fussy old lady, who keeps you trotting, is never satisfied, and worries you till you're ready to fly out of the window or cry?\"", "\"신경질적이고 까다로운 할머니랑 몇 시간씩 갇혀 지내 봐. 끊임없이 심부름시키지, 만족이라곤 없지, 창밖으로 뛰쳐나가거나 울고 싶어질 때까지 사람을 들들 볶는다니까.\""]], [["\"It's naughty to fret; but I do think washing dishes and keeping things tidy is the worst work in the world.", "\"불평하는 건 나쁜 거지만, 그래도 설거지하고 집 안 치우는 게 세상에서 제일 힘든 일 같아."], ["It makes me cross; and my hands get so stiff, I can't practise well at all;\"", "그러면 짜증이 나고, 손이 너무 뻣뻣해져서 연습도 제대로 못 하거든.\""], ["and Beth looked at her rough hands with a sigh that any one could hear that time.", "베스가 자기 거친 손을 내려다보며 한숨을 쉬었는데, 이번엔 누구나 들을 수 있는 한숨이었다."]], [["\"I don't believe any of you suffer as I do,\"", "\"너희 중 누구도 나만큼 고생하진 않을걸.\" 에이미가 외쳤다."], ["cried Amy; \"for you don't have to go to school with impertinent girls, who plague you if you don't know your lessons, and laugh at your dresses, and label your father if he isn't rich, and insult you when your nose isn't nice.\"", "\"너흰 버릇없는 애들이 있는 학교에 안 가도 되잖아. 그 애들은 수업을 모르면 괴롭히고, 옷을 비웃고, 아버지가 부자가 아니면 '아버지를 라벨'하고, 코가 예쁘지 않으면 모욕한단 말이야.\""]], [["\"If you mean _libel_, I'd say so, and not talk about _labels_, as if papa was a pickle-bottle,\"", "\"'중상모략'을 말하려는 거면 그렇게 말해. '라벨'이 뭐니, 아빠가 무슨 절임 병이라도 되는 것처럼.\""], ["advised Jo, laughing.", "조가 웃으며 충고했다."]], [["\"I know what I mean, and you needn't be _statirical_ about it.", "\"내가 무슨 말 하는지 알거든, 그렇게 '풍지적'으로 굴 거 없어."], ["It's proper to use good words, and improve your _vocabilary_,\"", "좋은 단어를 쓰고 '어회'를 늘리는 게 옳은 거야.\""], ["returned Amy, with dignity.", "에이미가 점잔을 빼며 대꾸했다."]], [["\"Don't peck at one another, children.", "\"서로 쪼아 대지 마, 얘들아."], ["Don't you wish we had the money papa lost when we were little, Jo?", "우리 어렸을 때 아빠가 잃어버린 돈이 있으면 좋겠지, 조?"], ["Dear me!", "어머나!"], ["how happy and good we'd be, if we had no worries!\"", "걱정거리만 없다면 우리가 얼마나 행복하고 착해질까!\""], ["said Meg, who could remember better times.", "더 나은 시절을 기억하는 메그가 말했다."]], [["\"You said the other day, you thought we were a deal happier than the King children, for they were fighting and fretting all the time, in spite of their money.\"", "\"언니가 며칠 전에 그랬잖아, 우리가 킹네 애들보다 훨씬 행복한 것 같다고. 걔넨 돈이 많은데도 늘 싸우고 짜증을 내잖아.\""]], [["\"So I did, Beth.", "\"내가 그랬지, 베스."], ["Well, I think we are; for, though we do have to work, we make fun for ourselves, and are a pretty jolly set, as Jo would say.\"", "음, 정말 그런 것 같아. 우리는 일을 해야 하지만 스스로 즐거움을 만들어 내고, 조 말마따나 꽤 유쾌한 패거리니까.\""]], [["\"Jo does use such slang words!\"", "\"조는 별 속어를 다 쓴다니까!\""], ["observed Amy, with a reproving look at the long figure stretched on the rug.", "에이미가 깔개에 길게 뻗은 형체를 나무라는 눈길로 보며 말했다."], ["Jo immediately sat up, put her hands in her pockets, and began to whistle.", "조는 곧장 일어나 앉아 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 휘파람을 불기 시작했다."]], [["\"Don't, Jo; it's so boyish!\"", "\"그러지 마, 조. 너무 사내애 같아!\""]], [["\"That's why I do it.\"", "\"그래서 하는 거야.\""]], [["\"I detest rude, unlady-like girls!\"", "\"난 거칠고 숙녀답지 못한 여자애가 정말 싫어!\""]], [["\"I hate affected, niminy-piminy chits!\"", "\"난 잘난 척하고 새침 떠는 계집애가 질색이야!\""]], [["\"'Birds in their little nests agree,'\"", "\"'작은 둥지 속의 새들은 사이좋게 지낸다네.'\""], ["sang Beth, the peace-maker, with such a funny face that both sharp voices softened to a laugh, and the \"pecking\" ended for that time.", "평화주의자 베스가 어찌나 우스운 얼굴로 노래를 불렀던지, 날 선 두 목소리가 웃음으로 누그러지면서 \"쪼아 대기\"는 그쯤에서 끝이 났다."]], [["\"Really, girls, you are both to be blamed,\"", "\"정말이지, 얘들아, 둘 다 잘못이야.\""], ["said Meg, beginning to lecture in her elder-sisterly fashion.", "메그가 큰언니다운 투로 훈계를 시작했다."], ["\"You are old enough to leave off boyish tricks, and to behave better, Josephine.", "\"이제 사내애 같은 장난은 그만두고 좀 더 얌전하게 굴 나이가 됐어, 조세핀."], ["It didn't matter so much when you were a little girl; but now you are so tall, and turn up your hair, you should remember that you are a young lady.\"", "어렸을 땐 별 상관 없었지만, 이제 키도 그렇게 크고 머리도 틀어 올렸으니 네가 숙녀라는 걸 기억해야지.\""]], [["\"I'm not!", "\"난 숙녀 아니야!"], ["and if turning up my hair makes me one, I'll wear it in two tails till I'm twenty,\"", "머리 틀어 올리는 게 숙녀가 되는 거라면 스무 살까지 두 갈래로 땋고 다닐 거야.\""], ["cried Jo, pulling off her net, and shaking down a chestnut mane.", "조가 머리그물을 벗어 던지며 밤색 갈기 같은 머리를 풀어 헤치고 외쳤다."], ["\"I hate to think I've got to grow up, and be Miss March, and wear long gowns, and look as prim as a China-aster!", "\"어른이 돼서 마치 양이 되고, 긴 가운을 입고, 과꽃처럼 새침하게 굴어야 한다니 생각만 해도 끔찍해!"], ["It's bad enough to be a girl, anyway, when I like boys' games and work and manners!", "어쨌든 여자로 태어난 것부터가 충분히 못마땅한데, 난 사내애들 놀이랑 일이랑 행동거지가 좋단 말이야!"], ["I can't get over my disappointment in not being a boy; and it's worse than ever now, for I'm dying to go and fight with papa, and I can only stay at home and knit, like a poky old woman!\"", "남자로 못 태어난 게 두고두고 분해. 지금은 그게 더 심해, 아빠랑 같이 가서 싸우고 싶어 죽겠는데, 굼뜬 할머니처럼 집에 들어앉아 뜨개질이나 해야 하잖아!\""], ["And Jo shook the blue army-sock till the needles rattled like castanets, and her ball bounded across the room.", "조는 바늘이 캐스터네츠처럼 달그락거릴 때까지 파란 군용 양말을 흔들어 댔고, 실뭉치가 방 저편으로 튕겨 나갔다."]], [["\"Poor Jo!", "\"가엾은 조!"], ["It's too bad, but it can't be helped; so you must try to be contented with making your name boyish, and playing brother to us girls,\"", "정말 안됐지만 어쩔 수 없는걸. 그러니 이름이라도 사내애처럼 만들고, 우리 여자애들한테 오빠 노릇 하는 걸로 만족하도록 해 봐.\""], ["said Beth, stroking the rough head at her knee with a hand that all the dish-washing and dusting in the world could not make ungentle in its touch.", "베스가 무릎에 기댄 텁수룩한 머리를 쓰다듬으며 말했는데, 세상의 그 모든 설거지와 먼지 떨이로도 그 손길의 다정함만은 거칠게 만들 수 없었다."]], [["\"As for you, Amy,\"", "\"너는 말이지, 에이미,\""], ["continued Meg, \"you are altogether too particular and prim.", "메그가 말을 이었다. \"넌 너무 까다롭고 새침해."], ["Your airs are funny now; but you'll grow up an affected little goose, if you don't take care.", "지금이야 네 으스대는 게 우습지만, 조심하지 않으면 자라서 잘난 척하는 멍청이가 될 거야."], ["I like your nice manners and refined ways of speaking, when you don't try to be elegant; but your absurd words are as bad as Jo's slang.\"", "네가 우아한 척하지 않을 때는 예쁜 행동거지랑 점잖은 말투가 마음에 들어. 하지만 그 터무니없는 단어들은 조의 속어만큼이나 나쁘다고.\""]], [["\"If Jo is a tom-boy and Amy a goose, what am I, please?\"", "\"조가 말괄량이고 에이미가 멍청이라면, 그럼 나는 뭐야?\""], ["asked Beth, ready to share the lecture.", "베스가 자기도 훈계를 같이 받겠다는 듯 물었다."]], [["\"You're a dear, and nothing else,\"", "\"넌 사랑스러운 아이지, 그것 말곤 없어.\""], ["answered Meg warmly; and no one contradicted her, for the \"Mouse\"", "메그가 따뜻하게 대답했고, 아무도 반박하지 않았다."], ["was the pet of the family.", "\"생쥐\"는 온 가족의 귀염둥이였으니까."]], [["As young readers like to know \"how people look,\"", "어린 독자들은 \"사람들이 어떻게 생겼는지\""], ["we will take this moment to give them a little sketch of the four sisters, who sat knitting away in the twilight, while the December snow fell quietly without, and the fire crackled cheerfully within.", "알고 싶어 하니, 이 대목에서 네 자매를 잠깐 스케치해 보기로 하자. 그들은 황혼 속에서 부지런히 뜨개질을 하고 있었고, 밖에서는 12월의 눈이 조용히 내렸으며, 안에서는 난롯불이 경쾌하게 타닥거렸다."], ["It was a comfortable old room, though the carpet was faded and the furniture very plain; for a good picture or two hung on the walls, books filled the recesses, chrysanthemums and Christmas roses bloomed in the windows, and a pleasant atmosphere of home-peace pervaded it.", "양탄자는 빛이 바랬고 가구는 무척 수수했지만, 아늑하고 오래된 방이었다. 벽에는 괜찮은 그림이 한두 점 걸려 있고, 벽감에는 책이 가득 꽂혀 있으며, 창가에는 국화와 크리스마스 장미가 피어 있고, 가정의 평화로운 분위기가 방 안에 가득 배어 있었으니까."]], [["Margaret, the eldest of the four, was sixteen, and very pretty, being plump and fair, with large eyes, plenty of soft, brown hair, a sweet mouth, and white hands, of which she was rather vain.", "네 자매 중 맏이인 마거릿은 열여섯 살이었고, 매우 예뻤다. 통통하고 살결이 희며, 큰 눈에 부드러운 갈색 머리가 풍성했고, 입매가 고왔으며, 손이 하얬는데 그 손을 은근히 자랑스러워했다."], ["Fifteen-year-old Jo was very tall, thin, and brown, and reminded one of a colt; for she never seemed to know what to do with her long limbs, which were very much in her way.", "열다섯 살 조는 키가 아주 크고 비쩍 말랐으며 가무잡잡해서 망아지를 떠올리게 했다. 자기한테 거추장스럽기만 한 긴 팔다리를 어떻게 해야 할지 도무지 모르는 것 같았으니까."], ["She had a decided mouth, a comical nose, and sharp, gray eyes, which appeared to see everything, and were by turns fierce, funny, or thoughtful.", "야무진 입매에 우스꽝스러운 코, 모든 걸 다 꿰뚫어 보는 듯한 날카로운 잿빛 눈을 가졌는데, 그 눈은 때로는 사납고 때로는 익살맞고 때로는 사색에 잠겨 있었다."], ["Her long, thick hair was her one beauty; but it was usually bundled into a net, to be out of her way.", "길고 숱 많은 머리카락이 그녀의 유일한 미인 요소였지만, 거추장스럽다고 보통 머리그물 속에 둘둘 말아 넣고 다녔다."], ["Round shoulders had Jo, big hands and feet, a fly-away look to her clothes, and the uncomfortable appearance of a girl who was rapidly shooting up into a woman, and didn't like it.", "조는 어깨가 둥글고, 손발이 컸으며, 옷매무새가 늘 헐렁했고, 빠르게 여인으로 자라는 게 영 마뜩잖은 소녀 특유의 어색한 분위기를 풍겼다."], ["Elizabeth--or Beth, as every one called her--was a rosy, smooth-haired, bright-eyed girl of thirteen, with a shy manner, a timid voice, and a peaceful expression, which was seldom disturbed. Her father called her \"Little Tranquillity,\"", "엘리자베스는 — 다들 베스라고 불렀다 — 발그레한 뺨에 매끄러운 머릿결, 초롱초롱한 눈을 지닌 열세 살 소녀로, 수줍은 태도와 소심한 목소리, 좀처럼 흐트러지지 않는 평온한 표정을 지녔다."], ["and the name suited her excellently; for she seemed to live in a happy world of her own, only venturing out to meet the few whom she trusted and loved.", "아버지는 그녀를 \"꼬마 평온이\"라고 불렀는데, 그 이름이 그녀에게 더없이 잘 어울렸다. 그녀는 자기만의 행복한 세계 속에서 사는 듯했고, 믿고 사랑하는 몇몇 사람을 만날 때만 그 세계 밖으로 조심스레 나왔으니까."], ["Amy, though the youngest, was a most important person,--in her own opinion at least.", "에이미는 막내였지만 가장 중요한 사람이었다 — 적어도 자기 생각으로는."], ["A regular snow-maiden, with blue eyes, and yellow hair, curling on her shoulders, pale and slender, and always carrying herself like a young lady mindful of her manners.", "파란 눈에 어깨까지 곱슬거리는 노란 머리를 늘어뜨린 영락없는 눈의 요정 같았고, 창백하고 가냘팠으며, 늘 예의범절을 의식하는 숙녀처럼 처신했다."], ["What the characters of the four sisters were we will leave to be found out.", "네 자매의 성격이 어떤지는 차차 알아 가도록 남겨 두기로 하자."]], [["The clock struck six; and, having swept up the hearth, Beth put a pair of slippers down to warm.", "시계가 여섯 시를 쳤다. 베스는 난로 앞을 쓸고 나서 슬리퍼 한 켤레를 내려놓아 데웠다."], ["Somehow the sight of the old shoes had a good effect upon the girls; for mother was coming, and every one brightened to welcome her.", "어쩐 일인지 그 낡은 신발을 보자 소녀들은 기분이 좋아졌다. 어머니가 오실 시간이라 다들 맞이할 채비로 환해졌으니까."], ["Meg stopped lecturing, and lighted the lamp, Amy got out of the easy-chair without being asked, and Jo forgot how tired she was as she sat up to hold the slippers nearer to the blaze.", "메그는 훈계를 멈추고 램프를 켰고, 에이미는 누가 시키지도 않았는데 안락의자에서 일어났으며, 조는 슬리퍼를 불길 가까이 대려고 일어나 앉으면서 자기가 얼마나 피곤한지도 잊어버렸다."]], [["\"They are quite worn out; Marmee must have a new pair.\"", "\"많이 닳았네. 엄마한테 새 걸 사 드려야겠어.\""]], [["\"I thought I'd get her some with my dollar,\"", "\"내 돈으로 한 켤레 사 드릴까 했는데.\""], ["said Beth.", "베스가 말했다."]], [["\"No, I shall!\"", "\"아니, 내가 할 거야!\""], ["cried Amy.", "에이미가 외쳤다."]], [["\"I'm the oldest,\"", "\"내가 제일 맏이잖아.\""], ["began Meg, but Jo cut in with a decided--", "메그가 입을 떼자 조가 단호하게 끼어들었다."]], [["\"I'm the man of the family now papa is away, and _I_ shall provide the slippers, for he told me to take special care of mother while he was gone.\"", "\"아빠가 안 계시니까 지금은 내가 이 집의 가장이야. 그러니까 슬리퍼는 내가 마련할 거야. 아빠가 떠나시면서 어머니를 각별히 잘 보살피라고 나한테 당부하셨거든.\""]], [["\"I'll tell you what we'll do,\"", "\"우리가 어떻게 할지 말해 줄게.\""], ["said Beth; \"let's each get her something for Christmas, and not get anything for ourselves.\"", "베스가 말했다. \"각자 크리스마스 선물을 엄마한테 하나씩 사 드리고, 우리 건 아무것도 사지 말자.\""]], [["\"That's like you, dear!", "\"역시 너답다, 얘!"], ["What will we get?\"", "뭘 사 드릴까?\""], ["exclaimed Jo.", "조가 소리쳤다."]], [["Every one thought soberly for a minute; then Meg announced, as if the idea was suggested by the sight of her own pretty hands, \"I shall give her a nice pair of gloves.\"", "다들 잠깐 진지하게 생각에 잠겼다. 그러더니 메그가, 자기 예쁜 손을 보고 떠올랐다는 듯이 말했다. \"난 좋은 장갑 한 켤레를 사 드려야지.\""]], [["\"Army shoes, best to be had,\"", "\"군화로, 살 수 있는 제일 좋은 걸로!\""], ["cried Jo.", "조가 외쳤다."]], [["\"Some handkerchiefs, all hemmed,\"", "\"손수건 몇 장, 가장자리를 다 감친 걸로.\""], ["said Beth.", "베스가 말했다."]], [["\"I'll get a little bottle of cologne; she likes it, and it won't cost much, so I'll have some left to buy my pencils,\"", "\"난 작은 오드콜로뉴 병을 살래. 엄마가 좋아하시고, 비싸지도 않으니까 연필 살 돈도 좀 남을 거야.\""], ["added Amy.", "에이미가 덧붙였다."]], [["\"How will we give the things?\"", "\"선물을 어떻게 드리지?\""], ["asked Meg.", "메그가 물었다."]], [["\"Put them on the table, and bring her in and see her open the bundles.", "\"탁자에 올려놓고, 엄마를 모셔 와서 꾸러미 푸는 걸 지켜보자."], ["Don't you remember how we used to do on our birthdays?\"", "우리 생일 때 어떻게 했는지 기억 안 나?\""], ["answered Jo.", "조가 대답했다."]], [["\"I used to be _so_ frightened when it was my turn to sit in the big chair with the crown on, and see you all come marching round to give the presents, with a kiss.", "\"내 차례가 돼서 왕관 쓰고 큰 의자에 앉아 있는 게 얼마나 무섭던지. 너희가 다들 빙 둘러서 행진하며 입을 맞추고 선물을 주는 거 말이야."], ["I liked the things and the kisses, but it was dreadful to have you sit looking at me while I opened the bundles,\"", "선물도 입맞춤도 좋았지만, 내가 꾸러미를 푸는 동안 너희가 앉아서 빤히 쳐다보는 건 정말 끔찍했어.\""], ["said Beth, who was toasting her face and the bread for tea, at the same time.", "베스가 말했는데, 그러면서도 차에 곁들일 빵과 자기 얼굴을 동시에 불에 익히고 있었다."]], [["\"Let Marmee think we are getting things for ourselves, and then surprise her.", "\"엄마한테는 우리가 각자 쓸 걸 사는 줄 알게 해 놓고, 깜짝 놀라게 해 드리자."], ["We must go shopping to-morrow afternoon, Meg; there is so much to do about the play for Christmas night,\"", "내일 오후엔 쇼핑하러 가야 해, 메그. 크리스마스 밤에 할 연극 준비할 게 잔뜩 있으니까.\""], ["said Jo, marching up and down, with her hands behind her back and her nose in the air.", "조가 두 손을 등 뒤로 하고 코를 치켜든 채 이리저리 행진하며 말했다."]], [["\"I don't mean to act any more after this time; I'm getting too old for such things,\"", "\"난 이번을 끝으로 연극은 그만둘 생각이야. 그런 거 하기엔 나이가 너무 들었어.\""], ["observed Meg, who was as much a child as ever about \"dressing-up\" frolics.", "메그가 말했는데, 그러면서도 \"변장\"하고 노는 일에 관해서라면 여전히 어린애 같았다."]], [["\"You won't stop, I know, as long as you can trail round in a white gown with your hair down, and wear gold-paper jewelry.", "\"언니가 흰 가운을 끌고 다니면서 머리 풀어 헤치고 금종이 장신구를 걸칠 수 있는 한, 절대 그만두지 못할걸."], ["You are the best actress we've got, and there'll be an end of everything if you quit the boards,\"", "언니가 우리 중에 제일 연기를 잘하는데, 언니가 무대를 떠나면 다 끝장이라고.\" 조가 말했다."], ["said Jo. \"We ought to rehearse to-night.", "\"오늘 밤에 연습해야 해."], ["Come here, Amy, and do the fainting scene, for you are as stiff as a poker in that.\"", "이리 와, 에이미, 기절하는 장면 좀 해 봐. 넌 그 장면에서 부지깽이처럼 뻣뻣하잖아.\""]], [["\"I can't help it; I never saw any one faint, and I don't choose to make myself all black and blue, tumbling flat as you do.", "\"나도 어쩔 수 없어. 누가 기절하는 걸 본 적이 없는걸, 게다가 언니처럼 풀썩 넘어져서 온몸에 멍이 들고 싶지도 않아."], ["If I can go down easily, I'll drop; if I can't, I shall fall into a chair and be graceful; I don't care if Hugo does come at me with a pistol,\"", "쉽게 쓰러질 수 있으면 그냥 쓰러지고, 안 되면 의자에 우아하게 주저앉을 거야. 휴고가 권총을 들고 나한테 달려들어도 난 상관없어.\""], ["returned Amy, who was not gifted with dramatic power, but was chosen because she was small enough to be borne out shrieking by the villain of the piece.", "에이미가 대꾸했다. 그녀는 연기에 재능을 타고나진 않았지만, 악당이 비명을 지르는 그녀를 번쩍 들어 안고 나갈 수 있을 만큼 몸집이 작아서 뽑힌 거였다."]], [["\"Do it this way; clasp your hands so, and stagger across the room, crying frantically, 'Roderigo!", "\"이렇게 해 봐. 두 손을 이렇게 마주 잡고, 방을 가로질러 비틀거리면서 미친 듯이 외치는 거야."], ["save me!", "'로드리고!"], ["save me!'\"", "살려 줘요!"], ["and away went Jo, with a melodramatic scream which was truly thrilling.", "살려 줘요!'\" 그러더니 조가 정말로 소름 끼치는 멜로드라마풍 비명을 지르며 시범을 보였다."]], [["Amy followed, but she poked her hands out stiffly before her, and jerked herself along as if she went by machinery; and her \"Ow!\"", "에이미가 따라 했지만, 두 손을 앞으로 뻣뻣하게 내밀고 기계처럼 덜컥덜컥 움직였다. 그녀의 \"아얏!\""], ["was more suggestive of pins being run into her than of fear and anguish.", "소리도 두려움과 고통이라기보다는 핀에 찔린 것 같은 느낌이었다."], ["Jo gave a despairing groan, and Meg laughed outright, while Beth let her bread burn as she watched the fun, with interest.", "조는 절망스러운 신음을 냈고, 메그는 큰 소리로 웃었으며, 베스는 그 재미난 광경에 정신이 팔린 나머지 빵이 타는 것도 내버려 두었다."]], [["\"It's no use!", "\"안 되겠다!"], ["Do the best you can when the time comes, and if the audience laugh, don't blame me.", "때가 되면 그냥 최선을 다해 봐, 그래서 관객이 웃어도 날 탓하진 마."], ["Come on, Meg.\"", "자, 메그, 어서.\""]], [["Then things went smoothly, for Don Pedro defied the world in a speech of two pages without a single break; Hagar, the witch, chanted an awful incantation over her kettleful of simmering toads, with weird effect; Roderigo rent his chains asunder manfully, and Hugo died in agonies of remorse and arsenic, with a wild \"Ha!", "그다음부터는 일이 순조롭게 풀렸다. 돈 페드로는 두 페이지짜리 대사를 단 한 번도 끊김 없이 읊으며 온 세상에 맞섰고, 마녀 하가르는 부글부글 끓는 두꺼비 가마솥 위에서 으스스한 효과를 내며 무시무시한 주문을 외웠으며, 로드리고는 사내답게 쇠사슬을 끊어 버렸고, 휴고는 회한과 비소의 고통 속에서 사납게 \"하!"], ["ha!\"", "하!\" 하며 죽어 갔다."]], [["\"It's the best we've had yet,\"", "\"지금까지 한 것 중에 제일 잘했어.\""], ["said Meg, as the dead villain sat up and rubbed his elbows.", "죽은 악당이 일어나 앉아 팔꿈치를 문지르며 메그가 말했다."]], [["\"I don't see how you can write and act such splendid things, Jo.", "\"어떻게 그렇게 멋진 걸 쓰고 또 연기까지 하는지 모르겠어, 조."], ["You're a regular Shakespeare!\"", "넌 정말 셰익스피어야!\""], ["exclaimed Beth, who firmly believed that her sisters were gifted with wonderful genius in all things.", "베스가 외쳤는데, 그녀는 언니들이 모든 일에 놀라운 천재성을 타고났다고 굳게 믿고 있었다."]], [["\"Not quite,\"", "\"그 정도는 아니지.\""], ["replied Jo modestly.", "조가 겸손하게 대답했다."], ["\"I do think 'The Witch's Curse, an Operatic Tragedy,'", "\"「마녀의 저주, 오페라 비극」이 제법 괜찮은 작품이라고 생각하긴 해."], ["is rather a nice thing; but I'd like to try Macbeth, if we only had a trap-door for Banquo.", "그래도 밴쿠오가 떨어질 함정문만 있으면 「맥베스」에 도전해 보고 싶어."], ["I always wanted to do the killing part.", "난 늘 살인 장면을 해 보고 싶었거든."], ["'Is that a dagger that I see before me?'\"", "'내 앞에 보이는 저것은 단검인가?'\""], ["muttered Jo, rolling her eyes and clutching at the air, as she had seen a famous tragedian do.", "조가 눈을 부라리고 허공을 움켜쥐며 중얼거렸는데, 어느 유명한 비극 배우가 하는 걸 본 적이 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"No, it's the toasting fork, with mother's shoe on it instead of the bread.", "\"아니, 그건 빵 대신 엄마 신발이 꽂힌 토스트용 포크야."], ["Beth's stage-struck!\"", "베스가 무대에 홀렸나 봐!\""], ["cried Meg, and the rehearsal ended in a general burst of laughter.", "메그가 외쳤고, 연습은 다 같이 웃음을 터뜨리며 끝이 났다."]], [["\"Glad to find you so merry, my girls,\"", "\"우리 딸들이 이렇게 즐거워하니 반갑구나.\""], ["said a cheery voice at the door, and actors and audience turned to welcome a tall, motherly lady, with a \"can-I-help-you\"", "문 앞에서 명랑한 목소리가 들려왔고, 배우들과 관객들이 키 크고 인자한 부인을 맞으려 돌아보았다. 그 부인에게서는 \"내가 도와줄까?\""], ["look about her which was truly delightful.", "하는 표정이 풍겨 나와 참으로 사랑스러웠다."], ["She was not elegantly dressed, but a noble-looking woman, and the girls thought the gray cloak and unfashionable bonnet covered the most splendid mother in the world.", "우아하게 차려입지는 않았지만 기품 있어 보이는 여인이었고, 소녀들은 그 회색 망토와 한물간 보닛이 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 어머니를 감싸고 있다고 여겼다."]], [["\"Well, dearies, how have you got on to-day?", "\"자, 얘들아, 오늘 하루 어떻게 보냈니?"], ["There was so much to do, getting the boxes ready to go to-morrow, that I didn't come home to dinner.", "내일 보낼 상자들을 챙기느라 할 일이 어찌나 많던지 점심때 집에 오지도 못했구나."], ["Has any one called, Beth?", "누구 다녀간 사람 있니, 베스?"], ["How is your cold, Meg?", "감기는 좀 어떠니, 메그?"], ["Jo, you look tired to death.", "조, 너 죽도록 피곤해 보이는구나."], ["Come and kiss me, baby.\"", "이리 와서 엄마한테 뽀뽀해 주렴, 아가.\""]], [["While making these maternal inquiries Mrs. March got her wet things off, her warm slippers on, and sitting down in the easy-chair, drew Amy to her lap, preparing to enjoy the happiest hour of her busy day.", "이렇게 엄마다운 질문을 던지는 동안 마치 부인은 젖은 옷을 벗고 따뜻한 슬리퍼를 신었으며, 안락의자에 앉아 에이미를 무릎으로 끌어당기고는 바쁜 하루 중 가장 행복한 시간을 즐길 채비를 했다."], ["The girls flew about, trying to make things comfortable, each in her own way.", "소녀들은 저마다의 방식으로 분주하게 움직이며 이것저것 편하게 해 드리려 애썼다."], ["Meg arranged the tea-table; Jo brought wood and set chairs, dropping, overturning, and clattering everything she touched; Beth trotted to and fro between parlor and kitchen, quiet and busy; while Amy gave directions to every one, as she sat with her hands folded.", "메그는 찻상을 차렸고, 조는 장작을 가져오고 의자를 놓다가 손대는 것마다 떨어뜨리고 엎고 달그락거렸으며, 베스는 거실과 부엌 사이를 조용히 부지런하게 오갔다. 한편 에이미는 두 손을 모은 채 앉아서 모두에게 이래라저래라 지시했다."]], [["As they gathered about the table, Mrs.", "다들 탁자에 둘러앉자 마치 부인이 유난히 행복한 얼굴로 말했다."], ["March said, with a particularly happy face, \"I've got a treat for you after supper.\"", "\"저녁 먹고 나서 너희한테 줄 즐거운 게 있단다.\""]], [["A quick, bright smile went round like a streak of sunshine.", "햇살이 한 줄기 스치듯, 빠르고 환한 미소가 빙 둘러 번졌다."], ["Beth clapped her hands, regardless of the biscuit she held, and Jo tossed up her napkin, crying, \"A letter!", "베스는 들고 있던 비스킷도 아랑곳없이 손뼉을 쳤고, 조는 냅킨을 위로 던지며 외쳤다."], ["a letter!", "\"편지다!"], ["Three cheers for father!\"", "편지야! 아빠 만세!\""]], [["\"Yes, a nice long letter.", "\"그래, 길고 좋은 편지란다."], ["He is well, and thinks he shall get through the cold season better than we feared.", "아버지는 건강하시고, 추운 계절을 우리가 걱정했던 것보다 잘 넘기실 것 같다는구나."], ["He sends all sorts of loving wishes for Christmas, and an especial message to you girls,\"", "크리스마스를 맞아 온갖 사랑의 인사를 보내셨고, 특별히 너희 딸들에게 전하는 말씀도 있단다.\""], ["said Mrs. March, patting her pocket as if she had got a treasure there.", "마치 부인이 주머니에 무슨 보물이라도 든 것처럼 그곳을 토닥이며 말했다."]], [["\"Hurry and get done!", "\"어서 다 먹어!"], ["Don't stop to quirk your little finger, and simper over your plate, Amy,\"", "새끼손가락 꼬부리고 접시 위에서 새침 떨고 있을 시간 없어, 에이미.\""], ["cried Jo, choking in her tea, and dropping her bread, butter side down, on the carpet, in her haste to get at the treat.", "조가 외쳤는데, 즐거운 소식이 너무 듣고 싶은 나머지 차에 사레가 들리고 빵을 버터 바른 면이 아래로 가게 양탄자에 떨어뜨렸다."]], [["Beth ate no more, but crept away, to sit in her shadowy corner and brood over the delight to come, till the others were ready.", "베스는 더 먹지 않고 살그머니 자기 그늘진 구석으로 가, 다른 식구들이 준비될 때까지 곧 다가올 즐거움을 곱씹으며 앉아 있었다."]], [["\"I think it was so splendid in father to go as a chaplain when he was too old to be drafted, and not strong enough for a soldier,\"", "\"아버지가 징집되기엔 너무 나이가 많고 군인이 되기엔 건강도 충분치 않으신데 종군 목사로 가신 건 정말 훌륭한 일이야.\""], ["said Meg warmly.", "메그가 따뜻하게 말했다."]], [["\"Don't I wish I could go as a drummer, a _vivan_--what's its name?", "\"나도 북 치는 병사나 종군… 뭐라더라?"], ["or a nurse, so I could be near him and help him,\"", "아니면 간호사로라도 가서 아빠 곁에서 도울 수 있으면 좋겠어.\""], ["exclaimed Jo, with a groan.", "조가 신음하듯 외쳤다."]], [["\"It must be very disagreeable to sleep in a tent, and eat all sorts of bad-tasting things, and drink out of a tin mug,\"", "\"천막에서 자고, 온갖 맛없는 걸 먹고, 양철 컵으로 물을 마셔야 한다니 정말 끔찍할 거야.\""], ["sighed Amy.", "에이미가 한숨지었다."]], [["\"When will he come home, Marmee?\"", "\"아빠는 언제 집에 오셔, 엄마?\""], ["asked Beth, with a little quiver in her voice.", "베스가 목소리를 살짝 떨며 물었다."]], [["\"Not for many months, dear, unless he is sick.", "\"여러 달 동안은 못 오신단다, 얘야. 편찮으시지 않은 한은."], ["He will stay and do his work faithfully as long as he can, and we won't ask for him back a minute sooner than he can be spared.", "가능한 한 오래 머무르며 맡은 일을 충실히 하실 거야. 그러니 우리도 아버지가 빠지셔도 될 때보다 단 일 분이라도 일찍 돌아오시라고 조르지 말자."], ["Now come and hear the letter.\"", "자, 이제 이리 와서 편지를 듣자꾸나.\""]], [["They all drew to the fire, mother in the big chair with Beth at her feet, Meg and Amy perched on either arm of the chair, and Jo leaning on the back, where no one would see any sign of emotion if the letter should happen to be touching.", "다들 난롯가로 다가왔다. 어머니는 큰 의자에 앉고 베스는 그 발치에 앉았으며, 메그와 에이미는 의자 양쪽 팔걸이에 걸터앉았고, 조는 의자 등받이에 기댔다. 편지에 혹시 가슴 뭉클한 대목이 나오더라도 누구에게도 감정의 기색을 들키지 않을 자리였다."]], [["Very few letters were written in those hard times that were not touching, especially those which fathers sent home.", "그 힘겨운 시절에 쓰인 편지 중 가슴 뭉클하지 않은 것은 거의 없었고, 특히 아버지들이 집으로 보내는 편지는 더욱 그러했다. 이 편지에는 견뎌 낸 고초나 맞닥뜨린 위험, 이겨 낸 향수병에 대해서는 별로 적혀 있지 않았다."], ["In this one little was said of the hardships endured, the dangers faced, or the homesickness conquered; it was a cheerful, hopeful letter, full of lively descriptions of camp life, marches, and military news; and only at the end did the writer's heart overflow with fatherly love and longing for the little girls at home.", "야영 생활과 행군, 군 소식에 대한 생생한 묘사로 가득한, 명랑하고 희망에 찬 편지였다. 그리고 마지막에 이르러서야 글쓴이의 마음이 집에 있는 어린 딸들을 향한 아버지의 사랑과 그리움으로 흘러넘쳤다."]], [["\"Give them all my dear love and a kiss.", "\"애들 모두에게 내 깊은 사랑과 입맞춤을 전해 주오."], ["Tell them I think of them by day, pray for them by night, and find my best comfort in their affection at all times.", "낮이면 그 애들을 생각하고 밤이면 그 애들을 위해 기도하며, 그 애들의 사랑에서 언제나 가장 큰 위안을 얻는다고 전해 주오."], ["A year seems very long to wait before I see them, but remind them that while we wait we may all work, so that these hard days need not be wasted.", "그 애들을 다시 보기까지 일 년은 무척 긴 기다림처럼 느껴지지만, 기다리는 동안 우리 모두 일할 수 있으니 이 힘든 나날을 헛되이 보낼 필요는 없다고 일러 주오."], ["I know they will remember all I said to them, that they will be loving children to you, will do their duty faithfully, fight their bosom enemies bravely, and conquer themselves so beautifully, that when I come back to them I may be fonder and prouder than ever of my little women.\"", "그 애들은 내가 한 말을 다 기억하리라 믿소. 당신에게 다정한 자식이 되고, 제 본분을 충실히 다하며, 마음속 적과 용감히 싸우고, 스스로를 아름답게 이겨 내어, 내가 돌아갔을 때 그 어느 때보다 우리 작은 아씨들을 더 사랑하고 더 자랑스러워할 수 있게 되리라고 말이오.\""]], [["Everybody sniffed when they came to that part; Jo wasn't ashamed of the great tear that dropped off the end of her nose, and Amy never minded the rumpling of her curls as she hid her face on her mother's shoulder and sobbed out, \"I _am_ a selfish girl!", "그 대목에 이르자 다들 코를 훌쩍였다. 조는 코끝에서 떨어지는 굵은 눈물방울을 부끄러워하지 않았고, 에이미는 엄마 어깨에 얼굴을 묻고 흐느끼느라 곱슬머리가 헝클어지는 것도 개의치 않았다."], ["but I'll truly try to be better, so he mayn't be disappointed in me by and by.\"", "\"난 정말 이기적인 애야! 그래도 정말 더 나아지려고 애쓸 거야, 그래서 나중에 아빠가 나한테 실망하시지 않게.\""]], [["\"We all will!\"", "\"우리 모두 그럴 거야!\""], ["cried Meg.", "메그가 외쳤다."], ["\"I think too much of my looks, and hate to work, but won't any more, if I can help it.\"", "\"난 외모에 너무 신경 쓰고 일하는 걸 싫어하지만, 이제 어쩔 수 없는 경우가 아니면 안 그럴 거야.\""]], [["\"I'll try and be what he loves to call me, 'a little woman,'", "\"난 아빠가 즐겨 부르시는 그 이름, '작은 아씨'에 어울리는 사람이 되려고 애쓸래."], ["and not be rough and wild; but do my duty here instead of wanting to be somewhere else,\"", "거칠고 사납게 굴지 않고, 어디 다른 데 가 있고 싶어 하는 대신 여기서 내 본분을 다할 거야.\""], ["said Jo, thinking that keeping her temper at home was a much harder task than facing a rebel or two down South.", "조가 말했다. 그러면서 집에서 성질을 다스리는 게 남부에 가서 반란군 한두 명을 상대하는 것보다 훨씬 더 힘든 일이라고 생각했다."]], [["Beth said nothing, but wiped away her tears with the blue army-sock, and began to knit with all her might, losing no time in doing the duty that lay nearest her, while she resolved in her quiet little soul to be all that father hoped to find her when the year brought round the happy coming home.", "베스는 아무 말도 하지 않았지만 파란 군용 양말로 눈물을 훔치고는 온 힘을 다해 뜨개질을 하기 시작했다. 가장 가까이 놓인 본분을 하는 데 한순간도 허비하지 않으면서, 조용한 작은 마음속으로 다짐했다. 일 년이 돌고 돌아 행복한 귀향이 찾아올 때 아버지가 바라던 바로 그런 딸이 되어 있겠다고."]], [["Mrs.", "마치 부인은 조의 말 뒤에 이어진 침묵을 깨며 명랑한 목소리로 말했다."], ["March broke the silence that followed Jo's words, by saying in her cheery voice, \"Do you remember how you used to play Pilgrim's Progress when you were little things?", "\"너희 어렸을 때 「천로역정」 놀이를 어떻게 하곤 했는지 기억하니?"], ["Nothing delighted you more than to have me tie my piece-bags on your backs for burdens, give you hats and sticks and rolls of paper, and let you travel through the house from the cellar, which was the City of Destruction, up, up, to the house-top, where you had all the lovely things you could collect to make a Celestial City.\"", "엄마가 헝겊 자루를 짐 삼아 너희 등에 묶어 주고, 모자랑 막대기랑 둘둘 만 종이를 주고, 멸망의 도시인 지하실에서 출발해 위로, 위로, 천상의 도시를 꾸미려고 모을 수 있는 온갖 예쁜 것들이 다 있는 옥상까지 집 안을 누비게 해 주는 것보다 너희를 더 즐겁게 한 건 없었지.\""]], [["\"What fun it was, especially going by the lions, fighting Apollyon, and passing through the Valley where the hobgoblins were!\"", "\"정말 재밌었지, 특히 사자들 옆을 지나가고, 아폴리온이랑 싸우고, 도깨비들이 있는 골짜기를 통과하는 거 말이야!\""], ["said Jo.", "조가 말했다."]], [["\"I liked the place where the bundles fell off and tumbled down stairs,\"", "\"난 짐 보따리가 떨어져서 계단 아래로 굴러떨어지는 대목이 좋았어.\""], ["said Meg.", "메그가 말했다."]], [["\"My favorite part was when we came out on the flat roof where our flowers and arbors and pretty things were, and all stood and sung for joy up there in the sunshine,\"", "\"내가 제일 좋아하던 부분은 우리 꽃이랑 정자랑 예쁜 것들이 있는 평평한 옥상으로 나와서, 다 같이 서서 햇살 아래 기뻐서 노래 부르던 거야.\""], ["said Beth, smiling, as if that pleasant moment had come back to her.", "베스가, 그 즐거운 순간이 되살아난 듯 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["\"I don't remember much about it, except that I was afraid of the cellar and the dark entry, and always liked the cake and milk we had up at the top.", "\"난 별로 기억 안 나. 지하실이랑 어두운 복도가 무서웠던 거랑, 늘 꼭대기에 올라가서 먹던 케이크랑 우유가 좋았던 것밖에."], ["If I wasn't too old for such things, I'd rather like to play it over again,\"", "이런 놀이 하기엔 너무 나이가 든 게 아니라면 다시 한번 해 보고 싶긴 해.\""], ["said Amy, who began to talk of renouncing childish things at the mature age of twelve.", "에이미가 말했는데, 그녀는 열두 살이라는 원숙한 나이에 어린애 같은 것들을 졸업하는 이야기를 막 시작하는 참이었다."]], [["\"We never are too old for this, my dear, because it is a play we are playing all the time in one way or another.", "\"우린 결코 이걸 하기에 나이가 든 게 아니란다, 얘야. 이건 우리가 늘 이런저런 방식으로 하고 있는 놀이니까."], ["Our burdens are here, our road is before us, and the longing for goodness and happiness is the guide that leads us through many troubles and mistakes to the peace which is a true Celestial City.", "우리의 짐은 여기 있고, 우리의 길은 우리 앞에 놓여 있으며, 선함과 행복을 향한 갈망이야말로 수많은 어려움과 실수를 헤치고 진정한 천상의 도시인 평화로 우리를 이끄는 안내자란다."], ["Now, my little pilgrims, suppose you begin again, not in play, but in earnest, and see how far on you can get before father comes home.\"", "자, 나의 작은 순례자들아, 이번엔 놀이가 아니라 진심으로 다시 시작해서, 아버지가 집에 오시기 전까지 얼마나 멀리 나아갈 수 있는지 한번 보면 어떻겠니.\""]], [["\"Really, mother?", "\"정말요, 엄마?"], ["Where are our bundles?\"", "우리 짐 보따리는 어디 있어요?\""], ["asked Amy, who was a very literal young lady.", "매우 곧이곧대로 생각하는 에이미가 물었다."]], [["\"Each of you told what your burden was just now, except Beth; I rather think she hasn't got any,\"", "\"베스만 빼고 방금 너희 각자 자기 짐이 뭔지 말했잖니. 베스는 짐이 없는 것 같구나.\""], ["said her mother.", "어머니가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, I have; mine is dishes and dusters, and envying girls with nice pianos, and being afraid of people.\"", "\"아니에요, 저도 있어요. 제 짐은 설거지랑 먼지 떨이, 그리고 좋은 피아노를 가진 애들을 부러워하는 거랑, 사람들을 무서워하는 거예요.\""]], [["Beth's bundle was such a funny one that everybody wanted to laugh; but nobody did, for it would have hurt her feelings very much.", "베스의 짐 보따리가 어찌나 우스웠던지 다들 웃고 싶어 했다. 하지만 아무도 웃지 않았다. 그랬다간 베스의 마음을 몹시 아프게 할 테니까."]], [["\"Let us do it,\"", "\"우리 그걸 해 보자.\""], ["said Meg thoughtfully.", "메그가 사려 깊게 말했다."], ["\"It is only another name for trying to be good, and the story may help us; for though we do want to be good, it's hard work, and we forget, and don't do our best.\"", "\"그건 그저 착해지려고 애쓴다는 걸 달리 부르는 말일 뿐이고, 그 이야기가 우리한테 도움이 될지도 몰라. 우린 착해지고 싶어 하지만 그게 힘든 일이라 자꾸 잊어버리고 최선을 다하지 않으니까.\""]], [["\"We were in the Slough of Despond to-night, and mother came and pulled us out as Help did in the book.", "\"우린 오늘 밤 절망의 늪에 빠져 있었는데, 책에서 '도움'이라는 인물이 그랬던 것처럼 엄마가 와서 우릴 끌어내 주신 거야."], ["We ought to have our roll of directions, like Christian.", "크리스천처럼 우리한테도 두루마리 지침서가 있어야 해."], ["What shall we do about that?\"", "그건 어떻게 하지?\""], ["asked Jo, delighted with the fancy which lent a little romance to the very dull task of doing her duty.", "조가 물었는데, 본분을 다하는 그 따분하기 짝이 없는 일에 약간의 낭만을 입혀 주는 그 상상에 신이 나 있었다."]], [["\"Look under your pillows, Christmas morning, and you will find your guide-book,\"", "\"크리스마스 아침에 베개 밑을 들춰 보렴, 그럼 너희 안내서를 찾게 될 거야.\""], ["replied Mrs. March.", "마치 부인이 대답했다."]], [["They talked over the new plan while old Hannah cleared the table; then out came the four little work-baskets, and the needles flew as the girls made sheets for Aunt March.", "늙은 해나가 식탁을 치우는 동안 그들은 새로운 계획에 대해 이야기를 나누었다. 그러고 나서 네 개의 작은 반짇고리가 나왔고, 소녀들이 마치 대고모에게 드릴 시트를 만드느라 바늘이 바삐 움직였다."], ["It was uninteresting sewing, but to-night no one grumbled.", "따분한 바느질이었지만 오늘 밤은 아무도 투덜대지 않았다."], ["They adopted Jo's plan of dividing the long seams into four parts, and calling the quarters Europe, Asia, Africa, and America, and in that way got on capitally, especially when they talked about the different countries as they stitched their way through them.", "그들은 긴 솔기를 네 부분으로 나누어 그 사분면을 각각 유럽, 아시아, 아프리카, 아메리카라고 부르는 조의 방식을 받아들였고, 그렇게 하니 일이 척척 잘 풀렸다. 특히 그 나라들을 한 땀 한 땀 누비며 여러 나라에 관해 이야기를 나눌 때 더욱 그랬다."]], [["At nine they stopped work, and sung, as usual, before they went to bed.", "아홉 시가 되자 그들은 일손을 멈추고 잠자리에 들기 전에 늘 하던 대로 노래를 불렀다."], ["No one but Beth could get much music out of the old piano; but she had a way of softly touching the yellow keys, and making a pleasant accompaniment to the simple songs they sung.", "낡은 피아노에서 그럴듯한 음악을 끌어낼 수 있는 사람은 베스뿐이었다. 그녀는 누런 건반을 부드럽게 짚어 그들이 부르는 소박한 노래에 듣기 좋은 반주를 넣는 재주가 있었다."], ["Meg had a voice like a flute, and she and her mother led the little choir.", "메그는 플루트 같은 목소리를 지녔고, 그녀와 어머니가 그 작은 합창단을 이끌었다."], ["Amy chirped like a cricket, and Jo wandered through the airs at her own sweet will, always coming out at the wrong place with a croak or a quaver that spoilt the most pensive tune.", "에이미는 귀뚜라미처럼 재잘거렸고, 조는 제멋대로 곡조 사이를 헤매다가 늘 엉뚱한 데서 까악거리거나 떨리는 소리를 내어 더없이 애잔한 곡조도 망쳐 놓았다."], ["They had always done this from the time they could lisp", "그들은 혀 짧은 소리로"]], [["\"Crinkle, crinkle, 'ittle 'tar,\"", "\"반짝, 반짝, 자근 별아,\""]], [["and it had become a household custom, for the mother was a born singer.", "를 부르던 시절부터 늘 이렇게 노래해 왔고, 어머니가 타고난 가수였기에 그것은 집안의 관습이 되었다. 아침이면 가장 먼저 들리는 소리가 종달새처럼 노래하며 집 안을 오가는 어머니의 목소리였고, 밤이면 가장 마지막에 들리는 소리도 똑같이 그 명랑한 소리였다."], ["The first sound in the morning was her voice, as she went about the house singing like a lark; and the last sound at night was the same cheery sound, for the girls never grew too old for that familiar lullaby.", "소녀들은 그 정겨운 자장가를 듣기에 나이가 너무 든 적이 결코 없었으니까. 2."]], [["# A MERRY CHRISTMAS.", "# A MERRY CHRISTMAS."]], [["Jo was the first to wake in the gray dawn of Christmas morning.", "즐거운 크리스마스\n크리스마스 아침, 잿빛 새벽에 제일 먼저 잠에서 깬 건 조였다."], ["No stockings hung at the fireplace, and for a moment she felt as much disappointed as she did long ago, when her little sock fell down because it was so crammed with goodies.", "난롯가에 양말은 걸려 있지 않았고, 잠시 동안 조는 오래전 양말에 맛난 것을 너무 가득 채운 나머지 작은 양말이 떨어졌을 때만큼이나 실망스러웠다."], ["Then she remembered her mother's promise, and, slipping her hand under her pillow, drew out a little crimson-covered book.", "그러다 어머니의 약속이 떠올라 베개 밑으로 손을 넣어 진홍색 표지의 작은 책을 한 권 꺼냈다. 조는 그 책을 아주 잘 알았다."], ["She knew it very well, for it was that beautiful old story of the best life ever lived, and Jo felt that it was a true guide-book for any pilgrim going the long journey.", "일찍이 살았던 가장 훌륭한 삶을 담은 그 아름답고 오래된 이야기였으니까. 조는 그 책이 먼 길을 떠나는 순례자라면 누구에게나 진정한 안내서가 되리라고 느꼈다."], ["She woke Meg with a \"Merry Christmas,\" and bade her see what was under her pillow.", "조는 \"메리 크리스마스\"라고 외치며 메그를 깨우고는 베개 밑을 살펴보라고 했다."], ["A green-covered book appeared, with the same picture inside, and a few words written by their mother, which made their one present very precious in their eyes.", "똑같은 그림이 안에 들어 있는 초록색 표지의 책이 나왔고, 어머니가 적어 놓은 몇 마디 글귀가 있어 그들의 하나뿐인 선물을 더없이 소중하게 만들었다."], ["Presently Beth and Amy woke, to rummage and find their little books also,--one dove-colored, the other blue; and all sat looking at and talking about them, while the east grew rosy with the coming day.", "곧이어 베스와 에이미도 깨어나 뒤적여서 자기들의 작은 책을 찾았는데, 하나는 비둘기색이고 다른 하나는 파란색이었다. 다들 앉아서 그 책들을 들여다보고 이야기를 나누는 동안 동녘이 밝아 오는 햇살로 발그레해졌다."]], [["In spite of her small vanities, Margaret had a sweet and pious nature, which unconsciously influenced her sisters, especially Jo, who loved her very tenderly, and obeyed her because her advice was so gently given.", "마거릿은 자잘한 허영심이 있긴 했지만 다정하고 경건한 성품을 지녔고, 그 성품이 자기도 모르게 동생들에게, 특히 조에게 영향을 미쳤다. 조는 마거릿을 무척이나 다정하게 사랑했고, 그녀의 충고가 워낙 부드럽게 주어졌기에 그 말을 잘 따랐다."]], [["\"Girls,\"", "\"얘들아,\""], ["said Meg seriously, looking from the tumbled head beside her to the two little night-capped ones in the room beyond, \"mother wants us to read and love and mind these books, and we must begin at once.", "메그가 옆에 헝클어진 머리를 한 동생에서 저쪽 방에 잠자리 모자를 쓴 두 동생에게로 눈길을 옮기며 진지하게 말했다. \"엄마는 우리가 이 책들을 읽고 사랑하고 마음에 새기길 바라셔."], ["We used to be faithful about it; but since father went away, and all this war trouble unsettled us, we have neglected many things.", "그러니 우리 당장 시작해야 해. 예전엔 우리도 이걸 충실히 했지만, 아버지가 떠나시고 이 전쟁 때문에 다들 마음이 어수선해지면서 많은 걸 소홀히 했잖아."], ["You can do as you please; but _I_ shall keep my book on the table here, and read a little every morning as soon as I wake, for I know it will do me good, and help me through the day.\"", "너희야 너희 좋을 대로 해도 되지만, 나는 내 책을 여기 탁자에 놓아두고 매일 아침 깨자마자 조금씩 읽을 거야. 그게 나한테 도움이 되고 하루를 잘 보내게 해 줄 거란 걸 아니까.\""]], [["Then she opened her new book and began to read.", "그러고 나서 메그는 새 책을 펴서 읽기 시작했다."], ["Jo put her arm round her, and, leaning cheek to cheek, read also, with the quiet expression so seldom seen on her restless face.", "조는 메그에게 팔을 두르고 뺨을 맞댄 채, 그 안절부절못하는 얼굴에서는 좀처럼 보기 힘든 차분한 표정으로 함께 읽었다."]], [["\"How good Meg is!", "\"메그 언니는 정말 착해!"], ["Come, Amy, let's do as they do.", "자, 에이미, 우리도 언니들처럼 하자."], ["I'll help you with the hard words, and they'll explain things if we don't understand,\"", "어려운 단어는 내가 도와주고, 이해 안 되는 건 언니들이 설명해 줄 거야.\""], ["whispered Beth, very much impressed by the pretty books and her sisters' example.", "베스가 그 예쁜 책들과 언니들의 본보기에 크게 감동받아 속삭였다."]], [["\"I'm glad mine is blue,\"", "\"내 건 파란색이라 좋아.\""], ["said Amy; and then the rooms were very still while the pages were softly turned, and the winter sunshine crept in to touch the bright heads and serious faces with a Christmas greeting.", "에이미가 말했다. 그러고는 책장이 부드럽게 넘어가고 겨울 햇살이 살며시 스며들어 그 밝은 머리들과 진지한 얼굴들에 크리스마스 인사를 건네는 동안, 방 안은 아주 고요해졌다."]], [["\"Where is mother?\"", "\"엄마 어디 계셔?\""], ["asked Meg, as she and Jo ran down to thank her for their gifts, half an hour later.", "삼십 분쯤 뒤에 메그가 선물에 대한 고마움을 전하려고 조와 함께 아래층으로 뛰어 내려오며 물었다."]], [["\"Goodness only knows.", "\"그건 하느님만 아시지."], ["Some poor creeter come a-beggin', and your ma went straight off to see what was needed.", "어떤 가엾은 사람이 동냥하러 왔는데, 너희 엄마가 곧장 나가서 뭐가 필요한지 보러 가셨어."], ["There never _was_ such a woman for givin' away vittles and drink, clothes and firin',\"", "먹을 거 마실 거 옷가지 땔감을 그렇게 퍼 주는 사람은 또 없을 거다.\""], ["replied Hannah, who had lived with the family since Meg was born, and was considered by them all more as a friend than a servant.", "해나가 대답했다. 그녀는 메그가 태어났을 때부터 이 집과 함께 살아온 사람으로, 식구들 모두 그녀를 하인이라기보다는 친구로 여겼다."]], [["\"She will be back soon, I think; so fry your cakes, and have everything ready,\"", "\"엄마 곧 돌아오실 거야. 그러니까 케이크를 굽고 모든 걸 준비해 놓자.\""], ["said Meg, looking over the presents which were collected in a basket and kept under the sofa, ready to be produced at the proper time.", "메그가, 바구니에 모아 소파 밑에 넣어 두었다가 때가 되면 꺼낼 선물들을 살펴보며 말했다."], ["\"Why, where is Amy's bottle of cologne?\"", "\"그런데 에이미의 오드콜로뉴 병은 어디 있지?\""], ["she added, as the little flask did not appear.", "그 작은 병이 보이지 않자 그녀가 덧붙였다."]], [["\"She took it out a minute ago, and went off with it to put a ribbon on it, or some such notion,\"", "\"좀 전에 꺼내 가던데. 리본을 묶는다나 뭐라나 하면서.\""], ["replied Jo, dancing about the room to take the first stiffness off the new army-slippers.", "조가 새 군용 슬리퍼의 뻣뻣함을 처음으로 풀어 보려고 방 안을 춤추듯 돌아다니며 대답했다."]], [["\"How nice my handkerchiefs look, don't they?", "\"내 손수건들 예쁘지 않아?"], ["Hannah washed and ironed them for me, and I marked them all myself,\"", "해나가 빨아서 다려 줬고, 글자는 내가 다 직접 새겼어.\""], ["said Beth, looking proudly at the somewhat uneven letters which had cost her such labor.", "베스가 그토록 애써서 새긴, 다소 들쭉날쭉한 글자들을 자랑스럽게 바라보며 말했다."]], [["\"Bless the child!", "\"어머나, 얘 좀 봐!"], ["she's gone and put 'Mother'", "'M."], ["on them instead of 'M. March.'", "마치'가 아니라 '어머니'라고 새겨 놨네."], ["How funny!\"", "정말 우습다!\""], ["cried Jo, taking up one.", "조가 손수건 하나를 집어 들며 외쳤다."]], [["\"Isn't it right?", "\"이게 맞는 거 아니야?"], ["I thought it was better to do it so, because Meg's initials are 'M.", "이렇게 하는 게 더 좋을 것 같았어. 메그 언니 이니셜도 'M."], ["M.,' and I don't want any one to use these but Marmee,\"", "M.'이잖아, 그리고 이건 엄마 말고 아무도 안 썼으면 좋겠거든.\""], ["said Beth, looking troubled.", "베스가 곤란한 표정으로 말했다."]], [["\"It's all right, dear, and a very pretty idea,--quite sensible, too, for no one can ever mistake now.", "\"맞아, 얘야, 아주 예쁜 생각이야. 게다가 아주 분별 있기도 하고."], ["It will please her very much, I know,\"", "이제 누구도 헷갈리지 않을 테니까. 엄마가 무척 좋아하실 거야, 틀림없어.\""], ["said Meg, with a frown for Jo and a smile for Beth.", "메그가 조에게는 눈살을 찌푸리고 베스에게는 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["\"There's mother.", "\"엄마 오신다."], ["Hide the basket, quick!\"", "바구니 어서 숨겨!\""], ["cried Jo, as a door slammed, and steps sounded in the hall.", "문이 쾅 닫히고 복도에서 발소리가 들리자 조가 외쳤다."]], [["Amy came in hastily, and looked rather abashed when she saw her sisters all waiting for her.", "에이미가 황급히 들어왔는데, 언니들이 모두 자기를 기다리고 있는 걸 보고는 다소 무안한 표정을 지었다."]], [["\"Where have you been, and what are you hiding behind you?\"", "\"어디 갔다 왔어, 그리고 등 뒤에 감춘 건 뭐야?\" 메그가 물었다."], ["asked Meg, surprised to see, by her hood and cloak, that lazy Amy had been out so early.", "게으른 에이미가 두건과 망토 차림인 걸 보고 그렇게 일찍 밖에 나갔다 온 게 놀라웠던 것이다."]], [["\"Don't laugh at me, Jo!", "\"비웃지 마, 조!"], ["I didn't mean any one should know till the time came.", "때가 될 때까진 아무도 몰랐으면 했어."], ["I only meant to change the little bottle for a big one, and I gave _all_ my money to get it, and I'm truly trying not to be selfish any more.\"", "난 그저 작은 병을 큰 걸로 바꾸고 싶었던 것뿐이야, 그래서 내 돈을 다 털어서 샀어. 난 정말 더는 이기적으로 굴지 않으려고 애쓰는 중이야.\""]], [["As she spoke, Amy showed the handsome flask which replaced the cheap one; and looked so earnest and humble in her little effort to forget herself that Meg hugged her on the spot, and Jo pronounced her \"a trump,\"", "에이미는 말하면서, 싸구려 병을 대신한 멋진 병을 내보였다."], ["while Beth ran to the window, and picked her finest rose to ornament the stately bottle.", "자기를 잊으려는 그 작은 노력 속에 너무나 진지하고 겸손해 보여서 메그는 그 자리에서 동생을 껴안았고, 조는 그녀를 \"멋진 녀석\"이라고 추켜세웠으며, 베스는 창가로 달려가 그 근사한 병을 장식할 가장 고운 장미 한 송이를 꺾어 왔다."]], [["\"You see I felt ashamed of my present, after reading and talking about being good this morning, so I ran round the corner and changed it the minute I was up: and I'm _so_ glad, for mine is the handsomest now.\"", "\"있잖아, 오늘 아침에 착해지자는 얘기를 읽고 나누고 나니까 내 선물이 부끄러웠어. 그래서 일어나자마자 모퉁이로 달려가서 바로 바꿔 왔지. 그래서 정말 기뻐, 이제 내 선물이 제일 멋지니까.\""]], [["Another bang of the street-door sent the basket under the sofa, and the girls to the table, eager for breakfast.", "다시 한번 현관문이 쾅 닫히는 소리에 바구니는 소파 밑으로 들어갔고, 소녀들은 아침을 먹고 싶어 안달하며 탁자로 모여들었다."]], [["\"Merry Christmas, Marmee!", "\"메리 크리스마스, 엄마! 앞으로도 즐거운 크리스마스 많이 맞으세요!"], ["Many of them!", "책 고마워요."], ["Thank you for our books; we read some, and mean to every day,\"", "좀 읽었고, 매일 읽을 거예요.\""], ["they cried, in chorus.", "그들이 입을 모아 외쳤다."]], [["\"Merry Christmas, little daughters!", "\"메리 크리스마스, 우리 딸들!"], ["I'm glad you began at once, and hope you will keep on.", "당장 시작했다니 기쁘구나, 앞으로도 계속하길 바란다."], ["But I want to say one word before we sit down.", "그런데 앉기 전에 한마디 하고 싶구나."], ["Not far away from here lies a poor woman with a little new-born baby.", "여기서 멀지 않은 곳에 갓 태어난 아기를 둔 가엾은 여인이 누워 있단다."], ["Six children are huddled into one bed to keep from freezing, for they have no fire.", "불기운이 없어서 아이 여섯이 얼어 죽지 않으려고 한 침대에 옹기종기 모여 있어."], ["There is nothing to eat over there; and the oldest boy came to tell me they were suffering hunger and cold.", "거기엔 먹을 게 하나도 없는데, 큰애가 와서 자기들이 추위와 굶주림에 시달리고 있다고 말해 주더구나."], ["My girls, will you give them your breakfast as a Christmas present?\"", "우리 딸들아, 너희 아침을 그 사람들한테 크리스마스 선물로 주겠니?\""]], [["They were all unusually hungry, having waited nearly an hour, and for a minute no one spoke; only a minute, for Jo exclaimed impetuously,--", "다들 거의 한 시간이나 기다린 터라 유난히 배가 고팠고, 잠깐 동안 아무도 말이 없었다. 정말 잠깐이었다. 조가 곧 충동적으로 외쳤으니까."]], [["\"I'm so glad you came before we began!\"", "\"우리가 먹기 전에 엄마가 와 줘서 정말 다행이에요!\""]], [["\"May I go and help carry the things to the poor little children?\"", "\"가서 그 가엾은 아이들한테 음식 나르는 걸 도와도 될까요?\""], ["asked Beth, eagerly.", "베스가 간절하게 물었다."]], [["\"_I_ shall take the cream and the muffins,\"", "\"난 크림이랑 머핀을 가져갈래요.\""], ["added Amy, heroically giving up the articles she most liked.", "에이미가, 자기가 제일 좋아하는 것들을 영웅처럼 내놓으며 덧붙였다."]], [["Meg was already covering the buckwheats, and piling the bread into one big plate.", "메그는 어느새 메밀 케이크를 덮고 빵을 큰 접시 하나에 차곡차곡 쌓고 있었다."]], [["\"I thought you'd do it,\"", "\"너희라면 그럴 줄 알았다.\""], ["said Mrs. March, smiling as if satisfied.", "마치 부인이 흡족한 듯 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["\"You shall all go and help me, and when we come back we will have bread and milk for breakfast, and make it up at dinner-time.\"", "\"다 같이 가서 엄마를 도와주렴. 그리고 돌아와서는 빵하고 우유로 아침을 먹고, 점심때 보충하자꾸나.\""]], [["They were soon ready, and the procession set out.", "그들은 곧 채비를 마쳤고, 행렬이 출발했다."], ["Fortunately it was early, and they went through back streets, so few people saw them, and no one laughed at the queer party.", "다행히 이른 시각이었고 뒷골목을 지나갔기 때문에 본 사람이 거의 없었고, 그 이상한 무리를 비웃는 이도 없었다."]], [["A poor, bare, miserable room it was, with broken windows, no fire, ragged bed-clothes, a sick mother, wailing baby, and a group of pale, hungry children cuddled under one old quilt, trying to keep warm.", "그곳은 가난하고 휑하니 비참한 방이었다. 창문은 깨져 있고, 불기운도 없으며, 침구는 너덜너덜했고, 병든 어머니와 보채는 아기, 그리고 따뜻한 온기를 지키려고 낡은 누비이불 하나 밑에 옹기종기 모인 창백하고 굶주린 아이들 한 무리가 있었다."], ["How the big eyes stared and the blue lips smiled as the girls went in!", "소녀들이 들어서자 그 큰 눈들이 얼마나 휘둥그레지고 새파란 입술들이 얼마나 환하게 웃던지!"]], [["\"Ach, mein Gott!", "\"아, 마인 고트!"], ["it is good angels come to us!\"", "우리한테 착한 천사들이 왔구나!\""], ["said the poor woman, crying for joy.", "가엾은 여인이 기뻐서 울며 말했다."]], [["\"Funny angels in hoods and mittens,\"", "\"두건 쓰고 벙어리장갑 낀 우스운 천사들이죠.\""], ["said Jo, and set them laughing.", "조가 말하자 다들 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["In a few minutes it really did seem as if kind spirits had been at work there.", "몇 분 만에 정말로 그곳에서 친절한 정령들이 일을 한 것만 같았다."], ["Hannah, who had carried wood, made a fire, and stopped up the broken panes with old hats and her own cloak.", "장작을 날라 온 해나는 불을 피우고 깨진 유리창을 낡은 모자들과 자기 망토로 틀어막았다."], ["Mrs. March gave the mother tea and gruel, and comforted her with promises of help, while she dressed the little baby as tenderly as if it had been her own.", "마치 부인은 어머니에게 차와 죽을 주고 도와주겠다는 약속으로 위로하면서, 마치 제 아기인 양 다정하게 갓난아기에게 옷을 입혔다."], ["The girls, meantime, spread the table, set the children round the fire, and fed them like so many hungry birds,--laughing, talking, and trying to understand the funny broken English.", "그동안 소녀들은 식탁을 차리고 아이들을 불가에 둘러앉힌 다음, 굶주린 새 떼처럼 그들을 먹였다. 웃고 떠들고, 우스꽝스럽게 더듬거리는 영어를 알아들으려 애쓰면서."]], [["\"Das ist gut!\"", "\"다스 이스트 구트!\""], ["\"Die Engel-kinder!\"", "\"디 엥겔킨더!\""], ["cried the poor things, as they ate, and warmed their purple hands at the comfortable blaze.", "가엾은 아이들이 먹으면서, 또 시퍼레진 손을 그 따뜻한 불길에 녹이면서 외쳤다."]], [["The girls had never been called angel children before, and thought it very agreeable, especially Jo, who had been considered a \"Sancho\" ever since she was born.", "소녀들은 전에 천사 아이들이라고 불려 본 적이 없었기에 그게 무척 흐뭇했고, 특히 태어났을 때부터 \"산초\"로 여겨져 온 조가 그러했다."], ["That was a very happy breakfast, though they didn't get any of it; and when they went away, leaving comfort behind, I think there were not in all the city four merrier people than the hungry little girls who gave away their breakfasts and contented themselves with bread and milk on Christmas morning.", "비록 그들은 한 입도 먹지 못했지만 더없이 행복한 아침이었다. 그리고 그들이 온기를 남겨 두고 돌아왔을 때, 크리스마스 아침에 자기 아침을 내주고 빵과 우유로 만족한 그 배고픈 어린 소녀들보다 더 즐거운 사람은 온 도시를 통틀어 넷도 없었으리라."]], [["\"That's loving our neighbor better than ourselves, and I like it,\"", "\"이게 바로 우리 자신보다 이웃을 더 사랑하는 거야, 난 이게 좋아.\""], ["said Meg, as they set out their presents, while their mother was upstairs collecting clothes for the poor Hummels.", "메그가, 어머니가 위층에서 가엾은 후멜네 식구들에게 줄 옷가지를 챙기는 동안 선물들을 차려 놓으며 말했다."]], [["Not a very splendid show, but there was a great deal of love done up in the few little bundles; and the tall vase of red roses, white chrysanthemums, and trailing vines, which stood in the middle, gave quite an elegant air to the table.", "그리 대단한 구경거리는 아니었지만, 그 몇 안 되는 작은 꾸러미들 속에는 엄청난 사랑이 담겨 있었다. 그리고 한가운데 놓인, 빨간 장미와 흰 국화, 늘어진 덩굴이 어우러진 키 큰 꽃병이 탁자에 제법 우아한 분위기를 더해 주었다."]], [["\"She's coming!", "\"엄마 오신다!"], ["Strike up, Beth!", "시작해, 베스!"], ["Open the door, Amy!", "문 열어, 에이미!"], ["Three cheers for Marmee!\"", "엄마 만세!\""], ["cried Jo, prancing about, while Meg went to conduct mother to the seat of honor.", "조가 깡충거리며 외쳤고, 메그는 어머니를 상석으로 모시러 갔다."]], [["Beth played her gayest march, Amy threw open the door, and Meg enacted escort with great dignity. Mrs.", "베스는 가장 흥겨운 행진곡을 연주했고, 에이미는 문을 활짝 열었으며, 메그는 더없이 위엄 있게 호위 역할을 해냈다."], ["March was both surprised and touched; and smiled with her eyes full as she examined her presents, and read the little notes which accompanied them.", "마치 부인은 놀라면서도 감동했다. 선물들을 살펴보고 거기 딸린 작은 쪽지들을 읽으며 눈에 가득 눈물이 고인 채 미소 지었다."], ["The slippers went on at once, a new handkerchief was slipped into her pocket, well scented with Amy's cologne, the rose was fastened in her bosom, and the nice gloves were pronounced a \"perfect fit.\"", "슬리퍼는 곧장 신었고, 에이미의 오드콜로뉴 향이 그윽한 새 손수건은 주머니에 넣었으며, 장미는 가슴에 꽂았고, 그 멋진 장갑은 \"꼭 맞는다\"는 평을 받았다."]], [["There was a good deal of laughing and kissing and explaining, in the simple, loving fashion which makes these home-festivals so pleasant at the time, so sweet to remember long afterward, and then all fell to work.", "이런 가정 축제를 그 순간에는 그토록 즐겁게, 또 오랜 세월이 지난 뒤에도 그토록 달콤하게 떠올리게 하는 소박하고 다정한 방식으로, 웃고 입 맞추고 설명하는 일이 한참 이어졌다. 그러고 나서 다들 일에 매달렸다."]], [["The morning charities and ceremonies took so much time that the rest of the day was devoted to preparations for the evening festivities.", "아침의 자선과 의식이 너무 많은 시간을 잡아먹은 탓에, 그날 남은 시간은 저녁의 축제 준비에 바쳐졌다."], ["Being still too young to go often to the theatre, and not rich enough to afford any great outlay for private performances, the girls put their wits to work, and--necessity being the mother of invention,--made whatever they needed.", "극장에 자주 가기엔 아직 너무 어렸고, 사적인 공연에 큰돈을 쓸 만큼 부유하지도 못했기에, 소녀들은 머리를 짜냈고 — 필요는 발명의 어머니라고 — 필요한 것은 무엇이든 손수 만들었다."], ["Very clever were some of their productions,--pasteboard guitars, antique lamps made of old-fashioned butter-boats covered with silver paper, gorgeous robes of old cotton, glittering with tin spangles from a pickle factory, and armor covered with the same useful diamond-shaped bits, left in sheets when the lids of tin preserve-pots were cut out.", "그들의 제작품 중에는 제법 기발한 것들이 있었다. 판지로 만든 기타, 은박지를 입힌 구식 버터 그릇으로 만든 골동품 램프, 절임 공장에서 나온 양철 장식 조각을 반짝반짝 붙인 낡은 무명천으로 만든 호화로운 의상, 그리고 양철 저장 단지 뚜껑을 도려낼 때 판으로 남는 똑같은 마름모꼴 조각들을 붙인 갑옷이 그러했다."], ["The furniture was used to being turned topsy-turvy, and the big chamber was the scene of many innocent revels.", "가구는 늘 위아래로 뒤집히곤 했고, 그 큰 방은 수많은 천진한 잔치의 무대가 되었다."]], [["No gentlemen were admitted; so Jo played male parts to her heart's content, and took immense satisfaction in a pair of russet-leather boots given her by a friend, who knew a lady who knew an actor.", "남자는 출입 금지였다. 그래서 조는 마음껏 남자 역할을 맡았고, 어느 친구가 준 적갈색 가죽 부츠 한 켤레에 무한한 만족을 느꼈다."], ["These boots, an old foil, and a slashed doublet once used by an artist for some picture, were Jo's chief treasures, and appeared on all occasions.", "그 친구는 어느 배우를 아는 어느 부인을 알았던 것이다. 이 부츠와 낡은 펜싱 검 한 자루, 그리고 예전에 어느 화가가 그림을 그릴 때 쓴 적이 있는 칼집 낸 더블릿이 조의 가장 큰 보물이었고, 모든 무대에 등장했다."], ["The smallness of the company made it necessary for the two principal actors to take several parts apiece; and they certainly deserved some credit for the hard work they did in learning three or four different parts, whisking in and out of various costumes, and managing the stage besides.", "출연진이 적다 보니 두 주연 배우가 각각 여러 역할을 맡아야 했다. 그들은 서너 가지 다른 배역을 외우고, 갖가지 의상을 후딱후딱 갈아입고, 게다가 무대까지 관리하느라 무던히 애를 썼으니 정말이지 칭찬받을 만했다."], ["It was excellent drill for their memories, a harmless amusement, and employed many hours which otherwise would have been idle, lonely, or spent in less profitable society.", "그건 그들의 기억력에 훌륭한 훈련이 되었고, 해롭지 않은 오락이었으며, 그러지 않았다면 한가하거나 외롭거나 덜 유익한 사람들과 어울리며 보냈을 많은 시간을 채워 주었다."]], [["On Christmas night, a dozen girls piled on to the bed which was the dress-circle, and sat before the blue and yellow chintz curtains in a most flattering state of expectancy.", "크리스마스 밤, 특별석인 침대 위로 열두어 명의 소녀가 우르르 올라가 앉아, 파란색과 노란색 사라사 무명 커튼 앞에서 더없이 흐뭇한 기대에 잠겨 있었다."], ["There was a good deal of rustling and whispering behind the curtain, a trifle of lamp-smoke, and an occasional giggle from Amy, who was apt to get hysterical in the excitement of the moment.", "커튼 뒤에서는 부스럭거리고 속닥거리는 소리, 약간의 램프 그을음, 그리고 그 순간의 흥분에 곧잘 발작적으로 들뜨곤 하는 에이미의 이따금씩 터지는 킥킥대는 웃음소리가 났다."], ["Presently a bell sounded, the curtains flew apart, and the Operatic Tragedy began.", "이윽고 종이 울리고, 커튼이 양쪽으로 휙 갈라지면서 오페라 비극이 시작되었다."]], [["\"A gloomy wood,\" according to the one play-bill, was represented by a few shrubs in pots, green baize on the floor, and a cave in the distance.", "한 장짜리 공연 안내문에 따른 \"음울한 숲\"은 화분 몇 개의 관목과 바닥에 깐 초록색 모직천, 그리고 저 멀리 동굴로 표현되었다."], ["This cave was made with a clothes-horse for a roof, bureaus for walls; and in it was a small furnace in full blast, with a black pot on it, and an old witch bending over it.", "이 동굴은 빨래 건조대를 지붕으로, 서랍장들을 벽으로 삼아 만들어졌다. 그 안에는 작은 화로가 활활 타오르며 위에 검은 솥이 얹혀 있었고, 늙은 마녀가 그 위로 몸을 굽히고 있었다."], ["The stage was dark, and the glow of the furnace had a fine effect, especially as real steam issued from the kettle when the witch took off the cover.", "무대는 어두웠고, 화로의 불빛은 멋진 효과를 냈는데, 특히 마녀가 뚜껑을 열자 솥에서 진짜 김이 피어오를 때 그러했다. 첫 전율이 가라앉을 잠깐의 틈이 주어졌다."], ["A moment was allowed for the first thrill to subside; then Hugo, the villain, stalked in with a clanking sword at his side, a slouched hat, black beard, mysterious cloak, and the boots.", "그러고는 악당 휴고가 옆구리에 절그럭거리는 검을 차고, 챙 늘어진 모자에 검은 수염, 신비로운 망토, 그리고 그 부츠를 신은 채 성큼성큼 들어왔다."], ["After pacing to and fro in much agitation, he struck his forehead, and burst out in a wild strain, singing of his hatred to Roderigo, his love for Zara, and his pleasing resolution to kill the one and win the other.", "몹시 흥분한 채 이리저리 거닐던 그는 이마를 탁 치더니, 격렬한 가락으로 로드리고에 대한 증오와 자라에 대한 사랑, 그리고 하나는 죽이고 다른 하나는 차지하겠다는 흐뭇한 결심을 노래하며 터뜨렸다. 휴고의 굵직한 목소리는, 감정이 북받칠 때 이따금 터지는 고함과 더불어 무척 인상적이었고, 그가 숨을 고르려 멈추는 순간 관객들은 박수를 보냈다."], ["The gruff tones of Hugo's voice, with an occasional shout when his feelings overcame him, were very impressive, and the audience applauded the moment he paused for breath.", "대중의 갈채에 익숙한 사람 특유의 분위기로 고개 숙여 인사한 그는 살금살금 동굴로 다가가, \"여봐라, 종복아! 네가 필요하다!\"라고 위엄 있게 명하며 하가르에게 나오라고 했다."], ["Bowing with the air of one accustomed to public praise, he stole to the cavern, and ordered Hagar to come forth with a commanding \"What ho, minion!", "회색 말털을 얼굴에 늘어뜨리고, 빨강과 검정이 어우러진 가운에 지팡이를 들고, 망토에 신비로운 부호들을 그려 넣은 메그가 나왔다. 휴고는 자라가 자기를 흠모하게 만들 묘약 하나와 로드리고를 없앨 묘약 하나를 요구했다."], ["I need thee!\"", "하가르는 멋들어진 극적인 가락으로 둘 다 주겠노라 약속하고는, 사랑의 묘약을 가져올 정령을 불러내기 시작했다."]], [["Out came Meg, with gray horse-hair hanging about her face, a red and black robe, a staff, and cabalistic signs upon her cloak. Hugo demanded a potion to make Zara adore him, and one to destroy Roderigo. Hagar, in a fine dramatic melody, promised both, and proceeded to call up the spirit who would bring the love philter:--", "\"이리로, 이리로, 네 집에서,\n바람의 정령이여, 너를 부르노라!"]], [["\"Hither, hither, from thy home, Airy sprite, I bid thee come! Born of roses, fed on dew, Charms and potions canst thou brew? Bring me here, with elfin speed, The fragrant philter which I need; Make it sweet and swift and strong, Spirit, answer now my song!\"", "장미에게서 태어나 이슬을 먹는 너,\n마법과 묘약을 빚을 줄 아느냐?"]], [["A soft strain of music sounded, and then at the back of the cave appeared a little figure in cloudy white, with glittering wings, golden hair, and a garland of roses on its head. Waving a wand, it sang,--", "요정의 빠르기로 내게 가져오라,\n내가 필요로 하는 향기로운 묘약을."]], [["\"Hither I come, From my airy home, Afar in the silver moon. Take the magic spell, And use it well, Or its power will vanish soon!\"", "달콤하고 빠르고 강하게 만들지니,\n정령이여, 이제 내 노래에 답하라!\""]], [["And, dropping a small, gilded bottle at the witch's feet, the spirit vanished. Another chant from Hagar produced another apparition,--not a lovely one; for, with a bang, an ugly black imp appeared, and, having croaked a reply, tossed a dark bottle at Hugo, and disappeared with a mocking laugh.", "부드러운 음악 가락이 울리더니, 동굴 뒤편에 반짝이는 날개와 금빛 머리, 머리에 장미 화관을 두른, 뽀얀 흰빛의 작은 형체가 나타났다."], ["Having warbled his thanks and put the potions in his boots, Hugo departed; and Hagar informed the audience that, as he had killed a few of her friends in times past, she has cursed him, and intends to thwart his plans, and be revenged on him.", "지팡이를 흔들며 그것이 노래했다."], ["Then the curtain fell, and the audience reposed and ate candy while discussing the merits of the play.", "\"이리로 내가 왔노라,"]], [["A good deal of hammering went on before the curtain rose again; but when it became evident what a masterpiece of stage-carpentering had been got up, no one murmured at the delay. It was truly superb! A tower rose to the ceiling; half-way up appeared a window, with a lamp burning at it, and behind the white curtain appeared Zara in a lovely blue and silver dress, waiting for Roderigo. He came in gorgeous array, with plumed cap, red cloak, chestnut love-locks, a guitar, and the boots, of course. Kneeling at the foot of the tower, he sang a serenade in melting tones. Zara replied, and, after a musical dialogue, consented to fly. Then came the grand effect of the play. Roderigo produced a rope-ladder, with five steps to it, threw up one end, and invited Zara to descend. Timidly she crept from her lattice, put her hand on Roderigo's shoulder, and was about to leap gracefully down, when, \"Alas! alas for Zara!\" she forgot her train,--it caught in the window; the tower tottered, leaned forward, fell with a crash, and buried the unhappy lovers in the ruins!", "저 멀리 은빛 달에 있는\n바람의 내 집에서."]], [["A universal shriek arose as the russet boots waved wildly from the wreck, and a golden head emerged, exclaiming, \"I told you so! I told you so!\" With wonderful presence of mind, Don Pedro, the cruel sire, rushed in, dragged out his daughter, with a hasty aside,--", "이 마법의 주문을 받아\n잘 쓰도록 하라,"]], [["\"Don't laugh! Act as if it was all right!\"--and, ordering Roderigo up, banished him from the kingdom with wrath and scorn.", "안 그러면 그 힘이 곧 사라지리니!\""], ["Though decidedly shaken by the fall of the tower upon him, Roderigo defied the old gentleman, and refused to stir.", "그러고는 작은 금빛 병을 마녀의 발치에 떨어뜨린 채 정령은 사라졌다. 하가르의 또 다른 주문이 또 다른 환영을 불러냈는데 — 이번엔 사랑스러운 게 아니었다."], ["This dauntless example fired Zara: she also defied her sire, and he ordered them both to the deepest dungeons of the castle.", "쾅 하는 소리와 함께 흉측한 검은 도깨비가 나타나, 깍깍대며 대답하더니 검은 병 하나를 휴고에게 던지고는 조롱하는 웃음과 함께 사라졌으니까. 휴고는 노래하듯 고마움을 읊고 묘약들을 부츠 속에 넣고는 떠났다."], ["A stout little retainer came in with chains, and led them away, looking very much frightened, and evidently forgetting the speech he ought to have made.", "그리고 하가르는 그가 예전에 자기 친구 몇을 죽인 탓에 자기가 그에게 저주를 걸어 두었고, 그의 계획을 좌절시키고 복수할 작정이라고 관객에게 알렸다. 그런 다음 막이 내렸고, 관객들은 쉬면서 사탕을 먹으며 연극의 장점에 대해 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["Act third was the castle hall; and here Hagar appeared, having come to free the lovers and finish Hugo. She hears him coming, and hides; sees him put the potions into two cups of wine, and bid the timid little servant \"Bear them to the captives in their cells, and tell them I shall come anon.\" The servant takes Hugo aside to tell him something, and Hagar changes the cups for two others which are harmless. Ferdinando, the \"minion,\" carries them away, and Hagar puts back the cup which holds the poison meant for Roderigo. Hugo, getting thirsty after a long warble, drinks it, loses his wits, and, after a good deal of clutching and stamping, falls flat and dies; while Hagar informs him what she has done in a song of exquisite power and melody.", "막이 다시 오르기 전에 한참 망치질 소리가 났다. 하지만 얼마나 대단한 무대 목공 걸작이 마련되었는지가 드러나자 아무도 그 지체에 대해 투덜거리지 않았다. 정말이지 장관이었다! 탑 하나가 천장까지 솟아올랐고, 그 중간쯤에 램프가 타오르는 창이 나타났으며, 흰 커튼 뒤에는 사랑스러운 파란색과 은색 드레스를 입은 자라가 로드리고를 기다리고 있었다. 그가 깃털 장식 모자에 빨간 망토, 밤색 애교머리, 기타, 그리고 물론 그 부츠까지 갖춘 화려한 차림으로 들어왔다. 탑 아래에 무릎을 꿇은 그는 애간장 녹는 가락으로 세레나데를 불렀다. 자라가 화답했고, 음악 같은 대화 끝에 함께 도망치기로 했다. 그러고는 연극의 절정 효과가 펼쳐졌다. 로드리고가 다섯 단짜리 밧줄 사다리를 꺼내 한쪽 끝을 위로 던지고는 자라에게 내려오라고 청했다. 자라는 겁먹은 듯 격자창에서 기어 나와 로드리고의 어깨에 손을 얹고는 우아하게 뛰어내리려던 참이었는데 — \"아아! 가엾은 자라여!\" — 그녀가 그만 드레스 자락을 깜빡하고 말았다. 자락이 창에 걸린 것이다. 탑이 휘청거리고, 앞으로 기울더니, 와르르 무너져 불운한 연인들을 잔해 속에 파묻어 버렸다! 적갈색 부츠가 잔해 속에서 마구 흔들리고 금빛 머리가 불쑥 튀어나와 \"내가 그랬잖아! 내가 그랬잖아!\" 하고 외치자, 사방에서 비명이 터져 나왔다. 놀라운 침착함을 발휘해 잔인한 아버지 돈 페드로가 달려들어 딸을 끌어내며 황급히 옆으로 속삭였다."]], [["This was a truly thrilling scene, though some persons might have thought that the sudden tumbling down of a quantity of long hair rather marred the effect of the villain's death. He was called before the curtain, and with great propriety appeared, leading Hagar, whose singing was considered more wonderful than all the rest of the performance put together.", "\"웃지 마! 다 괜찮은 것처럼 연기해!\" 그러고는 로드리고에게 일어나라 명하고, 분노와 경멸로 그를 왕국에서 추방했다. 탑이 자기 위로 무너진 탓에 적잖이 흔들렸으면서도 로드리고는 노인에게 맞서며 꼼짝도 하지 않으려 했다. 이 담대한 본보기에 자라도 불이 붙었다. 그녀 역시 아버지에게 맞섰고, 그러자 아버지는 둘 다 성의 가장 깊은 지하 감옥으로 보내라고 명했다. 땅딸막한 가신이 쇠사슬을 들고 들어와 둘을 끌고 나갔는데, 잔뜩 겁먹은 표정에 자기가 했어야 할 대사는 까맣게 잊은 게 분명했다."]], [["Act fourth displayed the despairing Roderigo on the point of stabbing himself, because he has been told that Zara has deserted him. Just as the dagger is at his heart, a lovely song is sung under his window, informing him that Zara is true, but in danger, and he can save her, if he will. A key is thrown in, which unlocks the door, and in a spasm of rapture he tears off his chains, and rushes away to find and rescue his lady-love.", "3막은 성의 대전(大殿)이었다. 여기서 하가르가 등장했는데, 연인들을 풀어 주고 휴고를 끝장내려고 온 것이었다. 그녀는 그가 오는 소리를 듣고 몸을 숨긴다. 그가 두 잔의 포도주에 묘약을 타고는 겁 많은 작은 하인에게 \"이걸 감방의 죄수들한테 가져가서, 내가 곧 가겠다고 전해라\"라고 이르는 것을 본다. 하인이 휴고를 한쪽으로 데려가 무슨 말을 하는 사이에 하가르가 두 잔을 해롭지 않은 다른 두 잔으로 바꿔치기한다. \"종복\" 페르디난도가 그것들을 가지고 나가고, 하가르는 로드리고를 노린 독이 든 잔을 도로 가져다 놓는다. 한참 노래를 부른 뒤 목이 마른 휴고가 그것을 마시고는 정신을 잃더니, 한참을 움켜쥐고 발을 구르다가 풀썩 쓰러져 죽는다. 그러는 동안 하가르는 자기가 무슨 짓을 했는지를 더없이 강렬하고 아름다운 가락의 노래로 그에게 알린다. 이것은 정말로 소름 끼치는 장면이었다. 비록 어떤 이들은 긴 머리카락이 갑자기 우수수 쏟아져 내린 것이 악당의 죽음이 자아내는 효과를 다소 망쳤다고 생각했을지도 모르지만. 그는 커튼 앞으로 불려 나왔고, 더없이 예의 바르게 하가르를 이끌고 나타났다. 하가르의 노래는 다른 모든 공연을 다 합친 것보다 더 훌륭하다는 평을 받았다."]], [["Act fifth opened with a stormy scene between Zara and Don Pedro.", "4막에서는 절망에 빠진 로드리고가 막 자기 몸을 찌르려는 참이었다. 자라가 자기를 버렸다는 말을 들었기 때문이다."], ["He wishes her to go into a convent, but she won't hear of it; and, after a touching appeal, is about to faint, when Roderigo dashes in and demands her hand.", "단검이 막 그의 심장에 닿으려는 찰나, 그의 창문 아래에서 사랑스러운 노래가 들려와, 자라는 변함없지만 위험에 처해 있으며 그가 마음만 먹으면 그녀를 구할 수 있다고 알린다. 열쇠 하나가 안으로 던져져 문을 열어 주고, 환희의 발작 속에서 그는 쇠사슬을 끊어 버리고는 사랑하는 여인을 찾아 구하러 달려 나간다."], ["Don Pedro refuses, because he is not rich.", "5막은 자라와 돈 페드로 사이의 격렬한 장면으로 열렸다."], ["They shout and gesticulate tremendously, but cannot agree, and Roderigo is about to bear away the exhausted Zara, when the timid servant enters with a letter and a bag from Hagar, who has mysteriously disappeared.", "그는 그녀가 수녀원에 들어가기를 바라지만, 그녀는 들으려고도 하지 않는다. 그리고 가슴 아픈 호소 끝에 막 기절하려는 찰나, 로드리고가 뛰어들어 그녀와의 결혼을 요구한다."], ["The latter informs the party that she bequeaths untold wealth to the young pair, and an awful doom to Don Pedro, if he doesn't make them happy.", "돈 페드로는 그가 부자가 아니라는 이유로 거절한다. 둘은 엄청나게 고함치고 손짓 발짓을 해 대지만 뜻을 모으지 못하고, 로드리고가 막 기진맥진한 자라를 데리고 나가려는데, 겁 많은 하인이 신비롭게 사라져 버린 하가르가 보낸 편지 한 통과 자루 하나를 들고 들어온다."], ["The bag is opened, and several quarts of tin money shower down upon the stage, till it is quite glorified with the glitter.", "편지는 하가르가 젊은 두 사람에게는 헤아릴 수 없는 부를, 그리고 돈 페드로가 그들을 행복하게 해 주지 않으면 그에게는 끔찍한 파멸을 남긴다고 일행에게 알린다. 자루를 열자 양철 돈 몇 쿼트가 무대 위로 쏟아져 내려 반짝임으로 온 무대가 그야말로 찬란해진다."], ["This entirely softens the \"stern sire\": he consents without a murmur, all join in a joyful chorus, and the curtain falls upon the lovers kneeling to receive Don Pedro's blessing in attitudes of the most romantic grace.", "이것이 그 \"냉혹한 아비\"를 완전히 누그러뜨린다. 그는 군말 없이 승낙하고, 모두가 기쁨에 찬 합창에 어우러지며, 더없이 낭만적이고 우아한 자세로 돈 페드로의 축복을 받으려 무릎 꿇은 연인들 위로 막이 내린다."]], [["Tumultuous applause followed, but received an unexpected check; for the cot-bed, on which the \"dress-circle\"", "우레와 같은 박수갈채가 뒤따랐지만, 뜻밖의 제동이 걸리고 말았다."], ["was built, suddenly shut up, and extinguished the enthusiastic audience.", "\"특별석\"을 떠받치던 간이침대가 갑자기 접히면서 열광하던 관객들을 깔아뭉갠 것이다."], ["Roderigo and Don Pedro flew to the rescue, and all were taken out unhurt, though many were speechless with laughter.", "로드리고와 돈 페드로가 구하러 날아갔고, 다들 다친 데 없이 끌려 나왔지만 여럿이 웃느라 말도 못 했다."], ["The excitement had hardly subsided, when Hannah appeared, with \"Mrs. March's compliments, and would the ladies walk down to supper.\"", "그 소동이 채 가라앉기도 전에 해나가 나타나 \"마치 부인께서 안부 전하시며, 아가씨들 저녁 드시러 내려오시랍니다\"라고 했다."]], [["This was a surprise, even to the actors; and, when they saw the table, they looked at one another in rapturous amazement.", "이것은 배우들에게조차 뜻밖의 일이었다. 그리고 그들은 식탁을 보고 황홀한 놀라움에 잠겨 서로를 쳐다보았다."], ["It was like Marmee to get up a little treat for them; but anything so fine as this was unheard-of since the departed days of plenty.", "그들에게 작은 깜짝 선물을 마련하는 건 엄마다운 일이었지만, 이렇게 근사한 건 풍족했던 옛 시절이 지나간 이래로 듣도 보도 못한 일이었다."], ["There was ice-cream,--actually two dishes of it, pink and white,--and cake and fruit and distracting French bonbons, and, in the middle of the table, four great bouquets of hot-house flowers!", "아이스크림이 — 그것도 분홍색과 흰색, 두 접시나 — 있었고, 케이크와 과일, 그리고 정신이 아찔할 만큼 멋진 프랑스 봉봉이 있었으며, 식탁 한가운데에는 온실 꽃으로 만든 커다란 꽃다발 네 개가 놓여 있었다!"]], [["It quite took their breath away; and they stared first at the table and then at their mother, who looked as if she enjoyed it immensely.", "다들 숨이 멎을 지경이었고, 식탁을 보았다가 다시 어머니를 보았다가 했는데, 어머니는 이걸 엄청나게 즐기는 듯한 표정이었다."]], [["\"Is it fairies?\"", "\"요정들이 한 거예요?\""], ["asked Amy,", "에이미가 물었다."]], [["\"It's Santa Claus,\"", "\"산타클로스야.\""], ["said Beth.", "베스가 말했다."]], [["\"Mother did it\"; and Meg smiled her sweetest, in spite of her gray beard and white eyebrows.", "\"엄마가 하셨어.\" 메그가, 회색 수염과 흰 눈썹을 붙인 채로도 더없이 다정한 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["\"Aunt March had a good fit, and sent the supper,\"", "\"마치 대고모가 잠깐 마음이 동하셔서 저녁을 보내신 거야.\""], ["cried Jo, with a sudden inspiration.", "조가 문득 떠오른 생각에 외쳤다."]], [["\"All wrong.", "\"다 틀렸어."], ["Old Mr. Laurence sent it,\"", "늙은 로런스 씨가 보내신 거란다.\""], ["replied Mrs. March.", "마치 부인이 대답했다."]], [["\"The Laurence boy's grandfather!", "\"로런스네 소년의 할아버지가요!"], ["What in the world put such a thing into his head?", "도대체 무슨 바람이 불어서 그런 생각을 하셨대요?"], ["We don't know him!\"", "우린 그분을 알지도 못하는데!\""], ["exclaimed Meg.", "메그가 외쳤다."]], [["\"Hannah told one of his servants about your breakfast party.", "\"해나가 그분 댁 하인 하나한테 너희 아침 잔치 얘기를 했단다."], ["He is an odd old gentleman, but that pleased him.", "별난 노신사이긴 한데, 그게 마음에 드셨나 봐."], ["He knew my father, years ago; and he sent me a polite note this afternoon, saying he hoped I would allow him to express his friendly feeling toward my children by sending them a few trifles in honor of the day.", "그분은 오래전에 내 아버지를 아셨고, 오늘 오후에 정중한 쪽지를 보내셨더구나. 이날을 기념해 너희한테 자잘한 것 몇 가지를 보내 우리 아이들을 향한 다정한 마음을 표현하게 해 주면 좋겠다고 말이야."], ["I could not refuse; and so you have a little feast at night to make up for the bread-and-milk breakfast.\"", "거절할 수가 없었지. 그래서 빵과 우유로 때운 아침을 보충하라고 너희가 밤에 이런 작은 잔치를 누리게 된 거란다.\""]], [["\"That boy put it into his head, I know he did!", "\"그 애가 그 생각을 불어넣은 거예요, 분명해요!"], ["He's a capital fellow, and I wish we could get acquainted.", "정말 멋진 녀석인데, 우리가 알고 지낼 수 있으면 좋겠어요."], ["He looks as if he'd like to know us; but he's bashful, and Meg is so prim she won't let me speak to him when we pass,\"", "우리랑 알고 지내고 싶어 하는 눈치인데, 부끄럼을 타는 데다 메그 언니가 어찌나 새침한지 우리가 지나칠 때 내가 말도 못 걸게 한다니까요.\""], ["said Jo, as the plates went round, and the ice began to melt out of sight, with \"Ohs!\"", "조가 말했는데, 그러는 동안 접시들이 돌고 아이스크림은 \"오!\"와 \"아!\""], ["and \"Ahs!\" of satisfaction.", "하는 만족스러운 탄성과 함께 눈앞에서 녹아 사라지기 시작했다."]], [["\"You mean the people who live in the big house next door, don't you?\"", "\"옆집 큰 저택에 사는 사람들 말하는 거지?\""], ["asked one of the girls.", "소녀 중 하나가 물었다."], ["\"My mother knows old Mr. Laurence; but says he's very proud, and doesn't like to mix with his neighbors.", "\"우리 엄마가 늙은 로런스 씨를 아는데, 무척 거만한 양반이라 이웃들이랑 어울리는 걸 싫어하신대."], ["He keeps his grandson shut up, when he isn't riding or walking with his tutor, and makes him study very hard.", "손자가 가정교사랑 말 타거나 산책할 때 빼고는 가둬 두고 공부를 아주 빡세게 시킨다나 봐."], ["We invited him to our party, but he didn't come.", "우리 파티에 초대했는데 안 왔어."], ["Mother says he's very nice, though he never speaks to us girls.\"", "우리 엄마 말로는 아주 좋은 애라는데, 우리 여자애들한텐 말도 안 걸어.\""]], [["\"Our cat ran away once, and he brought her back, and we talked over the fence, and were getting on capitally,--all about cricket, and so on,--when he saw Meg coming, and walked off.", "\"한번은 우리 고양이가 달아났는데 그 애가 데려다줬어. 그래서 우리가 울타리 너머로 이야기를 나눴는데, 크리켓이며 뭐며 얘기하면서 척척 잘 통하던 참이었거든."], ["I mean to know him some day; for he needs fun, I'm sure he does,\"", "그러다 메그 언니가 오는 걸 보더니 가 버리더라. 난 언젠가 그 애랑 꼭 알고 지낼 거야."], ["said Jo decidedly.", "그 앤 즐거움이 필요해, 분명 그래.\" 조가 단호하게 말했다."]], [["\"I like his manners, and he looks like a little gentleman; so I've no objection to your knowing him, if a proper opportunity comes.", "\"그 애 행동거지가 마음에 들고, 어린 신사 같아 보이더구나. 그러니 적당한 기회만 온다면 너희가 그 애와 알고 지내는 데 반대하지 않으마."], ["He brought the flowers himself; and I should have asked him in, if I had been sure what was going on upstairs.", "꽃은 그 애가 직접 가져왔단다. 위층에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 확실히 알았더라면 안으로 들이자고 했을 텐데."], ["He looked so wistful as he went away, hearing the frolic, and evidently having none of his own.\"", "떠나면서 그 흥겨운 소리를 듣는데, 정작 자기는 그런 즐거움이 하나도 없는 듯 어찌나 아쉬운 표정이던지.\""]], [["\"It's a mercy you didn't, mother!\"", "\"안 들이시길 천만다행이에요, 엄마!\""], ["laughed Jo, looking at her boots.", "조가 자기 부츠를 내려다보며 웃었다."], ["\"But we'll have another play, some time, that he _can_ see.", "\"하지만 언젠가 그 애가 볼 수 있는 다른 연극을 하면 되죠."], ["Perhaps he'll help act; wouldn't that be jolly?\"", "어쩌면 그 애가 연기를 도와줄지도 몰라요. 그럼 신나지 않겠어요?\""]], [["\"I never had such a fine bouquet before!", "\"이렇게 멋진 꽃다발은 처음 받아 봐!"], ["How pretty it is!\"", "정말 예쁘다!\""], ["And Meg examined her flowers with great interest.", "메그가 자기 꽃들을 큰 관심을 갖고 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["\"They _are_ lovely!", "\"정말 사랑스럽구나!"], ["But Beth's roses are sweeter to me,\"", "그래도 나한테는 베스의 장미가 더 향기롭단다.\""], ["said Mrs. March, smelling the half-dead posy in her belt.", "마치 부인이 허리띠에 꽂힌, 반쯤 시든 작은 꽃다발의 냄새를 맡으며 말했다."]], [["Beth nestled up to her, and whispered softly, \"I wish I could send my bunch to father.", "베스가 어머니에게 바싹 다가가 가만히 속삭였다. \"제 꽃다발을 아빠한테 보낼 수 있으면 좋겠어요."], ["I'm afraid he isn't having such a merry Christmas as we are.\"", "아빠는 우리만큼 즐거운 크리스마스를 보내고 계시지 못할 것 같아서요.\" 3."]], [["# THE LAURENCE BOY.", "# THE LAURENCE BOY."]], [["\"Jo! Jo!", "로런스네 소년\n\"조!"], ["where are you?\"", "조! 어디 있어?\""], ["cried Meg, at the foot of the garret stairs.", "메그가 다락방 계단 밑에서 외쳤다."]], [["\"Here!\"", "\"여기!\""], ["answered a husky voice from above; and, running up, Meg found her sister eating apples and crying over the \"Heir of Redclyffe,\"", "위에서 잠긴 목소리가 대답했다. 메그가 뛰어 올라가 보니, 동생은 양지바른 창가의 낡은 다리 셋 달린 소파에 누비이불을 두르고 앉아 사과를 먹으며 「레드클리프의 상속자」를 읽고 울고 있었다."], ["wrapped up in a comforter on an old three-legged sofa by the sunny window.", "여기가 조가 가장 좋아하는 안식처였다."], ["This was Jo's favorite refuge; and here she loved to retire with half a dozen russets and a nice book, to enjoy the quiet and the society of a pet rat who lived near by, and didn't mind her a particle.", "조용한 분위기와, 근처에 살면서 자기를 조금도 신경 쓰지 않는 애완 쥐 한 마리의 벗 삼음을 즐기려고, 적갈색 사과 대여섯 개와 괜찮은 책 한 권을 들고 이리로 틀어박히는 걸 좋아했다."], ["As Meg appeared, Scrabble whisked into his hole.", "메그가 나타나자 스크래블은 제 구멍으로 휙 달아났다."], ["Jo shook the tears off her cheeks, and waited to hear the news.", "조는 뺨에 흐른 눈물을 털어 내고 소식을 들으려고 기다렸다."]], [["\"Such fun!", "\"정말 신나!"], ["only see!", "이것 좀 봐!"], ["a regular note of invitation from Mrs.", "내일 밤 가드너 부인이 보낸 정식 초대장이야!\""], ["Gardiner for to-morrow night!\" cried Meg, waving the precious paper, and then proceeding to read it, with girlish delight.", "메그가 그 귀한 종이를 흔들며 외쳤고, 그러고는\n그것을 읽어 내려가는 그녀의 얼굴에 소녀다운 기쁨이 어렸다."]], [["\"'Mrs. Gardiner would be happy to see Miss March and Miss Josephine at a little dance on New-Year's Eve.'", "\"'가드너 부인은 새해 전야의 작은 무도회에 메그 양과 조세핀 양을 모시게 되어 기쁘겠습니다.'"], ["Marmee is willing we should go; now what _shall_ we wear?\"", "엄마가 가도 좋다고 하셨어. 그런데 우리 도대체 뭘 입지?\""]], [["\"What's the use of asking that, when you know we shall wear our poplins, because we haven't got anything else?\"", "\"그걸 왜 물어? 우리한텐 포플린 드레스밖에 없으니까 그거 입을 게 뻔한데.\""], ["answered Jo, with her mouth full.", "조가 입에 음식을 가득 문 채 대답했다."]], [["\"If I only had a silk!\"", "\"나한테 실크 드레스 하나만 있다면!\""], ["sighed Meg.", "메그가 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["\"Mother says I may when I'm eighteen, perhaps; but two years is an everlasting time to wait.\"", "\"엄마가 그러시는데, 아마 내가 열여덟 살이 되면 하나 사 주실 거래. 그런데 두 해를 기다리려면 끝도 없이 멀게만 느껴져.\""]], [["\"I'm sure our pops look like silk, and they are nice enough for us.", "\"우리 포플린도 실크처럼 보이는걸. 그리고 우리한텐 충분히 괜찮잖아."], ["Yours is as good as new, but I forgot the burn and the tear in mine.", "언니 건 거의 새것이나 다름없는데, 난 그만 내 옷에 탄 자국이랑 찢어진 데를 깜빡했지 뭐야."], ["Whatever shall I do?", "어쩌면 좋아?"], ["the burn shows badly, and I can't take any out.\"", "탄 자국이 너무 눈에 띄는데, 도려낼 수도 없고.\""]], [["\"You must sit still all you can, and keep your back out of sight; the front is all right. I shall have a new ribbon for my hair, and Marmee will lend me her little pearl pin, and my new slippers are lovely, and my gloves will do, though they aren't as nice as I'd like.\"", "\"넌 되도록 가만히 앉아서 등을 안 보이게 하면 돼. 앞쪽은 멀쩡하잖아. 난 머리에 맬 새 리본을 살 거고, 엄마가 작은 진주 핀을 빌려주신다고 하셨어. 내 새 슬리퍼는 정말 예쁘고, 장갑도 그럭저럭 괜찮아. 내가 바라는 만큼 고운 건 아니지만.\""]], [["\"Mine are spoilt with lemonade, and I can't get any new ones, so I shall have to go without,\"", "\"내 건 레모네이드 때문에 다 망가졌고, 새 걸 살 수도 없으니 그냥 끼지 말고 가야겠어.\" 조가 말했다."], ["said Jo, who never troubled herself much about dress.", "옷차림 따위로는 좀처럼 골머리를 앓지 않는 그녀였다."]], [["\"You _must_ have gloves, or I won't go,\"", "\"넌 반드시 장갑을 껴야 해. 안 그러면 나도 안 갈 거야.\""], ["cried Meg decidedly.", "메그가 단호하게 외쳤다. \"장갑은 다른 어떤 것보다 중요해."], ["\"Gloves are more important than anything else; you can't dance without them, and if you don't I should be _so_ mortified.\"", "장갑이 없으면 춤도 못 추는걸. 네가 장갑을 안 끼면 난 정말 창피해서 견딜 수가 없을 거야.\""]], [["\"Then I'll stay still.", "\"그럼 난 가만히 앉아 있을게. 사교 춤 같은 건 별로 좋아하지도 않아."], ["I don't care much for company dancing; it's no fun to go sailing round; I like to fly about and cut capers.\"", "미끄러지듯 빙빙 도는 게 무슨 재미야. 난 이리저리 날아다니면서 깡충깡충 뛰는 게 좋단 말이야.\""]], [["\"You can't ask mother for new ones, they are so expensive, and you are so careless.", "\"엄마한테 새 걸 사 달라고 할 순 없어. 너무 비싸고, 넌 또 워낙 덤벙대잖아."], ["She said, when you spoilt the others, that she shouldn't get you any more this winter.", "지난번에 네가 장갑을 못 쓰게 만들었을 때, 엄마가 이번 겨울엔 더 안 사 주신다고 하셨어."], ["Can't you make them do?\"", "그거 어떻게 좀 끼고 갈 순 없니?\""], ["asked Meg anxiously.", "메그가 근심스럽게 물었다."]], [["\"I can hold them crumpled up in my hand, so no one will know how stained they are; that's all I can do. No! I'll tell you how we can manage--each wear one good one and carry a bad one; don't you see?\"", "\"손안에 꼬깃꼬깃 쥐고 있으면 돼. 그럼 아무도 얼마나 얼룩졌는지 모를 거야. 내가 할 수 있는 건 그게 다야. 아니지! 어떻게 하면 되는지 내가 알려 줄게. 각자 멀쩡한 거 한 짝씩 끼고, 망가진 건 한 짝씩 손에 쥐고 가는 거야. 알겠어?\""]], [["\"Your hands are bigger than mine, and you will stretch my glove dreadfully,\"", "\"네 손이 내 손보다 큰데, 그러면 내 장갑이 끔찍하게 늘어날 거야.\""], ["began Meg, whose gloves were a tender point with her.", "메그가 입을 열었다. 그녀에게 장갑은 예민한 문제였다."]], [["\"Then I'll go without.", "\"그럼 난 그냥 안 끼고 갈래."], ["I don't care what people say!\"", "사람들이 뭐라든 상관없어!\""], ["cried Jo, taking up her book.", "조가 책을 집어 들며 외쳤다."]], [["\"You may have it, you may!", "\"껴도 돼, 껴!"], ["only don't stain it, and do behave nicely.", "그 대신 얼룩만 묻히지 말고, 얌전히 굴어야 해."], ["Don't put your hands behind you, or stare, or say 'Christopher Columbus!'", "손을 뒤로 돌리지도 말고, 빤히 쳐다보지도 말고, '크리스토퍼 콜럼버스!' 같은 말도 하지 마."], ["will you?\"", "알겠지?\""]], [["\"Don't worry about me; I'll be as prim as I can, and not get into any scrapes, if I can help it.", "\"내 걱정은 마. 최대한 점잖게 굴면서, 어떻게든 사고는 안 칠게."], ["Now go and answer your note, and let me finish this splendid story.\"", "자, 이제 가서 답장이나 써. 난 이 멋진 이야기를 마저 읽게.\""]], [["So Meg went away to \"accept with thanks,\"", "그래서 메그는 자리를 떠나 \"감사히 참석하겠다\"는 답장을 쓰고, 자기 옷을 살펴보고, 단 하나뿐인 진짜 레이스 주름 장식을 손질하면서 즐겁게 노래를 흥얼거렸다."], ["look over her dress, and sing blithely as she did up her one real lace frill; while Jo finished her story, her four apples, and had a game of romps with Scrabble.", "그동안 조는 이야기를 끝까지 읽고, 사과 네 알을 다 먹어 치우고, 스크래블과 한바탕 뛰어놀았다."]], [["On New-Year's Eve the parlor was deserted, for the two younger girls played dressing-maids, and the two elder were absorbed in the all-important business of \"getting ready for the party.\"", "새해 전야, 거실은 텅 비어 있었다. 두 동생은 옷 시중을 드느라, 두 언니는 \"무도회 갈 채비\"라는 더없이 중대한 일에 푹 빠져 있었기 때문이다."], ["Simple as the toilets were, there was a great deal of running up and down, laughing and talking, and at one time a strong smell of burnt hair pervaded the house.", "단장이라야 소박하기 그지없었지만, 위층 아래층을 오르내리고, 웃고 떠드는 소동이 한바탕 벌어졌고, 한때는 머리카락 타는 냄새가 집 안에 진동했다."], ["Meg wanted a few curls about her face, and Jo undertook to pinch the papered locks with a pair of hot tongs.", "메그가 얼굴 둘레에 곱슬머리를 좀 만들고 싶어 했고, 조가 종이로 만 머리 타래를 뜨거운 인두로 집어 보겠다고 나섰다."]], [["\"Ought they to smoke like that?\"", "\"머리카락이 저렇게 연기가 나도 괜찮은 거야?\""], ["asked Beth, from her perch on the bed.", "침대에 걸터앉은 베스가 물었다."]], [["\"It's the dampness drying,\"", "\"습기가 마르는 거야.\""], ["replied Jo.", "조가 대답했다."]], [["\"What a queer smell!", "\"무슨 냄새가 이렇게 이상해!"], ["it's like burnt feathers,\"", "깃털 타는 냄새 같아.\""], ["observed Amy, smoothing her own pretty curls with a superior air.", "에이미가 자기 예쁜 곱슬머리를 우쭐한 태도로 매만지며 말했다."]], [["\"There, now I'll take off the papers and you'll see a cloud of little ringlets,\"", "\"자, 이제 종이를 떼어 내면 작은 고수머리가 구름처럼 뭉게뭉게 나타날 거야.\""], ["said Jo, putting down the tongs.", "조가 인두를 내려놓으며 말했다."]], [["She did take off the papers, but no cloud of ringlets appeared, for the hair came with the papers, and the horrified hair-dresser laid a row of little scorched bundles on the bureau before her victim.", "조는 정말로 종이를 떼어 냈지만, 고수머리 구름은 나타나지 않았다. 머리카락이 종이에 딸려 나오는 바람에, 경악한 미용사는 희생자 앞 화장대 위에 자그마한 그을린 다발들을 한 줄로 늘어놓고 말았다."]], [["\"Oh, oh, oh!", "\"어머, 어머, 어머!"], ["what _have_ you done?", "너 도대체 무슨 짓을 한 거야?"], ["I'm spoilt!", "나 망했어!"], ["I can't go!", "이러고 어떻게 가!"], ["My hair, oh, my hair!\"", "내 머리, 아, 내 머리!\""], ["wailed Meg, looking with despair at the uneven frizzle on her forehead.", "메그가 이마 위로 들쭉날쭉 곱슬거리는 머리를 절망스레 바라보며 울부짖었다."]], [["\"Just my luck!", "\"내가 그렇지 뭐!"], ["you shouldn't have asked me to do it; I always spoil everything.", "언니가 나한테 시키지 말았어야 했어. 난 늘 모든 걸 망쳐 버린다니까."], ["I'm so sorry, but the tongs were too hot, and so I've made a mess,\"", "정말 미안해. 인두가 너무 뜨거워서 이렇게 엉망으로 만들어 버렸어.\""], ["groaned poor Jo, regarding the black pancakes with tears of regret.", "가엾은 조가 후회의 눈물을 머금고 그 까만 팬케이크들을 바라보며 끙끙댔다."]], [["\"It isn't spoilt; just frizzle it, and tie your ribbon so the ends come on your forehead a bit, and it will look like the last fashion.", "\"망친 게 아니야. 그냥 곱슬곱슬하게 손질하고, 리본을 매서 끝자락이 이마 위로 살짝 내려오게 하면, 최신 유행처럼 보일 거야."], ["I've seen many girls do it so,\"", "그렇게 하는 여자애들 많이 봤어.\""], ["said Amy consolingly.", "에이미가 위로하듯 말했다."]], [["\"Serves me right for trying to be fine.", "\"멋 좀 내 보겠다고 까불었으니 싸지."], ["I wish I'd let my hair alone,\"", "머리를 그냥 내버려 둘걸.\""], ["cried Meg petulantly.", "메그가 짜증스레 외쳤다."]], [["\"So do I, it was so smooth and pretty.", "\"나도 그래. 정말 매끄럽고 예뻤는데."], ["But it will soon grow out again,\"", "하지만 금방 다시 자랄 거야.\""], ["said Beth, coming to kiss and comfort the shorn sheep.", "베스가 다가와 털 깎인 양에게 입을 맞추고 다독여 주며 말했다."]], [["After various lesser mishaps, Meg was finished at last, and by the united exertions of the family Jo's hair was got up and her dress on.", "크고 작은 사고를 여러 차례 겪은 끝에, 마침내 메그의 단장이 끝났고, 온 식구가 힘을 합친 덕분에 조의 머리도 올려 묶이고 드레스도 입혀졌다."], ["They looked very well in their simple suits,--Meg in silvery drab, with a blue velvet snood, lace frills, and the pearl pin; Jo in maroon, with a stiff, gentlemanly linen collar, and a white chrysanthemum or two for her only ornament.", "소박한 차림이었지만 두 사람은 무척 보기 좋았다. 메그는 은빛 도는 회갈색 드레스에 파란 벨벳 머리띠와 레이스 주름 장식, 그리고 진주 핀을 달았고, 조는 적갈색 드레스에 빳빳하고 신사다운 리넨 깃을 세우고 흰 국화 한두 송이를 유일한 장식으로 꽂았다."], ["Each put on one nice light glove, and carried one soiled one, and all pronounced the effect \"quite easy and fine.\"", "둘은 각자 멀쩡한 밝은색 장갑 한 짝씩을 끼고, 얼룩진 한 짝씩을 손에 쥐었으며, 그 모양새가 \"꽤 편안하고 멋지다\"고 입을 모았다."], ["Meg's high-heeled slippers were very tight, and hurt her, though she would not own it, and Jo's nineteen hair-pins all seemed stuck straight into her head, which was not exactly comfortable; but, dear me, let us be elegant or die!", "메그의 굽 높은 슬리퍼는 너무 꽉 끼어 발이 아팠지만 그녀는 내색하지 않았고, 조의 머리에 꽂은 열아홉 개의 머리핀은 죄다 머리통에 곧장 꽂혀 들어가는 것만 같아 그리 편치는 않았다. 하지만 어쩌랴, 우아해지든가 죽든가 둘 중 하나인 것을!"]], [["\"Have a good time, dearies!\"", "\"즐거운 시간 보내렴, 얘들아!\""], ["said Mrs. March, as the sisters went daintily down the walk.", "자매가 산뜻한 걸음으로 길을 내려가자 마치 부인이 말했다."], ["\"Don't eat much supper, and come away at eleven, when I send Hannah for you.\"", "\"저녁은 많이 먹지 말고, 열한 시에 한나를 보낼 테니 그때 나오너라.\""], ["As the gate clashed behind them, a voice cried from a window,--", "등 뒤로 대문이 철컹 닫히는 순간, 창문에서 목소리가 들려왔다."]], [["\"Girls, girls!", "\"얘들아, 얘들아!"], ["_have_ you both got nice pocket-handkerchiefs?\"", "둘 다 깨끗한 손수건은 챙겼니?\""]], [["\"Yes, yes, spandy nice, and Meg has cologne on hers,\"", "\"네, 네, 아주 말끔한 걸로요. 메그 언니 건 향수까지 뿌렸어요.\""], ["cried Jo, adding, with a laugh, as they went on, \"I do believe Marmee would ask that if we were all running away from an earthquake.\"", "조가 외치고는, 걸어가면서 웃으며 덧붙였다. \"지진이 나서 우리가 다 도망치는 중이라도 엄마는 분명 저걸 물어보실 거야.\""]], [["\"It is one of her aristocratic tastes, and quite proper, for a real lady is always known by neat boots, gloves, and handkerchief,\"", "\"그건 엄마의 우아한 취향 가운데 하나고, 아주 옳은 거야. 진짜 숙녀는 깔끔한 부츠와 장갑, 손수건으로 알아보는 거니까.\""], ["replied Meg, who had a good many little \"aristocratic tastes\"", "메그가 대답했다."], ["of her own.", "그녀 역시 자기만의 자잘한 \"우아한 취향\"이 제법 많았다."]], [["\"Now don't forget to keep the bad breadth out of sight, Jo.", "\"자, 조, 망가진 자락 안 보이게 하는 거 잊지 마."], ["Is my sash right?", "내 허리띠 괜찮아?"], ["and does my hair look _very_ bad?\"", "그리고 내 머리 많이 흉해 보여?\""], ["said Meg, as she turned from the glass in Mrs. Gardiner's dressing-room, after a prolonged prink.", "가드너 부인의 화장실 거울 앞에서 한참을 매만지던 메그가 거울에서 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["\"I know I shall forget.", "\"난 분명 깜빡할 거야."], ["If you see me doing anything wrong, just remind me by a wink, will you?\"", "내가 뭔가 잘못하는 게 보이면, 그냥 눈짓으로 알려 줘. 알겠지?\""], ["returned Jo, giving her collar a twitch and her head a hasty brush.", "조가 깃을 홱 잡아당기고 머리를 허둥지둥 쓸어 넘기며 대꾸했다."]], [["\"No, winking isn't lady-like; I'll lift my eyebrows if anything is wrong, and nod if you are all right. Now hold your shoulders straight, and take short steps, and don't shake hands if you are introduced to any one: it isn't the thing.\"", "\"안 돼, 눈짓은 숙녀답지 못해. 뭔가 잘못되면 내가 눈썹을 치켜올릴게. 괜찮으면 고개를 끄덕이고. 자, 어깨 똑바로 펴고, 보폭은 작게 하고, 누구한테 소개받더라도 악수는 하지 마. 그건 예의가 아니거든.\""]], [["\"How _do_ you learn all the proper ways?", "\"언니는 그 많은 예법을 다 어떻게 익혔어?"], ["I never can.", "난 도저히 못 하겠어."], ["Isn't that music gay?\"", "저 음악 참 경쾌하지 않니?\""]], [["Down they went, feeling a trifle timid, for they seldom went to parties, and, informal as this little gathering was, it was an event to them. Mrs.", "두 사람은 아래층으로 내려갔는데, 좀처럼 무도회에 가 본 일이 없는 터라 약간 주눅이 들었고, 이 작은 모임이 비격식이라고는 해도 그들에게는 하나의 사건이었다."], ["Gardiner, a stately old lady, greeted them kindly, and handed them over to the eldest of her six daughters.", "위풍당당한 노부인인 가드너 부인이 다정하게 그들을 맞이하더니, 여섯 딸 가운데 맏이에게 넘겨주었다."], ["Meg knew Sallie, and was at her ease very soon; but Jo, who didn't care much for girls or girlish gossip, stood about, with her back carefully against the wall, and felt as much out of place as a colt in a flower-garden.", "메그는 샐리와 아는 사이여서 금세 마음이 편안해졌다. 그러나 여자애들이나 여자애들의 수다 따위엔 별 관심이 없는 조는, 등을 조심스레 벽에 기댄 채 우두커니 서서, 꽃밭에 들어선 망아지처럼 어색하기 짝이 없는 기분이었다."], ["Half a dozen jovial lads were talking about skates in another part of the room, and she longed to go and join them, for skating was one of the joys of her life.", "방 한쪽에서는 쾌활한 청년 대여섯이 스케이트 이야기를 나누고 있었는데, 스케이트는 그녀가 삶에서 누리는 즐거움 가운데 하나였기에 그쪽으로 가서 어울리고 싶어 좀이 쑤셨다."], ["She telegraphed her wish to Meg, but the eyebrows went up so alarmingly that she dared not stir.", "그녀는 메그에게 그 바람을 눈짓으로 알렸지만, 눈썹이 어찌나 무섭게 치켜올라가는지 감히 꼼짝도 할 수 없었다."], ["No one came to talk to her, and one by one the group near her dwindled away, till she was left alone.", "아무도 말을 걸어오지 않았고, 가까이 있던 무리도 하나둘 흩어지더니 마침내 그녀 혼자만 덩그러니 남았다."], ["She could not roam about and amuse herself, for the burnt breadth would show, so she stared at people rather forlornly till the dancing began.", "탄 자국이 드러날까 봐 이리저리 돌아다니며 혼자 즐길 수도 없었던 그녀는, 춤이 시작될 때까지 다소 처량하게 사람들을 바라보고만 있었다."], ["Meg was asked at once, and the tight slippers tripped about so briskly that none would have guessed the pain their wearer suffered smilingly.", "메그는 곧바로 춤 신청을 받았고, 꽉 끼는 슬리퍼를 신고도 어찌나 경쾌하게 발을 놀리는지, 그것을 신은 이가 웃으며 견디는 고통을 아무도 짐작하지 못했을 것이다."], ["Jo saw a big red-headed youth approaching her corner, and fearing he meant to engage her, she slipped into a curtained recess, intending to peep and enjoy herself in peace.", "조는 빨강머리의 덩치 큰 청년이 자기가 있는 구석으로 다가오는 것을 보고, 그가 춤을 청할까 봐 슬그머니 커튼이 드리워진 후미진 곳으로 숨어들었다. 거기서 조용히 내다보며 마음 편히 즐길 작정이었다."], ["Unfortunately, another bashful person had chosen the same refuge; for, as the curtain fell behind her, she found herself face to face with the \"Laurence boy.\"", "그런데 공교롭게도 다른 수줍은 사람 하나가 똑같은 피난처를 골라 둔 터라, 커튼이 뒤로 내려오는 순간 그녀는 \"로런스 도련님\"과 정면으로 마주치고 말았다."]], [["\"Dear me, I didn't know any one was here!\"", "\"어머, 누가 여기 있는 줄 몰랐어요!\""], ["stammered Jo, preparing to back out as speedily as she had bounced in.", "조가 더듬거리며, 뛰어든 만큼이나 잽싸게 물러서려 했다."]], [["But the boy laughed, and said pleasantly, though he looked a little startled,--", "그러나 소년은 약간 놀란 기색이면서도 유쾌하게 웃으며 말했다."]], [["\"Don't mind me; stay, if you like.\"", "\"신경 쓰지 마세요. 괜찮으면 그냥 계세요.\""]], [["\"Sha'n't I disturb you?\"", "\"제가 방해되지 않을까요?\""]], [["\"Not a bit; I only came here because I don't know many people, and felt rather strange at first, you know.\"", "\"전혀요. 저도 아는 사람이 별로 없어서 여기 온 거예요. 처음엔 좀 어색했거든요.\""]], [["\"So did I.", "\"저도 그랬어요."], ["Don't go away, please, unless you'd rather.\"", "더 좋으시다면 모를까, 부디 가지 마세요.\""]], [["The boy sat down again and looked at his pumps, till Jo said, trying to be polite and easy,--", "소년은 다시 자리에 앉아 자기 무도화만 내려다보았고, 마침내 조가 예의 바르고 편안해 보이려 애쓰며 입을 열었다."]], [["\"I think I've had the pleasure of seeing you before; you live near us, don't you?\"", "\"전에 한 번 뵌 적이 있는 것 같은데요. 우리 옆에 사시죠?\""]], [["\"Next door\"; and he looked up and laughed outright, for Jo's prim manner was rather funny when he remembered how they had chatted about cricket when he brought the cat home.", "\"바로 옆집이에요.\" 그러면서 그가 고개를 들고 큰 소리로 웃었다. 고양이를 데려다주었을 때 둘이 크리켓 이야기를 나누던 일이 떠오르자, 조의 점잔 빼는 태도가 어지간히 우스웠던 것이다."]], [["That put Jo at her ease; and she laughed too, as she said, in her heartiest way,--", "그 웃음에 조도 마음이 편해져서 함께 웃으며, 더없이 진심 어린 투로 말했다."]], [["\"We did have such a good time over your nice Christmas present.\"", "\"그 멋진 크리스마스 선물 덕분에 우리 정말 즐거웠어요.\""]], [["\"Grandpa sent it.\"", "\"할아버지가 보내신 거예요.\""]], [["\"But you put it into his head, didn't you, now?\"", "\"하지만 도련님이 할아버지께 그 생각을 심어 드린 거잖아요, 그렇죠?\""]], [["\"How is your cat, Miss March?\"", "\"고양이는 잘 있나요, 마치 양?\""], ["asked the boy, trying to look sober, while his black eyes shone with fun.", "소년이 짐짓 점잖은 표정을 지으려 했지만, 그의 까만 눈동자는 장난기로 반짝였다."]], [["\"Nicely, thank you, Mr.", "\"잘 있어요, 고마워요, 로런스 씨."], ["Laurence; but I am not Miss March, I'm only Jo,\"", "그런데 전 마치 양이 아니에요. 그냥 조예요.\""], ["returned the young lady.", "아가씨가 대꾸했다."]], [["\"I'm not Mr.", "\"전 로런스 씨가 아니에요."], ["Laurence, I'm only Laurie.\"", "그냥 로리예요.\""]], [["\"Laurie Laurence,--what an odd name!\"", "\"로리 로런스라니, 참 이상한 이름이네요!\""]], [["\"My first name is Theodore, but I don't like it, for the fellows called me Dora, so I made them say Laurie instead.\"", "\"원래 이름은 시어도어인데, 그게 싫어요. 애들이 절 도라라고 불렀거든요. 그래서 대신 로리라고 부르게 만들었죠.\""]], [["\"I hate my name, too--so sentimental!", "\"저도 제 이름이 싫어요. 너무 감상적이잖아요!"], ["I wish every one would say Jo, instead of Josephine.", "다들 조세핀 말고 조라고 불러 주면 좋겠어요."], ["How did you make the boys stop calling you Dora?\"", "애들이 도라라고 부르는 걸 어떻게 그만두게 했어요?\""]], [["\"I thrashed 'em.\"", "\"두들겨 팼죠.\""]], [["\"I can't thrash Aunt March, so I suppose I shall have to bear it\"; and Jo resigned herself with a sigh.", "\"난 마치 고모를 두들겨 팰 순 없으니, 그냥 참는 수밖에 없겠네요.\" 조가 한숨을 쉬며 체념했다."]], [["\"Don't you like to dance, Miss Jo?\"", "\"춤 추는 거 안 좋아해요, 조 양?\""], ["asked Laurie, looking as if he thought the name suited her.", "로리가, 그 이름이 그녀에게 잘 어울린다고 여기는 듯한 표정으로 물었다."]], [["\"I like it well enough if there is plenty of room, and every one is lively.", "\"공간이 넉넉하고 다들 활기차면 꽤 좋아해요."], ["In a place like this I'm sure to upset something, tread on people's toes, or do something dreadful, so I keep out of mischief, and let Meg sail about.", "그런데 이런 데서는 영락없이 뭔가를 엎거나, 남의 발을 밟거나, 끔찍한 사고를 치게 돼요. 그래서 말썽을 안 부리려고 가만히 있고, 메그 언니나 빙빙 돌게 두는 거죠."], ["Don't you dance?\"", "도련님은 춤 안 춰요?\""]], [["\"Sometimes; you see I've been abroad a good many years, and haven't been into company enough yet to know how you do things here.\"", "\"가끔요. 저는 외국에서 여러 해 살아서, 여기 사람들이 어떻게 하는지 알 만큼 사교 모임에 자주 나가 본 적이 없거든요.\""]], [["\"Abroad!\"", "\"외국에서요!\""], ["cried Jo.", "조가 외쳤다."], ["\"Oh, tell me about it!", "\"어머, 얘기 좀 해 줘요!"], ["I love dearly to hear people describe their travels.\"", "난 사람들이 여행담 들려주는 거 정말 좋아해요.\""]], [["Laurie didn't seem to know where to begin; but Jo's eager questions soon set him going, and he told her how he had been at school in Vevay, where the boys never wore hats, and had a fleet of boats on the lake, and for holiday fun went walking trips about Switzerland with their teachers.", "로리는 어디서부터 시작해야 할지 모르는 듯했지만, 조의 열띤 질문에 곧 입을 열었다. 그는 브베의 학교에 다녔던 이야기를 들려주었다. 그곳에서는 남학생들이 모자를 쓰는 법이 없었고, 호수에 보트 선단을 띄워 놓았으며, 휴일이면 선생님들과 함께 스위스 곳곳을 걸어서 여행하는 즐거움을 누렸다고 했다."]], [["\"Don't I wish I'd been there!\"", "\"나도 거기 가 봤더라면 얼마나 좋았을까!\""], ["cried Jo.", "조가 외쳤다."], ["\"Did you go to Paris?\"", "\"파리에도 갔어요?\""]], [["\"We spent last winter there.\"", "\"지난겨울을 거기서 보냈어요.\""]], [["\"Can you talk French?\"", "\"프랑스어 할 줄 알아요?\""]], [["\"We were not allowed to speak any thing else at Vevay.\"", "\"브베에서는 다른 말은 한마디도 못 쓰게 했는걸요.\""]], [["\"Do say some!", "\"좀 해 봐요!"], ["I can read it, but can't pronounce.\"", "난 읽을 줄은 알아도 발음은 못 하거든요.\""]], [["\"Quel nom a cette jeune demoiselle en les pantoufles jolis?\"", "\"Quel nom a cette jeune demoiselle en les pantoufles jolis?\""], ["said Laurie good-naturedly.", "로리가 너그럽게 말했다."]], [["\"How nicely you do it!", "\"발음 정말 잘하네요!"], ["Let me see,--you said, 'Who is the young lady in the pretty slippers,' didn't you?\"", "어디 보자, '저 예쁜 슬리퍼를 신은 아가씨는 누구인가요'라고 했죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["\"Oui, mademoiselle.\"", "\"Oui, mademoiselle.\""]], [["\"It's my sister Margaret, and you knew it was!", "\"우리 언니 마거릿이에요. 알면서 물어본 거죠!"], ["Do you think she is pretty?\"", "언니가 예쁜 것 같아요?\""]], [["\"Yes; she makes me think of the German girls, she looks so fresh and quiet, and dances like a lady.\"", "\"네. 정말 산뜻하고 차분해 보이고, 숙녀답게 춤추는 게 독일 아가씨들이 떠오르네요.\""]], [["Jo quite glowed with pleasure at this boyish praise of her sister, and stored it up to repeat to Meg.", "언니를 향한 이 소년다운 칭찬에 조는 기쁨으로 환하게 달아올라, 메그에게 그대로 전해 줄 요량으로 그 말을 마음속에 차곡차곡 담아 두었다. 둘은 함께 내다보고, 품평하고, 수다를 떨다가 어느새 오랜 친구라도 된 듯한 기분이 들었다."], ["Both peeped and criticised and chatted, till they felt like old acquaintances.", "로리의 수줍음도 금세 가셨다. 조의 신사다운 태도가 그를 즐겁게 하고 마음을 편하게 해 주었기 때문이다."], ["Laurie's bashfulness soon wore off; for Jo's gentlemanly demeanor amused and set him at his ease, and Jo was her merry self again, because her dress was forgotten, and nobody lifted their eyebrows at her. She liked the \"Laurence boy\"", "조 역시 다시 명랑한 본래 모습으로 돌아왔는데, 드레스 걱정도 잊었고 아무도 그녀에게 눈썹을 치켜올리지 않았기 때문이다."], ["better than ever, and took several good looks at him, so that she might describe him to the girls; for they had no brothers, very few male cousins, and boys were almost unknown creatures to them.", "그녀는 \"로런스 도련님\"이 그 어느 때보다 마음에 들었고, 여동생들에게 그를 묘사해 줄 수 있도록 몇 번이나 찬찬히 뜯어보았다. 그들에겐 남자 형제도 없었고, 사내 사촌도 거의 없어서, 소년이란 거의 미지의 생물이나 다름없었던 것이다."]], [["\"Curly black hair; brown skin; big, black eyes; handsome nose; fine teeth; small hands and feet; taller than I am; very polite, for a boy, and altogether jolly.", "'곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리에 까무잡잡한 피부, 크고 까만 눈, 잘생긴 코, 가지런한 치아, 작은 손발, 키는 나보다 크고, 소년치고는 무척 예의 바르고, 무엇보다 유쾌해."], ["Wonder how old he is?\"", "나이가 몇 살일까?'"]], [["It was on the tip of Jo's tongue to ask; but she checked herself in time, and, with unusual tact, tried to find out in a roundabout way.", "조는 그 말이 입 밖으로 튀어나오려는 걸 가까스로 참고, 평소답지 않은 재치를 발휘해 빙 둘러 알아내려 했다."]], [["\"I suppose you are going to college soon?", "\"머지않아 대학에 가시겠죠?"], ["I see you pegging away at your books,--no, I mean studying hard\"; and Jo blushed at the dreadful \"pegging\"", "한창 책에 코를 박고 끙끙대시는 거 봤어요. 아니, 열심히 공부하신다는 뜻이에요.\""], ["which had escaped her.", "그러고는 자기도 모르게 튀어나온 끔찍한 \"끙끙대다\"라는 말에 조는 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["Laurie smiled, but didn't seem shocked, and answered, with a shrug,--", "로리는 미소만 지을 뿐 별로 충격받은 기색 없이, 어깨를 으쓱하며 대답했다."]], [["\"Not for a year or two; I won't go before seventeen, anyway.\"", "\"앞으로 한두 해는 안 가요. 어쨌든 열일곱 살 전엔 안 갈 거예요.\""]], [["\"Aren't you but fifteen?\"", "\"열다섯밖에 안 됐어요?\""], ["asked Jo, looking at the tall lad, whom she had imagined seventeen already.", "조가, 이미 열일곱쯤 됐으리라 짐작했던 그 키 큰 소년을 바라보며 물었다."]], [["\"Sixteen, next month.\"", "\"다음 달이면 열여섯이에요.\""]], [["\"How I wish I was going to college!", "\"나도 대학에 갈 수 있다면 얼마나 좋을까!"], ["You don't look as if you liked it.\"", "도련님은 가는 게 별로 안 좋은가 봐요.\""]], [["\"I hate it!", "\"질색이에요!"], ["Nothing but grinding or skylarking.", "죽도록 공부만 하거나 멍청하게 노닥거리는 것뿐이거든요."], ["And I don't like the way fellows do either, in this country.\"", "게다가 이 나라 애들이 노는 방식도 마음에 안 들어요.\""]], [["\"What do you like?\"", "\"그럼 뭘 좋아해요?\""]], [["\"To live in Italy, and to enjoy myself in my own way.\"", "\"이탈리아에서 살면서, 내 방식대로 즐기는 거요.\""]], [["Jo wanted very much to ask what his own way was; but his black brows looked rather threatening as he knit them; so she changed the subject by saying, as her foot kept time, \"That's a splendid polka!", "조는 그 '내 방식'이 뭔지 몹시 묻고 싶었지만, 그가 검은 눈썹을 찌푸리는 모습이 어쩐지 위협적으로 보여, 발로 박자를 맞추며 화제를 돌렸다. \"저거 정말 멋진 폴카네요!"], ["Why don't you go and try it?\"", "가서 한번 춰 보지 그래요?\""]], [["\"If you will come too,\"", "\"같이 추신다면요.\""], ["he answered, with a gallant little bow.", "그가 정중하게 살짝 고개를 숙이며 대답했다."]], [["\"I can't; for I told Meg I wouldn't, because--\"", "\"난 못 춰요. 메그 언니한테 안 추겠다고 했거든요."], ["There Jo stopped, and looked undecided whether to tell or to laugh.", "왜냐하면…….\" 거기서 조는 말을 멈추고, 털어놓을지 웃어넘길지 망설이는 표정을 지었다."]], [["\"Because what?\"", "\"왜냐하면 뭔데요?\""], ["asked Laurie curiously.", "로리가 궁금해하며 물었다."]], [["\"You won't tell?\"", "\"말 안 할 거죠?\""]], [["\"Well, I have a bad trick of standing before the fire, and so I burn my frocks, and I scorched this one; and, though it's nicely mended, it shows, and Meg told me to keep still, so no one would see it. You may laugh, if you want to; it is funny, I know.\"", "\"절대 안 해요!\" \"음, 난 난롯불 앞에 서 있는 나쁜 버릇이 있어서, 드레스를 자주 태워 먹어요. 이것도 그렇게 그을려 버렸죠. 그래서, 잘 꿰매긴 했어도 자국이 보여요. 메그 언니가 아무도 못 보게 가만히 있으라고 했고요. 웃고 싶으면 웃어도 돼요. 우습다는 거 나도 알아요.\""]], [["But Laurie didn't laugh; he only looked down a minute, and the expression of his face puzzled Jo, when he said very gently,--", "그러나 로리는 웃지 않았다. 그저 잠깐 고개를 숙였는데, 그 표정이 조로서는 알 수 없었다. 이윽고 그가 아주 다정하게 말했다."]], [["\"Never mind that; I'll tell you how we can manage: there's a long hall out there, and we can dance grandly, and no one will see us.", "\"그런 건 신경 쓰지 마세요. 어떻게 하면 되는지 알려 줄게요."], ["Please come?\"", "저쪽에 긴 복도가 있는데, 거기서 신나게 춤추면 아무도 우리를 못 볼 거예요. 같이 가요, 네?\""]], [["Jo thanked him, and gladly went, wishing she had two neat gloves, when she saw the nice, pearl-colored ones her partner wore.", "조는 고맙다고 하고는 기꺼이 따라나섰는데, 짝꿍이 끼고 있는 고운 진주색 장갑을 보자 자기도 멀쩡한 장갑 두 짝이 있었으면 싶었다."], ["The hall was empty, and they had a grand polka; for Laurie danced well, and taught her the German step, which delighted Jo, being full of swing and spring.", "복도는 텅 비어 있었고, 둘은 멋진 폴카를 추었다. 로리는 춤 솜씨가 좋았고, 조에게 독일식 스텝을 가르쳐 주었는데, 휘돌고 튕기는 동작이 가득해서 조는 무척 신이 났다."], ["When the music stopped, they sat down on the stairs to get their breath; and Laurie was in the midst of an account of a students' festival at Heidelberg, when Meg appeared in search of her sister.", "음악이 멈추자 둘은 계단에 앉아 숨을 골랐고, 로리가 하이델베르크의 학생 축제 이야기를 한창 들려주고 있을 때, 메그가 동생을 찾아 나타났다."], ["She beckoned, and Jo reluctantly followed her into a side-room, where she found her on a sofa, holding her foot, and looking pale.", "그녀가 손짓하자 조는 마지못해 따라 옆방으로 갔는데, 메그는 소파에 앉아 발을 부여잡은 채 핼쑥한 얼굴을 하고 있었다."]], [["\"I've sprained my ankle.", "\"발목을 삐었어."], ["That stupid high heel turned, and gave me a sad wrench.", "그 멍청한 굽이 꺾이는 바람에 발이 심하게 접질렸어."], ["It aches so, I can hardly stand, and I don't know how I'm ever going to get home,\"", "너무 아파서 거의 서 있질 못하겠어. 이러고 어떻게 집에 가야 할지 모르겠다.\""], ["she said, rocking to and fro in pain.", "그녀가 아파서 몸을 앞뒤로 흔들며 말했다."]], [["\"I knew you'd hurt your feet with those silly shoes.", "\"그 바보 같은 신발 때문에 언니 발 다칠 줄 알았어."], ["I'm sorry.", "안됐다."], ["But I don't see what you can do, except get a carriage, or stay here all night,\"", "그렇지만 마차를 부르든가, 여기서 밤을 새우든가 하는 것 말고는 무슨 수가 있을지 모르겠어.\""], ["answered Jo, softly rubbing the poor ankle as she spoke.", "조가 그렇게 말하며 가엾은 발목을 살살 문질러 주었다."]], [["\"I can't have a carriage, without its costing ever so much.", "\"마차는 돈이 엄청나게 들어서 안 돼. 사실 부를 수나 있을지 모르겠어."], ["I dare say I can't get one at all; for most people come in their own, and it's a long way to the stable, and no one to send.\"", "다들 자기 마차를 타고 왔을 테고, 마구간까지는 멀고, 심부름 보낼 사람도 없잖아.\" \"내가 갔다 올게.\""]], [["\"I'll go.\" \"No, indeed!", "\"안 돼, 절대로!"], ["It's past nine, and dark as Egypt.", "아홉 시가 넘어서 밖이 칠흑같이 어두운걸."], ["I can't stop here, for the house is full.", "그렇다고 여기 머물 수도 없어. 집이 손님으로 꽉 찼거든."], ["Sallie has some girls staying with her.", "샐리네 묵는 여자애들도 있고."], ["I'll rest till Hannah comes, and then do the best I can.\"", "한나가 올 때까지 쉬다가, 어떻게든 해 봐야지.\""]], [["\"I'll ask Laurie; he will go,\"", "\"로리한테 부탁할게. 걔가 갔다 올 거야.\""], ["said Jo, looking relieved as the idea occurred to her.", "그 생각이 떠오르자 조가 마음이 놓인 표정으로 말했다."]], [["\"Mercy, no!", "\"맙소사, 안 돼!"], ["Don't ask or tell any one.", "아무한테도 묻지도 말고 말하지도 마."], ["Get me my rubbers, and put these slippers with our things.", "내 덧신이나 갖다주고, 이 슬리퍼는 우리 물건이랑 같이 챙겨 둬."], ["I can't dance any more; but as soon as supper is over, watch for Hannah, and tell me the minute she comes.\"", "이제 춤은 더 못 추겠어. 그래도 저녁 식사가 끝나는 대로 한나가 오는지 잘 봐 뒀다가, 오는 즉시 나한테 알려 줘.\""]], [["\"They are going out to supper now.", "\"지금 다들 저녁 먹으러 나가는걸."], ["I'll stay with you; I'd rather.\"", "난 언니랑 같이 있을래. 그게 더 좋아.\""]], [["\"No, dear, run along, and bring me some coffee.", "\"아니야, 얘, 어서 가서 커피나 좀 갖다줘."], ["I'm so tired, I can't stir!\"", "너무 지쳐서 꼼짝도 못 하겠어!\""]], [["So Meg reclined, with rubbers well hidden, and Jo went blundering away to the dining-room, which she found after going into a china-closet, and opening the door of a room where old Mr.", "그래서 메그는 덧신을 잘 감추고 비스듬히 기대앉았고, 조는 우당탕거리며 식당으로 향했다. 도자기 찬장으로 잘못 들어가 보고, 가드너 노인이 혼자 가벼운 요기를 하고 있는 방문을 열어 보고 나서야 겨우 식당을 찾아냈다."], ["Gardiner was taking a little private refreshment. Making a dart at the table, she secured the coffee, which she immediately spilt, thereby making the front of her dress as bad as the back.", "그녀는 식탁으로 잽싸게 달려들어 커피를 손에 넣었지만, 곧바로 쏟아 버리는 바람에 드레스 앞쪽도 뒤쪽만큼이나 엉망으로 만들고 말았다."]], [["\"Oh, dear, what a blunderbuss I am!\"", "\"어머나, 난 어쩜 이렇게 덤벙거릴까!\""], ["exclaimed Jo, finishing Meg's glove by scrubbing her gown with it.", "조가 외치며, 메그의 장갑으로 드레스를 박박 문질러 그 한 짝마저 못 쓰게 만들었다."]], [["\"Can I help you?\"", "\"제가 도와드릴까요?\""], ["said a friendly voice; and there was Laurie, with a full cup in one hand and a plate of ice in the other.", "다정한 목소리가 들려왔다. 한 손엔 가득 찬 잔을, 다른 손엔 얼음 접시를 든 로리였다."]], [["\"I was trying to get something for Meg, who is very tired, and some one shook me; and here I am, in a nice state,\"", "\"메그 언니한테 뭐 좀 갖다주려던 참이었어요. 언니가 많이 지쳐서요."], ["answered Jo, glancing dismally from the stained skirt to the coffee-colored glove.", "그런데 누가 절 밀치는 바람에, 이 꼴이 됐지 뭐예요.\" 조가 얼룩진 치마에서 커피색으로 물든 장갑으로 침울하게 눈길을 옮기며 대답했다."]], [["\"Too bad!", "\"저런!"], ["I was looking for some one to give this to.", "마침 이걸 줄 사람을 찾고 있었어요."], ["May I take it to your sister?\"", "제가 언니한테 갖다드려도 될까요?\""]], [["\"Oh, thank you!", "\"어머, 고마워요!"], ["I'll show you where she is.", "어디 있는지 알려 드릴게요."], ["I don't offer to take it myself, for I should only get into another scrape if I did.\"", "제가 직접 갖다주겠다고는 못 하겠어요. 그랬다간 또 사고나 칠 게 뻔하거든요.\""]], [["Jo led the way; and, as if used to waiting on ladies, Laurie drew up a little table, brought a second instalment of coffee and ice for Jo, and was so obliging that even particular Meg pronounced him a \"nice boy.\"", "조가 앞장섰다. 그리고 로리는 마치 숙녀 시중에 익숙한 사람처럼 작은 탁자를 끌어다 놓고, 조에게도 커피와 얼음을 두 번째로 가져다주며 어찌나 살뜰히 굴던지, 까다로운 메그조차 그를 \"착한 소년\"이라고 인정했다."], ["They had a merry time over the bonbons and mottoes, and were in the midst of a quiet game of \"Buzz,\"", "셋은 봉봉과 사탕 포장지에 적힌 글귀를 두고 즐거운 시간을 보냈고, 어슬렁어슬렁 끼어든 다른 젊은이 두세 명과 함께 \"버즈\""], ["with two or three other young people who had strayed in, when Hannah appeared.", "놀이를 조용히 한창 하고 있을 때 한나가 나타났다."], ["Meg forgot her foot, and rose so quickly that she was forced to catch hold of Jo, with an exclamation of pain.", "메그는 발 아픈 것도 잊고 너무 급히 일어서는 바람에, 아파서 짧은 소리를 내며 조를 붙잡고 말았다."]], [["\"Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["Don't say anything,\"", "아무 말도 하지 마.\""], ["she whispered, adding aloud, \"It's nothing.", "그녀가 속삭이고는, 큰 소리로 덧붙였다. \"아무것도 아니야."], ["I turned my foot a little, that's all\"; and limped up-stairs to put her things on.", "발을 살짝 접질렸을 뿐이야.\" 그러고는 절뚝이며 위층으로 올라가 채비를 했다."]], [["Hannah scolded, Meg cried, and Jo was at her wits'", "한나는 잔소리를 했고, 메그는 울었으며, 조는 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다."], ["end, till she decided to take things into her own hands.", "결국 그녀는 일을 직접 처리하기로 마음먹었다."], ["Slipping out, she ran down, and, finding a servant, asked if he could get her a carriage.", "슬그머니 빠져나가 아래층으로 달려간 그녀는, 하인을 발견하고 마차를 좀 구해 줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었다. 그런데 하필 그는 고용된 임시 웨이터라서 이 동네에 대해선 아무것도 몰랐다."], ["It happened to be a hired waiter, who knew nothing about the neighborhood; and Jo was looking round for help, when Laurie, who had heard what she said, came up, and offered his grandfather's carriage, which had just come for him, he said.", "조가 도움을 청할 사람이 없나 두리번거리고 있는데, 그녀의 말을 들은 로리가 다가오더니 자기 할아버지의 마차를 내주겠다고 했다. 마침 자기를 데리러 막 도착한 참이라는 것이었다."]], [["\"It's so early!", "\"아직 너무 이르잖아요!"], ["You can't mean to go yet?\"", "설마 벌써 가려는 건 아니죠?\""], ["began Jo, looking relieved, but hesitating to accept the offer.", "조가 마음이 놓이면서도 그 제안을 선뜻 받아들이기를 망설이며 입을 열었다."]], [["\"I always go early,--I do, truly!", "\"전 늘 일찍 가요. 정말이에요!"], ["Please let me take you home?", "집까지 모셔다드리게 해 주세요."], ["It's all on my way, you know, and it rains, they say.\"", "어차피 가는 길인 데다, 비도 온다잖아요.\""]], [["That settled it; and, telling him of Meg's mishap, Jo gratefully accepted, and rushed up to bring down the rest of the party.", "그 말에 결정이 났다. 조는 메그가 다친 일을 그에게 알리고는 고맙게 그 제안을 받아들였고, 위층으로 달려가 나머지 일행을 데리고 내려왔다."], ["Hannah hated rain as much as a cat does; so she made no trouble, and they rolled away in the luxurious close carriage, feeling very festive and elegant.", "한나는 고양이만큼이나 비를 싫어하는 터라 군말 없이 따랐고, 일행은 호화로운 밀폐 마차에 올라 무척 들뜨고 우아한 기분으로 굴러갔다."], ["Laurie went on the box; so Meg could keep her foot up, and the girls talked over their party in freedom.", "로리는 마부석에 올라탔고, 덕분에 메그는 발을 올려놓을 수 있었으며, 자매는 마음 편히 무도회 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["\"I had a capital time.", "\"난 정말 재미있었어."], ["Did you?\"", "언니는?\""], ["asked Jo, rumpling up her hair, and making herself comfortable.", "조가 머리를 헝클어뜨리며 편안하게 자세를 고쳐 앉고 물었다."]], [["\"Yes, till I hurt myself.", "\"응, 다치기 전까지는."], ["Sallie's friend, Annie Moffat, took a fancy to me, and asked me to come and spend a week with her, when Sallie does.", "샐리의 친구 애니 모팻이 나한테 마음에 들었나 봐. 샐리가 갈 때 자기 집에 와서 일주일 묵으라고 했어."], ["She is going in the spring, when the opera comes; and it will be perfectly splendid, if mother only lets me go,\"", "봄에 오페라가 올라올 때 간다는데, 엄마가 가게만 해 주신다면 정말 끝내주게 멋질 거야.\""], ["answered Meg, cheering up at the thought.", "메그가 그 생각에 기운을 차리며 대답했다."]], [["\"I saw you dancing with the red-headed man I ran away from.", "\"내가 도망쳐 나온 그 빨강머리 남자랑 언니가 춤추는 거 봤어."], ["Was he nice?\"", "괜찮은 사람이었어?\""]], [["\"Oh, very!", "\"응, 아주! 빨강이 아니라 적갈색 머리야."], ["His hair is auburn, not red; and he was very polite, and I had a delicious redowa with him.\"", "그리고 무척 예의 바르더라. 그 사람이랑 레도바를 황홀하게 췄어.\""]], [["\"He looked like a grasshopper in a fit, when he did the new step.", "\"새 스텝을 밟을 때 보니까 발작 일으킨 메뚜기 같던데."], ["Laurie and I couldn't help laughing.", "로리랑 나랑 웃음을 참을 수가 없었어."], ["Did you hear us?\"", "우리 웃는 소리 들었어?\""]], [["\"No; but it was very rude.", "\"아니. 그래도 그건 무척 무례한 짓이야."], ["What _were_ you about all that time, hidden away there?\"", "그렇게 거기 숨어서 그동안 도대체 뭘 한 거니?\""]], [["Jo told her adventures, and, by the time she had finished, they were at home. With many thanks, they said \"Good night,\"", "조가 자기 모험담을 들려주었고, 이야기를 마칠 즈음 마차는 집에 다다랐다."], ["and crept in, hoping to disturb no one; but the instant their door creaked, two little night-caps bobbed up, and two sleepy but eager voices cried out,--", "둘은 거듭 고맙다고 인사하며 \"안녕히 가세요\"라고 한 뒤, 아무도 깨우지 않으려고 살금살금 안으로 들어갔다. 그러나 문이 삐걱대는 순간, 작은 잠자리 모자 두 개가 불쑥 솟아오르더니, 졸음에 겨우면서도 들뜬 목소리 둘이 외쳤다."]], [["\"Tell about the party!", "\"무도회 얘기 해 줘!"], ["tell about the party!\"", "무도회 얘기 해 달라고!\""]], [["With what Meg called \"a great want of manners,\"", "메그가 \"예의범절이 영 모자란다\"고 한 행동이었지만, 조는 동생들 주려고 봉봉을 좀 챙겨 두었었다."], ["Jo had saved some bonbons for the little girls; and they soon subsided, after hearing the most thrilling events of the evening.", "그래서 두 아이는 그날 저녁의 가장 짜릿한 사건들을 듣고 나서 곧 잠잠해졌다."]], [["\"I declare, it really seems like being a fine young lady, to come home from the party in a carriage, and sit in my dressing-gown, with a maid to wait on me,\"", "\"정말이지, 무도회에서 마차 타고 집에 돌아와서, 시중드는 하녀까지 두고 실내복 차림으로 앉아 있으니, 진짜 우아한 아가씨가 된 기분이야.\" 조가 아르니카로 발을 싸매고 머리를 빗겨 주는 동안 메그가 말했다."], ["said Meg, as Jo bound up her foot with arnica, and brushed her hair.", "\"우아한 아가씨들이라고 해서 우리보다 조금이라도 더 즐겁진 않을걸. 우리 머리가 그을리고, 드레스가 낡고, 장갑이 한 짝뿐이고, 멍청하게 신은 굽 높은 슬리퍼 때문에 발목을 삐긴 했어도 말이야.\""]], [["\"I don't believe fine young ladies enjoy themselves a bit more than we do, in spite of our burnt hair, old gowns, one glove apiece, and tight slippers that sprain our ankles when we are silly enough to wear them.\"", "그리고 내 생각엔, 조의 말이 전적으로 옳았다."], ["And I think Jo was quite right.", "4."]], [["\"Oh dear, how hard it does seem to take up our packs and go on,\"", "짐. \"아휴, 다시 짐을 짊어지고 길을 나서기란 어쩜 이렇게도 힘이 들까.\""], ["sighed Meg, the morning after the party; for, now the holidays were over, the week of merry-making did not fit her for going on easily with the task she never liked.", "무도회 다음 날 아침, 메그가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. 이제 연휴가 끝났는데, 한 주 내내 즐기며 보낸 탓에 평소에도 좋아한 적 없는 일을 다시 수월하게 해 나갈 마음가짐이 도무지 되지 않았던 것이다."]], [["\"I wish it was Christmas or New-Year all the time; wouldn't it be fun?\"", "\"일 년 내내 크리스마스나 새해였으면 좋겠다. 그럼 얼마나 신날까?\""], ["answered Jo, yawning dismally.", "조가 침울하게 하품하며 대꾸했다."]], [["\"We shouldn't enjoy ourselves half so much as we do now.", "\"그럼 지금만큼 즐겁지도 않을걸."], ["But it does seem so nice to have little suppers and bouquets, and go to parties, and drive home, and read and rest, and not work.", "그래도 자그마한 저녁 만찬에 꽃다발도 받고, 무도회에 가고, 마차 타고 돌아오고, 책 읽고 쉬면서 일은 안 한다는 게 참 좋긴 해. 다른 사람들처럼 사는 거잖아."], ["It's like other people, you know, and I always envy girls who do such things; I'm so fond of luxury,\"", "난 그런 걸 하는 여자애들이 늘 부럽더라. 호사스러운 게 어찌나 좋은지.\""], ["said Meg, trying to decide which of two shabby gowns was the least shabby.", "메그가 후줄근한 드레스 두 벌 가운데 어느 쪽이 덜 후줄근한지 가려내려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["\"Well, we can't have it, so don't let us grumble, but shoulder our bundles and trudge along as cheerfully as Marmee does.", "\"뭐, 그렇게는 못 사니까 투덜대지 말고, 엄마처럼 짐을 둘러메고 명랑하게 터덜터덜 걸어가자고."], ["I'm sure Aunt March is a regular Old Man of the Sea to me, but I suppose when I've learned to carry her without complaining, she will tumble off, or get so light that I sha'n't mind her.\"", "나한테 마치 고모는 영락없는 '바다의 노인'이야. 그래도 불평 없이 짊어지는 법을 익히고 나면, 알아서 떨어져 나가든가, 아니면 너무 가벼워져서 신경도 안 쓰이게 되겠지.\""]], [["This idea tickled Jo's fancy, and put her in good spirits; but Meg didn't brighten, for her burden, consisting of four spoilt children, seemed heavier than ever.", "이 생각이 조의 상상력을 간질여 기분을 띄워 주었다. 하지만 메그는 도무지 밝아지지 않았다."], ["She hadn't heart enough even to make herself pretty, as usual, by putting on a blue neck-ribbon, and dressing her hair in the most becoming way.", "버릇없는 아이 넷으로 이루어진 그녀의 짐이 그 어느 때보다 무겁게만 느껴졌던 것이다. 그녀는 평소처럼 파란 목 리본을 매고 머리를 가장 어울리게 매만져 예쁘게 꾸밀 마음조차 들지 않았다."]], [["\"Where's the use of looking nice, when no one sees me but those cross midgets, and no one cares whether I'm pretty or not?\"", "\"심술쟁이 꼬맹이들 말고는 봐 줄 사람도 없고, 내가 예쁘든 말든 아무도 신경 안 쓰는데, 곱게 차려입은들 무슨 소용이야?\""], ["she muttered, shutting her drawer with a jerk.", "그녀가 서랍을 홱 닫으며 중얼거렸다."], ["\"I shall have to toil and moil all my days, with only little bits of fun now and then, and get old and ugly and sour, because I'm poor, and can't enjoy my life as other girls do.", "\"난 평생 죽도록 힘들게 일하면서, 어쩌다 한 번씩 자그마한 즐거움이나 누리다가, 늙고 못생기고 심술궂게 변해 가겠지. 가난한 데다, 다른 여자애들처럼 인생을 즐길 수도 없으니까."], ["It's a shame!\"", "정말 너무해!\""]], [["So Meg went down, wearing an injured look, and wasn't at all agreeable at breakfast-time.", "그래서 메그는 잔뜩 상처 입은 표정으로 아래층에 내려왔고, 아침 식사 시간 내내 영 기분이 좋지 않았다."], ["Every one seemed rather out of sorts, and inclined to croak.", "다들 어쩐지 짜증이 나서 투덜대고 싶은 기색이었다."], ["Beth had a headache, and lay on the sofa, trying to comfort herself with the cat and three kittens; Amy was fretting because her lessons were not learned, and she couldn't find her rubbers; Jo _would_ whistle and make a great racket getting ready; Mrs.", "베스는 머리가 아파 소파에 누운 채 고양이와 새끼 고양이 세 마리로 마음을 달래려 했고, 에이미는 숙제도 못 했는데 덧신마저 못 찾아 안달이었으며, 조는 휘파람을 불어 대며 채비하느라 한바탕 요란을 떨었다."], ["March was very busy trying to finish a letter, which must go at once; and Hannah had the grumps, for being up late didn't suit her.", "마치 부인은 당장 부쳐야 할 편지를 마무리하느라 무척 바빴고, 한나는 늦게까지 깨어 있던 게 영 맞지 않아 잔뜩 부어 있었다."]], [["\"There never _was_ such a cross family!\"", "\"이렇게 심술궂은 집은 정말 어디에도 없을 거야!\""], ["cried Jo, losing her temper when she had upset an inkstand, broken both boot-lacings, and sat down upon her hat.", "조가 잉크병을 엎고, 부츠 끈을 양쪽 다 끊어 먹고, 자기 모자 위에 주저앉고 나서 발끈하며 외쳤다."]], [["\"You're the crossest person in it!\"", "\"이 집에서 제일 심술궂은 사람은 언니거든!\""], ["returned Amy, washing out the sum, that was all wrong, with the tears that had fallen on her slate.", "에이미가 죄다 틀린 계산을, 석판 위로 떨어진 눈물로 지워 가며 받아쳤다."]], [["\"Beth, if you don't keep these horrid cats down cellar I'll have them drowned,\"", "\"베스, 이 끔찍한 고양이들 지하실에 안 가둬 두면 다 물에 빠뜨려 버릴 거야.\""], ["exclaimed Meg angrily, as she tried to get rid of the kitten, which had scrambled up her back, and stuck like a burr just out of reach.", "메그가 화를 내며 외쳤다. 새끼 고양이 한 마리가 등을 타고 기어 올라와 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 들러붙어 있는 걸 떼어 내려던 참이었다."]], [["Jo laughed, Meg scolded, Beth implored, and Amy wailed, because she couldn't remember how much nine times twelve was.", "조는 웃고, 메그는 잔소리하고, 베스는 애원하고, 에이미는 9 곱하기 12가 얼마였는지 기억나지 않아 울먹였다."]], [["\"Girls, girls, do be quiet one minute!", "\"얘들아, 얘들아, 잠깐만 좀 조용히 해 다오!"], ["I _must_ get this off by the early mail, and you drive me distracted with your worry,\"", "이걸 첫 우편에 꼭 부쳐야 하는데, 너희가 떠들어 대니 정신이 하나도 없구나.\""], ["cried Mrs. March, crossing out the third spoilt sentence in her letter.", "마치 부인이 편지에서 세 번째로 망친 문장을 그어 지우며 외쳤다."]], [["There was a momentary lull, broken by Hannah, who stalked in, laid two hot turn-overs on the table, and stalked out again.", "잠시 잠잠해졌다가, 한나가 성큼성큼 들어와 따끈한 턴오버 두 개를 식탁에 올려놓고는 다시 성큼성큼 나가면서 그 정적이 깨졌다. 이 턴오버는 하나의 관례였다."], ["These turn-overs were an institution; and the girls called them \"muffs,\"", "자매는 다른 게 없었으므로 그것을 \"토시\"라고 불렀는데, 추운 아침이면 따뜻한 파이가 손을 녹여 주어 큰 위안이 되었다."], ["for they had no others, and found the hot pies very comforting to their hands on cold mornings.", "한나는 아무리 바쁘거나 심사가 뒤틀려도 한 번도 그것 만드는 걸 잊지 않았다."], ["Hannah never forgot to make them, no matter how busy or grumpy she might be, for the walk was long and bleak; the poor things got no other lunch, and were seldom home before two.", "길은 멀고 황량한 데다, 가엾은 자매는 다른 점심거리도 없었고, 두 시 전에 집에 돌아오는 일이 좀처럼 없었기 때문이다. * 턴오버: 반죽을 반으로 접어 속을 넣고 구운 파이 : 옮긴이."]], [["\"Cuddle your cats, and get over your headache, Bethy.", "\"고양이들 꼭 끌어안고, 두통도 나으렴, 베스. 안녕히 계세요, 엄마."], ["Good-by, Marmee; we are a set of rascals this morning, but we'll come home regular angels.", "오늘 아침 우리가 영 못된 망나니들이었지만, 집에 올 때는 영락없는 천사가 되어 올게요."], ["Now then, Meg!\"", "자, 가자, 메그!\""], ["and Jo tramped away, feeling that the pilgrims were not setting out as they ought to do.", "그렇게 조는 성큼성큼 걸어 나가면서, 순례자들이 마땅히 그래야 할 모습으로 길을 나서지는 못했다고 느꼈다."]], [["They always looked back before turning the corner, for their mother was always at the window, to nod and smile, and wave her hand to them.", "자매는 모퉁이를 돌기 전에 늘 뒤를 돌아보았다. 엄마가 언제나 창가에 서서 고개를 끄덕이고 미소 지으며 손을 흔들어 주었기 때문이다."], ["Somehow it seemed as if they couldn't have got through the day without that; for, whatever their mood might be, the last glimpse of that motherly face was sure to affect them like sunshine.", "어쩐지 그것 없이는 하루를 견뎌 낼 수 없을 것만 같았다. 기분이 어떻든 간에, 마지막으로 흘끗 본 그 자애로운 얼굴은 어김없이 햇살처럼 그들의 마음에 와닿았다."]], [["\"If Marmee shook her fist instead of kissing her hand to us, it would serve us right, for more ungrateful wretches than we are were never seen,\"", "\"엄마가 우리한테 손에 입맞춰 흔들어 주는 대신 주먹을 휘두른다 해도 우린 싸. 우리보다 더 배은망덕한 못난이들은 세상에 없을 테니까.\""], ["cried Jo, taking a remorseful satisfaction in the snowy walk and bitter wind.", "조가 외치며, 눈 덮인 길과 매서운 바람 속에서 회한 섞인 만족을 느꼈다."]], [["\"Don't use such dreadful expressions,\"", "\"그런 끔찍한 말 좀 쓰지 마.\""], ["said Meg, from the depths of the vail in which she had shrouded herself like a nun sick of the world.", "세상에 진력난 수녀처럼 자기 몸을 휘감은 베일 속 깊은 곳에서 메그가 말했다."]], [["\"I like good strong words, that mean something,\"", "\"난 뭔가 뜻이 담긴, 야무지고 힘찬 말이 좋단 말이야.\""], ["replied Jo, catching her hat as it took a leap off her head, preparatory to flying away altogether.", "조가, 모자가 머리에서 폴짝 뛰어올라 아예 날아가 버릴 채비를 하는 걸 붙잡으며 대꾸했다."]], [["\"Call yourself any names you like; but _I_ am neither a rascal nor a wretch, and I don't choose to be called so.\"", "\"넌 너 좋을 대로 무슨 이름으로든 자처해. 그렇지만 나는 망나니도 아니고 못난이도 아니니까, 그렇게 불리고 싶지 않아.\""]], [["\"You're a blighted being, and decidedly cross to-day because you can't sit in the lap of luxury all the time.", "\"언니는 시들어 버린 가엾은 존재라서, 늘 호사의 무릎에만 앉아 있을 수 없다는 이유로 오늘 영 심술이 났구나."], ["Poor dear, just wait till I make my fortune, and you shall revel in carriages and ice-cream and high-heeled slippers and posies and red-headed boys to dance with.\"", "가엾은 우리 언니, 내가 한밑천 잡을 때까지 기다리기만 해. 그럼 마차에, 아이스크림에, 굽 높은 슬리퍼에, 꽃다발에, 같이 춤출 빨강머리 소년들까지 실컷 누리게 해 줄게.\""]], [["\"How ridiculous you are, Jo!\"", "\"넌 어쩜 그렇게 우스꽝스럽니, 조!\""], ["but Meg laughed at the nonsense, and felt better in spite of herself.", "그러면서도 메그는 그 허튼소리에 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 자기도 모르게 기분이 한결 나아졌다."]], [["\"Lucky for you I am; for if I put on crushed airs, and tried to be dismal, as you do, we should be in a nice state.", "\"내가 그런 게 언니한텐 다행이지. 만일 나까지 언니처럼 풀 죽은 척, 침울한 척하고 다니면 우리 꼴이 어떻게 되겠어."], ["Thank goodness, I can always find something funny to keep me up.", "다행히도 난 늘 기운 낼 우스운 거리를 찾아낼 줄 안다고."], ["Don't croak any more, but come home jolly, there's a dear.\"", "더는 투덜대지 말고, 집에 올 땐 명랑하게 와. 알겠지, 우리 언니.\""]], [["Jo gave her sister an encouraging pat on the shoulder as they parted for the day, each going a different way, each hugging her little warm turn-over, and each trying to be cheerful in spite of wintry weather, hard work, and the unsatisfied desires of pleasure-loving youth.", "조는 헤어지며 언니의 어깨를 격려하듯 토닥였다. 그렇게 둘은 각자 다른 길로 들어서서, 저마다 따뜻한 턴오버를 꼭 끌어안고, 겨울 날씨와 고된 일과 즐거움을 갈망하는 젊은 날의 채워지지 않는 욕구에도 불구하고 명랑해지려 애썼다."]], [["When Mr. March lost his property in trying to help an unfortunate friend, the two oldest girls begged to be allowed to do something toward their own support, at least.", "마치 씨가 불운한 친구를 도우려다 재산을 잃었을 때, 두 맏딸은 적어도 제 앞가림은 스스로 하게 해 달라고 졸랐다."], ["Believing that they could not begin too early to cultivate energy, industry, and independence, their parents consented, and both fell to work with the hearty good-will which in spite of all obstacles, is sure to succeed at last.", "활력과 근면, 자립심을 기르는 데에는 아무리 일찍 시작해도 이르지 않다고 믿었기에, 부모는 이를 허락했고, 둘은 온갖 장애에도 끝내 성공하고야 마는 진심 어린 의지를 품고 일에 뛰어들었다."], ["Margaret found a place as nursery governess, and felt rich with her small salary.", "마거릿은 보모 겸 가정교사 자리를 얻었고, 박봉이나마 그것으로 부자가 된 기분이었다."], ["As she said, she _was_ \"fond of luxury,\" and her chief trouble was poverty.", "그녀가 말했듯 그녀는 \"호사스러운 걸 좋아\"했고, 그녀의 가장 큰 괴로움은 가난이었다."], ["She found it harder to bear than the others, because she could remember a time when home was beautiful, life full of ease and pleasure, and want of any kind unknown.", "그녀는 다른 자매들보다 그것을 견디기가 더 힘들었다. 집이 아름답고, 삶이 안락과 즐거움으로 가득하며, 그 어떤 결핍도 모르던 시절을 기억하고 있었기 때문이다."], ["She tried not to be envious or discontented, but it was very natural that the young girl should long for pretty things, gay friends, accomplishments, and a happy life. At the Kings'", "시샘하거나 불만스러워하지 않으려 애썼지만, 어린 소녀가 예쁜 물건들과 화사한 친구들, 갖가지 교양과 행복한 삶을 갈망하는 것은 지극히 당연한 일이었다."], ["she daily saw all she wanted, for the children's older sisters were just out, and Meg caught frequent glimpses of dainty ball-dresses and bouquets, heard lively gossip about theatres, concerts, sleighing parties, and merry-makings of all kinds, and saw money lavished on trifles which would have been so precious to her.", "킹 씨네 집에서 그녀는 자기가 원하는 모든 것을 날마다 보았다. 그 집 아이들의 손위 언니들이 막 사교계에 나온 참이라, 메그는 우아한 무도회 드레스와 꽃다발을 심심찮게 흘끗거렸고, 극장이며 음악회며 썰매 모임이며 온갖 잔치판에 관한 활기찬 수다를 들었으며, 자기한테라면 그토록 귀했을 자질구레한 것들에 돈을 펑펑 쓰는 모습을 지켜보았다."], ["Poor Meg seldom complained, but a sense of injustice made her feel bitter toward every one sometimes, for she had not yet learned to know how rich she was in the blessings which alone can make life happy.", "가엾은 메그는 좀처럼 불평하지 않았지만, 부당하다는 느낌이 때로 그녀를 모든 사람에게 모질게 만들기도 했다. 삶을 행복하게 해 주는 유일한 것, 곧 자신이 누리는 축복 안에서 그녀가 얼마나 부유한지를 아직 깨우치지 못했던 것이다."]], [["Jo happened to suit Aunt March, who was lame, and needed an active person to wait upon her.", "조는 마침 마치 고모와 잘 맞았다. 다리가 불편한 고모에게는 시중들어 줄 활동적인 사람이 필요했던 것이다."], ["The childless old lady had offered to adopt one of the girls when the troubles came, and was much offended because her offer was declined.", "자식이 없는 이 노부인은 집안에 어려움이 닥쳤을 때 딸 가운데 하나를 입양하겠다고 제의했었는데, 그 제의가 거절당하자 몹시 언짢아했다."], ["Other friends told the Marches that they had lost all chance of being remembered in the rich old lady's will; but the unworldly Marches only said,--", "다른 친구들은 마치네 집안이 이 부유한 노부인의 유언장에 이름을 올릴 기회를 모조리 날려 버렸다고 했지만, 세속에 초연한 마치 가족은 이렇게만 말했다."]], [["\"We can't give up our girls for a dozen fortunes.", "\"열두 사람 몫의 재산을 준다 해도 우리 딸들을 내줄 순 없어요."], ["Rich or poor, we will keep together and be happy in one another.\"", "부유하든 가난하든, 우린 함께 모여 서로를 의지하며 행복할 겁니다.\""]], [["The old lady wouldn't speak to them for a time, but happening to meet Jo at a friend's, something in her comical face and blunt manners struck the old lady's fancy, and she proposed to take her for a companion.", "노부인은 한동안 그들에게 말을 걸지 않았지만, 어느 친구 집에서 우연히 조를 마주쳤을 때 그 우스꽝스러운 얼굴과 무뚝뚝한 태도의 무언가가 노부인의 마음에 들어, 조를 말동무로 데려가겠다고 제안했다."], ["This did not suit Jo at all; but she accepted the place since nothing better appeared, and, to every one's surprise, got on remarkably well with her irascible relative.", "조로서는 영 내키지 않았지만, 달리 더 나은 자리가 나타나지 않았으므로 받아들였고, 모두의 놀라움 속에 그 다혈질 친척과 놀랄 만큼 잘 지냈다. 이따금 폭풍이 일긴 했고, 한번은 조가 더는 못 견디겠다며 집으로 돌아와 버린 적도 있었다."], ["There was an occasional tempest, and once Jo had marched home, declaring she couldn't bear it any longer; but Aunt March always cleared up quickly, and sent for her back again with such urgency that she could not refuse, for in her heart she rather liked the peppery old lady.", "그러나 마치 고모는 늘 금세 마음이 개어, 거절할 수 없을 만큼 간곡히 사람을 보내 그녀를 다시 불러들였다. 사실 조는 마음속으로 그 깐깐한 노부인을 제법 좋아하는 편이었던 것이다."]], [["I suspect that the real attraction was a large library of fine books, which was left to dust and spiders since Uncle March died.", "내가 짐작하기로 진짜 매력은 좋은 책들로 가득한 커다란 서재였다. 마치 큰아버지가 돌아가신 뒤로 먼지와 거미가 차지하도록 방치된 곳이었다."], ["Jo remembered the kind old gentleman, who used to let her build railroads and bridges with his big dictionaries, tell her stories about the queer pictures in his Latin books, and buy her cards of gingerbread whenever he met her in the street.", "조는 그 인자한 노신사를 기억하고 있었다. 큰아버지는 그녀가 커다란 사전들로 철도와 다리를 쌓게 해 주었고, 라틴어 책에 실린 기묘한 그림들에 얽힌 이야기를 들려주었으며, 길에서 마주칠 때마다 진저브레드를 사 주곤 했다."], ["The dim, dusty room, with the busts staring down from the tall book-cases, the cosy chairs, the globes, and, best of all, the wilderness of books, in which she could wander where she liked, made the library a region of bliss to her.", "키 큰 책장 위에서 흉상들이 내려다보는, 어둑하고 먼지 낀 그 방, 아늑한 의자들과 지구본들, 그리고 무엇보다도 마음껏 거닐 수 있는 책의 황야가, 조에게는 그 서재를 더없는 행복의 영토로 만들어 주었다."], ["The moment Aunt March took her nap, or was busy with company, Jo hurried to this quiet place, and, curling herself up in the easy-chair, devoured poetry, romance, history, travels, and pictures, like a regular book-worm.", "마치 고모가 낮잠을 자거나 손님 접대로 바쁜 틈만 나면 조는 이 조용한 곳으로 달려가, 안락의자에 몸을 웅크리고는 영락없는 책벌레처럼 시며 소설이며 역사며 여행기며 그림책을 게걸스레 탐독했다."], ["But, like all happiness, it did not last long; for as sure as she had just reached the heart of the story, the sweetest verse of the song, or the most perilous adventure of her traveller, a shrill voice called, \"Josy-phine!", "그러나 모든 행복이 그렇듯, 그것도 오래가지는 못했다. 이야기의 한복판이나, 노래의 가장 감미로운 구절, 혹은 여행자의 가장 아슬아슬한 모험에 막 다다랐다 싶으면, 어김없이 \"조세-핀!"], ["Josy-phine!\"", "조세-핀!\""], ["and she had to leave her paradise to wind yarn, wash the poodle, or read Belsham's Essays by the hour together.", "하고 날카로운 목소리가 불러 댔고, 그녀는 자기 낙원을 떠나 실을 감거나, 푸들을 씻기거나, 벨샴의 수필을 몇 시간씩 내리 읽어 주어야 했다."]], [["Jo's ambition was to do something very splendid; what it was she had no idea, as yet, but left it for time to tell her; and, meanwhile, found her greatest affliction in the fact that she couldn't read, run, and ride as much as she liked. A quick temper, sharp tongue, and restless spirit were always getting her into scrapes, and her life was a series of ups and downs, which were both comic and pathetic. But the training she received at Aunt March's was just what she needed; and the thought that she was doing something to support herself made her happy, in spite of the perpetual \"Josy-phine!\"", "조의 야망은 무언가 아주 멋진 일을 해내는 것이었다. 그게 무엇인지는 아직 떠오르지 않아 시간이 일러 줄 때까지 미뤄 두고 있었다. 그러는 동안 그녀의 가장 큰 고통은 마음껏 책을 읽고, 뛰어다니고, 말을 탈 수 없다는 사실이었다. 욱하는 성미와 날카로운 혀, 가만있지 못하는 기질 탓에 늘 사고에 휘말렸고, 그녀의 삶은 우스우면서도 애처로운 기복의 연속이었다. 하지만 마치 고모 집에서 받은 단련이야말로 그녀에게 꼭 필요한 것이었다. 그리고 스스로 생계를 위해 무언가 하고 있다는 생각이, 끊임없이 들려오는 \"조세-핀!\" 소리에도 불구하고 그녀를 행복하게 했다."]], [["Beth was too bashful to go to school; it had been tried, but she suffered so much that it was given up, and she did her lessons at home, with her father.", "베스는 너무 수줍어서 학교에 다니지 못했다. 시도는 해 보았지만 어찌나 괴로워하는지 결국 그만두게 했고, 베스는 아버지와 함께 집에서 공부했다."], ["Even when he went away, and her mother was called to devote her skill and energy to Soldiers' Aid Societies, Beth went faithfully on by herself, and did the best she could.", "아버지가 떠나고, 어머니마저 군인 구호회에 그 솜씨와 기력을 바치라는 부름을 받았을 때에도, 베스는 혼자서 성실히 공부를 이어 가며 할 수 있는 데까지 최선을 다했다."], ["She was a housewifely little creature, and helped Hannah keep home neat and comfortable for the workers, never thinking of any reward but to be loved.", "베스는 살림꾼다운 자그마한 아이여서, 한나를 도와 일하는 사람들을 위해 집 안을 깔끔하고 안락하게 가꾸었으며, 사랑받는 것 말고는 어떤 보답도 바라지 않았다."], ["Long, quiet days she spent, not lonely nor idle, for her little world was peopled with imaginary friends, and she was by nature a busy bee.", "길고 조용한 나날을 보냈지만 외롭거나 빈둥거리지는 않았다. 그녀의 작은 세계는 상상 속의 친구들로 북적였고, 그녀는 천성이 바지런한 꿀벌이었으니까."], ["There were six dolls to be taken up and dressed every morning, for Beth was a child still, and loved her pets as well as ever.", "매일 아침 일으켜 옷을 입혀 주어야 할 인형이 여섯이나 되었는데, 베스는 여전히 어린아이였고 자기 애완물을 변함없이 아꼈던 것이다. 그 가운데 멀쩡하거나 예쁜 것은 하나도 없었다."], ["Not one whole or handsome one among them; all were outcasts till Beth took them in; for, when her sisters outgrew these idols, they passed to her, because Amy would have nothing old or ugly.", "모두 베스가 거두어들이기 전까지는 버림받은 신세였다. 언니들은 이 우상들을 다 가지고 놀고 나면 베스에게 물려주었는데, 에이미가 낡거나 못생긴 것이라면 질색했기 때문이다."], ["Beth cherished them all the more tenderly for that very reason, and set up a hospital for infirm dolls.", "바로 그 이유로 베스는 그것들을 더욱 살뜰히 보살폈고, 병든 인형들을 위한 병원까지 차렸다."], ["No pins were ever stuck into their cotton vitals; no harsh words or blows were ever given them; no neglect ever saddened the heart of the most repulsive: but all were fed and clothed, nursed and caressed, with an affection which never failed.", "그 솜뭉치 같은 몸에 핀이 꽂히는 일도, 모진 말이나 매질이 가해지는 일도 결코 없었고, 아무리 흉측한 인형도 무관심에 마음 상하는 일이 없었다. 모두가 한결같은 애정 속에 먹이고 입혀지고, 보살핌받고 어루만져졌다."], ["One forlorn fragment of _dollanity_ had belonged to Jo; and, having led a tempestuous life, was left a wreck in the rag-bag, from which dreary poorhouse it was rescued by Beth, and taken to her refuge.", "인형 신세의 그 처량한 한 조각은 본래 조의 것이었는데, 파란만장한 삶을 살다가 넝마 자루에 폐물로 처박혀 있던 것을, 베스가 그 음울한 구빈원에서 구해 내어 자기 피난처로 데려왔다."], ["Having no top to its head, she tied on a neat little cap, and, as both arms and legs were gone, she hid these deficiencies by folding it in a blanket, and devoting her best bed to this chronic invalid.", "머리 윗부분이 없어서 베스는 깔끔한 작은 모자를 씌워 주었고, 두 팔과 두 다리도 다 떨어져 나가서 담요로 둘둘 감싸 그 결함을 가리고는, 가장 좋은 침대를 이 만성 환자에게 내주었다. 만일 누군가 그 인형에게 쏟는 정성을 알았다면, 비록 웃음을 터뜨리면서도 마음이 뭉클해졌으리라."], ["If any one had known the care lavished on that dolly, I think it would have touched their hearts, even while they laughed.", "베스는 그 인형에게 꽃다발 조각을 가져다주고, 책을 읽어 주고, 외투 안에 숨겨 바깥바람을 쐬어 주었다."], ["She brought it bits of bouquets; she read to it, took it out to breathe the air, hidden under her coat; she sung it lullabys, and never went to bed without kissing its dirty face, and whispering tenderly, \"I hope you'll have a good night, my poor dear.\"", "자장가를 불러 주었고, 그 더러운 얼굴에 입맞추고 다정하게 속삭이지 않고는 결코 잠자리에 들지 않았다. \"잘 자렴, 가엾은 우리 아가.\""]], [["Beth had her troubles as well as the others; and not being an angel, but a very human little girl, she often \"wept a little weep,\"", "베스에게도 다른 자매들 못지않게 나름의 괴로움이 있었다. 그녀는 천사가 아니라 지극히 인간적인 어린 소녀였기에, 조의 말마따나 \"한바탕 작은 울음\"을 우는 일이 잦았다."], ["as Jo said, because she couldn't take music lessons and have a fine piano.", "음악 수업을 받을 수도, 좋은 피아노를 가질 수도 없었기 때문이다."], ["She loved music so dearly, tried so hard to learn, and practised away so patiently at the jingling old instrument, that it did seem as if some one (not to hint Aunt March) ought to help her.", "음악을 그토록 애틋이 사랑하고, 그토록 열심히 배우려 했으며, 그 삐걱대는 낡은 악기로 그토록 끈기 있게 연습하니, 정말이지 누군가(마치 고모를 두고 하는 말은 아니다) 그녀를 도와주어야 마땅할 것 같았다."], ["Nobody did, however, and nobody saw Beth wipe the tears off the yellow keys, that wouldn't keep in tune, when she was all alone.", "그러나 아무도 돕지 않았고, 혼자 있을 때 음정도 안 맞는 누런 건반 위로 베스가 눈물을 훔치는 것을 본 사람도 없었다."], ["She sang like a little lark about her work, never was too tired to play for Marmee and the girls, and day after day said hopefully to herself, \"I know I'll get my music some time, if I'm good.\"", "그녀는 일하면서 작은 종달새처럼 노래했고, 엄마와 자매들을 위해 연주하는 일에 결코 지치는 법이 없었으며, 날마다 스스로에게 희망에 차서 말했다. \"착하게 지내다 보면 언젠가 내 음악을 갖게 될 거야.\""]], [["There are many Beths in the world, shy and quiet, sitting in corners till needed, and living for others so cheerfully that no one sees the sacrifices till the little cricket on the hearth stops chirping, and the sweet, sunshiny presence vanishes, leaving silence and shadow behind.", "세상에는 베스 같은 사람이 많다. 수줍고 조용하게, 필요해질 때까지 구석에 앉아, 남을 위해 어찌나 명랑하게 살아가는지, 난롯가의 작은 귀뚜라미가 우는 소리를 그치고, 그 다정하고 햇살 같은 존재가 사라져 뒤에 정적과 그늘만 남기기 전까지는 아무도 그 희생을 알아채지 못한다."]], [["If anybody had asked Amy what the greatest trial of her life was, she would have answered at once, \"My nose.\"", "누가 에이미에게 인생의 가장 큰 시련이 무엇이냐고 물었다면, 그녀는 곧장 이렇게 대답했을 것이다. \"제 코예요.\""], ["When she was a baby, Jo had accidentally dropped her into the coal-hod, and Amy insisted that the fall had ruined her nose forever.", "에이미가 갓난아기였을 때, 조가 실수로 그녀를 석탄 통에 떨어뜨린 적이 있었는데, 에이미는 그 낙상 때문에 코가 영영 망가졌다고 우겼다."], ["It was not big, nor red, like poor \"Petrea's\"; it was only rather flat, and all the pinching in the world could not give it an aristocratic point.", "가엾은 \"페트레아\"의 코처럼 크거나 빨갛지는 않았다. 그저 좀 납작할 뿐이었고, 아무리 꼬집어 봐도 도무지 귀족적인 오뚝함을 얻을 수가 없었다."], ["No one minded it but herself, and it was doing its best to grow, but Amy felt deeply the want of a Grecian nose, and drew whole sheets of handsome ones to console herself. \"Little Raphael,\"", "본인 말고는 아무도 신경 쓰지 않았고, 코도 나름대로 자라느라 애쓰는 중이었지만, 에이미는 그리스식 코가 없다는 것을 뼈저리게 안타까워하며, 스스로를 위로하려고 잘생긴 코를 종이가 닳도록 그려 댔다."], ["as her sisters called her, had a decided talent for drawing, and was never so happy as when copying flowers, designing fairies, or illustrating stories with queer specimens of art.", "언니들이 \"꼬마 라파엘로\"라 부르던 그녀는 그림에 확실한 재능이 있어서, 꽃을 베껴 그리거나 요정을 도안하거나 기묘한 솜씨로 이야기에 삽화를 그릴 때만큼 행복한 적이 없었다."], ["Her teachers complained that, instead of doing her sums, she covered her slate with animals; the blank pages of her atlas were used to copy maps on; and caricatures of the most ludicrous description came fluttering out of all her books at unlucky moments.", "선생님들은 그녀가 셈은 안 하고 석판을 온통 동물로 뒤덮어 놓는다고 불평했다. 지도책의 빈 면은 지도를 베껴 그리는 데 쓰였고, 더없이 우스꽝스러운 풍자화들이 하필이면 곤란한 순간에 그녀의 책마다 팔랑팔랑 떨어져 나왔다."], ["She got through her lessons as well as she could, and managed to escape reprimands by being a model of deportment.", "그녀는 할 수 있는 데까지 수업을 따라갔고, 품행의 모범이 됨으로써 꾸지람을 피해 갔다."], ["She was a great favorite with her mates, being good-tempered, and possessing the happy art of pleasing without effort.", "성격이 좋고, 힘들이지 않고도 남을 즐겁게 하는 행복한 재주를 지닌 덕분에 그녀는 친구들에게 무척 인기가 많았다."], ["Her little airs and graces were much admired, so were her accomplishments; for beside her drawing, she could play twelve tunes, crochet, and read French without mispronouncing more than two thirds of the words.", "그녀의 자그마한 거드름과 우아한 태도는 큰 찬사를 받았고, 그녀의 교양도 그러했다. 그림 말고도 그녀는 곡을 열두 가지나 칠 줄 알았고, 코바늘뜨기를 할 줄 알았으며, 단어의 삼분의 이 넘게는 잘못 발음하지 않고 프랑스어를 읽을 줄 알았다."], ["She had a plaintive way of saying, \"When papa was rich we did so-and-so,\"", "그녀에게는 \"아빠가 부자였을 땐 우리도 이러저러했는데\"라고 구슬프게 말하는 버릇이 있었는데, 그게 무척 애처로웠다."], ["which was very touching; and her long words were considered \"perfectly elegant\" by the girls.", "그리고 그녀가 쓰는 긴 단어들은 여자애들 사이에서 \"완벽하게 우아하다\"는 평을 들었다."]], [["Amy was in a fair way to be spoilt; for every one petted her, and her small vanities and selfishnesses were growing nicely.", "에이미는 한창 버릇없어지기 좋은 길로 들어서고 있었다. 다들 그녀를 떠받들었고, 자그마한 허영과 이기심도 무럭무럭 자라났다."], ["One thing, however, rather quenched the vanities; she had to wear her cousin's clothes.", "다만 한 가지가 그 허영을 어지간히 꺾어 놓았으니, 바로 사촌의 옷을 물려 입어야 한다는 것이었다."], ["Now Florence's mamma hadn't a particle of taste, and Amy suffered deeply at having to wear a red instead of a blue bonnet, unbecoming gowns, and fussy aprons that did not fit.", "그런데 플로런스의 엄마는 안목이라곤 눈곱만큼도 없어서, 에이미는 파란 모자 대신 빨간 모자를 쓰고, 어울리지도 않는 드레스에, 몸에 맞지도 않는 요란한 앞치마를 둘러야 하는 게 여간 괴롭지 않았다."], ["Everything was good, well made, and little worn; but Amy's artistic eyes were much afflicted, especially this winter, when her school dress was a dull purple, with yellow dots, and no trimming.", "죄다 질 좋고, 바느질도 잘됐고, 거의 입지도 않은 새것이었지만, 에이미의 예술가다운 눈에는 큰 고역이었다. 특히 이번 겨울이 그랬으니, 학교에 입고 갈 드레스가 노란 점이 박힌 칙칙한 보라색에, 장식이라곤 하나도 없는 것이었기 때문이다."]], [["\"My only comfort,\"", "\"내 유일한 위안은,\""], ["she said to Meg, with tears in her eyes, \"is, that mother don't take tucks in my dresses whenever I'm naughty, as Maria Parks'", "그녀가 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며 메그에게 말했다. \"엄마가 마리아 파크스 엄마처럼 내가 못되게 굴 때마다 드레스 단을 줄여 버리진 않는다는 거야."], ["mother does.", "언니, 그건 정말 끔찍해."], ["My dear, it's really dreadful; for sometimes she is so bad, her frock is up to her knees, and she can't come to school.", "가끔 걔가 어찌나 못되게 구는지, 드레스가 무릎까지 올라가서 학교에도 못 와."], ["When I think of this _deggerredation_, I feel that I can bear even my flat nose and purple gown, with yellow sky-rockets on it.\"", "이런 '굴요글'을 생각하면, 난 납작한 코도, 노란 폭죽이 박힌 보라색 드레스도 견딜 만하다는 생각이 들어.\""]], [["Meg was Amy's confidant and monitor, and, by some strange attraction of opposites, Jo was gentle Beth's.", "메그는 에이미의 속내를 털어놓는 상대이자 잔소리꾼이었고, 정반대 것들이 끌리는 어떤 묘한 이끌림 때문인지, 조는 온화한 베스의 그런 상대였다."], ["To Jo alone did the shy child tell her thoughts; and over her big, harum-scarum sister, Beth unconsciously exercised more influence than any one in the family.", "수줍음 많은 그 아이는 오직 조에게만 속마음을 털어놓았다. 그리고 베스는, 덜렁대고 정신없는 그 언니에게, 자기도 모르는 새 집안의 그 누구보다 큰 영향을 미치고 있었다."], ["The two older girls were a great deal to one another, but each took one of the younger into her keeping, and watched over her in her own way; \"playing mother\"", "두 언니는 서로에게 무척 큰 존재였지만, 각자 동생 하나씩을 맡아 제 나름의 방식으로 보살폈다."], ["they called it, and put their sisters in the places of discarded dolls, with the maternal instinct of little women.", "그들은 그것을 \"엄마 놀이\"라 불렀고, 작은 아씨들다운 모성 본능으로, 내다 버린 인형 대신 동생들을 그 자리에 앉혀 놓았다."]], [["\"Has anybody got anything to tell?", "\"누구 뭐 들려줄 얘기 없어?"], ["It's been such a dismal day I'm really dying for some amusement,\"", "오늘 하루가 어찌나 따분했는지, 무슨 재밋거리라도 없으면 죽을 것 같아.\""], ["said Meg, as they sat sewing together that evening.", "그날 저녁 다 함께 바느질을 하며 앉아 있을 때 메그가 말했다."]], [["\"I had a queer time with aunt to-day, and, as I got the best of it, I'll tell you about it,\" began Jo, who dearly loved to tell stories. \"I was reading that everlasting Belsham, and droning away as I always do, for aunt soon drops off, and then I take out some nice book, and read like fury till she wakes up. I actually made myself sleepy; and, before she began to nod, I gave such a gape that she asked me what I meant by opening my mouth wide enough to take the whole book in at once.", "\"오늘 고모랑 좀 별난 일이 있었는데, 내가 한판 이겼거든. 그 얘기 해 줄게.\" 이야기 들려주는 걸 무척 좋아하는 조가 입을 열었다. \"내가 그 지긋지긋한 벨샴을 읽고 있었어. 늘 그렇듯이 단조롭게 웅얼웅얼 읽었지. 그래야 고모가 금방 졸거든. 그럼 난 좋은 책을 꺼내 들고, 고모가 깰 때까지 미친 듯이 읽는 거야. 그런데 진짜로 내가 졸려 버렸지 뭐야. 고모가 졸기도 전에 내가 어찌나 하품을 크게 했던지, 책을 통째로 한입에 삼킬 만큼 입을 그렇게 떡 벌리는 게 무슨 짓이냐고 물으시더라."]], [["\"'I wish I could, and be done with it,'", "\"'그럴 수 있다면 좋겠어요. 그래서 다 끝내 버리게요.'"], ["said I, trying not to be saucy.", "내가 건방지지 않게 굴려고 애쓰면서 대답했어."]], [["\"Then she gave me a long lecture on my sins, and told me to sit and think them over while she just 'lost'", "\"그랬더니 고모가 내 죄목을 두고 한참 잔소리를 늘어놓고는, 잠깐 '깜빡 잠드실' 동안 앉아서 그걸 곰곰이 반성하라고 하시더라."], ["herself for a moment.", "고모는 절대 금방 안 깨거든."], ["She never finds herself very soon; so the minute her cap began to bob, like a top-heavy dahlia, I whipped the 'Vicar of Wakefield' out of my pocket, and read away, with one eye on him, and one on aunt.", "그래서 고모 모자가 머리 무거운 달리아처럼 까딱거리기 시작하자마자, 난 주머니에서 '웨이크필드의 목사'를 잽싸게 꺼내 들고, 한 눈은 책에, 한 눈은 고모에게 두고 정신없이 읽었어."], ["I'd just got to where they all tumbled into the water, when I forgot, and laughed out loud.", "다들 물에 풍덩 빠지는 데까지 막 다다랐는데, 그만 깜빡하고 소리 내서 웃어 버렸지 뭐야."], ["Aunt woke up; and, being more good-natured after her nap, told me to read a bit, and show what frivolous work I preferred to the worthy and instructive Belsham.", "고모가 깨더라. 그런데 한숨 자고 나니 한결 마음이 너그러워졌는지, 좀 읽어 보라면서, 훌륭하고 유익한 벨샴보다 어떤 시시한 글이 그렇게 좋다는 건지 보여 달라고 하셨어."], ["I did my very best, and she liked it, though she only said,--", "난 정성껏 읽어 드렸고, 고모도 마음에 들어 했어. 비록 이렇게밖에 말씀 안 하셨지만."]], [["\"'I don't understand what it's all about.", "\"'대체 무슨 얘긴지 하나도 모르겠구나."], ["Go back and begin it, child.'", "도로 가서 처음부터 시작해라, 얘야.'"]], [["\"Back I went, and made the Primroses as interesting as ever I could.", "\"그래서 처음으로 돌아가서, 프림로즈 가족을 최대한 재미나게 들려드렸지. 한번은 짓궂은 마음이 들어서, 한창 짜릿한 대목에서 멈추고는 얌전하게 여쭸어."], ["Once I was wicked enough to stop in a thrilling place, and say meekly, 'I'm afraid it tires you, ma'am; sha'n't I stop now?'", "'아무래도 고모님이 피곤하실 것 같아요. 이제 그만 읽을까요?'"]], [["\"She caught up her knitting, which had dropped out of her hands, gave me a sharp look through her specs, and said, in her short way,--", "\"그러자 고모는 손에서 떨어뜨린 뜨개질감을 도로 집어 들고, 안경 너머로 날 매섭게 흘겨보더니, 그 무뚝뚝한 투로 말씀하셨어."]], [["\"'Finish the chapter, and don't be impertinent, miss.'\"", "\"'그 장이나 마저 읽고, 건방지게 굴지 마라, 아가씨.'\""]], [["\"Did she own she liked it?\"", "\"좋다고 인정은 하셨어?\""], ["asked Meg.", "메그가 물었다."]], [["\"Oh, bless you, no!", "\"어머, 천만에!"], ["but she let old Belsham rest; and, when I ran back after my gloves this afternoon, there she was, so hard at the Vicar that she didn't hear me laugh as I danced a jig in the hall, because of the good time coming.", "그래도 그 낡은 벨샴은 좀 쉬게 두셨지. 그리고 오늘 오후에 내가 장갑 가지러 다시 들어갔더니, 고모가 그 '목사'에 어찌나 푹 빠져 계신지, 내가 곧 올 좋은 시간 생각에 신나서 복도에서 지그 춤을 추며 웃는 소리도 못 들으시더라."], ["What a pleasant life she might have, if she only chose.", "마음만 먹으면 얼마나 즐겁게 사실 수 있을까."], ["I don't envy her much, in spite of her money, for after all rich people have about as many worries as poor ones, I think,\"", "돈이 많은데도 난 고모가 별로 안 부러워. 따지고 보면 부자들도 가난한 사람들 못지않게 근심이 많은 것 같거든.\""], ["added Jo.", "조가 덧붙였다."]], [["\"That reminds me,\"", "\"그러고 보니,\""], ["said Meg, \"that I've got something to tell.", "메그가 말했다. \"나도 들려줄 얘기가 있어."], ["It isn't funny, like Jo's story, but I thought about it a good deal as I came home.", "조 얘기처럼 우습진 않지만, 집에 오면서 한참 생각한 거야."], ["At the Kings to-day I found everybody in a flurry, and one of the children said that her oldest brother had done something dreadful, and papa had sent him away.", "오늘 킹 씨네 집에 갔더니 다들 허둥지둥 난리가 났더라. 아이 하나가 그러는데, 자기 맏오빠가 끔찍한 짓을 저질러서 아빠가 멀리 쫓아 보냈다는 거야."], ["I heard Mrs. King crying and Mr.", "킹 부인이 우는 소리하고 킹 씨가 큰 소리로 야단치는 소리가 들렸어."], ["King talking very loud, and Grace and Ellen turned away their faces when they passed me, so I shouldn't see how red their eyes were.", "그레이스랑 엘런은 내 앞을 지나갈 때, 눈이 얼마나 빨갛게 부었는지 내가 못 보게 얼굴을 돌리더라."], ["I didn't ask any questions, of course; but I felt so sorry for them, and was rather glad I hadn't any wild brothers to do wicked things and disgrace the family.\"", "물론 난 아무것도 안 물었지. 그런데 걔네가 어찌나 안됐던지, 못된 짓을 저질러 집안을 망신시킬 거친 오빠가 나한텐 없는 게 차라리 다행이다 싶었어.\""]], [["\"I think being disgraced in school is a great deal try_inger_ than anything bad boys can do,\"", "\"학교에서 망신당하는 게, 나쁜 남자애들이 저지를 수 있는 그 어떤 짓보다 훨씬 더 견디기 힘든 것 같아.\""], ["said Amy, shaking her head, as if her experience of life had been a deep one.", "에이미가 마치 인생의 깊은 경험이라도 한 사람처럼 고개를 절레절레 저으며 말했다."], ["\"Susie Perkins came to school to-day with a lovely red carnelian ring; I wanted it dreadfully, and wished I was her with all my might.", "\"오늘 수지 퍼킨스가 예쁜 빨간 홍옥수 반지를 끼고 학교에 왔어. 난 그게 끔찍이도 갖고 싶어서, 온 마음을 다해 내가 걔였으면 했지 뭐야."], ["Well, she drew a picture of Mr.", "그런데 글쎄, 걔가 데이비스 선생님 그림을 그렸어."], ["Davis, with a monstrous nose and a hump, and the words, 'Young ladies, my eye is upon you!' coming out of his mouth in a balloon thing.", "어마어마하게 큰 코에다 곱사등으로, 입에서 '아가씨들, 내 눈은 너희를 지켜보고 있다!'라는 말이 풍선 같은 데 담겨 나오게 말이야."], ["We were laughing over it, when all of a sudden his eye _was_ on us, and he ordered Susie to bring up her slate.", "다들 그걸 보며 웃고 있는데, 갑자기 선생님 눈이 정말로 우리한테 향한 거야."], ["She was _parry_lized with fright, but she went, and oh, what _do_ you think he did?", "선생님이 수지더러 석판을 가지고 나오라고 하셨어. 걔는 무서워서 굳어 버렸는데, 그래도 나갔지."], ["He took her by the ear, the ear!", "그런데, 선생님이 어떻게 하셨는지 알아?"], ["just fancy how horrid!--and led her to the recitation platform, and made her stand there half an hour, holding that slate so every one could see.\"", "걔 귀를, 귀를 잡고서, 어휴, 얼마나 끔찍한지 상상이나 해 봐! 낭독대로 끌고 가서, 다들 볼 수 있게 그 석판을 든 채로 거기 삼십 분이나 서 있게 했다니까.\""]], [["\"Didn't the girls laugh at the picture?\"", "\"여자애들이 그 그림 보고 안 웃었어?\""], ["asked Jo, who relished the scrape.", "그 소동을 흥미진진하게 듣던 조가 물었다."]], [["\"Laugh?", "\"웃긴!"], ["Not one!", "한 명도 안 웃었어."], ["They sat as still as mice; and Susie cried quarts, I know she did.", "다들 쥐 죽은 듯이 가만히 앉아 있었어. 그리고 수지는 진짜 양동이로 들이부을 만큼 울었어."], ["I didn't envy her then; for I felt that millions of carnelian rings wouldn't have made me happy, after that.", "분명히 그랬다니까. 그때는 걔가 하나도 안 부럽더라."], ["I never, never should have got over such a agonizing mortification.\"", "그런 일을 겪고 나니, 홍옥수 반지가 수백만 개 있어도 내가 행복해지진 않을 것 같았거든."], ["And Amy went on with her work, in the proud consciousness of virtue, and the successful utterance of two long words in a breath.", "난 그렇게 괴롭게 망신을 당하면, 절대로, 절대로 다시는 일어서지 못했을 거야.\" 그러고서 에이미는, 미덕을 지녔다는 뿌듯한 자부심과 긴 단어 두 개를 한숨에 무사히 내뱉었다는 성취감 속에 다시 바느질을 이어 갔다."]], [["\"I saw something that I liked this morning, and I meant to tell it at dinner, but I forgot,\"", "\"난 오늘 아침에 마음에 드는 걸 봤어. 점심때 얘기하려고 했는데 깜빡했지 뭐야.\""], ["said Beth, putting Jo's topsy-turvy basket in order as she talked.", "베스가 이야기하면서 조의 뒤죽박죽 바구니를 정리하며 말했다."], ["\"When I went to get some oysters for Hannah, Mr. Laurence was in the fish-shop; but he didn't see me, for I kept behind a barrel, and he was busy with Mr.", "\"한나 줄 굴을 사러 갔는데, 로런스 씨가 생선 가게에 계셨어."], ["Cutter, the fish-man.", "그런데 날 못 보셨어."], ["A poor woman came in, with a pail and a mop, and asked Mr.", "내가 통 뒤에 숨어 있었고, 그분은 생선 장수 커터 씨하고 한창 얘기 중이셨거든."], ["Cutter if he would let her do some scrubbing for a bit of fish, because she hadn't any dinner for her children, and had been disappointed of a day's work.", "가난한 아주머니 한 분이 양동이랑 대걸레를 들고 들어와서는, 생선 한 토막만 주면 청소를 좀 해 드리겠다고 커터 씨한테 물었어. 아이들 먹일 저녁거리가 없는데, 하루 일거리도 못 구했다면서."], ["Mr. Cutter was in a hurry, and said 'No,'", "커터 씨는 바빠서 좀 퉁명스럽게 '안 돼요'라고 했지."], ["rather crossly; so she was going away, looking hungry and sorry, when Mr. Laurence hooked up a big fish with the crooked end of his cane, and held it out to her.", "그래서 아주머니가 배고프고 속상한 얼굴로 돌아서 가려는데, 로런스 씨가 지팡이 굽은 끝으로 큼직한 생선 한 마리를 걸어 올리시더니 그걸 아주머니한테 내미셨어."], ["She was so glad and surprised, she took it right in her arms, and thanked him over and over.", "아주머니는 어찌나 기쁘고 놀랐는지, 그걸 덥석 품에 안고 몇 번이나 고맙다고 인사했어."], ["He told her to 'go along and cook it,' and she hurried off, so happy!", "그분이 '어서 가서 요리하세요'라고 하셨고, 아주머니는 그렇게 좋아하며 서둘러 가셨어!"], ["Wasn't it good of him?", "그분, 참 마음씨 곱지 않아?"], ["Oh, she did look so funny, hugging the big, slippery fish, and hoping Mr. Laurence's bed in heaven would be 'aisy.'\"", "아휴, 큼직하고 미끌거리는 생선을 끌어안고서, 로런스 씨의 천국 잠자리가 '펜안'하기를 바란다고 하는데, 정말 웃기더라.\""]], [["When they had laughed at Beth's story, they asked their mother for one; and, after a moment's thought, she said soberly,--", "베스의 이야기에 한바탕 웃고 나서, 자매는 엄마에게도 하나 들려달라고 청했다. 마치 부인은 잠시 생각하더니 차분히 말문을 열었다."]], [["\"As I sat cutting out blue flannel jackets to-day, at the rooms, I felt very anxious about father, and thought how lonely and helpless we should be, if anything happened to him.", "\"오늘 회관에서 파란 플란넬 재킷을 마름질하며 앉아 있는데, 아버지가 무척 걱정되더구나. 그이한테 무슨 일이라도 생긴다면 우리가 얼마나 외롭고 막막할까 하는 생각이 들었어."], ["It was not a wise thing to do; but I kept on worrying, till an old man came in, with an order for some clothes.", "현명한 짓은 아니었지. 그래도 자꾸 근심하고 있는데, 옷을 맞추러 한 노인이 들어왔단다."], ["He sat down near me, and I began to talk to him; for he looked poor and tired and anxious.", "그 사람이 내 옆에 앉기에 말을 걸었지. 가난하고 지치고 근심에 잠긴 얼굴이었거든."]], [["\"'Have you sons in the army?'", "\"'아드님이 군대에 있나요?' 내가 물었어."], ["I asked; for the note he brought was not to me.", "그이가 가져온 주문서는 내 앞으로 온 게 아니었으니까."]], [["\"'Yes, ma'am.", "\"'네, 부인."], ["I had four, but two were killed, one is a prisoner, and I'm going to the other, who is very sick in a Washington hospital,'", "넷이나 있었는데, 둘은 전사하고, 하나는 포로가 됐고, 지금은 워싱턴 병원에서 위독하게 앓고 있는 다른 아들한테 가는 길입니다.'"], ["he answered quietly.", "그이가 조용히 대답했어."]], [["\"'You have done a great deal for your country, sir,'", "\"'나라를 위해 정말 많은 일을 하셨군요, 선생님.'"], ["I said, feeling respect now, instead of pity.", "내가 말했지. 이제 연민이 아니라 존경심이 들었거든."]], [["\"'Not a mite more than I ought, ma'am.", "\"'제 도리만큼도 못 했는걸요, 부인. 쓸모만 있다면 저라도 갔겠지요."], ["I'd go myself, if I was any use; as I ain't, I give my boys, and give 'em free.'", "그런데 그렇질 못하니, 아들들을 보냈습니다. 아낌없이 보냈지요.'"]], [["\"He spoke so cheerfully, looked so sincere, and seemed so glad to give his all, that I was ashamed of myself.", "\"그이가 어찌나 쾌활하게 말하고, 어찌나 진실해 보이고, 자기가 가진 전부를 내준 걸 그토록 기뻐하는 듯하던지, 나 자신이 부끄러워지더구나."], ["I'd given one man, and thought it too much, while he gave four, without grudging them.", "난 한 사람을 보내 놓고도 너무 많이 바쳤다고 여겼는데, 그이는 넷을 내주면서도 조금도 아까워하지 않았으니까."], ["I had all my girls to comfort me at home; and his last son was waiting, miles away, to say 'good by' to him, perhaps!", "내겐 위로해 줄 너희가 다 집에 있는데, 그이의 마지막 남은 아들은 어쩌면 작별 인사를 하려고 몇십 리 밖에서 기다리고 있을지도 몰랐지!"], ["I felt so rich, so happy, thinking of my blessings, that I made him a nice bundle, gave him some money, and thanked him heartily for the lesson he had taught me.\"", "내가 누리는 축복들을 헤아리니 어찌나 부유하고 행복한 기분이 들던지, 그이에게 옷을 단단히 싸 주고, 돈도 조금 쥐여 주고, 깨우침을 준 것에 진심으로 고마워했단다.\""]], [["\"Tell another story, mother,--one with a moral to it, like this.", "\"엄마, 이런 거 말고 또 다른 얘기 해 줘요. 이렇게 교훈이 담긴 걸로요."], ["I like to think about them afterwards, if they are real, and not too preachy,\"", "진짜 있었던 일이고, 너무 설교 같지만 않으면, 나중에 두고두고 곱씹어 보는 게 좋거든요.\""], ["said Jo, after a minute's silence.", "잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤 조가 말했다."]], [["Mrs.", "마치 부인은 미소를 짓더니 곧바로 이야기를 시작했다."], ["March smiled, and began at once; for she had told stories to this little audience for many years, and knew how to please them.", "여러 해 동안 이 작은 청중에게 이야기를 들려준 터라, 그들을 어떻게 즐겁게 해야 하는지 잘 알고 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"Once upon a time, there were four girls, who had enough to eat and drink and wear, a good many comforts and pleasures, kind friends and parents, who loved them dearly, and yet they were not contented.\"", "\"옛날 옛적에, 네 자매가 있었단다. 먹고 마시고 입을 것이 넉넉했고, 갖가지 안락과 즐거움도 누렸으며, 다정한 친구들과 자기들을 끔찍이 사랑하는 부모도 있었지."], ["(Here the listeners stole sly looks at one another, and began to sew diligently.) \"These girls were anxious to be good, and made many excellent resolutions; but they did not keep them very well, and were constantly saying, 'If we only had this,'", "그런데도 그 애들은 만족할 줄 몰랐어.\" (여기서 듣고 있던 자매들은 슬그머니 서로를 흘끔거리고는 부지런히 바느질하기 시작했다.) \"이 자매들은 착해지고 싶어 했고, 훌륭한 결심도 숱하게 했단다."], ["or 'If we could only do that,' quite forgetting how much they already had, and how many pleasant things they actually could do.", "하지만 그 결심을 잘 지키지 못했고, 자기들이 이미 얼마나 많은 걸 가졌는지, 또 실제로 얼마나 많은 즐거운 일을 할 수 있는지는 까맣게 잊은 채, 늘 '이것만 있으면 좋을 텐데'라거나 '저것만 할 수 있으면 좋을 텐데'라고 입버릇처럼 말했지."], ["So they asked an old woman what spell they could use to make them happy, and she said, 'When you feel discontented, think over your blessings, and be grateful.'\"", "그래서 어떻게 하면 행복해질 수 있을지, 무슨 주문을 외면 되겠는지 한 노파에게 물었더니, 노파가 이렇게 일러 주었단다. '불만스러울 때면, 네가 받은 축복들을 곰곰이 헤아려 보고, 감사하렴.'\""], ["(Here Jo looked up quickly, as if about to speak, but changed her mind, seeing that the story was not done yet.)", "(여기서 조가 무슨 말이라도 하려는 듯 얼른 고개를 들었지만, 이야기가 아직 끝나지 않은 것을 보고 마음을 고쳐먹었다.)"]], [["\"Being sensible girls, they decided to try her advice, and soon were surprised to see how well off they were. One discovered that money couldn't keep shame and sorrow out of rich people's houses; another that, though she was poor, she was a great deal happier, with her youth, health, and good spirits, than a certain fretful, feeble old lady, who couldn't enjoy her comforts; a third that, disagreeable as it was to help get dinner, it was harder still to have to go begging for it; and the fourth, that even carnelian rings were not so valuable as good behavior. So they agreed to stop complaining, to enjoy the blessings already possessed, and try to deserve them, lest they should be taken away entirely, instead of increased; and I believe they were never disappointed, or sorry that they took the old woman's advice.\"", "\"분별 있는 아이들이었던지라, 그 충고를 따라 보기로 했고, 곧 자기들이 얼마나 풍족한지 깨닫고는 놀랐단다. 한 아이는 돈이 부잣집에서 부끄러움과 슬픔을 막아 주지 못한다는 걸 알게 됐어. 또 한 아이는, 자기가 비록 가난해도, 안락을 누리지도 못하는 어떤 안달복달하고 병약한 노부인보다, 젊음과 건강과 쾌활함을 지닌 자기가 훨씬 더 행복하다는 걸 깨달았지. 또 한 아이는, 저녁 차리는 일을 거드는 게 아무리 싫어도, 그걸 구걸하러 다녀야 하는 건 그보다 더 힘들다는 걸 알았어. 그리고 넷째 아이는, 홍옥수 반지조차 바른 행실만큼 값지지는 않다는 걸 깨달았단다. 그래서 그 애들은 불평을 그치고, 이미 가진 축복들을 누리며, 그것이 더 늘어나기는커녕 아예 빼앗겨 버리는 일이 없도록 그 축복에 어울리는 사람이 되려고 애쓰기로 뜻을 모았지. 그리고 내가 믿기로, 그 애들은 노파의 충고를 따른 것을 결코 후회하거나 아쉬워하지 않았단다.\""]], [["\"Now, Marmee, that is very cunning of you to turn our own stories against us, and give us a sermon instead of a romance!\"", "\"엄마, 우리 이야기를 우리한테 되돌려서 로맨스 대신 설교를 들려주다니, 참 약으시네요!\""], ["cried Meg.", "메그가 외쳤다."]], [["\"I like that kind of sermon.", "\"난 그런 설교가 좋아."], ["It's the sort father used to tell us,\"", "아빠가 들려주시던 그런 거잖아.\""], ["said Beth thoughtfully, putting the needles straight on Jo's cushion.", "베스가 조의 바늘방석에 바늘들을 가지런히 꽂으며 사려 깊게 말했다."]], [["\"I don't complain near as much as the others do, and I shall be more careful than ever now; for I've had warning from Susie's downfall,\"", "\"난 다른 애들만큼 불평하지도 않거니와, 이제부터는 그 어느 때보다 더 조심할 거야. 수지가 망신당한 걸 보고 미리 경고를 받은 셈이니까.\""], ["said Amy morally.", "에이미가 점잔을 빼며 말했다."]], [["\"We needed that lesson, and we won't forget it.", "\"우린 그 교훈이 필요했고, 잊지 말아야 해."], ["If we do, you just say to us, as old Chloe did in 'Uncle Tom,'", "혹시 잊거든, '톰 아저씨'에 나오는 늙은 클로이가 그랬던 것처럼 우리한테 이렇게 말해 줘."], ["'Tink ob yer marcies, chillen!", "'받은 은혜를 생각혀, 얘들아!"], ["tink ob yer marcies!'\"", "받은 은혜를 생각혀!'\" 조가 덧붙였다."], ["added Jo, who could not, for the life of her, help getting a morsel of fun out of the little sermon, though she took it to heart as much as any of them.", "그 작은 설교를 다른 누구 못지않게 마음 깊이 새기면서도, 거기서 우스갯거리 한 조각을 끄집어내지 않고는 도저히 못 배기는 그녀였다. 5."]], [["# BEING NEIGHBORLY.", "# BEING NEIGHBORLY."]], [["\"What in the world are you going to do now, Jo?\"", "이웃과 친해지기. \"대체 또 뭘 하려는 거야, 조?\""], ["asked Meg, one snowy afternoon, as her sister came tramping through the hall, in rubber boots, old sack and hood, with a broom in one hand and a shovel in the other.", "어느 눈 내리는 오후, 한 손엔 빗자루를, 다른 손엔 삽을 들고 고무장화에 낡은 외투와 두건 차림으로 복도를 쿵쿵 걸어오는 동생을 보고 메그가 물었다."]], [["\"Going out for exercise,\"", "\"운동하러 나가는 거야.\""], ["answered Jo, with a mischievous twinkle in her eyes.", "조가 눈을 짓궂게 반짝이며 대답했다."]], [["\"I should think two long walks this morning would have been enough!", "\"오늘 아침에 긴 산책을 두 번이나 했으면 충분할 텐데!"], ["It's cold and dull out; and I advise you to stay, warm and dry, by the fire, as I do,\"", "밖은 춥고 음산해. 나처럼 난롯가에서 따뜻하고 보송보송하게 있는 게 좋을 것 같은데.\""], ["said Meg, with a shiver.", "메그가 몸을 부르르 떨며 말했다."]], [["\"Never take advice!", "\"난 충고 같은 거 안 들어!"], ["Can't keep still all day, and, not being a pussycat, I don't like to doze by the fire.", "하루 종일 가만있을 순 없고, 난 고양이가 아니니까 난롯가에서 꾸벅꾸벅 조는 것도 싫어."], ["I like adventures, and I'm going to find some.\"", "난 모험이 좋고, 그래서 좀 찾으러 가는 거야.\""]], [["Meg went back to toast her feet and read \"Ivanhoe\"; and Jo began to dig paths with great energy.", "메그는 다시 발을 쬐며 '아이반호'를 읽으러 갔고, 조는 기운차게 길을 내기 시작했다."], ["The snow was light, and with her broom she soon swept a path all round the garden, for Beth to walk in when the sun came out; and the invalid dolls needed air.", "눈이 가벼워서, 빗자루로 정원을 빙 둘러 길을 금세 쓸어 놓았다. 해가 나면 베스가 거기를 걸을 수 있도록, 또 병든 인형들에게도 바람을 쐬어 줘야 했으니까."], ["Now, the garden separated the Marches' house from that of Mr.", "그런데 그 정원이 마치네 집과 로런스 씨네 집을 갈라놓고 있었다."], ["Laurence. Both stood in a suburb of the city, which was still country-like, with groves and lawns, large gardens, and quiet streets.", "두 집 다 도시의 외곽에 있었는데, 그곳은 아직 시골 분위기가 남아 있어 작은 숲과 잔디밭, 너른 정원과 한적한 거리가 있었다."], ["A low hedge parted the two estates.", "낮은 산울타리가 두 저택을 나누었다."], ["On one side was an old, brown house, looking rather bare and shabby, robbed of the vines that in summer covered its walls, and the flowers which then surrounded it.", "한쪽에는 낡고 갈색을 띤 집이 서 있었는데, 여름이면 담을 뒤덮던 덩굴과 둘레를 에워싸던 꽃들을 빼앗긴 채 다소 휑하고 초라해 보였다."], ["On the other side was a stately stone mansion, plainly betokening every sort of comfort and luxury, from the big coach-house and well-kept grounds to the conservatory and the glimpses of lovely things one caught between the rich curtains.", "다른 쪽에는 위풍당당한 석조 저택이 서 있어, 커다란 마차고와 잘 가꾼 부지에서부터 온실과 화려한 커튼 사이로 언뜻언뜻 비치는 어여쁜 것들에 이르기까지, 온갖 안락과 호사를 한눈에 드러냈다."], ["Yet it seemed a lonely, lifeless sort of house; for no children frolicked on the lawn, no motherly face ever smiled at the windows, and few people went in and out, except the old gentleman and his grandson.", "그런데도 어쩐지 외롭고 생기 없는 집 같았다. 잔디밭에서 뛰노는 아이도 없었고, 창가에 미소 짓는 자애로운 얼굴도 없었으며, 노신사와 그 손자 말고는 드나드는 사람도 거의 없었으니까."]], [["To Jo's lively fancy, this fine house seemed a kind of enchanted palace, full of splendors and delights, which no one enjoyed.", "조의 발랄한 상상 속에서, 이 멋진 집은 아무도 즐기지 못하는 온갖 화려함과 즐거움으로 가득 찬 일종의 마법에 걸린 궁전 같았다."], ["She had long wanted to behold these hidden glories, and to know the \"Laurence boy,\" who looked as if he would like to be known, if he only knew how to begin.", "그녀는 오래전부터 이 감추어진 영화를 두 눈으로 보고 싶었고, 시작할 방법만 안다면 알고 지내고 싶어 하는 듯한 그 \"로런스 도련님\"과 사귀고 싶었다."], ["Since the party, she had been more eager than ever, and had planned many ways of making friends with him; but he had not been seen lately, and Jo began to think he had gone away, when she one day spied a brown face at an upper window, looking wistfully down into their garden, where Beth and Amy were snow-balling one another.", "무도회 이후로 그 마음은 더욱 간절해져서, 그와 친구가 될 온갖 방법을 궁리했다. 그런데 요사이 그가 통 보이지 않아, 멀리 떠난 게 아닐까 생각하던 차에, 어느 날 위층 창가에서 까무잡잡한 얼굴 하나가 베스와 에이미가 눈싸움을 하고 있는 자기네 정원을 그리운 듯 내려다보는 것을 발견했다."]], [["\"That boy is suffering for society and fun,\"", "'저 애는 어울릴 사람과 재밋거리가 간절한 거야.'"], ["she said to herself.", "그녀는 속으로 생각했다."], ["\"His grandpa does not know what's good for him, and keeps him shut up all alone.", "'저 애 할아버지는 뭐가 저 애한테 좋은지도 모르고, 혼자 가둬만 두시는 거지."], ["He needs a party of jolly boys to play with, or somebody young and lively.", "같이 놀 유쾌한 남자애들이 한 무리 있거나, 아니면 누구든 젊고 활기찬 사람이 곁에 있어야 해."], ["I've a great mind to go over and tell the old gentleman so!\"", "정말이지 건너가서 그 노신사한테 그렇게 말씀드리고 싶다!'"]], [["The idea amused Jo, who liked to do daring things, and was always scandalizing Meg by her queer performances.", "대담한 일을 즐기고, 그 별난 행동으로 늘 메그를 기겁하게 만드는 조에게 그 생각은 흥미로웠다."], ["The plan of \"going over\" was not forgotten; and when the snowy afternoon came, Jo resolved to try what could be done.", "\"건너간다\"는 계획은 잊히지 않았고, 눈 내리는 오후가 찾아오자 조는 어디 한번 해 보기로 마음먹었다."], ["She saw Mr. Laurence drive off, and then sallied out to dig her way down to the hedge, where she paused, and took a survey.", "그녀는 로런스 씨가 마차를 타고 떠나는 것을 본 뒤 밖으로 나서서 산울타리까지 길을 냈고, 거기서 걸음을 멈추고 사방을 살폈다."], ["All quiet,--curtains down at the lower windows; servants out of sight, and nothing human visible but a curly black head leaning on a thin hand at the upper window.", "사방이 고요했다. 아래층 창문엔 커튼이 내려져 있고, 하인들도 보이지 않았으며, 사람이라곤 위층 창가에서 여윈 손에 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리를 괴고 있는 모습 말고는 보이지 않았다."]], [["\"There he is,\"", "'저기 있구나.'"], ["thought Jo, \"poor boy!", "조는 생각했다. '가엾어라!"], ["all alone and sick this dismal day.", "이 음산한 날에 혼자 아파서 저러고 있다니."], ["It's a shame!", "너무하잖아!"], ["I'll toss up a snow-ball, and make him look out, and then say a kind word to him.\"", "눈덩이를 던져서 내다보게 한 다음, 다정하게 한마디 건네야겠어.'"]], [["Up went a handful of soft snow, and the head turned at once, showing a face which lost its listless look in a minute, as the big eyes brightened and the mouth began to smile.", "부드러운 눈 한 줌이 날아오르자 그 머리가 곧장 돌아섰고, 큰 눈이 환해지며 입가에 미소가 번지자, 그 얼굴에서 무기력한 기색이 순식간에 사라졌다."], ["Jo nodded and laughed, and flourished her broom as she called out,--", "조는 고개를 끄덕이며 웃고는 빗자루를 흔들며 소리쳤다."]], [["\"How do you do?", "\"안녕하세요?"], ["Are you sick?\"", "아파요?\""]], [["Laurie opened the window, and croaked out as hoarsely as a raven,--", "로리가 창문을 열더니 까마귀처럼 쉰 목소리로 깍깍거리듯 말했다."]], [["\"Better, thank you.", "\"좀 나아졌어요, 고마워요."], ["I've had a bad cold, and been shut up a week.\"", "독한 감기에 걸려서 일주일째 갇혀 있었어요.\""]], [["\"I'm sorry.", "\"안됐네요."], ["What do you amuse yourself with?\"", "뭐 하면서 시간 보내요?\""]], [["\"Nothing; it's as dull as tombs up here.\"", "\"아무것도 안 해요. 여기 위는 무덤처럼 따분하다니까요.\""]], [["\"Don't you read?\"", "\"책은 안 읽어요?\""]], [["\"Not much; they won't let me.\"", "\"별로요. 못 읽게 해요.\""]], [["\"Can't somebody read to you?\"", "\"누가 읽어 줄 사람도 없어요?\""]], [["\"Grandpa does, sometimes; but my books don't interest him, and I hate to ask Brooke all the time.\"", "\"할아버지가 가끔 읽어 주세요. 그런데 제 책엔 통 흥미가 없으시고, 브룩 선생님한테 매번 부탁하기는 싫고요.\""]], [["\"Have some one come and see you, then.\"", "\"그럼 누구라도 보러 오라고 해요.\""]], [["\"There isn't any one I'd like to see.", "\"보고 싶은 사람이 없어요."], ["Boys make such a row, and my head is weak.\"", "남자애들은 어찌나 시끄러운지, 제 머리가 약해서요.\""]], [["\"Isn't there some nice girl who'd read and amuse you?", "\"읽어 주고 즐겁게 해 줄 마음씨 좋은 여자애는 없어요?"], ["Girls are quiet, and like to play nurse.\"", "여자애들은 조용하고, 간병 놀이도 좋아하는데.\""]], [["\"Don't know any.\"", "\"아는 애가 없어요.\""]], [["\"You know us,\"", "\"우리를 알잖아요.\""], ["began Jo, then laughed, and stopped.", "조가 입을 열다가, 웃음을 터뜨리며 멈추었다."]], [["\"So I do!", "\"아, 그렇네요!"], ["Will you come, please?\"", "와 주실래요, 부탁이에요?\""], ["cried Laurie.", "로리가 외쳤다."]], [["\"I'm not quiet and nice; but I'll come, if mother will let me.", "\"난 조용하지도, 마음씨 좋지도 않지만, 엄마가 허락하시면 갈게요."], ["I'll go ask her.", "가서 여쭤볼게요."], ["Shut that window, like a good boy, and wait till I come.\"", "착하게 그 창문 닫고, 내가 올 때까지 기다려요.\""]], [["With that, Jo shouldered her broom and marched into the house, wondering what they would all say to her.", "그렇게 말하고 조는 빗자루를 둘러메고 집 안으로 성큼성큼 들어가며, 식구들이 다들 뭐라고 할까 궁금해했다."], ["Laurie was in a flutter of excitement at the idea of having company, and flew about to get ready; for, as Mrs.", "손님이 온다는 생각에 잔뜩 들뜬 로리는 채비를 하느라 부산을 떨었다."], ["March said, he was \"a little gentleman,\" and did honor to the coming guest by brushing his curly pate, putting on a fresh collar, and trying to tidy up the room, which, in spite of half a dozen servants, was anything but neat.", "마치 부인의 말마따나 그는 \"어린 신사\"였기에, 곱슬머리를 빗어 넘기고 깨끗한 깃을 갈아 달고, 방을 좀 치우려 애쓰며 다가올 손님에게 예를 갖추었다."], ["Presently there came a loud ring, then a decided voice, asking for \"Mr.", "하인이 대여섯이나 있는데도 방은 깔끔함과는 거리가 멀었다."], ["Laurie,\" and a surprised-looking servant came running up to announce a young lady.", "이윽고 요란하게 초인종이 울리고, 이어 또렷한 목소리가 \"로리 도련님\"을 찾았으며, 어리둥절한 표정의 하인이 젊은 아가씨가 왔다고 알리러 뛰어 올라왔다."]], [["\"All right, show her up, it's Miss Jo,\"", "\"좋아, 올려보내요. 조 양이에요.\""], ["said Laurie, going to the door of his little parlor to meet Jo, who appeared, looking rosy and kind and quite at her ease, with a covered dish in one hand and Beth's three kittens in the other.", "로리가 자기 작은 응접실 문 앞으로 가서 조를 맞이하며 말했다. 조는 한 손엔 뚜껑 덮인 접시를, 다른 손엔 베스의 새끼 고양이 세 마리를 안고 나타났는데, 발그레한 얼굴에 다정하고 무척 편안해 보였다."]], [["\"Here I am, bag and baggage,\"", "\"짐 보따리까지 다 챙겨서 왔어요.\""], ["she said briskly.", "그녀가 활기차게 말했다."], ["\"Mother sent her love, and was glad if I could do anything for you.", "\"엄마가 안부 전하시면서, 내가 뭐라도 도움이 되면 좋겠다고 하셨어요."], ["Meg wanted me to bring some of her blanc-mange; she makes it very nicely, and Beth thought her cats would be comforting.", "메그 언니는 자기가 만든 블랑망제를 좀 가져가라고 했고요. 솜씨가 아주 좋거든요."], ["I knew you'd laugh at them, but I couldn't refuse, she was so anxious to do something.\"", "그리고 베스는 자기 고양이들이 위로가 될 거라고 했어요. 도련님이 보고 웃을 줄 알았지만, 베스가 뭐라도 하고 싶어서 어찌나 안달인지 거절할 수가 없었어요.\""]], [["It so happened that Beth's funny loan was just the thing; for, in laughing over the kits, Laurie forgot his bashfulness, and grew sociable at once.", "공교롭게도 베스의 우스운 빌림이 안성맞춤이었다. 새끼 고양이들을 보고 웃다 보니 로리는 수줍음을 잊고 단박에 붙임성 있게 굴기 시작했으니까."]], [["\"That looks too pretty to eat,\"", "\"먹기 아까울 만큼 예쁜데요.\""], ["he said, smiling with pleasure, as Jo uncovered the dish, and showed the blanc-mange, surrounded by a garland of green leaves, and the scarlet flowers of Amy's pet geranium.", "조가 접시 뚜껑을 열어, 푸른 잎사귀 화환과 에이미가 애지중지하는 제라늄의 진홍색 꽃에 둘러싸인 블랑망제를 보여 주자, 그가 기쁜 듯 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["\"It isn't anything, only they all felt kindly, and wanted to show it.", "\"별것 아니에요. 그저 다들 마음을 써서, 그걸 표현하고 싶어 했을 뿐이죠."], ["Tell the girl to put it away for your tea: it's so simple, you can eat it; and, being soft, it will slip down without hurting your sore throat.", "하녀더러 차 마실 때 드시게 치워 두라고 하세요. 워낙 부드러워서 그냥 먹어도 되고, 말랑말랑하니까 아픈 목을 안 다치고 술술 넘어갈 거예요."], ["What a cosy room this is!\"", "방이 참 아늑하네요!\""]], [["\"It might be if it was kept nice; but the maids are lazy, and I don't know how to make them mind.", "\"잘 가꾸기만 하면 그럴 텐데, 하녀들이 게을러서요. 게다가 전 걔네를 어떻게 부려야 할지도 모르고요."], ["It worries me, though.\"", "그래도 마음이 쓰이긴 해요.\""]], [["\"I'll right it up in two minutes; for it only needs to have the hearth brushed, so,--and the things made straight on the mantel-piece so,--and the books put here, and the bottles there, and your sofa turned from the light, and the pillows plumped up a bit.", "\"내가 이 분 안에 말끔히 정리해 줄게요. 난로만 이렇게 쓸어 주고, 벽난로 선반 위 물건들을 이렇게 가지런히 놓고, 책은 여기, 병은 저기 두고, 소파는 빛을 등지게 돌려놓고, 쿠션은 좀 통통하게 부풀려 주면 돼요."], ["Now, then, you're fixed.\"", "자, 이제 다 됐어요.\""]], [["And so he was; for, as she laughed and talked, Jo had whisked things into place, and given quite a different air to the room.", "정말 그러했다. 조는 웃고 떠들면서 물건들을 휙휙 제자리에 놓아, 방에 사뭇 다른 분위기를 입혀 놓았던 것이다."], ["Laurie watched her in respectful silence; and when she beckoned him to his sofa, he sat down with a sigh of satisfaction, saying gratefully,--", "로리는 공손한 침묵 속에 그녀를 지켜보다가, 그녀가 소파로 오라고 손짓하자 만족스러운 한숨을 내쉬며 앉아 고맙게 말했다."]], [["\"How kind you are!", "\"정말 친절하시네요!"], ["Yes, that's what it wanted.", "맞아요, 바로 이렇게 됐으면 했어요."], ["Now please take the big chair, and let me do something to amuse my company.\"", "자, 이제 그 큰 의자에 앉으세요. 제가 손님을 즐겁게 해 드릴 수 있게요.\""]], [["\"No; I came to amuse you. Shall I read aloud?\"", "\"아니에요, 난 도련님을 즐겁게 해 주러 왔는걸요."], ["and Jo looked affectionately toward some inviting books near by.", "책 읽어 줄까요?\" 그러면서 조는 근처에 놓인 마음을 끄는 책들을 정겹게 바라보았다."]], [["\"Thank you; I've read all those, and if you don't mind, I'd rather talk,\"", "\"고마워요. 그건 다 읽었어요."], ["answered Laurie.", "괜찮으시면 그냥 얘기나 했으면 좋겠어요.\" 로리가 대답했다."]], [["\"Not a bit; I'll talk all day if you'll only set me going.", "\"그래요. 말문만 터 주면 난 하루 종일도 떠들 수 있어요."], ["Beth says I never know when to stop.\"", "베스 말로는, 난 도무지 멈출 줄을 모른대요.\""]], [["\"Is Beth the rosy one, who stays at home a good deal, and sometimes goes out with a little basket?\"", "\"베스가 그 발그레한 얼굴에 집에 오래 있고, 가끔 작은 바구니를 들고 나가는 아이지?\""], ["asked Laurie, with interest.", "로리가 관심을 보이며 물었다."]], [["\"Yes, that's Beth; she's my girl, and a regular good one she is, too.\"", "\"맞아, 그 애가 베스야. 내 동생인데, 정말 착하기도 하지.\""]], [["\"The pretty one is Meg, and the curly-haired one is Amy, I believe?\"", "\"예쁜 애가 메그고, 곱슬머리가 에이미인 것 같은데?\""]], [["\"How did you find that out?\"", "\"그걸 어떻게 알았어?\""]], [["Laurie colored up, but answered frankly, \"Why, you see, I often hear you calling to one another, and when I'm alone up here, I can't help looking over at your house, you always seem to be having such good times. I beg your pardon for being so rude, but sometimes you forget to put down the curtain at the window where the flowers are; and when the lamps are lighted, it's like looking at a picture to see the fire, and you all round the table with your mother; her face is right opposite, and it looks so sweet behind the flowers, I can't help watching it. I haven't got any mother, you know;\" and Laurie poked the fire to hide a little twitching of the lips that he could not control.", "로리는 얼굴이 발그레해졌지만 솔직하게 대답했다. \"저, 그게 말이야, 너희가 서로 부르는 소리를 자주 듣거든. 그리고 여기 위에 혼자 있을 때면 너희 집을 자꾸 건너다보게 돼. 너희는 늘 그렇게 즐겁게 지내는 것 같아서. 이렇게 무례하게 군 거 미안해. 그런데 너희가 가끔 꽃이 놓인 창문에 커튼 내리는 걸 잊거든. 등불이 켜지면 난롯불이며, 엄마를 둘러싸고 식탁에 앉은 너희 모습이 꼭 그림을 보는 것 같아. 어머니 얼굴이 바로 맞은편에 있는데, 꽃들 뒤로 보이는 그 모습이 어찌나 다정한지 안 볼 수가 없어. 나는 엄마가 없잖아.\" 로리는 어쩔 수 없이 떨리는 입술을 감추려고 난로를 들쑤셨다."]], [["The solitary, hungry look in his eyes went straight to Jo's warm heart.", "그 눈에 어린 외롭고 허기진 빛이 조의 따뜻한 마음을 곧장 파고들었다."], ["She had been so simply taught that there was no nonsense in her head, and at fifteen she was as innocent and frank as any child.", "조는 워낙 단순하게 자라서 머릿속에 잔꾀라곤 없었고, 열다섯 살이지만 여느 아이처럼 천진하고 솔직했다. 로리는 병약하고 외로웠다."], ["Laurie was sick and lonely; and, feeling how rich she was in home-love and happiness, she gladly tried to share it with him.", "조는 자신이 가정의 사랑과 행복을 얼마나 풍족하게 누리고 있는지 새삼 느끼며, 기꺼이 그것을 그와 나누고 싶었다."], ["Her face was very friendly and her sharp voice unusually gentle as she said,--", "조는 무척 다정한 얼굴로, 평소의 날카로운 목소리와는 사뭇 다른 부드러운 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"We'll never draw that curtain any more, and I give you leave to look as much as you like.", "\"앞으로 그 커튼은 절대 안 내릴게. 그러니까 마음껏 봐도 좋아."], ["I just wish, though, instead of peeping, you'd come over and see us.", "그런데 말이야, 그렇게 훔쳐보지 말고 우리 집에 놀러 오면 좋겠어. 우리 엄마는 정말 멋진 분이라서 너한테 큰 도움이 될 거야."], ["Mother is so splendid, she'd do you heaps of good, and Beth would sing to you if _I_ begged her to, and Amy would dance; Meg and I would make you laugh over our funny stage properties, and we'd have jolly times.", "베스는 내가 부탁하면 너한테 노래도 불러 줄 거고, 에이미는 춤을 출 거야. 메그하고 나는 우리 우스꽝스러운 연극 소품들로 너를 웃겨 줄게."], ["Wouldn't your grandpa let you?\"", "우리 정말 재밌게 지낼 수 있어. 너희 할아버지가 허락 안 해 주실까?\""]], [["\"I think he would, if your mother asked him.", "\"너희 어머니가 부탁하시면 허락하실 것 같아. 겉보기엔 안 그래 보여도 무척 인자하신 분이거든."], ["He's very kind, though he does not look so; and he lets me do what I like, pretty much, only he's afraid I might be a bother to strangers,\"", "그리고 내가 하고 싶은 대로 거의 다 하게 해 주셔. 다만 내가 낯선 사람들한테 폐가 될까 봐 걱정하시는 거야.\""], ["began Laurie, brightening more and more.", "로리가 점점 더 밝아진 얼굴로 말문을 열었다."]], [["\"We are not strangers, we are neighbors, and you needn't think you'd be a bother.", "\"우린 낯선 사람들이 아니라 이웃이야. 그리고 네가 폐가 될 거라고 생각할 필요도 없어."], ["We _want_ to know you, and I've been trying to do it this ever so long.", "우리는 너랑 친해지고 싶어. 사실 나도 오래전부터 그러려고 애썼는걸."], ["We haven't been here a great while, you know, but we have got acquainted with all our neighbors but you.\"", "우리가 여기 온 지 그리 오래되진 않았지만, 너만 빼고 이웃들하곤 다 알고 지내거든.\""]], [["\"You see grandpa lives among his books, and doesn't mind much what happens outside.", "\"할아버지는 책 속에 파묻혀 사셔서 바깥일엔 별로 관심이 없으셔. 우리 가정교사인 브룩 선생님도 여기 안 머무시고."], ["Mr.", "그래서 같이 다닐 사람이 아무도 없어."], ["Brooke, my tutor, doesn't stay here, you know, and I have no one to go about with me, so I just stop at home and get on as I can.\"", "그냥 집에 있으면서 되는대로 지내는 거지.\""]], [["\"That's bad.", "\"그건 안 좋아."], ["You ought to make an effort, and go visiting everywhere you are asked; then you'll have plenty of friends, and pleasant places to go to.", "너도 좀 노력해서 초대받는 곳마다 다녀야 해. 그러면 친구도 많아지고 갈 만한 즐거운 곳도 생길 거야."], ["Never mind being bashful; it won't last long if you keep going.\"", "수줍어하는 건 걱정하지 마. 자꾸 다니다 보면 금세 사라질 테니까.\""]], [["Laurie turned red again, but wasn't offended at being accused of bashfulness; for there was so much good-will in Jo, it was impossible not to take her blunt speeches as kindly as they were meant.", "로리는 다시 얼굴이 빨개졌지만, 수줍어한다는 말을 들었다고 기분 나빠하지는 않았다. 조에겐 호의가 가득해서, 그 무뚝뚝한 말들도 그 본뜻 그대로 다정하게 받아들이지 않을 수 없었던 것이다."]], [["\"Do you like your school?\"", "\"학교는 마음에 들어?\""], ["asked the boy, changing the subject, after a little pause, during which he stared at the fire, and Jo looked about her, well pleased.", "잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 난로를 응시하던 소년이 화제를 바꾸며 물었다. 그동안 조는 흐뭇하게 주위를 둘러보고 있었다."]], [["\"Don't go to school; I'm a business man--girl, I mean.", "\"난 학교 안 다녀. 직장인이거든."], ["I go to wait on my great-aunt, and a dear, cross old soul she is, too,\"", "아니, 직장 다니는 여자애지. 큰할머니 시중을 들어 드리러 다녀."], ["answered Jo.", "그런데 정말 다정하면서도 까칠한 할머니야.\" 조가 대답했다."]], [["Laurie opened his mouth to ask another question; but remembering just in time that it wasn't manners to make too many inquiries into people's affairs, he shut it again, and looked uncomfortable.", "로리는 또 다른 질문을 하려고 입을 열었지만, 남의 사정을 너무 꼬치꼬치 캐묻는 건 예의가 아니라는 게 마침 떠올라 다시 입을 다물고는 어색한 표정을 지었다."], ["Jo liked his good breeding, and didn't mind having a laugh at Aunt March, so she gave him a lively description of the fidgety old lady, her fat poodle, the parrot that talked Spanish, and the library where she revelled.", "조는 그의 예의 바른 태도가 마음에 들었고, 또 큰할머니 흉을 보는 것쯤은 개의치 않았기에, 그 안절부절못하는 할머니며 살찐 푸들이며 스페인어를 지껄이는 앵무새며, 자기가 마음껏 누비는 서재까지 신나게 들려주었다."], ["Laurie enjoyed that immensely; and when she told about the prim old gentleman who came once to woo Aunt March, and, in the middle of a fine speech, how Poll had tweaked his wig off to his great dismay, the boy lay back and laughed till the tears ran down his cheeks, and a maid popped her head in to see what was the matter.", "로리는 그 이야기를 무척 재미있어했다. 그리고 조가 큰할머니에게 청혼하러 한번 찾아온 점잖빼는 노신사 이야기며, 그가 멋들어진 연설을 하던 도중에 앵무새 폴이 그의 가발을 홱 잡아채 버려 그를 기겁하게 만들었다는 대목을 들려주자, 소년은 뒤로 벌렁 누워 눈물이 뺨을 타고 흘러내릴 만큼 웃어 댔고, 하녀가 무슨 일인가 싶어 빼꼼 고개를 들이밀 정도였다."]], [["\"Oh!", "\"아!"], ["that does me no end of good.", "정말 속이 다 후련해진다."], ["Tell on, please,\"", "더 해 줘, 부탁이야.\""], ["he said, taking his face out of the sofa-cushion, red and shining with merriment.", "그는 소파 쿠션에서 얼굴을 떼며 말했는데, 그 얼굴은 유쾌함으로 발갛게 상기되어 반짝거렸다."]], [["Much elated with her success, Jo did \"tell on,\"", "자기 이야기의 성공에 한껏 우쭐해진 조는 정말로 \"이야기를 이어 나가\""], ["all about their plays and plans, their hopes and fears for father, and the most interesting events of the little world in which the sisters lived.", "자매들의 연극이며 계획, 아버지에 대한 희망과 걱정, 그리고 자매들이 사는 작은 세계에서 일어나는 가장 흥미로운 사건들까지 죄다 들려주었다."], ["Then they got to talking about books; and to Jo's delight, she found that Laurie loved them as well as she did, and had read even more than herself.", "그러다 둘은 책 이야기로 접어들었는데, 조는 로리가 자기만큼이나 책을 좋아하고, 게다가 자기보다 더 많이 읽었다는 사실을 알고 기뻐했다."]], [["\"If you like them so much, come down and see ours.", "\"책을 그렇게 좋아하면, 내려와서 우리 책들 좀 봐."], ["Grandpa is out, so you needn't be afraid,\"", "할아버지 안 계시니까 무서워할 거 없어.\""], ["said Laurie, getting up.", "로리가 일어서며 말했다."]], [["\"I'm not afraid of anything,\"", "\"난 무서운 거 하나도 없어.\""], ["returned Jo, with a toss of the head.", "조가 고개를 홱 젖히며 대꾸했다."]], [["\"I don't believe you are!\"", "\"정말 그럴 것 같아!\""], ["exclaimed the boy, looking at her with much admiration, though he privately thought she would have good reason to be a trifle afraid of the old gentleman, if she met him in some of his moods.", "소년이 그녀를 무척 감탄하는 눈길로 바라보며 소리쳤다. 비록 속으로는, 할아버지가 어떤 기분일 때 마주친다면 조도 좀 무서워할 만한 이유가 충분히 있을 거라고 생각했지만."]], [["The atmosphere of the whole house being summer-like, Laurie led the way from room to room, letting Jo stop to examine whatever struck her fancy; and so at last they came to the library, where she clapped her hands, and pranced, as she always did when especially delighted.", "집 안 전체가 여름처럼 따스했으므로, 로리는 이 방 저 방 앞장서서 안내하며 조가 마음 끌리는 것은 무엇이든 멈춰 서서 살펴보게 했다. 그렇게 해서 마침내 둘은 서재에 이르렀고, 조는 특별히 기쁠 때면 늘 그러듯 손뼉을 치며 깡충깡충 뛰었다."], ["It was lined with books, and there were pictures and statues, and distracting little cabinets full of coins and curiosities, and sleepy-hollow chairs, and queer tables, and bronzes; and, best of all, a great open fireplace, with quaint tiles all round it.", "서재는 책들로 빼곡했고, 그림과 조각상이 있었으며, 동전과 진기한 물건이 가득 든 정신 사나운 작은 진열장들과 푹 파묻히는 안락의자, 기묘한 탁자, 청동상들이 있었다. 무엇보다도 사방에 고풍스러운 타일이 둘러진 커다란 벽난로가 있었다."]], [["\"What richness!\"", "\"이렇게 풍족할 수가!\""], ["sighed Jo, sinking into the depth of a velvet chair, and gazing about her with an air of intense satisfaction.", "조는 벨벳 의자 깊숙이 파묻히며, 더없이 만족스러운 표정으로 주위를 둘러보면서 탄성을 질렀다."], ["\"Theodore Laurence, you ought to be the happiest boy in the world,\"", "\"시어도어 로런스, 넌 세상에서 제일 행복한 소년일 거야.\""], ["she added impressively.", "그녀가 힘주어 덧붙였다."]], [["\"A fellow can't live on books,\"", "\"사람이 책만으로 살 순 없잖아.\""], ["said Laurie, shaking his head, as he perched on a table opposite.", "로리가 맞은편 탁자에 걸터앉으며 고개를 저었다."]], [["Before he could say more, a bell rung, and Jo flew up, exclaiming with alarm, \"Mercy me!", "그가 더 말을 잇기도 전에 종소리가 울렸고, 조는 놀라서 펄쩍 뛰며 외쳤다. \"어머나!"], ["it's your grandpa!\"", "너희 할아버지셔!\""]], [["\"Well, what if it is?", "\"그러면 어때서?"], ["You are not afraid of anything, you know,\"", "넌 무서운 거 하나도 없다며.\""], ["returned the boy, looking wicked.", "소년이 짓궂은 표정으로 대꾸했다."]], [["\"I think I am a little bit afraid of him, but I don't know why I should be.", "\"좀 무서운 것 같긴 해. 그런데 왜 그래야 하는지 모르겠어."], ["Marmee said I might come, and I don't think you're any the worse for it,\"", "엄마가 와도 된다고 하셨고, 너한테 해될 것도 없잖아.\""], ["said Jo, composing herself, though she kept her eyes on the door.", "조는 문에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 마음을 가다듬으며 말했다."]], [["\"I'm a great deal better for it, and ever so much obliged.", "\"오히려 나한텐 큰 도움이 됐는걸. 정말 고마워."], ["I'm only afraid you are very tired talking to me; it was _so_ pleasant, I couldn't bear to stop,\"", "다만 나랑 얘기하느라 너무 지친 건 아닌지 걱정이야. 너무 즐거워서 도무지 멈출 수가 없었거든.\""], ["said Laurie gratefully.", "로리가 고마운 마음을 담아 말했다."]], [["\"The doctor to see you, sir,\"", "\"의사 선생님이 오셨어요, 도련님.\""], ["and the maid beckoned as she spoke.", "하녀가 말하면서 손짓을 했다."]], [["\"Would you mind if I left you for a minute?", "\"잠깐 자리를 비워도 괜찮을까?"], ["I suppose I must see him,\"", "선생님을 봬야 할 것 같아서.\""], ["said Laurie.", "로리가 말했다."]], [["\"Don't mind me.", "\"나는 신경 쓰지 마."], ["I'm as happy as a cricket here,\"", "여기서 더없이 행복하니까.\""], ["answered Jo.", "조가 대답했다."]], [["Laurie went away, and his guest amused herself in her own way.", "로리가 나가자, 손님은 자기 나름대로 시간을 즐겼다. 그녀가 노신사의 멋진 초상화 앞에 서 있을 때 문이 다시 열렸고, 조는 돌아보지도 않은 채 단호하게 말했다."], ["She was standing before a fine portrait of the old gentleman, when the door opened again, and, without turning, she said decidedly, \"I'm sure now that I shouldn't be afraid of him, for he's got kind eyes, though his mouth is grim, and he looks as if he had a tremendous will of his own.", "\"이제 확실해. 이분이 무섭지 않을 것 같아."], ["He isn't as handsome as _my_ grandfather, but I like him.\"", "입매는 험상궂고 자기 고집이 어마어마하게 셀 것 같긴 하지만, 눈이 인자하시거든. 우리 할아버지만큼 잘생기진 않으셨어도, 난 이분이 마음에 들어.\""]], [["\"Thank you, ma'am,\"", "\"고맙구나, 아가씨.\" 등 뒤에서 굵직한 목소리가 들려왔다."], ["said a gruff voice behind her; and there, to her great dismay, stood old Mr. Laurence.", "그리고 그녀가 기겁할 만하게도 거기, 늙은 로런스 씨가 서 있었다."]], [["Poor Jo blushed till she couldn't blush any redder, and her heart began to beat uncomfortably fast as she thought what she had said.", "가엾은 조는 더 이상 빨개질 수 없을 만큼 얼굴이 빨개졌고, 자기가 한 말을 떠올리자 심장이 불편할 만큼 빠르게 뛰기 시작했다. 잠시 도망치고 싶은 마음이 미친 듯이 솟구쳤다."], ["For a minute a wild desire to run away possessed her; but that was cowardly, and the girls would laugh at her: so she resolved to stay, and get out of the scrape as she could.", "하지만 그건 비겁한 짓이고, 자매들이 비웃을 터였다. 그래서 그녀는 남아서 이 곤경을 어떻게든 헤쳐 나가기로 마음먹었다."], ["A second look showed her that the living eyes, under the bushy gray eyebrows, were kinder even than the painted ones; and there was a sly twinkle in them, which lessened her fear a good deal.", "다시 보니 텁수룩한 잿빛 눈썹 아래 살아 있는 눈은 초상화 속 눈보다도 더 인자했다. 게다가 그 눈에는 장난기 어린 반짝임이 깃들어 있어 그녀의 두려움을 꽤나 누그러뜨렸다."], ["The gruff voice was gruffer than ever, as the old gentleman said abruptly, after that dreadful pause, \"So you're not afraid of me, hey?\"", "그 끔찍한 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 노신사가 어느 때보다 더 굵직한 목소리로 불쑥 말했다. \"그래, 내가 무섭지 않다고, 응?\""]], [["\"Not much, sir.\"", "\"별로요, 할아버지.\""]], [["\"And you don't think me as handsome as your grandfather?\"", "\"그리고 내가 네 할아버지만큼 잘생기지 않았다고?\""]], [["\"Not quite, sir.\"", "\"꼭 그렇진 않아요, 할아버지.\""]], [["\"And I've got a tremendous will, have I?\"", "\"내 고집이 어마어마하게 세다고, 그래?\""]], [["\"I only said I thought so.\"", "\"그럴 것 같다고만 했어요.\""]], [["\"But you like me, in spite of it?\"", "\"그래도 그런데도 내가 마음에 든다고?\""]], [["\"Yes, I do, sir.\"", "\"네, 그래요, 할아버지.\""]], [["That answer pleased the old gentleman; he gave a short laugh, shook hands with her, and, putting his finger under her chin, turned up her face, examined it gravely, and let it go, saying, with a nod, \"You've got your grandfather's spirit, if you haven't his face. He _was_ a fine man, my dear; but, what is better, he was a brave and an honest one, and I was proud to be his friend.\"", "그 대답이 노신사를 흡족하게 했다. 그는 짤막하게 웃고는 그녀와 악수를 한 뒤, 손가락을 그녀의 턱밑에 대고 얼굴을 들어 올려 진지하게 살펴보더니 놓아주며 고개를 끄덕였다. \"얼굴은 안 닮았어도 네 할아버지의 기개는 물려받았구나. 네 할아버지는 정말 훌륭한 분이었단다, 얘야. 하지만 그보다 더 훌륭한 건, 용감하고 정직한 분이었다는 거지. 나는 그분의 친구라는 게 자랑스러웠단다.\""]], [["\"Thank you, sir;\"", "\"고맙습니다, 할아버지.\""], ["and Jo was quite comfortable after that, for it suited her exactly.", "그 말을 듣고 나서 조는 마음이 아주 편안해졌다. 자기에게 딱 들어맞는 말이었기 때문이다."]], [["\"What have you been doing to this boy of mine, hey?\"", "\"내 손자한테는 무슨 짓을 한 게냐, 응?\""], ["was the next question, sharply put.", "다음 질문이 날카롭게 날아왔다."]], [["\"Only trying to be neighborly, sir;\"", "\"그저 이웃답게 지내려고 한 것뿐이에요, 할아버지.\""], ["and Jo told how her visit came about.", "그러고서 조는 어떻게 해서 찾아오게 됐는지 들려주었다."]], [["\"You think he needs cheering up a bit, do you?\"", "\"그 애한테 기운을 좀 북돋워 줘야 한다고 생각하는 게냐, 그래?\""]], [["\"Yes, sir; he seems a little lonely, and young folks would do him good perhaps.", "\"네, 할아버지. 그 애가 좀 외로워 보여서요."], ["We are only girls, but we should be glad to help if we could, for we don't forget the splendid Christmas present you sent us,\"", "또래 친구들이 도움이 될지도 모르잖아요. 우린 그저 여자애들일 뿐이지만, 도울 수 있다면 기꺼이 돕고 싶어요."], ["said Jo eagerly.", "할아버지가 보내 주신 그 멋진 크리스마스 선물도 잊지 않고 있거든요.\" 조가 열심히 말했다."]], [["\"Tut, tut, tut!", "\"쯧, 쯧, 쯧!"], ["that was the boy's affair.", "그건 그 녀석이 한 일이었지."], ["How is the poor woman?\"", "그 가엾은 부인은 어떻게 지내느냐?\""]], [["\"Doing nicely, sir;\"", "\"잘 지내고 있어요, 할아버지.\""], ["and off went Jo, talking very fast, as she told all about the Hummels, in whom her mother had interested richer friends than they were.", "그러고서 조는 후멜 가족 이야기를 신나게 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그녀의 어머니가 자기들보다 형편이 넉넉한 친구들을 그 가족에게 관심 갖도록 끌어들였던 것이다."]], [["\"Just her father's way of doing good.", "\"꼭 제 애비가 선행을 베풀던 방식이로구나."], ["I shall come and see your mother some fine day.", "언제 날 좋은 날에 너희 어머니를 뵈러 가마."], ["Tell her so.", "그렇게 전해 드려라."], ["There's the tea-bell; we have it early, on the boy's account.", "마침 차 마실 시간을 알리는 종이 울리는구나. 그 녀석 때문에 우린 차를 일찍 마신단다."], ["Come down, and go on being neighborly.\"", "내려가서, 계속 이웃답게 지내려무나.\""]], [["\"If you'd like to have me, sir.\"", "\"저를 원하신다면요, 할아버지.\""]], [["\"Shouldn't ask you, if I didn't;\"", "\"원하지 않으면 청하지도 않았지.\""], ["and Mr. Laurence offered her his arm with old-fashioned courtesy.", "그러고서 로런스 씨는 옛 방식의 정중함으로 그녀에게 팔을 내밀었다."]], [["\"What _would_ Meg say to this?\"", "'메그가 이걸 보면 뭐라고 할까?'"], ["thought Jo, as she was marched away, while her eyes danced with fun as she imagined herself telling the story at home.", "끌려가다시피 걸음을 옮기면서 조는 생각했다. 집에서 이 이야기를 들려주는 자기 모습을 그려 보자 그녀의 두 눈이 장난기로 반짝였다."]], [["\"Hey!", "\"이런!"], ["Why, what the dickens has come to the fellow?\"", "아니, 저 녀석한테 도대체 무슨 일이 생긴 게냐?\""], ["said the old gentleman, as Laurie came running down stairs, and brought up with a start of surprise at the astonishing sight of Jo arm-in-arm with his redoubtable grandfather.", "로리가 계단을 뛰어 내려오다가, 자기가 그토록 어려워하는 할아버지와 조가 팔짱을 끼고 있는 놀라운 광경에 흠칫 멈춰 서자 노신사가 말했다."]], [["\"I didn't know you'd come, sir,\"", "\"오신 줄 몰랐어요, 할아버지.\""], ["he began, as Jo gave him a triumphant little glance.", "조가 그에게 의기양양한 눈길을 살짝 던지는 가운데 그가 입을 열었다."]], [["\"That's evident, by the way you racket down stairs.", "\"계단을 그렇게 요란하게 내려오는 걸 보면 알 만하다."], ["Come to your tea, sir, and behave like a gentleman;\"", "차 마시러 가자꾸나, 도련님. 그리고 신사답게 굴어라.\""], ["and having pulled the boy's hair by way of a caress, Mr. Laurence walked on, while Laurie went through a series of comic evolutions behind their backs, which nearly produced an explosion of laughter from Jo.", "로런스 씨는 애정의 표시로 소년의 머리를 한번 헝클어뜨리고는 앞장서 걸어갔고, 그 사이 로리는 두 사람 등 뒤에서 우스꽝스러운 몸짓을 잇달아 해 보여 조가 하마터면 웃음을 터뜨릴 뻔하게 만들었다."]], [["The old gentleman did not say much as he drank his four cups of tea, but he watched the young people, who soon chatted away like old friends, and the change in his grandson did not escape him.", "노신사는 차를 넉 잔이나 마시는 동안 별말이 없었지만, 이내 오랜 친구처럼 재잘대는 두 젊은이를 지켜보았고, 손자에게 일어난 변화를 놓치지 않았다."], ["There was color, light, and life in the boy's face now, vivacity in his manner, and genuine merriment in his laugh.", "이제 소년의 얼굴에는 혈색과 빛과 생기가 돌았고, 몸짓에는 활기가, 웃음에는 진심에서 우러난 즐거움이 어려 있었다."]], [["\"She's right; the lad _is_ lonely.", "'저 애 말이 맞아. 우리 손자가 외로웠던 게야."], ["I'll see what these little girls can do for him,\" thought Mr.", "이 어린 아가씨들이 그 녀석한테 뭘 해 줄 수 있을지 지켜봐야겠다.'"], ["Laurence, as he looked and listened.", "로런스 씨는 두 사람을 보고 들으며 생각했다. 그는 조가 마음에 들었다."], ["He liked Jo, for her odd, blunt ways suited him; and she seemed to understand the boy almost as well as if she had been one herself.", "그 별나고 무뚝뚝한 태도가 자기와 잘 맞았던 것이다. 게다가 조는 마치 자기가 소년인 양 로리를 거의 완벽하게 이해하는 것 같았다."]], [["If the Laurences had been what Jo called \"prim and poky,\" she would not have got on at all, for such people always made her shy and awkward; but finding them free and easy, she was so herself, and made a good impression. When they rose she proposed to go, but Laurie said he had something more to show her, and took her away to the conservatory, which had been lighted for her benefit. It seemed quite fairylike to Jo, as she went up and down the walks, enjoying the blooming walls on either side, the soft light, the damp sweet air, and the wonderful vines and trees that hung above her,--while her new friend cut the finest flowers till his hands were full; then he tied them up, saying, with the happy look Jo liked to see, \"Please give these to your mother, and tell her I like the medicine she sent me very much.\"", "만약 로런스 집안이 조가 말하는 \"점잔 떨고 답답한\" 부류였다면 조는 영 어울리지 못했을 것이다. 그런 사람들 앞에선 늘 수줍고 어색해졌으니까. 하지만 그들이 격의 없고 편안하다는 걸 알자 조도 그렇게 굴었고, 좋은 인상을 남겼다. 모두 자리에서 일어났을 때 조는 가 봐야겠다고 했지만, 로리는 보여 줄 게 더 있다며 그녀를 데리고 온실로 갔다. 온실은 그녀를 위해 환히 밝혀져 있었다. 조에게는 그곳이 마치 동화 속처럼 보였다. 길을 오르락내리락하며 양옆으로 활짝 핀 꽃 벽이며 부드러운 빛, 촉촉하고 향긋한 공기, 머리 위에 드리운 신기한 덩굴과 나무들을 즐기는 동안, 새 친구는 두 손 가득 찰 때까지 가장 고운 꽃들을 잘랐다. 그러고는 그것들을 한데 묶으며, 조가 보기 좋아하는 행복한 표정으로 말했다. \"이거 너희 어머니께 드려. 그리고 보내 주신 약이 정말 마음에 든다고 전해 줘.\""]], [["They found Mr. Laurence standing before the fire in the great drawing-room, but Jo's attention was entirely absorbed by a grand piano, which stood open.", "두 사람은 로런스 씨가 커다란 응접실에서 난로 앞에 서 있는 걸 보았지만, 조의 시선은 온통 뚜껑이 열린 채 놓인 그랜드 피아노에 쏠려 있었다."]], [["\"Do you play?\"", "\"너 칠 줄 알아?\""], ["she asked, turning to Laurie with a respectful expression.", "조가 로리를 돌아보며 존경 어린 표정으로 물었다."]], [["\"Sometimes,\"", "\"가끔.\""], ["he answered modestly.", "그가 겸손하게 대답했다."]], [["\"Please do now.", "\"지금 좀 쳐 봐."], ["I want to hear it, so I can tell Beth.\"", "듣고 싶어. 그래야 베스한테 얘기해 줄 수 있거든.\""]], [["\"Won't you first?\"", "\"네가 먼저 치지 않을래?\""]], [["\"Don't know how; too stupid to learn, but I love music dearly.\"", "\"칠 줄 몰라. 배우기엔 너무 둔하거든. 그래도 음악은 정말 좋아해.\""]], [["So Laurie played, and Jo listened, with her nose luxuriously buried in heliotrope and tea-roses.", "그래서 로리가 연주했고, 조는 헬리오트로프와 티로즈에 코를 호사스럽게 파묻은 채 귀를 기울였다."], ["Her respect and regard for the \"Laurence boy\"", "\"로런스네 소년\"에 대한 그녀의 존경과 호감은 부쩍 커졌다."], ["increased very much, for he played remarkably well, and didn't put on any airs.", "그가 놀랄 만큼 연주를 잘하면서도 전혀 거드름을 피우지 않았기 때문이다."], ["She wished Beth could hear him, but she did not say so; only praised him till he was quite abashed, and his grandfather came to the rescue.", "베스도 이걸 들을 수 있다면 좋겠다 싶었지만, 그 말은 하지 않았다. 그저 그가 무척 멋쩍어할 만큼 칭찬을 늘어놓는 통에 할아버지가 구해 주러 나섰다."], ["\"That will do, that will do, young lady.", "\"그만, 그만하면 됐다, 아가씨."], ["Too many sugar-plums are not good for him.", "사탕을 너무 많이 주면 그 녀석한테 좋지 않아."], ["His music isn't bad, but I hope he will do as well in more important things.", "음악 솜씨가 나쁘진 않다만, 더 중요한 일들도 그만큼 잘해 줬으면 좋겠구나."], ["Going?", "가려고?"], ["Well, I'm much obliged to you, and I hope you'll come again.", "그래, 정말 고마웠다. 또 와 주려무나."], ["My respects to your mother.", "너희 어머니께 안부 전해 다오."], ["Good-night, Doctor Jo.\"", "잘 가거라, 의사 선생 조.\""]], [["He shook hands kindly, but looked as if something did not please him.", "그는 다정하게 악수했지만, 무언가 마음에 들지 않는 듯한 표정이었다."], ["When they got into the hall, Jo asked Laurie if she had said anything amiss.", "현관에 이르자 조는 로리에게 자기가 무슨 잘못된 말을 했느냐고 물었다."], ["He shook his head.", "로리는 고개를 저었다."]], [["\"No, it was me; he doesn't like to hear me play.\"", "\"아니야, 나 때문이야. 할아버지는 내가 피아노 치는 걸 듣기 싫어하셔.\""]], [["\"Why not?\"", "\"왜?\""]], [["\"I'll tell you some day.", "\"언젠가 얘기해 줄게."], ["John is going home with you, as I can't.\"", "내가 못 데려다주니까 존이 너랑 같이 갈 거야.\""]], [["\"No need of that; I am not a young lady, and it's only a step.", "\"그럴 필요 없어. 나는 아가씨도 아니고, 코앞이잖아."], ["Take care of yourself, won't you?\"", "몸조심해, 알았지?\""]], [["\"Yes; but you will come again, I hope?\"", "\"응. 그런데 또 와 줄 거지?\""]], [["\"If you promise to come and see us after you are well.\"", "\"네가 다 나은 뒤에 우리 집에 놀러 오겠다고 약속하면.\""]], [["\"I will.\"", "\"갈게.\""]], [["\"Good-night, Laurie!\"", "\"잘 자, 로리!\""]], [["\"Good-night, Jo, good-night!\"", "\"잘 자, 조, 잘 자!\""]], [["When all the afternoon's adventures had been told, the family felt inclined to go visiting in a body, for each found something very attractive in the big house on the other side of the hedge.", "그날 오후의 모험담을 다 전해 듣고 나자, 식구들은 다 함께 그 집에 놀러 가고 싶은 마음이 일었다. 산울타리 건너편 그 큰 집에서 저마다 무척 끌리는 무언가를 찾아냈기 때문이다."], ["Mrs. March wanted to talk of her father with the old man who had not forgotten him; Meg longed to walk in the conservatory; Beth sighed for the grand piano; and Amy was eager to see the fine pictures and statues.", "마치 부인은 자기 아버지를 잊지 않고 있는 그 노인과 아버지 이야기를 나누고 싶었고, 메그는 온실을 거닐고 싶어 안달이었으며, 베스는 그랜드 피아노를 생각하며 한숨을 쉬었고, 에이미는 멋진 그림과 조각상을 보고 싶어 했다."]], [["\"Mother, why didn't Mr. Laurence like to have Laurie play?\"", "\"엄마, 로런스 씨는 왜 로리가 피아노 치는 걸 싫어하셨을까요?\""], ["asked Jo, who was of an inquiring disposition.", "캐묻기 좋아하는 성미의 조가 물었다."]], [["\"I am not sure, but I think it was because his son, Laurie's father, married an Italian lady, a musician, which displeased the old man, who is very proud.", "\"확실하진 않다만, 아마 그분 아들, 그러니까 로리의 아버지가 이탈리아 출신 음악가 여인과 결혼한 게 마음에 안 드셨던 모양이다. 워낙 자존심이 강한 분이시니까."], ["The lady was good and lovely and accomplished, but he did not like her, and never saw his son after he married.", "그 부인은 선하고 사랑스럽고 재능도 많았지만, 그분은 그 며느리가 마음에 들지 않으셨고, 아들이 결혼한 뒤로는 다시는 아들을 보지 않으셨단다."], ["They both died when Laurie was a little child, and then his grandfather took him home.", "로리가 어린아이였을 때 두 분 다 세상을 떠났고, 그래서 할아버지가 그 애를 데려오신 거야."], ["I fancy the boy, who was born in Italy, is not very strong, and the old man is afraid of losing him, which makes him so careful.", "이탈리아에서 태어난 그 애가 그리 튼튼하진 못한 모양인데, 노인은 그 애마저 잃을까 두려워하시니 그렇게 애지중지하시는 거지. 로리가 음악을 좋아하는 건 어머니를 닮아 타고난 거란다."], ["Laurie comes naturally by his love of music, for he is like his mother, and I dare say his grandfather fears that he may want to be a musician; at any rate, his skill reminds him of the woman he did not like, and so he 'glowered,'", "짐작건대 할아버지는 그 애가 음악가가 되고 싶어 할까 봐 걱정하시는 것 같아. 어쨌든 그 애의 솜씨가 마음에 들지 않던 그 여인을 떠올리게 하니까, 조 말마따나 '얼굴을 찌푸리신'"], ["as Jo said.\"", "거지.\""]], [["\"Dear me, how romantic!\"", "\"어머, 어쩜 그렇게 낭만적일까!\""], ["exclaimed Meg.", "메그가 탄성을 질렀다."]], [["\"How silly!\"", "\"어쩜 그렇게 어리석을까!\""], ["said Jo.", "조가 말했다."], ["\"Let him be a musician, if he wants to, and not plague his life out sending him to college, when he hates to go.\"", "\"음악가가 되고 싶다면 되게 해 주면 되잖아. 가기도 싫어하는 애를 대학에 보내려고 들들 볶을 게 아니라.\""]], [["\"That's why he has such handsome black eyes and pretty manners, I suppose.", "\"그래서 그 애가 그렇게 멋진 검은 눈에 고운 매너를 지녔나 봐."], ["Italians are always nice,\"", "이탈리아 사람들은 늘 멋지잖아.\""], ["said Meg, who was a little sentimental.", "약간 감상적인 데가 있는 메그가 말했다."]], [["\"What do you know about his eyes and his manners?", "\"언니가 그 애 눈하고 매너에 대해 뭘 안다고 그래?"], ["You never spoke to him, hardly,\"", "말도 거의 안 해 봤으면서.\""], ["cried Jo, who was _not_ sentimental.", "감상적이지 않은 조가 소리쳤다."]], [["\"I saw him at the party, and what you tell shows that he knows how to behave.", "\"파티에서 봤는걸. 게다가 네가 들려준 얘기를 봐도 그 애가 처신할 줄 안다는 게 드러나잖아."], ["That was a nice little speech about the medicine mother sent him.\"", "엄마가 보낸 약 얘기, 그거 참 정겨운 인사말이었어.\""]], [["\"He meant the blanc-mange, I suppose.\"", "\"블랑망제를 두고 한 말이겠지, 뭐.\""]], [["\"How stupid you are, child!", "\"어쩜 그렇게 둔하니, 얘야!"], ["He meant you, of course.\"", "당연히 너를 두고 한 말이지.\""]], [["\"Did he?\"", "\"그랬어?\""], ["and Jo opened her eyes as if it had never occurred to her before.", "조는 그런 생각은 한 번도 해 본 적 없다는 듯 눈을 동그랗게 떴다."]], [["\"I never saw such a girl!", "\"너 같은 애는 처음 본다!"], ["You don't know a compliment when you get it,\"", "칭찬을 받고도 그게 칭찬인 줄 모르다니.\""], ["said Meg, with the air of a young lady who knew all about the matter.", "메그가 그런 일이라면 죄다 안다는 듯 다 큰 아가씨 같은 태도로 말했다."]], [["\"I think they are great nonsense, and I'll thank you not to be silly, and spoil my fun. Laurie's a nice boy, and I like him, and I won't have any sentimental stuff about compliments and such rubbish. We'll all be good to him, because he hasn't got any mother, and he _may_ come over and see us, mayn't he, Marmee?\"", "\"난 그런 거 다 쓸데없는 소리라고 봐. 그러니까 언니도 괜히 호들갑 떨면서 내 재미를 망치지 말아 줬으면 좋겠어. 로리는 좋은 애고, 나도 그 애가 마음에 들어. 그렇지만 칭찬이니 뭐니 하는 그런 감상적인 소리는 사양이야. 우리 다 그 애한테 잘해 주자. 엄마가 없는 애잖아. 그리고 그 애가 우리 집에 놀러 와도 되죠, 그렇죠, 엄마?\""]], [["\"Yes, Jo, your little friend is very welcome, and I hope Meg will remember that children should be children as long as they can.\"", "\"그럼, 조, 네 친구를 두 팔 벌려 환영하마. 그리고 메그도 아이들은 그럴 수 있는 동안엔 아이답게 지내야 한다는 걸 기억했으면 좋겠구나.\""]], [["\"I don't call myself a child, and I'm not in my teens yet,\"", "\"전 제가 어린애라고 생각 안 해요. 아직 십 대도 안 됐고요.\""], ["observed Amy.", "에이미가 한마디 했다."], ["\"What do you say, Beth?\"", "\"넌 어떻게 생각해, 베스?\""]], [["\"I was thinking about our 'Pilgrim's Progress,'\"", "\"난 우리 '천로역정' 생각을 하고 있었어.\""], ["answered Beth, who had not heard a word.", "한마디도 듣지 못한 베스가 대답했다."], ["\"How we got out of the Slough and through the Wicket Gate by resolving to be good, and up the steep hill by trying; and that maybe the house over there, full of splendid things, is going to be our Palace Beautiful.\"", "\"착해지기로 결심해서 우리가 어떻게 늪에서 빠져나와 좁은 문을 지났는지, 또 애를 써서 어떻게 가파른 언덕을 올라갔는지 말이야. 그리고 어쩌면 멋진 것들로 가득한 저 건너편 집이 우리의 아름다운 궁전이 될지도 모른다고.\""]], [["\"We have got to get by the lions, first,\"", "\"먼저 사자들부터 지나가야 해.\""], ["said Jo, as if she rather liked the prospect.", "조가 그 일을 은근히 반기는 듯한 투로 말했다."]], [["# BETH FINDS THE PALACE BEAUTIFUL.", "# BETH FINDS THE PALACE BEAUTIFUL."]], [["The big house did prove a Palace Beautiful, though it took some time for all to get in, and Beth found it very hard to pass the lions.", "큰 집은 정말 아름다운 궁전임이 드러났다. 모두가 그 안에 들어가기까지는 얼마간 시간이 걸렸고, 특히 베스는 사자들을 지나가기가 무척 힘겨웠지만 말이다."], ["Old Mr. Laurence was the biggest one; but after he had called, said something funny or kind to each one of the girls, and talked over old times with their mother, nobody felt much afraid of him, except timid Beth.", "늙은 로런스 씨가 가장 큰 사자였다."], ["The other lion was the fact that they were poor and Laurie rich; for this made them shy of accepting favors which they could not return.", "하지만 그가 한번 찾아와 소녀들 하나하나에게 재미있거나 다정한 말을 건네고, 그들의 어머니와 옛이야기를 나눈 뒤로는, 겁 많은 베스를 빼고는 아무도 그를 그리 무서워하지 않게 되었다."], ["But, after a while, they found that he considered them the benefactors, and could not do enough to show how grateful he was for Mrs.", "또 다른 사자는 자기들은 가난하고 로리는 부자라는 사실이었다. 이 때문에 보답할 수 없는 호의를 받기가 꺼려졌던 것이다."], ["March's motherly welcome, their cheerful society, and the comfort he took in that humble home of theirs.", "하지만 얼마 지나지 않아, 그들은 오히려 로리가 자기들을 은인으로 여기고 있으며, 마치 부인의 어머니 같은 환대와 그들의 명랑한 어울림, 그리고 그가 그 소박한 집에서 느낀 위안에 얼마나 고마워하는지를 보여 주려고 무엇이든 다 해 주려 한다는 걸 알게 되었다."], ["So they soon forgot their pride, and interchanged kindnesses without stopping to think which was the greater.", "그래서 그들은 곧 자존심을 잊고, 어느 쪽이 더 큰지 따지지도 않은 채 서로 친절을 주고받았다."]], [["All sorts of pleasant things happened about that time; for the new friendship flourished like grass in spring.", "그 무렵엔 온갖 즐거운 일이 일어났다. 새로 싹튼 우정이 봄날의 풀처럼 무성하게 자라났기 때문이다."], ["Every one liked Laurie, and he privately informed his tutor that \"the Marches were regularly splendid girls.\"", "다들 로리를 좋아했고, 로리는 가정교사에게 \"마치 자매들은 정말이지 더할 나위 없이 멋진 아가씨들\"이라고 슬쩍 일러바쳤다."], ["With the delightful enthusiasm of youth, they took the solitary boy into their midst, and made much of him, and he found something very charming in the innocent companionship of these simple-hearted girls.", "젊은이다운 흐뭇한 열정으로 그들은 외로운 소년을 자기들 한가운데로 받아들여 무척 아껴 주었고, 로리는 그 순진한 소녀들과의 천진한 어울림에서 더없이 매력적인 무언가를 찾아냈다."], ["Never having known mother or sisters, he was quick to feel the influences they brought about him; and their busy, lively ways made him ashamed of the indolent life he led.", "어머니도 누이도 가져 본 적이 없던 그는 그들이 자기 주위에 가져다주는 영향을 금세 느꼈다."], ["He was tired of books, and found people so interesting now that Mr.", "게다가 그들의 분주하고 활기찬 생활은 자기가 보내 온 게으른 삶을 부끄럽게 만들었다."], ["Brooke was obliged to make very unsatisfactory reports; for Laurie was always playing truant, and running over to the Marches.", "그는 책에 싫증이 났고, 이제는 사람들이 너무 흥미로워서 브룩 선생은 영 시원찮은 보고서를 올릴 수밖에 없었다. 로리가 늘 수업을 빼먹고 마치 집으로 달려갔기 때문이다."]], [["\"Never mind; let him take a holiday, and make it up afterwards,\"", "\"괜찮네. 휴일을 좀 갖게 두고, 나중에 만회하게 하지.\""], ["said the old gentleman.", "노신사가 말했다."], ["\"The good lady next door says he is studying too hard, and needs young society, amusement, and exercise.", "\"옆집 그 착한 부인 말이, 그 녀석이 공부를 너무 무리하게 해서 또래들과 어울리고, 놀고, 운동을 할 필요가 있다는군."], ["I suspect she is right, and that I've been coddling the fellow as if I'd been his grandmother.", "내 생각에도 그 말이 맞는 것 같아. 그동안 내가 마치 그 애 할미라도 되는 양 그 녀석을 너무 싸고돌았던 게지."], ["Let him do what he likes, as long as he is happy.", "행복하기만 하다면 하고 싶은 대로 하게 두세."], ["He can't get into mischief in that little nunnery over there; and Mrs. March is doing more for him than we can.\"", "저 건너편 작은 수녀원 같은 집에서야 못된 짓에 빠질 리 없고, 또 마치 부인이 우리가 해 줄 수 있는 것보다 더 많은 걸 그 애한테 해 주고 있으니 말일세.\""]], [["What good times they had, to be sure!", "정말이지 얼마나 즐거운 시절이었던가!"], ["Such plays and tableaux, such sleigh-rides and skating frolics, such pleasant evenings in the old parlor, and now and then such gay little parties at the great house.", "그런 연극과 활인화, 그런 썰매 타기와 신나는 스케이트 놀이, 낡은 응접실에서의 그토록 즐거운 저녁 시간, 그리고 이따금 큰 집에서 열리는 그런 흥겨운 작은 모임들."], ["Meg could walk in the conservatory whenever she liked, and revel in bouquets; Jo browsed over the new library voraciously, and convulsed the old gentleman with her criticisms; Amy copied pictures, and enjoyed beauty to her heart's content; and Laurie played \"lord of the manor\" in the most delightful style.", "메그는 마음 내킬 때면 언제든 온실을 거닐며 꽃다발 속에서 호사를 누렸고, 조는 새 서재를 게걸스럽게 누비며 자기 평론으로 노신사를 배꼽 잡게 했으며, 에이미는 그림을 모사하며 마음껏 아름다움을 즐겼고, 로리는 더없이 즐거운 모습으로 \"영주 노릇\"을 했다."]], [["But Beth, though yearning for the grand piano, could not pluck up courage to go to the \"Mansion of Bliss,\" as Meg called it.", "하지만 베스는 그랜드 피아노를 그토록 그리워하면서도, 메그가 \"행복의 저택\"이라 부르는 그곳에 갈 용기를 좀처럼 내지 못했다."], ["She went once with Jo; but the old gentleman, not being aware of her infirmity, stared at her so hard from under his heavy eyebrows, and said \"Hey!\"", "한번은 조와 함께 갔지만, 노신사가 그녀의 약한 성정을 모른 채 짙은 눈썹 아래로 어찌나 빤히 쳐다보며 어찌나 큰 소리로 \"응!\""], ["so loud, that he frightened her so much her \"feet chattered on the floor,\"", "하고 외쳤던지, 베스가 그 바람에 너무 겁에 질려 어머니에게 한 말로는 \"발이 마룻바닥에서 달가닥거렸다\"고 했다."], ["she told her mother; and she ran away, declaring she would never go there any more, not even for the dear piano.", "그녀는 달아나며 다시는 그곳에 가지 않겠다고, 그 사랑하는 피아노를 위해서라도 안 가겠다고 선언했다."], ["No persuasions or enticements could overcome her fear, till, the fact coming to Mr. Laurence's ear in some mysterious way, he set about mending matters.", "어떤 설득이나 꾐도 그녀의 두려움을 이기지 못했는데, 이 사실이 무슨 영문인지 로런스 씨의 귀에 들어가자 그가 일을 바로잡으려고 나섰다."], ["During one of the brief calls he made, he artfully led the conversation to music, and talked away about great singers whom he had seen, fine organs he had heard, and told such charming anecdotes that Beth found it impossible to stay in her distant corner, but crept nearer and nearer, as if fascinated.", "그는 짧게 들른 어느 날, 교묘하게 화제를 음악으로 끌고 가, 자기가 본 위대한 가수들이며 들어 본 훌륭한 오르간 이야기를 늘어놓고, 어찌나 매력적인 일화들을 들려주었던지, 베스는 도저히 저 멀리 구석에 머물러 있을 수가 없어 마치 홀린 듯 점점 더 가까이 다가왔다."], ["At the back of his chair she stopped, and stood listening, with her great eyes wide open, and her cheeks red with the excitement of this unusual performance.", "그러고는 그의 의자 등받이 뒤에서 멈춰 서서, 커다란 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 이 색다른 이야기의 흥분으로 뺨이 발그레해진 채 귀를 기울였다."], ["Taking no more notice of her than if she had been a fly, Mr.", "로런스 씨는 그녀가 마치 파리라도 되는 양 더 이상 아랑곳하지 않고, 로리의 수업과 선생들에 대해 이야기를 이어 갔다."], ["Laurence talked on about Laurie's lessons and teachers; and presently, as if the idea had just occurred to him, he said to Mrs. March,--", "그러다 이제 막 떠오른 생각인 양 마치 부인에게 말했다."]], [["\"The boy neglects his music now, and I'm glad of it, for he was getting too fond of it.", "\"그 녀석이 요즘 음악을 소홀히 하는데, 나야 다행이다 싶소. 너무 빠져들고 있었으니 말이오."], ["But the piano suffers for want of use.", "그런데 피아노가 안 쓰여서 영 망가지고 있단 말이지."], ["Wouldn't some of your girls like to run over, and practise on it now and then, just to keep it in tune, you know, ma'am?\"", "댁의 따님들 가운데 누가 가끔 건너와서 한 번씩 쳐 주면 어떻겠소? 그저 음을 맞춰 두려고 말이오, 부인.\""]], [["Beth took a step forward, and pressed her hands tightly together to keep from clapping them, for this was an irresistible temptation; and the thought of practising on that splendid instrument quite took her breath away.", "베스는 한 걸음 앞으로 나서며 손뼉을 치지 않으려고 두 손을 꼭 맞잡았다. 도저히 뿌리칠 수 없는 유혹이었기 때문이다."], ["Before Mrs. March could reply, Mr.", "그 훌륭한 악기로 연습한다는 생각만으로도 숨이 멎을 것 같았다."], ["Laurence went on with an odd little nod and smile,--", "마치 부인이 미처 대답하기도 전에, 로런스 씨가 묘하게 고개를 끄덕이고 미소 지으며 말을 이었다."]], [["\"They needn't see or speak to any one, but run in at any time; for I'm shut up in my study at the other end of the house, Laurie is out a great deal, and the servants are never near the drawing-room after nine o'clock.\"", "\"누구를 보거나 말을 걸 필요도 없소. 그저 아무 때나 들어와 치면 되오. 나는 집 저쪽 끝 서재에 틀어박혀 있고, 로리는 밖에 나가 있는 때가 많고, 하인들은 아홉 시가 지나면 응접실 근처엔 얼씬도 안 하니까.\""]], [["Here he rose, as if going, and Beth made up her mind to speak, for that last arrangement left nothing to be desired.", "여기서 그가 가려는 듯 자리에서 일어나자, 베스는 말을 하기로 마음먹었다. 그 마지막 조건이 더할 나위 없었기 때문이다."], ["\"Please tell the young ladies what I say; and if they don't care to come, why, never mind.\"", "\"내가 한 말을 따님들한테 전해 주시오. 만약 오고 싶지 않다면야, 뭐, 어쩔 수 없고.\""], ["Here a little hand slipped into his, and Beth looked up at him with a face full of gratitude, as she said, in her earnest yet timid way,--", "이때 작은 손 하나가 그의 손 안으로 슬며시 미끄러져 들어왔고, 베스는 고마움이 가득한 얼굴로 그를 올려다보며 진지하면서도 수줍은 투로 말했다."]], [["\"O sir, they do care, very, very much!\"", "\"오, 할아버지, 따님들 정말, 정말 많이 가고 싶어 해요!\""]], [["\"Are you the musical girl?\"", "\"네가 그 음악 좋아하는 아이냐?\""], ["he asked, without any startling \"Hey!\"", "그가 깜짝 놀랄 만한 \"응!\""], ["as he looked down at her very kindly.", "소리도 없이, 무척 인자하게 그녀를 내려다보며 물었다."]], [["\"I'm Beth.", "\"전 베스예요."], ["I love it dearly, and I'll come, if you are quite sure nobody will hear me--and be disturbed,\"", "음악을 정말 좋아해요. 아무도 제 소리를 안 듣고…"], ["she added, fearing to be rude, and trembling at her own boldness as she spoke.", "방해받지 않을 거라고 확실히 약속해 주신다면, 갈게요.\" 그녀는 무례할까 봐 걱정스러운 마음에 그렇게 덧붙였고, 말을 하면서 자신의 대담함에 스스로 떨었다."]], [["\"Not a soul, my dear.", "\"한 사람도 안 듣는단다, 얘야. 하루의 절반은 집이 텅 비어 있으니까."], ["The house is empty half the day; so come, and drum away as much as you like, and I shall be obliged to you.\"", "그러니 와서 마음껏 쳐 다오. 그러면 내가 오히려 고맙겠구나.\""]], [["\"How kind you are, sir!\"", "\"정말 친절하세요, 할아버지!\""]], [["Beth blushed like a rose under the friendly look he wore; but she was not frightened now, and gave the big hand a grateful squeeze, because she had no words to thank him for the precious gift he had given her.", "베스는 그가 짓는 다정한 표정에 장미처럼 얼굴을 붉혔다. 하지만 이제는 무섭지 않았고, 그 큰 손을 고마운 마음으로 꼭 쥐었다."], ["The old gentleman softly stroked the hair off her forehead, and, stooping down, he kissed her, saying, in a tone few people ever heard,--", "그가 자기에게 준 그 소중한 선물에 고마움을 표할 말을 도무지 찾을 수 없었기 때문이다. 노신사는 그녀의 이마에서 머리카락을 부드럽게 쓸어 올리고는, 몸을 굽혀 입을 맞추며, 좀처럼 사람들이 들어 본 적 없는 어조로 말했다."]], [["\"I had a little girl once, with eyes like these.", "\"내게도 한때 이런 눈을 가진 어린 딸이 있었단다."], ["God bless you, my dear!", "하느님의 축복이 함께하기를, 얘야!"], ["Good day, madam;\"", "그럼 안녕히 계시오, 부인.\""], ["and away he went, in a great hurry.", "그러고서 그는 몹시 서두르며 떠났다."]], [["Beth had a rapture with her mother, and then rushed up to impart the glorious news to her family of invalids, as the girls were not at home.", "베스는 어머니와 함께 한껏 기쁨에 젖었다가, 자매들이 집에 없었기에 자기의 병약한 식구들에게 그 영광스러운 소식을 알리러 위층으로 달려갔다."], ["How blithely she sung that evening, and how they all laughed at her, because she woke Amy in the night by playing the piano on her face in her sleep.", "그날 저녁 그녀는 어찌나 명랑하게 노래를 불렀던지. 그리고 한밤중에 잠결에 에이미의 얼굴 위에서 피아노 치는 시늉을 하다가 에이미를 깨우는 바람에 모두가 그녀를 두고 한바탕 웃었다."], ["Next day, having seen both the old and young gentleman out of the house, Beth, after two or three retreats, fairly got in at the side-door, and made her way, as noiselessly as any mouse, to the drawing-room, where her idol stood.", "이튿날, 노신사와 젊은 신사가 둘 다 집을 나서는 걸 확인한 베스는, 두세 번 물러섰다 하더니, 마침내 옆문으로 들어가, 생쥐처럼 소리 없이 자기 우상이 서 있는 응접실로 향했다. 물론 순전히 우연이겠지만, 피아노 위에는 예쁘고 쉬운 악보가 놓여 있었다."], ["Quite by accident, of course, some pretty, easy music lay on the piano; and, with trembling fingers, and frequent stops to listen and look about, Beth at last touched the great instrument, and straightway forgot her fear, herself, and everything else but the unspeakable delight which the music gave her, for it was like the voice of a beloved friend.", "떨리는 손가락으로, 귀를 기울이고 주위를 살피느라 자꾸만 멈추면서, 베스는 마침내 그 훌륭한 악기에 손을 댔고, 그 순간 두려움도, 자기 자신도, 그 음악이 안겨 주는 형언할 수 없는 기쁨 말고는 모든 것을 잊어버렸다. 그것은 사랑하는 친구의 목소리 같았으니까."]], [["She stayed till Hannah came to take her home to dinner; but she had no appetite, and could only sit and smile upon every one in a general state of beatitude.", "베스는 해나가 점심을 먹으러 데리러 올 때까지 거기 머물렀다. 하지만 입맛이 없어, 그저 앉아서 더없는 행복에 잠긴 채 모두를 향해 미소만 지을 뿐이었다."]], [["After that, the little brown hood slipped through the hedge nearly every day, and the great drawing-room was haunted by a tuneful spirit that came and went unseen. She never knew that Mr.", "그 뒤로 그 작은 갈색 모자는 거의 날마다 산울타리 사이를 빠져나갔고, 커다란 응접실에는 보이지 않게 왔다 가는 가락의 정령이 깃들었다."], ["Laurence often opened his study-door to hear the old-fashioned airs he liked; she never saw Laurie mount guard in the hall to warn the servants away; she never suspected that the exercise-books and new songs which she found in the rack were put there for her especial benefit; and when he talked to her about music at home, she only thought how kind he was to tell things that helped her so much.", "베스는 로런스 씨가 자기가 좋아하는 옛 가락을 들으려고 종종 서재 문을 열어 두는 줄 몰랐고, 로리가 하인들을 물리려고 현관에서 보초를 서는 것도 보지 못했으며, 악보꽂이에서 발견한 연습 교본과 새 노래책이 오로지 자기를 위해 거기 놓였다는 것도 짐작조차 못 했다. 그리고 그가 자기 집에서 음악 이야기를 들려줄 때면, 그저 자기에게 그토록 도움이 되는 이야기를 들려주시다니 참 친절하시다고만 생각했다."], ["So she enjoyed herself heartily, and found, what isn't always the case, that her granted wish was all she had hoped.", "그래서 베스는 진심으로 즐거워했고, 늘 그런 것만은 아닌 일, 곧 이루어진 소원이 바라던 그대로였다는 사실을 깨달았다."], ["Perhaps it was because she was so grateful for this blessing that a greater was given her; at any rate, she deserved both.", "어쩌면 이 축복에 그토록 감사했기에 더 큰 축복이 주어졌는지도 모른다. 어쨌든 그녀는 둘 다 받을 자격이 있었다."]], [["\"Mother, I'm going to work Mr. Laurence a pair of slippers.", "\"엄마, 저 로런스 할아버지께 슬리퍼 한 켤레를 떠 드리려고요."], ["He is so kind to me, I must thank him, and I don't know any other way.", "저한테 너무 친절하셔서 고마운 마음을 전해야 하는데, 다른 방법을 모르겠어요."], ["Can I do it?\"", "그래도 될까요?\""], ["asked Beth, a few weeks after that eventful call of his.", "그 뜻깊은 방문이 있고 몇 주 뒤, 베스가 물었다."]], [["\"Yes, dear.", "\"그럼, 얘야."], ["It will please him very much, and be a nice way of thanking him.", "무척 기뻐하실 거고, 고마움을 전하는 좋은 방법이지."], ["The girls will help you about them, and I will pay for the making up,\"", "언니들이 도와줄 거고, 마무리하는 데 드는 값은 내가 내주마.\" 마치 부인이 대답했다."], ["replied Mrs. March, who took peculiar pleasure in granting Beth's requests, because she so seldom asked anything for herself.", "베스가 자기 자신을 위해선 좀처럼 무얼 부탁하는 법이 없었기에, 부인은 베스의 부탁을 들어주는 일에서 각별한 기쁨을 느꼈다."]], [["After many serious discussions with Meg and Jo, the pattern was chosen, the materials bought, and the slippers begun.", "메그, 조와 여러 번 진지하게 의논한 끝에 무늬가 정해졌고, 재료를 사 왔으며, 슬리퍼 뜨기가 시작되었다."], ["A cluster of grave yet cheerful pansies, on a deeper purple ground, was pronounced very appropriate and pretty; and Beth worked away early and late, with occasional lifts over hard parts.", "좀 더 짙은 보라색 바탕에 진지하면서도 명랑한 팬지꽃을 모아 놓은 무늬가 무척 어울리고 예쁘다는 평을 받았다. 베스는 어려운 부분에서 가끔 도움을 받아 가며 아침저녁으로 부지런히 떴다."], ["She was a nimble little needle-woman, and they were finished before any one got tired of them.", "손놀림이 야무진 작은 바느질꾼이라, 누구도 싫증이 나기 전에 슬리퍼가 완성되었다."], ["Then she wrote a very short, simple note, and, with Laurie's help, got them smuggled on to the study-table one morning before the old gentleman was up.", "그런 다음 그녀는 아주 짧고 소박한 편지를 써서, 로리의 도움을 받아 어느 날 아침 노신사가 일어나기 전에 서재 탁자 위로 몰래 갖다 놓았다."]], [["When this excitement was over, Beth waited to see what would happen.", "이 흥분이 가라앉자, 베스는 무슨 일이 일어날지 기다렸다."], ["All that day passed, and a part of the next, before any acknowledgment arrived, and she was beginning to fear she had offended her crotchety friend.", "그날 하루가 다 가고 이튿날도 한나절이 지나도록 아무런 답례가 오지 않자, 그녀는 자기가 그 별난 친구의 기분을 상하게 한 건 아닌지 걱정되기 시작했다."], ["On the afternoon of the second day, she went out to do an errand, and give poor Joanna, the invalid doll, her daily exercise.", "이튿날 오후, 그녀는 심부름도 하고 병약한 인형 조애나에게 매일의 산책도 시켜 줄 겸 밖으로 나갔다."], ["As she came up the street, on her return, she saw three, yes, four, heads popping in and out of the parlor windows, and the moment they saw her, several hands were waved, and several joyful voices screamed,--", "돌아오는 길에 거리를 올라오던 그녀는 응접실 창문으로 머리 세 개가, 아니 네 개가 들락날락하는 것을 보았다. 그들이 그녀를 보자마자 여러 손이 흔들리고 여러 명랑한 목소리가 외쳐 댔다."]], [["\"Here's a letter from the old gentleman!", "\"할아버지한테서 편지가 왔어!"], ["Come quick, and read it!\"", "빨리 와서 읽어 봐!\""]], [["\"O Beth, he's sent you--\"", "\"오, 베스, 할아버지가 너한테 보내신 게—\""], ["began Amy, gesticulating with unseemly energy; but she got no further, for Jo quenched her by slamming down the window.", "에이미가 점잖지 못할 만큼 격하게 손짓하며 입을 열었다. 하지만 더 잇지 못했으니, 조가 창문을 쾅 닫아 그녀를 입막음했기 때문이다."], ["Beth hurried on in a flutter of suspense.", "베스는 마음 졸이며 두근거리는 가슴으로 서둘러 들어갔다."], ["At the door, her sisters seized and bore her to the parlor in a triumphal procession, all pointing, and all saying at once, \"Look there!", "문 앞에서 언니들이 그녀를 붙잡아 의기양양한 행렬을 이루며 응접실로 데려갔는데, 다들 손가락질을 해 대며 동시에 외쳤다. \"저기 봐!"], ["look there!\"", "저기 봐!\""], ["Beth did look, and turned pale with delight and surprise; for there stood a little cabinet-piano, with a letter lying on the glossy lid, directed, like a sign-board, to \"Miss Elizabeth March.\"", "베스는 정말로 보았고, 기쁨과 놀라움에 새파랗게 질렸다. 거기 작은 카비닛 피아노가 놓여 있었고, 그 윤기 나는 뚜껑 위에는 마치 간판처럼 \"엘리자베스 마치 양에게\"라고 받는 이가 적힌 편지 한 통이 놓여 있었기 때문이다."]], [["\"For me?\"", "\"내 거예요?\""], ["gasped Beth, holding on to Jo, and feeling as if she should tumble down, it was such an overwhelming thing altogether.", "베스가 조에게 매달린 채 헐떡이며 말했다. 도무지 압도되는 일이라 금방이라도 쓰러질 것만 같았다."]], [["\"Yes; all for you, my precious!", "\"그럼, 다 네 거야, 우리 보물!"], ["Isn't it splendid of him?", "할아버지 정말 멋지시지 않니?"], ["Don't you think he's the dearest old man in the world?", "세상에서 제일 다정한 할아버지 같지 않아?"], ["Here's the key in the letter.", "열쇠는 편지 안에 있어."], ["We didn't open it, but we are dying to know what he says,\"", "우린 안 뜯었어. 그래도 할아버지가 뭐라고 쓰셨는지 궁금해 죽겠어.\""], ["cried Jo, hugging her sister, and offering the note.", "조가 동생을 끌어안으며 편지를 내밀었다."]], [["\"You read it!", "\"언니가 읽어 줘!"], ["I can't, I feel so queer!", "난 못 읽겠어, 너무 이상한 기분이야!"], ["Oh, it is too lovely!\"", "아, 너무 사랑스러워!\""], ["and Beth hid her face in Jo's apron, quite upset by her present.", "베스는 선물에 완전히 마음이 흔들려 조의 앞치마에 얼굴을 파묻었다."]], [["Jo opened the paper, and began to laugh, for the first words she saw were,--", "조가 종이를 펼치고는 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 맨 먼저 눈에 들어온 글귀가 이러했기 때문이다. \"마치 양에게:"]], [["\"_Dear Madam_,--\"", "친애하는 부인께,—\""]], [["\"How nice it sounds!", "\"어쩜 그렇게 멋지게 들리니!"], ["I wish some one would write to me so!\"", "누가 나한테도 저렇게 편지를 써 줬으면!\""], ["said Amy, who thought the old-fashioned address very elegant.", "그 옛날식 호칭이 무척 우아하다고 생각한 에이미가 말했다."]], [["\"'I have had many pairs of slippers in my life, but I never had any that suited me so well as yours,'\" continued Jo.", "\"'나는 평생 슬리퍼를 여러 켤레 가져 보았지만, 양의 것만큼 내게 잘 맞는 것은 없었소.'\""], ["\"'Heart's-ease is my favorite flower, and these will always remind me of the gentle giver.", "조가 계속 읽어 나갔다. \"'팬지꽃은 내가 가장 좋아하는 꽃이라오."], ["I like to pay my debts; so I know you will allow \"the old gentleman\"", "이 슬리퍼는 언제나 다정한 선물의 임자를 떠올리게 해 줄 것이오."], ["to send you something which once belonged to the little granddaughter he lost.", "나는 빚 갚기를 좋아하니, \"이 노인\"이 잃은 어린 손녀에게 한때 속해 있던 물건을 양에게 보내는 것을 허락해 주리라 믿소."], ["With hearty thanks and best wishes, I remain,", "진심 어린 감사와 더없는 축복을 담아, 이만 줄이겠소."]], [["\"'Your grateful friend and humble servant,", "\"'양의 고마운 친구이자 보잘것없는 종,"]], [["\"There, Beth, that's an honor to be proud of, I'm sure!", "\"'제임스 로런스.'\" \"자, 베스, 이건 정말 자랑스러워할 만한 영광이야!"], ["Laurie told me how fond Mr. Laurence used to be of the child who died, and how he kept all her little things carefully.", "로런스 할아버지가 돌아가신 그 아이를 얼마나 아끼셨는지, 그 아이 물건을 하나하나 얼마나 소중히 간직해 오셨는지 로리가 얘기해 줬거든."], ["Just think, he's given you her piano.", "생각해 봐, 그 아이 피아노를 너한테 주신 거야."], ["That comes of having big blue eyes and loving music,\"", "큰 파란 눈을 가지고 음악을 사랑한 덕분이지.\""], ["said Jo, trying to soothe Beth, who trembled, and looked more excited than she had ever been before.", "조가 베스를 달래려고 말했다. 베스는 떨고 있었고, 이제껏 본 적 없을 만큼 흥분한 표정이었다."]], [["\"See the cunning brackets to hold candles, and the nice green silk, puckered up, with a gold rose in the middle, and the pretty rack and stool, all complete,\"", "\"양초 꽂는 앙증맞은 받침대 좀 봐. 그리고 가운데 금빛 장미가 달린 채 자글자글 주름 잡힌 고운 초록색 비단이며, 예쁜 악보꽂이에 의자까지, 죄다 갖춰져 있어.\""], ["added Meg, opening the instrument and displaying its beauties.", "메그가 악기를 열어 그 아름다움을 펼쳐 보이며 덧붙였다."]], [["\"'Your humble servant, James Laurence'; only think of his writing that to you.", "\"'양의 보잘것없는 종, 제임스 로런스'라니. 너한테 이렇게 써 주셨다고 생각해 봐."], ["I'll tell the girls.", "학교 애들한테 얘기해야지."], ["They'll think it's splendid,\"", "다들 정말 멋지다고 할 거야.\""], ["said Amy, much impressed by the note.", "그 편지에 무척 감동한 에이미가 말했다."]], [["\"Try it, honey.", "\"한번 쳐 보렴, 아가."], ["Let's hear the sound of the baby-pianny,\"", "우리 아기 피아노 소리 좀 들어 보자꾸나.\""], ["said Hannah, who always took a share in the family joys and sorrows.", "늘 식구들의 기쁨과 슬픔을 함께 나누는 해나가 말했다."]], [["So Beth tried it; and every one pronounced it the most remarkable piano ever heard.", "그래서 베스가 쳐 보았고, 모두 입을 모아 이제껏 들어 본 것 중 가장 놀라운 피아노라고 했다."], ["It had evidently been newly tuned and put in apple-pie order; but, perfect as it was, I think the real charm of it lay in the happiest of all happy faces which leaned over it, as Beth lovingly touched the beautiful black and white keys and pressed the bright pedals.", "새로 조율해서 더할 나위 없이 말끔하게 손봐 둔 게 분명했다. 하지만 그토록 완벽하긴 했어도, 그 진짜 매력은 베스가 사랑스럽게 그 아름다운 흑백 건반을 어루만지고 반짝이는 페달을 밟을 때, 그 위로 몸을 숙인 더없이 행복한 얼굴에 깃들어 있었다고 나는 생각한다."]], [["\"You'll have to go and thank him,\"", "\"가서 고맙다고 인사드려야겠다.\""], ["said Jo, by way of a joke; for the idea of the child's really going never entered her head.", "조가 농담 삼아 말했다. 그 애가 정말로 갈 거라는 생각은 머릿속에 떠오르지도 않았던 것이다."]], [["\"Yes, I mean to.", "\"응, 그럴 생각이야."], ["I guess I'll go now, before I get frightened thinking about it.\"", "생각하다가 겁먹기 전에 지금 가는 게 좋겠어.\""], ["And, to the utter amazement of the assembled family, Beth walked deliberately down the garden, through the hedge, and in at the Laurences' door.", "그러고는 모여 있던 식구들이 깜짝 놀라게도, 베스는 차분하게 정원을 가로질러 산울타리를 지나 로런스네 문 안으로 걸어 들어갔다."]], [["\"Well, I wish I may die if it ain't the queerest thing I ever see!", "\"세상에, 내 평생 본 것 중에 제일 별난 일이 아니면 내가 죽어도 좋아!"], ["The pianny has turned her head!", "피아노가 저 애 정신을 쏙 빼놨네!"], ["She'd never have gone in her right mind,\"", "제정신이라면 저렇게 들어갈 리가 없지.\""], ["cried Hannah, staring after her, while the girls were rendered quite speechless by the miracle.", "해나가 그 뒷모습을 멍하니 바라보며 소리쳤고, 소녀들은 그 기적에 그만 말문이 막혔다."]], [["They would have been still more amazed if they had seen what Beth did afterward.", "베스가 그 뒤에 한 일을 보았더라면 그들은 한층 더 놀랐을 것이다."], ["If you will believe me, she went and knocked at the study-door before she gave herself time to think; and when a gruff voice called out, \"Come in!\"", "정말이지, 베스는 미처 생각할 틈도 주지 않고 서재 문을 두드렸다."], ["she did go in, right up to Mr.", "그리고 굵직한 목소리가 \"들어와요!\""], ["Laurence, who looked quite taken aback, and held out her hand, saying, with only a small quaver in her voice, \"I came to thank you, sir, for--\"", "하고 외치자, 정말로 들어가, 사뭇 당황한 표정의 로런스 씨에게 곧장 다가가 손을 내밀며, 목소리에 약간의 떨림만 담아 말했다. \"고맙다는 인사를 드리러 왔어요, 할아버지, 그—\""], ["But she didn't finish; for he looked so friendly that she forgot her speech, and, only remembering that he had lost the little girl he loved, she put both arms round his neck, and kissed him.", "하지만 끝맺지 못했다. 그가 너무도 다정한 표정을 짓는 바람에 인사말을 잊어버렸고, 그저 그가 사랑하던 어린 딸을 잃었다는 사실만 떠올라, 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안고 입을 맞추었던 것이다."]], [["If the roof of the house had suddenly flown off, the old gentleman wouldn't have been more astonished; but he liked it,--oh, dear, yes, he liked it amazingly!--and was so touched and pleased by that confiding little kiss that all his crustiness vanished; and he just set her on his knee, and laid his wrinkled cheek against her rosy one, feeling as if he had got his own little granddaughter back again. Beth ceased to fear him from that moment, and sat there talking to him as cosily as if she had known him all her life; for love casts out fear, and gratitude can conquer pride. When she went home, he walked with her to her own gate, shook hands cordially, and touched his hat as he marched back again, looking very stately and erect, like a handsome, soldierly old gentleman, as he was.", "집 지붕이 갑자기 날아가 버렸다 해도 노신사가 이보다 더 놀라지는 못했을 것이다. 하지만 그는 그게 좋았다. 오, 정말이지 그렇고말고, 더없이 좋았다! 그 미더운 작은 입맞춤에 어찌나 감동하고 기뻤던지, 그의 무뚝뚝함이 죄다 사라져 버렸다. 그는 그녀를 무릎에 앉히고 주름진 뺨을 그녀의 발그레한 뺨에 갖다 댔는데, 마치 자기 어린 손녀를 다시 되찾은 듯한 기분이었다. 그 순간부터 베스는 그가 더 이상 무섭지 않았고, 마치 평생 알고 지낸 사이처럼 그와 정답게 이야기를 나누며 거기 앉아 있었다. 사랑은 두려움을 몰아내고, 감사는 자존심을 이길 수 있는 법이니까. 베스가 집으로 돌아갈 때, 그는 그녀의 집 대문까지 같이 걸어가 따뜻하게 악수하고는 모자에 손을 얹어 인사한 뒤 다시 돌아갔다. 무척이나 위엄 있고 꼿꼿한 모습이, 본래 그러했듯 잘생기고 군인 같은 노신사다웠다."]], [["When the girls saw that performance, Jo began to dance a jig, by way of expressing her satisfaction; Amy nearly fell out of the window in her surprise; and Meg exclaimed, with uplifted hands, \"Well, I do believe the world is coming to an end!\"", "소녀들이 그 광경을 보자, 조는 만족감을 표하느라 지그춤을 추기 시작했고, 에이미는 너무 놀라 하마터면 창밖으로 떨어질 뻔했으며, 메그는 두 손을 치켜들며 외쳤다. \"어머, 정말이지 세상이 끝나려나 봐!\""]], [["# AMY'S VALLEY OF HUMILIATION.", "# AMY'S VALLEY OF HUMILIATION."]], [["\"That boy is a perfect Cyclops, isn't he?\"", "\"저 애는 완전 키클롭스 같지 않아?\""], ["said Amy, one day, as Laurie clattered by on horseback, with a flourish of his whip as he passed.", "어느 날 로리가 말을 타고 채찍을 한번 휘두르며 요란하게 지나가자 에이미가 말했다."]], [["\"How dare you say so, when he's got both his eyes?", "\"어떻게 그런 말을 하니, 두 눈이 멀쩡한데?"], ["and very handsome ones they are, too,\"", "게다가 아주 잘생긴 눈이라고.\""], ["cried Jo, who resented any slighting remarks about her friend.", "자기 친구를 깔보는 말이라면 발끈하는 조가 소리쳤다."]], [["\"I didn't say anything about his eyes, and I don't see why you need fire up when I admire his riding.\"", "\"난 그 애 눈에 대해선 아무 말도 안 했어. 내가 그 애 말 타는 모습에 감탄하는데 왜 언니가 발끈하는지 모르겠네.\""]], [["\"Oh, my goodness!", "\"어머, 맙소사!"], ["that little goose means a centaur, and she called him a Cyclops,\"", "이 멍청한 애가 켄타우로스를 두고 키클롭스라고 했네.\""], ["exclaimed Jo, with a burst of laughter.", "조가 웃음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다."]], [["\"You needn't be so rude; it's only a 'lapse of lingy,' as Mr.", "\"그렇게 무례하게 굴 거 없잖아."], ["Davis says,\" retorted Amy, finishing Jo with her Latin.", "데이비스 선생님 말마따나 그냥 '말 실순'일 뿐인데.\""], ["\"I just wish I had a little of the money Laurie spends on that horse,\"", "에이미가 라틴어를 들먹이며 조의 말문을 막고는 받아쳤다. \"로리가 저 말한테 쓰는 돈의 조금만이라도 나한테 있었으면 좋겠어.\""], ["she added, as if to herself, yet hoping her sisters would hear.", "그녀가 혼잣말처럼 덧붙였지만, 실은 언니들이 듣기를 바라는 눈치였다."]], [["\"Why?\"", "\"왜?\""], ["asked Meg kindly, for Jo had gone off in another laugh at Amy's second blunder.", "메그가 다정하게 물었다. 조는 에이미의 두 번째 실수에 또 한바탕 웃음을 터뜨리고 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"I need it so much; I'm dreadfully in debt, and it won't be my turn to have the rag-money for a month.\"", "\"그게 너무 필요하거든. 빚이 어마어마한데, 헝겊 모은 돈 받을 차례가 돌아오려면 한 달이나 남았단 말이야.\""]], [["\"In debt, Amy?", "\"빚이라니, 에이미?"], ["What do you mean?\"", "그게 무슨 소리니?\""], ["and Meg looked sober.", "메그가 정색했다."]], [["\"Why, I owe at least a dozen pickled limes, and I can't pay them, you know, till I have money, for Marmee forbade my having anything charged at the shop.\"", "\"있잖아, 절인 라임을 적어도 열두 개나 빚졌는데 갚을 수가 없어. 돈이 생기기 전엔 안 되거든. 엄마가 가게에서 외상으로 사는 건 절대 안 된다고 하셨잖아.\""]], [["\"Tell me all about it.", "\"전부 다 얘기해 봐."], ["Are limes the fashion now?", "요즘은 라임이 유행이니?"], ["It used to be pricking bits of rubber to make balls;\"", "예전엔 고무 조각을 콕콕 찔러서 공 만드는 거였잖아.\""], ["and Meg tried to keep her countenance, Amy looked so grave and important.", "메그는 에이미가 너무도 진지하고 거창한 표정을 짓는 통에 웃음을 참으려고 애썼다."]], [["\"Why, you see, the girls are always buying them, and unless you want to be thought mean, you must do it, too.", "\"있잖아, 애들이 늘 그걸 사거든. 그러니 인색하다는 소리 듣기 싫으면 너도 사야 해."], ["It's nothing but limes now, for every one is sucking them in their desks in school-time, and trading them off for pencils, bead-rings, paper dolls, or something else, at recess.", "요즘은 죄다 라임뿐이야. 다들 수업 시간에 책상 밑에서 라임을 빨아 먹고, 쉬는 시간엔 연필이며 구슬 반지, 종이 인형 같은 걸로 바꾸거든."], ["If one girl likes another, she gives her a lime; if she's mad with her, she eats one before her face, and don't offer even a suck.", "어떤 애가 다른 애를 좋아하면 라임을 주고, 그 애한테 화가 났으면 그 애 앞에서 라임을 먹으면서 한 번 빨아 보라는 말도 안 해. 돌아가면서 대접하는데, 나도 그동안 엄청 많이 얻어먹고선 한 번도 갚질 못했어."], ["They treat by turns; and I've had ever so many, but haven't returned them; and I ought, for they are debts of honor, you know.\"", "그런데 갚아야만 해. 그게 명예가 걸린 빚이거든.\""]], [["\"How much will pay them off, and restore your credit?\"", "\"얼마면 그걸 다 갚고 네 신용을 되찾을 수 있겠니?\""], ["asked Meg, taking out her purse.", "메그가 지갑을 꺼내며 물었다."]], [["\"A quarter would more than do it, and leave a few cents over for a treat for you.", "\"25센트면 갚고도 남아서 언니 줄 거 사 먹을 돈도 몇 센트 남을 거야."], ["Don't you like limes?\"", "언니 라임 안 좋아해?\""]], [["\"Not much; you may have my share.", "\"별로. 내 몫은 네가 가지렴."], ["Here's the money.", "여기 돈 있다."], ["Make it last as long as you can, for it isn't very plenty, you know.\"", "넉넉지 않으니까 최대한 아껴 써.\""]], [["\"Oh, thank you!", "\"어머, 고마워!"], ["It must be so nice to have pocket-money!", "용돈이 있다는 건 정말 좋은 일인가 봐!"], ["I'll have a grand feast, for I haven't tasted a lime this week.", "거하게 잔치를 벌여야지. 이번 주엔 라임 맛도 못 봤거든."], ["I felt delicate about taking any, as I couldn't return them, and I'm actually suffering for one.\"", "갚지를 못하니까 얻어먹기가 영 미안했어. 그래서 정말이지 하나가 간절했단 말이야.\""]], [["Next day Amy was rather late at school; but could not resist the temptation of displaying, with pardonable pride, a moist brown-paper parcel, before she consigned it to the inmost recesses of her desk.", "이튿날 에이미는 학교에 좀 늦었다. 하지만 그 축축한 갈색 종이 꾸러미를 책상 깊숙한 구석에 넣어 두기 전에, 봐줄 만한 자부심을 담아 한번 자랑하고 싶은 유혹을 떨치지 못했다."], ["During the next few minutes the rumor that Amy March had got twenty-four delicious limes (she ate one on the way), and was going to treat, circulated through her \"set,\"", "이후 몇 분 사이에 에이미 마치가 맛있는 라임을 스물네 개나 손에 넣었고(오는 길에 하나 먹어 치웠다) 한턱낼 거라는 소문이 그녀의 \"패거리\""], ["and the attentions of her friends became quite overwhelming.", "사이에 쫙 퍼졌고, 친구들의 관심은 그야말로 쏟아져 들어왔다."], ["Katy Brown invited her to her next party on the spot; Mary Kingsley insisted on lending her her watch till recess; and Jenny Snow, a satirical young lady, who had basely twitted Amy upon her limeless state, promptly buried the hatchet, and offered to furnish answers to certain appalling sums.", "케이티 브라운은 그 자리에서 자기 다음 파티에 에이미를 초대했고, 메리 킹슬리는 쉬는 시간까지 자기 시계를 빌려주겠다고 우겼으며, 라임 없는 에이미의 처지를 비열하게 비아냥대던 빈정거리기 좋아하는 아가씨 제니 스노는 얼른 화해의 손길을 내밀며 끔찍한 산수 문제 몇 개의 답을 알려 주겠다고 했다."], ["But Amy had not forgotten Miss Snow's cutting remarks about \"some persons whose noses were not too flat to smell other people's limes, and stuck-up people, who were not too proud to ask for them;\"", "하지만 에이미는 \"남의 라임 냄새를 맡을 만큼 콧대가 낮지 않은 사람들, 또 그걸 달라고 할 만큼 콧대가 높지 않은 잘난 척하는 사람들\"에 대한 스노 양의 가시 돋친 말을 잊지 않았다."], ["and she instantly crushed \"that Snow girl's\"", "그래서 그녀는 \"갑자기 그렇게 친절한 척할 거 없어."], ["hopes by the withering telegram, \"You needn't be so polite all of a sudden, for you won't get any.\"", "너한텐 하나도 안 줄 테니까\"라는 매서운 통첩으로 \"저 스노라는 애\"의 기대를 단번에 짓밟아 버렸다."]], [["A distinguished personage happened to visit the school that morning, and Amy's beautifully drawn maps received praise, which honor to her foe rankled in the soul of Miss Snow, and caused Miss March to assume the airs of a studious young peacock.", "그날 아침 마침 어떤 귀빈이 학교를 방문했는데, 에이미가 아름답게 그린 지도가 칭찬을 받았다. 자기 원수에게 돌아간 이 영광이 스노 양의 가슴에 사무쳤고, 마치 양은 공부 잘하는 어린 공작새 같은 거드름을 피우게 만들었다."], ["But, alas, alas!", "하지만, 아아, 아아!"], ["pride goes before a fall, and the revengeful Snow turned the tables with disastrous success.", "교만은 패망의 선봉이라, 복수심에 불탄 스노가 처참한 성공을 거두며 형세를 뒤집어 놓았다."], ["No sooner had the guest paid the usual stale compliments, and bowed himself out, than Jenny, under pretence of asking an important question, informed Mr. Davis, the teacher, that Amy March had pickled limes in her desk.", "손님이 으레 하는 진부한 인사치레를 마치고 절을 하며 물러가기가 무섭게, 제니는 중요한 질문을 하는 척하며 교사인 데이비스 선생에게 에이미 마치가 책상 속에 절인 라임을 가지고 있다고 일러바쳤다."]], [["Now Mr. Davis had declared limes a contraband article, and solemnly vowed to publicly ferrule the first person who was found breaking the law.", "당시 데이비스 선생은 라임을 금지 품목으로 선언하고, 그 규칙을 어기다 들킨 첫 번째 사람은 공개적으로 회초리를 치겠다고 엄숙히 맹세한 터였다."], ["This much-enduring man had succeeded in banishing chewing-gum after a long and stormy war, had made a bonfire of the confiscated novels and newspapers, had suppressed a private post-office, had forbidden distortions of the face, nicknames, and caricatures, and done all that one man could do to keep half a hundred rebellious girls in order.", "이 인내심 많은 사내는 길고 격렬한 전쟁 끝에 껌을 몰아내는 데 성공했고, 압수한 소설과 신문을 모닥불에 태웠으며, 비밀 우체국을 폐쇄했고, 얼굴 찌푸리기, 별명 부르기, 풍자화 그리기를 금지했으며, 쉰 명에 달하는 반항적인 소녀들을 질서 잡으려고 한 사람이 할 수 있는 일은 죄다 했다. 사내아이들도 사람의 인내심을 시험하기에 충분하지만, 천만에!"], ["Boys are trying enough to human patience, goodness knows!", "여자아이들은 그보다 한없이 더하다. 특히 폭군 같은 성미에다, 블림버 박사만큼이나 가르치는 재능이라곤 없는 신경질적인 신사에게는."], ["but girls are infinitely more so, especially to nervous gentlemen, with tyrannical tempers, and no more talent for teaching than Dr. Blimber.", "데이비스 선생은 그리스어, 라틴어, 대수학, 온갖 학문에 막대한 지식을 갖춘지라 훌륭한 교사로 불렸다."], ["Mr. Davis knew any quantity of Greek, Latin, Algebra, and ologies of all sorts, so he was called a fine teacher; and manners, morals, feelings, and examples were not considered of any particular importance.", "매너며 도덕, 감정, 모범 따위는 그다지 중요하게 여겨지지 않았다."], ["It was a most unfortunate moment for denouncing Amy, and Jenny knew it. Mr.", "에이미를 고발하기에 그날은 더없이 불운한 순간이었고, 제니도 그걸 알고 있었다."], ["Davis had evidently taken his coffee too strong that morning; there was an east wind, which always affected his neuralgia; and his pupils had not done him the credit which he felt he deserved: therefore, to use the expressive, if not elegant, language of a school-girl, \"he was as nervous as a witch and as cross as a bear.\"", "데이비스 선생은 그날 아침 분명 커피를 너무 진하게 마신 것 같았고, 늘 그의 신경통을 건드리는 동풍이 불었으며, 게다가 학생들은 그가 받아 마땅하다고 여기는 만큼의 명예를 안겨 주지 못했던 것이다. 그러니 어느 여학생의 표현, 그다지 우아하지는 않지만 실감 나는 말을 빌리자면, \"그는 마녀처럼 신경이 곤두서 있고 곰처럼 심술이 났다.\""], ["The word \"limes\"", "\"라임\"이라는 말은 화약에 불을 댄 격이었다."], ["was like fire to powder; his yellow face flushed, and he rapped on his desk with an energy which made Jenny skip to her seat with unusual rapidity.", "그의 누런 얼굴이 벌게졌고, 어찌나 세게 책상을 두드렸던지 제니가 평소답지 않은 잽싼 속도로 제자리로 뛰어가 앉을 정도였다."]], [["\"Young ladies, attention, if you please!\"", "\"아가씨들, 주목하세요!\""]], [["At the stern order the buzz ceased, and fifty pairs of blue, black, gray, and brown eyes were obediently fixed upon his awful countenance.", "그 엄한 명령에 웅성거림이 멎었고, 파랗고 검고 잿빛이고 갈색인 쉰 쌍의 눈이 순순히 그의 무시무시한 얼굴에 고정되었다."]], [["\"Miss March, come to the desk.\"", "\"마치 양, 교탁으로 나오세요.\""]], [["Amy rose to comply with outward composure, but a secret fear oppressed her, for the limes weighed upon her conscience.", "에이미는 겉으로는 침착하게 따랐지만, 속으로는 두려움에 짓눌렸다. 라임이 양심을 무겁게 짓누르고 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"Bring with you the limes you have in your desk,\"", "\"책상 속에 있는 라임을 가지고 나오세요.\""], ["was the unexpected command which arrested her before she got out of her seat.", "자리에서 미처 일어서기도 전에 그녀를 멈춰 세운 뜻밖의 명령이었다."]], [["\"Don't take all,\"", "\"전부 다 가져가진 마.\""], ["whispered her neighbor, a young lady of great presence of mind.", "침착함이 대단한 아가씨인 옆자리 짝꿍이 속삭였다."]], [["Amy hastily shook out half a dozen, and laid the rest down before Mr.", "에이미는 황급히 여섯 개를 털어 내고는 나머지를 데이비스 선생 앞에 내려놓았다."], ["Davis, feeling that any man possessing a human heart would relent when that delicious perfume met his nose.", "사람의 마음을 가진 이라면 누구든 그 맛있는 향기가 코끝에 닿으면 누그러지리라 생각하면서."], ["Unfortunately, Mr. Davis particularly detested the odor of the fashionable pickle, and disgust added to his wrath.", "그러나 안타깝게도 데이비스 선생은 그 유행하는 절임의 냄새를 유독 질색했고, 혐오감이 그의 분노에 더해졌다."]], [["\"Is that all?\"", "\"그게 다인가요?\""]], [["\"Not quite,\"", "\"꼭 다는 아니에요.\""], ["stammered Amy.", "에이미가 더듬거렸다."]], [["\"Bring the rest immediately.\"", "\"당장 나머지를 가져오세요.\""]], [["With a despairing glance at her set, she obeyed.", "자기 패거리를 절망적인 눈빛으로 한번 바라본 뒤, 그녀는 시키는 대로 했다."]], [["\"You are sure there are no more?\"", "\"이제 정말 더 없는 게 확실하죠?\""]], [["\"I never lie, sir.\"", "\"전 거짓말 안 해요, 선생님.\""]], [["\"So I see.", "\"그래 보이는군요."], ["Now take these disgusting things two by two, and throw them out of the window.\"", "자, 이 역겨운 것들을 두 개씩 들고 창밖으로 던져 버리세요.\""]], [["There was a simultaneous sigh, which created quite a little gust, as the last hope fled, and the treat was ravished from their longing lips.", "마지막 희망이 사라지고 한턱이 그 간절한 입술에서 빼앗기는 순간, 일제히 새어 나온 한숨이 자그마한 돌풍을 일으킬 정도였다."], ["Scarlet with shame and anger, Amy went to and fro six dreadful times; and as each doomed couple--looking oh!", "수치심과 분노로 새빨개진 에이미는 끔찍하게도 여섯 번을 오갔다."], ["so plump and juicy--fell from her reluctant hands, a shout from the street completed the anguish of the girls, for it told them that their feast was being exulted over by the little Irish children, who were their sworn foes.", "그리고 통통하고 즙이 가득해 보이는 그 한 쌍 한 쌍의 가엾은 라임이 차마 놓고 싶지 않은 그녀의 손에서 떨어질 때마다, 거리에서 들려오는 환호성이 소녀들의 고통을 극에 달하게 했다. 그것은 자기들의 철천지원수인 동네 아일랜드 꼬마들이 자기들의 잔칫거리를 두고 환호하고 있다는 걸 알려 주었기 때문이다."], ["This--this was too much; all flashed indignant or appealing glances at the inexorable Davis, and one passionate lime-lover burst into tears.", "이건—이건 너무 심했다. 모두가 그 냉정한 데이비스 선생에게 분노 어린, 혹은 애원하는 눈빛을 번득였고, 라임을 끔찍이 좋아하는 한 아이는 그만 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["As Amy returned from her last trip, Mr. Davis gave a portentous \"Hem!\"", "에이미가 마지막 걸음을 마치고 돌아오자, 데이비스 선생은 불길하게 \"흠!\""], ["and said, in his most impressive manner,--", "하더니, 더없이 위엄 있는 태도로 말했다."]], [["\"Young ladies, you remember what I said to you a week ago.", "\"아가씨들, 일주일 전에 내가 한 말을 기억할 겁니다."], ["I am sorry this has happened, but I never allow my rules to be infringed, and I _never_ break my word.", "이런 일이 벌어져 유감이지만, 나는 내 규칙이 어겨지는 걸 결코 용납하지 않으며, 결코 내 말을 어기지 않습니다."], ["Miss March, hold out your hand.\"", "마치 양, 손을 내미세요.\""]], [["Amy started, and put both hands behind her, turning on him an imploring look which pleaded for her better than the words she could not utter. She was rather a favorite with \"old Davis,\"", "에이미는 움찔하며 두 손을 등 뒤로 감추고는, 차마 입 밖에 내지 못한 말을 대신해 자기를 변호해 주는 애원의 눈빛을 그에게 보냈다."], ["as, of course, he was called, and it's my private belief that he _would_ have broken his word if the indignation of one irrepressible young lady had not found vent in a hiss.", "그녀는 물론 다들 그렇게 부르듯 \"데이비스 영감\"에게 제법 귀여움을 받는 편이었다. 그래서 내 짐작으로는, 어쩔 줄 모르는 한 아가씨의 분노가 쉿 소리로 터져 나오지만 않았더라면 그가 정말로 자기 말을 어겼을지도 모른다."], ["That hiss, faint as it was, irritated the irascible gentleman, and sealed the culprit's fate.", "그 쉿 소리는 비록 희미했지만, 성마른 신사의 신경을 거슬렀고, 죄인의 운명을 결정지어 버렸다."]], [["\"Your hand, Miss March!\"", "\"손을 내미세요, 마치 양!\""], ["was the only answer her mute appeal received; and, too proud to cry or beseech, Amy set her teeth, threw back her head defiantly, and bore without flinching several tingling blows on her little palm.", "그녀의 말 없는 호소에 돌아온 대답은 그것뿐이었다. 그러자 에이미는 울거나 빌기엔 너무 자존심이 강해서, 이를 악물고 고개를 도전적으로 젖힌 채, 자그마한 손바닥에 떨어지는 따끔한 매를 몇 대 움찔하지도 않고 견뎌 냈다."], ["They were neither many nor heavy, but that made no difference to her.", "매는 많지도 무겁지도 않았지만, 그녀에겐 그게 아무 상관 없었다."], ["For the first time in her life she had been struck; and the disgrace, in her eyes, was as deep as if he had knocked her down.", "태어나서 처음으로 매를 맞은 것이었고, 그 치욕은 그녀가 보기엔 그가 자기를 때려눕힌 것이나 다름없을 만큼 깊었다."]], [["\"You will now stand on the platform till recess,\" said Mr.", "\"쉬는 시간까지 교단 위에 서 있도록 하세요.\""], ["Davis, resolved to do the thing thoroughly, since he had begun.", "데이비스 선생이 말했다. 일단 시작한 김에 철저히 해치우기로 작정한 것이다."]], [["That was dreadful.", "그건 끔찍한 일이었다."], ["It would have been bad enough to go to her seat, and see the pitying faces of her friends, or the satisfied ones of her few enemies; but to face the whole school, with that shame fresh upon her, seemed impossible, and for a second she felt as if she could only drop down where she stood, and break her heart with crying.", "자기 자리로 돌아가 친구들의 가엾어하는 얼굴이나 몇 안 되는 적들의 고소해하는 얼굴을 보는 것만으로도 충분히 괴로웠겠지만, 그 갓 당한 수치심을 안은 채 학교 전체와 마주한다는 건 도무지 불가능해 보였다. 한순간 그녀는 선 자리에 그대로 주저앉아 엉엉 울며 가슴이 미어질 것만 같았다."], ["A bitter sense of wrong, and the thought of Jenny Snow, helped her to bear it; and, taking the ignominious place, she fixed her eyes on the stove-funnel above what now seemed a sea of faces, and stood there, so motionless and white that the girls found it very hard to study, with that pathetic figure before them.", "부당함에 대한 쓰라린 감정과 제니 스노에 대한 생각이 그걸 견디게 해 주었다. 그녀는 그 치욕스러운 자리에 서서, 이제 얼굴들의 바다처럼 보이는 그 위로 난로 연통에 눈을 고정한 채, 어찌나 미동도 없이 새하얗게 서 있었던지, 소녀들은 그 애처로운 모습을 눈앞에 두고 공부하기가 무척 힘들었다."]], [["During the fifteen minutes that followed, the proud and sensitive little girl suffered a shame and pain which she never forgot.", "뒤이은 십오 분 동안, 자존심 세고 예민한 그 소녀는 결코 잊지 못할 수치와 고통을 겪었다."], ["To others it might seem a ludicrous or trivial affair, but to her it was a hard experience; for during the twelve years of her life she had been governed by love alone, and a blow of that sort had never touched her before.", "남들에겐 우습거나 사소한 일로 보였을지 모르나, 그녀에겐 혹독한 경험이었다. 열두 해를 사는 동안 그녀는 오로지 사랑으로만 다스려졌고, 그런 매질은 한 번도 닿아 본 적이 없었기 때문이다."], ["The smart of her hand and the ache of her heart were forgotten in the sting of the thought,--", "손바닥의 따가움도 가슴의 아픔도, 한 가지 생각이 안기는 쓰라림 앞에선 잊혔다."]], [["\"I shall have to tell at home, and they will be so disappointed in me!\"", "'집에 가서 얘기해야 할 텐데, 다들 나한테 너무 실망하실 거야!'"]], [["The fifteen minutes seemed an hour; but they came to an end at last, and the word \"Recess!\"", "십오 분이 한 시간 같았다."], ["had never seemed so welcome to her before.", "하지만 마침내 끝이 났고, \"쉬는 시간!\"이라는 말이 그녀에게 그토록 반갑게 들린 적은 없었다."]], [["\"You can go, Miss March,\"", "\"가도 좋아요, 마치 양.\""], ["said Mr. Davis, looking, as he felt, uncomfortable.", "데이비스 선생이 스스로 느끼기에도 거북한 표정으로 말했다."]], [["He did not soon forget the reproachful glance Amy gave him, as she went, without a word to any one, straight into the ante-room, snatched her things, and left the place \"forever,\" as she passionately declared to herself.", "그가 두고두고 잊지 못한 것은, 에이미가 누구에게도 한마디 없이 곧장 대기실로 들어가 자기 물건을 챙긴 뒤 스스로에게 격하게 선언하듯 \"영영\""], ["She was in a sad state when she got home; and when the older girls arrived, some time later, an indignation meeting was held at once. Mrs.", "그곳을 떠나며 그에게 던진 그 원망 어린 눈빛이었다."], ["March did not say much, but looked disturbed, and comforted her afflicted little daughter in her tenderest manner.", "집에 도착했을 때 그녀는 처량한 상태였다. 그리고 얼마 뒤 언니들이 돌아오자 곧바로 성토 대회가 열렸다."], ["Meg bathed the insulted hand with glycerine and tears; Beth felt that even her beloved kittens would fail as a balm for griefs like this; Jo wrathfully proposed that Mr.", "마치 부인은 별말은 하지 않았지만 심란한 표정으로, 상처받은 어린 딸을 더없이 다정하게 위로해 주었다."], ["Davis be arrested without delay; and Hannah shook her fist at the \"villain,\" and pounded potatoes for dinner as if she had him under her pestle.", "메그는 모욕당한 손을 글리세린과 눈물로 씻겨 주었고, 베스는 이런 슬픔에는 자기가 그토록 사랑하는 새끼 고양이들조차 위안이 못 되리라 느꼈으며, 조는 분개해서 데이비스 선생을 당장 잡아들여야 한다고 했고, 해나는 그 \"악당\"을 향해 주먹을 흔들며 마치 그를 절구통 아래 넣어 둔 양 저녁 식사에 쓸 감자를 짓이겼다."]], [["No notice was taken of Amy's flight, except by her mates; but the sharp-eyed demoiselles discovered that Mr.", "에이미가 도망쳐 나온 걸 알아챈 사람은 동무들밖에 없었다."], ["Davis was quite benignant in the afternoon, also unusually nervous.", "하지만 눈썰미 좋은 아가씨들은 오후 내내 데이비스 선생이 사뭇 인자해진 데다 평소답지 않게 신경이 곤두서 있다는 걸 알아챘다."], ["Just before school closed, Jo appeared, wearing a grim expression, as she stalked up to the desk, and delivered a letter from her mother; then collected Amy's property, and departed, carefully scraping the mud from her boots on the door-mat, as if she shook the dust of the place off her feet.", "학교가 파하기 직전, 조가 험상궂은 표정을 한 채 나타나 성큼성큼 교탁으로 걸어가더니 어머니의 편지를 건넸다. 그러고는 에이미의 물건을 챙겨, 마치 그곳의 먼지를 발에서 털어 내듯 문 앞 매트에 부츠의 진흙을 정성껏 닦아 내고는 떠났다."]], [["\"Yes, you can have a vacation from school, but I want you to study a little every day, with Beth,\"", "\"그래, 학교는 좀 쉬어도 된다. 하지만 베스랑 날마다 조금씩 공부는 해야 해.\""], ["said Mrs. March, that evening.", "그날 저녁 마치 부인이 말했다."], ["\"I don't approve of corporal punishment, especially for girls.", "\"나는 체벌에 찬성하지 않아."], ["I dislike Mr.", "특히 여자아이한테는 말이다."], ["Davis's manner of teaching, and don't think the girls you associate with are doing you any good, so I shall ask your father's advice before I send you anywhere else.\"", "데이비스 선생의 가르치는 방식도 마음에 안 들고, 네가 어울리는 아이들도 너한테 별 도움이 되는 것 같지 않구나. 그러니 다른 데 보내기 전에 네 아버지의 조언을 구해 봐야겠다.\""]], [["\"That's good!", "\"잘됐어요!"], ["I wish all the girls would leave, and spoil his old school.", "애들이 다 그만둬서 그 영감 학교를 망쳐 버렸으면 좋겠어요."], ["It's perfectly maddening to think of those lovely limes,\"", "그 사랑스러운 라임들을 생각하면 정말 미칠 것 같아요.\""], ["sighed Amy, with the air of a martyr.", "에이미가 순교자 같은 표정으로 한숨을 쉬었다."]], [["\"I am not sorry you lost them, for you broke the rules, and deserved some punishment for disobedience,\"", "\"네가 그걸 잃은 건 안됐다고 생각 안 한다. 규칙을 어겼으니, 불복종에 대한 벌을 좀 받아 마땅했지.\""], ["was the severe reply, which rather disappointed the young lady, who expected nothing but sympathy.", "엄한 대답이 돌아오자, 동정밖에 기대하지 않았던 어린 아가씨는 적잖이 실망했다."]], [["\"Do you mean you are glad I was disgraced before the whole school?\"", "\"그럼 제가 학교 전체 앞에서 망신당한 게 기쁘시다는 거예요?\""], ["cried Amy.", "에이미가 외쳤다."]], [["\"I should not have chosen that way of mending a fault,\"", "\"잘못을 고치는 데 나라면 그런 방법을 택하진 않았을 게다.\" 어머니가 대답했다."], ["replied her mother; \"but I'm not sure that it won't do you more good than a milder method.", "\"하지만 그게 더 부드러운 방법보다 너한테 더 큰 도움이 안 되리라고 장담은 못 하겠구나. 너는 점점 좀 우쭐대는 데가 생기고 있어, 얘야."], ["You are getting to be rather conceited, my dear, and it is quite time you set about correcting it.", "이제 그걸 바로잡기 시작할 때가 됐단다. 너한테는 자그마한 재능과 미덕이 여럿 있지만, 그걸 굳이 드러낼 필요는 없어."], ["You have a good many little gifts and virtues, but there is no need of parading them, for conceit spoils the finest genius.", "자만은 아무리 빼어난 재능도 망쳐 버리거든. 진짜 재능이나 선함이 오래 묻혀 있을 위험은 별로 없단다."], ["There is not much danger that real talent or goodness will be overlooked long; even if it is, the consciousness of possessing and using it well should satisfy one, and the great charm of all power is modesty.\"", "설령 그렇다 해도, 그걸 지니고 잘 쓰고 있다는 자각만으로 만족할 수 있어야 하지. 모든 힘의 가장 큰 매력은 겸손에 있는 거란다.\""]], [["\"So it is!\"", "\"정말 그래요!\""], ["cried Laurie, who was playing chess in a corner with Jo.", "한구석에서 조와 체스를 두던 로리가 외쳤다."], ["\"I knew a girl, once, who had a really remarkable talent for music, and she didn't know it; never guessed what sweet little things she composed when she was alone, and wouldn't have believed it if any one had told her.\"", "\"전에 음악에 정말 놀라운 재능을 지닌 여자애를 한 명 알았는데요, 그 애는 자기 재능을 몰랐어요. 혼자 있을 때 자기가 얼마나 사랑스러운 곡들을 짓는지 짐작도 못 했고, 누가 말해 줘도 믿지 않았을걸요.\""]], [["\"I wish I'd known that nice girl; maybe she would have helped me, I'm so stupid,\"", "\"나도 그 좋은 애를 알았으면 좋았을 텐데. 나처럼 멍청한 애를 도와줬을지도 모르잖아.\""], ["said Beth, who stood beside him, listening eagerly.", "로리 곁에 서서 열심히 듣고 있던 베스가 말했다."]], [["\"You do know her, and she helps you better than any one else could,\"", "\"넌 이미 그 애를 알아. 게다가 그 애는 누구보다도 너를 잘 도와주고 있는걸.\""], ["answered Laurie, looking at her with such mischievous meaning in his merry black eyes, that Beth suddenly turned very red, and hid her face in the sofa-cushion, quite overcome by such an unexpected discovery.", "로리가 명랑한 검은 눈에 어찌나 장난기 어린 의미를 담아 그녀를 바라보았던지, 베스는 갑자기 새빨개져서, 그 뜻밖의 깨달음에 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 소파 쿠션에 얼굴을 파묻었다."]], [["Jo let Laurie win the game, to pay for that praise of her Beth, who could not be prevailed upon to play for them after her compliment.", "조는 자기 베스를 그렇게 칭찬해 준 보답으로 로리가 게임을 이기게 해 주었다. 칭찬을 받은 뒤로 베스는 아무리 졸라도 그들에게 연주를 들려주려 하지 않았기 때문이다."], ["So Laurie did his best, and sung delightfully, being in a particularly lively humor, for to the Marches he seldom showed the moody side of his character.", "그래서 로리는 자기 솜씨를 한껏 발휘해 멋들어지게 노래를 불렀다. 유독 명랑한 기분이었던 것이다."], ["When he was gone, Amy, who had been pensive all the evening, said suddenly, as if busy over some new idea,--", "마치 자매들 앞에서 그는 자기 성격의 침울한 면을 좀처럼 드러내지 않았다. 그가 가고 나자, 저녁 내내 골똘하던 에이미가 무슨 새로운 생각에 골몰한 듯 불쑥 말했다."]], [["\"Is Laurie an accomplished boy?\"", "\"로리는 교양 있는 애인가요?\""]], [["\"Yes; he has had an excellent education, and has much talent; he will make a fine man, if not spoilt by petting,\"", "\"그럼. 훌륭한 교육을 받았고, 재능도 많지."], ["replied her mother.", "응석받이로 망치지만 않는다면 멋진 사내가 될 게다.\" 어머니가 대답했다."]], [["\"And he isn't conceited, is he?\"", "\"그리고 우쭐대지도 않죠, 그렇죠?\""], ["asked Amy.", "에이미가 물었다."]], [["\"Not in the least; that is why he is so charming, and we all like him so much.\"", "\"전혀 그렇지 않지. 그래서 그렇게 매력적이고, 우리 모두가 그 애를 그토록 좋아하는 거란다.\""]], [["\"I see; it's nice to have accomplishments, and be elegant; but not to show off, or get perked up,\"", "\"알겠어요. 교양 있고 우아한 건 좋지만, 으스대거나 잘난 척하면 안 된다는 거죠.\""], ["said Amy thoughtfully.", "에이미가 생각에 잠겨 말했다."]], [["\"These things are always seen and felt in a person's manner and conversation, if modestly used; but it is not necessary to display them,\"", "\"겸손하게 지니고만 있으면 그런 것들은 그 사람의 태도와 대화에서 언제나 드러나고 느껴지는 법이란다. 굳이 과시할 필요가 없어.\""], ["said Mrs. March.", "마치 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Any more than it's proper to wear all your bonnets and gowns and ribbons at once, that folks may know you've got them,\"", "\"그건 마치 자기가 가진 걸 남들이 다 알게 하려고 모자며 드레스며 리본을 한꺼번에 죄다 걸치고 다니는 게 점잖지 못한 거랑 똑같네.\""], ["added Jo; and the lecture ended in a laugh.", "조가 덧붙였고, 그 설교는 한바탕 웃음으로 끝이 났다."]], [["# JO MEETS APOLLYON.", "# JO MEETS APOLLYON."]], [["\"Girls, where are you going?\"", "\"언니들, 어디 가?\""], ["asked Amy, coming into their room one Saturday afternoon, and finding them getting ready to go out, with an air of secrecy which excited her curiosity.", "어느 토요일 오후, 에이미가 언니들의 방에 들어왔다가 그들이 외출 채비를 하고 있는 걸 보고 물었다. 무언가 비밀스러운 낌새가 그녀의 호기심을 자극했다."]], [["\"Never mind; little girls shouldn't ask questions,\"", "\"신경 쓰지 마. 어린애는 그런 거 묻는 거 아니야.\""], ["returned Jo sharply.", "조가 쌀쌀맞게 대꾸했다."]], [["Now if there _is_ anything mortifying to our feelings, when we are young, it is to be told that; and to be bidden to \"run away, dear,\"", "그런데 어릴 적에 우리 감정을 가장 자존심 상하게 하는 게 있다면, 바로 그런 말을 듣는 것이다. 게다가 \"저리 가렴, 얘야\""], ["is still more trying to us.", "하는 말은 한층 더 견디기 힘든 법이다."], ["Amy bridled up at this insult, and determined to find out the secret, if she teased for an hour.", "에이미는 이 모욕에 발끈해서, 한 시간을 졸라서라도 그 비밀을 알아내고 말겠다고 결심했다."], ["Turning to Meg, who never refused her anything very long, she said coaxingly, \"Do tell me!", "자기 부탁이라면 오래 거절하는 법이 없는 메그를 돌아보며 그녀는 어르듯 말했다. \"제발 좀 말해 줘!"], ["I should think you might let me go, too; for Beth is fussing over her piano, and I haven't got anything to do, and am _so_ lonely.\"", "나도 같이 가게 해 줄 수 있잖아. 베스는 피아노에 매달려 있고, 난 할 일도 없어서 너무 외롭단 말이야.\""]], [["\"I can't, dear, because you aren't invited,\" began Meg; but Jo broke in impatiently, \"Now, Meg, be quiet, or you will spoil it all. You can't go, Amy; so don't be a baby, and whine about it.\"", "\"안 돼, 얘야. 넌 초대받지 않았거든.\" 메그가 입을 열었다. 하지만 조가 짜증스럽게 끼어들었다. \"메그 언니, 가만히 좀 있어. 안 그러면 다 망쳐 놓겠어. 넌 못 가, 에이미. 그러니까 아기처럼 굴면서 칭얼대지 마.\""]], [["\"You are going somewhere with Laurie, I know you are; you were whispering and laughing together, on the sofa, last night, and you stopped when I came in.", "\"언니들 로리랑 어디 가는 거지, 분명해. 어젯밤 소파에서 둘이 속닥거리면서 웃다가, 내가 들어가니까 뚝 그쳤잖아."], ["Aren't you going with him?\"", "로리랑 가는 거 아니야?\""]], [["\"Yes, we are; now do be still, and stop bothering.\"", "\"맞아, 로리랑 가. 그러니 이제 좀 가만히 있고, 그만 좀 귀찮게 해.\""]], [["Amy held her tongue, but used her eyes, and saw Meg slip a fan into her pocket.", "에이미는 입을 다물었지만 눈을 굴렸고, 메그가 부채를 주머니에 슬쩍 넣는 걸 보았다."]], [["\"I know!", "\"알았다! 알았어!"], ["I know!", "언니들 '일곱 성' 보러 극장에 가는 거지!\""], ["you're going to the theatre to see the 'Seven Castles!'\"", "그녀가 외치고는 단호하게 덧붙였다. \"그럼 나도 갈 거야."], ["she cried; adding resolutely, \"and I _shall_ go, for mother said I might see it; and I've got my rag-money, and it was mean not to tell me in time.\"", "엄마가 봐도 된다고 하셨고, 나도 헝겊 모은 돈 있단 말이야. 진작 말 안 해 준 건 너무했어.\""]], [["\"Just listen to me a minute, and be a good child,\"", "\"잠깐만 내 말 좀 들어 봐, 착하게 굴고.\""], ["said Meg soothingly.", "메그가 달래듯 말했다."], ["\"Mother doesn't wish you to go this week, because your eyes are not well enough yet to bear the light of this fairy piece.", "\"엄마는 이번 주에 네가 가는 걸 원치 않으셔. 네 눈이 아직 이 동화극의 환한 빛을 견딜 만큼 다 낫지 않았거든."], ["Next week you can go with Beth and Hannah, and have a nice time.\"", "다음 주에 베스랑 해나랑 같이 가서 즐겁게 보면 돼.\""]], [["\"I don't like that half as well as going with you and Laurie.", "\"그건 언니들이랑 로리랑 가는 거의 절반만큼도 재미없잖아. 제발 가게 해 줘."], ["Please let me; I've been sick with this cold so long, and shut up, I'm dying for some fun.", "이 감기로 이렇게 오래 앓으면서 집에만 갇혀 있었더니, 재미있는 게 너무 그리워 죽겠어. 그래 줘, 메그 언니!"], ["Do, Meg! I'll be ever so good,\"", "정말 착하게 굴게.\""], ["pleaded Amy, looking as pathetic as she could.", "에이미가 최대한 애처로운 표정을 지으며 애원했다."]], [["\"Suppose we take her.", "\"데려가는 게 어떨까."], ["I don't believe mother would mind, if we bundle her up well,\"", "잘 싸매면 엄마도 괜찮다고 하실 것 같은데.\""], ["began Meg.", "메그가 입을 열었다."]], [["\"If _she_ goes _I_ sha'n't; and if I don't, Laurie won't like it; and it will be very rude, after he invited only us, to go and drag in Amy.", "\"쟤가 가면 난 안 가. 그리고 내가 안 가면 로리도 싫어할 거야."], ["I should think she'd hate to poke herself where she isn't wanted,\"", "우리만 초대해 놨는데 에이미를 끌고 가면 너무 무례하잖아. 자기가 환영받지 못하는 데에 끼어들기 싫어하는 게 당연하다고 봐.\""], ["said Jo crossly, for she disliked the trouble of overseeing a fidgety child, when she wanted to enjoy herself.", "조가 짜증스럽게 말했다. 모처럼 즐기고 싶은데 안절부절못하는 어린애를 챙겨야 하는 수고가 싫었던 것이다."]], [["Her tone and manner angered Amy, who began to put her boots on, saying, in her most aggravating way, \"I _shall_ go; Meg says I may; and if I pay for myself, Laurie hasn't anything to do with it.\"", "그 말투와 태도가 에이미를 화나게 했고, 그녀는 더없이 약 올리는 투로 부츠를 신으며 말했다. \"난 갈 거야. 메그가 가도 된댔어. 그리고 내 표 값은 내가 내면 로리랑은 아무 상관 없잖아.\""]], [["\"You can't sit with us, for our seats are reserved, and you mustn't sit alone; so Laurie will give you his place, and that will spoil our pleasure; or he'll get another seat for you, and that isn't proper, when you weren't asked. You sha'n't stir a step; so you may just stay where you are,\" scolded Jo, crosser than ever, having just pricked her finger in her hurry.", "\"넌 우리랑 같이 못 앉아. 우리 자리는 예약돼 있거든. 그렇다고 너 혼자 앉을 수도 없으니, 로리가 자기 자리를 너한테 양보할 테고, 그럼 우리 즐거움을 다 망쳐 놓는 거야. 아니면 로리가 네 자리를 따로 구해 줘야 하는데, 초대받지도 않았는데 그건 점잖지 못한 일이지. 넌 한 발짝도 못 움직여. 그러니 그냥 있던 자리에 있어.\" 조가 어느 때보다 더 짜증을 내며 야단쳤다. 서두르다가 막 손가락을 바늘에 찔린 참이었다."]], [["Sitting on the floor, with one boot on, Amy began to cry, and Meg to reason with her, when Laurie called from below, and the two girls hurried down, leaving their sister wailing; for now and then she forgot her grown-up ways, and acted like a spoilt child. Just as the party was setting out, Amy called over the banisters, in a threatening tone, \"You'll be sorry for this, Jo March; see if you ain't.\"", "부츠 한 짝만 신은 채 바닥에 앉아 에이미가 울음을 터뜨렸고, 메그가 그녀를 타이르고 있을 때 아래에서 로리가 불렀다. 두 소녀는 울고 있는 동생을 남겨 둔 채 서둘러 내려갔다. 에이미는 이따금 다 큰 척하는 태도를 잊고 응석받이 어린애처럼 굴곤 했던 것이다. 일행이 막 나서려는 참에, 에이미가 난간 너머로 위협하는 투로 외쳤다. \"이 일 후회하게 될 거야, 조 마치. 두고 봐.\""]], [["\"Fiddlesticks!\"", "\"쓸데없는 소리!\""], ["returned Jo, slamming the door.", "조가 문을 쾅 닫으며 대꾸했다."]], [["They had a charming time, for \"The Seven Castles of the Diamond Lake\"", "그들은 즐거운 시간을 보냈다. \"다이아몬드 호수의 일곱 성\"은 더 바랄 게 없을 만큼 화려하고 멋졌으니까."], ["were as brilliant and wonderful as heart could wish.", "하지만 우스꽝스러운 빨간 도깨비들과 반짝이는 요정들, 으리으리한 왕자와 공주들에도 불구하고, 조의 즐거움에는 한 방울의 씁쓸함이 섞여 있었다."], ["But, in spite of the comical red imps, sparkling elves, and gorgeous princes and princesses, Jo's pleasure had a drop of bitterness in it; the fairy queen's yellow curls reminded her of Amy; and between the acts she amused herself with wondering what her sister would do to make her \"sorry for it.\"", "요정 여왕의 노란 곱슬머리가 에이미를 떠올리게 했던 것이다. 막간마다 그녀는 동생이 자기를 \"후회하게\""], ["She and Amy had had many lively skirmishes in the course of their lives, for both had quick tempers, and were apt to be violent when fairly roused.", "만들려고 무슨 짓을 할지 궁리하며 마음을 달랬다. 그녀와 에이미는 살아오는 동안 여러 차례 한바탕씩 다투었다."], ["Amy teased Jo, and Jo irritated Amy, and semi-occasional explosions occurred, of which both were much ashamed afterward.", "둘 다 성미가 급한 데다, 일단 제대로 발끈하면 격해지기 쉬웠기 때문이다. 에이미가 조를 약 올리면 조가 에이미를 자극했고, 그렇게 이따금 폭발이 일어났는데, 나중에는 둘 다 무척 부끄러워했다."], ["Although the oldest, Jo had the least self-control, and had hard times trying to curb the fiery spirit which was continually getting her into trouble; her anger never lasted long, and, having humbly confessed her fault, she sincerely repented, and tried to do better.", "맏이긴 했어도 조는 자제력이 가장 부족했고, 늘 자기를 곤경에 빠뜨리는 그 불같은 성미를 다스리려 애쓰느라 고생이 많았다. 그녀의 화는 오래가지 않았고, 자기 잘못을 겸손하게 인정한 뒤엔 진심으로 뉘우치며 더 잘하려고 애썼다."], ["Her sisters used to say that they rather liked to get Jo into a fury, because she was such an angel afterward.", "자매들은 조를 화나게 만드는 게 은근히 재미있다고들 했다. 화내고 난 뒤엔 어찌나 천사 같았던지."], ["Poor Jo tried desperately to be good, but her bosom enemy was always ready to flame up and defeat her; and it took years of patient effort to subdue it.", "가엾은 조는 착해지려고 필사적으로 애썼지만, 그녀의 마음속 원수는 늘 활활 타올라 그녀를 무너뜨릴 채비가 되어 있었다. 그걸 잠재우는 데는 여러 해에 걸친 끈기 있는 노력이 필요했다."]], [["When they got home, they found Amy reading in the parlor.", "집에 돌아오니 에이미가 응접실에서 책을 읽고 있었다."], ["She assumed an injured air as they came in; never lifted her eyes from her book, or asked a single question.", "그들이 들어와도 그녀는 부루퉁한 태도를 지으며, 책에서 눈을 떼지도, 한마디 묻지도 않았다."], ["Perhaps curiosity might have conquered resentment, if Beth had not been there to inquire, and receive a glowing description of the play.", "어쩌면 호기심이 원망을 이겼을지도 모르나, 마침 베스가 거기 있어 이것저것 물어보고는 연극에 대한 신나는 묘사를 들었다."], ["On going up to put away her best hat, Jo's first look was toward the bureau; for, in their last quarrel, Amy had soothed her feelings by turning Jo's top drawer upside down on the floor.", "외출용 모자를 치우러 위층으로 올라간 조가 맨 먼저 눈길을 준 곳은 서랍장이었다. 지난번 다툼 때 에이미가 조의 맨 위 서랍을 바닥에 거꾸로 쏟아 분풀이를 했던 것이다."], ["Everything was in its place, however; and after a hasty glance into her various closets, bags, and boxes, Jo decided that Amy had forgiven and forgotten her wrongs.", "하지만 모든 게 제자리에 있었다. 여러 벽장과 가방, 상자들을 후딱 살펴본 뒤, 조는 에이미가 자기에게 당한 억울함을 용서하고 잊었다고 결론지었다."]], [["There Jo was mistaken; for next day she made a discovery which produced a tempest.", "하지만 조의 생각은 틀렸다. 이튿날 그녀는 폭풍을 불러일으킬 사실을 발견하게 되었으니까."], ["Meg, Beth, and Amy were sitting together, late in the afternoon, when Jo burst into the room, looking excited, and demanding breathlessly, \"Has any one taken my book?\"", "늦은 오후, 메그와 베스, 에이미가 함께 앉아 있을 때 조가 흥분한 얼굴로 방에 뛰어들며 숨도 못 쉬고 다그쳤다. \"누가 내 책 가져갔어?\""]], [["Meg and Beth said \"No,\" at once, and looked surprised; Amy poked the fire, and said nothing.", "메그와 베스는 곧장 \"아니\"라고 하며 놀란 표정을 지었지만, 에이미는 난로만 들쑤시며 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Jo saw her color rise, and was down upon her in a minute.", "조는 그녀의 얼굴이 붉어지는 걸 보고 단숨에 덮쳐들었다."]], [["\"Amy, you've got it?\"", "\"에이미, 네가 가져갔지?\""]], [["\"No, I haven't.\"", "\"아니야, 안 가져갔어.\""]], [["\"You know where it is, then?\"", "\"그럼 어디 있는지 알지?\""]], [["\"No, I don't.\"", "\"아니, 몰라.\""]], [["\"That's a fib!\"", "\"거짓말!\""], ["cried Jo, taking her by the shoulders, and looking fierce enough to frighten a much braver child than Amy.", "조가 에이미의 어깨를 붙잡고, 에이미보다 훨씬 더 용감한 아이라도 겁먹게 할 만큼 사나운 표정으로 소리쳤다."]], [["\"It isn't.", "\"거짓말 아니야."], ["I haven't got it, don't know where it is now, and don't care.\"", "난 안 가졌고, 지금 어디 있는지도 모르고, 알고 싶지도 않아.\""]], [["\"You know something about it, and you'd better tell at once, or I'll make you,\"", "\"넌 뭔가 알고 있어. 당장 말하는 게 좋을 거야."], ["and Jo gave her a slight shake.", "안 그러면 말하게 만들 테니까.\" 조가 그녀를 살짝 흔들었다."]], [["\"Scold as much as you like, you'll never see your silly old book again,\"", "\"마음껏 야단쳐 봐. 언니의 그 한심한 낡은 책은 두 번 다시 못 볼 테니까.\""], ["cried Amy, getting excited in her turn.", "에이미도 덩달아 흥분하며 소리쳤다."]], [["\"Why not?\"", "\"왜 못 봐?\""]], [["\"I burnt it up.\"", "\"내가 태워 버렸거든.\""]], [["\"What!", "\"뭐!"], ["my little book I was so fond of, and worked over, and meant to finish before father got home?", "내가 그렇게 아끼고, 그토록 공들였고, 아버지 돌아오시기 전에 끝내려던 내 그 작은 책을?"], ["Have you really burnt it?\"", "정말 그걸 태웠다고?\""], ["said Jo, turning very pale, while her eyes kindled and her hands clutched Amy nervously.", "조가 새파랗게 질리며 말했다. 두 눈에는 불이 일었고, 두 손은 에이미를 신경질적으로 움켜쥐었다."]], [["\"Yes, I did!", "\"그래, 태웠어!"], ["I told you I'd make you pay for being so cross yesterday, and I have, so--\"", "어제 그렇게 심술부린 대가를 치르게 하겠다고 했잖아. 그래서 치르게 한 거야, 그러니까—\""]], [["Amy got no farther, for Jo's hot temper mastered her, and she shook Amy till her teeth chattered in her head; crying, in a passion of grief and anger,--", "에이미는 더 잇지 못했다. 조의 불같은 성미가 그녀를 사로잡아, 이가 딱딱 부딪칠 만큼 에이미를 마구 흔들며, 슬픔과 분노로 격해져 외쳤다."]], [["\"You wicked, wicked girl!", "\"이 못된, 못된 것!"], ["I never can write it again, and I'll never forgive you as long as I live.\"", "난 그걸 다시는 못 써. 그리고 평생 너를 용서하지 않을 거야.\""]], [["Meg flew to rescue Amy, and Beth to pacify Jo, but Jo was quite beside herself; and, with a parting box on her sister's ear, she rushed out of the room up to the old sofa in the garret, and finished her fight alone.", "메그가 에이미를 구하러, 베스가 조를 달래러 달려들었지만, 조는 완전히 제정신이 아니었다. 마지막으로 동생의 따귀를 한 대 올려붙이고는 방을 뛰쳐나가 다락방의 낡은 소파로 올라가, 혼자서 분을 삭였다."]], [["The storm cleared up below, for Mrs.", "아래층의 폭풍은 가라앉았다."], ["March came home, and, having heard the story, soon brought Amy to a sense of the wrong she had done her sister.", "마치 부인이 집에 돌아와 사연을 듣고는, 얼마 안 가 에이미에게 언니에게 저지른 잘못을 깨닫게 해 주었기 때문이다."], ["Jo's book was the pride of her heart, and was regarded by her family as a literary sprout of great promise.", "조의 책은 그녀의 자랑이요 마음의 보배였고, 식구들은 그것을 큰 가능성을 지닌 문학의 새싹으로 여겼다."], ["It was only half a dozen little fairy tales, but Jo had worked over them patiently, putting her whole heart into her work, hoping to make something good enough to print.", "고작 자그마한 동화 여섯 편에 지나지 않았지만, 조는 인쇄해도 좋을 만큼 훌륭한 무언가를 만들어 내기를 바라며, 온 마음을 쏟아 끈기 있게 매달려 왔다."], ["She had just copied them with great care, and had destroyed the old manuscript, so that Amy's bonfire had consumed the loving work of several years.", "그녀는 막 그것들을 무척 정성스레 옮겨 적고 낡은 원고를 없애 버린 터라, 에이미가 지른 불은 몇 해에 걸쳐 정성껏 쓴 작품을 모조리 태워 버린 셈이었다."], ["It seemed a small loss to others, but to Jo it was a dreadful calamity, and she felt that it never could be made up to her.", "남들에게는 사소한 손실로 보였겠지만, 조에게는 끔찍한 재앙이었고, 무엇으로도 결코 보상받을 수 없는 일이라는 느낌이 들었다."], ["Beth mourned as for a departed kitten, and Meg refused to defend her pet; Mrs.", "베스는 새끼 고양이가 죽기라도 한 것처럼 슬퍼했고, 메그는 자기 동생을 두둔하려 들지 않았다."], ["March looked grave and grieved, and Amy felt that no one would love her till she had asked pardon for the act which she now regretted more than any of them.", "마치 부인은 침통하고 슬픈 표정을 지었으며, 에이미는 자기가 한 짓을 그 누구보다 더 후회하면서도, 용서를 빌기 전에는 아무도 자기를 사랑해 주지 않으리라는 생각이 들었다."]], [["When the tea-bell rung, Jo appeared, looking so grim and unapproachable that it took all Amy's courage to say meekly,--", "차 마실 시간을 알리는 종이 울리자, 조가 나타났다. 너무도 험악하고 다가갈 수 없는 표정이어서, 에이미는 온 용기를 다 끌어모은 뒤에야 겨우 고분고분하게 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["\"Please forgive me, Jo; I'm very, very sorry.\"", "\"미안해, 조 언니. 정말, 정말 잘못했어.\""]], [["\"I never shall forgive you,\"", "\"절대 용서 안 해.\""], ["was Jo's stern answer; and, from that moment, she ignored Amy entirely.", "조는 매몰차게 대답했다. 그리고 그 순간부터 에이미를 완전히 못 본 척했다."]], [["No one spoke of the great trouble,--not even Mrs.", "아무도 그 큰일에 대해 말을 꺼내지 않았다. 마치 부인조차도."], ["March,--for all had learned by experience that when Jo was in that mood words were wasted; and the wisest course was to wait till some little accident, or her own generous nature, softened Jo's resentment, and healed the breach.", "조가 그런 기분일 때는 말을 해 봤자 소용없다는 걸 다들 경험으로 알고 있었기 때문이다. 가장 현명한 방법은 사소한 일이 생기거나, 조 자신의 너그러운 성품이 발동하여 노여움이 누그러지고 갈라진 틈이 아물 때까지 기다리는 것이었다."], ["It was not a happy evening; for, though they sewed as usual, while their mother read aloud from Bremer, Scott, or Edgeworth, something was wanting, and the sweet home-peace was disturbed.", "그날 저녁은 즐겁지 못했다. 평소처럼 어머니가 브레머나 스콧, 에지워스의 책을 소리 내어 읽어 주는 동안 다들 바느질을 했지만, 무언가가 빠진 듯했고 정겨운 가정의 평화가 깨어져 있었다."], ["They felt this most when singing-time came; for Beth could only play, Jo stood dumb as a stone, and Amy broke down, so Meg and mother sung alone.", "노래할 시간이 되자 그 사실이 가장 뚜렷이 느껴졌다. 베스는 그저 연주만 했고, 조는 돌처럼 입을 다물고 서 있었으며, 에이미는 그만 목이 메어 버려서, 결국 메그와 어머니만 둘이서 노래했다."], ["But, in spite of their efforts to be as cheery as larks, the flute-like voices did not seem to chord as well as usual, and all felt out of tune.", "하지만 종달새처럼 명랑하게 굴어 보려고 애를 써도, 피리 같은 목소리들이 평소처럼 잘 어우러지지 않았고, 다들 어딘가 어긋난 기분이었다."]], [["As Jo received her good-night kiss, Mrs. March whispered gently,--", "조가 잘 자라는 입맞춤을 받을 때, 마치 부인이 다정하게 속삭였다."]], [["\"My dear, don't let the sun go down upon your anger; forgive each other, help each other, and begin again to-morrow.\"", "\"얘야, 노여움을 품은 채 해가 지게 하지 말거라. 서로 용서하고, 서로 돕고, 내일 다시 시작하렴.\""]], [["Jo wanted to lay her head down on that motherly bosom, and cry her grief and anger all away; but tears were an unmanly weakness, and she felt so deeply injured that she really _couldn't_ quite forgive yet.", "조는 그 어머니의 품에 머리를 묻고, 슬픔과 노여움을 죄다 울어서 씻어 내고 싶었다. 하지만 눈물은 사내답지 못한 나약함이었고, 마음에 너무 깊은 상처를 입은 터라 아직은 정말 도저히 용서할 수가 없었다."], ["So she winked hard, shook her head, and said, gruffly because Amy was listening,--", "그래서 조는 눈을 꾹 깜빡이고 고개를 저으며, 에이미가 듣고 있었던 탓에 퉁명스럽게 말했다."]], [["\"It was an abominable thing, and she don't deserve to be forgiven.\"", "\"정말 못된 짓이었어요. 그 앤 용서받을 자격이 없어요.\""]], [["With that she marched off to bed, and there was no merry or confidential gossip that night.", "그 말을 끝으로 조는 성큼성큼 자러 가 버렸고, 그날 밤에는 즐겁거나 다정한 수다가 오가지 않았다."]], [["Amy was much offended that her overtures of peace had been repulsed, and began to wish she had not humbled herself, to feel more injured than ever, and to plume herself on her superior virtue in a way which was particularly exasperating.", "에이미는 화해의 손길이 거절당한 것이 무척 서운했고, 괜히 자존심을 굽혔다는 생각이 들기 시작했다. 그러자 어느 때보다 더 상처받은 기분이 들었고, 자기가 더 도덕적으로 우월하다며 으스대기 시작했는데, 그 태도가 유독 사람 속을 긁었다."], ["Jo still looked like a thunder-cloud, and nothing went well all day.", "조는 여전히 먹구름 같은 얼굴이었고, 하루 종일 되는 일이 하나도 없었다. 아침에는 살을 에는 듯이 추웠다."], ["It was bitter cold in the morning; she dropped her precious turn-over in the gutter, Aunt March had an attack of fidgets, Meg was pensive, Beth _would_ look grieved and wistful when she got home, and Amy kept making remarks about people who were always talking about being good, and yet wouldn't try, when other people set them a virtuous example.", "조는 아끼던 파이를 도랑에 떨어뜨렸고, 마치 대고모는 안절부절못하며 신경질을 부렸으며, 메그는 시무룩했고, 베스는 집에 돌아오면 어김없이 슬프고 안타까운 표정을 지었다. 게다가 에이미는, 남들이 착하게 사는 모범을 보여 줘도 늘 착해지겠다는 말만 입에 달고 살면서 정작 노력은 안 하는 사람들에 대해 계속 한마디씩 던졌다."]], [["\"Everybody is so hateful, I'll ask Laurie to go skating.", "\"다들 어찌나 밉살스러운지, 로리한테 스케이트 타러 가자고 해야겠어."], ["He is always kind and jolly, and will put me to rights, I know,\"", "걘 늘 친절하고 명랑하니까, 분명히 내 기분을 풀어 줄 거야.\""], ["said Jo to herself, and off she went.", "조는 혼잣말을 하더니 휙 나가 버렸다."]], [["Amy heard the clash of skates, and looked out with an impatient exclamation,--", "에이미는 스케이트 부딪치는 소리를 듣고, 답답하다는 듯 소리치며 밖을 내다보았다."]], [["\"There!", "\"저것 봐!"], ["she promised I should go next time, for this is the last ice we shall have.", "다음번엔 날 데려가겠다고 약속해 놓고선. 이번이 올해 마지막으로 얼음을 탈 기회란 말이야."], ["But it's no use to ask such a cross-patch to take me.\"", "하지만 저렇게 심술이 난 애한테 데려가 달라고 해 봤자 소용없겠지.\""]], [["\"Don't say that; you _were_ very naughty, and it _is_ hard to forgive the loss of her precious little book; but I think she might do it now, and I guess she will, if you try her at the right minute,\" said Meg. \"Go after them; don't say anything till Jo has got good-natured with Laurie, then take a quiet minute, and just kiss her, or do some kind thing, and I'm sure she'll be friends again, with all her heart.\"", "\"그렇게 말하지 마. 네가 정말 못되게 굴긴 했잖아. 조 언니가 그 소중한 작은 책을 잃은 걸 용서하기란 쉽지 않은 일이야. 그래도 이제는 용서할 만하다고 봐. 적당한 순간을 잘 골라서 다가가면, 분명 용서해 줄 거야.\" 메그가 말했다. \"언니랑 로리 뒤를 따라가. 조 언니가 로리랑 어울리면서 기분이 좋아질 때까지는 아무 말도 하지 말고. 그러다 조용한 틈을 봐서 그냥 입맞춤을 하든지, 뭐든 다정한 일을 해 봐. 그럼 진심으로 다시 사이좋게 지내려 할 거야, 틀림없이.\""]], [["\"I'll try,\"", "\"해 볼게.\" 에이미가 말했다."], ["said Amy, for the advice suited her; and, after a flurry to get ready, she ran after the friends, who were just disappearing over the hill.", "그 조언이 마음에 들었기 때문이다. 부랴부랴 채비를 마친 뒤, 에이미는 마침 언덕 너머로 사라지려는 두 사람을 뒤쫓아 달려갔다."]], [["It was not far to the river, but both were ready before Amy reached them.", "강까지는 멀지 않았지만, 에이미가 따라잡기도 전에 둘은 채비를 다 마친 상태였다."], ["Jo saw her coming, and turned her back; Laurie did not see, for he was carefully skating along the shore, sounding the ice, for a warm spell had preceded the cold snap.", "조는 에이미가 오는 것을 보고 등을 돌려 버렸다. 로리는 보지 못했는데, 추위가 닥치기 전에 따뜻한 날씨가 며칠 이어졌던 탓에, 강가를 따라 조심조심 스케이트를 지치며 얼음 상태를 살피고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["\"I'll go on to the first bend, and see if it's all right, before we begin to race,\"", "\"경주를 시작하기 전에, 첫 번째 굽이까지 가서 괜찮은지 봐 둘게.\""], ["Amy heard him say, as he shot away, looking like a young Russian, in his fur-trimmed coat and cap.", "로리가 모피로 가장자리를 두른 외투와 모자 차림으로 어린 러시아 소년처럼 쏜살같이 달려 나가며 하는 말이 에이미의 귀에 들렸다."]], [["Jo heard Amy panting after her run, stamping her feet and blowing her fingers, as she tried to put her skates on; but Jo never turned, and went slowly zigzagging down the river, taking a bitter, unhappy sort of satisfaction in her sister's troubles.", "조는 에이미가 달려온 탓에 숨을 헐떡이고, 발을 동동 구르며 손가락에 입김을 불면서 스케이트를 신으려고 애쓰는 소리를 들었다. 하지만 조는 돌아보지 않고, 강을 따라 천천히 지그재그로 미끄러져 내려갔다."], ["She had cherished her anger till it grew strong, and took possession of her, as evil thoughts and feelings always do, unless cast out at once.", "동생이 곤란해하는 모습에서 씁쓸하고 불행한 만족감을 느끼면서. 조는 노여움을 품고 또 품어서 그것이 점점 강해지도록 내버려 두었고, 결국 노여움이 조를 사로잡고 말았다."], ["As Laurie turned the bend, he shouted back,--", "사악한 생각과 감정이란 곧바로 내쫓지 않으면 늘 그렇게 마음을 차지해 버리는 법이다. 로리가 굽이를 돌면서 뒤돌아 외쳤다."]], [["\"Keep near the shore; it isn't safe in the middle.\"", "\"강가 가까이 붙어! 한가운데는 위험해!\""]], [["Jo heard, but Amy was just struggling to her feet, and did not catch a word.", "조에게는 들렸지만, 에이미는 마침 안간힘을 쓰며 일어서던 참이라 한마디도 알아듣지 못했다."], ["Jo glanced over her shoulder, and the little demon she was harboring said in her ear,--", "조가 어깨 너머로 흘끗 보았더니, 마음속에 품고 있던 작은 악마가 귓가에 속삭였다."]], [["\"No matter whether she heard or not, let her take care of herself.\"", "\"걔가 들었든 안 들었든 무슨 상관이야. 자기 일은 자기가 알아서 챙기라지.\""]], [["Laurie had vanished round the bend; Jo was just at the turn, and Amy, far behind, striking out toward the smoother ice in the middle of the river. For a minute Jo stood still, with a strange feeling at her heart; then she resolved to go on, but something held and turned her round, just in time to see Amy throw up her hands and go down, with the sudden crash of rotten ice, the splash of water, and a cry that made Jo's heart stand still with fear. She tried to call Laurie, but her voice was gone; she tried to rush forward, but her feet seemed to have no strength in them; and, for a second, she could only stand motionless, staring, with a terror-stricken face, at the little blue hood above the black water. Something rushed swiftly by her, and Laurie's voice cried out,--", "로리는 굽이를 돌아 사라진 뒤였다. 조는 막 굽이에 다다른 참이었고, 한참 뒤처져 있던 에이미는 강 한가운데의 더 매끄러운 얼음을 향해 나아가고 있었다. 잠시 조는 가슴에 이상한 느낌이 든 채 가만히 서 있었다. 그러다 계속 가기로 마음먹었지만, 무언가가 조를 붙들어 돌려세웠다. 마침 그때, 에이미가 두 팔을 번쩍 치켜들며 가라앉는 모습이 보였다. 썩은 얼음이 갑작스레 쩍 갈라지는 소리, 물이 튀는 소리, 그리고 조의 심장을 두려움으로 멎게 하는 비명. 조는 로리를 부르려 했지만 목소리가 나오지 않았고, 앞으로 달려가려 했지만 발에 힘이 하나도 없는 것 같았다. 한순간, 조는 그저 꼼짝 못 한 채, 공포에 질린 얼굴로 검은 물 위에 떠 있는 작고 파란 모자만 멍하니 바라볼 수밖에 없었다. 무언가가 조의 곁을 휙 스쳐 지나갔고, 로리의 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["\"Bring a rail; quick, quick!\"", "\"가로대 가져와! 빨리, 빨리!\""]], [["How she did it, she never knew; but for the next few minutes she worked as if possessed, blindly obeying Laurie, who was quite self-possessed, and, lying flat, held Amy up by his arm and hockey till Jo dragged a rail from the fence, and together they got the child out, more frightened than hurt.", "어떻게 해냈는지 조 자신도 알지 못했다. 하지만 그 뒤 몇 분 동안 조는 무언가에 홀린 듯이, 로리가 시키는 대로 정신없이 움직였다. 로리는 무척 침착하게, 몸을 납작 엎드린 채 팔과 하키 채로 에이미를 받쳐 들고 있었고, 그사이 조가 울타리에서 가로대를 뽑아 왔다. 둘이 힘을 합쳐 아이를 끌어냈는데, 다친 것보다는 겁에 질린 정도였다."]], [["\"Now then, we must walk her home as fast as we can; pile our things on her, while I get off these confounded skates,\" cried Laurie, wrapping his coat round Amy, and tugging away at the straps, which never seemed so intricate before.", "\"자, 이제 최대한 빨리 데리고 걸어가야 해. 우리 옷가지를 죄다 이 애한테 덮어 줘. 그동안 나는 이 빌어먹을 스케이트를 벗을 테니까.\" 로리가 외쳤다. 그러면서 외투로 에이미를 감싸고는, 어느 때보다 더 풀기 어렵게 느껴지는 끈을 잡아당겼다."]], [["Shivering, dripping, and crying, they got Amy home; and, after an exciting time of it, she fell asleep, rolled in blankets, before a hot fire.", "부들부들 떨고, 물을 뚝뚝 흘리고, 울먹이면서 그들은 에이미를 집으로 데려왔다. 한바탕 소동을 치른 뒤, 에이미는 활활 타오르는 난롯불 앞에서 담요에 둘둘 말린 채 잠이 들었다."], ["During the bustle Jo had scarcely spoken; but flown about, looking pale and wild, with her things half off, her dress torn, and her hands cut and bruised by ice and rails, and refractory buckles.", "그 북새통 내내 조는 거의 입을 열지 않았다. 그저 창백하고 넋이 나간 얼굴로 이리저리 뛰어다녔는데, 옷은 반쯤 벗겨졌고, 드레스는 찢어졌으며, 손은 얼음과 가로대와 말 안 듣는 버클에 베이고 멍이 들어 있었다."], ["When Amy was comfortably asleep, the house quiet, and Mrs. March sitting by the bed, she called Jo to her, and began to bind up the hurt hands.", "에이미가 편안히 잠들고 집 안이 조용해지자, 침대 곁에 앉아 있던 마치 부인이 조를 불러서는 상처 난 손을 싸매 주기 시작했다."]], [["\"Are you sure she is safe?\"", "\"정말 괜찮은 거예요?\""], ["whispered Jo, looking remorsefully at the golden head, which might have been swept away from her sight forever under the treacherous ice.", "조는 황금빛 머리를 후회 어린 눈길로 바라보며 속삭였다. 그 머리는 음흉한 얼음 밑으로 영영 자기 눈앞에서 휩쓸려 사라질 뻔하지 않았던가."]], [["\"Quite safe, dear; she is not hurt, and won't even take cold, I think, you were so sensible in covering and getting her home quickly,\"", "\"아주 괜찮단다, 얘야. 다친 데도 없고, 감기조차 안 걸릴 것 같구나."], ["replied her mother cheerfully.", "네가 그렇게 분별 있게 잘 덮어서 얼른 집으로 데려왔으니 말이다.\" 어머니가 밝게 대답했다."]], [["\"Laurie did it all; I only let her go. Mother, if she _should_ die, it would be my fault\"; and Jo dropped down beside the bed, in a passion of penitent tears, telling all that had happened, bitterly condemning her hardness of heart, and sobbing out her gratitude for being spared the heavy punishment which might have come upon her.", "\"전부 로리가 한 거예요. 저는 그냥 에이미를 내버려 뒀고요. 엄마, 만약에 에이미가 죽기라도 했으면, 그건 다 제 잘못이었을 거예요.\" 조는 침대 곁에 털썩 주저앉아, 뉘우치는 눈물을 쏟으며 그동안 있었던 일을 전부 털어놓았다. 자기 마음이 모질었던 것을 호되게 자책하면서, 자기에게 닥쳤을지도 모르는 무거운 벌을 면하게 된 데 대한 고마움을 흐느끼며 토해 냈다."]], [["\"It's my dreadful temper!", "\"제 이 끔찍한 성질 때문이에요!"], ["I try to cure it; I think I have, and then it breaks out worse than ever.", "고치려고 애쓰는데, 고쳤다 싶으면 또 전보다 더 심하게 터져 나와요."], ["O mother, what shall I do?", "아, 엄마, 어떡하면 좋아요?"], ["what shall I do?\"", "어떡하면 좋죠?\""], ["cried poor Jo, in despair.", "가엾은 조가 절망에 빠져 외쳤다."]], [["\"Watch and pray, dear; never get tired of trying; and never think it is impossible to conquer your fault,\"", "\"깨어 있고 기도하렴, 얘야. 노력하는 일에 결코 지치지 말고, 네 결점을 이겨 내는 일이 불가능하다고는 절대 생각하지 말거라.\""], ["said Mrs.", "마치 부인이 헝클어진 머리를 자기 어깨로 끌어당기며 말했다."], ["March, drawing the blowzy head to her shoulder, and kissing the wet cheek so tenderly that Jo cried harder than ever.", "그러고는 젖은 뺨에 어찌나 다정하게 입을 맞추는지, 조는 그만 더 서럽게 울고 말았다."]], [["\"You don't know, you can't guess how bad it is!", "\"엄마는 모르세요, 이게 얼마나 나쁜지 짐작도 못 하실 거예요!"], ["It seems as if I could do anything when I'm in a passion; I get so savage, I could hurt any one, and enjoy it.", "욱하면 무슨 짓이든 할 수 있을 것 같아요. 어찌나 사나워지는지, 누굴 해치고도 그걸 즐길 수 있을 것 같다고요."], ["I'm afraid I _shall_ do something dreadful some day, and spoil my life, and make everybody hate me.", "언젠가는 끔찍한 짓을 저질러서 제 인생을 망치고, 다들 절 미워하게 만들까 봐 무서워요."], ["O mother, help me, do help me!\"", "아, 엄마, 도와주세요, 제발 좀 도와주세요!\""]], [["\"I will, my child, I will.", "\"도와주마, 얘야, 도와주고말고."], ["Don't cry so bitterly, but remember this day, and resolve, with all your soul, that you will never know another like it.", "그렇게 서럽게 울지는 말고, 오늘을 기억하렴. 그리고 두 번 다시 이런 날을 겪지 않겠다고, 온 마음을 다해 결심하렴."], ["Jo, dear, we all have our temptations, some far greater than yours, and it often takes us all our lives to conquer them.", "조야, 우리 모두에게는 유혹이 있단다. 어떤 건 네 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 것도 있지."], ["You think your temper is the worst in the world; but mine used to be just like it.\"", "그리고 그걸 이겨 내는 데 평생이 걸리는 경우도 많아. 너는 네 성질이 세상에서 제일 못됐다고 여기겠지만, 내 성질도 꼭 그랬단다.\""]], [["\"Yours, mother?", "\"엄마가요?"], ["Why, you are never angry!\"", "아니, 엄마는 화내는 법이 없잖아요!\""], ["and, for the moment, Jo forgot remorse in surprise.", "그 순간 조는 놀란 나머지 후회마저 잊어버렸다."]], [["\"I've been trying to cure it for forty years, and have only succeeded in controlling it. I am angry nearly every day of my life, Jo; but I have learned not to show it; and I still hope to learn not to feel it, though it may take me another forty years to do so.\"", "\"마흔 해 동안 그걸 고치려고 애써 왔지만, 겨우 다스리는 데까지밖에 못 왔단다. 나는 거의 매일같이 화가 난단다, 조야. 다만 그걸 드러내지 않는 법을 배웠을 뿐이지. 그리고 언젠가는 화를 느끼지 않는 법까지 배우길 여전히 바라고 있어. 비록 그러기까지 또 마흔 해가 걸릴지도 모르지만 말이다.\""]], [["The patience and the humility of the face she loved so well was a better lesson to Jo than the wisest lecture, the sharpest reproof.", "조가 그토록 사랑하는 그 얼굴에 어린 인내와 겸손은, 그 어떤 현명한 훈계나 따끔한 꾸지람보다 더 좋은 가르침이 되었다. 자기에게 베풀어진 공감과 신뢰에 조는 곧바로 위로를 받았다."], ["She felt comforted at once by the sympathy and confidence given her; the knowledge that her mother had a fault like hers, and tried to mend it, made her own easier to bear and strengthened her resolution to cure it; though forty years seemed rather a long time to watch and pray, to a girl of fifteen.", "어머니에게도 자기와 같은 결점이 있고, 그걸 고치려고 애써 왔다는 사실을 아니, 자신의 결점도 한결 견디기 쉬워졌고 그걸 고치겠다는 결심도 더 단단해졌다. 비록 깨어 있고 기도하는 데 마흔 해라니, 열다섯 살 소녀에게는 꽤 긴 세월처럼 느껴지긴 했지만 말이다."]], [["\"Mother, are you angry when you fold your lips tight together, and go out of the room sometimes, when Aunt March scolds, or people worry you?\"", "\"엄마, 마치 대고모님이 야단치시거나 사람들이 엄마를 성가시게 할 때, 가끔 입술을 꾹 다물고 방을 나가시잖아요. 그게 화가 나신 거예요?\""], ["asked Jo, feeling nearer and dearer to her mother than ever before.", "조는 그 어느 때보다 어머니가 가깝고 소중하게 느껴져서 물었다."]], [["\"Yes, I've learned to check the hasty words that rise to my lips; and when I feel that they mean to break out against my will, I just go away a minute, and give myself a little shake, for being so weak and wicked,\"", "\"그래, 입가로 치밀어 오르는 성급한 말들을 억누르는 법을 배웠단다. 그 말들이 내 뜻과는 상관없이 터져 나오려 한다 싶으면, 잠깐 자리를 피해서, 그렇게 나약하고 못된 나 자신을 몸을 살짝 흔들며 다잡는 거지.\""], ["answered Mrs.", "마치 부인이 한숨 섞인 미소를 지으며 대답했다."], ["March, with a sigh and a smile, as she smoothed and fastened up Jo's dishevelled hair.", "그러면서 헝클어진 조의 머리를 매만지고 단정히 묶어 주었다."]], [["\"How did you learn to keep still?", "\"가만히 참는 법을 어떻게 배우셨어요? 바로 그게 제 고민이거든요."], ["That is what troubles me--for the sharp words fly out before I know what I'm about; and the more I say the worse I get, till it's a pleasure to hurt people's feelings, and say dreadful things.", "무슨 말을 하는지 미처 깨닫기도 전에 날카로운 말들이 튀어나오니까요. 게다가 말을 하면 할수록 더 심해져서, 나중엔 남의 마음을 다치게 하고 끔찍한 말을 하는 게 즐거워질 지경이에요."], ["Tell me how you do it, Marmee dear.\"", "어떻게 하시는 건지 가르쳐 주세요, 엄마.\""]], [["\"My good mother used to help me--\"", "\"우리 착한 어머니께서 나를 도와주시곤 했단다.\""]], [["\"As you do us--\"", "\"지금 엄마가 우릴 도와주시는 것처럼요.\""], ["interrupted Jo, with a grateful kiss.", "조가 고마운 마음에 입을 맞추며 끼어들었다."]], [["\"But I lost her when I was a little older than you are, and for years had to struggle on alone, for I was too proud to confess my weakness to any one else. I had a hard time, Jo, and shed a good many bitter tears over my failures; for, in spite of my efforts, I never seemed to get on. Then your father came, and I was so happy that I found it easy to be good. But by and by, when I had four little daughters round me, and we were poor, then the old trouble began again; for I am not patient by nature, and it tried me very much to see my children wanting anything.\"", "\"하지만 지금 네 나이보다 조금 더 먹었을 무렵에 그분을 여의었고, 그 뒤로 여러 해 동안 홀로 싸워 나가야 했단다. 다른 누구에게 내 약점을 털어놓기엔 자존심이 너무 셌거든. 힘든 시절이었어, 조야. 내 실패 때문에 쓰디쓴 눈물도 숱하게 흘렸지. 아무리 애를 써도 도무지 나아지는 것 같지 않았으니까. 그러다 네 아버지를 만났고, 어찌나 행복했던지 착해지기가 한결 쉬워지더구나. 그런데 얼마 안 가서, 어린 딸 넷을 곁에 두고 살림은 가난하니, 옛날의 그 골칫거리가 또 시작됐단다. 나는 천성이 참을성 있는 사람이 아니거든. 내 아이들이 뭔가를 갖고 싶어 하면서도 갖지 못하는 걸 보는 게 너무도 괴로웠어.\""]], [["\"Poor mother!", "\"가엾은 엄마!"], ["what helped you then?\"", "그땐 뭐가 도움이 됐어요?\""]], [["\"Your father, Jo.", "\"네 아버지란다, 조야. 그분은 결코 참을성을 잃는 법이 없고, 의심하거나 불평하는 법도 없단다."], ["He never loses patience,--never doubts or complains,--but always hopes, and works and waits so cheerfully, that one is ashamed to do otherwise before him.", "늘 희망을 품고, 어찌나 명랑하게 일하며 기다리시는지, 그분 앞에서는 차마 다르게 굴기가 부끄러워지지. 그분은 나를 돕고 위로해 주셨고, 내 딸들이 갖추기를 바라는 모든 미덕을 내가 먼저 실천하려고 애써야 한다는 걸 일깨워 주셨단다."], ["He helped and comforted me, and showed me that I must try to practise all the virtues I would have my little girls possess, for I was their example.", "내가 너희의 본보기였으니까. 나 자신을 위해서보다 너희를 위해서 노력하는 게 더 쉬웠어."], ["It was easier to try for your sakes than for my own; a startled or surprised look from one of you, when I spoke sharply, rebuked me more than any words could have done; and the love, respect, and confidence of my children was the sweetest reward I could receive for my efforts to be the woman I would have them copy.\"", "내가 날카롭게 말할 때 너희 중 누군가가 깜짝 놀라거나 어리둥절해하던 그 표정이, 그 어떤 말보다도 나를 더 호되게 꾸짖었단다. 그리고 내 아이들의 사랑과 존경과 신뢰야말로, 너희가 본받았으면 하는 그런 여자가 되려고 애쓴 내 노력에 대한 가장 달콤한 보상이었지.\""]], [["\"O mother, if I'm ever half as good as you, I shall be satisfied,\"", "\"아, 엄마, 제가 언젠가 엄마의 반만큼이라도 착해진다면, 그걸로 만족할 거예요.\""], ["cried Jo, much touched.", "조가 크게 감동하여 외쳤다."]], [["\"I hope you will be a great deal better, dear; but you must keep watch over your 'bosom enemy,'", "\"네가 훨씬 더 나은 사람이 되길 바란단다, 얘야. 하지만 네 아버지가 '가슴속의 적'이라고 부르는 그것을 늘 경계해야 해."], ["as father calls it, or it may sadden, if not spoil your life.", "안 그러면 네 인생을 망치진 않더라도 슬프게 만들 수 있으니까."], ["You have had a warning; remember it, and try with heart and soul to master this quick temper, before it brings you greater sorrow and regret than you have known to-day.\"", "너는 한 번 경고를 받은 셈이야. 그걸 기억하고, 오늘 겪은 것보다 더 큰 슬픔과 후회를 불러오기 전에, 온 마음과 영혼을 다해 그 욱하는 성질을 다스리려고 애쓰렴.\""]], [["\"I will try, mother; I truly will.", "\"노력할게요, 엄마. 정말로 그럴게요."], ["But you must help me, remind me, and keep me from flying out.", "하지만 엄마가 도와주셔야 해요. 일깨워 주시고, 제가 욱하지 않게 막아 주세요."], ["I used to see father sometimes put his finger on his lips, and look at you with a very kind, but sober face, and you always folded your lips tight or went away: was he reminding you then?\"", "예전에 아버지가 가끔 손가락을 입술에 갖다 대고, 무척 다정하지만 진지한 얼굴로 엄마를 바라보시는 걸 봤어요. 그러면 엄마는 늘 입술을 꼭 다물거나 자리를 피하셨고요."], ["asked Jo softly.", "그게 그때 아버지가 엄마를 일깨워 주시던 거였어요?\" 조가 부드럽게 물었다."]], [["\"Yes; I asked him to help me so, and he never forgot it, but saved me from many a sharp word by that little gesture and kind look.\"", "\"그래. 내가 그렇게 도와 달라고 부탁했단다. 그분은 결코 그걸 잊지 않으셨고, 그 작은 몸짓과 다정한 눈길로 내가 날카로운 말을 숱하게 내뱉지 않도록 막아 주셨지.\""]], [["Jo saw that her mother's eyes filled and her lips trembled, as she spoke; and, fearing that she had said too much, she whispered anxiously, \"Was it wrong to watch you, and to speak of it?", "조는 어머니가 그렇게 말하면서 눈에 눈물이 차오르고 입술이 떨리는 것을 보았다. 자기가 말을 너무 많이 한 게 아닌가 싶어, 조는 걱정스레 속삭였다."], ["I didn't mean to be rude, but it's so comfortable to say all I think to you, and feel so safe and happy here.\"", "\"제가 엄마를 지켜보고, 또 그 얘길 꺼낸 게 잘못이었어요? 무례하게 굴려던 건 아니었는데, 엄마한테는 생각하는 걸 죄다 말하는 게 너무 편하고, 여기 있으면 마음이 너무 든든하고 행복해서요.\""]], [["\"My Jo, you may say anything to your mother, for it is my greatest happiness and pride to feel that my girls confide in me, and know how much I love them.\"", "\"우리 조, 엄마한테는 무슨 말이든 해도 된단다. 내 딸들이 나를 믿어 주고, 내가 자기들을 얼마나 사랑하는지 알아준다고 느끼는 게, 나에게는 가장 큰 행복이자 자랑이거든.\""]], [["\"I thought I'd grieved you.\"", "\"엄마를 슬프게 해 드린 줄 알았어요.\""]], [["\"No, dear; but speaking of father reminded me how much I miss him, how much I owe him, and how faithfully I should watch and work to keep his little daughters safe and good for him.\"", "\"아니란다, 얘야. 다만 네 아버지 얘기를 하다 보니, 그분이 얼마나 그리운지, 그분께 얼마나 큰 빚을 졌는지, 그리고 그분의 어린 딸들을 안전하고 착하게 지켜 드리려고 내가 얼마나 한결같이 깨어 있고 애써야 하는지가 새삼 떠올랐을 뿐이야.\""]], [["\"Yet you told him to go, mother, and didn't cry when he went, and never complain now, or seem as if you needed any help,\"", "\"그래도 엄마는 아버지더러 가라고 하셨고, 떠나실 때도 울지 않으셨고, 지금도 불평 한마디 없고, 도움이 필요한 기색도 전혀 없잖아요.\""], ["said Jo, wondering.", "조가 의아해하며 말했다."]], [["\"I gave my best to the country I love, and kept my tears till he was gone.", "\"내가 사랑하는 나라에 내가 가진 가장 소중한 것을 바쳤고, 그분이 떠나실 때까지 눈물을 참았단다."], ["Why should I complain, when we both have merely done our duty and will surely be the happier for it in the end?", "우리 둘 다 그저 우리의 본분을 다했을 뿐이고, 끝내는 분명 그 덕분에 더 행복해질 텐데, 내가 왜 불평하겠니?"], ["If I don't seem to need help, it is because I have a better friend, even than father, to comfort and sustain me.", "내가 도움이 필요 없어 보인다면, 그건 나에게 네 아버지보다도 더 좋은 친구가 있어서, 그분이 나를 위로하고 지탱해 주시기 때문이란다."], ["My child, the troubles and temptations of your life are beginning, and may be many; but you can overcome and outlive them all if you learn to feel the strength and tenderness of your Heavenly Father as you do that of your earthly one.", "얘야, 네 인생의 시련과 유혹은 이제 막 시작됐고, 앞으로 숱하게 많을지도 몰라. 하지만 이 땅의 아버지에게서 느끼는 것처럼 하늘에 계신 아버지의 힘과 다정함을 느끼는 법을 배운다면, 너는 그 모든 걸 이겨 내고 끝까지 살아남을 수 있단다."], ["The more you love and trust Him, the nearer you will feel to Him, and the less you will depend on human power and wisdom.", "그분을 사랑하고 믿을수록, 너는 그분을 더 가까이 느끼게 되고, 인간의 힘과 지혜에 덜 기대게 될 거야."], ["His love and care never tire or change, can never be taken from you, but may become the source of life-long peace, happiness, and strength.", "그분의 사랑과 보살핌은 결코 지치거나 변하지 않고, 결코 네게서 빼앗길 수도 없으며, 도리어 평생토록 이어지는 평화와 행복과 힘의 원천이 될 수 있단다."], ["Believe this heartily, and go to God with all your little cares, and hopes, and sins, and sorrows, as freely and confidingly as you come to your mother.\"", "이걸 진심으로 믿고, 네 사소한 걱정과 희망과 죄와 슬픔을 모두 안고서, 엄마에게 오듯 그렇게 거리낌 없이 믿음을 갖고 하느님께 가렴.\""]], [["Jo's only answer was to hold her mother close, and, in the silence which followed, the sincerest prayer she had ever prayed left her heart without words; for in that sad, yet happy hour, she had learned not only the bitterness of remorse and despair, but the sweetness of self-denial and self-control; and, led by her mother's hand, she had drawn nearer to the Friend who welcomes every child with a love stronger than that of any father, tenderer than that of any mother.", "조의 유일한 대답은 어머니를 꼭 끌어안는 것이었다. 그리고 뒤이은 침묵 속에서, 조가 일찍이 드려 본 적 없는 가장 진실한 기도가, 말없이 그 마음에서 흘러나왔다. 슬프지만 행복한 그 시간에, 조는 후회와 절망의 쓰라림뿐만 아니라 자기를 누르고 다스리는 일의 달콤함도 배웠으니까. 그리고 어머니의 손에 이끌려, 그 어떤 아버지의 사랑보다 강하고 그 어떤 어머니의 사랑보다 다정한 마음으로 모든 아이를 맞아 주시는 그 친구에게 한 걸음 더 다가갔던 것이다."]], [["Amy stirred, and sighed in her sleep; and, as if eager to begin at once to mend her fault, Jo looked up with an expression on her face which it had never worn before.", "에이미가 몸을 뒤척이며 잠결에 한숨을 쉬었다. 그러자 마치 당장이라도 자기 잘못을 바로잡고 싶다는 듯, 조가 고개를 들었는데, 그 얼굴에는 일찍이 한 번도 떠오른 적 없는 표정이 어려 있었다."]], [["\"I let the sun go down on my anger; I wouldn't forgive her, and to-day, if it hadn't been for Laurie, it might have been too late!", "\"노여움을 품은 채 해가 지게 내버려 뒀어. 끝내 용서하지 않았고."], ["How could I be so wicked?\"", "오늘 로리가 아니었으면, 영영 너무 늦어 버렸을지도 몰라!"], ["said Jo, half aloud, as she leaned over her sister, softly stroking the wet hair scattered on the pillow.", "내가 어쩜 그렇게 못됐을까?\" 조는 동생 위로 몸을 기울여, 베개 위에 흩어진 젖은 머리카락을 부드럽게 쓰다듬으며 반쯤 소리 내어 말했다."]], [["As if she heard, Amy opened her eyes, and held out her arms, with a smile that went straight to Jo's heart.", "그 말이 들리기라도 한 듯, 에이미가 눈을 떴다. 그러고는 조의 가슴에 곧장 와닿는 미소를 지으며 두 팔을 내밀었다."], ["Neither said a word, but they hugged one another close, in spite of the blankets, and everything was forgiven and forgotten in one hearty kiss.", "둘 다 아무 말도 하지 않았지만, 담요에 싸인 채로 서로를 꼭 끌어안았고, 진심 어린 입맞춤 한 번으로 모든 것이 용서되고 잊혔다. 9장"]], [["# MEG GOES TO VANITY FAIR.", "# MEG GOES TO VANITY FAIR."]], [["\"I do think it was the most fortunate thing in the world that those children should have the measles just now,\"", "메그, 허영의 시장에 가다\n\"바로 이맘때 그 애들이 홍역에 걸렸다는 게, 세상에 이렇게 운 좋은 일이 또 있을까 싶어.\""], ["said Meg, one April day, as she stood packing the \"go abroady\"", "어느 4월의 어느 날, 메그가 자기 방에서 동생들에게 둘러싸인 채 \"나들이용\""], ["trunk in her room, surrounded by her sisters.", "트렁크를 꾸리며 서서 말했다."]], [["\"And so nice of Annie Moffat not to forget her promise.", "\"게다가 애니 모펏이 약속을 잊지 않다니 정말 고마운 일이야."], ["A whole fortnight of fun will be regularly splendid,\"", "꼬박 두 주 동안 즐겁게 놀 텐데, 더없이 근사할 거야.\""], ["replied Jo, looking like a windmill, as she folded skirts with her long arms.", "조가 대답했다. 긴 팔로 치마를 개키는 모습이 꼭 풍차 같았다."]], [["\"And such lovely weather; I'm so glad of that,\"", "\"날씨도 어쩜 이렇게 좋을까. 그게 너무 다행이야.\""], ["added Beth, tidily sorting neck and hair ribbons in her best box, lent for the great occasion.", "베스가 거들었다. 이 큰일을 위해 빌려 온 자기의 가장 좋은 상자에, 목에 두르는 리본과 머리 리본을 정성껏 정리하면서."]], [["\"I wish I was going to have a fine time, and wear all these nice things,\"", "\"나도 멋지게 놀면서 이 예쁜 것들을 죄다 입어 봤으면 좋겠다.\""], ["said Amy, with her mouth full of pins, as she artistically replenished her sister's cushion.", "에이미가 핀을 입에 한가득 문 채로 말하며, 언니의 바늘꽂이를 솜씨 좋게 다시 채워 넣었다."]], [["\"I wish you were all going; but, as you can't, I shall keep my adventures to tell you when I come back.", "\"너희도 다 같이 가면 좋겠는데. 그럴 수가 없으니, 돌아와서 내 모험담을 들려줄게."], ["I'm sure it's the least I can do, when you have been so kind, lending me things, and helping me get ready,\"", "너희가 이렇게 친절하게 물건도 빌려주고 채비도 도와줬으니, 그 정도는 해 줘야지.\""], ["said Meg, glancing round the room at the very simple outfit, which seemed nearly perfect in their eyes.", "메그가 방을 둘러보며 말했다. 무척 소박한 옷가지였지만, 동생들 눈에는 거의 완벽해 보였다."]], [["\"What did mother give you out of the treasure-box?\"", "\"엄마가 보물 상자에서 뭘 주셨어?\" 에이미가 물었다."], ["asked Amy, who had not been present at the opening of a certain cedar chest, in which Mrs.", "에이미는 마치 부인이 삼나무 궤를 열 때 그 자리에 없었던 것이다."], ["March kept a few relics of past splendor, as gifts for her girls when the proper time came.", "그 궤에는 지난날의 화려했던 시절의 유품 몇 가지가 들어 있었는데, 부인은 때가 되면 딸들에게 선물로 주려고 그것들을 간직해 두고 있었다."]], [["\"A pair of silk stockings, that pretty carved fan, and a lovely blue sash.", "\"비단 양말 한 켤레랑, 저 예쁜 조각 부채, 그리고 사랑스러운 파란 띠."], ["I wanted the violet silk; but there isn't time to make it over, so I must be contented with my old tarlatan.\"", "난 보라색 비단이 갖고 싶었는데, 그걸 고쳐 입을 시간이 없으니, 낡은 모슬린으로 만족해야지.\""]], [["\"It will look nicely over my new muslin skirt, and the sash will set it off beautifully.", "\"내 새 모슬린 치마 위에 받쳐 입으면 잘 어울릴 거야. 게다가 그 띠를 두르면 아주 돋보일걸."], ["I wish I hadn't smashed my coral bracelet, for you might have had it,\"", "내 산호 팔찌를 깨뜨리지만 않았어도 언니한테 줬을 텐데, 아쉽다.\""], ["said Jo, who loved to give and lend, but whose possessions were usually too dilapidated to be of much use.", "조가 말했다. 조는 무엇이든 주거나 빌려주기를 좋아했지만, 가진 물건들이 대개 너무 낡아서 별 쓸모가 없었다."]], [["\"There is a lovely old-fashioned pearl set in the treasure-box; but mother said real flowers were the prettiest ornament for a young girl, and Laurie promised to send me all I want,\" replied Meg. \"Now, let me see; there's my new gray walking-suit--just curl up the feather in my hat, Beth,--then my poplin, for Sunday, and the small party,--it looks heavy for spring, doesn't it? The violet silk would be so nice; oh, dear!\"", "\"보물 상자에 사랑스러운 옛날식 진주 장신구 한 벌이 있긴 한데, 엄마가 그러시더라, 어린 처녀한테는 생화가 가장 예쁜 장식이라고. 게다가 로리가 내가 원하는 만큼 다 보내 주겠다고 약속했어.\" 메그가 대답했다. \"자, 어디 보자. 새로 산 회색 외출복이 있고. 베스, 내 모자에 달린 깃털 좀 동그랗게 말아 줄래? 그리고 일요일이랑 작은 파티에 입을 포플린이 있는데, 봄옷치고는 좀 무거워 보이지 않니? 보라색 비단이면 참 좋았을 텐데. 아유, 정말!\""]], [["\"Never mind; you've got the tarlatan for the big party, and you always look like an angel in white,\" said Amy, brooding over the little store of finery in which her soul delighted.", "\"괜찮아. 큰 파티에는 모슬린 드레스가 있잖아. 게다가 언니는 흰옷만 입으면 늘 천사 같아 보여.\" 에이미가 말했다. 자기가 마음속으로 좋아해 마지않는 그 자그마한 고운 옷가지들을 골똘히 들여다보면서."]], [["\"It isn't low-necked, and it doesn't sweep enough, but it will have to do.", "\"목이 깊이 파인 것도 아니고, 자락도 충분히 끌리지 않지만, 그걸로 버텨야지."], ["My blue house-dress looks so well, turned and freshly trimmed, that I feel as if I'd got a new one.", "내 파란 평상복은 안팎을 뒤집어서 새로 단을 손봤더니 어찌나 멀쩡해 보이는지, 마치 새 옷이라도 생긴 기분이야."], ["My silk sacque isn't a bit the fashion, and my bonnet doesn't look like Sallie's; I didn't like to say anything, but I was sadly disappointed in my umbrella.", "내 비단 재킷은 도무지 유행에 안 맞고, 내 보닛도 샐리 것 같지가 않아. 뭐라고 말은 안 했지만, 우산은 정말 실망스러웠어."], ["I told mother black, with a white handle, but she forgot, and bought a green one, with a yellowish handle.", "엄마한테 흰 손잡이가 달린 검정으로 해 달라고 했는데, 깜박하시고는 누르스름한 손잡이가 달린 초록색을 사 오셨지 뭐야."], ["It's strong and neat, so I ought not to complain, but I know I shall feel ashamed of it beside Annie's silk one with a gold top,\"", "튼튼하고 깔끔하니까 불평하면 안 되겠지만, 애니의 금색 꼭지가 달린 비단 우산 옆에 두면 분명히 부끄러울 거야.\""], ["sighed Meg, surveying the little umbrella with great disfavor.", "메그가 한숨을 쉬며 그 작은 우산을 무척 못마땅하게 훑어보았다."]], [["\"Change it,\"", "\"바꿔.\""], ["advised Jo.", "조가 권했다."]], [["\"I won't be so silly, or hurt Marmee's feelings, when she took so much pains to get my things.", "\"그렇게 어리석게 굴진 않을 거야. 엄마가 그렇게 애써서 내 물건들을 마련해 주셨는데, 엄마 마음을 상하게 할 순 없잖아."], ["It's a nonsensical notion of mine, and I'm not going to give up to it.", "다 내 어처구니없는 생각일 뿐이니까, 거기에 휘둘리진 않을 거야."], ["My silk stockings and two pairs of new gloves are my comfort.", "비단 양말이랑 새 장갑 두 켤레가 내 위안거리지."], ["You are a dear, to lend me yours, Jo.", "조, 네 걸 빌려주다니 정말 고마워."], ["I feel so rich, and sort of elegant, with two new pairs, and the old ones cleaned up for common;\"", "새 거 두 켤레에다, 낡은 건 깨끗이 손질해서 평소용으로 쓰면 되니까, 어쩐지 부자가 된 것 같고 우아해진 기분이야.\""], ["and Meg took a refreshing peep at her glove-box.", "메그가 장갑 상자를 흐뭇하게 들여다보았다."]], [["\"Annie Moffat has blue and pink bows on her night-caps; would you put some on mine?\"", "\"애니 모펏은 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자에 파란색이랑 분홍색 매듭을 달았던데, 내 것에도 좀 달아 줄래?\""], ["she asked, as Beth brought up a pile of snowy muslins, fresh from Hannah's hands.", "메그가 물었다. 마침 베스가 해나의 손을 갓 거친 새하얀 모슬린을 한 무더기 들고 왔다."]], [["\"No, I wouldn't; for the smart caps won't match the plain gowns, without any trimming on them.", "\"아니, 난 안 달겠어. 그 멋진 모자는 아무 장식 없는 수수한 잠옷이랑 안 어울리니까."], ["Poor folks shouldn't rig,\"", "가난한 사람은 함부로 치장하면 안 돼.\""], ["said Jo decidedly.", "조가 딱 잘라 말했다."]], [["\"I wonder if I shall _ever_ be happy enough to have real lace on my clothes, and bows on my caps?\"", "\"나도 언젠가는 옷에 진짜 레이스를 달고, 모자에 매듭을 달 만큼 형편이 좋아지는 날이 올까?\""], ["said Meg impatiently.", "메그가 안달하며 말했다."]], [["\"You said the other day that you'd be perfectly happy if you could only go to Annie Moffat's,\"", "\"언닌 며칠 전에 애니 모펏 집에 가기만 하면 더 바랄 게 없을 만큼 행복하겠다고 했잖아.\""], ["observed Beth, in her quiet way.", "베스가 특유의 조용한 말투로 일러 주었다."]], [["\"So I did!", "\"그랬지! 그래, 난 행복해."], ["Well, I _am_ happy, and I _won't_ fret; but it does seem as if the more one gets the more one wants, doesn't it?", "그러니까 투정 안 부릴 거야. 하지만 사람이란 가지면 가질수록 더 갖고 싶어지는 것 같지 않니?"], ["There, now, the trays are ready, and everything in but my ball-dress, which I shall leave for mother to pack,\"", "자, 이제 칸막이 칸은 다 채웠고, 무도회 드레스만 빼고 전부 넣었어. 그건 엄마한테 꾸려 달라고 남겨 둘래.\""], ["said Meg, cheering up, as she glanced from the half-filled trunk to the many-times pressed and mended white tarlatan, which she called her \"ball-dress,\"", "메그가 기운을 차리며 말했다. 그러고는 반쯤 채운 트렁크에서, 여러 번 다리미질하고 기워 입은 그 흰 모슬린 드레스로 눈길을 옮겼다."], ["with an important air.", "메그는 그 드레스를 자못 거창하게 자기의 \"무도회 드레스\"라고 불렀다."]], [["The next day was fine, and Meg departed, in style, for a fortnight of novelty and pleasure. Mrs.", "이튿날은 날씨가 좋았고, 메그는 두 주간의 새롭고 즐거운 나날을 향해 한껏 차려입고 떠났다."], ["March had consented to the visit rather reluctantly, fearing that Margaret would come back more discontented than she went.", "마치 부인은 이번 방문을 다소 마지못해 허락했다. 마거릿이 떠날 때보다 더 불만에 가득 차서 돌아올까 봐 걱정스러웠던 것이다."], ["But she had begged so hard, and Sallie had promised to take good care of her, and a little pleasure seemed so delightful after a winter of irksome work, that the mother yielded, and the daughter went to take her first taste of fashionable life.", "하지만 메그가 어찌나 간곡히 졸랐는지, 샐리도 잘 돌봐 주겠다고 약속한 데다, 지긋지긋한 일에 시달린 겨울 끝에 잠깐의 즐거움이 무척 달콤해 보이기도 해서, 어머니는 결국 뜻을 굽혔고, 딸은 처음으로 사교계 생활을 맛보러 떠났다."]], [["The Moffats _were_ very fashionable, and simple Meg was rather daunted, at first, by the splendor of the house and the elegance of its occupants.", "모펏 집안 사람들은 정말이지 무척 사교계다웠고, 소박한 메그는 처음에는 그 집의 으리으리함과 식구들의 우아함에 다소 주눅이 들었다."], ["But they were kindly people, in spite of the frivolous life they led, and soon put their guest at her ease.", "그러나 그들은 경박한 삶을 살기는 해도 친절한 사람들이어서, 이내 손님을 편안하게 해 주었다."], ["Perhaps Meg felt, without understanding why, that they were not particularly cultivated or intelligent people, and that all their gilding could not quite conceal the ordinary material of which they were made.", "어쩌면 메그는, 왜 그런지는 몰라도, 그들이 특별히 교양 있거나 똑똑한 사람들은 아니며, 그 모든 겉치레로도 그들의 평범한 본바탕을 다 가리지는 못한다는 것을 느꼈는지도 모른다."], ["It certainly was agreeable to fare sumptuously, drive in a fine carriage, wear her best frock every day, and do nothing but enjoy herself.", "호화롭게 먹고, 멋진 마차를 타고, 매일 가장 좋은 옷을 입고, 그저 즐기기만 하는 것은 분명 즐거운 일이었다. 그건 메그에게 딱 들어맞았다."], ["It suited her exactly; and soon she began to imitate the manners and conversation of those about her; to put on little airs and graces, use French phrases, crimp her hair, take in her dresses, and talk about the fashions as well as she could.", "그래서 이내 메그는 주위 사람들의 몸가짐과 말투를 흉내 내기 시작했다. 거드름을 피우며 우아한 척하고, 프랑스어 표현을 쓰고, 머리를 곱슬곱슬하게 지지고, 드레스를 몸에 꼭 맞게 줄이고, 할 수 있는 한 유행 이야기를 늘어놓았다."], ["The more she saw of Annie Moffat's pretty things, the more she envied her, and sighed to be rich.", "애니 모펏의 예쁜 물건들을 보면 볼수록 메그는 애니가 부러워졌고, 부자였으면 하고 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["Home now looked bare and dismal as she thought of it, work grew harder than ever, and she felt that she was a very destitute and much-injured girl, in spite of the new gloves and silk stockings.", "이제 집을 떠올리면 휑하고 음울해 보였고, 일은 어느 때보다 고되게 느껴졌으며, 새 장갑과 비단 양말이 있는데도 자기가 무척 가난하고 박복한 처녀라는 생각이 들었다."]], [["She had not much time for repining, however, for the three young girls were busily employed in \"having a good time.\"", "그러나 한탄할 틈은 그리 많지 않았다. 세 소녀가 \"신나게 노는\""], ["They shopped, walked, rode, and called all day; went to theatres and operas, or frolicked at home in the evening; for Annie had many friends, and knew how to entertain them.", "데 한창 바빴기 때문이다. 그들은 하루 종일 쇼핑을 하고, 산책하고, 마차를 타고, 여기저기 방문을 다녔다."], ["Her older sisters were very fine young ladies, and one was engaged, which was extremely interesting and romantic, Meg thought.", "극장과 오페라에 가거나, 저녁이면 집에서 떠들썩하게 놀았다. 애니에게는 친구가 많았고, 그들을 대접하는 법도 잘 알았다."], ["Mr. Moffat was a fat, jolly old gentleman, who knew her father; and Mrs.", "애니의 언니들은 무척 세련된 아가씨들이었는데, 그중 하나는 약혼한 사이여서, 메그가 보기에 그게 더없이 흥미롭고 낭만적이었다."], ["Moffat, a fat, jolly old lady, who took as great a fancy to Meg as her daughter had done.", "모펏 씨는 메그의 아버지를 아는, 뚱뚱하고 유쾌한 노신사였다. 모펏 부인도 뚱뚱하고 유쾌한 노부인이었는데, 자기 딸 못지않게 메그를 마음에 들어 했다."], ["Every one petted her; and \"Daisy,\" as they called her, was in a fair way to have her head turned.", "다들 메그를 떠받들었고, 그들이 \"데이지\"라고 부르는 메그는 자칫 우쭐해질 판이었다."]], [["When the evening for the \"small party\"", "\"작은 파티\" 열리는 저녁이 되자, 포플린은 영 어울리지 않는다는 걸 알게 되었다."], ["came, she found that the poplin wouldn't do at all, for the other girls were putting on thin dresses, and making themselves very fine indeed; so out came the tarlatan, looking older, limper, and shabbier than ever beside Sallie's crisp new one.", "다른 소녀들은 얇은 드레스를 차려입고 한껏 멋을 부렸기 때문이다. 그래서 모슬린 드레스를 꺼냈는데, 샐리의 빳빳한 새 드레스 옆에 두니 어느 때보다 낡고 후줄근하고 초라해 보였다."], ["Meg saw the girls glance at it and then at one another, and her cheeks began to burn, for, with all her gentleness, she was very proud.", "메그는 소녀들이 그 드레스를 흘끔거리다가 서로 눈짓을 주고받는 것을 보았고, 두 뺨이 화끈거리기 시작했다. 메그는 그토록 온순한데도 자존심만은 무척 셌던 것이다."], ["No one said a word about it, but Sallie offered to dress her hair, and Annie to tie her sash, and Belle, the engaged sister, praised her white arms; but in their kindness Meg saw only pity for her poverty, and her heart felt very heavy as she stood by herself, while the others laughed, chattered, and flew about like gauzy butterflies.", "누구도 그것에 대해 한마디도 하지 않았다. 샐리는 머리를 만져 주겠다고 했고, 애니는 띠를 매 주겠다고 했으며, 약혼한 언니인 벨은 메그의 흰 팔을 칭찬했다."], ["The hard, bitter feeling was getting pretty bad, when the maid brought in a box of flowers.", "하지만 메그는 그 친절 속에서 자기의 가난에 대한 동정밖에 보지 못했고, 다른 이들이 웃고 떠들며 얇은 천 나비처럼 이리저리 날아다니는 동안, 혼자 우두커니 서 있자니 마음이 무척 무거웠다."], ["Before she could speak, Annie had the cover off, and all were exclaiming at the lovely roses, heath, and fern within.", "모질고 씁쓸한 감정이 꽤나 심해지려던 참에, 하녀가 꽃 상자를 하나 들고 들어왔다. 메그가 미처 입을 떼기도 전에 애니가 뚜껑을 열어젖혔고, 다들 그 안에 든 사랑스러운 장미와 히스, 고사리를 보고 탄성을 질렀다."]], [["\"It's for Belle, of course; George always sends her some, but these are altogether ravishing,\"", "\"물론 벨 언니 거겠지. 조지가 늘 언니한테 꽃을 보내잖아."], ["cried Annie, with a great sniff.", "근데 이건 정말이지 황홀한걸.\" 애니가 한껏 향기를 들이마시며 외쳤다."]], [["\"They are for Miss March, the man said.", "\"마치 양에게 온 거래요, 배달 온 사람이 그러던데요."], ["And here's a note,\"", "여기 쪽지도 있고요.\""], ["put in the maid, holding it to Meg.", "하녀가 끼어들며 메그에게 쪽지를 내밀었다."]], [["\"What fun!", "\"어머나, 재미있다!"], ["Who are they from?", "누가 보낸 거야?"], ["Didn't know you had a lover,\"", "너한테 연인이 있는 줄은 몰랐는걸.\""], ["cried the girls, fluttering about Meg in a high state of curiosity and surprise.", "소녀들이 호기심과 놀라움에 잔뜩 들떠서 메그 주위로 몰려들며 외쳤다."]], [["\"The note is from mother, and the flowers from Laurie,\"", "\"쪽지는 엄마한테서, 꽃은 로리한테서 온 거야.\""], ["said Meg simply, yet much gratified that he had not forgotten her.", "메그가 담담하게 말했지만, 로리가 자기를 잊지 않았다는 사실에 무척 기뻐했다."]], [["\"Oh, indeed!\" said Annie, with a funny look, as Meg slipped the note into her pocket, as a sort of talisman against envy, vanity, and false pride; for the few loving words had done her good, and the flowers cheered her up by their beauty.", "\"어머나, 그래?\" 애니가 묘한 표정을 지으며 말했다. 메그는 그 쪽지를 호주머니에 슬쩍 넣었다. 시기와 허영과 그릇된 자존심을 막아 주는 일종의 부적처럼. 그 몇 마디 다정한 말이 메그에게 큰 힘이 되었고, 꽃은 그 아름다움으로 메그의 기운을 북돋아 주었던 것이다."]], [["Feeling almost happy again, she laid by a few ferns and roses for herself, and quickly made up the rest in dainty bouquets for the breasts, hair, or skirts of her friends, offering them so prettily that Clara, the elder sister, told her she was \"the sweetest little thing she ever saw;\"", "거의 다시 행복해진 메그는 자기 몫으로 고사리와 장미 몇 송이를 따로 챙겨 두고, 나머지로는 친구들의 가슴이며 머리며 치마에 꽂을 앙증맞은 꽃다발을 재빨리 만들었다. 어찌나 예쁘게 건네는지, 맏언니인 클라라는 메그더러 \"여태껏 본 중에 가장 사랑스러운 아이\"라고 했고, 다들 그 작은 정성에 무척 흐뭇해하는 듯했다."], ["and they looked quite charmed with her small attention.", "어쩐 일인지 그 친절한 행동이 메그의 침울함을 말끔히 씻어 주었다."], ["Somehow the kind act finished her despondency; and when all the rest went to show themselves to Mrs.", "나머지가 모펏 부인에게 차림새를 보여 주러 간 사이, 메그는 거울 속에서 행복하고 눈이 반짝이는 얼굴을 보았다."], ["Moffat, she saw a happy, bright-eyed face in the mirror, as she laid her ferns against her rippling hair, and fastened the roses in the dress that didn't strike her as so _very_ shabby now.", "물결치는 머리에 고사리를 갖다 대고, 이제는 그다지 초라해 보이지 않는 드레스에 장미를 꽂으면서."]], [["She enjoyed herself very much that evening, for she danced to her heart's content; every one was very kind, and she had three compliments.", "그날 저녁 메그는 무척 즐거웠다. 마음껏 춤을 추었으니까."], ["Annie made her sing, and some one said she had a remarkably fine voice; Major Lincoln asked who \"the fresh little girl, with the beautiful eyes,\"", "다들 친절했고, 칭찬도 세 번이나 들었다. 애니가 노래를 시켰는데, 누군가 메그의 목소리가 유난히 곱다고 했다."], ["was; and Mr. Moffat insisted on dancing with her, because she \"didn't dawdle, but had some spring in her,\"", "링컨 소령은 \"눈이 아름다운 저 풋풋한 어린 아가씨\"가 누구냐고 물었다."], ["as he gracefully expressed it.", "모펏 씨는 메그가 \"꾸물대지 않고 활기가 넘친다\"며 한사코 메그와 춤을 추자고 했다."], ["So, altogether, she had a very nice time, till she overheard a bit of a conversation, which disturbed her extremely.", "그가 점잖게 표현한 말이었다. 그래서 메그는 더없이 즐거운 시간을 보냈는데, 어쩌다 대화 한 토막을 엿듣고는 몹시 마음이 어지러워졌다."], ["She was sitting just inside the conservatory, waiting for her partner to bring her an ice, when she heard a voice ask, on the other side of the flowery wall,--", "메그가 마침 온실 바로 안쪽에 앉아 춤 상대가 아이스크림을 가져다주기를 기다리고 있을 때, 꽃이 우거진 벽 너머에서 누군가가 묻는 소리가 들려왔다."]], [["\"How old is he?\"", "\"그 애 몇 살이래요?\""]], [["\"Sixteen or seventeen, I should say,\"", "\"열여섯이나 열일곱쯤 됐을 거예요.\""], ["replied another voice.", "다른 목소리가 대답했다."]], [["\"It would be a grand thing for one of those girls, wouldn't it?", "\"저 자매 중 누구한테든 대단한 행운이겠죠, 안 그래요?"], ["Sallie says they are very intimate now, and the old man quite dotes on them.\"", "샐리 말로는 지금 둘이 무척 가깝다던데, 그 노인네가 아주 그 애들한테 푹 빠졌다잖아요.\""]], [["\"Mrs M. has made her plans, I dare say, and will play her cards well, early as it is.", "\"마치 부인이 다 계획을 세워 뒀겠죠, 아무렴."], ["The girl evidently doesn't think of it yet,\"", "아직 이르긴 해도 패를 잘 굴릴 거예요. 정작 그 애는 아직 그런 생각은 못 하는 게 분명하지만요.\""], ["said Mrs. Moffat.", "모펏 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"She told that fib about her mamma, as if she did know, and colored up when the flowers came, quite prettily.", "\"엄마 얘길 하면서 그렇게 거짓말을 하는 걸 보면, 다 알고 있는 것 같던데요. 게다가 꽃이 왔을 때 얼굴까지 발그레해지더라니까요, 어찌나 예쁘던지."], ["Poor thing!", "가엾기도 하지!"], ["she'd be so nice if she was only got up in style.", "제대로 차려입히기만 하면 얼마나 곱겠어요."], ["Do you think she'd be offended if we offered to lend her a dress for Thursday?\"", "목요일에 우리가 드레스를 빌려주겠다고 하면 기분 나빠할까요?\""], ["asked another voice.", "또 다른 목소리가 물었다."]], [["\"She's proud, but I don't believe she'd mind, for that dowdy tarlatan is all she has got.", "\"자존심이 세긴 해도, 싫다고는 안 할걸요. 가진 거라곤 그 촌스러운 모슬린 드레스뿐이니까요."], ["She may tear it to-night, and that will be a good excuse for offering a decent one.\"", "오늘 밤 그게 찢어지기라도 하면, 그게 마침 멀쩡한 걸 빌려줄 좋은 핑계가 되겠죠.\""]], [["\"We'll see.", "\"두고 보자고요."], ["I shall ask young Laurence, as a compliment to her, and we'll have fun about it afterward.\"", "그 애 체면을 세워 주는 뜻에서 어린 로런스를 초대해야겠어요. 그러고 나서 그 일로 한바탕 재미나게 굴어 보자고요.\""]], [["Here Meg's partner appeared, to find her looking much flushed and rather agitated. She _was_ proud, and her pride was useful just then, for it helped her hide her mortification, anger, and disgust at what she had just heard; for, innocent and unsuspicious as she was, she could not help understanding the gossip of her friends.", "이때 메그의 춤 상대가 나타났다가, 메그가 얼굴이 잔뜩 상기되고 다소 동요한 모습인 것을 발견했다."], ["She tried to forget it, but could not, and kept repeating to herself, \"Mrs. M.", "메그는 정말 자존심이 셌고, 바로 그 순간만은 그 자존심이 도움이 되었다."], ["has made her plans,\" \"that fib about her mamma,\"", "방금 들은 말에 느낀 굴욕과 분노와 역겨움을 감추는 데 보탬이 됐으니까."], ["and \"dowdy tarlatan,\" till she was ready to cry, and rush home to tell her troubles and ask for advice.", "메그는 순진하고 의심 없는 사람이었지만, 그래도 친구들의 험담을 알아듣지 않을 수 없었다."], ["As that was impossible, she did her best to seem gay; and, being rather excited, she succeeded so well that no one dreamed what an effort she was making.", "메그는 그것을 잊으려 애썼지만 그럴 수 없었고, \"마치 부인이 다 계획을 세워 뒀다\", \"엄마 얘기로 거짓말을 한다\", \"촌스러운 모슬린 드레스\"라는 말을 속으로 되뇌고 또 되뇌다가, 그만 울음을 터뜨리고 집으로 달려가 고민을 털어놓고 조언을 구하고 싶어졌다."], ["She was very glad when it was all over, and she was quiet in her bed, where she could think and wonder and fume till her head ached and her hot cheeks were cooled by a few natural tears.", "그럴 수 없는 노릇이었기에, 메그는 명랑해 보이려고 최선을 다했다. 다소 흥분한 상태였던 덕에 어찌나 잘해 냈던지, 메그가 얼마나 애를 쓰고 있는지는 아무도 짐작조차 하지 못했다."], ["Those foolish, yet well-meant words, had opened a new world to Meg, and much disturbed the peace of the old one, in which, till now, she had lived as happily as a child.", "모든 것이 끝나고 침대에 조용히 누워, 머리가 지끈거리고 달아오른 두 뺨이 자연스레 흐른 몇 방울 눈물에 식혀질 때까지 마음껏 생각하고 의아해하고 속을 끓일 수 있게 되자, 메그는 무척 기뻤다."], ["Her innocent friendship with Laurie was spoilt by the silly speeches she had overheard; her faith in her mother was a little shaken by the worldly plans attributed to her by Mrs.", "그 어리석지만 악의는 없는 말들이 메그에게 새로운 세계를 열어 보였고, 지금껏 어린아이처럼 행복하게 살아온 옛 세계의 평화를 크게 흩뜨려 놓았다. 로리와의 순진한 우정은 엿들은 그 시시한 말 때문에 망가졌다."], ["Moffat, who judged others by herself; and the sensible resolution to be contented with the simple wardrobe which suited a poor man's daughter, was weakened by the unnecessary pity of girls who thought a shabby dress one of the greatest calamities under heaven.", "자기 잣대로 남을 재는 모펏 부인이 갖다 붙인 그 세속적인 계획 때문에 어머니에 대한 믿음도 조금 흔들렸다. 그리고 가난한 사람의 딸에게 어울리는 소박한 옷차림에 만족하겠다던 그 현명한 결심도, 초라한 드레스를 천하에 가장 큰 재앙쯤으로 여기는 소녀들의 쓸데없는 동정에 약해지고 말았다."]], [["Poor Meg had a restless night, and got up heavy-eyed, unhappy, half resentful toward her friends, and half ashamed of herself for not speaking out frankly, and setting everything right.", "가엾은 메그는 잠 못 이루는 밤을 보내고, 눈이 퉁퉁 부은 채 일어났다. 마음이 울적했고, 친구들에게 반쯤은 화가 났으며, 솔직하게 털어놓고 모든 걸 바로잡지 못한 자신이 반쯤은 부끄러웠다."], ["Everybody dawdled that morning, and it was noon before the girls found energy enough even to take up their worsted work.", "그날 아침엔 다들 늑장을 부려서, 정오가 다 되어서야 소녀들은 털실 자수라도 손에 들 만큼 기운을 차렸다. 친구들의 태도에서 무언가가 메그의 눈에 대뜸 들어왔다."], ["Something in the manner of her friends struck Meg at once; they treated her with more respect, she thought; took quite a tender interest in what she said, and looked at her with eyes that plainly betrayed curiosity.", "메그가 보기에 다들 자기를 한층 정중하게 대했다. 메그가 하는 말에 자못 다정한 관심을 보였고, 호기심이 빤히 드러나는 눈으로 메그를 바라보았다."], ["All this surprised and flattered her, though she did not understand it till Miss Belle looked up from her writing, and said, with a sentimental air,--", "이 모든 것이 메그를 놀라게 하면서도 우쭐하게 했지만, 벨 양이 편지를 쓰다 고개를 들고는 감상적인 투로 말하기 전까지는 도무지 영문을 알 수 없었다."]], [["\"Daisy, dear, I've sent an invitation to your friend, Mr.", "\"데이지, 얘. 네 친구 로런스 씨한테 목요일 초대장을 보냈단다."], ["Laurence, for Thursday.", "우리도 그분을 알고 싶고, 그게 너한테도 합당한 예우니까.\""], ["We should like to know him, and it's only a proper compliment to you.\"", "메그는 얼굴을 붉혔지만, 소녀들을 골려 주고 싶은 장난기가 발동해서 짐짓 새침하게 대답했다."]], [["Meg colored, but a mischievous fancy to tease the girls made her reply demurely,--", "\"정말 친절하시네요. 하지만 그분은 오지 않으실 것 같아요.\""]], [["\"You are very kind, but I'm afraid he won't come.\" \"Why not, _chérie_?\"", "\"왜 안 오신다는 거니, 셰리(chérie : 프랑스어로 '귀여운 아가씨'라는 뜻 : 옮긴이)?\""], ["asked Miss Belle.", "벨 양이 물었다."]], [["\"He's too old.\"", "\"너무 연세가 많으셔서요.\""]], [["\"My child, what do you mean?", "\"얘, 그게 무슨 소리니?"], ["What is his age, I beg to know!\"", "그분 연세가 대체 어떻게 되는데?\""], ["cried Miss Clara.", "클라라 양이 외쳤다."]], [["\"Nearly seventy, I believe,\"", "\"아마 일흔 가까이 되셨을걸요.\""], ["answered Meg, counting stitches, to hide the merriment in her eyes.", "메그가 눈에 어린 웃음기를 감추려고 뜨개코를 세며 대답했다."]], [["\"You sly creature!", "\"이 능청맞은 것 같으니!"], ["Of course we meant the young man,\"", "당연히 우리가 말한 건 그 젊은 분이지.\""], ["exclaimed Miss Belle, laughing.", "벨 양이 웃음을 터뜨리며 소리쳤다."]], [["\"There isn't any; Laurie is only a little boy,\"", "\"그런 사람은 없는데요. 로리는 그저 어린 남자애일 뿐인걸요.\""], ["and Meg laughed also at the queer look which the sisters exchanged as she thus described her supposed lover.", "메그도 따라 웃었다. 자기가 사귄다는 그 연인을 이렇게 묘사하자 자매가 주고받는 묘한 눈길을 보고서."]], [["\"About your age,\"", "\"네 또래쯤 되는?\""], ["Nan said.", "낸이 말했다."]], [["\"Nearer my sister Jo's; _I_ am seventeen in August,\"", "\"우리 언니 조에 더 가까워요. 저는 8월이면 열일곱 살이 되거든요.\""], ["returned Meg, tossing her head.", "메그가 고개를 까딱하며 대꾸했다."]], [["\"It's very nice of him to send you flowers, isn't it?\"", "\"그분이 너한테 꽃을 보내다니, 참 다정하지 않니?\""], ["said Annie, looking wise about nothing.", "애니가 아무것도 아닌 일에 다 안다는 듯한 표정으로 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, he often does, to all of us; for their house is full, and we are so fond of them.", "\"네, 종종 보내 주세요, 우리 모두한테요. 그 집은 사람이 북적이고, 우리도 그 집 식구들을 무척 좋아하거든요."], ["My mother and old Mr. Laurence are friends, you know, so it is quite natural that we children should play together;\"", "우리 엄마랑 로런스 할아버지가 친구 사이시니까, 우리 같은 애들이 함께 어울려 노는 게 지극히 자연스러운 일이죠.\""], ["and Meg hoped they would say no more.", "메그는 그들이 더는 말을 꺼내지 않기를 바랐다."]], [["\"It's evident Daisy isn't out yet,\"", "\"데이지가 아직 사교계에 나오지 않은 게 분명하네.\""], ["said Miss Clara to Belle, with a nod.", "클라라 양이 벨에게 고갯짓을 하며 말했다."]], [["\"Quite a pastoral state of innocence all round,\"", "\"하나같이 목가적이리만치 순진한 상태로구나.\""], ["returned Miss Belle, with a shrug.", "벨 양이 어깨를 으쓱하며 대꾸했다."]], [["\"I'm going out to get some little matters for my girls; can I do anything for you, young ladies?\"", "\"내 딸들한테 자질구레한 것 좀 사다 주러 나가는데, 아가씨들, 뭐 필요한 거라도 있나요?\""], ["asked Mrs. Moffat, lumbering in, like an elephant, in silk and lace.", "모펏 부인이 비단과 레이스를 두른 채 코끼리처럼 육중하게 들어오며 물었다."]], [["\"No, thank you, ma'am,\"", "\"아뇨, 괜찮아요, 부인.\""], ["replied Sallie.", "샐리가 대답했다."], ["\"I've got my new pink silk for Thursday, and don't want a thing.\"", "\"목요일에 입을 새 분홍 비단 드레스를 마련해 둬서, 필요한 게 하나도 없어요.\""]], [["\"Nor I,--\"", "\"저도 그래—\""], ["began Meg, but stopped, because it occurred to her that she _did_ want several things, and could not have them.", "메그가 입을 떼다가 멈췄다. 자기에게는 필요한 게 여러 가지 있지만, 그걸 가질 수 없다는 생각이 퍼뜩 들었기 때문이다."]], [["\"What shall you wear?\"", "\"넌 뭘 입을 거니?\""], ["asked Sallie.", "샐리가 물었다."]], [["\"My old white one again, if I can mend it fit to be seen; it got sadly torn last night,\"", "\"그 낡은 흰 드레스를 다시 입을래. 봐 줄 만하게 손볼 수만 있다면."], ["said Meg, trying to speak quite easily, but feeling very uncomfortable.", "어젯밤에 어쩌다 심하게 찢어졌거든.\" 메그가 아주 태연하게 말하려 했지만, 속으로는 무척 불편했다."]], [["\"Why don't you send home for another?\"", "\"왜 집에 사람을 보내서 다른 걸 가져오라고 안 하니?\""], ["said Sallie, who was not an observing young lady.", "샐리가 말했다. 눈치가 빠른 아가씨는 못 되었던 것이다."]], [["\"I haven't got any other.\"", "\"다른 게 없거든.\""], ["It cost Meg an effort to say that, but Sallie did not see it, and exclaimed, in amiable surprise,--", "그 말을 하기까지 메그는 한참 애를 먹었지만, 샐리는 그걸 알아채지 못하고 천연덕스럽게 놀라며 외쳤다."]], [["\"Only that?", "\"그게 다라고?"], ["How funny--\"", "어머, 이상하기도 하지—\""], ["She did not finish her speech, for Belle shook her head at her, and broke in, saying kindly,--", "샐리는 말을 끝맺지 못했다. 벨이 고개를 저어 막고는, 다정하게 끼어들었기 때문이다."]], [["\"Not at all; where is the use of having a lot of dresses when she isn't out? There's no need of sending home, Daisy, even if you had a dozen, for I've got a sweet blue silk laid away, which I've outgrown, and you shall wear it, to please me, won't you, dear?\"", "\"전혀 이상할 거 없어. 아직 사교계에 나오지도 않았는데 드레스가 많아서 뭐 하겠어? 데이지, 설령 네게 드레스가 열두 벌이 있다 해도 집에 가지러 보낼 것까진 없어. 나한테 사랑스러운 파란 비단 드레스가 한 벌 따로 넣어 둔 게 있거든. 내가 입기엔 작아져서 말이야. 네가 그걸 입어 주면 내 기분이 좋겠어, 그래 줄 거지, 응?\""]], [["\"You are very kind, but I don't mind my old dress, if you don't; it does well enough for a little girl like me,\"", "\"정말 친절하시네요. 하지만 언니가 괜찮으시다면 전 제 낡은 드레스도 상관없어요."], ["said Meg.", "저 같은 어린애한테는 그걸로 충분하니까요.\" 메그가 말했다."]], [["\"Now do let me please myself by dressing you up in style.", "\"자, 너를 멋지게 차려입혀서 내 기분 좀 좋게 해 줘."], ["I admire to do it, and you'd be a regular little beauty, with a touch here and there.", "난 그런 걸 좋아하거든. 여기저기 조금만 손보면 넌 영락없는 작은 미인이 될 거야."], ["I sha'n't let any one see you till you are done, and then we'll burst upon them like Cinderella and her godmother, going to the ball,\"", "다 끝날 때까지 아무한테도 안 보여 주다가, 무도회에 가는 신데렐라와 그 대모처럼 짠 하고 나타나는 거지.\""], ["said Belle, in her persuasive tone.", "벨이 구슬리는 투로 말했다."]], [["Meg couldn't refuse the offer so kindly made, for a desire to see if she would be \"a little beauty\"", "이토록 친절하게 건넨 제안을 메그는 거절할 수 없었다."], ["after touching up, caused her to accept, and forget all her former uncomfortable feelings towards the Moffats.", "조금만 손보면 자기가 정말 \"작은 미인\"이 되는지 보고 싶은 마음에 그 제안을 받아들였고, 모펏 집안 사람들에게 품었던 거북한 감정도 죄다 잊어버렸다."]], [["On the Thursday evening, Belle shut herself up with her maid; and, between them, they turned Meg into a fine lady.", "목요일 저녁, 벨은 하녀와 함께 방에 틀어박혔다. 둘이서 메그를 어엿한 숙녀로 탈바꿈시켰다."], ["They crimped and curled her hair, they polished her neck and arms with some fragrant powder, touched her lips with coralline salve, to make them redder, and Hortense would have added \"a _soupçon_ of rouge,\"", "메그의 머리를 지지고 곱슬곱슬하게 말았고, 향기로운 분으로 목과 팔을 매끄럽게 다듬었으며, 입술을 더 붉게 하려고 산호빛 연지를 발랐다. 메그가 한사코 마다하지 않았더라면, 오르탕스는 \"볼연지도 조금\""], ["if Meg had not rebelled.", "더했을 것이다."], ["They laced her into a sky-blue dress, which was so tight she could hardly breathe, and so low in the neck that modest Meg blushed at herself in the mirror.", "둘은 하늘빛 드레스 끈을 졸라매 메그를 그 안에 욱여넣었는데, 어찌나 꼭 끼는지 숨도 제대로 못 쉴 지경이었고, 목이 어찌나 깊이 파였는지 정숙한 메그는 거울 속 자기 모습에 얼굴을 붉혔다. 거기에 은 세공 장신구 한 벌을 더했다."], ["A set of silver filagree was added, bracelets, necklace, brooch, and even ear-rings, for Hortense tied them on, with a bit of pink silk, which did not show.", "팔찌, 목걸이, 브로치, 심지어 귀걸이까지. 오르탕스가 분홍 비단실 한 가닥으로 귀걸이를 매어 주었는데, 그건 겉으로 드러나지 않았다."], ["A cluster of tea-rosebuds at the bosom, and a _ruche_, reconciled Meg to the display of her pretty white shoulders, and a pair of high-heeled blue silk boots satisfied the last wish of her heart.", "가슴께에 단 찻장미 꽃봉오리 한 다발과 주름 장식 덕에, 메그도 그 예쁜 흰 어깨를 드러내는 걸 그럭저럭 받아들이게 되었고, 굽 높은 파란 비단 부츠 한 켤레가 메그의 마지막 소원까지 채워 주었다."], ["A laced handkerchief, a plumy fan, and a bouquet in a silver holder finished her off; and Miss Belle surveyed her with the satisfaction of a little girl with a newly dressed doll.", "레이스 손수건과 깃털 부채, 은제 꽃대에 꽂은 꽃다발로 마무리하자, 벨 양은 새로 옷을 입힌 인형을 든 어린 소녀처럼 흐뭇하게 메그를 바라보았다."]], [["\"Mademoiselle is charmante, très jolie, is she not?\"", "\"마드무아젤은 정말 매력적이고, 무척 아름다워요, 안 그래요?\""], ["cried Hortense, clasping her hands in an affected rapture.", "오르탕스가 짐짓 황홀한 척 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤다."]], [["\"Come and show yourself,\"", "\"가서 네 모습을 보여 줘.\""], ["said Miss Belle, leading the way to the room where the others were waiting.", "벨 양이 다른 사람들이 기다리고 있는 방으로 앞장서며 말했다."]], [["As Meg went rustling after, with her long skirts trailing, her ear-rings tinkling, her curls waving, and her heart beating, she felt as if her \"fun\"", "긴 치맛자락을 끌고, 귀걸이를 짤랑이고, 곱슬머리를 출렁이고, 가슴을 콩닥거리며 메그가 사각사각 소리를 내며 뒤따라 들어갈 때, 마침내 자기의 \"즐거운 시간\"이 정말로 시작되었다는 기분이 들었다."], ["had really begun at last, for the mirror had plainly told her that she _was_ \"a little beauty.\"", "거울이 분명히 일러 주었으니까, 자기가 정말로 \"작은 미인\"이라고."], ["Her friends repeated the pleasing phrase enthusiastically; and, for several minutes, she stood, like the jackdaw in the fable, enjoying her borrowed plumes, while the rest chattered like a party of magpies.", "친구들이 그 듣기 좋은 말을 열렬히 되풀이했고, 메그는 한동안 우화 속의 갈까마귀처럼, 빌려 온 깃털을 뽐내며 서 있었다. 그동안 나머지는 한 무리의 까치 떼처럼 재잘거렸다."]], [["\"While I dress, do you drill her, Nan, in the management of her skirt, and those French heels, or she will trip herself up.", "\"내가 옷 입는 동안, 낸, 네가 데이지한테 치마 다루는 법이랑 저 프랑스식 굽을 어떻게 다뤄야 하는지 좀 일러 줘. 안 그러면 제 발에 걸려 넘어질 테니까."], ["Take your silver butterfly, and catch up that long curl on the left side of her head, Clara, and don't any of you disturb the charming work of my hands,\"", "클라라, 너는 네 은나비를 가져다가 머리 왼쪽에 늘어진 저 긴 곱슬머리를 추켜올려 줘. 그리고 누구든 내가 정성껏 매만진 작품을 흐트러뜨리면 안 돼.\""], ["said Belle, as she hurried away, looking well pleased with her success.", "벨이 자기 솜씨에 무척 흡족한 얼굴로 서둘러 나가며 말했다."]], [["\"I'm afraid to go down, I feel so queer and stiff and half-dressed,\"", "\"내려가기가 무서워. 어쩐지 어색하고 뻣뻣하고, 옷도 반밖에 안 걸친 것 같아서.\""], ["said Meg to Sallie, as the bell rang, and Mrs. Moffat sent to ask the young ladies to appear at once.", "종이 울리고 모펏 부인이 아가씨들더러 어서 모습을 보이라고 사람을 보내자, 메그가 샐리에게 말했다."]], [["\"You don't look a bit like yourself, but you are very nice.", "\"넌 너 같지가 않아. 하지만 정말 근사한걸."], ["I'm nowhere beside you, for Belle has heaps of taste, and you're quite French, I assure you.", "네 옆에 서면 난 명함도 못 내밀겠어. 벨 언니는 안목이 대단하고, 너는 영락없이 프랑스풍이야, 정말이라니까."], ["Let your flowers hang; don't be so careful of them, and be sure you don't trip,\"", "꽃은 그냥 늘어뜨려 둬. 그렇게 신경 쓰지 말고, 절대 넘어지지 않게 조심해.\""], ["returned Sallie, trying not to care that Meg was prettier than herself.", "샐리가 대답했다. 메그가 자기보다 더 예쁘다는 게 신경 쓰이지 않는 척하면서."]], [["Keeping that warning carefully in mind, Margaret got safely down stairs, and sailed into the drawing-rooms, where the Moffats and a few early guests were assembled.", "그 주의를 마음에 단단히 새긴 채, 마거릿은 무사히 아래층으로 내려가, 모펏 집안 사람들과 일찍 온 손님 몇몇이 모여 있는 응접실로 미끄러져 들어갔다."], ["She very soon discovered that there is a charm about fine clothes which attracts a certain class of people, and secures their respect.", "메그는 곧 멋진 옷에는 어떤 매력이 있어서, 특정 부류의 사람들을 끌어당기고 그들의 존경을 얻어 낸다는 것을 깨달았다. 전에는 메그에게 눈길조차 주지 않던 아가씨 여럿이 별안간 다정하게 굴었다."], ["Several young ladies, who had taken no notice of her before, were very affectionate all of a sudden; several young gentlemen, who had only stared at her at the other party, now not only stared, but asked to be introduced, and said all manner of foolish but agreeable things to her; and several old ladies, who sat on sofas, and criticised the rest of the party, inquired who she was, with an air of interest.", "다른 파티에서 메그를 빤히 쳐다보기만 하던 청년 여럿이 이제는 쳐다볼 뿐 아니라 소개해 달라고 청하고는, 온갖 시답잖지만 듣기 좋은 말을 늘어놓았다. 그리고 소파에 앉아 나머지 손님들을 품평하던 노부인 여럿이 관심 어린 표정으로 메그가 누구냐고 물었다."], ["She heard Mrs. Moffat reply to one of them,--", "메그는 모펏 부인이 그중 한 사람에게 대답하는 소리를 들었다."]], [["\"Daisy March--father a colonel in the army--one of our first families, but reverses of fortune, you know; intimate friends of the Laurences; sweet creature, I assure you; my Ned is quite wild about her.\"", "\"데이지 마치예요. 아버지가 군대 대령인데, 우리 동네에서 손꼽히는 집안이었답니다. 하지만 가세가 기울었지요, 아시잖아요. 로런스 집안과는 막역한 사이고요. 정말 사랑스러운 아이예요. 우리 네드가 아주 그 애한테 푹 빠졌답니다.\""]], [["\"Dear me!\"", "\"어머나!\""], ["said the old lady, putting up her glass for another observation of Meg, who tried to look as if she had not heard, and been rather shocked at Mrs.", "노부인이 메그를 다시 한번 살펴보려고 안경을 추켜올리며 말했다."], ["Moffat's fibs.", "메그는 못 들은 척하려 애썼지만, 모펏 부인의 거짓말에 자못 충격을 받았다."]], [["The \"queer feeling\"", "그 \"어색한 기분\"은 가시지 않았지만, 메그는 자기가 어엿한 숙녀라는 새 역할을 연기하고 있다고 상상하며 그럭저럭 잘 해 나갔다."], ["did not pass away, but she imagined herself acting the new part of fine lady, and so got on pretty well, though the tight dress gave her a side-ache, the train kept getting under her feet, and she was in constant fear lest her ear-rings should fly off, and get lost or broken.", "비록 꽉 끼는 드레스 탓에 옆구리가 결리고, 치맛자락이 자꾸 발에 걸렸으며, 귀걸이가 튕겨 나가 잃어버리거나 부서질까 봐 줄곧 마음을 졸였지만 말이다. 메그가 부채를 살랑이며, 재치 있는 척 애쓰는 한 청년의 시시한 농담에 웃고 있을 때, 갑자기 웃음을 멈추고 당황한 표정을 지었다."], ["She was flirting her fan and laughing at the feeble jokes of a young gentleman who tried to be witty, when she suddenly stopped laughing and looked confused; for, just opposite, she saw Laurie.", "바로 맞은편에 로리가 보였기 때문이다. 로리는 노골적으로 놀란, 그리고 메그가 보기엔 못마땅해하는 표정으로 메그를 쳐다보고 있었다."], ["He was staring at her with undisguised surprise, and disapproval also, she thought; for, though he bowed and smiled, yet something in his honest eyes made her blush, and wish she had her old dress on.", "고개를 숙여 인사하고 미소를 짓긴 했지만, 그 정직한 눈빛 속의 무언가가 메그를 얼굴 붉히게 했고, 차라리 낡은 옷을 입고 있었으면 싶게 만들었다."], ["To complete her confusion, she saw Belle nudge Annie, and both glance from her to Laurie, who, she was happy to see, looked unusually boyish and shy.", "메그의 당혹감을 완성하려는 듯, 벨이 애니를 쿡 찌르는 게 보였고, 둘 다 메그에게서 로리에게로 눈길을 옮겼다. 로리는 평소와 달리 어린애같이 수줍어 보였는데, 메그는 그 모습을 보고 다행이라 여겼다."]], [["\"Silly creatures, to put such thoughts into my head!", "\"바보 같은 것들, 내 머릿속에 그런 생각을 집어넣다니! 신경 안 쓸 거야."], ["I won't care for it, or let it change me a bit,\"", "그런 것 때문에 내가 조금이라도 달라지진 않을 거야.\""], ["thought Meg, and rustled across the room to shake hands with her friend.", "메그는 그렇게 생각하며, 사각사각 소리를 내며 방을 가로질러 가서 친구와 악수를 했다."]], [["\"I'm glad you came, I was afraid you wouldn't,\"", "\"네가 와 줘서 기뻐. 안 올까 봐 걱정했거든.\""], ["she said, with her most grown-up air.", "메그가 더없이 어른스러운 태도로 말했다."]], [["\"Jo wanted me to come, and tell her how you looked, so I did;\"", "\"조가 가서 네가 어떻게 차려입었는지 보고 와서 얘기해 달라고 해서 왔어.\""], ["answered Laurie, without turning his eyes upon her, though he half smiled at her maternal tone.", "로리가 메그에게서 눈길을 돌린 채 대답했다. 메그의 어머니 같은 말투에 반쯤 웃긴 했지만."]], [["\"What shall you tell her?\"", "\"조한테 뭐라고 할 건데?\""], ["asked Meg, full of curiosity to know his opinion of her, yet feeling ill at ease with him, for the first time.", "메그가 자기에 대한 로리의 생각이 궁금해서 물었다. 그러면서도 난생처음으로 로리가 거북하게 느껴졌다."]], [["\"I shall say I didn't know you; for you look so grown-up, and unlike yourself, I'm quite afraid of you,\"", "\"널 몰라봤다고 할 거야. 너무 어른스러워지고 너답지가 않아서, 어쩐지 네가 무섭기까지 하다고.\""], ["he said, fumbling at his glove-button.", "로리가 장갑 단추를 만지작거리며 말했다."]], [["\"How absurd of you!", "\"별소릴 다 하네!"], ["The girls dressed me up for fun, and I rather like it.", "애들이 재미 삼아 날 꾸며 준 건데, 난 꽤 마음에 들어."], ["Wouldn't Jo stare if she saw me?\"", "조가 날 보면 눈이 휘둥그레지지 않을까?\""], ["said Meg, bent on making him say whether he thought her improved or not.", "메그가 말했다. 자기가 더 나아 보이는지 아닌지 로리 입에서 기어이 듣고야 말겠다는 심산으로."]], [["\"Yes, I think she would,\"", "\"응, 그럴 것 같아.\""], ["returned Laurie gravely.", "로리가 진지하게 대답했다."]], [["\"Don't you like me so?\"", "\"이런 내가 마음에 안 들어?\""], ["asked Meg.", "메그가 물었다."]], [["\"No, I don't,\"", "\"응, 안 들어.\""], ["was the blunt reply.", "무뚝뚝한 대답이었다."]], [["\"Why not?\"", "\"왜?\""], ["in an anxious tone.", "불안한 목소리로."]], [["He glanced at her frizzled head, bare shoulders, and fantastically trimmed dress, with an expression that abashed her more than his answer, which had not a particle of his usual politeness about it.", "로리는 지진 머리며 드러난 어깨며 별나게 치장한 드레스를 흘끔 보았다. 그 표정은 평소의 예의라곤 눈곱만큼도 없는 그 대답보다도 메그를 더 무안하게 했다."]], [["\"I don't like fuss and feathers.\"", "\"난 호들갑이랑 깃털 장식이 싫어.\""]], [["That was altogether too much from a lad younger than herself; and Meg walked away, saying petulantly,--", "자기보다 어린 녀석한테서 나온 말치고는 너무 지나쳤다. 메그는 발끈해서 한마디 내뱉으며 자리를 떴다."]], [["\"You are the rudest boy I ever saw.\"", "\"넌 내가 본 중에 제일 무례한 애야.\""]], [["Feeling very much ruffled, she went and stood at a quiet window, to cool her cheeks, for the tight dress gave her an uncomfortably brilliant color.", "잔뜩 기분이 상한 메그는 조용한 창가로 가서 섰다. 꽉 끼는 드레스 탓에 거북할 만큼 얼굴이 발갛게 달아올라서, 두 뺨을 식히려는 것이었다."], ["As she stood there, Major Lincoln passed by; and, a minute after, she heard him saying to his mother,--", "메그가 거기 서 있는데 링컨 소령이 지나갔고, 잠시 후 그가 자기 어머니에게 하는 말이 들려왔다."]], [["\"They are making a fool of that little girl; I wanted you to see her, but they have spoilt her entirely; she's nothing but a doll, to-night.\"", "\"저 사람들이 저 어린 아가씨를 우스운 꼴로 만들어 놨네요. 어머니께 보여 드리고 싶었는데, 아주 망쳐 놨어요. 오늘 밤 저 애는 인형이나 다름없어요.\""]], [["\"Oh, dear!\"", "\"어머, 어떡해!\" 메그가 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["sighed Meg; \"I wish I'd been sensible, and worn my own things; then I should not have disgusted other people, or felt so uncomfortable and ashamed myself.\"", "\"분별 있게 굴어서 내 옷을 입을걸. 그랬으면 남들 눈에 거슬리지도 않았을 테고, 나도 이렇게 거북하고 부끄럽지 않았을 텐데.\""]], [["She leaned her forehead on the cool pane, and stood half hidden by the curtains, never minding that her favorite waltz had begun, till some one touched her; and, turning, she saw Laurie, looking penitent, as he said, with his very best bow, and his hand out,--", "메그는 서늘한 유리창에 이마를 기대고, 커튼에 반쯤 가려진 채 서 있었다. 가장 좋아하는 왈츠가 시작된 것도 아랑곳하지 않고서. 그러다 누군가 메그를 건드렸고, 돌아보니 뉘우치는 표정의 로리가 더없이 정중하게 고개를 숙이고 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["\"Please forgive my rudeness, and come and dance with me.\"", "\"무례하게 굴어서 미안해. 나랑 춤추자.\""]], [["\"I'm afraid it will be too disagreeable to you,\"", "\"너한텐 너무 거슬릴 것 같은데.\""], ["said Meg, trying to look offended, and failing entirely.", "메그가 화난 척하려 했지만, 영 그러지 못한 채 말했다."]], [["\"Not a bit of it; I'm dying to do it.", "\"전혀 안 그래. 정말 추고 싶어 죽겠는걸."], ["Come, I'll be good; I don't like your gown, but I do think you are--just splendid;\"", "자, 얌전히 굴게. 네 드레스는 마음에 안 들지만, 넌 정말이지—그냥 눈부셔.\""], ["and he waved his hands, as if words failed to express his admiration.", "로리가 두 손을 휘저었다. 말로는 자기의 감탄을 다 표현할 수 없다는 듯이."]], [["Meg smiled and relented, and whispered, as they stood waiting to catch the time,--", "메그는 미소 지으며 마음을 풀었고, 박자를 맞추려고 둘이 서서 기다리는 사이에 속삭였다."]], [["\"Take care my skirt don't trip you up; it's the plague of my life, and I was a goose to wear it.\"", "\"내 치마에 걸려 넘어지지 않게 조심해. 정말 골칫거리야. 이런 걸 입다니 내가 바보였어.\""]], [["\"Pin it round your neck, and then it will be useful,\"", "\"치마를 목에 둘러 매. 그럼 쓸모라도 있겠지.\""], ["said Laurie, looking down at the little blue boots, which he evidently approved of.", "로리가 작은 파란 부츠를 내려다보며 말했다. 그 부츠만은 마음에 드는 게 분명했다."]], [["Away they went, fleetly and gracefully; for, having practised at home, they were well matched, and the blithe young couple were a pleasant sight to see, as they twirled merrily round and round, feeling more friendly than ever after their small tiff.", "둘은 날렵하고 우아하게 춤을 추기 시작했다. 집에서 연습한 적이 있어 호흡이 잘 맞았고, 사소한 다툼 끝에 어느 때보다 다정해진 이 명랑한 한 쌍이 즐겁게 빙글빙글 도는 모습은 보기에 흐뭇했다."]], [["\"Laurie, I want you to do me a favor; will you?\"", "\"로리, 부탁 하나만 들어줄래?\" 메그가 말했다."], ["said Meg, as he stood fanning her, when her breath gave out, which it did very soon, though she would not own why.", "숨이 차자 로리가 곁에 서서 부채질을 해 줄 때였는데, 메그는 이유를 밝히려 들지 않았지만 숨은 금세 차올랐던 것이다."]], [["\"Won't I!\"", "\"안 들어줄 리가!\""], ["said Laurie, with alacrity.", "로리가 선뜻 대답했다."]], [["\"Please don't tell them at home about my dress to-night.", "\"오늘 밤 내 드레스 얘기, 집에 가서 하지 말아 줘."], ["They won't understand the joke, and it will worry mother.\"", "식구들은 이 장난을 이해 못 할 거고, 엄마는 걱정하실 테니까.\""]], [["\"Then why did you do it?\"", "\"그럼 왜 그런 짓을 했는데?\""], ["said Laurie's eyes, so plainly that Meg hastily added,--", "로리의 눈이 어찌나 빤히 그렇게 말하는지, 메그가 황급히 덧붙였다."]], [["\"I shall tell them, myself, all about it, and ''fess' to mother how silly I've been.", "\"내가 직접 식구들한테 다 얘기하고, 엄마한테 내가 얼마나 바보 같았는지 '고백'할 거야."], ["But I'd rather do it myself; so you'll not tell, will you?\"", "그래도 내가 직접 하는 게 낫겠어. 그러니까 너는 말 안 할 거지?\""]], [["\"I give you my word I won't; only what shall I say when they ask me?\"", "\"맹세코 안 할게. 그런데 식구들이 물어보면 난 뭐라고 해?\""]], [["\"Just say I looked pretty well, and was having a good time.\"", "\"그냥 내가 꽤 예뻐 보였고, 즐겁게 놀았다고 해 줘.\""]], [["\"I'll say the first, with all my heart; but how about the other?", "\"앞엣것은 진심으로 그렇게 말할게. 그런데 뒤엣것은 어쩌지?"], ["You don't look as if you were having a good time; are you?\"", "넌 즐겁게 노는 것 같지가 않은데, 정말 그래?\""], ["and Laurie looked at her with an expression which made her answer, in a whisper,--", "로리가 어떤 표정으로 메그를 바라보았는데, 그 바람에 메그는 속삭이듯 대답하고 말았다."]], [["\"No; not just now.", "\"아니, 지금은 아냐. 날 못됐다고 생각하진 마."], ["Don't think I'm horrid; I only wanted a little fun, but this sort doesn't pay, I find, and I'm getting tired of it.\"", "그냥 좀 즐겁게 놀고 싶었을 뿐인데, 이런 식의 즐거움은 별 보람이 없다는 걸 알겠어. 이젠 슬슬 지겨워지고 있고.\""]], [["\"Here comes Ned Moffat; what does he want?\"", "\"네드 모펏이 오네. 쟤가 뭘 바라는 거야?\""], ["said Laurie, knitting his black brows, as if he did not regard his young host in the light of a pleasant addition to the party.", "로리가 검은 눈썹을 찌푸리며 말했다. 자기네 젊은 주인을 파티에 반가운 손님으로는 여기지 않는다는 듯이."]], [["\"He put his name down for three dances, and I suppose he's coming for them.", "\"춤을 세 번 추겠다고 이름을 적어 뒀거든. 그걸 추러 오나 봐."], ["What a bore!\"", "정말 따분해!\""], ["said Meg, assuming a languid air, which amused Laurie immensely.", "메그가 나른한 척하며 말했고, 로리는 그 모습이 무척 우스웠다."]], [["He did not speak to her again till supper-time, when he saw her drinking champagne with Ned and his friend Fisher, who were behaving \"like a pair of fools,\" as Laurie said to himself, for he felt a brotherly sort of right to watch over the Marches, and fight their battles whenever a defender was needed.", "로리는 저녁 식사 시간이 될 때까지 메그에게 다시 말을 걸지 않았다. 그러다 메그가 네드와 그의 친구 피셔와 함께 샴페인을 마시는 것을 보았다. 로리가 보기에 그 둘은 \"한 쌍의 바보처럼\" 굴고 있었다. 로리는 마치 집안에 대해 오빠 같은 권리를 느끼고 있어서, 보호자가 필요할 때면 언제든 그들 편에 서서 싸워 주려 했던 것이다."]], [["\"You'll have a splitting headache to-morrow, if you drink much of that.", "\"그렇게 많이 마시면 내일 머리가 깨질 듯이 아플 거야. 나라면 안 그러겠어, 메그."], ["I wouldn't Meg; your mother doesn't like it, you know,\"", "너희 어머니가 싫어하시는 거 너도 알잖아.\""], ["he whispered, leaning over her chair, as Ned turned to refill her glass, and Fisher stooped to pick up her fan.", "네드가 메그의 잔을 다시 채우려고 돌아서고 피셔가 부채를 주우려고 몸을 굽힌 사이, 로리가 메그의 의자 위로 몸을 기울이며 속삭였다."]], [["\"I'm not Meg, to-night; I'm 'a doll,'", "\"오늘 밤 난 메그가 아니야."], ["who does all sorts of crazy things.", "난 온갖 미친 짓을 다 하는 '인형'이거든."], ["To-morrow I shall put away my 'fuss and feathers,' and be desperately good again,\"", "내일이면 내 '호들갑이랑 깃털 장식'은 치워 버리고, 다시 끔찍하게 착해질 거야.\""], ["she answered, with an affected little laugh.", "메그가 짐짓 가볍게 웃으며 대답했다."]], [["\"Wish to-morrow was here, then,\"", "\"그럼 차라리 내일이 와 버렸으면 좋겠네.\""], ["muttered Laurie, walking off, ill-pleased at the change he saw in her.", "로리가 중얼거리며 자리를 떴다. 메그에게서 보이는 변화가 영 못마땅했던 것이다."]], [["Meg danced and flirted, chattered and giggled, as the other girls did; after supper she undertook the German, and blundered through it, nearly upsetting her partner with her long skirt, and romping in a way that scandalized Laurie, who looked on and meditated a lecture.", "메그는 다른 소녀들이 하는 대로 춤추고 시시덕거리고 재잘대며 키득거렸다. 저녁 식사 뒤에는 독일식 춤에 나섰다가 우왕좌왕하며 헤맸고, 긴 치마로 춤 상대를 하마터면 넘어뜨릴 뻔했으며, 어찌나 분방하게 까불었던지 곁에서 지켜보던 로리가 한마디 해야겠다고 마음먹을 정도였다."], ["But he got no chance to deliver it, for Meg kept away from him till he came to say good-night.", "하지만 그럴 기회는 오지 않았다. 메그가 작별 인사를 하러 올 때까지 로리를 피해 다녔기 때문이다."]], [["\"Remember!\"", "\"잊지 마!\""], ["she said, trying to smile, for the splitting headache had already begun.", "메그가 미소 지으려 애쓰며 말했다. 머리가 깨질 듯한 두통이 벌써 시작된 터였다."]], [["\"Silence à la mort,\"", "\"죽음처럼 침묵하리(Silence à la mort : 프랑스어 : 옮긴이).\""], ["replied Laurie, with a melodramatic flourish, as he went away.", "로리가 멜로드라마처럼 과장된 몸짓으로 대답하고는 자리를 떴다."]], [["This little bit of by-play excited Annie's curiosity; but Meg was too tired for gossip, and went to bed, feeling as if she had been to a masquerade, and hadn't enjoyed herself as much as she expected.", "이 자그마한 곁다리 연극이 애니의 호기심을 자극했지만, 메그는 너무 지쳐서 수다를 떨 기운도 없이 잠자리에 들었다. 마치 가장무도회에라도 다녀온 듯한 기분이었고, 기대했던 만큼 즐겁지도 않았다."], ["She was sick all the next day, and on Saturday went home, quite used up with her fortnight's fun, and feeling that she had \"sat in the lap of luxury\"", "이튿날 메그는 종일 앓았고, 토요일에 집으로 돌아왔다. 두 주간의 즐거움에 완전히 녹초가 된 채, \"호사의 무릎에 앉아\""], ["long enough.", "지낼 만큼 지냈다는 기분으로."]], [["\"It does seem pleasant to be quiet, and not have company manners on all the time.", "\"조용히 지내면서 늘 손님 노릇 하지 않아도 되니, 정말 좋다."], ["Home _is_ a nice place, though it isn't splendid,\"", "집은 으리으리하진 않아도, 역시 좋은 곳이야.\""], ["said Meg, looking about her with a restful expression, as she sat with her mother and Jo on the Sunday evening.", "일요일 저녁, 어머니와 조와 함께 앉아 있던 메그가 편안한 표정으로 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["\"I'm glad to hear you say so, dear, for I was afraid home would seem dull and poor to you, after your fine quarters,\" replied her mother, who had given her many anxious looks that day; for motherly eyes are quick to see any change in children's faces.", "\"네 입에서 그런 말을 들으니 기쁘구나, 얘야. 그렇게 좋은 데서 지내다 오면 집이 칙칙하고 초라해 보일까 봐 걱정했거든.\" 어머니가 대답했다. 어머니는 그날 메그에게 여러 번 근심 어린 눈길을 보냈다. 어머니의 눈은 아이들 얼굴에 나타난 사소한 변화도 재빨리 알아채는 법이니까."]], [["Meg had told her adventures gayly, and said over and over what a charming time she had had; but something still seemed to weigh upon her spirits, and, when the younger girls were gone to bed, she sat thoughtfully staring at the fire, saying little, and looking worried.", "메그는 자기 모험담을 신나게 들려주었고, 얼마나 즐거웠는지 거듭 이야기했다. 하지만 무언가가 여전히 그 마음을 짓누르는 듯했고, 어린 동생들이 잠자리에 든 뒤에는 골똘히 난롯불을 바라보며 말수가 적어지고 근심스러운 표정을 지었다."], ["As the clock struck nine, and Jo proposed bed, Meg suddenly left her chair, and, taking Beth's stool, leaned her elbows on her mother's knee, saying bravely,--", "시계가 아홉 시를 치고 조가 잘 시간이라고 하자, 메그가 별안간 의자에서 일어나 베스의 발판을 가져다가 어머니 무릎에 팔꿈치를 괴고는 용기를 내어 말했다."]], [["\"Marmee, I want to ''fess.'\"", "\"엄마, 저 '고백'하고 싶어요.\""]], [["\"I thought so; what is it, dear?\"", "\"그럴 줄 알았다. 무슨 일이니, 얘야?\""]], [["\"Shall I go away?\"", "\"난 나가 있을까?\""], ["asked Jo discreetly.", "조가 눈치껏 물었다."]], [["\"Of course not; don't I always tell you everything?", "\"무슨 소리야, 당연히 아니지. 내가 너한테 뭐든 다 얘기하잖아?"], ["I was ashamed to speak of it before the children, but I want you to know all the dreadful things I did at the Moffat's.\"", "동생들 앞에서 말하기는 부끄러웠지만, 내가 모펏 집에서 저지른 끔찍한 일들을 네가 다 알았으면 좋겠어.\""]], [["\"We are prepared,\"", "\"각오는 돼 있단다.\""], ["said Mrs. March, smiling, but looking a little anxious.", "마치 부인이 미소를 지으면서도 조금 걱정스러운 표정으로 말했다."]], [["\"I told you they dressed me up, but I didn't tell you that they powdered and squeezed and frizzled, and made me look like a fashion-plate.", "\"그 사람들이 날 차려입혀 줬다고는 말씀드렸지만, 분을 바르고 몸을 졸라매고 머리를 지져서, 절 패션 화보 속 사람처럼 만들어 놨다는 건 말 안 했어요. 로리는 제가 단정치 못하다고 생각했어요."], ["Laurie thought I wasn't proper; I know he did, though he didn't say so, and one man called me 'a doll.'", "입 밖에 내진 않았지만 분명 그랬어요. 게다가 어떤 남자는 절 '인형'이라고 불렀고요."], ["I knew it was silly, but they flattered me, and said I was a beauty, and quantities of nonsense, so I let them make a fool of me.\"", "어리석은 짓인 줄 알면서도, 그 사람들이 절 추켜세우며 미인이라느니 온갖 허튼소리를 늘어놓는 통에, 그만 절 우스운 꼴로 만들도록 내버려 뒀지 뭐예요.\""]], [["\"Is that all?\"", "\"그게 다야?\""], ["asked Jo, as Mrs.", "조가 물었다."], ["March looked silently at the downcast face of her pretty daughter, and could not find it in her heart to blame her little follies.", "마치 부인은 예쁜 딸의 풀 죽은 얼굴을 말없이 바라보며, 차마 그 작은 어리석음들을 나무랄 마음이 들지 않았던 것이다."]], [["\"No; I drank champagne and romped and tried to flirt, and was altogether abominable,\"", "\"아뇨. 샴페인을 마시고, 까불고, 시시덕거리려 들기까지 했어요."], ["said Meg self-reproachfully.", "정말이지 형편없었죠.\" 메그가 자책하며 말했다."]], [["\"There is something more, I think;\"", "\"더 있는 것 같은데.\""], ["and Mrs. March smoothed the soft cheek, which suddenly grew rosy, as Meg answered slowly,--", "마치 부인이 부드러운 뺨을 쓰다듬자, 메그가 천천히 대답하면서 그 뺨이 갑자기 발그레해졌다."]], [["\"Yes; it's very silly, but I want to tell it, because I hate to have people say and think such things about us and Laurie.\"", "\"네. 정말 어리석은 일이지만, 그래도 말씀드리고 싶어요. 사람들이 우리랑 로리에 대해 그런 말을 하고 그런 생각을 하는 게 너무 싫거든요.\""]], [["Then she told the various bits of gossip she had heard at the Moffats; and, as she spoke, Jo saw her mother fold her lips tightly, as if ill pleased that such ideas should be put into Meg's innocent mind.", "그러고 나서 메그는 모펏 집에서 들은 갖가지 험담을 들려주었다. 메그가 말하는 동안, 조는 어머니가 입술을 꼭 다무는 것을 보았다. 그런 생각이 메그의 순진한 마음에 심겼다는 게 못마땅하다는 듯이."]], [["\"Well, if that isn't the greatest rubbish I ever heard,\"", "\"세상에, 그렇게 터무니없는 헛소리는 처음 들어 봐.\""], ["cried Jo indignantly.", "조가 분개하여 외쳤다."], ["\"Why didn't you pop out and tell them so, on the spot?\"", "\"왜 그 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 그렇다고 쏘아붙이지 않았어?\""]], [["\"I couldn't, it was so embarrassing for me.", "\"그럴 수가 없었어. 너무 난처했거든."], ["I couldn't help hearing, at first, and then I was so angry and ashamed, I didn't remember that I ought to go away.\"", "처음엔 듣지 않을 수가 없었고, 그다음엔 어찌나 화가 나고 부끄럽던지, 자리를 떠야 한다는 생각조차 못 했어.\""]], [["\"Just wait till _I_ see Annie Moffat, and I'll show you how to settle such ridiculous stuff.", "\"애니 모펏을 만나기만 해 봐. 그런 어처구니없는 소리를 어떻게 잠재우는지 보여 줄 테니까."], ["The idea of having 'plans,' and being kind to Laurie, because he's rich, and may marry us by and by!", "'계획'이라니, 그리고 로리가 부자라서, 나중에 우리 중 누구랑 결혼할지도 모른다고 걔한테 잘해 준다니!"], ["Won't he shout, when I tell him what those silly things say about us poor children?\"", "그 바보 같은 것들이 우리 가난한 애들에 대해 떠드는 소릴 로리한테 일러 주면, 걔가 얼마나 깔깔거릴까?\""], ["and Jo laughed, as if, on second thoughts, the thing struck her as a good joke.", "조가 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 다시 생각해 보니 그게 그럴듯한 농담으로 여겨졌던 모양이다."]], [["\"If you tell Laurie, I'll never forgive you!", "\"로리한테 말하면, 너 평생 용서 안 해!"], ["She mustn't, must she, mother?\"", "그러면 안 되죠, 엄마?\""], ["said Meg, looking distressed.", "메그가 괴로운 표정으로 말했다."]], [["\"No; never repeat that foolish gossip, and forget it as soon as you can,\" said Mrs. March gravely. \"I was very unwise to let you go among people of whom I know so little,--kind, I dare say, but worldly, ill-bred, and full of these vulgar ideas about young people. I am more sorry than I can express for the mischief this visit may have done you, Meg.\"", "\"안 되고말고. 그런 어리석은 험담은 두 번 다시 입에 올리지 말고, 되도록 빨리 잊어버리렴.\" 마치 부인이 진지하게 말했다. \"내가 잘 알지도 못하는 사람들 틈에 너를 보낸 게 무척 경솔했구나. 친절한 사람들이긴 하겠지만, 세속적이고, 교양 없고, 젊은이들에 대해 그런 천박한 생각으로 가득 차 있으니. 이번 방문이 너한테 끼쳤을지 모를 해악이, 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 안타깝구나, 메그.\" \"안타까워하지 마세요. 그것 때문에 상처받지 않을 거예요. 나쁜 건 다 잊어버리고 좋은 것만 기억할래요. 정말 즐거운 일도 많았으니까, 보내 주셔서 정말 고마워요. 감상에 젖거나 불만스러워하지 않을 거예요, 엄마. 제가 어리석은 어린애라는 거 잘 알아요. 그러니 혼자 앞가림할 수 있을 만큼 자랄 때까지 엄마 곁에 있을래요. 하지만 칭찬받고 우러름받는 건 역시 기분 좋은 일이라, 솔직히 그게 좋다는 말은 안 할 수가 없네요.\" 메그가 그렇게 고백하면서 반쯤 부끄러운 표정을 지었다."]], [["\"Don't be sorry, I won't let it hurt me; I'll forget all the bad, and remember only the good; for I did enjoy a great deal, and thank you very much for letting me go.", "\"그건 지극히 자연스러운 일이고, 그 좋아하는 마음이 집착이 되어 어리석거나 처녀답지 못한 짓을 하게 만들지만 않는다면 전혀 해로울 게 없단다. 가질 만한 가치가 있는 칭찬이 무엇인지 알아보고 귀히 여기는 법을 배우렴."], ["I'll not be sentimental or dissatisfied, mother; I know I'm a silly little girl, and I'll stay with you till I'm fit to take care of myself.", "그리고 예쁠 뿐 아니라 겸손해서 훌륭한 사람들의 감탄을 자아내는 사람이 되거라, 메그.\" 마거릿이 잠시 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있는 동안, 조는 두 손을 등 뒤로 한 채 흥미로우면서도 약간 어리둥절한 표정으로 서 있었다."], ["But it _is_ nice to be praised and admired, and I can't help saying I like it,\"", "메그가 얼굴을 붉히며 감탄이니 연인이니 그런 것들에 대해 이야기하는 모습은 처음 보았으니까."], ["said Meg, looking half ashamed of the confession.", "조는 지난 두 주 동안 언니가 놀랄 만큼 어른이 되어, 자기는 따라갈 수 없는 세계로 멀어져 가고 있는 듯한 기분이 들었다."]], [["\"That is perfectly natural, and quite harmless, if the liking does not become a passion, and lead one to do foolish or unmaidenly things. Learn to know and value the praise which is worth having, and to excite the admiration of excellent people by being modest as well as pretty, Meg.\"", "\"엄마, 엄마도 모펏 부인이 말한 그런 '계획'이 있으세요?\" 메그가 수줍게 물었다. \"그럼, 얘야, 아주 많단다. 어느 어머니나 그렇지. 다만 내 계획은 모펏 부인의 것과는 좀 다를 거다. 그중 몇 가지를 너희한테 말해 주마. 매우 중대한 문제에 대해, 너의 그 낭만적인 작은 머리와 마음을 바로잡아 줄 말 한마디를 건넬 때가 왔으니 말이다. 너는 아직 어리지만, 내 말을 못 알아들을 만큼 어리지는 않아. 게다가 너 같은 여자아이한테 그런 이야기를 들려주기에는 어머니의 입이 가장 알맞은 법이지. 조, 네 차례도 언젠가 올 테니, 내 '계획'을 잘 들어 두렴. 그리고 그게 좋은 계획이라면, 그걸 이루도록 나를 도와주고.\""]], [["Margaret sat thinking a moment, while Jo stood with her hands behind her, looking both interested and a little perplexed; for it was a new thing to see Meg blushing and talking about admiration, lovers, and things of that sort; and Jo felt as if, during that fortnight, her sister had grown up amazingly, and was drifting away from her into a world where she could not follow.", "조는 의자 팔걸이에 가서 앉았다. 자기들이 무슨 무척 엄숙한 일에라도 동참하려는 거라고 여기는 표정으로. 마치 부인은 두 딸의 손을 하나씩 잡고, 두 어린 얼굴을 애틋하게 바라보며, 진지하면서도 명랑한 특유의 말투로 말했다. \"나는 내 딸들이 아름답고, 교양 있고, 착하기를 바란단다. 사랑받고, 존경받기를 바라지. 행복한 젊은 시절을 보내고, 좋은 사람과 현명하게 짝을 이루며, 하느님이 보시기에 합당한 만큼의 근심과 슬픔만 겪으면서, 쓸모 있고 즐거운 삶을 살기를 바라. 좋은 남자에게 사랑받고 선택받는 것은 여자에게 일어날 수 있는 가장 좋고 가장 달콤한 일이란다. 그러니 내 딸들도 이 아름다운 경험을 알게 되길 진심으로 바라지. 그걸 생각하는 건 자연스러운 일이야, 메그. 그걸 바라며 기다리는 건 옳은 일이고, 그걸 위해 준비하는 건 현명한 일이지. 그래야 행복한 때가 왔을 때, 그 책임을 감당할 준비가 되어 있고 그 기쁨을 누릴 자격이 있다고 느낄 수 있을 테니까. 얘들아, 나는 너희를 위해 야심을 품고 있단다. 하지만 너희가 세상에서 돋보이게 하려는 야심은 아니야. 단지 부자라는 이유로 부자와 결혼하거나, 사랑이 없어 집이라고 할 수도 없는 으리으리한 저택을 차지하려는 게 아니란다. 돈은 꼭 필요하고 귀한 것이며, 잘 쓰면 고귀한 것이기도 해. 하지만 나는 너희가 돈을 좇아야 할 첫째가는, 또는 유일한 보상이라고 여기는 건 결코 바라지 않아. 자존심도 평온함도 없이 왕좌에 앉은 여왕이 되느니, 차라리 행복하고 사랑받고 만족하며 사는 가난한 사람의 아내가 되는 너희를 보는 편이 더 좋겠다.\""]], [["\"Mother, do you have 'plans,' as Mrs.", "\"가난한 여자들은 자기를 내세우지 않으면 가망이 없다고, 벨 언니가 그러던데요.\""], ["Moffat said?\"", "메그가 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["asked Meg bashfully.", "\"그럼 우린 노처녀가 되면 되지.\" 조가 씩씩하게 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, my dear, I have a great many; all mothers do, but mine differ somewhat from Mrs.", "\"옳은 말이다, 조. 불행한 아내가 되거나, 남편감을 찾아 이리저리 뛰어다니는 처녀답지 못한 여자가 되느니, 행복한 노처녀가 되는 편이 낫지.\""], ["Moffat's, I suspect.", "마치 부인이 단호하게 말했다. \"걱정하지 마라, 메그."], ["I will tell you some of them, for the time has come when a word may set this romantic little head and heart of yours right, on a very serious subject.", "가난이 진실한 연인을 물러서게 하는 일은 드무니까. 내가 아는 가장 훌륭하고 가장 존경받는 여자들 중에는 가난한 처녀였던 이들도 있었지만, 사랑받을 만한 사람들이라 노처녀로 남도록 내버려 두질 않았단다."], ["You are young, Meg, but not too young to understand me; and mothers'", "이런 일들은 시간에 맡겨 두렴. 이 집을 행복하게 만들어서, 너희만의 가정을 꾸릴 기회가 온다면 그럴 자격을 갖추고, 그렇지 않더라도 여기서 만족하며 지낼 수 있도록 하렴."], ["lips are the fittest to speak of such things to girls like you.", "한 가지만 기억해 다오, 얘들아. 어머니는 언제나 너희의 비밀을 들어 줄 준비가 되어 있고, 아버지는 너희의 친구가 되어 줄 준비가 되어 있단다."], ["Jo, your turn will come in time, perhaps, so listen to my 'plans,'", "그리고 우리 두 사람은, 결혼을 하든 안 하든, 우리 딸들이 우리 삶의 자랑이자 위안이 되어 주리라 믿고 또 바란단다.\""], ["and help me carry them out, if they are good.\"", "\"그럴게요, 엄마, 꼭 그럴게요!\" 어머니가 잘 자라고 인사하자, 두 딸이 진심을 다해 외쳤다."]], [["Jo went and sat on one arm of the chair, looking as if she thought they were about to join in some very solemn affair.", "10장\nP."], ["Holding a hand of each, and watching the two young faces wistfully, Mrs.", "C.와 P."], ["March said, in her serious yet cheery way,--", "O."]], [["\"I want my daughters to be beautiful, accomplished, and good; to be admired, loved, and respected; to have a happy youth, to be well and wisely married, and to lead useful, pleasant lives, with as little care and sorrow to try them as God sees fit to send. To be loved and chosen by a good man is the best and sweetest thing which can happen to a woman; and I sincerely hope my girls may know this beautiful experience. It is natural to think of it, Meg; right to hope and wait for it, and wise to prepare for it; so that, when the happy time comes, you may feel ready for the duties and worthy of the joy. My dear girls, I _am_ ambitious for you, but not to have you make a dash in the world,--marry rich men merely because they are rich, or have splendid houses, which are not homes because love is wanting. Money is a needful and precious thing,--and, when well used, a noble thing,--but I never want you to think it is the first or only prize to strive for. I'd rather see you poor men's wives, if you were happy, beloved, contented, than queens on thrones, without self-respect and peace.\"", "봄이 다가오자 새로운 놀이들이 유행하기 시작했고, 길어진 낮 덕분에 온갖 일과 놀이를 즐길 긴 오후가 생겼다. 정원을 손봐야 했는데, 자매들은 저마다 그 작은 땅의 한 구획씩을 맡아 마음대로 가꾸기로 했다. 해나는 늘 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"저 정원들이 중국에 있어도, 어느 게 누구 건지 단박에 알아볼 거예요.\" 정말 그럴 만했다. 소녀들의 취향이 각자의 성격만큼이나 제각각이었으니까. 메그의 정원에는 장미와 헬리오트로프, 도금양, 그리고 작은 오렌지나무가 있었다. 조의 화단은 두 해 연속으로 같은 적이 없었다. 늘 이런저런 시도를 했기 때문이다. 올해는 해바라기밭으로 꾸밀 작정이었는데, 그 명랑하고 위로 쭉쭉 뻗는 식물의 씨앗으로 \"코클탑 아주머니\"와 그 병아리 식구들을 먹일 참이었다. 베스의 정원에는 옛날식의 향기로운 꽃들이 있었다. 스위트피와 물푸레향, 참제비고깔, 패랭이꽃, 팬지, 그리고 사철쑥. 거기에 새를 위한 별꽃과 고양이를 위한 개박하도 있었다. 에이미의 정원에는 정자가 하나 있었는데, 다소 작고 집게벌레가 들끓긴 해도 보기에는 무척 예뻤다. 인동덩굴과 나팔꽃이 색색의 뿔과 종 모양 꽃을 우아한 화환처럼 온통 드리우고 있었고, 키 큰 흰 백합과 섬세한 고사리, 그리고 거기서 기꺼이 꽃피우려는 화사하고 그림 같은 식물들도 잔뜩 있었다. 날씨 좋은 날에는 정원 가꾸기와 산책, 강에서 배 젓기, 꽃 찾기로 시간을 보냈다. 비 오는 날에는 집 안에서 즐기는 놀이가 있었는데, 어떤 건 오래된 것이고 어떤 건 새것이었지만, 하나같이 어느 정도 독창적이었다. 그중 하나가 \"P. C.\"였다. 비밀 결사가 유행이었던 터라, 하나쯤 만드는 게 마땅하다고 여겨졌고, 소녀들이 모두 디킨스를 흠모했기에, 자기들을 피크위크 클럽이라고 불렀다. 몇 번 거른 적은 있지만, 그들은 이 모임을 일 년이나 이어 왔고, 매주 토요일 저녁이면 커다란 다락방에 모였다. 그때의 의식은 다음과 같았다. 탁자 앞에 의자 세 개를 한 줄로 놓고, 탁자 위에는 등불 하나와 더불어 저마다 색깔이 다른 큼직한 \"P. C.\" 글자가 박힌 흰 배지 네 개, 그리고 다들 무언가 한 편씩 기고하는 주간 신문 \"피크위크 문집\"을 올려 두었다. 펜과 잉크에 흠뻑 빠져 사는 조가 편집장이었다. 일곱 시가 되면 회원 네 명이 클럽 방으로 올라가, 배지를 머리에 둘러 묶고, 무척 엄숙하게 자리에 앉았다. 메그는 맏이라서 새뮤얼 피크위크였고, 조는 문학적인 기질이 있어서 오거스터스 스노드그래스였으며, 베스는 통통하고 발그레해서 트레이시 텁먼, 그리고 늘 안 되는 일을 해 보려고 애쓰는 에이미는 너새니얼 윙클이었다. 회장인 피크위크가 신문을 낭독했는데, 거기에는 창작 이야기와 시, 동네 소식, 우스운 광고, 그리고 서로의 결점과 부족한 점을 너그럽게 일깨워 주는 충고가 가득했다."]], [["\"Poor girls don't stand any chance, Belle says, unless they put themselves forward,\"", "한번은 피크위크 씨가 알이 없는 안경을 쓰고, 탁자를 톡톡 두드리고, 헛기침을 한 다음, 의자를 뒤로 젖히고 앉아 있던 스노드그래스 씨를 똑바로 노려보았다. 그가 자세를 바로 할 때까지 그러고 있다가, 낭독을 시작했다."], ["sighed Meg.", "피크위크 문집"]], [["\"Then we'll be old maids,\" said Jo stoutly.", "18××년 5월 20일\n시인의 모퉁이"]], [["\"Right, Jo; better be happy old maids than unhappy wives, or unmaidenly girls, running about to find husbands,\" said Mrs. March decidedly. \"Don't be troubled, Meg; poverty seldom daunts a sincere lover. Some of the best and most honored women I know were poor girls, but so love-worthy that they were not allowed to be old maids. Leave these things to time; make this home happy, so that you may be fit for homes of your own, if they are offered you, and contented here if they are not. One thing remember, my girls: mother is always ready to be your confidant, father to be your friend; and both of us trust and hope that our daughters, whether married or single, will be the pride and comfort of our lives.\"", "기념일 송시\n오늘 밤 우리 다시 모여 기리네"]], [["\"We will, Marmee, we will!\" cried both, with all their hearts, as she bade them good-night.", "배지와 엄숙한 의식으로,\n우리의 쉰두 번째 기념일을,"]], [["# THE P. C. AND P. O.", "# THE P. C. AND P. O."]], [["As spring came on, a new set of amusements became the fashion, and the lengthening days gave long afternoons for work and play of all sorts. The garden had to be put in order, and each sister had a quarter of the little plot to do what she liked with. Hannah used to say, \"I'd know which each of them gardings belonged to, ef I see 'em in Chiny;\" and so she might, for the girls' tastes differed as much as their characters. Meg's had roses and heliotrope, myrtle, and a little orange-tree in it. Jo's bed was never alike two seasons, for she was always trying experiments; this year it was to be a plantation of sun-flowers, the seeds of which cheerful and aspiring plant were to feed \"Aunt Cockle-top\" and her family of chicks. Beth had old-fashioned, fragrant flowers in her garden,--sweet peas and mignonette, larkspur, pinks, pansies, and southernwood, with chickweed for the bird, and catnip for the pussies. Amy had a bower in hers,--rather small and earwiggy, but very pretty to look at,--with honeysuckles and morning-glories hanging their colored horns and bells in graceful wreaths all over it; tall, white lilies, delicate ferns, and as many brilliant, picturesque plants as would consent to blossom there.", "이 피크위크 홀에서. 우리 모두 더없이 건강하게 모였고,"]], [["Gardening, walks, rows on the river, and flower-hunts employed the fine days; and for rainy ones, they had house diversions,--some old, some new,--all more or less original. One of these was the \"P. C.\"; for, as secret societies were the fashion, it was thought proper to have one; and, as all of the girls admired Dickens, they called themselves the Pickwick Club. With a few interruptions, they had kept this up for a year, and met every Saturday evening in the big garret, on which occasions the ceremonies were as follows: Three chairs were arranged in a row before a table, on which was a lamp, also four white badges, with a big \"P. C.\" in different colors on each, and the weekly newspaper, called \"The Pickwick Portfolio,\" to which all contributed something; while Jo, who revelled in pens and ink, was the editor. At seven o'clock, the four members ascended to the club-room, tied their badges round their heads, and took their seats with great solemnity. Meg, as the eldest, was Samuel Pickwick; Jo, being of a literary turn, Augustus Snodgrass; Beth, because she was round and rosy, Tracy Tupman, and Amy, who was always trying to do what she couldn't, was Nathaniel Winkle. Pickwick, the president, read the paper, which was filled with original tales, poetry, local news, funny advertisements, and hints, in which they good-naturedly reminded each other of their faults and short-comings.", "우리 작은 무리에서 빠진 이 하나 없네. 다시금 낯익은 얼굴들을 마주하고,"]], [["On one occasion, Mr. Pickwick put on a pair of spectacles without any glasses, rapped upon the table, hemmed, and, having stared hard at Mr.", "정다운 손을 마주 잡네."], ["Snodgrass, who was tilting back in his chair, till he arranged himself properly, began to read:--", "언제나 제자리를 지키는 우리 피크위크를,"]], [["The Pickwick Portfolio.", "우리는 경의를 담아 맞이하네. 코에 안경을 걸친 채, 그가"]], [["Poet's Corner.", "알차게 채운 우리 주간지를 읽어 내릴 때."]], [["# ANNIVERSARY ODE.", "# ANNIVERSARY ODE."]], [["Again we meet to celebrate With badge and solemn rite, Our fifty-second anniversary, In Pickwick Hall, to-night.", "비록 그가 감기로 고생하고 있어도,\n우리는 그의 말을 들어 기쁘니,"]], [["We all are here in perfect health, None gone from our small band; Again we see each well-known face, And press each friendly hand.", "그의 입에서 지혜로운 말씀이 떨어지는 까닭에,\n곤돌라가 끽끽거리며 삐걱대도."]], [["Our Pickwick, always at his post, With reverence we greet, As, spectacles on nose, he reads Our well-filled weekly sheet.", "키 큰 육 척의 스노드그래스가 우뚝 솟아오르네,\n코끼리처럼 우아한 자태로,"]], [["Although he suffers from a cold, We joy to hear him speak, For words of wisdom from him fall, In spite of croak or squeak.", "갈색의 유쾌한 얼굴로\n좌중을 환히 비추네."]], [["Old six-foot Snodgrass looms on high, With elephantine grace, And beams upon the company, With brown and jovial face.", "그의 눈에는 시의 불꽃이 타오르고,\n제 운명과 맞서 싸우는도다."]], [["Poetic fire lights up his eye, He struggles 'gainst his lot. Behold ambition on his brow, And on his nose a blot!", "보라, 그 이마에 서린 야망을,\n그리고 코에 묻은 얼룩을!"]], [["Next our peaceful Tupman comes, So rosy, plump, and sweet. Who chokes with laughter at the puns, And tumbles off his seat.", "다음으로 우리의 온화한 터프먼이 오네,\n장밋빛으로 통통하고 사랑스러운 그가."]], [["Prim little Winkle too is here, With every hair in place, A model of propriety, Though he hates to wash his face.", "익살에 웃음을 참지 못해 컥컥대다가\n의자에서 굴러떨어지고 마는도다."]], [["The year is gone, we still unite To joke and laugh and read, And tread the path of literature That doth to glory lead.", "깔끔한 꼬마 윙클도 여기 있네,\n머리카락 한 올 흐트러짐 없이,"]], [["Long may our paper prosper well, Our club unbroken be, And coming years their blessings pour On the useful, gay \"P. C.\"", "예의범절의 본보기 같지만,\n세수하기는 끔찍이도 싫어하지."]], [["# A TALE OF VENICE.", "# A TALE OF VENICE."]], [["Gondola after gondola swept up to the marble steps, and left its lovely load to swell the brilliant throng that filled the stately halls of Count de Adelon. Knights and ladies, elves and pages, monks and flower-girls, all mingled gayly in the dance. Sweet voices and rich melody filled the air; and so with mirth and music the masquerade went on.", "한 해가 지나도, 우리는 여전히 모여\n농담하고 웃고 글을 읽으며,"]], [["\"Has your Highness seen the Lady Viola to-night?\" asked a gallant troubadour of the fairy queen who floated down the hall upon his arm.", "영광으로 이끄는\n문학의 길을 걸어가네."]], [["\"Yes; is she not lovely, though so sad! Her dress is well chosen, too, for in a week she weds Count Antonio, whom she passionately hates.\"", "우리의 신문이여 오래도록 번창하라,\n우리의 클럽이 깨지지 않기를,"]], [["\"By my faith, I envy him. Yonder he comes, arrayed like a bridegroom, except the black mask. When that is off we shall see how he regards the fair maid whose heart he cannot win, though her stern father bestows her hand,\" returned the troubadour.", "그리고 다가오는 세월이 그 축복을\n유익하고 흥겨운 \"피클럽\"에 쏟아붓기를."]], [["\"'Tis whispered that she loves the young English artist who haunts her steps, and is spurned by the old count,\"", "A. 스노드그래스."], ["said the lady, as they joined the dance.", "가면을 쓴 결혼식."]], [["The revel was at its height when a priest appeared, and, withdrawing the young pair to an alcove hung with purple velvet, he motioned them to kneel.", "베네치아 이야기. 곤돌라가 줄지어 대리석 계단으로 미끄러져 들어와, 그 사랑스러운 짐을 부려 놓으니, 데 아델롱 백작의 위풍당당한 홀을 가득 메운 화려한 무리는 더욱 불어났다."], ["Instant silence fell upon the gay throng; and not a sound, but the dash of fountains or the rustle of orange-groves sleeping in the moonlight, broke the hush, as Count de Adelon spoke thus:--", "기사와 귀부인, 요정과 시동, 수도사와 꽃 파는 소녀가 모두 흥겹게 어우러져 춤을 추었다. 감미로운 목소리와 풍성한 선율이 공중에 가득했고, 그렇게 환희와 음악 속에서 가장무도회는 계속되었다."]], [["\"My lords and ladies, pardon the ruse by which I have gathered you here to witness the marriage of my daughter. Father, we wait your services.\"", "\"전하, 오늘 밤 비올라 아가씨를 보셨습니까?\" 늠름한 음유시인이 자기 팔에 기대어 홀을 흘러내리듯 거니는 요정 여왕에게 물었다. \"보았네. 그토록 슬픈데도 어찌 그리 사랑스러운지! 옷차림도 잘 골랐구나. 일주일 후면 안토니오 백작과 혼인하니까. 그녀가 그토록 열렬히 미워하는 사람이건만.\""]], [["All eyes turned toward the bridal party, and a low murmur of amazement went through the throng, for neither bride nor groom removed their masks. Curiosity and wonder possessed all hearts, but respect restrained all tongues till the holy rite was over. Then the eager spectators gathered round the count, demanding an explanation.", "\"맹세코, 그가 부럽습니다. 저기 그가 오는군요, 검은 가면만 빼면 신랑처럼 차려입었습니다. 저 가면을 벗으면 그가 그 아리따운 처녀를 어떻게 바라보는지 알게 되겠지요. 그녀의 매정한 아버지가 손을 내어 준다 해도, 그는 그녀의 마음을 얻지 못하니까요.\" 음유시인이 대답했다. \"소문에는 그녀가 자기 발길을 좇아다니는 젊은 영국 화가를 사랑한다는군요. 늙은 백작이 그를 내쳤다고 합니다.\" 귀부인이 말하며, 두 사람은 춤판에 끼어들었다."]], [["\"Gladly would I give it if I could; but I only know that it was the whim of my timid Viola, and I yielded to it.", "흥겨움이 절정에 이르렀을 때 한 사제가 나타나, 젊은 한 쌍을 자줏빛 벨벳이 드리워진 골방으로 데려가더니, 무릎을 꿇으라고 손짓했다. 흥겹던 무리에 순식간에 정적이 내려앉았다."], ["Now, my children, let the play end. Unmask, and receive my blessing.\"", "분수가 부서지는 소리와 달빛 속에 잠든 오렌지 숲이 사각이는 소리 외에는 그 어떤 소리도 정적을 깨지 못했으니, 데 아델롱 백작이 이렇게 말하는 동안이었다."]], [["But neither bent the knee; for the young bridegroom replied, in a tone that startled all listeners, as the mask fell, disclosing the noble face of Ferdinand Devereux, the artist lover; and, leaning on the breast where now flashed the star of an English earl, was the lovely Viola, radiant with joy and beauty.", "\"신사 숙녀 여러분, 제 딸의 혼례를 지켜봐 주십사 이리로 여러분을 모은 술책을 용서하시오. 신부님, 의식을 기다리고 있습니다.\" 모든 눈이 신혼부부 쪽으로 쏠렸고, 무리 사이로 나직한 경탄의 웅성거림이 번졌다. 신부도 신랑도 가면을 벗지 않았기 때문이다. 호기심과 의아함이 모든 가슴을 사로잡았으나, 신성한 의식이 끝날 때까지 경의가 모든 입을 억눌렀다. 그러다 의식이 끝나자 애가 단 구경꾼들이 백작 주위로 모여들어 해명을 요구했다."]], [["\"My lord, you scornfully bade me claim your daughter when I could boast as high a name and vast a fortune as the Count Antonio. I can do more; for even your ambitious soul cannot refuse the Earl of Devereux and De Vere, when he gives his ancient name and boundless wealth in return for the beloved hand of this fair lady, now my wife.\"", "\"내가 알기만 한다면 기꺼이 말해 주겠소. 허나 나는 그저 그것이 수줍은 내 딸 비올라의 변덕이었고, 내가 거기에 따라 주었다는 것밖에 모르오. 자, 얘들아, 이제 연극을 끝내자꾸나. 가면을 벗고 내 축복을 받으렴.\" 그러나 둘 중 누구도 무릎을 꿇지 않았다. 젊은 신랑이, 가면이 떨어져 화가 연인 페르디난드 디브로의 고결한 얼굴이 드러나는 가운데, 듣는 이들 모두를 놀라게 하는 어조로 대답했기 때문이다. 그리고 이제 영국 백작의 별 모양 훈장이 번쩍이는 그 가슴에 기댄 것은, 기쁨과 아름다움으로 빛나는 사랑스러운 비올라였다."]], [["The count stood like one changed to stone; and, turning to the bewildered crowd, Ferdinand added, with a gay smile of triumph, \"To you, my gallant friends, I can only wish that your wooing may prosper as mine has done; and that you may all win as fair a bride as I have, by this masked marriage.\"", "\"백작이시여, 당신은 제가 안토니오 백작만큼 높은 가문과 막대한 재산을 자랑할 수 있을 때 당신의 딸을 데려가라고 비웃으며 말씀하셨지요. 저는 그 이상을 할 수 있습니다. 디브로 앤드 디비어 백작이 자신의 유서 깊은 가문과 무한한 재산을 내어 주고 이 아리따운 여인, 이제는 제 아내가 된 사람의 사랑하는 손을 얻겠다는데, 당신의 야심 찬 영혼인들 어찌 거절할 수 있겠습니까.\" 백작은 돌로 변한 사람처럼 우뚝 서 있었다. 페르디난드는 어리둥절한 무리 쪽으로 돌아서서, 승리의 흥겨운 미소를 지으며 덧붙였다. \"늠름한 벗들이여, 그대들에게 바라노니, 그대들의 구애도 나의 것처럼 무르익기를. 그리고 이 가면을 쓴 결혼식으로 내가 그러했듯이, 그대들 모두가 이토록 아리따운 신부를 얻게 되기를.\""]], [["* * * * *", "S. 픽윅."]], [["Why is the P. C.", "피클럽은 어째서 바벨탑과 같은가?"], ["like the Tower of Babel? It is full of unruly members.", "제멋대로 구는 회원들로 가득하기 때문이다."]], [["* * * * *", "호박 한 통의 역사."]], [["# THE HISTORY OF A SQUASH.", "# THE HISTORY OF A SQUASH."]], [["Once upon a time a farmer planted a little seed in his garden, and after a while it sprouted and became a vine, and bore many squashes.", "옛날 옛적에 한 농부가 자기 밭에 작은 씨앗 하나를 심었는데, 얼마 지나자 그것이 싹을 틔워 덩굴이 되었고 호박을 잔뜩 맺었다."], ["One day in October, when they were ripe, he picked one and took it to market.", "시월 어느 날, 호박이 다 익자 농부는 하나를 따서 장에 가져갔다."], ["A grocer-man bought and put it in his shop.", "어느 식료품 장수가 그것을 사다 가게에 놓아두었다."], ["That same morning, a little girl, in a brown hat and blue dress, with a round face and snub nose, went and bought it for her mother.", "바로 그날 아침, 갈색 모자에 파란 원피스를 입고, 둥근 얼굴에 들창코를 한 어린 소녀가 와서 자기 엄마를 위해 그것을 샀다. 소녀는 호박을 낑낑대며 집으로 날라다 잘라서 큰 솥에 삶았다."], ["She lugged it home, cut it up, and boiled it in the big pot; mashed some of it, with salt and butter, for dinner; and to the rest she added a pint of milk, two eggs, four spoons of sugar, nutmeg, and some crackers; put it in a deep dish, and baked it till it was brown and nice; and next day it was eaten by a family named March.", "그중 일부는 소금과 버터를 넣고 으깨어 저녁상에 올렸고, 나머지에는 우유 한 파인트와 달걀 두 개, 설탕 네 숟갈, 육두구, 그리고 크래커를 좀 넣어, 깊은 접시에 담아 노릇노릇 먹음직스러워질 때까지 구웠다. 그리고 이튿날 그것은 마치라는 가족이 먹어 치웠다."], ["T.", "T."], ["TUPMAN.", "터프먼."]], [["* * * * *", "픽윅 씨, 귀하께,"]], [["I address you upon the subject of sin the sinner I mean is a man named Winkle who makes trouble in his club by laughing and sometimes won't write his piece in this fine paper I hope you will pardon his badness and let him send a French fable because he can't write out of his head as he has so many lessons to do and no brains in future I will try to take time by the fetlock and prepare some work which will be all _commy la fo_ that means all right I am in haste as it is nearly school time", "저는 죄에 관한 문제로 귀하께 말씀드립니다 제가 말하는 죄인은 윙클이라는 사람으로 웃어서 클럽에 말썽을 일으키고 가끔은 이 훌륭한 신문에 제 글을 안 쓰려 합니다 부디 그의 나쁜 짓을 용서하시고 프랑스 우화를 보내도록 허락해 주시기 바랍니다 왜냐하면 그는 할 공부가 너무 많고 머리에 든 것이 없어 제 머리로 글을 못 짓기 때문입니다 앞으로는 시간의 발목을 잡아 일을 좀 준비해 보겠습니다 그러면 다 콤미 라 포 할 것인데 그것은 다 괜찮다는 뜻입니다 거의 등교 시간이라 바쁩니다\n존경을 담아 올림, N. 윙클."]], [["Yours respectably, N.", "(위의 글은 지난날의 비행을 사내답고 보기 좋게 인정한 것이다."], ["WINKLE.", "우리 젊은 벗이 구두점을 공부한다면 더욱 좋겠다.)\n슬픈 사고."]], [["[The above is a manly and handsome acknowledgment of past misdemeanors. If our young friend studied punctuation, it would be well.]", "지난 금요일, 우리는 지하실에서 일어난 격렬한 충격에 깜짝 놀랐고, 뒤이어 비탄에 잠긴 비명이 들려왔다. 한 무리가 되어 지하실로 달려간 우리는, 사랑하는 우리 회장님이 집안일에 쓸 장작을 가지러 갔다가 발이 걸려 넘어져 바닥에 엎어진 것을 발견했다. 완전한 폐허의 장면이 우리 눈앞에 펼쳐졌으니, 픽윅 씨는 넘어지면서 머리와 어깨를 물통에 처박았고, 무른 비누 한 통을 사내다운 몸 위에 엎어 버렸으며, 옷을 심하게 찢어 놓았기 때문이다. 이 위태로운 처지에서 끌어내어진 후, 그가 여러 군데 멍든 것 말고는 다친 데가 없음이 밝혀졌고, 덧붙여 기쁘게 알리노니, 지금은 잘 회복하고 있다. 편집장."]], [["# A SAD ACCIDENT.", "# A SAD ACCIDENT."]], [["On Friday last, we were startled by a violent shock in our basement, followed by cries of distress.", "공공의 사별."], ["On rushing, in a body, to the cellar, we discovered our beloved President prostrate upon the floor, having tripped and fallen while getting wood for domestic purposes.", "우리는 소중한 벗, 스노볼 팻 포 부인의 갑작스럽고 불가사의한 실종을 기록해야 하는 가슴 아픈 의무를 진다."], ["A perfect scene of ruin met our eyes; for in his fall Mr. Pickwick had plunged his head and shoulders into a tub of water, upset a keg of soft soap upon his manly form, and torn his garments badly.", "이 사랑스럽고 정 깊은 고양이는 따뜻하고 흠모하는 벗들로 이루어진 커다란 모임의 귀염둥이였다."], ["On being removed from this perilous situation, it was discovered that he had suffered no injury but several bruises; and, we are happy to add, is now doing well. ED.", "그 미모는 모든 눈길을 사로잡았고, 그 우아함과 미덕은 모든 가슴에 사랑받았으며, 그 상실은 온 공동체가 깊이 느끼는 바이다."]], [["*************************************************************** * * * THE PUBLIC BEREAVEMENT. * * * * It is our painful duty to record the sudden and mysterious * * disappearance of our cherished friend, Mrs.", "마지막으로 목격되었을 때, 그녀는 대문가에 앉아 푸줏간 수레를 지켜보고 있었다."], ["Snowball Pat * * Paw. This lovely and beloved cat was the pet of a large * * circle of warm and admiring friends; for her beauty * * attracted all eyes, her graces and virtues endeared her to * * all hearts, and her loss is deeply felt by the whole * * community.", "그 매력에 홀린 어느 악당이 비열하게 그녀를 훔쳐 간 것이 아닌가 우려된다."], ["* * * * When last seen, she was sitting at the gate, watching the * * butcher's cart; and it is feared that some villain, tempted * * by her charms, basely stole her. Weeks have passed, but no * * trace of her has been discovered; and we relinquish all * * hope, tie a black ribbon to her basket, set aside her dish, * * and weep for her as one lost to us forever.", "몇 주가 지났으나 그녀의 흔적은 발견되지 않았으니, 우리는 모든 희망을 접고, 그녀의 바구니에 검은 리본을 매고, 밥그릇을 치워 두고, 영영 잃어버린 이를 위하듯 그녀를 위해 운다."], ["* * * ***************************************************************", "마음을 함께하는 벗이 다음의 보석 같은 시를 보내왔다."]], [["A sympathizing friend sends the following gem:--", "S. B. 팻 포를 위한\n애가."]], [["# FOR S. B. PAT PAW.", "# FOR S. B. PAT PAW."]], [["We mourn the loss of our little pet, And sigh o'er her hapless fate, For never more by the fire she'll sit, Nor play by the old green gate.", "우리는 우리의 작은 귀염둥이를 잃고 슬퍼하며,\n그 가엾은 운명을 두고 한숨짓네,"]], [["The little grave where her infant sleeps, Is 'neath the chestnut tree; But o'er _her_ grave we may not weep, We know not where it may be.", "다시는 난롯가에 앉지 못할 테고,\n낡은 푸른 대문가에서 놀지 못할 테니."]], [["Her empty bed, her idle ball, Will never see her more; No gentle tap, no loving purr Is heard at the parlor-door.", "그 새끼가 잠든 작은 무덤은\n밤나무 아래 있건만,"]], [["Another cat comes after her mice, A cat with a dirty face; But she does not hunt as our darling did, Nor play with her airy grace.", "그녀의 무덤 위에서는 울 수가 없네,\n그것이 어디 있는지 알 수 없으니."]], [["Her stealthy paws tread the very hall Where Snowball used to play, But she only spits at the dogs our pet So gallantly drove away.", "텅 빈 잠자리, 버려진 공은\n다시는 그녀를 보지 못하리,"]], [["She is useful and mild, and does her best, But she is not fair to see; And we cannot give her your place, dear, Nor worship her as we worship thee. A. S.", "응접실 문가에서는\n상냥한 발톱질도, 사랑스러운 가르랑 소리도 들리지 않네."]], [["MISS ORANTHY BLUGGAGE, the accomplished Strong-Minded Lecturer, will deliver her famous Lecture on \"WOMAN AND HER POSITION,\" at Pickwick Hall, next Saturday Evening, after the usual performances.", "다른 고양이가 그녀의 쥐를 쫓아 들어왔으니,\n얼굴이 더러운 고양이라네."]], [["A WEEKLY MEETING will be held at Kitchen Place, to teach young ladies how to cook. Hannah Brown will preside; and all are invited to attend.", "허나 그것은 우리 귀염둥이처럼 사냥하지 못하고,\n그 사뿐한 우아함으로 놀지도 못하네."]], [["THE DUSTPAN SOCIETY will meet on Wednesday next, and parade in the upper story of the Club House. All members to appear in uniform and shoulder their brooms at nine precisely.", "그 살금대는 발이 바로 그 홀을 밟고 다니네,\n스노볼이 놀곤 하던 그곳을."]], [["MRS. BETH BOUNCER will open her new assortment of Doll's Millinery next week. The latest Paris Fashions have arrived, and orders are respectfully solicited.", "허나 그것은 우리 귀염둥이가 그토록\n늠름하게 쫓아 버린 개들에게 침이나 뱉을 뿐."]], [["A NEW PLAY will appear at the Barnville Theatre, in the course of a few weeks, which will surpass anything ever seen on the American stage. \"THE GREEK SLAVE, or Constantine the Avenger,\" is the name of this thrilling drama!!!", "쓸모 있고 온순하며 제 딴엔 애를 쓰지만,\n보기에 아름답지는 못하네."]], [["If S. P. didn't use so much soap on his hands, he wouldn't always be late at breakfast. A. S. is requested not to whistle in the street. T. T. please don't forget Amy's napkin. N. W. must not fret because his dress has not nine tucks.", "그러니 우리는 그것에게 네 자리를 줄 수 없단다, 얘야,\n우리가 너를 떠받들 듯 그것을 떠받들 수도 없단다."]], [["Meg--Good. Jo--Bad.", "A."], ["Beth--Very good.", "S."], ["Amy--Middling.", "광고."]], [["As the President finished reading the paper (which I beg leave to assure my readers is a _bona fide_ copy of one written by _bona fide_ girls once upon a time), a round of applause followed, and then Mr.", "박식한 강성 여성 강연자, 오랜시 블러기지 양이 그 유명한 강연 \"여성과 그 지위\"를, 다음 토요일 저녁, 평소의 공연이 끝난 후, 픽윅 홀에서 행할 예정입니다. 부엌터에서 매주 모임이 열려 젊은 숙녀들에게 요리하는 법을 가르칠 것입니다."], ["Snodgrass rose to make a proposition.", "해나 브라운이 사회를 맡을 것이며, 누구나 참석을 환영합니다."]], [["\"Mr. President and gentlemen,\"", "쓰레받기 협회가 다음 수요일에 모여, 클럽 회관 위층에서 행진을 합니다."], ["he began, assuming a parliamentary attitude and tone, \"I wish to propose the admission of a new member,--one who highly deserves the honor, would be deeply grateful for it, and would add immensely to the spirit of the club, the literary value of the paper, and be no end jolly and nice.", "모든 회원은 제복 차림으로 나와 아홉 시 정각에 빗자루를 어깨에 멜 것."], ["I propose Mr. Theodore Laurence as an honorary member of the P.", "베스 바운서 부인이 다음 주에 인형 모자 신상품을 선보입니다."], ["C. Come now, do have him.\"", "최신 파리 유행품이 도착했으며, 주문을 정중히 받습니다."]], [["Jo's sudden change of tone made the girls laugh; but all looked rather anxious, and no one said a word, as Snodgrass took his seat.", "새 연극이 몇 주 안에 반빌 극장에서 막을 올릴 것이니, 미국 무대에서 일찍이 본 그 무엇도 능가할 것입니다. \"그리스인 노예, 또는 복수자 콘스탄틴\"이 이 짜릿한 드라마의 제목입니다!!! 귀띔."]], [["\"We'll put it to vote,\"", "S. P.가 손에 비누를 그렇게 많이 쓰지 않는다면, 늘 아침 식사에 늦지는 않을 것이다."], ["said the President.", "A. S.는 길에서 휘파람을 불지 말 것을 부탁받는다."], ["\"All in favor of this motion please to manifest it by saying 'Ay.'\"", "T. T.는 에이미의 냅킨을 잊지 말기를 바란다."], ["A loud response from Snodgrass, followed, to everybody's surprise, by a timid one from Beth.", "N. W.는 자기 옷에 주름이 아홉 개 안 잡혔다고 안달하면 안 된다."]], [["\"Contrary minded say 'No.'\"", "주간 보고. 메그 — 양호."]], [["Meg and Amy were contrary minded; and Mr. Winkle rose to say, with great elegance, \"We don't wish any boys; they only joke and bounce about.", "조 — 불량."], ["This is a ladies' club, and we wish to be private and proper.\"", "베스 — 매우 양호."]], [["\"I'm afraid he'll laugh at our paper, and make fun of us afterward,\"", "에이미 — 보통."], ["observed Pickwick, pulling the little curl on her forehead, as she always did when doubtful.", "회장이 신문 낭독을 마치자(독자 여러분께 굳이 알려 드리거니와, 이것은 옛날 옛적 실제 소녀들이 쓴 진짜 신문을 충실히 옮긴 것이다), 한바탕 박수가 뒤따랐고, 이어 스노드그래스 씨가 일어나 한 가지 제안을 했다."]], [["Up rose Snodgrass, very much in earnest.", "\"회장님, 그리고 신사 여러분.\" 그녀는 의회식 자세와 어조를 취하며 운을 뗐다."], ["\"Sir, I give you my word as a gentleman, Laurie won't do anything of the sort.", "\"저는 새 회원의 가입을 제안하고자 합니다. 그 영예를 받을 자격이 충분하고, 그 영예에 깊이 감사할 것이며, 클럽의 활기와 신문의 문학적 가치에 엄청나게 보탬이 될 데다, 한없이 유쾌하고 좋은 사람입니다."], ["He likes to write, and he'll give a tone to our contributions, and keep us from being sentimental, don't you see?", "저는 시어도어 로런스 씨를 피클럽의 명예 회원으로 추천합니다. 자, 어서요, 받아들여 주세요.\""], ["We can do so little for him, and he does so much for us, I think the least we can do is to offer him a place here, and make him welcome if he comes.\"", "조의 갑작스러운 어조 변화에 소녀들은 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그러나 다들 다소 불안한 기색이었고, 스노드그래스가 자리에 앉을 때까지 아무도 한마디 하지 않았다."]], [["This artful allusion to benefits conferred brought Tupman to his feet, looking as if he had quite made up his mind.", "\"표결에 부치겠습니다.\" 회장이 말했다. \"이 동의에 찬성하시는 분은 '찬성'이라고 말하여 의사를 표시해 주세요.\""]], [["\"Yes, we ought to do it, even if we _are_ afraid.", "스노드그래스가 큰 소리로 응답했고, 뒤이어 모두를 놀라게 하며 베스가 머뭇머뭇 응답했다."], ["I say he _may_ come, and his grandpa, too, if he likes.\"", "\"반대하시는 분은 '반대'라고 하세요.\""]], [["This spirited burst from Beth electrified the club, and Jo left her seat to shake hands approvingly. \"Now then, vote again.", "메그와 에이미가 반대 의견이었다."], ["Everybody remember it's our Laurie, and say 'Ay!'\"", "윙클 씨가 일어나 매우 우아하게 말했다."], ["cried Snodgrass excitedly.", "\"우리는 사내아이는 누구도 원치 않아요."], ["\"Ay! ay!", "걔들은 농담이나 하고 이리저리 까불기만 하잖아요."], ["ay!\" replied three voices at once.", "이건 숙녀들의 클럽이고, 우리는 사적이고 품위 있게 지내고 싶다고요.\""]], [["\"Good! Bless you!", "\"걔가 우리 신문을 비웃고, 나중에 우리를 놀려 댈까 봐 걱정이에요.\""], ["Now, as there's nothing like 'taking time by the _fetlock_,'", "픽윅이 말하며, 미심쩍을 때면 늘 그러듯 이마에 드리운 작은 곱슬머리를 잡아당겼다. 스노드그래스가 매우 진지하게 일어섰다."], ["as Winkle characteristically observes, allow me to present the new member;\"", "\"회장님, 신사로서 맹세하건대, 로리는 절대 그런 짓은 안 할 겁니다. 걔는 글쓰기를 좋아하고, 우리 기고문에 격조를 더해 줄 거고, 우리가 감상적으로 흐르지 않게 막아 줄 거예요, 아시겠어요?"], ["and, to the dismay of the rest of the club, Jo threw open the door of the closet, and displayed Laurie sitting on a rag-bag, flushed and twinkling with suppressed laughter.", "우리는 걔를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 게 거의 없는데, 걔는 우리를 위해 그토록 많은 걸 해 주잖아요. 그러니 최소한 여기 자리 하나 내주고, 걔가 오면 반갑게 맞아 주는 게 우리가 할 수 있는 일이라고 생각해요.\""]], [["\"You rogue! you traitor!", "베푼 은혜를 이렇게 교묘히 들먹이자 터프먼이 자리에서 일어났는데, 마음을 단단히 정한 듯한 표정이었다."], ["Jo, how could you?\"", "\"그래요, 우리가 비록 겁이 난다 해도, 그렇게 해야 해요."], ["cried the three girls, as Snodgrass led her friend triumphantly forth; and, producing both a chair and a badge, installed him in a jiffy.", "걔가 와도 좋다고 말하겠어요. 걔가 원하면 그 할아버지도요.\""]], [["\"The coolness of you two rascals is amazing,\" began Mr.", "베스의 이 활기찬 외침은 클럽을 짜릿하게 했고, 조는 찬성의 뜻으로 악수하려고 자리에서 일어났다."], ["Pickwick, trying to get up an awful frown, and only succeeding in producing an amiable smile.", "\"자 그럼, 다시 표결합시다."], ["But the new member was equal to the occasion; and, rising, with a grateful salutation to the Chair, said, in the most engaging manner, \"Mr.", "다들 우리 로리라는 거 기억하고, '찬성!'이라고 외쳐요!\""], ["President and ladies,--I beg pardon, gentlemen,--allow me to introduce myself as Sam Weller, the very humble servant of the club.\"", "스노드그래스가 흥분하여 소리쳤다."]], [["\"Good!", "\"찬성!"], ["good!\"", "찬성!"], ["cried Jo, pounding with the handle of the old warming-pan on which she leaned.", "찬성!\" 세 목소리가 한꺼번에 대답했다."]], [["\"My faithful friend and noble patron,\"", "\"좋아요! 고마워요!"], ["continued Laurie, with a wave of the hand, \"who has so flatteringly presented me, is not to be blamed for the base stratagem of to-night.", "자, 윙클이 그 특유의 말투로 짚었듯 '시간의 발목을 잡는 것'만 한 게 없으니, 새 회원을 소개하게 해 주세요.\""], ["I planned it, and she only gave in after lots of teasing.\"", "그러더니 나머지 클럽 회원들이 경악하는 가운데, 조가 벽장 문을 활짝 열어젖히자, 로리가 헝겊 자루 위에 앉아 웃음을 꾹 참느라 얼굴이 발그레해진 채 눈을 반짝이고 있는 것이 드러났다."]], [["\"Come now, don't lay it all on yourself; you know I proposed the cupboard,\"", "\"이 장난꾸러기! 이 배신자!"], ["broke in Snodgrass, who was enjoying the joke amazingly.", "조, 어떻게 그럴 수 있어?\" 세 소녀가 외쳤고, 그러는 사이 스노드그래스는 의기양양하게 자기 벗을 앞으로 데리고 나와, 의자와 배지를 둘 다 마련해 순식간에 그를 자리에 앉혔다."]], [["\"Never you mind what she says.", "\"이 두 악당의 태연함이란 놀랍기 짝이 없군요.\""], ["I'm the wretch that did it, sir,\"", "픽윅 씨가 무섭게 인상을 쓰려 애쓰며 운을 뗐지만, 결국 인자한 미소만 짓고 말았다."], ["said the new member, with a Welleresque nod to Mr. Pickwick.", "그러나 새 회원은 그 상황에 능히 대처했다."], ["\"But on my honor, I never will do so again, and henceforth _dewote_ myself to the interest of this immortal club.\" \"Hear!", "그는 일어나 의장석에 감사의 인사를 올리며, 더없이 매력적인 태도로 말했다."], ["hear!\" cried Jo, clashing the lid of the warming-pan like a cymbal.", "\"회장님, 그리고 숙녀 여러분 — 아, 죄송합니다, 신사 여러분 — 클럽의 더없이 미천한 종, 샘 웰러로 제 소개를 하게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["\"Go on, go on!\"", "\"좋아! 좋아!\""], ["added Winkle and Tupman, while the President bowed benignly.", "조가 기대고 있던 낡은 침대 데우개 손잡이로 쾅쾅 두드리며 외쳤다."]], [["\"I merely wish to say, that as a slight token of my gratitude for the honor done me, and as a means of promoting friendly relations between adjoining nations, I have set up a post-office in the hedge in the lower corner of the garden; a fine, spacious building, with padlocks on the doors, and every convenience for the mails,--also the females, if I may be allowed the expression.", "\"저를 이토록 과분하게 소개해 준 제 충실한 벗이자 고귀한 후원자는,\" 로리가 손을 한 번 휘저으며 말을 이었다."], ["It's the old martin-house; but I've stopped up the door, and made the roof open, so it will hold all sorts of things, and save our valuable time.", "\"오늘 밤의 이 비열한 계략을 두고 탓할 사람이 아닙니다. 제가 그것을 꾸몄고, 그녀는 한참 졸라 댄 끝에야 넘어왔을 뿐입니다.\""], ["Letters, manuscripts, books, and bundles can be passed in there; and, as each nation has a key, it will be uncommonly nice, I fancy.", "\"이봐, 다 자기 탓으로만 돌리지 마. 벽장은 내가 제안한 거 너도 알잖아.\""], ["Allow me to present the club key; and, with many thanks for your favor, take my seat.\"", "스노드그래스가 끼어들었는데, 이 농담을 엄청나게 즐기고 있었다."]], [["Great applause as Mr. Weller deposited a little key on the table, and subsided; the warming-pan clashed and waved wildly, and it was some time before order could be restored. A long discussion followed, and every one came out surprising, for every one did her best; so it was an unusually lively meeting, and did not adjourn till a late hour, when it broke up with three shrill cheers for the new member.", "\"그녀가 하는 말은 신경 쓰지 마십시오. 그 짓을 한 비열한 놈은 접니다, 회장님.\" 새 회원이 픽윅 씨에게 웰러식으로 고개를 까딱하며 말했다. \"허나 명예를 걸고, 다시는 그러지 않을 것이며, 이제부터 이 불멸의 클럽의 이익에 저 자신을 바치겠습니다.\" \"옳소! 옳소!\" 조가 침대 데우개 뚜껑을 심벌즈처럼 쨍그랑 부딪치며 외쳤다."]], [["No one ever regretted the admittance of Sam Weller, for a more devoted, well-behaved, and jovial member no club could have.", "\"계속해, 계속해!\" 윙클과 터프먼이 덧붙였고, 그러는 동안 회장은 자애롭게 고개를 숙였다."], ["He certainly did add \"spirit\"", "\"제게 베풀어 주신 영예에 대한 감사의 작은 표시로서, 그리고 이웃한 두 나라 사이의 우호 관계를 증진하는 수단으로서, 한 가지만 말씀드리고자 합니다. 저는 정원 아래쪽 모퉁이의 산울타리에 우체국을 세웠습니다."], ["to the meetings, and \"a tone\"", "멋지고 널찍한 건물로, 문에는 자물쇠가 달려 있고, 우편물을 위한 온갖 편의가 갖춰져 있습니다 — 또한 여성용 우편물을 위해서도요, 이런 표현을 써도 된다면 말입니다."], ["to the paper; for his orations convulsed his hearers, and his contributions were excellent, being patriotic, classical, comical, or dramatic, but never sentimental.", "그건 낡은 제비집인데, 제가 입구를 막고 지붕을 열리게 만들어 놓아서, 온갖 것을 담을 수 있고 우리의 귀중한 시간도 아껴 줄 겁니다. 편지, 원고, 책, 꾸러미를 거기로 주고받을 수 있고, 각 나라가 열쇠를 하나씩 가지고 있으니, 참으로 멋질 것이라 생각합니다."], ["Jo regarded them as worthy of Bacon, Milton, or Shakespeare; and remodelled her own works with good effect, she thought.", "클럽 열쇠를 드리겠습니다. 그리고 여러분의 호의에 거듭 감사드리며, 제 자리로 돌아가겠습니다.\""]], [["The P. O.", "웰러 씨가 작은 열쇠 하나를 탁자 위에 올려놓고 자리에 앉자 큰 박수가 터졌다."], ["was a capital little institution, and flourished wonderfully, for nearly as many queer things passed through it as through the real office.", "침대 데우개가 쨍그랑거리며 미친 듯이 흔들렸고, 질서가 회복되기까지는 한참이 걸렸다. 긴 토론이 이어졌고, 저마다 최선을 다한 덕에 모두가 놀라운 모습을 보였다."], ["Tragedies and cravats, poetry and pickles, garden-seeds and long letters, music and gingerbread, rubbers, invitations, scoldings and puppies.", "그리하여 그 모임은 유난히 활기찼고, 늦은 시각이 되어서야 새 회원을 위한 세 차례의 날카로운 환호와 함께 산회했다. 샘 웰러를 받아들인 것을 후회한 사람은 아무도 없었으니, 그보다 더 헌신적이고 예의 바르며 유쾌한 회원은 어느 클럽에도 없었을 것이기 때문이다."], ["The old gentleman liked the fun, and amused himself by sending odd bundles, mysterious messages, and funny telegrams; and his gardener, who was smitten with Hannah's charms, actually sent a love-letter to Jo's care.", "그는 확실히 모임에 \"활기\"를 더했고 신문에 \"격조\"를 더했다. 그의 연설은 듣는 이들을 자지러지게 했고, 그의 기고문은 애국적이거나 고전적이거나 우스꽝스럽거나 극적이긴 해도 결코 감상적이지 않아 일품이었다."], ["How they laughed when the secret came out, never dreaming how many love-letters that little post-office would hold in the years to come!", "조는 그것을 베이컨이나 밀턴, 혹은 셰익스피어에 견줄 만하다고 여겼고, 자기 작품도 거기에 맞춰 고쳐 쓰니 효과가 좋다고 생각했다."]], [["\"The first of June!", "우체국은 굉장히 훌륭한 작은 기관으로 놀랍도록 번창했으니, 진짜 우체국 못지않게 별의별 희한한 것들이 그곳을 거쳐 갔기 때문이다."], ["The Kings are off to the seashore to-morrow, and I'm free.", "비극과 넥타이, 시와 피클, 정원 씨앗과 긴 편지, 악보와 생강빵, 덧신, 초대장, 잔소리, 그리고 강아지까지."], ["Three months' vacation,--how I shall enjoy it!\"", "노신사는 그 재미를 좋아해서, 별난 꾸러미와 알쏭달쏭한 전갈과 우스운 전보를 보내며 즐거워했다."], ["exclaimed Meg, coming home one warm day to find Jo laid upon the sofa in an unusual state of exhaustion, while Beth took off her dusty boots, and Amy made lemonade for the refreshment of the whole party.", "해나의 매력에 푹 빠진 그의 정원사는 실제로 연애편지를 조 앞으로 보내기도 했다. 비밀이 드러났을 때 다들 얼마나 웃었던지, 그 작은 우체국이 다가올 세월에 얼마나 많은 연애편지를 품게 될지는 꿈에도 모른 채 말이다!"]], [["\"Aunt March went to-day, for which, oh, be joyful!\" said Jo. \"I was mortally afraid she'd ask me to go with her; if she had, I should have felt as if I ought to do it; but Plumfield is about as gay as a churchyard, you know, and I'd rather be excused. We had a flurry getting the old lady off, and I had a fright every time she spoke to me, for I was in such a hurry to be through that I was uncommonly helpful and sweet, and feared she'd find it impossible to part from me. I quaked till she was fairly in the carriage, and had a final fright, for, as it drove off, she popped out her head, saying, 'Josy-phine, won't you--?' I didn't hear any more, for I basely turned and fled; I did actually run, and whisked round the corner, where I felt safe.\"", "\"유월 첫날이야! 킹 씨네가 내일 바닷가로 떠나니까 나는 자유야. 석 달의 방학이라니 — 얼마나 즐길 작정인지!\" 어느 무더운 날 집에 돌아온 메그가 외쳤다. 조는 보기 드물게 기진맥진한 모습으로 소파에 누워 있었고, 베스는 언니의 먼지투성이 부츠를 벗겨 주고 있었으며, 에이미는 일행 모두의 원기 회복을 위해 레모네이드를 만들고 있었다. \"마치 대고모님이 오늘 떠나셨어. 그러니, 아, 기뻐하자고!\" 조가 말했다. \"고모님이 같이 가자고 하실까 봐 죽도록 겁났거든. 그러셨다면 나도 가야 할 것만 같았을 거야. 그렇지만 플럼필드는 알다시피 교회 묘지만큼이나 흥겨운 곳이잖아, 그래서 난 빠지는 편이 나아. 늙으신 고모님을 떠나보내느라 한바탕 부산을 떨었는데, 고모님이 내게 말을 거실 때마다 가슴이 철렁했어. 빨리 일을 마치고 싶은 마음이 어찌나 굴뚝같던지 유난히 살갑고 곰살맞게 굴었거든. 그래서 고모님이 나랑 못 헤어지겠다고 하실까 봐 겁났던 거야. 고모님이 마차에 완전히 올라타실 때까지 벌벌 떨었는데, 마지막에 또 한 번 기겁했어. 마차가 떠나는데 고모님이 고개를 쑥 내밀고 이러시지 뭐야. '조지핀, 너 ─ 안 하련?' 그 뒤는 못 들었어, 비열하게도 돌아서서 줄행랑쳤거든. 정말로 달렸다니까, 모퉁이를 휙 돌고 나서야 안심이 됐어.\""]], [["\"Poor old Jo!", "\"불쌍한 우리 조!"], ["she came in looking as if bears were after her,\"", "곰한테 쫓기기라도 한 것 같은 꼴로 들어왔다니까.\""], ["said Beth, as she cuddled her sister's feet with a motherly air.", "베스가 엄마 같은 손길로 언니의 발을 감싸며 말했다."]], [["\"Aunt March is a regular samphire, is she not?\"", "\"마치 대고모님은 영락없는 함초 같지 않니?\""], ["observed Amy, tasting her mixture critically.", "에이미가 자기가 만든 음료를 꼼꼼히 맛보며 말했다."]], [["\"She means _vampire_, not sea-weed; but it doesn't matter; it's too warm to be particular about one's parts of speech,\"", "\"걔가 말하려는 건 흡혈귀지, 해초가 아니야. 그래도 상관없어."], ["murmured Jo.", "자기가 쓰는 단어 따위에 까다롭게 굴기엔 너무 더우니까.\" 조가 중얼거렸다."]], [["\"What shall you do all your vacation?\"", "\"넌 방학 내내 뭐 할 거야?\""], ["asked Amy, changing the subject, with tact.", "에이미가 눈치껏 화제를 돌리며 물었다."]], [["\"I shall lie abed late, and do nothing,\"", "\"난 늦잠 자고 아무것도 안 할래.\""], ["replied Meg, from the depths of the rocking-chair.", "흔들의자 깊숙이 파묻힌 메그가 대답했다."], ["\"I've been routed up early all winter, and had to spend my days working for other people; so now I'm going to rest and revel to my heart's content.\"", "\"겨우내 일찍 깨워지는 통에 종일 남을 위해 일해야 했으니까, 이젠 실컷 쉬면서 마음껏 즐길 거야.\""]], [["\"No,\"", "\"아니야.\""], ["said Jo; \"that dozy way wouldn't suit me.", "조가 말했다. \"그렇게 빈둥대는 건 나랑 안 맞아."], ["I've laid in a heap of books, and I'm going to improve my shining hours reading on my perch in the old apple-tree, when I'm not having l------\"", "책을 잔뜩 쌓아 뒀거든, 나는 늙은 사과나무 위 내 자리에 올라앉아 책을 읽으면서 이 빛나는 시간을 알차게 쓸 거야. 종달새 흉내를 ─\""]], [["\"Don't say 'larks!'\"", "\"'종달새 흉내'라고 하지 마!\""], ["implored Amy, as a return snub for the \"samphire\" correction.", "에이미가 '함초'를 고쳐 준 데 대한 앙갚음으로 애원했다."]], [["\"I'll say 'nightingales,'", "\"그럼 로리랑 같이 '나이팅게일'이라고 하지 뭐."], ["then, with Laurie; that's proper and appropriate, since he's a warbler.\"", "걔는 지저귀는 새니까, 그게 적절하고 알맞겠네.\""]], [["\"Don't let us do any lessons, Beth, for a while, but play all the time, and rest, as the girls mean to,\"", "\"우리도 한동안 공부는 하지 말자, 베스. 언니들이 그러려는 것처럼 내내 놀면서 쉬자고.\""], ["proposed Amy.", "에이미가 제안했다."]], [["\"Well, I will, if mother doesn't mind.", "\"음, 엄마가 괜찮다고 하시면 그럴래."], ["I want to learn some new songs, and my children need fitting up for the summer; they are dreadfully out of order, and really suffering for clothes.\"", "새 노래 몇 곡을 배우고 싶고, 내 아이들도 여름맞이로 단장해 줘야 하거든. 걔들 꼴이 말이 아니게 엉망이라 정말이지 옷이 없어 고생하고 있어.\""]], [["\"May we, mother?\"", "\"그래도 돼요, 엄마?\""], ["asked Meg, turning to Mrs. March, who sat sewing, in what they called \"Marmee's corner.\"", "메그가 그들이 \"엄마의 구석\"이라고 부르는 곳에 앉아 바느질하던 마치 부인을 돌아보며 물었다."]], [["\"You may try your experiment for a week, and see how you like it.", "\"일주일 동안 시험 삼아 해 보고, 마음에 드는지 보렴."], ["I think by Saturday night you will find that all play and no work is as bad as all work and no play.\"", "토요일 밤쯤이면 놀기만 하고 일을 안 하는 것이 일만 하고 놀지 않는 것만큼이나 나쁘다는 걸 알게 될 것 같구나.\""]], [["\"Oh, dear, no!", "\"어머, 그럴 리가요!"], ["it will be delicious, I'm sure,\"", "분명 근사할 거예요.\""], ["said Meg complacently.", "메그가 흡족하게 말했다."]], [["\"I now propose a toast, as my 'friend and pardner, Sairy Gamp,'", "\"이제 내 '벗이자 동무인 새리 갬프'가 말하듯 건배를 제안할게."], ["says. Fun forever, and no grubbing!\"", "영원한 즐거움이여, 그리고 고된 일은 없기를!\""], ["cried Jo, rising, glass in hand, as the lemonade went round.", "조가 잔을 손에 들고 일어서며 외쳤고, 레모네이드 잔이 한 바퀴 돌았다."]], [["They all drank it merrily, and began the experiment by lounging for the rest of the day.", "다들 흥겹게 잔을 비웠고, 그날 남은 시간을 빈둥대며 보내는 것으로 그 시험을 시작했다."], ["Next morning, Meg did not appear till ten o'clock; her solitary breakfast did not taste nice, and the room seemed lonely and untidy; for Jo had not filled the vases, Beth had not dusted, and Amy's books lay scattered about.", "이튿날 아침, 메그는 열 시가 되어서야 모습을 드러냈다. 혼자 먹는 아침 식사는 맛이 없었고, 방은 외롭고 어수선해 보였다."], ["Nothing was neat and pleasant but \"Marmee's corner,\" which looked as usual; and there Meg sat, to \"rest and read,\"", "조가 꽃병을 채워 두지 않았고, 베스는 먼지를 닦지 않았으며, 에이미의 책들은 여기저기 흩어져 있었던 탓이다."], ["which meant yawn, and imagine what pretty summer dresses she would get with her salary.", "평소와 다름없어 보이는 \"엄마의 구석\"만 빼고는 무엇 하나 깔끔하고 산뜻한 데가 없었다."], ["Jo spent the morning on the river, with Laurie, and the afternoon reading and crying over \"The Wide, Wide World,\" up in the apple-tree.", "메그는 거기 앉아 \"쉬면서 책을 읽었\"는데, 그건 곧 하품을 하며 봉급으로 어떤 예쁜 여름 원피스를 살까 상상하는 것이었다."], ["Beth began by rummaging everything out of the big closet, where her family resided; but, getting tired before half done, she left her establishment topsy-turvy, and went to her music, rejoicing that she had no dishes to wash.", "조는 오전을 로리와 강에서 보냈고, 오후에는 사과나무 위에 올라가 『넓고 넓은 세상』을 읽으며 울었다. 베스는 자기 식구들이 사는 큰 벽장에서 모든 걸 끄집어내는 것으로 시작했지만, 절반도 하기 전에 지쳐서 살림살이를 뒤죽박죽인 채 내버려 두고, 설거지할 그릇이 없는 것을 기뻐하며 음악으로 향했다."], ["Amy arranged her bower, put on her best white frock, smoothed her curls, and sat down to draw, under the honeysuckles, hoping some one would see and inquire who the young artist was.", "에이미는 자기 정자를 꾸미고, 제일 좋은 흰 원피스를 입고, 곱슬머리를 매만진 다음, 인동덩굴 아래 앉아 그림을 그렸다. 누군가가 보고서 저 젊은 화가가 누구냐고 물어봐 주기를 바라면서."], ["As no one appeared but an inquisitive daddy-long-legs, who examined her work with interest, she went to walk, got caught in a shower, and came home dripping.", "그러나 호기심 많은 갈처지거미 한 마리만 나타나 흥미롭게 그녀의 그림을 살펴볼 뿐 아무도 오지 않자, 에이미는 산책을 나갔다가 소나기를 만나 흠뻑 젖은 채 집에 돌아왔다."]], [["At tea-time they compared notes, and all agreed that it had been a delightful, though unusually long day.", "차 마실 시간에 다들 그날 있었던 일을 나누었고, 비록 유난히 긴 하루였지만 즐거웠다는 데 모두 동의했다."], ["Meg, who went shopping in the afternoon, and got a \"sweet blue muslin,\" had discovered, after she had cut the breadths off, that it wouldn't wash, which mishap made her slightly cross.", "오후에 장을 보러 가서 \"고운 파란 모슬린\"을 산 메그는 천을 마름질해 잘라 낸 뒤에야 그 천이 물빨래가 안 된다는 걸 알게 되어, 그 낭패에 조금 짜증이 나 있었다."], ["Jo had burnt the skin off her nose boating, and got a raging headache by reading too long.", "조는 뱃놀이를 하다가 콧등 살갗이 다 타 버렸고, 너무 오래 책을 읽은 탓에 머리가 깨질 듯 아팠다."], ["Beth was worried by the confusion of her closet, and the difficulty of learning three or four songs at once; and Amy deeply regretted the damage done her frock, for Katy Brown's party was to be the next day; and now, like Flora McFlimsey, she had \"nothing to wear.\"", "베스는 어수선한 벽장과 한꺼번에 서너 곡을 익히는 어려움에 골머리를 앓았다. 그리고 에이미는 원피스가 망가진 것을 몹시 안타까워했으니, 다음 날이 케이티 브라운의 파티인데 이제 플로라 맥플림지처럼 \"입을 옷이 없었\"기 때문이다."], ["But these were mere trifles; and they assured their mother that the experiment was working finely.", "그러나 이런 건 사소한 일들이었고, 그들은 엄마에게 이 시험이 아주 잘되어 가고 있다고 장담했다."], ["She smiled, said nothing, and, with Hannah's help, did their neglected work, keeping home pleasant, and the domestic machinery running smoothly.", "엄마는 미소만 지을 뿐 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 해나의 도움을 받아 그들이 소홀히 한 일을 해내며, 집안을 안락하게 하고 살림이라는 기계가 매끄럽게 돌아가게 했다."], ["It was astonishing what a peculiar and uncomfortable state of things was produced by the \"resting and revelling\" process.", "그렇게 별나고 불편한 상황이 점점 더 길게 이어지니 참으로 놀라웠다."], ["The days kept getting longer and longer; the weather was unusually variable, and so were tempers; an unsettled feeling possessed every one, and Satan found plenty of mischief for the idle hands to do.", "날씨가 유난히 변덕스러웠고, 성미들도 그러했다. 모두가 안절부절못하는 기분에 사로잡혔고, 사탄은 한가한 손에게 시킬 못된 장난을 잔뜩 찾아 주었다."], ["As the height of luxury, Meg put out some of her sewing, and then found time hang so heavily that she fell to snipping and spoiling her clothes, in her attempts to furbish them up à la Moffat.", "호사의 극치로, 메그는 자기 바느질감을 일부 남에게 맡겼는데, 그러고 나니 시간이 어찌나 안 가던지, 결국 모팻 풍으로 옷을 손보겠답시고 옷을 자르고 망쳐 놓기에 이르렀다."], ["Jo read till her eyes gave out, and she was sick of books; got so fidgety that even good-natured Laurie had a quarrel with her, and so reduced in spirits that she desperately wished she had gone with Aunt March.", "조는 눈이 빠지도록 책을 읽다가 책이라면 진절머리가 났고, 어찌나 안달복달했던지 그 사람 좋은 로리마저도 그녀와 다투었으며, 기운이 어찌나 빠졌던지 마치 대고모님과 함께 갈걸 그랬다고 간절히 바랄 지경이었다."], ["Beth got on pretty well, for she was constantly forgetting that it was to be _all play, and no work_, and fell back into her old ways now and then; but something in the air affected her, and, more than once, her tranquillity was much disturbed; so much so, that, on one occasion, she actually shook poor dear Joanna, and told her she was \"a fright.\"", "베스는 그럭저럭 잘 지냈으니, 일을 안 하고 내내 놀기로 했다는 걸 자꾸만 잊고 이따금 옛 버릇으로 되돌아갔기 때문이다. 그러나 무언가 공기 중에 떠도는 것이 그녀에게 영향을 미쳐, 한두 번이 아니게 그 마음의 평온이 크게 흐트러졌다."], ["Amy fared worst of all, for her resources were small; and when her sisters left her to amuse and care for herself, she soon found that accomplished and important little self a great burden.", "어찌나 심했던지, 한번은 가엾고 사랑스러운 조애나를 실제로 흔들어 대며 \"꼴이 흉측하다\"고 말하기까지 했다. 에이미는 누구보다 형편이 나빴으니, 가진 밑천이 적었기 때문이다."], ["She didn't like dolls, fairy-tales were childish, and one couldn't draw all the time; tea-parties didn't amount to much, neither did picnics, unless very well conducted.", "언니들이 그녀를 내버려 두어 혼자 놀고 알아서 지내야 하게 되자, 그녀는 곧 그 잘난 척하는 중요한 자기 자신이 큰 짐이라는 걸 깨달았다. 인형은 싫었고, 동화는 유치했으며, 누구라도 종일 그림만 그릴 수는 없는 노릇이었다."], ["\"If one could have a fine house, full of nice girls, or go travelling, the summer would be delightful; but to stay at home with three selfish sisters and a grown-up boy was enough to try the patience of a Boaz,\"", "다과회도 별것 아니었고, 소풍 역시 아주 잘 꾸려지지 않는 한 마찬가지였다. \"근사한 집에 좋은 여자애들이 가득하다거나 여행이라도 갈 수 있다면 여름이 즐거울 텐데, 이기적인 언니 셋이랑 다 큰 사내애 하나랑 집에만 있으려니 보아스의 인내심도 시험에 들 지경이지.\""], ["complained Miss Malaprop, after several days devoted to pleasure, fretting, and _ennui_.", "며칠을 즐거움과 짜증과 권태에 바친 끝에 맬러프롭 양이 투덜거렸다."]], [["No one would own that they were tired of the experiment; but, by Friday night, each acknowledged to herself that she was glad the week was nearly done.", "시험에 싫증이 났다고 인정하려는 사람은 아무도 없었다."], ["Hoping to impress the lesson more deeply, Mrs.", "그러나 금요일 밤이 되자, 저마다 일주일이 거의 끝나 가는 게 다행이라고 속으로 인정했다."], ["March, who had a good deal of humor, resolved to finish off the trial in an appropriate manner; so she gave Hannah a holiday, and let the girls enjoy the full effect of the play system.", "마치 부인은 익살기가 다분한 사람이라, 이 교훈을 더 깊이 새겨 주고자 그 시험을 적절한 방식으로 마무리하기로 마음먹었다. 그래서 해나에게 하루 휴가를 주고, 소녀들이 노는 제도의 효과를 톡톡히 누리도록 내버려 두었다."]], [["When they got up on Saturday morning, there was no fire in the kitchen, no breakfast in the dining-room, and no mother anywhere to be seen.", "토요일 아침에 일어났을 때, 부엌에는 불이 없었고, 식당에는 아침 식사가 없었으며, 엄마는 어디에도 보이지 않았다."]], [["\"Mercy on us!", "\"맙소사!"], ["what _has_ happened?\"", "대체 무슨 일이 일어난 거야?\""], ["cried Jo, staring about her in dismay.", "조가 어쩔 줄 몰라 사방을 두리번거리며 외쳤다."]], [["Meg ran upstairs, and soon came back again, looking relieved, but rather bewildered, and a little ashamed.", "메그는 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔다가 이내 다시 내려왔는데, 안도한 표정이면서도 어딘지 어리둥절하고 약간 부끄러워하는 기색이었다."]], [["\"Mother isn't sick, only very tired, and she says she is going to stay quietly in her room all day, and let us do the best we can.", "\"엄마는 편찮으신 건 아니고, 그냥 몹시 피곤하시대. 그래서 오늘은 종일 방에서 조용히 쉬면서 우리가 알아서 하게 두시겠대."], ["It's a very queer thing for her to do, she doesn't act a bit like herself; but she says it has been a hard week for her, so we mustn't grumble, but take care of ourselves.\"", "엄마가 이러시는 건 정말 별일이야, 평소 엄마 같지가 않아. 그래도 이번 주가 힘드셨다니까, 우린 투덜대지 말고 알아서 챙기자.\""]], [["\"That's easy enough, and I like the idea; I'm aching for something to do--that is, some new amusement, you know,\"", "\"그쯤이야 식은 죽 먹기지, 게다가 그 생각 마음에 들어. 난 뭐든 할 게 없나 근질근질하던 참이거든 — 그러니까, 뭔가 새로운 재밋거리 말이야.\""], ["added Jo quickly.", "조가 얼른 덧붙였다."]], [["In fact it _was_ an immense relief to them all to have a little work, and they took hold with a will, but soon realized the truth of Hannah's saying, \"Housekeeping ain't no joke.\"", "사실 그들 모두에게 약간의 일거리가 생긴 것은 엄청난 위안이었고, 그래서 의욕을 가지고 달려들었지만, 곧 \"살림은 장난이 아니다\"라는 해나의 말이 진실임을 깨달았다."], ["There was plenty of food in the larder, and, while Beth and Amy set the table, Meg and Jo got breakfast, wondering, as they did so, why servants ever talked about hard work.", "식료품 저장실에는 먹을 것이 잔뜩 있었고, 베스와 에이미가 상을 차리는 동안 메그와 조는 아침 식사를 준비했다. 그러면서 두 사람은 어째서 하인들이 힘든 일이니 어쩌니 떠드는지 의아해했다."]], [["\"I shall take some up to mother, though she said we were not to think of her, for she'd take care of herself,\"", "\"엄마 신경 쓰지 말라고, 알아서 챙기시겠다고 하셨지만, 그래도 좀 가져다드려야겠어.\" 메그가 말했다."], ["said Meg, who presided, and felt quite matronly behind the teapot.", "메그는 좌장 노릇을 하며 찻주전자 뒤에 앉아 자못 안주인 같은 기분이 들었다."]], [["So a tray was fitted out before any one began, and taken up, with the cook's compliments.", "그래서 누가 먹기 시작하기도 전에 쟁반이 차려졌고, 요리사의 인사와 함께 위로 올려졌다."], ["The boiled tea was very bitter, the omelette scorched, and the biscuits speckled with saleratus; but Mrs.", "끓인 차는 무척 썼고, 오믈렛은 탔으며, 비스킷에는 베이킹소다가 알록달록 박혀 있었다."], ["March received her repast with thanks, and laughed heartily over it after Jo was gone.", "그러나 마치 부인은 고맙다며 그 끼니를 받아 들었고, 조가 가고 난 뒤에는 한바탕 호탕하게 웃었다."]], [["\"Poor little souls, they will have a hard time, I'm afraid; but they won't suffer, and it will do them good,\"", "\"가엾은 것들, 한동안 고생깨나 하겠구나. 그래도 크게 탈 날 일은 없을 테고, 이게 다 약이 될 거야.\""], ["she said, producing the more palatable viands with which she had provided herself, and disposing of the bad breakfast, so that their feelings might not be hurt,--a motherly little deception, for which they were grateful.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하며, 미리 챙겨 둔 좀 더 입에 맞는 음식을 꺼내고, 아이들의 마음이 상하지 않도록 형편없는 아침상을 처리했다. 엄마다운 작은 속임수였고, 아이들은 그것을 고마워했다."]], [["Many were the complaints below, and great the chagrin of the head cook at her failures.", "아래층에서는 불평이 많았고, 수석 요리사는 자기 실패작에 크게 풀이 죽었다."], ["\"Never mind, I'll get the dinner, and be servant; you be mistress, keep your hands nice, see company, and give orders,\"", "\"괜찮아, 저녁은 내가 차리고 하인 노릇을 할게. 언니는 안주인이 되어 손을 곱게 간수하고, 손님을 맞고, 명령이나 내려.\""], ["said Jo, who knew still less than Meg about culinary affairs.", "메그보다 요리에 대해 아는 게 더 없는 조가 말했다."]], [["This obliging offer was gladly accepted; and Margaret retired to the parlor, which she hastily put in order by whisking the litter under the sofa, and shutting the blinds, to save the trouble of dusting.", "이 고마운 제안은 기꺼이 받아들여졌고, 마거릿은 응접실로 물러나, 먼지 닦는 수고를 덜려고 어질러진 것들을 소파 밑으로 휙 쓸어 넣고 덧문을 닫아 부랴부랴 정돈했다."], ["Jo, with perfect faith in her own powers, and a friendly desire to make up the quarrel, immediately put a note in the office, inviting Laurie to dinner.", "조는 자기 능력에 대한 완벽한 믿음과, 다툼을 풀려는 다정한 마음에서, 곧장 우체국에 로리를 저녁 식사에 초대하는 쪽지를 넣었다."]], [["\"You'd better see what you have got before you think of having company,\"", "\"손님 부를 생각을 하기 전에 가진 게 뭐가 있는지부터 봐 두는 게 좋을걸.\""], ["said Meg, when informed of the hospitable but rash act.", "그 후하지만 경솔한 행동을 전해 들은 메그가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, there's corned beef and plenty of potatoes; and I shall get some asparagus, and a lobster, 'for a relish,'", "\"아, 코드비프랑 감자가 잔뜩 있고, 아스파라거스도 좀 사 올 거고, 해나 말마따나 '입맛 돋우게'"], ["as Hannah says.", "바닷가재도 사 올 거야."], ["We'll have lettuce, and make a salad.", "상추도 사다가 샐러드를 만들 거고."], ["I don't know how, but the book tells.", "어떻게 하는진 모르지만 책에 다 나와 있어."], ["I'll have blanc-mange and strawberries for dessert; and coffee, too, if you want to be elegant.\"", "후식으로는 블랑망제랑 딸기를 낼 거야. 그리고 우아하게 차리고 싶으면 커피도 내고.\""]], [["\"Don't try too many messes, Jo, for you can't make anything but gingerbread and molasses candy, fit to eat.", "\"너무 여러 가지 음식 만들려고 하지 마, 조. 넌 생강빵이랑 당밀 사탕 말고는 먹을 만한 걸 만들 줄 모르잖아."], ["I wash my hands of the dinner-party; and, since you have asked Laurie on your own responsibility, you may just take care of him.\"", "난 이 만찬에서 손 뗄래. 그리고 네 책임으로 로리를 불렀으니까, 걔는 네가 알아서 챙겨.\""]], [["\"I don't want you to do anything but be civil to him, and help to the pudding.", "\"언니는 걔한테 그냥 예의 바르게 굴고, 푸딩 담는 거나 거들어 주면 돼."], ["You'll give me your advice if I get in a muddle, won't you?\"", "내가 갈팡질팡하면 조언 정도는 해 줄 거지?\""], ["asked Jo, rather hurt.", "조가 다소 서운해하며 물었다."]], [["\"Yes; but I don't know much, except about bread, and a few trifles.", "\"그래. 그래도 난 빵이랑 자잘한 것 몇 가지밖에 잘 몰라."], ["You had better ask mother's leave before you order anything,\"", "뭐든 주문하기 전에 엄마한테 먼저 허락받는 게 좋을 거야.\""], ["returned Meg prudently.", "메그가 신중하게 대답했다."]], [["\"Of course I shall; I'm not a fool,\"", "\"당연히 그래야지. 나도 바보는 아니라고.\""], ["and Jo went off in a huff at the doubts expressed of her powers.", "그러고는 조가 자기 능력을 의심받은 데 골이 나서 휙 가 버렸다."]], [["\"Get what you like, and don't disturb me; I'm going out to dinner, and can't worry about things at home,\"", "\"먹고 싶은 거 사고, 날 귀찮게 하지 말아라. 난 저녁 먹으러 나갈 거고, 집안일은 신경 쓸 수가 없구나.\""], ["said Mrs. March, when Jo spoke to her.", "조가 말을 건네자 마치 부인이 말했다."], ["\"I never enjoyed housekeeping, and I'm going to take a vacation to-day, and read, write, go visiting, and amuse myself.\"", "\"난 살림을 즐겨 본 적이 없어서, 오늘은 휴가를 좀 내서 책 읽고, 글 쓰고, 마실 다니고, 즐기련다.\""]], [["The unusual spectacle of her busy mother rocking comfortably, and reading, early in the morning, made Jo feel as if some natural phenomenon had occurred, for an eclipse, an earthquake, or a volcanic eruption would hardly have seemed stranger.", "부산하던 엄마가 이른 아침에 흔들의자에 편안히 앉아 책을 읽는 진귀한 광경에 조는 무슨 자연 현상이라도 일어난 듯한 기분이 들었으니, 일식이나 지진, 화산 폭발이라 한들 이보다 더 이상해 보이지는 않았을 터였다."]], [["\"Everything is out of sorts, somehow,\"", "\"어쩐지 죄다 뒤숭숭해.\""], ["she said to herself, going down stairs.", "조는 아래층으로 내려가며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["\"There's Beth crying; that's a sure sign that something is wrong with this family.", "\"베스가 울고 있잖아. 저건 이 집에 뭔가 잘못됐다는 확실한 신호야."], ["If Amy is bothering, I'll shake her.\"", "에이미가 속을 썩이면, 흔들어 줘야지.\""]], [["Feeling very much out of sorts herself, Jo hurried into the parlor to find Beth sobbing over Pip, the canary, who lay dead in the cage, with his little claws pathetically extended, as if imploring the food for want of which he had died.", "스스로도 영 마음이 뒤숭숭한 조가 응접실로 서둘러 들어가 보니, 베스가 새장 안에서 죽어 있는 카나리아 핍을 두고 흐느끼고 있었다. 핍은 먹을 것이 없어 굶어 죽었음을 애원하기라도 하듯, 작은 발톱을 애처롭게 뻗은 채였다."]], [["\"It's all my fault--I forgot him--there isn't a seed or a drop left.", "\"다 내 잘못이야 — 내가 얘를 잊어버렸어 — 씨앗 한 톨, 물 한 방울 안 남았잖아."], ["O Pip!", "오, 핍!"], ["O Pip!", "오, 핍!"], ["how could I be so cruel to you?\"", "내가 어쩌면 너한테 이렇게 모질 수 있었을까?\""], ["cried Beth, taking the poor thing in her hands, and trying to restore him.", "베스가 가엾은 것을 두 손에 받쳐 들고 되살려 보려 애쓰며 외쳤다."]], [["Jo peeped into his half-open eye, felt his little heart, and finding him stiff and cold, shook her head, and offered her domino-box for a coffin.", "조는 반쯤 뜬 핍의 눈을 들여다보고, 작은 심장을 만져 보더니, 뻣뻣하고 차가운 것을 알고는 고개를 가로젓고, 관으로 쓰라며 자기 도미노 상자를 내주었다."]], [["\"Put him in the oven, and maybe he will get warm and revive,\"", "\"오븐에 넣어 보자, 어쩌면 따뜻해져서 되살아날지도 몰라.\""], ["said Amy hopefully.", "에이미가 희망 어린 말을 했다."]], [["\"He's been starved, and he sha'n't be baked, now he's dead.", "\"얜 굶어 죽은 거야, 이제 죽었는데 굽기까지 할 순 없어."], ["I'll make him a shroud, and he shall be buried in the garden; and I'll never have another bird, never, my Pip!", "내가 수의를 만들어서 정원에 묻어 줄 거야. 그리고 다시는 새를 키우지 않을 거야, 절대로, 내 핍!"], ["for I am too bad to own one,\"", "난 새를 키울 자격도 없을 만큼 못된 애니까.\""], ["murmured Beth, sitting on the floor with her pet folded in her hands.", "베스가 자기 귀염둥이를 두 손에 감싸 든 채 바닥에 앉아 중얼거렸다."]], [["\"The funeral shall be this afternoon, and we will all go.", "\"장례식은 오늘 오후에 치르자, 우리 다 같이 가는 거야. 자, 울지 마, 베스."], ["Now, don't cry, Bethy; it's a pity, but nothing goes right this week, and Pip has had the worst of the experiment.", "안된 일이지만, 이번 주엔 되는 일이 하나도 없네. 핍이 이 시험의 가장 큰 화를 입은 거야."], ["Make the shroud, and lay him in my box; and, after the dinner-party, we'll have a nice little funeral,\"", "수의를 만들어서 내 상자에 뉘어 둬. 그리고 저녁 식사가 끝나면 멋진 작은 장례식을 치르자.\""], ["said Jo, beginning to feel as if she had undertaken a good deal.", "조가 말했지만, 일을 너무 많이 떠맡은 것 같은 기분이 들기 시작했다."]], [["Leaving the others to console Beth, she departed to the kitchen, which was in a most discouraging state of confusion.", "다른 식구들에게 베스를 위로하는 일을 맡기고, 조는 부엌으로 향했는데, 그곳은 더없이 의기소침해질 만큼 난장판이었다."], ["Putting on a big apron, she fell to work, and got the dishes piled up ready for washing, when she discovered that the fire was out.", "큰 앞치마를 두르고 일에 달려들어, 그릇을 설거지할 수 있게 차곡차곡 쌓아 놓고 보니, 불이 꺼져 있었다."]], [["\"Here's a sweet prospect!\"", "\"이거 참 근사한 앞날이군!\""], ["muttered Jo, slamming the stove-door open, and poking vigorously among the cinders.", "조가 화덕 문을 쾅 열어젖히고 잿더미 속을 힘차게 쑤셔 대며 투덜거렸다."]], [["Having rekindled the fire, she thought she would go to market while the water heated.", "불을 다시 지핀 다음, 그녀는 물이 데워지는 동안 장을 보러 가야겠다고 생각했다."], ["The walk revived her spirits; and, flattering herself that she had made good bargains, she trudged home again, after buying a very young lobster, some very old asparagus, and two boxes of acid strawberries.", "걷고 나니 기운이 살아났고, 흥정을 잘했다고 스스로 우쭐해하며, 아주 어린 바닷가재 한 마리와 아주 늙은 아스파라거스 약간, 그리고 신 딸기 두 상자를 산 뒤 터덜터덜 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["By the time she got cleared up, the dinner arrived, and the stove was red-hot.", "다 정리하고 나니 장 봐 온 것이 도착했고, 화덕은 시뻘겋게 달아올라 있었다."], ["Hannah had left a pan of bread to rise, Meg had worked it up early, set it on the hearth for a second rising, and forgotten it.", "해나가 부풀라고 빵 반죽 한 판을 놓아두었는데, 메그가 그것을 일찌감치 치대어 두 번째로 부풀라고 화덕가에 올려놓고는 잊어버린 것이었다."], ["Meg was entertaining Sallie Gardiner in the parlor, when the door flew open, and a floury, crocky, flushed, and dishevelled figure appeared, demanding tartly,--", "메그가 응접실에서 샐리 가드너를 대접하고 있는데, 문이 벌컥 열리더니, 밀가루투성이에 그을음투성이로 얼굴이 벌게지고 머리가 헝클어진 형체가 나타나, 톡 쏘듯 캐물었다."]], [["\"I say, isn't bread 'riz'", "\"있잖아, 빵이 판에서 넘쳐 나오면 충분히 '부푼'"], ["enough when it runs over the pans?\"", "거 아니야?\""]], [["Sallie began to laugh; but Meg nodded, and lifted her eyebrows as high as they would go, which caused the apparition to vanish, and put the sour bread into the oven without further delay.", "샐리가 웃기 시작했지만, 메그는 고개를 끄덕이며 눈썹을 한껏 치켜올렸다. 그러자 그 유령 같은 형체는 사라졌고, 더는 지체 없이 시큼해진 빵을 화덕에 넣었다."], ["Mrs. March went out, after peeping here and there to see how matters went, also saying a word of comfort to Beth, who sat making a winding-sheet, while the dear departed lay in state in the domino-box.", "마치 부인은 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지 여기저기 들여다본 다음, 도미노 상자 안에 고이 안치된 망자를 곁에 두고 수의를 짓고 있는 베스에게 위로의 한마디를 건네고는 집을 나섰다."], ["A strange sense of helplessness fell upon the girls as the gray bonnet vanished round the corner; and despair seized them, when, a few minutes later, Miss Crocker appeared, and said she'd come to dinner.", "회색 보닛이 모퉁이를 돌아 사라지자 소녀들에게는 야릇한 무력감이 엄습했다. 그리고 몇 분 후, 크로커 양이 나타나 저녁을 먹으러 왔다고 하자, 그들은 절망에 사로잡혔다."], ["Now, this lady was a thin, yellow spinster, with a sharp nose and inquisitive eyes, who saw everything, and gossiped about all she saw.", "그런데 이 부인은 뾰족한 코에 캐묻기 좋아하는 눈을 가진, 비쩍 마른 누런 노처녀로, 무엇이든 다 보고는 본 것을 죄다 떠벌리고 다니는 사람이었다."], ["They disliked her, but had been taught to be kind to her, simply because she was old and poor, and had few friends.", "그들은 그녀를 싫어했지만, 단지 그녀가 늙고 가난하며 벗이 거의 없다는 이유로 그녀에게 친절하라고 배워 온 터였다."], ["So Meg gave her the easy-chair, and tried to entertain her, while she asked questions, criticised everything, and told stories of the people whom she knew.", "그래서 메그는 그녀에게 안락의자를 내주고 즐겁게 해 주려 애썼고, 그러는 동안 그녀는 이것저것 캐묻고, 무엇이든 트집을 잡고, 자기가 아는 사람들 이야기를 늘어놓았다."]], [["Language cannot describe the anxieties, experiences, and exertions which Jo underwent that morning; and the dinner she served up became a standing joke.", "그날 아침 조가 겪은 근심과 경험과 노고는 말로 다 할 수 없으며, 그녀가 차려 낸 저녁 식사는 두고두고 우스갯거리가 되었다."], ["Fearing to ask any more advice, she did her best alone, and discovered that something more than energy and good-will is necessary to make a cook.", "더는 조언을 구하기가 겁이 나서, 그녀는 혼자 최선을 다했고, 요리사가 되려면 의욕과 선의 이상의 무언가가 필요하다는 걸 깨달았다."], ["She boiled the asparagus for an hour, and was grieved to find the heads cooked off and the stalks harder than ever.", "아스파라거스를 한 시간이나 삶고 보니, 머리 부분은 푹 삶겨 떨어져 나가고 줄기는 어느 때보다 질겨져, 마음이 상했다."], ["The bread burnt black; for the salad-dressing so aggravated her, that she let everything else go till she had convinced herself that she could not make it fit to eat.", "빵은 시커멓게 탔다. 샐러드 드레싱이 어찌나 약을 올리는지, 그것을 먹을 만하게 만들 수 없다는 걸 스스로 확인할 때까지 다른 모든 걸 내팽개쳤기 때문이다."], ["The lobster was a scarlet mystery to her, but she hammered and poked, till it was unshelled, and its meagre proportions concealed in a grove of lettuce-leaves.", "바닷가재는 그녀에게 새빨간 수수께끼였지만, 두드리고 쑤신 끝에 껍데기를 벗겨, 그 빈약한 살점을 상추 잎 덤불 속에 감추었다."], ["The potatoes had to be hurried, not to keep the asparagus waiting, and were not done at last.", "감자는 아스파라거스를 기다리게 하지 않으려고 서둘러야 했는데, 결국 다 익지 못했다."], ["The blanc-mange was lumpy, and the strawberries not as ripe as they looked, having been skilfully \"deaconed.\"", "블랑망제는 멍울이 졌고, 딸기는 보기와 달리 그리 익지 않은 데다 솜씨 좋게 \"윗물만 좋아 보이게\" 골라 담긴 것이었다."]], [["\"Well, they can eat beef, and bread and butter, if they are hungry; only it's mortifying to have to spend your whole morning for nothing,\"", "\"뭐, 배가 고프면 소고기랑 버터 바른 빵을 먹으면 되지. 다만 아침 내내 헛고생한 게 분할 뿐이야.\""], ["thought Jo, as she rang the bell half an hour later than usual, and stood, hot, tired, and dispirited, surveying the feast spread for Laurie, accustomed to all sorts of elegance, and Miss Crocker, whose curious eyes would mark all failures, and whose tattling tongue would report them far and wide.", "조는 평소보다 삼십 분이나 늦게 종을 울리며 이렇게 생각했고, 덥고 지치고 풀이 죽은 채로, 온갖 우아한 것에 익숙한 로리와, 그 호기심 어린 눈으로 실패작이란 실패작은 죄다 짚어 내고 그 수다스러운 혀로 사방에 퍼뜨릴 크로커 양을 위해 차려진 잔칫상을 둘러보고 서 있었다."]], [["Poor Jo would gladly have gone under the table, as one thing after another was tasted and left; while Amy giggled, Meg looked distressed, Miss Crocker pursed up her lips, and Laurie talked and laughed with all his might, to give a cheerful tone to the festive scene.", "가엾은 조는 음식이 하나하나 맛만 보고 남겨지는 동안 차라리 식탁 밑으로 기어들어 가고 싶은 심정이었다. 그러는 동안 에이미는 킥킥거렸고, 메그는 괴로운 표정을 지었으며, 크로커 양은 입술을 오므렸고, 로리는 이 잔치 자리에 흥겨운 분위기를 돋우려고 온 힘을 다해 이야기하고 웃어 댔다."], ["Jo's one strong point was the fruit, for she had sugared it well, and had a pitcher of rich cream to eat with it.", "조의 한 가지 강점은 과일이었으니, 설탕을 충분히 뿌려 두었고 함께 먹을 진한 크림을 한 주전자 마련해 두었기 때문이다."], ["Her hot cheeks cooled a trifle, and she drew a long breath, as the pretty glass plates went round, and every one looked graciously at the little rosy islands floating in a sea of cream.", "예쁜 유리 접시가 한 바퀴 돌고, 다들 크림의 바다에 떠 있는 작고 발그레한 섬들을 흐뭇하게 바라보자, 달아올랐던 조의 두 뺨이 조금 식었고, 그녀는 긴 숨을 내쉬었다."], ["Miss Crocker tasted first, made a wry face, and drank some water hastily.", "크로커 양이 먼저 맛을 보더니, 얼굴을 찡그리고는 황급히 물을 들이켰다."], ["Jo, who had refused, thinking there might not be enough, for they dwindled sadly after the picking over, glanced at Laurie, but he was eating away manfully, though there was a slight pucker about his mouth, and he kept his eye fixed on his plate.", "골라낸 뒤에 딸기 양이 안타깝게 줄어든 터라 모자랄까 봐 사양했던 조는 로리를 흘끗 보았는데, 그는 입가가 약간 일그러지고 시선을 줄곧 접시에 고정한 채이긴 했어도 사내답게 꿋꿋이 먹고 있었다."], ["Amy, who was fond of delicate fare, took a heaping spoonful, choked, hid her face in her napkin, and left the table precipitately.", "입맛이 까다로운 에이미는 한 숟갈 수북이 떠 넣더니, 컥컥거리며 냅킨에 얼굴을 묻고는 황급히 식탁에서 일어났다."]], [["\"Oh, what is it?\"", "\"어머, 왜 그래?\""], ["exclaimed Jo trembling.", "조가 떨며 소리쳤다."]], [["\"Salt instead of sugar, and the cream is sour,\"", "\"설탕 대신 소금이 들어갔고, 크림은 쉬었어.\""], ["replied Meg, with a tragic gesture.", "메그가 비극적인 몸짓으로 대답했다."]], [["Jo uttered a groan, and fell back in her chair; remembering that she had given a last hasty powdering to the berries out of one of the two boxes on the kitchen table, and had neglected to put the milk in the refrigerator.", "조는 신음을 토하며 의자에 털썩 기댔다. 부엌 탁자 위 두 상자 중 하나에서 마지막으로 딸기에 황급히 가루를 뿌렸던 것과, 우유를 냉장고에 넣어 두는 걸 잊었던 것이 떠올랐기 때문이다."], ["She turned scarlet, and was on the verge of crying, when she met Laurie's eyes, which _would_ look merry in spite of his heroic efforts; the comical side of the affair suddenly struck her, and she laughed till the tears ran down her cheeks.", "그녀는 얼굴이 새빨개져서 막 울음을 터뜨리려던 참에 로리의 눈과 마주쳤는데, 그 눈은 그가 그토록 영웅적으로 애를 쓰는데도 기어이 즐거운 빛을 띠고 있었다. 문득 이 일의 우스운 면이 그녀의 마음을 때렸고, 그녀는 두 뺨에 눈물이 흐를 만큼 웃어 댔다."], ["So did every one else, even \"Croaker,\"", "다른 사람들도 모두, 소녀들이 \"투덜이\"라 부르는 그 노부인까지도 그랬다."], ["as the girls called the old lady; and the unfortunate dinner ended gayly, with bread and butter, olives and fun.", "그리하여 불운한 저녁 식사는 버터 바른 빵과 올리브와 웃음과 더불어 흥겹게 끝이 났다."]], [["\"I haven't strength of mind enough to clear up now, so we will sober ourselves with a funeral,\"", "\"지금은 치울 마음의 여력이 없으니, 장례식으로 우리 마음을 좀 가라앉히자.\" 일어서며 조가 말했다."], ["said Jo, as they rose; and Miss Crocker made ready to go, being eager to tell the new story at another friend's dinner-table.", "그리고 크로커 양은 다른 벗의 저녁 식탁에서 이 새로운 이야기를 들려주고 싶어 안달이 나서, 떠날 채비를 했다."]], [["They did sober themselves, for Beth's sake; Laurie dug a grave under the ferns in the grove, little Pip was laid in, with many tears, by his tender-hearted mistress, and covered with moss, while a wreath of violets and chickweed was hung on the stone which bore his epitaph, composed by Jo, while she struggled with the dinner:--", "그들은 베스를 위해 정말로 마음을 가라앉혔다. 로리가 숲속 고사리 아래 무덤을 팠고, 마음씨 고운 주인 베스가 숱한 눈물과 함께 어린 핍을 뉘었으며, 이끼로 덮어 주었다. 한편 핍의 비문이 새겨진 돌에는 제비꽃과 별꽃으로 엮은 화환이 걸렸는데, 그 비문은 조가 저녁 식사와 씨름하는 동안 지은 것이었다. \"여기 핍 마치 잠들다,"]], [["\"Here lies Pip March, Who died the 7th of June; Loved and lamented sore, And not forgotten soon.\"", "유월 칠일에 죽다. 사랑받고 깊이 애도되며,"]], [["At the conclusion of the ceremonies, Beth retired to her room, overcome with emotion and lobster; but there was no place of repose, for the beds were not made, and she found her grief much assuaged by beating up pillows and putting things in order.", "이내 잊히지 않으리.\" 의식이 끝나자, 베스는 북받치는 감정과 바닷가재 탓에 기진하여 자기 방으로 물러났다."], ["Meg helped Jo clear away the remains of the feast, which took half the afternoon, and left them so tired that they agreed to be contented with tea and toast for supper.", "그러나 침대가 정돈되어 있지 않아 쉴 자리가 없었고, 베개를 두드려 부풀리고 물건을 제자리에 두다 보니 슬픔이 훨씬 누그러지는 걸 느꼈다. 메그는 잔치의 잔해를 치우는 조를 도왔는데, 그 일에 오후의 절반이 걸려 둘 다 어찌나 지쳤던지, 저녁은 차와 토스트로 만족하기로 했다."], ["Laurie took Amy to drive, which was a deed of charity, for the sour cream seemed to have had a bad effect upon her temper. Mrs.", "로리는 에이미를 마차에 태워 드라이브를 시켜 주었는데, 그것은 자선 행위였으니, 쉰 크림이 그녀의 성미에 나쁜 영향을 미친 듯했기 때문이다."], ["March came home to find the three older girls hard at work in the middle of the afternoon; and a glance at the closet gave her an idea of the success of one part of the experiment.", "마치 부인이 집에 돌아와 보니 위의 세 딸이 오후의 한복판에 부지런히 일하고 있었고, 벽장을 한 번 흘끗 본 것만으로도 시험의 한 부분이 얼마나 성공했는지를 짐작할 수 있었다."]], [["Before the housewives could rest, several people called, and there was a scramble to get ready to see them; then tea must be got, errands done; and one or two necessary bits of sewing neglected till the last minute.", "이 살림꾼들이 미처 쉬기도 전에 몇 사람이 찾아왔고, 그들을 맞이할 채비를 하느라 한바탕 부산을 떨었다. 그러고는 차를 준비해야 했고, 심부름도 해야 했으며, 꼭 해야 할 바느질 한두 가지는 마지막 순간까지 미뤄졌다."], ["As twilight fell, dewy and still, one by one they gathered in the porch where the June roses were budding beautifully, and each groaned or sighed as she sat down, as if tired or troubled.", "이슬이 내려 고요한 황혼이 깔리자, 그들은 유월의 장미가 아름답게 봉오리를 맺고 있는 현관에 하나둘 모여들었고, 저마다 지쳤거나 시름에 잠긴 듯 자리에 앉으며 끙끙대거나 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["\"What a dreadful day this has been!\"", "\"오늘 정말 끔찍한 하루였어!\""], ["begun Jo, usually the first to speak.", "늘 제일 먼저 입을 떼는 조가 운을 뗐다."]], [["\"It has seemed shorter than usual, but _so_ uncomfortable,\"", "\"평소보다 짧게 느껴지긴 했는데, 정말이지 불편한 하루였어.\""], ["said Meg.", "메그가 말했다."]], [["\"Not a bit like home,\"", "\"우리 집 같지가 않았어.\""], ["added Amy.", "에이미가 덧붙였다."]], [["\"It can't seem so without Marmee and little Pip,\"", "\"엄마랑 어린 핍이 없으니 그럴 수밖에.\""], ["sighed Beth, glancing, with full eyes, at the empty cage above her head.", "베스가 한숨지으며, 눈물 그렁그렁한 눈으로 머리 위 텅 빈 새장을 올려다보았다."]], [["\"Here's mother, dear, and you shall have another bird to-morrow, if you want it.\"", "\"여기 엄마 왔다, 얘야. 그리고 원한다면 내일 새를 한 마리 더 사 줄게.\""]], [["As she spoke, Mrs. March came and took her place among them, looking as if her holiday had not been much pleasanter than theirs.", "이렇게 말하며 마치 부인이 다가와 아이들 사이에 자리를 잡았는데, 그녀의 휴일도 그들의 하루보다 그리 즐겁지는 않았던 듯한 표정이었다."]], [["\"Are you satisfied with your experiment, girls, or do you want another week of it?\"", "\"얘들아, 너희 시험에 만족하니, 아니면 일주일 더 해 보고 싶으니?\""], ["she asked, as Beth nestled up to her, and the rest turned toward her with brightening faces, as flowers turn toward the sun.", "그녀가 묻자, 베스는 엄마 품에 파고들었고, 나머지 아이들은 꽃이 해를 향하듯 환해진 얼굴로 그녀를 돌아보았다."]], [["\"I don't!\"", "\"전 싫어요!\""], ["cried Jo decidedly.", "조가 단호하게 외쳤다."]], [["\"Nor I,\"", "\"저도요.\""], ["echoed the others.", "다른 아이들도 따라 외쳤다."]], [["\"You think, then, that it is better to have a few duties, and live a little for others, do you?\"", "\"그렇다면, 해야 할 일이 조금은 있고, 남을 위해 조금은 살아가는 편이 더 낫다고 생각하는 거니?\""]], [["\"Lounging and larking doesn't pay,\"", "\"빈둥대고 놀기만 하는 건 남는 게 없어요.\""], ["observed Jo, shaking her head.", "조가 고개를 가로저으며 말했다."], ["\"I'm tired of it, and mean to go to work at something right off.\"", "\"이젠 진절머리가 나요, 당장 뭔가 일에 매달릴 거예요.\""]], [["\"Suppose you learn plain cooking; that's a useful accomplishment, which no woman should be without,\"", "\"평범한 요리를 배우는 게 어떻겠니. 그건 유익한 재주라서, 어떤 여자도 모르고 지내선 안 되는 거야.\""], ["said Mrs.", "마치 부인이 조의 만찬을 떠올리며 소리 없이 웃으며 말했다."], ["March, laughing inaudibly at the recollection of Jo's dinner-party; for she had met Miss Crocker, and heard her account of it.", "그녀는 크로커 양과 마주쳐 그 일에 대한 이야기를 들었던 것이다."]], [["\"Mother, did you go away and let everything be, just to see how we'd get on?\"", "\"엄마, 그럼 우리가 어떻게 헤쳐 나가는지 보려고 일부러 나가서 다 내버려 두신 거예요?\""], ["cried Meg, who had had suspicions all day.", "종일 미심쩍어하던 메그가 외쳤다."]], [["\"Yes; I wanted you to see how the comfort of all depends on each doing her share faithfully.", "\"그래. 너희가 저마다 자기 몫을 충실히 해내는 데 모두의 안락이 달려 있다는 걸 알았으면 했단다."], ["While Hannah and I did your work, you got on pretty well, though I don't think you were very happy or amiable; so I thought, as a little lesson, I would show you what happens when every one thinks only of herself.", "해나와 내가 너희 일을 하는 동안에는 너희가 제법 잘 지냈지. 그렇게 행복하거나 상냥하진 않았던 것 같지만 말이야."], ["Don't you feel that it is pleasanter to help one another, to have daily duties which make leisure sweet when it comes, and to bear and forbear, that home may be comfortable and lovely to us all?\"", "그래서 작은 교훈으로, 다들 자기 생각만 할 때 어떤 일이 벌어지는지 보여 주려 했던 거야. 서로 돕고, 여가가 왔을 때 그것을 달콤하게 해 주는 나날의 의무를 갖고, 집이 우리 모두에게 안락하고 사랑스러운 곳이 되도록 참고 양보하는 게 더 즐겁다는 걸 이제 알겠지?\""]], [["\"We do, mother, we do!\"", "\"알아요, 엄마, 정말 알아요!\""], ["cried the girls.", "소녀들이 외쳤다."]], [["\"Then let me advise you to take up your little burdens again; for though they seem heavy sometimes, they are good for us, and lighten as we learn to carry them.", "\"그럼 너희에게 권하건대, 너희의 작은 짐을 다시 짊어지렴. 그것이 때로는 무거워 보여도 우리에게 이로운 것이고, 짊어지는 법을 배워 갈수록 가벼워지는 법이란다."], ["Work is wholesome, and there is plenty for every one; it keeps us from _ennui_ and mischief, is good for health and spirits, and gives us a sense of power and independence better than money or fashion.\"", "일은 몸에 좋은 것이고, 누구에게나 할 일은 넘치도록 많아. 일은 우리를 권태와 못된 장난에서 지켜 주고, 건강과 기분에 이로우며, 돈이나 유행보다 나은 힘과 독립의 감각을 안겨 주지.\""]], [["\"We'll work like bees, and love it too; see if we don't!\"", "\"우린 꿀벌처럼 일할 거예요, 그리고 그걸 좋아하기까지 할 거예요. 두고 보세요!\""], ["said Jo.", "조가 말했다."], ["\"I'll learn plain cooking for my holiday task; and the next dinner-party I have shall be a success.\"", "\"전 방학 숙제로 평범한 요리를 배울 거예요. 그래서 다음에 만찬을 열 땐 꼭 성공할 거예요.\""]], [["\"I'll make the set of shirts for father, instead of letting you do it, Marmee.", "\"엄마가 하시게 두는 대신, 제가 아빠 셔츠 한 벌을 지을게요, 엄마."], ["I can and I will, though I'm not fond of sewing; that will be better than fussing over my own things, which are plenty nice enough as they are,\"", "바느질을 좋아하진 않지만 할 수 있고, 또 할 거예요. 그게 지금 이대로도 충분히 좋은 내 옷가지나 만지작거리는 것보다 나을 거예요.\""], ["said Meg.", "메그가 말했다."]], [["\"I'll do my lessons every day, and not spend so much time with my music and dolls.", "\"전 매일 공부를 할 거고, 음악이랑 인형에 시간을 그렇게 많이 쏟지 않을래요."], ["I am a stupid thing, and ought to be studying, not playing,\"", "전 멍청한 애라서, 놀 게 아니라 공부를 해야 해요.\" 이것이 베스의 결심이었다."], ["was Beth's resolution; while Amy followed their example by heroically declaring, \"I shall learn to make button-holes, and attend to my parts of speech.\"", "한편 에이미도 그들을 본받아 영웅적으로 선언했다. \"전 단춧구멍 만드는 법을 배우고, 제 품사에 신경 쓸 거예요.\""]], [["\"Very good!", "\"아주 좋아!"], ["then I am quite satisfied with the experiment, and fancy that we shall not have to repeat it; only don't go to the other extreme, and delve like slaves.", "그럼 난 이 시험에 아주 만족하고, 다시 되풀이할 필요는 없을 것 같구나. 다만 다른 극단으로 치달아 노예처럼 죽도록 일하지는 마라."], ["Have regular hours for work and play; make each day both useful and pleasant, and prove that you understand the worth of time by employing it well.", "일하는 시간과 노는 시간을 정해 두고, 하루하루를 유익하면서도 즐겁게 보내며, 시간을 잘 써서 그 가치를 안다는 걸 증명하렴."], ["Then youth will be delightful, old age will bring few regrets, and life become a beautiful success, in spite of poverty.\"", "그러면 젊음은 즐거울 것이고, 노년에는 후회가 적을 것이며, 가난 속에서도 인생은 아름다운 성공이 될 거야.\""]], [["\"We'll remember, mother!\"", "\"명심할게요, 엄마!\""], ["and they did.", "그리고 그들은 정말로 그렇게 했다."]], [["# CAMP LAURENCE.", "# CAMP LAURENCE."]], [["Beth was post-mistress, for, being most at home, she could attend to it regularly, and dearly liked the daily task of unlocking the little door and distributing the mail.", "베스가 우체국장이었으니, 가장 집에 많이 있는 데다 그 일을 꼬박꼬박 챙길 수 있었고, 매일 그 작은 문을 열어 우편물을 나눠 주는 일을 무척 좋아했기 때문이다."], ["One July day she came in with her hands full, and went about the house leaving letters and parcels, like the penny post.", "칠월의 어느 날, 그녀는 두 손 가득 우편물을 안고 들어와, 동전 우편배달부처럼 집안을 돌며 편지와 소포를 놓아두었다."]], [["\"Here's your posy, mother!", "\"엄마, 꽃다발 왔어요!"], ["Laurie never forgets that,\"", "로리는 이걸 절대 잊는 법이 없다니까.\""], ["she said, putting the fresh nosegay in the vase that stood in \"Marmee's corner,\"", "그녀가 \"엄마의 구석\"에 놓인 꽃병에 싱싱한 꽃다발을 꽂으며 말했다."], ["and was kept supplied by the affectionate boy.", "그 꽃병은 다정한 그 소년 덕에 늘 꽃이 채워져 있었다."]], [["\"Miss Meg March, one letter and a glove,\"", "\"메그 마치 양, 편지 한 통과 장갑 한 짝.\""], ["continued Beth, delivering the articles to her sister, who sat near her mother, stitching wristbands.", "베스가 어머니 곁에 앉아 소맷부리를 깁고 있는 언니에게 물건을 건네며 말을 이었다."]], [["\"Why, I left a pair over there, and here is only one,\"", "\"어머, 난 저기 한 켤레를 두고 왔는데, 여긴 한 짝뿐이네.\""], ["said Meg, looking at the gray cotton glove.", "메그가 회색 무명 장갑을 보며 말했다."]], [["\"Didn't you drop the other in the garden?\"", "\"나머지 한 짝은 정원에 떨어뜨린 거 아니야?\""]], [["\"No, I'm sure I didn't; for there was only one in the office.\"", "\"아니야, 그러지 않은 게 분명해. 우체국엔 한 짝밖에 없었거든.\""]], [["\"I hate to have odd gloves!", "\"짝 안 맞는 장갑은 정말 질색인데!"], ["Never mind, the other may be found.", "괜찮아, 나머지 한 짝도 나오겠지."], ["My letter is only a translation of the German song I wanted; I think Mr.", "내 편지는 그냥 내가 갖고 싶었던 독일 노래 번역일 뿐이야."], ["Brooke did it, for this isn't Laurie's writing.\"", "브룩 씨가 해 준 것 같아, 로리 글씨가 아니거든.\""]], [["Mrs.", "마치 부인은 메그를 흘끗 보았다."], ["March glanced at Meg, who was looking very pretty in her gingham morning-gown, with the little curls blowing about her forehead, and very womanly, as she sat sewing at her little work-table, full of tidy white rolls; so unconscious of the thought in her mother's mind as she sewed and sung, while her fingers flew, and her thoughts were busied with girlish fancies as innocent and fresh as the pansies in her belt, that Mrs.", "깅엄 아침 가운 차림에 이마 위로 작은 곱슬머리가 나풀거리는 메그는 무척 예뻤고, 정갈하게 둘둘 만 흰 천이 가득한 작은 반짇고리 곁에 앉아 바느질하는 모습이 무척 여인다웠다. 손가락은 부지런히 움직이고, 생각은 허리띠에 꽂힌 팬지꽃처럼 순수하고 싱그러운 소녀다운 공상에 잠긴 채, 바느질하며 노래하는 메그는 어머니의 마음속 생각을 까맣게 모르고 있었다."], ["March smiled, and was satisfied.", "그래서 마치 부인은 미소 지으며 흐뭇해했다."]], [["\"Two letters for Doctor Jo, a book, and a funny old hat, which covered the whole post-office, stuck outside,\"", "\"조 박사님께 편지 두 통, 책 한 권, 그리고 우체국 전체를 뒤덮을 만큼 우스꽝스러운 낡은 모자가 바깥에 박혀 있어.\""], ["said Beth, laughing, as she went into the study, where Jo sat writing.", "베스가 웃으며 말하고는, 조가 앉아 글을 쓰고 있는 서재로 들어갔다."]], [["\"What a sly fellow Laurie is!", "\"로리는 정말 능청맞은 녀석이야!"], ["I said I wished bigger hats were the fashion, because I burn my face every hot day. He said, 'Why mind the fashion?", "더운 날마다 얼굴이 다 타니까 더 큰 모자가 유행이면 좋겠다고 했더니, 걔가 '유행 따위 신경 쓸 게 뭐람?"], ["Wear a big hat, and be comfortable!'", "큰 모자를 쓰고 편하게 지내!'라고 하잖아."], ["I said I would if I had one, and he has sent me this, to try me.", "나도 모자가 있으면 그러겠다고 했더니, 나를 시험하려고 이걸 보낸 거지."], ["I'll wear it, for fun, and show him I _don't_ care for the fashion;\"", "난 재미 삼아 쓰고서, 내가 유행 따위 신경 안 쓴다는 걸 걔한테 보여 줄 거야.\""], ["and, hanging the antique broad-brim on a bust of Plato, Jo read her letters.", "그러고는 그 고풍스러운 챙 넓은 모자를 플라톤 흉상에 걸어 두고, 조는 편지를 읽었다."]], [["One from her mother made her cheeks glow and her eyes fill, for it said to her,--", "어머니가 보낸 한 통은 그녀의 두 뺨을 달아오르게 하고 눈가를 적셨으니, 거기엔 이렇게 쓰여 있었다."]], [["\"I write a little word to tell you with how much satisfaction I watch your efforts to control your temper.", "\"사랑하는 딸에게,\n네가 성미를 다스리려고 애쓰는 모습을 얼마나 흐뭇하게 지켜보고 있는지 알려 주려고 짧은 글을 적는다."], ["You say nothing about your trials, failures, or successes, and think, perhaps, that no one sees them but the Friend whose help you daily ask, if I may trust the well-worn cover of your guide-book.", "너는 네 시련이나 실패, 성공에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않고, 어쩌면 매일 도움을 구하는 그 벗 말고는 아무도 그것을 보지 못한다고 생각하는지도 모르겠구나. 네 안내서의 닳고 닳은 표지를 믿어도 된다면 말이야."], ["_I_, too, have seen them all, and heartily believe in the sincerity of your resolution, since it begins to bear fruit.", "나 역시 그 모든 것을 보았고, 네 결심이 진심임을 진심으로 믿는단다. 이제 그것이 열매를 맺기 시작했으니까."], ["Go on, dear, patiently and bravely, and always believe that no one sympathizes more tenderly with you than your loving", "계속하렴, 얘야, 참을성 있게 그리고 용감하게. 그리고 너를 사랑하는 이 엄마보다 더 다정하게 너와 마음을 함께하는 사람은 없다는 걸 언제나 믿으렴."]], [["\"That does me good!", "엄마가.\" \"이게 큰 힘이 돼!"], ["that's worth millions of money and pecks of praise.", "이건 수백만 금이나 산더미 같은 칭찬보다 값진 거야."], ["O Marmee, I do try!", "오 엄마, 저 정말 노력하고 있어요!"], ["I will keep on trying, and not get tired, since I have you to help me.\"", "저를 도와주실 엄마가 계시니, 계속 노력하고 지치지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["Laying her head on her arms, Jo wet her little romance with a few happy tears, for she _had_ thought that no one saw and appreciated her efforts to be good; and this assurance was doubly precious, doubly encouraging, because unexpected, and from the person whose commendation she most valued.", "조는 두 팔에 머리를 묻고, 자신의 작은 소설을 행복한 눈물 몇 방울로 적셨다. 자신이 착해지려고 애쓰는 것을 아무도 보지도 알아주지도 않는다고 여겨 왔기 때문이다."], ["Feeling stronger than ever to meet and subdue her Apollyon, she pinned the note inside her frock, as a shield and a reminder, lest she be taken unaware, and proceeded to open her other letter, quite ready for either good or bad news.", "게다가 이 다짐은 뜻밖이었고, 그것도 자신이 가장 소중히 여기는 사람의 칭찬이었기에, 갑절로 귀하고 갑절로 힘이 났다. 자신의 아폴리온과 맞서 싸워 무찌를 힘이 그 어느 때보다 강해진 것을 느끼며, 그녀는 불시에 당하지 않도록 방패이자 일깨움으로 삼고자 그 쪽지를 옷 안쪽에 핀으로 꽂았다."], ["In a big, dashing hand, Laurie wrote,--", "그러고는 좋은 소식이든 나쁜 소식이든 받아들일 채비를 갖추고서 다른 편지를 펼쳤다. 큼직하고 활달한 글씨로 로리는 이렇게 썼다."]], [["\"DEAR JO, What ho!", "\"조에게,\n이게 웬일이냐!"]], [["Some English girls and boys are coming to see me to-morrow and I want to have a jolly time.", "내일 영국 남자애들이랑 여자애들 몇이 나를 보러 오는데, 신나게 놀고 싶어."], ["If it's fine, I'm going to pitch my tent in Longmeadow, and row up the whole crew to lunch and croquet,--have a fire, make messes, gypsy fashion, and all sorts of larks.", "날이 좋으면 롱메도에 천막을 치고, 다 함께 노 저어 올라가 점심도 먹고 크로케도 할 거야. 불도 피우고, 집시처럼 음식도 만들고, 별의별 신나는 놀이를 다 할 거야."], ["They are nice people, and like such things.", "다들 좋은 애들이고, 이런 걸 좋아해."], ["Brooke will go, to keep us boys steady, and Kate Vaughn will play propriety for the girls.", "브룩 선생님도 우리 사내애들을 점잖게 단속하러 갈 거고, 케이트 본이 여자애들 인솔자 노릇을 할 거야."], ["I want you all to come; can't let Beth off, at any price, and nobody shall worry her.", "너희 모두 왔으면 해. 베스도 무슨 일이 있어도 빠지면 안 돼, 아무도 걜 괴롭히지 못하게 할게."], ["Don't bother about rations,--I'll see to that, and everything else,--only do come, there's a good fellow!", "먹을 것 걱정은 하지 마 ─ 그건 내가 알아서 할게, 다른 것도 다. 그저 꼭 와 줘, 착한 친구니까!"]], [["\"In a tearing hurry, Yours ever, LAURIE.\"", "정신없이 바쁜 와중에,\n언제나 너의, 로리.\""]], [["\"Here's richness!\"", "\"이거 횡재네!\""], ["cried Jo, flying in to tell the news to Meg.", "조가 메그에게 소식을 전하러 날듯이 뛰어 들어가며 외쳤다."]], [["\"Of course we can go, mother?", "\"우리 당연히 가도 되죠, 엄마?"], ["it will be such a help to Laurie, for I can row, and Meg see to the lunch, and the children be useful in some way.\"", "로리한테 큰 도움이 될 거예요, 제가 노를 저을 수 있고, 메그 언니가 점심을 챙기고, 동생들도 어떻게든 쓸모가 있을 거잖아요.\""]], [["\"I hope the Vaughns are not fine, grown-up people.", "\"본 씨네가 점잖은 어른들이 아니었으면 좋겠는데."], ["Do you know anything about them, Jo?\"", "조, 너 그 사람들에 대해 뭐 좀 아니?\""], ["asked Meg.", "메그가 물었다."]], [["\"Only that there are four of them.", "\"넷이 있다는 것밖에 몰라."], ["Kate is older than you, Fred and Frank (twins) about my age, and a little girl (Grace), who is nine or ten.", "케이트는 언니보다 나이가 많고, 프레드랑 프랭크(쌍둥이야)는 나랑 비슷한 또래고, 어린 여자애(그레이스)는 아홉이나 열 살쯤이래."], ["Laurie knew them abroad, and liked the boys; I fancied, from the way he primmed up his mouth in speaking of her, that he didn't admire Kate much.\"", "로리가 외국에서 알게 됐는데, 남자애들은 좋아했어. 케이트 얘기를 할 때 입을 새침하게 오므리는 걸 보니, 케이트는 그다지 마음에 안 드는 것 같더라.\""]], [["\"I'm so glad my French print is clean; it's just the thing, and so becoming!\"", "\"내 프랑스 사라사 옷이 깨끗해서 정말 다행이야. 그게 딱 좋고, 또 얼마나 잘 어울리는데!\""], ["observed Meg complacently.", "메그가 흡족하게 말했다."], ["\"Have you anything decent, Jo?\"", "\"조, 넌 입을 만한 게 좀 있니?\""]], [["\"Scarlet and gray boating suit, good enough for me.", "\"빨강이랑 회색 뱃놀이옷, 나한테는 그거면 충분해."], ["I shall row and tramp about, so I don't want any starch to think of.", "난 노 젓고 여기저기 쏘다닐 거니까, 풀 먹인 옷 따위는 신경 쓰고 싶지 않거든."], ["You'll come, Bethy?\"", "너도 올 거지, 베스?\""]], [["\"If you won't let any of the boys talk to me.\"", "\"남자애들 누구도 나한테 말 안 걸게 해 준다면.\""]], [["\"Not a boy!\"", "\"한 명도!\""]], [["\"I like to please Laurie; and I'm not afraid of Mr.", "\"난 로리를 기쁘게 해 주고 싶어. 그리고 브룩 선생님은 워낙 친절하셔서 무섭지 않아."], ["Brooke, he is so kind; but I don't want to play, or sing, or say anything.", "그렇지만 난 놀고 싶지도, 노래하고 싶지도, 무슨 말을 하고 싶지도 않아."], ["I'll work hard, and not trouble any one; and you'll take care of me, Jo, so I'll go.\"", "열심히 일하면서 아무도 성가시게 안 할게. 그리고 언니가 날 챙겨 줄 테니까, 갈래.\""]], [["\"That's my good girl; you do try to fight off your shyness, and I love you for it.", "\"착하기도 하지. 넌 정말로 그 부끄럼을 떨쳐 내려고 애쓰는구나, 그래서 난 네가 좋아."], ["Fighting faults isn't easy, as I know; and a cheery word kind of gives a lift.", "잘못과 싸우는 게 쉽지 않다는 걸 나도 알거든. 그럴 때 다정한 말 한마디가 힘이 되어 주잖니."], ["Thank you, mother,\"", "고마워요, 엄마.\""], ["and Jo gave the thin cheek a grateful kiss, more precious to Mrs. March than if it had given back the rosy roundness of her youth.", "그러고는 조가 그 야윈 뺨에 고마운 입맞춤을 했는데, 마치 부인에게는 그것이 젊은 날의 발그레하고 통통하던 뺨을 되찾는 것보다 더 값진 것이었다."]], [["\"I had a box of chocolate drops, and the picture I wanted to copy,\"", "\"난 초콜릿 드롭스 한 상자랑, 내가 베껴 그리고 싶었던 그림을 받았어.\""], ["said Amy, showing her mail.", "에이미가 자기 우편물을 보이며 말했다."]], [["\"And I got a note from Mr.", "\"그리고 난 로런스 할아버지한테 쪽지를 받았어."], ["Laurence, asking me to come over and play to him to-night, before the lamps are lighted, and I shall go,\"", "오늘 밤 등불을 켜기 전에 건너와서 연주해 달라고 하셔서, 갈 거야.\""], ["added Beth, whose friendship with the old gentleman prospered finely.", "노신사와의 우정이 무르익어 가던 베스가 덧붙였다."]], [["\"Now let's fly round, and do double duty to-day, so that we can play to-morrow with free minds,\"", "\"자, 이제 부지런히 움직여서 오늘은 두 배로 일하자. 그래야 내일은 홀가분한 마음으로 놀 수 있으니까.\""], ["said Jo, preparing to replace her pen with a broom.", "조가 펜을 빗자루로 바꿔 들 채비를 하며 말했다."]], [["When the sun peeped into the girls' room early next morning, to promise them a fine day, he saw a comical sight.", "이튿날 이른 아침, 좋은 날씨를 약속하려고 햇살이 소녀들의 방을 빼꼼 들여다보았을 때, 그 눈에 우스꽝스러운 광경이 들어왔다."], ["Each had made such preparation for the fête as seemed necessary and proper.", "저마다 이 잔치를 위해 필요하고 마땅해 보이는 채비를 해 둔 것이었다."], ["Meg had an extra row of little curl-papers across her forehead, Jo had copiously anointed her afflicted face with cold cream, Beth had taken Joanna to bed with her to atone for the approaching separation, and Amy had capped the climax by putting a clothes-pin on her nose, to uplift the offending feature.", "메그는 이마에 작은 종이 머리 마는 것을 한 줄 더 둘렀고, 조는 화상 입은 얼굴에 콜드크림을 듬뿍 발랐으며, 베스는 다가올 이별을 보상하려고 조애나를 데리고 잤고, 에이미는 그 코를 추켜올려 주려고 거슬리는 코에 빨래집게를 집어 화룡점정을 찍었다."], ["It was one of the kind artists use to hold the paper on their drawing-boards, therefore quite appropriate and effective for the purpose to which it was now put.", "그것은 화가들이 그림판에 종이를 고정하는 데 쓰는 종류라, 이제 쓰인 용도에 아주 적절하고 효과적이었다."], ["This funny spectacle appeared to amuse the sun, for he burst out with such radiance that Jo woke up, and roused all her sisters by a hearty laugh at Amy's ornament.", "이 우스운 광경이 해를 즐겁게 했는지, 그가 어찌나 환하게 터져 나왔던지 조가 잠에서 깼고, 에이미의 장식을 보고 한바탕 호탕하게 웃어 동생들을 모두 깨웠다."]], [["Sunshine and laughter were good omens for a pleasure party, and soon a lively bustle began in both houses.", "햇살과 웃음은 놀이 모임에 좋은 징조였고, 곧 양쪽 집에서 활기찬 부산함이 시작되었다."], ["Beth, who was ready first, kept reporting what went on next door, and enlivened her sisters' toilets by frequent telegrams from the window.", "제일 먼저 채비를 마친 베스는 옆집에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 줄곧 알렸고, 창가에서 수시로 전보를 띄워 언니 동생의 단장에 활기를 불어넣었다."]], [["\"There goes the man with the tent! I see Mrs.", "\"천막 든 사람이 간다!"], ["Barker doing up the lunch in a hamper and a great basket. Now Mr.", "바커 부인이 광주리랑 큼지막한 바구니에 점심을 담는 게 보여."], ["Laurence is looking up at the sky, and the weathercock; I wish he would go, too.", "이제 로런스 할아버지가 하늘이랑 바람개비를 올려다보셔. 할아버지도 같이 가시면 좋겠다."], ["There's Laurie, looking like a sailor,--nice boy!", "저기 로리가 있는데, 꼭 선원 같아 ─ 멋진 녀석!"], ["Oh, mercy me!", "어머, 맙소사!"], ["here's a carriage full of people--a tall lady, a little girl, and two dreadful boys.", "여기 사람들로 가득 찬 마차가 왔어 ─ 키 큰 부인이랑, 어린 여자애랑, 끔찍한 남자애 둘."], ["One is lame; poor thing, he's got a crutch.", "한 명은 다리를 절어. 가엾어라, 목발을 짚었네."], ["Laurie didn't tell us that.", "로리가 그 얘긴 안 해 줬잖아."], ["Be quick, girls!", "서둘러, 얘들아!"], ["it's getting late.", "늦겠어."], ["Why, there is Ned Moffat, I do declare.", "어머, 저기 네드 모팻이잖아, 정말이야."], ["Look, Meg, isn't that the man who bowed to you one day, when we were shopping?\"", "봐 봐, 메그 언니, 저 사람 우리가 장 보러 갔을 때 언니한테 인사했던 그 사람 아니야?\""]], [["\"So it is.", "\"맞네."], ["How queer that he should come.", "저 사람이 오다니 참 묘하다."], ["I thought he was at the Mountains.", "산에 가 있는 줄 알았는데."], ["There is Sallie; I'm glad she got back in time.", "저기 샐리도 있어. 제때 돌아와서 다행이야."], ["Am I all right, Jo?\"", "나 괜찮아 보여, 조?\""], ["cried Meg, in a flutter.", "메그가 들떠서 외쳤다."]], [["\"A regular daisy.", "\"영락없는 데이지꽃이야."], ["Hold up your dress and put your hat straight; it looks sentimental tipped that way, and will fly off at the first puff.", "옷자락 잡고 모자 똑바로 써. 그렇게 비스듬히 쓰니까 감상적으로 보이고, 바람 한 번 불면 날아가겠어."], ["Now, then, come on!\"", "자 그럼, 가자!\""]], [["\"O Jo, you are not going to wear that awful hat?", "\"오 조, 너 그 끔찍한 모자 쓰고 가려는 건 아니지?"], ["It's too absurd!", "너무 우스꽝스러워!"], ["You shall _not_ make a guy of yourself,\"", "너 스스로를 웃음거리로 만들면 안 돼.\""], ["remonstrated Meg, as Jo tied down, with a red ribbon, the broad-brimmed, old-fashioned Leghorn Laurie had sent for a joke.", "조가 로리가 장난삼아 보낸 그 챙 넓은 구식 밀짚모자를 빨간 리본으로 동여매자, 메그가 나무랐다."]], [["\"I just will, though, for it's capital,--so shady, light, and big.", "\"아니, 난 쓸 거야, 이게 일품이거든 ─ 그늘도 지고, 가볍고, 큼직하잖아."], ["It will make fun; and I don't mind being a guy if I'm comfortable.\"", "재미도 있을 거고. 편하기만 하면 웃음거리가 되는 것쯤은 상관없어.\""], ["With that Jo marched straight away, and the rest followed,--a bright little band of sisters, all looking their best, in summer suits, with happy faces under the jaunty hat-brims.", "그 말과 함께 조는 곧장 성큼성큼 걸어 나갔고, 나머지가 뒤따랐다 ─ 챙 끝 멋스럽게 늘어진 모자 아래로 행복한 얼굴을 하고, 여름옷을 차려입은 채 저마다 가장 멋진 모습을 한 한 무리의 밝은 자매들이었다."]], [["Laurie ran to meet, and present them to his friends, in the most cordial manner.", "로리가 달려 나와 그들을 맞이하고는, 더없이 다정한 태도로 자기 친구들에게 소개했다."], ["The lawn was the reception-room, and for several minutes a lively scene was enacted there.", "잔디밭이 응접실이 되어, 한동안 그곳에서 활기찬 장면이 펼쳐졌다."], ["Meg was grateful to see that Miss Kate, though twenty, was dressed with a simplicity which American girls would do well to imitate; and she was much flattered by Mr. Ned's assurances that he came especially to see her.", "메그는 케이트 양이 스무 살인데도 미국 소녀들이 본받으면 좋을 만큼 소박하게 차려입은 것을 보고 흐뭇했고, 자기를 보러 특별히 왔다는 네드 씨의 장담에 으쓱했다."], ["Jo understood why Laurie \"primmed up his mouth\" when speaking of Kate, for that young lady had a stand-off-don't-touch-me air, which contrasted strongly with the free and easy demeanor of the other girls.", "조는 로리가 케이트 이야기를 할 때 어째서 \"입을 새침하게 오므렸\"는지 알 수 있었으니, 그 아가씨는 가까이 오지 마 손대지 마 하는 분위기를 풍겨, 다른 소녀들의 자유롭고 편안한 태도와 뚜렷이 대조되었기 때문이다."], ["Beth took an observation of the new boys, and decided that the lame one was not \"dreadful,\" but gentle and feeble, and she would be kind to him on that account.", "베스는 새로 온 남자애들을 살피더니, 다리 저는 아이는 \"끔찍하\"기는커녕 온순하고 가냘프다고 판단하고, 그 점 때문에 그에게 친절하게 대해 주리라 마음먹었다."], ["Amy found Grace a well-mannered, merry little person; and after staring dumbly at one another for a few minutes, they suddenly became very good friends.", "에이미는 그레이스가 예의 바르고 명랑한 어린 아가씨임을 알게 되었고, 몇 분간 말없이 서로를 빤히 쳐다보던 끝에 두 사람은 어느새 아주 좋은 친구가 되었다."]], [["Tents, lunch, and croquet utensils having been sent on beforehand, the party was soon embarked, and the two boats pushed off together, leaving Mr.", "천막과 점심과 크로케 용구가 미리 보내진 터라, 일행은 곧 배에 올랐고, 로런스 할아버지가 강가에서 모자를 흔드는 가운데 두 배가 함께 출발했다."], ["Laurence waving his hat on the shore. Laurie and Jo rowed one boat; Mr.", "로리와 조가 한 배의 노를 저었고, 브룩 씨와 네드가 다른 배를 저었다."], ["Brooke and Ned the other; while Fred Vaughn, the riotous twin, did his best to upset both by paddling about in a wherry like a disturbed water-bug.", "한편 떠들썩한 쌍둥이 프레드 본은 성가신 물벌레처럼 거룻배를 타고 이리저리 휘젓고 다니며 두 배를 다 뒤집어 놓으려고 기를 썼다. 조의 우스운 모자는 감사의 표결을 받을 만했으니, 두루 쓸모가 있었기 때문이다."], ["Jo's funny hat deserved a vote of thanks, for it was of general utility; it broke the ice in the beginning, by producing a laugh; it created quite a refreshing breeze, flapping to and fro, as she rowed, and would make an excellent umbrella for the whole party, if a shower came up, she said. Kate looked rather amazed at Jo's proceedings, especially as she exclaimed \"Christopher Columbus!\"", "그것은 시작부터 웃음을 자아내어 어색함을 깨뜨렸고, 조가 노를 저을 때 앞뒤로 펄럭이며 제법 시원한 바람을 일으켰으며, 소나기가 쏟아지면 일행 전체를 위한 훌륭한 우산이 될 거라고 그녀는 말했다."], ["when she lost her oar; and Laurie said, \"My dear fellow, did I hurt you?\" when he tripped over her feet in taking his place.", "케이트는 조의 행동에 다소 어리둥절한 표정이었는데, 특히 노를 놓쳤을 때 \"콜럼버스 만세!\"라고 외치고, 로리가 자기 자리에 앉다가 그녀의 발에 걸려 넘어지며 \"이런, 친구, 내가 아프게 했나?\"라고 했을 때 더욱 그러했다."], ["But after putting up her glass to examine the queer girl several times, Miss Kate decided that she was \"odd, but rather clever,\" and smiled upon her from afar.", "그러나 안경을 추켜올려 그 별난 소녀를 여러 번 살펴본 끝에, 케이트 양은 그녀가 \"별나긴 해도 제법 영리하다\"고 판단하고, 멀찍이서 그녀에게 미소를 지어 보였다."]], [["Meg, in the other boat, was delightfully situated, face to face with the rowers, who both admired the prospect, and feathered their oars with uncommon \"skill and dexterity.\" Mr.", "다른 배에 탄 메그는 노 젓는 두 사람과 마주 보는, 더없이 흐뭇한 자리에 앉아 있었는데, 두 사람은 경치도 감탄하며 보고, 보기 드문 \"솜씨와 능란함\"으로 노를 깃처럼 눕혀 저었다."], ["Brooke was a grave, silent young man, with handsome brown eyes and a pleasant voice.", "브룩 씨는 진중하고 과묵한 청년으로, 잘생긴 갈색 눈에 듣기 좋은 목소리를 지녔다."], ["Meg liked his quiet manners, and considered him a walking encyclopædia of useful knowledge.", "메그는 그의 차분한 태도가 마음에 들었고, 그를 유익한 지식이 가득한 걸어다니는 백과사전이라 여겼다."], ["He never talked to her much; but he looked at her a good deal, and she felt sure that he did not regard her with aversion.", "그는 그녀에게 말을 많이 거는 법이 없었지만, 그녀를 꽤 자주 바라보았고, 메그는 그가 자기를 싫어하는 눈빛으로 보지는 않는다고 확신했다."], ["Ned, being in college, of course put on all the airs which Freshmen think it their bounden duty to assume; he was not very wise, but very good-natured, and altogether an excellent person to carry on a picnic.", "네드는 대학생인지라, 물론 신입생이라면 으레 떠맡아야 할 의무로 여기는 온갖 거드름을 다 피웠다. 그는 그리 똑똑하지는 않았지만 매우 서글서글했고, 두루 보아 소풍을 이끌어 가기에 더없이 좋은 사람이었다."], ["Sallie Gardiner was absorbed in keeping her white piqué dress clean, and chattering with the ubiquitous Fred, who kept Beth in constant terror by his pranks.", "샐리 가드너는 흰 피케 원피스를 깨끗이 간수하는 데 골몰하면서, 어디에나 끼어드는 프레드와 수다를 떨었는데, 프레드는 그 장난질로 베스를 줄곧 공포에 떨게 했다."]], [["It was not far to Longmeadow; but the tent was pitched and the wickets down by the time they arrived.", "롱메도까지는 멀지 않았지만, 그들이 도착했을 때는 이미 천막이 쳐지고 크로케 골문이 세워져 있었다."], ["A pleasant green field, with three wide-spreading oaks in the middle, and a smooth strip of turf for croquet.", "가운데에 넓게 가지를 드리운 떡갈나무 세 그루가 선, 기분 좋은 푸른 들판이었고, 크로케를 할 매끈한 잔디밭이 펼쳐져 있었다."]], [["\"Welcome to Camp Laurence!\"", "\"로런스 캠프에 온 걸 환영해!\""], ["said the young host, as they landed, with exclamations of delight.", "그들이 환호성을 지르며 배에서 내리자, 젊은 주인이 말했다."]], [["\"Brooke is commander-in-chief; I am commissary-general; the other fellows are staff-officers; and you, ladies, are company.", "\"브룩 선생님이 총사령관이고, 난 병참감, 나머지 녀석들은 참모 장교, 그리고 숙녀 여러분, 당신들은 부대원입니다."], ["The tent is for your especial benefit, and that oak is your drawing-room; this is the mess-room, and the third is the camp-kitchen.", "천막은 특별히 여러분을 위한 거고, 저 떡갈나무는 여러분의 응접실, 이쪽은 식당, 그리고 세 번째 나무는 야영지 부엌입니다."], ["Now, let's have a game before it gets hot, and then we'll see about dinner.\"", "자, 더워지기 전에 한 게임 하고, 그다음에 저녁을 어떻게 할지 봅시다.\""]], [["Frank, Beth, Amy, and Grace sat down to watch the game played by the other eight.", "프랭크와 베스, 에이미, 그레이스는 앉아서 나머지 여덟 명이 하는 경기를 구경했다."], ["Mr. Brooke chose Meg, Kate, and Fred; Laurie took Sallie, Jo, and Ned.", "브룩 씨는 메그와 케이트, 프레드를 골랐고, 로리는 샐리와 조, 네드를 데려갔다."], ["The Englishers played well; but the Americans played better, and contested every inch of the ground as strongly as if the spirit of '76 inspired them.", "영국 아이들은 잘했지만, 미국 아이들은\n더 잘했다. 마치 76년의 정신(미국 독립 정신을 가리킴 : 옮긴이)이 들린 사람들처럼 한 치의 땅도 끝까지 다투었다."], ["Jo and Fred had several skirmishes, and once narrowly escaped high words.", "조와 프레드는 몇 차례 가벼운 충돌을 빚었고, 한 번은 험한 말이 오갈 뻔한 것을 간신히 피했다."], ["Jo was through the last wicket, and had missed the stroke, which failure ruffled her a good deal.", "조는 마지막 위켓을 통과했다가 타구를 놓쳤는데, 그 실수에 적잖이 부아가 치밀었다."], ["Fred was close behind her, and his turn came before hers; he gave a stroke, his ball hit the wicket, and stopped an inch on the wrong side.", "프레드는 바로 그녀 뒤에 있었고, 차례가 그녀보다 먼저 돌아왔다. 그가 공을 쳤더니 공이 위켓에 맞고 엉뚱한 쪽으로 한 치쯤 빗나가 멈췄다."], ["No one was very near; and running up to examine, he gave it a sly nudge with his toe, which put it just an inch on the right side.", "가까이에 아무도 없자, 그는 살피러 달려가는 척하며 발끝으로 슬그머니 공을 툭 밀어 정확히 한 치 옳은 쪽으로 옮겨 놓았다."]], [["\"I'm through!", "\"통과!"], ["Now, Miss Jo, I'll settle you, and get in first,\"", "자, 조 양, 내가 당신을 처리하고 먼저 들어가겠어.\""], ["cried the young gentleman, swinging his mallet for another blow.", "젊은 신사는 다시 한 번 치려고 나무망치를 휘두르며 외쳤다."]], [["\"You pushed it; I saw you; it's my turn now,\"", "\"당신이 공을 밀었잖아. 내가 봤어."], ["said Jo sharply.", "이제 내 차례야.\" 조가 날카롭게 말했다."]], [["\"Upon my word, I didn't move it; it rolled a bit, perhaps, but that is allowed; so stand off, please, and let me have a go at the stake.\"", "\"맹세컨대 난 건드리지 않았어. 약간 굴러갔을지는 몰라도 그건 허용되는 거야. 그러니 비켜 줘, 말뚝을 한 번 칠 테니.\""]], [["\"We don't cheat in America, but you can, if you choose,\"", "\"미국에서는 속임수를 쓰지 않아. 당신이 원하면 쓰겠지만.\""], ["said Jo angrily.", "조가 화를 내며 말했다."]], [["\"Yankees are a deal the most tricky, everybody knows.", "\"양키들이야말로 제일 잔꾀를 잘 부린다는 건 누구나 다 아는 사실이지."], ["There you go!\"", "자, 받아라!\""], ["returned Fred, croqueting her ball far away.", "프레드는 이렇게 받아치며 그녀의 공을 저 멀리 쳐 날려 버렸다."]], [["Jo opened her lips to say something rude, but checked herself in time, colored up to her forehead, and stood a minute, hammering down a wicket with all her might, while Fred hit the stake, and declared himself out with much exultation.", "조는 무슨 무례한 말을 하려고 입을 열었지만, 때맞춰 자신을 다잡고 이마까지 새빨개진 채로 잠시 서서 위켓 하나를 있는 힘껏 땅에 박았다. 그러는 동안 프레드는 말뚝을 맞히고 의기양양하게 자기가 끝났다고 선언했다."], ["She went off to get her ball, and was a long time finding it, among the bushes; but she came back, looking cool and quiet, and waited her turn patiently.", "그녀는 공을 가지러 갔고, 덤불 사이에서 한참을 찾았다. 그러나 돌아왔을 때는 차분하고 침착한 얼굴이었고, 참을성 있게 자기 차례를 기다렸다."], ["It took several strokes to regain the place she had lost; and, when she got there, the other side had nearly won, for Kate's ball was the last but one, and lay near the stake.", "잃어버린 자리를 되찾는 데 몇 번의 타구가 필요했다. 거기에 이르렀을 때는 상대편이 거의 이긴 상태였으니, 케이트의 공이 마지막에서 두 번째로 말뚝 가까이에 놓여 있었기 때문이다."]], [["\"By George, it's all up with us!", "\"이런, 우린 다 틀렸어!"], ["Good-by, Kate.", "잘 가, 케이트."], ["Miss Jo owes me one, so you are finished,\"", "조 양이 나한테 한 방 빚졌으니, 넌 끝난 거야.\""], ["cried Fred excitedly, as they all drew near to see the finish.", "모두가 결말을 보려고 가까이 다가서자 프레드가 들뜬 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["\"Yankees have a trick of being generous to their enemies,\"", "\"양키들은 적에게 너그러운 버릇이 있지.\""], ["said Jo, with a look that made the lad redden, \"especially when they beat them,\"", "조가 소년의 얼굴을 붉히게 만드는 눈빛으로 말했다. \"특히 그들을 이겼을 때는.\""], ["she added, as, leaving Kate's ball untouched, she won the game by a clever stroke.", "그녀는 이렇게 덧붙이며 케이트의 공은 건드리지 않은 채 멋진 한 타로 게임을 이겼다."]], [["Laurie threw up his hat; then remembered that it wouldn't do to exult over the defeat of his guests, and stopped in the middle of a cheer to whisper to his friend,--", "로리는 모자를 공중으로 던졌다. 그러다 손님들이 진 것을 두고 우쭐대서는 안 된다는 데 생각이 미쳐, 환호하다 말고 멈추어 친구에게 속삭였다."]], [["\"Good for you, Jo! He did cheat, I saw him; we can't tell him so, but he won't do it again, take my word for it.\"", "\"잘했어, 조! 그 녀석이 속임수를 쓴 거 맞아. 내가 봤어. 대놓고 말할 순 없지만, 다시는 못 그럴 거야. 내 말 믿어.\""]], [["Meg drew her aside, under pretence of pinning up a loose braid, and said approvingly,--", "메그는 풀린 머리 가닥을 매만져 주는 척 그녀를 옆으로 끌고 가서 칭찬하듯 말했다."]], [["\"It was dreadfully provoking; but you kept your temper, and I'm so glad, Jo.\"", "\"정말 약 오르는 일이었는데, 그래도 화를 참았구나. 정말 다행이야, 조.\""]], [["\"Don't praise me, Meg, for I could box his ears this minute.", "\"칭찬하지 마, 메그. 난 지금 당장이라도 그 녀석 따귀를 갈길 수 있을 것 같으니까."], ["I should certainly have boiled over if I hadn't stayed among the nettles till I got my rage under enough to hold my tongue.", "입을 다물 만큼 화가 가라앉을 때까지 쐐기풀 사이에 있지 않았다면 분명 끓어 넘쳤을 거야."], ["It's simmering now, so I hope he'll keep out of my way,\"", "지금도 부글부글 끓고 있으니, 그 녀석이 내 앞에 얼씬거리지 않으면 좋겠어.\""], ["returned Jo, biting her lips, as she glowered at Fred from under her big hat.", "조는 커다란 모자 아래로 프레드를 노려보며 입술을 깨물고 대꾸했다."]], [["\"Time for lunch,\" said Mr.", "\"점심 먹을 시간이군요.\""], ["Brooke, looking at his watch.", "브룩 씨가 회중시계를 보며 말했다."], ["\"Commissary-general, will you make the fire and get water, while Miss March, Miss Sallie, and I spread the table?", "\"병참 장교 양반, 메그 양과 샐리 양과 내가 식탁을 차리는 동안 불을 피우고 물을 길어 오겠소?"], ["Who can make good coffee?\"", "커피는 누가 잘 끓이지요?\""]], [["\"Jo can,\" said Meg, glad to recommend her sister.", "\"조가 잘 끓여요.\""], ["So Jo, feeling that her late lessons in cookery were to do her honor, went to preside over the coffee-pot, while the children collected dry sticks, and the boys made a fire, and got water from a spring near by.", "메그가 동생을 추천하게 되어 기뻐하며 말했다. 그래서 조는 최근에 배운 요리 솜씨가 빛을 발하게 되었다는 생각에 커피 주전자를 맡으러 갔고, 그동안 아이들은 마른 나뭇가지를 모았으며 소년들은 불을 피우고 근처 샘에서 물을 길어 왔다."], ["Miss Kate sketched, and Frank talked to Beth, who was making little mats of braided rushes to serve as plates.", "케이트 양은 그림을 그렸고, 프랭크는 골풀을 엮어 접시 대신 쓸 작은 깔개를 만들고 있던 베스에게 말을 걸었다."]], [["The commander-in-chief and his aids soon spread the table-cloth with an inviting array of eatables and drinkables, prettily decorated with green leaves.", "총사령관과 부관들은 곧 식탁보를 펴고 그 위에 먹을거리와 마실 거리를 먹음직스럽게 차려 놓은 다음 푸른 잎사귀로 예쁘게 장식했다."], ["Jo announced that the coffee was ready, and every one settled themselves to a hearty meal; for youth is seldom dyspeptic, and exercise develops wholesome appetites.", "조가 커피가 다 됐다고 알리자, 모두가 자리를 잡고 푸짐한 식사에 들어갔다. 젊은이들에게는 좀처럼 소화 불량이 없는 데다, 운동은 건강한 식욕을 돋우는 법이니까."], ["A very merry lunch it was; for everything seemed fresh and funny, and frequent peals of laughter startled a venerable horse who fed near by.", "무척 유쾌한 점심이었다. 모든 것이 신선하고 우스워 보였고, 잦은 웃음소리가 근처에서 풀을 뜯던 늙은 말을 놀라게 했다."], ["There was a pleasing inequality in the table, which produced many mishaps to cups and plates; acorns dropped into the milk, little black ants partook of the refreshments without being invited, and fuzzy caterpillars swung down from the tree, to see what was going on.", "식탁이 기분 좋게 울퉁불퉁한 탓에 컵과 접시에 갖은 사고가 일어났다. 도토리가 우유 속으로 떨어졌고, 초대받지 않은 작고 까만 개미들이 음식을 나누어 먹었으며, 보송보송한 애벌레들이 무슨 일인가 구경하려고 나무에서 대롱대롱 내려왔다."], ["Three white-headed children peeped over the fence, and an objectionable dog barked at them from the other side of the river with all his might and main.", "머리가 하얀 아이 셋이 울타리 너머로 빼꼼 들여다보았고, 강 건너편에서는 못마땅한 개 한 마리가 죽을힘을 다해 그들을 향해 짖어 댔다."]], [["\"There's salt here, if you prefer it,\"", "\"소금이 여기 있어, 그게 더 좋다면.\""], ["said Laurie, as he handed Jo a saucer of berries.", "로리가 조에게 산딸기 한 접시를 건네며 말했다."]], [["\"Thank you, I prefer spiders,\"", "\"고마워, 난 거미가 더 좋은데.\""], ["she replied, fishing up two unwary little ones who had gone to a creamy death.", "그녀는 크림 같은 죽음을 맞으러 들어간 부주의한 작은 거미 두 마리를 건져 올리며 대꾸했다."], ["\"How dare you remind me of that horrid dinner-party, when yours is so nice in every way?\"", "\"당신네 식사는 어느 모로 보나 이렇게 멋진데, 어떻게 그 끔찍한 만찬 파티를 떠올리게 하는 거야?\""], ["added Jo, as they both laughed, and ate out of one plate, the china having run short.", "조는 이렇게 덧붙였고, 두 사람은 함께 웃으며 한 접시에서 먹었다. 도자기 그릇이 모자랐기 때문이다."]], [["\"I had an uncommonly good time that day, and haven't got over it yet.", "\"그날 난 정말 좀처럼 없을 만큼 즐거웠고, 아직도 그 기분에서 못 벗어났어. 알다시피 그건 내 공이 아니야."], ["This is no credit to me, you know; I don't do anything; it's you and Meg and Brooke who make it go, and I'm no end obliged to you.", "난 아무것도 안 했거든. 잘 돌아가게 만든 건 너랑 메그랑 브룩 선생님이지."], ["What shall we do when we can't eat any more?\"", "정말 너희한테 고마워. 더는 먹을 수 없게 되면 우린 뭘 하지?\""], ["asked Laurie, feeling that his trump card had been played when lunch was over.", "점심이 끝나면 자신의 으뜸패를 다 썼다는 기분이 들어 로리가 물었다."]], [["\"Have games, till it's cooler.", "\"좀 시원해질 때까지 놀이를 하자."], ["I brought 'Authors,'", "내가 '작가들'"], ["and I dare say Miss Kate knows something new and nice.", "카드를 가져왔고, 케이트 양도 분명히 뭔가 새롭고 재미있는 걸 알 거야. 가서 물어봐."], ["Go and ask her; she's company, and you ought to stay with her more.\"", "손님이잖아. 너도 그분이랑 더 어울려야 해.\""]], [["\"Aren't you company too?", "\"너도 손님 아니야?"], ["I thought she'd suit Brooke; but he keeps talking to Meg, and Kate just stares at them through that ridiculous glass of hers.", "케이트가 브룩 선생님이랑 잘 맞을 줄 알았는데, 선생님은 줄곧 메그랑만 이야기하고, 케이트는 그 우스꽝스러운 안경 너머로 둘을 빤히 쳐다보기만 하잖아."], ["I'm going, so you needn't try to preach propriety, for you can't do it, Jo.\"", "나 갈게, 그러니 예의범절 설교 따위는 하지 마. 넌 그런 거 못 하니까, 조.\""]], [["Miss Kate did know several new games; and as the girls would not, and the boys could not, eat any more, they all adjourned to the drawing-room to play \"Rigmarole.\"", "케이트 양은 정말 새로운 놀이를 여럿 알고 있었다. 소녀들은 더 먹으려 들지 않았고 소년들은 더 먹을 수가 없었으므로, 모두 응접실로 자리를 옮겨 '횡설수설'을 하기로 했다."]], [["\"One person begins a story, any nonsense you like, and tells as long as he pleases, only taking care to stop short at some exciting point, when the next takes it up and does the same.", "\"한 사람이 이야기를 시작하는데, 아무 엉터리 같은 내용이라도 좋아요. 원하는 만큼 길게 풀어 나가다가 흥미진진한 대목에서 딱 멈추면 다음 사람이 이어받아 똑같이 하는 거예요."], ["It's very funny when well done, and makes a perfect jumble of tragical comical stuff to laugh over.", "잘만 하면 무척 재미있어서, 비극과 희극이 완전히 뒤죽박죽 섞인 우스운 이야기가 만들어지지요."], ["Please start it, Mr. Brooke,\"", "시작해 주세요, 브룩 씨.\""], ["said Kate, with a commanding air, which surprised Meg, who treated the tutor with as much respect as any other gentleman.", "케이트가 분부하는 듯한 태도로 말하자, 가정 교사를 다른 어느 신사 못지않게 깍듯이 대하던 메그는 깜짝 놀랐다."]], [["Lying on the grass at the feet of the two young ladies, Mr.", "두 젊은 숙녀의 발치 풀밭에 누운 브룩 씨는 순순히 이야기를 시작했다."], ["Brooke obediently began the story, with the handsome brown eyes steadily fixed upon the sunshiny river.", "잘생긴 갈색 눈은 햇살 반짝이는 강물에 줄곧 고정한 채로."]], [["\"Once on a time, a knight went out into the world to seek his fortune, for he had nothing but his sword and his shield.", "\"옛날 옛적에, 한 기사가 운명을 찾아 세상으로 나섰습니다. 가진 것이라곤 칼과 방패뿐이었으니까요."], ["He travelled a long while, nearly eight-and-twenty years, and had a hard time of it, till he came to the palace of a good old king, who had offered a reward to any one who would tame and train a fine but unbroken colt, of which he was very fond.", "그는 오랫동안, 거의 스물여덟 해를 떠돌며 갖은 고생을 하다가 마침내 어느 인자한 노왕의 궁전에 이르렀습니다. 왕은 자신이 무척 아끼는, 훌륭하지만 길들지 않은 망아지 한 마리를 길들여 훈련시키는 자에게 상을 내리겠노라 약속한 터였지요."], ["The knight agreed to try, and got on slowly but surely; for the colt was a gallant fellow, and soon learned to love his new master, though he was freakish and wild.", "기사는 해 보겠다고 했고, 더디지만 착실하게 일을 진척시켰습니다. 망아지는 기개가 넘치는 녀석이라, 변덕스럽고 사납기는 해도 곧 새 주인을 사랑하게 되었거든요."], ["Every day, when he gave his lessons to this pet of the king's, the knight rode him through the city; and, as he rode, he looked everywhere for a certain beautiful face, which he had seen many times in his dreams, but never found.", "날마다 왕이 아끼는 이 녀석을 가르칠 때면 기사는 그 말을 타고 시내를 누볐는데, 말을 달리면서 어떤 아름다운 얼굴을 찾아 사방을 살폈습니다. 꿈에서 여러 번 보았으나 한 번도 찾지 못한 얼굴이었지요."], ["One day, as he went prancing down a quiet street, he saw at the window of a ruinous castle the lovely face.", "그러던 어느 날, 그가 한적한 거리를 의기양양하게 지나가는데, 다 허물어져 가는 성의 창문에 그 사랑스러운 얼굴이 보이는 게 아니겠습니까."], ["He was delighted, inquired who lived in this old castle, and was told that several captive princesses were kept there by a spell, and spun all day to lay up money to buy their liberty.", "그는 뛸 듯이 기뻐하며 이 낡은 성에 누가 사는지 물었고, 마법에 걸린 갇힌 공주 여럿이 그곳에 갇혀, 자유를 살 돈을 모으려고 온종일 실을 잣는다는 이야기를 들었습니다."], ["The knight wished intensely that he could free them; but he was poor, and could only go by each day, watching for the sweet face, and longing to see it out in the sunshine.", "기사는 그들을 풀어 주고 싶은 마음이 간절했지만, 가난한 처지라 날마다 그 앞을 지나며 그 고운 얼굴을 살피고, 그 얼굴이 햇살 속으로 나오기를 갈망하는 것밖에 할 수 없었지요."], ["At last, he resolved to get into the castle and ask how he could help them.", "마침내 그는 성안으로 들어가 어떻게 그들을 도울 수 있을지 물어보기로 결심했습니다."], ["He went and knocked; the great door flew open, and he beheld--\"", "그가 가서 문을 두드리자, 커다란 문이 활짝 열렸고, 그의 눈앞에 나타난 것은—\""]], [["\"A ravishingly lovely lady, who exclaimed, with a cry of rapture, 'At last!", "\"황홀하리만치 아름다운 귀부인이었으니, 그녀는 환희에 찬 목소리로 외쳤어요. '드디어!"], ["at last!'\"", "드디어!'\""], ["continued Kate, who had read French novels, and admired the style.", "케이트가 이야기를 이어받았다. 프랑스 소설을 읽어 그 문체를 흠모하던 터였다."], ["\"''Tis she!'", "\"'바로 그녀로구나!'"], ["cried Count Gustave, and fell at her feet in an ecstasy of joy.", "구스타브 백작은 이렇게 외치며 기쁨에 겨워 그녀의 발치에 쓰러졌습니다."], ["'Oh, rise!'", "'오, 일어나세요!'"], ["she said, extending a hand of marble fairness.", "그녀는 대리석처럼 흰 손을 내밀며 말했지요. '안 됩니다!"], ["'Never! till you tell me how I may rescue you,'", "어떻게 하면 당신을 구할 수 있는지 말씀해 주시기 전에는!'"], ["swore the knight, still kneeling.", "기사는 여전히 무릎을 꿇은 채 맹세했어요."], ["'Alas, my cruel fate condemns me to remain here till my tyrant is destroyed.'", "'아아, 잔인한 운명이 저를 이곳에 묶어 두는군요, 저를 핍박하는 자가 사라질 때까지는.'"], ["'Where is the villain?'", "'그 악당은 어디 있습니까?'"], ["'In the mauve salon.", "'연보랏빛 응접실에요."], ["Go, brave heart, and save me from despair.'", "가세요, 용감한 분이여, 저를 절망에서 구해 주세요.'"], ["'I obey, and return victorious or dead!'", "'분부 받들겠습니다. 승리하지 못하면 주검이 되어 돌아오겠소!'"], ["With these thrilling words he rushed away, and flinging open the door of the mauve salon, was about to enter, when he received--\"", "이 가슴 떨리는 말을 남기고 그는 달려갔고, 연보랏빛 응접실 문을 벌컥 열어젖히며 막 들어서려는 순간, 그가 맞은 것은—\""]], [["\"A stunning blow from the big Greek lexicon, which an old fellow in a black gown fired at him,\"", "\"검은 가운을 입은 늙은이가 던진 커다란 그리스어 사전에 호되게 한 방 맞은 거였죠.\""], ["said Ned.", "네드가 말했다."], ["\"Instantly Sir What's-his-name recovered himself, pitched the tyrant out of the window, and turned to join the lady, victorious, but with a bump on his brow; found the door locked, tore up the curtains, made a rope ladder, got half-way down when the ladder broke, and he went head first into the moat, sixty feet below.", "\"곧바로 정신을 차린 아무개 경은 그 폭군을 창밖으로 내던지고, 승리한 몸으로 숙녀에게 가려고 돌아섰지만, 이마에 혹이 하나 났답니다. 문이 잠긴 걸 발견하자 커튼을 찢어 밧줄 사다리를 만들고는 절반쯤 내려갔는데, 사다리가 끊어지는 바람에 머리부터 곤두박질쳐 18미터 아래 해자로 떨어졌어요."], ["Could swim like a duck, paddled round the castle till he came to a little door guarded by two stout fellows; knocked their heads together till they cracked like a couple of nuts, then, by a trifling exertion of his prodigious strength, he smashed in the door, went up a pair of stone steps covered with dust a foot thick, toads as big as your fist, and spiders that would frighten you into hysterics, Miss March.", "오리처럼 헤엄칠 줄 알았던 그는 성 둘레를 첨벙첨벙 헤엄쳐 다니다가 건장한 사내 둘이 지키는 작은 문에 다다랐고, 두 놈의 머리를 마주 부딪쳐 호두 두 알처럼 쪼개 버렸어요. 그러고는 어마어마한 힘을 살짝만 써서 문을 부수고 들어가, 먼지가 30센티미터나 쌓인 돌계단을 올랐는데, 거기엔 주먹만 한 두꺼비들이며 보기만 해도 발작을 일으킬 만한 거미들이 우글거렸답니다, 메그 양."], ["At the top of these steps he came plump upon a sight that took his breath away and chilled his blood--\"", "그 계단 꼭대기에서 그는 숨이 턱 막히고 피가 얼어붙는 광경과 정면으로 맞닥뜨렸으니—\""]], [["\"A tall figure, all in white with a veil over its face and a lamp in its wasted hand,\" went on Meg.", "\"키 큰 형체가 온통 흰옷을 두르고, 얼굴에는 베일을 드리우고, 앙상한 손에는 등불을 들고 있었어.\""], ["\"It beckoned, gliding noiselessly before him down a corridor as dark and cold as any tomb.", "메그가 이야기를 이어 갔다. \"그것은 손짓하며 소리 없이 미끄러지듯 그의 앞에서 무덤처럼 어둡고 차가운 복도를 따라 나아갔지."], ["Shadowy effigies in armor stood on either side, a dead silence reigned, the lamp burned blue, and the ghostly figure ever and anon turned its face toward him, showing the glitter of awful eyes through its white veil.", "양옆에는 갑옷을 두른 그림자 같은 조각상들이 서 있었고, 죽은 듯한 정적이 감돌았으며, 등불은 푸르게 타올랐어. 유령 같은 형체는 이따금 그를 향해 고개를 돌렸고, 그때마다 흰 베일 너머로 무시무시한 두 눈이 번뜩였지."], ["They reached a curtained door, behind which sounded lovely music; he sprang forward to enter, but the spectre plucked him back, and waved threateningly before him a--\"", "그들은 커튼이 드리운 문에 이르렀는데, 그 뒤에서 아름다운 음악 소리가 들려왔어. 그가 들어가려고 앞으로 뛰어들었지만, 망령이 그를 홱 잡아당기더니 그의 눈앞에서 위협하듯 흔들어 댄 것은—\""]], [["\"Snuff-box,\"", "\"코담뱃갑이었어.\""], ["said Jo, in a sepulchral tone, which convulsed the audience.", "조가 무덤 속에서 울리는 듯한 목소리로 말하자, 듣던 사람들이 자지러졌다."], ["\"'Thankee,'", "\"'고맙소.'"], ["said the knight politely, as he took a pinch, and sneezed seven times so violently that his head fell off.", "기사는 정중히 말하며 담배를 한 줌 집어 들었고, 어찌나 세게 일곱 번이나 재채기를 했는지 머리가 뚝 떨어져 버렸어. '하!"], ["'Ha!", "하!'"], ["ha!'", "유령이 웃었지."], ["laughed the ghost; and having peeped through the key-hole at the princesses spinning away for dear life, the evil spirit picked up her victim and put him in a large tin box, where there were eleven other knights packed together without their heads, like sardines, who all rose and began to--\"", "그러고는 열쇠 구멍으로 죽을힘을 다해 실을 잣는 공주들을 들여다본 다음, 그 악령은 제 희생자를 집어 들어 커다란 양철 상자에 넣었는데, 그 안에는 머리 없는 다른 기사가 열한 명이나 정어리처럼 차곡차곡 들어 있었어. 그들은 모두 일어나서—\""]], [["\"Dance a hornpipe,\"", "\"호른파이프 춤을 추기 시작했지.\""], ["cut in Fred, as Jo paused for breath; \"and, as they danced, the rubbishy old castle turned to a man-of-war in full sail.", "조가 숨을 돌리려 멈추자 프레드가 끼어들었다. \"그들이 춤을 추는 동안, 그 다 쓰러져 가는 낡은 성은 돛을 활짝 편 군함으로 변했어."], ["'Up with the jib, reef the tops'l halliards, helm hard a lee, and man the guns!'", "'삼각돛을 올려라, 윗돛 활대를 줄여라, 키를 바람 아래로 바짝 돌리고, 대포 앞에 붙어라!'"], ["roared the captain, as a Portuguese pirate hove in sight, with a flag black as ink flying from her foremast.", "선장이 고함쳤지. 포르투갈 해적선이 시야에 들어왔는데, 앞돛대에 잉크처럼 새까만 깃발을 휘날리고 있었거든."], ["'Go in and win, my hearties!'", "'돌격해서 이겨라, 용맹한 친구들아!'"], ["says the captain; and a tremendous fight begun.", "선장이 말했고, 어마어마한 전투가 시작됐어."], ["Of course the British beat; they always do.\"", "물론 영국이 이겼지. 영국은 늘 이기니까.\""]], [["\"No, they don't!\"", "\"아니, 안 이겨!\""], ["cried Jo, aside.", "조가 옆에서 외쳤다."]], [["\"Having taken the pirate captain prisoner, sailed slap over the schooner, whose decks were piled with dead, and whose lee-scuppers ran blood, for the order had been 'Cutlasses, and die hard!'", "\"해적 선장을 포로로 잡은 다음, 단숨에 그 스쿠너선을 들이받았는데, 그 갑판에는 시체가 산더미처럼 쌓이고 배수구로는 피가 흘렀어. '단검을 들고 끝까지 싸워라!'는 명령이 떨어졌었거든."], ["'Bosen's mate, take a bight of the flying-jib sheet, and start this villain if he don't confess his sins double quick,'", "'갑판장 조수, 삼각돛 밧줄을 한 가닥 잡아채서 이 악당이 당장 죄를 자백하지 않거든 따끔하게 손봐 줘라.'"], ["said the British captain.", "영국 선장이 말했지."], ["The Portuguese held his tongue like a brick, and walked the plank, while the jolly tars cheered like mad.", "포르투갈 놈은 벽돌처럼 입을 꾹 다물고 널빤지 위를 걸어 바다로 떨어졌고, 흥겨운 선원들은 미친 듯이 환호했어."], ["But the sly dog dived, came up under the man-of-war, scuttled her, and down she went, with all sail set, 'To the bottom of the sea, sea, sea,'", "그런데 그 교활한 놈이 물속으로 잠수해서는 군함 밑으로 올라와 배 밑창에 구멍을 뚫었고, 배는 돛을 활짝 편 채로 '바다 밑바닥으로, 밑바닥으로, 밑바닥으로'"], ["where--\"", "가라앉았는데, 거기에서—\""]], [["\"Oh, gracious!", "\"어머, 세상에!"], ["what _shall_ I say?\"", "난 대체 뭐라고 해야 해?\""], ["cried Sallie, as Fred ended his rigmarole, in which he had jumbled together, pell-mell, nautical phrases and facts, out of one of his favorite books.", "프레드가 횡설수설을 끝내자 샐리가 외쳤다. 그는 자기가 좋아하는 책에서 따온 항해 용어와 사실들을 마구잡이로 뒤섞어 놓은 참이었다."], ["\"Well they went to the bottom, and a nice mermaid welcomed them, but was much grieved on finding the box of headless knights, and kindly pickled them in brine, hoping to discover the mystery about them; for, being a woman, she was curious.", "\"어, 그래서 그들은 바다 밑바닥으로 가라앉았고, 어여쁜 인어가 그들을 맞이했어. 그런데 머리 없는 기사들이 든 상자를 발견하고는 무척 슬퍼하면서, 그들에 얽힌 수수께끼를 밝혀 보려고 친절하게 소금물에 절여 두었지."], ["By and by a diver came down, and the mermaid said, 'I'll give you this box of pearls if you can take it up;'", "인어도 여자인지라 호기심이 많았거든. 이윽고 잠수부 하나가 내려오자 인어가 말했어."], ["for she wanted to restore the poor things to life, and couldn't raise the heavy load herself.", "'이 진주 상자를 끌어 올려 주면 당신께 드릴게요.' 인어는 그 가엾은 이들을 되살리고 싶었지만 그 무거운 짐을 혼자서는 들어 올릴 수 없었거든."], ["So the diver hoisted it up, and was much disappointed, on opening it, to find no pearls.", "그래서 잠수부가 상자를 끌어 올렸는데, 열어 보니 진주는 없어서 무척 실망했지."], ["He left it in a great lonely field, where it was found by a--\"", "그는 상자를 외딴 넓은 들판에 버려두었고, 그것을 발견한 건—\""]], [["\"Little goose-girl, who kept a hundred fat geese in the field,\"", "\"들판에서 살진 거위 백 마리를 치던 어린 거위지기 소녀였어.\""], ["said Amy, when Sallie's invention gave out.", "샐리의 상상력이 바닥나자 에이미가 말했다."], ["\"The little girl was sorry for them, and asked an old woman what she should do to help them.", "\"그 어린 소녀는 그들이 가여워서 늙은 할멈에게 어떻게 하면 도울 수 있느냐고 물었어."], ["'Your geese will tell you, they know everything,'", "'네 거위들이 일러 줄 게다, 그것들은 뭐든 다 아니까.'"], ["said the old woman.", "할멈이 말했지."], ["So she asked what she should use for new heads, since the old ones were lost, and all the geese opened their hundred mouths and screamed--\"", "그래서 소녀는 옛 머리들은 잃어버렸으니 새 머리로 무얼 쓰면 되겠느냐고 물었고, 거위 백 마리가 일제히 백 개의 입을 벌리고 꽥꽥 외쳤어—\""]], [["\"'Cabbages!'\"", "\"'양배추!'\""], ["continued Laurie promptly.", "로리가 냉큼 이어받았다."], ["\"'Just the thing,'", "\"'그거 딱 좋겠다.'"], ["said the girl, and ran to get twelve fine ones from her garden.", "소녀가 말하며 텃밭으로 달려가 좋은 놈으로 열두 개를 따 왔어."], ["She put them on, the knights revived at once, thanked her, and went on their way rejoicing, never knowing the difference, for there were so many other heads like them in the world that no one thought anything of it.", "그걸 얹어 주자 기사들은 곧바로 되살아나 소녀에게 고맙다고 인사하고는 기뻐하며 길을 떠났는데, 자기들이 달라진 줄은 전혀 몰랐지. 세상에는 그들과 똑같은 머리가 하도 많아서 아무도 대수롭게 여기지 않았거든."], ["The knight in whom I'm interested went back to find the pretty face, and learned that the princesses had spun themselves free, and all gone to be married, but one.", "내가 관심 있는 그 기사는 그 고운 얼굴을 찾으러 돌아갔다가, 공주들이 실을 자아 스스로 자유로워졌고 한 사람만 빼고 다들 시집가 버렸다는 걸 알게 됐어."], ["He was in a great state of mind at that; and mounting the colt, who stood by him through thick and thin, rushed to the castle to see which was left.", "그는 그 사실에 마음이 무척 어지러워졌지. 그래서 험한 일 궂은일 마다 않고 곁을 지켜 준 그 망아지에 올라타고, 누가 남았는지 보려고 성으로 달려갔어."], ["Peeping over the hedge, he saw the queen of his affections picking flowers in her garden.", "울타리 너머로 빼꼼 들여다보니, 마음속 여왕이 정원에서 꽃을 따고 있는 게 보였어."], ["'Will you give me a rose?' said he.", "'제게 장미 한 송이를 주시겠습니까?'"], ["'You must come and get it.", "그가 말했지. '와서 가져가셔야 해요."], ["I can't come to you; it isn't proper,' said she, as sweet as honey.", "제가 당신께 갈 수는 없답니다, 그건 예의가 아니니까요.'"], ["He tried to climb over the hedge, but it seemed to grow higher and higher; then he tried to push through, but it grew thicker and thicker, and he was in despair.", "그녀가 꿀처럼 달콤하게 말했어. 그는 울타리를 넘어가려 했지만 울타리는 점점 더 높아지는 듯했고, 헤치고 들어가려 했더니 점점 더 빽빽해져서 그는 절망에 빠졌어."], ["So he patiently broke twig after twig, till he had made a little hole, through which he peeped, saying imploringly, 'Let me in!", "그래서 그는 참을성 있게 잔가지를 하나하나 꺾어 마침내 작은 구멍을 냈고, 그 사이로 들여다보며 애원하듯 말했지. '들여보내 주세요!"], ["let me in!'", "들여보내 주세요!'"], ["But the pretty princess did not seem to understand, for she picked her roses quietly, and left him to fight his way in.", "그런데 그 어여쁜 공주는 알아듣지 못하는 모양이었어. 잠자코 장미를 따면서 그가 알아서 길을 뚫고 들어오게 내버려 두었거든."], ["Whether he did or not, Frank will tell you.\"", "그가 들어갔는지 못 들어갔는지는 프랭크가 말해 줄 거야.\""]], [["\"I can't; I'm not playing, I never do,\"", "\"난 못 해. 나 안 하잖아, 한 번도 안 했어.\""], ["said Frank, dismayed at the sentimental predicament out of which he was to rescue the absurd couple.", "프랭크가 말했다. 자기더러 그 우스꽝스러운 한 쌍을 구해 내라며 떠넘긴 그 감상적인 곤경에 당황한 것이다."], ["Beth had disappeared behind Jo, and Grace was asleep.", "베스는 조의 등 뒤로 사라져 버렸고, 그레이스는 잠들어 있었다."]], [["\"So the poor knight is to be left sticking in the hedge, is he?\" asked Mr.", "\"그래서 그 가엾은 기사는 울타리에 끼인 채로 남겨지게 되는 건가?\""], ["Brooke, still watching the river, and playing with the wild rose in his button-hole.", "브룩 씨가 여전히 강물을 바라보며, 단춧구멍에 꽂은 들장미를 만지작거리며 물었다."]], [["\"I guess the princess gave him a posy, and opened the gate, after a while,\"", "\"내 생각엔 공주가 한참 있다가 그에게 꽃다발을 건네주고 문을 열어 줬을 것 같은데요.\""], ["said Laurie, smiling to himself, as he threw acorns at his tutor.", "로리가 혼자 빙그레 웃으며 가정 교사에게 도토리를 던지며 말했다."]], [["\"What a piece of nonsense we have made!", "\"이런 엉터리 이야기를 만들어 냈군요!"], ["With practice we might do something quite clever.", "연습하면 꽤 그럴듯한 걸 만들 수 있겠어요."], ["Do you know 'Truth'?\"", "'진실 게임' 아세요?\""], ["asked Sallie, after they had laughed over their story.", "다들 자기들 이야기를 두고 한바탕 웃은 뒤 샐리가 물었다."]], [["\"I hope so,\"", "\"그랬으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["said Meg soberly.", "메그가 진지하게 말했다."]], [["\"The game, I mean?\"", "\"게임 말이에요.\""]], [["\"What is it?\"", "\"그게 뭔데?\""], ["said Fred.", "프레드가 물었다."]], [["\"Why, you pile up your hands, choose a number, and draw out in turn, and the person who draws at the number has to answer truly any questions put by the rest.", "\"있잖아, 손을 차곡차곡 포갠 다음 숫자를 하나 정하고, 차례대로 손을 빼는 거야. 그 숫자에서 빠지는 사람이 나머지 사람들이 묻는 어떤 질문에든 솔직하게 대답해야 해."], ["It's great fun.\"", "굉장히 재미있어.\""]], [["\"Let's try it,\"", "\"한번 해 보자.\""], ["said Jo, who liked new experiments.", "새로운 시도를 좋아하는 조가 말했다."]], [["Miss Kate and Mr.", "케이트 양과 브룩 씨, 메그, 네드는 빠지겠다고 했지만, 프레드와 샐리, 조, 로리는 손을 포개고 차례로 뺐다."], ["Brooke, Meg, and Ned declined, but Fred, Sallie, Jo, and Laurie piled and drew; and the lot fell to Laurie.", "그리고 제비가 로리에게 떨어졌다."]], [["\"Who are your heroes?\"", "\"네 영웅은 누구야?\""], ["asked Jo.", "조가 물었다."]], [["\"Grandfather and Napoleon.\"", "\"할아버지하고 나폴레옹.\""]], [["\"Which lady here do you think prettiest?\"", "\"여기 있는 숙녀 중에 누가 제일 예쁜 것 같아?\""], ["said Sallie.", "샐리가 말했다."]], [["\"Margaret.\"", "\"마거릿.\""]], [["\"Which do you like best?\"", "\"누가 제일 좋아?\""], ["from Fred.", "프레드가 던졌다."]], [["\"Jo, of course.\"", "\"당연히 조지.\""]], [["\"What silly questions you ask!\"", "\"무슨 그런 시시한 질문을 하니!\""], ["and Jo gave a disdainful shrug as the rest laughed at Laurie's matter-of-fact tone.", "나머지 사람들이 로리의 무덤덤한 말투에 웃음을 터뜨리자 조는 멸시하듯 어깨를 으쓱했다."]], [["\"Try again; Truth isn't a bad game,\"", "\"다시 해 봐. 진실 게임도 나쁘진 않네.\""], ["said Fred.", "프레드가 말했다."]], [["\"It's a very good one for you,\"", "\"너한텐 아주 좋은 게임이지.\""], ["retorted Jo, in a low voice.", "조가 낮은 목소리로 받아쳤다."]], [["Her turn came next.", "다음은 그녀의 차례였다."]], [["\"What is your greatest fault?\"", "\"네 가장 큰 결점이 뭐야?\""], ["asked Fred, by way of testing in her the virtue he lacked himself.", "프레드가 정작 자기에게 없는 미덕을 그녀에게서 시험해 보려는 듯 물었다."]], [["\"A quick temper.\"", "\"불같은 성질.\""]], [["\"What do you most wish for?\"", "\"가장 갖고 싶은 게 뭐야?\""], ["said Laurie.", "로리가 물었다."]], [["\"A pair of boot-lacings,\"", "\"구두끈 한 켤레.\""], ["returned Jo, guessing and defeating his purpose.", "조는 그의 속셈을 알아채고 김을 빼며 대꾸했다."]], [["\"Not a true answer; you must say what you really do want most.\"", "\"솔직한 대답이 아니잖아. 정말로 가장 원하는 걸 말해야지.\""]], [["\"Genius; don't you wish you could give it to me, Laurie?\"", "\"천재성. 너 그거 나한테 줄 수 있으면 좋겠지, 로리?\""], ["and she slyly smiled in his disappointed face.", "그녀는 실망한 그의 얼굴을 보며 짓궂게 웃었다."]], [["\"What virtues do you most admire in a man?\"", "\"남자에게서 가장 우러러보는 미덕은 뭐야?\""], ["asked Sallie.", "샐리가 물었다."]], [["\"Courage and honesty.\"", "\"용기하고 정직함.\""]], [["\"Now my turn,\"", "\"이제 내 차례군.\""], ["said Fred, as his hand came last.", "프레드가 말했다. 그의 손이 마지막으로 남았던 것이다."]], [["\"Let's give it to him,\"", "\"이 녀석한테 한 방 먹이자.\""], ["whispered Laurie to Jo, who nodded, and asked at once,--", "로리가 조에게 속삭였고, 조는 고개를 끄덕이고는 곧바로 물었다."]], [["\"Didn't you cheat at croquet?\"", "\"크로케에서 속임수 쓴 거 아니야?\""]], [["\"Well, yes, a little bit.\"", "\"뭐, 그래, 좀 썼지.\""]], [["\"Good!", "\"좋았어!"], ["Didn't you take your story out of 'The Sea-Lion?'\"", "네 이야기, 『바다사자』에서 베껴 온 거 아니야?\""], ["said Laurie. \"Rather.\"", "로리가 말했다."]], [["\"Don't you think the English nation perfect in every respect?\"", "\"뭐, 그런 셈이지.\" \"영국 민족이 모든 면에서 완벽하다고 생각하지 않니?\""], ["asked Sallie.", "샐리가 물었다."]], [["\"I should be ashamed of myself if I didn't.\"", "\"그렇게 생각하지 않는다면 내 자신이 부끄러울 거야.\""]], [["\"He's a true John Bull. Now, Miss Sallie, you shall have a chance without waiting to draw.", "\"역시 진짜 존 불(전형적인 영국인을 뜻하는 별칭 : 옮긴이)이군."], ["I'll harrow up your feelings first, by asking if you don't think you are something of a flirt,\"", "자, 샐리 양, 이번엔 손을 뽑지 않아도 기회를 드리지. 우선 당신 마음을 들쑤셔 놓겠어."], ["said Laurie, as Jo nodded to Fred, as a sign that peace was declared.", "당신, 자기가 좀 바람기 있다고 생각하지 않나요?\" 로리가 조에게 고개를 끄덕여 화해가 성립됐다는 신호를 보내며 말했다."]], [["\"You impertinent boy!", "\"이 버릇없는 녀석!"], ["of course I'm not,\"", "당연히 아니지.\""], ["exclaimed Sallie, with an air that proved the contrary.", "샐리가 그 반대임을 증명하는 듯한 태도로 외쳤다."]], [["\"What do you hate most?\"", "\"제일 싫은 게 뭐예요?\""], ["asked Fred.", "프레드가 물었다."]], [["\"Spiders and rice-pudding.\"", "\"거미하고 쌀 푸딩.\""]], [["\"What do you like best?\"", "\"제일 좋아하는 건요?\""], ["asked Jo.", "조가 물었다."]], [["\"Dancing and French gloves.\"", "\"춤하고 프랑스제 장갑.\""]], [["\"Well, _I_ think Truth is a very silly play; let's have a sensible game of Authors, to refresh our minds,\"", "\"음, 내 생각엔 진실 게임은 정말 시시한 놀이야. 머리도 식힐 겸 '작가들'이나 점잖게 한판 하자.\""], ["proposed Jo.", "조가 제안했다."]], [["Ned, Frank, and the little girls joined in this, and, while it went on, the three elders sat apart, talking.", "네드와 프랭크와 어린 소녀들이 이 놀이에 끼었고, 놀이가 이어지는 동안 손위 셋은 따로 앉아 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Miss Kate took out her sketch again, and Margaret watched her, while Mr. Brooke lay on the grass, with a book, which he did not read.", "케이트 양은 다시 스케치북을 꺼냈고, 마거릿은 그녀를 지켜보았으며, 브룩 씨는 풀밭에 누워 책을 들고 있었으나 읽지는 않았다."]], [["\"How beautifully you do it!", "\"정말 잘 그리시네요!"], ["I wish I could draw,\"", "저도 그림을 그릴 수 있으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["said Meg, with mingled admiration and regret in her voice.", "메그가 감탄과 아쉬움이 뒤섞인 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"Why don't you learn?", "\"왜 배우지 않으세요?"], ["I should think you had taste and talent for it,\"", "보아하니 당신은 그림에 안목도 재능도 있을 것 같은데요.\""], ["replied Miss Kate graciously.", "케이트 양이 너그러이 대꾸했다."]], [["\"I haven't time.\"", "\"시간이 없어서요.\""]], [["\"Your mamma prefers other accomplishments, I fancy.", "\"어머님이 다른 재능을 더 좋아하시는 모양이군요."], ["So did mine; but I proved to her that I had talent, by taking a few lessons privately, and then she was quite willing I should go on.", "우리 어머니도 그러셨는데, 제가 몰래 몇 차례 수업을 받아서 재능이 있다는 걸 증명해 보였더니 그제야 계속해도 좋다고 흔쾌히 허락하셨답니다. 당신도 가정 교사를 두고 그렇게 해 보면 어때요?\""], ["Can't you do the same with your governess?\"", "\"저한텐 가정 교사가 없어요.\""]], [["\"I have none.\"", "\"아, 깜박했네요."], ["\"I forgot; young ladies in America go to school more than with us.", "미국 아가씨들은 우리보다 학교를 더 많이 다니지요."], ["Very fine schools they are, too, papa says.", "게다가 아주 훌륭한 학교들이라고 아빠가 그러시더군요."], ["You go to a private one, I suppose?\"", "당신도 사립 학교에 다니나 보죠?\""]], [["\"I don't go at all; I am a governess myself.\"", "\"전 학교에 아예 안 다녀요. 제가 바로 가정 교사거든요.\""]], [["\"Oh, indeed!\"", "\"어머, 그러세요!\""], ["said Miss Kate; but she might as well have said, \"Dear me, how dreadful!\"", "케이트 양은 이렇게 말했지만, 그 어조에는 \"저런, 어쩌면 그리 끔찍할까!\""], ["for her tone implied it, and something in her face made Meg color, and wish she had not been so frank.", "하는 뜻이 담겨 있었고, 표정에 어린 무언가에 메그는 얼굴이 붉어지며 그렇게까지 솔직하게 말하지 말걸 하고 후회했다."]], [["Mr.", "브룩 씨가 고개를 들어 얼른 말했다."], ["Brooke looked up, and said quickly, \"Young ladies in America love independence as much as their ancestors did, and are admired and respected for supporting themselves.\"", "\"미국 아가씨들은 자기네 선조들 못지않게 독립을 사랑하지요. 그래서 자기 힘으로 살아가는 모습이 칭송받고 존경받습니다.\""]], [["\"Oh, yes; of course it's very nice and proper in them to do so.", "\"아, 그럼요. 물론 그렇게 하는 건 아주 보기 좋고 마땅한 일이지요."], ["We have many most respectable and worthy young women, who do the same and are employed by the nobility, because, being the daughters of gentlemen, they are both well-bred and accomplished, you know,\"", "우리에게도 그렇게 하는 점잖고 훌륭한 아가씨들이 많은데, 신사의 딸들인 만큼 교양 있고 재주도 뛰어나서 귀족들이 고용한답니다.\" 케이트 양이 짐짓 윗사람 행세하는 어조로 말했다."], ["said Miss Kate, in a patronizing tone, that hurt Meg's pride, and made her work seem not only more distasteful, but degrading.", "그 말은 메그의 자존심에 상처를 주었고, 자기 일이 한층 더 달갑지 않을 뿐 아니라 천하게까지 여겨지게 만들었다."]], [["\"Did the German song suit, Miss March?\" inquired Mr.", "\"그 독일어 노래는 마음에 드셨나요, 메그 양?\""], ["Brooke, breaking an awkward pause.", "브룩 씨가 어색한 침묵을 깨며 물었다."]], [["\"Oh, yes!", "\"아, 네!"], ["it was very sweet, and I'm much obliged to whoever translated it for me;\"", "정말 감미로웠어요. 그걸 번역해 주신 분이 누구든 무척 고맙고요.\""], ["and Meg's downcast face brightened as she spoke.", "그렇게 말하는 메그의 풀 죽었던 얼굴이 환해졌다."]], [["\"Don't you read German?\"", "\"독일어 못 읽으세요?\""], ["asked Miss Kate, with a look of surprise.", "케이트 양이 놀란 표정으로 물었다."]], [["\"Not very well.", "\"잘은 못 읽어요."], ["My father, who taught me, is away, and I don't get on very fast alone, for I've no one to correct my pronunciation.\"", "저를 가르쳐 주시던 아버지가 멀리 계셔서, 혼자서는 잘 늘지 않더라고요. 발음을 바로잡아 줄 사람이 없으니까요.\""]], [["\"Try a little now; here is Schiller's 'Mary Stuart,'", "\"지금 조금 해 보세요."], ["and a tutor who loves to teach,\" and Mr.", "여기 실러의 『마리아 슈투아르트』가 있고, 가르치기 좋아하는 선생도 있으니.\""], ["Brooke laid his book on her lap, with an inviting smile.", "브룩 씨가 권하는 듯한 미소를 지으며 책을 그녀의 무릎 위에 놓았다."]], [["\"It's so hard I'm afraid to try,\"", "\"너무 어려워서 엄두가 안 나요.\""], ["said Meg, grateful, but bashful in the presence of the accomplished young lady beside her.", "메그는 고마우면서도 옆에 앉은 재주 많은 아가씨 앞에서 부끄러워하며 말했다."]], [["\"I'll read a bit to encourage you;\"", "\"용기를 북돋아 드리려고 제가 조금 읽어 볼게요.\""], ["and Miss Kate read one of the most beautiful passages, in a perfectly correct but perfectly expressionless manner.", "케이트 양은 가장 아름다운 대목 가운데 하나를 흠잡을 데 없이 정확하지만 더없이 무미건조하게 읽었다."]], [["Mr. Brooke made no comment, as she returned the book to Meg, who said innocently,--", "브룩 씨는 케이트 양이 책을 다시 메그에게 돌려줄 때 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 메그는 천진하게 말했다."]], [["\"I thought it was poetry.\"", "\"전 그게 시인 줄 알았어요.\""]], [["\"Some of it is.", "\"일부는 시랍니다."], ["Try this passage.\"", "이 대목을 한번 해 보세요.\""]], [["There was a queer smile about Mr. Brooke's mouth as he opened at poor Mary's lament.", "브룩 씨가 가엾은 마리아의 비탄이 담긴 대목을 펼칠 때 그의 입가에는 묘한 미소가 어렸다."]], [["Meg, obediently following the long grass-blade which her new tutor used to point with, read slowly and timidly, unconsciously making poetry of the hard words by the soft intonation of her musical voice.", "메그는 새 선생이 짚어 주는 기다란 풀잎을 고분고분 따라가며 더듬더듬 수줍게 읽어 나갔는데, 음악적인 목소리의 부드러운 억양 덕에 어려운 단어들이 저도 모르게 시가 되었다."], ["Down the page went the green guide, and presently, forgetting her listener in the beauty of the sad scene, Meg read as if alone, giving a little touch of tragedy to the words of the unhappy queen.", "푸른 길잡이가 책장을 따라 내려갔고, 이윽고 슬픈 장면의 아름다움에 빠져 듣는 이를 잊어버린 메그는 마치 혼자 있는 듯이 읽으며, 불행한 여왕의 대사에 비극의 빛깔을 살짝 입혔다."], ["If she had seen the brown eyes then, she would have stopped short; but she never looked up, and the lesson was not spoiled for her.", "만약 그때 그 갈색 눈을 보았다면 그녀는 읽다 말고 멈추었을 테지만, 한 번도 고개를 들지 않은 덕에 그녀의 수업은 망쳐지지 않았다."]], [["\"Very well indeed!\"", "\"정말 훌륭해요!\""], ["said Mr.", "메그가 멈추자 브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["Brooke, as she paused, quite ignoring her many mistakes, and looking as if he did, indeed, \"love to teach.\"", "그녀의 숱한 실수는 모르는 체하면서, 정말로 \"가르치기를 좋아하는\" 사람 같은 표정으로."]], [["Miss Kate put up her glass, and, having taken a survey of the little tableau before her, shut her sketch-book, saying, with condescension,--", "케이트 양은 안경을 들어 눈앞의 자그마한 정경을 한 차례 살피고는 스케치북을 덮으며 거들먹거리는 투로 말했다."]], [["\"You've a nice accent, and, in time, will be a clever reader.", "\"발음이 좋으시네요. 시간이 지나면 글 읽는 솜씨가 제법 늘겠어요."], ["I advise you to learn, for German is a valuable accomplishment to teachers.", "배워 두시라고 권하고 싶어요. 독일어는 가정 교사에게 값진 재주거든요."], ["I must look after Grace, she is romping;\"", "전 그레이스를 좀 돌봐야겠어요, 너무 뛰어다녀서요.\""], ["and Miss Kate strolled away, adding to herself, with a shrug, \"I didn't come to chaperone a governess, though she _is_ young and pretty.", "케이트 양은 이렇게 말하고 어슬렁어슬렁 자리를 떴고, 어깨를 으쓱하며 속으로 덧붙였다. '난 가정 교사 시중을 들러 온 게 아니야, 어리고 예쁘긴 하지만."], ["What odd people these Yankees are; I'm afraid Laurie will be quite spoilt among them.\"", "이 양키들은 어쩜 이리 별난 사람들일까. 로리가 이런 사람들 틈에서 완전히 망가지지나 않을까 걱정이네.'"]], [["\"I forgot that English people rather turn up their noses at governesses, and don't treat them as we do,\"", "\"영국 사람들이 가정 교사를 좀 깔보고, 우리처럼 대해 주지 않는다는 걸 깜박했어요.\""], ["said Meg, looking after the retreating figure with an annoyed expression.", "메그가 멀어져 가는 뒷모습을 짜증스러운 표정으로 바라보며 말했다."]], [["\"Tutors, also, have rather a hard time of it there, as I know to my sorrow.", "\"가정 교사들도 거기서는 꽤 고생을 한답니다. 저도 뼈저리게 겪어서 알지요."], ["There's no place like America for us workers, Miss Margaret;\"", "우리처럼 일하는 사람에게 미국만 한 곳이 없어요, 마거릿 양.\""], ["and Mr. Brooke looked so contented and cheerful, that Meg was ashamed to lament her hard lot.", "그렇게 말하는 브룩 씨의 표정이 어찌나 흡족하고 명랑한지, 메그는 자기 처지를 한탄한 것이 부끄러워졌다."]], [["\"I'm glad I live in it then.", "\"그럼 제가 미국에 사는 게 다행이네요."], ["I don't like my work, but I get a good deal of satisfaction out of it after all, so I won't complain; I only wish I liked teaching as you do.\"", "제 일이 좋지는 않지만, 그래도 결국엔 거기서 적잖은 보람을 얻으니 불평하지 않을래요. 다만 선생님처럼 저도 가르치는 일을 좋아할 수 있으면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["\"I think you would if you had Laurie for a pupil.", "\"로리 같은 학생이 있으면 좋아하게 될 거예요."], ["I shall be very sorry to lose him next year,\" said Mr.", "내년에 그 아이를 떠나보내야 한다니 무척 섭섭하군요.\""], ["Brooke, busily punching holes in the turf.", "브룩 씨가 잔디밭에 부지런히 구멍을 뚫으며 말했다."]], [["\"Going to college, I suppose?\"", "\"대학에 가나 보죠?\""], ["Meg's lips asked that question, but her eyes added, \"And what becomes of you?\"", "메그의 입은 그렇게 물었지만, 그녀의 눈은 \"그럼 선생님은 어떻게 되나요?\" 하고 덧붙이고 있었다."]], [["\"Yes; it's high time he went, for he is ready; and as soon as he is off, I shall turn soldier.", "\"네. 갈 때가 됐어요, 준비도 됐고요."], ["I am needed.\"", "그 아이가 떠나는 대로 저는 군인이 되려고 합니다. 제가 필요하니까요.\""]], [["\"I am glad of that!\"", "\"그거 잘됐네요!\""], ["exclaimed Meg.", "메그가 외쳤다."], ["\"I should think every young man would want to go; though it is hard for the mothers and sisters who stay at home,\"", "\"젊은 남자라면 누구나 가고 싶어 할 것 같아요. 집에 남는 어머니와 누이들에게는 힘든 일이지만요.\""], ["she added sorrowfully.", "그녀가 서글프게 덧붙였다."]], [["\"I have neither, and very few friends, to care whether I live or die,\" said Mr.", "\"저는 어머니도 누이도 없고, 제가 살든 죽든 마음 써 줄 친구도 거의 없답니다.\""], ["Brooke, rather bitterly, as he absently put the dead rose in the hole he had made and covered it up, like a little grave.", "브룩 씨가 다소 씁쓸하게 말하며, 무심코 시든 장미를 자기가 파 놓은 구멍에 넣고는 작은 무덤처럼 흙으로 덮었다."]], [["\"Laurie and his grandfather would care a great deal, and we should all be very sorry to have any harm happen to you,\"", "\"로리하고 그 애 할아버지는 무척 마음 쓰실 거예요. 그리고 선생님께 무슨 나쁜 일이라도 생긴다면 저희 모두 정말 슬플 거예요.\""], ["said Meg heartily.", "메그가 진심으로 말했다."]], [["\"Thank you; that sounds pleasant,\"", "\"고마워요. 듣기 좋은 말이군요.\""], ["began Mr. Brooke, looking cheerful again; but before he could finish his speech, Ned, mounted on the old horse, came lumbering up to display his equestrian skill before the young ladies, and there was no more quiet that day.", "브룩 씨가 다시 밝은 얼굴로 입을 떼었지만, 미처 말을 맺기도 전에 네드가 늙은 말에 올라타고 숙녀들 앞에서 승마 솜씨를 뽐내려고 터덜터덜 다가왔고, 그날은 더 이상 조용할 틈이 없었다."]], [["\"Don't you love to ride?\"", "\"넌 말 타는 거 좋아하지 않니?\""], ["asked Grace of Amy, as they stood resting, after a race round the field with the others, led by Ned.", "네드를 따라 다른 사람들과 들판을 한 바퀴 돌며 달린 뒤 쉬고 있을 때 그레이스가 에이미에게 물었다."]], [["\"I dote upon it; my sister Meg used to ride when papa was rich, but we don't keep any horses now, except Ellen Tree,\"", "\"무척 좋아해. 우리 언니 메그도 아빠가 부자였을 땐 말을 탔는데, 지금은 엘런 트리 말고는 말이 없어.\""], ["added Amy, laughing.", "에이미가 웃으며 덧붙였다."]], [["\"Tell me about Ellen Tree; is it a donkey?\"", "\"엘런 트리에 대해 얘기해 줘. 당나귀야?\""], ["asked Grace curiously.", "그레이스가 궁금해하며 물었다."]], [["\"Why, you see, Jo is crazy about horses, and so am I, but we've only got an old side-saddle, and no horse.", "\"있잖아, 조 언니가 말이라면 사족을 못 쓰는데, 나도 그렇거든. 그런데 우리한텐 낡은 곁안장 하나만 있고 말은 없어."], ["Out in our garden is an apple-tree, that has a nice low branch; so Jo put the saddle on it, fixed some reins on the part that turns up, and we bounce away on Ellen Tree whenever we like.\"", "우리 정원에 사과나무가 있는데, 낮게 뻗은 가지가 마침 알맞아. 그래서 조 언니가 거기에 안장을 얹고, 위로 휘어진 부분에 고삐를 매어서, 우린 내키면 언제든 엘런 트리를 타고 깡충깡충 달린단다.\""]], [["\"How funny!\"", "\"정말 우습다!\""], ["laughed Grace.", "그레이스가 웃었다. \"난 집에 조랑말이 있어서, 프레드랑 케이트랑 거의 매일 공원에서 말을 타."], ["\"I have a pony at home, and ride nearly every day in the park, with Fred and Kate; it's very nice, for my friends go too, and the Row is full of ladies and gentlemen.\"", "정말 좋아, 친구들도 같이 가거든. 로(승마용 산책로 : 옮긴이)에는 신사 숙녀들이 가득하지.\""]], [["\"Dear, how charming!", "\"어머, 얼마나 멋질까!"], ["I hope I shall go abroad some day; but I'd rather go to Rome than the Row,\"", "나도 언젠가 외국에 가 봤으면 좋겠어. 그래도 로보다는 로마에 가고 싶어.\""], ["said Amy, who had not the remotest idea what the Row was, and wouldn't have asked for the world.", "에이미가 말했다. 로가 무엇인지 전혀 몰랐지만 죽어도 묻지는 않을 작정이었다."]], [["Frank, sitting just behind the little girls, heard what they were saying, and pushed his crutch away from him with an impatient gesture as he watched the active lads going through all sorts of comical gymnastics.", "어린 소녀들 바로 뒤에 앉아 있던 프랭크는 그들의 이야기를 듣고는, 날렵한 소년들이 온갖 우스꽝스러운 체조 동작을 펼치는 것을 지켜보다가 답답하다는 듯 목발을 멀찍이 밀쳐 냈다. 흩어진 '작가들'"], ["Beth, who was collecting the scattered Author-cards, looked up, and said, in her shy yet friendly way,--", "카드를 모으고 있던 베스가 고개를 들어, 수줍지만 다정한 그녀 특유의 말투로 말했다."]], [["\"I'm afraid you are tired; can I do anything for you?\"", "\"피곤하신가 봐요. 제가 뭐 도와드릴까요?\""]], [["\"Talk to me, please; it's dull, sitting by myself,\"", "\"제발 말 좀 걸어 줘요. 혼자 앉아 있으니 따분해서요.\""], ["answered Frank, who had evidently been used to being made much of at home.", "집에서 늘 떠받들어진 게 분명한 프랭크가 대답했다."]], [["If he had asked her to deliver a Latin oration, it would not have seemed a more impossible task to bashful Beth; but there was no place to run to, no Jo to hide behind now, and the poor boy looked so wistfully at her, that she bravely resolved to try.", "그가 라틴어로 연설을 해 달라고 부탁했더라도 수줍음 많은 베스에게는 그보다 더 불가능한 일로 느껴지지 않았을 것이다. 하지만 지금은 달아날 곳도 없고, 뒤에 숨을 조도 없었으며, 그 가엾은 소년이 어찌나 간절하게 그녀를 바라보던지, 그녀는 용감하게 해 보기로 마음먹었다."]], [["\"What do you like to talk about?\"", "\"무슨 얘기 하는 거 좋아하세요?\""], ["she asked, fumbling over the cards, and dropping half as she tried to tie them up.", "베스가 카드를 만지작거리다가, 묶으려던 카드를 절반이나 떨어뜨리며 물었다."]], [["\"Well, I like to hear about cricket and boating and hunting,\"", "\"음, 난 크리켓이나 보트 타기, 사냥 얘기 듣는 걸 좋아해요.\""], ["said Frank, who had not yet learned to suit his amusements to his strength.", "아직 자기 형편에 맞게 취미를 고를 줄 모르는 프랭크가 말했다."]], [["\"My heart!", "'어쩌나!"], ["what shall I do?", "어떡하지?"], ["I don't know anything about them,\"", "난 그런 거 하나도 모르는데.' 베스는 생각했다."], ["thought Beth; and, forgetting the boy's misfortune in her flurry, she said, hoping to make him talk, \"I never saw any hunting, but I suppose you know all about it.\"", "그러고는 허둥대는 통에 소년의 불행한 처지를 깜박 잊고는, 그가 말을 하게 만들 작정으로 말했다. \"전 사냥하는 걸 한 번도 못 봤지만, 그쪽은 다 잘 아시겠죠.\""]], [["\"I did once; but I can never hunt again, for I got hurt leaping a confounded five-barred gate; so there are no more horses and hounds for me,\" said Frank, with a sigh that made Beth hate herself for her innocent blunder.", "\"한때는 알았죠. 하지만 이젠 다시는 사냥을 못 해요. 빌어먹을 다섯 막대기 울타리를 뛰어넘다가 다쳤거든요. 그래서 저한텐 이제 말도 사냥개도 없답니다.\" 프랭크가 한숨을 내쉬며 말했고, 그 한숨에 베스는 무심코 저지른 실수 때문에 자신이 미워졌다."]], [["\"Your deer are much prettier than our ugly buffaloes,\"", "\"그쪽 나라 사슴이 우리 못생긴 들소보다 훨씬 더 예뻐요.\" 그녀는 도움을 청하듯 대초원으로 화제를 돌리며 말했다."], ["she said, turning to the prairies for help, and feeling glad that she had read one of the boys' books in which Jo delighted.", "조가 즐겨 읽던 소년용 책 가운데 한 권을 읽어 둔 게 다행이라는 생각이 들었다."]], [["Buffaloes proved soothing and satisfactory; and, in her eagerness to amuse another, Beth forgot herself, and was quite unconscious of her sisters'", "들소 이야기는 마음을 누그러뜨리고 흡족하게 만드는 것으로 드러났다."], ["surprise and delight at the unusual spectacle of Beth talking away to one of the dreadful boys, against whom she had begged protection.", "다른 사람을 즐겁게 해 주려는 열의에 빠져 베스는 자신을 잊었고, 보호해 달라고 애원하던 그 무서운 소년들 가운데 하나와 신나게 떠들고 있는 보기 드문 광경에 언니들이 놀라며 기뻐하는 것도 전혀 알아채지 못했다."]], [["\"Bless her heart!", "\"어쩜 저렇게 착할까!"], ["She pities him, so she is good to him,\"", "그 애가 가여워서 잘해 주는 거야.\""], ["said Jo, beaming at her from the croquet-ground.", "조가 크로케 경기장에서 베스를 향해 함박웃음을 지으며 말했다."]], [["\"I always said she was a little saint,\"", "\"내가 늘 말했잖아, 쟤는 자그마한 성녀라고.\""], ["added Meg, as if there could be no further doubt of it.", "메그가 이제 그 점에 더는 의심의 여지가 없다는 듯이 덧붙였다."]], [["\"I haven't heard Frank laugh so much for ever so long,\"", "\"프랭크가 저렇게 많이 웃는 건 정말 오랜만이야.\""], ["said Grace to Amy, as they sat discussing dolls, and making tea-sets out of the acorn-cups.", "그레이스가 도토리 깍지로 찻잔 세트를 만들며 인형 이야기를 나누다가 에이미에게 말했다."]], [["\"My sister Beth is a very _fastidious_ girl, when she likes to be,\"", "\"우리 베스 언니는 마음만 먹으면 아주 까다로운 애야.\""], ["said Amy, well pleased at Beth's success. She meant \"fascinating,\"", "베스의 성공에 흐뭇해진 에이미가 말했다."], ["but as Grace didn't know the exact meaning of either word, \"fastidious\" sounded well, and made a good impression.", "그녀가 하려던 말은 \"매력적인\"이었지만, 그레이스가 두 단어의 정확한 뜻을 몰랐던 덕에 \"까다로운\"도 그럴듯하게 들렸고 좋은 인상을 남겼다."]], [["An impromptu circus, fox and geese, and an amicable game of croquet, finished the afternoon.", "즉석에서 벌인 곡예와 여우와 거위 놀이, 그리고 사이좋게 한판 벌인 크로케로 오후가 마무리되었다."], ["At sunset the tent was struck, hampers packed, wickets pulled up, boats loaded, and the whole party floated down the river, singing at the tops of their voices.", "해 질 녘에 천막을 걷고, 바구니를 꾸리고, 위켓을 뽑고, 보트에 짐을 실은 다음, 일행은 모두 목청껏 노래를 부르며 강을 따라 떠내려갔다."], ["Ned, getting sentimental, warbled a serenade with the pensive refrain,--", "네드는 감상에 젖어 구슬픈 후렴이 담긴 세레나데를 흥얼거렸다."]], [["\"Alone, alone, ah!", "\"외로이, 외로이, 아아!"], ["woe, alone,\"", "슬프도다, 외로이,\""]], [["and at the lines--", "그리고 이런 구절에 이르러—"]], [["\"We each are young, we each have a heart, Oh, why should we stand thus coldly apart?\"", "\"우리는 저마다 젊고, 우리는 저마다 가슴을 지녔거늘,\n아, 어찌하여 우리는 이리 차갑게 떨어져 있어야 하나?\""]], [["he looked at Meg with such a lackadaisical expression that she laughed outright and spoilt his song.", "그가 어찌나 나른한 표정으로 메그를 바라보던지, 그녀는 그만 웃음을 터뜨려 그의 노래를 망쳐 버렸다."]], [["\"How can you be so cruel to me?\"", "\"어떻게 나한테 그렇게 모질 수가 있어요?\""], ["he whispered, under cover of a lively chorus.", "그가 흥겨운 합창 소리에 묻어 속삭였다."], ["\"You've kept close to that starched-up Englishwoman all day, and now you snub me.\"", "\"온종일 그 빳빳하게 풀 먹인 영국 여자한테 딱 붙어 있더니, 이제는 날 쌀쌀맞게 대하는군요.\""]], [["\"I didn't mean to; but you looked so funny I really couldn't help it,\"", "\"그럴 생각은 아니었어요. 하지만 표정이 어찌나 우습던지 정말 어쩔 수가 없었어요.\""], ["replied Meg, passing over the first part of his reproach; for it was quite true that she _had_ shunned him, remembering the Moffat party and the talk after it.", "메그는 그의 책망 가운데 앞부분만 슬쩍 넘기며 대답했다. 사실 그녀가 모팻 파티와 그 뒤에 오간 말을 떠올리고 그를 피한 것은 분명했으니까."]], [["Ned was offended, and turned to Sallie for consolation, saying to her rather pettishly, \"There isn't a bit of flirt in that girl, is there?\"", "기분이 상한 네드는 위로를 받으러 샐리에게로 돌아서며 다소 뾰로통하게 말했다. \"저 여자한텐 바람기라곤 눈곱만큼도 없어, 그렇지?\""]], [["\"Not a particle; but she's a dear,\"", "\"손톱만큼도 없지. 하지만 사랑스러운 애야.\""], ["returned Sallie, defending her friend even while confessing her short-comings.", "샐리가 친구의 흠을 인정하면서도 그녀를 두둔하며 대꾸했다."]], [["\"She's not a stricken deer, any way,\"", "\"어쨌든 상심한 사슴은 아니로군.\""], ["said Ned, trying to be witty, and succeeding as well as very young gentlemen usually do.", "네드가 재치를 부려 보려고 한 말이었는데, 그 솜씨는 여느 젊은 신사들과 별반 다르지 않았다."]], [["On the lawn, where it had gathered, the little party separated with cordial good-nights and good-byes, for the Vaughns were going to Canada.", "일행이 모여 든 잔디밭에서 사람들은 정겹게 인사를 나누고 작별을 고하며 흩어졌다. 본 씨네 식구들이 캐나다로 떠날 참이었기 때문이다."], ["As the four sisters went home through the garden, Miss Kate looked after them, saying, without the patronizing tone in her voice, \"In spite of their demonstrative manners, American girls are very nice when one knows them.\"", "네 자매가 정원을 가로질러 집으로 가는데, 케이트 양이 그들의 뒷모습을 바라보며 거들먹거리는 기색 없는 목소리로 말했다. \"감정을 드러내는 태도가 좀 그렇긴 해도, 미국 아가씨들은 알고 보면 무척 괜찮아요.\""]], [["\"I quite agree with you,\"", "\"전적으로 같은 생각입니다.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke.", "브룩 씨가 말했다."]], [["# CASTLES IN THE AIR.", "# CASTLES IN THE AIR."]], [["Laurie lay luxuriously swinging to and fro in his hammock, one warm September afternoon, wondering what his neighbors were about, but too lazy to go and find out.", "공중누각\n9월의 어느 따스한 오후, 로리는 해먹에 누워 한가로이 앞뒤로 흔들거리며, 이웃집 식구들이 뭘 하고 있을까 궁금해했지만 가서 알아볼 만큼 부지런하지는 못했다."], ["He was in one of his moods; for the day had been both unprofitable and unsatisfactory, and he was wishing he could live it over again.", "그날따라 기분이 좋지 않았다."], ["The hot weather made him indolent, and he had shirked his studies, tried Mr.", "하루가 보람도 없고 마음에도 차지 않아, 다시 한번 살 수 있다면 좋겠다는 생각이 들 정도였다."], ["Brooke's patience to the utmost, displeased his grandfather by practising half the afternoon, frightened the maid-servants half out of their wits, by mischievously hinting that one of his dogs was going mad, and, after high words with the stable-man about some fancied neglect of his horse, he had flung himself into his hammock, to fume over the stupidity of the world in general, till the peace of the lovely day quieted him in spite of himself.", "무더운 날씨에 나른해진 그는 공부를 게을리하고, 브룩 씨의 인내심을 한계까지 시험했으며, 오후 내내 피아노를 친답시고 할아버지를 언짢게 했고, 자기 개 한 마리가 미치려 한다고 짓궂게 흘려 하녀들의 혼을 쏙 빼놓았다. 게다가 자기 말을 소홀히 했다는 괜한 트집을 잡아 마부와 험한 말을 주고받은 뒤, 해먹에 몸을 던지고 누워 세상이 온통 멍청하다며 씩씩거렸다."], ["Staring up into the green gloom of the horse-chestnut trees above him, he dreamed dreams of all sorts, and was just imagining himself tossing on the ocean, in a voyage round the world, when the sound of voices brought him ashore in a flash.", "그러다 아름다운 하루의 평온함이 본인의 뜻과 상관없이 그를 달래 주었다. 머리 위 마로니에의 푸른 그늘을 올려다보며 그는 온갖 꿈을 꾸었고, 마침 세계 일주 항해를 하며 대양 위에서 출렁이는 자신을 상상하던 참인데, 말소리가 들려와 순식간에 그를 뭍으로 끌어내렸다."], ["Peeping through the meshes of the hammock, he saw the Marches coming out, as if bound on some expedition.", "해먹의 그물코 사이로 내다보니 마치 집 식구들이 무슨 나들이라도 가는 듯이 밖으로 나오고 있었다."]], [["\"What in the world are those girls about now?\"", "'대체 저 애들이 지금 뭘 하려는 거지?'"], ["thought Laurie, opening his sleepy eyes to take a good look, for there was something rather peculiar in the appearance of his neighbors.", "로리는 졸린 눈을 뜨고 자세히 살피려 했다. 이웃집 식구들의 행색에 뭔가 좀 별난 데가 있었던 것이다."], ["Each wore a large, flapping hat, a brown linen pouch slung over one shoulder, and carried a long staff.", "저마다 챙이 넓어 펄럭이는 모자를 쓰고, 갈색 리넨 자루를 한쪽 어깨에 둘러메고, 긴 막대기를 들고 있었다."], ["Meg had a cushion, Jo a book, Beth a basket, and Amy a portfolio.", "메그는 방석을, 조는 책을, 베스는 바구니를, 에이미는 화첩을 들고 있었다."], ["All walked quietly through the garden, out at the little back gate, and began to climb the hill that lay between the house and river.", "다들 정원을 조용히 가로질러 작은 뒷문으로 나가더니, 집과 강 사이에 있는 언덕을 오르기 시작했다."]], [["\"Well, that's cool!\"", "'야, 너무하네!'"], ["said Laurie to himself, \"to have a picnic and never ask me.", "로리는 속으로 중얼거렸다. '소풍을 가면서 나한테는 한마디도 안 하다니."], ["They can't be going in the boat, for they haven't got the key.", "보트를 타려는 건 아닐 거야, 열쇠가 없으니까."], ["Perhaps they forgot it; I'll take it to them, and see what's going on.\"", "어쩌면 깜박했을지도 몰라. 내가 갖다주면서 무슨 일인지 봐야겠다.'"]], [["Though possessed of half a dozen hats, it took him some time to find one; then there was a hunt for the key, which was at last discovered in his pocket; so that the girls were quite out of sight when he leaped the fence and ran after them.", "모자가 여섯 개나 있었는데도 하나 찾는 데 시간이 좀 걸렸다. 그다음엔 열쇠를 뒤졌는데, 결국 자기 주머니에서 나왔다."], ["Taking the shortest way to the boat-house, he waited for them to appear: but no one came, and he went up the hill to take an observation.", "그래서 그가 울타리를 뛰어넘어 뒤쫓아 갔을 때는 소녀들이 이미 시야에서 사라진 뒤였다. 보트 창고로 가는 지름길을 택해 그들이 나타나기를 기다렸지만 아무도 오지 않자, 그는 언덕에 올라가 살펴보기로 했다."], ["A grove of pines covered one part of it, and from the heart of this green spot came a clearer sound than the soft sigh of the pines or the drowsy chirp of the crickets.", "언덕 한쪽은 소나무 숲으로 덮여 있었는데, 그 푸른 곳 한복판에서 솔잎의 나직한 한숨이나 귀뚜라미의 졸린 울음보다 또렷한 소리가 들려왔다."]], [["\"Here's a landscape!\"", "'이거 멋진 풍경인데!'"], ["thought Laurie, peeping through the bushes, and looking wide-awake and good-natured already.", "로리는 덤불 사이로 들여다보며 생각했다. 어느새 잠기운이 가시고 기분도 좋아져 있었다."]], [["It _was_ rather a pretty little picture; for the sisters sat together in the shady nook, with sun and shadow flickering over them, the aromatic wind lifting their hair and cooling their hot cheeks, and all the little wood-people going on with their affairs as if these were no strangers, but old friends.", "정말이지 꽤 어여쁜 그림 같은 정경이었다. 자매들은 그늘진 후미진 곳에 함께 앉아 있었고, 그들 위로 햇살과 그림자가 어른거렸으며, 향긋한 바람이 머리카락을 들어 올려 달아오른 뺨을 식혀 주었고, 작은 숲속 식구들은 그들을 낯선 이가 아니라 오랜 친구라도 되는 양 제 할 일을 계속하고 있었다."], ["Meg sat upon her cushion, sewing daintily with her white hands, and looking as fresh and sweet as a rose, in her pink dress, among the green.", "메그는 방석에 앉아 하얀 손으로 곱게 바느질을 하며, 분홍 드레스 차림으로 초록빛 속에서 장미처럼 싱그럽고 어여뻤다."], ["Beth was sorting the cones that lay thick under the hemlock near by, for she made pretty things of them.", "베스는 근처 솔송나무 아래 수북이 떨어진 솔방울을 골라 모으고 있었으니, 그것으로 예쁜 물건을 만들곤 했기 때문이다."], ["Amy was sketching a group of ferns, and Jo was knitting as she read aloud.", "에이미는 한 무리의 고사리를 스케치하고 있었고, 조는 책을 소리 내어 읽으면서 뜨개질을 했다."], ["A shadow passed over the boy's face as he watched them, feeling that he ought to go away, because uninvited; yet lingering, because home seemed very lonely, and this quiet party in the woods most attractive to his restless spirit.", "그들을 지켜보는 소년의 얼굴에 그늘이 스쳤다. 초대받지 않았으니 가야 한다는 생각이 들면서도, 집은 너무나 쓸쓸하고 숲속의 이 조용한 모임은 들떠 있는 그의 마음에 더없이 끌렸기에 차마 발길이 떨어지지 않았다."], ["He stood so still that a squirrel, busy with its harvesting, ran down a pine close beside him, saw him suddenly and skipped back, scolding so shrilly that Beth looked up, espied the wistful face behind the birches, and beckoned with a reassuring smile.", "그가 어찌나 가만히 서 있었던지, 먹이를 거두느라 분주하던 다람쥐 한 마리가 바로 옆 소나무를 타고 내려오다가 그를 보고는 화들짝 되돌아 올라가며 어찌나 날카롭게 꾸짖어 대던지, 베스가 고개를 들었다가 자작나무 뒤의 간절한 얼굴을 발견하고는 안심시키는 미소를 지으며 손짓했다."]], [["\"May I come in, please?", "\"저 들어가도 될까요?"], ["or shall I be a bother?\"", "아니면 방해가 될까요?\""], ["he asked, advancing slowly.", "그가 천천히 다가서며 물었다."]], [["Meg lifted her eyebrows, but Jo scowled at her defiantly, and said, at once, \"Of course you may.", "메그가 눈썹을 치켜올렸지만, 조는 도전하듯 언니를 노려보며 곧바로 말했다. \"당연히 되지."], ["We should have asked you before, only we thought you wouldn't care for such a girl's game as this.\"", "진작 부르려고 했는데, 이런 여자애들 놀이를 네가 좋아하지 않을 줄 알았거든.\""]], [["\"I always liked your games; but if Meg doesn't want me, I'll go away.\"", "\"난 너희 놀이라면 늘 좋았어. 그래도 메그가 싫다면 갈게.\""]], [["\"I've no objection, if you do something; it's against the rules to be idle here,\"", "\"네가 뭐라도 한다면 나는 반대하지 않아. 여기서는 빈둥거리는 게 규칙 위반이거든.\""], ["replied Meg, gravely but graciously.", "메그가 점잖지만 상냥하게 대답했다."]], [["\"Much obliged; I'll do anything if you'll let me stop a bit, for it's as dull as the Desert of Sahara down there.", "\"고마워. 잠깐만 있게 해 주면 뭐든 할게."], ["Shall I sew, read, cone, draw, or do all at once?", "저 아래는 사하라 사막처럼 따분하거든. 바느질을 할까, 책을 읽을까, 솔방울을 주울까, 그림을 그릴까, 아니면 한꺼번에 다 할까?"], ["Bring on your bears; I'm ready,\"", "어떤 곰이든 데려와, 난 준비됐어.\""], ["and Laurie sat down, with a submissive expression delightful to behold.", "로리가 보기에도 흐뭇할 만큼 고분고분한 표정으로 자리에 앉으며 말했다."]], [["\"Finish this story while I set my heel,\"", "\"내가 발뒤꿈치 뜨는 동안 이 이야기 마저 읽어 줘.\""], ["said Jo, handing him the book.", "조가 그에게 책을 건네며 말했다."]], [["\"Yes'm,\"", "\"네, 알겠습니다.\""], ["was the meek answer, as he began, doing his best to prove his gratitude for the favor of an admission into the \"Busy Bee Society.\"", "그는 고분고분 대답하며 읽기 시작했고, '부지런한 꿀벌 모임'에 끼워 준 호의에 대한 고마움을 증명하려고 최선을 다했다."], ["The story was not a long one, and, when it was finished, he ventured to ask a few questions as a reward of merit.", "이야기는 길지 않았고, 다 읽고 나서 그는 잘한 데 대한 상으로 몇 가지를 물어봐도 되느냐고 조심스레 청했다."]], [["\"Please, ma'am, could I inquire if this highly instructive and charming institution is a new one?\"", "\"저, 여쭤봐도 될까요, 이 대단히 유익하고 멋진 모임이 새로 생긴 건가요?\""]], [["\"Would you tell him?\"", "\"얘기해 줘도 될까?\""], ["asked Meg of her sisters.", "메그가 동생들에게 물었다."]], [["\"He'll laugh,\"", "\"비웃을걸.\""], ["said Amy warningly.", "에이미가 주의를 주듯 말했다."]], [["\"Who cares?\"", "\"비웃으면 어때?\""], ["said Jo.", "조가 말했다."]], [["\"I guess he'll like it,\"", "\"좋아할 것 같은데.\""], ["added Beth.", "베스가 덧붙였다."]], [["\"Of course I shall!", "\"당연히 좋아하지!"], ["I give you my word I won't laugh.", "절대 안 웃겠다고 약속할게."], ["Tell away, Jo, and don't be afraid.\"", "얘기해 줘, 조, 겁내지 말고.\""]], [["\"The idea of being afraid of you!", "\"너를 겁낸다니 말도 안 돼!"], ["Well, you see we used to play 'Pilgrim's Progress,'", "그러니까 우린 예전에 '천로역정'"], ["and we have been going on with it in earnest, all winter and summer.\"", "놀이를 하곤 했는데, 겨울 내내 여름 내내 그걸 진지하게 계속해 온 거야.\""]], [["\"Yes, I know,\"", "\"응, 알아.\""], ["said Laurie, nodding wisely.", "로리가 아는 체하며 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["\"Who told you?\"", "\"누가 말해 줬어?\""], ["demanded Jo.", "조가 따져 물었다."]], [["\"Spirits.\"", "\"영혼들이.\""]], [["\"No, I did; I wanted to amuse him one night when you were all away, and he was rather dismal.", "\"아니야, 내가 그랬어. 언니들이 다 나가고 없던 어느 날 밤에, 이 애가 좀 시무룩하길래 즐겁게 해 주고 싶었거든."], ["He did like it, so don't scold, Jo,\"", "정말 좋아했으니까 나무라지 마, 조.\""], ["said Beth meekly.", "베스가 풀 죽은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"You can't keep a secret.", "\"넌 비밀을 못 지킨다니까."], ["Never mind; it saves trouble now.\"", "괜찮아, 덕분에 수고를 덜었으니까.\""]], [["\"Go on, please,\"", "\"계속해 줘.\""], ["said Laurie, as Jo became absorbed in her work, looking a trifle displeased.", "조가 살짝 못마땅한 얼굴로 자기 뜨개질에 몰두하자 로리가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, didn't she tell you about this new plan of ours?", "\"아, 베스가 우리 이 새 계획 얘기는 안 해 줬구나?"], ["Well, we have tried not to waste our holiday, but each has had a task, and worked at it with a will.", "그게, 우린 방학을 헛되이 보내지 않으려고 저마다 할 일을 하나씩 정해서 마음을 다해 해 왔어."], ["The vacation is nearly over, the stints are all done, and we are ever so glad that we didn't dawdle.\"", "방학이 거의 끝나가는데 정해 둔 몫을 다 끝냈으니, 빈둥거리지 않은 게 정말 기뻐.\""]], [["\"Yes, I should think so;\"", "\"응, 그렇겠다.\""], ["and Laurie thought regretfully of his own idle days.", "로리는 자신이 빈둥거린 날들을 떠올리며 후회스러워했다."]], [["\"Mother likes to have us out of doors as much as possible; so we bring our work here, and have nice times.", "\"엄마는 우리가 되도록 밖에 많이 나가 있길 바라셔. 그래서 우린 일거리를 여기로 가져와서 즐겁게 지내."], ["For the fun of it we bring our things in these bags, wear the old hats, use poles to climb the hill, and play pilgrims, as we used to do years ago.", "재미 삼아서 이 자루에 물건을 담고, 낡은 모자를 쓰고, 막대기로 언덕을 오르면서, 몇 년 전에 하던 것처럼 순례자 놀이를 하는 거야."], ["We call this hill the 'Delectable Mountain,' for we can look far away and see the country where we hope to live some time.\"", "우린 이 언덕을 '기쁨의 산'이라고 부르는데, 여기서 멀리까지 내다보면 우리가 언젠가 살았으면 하는 고장이 보이거든.\""]], [["Jo pointed, and Laurie sat up to examine; for through an opening in the wood one could look across the wide, blue river, the meadows on the other side, far over the outskirts of the great city, to the green hills that rose to meet the sky.", "조가 손가락으로 가리키자, 로리가 몸을 일으켜 살펴보았다. 숲 사이로 트인 곳을 통해, 넓고 푸른 강 너머 건너편 초원, 그리고 저 멀리 대도시의 변두리 너머로, 하늘에 맞닿을 듯 솟아오른 푸른 언덕들이 한눈에 들어왔다."], ["The sun was low, and the heavens plowed with the splendor of an autumn sunset.", "해는 나직이 걸렸고, 하늘은 가을 노을의 찬란함으로 일구어진 듯했다."], ["Gold and purple clouds lay on the hill-tops; and rising high into the ruddy light were silvery white peaks, that shone like the airy spires of some Celestial City.", "황금빛과 보랏빛 구름이 산마루에 걸려 있었고, 붉은 빛 속으로 높이 솟은 은백색 봉우리들은 어느 천상의 도시의 가뭇한 첨탑처럼 반짝였다."]], [["\"How beautiful that is!\"", "\"정말 아름답다!\""], ["said Laurie softly, for he was quick to see and feel beauty of any kind.", "로리가 나직이 말했다. 그는 어떤 종류든 아름다움을 재빨리 알아보고 느끼는 사람이었으니까."]], [["\"It's often so; and we like to watch it, for it is never the same, but always splendid,\"", "\"자주 저래. 우린 저걸 보는 게 좋아, 한 번도 같은 적이 없으면서 늘 멋지거든.\""], ["replied Amy, wishing she could paint it.", "에이미가 저것을 그림으로 옮길 수 있으면 좋겠다고 바라며 대답했다."]], [["\"Jo talks about the country where we hope to live some time,--the real country, she means, with pigs and chickens, and haymaking.", "\"조 언니는 우리가 언젠가 살았으면 하는 고장 얘기를 하는데, 언니가 말하는 건 진짜 시골이야. 돼지랑 닭이 있고, 건초를 만드는 그런 곳 말이야."], ["It would be nice, but I wish the beautiful country up there was real, and we could ever go to it,\"", "그것도 좋겠지만, 난 저 위에 있는 저 아름다운 고장이 진짜라서 우리가 언젠가 갈 수 있으면 좋겠어.\""], ["said Beth musingly.", "베스가 생각에 잠겨 말했다."]], [["\"There is a lovelier country even than that, where we _shall_ go, by and by, when we are good enough,\"", "\"저것보다 훨씬 더 사랑스러운 고장이 있단다. 우리가 충분히 착해지면 머지않아 그곳으로 가게 될 거야.\""], ["answered Meg, with her sweet voice.", "메그가 고운 목소리로 대답했다."]], [["\"It seems so long to wait, so hard to do; I want to fly away at once, as those swallows fly, and go in at that splendid gate.\"", "\"기다리려니 너무 오래 걸리고, 하기도 너무 어려워. 난 저 제비들이 날아가듯 당장 날아가서 저 찬란한 문으로 들어가고 싶어.\""]], [["\"You'll get there, Beth, sooner or later; no fear of that,\" said Jo; \"I'm the one that will have to fight and work, and climb and wait, and maybe never get in after all.\"", "\"넌 가게 될 거야, 베스, 머지않아. 그건 염려 마.\" 조가 말했다. \"싸우고 일하고, 오르고 기다려야 하는 건 나야. 그러고도 결국 못 들어갈지도 모르고.\""]], [["\"You'll have me for company, if that's any comfort.", "\"위안이 될지 모르겠지만, 내가 너랑 동무해 줄게."], ["I shall have to do a deal of travelling before I come in sight of your Celestial City.", "너의 그 천상의 도시가 눈에 들어오기까지 나는 엄청나게 먼 길을 가야 할 테니까."], ["If I arrive late, you'll say a good word for me, won't you, Beth?\"", "내가 늦게 도착하거든 나 좀 잘 말해 줄 거지, 베스?\""]], [["Something in the boy's face troubled his little friend; but she said cheerfully, with her quiet eyes on the changing clouds, \"If people really want to go, and really try all their lives, I think they will get in; for I don't believe there are any locks on that door, or any guards at the gate. I always imagine it is as it is in the picture, where the shining ones stretch out their hands to welcome poor Christian as he comes up from the river.\"", "소년의 얼굴에 어린 무언가가 그의 어린 친구를 마음 쓰이게 했다. 하지만 베스는 변해 가는 구름에 잔잔한 눈길을 둔 채 밝게 말했다. \"정말로 가고 싶어 하고, 평생토록 정말로 애쓴다면, 누구든 들어가게 될 거라고 난 믿어. 그 문에는 자물쇠도 없고, 문지기도 없을 것 같거든. 난 늘 그곳이 그림 속에서랑 똑같을 거라고 상상해. 빛나는 이들이 강에서 올라오는 가엾은 크리스천을 맞이하려고 손을 내미는 그 그림 말이야.\""]], [["\"Wouldn't it be fun if all the castles in the air which we make could come true, and we could live in them?\"", "\"우리가 짓는 공중누각이 다 진짜가 돼서 그 안에서 살 수 있다면 얼마나 재미있을까?\""], ["said Jo, after a little pause.", "잠시 사이를 두었다가 조가 말했다."]], [["\"I've made such quantities it would be hard to choose which I'd have,\"", "\"난 하도 많이 지어 놔서 어느 걸 가질지 고르기도 힘들겠어.\""], ["said Laurie, lying flat, and throwing cones at the squirrel who had betrayed him.", "로리가 벌렁 누워, 자기를 들킨 그 다람쥐에게 솔방울을 던지며 말했다."]], [["\"You'd have to take your favorite one.", "\"제일 마음에 드는 걸 가져야지."], ["What is it?\"", "그게 뭔데?\""], ["asked Meg.", "메그가 물었다."]], [["\"If I tell mine, will you tell yours?\"", "\"내 걸 말하면 네 것도 말해 줄래?\""]], [["\"Yes, if the girls will too.\"", "\"응, 다른 애들도 말한다면.\""]], [["\"We will.", "\"우리도 말할게."], ["Now, Laurie.\"", "자, 로리.\""]], [["\"After I'd seen as much of the world as I want to, I'd like to settle in Germany, and have just as much music as I choose.", "\"내가 보고 싶은 만큼 세상을 다 본 다음엔, 독일에 자리를 잡고 마음껏 음악을 누리고 싶어."], ["I'm to be a famous musician myself, and all creation is to rush to hear me; and I'm never to be bothered about money or business, but just enjoy myself, and live for what I like.", "나는 유명한 음악가가 될 거고, 온 세상이 내 연주를 들으러 몰려드는 거야. 그리고 돈이나 사업 따위로는 골치 썩일 일 없이, 그저 즐기면서 내가 좋아하는 걸 위해 사는 거지."], ["That's my favorite castle.", "그게 내가 제일 좋아하는 누각이야."], ["What's yours, Meg?\"", "네 건 뭐야, 메그?\""]], [["Margaret seemed to find it a little hard to tell hers, and waved a brake before her face, as if to disperse imaginary gnats, while she said slowly, \"I should like a lovely house, full of all sorts of luxurious things,--nice food, pretty clothes, handsome furniture, pleasant people, and heaps of money.", "마거릿은 자기 것을 말하기가 좀 어려운 모양이었다. 상상 속 모기라도 쫓듯 양치류 잎을 얼굴 앞에서 흔들며 천천히 말했다."], ["I am to be mistress of it, and manage it as I like, with plenty of servants, so I never need work a bit.", "\"난 온갖 호사스러운 것들로 가득한 사랑스러운 집을 갖고 싶어. 맛있는 음식, 예쁜 옷, 멋진 가구, 유쾌한 사람들, 그리고 돈도 산더미처럼."], ["How I should enjoy it!", "내가 그 집의 안주인이 되어서 마음대로 꾸려 나가는 거야. 하인도 많아서 내가 손끝 하나 까딱할 필요가 없는 거지."], ["for I wouldn't be idle, but do good, and make every one love me dearly.\"", "얼마나 즐거울까! 난 빈둥거리지 않고 좋은 일을 하면서 모두가 나를 진심으로 사랑하게 만들 거야.\""]], [["\"Wouldn't you have a master for your castle in the air?\"", "\"네 공중누각엔 주인 양반은 없는 거야?\""], ["asked Laurie slyly.", "로리가 짓궂게 물었다."]], [["\"I said 'pleasant people,' you know;\"", "\"내가 '유쾌한 사람들'이라고 했잖아.\""], ["and Meg carefully tied up her shoe as she spoke, so that no one saw her face.", "메그는 이렇게 말하며 아무도 자기 얼굴을 못 보도록 조심스레 신발 끈을 묶었다."]], [["\"Why don't you say you'd have a splendid, wise, good husband, and some angelic little children?", "\"근사하고 지혜롭고 착한 남편에다 천사 같은 아이들도 몇 두고 싶다고 왜 말하지 않아?"], ["You know your castle wouldn't be perfect without,\"", "그게 없으면 네 누각도 완벽하지 않을 거라는 거 너도 알잖아.\""], ["said blunt Jo, who had no tender fancies yet, and rather scorned romance, except in books.", "조가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. 아직 다정한 공상이라곤 없었고, 책 속에서가 아니면 연애담을 다소 깔보는 그녀였으니까."]], [["\"You'd have nothing but horses, inkstands, and novels in yours,\"", "\"네 누각엔 말이랑 잉크병이랑 소설책밖에 없겠지.\""], ["answered Meg petulantly.", "메그가 뾰로통하게 받아쳤다."]], [["\"Wouldn't I, though?", "\"왜 아니겠어?"], ["I'd have a stable full of Arabian steeds, rooms piled with books, and I'd write out of a magic inkstand, so that my works should be as famous as Laurie's music.", "난 마구간 가득 아라비아산 준마를 채우고, 방마다 책을 산더미처럼 쌓아 둘 거야. 그리고 마법의 잉크병으로 글을 써서, 내 작품이 로리의 음악만큼 유명해지게 만들 거야."], ["I want to do something splendid before I go into my castle,--something heroic or wonderful, that won't be forgotten after I'm dead.", "난 내 누각으로 들어가기 전에 뭔가 근사한 일을 하고 싶어. 영웅적이거나 놀라운 일, 내가 죽고 나서도 잊히지 않을 그런 일 말이야."], ["I don't know what, but I'm on the watch for it, and mean to astonish you all, some day.", "그게 뭔지는 모르겠지만, 늘 눈을 부릅뜨고 찾고 있어. 언젠가 너희 모두를 깜짝 놀라게 할 작정이야."], ["I think I shall write books, and get rich and famous: that would suit me, so that is _my_ favorite dream.\"", "난 책을 써서 부자가 되고 유명해질 것 같아. 그게 내 적성에 맞으니까, 그게 바로 내가 제일 좋아하는 꿈이야.\""]], [["\"Mine is to stay at home safe with father and mother, and help take care of the family,\"", "\"내 꿈은 아버지 어머니랑 안전하게 집에 머물면서 식구들 보살피는 걸 돕는 거야.\""], ["said Beth contentedly.", "베스가 흡족하게 말했다."]], [["\"Don't you wish for anything else?\"", "\"넌 다른 건 바라지 않아?\""], ["asked Laurie.", "로리가 물었다."]], [["\"Since I had my little piano, I am perfectly satisfied.", "\"내 작은 피아노가 생긴 뒤로는 완전히 만족스러워."], ["I only wish we may all keep well and be together; nothing else.\"", "그저 우리 모두 건강하게 함께 지낼 수 있기만 바랄 뿐, 다른 건 없어.\""]], [["\"I have ever so many wishes; but the pet one is to be an artist, and go to Rome, and do fine pictures, and be the best artist in the whole world,\"", "\"난 바라는 게 엄청 많은데, 그중 으뜸은 화가가 돼서 로마에 가는 거야. 멋진 그림을 그려서 온 세상에서 제일가는 화가가 되는 거지.\""], ["was Amy's modest desire.", "에이미가 조심스레 소망을 밝혔다."]], [["\"We're an ambitious set, aren't we?", "\"우린 야심 찬 무리네, 그렇지?"], ["Every one of us, but Beth, wants to be rich and famous, and gorgeous in every respect.", "베스만 빼고 다들 부자에 유명인이 되고, 모든 면에서 으리으리해지고 싶어 하니."], ["I do wonder if any of us will ever get our wishes,\"", "우리 중에 누구라도 정말 소원을 이루게 될지 궁금해.\""], ["said Laurie, chewing grass, like a meditative calf.", "로리가 생각에 잠긴 송아지처럼 풀잎을 씹으며 말했다."]], [["\"I've got the key to my castle in the air; but whether I can unlock the door remains to be seen,\"", "\"난 내 공중누각 열쇠를 손에 넣었어. 다만 그 문을 열 수 있을지는 두고 봐야겠지.\""], ["observed Jo mysteriously.", "조가 의미심장하게 말했다."]], [["\"I've got the key to mine, but I'm not allowed to try it.", "\"나도 내 거 열쇠는 있는데, 써 볼 수가 없어."], ["Hang college!\"", "대학이고 뭐고!\""], ["muttered Laurie, with an impatient sigh.", "로리가 답답한 듯 한숨을 내쉬며 중얼거렸다."]], [["\"Here's mine!\"", "\"내 건 여기 있어!\""], ["and Amy waved her pencil.", "에이미가 연필을 흔들어 보였다."]], [["\"I haven't got any,\"", "\"난 아무것도 없어.\""], ["said Meg forlornly.", "메그가 풀이 죽어 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, you have,\"", "\"아니, 있어.\""], ["said Laurie at once.", "로리가 곧바로 말했다."]], [["\"Where?\"", "\"어디에?\""]], [["\"In your face.\"", "\"네 얼굴에.\""]], [["\"Nonsense; that's of no use.\"", "\"말도 안 돼. 그런 건 아무 쓸모도 없어.\""]], [["\"Wait and see if it doesn't bring you something worth having,\"", "\"두고 봐, 그게 너한테 가질 만한 뭔가를 가져다주는지 안 가져다주는지.\""], ["replied the boy, laughing at the thought of a charming little secret which he fancied he knew.", "자기가 안다고 여기는 어떤 사랑스러운 작은 비밀을 떠올리며 소년이 웃으며 대답했다."]], [["Meg colored behind the brake, but asked no questions, and looked across the river with the same expectant expression which Mr. Brooke had worn when he told the story of the knight.", "메그는 양치류 잎 뒤에서 얼굴을 붉혔지만 아무것도 묻지 않았고, 브룩 씨가 그 기사 이야기를 들려줄 때 짓던 것과 똑같은 기대 어린 표정으로 강 건너편을 바라보았다."]], [["\"If we are all alive ten years hence, let's meet, and see how many of us have got our wishes, or how much nearer we are then than now,\"", "\"우리 모두 지금부터 십 년 뒤에도 살아 있거든, 만나서 우리 중 몇이나 소원을 이루었는지, 아니면 그때는 지금보다 얼마나 더 가까워졌는지 보자.\""], ["said Jo, always ready with a plan.", "언제나 계획을 척척 내놓는 조가 말했다."]], [["\"Bless me!", "\"맙소사!"], ["how old I shall be,--twenty-seven!\"", "그럼 난 얼마나 나이를 먹는 거야, 스물일곱이라니!\""], ["exclaimed Meg who felt grown up already, having just reached seventeen.", "이제 막 열일곱이 되었으면서 벌써 다 큰 어른 같은 기분이던 메그가 외쳤다."]], [["\"You and I shall be twenty-six, Teddy, Beth twenty-four, and Amy twenty-two.", "\"너랑 나는 스물여섯, 테디, 베스는 스물넷, 에이미는 스물둘이겠네."], ["What a venerable party!\"", "정말 점잖은 모임이겠다!\""], ["said Jo.", "조가 말했다."]], [["\"I hope I shall have done something to be proud of by that time; but I'm such a lazy dog, I'm afraid I shall 'dawdle,'", "\"그때쯤이면 자랑스러워할 만한 일을 뭐라도 해 놨으면 좋겠어. 하지만 난 워낙 게으른 놈이라, '빈둥거리고'"], ["Jo.\"", "있을까 봐 걱정이야, 조.\""]], [["\"You need a motive, mother says; and when you get it, she is sure you'll work splendidly.\"", "\"엄마가 그러시는데, 넌 동기가 필요한 거래. 그걸 손에 넣으면 분명히 멋지게 해낼 거라고 확신하셔.\""]], [["\"Is she?", "\"그러셔?"], ["By Jupiter I will, if I only get the chance!\"", "맹세코 해낼 거야, 기회만 잡는다면!\" 로리가 갑자기 기운이 솟아 벌떡 일어나 앉으며 외쳤다."], ["cried Laurie, sitting up with sudden energy.", "\"할아버지를 기쁘게 해 드리는 것만으로 만족해야 마땅하고, 나도 애를 쓰긴 해."], ["\"I ought to be satisfied to please grandfather, and I do try, but it's working against the grain, you see, and comes hard.", "그런데 그게 영 마음과는 어긋나는 일이라 힘이 들어. 할아버지는 당신이 그러셨던 것처럼 나를 인도 무역상으로 만들고 싶어 하시는데, 난 차라리 총 맞는 게 낫겠어."], ["He wants me to be an India merchant, as he was, and I'd rather be shot.", "차나 비단, 향신료 따위, 할아버지의 그 낡은 배들이 실어 오는 온갖 잡동사니가 다 싫어. 그게 내 거가 되면 죄다 바다 밑으로 가라앉아 버리든 말든 상관없어."], ["I hate tea and silk and spices, and every sort of rubbish his old ships bring, and I don't care how soon they go to the bottom when I own them.", "대학에 가는 걸로 만족하셔야 할 텐데. 내가 사 년을 바치면 사업에서는 놓아주셔야 마땅하잖아."], ["Going to college ought to satisfy him, for if I give him four years he ought to let me off from the business; but he's set, and I 've got to do just as he did, unless I break away and please myself, as my father did.", "그런데도 할아버지는 고집을 부리셔. 내가 우리 아버지처럼 뛰쳐나가 내 멋대로 살지 않는 한, 할아버지가 하신 그대로 따라야만 하는 거지."], ["If there was any one left to stay with the old gentleman, I'd do it to-morrow.\"", "할아버지 곁을 지켜 줄 사람이 누구라도 남아 있다면, 난 당장 내일이라도 그렇게 할 거야.\""]], [["Laurie spoke excitedly, and looked ready to carry his threat into execution on the slightest provocation; for he was growing up very fast, and, in spite of his indolent ways, had a young man's hatred of subjection, a young man's restless longing to try the world for himself.", "로리는 흥분해서 말했고, 자그마한 빌미만 생겨도 그 협박을 실행에 옮길 듯한 얼굴이었다. 그는 무척 빠르게 자라고 있었고, 게으른 태도와는 달리 속박을 싫어하는 젊은이의 마음과, 제 힘으로 세상에 부딪쳐 보고 싶어 하는 젊은이의 들뜬 갈망을 품고 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"I advise you to sail away in one of your ships, and never come home again till you have tried your own way,\"", "\"네 배 가운데 하나에 올라타고 멀리 떠나서, 네 방식대로 해 보기 전엔 절대 집에 돌아오지 마.\" 조가 말했다."], ["said Jo, whose imagination was fired by the thought of such a daring exploit, and whose sympathy was excited by what she called \"Teddy's wrongs.\"", "그런 대담한 모험을 떠올리자 상상력에 불이 붙었고, 그녀가 \"테디가 당하는 부당한 일\"이라고 부르는 것 때문에 동정심이 솟았던 것이다."]], [["\"That's not right, Jo; you mustn't talk in that way, and Laurie mustn't take your bad advice.", "\"그건 옳지 않아, 조. 너도 그렇게 말하면 안 되고, 로리도 네 나쁜 충고를 따르면 안 돼."], ["You should do just what your grandfather wishes, my dear boy,\"", "넌 할아버지가 바라시는 대로 해야 해, 얘야.\""], ["said Meg, in her most maternal tone.", "메그가 더없이 어머니다운 목소리로 말했다."], ["\"Do your best at college, and, when he sees that you try to please him, I'm sure he won't be hard or unjust to you.", "\"대학에서 최선을 다하렴. 네가 당신을 기쁘게 하려고 애쓰는 걸 보시면, 분명 너한테 모질거나 부당하게 굴지 않으실 거야."], ["As you say, there is no one else to stay with and love him, and you'd never forgive yourself if you left him without his permission.", "네 말대로 곁에 머물며 할아버지를 사랑해 드릴 사람이 달리 없잖니. 허락도 없이 떠나 버린다면 넌 절대 자신을 용서하지 못할 거야."], ["Don't be dismal or fret, but do your duty; and you'll get your reward, as good Mr.", "우울해하거나 안달하지 말고 네 도리를 다해."], ["Brooke has, by being respected and loved.\"", "그러면 착한 브룩 선생님처럼 존경받고 사랑받는 보답을 얻게 될 거야.\""]], [["\"What do you know about him?\"", "\"넌 그 사람에 대해 뭘 안다고 그래?\""], ["asked Laurie, grateful for the good advice, but objecting to the lecture, and glad to turn the conversation from himself, after his unusual outbreak.", "로리가 물었다. 좋은 충고는 고마웠지만 설교는 마뜩잖았고, 평소답지 않게 속을 터뜨린 뒤라 화제를 자기에게서 돌릴 수 있어 다행이었다."]], [["\"Only what your grandpa told us about him,--how he took good care of his own mother till she died, and wouldn't go abroad as tutor to some nice person, because he wouldn't leave her; and how he provides now for an old woman who nursed his mother; and never tells any one, but is just as generous and patient and good as he can be.\"", "\"네 할아버지가 그분에 대해 우리한테 들려주신 것밖에 몰라. 그분이 어머니가 돌아가실 때까지 정성껏 보살펴 드렸고, 어머니를 두고 떠날 수 없다며 어느 훌륭한 분의 가정 교사로 외국에 가는 것도 거절했다는 거랑, 지금은 자기 어머니를 돌봐 준 노부인을 부양하면서도 누구한테도 그 얘기를 안 하고, 그저 더없이 너그럽고 참을성 있고 착하게 지낸다는 거 말이야.\""]], [["\"So he is, dear old fellow!\"", "\"그래, 정말 그래, 그 좋은 양반!\""], ["said Laurie heartily, as Meg paused, looking flushed and earnest with her story.", "메그가 자기 이야기로 얼굴이 상기되고 진지해진 채 말을 멈추자 로리가 진심으로 말했다."], ["\"It's like grandpa to find out all about him, without letting him know, and to tell all his goodness to others, so that they might like him.", "\"그분 모르게 그분에 대해 다 알아내고, 그분의 착한 마음씨를 남들한테 죄다 얘기해서 사람들이 그분을 좋아하게 만드는 건 우리 할아버지다워."], ["Brooke couldn't understand why your mother was so kind to him, asking him over with me, and treating him in her beautiful friendly way.", "브룩 선생님은 너희 어머니가 왜 자기한테 그렇게 잘해 주시는지, 나랑 함께 초대하고 그 아름답고 다정한 태도로 대해 주시는지 영문을 몰라 하셨어."], ["He thought she was just perfect, and talked about it for days and days, and went on about you all in flaming style.", "너희 어머니가 더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 분이라고 여기시면서 며칠이고 그 얘기를 하셨고, 너희 모두에 대해서도 입에 침이 마르도록 칭찬하셨어."], ["If ever I do get my wish, you see what I'll do for Brooke.\"", "내가 소원을 이루게 되면, 내가 브룩 선생님을 위해 뭘 할지 두고 봐.\""]], [["\"Begin to do something now, by not plaguing his life out,\"", "\"이제부터 뭐라도 시작해, 그분 속을 그만 썩이는 것부터.\""], ["said Meg sharply.", "메그가 날카롭게 말했다."]], [["\"How do you know I do, miss?\"", "\"내가 그런다는 걸 어떻게 알아, 아가씨?\""]], [["\"I can always tell by his face, when he goes away.", "\"난 그분이 돌아가실 때 얼굴만 보면 늘 알 수 있어."], ["If you have been good, he looks satisfied and walks briskly; if you have plagued him, he's sober and walks slowly, as if he wanted to go back and do his work better.\"", "네가 착하게 굴었으면 만족스러운 표정으로 활기차게 걸어가시고, 네가 속을 썩였으면 침울한 얼굴로 천천히 걸어가시거든, 마치 되돌아가서 자기 일을 더 잘하고 싶다는 듯이.\""]], [["\"Well, I like that!", "\"이거 참 어이없네!"], ["So you keep an account of my good and bad marks in Brooke's face, do you?", "그러니까 네가 브룩 선생님 얼굴에서 내 잘잘못 점수를 매겨 둔다, 이거지?"], ["I see him bow and smile as he passes your window, but I didn't know you'd got up a telegraph.\"", "그분이 너희 집 창문 앞을 지나며 인사하고 미소 짓는 건 봤지만, 너희가 그런 전신기를 차려 놓은 줄은 몰랐는걸.\""]], [["\"We haven't; don't be angry, and oh, don't tell him I said anything!", "\"그런 건 없어. 화내지 말고, 아 제발, 내가 무슨 말 했다고 그분한테 이르진 마!"], ["It was only to show that I cared how you get on, and what is said here is said in confidence, you know,\"", "그저 네가 어떻게 지내는지 내가 마음 쓴다는 걸 보이려던 것뿐이야. 그리고 여기서 한 말은 비밀로 한 거라는 거 알잖아.\""], ["cried Meg, much alarmed at the thought of what might follow from her careless speech.", "메그가 자기 경솔한 말에서 뭐가 비롯될지 생각하며 몹시 당황해 외쳤다."]], [["\"_I_ don't tell tales,\"", "\"난 고자질 같은 거 안 해.\""], ["replied Laurie, with his \"high and mighty\" air, as Jo called a certain expression which he occasionally wore.", "로리가 조가 이따금 그가 짓는 어떤 표정을 두고 \"거만하고 도도하다\"라고 부르던 바로 그 태도로 대답했다."], ["\"Only if Brooke is going to be a thermometer, I must mind and have fair weather for him to report.\"", "\"다만 브룩 선생님이 온도계 노릇을 할 작정이라면, 나도 정신 차리고 그분이 좋은 날씨를 보고할 수 있게 해 드려야겠군.\""]], [["\"Please don't be offended.", "\"제발 기분 상해하지 마."], ["I didn't mean to preach or tell tales or be silly; I only thought Jo was encouraging you in a feeling which you'd be sorry for, by and by.", "난 설교를 하거나 고자질을 하거나 어리석게 굴 생각은 없었어. 그저 조가 네가 나중에 후회할 마음을 부추기는 것 같아서 그랬어."], ["You are so kind to us, we feel as if you were our brother, and say just what we think.", "넌 우리한테 어찌나 잘해 주는지, 우린 네가 우리 오빠 같아서 생각하는 대로 다 말하는 거야."], ["Forgive me, I meant it kindly.\"", "용서해 줘, 좋은 뜻으로 한 말이었어.\""], ["And Meg offered her hand with a gesture both affectionate and timid.", "메그가 다정하면서도 머뭇거리는 몸짓으로 손을 내밀었다."]], [["Ashamed of his momentary pique, Laurie squeezed the kind little hand, and said frankly, \"I'm the one to be forgiven; I'm cross, and have been out of sorts all day. I like to have you tell me my faults and be sisterly, so don't mind if I am grumpy sometimes; I thank you all the same.\"", "순간의 짜증이 부끄러워진 로리는 그 친절한 작은 손을 꼭 쥐고 솔직하게 말했다. \"용서받아야 할 사람은 나야. 내가 까칠하게 굴었고, 하루 종일 심사가 뒤틀려 있었거든. 난 네가 내 결점을 말해 주고 누나처럼 대해 주는 게 좋아. 그러니 내가 가끔 투덜대더라도 신경 쓰지 마, 어쨌든 고마워.\""]], [["Bent on showing that he was not offended, he made himself as agreeable as possible,--wound cotton for Meg, recited poetry to please Jo, shook down cones for Beth, and helped Amy with her ferns, proving himself a fit person to belong to the \"Busy Bee Society.\"", "기분이 상하지 않았다는 걸 보여 주려고 단단히 마음먹은 그는 더없이 싹싹하게 굴었다. 메그를 위해 무명실을 감아 주고, 조를 즐겁게 하려고 시를 읊고, 베스를 위해 솔방울을 흔들어 떨어뜨리고, 에이미의 고사리 작업을 거들면서, 자신이 '부지런한 꿀벌 모임'에 들어올 만한 사람임을 증명했다."], ["In the midst of an animated discussion on the domestic habits of turtles (one of those amiable creatures having strolled up from the river), the faint sound of a bell warned them that Hannah had put the tea \"to draw,\"", "거북의 생활 습성에 대해 한창 활기차게 토론하던 중에(그 온순한 녀석 한 마리가 강에서 어슬렁어슬렁 올라온 참이었다), 어렴풋한 종소리가 들려와 해나가 차를 \"우려\""], ["and they would just have time to get home to supper.", "놓았으니 저녁을 먹으러 집에 갈 시간이 빠듯하다는 걸 일러 주었다."]], [["\"May I come again?\"", "\"또 와도 돼?\""], ["asked Laurie.", "로리가 물었다."]], [["\"Yes, if you are good, and love your book, as the boys in the primer are told to do,\"", "\"응, 입문서 속 소년들이 들은 대로, 네가 착하게 굴고 네 책을 사랑한다면.\""], ["said Meg smiling.", "메그가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["\"I'll try.\"", "\"노력할게.\""]], [["\"Then you may come, and I'll teach you to knit as the Scotchmen do; there's a demand for socks just now,\"", "\"그럼 와도 좋아. 그리고 내가 스코틀랜드 사람들처럼 뜨개질하는 법을 가르쳐 줄게."], ["added Jo, waving hers, like a big blue worsted banner, as they parted at the gate.", "요즘 양말이 잘 나가거든.\" 조가 헤어지는 문간에서, 자기 뜨개질감을 커다란 파란 털실 깃발처럼 흔들며 덧붙였다."]], [["That night, when Beth played to Mr. Laurence in the twilight, Laurie, standing in the shadow of the curtain, listened to the little David, whose simple music always quieted his moody spirit, and watched the old man, who sat with his gray head on his hand, thinking tender thoughts of the dead child he had loved so much. Remembering the conversation of the afternoon, the boy said to himself, with the resolve to make the sacrifice cheerfully, \"I'll let my castle go, and stay with the dear old gentleman while he needs me, for I am all he has.\"", "그날 밤, 베스가 황혼 녘에 로렌스 씨에게 피아노를 연주해 드릴 때, 로리는 커튼 그늘에 서서 그 어린 다윗의 연주에 귀를 기울였다. 그 소박한 음악은 언제나 그의 침울한 마음을 가라앉혀 주었으니까. 그리고 그는 백발의 머리를 한 손에 괴고 앉아, 그토록 사랑했던 죽은 아이를 다정하게 떠올리는 노인을 바라보았다. 오후의 대화를 떠올리며, 그 희생을 기꺼이 치르겠다는 다짐을 품고 소년은 속으로 말했다. '내 누각은 포기하자. 그리고 저 다정한 노인이 나를 필요로 하시는 한 곁을 지키자. 내가 할아버지에게는 전부니까.' 비밀"]], [["Jo was very busy in the garret, for the October days began to grow chilly, and the afternoons were short.", "조는 다락방에서 무척 바빴다. 10월의 날들이 쌀쌀해지기 시작했고 오후가 짧아졌기 때문이다."], ["For two or three hours the sun lay warmly in the high window, showing Jo seated on the old sofa, writing busily, with her papers spread out upon a trunk before her, while Scrabble, the pet rat, promenaded the beams overhead, accompanied by his oldest son, a fine young fellow, who was evidently very proud of his whiskers.", "두세 시간 동안 높다란 창으로 햇살이 따스하게 들어와, 낡은 소파에 앉아 부지런히 글을 쓰는 조를 비추었다. 그녀 앞 트렁크 위에는 원고가 펼쳐져 있었고, 머리 위 들보에서는 애완 쥐 스크래블이 산책을 했는데, 자기 수염을 무척 자랑스러워하는 게 분명한 훤칠한 맏아들을 거느리고 있었다."], ["Quite absorbed in her work, Jo scribbled away till the last page was filled, when she signed her name with a flourish, and threw down her pen, exclaiming,--", "일에 푹 빠진 조는 마지막 장이 다 채워질 때까지 끄적거리다가, 자기 이름을 멋들어지게 휘갈겨 서명하고는 펜을 내던지며 외쳤다."]], [["\"There, I've done my best!", "\"자, 최선을 다했어!"], ["If this won't suit I shall have to wait till I can do better.\"", "이걸로 안 되면 더 잘 쓸 수 있을 때까지 기다려야겠지.\""]], [["Lying back on the sofa, she read the manuscript carefully through, making dashes here and there, and putting in many exclamation points, which looked like little balloons; then she tied it up with a smart red ribbon, and sat a minute looking at it with a sober, wistful expression, which plainly showed how earnest her work had been.", "소파에 등을 기대고 누워, 그녀는 원고를 처음부터 끝까지 꼼꼼히 읽으며 여기저기 줄을 긋고, 작은 풍선처럼 보이는 느낌표를 잔뜩 집어넣었다. 그러고는 산뜻한 빨간 리본으로 원고를 묶고는, 잠시 진지하고 간절한 표정으로 그것을 바라보며 앉아 있었는데, 그 표정에는 그녀의 작업이 얼마나 진심이었는지가 고스란히 드러나 있었다."], ["Jo's desk up here was an old tin kitchen, which hung against the wall.", "이 위쪽에 있는 조의 책상은 벽에 걸린 낡은 양철 부엌 살림이었다."], ["In it she kept her papers and a few books, safely shut away from Scrabble, who, being likewise of a literary turn, was fond of making a circulating library of such books as were left in his way, by eating the leaves.", "그녀는 그 안에 원고와 책 몇 권을 넣어, 스크래블에게서 안전하게 닫아 두었다. 그 녀석도 문학적인 취향이 있는 터라, 손닿는 곳에 있는 책이라면 그 책장을 갉아 먹어 일종의 순회 도서관으로 삼기를 즐겼던 것이다."], ["From this tin receptacle Jo produced another manuscript; and, putting both in her pocket, crept quietly down stairs, leaving her friends to nibble her pens and taste her ink.", "조는 이 양철 보관함에서 또 다른 원고를 꺼냈고, 둘 다 주머니에 넣은 다음, 자기 친구들이 펜을 갉고 잉크를 맛보도록 내버려 둔 채 조용히 아래층으로 살금살금 내려갔다."]], [["She put on her hat and jacket as noiselessly as possible, and, going to the back entry window, got out upon the roof of a low porch, swung herself down to the grassy bank, and took a roundabout way to the road.", "그녀는 되도록 소리를 내지 않고 모자와 재킷을 걸친 다음, 뒤쪽 통로 창으로 가서 낮은 현관 지붕 위로 빠져나가, 풀이 우거진 둑으로 몸을 흔들어 내려서서는 빙 둘러 가는 길로 큰길에 이르렀다."], ["Once there, she composed herself, hailed a passing omnibus, and rolled away to town, looking very merry and mysterious.", "일단 거기 이르자 그녀는 마음을 가다듬고 지나가는 승합 마차를 불러 세워 시내로 굴러갔는데, 무척 즐겁고도 알 수 없는 표정이었다."]], [["If any one had been watching her, he would have thought her movements decidedly peculiar; for, on alighting, she went off at a great pace till she reached a certain number in a certain busy street; having found the place with some difficulty, she went into the door-way, looked up the dirty stairs, and, after standing stock still a minute, suddenly dived into the street, and walked away as rapidly as she came.", "누가 그녀를 지켜보았다면 그 행동이 사뭇 별나다고 여겼을 것이다. 마차에서 내린 그녀는 어느 번잡한 거리의 어떤 번지에 이를 때까지 빠른 걸음으로 내달았다."], ["This manœuvre she repeated several times, to the great amusement of a black-eyed young gentleman lounging in the window of a building opposite.", "어렵사리 그곳을 찾아낸 그녀는 출입구로 들어가 지저분한 계단을 올려다보더니, 잠시 꼼짝 않고 서 있다가 별안간 거리로 뛰쳐나와 왔던 것만큼이나 빠르게 걸어가 버렸다."], ["On returning for the third time, Jo gave herself a shake, pulled her hat over her eyes, and walked up the stairs, looking as if she were going to have all her teeth out.", "이런 동작을 그녀는 몇 번이나 되풀이했고, 맞은편 건물 창가에서 빈둥거리던 검은 눈의 젊은 신사가 그것을 무척 재미있어했다. 세 번째로 되돌아왔을 때, 조는 몸을 한 번 부르르 떨고는 모자를 눈 위로 푹 눌러쓰고서, 마치 이를 죄다 뽑으러 가는 사람 같은 얼굴로 계단을 올라갔다."]], [["There was a dentist's sign, among others, which adorned the entrance, and, after staring a moment at the pair of artificial jaws which slowly opened and shut to draw attention to a fine set of teeth, the young gentleman put on his coat, took his hat, and went down to post himself in the opposite door-way, saying, with a smile and a shiver,--", "출입구를 장식한 여러 간판 가운데 치과 간판이 하나 있었다. 멋진 치아 한 벌에 눈길을 끌려고 천천히 벌어졌다 닫혔다 하는 인공 턱 한 쌍을 잠시 빤히 쳐다본 뒤, 그 젊은 신사는 외투를 걸치고 모자를 집어 들더니 아래로 내려가 맞은편 출입구에 자리를 잡았다. 그러고는 미소를 지으면서도 몸서리를 치며 말했다."]], [["\"It's like her to come alone, but if she has a bad time she'll need some one to help her home.\"", "\"혼자 오는 게 그 애답지. 하지만 힘든 일을 겪고 나면 집까지 데려다줄 사람이 필요할 거야.\""]], [["In ten minutes Jo came running down stairs with a very red face, and the general appearance of a person who had just passed through a trying ordeal of some sort.", "십 분 뒤, 조가 얼굴이 새빨개져서는, 무슨 고된 시련이라도 막 치르고 나온 사람 같은 행색으로 계단을 뛰어 내려왔다."], ["When she saw the young gentleman she looked anything but pleased, and passed him with a nod; but he followed, asking with an air of sympathy,--", "젊은 신사를 보자 그녀는 도무지 반갑지 않은 표정으로 고개만 까딱하고 지나쳤지만, 그는 동정하는 기색으로 따라오며 물었다."]], [["\"Did you have a bad time?\"", "\"많이 힘들었어?\""]], [["\"Not very.\"", "\"별로.\""]], [["\"You got through quickly.\"", "\"빨리 끝났네.\""]], [["\"Yes, thank goodness!\"", "\"응, 다행히도!\""]], [["\"Why did you go alone?\"", "\"왜 혼자 갔어?\""]], [["\"Didn't want any one to know.\"", "\"아무도 몰랐으면 해서.\""]], [["\"You're the oddest fellow I ever saw.", "\"넌 내가 본 사람 중에 제일 별난 애야."], ["How many did you have out?\"", "몇 개나 뽑았어?\""]], [["Jo looked at her friend as if she did not understand him; then began to laugh, as if mightily amused at something.", "조는 무슨 말인지 모르겠다는 듯이 친구를 바라보다가, 무언가가 어지간히 우습다는 듯 웃기 시작했다."]], [["\"There are two which I want to have come out, but I must wait a week.\"", "\"뽑아냈으면 하는 게 두 개 있긴 한데, 일주일은 기다려야 해.\""]], [["\"What are you laughing at?", "\"뭐가 그렇게 우스워?"], ["You are up to some mischief, Jo,\"", "너 무슨 꿍꿍이가 있구나, 조.\""], ["said Laurie, looking mystified.", "로리가 어리둥절한 표정으로 말했다."]], [["\"So are you.", "\"너야말로 그렇지."], ["What were you doing, sir, up in that billiard saloon?\"", "거기 위 당구장에서 뭐 하고 있었어, 도련님?\""]], [["\"Begging your pardon, ma'am, it wasn't a billiard saloon, but a gymnasium, and I was taking a lesson in fencing.\"", "\"실례지만, 아가씨, 거긴 당구장이 아니라 체육관이었고, 난 펜싱 수업을 받고 있었답니다.\""]], [["\"I'm glad of that.\"", "\"그거 다행이네.\""]], [["\"Why?\"", "\"왜?\""]], [["\"You can teach me, and then when we play Hamlet, you can be Laertes, and we'll make a fine thing of the fencing scene.\"", "\"네가 나한테 가르쳐 주면, 우리가 햄릿을 공연할 때 네가 레어티스를 맡아서 펜싱 장면을 멋지게 만들 수 있잖아.\""]], [["Laurie burst out with a hearty boy's laugh, which made several passers-by smile in spite of themselves.", "로리가 소년다운 호탕한 웃음을 터뜨리자, 지나가던 사람 몇몇이 자기도 모르게 미소를 지었다."]], [["\"I'll teach you whether we play Hamlet or not; it's grand fun, and will straighten you up capitally.", "\"햄릿을 공연하든 안 하든 내가 가르쳐 줄게. 정말 신나는 데다 네 자세도 멋지게 곧게 펴 줄 거야."], ["But I don't believe that was your only reason for saying 'I'm glad,' in that decided way; was it, now?\"", "그런데 네가 그렇게 단호하게 '다행이네'라고 한 게 그 이유만은 아닌 것 같은데, 안 그래?\""]], [["\"No, I was glad that you were not in the saloon, because I hope you never go to such places.", "\"응, 난 네가 당구장에 없었던 게 다행이었어. 네가 그런 데는 절대 가지 않길 바라거든."], ["Do you?\"", "가니?\""]], [["\"Not often.\"", "\"자주는 안 가.\""]], [["\"I wish you wouldn't.\"", "\"안 갔으면 좋겠어.\""]], [["\"It's no harm, Jo.", "\"별일 아니야, 조."], ["I have billiards at home, but it's no fun unless you have good players; so, as I'm fond of it, I come sometimes and have a game with Ned Moffat or some of the other fellows.\"", "우리 집에도 당구대가 있지만, 잘 치는 상대가 없으면 재미가 없거든. 그래서 난 당구를 좋아하니까, 가끔 가서 네드 모팻이나 다른 친구들이랑 한판 치는 거야.\""]], [["\"Oh dear, I'm so sorry, for you'll get to liking it better and better, and will waste time and money, and grow like those dreadful boys.", "\"어머, 어쩌나, 정말 안타까워. 그러다 점점 더 좋아하게 돼서 시간이랑 돈을 낭비하고, 그 끔찍한 애들처럼 돼 버릴 거 아냐."], ["I did hope you'd stay respectable, and be a satisfaction to your friends,\"", "난 정말 네가 점잖게 지내면서 친구들한테 기쁨이 돼 주길 바랐는데.\""], ["said Jo, shaking her head.", "조가 고개를 저으며 말했다."]], [["\"Can't a fellow take a little innocent amusement now and then without losing his respectability?\"", "\"가끔 죄 없는 즐길 거리 좀 즐긴다고 사내가 점잖음을 잃기야 하겠어?\""], ["asked Laurie, looking nettled.", "로리가 발끈한 표정으로 물었다."]], [["\"That depends upon how and where he takes it.", "\"그건 어떻게, 어디서 즐기느냐에 달렸지."], ["I don't like Ned and his set, and wish you'd keep out of it.", "난 네드랑 그 패거리가 싫어. 그러니 너도 거기 끼지 않았으면 좋겠어."], ["Mother won't let us have him at our house, though he wants to come; and if you grow like him she won't be willing to have us frolic together as we do now.\"", "엄마는 네드가 우리 집에 오고 싶어 해도 못 오게 하셔. 그리고 네가 그 애처럼 되면, 엄마는 우리가 지금처럼 너랑 어울려 노는 걸 더는 허락하지 않으실 거야.\""]], [["\"Won't she?\"", "\"안 하실까?\""], ["asked Laurie anxiously.", "로리가 걱정스레 물었다."]], [["\"No, she can't bear fashionable young men, and she'd shut us all up in bandboxes rather than have us associate with them.\"", "\"응, 엄마는 멋만 부리는 젊은 남자들을 못 견뎌 하셔. 우리가 그런 애들이랑 어울리게 하느니 우리를 모자 상자 속에 죄다 가둬 버리실걸.\""]], [["\"Well, she needn't get out her bandboxes yet; I'm not a fashionable party, and don't mean to be; but I do like harmless larks now and then, don't you?\"", "\"그래도 아직 모자 상자를 꺼내실 필요는 없어. 난 멋만 부리는 부류도 아니고, 그럴 생각도 없으니까. 다만 가끔 해롭지 않은 장난은 좋아해, 너도 그렇잖아?\""]], [["\"Yes, nobody minds them, so lark away, but don't get wild, will you?", "\"응, 그건 아무도 뭐라 안 하니까 실컷 장난쳐도 돼. 하지만 막 나가진 않을 거지?"], ["or there will be an end of all our good times.\"", "안 그러면 우리 좋은 시간도 다 끝나 버릴 테니까.\""]], [["\"I'll be a double-distilled saint.\"", "\"난 두 번 정제한 성인군자가 될게.\""]], [["\"I can't bear saints: just be a simple, honest, respectable boy, and we'll never desert you.", "\"난 성인군자는 질색이야. 그저 소박하고 정직하고 점잖은 남자애가 돼 줘."], ["I don't know what I _should_ do if you acted like Mr.", "그럼 우린 절대 널 버리지 않을 테니까. 네가 킹 씨네 아들처럼 군다면 난 어떻게 해야 할지 정말 모르겠어."], ["King's son; he had plenty of money, but didn't know how to spend it, and got tipsy and gambled, and ran away, and forged his father's name, I believe, and was altogether horrid.\"", "그 애는 돈은 넘쳐 났지만 쓸 줄을 몰라서, 술에 절고 노름을 하고 도망쳤다가, 아버지 이름을 위조하기까지 했다나 봐. 어쨌든 아주 형편없었대.\""]], [["\"You think I'm likely to do the same?", "\"넌 내가 그렇게 될 것 같아?"], ["Much obliged.\"", "참 고맙기도 하다.\""]], [["\"No, I don't--oh, _dear_, no!--but I hear people talking about money being such a temptation, and I sometimes wish you were poor; I shouldn't worry then.\"", "\"아니, 그렇진 않아. 어머, 세상에, 절대 아니지! 다만 사람들이 돈이 그렇게 큰 유혹이라고 떠드는 걸 들으면, 난 가끔 네가 차라리 가난했으면 싶어. 그럼 걱정 안 할 텐데.\""]], [["\"Do you worry about me, Jo?\"", "\"넌 내 걱정을 하는 거야, 조?\""]], [["\"A little, when you look moody or discontented, as you sometimes do; for you've got such a strong will, if you once get started wrong, I'm afraid it would be hard to stop you.\"", "『난 가끔 네가 침울하거나 불만스러운 표정을 지을 때면 조금 걱정이 돼. 넌 의지가 워낙 강해서, 한번 잘못된 길로 들어서면 멈추게 하기가 어려울 것 같거든.』"]], [["Laurie walked in silence a few minutes, and Jo watched him, wishing she had held her tongue, for his eyes looked angry, though his lips still smiled as if at her warnings.", "로리는 잠시 말없이 걸었고, 조는 괜히 입을 놀렸다 싶어 후회하면서 그를 지켜보았다. 그의 입가에는 그녀의 충고를 비웃기라도 하듯 미소가 걸려 있었지만, 눈빛은 화가 나 보였다."]], [["\"Are you going to deliver lectures all the way home?\" he asked presently.", "『집까지 가는 내내 설교만 늘어놓을 작정이야?』 이윽고 그가 물었다."]], [["\"Of course not; why?\"", "『그럴 리가. 왜?』"]], [["\"Because if you are, I'll take a 'bus; if you are not, I'd like to walk with you, and tell you something very interesting.\"", "『그럴 거면 난 합승마차를 탈 거고, 아니라면 너랑 같이 걸으면서 아주 재미있는 얘길 하나 들려줄게.』"]], [["\"I won't preach any more, and I'd like to hear the news immensely.\"", "『더는 설교 안 할 테니까, 그 얘기 무지하게 듣고 싶다.』"]], [["\"Very well, then; come on.", "『좋아, 그럼. 가자."], ["It's a secret, and if I tell you, you must tell me yours.\"", "비밀인데, 내가 말해 주면 너도 네 비밀을 말해 줘야 해.』"]], [["\"I haven't got any,\"", "『난 비밀 같은 거 없는데.』 조가 입을 떼다 말고 뚝 멈췄다."], ["began Jo, but stopped suddenly, remembering that she had.", "자기에게도 비밀이 있다는 사실이 떠올랐던 것이다."]], [["\"You know you have,--you can't hide anything; so up and 'fess, or I won't tell,\"", "『있으면서. 넌 아무것도 못 숨겨."], ["cried Laurie.", "그러니까 어서 털어놔, 안 그러면 나도 말 안 할 테니까.』 로리가 소리쳤다."]], [["\"Is your secret a nice one?\"", "『네 비밀은 괜찮은 거야?』"]], [["\"Oh, isn't it!", "『그럼, 끝내주지!"], ["all about people you know, and such fun!", "네가 아는 사람들 얘기라 얼마나 재밌다고!"], ["You ought to hear it, and I've been aching to tell it this long time.", "넌 꼭 들어야 해. 나도 이걸 말하고 싶어서 한참을 좀이 쑤셨거든."], ["Come, you begin.\"", "자, 네가 먼저.』"]], [["\"You'll not say anything about it at home, will you?\"", "『집에 가서 아무 말도 안 할 거지?』\n『한마디도 안 해.』"]], [["\"Not a word.\" \"And you won't tease me in private?\"", "『그리고 둘만 있을 때 날 놀려먹지도 않을 거지?』"]], [["\"I never tease.\"", "『난 놀려먹은 적 없어.』"]], [["\"Yes, you do; you get everything you want out of people.", "『있거든. 넌 사람들한테서 원하는 건 죄다 끄집어내잖아."], ["I don't know how you do it, but you are a born wheedler.\"", "어떻게 하는지는 모르겠지만, 넌 타고난 살살이야.』"]], [["\"Thank you; fire away.\"", "『고맙군. 어서 말해 봐.』"]], [["\"Well, I've left two stories with a newspaper man, and he's to give his answer next week,\" whispered Jo, in her confidant's ear.", "『그게, 내가 신문사 사람한테 소설을 두 편 맡겼는데, 다음 주에 답을 준대.』 조가 비밀을 털어놓는 상대의 귀에 대고 속삭였다."]], [["\"Hurrah for Miss March, the celebrated American authoress!\"", "『미국의 저명한 여류 작가, 마치 양 만세!』 로리가 모자를 하늘로 던졌다가 다시 받으며 외쳤다."], ["cried Laurie, throwing up his hat and catching it again, to the great delight of two ducks, four cats, five hens, and half a dozen Irish children; for they were out of the city now.", "그 바람에 오리 두 마리, 고양이 네 마리, 암탉 다섯 마리, 그리고 아일랜드 아이들 여섯 명이 신이 났다. 두 사람은 어느새 도시를 벗어나 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"Hush!", "『쉿! 아마 아무 일도 안 될 거야."], ["It won't come to anything, I dare say; but I couldn't rest till I had tried, and I said nothing about it, because I didn't want any one else to be disappointed.\"", "그래도 한번 해 보지 않고는 견딜 수가 없었어. 누구한테도 말 안 한 건, 괜히 다른 사람까지 실망하는 게 싫어서야.』"]], [["\"It won't fail.", "『실패할 리 없어."], ["Why, Jo, your stories are works of Shakespeare, compared to half the rubbish that is published every day.", "조, 매일같이 쏟아지는 그 쓰레기 같은 글들 절반에 비하면, 네 소설은 셰익스피어 작품이라고."], ["Won't it be fun to see them in print; and sha'n't we feel proud of our authoress?\"", "활자로 박혀 나온 걸 보면 얼마나 재밌을까. 우리 여류 작가가 얼마나 자랑스러울까!』"]], [["Jo's eyes sparkled, for it is always pleasant to be believed in; and a friend's praise is always sweeter than a dozen newspaper puffs.", "조의 눈이 반짝였다. 누군가 자기를 믿어 준다는 건 언제나 기분 좋은 일이고, 친구의 칭찬은 신문에 실린 수십 마디 찬사보다 늘 더 달콤한 법이다."]], [["\"Where's _your_ secret?", "『그래서 네 비밀은?"], ["Play fair, Teddy, or I'll never believe you again,\"", "공평하게 해, 테디."], ["she said, trying to extinguish the brilliant hopes that blazed up at a word of encouragement.", "안 그러면 다신 널 안 믿을 거야.』 조는 격려의 말 한마디에 활활 타오른 눈부신 희망을 애써 꺼뜨리며 말했다."]], [["\"I may get into a scrape for telling; but I didn't promise not to, so I will, for I never feel easy in my mind till I've told you any plummy bit of news I get.", "『이걸 말하면 나 곤란해질지도 몰라. 그래도 말 안 하겠다고 약속한 적은 없으니까 할게."], ["I know where Meg's glove is.\"", "솔깃한 소식을 들으면 너한테 털어놓기 전엔 영 마음이 편치 않거든. 나 메그 누나 장갑이 어디 있는지 알아.』"]], [["\"Is that all?\" said Jo, looking disappointed, as Laurie nodded and twinkled, with a face full of mysterious intelligence.", "『그게 다야?』 로리가 알 듯 말 듯 신비로운 표정을 잔뜩 짓고서 고개를 끄덕이며 눈을 반짝이자, 조는 실망한 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["\"It's quite enough for the present, as you'll agree when I tell you where it is.\"", "『지금으로선 그걸로 충분해. 장갑이 어디 있는지 들으면 너도 그렇게 생각할걸.』"]], [["\"Tell, then.\"", "『그럼 말해 봐.』"]], [["Laurie bent, and whispered three words in Jo's ear, which produced a comical change.", "로리는 몸을 숙여 조의 귀에 세 마디를 속삭였고, 그러자 우스꽝스러운 변화가 일어났다."], ["She stood and stared at him for a minute, looking both surprised and displeased, then walked on, saying sharply, \"How do you know?\"", "조는 잠시 그 자리에 멈춰 서서 놀라움과 못마땅함이 뒤섞인 얼굴로 그를 빤히 쳐다보다가, 다시 걸음을 옮기며 날카롭게 물었다. 『어떻게 알았어?』"]], [["\"Saw it.\"", "『봤거든.』"]], [["\"Where?\"", "『어디서?』"]], [["\"Pocket.\"", "『주머니에서.』"]], [["\"All this time?\"", "『그동안 내내?』"]], [["\"Yes; isn't that romantic?\"", "『응. 낭만적이지 않아?』"]], [["\"No, it's horrid.\"", "『아니, 끔찍해.』"]], [["\"Don't you like it?\"", "『맘에 안 들어?』"]], [["\"Of course I don't.", "『당연하지. 말도 안 돼."], ["It's ridiculous; it won't be allowed.", "그런 건 허락할 수 없어. 세상에!"], ["My patience! what would Meg say?\"", "메그 언니가 알면 뭐라고 하겠어?』"]], [["\"You are not to tell any one; mind that.\"", "『아무한테도 말하면 안 돼. 명심해.』"]], [["\"I didn't promise.\"", "『나 약속 안 했어.』"]], [["\"That was understood, and I trusted you.\"", "『그건 당연한 거고, 난 너를 믿었어.』"]], [["\"Well, I won't for the present, any way; but I'm disgusted, and wish you hadn't told me.\"", "『뭐, 일단 지금은 말 안 할게. 그래도 정말 역겨워. 차라리 말해 주지 말걸 그랬어.』"]], [["\"I thought you'd be pleased.\"", "『난 네가 좋아할 줄 알았지.』"]], [["\"At the idea of anybody coming to take Meg away?", "『누가 메그 언니를 데려가려 한다는 생각에?"], ["No, thank you.\"", "사양하겠어.』"]], [["\"You'll feel better about it when somebody comes to take you away.\"", "『누군가 널 데려가려 하면 그땐 기분이 좀 나아질걸.』"]], [["\"I'd like to see any one try it,\" cried Jo fiercely.", "『누가 한번 그래 보라지.』 조가 사납게 외쳤다."]], [["\"So should I!\" and Laurie chuckled at the idea.", "『나도 그게 보고 싶군!』 로리가 그 생각에 킥킥거렸다."]], [["\"I don't think secrets agree with me; I feel rumpled up in my mind since you told me that,\"", "『난 비밀이랑 영 안 맞나 봐."], ["said Jo, rather ungratefully.", "네가 그 얘기 한 뒤로 마음이 다 구겨진 기분이야.』 조가 다소 고마운 줄도 모르고 말했다."]], [["\"Race down this hill with me, and you'll be all right,\"", "『나랑 이 언덕 아래까지 달리기 시합하자."], ["suggested Laurie.", "그럼 괜찮아질 거야.』 로리가 제안했다."]], [["No one was in sight; the smooth road sloped invitingly before her; and finding the temptation irresistible, Jo darted away, soon leaving hat and comb behind her, and scattering hair-pins as she ran. Laurie reached the goal first, and was quite satisfied with the success of his treatment; for his Atalanta came panting up, with flying hair, bright eyes, ruddy cheeks, and no signs of dissatisfaction in her face.", "주위엔 아무도 없었다. 매끄러운 길이 눈앞으로 비스듬히 뻗어 있어 절로 마음을 끌었다. 도저히 뿌리칠 수 없는 유혹에 조는 쏜살같이 내달렸고, 금세 모자와 빗을 뒤에 떨어뜨린 채 머리핀까지 흩뿌리며 달렸다. 로리가 먼저 결승점에 닿았고, 자기 처방이 성공한 데 무척 흡족해했다. 그의 아탈란타가 헝클어진 머리를 날리며, 눈을 빛내고 두 뺨이 발그레해진 채로, 얼굴에 불만이라곤 조금도 없이 헐떡이며 다가왔으니 말이다."]], [["\"I wish I was a horse; then I could run for miles in this splendid air, and not lose my breath.", "『내가 말이었으면 좋겠어. 그럼 이 멋진 공기 속을 몇 마일이고 달려도 숨이 안 찰 텐데."], ["It was capital; but see what a guy it's made me.", "정말 끝내줬어."], ["Go, pick up my things, like a cherub as you are,\"", "그런데 이것 봐, 내 꼴이 영 우습게 됐잖아."], ["said Jo, dropping down under a maple-tree, which was carpeting the bank with crimson leaves.", "가서 내 물건들 좀 주워 와, 천사 같은 녀석.』 조는 단풍나무 아래 털썩 주저앉으며 말했다. 그 나무는 진홍빛 잎으로 둑을 카펫처럼 뒤덮고 있었다."]], [["Laurie leisurely departed to recover the lost property, and Jo bundled up her braids, hoping no one would pass by till she was tidy again.", "로리는 잃어버린 물건들을 찾으러 느긋하게 떠났고, 조는 다시 단정해질 때까지 아무도 지나가지 않기를 바라며 땋은 머리를 둘둘 말아 올렸다."], ["But some one did pass, and who should it be but Meg, looking particularly ladylike in her state and festival suit, for she had been making calls.", "그런데 정말로 누군가 지나갔으니, 다름 아닌 메그였다. 외출했다 오는 길이라, 나들이옷에 외출복까지 차려입은 메그는 유난히 숙녀다워 보였다."]], [["\"What in the world are you doing here?\" she asked, regarding her dishevelled sister with well-bred surprise.", "『대체 여기서 뭐 하는 거야?』 메그가 헝클어진 동생을 보고 점잖게 놀라며 물었다."]], [["\"Getting leaves,\" meekly answered Jo, sorting the rosy handful she had just swept up.", "『나뭇잎 줍고 있어.』 조가 방금 쓸어 모은 발그레한 한 줌을 추리며 얌전히 대답했다."]], [["\"And hair-pins,\" added Laurie, throwing half a dozen into Jo's lap.", "『머리핀도요.』 로리가 머리핀 여섯 개쯤을 조의 무릎에 던지며 덧붙였다."], ["\"They grow on this road, Meg; so do combs and brown straw hats.\"", "『이 길에선 이런 게 자라나거든요, 메그 누나. 빗이랑 갈색 밀짚모자도요.』"]], [["\"You have been running, Jo; how could you?", "『너 또 달렸구나, 조. 어떻게 그럴 수 있어?"], ["When _will_ you stop such romping ways?\" said Meg reprovingly, as she settled her cuffs, and smoothed her hair, with which the wind had taken liberties.", "그 우당탕거리는 버릇은 대체 언제 그만둘 거야?』 메그가 나무라듯 말하며, 바람에 흐트러진 머리를 매만지고 소맷부리를 단정히 했다."]], [["\"Never till I'm stiff and old, and have to use a crutch.", "『뻣뻣하게 늙어서 지팡이 짚을 때까지는 안 그만둘 거야. 날 제 나이보다 빨리 어른으로 만들려고 하지 마, 언니."], ["Don't try to make me grow up before my time, Meg: it's hard enough to have you change all of a sudden; let me be a little girl as long as I can.\"", "언니가 이렇게 갑자기 변하는 것만으로도 충분히 힘들단 말이야. 그러니까 나는 할 수 있는 한 오래오래 어린애로 있게 해 줘.』"]], [["As she spoke, Jo bent over the leaves to hide the trembling of her lips; for lately she had felt that Margaret was fast getting to be a woman, and Laurie's secret made her dread the separation which must surely come some time, and now seemed very near.", "말하면서 조는 떨리는 입술을 감추려고 나뭇잎 위로 몸을 숙였다. 요즘 들어 마거릿이 빠르게 어엿한 여인이 되어 가는 걸 느끼던 차에, 로리의 비밀까지 듣고 나니 언젠가 반드시 닥칠, 그리고 이제는 무척 가까워 보이는 이별이 두려워졌던 것이다."], ["He saw the trouble in her face, and drew Meg's attention from it by asking quickly, \"Where have you been calling, all so fine?\"", "로리는 그녀의 얼굴에 어린 근심을 알아채고, 얼른 화제를 돌리려 메그에게 물었다. 『그렇게 곱게 차려입고 어디 다녀오는 거예요?』"]], [["\"At the Gardiners', and Sallie has been telling me all about Belle Moffat's wedding.", "『가드너 댁에. 샐리가 벨 모펏 결혼식 얘기를 죄다 들려줬어."], ["It was very splendid, and they have gone to spend the winter in Paris.", "정말 굉장했대. 그 부부는 파리에서 겨울을 나러 떠났다지 뭐야."], ["Just think how delightful that must be!\"", "그게 얼마나 멋질지 한번 생각해 봐!』"]], [["\"Do you envy her, Meg?\" said Laurie.", "『부러워요, 메그 누나?』 로리가 물었다."]], [["\"I'm afraid I do.\"", "『그런 것 같아.』"]], [["\"I'm glad of it!\" muttered Jo, tying on her hat with a jerk.", "『그거 잘됐네!』 조가 모자를 홱 잡아당겨 묶으며 중얼거렸다."]], [["\"Why?\" asked Meg, looking surprised.", "『왜?』 메그가 놀란 얼굴로 물었다."]], [["\"Because if you care much about riches, you will never go and marry a poor man,\" said Jo, frowning at Laurie, who was mutely warning her to mind what she said.", "『언니가 재물을 그렇게 좋아한다면, 가난한 남자한텐 시집갈 일이 없을 테니까.』 조는 자기 말조심하라고 말없이 눈치를 주는 로리를 향해 인상을 찌푸리며 말했다."]], [["\"I shall never '_go_ and marry'", "『난 누구한테든 \"시집가는\""], ["any one,\" observed Meg, walking on with great dignity, while the others followed, laughing, whispering, skipping stones, and \"behaving like children,\"", "일 같은 건 없을 거야.』 메그가 자못 위엄 있게 걸음을 옮기며 말했고, 나머지 둘은 그 뒤를 따르며 웃고 속닥이고 물수제비를 떴다."], ["as Meg said to herself, though she might have been tempted to join them if she had not had her best dress on.", "메그는 속으로 \"꼭 어린애들 같다니까\" 하고 생각했지만, 제일 좋은 옷만 입지 않았더라면 자기도 끼고 싶었을지 모른다."]], [["For a week or two, Jo behaved so queerly that her sisters were quite bewildered. She rushed to the door when the postman rang; was rude to Mr.", "한두 주 동안 조의 행동이 어찌나 별나던지 자매들은 어리둥절하기 짝이 없었다."], ["Brooke whenever they met; would sit looking at Meg with a woe-begone face, occasionally jumping up to shake, and then to kiss her, in a very mysterious manner; Laurie and she were always making signs to one another, and talking about \"Spread Eagles,\"", "우체부가 종을 울리면 문으로 달려나갔고, 브룩 씨를 만날 때마다 무례하게 굴었으며, 비탄에 잠긴 얼굴로 메그를 멍하니 바라보다가 이따금 벌떡 일어나 흔들어 대더니 또 입을 맞추곤 했는데, 그 모양이 영 알쏭달쏭했다. 게다가 로리와 조는 늘 서로에게 신호를 주고받으며 \"독수리 날개를 활짝\"이 어쩌고 떠들어 댔으니, 자매들은 둘 다 제정신이 아니라고 단언할 지경이었다."], ["till the girls declared they had both lost their wits. On the second Saturday after Jo got out of the window, Meg, as she sat sewing at her window, was scandalized by the sight of Laurie chasing Jo all over the garden, and finally capturing her in Amy's bower.", "조가 창문으로 빠져나간 그날로부터 두 번째 토요일, 창가에 앉아 바느질을 하던 메그는 로리가 정원을 가로지르며 조를 쫓아다니다 마침내 에이미의 정자에서 붙잡는 광경에 기겁했다."], ["What went on there, Meg could not see; but shrieks of laughter were heard, followed by the murmur of voices and a great flapping of newspapers.", "그 안에서 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 메그는 볼 수 없었지만, 까르르 터지는 웃음소리에 이어 두런두런 말소리와 신문지 퍼덕이는 소리가 요란하게 들려왔다."]], [["\"What shall we do with that girl?", "『저 애를 대체 어쩌면 좋아?"], ["She never _will_ behave like a young lady,\" sighed Meg, as she watched the race with a disapproving face.", "끝까지 숙녀답게 굴 줄을 모른다니까.』 메그가 못마땅한 얼굴로 그 추격전을 지켜보며 한숨지었다."]], [["\"I hope she won't; she is so funny and dear as she is,\"", "『난 안 그랬으면 좋겠어. 언니는 지금 그대로가 너무 재밌고 사랑스럽잖아.』 베스가 말했다."], ["said Beth, who had never betrayed that she was a little hurt at Jo's having secrets with any one but her.", "베스는 조가 자기 말고 다른 누군가와 비밀을 나눈다는 사실에 조금 서운했지만, 한 번도 그런 내색을 한 적이 없었다."]], [["\"It's very trying, but we never can make her _commy la fo_,\"", "『정말 골치 아파. 하지만 우린 언니를 도무지 \"콩미 라 포(품위 있게 : 옮긴이)\"로 만들 수가 없나 봐.』 에이미가 덧붙였다."], ["added Amy, who sat making some new frills for herself, with her curls tied up in a very becoming way,--two agreeable things, which made her feel unusually elegant and ladylike.", "그녀는 한쪽에 앉아 자기가 쓸 새 주름 장식을 만드는 중이었는데, 곱슬머리를 아주 예쁘게 묶어 올린 데다 그 두 가지 흐뭇한 일 덕분에 평소보다 우아하고 숙녀다운 기분에 젖어 있었다."]], [["In a few minutes Jo bounced in, laid herself on the sofa, and affected to read.", "잠시 후 조가 통통 튀어 들어오더니 소파에 벌렁 드러누워 책 읽는 시늉을 했다."]], [["\"Have you anything interesting there?\" asked Meg, with condescension.", "『거기 뭐 재밌는 거라도 있어?』 메그가 짐짓 너그러운 척 물었다."]], [["\"Nothing but a story; won't amount to much, I guess,\" returned Jo, carefully keeping the name of the paper out of sight.", "『그냥 소설일 뿐인데, 별거 아닌 것 같아.』 조가 신문 제목을 들키지 않으려고 조심하며 대꾸했다."]], [["\"You'd better read it aloud; that will amuse us and keep you out of mischief,\"", "『소리 내서 읽어 봐."], ["said Amy, in her most grown-up tone.", "그럼 우리도 재밌고, 너도 딴짓 안 하고 좋잖아.』 에이미가 어른스러운 척 잔뜩 말투를 꾸며 말했다."]], [["\"What's the name?\" asked Beth, wondering why Jo kept her face behind the sheet.", "『제목이 뭐야?』 베스가 조가 왜 자꾸 신문 뒤로 얼굴을 가리는지 의아해하며 물었다."]], [["\"The Rival Painters.\"", "『경쟁하는 화가들.』"]], [["\"That sounds well; read it,\"", "『재밌겠다."], ["said Meg.", "읽어 봐.』 메그가 말했다."]], [["With a loud \"Hem!\" and a long breath, Jo began to read very fast.", "『흠!』 조는 큰 소리로 헛기침을 하고 길게 숨을 들이쉰 뒤 아주 빠르게 읽어 나갔다."], ["The girls listened with interest, for the tale was romantic, and somewhat pathetic, as most of the characters died in the end.", "자매들은 흥미진진하게 귀를 기울였다. 이야기는 낭만적이었고, 끝에 가서 등장인물 대부분이 죽는 바람에 다소 애처롭기도 했다."]], [["\"I like that about the splendid picture,\" was Amy's approving remark, as Jo paused.", "『그 멋진 그림 얘기가 마음에 들어.』 조가 잠시 멈추자 에이미가 흡족하다는 듯 한마디 했다."]], [["\"I prefer the lovering part.", "『난 사랑하는 대목이 더 좋아."], ["Viola and Angelo are two of our favorite names; isn't that queer?\"", "비올라랑 안젤로는 우리가 좋아하는 이름이잖아. 신기하지 않니?』 메그가 눈물을 훔치며 말했다."], ["said Meg, wiping her eyes, for the \"lovering part\" was tragical.", "\"사랑하는 대목\"이 비극적이었던 것이다."]], [["\"Who wrote it?\" asked Beth, who had caught a glimpse of Jo's face.", "『이거 누가 쓴 거야?』 조의 얼굴을 슬쩍 엿본 베스가 물었다."]], [["The reader suddenly sat up, cast away the paper, displaying a flushed countenance, and, with a funny mixture of solemnity and excitement, replied in a loud voice, \"Your sister.\"", "읽던 사람이 별안간 벌떡 일어나 신문을 내던지자, 발갛게 상기된 얼굴이 드러났다. 그러더니 엄숙함과 흥분이 우습게 뒤섞인 표정으로 큰 소리로 대답했다. 『너희 언니지.』"]], [["\"You?\" cried Meg, dropping her work.", "『언니가?』 메그가 바느질감을 떨어뜨리며 소리쳤다."]], [["\"It's very good,\" said Amy critically.", "『정말 잘 썼다.』 에이미가 비평가라도 된 듯 말했다."]], [["\"I knew it!", "『그럴 줄 알았어!"], ["I knew it!", "그럴 줄 알았다고!"], ["O my Jo, I _am_ so proud!\" and Beth ran to hug her sister, and exult over this splendid success.", "아, 우리 조 언니, 나 정말 자랑스러워!』 베스가 언니에게 달려가 끌어안으며 이 눈부신 성공을 함께 기뻐했다."]], [["Dear me, how delighted they all were, to be sure! how Meg wouldn't believe it till she saw the words, \"Miss Josephine March,\"", "세상에, 다들 어찌나 좋아하던지!"], ["actually printed in the paper; how graciously Amy criticised the artistic parts of the story, and offered hints for a sequel, which unfortunately couldn't be carried out, as the hero and heroine were dead; how Beth got excited, and skipped and sung with joy; how Hannah came in to exclaim \"Sakes alive, well I never!\"", "메그는 신문에 박힌 \"조세핀 마치 양\"이라는 글자를 두 눈으로 확인하고서야 믿었고, 에이미는 이야기의 예술적인 부분을 점잖게 비평하며 후속편에 쓸 만한 조언까지 내놓았지만, 안타깝게도 주인공 남녀가 죽어 버린 탓에 실현될 수는 없었다. 베스는 신이 나서 깡충깡충 뛰며 노래를 불렀고, 해나는 \"어머나 세상에, 내 살다 살다 이런 일은 처음일세!\""], ["in great astonishment at \"that Jo's doin's;\" how proud Mrs.", "하고 들어와 \"저 조 아씨가 한 일\"에 입을 떡 벌리며 감탄했다."], ["March was when she knew it; how Jo laughed, with tears in her eyes, as she declared she might as well be a peacock and done with it; and how the \"Spread Eagle\"", "마치 부인은 소식을 듣고 무척이나 자랑스러워했다. 조는 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채로 웃으며, 이제 아주 공작새가 되어 버려도 그만이라고 너스레를 떨었다."], ["might be said to flap his wings triumphantly over the House of March, as the paper passed from hand to hand.", "그리고 신문이 이 손에서 저 손으로 건네지는 동안, \"활짝 펼친 독수리\"가 마치 가문 위로 의기양양하게 날갯짓을 한다고 해도 좋을 만했다."]], [["\"Tell us all about it.\" \"When did it come?\" \"How much did you get for it?\" \"What _will_ father say?\" \"Won't Laurie laugh?\" cried the family, all in one breath, as they clustered about Jo; for these foolish, affectionate people made a jubilee of every little household joy.", "『다 얘기해 봐.』 『언제 온 거야?』 『얼마 받았어?』 『아버지가 뭐라고 하실까?』 『로리가 웃겠지?』 식구들이 조를 빙 둘러싸고서 한꺼번에 외쳐 댔다. 이 어리석고도 다정한 사람들은 집안의 자그마한 기쁨 하나하나를 죄다 축제로 만들었던 것이다."]], [["\"Stop jabbering, girls, and I'll tell you everything,\"", "『재잘대지 좀 말아 봐. 그럼 죄다 얘기해 줄 테니까.』 조가 말했다."], ["said Jo, wondering if Miss Burney felt any grander over her \"Evelina\"", "그러면서 버니 양은 「에벨리나」를 두고 자기가 「경쟁하는 화가들」을 두고 느끼는 것보다 조금이라도 더 우쭐한 기분이 들었을까 궁금해했다. 조는 자기가 소설을 어떻게 처리했는지 들려준 뒤 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["than she did over her \"Rival Painters.\"", "『답을 들으러 갔더니, 그 사람이 둘 다 마음에 든다면서도 초보자한테는 원고료를 안 주고 신문에 실어 주면서 소설을 봐주는 거래. 좋은 연습이 될 거고, 초보자가 실력이 늘면 누구든 돈을 줄 거라더라."], ["Having told how she disposed of her tales, Jo added, \"And when I went to get my answer, the man said he liked them both, but didn't pay beginners, only let them print in his paper, and noticed the stories.", "그래서 두 편을 그냥 줬어. 그랬더니 오늘 이게 온 거야."], ["It was good practice, he said; and when the beginners improved, any one would pay.", "로리가 이걸 들고 있는 날 붙잡고는 기어이 보겠다고 우겨서 보여 줬지. 그랬더니 잘 썼다면서, 나더러 더 쓰라고, 다음 건 자기가 원고료를 받게 해 주겠대."], ["So I let him have the two stories, and to-day this was sent to me, and Laurie caught me with it, and insisted on seeing it, so I let him; and he said it was good, and I shall write more, and he's going to get the next paid for, and I _am_ so happy, for in time I may be able to support myself and help the girls.\"", "나 정말 행복해. 머잖아 내 힘으로 먹고살면서 다들 도울 수 있을지도 모르잖아.』"]], [["Jo's breath gave out here; and, wrapping her head in the paper, she bedewed her little story with a few natural tears; for to be independent, and earn the praise of those she loved were the dearest wishes of her heart, and this seemed to be the first step toward that happy end.", "여기서 조는 숨이 차올랐다. 그러더니 신문지에 얼굴을 파묻고는 자기의 자그마한 소설 위로 자연스레 솟구친 눈물 몇 방울을 떨구었다. 자립해서, 사랑하는 사람들에게 칭찬받는 것이야말로 그녀가 마음속 깊이 가장 간절히 바라던 소망이었고, 이 일이 바로 그 행복한 끝을 향한 첫걸음처럼 여겨졌던 것이다."]], [["# A TELEGRAM.", "# A TELEGRAM."]], [["\"November is the most disagreeable month in the whole year,\" said Margaret, standing at the window one dull afternoon, looking out at the frost-bitten garden.", "15장 전보\n『11월은 한 해를 통틀어 가장 고약한 달이야.』 어느 흐린 오후, 마거릿이 창가에 서서 서리 맞은 정원을 내다보며 말했다."]], [["\"That's the reason I was born in it,\" observed Jo pensively, quite unconscious of the blot on her nose.", "『그러니까 내가 11월에 태어났지.』 조가 코에 묻은 잉크 얼룩도 모른 채 시무룩하게 말했다."]], [["\"If something very pleasant should happen now, we should think it a delightful month,\"", "『지금 아주 즐거운 일이 하나라도 생기면, 우린 11월을 근사한 달이라고 여길걸.』 11월조차 희망적으로 바라보는 베스가 말했다."], ["said Beth, who took a hopeful view of everything, even November.", "베스는 무엇이든 좋게 보는 아이였다."]], [["\"I dare say; but nothing pleasant ever _does_ happen in this family,\"", "『그렇겠지. 하지만 이 집안엔 즐거운 일이라곤 도통 안 생기잖아.』 심사가 뒤틀린 메그가 말했다."], ["said Meg, who was out of sorts. \"We go grubbing along day after day, without a bit of change, and very little fun.", "『변화라곤 눈곱만큼도 없이, 재미라곤 거의 없이, 날이면 날마다 죽어라 일만 하고."], ["We might as well be in a treadmill.\"", "차라리 쳇바퀴 도는 다람쥐 신세가 낫겠다.』"]], [["\"My patience, how blue we are!\" cried Jo.", "『세상에, 우리 왜 이렇게 우울해!』 조가 외쳤다."], ["\"I don't much wonder, poor dear, for you see other girls having splendid times, while you grind, grind, year in and year out.", "『하긴, 우리 가엾은 언니를 탓할 수도 없지. 다른 애들은 신나게 놀러 다니는데 언니는 일 년 내내 갈고 또 갈아 대니까."], ["Oh, don't I wish I could manage things for you as I do for my heroines!", "아, 내가 소설 속 주인공들한테 하듯 언니 일도 척척 처리해 줄 수 있다면!"], ["You're pretty enough and good enough already, so I'd have some rich relation leave you a fortune unexpectedly; then you'd dash out as an heiress, scorn every one who has slighted you, go abroad, and come home my Lady Something, in a blaze of splendor and elegance.\"", "언니는 이미 충분히 예쁘고 충분히 착하니까, 부유한 친척이 느닷없이 언니한테 큰 재산을 물려주게 하는 거야. 그럼 언니는 상속녀가 되어 화려하게 등장해서, 언니를 무시했던 사람들을 죄다 비웃어 주고, 외국에 나갔다가 눈부시게 우아하고 화려한 모습으로 무슨무슨 귀부인이 되어 돌아오는 거지.』"]], [["\"People don't have fortunes left them in that style now-a-days; men have to work, and women to marry for money.", "『요즘 세상엔 그런 식으로 재산을 물려받는 사람 없어. 남자는 일을 해야 하고, 여자는 돈 보고 시집을 가야 하는걸."], ["It's a dreadfully unjust world,\" said Meg bitterly.", "정말이지 끔찍하게 불공평한 세상이야.』 메그가 씁쓸하게 말했다."]], [["\"Jo and I are going to make fortunes for you all; just wait ten years, and see if we don't,\"", "『조 언니랑 내가 다들 부자로 만들어 줄 테니까, 딱 십 년만 기다려 봐."], ["said Amy, who sat in a corner, making mud pies, as Hannah called her little clay models of birds, fruit, and faces.", "우리가 안 그러나.』 한쪽 구석에 앉아 진흙 파이를 만들던 에이미가 말했다. 해나는 에이미가 빚는 그 작은 점토 새며 과일이며 얼굴 모형을 그렇게 불렀다."]], [["\"Can't wait, and I'm afraid I haven't much faith in ink and dirt, though I'm grateful for your good intentions.\"", "『못 기다려. 그리고 잉크랑 진흙은 영 미덥지가 않은걸. 그래도 너희 마음 씀씀이는 고맙다.』"]], [["Meg sighed, and turned to the frost-bitten garden again; Jo groaned, and leaned both elbows on the table in a despondent attitude, but Amy spatted away energetically; and Beth, who sat at the other window, said, smiling, \"Two pleasant things are going to happen right away: Marmee is coming down the street, and Laurie is tramping through the garden as if he had something nice to tell.\"", "메그는 한숨을 쉬며 다시 서리 맞은 정원으로 고개를 돌렸다. 조는 끙 소리를 내며 두 팔꿈치를 식탁에 괸 채 풀이 죽었지만, 에이미는 기운차게 점토를 두드려 댔다. 그리고 맞은편 창가에 앉아 있던 베스가 미소 지으며 말했다. 『지금 당장 즐거운 일이 두 가지나 생길 거야. 엄마가 길을 따라 오고 계시고, 로리가 무슨 좋은 소식이라도 있는 양 정원을 성큼성큼 가로질러 오고 있어.』"]], [["In they both came, Mrs.", "두 사람이 함께 들어섰다."], ["March with her usual question, \"Any letter from father, girls?\" and Laurie to say in his persuasive way, \"Won't some of you come for a drive?", "마치 부인은 늘 하던 대로 『얘들아, 아버지한테서 편지 왔니?』 하고 물었고, 로리는 특유의 설득하는 말투로 말했다."], ["I've been working away at mathematics till my head is in a muddle, and I'm going to freshen my wits by a brisk turn.", "『누구 마차 드라이브하러 갈 사람 없어요? 머리가 뒤죽박죽될 때까지 수학을 붙잡고 씨름했더니, 신나게 한 바퀴 돌면서 머리 좀 식히려고요."], ["It's a dull day, but the air isn't bad, and I'm going to take Brooke home, so it will be gay inside, if it isn't out.", "날이 흐리긴 해도 공기는 나쁘지 않아요. 브룩 선생님도 댁까지 모셔다드릴 거라, 밖은 어떨지 몰라도 안은 즐거울 거예요."], ["Come, Jo, you and Beth will go, won't you?\"", "가요, 조. 조랑 베스는 갈 거지?』"]], [["\"Of course we will.\"", "『당연히 가야지.』"]], [["\"Much obliged, but I'm busy;\"", "『고맙지만 난 바빠.』 메그가 바느질 바구니를 홱 꺼내며 말했다."], ["and Meg whisked out her work-basket, for she had agreed with her mother that it was best, for her at least, not to drive often with the young gentleman.", "적어도 자기만큼은 그 젊은 신사와 너무 자주 마차를 타고 다니지 않는 게 좋겠다고 어머니와 합의해 둔 터였다."]], [["\"We three will be ready in a minute,\" cried Amy, running away to wash her hands.", "『우리 셋은 금방 준비할게요.』 에이미가 손을 씻으러 후다닥 달려가며 외쳤다."]], [["\"Can I do anything for you, Madam Mother?\" asked Laurie, leaning over Mrs.", "『제가 뭐 도와드릴 일 없을까요, 어머님?』 로리가 마치 부인의 의자에 몸을 기대며 물었다."], ["March's chair, with the affectionate look and tone he always gave her.", "그는 늘 부인에게 다정한 눈빛과 말투를 건넸다."]], [["\"No, thank you, except call at the office, if you'll be so kind, dear.", "『고맙지만 없단다. 그래도 괜찮다면 우체국에 좀 들러 주겠니, 얘야."], ["It's our day for a letter, and the postman hasn't been.", "오늘이 편지 오는 날인데 우체부가 아직 안 왔구나."], ["Father is as regular as the sun, but there's some delay on the way, perhaps.\"", "아버지는 해처럼 어김없는 분인데, 아마 오는 길에 무슨 사정이 생긴 모양이야.』"]], [["A sharp ring interrupted her, and a minute after Hannah came in with a letter.", "날카로운 초인종 소리가 그녀의 말을 끊었고, 잠시 후 해나가 편지 한 통을 들고 들어왔다."]], [["\"It's one of them horrid telegraph things, mum,\" she said, handing it as if she was afraid it would explode and do some damage.", "『그 고약한 전보라는 거예요, 마님.』 해나가 마치 그게 터져서 무슨 사달이라도 낼까 봐 겁내듯 건네며 말했다."]], [["At the word \"telegraph,\" Mrs.", "\"전보\"라는 말에 마치 부인은 편지를 낚아채 두 줄짜리 내용을 읽더니, 그 작은 종잇장이 심장에 총알이라도 박은 듯 새하얗게 질려 의자에 털썩 기댔다."], ["March snatched it, read the two lines it contained, and dropped back into her chair as white as if the little paper had sent a bullet to her heart.", "로리가 물을 가지러 아래층으로 뛰어 내려가는 동안, 메그와 해나가 부인을 부축했고, 조는 겁에 질린 목소리로 소리 내어 읽었다."], ["Laurie dashed down stairs for water, while Meg and Hannah supported her, and Jo read aloud, in a frightened voice,--", "『마치 부인께,"]], [["\"Your husband is very ill.", "남편께서 위독하십니다."], ["Come at once. \"S.", "즉시 와 주십시오."], ["HALE, \"Blank Hospital, Washington\"", "S. 헤일, 워싱턴 ◯◯ 병원』"]], [["How still the room was as they listened breathlessly, how strangely the day darkened outside, and how suddenly the whole world seemed to change, as the girls gathered about their mother, feeling as if all the happiness and support of their lives was about to be taken from them. Mrs. March was herself again directly; read the message over, and stretched out her arms to her daughters, saying, in a tone they never forgot, \"I shall go at once, but it may be too late. O children, children, help me to bear it!\"", "자매들이 숨죽여 귀 기울이던 그 순간, 방 안은 어찌나 고요했던지. 바깥은 어찌나 기이하게 어두워졌는지. 그리고 온 세상이 어찌나 별안간 뒤바뀐 듯했는지. 자매들은 어머니를 빙 둘러쌌고, 삶의 모든 행복과 버팀목이 곧 빼앗길 것만 같았다. 마치 부인은 이내 정신을 차렸다. 전보를 다시 한번 읽고는 딸들에게 두 팔을 벌리며, 두고두고 잊지 못할 목소리로 말했다. 『지금 당장 떠나마. 하지만 너무 늦었을지도 몰라. 아, 얘들아, 얘들아, 이걸 견디게 도와주렴!』"]], [["For several minutes there was nothing but the sound of sobbing in the room, mingled with broken words of comfort, tender assurances of help, and hopeful whispers that died away in tears.", "몇 분 동안 방 안에는 흐느낌 소리뿐이었다. 거기에 더듬거리는 위로의 말, 돕겠다는 다정한 다짐, 그리고 눈물 속에 사그라드는 희망 어린 속삭임이 뒤섞였다."], ["Poor Hannah was the first to recover, and with unconscious wisdom she set all the rest a good example; for, with her, work was the panacea for most afflictions.", "가엾은 해나가 가장 먼저 정신을 차리고는, 자기도 모르게 지혜를 발휘해 나머지 사람들에게 좋은 본보기가 되었다. 그녀에게 일이란 거의 모든 괴로움을 다스리는 만병통치약이었던 것이다."]], [["\"The Lord keep the dear man!", "『하느님, 우리 주인어른을 지켜 주소서!"], ["I won't waste no time a cryin', but git your things ready right away, mum,\" she said, heartily, as she wiped her face on her apron, gave her mistress a warm shake of the hand with her own hard one, and went away, to work like three women in one.", "저는 울면서 시간 낭비 안 하고, 당장 마님 짐부터 꾸리겠습니다.』 해나는 진심을 담아 말하고는, 앞치마로 얼굴을 닦고 거친 손으로 안주인의 손을 따뜻하게 꼭 쥔 뒤, 혼자서 세 사람 몫의 일을 하러 나갔다."]], [["\"She's right; there's no time for tears now.", "『해나 말이 맞아. 지금은 눈물 흘릴 때가 아니야."], ["Be calm, girls, and let me think.\"", "얘들아, 진정하렴. 어미가 생각 좀 하게.』"]], [["They tried to be calm, poor things, as their mother sat up, looking pale, but steady, and put away her grief to think and plan for them.", "가엾은 자매들은 진정하려 애썼다. 어머니가 몸을 일으켜 앉았다. 창백하지만 굳건한 얼굴로, 슬픔은 한쪽으로 밀어 두고 아이들을 위해 궁리하고 계획을 세웠다."]], [["\"Where's Laurie?\" she asked presently, when she had collected her thoughts, and decided on the first duties to be done.", "『로리는 어디 있니?』 생각을 추스르고 가장 먼저 해야 할 일들을 정한 뒤, 부인이 이내 물었다."]], [["\"Here, ma'am. Oh, let me do something!\"", "『여기 있어요, 부인."], ["cried the boy, hurrying from the next room, whither he had withdrawn, feeling that their first sorrow was too sacred for even his friendly eyes to see.", "아, 저도 뭐든 하게 해 주세요!』 소년이 옆방에서 황급히 달려 나오며 외쳤다. 그는 이들의 첫 슬픔이 너무도 신성해 자기처럼 다정한 눈길조차 들여다봐선 안 될 것 같아 그 방으로 물러나 있던 참이었다."]], [["\"Send a telegram saying I will come at once.", "『전보를 쳐서 즉시 가겠다고 전해 다오."], ["The next train goes early in the morning. I'll take that.\"", "다음 기차가 내일 아침 일찍 떠나니, 그걸 타마.』"]], [["\"What else?", "『그 밖에는요?"], ["The horses are ready; I can go anywhere, do anything,\"", "말은 준비됐어요."], ["he said, looking ready to fly to the ends of the earth.", "어디든 가고, 뭐든 할게요.』 그는 당장이라도 땅끝까지 날아갈 듯한 기세로 말했다."]], [["\"Leave a note at Aunt March's.", "『마치 고모님 댁에 쪽지를 좀 전해 주렴."], ["Jo, give me that pen and paper.\"", "조, 펜이랑 종이 좀 다오.』"]], [["Tearing off the blank side of one of her newly copied pages, Jo drew the table before her mother, well knowing that money for the long, sad journey must be borrowed, and feeling as if she could do anything to add a little to the sum for her father.", "조는 방금 정서한 원고 가운데 한 장을 골라 뒷면 빈 곳을 찢어 내고, 어머니 앞으로 탁자를 끌어다 놓았다. 길고 서글픈 여정에 쓸 돈을 빌려야 한다는 걸 잘 알았기에, 아버지를 위해서라면 그 액수에 한 푼이라도 보탤 수만 있다면 무엇이든 할 수 있을 것 같았다."]], [["\"Now go, dear; but don't kill yourself driving at a desperate pace; there is no need of that.\"", "『이제 가 보렴, 얘야. 하지만 무작정 내달리다 몸 상하게 하진 말고. 그럴 필요 없으니까.』"]], [["Mrs.", "마치 부인의 당부는 보나 마나 허사였다."], ["March's warning was evidently thrown away; for five minutes later Laurie tore by the window on his own fleet horse, riding as if for his life.", "오 분 뒤, 로리가 자기의 날쌘 말을 타고 목숨이라도 건 듯 창문 앞을 쏜살같이 지나갔으니 말이다."]], [["\"Jo, run to the rooms, and tell Mrs.", "『조, 킹 부인 댁으로 달려가서 내가 못 간다고 전하렴."], ["King that I can't come.", "가는 길에 이것들 좀 사 오고."], ["On the way get these things.", "적어 줄게. 다 필요한 것들이야."], ["I'll put them down; they'll be needed, and I must go prepared for nursing.", "간병할 채비를 단단히 하고 가야 하거든. 병원 물품이 늘 좋은 건 아니니까."], ["Hospital stores are not always good.", "베스, 너는 로런스 어른께 가서 오래된 포도주를 두어 병만 부탁드려 봐."], ["Beth, go and ask Mr.", "아버지를 위해서라면 구걸하는 것도 부끄럽지 않아."], ["Laurence for a couple of bottles of old wine: I'm not too proud to beg for father; he shall have the best of everything.", "아버지껜 뭐든 가장 좋은 걸 드려야지. 에이미, 해나한테 검은 트렁크를 내려 달라고 전하렴."], ["Amy, tell Hannah to get down the black trunk; and, Meg, come and help me find my things, for I'm half bewildered.\"", "그리고 메그, 너는 와서 내 짐 찾는 것 좀 도와줘. 정신이 반쯤 나간 것 같구나.』"]], [["Writing, thinking, and directing, all at once, might well bewilder the poor lady, and Meg begged her to sit quietly in her room for a little while, and let them work.", "쓰고, 생각하고, 지시하는 일을 한꺼번에 하려니 가엾은 부인은 머리가 어지러울 만도 했다. 메그는 부인에게 잠시 방에서 조용히 앉아 쉬시라고, 나머지는 자기들이 하겠다고 졸랐다."], ["Every one scattered like leaves before a gust of wind; and the quiet, happy household was broken up as suddenly as if the paper had been an evil spell.", "모두가 돌풍 앞의 나뭇잎처럼 흩어졌다. 그리고 그 종잇장이 무슨 사악한 주문이라도 되는 양, 조용하고 행복하던 집안은 순식간에 산산이 흐트러졌다."]], [["Mr. Laurence came hurrying back with Beth, bringing every comfort the kind old gentleman could think of for the invalid, and friendliest promises of protection for the girls during the mother's absence, which comforted her very much.", "로런스 씨가 베스와 함께 부랴부랴 돌아왔다."], ["There was nothing he didn't offer, from his own dressing-gown to himself as escort.", "친절한 노신사는 환자를 위해 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 위안거리를 챙겨 왔고, 어머니가 없는 동안 딸들을 지켜 주겠다는 더없이 다정한 약속까지 건네어 부인의 마음을 크게 위로했다. 그는 자기 실내복부터 길동무가 되어 드리겠다는 제안까지, 못 내놓을 것이 없었다."], ["But that last was impossible. Mrs.", "하지만 마지막 것만큼은 불가능했다."], ["March would not hear of the old gentleman's undertaking the long journey; yet an expression of relief was visible when he spoke of it, for anxiety ill fits one for travelling.", "마치 부인은 노신사가 그 먼 길을 함께하겠다는 말을 도무지 받아들이려 하지 않았다. 그래도 그가 그 얘길 꺼냈을 때 부인의 얼굴에 안도하는 기색이 비쳤으니, 근심에 싸인 사람에게 여행길 동행이란 영 거추장스러운 법이었던 것이다."], ["He saw the look, knit his heavy eyebrows, rubbed his hands, and marched abruptly away, saying he'd be back directly.", "노신사는 그 표정을 알아채고는 짙은 눈썹을 찌푸리고 두 손을 비비더니, 금방 돌아오겠다는 말을 남기고 휙 가 버렸다."], ["No one had time to think of him again till, as Meg ran through the entry, with a pair of rubbers in one hand and a cup of tea in the other, she came suddenly upon Mr. Brooke.", "아무도 그를 다시 떠올릴 겨를이 없었는데, 메그가 한 손에 덧신을, 다른 한 손에 찻잔을 들고 현관을 가로질러 달려가다가 그만 브룩 씨와 딱 마주쳤다."]], [["\"I'm very sorry to hear of this, Miss March,\" he said, in the kind, quiet tone which sounded very pleasantly to her perturbed spirit.", "『이런 일을 듣게 되어 정말 유감입니다, 마치 양.』 그가 친절하고 차분한 목소리로 말했는데, 그 음성은 심란한 그녀의 마음에 무척 다정하게 들렸다."], ["\"I came to offer myself as escort to your mother. Mr.", "『어머님의 길동무가 되어 드리고자 찾아왔습니다."], ["Laurence has commissions for me in Washington, and it will give me real satisfaction to be of service to her there.\"", "로런스 어른께서 워싱턴에 처리할 일을 맡기셨거든요. 거기서 어머님께 도움을 드릴 수 있다면 저로서도 정말 기쁜 일이고요.』"]], [["Down dropped the rubbers, and the tea was very near following, as Meg put out her hand, with a face so full of gratitude, that Mr.", "덧신이 툭 떨어졌고, 찻잔도 하마터면 따라 떨어질 뻔했다."], ["Brooke would have felt repaid for a much greater sacrifice than the trifling one of time and comfort which he was about to make.", "메그가 손을 내미는데, 그 얼굴에 어찌나 고마움이 가득하던지, 브룩 씨로서는 이제부터 치를 사소한 시간과 수고쯤은 물론이고 그보다 훨씬 큰 희생을 했더라도 충분히 보상받았다고 느꼈을 것이다."]], [["\"How kind you all are!", "『다들 어쩜 이렇게 친절하실까요!"], ["Mother will accept, I'm sure; and it will be such a relief to know that she has some one to take care of her.", "엄마도 분명 받아들이실 거예요. 곁에서 엄마를 돌봐 줄 분이 있다는 걸 알면 얼마나 마음이 놓일지 몰라요."], ["Thank you very, very much!\"", "정말, 정말 고맙습니다!』"]], [["Meg spoke earnestly, and forgot herself entirely till something in the brown eyes looking down at her made her remember the cooling tea, and lead the way into the parlor, saying she would call her mother.", "메그는 진심으로 말했고, 자기 자신은 까맣게 잊고 있었는데, 자기를 내려다보는 갈색 눈동자에 담긴 무언가에 식어 가는 차가 퍼뜩 떠올랐다. 그녀는 어머니를 불러오겠다고 말하며 그를 응접실로 안내했다."]], [["Everything was arranged by the time Laurie returned with a note from Aunt March, enclosing the desired sum, and a few lines repeating what she had often said before,--that she had always told them it was absurd for March to go into the army, always predicted that no good would come of it, and she hoped they would take her advice next time.", "로리가 마치 고모의 쪽지를 들고 돌아왔을 무렵엔 모든 준비가 끝나 있었다. 쪽지에는 부탁한 돈과 함께, 전에도 여러 번 한 말을 되풀이하는 몇 줄이 적혀 있었다."], ["Mrs.", "마치가 군대에 들어가는 건 어처구니없는 짓이라고 늘 일러두지 않았느냐, 그래 봐야 좋을 게 하나도 없을 거라고 늘 예언하지 않았느냐, 다음번엔 부디 자기 충고를 새겨들으면 좋겠다는 내용이었다."], ["March put the note in the fire, the money in her purse, and went on with her preparations, with her lips folded tightly, in a way which Jo would have understood if she had been there.", "마치 부인은 쪽지를 난롯불에 던지고 돈은 지갑에 넣은 뒤, 입술을 꾹 다문 채로 채비를 이어 갔다. 만약 조가 그 자리에 있었다면 그 표정의 뜻을 알아챘을 것이다."]], [["The short afternoon wore away; all the other errands were done, and Meg and her mother busy at some necessary needle-work, while Beth and Amy got tea, and Hannah finished her ironing with what she called a \"slap and a bang,\" but still Jo did not come. They began to get anxious; and Laurie went off to find her, for no one ever knew what freak Jo might take into her head. He missed her, however, and she came walking in with a very queer expression of countenance, for there was a mixture of fun and fear, satisfaction and regret, in it, which puzzled the family as much as did the roll of bills she laid before her mother, saying, with a little choke in her voice, \"That's my contribution towards making father comfortable and bringing him home!\"", "짧은 오후가 저물어 갔다. 다른 심부름은 죄다 끝났고, 메그와 어머니는 꼭 필요한 바느질에 매달렸으며, 베스와 에이미는 차를 준비했고, 해나는 자기 말마따나 \"탁탁 쾅쾅\" 하며 다림질을 마쳤지만, 조는 여태 돌아오지 않았다. 다들 슬슬 걱정이 되기 시작했고, 로리가 그녀를 찾으러 나섰다. 조가 무슨 엉뚱한 생각을 머릿속에 떠올릴지는 아무도 몰랐기 때문이다. 그런데 로리는 그녀와 엇갈렸고, 조는 아주 묘한 표정을 지은 채 걸어 들어왔다. 그 얼굴엔 재미와 두려움, 만족과 후회가 뒤섞여 있어 식구들을 어리둥절하게 했는데, 그녀가 어머니 앞에 내려놓은 지폐 뭉치도 그에 못지않게 식구들을 어리둥절하게 했다. 조가 조금 목멘 소리로 말했다. 『이건 아버지를 편안하게 해 드리고 집으로 모셔 오는 데 보태는 내 몫이야!』"]], [["\"My dear, where did you get it?", "『얘야, 이걸 어디서 났니?"], ["Twenty-five dollars!", "이십오 달러나!"], ["Jo, I hope you haven't done anything rash?", "조, 설마 무슨 경솔한 짓을 한 건 아니겠지?』"]], [["\"No, it's mine honestly; I didn't beg, borrow, or steal it. I earned it; and I don't think you'll blame me, for I only sold what was my own.\"", "『아니야, 떳떳하게 번 내 돈이야. 구걸한 것도, 빌린 것도, 훔친 것도 아니야. 내가 벌었어. 엄마도 날 나무라진 못할걸. 내 것을 팔았을 뿐이니까.』"]], [["As she spoke, Jo took off her bonnet, and a general outcry arose, for all her abundant hair was cut short.", "말하면서 조가 모자를 벗자, 일제히 탄성이 터져 나왔다. 그 풍성하던 머리카락이 죄다 짧게 잘려 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"Your hair!", "『네 머리카락이!"], ["Your beautiful hair!\" \"O Jo, how could you?", "그 아름다운 머리카락이!』 『아, 조, 어떻게 그럴 수가?"], ["Your one beauty.\" \"My dear girl, there was no need of this.\"", "네 유일한 자랑이었는데.』 『얘야, 그럴 것까진 없었는데.』 『이제 내 조 같지가 않구나."], ["\"She doesn't look like my Jo any more, but I love her dearly for it!\"", "그래도 그렇게 해 준 게 너무 사랑스러워!』"]], [["As every one exclaimed, and Beth hugged the cropped head tenderly, Jo assumed an indifferent air, which did not deceive any one a particle, and said, rumpling up the brown bush, and trying to look as if she liked it, \"It doesn't affect the fate of the nation, so don't wail, Beth. It will be good for my vanity; I was getting too proud of my wig. It will do my brains good to have that mop taken off; my head feels deliciously light and cool, and the barber said I could soon have a curly crop, which will be boyish, becoming, and easy to keep in order. I'm satisfied; so please take the money, and let's have supper.\"", "저마다 탄성을 쏟아 내고 베스가 짧아진 머리를 다정하게 끌어안는 동안, 조는 짐짓 아무렇지 않은 척했지만 그 누구도 손톱만큼도 속아 넘어가지 않았다. 조는 갈색 더부룩한 머리를 마구 헝클며 마음에 든다는 표정을 지어 보이려 애쓰며 말했다. 『이게 나라의 운명을 바꾸는 것도 아닌데, 그만 울어, 베스. 내 허영심에도 약이 될 거야. 내 가발을 너무 자랑스러워하던 참이었거든. 이 덥수룩한 걸 떼어 내니 머리도 맑아지겠지. 머리가 기분 좋게 가볍고 시원해. 이발사 말이 곧 곱슬곱슬하게 자랄 거래. 그럼 소년처럼 보이고, 잘 어울리고, 손질하기도 쉬울 거라더라. 난 만족해. 그러니까 그 돈 받아 줘, 그리고 저녁 먹자.』"]], [["\"Tell me all about it, Jo.", "『다 얘기해 봐, 조."], ["_I_ am not quite satisfied, but I can't blame you, for I know how willingly you sacrificed your vanity, as you call it, to your love.", "난 영 만족스럽진 않다만, 그렇다고 널 나무랄 순 없구나. 네가 사랑하는 사람을 위해 네 말마따나 허영심을 얼마나 기꺼이 희생했는지 아니까."], ["But, my dear, it was not necessary, and I'm afraid you will regret it, one of these days,\"", "하지만 얘야, 그럴 것까진 없었어."], ["said Mrs. March.", "머잖아 네가 후회하게 될까 봐 걱정이구나.』 마치 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"No, I won't!\" returned Jo stoutly, feeling much relieved that her prank was not entirely condemned.", "『아니야, 절대 안 그럴 거야!』 조가 야무지게 대꾸하며, 자기 장난이 아주 호되게 야단맞지는 않은 데 한결 마음을 놓았다."]], [["\"What made you do it?\" asked Amy, who would as soon have thought of cutting off her head as her pretty hair.", "『대체 왜 그랬어?』 자기 머리를 자르느니 차라리 목을 자르는 게 낫다고 여길 에이미가 물었다."]], [["\"Well, I was wild to do something for father,\"", "『그게, 아버지를 위해 뭐라도 하고 싶어서 안달이 났거든.』 식탁에 둘러앉으며 조가 대답했다."], ["replied Jo, as they gathered about the table, for healthy young people can eat even in the midst of trouble.", "건강한 젊은이들은 근심 한가운데서도 밥은 먹는 법이니까. 『난 엄마만큼이나 돈 빌리는 게 싫어."], ["\"I hate to borrow as much as mother does, and I knew Aunt March would croak; she always does, if you ask for a ninepence.", "그리고 마치 고모님은 분명 잔소리를 늘어놓으실 거야. 단돈 구 펜스만 달라고 해도 늘 그러시잖아."], ["Meg gave all her quarterly salary toward the rent, and I only got some clothes with mine, so I felt wicked, and was bound to have some money, if I sold the nose off my face to get it.\"", "메그 언니는 분기 봉급을 죄다 집세에 보탰는데, 난 내 몫으로 옷 몇 벌만 산 거잖아. 그래서 영 마음이 찔리던 차에, 내 코를 떼어 팔아서라도 돈을 좀 마련해야겠다 싶었어.』"]], [["\"You needn't feel wicked, my child: you had no winter things, and got the simplest with your own hard earnings,\"", "『찔릴 것 없단다, 얘야."], ["said Mrs. March, with a look that warmed Jo's heart.", "너는 겨울옷이 하나도 없었고, 네가 힘들게 번 돈으로 제일 검소한 것만 샀잖니.』 마치 부인이 조의 마음을 따뜻하게 데우는 눈빛으로 말했다."]], [["\"I hadn't the least idea of selling my hair at first, but as I went along I kept thinking what I could do, and feeling as if I'd like to dive into some of the rich stores and help myself. In a barber's window I saw tails of hair with the prices marked; and one black tail, not so thick as mine, was forty dollars. It came over me all of a sudden that I had one thing to make money out of, and without stopping to think, I walked in, asked if they bought hair, and what they would give for mine.\"", "『처음엔 머리카락을 팔 생각은 눈곱만큼도 없었어. 그런데 길을 걷다 보니 자꾸 내가 뭘 할 수 있을까 궁리하게 되더라고. 그 으리으리한 가게들에 뛰어들어 막 집어 오고 싶은 심정이었지. 그러다 어느 이발소 진열창에 값을 매겨 놓은 머리타래들이 보이는 거야. 그중 내 것만큼 숱이 많지도 않은 검은 머리타래가 사십 달러더라고. 그 순간 문득, 나한테도 돈 될 게 하나 있구나 싶었어. 그래서 생각할 겨를도 없이 안으로 들어가서, 머리카락을 사느냐, 내 걸 사면 얼마나 주겠느냐고 물어봤지.』"]], [["\"I don't see how you dared to do it,\" said Beth, in a tone of awe.", "『어떻게 그럴 엄두를 냈는지 모르겠어.』 베스가 경외하는 듯한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, he was a little man who looked as if he merely lived to oil his hair. He rather stared, at first, as if he wasn't used to having girls bounce into his shop and ask him to buy their hair. He said he didn't care about mine, it wasn't the fashionable color, and he never paid much for it in the first place; the work put into it made it dear, and so on. It was getting late, and I was afraid, if it wasn't done right away, that I shouldn't have it done at all, and you know when I start to do a thing, I hate to give it up; so I begged him to take it, and told him why I was in such a hurry. It was silly, I dare say, but it changed his mind, for I got rather excited, and told the story in my topsy-turvy way, and his wife heard, and said so kindly,--", "『아, 머리에 기름 바르는 게 사는 낙인 것처럼 보이는 자그마한 남자였어. 처음엔 빤히 쳐다보더라고. 여자애가 가게에 불쑥 들어와서 머리카락을 사라고 하는 게 영 익숙지 않은 모양이었어. 자기는 내 머리카락엔 관심 없다나. 유행하는 색도 아니고, 애초에 그런 머리는 값을 별로 안 쳐준다는 거야. 손이 많이 가서 비싸지는 거라나 어쩌나. 시간은 점점 늦어 가는데, 지금 당장 안 해 버리면 영영 못 할 것만 같았어. 그리고 엄마도 알다시피 난 한번 하기로 마음먹은 일은 도중에 포기하는 게 죽기보다 싫잖아. 그래서 제발 사 달라고 사정하면서, 왜 이렇게 급한지 사연을 다 털어놨지. 어리석은 짓이었는지도 모르지만, 그 덕에 그 사람 마음이 바뀌었어. 내가 좀 흥분해서 두서없이 사연을 늘어놓는 걸 그 사람 아내가 듣고는, 어찌나 다정하게 말하던지.』"]], [["\"'Take it, Thomas, and oblige the young lady; I'd do as much for our Jimmy any day if I had a spire of hair worth selling.'\"", "『여보, 토머스, 그냥 사 줘요, 이 아가씨 좀 도와줘요. 우리 지미한테라면 나도 언제든 그렇게 할 거예요, 팔 만한 머리카락이 한 올이라도 있다면요.』"]], [["\"Who was Jimmy?\" asked Amy, who liked to have things explained as they went along.", "『지미가 누구야?』 이야기가 진행되는 대로 그때그때 설명 듣기를 좋아하는 에이미가 물었다."]], [["\"Her son, she said, who was in the army.", "『군대에 간 아들이래."], ["How friendly such things make strangers feel, don't they?", "그런 얘길 하니까 낯선 사람끼리도 금세 친근해지지 않니?"], ["She talked away all the time the man clipped, and diverted my mind nicely.\"", "그 아주머니는 남자가 머리를 자르는 내내 쉴 새 없이 이야기를 해서 내 마음을 잘 달래 줬어.』"]], [["\"Didn't you feel dreadfully when the first cut came?\" asked Meg, with a shiver.", "『처음 가위가 들어갈 때 끔찍하지 않았어?』 메그가 몸을 떨며 물었다."]], [["\"I took a last look at my hair while the man got his things, and that was the end of it.", "『남자가 도구를 챙기는 동안 내 머리카락을 마지막으로 한번 봤어. 그게 끝이었지."], ["I never snivel over trifles like that; I will confess, though, I felt queer when I saw the dear old hair laid out on the table, and felt only the short, rough ends on my head.", "난 그런 사소한 일로 훌쩍거리진 않아. 그래도 솔직히, 그 정든 머리카락이 탁자 위에 늘어진 걸 보고 머리엔 짧고 까칠한 끄트머리만 남은 걸 느끼니까 기분이 묘하긴 하더라."], ["It almost seemed as if I'd an arm or a leg off.", "팔이나 다리 한쪽이 떨어져 나간 것만 같았어."], ["The woman saw me look at it, and picked out a long lock for me to keep.", "그 아주머니가 내가 머리카락 쳐다보는 걸 보고는, 간직하라고 긴 머리타래를 하나 골라 줬어. 그걸 엄마한테 줄게, 마미."], ["I'll give it to you, Marmee, just to remember past glories by; for a crop is so comfortable I don't think I shall ever have a mane again.\"", "옛 영광을 추억하시라고. 이렇게 짧은 머리가 어찌나 편한지, 다신 갈기 같은 머리를 기를 일은 없을 것 같아.』"]], [["Mrs. March folded the wavy, chestnut lock, and laid it away with a short gray one in her desk. She only said \"Thank you, deary,\" but something in her face made the girls change the subject, and talk as cheerfully as they could about Mr. Brooke's kindness, the prospect of a fine day to-morrow, and the happy times they would have when father came home to be nursed.", "마치 부인은 물결치는 밤색 머리타래를 접어, 짧은 잿빛 머리타래 하나와 함께 책상 안에 넣어 두었다. 부인은 그저 『고맙다, 우리 딸』이라고만 했지만, 그 얼굴에 어린 무언가에 자매들은 화제를 바꾸어, 브룩 씨의 친절이며, 내일 날이 갤 것 같다는 이야기며, 아버지가 집에 돌아와 간호받을 때 누릴 행복한 시간에 대해 애써 명랑하게 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["No one wanted to go to bed, when, at ten o'clock, Mrs.", "열 시가 되어 마치 부인이 마지막 일감을 마저 끝내고 『자, 얘들아』라고 했을 때, 아무도 잠자리에 들고 싶어 하지 않았다."], ["March put by the last finished job, and said, \"Come, girls.\"", "베스가 피아노 앞으로 가서 아버지가 가장 좋아하는 찬송가를 연주했다."], ["Beth went to the piano and played the father's favorite hymn; all began bravely, but broke down one by one, till Beth was left alone, singing with all her heart, for to her music was always a sweet consoler.", "다들 씩씩하게 부르기 시작했지만 하나둘 목이 메었고, 결국 베스만 홀로 남아 온 마음을 다해 노래했다. 베스에게 음악은 언제나 다정한 위로였던 것이다."]], [["\"Go to bed and don't talk, for we must be up early, and shall need all the sleep we can get.", "『가서 자거라, 더는 떠들지 말고. 우린 일찍 일어나야 하니까 잘 수 있을 때 푹 자 둬야 해."], ["Good-night, my darlings,\" said Mrs.", "잘 자렴, 내 사랑하는 아이들아.』 찬송가가 끝나자 마치 부인이 말했다."], ["March, as the hymn ended, for no one cared to try another.", "아무도 다른 곡을 더 불러 보려 하지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["They kissed her quietly, and went to bed as silently as if the dear invalid lay in the next room.", "자매들은 조용히 어머니에게 입을 맞추고는, 마치 사랑하는 환자가 옆방에 누워 있기라도 한 듯 소리 없이 잠자리에 들었다."], ["Beth and Amy soon fell asleep in spite of the great trouble, but Meg lay awake, thinking the most serious thoughts she had ever known in her short life.", "베스와 에이미는 큰 근심에도 이내 잠들었지만, 메그는 짧은 생애에 처음 겪는 더없이 심각한 생각에 잠겨 말똥말똥 누워 있었다."], ["Jo lay motionless, and her sister fancied that she was asleep, till a stifled sob made her exclaim, as she touched a wet cheek,--", "조는 미동도 없이 누워 있어 언니는 동생이 잠든 줄로만 알았는데, 억눌린 흐느낌이 새어 나오자 젖은 뺨에 손을 대며 외쳤다."]], [["\"Jo, dear, what is it?", "『조, 얘, 왜 그래?"], ["Are you crying about father?\"", "아버지 때문에 우는 거야?』"]], [["\"No, not now.\"", "『아니, 지금은 아니야.』"]], [["\"What then?\"", "『그럼 뭔데?』"]], [["\"My--my hair!\" burst out poor Jo, trying vainly to smother her emotion in the pillow.", "『내, 내 머리카락!』 가엾은 조가 베개에 감정을 묻어 보려 했지만 헛되이 터뜨리고 말았다."]], [["It did not sound at all comical to Meg, who kissed and caressed the afflicted heroine in the tenderest manner.", "메그에게는 그 말이 조금도 우습게 들리지 않았다. 메그는 더없이 다정하게 슬픔에 잠긴 동생에게 입 맞추고 어루만졌다."]], [["\"I'm not sorry,\" protested Jo, with a choke.", "『후회하는 건 아니야.』 조가 목멘 소리로 항변했다."], ["\"I'd do it again to-morrow, if I could.", "『할 수만 있다면 내일 또 그럴 거야."], ["It's only the vain, selfish part of me that goes and cries in this silly way.", "그저 내 안의 허영심 많고 이기적인 부분이 이렇게 바보같이 우는 것뿐이야."], ["Don't tell any one, it's all over now.", "아무한테도 말하지 마, 이젠 다 괜찮으니까."], ["I thought you were asleep, so I just made a little private moan for my one beauty.", "언니가 잠든 줄 알고, 내 유일한 자랑을 두고 혼자 조금 끙끙댄 거야."], ["How came you to be awake?\"", "언닌 왜 안 자고 있었어?』"]], [["\"I can't sleep, I'm so anxious,\" said Meg.", "『잠이 안 와, 너무 걱정돼서.』 메그가 말했다."]], [["\"Think about something pleasant, and you'll soon drop off.\"", "『뭔가 즐거운 걸 생각해 봐. 그럼 금방 잠들 거야.』"]], [["\"I tried it, but felt wider awake than ever.\"", "『해 봤는데, 오히려 정신이 더 말똥말똥해지는걸.』"]], [["\"What did you think of?\"", "『뭘 생각했는데?』"]], [["\"Handsome faces,--eyes particularly,\"", "『잘생긴 얼굴들."], ["answered Meg, smiling to herself, in the dark.", "특히 눈을 생각했어.』 메그가 어둠 속에서 혼자 미소 지으며 대답했다."]], [["\"What color do you like best?\"", "『무슨 색이 제일 좋아?』"]], [["\"Brown--that is, sometimes; blue are lovely.\"", "『갈색. 그러니까 가끔은. 파란색도 예쁘고.』"]], [["Jo laughed, and Meg sharply ordered her not to talk, then amiably promised to make her hair curl, and fell asleep to dream of living in her castle in the air.", "조가 웃자 메그는 그만 떠들라고 매몰차게 명했다가, 곧 다정하게 머리를 곱슬곱슬하게 만들어 주겠다고 약속하고는, 자기만의 공중누각에 사는 꿈을 꾸며 잠들었다."]], [["The clocks were striking midnight, and the rooms were very still, as a figure glided quietly from bed to bed, smoothing a coverlid here, settling a pillow there, and pausing to look long and tenderly at each unconscious face, to kiss each with lips that mutely blessed, and to pray the fervent prayers which only mothers utter.", "자정을 알리는 시계 소리가 울리고 방마다 고요한데, 한 사람이 침대에서 침대로 가만가만 옮겨 다녔다. 여기선 이불을 매만지고 저기선 베개를 바로 놓으며, 잠든 줄도 모르는 얼굴 하나하나를 오래도록 다정하게 들여다보려 멈춰 서고, 말없이 축복하는 입술로 하나하나에 입을 맞추고, 오직 어머니만이 올릴 수 있는 간절한 기도를 드렸다."], ["As she lifted the curtain to look out into the dreary night, the moon broke suddenly from behind the clouds, and shone upon her like a bright, benignant face, which seemed to whisper in the silence, \"Be comforted, dear soul!", "그녀가 적막한 밤을 내다보려 커튼을 걷자, 구름 뒤에서 달이 불쑥 솟아올라 밝고 인자한 얼굴인 양 그녀를 비추었다. 그 달이 고요 속에서 이렇게 속삭이는 듯했다."], ["There is always light behind the clouds.\"", "『위안을 얻으렴, 가여운 영혼아! 구름 뒤에는 언제나 빛이 있단다.』\n16장 편지"]], [["In the cold gray dawn the sisters lit their lamp, and read their chapter with an earnestness never felt before; for now the shadow of a real trouble had come, the little books were full of help and comfort; and, as they dressed, they agreed to say good-by cheerfully and hopefully, and send their mother on her anxious journey unsaddened by tears or complaints from them.", "차가운 잿빛 새벽에 자매들은 등불을 켜고, 일찍이 느껴 본 적 없는 간절함으로 성경을 읽었다. 이제 진짜 시련의 그림자가 드리우자, 그 작은 책들은 도움과 위로로 가득했던 것이다."], ["Everything seemed very strange when they went down,--so dim and still outside, so full of light and bustle within.", "그리고 옷을 입으며, 그들은 명랑하고 희망차게 작별 인사를 건네어, 근심 어린 길을 떠나는 어머니를 자기들의 눈물이나 푸념으로 슬프게 하지 말자고 다짐했다. 아래층으로 내려가니 모든 게 무척 낯설게 느껴졌다."], ["Breakfast at that early hour seemed odd, and even Hannah's familiar face looked unnatural as she flew about her kitchen with her night-cap on.", "바깥은 어둑하고 고요한데, 안은 불빛과 부산함으로 가득했다. 그렇게 이른 시각의 아침 식사는 어색하기만 했고, 잠자리 모자를 쓴 채 부엌을 분주히 오가는 해나의 낯익은 얼굴마저 어쩐지 낯설어 보였다."], ["The big trunk stood ready in the hall, mother's cloak and bonnet lay on the sofa, and mother herself sat trying to eat, but looking so pale and worn with sleeplessness and anxiety that the girls found it very hard to keep their resolution.", "커다란 트렁크가 현관에 채비를 갖추고 놓였고, 어머니의 망토와 보닛은 소파 위에 놓여 있었다. 어머니는 억지로 뭐라도 먹어 보려 앉아 있었지만, 못 잔 데다 근심에 시달려 어찌나 창백하고 수척해 보이는지, 자매들은 다짐을 지키기가 무척이나 힘들었다."], ["Meg's eyes kept filling in spite of herself; Jo was obliged to hide her face in the kitchen roller more than once; and the little girls' wore a grave, troubled expression, as if sorrow was a new experience to them.", "메그는 저도 모르게 자꾸 눈물이 차올랐고, 조는 부엌 수건에 얼굴을 파묻기를 한두 번이 아니었으며, 어린 두 자매는 슬픔이 처음 겪는 일이기라도 한 듯 심각하고 근심 어린 표정을 짓고 있었다."]], [["Nobody talked much, but as the time drew very near, and they sat waiting for the carriage, Mrs.", "다들 말이 없었지만, 떠날 시간이 코앞에 다가와 마차를 기다리며 앉아 있을 때, 마치 부인이 자기 주위에서 부산을 떨던 딸들에게 말했다."], ["March said to the girls, who were all busied about her, one folding her shawl, another smoothing out the strings of her bonnet, a third putting on her overshoes, and a fourth fastening up her travelling bag,--", "한 아이는 숄을 개고, 또 한 아이는 보닛 끈을 매만지고, 또 다른 아이는 덧신을 신겨 주고, 마지막 아이는 여행 가방을 잠그고 있었다."]], [["\"Children, I leave you to Hannah's care and Mr. Laurence's protection.", "『얘들아, 너희를 해나의 보살핌과 로런스 어른의 보호에 맡기고 가마."], ["Hannah is faithfulness itself, and our good neighbor will guard you as if you were his own.", "해나는 그야말로 성실함 그 자체고, 우리 좋은 이웃은 너희를 친자식처럼 지켜 주실 거야."], ["I have no fears for you, yet I am anxious that you should take this trouble rightly.", "너희 걱정은 안 한다만, 너희가 이 시련을 올바르게 받아들였으면 싶구나."], ["Don't grieve and fret when I am gone, or think that you can comfort yourselves by being idle and trying to forget.", "내가 떠난 뒤에 슬퍼하거나 안달하지 말고, 빈둥거리며 잊어 보려는 걸로 위안을 삼을 수 있다고도 여기지 말렴. 평소처럼 너희 할 일을 해 나가거라."], ["Go on with your work as usual, for work is a blessed solace.", "일이야말로 더없이 복된 위안이니까. 희망을 품고 부지런히 지내렴."], ["Hope and keep busy; and whatever happens, remember that you never can be fatherless.\"", "그리고 무슨 일이 있어도, 너희에게 아버지가 안 계신 일은 결코 없으리란 걸 기억하렴.』"]], [["\"Yes, mother.\"", "『네, 엄마.』"]], [["\"Meg, dear, be prudent, watch over your sisters, consult Hannah, and, in any perplexity, go to Mr. Laurence.", "『메그, 얘야, 신중하게 행동하고, 동생들을 보살피고, 해나와 의논하고, 곤란한 일이 생기면 로런스 어른을 찾아가렴."], ["Be patient, Jo, don't get despondent or do rash things; write to me often, and be my brave girl, ready to help and cheer us all.", "조, 인내심을 갖고, 낙담하거나 경솔한 짓을 하지 마라. 자주 편지하고, 우리 모두를 돕고 기운 나게 해 줄 씩씩한 우리 딸이 되어 다오."], ["Beth, comfort yourself with your music, and be faithful to the little home duties; and you, Amy, help all you can, be obedient, and keep happy safe at home.\"", "베스, 너는 음악으로 마음을 달래고, 소소한 집안일에 충실하렴. 그리고 에이미, 너는 할 수 있는 한 다 거들고, 말 잘 듣고, 집에서 즐겁고 안전하게 잘 지내거라.』"]], [["\"We will, mother!", "『그럴게요, 엄마!"], ["we will!\"", "그럴게요!』"]], [["The rattle of an approaching carriage made them all start and listen.", "다가오는 마차 소리가 덜컹덜컹 들려오자 다들 흠칫하며 귀를 기울였다. 그것이 가장 힘든 순간이었지만, 자매들은 잘 견뎌 냈다."], ["That was the hard minute, but the girls stood it well: no one cried, no one ran away or uttered a lamentation, though their hearts were very heavy as they sent loving messages to father, remembering, as they spoke, that it might be too late to deliver them.", "아무도 울지 않았고, 달아나거나 한탄을 내뱉는 사람도 없었다. 비록 아버지에게 사랑의 안부를 전하며 가슴이 한없이 무거웠지만, 그리고 말을 하면서도 그것을 전하기엔 너무 늦었을지도 모른다는 생각이 들었지만 말이다."], ["They kissed their mother quietly, clung about her tenderly, and tried to wave their hands cheerfully when she drove away.", "자매들은 조용히 어머니에게 입을 맞추고, 다정하게 매달렸으며, 어머니가 마차를 타고 떠날 때 애써 명랑하게 손을 흔들었다."]], [["Laurie and his grandfather came over to see her off, and Mr. Brooke looked so strong and sensible and kind that the girls christened him \"Mr. Greatheart\" on the spot.", "로리와 그의 할아버지가 배웅하러 건너왔고, 브룩 씨가 어찌나 듬직하고 분별 있고 다정해 보이던지, 자매들은 그 자리에서 그를 \"그레이트하트 씨\"라고 이름 붙였다."]], [["\"Good-by, my darlings! God bless and keep us all!\"", "『잘 있으렴, 내 사랑하는 아이들아!"], ["whispered Mrs. March, as she kissed one dear little face after the other, and hurried into the carriage.", "하느님께서 우리 모두를 축복하시고 지켜 주시기를!』 마치 부인이 사랑스러운 작은 얼굴들에 차례로 입을 맞추며 속삭이고는, 서둘러 마차에 올랐다."]], [["As she rolled away, the sun came out, and, looking back, she saw it shining on the group at the gate, like a good omen.", "마차가 굴러가기 시작하자 해가 났고, 부인이 뒤돌아보니 그 햇살이 대문 앞에 모인 무리 위로 비치고 있어 마치 좋은 징조 같았다. 자매들도 그것을 보고 미소 지으며 손을 흔들었다."], ["They saw it also, and smiled and waved their hands; and the last thing she beheld, as she turned the corner, was the four bright faces, and behind them, like a body-guard, old Mr. Laurence, faithful Hannah, and devoted Laurie.", "그리고 모퉁이를 돌며 부인이 마지막으로 본 것은 환한 얼굴 넷이었고, 그 뒤로는 호위병처럼 늙은 로런스 씨와 충직한 해나, 헌신적인 로리가 서 있었다."]], [["\"How kind every one is to us!\" she said, turning to find fresh proof of it in the respectful sympathy of the young man's face.", "『다들 우리한테 어쩜 이리도 친절할까!』 부인이 그 새로운 증거를 찾으려는 듯 고개를 돌리니, 젊은이의 얼굴에 어린 공손한 연민이 보였다."]], [["\"I don't see how they can help it,\" returned Mr.", "『안 그러고는 못 배기는걸요.』 브룩 씨가 어찌나 옮아 오게 웃던지 마치 부인도 따라 웃지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Brooke, laughing so infectiously that Mrs. March could not help smiling; and so the long journey began with the good omens of sunshine, smiles, and cheerful words.", "그렇게 그 긴 여정은 햇살과 미소와 다정한 말이라는 좋은 징조 속에 시작되었다."]], [["\"I feel as if there had been an earthquake,\" said Jo, as their neighbors went home to breakfast, leaving them to rest and refresh themselves.", "『꼭 지진이라도 난 것 같아.』 이웃들이 자매들을 쉬며 기운 차리도록 두고 아침을 먹으러 집으로 돌아가자 조가 말했다."]], [["\"It seems as if half the house was gone,\" added Meg forlornly.", "『집 절반이 사라진 것 같아.』 메그가 쓸쓸하게 덧붙였다."]], [["Beth opened her lips to say something, but could only point to the pile of nicely-mended hose which lay on mother's table, showing that even in her last hurried moments she had thought and worked for them.", "베스가 무슨 말을 하려고 입을 떼었지만, 그저 어머니의 탁자 위에 놓인, 정성껏 기운 양말 더미를 가리킬 수밖에 없었다. 그것은 어머니가 그 마지막 황망한 순간에도 자기들을 생각하며 손을 놀렸다는 증거였다."], ["It was a little thing, but it went straight to their hearts; and, in spite of their brave resolutions, they all broke down, and cried bitterly.", "자그마한 일이었지만, 그것이 곧장 자매들의 가슴을 파고들었다. 그리고 씩씩한 다짐에도 불구하고, 다들 그만 무너져 서럽게 울고 말았다."]], [["Hannah wisely allowed them to relieve their feelings, and, when the shower showed signs of clearing up, she came to the rescue, armed with a coffee-pot.", "해나는 슬기롭게도 그들이 감정을 풀어내도록 내버려 두었다가, 그 소나기가 그칠 기미를 보이자 커피 주전자로 무장한 채 구원에 나섰다."]], [["\"Now, my dear young ladies, remember what your ma said, and don't fret.", "『자, 우리 아가씨들, 어머님이 하신 말씀 잊지 말고 안달하지 마세요."], ["Come and have a cup of coffee all round, and then let's fall to work, and be a credit to the family.\"", "다 같이 커피 한 잔씩 마시고, 그런 다음 일에 매달려서 이 집안의 자랑이 되자고요.』"]], [["Coffee was a treat, and Hannah showed great tact in making it that morning.", "커피는 별미였고, 그날 아침 해나는 그것을 별미로 만드는 데 대단한 솜씨를 발휘했다."], ["No one could resist her persuasive nods, or the fragrant invitation issuing from the nose of the coffee-pot.", "권하듯 끄덕이는 그녀의 고갯짓이나, 주전자 주둥이에서 풍겨 나오는 향긋한 초대를 누구도 뿌리칠 수 없었다."], ["They drew up to the table, exchanged their handkerchiefs for napkins, and in ten minutes were all right again.", "자매들은 식탁에 둘러앉아 손수건을 냅킨으로 바꿔 들었고, 십 분 만에 다들 다시 멀쩡해졌다."]], [["\"'Hope and keep busy;'", "『\"희망을 품고 부지런히 지내자.\""], ["that's the motto for us, so let's see who will remember it best.", "그게 우리 좌우명이니까, 누가 제일 잘 기억하나 보자고."], ["I shall go to Aunt March, as usual.", "난 평소처럼 마치 고모님 댁에 갈 거야."], ["Oh, won't she lecture though!\" said Jo, as she sipped with returning spirit.", "아휴, 또 얼마나 잔소릴 늘어놓으실까!』 조가 기운을 되찾아 커피를 홀짝이며 말했다."]], [["\"I shall go to my Kings, though I'd much rather stay at home and attend to things here,\"", "『난 킹 댁에 가야지."], ["said Meg, wishing she hadn't made her eyes so red.", "정말이지 집에 남아 여기 일을 돌보고 싶긴 하지만.』 메그가 눈이 너무 빨개지지 않았으면 좋겠다고 바라며 말했다."]], [["\"No need of that; Beth and I can keep house perfectly well,\"", "『그럴 필요 없어."], ["put in Amy, with an important air.", "베스랑 나랑 둘이서 집안일 거뜬히 해낼 수 있어.』 에이미가 자못 으스대는 투로 끼어들었다."]], [["\"Hannah will tell us what to do; and we'll have everything nice when you come home,\"", "『해나가 우리한테 뭘 할지 일러 줄 거야."], ["added Beth, getting out her mop and dish-tub without delay.", "언니들이 돌아올 때쯤엔 죄다 말끔하게 해 놓을게.』 베스가 지체 없이 대걸레와 설거지통을 꺼내며 덧붙였다."]], [["\"I think anxiety is very interesting,\" observed Amy, eating sugar, pensively.", "『난 걱정하는 게 무척 흥미로운 일 같아.』 에이미가 시무룩하게 설탕을 집어 먹으며 한마디 했다."]], [["The girls couldn't help laughing, and felt better for it, though Meg shook her head at the young lady who could find consolation in a sugar-bowl.", "자매들은 웃지 않을 수 없었고, 그 덕에 한결 기분이 나아졌다. 비록 메그는 설탕 그릇에서 위안을 찾는 이 아가씨를 향해 고개를 절레절레 흔들었지만 말이다."]], [["The sight of the turn-overs made Jo sober again; and when the two went out to their daily tasks, they looked sorrowfully back at the window where they were accustomed to see their mother's face.", "빵 만두를 보자 조는 다시 진지해졌다. 그리고 두 사람이 매일 할 일을 하러 나설 때, 그들은 늘 어머니의 얼굴을 보곤 하던 창문을 서글피 돌아보았다."], ["It was gone; but Beth had remembered the little household ceremony, and there she was, nodding away at them like a rosy-faced mandarin.", "그 얼굴은 없었다. 하지만 베스가 그 자그마한 집안 의식을 잊지 않고 거기 서서, 발그레한 얼굴의 만다린 인형처럼 그들을 향해 고개를 끄덕이고 있었다."]], [["\"That's so like my Beth!\" said Jo, waving her hat, with a grateful face.", "『꼭 우리 베스답네!』 조가 고마운 얼굴로 모자를 흔들며 말했다."], ["\"Good-by, Meggy; I hope the Kings won't train to-day.", "『잘 가, 메기 언니. 킹 댁 아이들이 오늘은 진을 빼지 말아야 할 텐데."], ["Don't fret about father, dear,\" she added, as they parted.", "아버지 걱정은 말고, 언니.』 헤어지면서 조가 덧붙였다."]], [["\"And I hope Aunt March won't croak.", "『그리고 마치 고모님이 잔소리 안 하셔야 할 텐데."], ["Your hair _is_ becoming, and it looks very boyish and nice,\"", "네 머리 잘 어울려."], ["returned Meg, trying not to smile at the curly head, which looked comically small on her tall sister's shoulders.", "소년 같고 아주 보기 좋다.』 메그가 대꾸하며, 키 큰 동생의 어깨 위에 우스꽝스러울 만큼 작아 보이는 곱슬머리를 보고 웃음을 참았다."]], [["\"That's my only comfort;\" and, touching her hat _à la_ Laurie, away went Jo, feeling like a shorn sheep on a wintry day.", "『그게 내 유일한 위안이지.』 조는 로리처럼 모자에 손을 살짝 갖다 대며 인사하고는, 추운 겨울날 털 깎인 양이라도 된 기분으로 떠나갔다."]], [["News from their father comforted the girls very much; for, though dangerously ill, the presence of the best and tenderest of nurses had already done him good.", "아버지의 소식은 자매들에게 큰 위안이 되었다. 비록 위독하긴 했지만, 더없이 훌륭하고 다정한 간호사가 곁에 있다는 것만으로도 벌써 차도가 있었던 것이다."], ["Mr. Brooke sent a bulletin every day, and, as the head of the family, Meg insisted on reading the despatches, which grew more and more cheering as the week passed.", "브룩 씨가 날마다 병세를 알려 왔고, 집안의 가장으로서 메그가 그 소식을 자기가 읽겠다고 우겼는데, 한 주가 지날수록 소식은 점점 더 반가워졌다."], ["At first, every one was eager to write, and plump envelopes were carefully poked into the letter-box by one or other of the sisters, who felt rather important with their Washington correspondence.", "처음엔 다들 앞다투어 편지를 쓰려 했고, 자매들 중 누군가가 두툼한 봉투를 우체통에 정성껏 밀어 넣곤 했다. 워싱턴과 편지를 주고받는다는 게 어쩐지 자못 대단하게 느껴졌던 것이다."], ["As one of these packets contained characteristic notes from the party, we will rob an imaginary mail, and read them:--", "그 묶음 중 하나에 식구들 저마다의 개성이 담긴 편지들이 들어 있었으니, 우리는 상상 속 우편물을 슬쩍 가로채 그것들을 읽어 보기로 하자. 『더없이 사랑하는 엄마께,"]], [["\"It is impossible to tell you how happy your last letter made us, for the news was so good we couldn't help laughing and crying over it. How very kind Mr.", "지난번 엄마 편지가 우릴 얼마나 행복하게 했는지 이루 다 말할 수가 없어요."], ["Brooke is, and how fortunate that Mr.", "소식이 어찌나 좋던지 웃다가 울다가 했답니다."], ["Laurence's business detains him near you so long, since he is so useful to you and father.", "브룩 씨는 정말 친절하시고, 로런스 어른의 볼일이 그렇게 오래 엄마 가까이 그분을 붙잡아 두다니 또 얼마나 다행인지요. 그분이 엄마와 아버지께 큰 도움이 되어 드리니까요."], ["The girls are all as good as gold.", "동생들은 다들 더없이 착하게 지내요."], ["Jo helps me with the sewing, and insists on doing all sorts of hard jobs.", "조는 제 바느질을 돕고, 온갖 힘든 일을 죄다 자기가 하겠다고 우긴답니다."], ["I should be afraid she might overdo, if I didn't know that her 'moral fit' wouldn't last long.", "조의 \"도덕적 발작\"이 오래가지 않으리란 걸 몰랐다면, 무리하다 탈이라도 날까 걱정했을 거예요."], ["Beth is as regular about her tasks as a clock, and never forgets what you told her.", "베스는 시계처럼 어김없이 제 할 일을 하고, 엄마가 일러 준 걸 절대 잊는 법이 없어요."], ["She grieves about father, and looks sober except when she is at her little piano.", "아버지 일로 슬퍼하고, 작은 피아노 앞에 앉아 있을 때 말고는 표정이 시무룩해요."], ["Amy minds me nicely, and I take great care of her.", "에이미는 제 말을 잘 듣고, 저도 그 애를 정성껏 돌보고 있어요."], ["She does her own hair, and I am teaching her to make button-holes and mend her stockings.", "머리도 혼자 손질하고, 제가 단춧구멍 내는 법이랑 양말 깁는 법을 가르치는 중이에요."], ["She tries very hard, and I know you will be pleased with her improvement when you come. Mr.", "무척 열심히 하니까, 엄마가 돌아오시면 그 애가 부쩍 나아진 걸 보고 분명 기뻐하실 거예요."], ["Laurence watches over us like a motherly old hen, as Jo says; and Laurie is very kind and neighborly.", "로런스 어른은, 조 말마따나, 어미 닭처럼 우릴 보살펴 주세요. 그리고 로리는 무척 친절하고 이웃다워요."], ["He and Jo keep us merry, for we get pretty blue sometimes, and feel like orphans, with you so far away.", "그 애랑 조가 우릴 즐겁게 해 준답니다. 엄마가 그렇게 멀리 계시니 우리도 가끔은 꽤나 울적해져서 고아가 된 기분이 들거든요."], ["Hannah is a perfect saint; she does not scold at all, and always calls me Miss 'Margaret,'", "해나는 그야말로 성인이에요. 야단이라곤 도통 안 치고, 늘 저를 \"마거릿 아씨\"라고 부르는데, 그게 아주 격에 맞잖아요, 그렇죠?"], ["which is quite proper, you know, and treats me with respect.", "저를 깍듯이 대해 준답니다. 우린 다들 건강하고 바쁘게 지내요."], ["We are all well and busy; but we long, day and night, to have you back.", "하지만 밤낮으로 엄마가 돌아오시기만을 손꼽아 기다린답니다. 아버지께 제 더없는 사랑을 전해 주세요."], ["Give my dearest love to father, and believe me, ever your own \"MEG.\"", "그리고 언제까지나 엄마의 딸임을 믿어 주세요. 메그 올림.』"]], [["This note, prettily written on scented paper, was a great contrast to the next, which was scribbled on a big sheet of thin foreign paper, ornamented with blots and all manner of flourishes and curly-tailed letters:--", "향기 나는 종이에 곱게 쓴 이 편지는 다음 편지와 큰 대조를 이루었다. 그 편지는 커다란 얇은 외제 종이에 갈겨썼는데, 잉크 얼룩에다 온갖 멋 부린 장식과 끄트머리가 동그랗게 말린 글자들로 치장되어 있었다. 『소중한 우리 마미께,"]], [["\"Three cheers for dear father!", "사랑하는 아버지를 위해 만세 삼창!"], ["Brooke was a trump to telegraph right off, and let us know the minute he was better.", "브룩 씨는 정말 멋진 분이에요. 아버지가 차도를 보이자마자 그 순간 바로 전보를 쳐서 알려 줬거든요."], ["I rushed up garret when the letter came, and tried to thank God for being so good to us; but I could only cry, and say, 'I'm glad!", "편지가 왔을 때 저는 다락방으로 뛰어 올라가서, 우리한테 이리도 잘해 주신 하느님께 감사드리려 했어요. 그런데 그저 울면서 \"기뻐요!"], ["I'm glad!'", "기뻐요!\""], ["Didn't that do as well as a regular prayer?", "하는 말밖에 못 했지 뭐예요. 그래도 정식 기도만큼은 됐겠죠?"], ["for I felt a great many in my heart.", "제 마음속엔 수없이 많은 기도가 있었으니까요. 우린 정말 재밌게 지내요."], ["We have such funny times; and now I can enjoy them, for every one is so desperately good, it's like living in a nest of turtle-doves.", "그리고 이젠 그걸 즐길 수 있어요. 다들 어찌나 끔찍하게 착한지, 꼭 멧비둘기 둥지 속에 사는 것 같거든요."], ["You'd laugh to see Meg head the table and try to be motherish.", "메그 언니가 식탁 상석에 앉아 엄마 노릇을 하려고 애쓰는 걸 보면 엄마도 웃으실 거예요."], ["She gets prettier every day, and I'm in love with her sometimes.", "언니는 날이 갈수록 예뻐져서, 가끔은 제가 다 반할 지경이에요. 동생들은 영락없는 천사들이고, 저는, 뭐, 저는 조죠."], ["The children are regular archangels, and I--well, I'm Jo, and never shall be anything else.", "앞으로도 영영 다른 사람은 못 될 거고요. 아 참, 로리랑 하마터면 다툴 뻔한 얘길 해 드려야겠네요."], ["Oh, I must tell you that I came near having a quarrel with Laurie.", "제가 사소하고 어리석은 일을 두고 속에 있는 말을 다 해 버렸더니, 그 애가 기분 나빠 했어요. 제 말이 옳긴 했지만, 말투가 영 마땅찮았던 거예요."], ["I freed my mind about a silly little thing, and he was offended.", "그 애는 제가 사과할 때까진 다신 안 오겠다면서 집으로 가 버렸어요. 저도 절대 사과 안 하겠다며 화를 냈고요."], ["I was right, but didn't speak as I ought, and he marched home, saying he wouldn't come again till I begged pardon.", "하루 종일 그러고 있으려니 마음이 안 좋아서, 엄마가 무척 보고 싶었어요. 로리랑 저는 둘 다 자존심이 어찌나 센지 사과하기가 참 어렵거든요."], ["I declared I wouldn't, and got mad.", "그래도 제가 옳았으니까, 그 애가 먼저 굽히고 들어올 줄 알았어요."], ["It lasted all day; I felt bad, and wanted you very much.", "그런데 안 오더라고요. 그러다 밤이 되어서야, 에이미가 강에 빠졌을 때 엄마가 하신 말씀이 떠올랐어요."], ["Laurie and I are both so proud, it's hard to beg pardon; but I thought he'd come to it, for I _was_ in the right.", "제 작은 책을 읽으니 마음이 좀 나아졌고, 제 노여움을 품은 채로 해를 넘기지 말자고 마음먹고는, 로리한테 미안하다고 말하려고 한달음에 달려갔어요."], ["He didn't come; and just at night I remembered what you said when Amy fell into the river.", "그런데 대문에서 그 애를 만났지 뭐예요. 그 애도 똑같은 마음으로 오던 길이었던 거예요."], ["I read my little book, felt better, resolved not to let the sun set on _my_ anger, and ran over to tell Laurie I was sorry.", "우린 둘 다 웃음을 터뜨리며 서로에게 사과했고, 다시 다 풀려서 편안해졌답니다. 어제 해나가 빨래하는 걸 돕다가 \"시\"를 한 편 지었어요."], ["I met him at the gate, coming for the same thing.", "아버지가 제 어설픈 끄적임을 좋아하시니까, 아버지를 즐겁게 해 드리려고 같이 넣어요."], ["We both laughed, begged each other's pardon, and felt all good and comfortable again.", "아버지를 세상에서 제일 사랑스럽게 꼭 안아 드리고, 엄마도 엄마 자신한테 열두 번쯤 입맞춰 주세요."]], [["\"I made a 'pome' yesterday, when I was helping Hannah wash; and, as father likes my silly little things, I put it in to amuse him. Give him the lovingest hug that ever was, and kiss yourself a dozen times for your \"TOPSY-TURVY JO.\"", "뒤죽박죽 조 올림.』\n『비눗물에서 부르는 노래"]], [["\"Queen of my tub, I merrily sing, While the white foam rises high; And sturdily wash and rinse and wring, And fasten the clothes to dry; Then out in the free fresh air they swing, Under the sunny sky.", "내 빨래통의 여왕, 나는 즐거이 노래하네, 하얀 거품이 높이 솟아오를 때. 씩씩하게 빨고 헹구고 비틀어 짜고, 빨랫줄에 옷가지를 널어 말리지. 그러면 시원하고 상쾌한 바깥 공기 속에 옷들이 나부끼네, 햇살 비치는 하늘 아래에서. 우리 마음과 영혼에서도 빨아 낼 수 있다면, 한 주 동안 묻은 얼룩을 말끔히. 물과 공기가 마법을 부려 우리 자신도 저 옷들처럼 깨끗하게 해 준다면, 그땐 이 땅에도 정녕 찬란한 빨랫날이 찾아오리라!"]], [["\"I wish we could wash from our hearts and souls The stains of the week away, And let water and air by their magic make Ourselves as pure as they; Then on the earth there would be indeed A glorious washing-day!", "쓸모 있는 삶의 오솔길을 따라서는 마음의 평안이 늘 꽃피우리. 바쁜 마음에는 슬픔이나 근심이나 우울을 생각할 겨를이 없으니. 근심스러운 생각들도 쓸어 낼 수 있으리, 우리가 씩씩하게 빗자루를 휘두를 때. 내게 할 일이 주어진 것이 기쁘다네, 날마다 매달려 애쓸 일이. 그것은 내게 건강과 힘과 희망을 가져다주니, 나는 기꺼이 이렇게 말하기를 배운다네. \"머리야, 너는 생각해도 좋아, 마음아, 너는 느껴도 좋아, 하지만 손아, 너는 언제나 일하여라!\"』"]], [["\"Along the path of a useful life, Will heart's-ease ever bloom; The busy mind has no time to think Of sorrow or care or gloom; And anxious thoughts may be swept away, As we bravely wield a broom.", "『사랑하는 엄마께,\n제 사랑과, 아버지께 보여 드리려고 집 안에 고이 간직해 둔 그 뿌리에서 딴 팬지꽃을 눌러 말린 것 몇 송이를 보낼 자리밖에 없네요. 저는 아침마다 책을 읽고, 하루 종일 착하게 지내려 애쓰고, 아버지가 즐겨 부르던 곡조를 부르며 잠들어요. 이제 \"충직한 자들의 땅\"은 못 부르겠어요. 그 노랜 자꾸 눈물이 나거든요. 다들 무척 친절하고, 우린 엄마가 안 계셔도 더없이 행복하게 지내요. 에이미가 남은 자리를 쓰고 싶다니까 저는 이만 줄여야겠어요. 냄비 받침에 천 씌우는 거 잊지 않았고, 매일 시계태엽을 감고 방마다 환기도 시켜요."]], [["\"I am glad a task to me is given, To labor at day by day; For it brings me health and strength and hope, And I cheerfully learn to say,-- 'Head, you may think, Heart, you may feel, But, Hand, you shall work alway!'\"", "아버지께서 제 것이라고 하시는 그 뺨에 입맞춤해 주세요. 아, 부디 어서 돌아오세요, 엄마를 사랑하는\n어린 베스가.』"]], [["\"There is only room for me to send my love, and some pressed pansies from the root I have been keeping safe in the house for father to see.", "『사랑하는 엄마께,\n우리는 모두 잘 지내요 저는 늘 공부를 하고 절대로 언니들을 협동하지 않아요—메그 언니가 반박이라는 뜻이랬는데 그래서 두 단어를 다 적으니 엄마가 더 알맞은 걸 고르세요. 메그 언니는 저한테 큰 위로가 돼 주고 매일 차 마실 때 젤리를 먹게 해 줘요 그게 저한테 그렇게 좋대요 조 언니 말로는 그래야 제 성질이 곱게 유지된다나 봐요."], ["I read every morning, try to be good all day, and sing myself to sleep with father's tune.", "로리는 이제 제가 거의 십 대인데도 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 공손하지가 않아요, 저를 병아리라고 부르고 제가 해티 킹처럼 메르시나 봉주르라고 하면 프랑스어를 아주 빠르게 지껄여 대서 제 기분을 상하게 해요."], ["I can't sing 'Land of the Leal' now; it makes me cry.", "제 파란 원피스 소매가 죄다 해져서 메그 언니가 새 소매를 달아 줬는데 앞판 전체가 잘못 나와서 원피스보다 더 파래요."], ["Every one is very kind, and we are as happy as we can be without you.", "속상했지만 안달하진 않았어요 저는 제 어려움을 잘 견디거든요 하지만 해나가 제 앞치마에 풀을 좀 더 먹여 주고 매일 메밀 빵을 만들어 주면 정말 좋겠어요."], ["Amy wants the rest of the page, so I must stop.", "그래 줄 수 없을까요? 저 그 물음표 잘 만들지 않았어요?"], ["I didn't forget to cover the holders, and I wind the clock and air the rooms every day.", "메그 언니가 제 구두점이랑 철자가 형편없다고 해서 부끄러워요 하지만 어머 저는 할 일이 너무 많아서 멈출 수가 없어요. 안녕히 계세요, 아빠께 사랑을 가득가득 보내요."]], [["\"Kiss dear father on the cheek he calls mine. Oh, do come soon to your loving \"LITTLE BETH.\"", "엄마를 사랑하는 딸, 에이미 커티스 마치 올림.』\n* 협동·반박: 에이미가 \"반박하다(contradict)\"를 \"협동하다(corroborate)\"로 잘못 쓴 것을 그대로 옮긴 것이다 : 옮긴이."]], [["\"We are all well I do my lessons always and never corroberate the girls--Meg says I mean contradick so I put in both words and you can take the properest.", "『친애하는 마치 마님께,\n저희가 아주 잘 지낸다는 말씀 한 줄 적습니다요. 아씨들은 영리하고 야무지게 잘들 돌아댕깁니다요."], ["Meg is a great comfort to me and lets me have jelly every night at tea its so good for me Jo says because it keeps me sweet tempered.", "메그 아씨는 어엿한 살림꾼이 되실 겁니다요. 그쪽에 취미가 있으셔서 일머리를 놀랍도록 빨리 익히세요."], ["Laurie is not as respeckful as he ought to be now I am almost in my teens, he calls me Chick and hurts my feelings by talking French to me very fast when I say Merci or Bon jour as Hattie King does.", "조 아씨는 척척 해치우는 데는 당할 사람이 없는데, 먼저 따져 보질 않으셔서 어디서 일을 끝맺으실지 도통 알 수가 없습죠. 월요일에 빨래 한 통을 다 해 놓으셨는데, 헹구기도 전에 풀을 먹이질 않나, 분홍 옥양목 원피스를 파랗게 물들여 놓으셔서 제가 웃다가 죽는 줄 알았습니다요."], ["The sleeves of my blue dress were all worn out, and Meg put in new ones, but the full front came wrong and they are more blue than the dress.", "베스 아씨는 더없이 착한 아이로, 손이 야무지고 미더워서 저한테 큰 도움이 됩죠. 뭐든 배우려 하고, 정말이지 제 나이답지 않게 장도 잘 봐 와요."], ["I felt bad but did not fret I bear my troubles well but I do wish Hannah would put more starch in my aprons and have buckwheats every day.", "또 제 도움을 받아 가며 장부도 어찌나 잘 적는지 놀랍습니다요. 여태 아주 알뜰하게 살림을 꾸려 왔습니다요."], ["Can't she? Didn't I make that interrigation point nice?", "마님 뜻대로 아씨들한텐 커피는 일주일에 한 번만 드리고, 늘 소박하고 몸에 좋은 음식만 먹입죠."], ["Meg says my punchtuation and spelling are disgraceful and I am mortyfied but dear me I have so many things to do, I can't stop.", "에이미 아씨는 제일 좋은 옷을 입고 단것을 먹으며 안달하지 않고 잘 지냅니다요. 로리 도련님은 여전히 장난기가 넘쳐서 집을 뒤집어 놓기 일쑤지만, 아씨들 기운을 북돋아 주니 저도 마음껏 놀게 둡니다요."], ["Adieu, I send heaps of love to Papa.", "노어르신은 온갖 것들을 잔뜩 보내 주시는데 좀 성가시긴 해도 뜻은 좋으시니, 제가 뭐라 할 처지는 아닙죠."], ["\"Your affectionate daughter, \"AMY CURTIS MARCH.\"", "빵 반죽이 부풀었으니 이만 줄입니다요. 마치 어른께 안부 여쭙고, 부디 그 폐렴이라는 것과는 영영 작별하셨기를 바랍니다요."]], [["\"I jes drop a line to say we git on fust rate. The girls is clever and fly round right smart. Miss Meg is going to make a proper good housekeeper; she hes the liking for it, and gits the hang of things surprisin quick. Jo doos beat all for goin ahead, but she don't stop to cal'k'late fust, and you never know where she's like to bring up. She done out a tub of clothes on Monday, but she starched 'em afore they was wrenched, and blued a pink calico dress till I thought I should a died a laughin. Beth is the best of little creeters, and a sight of help to me, bein so forehanded and dependable. She tries to learn everything, and really goes to market beyond her years; likewise keeps accounts, with my help, quite wonderful. We have got on very economical so fur; I don't let the girls hev coffee only once a week, accordin to your wish, and keep em on plain wholesome vittles. Amy does well about frettin, wearin her best clothes and eatin sweet stuff. Mr. Laurie is as full of didoes as usual, and turns the house upside down frequent; but he heartens up the girls, and so I let em hev full swing. The old gentleman sends heaps of things, and is rather wearin, but means wal, and it aint my place to say nothin. My bread is riz, so no more at this time. I send my duty to Mr. March, and hope he's seen the last of his Pewmonia. \"Yours Respectful, \"HANNAH MULLET.\"", "삼가 올림, 해나 멀릿.』\n* 폐렴: 해나가 \"폐렴(Pneumonia)\"을 \"퓨모니아(Pewmonia)\"로 잘못 쓴 것을 옮긴 것이다 : 옮긴이."]], [["\"All serene on the Rappahannock, troops in fine condition, commissary department well conducted, the Home Guard under Colonel Teddy always on duty, Commander-in-chief General Laurence reviews the army daily, Quartermaster Mullett keeps order in camp, and Major Lion does picket duty at night.", "『제2병동 수간호사께,\n래퍼해녹 강가는 모두 평온하고, 부대 상태 양호하며, 보급부는 잘 운영되고 있음. 테디 대령 휘하의 향토방위대는 늘 근무 중이고, 총사령관 로런스 장군이 매일 군대를 사열하며, 병참장교 멀릿이 야영지 질서를 유지하고, 라이언 소령이 야간 보초를 섬."], ["A salute of twenty-four guns was fired on receipt of good news from Washington, and a dress parade took place at head-quarters.", "워싱턴에서 온 희소식을 받고 스물네 발의 예포를 발사했으며, 사령부에서 사열식이 거행됨."], ["Commander-in-chief sends best wishes, in which he is heartily joined by \"COLONEL TEDDY.\"", "총사령관이 안부를 전하며, 여기에 진심으로 뜻을 같이하는 사람,"]], [["\"The little girls are all well; Beth and my boy report daily; Hannah is a model servant, and guards pretty Meg like a dragon. Glad the fine weather holds; pray make Brooke useful, and draw on me for funds if expenses exceed your estimate. Don't let your husband want anything. Thank God he is mending. \"Your sincere friend and servant, \"JAMES LAURENCE.\"", "테디 대령.』\n『친애하는 부인께,"]], [["# LITTLE FAITHFUL.", "# LITTLE FAITHFUL."]], [["For a week the amount of virtue in the old house would have supplied the neighborhood.", "어린 아가씨들은 모두 잘 있습니다. 베스와 우리 아이가 매일 소식을 전합니다."], ["It was really amazing, for every one seemed in a heavenly frame of mind, and self-denial was all the fashion.", "해나는 모범적인 일꾼으로, 어여쁜 메그를 용처럼 지키고 있습니다. 좋은 날씨가 이어져 다행입니다."], ["Relieved of their first anxiety about their father, the girls insensibly relaxed their praiseworthy efforts a little, and began to fall back into the old ways.", "부디 브룩을 요긴하게 부리시고, 비용이 예상을 넘으면 제게 청구하십시오. 남편분께서 부족한 것이 없도록 해 주십시오."], ["They did not forget their motto, but hoping and keeping busy seemed to grow easier; and after such tremendous exertions, they felt that Endeavor deserved a holiday, and gave it a good many.", "차도가 있다니 하느님께 감사드립니다. 당신의 진실한 벗이자 종, 제임스 로런스.』"]], [["Jo caught a bad cold through neglect to cover the shorn head enough, and was ordered to stay at home till she was better, for Aunt March didn't like to hear people read with colds in their heads.", "17장 충직한 어린 사람\n한 주 동안 이 오래된 집에 쌓인 미덕의 양은 온 동네에 나누어 줘도 될 만했다."], ["Jo liked this, and after an energetic rummage from garret to cellar, subsided on the sofa to nurse her cold with arsenicum and books. Amy found that housework and art did not go well together, and returned to her mud pies.", "정말이지 놀라울 지경이었으니, 다들 천국에라도 있는 듯한 마음가짐이었고, 극기야말로 최고의 유행이었다."], ["Meg went daily to her pupils, and sewed, or thought she did, at home, but much time was spent in writing long letters to her mother, or reading the Washington despatches over and over. Beth kept on, with only slight relapses into idleness or grieving.", "아버지에 대한 첫 근심을 덜자 자매들은 자기도 모르게 그 갸륵한 노력을 조금씩 늦추기 시작했고, 다시 옛 버릇으로 돌아갔다."], ["All the little duties were faithfully done each day, and many of her sisters' also, for they were forgetful, and the house seemed like a clock whose pendulum was gone a-visiting.", "좌우명을 잊은 건 아니었지만, 희망을 품고 부지런히 지내는 일이 점점 수월해지는 듯했다."], ["When her heart got heavy with longings for mother or fears for father, she went away into a certain closet, hid her face in the folds of a certain dear old gown, and made her little moan and prayed her little prayer quietly by herself. Nobody knew what cheered her up after a sober fit, but every one felt how sweet and helpful Beth was, and fell into a way of going to her for comfort or advice in their small affairs.", "그렇게 어마어마하게 애를 쓰고 나니, '노력'에도 휴일이 마땅하다 싶어 휴일을 꽤 여러 날 주었다."]], [["All were unconscious that this experience was a test of character; and, when the first excitement was over, felt that they had done well, and deserved praise. So they did; but their mistake was in ceasing to do well, and they learned this lesson through much anxiety and regret.", "조는 짧아진 머리를 충분히 덮지 않아 독한 감기에 걸렸고, 나을 때까지 집에 있으라는 명을 받았다. 마치 고모가 머릿속에 감기 든 사람이 책 읽는 소리를 듣기 싫어했던 것이다. 조는 이게 마음에 들어서, 다락방부터 지하실까지 기운차게 뒤지고 다닌 뒤 소파에 자리 잡고 비소제와 책으로 감기를 다스렸다. 에이미는 집안일과 예술이 영 어울리지 않는다는 걸 깨닫고 다시 진흙 파이로 돌아갔다. 메그는 날마다 가르치는 아이들에게 갔고, 집에서는 바느질을 하거나 한다고 여겼지만, 사실은 어머니에게 긴 편지를 쓰거나 워싱턴에서 온 소식을 읽고 또 읽느라 많은 시간을 보냈다. 베스는 이따금 게으름을 피우거나 슬픔에 잠기는 일이 잠깐 있긴 했어도 꾸준히 제 할 일을 해 나갔다. 소소한 집안일은 날마다 빠짐없이 충실히 해냈고, 언니들 몫까지 도맡아 했다. 언니들은 잘 잊어버렸고, 집은 마치 추가 마실이라도 나간 시계 같았던 것이다. 어머니가 그립거나 아버지가 걱정되어 마음이 무거워지면, 베스는 어떤 벽장 안으로 들어가 어떤 정든 낡은 가운 자락에 얼굴을 묻고, 혼자 조용히 조그맣게 흐느끼며 조그맣게 기도를 올렸다. 베스가 침울한 발작 뒤에 어떻게 기운을 차렸는지는 아무도 몰랐지만, 다들 베스가 얼마나 사랑스럽고 의지가 되는지를 느꼈고, 사소한 일에 위로나 조언을 구할 때면 으레 그 애를 찾게 되었다. 이 경험이 인격의 시험이라는 걸 다들 알아채지 못했다. 그리고 처음의 흥분이 가시자, 자기들이 잘해 냈으니 칭찬받아 마땅하다고 여겼다. 정말 그러긴 했다. 하지만 그들의 잘못은 잘하던 것을 그만둔 데 있었으니, 그들은 숱한 근심과 후회를 치르고 나서야 이 교훈을 배웠다."]], [["\"Meg, I wish you'd go and see the Hummels; you know mother told us not to forget them,\"", "『메그 언니, 언니가 험멜네 좀 다녀와 줬으면 좋겠어."], ["said Beth, ten days after Mrs. March's departure.", "알잖아, 엄마가 그 집 사람들 잊지 말라고 하셨잖아.』 마치 부인이 떠난 지 열흘째 되던 날, 베스가 말했다."]], [["\"I'm too tired to go this afternoon,\" replied Meg, rocking comfortably as she sewed.", "『오늘 오후엔 너무 피곤해서 못 가겠어.』 메그가 편안히 흔들의자에 앉아 바느질하며 대꾸했다."]], [["\"Can't you, Jo?\" asked Beth.", "『조 언니는 못 가?』 베스가 물었다."]], [["\"Too stormy for me with my cold.\"", "『감기 든 데다 날씨가 너무 사나워서 안 되겠어.』"]], [["\"I thought it was almost well.\"", "『거의 다 나은 줄 알았는데.』"]], [["\"It's well enough for me to go out with Laurie, but not well enough to go to the Hummels',\" said Jo, laughing, but looking a little ashamed of her inconsistency.", "『로리랑 나갈 만큼은 나았지만, 험멜네까지 갈 만큼은 아니야.』 조가 웃으며 말했지만, 자기 모순에 좀 멋쩍어하는 기색이었다."]], [["\"Why don't you go yourself?\" asked Meg.", "『네가 직접 가면 되잖아.』 메그가 물었다."]], [["\"I _have_ been every day, but the baby is sick, and I don't know what to do for it.", "『난 매일 다녀왔단 말이야."], ["Mrs.", "그런데 그 집 아기가 아픈데 어떻게 해 줘야 할지 모르겠어."], ["Hummel goes away to work, and Lottchen takes care of it; but it gets sicker and sicker, and I think you or Hannah ought to go.\"", "험멜 부인은 일하러 나가고 로트헨이 아기를 돌보는데, 점점 더 심해지기만 해. 언니나 해나가 가 봐야 할 것 같아.』"]], [["Beth spoke earnestly, and Meg promised she would go to-morrow.", "베스가 간곡하게 말하자, 메그는 내일 다녀오겠다고 약속했다."]], [["\"Ask Hannah for some nice little mess, and take it round, Beth; the air will do you good,\"", "『해나한테 맛있는 음식 좀 얻어서 가져다주렴, 베스. 바람 쐬면 너한테도 좋을 거야.』 조가 말하고는 미안한 듯 덧붙였다."], ["said Jo, adding apologetically, \"I'd go, but I want to finish my writing.\"", "『나도 가고 싶지만, 글을 마저 끝내고 싶어서.』"]], [["\"My head aches and I'm tired, so I thought may be some of you would go,\" said Beth.", "『머리도 아프고 피곤해서, 너희 중 누가 가 주려나 했지.』 베스가 말했다."]], [["\"Amy will be in presently, and she will run down for us,\" suggested Meg.", "『에이미가 곧 들어올 테니까, 그 애가 우리 대신 달려가 줄 거야.』 메그가 제안했다."]], [["\"Well, I'll rest a little and wait for her.\"", "『그래, 그럼 좀 쉬면서 그 애를 기다릴게.』"]], [["So Beth lay down on the sofa, the others returned to their work, and the Hummels were forgotten.", "그렇게 베스는 소파에 누웠고, 나머지는 다시 하던 일로 돌아갔으며, 험멜네는 잊히고 말았다. 한 시간이 지났다."], ["An hour passed: Amy did not come; Meg went to her room to try on a new dress; Jo was absorbed in her story, and Hannah was sound asleep before the kitchen fire, when Beth quietly put on her hood, filled her basket with odds and ends for the poor children, and went out into the chilly air, with a heavy head, and a grieved look in her patient eyes.", "에이미는 오지 않았다. 메그는 새 원피스를 입어 보러 자기 방으로 갔고, 조는 이야기에 푹 빠져 있었으며, 해나는 부엌 난롯불 앞에서 곤히 잠들어 있었다."], ["It was late when she came back, and no one saw her creep upstairs and shut herself into her mother's room.", "그때 베스가 조용히 두건을 쓰고, 바구니에 가난한 아이들에게 줄 자질구레한 것들을 채워, 무거운 머리와 인내심 어린 두 눈에 서글픈 빛을 띤 채 쌀쌀한 바깥으로 나갔다."], ["Half an hour after Jo went to \"mother's closet\"", "베스가 돌아온 건 늦은 시각이었고, 그 애가 살그머니 위층으로 올라가 어머니 방에 들어가 문을 닫는 걸 본 사람은 아무도 없었다."], ["for something, and there found Beth sitting on the medicine chest, looking very grave, with red eyes, and a camphor-bottle in her hand.", "삼십 분 뒤 조가 뭘 가지러 \"엄마 벽장\"에 갔다가, 베스가 약상자 위에 앉아 있는 걸 발견했다. 눈이 빨갛고 손에는 장뇌 병을 든 채, 무척이나 심각한 얼굴을 하고 있었다."]], [["\"Christopher Columbus!", "『맙소사!"], ["What's the matter?\" cried Jo, as Beth put out her hand as if to warn her off, and asked quickly,--", "무슨 일이야?』 베스가 가까이 오지 말라는 듯 손을 내밀며 다급히 묻자 조가 외쳤다."]], [["\"You've had the scarlet fever, haven't you?\"", "『언니 성홍열 앓은 적 있지?』"]], [["\"Years ago, when Meg did.", "『옛날에 메그 언니랑 같이 앓았지."], ["Why?\"", "왜?』"]], [["\"Then I'll tell you.", "『그럼 말할게."], ["Oh, Jo, the baby's dead!\"", "아, 조 언니, 그 아기가 죽었어!』"]], [["\"What baby?\"", "『무슨 아기?』"]], [["\"Mrs. Hummel's; it died in my lap before she got home,\"", "『험멜 부인네 아기."], ["cried Beth, with a sob.", "부인이 집에 오기도 전에 내 무릎에서 죽었어.』 베스가 흐느끼며 외쳤다."]], [["\"My poor dear, how dreadful for you!", "『가엾은 우리 베스, 얼마나 끔찍했을까!"], ["I ought to have gone,\" said Jo, taking her sister in her arms as she sat down in her mother's big chair, with a remorseful face.", "내가 갔어야 했는데.』 조가 후회 가득한 얼굴로 어머니의 커다란 의자에 앉으며 동생을 두 팔로 끌어안았다."]], [["\"It wasn't dreadful, Jo, only so sad!", "『끔찍하진 않았어, 조 언니. 그냥 너무 슬펐어!"], ["I saw in a minute that it was sicker, but Lottchen said her mother had gone for a doctor, so I took baby and let Lotty rest.", "아기가 더 안 좋아진 게 한눈에 보였는데, 로트헨이 자기 엄마가 의사를 부르러 갔다길래, 내가 아기를 안고 로티를 좀 쉬게 했어."], ["It seemed asleep, but all of a sudden it gave a little cry, and trembled, and then lay very still.", "잠든 것 같았는데, 갑자기 가냘프게 한번 울더니, 부르르 떨고는, 아주 가만히 있는 거야."], ["I tried to warm its feet, and Lotty gave it some milk, but it didn't stir, and I knew it was dead.\"", "발을 따뜻하게 해 주려고 했고, 로티가 우유도 좀 먹였는데, 꿈쩍도 안 해서, 죽은 걸 알았어.』"]], [["\"Don't cry, dear!", "『울지 마, 얘."], ["What did you do?\"", "그래서 어떻게 했어?』"]], [["\"I just sat and held it softly till Mrs. Hummel came with the doctor.", "『그냥 앉아서 살며시 안고 있었어, 험멜 부인이 의사 선생님이랑 올 때까지."], ["He said it was dead, and looked at Heinrich and Minna, who have got sore throats.", "선생님이 아기가 죽었다고 하고는, 목이 부어오른 하인리히랑 미나를 보더라."], ["'Scarlet fever, ma'am.", "\"성홍열입니다, 부인."], ["Ought to have called me before,'", "진작 절 불렀어야죠.\""], ["he said crossly. Mrs.", "하고 퉁명스럽게 말했어."], ["Hummel told him she was poor, and had tried to cure baby herself, but now it was too late, and she could only ask him to help the others, and trust to charity for his pay.", "험멜 부인이 자긴 가난해서 직접 아기를 고쳐 보려 했다고, 그런데 이젠 너무 늦었으니 다른 아이들이라도 살려 달라고, 진료비는 자비를 베풀어 달라고 부탁했어."], ["He smiled then, and was kinder; but it was very sad, and I cried with them till he turned round, all of a sudden, and told me to go home and take belladonna right away, or I'd have the fever.\"", "그제야 선생님이 미소를 짓고 좀 더 다정해졌어. 하지만 너무 슬퍼서 나도 그 사람들이랑 같이 울었는데, 선생님이 별안간 돌아서더니 나더러 당장 집에 가서 벨라도나를 먹으라고, 안 그러면 나도 열병에 걸린다고 했어.』"]], [["\"No, you won't!\" cried Jo, hugging her close, with a frightened look.", "『아니, 언닌 안 걸릴 거야!』 조가 겁에 질린 얼굴로 동생을 꼭 끌어안으며 외쳤다."], ["\"O Beth, if you should be sick I never could forgive myself!", "『아, 베스, 언니가 아프기라도 하면 난 나 자신을 영영 용서 못 할 거야!"], ["What _shall_ we do?\"", "우리 어떡하지?』"]], [["\"Don't be frightened, I guess I shan't have it badly.", "『겁먹지 마. 심하게 걸리진 않을 것 같아."], ["I looked in mother's book, and saw that it begins with headache, sore throat, and queer feelings like mine, so I did take some belladonna, and I feel better,\"", "엄마 책을 봤더니, 두통이랑 인후통이랑 나처럼 이상한 느낌으로 시작한다고 적혀 있더라고."], ["said Beth, laying her cold hands on her hot forehead, and trying to look well.", "그래서 벨라도나를 좀 먹었더니 한결 나아.』 베스가 차가운 손을 뜨거운 이마에 갖다 대며, 멀쩡해 보이려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["\"If mother was only at home!\"", "『엄마가 집에 계시기만 했어도!』 조가 책을 움켜쥐며 외쳤다."], ["exclaimed Jo, seizing the book, and feeling that Washington was an immense way off.", "워싱턴이 까마득히 멀게만 느껴졌다. 조는 한 쪽을 읽고, 베스를 보고, 이마를 짚어 보고, 목구멍을 들여다본 뒤, 심각하게 말했다."], ["She read a page, looked at Beth, felt her head, peeped into her throat, and then said gravely; \"You've been over the baby every day for more than a week, and among the others who are going to have it; so I'm afraid _you_ are going to have it, Beth.", "『언니, 일주일 넘게 매일 그 아기 곁에 있었던 데다, 앞으로 그 병에 걸릴 다른 애들 사이에도 있었잖아. 그러니까 아무래도 언니도 걸릴 것 같아, 베스."], ["I'll call Hannah, she knows all about sickness.\"", "해나를 부를게. 그 사람은 병이라면 훤히 아니까.』"]], [["\"Don't let Amy come; she never had it, and I should hate to give it to her.", "『에이미는 못 오게 해. 그 앤 한 번도 안 걸렸으니까, 옮기면 정말 싫을 거야."], ["Can't you and Meg have it over again?\" asked Beth, anxiously.", "언니랑 메그 언니는 다시 걸리진 않을까?』 베스가 근심스레 물었다."]], [["\"I guess not; don't care if I do; serve me right, selfish pig, to let you go, and stay writing rubbish myself!\"", "『안 걸릴 것 같아. 걸려도 상관없어."], ["muttered Jo, as she went to consult Hannah.", "언니를 보내 놓고 나 혼자 앉아 시답잖은 글이나 쓰고 있었으니, 이기적인 돼지 같으니, 그래도 싸지!』 조는 중얼거리며 해나에게 의논하러 갔다."]], [["The good soul was wide awake in a minute, and took the lead at once, assuring Jo that there was no need to worry; every one had scarlet fever, and, if rightly treated, nobody died,--all of which Jo believed, and felt much relieved as they went up to call Meg.", "마음씨 고운 해나는 순식간에 잠이 확 깨어 곧장 일을 떠맡았다. 걱정할 것 없다고, 성홍열은 누구나 앓는 병이며 제대로 치료만 하면 죽는 사람은 없다고 조를 안심시켰다. 조는 그 말을 다 믿고는 한결 마음이 놓인 채, 두 사람은 메그를 부르러 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["\"Now I'll tell you what we'll do,\" said Hannah, when she had examined and questioned Beth; \"we will have Dr.", "『자, 우리가 어떻게 할지 일러 드릴게요.』 베스를 살펴보고 이것저것 물어본 뒤 해나가 말했다."], ["Bangs, just to take a look at you, dear, and see that we start right; then we'll send Amy off to Aunt March's, for a spell, to keep her out of harm's way, and one of you girls can stay at home and amuse Beth for a day or two.\"", "『뱅스 선생님을 모셔다가, 그저 아가씨를 한번 봐 주십사 하는 거예요, 우리가 제대로 시작하나 보게요. 그런 다음 에이미 아가씨는 마치\n마치네 고모님 댁으로 한동안 보내서 위험을 피하게 하고, 너희 중 하나가 집에 남아 하루 이틀 베스를 돌봐 주면 되겠다.\""]], [["\"I shall stay, of course; I'm oldest,\"", "\"내가 남을게요, 당연히. 내가 맏이잖아요.\""], ["began Meg, looking anxious and self-reproachful.", "메그가 걱정스럽고 자책하는 얼굴로 입을 열었다."]], [["\"_I_ shall, because it's my fault she is sick; I told mother I'd do the errands, and I haven't,\"", "\"내가 남을래요. 베스가 아픈 게 내 잘못이니까."], ["said Jo decidedly.", "엄마한테 심부름은 내가 한다고 해 놓고 안 했잖아요.\" 조가 단호하게 말했다."]], [["\"Which will you have, Beth?", "\"누가 좋겠니, 베스?"], ["there ain't no need of but one,\"", "한 사람만 있으면 되니까.\""], ["said Hannah.", "해나가 물었다."]], [["\"Jo, please;\"", "\"조, 언니가 좋아요.\""], ["and Beth leaned her head against her sister, with a contented look, which effectually settled that point.", "베스가 만족스러운 표정으로 언니 어깨에 머리를 기댔고, 그 한마디로 문제는 깔끔하게 정리되었다."]], [["\"I'll go and tell Amy,\"", "\"내가 가서 에이미한테 말할게.\""], ["said Meg, feeling a little hurt, yet rather relieved, on the whole, for she did not like nursing, and Jo did.", "메그는 조금 서운하면서도 전체적으로는 오히려 마음이 놓였다. 그녀는 간병이 싫었고, 조는 좋아했기 때문이다."]], [["Amy rebelled outright, and passionately declared that she had rather have the fever than go to Aunt March.", "에이미는 대놓고 반항하며, 마치 고모님 댁에 가느니 차라리 자기가 열병을 앓는 게 낫겠다고 격하게 선언했다."], ["Meg reasoned, pleaded, and commanded: all in vain.", "메그가 타이르고, 사정하고, 명령까지 해 봤지만 다 소용없었다."], ["Amy protested that she would _not_ go; and Meg left her in despair, to ask Hannah what should be done.", "에이미는 절대 안 가겠다고 버텼고, 메그는 절망한 채 해나에게 어떻게 해야 할지 물어보러 갔다."], ["Before she came back, Laurie walked into the parlor to find Amy sobbing, with her head in the sofa-cushions.", "메그가 돌아오기 전에 로리가 거실로 들어왔다가, 소파 쿠션에 머리를 파묻고 흐느끼고 있는 에이미를 발견했다."], ["She told her story, expecting to be consoled; but Laurie only put his hands in his pockets and walked about the room, whistling softly, as he knit his brows in deep thought.", "에이미는 위로받기를 기대하며 사정을 털어놓았다. 그러나 로리는 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 방 안을 거닐며, 미간을 잔뜩 찌푸리고 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯 나직이 휘파람만 불 뿐이었다."], ["Presently he sat down beside her, and said, in his most wheedlesome tone, \"Now be a sensible little woman, and do as they say.", "이윽고 그는 에이미 곁에 앉아 더없이 살살 구슬리는 어조로 말했다. \"자, 이제 분별 있는 작은 숙녀답게 굴고, 어른들이 시키는 대로 해."], ["No, don't cry, but hear what a jolly plan I've got.", "아니, 울지 말고 내가 짜낸 끝내주는 계획을 좀 들어 봐."], ["You go to Aunt March's, and I'll come and take you out every day, driving or walking, and we'll have capital times.", "너는 마치네 고모님 댁에 가고, 내가 매일 가서 너를 데리고 나오는 거야. 마차도 타고 산책도 하고, 신나게 노는 거지."], ["Won't that be better than moping here?\"", "여기서 시무룩하게 있는 것보다 그게 낫지 않겠어?\""]], [["\"I don't wish to be sent off as if I was in the way,\"", "\"내가 거치적거린다고 쫓겨나는 건 싫어.\""], ["began Amy, in an injured voice.", "에이미가 서운한 목소리로 말문을 열었다."]], [["\"Bless your heart, child, it's to keep you well.", "\"이런, 얘야, 그건 네가 건강하라고 그러는 거야."], ["You don't want to be sick, do you?\"", "너도 아프기는 싫잖아, 안 그래?\""]], [["\"No, I'm sure I don't; but I dare say I shall be, for I've been with Beth all the time.\"", "\"응, 정말 싫어. 하지만 난 분명히 걸리고 말 거야. 베스랑 줄곧 같이 있었으니까.\""]], [["\"That's the very reason you ought to go away at once, so that you may escape it.", "\"바로 그래서 당장 떠나야 한다는 거야. 그래야 병을 피할 수 있으니까."], ["Change of air and care will keep you well, I dare say; or, if it does not entirely, you will have the fever more lightly.", "공기를 바꾸고 몸을 잘 챙기면 건강을 지킬 수 있을 거야. 설령 완전히 막지는 못한다 해도 훨씬 가볍게 앓고 넘어갈 수 있어."], ["I advise you to be off as soon as you can, for scarlet fever is no joke, miss.\"", "되도록 빨리 떠나는 게 좋겠어. 성홍열은 우습게 볼 게 아니거든, 아가씨.\""]], [["\"But it's dull at Aunt March's, and she is so cross,\"", "\"하지만 고모님 댁은 따분하고, 고모님은 너무 까다로우신걸.\""], ["said Amy, looking rather frightened.", "에이미가 겁먹은 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["\"It won't be dull with me popping in every day to tell you how Beth is, and take you out gallivanting.", "\"내가 매일 들러서 베스가 어떤지 알려 주고 너를 데리고 놀러 다닐 텐데 따분할 리가 있나."], ["The old lady likes me, and I'll be as sweet as possible to her, so she won't peck at us, whatever we do.\"", "그 어르신은 나를 좋아하시니까, 내가 최대한 싹싹하게 굴게. 그러면 우리가 뭘 하든 고모님이 잔소리하지 않으실 거야.\""]], [["\"Will you take me out in the trotting wagon with Puck?\"", "\"퍽이 끄는 경마차에 태워 줄 거지?\""]], [["\"On my honor as a gentleman.\"", "\"신사의 명예를 걸고.\""]], [["\"And come every single day?\"", "\"매일매일 빠짐없이 오고?\""]], [["\"See if I don't.\"", "\"내가 안 오나 두고 봐.\""]], [["\"And bring me back the minute Beth is well?\"", "\"베스가 낫는 순간 곧장 집에 데려다주고?\""]], [["\"The identical minute.\"", "\"바로 그 순간에.\""]], [["\"And go to the theatre, truly?\"", "\"극장에도 데려가 줄 거야, 정말?\""]], [["\"A dozen theatres, if we may.\"", "\"가능하면 극장을 열두 군데라도.\""]], [["\"Well--I guess--I will,\"", "\"음… 그럼…"], ["said Amy slowly.", "갈래.\" 에이미가 천천히 말했다."]], [["\"Good girl!", "\"착하다!"], ["Call Meg, and tell her you'll give in,\"", "메그를 불러서 항복하겠다고 말하렴.\""], ["said Laurie, with an approving pat, which annoyed Amy more than the \"giving in.\"", "로리가 칭찬하듯 토닥였는데, 그 행동이 '항복'이라는 말보다 에이미를 더 짜증 나게 했다."]], [["Meg and Jo came running down to behold the miracle which had been wrought; and Amy, feeling very precious and self-sacrificing, promised to go, if the doctor said Beth was going to be ill.", "메그와 조가 기적이 일어난 광경을 보려고 뛰어 내려왔다. 에이미는 자기가 무척 귀하고 갸륵한 희생을 한다는 기분에 젖어, 의사가 베스가 아플 거라고 진단하면 가겠노라고 약속했다."]], [["\"How is the little dear?\"", "\"우리 꼬마는 좀 어때?\""], ["asked Laurie; for Beth was his especial pet, and he felt more anxious about her than he liked to show.", "로리가 물었다. 베스는 그가 특별히 아끼는 동생이었고, 그는 겉으로 드러내고 싶은 것보다 훨씬 더 그녀를 걱정하고 있었다."]], [["\"She is lying down on mother's bed, and feels better.", "\"엄마 침대에 누워 있는데, 좀 나아진 것 같아."], ["The baby's death troubled her, but I dare say she has only got cold.", "아기가 죽은 일로 충격을 받긴 했지만, 내 생각엔 그냥 감기에 걸린 것 같아."], ["Hannah _says_ she thinks so; but she _looks_ worried, and that makes me fidgety,\"", "해나도 그렇게 생각한다고 말은 하는데, 표정이 영 걱정스러워 보여서 나도 안절부절못하겠어.\""], ["answered Meg.", "메그가 대답했다."]], [["\"What a trying world it is!\"", "\"세상은 어쩜 이렇게 사람을 시험에 들게 할까!\""], ["said Jo, rumpling up her hair in a fretful sort of way.", "조가 짜증 섞인 손길로 머리를 헝클어뜨리며 말했다."], ["\"No sooner do we get out of one trouble than down comes another.", "\"한 가지 걱정에서 빠져나오기가 무섭게 또 다른 걱정이 닥치잖아."], ["There doesn't seem to be anything to hold on to when mother's gone; so I'm all at sea.\"", "엄마가 안 계시니까 도무지 붙잡을 게 없는 것 같아. 그래서 나는 갈피를 못 잡겠어.\""]], [["\"Well, don't make a porcupine of yourself, it isn't becoming.", "\"이런, 고슴도치처럼 굴지 마. 어울리지도 않아."], ["Settle your wig, Jo, and tell me if I shall telegraph to your mother, or do anything?\"", "그 가발 같은 머리나 좀 다듬으렴, 조. 그리고 내가 너희 어머니한테 전보를 칠까, 아니면 뭐든 해 줄까?\""], ["asked Laurie, who never had been reconciled to the loss of his friend's one beauty.", "로리가 물었다. 그는 친구의 단 하나뿐인 자랑거리를 잃은 일을 영영 받아들이지 못하고 있었다."]], [["\"That is what troubles me,\"", "\"바로 그게 걱정이야.\""], ["said Meg.", "메그가 말했다."], ["\"I think we ought to tell her if Beth is really ill, but Hannah says we mustn't, for mother can't leave father, and it will only make them anxious.", "\"베스가 정말 아픈 거라면 엄마한테 알려야 한다고 생각하는데, 해나는 안 된대. 엄마는 아빠 곁을 떠날 수 없는 데다, 괜히 두 분만 더 걱정하게 만들 뿐이라는 거야."], ["Beth won't be sick long, and Hannah knows just what to do, and mother said we were to mind her, so I suppose we must, but it doesn't seem quite right to me.\"", "베스가 오래 앓지는 않을 거고, 해나는 어떻게 해야 하는지 잘 알고, 엄마도 해나 말을 잘 들으라고 하셨으니까 그래야겠지. 그런데 나한테는 영 옳은 일 같지가 않아.\""]], [["\"Hum, well, I can't say; suppose you ask grandfather after the doctor has been.\"", "\"흠, 글쎄, 나도 뭐라 말은 못 하겠다. 의사 선생님이 다녀가신 다음에 할아버지한테 한번 여쭤보는 게 어때?\""]], [["\"We will.", "\"그러자."], ["Jo, go and get Dr. Bangs at once,\"", "조, 당장 가서 뱅스 선생님 좀 모셔 와.\""], ["commanded Meg; \"we can't decide anything till he has been.\"", "메그가 지시했다. \"선생님이 다녀가시기 전에는 아무것도 결정할 수 없으니까.\""]], [["\"Stay where you are, Jo; I'm errand-boy to this establishment,\"", "\"그냥 있어, 조. 이 집 심부름꾼은 나니까.\""], ["said Laurie, taking up his cap.", "로리가 모자를 집어 들며 말했다."]], [["\"I'm afraid you are busy,\"", "\"넌 바쁠 텐데.\""], ["began Meg.", "메그가 말문을 열었다."]], [["\"No, I've done my lessons for the day.\"", "\"아니, 오늘 공부는 다 끝냈어.\""]], [["\"Do you study in vacation time?\"", "\"방학에도 공부를 해?\""], ["asked Jo.", "조가 물었다."]], [["\"I follow the good example my neighbors set me,\"", "\"이웃이 보여 준 훌륭한 본보기를 따르는 거지.\""], ["was Laurie's answer, as he swung himself out of the room.", "로리는 그렇게 대답하며 몸을 휙 날려 방을 나섰다."]], [["\"I have great hopes of my boy,\"", "\"내 친구한테 거는 기대가 크다니까.\""], ["observed Jo, watching him fly over the fence with an approving smile.", "그가 울타리를 훌쩍 뛰어넘는 모습을 흐뭇한 미소로 바라보며 조가 말했다."]], [["\"He does very well--for a boy,\"", "\"꽤 잘하는 편이지, 남자애치고는.\""], ["was Meg's somewhat ungracious answer, for the subject did not interest her.", "메그가 다소 무뚝뚝하게 대꾸했다. 그녀에게는 이 화제가 별 흥미를 끌지 못했던 것이다."]], [["Dr.", "뱅스 선생이 다녀갔다."], ["Bangs came, said Beth had symptoms of the fever, but thought she would have it lightly, though he looked sober over the Hummel story.", "그는 베스에게 열병 증상이 있다고 했지만, 가볍게 앓고 넘어갈 것 같다고 보았다. 다만 후멜네 이야기를 들었을 때는 표정이 굳어졌다."], ["Amy was ordered off at once, and provided with something to ward off danger, she departed in great state, with Jo and Laurie as escort.", "에이미는 당장 떠나라는 명령을 받았고, 위험을 막아 줄 약을 챙긴 채, 조와 로리를 호위로 거느리고 거창하게 길을 나섰다."]], [["Aunt March received them with her usual hospitality.", "마치 고모는 평소처럼 환대하며 그들을 맞았다."]], [["\"What do you want now?\"", "\"이번엔 또 뭐냐?\""], ["she asked, looking sharply over her spectacles, while the parrot, sitting on the back of her chair, called out,--", "고모가 안경 너머로 날카롭게 쳐다보며 물었다. 그러는 동안 의자 등받이에 앉은 앵무새가 소리를 질렀다."]], [["\"Go away.", "\"저리 가."], ["No boys allowed here.\"", "여긴 남자애 출입 금지.\""]], [["Laurie retired to the window, and Jo told her story.", "로리는 창가로 물러났고, 조가 사정을 이야기했다."]], [["\"No more than I expected, if you are allowed to go poking about among poor folks.", "\"가난뱅이들 틈에 함부로 들락거리게 내버려 뒀으니 이쯤은 예상한 바다. 에이미는 아프지만 않으면 남아서 제 몫을 하면 되겠지."], ["Amy can stay and make herself useful if she isn't sick, which I've no doubt she will be,--looks like it now.", "하긴 분명 앓게 될 게다. 지금도 그래 보이는구나."], ["Don't cry, child, it worries me to hear people sniff.\"", "울지 마라, 얘야. 사람이 훌쩍거리는 소리는 듣기만 해도 신경 쓰이니까.\""]], [["Amy _was_ on the point of crying, but Laurie slyly pulled the parrot's tail, which caused Polly to utter an astonished croak, and call out,--\n\"Bless my boots!\"", "에이미는 막 울음을 터뜨리려던 참이었는데, 로리가 슬쩍 앵무새 꼬리를 잡아당겼다. 그 바람에 폴리가 깜짝 놀라 꽥 소리를 지르더니 너무나 우스꽝스러운 목소리로 \"어이쿠 깜짝이야!\""], ["in such a funny way, that she laughed instead.", "하고 외치는 바람에, 에이미는 울음 대신 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["\"What do you hear from your mother?\"", "\"네 어미한테서는 무슨 소식이 있더냐?\""], ["asked the old lady gruffly.", "고모가 퉁명스럽게 물었다."]], [["\"Father is much better,\"", "\"아버지는 많이 좋아지셨어요.\""], ["replied Jo, trying to keep sober.", "조가 웃음을 참으며 대답했다."]], [["\"Oh, is he?", "\"오, 그래?"], ["Well, that won't last long, I fancy; March never had any stamina,\"", "글쎄, 오래는 못 가지 싶다만. 마치 집안은 원래 끈기라곤 없으니까.\""], ["was the cheerful reply.", "고모가 쾌활하게 대꾸했다."]], [["\"Ha, ha!", "\"하, 하!"], ["never say die, take a pinch of snuff, good by, good by!\"", "죽는소리 말고 코담배나 한 줌 들이켜라, 잘 가, 잘 가!\""], ["squalled Polly, dancing on her perch, and clawing at the old lady's cap as Laurie tweaked him in the rear.", "폴리가 횃대 위에서 춤추듯 까불며 꽥꽥거렸다. 로리가 뒤에서 꼬리를 잡아당기자, 폴리는 고모의 모자를 발톱으로 할퀴어 댔다."]], [["\"Hold your tongue, you disrespectful old bird!", "\"입 다물지 못해, 이 버르장머리 없는 늙은 새야!"], ["and, Jo, you'd better go at once; it isn't proper to be gadding about so late with a rattle-pated boy like--\"", "그리고 조, 너는 어서 가는 게 좋겠다. 저렇게 머리에 든 것 없는 사내애랑 이 늦은 시각에 쏘다니는 건 점잖지 못한 일이니—\""]], [["\"Hold your tongue, you disrespectful old bird!\"", "\"입 다물지 못해, 이 버르장머리 없는 늙은 새야!\""], ["cried Polly, tumbling off the chair with a bounce, and running to peck the \"rattle-pated\"", "폴리가 의자에서 폴짝 뛰어내리더니, 방금 한 말에 웃음을 참지 못해 들썩거리던 그 '머리에 든 것 없는'"], ["boy, who was shaking with laughter at the last speech.", "사내애를 쪼아 대려고 달려가며 외쳤다."]], [["\"I don't think I _can_ bear it, but I'll try,\"", "'도저히 못 견딜 것 같지만, 그래도 한번 견뎌 봐야지.'"], ["thought Amy, as she was left alone with Aunt March.", "에이미는 고모와 단둘이 남게 되자 그렇게 생각했다."]], [["\"Get along, you fright!\"", "\"썩 꺼지지 못해, 이 흉측한 것아!\""], ["screamed Polly; and at that rude speech Amy could not restrain a sniff.", "폴리가 빽 소리를 질렀고, 그 무례한 말에 에이미는 끝내 훌쩍이고 말았다."]], [["# DARK DAYS.", "# DARK DAYS."]], [["Beth did have the fever, and was much sicker than any one but Hannah and the doctor suspected.", "암울한 나날들. 베스는 정말로 열병에 걸렸고, 해나와 의사 말고는 누구도 짐작하지 못할 만큼 심하게 앓았다."], ["The girls knew nothing about illness, and Mr. Laurence was not allowed to see her, so Hannah had everything all her own way, and busy Dr.", "자매들은 병에 대해 아는 게 없었고, 로런스 씨도 베스를 보지 못하게 한 터라, 모든 일은 해나가 도맡아 처리했다."], ["Bangs did his best, but left a good deal to the excellent nurse.", "바쁜 뱅스 선생은 최선을 다했지만 많은 부분을 그 훌륭한 간병인에게 맡겼다."], ["Meg stayed at home, lest she should infect the Kings, and kept house, feeling very anxious and a little guilty when she wrote letters in which no mention was made of Beth's illness.", "메그는 킹네 아이들에게 병을 옮길까 봐 집에 머물며 살림을 꾸렸는데, 베스의 병에 대해서는 한마디도 적지 않은 편지를 쓸 때면 몹시 불안하고 조금은 죄스러운 마음이 들었다."], ["She could not think it right to deceive her mother, but she had been bidden to mind Hannah, and Hannah wouldn't hear of \"Mrs. March bein'", "메그는 어머니를 속이는 게 옳다고는 도무지 생각할 수 없었지만, 해나의 말을 따르라는 당부를 받은 데다, 해나는 \"이만한 일로 마치 부인한테 알려서 걱정을 끼친다\"는 건 들으려고도 하지 않았다."], ["told, and worried just for sech a trifle.\"", "조는 밤낮으로 베스에게 매달렸다."], ["Jo devoted herself to Beth day and night; not a hard task, for Beth was very patient, and bore her pain uncomplainingly as long as she could control herself.", "베스가 무척 잘 참아 내고, 스스로를 다스릴 수 있는 한 불평 없이 고통을 견뎌 냈으므로 그리 힘든 일은 아니었다. 그러나 열이 발작처럼 치솟을 때면 베스가 쉰 듯 끊어지는 목소리로 헛소리를 늘어놓기 시작하는 때가 찾아왔다."], ["But there came a time when during the fever fits she began to talk in a hoarse, broken voice, to play on the coverlet, as if on her beloved little piano, and try to sing with a throat so swollen that there was no music left; a time when she did not know the familiar faces round her, but addressed them by wrong names, and called imploringly for her mother.", "그녀는 마치 사랑하는 작은 피아노를 치듯 이불을 두드렸고, 음악이라곤 남아 있지 않을 만큼 퉁퉁 부은 목으로 노래를 부르려 했다. 둘러선 낯익은 얼굴들을 알아보지 못하고 엉뚱한 이름으로 부르며, 애원하듯 어머니를 찾는 때가 온 것이다."], ["Then Jo grew frightened, Meg begged to be allowed to write the truth, and even Hannah said she \"would think of it, though there was no danger _yet_.\"", "그제야 조는 겁에 질렸고, 메그는 사실대로 편지를 쓰게 해 달라고 애원했다. 해나조차도 \"아직 위험한 건 아니지만, 한번 생각해 보겠다\"고 말했다."], ["A letter from Washington added to their trouble, for Mr.", "게다가 워싱턴에서 온 편지가 그들의 근심을 더했다."], ["March had had a relapse, and could not think of coming home for a long while.", "마치 씨가 다시 병세가 도져서, 한동안 집에 돌아올 엄두를 내지 못한다는 것이었다."]], [["How dark the days seemed now, how sad and lonely the house, and how heavy were the hearts of the sisters as they worked and waited, while the shadow of death hovered over the once happy home!", "이제 그 나날들은 얼마나 어둡게 느껴졌으며, 집안은 얼마나 슬프고 적막했던가. 한때 행복했던 보금자리에 죽음의 그림자가 어른거리는 동안, 일을 하며 기다리는 자매들의 마음은 또 얼마나 무거웠던가!"], ["Then it was that Margaret, sitting alone with tears dropping often on her work, felt how rich she had been in things more precious than any luxuries money could buy,--in love, protection, peace, and health, the real blessings of life.", "바로 그때, 혼자 앉아 바느질감에 자꾸만 눈물을 떨구던 마거릿은, 자신이 돈으로 살 수 있는 그 어떤 사치보다 훨씬 더 소중한 것들—사랑과 보호, 평화와 건강이라는, 삶의 진정한 축복들—을 얼마나 넉넉히 누려 왔는지를 깨달았다."], ["Then it was that Jo, living in the darkened room, with that suffering little sister always before her eyes, and that pathetic voice sounding in her ears, learned to see the beauty and the sweetness of Beth's nature, to feel how deep and tender a place she filled in all hearts, and to acknowledge the worth of Beth's unselfish ambition, to live for others, and make home happy by the exercise of those simple virtues which all may possess, and which all should love and value more than talent, wealth, or beauty.", "바로 그때, 어두운 방에 틀어박혀 고통받는 어린 동생을 늘 눈앞에 두고, 그 애처로운 목소리를 줄곧 귀에 담고 지내던 조는, 베스의 천성에 깃든 아름다움과 다정함을 보는 법을 배웠다. 베스가 모든 이의 마음속에서 얼마나 깊고 따뜻한 자리를 채우고 있는지를 느꼈고, 남을 위해 살고, 누구나 지닐 수 있으며 누구나 재능이나 부나 미모보다 더 사랑하고 귀히 여겨야 할 그 소박한 미덕을 실천함으로써 가정을 행복하게 만들겠다는 베스의 욕심 없는 포부가 얼마나 값진 것인지를 인정하게 되었다."], ["And Amy, in her exile, longed eagerly to be at home, that she might work for Beth, feeling now that no service would be hard or irksome, and remembering, with regretful grief, how many neglected tasks those willing hands had done for her.", "그리고 유배지에 있던 에이미는 집으로 돌아가 베스를 위해 일하기를 간절히 바랐다. 이제는 어떤 수고도 힘들거나 성가시지 않을 것 같았고, 그 기꺼운 손길이 자신을 위해 해 준, 미처 고마워할 줄 몰랐던 그 많은 일들을 떠올리며 회한에 잠겼다."], ["Laurie haunted the house like a restless ghost, and Mr.", "로리는 안절부절못하는 유령처럼 그 집 주위를 맴돌았고, 로런스 씨는 그랜드피아노에 자물쇠를 채워 버렸다."], ["Laurence locked the grand piano, because he could not bear to be reminded of the young neighbor who used to make the twilight pleasant for him.", "황혼녘을 즐겁게 해 주던 어린 이웃이 떠오르는 것을 차마 견딜 수 없었기 때문이다."], ["Every one missed Beth.", "모두가 베스를 그리워했다."], ["The milkman, baker, grocer, and butcher inquired how she did; poor Mrs.", "우유 배달부도, 빵집 주인도, 식료품점 주인도, 정육점 주인도 베스의 안부를 물었다."], ["Hummel came to beg pardon for her thoughtlessness, and to get a shroud for Minna; the neighbors sent all sorts of comforts and good wishes, and even those who knew her best were surprised to find how many friends shy little Beth had made.", "가엾은 후멜 부인이 자기 경솔함을 사죄하고 미나에게 입힐 수의를 얻으러 찾아왔으며, 이웃들은 온갖 위로의 음식과 안부를 보내왔다. 그녀를 가장 잘 안다고 여겼던 사람들조차, 수줍은 꼬마 베스가 그토록 많은 친구를 사귀어 두었다는 사실에 놀랐다."]], [["Meanwhile she lay on her bed with old Joanna at her side, for even in her wanderings she did not forget her forlorn _protégé_.", "그러는 동안 베스는 침대에 누워 있었고, 곁에는 낡은 조애나가 함께 있었다. 헛소리를 하는 와중에도 그녀는 그 가엾고 버림받은 인형을 잊지 않았던 것이다."], ["She longed for her cats, but would not have them brought, lest they should get sick; and, in her quiet hours, she was full of anxiety about Jo.", "베스는 고양이들을 보고 싶어 했지만, 행여 병이 옮을까 봐 데려오게 하지는 않았다. 그리고 정신이 맑은 시간이면 조를 걱정하는 마음으로 가득했다."], ["She sent loving messages to Amy, bade them tell her mother that she would write soon; and often begged for pencil and paper to try to say a word, that father might not think she had neglected him.", "에이미에게는 사랑을 담은 말을 전했고, 어머니에게는 곧 편지를 쓰겠다고 전해 달라고 했다. 또 아버지가 자신이 무심했다고 여기지 않도록 한마디라도 적어 보려고 자주 연필과 종이를 찾았다."], ["But soon even these intervals of consciousness ended, and she lay hour after hour, tossing to and fro, with incoherent words on her lips, or sank into a heavy sleep which brought her no refreshment.", "그러나 곧 그렇게 정신이 드는 순간들마저 끝나 버렸고, 베스는 입가에 두서없는 말을 흘리며 몇 시간이고 이리저리 뒤척이거나, 아무런 기운도 되찾지 못하는 깊은 잠 속으로 가라앉았다."], ["Dr. Bangs came twice a day, Hannah sat up at night, Meg kept a telegram in her desk all ready to send off at any minute, and Jo never stirred from Beth's side.", "뱅스 선생은 하루에 두 번씩 다녀갔고, 해나는 밤을 새워 곁을 지켰으며, 메그는 언제든 즉시 부칠 수 있도록 전보를 책상에 준비해 두었고, 조는 베스 곁을 한시도 떠나지 않았다."]], [["The first of December was a wintry day indeed to them, for a bitter wind blew, snow fell fast, and the year seemed getting ready for its death.", "12월 초하루는 그들에게 그야말로 겨울다운 하루였다. 매서운 바람이 불고, 눈이 펑펑 쏟아졌으며, 한 해가 죽음을 맞을 채비를 하는 듯했다."], ["When Dr. Bangs came that morning, he looked long at Beth, held the hot hand in both his own a minute, and laid it gently down, saying, in a low tone, to Hannah,--", "그날 아침 뱅스 선생은 베스를 한참 들여다보더니, 그 뜨거운 손을 자기 두 손으로 잠시 감싸 쥐었다가 가만히 내려놓고는, 해나에게 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"If Mrs. March _can_ leave her husband, she'd better be sent for.\"", "\"마치 부인이 남편 곁을 떠나올 수 있다면, 오시라고 기별하는 게 좋겠소.\""]], [["Hannah nodded without speaking, for her lips twitched nervously; Meg dropped down into a chair as the strength seemed to go out of her limbs at the sound of those words; and Jo, after standing with a pale face for a minute, ran to the parlor, snatched up the telegram, and, throwing on her things, rushed out into the storm.", "해나는 아무 말 없이 고개만 끄덕였다. 입술이 불안하게 떨렸기 때문이다."], ["She was soon back, and, while noiselessly taking off her cloak, Laurie came in with a letter, saying that Mr.", "메그는 그 말을 듣는 순간 사지에서 힘이 빠져나가는 듯 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다. 조는 잠시 핏기 없는 얼굴로 서 있다가 거실로 달려가 전보를 낚아채더니, 옷을 걸치고 폭풍 속으로 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["March was mending again.", "조는 금세 돌아왔고, 소리 없이 망토를 벗고 있을 때 로리가 편지 한 통을 들고 들어와, 마치 씨가 다시 회복되고 있다는 소식을 전했다."], ["Jo read it thankfully, but the heavy weight did not seem lifted off her heart, and her face was so full of misery that Laurie asked quickly,--", "조는 고마운 마음으로 편지를 읽었지만, 가슴을 짓누르던 무거운 짐은 도무지 가시지 않는 듯했다. 그 얼굴에 비참함이 가득해서, 로리가 다급히 물었다."]], [["\"What is it?", "\"무슨 일이야?"], ["is Beth worse?\"", "베스가 더 나빠졌어?\""]], [["\"I've sent for mother,\"", "\"엄마한테 기별했어.\""], ["said Jo, tugging at her rubber boots with a tragical expression.", "조가 비극적인 표정으로 고무장화를 잡아당기며 말했다."]], [["\"Good for you, Jo!", "\"잘했어, 조!"], ["Did you do it on your own responsibility?\"", "네가 알아서 한 거야?\""], ["asked Laurie, as he seated her in the hall chair, and took off the rebellious boots, seeing how her hands shook.", "로리가 물으며, 조의 손이 떨리는 것을 보고 그녀를 현관 의자에 앉히고 말 안 듣는 장화를 벗겨 주었다."]], [["\"No, the doctor told us to.\"", "\"아니, 의사 선생님이 그러라고 하셨어.\""]], [["\"O Jo, it's not so bad as that?\"", "\"오, 조, 그렇게까지 안 좋은 거야?\""], ["cried Laurie, with a startled face.", "로리가 깜짝 놀란 얼굴로 외쳤다."]], [["\"Yes, it is; she doesn't know us, she doesn't even talk about the flocks of green doves, as she calls the vine-leaves on the wall; she doesn't look like my Beth, and there's nobody to help us bear it; mother and father both gone, and God seems so far away I can't find Him.\"", "\"응, 그래. 베스가 우리를 못 알아봐. 벽에 붙은 덩굴잎을 보고 '초록 비둘기 떼'라고 부르곤 했는데, 이젠 그 얘기조차 안 해. 내가 알던 베스 같지가 않아. 그런데 이걸 견디게 도와줄 사람이 아무도 없어. 엄마도 아빠도 다 떠나 계시고, 하느님은 너무 멀리 계신 것 같아서 도무지 찾을 수가 없어.\""]], [["As the tears streamed fast down poor Jo's cheeks, she stretched out her hand in a helpless sort of way, as if groping in the dark, and Laurie took it in his, whispering, as well as he could, with a lump in his throat,--", "가엾은 조의 뺨으로 눈물이 줄줄 흘러내리는 가운데, 그녀는 어둠 속을 더듬듯 막막하게 손을 내밀었다. 로리는 그 손을 자기 손으로 감싸 쥐고, 목이 멘 채로나마 애써 속삭였다."]], [["\"I'm here.", "\"내가 여기 있잖아."], ["Hold on to me, Jo, dear!\"", "나를 꼭 붙잡아, 조.\""]], [["She could not speak, but she did \"hold on,\"", "조는 말은 할 수 없었지만 정말로 그를 \"꼭 붙잡았다.\""], ["and the warm grasp of the friendly human hand comforted her sore heart, and seemed to lead her nearer to the Divine arm which alone could uphold her in her trouble.", "다정한 사람의 따스한 손길이 그녀의 쓰라린 마음을 위로해 주었고, 그 고난 속에서 오직 자신을 떠받쳐 줄 수 있는 신성한 팔에 한 걸음 더 다가서게 이끌어 주는 듯했다."], ["Laurie longed to say something tender and comfortable, but no fitting words came to him, so he stood silent, gently stroking her bent head as her mother used to do.", "로리는 뭔가 다정하고 위안이 될 말을 건네고 싶었지만, 마땅한 말이 떠오르지 않았다. 그래서 그는 말없이 서서, 예전에 조의 어머니가 그러던 것처럼 숙인 그녀의 머리를 가만히 쓰다듬었다."], ["It was the best thing he could have done; far more soothing than the most eloquent words, for Jo felt the unspoken sympathy, and, in the silence, learned the sweet solace which affection administers to sorrow.", "그것은 그가 할 수 있는 가장 좋은 일이었다. 그 어떤 유려한 말보다 훨씬 위로가 되었으니, 조는 그 무언의 공감을 느꼈고, 그 침묵 속에서 애정이 슬픔에 베푸는 달콤한 위안을 깨달았다."], ["Soon she dried the tears which had relieved her, and looked up with a grateful face.", "이윽고 조는 마음을 가볍게 해 준 눈물을 닦고, 고마운 얼굴로 고개를 들었다."]], [["\"Thank you, Teddy, I'm better now; I don't feel so forlorn, and will try to bear it if it comes.\"", "\"고마워, 테디. 이제 좀 나아졌어. 그렇게 막막하지가 않아. 그런 일이 닥치더라도 견뎌 보도록 애써 볼게.\""]], [["\"Keep hoping for the best; that will help you, Jo.", "\"늘 좋은 쪽으로 희망을 품어. 그게 너한테 힘이 될 거야, 조."], ["Soon your mother will be here, and then everything will be right.\"", "곧 너희 어머니가 오실 테고, 그러면 다 잘될 거야.\""]], [["\"I'm so glad father is better; now she won't feel so bad about leaving him.", "\"아빠가 좋아지셔서 정말 다행이야. 이제 엄마도 아빠를 두고 오는 걸 그렇게 마음 아파하지 않으셔도 되니까."], ["Oh, me!", "아, 정말!"], ["it does seem as if all the troubles came in a heap, and I got the heaviest part on my shoulders,\"", "모든 근심거리가 한꺼번에 들이닥쳐서, 그중에서도 제일 무거운 짐이 내 어깨에 얹힌 것만 같아.\""], ["sighed Jo, spreading her wet handkerchief over her knees to dry.", "조가 한숨을 쉬며, 젖은 손수건을 무릎 위에 펼쳐 말렸다."]], [["\"Doesn't Meg pull fair?\"", "\"메그가 제 몫을 안 하는 거야?\""], ["asked Laurie, looking indignant.", "로리가 발끈한 표정으로 물었다."]], [["\"Oh, yes; she tries to, but she can't love Bethy as I do; and she won't miss her as I shall.", "\"아, 아니야. 메그도 애쓰긴 해."], ["Beth is my conscience, and I _can't_ give her up.", "하지만 나만큼 베스를 사랑하진 못해. 또 나처럼 베스를 그리워하지도 않을 거야."], ["I can't!", "베스는 내 양심 같은 존재라서, 도저히 그 애를 놓아줄 수가 없어."], ["I can't!\"", "못 해! 절대 못 해!\""]], [["Down went Jo's face into the wet handkerchief, and she cried despairingly; for she had kept up bravely till now, and never shed a tear.", "조의 얼굴이 다시 젖은 손수건 속에 파묻혔고, 그녀는 절망적으로 흐느꼈다. 지금까지 용감하게 버티며 눈물 한 방울 보이지 않았던 그녀였다."], ["Laurie drew his hand across his eyes, but could not speak till he had subdued the choky feeling in his throat and steadied his lips.", "로리는 손등으로 눈가를 훔쳤지만, 목구멍에 치미는 먹먹한 느낌을 가라앉히고 입술을 다잡고서야 겨우 입을 뗄 수 있었다. 사내답지 못한 일인지도 모르지만, 그로서는 어쩔 수 없었다."], ["It might be unmanly, but he couldn't help it, and I am glad of it.", "그리고 나는 그 점이 다행스럽다. 이윽고 조의 흐느낌이 잦아들자, 그는 희망을 담아 말했다."], ["Presently, as Jo's sobs quieted, he said hopefully, \"I don't think she will die; she's so good, and we all love her so much, I don't believe God will take her away yet.\"", "\"베스는 죽지 않을 거야. 그렇게 착하고, 우리 모두가 그렇게 사랑하는데, 하느님이 그 애를 아직 데려가실 리 없어.\""]], [["\"The good and dear people always do die,\"", "\"착하고 사랑스러운 사람들이 늘 먼저 죽는걸.\""], ["groaned Jo, but she stopped crying, for her friend's words cheered her up, in spite of her own doubts and fears.", "조가 신음하듯 말했다. 그러나 자신의 의심과 두려움에도 불구하고 친구의 말이 힘을 북돋아 준 덕에, 그녀는 울음을 그쳤다."]], [["\"Poor girl, you're worn out.", "\"가엾은 것, 너 완전히 지쳤구나."], ["It isn't like you to be forlorn.", "막막해하는 건 너답지 않아."], ["Stop a bit; I'll hearten you up in a jiffy.\"", "잠깐만 기다려. 금방 기운 나게 해 줄게.\""]], [["Laurie went off two stairs at a time, and Jo laid her wearied head down on Beth's little brown hood, which no one had thought of moving from the table where she left it. It must have possessed some magic, for the submissive spirit of its gentle owner seemed to enter into Jo; and, when Laurie came running down with a glass of wine, she took it with a smile, and said bravely, \"I drink--Health to my Beth! You are a good doctor, Teddy, and _such_ a comfortable friend; how can I ever pay you?\" she added, as the wine refreshed her body, as the kind words had done her troubled mind.", "로리는 한 번에 계단 두 개씩 뛰어 내려갔고, 조는 베스가 식탁에 두고 간 작은 갈색 모자에 지친 머리를 기댔다. 아무도 그것을 치울 생각을 못 했던 것이다. 그 모자에는 무슨 마력이라도 깃들어 있었던 모양이다. 그 온화한 주인의 순한 마음씨가 조에게로 스며드는 듯했기 때문이다. 로리가 포도주 한 잔을 들고 뛰어 내려오자, 조는 미소를 지으며 잔을 받아 들고 씩씩하게 말했다. \"우리 베스의 건강을 위하여, 한잔할게! 넌 정말 좋은 의사야, 테디. 게다가 이렇게나 든든한 친구라니. 내가 어떻게 다 갚지?\" 친절한 말이 어지러운 마음을 달래 주었듯, 포도주가 몸에 생기를 불어넣자 조가 덧붙였다."]], [["\"I'll send in my bill, by and by; and to-night I'll give you something that will warm the cockles of your heart better than quarts of wine,\"", "\"청구서는 나중에 보낼게. 그리고 오늘 밤엔 포도주 몇 잔보다 훨씬 더 네 마음속 깊은 곳까지 따뜻하게 데워 줄 걸 안겨 줄게.\""], ["said Laurie, beaming at her with a face of suppressed satisfaction at something.", "로리가 뭔가 흡족함을 애써 누른 얼굴로 그녀를 향해 환하게 웃으며 말했다."]], [["\"What is it?\"", "\"그게 뭔데?\""], ["cried Jo, forgetting her woes for a minute, in her wonder.", "조가 잠시 슬픔도 잊은 채 궁금해하며 외쳤다."]], [["\"I telegraphed to your mother yesterday, and Brooke answered she'd come at once, and she'll be here to-night, and everything will be all right. Aren't you glad I did it?\"", "\"어제 너희 어머니한테 전보를 쳤거든. 그랬더니 브룩 선생님이 곧 오시겠다고 답을 보내왔어. 오늘 밤 도착하실 거야. 그러면 다 잘될 거라고. 내가 그렇게 해서 기쁘지 않아?\""]], [["Laurie spoke very fast, and turned red and excited all in a minute, for he had kept his plot a secret, for fear of disappointing the girls or harming Beth.", "로리는 무척 빠르게 말했고, 순식간에 얼굴이 빨개지며 들떴다. 자매들을 실망시키거나 베스에게 해가 될까 봐 자기 계획을 비밀에 부쳐 왔던 것이다."], ["Jo grew quite white, flew out of her chair, and the moment he stopped speaking she electrified him by throwing her arms round his neck, and crying out, with a joyful cry, \"O Laurie!", "조는 새파랗게 질려 의자에서 벌떡 일어나더니, 그가 말을 마치는 순간 두 팔로 그의 목을 와락 끌어안으며 기쁨에 겨운 소리로 외쳐 그를 깜짝 놀라게 했다. \"오, 로리!"], ["O mother!", "오, 엄마!"], ["I _am_ so glad!\"", "정말, 정말 기뻐!\" 조는 다시 울지는 않았지만, 격하게 웃음을 터뜨리며 부들부들 떨었고, 갑작스러운 소식에 잠시 정신이 얼떨떨한 듯 친구에게 매달렸다."], ["She did not weep again, but laughed hysterically, and trembled and clung to her friend as if she was a little bewildered by the sudden news.", "로리는 분명 놀랐으면서도 침착하게 행동했다. 그는 조의 등을 다독여 달래 주었고, 그녀가 진정하는 것을 보고는 내친김에 수줍게 입맞춤을 한두 번 했는데, 그 바람에 조는 단번에 정신이 들었다."], ["Laurie, though decidedly amazed, behaved with great presence of mind; he patted her back soothingly, and, finding that she was recovering, followed it up by a bashful kiss or two, which brought Jo round at once.", "그녀는 난간을 붙든 채 그를 가만히 밀어내며 숨 가쁘게 말했다. \"아, 이러지 마!"], ["Holding on to the banisters, she put him gently away, saying breathlessly, \"Oh, don't!", "일부러 그런 건 아니야. 내가 너무 경솔했어."], ["I didn't mean to; it was dreadful of me; but you were such a dear to go and do it in spite of Hannah that I couldn't help flying at you.", "그래도 네가 해나의 반대를 무릅쓰고 그 일을 해 줄 만큼 정말 고마운 친구라서, 너한테 와락 안기는 걸 참을 수가 없었어. 어떻게 된 건지 다 얘기해 줘."], ["Tell me all about it, and don't give me wine again; it makes me act so.\"", "그리고 다시는 나한테 포도주 주지 마. 마시면 내가 이렇게 행동하잖아.\""]], [["\"I don't mind,\"", "\"난 괜찮은데.\""], ["laughed Laurie, as he settled his tie.", "로리가 넥타이를 바로잡으며 웃었다."], ["\"Why, you see I got fidgety, and so did grandpa.", "\"그게 말이야, 내가 안절부절못했거든. 할아버지도 그러셨고."], ["We thought Hannah was overdoing the authority business, and your mother ought to know.", "우리는 해나가 권한을 너무 휘두른다고, 너희 어머니가 사실을 아셔야 한다고 생각했어. 만에 하나 베스가…"], ["She'd never forgive us if Beth--well, if anything happened, you know.", "그러니까, 무슨 일이라도 생기면 너희 어머니는 우리를 결코 용서하지 않으실 거 아니야."], ["So I got grandpa to say it was high time we did something, and off I pelted to the office yesterday, for the doctor looked sober, and Hannah most took my head off when I proposed a telegram.", "그래서 할아버지더러 이제 우리가 뭔가 할 때가 됐다고 말씀해 달라고 했고, 어제 전보국으로 달려갔지. 의사 선생님 표정이 심상치 않았던 데다, 내가 전보 얘기를 꺼냈을 때 해나가 하마터면 내 머리를 물어뜯을 뻔했거든."], ["I never _can_ bear to be 'lorded over;'", "난 누가 나한테 '윗사람 행세'하는 건 도무지 못 참아."], ["so that settled my mind, and I did it.", "그래서 마음을 굳혔고, 일을 저질러 버린 거야."], ["Your mother will come, I know, and the late train is in at two, A.M.", "너희 어머니는 분명 오실 거고, 막차가 새벽 두 시에 도착해. 내가 모시러 갈게."], ["I shall go for her; and you've only got to bottle up your rapture, and keep Beth quiet, till that blessed lady gets here.\"", "너는 그저 그 복된 분이 도착하실 때까지 기쁨을 꾹 눌러 담고, 베스를 조용히 돌보고 있기만 하면 돼.\""]], [["\"Laurie, you're an angel!", "\"로리, 넌 천사야!"], ["How shall I ever thank you?\"", "내가 어떻게 고마움을 다 갚지?\""]], [["\"Fly at me again; I rather like it,\"", "\"또 나한테 와락 안겨 봐. 난 그게 꽤 좋던데.\""], ["said Laurie, looking mischievous,--a thing he had not done for a fortnight.", "로리가 장난기 어린 표정을 지으며 말했다. 그가 그런 표정을 지은 건 두 주 만이었다."]], [["\"No, thank you.", "\"아니, 사양할게."], ["I'll do it by proxy, when your grandpa comes.", "그건 너희 할아버지가 오시면 대신 그분께 할 거야."], ["Don't tease, but go home and rest, for you'll be up half the night.", "짓궂게 굴지 말고 어서 집에 가서 쉬어. 밤을 거의 새워야 할 테니까."], ["Bless you, Teddy, bless you!\"", "고마워, 테디, 정말 고마워!\""]], [["Jo had backed into a corner; and, as she finished her speech, she vanished precipitately into the kitchen, where she sat down upon a dresser, and told the assembled cats that she was \"happy, oh, _so_ happy!\"", "조는 어느새 구석으로 물러나 있었고, 말을 끝마치기가 무섭게 부엌으로 후다닥 사라졌다. 그녀는 찬장에 걸터앉아, 모여 있는 고양이들에게 자기가 \"행복하다고, 오, 정말 행복하다고!\""], ["while Laurie departed, feeling that he had made rather a neat thing of it.", "떠들어 댔다. 그러는 동안 로리는, 자기가 꽤 깔끔하게 일을 해냈다는 뿌듯함을 느끼며 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["\"That's the interferingest chap I ever see; but I forgive him, and do hope Mrs.", "\"저렇게 참견 잘하는 녀석은 내 평생 처음 본다니까. 그래도 용서해 줘야지."], ["March is coming on right away,\"", "마치 부인이 곧장 오시면 정말 좋겠구나.\""], ["said Hannah, with an air of relief, when Jo told the good news.", "조가 기쁜 소식을 전하자, 해나가 안도하는 표정으로 말했다."]], [["Meg had a quiet rapture, and then brooded over the letter, while Jo set the sick-room in order, and Hannah \"knocked up a couple of pies in case of company unexpected.\" A breath of fresh air seemed to blow through the house, and something better than sunshine brightened the quiet rooms. Everything appeared to feel the hopeful change; Beth's bird began to chirp again, and a half-blown rose was discovered on Amy's bush in the window; the fires seemed to burn with unusual cheeriness; and every time the girls met, their pale faces broke into smiles as they hugged one another, whispering encouragingly, \"Mother's coming, dear! mother's coming!\" Every one rejoiced but Beth; she lay in that heavy stupor, alike unconscious of hope and joy, doubt and danger. It was a piteous sight,--the once rosy face so changed and vacant, the once busy hands so weak and wasted, the once smiling lips quite dumb, and the once pretty, well-kept hair scattered rough and tangled on the pillow. All day she lay so, only rousing now and then to mutter, \"Water!\" with lips so parched they could hardly shape the word; all day Jo and Meg hovered over her, watching, waiting, hoping, and trusting in God and mother; and all day the snow fell, the bitter wind raged, and the hours dragged slowly by. But night came at last; and every time the clock struck, the sisters, still sitting on either side the bed, looked at each other with brightening eyes, for each hour brought help nearer. The doctor had been in to say that some change, for better or worse, would probably take place about midnight, at which time he would return.", "메그는 조용히 기쁨에 젖었다가 편지를 곰곰이 들여다보았다. 그동안 조는 병실을 정돈했고, 해나는 \"갑자기 손님이 올지도 모르니\" 파이 두어 개를 뚝딱 만들어 냈다. 집 안으로 신선한 공기 한 줄기가 불어 드는 듯했고, 햇살보다 더 좋은 무언가가 고요한 방들을 환하게 밝혔다. 모든 것이 그 희망찬 변화를 느끼는 듯했다. 베스의 새가 다시 지저귀기 시작했고, 창가에 놓인 에이미의 화분에서 반쯤 핀 장미 한 송이가 발견되었다. 난롯불도 유난히 명랑하게 타오르는 듯했다. 자매들은 마주칠 때마다 창백한 얼굴에 웃음꽃을 피우며 서로 끌어안고는, 힘을 북돋우듯 속삭였다. \"엄마가 오신대! 엄마가 오신대!\" 베스만 빼고 모두가 기뻐했다. 베스는 그 깊은 혼수상태에 빠져, 희망도 기쁨도, 의심도 위험도 한결같이 알지 못한 채 누워 있었다. 그것은 가엾은 광경이었다. 한때 발그레했던 얼굴은 그토록 변하여 멍하니 비어 있었고, 한때 부지런하던 손은 그토록 힘없이 야위었으며, 한때 미소 짓던 입술은 아무 말도 못 한 채 굳어 있었고, 한때 곱고 단정하던 머리칼은 베개 위에 거칠게 헝클어진 채 흩어져 있었다. 베스는 온종일 그렇게 누워, 이따금 정신이 들 때만 바싹 마른 입술로 겨우 단어 모양을 만들며 \"물!\" 하고 중얼거릴 뿐이었다. 조와 메그는 온종일 그 곁을 맴돌며 지켜보고, 기다리고, 희망을 품고, 하느님과 어머니를 믿었다. 그리고 온종일 눈이 내리고, 매서운 바람이 사납게 몰아쳤으며, 시간은 더디게 흘러갔다. 그러나 마침내 밤이 찾아왔다. 시계가 울릴 때마다, 여전히 침대 양옆에 앉아 있던 자매들은 환해지는 눈빛으로 서로를 바라보았다. 한 시간 한 시간이 도움을 더 가까이 데려다주었기 때문이다. 의사는 다녀가며, 자정 무렵에 좋든 나쁘든 어떤 변화가 나타날 듯하다고, 그 시각에 다시 오겠다고 말했다."]], [["Hannah, quite worn out, lay down on the sofa at the bed's foot, and fell fast asleep; Mr.", "녹초가 된 해나는 침대 발치 소파에 누워 이내 깊이 잠들었다."], ["Laurence marched to and fro in the parlor, feeling that he would rather face a rebel battery than Mrs.", "로런스 씨는 거실을 오락가락하며, 마치 부인이 들어설 때 그 근심 어린 얼굴을 마주하느니 차라리 적의 포대를 마주하는 편이 낫겠다고 느꼈다."], ["March's anxious countenance as she entered; Laurie lay on the rug, pretending to rest, but staring into the fire with the thoughtful look which made his black eyes beautifully soft and clear.", "로리는 깔개 위에 누워 쉬는 척했지만, 사색에 잠긴 표정으로 난롯불을 들여다보고 있었다. 그 표정이 그의 검은 눈동자를 더없이 부드럽고 맑게 만들어 주었다."]], [["The girls never forgot that night, for no sleep came to them as they kept their watch, with that dreadful sense of powerlessness which comes to us in hours like those.", "자매들은 그날 밤을 결코 잊지 못했다. 밤을 새워 곁을 지키는 동안 그들에게는 잠이 오지 않았고, 그런 시각이면 우리에게 찾아오기 마련인 그 끔찍한 무력감이 짓눌렀던 것이다."]], [["\"If God spares Beth I never will complain again,\"", "\"하느님이 베스를 살려 주신다면, 다시는 절대 불평하지 않을 거야.\""], ["whispered Meg earnestly.", "메그가 간절히 속삭였다."]], [["\"If God spares Beth I'll try to love and serve Him all my life,\"", "\"하느님이 베스를 살려 주신다면, 평생토록 그분을 사랑하고 섬기려고 애쓸 거야.\""], ["answered Jo, with equal fervor.", "조도 그에 못지않은 열의로 답했다."]], [["\"I wish I had no heart, it aches so,\"", "\"마음이라는 게 없었으면 좋겠어. 너무 아파서.\""], ["sighed Meg, after a pause.", "메그가 잠시 침묵하다 한숨지었다."]], [["\"If life is often as hard as this, I don't see how we ever shall get through it,\"", "\"인생이 늘 이만큼 힘들다면, 우리가 어떻게 이걸 다 헤쳐 나갈 수 있을지 모르겠어.\""], ["added her sister despondently.", "동생이 낙담한 듯 덧붙였다."]], [["Here the clock struck twelve, and both forgot themselves in watching Beth, for they fancied a change passed over her wan face.", "그때 시계가 열두 시를 알렸고, 두 사람은 자기들을 잊은 채 베스를 지켜보았다. 그녀의 핼쑥한 얼굴 위로 어떤 변화가 스치는 것 같았기 때문이다."], ["The house was still as death, and nothing but the wailing of the wind broke the deep hush.", "집 안은 죽은 듯이 고요했고, 흐느끼는 바람 소리 말고는 그 깊은 정적을 깨뜨리는 것이 아무것도 없었다."], ["Weary Hannah slept on, and no one but the sisters saw the pale shadow which seemed to fall upon the little bed.", "지친 해나는 계속 잠들어 있었고, 그 작은 침대 위에 드리우는 듯한 창백한 그림자를 본 사람은 두 자매뿐이었다."], ["An hour went by, and nothing happened except Laurie's quiet departure for the station.", "한 시간이 지났지만, 로리가 역으로 조용히 떠난 것 말고는 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다."], ["Another hour,--still no one came; and anxious fears of delay in the storm, or accidents by the way, or, worst of all, a great grief at Washington, haunted the poor girls.", "또 한 시간이 지났다. 여전히 아무도 오지 않았고, 폭풍 속에서 지체되거나, 도중에 사고가 났거나, 무엇보다도 워싱턴에 큰 슬픔이 닥친 게 아닐까 하는 불안한 두려움이 가엾은 자매들을 사로잡았다."]], [["It was past two, when Jo, who stood at the window thinking how dreary the world looked in its winding-sheet of snow, heard a movement by the bed, and, turning quickly, saw Meg kneeling before their mother's easy-chair, with her face hidden. A dreadful fear passed coldly over Jo, as she thought, \"Beth is dead, and Meg is afraid to tell me.\"", "두 시가 넘은 무렵이었다. 창가에 서서 눈으로 뒤덮인 수의 같은 세상이 얼마나 황량해 보이는지 생각하던 조가 침대 쪽에서 어떤 움직임을 듣고 재빨리 돌아보니, 메그가 어머니의 안락의자 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 얼굴을 파묻고 있었다. 끔찍한 두려움이 싸늘하게 조를 스치고 지나갔다. '베스가 죽었구나. 그래서 메그가 차마 나한테 말을 못 하는 거야.'"]], [["She was back at her post in an instant, and to her excited eyes a great change seemed to have taken place.", "조는 순식간에 제자리로 돌아왔고, 흥분한 그녀의 눈에는 엄청난 변화가 일어난 듯 보였다."], ["The fever flush and the look of pain were gone, and the beloved little face looked so pale and peaceful in its utter repose, that Jo felt no desire to weep or to lament.", "열에 들뜬 홍조와 고통스러운 기색이 사라지고, 그 사랑스러운 작은 얼굴이 완전한 안식 속에서 너무도 창백하고 평온해 보여서, 조는 울거나 슬퍼할 마음조차 들지 않았다. 가장 사랑하는 동생 위로 몸을 깊이 숙인 채, 그녀는 온 마음을 입술에 담아 그 축축한 이마에 입을 맞추고 가만히 속삭였다."], ["Leaning low over this dearest of her sisters, she kissed the damp forehead with her heart on her lips, and softly whispered, \"Good-by, my Beth; good-by!\"", "\"잘 가, 베스야. 잘 가!\""]], [["As if waked by the stir, Hannah started out of her sleep, hurried to the bed, looked at Beth, felt her hands, listened at her lips, and then, throwing her apron over her head, sat down to rock to and fro, exclaiming, under her breath, \"The fever's turned; she's sleepin'", "그 기척에 깬 듯 해나가 잠에서 흠칫 깨어나 침대로 황급히 다가가더니, 베스를 들여다보고, 손을 만져 보고, 입가에 귀를 대고 숨소리를 듣고는, 앞치마를 머리 위로 뒤집어쓰고 주저앉아 몸을 앞뒤로 흔들며 낮은 소리로 외쳤다. \"열이 내렸어."], ["nat'ral; her skin's damp, and she breathes easy.", "자연스럽게 잠든 거라고. 살갗이 촉촉하고 숨도 편안해졌어."], ["Praise be given!", "하느님 감사합니다!"], ["Oh, my goodness me!\"", "아이고, 세상에나!\""]], [["Before the girls could believe the happy truth, the doctor came to confirm it.", "자매들이 그 기쁜 사실을 채 믿기도 전에, 의사가 와서 그것을 확인해 주었다."], ["He was a homely man, but they thought his face quite heavenly when he smiled, and said, with a fatherly look at them, \"Yes, my dears, I think the little girl will pull through this time.", "그는 못생긴 사람이었지만, 그가 미소 지으며 그들을 아버지처럼 바라보고 말했을 때, 그 얼굴은 더없이 천사 같아 보였다. \"그래요, 얘들아, 이번에는 이 어린 아가씨가 잘 이겨 낼 것 같구나."], ["Keep the house quiet; let her sleep, and when she wakes, give her--\"", "집 안을 조용히 하고, 푹 자게 두렴. 그리고 깨어나면 이걸 먹이고—\""]], [["What they were to give, neither heard; for both crept into the dark hall, and, sitting on the stairs, held each other close, rejoicing with hearts too full for words.", "무엇을 먹이라는 것이었는지, 둘 중 누구도 듣지 못했다. 두 사람 다 어두운 복도로 살그머니 빠져나가, 계단에 앉아 서로를 꼭 끌어안고는, 말로 다 못 할 만큼 벅찬 마음으로 기뻐했기 때문이다."], ["When they went back to be kissed and cuddled by faithful Hannah, they found Beth lying, as she used to do, with her cheek pillowed on her hand, the dreadful pallor gone, and breathing quietly, as if just fallen asleep.", "충실한 해나에게 입맞춤과 포옹을 받으러 다시 들어갔을 때, 베스는 예전처럼 한쪽 뺨을 손에 베고 누워 있었고, 그 무서운 창백함은 사라지고 없었으며, 막 잠든 사람처럼 고요하게 숨 쉬고 있었다."]], [["\"If mother would only come now!\"", "\"이제 엄마만 오시면 좋으련만!\""], ["said Jo, as the winter night began to wane.", "겨울밤이 차츰 물러가기 시작할 무렵 조가 말했다."]], [["\"See,\" said Meg, coming up with a white, half-opened rose, \"I thought this would hardly be ready to lay in Beth's hand to-morrow if she--went away from us. But it has blossomed in the night, and now I mean to put it in my vase here, so that when the darling wakes, the first thing she sees will be the little rose, and mother's face.\"", "\"이것 봐.\" 메그가 반쯤 핀 하얀 장미 한 송이를 들고 다가오며 말했다. \"혹시 베스가… 우리 곁을 떠난다면, 내일 그 애 손에 쥐여 주기에 이게 좀처럼 피지 못할 줄 알았는데. 그런데 밤새 꽃을 피웠지 뭐야. 이제 여기 내 꽃병에 꽂아 둘래. 그러면 그 애가 깨어났을 때 제일 먼저 보는 게 이 작은 장미랑 엄마 얼굴일 테니까.\""]], [["Never had the sun risen so beautifully, and never had the world seemed so lovely, as it did to the heavy eyes of Meg and Jo, as they looked out in the early morning, when their long, sad vigil was done.", "길고 슬픈 밤샘이 끝난 이른 아침, 밖을 내다보는 메그와 조의 무거운 눈에 비친 것만큼, 해가 그토록 아름답게 떠오른 적도, 세상이 그토록 사랑스러워 보인 적도 없었다."]], [["\"It looks like a fairy world,\"", "\"꼭 요정 나라 같아.\""], ["said Meg, smiling to herself, as she stood behind the curtain, watching the dazzling sight.", "메그가 커튼 뒤에 서서 눈부신 광경을 바라보며 혼자 미소 지었다."]], [["\"Hark!\"", "\"쉿!\""], ["cried Jo, starting to her feet.", "조가 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다."]], [["Yes, there was a sound of bells at the door below, a cry from Hannah, and then Laurie's voice saying, in a joyful whisper, \"Girls, she's come! she's come!\"", "정말이었다. 아래쪽 현관에서 방울 소리가 났고, 해나의 외침이 들리더니, 이어 로리의 목소리가 기쁨에 찬 속삭임으로 말했다. \"얘들아, 오셨어! 오셨어!\" 에이미의 유언장."]], [["# AMY'S WILL.", "# AMY'S WILL."]], [["While these things were happening at home, Amy was having hard times at Aunt March's.", "집에서 이런 일들이 벌어지는 동안, 에이미는 마치 고모 댁에서 힘든 시간을 보내고 있었다."], ["She felt her exile deeply, and, for the first time in her life, realized how much she was beloved and petted at home.", "그녀는 유배 생활을 절절히 느꼈고, 난생처음 자신이 집에서 얼마나 많은 사랑과 귀여움을 받아 왔는지를 깨달았다. 마치 고모는 누구도 어하지 않았다."], ["Aunt March never petted any one; she did not approve of it; but she meant to be kind, for the well-behaved little girl pleased her very much, and Aunt March had a soft place in her old heart for her nephew's children, though she didn't think proper to confess it.", "그런 것을 탐탁지 않게 여겼던 것이다. 하지만 고모도 다정하게 대하려는 마음은 있었으니, 얌전한 그 꼬마가 무척 마음에 들었기 때문이다."], ["She really did her best to make Amy happy, but, dear me, what mistakes she made!", "게다가 마치 고모의 늙은 가슴 한구석에는, 입 밖에 내는 것을 점잖지 못하다 여겼을 뿐, 조카의 아이들을 향한 살뜰한 정이 자리하고 있었다."], ["Some old people keep young at heart in spite of wrinkles and gray hairs, can sympathize with children's little cares and joys, make them feel at home, and can hide wise lessons under pleasant plays, giving and receiving friendship in the sweetest way.", "고모는 정말 에이미를 행복하게 해 주려고 애를 썼지만, 아이고 저런, 어찌나 실수를 많이 했던지! 어떤 노인들은 주름과 백발에도 불구하고 마음만은 젊어서, 아이들의 자잘한 근심과 기쁨에 공감하고, 아이들을 편안하게 해 주며, 즐거운 놀이 속에 지혜로운 가르침을 슬쩍 감추고, 더없이 다정한 방식으로 우정을 주고받는다."], ["But Aunt March had not this gift, and she worried Amy very much with her rules and orders, her prim ways, and long, prosy talks.", "그러나 마치 고모에게는 그런 재주가 없었고, 자신의 규칙과 명령, 새침한 태도, 길고 따분한 설교로 에이미를 몹시 들볶았다."], ["Finding the child more docile and amiable than her sister, the old lady felt it her duty to try and counteract, as far as possible, the bad effects of home freedom and indulgence.", "고모는 그 아이가 언니보다 한결 고분고분하고 상냥한 것을 알고는, 집에서의 자유와 응석이 끼친 나쁜 영향을 되도록 바로잡는 것이 자기 의무라고 여겼다."], ["So she took Amy in hand, and taught her as she herself had been taught sixty years ago,--a process which carried dismay to Amy's soul, and made her feel like a fly in the web of a very strict spider.", "그리하여 고모는 에이미를 손에 쥐고, 자기가 육십 년 전에 배운 그대로 가르쳤다. 그 과정은 에이미의 영혼에 절망을 가져다주었고, 그녀를 마치 무척이나 엄격한 거미의 거미줄에 걸린 파리 신세처럼 느끼게 했다."]], [["She had to wash the cups every morning, and polish up the old-fashioned spoons, the fat silver teapot, and the glasses, till they shone.", "에이미는 아침마다 찻잔을 씻고, 구식 숟가락이며 통통한 은제 찻주전자며 유리잔을 반들반들 윤이 나도록 닦아야 했다."], ["Then she must dust the room, and what a trying job that was!", "그런 다음에는 방의 먼지를 털어야 했는데, 그게 얼마나 진을 빼는 일이었던지!"], ["Not a speck escaped Aunt March's eye, and all the furniture had claw legs, and much carving, which was never dusted to suit.", "티끌 하나도 마치 고모의 눈을 벗어나지 못했고, 가구마다 갈고리 모양 다리에 정교한 조각이 가득해서 아무리 닦아도 고모 마음에 차게 할 수가 없었다."], ["Then Polly must be fed, the lap-dog combed, and a dozen trips upstairs and down, to get things, or deliver orders, for the old lady was very lame, and seldom left her big chair.", "그러고 나면 폴리에게 모이를 주고, 무릎에 앉히는 강아지를 빗질해 주고, 물건을 가져오거나 분부를 전하느라 위아래층을 열두 번씩 오르내려야 했다. 고모는 다리가 몹시 불편해서 좀처럼 그 큰 의자를 떠나지 않았던 것이다."], ["After these tiresome labors, she must do her lessons, which was a daily trial of every virtue she possessed.", "이 진 빠지는 노동을 마치고 나면 공부를 해야 했는데, 이는 에이미가 지닌 온갖 미덕을 날마다 시험하는 일이었다."], ["Then she was allowed one hour for exercise or play, and didn't she enjoy it?", "그런 다음에야 운동이나 놀이를 위한 한 시간이 주어졌는데, 그 시간을 어찌나 만끽했던지!"], ["Laurie came every day, and wheedled Aunt March, till Amy was allowed to go out with him, when they walked and rode, and had capital times.", "로리가 날마다 찾아와 마치 고모를 살살 구슬려, 에이미가 그와 함께 외출할 수 있게 해 주었고, 그러면 둘은 걷기도 하고 마차도 타며 신나게 놀았다. 저녁을 먹고 나면 에이미는 책을 소리 내어 읽어야 했고, 고모가 잠든 동안 가만히 앉아 있어야 했다."], ["After dinner, she had to read aloud, and sit still while the old lady slept, which she usually did for an hour, as she dropped off over the first page.", "고모는 대개 첫 장을 넘기다 말고 곯아떨어져서는 한 시간씩 잤다. 그다음에는 조각보나 수건이 나타났고, 에이미는 겉으로는 고분고분, 속으로는 반항하며 땅거미가 질 때까지 바느질을 했다."], ["Then patchwork or towels appeared, and Amy sewed with outward meekness and inward rebellion till dusk, when she was allowed to amuse herself as she liked till tea-time.", "그러고 나면 차 마실 시간까지 마음대로 놀아도 좋다는 허락이 떨어졌다. 그중에서도 저녁 시간이 가장 끔찍했다."], ["The evenings were the worst of all, for Aunt March fell to telling long stories about her youth, which were so unutterably dull that Amy was always ready to go to bed, intending to cry over her hard fate, but usually going to sleep before she had squeezed out more than a tear or two.", "마치 고모가 자기 젊은 시절 이야기를 길게 늘어놓기 시작했는데, 어찌나 이루 말할 수 없이 따분한지, 에이미는 늘 자기 가혹한 신세를 한탄하며 울 작정으로 잠자리에 들 채비를 했다. 하지만 대개는 눈물 한두 방울을 채 짜내기도 전에 잠들어 버리곤 했다."]], [["If it had not been for Laurie, and old Esther, the maid, she felt that she never could have got through that dreadful time.", "로리와 늙은 하녀 에스터가 아니었다면, 에이미는 그 끔찍한 시간을 도저히 견뎌 내지 못했을 것이라고 느꼈다."], ["The parrot alone was enough to drive her distracted, for he soon felt that she did not admire him, and revenged himself by being as mischievous as possible.", "앵무새 한 마리만으로도 그녀를 정신없게 만들기에 충분했다. 폴리는 에이미가 자기를 좋아하지 않는다는 걸 금세 알아채고는, 할 수 있는 한 못되게 굴어 앙갚음을 했다."], ["He pulled her hair whenever she came near him, upset his bread and milk to plague her when she had newly cleaned his cage, made Mop bark by pecking at him while Madam dozed; called her names before company, and behaved in all respects like a reprehensible old bird.", "에이미가 가까이 다가가기만 하면 머리칼을 잡아당겼고, 새장을 갓 청소해 놓으면 빵과 우유를 엎질러 골탕을 먹였으며, 마님이 졸고 있을 때 모프를 쪼아 짖게 만들었다. 손님 앞에서 에이미에게 욕설을 퍼붓는 등, 모든 면에서 괘씸하기 짝이 없는 늙은 새처럼 굴었다."], ["Then she could not endure the dog,--a fat, cross beast, who snarled and yelped at her when she made his toilet, and who lay on his back, with all his legs in the air and a most idiotic expression of countenance when he wanted something to eat, which was about a dozen times a day.", "게다가 에이미는 그 개도 견딜 수가 없었다. 살이 뒤룩뒤룩 찐 심술궂은 짐승으로, 단장을 해 주려고만 하면 으르렁대고 깽깽거렸으며, 먹을 게 탐날 때면—하루에 열두 번쯤은 그랬다—발랑 누워서 네 다리를 죄다 공중에 쳐든 채 더없이 멍청한 표정을 지었다."], ["The cook was bad-tempered, the old coachman deaf, and Esther the only one who ever took any notice of the young lady.", "요리사는 성미가 고약했고, 늙은 마부는 귀가 먹었으며, 그 어린 아가씨에게 조금이라도 관심을 보이는 사람은 에스터뿐이었다."]], [["Esther was a Frenchwoman, who had lived with \"Madame,\"", "에스터는 프랑스 여자로, 자기가 \"마님\"이라 부르는 안주인과 여러 해를 함께 살아온 사람이었다."], ["as she called her mistress, for many years, and who rather tyrannized over the old lady, who could not get along without her.", "그녀는 자기 없이는 지낼 수 없는 그 노부인에게 오히려 군림하다시피 했다. 본명은 에스텔이었지만, 마치 고모가 이름을 바꾸라고 명령했고, 그녀는 다시는 종교를 바꾸라는 말을 듣지 않는다는 조건으로 그 명령에 따랐다."], ["Her real name was Estelle, but Aunt March ordered her to change it, and she obeyed, on condition that she was never asked to change her religion.", "에스터는 마드무아젤을 무척 마음에 들어 했고, 에이미가 곁에 앉아 자기가 마님의 레이스를 손질하는 동안, 프랑스에서 살던 시절의 별난 이야기들을 들려주며 즐겁게 해 주었다."], ["She took a fancy to Mademoiselle, and amused her very much, with odd stories of her life in France, when Amy sat with her while she got up Madame's laces.", "또 그 큰 집 안을 마음껏 돌아다니며, 커다란 옷장과 오래된 궤짝 속에 간직된 진기하고 예쁜 물건들을 구경하게 해 주었다. 마치 고모는 까치처럼 물건을 잔뜩 쟁여 두는 사람이었던 것이다."], ["She also allowed her to roam about the great house, and examine the curious and pretty things stored away in the big wardrobes and the ancient chests; for Aunt March hoarded like a magpie.", "에이미가 가장 좋아한 것은 인도풍 장식장이었는데, 별난 서랍과 작은 칸들, 비밀 공간들로 가득했고, 그 안에는 온갖 장신구가 들어 있었다. 어떤 것은 값진 것이었고, 어떤 것은 그저 진기한 것이었으며, 하나같이 다소간 골동품이었다."], ["Amy's chief delight was an Indian cabinet, full of queer drawers, little pigeon-holes, and secret places, in which were kept all sorts of ornaments, some precious, some merely curious, all more or less antique.", "이 물건들을 살펴보고 정리하는 일은 에이미에게 큰 만족을 주었는데, 특히 보석함이 그러했다. 벨벳 방석 위에는 사십 년 전 한 미인을 빛내 주던 장신구들이 놓여 있었다."], ["To examine and arrange these things gave Amy great satisfaction, especially the jewel-cases, in which, on velvet cushions, reposed the ornaments which had adorned a belle forty years ago.", "마치 고모가 사교계에 처음 나설 때 걸쳤던 석류석 세트, 결혼식 날 아버지가 준 진주, 연인이 준 다이아몬드, 흑옥으로 만든 상복용 반지와 핀, 죽은 친구들의 초상이 든 별난 로켓에는 안쪽에 머리카락으로 만든 수양버들 무늬가 들어 있었다."], ["There was the garnet set which Aunt March wore when she came out, the pearls her father gave her on her wedding-day, her lover's diamonds, the jet mourning rings and pins, the queer lockets, with portraits of dead friends, and weeping willows made of hair inside; the baby bracelets her one little daughter had worn; Uncle March's big watch, with the red seal so many childish hands had played with, and in a box, all by itself, lay Aunt March's wedding-ring, too small now for her fat finger, but put carefully away, like the most precious jewel of them all.", "하나뿐인 어린 딸이 차던 아기 팔찌, 그토록 많은 아이 손이 가지고 놀던 붉은 인장이 달린 마치 고모부의 커다란 회중시계도 있었다. 그리고 상자 하나에는 마치 고모의 결혼반지가 따로 들어 있었는데, 이제는 통통해진 손가락에 끼우기엔 너무 작았지만, 그 모든 보석 중에서도 가장 귀한 것인 양 고이 간직되어 있었다."]], [["\"Which would Mademoiselle choose if she had her will?\"", "\"마드무아젤은 마음대로 고를 수 있다면 어느 걸 고르시겠어요?\""], ["asked Esther, who always sat near to watch over and lock up the valuables.", "에스터가 물었다. 그녀는 늘 가까이 앉아 귀중품을 살피고 잠가 두곤 했다."]], [["\"I like the diamonds best, but there is no necklace among them, and I'm fond of necklaces, they are so becoming.", "\"난 다이아몬드가 제일 좋은데, 그중엔 목걸이가 없네. 난 목걸이를 좋아하거든."], ["I should choose this if I might,\"", "잘 어울리니까. 고를 수 있다면 이걸 고를래.\""], ["replied Amy, looking with great admiration at a string of gold and ebony beads, from which hung a heavy cross of the same.", "에이미가 대답하며, 금과 흑단 구슬을 꿴 줄에 똑같은 재질의 묵직한 십자가가 매달린 것을 한껏 감탄 어린 눈으로 바라보았다."]], [["\"I, too, covet that, but not as a necklace; ah, no!", "\"저도 그게 탐나요. 하지만 목걸이로서는 아니에요."], ["to me it is a rosary, and as such I should use it like a good Catholic,\"", "아, 그럼요! 제게 그건 묵주랍니다."], ["said Esther, eying the handsome thing wistfully.", "그러니 저라면 착실한 가톨릭 신자답게 그 용도로 쓰겠어요.\" 에스터가 그 아름다운 물건을 아쉬운 듯 바라보며 말했다."]], [["\"Is it meant to use as you use the string of good-smelling wooden beads hanging over your glass?\"", "\"그게, 아주머니가 거울 위에 걸어 둔 좋은 향이 나는 나무 구슬 줄처럼 쓰는 거예요?\""], ["asked Amy.", "에이미가 물었다."]], [["\"Truly, yes, to pray with.", "\"네, 정말 그래요."], ["It would be pleasing to the saints if one used so fine a rosary as this, instead of wearing it as a vain bijou.\"", "기도할 때 쓰는 거죠. 이렇게 고운 묵주를 헛된 장신구로 걸치는 대신 기도에 쓴다면, 성인들께서도 기뻐하실 거예요.\""]], [["\"You seem to take a great deal of comfort in your prayers, Esther, and always come down looking quiet and satisfied.", "\"아주머니는 기도하면서 큰 위안을 얻나 봐요, 에스터. 늘 차분하고 흡족한 얼굴로 내려오시잖아요."], ["I wish I could.\"", "나도 그럴 수 있으면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["\"If Mademoiselle was a Catholic, she would find true comfort; but, as that is not to be, it would be well if you went apart each day, to meditate and pray, as did the good mistress whom I served before Madame.", "\"마드무아젤이 가톨릭 신자라면 참된 위안을 얻으실 텐데요. 하지만 그럴 수 없으니, 제가 마님 이전에 모셨던 좋은 안주인처럼, 날마다 따로 한적한 데로 가서 사색하고 기도하시면 좋을 거예요."], ["She had a little chapel, and in it found solacement for much trouble.\"", "그분께는 작은 예배실이 있었는데, 그 안에서 숱한 시름을 달래셨답니다.\""]], [["\"Would it be right for me to do so too?\"", "\"나도 그렇게 하는 게 옳을까요?\""], ["asked Amy, who, in her loneliness, felt the need of help of some sort, and found that she was apt to forget her little book, now that Beth was not there to remind her of it.", "에이미가 물었다. 외로움 속에서 그녀는 무언가 도움이 필요하다고 느끼던 참이었고, 베스가 곁에서 일러 주지 않으니 자기 작은 책을 잊어버리기 일쑤라는 걸 깨닫고 있었다."]], [["\"It would be excellent and charming; and I shall gladly arrange the little dressing-room for you if you like it.", "\"아주 훌륭하고 멋진 일이죠. 마드무아젤이 좋으시다면 제가 기꺼이 작은 화장방을 꾸며 드릴게요."], ["Say nothing to Madame, but when she sleeps go you and sit alone a while to think good thoughts, and pray the dear God to preserve your sister.\"", "마님께는 아무 말씀 마시고, 마님이 주무실 때 거기로 가서 잠시 혼자 앉아 좋은 생각을 하고, 동생을 지켜 달라고 하느님께 기도하세요.\""]], [["Esther was truly pious, and quite sincere in her advice; for she had an affectionate heart, and felt much for the sisters in their anxiety.", "에스터는 진심으로 신앙심이 깊었고, 그 조언도 무척 진실한 것이었다. 다정한 마음씨를 지닌 그녀는 근심에 잠긴 자매들을 깊이 가엾게 여겼던 것이다."], ["Amy liked the idea, and gave her leave to arrange the light closet next her room, hoping it would do her good.", "에이미는 그 생각이 마음에 들어, 자기 방 옆의 환한 벽장을 꾸며도 좋다고 허락하며, 그것이 자기에게 도움이 되기를 바랐다."]], [["\"I wish I knew where all these pretty things would go when Aunt March dies,\"", "\"마치 고모님이 돌아가시면 이 예쁜 물건들이 다 어디로 갈지 알았으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["she said, as she slowly replaced the shining rosary, and shut the jewel-cases one by one.", "에이미가 반짝이는 묵주를 천천히 제자리에 놓고 보석함을 하나하나 닫으며 말했다."]], [["\"To you and your sisters.", "\"아가씨하고 언니들한테 가요. 제가 알아요."], ["I know it; Madame confides in me; I witnessed her will, and it is to be so,\"", "마님이 저한테 털어놓으셨거든요. 제가 유언장 증인을 섰으니까요."], ["whispered Esther, smiling.", "그렇게 되어 있어요.\" 에스터가 미소 지으며 속삭였다."]], [["\"How nice!", "\"잘됐다!"], ["but I wish she'd let us have them now.", "그래도 지금 우리한테 주시면 좋을 텐데."], ["Pro-cras-ti-nation is not agreeable,\"", "미-루-는 건 별로거든요.\""], ["observed Amy, taking a last look at the diamonds.", "에이미가 다이아몬드를 마지막으로 한 번 더 바라보며 말했다."]], [["\"It is too soon yet for the young ladies to wear these things. The first one who is affianced will have the pearls--Madame has said it; and I have a fancy that the little turquoise ring will be given to you when you go, for Madame approves your good behavior and charming manners.\"", "\"아가씨들이 이런 걸 차기엔 아직 너무 일러요. 제일 먼저 약혼하는 분이 진주를 갖게 될 거예요. 마님이 그렇게 말씀하셨거든요. 그리고 제 짐작에는, 아가씨가 떠날 때 저 작은 터키석 반지를 주실 것 같아요. 마님이 아가씨의 얌전한 행실과 사랑스러운 몸가짐을 흡족해하시니까요.\""]], [["\"Do you think so?", "\"정말요?"], ["Oh, I'll be a lamb, if I can only have that lovely ring!", "아, 저 예쁜 반지만 가질 수 있다면 양처럼 얌전해질래요!"], ["It's ever so much prettier than Kitty Bryant's.", "키티 브라이언트 것보다 훨씬 예쁜걸."], ["I do like Aunt March, after all;\"", "역시 마치 고모님이 난 좋아요.\""], ["and Amy tried on the blue ring with a delighted face, and a firm resolve to earn it.", "에이미는 기뻐하는 얼굴로 그 파란 반지를 끼워 보며, 꼭 그것을 얻어 내고 말겠다고 굳게 마음먹었다."]], [["From that day she was a model of obedience, and the old lady complacently admired the success of her training.", "그날부터 에이미는 순종의 본보기가 되었고, 노부인은 자기 교육이 거둔 성과에 흐뭇하게 감탄했다."], ["Esther fitted up the closet with a little table, placed a footstool before it, and over it a picture taken from one of the shut-up rooms.", "에스터는 벽장에 작은 탁자를 들이고, 그 앞에 발받침을 놓고, 위에는 잠긴 방들 중 한 곳에서 가져온 그림을 걸어 주었다."], ["She thought it was of no great value, but, being appropriate, she borrowed it, well knowing that Madame would never know it, nor care if she did.", "에스터는 그 그림이 그리 값진 것은 아니라고 여겼지만, 마침맞은 그림이라 빌려 온 것이었다. 마님이 결코 알아채지 못할 것이며, 안다 한들 개의치 않으리라는 걸 잘 알았던 것이다."], ["It was, however, a very valuable copy of one of the famous pictures of the world, and Amy's beauty-loving eyes were never tired of looking up at the sweet face of the divine mother, while tender thoughts of her own were busy at her heart.", "그러나 그것은 사실 세계의 유명한 그림 중 하나를 매우 값지게 모사한 것이었고, 아름다움을 사랑하는 에이미의 눈은 거룩한 성모의 다정한 얼굴을 올려다보는 데 도무지 싫증을 내지 않았다. 그러는 동안 그녀의 마음속에서는 자신만의 애틋한 생각들이 분주히 일었다."], ["On the table she laid her little Testament and hymn-book, kept a vase always full of the best flowers Laurie brought her, and came every day to \"sit alone, thinking good thoughts, and praying the dear God to preserve her sister.\"", "탁자 위에는 작은 신약성서와 찬송가책을 올려 두었고, 로리가 가져다주는 가장 좋은 꽃들로 꽃병을 늘 가득 채워 두었으며, 날마다 그곳에 와서 \"혼자 앉아 좋은 생각을 하고, 동생을 지켜 달라고 하느님께 기도\"했다."], ["Esther had given her a rosary of black beads, with a silver cross, but Amy hung it up and did not use it, feeling doubtful as to its fitness for Protestant prayers.", "에스터는 에이미에게 은 십자가가 달린 검은 구슬 묵주를 주었지만, 에이미는 그것을 걸어 두기만 하고 쓰지는 않았다. 개신교 기도에 어울리는 것인지 미심쩍었기 때문이다."]], [["The little girl was very sincere in all this, for, being left alone outside the safe home-nest, she felt the need of some kind hand to hold by so sorely, that she instinctively turned to the strong and tender Friend, whose fatherly love most closely surrounds his little children.", "이 모든 일에서 그 어린 소녀는 무척 진실했다. 안전한 가정의 둥지 밖에 홀로 남겨지고 보니, 매달릴 다정한 손길이 너무도 절실하여, 그녀는 본능적으로 그 강하고 다정한 친구께로 돌아섰다."], ["She missed her mother's help to understand and rule herself, but having been taught where to look, she did her best to find the way, and walk in it confidingly.", "아버지의 사랑으로 자신의 어린 자녀들을 가장 가까이서 감싸 안아 주시는 분께로. 에이미는 자신을 이해하고 다스리는 데 어머니의 도움이 그리웠지만, 어디를 바라봐야 하는지를 배워 둔 터라, 그 길을 찾아 미덥게 그 위를 걸어가려고 최선을 다했다."], ["But Amy was a young pilgrim, and just now her burden seemed very heavy.", "그러나 에이미는 어린 순례자였고, 지금으로서는 그 짐이 무척 무겁게 느껴졌다."], ["She tried to forget herself, to keep cheerful, and be satisfied with doing right, though no one saw or praised her for it.", "그녀는 자신을 잊고, 명랑한 마음을 유지하고, 아무도 보아 주거나 칭찬해 주지 않더라도 옳은 일을 하는 것으로 만족하려고 애썼다."], ["In her first effort at being very, very good, she decided to make her will, as Aunt March had done; so that if she _did_ fall ill and die, her possessions might be justly and generously divided.", "아주, 아주 착해지려는 첫 시도로, 에이미는 마치 고모가 그랬듯 자기 유언장을 작성하기로 마음먹었다. 정말로 자기가 병에 걸려 죽기라도 하면, 자기 소유물이 공정하고 너그럽게 나누어지도록 하기 위해서였다."], ["It cost her a pang even to think of giving up the little treasures which in her eyes were as precious as the old lady's jewels.", "자기 눈에는 고모의 보석들만큼이나 소중한 그 작은 보물들을 내어 줄 생각만 해도 가슴이 아렸다."]], [["During one of her play-hours she wrote out the important document as well as she could, with some help from Esther as to certain legal terms, and, when the good-natured Frenchwoman had signed her name, Amy felt relieved, and laid it by to show Laurie, whom she wanted as a second witness.", "놀이 시간 중 하루를 골라, 에이미는 그 중요한 문서를 나름대로 정성껏 써 내려갔는데, 몇몇 법률 용어는 에스터의 도움을 받았다. 그 마음씨 고운 프랑스 여자가 자기 이름을 서명해 주자, 에이미는 안도하며 그것을 잘 챙겨 두었다."], ["As it was a rainy day, she went upstairs to amuse herself in one of the large chambers, and took Polly with her for company.", "두 번째 증인으로 세우고 싶은 로리에게 보여 줄 작정이었다. 그날은 비가 와서, 에이미는 큰 침실 중 한 곳에서 혼자 놀려고 위층으로 올라가며, 말동무 삼아 폴리를 데려갔다."], ["In this room there was a wardrobe full of old-fashioned costumes, with which Esther allowed her to play, and it was her favorite amusement to array herself in the faded brocades, and parade up and down before the long mirror, making stately courtesies, and sweeping her train about, with a rustle which delighted her ears.", "그 방에는 구식 의상으로 가득 찬 옷장이 있었는데, 에스터가 그 옷들을 가지고 놀게 해 주었던 것이다. 에이미가 가장 좋아하는 놀이는, 빛바랜 비단옷으로 차려입고, 긴 거울 앞을 오르락내리락 거닐며, 점잔 빼며 인사를 하고, 옷자락을 사르락사르락 끌고 다니는 것이었다."], ["So busy was she on this day that she did not hear Laurie's ring, nor see his face peeping in at her, as she gravely promenaded to and fro, flirting her fan and tossing her head, on which she wore a great pink turban, contrasting oddly with her blue brocade dress and yellow quilted petticoat.", "그 소리가 귀를 즐겁게 했던 것이다. 이날도 어찌나 그 놀이에 빠져 있었던지, 로리가 초인종을 누르는 소리도 듣지 못했고, 자기를 들여다보는 그의 얼굴도 보지 못했다."], ["She was obliged to walk carefully, for she had on high-heeled shoes, and, as Laurie told Jo afterward, it was a comical sight to see her mince along in her gay suit, with Polly sidling and bridling just behind her, imitating her as well as he could, and occasionally stopping to laugh or exclaim, \"Ain't we fine?", "그녀는 자못 점잖게 이리저리 거닐며, 부채를 살랑살랑 흔들고 고개를 까닥거렸다. 머리에는 커다란 분홍색 터번을 쓰고 있었는데, 파란 비단 드레스에 노란 누비 속치마와 묘하게 어우러졌다."], ["Get along, you fright!", "굽 높은 구두를 신은 탓에 조심조심 걸어야 했는데, 나중에 로리가 조에게 말했듯이, 그 화사한 차림으로 종종걸음 치는 모습은 우스꽝스러운 광경이었다. 게다가 폴리가 바로 뒤에서 옆으로 뒤뚱거리며 거드름을 피우면서, 할 수 있는 한 에이미를 흉내 냈고, 이따금 멈춰 서서 웃거나 이렇게 외치곤 했다."], ["Hold your tongue!", "\"우리 멋지지? 썩 꺼져, 이 흉측한 것아!"], ["Kiss me, dear!", "입 다물어! 입 맞춰 줘, 얘야!"], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["ha!\"", "하!\""]], [["Having with difficulty restrained an explosion of merriment, lest it should offend her majesty, Laurie tapped, and was graciously received.", "여왕 폐하의 심기를 거스를까 봐 터져 나오려는 웃음을 가까스로 참고서, 로리는 문을 두드렸고, 정중한 환대를 받았다."]], [["\"Sit down and rest while I put these things away; then I want to consult you about a very serious matter,\"", "\"앉아서 쉬고 있어. 내가 이것들 좀 치울게."], ["said Amy, when she had shown her splendor, and driven Polly into a corner.", "그러고 나서 아주 진지한 문제로 너랑 상의하고 싶거든.\" 에이미가 자기 화려한 차림을 다 보여 주고, 폴리를 구석으로 몰아넣은 뒤 말했다."], ["\"That bird is the trial of my life,\"", "\"저 새는 내 인생의 골칫거리야.\""], ["she continued, removing the pink mountain from her head, while Laurie seated himself astride of a chair.", "그녀가 머리에서 분홍색 산더미를 벗어 내리며 말을 이었다. 로리는 의자에 거꾸로 걸터앉았다."], ["\"Yesterday, when aunt was asleep, and I was trying to be as still as a mouse, Polly began to squall and flap about in his cage; so I went to let him out, and found a big spider there.", "\"어제 고모님이 주무시고, 내가 생쥐처럼 쥐 죽은 듯 있으려고 애쓰는데, 폴리가 새장 안에서 빽빽거리며 푸드덕거리는 거야. 그래서 꺼내 주려고 갔더니, 글쎄 거기 커다란 거미가 있지 뭐야."], ["I poked it out, and it ran under the bookcase; Polly marched straight after it, stooped down and peeped under the bookcase, saying, in his funny way, with a cock of his eye, 'Come out and take a walk, my dear.'", "내가 거미를 쿡 찔러서 내쫓으니까, 그게 책장 밑으로 도망쳤어. 폴리가 곧장 그 뒤를 졸졸 따라가더니, 몸을 굽히고 책장 밑을 들여다보면서, 그 우스운 말투로 한쪽 눈을 까닥하며 이러는 거야."], ["I _couldn't_ help laughing, which made Poll swear, and aunt woke up and scolded us both.\"", "'나와서 산책이나 하지, 얘야.' 나도 모르게 웃음이 터졌고, 그 바람에 폴리가 욕을 해 대니까, 고모님이 깨셔서 우리 둘 다 야단치셨지 뭐야.\""]], [["\"Did the spider accept the old fellow's invitation?\"", "\"그래서 거미가 그 영감님 초대에 응하던?\""], ["asked Laurie, yawning.", "로리가 하품하며 물었다."]], [["\"Yes; out it came, and away ran Polly, frightened to death, and scrambled up on aunt's chair, calling out, 'Catch her!", "\"응. 거미가 기어 나오니까, 폴리는 기겁을 하고 도망쳐서 고모님 의자 위로 기어 올라가더니, 내가 거미를 쫓는 동안 '저거 잡아!"], ["catch her!", "잡아!"], ["catch her!'", "잡아!'"], ["as I chased the spider.", "하고 외쳐 댔어.\""]], [["\"That's a lie! Oh lor!\"", "\"거짓말!"], ["cried the parrot, pecking at Laurie's toes.", "어이쿠 맙소사!\" 앵무새가 로리의 발가락을 쪼며 외쳤다."]], [["\"I'd wring your neck if you were mine, you old torment,\"", "\"네가 내 거였으면 목을 비틀어 버렸을 텐데, 이 늙은 골칫덩어리야.\""], ["cried Laurie, shaking his fist at the bird, who put his head on one side, and gravely croaked, \"Allyluyer!", "로리가 새를 향해 주먹을 흔들며 외치자, 폴리는 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃하고는 점잖게 깍깍거렸다. \"할렐루야!"], ["bless your buttons, dear!\"", "복 받으세요, 얘야!\""]], [["\"Now I'm ready,\"", "\"이제 됐어.\""], ["said Amy, shutting the wardrobe, and taking a paper out of her pocket.", "에이미가 옷장을 닫고 주머니에서 종이 한 장을 꺼내며 말했다."], ["\"I want you to read that, please, and tell me if it is legal and right.", "\"이거 좀 읽어 봐 줘. 그리고 이게 법적으로 맞고 제대로 된 건지 말해 줘."], ["I felt that I ought to do it, for life is uncertain and I don't want any ill-feeling over my tomb.\"", "난 이걸 꼭 해 둬야 할 것 같았어. 인생이란 알 수 없는 거고, 내 무덤을 두고 서로 감정 상하는 일은 없었으면 하니까.\""]], [["Laurie bit his lips, and turning a little from the pensive speaker, read the following document, with praiseworthy gravity, considering the spelling:--", "로리는 입술을 깨물고, 수심에 잠긴 그 작은 연설가에게서 살짝 몸을 돌린 채, 다음 문서를 읽어 내려갔다. 철자법을 감안하면 칭찬할 만큼 진지한 태도였다. \"나의 마지막 유언장과 유언.\""]], [["\"I, Amy Curtis March, being in my sane mind, do give and bequeethe all my earthly property--viz.", "\"나, 에이미 커티스 마치는, 멀쩡한 정신으로, 나의 모든 현세 재산을 다음과 같이 주고 물려준다."], ["to wit:--namely", "즉, 곧, 말하자면—\""]], [["\"To my father, my best pictures, sketches, maps, and works of art, including frames.", "\"아버지께는, 나의 가장 좋은 그림들과 스케치, 지도, 그리고 액자를 포함한 미술 작품들을."], ["Also my $100, to do what he likes with.", "또 나의 100달러를, 마음대로 쓰시도록.\""]], [["\"To my mother, all my clothes, except the blue apron with pockets,--also my likeness, and my medal, with much love.", "\"어머니께는, 주머니 달린 파란 앞치마만 빼고 나의 모든 옷을. 또 나의 초상화와 메달을, 깊은 사랑과 함께.\""]], [["\"To my dear sister Margaret, I give my turkquoise ring (if I get it), also my green box with the doves on it, also my piece of real lace for her neck, and my sketch of her as a memorial of her 'little girl.'", "\"사랑하는 언니 마거릿에게는, 나의 터키석 반지를 (내가 갖게 된다면) 준다. 또 비둘기 무늬가 그려진 초록 상자도, 그리고 목에 두를 진짜 레이스 한 조각과, 언니의 '어린 동생'을 기리는 뜻으로 내가 그린 언니 스케치도.\""]], [["\"To Jo I leave my breast-pin, the one mended with sealing wax, also my bronze inkstand--she lost the cover--and my most precious plaster rabbit, because I am sorry I burnt up her story.", "\"조 언니에게는 나의 브로치를 남긴다. 봉랍으로 고친 그거 말이야. 또 청동 잉크스탠드도—뚜껑은 언니가 잃어버렸지만—그리고 내가 가장 아끼는 석고 토끼도. 언니 이야기를 태워 버린 게 미안하니까.\""]], [["\"To Beth (if she lives after me) I give my dolls and the little bureau, my fan, my linen collars and my new slippers if she can wear them being thin when she gets well.", "\"베스에게는 (나보다 오래 산다면) 나의 인형들과 작은 서랍장, 부채, 아마포 깃, 그리고 새 슬리퍼를 준다. 다 나으면 몸이 야월 테니 신을 수 있다면."], ["And I herewith also leave her my regret that I ever made fun of old Joanna.", "그리고 늙은 조애나를 놀려 댄 것에 대한 후회도 이로써 함께 남긴다.\""]], [["\"To my friend and neighbor Theodore Laurence I bequeethe my paper marshay portfolio, my clay model of a horse though he did say it hadn't any neck. Also in return for his great kindness in the hour of affliction any one of my artistic works he likes, Noter Dame is the best.", "\"나의 친구이자 이웃인 시어도어 로런스에게는 나의 종이 죽 포트폴리오를 물려준다. 또 내가 만든 점토 말 모형도. 비록 그게 목이 없다고 말하긴 했지만. 또 시련의 시간에 베풀어 준 그 큰 친절에 대한 보답으로, 내 미술 작품 중 그가 좋아하는 것 무엇이든 하나를. 노트르담이 제일 잘된 거야.\""]], [["\"To our venerable benefactor Mr.", "\"우리의 존경하는 은인 로런스 씨께는, 뚜껑에 거울이 달린 자주색 상자를 남긴다."], ["Laurence I leave my purple box with a looking glass in the cover which will be nice for his pens and remind him of the departed girl who thanks him for his favors to her family, specially Beth.", "그분 펜을 넣어 두기에 좋을 것이고, 자기 가족에게, 특히 베스에게 베풀어 주신 호의에 감사하는 떠난 소녀를 떠올리시게 할 것이다.\""]], [["\"I wish my favorite playmate Kitty Bryant to have the blue silk apron and my gold-bead ring with a kiss.", "\"내가 가장 좋아하는 놀이 동무 키티 브라이언트에게는 파란 비단 앞치마와 금구슬 반지를, 입맞춤과 함께 주고 싶다.\""]], [["\"To Hannah I give the bandbox she wanted and all the patch work I leave hoping she 'will remember me, when it you see.'", "\"해나에게는 갖고 싶어 하던 모자 상자와, 내가 남기는 조각보를 다 준다. '이걸 볼 때마다 나를 기억해' 주기를 바라면서.\""]], [["\"And now having disposed of my most valuable property I hope all will be satisfied and not blame the dead.", "\"이제 나의 가장 값진 재산을 다 처분했으니, 모두가 만족하고 죽은 자를 탓하지 않기를 바란다."], ["I forgive every one, and trust we may all meet when the trump shall sound.", "나는 모든 이를 용서하며, 나팔 소리가 울릴 때 우리 모두 다시 만나기를 믿는다."], ["Amen.", "아멘.\""]], [["\"To this will and testiment I set my hand and seal on this 20th day of Nov. Anni Domino 1861.", "\"이 유언장과 유언에, 나는 1861년 주님의 해 11월 20일에 나의 손으로 서명하고 봉인한다.\""], ["\"AMY CURTIS MARCH.", "\"에이미 커티스 마치."]], [["The last name was written in pencil, and Amy explained that he was to rewrite it in ink, and seal it up for her properly.", "증인: 에스텔 발노르, 시어도어 로런스.\" 마지막 이름은 연필로 적혀 있었고, 에이미는 그가 그것을 잉크로 다시 써서 자기를 위해 제대로 봉해 주어야 한다고 설명했다."]], [["\"What put it into your head?", "\"이런 생각을 어떻게 한 거야?"], ["Did any one tell you about Beth's giving away her things?\"", "누가 베스가 자기 물건들을 나눠 주려 한다는 얘길 해 줬어?\""], ["asked Laurie soberly, as Amy laid a bit of red tape, with sealing-wax, a taper, and a standish before him.", "로리가 진지하게 물었다. 에이미가 봉랍이 묻은 붉은 끈 한 조각과 가느다란 양초, 잉크 받침대를 그의 앞에 놓아 주는 동안이었다."]], [["She explained; and then asked anxiously, \"What about Beth?\"", "에이미는 자초지종을 설명하더니, 불안하게 물었다. \"베스는 어때?\""]], [["\"I'm sorry I spoke; but as I did, I'll tell you.", "\"괜히 말을 꺼냈네. 하지만 이미 했으니까 얘기해 줄게."], ["She felt so ill one day that she told Jo she wanted to give her piano to Meg, her cats to you, and the poor old doll to Jo, who would love it for her sake.", "어느 날 베스가 너무 아파서, 조한테 피아노는 메그한테, 고양이들은 너한테, 그리고 그 가엾고 낡은 인형은 조한테 주고 싶다고 했대. 자기를 봐서라도 조가 인형을 아껴 줄 테니까."], ["She was sorry she had so little to give, and left locks of hair to the rest of us, and her best love to grandpa.", "베스는 줄 게 너무 적어서 미안하다며, 나머지 우리한테는 머리카락 한 줌씩을 남기고, 할아버지께는 가장 큰 사랑을 전했어."], ["_She_ never thought of a will.\"", "베스는 유언장 같은 건 생각도 못 했지.\""]], [["Laurie was signing and sealing as he spoke, and did not look up till a great tear dropped on the paper.", "로리는 말을 하면서 서명하고 봉인하느라, 종이 위로 굵은 눈물 한 방울이 떨어질 때까지 고개를 들지 않았다."], ["Amy's face was full of trouble; but she only said, \"Don't people put sort of postscripts to their wills, sometimes?\"", "에이미의 얼굴은 시름으로 가득했지만, 그녀는 이렇게만 말했다. \"사람들이 유언장에 가끔 추신 같은 걸 덧붙이기도 하지 않아?\""]], [["\"Yes; 'codicils,'", "\"응."], ["they call them.\"", "'추가 조항'이라고들 하지.\""]], [["\"Put one in mine then--that I wish _all_ my curls cut off, and given round to my friends. I forgot it; but I want it done, though it will spoil my looks.\"", "\"그럼 내 것에도 하나 넣어 줘. 내 곱슬머리를 전부 잘라서 친구들한테 나눠 주고 싶다고. 깜빡했어. 그렇게 하고 싶어. 내 외모가 망가지겠지만.\""]], [["Laurie added it, smiling at Amy's last and greatest sacrifice.", "로리는 에이미의 마지막이자 가장 큰 희생에 미소 지으며 그것을 덧붙여 주었다."], ["Then he amused her for an hour, and was much interested in all her trials.", "그러고 나서 한 시간 동안 그녀를 즐겁게 해 주었고, 그녀가 겪은 온갖 시련을 무척 흥미롭게 들어 주었다."], ["But when he came to go, Amy held him back to whisper, with trembling lips, \"Is there really any danger about Beth?\"", "그러나 돌아가려고 일어서자, 에이미가 그를 붙잡고 떨리는 입술로 속삭였다. \"베스가 정말로 위험해?\""]], [["\"I'm afraid there is; but we must hope for the best, so don't cry, dear;\"", "\"그런 것 같아. 하지만 우린 좋은 쪽으로 희망을 품어야 해."], ["and Laurie put his arm about her with a brotherly gesture which was very comforting.", "그러니 울지 마, 얘야.\" 로리는 형제처럼 다정한 몸짓으로 그녀를 한 팔로 감쌌고, 그것은 큰 위로가 되었다."]], [["When he had gone, she went to her little chapel, and, sitting in the twilight, prayed for Beth, with streaming tears and an aching heart, feeling that a million turquoise rings would not console her for the loss of her gentle little sister.", "그가 떠나자, 에이미는 자기 작은 예배실로 가서, 황혼 속에 앉아 베스를 위해 기도했다. 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 가슴이 미어지는 채로, 터키석 반지가 백만 개 있어도 그 다정한 어린 동생을 잃은 슬픔은 위로받을 수 없으리라는 것을 느끼면서. 은밀한 이야기."]], [["I don't think I have any words in which to tell the meeting of the mother and daughters; such hours are beautiful to live, but very hard to describe, so I will leave it to the imagination of my readers, merely saying that the house was full of genuine happiness, and that Meg's tender hope was realized; for when Beth woke from that long, healing sleep, the first objects on which her eyes fell _were_ the little rose and mother's face.", "어머니와 딸들의 재회를 묘사할 말을 나는 도무지 찾지 못하겠다. 그런 시간은 살아 내기에는 더없이 아름답지만 글로 옮기기는 무척 어려운 법이니, 그저 독자 여러분의 상상에 맡기고, 다만 집 안이 진정한 행복으로 가득했으며 메그의 애틋한 희망이 이루어졌다는 것만 말해 두겠다."], ["Too weak to wonder at anything, she only smiled, and nestled close into the loving arms about her, feeling that the hungry longing was satisfied at last.", "베스가 그 길고 치유의 잠에서 깨어났을 때, 그 눈에 맨 처음 들어온 것이 정말로 작은 장미와 어머니의 얼굴이었던 것이다."], ["Then she slept again, and the girls waited upon their mother, for she would not unclasp the thin hand which clung to hers even in sleep.", "너무 기운이 없어 무엇에도 놀랄 수 없었던 베스는 그저 미소만 지으며, 자기를 감싸는 사랑의 품속으로 바싹 파고들었다. 마침내 그 굶주린 갈망이 채워졌다고 느끼면서."], ["Hannah had \"dished up\"", "그러고는 다시 잠들었고, 자매들은 어머니의 시중을 들었다."], ["an astonishing breakfast for the traveller, finding it impossible to vent her excitement in any other way; and Meg and Jo fed their mother like dutiful young storks, while they listened to her whispered account of father's state, Mr.", "어머니가 잠결에도 자기 손을 꼭 쥔 그 야윈 손을 차마 풀지 못했기 때문이다. 해나는 흥분을 달리 풀 길이 없자, 먼 길을 온 분을 위해 놀라운 아침상을 \"차려 냈다.\""], ["Brooke's promise to stay and nurse him, the delays which the storm occasioned on the homeward journey, and the unspeakable comfort Laurie's hopeful face had given her when she arrived, worn out with fatigue, anxiety, and cold.", "메그와 조는 효성스러운 아기 황새들처럼 어머니에게 음식을 떠먹이며, 어머니가 속삭이듯 들려주는 이야기에 귀를 기울였다. 아버지의 상태며, 남아서 아버지를 간병하겠다는 브룩 씨의 약속이며, 돌아오는 길에 폭풍이 빚은 지체며, 피로와 근심과 추위에 녹초가 된 채 도착했을 때 로리의 희망찬 얼굴이 안겨 준 이루 말할 수 없는 위안에 대해서."]], [["What a strange, yet pleasant day that was!", "얼마나 기이하면서도 즐거운 하루였던가!"], ["so brilliant and gay without, for all the world seemed abroad to welcome the first snow; so quiet and reposeful within, for every one slept, spent with watching, and a Sabbath stillness reigned through the house, while nodding Hannah mounted guard at the door.", "밖은 그토록 눈부시고 화사했으니, 마치 온 세상이 첫눈을 맞이하러 나온 듯했다. 안은 그토록 고요하고 평온했으니, 모두가 밤샘에 지쳐 잠들었고, 졸린 해나가 문간에서 보초를 서는 동안 집 안에는 안식일 같은 정적이 감돌았다."], ["With a blissful sense of burdens lifted off, Meg and Jo closed their weary eyes, and lay at rest, like storm-beaten boats, safe at anchor in a quiet harbor.", "짐을 벗어 던진 듯한 더없는 안도감 속에서, 메그와 조는 지친 눈을 감고 누워 쉬었다. 마치 폭풍에 시달리다 고요한 항구에 무사히 닻을 내린 배들처럼."], ["Mrs. March would not leave Beth's side, but rested in the big chair, waking often to look at, touch, and brood over her child, like a miser over some recovered treasure.", "마치 부인은 베스 곁을 떠나지 않고 큰 의자에서 쉬면서도, 마치 되찾은 보물을 지켜보는 구두쇠처럼, 자주 깨어나 아이를 바라보고, 만져 보고, 골똘히 들여다보았다."]], [["Laurie, meanwhile, posted off to comfort Amy, and told his story so well that Aunt March actually \"sniffed\" herself, and never once said, \"I told you so.\"", "한편 로리는 에이미를 위로하러 부리나케 달려가서, 어찌나 이야기를 잘했던지 마치 고모마저 자기도 모르게 \"훌쩍\"였고, 단 한 번도 \"내가 그럴 줄 알았다\"는 말을 하지 않았다."], ["Amy came out so strong on this occasion that I think the good thoughts in the little chapel really began to bear fruit.", "이 자리에서 에이미가 어찌나 의젓하게 처신했던지, 나는 그 작은 예배실에서의 좋은 생각들이 정말로 열매를 맺기 시작했다고 본다."], ["She dried her tears quickly, restrained her impatience to see her mother, and never even thought of the turquoise ring, when the old lady heartily agreed in Laurie's opinion, that she behaved \"like a capital little woman.\"", "에이미는 얼른 눈물을 닦았고, 어머니를 보고 싶은 조바심을 억눌렀으며, 노부인이 에이미가 \"훌륭한 작은 숙녀답게 처신했다\"는 로리의 의견에 진심으로 동의했을 때, 터키석 반지 생각조차 하지 않았다."], ["Even Polly seemed impressed, for he called her \"good girl,\" blessed her buttons, and begged her to \"come and take a walk, dear,\"", "폴리마저 감명을 받은 듯, 에이미를 \"착한 아이\"라 부르고, 복을 빌어 주며, 더없이 상냥한 어조로 \"이리 와서 산책하지, 얘야\""], ["in his most affable tone.", "하고 졸랐다."], ["She would very gladly have gone out to enjoy the bright wintry weather; but, discovering that Laurie was dropping with sleep in spite of manful efforts to conceal the fact, she persuaded him to rest on the sofa, while she wrote a note to her mother.", "에이미는 화창한 겨울 날씨를 즐기러 기꺼이 밖으로 나가고 싶었다. 하지만 로리가 사내답게 애써 감추려는데도 졸음에 겨워 꾸벅거리는 것을 알아채고는, 그를 구슬려 소파에서 쉬게 하고, 자신은 어머니에게 보낼 편지를 썼다."], ["She was a long time about it; and, when she returned, he was stretched out, with both arms under his head, sound asleep, while Aunt March had pulled down the curtains, and sat doing nothing in an unusual fit of benignity.", "한참 걸려 편지를 다 쓰고 돌아와 보니, 로리는 두 팔을 머리 밑에 받친 채 길게 뻗어 깊이 잠들어 있었고, 마치 고모는 커튼을 내리고서 보기 드물게 너그러운 마음이 들었는지 아무것도 하지 않고 앉아 있었다."]], [["After a while, they began to think he was not going to wake till night, and I'm not sure that he would, had he not been effectually roused by Amy's cry of joy at sight of her mother.", "얼마쯤 지나자, 그들은 로리가 밤이 될 때까지 깨지 않을 거라고 생각하기 시작했다. 어머니를 본 에이미의 기쁨에 찬 외침이 그를 확실하게 깨우지 않았더라면, 정말 그랬을지도 모를 일이다."], ["There probably were a good many happy little girls in and about the city that day, but it is my private opinion that Amy was the happiest of all, when she sat in her mother's lap and told her trials, receiving consolation and compensation in the shape of approving smiles and fond caresses.", "그날 그 도시 안팎에는 행복한 어린 소녀가 꽤 많았을 테지만, 어머니의 무릎에 앉아 자기가 겪은 시련을 이야기하며, 흐뭇한 미소와 다정한 손길의 형태로 위로와 보상을 받던 그 순간의 에이미가 그중 가장 행복했으리라는 것이 나의 사사로운 견해다."], ["They were alone together in the chapel, to which her mother did not object when its purpose was explained to her.", "두 사람은 예배실에 단둘이 있었는데, 그 용도를 설명해 드리자 어머니도 반대하지 않았다."]], [["\"On the contrary, I like it very much, dear,\"", "\"오히려 난 그게 무척 마음에 드는구나, 얘야.\""], ["looking from the dusty rosary to the well-worn little book, and the lovely picture with its garland of evergreen.", "어머니는 먼지 쌓인 묵주에서 손때 묻은 작은 책으로, 그리고 상록수 화환을 두른 사랑스러운 그림으로 눈길을 옮기며 말했다."], ["\"It is an excellent plan to have some place where we can go to be quiet, when things vex or grieve us.", "\"일이 우리를 짜증 나게 하거나 슬프게 할 때 조용히 가 있을 수 있는 곳을 마련해 두는 건 아주 좋은 방법이야."], ["There are a good many hard times in this life of ours, but we can always bear them if we ask help in the right way.", "이 인생에는 힘든 때가 참 많지만, 올바른 방식으로 도움을 청한다면 우린 언제든 그것들을 견뎌 낼 수 있단다."], ["I think my little girl is learning this?\"", "우리 딸이 이걸 배우고 있는 모양이지?\""]], [["\"Yes, mother; and when I go home I mean to have a corner in the big closet to put my books, and the copy of that picture which I've tried to make. The woman's face is not good,--it's too beautiful for me to draw,--but the baby is done better, and I love it very much. I like to think He was a little child once, for then I don't seem so far away, and that helps me.\"", "\"네, 엄마. 그리고 집에 가면, 큰 벽장 한구석에 제 책들이랑, 제가 따라 그려 본 저 그림 모사본을 둘 거예요. 여자분 얼굴은 잘 못 그렸어요. 제가 그리기엔 너무 아름다워서요. 하지만 아기는 더 잘 그렸고, 전 그게 무척 좋아요. 그분도 한때는 어린아이였다는 걸 생각하는 게 좋아요. 그러면 그분이 그렇게 멀게 느껴지지 않거든요. 그게 제게 힘이 돼요.\""]], [["As Amy pointed to the smiling Christ-child on his mother's knee, Mrs. March saw something on the lifted hand that made her smile.", "에이미가 어머니 무릎에 앉아 미소 짓는 아기 예수를 가리켰을 때, 마치 부인은 그 치켜든 손에 있는 무언가를 보고 미소를 지었다."], ["She said nothing, but Amy understood the look, and, after a minute's pause, she added gravely,--", "어머니는 아무 말도 하지 않았지만, 에이미는 그 눈길을 알아채고, 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 진지하게 덧붙였다."]], [["\"I wanted to speak to you about this, but I forgot it.", "\"이것에 대해 엄마한테 말씀드리려고 했는데, 깜빡했어요."], ["Aunt gave me the ring to-day; she called me to her and kissed me, and put it on my finger, and said I was a credit to her, and she'd like to keep me always.", "고모님이 오늘 이 반지를 주셨어요. 저를 부르시더니 입을 맞춰 주시고, 제 손가락에 끼워 주시면서, 제가 당신께 자랑거리라고, 저를 늘 곁에 두고 싶다고 하셨어요."], ["She gave that funny guard to keep the turquoise on, as it's too big.", "터키석이 너무 커서 빠지지 않게 잡아 주는 이 우스운 덧반지도 주셨고요."], ["I'd like to wear them, mother; can I?\"", "이걸 끼고 싶어요, 엄마. 그래도 돼요?\""]], [["\"They are very pretty, but I think you're rather too young for such ornaments, Amy,\"", "\"아주 예쁘긴 하구나. 하지만 넌 그런 장신구를 끼기엔 좀 어린 것 같은데, 에이미.\""], ["said Mrs.", "마치 부인이 그 통통한 작은 손을 바라보며 말했다."], ["March, looking at the plump little hand, with the band of sky-blue stones on the forefinger, and the quaint guard, formed of two tiny, golden hands clasped together.", "집게손가락에는 하늘빛 보석이 박힌 반지가, 그리고 작고 앙증맞은 두 황금 손이 마주 잡은 모양으로 된 별난 덧반지가 끼워져 있었다."]], [["\"I'll try not to be vain,\"", "\"허영을 부리지 않으려고 애쓸게요.\""], ["said Amy.", "에이미가 말했다."], ["\"I don't think I like it only because it's so pretty; but I want to wear it as the girl in the story wore her bracelet, to remind me of something.\"", "\"이게 그냥 예뻐서 좋은 건 아닌 것 같아요. 이야기 속 소녀가 무언가를 떠올리려고 팔찌를 찼던 것처럼, 저도 그렇게 끼고 싶은 거예요.\""]], [["\"Do you mean Aunt March?\"", "\"마치 고모님을 떠올리려고?\""], ["asked her mother, laughing.", "어머니가 웃으며 물었다."]], [["\"No, to remind me not to be selfish.\"", "\"아니요. 이기적으로 굴지 말자고 떠올리려고요.\""], ["Amy looked so earnest and sincere about it, that her mother stopped laughing, and listened respectfully to the little plan.", "에이미가 어찌나 진지하고 진실하게 그 말을 했던지, 어머니는 웃음을 멈추고 그 작은 계획을 정중히 귀담아들었다."]], [["\"I've thought a great deal lately about my 'bundle of naughties,'", "\"전 요즘 제 '못된 점 보따리'에 대해 많이 생각해 봤는데, 이기적인 게 그중에서 제일 큰 거예요."], ["and being selfish is the largest one in it; so I'm going to try hard to cure it, if I can.", "그러니 할 수 있다면 그걸 고치려고 열심히 애쓸 거예요. 베스는 이기적이지 않잖아요."], ["Beth isn't selfish, and that's the reason every one loves her and feels so bad at the thoughts of losing her.", "그래서 다들 그 애를 사랑하고, 그 애를 잃을 생각에 그렇게 마음 아파하는 거고요. 만약 제가 아팠다면 사람들이 그 절반만큼도 마음 아파하지 않을 거예요."], ["People wouldn't feel half so bad about me if I was sick, and I don't deserve to have them; but I'd like to be loved and missed by a great many friends, so I'm going to try and be like Beth all I can.", "저는 그럴 자격도 없고요. 하지만 저도 많은 친구한테 사랑받고, 또 그리워하는 사람이 되고 싶어요."], ["I'm apt to forget my resolutions; but if I had something always about me to remind me, I guess I should do better.", "그래서 할 수 있는 한 베스처럼 되려고 애쓸 거예요. 전 결심을 잘 잊어버리는데, 늘 곁에 뭔가 떠올리게 해 줄 게 있으면 더 잘할 수 있을 것 같아요."], ["May I try this way?\"", "이런 식으로 해 봐도 될까요?\""]], [["\"Yes; but I have more faith in the corner of the big closet. Wear your ring, dear, and do your best; I think you will prosper, for the sincere wish to be good is half the battle. Now I must go back to Beth. Keep up your heart, little daughter, and we will soon have you home again.\"", "\"그래. 하지만 난 큰 벽장 한구석을 더 믿는단다. 반지를 끼렴, 얘야. 그리고 최선을 다하렴. 넌 잘해 낼 거야. 착해지고자 하는 진실한 소망이야말로 절반의 승리니까. 자, 이제 난 베스한테 가 봐야겠다. 기운 내렴, 우리 막내딸. 곧 너도 집으로 데려올 테니까.\""]], [["That evening, while Meg was writing to her father, to report the traveller's safe arrival, Jo slipped up stairs into Beth's room, and, finding her mother in her usual place, stood a minute twisting her fingers in her hair, with a worried gesture and an undecided look.", "그날 저녁, 메그가 아버지에게 길손이 무사히 도착했음을 알리는 편지를 쓰는 동안, 조는 살그머니 위층 베스의 방으로 올라갔다. 어머니가 늘 있던 자리에 있는 것을 보고, 조는 잠시 서서 근심스러운 몸짓으로 손가락에 머리칼을 배배 꼬며, 머뭇거리는 표정을 지었다."]], [["\"What is it, deary?\" asked Mrs.", "\"무슨 일이니, 얘야?\""], ["March, holding out her hand, with a face which invited confidence.", "마치 부인이 속내를 털어놓으라는 듯한 얼굴로 손을 내밀며 물었다."]], [["\"I want to tell you something, mother.\"", "\"엄마한테 할 얘기가 있어요.\" \"메그에 관한 거니?\""]], [["\"About Meg?\" \"How quickly you guessed!", "\"어떻게 그렇게 금방 알아채셨어요!"], ["Yes, it's about her, and though it's a little thing, it fidgets me.\"", "네, 언니에 관한 건데, 별것 아닌 일이긴 하지만 제 마음이 영 편치 않아서요.\""]], [["\"Beth is asleep; speak low, and tell me all about it.", "\"베스가 자고 있으니, 목소리를 낮추고 다 얘기해 보렴."], ["That Moffat hasn't been here, I hope?\"", "그 모팻이라는 작자가 여기 왔던 건 아니겠지?\""], ["asked Mrs. March rather sharply.", "마치 부인이 다소 날카롭게 물었다."]], [["\"No, I should have shut the door in his face if he had,\"", "\"아뇨, 만약 왔다면 제가 코앞에서 문을 닫아 버렸을걸요.\""], ["said Jo, settling herself on the floor at her mother's feet.", "조가 어머니 발치 바닥에 자리를 잡고 앉으며 말했다."], ["\"Last summer Meg left a pair of gloves over at the Laurences', and only one was returned.", "\"지난여름에 메그 언니가 로런스 댁에 장갑 한 켤레를 두고 왔는데, 한 짝만 돌아왔어요."], ["We forgot all about it, till Teddy told me that Mr. Brooke had it.", "우린 그 일을 까맣게 잊고 있었는데, 테디가 브룩 선생님이 그걸 갖고 있다고 알려 줬어요."], ["He kept it in his waistcoat pocket, and once it fell out, and Teddy joked him about it, and Mr.", "조끼 주머니에 넣고 다녔는데, 한번은 그게 떨어지니까 테디가 그걸 가지고 놀려 댔대요."], ["Brooke owned that he liked Meg, but didn't dare say so, she was so young and he so poor.", "그러자 브룩 선생님이 메그 언니를 좋아한다고 털어놓으면서, 언니가 너무 어리고 자기는 너무 가난해서 차마 말은 못 하겠다고 했대요."], ["Now, isn't it a _dread_ful state of things?\"", "자, 이거 정말 끔찍한 상황 아니에요?\""]], [["\"Do you think Meg cares for him?\" asked Mrs.", "\"넌 메그가 그 사람한테 마음이 있는 것 같니?\""], ["March, with an anxious look.", "마치 부인이 근심 어린 표정으로 물었다."]], [["\"Mercy me!", "\"아유, 참!"], ["I don't know anything about love and such nonsense!\"", "전 사랑이니 그런 시시한 것에 대해선 아무것도 몰라요!\""], ["cried Jo, with a funny mixture of interest and contempt.", "조가 흥미와 경멸이 우습게 뒤섞인 어조로 외쳤다."], ["\"In novels, the girls show it by starting and blushing, fainting away, growing thin, and acting like fools.", "\"소설에서는 여자애들이 깜짝 놀라고 얼굴을 붉히고, 기절하고, 비쩍 마르고, 바보처럼 굴면서 그 마음을 드러내잖아요. 그런데 메그 언니는 그런 짓을 전혀 안 해요."], ["Now Meg does not do anything of the sort: she eats and drinks and sleeps, like a sensible creature: she looks straight in my face when I talk about that man, and only blushes a little bit when Teddy jokes about lovers.", "멀쩡한 사람처럼 잘 먹고, 잘 마시고, 잘 자요. 제가 그 사람 얘길 꺼내도 제 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보고, 테디가 연인 어쩌고 놀려 댈 때만 살짝 얼굴을 붉힐 뿐이에요."], ["I forbid him to do it, but he doesn't mind me as he ought.\"", "제가 테디한테 그러지 말라고 했는데, 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 제 말을 듣질 않네요.\""]], [["\"Then you fancy that Meg is _not_ interested in John?\"", "\"그럼 넌 메그가 존한테 관심이 없다고 보는 거니?\""]], [["\"Who?\"", "\"누구요?\""], ["cried Jo, staring.", "조가 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 외쳤다."]], [["\"Mr. Brooke.", "\"브룩 선생님 말이다."], ["I call him 'John'", "난 이제 그분을 '존'이라고 부른단다."], ["now; we fell into the way of doing so at the hospital, and he likes it.\"", "병원에서 그렇게 부르게 됐는데, 그분도 좋아하시더구나.\""]], [["\"Oh, dear!", "\"아이고, 이런!"], ["I know you'll take his part: he's been good to father, and you won't send him away, but let Meg marry him, if she wants to.", "엄마가 그분 편을 드실 줄 알았어요. 아빠한테 잘해 드렸으니, 엄마는 그분을 내치지 않고, 메그 언니가 원하면 결혼하게 두실 거잖아요."], ["Mean thing!", "비겁하긴!"], ["to go petting papa and helping you, just to wheedle you into liking him;\"", "아빠한테 살갑게 굴고 엄마를 도운 게 다 엄마 마음을 사려는 수작이었어.\""], ["and Jo pulled her hair again with a wrathful tweak.", "조가 다시 화난 듯 머리칼을 홱 잡아당겼다."]], [["\"My dear, don't get angry about it, and I will tell you how it happened.", "\"얘야, 그 일로 화내지 마. 어떻게 된 일인지 말해 줄 테니."], ["John went with me at Mr. Laurence's request, and was so devoted to poor father that we couldn't help getting fond of him.", "존은 로런스 씨의 부탁으로 나와 함께 갔는데, 가엾은 네 아버지께 어찌나 헌신적이던지 우리도 그분을 좋아하지 않을 수가 없었단다."], ["He was perfectly open and honorable about Meg, for he told us he loved her, but would earn a comfortable home before he asked her to marry him.", "그분은 메그에 대해 더없이 솔직하고 떳떳했어. 메그를 사랑한다고 말하면서도, 청혼하기 전에 먼저 안락한 보금자리를 마련하겠다고 했으니까."], ["He only wanted our leave to love her and work for her, and the right to make her love him if he could.", "그분은 그저 메그를 사랑하고 메그를 위해 일할 수 있도록 우리 허락을 구한 것뿐이고, 할 수만 있다면 메그도 자기를 사랑하게 만들 권리를 바란 것뿐이었어."], ["He is a truly excellent young man, and we could not refuse to listen to him; but I will not consent to Meg's engaging herself so young.\"", "그분은 정말 훌륭한 청년이라, 우리도 그 말을 들어주지 않을 수 없었단다. 하지만 메그가 이렇게 어린 나이에 약혼하는 것에는 동의하지 않을 거야.\""]], [["\"Of course not; it would be idiotic!", "\"당연하죠. 그건 바보 같은 짓이에요!"], ["I knew there was mischief brewing; I felt it; and now it's worse than I imagined.", "뭔가 수상한 일이 벌어지고 있다는 걸 알았어요. 느낌이 왔거든요."], ["I just wish I could marry Meg myself, and keep her safe in the family.\"", "그런데 이젠 제 상상보다 더 나빠졌네요. 차라리 제가 메그 언니랑 결혼해서, 언니를 우리 식구로 안전하게 붙잡아 둘 수 있으면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["This odd arrangement made Mrs.", "이 엉뚱한 발상에 마치 부인은 미소를 지었지만, 진지하게 말했다."], ["March smile; but she said gravely, \"Jo, I confide in you, and don't wish you to say anything to Meg yet.", "\"조, 난 너를 믿는단다. 그러니 아직은 메그한테 아무 말도 하지 말아 다오."], ["When John comes back, and I see them together, I can judge better of her feelings toward him.\"", "존이 돌아오고, 두 사람이 함께 있는 걸 보면, 메그가 그분을 어떻게 생각하는지 더 잘 판단할 수 있을 테니.\""]], [["\"She'll see his in those handsome eyes that she talks about, and then it will be all up with her.", "\"언니는 늘 입에 올리는 그 잘생긴 눈동자 속에서 그 사람 마음을 읽어 낼 거고, 그럼 언니는 끝장이에요."], ["She's got such a soft heart, it will melt like butter in the sun if any one looks sentimentally at her.", "언니는 마음이 어찌나 여린지, 누가 다정한 눈길로 쳐다보기만 해도 햇볕에 놓인 버터처럼 녹아 버릴걸요. 언니는 그 사람이 보낸 짧은 소식을 엄마 편지보다 더 열심히 읽었고, 제가 그 얘길 하니까 저를 꼬집었어요."], ["She read the short reports he sent more than she did your letters, and pinched me when I spoke of it, and likes brown eyes, and doesn't think John an ugly name, and she'll go and fall in love, and there's an end of peace and fun, and cosy times together.", "그리고 갈색 눈을 좋아하고, 존이라는 이름이 못생긴 이름은 아니라고 생각하죠. 그러니 언니는 그냥 사랑에 빠져 버릴 거고, 그러면 우리의 평화와 즐거움, 함께 보내던 아늑한 시간도 다 끝장이에요."], ["I see it all!", "다 보여요! 두 사람은 온 집 안을 사랑에 빠져 돌아다닐 거고, 우린 그걸 피해 다녀야 할걸요."], ["they'll go lovering around the house, and we shall have to dodge; Meg will be absorbed, and no good to me any more; Brooke will scratch up a fortune somehow, carry her off, and make a hole in the family; and I shall break my heart, and everything will be abominably uncomfortable.", "메그 언니는 그 사람한테 푹 빠져서 더는 저한테 아무 쓸모도 없어질 거예요. 브룩 선생님은 어떻게든 재산을 그러모아서 언니를 데려가 버릴 거고, 우리 식구에 구멍을 낼 거예요."], ["Oh, dear me!", "그럼 전 가슴이 찢어질 거고, 모든 게 끔찍하게 불편해질 거예요."], ["why weren't we all boys, then there wouldn't be any bother.\"", "아유, 정말! 우리가 다 남자로 태어났더라면 이런 골칫거리는 없었을 텐데.\""]], [["Jo leaned her chin on her knees, in a disconsolate attitude, and shook her fist at the reprehensible John.", "조는 낙담한 자세로 무릎에 턱을 괴고, 그 괘씸한 존을 향해 주먹을 흔들었다."], ["Mrs. March sighed, and Jo looked up with an air of relief.", "마치 부인이 한숨을 쉬자, 조가 안도하는 표정으로 고개를 들었다."]], [["\"You don't like it, mother?", "\"엄마도 이게 싫으신 거죠?"], ["I'm glad of it.", "정말 다행이에요."], ["Let's send him about his business, and not tell Meg a word of it, but all be happy together as we always have been.\"", "그 사람한테 제 갈 길 가라고 하고, 메그 언니한테는 한마디도 하지 말고, 우리끼리 늘 그래 왔듯이 다 같이 행복하게 지내요.\""]], [["\"I did wrong to sigh, Jo.", "\"내가 한숨을 쉰 건 잘못이구나, 조."], ["It is natural and right you should all go to homes of your own, in time; but I do want to keep my girls as long as I can; and I am sorry that this happened so soon, for Meg is only seventeen, and it will be some years before John can make a home for her.", "때가 되면 너희가 모두 저마다의 가정을 꾸려 떠나는 건 자연스럽고 옳은 일이야. 하지만 난 내 딸들을 되도록 오래 곁에 두고 싶구나—\""], ["Your father and I have agreed that she shall not bind herself in any way, nor be married, before twenty.", "있단다. 그리고 이 일이 너무 일찍 일어나서 안타깝구나."], ["If she and John love one another, they can wait, and test the love by doing so.", "메그는 이제 겨우 열일곱 살이고, 존이 메그에게 가정을 꾸려 줄 수 있으려면 몇 해는 더 있어야 할 테니까. 네 아버지와 나는 메그가 어떤 식으로든 자신을 매어 두지 않기로, 또 스무 살 전에는 결혼하지 않기로 합의했단다."], ["She is conscientious, and I have no fear of her treating him unkindly.", "메그와 존이 서로 사랑한다면 기다릴 수 있고, 그렇게 함으로써 그 사랑을 시험해 볼 수 있겠지. 그 애는 양심적인 아이라, 존을 매정하게 대할까 봐 걱정하지는 않아."], ["My pretty, tender-hearted girl!", "예쁘고 마음씨 고운 우리 딸!"], ["I hope things will go happily with her.\"", "그 애에게 모든 일이 행복하게 풀리기를 바란단다.\""]], [["\"Hadn't you rather have her marry a rich man?\"", "\"엄마는 메그가 부자랑 결혼하는 편이 더 낫다고 생각하시는 거 아니에요?\""], ["asked Jo, as her mother's voice faltered a little over the last words.", "어머니의 목소리가 마지막 말에서 살짝 떨리는 것을 보고 조가 물었다."]], [["\"Money is a good and useful thing, Jo; and I hope my girls will never feel the need of it too bitterly, nor be tempted by too much.", "\"돈은 좋고 쓸모 있는 거란다, 조. 우리 딸들이 돈이 없어서 너무 쓰라리게 아쉬워하는 일도 없고, 또 너무 많은 돈에 휘둘리는 일도 없기를 바라지."], ["I should like to know that John was firmly established in some good business, which gave him an income large enough to keep free from debt and make Meg comfortable.", "나는 존이 빚 없이 지내고 메그를 편안하게 해 줄 만큼 충분한 수입이 나오는 어떤 좋은 일자리에 든든히 자리 잡았다는 걸 알게 되면 좋겠어. 나는 우리 딸들이 굉장한 재산이나 화려한 지위, 대단한 명성을 누리기를 욕심내지는 않는단다."], ["I'm not ambitious for a splendid fortune, a fashionable position, or a great name for my girls.", "만약 사랑과 미덕에 더해 지위와 돈까지 따라온다면 고맙게 받아들이고 너희의 행운을 함께 기뻐하겠지."], ["If rank and money come with love and virtue, also, I should accept them gratefully, and enjoy your good fortune; but I know, by experience, how much genuine happiness can be had in a plain little house, where the daily bread is earned, and some privations give sweetness to the few pleasures.", "하지만 나는 경험으로 안단다. 날마다 양식을 벌어야 하고 약간의 궁핍이 몇 안 되는 즐거움에 더 큰 단맛을 주는, 그런 소박하고 작은 집에서도 얼마나 진정한 행복을 누릴 수 있는지를."], ["I am content to see Meg begin humbly, for, if I am not mistaken, she will be rich in the possession of a good man's heart, and that is better than a fortune.\"", "나는 메그가 조촐하게 시작하는 걸 봐도 만족스럽단다. 내 짐작이 틀리지 않다면 그 애는 좋은 사람의 마음을 차지하는 부자가 될 테고, 그건 재산보다 더 나은 것이니까.\""]], [["\"I understand, mother, and quite agree; but I'm disappointed about Meg, for I'd planned to have her marry Teddy by and by, and sit in the lap of luxury all her days.", "\"무슨 말씀인지 알겠어요, 엄마. 저도 전적으로 동의해요."], ["Wouldn't it be nice?\"", "하지만 메그 일은 좀 실망스러워요. 저는 나중에 메그를 테디랑 결혼시켜서 평생 호강하며 살게 하려고 계획을 세워 뒀거든요."], ["asked Jo, looking up, with a brighter face.", "그러면 멋지지 않을까요?\" 조가 한결 밝아진 얼굴로 고개를 들며 물었다."]], [["\"He is younger than she, you know,\"", "\"걔는 메그보다 어리잖니.\""], ["began Mrs. March; but Jo broke in,--", "마치 부인이 입을 열었지만, 조가 끼어들었다."]], [["\"Only a little; he's old for his age, and tall; and can be quite grown-up in his manners if he likes.", "\"조금밖에 안 어려요. 테디는 나이에 비해 어른스럽고 키도 크고, 마음만 먹으면 행동거지가 꽤 어른스러워질 수 있다고요."], ["Then he's rich and generous and good, and loves us all; and _I_ say it's a pity my plan is spoilt.\"", "게다가 부자에다 너그럽고 착하고, 우리 모두를 좋아하잖아요. 제 계획이 틀어진 게 정말 아쉽다니까요.\""]], [["\"I'm afraid Laurie is hardly grown up enough for Meg, and altogether too much of a weathercock, just now, for any one to depend on.", "\"로리는 메그한테는 아직 그다지 어른스럽지 못한 데다, 지금으로선 누가 기댈 만한 사람이라기엔 너무 변덕쟁이란다. 계획 같은 건 세우지 말렴, 조."], ["Don't make plans, Jo; but let time and their own hearts mate your friends.", "시간과 그 애들 자신의 마음이 네 친구들의 짝을 맺어 주게 두자꾸나."], ["We can't meddle safely in such matters, and had better not get 'romantic rubbish,'", "그런 일에는 우리가 함부로 끼어들 수 없으니, 네가 말하는 '낭만 어쩌고 하는 헛것'"], ["as you call it, into our heads, lest it spoil our friendship.\"", "같은 걸 머릿속에 넣어 두지 않는 게 좋겠다. 자칫하면 우정을 망칠 수도 있으니까.\""]], [["\"Well, I won't; but I hate to see things going all criss-cross and getting snarled up, when a pull here and a snip there would straighten it out.", "\"네, 안 그럴게요. 하지만 여기 한 번 잡아당기고 저기 한 번 싹둑 자르면 곧게 펼 수 있는데, 일이 죄다 비뚤어지고 엉켜 가는 걸 보는 건 정말 싫어요."], ["I wish wearing flat-irons on our heads would keep us from growing up.", "우리가 머리에 다리미라도 이고 있으면 자라지 않을 텐데."], ["But buds will be roses, and kittens, cats,--more's the pity!\"", "하지만 꽃봉오리는 장미가 되고 새끼 고양이는 고양이가 되겠죠. 더더욱 안타까운 일이라니까요!\""]], [["\"What's that about flat-irons and cats?\"", "\"다리미랑 고양이가 어쨌다는 거야?\""], ["asked Meg, as she crept into the room, with the finished letter in her hand.", "다 쓴 편지를 손에 들고 방으로 슬며시 들어오며 메그가 물었다."]], [["\"Only one of my stupid speeches.", "\"그냥 내가 한 멍청한 소리야."], ["I'm going to bed; come, Peggy,\"", "난 이제 자러 갈래. 가자, 페기.\""], ["said Jo, unfolding herself, like an animated puzzle.", "살아 움직이는 수수께끼 인형처럼 몸을 펴며 조가 말했다."]], [["\"Quite right, and beautifully written.", "\"아주 잘 썼구나, 글도 곱고."], ["Please add that I send my love to John,\" said Mrs.", "내가 존에게 사랑을 전한다고 한 줄 덧붙여 주렴.\""], ["March, as she glanced over the letter, and gave it back.", "편지를 훑어보고 돌려주며 마치 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Do you call him 'John'?\"", "\"엄마는 그분을 '존'이라고 부르세요?\""], ["asked Meg, smiling, with her innocent eyes looking down into her mother's.", "천진한 눈으로 어머니의 눈을 내려다보며 메그가 미소 띤 채 물었다."]], [["\"Yes; he has been like a son to us, and we are very fond of him,\"", "\"그래, 그 사람은 우리에게 아들이나 다름없었고, 우리 모두 그를 무척 아낀단다.\""], ["replied Mrs. March, returning the look with a keen one.", "마치 부인이 날카로운 눈빛으로 그 시선을 받으며 대답했다."]], [["\"I'm glad of that, he is so lonely.", "\"그렇다니 기뻐요, 그분은 너무 외로우니까요."], ["Good-night, mother, dear.", "안녕히 주무세요, 사랑하는 엄마."], ["It is so inexpressibly comfortable to have you here,\"", "엄마가 여기 계시니 말로 다 못 할 만큼 마음이 놓여요.\""], ["was Meg's quiet answer.", "메그가 조용히 대답했다."]], [["The kiss her mother gave her was a very tender one; and, as she went away, Mrs.", "어머니가 메그에게 해 준 입맞춤은 더없이 다정했다."], ["March said, with a mixture of satisfaction and regret, \"She does not love John yet, but will soon learn to.\"", "그리고 메그가 방을 나간 뒤, 마치 부인은 만족과 아쉬움이 뒤섞인 목소리로 말했다. \"저 애는 아직 존을 사랑하진 않지만, 머지않아 사랑하는 법을 배우게 되겠지.\""]], [["# LAURIE MAKES MISCHIEF, AND JO MAKES PEACE.", "# LAURIE MAKES MISCHIEF, AND JO MAKES PEACE."]], [["Jo's face was a study next day, for the secret rather weighed upon her, and she found it hard not to look mysterious and important.", "로리가 말썽을 부리고, 조가 화해를 시키다\n이튿날 조의 얼굴은 그야말로 볼만했다. 비밀이 어지간히 마음을 짓눌러서, 신비롭고 거창한 표정을 짓지 않으려고 무던히 애를 써야 했던 것이다."], ["Meg observed it, but did not trouble herself to make inquiries, for she had learned that the best way to manage Jo was by the law of contraries, so she felt sure of being told everything if she did not ask.", "메그도 그걸 눈치챘지만 굳이 캐물으려 들지는 않았다. 조를 다루는 가장 좋은 방법은 거꾸로 하는 것임을 익히 알고 있었기에, 묻지만 않으면 다 털어놓으리라고 확신했던 것이다."], ["She was rather surprised, therefore, when the silence remained unbroken, and Jo assumed a patronizing air, which decidedly aggravated Meg, who in her turn assumed an air of dignified reserve, and devoted herself to her mother.", "그래서 침묵이 깨지지 않은 채로 이어지고, 조가 사뭇 잘난 체하는 태도를 취하자 메그는 적잖이 놀랐다. 그 태도에 약이 오른 메그는 이번에는 자기대로 위엄 있게 새침한 태도를 취하며 어머니 곁에만 붙어 있었다."], ["This left Jo to her own devices; for Mrs.", "그 바람에 조는 제멋대로 지낼 수밖에 없었다."], ["March had taken her place as nurse, and bade her rest, exercise, and amuse herself after her long confinement.", "마치 부인이 간병인 자리를 대신 맡으면서, 오랫동안 갇혀 지냈으니 이제 쉬고 운동도 하고 즐겁게 보내라고 일렀기 때문이다. 에이미가 떠나고 없으니 조의 유일한 피난처는 로리뿐이었다."], ["Amy being gone, Laurie was her only refuge; and, much as she enjoyed his society, she rather dreaded him just then, for he was an incorrigible tease, and she feared he would coax her secret from her.", "그런데 그와 어울리는 게 즐겁기는 해도, 그 무렵 조는 오히려 로리가 두려웠다. 도무지 고쳐지지 않는 짓궂은 장난꾼인 그가 비밀을 슬쩍 꾀어내려 들 것 같았기 때문이다."]], [["She was quite right, for the mischief-loving lad no sooner suspected a mystery than he set himself to find it out, and led Jo a trying life of it.", "조의 짐작은 그대로 들어맞았다. 장난을 좋아하는 그 녀석은 뭔가 비밀이 있다는 낌새를 챘다 하면 곧장 그것을 캐내려 들었고, 그래서 조를 한껏 시달리게 했다."], ["He wheedled, bribed, ridiculed, threatened, and scolded; affected indifference, that he might surprise the truth from her; declared he knew, then that he didn't care; and, at last, by dint of perseverance, he satisfied himself that it concerned Meg and Mr.", "그는 살살 꾀기도 하고, 매수도 하고, 비웃기도 하고, 으르기도 하고, 나무라기도 했다. 진실을 불쑥 끌어내려고 짐짓 관심 없는 척도 했다."], ["Brooke.", "다 안다고 큰소리쳤다가, 다음엔 알 게 뭐냐는 듯 굴기도 했다."], ["Feeling indignant that he was not taken into his tutor's confidence, he set his wits to work to devise some proper retaliation for the slight.", "그러다 끝내 끈질긴 노력 덕분에, 그게 메그와 브룩 씨에 관한 일이라는 걸 스스로 확신하게 되었다. 자기 가정교사가 자신을 믿고 털어놓지 않았다는 데에 분이 난 로리는, 그 무시당한 일에 대해 그럴듯하게 앙갚음할 방법을 짜내려고 머리를 굴렸다."]], [["Meg meanwhile had apparently forgotten the matter, and was absorbed in preparations for her father's return; but all of a sudden a change seemed to come over her, and, for a day or two, she was quite unlike herself.", "그동안 메그는 그 일을 까맣게 잊은 듯, 아버지가 돌아올 준비에 푹 빠져 있었다. 그런데 별안간 무언가 변화가 찾아온 듯, 하루 이틀 동안 메그는 영 평소답지 않았다."], ["She started when spoken to, blushed when looked at, was very quiet, and sat over her sewing, with a timid, troubled look on her face.", "누가 말을 걸면 흠칫 놀라고, 누가 쳐다보면 얼굴을 붉혔으며, 무척 말이 없어지고, 겁먹은 듯 근심스러운 표정으로 바느질에만 매달려 있었다."], ["To her mother's inquiries she answered that she was quite well, and Jo's she silenced by begging to be let alone.", "어머니가 어디 아프냐고 물으면 아주 괜찮다고 대답했고, 조에게는 제발 좀 내버려 두라고 사정하며 입을 다물게 했다."]], [["\"She feels it in the air--love, I mean--and she's going very fast.", "\"메그는 그 기운을 공기로 느끼는 거예요. 사랑 말이에요."], ["She's got most of the symptoms,--is twittery and cross, doesn't eat, lies awake, and mopes in corners.", "그것도 아주 빠르게 빠져들고 있어요. 증상이란 증상은 거의 다 나타났다고요."], ["I caught her singing that song he gave her, and once she said 'John,'", "안절부절못하고 까칠해지고, 잘 먹지도 않고, 잠도 못 자고, 구석에서 시무룩하게 있고요. 그 사람이 준 노래를 흥얼거리는 걸 잡아낸 적도 있어요."], ["as you do, and then turned as red as a poppy.", "한번은 엄마처럼 '존'이라고 부르더니, 양귀비처럼 새빨개졌다니까요."], ["Whatever shall we do?\"", "대체 어쩌면 좋아요?\""], ["said Jo, looking ready for any measures, however violent.", "조가 아무리 과격한 수단이라도 마다하지 않을 기세로 말했다."]], [["\"Nothing but wait.", "\"기다리는 것밖에 없단다."], ["Let her alone, be kind and patient, and father's coming will settle everything,\"", "그냥 내버려 두고, 다정하고 끈기 있게 대하렴. 아버지가 오시면 모든 일이 정리될 거야.\""], ["replied her mother.", "어머니가 대답했다."]], [["\"Here's a note to you, Meg, all sealed up.", "\"메그 언니, 언니한테 온 쪽지야. 봉인까지 다 돼 있어."], ["How odd!", "이상하다!"], ["Teddy never seals mine,\"", "테디는 내 건 한 번도 봉한 적이 없는데.\""], ["said Jo, next day, as she distributed the contents of the little post-office.", "이튿날, 그 작은 우체국의 내용물을 나눠 주며 조가 말했다."]], [["Mrs. March and Jo were deep in their own affairs, when a sound from Meg made them look up to see her staring at her note, with a frightened face.", "마치 부인과 조가 저마다 제 일에 푹 빠져 있는데, 메그 쪽에서 무슨 소리가 나서 고개를 들어 보니, 메그가 겁에 질린 얼굴로 쪽지를 뚫어져라 보고 있었다."]], [["\"My child, what is it?\"", "\"얘야, 무슨 일이니?\""], ["cried her mother, running to her, while Jo tried to take the paper which had done the mischief.", "어머니가 메그에게 달려가며 외쳤고, 그사이 조는 일을 저지른 그 종이를 빼앗으려 했다."]], [["\"It's all a mistake--he didn't send it.", "\"전부 오해였어요. 그 사람은 이걸 보낸 적이 없대요."], ["O Jo, how could you do it?\"", "아, 조, 어떻게 이럴 수가 있어?\""], ["and Meg hid her face in her hands, crying as if her heart was quite broken.", "메그는 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고, 가슴이 완전히 찢어진 듯 울었다."]], [["\"Me!", "\"나라고!"], ["I've done nothing!", "난 아무것도 안 했어!"], ["What's she talking about?\"", "대체 무슨 소릴 하는 거야?\""], ["cried Jo, bewildered.", "영문을 모른 조가 외쳤다."]], [["Meg's mild eyes kindled with anger as she pulled a crumpled note from her pocket, and threw it at Jo, saying reproachfully,--", "메그의 순한 두 눈에 노여움이 타오르더니, 호주머니에서 구겨진 쪽지를 꺼내 조에게 던지며 원망스럽게 말했다."]], [["\"You wrote it, and that bad boy helped you.", "\"네가 쓰고, 그 못된 녀석이 거든 거잖아."], ["How could you be so rude, so mean, and cruel to us both?\"", "어쩜 우리 둘한테 이렇게 무례하고, 못되고, 잔인할 수가 있어?\""]], [["Jo hardly heard her, for she and her mother were reading the note, which was written in a peculiar hand.", "조는 그 말이 거의 귀에 들어오지 않았다. 어머니와 함께 그 쪽지를 읽고 있었기 때문인데, 거기엔 묘한 필체로 이렇게 적혀 있었다."]], [["\"I can no longer restrain my passion, and must know my fate before I return.", "\"내 가장 사랑하는 마거릿,\n더는 내 마음을 억누를 수가 없어, 돌아가기 전에 내 운명을 알아야겠소."], ["I dare not tell your parents yet, but I think they would consent if they knew that we adored one another.", "아직 당신 부모님께 말씀드릴 엄두는 나지 않지만, 우리가 서로 사모하고 있다는 걸 아시면 허락해 주시리라 생각하오."], ["Mr. Laurence will help me to some good place, and then, my sweet girl, you will make me happy.", "로런스 씨가 내게 좋은 자리를 마련해 주실 테니, 그러면, 사랑하는 그대여, 당신이 나를 행복하게 해 주오."], ["I implore you to say nothing to your family yet, but to send one word of hope through Laurie to \"Your devoted JOHN.\"", "부디 아직은 가족에게 아무 말도 말고, 로리를 통해 희망의 한 마디만 전해 주기를 간청하오."]], [["\"Oh, the little villain!", "당신에게 헌신하는 존이.\""], ["that's the way he meant to pay me for keeping my word to mother.", "\"아, 그 쪼끄만 악당! 엄마와 한 약속을 지킨 대가를 이런 식으로 갚으려고 했군."], ["I'll give him a hearty scolding, and bring him over to beg pardon,\"", "내가 단단히 혼쭐을 내서, 이리로 끌고 와 용서를 빌게 만들 거야.\""], ["cried Jo, burning to execute immediate justice.", "당장이라도 응징에 나설 기세로 조가 외쳤다."], ["But her mother held her back, saying, with a look she seldom wore,--", "그러나 어머니가 좀처럼 짓지 않는 표정으로 조를 붙들며 말했다."]], [["\"Stop, Jo, you must clear yourself first.", "\"멈추렴, 조. 먼저 네 결백부터 밝혀야지."], ["You have played so many pranks, that I am afraid you have had a hand in this.\"", "넌 워낙 장난을 많이 쳐 왔으니, 이번 일에도 네가 한몫 거든 게 아닐까 걱정스럽구나.\""]], [["\"On my word, mother, I haven't!", "\"맹세코, 엄마, 전 아니에요!"], ["I never saw that note before, and don't know anything about it, as true as I live!\"", "그 쪽지는 처음 봤고, 이 일에 대해 아무것도 몰라요, 정말이라니까요!\""], ["said Jo, so earnestly that they believed her.", "조가 어찌나 진지하게 말하던지 두 사람은 그 말을 믿었다."], ["\"If I _had_ taken a part in it I'd have done it better than this, and have written a sensible note.", "\"내가 정말 끼어들었다면 이것보다는 훨씬 잘 썼을 거고, 말이 되는 쪽지를 썼겠지."], ["I should think you'd have known Mr. Brooke wouldn't write such stuff as that,\"", "브룩 씨가 이런 시답잖은 글을 쓸 사람이 아니라는 건 진작 알았어야지.\""], ["she added, scornfully tossing down the paper.", "그러면서 조는 그 종이를 경멸하듯 내던졌다."]], [["\"It's like his writing,\"", "\"그분 글씨랑 비슷한데.\""], ["faltered Meg, comparing it with the note in her hand.", "메그가 손에 든 쪽지와 견주어 보며 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"O Meg, you didn't answer it?\"", "\"아, 메그, 답장한 건 아니지?\""], ["cried Mrs. March quickly.", "마치 부인이 다급히 외쳤다."]], [["\"Yes, I did!\"", "\"했어요!\""], ["and Meg hid her face again, overcome with shame.", "메그는 부끄러움에 못 이겨 다시 얼굴을 가렸다."]], [["\"Here's a scrape!", "\"이거 큰일 났네!"], ["_Do_ let me bring that wicked boy over to explain, and be lectured.", "제발 그 못된 녀석을 데려와 설명하게 하고 따끔하게 혼내게 해 줘요."], ["I can't rest till I get hold of him;\"", "그 녀석을 붙잡기 전엔 가만히 못 있겠어.\""], ["and Jo made for the door again.", "조가 다시 문 쪽으로 향했다."]], [["\"Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["let me manage this, for it is worse than I thought.", "이 일은 내가 처리하마. 생각보다 더 고약하구나."], ["Margaret, tell me the whole story,\" commanded Mrs.", "마거릿, 자초지종을 다 말해 보렴.\""], ["March, sitting down by Meg, yet keeping hold of Jo, lest she should fly off.", "마치 부인이 메그 곁에 앉으며 분부했다. 그러면서도 조가 날아가 버리기라도 할까 봐 조를 붙든 채였다."]], [["\"I received the first letter from Laurie, who didn't look as if he knew anything about it,\"", "\"첫 번째 편지는 로리한테서 받았어요. 걔는 이 일에 대해 아무것도 모르는 것 같았고요.\""], ["began Meg, without looking up.", "메그가 고개도 들지 못한 채 입을 열었다."], ["\"I was worried at first, and meant to tell you; then I remembered how you liked Mr.", "\"처음엔 걱정스러워서 엄마한테 말씀드리려고 했어요."], ["Brooke, so I thought you wouldn't mind if I kept my little secret for a few days.", "그러다 엄마가 브룩 씨를 좋게 보시는 게 떠올라서, 며칠쯤 제 작은 비밀로 간직해도 괜찮으시겠거니 했어요."], ["I'm so silly that I liked to think no one knew; and, while I was deciding what to say, I felt like the girls in books, who have such things to do.", "제가 어리석어서, 아무도 모른다고 생각하는 게 좋았던 거예요. 뭐라고 답할지 정하는 동안, 이런 일을 겪는 책 속 소녀들이 된 기분이었어요."], ["Forgive me, mother, I'm paid for my silliness now; I never can look him in the face again.\"", "용서해 주세요, 엄마, 어리석은 짓의 대가를 지금 치르고 있어요. 이제 다시는 그분 얼굴을 못 볼 거예요.\""]], [["\"What did you say to him?\"", "\"그 사람에게 뭐라고 했니?\""], ["asked Mrs. March.", "마치 부인이 물었다."]], [["\"I only said I was too young to do anything about it yet; that I didn't wish to have secrets from you, and he must speak to father.", "\"그냥 저는 아직 그런 일을 하기엔 너무 어리고, 엄마한테 비밀을 두고 싶지 않으니 아버지께 말씀드려야 한다고만 했어요."], ["I was very grateful for his kindness, and would be his friend, but nothing more, for a long while.\"", "친절히 대해 주셔서 정말 감사하고, 한동안은 친구로만 지내고 싶을 뿐 그 이상은 아니라고요.\""]], [["Mrs. March smiled, as if well pleased, and Jo clapped her hands, exclaiming, with a laugh,--", "마치 부인은 흡족하다는 듯 미소 지었고, 조는 손뼉을 치며 웃음 섞인 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["\"You are almost equal to Caroline Percy, who was a pattern of prudence!", "\"언니 거의 신중함의 본보기였던 캐럴라인 퍼시급이네! 더 말해 봐, 메그 언니."], ["Tell on, Meg. What did he say to that?\"", "그랬더니 그 사람이 뭐래?\""]], [["\"He writes in a different way entirely, telling me that he never sent any love-letter at all, and is very sorry that my roguish sister, Jo, should take such liberties with our names.", "\"완전히 다른 투로 답장이 왔어. 자긴 연애편지 같은 건 절대 보낸 적이 없고, 장난기 많은 우리 동생 조가 우리 이름을 그렇게 함부로 가져다 쓴 게 너무 안타깝다는 거야."], ["It's very kind and respectful, but think how dreadful for me!\"", "정말 친절하고 정중하긴 한데, 나로선 이게 얼마나 끔찍한 일인지 생각해 봐!\""]], [["Meg leaned against her mother, looking the image of despair, and Jo tramped about the room, calling Laurie names.", "메그는 절망 그 자체인 얼굴로 어머니에게 기댔고, 조는 로리를 욕하며 방 안을 쿵쿵 걸어 다녔다."], ["All of a sudden she stopped, caught up the two notes, and, after looking at them closely, said decidedly, \"I don't believe Brooke ever saw either of these letters.", "그러다 별안간 걸음을 멈추고는 두 쪽지를 집어 들어 찬찬히 살펴보더니 단호하게 말했다. \"브룩 씨는 이 두 편지를 다 본 적도 없을걸."], ["Teddy wrote both, and keeps yours to crow over me with, because I wouldn't tell him my secret.\"", "테디가 둘 다 쓴 거야. 그리고 내가 비밀을 안 알려 줬다고, 나한테 으스대려고 언니 답장을 가지고 있는 거라고.\""]], [["\"Don't have any secrets, Jo; tell it to mother, and keep out of trouble, as I should have done,\"", "\"비밀 같은 건 갖지 마, 조. 엄마한테 털어놓고 괜한 곤란에 빠지지 마."], ["said Meg warningly.", "나도 그랬어야 했어.\" 메그가 타이르듯 말했다."]], [["\"Bless you, child!", "\"걱정 마, 언니!"], ["Mother told me.\"", "엄마가 나한테 다 말씀해 주셨거든.\""]], [["\"That will do, Jo.", "\"그만하렴, 조."], ["I'll comfort Meg while you go and get Laurie.", "네가 가서 로리를 데려오는 동안 내가 메그를 다독이마."], ["I shall sift the matter to the bottom, and put a stop to such pranks at once.\"", "이 일을 바닥까지 샅샅이 가려내서, 이런 장난을 당장 끝낼 거야.\""]], [["Away ran Jo, and Mrs. March gently told Meg Mr.", "조가 달려 나가자, 마치 부인은 브룩 씨의 진짜 마음을 메그에게 살며시 일러 주었다."], ["Brooke's real feelings.", "\"자, 얘야, 네 마음은 어떠니?"], ["\"Now, dear, what are your own? Do you love him enough to wait till he can make a home for you, or will you keep yourself quite free for the present?\"", "그가 너를 위해 가정을 꾸릴 수 있을 때까지 기다릴 만큼 그를 사랑하니, 아니면 지금으로선 아무 데도 매이지 않고 자유롭게 지내고 싶니?\""]], [["\"I've been so scared and worried, I don't want to have anything to do with lovers for a long while,--perhaps never,\"", "\"너무 놀라고 마음을 졸여서, 한동안은, 아니 어쩌면 영영, 연인이니 뭐니 하는 것과는 엮이고 싶지 않아요.\""], ["answered Meg petulantly.", "메그가 토라진 듯 대답했다."], ["\"If John _doesn't_ know anything about this nonsense, don't tell him, and make Jo and Laurie hold their tongues.", "\"존이 이 어처구니없는 일에 대해 정말 아무것도 모른다면, 그분께는 말하지 마세요. 그리고 조랑 로리도 입 다물게 해 주세요."], ["I won't be deceived and plagued and made a fool of,--it's a shame!\"", "속고, 시달리고, 바보 취급당하는 건 싫어요. 정말 너무해요!\""]], [["Seeing that Meg's usually gentle temper was roused and her pride hurt by this mischievous joke, Mrs. March soothed her by promises of entire silence, and great discretion for the future.", "평소 온화하던 메그의 성미가 들끓고, 이 짓궂은 장난에 자존심이 상했음을 알아챈 마치 부인은, 철저히 비밀을 지키고 앞으로 매사 신중히 하겠노라 약속하며 메그를 달랬다."], ["The instant Laurie's step was heard in the hall, Meg fled into the study, and Mrs. March received the culprit alone.", "현관에서 로리의 발소리가 들리는 순간, 메그는 서재로 도망쳐 버렸고, 마치 부인은 혼자서 그 죄인을 맞이했다."], ["Jo had not told him why he was wanted, fearing he wouldn't come; but he knew the minute he saw Mrs. March's face, and stood twirling his hat, with a guilty air which convicted him at once.", "조는 로리가 안 올까 봐 왜 부르는지 말하지 않았지만, 로리는 마치 부인의 얼굴을 보는 순간 곧바로 알아챘고, 모자를 빙빙 돌리며 죄지은 기색으로 서 있었는데, 그 모습만으로도 이미 유죄였다."], ["Jo was dismissed, but chose to march up and down the hall like a sentinel, having some fear that the prisoner might bolt.", "조는 물러가라는 말을 들었지만, 죄수가 달아나기라도 할까 봐 다소 걱정스러워, 보초처럼 현관을 왔다 갔다 하기로 했다."], ["The sound of voices in the parlor rose and fell for half an hour; but what happened during that interview the girls never knew.", "응접실에서 들려오는 목소리가 반 시간 동안 높아졌다 낮아졌다 했지만, 그 면담 중에 무슨 일이 오갔는지는 자매들도 끝내 알지 못했다."]], [["When they were called in, Laurie was standing by their mother, with such a penitent face that Jo forgave him on the spot, but did not think it wise to betray the fact.", "자매들이 안으로 불려 들어갔을 때, 로리는 어머니 곁에 어찌나 뉘우치는 얼굴로 서 있었던지, 조는 그 자리에서 그를 용서해 버렸다. 다만 그 사실을 드러내는 게 현명하다고는 여기지 않았다."], ["Meg received his humble apology, and was much comforted by the assurance that Brooke knew nothing of the joke.", "메그는 그의 겸허한 사과를 받아들였고, 브룩이 그 장난에 대해 전혀 모른다는 다짐을 듣고 크게 마음이 놓였다."]], [["\"I'll never tell him to my dying day,--wild horses sha'n't drag it out of me; so you'll forgive me, Meg, and I'll do anything to show how out-and-out sorry I am,\" he added, looking very much ashamed of himself.", "\"죽는 날까지 절대 그한테 말 안 할게. 사나운 말들이 끌어당겨도 입 밖에 안 낼 거야. 그러니 날 용서해 줘, 메그. 내가 얼마나 철저히 미안해하는지 보여 줄 수만 있다면 뭐든 할게.\" 로리가 잔뜩 부끄러워하는 기색으로 덧붙였다."]], [["\"I'll try; but it was a very ungentlemanly thing to do.", "\"노력해 볼게. 하지만 정말이지 신사답지 못한 짓이었어."], ["I didn't think you could be so sly and malicious, Laurie,\"", "네가 그렇게 음흉하고 심술궂을 줄은 몰랐어, 로리.\""], ["replied Meg, trying to hide her maidenly confusion under a gravely reproachful air.", "메그가 처녀다운 당혹감을 짐짓 엄숙하게 나무라는 태도 뒤에 감추려 애쓰며 대답했다."]], [["\"It was altogether abominable, and I don't deserve to be spoken to for a month; but you will, though, won't you?\"", "\"정말이지 끔찍한 짓이었어. 한 달쯤은 나한테 말도 걸지 말아야 마땅하지만, 그래도 말은 걸어 줄 거지, 응?\""], ["and Laurie folded his hands together with such an imploring gesture, as he spoke in his irresistibly persuasive tone, that it was impossible to frown upon him, in spite of his scandalous behavior.", "로리가 거부할 수 없을 만큼 사람을 홀리는 어조로 말하며 두 손을 모아 어찌나 애원하는 시늉을 하던지, 그 망측한 짓에도 불구하고 도저히 그를 향해 눈살을 찌푸릴 수 없었다."], ["Meg pardoned him, and Mrs. March's grave face relaxed, in spite of her efforts to keep sober, when she heard him declare that he would atone for his sins by all sorts of penances, and abase himself like a worm before the injured damsel.", "메그는 그를 용서했고, 로리가 온갖 고행으로 자신의 죄를 속죄하고 상처 입은 아가씨 앞에서 벌레처럼 납작 엎드리겠노라고 선언하는 것을 듣자, 마치 부인도 짐짓 근엄함을 지키려 애썼지만 굳었던 표정이 그만 풀어지고 말았다."]], [["Jo stood aloof, meanwhile, trying to harden her heart against him, and succeeding only in primming up her face into an expression of entire disapprobation.", "그러는 동안 조는 한 발 물러나, 그에게 마음을 모질게 먹으려 애썼지만, 결국 얼굴을 새침하게 굳혀 완전한 못마땅함의 표정을 짓는 데만 성공할 뿐이었다."], ["Laurie looked at her once or twice, but, as she showed no sign of relenting, he felt injured, and turned his back on her till the others were done with him, when he made her a low bow, and walked off without a word.", "로리가 한두 번 그쪽을 쳐다봤지만, 조가 누그러질 기미를 도통 보이지 않자 서운한 마음이 들었던지, 다른 사람들과 이야기가 끝날 때까지 조에게 등을 돌리고 있다가, 마지막에 정중히 허리를 굽혀 인사하고는 한마디도 없이 가 버렸다."]], [["As soon as he had gone, she wished she had been more forgiving; and when Meg and her mother went upstairs, she felt lonely, and longed for Teddy.", "그가 가 버리자마자 조는 좀 더 너그럽게 굴걸 그랬다고 후회했다. 그리고 메그와 어머니가 위층으로 올라가자, 조는 외로워졌고 테디가 그리워졌다."], ["After resisting for some time, she yielded to the impulse, and, armed with a book to return, went over to the big house.", "한참 마음을 다잡아 보다가 결국 충동에 굴복한 조는, 돌려줄 책 한 권을 들고 큰 집으로 건너갔다."]], [["\"Is Mr. Laurence in?\"", "\"로런스 씨 계세요?\""], ["asked Jo, of a housemaid, who was coming down stairs.", "계단을 내려오는 하녀에게 조가 물었다."]], [["\"Yes, miss; but I don't believe he's seeable just yet.\"", "\"네, 아가씨. 하지만 지금은 뵙기 어려우실 것 같은데요.\""]], [["\"Why not?", "\"왜요?"], ["is he ill?\"", "편찮으세요?\""]], [["\"La, no, miss, but he's had a scene with Mr.", "\"아유, 아니에요, 아가씨."], ["Laurie, who is in one of his tantrums about something, which vexes the old gentleman, so I dursn't go nigh him.\"", "그런데 로리 도련님이 무슨 일로 잔뜩 심통이 나서 한바탕 소동을 벌이는 바람에 어르신이 단단히 언짢으셔서요. 그래서 저는 곁에 얼씬도 못 하고 있어요.\""]], [["\"Where is Laurie?\"", "\"로리는 어디 있는데요?\""]], [["\"Shut up in his room, and he won't answer, though I've been a-tapping.", "\"방에 틀어박혀서는, 제가 아무리 두드려도 대답을 안 하세요."], ["I don't know what's to become of the dinner, for it's ready, and there's no one to eat it.\"", "저녁이 다 차려졌는데 먹을 사람이 없으니, 이걸 어쩌면 좋을지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["\"I'll go and see what the matter is.", "\"제가 가서 무슨 일인지 볼게요."], ["I'm not afraid of either of them.\"", "저는 두 분 다 안 무서우니까요.\""]], [["Up went Jo, and knocked smartly on the door of Laurie's little study.", "조는 위층으로 올라가 로리의 작은 서재 문을 똑똑 세게 두드렸다."]], [["\"Stop that, or I'll open the door and make you!\"", "\"그만두지 못해, 안 그러면 문을 열고 가만 안 둘 테니까!\""], ["called out the young gentleman, in a threatening tone.", "그 도련님이 으름장 놓는 투로 소리쳤다."]], [["Jo immediately knocked again; the door flew open, and in she bounced, before Laurie could recover from his surprise.", "조는 곧바로 다시 두드렸다. 문이 벌컥 열렸고, 로리가 놀라 정신을 차리기도 전에 조가 안으로 뛰어들었다."], ["Seeing that he really _was_ out of temper, Jo, who knew how to manage him, assumed a contrite expression, and going artistically down upon her knees, said meekly, \"Please forgive me for being so cross.", "그가 정말로 화가 나 있다는 걸 본 조는, 그를 다루는 법을 잘 아는지라, 뉘우치는 표정을 지으며 멋들어지게 무릎을 꿇고는 다소곳이 말했다. \"내가 너무 못되게 군 거 용서해 줘."], ["I came to make it up, and can't go away till I have.\"", "화해하러 왔는데, 화해 전엔 못 가.\""]], [["\"It's all right.", "\"됐어."], ["Get up, and don't be a goose, Jo,\"", "일어나, 바보같이 굴지 마, 조.\""], ["was the cavalier reply to her petition.", "그녀의 청에 돌아온 건 무뚝뚝한 대답이었다."]], [["\"Thank you; I will.", "\"고마워, 일어날게."], ["Could I ask what's the matter?", "무슨 일인지 물어봐도 돼?"], ["You don't look exactly easy in your mind.\"", "마음이 영 편치 않아 보여서.\""]], [["\"I've been shaken, and I won't bear it!\"", "\"누가 날 마구 흔들어 댔어, 난 그런 거 못 참아!\""], ["growled Laurie indignantly.", "로리가 분에 차 으르렁거렸다."]], [["\"Who did it?\"", "\"누가?\""], ["demanded Jo.", "조가 다그쳤다."]], [["\"Grandfather; if it had been any one else I'd have--\"", "\"할아버지가. 다른 사람이었으면 내가 가만……\""], ["and the injured youth finished his sentence by an energetic gesture of the right arm.", "분이 난 그 청년은 오른팔을 힘차게 휘두르는 동작으로 말을 맺었다."]], [["\"That's nothing; I often shake you, and you don't mind,\"", "\"그게 뭐 대수라고. 나도 너 자주 흔들어 대는데, 넌 신경도 안 쓰잖아.\""], ["said Jo soothingly.", "조가 달래듯 말했다."]], [["\"Pooh!", "\"피!"], ["you're a girl, and it's fun; but I'll allow no man to shake _me_.\"", "넌 여자애니까 그건 장난이지. 하지만 남자가 날 흔드는 건 절대 못 참아.\""]], [["\"I don't think any one would care to try it, if you looked as much like a thunder-cloud as you do now.", "\"지금처럼 먹구름 같은 얼굴을 하고 있으면 아무도 너 흔들 엄두는 안 낼 것 같은데."], ["Why were you treated so?\"", "왜 그런 대접을 받은 거야?\""]], [["\"Just because I wouldn't say what your mother wanted me for.", "\"그냥 너희 어머니가 날 왜 부르셨는지 말하지 않았다고 그런 거야."], ["I'd promised not to tell, and of course I wasn't going to break my word.\"", "말 안 하겠다고 약속했으니, 당연히 약속을 깰 순 없었지.\""]], [["\"Couldn't you satisfy your grandpa in any other way?\"", "\"다른 방법으로 할아버지를 납득시킬 순 없었어?\""]], [["\"No; he _would_ have the truth, the whole truth, and nothing but the truth.", "\"없었어. 할아버지는 진실을, 오로지 진실만을, 남김없이 다 들으려 하셨거든."], ["I'd have told my part of the scrape, if I could without bringing Meg in.", "메그를 끌어들이지 않고 말할 수 있었다면, 내 쪽 사정은 얘기했을 거야."], ["As I couldn't, I held my tongue, and bore the scolding till the old gentleman collared me.", "근데 그럴 수가 없으니 입을 다물고 야단을 견뎠지. 그러다 할아버지가 내 멱살을 잡으시는 거야."], ["Then I got angry, and bolted, for fear I should forget myself.\"", "그때 화가 치밀어서, 나도 모르게 무슨 짓을 할까 봐 뛰쳐나와 버렸어.\""]], [["\"It wasn't nice, but he's sorry, I know; so go down and make up.", "\"잘한 일은 아니지만, 할아버지도 후회하고 계실 거야, 분명히. 그러니까 내려가서 화해해."], ["I'll help you.\"", "내가 도와줄게.\""]], [["\"Hanged if I do!", "\"내가 그러면 사람이 아니지!"], ["I'm not going to be lectured and pummelled by every one, just for a bit of a frolic.", "그깟 장난 좀 쳤다고 아무한테나 잔소리 듣고 두들겨 맞을 순 없어."], ["I _was_ sorry about Meg, and begged pardon like a man; but I won't do it again, when I wasn't in the wrong.\"", "메그 일은 정말 미안해서 사내답게 용서를 빌었어. 하지만 잘못한 것도 없는데 또 그러진 않을 거야.\""]], [["\"He didn't know that.\"", "\"할아버지는 그걸 모르셨잖아.\""]], [["\"He ought to trust me, and not act as if I was a baby.", "\"날 믿어 주셔야지, 아기 다루듯 하면 안 되는 거잖아."], ["It's no use, Jo; he's got to learn that I'm able to take care of myself, and don't need any one's apron-string to hold on by.\"", "소용없어, 조. 할아버지는 내가 내 한 몸 건사할 수 있고, 누구 치맛자락 붙잡고 매달릴 필요 없다는 걸 아셔야 해.\""]], [["\"What pepper-pots you are!\" sighed Jo.", "\"너희 정말 둘 다 고추처럼 맵구나!\""], ["\"How do you mean to settle this affair?\"", "조가 한숨을 쉬었다. \"이 일을 어떻게 마무리할 생각인데?\""]], [["\"Well, he ought to beg pardon, and believe me when I say I can't tell him what the fuss's about.\"", "\"음, 할아버지가 먼저 사과하셔야지. 그리고 이 소동이 뭣 때문인지 말씀드릴 수 없다고 할 때 그 말을 믿어 주셔야 하고.\""]], [["\"Bless you!", "\"세상에!"], ["he won't do that.\"", "할아버지가 그러실 리 없잖아.\""]], [["\"I won't go down till he does.\"", "\"그럼 그러실 때까진 안 내려가.\""]], [["\"Now, Teddy, be sensible; let it pass, and I'll explain what I can.", "\"이봐, 테디, 좀 분별 있게 굴어. 그냥 넘겨, 내가 말할 수 있는 데까진 설명해 줄게."], ["You can't stay here, so what's the use of being melodramatic?\"", "넌 여기 계속 있을 순 없잖아, 그러니 신파극처럼 굴어 봐야 무슨 소용이야?\""]], [["\"I don't intend to stay here long, any way.", "\"어차피 여기 오래 있을 생각도 없어."], ["I'll slip off and take a journey somewhere, and when grandpa misses me he'll come round fast enough.\"", "슬쩍 빠져나가 어디로든 여행이나 떠날 거야. 그럼 할아버지가 날 찾다가 금방 누그러지시겠지.\""]], [["\"I dare say; but you ought not to go and worry him.\"", "\"그럴지도 모르지. 하지만 떠나서 할아버지 속을 태우면 안 돼.\""]], [["\"Don't preach.", "\"설교는 그만해. 워싱턴에 가서 브룩을 만날 거야."], ["I'll go to Washington and see Brooke; it's gay there, and I'll enjoy myself after the troubles.\"", "거긴 신나잖아. 이 골치 아픈 일들도 다 겪었으니, 좀 즐겨야지.\""]], [["\"What fun you'd have!", "\"얼마나 재미있을까!"], ["I wish I could run off too,\"", "나도 같이 달아날 수 있으면 좋겠다.\""], ["said Jo, forgetting her part of Mentor in lively visions of martial life at the capital.", "조가 멘토 역할은 까맣게 잊은 채, 수도의 군대 생활을 생생히 그려 보며 말했다."]], [["\"Come on, then!", "\"그럼 같이 가자!"], ["Why not?", "왜 안 돼?"], ["You go and surprise your father, and I'll stir up old Brooke.", "넌 가서 아버지를 깜짝 놀라게 해 드리고, 난 브룩 영감을 흔들어 깨우는 거지."], ["It would be a glorious joke; let's do it, Jo.", "정말 멋진 장난이 될 거야. 하자, 조."], ["We'll leave a letter saying we are all right, and trot off at once.", "우린 잘 있다는 편지 한 통 남기고 당장 떠나는 거야."], ["I've got money enough; it will do you good, and be no harm, as you go to your father.\"", "돈은 내가 충분히 있어. 너한테도 좋을 거고, 아버지한테 가는 거니까 나쁠 것도 없잖아.\""]], [["For a moment Jo looked as if she would agree; for, wild as the plan was, it just suited her.", "한순간 조는 그러자고 할 듯한 표정이었다. 무모한 계획이긴 해도, 딱 조의 마음에 들었던 것이다."], ["She was tired of care and confinement, longed for change, and thoughts of her father blended temptingly with the novel charms of camps and hospitals, liberty and fun.", "조는 보살핌과 갇힌 생활에 진력이 났고 변화를 갈망하던 차였다. 게다가 아버지 생각이, 야영지와 병원, 자유와 즐거움이라는 새로운 매력과 솔깃하게 뒤섞였다."], ["Her eyes kindled as they turned wistfully toward the window, but they fell on the old house opposite, and she shook her head with sorrowful decision.", "창문 쪽으로 아련히 향하던 조의 두 눈이 반짝였지만, 그 시선이 맞은편 낡은 집에 가닿자, 조는 서글픈 결심으로 고개를 저었다."]], [["\"If I was a boy, we'd run away together, and have a capital time; but as I'm a miserable girl, I must be proper, and stop at home.", "\"내가 남자애였다면 같이 달아나서 신나게 보냈을 거야. 하지만 난 처량한 여자애니까, 얌전히 굴고 집에 붙어 있어야지."], ["Don't tempt me, Teddy, it's a crazy plan.\"", "날 부추기지 마, 테디, 그건 미친 계획이야.\""]], [["\"That's the fun of it,\"", "\"그게 바로 재미있는 거지.\""], ["began Laurie, who had got a wilful fit on him, and was possessed to break out of bounds in some way.", "고집이 발동한 데다 어떻게든 울타리를 박차고 나가고 싶어 안달이 난 로리가 입을 열었다."]], [["\"Hold your tongue!\"", "\"입 다물어!\""], ["cried Jo, covering her ears.", "조가 두 귀를 막으며 외쳤다."], ["\"'Prunes and prisms'", "\"'얌전하고 새침하게'"], ["are my doom, and I may as well make up my mind to it.", "사는 게 내 운명이야, 그러니 그냥 마음을 정하는 편이 낫겠지."], ["I came here to moralize, not to hear about things that make me skip to think of.\"", "난 여기 교훈을 늘어놓으러 온 거지, 생각만 해도 가슴이 뛰는 그런 얘기나 들으러 온 게 아니라고.\""]], [["\"I know Meg would wet-blanket such a proposal, but I thought you had more spirit,\"", "\"메그라면 그런 제안에 찬물을 끼얹을 줄 알았지만, 넌 좀 더 배짱이 있는 줄 알았는데.\""], ["began Laurie insinuatingly.", "로리가 슬쩍 부추기듯 운을 뗐다."]], [["\"Bad boy, be quiet!", "\"못된 녀석, 조용히 좀 해!"], ["Sit down and think of your own sins, don't go making me add to mine.", "앉아서 네 죄나 반성해, 내 죄까지 보태게 만들지 말고."], ["If I get your grandpa to apologize for the shaking, will you give up running away?\"", "내가 너희 할아버지한테 흔든 일 사과하시게 만들면, 달아나려는 거 그만둘래?\""], ["asked Jo seriously.", "조가 진지하게 물었다."]], [["\"Yes, but you won't do it,\"", "\"그래, 하지만 넌 못 할걸.\""], ["answered Laurie, who wished \"to make up,\"", "로리가 대답했다. 사실 그도 '화해하고'"], ["but felt that his outraged dignity must be appeased first.", "싶었지만, 짓밟힌 자존심부터 달래야 한다고 느꼈던 것이다."]], [["\"If I can manage the young one I can the old one,\"", "\"어린 녀석을 다룰 수 있으면, 늙은 어른도 다룰 수 있지.\""], ["muttered Jo, as she walked away, leaving Laurie bent over a railroad map, with his head propped up on both hands.", "조는 중얼거리며 걸어 나갔고, 로리는 두 손으로 머리를 괸 채 철도 지도 위로 몸을 숙이고 있었다."]], [["\"Come in!\"", "\"들어오게!\""], ["and Mr. Laurence's gruff voice sounded gruffer than ever, as Jo tapped at his door.", "조가 문을 똑똑 두드리자, 로런스 씨의 퉁명스러운 목소리가 어느 때보다 퉁명스럽게 울렸다."]], [["\"It's only me, sir, come to return a book,\"", "\"저예요, 어르신. 책 돌려드리러 왔어요.\""], ["she said blandly, as she entered.", "조가 들어서며 상냥하게 말했다."]], [["\"Want any more?\"", "\"더 볼 텐가?\""], ["asked the old gentleman, looking grim and vexed, but trying not to show it.", "어르신이 험상궂고 언짢은 얼굴로, 그러나 그걸 안 드러내려 애쓰며 물었다."]], [["\"Yes, please.", "\"네, 그러고 싶어요."], ["I like old Sam so well, I think I'll try the second volume,\"", "새뮤얼 영감 이야기가 어찌나 마음에 들던지, 두 번째 권도 읽어 보려고요.\" 조가 대답했다."], ["returned Jo, hoping to propitiate him by accepting a second dose of Boswell's \"Johnson,\" as he had recommended that lively work.", "로런스 씨가 권한 그 활기찬 책, 보즈웰이 쓴 『존슨전』을 두 번째로 받아 들임으로써 그의 기분을 풀어 주려는 속셈이었다."]], [["The shaggy eyebrows unbent a little, as he rolled the steps toward the shelf where the Johnsonian literature was placed.", "덥수룩한 눈썹이 조금 펴지면서, 그가 존슨 관련 서적이 꽂힌 서가 쪽으로 사다리를 굴려 갔다. 조는 폴짝 올라가 맨 위 계단에 걸터앉아, 제 책을 찾는 척하면서도, 사실은 이 방문의 위험천만한 목적을 어떻게 꺼내야 가장 좋을지 궁리하고 있었다."], ["Jo skipped up, and, sitting on the top step, affected to be searching for her book, but was really wondering how best to introduce the dangerous object of her visit. Mr.", "로런스 씨는 조의 머릿속에서 뭔가 꿍꿍이가 무르익고 있음을 눈치챈 듯했다."], ["Laurence seemed to suspect that something was brewing in her mind; for, after taking several brisk turns about the room, he faced round on her, speaking so abruptly that \"Rasselas\" tumbled face downward on the floor.", "방 안을 몇 차례 성큼성큼 거닐더니, 조를 홱 돌아보며 어찌나 불쑥 말을 꺼내던지, 『라셀라스』가 바닥에 엎어지며 나뒹굴 정도였다."]], [["\"What has that boy been about?", "\"그 녀석이 대체 무슨 짓을 한 거냐?"], ["Don't try to shield him.", "감쌀 생각 말고."], ["I know he has been in mischief by the way he acted when he came home.", "집에 돌아왔을 때 행동거지를 보니, 무슨 못된 짓을 저지른 게 분명해."], ["I can't get a word from him; and when I threatened to shake the truth out of him he bolted upstairs, and locked himself into his room.\"", "그 녀석한테선 한마디도 들을 수가 없어. 진실을 흔들어서라도 끄집어내겠다고 으름장을 놨더니, 위층으로 뛰어 올라가 제 방에 틀어박혀 버렸다.\""]], [["\"He did do wrong, but we forgave him, and all promised not to say a word to any one,\"", "\"잘못을 하긴 했어요. 하지만 저희가 용서했고, 다들 누구에게도 한마디 안 하기로 약속했거든요.\""], ["began Jo reluctantly.", "조가 마지못해 입을 뗐다."]], [["\"That won't do; he shall not shelter himself behind a promise from you soft-hearted girls.", "\"그건 안 될 말이지. 그 녀석이 너희 마음 여린 처녀들이 한 약속 뒤에 숨게 둘 순 없어."], ["If he's done anything amiss, he shall confess, beg pardon, and be punished.", "잘못한 게 있다면, 고백하고, 용서를 빌고, 벌을 받아야지. 어서 말해 보거라, 조."], ["Out with it, Jo, I won't be kept in the dark.\"", "난 어둠 속에 갇혀 있진 않을 테니.\""]], [["Mr.", "로런스 씨가 어찌나 무섭게 보이고 또 날카롭게 말하던지, 조는 할 수만 있다면 기꺼이 달아났을 것이다."], ["Laurence looked so alarming and spoke so sharply that Jo would have gladly run away, if she could, but she was perched aloft on the steps, and he stood at the foot, a lion in the path, so she had to stay and brave it out.", "하지만 조는 사다리 높은 곳에 올라앉아 있었고, 그는 길목을 지키는 사자처럼 사다리 발치에 버티고 서 있었으니, 조는 남아서 정면으로 맞서는 수밖에 없었다."]], [["\"Indeed, sir, I cannot tell; mother forbade it.", "\"정말이지, 어르신, 말씀드릴 수가 없어요."], ["Laurie has confessed, asked pardon, and been punished quite enough.", "엄마가 그러지 말라고 하셨거든요. 로리는 고백도 하고, 용서도 빌었고, 벌도 충분히 받았어요."], ["We don't keep silence to shield him, but some one else, and it will make more trouble if you interfere.", "저희가 입을 다무는 건 로리를 감싸려는 게 아니라 다른 사람 때문이에요. 어르신이 끼어드시면 일이 더 커질 거예요."], ["Please don't; it was partly my fault, but it's all right now; so let's forget it, and talk about the 'Rambler,'", "부디 그러지 마세요. 일부는 제 잘못이기도 했지만, 이제 다 잘 풀렸으니까요."], ["or something pleasant.\"", "그러니 이 일은 잊고, 『램블러』 같은 거나, 뭔가 즐거운 얘기나 해요.\""]], [["\"Hang the 'Rambler!'", "\"『램블러』는 집어치워!"], ["come down and give me your word that this harum-scarum boy of mine hasn't done anything ungrateful or impertinent.", "내려와서 내게 약속하거라. 그 덜렁대는 내 손자 녀석이 배은망덕하거나 무례한 짓은 하지 않았다고 말이다."], ["If he has, after all your kindness to him, I'll thrash him with my own hands.\"", "그 모든 친절을 베풀어 줬는데도 그런 짓을 했다면, 내 이 손으로 직접 그놈을 매질할 테니.\""]], [["The threat sounded awful, but did not alarm Jo, for she knew the irascible old gentleman would never lift a finger against his grandson, whatever he might say to the contrary.", "그 으름장은 무시무시하게 들렸지만 조를 겁먹게 하진 못했다. 성마른 이 어르신이 말로는 무어라 하든, 결코 손자에게 손가락 하나 까딱하지 않으리라는 걸 조는 알고 있었으니까."], ["She obediently descended, and made as light of the prank as she could without betraying Meg or forgetting the truth.", "조는 순순히 내려와, 메그를 들춰내지도 진실을 저버리지도 않는 선에서 최대한 그 장난을 대수롭지 않은 일로 둘러댔다."]], [["\"Hum--ha--well, if the boy held his tongue because he promised, and not from obstinacy, I'll forgive him.", "\"흠……허……그래, 그 녀석이 고집 때문이 아니라 약속을 지키느라 입을 다문 거라면, 용서하마."], ["He's a stubborn fellow, and hard to manage,\" said Mr.", "워낙 고집불통이라 다루기 힘든 녀석이지.\""], ["Laurence, rubbing up his hair till it looked as if he had been out in a gale, and smoothing the frown from his brow with an air of relief.", "로런스 씨가 머리카락을 어찌나 비벼 대던지 폭풍 속에라도 다녀온 듯했고, 안도하는 기색으로 이마의 주름을 펴며 말했다."]], [["\"So am I; but a kind word will govern me when all the king's horses and all the king's men couldn't,\"", "\"저도 그래요. 하지만 다정한 말 한마디면 임금님의 말과 임금님의 신하가 다 나서도 못 하는 걸 해낼 수 있죠.\""], ["said Jo, trying to say a kind word for her friend, who seemed to get out of one scrape only to fall into another.", "조가 친구를 위해 좋은 말 한마디 거들려고 애쓰며 말했다. 하나의 곤경에서 빠져나오나 싶으면 또 다른 곤경에 빠지는 친구였다."]], [["\"You think I'm not kind to him, hey?\"", "\"내가 그 녀석한테 다정하지 않다고 생각하는 게냐, 응?\""], ["was the sharp answer.", "날카로운 대꾸가 돌아왔다."]], [["\"Oh, dear, no, sir; you are rather too kind sometimes, and then just a trifle hasty when he tries your patience.", "\"아유, 아니에요, 어르신. 어르신은 가끔 너무 다정하실 때도 있는걸요."], ["Don't you think you are?\"", "그러다 그 애가 인내심을 시험하면, 아주 조금 성급해지시는 거죠. 안 그러세요?\""]], [["Jo was determined to have it out now, and tried to look quite placid, though she quaked a little after her bold speech.", "조는 이번엔 끝장을 보겠다고 작정하고, 그 대담한 말을 내뱉고서 속으로는 조금 떨면서도 짐짓 아주 태연한 척했다."], ["To her great relief and surprise, the old gentleman only threw his spectacles on to the table with a rattle, and exclaimed frankly,--", "그런데 어르신은, 조의 크나큰 안도와 놀라움 속에, 안경을 탁자 위에 달그락 내던지며 솔직하게 외칠 뿐이었다."]], [["\"You're right, girl, I am!", "\"네 말이 맞다, 얘야, 그래!"], ["I love the boy, but he tries my patience past bearing, and I don't know how it will end, if we go on so.\"", "난 그 녀석을 사랑하지만, 그놈은 참다 참다 못 견딜 만큼 내 인내심을 시험하지. 이대로 계속 가다간 어떻게 끝날지 나도 모르겠구나.\""]], [["\"I'll tell you, he'll run away.\"", "\"제가 말씀드릴게요. 로리는 달아날 거예요.\""], ["Jo was sorry for that speech the minute it was made; she meant to warn him that Laurie would not bear much restraint, and hoped he would be more forbearing with the lad.", "조는 그 말을 내뱉는 순간 후회했다. 로리가 지나친 속박은 견디지 못한다는 걸 경고해서, 어르신이 그 애에게 좀 더 너그러워지기를 바랐을 뿐이었는데."]], [["Mr. Laurence's ruddy face changed suddenly, and he sat down, with a troubled glance at the picture of a handsome man, which hung over his table.", "로런스 씨의 불그레한 얼굴이 갑자기 변하더니, 그는 자리에 앉아 탁자 위에 걸린 잘생긴 남자의 초상화를 근심스러운 눈길로 바라보았다."], ["It was Laurie's father, who _had_ run away in his youth, and married against the imperious old man's will.", "그건 로리의 아버지였는데, 그는 젊은 시절 정말로 집을 뛰쳐나가, 위압적인 노인의 뜻을 거슬러 결혼했던 것이다."], ["Jo fancied he remembered and regretted the past, and she wished she had held her tongue.", "조는 어르신이 지난 일을 떠올리며 후회하는 거라고 짐작했고, 괜한 말을 했다고 후회했다."]], [["\"He won't do it unless he is very much worried, and only threatens it sometimes, when he gets tired of studying.", "\"로리는 어지간히 속이 상하지 않는 한 그러진 않을 거예요. 공부에 지칠 때나 가끔 으름장처럼 그러는 거죠."], ["I often think I should like to, especially since my hair was cut; so, if you ever miss us, you may advertise for two boys, and look among the ships bound for India.\"", "저도 그러고 싶다는 생각을 자주 해요, 특히 머리를 자른 뒤로요. 그러니 저희가 사라지면, 사내아이 둘을 찾는다고 광고를 내시고, 인도로 가는 배들 사이를 뒤져 보시면 될 거예요.\""]], [["She laughed as she spoke, and Mr.", "조는 말하면서 웃었고, 로런스 씨도 안도하는 기색이었다."], ["Laurence looked relieved, evidently taking the whole as a joke.", "이 모든 걸 농담으로 받아들인 게 분명했다."]], [["\"You hussy, how dare you talk in that way?", "\"이런 발칙한 것, 어디서 감히 그런 식으로 말하느냐?"], ["Where's your respect for me, and your proper bringing up?", "나에 대한 존경심이며, 제대로 받은 교육은 다 어디 갔어?"], ["Bless the boys and girls!", "사내아이들이고 계집아이들이고 다 어쩌면 좋을꼬!"], ["What torments they are; yet we can't do without them,\"", "어찌나 사람 애를 먹이는지. 그래도 너희가 없으면 우린 못 살지.\""], ["he said, pinching her cheeks good-humoredly.", "어르신이 다정하게 조의 두 볼을 꼬집으며 말했다."], ["\"Go and bring that boy down to his dinner, tell him it's all right, and advise him not to put on tragedy airs with his grandfather.", "\"가서 그 녀석을 저녁 먹으러 데려오너라. 다 괜찮다고 전하고, 제 할아비 앞에서 비극 배우처럼 처량한 척하지 말라고 일러라."], ["I won't bear it.\"", "그런 꼴은 못 봐주겠으니.\""]], [["\"He won't come, sir; he feels badly because you didn't believe him when he said he couldn't tell.", "\"안 올 거예요, 어르신. 자기가 말할 수 없다고 했을 때 어르신이 안 믿어 주셔서 속상해하고 있거든요."], ["I think the shaking hurt his feelings very much.\"", "흔드신 게 그 애 마음을 단단히 상하게 한 것 같아요.\""]], [["Jo tried to look pathetic, but must have failed, for Mr.", "조가 애처로운 표정을 지어 보이려 했지만 실패한 게 분명했다."], ["Laurence began to laugh, and she knew the day was won.", "로런스 씨가 웃기 시작했고, 조는 오늘의 승리를 거뒀음을 알았다."]], [["\"I'm sorry for that, and ought to thank him for not shaking _me_, I suppose.", "\"그거 안됐구나. 그 녀석이 날 안 흔들어 준 걸 고마워해야 할 판이로군."], ["What the dickens does the fellow expect?\"", "대체 그놈은 뭘 바라는 게야?\""], ["and the old gentleman looked a trifle ashamed of his own testiness.", "어르신은 자신의 성마름이 조금은 부끄러운 기색이었다."]], [["\"If I were you, I'd write him an apology, sir.", "\"제가 어르신이라면, 사과 편지를 써 보내겠어요."], ["He says he won't come down till he has one, and talks about Washington, and goes on in an absurd way.", "그 애는 사과를 받기 전엔 안 내려온다고 하고, 워싱턴이 어쩌고 하면서 어처구니없게 굴고 있거든요."], ["A formal apology will make him see how foolish he is, and bring him down quite amiable.", "격식 차린 사과 편지를 받으면 자기가 얼마나 어리석은지 깨닫고, 아주 고분고분하게 내려올 거예요. 한번 해 보세요."], ["Try it; he likes fun, and this way is better than talking.", "그 앤 장난을 좋아하니까, 이 방법이 말로 하는 것보다 나아요."], ["I'll carry it up, and teach him his duty.\"", "제가 위층으로 가져가서 그 애한테 도리를 가르치고 올게요.\""]], [["Mr.", "로런스 씨는 조를 날카롭게 한번 쳐다보고는 안경을 쓰며 천천히 말했다."], ["Laurence gave her a sharp look, and put on his spectacles, saying slowly, \"You're a sly puss, but I don't mind being managed by you and Beth.", "\"넌 참 약은 고양이로구나. 하지만 너랑 베스한테라면 휘둘려도 싫지 않지."], ["Here, give me a bit of paper, and let us have done with this nonsense.\"", "자, 종이 한 장 다오, 이 어처구니없는 일을 끝내자꾸나.\""]], [["The note was written in the terms which one gentleman would use to another after offering some deep insult. Jo dropped a kiss on the top of Mr.", "그 편지는 한 신사가 다른 신사에게 깊은 모욕을 준 뒤에나 쓸 법한 격식 있는 말투로 쓰였다."], ["Laurence's bald head, and ran up to slip the apology under Laurie's door, advising him, through the key-hole, to be submissive, decorous, and a few other agreeable impossibilities.", "조는 로런스 씨의 벗어진 정수리에 입맞춤을 떨구고는, 위층으로 달려가 그 사과 편지를 로리의 문틈으로 밀어 넣었다. 그러면서 열쇠 구멍 너머로, 고분고분하고 예의 바르게 굴라고, 그리고 그 밖에 몇 가지 듣기 좋되 도무지 될 성싶지 않은 것들을 당부했다."], ["Finding the door locked again, she left the note to do its work, and was going quietly away, when the young gentleman slid down the banisters, and waited for her at the bottom, saying, with his most virtuous expression of countenance, \"What a good fellow you are, Jo!", "문이 또 잠겨 있는 걸 알고는, 편지가 알아서 제 몫을 하도록 두고 조용히 떠나려는데, 그 도련님이 난간을 타고 미끄러져 내려와 계단 밑에서 기다리고 있다가, 더없이 점잖은 표정으로 말했다. \"넌 정말 좋은 녀석이야, 조!"], ["Did you get blown up?\"", "너도 한바탕 야단맞았어?\""], ["he added, laughing.", "그가 웃으며 덧붙였다."]], [["\"No; he was pretty mild, on the whole.\"", "\"아니. 어르신은 전체적으로 꽤 부드러우셨어.\""]], [["\"Ah!", "\"아!"], ["I got it all round; even you cast me off over there, and I felt just ready to go to the deuce,\"", "난 사방에서 다 당했어. 너까지 저쪽에서 날 내쳤으니, 그냥 지옥에라도 갈 기분이었다고.\""], ["he began apologetically.", "그가 변명하듯 운을 뗐다."]], [["\"Don't talk in that way; turn over a new leaf and begin again, Teddy, my son.\"", "\"그런 식으로 말하지 마. 새 마음으로 다시 시작하는 거야, 테디, 내 아들아.\""]], [["\"I keep turning over new leaves, and spoiling them, as I used to spoil my copy-books; and I make so many beginnings there never will be an end,\"", "\"새 마음으로 계속 다시 시작하긴 하는데, 자꾸 망쳐 버려. 옛날에 공책 망치던 것처럼 말이야."], ["he said dolefully.", "시작은 어찌나 많이 하는지, 끝은 영영 안 날 거야.\" 그가 풀이 죽어 말했다."]], [["\"Go and eat your dinner; you'll feel better after it.", "\"가서 저녁이나 먹어. 먹고 나면 기분이 나아질 거야."], ["Men always croak when they are hungry,\"", "남자들은 배가 고프면 늘 투덜대거든.\""], ["and Jo whisked out at the front door after that.", "그 말을 남기고 조는 휙 정문 밖으로 나가 버렸다."]], [["\"That's a 'label' on my 'sect,'\"", "\"내 '종족'에게 붙은 '딱지'로군.\""], ["answered Laurie, quoting Amy, as he went to partake of humble-pie dutifully with his grandfather, who was quite saintly in temper and overwhelmingly respectful in manner all the rest of the day.", "로리는 에이미의 말을 흉내 내어 대답하고는, 할아버지와 함께 얌전히 굴욕의 만찬을 들러 갔다. 할아버지는 그날 남은 시간 내내 성인군자처럼 너그러운 성미에, 더할 나위 없이 깍듯한 태도였다."]], [["Every one thought the matter ended and the little cloud blown over; but the mischief was done, for, though others forgot it, Meg remembered.", "다들 일이 마무리되고 작은 먹구름도 걷혔다고 여겼다. 하지만 화근은 이미 뿌려진 뒤였다."], ["She never alluded to a certain person, but she thought of him a good deal, dreamed dreams more than ever; and once Jo, rummaging her sister's desk for stamps, found a bit of paper scribbled over with the words, \"Mrs.", "다른 사람들은 다 잊었어도 메그는 잊지 않았던 것이다. 그녀는 어떤 사람에 대해 한마디도 입에 올리지 않았지만, 속으로는 그를 꽤나 자주 떠올렸고, 그 어느 때보다 더 많은 꿈을 꾸었다."], ["John Brooke;\"", "한번은 조가 우표를 찾으려고 언니의 책상을 뒤지다가 \"존 브룩 부인\"이라는 글자가 잔뜩 끄적여진 종이쪽지를 발견하고는, 비극 배우처럼 신음을 내뱉으며 그것을 불 속에 던져 버렸다."], ["whereat she groaned tragically, and cast it into the fire, feeling that Laurie's prank had hastened the evil day for her.", "로리의 장난이 자신에게는 불행한 날을 앞당겨 버렸다는 기분이 들었던 것이다. 즐거운 초원"]], [["# PLEASANT MEADOWS.", "# PLEASANT MEADOWS."]], [["Like sunshine after storm were the peaceful weeks which followed.", "폭풍 뒤의 햇살 같은 것이 바로 그 뒤로 이어진 평온한 몇 주였다."], ["The invalids improved rapidly, and Mr. March began to talk of returning early in the new year.", "환자들은 빠르게 회복했고, 마치 씨는 새해 초에 돌아올 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다."], ["Beth was soon able to lie on the study sofa all day, amusing herself with the well-beloved cats, at first, and, in time, with doll's sewing, which had fallen sadly behindhand.", "머지않아 베스는 하루 종일 서재 소파에 누워 지낼 수 있게 되었는데, 처음엔 그토록 아끼는 고양이들과 놀며 시간을 보내다가, 차츰 한참 밀려 있던 인형 옷 바느질로 마음을 달랬다."], ["Her once active limbs were so stiff and feeble that Jo took her a daily airing about the house in her strong arms.", "한때 그토록 활발했던 팔다리가 어찌나 뻣뻣하고 힘이 없던지, 조는 날마다 베스를 튼튼한 두 팔로 안아 집 안을 돌며 바람을 쐬어 주었다."], ["Meg cheerfully blackened and burnt her white hands cooking delicate messes for \"the dear;\"", "메그는 \"우리 귀염둥이\"를 위해 정성스러운 음식을 만드느라 새하얀 손을 그을리고 데어 가면서도 기꺼이 그렇게 했다."], ["while Amy, a loyal slave of the ring, celebrated her return by giving away as many of her treasures as she could prevail on her sisters to accept.", "한편 그 반지의 충직한 종, 에이미는 자기 보물들을 언니 동생들이 받아 주는 대로 한껏 나눠 주는 것으로 자신의 귀환을 자축했다."]], [["As Christmas approached, the usual mysteries began to haunt the house, and Jo frequently convulsed the family by proposing utterly impossible or magnificently absurd ceremonies, in honor of this unusually merry Christmas.", "크리스마스가 다가오면서, 으레 그렇듯 비밀스러운 분위기가 집 안을 감돌기 시작했다. 조는 이 유난히 즐거운 크리스마스를 기리겠다며 도무지 말도 안 되거나 거창하게 터무니없는 의식들을 제안해 식구들을 곧잘 웃음바다로 만들었다."], ["Laurie was equally impracticable, and would have had bonfires, sky-rockets, and triumphal arches, if he had had his own way.", "로리도 못지않게 비현실적이어서, 제멋대로 하게 뒀다면 모닥불에 폭죽에 개선문까지 동원했을 것이다."], ["After many skirmishes and snubbings, the ambitious pair were considered effectually quenched, and went about with forlorn faces, which were rather belied by explosions of laughter when the two got together.", "여러 번의 옥신각신과 핀잔 끝에, 이 야심만만한 두 사람은 완전히 진압된 것으로 여겨졌고, 풀 죽은 얼굴로 돌아다녔다. 하지만 둘이 함께 있을 때면 터져 나오는 폭소가 그 시무룩한 얼굴이 거짓임을 여실히 드러냈다."]], [["Several days of unusually mild weather fitly ushered in a splendid Christmas Day. Hannah \"felt in her bones\"", "유난히 포근한 날씨가 며칠 이어지더니, 마침 멋진 크리스마스 날을 열어 주었다."], ["that it was going to be an unusually fine day, and she proved herself a true prophetess, for everybody and everything seemed bound to produce a grand success.", "해나는 유난히 좋은 날이 되리라고 \"뼛속으로 느꼈는데\", 과연 그녀는 진짜 예언자임이 드러났다. 모든 사람과 모든 일이 큰 성공을 거두기로 작정한 듯했으니 말이다."], ["To begin with, Mr. March wrote that he should soon be with them; then Beth felt uncommonly well that morning, and, being dressed in her mother's gift,--a soft crimson merino wrapper,--was borne in triumph to the window to behold the offering of Jo and Laurie.", "우선 마치 씨가 곧 식구들과 함께하게 될 거라는 편지를 보내왔다."], ["The Unquenchables had done their best to be worthy of the name, for, like elves, they had worked by night, and conjured up a comical surprise.", "그리고 베스가 그날 아침 유난히 몸 상태가 좋아서, 어머니가 준 선물인 부드러운 진홍색 메리노 모직 실내복을 입고는, 의기양양하게 창가로 옮겨져 조와 로리의 선물을 보게 되었다. '진압 불가능한 두 사람'은 그 이름값을 하려고 최선을 다했으니, 요정들처럼 밤새 작업해서 우스꽝스러운 깜짝 선물을 마련해 둔 것이다."], ["Out in the garden stood a stately snow-maiden, crowned with holly, bearing a basket of fruit and flowers in one hand, a great roll of new music in the other, a perfect rainbow of an Afghan round her chilly shoulders, and a Christmas carol issuing from her lips, on a pink paper streamer:--", "정원에는 호랑가시나무로 관을 쓴 위풍당당한 눈 처녀가 서 있었는데, 한 손에는 과일과 꽃이 담긴 바구니를, 다른 손에는 새 악보 한 다발을 들고, 시린 어깨엔 완벽한 무지개 같은 아프간 숄을 두르고, 입가에선 분홍색 종이 띠에 적힌 크리스마스 캐럴이 흘러나오고 있었다. \"베스에게 보내는 융프라우."]], [["\"God bless you, dear Queen Bess!", "신의 가호가 함께하기를, 사랑하는 베스 여왕이여!"], ["May nothing you dismay, But health and peace and happiness Be yours, this Christmas Day.", "어떤 일에도 마음 졸이지 말기를, 오직 건강과 평화와 행복만이 이 크리스마스 날 그대 것이 되기를."]], [["\"Here's fruit to feed our busy bee, And flowers for her nose; Here's music for her pianee, An Afghan for her toes.", "여기 우리 부지런한 꿀벌을 먹일 과일이 있고, 코끝에 향기 더할 꽃이 있어요. 여기 그대 피아노를 위한 악보가 있고, 발끝을 덮을 아프간 숄이 있지요."]], [["\"A portrait of Joanna, see, By Raphael No.", "조애나의 초상화도 보세요, 라파엘로 2세가 그렸답니다."], ["2, Who labored with great industry To make it fair and true.", "참되고 곱게 그려 내려고 무던히도 애를 썼지요."]], [["\"Accept a ribbon red, I beg, For Madam Purrer's tail; And ice-cream made by lovely Peg,-- A Mont Blanc in a pail.", "부디 빨간 리본 하나 받아 주세요, 야옹이 마님의 꼬리에 매시라고요. 그리고 사랑스러운 페그가 만든 아이스크림은 양동이에 담긴 몽블랑이랍니다."]], [["\"Their dearest love my makers laid Within my breast of snow: Accept it, and the Alpine maid, From Laurie and from Jo.\"", "저를 만든 이들이 가장 깊은 사랑을 이 눈의 가슴속에 담아 두었어요. 그 사랑을, 그리고 이 알프스 처녀를 받아 주세요, 로리와 조가 보냅니다.\""]], [["How Beth laughed when she saw it, how Laurie ran up and down to bring in the gifts, and what ridiculous speeches Jo made as she presented them!", "그것을 보았을 때 베스가 얼마나 웃었는지, 선물들을 들이느라 로리가 얼마나 오르락내리락 뛰어다녔는지, 그리고 그것들을 건네며 조가 얼마나 우스꽝스러운 말들을 늘어놓았는지!"]], [["\"I'm so full of happiness, that, if father was only here, I couldn't hold one drop more,\"", "\"행복이 너무 가득 차서, 아버지만 여기 계셨다면 단 한 방울도 더는 못 담을 것 같아.\""], ["said Beth, quite sighing with contentment as Jo carried her off to the study to rest after the excitement, and to refresh herself with some of the delicious grapes the \"Jungfrau\" had sent her.", "조가 흥분을 가라앉히고 쉬게 하려고 베스를 서재로 데려가, '융프라우'가 보내 준 그 맛있는 포도로 기운을 돋게 해 주는 동안 베스가 더없이 흡족한 한숨을 내쉬며 말했다."]], [["\"So am I,\"", "\"나도 그래.\""], ["added Jo, slapping the pocket wherein reposed the long-desired Undine and Sintram.", "그토록 갖고 싶었던 『운디네와 진트람』이 들어 있는 호주머니를 툭툭 치며 조가 덧붙였다."]], [["\"I'm sure I am,\"", "\"난 정말 그렇고말고.\""], ["echoed Amy, poring over the engraved copy of the Madonna and Child, which her mother had given her, in a pretty frame.", "어머니가 예쁜 액자에 넣어 준 성모자상 판화를 들여다보며 에이미가 맞장구쳤다."]], [["\"Of course I am!\"", "\"당연히 나도지!\""], ["cried Meg, smoothing the silvery folds of her first silk dress; for Mr.", "자기 생애 첫 비단 드레스의 은빛 주름을 매만지며 메그가 외쳤다."], ["Laurence had insisted on giving it.", "로런스 씨가 한사코 선물한 드레스였다."]], [["\"How can _I_ be otherwise?\" said Mrs.", "\"내가 어찌 안 그럴 수 있겠니?\""], ["March gratefully, as her eyes went from her husband's letter to Beth's smiling face, and her hand caressed the brooch made of gray and golden, chestnut and dark brown hair, which the girls had just fastened on her breast.", "마치 부인이 고마운 마음으로 말했다. 그녀의 두 눈은 남편의 편지에서 베스의 미소 띤 얼굴로 옮겨 갔고, 한 손으로는 회색과 금색, 밤색과 짙은 갈색 머리카락으로 만든, 딸들이 방금 가슴에 달아 준 브로치를 어루만지고 있었다."]], [["Now and then, in this work-a-day world, things do happen in the delightful story-book fashion, and what a comfort that is.", "이 일에 매인 세상살이에서도, 이따금은 이렇게 즐거운 동화책 같은 일이 정말로 일어나곤 하니, 그 얼마나 위안이 되는지."], ["Half an hour after every one had said they were so happy they could only hold one drop more, the drop came.", "모두가 너무 행복해서 단 한 방울밖에는 더 담을 수 없겠다고 말한 지 반 시간 만에, 그 한 방울이 찾아왔다."], ["Laurie opened the parlor door, and popped his head in very quietly.", "로리가 응접실 문을 열고 아주 조용히 고개만 쏙 들이밀었다."], ["He might just as well have turned a somersault and uttered an Indian war-whoop; for his face was so full of suppressed excitement and his voice so treacherously joyful, that every one jumped up, though he only said, in a queer, breathless voice, \"Here's another Christmas present for the March family.\"", "그가 차라리 공중제비를 돌며 인디언의 함성이라도 질렀더라면 좋았을 것이다. 그의 얼굴엔 억눌린 흥분이 가득하고 목소리는 어찌나 들뜬 기쁨을 감추지 못하던지, 그저 묘하게 숨 가쁜 목소리로 \"마치 가족에게 줄 크리스마스 선물이 하나 더 있습니다\"라고만 했는데도 모두가 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["Before the words were well out of his mouth, he was whisked away somehow, and in his place appeared a tall man, muffled up to the eyes, leaning on the arm of another tall man, who tried to say something and couldn't.", "그 말이 채 입 밖으로 다 나오기도 전에, 로리는 어떻게 된 영문인지 휙 옆으로 비켜났고, 그 자리에 키 큰 남자가 나타났다. 눈만 빼고 온통 동여맨 채, 무어라 말하려 했지만 끝내 말을 잇지 못하는 또 다른 키 큰 남자의 팔에 기대 있었다."], ["Of course there was a general stampede; and for several minutes everybody seemed to lose their wits, for the strangest things were done, and no one said a word.", "당연히 한바탕 난리가 났다. 몇 분 동안 다들 넋을 놓은 듯했으니, 더없이 기묘한 일들이 벌어졌고 아무도 한마디 하지 않았다."], ["Mr.", "마치 씨는 사랑하는 네 쌍의 팔에 둘러싸여 모습이 보이지 않게 되었다."], ["March became invisible in the embrace of four pairs of loving arms; Jo disgraced herself by nearly fainting away, and had to be doctored by Laurie in the china-closet; Mr.", "조는 거의 까무러칠 뻔하는 추태를 부려, 도자기 보관실에서 로리에게 응급조치를 받아야 했다. 브룩 씨는, 본인이 어쩐지 횡설수설 해명한 바로는, 완전히 실수로 메그에게 입을 맞추고 말았다."], ["Brooke kissed Meg entirely by mistake, as he somewhat incoherently explained; and Amy, the dignified, tumbled over a stool, and, never stopping to get up, hugged and cried over her father's boots in the most touching manner. Mrs.", "그리고 그 점잖던 에이미는 발판에 걸려 나뒹굴고는, 일어날 새도 없이 그대로 아버지의 부츠를 끌어안고 더없이 가슴 뭉클하게 울었다."], ["March was the first to recover herself, and held up her hand with a warning, \"Hush!", "가장 먼저 정신을 차린 사람은 마치 부인이었다. 그녀가 손을 들어 올리며 주의를 주었다."], ["remember Beth!\"", "\"쉿! 베스를 생각해야지!\""]], [["But it was too late; the study door flew open, the little red wrapper appeared on the threshold,--joy put strength into the feeble limbs,--and Beth ran straight into her father's arms. Never mind what happened just after that; for the full hearts overflowed, washing away the bitterness of the past, and leaving only the sweetness of the present.", "하지만 이미 늦었다. 서재 문이 벌컥 열리고, 작고 빨간 실내복이 문턱에 나타났다. 기쁨이 그 가냘픈 팔다리에 힘을 불어넣었던 것이다. 베스는 곧장 아버지의 품으로 달려들었다. 그 직후에 무슨 일이 일어났는지는 신경 쓰지 않아도 된다. 벅찬 가슴들이 넘쳐흘러 지난날의 쓰라림을 다 씻어 내고, 오직 현재의 달콤함만을 남겨 두었으니까."]], [["It was not at all romantic, but a hearty laugh set everybody straight again, for Hannah was discovered behind the door, sobbing over the fat turkey, which she had forgotten to put down when she rushed up from the kitchen.", "도무지 낭만적이라곤 할 수 없었지만, 진심 어린 웃음 한바탕이 모두를 다시 제정신으로 돌려놓았다. 해나가 부엌에서 뛰어 올라오면서 미처 내려놓지 못한 살진 칠면조를 끌어안고 문 뒤에서 흐느끼고 있는 게 발각된 것이다."], ["As the laugh subsided, Mrs. March began to thank Mr.", "웃음이 잦아들자, 마치 부인은 남편을 충실히 돌봐 준 브룩 씨에게 고마움을 전하기 시작했다."], ["Brooke for his faithful care of her husband, at which Mr. Brooke suddenly remembered that Mr.", "그러자 브룩 씨는 마치 씨에게 휴식이 필요하다는 게 별안간 떠오른 듯, 로리를 붙들고는 황급히 물러갔다."], ["March needed rest, and, seizing Laurie, he precipitately retired. Then the two invalids were ordered to repose, which they did, by both sitting in one big chair, and talking hard.", "그러고 나서 두 환자에게 안정을 취하라는 명이 떨어졌는데, 두 사람은 하나의 큰 의자에 함께 앉아 쉴 새 없이 이야기를 나누는 것으로 그 명을 따랐다."]], [["Mr. March told how he had longed to surprise them, and how, when the fine weather came, he had been allowed by his doctor to take advantage of it; how devoted Brooke had been, and how he was altogether a most estimable and upright young man.", "마치 씨는 식구들을 깜짝 놀라게 해 주고 싶어 얼마나 애가 탔는지, 그리고 날씨가 좋아지자 의사가 그 기회를 잡도록 허락해 준 일이며, 브룩이 얼마나 헌신적이었는지, 또 그가 모든 면에서 더없이 존경스럽고 올곧은 청년인지를 들려주었다."], ["Why Mr. March paused a minute just there, and, after a glance at Meg, who was violently poking the fire, looked at his wife with an inquiring lift of the eyebrows, I leave you to imagine; also why Mrs.", "마치 씨가 바로 그 대목에서 잠시 말을 멈추고는, 난롯불을 사납게 쑤석거리고 있는 메그를 한번 흘끗 보더니, 눈썹을 캐묻듯 치켜올리며 아내를 바라본 까닭은, 여러분의 상상에 맡기겠다."], ["March gently nodded her head, and asked, rather abruptly, if he wouldn't have something to eat.", "또한 마치 부인이 가만히 고개를 끄덕이고는, 다소 불쑥, 뭐 좀 드시지 않겠느냐고 물은 까닭도. 조는 그 눈빛을 보았고 그 뜻을 알아챘다."], ["Jo saw and understood the look; and she stalked grimly away to get wine and beef-tea, muttering to herself, as she slammed the door, \"I hate estimable young men with brown eyes!\"", "그래서 포도주와 쇠고기 수프를 가지러 험상궂은 표정으로 성큼성큼 걸어 나가며, 문을 쾅 닫고는 혼자 중얼거렸다. \"갈색 눈을 한 존경스러운 청년들이라면 딱 질색이야!\""]], [["There never _was_ such a Christmas dinner as they had that day.", "그날 그들이 보낸 것 같은 크리스마스 만찬은 일찍이 없었다. 해나가 속을 채우고 노릇하게 굽고 장식까지 해서 올려 보낸 그 살진 칠면조는 그야말로 볼만한 광경이었다."], ["The fat turkey was a sight to behold, when Hannah sent him up, stuffed, browned, and decorated; so was the plum-pudding, which quite melted in one's mouth; likewise the jellies, in which Amy revelled like a fly in a honey-pot.", "입안에서 사르르 녹는 플럼 푸딩도 그랬고, 에이미가 꿀단지에 빠진 파리처럼 흠뻑 즐긴 젤리도 마찬가지였다. 모든 게 잘되었으니, 해나의 말마따나 천만다행이었다."], ["Everything turned out well, which was a mercy, Hannah said, \"For my mind was that flustered, mum, that it's a merrycle I didn't roast the pudding, and stuff the turkey with raisins, let alone bilin' of it in a cloth.\"", "\"제 정신이 어찌나 얼떨떨하던지요, 마님, 푸딩을 오븐에 굽고 칠면조 속에 건포도를 채우지 않은 게, 아니, 천에 싸서 삶지 않은 게 기적이라니까요.\""]], [["Mr. Laurence and his grandson dined with them, also Mr.", "로런스 씨와 그의 손자도 함께 만찬을 들었고, 브룩 씨도 그랬다."], ["Brooke,--at whom Jo glowered darkly, to Laurie's infinite amusement.", "조가 그를 향해 험상궂게 노려보는 통에 로리는 한없이 즐거워했다."], ["Two easy-chairs stood side by side at the head of the table, in which sat Beth and her father, feasting modestly on chicken and a little fruit.", "식탁 상석에는 안락의자 두 개가 나란히 놓였고, 그 자리에 베스와 그녀의 아버지가 앉아 닭고기와 약간의 과일로 조촐하게 식사를 즐겼다."], ["They drank healths, told stories, sung songs, \"reminisced,\" as the old folks say, and had a thoroughly good time.", "그들은 서로의 건강을 빌며 잔을 들고, 이야기를 나누고, 노래를 부르고, 노인네들 말마따나 \"옛일을 떠올리며\", 더없이 즐거운 시간을 보냈다."], ["A sleigh-ride had been planned, but the girls would not leave their father; so the guests departed early, and, as twilight gathered, the happy family sat together round the fire.", "썰매를 타러 가기로 했었지만 딸들이 아버지 곁을 떠나려 하지 않아서, 손님들은 일찌감치 돌아갔다. 그리고 땅거미가 내려앉을 무렵, 행복한 가족은 난롯가에 둘러앉았다."]], [["\"Just a year ago we were groaning over the dismal Christmas we expected to have.", "\"꼭 일 년 전에 우린 음울한 크리스마스를 보내게 됐다고 끙끙 앓았었잖아."], ["Do you remember?\"", "다들 기억해?\""], ["asked Jo, breaking a short pause which had followed a long conversation about many things.", "여러 가지 이야기를 길게 나눈 뒤 찾아온 짧은 침묵을 깨며 조가 물었다."]], [["\"Rather a pleasant year on the whole!\"", "\"전체적으로 보면 꽤 즐거운 한 해였어!\""], ["said Meg, smiling at the fire, and congratulating herself on having treated Mr. Brooke with dignity.", "메그가 난롯불을 향해 미소 지으며, 브룩 씨를 위엄 있게 대했노라 스스로를 대견해하며 말했다."]], [["\"I think it's been a pretty hard one,\"", "\"난 꽤 힘든 한 해였던 것 같아.\""], ["observed Amy, watching the light shine on her ring, with thoughtful eyes.", "자기 반지에 비치는 불빛을 사려 깊은 눈으로 바라보며 에이미가 말했다."]], [["\"I'm glad it's over, because we've got you back,\"", "\"난 이 해가 끝나서 기뻐, 아버지가 우리 곁으로 돌아오셨으니까.\""], ["whispered Beth, who sat on her father's knee.", "아버지 무릎에 앉은 베스가 속삭였다."]], [["\"Rather a rough road for you to travel, my little pilgrims, especially the latter part of it.", "\"너희가 걸어온 길이 꽤나 험했지, 나의 어린 순례자들아, 특히 그 끝자락이 말이다."], ["But you have got on bravely; and I think the burdens are in a fair way to tumble off very soon,\"", "하지만 너희는 용감하게 헤쳐 왔어. 그리고 그 짐들도 머지않아 훌훌 벗겨질 것 같구나.\""], ["said Mr. March, looking with fatherly satisfaction at the four young faces gathered round him.", "자기 주위에 둘러앉은 네 앳된 얼굴을 아버지다운 흐뭇함으로 바라보며 마치 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"How do you know?", "\"어떻게 아세요?"], ["Did mother tell you?\"", "엄마가 말씀해 주셨어요?\""], ["asked Jo.", "조가 물었다."]], [["\"Not much; straws show which way the wind blows, and I've made several discoveries to-day.\"", "\"많이는 아니란다. 지푸라기 하나로도 바람이 어느 쪽으로 부는지 알 수 있는 법이지. 오늘 몇 가지를 발견했단다.\""]], [["\"Oh, tell us what they are!\"", "\"아, 그게 뭔지 말씀해 주세요!\""], ["cried Meg, who sat beside him.", "아버지 곁에 앉은 메그가 외쳤다."]], [["\"Here is one;\"", "\"여기 하나 있구나.\""], ["and taking up the hand which lay on the arm of his chair, he pointed to the roughened forefinger, a burn on the back, and two or three little hard spots on the palm.", "그러면서 의자 팔걸이에 놓인 손을 들어, 거칠어진 집게손가락과 손등의 화상 자국, 그리고 손바닥의 두어 군데 굳은살을 가리켰다."], ["\"I remember a time when this hand was white and smooth, and your first care was to keep it so.", "\"이 손이 희고 매끄럽던 시절, 그것을 그대로 지키는 게 네 첫째 관심사였던 때가 기억나는구나."], ["It was very pretty then, but to me it is much prettier now,--for in these seeming blemishes I read a little history.", "그때도 무척 예뻤지만, 내겐 지금이 훨씬 더 예쁘단다. 흠집처럼 보이는 이것들 속에서 작은 역사를 읽을 수 있으니까."], ["A burnt-offering has been made of vanity; this hardened palm has earned something better than blisters; and I'm sure the sewing done by these pricked fingers will last a long time, so much good-will went into the stitches.", "허영이 번제물로 바쳐졌고, 이 굳어진 손바닥은 물집보다 더 나은 무언가를 벌어들였지. 그리고 이 바늘에 찔린 손가락들이 해낸 바느질은 오래도록 갈 거라고 확신한다."], ["Meg, my dear, I value the womanly skill which keeps home happy more than white hands or fashionable accomplishments.", "그만큼 많은 정성이 그 한 땀 한 땀에 담겼으니까. 메그, 얘야, 나는 가정을 행복하게 지키는 여인다운 솜씨를, 흰 손이나 세련된 교양보다 더 귀히 여긴단다."], ["I'm proud to shake this good, industrious little hand, and hope I shall not soon be asked to give it away.\"", "이 착하고 부지런한 작은 손을 잡게 되어 자랑스럽고, 머지않아 이 손을 누군가에게 내어 달라는 부탁을 받는 일은 없기를 바란다.\""]], [["If Meg had wanted a reward for hours of patient labor, she received it in the hearty pressure of her father's hand and the approving smile he gave her.", "여러 시간 묵묵히 일한 데 대한 보상을 메그가 바랐다면, 그녀는 아버지의 진심 어린 손길과 그가 보내 준 흐뭇한 미소 속에서 그것을 받은 셈이었다."]], [["\"What about Jo?", "\"조는요?"], ["Please say something nice; for she has tried so hard, and been so very, very good to me,\"", "좋은 말씀 좀 해 주세요. 조는 정말 애를 많이 썼고, 저한테도 너무너무 잘해 줬거든요.\""], ["said Beth, in her father's ear.", "베스가 아버지의 귀에 대고 말했다."]], [["He laughed, and looked across at the tall girl who sat opposite, with an unusually mild expression in her brown face.", "그는 웃으며 맞은편에 앉은 키 큰 딸을 건너다보았다. 그 갈색 얼굴엔 유난히 온화한 빛이 어려 있었다."]], [["\"In spite of the curly crop, I don't see the 'son Jo' whom I left a year ago,\"", "\"곱슬곱슬한 짧은 머리에도 불구하고, 일 년 전에 내가 두고 떠난 '아들 조'는 보이지 않는구나.\""], ["said Mr. March.", "마치 씨가 말했다."], ["\"I see a young lady who pins her collar straight, laces her boots neatly, and neither whistles, talks slang, nor lies on the rug as she used to do.", "\"옷깃을 반듯이 여미고, 부츠 끈을 단정히 매고, 예전처럼 휘파람을 불거나 속어를 쓰거나 양탄자 위에 드러눕지도 않는 한 아가씨가 보이는걸. 지금은 간병과 근심으로 얼굴이 좀 야위고 창백하지만, 난 그 얼굴을 보는 게 좋단다."], ["Her face is rather thin and pale, just now, with watching and anxiety; but I like to look at it, for it has grown gentler, and her voice is lower; she doesn't bounce, but moves quietly, and takes care of a certain little person in a motherly way which delights me.", "더 부드러워졌고, 목소리도 낮아졌으니까. 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰지 않고 조용히 움직이며, 어떤 작은 사람을 어머니처럼 보살피는데, 그 모습이 날 기쁘게 하는구나."], ["I rather miss my wild girl; but if I get a strong, helpful, tender-hearted woman in her place, I shall feel quite satisfied.", "내 거친 딸이 좀 그립기도 하지만, 그 자리에 강하고, 든든하고, 마음씨 다정한 여인을 얻게 된다면, 나는 더없이 만족할 거야."], ["I don't know whether the shearing sobered our black sheep, but I do know that in all Washington I couldn't find anything beautiful enough to be bought with the five-and-twenty dollars which my good girl sent me.\"", "머리를 자른 일이 우리 검은 양을 차분하게 만들었는지는 모르겠다만, 워싱턴을 다 뒤져도 내 착한 딸이 보내 준 이십오 달러로 살 만한 아름다운 물건을 도무지 찾을 수 없었다는 것만은 분명히 안단다.\""]], [["Jo's keen eyes were rather dim for a minute, and her thin face grew rosy in the firelight, as she received her father's praise, feeling that she did deserve a portion of it.", "조의 날카로운 두 눈이 잠시 흐릿해졌고, 그 야윈 얼굴이 난롯불빛 속에 발그레해졌다. 아버지의 칭찬을 받으며, 자신이 그 칭찬의 얼마쯤은 받을 자격이 있다고 느꼈던 것이다."]], [["\"Now Beth,\"", "\"이제 베스 차례예요.\""], ["said Amy, longing for her turn, but ready to wait.", "에이미가 자기 차례를 고대하면서도 기꺼이 기다릴 준비를 한 채 말했다."]], [["\"There's so little of her, I'm afraid to say much, for fear she will slip away altogether, though she is not so shy as she used to be,\"", "\"이 아이는 너무 작아서, 자칫 아주 미끄러져 사라져 버릴까 봐 많은 말은 못 하겠구나. 예전만큼 수줍어하진 않게 됐다만.\""], ["began their father cheerfully; but recollecting how nearly he _had_ lost her, he held her close, saying tenderly, with her cheek against his own, \"I've got you safe, my Beth, and I'll keep you so, please God.\"", "아버지가 명랑하게 입을 떼다가, 이 아이를 하마터면 잃을 뻔했던 일을 떠올리고는, 자기 뺨에 베스의 뺨을 맞댄 채 다정하게 말하며 아이를 꼭 끌어안았다. \"이제 너를 안전하게 데려왔으니, 신의 가호로 너를 이대로 지켜 줄 거야, 나의 베스.\""]], [["After a minute's silence, he looked down at Amy, who sat on the cricket at his feet, and said, with a caress of the shining hair,--", "잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 그는 자기 발치의 작은 의자에 앉은 에이미를 내려다보며, 그 반짝이는 머리카락을 어루만지면서 말했다."]], [["\"I observed that Amy took drumsticks at dinner, ran errands for her mother all the afternoon, gave Meg her place to-night, and has waited on every one with patience and good-humor.", "\"보니 에이미는 저녁 식사 때 닭다리를 집어 들고, 오후 내내 어머니 심부름을 다니고, 오늘 밤엔 메그에게 제 자리를 양보하고, 모두를 참을성 있고 상냥하게 시중들더구나."], ["I also observe that she does not fret much nor look in the glass, and has not even mentioned a very pretty ring which she wears; so I conclude that she has learned to think of other people more and of herself less, and has decided to try and mould her character as carefully as she moulds her little clay figures.", "또 보니 별로 보채지도 않고, 거울을 들여다보지도 않으며, 자기가 낀 무척 예쁜 반지에 대해서도 일언반구 없더구나. 그러니 이 아이가 다른 사람을 더 많이, 자기 자신은 덜 생각하는 법을 배웠고, 그 작은 진흙 인형들을 정성껏 빚듯이 제 인격도 정성껏 빚어 보기로 마음먹었구나 짐작하게 된단다."], ["I am glad of this; for though I should be very proud of a graceful statue made by her, I shall be infinitely prouder of a lovable daughter, with a talent for making life beautiful to herself and others.\"", "나는 이것이 기쁘구나. 이 아이가 만든 우아한 조각상이라면 무척 자랑스럽겠지만, 자기 자신과 다른 이들에게 삶을 아름답게 만드는 재능을 지닌, 사랑스러운 딸이라면 한없이 더 자랑스러울 테니까.\""]], [["\"What are you thinking of, Beth?\"", "\"무슨 생각하니, 베스?\""], ["asked Jo, when Amy had thanked her father and told about her ring.", "에이미가 아버지에게 고마움을 전하고 자기 반지 이야기를 한 뒤, 조가 물었다."]], [["\"I read in 'Pilgrim's Progress'", "\"오늘 『천로역정』에서 읽었어."], ["to-day, how, after many troubles, Christian and Hopeful came to a pleasant green meadow, where lilies bloomed all the year round, and there they rested happily, as we do now, before they went on to their journey's end,\"", "크리스천과 호프풀이 숱한 고난 끝에, 일 년 내내 백합이 피어나는 어느 아름답고 푸른 초원에 다다랐대. 거기서 둘은 여정의 끝으로 나아가기 전에, 지금 우리처럼 행복하게 쉬었다는 거야.\""], ["answered Beth; adding, as she slipped out of her father's arms, and went slowly to the instrument, \"It's singing time now, and I want to be in my old place.", "베스가 대답했다. 그러고는 아버지의 품에서 미끄러져 나와 천천히 피아노 쪽으로 걸어가며 덧붙였다."], ["I'll try to sing the song of the shepherd-boy which the Pilgrims heard.", "\"이제 노래할 시간이에요, 내 옛 자리에 앉고 싶어요. 순례자들이 들었던 그 양치기 소년의 노래를 불러 볼게요."], ["I made the music for father, because he likes the verses.\"", "그 시를 아버지가 좋아하셔서, 제가 곡을 붙였거든요.\""]], [["So, sitting at the dear little piano, Beth softly touched the keys, and, in the sweet voice they had never thought to hear again, sung to her own accompaniment the quaint hymn, which was a singularly fitting song for her:--", "그렇게, 사랑스러운 작은 피아노 앞에 앉아, 베스는 건반을 가만히 어루만지며, 다시는 듣지 못하리라 여겼던 그 고운 목소리로, 제 반주에 맞추어 그 예스러운 찬가를 불렀다. 그것은 그녀에게 유난히도 잘 어울리는 노래였다."]], [["\"He that is down need fear no fall, He that is low no pride; He that is humble ever shall Have God to be his guide.", "\"낮은 곳에 있는 이는 떨어질 일을 두려워 말지니, 아래에 있는 이에겐 교만이 없도다. 겸손한 이는 언제까지나 하느님을 길잡이로 모시리라."]], [["\"I am content with what I have, Little be it or much; And, Lord!", "내게 있는 것에 나는 만족하노라, 그것이 적든 많든. 그리고, 주여!"], ["contentment still I crave, Because Thou savest such.", "나는 늘 만족을 구하나니, 주께서 그런 이들을 구원하시기 때문이라."]], [["\"Fulness to them a burden is, That go on pilgrimage; Here little, and hereafter bliss, Is best from age to age!\"", "순례의 길을 가는 이들에게는 넘침이란 도리어 짐이로다. 이승에선 적게, 저승에선 더없는 복을 누림이 대대로 가장 좋은 것이로다!\""]], [["# AUNT MARCH SETTLES THE QUESTION.", "# AUNT MARCH SETTLES THE QUESTION."]], [["Like bees swarming after their queen, mother and daughters hovered about Mr. March the next day, neglecting everything to look at, wait upon, and listen to the new invalid, who was in a fair way to be killed by kindness.", "마치 대고모가 매듭을 짓다\n여왕벌을 좇아 떼 지어 모여드는 벌들처럼, 이튿날 어머니와 딸들은 마치 씨 주위를 맴돌며, 모든 걸 제쳐 두고 이 새로운 환자를 보살피고, 시중들고, 그의 말에 귀 기울였다."], ["As he sat propped up in a big chair by Beth's sofa, with the other three close by, and Hannah popping in her head now and then, \"to peek at the dear man,\"", "그 바람에 마치 씨는 친절에 치여 죽을 지경이었다. 그가 베스의 소파 옆 큰 의자에 등을 기대고 앉고, 나머지 셋이 바짝 곁에 붙고, 해나가 이따금 \"그 귀하신 양반을 한 번씩 들여다보러\""], ["nothing seemed needed to complete their happiness.", "고개를 들이미는 것까지 더하니, 그들의 행복을 완성하는 데 더 필요한 것은 없어 보였다."], ["But something _was_ needed, and the elder ones felt it, though none confessed the fact. Mr.", "하지만 무언가가 정말로 필요했고, 어른들은 그걸 느끼고 있었다."], ["and Mrs. March looked at one another with an anxious expression, as their eyes followed Meg.", "아무도 입 밖에 내지는 않았지만 말이다."], ["Jo had sudden fits of sobriety, and was seen to shake her fist at Mr.", "마치 부부는 두 눈으로 메그를 좇으며 근심 어린 표정으로 서로를 바라보았다."], ["Brooke's umbrella, which had been left in the hall; Meg was absent-minded, shy, and silent, started when the bell rang, and colored when John's name was mentioned; Amy said \"Every one seemed waiting for something, and couldn't settle down, which was queer, since father was safe at home,\"", "조는 별안간 진지해지는 순간들이 있었고, 현관에 놓인 브룩 씨의 우산을 향해 주먹을 휘두르는 모습이 목격되기도 했다. 메그는 정신이 딴 데 가 있고, 수줍어하고, 말이 없었으며, 초인종이 울리면 흠칫 놀라고 존의 이름이 나오면 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["and Beth innocently wondered why their neighbors didn't run over as usual.", "에이미는 \"다들 뭔가를 기다리는 것 같은데, 아버지가 무사히 집에 돌아오셨으니 이상하게도 마음을 가라앉히질 못한다\"고 했고, 베스는 이웃들이 왜 평소처럼 놀러 오지 않는지 천진하게 궁금해했다."]], [["Laurie went by in the afternoon, and, seeing Meg at the window, seemed suddenly possessed with a melodramatic fit, for he fell down upon one knee in the snow, beat his breast, tore his hair, and clasped his hands imploringly, as if begging some boon; and when Meg told him to behave himself and go away, he wrung imaginary tears out of his handkerchief, and staggered round the corner as if in utter despair.", "오후에 로리가 지나가다가 창가에 있는 메그를 보고는, 별안간 신파극에 들린 듯, 눈 위에 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 제 가슴을 치고 머리를 쥐어뜯으며 무슨 은혜라도 구걸하듯 두 손을 모아 애원하는 시늉을 했다. 메그가 점잖게 굴고 가 버리라고 하자, 로리는 손수건으로 있지도 않은 눈물을 짜내고는, 완전히 절망에 빠진 듯 비틀거리며 모퉁이를 돌아 사라졌다."]], [["\"What does the goose mean?\"", "\"저 바보가 무슨 짓이야?\""], ["said Meg, laughing, and trying to look unconscious.", "메그가 웃으면서, 짐짓 아무것도 모르는 척하려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["\"He's showing you how your John will go on by and by.", "\"언니의 존이 머지않아 어떻게 굴지 보여 주는 거지."], ["Touching, isn't it?\"", "감동적이지 않아?\""], ["answered Jo scornfully.", "조가 경멸하듯 대꾸했다."]], [["\"Don't say _my John_, it isn't proper or true;\"", "\"내 존이라고 하지 마, 그건 적절하지도, 사실도 아니야.\""], ["but Meg's voice lingered over the words as if they sounded pleasant to her.", "그러면서도 메그의 목소리는 그 말이 듣기 좋기라도 한 듯 그 말 위에 머뭇거렸다. \"제발 날 좀 괴롭히지 마, 조."], ["\"Please don't plague me, Jo; I've told you I don't care _much_ about him, and there isn't to be anything said, but we are all to be friendly, and go on as before.\"", "그 사람한테 별 관심 없다고 했잖아. 아무 말도 오갈 일 없고, 우린 다 그냥 사이좋게, 전처럼 지내면 돼.\""]], [["\"We can't, for something _has_ been said, and Laurie's mischief has spoilt you for me.", "\"그럴 순 없어, 이미 무슨 말이 오갔고, 로리의 장난이 언니를 망쳐서 내 언니가 아니게 만들어 버렸으니까."], ["I see it, and so does mother; you are not like your old self a bit, and seem ever so far away from me.", "나도 알고 엄마도 아셔. 언니는 예전의 언니랑은 조금도 같지 않고, 나한테서 한없이 멀어진 것 같아."], ["I don't mean to plague you, and will bear it like a man, but I do wish it was all settled.", "언니를 괴롭히려는 게 아니라 사내답게 견뎌 보려는 거야. 그래도 이 일이 다 매듭지어졌으면 좋겠어."], ["I hate to wait; so if you mean ever to do it, make haste and have it over quickly,\"", "난 기다리는 게 싫거든. 그러니까 언젠가 할 거라면, 서둘러서 후딱 끝내 버려.\""], ["said Jo pettishly.", "조가 토라진 듯 말했다."]], [["\"_I_ can't say or do anything till he speaks, and he won't, because father said I was too young,\"", "\"그분이 먼저 말을 꺼내기 전엔 나도 아무 말, 아무 일도 못 해. 그런데 그분은 말을 안 꺼낼 거야."], ["began Meg, bending over her work, with a queer little smile, which suggested that she did not quite agree with her father on that point.", "아버지가 내가 너무 어리다고 하셨으니까.\" 메그가 일감 위로 고개를 숙인 채, 그 점에 대해서는 아버지 말씀에 썩 동의하지 않는다는 듯한 묘한 미소를 띠며 입을 뗐다."]], [["\"If he did speak, you wouldn't know what to say, but would cry or blush, or let him have his own way, instead of giving a good, decided, No.\"", "\"그 사람이 정말 말을 꺼내도, 언니는 뭐라고 해야 할지 몰라서 울거나 얼굴을 붉히거나, 아니면 그 사람 뜻대로 하게 둘걸. 야무지고 단호하게 '싫어요' 한마디 하는 대신에 말이야.\""]], [["\"I'm not so silly and weak as you think.", "\"네 생각만큼 내가 그렇게 바보 같고 마음 약하진 않거든. 무슨 말을 할지 다 정해 뒀어."], ["I know just what I should say, for I've planned it all, so I needn't be taken unawares; there's no knowing what may happen, and I wished to be prepared.\"", "다 계획해 뒀으니까 불시에 당할 일은 없어. 무슨 일이 생길지 모르는 거니까, 미리 대비해 두고 싶었어.\""]], [["Jo couldn't help smiling at the important air which Meg had unconsciously assumed, and which was as becoming as the pretty color varying in her cheeks.", "조는 메그가 자기도 모르게 짐짓 짓고 있는 그 거창한 태도에 웃음을 참을 수 없었다. 그 태도는 두 뺨에 오르내리는 고운 홍조만큼이나 잘 어울렸다."]], [["\"Would you mind telling me what you'd say?\"", "\"무슨 말을 할 건지 나한테 말해 줄 수 있어?\""], ["asked Jo more respectfully.", "조가 한결 공손하게 물었다."]], [["\"Not at all; you are sixteen now, quite old enough to be my confidant, and my experience will be useful to you by and by, perhaps, in your own affairs of this sort.\"", "\"못 할 것도 없지. 너도 이제 열여섯이니, 내 비밀을 털어놓을 만큼은 충분히 컸고, 내 경험이 언젠가 너도 이런 종류의 일을 겪을 때 도움이 될지도 모르니까.\""]], [["\"Don't mean to have any; it's fun to watch other people philander, but I should feel like a fool doing it myself,\"", "\"난 그런 거 안 겪을 거야. 다른 사람들이 사랑 놀음하는 걸 구경하는 건 재밌지만, 내가 직접 하면 바보가 된 기분일 것 같아.\""], ["said Jo, looking alarmed at the thought.", "그 생각만으로도 질겁한 표정을 지으며 조가 말했다."]], [["\"I think not, if you liked any one very much, and he liked you.\"", "\"그렇지 않을걸. 네가 누군가를 무척 좋아하고, 그 사람도 너를 좋아한다면 말이야.\""], ["Meg spoke as if to herself, and glanced out at the lane, where she had often seen lovers walking together in the summer twilight.", "메그가 혼잣말하듯 말하며, 여름 땅거미 속에서 연인들이 함께 거니는 모습을 자주 보았던 그 오솔길 쪽을 흘끗 내다보았다."]], [["\"I thought you were going to tell your speech to that man,\"", "\"그 사람한테 할 말을 나한테 들려준다며.\""], ["said Jo, rudely shortening her sister's little reverie.", "조가 언니의 작은 몽상을 무례하게 끊으며 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, I should merely say, quite calmly and decidedly, 'Thank you, Mr.", "\"아, 난 그냥 아주 차분하고 단호하게 이렇게만 말할 거야."], ["Brooke, you are very kind, but I agree with father that I am too young to enter into any engagement at present; so please say no more, but let us be friends as we were.'\"", "'고마워요, 브룩 씨, 정말 친절하시지만, 저는 아직 어떤 약혼을 하기엔 너무 어리다는 아버지 말씀에 동의해요. 그러니 더는 말씀하지 마시고, 우리 예전처럼 친구로 지내요.'\""]], [["\"Hum!", "\"흠!"], ["that's stiff and cool enough.", "충분히 딱딱하고 쌀쌀맞긴 하네."], ["I don't believe you'll ever say it, and I know he won't be satisfied if you do.", "하지만 난 언니가 그 말을 끝내 못 할 거라고 봐. 그리고 설령 한다 해도 그 사람이 그걸로 만족 안 할 거란 것도 알아."], ["If he goes on like the rejected lovers in books, you'll give in, rather than hurt his feelings.\"", "그 사람이 책 속에서 퇴짜 맞은 연인들처럼 군다면, 언니는 그 사람 마음 상하게 하느니 차라리 굽히고 말걸.\""]], [["\"No, I won't.", "\"안 그래."], ["I shall tell him I've made up my mind, and shall walk out of the room with dignity.\"", "마음을 굳혔다고 말하고, 위엄 있게 방에서 걸어 나올 거야.\""]], [["Meg rose as she spoke, and was just going to rehearse the dignified exit, when a step in the hall made her fly into her seat, and begin to sew as if her life depended on finishing that particular seam in a given time.", "메그는 그렇게 말하면서 일어나, 그 위엄 있게 나가는 모습을 막 연습해 보려던 참이었다. 그런데 현관에서 발소리가 나자 메그는 후다닥 제자리로 날아가 앉아, 정해진 시간 안에 그 솔기 하나를 다 꿰매지 못하면 큰일이라도 나는 양 바느질을 시작했다."], ["Jo smothered a laugh at the sudden change, and, when some one gave a modest tap, opened the door with a grim aspect, which was anything but hospitable.", "조는 그 갑작스러운 변화에 웃음을 삼켰고, 누군가 조심스레 똑똑 두드리자, 도무지 손님맞이라곤 할 수 없는 험상궂은 얼굴로 문을 열었다."]], [["\"Good afternoon.", "\"안녕하세요."], ["I came to get my umbrella,--that is, to see how your father finds himself to-day,\"", "제 우산을 가지러 왔어요. 아니, 그러니까, 오늘 아버님께서 좀 어떠신지 보러 왔습니다.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke, getting a trifle confused as his eye went from one tell-tale face to the other.", "브룩 씨가, 시선이 한쪽의 속내 드러난 얼굴에서 다른 쪽 얼굴로 옮겨 가며 조금 당황한 채 말했다."]], [["\"It's very well, he's in the rack, I'll get him, and tell it you are here,\"", "\"아주 잘 계세요, 그건 우산꽂이에 있고요, 제가 가져올게요, 그리고 아저씨 오셨다고 전할게요.\""], ["and having jumbled her father and the umbrella well together in her reply, Jo slipped out of the room to give Meg a chance to make her speech and air her dignity.", "그렇게 대답 속에 아버지와 우산을 한데 뒤죽박죽 섞어 버린 조는, 메그가 그 할 말을 하고 위엄을 뽐낼 기회를 주려고 방에서 슬쩍 빠져나갔다."], ["But the instant she vanished, Meg began to sidle towards the door, murmuring,--", "그러나 조가 사라지기가 무섭게, 메그는 문 쪽으로 슬금슬금 다가가며 중얼거렸다."]], [["\"Mother will like to see you.", "\"엄마가 보면 반가워하실 거예요."], ["Pray sit down, I'll call her.\"", "부디 앉으세요, 제가 엄마를 불러올게요.\""]], [["\"Don't go; are you afraid of me, Margaret?\"", "\"가지 마세요. 내가 무섭습니까, 마거릿?\""], ["and Mr. Brooke looked so hurt that Meg thought she must have done something very rude.", "브룩 씨가 어찌나 상처받은 표정을 짓던지, 메그는 자신이 뭔가 아주 무례한 짓을 한 게 틀림없다고 생각했다."], ["She blushed up to the little curls on her forehead, for he had never called her Margaret before, and she was surprised to find how natural and sweet it seemed to hear him say it.", "메그는 이마의 잔 곱슬머리까지 새빨개졌다. 그가 그녀를 마거릿이라고 부른 건 처음이었고, 그가 그렇게 부르는 걸 듣는 게 얼마나 자연스럽고 달콤한지 깨닫고는 스스로도 놀랐던 것이다."], ["Anxious to appear friendly and at her ease, she put out her hand with a confiding gesture, and said gratefully,--", "다정하고 편안해 보이고 싶은 마음에, 메그는 미덥다는 듯한 몸짓으로 손을 내밀며 고마운 마음으로 말했다."]], [["\"How can I be afraid when you have been so kind to father?", "\"아버지께 그토록 친절히 대해 주셨는데 제가 어떻게 무서워하겠어요?"], ["I only wish I could thank you for it.\"", "그저 그 일에 대해 감사를 드릴 수만 있다면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["\"Shall I tell you how?\" asked Mr.", "\"어떻게 하면 되는지 말씀드릴까요?\""], ["Brooke, holding the small hand fast in both his own, and looking down at Meg with so much love in the brown eyes, that her heart began to flutter, and she both longed to run away and to stop and listen.", "브룩 씨가 그 작은 손을 자기 두 손으로 꼭 잡고, 그 갈색 눈에 어찌나 사랑을 가득 담아 메그를 내려다보던지, 그녀의 가슴이 두근거리기 시작했다. 도망쳐 버리고 싶으면서도 한편으론 멈춰 서서 듣고 싶었다."]], [["\"Oh no, please don't--I'd rather not,\"", "\"아, 아니에요, 제발 그러지 마세요."], ["she said, trying to withdraw her hand, and looking frightened in spite of her denial.", "안 듣는 게 낫겠어요.\" 손을 빼내려 애쓰며, 아니라고 하면서도 겁먹은 얼굴로 메그가 말했다."]], [["\"I won't trouble you, I only want to know if you care for me a little, Meg.", "\"성가시게 하진 않을게요. 그저 당신이 나를 조금이라도 마음에 두는지 알고 싶을 뿐이에요, 메그."], ["I love you so much, dear,\"", "난 당신을 너무나 사랑해요, 그대.\""], ["added Mr. Brooke tenderly.", "브룩 씨가 다정하게 덧붙였다."]], [["This was the moment for the calm, proper speech, but Meg didn't make it; she forgot every word of it, hung her head, and answered, \"I don't know,\"", "지금이야말로 그 차분하고 격식 있는 대사를 할 순간이었지만, 메그는 그러지 못했다."], ["so softly, that John had to stoop down to catch the foolish little reply.", "그 말을 한 마디도 빠짐없이 다 잊어버린 채 고개를 떨구고는, \"모르겠어요\"라고 어찌나 작게 대답하던지, 존은 그 어리석은 작은 대답을 들으려고 몸을 굽혀야 했다."]], [["He seemed to think it was worth the trouble, for he smiled to himself as if quite satisfied, pressed the plump hand gratefully, and said, in his most persuasive tone, \"Will you try and find out?", "그는 그게 그럴 만한 값어치가 있다고 여긴 듯, 더없이 흡족한 양 혼자 미소 지으며, 그 통통한 손을 고맙다는 듯 꼭 쥐고는, 더없이 설득력 있는 어조로 말했다. \"한번 알아봐 주겠어요?"], ["I want to know _so_ much; for I can't go to work with any heart until I learn whether I am to have my reward in the end or not.\"", "난 너무나 알고 싶어요. 내가 끝내 보상을 받게 될지 아닐지를 알기 전엔, 도무지 일에 마음을 붙일 수가 없으니까요.\""]], [["\"I'm too young,\"", "\"전 너무 어려요.\""], ["faltered Meg, wondering why she was so fluttered, yet rather enjoying it.", "메그가 더듬더듬 말했다. 왜 이렇게 가슴이 두근거리는지 의아하면서도, 한편으론 그게 제법 즐겁기도 했다."]], [["\"I'll wait; and in the meantime, you could be learning to like me.", "\"기다릴게요. 그동안 당신은 나를 좋아하는 법을 배워 갈 수 있을 테고요."], ["Would it be a very hard lesson, dear?\"", "그게 그렇게 어려운 공부일까요, 그대?\""]], [["\"Not if I chose to learn it, but--\"", "\"제가 배우기로 마음먹는다면 어렵진 않겠지만, 하지만……\""]], [["\"Please choose to learn, Meg.", "\"부디 배우기로 마음먹어 줘요, 메그."], ["I love to teach, and this is easier than German,\"", "난 가르치는 걸 좋아하는데, 이건 독일어보다 쉬우니까요.\""], ["broke in John, getting possession of the other hand, so that she had no way of hiding her face, as he bent to look into it.", "존이 끼어들며 다른 한 손마저 잡아 버려서, 그가 얼굴을 들여다보려고 몸을 굽혔을 때 메그는 얼굴을 가릴 길이 없었다."]], [["His tone was properly beseeching; but, stealing a shy look at him, Meg saw that his eyes were merry as well as tender, and that he wore the satisfied smile of one who had no doubt of his success.", "그의 어조는 더없이 간곡했다. 하지만 수줍게 그를 훔쳐보던 메그는, 그의 눈이 다정할 뿐 아니라 장난기로도 반짝이고, 자기 성공을 추호도 의심하지 않는 사람의 흡족한 미소를 띠고 있는 걸 보았다."], ["This nettled her; Annie Moffat's foolish lessons in coquetry came into her mind, and the love of power, which sleeps in the bosoms of the best of little women, woke up all of a sudden and took possession of her.", "이것이 메그의 화를 돋웠다. 애니 모팻이 가르쳐 준 그 어리석은 교태의 수법이 머릿속에 떠올랐고, 더없이 착한 작은 아씨들의 가슴속에도 잠들어 있는 그 군림하고 싶은 욕망이 별안간 깨어나 그녀를 사로잡았다."], ["She felt excited and strange, and, not knowing what else to do, followed a capricious impulse, and, withdrawing her hands, said petulantly, \"I _don't_ choose.", "메그는 흥분되고 묘한 기분이 들어, 달리 어쩔 줄을 몰라 변덕스러운 충동을 따라 두 손을 빼내고는 토라진 듯 말했다. \"전 마음먹지 않을 거예요."], ["Please go away and let me be!\"", "제발 가 주시고 절 좀 내버려 두세요!\""]], [["Poor Mr.", "가엾은 브룩 씨는 공중에 지어 올린 자신의 아름다운 성이 귓전에서 와르르 무너져 내리는 듯한 표정이었다."], ["Brooke looked as if his lovely castle in the air was tumbling about his ears, for he had never seen Meg in such a mood before, and it rather bewildered him.", "메그가 이런 기분에 빠진 모습을 한 번도 본 적이 없었던 그는, 적잖이 어리둥절했다."]], [["\"Do you really mean that?\"", "\"정말 진심으로 하는 말입니까?\""], ["he asked anxiously, following her as she walked away.", "그가 걸어가는 메그를 따라가며 근심스럽게 물었다."]], [["\"Yes, I do; I don't want to be worried about such things.", "\"네, 그래요. 이런 일로 시달리고 싶지 않아요."], ["Father says I needn't; it's too soon and I'd rather not.\"", "아버지도 그럴 필요 없다고 하셨고요. 너무 이르고, 전 그러고 싶지 않아요.\""]], [["\"Mayn't I hope you'll change your mind by and by?", "\"머지않아 마음이 바뀌기를 바라도 될까요?"], ["I'll wait, and say nothing till you have had more time.", "기다릴게요, 그리고 당신이 좀 더 시간을 가질 때까지 아무 말 안 할게요."], ["Don't play with me, Meg.", "날 가지고 놀지 말아요, 메그."], ["I didn't think that of you.\"", "당신이 그럴 줄은 몰랐어요.\""]], [["\"Don't think of me at all.", "\"제 생각은 아예 하지 마세요."], ["I'd rather you wouldn't,\"", "그러지 않으셨으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["said Meg, taking a naughty satisfaction in trying her lover's patience and her own power.", "연인의 인내심과 자기 자신의 힘을 시험해 보는 데서 짓궂은 만족을 느끼며 메그가 말했다."]], [["He was grave and pale now, and looked decidedly more like the novel heroes whom she admired; but he neither slapped his forehead nor tramped about the room, as they did; he just stood looking at her so wistfully, so tenderly, that she found her heart relenting in spite of her. What would have happened next I cannot say, if Aunt March had not come hobbling in at this interesting minute.", "이제 그는 진지하고 창백해져서, 그녀가 흠모하던 소설 속 영웅들을 한결 더 닮아 보였다. 그러나 그는 그들처럼 제 이마를 치지도, 방 안을 쿵쿵 거닐지도 않았다. 그저 서서 메그를 어찌나 애틋하게, 어찌나 다정하게 바라보던지, 메그는 자기도 모르게 마음이 누그러지는 걸 느꼈다. 그다음에 무슨 일이 일어났을지는 나도 모르겠다. 바로 이 흥미로운 순간에 마치 대고모가 절뚝거리며 들어오지 않았더라면 말이다."]], [["The old lady couldn't resist her longing to see her nephew; for she had met Laurie as she took her airing, and, hearing of Mr.", "노부인은 조카를 보고 싶은 마음을 도무지 억누를 수가 없었다. 바람을 쐬러 나갔다가 로리를 만나, 마치 씨가 돌아왔다는 소식을 듣고는 곧장 그를 보러 마차를 몰아온 것이다."], ["March's arrival, drove straight out to see him. The family were all busy in the back part of the house, and she had made her way quietly in, hoping to surprise them.", "식구들은 모두 집 뒤편에서 분주했고, 노부인은 그들을 깜짝 놀라게 해 줄 셈으로 조용히 안으로 들어섰다."], ["She did surprise two of them so much that Meg started as if she had seen a ghost, and Mr. Brooke vanished into the study.", "그런데 그 둘을 어찌나 깜짝 놀라게 했던지, 메그는 귀신이라도 본 듯 흠칫했고 브룩 씨는 서재로 사라져 버렸다."]], [["\"Bless me, what's all this?\"", "\"세상에, 이게 다 무슨 일이냐?\""], ["cried the old lady, with a rap of her cane, as she glanced from the pale young gentleman to the scarlet young lady.", "노부인이 지팡이를 탁 치며, 창백한 청년에게서 새빨개진 아가씨에게로 시선을 옮기며 외쳤다."]], [["\"It's father's friend.", "\"아버지 친구분이세요."], ["I'm _so_ surprised to see you!\"", "대고모님을 뵈니 너무 놀랐어요!\""], ["stammered Meg, feeling that she was in for a lecture now.", "이제 잔소리를 듣게 생겼다고 느끼며 메그가 더듬거렸다."]], [["\"That's evident,\"", "\"그건 한눈에 보이는구나.\""], ["returned Aunt March, sitting down.", "마치 대고모가 자리에 앉으며 대꾸했다."], ["\"But what is father's friend saying to make you look like a peony?", "\"그런데 아버지 친구가 무슨 말을 했길래 네가 작약처럼 빨개진 게냐?"], ["There's mischief going on, and I insist upon knowing what it is,\"", "무슨 수상한 일이 벌어지고 있어, 그게 뭔지 내 꼭 알아야겠다.\""], ["with another rap.", "그러면서 또 한 번 지팡이를 탁 쳤다."]], [["\"We were merely talking. Mr.", "\"그냥 이야기만 나누고 있었어요."], ["Brooke came for his umbrella,\"", "브룩 씨는 우산을 가지러 오셨고요.\""], ["began Meg, wishing that Mr. Brooke and the umbrella were safely out of the house.", "브룩 씨와 그 우산이 어서 무사히 집 밖으로 나가 있기를 바라며 메그가 입을 뗐다."]], [["\"Brooke?", "\"브룩?"], ["That boy's tutor? Ah!", "그 도련님의 가정교사 말이냐?"], ["I understand now.", "아! 이제 알겠다."], ["I know all about it.", "다 알겠어."], ["Jo blundered into a wrong message in one of your father's letters, and I made her tell me.", "조가 네 아버지 편지에 엉뚱한 전갈을 잘못 끼워 넣었길래, 내가 그 애한테 다 말하게 했지."], ["You haven't gone and accepted him, child?\"", "설마 그 사람을 받아들인 건 아니겠지, 얘야?\""], ["cried Aunt March, looking scandalized.", "마치 대고모가 기겁한 얼굴로 외쳤다."]], [["\"Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["he'll hear.", "들으시겠어요."], ["Sha'n't I call mother?\"", "엄마를 불러올까요?\""], ["said Meg, much troubled.", "메그가 몹시 난처해하며 말했다."]], [["\"Not yet.", "\"아직은 됐다."], ["I've something to say to you, and I must free my mind at once.", "너한테 할 말이 있어, 당장 속 시원히 털어놔야겠다."], ["Tell me, do you mean to marry this Cook?", "말해 보거라, 너 이 쿡인지 뭔지 하는 사람과 결혼할 셈이냐?"], ["If you do, not one penny of my money ever goes to you.", "만약 그런다면, 내 돈은 한 푼도 너한테 안 갈 줄 알아라."], ["Remember that, and be a sensible girl,\"", "그걸 명심하고, 분별 있는 아이가 되거라.\""], ["said the old lady impressively.", "노부인이 위엄 있게 말했다."]], [["Now Aunt March possessed in perfection the art of rousing the spirit of opposition in the gentlest people, and enjoyed doing it.", "그런데 마치 대고모는 더없이 온순한 사람의 가슴속에서도 반항심을 부추기는 재주를 완벽하게 지니고 있었고, 그렇게 하는 걸 즐겼다."], ["The best of us have a spice of perversity in us, especially when we are young and in love.", "우리 중에 가장 착한 사람도 청개구리 같은 구석이 조금은 있기 마련인데, 젊고 사랑에 빠졌을 때는 더더욱 그렇다."], ["If Aunt March had begged Meg to accept John Brooke, she would probably have declared she couldn't think of it; but as she was peremptorily ordered _not_ to like him, she immediately made up her mind that she would.", "만약 마치 대고모가 존 브룩을 받아들이라고 사정했더라면, 메그는 아마 그건 생각조차 할 수 없다고 잘라 말했을 것이다. 그러나 그를 좋아하지 말라고 단호하게 명령받자, 메그는 곧장 그러기로 마음을 굳혀 버렸다."], ["Inclination as well as perversity made the decision easy, and, being already much excited, Meg opposed the old lady with unusual spirit.", "마음이 그쪽으로 기운 데다 반항심까지 더해져 결심은 쉬웠고, 이미 한껏 흥분해 있던 메그는 유난한 기세로 노부인에게 맞섰다."]], [["\"I shall marry whom I please, Aunt March, and you can leave your money to any one you like,\"", "\"전 제가 원하는 사람과 결혼할 거예요, 대고모님. 그리고 대고모님 돈은 마음에 드는 누구에게든 물려주시면 돼요.\""], ["she said, nodding her head with a resolute air.", "메그가 단호한 기색으로 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["\"Highty tighty!", "\"흥, 별꼴이야!"], ["Is that the way you take my advice, miss?", "그게 내 충고를 받아들이는 태도냐, 아가씨?"], ["You'll be sorry for it, by and by, when you've tried love in a cottage, and found it a failure.\"", "두고 보거라, 오두막에서의 사랑이란 걸 겪어 보고 그게 실패라는 걸 알게 되면, 머지않아 후회할 테니.\""]], [["\"It can't be a worse one than some people find in big houses,\"", "\"큰 집에서 어떤 사람들이 겪는 것보다 더 나쁘진 않을 거예요.\""], ["retorted Meg.", "메그가 받아쳤다."]], [["Aunt March put on her glasses and took a look at the girl, for she did not know her in this new mood. Meg hardly knew herself, she felt so brave and independent,--so glad to defend John, and assert her right to love him, if she liked. Aunt March saw that she had begun wrong, and, after a little pause, made a fresh start, saying, as mildly as she could, \"Now, Meg, my dear, be reasonable, and take my advice. I mean it kindly, and don't want you to spoil your whole life by making a mistake at the beginning. You ought to marry well, and help your family; it's your duty to make a rich match, and it ought to be impressed upon you.\"", "마치 대고모는 안경을 쓰고 아가씨를 찬찬히 들여다보았다. 이런 새로운 모습의 메그는 도무지 알아볼 수가 없었던 것이다. 메그 자신도 자신이 낯설게 느껴졌다. 그만큼\n용감하고 당당하게 — 마음만 먹으면 존을 변호하고, 그를 사랑할 권리를 주장하게 되어 차라리 기뻤다. 마치 대고모는 시작을 잘못했다는 걸 깨닫고는, 잠시 뜸을 들인 후 다시 말을 꺼냈다. 가능한 한 부드러운 목소리로. \"메그, 얘야, 이성적으로 생각하고 내 충고를 받아들이렴. 다 너 잘되라고 하는 말이야. 첫 단추를 잘못 끼워서 네 인생 전체를 망치는 꼴은 보고 싶지 않아. 너는 좋은 곳으로 시집가서 가족을 도와야 해. 부잣집에 시집가는 게 네 의무이고, 그 사실을 분명히 새겨 두어야 한다.\""]], [["\"Father and mother don't think so; they like John, though he _is_ poor.\"", "\"엄마 아빠는 그렇게 생각하지 않으세요. 두 분은 존을 좋아하시는걸요. 그가 가난하긴 해도요.\""]], [["\"Your parents, my dear, have no more worldly wisdom than two babies.\"", "\"네 부모님은, 얘야, 갓난아기 둘만큼도 세상 물정을 모르는 분들이란다.\""]], [["\"I'm glad of it,\"", "\"그래서 다행이에요.\""], ["cried Meg stoutly.", "메그가 단호하게 외쳤다."]], [["Aunt March took no notice, but went on with her lecture.", "마치 대고모는 들은 척도 않고 잔소리를 계속했다."], ["\"This Rook is poor, and hasn't got any rich relations, has he?\"", "\"이 루크라는 남자는 가난한 데다 부자 친척도 하나 없지, 안 그러냐?\""]], [["\"No; but he has many warm friends.\"", "\"네. 하지만 그에게는 따뜻한 친구가 많아요.\""]], [["\"You can't live on friends; try it, and see how cool they'll grow.", "\"친구를 먹고 살 순 없어. 어디 한번 그래 보렴."], ["He hasn't any business, has he?\"", "친구들이 얼마나 쌀쌀맞게 식어 가는지 보게 될 테니. 그 사람, 사업도 없지?\""]], [["\"Not yet; Mr.", "\"아직은요."], ["Laurence is going to help him.\"", "로런스 씨가 도와주실 거예요.\""]], [["\"That won't last long.", "\"그것도 오래 못 갈 거다."], ["James Laurence is a crotchety old fellow, and not to be depended on.", "제임스 로런스는 괴팍한 늙은이라 믿을 만한 사람이 못 돼."], ["So you intend to marry a man without money, position, or business, and go on working harder than you do now, when you might be comfortable all your days by minding me and doing better?", "그러니까 너는 돈도, 지위도, 일자리도 없는 남자와 결혼해서, 지금보다 더 고되게 일하면서 살겠다는 거구나. 이 늙은이 말 잘 들어서 더 나은 길을 택하면 평생 편하게 살 수 있는데 말이야."], ["I thought you had more sense, Meg.\"", "나는 네가 좀 더 분별 있는 줄 알았다, 메그.\""]], [["\"I couldn't do better if I waited half my life!", "\"제 인생의 절반을 기다린대도 더 나은 사람은 못 만나요! 존은 착하고 현명해요."], ["John is good and wise; he's got heaps of talent; he's willing to work, and sure to get on, he's so energetic and brave.", "재능도 넘치고, 기꺼이 일하려 하고, 분명히 성공할 거예요. 그렇게 정력적이고 용감하니까요."], ["Every one likes and respects him, and I'm proud to think he cares for me, though I'm so poor and young and silly,\"", "다들 그를 좋아하고 존경해요. 저는 이렇게 가난하고 어리고 어리석은데도 그가 저를 마음에 두어 준다는 게 자랑스러워요.\""], ["said Meg, looking prettier than ever in her earnestness.", "메그가 말했다. 진심을 담아 말하는 그 모습이 그 어느 때보다 예뻐 보였다."]], [["\"He knows _you_ have got rich relations, child; that's the secret of his liking, I suspect.\"", "\"그 사람은 네게 부자 친척이 있다는 걸 알고 있어, 얘야. 그게 그 사람이 너를 좋아하는 속셈인 게지, 내 짐작엔.\""]], [["\"Aunt March, how dare you say such a thing?", "\"마치 대고모, 어떻게 그런 말씀을 하실 수 있어요?"], ["John is above such meanness, and I won't listen to you a minute if you talk so,\"", "존은 그런 비열한 짓을 할 사람이 아니에요. 그런 식으로 말씀하시면 한순간도 듣고 있지 않겠어요.\""], ["cried Meg indignantly, forgetting everything but the injustice of the old lady's suspicions.", "메그가 분개하며 외쳤다. 노부인의 부당한 의심 말고는 모든 것을 잊어버린 채였다."], ["\"My John wouldn't marry for money, anymore than I would.", "\"내 존은 저처럼 돈을 보고 결혼할 사람이 아니에요."], ["We are willing to work, and we mean to wait.", "우리는 기꺼이 일할 거고, 기다릴 작정이에요. 저는 가난을 두려워하지 않아요."], ["I'm not afraid of being poor, for I've been happy so far, and I know I shall be with him, because he loves me, and I--\"", "지금까지도 행복했고, 그 사람과 함께라면 앞으로도 행복할 거라는 걸 아니까요. 그는 저를 사랑하고, 저도—\""]], [["Meg stopped there, remembering all of a sudden that she hadn't made up her mind; that she had told \"her John\"", "메그는 거기서 말을 멈췄다."], ["to go away, and that he might be overhearing her inconsistent remarks.", "아직 마음을 정하지 못했다는 사실이, '자기 존'에게 떠나라고 말했다는 사실이, 그리고 그가 이 앞뒤 안 맞는 말을 엿듣고 있을지도 모른다는 사실이 문득 떠올랐기 때문이다."]], [["Aunt March was very angry, for she had set her heart on having her pretty niece make a fine match, and something in the girl's happy young face made the lonely old woman feel both sad and sour.", "마치 대고모는 몹시 화가 났다. 예쁜 조카가 번듯한 혼처에 시집가기를 마음속으로 바라고 있었던 탓이었다. 게다가 그 아이의 행복에 찬 젊은 얼굴에 어린 무언가가 외로운 노부인을 서글프고도 심술궂은 기분에 빠지게 했다."]], [["\"Well, I wash my hands of the whole affair!", "\"좋아, 이 일에서 난 손을 떼마!"], ["You are a wilful child, and you've lost more than you know by this piece of folly.", "너는 고집불통 아이고, 이 어리석은 짓으로 네가 아는 것보다 훨씬 많은 걸 잃은 거야."], ["No, I won't stop; I'm disappointed in you, and haven't spirits to see your father now.", "아니, 안 있어. 너한테 실망했으니, 지금은 네 아버지를 볼 기분도 아니다."], ["Don't expect anything from me when you are married; your Mr.", "결혼하고 나서 나한테는 아무것도 기대하지 마라."], ["Book's friends must take care of you.", "네 그 부크 씨인지 하는 사람의 친구들이 너를 돌봐 줘야겠지."], ["I'm done with you forever.\"", "너랑은 영영 끝이다.\""]], [["And, slamming the door in Meg's face, Aunt March drove off in high dudgeon.", "그러더니 마치 대고모는 메그의 면전에서 문을 쾅 닫고 잔뜩 화가 나서 마차를 몰고 떠나 버렸다."], ["She seemed to take all the girl's courage with her; for, when left alone, Meg stood a moment, undecided whether to laugh or cry.", "노부인이 그 아이의 용기를 죄다 가져가 버린 것 같았다. 혼자 남겨진 메그는 웃어야 할지 울어야 할지 결정하지 못한 채 한순간 우두커니 서 있었다."], ["Before she could make up her mind, she was taken possession of by Mr.", "그러나 미처 마음을 정하기도 전에 브룩 씨에게 사로잡히고 말았다. 그가 단숨에 말했다."], ["Brooke, who said, all in one breath, \"I couldn't help hearing, Meg.", "\"안 들으려 해도 들리는 걸 어떡해요, 메그. 나를 변호해 줘서 고마워요."], ["Thank you for defending me, and Aunt March for proving that you _do_ care for me a little bit.\"", "그리고 마치 대고모님께도 고맙군요. 당신이 나를 조금은 마음에 두고 있다는 걸 증명해 주셨으니.\""]], [["\"I didn't know how much, till she abused you,\"", "\"얼마나 마음에 두고 있었는지는 저도 몰랐어요. 대고모님이 당신을 헐뜯기 전까지는요.\""], ["began Meg.", "메그가 입을 열었다."]], [["\"And I needn't go away, but may stay and be happy, may I, dear?\"", "\"그럼 나는 떠나지 않아도 되고, 여기 남아 행복해도 되는 거죠, 그래도 되죠, 여보?\""]], [["Here was another fine chance to make the crushing speech and the stately exit, but Meg never thought of doing either, and disgraced herself forever in Jo's eyes by meekly whispering, \"Yes, John,\"", "여기서 또 한 번 단호한 말을 내뱉고 위엄 있게 퇴장할 좋은 기회가 있었지만, 메그는 둘 중 어느 쪽도 할 생각을 하지 못했다. 그러고는 조의 눈에 영원히 체면을 잃을 짓을 하고 말았으니, 순순히 \"네, 존\""], ["and hiding her face on Mr. Brooke's waistcoat.", "하고 속삭이며 브룩 씨의 조끼에 얼굴을 묻어 버린 것이다."]], [["Fifteen minutes after Aunt March's departure, Jo came softly down stairs, paused an instant at the parlor door, and, hearing no sound within, nodded and smiled, with a satisfied expression, saying to herself, \"She has sent him away as we planned, and that affair is settled. I'll go and hear the fun, and have a good laugh over it.\"", "마치 대고모가 떠난 지 십오 분 후, 조가 살그머니 아래층으로 내려와 거실 문 앞에서 잠시 멈춰 섰다. 안에서 아무 소리도 들리지 않자, 만족스러운 표정으로 고개를 끄덕이며 미소 지었다. 그러고는 혼잣말을 했다. \"우리가 계획한 대로 언니가 그 사람을 돌려보냈구나. 그 일은 마무리됐어. 가서 그 재미난 얘기나 들으면서 실컷 웃어야지.\""]], [["But poor Jo never got her laugh, for she was transfixed upon the threshold by a spectacle which held her there, staring with her mouth nearly as wide open as her eyes.", "하지만 가엾은 조는 끝내 웃지 못했다. 문지방 위에서 어떤 광경에 못 박힌 듯 굳어 버렸기 때문이다."], ["Going in to exult over a fallen enemy, and to praise a strong-minded sister for the banishment of an objectionable lover, it certainly _was_ a shock to behold the aforesaid enemy serenely sitting on the sofa, with the strong-minded sister enthroned upon his knee, and wearing an expression of the most abject submission.", "그 광경에 붙들린 채, 입을 눈만큼이나 떡 벌리고 멍하니 바라볼 수밖에 없었다. 패배한 적을 두고 기뻐하고, 못마땅한 연인을 내쫓은 의지 굳은 언니를 칭찬하려고 들어선 참이었다."], ["Jo gave a sort of gasp, as if a cold shower-bath had suddenly fallen upon her,--for such an unexpected turning of the tables actually took her breath away.", "그러니 그 적이라는 자가 소파에 태평하게 앉아 있고, 의지 굳은 언니가 그의 무릎 위에 옥좌에 오른 듯 앉아, 더없이 비굴하게 복종하는 표정을 짓고 있는 모습을 본 것은 정말이지 충격이었다. 조는 마치 갑자기 찬물 세례라도 뒤집어쓴 듯 숨이 턱 막혔다."], ["At the odd sound, the lovers turned and saw her.", "이렇게 예상치 못하게 형세가 뒤바뀌니 실제로 숨이 멎을 지경이었던 것이다. 그 묘한 소리에 두 연인이 돌아보고 그녀를 알아차렸다."], ["Meg jumped up, looking both proud and shy; but \"that man,\"", "메그가 벌떡 일어났다. 자랑스러우면서도 수줍은 표정이었다."], ["as Jo called him, actually laughed, and said coolly, as he kissed the astonished new-comer, \"Sister Jo, congratulate us!\"", "그러나 조가 '저 남자'라고 부르는 그자는 정말로 웃음을 터뜨리고는, 어안이 벙벙한 새 식구에게 입을 맞추며 태연하게 말했다. \"처제, 우리를 축하해 줘요!\""]], [["That was adding insult to injury,--it was altogether too much,--and, making some wild demonstration with her hands, Jo vanished without a word. Rushing upstairs, she startled the invalids by exclaiming tragically, as she burst into the room, \"Oh, _do_ somebody go down quick; John Brooke is acting dreadfully, and Meg likes it!\"", "이건 상처에 모욕을 더하는 격이었다. 도무지 견딜 수 없는 노릇이었다. 조는 두 손으로 알 수 없는 격한 몸짓을 하더니 한마디 말도 없이 사라져 버렸다. 위층으로 달려 올라간 그녀는 방으로 뛰어들며 비극적으로 외쳐 환자들을 놀라게 했다. \"오, 누가 좀 얼른 내려가 봐요. 존 브룩이 끔찍한 짓을 하고 있는데, 메그 언니가 좋아한다고요!\""]], [["Mr. and Mrs.", "마치 부부가 서둘러 방을 나섰다."], ["March left the room with speed; and, casting herself upon the bed, Jo cried and scolded tempestuously as she told the awful news to Beth and Amy.", "조는 침대에 몸을 던지고는 베스와 에이미에게 그 끔찍한 소식을 전하면서 폭풍처럼 울고 나무라기를 거듭했다."], ["The little girls, however, considered it a most agreeable and interesting event, and Jo got little comfort from them; so she went up to her refuge in the garret, and confided her troubles to the rats.", "그러나 어린 두 동생은 그것을 더없이 즐겁고 흥미로운 사건으로 여겼다. 조는 동생들에게서 별 위안을 얻지 못하자, 다락방의 은신처로 올라가 쥐들에게 자신의 고민을 털어놓았다."]], [["Nobody ever knew what went on in the parlor that afternoon; but a great deal of talking was done, and quiet Mr.", "그날 오후 거실에서 무슨 일이 있었는지는 아무도 알지 못했다."], ["Brooke astonished his friends by the eloquence and spirit with which he pleaded his suit, told his plans, and persuaded them to arrange everything just as he wanted it.", "그러나 무척 많은 이야기가 오갔고, 과묵한 브룩 씨는 청혼을 호소하고 자신의 계획을 밝히며, 모든 일을 자기가 원하는 대로 정하도록 설득하는 그 웅변과 패기로 친구들을 놀라게 했다."]], [["The tea-bell rang before he had finished describing the paradise which he meant to earn for Meg, and he proudly took her in to supper, both looking so happy that Jo hadn't the heart to be jealous or dismal.", "그가 메그를 위해 일구겠다는 낙원을 미처 다 그려 보이기도 전에 차 마실 시간을 알리는 종이 울렸다. 그는 자랑스럽게 그녀를 데리고 저녁 식탁으로 갔다."], ["Amy was very much impressed by John's devotion and Meg's dignity.", "두 사람이 어찌나 행복해 보이던지 조도 차마 질투하거나 침울해할 마음이 들지 않았다."], ["Beth beamed at them from a distance, while Mr. and Mrs.", "에이미는 존의 헌신과 메그의 기품에 깊은 감명을 받았다."], ["March surveyed the young couple with such tender satisfaction that it was perfectly evident Aunt March was right in calling them as \"unworldly as a pair of babies.\"", "베스는 멀찍이서 두 사람을 향해 환히 웃었고, 마치 부부는 그 젊은 한 쌍을 어찌나 다정하고 흐뭇하게 바라보았던지, 마치 대고모가 두 사람을 '갓난아기 한 쌍처럼 세상 물정 모른다'고 한 말이 옳았음이 확연히 드러났다."], ["No one ate much, but every one looked very happy, and the old room seemed to brighten up amazingly when the first romance of the family began there.", "다들 많이 먹지는 못했지만 모두가 무척 행복해 보였고, 이 집안 최초의 로맨스가 그곳에서 시작되니 그 오래된 방이 놀라우리만치 환해지는 것 같았다."]], [["\"You can't say nothing pleasant ever happens now, can you, Meg?\"", "\"이제 좋은 일이라곤 도통 일어나지 않는다는 말은 못 하겠지, 언니?\""], ["said Amy, trying to decide how she would group the lovers in the sketch she was planning to take.", "에이미가 말했다. 그녀는 그리려고 마음먹은 스케치 속에 두 연인을 어떻게 배치할지 궁리하는 중이었다."]], [["\"No, I'm sure I can't.", "\"그래, 정말 못 하겠다."], ["How much has happened since I said that!", "내가 그 말을 한 뒤로 얼마나 많은 일이 일어났는지!"], ["It seems a year ago,\"", "일 년 전 일처럼 느껴지는걸.\""], ["answered Meg, who was in a blissful dream, lifted far above such common things as bread and butter.", "메그가 대답했다. 그녀는 더없이 행복한 꿈에 잠겨, 빵이니 버터니 하는 그런 평범한 것들 따위는 까마득히 초월한 상태였다."]], [["\"The joys come close upon the sorrows this time, and I rather think the changes have begun,\"", "\"이번에는 기쁜 일이 슬픈 일에 바짝 잇따라 찾아오는구나. 변화가 시작된 것 같아.\""], ["said Mrs.", "마치 부인이 말했다."], ["March.", "\"대부분의 가정에는 이따금 사건으로 가득한 한 해가 찾아온단다."], ["\"In most families there comes, now and then, a year full of events; this has been such an one, but it ends well, after all.\"", "올해가 바로 그런 해였지. 하지만 결국 좋게 끝나니 다행이야.\""]], [["\"Hope the next will end better,\"", "\"내년은 더 좋게 끝났으면 좋겠네요.\" 조가 중얼거렸다."], ["muttered Jo, who found it very hard to see Meg absorbed in a stranger before her face; for Jo loved a few persons very dearly, and dreaded to have their affection lost or lessened in any way.", "메그가 눈앞에서 낯선 남자에게 빠져 있는 모습을 지켜보기가 무척 괴로웠던 것이다. 조는 몇몇 사람을 무척 애틋하게 사랑했고, 그 애정이 어떤 식으로든 사라지거나 옅어지는 것을 두려워했다."]], [["\"I hope the third year from this _will_ end better; I mean it shall, if I live to work out my plans,\"", "\"지금부터 삼 년째 되는 해는 분명 더 좋게 끝날 겁니다. 그렇게 만들 거예요."], ["said Mr.", "제가 살아서 계획을 이뤄 낸다면요.\""], ["Brooke, smiling at Meg, as if everything had become possible to him now.", "브룩 씨가 메그를 보며 미소 지으며 말했다. 마치 이제 자기에게는 모든 일이 가능해진 듯한 표정이었다."]], [["\"Doesn't it seem very long to wait?\"", "\"기다리기엔 너무 긴 시간 아니에요?\""], ["asked Amy, who was in a hurry for the wedding.", "에이미가 물었다. 그녀는 결혼식을 보고 싶어 안달이 나 있었다."]], [["\"I've got so much to learn before I shall be ready, it seems a short time to me,\"", "\"준비가 되기까지 배워야 할 게 너무 많아서, 나한텐 짧은 시간으로 느껴지는걸.\""], ["answered Meg, with a sweet gravity in her face, never seen there before.", "메그가 대답했다. 그 얼굴에는 전에는 본 적 없는 다정한 진지함이 어려 있었다."]], [["\"You have only to wait; _I_ am to do the work,\" said John, beginning his labors by picking up Meg's napkin, with an expression which caused Jo to shake her head, and then say to herself, with an air of relief, as the front door banged, \"Here comes Laurie. Now we shall have a little sensible conversation.\"", "\"당신은 기다리기만 하면 돼요. 일은 내가 할 테니까요.\" 존이 말하며, 그 노고의 첫걸음으로 메그의 냅킨을 주워 올렸다. 그 표정에 조는 고개를 절레절레 흔들었다. 그러고는 현관문이 쾅 닫히는 소리에 마음이 놓인다는 듯 혼잣말을 했다. \"로리가 오는군. 이제야 분별 있는 대화를 좀 나눌 수 있겠어.\""]], [["But Jo was mistaken; for Laurie came prancing in, overflowing with spirits, bearing a great bridal-looking bouquet for \"Mrs.", "하지만 조의 생각은 빗나갔다. 로리가 의기양양하게 활기를 뿜으며 깡충깡충 들어왔으니까."], ["John Brooke,\" and evidently laboring under the delusion that the whole affair had been brought about by his excellent management.", "'존 브룩 부인'에게 줄 신부에게나 어울릴 법한 커다란 꽃다발을 들고서, 이 모든 일이 자신의 훌륭한 솜씨로 성사되었다는 착각에 단단히 빠져 있는 게 분명했다."]], [["\"I knew Brooke would have it all his own way, he always does; for when he makes up his mind to accomplish anything, it's done, though the sky falls,\"", "\"브룩이 자기 뜻대로 다 해낼 줄 알았어. 그 친구는 늘 그렇거든."], ["said Laurie, when he had presented his offering and his congratulations.", "무슨 일이든 이루겠다고 마음먹으면, 하늘이 무너져도 해내고 마니까.\" 로리가 선물과 축하 인사를 건넨 뒤 말했다."]], [["\"Much obliged for that recommendation.", "\"그렇게 추천해 줘서 정말 고맙군."], ["I take it as a good omen for the future, and invite you to my wedding on the spot,\"", "미래에 대한 좋은 징조로 받아들이고, 그 자리에서 자네를 내 결혼식에 초대하겠네.\""], ["answered Mr.", "브룩 씨가 대답했다."], ["Brooke, who felt at peace with all mankind, even his mischievous pupil.", "그는 온 인류와, 심지어 자신의 장난꾸러기 제자와도 평화로운 기분이었다."]], [["\"I'll come if I'm at the ends of the earth; for the sight of Jo's face alone, on that occasion, would be worth a long journey.", "\"제가 땅끝에 있더라도 가겠습니다. 그날 조의 얼굴만 봐도 먼 길 갈 가치가 충분할 테니까요."], ["You don't look festive, ma'am; what's the matter?\"", "표정이 영 즐거워 보이지 않네, 아가씨."], ["asked Laurie, following her into a corner of the parlor, whither all had adjourned to greet Mr.", "무슨 일이야?\" 로리가 물으며 그녀를 따라 거실 한구석으로 갔다."], ["Laurence.", "다들 로런스 씨를 맞이하러 자리를 옮긴 참이었다."]], [["\"I don't approve of the match, but I've made up my mind to bear it, and shall not say a word against it,\"", "\"난 이 결혼이 마음에 안 들지만, 참기로 마음먹었으니 반대하는 말은 한마디도 안 할 거야.\" 조가 엄숙하게 말했다."], ["said Jo solemnly. \"You can't know how hard it is for me to give up Meg,\"", "\"메그 언니를 떠나보내는 게 나한테 얼마나 힘든 일인지 넌 모를 거야.\""], ["she continued, with a little quiver in her voice.", "그녀가 목소리를 가늘게 떨며 말을 이었다."]], [["\"You don't give her up.", "\"언니를 떠나보내는 게 아니야."], ["You only go halves,\"", "그냥 반으로 나누는 것뿐이지.\""], ["said Laurie consolingly.", "로리가 위로하듯 말했다."]], [["\"It never can be the same again.", "\"다시는 예전 같을 수 없어."], ["I've lost my dearest friend,\"", "가장 소중한 친구를 잃은 거라고.\""], ["sighed Jo.", "조가 한숨을 쉬었다."]], [["\"You've got me, anyhow.", "\"어쨌든 나한테는 네가 있잖아."], ["I'm not good for much, I know; but I'll stand by you, Jo, all the days of my life; upon my word I will!\"", "내가 변변치 못한 거 나도 알아. 하지만 나는 평생토록 네 곁을 지킬 거야, 조."], ["and Laurie meant what he said.", "맹세코 그럴게!\" 로리는 진심으로 한 말이었다."]], [["\"I know you will, and I'm ever so much obliged; you are always a great comfort to me, Teddy,\"", "\"네가 그러리라는 거 알아. 정말 고마워."], ["returned Jo, gratefully shaking hands.", "너는 늘 나한테 큰 위안이 돼, 테디.\" 조가 고마운 마음으로 그의 손을 잡으며 대답했다."]], [["\"Well, now, don't be dismal, there's a good fellow.", "\"자, 그러니까 침울해하지 마, 착하지."], ["It's all right, you see.", "다 잘된 일이잖아. 봐."], ["Meg is happy; Brooke will fly round and get settled immediately; grandpa will attend to him, and it will be very jolly to see Meg in her own little house.", "메그는 행복하고, 브룩은 부지런히 움직여서 금방 자리 잡을 거야. 할아버지가 그를 챙겨 주실 거고, 메그가 자기만의 작은 집에 사는 모습을 보면 무척 흐뭇할 거라고."], ["We'll have capital times after she is gone, for I shall be through college before long, and then we'll go abroad, or some nice trip or other.", "언니가 떠나도 우린 신나게 지낼 수 있어. 나도 머지않아 대학을 마칠 테니, 그러면 우리 외국에 가거나 어디 근사한 여행이라도 떠나자."], ["Wouldn't that console you?\"", "그러면 위로가 되지 않겠어?\""]], [["\"I rather think it would; but there's no knowing what may happen in three years,\"", "\"그럴 것도 같네. 하지만 삼 년 안에 무슨 일이 일어날지 누가 알겠어.\""], ["said Jo thoughtfully.", "조가 생각에 잠겨 말했다."]], [["\"That's true.", "\"그건 그래."], ["Don't you wish you could take a look forward, and see where we shall all be then?", "너는 앞일을 미리 내다보고서 우리 모두가 그때 어디에 있을지 보고 싶지 않아? 나는 그래.\""], ["I do,\" returned Laurie.", "로리가 대답했다."]], [["\"I think not, for I might see something sad; and every one looks so happy now, I don't believe they could be much improved,\" and Jo's eyes went slowly round the room, brightening as they looked, for the prospect was a pleasant one.", "\"난 안 보고 싶어. 슬픈 걸 보게 될지도 모르니까. 그리고 지금 다들 너무 행복해 보여서, 이보다 더 나아질 수는 없을 것 같거든.\" 조의 눈길이 천천히 방 안을 돌았다. 보는 대로 눈빛이 환해졌으니, 눈앞에 펼쳐진 광경이 흐뭇했기 때문이다."]], [["Father and mother sat together, quietly re-living the first chapter of the romance which for them began some twenty years ago.", "아버지와 어머니가 나란히 앉아, 한 이십 년 전 두 사람에게 시작된 로맨스의 첫 장을 조용히 다시 살아 보고 있었다. 에이미는 두 연인을 그리고 있었다."], ["Amy was drawing the lovers, who sat apart in a beautiful world of their own, the light of which touched their faces with a grace the little artist could not copy.", "그들은 자기들만의 아름다운 세계에 떨어져 앉아 있었고, 그 세계의 빛이 어린 화가로서는 도저히 옮겨 그릴 수 없는 우아함으로 두 사람의 얼굴을 어루만지고 있었다."], ["Beth lay on her sofa, talking cheerily with her old friend, who held her little hand as if he felt that it possessed the power to lead him along the peaceful way she walked.", "베스는 소파에 누워 오랜 벗과 즐겁게 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 벗은 그녀의 작은 손을, 마치 그 손에 그녀가 걷는 평화로운 길로 자신을 이끌어 줄 힘이 있다고 느끼는 듯 꼭 쥐고 있었다."], ["Jo lounged in her favorite low seat, with the grave, quiet look which best became her; and Laurie, leaning on the back of her chair, his chin on a level with her curly head, smiled with his friendliest aspect, and nodded at her in the long glass which reflected them both.", "조는 자신이 가장 좋아하는 낮은 자리에 비스듬히 기대어 앉아 있었다. 그녀에게 가장 잘 어울리는 진지하고 차분한 표정으로."], ["* * * * *", "그리고 로리는 그녀의 의자 등받이에 기대어, 턱을 그녀의 곱슬머리와 같은 높이에 둔 채, 더없이 다정한 표정으로 미소 지으며, 두 사람을 함께 비추고 있는 기다란 거울 속 그녀를 향해 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["So grouped, the curtain falls upon Meg, Jo, Beth, and Amy.", "이렇게 한자리에 모인 메그, 조, 베스, 그리고 에이미 위로 막이 내린다."], ["Whether it ever rises again, depends upon the reception given to the first act of the domestic drama called \"LITTLE WOMEN.\"", "그 막이 다시 오를지 어떨지는, 「작은 아씨들」이라 불리는 가정극의 제1막이 어떤 반응을 얻느냐에 달려 있다. 스물넷"]], [["The Second Part", "이런저런 이야기"]], [["In order that we may start afresh, and go to Meg's wedding with free minds, it will be well to begin with a little gossip about the Marches.", "다시 새롭게 시작하여 홀가분한 마음으로 메그의 결혼식에 가려면, 마치 가족에 관한 잡담을 좀 늘어놓는 것으로 시작하는 게 좋겠다."], ["And here let me premise, that if any of the elders think there is too much \"lovering\"", "그리고 여기서 미리 밝혀 두자면, 혹시 어른들 가운데 누군가 이 이야기에 '연애'가 너무 많다고 여긴다면(아무래도 그럴 것 같아 걱정이다."], ["in the story, as I fear they may (I'm not afraid the young folks will make that objection), I can only say with Mrs.", "젊은 독자들이 그런 트집을 잡으리라고는 걱정하지 않지만), 나는 마치 부인의 말로 답할 수밖에 없다."], ["March, \"What _can_ you expect when I have four gay girls in the house, and a dashing young neighbor over the way?\"", "\"집 안에 발랄한 처녀가 넷이나 있고, 길 건너에 활기찬 젊은 이웃까지 있는데, 그럼 무얼 기대하겠어요?\""]], [["The three years that have passed have brought but few changes to the quiet family. The war is over, and Mr.", "지난 삼 년은 이 조용한 가족에게 변화를 거의 가져오지 않았다."], ["March safely at home, busy with his books and the small parish which found in him a minister by nature as by grace,--a quiet, studious man, rich in the wisdom that is better than learning, the charity which calls all mankind \"brother,\"", "전쟁은 끝났고 마치 씨는 무사히 집에 돌아와, 책과 작은 교구 일에 분주했다. 그 교구는 그에게서, 은총으로뿐 아니라 천성으로도 목사인 사람을 발견한 터였다."], ["the piety that blossoms into character, making it august and lovely.", "그는 조용하고 학구적인 사람으로, 학식보다 나은 지혜와, 온 인류를 '형제'라 부르는 자비심과, 인격으로 피어나 그 인격을 위엄 있고 사랑스럽게 만드는 경건함이 풍부한 사람이었다."]], [["These attributes, in spite of poverty and the strict integrity which shut him out from the more worldly successes, attracted to him many admirable persons, as naturally as sweet herbs draw bees, and as naturally he gave them the honey into which fifty years of hard experience had distilled no bitter drop. Earnest young men found the gray-headed scholar as young at heart as they; thoughtful or troubled women instinctively brought their doubts and sorrows to him, sure of finding the gentlest sympathy, the wisest counsel; sinners told their sins to the pure-hearted old man, and were both rebuked and saved; gifted men found a companion in him; ambitious men caught glimpses of nobler ambitions than their own; and even worldlings confessed that his beliefs were beautiful and true, although \"they wouldn't pay.\"", "이러한 자질은, 가난과, 더 세속적인 성공으로부터 그를 멀어지게 한 엄격한 정직함에도 불구하고, 향긋한 풀이 벌을 끌어들이듯 자연스럽게 수많은 훌륭한 사람들을 그에게로 이끌었다. 그리고 그 역시 자연스럽게 그들에게 꿀을 내주었으니, 오십 년에 걸친 고된 경험이 빚어 낸 그 꿀에는 쓴 방울이 한 점도 섞여 있지 않았다. 진지한 젊은이들은 그 백발의 학자가 자기들 못지않게 젊은 마음을 지녔음을 알아보았다. 사려 깊거나 괴로움에 빠진 여인들은 본능적으로 그에게 자신의 의혹과 슬픔을 가져왔다. 더없이 다정한 공감과 더없이 현명한 조언을 분명 얻으리라 믿으면서. 죄인들은 이 순결한 마음의 노인에게 자신의 죄를 고백했고, 꾸짖음과 구원을 함께 받았다. 재능 있는 사람들은 그에게서 벗을 찾았다. 야심 있는 사람들은 자신의 것보다 고귀한 야망을 어렴풋이 엿보았다. 그리고 세속적인 사람들조차 그의 신념이 '돈은 안 될지언정' 아름답고 참되다는 것을 인정했다."]], [["To outsiders, the five energetic women seemed to rule the house, and so they did in many things; but the quiet scholar, sitting among his books, was still the head of the family, the household conscience, anchor, and comforter; for to him the busy, anxious women always turned in troublous times, finding him, in the truest sense of those sacred words, husband and father.", "바깥 사람들에게는 그 활기찬 다섯 여인이 집안을 다스리는 것처럼 보였고, 실제로 많은 일에서 그러했다. 그러나 책들 사이에 앉아 있는 그 조용한 학자가 여전히 한 집안의 가장이요, 집안의 양심이며, 닻이고, 위로하는 이였다. 분주하고 근심 많은 여인들은 곤란한 때면 언제나 그에게로 향했고, 그 거룩한 말의 가장 참된 의미에서 남편이자 아버지인 그를 발견했으니까."]], [["The girls gave their hearts into their mother's keeping, their souls into their father's; and to both parents, who lived and labored so faithfully for them, they gave a love that grew with their growth, and bound them tenderly together by the sweetest tie which blesses life and outlives death.", "딸들은 마음은 어머니의 보살핌에, 영혼은 아버지의 보살핌에 맡겼다. 그리고 자신들을 위해 그토록 충실히 살고 애쓰는 두 부모에게, 딸들은 자라남과 더불어 함께 자라난 사랑을 바쳤다. 그 사랑은 삶을 축복하고 죽음보다도 오래 살아남는 더없이 달콤한 끈으로 그들을 다정하게 한데 묶어 주었다."]], [["Mrs. March is as brisk and cheery, though rather grayer, than when we saw her last, and just now so absorbed in Meg's affairs that the hospitals and homes, still full of wounded \"boys\"", "마치 부인은 우리가 마지막으로 보았을 때보다 머리가 좀 더 세긴 했지만 여전히 활기차고 명랑했다."], ["and soldiers' widows, decidedly miss the motherly missionary's visits.", "다만 지금은 메그의 일에 어찌나 골몰해 있는지, 여전히 부상당한 '병사들'과 군인의 미망인들로 가득한 병원과 보호소에서는 어머니 같은 그 선교사의 방문이 끊겨 확연히 아쉬워하고 있었다."]], [["John Brooke did his duty manfully for a year, got wounded, was sent home, and not allowed to return.", "존 브룩은 일 년 동안 사내답게 의무를 다하다가 부상을 입어 집으로 돌려보내졌고, 복귀를 허락받지 못했다."], ["He received no stars or bars, but he deserved them, for he cheerfully risked all he had; and life and love are very precious when both are in full bloom.", "그는 훈장도 계급장도 받지 못했지만 받아 마땅한 사람이었다."], ["Perfectly resigned to his discharge, he devoted himself to getting well, preparing for business, and earning a home for Meg.", "자신이 가진 모든 것을 기꺼이 걸었으니까. 그리고 삶과 사랑이 둘 다 한창 피어날 때면 그 둘은 무척 소중한 법이다."], ["With the good sense and sturdy independence that characterized him, he refused Mr.", "그는 제대를 흔쾌히 받아들이고는, 건강을 되찾고, 일을 준비하고, 메그를 위해 보금자리를 마련하는 데 몰두했다."], ["Laurence's more generous offers, and accepted the place of book-keeper feeling better satisfied to begin with an honestly-earned salary, than by running any risks with borrowed money.", "그를 특징짓는 분별력과 굳건한 독립심으로, 그는 로런스 씨의 더 후한 제안을 거절하고 경리 자리를 받아들였다. 빌린 돈으로 위험을 무릅쓰느니, 정직하게 번 봉급으로 시작하는 편이 더 만족스러웠던 것이다."]], [["Meg had spent the time in working as well as waiting, growing womanly in character, wise in housewifely arts, and prettier than ever; for love is a great beautifier.", "메그는 그동안 기다리면서도 부지런히 일하며 시간을 보냈다. 인격은 여인다워졌고, 살림 솜씨에 현명해졌으며, 그 어느 때보다 예뻐졌다."], ["She had her girlish ambitions and hopes, and felt some disappointment at the humble way in which the new life must begin.", "사랑은 대단한 미용사니까. 그녀에게도 소녀다운 야망과 희망이 있었기에, 새 삶을 이토록 초라하게 시작해야 한다는 데 다소 실망스럽기도 했다."], ["Ned Moffat had just married Sallie Gardiner, and Meg couldn't help contrasting their fine house and carriage, many gifts, and splendid outfit, with her own, and secretly wishing she could have the same.", "네드 모팻이 막 샐리 가드너와 결혼한 참이라, 메그는 그들의 멋진 집과 마차, 숱한 선물과 화려한 혼수를 자기 것과 견주어 보지 않을 수 없었고, 자기도 그런 걸 가질 수 있다면 하고 남몰래 바랐다."], ["But somehow envy and discontent soon vanished when she thought of all the patient love and labor John had put into the little home awaiting her; and when they sat together in the twilight, talking over their small plans, the future always grew so beautiful and bright that she forgot Sallie's splendor, and felt herself the richest, happiest girl in Christendom.", "그러나 어찌 된 일인지, 존이 자기를 기다리는 그 작은 집에 쏟아부은 그 모든 끈기 있는 사랑과 수고를 떠올리면 부러움도 불만도 이내 사라졌다. 그리고 둘이 황혼 녘에 나란히 앉아 자기들의 소박한 계획을 이야기하다 보면, 미래는 언제나 어찌나 아름답고 환하게 펼쳐지던지, 메그는 샐리의 화려함 따위는 잊어버리고 자신이 온 기독교 세계에서 가장 부유하고 행복한 처녀라고 느끼는 것이었다."]], [["Jo never went back to Aunt March, for the old lady took such a fancy to Amy that she bribed her with the offer of drawing lessons from one of the best teachers going; and for the sake of this advantage, Amy would have served a far harder mistress.", "조는 마치 대고모에게 다시 가지 않았다. 노부인이 에이미를 어찌나 마음에 들어 했던지, 당대 최고의 선생들 중 한 명에게 그림 수업을 받게 해 주겠다는 제안으로 에이미를 구슬렸기 때문이다."], ["So she gave her mornings to duty, her afternoons to pleasure, and prospered finely.", "그 혜택을 위해서라면 에이미는 훨씬 더 까다로운 주인이라도 모셨을 것이다. 그래서 에이미는 오전은 의무에, 오후는 즐거움에 바치며 훌륭하게 잘 지냈다."], ["Jo, meantime, devoted herself to literature and Beth, who remained delicate long after the fever was a thing of the past.", "한편 조는 문학에, 그리고 베스에게 헌신했다. 베스는 열병이 다 지나간 후로도 오래도록 허약한 채였다."], ["Not an invalid exactly, but never again the rosy, healthy creature she had been; yet always hopeful, happy, and serene, busy with the quiet duties she loved, every one's friend, and an angel in the house, long before those who loved her most had learned to know it.", "딱히 환자라고는 할 수 없었지만, 예전의 그 발그레하고 건강한 아이로 다시는 돌아가지 못했다. 그래도 늘 희망에 차 행복하고 평온했으며, 자기가 좋아하는 조용한 일들로 분주했고, 모두의 벗이었으며, 그녀를 가장 사랑하는 이들이 그 사실을 채 알아차리기도 훨씬 전부터 이미 그 집안의 천사였다."]], [["As long as \"The Spread Eagle\" paid her a dollar a column for her \"rubbish,\"", "「독수리 깃발」이 그녀가 '쓰레기'라 부르는 글에 한 단 당 1달러를 쳐주는 한, 조는 자신을 재력가로 여기며 부지런히 자잘한 로맨스를 지어냈다."], ["as she called it, Jo felt herself a woman of means, and spun her little romances diligently.", "그러나 분주한 머리와 야심 찬 마음속에서는 원대한 계획이 끓어오르고 있었고, 다락방의 낡은 양철 부엌에는 잉크 얼룩진 원고 더미가 조금씩 쌓여 가고 있었다."], ["But great plans fermented in her busy brain and ambitious mind, and the old tin kitchen in the garret held a slowly increasing pile of blotted manuscript, which was one day to place the name of March upon the roll of fame.", "그것은 언젠가 마치라는 이름을 명성의 명부에 올려놓게 될 것이었다."]], [["Laurie, having dutifully gone to college to please his grandfather, was now getting through it in the easiest possible manner to please himself. A universal favorite, thanks to money, manners, much talent, and the kindest heart that ever got its owner into scrapes by trying to get other people out of them, he stood in great danger of being spoilt, and probably would have been, like many another promising boy, if he had not possessed a talisman against evil in the memory of the kind old man who was bound up in his success, the motherly friend who watched over him as if he were her son, and last, but not least by any means, the knowledge that four innocent girls loved, admired, and believed in him with all their hearts.", "로리는 할아버지를 기쁘게 해 드리려고 의무적으로 대학에 들어가긴 했지만, 이제는 자기 자신을 기쁘게 하려고 가능한 한 가장 수월한 방식으로 학업을 헤쳐 나가고 있었다. 돈과 매너와 넘치는 재능, 그리고 남을 곤경에서 빼내려다 정작 자기가 곤경에 빠지곤 하는 더없이 친절한 마음 덕분에 그는 누구에게나 인기 있었다. 그런 만큼 버릇이 잘못 들 위험이 컸고, 여느 장래가 촉망되는 소년들이 그러하듯 실제로 그렇게 되었을지도 모른다. 만일 그에게 악을 막아 줄 부적이 없었더라면 말이다. 그 부적이란, 그의 성공에 자기 인생을 건 친절한 노인에 대한 기억과, 자기 친아들인 양 그를 지켜봐 주는 어머니 같은 벗과, 그리고 무엇보다도 결코 사소하지 않은, 순진한 네 소녀가 진심을 다해 자신을 사랑하고 우러르며 믿어 준다는 사실이었다."]], [["Being only \"a glorious human boy,\" of course he frolicked and flirted, grew dandified, aquatic, sentimental, or gymnastic, as college fashions ordained; hazed and was hazed, talked slang, and more than once came perilously near suspension and expulsion.", "그저 '근사한 인간 소년'에 지나지 않았으니, 그는 당연히 까불고 시시덕거렸고, 대학의 유행이 명하는 대로 멋쟁이가 되었다가, 보트를 탔다가, 감상에 젖었다가, 운동에 빠졌다가 했다."], ["But as high spirits and the love of fun were the causes of these pranks, he always managed to save himself by frank confession, honorable atonement, or the irresistible power of persuasion which he possessed in perfection.", "신입생을 괴롭히기도 하고 자기도 괴롭힘을 당했으며, 속어를 써 대고, 한두 번이 아니게 정학과 퇴학의 위태로운 문턱까지 갔다. 그러나 이런 장난들의 원인이 넘치는 혈기와 재미를 좋아하는 마음이었던 만큼, 그는 늘 솔직한 고백이나 떳떳한 속죄, 또는 그가 완벽하게 지닌 거부할 수 없는 설득력으로 곤경을 모면해 냈다."], ["In fact, he rather prided himself on his narrow escapes, and liked to thrill the girls with graphic accounts of his triumphs over wrathful tutors, dignified professors, and vanquished enemies. The \"men of my class\"", "사실 그는 자신이 아슬아슬하게 위기를 넘긴 일들을 은근히 자랑스러워했고, 노발대발하는 조교들과 점잖은 교수들, 무릎 꿇린 적들을 상대로 거둔 승리를 생생하게 들려주며 소녀들을 짜릿하게 해 주기를 즐겼다."], ["were heroes in the eyes of the girls, who never wearied of the exploits of \"our fellows,\" and were frequently allowed to bask in the smiles of these great creatures, when Laurie brought them home with him.", "'우리 학년 친구들'은 소녀들 눈에 영웅이었고, 소녀들은 '우리 패거리'의 무용담에 결코 싫증 내는 법이 없었으며, 로리가 그들을 집에 데려오면 종종 이 대단한 인물들의 미소를 쬐는 영광을 누리곤 했다."]], [["Amy especially enjoyed this high honor, and became quite a belle among them; for her ladyship early felt and learned to use the gift of fascination with which she was endowed.", "특히 에이미가 이 대단한 영광을 즐겨, 그들 사이에서 제법 인기 있는 아가씨가 되었다. 우리 아가씨께서는 자신에게 타고난 매혹의 재능을 일찍부터 알아차리고 부려 먹는 법을 익혔으니까."], ["Meg was too much absorbed in her private and particular John to care for any other lords of creation, and Beth too shy to do more than peep at them, and wonder how Amy dared to order them about so; but Jo felt quite in her element, and found it very difficult to refrain from imitating the gentlemanly attitudes, phrases, and feats, which seemed more natural to her than the decorums prescribed for young ladies.", "메그는 자신만의 특별한 존에게 너무 푹 빠져 있어서 다른 어떤 만물의 영장에게도 관심이 없었고, 베스는 너무 수줍어서 그들을 슬쩍 엿보며 에이미가 어떻게 그들을 저렇게 마음대로 부릴 수 있는지 신기해하는 것 말고는 더 하지 못했다. 그러나 조는 완전히 제 세상을 만난 기분이었고, 신사다운 자세며 말투며 재주를 따라 하고 싶은 마음을 억누르기가 무척 힘들었다."], ["They all liked Jo immensely, but never fell in love with her, though very few escaped without paying the tribute of a sentimental sigh or two at Amy's shrine.", "그녀에게는 그런 것들이 젊은 숙녀에게 정해진 예법보다 더 자연스러웠다. 다들 조를 무척 좋아했지만 사랑에 빠지지는 않았다."], ["And speaking of sentiment brings us very naturally to the \"Dove-cote.\"", "반면에 에이미라는 제단 앞에서 감상적인 한숨 한두 번을 바치는 공물 없이 빠져나가는 이는 거의 없었다. 감상 이야기가 나온 김에, 우리는 아주 자연스럽게 '비둘기 둥지'로 넘어가게 된다."]], [["That was the name of the little brown house which Mr.", "그것은 브룩 씨가 메그의 첫 보금자리로 마련한 작은 갈색 집의 이름이었다. 로리가 그렇게 이름 붙였는데, 그 다정한 연인들에게 더없이 잘 어울린다고 했다."], ["Brooke had prepared for Meg's first home.", "\"한 쌍의 멧비둘기처럼 부리부리 입을 맞추다가 구구 우는\" 사이라나."], ["Laurie had christened it, saying it was highly appropriate to the gentle lovers, who \"went on together like a pair of turtle-doves, with first a bill and then a coo.\"", "그것은 자그마한 집이었다. 뒤에는 작은 정원이 있고, 앞에는 손수건만 한 잔디밭이 있었다."], ["It was a tiny house, with a little garden behind, and a lawn about as big as a pocket-handkerchief in front.", "메그는 여기에 분수와 관목, 그리고 사랑스러운 꽃들을 흐드러지게 가꿀 작정이었다. 다만 지금 당장은 그 분수란 게 풍상에 시달린 항아리 하나로 대신되어 있었는데, 다 부서진 개숫물 그릇과 영락없이 닮은 꼴이었다."], ["Here Meg meant to have a fountain, shrubbery, and a profusion of lovely flowers; though just at present, the fountain was represented by a weather-beaten urn, very like a dilapidated slop-bowl; the shrubbery consisted of several young larches, undecided whether to live or die; and the profusion of flowers was merely hinted by regiments of sticks, to show where seeds were planted.", "관목이라야 어린 낙엽송 몇 그루로, 살아야 할지 죽어야 할지 마음을 못 정한 모양새였다. 그리고 흐드러진 꽃이란 것은, 씨를 심어 둔 자리를 표시하느라 줄지어 꽂아 둔 막대들로 그저 암시되어 있을 뿐이었다."], ["But inside, it was altogether charming, and the happy bride saw no fault from garret to cellar.", "그러나 안으로 들어가면 그곳은 정말이지 매력적이었고, 행복한 신부는 다락에서 지하실까지 흠 하나 발견하지 못했다. 하기야 현관 복도가 어찌나 좁던지, 피아노가 없는 게 다행이었다."], ["To be sure, the hall was so narrow, it was fortunate that they had no piano, for one never could have been got in whole; the dining-room was so small that six people were a tight fit; and the kitchen stairs seemed built for the express purpose of precipitating both servants and china pell-mell into the coal-bin.", "한 대를 통째로 들여놓을 수가 없었을 테니까. 식당은 어찌나 작은지 여섯 명이면 빠듯하게 들어찼고, 부엌 계단은 하인이며 그릇이며 죄다 석탄 통 속으로 곤두박질치게 하려고 일부러 지은 것만 같았다."], ["But once get used to these slight blemishes, and nothing could be more complete, for good sense and good taste had presided over the furnishing, and the result was highly satisfactory.", "그러나 이런 자잘한 흠들에 한번 익숙해지고 나면, 이보다 더 완벽할 수 없었다. 분별과 안목이 가구를 들이는 일을 도맡았고, 그 결과는 무척 만족스러웠으니까."], ["There were no marble-topped tables, long mirrors, or lace curtains in the little parlor, but simple furniture, plenty of books, a fine picture or two, a stand of flowers in the bay-window, and, scattered all about, the pretty gifts which came from friendly hands, and were the fairer for the loving messages they brought.", "그 작은 거실에는 대리석 상판 탁자도, 기다란 거울도, 레이스 커튼도 없었지만, 소박한 가구와 넉넉한 책들, 멋진 그림 한두 점, 퇴창에 놓인 화분대가 있었고, 여기저기 흩어진 채로 다정한 손길에서 온 예쁜 선물들이 놓여 있었다. 그 선물들은 함께 실려 온 애정 어린 인사말 덕분에 한결 더 아름다웠다."]], [["I don't think the Parian Psyche Laurie gave lost any of its beauty because John put up the bracket it stood upon; that any upholsterer could have draped the plain muslin curtains more gracefully than Amy's artistic hand; or that any store-room was ever better provided with good wishes, merry words, and happy hopes, than that in which Jo and her mother put away Meg's few boxes, barrels, and bundles; and I am morally certain that the spandy-new kitchen never _could_ have looked so cosey and neat if Hannah had not arranged every pot and pan a dozen times over, and laid the fire all ready for lighting, the minute \"Mis. Brooke came home.\" I also doubt if any young matron ever began life with so rich a supply of dusters, holders, and piece-bags; for Beth made enough to last till the silver wedding came round, and invented three different kinds of dishcloths for the express service of the bridal china.", "로리가 준 패리언 도자기 프시케 상이 그 받침대를 존이 직접 달았다 해서 그 아름다움을 조금이라도 잃었다고는 생각지 않는다. 어떤 실내장식가가 단다 한들 에이미의 예술적인 솜씨보다 그 수수한 모슬린 커튼을 더 우아하게 드리울 수 있었으리라고도, 또 조와 어머니가 메그의 몇 안 되는 상자와 통과 꾸러미를 정리해 넣은 그 곳간보다 좋은 소망과 즐거운 말과 행복한 희망이 더 잘 갖춰진 곳간이 일찍이 있었으리라고도 생각지 않는다. 그리고 나는 양심을 걸고 단언하건대, 한나가 모든 냄비와 팬을 열두 번씩 거듭 정돈하고, '브룩 마님이 집에 오시는' 그 순간 바로 불을 붙일 수 있게 아궁이를 다 준비해 두지 않았더라면, 그 산뜻한 새 부엌이 결코 그토록 아늑하고 깔끔해 보일 수 없었을 것이다. 또 나는 의심스럽다. 일찍이 어떤 젊은 새댁이 그토록 풍족하게 갖춰진 먼지떨이와 냄비 받침과 헝겊 주머니를 가지고 살림을 시작했을지. 베스가 은혼식이 돌아올 때까지 쓰고도 남을 만큼 만들어 주었고, 신혼 식기를 닦는 일에만 쓰라고 세 가지 종류의 행주까지 고안해 냈으니 말이다."]], [["People who hire all these things done for them never know what they lose; for the homeliest tasks get beautified if loving hands do them, and Meg found so many proofs of this, that everything in her small nest, from the kitchen roller to the silver vase on her parlor table, was eloquent of home love and tender forethought.", "이런 일들을 죄다 남에게 시키며 사는 사람들은 자기가 무엇을 잃는지 결코 알지 못한다. 아무리 보잘것없는 일도 사랑하는 손길이 하면 아름다워지는 법이니까. 메그는 이를 증명하는 숱한 증거들을 찾아냈으니, 부엌의 밀방망이에서 거실 탁자 위의 은화병에 이르기까지, 그 작은 보금자리의 모든 것이 가정의 사랑과 다정한 배려를 웅변하고 있었다."]], [["What happy times they had planning together, what solemn shopping excursions; what funny mistakes they made, and what shouts of laughter arose over Laurie's ridiculous bargains.", "함께 계획을 짜며 보낸 그 행복한 시간들이라니, 그 엄숙한 장보기 나들이라니. 또 얼마나 우스운 실수들을 저질렀던지, 그리고 로리가 사 온 어이없는 물건들을 두고 얼마나 큰 웃음이 터져 나왔던지."], ["In his love of jokes, this young gentleman, though nearly through college, was as much of a boy as ever.", "농담을 좋아하는 이 젊은 신사는 대학을 거의 마쳐 가는데도 여전히 영락없는 소년이었다."], ["His last whim had been to bring with him, on his weekly visits, some new, useful, and ingenious article for the young housekeeper.", "그의 최근 변덕은, 매주 찾아올 때마다 젊은 살림꾼을 위해 무언가 새롭고 쓸모 있고 기발한 물건을 가져오는 것이었다."], ["Now a bag of remarkable clothes-pins; next, a wonderful nutmeg-grater, which fell to pieces at the first trial; a knife-cleaner that spoilt all the knives; or a sweeper that picked the nap neatly off the carpet, and left the dirt; labor-saving soap that took the skin off one's hands; infallible cements which stuck firmly to nothing but the fingers of the deluded buyer; and every kind of tin-ware, from a toy savings-bank for odd pennies, to a wonderful boiler which would wash articles in its own steam, with every prospect of exploding in the process.", "어느 주에는 유별난 빨래집게 한 봉지를, 그다음에는 첫 시험에 산산조각 나 버린 놀라운 육두구 강판을, 또 칼을 죄다 망쳐 놓는 칼 가는 기구를, 아니면 카펫에서 보풀만 말끔히 떼어 가고 먼지는 그대로 남기는 청소기를, 손 살갗을 벗겨 놓는 일손 더는 비누를, 속아 산 사람의 손가락 말고는 어디에도 단단히 붙지 않는 절대 접착제를, 그리고 자투리 동전을 넣는 장난감 저금통에서부터 김을 내며 물건을 빠는, 그러나 그 과정에서 폭발할 가능성이 다분한 놀라운 보일러에 이르기까지 온갖 양철 제품을 가져왔다."]], [["In vain Meg begged him to stop.", "메그가 제발 그만하라고 사정해도 소용없었다."], ["John laughed at him, and Jo called him \"Mr. Toodles.\"", "존은 그를 보고 웃었고, 조는 그를 '투들스 씨'라고 불렀다."], ["He was possessed with a mania for patronizing Yankee ingenuity, and seeing his friends fitly furnished forth.", "그는 양키의 창의력에 후원자 노릇 하면서 자기 친구들이 알맞게 살림을 갖추는 걸 보고 싶다는 열병에 단단히 사로잡혀 있었다."], ["So each week beheld some fresh absurdity.", "그래서 매주 무슨 새로운 우스꽝스러운 물건이 나타나는 것이었다."]], [["Everything was done at last, even to Amy's arranging different colored soaps to match the different colored rooms, and Beth's setting the table for the first meal.", "마침내 모든 일이 끝났다. 에이미가 방마다 다른 색깔에 맞춰 색색의 비누를 배치하는 일까지, 그리고 베스가 첫 식사를 위해 식탁을 차리는 일까지."]], [["\"Are you satisfied?", "\"만족하니?"], ["Does it seem like home, and do you feel as if you should be happy here?\"", "집 같은 느낌이 들어? 여기서 행복할 것 같아?\""], ["asked Mrs.", "마치 부인이 물었다."], ["March, as she and her daughter went through the new kingdom, arm-in-arm; for just then they seemed to cling together more tenderly than ever.", "그녀와 딸은 팔짱을 끼고 새 왕국을 둘러보는 중이었다. 바로 그때 두 사람은 그 어느 때보다 다정하게 서로에게 매달려 있는 듯했다."]], [["\"Yes, mother, perfectly satisfied, thanks to you all, and _so_ happy that I can't talk about it,\" answered Meg, with a look that was better than words.", "\"네, 엄마, 더없이 만족해요. 다 여러분 덕분이에요. 그리고 너무너무 행복해서 말로는 다 못 하겠어요.\" 메그가 대답했다. 그 표정이 어떤 말보다도 많은 것을 담고 있었다."]], [["\"If she only had a servant or two it would be all right,\"", "\"하인이 한둘만 있으면 다 좋을 텐데.\""], ["said Amy, coming out of the parlor, where she had been trying to decide whether the bronze Mercury looked best on the whatnot or the mantle-piece.", "에이미가 거실에서 나오며 말했다. 그녀는 그 안에서 청동 머큐리 상을 장식장 위에 두는 게 나을지 벽난로 선반 위에 두는 게 나을지 결정하려 애쓰던 참이었다."]], [["\"Mother and I have talked that over, and I have made up my mind to try her way first.", "\"엄마하고 그 얘기를 나눠 봤는데, 우선은 엄마 방식대로 해 보기로 마음먹었어."], ["There will be so little to do, that, with Lotty to run my errands and help me here and there, I shall only have enough work to keep me from getting lazy or homesick,\"", "할 일이 워낙 적을 테니까, 로티가 심부름을 다녀 주고 여기저기 거들어 주면, 게으름 피우거나 향수병에 걸리지 않을 만큼만 일하게 되는 거지.\""], ["answered Meg tranquilly.", "메그가 차분하게 대답했다."]], [["\"Sallie Moffat has four,\"", "\"샐리 모팻은 넷이나 두는데.\""], ["began Amy.", "에이미가 입을 열었다."]], [["\"If Meg had four the house wouldn't hold them, and master and missis would have to camp in the garden,\"", "\"메그 언니가 넷이나 두면 집이 못 버틸걸. 주인 양반하고 마님은 정원에서 노숙해야 할 거야.\""], ["broke in Jo, who, enveloped in a big blue pinafore, was giving the last polish to the door-handles.", "조가 끼어들었다. 그녀는 커다란 파란 앞치마를 두른 채 문손잡이에 마지막 광을 내고 있었다."]], [["\"Sallie isn't a poor man's wife, and many maids are in keeping with her fine establishment.", "\"샐리는 가난한 사람의 아내가 아니고, 그 화려한 살림에는 하녀가 많은 게 어울리지."], ["Meg and John begin humbly, but I have a feeling that there will be quite as much happiness in the little house as in the big one.", "메그하고 존은 소박하게 시작하지만, 나는 그 작은 집에도 큰 집 못지않은 행복이 가득하리라는 느낌이 들어."], ["It's a great mistake for young girls like Meg to leave themselves nothing to do but dress, give orders, and gossip.", "메그 같은 젊은 처녀들이 차려입고, 지시 내리고, 잡담이나 하는 것 말고는 할 일이 없게 되는 건 큰 잘못이란다. 나도 처음 결혼했을 때는 새 옷이 닳거나 해어지기를 간절히 바라곤 했어."], ["When I was first married, I used to long for my new clothes to wear out or get torn, so that I might have the pleasure of mending them; for I got heartily sick of doing fancy work and tending my pocket handkerchief.\"", "그걸 깁는 즐거움이라도 누리고 싶어서 말이야. 수예나 하고 손수건이나 만지작거리는 일에 진절머리가 났거든.\""]], [["\"Why didn't you go into the kitchen and make messes, as Sallie says she does, to amuse herself, though they never turn out well, and the servants laugh at her,\"", "\"엄마는 왜 샐리처럼 부엌에 들어가서 음식이나 만들면서 시간을 보내지 않으셨어요? 샐리는 그렇게 한다잖아요, 재미 삼아서요."], ["said Meg.", "그래 봤자 제대로 되는 법이 없어서 하인들이 비웃는다지만요.\" 메그가 말했다."]], [["\"I did, after a while; not to 'mess,' but to learn of Hannah how things should be done, that my servants need _not_ laugh at me. It was play then; but there came a time when I was truly grateful that I not only possessed the will but the power to cook wholesome food for my little girls, and help myself when I could no longer afford to hire help. You begin at the other end, Meg, dear; but the lessons you learn now will be of use to you by and by, when John is a richer man, for the mistress of a house, however splendid, should know how work ought to be done, if she wishes to be well and honestly served.\"", "\"나도 얼마 지나서는 그랬단다. '음식 장난'을 치려던 게 아니라, 한나에게 일을 어떻게 해야 하는지 배우려고 말이야. 내 하인들이 나를 비웃지 못하게 하려고. 그때는 놀이였지. 하지만 내가 어린 딸들에게 몸에 좋은 음식을 해 먹일 의지뿐 아니라 능력까지 갖추고 있다는 사실에, 그리고 더는 일손을 살 형편이 못 될 때 스스로 해낼 수 있다는 사실에 진심으로 감사하게 되는 날이 왔단다. 너는 정반대 쪽 끝에서 시작하는구나, 메그. 하지만 지금 배우는 것들이 나중에, 존이 더 부유해졌을 때 네게 쓸모가 있을 거야. 한 집안의 안주인이라면, 아무리 화려한 집이라도, 일이 어떻게 돌아가야 하는지를 알아야 하니까. 제대로 정직한 시중을 받고 싶다면 말이야.\""]], [["\"Yes, mother, I'm sure of that,\"", "\"네, 엄마, 그건 분명해요.\""], ["said Meg, listening respectfully to the little lecture; for the best of women will hold forth upon the all-absorbing subject of housekeeping.", "메그가 그 짤막한 가르침에 공손히 귀를 기울이며 말했다. 아무리 훌륭한 여인이라도 살림이라는 더없이 마음을 사로잡는 주제에 대해서는 일장 연설을 늘어놓기 마련이니까."], ["\"Do you know I like this room most of all in my baby-house,\"", "\"있잖아요, 저는 우리 인형의 집에서 이 방이 제일 좋아요.\""], ["added Meg, a minute after, as they went upstairs, and she looked into her well-stored linen-closet.", "잠시 후 메그가 덧붙였다. 두 사람이 위층으로 올라가던 참이었고, 그녀가 그득하게 채워진 리넨 벽장 안을 들여다보던 참이었다."]], [["Beth was there, laying the snowy piles smoothly on the shelves, and exulting over the goodly array.", "베스가 거기 있었다. 눈처럼 흰 무더기를 선반 위에 가지런히 올려놓으며, 그 푸짐한 진열에 흐뭇해하고 있었다."], ["All three laughed as Meg spoke; for that linen-closet was a joke.", "메그가 말하자 셋 다 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그 리넨 벽장은 하나의 우스개였기 때문이다."], ["You see, having said that if Meg married \"that Brooke\"", "사연인즉슨 이러했다. 메그가 '그 브룩이라는 자'와 결혼한다면 자기 돈은 한 푼도 못 받을 거라고 말해 놓은 터라, 마치 대고모는 시간이 흘러 노여움이 가라앉고 그 맹세를 후회하게 되자 다소 난처한 처지가 되었다."], ["she shouldn't have a cent of her money, Aunt March was rather in a quandary, when time had appeased her wrath and made her repent her vow.", "노부인은 결코 자기 말을 어기는 법이 없는지라, 어떻게 그 말을 비껴갈지 몹시 머리를 썩였고, 마침내 스스로 흡족할 만한 묘책을 짜냈다. 플로렌스의 엄마인 캐럴 부인에게 집안 리넨과 식탁보를 넉넉하게 사서, 만들게 하고, 이름까지 새겨, 그것을 자기 선물로 보내라고 시킨 것이다."], ["She never broke her word, and was much exercised in her mind how to get round it, and at last devised a plan whereby she could satisfy herself.", "그 일은 모두 충실하게 이행되었다. 그러나 비밀이 새어 나갔고, 온 가족이 그것을 무척 즐거워했다."], ["Mrs. Carrol, Florence's mamma, was ordered to buy, have made, and marked, a generous supply of house and table linen, and send it as _her_ present, all of which was faithfully done; but the secret leaked out, and was greatly enjoyed by the family; for Aunt March tried to look utterly unconscious, and insisted that she could give nothing but the old-fashioned pearls, long promised to the first bride.", "마치 대고모가 짐짓 아무것도 모르는 척하면서, 첫 신부에게 오래전에 약속해 둔 그 구식 진주 말고는 아무것도 줄 수 없다고 우겨 댔으니 말이다."]], [["\"That's a housewifely taste which I am glad to see.", "\"그게 바로 살림꾼다운 안목인데, 나는 그게 보기 좋더라."], ["I had a young friend who set up housekeeping with six sheets, but she had finger bowls for company, and that satisfied her,\" said Mrs.", "내게 시트 여섯 장으로 살림을 차린 젊은 친구가 하나 있었는데, 그러면서도 손님 접대용으로 핑거볼은 갖추고 있었고, 그걸로 흡족해하더구나.\""], ["March, patting the damask table-cloths, with a truly feminine appreciation of their fineness.", "마치 부인이 말했다. 다마스크 식탁보를 토닥이며, 그 고운 천을 진정 여성다운 안목으로 음미하면서."]], [["\"I haven't a single finger-bowl, but this is a 'set out' that will last me all my days, Hannah says;\"", "\"저는 핑거볼은 하나도 없지만, 이건 한나 말로는 제가 평생 쓸 수 있는 '혼수'래요.\""], ["and Meg looked quite contented, as well she might.", "메그가 무척 흐뭇한 표정으로 말했다. 그럴 만도 했다."]], [["\"Toodles is coming,\"", "\"투들스가 온다!\""], ["cried Jo from below; and they all went down to meet Laurie, whose weekly visit was an important event in their quiet lives.", "아래층에서 조가 외쳤다. 다들 로리를 맞이하러 내려갔다."], ["A tall, broad-shouldered young fellow, with a cropped head, a felt-basin of a hat, and a fly-away coat, came tramping down the road at a great pace, walked over the low fence without stopping to open the gate, straight up to Mrs.", "그의 매주 방문은 이들의 조용한 삶에서 중요한 사건이었다. 키 크고 어깨 넓은 젊은 사내가, 짧게 깎은 머리에 대야처럼 생긴 펠트 모자를 쓰고 펄럭이는 외투를 걸친 채, 성큼성큼 길을 따라 내려왔다."], ["March, with both hands out, and a hearty--", "그러더니 대문을 열려고 멈추지도 않고 낮은 울타리를 넘어, 곧장 마치 부인에게로 두 손을 내밀며 다가와 진심 어린 인사를 건넸다."]], [["\"Here I am, mother!", "\"저 왔어요, 어머니!"], ["Yes, it's all right.\"", "네, 다 잘됐어요.\""]], [["The last words were in answer to the look the elder lady gave him; a kindly questioning look, which the handsome eyes met so frankly that the little ceremony closed, as usual, with a motherly kiss.", "마지막 말은 노부인이 그에게 보낸 눈길에 대한 대답이었다. 다정하게 묻는 그 눈길을, 그 잘생긴 두 눈이 어찌나 솔직하게 마주했던지, 그 자그마한 의식은 여느 때처럼 어머니다운 입맞춤으로 마무리되었다."]], [["\"For Mrs. John Brooke, with the maker's congratulations and compliments.", "\"존 브룩 부인께, 만든 사람의 축하와 찬사를 담아서요."], ["Bless you, Beth!", "잘 있었니, 베스!"], ["What a refreshing spectacle you are, Jo.", "너는 어쩜 이리 보기만 해도 상쾌하니, 조."], ["Amy, you are getting altogether too handsome for a single lady.\"", "에이미, 너는 미혼 숙녀치고는 너무 예뻐지고 있어.\""]], [["As Laurie spoke, he delivered a brown paper parcel to Meg, pulled Beth's hair-ribbon, stared at Jo's big pinafore, and fell into an attitude of mock rapture before Amy, then shook hands all round, and every one began to talk.", "로리는 이렇게 말하면서 갈색 종이 꾸러미를 메그에게 건네주고, 베스의 머리 리본을 잡아당기고, 조의 커다란 앞치마를 빤히 쳐다보고, 에이미 앞에서는 짐짓 황홀해하는 자세를 취하더니, 죽 돌아가며 모두와 악수를 했다. 그러자 모두가 입을 열기 시작했다."]], [["\"Where is John?\"", "\"존은 어디 있어요?\""], ["asked Meg anxiously.", "메그가 걱정스레 물었다."]], [["\"Stopped to get the license for to-morrow, ma'am.\"", "\"내일 쓸 혼인 허가증을 받느라 들렀어요, 부인.\""]], [["\"Which side won the last match, Teddy?\"", "\"마지막 경기는 어느 편이 이겼어, 테디?\""], ["inquired Jo, who persisted in feeling an interest in manly sports, despite her nineteen years.", "조가 물었다. 그녀는 열아홉 나이에도 사내들의 운동 경기에 한사코 관심을 두었다."]], [["\"Ours, of course.", "\"당연히 우리 편이지."], ["Wish you'd been there to see.\"", "네가 와서 봤으면 좋았을 텐데.\""]], [["\"How is the lovely Miss Randal?\"", "\"그 어여쁜 랜들 양은 어떻게 지내?\""], ["asked Amy, with a significant smile.", "에이미가 의미심장한 미소를 띠며 물었다."]], [["\"More cruel than ever; don't you see how I'm pining away?\"", "\"그 어느 때보다 매정하지. 내가 어떻게 애를 태우고 있는지 안 보여?\""], ["and Laurie gave his broad chest a sounding slap and heaved a melodramatic sigh.", "로리는 자기 넓은 가슴을 철썩 소리 나게 치고는 연극조로 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["\"What's the last joke?", "\"이번엔 무슨 우스운 물건이야?"], ["Undo the bundle and see, Meg,\"", "꾸러미를 풀어 봐, 메그.\""], ["said Beth, eying the knobby parcel with curiosity.", "베스가 울퉁불퉁한 꾸러미를 궁금한 눈으로 바라보며 말했다."]], [["\"It's a useful thing to have in the house in case of fire or thieves,\"", "\"불이 나거나 도둑이 들었을 때 집에 두면 쓸모 있는 물건이지.\""], ["observed Laurie, as a watchman's rattle appeared, amid the laughter of the girls.", "로리가 말했다. 소녀들의 웃음 속에서 야경꾼의 딸랑이가 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["\"Any time when John is away, and you get frightened, Mrs.", "\"존이 없을 때 언제든 겁이 나면, 메그 부인, 그냥 이걸 앞 창문 밖으로 휘두르기만 해요."], ["Meg, just swing that out of the front window, and it will rouse the neighborhood in a jiffy.", "그럼 눈 깜짝할 새에 온 동네가 다 깨어날 테니까."], ["Nice thing, isn't it?\"", "괜찮은 물건이죠?\""], ["and Laurie gave them a sample of its powers that made them cover up their ears.", "로리는 그것의 위력을 한 차례 시범으로 보여 주었고, 모두 두 귀를 틀어막아야 했다."]], [["\"There's gratitude for you!", "\"그래, 고맙기도 하지!"], ["and speaking of gratitude reminds me to mention that you may thank Hannah for saving your wedding-cake from destruction.", "고맙다는 말이 나온 김에 한마디 하자면, 너희 웨딩 케이크가 박살 나는 걸 막아 준 한나에게 고마워해야 할 거야."], ["I saw it going into your house as I came by, and if she hadn't defended it manfully I'd have had a pick at it, for it looked like a remarkably plummy one.\"", "오는 길에 그게 너희 집으로 들어가는 걸 봤는데, 한나가 사내처럼 그걸 지켜 내지 않았더라면 내가 한 입 떼어 갔을 거야. 자두가 어찌나 듬뿍 든 것 같던지.\""]], [["\"I wonder if you will ever grow up, Laurie,\"", "\"넌 도대체 언제쯤 철이 들려나, 로리.\""], ["said Meg, in a matronly tone.", "메그가 부인다운 어조로 말했다."]], [["\"I'm doing my best, ma'am, but can't get much higher, I'm afraid, as six feet is about all men can do in these degenerate days,\"", "\"최선을 다하고 있어요, 부인. 하지만 더는 못 클까 봐 걱정이네요."], ["responded the young gentleman, whose head was about level with the little chandelier.", "이 타락한 시대에 사내가 클 수 있는 키라야 6피트가 고작이니 말이에요.\" 젊은 신사가 대답했다."], ["\"I suppose it would be profanation to eat anything in this spick and span new bower, so, as I'm tremendously hungry, I propose an adjournment,\"", "그의 머리는 작은 샹들리에와 거의 같은 높이에 있었다. \"이 말끔하고 새것 티 나는 보금자리에서 뭘 먹는 건 신성모독일 테니, 내가 지독하게 배가 고파서 하는 말인데, 자리를 옮길 것을 제안하는 바입니다.\""], ["he added presently.", "그가 곧이어 덧붙였다."]], [["\"Mother and I are going to wait for John.", "\"엄마하고 나는 존을 기다릴 거야."], ["There are some last things to settle,\"", "마지막으로 정할 일이 좀 남아 있거든.\""], ["said Meg, bustling away.", "메그가 부산히 자리를 뜨며 말했다."]], [["\"Beth and I are going over to Kitty Bryant's to get more flowers for to-morrow,\"", "\"베스하고 나는 키티 브라이언트네 집에 가서 내일 쓸 꽃을 더 가져올 거야.\""], ["added Amy, tying a picturesque hat over her picturesque curls, and enjoying the effect as much as anybody.", "에이미가 덧붙였다. 그림 같은 곱슬머리 위에 그림 같은 모자를 동여매며, 그 효과를 누구 못지않게 즐기면서."]], [["\"Come, Jo, don't desert a fellow.", "\"가자, 조, 친구를 저버리지 말고."], ["I'm in such a state of exhaustion I can't get home without help.", "어찌나 기진맥진한지 도와주지 않으면 집까지 못 가겠다."], ["Don't take off your apron, whatever you do; it's peculiarly becoming,\"", "앞치마는 절대 벗지 마. 그게 유난히 잘 어울리거든.\""], ["said Laurie, as Jo bestowed his especial aversion in her capacious pocket, and offered him her arm to support his feeble steps.", "로리가 말했다. 조는 그가 유독 질색하는 그 물건을 자신의 널찍한 주머니에 챙겨 넣고는, 비틀거리는 그의 발걸음을 부축하라고 팔을 내밀었다."]], [["\"Now, Teddy, I want to talk seriously to you about to-morrow,\"", "\"자, 테디, 내일 일에 대해 진지하게 얘기 좀 하고 싶어.\""], ["began Jo, as they strolled away together.", "둘이 함께 거닐며 조가 입을 열었다."], ["\"You _must_ promise to behave well, and not cut up any pranks, and spoil our plans.\"", "\"넌 반드시 얌전히 굴겠다고, 무슨 장난도 치지 않겠다고, 우리 계획을 망치지 않겠다고 약속해야 해.\""]], [["\"Not a prank.\"", "\"장난 안 칠게.\""]], [["\"And don't say funny things when we ought to be sober.\"", "\"그리고 우리가 진지해야 할 때 우스운 소리도 하지 말고.\""]], [["\"I never do; you are the one for that.\"", "\"난 그런 적 없어. 그건 네가 잘하는 거지.\""]], [["\"And I implore you not to look at me during the ceremony; I shall certainly laugh if you do.\"", "\"그리고 제발 부탁인데, 예식 중에 나를 쳐다보지 마. 네가 쳐다보면 난 분명히 웃음이 터질 테니까.\""]], [["\"You won't see me; you'll be crying so hard that the thick fog round you will obscure the prospect.\"", "\"넌 날 못 볼걸. 어찌나 펑펑 우는지 네 주위에 자욱한 안개가 시야를 가려 버릴 테니까.\""]], [["\"I never cry unless for some great affliction.\"", "\"난 큰 슬픔이 아니고서는 절대 안 울어.\""]], [["\"Such as fellows going to college, hey?\"", "\"이를테면 친구들이 대학에 가는 거 같은 거 말이야, 응?\""], ["cut in Laurie, with a suggestive laugh.", "로리가 의미심장하게 웃으며 끼어들었다."]], [["\"Don't be a peacock.", "\"잘난 척 좀 작작 해."], ["I only moaned a trifle to keep the girls company.\"", "난 그저 여자애들 장단 맞춰 주느라 조금 훌쩍였을 뿐이야.\""]], [["\"Exactly.", "\"바로 그거지."], ["I say, Jo, how is grandpa this week; pretty amiable?\"", "그건 그렇고, 조, 이번 주 할아버지는 좀 어때? 꽤 너그러우셔?\""]], [["\"Very; why, have you got into a scrape, and want to know how he'll take it?\"", "\"아주 그러셔. 왜, 너 무슨 사고라도 쳐 놓고 할아버지가 어떻게 받아들이실지 알고 싶은 거야?\""], ["asked Jo rather sharply.", "조가 다소 날카롭게 물었다."]], [["\"Now, Jo, do you think I'd look your mother in the face, and say 'All right,'", "\"이봐, 조, 너 정말 내가 네 어머니 얼굴을 똑바로 보면서 '다 잘됐어요' 하고 말할 사람으로 보여?"], ["if it wasn't?\"", "그게 사실이 아니라면 말이야.\""], ["and Laurie stopped short, with an injured air.", "로리가 발걸음을 뚝 멈추고는 억울하다는 표정을 지었다."]], [["\"No, I don't.\"", "\"아니, 안 그래.\""]], [["\"Then don't go and be suspicious; I only want some money,\"", "\"그럼 의심하지 마. 난 그저 돈이 좀 필요할 뿐이야.\""], ["said Laurie, walking on again, appeased by her hearty tone.", "로리가 다시 걸음을 옮기며 말했다. 그녀의 진심 어린 어조에 마음이 풀린 것이다."]], [["\"You spend a great deal, Teddy.\"", "\"넌 돈을 너무 많이 써, 테디.\""]], [["\"Bless you, _I_ don't spend it; it spends itself, somehow, and is gone before I know it.\"", "\"무슨 소리, 나는 돈을 안 써. 그게 어떻게든 알아서 써지고, 내가 알아차리기도 전에 사라져 버린다니까.\""]], [["\"You are so generous and kind-hearted that you let people borrow, and can't say 'No' to any one.", "\"넌 너무 후하고 마음이 여려서 남들이 빌려 가게 내버려 두고, 누구한테도 '안 돼'라는 말을 못 하잖아."], ["We heard about Henshaw, and all you did for him.", "우리도 헨쇼 얘기랑, 네가 그를 위해 해 준 일들을 다 들었어."], ["If you always spent money in that way, no one would blame you,\"", "네가 늘 그런 식으로 돈을 쓴다면 아무도 너를 탓하지 않을 거야.\""], ["said Jo warmly.", "조가 따뜻하게 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, he made a mountain out of a mole-hill.", "\"아, 걔가 별것 아닌 일을 침소봉대한 거야."], ["You wouldn't have me let that fine fellow work himself to death, just for the want of a little help, when he is worth a dozen of us lazy chaps, would you?\"", "그렇게 훌륭한 친구가, 우리 게으른 녀석들 열 명을 합친 것보다 나은 친구가, 그저 약간의 도움이 없어서 죽도록 일하다 쓰러지게 내버려 두라는 거야, 설마?\""]], [["\"Of course not; but I don't see the use of your having seventeen waistcoats, endless neckties, and a new hat every time you come home.", "\"당연히 아니지. 하지만 네가 조끼를 열일곱 벌이나 두고, 끝도 없이 넥타이를 갖고, 집에 올 때마다 새 모자를 쓰는 건 무슨 소용인지 모르겠어."], ["I thought you'd got over the dandy period; but every now and then it breaks out in a new spot.", "난 네가 멋쟁이 시기는 지난 줄 알았는데, 그게 이따금 새로운 데서 또 터져 나오더라."], ["Just now it's the fashion to be hideous,--to make your head look like a scrubbing-brush, wear a strait-jacket, orange gloves, and clumping, square-toed boots.", "요즘은 흉하게 꾸미는 게 유행이라며. 머리를 솔처럼 보이게 하고, 구속복 같은 옷을 입고, 주황색 장갑을 끼고, 투박하고 코가 네모난 부츠를 신고."], ["If it was cheap ugliness, I'd say nothing; but it costs as much as the other, and I don't get any satisfaction out of it.\"", "그게 싸게 먹히는 추함이라면 나도 아무 말 안 하겠어. 하지만 멀쩡한 것만큼이나 돈이 들면서, 난 거기서 아무 만족도 못 얻으니 하는 말이야.\""]], [["Laurie threw back his head, and laughed so heartily at this attack, that the felt-basin fell off, and Jo walked on it, which insult only afforded him an opportunity for expatiating on the advantages of a rough-and-ready costume, as he folded up the maltreated hat, and stuffed it into his pocket.", "로리는 고개를 뒤로 젖히고 이 공격에 어찌나 호탕하게 웃어 댔던지, 대야 같은 펠트 모자가 떨어졌고, 조가 그 위를 밟고 지나가 버렸다. 그러나 이 모욕은 그에게 도리어 수수하고 실용적인 차림새의 장점을 늘어놓을 기회만 주었을 뿐이다. 그는 짓밟힌 모자를 접어 주머니에 쑤셔 넣으며 그렇게 했다."]], [["\"Don't lecture any more, there's a good soul!", "\"잔소리 좀 그만해, 착하지!"], ["I have enough all through the week, and like to enjoy myself when I come home.", "일주일 내내 잔소리는 실컷 듣고, 집에 오면 좀 즐기고 싶단 말이야."], ["I'll get myself up regardless of expense, to-morrow, and be a satisfaction to my friends.\"", "내일은 돈 아끼지 않고 쫙 빼입어서 내 친구들을 흡족하게 해 줄게.\""]], [["\"I'll leave you in peace if you'll _only_ let your hair grow.", "\"네가 머리만 좀 기른다면 너를 가만 내버려 두겠어."], ["I'm not aristocratic, but I do object to being seen with a person who looks like a young prize-fighter,\"", "내가 무슨 귀족 취향은 아니지만, 풋내기 권투 선수처럼 생긴 사람하고 같이 다니는 모습을 남들한테 보이는 건 정말 싫거든.\""], ["observed Jo severely.", "조가 매섭게 한마디 했다."]], [["\"This unassuming style promotes study; that's why we adopt it,\" returned Laurie, who certainly could not be accused of vanity, having voluntarily sacrificed a handsome curly crop to the demand for quarter-of-an-inch-long stubble.", "\"이 수수한 스타일이 공부를 북돋아 주거든. 그래서 우리가 이걸 택하는 거야.\" 로리가 대꾸했다. 그는 분명 허영을 부린다고는 할 수 없는 사람이었다. 멋진 곱슬머리를 자진해서 희생하고 4분의 1인치짜리 짧은 까까머리로 만든 사람이었으니."]], [["\"By the way, Jo, I think that little Parker is really getting desperate about Amy.", "\"그건 그렇고, 조, 내 생각에 그 파커라는 꼬마가 에이미한테 진짜 푹 빠진 것 같아."], ["He talks of her constantly, writes poetry, and moons about in a most suspicious manner.", "노상 걔 얘기를 하고, 시를 쓰고, 영 수상쩍게 넋을 놓고 다니더라고."], ["He'd better nip his little passion in the bud, hadn't he?\"", "그 풋사랑은 싹부터 잘라 버리는 게 낫지 않겠어?\""], ["added Laurie, in a confidential, elder-brotherly tone, after a minute's silence.", "잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 로리가 은밀히 형 같은 어조로 덧붙였다."]], [["\"Of course he had; we don't want any more marrying in this family for years to come.", "\"당연히 그래야지. 우린 앞으로 몇 년 동안은 이 집에서 더 이상 결혼하는 사람이 안 나왔으면 좋겠어."], ["Mercy on us, what _are_ the children thinking of?\"", "맙소사, 그 어린것들이 대체 무슨 생각이람?\""], ["and Jo looked as much scandalized as if Amy and little Parker were not yet in their teens.", "조는 에이미와 꼬마 파커가 아직 십 대도 안 된 양 어처구니없어하는 표정을 지었다."]], [["\"It's a fast age, and I don't know what we are coming to, ma'am.", "\"속도가 빠른 시대잖아. 우리가 대체 어찌 되려는 건지 모르겠다니까, 부인."], ["You are a mere infant, but you'll go next, Jo, and we'll be left lamenting,\"", "너는 한낱 갓난아기지만, 다음 차례는 너야, 조. 그러면 우린 슬피 한탄하는 신세로 남겨지겠지.\""], ["said Laurie, shaking his head over the degeneracy of the times.", "로리가 시대의 타락을 두고 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다."]], [["\"Don't be alarmed; I'm not one of the agreeable sort.", "\"걱정 마. 난 그런 호감 사는 부류가 아니거든."], ["Nobody will want me, and it's a mercy, for there should always be one old maid in a family.\"", "아무도 날 원하지 않을 테고, 그게 다행이야. 집안에는 으레 노처녀가 하나쯤 있어야 하는 법이니까.\""]], [["\"You won't give any one a chance,\"", "\"넌 누구한테도 기회를 안 주잖아.\""], ["said Laurie, with a sidelong glance, and a little more color than before in his sunburnt face.", "로리가 곁눈질하며 말했다. 그의 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴에 아까보다 조금 더 붉은 기가 돌았다."], ["\"You won't show the soft side of your character; and if a fellow gets a peep at it by accident, and can't help showing that he likes it, you treat him as Mrs.", "\"넌 네 성격의 부드러운 면을 안 보여 줘. 그러다 어쩌다 어떤 녀석이 그걸 우연히 엿보고, 그게 마음에 든다는 걸 차마 감추지 못하면, 넌 그를 거미지 부인이 제 연인을 대하듯 다뤄 버리지."], ["Gummidge did her sweetheart,--throw cold water over him,--and get so thorny no one dares touch or look at you.\"", "찬물을 끼얹어 버리고는, 어찌나 가시를 곤두세우는지 아무도 감히 너를 건드리거나 쳐다보지도 못하게 만든다고.\""]], [["\"I don't like that sort of thing; I'm too busy to be worried with nonsense, and I think it's dreadful to break up families so.", "거미지 부인: 찰스 디킨스의 「데이비드 코퍼필드」에 나오는, 늘 신세를 한탄하는 인물. \"난 그런 거 싫어."], ["Now don't say any more about it; Meg's wedding has turned all our heads, and we talk of nothing but lovers and such absurdities.", "난 너무 바빠서 그런 시답잖은 일로 골치 썩일 틈이 없고, 그렇게 가족을 갈라놓는 건 끔찍한 일이라고 생각해. 자, 이제 그 얘기는 그만하자."], ["I don't wish to get cross, so let's change the subject;\"", "메그 언니 결혼식 때문에 우리 모두 머리가 어떻게 됐나 봐. 노상 연인이니 뭐니 그런 어처구니없는 얘기만 하고 있잖아."], ["and Jo looked quite ready to fling cold water on the slightest provocation.", "난 화내고 싶지 않으니까, 화제를 바꾸자.\" 조는 조금만 자극받아도 당장 찬물이라도 끼얹을 듯한 표정이었다."]], [["Whatever his feelings might have been, Laurie found a vent for them in a long low whistle, and the fearful prediction, as they parted at the gate, \"Mark my words, Jo, you'll go next.\"", "그의 속마음이 어떠했든, 로리는 길고 낮은 휘파람으로 그것을 풀어냈다. 그리고 대문 앞에서 헤어지며 무시무시한 예언을 남겼다. \"내 말 새겨들어, 조, 다음 차례는 너야.\" 스물다섯"]], [["# THE FIRST WEDDING.", "# THE FIRST WEDDING."]], [["The June roses over the porch were awake bright and early on that morning, rejoicing with all their hearts in the cloudless sunshine, like friendly little neighbors, as they were. Quite flushed with excitement were their ruddy faces, as they swung in the wind, whispering to one another what they had seen; for some peeped in at the dining-room windows, where the feast was spread, some climbed up to nod and smile at the sisters as they dressed the bride, others waved a welcome to those who came and went on various errands in garden, porch, and hall, and all, from the rosiest full-blown flower to the palest baby-bud, offered their tribute of beauty and fragrance to the gentle mistress who had loved and tended them so long.", "첫 번째 결혼식\n그날 아침 현관 위 6월의 장미들은 밝고 이른 시각에 깨어나, 구름 한 점 없는 햇살 속에서 온 마음으로 기뻐했다. 정다운 작은 이웃들답게 말이다. 바람에 흔들리며 자기들이 본 것을 서로에게 속삭이느라 그 발그레한 얼굴들이 흥분으로 한껏 달아올라 있었다. 몇몇은 잔치가 차려진 식당 창문 안을 들여다보았고, 몇몇은 기어올라가 신부를 단장시키는 자매들에게 고개를 끄덕이며 미소 지었으며, 또 몇몇은 정원과 현관과 복도에서 이런저런 심부름으로 오가는 이들에게 환영의 손짓을 보냈다. 그리고 모두가, 가장 발그레하게 활짝 핀 꽃부터 가장 창백한 어린 봉오리까지, 그토록 오래 자기들을 사랑하고 돌봐 준 다정한 안주인에게 아름다움과 향기의 공물을 바쳤다."]], [["Meg looked very like a rose herself; for all that was best and sweetest in heart and soul seemed to bloom into her face that day, making it fair and tender, with a charm more beautiful than beauty.", "메그 자신도 한 송이 장미처럼 보였다. 그날 그녀의 마음과 영혼에서 가장 선하고 가장 다정한 모든 것이 얼굴에 활짝 피어난 것 같았고, 그리하여 그 얼굴은 곱고도 부드러웠으며, 미모보다 더 아름다운 매력을 띠고 있었다."], ["Neither silk, lace, nor orange-flowers would she have.", "그녀는 비단도, 레이스도, 오렌지꽃도 마다했다."], ["\"I don't want to look strange or fixed up to-day,\"", "\"오늘은 낯설어 보이거나 잔뜩 꾸민 것처럼 보이고 싶지 않아.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"I don't want a fashionable wedding, but only those about me whom I love, and to them I wish to look and be my familiar self.\"", "\"유행을 따른 결혼식이 아니라, 그저 내가 사랑하는 사람들만 곁에 있었으면 좋겠어. 그리고 그 사람들에게는 평소 그대로의 내 모습으로 보이고, 또 그렇게 있고 싶어.\""]], [["So she made her wedding gown herself, sewing into it the tender hopes and innocent romances of a girlish heart.", "그래서 그녀는 웨딩드레스를 직접 만들었다. 소녀다운 마음의 다정한 희망과 순수한 로맨스를 한 땀 한 땀 그 안에 꿰매어 넣으면서."], ["Her sisters braided up her pretty hair, and the only ornaments she wore were the lilies of the valley, which \"her John\"", "자매들이 그녀의 고운 머리를 땋아 올려 주었고, 그녀가 단 유일한 장신구는 은방울꽃이었다."], ["liked best of all the flowers that grew.", "'자기 존'이 온갖 꽃들 중에서 가장 좋아하는 꽃이었다."]], [["\"You _do_ look just like our own dear Meg, only so very sweet and lovely that I should hug you if it wouldn't crumple your dress,\"", "\"정말이지 우리 사랑하는 메그 언니 그대로네. 다만 너무너무 곱고 사랑스러워서, 드레스가 구겨지지만 않는다면 와락 껴안아 주고 싶을 정도야.\""], ["cried Amy, surveying her with delight, when all was done.", "모든 단장이 끝나자 에이미가 흐뭇하게 그녀를 바라보며 외쳤다."]], [["\"Then I am satisfied.", "\"그럼 됐어. 하지만 다들 나를 껴안고 입맞춰 줘, 드레스 따위는 신경 쓰지 말고."], ["But please hug and kiss me, every one, and don't mind my dress; I want a great many crumples of this sort put into it to-day;\"", "오늘만큼은 이런 구김살이 잔뜩 들어갔으면 좋겠으니까.\" 메그가 자매들에게 두 팔을 벌렸다."], ["and Meg opened her arms to her sisters, who clung about her with April faces for a minute, feeling that the new love had not changed the old.", "자매들은 4월처럼 환한 얼굴로 잠시 그녀에게 매달렸다. 새로운 사랑이 옛 사랑을 바꾸어 놓지 않았음을 느끼면서."]], [["\"Now I'm going to tie John's cravat for him, and then to stay a few minutes with father quietly in the study;\"", "\"이제 가서 존의 넥타이를 매 주고, 그다음엔 서재에서 아버지와 조용히 잠깐 함께 있을 거야.\""], ["and Meg ran down to perform these little ceremonies, and then to follow her mother wherever she went, conscious that, in spite of the smiles on the motherly face, there was a secret sorrow hid in the motherly heart at the flight of the first bird from the nest.", "메그는 이 자그마한 의식들을 치르러 아래층으로 달려 내려갔고, 그러고는 어머니가 가는 곳마다 따라다녔다. 그 어머니다운 얼굴에 떠오른 미소에도 불구하고, 어머니의 가슴속에는 둥지를 떠나는 첫 새를 두고 남모를 슬픔이 감추어져 있다는 것을 의식하면서."]], [["As the younger girls stand together, giving the last touches to their simple toilet, it may be a good time to tell of a few changes which three years have wrought in their appearance; for all are looking their best just now.", "동생들이 나란히 서서 소박한 몸단장에 마지막 손질을 하고 있는 지금이, 삼 년이라는 세월이 그들의 모습에 가져온 몇 가지 변화를 이야기하기에 좋은 때일 듯하다. 다들 지금 한창 가장 좋은 모습을 하고 있으니 말이다."]], [["Jo's angles are much softened; she has learned to carry herself with ease, if not grace.", "조의 모난 데가 한결 부드러워졌다. 우아하지는 않을지언정 몸가짐에 여유가 생겼다."], ["The curly crop has lengthened into a thick coil, more becoming to the small head atop of the tall figure.", "짧은 곱슬머리는 길어져 굵은 쪽으로 틀어 올려졌는데, 큰 키 위에 얹힌 작은 머리에 한결 잘 어울렸다."], ["There is a fresh color in her brown cheeks, a soft shine in her eyes, and only gentle words fall from her sharp tongue to-day.", "갈색 뺨에는 생기 있는 혈색이 돌고, 눈에는 부드러운 빛이 어렸으며, 오늘만큼은 그 매서운 혀에서도 다정한 말만 흘러나왔다."]], [["Beth has grown slender, pale, and more quiet than ever; the beautiful, kind eyes are larger, and in them lies an expression that saddens one, although it is not sad itself. It is the shadow of pain which touches the young face with such pathetic patience; but Beth seldom complains, and always speaks hopefully of \"being better soon.\"", "베스는 야위고 창백해졌으며, 그 어느 때보다 말수가 줄었다. 아름답고 다정한 두 눈은 더 커졌고, 그 안에는 보는 이를 서글프게 하는 어떤 표정이 어려 있었다. 정작 그 표정 자체가 슬픈 것은 아니었지만. 그것은 그 어린 얼굴을 그토록 애처로운 인내로 어루만지는 고통의 그림자였다. 그러나 베스는 좀처럼 불평하는 법이 없었고, 늘 \"곧 나아질 거야\"라며 희망에 차서 말하곤 했다."]], [["Amy is with truth considered \"the flower of the family;\"", "에이미는 사실 그대로 '집안의 꽃'으로 여겨졌다."], ["for at sixteen she has the air and bearing of a full-grown woman--not beautiful, but possessed of that indescribable charm called grace.", "열여섯에 그녀는 어엿한 성인 여성의 분위기와 자태를 갖추었으니까. 미인은 아니었지만, 우아함이라 불리는 그 형언할 수 없는 매력을 지니고 있었다."], ["One saw it in the lines of her figure, the make and motion of her hands, the flow of her dress, the droop of her hair,--unconscious, yet harmonious, and as attractive to many as beauty itself.", "그것은 그녀의 몸매 선에서, 손의 생김새와 움직임에서, 드레스가 흘러내리는 모양에서, 머리카락이 드리워진 모양에서 드러났다. 의식하지 않은 것이면서도 조화로웠고, 많은 이들에게 미모 그 자체만큼이나 매력적이었다."], ["Amy's nose still afflicted her, for it never _would_ grow Grecian; so did her mouth, being too wide, and having a decided chin.", "에이미의 코는 여전히 그녀의 골칫거리였으니, 도무지 그리스풍으로 자라 주지 않았던 것이다. 입도 마찬가지여서 너무 넓었고, 턱은 또렷이 도드라졌다."], ["These offending features gave character to her whole face, but she never could see it, and consoled herself with her wonderfully fair complexion, keen blue eyes, and curls, more golden and abundant than ever.", "이 거슬리는 이목구비가 그녀의 얼굴 전체에 개성을 부여했지만, 정작 그녀는 그것을 알아보지 못했고, 놀랍도록 흰 살결과 또렷한 푸른 눈, 그리고 그 어느 때보다 더 금빛으로 풍성해진 곱슬머리로 스스로를 위로했다."]], [["All three wore suits of thin silver gray (their best gowns for the summer), with blush-roses in hair and bosom; and all three looked just what they were,--fresh-faced, happy-hearted girls, pausing a moment in their busy lives to read with wistful eyes the sweetest chapter in the romance of womanhood.", "세 사람 모두 얇은 은회색 정장(여름철 가장 좋은 드레스)을 입고, 머리와 가슴에는 연분홍 장미를 달았다. 그리고 세 사람 모두 꼭 있는 그대로의 모습으로 보였다. 싱그러운 얼굴에 행복한 마음을 지닌 처녀들이, 분주한 삶 속에서 잠시 멈추어 서서 여성의 로맨스 가운데 가장 달콤한 장을 동경 어린 눈으로 읽고 있는 모습이었다."]], [["There were to be no ceremonious performances, everything was to be as natural and homelike as possible; so when Aunt March arrived, she was scandalized to see the bride come running to welcome and lead her in, to find the bridegroom fastening up a garland that had fallen down, and to catch a glimpse of the paternal minister marching upstairs with a grave countenance, and a wine-bottle under each arm.", "격식을 차린 절차는 일절 없을 것이었고, 모든 것이 가능한 한 자연스럽고 가정적으로 치러질 것이었다. 그래서 마치 대고모가 도착했을 때, 그녀는 그 광경에 어처구니가 없었다. 신부가 자기를 맞이하러 달려 나와 안으로 이끄는가 하면, 신랑이 떨어진 꽃 장식을 다시 매다는 모습이 눈에 들어왔고, 부친이자 목사인 이가 양팔에 포도주병을 하나씩 낀 채 근엄한 얼굴로 위층으로 올라가는 모습이 언뜻 보였으니 말이다."]], [["\"Upon my word, here's a state of things!\"", "\"세상에, 이게 무슨 꼴이람!\""], ["cried the old lady, taking the seat of honor prepared for her, and settling the folds of her lavender _moire_ with a great rustle.", "노부인이 외쳤다. 자기를 위해 마련된 상석에 앉으며, 라벤더색 모아레 옷자락을 요란하게 바스락거리며 여미면서."], ["\"You oughtn't to be seen till the last minute, child.\"", "\"신부는 마지막 순간까지 모습을 보여선 안 되는 거야, 얘야.\""]], [["\"I'm not a show, aunty, and no one is coming to stare at me, to criticise my dress, or count the cost of my luncheon.", "\"전 구경거리가 아니에요, 대고모님. 게다가 누가 절 빤히 쳐다보거나, 제 드레스를 흠잡거나, 점심값을 따져 보러 오는 것도 아닌걸요."], ["I'm too happy to care what any one says or thinks, and I'm going to have my little wedding just as I like it.", "전 누가 무슨 말을 하든 무슨 생각을 하든 신경 쓰지 않을 만큼 행복하고, 제 작은 결혼식을 제가 원하는 그대로 치를 거예요."], ["John, dear, here's your hammer;\"", "존, 여기 망치 있어요.\""], ["and away went Meg to help \"that man\" in his highly improper employment.", "그러고서 메그는 '저 남자'가 하던 그 지극히 부적절한 일을 도우러 가 버렸다."]], [["Mr. Brooke didn't even say \"Thank you,\"", "브룩 씨는 \"고마워요\"라는 말조차 하지 않았지만, 그 낭만이라곤 없는 연장을 집으려 몸을 굽히면서, 접이문 뒤에서 자신의 어린 신부에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["but as he stooped for the unromantic tool, he kissed his little bride behind the folding-door, with a look that made Aunt March whisk out her pocket-handkerchief, with a sudden dew in her sharp old eyes.", "그 눈빛에 마치 대고모는 손수건을 홱 꺼내 들었고, 매서운 노안에 불현듯 이슬이 맺혔다."]], [["A crash, a cry, and a laugh from Laurie, accompanied by the indecorous exclamation, \"Jupiter Ammon!", "쨍그랑 소리, 외마디 비명, 그리고 로리의 웃음소리가, \"주피터 암몬이시여! 조가 또 케이크를 엎었어요!\""], ["Jo's upset the cake again!\"", "하는 무례한 외침과 함께 터져 나와 한바탕 소동이 일었다."], ["caused a momentary flurry, which was hardly over when a flock of cousins arrived, and \"the party came in,\" as Beth used to say when a child.", "그 소동이 채 가라앉기도 전에 사촌들이 떼 지어 도착했고, 베스가 어렸을 때 곧잘 말하던 대로 \"손님들이 들이닥쳤다.\""]], [["\"Don't let that young giant come near me; he worries me worse than mosquitoes,\"", "\"저 젊은 거인이 내 근처엔 얼씬도 못 하게 해라. 모기보다 더 성가신 녀석이니.\""], ["whispered the old lady to Amy, as the rooms filled, and Laurie's black head towered above the rest.", "노부인이 에이미에게 속삭였다. 방마다 사람들로 들어차고, 로리의 검은 머리가 다른 이들 위로 우뚝 솟아 있을 때였다."]], [["\"He has promised to be very good to-day, and he _can_ be perfectly elegant if he likes,\"", "\"오늘은 아주 얌전히 굴겠다고 약속했고, 마음만 먹으면 더없이 우아하게 굴 수 있는 애예요.\""], ["returned Amy, gliding away to warn Hercules to beware of the dragon, which warning caused him to haunt the old lady with a devotion that nearly distracted her.", "에이미가 대답하며, 헤라클레스에게 그 용을 조심하라 일러 주러 슬며시 자리를 떴다. 그 경고에 로리는 도리어 노부인 곁을 떠나지 않고 어찌나 극진하게 시중을 들었던지, 노부인은 거의 정신이 다 나갈 지경이었다."]], [["There was no bridal procession, but a sudden silence fell upon the room as Mr.", "신부 행렬 같은 건 없었지만, 마치 씨와 젊은 한 쌍이 푸른 아치 아래 자리를 잡자 방 안에 문득 정적이 내려앉았다."], ["March and the young pair took their places under the green arch.", "어머니와 자매들이 메그를 떠나보내기 아쉬운 듯 바짝 모여들었다."], ["Mother and sisters gathered close, as if loath to give Meg up; the fatherly voice broke more than once, which only seemed to make the service more beautiful and solemn; the bridegroom's hand trembled visibly, and no one heard his replies; but Meg looked straight up in her husband's eyes, and said, \"I will!\"", "아버지의 목소리가 한 번 이상 갈라졌는데, 그것은 오히려 그 예식을 한층 더 아름답고 엄숙하게 만드는 것 같았다. 신랑의 손은 눈에 띄게 떨렸고, 그의 대답을 들은 사람은 아무도 없었다."], ["with such tender trust in her own face and voice that her mother's heart rejoiced, and Aunt March sniffed audibly.", "그러나 메그는 남편의 두 눈을 똑바로 올려다보며 \"네, 그러겠습니다!\"라고 말했다. 그 얼굴과 목소리에 어찌나 다정한 믿음이 담겨 있던지, 어머니의 가슴은 기쁨으로 벅찼고, 마치 대고모는 들릴 만큼 코를 훌쩍였다."]], [["Jo did _not_ cry, though she was very near it once, and was only saved from a demonstration by the consciousness that Laurie was staring fixedly at her, with a comical mixture of merriment and emotion in his wicked black eyes. Beth kept her face hidden on her mother's shoulder, but Amy stood like a graceful statue, with a most becoming ray of sunshine touching her white forehead and the flower in her hair.", "조는 울지 않았다. 한 번은 울 뻔했지만 말이다. 다만 로리가 그 짓궂은 검은 눈에 익살과 감정이 우스꽝스럽게 뒤섞인 표정으로 자기를 빤히 쳐다보고 있다는 것을 의식한 덕분에 가까스로 눈물을 보이지 않을 수 있었다. 베스는 어머니의 어깨에 얼굴을 묻고 있었지만, 에이미는 우아한 조각상처럼 서 있었다. 더없이 잘 어울리는 한 줄기 햇살이 그녀의 흰 이마와 머리에 꽂은 꽃을 어루만지고 있었다."]], [["It wasn't at all the thing, I'm afraid, but the minute she was fairly married, Meg cried, \"The first kiss for Marmee!\"", "이건 도무지 격식에 맞는 일이 아니었지 싶지만, 결혼이 완전히 성립된 그 순간, 메그가 외쳤다. \"첫 입맞춤은 엄마에게!\""], ["and, turning, gave it with her heart on her lips.", "그러고는 돌아서서 마음을 입술에 담아 그 입맞춤을 했다."], ["During the next fifteen minutes she looked more like a rose than ever, for every one availed themselves of their privileges to the fullest extent, from Mr.", "이어진 십오 분 동안 그녀는 그 어느 때보다 한 송이 장미처럼 보였다. 로런스 씨부터 늙은 한나에 이르기까지 모두가 저마다의 권리를 한껏 누렸기 때문이다."], ["Laurence to old Hannah, who, adorned with a head-dress fearfully and wonderfully made, fell upon her in the hall, crying, with a sob and a chuckle, \"Bless you, deary, a hundred times!", "한나는 무시무시하고도 신묘하게 만들어진 머리 장식으로 치장한 채, 복도에서 그녀에게 와락 달려들어 흐느낌과 너털웃음을 섞어 가며 외쳤다. \"복 받으세요, 아가씨, 백 번이라도요!"], ["The cake ain't hurt a mite, and everything looks lovely.\"", "케이크는 조금도 안 상했고, 모든 게 다 사랑스럽기만 하답니다.\""]], [["Everybody cleared up after that, and said something brilliant, or tried to, which did just as well, for laughter is ready when hearts are light.", "그 후로 모두가 마음을 가다듬고는, 무언가 재치 있는 말을 하거나, 적어도 하려고 애썼는데, 그것만으로도 충분했다. 마음이 가벼울 때면 웃음은 언제든 터져 나올 채비가 되어 있는 법이니까."], ["There was no display of gifts, for they were already in the little house, nor was there an elaborate breakfast, but a plentiful lunch of cake and fruit, dressed with flowers.", "선물을 늘어놓는 일은 없었다. 이미 다 그 작은 집에 가 있었으니까."], ["Mr.", "거창한 아침 식사도 없었고, 다만 꽃으로 장식한 케이크와 과일을 푸짐하게 차린 점심이 있었다."], ["Laurence and Aunt March shrugged and smiled at one another when water, lemonade, and coffee were found to be the only sorts of nectar which the three Hebes carried round.", "물과 레모네이드와 커피가 세 명의 헤베가 들고 도는 유일한 종류의 음료임이 드러나자, 로런스 씨와 마치 대고모는 서로를 보며 어깨를 으쓱하고 미소 지었다. 그러나 아무도 아무 말 하지 않았다."], ["No one said anything, however, till Laurie, who insisted on serving the bride, appeared before her, with a loaded salver in his hand and a puzzled expression on his face.", "신부 시중을 한사코 들겠다던 로리가 잔뜩 채운 쟁반을 손에 들고 어리둥절한 표정으로 그녀 앞에 나타나기 전까지는. 헤베: 그리스 신화에서 신들에게 음료를 따라 주던 청춘의 여신."]], [["\"Has Jo smashed all the bottles by accident?\"", "\"조가 실수로 병을 죄다 깨뜨린 거예요?\""], ["he whispered, \"or am I merely laboring under a delusion that I saw some lying about loose this morning?\"", "그가 속삭였다. \"아니면 오늘 아침 여기저기 병이 굴러다니는 걸 봤다고 제가 그냥 착각하는 건가요?\""]], [["\"No; your grandfather kindly offered us his best, and Aunt March actually sent some, but father put away a little for Beth, and despatched the rest to the Soldiers'", "\"아니야. 너희 할아버지께서 친절하게도 가장 좋은 걸 내주셨고, 마치 대고모님도 실제로 좀 보내 주셨어."], ["Home.", "하지만 아버지가 베스를 위해 조금 따로 챙겨 두시고는, 나머지는 상이용사 요양소로 보내셨지."], ["You know he thinks that wine should be used only in illness, and mother says that neither she nor her daughters will ever offer it to any young man under her roof.\"", "알다시피 아버지는 포도주란 병이 났을 때만 써야 한다고 생각하시거든. 그리고 엄마는, 당신도 당신 딸들도 이 지붕 아래에서는 어떤 젊은이에게도 결코 술을 권하지 않을 거라고 하셨어.\""]], [["Meg spoke seriously, and expected to see Laurie frown or laugh; but he did neither, for after a quick look at her, he said, in his impetuous way, \"I like that! for I've seen enough harm done to wish other women would think as you do.\"", "메그는 진지하게 말하며, 로리가 인상을 찌푸리거나 웃음을 터뜨리리라 예상했다. 그러나 그는 둘 중 어느 것도 하지 않았다. 그녀를 흘끗 보더니, 그 특유의 충동적인 투로 말했다. \"그거 마음에 드네요! 저도 그 폐해를 충분히 봐 와서, 다른 여자들도 부인처럼 생각해 주었으면 싶거든요.\""]], [["\"You are not made wise by experience, I hope?\"", "\"너는 경험으로 그걸 깨우친 건 아니길 바라.\""], ["and there was an anxious accent in Meg's voice.", "메그의 목소리에 근심 어린 가락이 묻어났다."]], [["\"No; I give you my word for it.", "\"아니에요. 맹세코 그건 아니에요."], ["Don't think too well of me, either; this is not one of my temptations.", "그렇다고 저를 너무 좋게 보지도 마세요. 이건 제 유혹거리 축에도 못 끼니까요."], ["Being brought up where wine is as common as water, and almost as harmless, I don't care for it; but when a pretty girl offers it, one doesn't like to refuse, you see.\"", "포도주가 물만큼이나 흔하고, 또 물만큼이나 해롭지 않은 곳에서 자라다 보니, 저는 거기에 별 관심이 없어요. 다만 예쁜 아가씨가 권할 때는, 보다시피 거절하기가 좀 그렇잖아요.\""]], [["\"But you will, for the sake of others, if not for your own.", "\"하지만 너는 거절할 거야. 네 자신을 위해서가 아니라면 다른 사람들을 위해서라도."], ["Come, Laurie, promise, and give me one more reason to call this the happiest day of my life.\"", "자, 로리, 약속해. 그래서 오늘을 내 인생에서 가장 행복한 날이라 부를 이유를 하나 더 만들어 줘.\""]], [["A demand so sudden and so serious made the young man hesitate a moment, for ridicule is often harder to bear than self-denial.", "이토록 갑작스럽고도 진지한 요구에 젊은이는 잠시 망설였다. 조롱은 종종 극기보다 견디기가 더 힘든 법이니까."], ["Meg knew that if he gave the promise he would keep it at all costs; and, feeling her power, used it as a woman may for her friend's good.", "메그는 만일 그가 약속한다면 무슨 일이 있어도 그것을 지키리라는 것을 알았기에, 자신의 힘을 느끼며 그것을 한 여인이 벗의 이로움을 위해 쓸 법한 방식으로 썼다."], ["She did not speak, but she looked up at him with a face made very eloquent by happiness, and a smile which said, \"No one can refuse me anything to-day.\"", "그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 다만 행복으로 한껏 웅변적이 된 얼굴로 그를 올려다보았고, \"오늘은 누구도 내 청을 거절할 수 없어요\"라고 말하는 듯한 미소를 지었다."], ["Laurie certainly could not; and, with an answering smile, he gave her his hand, saying heartily, \"I promise, Mrs.", "로리는 분명 거절할 수 없었다. 화답하는 미소와 함께 그는 그녀에게 손을 내밀며 진심으로 말했다."], ["Brooke!\"", "\"약속할게요, 브룩 부인!\""]], [["\"I thank you, very, very much.\"", "\"정말, 정말 고마워.\""]], [["\"And I drink 'long life to your resolution,' Teddy,\"", "\"그럼 나는 '네 결심이여 만수무강하라'를 위해 건배하겠어, 테디.\""], ["cried Jo, baptizing him with a splash of lemonade, as she waved her glass, and beamed approvingly upon him.", "조가 외치며, 잔을 흔들어 레모네이드를 한 줄기 끼얹어 그에게 세례를 베풀고는, 흐뭇하게 그를 향해 환히 웃었다."]], [["So the toast was drunk, the pledge made, and loyally kept, in spite of many temptations; for, with instinctive wisdom, the girls had seized a happy moment to do their friend a service, for which he thanked them all his life.", "그리하여 건배가 이루어졌고, 맹세가 세워졌으며, 숱한 유혹에도 불구하고 충실히 지켜졌다. 본능적인 지혜로 소녀들이 행복한 한순간을 붙들어 벗에게 봉사를 베푼 것이었고, 그는 평생토록 그들에게 감사했다."]], [["After lunch, people strolled about, by twos and threes, through house and garden, enjoying the sunshine without and within.", "점심 식사 후 사람들은 둘씩 셋씩 짝지어 집과 정원을 거닐며, 안팎의 햇살을 만끽했다."], ["Meg and John happened to be standing together in the middle of the grass-plot, when Laurie was seized with an inspiration which put the finishing touch to this unfashionable wedding.", "메그와 존이 마침 잔디밭 한가운데 함께 서 있을 때, 로리에게 영감이 떠올랐고, 그것이 이 격식 없는 결혼식에 마지막 화룡점정을 찍었다."]], [["\"All the married people take hands and dance round the new-made husband and wife, as the Germans do, while we bachelors and spinsters prance in couples outside!\"", "\"기혼자들은 모두 손을 잡고 갓 부부가 된 신랑 신부를 둘러싸고 빙빙 돌아요, 독일 사람들이 하듯이요. 그동안 우리 미혼 총각 처녀들은 바깥에서 짝지어 깡충깡충 추는 거예요!\""], ["cried Laurie, promenading down the path with Amy, with such infectious spirit and skill that every one else followed their example without a murmur. Mr.", "로리가 외치며 에이미와 함께 길을 따라 거니는데, 그 흥과 솜씨가 어찌나 사람을 끌어들이던지 다른 이들도 군말 없이 그들을 따라 했다."], ["and Mrs. March, Aunt and Uncle Carrol, began it; others rapidly joined in; even Sallie Moffat, after a moment's hesitation, threw her train over her arm, and whisked Ned into the ring.", "마치 부부와 캐럴 이모 내외가 먼저 시작했고, 다른 이들이 빠르게 합류했다."], ["But the crowning joke was Mr.", "샐리 모팻조차 잠시 머뭇거리더니 옷자락을 팔에 척 걸치고는 네드를 휙 끌어들여 원 안으로 들어왔다. 그러나 가장 압권은 로런스 씨와 마치 대고모였다."], ["Laurence and Aunt March; for when the stately old gentleman _chasséed_ solemnly up to the old lady, she just tucked her cane under her arm, and hopped briskly away to join hands with the rest, and dance about the bridal pair, while the young folks pervaded the garden, like butterflies on a midsummer day.", "그 위엄 있는 노신사가 근엄하게 미끄러지듯 스텝을 밟으며 노부인에게 다가가자, 그녀가 지팡이를 팔 밑에 척 끼우고는 경쾌하게 깡충깡충 뛰어가 다른 이들과 손을 맞잡고 신랑 신부를 둘러싸고 춤을 추었으니. 그러는 동안 젊은이들은 한여름의 나비처럼 정원을 누비고 다녔다."]], [["Want of breath brought the impromptu ball to a close, and then people began to go.", "숨이 차오르자 이 즉흥 무도회는 막을 내렸고, 그러고 나서 사람들이 하나둘 돌아가기 시작했다."]], [["\"I wish you well, my dear, I heartily wish you well; but I think you'll be sorry for it,\" said Aunt March to Meg, adding to the bridegroom, as he led her to the carriage, \"You've got a treasure, young man, see that you deserve it.\"", "\"잘 살길 바라, 얘야, 진심으로 잘 살길 바란다. 하지만 내 생각엔 네가 후회하게 될 것 같구나.\" 마치 대고모가 메그에게 말했다. 그러고는 신랑이 자기를 마차로 안내하자 그에게 덧붙였다. \"자네는 보물을 얻은 거네, 젊은이. 그 보물에 걸맞은 사람이 되도록 하게.\""]], [["\"That is the prettiest wedding I've been to for an age, Ned, and I don't see why, for there wasn't a bit of style about it,\"", "\"이렇게 사랑스러운 결혼식은 정말 오랜만에 가 보네요, 네드."], ["observed Mrs.", "왜 그런지는 모르겠어요."], ["Moffat to her husband, as they drove away.", "격식이라곤 눈곱만치도 없었는데 말이에요.\" 모팻 부인이 마차를 몰고 떠나며 남편에게 말했다."]], [["\"Laurie, my lad, if you ever want to indulge in this sort of thing, get one of those little girls to help you, and I shall be perfectly satisfied,\"", "\"로리, 얘야, 네가 언젠가 이런 걸 한번 누려 보고 싶거든, 저 어린 아가씨들 중 하나에게 도와 달라고 하렴. 그러면 나는 더없이 흡족할 거다.\""], ["said Mr.", "로런스 씨가 말했다."], ["Laurence, settling himself in his easy-chair to rest, after the excitement of the morning.", "아침나절의 흥분이 지나고 안락의자에 몸을 묻고 쉬면서."]], [["\"I'll do my best to gratify you, sir,\"", "\"최선을 다해 할아버지를 흡족하게 해 드리지요.\""], ["was Laurie's unusually dutiful reply, as he carefully unpinned the posy Jo had put in his button-hole.", "로리가 평소답지 않게 고분고분 대답했다. 그러면서 조가 자기 단춧구멍에 꽂아 준 꽃을 조심스레 빼냈다."]], [["The little house was not far away, and the only bridal journey Meg had was the quiet walk with John, from the old home to the new.", "그 작은 집은 멀지 않았고, 메그에게 주어진 유일한 신혼여행이라곤 옛집에서 새집까지 존과 함께 걷는 조용한 산책뿐이었다."], ["When she came down, looking like a pretty Quakeress in her dove-colored suit and straw bonnet tied with white, they all gathered about her to say \"good-by,\"", "그녀가 비둘기색 정장 차림에 흰 끈으로 묶은 밀짚 보닛을 쓰고, 어여쁜 퀘이커 교도처럼 보이는 모습으로 내려오자, 모두가 그녀 주위로 모여들어 \"잘 가\"라는 인사를 건넸다."], ["as tenderly as if she had been going to make the grand tour.", "마치 그녀가 세계 일주 대여행이라도 떠나는 양 다정하게."]], [["\"Don't feel that I am separated from you, Marmee dear, or that I love you any the less for loving John so much,\"", "\"제가 엄마에게서 떨어져 나간다고 느끼지 마세요, 사랑하는 엄마. 존을 이렇게 많이 사랑한다고 해서 엄마를 조금이라도 덜 사랑하는 거라고도 생각하지 마시고요.\""], ["she said, clinging to her mother, with full eyes, for a moment.", "그녀가 눈물 그렁한 눈으로 어머니에게 잠시 매달리며 말했다."], ["\"I shall come every day, father, and expect to keep my old place in all your hearts, though I _am_ married.", "\"매일 올 거예요, 아버지. 그리고 비록 결혼은 했지만, 여러분 모두의 마음속에서 제 옛 자리를 그대로 지킬 작정이에요."], ["Beth is going to be with me a great deal, and the other girls will drop in now and then to laugh at my housekeeping struggles.", "베스가 저랑 자주 함께 지낼 거고, 다른 동생들도 이따금 들러서 제 살림 분투기를 보며 웃어 주겠지요."], ["Thank you all for my happy wedding-day.", "다들 제게 행복한 결혼식을 만들어 줘서 고마워요."], ["Good-by, good-by!\"", "잘 있어요, 잘 있어요!\""]], [["They stood watching her, with faces full of love and hope and tender pride, as she walked away, leaning on her husband's arm, with her hands full of flowers, and the June sunshine brightening her happy face,--and so Meg's married life began.", "그들은 사랑과 희망과 다정한 자랑스러움이 가득한 얼굴로 그녀를 지켜보며 서 있었다. 그녀가 남편의 팔에 기대어, 두 손 가득 꽃을 들고, 6월의 햇살이 그 행복한 얼굴을 환히 비추는 가운데 걸어가는 모습을. 그리하여 메그의 결혼 생활이 시작되었다. 스물여섯"]], [["# ARTISTIC ATTEMPTS.", "# ARTISTIC ATTEMPTS."]], [["It takes people a long time to learn the difference between talent and genius, especially ambitious young men and women.", "예술적 시도\n사람들이 재능과 천재의 차이를 깨닫는 데는 오랜 시간이 걸린다. 특히 야심 찬 젊은 남녀가 그러하다."], ["Amy was learning this distinction through much tribulation; for, mistaking enthusiasm for inspiration, she attempted every branch of art with youthful audacity.", "에이미는 숱한 시련을 겪으며 이 차이를 배워 나가고 있었다. 열정을 영감으로 착각한 나머지, 그녀는 젊은이다운 대담함으로 미술의 온갖 분야에 손을 댔던 것이다."], ["For a long time there was a lull in the \"mud-pie\"", "한동안은 '진흙 파이'"], ["business, and she devoted herself to the finest pen-and-ink drawing, in which she showed such taste and skill that her graceful handiwork proved both pleasant and profitable.", "사업이 잠잠했고, 그녀는 더없이 섬세한 펜화에 몰두했다. 거기서 그녀는 어찌나 안목과 솜씨를 발휘했던지, 그 우아한 작품들은 즐거움이자 돈벌이가 되어 주었다."], ["But overstrained eyes soon caused pen and ink to be laid aside for a bold attempt at poker-sketching.", "그러나 눈을 너무 혹사한 탓에 펜과 잉크는 이내 밀쳐 두고, 인두 그림이라는 대담한 시도에 나서게 되었다."], ["While this attack lasted, the family lived in constant fear of a conflagration; for the odor of burning wood pervaded the house at all hours; smoke issued from attic and shed with alarming frequency, red-hot pokers lay about promiscuously, and Hannah never went to bed without a pail of water and the dinner-bell at her door, in case of fire.", "이 발작이 이어지는 동안 온 가족은 불이 날까 봐 끊임없이 마음을 졸였다. 나무 타는 냄새가 시도 때도 없이 집 안에 진동했고, 다락방과 헛간에서 연기가 놀랍도록 자주 피어올랐으며, 시뻘겋게 달군 인두가 여기저기 어지러이 놓여 있었고, 한나는 불이 날까 봐 물 한 동이와 저녁 식사용 종을 문가에 두지 않고서는 결코 잠자리에 들지 않았다."], ["Raphael's face was found boldly executed on the under side of the moulding-board, and Bacchus on the head of a beer-barrel; a chanting cherub adorned the cover of the sugar-bucket, and attempts to portray Romeo and Juliet supplied kindlings for some time.", "라파엘로의 얼굴이 반죽판 밑면에 대담하게 새겨진 채 발견되었고, 맥주 통 머리에는 바쿠스가, 설탕 통 뚜껑에는 노래하는 천사가 그려져 있었으며, 로미오와 줄리엣을 그리려던 시도들은 한동안 불쏘시개 노릇을 했다."]], [["From fire to oil was a natural transition for burnt fingers, and Amy fell to painting with undiminished ardor.", "불에 덴 손가락으로서는 불에서 기름으로 옮겨 가는 게 자연스러운 수순이었고, 에이미는 식지 않은 열정으로 유화에 빠져들었다."], ["An artist friend fitted her out with his cast-off palettes, brushes, and colors; and she daubed away, producing pastoral and marine views such as were never seen on land or sea.", "화가인 친구 하나가 자신이 쓰던 팔레트와 붓과 물감을 그녀에게 마련해 주었고, 그녀는 마구 칠해 댔다. 그렇게 해서 땅에서도 바다에서도 결코 본 적 없는 목가화와 바다 그림을 만들어 냈다."], ["Her monstrosities in the way of cattle would have taken prizes at an agricultural fair; and the perilous pitching of her vessels would have produced sea-sickness in the most nautical observer, if the utter disregard to all known rules of shipbuilding and rigging had not convulsed him with laughter at the first glance.", "그녀가 그린 소 따위의 괴물들은 농업 박람회에서 상을 탔을 법했고, 위태롭게 출렁이는 그녀의 배들은 아무리 뱃멀미에 이골이 난 관객이라도 멀미를 일으켰을 법했다. 다만 알려진 모든 조선과 삭구의 법칙을 깡그리 무시한 탓에 첫눈에 보는 이가 웃음을 터뜨리지만 않았다면 말이다."], ["Swarthy boys and dark-eyed Madonnas, staring at you from one corner of the studio, suggested Murillo; oily-brown shadows of faces, with a lurid streak in the wrong place, meant Rembrandt; buxom ladies and dropsical infants, Rubens; and Turner appeared in tempests of blue thunder, orange lightning, brown rain, and purple clouds, with a tomato-colored splash in the middle, which might be the sun or a buoy, a sailor's shirt or a king's robe, as the spectator pleased.", "화실 한 귀퉁이에서 당신을 빤히 쳐다보는 가무잡잡한 소년들과 검은 눈의 성모들은 무리요를 떠올리게 했고, 엉뚱한 자리에 섬뜩한 줄무늬가 들어간 기름진 갈색의 얼굴 그림자들은 렘브란트를 뜻했으며, 풍만한 여인들과 부종 걸린 아기들은 루벤스를, 그리고 터너는 푸른 천둥과 주황색 번개와 갈색 비와 보랏빛 구름의 폭풍 속에 등장했는데, 그 한가운데 토마토색 얼룩이 하나 있어서, 보는 사람 마음대로 그것은 태양일 수도 부표일 수도, 선원의 셔츠일 수도 왕의 예복일 수도 있었다."]], [["Charcoal portraits came next; and the entire family hung in a row, looking as wild and crocky as if just evoked from a coal-bin.", "다음은 목탄 초상화 차례였다. 온 가족이 한 줄로 죽 걸렸는데, 방금 석탄 통에서 불려 나온 것처럼 거칠고 검댕투성이로 보였다."], ["Softened into crayon sketches, they did better; for the likenesses were good, and Amy's hair, Jo's nose, Meg's mouth, and Laurie's eyes were pronounced \"wonderfully fine.\"", "크레용 스케치로 부드럽게 하니 한결 나았다. 닮은 구석이 제법 있어서, 에이미의 머리카락, 조의 코, 메그의 입, 로리의 눈은 \"놀랍도록 잘 그렸다\"는 평을 받았다."], ["A return to clay and plaster followed, and ghostly casts of her acquaintances haunted corners of the house, or tumbled off closet-shelves on to people's heads.", "이어서 점토와 석고로 돌아왔고, 지인들의 으스스한 본뜬 상이 집 안 구석구석에 출몰하거나, 벽장 선반에서 굴러떨어져 사람들 머리 위로 떨어지곤 했다."], ["Children were enticed in as models, till their incoherent accounts of her mysterious doings caused Miss Amy to be regarded in the light of a young ogress. Her efforts in this line, however, were brought to an abrupt close by an untoward accident, which quenched her ardor.", "아이들이 모델로 꾀어 들여졌는데, 급기야 그녀가 벌이는 알 수 없는 짓에 대한 아이들의 횡설수설한 설명 탓에 에이미 양은 어린 식인귀 취급을 받기에 이르렀다."], ["Other models failing her for a time, she undertook to cast her own pretty foot, and the family were one day alarmed by an unearthly bumping and screaming, and running to the rescue, found the young enthusiast hopping wildly about the shed, with her foot held fast in a pan-full of plaster, which had hardened with unexpected rapidity.", "그러나 이 방면에서의 그녀의 노력은 한 가지 불운한 사고로 별안간 막을 내렸고, 그 사고가 그녀의 열정을 꺼뜨렸다. 한동안 다른 모델들이 구해지지 않자, 그녀는 자기 자신의 예쁜 발을 본뜨는 일에 나섰다."], ["With much difficulty and some danger she was dug out; for Jo was so overcome with laughter while she excavated, that her knife went too far, cut the poor foot, and left a lasting memorial of one artistic attempt, at least.", "그러던 어느 날, 가족은 섬뜩한 쿵쿵 소리와 비명에 깜짝 놀랐고, 구하러 달려가 보니,\n헛간 안을 정신없이 폴짝거리는 어린 예술가의 발이, 예상보다 훨씬 빨리 굳어 버린 석고가 가득한 냄비 속에 꼼짝없이 박혀 있었다."]], [["After this Amy subsided, till a mania for sketching from nature set her to haunting river, field, and wood, for picturesque studies, and sighing for ruins to copy.", "상당한 어려움과 약간의 위험을 무릅쓰고 나서야 에이미는 그 속에서 파내어졌다. 조가 발을 파내는 동안 웃음을 참지 못한 탓에 칼이 너무 깊이 들어가, 가엾은 발을 베어 적어도 한 번의 예술적 시도에 길이 남을 기념물을 새겨 놓고 만 것이었다."], ["She caught endless colds sitting on damp grass to book \"a delicious bit,\"", "그 일이 있은 뒤로 에이미는 한동안 잠잠했다."], ["composed of a stone, a stump, one mushroom, and a broken mullein-stalk, or \"a heavenly mass of clouds,\"", "그러다 자연을 사생하고 싶다는 열병에 사로잡혀, 그림 같은 풍경을 찾아 강과 들판과 숲을 헤매고, 베껴 그릴 폐허가 없다며 한숨짓기 시작했다."], ["that looked like a choice display of feather-beds when done. She sacrificed her complexion floating on the river in the midsummer sun, to study light and shade, and got a wrinkle over her nose, trying after \"points of sight,\"", "돌멩이 하나, 그루터기 하나, 버섯 하나, 부러진 우단담배풀 줄기로 이루어진 \"기막히게 멋진 한 폭\"을 화첩에 담겠다고 축축한 풀밭에 앉아 있다가 끝없이 감기에 걸렸고, 다 그려 놓고 보면 깃털 이불을 죽 늘어놓은 것처럼 보이는 \"천상의 구름 떼\"를 그리겠다고도 했다."], ["or whatever the squint-and-string performance is called.", "빛과 그림자를 연구한답시고 한여름 뙤약볕 아래 강에 떠 있느라 고운 피부를 망쳤고, \"시점(視點)\"인지 뭔지, 실눈을 뜨고 실로 재는 그 일을 하느라 콧잔등에 주름이 잡혔다."]], [["If \"genius is eternal patience,\"", "미켈란젤로의 말마따나 \"천재란 영원한 인내\"라면, 에이미에게도 그 신성한 자질에 대한 권리가 어느 정도는 있었다."], ["as Michael Angelo affirms, Amy certainly had some claim to the divine attribute, for she persevered in spite of all obstacles, failures, and discouragements, firmly believing that in time she should do something worthy to be called \"high art.\"", "온갖 장애와 실패, 좌절에도 굴하지 않고 끈질기게 매달리며, 언젠가는 \"고매한 예술\"이라 불릴 만한 무언가를 해내리라 굳게 믿었으니 말이다."]], [["She was learning, doing, and enjoying other things, meanwhile, for she had resolved to be an attractive and accomplished woman, even if she never became a great artist.", "그러는 동안에도 에이미는 다른 여러 가지를 배우고, 해내고, 즐겼다. 위대한 예술가가 되지 못하더라도 매력 있고 교양 있는 여인이 되겠다고 마음먹은 까닭이었다."], ["Here she succeeded better; for she was one of those happily created beings who please without effort, make friends everywhere, and take life so gracefully and easily that less fortunate souls are tempted to believe that such are born under a lucky star.", "이 점에서는 더 잘 해냈다. 에이미는 애쓰지 않고도 사람들을 기쁘게 하고, 어디서든 친구를 사귀며, 인생을 더없이 우아하고 손쉽게 헤쳐 나가는, 행복하게 태어난 부류 가운데 하나여서, 덜 운 좋은 이들은 그런 사람은 행운의 별 아래 태어난 게 분명하다고 믿고 싶어질 정도였다."], ["Everybody liked her, for among her good gifts was tact.", "모두가 에이미를 좋아했다. 그녀가 지닌 여러 장점 가운데 하나가 바로 눈치였기 때문이다."], ["She had an instinctive sense of what was pleasing and proper, always said the right thing to the right person, did just what suited the time and place, and was so self-possessed that her sisters used to say, \"If Amy went to court without any rehearsal beforehand, she'd know exactly what to do.\"", "무엇이 사람을 기쁘게 하고 무엇이 알맞은지를 본능적으로 알아챘고, 늘 알맞은 사람에게 알맞은 말을 했으며, 때와 장소에 꼭 맞게 행동했고, 어찌나 침착한지 언니들이 이런 말을 할 정도였다. \"에이미는 미리 연습 한 번 안 하고 궁궐에 가도 어떻게 처신해야 할지 정확히 알 거야.\""]], [["One of her weaknesses was a desire to move in \"our best society,\" without being quite sure what the _best_ really was.", "에이미의 약점 가운데 하나는 \"우리네 상류 사회\"에서 어울리고 싶어 한다는 것이었는데, 정작 진정한 상류가 무엇인지는 잘 알지 못했다."], ["Money, position, fashionable accomplishments, and elegant manners were most desirable things in her eyes, and she liked to associate with those who possessed them, often mistaking the false for the true, and admiring what was not admirable.", "돈과 지위, 세련된 교양과 우아한 몸가짐이 그녀의 눈에는 가장 탐나는 것들이었고, 그런 것을 지닌 이들과 어울리기를 좋아한 나머지, 가짜를 진짜로 착각하고 감탄할 만하지 않은 것에 감탄하는 일도 잦았다."], ["Never forgetting that by birth she was a gentlewoman, she cultivated her aristocratic tastes and feelings, so that when the opportunity came she might be ready to take the place from which poverty now excluded her.", "태생이 귀부인이라는 사실을 결코 잊지 않았기에, 귀족다운 취향과 감성을 가다듬어, 기회가 오면 지금은 가난이 가로막고 있는 그 자리를 차지할 준비가 되어 있고자 했다."]], [["\"My lady,\" as her friends called her, sincerely desired to be a genuine lady, and was so at heart, but had yet to learn that money cannot buy refinement of nature, that rank does not always confer nobility, and that true breeding makes itself felt in spite of external drawbacks.", "친구들이 부르듯 \"우리 마님\"은 진심으로 진정한 숙녀가 되기를 바랐고, 마음속으로는 이미 그러했지만, 돈으로 천성의 우아함을 살 수 없다는 것, 지위가 늘 고귀함을 부여하지는 않는다는 것, 그리고 참된 교양은 외형적 결점에도 불구하고 저절로 드러난다는 것은 아직 배워야 할 숙제였다."]], [["\"I want to ask a favor of you, mamma,\"", "\"엄마, 부탁드릴 일이 있어요.\""], ["Amy said, coming in, with an important air, one day.", "어느 날 에이미가 자못 진지한 태도로 들어서며 말했다."]], [["\"Well, little girl, what is it?\"", "\"그래, 우리 막내, 뭔데?\""], ["replied her mother, in whose eyes the stately young lady still remained \"the baby.\"", "어머니가 대답했다. 어머니의 눈에는 이 의젓한 아가씨가 여전히 \"갓난아기\"로 남아 있었다."]], [["\"Our drawing class breaks up next week, and before the girls separate for the summer, I want to ask them out here for a day.", "\"우리 그림 수업이 다음 주에 끝나는데요, 여자애들이 여름 동안 흩어지기 전에 하루 여기로 초대하고 싶어요."], ["They are wild to see the river, sketch the broken bridge, and copy some of the things they admire in my book.", "다들 강을 보고, 부서진 다리를 사생하고, 제 화첩에서 마음에 드는 걸 베껴 그리고 싶어 안달이거든요."], ["They have been very kind to me in many ways, and I am grateful, for they are all rich, and know I am poor, yet they never made any difference.\"", "그 애들이 여러모로 저한테 참 잘해 줬어요. 다들 부자고 제가 가난한 걸 알면서도 전혀 차별하지 않았으니, 고마운 마음이에요.\""]], [["\"Why should they?\"", "\"왜 차별을 하겠니?\""], ["and Mrs. March put the question with what the girls called her \"Maria Theresa air.\"", "마치 부인은 딸들이 \"마리아 테레지아 같은 위엄\"이라 부르는 태도로 그렇게 물었다."]], [["\"You know as well as I that it _does_ make a difference with nearly every one, so don't ruffle up, like a dear, motherly hen, when your chickens get pecked by smarter birds; the ugly duckling turned out a swan, you know;\" and Amy smiled without bitterness, for she possessed a happy temper and hopeful spirit.", "\"거의 누구한테나 차별이라는 게 정말 있다는 건 엄마도 저만큼 잘 아시잖아요. 그러니까 새끼 닭들이 더 영악한 새들한테 쪼였다고 다정한 어미 닭처럼 깃털을 곤두세우진 마세요. 미운 오리 새끼도 백조가 됐잖아요.\" 에이미는 모진 데 없이 웃었다. 천성이 밝고 희망에 찬 영혼이었던 것이다."]], [["Mrs. March laughed, and smoothed down her maternal pride as she asked,--", "마치 부인은 웃으며 모성의 자부심을 다독이고는 물었다."]], [["\"Well, my swan, what is your plan?\"", "\"그래, 우리 백조님, 계획이 뭐지?\""]], [["\"I should like to ask the girls out to lunch next week, to take them a drive to the places they want to see, a row on the river, perhaps, and make a little artistic _fête_ for them.\"", "\"다음 주에 그 애들을 점심에 초대하고, 보고 싶어 하는 곳들로 마차 산책도 시켜 주고, 어쩌면 강에서 뱃놀이도 하면서 작은 예술 축제를 열어 주고 싶어요.\""]], [["\"That looks feasible.", "\"그럴듯하구나."], ["What do you want for lunch?", "점심은 뭘 차릴 거니?"], ["Cake, sandwiches, fruit, and coffee will be all that is necessary, I suppose?\"", "케이크에 샌드위치, 과일하고 커피면 충분하지 않을까?\""]], [["\"Oh dear, no!", "\"어머, 안 돼요!"], ["we must have cold tongue and chicken, French chocolate and ice-cream, besides.", "차가운 소 혀 요리에 닭고기, 거기다 프랑스식 초콜릿에 아이스크림까지 있어야죠."], ["The girls are used to such things, and I want my lunch to be proper and elegant, though I _do_ work for my living.\"", "그 애들은 그런 것에 익숙하단 말이에요. 비록 제가 생계를 위해 일하는 처지지만, 점심만큼은 격식 있고 우아하게 차리고 싶어요.\""]], [["\"How many young ladies are there?\"", "\"아가씨가 몇이나 되는데?\""], ["asked her mother, beginning to look sober.", "어머니가 차츰 표정이 굳어지며 물었다."]], [["\"Twelve or fourteen in the class, but I dare say they won't all come.\"", "\"수업엔 열둘이나 열네 명쯤 있는데, 다 오진 않을 거예요.\""]], [["\"Bless me, child, you will have to charter an omnibus to carry them about.\"", "\"세상에, 얘야, 그 애들을 다 실어 나르려면 합승마차라도 한 대 빌려야겠다.\""]], [["\"Why, mother, how _can_ you think of such a thing?", "\"엄마도 참, 어쩜 그런 생각을 다 하세요?"], ["Not more than six or eight will probably come, so I shall hire a beach-wagon, and borrow Mr.", "많아야 예닐곱 명쯤 올 테니까, 전 바닷가용 마차를 한 대 빌리고, 로렌스 씨네 '체리바운스'를 빌릴 거예요.\""], ["Laurence's cherry-bounce.\" (Hannah's pronunciation of _char-à-banc_.)", "(해나가 '샤라방(char-à-banc)'을 그렇게 발음했다.)"]], [["\"All this will be expensive, Amy.\"", "\"그러면 다 돈이 들 텐데, 에이미.\""]], [["\"Not very; I've calculated the cost, and I'll pay for it myself.\"", "\"그렇게 많이 들진 않아요. 비용은 다 계산해 뒀고, 제가 직접 낼 거예요.\""]], [["\"Don't you think, dear, that as these girls are used to such things, and the best we can do will be nothing new, that some simpler plan would be pleasanter to them, as a change, if nothing more, and much better for us than buying or borrowing what we don't need, and attempting a style not in keeping with our circumstances?\"", "\"얘야, 그 애들은 그런 것에 익숙하니까 우리가 아무리 잘 차려 봐야 새로울 게 없을 텐데, 그럴 바엔 좀 더 소박한 계획이 그 애들한테도 색다른 즐거움이 되지 않겠니? 필요도 없는 걸 사거나 빌리고 우리 형편에 맞지도 않는 격식을 흉내 내는 것보다, 우리한테도 훨씬 나을 테고 말이야.\""]], [["\"If I can't have it as I like, I don't care to have it at all.", "\"제 뜻대로 못 할 거면 차라리 안 하는 게 나아요."], ["I know that I can carry it out perfectly well, if you and the girls will help a little; and I don't see why I can't if I'm willing to pay for it,\"", "엄마랑 언니들이 조금만 도와주면 완벽하게 해낼 수 있어요. 제가 돈을 내겠다는데 못 할 게 뭐 있어요.\""], ["said Amy, with the decision which opposition was apt to change into obstinacy.", "에이미가 단호하게 말했다. 반대에 부딪히면 그 단호함이 곧잘 고집으로 바뀌곤 했다."]], [["Mrs.", "마치 부인은 경험이 더없이 훌륭한 스승임을 알고 있었기에, 가능한 경우에는 아이들이 스스로 교훈을 깨치게 내버려 두었다."], ["March knew that experience was an excellent teacher, and when it was possible she left her children to learn alone the lessons which she would gladly have made easier, if they had not objected to taking advice as much as they did salts and senna.", "기꺼이 그 길을 쉽게 만들어 주고 싶었지만, 아이들은 마치 설사약이나 센나 차를 거부하듯 충고 듣기를 마다했던 것이다."]], [["\"Very well, Amy; if your heart is set upon it, and you see your way through without too great an outlay of money, time, and temper, I'll say no more.", "\"좋아, 에이미. 정 마음을 굳혔고, 돈과 시간과 기분을 너무 많이 쏟지 않고 해낼 길이 보인다면 더는 말리지 않으마."], ["Talk it over with the girls, and whichever way you decide, I'll do my best to help you.\"", "친구들이랑 의논해 보렴. 어느 쪽으로 결정하든 내가 힘껏 도와줄게.\""]], [["\"Thanks, mother; you are always _so_ kind;\"", "\"고마워요, 엄마. 엄마는 언제나 너무너무 다정해요.\""], ["and away went Amy to lay her plan before her sisters.", "그러고는 에이미가 언니들에게 계획을 알리러 쪼르르 달려갔다."]], [["Meg agreed at once, and promised her aid, gladly offering anything she possessed, from her little house itself to her very best salt-spoons.", "메그는 곧장 찬성하며 도와주겠다고 약속했고, 자기 작은 집부터 가장 좋은 소금 숟가락까지 가진 것이라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 내놓겠다고 했다."], ["But Jo frowned upon the whole project, and would have nothing to do with it at first.", "하지만 조는 그 계획 전체를 못마땅해하며 처음에는 거들떠보지도 않으려 했다."]], [["\"Why in the world should you spend your money, worry your family, and turn the house upside down for a parcel of girls who don't care a sixpence for you?", "\"도대체 왜 너한테 동전 한 닢어치도 관심 없는 계집애들 무리를 위해서 돈을 쓰고, 식구들을 들볶고, 집 안을 발칵 뒤집어 놓겠다는 거야?"], ["I thought you had too much pride and sense to truckle to any mortal woman just because she wears French boots and rides in a _coupé_,\"", "난 네가 자존심도 분별도 넘쳐서, 프랑스제 부츠나 신고 사륜마차나 타고 다닌다고 어떤 여자한테든 굽실거릴 애는 아니라고 생각했는데.\""], ["said Jo, who, being called from the tragical climax of her novel, was not in the best mood for social enterprises.", "소설의 비극적 절정에서 불려 나온 조는 사교 행사 같은 데 가장 어울리지 않는 기분이었다."]], [["\"I _don't_ truckle, and I hate being patronized as much as you do!\"", "\"난 굽실거리지 않아. 그리고 언니만큼이나 나도 동정받는 거 싫어!\""], ["returned Amy indignantly, for the two still jangled when such questions arose.", "에이미가 발끈하며 받아쳤다. 이런 문제가 나올 때면 둘은 여전히 티격태격했다."], ["\"The girls do care for me, and I for them, and there's a great deal of kindness and sense and talent among them, in spite of what you call fashionable nonsense.", "\"그 애들은 정말 날 좋아하고 나도 그 애들을 좋아해. 언니가 유행 좇는 헛짓이라고 부르는 거 말고도, 그 애들 사이엔 친절과 분별과 재능이 가득하다고."], ["You don't care to make people like you, to go into good society, and cultivate your manners and tastes.", "언니는 사람들이 자기를 좋아하게 만들거나, 좋은 사교계에 드나들거나, 몸가짐과 취향을 가다듬는 일엔 관심이 없잖아."], ["I do, and I mean to make the most of every chance that comes.", "난 관심 있고, 다가오는 기회를 하나도 빠짐없이 살릴 거야."], ["_You_ can go through the world with your elbows out and your nose in the air, and call it independence, if you like.", "언니야 팔꿈치를 내밀고 코를 치켜든 채 세상을 헤쳐 나가면서 그걸 독립이라고 부르고 싶으면 그러든가."], ["That's not my way.\"", "그건 내 방식이 아니야.\""]], [["When Amy whetted her tongue and freed her mind she usually got the best of it, for she seldom failed to have common sense on her side, while Jo carried her love of liberty and hate of conventionalities to such an unlimited extent that she naturally found herself worsted in an argument.", "에이미가 혀를 벼리고 속마음을 거침없이 쏟아 낼 때면 대개 그녀가 이겼다. 상식이 그녀 편에 서 있는 일이 좀처럼 빠지지 않았던 반면, 조는 자유에 대한 사랑과 인습에 대한 미움을 워낙 끝없이 밀어붙이는 통에 자연히 말다툼에서 밀리곤 했다."], ["Amy's definition of Jo's idea of independence was such a good hit that both burst out laughing, and the discussion took a more amiable turn.", "조의 독립관에 대한 에이미의 정의가 어찌나 정곡을 찔렀던지, 둘은 그만 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 논쟁은 한결 누그러진 방향으로 흘러갔다."], ["Much against her will, Jo at length consented to sacrifice a day to Mrs.", "내키지 않았지만 조도 마침내 하루쯤 세상의 눈치(그런디 부인)에 양보하여, 자기가 \"말도 안 되는 일\"이라 여기던 그 일을 동생이 해내도록 돕기로 했다."], ["Grundy, and help her sister through what she regarded as \"a nonsensical business.\"", "* 그런디 부인 : 영국에서 '세상의 평판·체면'을 빗대는 가공의 인물명."]], [["The invitations were sent, nearly all accepted, and the following Monday was set apart for the grand event.", "초대장이 보내졌고, 거의 모두가 수락했으며, 다음 월요일이 그 성대한 행사를 위한 날로 정해졌다."], ["Hannah was out of humor because her week's work was deranged, and prophesied that \"ef the washin' and ironin'", "해나는 한 주의 일과가 뒤죽박죽이 되어 심기가 불편했고, \"빨래와 다림질을 제때 안 하면 어디서든 되는 일이 하나도 없을 거\"라고 예언했다."], ["warn't done reg'lar nothin' would go well anywheres.\"", "가사라는 기계의 태엽에 생긴 이 차질은 일 전체에 나쁜 영향을 미쳤다."], ["This hitch in the mainspring of the domestic machinery had a bad effect upon the whole concern; but Amy's motto was \"Nil desperandum,\"", "하지만 에이미의 좌우명은 \"절대 절망하지 말라\"였고, 무엇을 할지 마음을 정한 이상 온갖 장애를 무릅쓰고 밀고 나갔다."], ["and having made up her mind what to do, she proceeded to do it in spite of all obstacles.", "우선 해나의 요리부터 잘되지 않았다."], ["To begin with, Hannah's cooking didn't turn out well: the chicken was tough, the tongue too salt, and the chocolate wouldn't froth properly.", "닭고기는 질겼고, 소 혀는 너무 짰으며, 초콜릿은 거품이 제대로 일지 않았다."], ["Then the cake and ice cost more than Amy expected, so did the wagon; and various other expenses, which seemed trifling at the outset, counted up rather alarmingly afterward.", "게다가 케이크와 얼음이 에이미가 예상한 것보다 비쌌고, 마차도 마찬가지였으며, 처음엔 사소해 보이던 이런저런 비용이 나중에 합쳐 보니 꽤 아찔할 만큼 불어났다."], ["Beth got cold and took to her bed, Meg had an unusual number of callers to keep her at home, and Jo was in such a divided state of mind that her breakages, accidents, and mistakes were uncommonly numerous, serious, and trying.", "베스는 감기에 걸려 자리에 누웠고, 메그는 유난히 손님이 많이 찾아와 집을 비울 수 없었으며, 조는 마음이 너무 갈팡질팡한 나머지 깨뜨리고 사고 치고 실수하는 일이 유난히 잦고 심각하고 골치 아팠다."]], [["\"If it hadn't been for mother I never should have got through,\"", "\"엄마가 아니었으면 난 절대 못 해냈을 거야.\""], ["as Amy declared afterward, and gratefully remembered when \"the best joke of the season\" was entirely forgotten by everybody else.", "에이미는 나중에 이렇게 단언했고, 다른 식구들 모두가 \"그해 최고의 우스개\"를 까맣게 잊은 뒤에도 고마운 마음으로 그 일을 기억했다."]], [["If it was not fair on Monday, the young ladies were to come on Tuesday,--an arrangement which aggravated Jo and Hannah to the last degree.", "월요일에 날이 좋지 않으면 아가씨들은 화요일에 오기로 했는데, 이 약속은 조와 해나를 더할 나위 없이 짜증 나게 했다."], ["On Monday morning the weather was in that undecided state which is more exasperating than a steady pour.", "월요일 아침, 날씨는 줄기차게 쏟아붓는 비보다 더 사람 속을 뒤집어 놓는 그 어정쩡한 상태였다."], ["It drizzled a little, shone a little, blew a little, and didn't make up its mind till it was too late for any one else to make up theirs.", "조금 부슬거리다 조금 갰다가 조금 바람이 불더니, 누구든 마음을 정하기엔 이미 너무 늦은 뒤에야 겨우 마음을 정했다. 에이미는 동틀 녘에 일어나, 집을 정돈하려고 식구들을 침대에서 끌어내고 아침을 후딱 먹게 다그쳤다."], ["Amy was up at dawn, hustling people out of their beds and through their breakfasts, that the house might be got in order.", "거실이 유난히 초라해 보였지만, 없는 것을 한탄하느라 멈춰 서지 않고, 있는 것을 솜씨껏 최대한 활용했다."], ["The parlor struck her as looking uncommonly shabby; but without stopping to sigh for what she had not, she skilfully made the best of what she had, arranging chairs over the worn places in the carpet, covering stains on the walls with pictures framed in ivy, and filling up empty corners with home-made statuary, which gave an artistic air to the room, as did the lovely vases of flowers Jo scattered about.", "카펫에서 닳은 자리에는 의자를 놓아 가리고, 벽의 얼룩은 담쟁이덩굴로 액자 두른 그림으로 덮었으며, 빈 구석은 손수 만든 조각상으로 채워 방에 예술적인 분위기를 더했다. 조가 여기저기 흩어 놓은 사랑스러운 꽃병들도 한몫했다."]], [["The lunch looked charmingly; and as she surveyed it, she sincerely hoped it would taste well, and that the borrowed glass, china, and silver would get safely home again.", "점심상은 보기에 멋졌다. 에이미는 상을 둘러보며 맛도 좋기를, 그리고 빌려 온 유리잔과 도자기와 은식기가 무사히 제집으로 돌아가기를 진심으로 빌었다."], ["The carriages were promised, Meg and mother were all ready to do the honors, Beth was able to help Hannah behind the scenes, Jo had engaged to be as lively and amiable as an absent mind, an aching head, and a very decided disapproval of everybody and everything would allow, and, as she wearily dressed, Amy cheered herself with anticipations of the happy moment, when, lunch safely over, she should drive away with her friends for an afternoon of artistic delights; for the \"cherry-bounce\"", "마차도 약속받았고, 메그와 어머니는 손님 맞을 채비를 마쳤으며, 베스는 무대 뒤에서 해나를 거들 수 있을 만했고, 조는 정신이 다른 데 가 있고 머리가 지끈거리고 모든 사람과 모든 일이 영 못마땅한 와중에도 허락하는 한껏 활기차고 상냥하게 굴겠다고 약속해 둔 터였다. 지친 몸으로 옷을 차려입으면서 에이미는, 점심이 무사히 끝나고 친구들과 마차를 몰고 나가 예술적 기쁨으로 가득한 오후를 보낼 그 행복한 순간을 그리며 스스로를 다독였다."], ["and the broken bridge were her strong points.", "\"체리바운스\"와 부서진 다리야말로 그녀가 자신 있게 내세우는 부분이었으니 말이다."]], [["Then came two hours of suspense, during which she vibrated from parlor to porch, while public opinion varied like the weathercock.", "이어 두 시간 동안의 초조한 기다림이 찾아왔고, 그동안 에이미는 거실과 현관을 오가며 마음을 졸였으며, 여론은 바람개비처럼 변덕을 부렸다."], ["A smart shower at eleven had evidently quenched the enthusiasm of the young ladies who were to arrive at twelve, for nobody came; and at two the exhausted family sat down in a blaze of sunshine to consume the perishable portions of the feast, that nothing might be lost.", "열한 시에 한차례 세차게 퍼부은 소나기가, 열두 시에 오기로 한 아가씨들의 열정을 분명히 식혀 버린 듯, 아무도 오지 않았다. 그리고 두 시가 되자 기진맥진한 식구들은 쨍쨍 내리쬐는 햇살 속에 둘러앉아, 무엇 하나 버려지지 않도록 잔치 음식 가운데 상하기 쉬운 것들을 먹어 치웠다."]], [["\"No doubt about the weather to-day; they will certainly come, so we must fly round and be ready for them,\"", "\"오늘은 날씨가 의심할 여지 없잖아. 그 애들은 틀림없이 올 테니까, 우리 서둘러서 맞을 준비를 해야 해.\""], ["said Amy, as the sun woke her next morning.", "이튿날 아침 햇살에 잠을 깬 에이미가 말했다."], ["She spoke briskly, but in her secret soul she wished she had said nothing about Tuesday, for her interest, like her cake, was getting a little stale.", "말투는 씩씩했지만, 속으로는 화요일 얘기를 꺼내지 말걸 그랬다고 후회하고 있었다. 케이크처럼 그녀의 흥미도 조금씩 김이 빠지고 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"I can't get any lobsters, so you will have to do without salad to-day,\"", "\"바닷가재를 도무지 못 구하겠구나. 그러니 오늘은 샐러드 없이 지내야겠다.\""], ["said Mr. March, coming in half an hour later, with an expression of placid despair.", "삼십 분쯤 뒤에 들어온 마치 씨가 차분한 체념의 표정으로 말했다."]], [["\"Use the chicken, then; the toughness won't matter in a salad,\"", "\"그럼 닭고기를 쓰면 되죠. 샐러드에 넣으면 질긴 건 상관없으니까요.\""], ["advised his wife.", "부인이 일러 주었다."]], [["\"Hannah left it on the kitchen-table a minute, and the kittens got at it.", "\"해나가 잠깐 부엌 식탁에 올려놨더니 새끼 고양이들이 손을 댔지 뭐야."], ["I'm very sorry, Amy,\"", "정말 미안해, 에이미.\""], ["added Beth, who was still a patroness of cats.", "여전히 고양이들의 후원자인 베스가 덧붙였다."]], [["\"Then I _must_ have a lobster, for tongue alone won't do,\"", "\"그럼 꼭 바닷가재를 구해야 해. 소 혀만으론 안 되니까.\""], ["said Amy decidedly.", "에이미가 단호하게 말했다."]], [["\"Shall I rush into town and demand one?\"", "\"내가 읍내로 달려가서 한 마리 구해 올까?\""], ["asked Jo, with the magnanimity of a martyr.", "조가 순교자 같은 도량을 보이며 물었다."]], [["\"You'd come bringing it home under your arm, without any paper, just to try me.", "\"언니는 날 약 올리려고 종이에 싸지도 않은 채 그냥 팔에 끼고 들고 올걸."], ["I'll go myself,\"", "내가 직접 갈래.\""], ["answered Amy, whose temper was beginning to fail.", "에이미가 대답했다. 그녀의 인내심도 슬슬 바닥나기 시작했다."]], [["Shrouded in a thick veil and armed with a genteel travelling-basket, she departed, feeling that a cool drive would soothe her ruffled spirit, and fit her for the labors of the day.", "두꺼운 베일을 둘러쓰고 점잖은 여행용 바구니로 무장한 에이미는 길을 나섰다. 시원한 마차 산책이 헝클어진 마음을 달래 주고 그날의 고된 일을 견딜 채비를 해 주리라 여기면서."], ["After some delay, the object of her desire was procured, likewise a bottle of dressing, to prevent further loss of time at home, and off she drove again, well pleased with her own forethought.", "다소 지체된 끝에 원하던 물건을 손에 넣었고, 집에서 시간을 더 허비하지 않도록 드레싱 한 병도 함께 사서, 자기 선견지명에 흐뭇해하며 다시 마차를 몰고 떠났다."]], [["As the omnibus contained only one other passenger, a sleepy old lady, Amy pocketed her veil, and beguiled the tedium of the way by trying to find out where all her money had gone to.", "합승마차에는 졸고 있는 노부인 한 사람뿐이었으므로, 에이미는 베일을 주머니에 넣고, 그 많은 돈이 다 어디로 갔는지 따져 보며 길의 지루함을 달랬다."], ["So busy was she with her card full of refractory figures that she did not observe a new-comer, who entered without stopping the vehicle, till a masculine voice said, \"Good-morning, Miss March,\" and, looking up, she beheld one of Laurie's most elegant college friends.", "좀처럼 말을 안 듣는 숫자가 빼곡한 카드에 정신이 팔린 나머지, 마차를 세우지도 않고 올라탄 새 승객을 알아채지 못했는데, 남자 목소리가 \"안녕하세요, 마치 양\"이라고 하기에 고개를 들어 보니, 로리의 대학 친구 가운데 가장 우아한 축에 드는 사람이었다."], ["Fervently hoping that he would get out before she did, Amy utterly ignored the basket at her feet, and, congratulating herself that she had on her new travelling dress, returned the young man's greeting with her usual suavity and spirit.", "그가 자기보다 먼저 내리기를 간절히 빌면서, 에이미는 발치의 바구니를 철저히 못 본 척했고, 새 여행용 드레스를 입고 있다는 데 스스로를 다행스럽게 여기며, 평소의 상냥함과 활기로 청년의 인사에 답했다."]], [["They got on excellently; for Amy's chief care was soon set at rest by learning that the gentleman would leave first, and she was chatting away in a peculiarly lofty strain, when the old lady got out.", "둘은 더없이 잘 어울려 이야기를 나누었다. 그 신사가 먼저 내릴 것임을 알게 되자 에이미의 가장 큰 걱정거리가 이내 가라앉았고, 그녀가 유난히 도도한 어조로 한창 수다를 떨고 있을 때 노부인이 내렸다."], ["In stumbling to the door, she upset the basket, and--oh, horror!--the lobster, in all its vulgar size and brilliancy, was revealed to the highborn eyes of a Tudor.", "문 쪽으로 비틀거리며 가던 노부인이 그만 바구니를 엎고 말았으니 — 아, 끔찍해라! — 그 천박할 만큼 크고 번지르르한 바닷가재가 튜더라는 귀하신 분의 눈앞에 고스란히 드러나고 말았다."]], [["\"By Jove, she's forgotten her dinner!\"", "\"저런, 저분이 저녁거리를 두고 내리셨네요!\""], ["cried the unconscious youth, poking the scarlet monster into its place with his cane, and preparing to hand out the basket after the old lady.", "영문을 모르는 청년이 외치며, 지팡이로 그 새빨간 괴물을 제자리에 쑤셔 넣고는, 노부인을 뒤따라 바구니를 건네주려 했다."]], [["\"Please don't--it's--it's mine,\"", "\"제발 그러지 마세요. 그건…"], ["murmured Amy, with a face nearly as red as her fish.", "그건 제 거예요.\" 에이미가 제 생선만큼이나 새빨개진 얼굴로 우물거렸다."]], [["\"Oh, really, I beg pardon; it's an uncommonly fine one, isn't it?\"", "\"아, 이런, 실례했습니다. 아주 굉장한 놈이네요, 그렇죠?\""], ["said Tudor, with great presence of mind, and an air of sober interest that did credit to his breeding.", "튜더가 침착하게, 그리고 그의 교양을 빛내 주는 진지한 관심의 표정으로 말했다."]], [["Amy recovered herself in a breath, set her basket boldly on the seat, and said, laughing,--", "에이미는 한 호흡 만에 평정을 되찾고, 보란 듯이 바구니를 좌석에 올려놓더니 웃으며 말했다."]], [["\"Don't you wish you were to have some of the salad he's to make, and to see the charming young ladies who are to eat it?\"", "\"이놈으로 만들 샐러드를 좀 맛보고, 그걸 먹을 매력적인 아가씨들도 보고 싶지 않으세요?\""]], [["Now that was tact, for two of the ruling foibles of the masculine mind were touched: the lobster was instantly surrounded by a halo of pleasing reminiscences, and curiosity about \"the charming young ladies\"", "이거야말로 눈치였다. 남자 마음을 좌우하는 두 가지 약점을 건드렸으니 말이다."], ["diverted his mind from the comical mishap.", "바닷가재는 순식간에 흐뭇한 추억의 후광에 둘러싸였고, \"매력적인 아가씨들\"에 대한 호기심이 그의 마음을 그 우스꽝스러운 사고에서 딴 데로 돌려놓았다."]], [["\"I suppose he'll laugh and joke over it with Laurie, but I sha'n't see them; that's a comfort,\"", "'저 사람이 로리한테 이 얘길 하면서 웃고 농담을 하겠지. 하지만 난 그 자리에 없을 테니, 그나마 다행이야.'"], ["thought Amy, as Tudor bowed and departed.", "튜더가 인사하고 떠나는 동안 에이미는 그렇게 생각했다."]], [["She did not mention this meeting at home (though she discovered that, thanks to the upset, her new dress was much damaged by the rivulets of dressing that meandered down the skirt), but went through with the preparations which now seemed more irksome than before; and at twelve o'clock all was ready again.", "집에서는 이 만남을 입에 올리지 않았다(다만 바구니가 엎어진 탓에 드레싱이 도랑처럼 치맛자락을 타고 흘러내려 새 드레스가 몹시 망가졌다는 건 알아챘다). 그저 이제는 어제보다 더 성가시게 느껴지는 준비를 마저 해 나갔고, 열두 시가 되자 모든 게 다시 채비되었다."], ["Feeling that the neighbors were interested in her movements, she wished to efface the memory of yesterday's failure by a grand success to-day; so she ordered the \"cherry-bounce,\"", "이웃들이 자기 거동에 관심을 두고 있다고 느낀 에이미는 어제의 실패를 오늘의 대성공으로 지워 버리고 싶었다."], ["and drove away in state to meet and escort her guests to the banquet.", "그래서 \"체리바운스\"를 시켜 위풍당당하게 손님들을 마중하러, 잔치에 모셔 오려고 마차를 몰고 떠났다."]], [["\"There's the rumble, they're coming!", "\"덜컹거리는 소리야, 오나 봐! 내가 현관에 나가서 맞아야겠다."], ["I'll go into the porch to meet them; it looks hospitable, and I want the poor child to have a good time after all her trouble,\"", "그래야 손님 대접하는 티가 나지. 그 가엾은 애가 그 고생을 했으니 부디 즐거운 시간을 보냈으면 좋겠구나.\""], ["said Mrs. March, suiting the action to the word.", "마치 부인이 말하며 곧장 그렇게 행동에 옮겼다."], ["But after one glance, she retired, with an indescribable expression, for, looking quite lost in the big carriage, sat Amy and one young lady.", "그런데 한 번 흘긋 보고는 무어라 형용할 수 없는 표정으로 도로 들어왔으니, 그 커다란 마차 안에 영 어색해 보이게 앉아 있는 건 에이미와 아가씨 단 한 명뿐이었다."]], [["\"Run, Beth, and help Hannah clear half the things off the table; it will be too absurd to put a luncheon for twelve before a single girl,\"", "\"베스, 어서 가서 해나가 식탁에서 음식 절반을 치우는 거 좀 도와! 계집애 하나 앞에 열둘이 먹을 점심을 떡하니 차려 놓는 것만큼 우스운 일도 없잖아.\""], ["cried Jo, hurrying away to the lower regions, too excited to stop even for a laugh.", "조가 외치며 웃음 한 번 참지 못할 만큼 들떠 아래층으로 후다닥 내려갔다."]], [["In came Amy, quite calm, and delightfully cordial to the one guest who had kept her promise; the rest of the family, being of a dramatic turn, played their parts equally well, and Miss Eliott found them a most hilarious set; for it was impossible to entirely control the merriment which possessed them. The remodelled lunch being gayly partaken of, the studio and garden visited, and art discussed with enthusiasm, Amy ordered a buggy (alas for the elegant cherry-bounce!) and drove her friend quietly about the neighborhood till sunset, when \"the party went out.\"", "에이미가 들어왔다. 아주 침착했고, 약속을 지킨 단 한 명의 손님에게 더없이 다정했다. 극적인 기질을 타고난 나머지 식구들도 저마다 제 역할을 똑같이 잘 해냈고, 엘리엇 양은 이 집 사람들이 무척이나 유쾌한 무리라고 여겼다. 그들을 사로잡은 웃음기를 도무지 완전히 다스릴 수가 없었던 것이다. 새로 차린 점심을 즐겁게 나눠 먹고, 작업실과 정원을 둘러보고, 예술을 열정적으로 논한 뒤, 에이미는 이륜마차를 부르고는(아아, 우아한 체리바운스는 어디로!) 해 질 무렵까지 친구를 태우고 동네를 조용히 돌았다. 그러고 나서 \"손님이 돌아갔다.\""]], [["As she came walking in, looking very tired, but as composed as ever, she observed that every vestige of the unfortunate _fête_ had disappeared, except a suspicious pucker about the corners of Jo's mouth.", "몹시 지쳐 보였지만 여느 때처럼 침착한 모습으로 걸어 들어온 에이미는, 그 불운한 축제의 흔적이 죄다 사라지고 없다는 걸 알아챘다. 다만 조의 입가에 수상쩍게 잡혀 있는 주름만은 빼고."]], [["\"You've had a lovely afternoon for your drive, dear,\"", "\"오늘 마차 산책하기 참 좋은 오후였겠구나, 얘야.\""], ["said her mother, as respectfully as if the whole twelve had come.", "어머니가 마치 열둘이 다 온 것처럼 정중하게 말했다."]], [["\"Miss Eliott is a very sweet girl, and seemed to enjoy herself, I thought,\"", "\"엘리엇 양은 참 상냥한 아가씨더라. 보니까 꽤 즐거워하는 것 같던데.\""], ["observed Beth, with unusual warmth.", "베스가 평소답지 않게 다정한 어조로 말했다."]], [["\"Could you spare me some of your cake?", "\"네 케이크 좀 나눠 줄 수 있겠니?"], ["I really need some, I have so much company, and I can't make such delicious stuff as yours,\"", "사실 좀 필요해. 손님이 워낙 많은데, 난 네 것처럼 맛있는 걸 못 만들거든.\""], ["asked Meg soberly.", "메그가 정색하며 청했다."]], [["\"Take it all; I'm the only one here who likes sweet things, and it will mould before I can dispose of it,\"", "\"다 가져가. 단걸 좋아하는 건 여기서 나뿐인데, 내가 처치하기도 전에 곰팡이가 슬 테니까.\""], ["answered Amy, thinking with a sigh of the generous store she had laid in for such an end as this.", "에이미가 대답하며, 이런 결말을 위해 넉넉히 마련해 둔 음식들을 떠올리고는 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["\"It's a pity Laurie isn't here to help us,\"", "\"로리가 거들러 와 줬으면 좋았을 텐데.\""], ["began Jo, as they sat down to ice-cream and salad for the second time in two days.", "이틀 만에 두 번째로 아이스크림과 샐러드를 앞에 두고 둘러앉으며 조가 입을 뗐다."]], [["A warning look from her mother checked any further remarks, and the whole family ate in heroic silence, till Mr.", "어머니의 경고하는 눈짓에 더 이상의 말은 끊겼고, 온 식구가 비장한 침묵 속에 음식을 먹었다. 그러다 마치 씨가 점잖게 한마디 했다."], ["March mildly observed, \"Salad was one of the favorite dishes of the ancients, and Evelyn\"--here a general explosion of laughter cut short the \"history of sallets,\"", "\"샐러드는 고대인들이 즐겨 먹던 요리 가운데 하나였지. 그리고 이블린은—\""], ["to the great surprise of the learned gentleman.", "여기서 일제히 터져 나온 웃음이 \"샐러드의 역사\"를 뚝 끊어 놓았고, 박식한 신사는 크게 놀랐다."]], [["\"Bundle everything into a basket and send it to the Hummels: Germans like messes.", "\"전부 바구니에 담아서 후멜네 집에 보내. 독일 사람들은 잡탕을 좋아하니까."], ["I'm sick of the sight of this; and there's no reason you should all die of a surfeit because I've been a fool,\"", "난 이걸 보는 것만으로도 진절머리가 나. 내가 바보짓을 했다고 다들 과식해서 죽을 까닭은 없잖아.\""], ["cried Amy, wiping her eyes.", "에이미가 눈가를 훔치며 외쳤다."]], [["\"I thought I _should_ have died when I saw you two girls rattling about in the what-you-call-it, like two little kernels in a very big nutshell, and mother waiting in state to receive the throng,\" sighed Jo, quite spent with laughter.", "\"네가 그 뭐라더라 그 마차에 둘이 덜컹덜컹 타고 오는 걸 봤을 때 난 정말 죽는 줄 알았어. 어찌나 커다란 호두 껍데기에 든 알맹이 두 알 같던지. 게다가 엄마는 손님 무리를 맞겠다고 위풍당당하게 기다리고 계셨잖아.\" 조가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. 웃다 지쳐 기진맥진해 있었다."]], [["\"I'm very sorry you were disappointed, dear, but we all did our best to satisfy you,\"", "\"네가 실망해서 참 안됐구나, 얘야. 그래도 우린 다들 널 만족시키려고 최선을 다했단다.\""], ["said Mrs. March, in a tone full of motherly regret.", "마치 부인이 어머니로서의 안타까움이 가득 밴 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"I _am_ satisfied; I've done what I undertook, and it's not my fault that it failed; I comfort myself with that,\"", "\"전 만족해요. 제가 맡은 일을 해냈고, 그게 실패한 건 제 잘못이 아니잖아요."], ["said Amy, with a little quiver in her voice.", "그렇게 위안 삼을래요.\" 에이미가 목소리를 살짝 떨며 말했다."], ["\"I thank you all very much for helping me, and I'll thank you still more if you won't allude to it for a month, at least.\"", "\"도와주셔서 다들 정말 고마워요. 그리고 적어도 한 달 동안은 이 일을 입에 올리지 말아 주시면 더 고맙겠어요.\""]], [["No one did for several months; but the word \"_fête_\"", "여러 달 동안 아무도 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["always produced a general smile, and Laurie's birthday gift to Amy was a tiny coral lobster in the shape of a charm for her watch-guard.", "하지만 \"축제\"라는 말만 나오면 모두 빙그레 웃었고, 로리가 에이미에게 준 생일 선물은 시곗줄에 다는 장식용으로 만든 자그마한 산호 바닷가재였다."]], [["# LITERARY LESSONS.", "# LITERARY LESSONS."]], [["Fortune suddenly smiled upon Jo, and dropped a good-luck penny in her path.", "조에게 갑자기 행운이 미소를 지으며, 그녀의 앞길에 운수 좋은 동전 한 닢을 떨어뜨렸다."], ["Not a golden penny, exactly, but I doubt if half a million would have given more real happiness than did the little sum that came to her in this wise.", "꼭 황금 동전이라 할 수는 없었지만, 이런 식으로 그녀에게 들어온 그 작은 돈만큼의 진정한 행복을 오십만 달러가 줄 수 있었을지는 의문이다."]], [["Every few weeks she would shut herself up in her room, put on her scribbling suit, and \"fall into a vortex,\"", "조는 몇 주에 한 번씩 방에 틀어박혀, 끄적이는 작업복을 차려입고, 본인 말마따나 \"소용돌이에 빠져들어\", 온 마음과 영혼을 쏟아 소설을 써 내려갔다."], ["as she expressed it, writing away at her novel with all her heart and soul, for till that was finished she could find no peace.", "그것을 끝마치기 전에는 도무지 마음이 편치 않았기 때문이다."], ["Her \"scribbling suit\" consisted of a black woollen pinafore on which she could wipe her pen at will, and a cap of the same material, adorned with a cheerful red bow, into which she bundled her hair when the decks were cleared for action.", "그녀의 \"끄적이는 작업복\"이란, 펜을 마음대로 닦을 수 있는 검은 모직 앞치마와, 그것과 같은 천에 명랑한 빨간 리본을 단 모자였는데, 작업 채비를 마치면 그 안에 머리를 둘둘 말아 넣었다."], ["This cap was a beacon to the inquiring eyes of her family, who during these periods kept their distance, merely popping in their heads semi-occasionally, to ask, with interest, \"Does genius burn, Jo?\"", "이 모자는 식구들의 궁금한 눈길을 위한 봉화였다. 그런 시기에 식구들은 거리를 두면서, 이따금 고개만 빼꼼 들이밀고는 흥미롭게 물었다."], ["They did not always venture even to ask this question, but took an observation of the cap, and judged accordingly.", "\"조, 천재성이 불타오르고 있니?\" 식구들은 늘 감히 이 질문을 던지지도 못한 채, 모자를 관찰하고 그에 따라 판단하곤 했다."], ["If this expressive article of dress was drawn low upon the forehead, it was a sign that hard work was going on; in exciting moments it was pushed rakishly askew; and when despair seized the author it was plucked wholly off, and cast upon the floor.", "이 표정 풍부한 옷가지가 이마 위로 푹 눌러쓰여 있으면 한창 힘든 작업이 진행 중이라는 표시였고, 흥이 오른 순간에는 멋들어지게 비스듬히 젖혀졌으며, 절망이 작가를 사로잡으면 모자는 통째로 벗겨져 바닥에 내동댕이쳐졌다."], ["At such times the intruder silently withdrew; and not until the red bow was seen gayly erect upon the gifted brow, did any one dare address Jo.", "그럴 때면 침입자는 말없이 물러갔고, 그 빨간 리본이 재능 있는 이마 위에 명랑하게 곧추선 모습이 보이기 전에는 아무도 감히 조에게 말을 걸지 못했다."]], [["She did not think herself a genius by any means; but when the writing fit came on, she gave herself up to it with entire abandon, and led a blissful life, unconscious of want, care, or bad weather, while she sat safe and happy in an imaginary world, full of friends almost as real and dear to her as any in the flesh.", "조는 결코 스스로를 천재라 여기지 않았다. 하지만 글 쓰고 싶은 발작이 도지면 그것에 온전히 몸을 내맡긴 채 더없이 행복한 나날을 보냈다."], ["Sleep forsook her eyes, meals stood untasted, day and night were all too short to enjoy the happiness which blessed her only at such times, and made these hours worth living, even if they bore no other fruit.", "결핍도, 근심도, 궂은 날씨도 잊은 채, 살아 있는 누구 못지않게 진짜 같고 사랑스러운 친구들로 가득한 상상의 세계 속에서 안전하고 행복하게 앉아 있었다. 잠이 두 눈에서 달아났고, 끼니는 손도 안 댄 채 놓여 있었으며, 오로지 그런 때에만 그녀에게 깃들어, 다른 열매를 맺지 못하더라도 그 시간만큼은 살 만한 가치가 있게 해 주는 그 행복을 누리기엔 밤낮이 모두 너무 짧았다."], ["The divine afflatus usually lasted a week or two, and then she emerged from her \"vortex,\" hungry, sleepy, cross, or despondent.", "이 신성한 영감은 보통 한두 주 이어졌고, 그러고 나면 조는 배고프고 졸리고 짜증 나고 풀이 죽은 채 \"소용돌이\"에서 빠져나왔다."]], [["She was just recovering from one of these attacks when she was prevailed upon to escort Miss Crocker to a lecture, and in return for her virtue was rewarded with a new idea.", "이런 발작에서 막 회복되어 갈 무렵, 조는 크로커 양을 강연에 데려다주는 임무를 떠맡게 되었는데, 그 선행의 대가로 새로운 발상이라는 보상을 받았다."], ["It was a People's Course, the lecture on the Pyramids, and Jo rather wondered at the choice of such a subject for such an audience, but took it for granted that some great social evil would be remedied or some great want supplied by unfolding the glories of the Pharaohs to an audience whose thoughts were busy with the price of coal and flour, and whose lives were spent in trying to solve harder riddles than that of the Sphinx.", "'민중을 위한 강좌'였고, 강연 주제는 피라미드였다. 조는 그런 청중에게 그런 주제를 골랐다는 게 다소 의아했지만, 석탄과 밀가루 값에 골몰하고 스핑크스의 수수께끼보다 더 어려운 수수께끼를 풀려 애쓰며 살아가는 청중에게 파라오의 영광을 펼쳐 보임으로써 어떤 거대한 사회악이 바로잡히거나 어떤 거대한 필요가 채워지려나 보다고 여기며 넘겼다."]], [["They were early; and while Miss Crocker set the heel of her stocking, Jo amused herself by examining the faces of the people who occupied the seat with them.", "그들은 일찌감치 도착했고, 크로커 양이 양말 뒤꿈치를 뜨는 동안, 조는 같은 좌석에 앉은 사람들의 얼굴을 살피며 시간을 보냈다."], ["On her left were two matrons, with massive foreheads, and bonnets to match, discussing Woman's Rights and making tatting.", "왼쪽에는 떡 벌어진 이마에 그에 어울리는 보닛을 쓴 부인 둘이 여성의 권리를 논하며 태팅 레이스를 뜨고 있었다."], ["Beyond sat a pair of humble lovers, artlessly holding each other by the hand, a sombre spinster eating peppermints out of a paper bag, and an old gentleman taking his preparatory nap behind a yellow bandanna.", "그 너머에는 천진하게 손을 맞잡은 수수한 연인 한 쌍, 종이봉투에서 박하사탕을 꺼내 먹는 침울한 노처녀, 노란 손수건 뒤에서 미리 한숨 자 두는 노신사가 있었다."], ["On her right, her only neighbor was a studious-looking lad absorbed in a newspaper.", "오른쪽 유일한 옆자리에는 신문에 푹 빠진 공부깨나 한 듯한 소년이 있었다."]], [["It was a pictorial sheet, and Jo examined the work of art nearest her, idly wondering what unfortuitous concatenation of circumstances needed the melodramatic illustration of an Indian in full war costume, tumbling over a precipice with a wolf at his throat, while two infuriated young gentlemen, with unnaturally small feet and big eyes, were stabbing each other close by, and a dishevelled female was flying away in the background with her mouth wide open.", "그것은 그림이 실린 신문이었고, 조는 자기 가까이에 있는 그 예술 작품을 들여다보며, 늑대에게 목을 물린 채 정장 차림으로 절벽에서 굴러떨어지는 인디언, 그 옆에서 부자연스럽게 작은 발과 커다란 눈을 한 두 청년이 서로를 칼로 찌르는 모습, 그리고 배경에서 입을 헤벌린 채 산발한 여자가 달아나는 장면을 담은 이 통속적인 삽화를 어떤 기막힌 사건의 우연한 얽힘이 필요로 했을까 하고 멍하니 궁금해했다."], ["Pausing to turn a page, the lad saw her looking, and, with boyish good-nature, offered half his paper, saying bluntly, \"Want to read it?", "한 면을 넘기려다 소년은 그녀가 보고 있다는 걸 알아챘고, 소년다운 호의로 신문의 절반을 내밀며 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"읽으실래요?"], ["That's a first-rate story.\"", "끝내주는 이야기예요.\""]], [["Jo accepted it with a smile, for she had never outgrown her liking for lads, and soon found herself involved in the usual labyrinth of love, mystery, and murder, for the story belonged to that class of light literature in which the passions have a holiday, and when the author's invention fails, a grand catastrophe clears the stage of one half the _dramatis personæ_, leaving the other half to exult over their downfall.", "조는 미소 지으며 받아 들었다. 사내아이들을 좋아하는 마음만큼은 나이가 들어도 여전했던 것이다. 그러고는 이내 사랑과 비밀과 살인이 뒤엉킨 여느 미궁 속으로 빠져들었다. 그 이야기는 격정이 휴일을 만끽하는 부류의 가벼운 읽을거리에 속했고, 작가의 창의력이 바닥나면 거대한 참사가 등장인물 절반을 무대에서 싹 치워 버리고, 남은 절반에게 그들의 몰락을 두고 의기양양해할 거리를 남겨 주는 식이었다."]], [["\"Prime, isn't it?\"", "\"끝내주죠, 그렇죠?\""], ["asked the boy, as her eye went down the last paragraph of her portion.", "그녀의 눈이 자기 몫의 마지막 단락을 훑어 내려가자 소년이 물었다."]], [["\"I think you and I could do as well as that if we tried,\"", "\"우리 둘이 해 봐도 이만큼은 쓸 수 있을 것 같은데.\""], ["returned Jo, amused at his admiration of the trash.", "조가 그 시시한 글에 대한 소년의 감탄이 우스워 대꾸했다."]], [["\"I should think I was a pretty lucky chap if I could.", "\"제가 그럴 수 있다면 꽤나 운 좋은 녀석이라고 생각할걸요."], ["She makes a good living out of such stories, they say;\"", "듣자 하니 이런 이야기로 잘 먹고산다던데요.\""], ["and he pointed to the name of Mrs.", "그러면서 소년이 이야기 제목 아래에 적힌 '슬랭 노스버리 부인(Mrs."], ["S.", "S."], ["L.", "L."], ["A.", "A."], ["N. G.", "N."], ["Northbury, under the title of the tale.", "G. Northbury)'이라는 이름을 가리켰다."]], [["\"Do you know her?\"", "\"그 작가를 아니?\""], ["asked Jo, with sudden interest.", "조가 갑자기 흥미를 보이며 물었다."]], [["\"No; but I read all her pieces, and I know a fellow who works in the office where this paper is printed.\"", "\"아뇨. 근데 그 사람 글은 다 읽어요. 그리고 이 신문 찍는 사무실에서 일하는 친구를 하나 알거든요.\""]], [["\"Do you say she makes a good living out of stories like this?\"", "\"이런 이야기로 잘 먹고산다고?\""], ["and Jo looked more respectfully at the agitated group and thickly-sprinkled exclamation-points that adorned the page.", "조는 그 면을 장식한 흥분한 인물 무리와 빽빽이 찍힌 느낌표들을 한층 존경스러운 눈길로 바라보았다."]], [["\"Guess she does!", "\"그럼요!"], ["She knows just what folks like, and gets paid well for writing it.\"", "그 작가는 사람들이 뭘 좋아하는지 딱 알고, 그걸 써서 돈도 두둑이 받는걸요.\""]], [["Here the lecture began, but Jo heard very little of it, for while Prof.", "이때 강연이 시작되었지만 조는 거의 귀에 들어오지 않았다."], ["Sands was prosing away about Belzoni, Cheops, scarabei, and hieroglyphics, she was covertly taking down the address of the paper, and boldly resolving to try for the hundred-dollar prize offered in its columns for a sensational story.", "샌즈 교수가 벨초니와 케오프스, 풍뎅이 부적과 상형문자에 관해 지루하게 늘어놓는 동안, 그녀는 슬그머니 그 신문의 주소를 받아 적으며, 거기 지면에 내건 선정 소설 백 달러 상금에 한번 도전해 보겠다고 대담하게 결심했다."], ["By the time the lecture ended and the audience awoke, she had built up a splendid fortune for herself (not the first founded upon paper), and was already deep in the concoction of her story, being unable to decide whether the duel should come before the elopement or after the murder.", "강연이 끝나고 청중이 잠에서 깰 무렵, 그녀는 이미 스스로를 위해 굉장한 부를 쌓아 올렸고(종이 위에 세운 첫 번째 재산은 아니었지만), 결투를 사랑의 도피 앞에 둘지 살인 뒤에 둘지 정하지 못한 채, 벌써 이야기 짜내기에 깊이 빠져 있었다."]], [["She said nothing of her plan at home, but fell to work next day, much to the disquiet of her mother, who always looked a little anxious when \"genius took to burning.\"", "조는 집에서는 이 계획에 대해 한마디도 하지 않았지만, 이튿날 작업에 착수했고, 이는 \"천재성이 불타오를\" 때면 늘 조금 불안한 표정을 짓던 어머니를 적잖이 불안하게 했다."], ["Jo had never tried this style before, contenting herself with very mild romances for the \"Spread Eagle.\"", "조는 전에 이런 문체를 시도해 본 적이 없었고, \"스프레드 이글\"지에 아주 점잖은 연애담이나 실으며 만족해 왔다."], ["Her theatrical experience and miscellaneous reading were of service now, for they gave her some idea of dramatic effect, and supplied plot, language, and costumes.", "이제는 연극 경험과 잡다한 독서가 도움이 되었다. 그것들이 극적 효과에 대한 어느 정도의 감각을 주었고, 줄거리와 언어와 의상을 마련해 주었으니 말이다."], ["Her story was as full of desperation and despair as her limited acquaintance with those uncomfortable emotions enabled her to make it, and, having located it in Lisbon, she wound up with an earthquake, as a striking and appropriate _dénouement_.", "그녀의 이야기는, 그 불편한 감정들을 겪어 본 일이 빈약한 자기 처지에서 빚어낼 수 있는 한껏, 발악과 절망으로 가득했다. 그리고 무대를 리스본으로 잡은 그녀는, 이야기를 인상적이고 적절한 결말이랍시고 지진으로 마무리 지었다."], ["The manuscript was privately despatched, accompanied by a note, modestly saying that if the tale didn't get the prize, which the writer hardly dared expect, she would be very glad to receive any sum it might be considered worth.", "원고는 은밀히 부쳐졌고, 만약 이 이야기가 상을 받지 못한다면 — 작가로서는 감히 기대하기 어려운 일이지만 — 그 가치만큼 어떤 금액이든 받게 된다면 무척 기쁘겠다고 겸손하게 적은 쪽지가 함께 따라갔다."]], [["Six weeks is a long time to wait, and a still longer time for a girl to keep a secret; but Jo did both, and was just beginning to give up all hope of ever seeing her manuscript again, when a letter arrived which almost took her breath away; for on opening it, a check for a hundred dollars fell into her lap. For a minute she stared at it as if it had been a snake, then she read her letter and began to cry. If the amiable gentleman who wrote that kindly note could have known what intense happiness he was giving a fellow-creature, I think he would devote his leisure hours, if he has any, to that amusement; for Jo valued the letter more than the money, because it was encouraging; and after years of effort it was _so_ pleasant to find that she had learned to do something, though it was only to write a sensation story.", "육 주는 기다리기에 긴 시간이고, 소녀가 비밀을 지키기엔 더더욱 긴 시간이다. 하지만 조는 그 둘 다를 해냈고, 다시는 제 원고를 볼 수 없으리라는 희망을 막 접으려던 참에, 거의 숨이 멎을 만한 편지 한 통이 도착했다. 봉투를 뜯자 백 달러짜리 수표가 무릎 위로 툭 떨어졌던 것이다. 잠시 그녀는 그것을 뱀이라도 되는 양 빤히 쳐다보더니, 편지를 읽고는 울기 시작했다. 그 다정한 쪽지를 써 보낸 친절한 신사가, 자기가 한 사람에게 얼마나 강렬한 행복을 안겨 주었는지 알 수 있었더라면, 여가 시간이 있다면 그 시간을 죄다 이 즐거움에 바쳤으리라 싶다. 조는 그 편지를 돈보다 더 귀하게 여겼으니, 그것이 격려가 되었기 때문이다. 그리고 여러 해 애쓴 끝에, 비록 선정 소설 한 편을 쓴 것에 지나지 않더라도, 자기가 무언가를 해낼 줄 알게 되었다는 사실을 알게 된 것은 너무도 기뻤다."]], [["A prouder young woman was seldom seen than she, when, having composed herself, she electrified the family by appearing before them with the letter in one hand, the check in the other, announcing that she had won the prize.", "마음을 가다듬은 뒤, 한 손에는 편지를, 다른 손에는 수표를 들고 식구들 앞에 나타나 상을 탔다고 알렸을 때의 조만큼 의기양양한 젊은 여인도 좀처럼 없었으리라."], ["Of course there was a great jubilee, and when the story came every one read and praised it; though after her father had told her that the language was good, the romance fresh and hearty, and the tragedy quite thrilling, he shook his head, and said in his unworldly way,--", "물론 대단한 축하 잔치가 벌어졌고, 이야기가 도착하자 모두가 읽고 칭찬했다. 다만 아버지는 문장이 좋고 이야기가 신선하고 진솔하며 비극이 꽤 손에 땀을 쥐게 한다고 말한 뒤, 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 세속에 물들지 않은 특유의 말투로 말했다."]], [["\"You can do better than this, Jo.", "\"넌 이것보다 더 잘 쓸 수 있단다, 조."], ["Aim at the highest, and never mind the money.\"", "가장 높은 데를 겨냥하고, 돈일랑 신경 쓰지 마라.\""]], [["\"_I_ think the money is the best part of it.", "\"전 돈이 제일 좋은 부분 같은데요."], ["What _will_ you do with such a fortune?\"", "그렇게 큰돈으로 뭘 하실 거예요?\""], ["asked Amy, regarding the magic slip of paper with a reverential eye.", "에이미가 그 마법 같은 종잇조각을 경건한 눈길로 바라보며 물었다."]], [["\"Send Beth and mother to the seaside for a month or two,\"", "\"베스랑 엄마를 한두 달 바닷가에 보내 드릴 거야.\""], ["answered Jo promptly.", "조가 선뜻 대답했다."]], [["\"Oh, how splendid!", "\"어머, 정말 멋지다!"], ["No, I can't do it, dear, it would be so selfish,\"", "아니야, 그럴 순 없어, 언니. 그건 너무 이기적인걸.\""], ["cried Beth, who had clapped her thin hands, and taken a long breath, as if pining for fresh ocean-breezes; then stopped herself, and motioned away the check which her sister waved before her.", "베스가 외쳤다. 그녀는 신선한 바닷바람을 못내 그리워하듯 야윈 두 손을 마주치고 길게 숨을 들이쉬었다가, 곧 스스로를 멈추고는 언니가 눈앞에 흔드는 수표를 손짓으로 물렸다."]], [["\"Ah, but you shall go, I've set my heart on it; that's what I tried for, and that's why I succeeded.", "\"아니야, 넌 꼭 가야 해. 난 이걸 작정했단 말이야."], ["I never get on when I think of myself alone, so it will help me to work for you, don't you see?", "이게 바로 내가 노린 거고, 그래서 내가 성공한 거라고. 난 나 혼자만 생각하면 영 일이 안 풀리거든."], ["Besides, Marmee needs the change, and she won't leave you, so you _must_ go.", "그러니까 널 위해서 일하면 도움이 될 거야, 알겠지? 게다가 엄마도 기분 전환이 필요한데, 엄마는 널 두고는 안 가실 테니까, 네가 꼭 가야 해."], ["Won't it be fun to see you come home plump and rosy again? Hurrah for Dr.", "네가 통통하고 발그레해져서 돌아오는 걸 보면 얼마나 즐겁겠어?"], ["Jo, who always cures her patients!\"", "늘 환자를 낫게 하는 조 박사 만세!\""]], [["To the sea side they went, after much discussion; and though Beth didn't come home as plump and rosy as could be desired, she was much better, while Mrs.", "여러 번 의논한 끝에 두 사람은 바닷가로 떠났다. 베스가 바라는 만큼 통통하고 발그레해져서 돌아오지는 못했지만 한결 좋아졌고, 마치 부인은 열 살은 젊어진 기분이라고 단언했다."], ["March declared she felt ten years younger; so Jo was satisfied with the investment of her prize money, and fell to work with a cheery spirit, bent on earning more of those delightful checks.", "그래서 조는 상금을 그렇게 투자한 데 만족했고, 그 기분 좋은 수표를 더 많이 벌어들이겠다고 마음먹은 채 명랑한 기운으로 작업에 매달렸다."], ["She did earn several that year, and began to feel herself a power in the house; for by the magic of a pen, her \"rubbish\" turned into comforts for them all.", "그해에 조는 정말로 수표를 여러 장 벌었고, 집안에서 스스로를 하나의 힘으로 느끼기 시작했다."], ["\"The Duke's Daughter\" paid the butcher's bill, \"A Phantom Hand\"", "펜의 마법으로 그녀의 \"쓰레기\"가 식구 모두를 위한 안락으로 바뀌었으니 말이다."], ["put down a new carpet, and the \"Curse of the Coventrys\" proved the blessing of the Marches in the way of groceries and gowns.", "\"공작의 딸\"은 정육점 외상값을 갚아 주었고, \"유령의 손\"은 새 카펫을 깔아 주었으며, \"코번트리가의 저주\"는 식료품과 옷가지의 형태로 마치가의 축복임이 드러났다."]], [["Wealth is certainly a most desirable thing, but poverty has its sunny side, and one of the sweet uses of adversity is the genuine satisfaction which comes from hearty work of head or hand; and to the inspiration of necessity, we owe half the wise, beautiful, and useful blessings of the world.", "부유함이란 분명 더없이 탐나는 것이지만, 가난에도 양지바른 구석은 있는 법이다. 역경의 달콤한 쓰임새 가운데 하나는 머리나 손을 부지런히 놀려 일한 데서 오는 진정한 만족이며, 세상의 지혜롭고 아름답고 유익한 축복의 절반은 필요라는 영감 덕분이다."], ["Jo enjoyed a taste of this satisfaction, and ceased to envy richer girls, taking great comfort in the knowledge that she could supply her own wants, and need ask no one for a penny.", "조는 이런 만족을 한 자락 맛보았고, 더 부유한 처녀들을 부러워하기를 그만두었다. 제 필요는 제 손으로 채울 수 있고, 누구에게도 동전 한 닢 손 벌릴 일이 없다는 사실에서 큰 위안을 얻었던 것이다."]], [["Little notice was taken of her stories, but they found a market; and, encouraged by this fact, she resolved to make a bold stroke for fame and fortune.", "그녀의 이야기들은 별다른 주목을 받지 못했지만, 그래도 팔릴 곳을 찾았다. 이 사실에 고무된 조는 명성과 부를 노린 대담한 한 수를 두기로 마음먹었다."], ["Having copied her novel for the fourth time, read it to all her confidential friends, and submitted it with fear and trembling to three publishers, she at last disposed of it, on condition that she would cut it down one third, and omit all the parts which she particularly admired.", "소설을 네 번째로 베껴 쓰고, 가까운 친구들에게 죄다 읽어 주고, 두렵고 떨리는 마음으로 출판업자 셋에게 들이민 끝에, 마침내 분량을 삼분의 일로 줄이고 자기가 특히 아끼는 부분을 죄다 빼낸다는 조건으로 그것을 넘기게 되었다."]], [["\"Now I must either bundle it back into my tin-kitchen to mould, pay for printing it myself, or chop it up to suit purchasers, and get what I can for it.", "\"이제 이걸 도로 양철 부엌에 처박아 곰팡이가 슬게 두든가, 내 돈으로 출판하든가, 아니면 사겠다는 사람들 입맛에 맞게 잘게 썰어서 받을 수 있는 만큼 받든가 해야 해."], ["Fame is a very good thing to have in the house, but cash is more convenient; so I wish to take the sense of the meeting on this important subject,\"", "명성이라는 게 집에 두면 참 좋긴 한데, 현금이 더 편리하단 말이지. 그러니 이 중대한 사안에 대해 회의의 의견을 듣고 싶어.\""], ["said Jo, calling a family council.", "조가 가족회의를 소집하며 말했다."]], [["\"Don't spoil your book, my girl, for there is more in it than you know, and the idea is well worked out.", "\"네 책을 망치지 마라, 얘야. 네가 아는 것보다 그 안에는 더 많은 게 담겨 있고, 발상도 잘 풀어냈으니까."], ["Let it wait and ripen,\"", "묵혀 두고 무르익게 두렴.\" 이것이 아버지의 충고였다."], ["was her father's advice; and he practised as he preached, having waited patiently thirty years for fruit of his own to ripen, and being in no haste to gather it, even now, when it was sweet and mellow.", "그리고 아버지는 설교한 대로 실천하는 사람이었다. 자신의 결실이 무르익기를 삼십 년이나 참을성 있게 기다려 왔고, 이제 그것이 달고 농익은 지금조차 서둘러 거두려 하지 않았으니 말이다."]], [["\"It seems to me that Jo will profit more by making the trial than by waiting,\"", "\"내 생각엔 조가 기다리는 것보다 한번 시도해 보는 데서 더 많은 걸 얻을 것 같아요.\""], ["said Mrs. March.", "마치 부인이 말했다."], ["\"Criticism is the best test of such work, for it will show her both unsuspected merits and faults, and help her to do better next time.", "\"이런 작업에는 비평이 가장 좋은 시금석이거든요. 미처 몰랐던 장점과 결점을 둘 다 보여 줘서, 다음번엔 더 잘하게 도와줄 테니까요."], ["We are too partial; but the praise and blame of outsiders will prove useful, even if she gets but little money.\"", "우린 너무 편들기 마련이지만, 바깥사람들의 칭찬과 비난은, 설령 돈은 얼마 못 벌더라도 유익할 거예요.\""]], [["\"Yes,\"", "\"맞아요.\""], ["said Jo, knitting her brows, \"that's just it; I've been fussing over the thing so long, I really don't know whether it's good, bad, or indifferent.", "조가 눈썹을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"바로 그거예요."], ["It will be a great help to have cool, impartial persons take a look at it, and tell me what they think of it.\"", "이걸 하도 오래 붙들고 끙끙댔더니, 이게 좋은지 나쁜지 그저 그런지 정말 모르겠어요. 냉정하고 공정한 사람들이 한번 봐 주고 어떻게 생각하는지 말해 주면 큰 도움이 될 거예요.\""]], [["\"I wouldn't leave out a word of it; you'll spoil it if you do, for the interest of the story is more in the minds than in the actions of the people, and it will be all a muddle if you don't explain as you go on,\" said Meg, who firmly believed that this book was the most remarkable novel ever written.", "\"난 한 마디도 빼지 않겠어. 그러면 책을 망친다고. 이 이야기의 묘미는 인물들의 행동보다 마음속에 더 있는데, 진행하면서 설명을 안 하면 죄다 뒤죽박죽이 될 거야.\" 메그가 말했다. 이 책이야말로 일찍이 쓰인 어떤 소설보다 뛰어난 작품이라고 그녀는 굳게 믿고 있었다."]], [["\"But Mr. Allen says, 'Leave out the explanations, make it brief and dramatic, and let the characters tell the story,'\"", "\"하지만 앨런 씨는 '설명은 빼고, 짧고 극적으로 만들어서, 인물들이 이야기를 끌어가게 하라'고 하시던데.\""], ["interrupted Jo, turning to the publisher's note.", "조가 출판업자의 쪽지로 화제를 돌리며 끼어들었다."]], [["\"Do as he tells you; he knows what will sell, and we don't.", "\"그분 말씀대로 해. 그분은 뭐가 팔릴지 알고, 우린 모르니까."], ["Make a good, popular book, and get as much money as you can.", "좋고 인기 있는 책을 만들어서 돈을 최대한 많이 벌어."], ["By and by, when, you've got a name, you can afford to digress, and have philosophical and metaphysical people in your novels,\"", "그러다 이름이 알려지면, 그때 가서 여담도 늘어놓고 소설에 철학적이고 형이상학적인 인물도 집어넣을 여유가 생길 거야.\""], ["said Amy, who took a strictly practical view of the subject.", "이 문제를 철저히 실용적인 관점에서 본 에이미가 말했다."]], [["\"Well,\"", "\"글쎄.\""], ["said Jo, laughing, \"if my people _are_ 'philosophical and metaphysical,'", "조가 웃으며 말했다. \"내 인물들이 '철학적이고 형이상학적'이라면 그건 내 잘못이 아니야."], ["it isn't my fault, for I know nothing about such things, except what I hear father say, sometimes.", "난 그런 것에 대해선 아버지가 가끔 하시는 말씀 말고는 아무것도 모르니까."], ["If I've got some of his wise ideas jumbled up with my romance, so much the better for me.", "아버지의 지혜로운 생각이 내 연애담에 섞여 들어갔다면, 나한테야 잘된 일이지."], ["Now, Beth, what do you say?\"", "자, 베스, 넌 어떻게 생각해?\""]], [["\"I should so like to see it printed _soon_,\" was all Beth said, and smiled in saying it; but there was an unconscious emphasis on the last word, and a wistful look in the eyes that never lost their childlike candor, which chilled Jo's heart, for a minute, with a foreboding fear, and decided her to make her little venture \"soon.\"", "\"난 이게 어서 출판되는 걸 봤으면 좋겠어.\" 베스가 한 말은 이게 전부였고, 그렇게 말하며 미소를 지었다. 하지만 마지막 한마디에 자기도 모르게 힘이 실려 있었고, 어린아이 같은 천진함을 결코 잃은 적 없는 그 두 눈에 어린 애틋한 빛이, 한순간 조의 마음을 불길한 두려움으로 서늘하게 만들었다. 그리하여 조는 자신의 작은 모험을 \"어서\" 감행하기로 마음을 정했다."]], [["So, with Spartan firmness, the young authoress laid her first-born on her table, and chopped it up as ruthlessly as any ogre.", "그리하여 스파르타 사람 같은 결연함으로, 젊은 여류 작가는 자신의 첫 소생을 책상에 뉘어 놓고, 여느 식인귀 못지않게 무자비하게 잘게 잘라 냈다."], ["In the hope of pleasing every one, she took every one's advice; and, like the old man and his donkey in the fable, suited nobody.", "모두를 기쁘게 하려는 바람으로 모두의 충고를 따랐고, 우화에 나오는 노인과 당나귀처럼, 결국 아무도 흡족하게 하지 못했다."]], [["Her father liked the metaphysical streak which had unconsciously got into it; so that was allowed to remain, though she had her doubts about it.", "아버지는 저도 모르게 스며든 형이상학적 색채를 마음에 들어 했고, 그래서 그 부분은 — 조 자신은 미심쩍어했지만 — 그대로 남겨 두었다."], ["Her mother thought that there _was_ a trifle too much description; out, therefore, it nearly all came, and with it many necessary links in the story.", "어머니는 묘사가 조금 지나치게 많다고 여겼고, 그래서 그것은 거의 다 빠졌으며, 그와 함께 이야기에 꼭 필요한 연결 고리도 여럿 사라졌다."], ["Meg admired the tragedy; so Jo piled up the agony to suit her, while Amy objected to the fun, and, with the best intentions in life, Jo quenched the sprightly scenes which relieved the sombre character of the story.", "메그는 비극을 좋아했고, 그래서 조는 메그의 입맛에 맞게 비통함을 잔뜩 쌓아 올렸다. 반면 에이미는 익살스러운 부분을 못마땅해했고, 그래서 더없이 좋은 의도로, 조는 이야기의 침울한 성격을 누그러뜨려 주던 발랄한 장면들을 꺼 버렸다."], ["Then, to complete the ruin, she cut it down one third, and confidingly sent the poor little romance, like a picked robin, out into the big, busy world, to try its fate.", "그러고는 파국을 완성하듯 분량을 삼분의 일로 줄여, 깃털 다 뽑힌 울새 같은 그 가엾은 작은 연애담을, 운명을 시험하라며 크고 분주한 세상으로 천연덕스럽게 내보냈다."]], [["Well, it was printed, and she got three hundred dollars for it; likewise plenty of praise and blame, both so much greater than she expected that she was thrown into a state of bewilderment, from which it took her some time to recover.", "자, 책은 출판되었고, 조는 그 대가로 삼백 달러를 받았다. 그와 함께 칭찬과 비난도 잔뜩 받았는데, 둘 다 기대보다 훨씬 커서 그녀는 한동안 헤어나오는 데 시간이 걸린 어리둥절한 상태에 빠지고 말았다."]], [["\"You said, mother, that criticism would help me; but how can it, when it's so contradictory that I don't know whether I've written a promising book or broken all the ten commandments?\"", "\"엄마는 비평이 절 도와줄 거라고 하셨잖아요. 그런데 어떻게 도움이 돼요?"], ["cried poor Jo, turning over a heap of notices, the perusal of which filled her with pride and joy one minute, wrath and dire dismay the next.", "어찌나 모순투성이인지, 제가 가망 있는 책을 쓴 건지 십계명을 죄다 어긴 건지 모르겠다고요!\" 가엾은 조가 외치며 한 무더기의 평을 뒤적였는데, 그것들을 읽노라면 한순간 자부심과 기쁨이 차올랐다가 다음 순간 분노와 끔찍한 낙담이 밀려왔다."], ["\"This man says 'An exquisite book, full of truth, beauty, and earnestness; all is sweet, pure, and healthy,'\"", "\"이 사람은 '진실과 아름다움과 진지함으로 가득한 더없이 훌륭한 책. 모든 것이 달콤하고 순수하고 건강하다'고 했어요.\""], ["continued the perplexed authoress.", "갈피를 못 잡은 여류 작가가 말을 이었다."], ["\"The next, 'The theory of the book is bad, full of morbid fancies, spiritualistic ideas, and unnatural characters.'", "\"그다음 사람은 '이 책의 주제 의식은 형편없고, 병적인 공상과 심령주의 사상과 부자연스러운 인물로 가득하다'고 했고요."], ["Now, as I had no theory of any kind, don't believe in Spiritualism, and copied my characters from life, I don't see how this critic _can_ be right.", "그런데 전 무슨 주제 의식 같은 건 아예 없었고, 심령주의도 안 믿고, 인물도 실제 인생에서 베껴 왔으니, 이 비평가가 어떻게 맞을 수가 있겠어요."], ["Another says, 'It's one of the best American novels which has appeared for years' (I know better than that); and the next asserts that 'though it is original, and written with great force and feeling, it is a dangerous book.'", "또 누구는 '수년 만에 나온 최고의 미국 소설 가운데 하나'라고 했는데(저도 그 정도는 아니란 거 알아요), 바로 다음 사람은 '독창적이고 대단한 힘과 감정으로 쓰였지만, 위험한 책'이라고 단언하네요."], ["'Tisn't!", "위험하긴요!"], ["Some make fun of it, some over-praise, and nearly all insist that I had a deep theory to expound, when I only wrote it for the pleasure and the money.", "누구는 비웃고, 누구는 지나치게 떠받들고, 거의 다들 제가 무슨 심오한 주제를 펼치려 했다고 우기는데, 전 그저 즐거움과 돈을 위해 썼을 뿐이라고요."], ["I wish I'd printed it whole or not at all, for I do hate to be so misjudged.\"", "차라리 통째로 내든가 아예 안 낼걸 그랬어요. 이렇게 오해받는 건 정말 싫으니까요.\""]], [["Her family and friends administered comfort and commendation liberally; yet it was a hard time for sensitive, high-spirited Jo, who meant so well, and had apparently done so ill. But it did her good, for those whose opinion had real value gave her the criticism which is an author's best education; and when the first soreness was over, she could laugh at her poor little book, yet believe in it still, and feel herself the wiser and stronger for the buffeting she had received.", "식구들과 친구들이 위로와 칭찬을 아낌없이 베풀었다. 그래도 그토록 좋은 뜻으로 했건만 겉보기엔 그토록 형편없이 해낸 듯한, 예민하고 기개 넘치는 조에게는 힘든 시절이었다. 하지만 그것은 그녀에게 약이 되었다. 정말로 가치 있는 의견을 지닌 이들이 작가에게 최고의 교육이 되는 비평을 들려주었기 때문이다. 그리고 처음의 쓰라림이 가시고 나자, 조는 자신의 가엾은 작은 책을 두고 웃을 수 있게 되었으면서도 여전히 그 책을 믿었고, 자기가 받은 매질 덕분에 한층 지혜로워지고 단단해졌음을 느꼈다."]], [["\"Not being a genius, like Keats, it won't kill me,\"", "\"난 키츠 같은 천재가 아니니까, 이런 일로 죽진 않아.\" 조가 꿋꿋하게 말했다."], ["she said stoutly; \"and I've got the joke on my side, after all; for the parts that were taken straight out of real life are denounced as impossible and absurd, and the scenes that I made up out of my own silly head are pronounced 'charmingly natural, tender, and true.'", "\"게다가 어쨌든 우스개는 내 편이야. 실제 인생에서 곧이곧대로 가져온 부분은 말도 안 되고 터무니없다고 욕먹는데, 내 어리석은 머릿속에서 지어낸 장면은 '매력적이고 자연스럽고 다정하고 진실하다'고 평가받으니 말이야."], ["So I'll comfort myself with that; and when I'm ready, I'll up again and take another.\"", "그러니 그걸로 위안 삼을래. 그리고 준비가 되면 다시 일어나서 또 한 편 써 볼 거야.\""]], [["# DOMESTIC EXPERIENCES.", "# DOMESTIC EXPERIENCES."]], [["Like most other young matrons, Meg began her married life with the determination to be a model housekeeper.", "여느 젊은 새색시들이 대개 그렇듯, 메그도 모범적인 살림꾼이 되겠다는 다짐과 함께 결혼 생활을 시작했다."], ["John should find home a paradise; he should always see a smiling face, should fare sumptuously every day, and never know the loss of a button.", "존에게 집은 낙원이어야 했다. 그는 언제나 미소 띤 얼굴을 보아야 하고, 날마다 진수성찬을 누려야 하며, 단추가 떨어진 일 따위는 결코 알아서는 안 되었다."], ["She brought so much love, energy, and cheerfulness to the work that she could not but succeed, in spite of some obstacles.", "메그는 그 일에 어찌나 많은 사랑과 활력과 명랑함을 쏟아부었던지, 몇몇 장애가 있긴 했어도 성공하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Her paradise was not a tranquil one; for the little woman fussed, was over-anxious to please, and bustled about like a true Martha, cumbered with many cares.", "다만 그 낙원은 그리 평온하지만은 않았다. 이 작은 부인은 안달복달했고, 남편을 기쁘게 하려고 지나치게 마음을 졸였으며, 갖은 근심에 시달리는 진짜 마르다처럼 부산을 떨었던 것이다."], ["She was too tired, sometimes, even to smile; John grew dyspeptic after a course of dainty dishes, and ungratefully demanded plain fare.", "어떤 때는 너무 지쳐 미소조차 짓지 못했다. 존은 진귀한 요리가 연달아 나오자 소화불량에 걸려, 고마운 줄도 모르고 소박한 음식을 달라고 했다."], ["As for buttons, she soon learned to wonder where they went, to shake her head over the carelessness of men, and to threaten to make him sew them on himself, and then see if _his_ work would stand impatient tugs and clumsy fingers any better than hers.", "단추로 말하자면, 메그는 이내 그것들이 다 어디로 가는지 의아해하고, 남자들의 칠칠치 못함에 고개를 절레절레 흔들며, 직접 달아 보라고, 그래서 당신 솜씨가 성마른 잡아당김과 서툰 손가락을 내 솜씨보다 더 잘 견뎌 내는지 두고 보라고 으름장을 놓는 법을 배우게 되었다."]], [["They were very happy, even after they discovered that they couldn't live on love alone.", "* 마르다 : 성경에서 손님 접대로 분주했던 여인. 두 사람은 사랑만으로는 살 수 없다는 걸 깨달은 뒤에도 무척 행복했다."], ["John did not find Meg's beauty diminished, though she beamed at him from behind the familiar coffee-pot; nor did Meg miss any of the romance from the daily parting, when her husband followed up his kiss with the tender inquiry, \"Shall I send home veal or mutton for dinner, darling?\"", "메그가 낯익은 커피 주전자 너머에서 환하게 미소 지어 보여도 존은 그녀의 아름다움이 줄었다고 여기지 않았고, 메그 역시 날마다의 작별에서 낭만이 사라졌다고 느끼지 않았다. 남편이 입맞춤 뒤에 다정하게 \"여보, 저녁에 송아지 고기를 보낼까, 양고기를 보낼까?\""], ["The little house ceased to be a glorified bower, but it became a home, and the young couple soon felt that it was a change for the better.", "하고 물을 때조차 말이다. 작은 집은 더 이상 찬란한 신혼의 보금자리가 아니었지만 하나의 가정이 되었고, 젊은 부부는 곧 그것이 더 나은 방향으로의 변화임을 느꼈다."], ["At first they played keep-house, and frolicked over it like children; then John took steadily to business, feeling the cares of the head of a family upon his shoulders; and Meg laid by her cambric wrappers, put on a big apron, and fell to work, as before said, with more energy than discretion.", "처음에 둘은 소꿉놀이하듯 살림을 꾸리며 아이들처럼 그것을 즐겼다. 그러다 존은 한 가정의 가장이라는 책임이 어깨에 얹힌 것을 느끼며 착실히 사업에 매진했고, 메그는 고운 무명 실내복을 치워 두고 커다란 앞치마를 두른 채, 앞서 말했듯 분별보다는 의욕을 앞세워 일에 매달렸다."]], [["While the cooking mania lasted she went through Mrs. Cornelius's Receipt Book as if it were a mathematical exercise, working out the problems with patience and care.", "요리에 대한 열병이 이어지는 동안 메그는 코닐리어스 부인의 요리책을\n자, 모든 일을 수학 문제 풀듯 정성껏 인내심을 갖고 차근차근 해 나갔다."], ["Sometimes her family were invited in to help eat up a too bounteous feast of successes, or Lotty would be privately despatched with a batch of failures, which were to be concealed from all eyes in the convenient stomachs of the little Hummels.", "어떤 날은 성공작이 너무 풍성하게 쏟아져 식구들을 불러다 함께 먹어 치우게 하기도 했고, 어떤 날은 실패작을 한 무더기 로티 편에 몰래 들려 보내, 휴멜네 어린 것들의 만만한 뱃속에 감쪽같이 감춰 버리기도 했다."], ["An evening with John over the account-books usually produced a temporary lull in the culinary enthusiasm, and a frugal fit would ensue, during which the poor man was put through a course of bread-pudding, hash, and warmed-over coffee, which tried his soul, although he bore it with praiseworthy fortitude.", "존과 함께 가계부를 들여다보며 저녁을 보낸 뒤에는 으레 요리에 대한 열정이 잠시 가라앉으면서 절약하는 시기가 찾아왔고, 그동안 가엾은 남편은 빵 푸딩과 다진 고기 요리, 그리고 데운 커피를 줄줄이 견뎌야 했는데, 그것은 그의 영혼을 시험하는 일이었지만 그는 칭찬받아 마땅할 만큼 꿋꿋이 참아 냈다."], ["Before the golden mean was found, however, Meg added to her domestic possessions what young couples seldom get on long without,--a family jar.", "그러나 황금률을 찾기도 전에 메그는 젊은 부부가 오래 지내다 보면 으레 갖게 마련인 것 하나를 살림살이에 보태고 말았다. 바로 집안싸움이었다."]], [["Fired with a housewifely wish to see her store-room stocked with home-made preserves, she undertook to put up her own currant jelly.", "저장실을 직접 만든 잼으로 가득 채우고 싶다는 주부다운 소망에 불타올라, 메그는 손수 까치밥나무 젤리를 만들어 보기로 했다. 존에게는 작은 단지를 열두 개쯤 주문해 오고, 설탕도 넉넉히 더 사 오라고 부탁했다."], ["John was requested to order home a dozen or so of little pots, and an extra quantity of sugar, for their own currants were ripe, and were to be attended to at once.", "자기 집 까치밥나무 열매가 다 익어 당장 손을 봐야 했기 때문이다. 존은 \"내 아내\"가 무엇이든 척척 해낸다고 굳게 믿었고, 아내의 솜씨를 당연한 듯 자랑스러워했기에, 아내를 흐뭇하게 해 주고, 단 한 번뿐인 열매 수확을 더없이 보기 좋은 모양으로 갈무리해 겨우내 먹기로 마음먹었다."], ["As John firmly believed that \"my wife\"", "그리하여 집으로 사랑스러운 작은 단지 네 다스와 설탕 반 통, 그리고 까치밥나무 열매를 따 줄 사내아이 하나가 들어왔다."], ["was equal to anything, and took a natural pride in her skill, he resolved that she should be gratified, and their only crop of fruit laid by in a most pleasing form for winter use.", "예쁜 머리카락을 작은 모자 속에 밀어 넣고, 팔을 팔꿈치까지 걷어붙인 채, 가슴받이가 달렸는데도 어딘가 멋스러워 보이는 체크무늬 앞치마를 두른 젊은 주부는 일에 달려들었다."], ["Home came four dozen delightful little pots, half a barrel of sugar, and a small boy to pick the currants for her.", "성공을 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 한나가 이 일을 수백 번이나 하는 것을 보지 않았던가?"], ["With her pretty hair tucked into a little cap, arms bared to the elbow, and a checked apron which had a coquettish look in spite of the bib, the young housewife fell to work, feeling no doubts about her success; for hadn't she seen Hannah do it hundreds of times?", "늘어선 단지들이 처음에는 좀 놀라웠지만, 존이 젤리를 워낙 좋아하는 데다, 그 예쁜 작은 병들이 맨 위 선반에 놓이면 얼마나 보기 좋을까 싶어, 메그는 그것을 죄다 채우기로 마음먹고는, 열매를 따고 끓이고 거르고 젤리를 만드느라 부산을 떨며 긴 하루를 보냈다. 그녀는 최선을 다했다."], ["The array of pots rather amazed her at first, but John was so fond of jelly, and the nice little jars would look so well on the top shelf, that Meg resolved to fill them all, and spent a long day picking, boiling, straining, and fussing over her jelly.", "코닐리어스 부인에게 조언도 구했다. 한나가 했던 일 가운데 자기가 빠뜨린 게 무엇인지 머리를 쥐어짜며 떠올려 보았다."], ["She did her best; she asked advice of Mrs.", "다시 끓이고, 설탕을 더 넣고, 다시 걸렀다."], ["Cornelius; she racked her brain to remember what Hannah did that she had left undone; she reboiled, resugared, and restrained, but that dreadful stuff wouldn't \"_jell_.\"", "그래도 그 지긋지긋한 것은 도무지 \"굳질\" 않았다."]], [["She longed to run home, bib and all, and ask mother to lend a hand, but John and she had agreed that they would never annoy any one with their private worries, experiments, or quarrels.", "가슴받이 앞치마를 두른 채로라도 친정으로 달려가 엄마에게 손을 좀 빌려 달라고 하고 싶었지만, 존과 메그는 자기들끼리의 걱정거리나 시행착오, 다툼으로 결코 남을 성가시게 하지 않기로 약속했었다."], ["They had laughed over that last word as if the idea it suggested was a most preposterous one; but they had held to their resolve, and whenever they could get on without help they did so, and no one interfered, for Mrs.", "두 사람은 그 마지막 단어를 두고, 그게 가당치도 않은 생각이라는 듯 함께 웃었었다. 그래도 그들은 그 결심을 지켰고, 도움 없이 해낼 수 있을 때면 언제나 그렇게 했으며, 아무도 끼어들지 않았다."], ["March had advised the plan.", "마치 부인이 그렇게 하라고 일러 준 방식이었기 때문이다."], ["So Meg wrestled alone with the refractory sweetmeats all that hot summer day, and at five o'clock sat down in her topsy-turvy kitchen, wrung her bedaubed hands, lifted up her voice and wept.", "그래서 메그는 그 무더운 여름날 내내 말 안 듣는 단것과 홀로 씨름하다가, 다섯 시가 되자 엉망진창이 된 부엌에 주저앉아, 끈적끈적해진 두 손을 비틀어 짜며 목 놓아 울었다."]], [["Now, in the first flush of the new life, she had often said,--", "자, 새로운 삶의 첫 설렘에 젖어 있을 적에 메그는 곧잘 이렇게 말하곤 했다."]], [["\"My husband shall always feel free to bring a friend home whenever he likes.", "\"우리 남편은 언제든 마음 내킬 때 친구를 집에 데려와도 괜찮다고 늘 느낄 수 있어야 해."], ["I shall always be prepared; there shall be no flurry, no scolding, no discomfort, but a neat house, a cheerful wife, and a good dinner.", "난 언제나 준비가 되어 있을 거야. 허둥대거나 잔소리하거나 불편해하는 일 없이, 깔끔한 집과 명랑한 아내, 그리고 맛있는 저녁이 있는 거지."], ["John, dear, never stop to ask my leave, invite whom you please, and be sure of a welcome from me.\"", "존, 여보, 절대 내 허락을 구하느라 멈추지 말고, 좋아하는 사람을 마음껏 초대해요. 내가 반갑게 맞아 줄 테니 안심해도 돼요.\""]], [["How charming that was, to be sure!", "정말이지 얼마나 매력적인 말인가!"], ["John quite glowed with pride to hear her say it, and felt what a blessed thing it was to have a superior wife.", "존은 그 말을 듣고 자랑스러움에 한껏 들떴고, 자기보다 나은 아내를 둔다는 게 얼마나 큰 축복인지 실감했다."], ["But, although they had had company from time to time, it never happened to be unexpected, and Meg had never had an opportunity to distinguish herself till now.", "그러나 이따금 손님을 맞기는 했어도, 어쩌다 예고 없이 닥친 적은 한 번도 없었기에, 메그는 지금까지 자신의 솜씨를 뽐낼 기회가 없었다."], ["It always happens so in this vale of tears; there is an inevitability about such things which we can only wonder at, deplore, and bear as we best can.", "이 눈물의 골짜기에서는 늘 이런 식이다. 그런 일들에는 우리가 그저 놀라워하고, 한탄하고, 되도록 잘 견뎌 내는 수밖에 없는 어떤 필연이 깃들어 있다."]], [["If John had not forgotten all about the jelly, it really would have been unpardonable in him to choose that day, of all the days in the year, to bring a friend home to dinner unexpectedly.", "존이 젤리에 관한 일을 까맣게 잊지만 않았더라도, 일 년 중 하필 그날을, 친구를 예고 없이 저녁 식사에 데려올 날로 고른 것은 정말 용서받기 힘든 일이었다."], ["Congratulating himself that a handsome repast had been ordered that morning, feeling sure that it would be ready to the minute, and indulging in pleasant anticipations of the charming effect it would produce, when his pretty wife came running out to meet him, he escorted his friend to his mansion, with the irrepressible satisfaction of a young host and husband.", "그날 아침 푸짐한 식사를 주문해 두었다고 흐뭇해하며, 그것이 어김없이 제시간에 준비되어 있으리라 확신하면서, 예쁜 아내가 자기를 맞으러 달려 나올 때 그 식사가 빚어낼 근사한 효과를 즐겁게 그리며, 존은 젊은 주인이자 남편으로서의 억누를 수 없는 만족감을 안고 친구를 자기 저택으로 안내했다."]], [["It is a world of disappointments, as John discovered when he reached the Dove-cote.", "이 세상은 실망의 연속이라는 것을, 존은 비둘기 둥지에 다다랐을 때 깨달았다."], ["The front door usually stood hospitably open; now it was not only shut, but locked, and yesterday's mud still adorned the steps.", "현관문은 늘 손님을 반기듯 활짝 열려 있었는데, 지금은 닫혀 있을 뿐 아니라 잠겨 있었고, 어제 묻은 진흙이 아직도 계단을 장식하고 있었다."], ["The parlor-windows were closed and curtained, no picture of the pretty wife sewing on the piazza, in white, with a distracting little bow in her hair, or a bright-eyed hostess, smiling a shy welcome as she greeted her guest.", "응접실 창문은 닫힌 채 커튼이 드리워져 있었고, 흰옷을 입고 머리에 정신을 쏙 빼놓는 작은 리본을 단 예쁜 아내가 베란다에서 바느질하는 그림 같은 모습도, 손님을 맞으며 수줍게 미소 짓는 눈동자 반짝이는 안주인의 모습도 없었다. 그런 것은 전혀 없었다."], ["Nothing of the sort, for not a soul appeared, but a sanguinary-looking boy asleep under the currant-bushes.", "까치밥나무 덤불 아래 핏빛으로 물든 듯한 꼴을 한 사내아이가 잠들어 있을 뿐, 사람이라곤 그림자도 보이지 않았다."]], [["\"I'm afraid something has happened.", "\"무슨 일이 생긴 것 같군."], ["Step into the garden, Scott, while I look up Mrs.", "스콧, 잠깐 정원에 들어가 있게. 그동안 내가 브룩 부인을 찾아볼 테니.\""], ["Brooke,\" said John, alarmed at the silence and solitude.", "존이 그 적막과 고요에 놀라 말했다."]], [["Round the house he hurried, led by a pungent smell of burnt sugar, and Mr. Scott strolled after him, with a queer look on his face.", "그는 설탕 타는 매캐한 냄새에 이끌려 집을 빙 돌아 서둘러 갔고, 스콧 씨는 야릇한 표정을 지으며 그 뒤를 어슬렁어슬렁 따라갔다."], ["He paused discreetly at a distance when Brooke disappeared; but he could both see and hear, and, being a bachelor, enjoyed the prospect mightily.", "브룩이 사라지자 그는 눈치껏 멀찍이 떨어져 멈춰 섰다. 하지만 보이고 들리는 것은 다 알 수 있었고, 총각이었던 터라 그 광경을 무척이나 즐겼다."]], [["In the kitchen reigned confusion and despair; one edition of jelly was trickled from pot to pot, another lay upon the floor, and a third was burning gayly on the stove.", "부엌은 혼란과 절망이 지배하고 있었다. 한쪽 젤리는 이 단지에서 저 단지로 줄줄 흘러내렸고, 또 한쪽은 바닥에 널브러져 있었으며, 세 번째 것은 난로 위에서 신나게 타고 있었다."], ["Lotty, with Teutonic phlegm, was calmly eating bread and currant wine, for the jelly was still in a hopelessly liquid state, while Mrs.", "로티는 게르만 사람 특유의 무신경함으로 빵과 까치밥나무주를 태연히 먹고 있었다. 젤리가 아직도 가망 없이 액체 상태였기 때문이다."], ["Brooke, with her apron over her head, sat sobbing dismally.", "한편 브룩 부인은 앞치마를 머리에 뒤집어쓴 채 앉아 처량하게 흐느끼고 있었다."]], [["\"My dearest girl, what is the matter?\"", "\"여보, 무슨 일이오?\""], ["cried John, rushing in, with awful visions of scalded hands, sudden news of affliction, and secret consternation at the thought of the guest in the garden.", "존이 데인 손이며, 갑작스러운 비보(悲報)며, 정원에 있는 손님 생각에 속으로 아찔해하며 끔찍한 상상에 사로잡혀 뛰어 들어오며 외쳤다."]], [["\"O John, I _am_ so tired and hot and cross and worried!", "\"오, 존, 나 정말 너무 지치고 덥고 짜증 나고 속상해!"], ["I've been at it till I'm all worn out.", "이러다 완전히 녹초가 될 때까지 매달렸다고."], ["Do come and help me or I _shall_ die!\"", "와서 좀 도와줘요, 안 그럼 나 죽을 것 같아!\""], ["and the exhausted housewife cast herself upon his breast, giving him a sweet welcome in every sense of the word, for her pinafore had been baptized at the same time as the floor.", "기진맥진한 주부는 남편의 가슴에 몸을 던졌고, 말 그대로 온갖 의미로 달콤한 환영을 안겨 주었다. 그녀의 앞치마가 바닥과 동시에 세례를 받았던 까닭이다."]], [["\"What worries you, dear?", "\"뭐가 그리 속상하오, 여보?"], ["Has anything dreadful happened?\"", "무슨 끔찍한 일이라도 났소?\""], ["asked the anxious John, tenderly kissing the crown of the little cap, which was all askew.", "걱정스러운 존이 비뚜름하게 돌아간 작은 모자의 꼭대기에 다정하게 입을 맞추며 물었다."]], [["\"Yes,\"", "\"응.\""], ["sobbed Meg despairingly.", "메그가 절망스럽게 흐느꼈다."]], [["\"Tell me quick, then.", "\"그럼 어서 말해 봐요."], ["Don't cry, I can bear anything better than that.", "울지 말고. 그것 말고는 뭐든 견딜 수 있으니까."], ["Out with it, love.\"", "말해 봐요, 여보.\""]], [["\"The--the jelly won't jell and I don't know what to do!\"", "\"그…… 그 젤리가 안 굳어, 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어!\""]], [["John Brooke laughed then as he never dared to laugh afterward; and the derisive Scott smiled involuntarily as he heard the hearty peal, which put the finishing stroke to poor Meg's woe.", "존 브룩은 그때 이후로는 감히 다시 웃어 보지 못할 만큼 호탕하게 웃어 댔다. 그리고 빈정대던 스콧도 그 호탕한 웃음소리를 듣고는 저도 모르게 빙긋 웃었는데, 그 웃음이 가엾은 메그의 슬픔에 마지막 일격을 가하고 말았다."]], [["\"Is that all?", "\"그게 다요?"], ["Fling it out of window, and don't bother any more about it.", "창밖으로 내던져 버리고 더는 신경 쓰지 마요. 원한다면 몇 쿼트씩이라도 사다 줄 테니까."], ["I'll buy you quarts if you want it; but for heaven's sake don't have hysterics, for I've brought Jack Scott home to dinner, and--\"", "하지만 제발 신경질은 부리지 말아요. 잭 스콧을 저녁 식사에 데려왔으니까, 그리고……\""]], [["John got no further, for Meg cast him off, and clasped her hands with a tragic gesture as she fell into a chair, exclaiming in a tone of mingled indignation, reproach, and dismay,--", "존은 더 말을 잇지 못했다. 메그가 그를 떼밀어 내고는 비극적인 몸짓으로 두 손을 맞잡으며 의자에 털썩 주저앉아, 분노와 책망과 낙담이 뒤섞인 어조로 외쳤기 때문이다."]], [["\"A man to dinner, and everything in a mess!", "\"손님이 저녁을 먹으러 오는데, 죄다 엉망진창이라고!"], ["John Brooke, how _could_ you do such a thing?\"", "존 브룩, 어쩜 그럴 수가 있어요?\""]], [["\"Hush, he's in the garden!", "\"쉿, 그 사람 정원에 있어요!"], ["I forgot the confounded jelly, but it can't be helped now,\"", "내가 그 망할 젤리를 깜빡했지만, 이젠 어쩔 수 없잖소.\""], ["said John, surveying the prospect with an anxious eye.", "존이 근심 어린 눈으로 형세를 살피며 말했다."]], [["\"You ought to have sent word, or told me this morning, and you ought to have remembered how busy I was,\"", "\"미리 기별을 하든가, 아니면 오늘 아침에 말을 했어야죠. 그리고 내가 얼마나 바빴는지 생각이라도 했어야 하고요.\""], ["continued Meg petulantly; for even turtle-doves will peck when ruffled.", "메그가 토라져서 계속 쏘아붙였다. 산비둘기도 깃털이 헝클어지면 쪼아 대는 법이니까."]], [["\"I didn't know it this morning, and there was no time to send word, for I met him on the way out.", "\"오늘 아침엔 나도 몰랐고, 나오는 길에 그 친구를 만났으니 기별을 보낼 새도 없었소."], ["I never thought of asking leave, when you have always told me to do as I liked.", "당신이 늘 좋을 대로 하라고 했으니, 허락을 구할 생각은 한 번도 안 했지."], ["I never tried it before, and hang me if I ever do again!\"", "전엔 이런 적이 없었고, 다시 또 그러면 내가 사람이 아니지!\""], ["added John, with an aggrieved air.", "존이 억울하다는 듯 덧붙였다."]], [["\"I should hope not!", "\"그러길 바라요!"], ["Take him away at once; I can't see him, and there isn't any dinner.\"", "당장 그 사람 데리고 나가요. 난 그 사람 못 봐요, 게다가 저녁거리도 없다고요.\""]], [["\"Well, I like that!", "\"이거 참 기가 막히는군!"], ["Where's the beef and vegetables I sent home, and the pudding you promised?\"", "내가 보내 준 쇠고기랑 채소는 어디 가고, 당신이 만들겠다던 푸딩은 또 어디 갔소?\""], ["cried John, rushing to the larder.", "존이 식료품 저장실로 달려가며 외쳤다."]], [["\"I hadn't time to cook anything; I meant to dine at mother's.", "\"아무것도 요리할 시간이 없었어요. 친정에서 저녁을 먹을 생각이었다고요."], ["I'm sorry, but I was _so_ busy;\"", "미안해요, 하지만 내가 너무 바빴어요.\""], ["and Meg's tears began again.", "그러더니 메그의 눈물이 다시 터지기 시작했다."]], [["John was a mild man, but he was human; and after a long day's work, to come home tired, hungry, and hopeful, to find a chaotic house, an empty table, and a cross wife was not exactly conducive to repose of mind or manner.", "존은 온순한 사람이었지만, 그도 사람이었다. 하루 종일 일하고 지치고 배고프고 기대에 부풀어 집에 돌아왔는데, 난장판이 된 집과 텅 빈 식탁, 그리고 골이 난 아내를 마주하게 된 것은 마음의 평온이나 점잖은 태도를 지키는 데 그리 보탬이 되는 일은 아니었다."], ["He restrained himself, however, and the little squall would have blown over, but for one unlucky word.", "그래도 그는 자제했고, 그 작은 풍파도 한마디 불운한 말만 아니었다면 그냥 지나갔을 터였다."]], [["\"It's a scrape, I acknowledge; but if you will lend a hand, we'll pull through, and have a good time yet.", "\"낭패스러운 일인 건 인정해요. 하지만 당신이 거들어만 준다면 어떻게든 헤쳐 나가서 그래도 즐거운 시간을 보낼 수 있을 거요."], ["Don't cry, dear, but just exert yourself a bit, and knock us up something to eat.", "울지 말고, 여보, 조금만 힘을 내서 뭐든 먹을 걸 좀 차려 주구려."], ["We're both as hungry as hunters, so we sha'n't mind what it is.", "우리 둘 다 사냥꾼처럼 배가 고프니, 뭐든 상관없소."], ["Give us the cold meat, and bread and cheese; we won't ask for jelly.\"", "찬 고기랑 빵이랑 치즈를 내주구려. 젤리까진 바라지도 않을 테니.\""]], [["He meant it for a good-natured joke; but that one word sealed his fate.", "그는 좋은 뜻으로 농담을 한 것이었지만, 그 한마디가 그의 운명을 결정짓고 말았다."], ["Meg thought it was _too_ cruel to hint about her sad failure, and the last atom of patience vanished as he spoke.", "메그는 자신의 서글픈 실패를 들먹이는 것이 너무도 잔인하다고 여겼고, 그가 말하는 순간 마지막 남은 인내심의 한 조각마저 사라져 버렸다."]], [["\"You must get yourself out of the scrape as you can; I'm too used up to 'exert'", "\"이 낭패는 당신 알아서 헤쳐 나가요. 난 누구를 위해서 '힘을 낼'"], ["myself for any one.", "만큼 기운이 남아 있질 않으니까."], ["It's like a man to propose a bone and vulgar bread and cheese for company.", "손님한테 뼈다귀에다 천박하게 빵이랑 치즈나 내놓자는 게 딱 남자다운 발상이지."], ["I won't have anything of the sort in my house.", "우리 집에서 그런 일은 절대 못 해요."], ["Take that Scott up to mother's, and tell him I'm away, sick, dead,--anything.", "그 스콧이라는 사람 친정으로 데려가서, 내가 집에 없다고, 아프다고, 죽었다고, 뭐라고든 말해요."], ["I won't see him, and you two can laugh at me and my jelly as much as you like: you won't have anything else here;\"", "난 그 사람 안 만날 거고, 당신네 둘이서 나랑 내 젤리를 실컷 비웃어요. 여기선 그것 말고 다른 건 아무것도 못 얻을 테니까요.\""], ["and having delivered her defiance all in one breath, Meg cast away her pinafore, and precipitately left the field to bemoan herself in her own room.", "그렇게 단숨에 도전장을 내던진 메그는 앞치마를 벗어 던지고는, 자기 방으로 가서 신세 한탄이나 하려고 황급히 전장을 떠나 버렸다."]], [["What those two creatures did in her absence, she never knew; but Mr.", "그 두 사람이 메그가 없는 동안 무엇을 했는지, 그녀는 끝내 알지 못했다."], ["Scott was not taken \"up to mother's,\"", "다만 스콧 씨가 \"친정으로\" 끌려가지 않은 것만은 분명했다."], ["and when Meg descended, after they had strolled away together, she found traces of a promiscuous lunch which filled her with horror.", "두 사람이 함께 어슬렁어슬렁 나가 버린 뒤 메그가 아래층으로 내려와 보니, 닥치는 대로 차려 먹은 점심의 흔적이 있어 그녀를 소름 끼치게 했다."], ["Lotty reported that they had eaten \"a much, and greatly laughed, and the master bid her throw away all the sweet stuff, and hide the pots.\"", "로티의 전언에 따르면, 두 사람은 \"엄청 많이 먹고 크게 웃었으며, 주인어른이 그 단것은 죄다 버리고 단지는 숨겨 두라고 일렀다\"고 했다."]], [["Meg longed to go and tell mother; but a sense of shame at her own short-comings, of loyalty to John, \"who might be cruel, but nobody should know it,\"", "메그는 친정으로 달려가 엄마에게 죄다 털어놓고 싶었다. 하지만 자신의 부족함에 대한 부끄러움과, \"잔인할지언정 아무에게도 알려서는 안 될\""], ["restrained her; and after a summary clearing up, she dressed herself prettily, and sat down to wait for John to come and be forgiven.", "존에 대한 의리가 그녀를 붙잡았다. 그래서 후딱 뒷정리를 마친 뒤, 그녀는 예쁘게 차려입고 앉아, 존이 돌아와 용서받기를 기다렸다."]], [["Unfortunately, John didn't come, not seeing the matter in that light.", "안타깝게도, 존은 그 일을 그런 식으로 보지 않았던 터라 돌아오지 않았다."], ["He had carried it off as a good joke with Scott, excused his little wife as well as he could, and played the host so hospitably that his friend enjoyed the impromptu dinner, and promised to come again.", "그는 스콧과 함께 그 일을 좋은 농담거리로 넘겨 버렸고, 어린 아내를 되도록 잘 감싸 주었으며, 손님 대접을 어찌나 극진히 했던지 친구는 그 즉석 저녁을 흡족하게 즐기고는 또 오겠노라 약속했다."], ["But John was angry, though he did not show it; he felt that Meg had got him into a scrape, and then deserted him in his hour of need.", "그러나 존은 내색은 하지 않았어도 화가 나 있었다. 메그가 자기를 곤경에 빠뜨려 놓고는, 정작 도움이 절실한 순간에 자기를 저버렸다는 생각이 들었다."], ["\"It wasn't fair to tell a man to bring folks home any time, with perfect freedom, and when he took you at your word, to flame up and blame him, and leave him in the lurch, to be laughed at or pitied.", "\"언제든 마음껏 자유롭게 사람을 데려오라고 해 놓고, 막상 그 말대로 했더니 발끈해서 비난을 퍼붓고, 비웃음거리나 동정거리가 되도록 사람을 곤경에 내팽개치다니, 그건 공정하지 않아."], ["No, by George, it wasn't!", "천만에, 절대 공정하지 않지!"], ["and Meg must know it.\"", "메그도 그걸 알아야 해.\""], ["He had fumed inwardly during the feast, but when the flurry was over, and he strolled home, after seeing Scott off, a milder mood came over him.", "그는 식사 내내 속으로 부글부글 끓었다. 하지만 소동이 끝나고 스콧을 배웅한 뒤 어슬렁어슬렁 집으로 걸어오자, 한결 누그러진 마음이 찾아들었다."], ["\"Poor little thing!", "\"가엾은 사람!"], ["it was hard upon her when she tried so heartily to please me.", "그렇게 진심으로 나를 기쁘게 해 주려 했는데, 그게 너무 힘에 부쳤던 거지."], ["She was wrong, of course, but then she was young.", "물론 잘못이야 했지만, 아직 젊으니까. 내가 참고 가르쳐 줘야지.\""], ["I must be patient and teach her.\"", "그는 아내가 친정에 가지 않았기를 바랐다."], ["He hoped she had not gone home--he hated gossip and interference.", "남의 입에 오르내리거나 누가 끼어드는 게 질색이었던 것이다."], ["For a minute he was ruffled again at the mere thought of it; and then the fear that Meg would cry herself sick softened his heart, and sent him on at a quicker pace, resolving to be calm and kind, but firm, quite firm, and show her where she had failed in her duty to her spouse.", "그 생각만으로도 그는 잠시 또 마음이 거슬렸다. 하지만 메그가 울다 못해 병이라도 나면 어쩌나 하는 두려움에 마음이 누그러져, 침착하고 다정하되 단호하게, 아주 단호하게 굴면서 아내가 남편에 대한 도리에서 어디를 잘못했는지 일러 주리라 마음먹고는 걸음을 재촉했다."]], [["Meg likewise resolved to be \"calm and kind, but firm,\"", "메그 역시 \"침착하고 다정하되 단호하게\""], ["and show _him_ his duty.", "굴면서 남편에게 그의 도리를 일깨워 주리라 마음먹었다."], ["She longed to run to meet him, and beg pardon, and be kissed and comforted, as she was sure of being; but, of course, she did nothing of the sort, and when she saw John coming, began to hum quite naturally, as she rocked and sewed, like a lady of leisure in her best parlor.", "그녀는 그를 맞으러 달려 나가 용서를 빌고, 틀림없이 그러리라 확신하는 대로 입맞춤과 위로를 받고 싶었다. 그러나 물론 그녀는 그런 짓은 전혀 하지 않았고, 존이 오는 것을 보자 한가로운 안주인이 자기 집 가장 좋은 응접실에 앉아 있기라도 한 듯, 흔들의자에 앉아 바느질을 하며 아주 자연스럽게 콧노래를 흥얼거리기 시작했다."]], [["John was a little disappointed not to find a tender Niobe; but, feeling that his dignity demanded the first apology, he made none, only came leisurely in, and laid himself upon the sofa, with the singularly relevant remark,--", "존은 다정한 니오베를 만나지 못해 조금 실망했다. 하지만 자신의 체면상 먼저 사과를 받아야 마땅하다고 느꼈기에, 그는 아무 사과도 하지 않고, 그저 느긋이 들어와 소파에 몸을 누이고는, 묘하게 들어맞는 말을 했다."]], [["\"We are going to have a new moon, my dear.\"", "\"여보, 곧 초승달이 뜨겠구려.\""]], [["\"I've no objection,\"", "\"난 별 이의 없어요.\""], ["was Meg's equally soothing remark.", "메그도 그에 못지않게 누그러뜨리는 대꾸를 했다."]], [["A few other topics of general interest were introduced by Mr. Brooke, and wet-blanketed by Mrs.", "브룩 씨가 두루 관심 가질 만한 화제를 몇 가지 더 꺼냈으나 브룩 부인이 찬물을 끼얹어, 대화는 시들해졌다."], ["Brooke, and conversation languished. John went to one window, unfolded his paper, and wrapped himself in it, figuratively speaking.", "존은 한쪽 창가로 가서 신문을 펼쳐 들고는, 말하자면 그 속에 자신을 파묻어 버렸다."], ["Meg went to the other window, and sewed as if new rosettes for her slippers were among the necessaries of life.", "메그는 다른 쪽 창가로 가서, 마치 슬리퍼에 달 새 장미 매듭 장식이 살아가는 데 꼭 필요한 물건이라도 되는 양 바느질을 했다."], ["Neither spoke; both looked quite \"calm and firm,\"", "둘 다 입을 열지 않았다. 둘 다 자못 \"침착하고 단호해\""], ["and both felt desperately uncomfortable.", "보였고, 둘 다 못 견디게 불편한 심정이었다."]], [["\"Oh dear,\"", "\"아이참.\" 메그는 생각했다."], ["thought Meg, \"married life is very trying, and does need infinite patience, as well as love, as mother says.\"", "\"결혼 생활이란 참 고된 거구나. 엄마 말씀처럼 사랑뿐 아니라 한없는 인내심도 필요한 거야.\""], ["The word \"mother\" suggested other maternal counsels, given long ago, and received with unbelieving protests.", "'엄마'라는 단어가 오래전에 들려주셨던, 그때는 믿지 못하겠다며 항변하면서 받아들였던 다른 모성의 충고들을 떠올리게 했다."]], [["\"John is a good man, but he has his faults, and you must learn to see and bear with them, remembering your own.", "\"존은 좋은 사람이지만, 그에게도 결점은 있단다. 너는 네 자신의 결점도 떠올리면서, 그 결점들을 보아 내고 견디는 법을 배워야 해."], ["He is very decided, but never will be obstinate, if you reason kindly, not oppose impatiently.", "그 사람은 무척 단호하지만, 네가 다정하게 이치를 따져 설득하고 성급하게 맞서지만 않는다면 결코 고집을 부리지는 않을 거야."], ["He is very accurate, and particular about the truth--a good trait, though you call him 'fussy.'", "그 사람은 아주 꼼꼼하고 진실에 까다로운데, 그건 좋은 점이란다, 네가 '유난스럽다'고 하더라도 말이야."], ["Never deceive him by look or word, Meg, and he will give you the confidence you deserve, the support you need.", "메그야, 절대로 눈빛으로든 말로든 그를 속이지 마라. 그러면 그 사람은 네가 받아 마땅한 신뢰와 네게 필요한 든든한 버팀목이 되어 줄 거야."], ["He has a temper, not like ours,--one flash, and then all over,--but the white, still anger, that is seldom stirred, but once kindled, is hard to quench.", "그 사람에게도 성미가 있는데, 우리 같은 성미는 아니란다. 우리 것은 한 번 번쩍하고는 곧 사그라지지만, 그 사람 것은 좀처럼 일지 않는 희고 고요한 분노여서, 한번 불붙으면 좀처럼 꺼뜨리기 어렵단다."], ["Be careful, very careful, not to wake this anger against yourself, for peace and happiness depend on keeping his respect.", "조심해라, 아주 조심해서, 너 자신에게 그 분노가 일지 않게 하렴. 평화와 행복이란 그 사람의 존중을 잃지 않는 데 달려 있으니까."], ["Watch yourself, be the first to ask pardon if you both err, and guard against the little piques, misunderstandings, and hasty words that often pave the way for bitter sorrow and regret.\"", "너 자신을 잘 살피고, 둘 다 잘못했을 땐 먼저 용서를 구하렴. 그리고 흔히 쓰디쓴 슬픔과 후회로 가는 길을 닦곤 하는 사소한 토라짐과 오해, 성급한 말들을 경계하렴.\""]], [["These words came back to Meg, as she sat sewing in the sunset, especially the last.", "이 말들이 노을 속에서 바느질을 하며 앉아 있는 메그의 마음속에 되살아났다. 특히 그 마지막 대목이."], ["This was the first serious disagreement; her own hasty speeches sounded both silly and unkind, as she recalled them, her own anger looked childish now, and thoughts of poor John coming home to such a scene quite melted her heart.", "이번이 처음으로 겪는 심각한 불화였다. 자신이 성급하게 내뱉었던 말들을 떠올려 보니 어리석고도 매정하게 들렸고, 자신의 분노가 이제는 유치해 보였으며, 가엾은 존이 그런 광경이 펼쳐진 집에 돌아온다는 생각에 그녀의 마음이 완전히 녹아내렸다."], ["She glanced at him with tears in her eyes, but he did not see them; she put down her work and got up, thinking, \"I _will_ be the first to say, 'Forgive me,'\"", "그녀는 눈물이 그렁그렁한 눈으로 그를 흘끔 보았지만, 그는 그것을 보지 못했다. 그녀는 바느질감을 내려놓고 일어서며 \"내가 먼저 '용서해 줘요'라고 말할 거야\"라고 생각했지만, 그는 그 소리를 못 들은 듯했다."], ["but he did not seem to hear her; she went very slowly across the room, for pride was hard to swallow, and stood by him, but he did not turn his head.", "자존심을 삼키기가 힘들어, 그녀는 아주 천천히 방을 가로질러 가서 그의 곁에 섰지만, 그는 고개를 돌리지 않았다. 잠시 그녀는 정말이지 도저히 못 하겠다는 심정이 들었다."], ["For a minute she felt as if she really couldn't do it; then came the thought, \"This is the beginning, I'll do my part, and have nothing to reproach myself with,\"", "그때 이런 생각이 떠올랐다. \"이게 시작이야."], ["and stooping down, she softly kissed her husband on the forehead.", "내 몫을 다하면 스스로 책망할 일이 없을 거야.\" 그러고는 몸을 숙여 남편의 이마에 살며시 입을 맞추었다."], ["Of course that settled it; the penitent kiss was better than a world of words, and John had her on his knee in a minute, saying tenderly,--", "물론 그것으로 모든 게 해결되었다. 뉘우치는 입맞춤은 그 어떤 말보다도 나았고, 존은 이내 그녀를 무릎에 앉히고는 다정하게 말했다."]], [["\"It was too bad to laugh at the poor little jelly-pots.", "\"가엾은 젤리 단지를 두고 웃다니 정말 못됐어."], ["Forgive me, dear, I never will again!\"", "용서해 줘요, 여보, 다시는 안 그럴 테니!\""]], [["But he did, oh bless you, yes, hundreds of times, and so did Meg, both declaring that it was the sweetest jelly they ever made; for family peace was preserved in that little family jar.", "하지만 그는 다시 그랬다. 오, 어쩌랴, 그것도 수백 번이나. 메그도 마찬가지였다. 둘 다 그게 자기들이 만든 가장 달콤한 젤리였다고 입을 모았다. 그 작은 집안싸움 속에서 가정의 평화가 지켜졌으니 말이다."]], [["After this, Meg had Mr. Scott to dinner by special invitation, and served him up a pleasant feast without a cooked wife for the first course; on which occasion she was so gay and gracious, and made everything go off so charmingly, that Mr.", "이 일이 있은 뒤, 메그는 특별히 초대해 스콧 씨를 저녁 식사에 모셨고, 이번에는 첫 코스로 익어 버린 아내를 내놓는 일 없이 즐거운 잔칫상을 차려 냈다."], ["Scott told John he was a happy fellow, and shook his head over the hardships of bachelorhood all the way home.", "그날 메그가 어찌나 명랑하고 우아하게, 모든 일을 어찌나 근사하게 진행했던지, 스콧 씨는 존에게 자네는 참 행복한 사람이라고 말하고는, 집으로 돌아가는 내내 총각 신세의 고달픔에 고개를 절레절레 흔들었다."]], [["In the autumn, new trials and experiences came to Meg.", "가을이 되자 메그에게 새로운 시련과 경험이 찾아왔다."], ["Sallie Moffat renewed her friendship, was always running out for a dish of gossip at the little house, or inviting \"that poor dear\"", "샐리 모펏이 다시 우정을 다지면서, 수다를 떨려고 작은 집으로 줄곧 달려오거나, 큰 집으로 와서 하루를 보내라고 \"그 가엾은 것\"을 초대하곤 했다. 그것은 즐거운 일이었다."], ["to come in and spend the day at the big house.", "흐린 날이면 메그는 종종 외로움을 느꼈으니까."], ["It was pleasant, for in dull weather Meg often felt lonely; all were busy at home, John absent till night, and nothing to do but sew, or read, or potter about.", "친정 식구들은 다들 바빴고, 존은 밤이 되어야 돌아왔으며, 할 일이라곤 바느질하거나 책을 읽거나 빈둥거리는 것뿐이었다."], ["So it naturally fell out that Meg got into the way of gadding and gossiping with her friend.", "그래서 메그가 친구와 어울려 쏘다니며 수다 떠는 데 빠져든 것도 자연스러운 일이었다."], ["Seeing Sallie's pretty things made her long for such, and pity herself because she had not got them.", "샐리의 예쁜 물건들을 보고 있으면 그런 것들이 갖고 싶어졌고, 그것들을 가지지 못한 자신이 가여워졌다."], ["Sallie was very kind, and often offered her the coveted trifles; but Meg declined them, knowing that John wouldn't like it; and then this foolish little woman went and did what John disliked infinitely worse.", "샐리는 무척 친절해서 탐나는 그 자잘한 물건들을 곧잘 주려 했지만, 메그는 존이 싫어하리란 걸 알기에 사양했다. 그러고는 이 어리석은 작은 여인은 존이 그보다 한없이 더 싫어할 일을 저지르고 말았다."]], [["She knew her husband's income, and she loved to feel that he trusted her, not only with his happiness, but what some men seem to value more,--his money.", "메그는 남편의 수입을 알고 있었고, 그가 자신의 행복뿐 아니라 어떤 남자들은 그보다 더 귀히 여기는 듯한 것, 곧 돈까지 맡겨 준다는 사실을 느끼는 것이 좋았다."], ["She knew where it was, was free to take what she liked, and all he asked was that she should keep account of every penny, pay bills once a month, and remember that she was a poor man's wife.", "그녀는 돈이 어디 있는지 알았고, 마음에 드는 것은 마음껏 가져다 쓸 수 있었으며, 그가 바라는 거라곤 그저 한 푼까지 장부에 적고, 한 달에 한 번 청구서를 치르며, 자신이 가난한 사람의 아내임을 잊지 말라는 것뿐이었다. 지금까지는 잘해 왔다."], ["Till now, she had done well, been prudent and exact, kept her little account-books neatly, and showed them to him monthly without fear.", "알뜰하고 정확하게, 작은 가계부를 깔끔하게 적어 두었다가 매달 거리낌 없이 그에게 보여 주었다. 그러나 그 가을, 뱀이 메그의 낙원으로 기어들어, 현대의 수많은 이브가 그러하듯, 사과가 아니라 옷으로 그녀를 꾀었다."], ["But that autumn the serpent got into Meg's paradise, and tempted her, like many a modern Eve, not with apples, but with dress.", "메그는 동정받으며 가난뱅이 취급당하는 것이 싫었다. 그것이 그녀를 못 견디게 했지만, 그녀는 그렇다고 털어놓기가 부끄러웠다."], ["Meg didn't like to be pitied and made to feel poor; it irritated her, but she was ashamed to confess it, and now and then she tried to console herself by buying something pretty, so that Sallie needn't think she had to economize.", "그래서 이따금 예쁜 물건을 사며 스스로를 달래려 했으니, 샐리가 자기를 절약해야 하는 처지로 여기지 않게끔 하려는 것이었다. 그러고 나면 늘 죄책감이 들었다."], ["She always felt wicked after it, for the pretty things were seldom necessaries; but then they cost so little, it wasn't worth worrying about; so the trifles increased unconsciously, and in the shopping excursions she was no longer a passive looker-on.", "그 예쁜 물건들이란 좀처럼 꼭 필요한 것이 아니었으니까. 하지만 워낙 값이 싸니 걱정할 거리도 못 된다 싶었고, 그렇게 자잘한 물건들이 자기도 모르게 늘어 갔으며, 쇼핑을 다닐 때 그녀는 더는 가만히 구경만 하는 사람이 아니었다."]], [["But the trifles cost more than one would imagine; and when she cast up her accounts at the end of the month, the sum total rather scared her.", "하지만 그 자잘한 물건들은 생각보다 값이 더 나갔다. 그래서 월말에 장부를 결산해 보니, 총액이 그녀를 적잖이 놀라게 했다."], ["John was busy that month, and left the bills to her; the next month he was absent; but the third he had a grand quarterly settling up, and Meg never forgot it.", "그달에 존은 바빠서 청구서를 그녀에게 맡겨 두었다. 다음 달에는 그가 집을 비웠다."], ["A few days before she had done a dreadful thing, and it weighed upon her conscience.", "하지만 셋째 달에는 분기별로 거창하게 결산을 하게 되었고, 메그는 그날을 영영 잊지 못했다. 며칠 전 그녀는 끔찍한 짓을 저질렀고, 그것이 양심을 짓눌렀다."], ["Sallie had been buying silks, and Meg longed for a new one,--just a handsome light one for parties, her black silk was so common, and thin things for evening wear were only proper for girls.", "샐리가 비단을 여러 필 사들였던 터라, 메그도 새 비단옷이 하나 갖고 싶었다. 그저 파티에 입고 갈 고운 연한 빛깔의 옷이면 됐다."], ["Aunt March usually gave the sisters a present of twenty-five dollars apiece at New Year; that was only a month to wait, and here was a lovely violet silk going at a bargain, and she had the money, if she only dared to take it.", "검은 비단옷은 너무 흔했고, 저녁 모임에 입을 얇은 옷이란 처녀들에게나 어울리는 것이었으니까. 마치 대고모는 새해마다 자매들에게 으레 한 사람당 이십오 달러씩을 선물로 주었다."], ["John always said what was his was hers; but would he think it right to spend not only the prospective five-and-twenty, but another five-and-twenty out of the household fund?", "한 달만 기다리면 될 일이었고, 여기 사랑스러운 보랏빛 비단이 헐값에 나와 있었으며, 그녀에게는 그 돈이 있었다. 차마 손을 댈 용기만 낸다면 말이다."], ["That was the question.", "존은 늘 자기 것이 곧 그녀 것이라고 말했다. 하지만 앞으로 받을 이십오 달러뿐 아니라 살림 비용에서 또 이십오 달러까지 쓰는 것을 그가 옳다고 여길까?"], ["Sallie had urged her to do it, had offered to loan the money, and with the best intentions in life, had tempted Meg beyond her strength.", "그것이 문제였다. 샐리는 그렇게 하라고 부추겼고, 돈을 빌려주겠다고까지 했으며, 더없이 좋은 뜻에서 메그를 그녀의 분수에 넘치도록 유혹했다."], ["In an evil moment the shopman held up the lovely, shimmering folds, and said, \"A bargain, I assure you, ma'am.\"", "불행한 순간에 점원이 사랑스럽게 어른거리는 비단 주름을 펼쳐 들며 말했다. \"거저나 다름없어요, 부인, 정말입니다.\""], ["She answered, \"I'll take it;\"", "그녀는 대답했다. \"그걸로 할게요.\""], ["and it was cut off and paid for, and Sallie had exulted, and she had laughed as if it were a thing of no consequence, and driven away, feeling as if she had stolen something, and the police were after her.", "그러자 비단이 잘려 나가고 값이 치러졌으며, 샐리는 의기양양해했고, 그녀는 아무것도 아닌 일인 양 웃었다. 그러고는 마치 무언가를 훔쳐서 경찰이 뒤쫓기라도 하는 듯한 기분으로 마차를 몰아 떠났다."]], [["When she got home, she tried to assuage the pangs of remorse by spreading forth the lovely silk; but it looked less silvery now, didn't become her, after all, and the words \"fifty dollars\"", "집에 돌아온 그녀는 사랑스러운 비단을 펼쳐 놓으며 후회의 가책을 달래 보려 했다. 하지만 이제 그것은 덜 은빛으로 보였고, 알고 보니 자신에게 어울리지도 않았으며, \"오십 달러\"라는 글자가 폭마다 무늬처럼 찍혀 있는 것만 같았다."], ["seemed stamped like a pattern down each breadth.", "그녀는 그것을 치워 두었다."], ["She put it away; but it haunted her, not delightfully, as a new dress should, but dreadfully, like the ghost of a folly that was not easily laid.", "하지만 그것은 그녀를 따라다니며 괴롭혔다. 새 옷이 으레 그러하듯 즐겁게가 아니라, 쉽사리 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 어리석은 짓의 유령처럼 끔찍하게."], ["When John got out his books that night, Meg's heart sank, and for the first time in her married life, she was afraid of her husband.", "그날 밤 존이 장부를 꺼내 들자 메그는 가슴이 철렁 내려앉았고, 결혼 생활을 통틀어 처음으로 남편이 두려워졌다."], ["The kind, brown eyes looked as if they could be stern; and though he was unusually merry, she fancied he had found her out, but didn't mean to let her know it.", "그 다정한 갈색 눈이 엄해질 수도 있을 것처럼 보였다. 그리고 그가 여느 때와 달리 즐거워하긴 했지만, 그녀는 그가 자신의 잘못을 다 알아채고도 모른 척 넘어가려는 것이라 짐작했다."], ["The house-bills were all paid, the books all in order.", "살림 청구서는 모두 치러졌고, 장부도 다 정돈되어 있었다."], ["John had praised her, and was undoing the old pocket-book which they called the \"bank,\"", "존은 그녀를 칭찬하고는, 그들이 \"은행\"이라 부르던 낡은 지갑을 풀고 있었다."], ["when Meg, knowing that it was quite empty, stopped his hand, saying nervously,--", "그것이 텅 비어 있다는 걸 아는 메그가 초조하게 그의 손을 막으며 말했다."]], [["\"You haven't seen my private expense book yet.\"", "\"내 개인 지출 장부는 아직 안 봤잖아요.\""]], [["John never asked to see it; but she always insisted on his doing so, and used to enjoy his masculine amazement at the queer things women wanted, and made him guess what \"piping\"", "존은 한 번도 그것을 보자고 한 적이 없었지만, 그녀가 늘 그걸 보라고 우겼다. 여자들이 원하는 별난 물건들에 그가 남자답게 어리둥절해하는 모습을 그녀는 즐겼던 것이다."], ["was, demand fiercely the meaning of a \"hug-me-tight,\" or wonder how a little thing composed of three rosebuds, a bit of velvet, and a pair of strings, could possibly be a bonnet, and cost five or six dollars.", "\"파이핑\"이 뭐냐고 알아맞혀 보라고 하거나, \"허그미타이트\"가 무슨 뜻이냐고 짐짓 다그쳐 묻게 하거나, 장미 봉오리 세 개와 벨벳 한 조각, 끈 한 쌍으로 이루어진 자그마한 물건이 어떻게 보닛이라는 것이며 어떻게 대여섯 달러나 할 수 있는지 의아해하게 만들었던 것이다."], ["That night he looked as if he would like the fun of quizzing her figures and pretending to be horrified at her extravagance, as he often did, being particularly proud of his prudent wife.", "그날 밤 그는 평소처럼, 그녀가 적어 놓은 숫자를 가지고 놀려 대고 그녀의 낭비에 질겁한 척하는 재미를 즐기고 싶어 하는 듯 보였다. 알뜰한 아내를 유난히 자랑스러워하던 그였으니까."]], [["The little book was brought slowly out, and laid down before him.", "작은 장부가 천천히 꺼내져 그의 앞에 놓였다."], ["Meg got behind his chair under pretence of smoothing the wrinkles out of his tired forehead, and standing there, she said, with her panic increasing with every word,--", "메그는 지친 그의 이마에 잡힌 주름을 펴 준다는 핑계로 의자 뒤로 가서 섰고, 거기 선 채 말마다 점점 더해 가는 공포를 안고 입을 열었다."]], [["\"John, dear, I'm ashamed to show you my book, for I've really been dreadfully extravagant lately. I go about so much I must have things, you know, and Sallie advised my getting it, so I did; and my New-Year's money will partly pay for it: but I was sorry after I'd done it, for I knew you'd think it wrong in me.\"", "\"존, 여보, 내 장부를 보여 주기가 부끄러워요. 사실 요즘 끔찍하게 낭비를 했거든요. 워낙 여기저기 다니다 보니 이것저것 필요해서 그래요, 알잖아요. 샐리가 사라고 권해서 사긴 했어요. 새해에 받을 돈으로 얼마쯤은 치를 수 있을 거예요. 하지만 하고 나서 후회했어요. 당신이 내가 잘못했다고 여길 줄 알았으니까요.\""]], [["John laughed, and drew her round beside him, saying good-humoredly, \"Don't go and hide. I won't beat you if you _have_ got a pair of killing boots; I'm rather proud of my wife's feet, and don't mind if she does pay eight or nine dollars for her boots, if they are good ones.\"", "존은 웃으며 그녀를 끌어다 곁에 앉히고는 다정하게 말했다. \"가서 숨고 그러지 말아요. 당신이 사람 죽이게 멋진 부츠를 한 켤레 샀다 해도 때리진 않을 테니까. 난 아내 발이 예쁜 게 좀 자랑스럽거든. 좋은 거라면 부츠에 팔구 달러쯤 치른다 해도 상관없소.\""]], [["That had been one of her last \"trifles,\"", "그것이 바로 그녀가 최근에 산 \"자잘한 물건들\""], ["and John's eye had fallen on it as he spoke.", "가운데 하나였고, 그가 그 말을 하는 동안 그의 눈길이 마침 거기에 떨어진 것이었다."], ["\"Oh, what _will_ he say when he comes to that awful fifty dollars!\"", "'오, 저 끔찍한 오십 달러에 이르면 뭐라고 할까!'"], ["thought Meg, with a shiver.", "메그는 몸을 떨며 생각했다."]], [["\"It's worse than boots, it's a silk dress,\"", "\"부츠보다 더한 거예요. 비단 드레스예요.\""], ["she said, with the calmness of desperation, for she wanted the worst over.", "그녀는 자포자기한 침착함으로 말했다. 최악의 고비를 어서 넘기고 싶었던 것이다."]], [["\"Well, dear, what is the 'dem'd total,' as Mr. Mantalini says?\"", "\"음, 여보, 그래서 그 '망할 놈의 총액'이 얼마요, 만탈리니 씨 말마따나?\""]], [["That didn't sound like John, and she knew he was looking up at her with the straightforward look that she had always been ready to meet and answer with one as frank till now.", "그건 존답지 않은 말이었다. 그가 지금까지는 늘 그녀가 거리낌 없이 마주하고 거리낌 없이 답해 오던 그 솔직한 눈빛으로 자신을 올려다보고 있다는 걸 그녀는 알았다."], ["She turned the page and her head at the same time, pointing to the sum which would have been bad enough without the fifty, but which was appalling to her with that added.", "그녀는 페이지를 넘기면서 동시에 고개를 돌려, 오십 달러가 없어도 충분히 고약했을, 그러나 그 돈이 더해지니 그녀에게는 소름 끼치는 그 액수를 가리켰다. 잠시 방 안이 아주 고요했다."], ["For a minute the room was very still; then John said slowly,--but she could feel it cost him an effort to express no displeasure,--", "이윽고 존이 천천히 말했다. 불쾌함을 드러내지 않으려고 애쓰는 것이 그녀에게도 느껴졌다."]], [["\"Well, I don't know that fifty is much for a dress, with all the furbelows and notions you have to have to finish it off these days.\"", "\"음, 요즘은 옷 하나를 마무리하는 데 온갖 주름 장식이며 자질구레한 것들이 다 있어야 하니, 드레스 하나에 오십 달러가 그리 많은 건진 모르겠구려.\""]], [["\"It isn't made or trimmed,\"", "\"아직 짓지도, 장식을 달지도 않은 거예요.\""], ["sighed Meg faintly, for a sudden recollection of the cost still to be incurred quite overwhelmed her.", "메그가 가냘프게 한숨지었다. 앞으로 더 들어갈 비용이 문득 떠올라 그녀를 완전히 짓눌렀던 것이다."]], [["\"Twenty-five yards of silk seems a good deal to cover one small woman, but I've no doubt my wife will look as fine as Ned Moffat's when she gets it on,\"", "\"비단 스물다섯 마(碼)면 자그마한 여자 하나 감싸기엔 꽤 많은 것 같긴 한데, 내 아내가 그걸 입으면 네드 모펏 부인 못지않게 근사해 보이겠지.\""], ["said John dryly.", "존이 무덤덤하게 말했다."]], [["\"I know you are angry, John, but I can't help it.", "\"당신이 화났다는 거 알아요, 존. 하지만 어쩔 수가 없었어요."], ["I don't mean to waste your money, and I didn't think those little things would count up so.", "당신 돈을 헤프게 쓸 생각은 없었고, 그 자잘한 것들이 그렇게나 쌓일 줄도 몰랐어요."], ["I can't resist them when I see Sallie buying all she wants, and pitying me because I don't.", "샐리가 원하는 건 다 사 대면서, 못 사는 나를 가엾어하는 걸 보면 도무지 못 참겠어요."], ["I try to be contented, but it is hard, and I'm tired of being poor.\"", "만족하려고 애써 보지만, 너무 힘들어요. 이젠 가난한 게 지긋지긋해요.\""]], [["The last words were spoken so low she thought he did not hear them, but he did, and they wounded him deeply, for he had denied himself many pleasures for Meg's sake.", "마지막 말은 어찌나 나직이 했던지 그녀는 그가 듣지 못했으리라 여겼다. 하지만 그는 들었고, 그 말은 그에게 깊은 상처를 주었다."], ["She could have bitten her tongue out the minute she had said it, for John pushed the books away, and got up, saying, with a little quiver in his voice, \"I was afraid of this; I do my best, Meg.\"", "메그를 위해 그는 숱한 즐거움을 마다해 왔으니까. 그 말을 내뱉은 순간 그녀는 혀라도 깨물고 싶은 심정이었다."], ["If he had scolded her, or even shaken her, it would not have broken her heart like those few words.", "존이 장부를 밀쳐 내고 일어서며, 목소리에 가벼운 떨림을 담아 말했기 때문이다. \"이렇게 될까 봐 걱정했소."], ["She ran to him and held him close, crying, with repentant tears, \"O John, my dear, kind, hard-working boy, I didn't mean it!", "난 최선을 다하고 있어요, 메그.\" 그가 그녀를 꾸짖었거나, 심지어 마구 흔들어 댔더라도, 그 몇 마디만큼 그녀의 가슴을 무너뜨리지는 못했을 것이다."], ["It was so wicked, so untrue and ungrateful, how could I say it!", "그녀는 그에게 달려가 꼭 끌어안고는, 뉘우치는 눈물을 쏟으며 울부짖었다. \"오, 존, 다정하고 착하고 부지런히 일하는 내 사람, 진심이 아니었어요!"], ["Oh, how could I say it!\"", "그건 너무 못되고, 너무 사실이 아니고, 너무 배은망덕한 말이었어요, 내가 어떻게 그런 말을 했을까! 오, 어떻게 그런 말을!\""]], [["He was very kind, forgave her readily, and did not utter one reproach; but Meg knew that she had done and said a thing which would not be forgotten soon, although he might never allude to it again. She had promised to love him for better for worse; and then she, his wife, had reproached him with his poverty, after spending his earnings recklessly. It was dreadful; and the worst of it was John went on so quietly afterward, just as if nothing had happened, except that he stayed in town later, and worked at night when she had gone to cry herself to sleep. A week of remorse nearly made Meg sick; and the discovery that John had countermanded the order for his new great-coat reduced her to a state of despair which was pathetic to behold. He had simply said, in answer to her surprised inquiries as to the change, \"I can't afford it, my dear.\"", "그는 무척 다정하게 선뜻 용서해 주었고, 단 한마디 책망도 하지 않았다. 하지만 메그는 자신이, 비록 그가 다시는 입에 올리지 않을지라도, 좀처럼 잊히지 않을 짓과 말을 저질렀음을 알았다. 그녀는 좋을 때나 궂을 때나 그를 사랑하겠노라 맹세했었다. 그런데 그의 아내인 그녀가, 그가 벌어 온 돈을 무모하게 써 놓고는, 그의 가난을 들어 그를 책망한 것이었다. 끔찍한 일이었다. 그중에서도 가장 견디기 힘든 것은, 그 뒤로 존이 너무도 조용히 지냈다는 점이었다. 마치 아무 일도 없었던 것처럼. 다만 시내에 더 늦게까지 머물렀고, 그녀가 울다 잠든 밤이면 밤늦도록 일을 했다. 일주일 동안의 가책에 메그는 거의 앓아누울 지경이었다. 그리고 존이 새 외투 주문을 취소했다는 사실을 알게 되자, 그녀는 차마 보기 딱할 만큼 절망에 빠지고 말았다. 무엇이 달라졌느냐는 놀란 물음에 그는 그저 이렇게 대답했을 뿐이었다. \"그럴 형편이 못 돼서요, 여보.\""]], [["Meg said no more, but a few minutes after he found her in the hall, with her face buried in the old great-coat, crying as if her heart would break.", "메그는 더 말하지 않았다. 하지만 몇 분 뒤 그는 현관에서 그녀를 발견했다. 낡은 외투에 얼굴을 파묻은 채, 가슴이 미어지도록 울고 있는 그녀를."]], [["They had a long talk that night, and Meg learned to love her husband better for his poverty, because it seemed to have made a man of him, given him the strength and courage to fight his own way, and taught him a tender patience with which to bear and comfort the natural longings and failures of those he loved.", "그날 밤 두 사람은 오래도록 이야기를 나누었고, 메그는 남편의 가난 때문에 그를 더욱 사랑하게 되었다. 그 가난이야말로 그를 한 사람의 어른으로 만들어, 제힘으로 길을 헤쳐 나갈 힘과 용기를 주었고, 사랑하는 이들의 어쩔 수 없는 갈망과 실패를 너그러이 견디고 다독여 줄 다정한 인내심을 가르쳐 준 듯했기 때문이다."]], [["Next day she put her pride in her pocket, went to Sallie, told the truth, and asked her to buy the silk as a favor.", "이튿날 그녀는 자존심을 주머니에 넣어 두고 샐리를 찾아가 사실대로 털어놓고는, 부탁이니 그 비단을 사 달라고 청했다."], ["The good-natured Mrs. Moffat willingly did so, and had the delicacy not to make her a present of it immediately afterward.", "마음씨 고운 모펏 부인은 기꺼이 그렇게 해 주었고, 곧바로 그것을 메그에게 선물로 주는 무례를 범하지 않을 만큼 세심한 배려를 보였다."], ["Then Meg ordered home the great-coat, and, when John arrived, she put it on, and asked him how he liked her new silk gown.", "그런 다음 메그는 외투를 집으로 주문해 두었다가, 존이 돌아오자 그것을 입혀 주고는 자기 새 비단 드레스가 어떠냐고 물었다."], ["One can imagine what answer he made, how he received his present, and what a blissful state of things ensued.", "그가 어떤 대답을 했을지, 자신의 선물을 어떻게 받았을지, 그리고 얼마나 행복한 상황이 뒤따랐을지는 능히 짐작할 수 있으리라."], ["John came home early, Meg gadded no more; and that great-coat was put on in the morning by a very happy husband, and taken off at night by a most devoted little wife.", "존은 일찍 집에 돌아왔고, 메그는 더는 쏘다니지 않았다. 그리고 그 외투는 아침이면 더없이 행복한 남편이 걸쳤고, 밤이면 더없이 헌신적인 작은 아내가 벗겨 주었다."], ["So the year rolled round, and at midsummer there came to Meg a new experience,--the deepest and tenderest of a woman's life.", "그렇게 한 해가 흘러갔고, 한여름에 이르러 메그에게 새로운 경험이 찾아왔으니, 여자의 삶에서 가장 깊고 가장 다정한 경험이었다."]], [["Laurie came sneaking into the kitchen of the Dove-cote, one Saturday, with an excited face, and was received with the clash of cymbals; for Hannah clapped her hands with a saucepan in one and the cover in the other.", "어느 토요일, 로리가 들뜬 얼굴로 비둘기 둥지의 부엌으로 살그머니 들어왔다가 심벌즈가 쩌렁쩌렁 울리는 환영을 받았다. 한나가 한 손엔 냄비를, 다른 손엔 뚜껑을 든 채 손뼉을 쳤던 것이다."]], [["\"How's the little mamma?", "\"꼬마 엄마는 좀 어때요?"], ["Where is everybody?", "다들 어디 있고요?"], ["Why didn't you tell me before I came home?\"", "내가 집에 돌아오기 전에 왜 말 안 해 주셨어요?\""], ["began Laurie, in a loud whisper.", "로리가 큰 소리로 속삭이듯 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["\"Happy as a queen, the dear!", "\"여왕님처럼 행복하지요, 그 귀한 양반!"], ["Every soul of 'em is upstairs a worshipin'; we didn't want no hurrycanes round.", "식구들 죄다 위층에서 떠받들고 있다우. 우린 그 근처에서 무슨 회오리바람 같은 건 일으키고 싶지 않았으니까."], ["Now you go into the parlor, and I'll send 'em down to you,\"", "자, 응접실로 들어가 있으면 내가 다들 내려보내 드리리다.\""], ["with which somewhat involved reply Hannah vanished, chuckling ecstatically.", "그렇게 다소 뒤죽박죽인 대답을 남기고 한나는 기뻐서 낄낄대며 사라졌다."]], [["Presently Jo appeared, proudly bearing a flannel bundle laid forth upon a large pillow.", "이윽고 조가 큼직한 베개 위에 펼쳐 올린 플란넬 꾸러미를 자랑스레 받쳐 들고 나타났다."], ["Jo's face was very sober, but her eyes twinkled, and there was an odd sound in her voice of repressed emotion of some sort.", "조의 얼굴은 무척 진지했지만, 두 눈은 반짝였고, 목소리에는 무언가 억눌린 감정의 묘한 울림이 배어 있었다."]], [["\"Shut your eyes and hold out your arms,\"", "\"눈 감고 두 팔 내밀어 봐.\""], ["she said invitingly.", "조가 부추기듯 말했다."]], [["Laurie backed precipitately into a corner, and put his hands behind him with an imploring gesture: \"No, thank you, I'd rather not.", "로리는 황급히 구석으로 물러서며 두 손을 뒤로 돌리고는 사정하는 몸짓을 했다. \"아니, 됐어."], ["I shall drop it or smash it, as sure as fate.\"", "그냥 안 할래. 떨어뜨리거나 짓뭉개거나, 틀림없이 그럴 테니까.\""]], [["\"Then you sha'n't see your nevvy,\"", "\"그럼 네 조카는 못 보지.\""], ["said Jo decidedly, turning as if to go.", "조가 단호하게 말하며 돌아서서 가려는 듯한 시늉을 했다."]], [["\"I will, I will!", "\"볼게, 볼게!"], ["only you must be responsible for damages;\"", "대신 무슨 일 생기면 네가 책임져야 해.\""], ["and, obeying orders, Laurie heroically shut his eyes while something was put into his arms.", "그리고 명령에 따라 로리가 비장하게 눈을 감자 무언가가 그의 두 팔에 안겨졌다."], ["A peal of laughter from Jo, Amy, Mrs. March, Hannah, and John caused him to open them the next minute, to find himself invested with two babies instead of one.", "조와 에이미, 마치 부인, 한나, 그리고 존이 한꺼번에 터뜨린 웃음소리에 그는 다음 순간 눈을 떴고, 자신이 아기 하나가 아니라 둘을 떠안고 있다는 걸 알아챘다."]], [["No wonder they laughed, for the expression of his face was droll enough to convulse a Quaker, as he stood and stared wildly from the unconscious innocents to the hilarious spectators, with such dismay that Jo sat down on the floor and screamed.", "그들이 웃은 것도 무리는 아니었다. 영문도 모르는 천진한 것들에서 깔깔대는 구경꾼들에게로 그가 눈을 휘둥그렇게 뜬 채 어찌나 낭패스러운 표정으로 번갈아 쳐다보던지, 그 표정이 퀘이커교도라도 자지러지게 할 만큼 우스꽝스러웠고, 조는 바닥에 주저앉아 비명을 질러 댔다."]], [["\"Twins, by Jupiter!\"", "\"쌍둥이라니, 맙소사!\""], ["was all he said for a minute; then, turning to the women with an appealing look that was comically piteous, he added, \"Take 'em quick, somebody!", "그가 잠시 동안 할 수 있는 말은 그게 다였다. 그러더니 우습도록 가련하게 애원하는 표정으로 여자들 쪽을 돌아보며 덧붙였다."], ["I'm going to laugh, and I shall drop 'em.\"", "\"누가 좀 얼른 받아 줘요! 웃음이 터질 것 같은데, 이러다 떨어뜨리겠어요.\""]], [["John rescued his babies, and marched up and down, with one on each arm, as if already initiated into the mysteries of baby-tending, while Laurie laughed till the tears ran down his cheeks.", "존이 자기 아기들을 구해 냈고, 한쪽 팔에 하나씩 안은 채 마치 이미 아기 돌보기의 비법을 다 터득한 사람처럼 왔다 갔다 거닐었다. 그동안 로리는 두 뺨에 눈물이 흐를 만큼 웃어 댔다."]], [["\"It's the best joke of the season, isn't it?", "\"올 들어 최고의 농담이지, 안 그래요?"], ["I wouldn't have you told, for I set my heart on surprising you, and I flatter myself I've done it,\"", "미리 알려 주고 싶지 않았어요. 깜짝 놀라게 해 줄 작정이었거든요."], ["said Jo, when she got her breath.", "제대로 해낸 것 같아 뿌듯하네요.\" 숨을 돌린 조가 말했다."]], [["\"I never was more staggered in my life.", "\"내 평생 이렇게 얼떨떨해 본 적은 처음이야."], ["Isn't it fun?", "정말 재미있지 않아요?"], ["Are they boys?", "둘 다 사내아인가요?"], ["What are you going to name them?", "이름은 뭐라고 지을 거예요?"], ["Let's have another look.", "한 번 더 보게 해 줘요."], ["Hold me up, Jo; for upon my life it's one too many for me,\"", "좀 붙잡아 줘, 조. 정말이지 나한테는 하나로도 벅차니까.\""], ["returned Laurie, regarding the infants with the air of a big, benevolent Newfoundland looking at a pair of infantile kittens.", "로리가 마치 덩치 크고 인자한 뉴펀들랜드견이 새끼 고양이 한 쌍을 바라보듯 아기들을 들여다보며 대꾸했다."]], [["\"Boy and girl. Aren't they beauties?\"", "\"사내아이 하나, 계집아이 하나야."], ["said the proud papa, beaming upon the little, red squirmers as if they were unfledged angels.", "예쁘지 않아요?\" 자랑스러운 아빠가 마치 갓 깃털도 나지 않은 천사라도 되는 양 발갛게 꼬물거리는 것들을 향해 환히 웃으며 말했다."]], [["\"Most remarkable children I ever saw.", "\"내 평생 본 아이들 중에 가장 비범한 아이들이에요."], ["Which is which?\"", "어느 쪽이 어느 쪽인가요?\""], ["and Laurie bent like a well-sweep to examine the prodigies.", "그러면서 로리는 우물의 두레박 장대처럼 허리를 숙여 그 신동들을 살펴보았다."]], [["\"Amy put a blue ribbon on the boy and a pink on the girl, French fashion, so you can always tell.", "\"에이미가 프랑스식으로 사내아이한텐 파란 리본을, 계집아이한텐 분홍 리본을 매 줬으니까 늘 구별할 수 있어요."], ["Besides, one has blue eyes and one brown.", "게다가 하나는 눈이 파랗고 하나는 갈색이고요."], ["Kiss them, Uncle Teddy,\"", "입 맞춰 줘요, 테디 삼촌.\""], ["said wicked Jo.", "짓궂은 조가 말했다."]], [["\"I'm afraid they mightn't like it,\"", "\"얘들이 싫어할까 봐 걱정인데.\""], ["began Laurie, with unusual timidity in such matters.", "로리가 그런 일에는 영 어울리지 않게 머뭇거리며 운을 뗐다."]], [["\"Of course they will; they are used to it now.", "\"당연히 좋아하지요. 이젠 익숙하니까요."], ["Do it this minute, sir!\"", "당장 해요, 어서!\" 조가 명령했다."], ["commanded Jo, fearing he might propose a proxy.", "그가 대신 시킬 사람이라도 내세울까 봐 걱정스러웠던 것이다."]], [["Laurie screwed up his face, and obeyed with a gingerly peck at each little cheek that produced another laugh, and made the babies squeal.", "로리는 얼굴을 잔뜩 찡그리고는 명령에 따라 두 뺨에 조심조심 입을 맞추었는데, 그 바람에 또 한바탕 웃음이 터졌고 아기들은 꽥꽥 울어 댔다."]], [["\"There, I knew they didn't like it!", "\"거봐요, 싫어할 줄 알았어요!"], ["That's the boy; see him kick; he hits out with his fists like a good one.", "이쪽이 사내아이네요. 발버둥 치는 것 좀 봐요."], ["Now then, young Brooke, pitch into a man of your own size, will you?\"", "주먹을 제법 잘도 휘두르네. 자, 어린 브룩, 네 덩치에 맞는 상대한테나 덤벼 보시지?\""], ["cried Laurie, delighted with a poke in the face from a tiny fist, flapping aimlessly about.", "자그마한 주먹이 허공을 아무렇게나 휘젓다가 자기 얼굴을 콕 찌르자 로리가 기뻐서 외쳤다."]], [["\"He's to be named John Laurence, and the girl Margaret, after mother and grandmother.", "\"이 애 이름은 엄마랑 할머니 이름을 따서 존 로렌스로 짓고, 계집애는 마거릿으로 지을 거예요."], ["We shall call her Daisy, so as not to have two Megs, and I suppose the mannie will be Jack, unless we find a better name,\"", "메그가 둘이 되지 않게 얘는 데이지라고 부를 거고요. 사내 녀석은 더 나은 이름을 찾지 못하면 잭이 되겠죠.\""], ["said Amy, with aunt-like interest.", "에이미가 이모다운 관심을 보이며 말했다."]], [["\"Name him Demijohn, and call him 'Demi' for short,\"", "\"데미존이라고 짓고, 줄여서 '데미'라고 불러.\""], ["said Laurie.", "로리가 말했다."]], [["\"Daisy and Demi,--just the thing!", "\"데이지랑 데미라니, 딱 좋네!"], ["I _knew_ Teddy would do it,\"", "테디가 해낼 줄 알았다니까.\""], ["cried Jo, clapping her hands.", "조가 손뼉을 치며 외쳤다."]], [["Teddy certainly had done it that time, for the babies were \"Daisy\"", "테디는 이번만큼은 정말로 해낸 셈이었다. 그 아기들은 끝까지 \"데이지\"와 \"데미\"로 불렸으니 말이다."], ["and \"Demi\" to the end of the chapter.", "방문"]], [["\"Come, Jo, it's time.\"", "\"조, 어서, 시간 됐어.\""]], [["\"For what?\"", "\"무슨 시간?\""]], [["\"You don't mean to say you have forgotten that you promised to make half a dozen calls with me to-day?\"", "\"설마 오늘 나랑 여섯 군데 방문하기로 약속한 걸 잊었다는 말은 아니겠지?\""]], [["\"I've done a good many rash and foolish things in my life, but I don't think I ever was mad enough to say I'd make six calls in one day, when a single one upsets me for a week.\"", "\"내 평생 경솔하고 어리석은 짓을 숱하게 해 왔지만, 한 군데만 다녀와도 일주일을 시달리는 내가 하루에 여섯 군데를 방문하겠다고 할 만큼 제정신이 아니었던 적은 없는 것 같은데.\""]], [["\"Yes, you did; it was a bargain between us.", "\"아니, 그랬어. 우리 둘이 약속한 거잖아."], ["I was to finish the crayon of Beth for you, and you were to go properly with me, and return our neighbors' visits.\"", "난 언니 대신 베스의 크레용 그림을 마무리해 주고, 언니는 제대로 차려입고 나랑 같이 가서 이웃들 방문 답례를 하기로.\""]], [["\"If it was fair--that was in the bond; and I stand to the letter of my bond, Shylock.", "\"날이 맑으면, 그게 약속의 조건이었어. 난 약속의 문구 그대로 지킬 거야, 샤일록 양반."], ["There is a pile of clouds in the east; it's _not_ fair, and I don't go.\"", "동쪽에 구름이 잔뜩 끼어 있잖아. 날이 안 맑으니까, 난 안 가.\""]], [["\"Now, that's shirking.", "\"그건 발뺌이야. 이렇게 화창한 날에 비 올 기미도 없고, 언니는 약속 잘 지키는 걸 자랑으로 여기잖아."], ["It's a lovely day, no prospect of rain, and you pride yourself on keeping promises; so be honorable; come and do your duty, and then be at peace for another six months.\"", "그러니 신의를 지켜. 가서 도리를 다하고 나면, 그다음 여섯 달은 마음 편히 지낼 수 있을 거 아냐.\""]], [["At that minute Jo was particularly absorbed in dressmaking; for she was mantua-maker general to the family, and took especial credit to herself because she could use a needle as well as a pen.", "그 순간 조는 옷 짓는 일에 유난히 푹 빠져 있었다. 집안의 재봉 총책임자였던 그녀는 펜만큼이나 바늘도 잘 다룬다는 것을 스스로 대견히 여겼던 것이다."], ["It was very provoking to be arrested in the act of a first trying-on, and ordered out to make calls in her best array, on a warm July day.", "처음 가봉을 해 보는 와중에 붙들려, 무더운 7월의 어느 날 가장 좋은 옷으로 차려입고 방문을 나가라는 명령을 받는 것은 여간 짜증 나는 일이 아니었다."], ["She hated calls of the formal sort, and never made any till Amy compelled her with a bargain, bribe, or promise.", "그녀는 격식 차린 방문이라면 질색이어서, 에이미가 거래나 미끼, 약속으로 억지로 끌어내지 않는 한 결코 그런 데를 가는 법이 없었다."], ["In the present instance, there was no escape; and having clashed her scissors rebelliously, while protesting that she smelt thunder, she gave in, put away her work, and taking up her hat and gloves with an air of resignation, told Amy the victim was ready.", "이번 경우에는 빠져나갈 길이 없었다. 그래서 천둥 냄새가 난다고 투덜대며 가위를 반항적으로 쩔그럭거리다가, 끝내 손을 들고는 일감을 치워 두고, 체념한 기색으로 모자와 장갑을 집어 들며 제물이 준비됐다고 에이미에게 말했다."]], [["\"Jo March, you are perverse enough to provoke a saint!", "\"조 마치, 언니는 성인군자도 화나게 할 만큼 심술궂어!"], ["You don't intend to make calls in that state, I hope,\"", "설마 그 꼴로 방문을 나갈 셈은 아니겠지.\""], ["cried Amy, surveying her with amazement.", "에이미가 어이없다는 듯 언니를 훑어보며 외쳤다."]], [["\"Why not?", "\"왜 안 돼?"], ["I'm neat and cool and comfortable; quite proper for a dusty walk on a warm day.", "난 단정하고 시원하고 편한걸. 더운 날 먼지 길을 걷기엔 딱 알맞지."], ["If people care more for my clothes than they do for me, I don't wish to see them.", "사람들이 나라는 사람보다 내 옷에 더 관심을 둔다면, 난 그런 사람들 보고 싶지도 않아. 넌 두 사람 몫으로 차려입고 마음껏 우아하게 굴면 되잖아."], ["You can dress for both, and be as elegant as you please: it pays for you to be fine; it doesn't for me, and furbelows only worry me.\"", "너야 곱게 꾸미면 본전을 뽑지만, 난 안 그래. 게다가 주름 장식은 나만 성가시게 할 뿐이라고.\""]], [["\"Oh dear!\"", "\"아이참!\""], ["sighed Amy; \"now she's in a contrary fit, and will drive me distracted before I can get her properly ready.", "에이미가 한숨지었다. \"이제 또 청개구리 심보가 도졌으니, 제대로 채비를 시키기도 전에 날 미치게 만들겠네."], ["I'm sure it's no pleasure to me to go to-day, but it's a debt we owe society, and there's no one to pay it but you and me.", "오늘 나가는 게 나라고 뭐 즐거워서 그러는 건 아니야. 하지만 이건 우리가 사교계에 진 빚이고, 그걸 갚을 사람이라곤 언니랑 나밖에 없잖아."], ["I'll do anything for you, Jo, if you'll only dress yourself nicely, and come and help me do the civil.", "뭐든 다 해 줄게, 조, 제발 곱게만 차려입고 와서 예의 차리는 거 좀 거들어 줘."], ["You can talk so well, look so aristocratic in your best things, and behave so beautifully, if you try, that I'm proud of you.", "언니는 마음만 먹으면 말도 어찌나 잘하고, 좋은 옷을 입으면 어찌나 기품 있어 보이고, 행동도 어찌나 멋스러운지, 내가 다 자랑스럽다니까."], ["I'm afraid to go alone; do come and take care of me.\"", "혼자 가긴 무서워. 제발 같이 가서 날 좀 지켜 줘.\""]], [["\"You're an artful little puss to flatter and wheedle your cross old sister in that way.", "\"심술궂은 늙은 언니를 그런 식으로 살살 구슬려 꾀어내다니, 넌 참 교활한 새끼 고양이로구나."], ["The idea of my being aristocratic and well-bred, and your being afraid to go anywhere alone! I don't know which is the most absurd.", "내가 기품 있고 예의 바른 사람이라느니, 네가 어디 혼자 가기 무섭다느니, 어느 쪽이 더 터무니없는지 모르겠다."], ["Well, I'll go if I must, and do my best.", "좋아, 꼭 가야 한다면 가서 최선을 다하지."], ["You shall be commander of the expedition, and I'll obey blindly; will that satisfy you?\"", "네가 이 원정대의 대장이 되면, 난 무조건 따를게. 그럼 됐니?\""], ["said Jo, with a sudden change from perversity to lamb-like submission.", "조가 청개구리 심보에서 어린양 같은 순종으로 돌변하며 말했다."]], [["\"You're a perfect cherub!", "\"언니는 완벽한 천사야!"], ["Now put on all your best things, and I'll tell you how to behave at each place, so that you will make a good impression.", "자, 가장 좋은 옷을 다 갖춰 입어. 그리고 가는 곳마다 어떻게 처신해야 좋은 인상을 줄지 내가 일러 줄게."], ["I want people to like you, and they would if you'd only try to be a little more agreeable.", "사람들이 언니를 좋아해 줬으면 해. 언니가 조금만 더 상냥하게 굴려고 애쓰면 다들 좋아할 거야."], ["Do your hair the pretty way, and put the pink rose in your bonnet; it's becoming, and you look too sober in your plain suit.", "머리는 예쁘게 손질하고, 보닛에는 분홍 장미를 꽂아. 그게 잘 어울려."], ["Take your light gloves and the embroidered handkerchief.", "그 밋밋한 옷차림으론 너무 칙칙해 보이거든."], ["We'll stop at Meg's, and borrow her white sunshade, and then you can have my dove-colored one.\"", "연한 빛깔 장갑이랑 수놓은 손수건도 챙기고. 메그 언니네 들러서 흰 양산을 빌리면, 언닌 내 비둘기빛 양산을 쓰면 돼.\""]], [["While Amy dressed, she issued her orders, and Jo obeyed them; not without entering her protest, however, for she sighed as she rustled into her new organdie, frowned darkly at herself as she tied her bonnet strings in an irreproachable bow, wrestled viciously with pins as she put on her collar, wrinkled up her features generally as she shook out the handkerchief, whose embroidery was as irritating to her nose as the present mission was to her feelings; and when she had squeezed her hands into tight gloves with three buttons and a tassel, as the last touch of elegance, she turned to Amy with an imbecile expression of countenance, saying meekly,--", "에이미는 채비를 하면서 이런저런 지시를 내렸고, 조는 그대로 따랐다. 물론 군말 없이는 아니었다. 새 오건디 드레스를 사각거리며 입을 때는 한숨을 쉬었고, 보닛 끈을 나무랄 데 없는 매듭으로 맬 때는 자기 자신을 향해 어둡게 눈살을 찌푸렸으며, 깃을 달 때는 핀과 사납게 씨름했고, 손수건을 털어 펼칠 때는 얼굴을 온통 잔뜩 찌푸렸다. 그 손수건의 자수는 이번 임무가 그녀의 심사를 긁는 만큼이나 그녀의 코를 간질였다. 그러고는 우아함의 마지막 손질로, 단추 세 개에 술이 달린 꽉 끼는 장갑 속에 두 손을 우겨 넣고는, 얼빠진 표정으로 에이미를 돌아보며 고분고분 말했다."]], [["\"I'm perfectly miserable; but if you consider me presentable, I die happy.\"", "\"완전히 비참한 기분이지만, 네가 보기에 봐줄 만하다면, 난 행복하게 죽을게.\""]], [["\"You are highly satisfactory; turn slowly round, and let me get a careful view.\" Jo revolved, and Amy gave a touch here and there, then fell back, with her head on one side, observing graciously, \"Yes, you'll do; your head is all I could ask, for that white bonnet _with_ the rose is quite ravishing. Hold back your shoulders, and carry your hands easily, no matter if your gloves do pinch. There's one thing you can do well, Jo, that is, wear a shawl--I can't; but it's very nice to see you, and I'm so glad Aunt March gave you that lovely one; it's simple, but handsome, and those folds over the arm are really artistic. Is the point of my mantle in the middle, and have I looped my dress evenly? I like to show my boots, for my feet _are_ pretty, though my nose isn't.\"", "\"아주 흡족해. 천천히 한 바퀴 돌아 봐. 내가 꼼꼼히 살펴보게.\" 조가 빙글 돌자, 에이미가 여기저기 손을 대 매만지더니, 한 발짝 물러서서 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃하며 흐뭇하게 평했다. \"그래, 그만하면 됐어. 머리는 더 바랄 게 없네. 장미 꽂은 그 흰 보닛은 정말이지 황홀하니까. 어깨는 쫙 펴고, 손은 편하게 놀려. 장갑이 좀 낀다고 해도 상관없어. 언니가 잘하는 게 하나 있는데, 바로 숄을 두르는 거야. 난 못 하는데, 언니가 두른 모습은 정말 보기 좋아. 마치 대고모가 그 사랑스러운 걸 언니한테 줘서 어찌나 다행인지. 수수하지만 근사하고, 팔에 드리워진 그 주름이 정말 멋스럽거든. 내 망토 끝자락이 가운데로 잘 와 있어? 드레스 자락은 고르게 걷어 올렸고? 난 부츠를 좀 내보이고 싶어. 코는 안 예뻐도 발은 예쁘니까.\""]], [["\"You are a thing of beauty and a joy forever,\"", "\"넌 영원토록 변치 않을 아름다움이요 기쁨이로다.\""], ["said Jo, looking through her hand with the air of a connoisseur at the blue feather against the gold hair.", "조가 금빛 머리카락에 어우러진 파란 깃털을 감식가라도 된 양 손으로 가린 채 들여다보며 말했다."], ["\"Am I to drag my best dress through the dust, or loop it up, please, ma'am?\"", "\"내 가장 좋은 드레스를 먼지 속에 질질 끌고 다니랍니까, 아니면 걷어 올릴까요, 마님?\""]], [["\"Hold it up when you walk, but drop it in the house; the sweeping style suits you best, and you must learn to trail your skirts gracefully.", "\"걸을 땐 들어 올리고, 집 안에선 늘어뜨려. 끌리는 차림새가 언니한텐 제일 잘 어울리니까, 치맛자락을 우아하게 끄는 법을 익혀야 해."], ["You haven't half buttoned one cuff; do it at once.", "한쪽 소맷부리 단추를 반밖에 안 채웠잖아. 당장 채워."], ["You'll never look finished if you are not careful about the little details, for they make up the pleasing whole.\"", "자잘한 부분에 신경 쓰지 않으면 절대 마무리가 깔끔해 보이지 않아. 그런 것들이 모여서 보기 좋은 전체를 이루는 거니까.\""]], [["Jo sighed, and proceeded to burst the buttons off her glove, in doing up her cuff; but at last both were ready, and sailed away, looking as \"pretty as picters,\"", "조는 한숨을 쉬며 소맷부리를 채우다가 장갑 단추를 죄다 뜯어 버리고 말았다. 하지만 마침내 둘 다 채비를 마치고 미끄러지듯 길을 나섰고, 한나가 위층 창밖으로 몸을 내밀어 그들을 지켜보며 말한 대로 \"그림처럼 예쁜\""], ["Hannah said, as she hung out of the upper window to watch them.", "모습이었다."]], [["\"Now, Jo dear, the Chesters consider themselves very elegant people, so I want you to put on your best deportment.", "\"자, 조 언니, 체스터네 사람들은 자기들이 무척 우아한 사람들이라고 여기니까, 최대한 점잖게 굴어 줘."], ["Don't make any of your abrupt remarks, or do anything odd, will you?", "불쑥불쑥 내뱉는 말도 하지 말고, 별난 짓도 하지 마, 알았지?"], ["Just be calm, cool, and quiet,--that's safe and ladylike; and you can easily do it for fifteen minutes,\"", "그냥 차분하고 침착하고 조용하게만 있어. 그게 안전하고 숙녀다운 거야."], ["said Amy, as they approached the first place, having borrowed the white parasol and been inspected by Meg, with a baby on each arm.", "십오 분쯤이야 언니도 쉽게 할 수 있잖아.\" 흰 양산을 빌려 들고, 양팔에 아기를 하나씩 안은 메그의 점검까지 받고 나서 첫 번째 집으로 다가가며 에이미가 말했다."]], [["\"Let me see.", "\"어디 보자."], ["'Calm, cool, and quiet,'--yes, I think I can promise that.", "'차분하고 침착하고 조용하게'라. 그래, 그건 약속할 수 있을 것 같아."], ["I've played the part of a prim young lady on the stage, and I'll try it off.", "무대에서 새침한 아가씨 역도 해 봤으니, 무대 밖에서도 한번 해 보지."], ["My powers are great, as you shall see; so be easy in your mind, my child.\"", "내 연기력이 대단하다는 걸 곧 알게 될 거야. 그러니 마음 푹 놓아라, 우리 아가.\""]], [["Amy looked relieved, but naughty Jo took her at her word; for, during the first call, she sat with every limb gracefully composed, every fold correctly draped, calm as a summer sea, cool as a snow-bank, and as silent as a sphinx.", "에이미는 마음이 놓이는 듯했지만, 짓궂은 조는 그 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들였다. 첫 번째 방문 내내 조는 사지를 우아하게 가지런히 모으고, 주름 하나하나를 똑바로 드리운 채, 여름 바다처럼 차분하고 눈더미처럼 냉랭하며 스핑크스처럼 말이 없었다."], ["In vain Mrs. Chester alluded to her \"charming novel,\"", "체스터 부인이 그녀의 \"멋진 소설\"을 들먹여도, 체스터 가의 아가씨들이 파티며 소풍이며 오페라며 유행 이야기를 꺼내도 헛수고였다."], ["and the Misses Chester introduced parties, picnics, the opera, and the fashions; each and all were answered by a smile, a bow, and a demure \"Yes\" or \"No,\"", "그 모든 말에 돌아오는 것이라곤 미소와 목례, 그리고 냉기를 잔뜩 머금은 새침한 \"네\"나 \"아니요\"뿐이었다."], ["with the chill on. In vain Amy telegraphed the word \"Talk,\"", "에이미가 \"말 좀 해\"라고 눈짓을 보내고, 말을 끌어내 보려 애쓰고, 몰래 발로 슬쩍슬쩍 찔러 봐도 다 헛일이었다."], ["tried to draw her out, and administered covert pokes with her foot. Jo sat as if blandly unconscious of it all, with deportment like Maud's face, \"icily regular, splendidly null.\"", "조는 그 모든 걸 천연덕스럽게 모르는 척 앉아 있었고, 그 처신은 모드의 얼굴처럼 \"차갑도록 반듯하고, 훌륭하도록 무미건조\"했다."]], [["\"What a haughty, uninteresting creature that oldest Miss March is!\"", "\"저 큰언니 마치 양은 어쩌면 저렇게 거만하고 재미없는 사람일까!\""], ["was the unfortunately audible remark of one of the ladies, as the door closed upon their guests.", "손님들 등 뒤로 문이 닫히자 한 부인이 안타깝게도 다 들리도록 한 말이었다."], ["Jo laughed noiselessly all through the hall, but Amy looked disgusted at the failure of her instructions, and very naturally laid the blame upon Jo.", "조는 복도를 지나는 내내 소리 없이 웃었지만, 에이미는 자기 지시가 실패로 돌아간 데 진저리를 치며 아주 자연스럽게 그 탓을 조에게 돌렸다."]], [["\"How could you mistake me so?", "\"어쩜 날 그렇게 오해할 수가 있어?"], ["I merely meant you to be properly dignified and composed, and you made yourself a perfect stock and stone.", "난 그저 언니가 점잖고 차분하게 처신하라는 뜻이었는데, 언니는 완전히 나무토막이나 돌덩이가 돼 버렸잖아."], ["Try to be sociable at the Lambs', gossip as other girls do, and be interested in dress and flirtations and whatever nonsense comes up.", "램네 집에선 좀 싹싹하게 굴어 봐. 다른 여자애들처럼 수다도 떨고, 옷이며 연애 이야기며 뭐든 시답잖은 화제에도 관심을 보이라고."], ["They move in the best society, are valuable persons for us to know, and I wouldn't fail to make a good impression there for anything.\"", "그 집은 최고의 사교계에서 노는 사람들이라 우리가 알아 두면 요긴한 분들이니까, 거기서만은 무슨 일이 있어도 좋은 인상을 남기고 싶어.\""]], [["\"I'll be agreeable; I'll gossip and giggle, and have horrors and raptures over any trifle you like.", "\"상냥하게 굴게. 수다도 떨고 까르르 웃고, 네가 원하는 무슨 시시한 일에든 질겁도 하고 황홀해하기도 할게."], ["I rather enjoy this, and now I'll imitate what is called 'a charming girl;'", "이건 좀 재미있는데. 자, 이제 소위 '매력적인 아가씨'라는 걸 흉내 내 볼게."], ["I can do it, for I have May Chester as a model, and I'll improve upon her.", "메이 체스터를 본으로 삼으면 되니 할 수 있어. 게다가 더 멋지게 해낼 거야."], ["See if the Lambs don't say, 'What a lively, nice creature that Jo March is!'\"", "램네 사람들이 '저 조 마치라는 아가씨, 어쩜 저렇게 발랄하고 사랑스러울까!' 하지 않나 봐.\""]], [["Amy felt anxious, as well she might, for when Jo turned freakish there was no knowing where she would stop.", "에이미는 불안해졌는데, 그럴 만도 했다. 조가 일단 변덕을 부리기 시작하면 어디까지 갈지 알 수 없었으니까."], ["Amy's face was a study when she saw her sister skim into the next drawing-room, kiss all the young ladies with effusion, beam graciously upon the young gentlemen, and join in the chat with a spirit which amazed the beholder. Amy was taken possession of by Mrs.", "언니가 다음 응접실로 사뿐히 들어가서는, 젊은 아가씨들 모두에게 호들갑스럽게 입을 맞추고, 젊은 신사들에게 환하게 미소를 지으며, 구경하던 사람이 다 놀랄 만큼 활기차게 잡담에 끼어드는 모습을 본 에이미의 표정은 가관이었다."], ["Lamb, with whom she was a favorite, and forced to hear a long account of Lucretia's last attack, while three delightful young gentlemen hovered near, waiting for a pause when they might rush in and rescue her.", "에이미는 자기를 아끼는 램 부인에게 붙들려, 루크리셔가 지난번에 또 발작을 일으킨 이야기를 길게 들어 주어야 했다. 그동안 멋진 젊은 신사 셋이 곁을 맴돌며, 그녀를 구해 낼 틈이 나기만을 기다렸다."], ["So situated, she was powerless to check Jo, who seemed possessed by a spirit of mischief, and talked away as volubly as the old lady.", "그런 처지라 에이미는 조를 막을 도리가 없었는데, 조는 마치 장난기 어린 무언가에 씐 듯 그 노부인 못지않게 거침없이 떠들어 댔다."], ["A knot of heads gathered about her, and Amy strained her ears to hear what was going on; for broken sentences filled her with alarm, round eyes and uplifted hands tormented her with curiosity, and frequent peals of laughter made her wild to share the fun.", "사람들 머리가 그녀 주위로 한 무리 모여들자, 에이미는 무슨 이야기가 오가는지 들으려고 귀를 곤두세웠다. 토막토막 끊긴 말들이 그녀를 불안하게 했고, 동그래진 눈과 치켜든 손들이 궁금증으로 그녀를 애태웠으며, 잦은 웃음보가 터질 때마다 그녀는 그 재미에 끼어들지 못해 안달이 났다."], ["One may imagine her suffering on overhearing fragments of this sort of conversation:--", "이런 식의 대화 조각들을 엿들으며 그녀가 얼마나 괴로웠을지 능히 짐작이 가리라."]], [["\"She rides splendidly,--who taught her?\"", "\"말을 정말 잘 타던데요. 누가 가르쳤어요?\""]], [["\"No one; she used to practise mounting, holding the reins, and sitting straight on an old saddle in a tree. Now she rides anything, for she doesn't know what fear is, and the stable-man lets her have horses cheap, because she trains them to carry ladies so well. She has such a passion for it, I often tell her if everything else fails she can be a horse-breaker, and get her living so.\"", "\"아무도요. 나무에 얹어 둔 헌 안장에 올라타고, 고삐를 잡고, 똑바로 앉는 연습을 하곤 했죠. 이젠 무슨 말이든 다 타요. 두려움이 뭔지를 모르거든요. 마구간지기가 말을 싸게 빌려주기까지 한답니다. 동생이 말을 길들여서 숙녀들도 잘 태우게 해 주니까요. 어찌나 좋아하는지, 제가 종종 그래요. 다른 일이 다 안 풀리면 말 조련사가 돼서 먹고살면 되겠다고요.\""]], [["At this awful speech Amy contained herself with difficulty, for the impression was being given that she was rather a fast young lady, which was her especial aversion.", "이 끔찍한 말에 에이미는 가까스로 마음을 추슬렀다. 자신이 꽤나 말괄량이 아가씨라는 인상이 심어지고 있었으니까."], ["But what could she do?", "그건 그녀가 무엇보다도 질색하는 것이었다."], ["for the old lady was in the middle of her story, and long before it was done Jo was off again, making more droll revelations, and committing still more fearful blunders.", "하지만 어쩌겠는가? 노부인은 한창 이야기를 늘어놓는 중이었고, 그 이야기가 채 끝나기도 한참 전에 조는 또다시 입을 놀려, 더 익살스러운 폭로를 하고, 더더욱 끔찍한 실수를 저지르고 있었다."]], [["\"Yes, Amy was in despair that day, for all the good beasts were gone, and of three left, one was lame, one blind, and the other so balky that you had to put dirt in his mouth before he would start.", "\"네, 에이미가 그날 절망에 빠졌었죠. 쓸 만한 짐승들은 죄다 나가 버리고, 남은 세 마리 중에 하나는 절름발이, 하나는 장님, 나머지 하나는 어찌나 고집이 센지 입에 흙을 물려야만 겨우 움직이는 녀석이었거든요."], ["Nice animal for a pleasure party, wasn't it?\"", "나들이 가기엔 참 근사한 짐승이었죠, 안 그래요?\""]], [["\"Which did she choose?\"", "\"그래서 어느 걸 골랐나요?\""], ["asked one of the laughing gentlemen, who enjoyed the subject.", "그 화제를 즐기던 웃음 띤 신사 중 하나가 물었다."]], [["\"None of them; she heard of a young horse at the farmhouse over the river, and, though a lady had never ridden him, she resolved to try, because he was handsome and spirited.", "\"하나도 안 골랐어요. 강 건너 농가에 어린 말이 한 마리 있다는 소릴 듣고는, 숙녀가 타 본 적이 없는 말인데도 한번 타 보기로 작정했죠."], ["Her struggles were really pathetic; there was no one to bring the horse to the saddle, so she took the saddle to the horse.", "잘생기고 기운이 넘쳤거든요. 동생이 어찌나 애를 썼는지 정말 눈물겨웠어요."], ["My dear creature, she actually rowed it over the river, put it on her head, and marched up to the barn to the utter amazement of the old man!\"", "말을 안장 있는 데로 데려올 사람이 없으니까, 글쎄 안장을 말한테 가져갔지 뭐예요. 세상에, 그걸 손수 배에 실어 강을 건너서는, 머리에 이고 헛간까지 걸어가는 통에 그 영감님이 입을 떡 벌리고 말았죠!\""]], [["\"Did she ride the horse?\"", "\"그래서 그 말을 탔나요?\""]], [["\"Of course she did, and had a capital time.", "\"그럼요, 탔고말고요. 아주 신나게 탔는걸요."], ["I expected to see her brought home in fragments, but she managed him perfectly, and was the life of the party.\"", "전 동생이 산산조각으로 실려 올 줄 알았는데, 어찌나 능숙하게 다루던지, 그 나들이의 활력소가 됐지 뭐예요.\""]], [["\"Well, I call that plucky!\" and young Mr.", "\"이야, 그거 참 대단한 배짱이군요!\""], ["Lamb turned an approving glance upon Amy, wondering what his mother could be saying to make the girl look so red and uncomfortable.", "젊은 램 씨가 에이미에게 흐뭇한 눈길을 던지며, 어머니가 무슨 말을 했기에 저 아가씨가 저토록 얼굴을 붉히며 불편해하는지 의아해했다."]], [["She was still redder and more uncomfortable a moment after, when a sudden turn in the conversation introduced the subject of dress. One of the young ladies asked Jo where she got the pretty drab hat she wore to the picnic; and stupid Jo, instead of mentioning the place where it was bought two years ago, must needs answer, with unnecessary frankness, \"Oh, Amy painted it; you can't buy those soft shades, so we paint ours any color we like. It's a great comfort to have an artistic sister.\"", "잠시 뒤 대화가 갑자기 옷 이야기로 흘러가자, 에이미는 더더욱 얼굴을 붉히며 더욱 불편해했다. 젊은 아가씨 하나가 조에게 소풍 때 쓴 그 예쁜 담갈색 모자를 어디서 샀느냐고 물었다. 그러자 멍청한 조는 그게 이 년 전에 산 곳을 말하는 대신, 굳이 쓸데없이 솔직하게 이렇게 대답하고 말았다. \"아, 그거 에이미가 칠한 거예요. 그렇게 은은한 색조는 사고 싶어도 못 사거든요. 그래서 우린 직접 원하는 색을 칠하죠. 예술가 동생을 둔다는 건 참 큰 위안이에요.\""]], [["\"Isn't that an original idea?\"", "\"그거 참 기발한 생각이네요!\""], ["cried Miss Lamb, who found Jo great fun.", "조가 무척 재미있다고 여긴 램 양이 외쳤다."]], [["\"That's nothing compared to some of her brilliant performances.", "\"그건 동생의 빛나는 솜씨에 비하면 아무것도 아니에요."], ["There's nothing the child can't do.", "저 애가 못 하는 게 없거든요."], ["Why, she wanted a pair of blue boots for Sallie's party, so she just painted her soiled white ones the loveliest shade of sky-blue you ever saw, and they looked exactly like satin,\"", "글쎄, 샐리네 파티에 신을 파란 부츠가 필요하니까, 더러워진 흰 부츠를 여태 본 적 없는 사랑스러운 하늘빛으로 칠해 버렸는데, 영락없이 새틴처럼 보였다니까요.\""], ["added Jo, with an air of pride in her sister's accomplishments that exasperated Amy till she felt that it would be a relief to throw her card-case at her.", "조가 동생의 재주를 자랑스러워하는 기색을 띠며 덧붙였는데, 그 바람에 에이미는 명함 갑이라도 던지면 속이 후련하겠다 싶을 만큼 약이 올랐다."]], [["\"We read a story of yours the other day, and enjoyed it very much,\"", "\"요전 날 댁이 쓰신 소설을 읽었는데, 아주 재미있게 봤어요.\""], ["observed the elder Miss Lamb, wishing to compliment the literary lady, who did not look the character just then, it must be confessed.", "손위 램 양이 그 여류 작가를 추어올리고 싶어 한마디 했다. 하기야 그 순간만큼은 작가다운 구석이라곤 도무지 보이지 않았다는 점은 인정해야겠지만."]], [["Any mention of her \"works\"", "자기 \"작품\"을 입에 올리는 건 무엇이든 조에게 안 좋은 영향을 끼쳤다."], ["always had a bad effect upon Jo, who either grew rigid and looked offended, or changed the subject with a _brusque_ remark, as now.", "조는 뻣뻣하게 굳어 기분 상한 표정을 짓거나, 아니면 지금처럼 무뚝뚝한 말로 화제를 돌려 버리곤 했다."], ["\"Sorry you could find nothing better to read.", "\"더 나은 읽을거리를 못 찾으셨다니 안됐네요."], ["I write that rubbish because it sells, and ordinary people like it.", "전 그런 쓰레기를 팔리니까 쓰는 거예요. 평범한 사람들이 좋아하거든요."], ["Are you going to New York this winter?\"", "이번 겨울에 뉴욕에 가세요?\""]], [["As Miss Lamb had \"enjoyed\"", "램 양은 그 소설을 \"재미있게\""], ["the story, this speech was not exactly grateful or complimentary.", "봤던 터라, 이 말은 딱히 고맙다거나 칭찬으로 들릴 만한 게 아니었다."], ["The minute it was made Jo saw her mistake; but, fearing to make the matter worse, suddenly remembered that it was for her to make the first move toward departure, and did so with an abruptness that left three people with half-finished sentences in their mouths.", "말을 내뱉은 순간 조는 자기 실수를 깨달았다. 하지만 일을 더 그르칠까 두려워, 문득 자리를 뜨자고 먼저 나서야 하는 사람이 자기라는 걸 떠올리고는, 어찌나 불쑥 일어섰던지 세 사람이 하던 말을 입 안에 반토막씩 문 채로 남겨 두고 말았다."]], [["\"Amy, we _must_ go.", "\"에이미, 우리 가야 해."], ["_Good_-by, dear; _do_ come and see us; we are _pining_ for a visit.", "안녕히 계세요, 여러분. 꼭 놀러 오세요."], ["I don't dare to ask _you_, Mr.", "다들 와 주시기만을 애타게 기다릴 테니까요."], ["Lamb; but if you _should_ come, I don't think I shall have the heart to send you away.\"", "램 씨, 당신을 차마 청하진 못하겠지만, 혹시라도 와 주신다면 차마 돌려보낼 엄두가 안 날 것 같아요.\""], ["Jo said this with such a droll imitation of May Chester's gushing style that Amy got out of the room as rapidly as possible, feeling a strong desire to laugh and cry at the same time.", "조가 메이 체스터의 호들갑스러운 말투를 어찌나 익살스럽게 흉내 냈던지, 에이미는 웃고 싶으면서도 울고 싶은 심정에 사로잡혀 가능한 한 잽싸게 방을 빠져나갔다."]], [["\"Didn't I do that well?\"", "\"내가 그거 잘하지 않았니?\""], ["asked Jo, with a satisfied air, as they walked away.", "조가 흐뭇한 기색으로 물으며 걸어 나갔다."]], [["\"Nothing could have been worse,\"", "\"그보다 더 못할 순 없었을걸.\""], ["was Amy's crushing reply.", "에이미의 대답이 비수처럼 꽂혔다."], ["\"What possessed you to tell those stories about my saddle, and the hats and boots, and all the rest of it?\"", "\"대체 뭐에 씌어서 내 안장 얘기며, 모자며 부츠며, 그 나머지 죄다 떠벌린 거야?\""]], [["\"Why, it's funny, and amuses people.", "\"왜, 재미있잖아. 사람들도 즐거워하고."], ["They know we are poor, so it's no use pretending that we have grooms, buy three or four hats a season, and have things as easy and fine as they do.\"", "우리가 가난한 거야 다들 아는데, 마부를 둔 척, 철마다 모자를 서너 개씩 사는 척, 자기들처럼 만사가 수월하고 호사스러운 척해 봤자 무슨 소용이야.\""]], [["\"You needn't go and tell them all our little shifts, and expose our poverty in that perfectly unnecessary way.", "\"우리 살림 꾸리는 그 온갖 군색한 수단을 굳이 죄다 일러바치고, 그렇게 쓸데없이 우리 가난을 까발릴 건 없잖아."], ["You haven't a bit of proper pride, and never will learn when to hold your tongue and when to speak,\"", "언니는 변변한 자존심이라곤 눈곱만큼도 없고, 언제 입을 다물고 언제 입을 열어야 할지 영영 못 배울 거야.\""], ["said Amy despairingly.", "에이미가 절망스럽게 말했다."]], [["Poor Jo looked abashed, and silently chafed the end of her nose with the stiff handkerchief, as if performing a penance for her misdemeanors.", "가엾은 조는 무안한 얼굴로, 마치 자기 잘못을 속죄라도 하듯 빳빳한 손수건으로 말없이 코끝을 문질렀다."]], [["\"How shall I behave here?\"", "\"여기선 어떻게 처신할까?\""], ["she asked, as they approached the third mansion.", "세 번째 저택으로 다가가며 조가 물었다."]], [["\"Just as you please; I wash my hands of you,\"", "\"맘대로 해. 난 언니한테서 손 뗐으니까.\""], ["was Amy's short answer.", "에이미가 짤막하게 대꾸했다."]], [["\"Then I'll enjoy myself.", "\"그럼 난 마음껏 즐겨야지."], ["The boys are at home, and we'll have a comfortable time.", "사내아이들이 집에 있을 테니, 우린 편하게 어울려 놀 거야."], ["Goodness knows I need a little change, for elegance has a bad effect upon my constitution,\"", "정말이지 나도 기분 전환이 좀 필요했어. 우아하게 구는 건 내 체질에 안 맞거든.\""], ["returned Jo gruffly, being disturbed by her failures to suit.", "자기가 영 맘에 안 차게 처신한 일에 심사가 뒤틀린 조가 무뚝뚝하게 대꾸했다."]], [["An enthusiastic welcome from three big boys and several pretty children speedily soothed her ruffled feelings; and, leaving Amy to entertain the hostess and Mr.", "덩치 큰 사내아이 셋과 예쁘장한 아이들 몇몇이 열렬히 반겨 주자 헝클어졌던 그녀의 마음도 금세 누그러졌다."], ["Tudor, who happened to be calling likewise, Jo devoted herself to the young folks, and found the change refreshing.", "에이미에게 안주인과, 마침 함께 방문 와 있던 튜더 씨를 상대하도록 맡기고, 조는 젊은 패거리에게 푹 빠져들었으니, 그 기분 전환이 더없이 상쾌했다."], ["She listened to college stories with deep interest, caressed pointers and poodles without a murmur, agreed heartily that \"Tom Brown was a brick,\"", "그녀는 대학 시절 이야기에 깊은 관심을 보이며 귀를 기울였고, 군말 없이 포인터며 푸들을 쓰다듬었으며, \"톰 브라운은 정말 멋진 녀석\"이라는, 칭찬치고는 점잖지 못한 표현에도 진심으로 맞장구를 쳤다."], ["regardless of the improper form of praise; and when one lad proposed a visit to his turtle-tank, she went with an alacrity which caused mamma to smile upon her, as that motherly lady settled the cap which was left in a ruinous condition by filial hugs, bear-like but affectionate, and dearer to her than the most faultless _coiffure_ from the hands of an inspired Frenchwoman.", "그러다 한 사내아이가 자기 거북이 수조를 보러 가자고 하자, 그녀는 냉큼 따라나섰고, 그 모습에 그 집 안주인은 흐뭇하게 미소 지었다. 그 인자한 부인은 자식들의 곰처럼 거칠지만 다정한 포옹에 엉망이 된 모자를 매만지면서도, 그 모자가 영감받은 프랑스 여인의 손길로 빚어낸 흠잡을 데 없는 머리 모양보다 더 소중하다고 여겼던 것이다."]], [["Leaving her sister to her own devices, Amy proceeded to enjoy herself to her heart's content.", "언니를 제멋대로 하도록 내버려 두고, 에이미는 마음껏 즐기기 시작했다. 튜더 씨의 삼촌은 생존해 있는 어느 귀족의 육촌뻘 되는 영국 부인과 결혼한 사이였고, 에이미는 그 집안 전체를 깍듯이 떠받들었다."], ["Mr.", "미국에서 나고 자랐으면서도 그녀에게는 작위에 대한 그 경외심이 깃들어 있었으니, 그것은 우리 가운데 가장 훌륭한 이들조차 떨쳐 내지 못하는 마음이요, 왕에 대한 옛 믿음을 향한 인정하기 싫은 충심이었다. 바로 몇 해 전, 노란 머리의 어린 왕족 소년이 찾아왔을 때 이 땅에서 가장 민주적인 나라마저 들끓게 만들었던 그 마음 말이다."], ["Tudor's uncle had married an English lady who was third cousin to a living lord, and Amy regarded the whole family with great respect; for, in spite of her American birth and breeding, she possessed that reverence for titles which haunts the best of us,--that unacknowledged loyalty to the early faith in kings which set the most democratic nation under the sun in a ferment at the coming of a royal yellow-haired laddie, some years ago, and which still has something to do with the love the young country bears the old, like that of a big son for an imperious little mother, who held him while she could, and let him go with a farewell scolding when he rebelled.", "그리고 그것은 지금도, 이 젊은 나라가 늙은 나라에 품는 애정과 얼마간 관련이 있는데, 그 애정이란 마치 위압적인 작은 어머니를 향한 덩치 큰 아들의 마음과도 같다. 그 어머니는 붙잡을 수 있는 한 그를 곁에 붙들어 두다가, 그가 반항하자 작별의 꾸지람과 함께 그를 떠나보냈던 것이다."], ["But even the satisfaction of talking with a distant connection of the British nobility did not render Amy forgetful of time; and when the proper number of minutes had passed, she reluctantly tore herself from this aristocratic society, and looked about for Jo, fervently hoping that her incorrigible sister would not be found in any position which should bring disgrace upon the name of March.", "하지만 영국 귀족의 먼 친척과 이야기를 나누는 그 만족감조차 에이미가 시간 가는 줄을 잊게 하지는 못했다. 정해진 분수의 시간이 흐르자, 그녀는 아쉬운 마음으로 이 귀족적인 모임에서 몸을 떼어 내고는, 그 구제 불능 언니가 마치 가문에 망신을 끼칠 만한 꼴로 있지나 않기를 간절히 바라며 조를 찾아 두리번거렸다."]], [["It might have been worse, but Amy considered it bad; for Jo sat on the grass, with an encampment of boys about her, and a dirty-footed dog reposing on the skirt of her state and festival dress, as she related one of Laurie's pranks to her admiring audience.", "더 고약할 수도 있었겠지만, 에이미가 보기엔 그것만으로도 충분히 고약했다. 조는 사내아이들에게 진을 친 듯 빙 둘러싸여 잔디밭에 앉아, 발이 더러운 개 한 마리를 자신의 나들이용 정장 치맛자락에 떡하니 눕혀 둔 채, 로리가 저지른 장난 하나를 감탄하는 청중에게 들려주고 있었다."], ["One small child was poking turtles with Amy's cherished parasol, a second was eating gingerbread over Jo's best bonnet, and a third playing ball with her gloves.", "어린아이 하나는 에이미가 애지중지하는 양산으로 거북이들을 쿡쿡 찔러 대고 있었고, 둘째 녀석은 조의 가장 좋은 보닛 위에서 생강과자를 먹고 있었으며, 셋째 녀석은 조의 장갑으로 공놀이를 하고 있었다. 하지만 다들 신나게 놀고 있었다."], ["But all were enjoying themselves; and when Jo collected her damaged property to go, her escort accompanied her, begging her to come again, \"it was such fun to hear about Laurie's larks.\"", "조가 망가진 자기 물건들을 챙겨 들고 떠나려 하자, 호위대가 그녀를 배웅하며 또 와 달라고 졸랐다. \"로리의 장난 이야기를 듣는 게 어찌나 재미있던지\"라면서."]], [["\"Capital boys, aren't they?", "\"멋진 녀석들이야, 안 그래?"], ["I feel quite young and brisk again after that,\"", "저러고 나니 다시 젊고 생기가 나는 기분인걸.\""], ["said Jo, strolling along with her hands behind her, partly from habit, partly to conceal the bespattered parasol.", "조가 두 손을 등 뒤로 돌린 채 걸으며 말했다. 반쯤은 버릇이었고, 반쯤은 흙탕이 튄 양산을 감추려는 속셈이었다."]], [["\"Why do you always avoid Mr. Tudor?\"", "\"언닌 왜 늘 튜더 씨를 피해?\""], ["asked Amy, wisely refraining from any comment upon Jo's dilapidated appearance.", "에이미가 조의 추레해진 행색에 대해서는 슬기롭게 한마디도 하지 않으면서 물었다."]], [["\"Don't like him; he puts on airs, snubs his sisters, worries his father, and doesn't speak respectfully of his mother.", "\"그 사람이 싫으니까. 거들먹거리고, 누이들을 면박 주고, 아버지를 속 썩이고, 어머니 얘긴 함부로 하잖아."], ["Laurie says he is fast, and _I_ don't consider him a desirable acquaintance; so I let him alone.\"", "로리 말로는 행실이 방탕하다던데, 난 그런 사람이랑 알고 지내고 싶지 않아. 그래서 상대 안 하는 거야.\""]], [["\"You might treat him civilly, at least.", "\"적어도 예의는 갖춰야지."], ["You gave him a cool nod; and just now you bowed and smiled in the politest way to Tommy Chamberlain, whose father keeps a grocery store.", "언닌 그 사람한테 쌀쌀맞게 목례만 하고선, 방금 전엔 토미 체임벌린한테는 더없이 깍듯하게 허리 굽혀 인사하고 미소까지 지었잖아."], ["If you had just reversed the nod and the bow, it would have been right,\"", "토미 아버진 식료품 가게나 하는데. 그 목례랑 인사를 서로 바꿔서 했다면 딱 맞았을 거야.\""], ["said Amy reprovingly.", "에이미가 나무라듯 말했다."]], [["\"No, it wouldn't,\" returned perverse Jo; \"I neither like, respect, nor admire Tudor, though his grandfather's uncle's nephew's niece _was_ third cousin to a lord. Tommy is poor and bashful and good and very clever; I think well of him, and like to show that I do, for he _is_ a gentleman in spite of the brown-paper parcels.\"", "\"천만에, 안 그래.\" 청개구리 같은 조가 대꾸했다. \"난 튜더가 싫고, 존경하지도, 우러르지도 않아. 비록 그 사람 할아버지의 삼촌의 조카의 조카딸이 어느 귀족의 육촌이었다 해도 말이야. 토미는 가난하고 숫기 없고 착하고 무척 똑똑해. 난 그 애를 좋게 보고, 그걸 보여 주는 게 좋아. 갈색 종이 꾸러미를 들고 다닐지언정 그 앤 신사니까.\""]], [["\"It's no use trying to argue with you,\"", "\"언니랑은 따져 봤자 소용이 없어.\""], ["began Amy.", "에이미가 입을 떼었다."]], [["\"Not the least, my dear,\" interrupted Jo; \"so let us look amiable, and drop a card here, as the Kings are evidently out, for which I'm deeply grateful.\"", "\"눈곱만큼도 소용없지, 우리 아가.\" 조가 가로막았다. \"그러니 우린 상냥한 얼굴이나 하고, 여긴 명함이나 한 장 두고 가자. 킹네 사람들은 보아하니 외출 중인 모양이니까. 정말 다행이다 싶어.\""]], [["The family card-case having done its duty, the girls walked on, and Jo uttered another thanksgiving on reaching the fifth house, and being told that the young ladies were engaged.", "가족 명함 갑이 제 소임을 다하자, 자매는 다시 걸음을 옮겼고, 다섯 번째 집에 이르러 그 집 아가씨들이 약속이 있다는 말을 듣자 조는 또 한 번 감사 기도를 올렸다."]], [["\"Now let us go home, and never mind Aunt March to-day.", "\"이제 집에 가자. 오늘 마치 대고모는 그냥 넘어가도 되잖아."], ["We can run down there any time, and it's really a pity to trail through the dust in our best bibs and tuckers, when we are tired and cross.\"", "거긴 아무 때나 들를 수 있고, 지치고 짜증 난 마당에 제일 좋은 옷차림으로 먼지 속을 끌고 다니긴 정말 아깝다고.\""]], [["\"Speak for yourself, if you please.", "\"그건 언니 사정이고."], ["Aunt likes to have us pay her the compliment of coming in style, and making a formal call; it's a little thing to do, but it gives her pleasure, and I don't believe it will hurt your things half so much as letting dirty dogs and clumping boys spoil them.", "대고모는 우리가 격식을 차려 와서 정식으로 문안 인사를 올리는 걸 좋아하셔. 별것 아닌 일이지만 그분을 기쁘게 해 드리는 일이고, 더러운 개들이며 우당탕대는 사내아이들이 언니 옷을 망치게 내버려 두는 것에 비하면 옷이 절반도 안 상할 거야."], ["Stoop down, and let me take the crumbs off of your bonnet.\"", "허리 좀 숙여 봐. 언니 보닛에 묻은 부스러기 좀 떼어 줄게.\""]], [["\"What a good girl you are, Amy!\"", "\"넌 참 착한 아이야, 에이미!\""], ["said Jo, with a repentant glance from her own damaged costume to that of her sister, which was fresh and spotless still.", "조가 망가진 자기 차림새와, 여전히 산뜻하고 티 한 점 없는 동생의 차림새를 견주어 보며 뉘우치는 눈길로 말했다."], ["\"I wish it was as easy for me to do little things to please people as it is for you.", "\"나도 너처럼 사람들 기쁘게 하는 자잘한 일을 손쉽게 할 수 있으면 좋겠어. 생각이야 하지만, 그걸 하는 데 너무 품이 들어서 말이야."], ["I think of them, but it takes too much time to do them; so I wait for a chance to confer a great favor, and let the small ones slip; but they tell best in the end, I fancy.\"", "그래서 큰 호의를 베풀 기회가 오기를 기다리느라 작은 것들은 흘려보내곤 하지. 하지만 결국엔 그 작은 것들이 제일 빛을 발하는 것 같아.\""]], [["Amy smiled, and was mollified at once, saying with a maternal air,--", "에이미는 미소를 지으며 금세 누그러져, 어머니 같은 태도로 말했다."]], [["\"Women should learn to be agreeable, particularly poor ones; for they have no other way of repaying the kindnesses they receive.", "\"여자라면, 특히 가난한 여자라면 상냥해지는 법을 익혀야 해. 받은 친절을 갚을 길이 그것밖에 없으니까."], ["If you'd remember that, and practise it, you'd be better liked than I am, because there is more of you.\"", "언니가 그걸 명심하고 몸에 익힌다면, 나보다 더 사랑받을 거야. 언닌 속이 더 깊으니까.\""]], [["\"I'm a crotchety old thing, and always shall be, but I'm willing to own that you are right; only it's easier for me to risk my life for a person than to be pleasant to him when I don't feel like it.", "\"난 별난 늙은이고, 앞으로도 늘 그럴 테지만, 네 말이 옳다는 건 기꺼이 인정해. 다만 난 누구한테 마음이 안 내킬 때 상냥하게 구는 것보다, 차라리 그 사람을 위해 목숨을 거는 게 더 쉬워."], ["It's a great misfortune to have such strong likes and dislikes, isn't it?\"", "호불호가 이렇게 분명하다는 건 참 큰 불행이야, 그렇지?\""]], [["\"It's a greater not to be able to hide them. I don't mind saying that I don't approve of Tudor any more than you do; but I'm not called upon to tell him so; neither are you, and there is no use in making yourself disagreeable because he is.\"", "\"그걸 감추지 못하는 게 더 큰 불행이지. 나도 언니 못지않게 튜더가 마뜩잖다는 거 숨길 생각 없어. 하지만 굳이 그 사람한테 그렇다고 말할 까닭은 없잖아. 언니도 그렇고. 그 사람이 마음에 안 든다고 굳이 언니가 까칠하게 굴어 봤자 아무 소용 없어.\""]], [["\"But I think girls ought to show when they disapprove of young men; and how can they do it except by their manners? Preaching does not do any good, as I know to my sorrow, since I've had Teddy to manage; but there are many little ways in which I can influence him without a word, and I say we _ought_ to do it to others if we can.\"", "\"하지만 난 여자들이 젊은 남자를 못마땅해할 땐 그걸 드러내야 한다고 봐. 처신 말고 무슨 수로 그걸 보여 주겠어? 설교는 아무 소용이 없어. 테디를 다뤄 보고 뼈저리게 깨달았지. 하지만 말 한마디 없이도 그 애한테 영향을 줄 자잘한 방법이 얼마든지 있어. 그러니 우리도 할 수만 있다면 다른 사람들한테도 그래야 한다는 거야.\""]], [["\"Teddy is a remarkable boy, and can't be taken as a sample of other boys,\"", "\"테디는 유별난 애라 다른 남자들의 본보기로 삼을 순 없어.\" 에이미가 자못 단호한 확신의 어조로 말했는데, 그 \"유별난 애\"가 이 말을 들었더라면 자지러졌을 것이다."], ["said Amy, in a tone of solemn conviction, which would have convulsed the \"remarkable boy,\" if he had heard it.", "\"우리가 미녀이거나 재산과 지위를 갖춘 여자라면 어쩌면 뭔가 할 수 있을지도 몰라."], ["\"If we were belles, or women of wealth and position, we might do something, perhaps; but for us to frown at one set of young gentlemen because we don't approve of them, and smile upon another set because we do, wouldn't have a particle of effect, and we should only be considered odd and puritanical.\"", "하지만 우리 같은 처지에 어떤 젊은 신사들이 마음에 안 든다고 인상을 쓰고, 어떤 이들은 마음에 든다고 미소를 보낸다 한들 눈곱만큼도 효과가 없을걸. 그저 별나고 청교도처럼 깐깐하다는 소리나 듣겠지.\""]], [["\"So we are to countenance things and people which we detest, merely because we are not belles and millionaires, are we?", "\"그럼 우린 미녀도 백만장자도 아니라는 이유만으로, 우리가 질색하는 일이며 사람들을 그저 두둔해 줘야 한다 이거지?"], ["That's a nice sort of morality.\"", "그것참 훌륭한 도덕이로구나.\""]], [["\"I can't argue about it, I only know that it's the way of the world; and people who set themselves against it only get laughed at for their pains.", "\"난 그거에 대해선 따질 줄 모르겠고, 다만 그게 세상 이치라는 것만은 알아. 거기에 맞서는 사람들은 애만 쓰고 비웃음이나 사는 거야."], ["I don't like reformers, and I hope you will never try to be one.\"", "난 개혁가들이 싫어. 언니도 부디 그런 사람이 되려고 하지 않았으면 좋겠어.\""]], [["\"I do like them, and I shall be one if I can; for in spite of the laughing, the world would never get on without them. We can't agree about that, for you belong to the old set, and I to the new: you will get on the best, but I shall have the liveliest time of it. I should rather enjoy the brickbats and hooting, I think.\"", "\"난 그 사람들이 좋아. 그리고 할 수만 있다면 나도 그런 사람이 될 거야. 비웃음을 사긴 해도, 그런 사람들이 없으면 세상은 결코 나아가지 못할 테니까. 이건 우리 둘이 영영 합의 못 볼 문제야. 넌 낡은 패에 속하고, 난 새 패에 속하니까. 넌 잘 살아 나가겠지만, 난 누구보다 활기찬 삶을 살 거야. 돌팔매질이나 야유쯤은 차라리 즐길 수 있을 것 같아.\""]], [["\"Well, compose yourself now, and don't worry aunt with your new ideas.\"", "\"자, 이제 그만 마음 좀 가라앉히고, 대고모 앞에서 그 새로운 생각으로 속 썩이지 마.\""]], [["\"I'll try not to, but I'm always possessed to burst out with some particularly blunt speech or revolutionary sentiment before her; it's my doom, and I can't help it.\"", "\"안 그러려고 애써 보겠지만, 난 늘 대고모 앞에만 가면 유난히 무뚝뚝한 말이나 혁명 같은 소리를 불쑥 내뱉고 싶어 안달이 나. 그게 내 팔자인걸, 어쩔 수가 없어.\""]], [["They found Aunt Carrol with the old lady, both absorbed in some very interesting subject; but they dropped it as the girls came in, with a conscious look which betrayed that they had been talking about their nieces.", "가 보니 캐럴 고모가 노부인과 함께 있었는데, 둘 다 무척 흥미로운 어떤 화제에 푹 빠져 있었다. 하지만 자매가 들어서자 그들은 그 이야기를 뚝 그쳤고, 자기네 조카딸들 얘기를 하고 있었음을 들켜 버린 멋쩍은 기색이 역력했다."], ["Jo was not in a good humor, and the perverse fit returned; but Amy, who had virtuously done her duty, kept her temper, and pleased everybody, was in a most angelic frame of mind.", "조는 기분이 좋지 않았던 데다 그 청개구리 심보가 다시 도졌다. 하지만 갸륵하게도 제 도리를 다하고 성미를 잘 다스려 모두를 흐뭇하게 한 에이미는 더없이 천사 같은 마음 상태였다."], ["This amiable spirit was felt at once, and both the aunts \"my deared\"", "이 사랑스러운 기색은 대번에 전해져, 두 고모할머니는 그녀를 다정하게 \"아가, 아가\""], ["her affectionately, looking what they afterwards said emphatically,--\"That child improves every day.\"", "하고 불렀고, 나중에 힘주어 말한 대로 이런 눈빛을 보냈다. \"저 애는 날마다 더 나아진다니까.\""]], [["\"Are you going to help about the fair, dear?\" asked Mrs.", "\"바자회는 거들 거니, 얘야?\""], ["Carrol, as Amy sat down beside her with the confiding air elderly people like so well in the young.", "에이미가 곁에 앉자 캐럴 부인이 물었다. 어른들이 젊은이에게서 그토록 좋아하는, 그 미덥게 따르는 태도를 보이며 앉은 에이미에게."]], [["\"Yes, aunt. Mrs.", "\"네, 고모할머니."], ["Chester asked me if I would, and I offered to tend a table, as I have nothing but my time to give.\"", "체스터 부인이 도와주겠느냐고 물으셔서, 제가 가판대 하나를 맡겠다고 했어요. 저야 내드릴 거라곤 시간밖에 없으니까요.\""]], [["\"I'm not,\"", "\"난 안 해요.\""], ["put in Jo decidedly.", "조가 단호하게 끼어들었다."], ["\"I hate to be patronized, and the Chesters think it's a great favor to allow us to help with their highly connected fair.", "\"전 누가 베푸는 척하는 게 질색이거든요. 게다가 체스터네 사람들은 자기네 그 으리으리한 바자회 일을 우리한테 거들게 해 주는 걸 무슨 큰 은혜라도 베푸는 줄 알아요."], ["I wonder you consented, Amy: they only want you to work.\"", "에이미가 어떻게 승낙했는지 모르겠네요. 그 사람들은 그저 너를 부려 먹고 싶은 것뿐이야.\""]], [["\"I am willing to work: it's for the freedmen as well as the Chesters, and I think it very kind of them to let me share the labor and the fun.", "\"난 기꺼이 일하겠어. 그건 체스터네 사람들만이 아니라 해방 노예들을 위한 일이기도 하고, 내가 그 수고와 즐거움을 함께 나누게 해 주는 게 난 무척 고마운 일이라고 봐."], ["Patronage does not trouble me when it is well meant.\"", "좋은 뜻에서 베푸는 거라면 난 그런 호의가 거슬리지 않거든.\""]], [["\"Quite right and proper.", "\"아주 옳고 마땅한 말이다."], ["I like your grateful spirit, my dear; it's a pleasure to help people who appreciate our efforts: some do not, and that is trying,\"", "난 네 그 고마워하는 마음씨가 좋구나, 얘야. 우리 정성을 알아주는 사람들을 돕는 건 즐거운 일이지."], ["observed Aunt March, looking over her spectacles at Jo, who sat apart, rocking herself, with a somewhat morose expression.", "더러 그러지 않는 이들도 있는데, 그게 참 사람을 힘 빠지게 하거든.\" 마치 대고모가 안경 너머로, 다소 시무룩한 표정으로 한쪽에 떨어져 몸을 흔들고 있는 조를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["If Jo had only known what a great happiness was wavering in the balance for one of them, she would have turned dovelike in a minute; but, unfortunately, we don't have windows in our breasts, and cannot see what goes on in the minds of our friends; better for us that we cannot as a general thing, but now and then it would be such a comfort, such a saving of time and temper.", "만약 둘 중 하나에게 얼마나 큰 행복이 저울 위에서 위태롭게 흔들리고 있는지를 조가 알기만 했더라도, 그녀는 당장 비둘기처럼 온순해졌으련만. 안타깝게도 우리 가슴에는 창문이 달려 있지 않아서, 친구의 마음속에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 들여다볼 수가 없다."], ["By her next speech, Jo deprived herself of several years of pleasure, and received a timely lesson in the art of holding her tongue.", "대개는 그러는 편이 우리에게 이롭지만, 가끔은 그것이 얼마나 위안이 될지, 또 얼마나 시간과 감정을 아껴 줄지 모른다. 조는 다음에 내뱉은 말 한마디로 몇 해에 걸친 즐거움을 스스로 날려 버렸고, 입을 다무는 법에 관한 때맞은 교훈을 얻었다."]], [["\"I don't like favors; they oppress and make me feel like a slave.", "\"전 호의를 받는 게 싫어요. 부담스럽기도 하고, 꼭 노예가 된 기분이 들거든요."], ["I'd rather do everything for myself, and be perfectly independent.\"", "차라리 뭐든 제 손으로 하고, 완전히 독립적으로 사는 게 나아요.\""]], [["\"Ahem!\"", "\"에헴!\""], ["coughed Aunt Carrol softly, with a look at Aunt March.", "캐럴 고모할머니가 마치 고모할머니를 흘낏 쳐다보며 나직이 헛기침을 했다."]], [["\"I told you so,\"", "\"내가 그랬잖니.\""], ["said Aunt March, with a decided nod to Aunt Carrol.", "마치 고모할머니가 캐럴 고모할머니에게 단호히 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["Mercifully unconscious of what she had done, Jo sat with her nose in the air, and a revolutionary aspect which was anything but inviting.", "자신이 무슨 짓을 저질렀는지 다행히도 까맣게 모른 채, 조는 코를 치켜들고 앉아 있었는데, 그 반항적인 표정이라곤 도무지 호감을 살 만한 것이 못 되었다."]], [["\"Do you speak French, dear?\"", "\"얘야, 너는 프랑스어를 할 줄 아니?\""], ["asked Mrs. Carrol, laying her hand on Amy's.", "캐럴 부인이 에이미의 손 위에 자기 손을 얹으며 물었다."]], [["\"Pretty well, thanks to Aunt March, who lets Esther talk to me as often as I like,\"", "\"꽤 잘해요. 다 마치 고모할머니 덕분이죠."], ["replied Amy, with a grateful look, which caused the old lady to smile affably.", "제가 원할 때마다 에스더하고 얘기하게 해 주셨거든요.\" 에이미가 감사 어린 눈빛으로 대답하자, 노부인은 흐뭇하게 미소 지었다."]], [["\"How are you about languages?\"", "\"너는 외국어에 어떠냐?\""], ["asked Mrs. Carrol of Jo.", "캐럴 부인이 조에게 물었다."]], [["\"Don't know a word; I'm very stupid about studying anything; can't bear French, it's such a slippery, silly sort of language,\" was the _brusque_ reply.", "\"한마디도 못해요. 전 뭘 공부하는 데는 영 머리가 나빠서요. 프랑스어라면 질색이에요. 그렇게 미끈거리고 어리석은 언어가 또 없거든요.\" 퉁명스러운 대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["Another look passed between the ladies, and Aunt March said to Amy, \"You are quite strong and well, now, dear, I believe?", "두 부인 사이에 또 한 차례 눈길이 오갔고, 마치 고모할머니가 에이미에게 말했다. \"얘야, 이제 너는 아주 튼튼하고 건강하지, 아마?"], ["Eyes don't trouble you any more, do they?\"", "눈도 더는 말썽을 부리지 않고, 그렇지?\""]], [["\"Not at all, thank you, ma'am.", "\"전혀요, 고맙습니다, 고모할머니. 아주 건강해요."], ["I'm very well, and mean to do great things next winter, so that I may be ready for Rome, whenever that joyful time arrives.\"", "그래서 다음 겨울에는 큰 공부를 할 작정이에요. 그 즐거운 날이 언제 오든 로마에 갈 채비를 해 두려고요.\""]], [["\"Good girl!", "\"착하기도 하지!"], ["You deserve to go, and I'm sure you will some day,\"", "갈 자격이 있고말고. 언젠가 꼭 가게 될 거란다.\""], ["said Aunt March, with an approving pat on the head, as Amy picked up her ball for her.", "에이미가 떨어진 털실 뭉치를 주워 주자, 마치 고모할머니가 머리를 쓰다듬으며 흐뭇하게 말했다."]], [["\"Cross-patch, draw the latch, Sit by the fire and spin,\"", "\"심술쟁이야, 빗장을 걸고 난롯가에 앉아 실을 잣거라.\""]], [["squalled Polly, bending down from his perch on the back of her chair to peep into Jo's face, with such a comical air of impertinent inquiry that it was impossible to help laughing.", "폴리가 의자 등받이 위 횃대에서 몸을 숙여 조의 얼굴을 들여다보며 꽥꽥거렸는데, 그 무례하게 캐묻는 듯한 우스꽝스러운 태도가 어찌나 익살맞은지 웃지 않고는 배길 수가 없었다."]], [["\"Most observing bird,\"", "\"여간 눈썰미 있는 새가 아니구나.\""], ["said the old lady.", "노부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Come and take a walk, my dear?\"", "\"같이 산책하러 갈까, 얘야?\""], ["cried Polly, hopping toward the china-closet, with a look suggestive of lump-sugar.", "폴리가 각설탕을 떠올리게 하는 표정으로 도자기장 쪽으로 폴짝폴짝 뛰어가며 소리쳤다."]], [["\"Thank you, I will.", "\"고마워, 그러자."], ["Come, Amy;\"", "가자, 에이미.\""], ["and Jo brought the visit to an end, feeling more strongly than ever that calls did have a bad effect upon her constitution.", "조는 이렇게 방문을 끝맺으면서, 남의 집을 찾아가는 일이 자기 체질에는 영 안 맞는다는 사실을 그 어느 때보다 강하게 느꼈다."], ["She shook hands in a gentlemanly manner, but Amy kissed both the aunts, and the girls departed, leaving behind them the impression of shadow and sunshine; which impression caused Aunt March to say, as they vanished,--", "조는 사내답게 악수를 했지만, 에이미는 두 고모할머니에게 입을 맞추었다. 두 소녀가 떠난 자리에는 그늘과 햇살 같은 인상이 남았고, 그 인상 때문에 둘이 사라지자 마치 고모할머니가 이렇게 말했다."]], [["\"You'd better do it, Mary; I'll supply the money,\"", "\"그렇게 하는 게 좋겠다, 메리. 돈은 내가 대마.\""], ["and Aunt Carrol to reply decidedly, \"I certainly will, if her father and mother consent.\"", "그러자 캐럴 고모할머니가 단호하게 대답했다. \"애 부모만 동의한다면야 당연히 그래야지요.\""]], [["Mrs. Chester's fair was so very elegant and select that it was considered a great honor by the young ladies of the neighborhood to be invited to take a table, and every one was much interested in the matter.", "체스터 부인의 자선 바자회는 어찌나 우아하고 격조 높은 행사인지, 동네 젊은 아가씨들 사이에서는 판매대를 하나 맡아 달라는 초대를 받는 것이 대단한 영광으로 여겨졌고, 다들 그 일에 큰 관심을 쏟았다."], ["Amy was asked, but Jo was not, which was fortunate for all parties, as her elbows were decidedly akimbo at this period of her life, and it took a good many hard knocks to teach her how to get on easily.", "에이미는 초대를 받았지만 조는 받지 못했는데, 이는 모두에게 다행스러운 일이었다. 이 무렵 조는 양 팔꿈치를 잔뜩 치켜든 채로 살았고, 세상을 무던하게 헤쳐 나가는 법을 깨우치기까지 호된 부딪침을 숱하게 겪어야 했기 때문이다."], ["The \"haughty, uninteresting creature\" was let severely alone; but Amy's talent and taste were duly complimented by the offer of the art-table, and she exerted herself to prepare and secure appropriate and valuable contributions to it.", "\"거만하고 재미라곤 없는 인간\"은 매정하게 따돌림을 당했지만, 에이미의 재능과 안목은 미술 판매대를 맡아 달라는 제안으로 마땅한 인정을 받았고, 에이미는 그 판매대에 어울리는 값진 출품작을 준비하고 확보하느라 온 힘을 쏟았다."]], [["Everything went on smoothly till the day before the fair opened; then there occurred one of the little skirmishes which it is almost impossible to avoid, when some five and twenty women, old and young, with all their private piques and prejudices, try to work together.", "바자회가 열리기 전날까지는 모든 일이 순조롭게 흘러갔다. 그러다 작은 다툼 하나가 벌어졌는데, 노소를 막론한 스물다섯 명 남짓한 여자들이 저마다의 사사로운 앙심과 편견을 안고 함께 일하려 들 때면 거의 피할 수 없는 종류의 충돌이었다."], ["May Chester was rather jealous of Amy because the latter was a greater favorite than herself, and, just at this time, several trifling circumstances occurred to increase the feeling.", "메이 체스터는 에이미를 다소 질투했다. 에이미가 자기보다 더 많은 사랑을 받았기 때문인데, 마침 이 무렵 그 감정을 부추기는 사소한 일이 몇 가지 겹쳐 일어났다."], ["Amy's dainty pen-and-ink work entirely eclipsed May's painted vases,--that was one thorn; then the all-conquering Tudor had danced four times with Amy, at a late party, and only once with May,--that was thorn number two; but the chief grievance that rankled in her soul, and gave her an excuse for her unfriendly conduct, was a rumor which some obliging gossip had whispered to her, that the March girls had made fun of her at the Lambs'.", "에이미의 정교한 펜화가 메이가 그린 꽃병 그림을 완전히 무색하게 만든 것이 첫 번째 가시였다. 그다음으로 모든 이를 사로잡는 튜더가 얼마 전 어느 파티에서 에이미와는 네 번 춤을 추고 메이와는 단 한 번밖에 추지 않은 것이 두 번째 가시였다."], ["All the blame of this should have fallen upon Jo, for her naughty imitation had been too lifelike to escape detection, and the frolicsome Lambs had permitted the joke to escape.", "그러나 그 영혼 속에서 가장 사무치게 곪아 들며 비우호적인 행동의 빌미가 되어 준 가장 큰 불만은, 어떤 친절한 수다쟁이가 귀띔해 준 소문이었다. 즉, 마치 집안 딸들이 램네 집에서 자기를 흉봤다는 것이었다."], ["No hint of this had reached the culprits, however, and Amy's dismay can be imagined, when, the very evening before the fair, as she was putting the last touches to her pretty table, Mrs.", "이 일의 책임은 마땅히 조에게 돌아갔어야 했다. 조의 짓궂은 흉내가 너무도 실감 나서 들키지 않을 수가 없었던 데다, 장난기 많은 램네 사람들이 그 농담이 새어 나가도록 내버려 두었기 때문이다."], ["Chester, who, of course, resented the supposed ridicule of her daughter, said, in a bland tone, but with a cold look,--", "그러나 정작 죄를 지은 당사자들에게는 이런 낌새가 조금도 전해지지 않았으니, 바자회 바로 전날 저녁, 에이미가 자신의 예쁜 판매대에 마지막 손질을 하고 있을 때 자기 딸이 비웃음을 당했다고 여겨 당연히 분개한 체스터 부인이 부드러운 말투에 차가운 눈빛으로 이렇게 말했을 때, 에이미가 얼마나 당혹스러웠을지는 충분히 짐작할 수 있을 것이다."]], [["\"I find, dear, that there is some feeling among the young ladies about my giving this table to any one but my girls.", "\"얘야, 내가 알아보니 이 판매대를 우리 애들이 아닌 다른 사람에게 맡긴 것을 두고 젊은 아가씨들 사이에 말이 좀 있더구나."], ["As this is the most prominent, and some say the most attractive table of all, and they are the chief getters-up of the fair, it is thought best for them to take this place.", "이게 가장 눈에 띄는 데다, 어떤 이들은 모든 판매대 중에서 제일 매력적이라고도 하고, 또 우리 애들이 이 바자회를 주도해서 꾸린 만큼, 그 애들이 이 자리를 맡는 편이 가장 좋다는 의견이거든. 미안하구나."], ["I'm sorry, but I know you are too sincerely interested in the cause to mind a little personal disappointment, and you shall have another table if you like.\"", "하지만 너는 진심으로 이 일에 마음을 쏟고 있으니 사소한 개인적 실망쯤은 개의치 않으리라 믿는다. 원한다면 다른 판매대를 하나 줄게.\""]], [["Mrs.", "체스터 부인은 이 짧은 말을 늘어놓기가 쉬울 거라고 미리 짐작했었다."], ["Chester had fancied beforehand that it would be easy to deliver this little speech; but when the time came, she found it rather difficult to utter it naturally, with Amy's unsuspicious eyes looking straight at her, full of surprise and trouble.", "그러나 막상 그 순간이 닥치자, 놀라움과 곤혹스러움이 가득한 에이미의 의심 없는 두 눈이 자기를 똑바로 바라보고 있으니, 그 말을 자연스럽게 내뱉기가 여간 어려운 것이 아니었다."]], [["Amy felt that there was something behind this, but could not guess what, and said quietly, feeling hurt, and showing that she did,--", "에이미는 이 말 뒤에 뭔가가 숨어 있다는 것을 느꼈지만 그것이 무엇인지는 짐작하지 못했고, 상처받은 기색을 그대로 드러내며 조용히 말했다."]], [["\"Perhaps you had rather I took no table at all?\"", "\"그럼 차라리 제가 판매대를 아예 안 맡는 편이 나으시겠어요?\""]], [["\"Now, my dear, don't have any ill feeling, I beg; it's merely a matter of expediency, you see; my girls will naturally take the lead, and this table is considered their proper place. _I_ think it very appropriate to you, and feel very grateful for your efforts to make it so pretty; but we must give up our private wishes, of course, and I will see that you have a good place elsewhere. Wouldn't you like the flower-table? The little girls undertook it, but they are discouraged. You could make a charming thing of it, and the flower-table is always attractive, you know.\"", "\"얘야, 제발 나쁘게 받아들이지 말아 다오. 그저 편의를 따진 것뿐이란다, 알겠니? 우리 애들이 당연히 앞장을 서야 하고, 이 판매대는 그 애들에게 어울리는 자리로 여겨지니까. 나는 너에게 아주 잘 맞는다고 생각한단다. 이렇게 예쁘게 꾸미려고 애써 줘서 정말 고맙고. 하지만 우리 모두 사사로운 바람은 접어야 하지 않겠니. 다른 좋은 자리를 꼭 마련해 주마. 꽃 판매대는 어떻겠니? 어린 여자애들이 맡았는데, 그 애들이 기가 좀 꺾여 있단다. 너라면 아주 멋지게 꾸밀 수 있을 거야. 알다시피 꽃 판매대는 언제나 인기가 많잖니.\""]], [["\"Especially to gentlemen,\"", "\"특히 신사분들한테요.\""], ["added May, with a look which enlightened Amy as to one cause of her sudden fall from favor.", "메이가 한마디 덧붙였는데, 그 눈빛이 에이미에게 자신이 갑작스레 총애를 잃게 된 한 가지 까닭을 알려 주었다."], ["She colored angrily, but took no other notice of that girlish sarcasm, and answered, with unexpected amiability,--", "에이미는 화가 나서 얼굴이 붉어졌지만, 그 어린애 같은 빈정거림에는 더는 아랑곳하지 않고 뜻밖의 상냥함으로 대답했다."]], [["\"It shall be as you please, Mrs. Chester.", "\"부인 뜻대로 하세요, 체스터 부인."], ["I'll give up my place here at once, and attend to the flowers, if you like.\"", "원하신다면 지금 당장 이 자리를 내드리고 꽃을 맡을게요.\""]], [["\"You can put your own things on your own table, if you prefer,\"", "\"네 물건은 네 판매대에 그대로 놓아도 돼. 그러고 싶다면 말이야.\""], ["began May, feeling a little conscience-stricken, as she looked at the pretty racks, the painted shells, and quaint illuminations Amy had so carefully made and so gracefully arranged.", "메이가 운을 떼었는데, 에이미가 그토록 정성스레 만들어 우아하게 배치해 둔 예쁜 받침대며 채색한 조개껍데기며 고풍스러운 채식화를 바라보고 있자니 양심에 조금 찔리는 모양이었다."], ["She meant it kindly, but Amy mistook her meaning, and said quickly,--", "좋은 뜻으로 한 말이었으나, 에이미는 그 뜻을 오해하고 얼른 대꾸했다."]], [["\"Oh, certainly, if they are in your way;\"", "\"아, 물론이죠, 거치적거리신다면요.\""], ["and sweeping her contributions into her apron, pell-mell, she walked off, feeling that herself and her works of art had been insulted past forgiveness.", "그러고는 자기 출품작을 앞치마에 마구잡이로 쓸어 담더니, 자기 자신과 자기 예술 작품이 도저히 용서 못 할 만큼 모욕당했다고 느끼며 걸어 나갔다."]], [["\"Now she's mad.", "\"이제 쟤 단단히 화났네."], ["Oh, dear, I wish I hadn't asked you to speak, mamma,\"", "아이참, 엄마한테 말해 달라고 부탁하지 말걸 그랬어.\""], ["said May, looking disconsolately at the empty spaces on her table.", "메이가 자기 판매대에 휑하니 빈자리를 쓸쓸하게 바라보며 말했다."]], [["\"Girls' quarrels are soon over,\"", "\"여자애들 싸움은 금세 가라앉는 법이란다.\""], ["returned her mother, feeling a trifle ashamed of her own part in this one, as well she might.", "어머니가 대답했지만, 자신도 이 다툼에 한몫했다는 사실에 조금은 부끄러움을 느꼈는데, 그럴 만도 했다."]], [["The little girls hailed Amy and her treasures with delight, which cordial reception somewhat soothed her perturbed spirit, and she fell to work, determined to succeed florally, if she could not artistically.", "어린 여자애들은 에이미와 그 보물 같은 물건들을 반갑게 맞이했고, 그 따뜻한 환대 덕분에 에이미의 뒤숭숭했던 마음이 다소 가라앉았다. 에이미는 예술적으로는 못 해도 꽃으로는 기어이 성공하리라 다짐하며 일에 매달렸다."], ["But everything seemed against her: it was late, and she was tired; every one was too busy with their own affairs to help her; and the little girls were only hindrances, for the dears fussed and chattered like so many magpies, making a great deal of confusion in their artless efforts to preserve the most perfect order.", "그러나 모든 것이 그녀를 가로막는 듯했다. 시간은 늦었고, 그녀는 지쳐 있었으며, 다들 제 일에 너무 바빠 도와줄 사람이 없었고, 어린 여자애들은 그저 방해만 될 뿐이었다."], ["The evergreen arch wouldn't stay firm after she got it up, but wiggled and threatened to tumble down on her head when the hanging baskets were filled; her best tile got a splash of water, which left a sepia tear on the Cupid's cheek; she bruised her hands with hammering, and got cold working in a draught, which last affliction filled her with apprehensions for the morrow.", "그 귀여운 것들이 까치 떼처럼 부산을 떨고 재잘대면서, 완벽한 질서를 지키겠다는 천진한 노력으로 오히려 큰 혼란만 일으켰던 것이다. 상록수 아치는 세워 놓고 나니 좀처럼 단단히 서 있질 못하고 흔들거렸고, 매다는 바구니에 꽃을 채우자 그녀 머리 위로 와르르 무너질 듯했다."], ["Any girl-reader who has suffered like afflictions will sympathize with poor Amy, and wish her well through with her task.", "그녀가 가장 아끼는 타일에는 물이 튀어 큐피드의 뺨에 갈색 눈물 자국이 남았고, 망치질을 하다 손에 멍이 들었으며, 외풍 속에서 일하다 감기에 걸렸는데, 마지막 이 시련은 그녀에게 이튿날에 대한 걱정을 한가득 안겨 주었다. 이와 같은 고초를 겪어 본 여자 독자라면 가엾은 에이미에게 공감하며, 그녀가 그 일을 무사히 끝마치기를 빌어 줄 것이다."]], [["There was great indignation at home when she told her story that evening.", "그날 저녁 에이미가 자초지종을 털어놓자 집 안에는 큰 분노가 일었다."], ["Her mother said it was a shame, but told her she had done right; Beth declared she wouldn't go to the fair at all; and Jo demanded why she didn't take all her pretty things and leave those mean people to get on without her.", "어머니는 너무하다고 하면서도, 에이미가 옳게 처신했다고 말해 주었다. 베스는 자기는 바자회에 아예 가지 않겠다고 잘라 말했고, 조는 어째서 예쁜 물건들을 죄다 챙겨 와서 그 못된 사람들끼리 알아서 하게 내버려 두지 않았느냐고 따져 물었다."]], [["\"Because they are mean is no reason why I should be.", "\"그 사람들이 못됐다고 해서 나까지 못되게 굴어야 할 이유는 없잖아."], ["I hate such things, and though I think I've a right to be hurt, I don't intend to show it.", "난 그런 게 싫어. 상처받을 만하다고 생각은 하지만, 그걸 내색할 마음은 없어."], ["They will feel that more than angry speeches or huffy actions, won't they, Marmee?\"", "그러는 편이 화난 말이나 토라진 행동보다 그 사람들한테 더 사무칠 거야, 그렇지 않아요, 엄마?\""]], [["\"That's the right spirit, my dear; a kiss for a blow is always best, though it's not very easy to give it sometimes,\"", "\"바로 그 마음이 옳단다, 얘야. 때리는 손에 입맞춤으로 갚는 게 언제나 최선이지, 비록 가끔은 그러기가 그리 쉽지 않더라도 말이야.\""], ["said her mother, with the air of one who had learned the difference between preaching and practising.", "어머니가 설교와 실천의 차이를 깨우친 사람다운 태도로 말했다."]], [["In spite of various very natural temptations to resent and retaliate, Amy adhered to her resolution all the next day, bent on conquering her enemy by kindness.", "분개하고 보복하고 싶은 지극히 자연스러운 유혹이 여러 차례 일었지만, 에이미는 친절로 적을 이기겠다고 마음먹은 채 이튿날 내내 그 결심을 지켰다."], ["She began well, thanks to a silent reminder that came to her unexpectedly, but most opportunely.", "뜻밖에, 그러나 더없이 때맞춰 찾아온 어떤 말없는 깨우침 덕분에 에이미는 좋은 출발을 끊었다."], ["As she arranged her table that morning, while the little girls were in an ante-room filling the baskets, she took up her pet production,--a little book, the antique cover of which her father had found among his treasures, and in which, on leaves of vellum, she had beautifully illuminated different texts.", "그날 아침, 어린 여자애들이 곁방에서 바구니를 채우는 동안 판매대를 정리하다가, 에이미는 자기가 가장 아끼는 작품을 집어 들었다. 작은 책이었는데, 아버지가 자신의 소장품 가운데서 찾아낸 고풍스러운 표지를 단 것으로, 양피지 책장마다 여러 성구를 아름답게 채식해 둔 것이었다."], ["As she turned the pages, rich in dainty devices, with very pardonable pride, her eye fell upon one verse that made her stop and think.", "정교한 도안으로 가득한 책장을 꽤나 봐줄 만한 자부심을 느끼며 넘기다가, 에이미의 눈길이 한 구절에 멈춰 그녀를 잠시 생각에 잠기게 했다."], ["Framed in a brilliant scroll-work of scarlet, blue, and gold, with little spirits of good-will helping one another up and down among the thorns and flowers, were the words, \"Thou shalt love thy neighbor as thyself.\"", "진홍과 파랑과 금빛의 화려한 당초무늬 테두리 안에, 가시와 꽃 사이를 오르내리며 서로를 돕는 작은 선의의 정령들과 함께, 이런 말이 적혀 있었다. \"네 이웃을 네 몸과 같이 사랑하라.\""]], [["\"I ought, but I don't,\"", "'나도 그래야 하는데, 그러질 못하잖아.'"], ["thought Amy, as her eye went from the bright page to May's discontented face behind the big vases, that could not hide the vacancies her pretty work had once filled.", "에이미는 그렇게 생각하며, 밝은 책장에서 큰 꽃병들 뒤편 메이의 불만 어린 얼굴로 눈길을 옮겼다. 그 꽃병들도 에이미의 예쁜 작품들이 한때 채웠던 빈자리를 가리지는 못했다."], ["Amy stood a minute, turning the leaves in her hand, reading on each some sweet rebuke for all heart-burnings and uncharitableness of spirit.", "에이미는 잠시 그 자리에 서서 손안의 책장을 넘기며, 가슴속의 온갖 앙금과 매정한 마음을 향한 다정한 꾸짖음을 한 장 한 장에서 읽어 나갔다. 거리에서, 학교에서, 일터나 가정에서, 그것을 의식하지 못하는 설교자들이 날마다 우리에게 지혜롭고 참된 설교를 숱하게 들려준다."], ["Many wise and true sermons are preached us every day by unconscious ministers in street, school, office, or home; even a fair-table may become a pulpit, if it can offer the good and helpful words which are never out of season.", "자선 바자회 판매대조차도, 철 지나는 법 없는 선하고 이로운 말을 건넬 수만 있다면 하나의 강단이 될 수 있는 것이다. 바로 그때 그 자리에서, 에이미의 양심은 그 성구로 짤막한 설교를 베풀었다."], ["Amy's conscience preached her a little sermon from that text, then and there; and she did what many of us do not always do,--took the sermon to heart, and straightway put it in practice.", "그리고 에이미는 우리 대다수가 늘 하지는 못하는 일을 했다. 그 설교를 가슴에 새기고는, 곧바로 실천에 옮긴 것이다."]], [["A group of girls were standing about May's table, admiring the pretty things, and talking over the change of saleswomen.", "여자애들 한 무리가 메이의 판매대 주위에 둘러서서 예쁜 물건들에 감탄하며 판매원이 바뀐 일을 두고 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["They dropped their voices, but Amy knew they were speaking of her, hearing one side of the story, and judging accordingly.", "그들은 목소리를 낮추었지만, 에이미는 그들이 자기 이야기를 하고 있다는 걸 알았다. 그들은 이야기의 한쪽 면만 듣고서 거기에 맞춰 판단하고 있었다."], ["It was not pleasant, but a better spirit had come over her, and presently a chance offered for proving it.", "유쾌한 일은 아니었지만, 에이미에게는 더 나은 마음이 깃들어 있었고, 마침 그것을 증명할 기회가 찾아왔다."], ["She heard May say sorrowfully,--", "메이가 서글프게 말하는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["\"It's too bad, for there is no time to make other things, and I don't want to fill up with odds and ends.", "\"너무 안타까워. 다른 걸 만들 시간도 없고, 자질구레한 것들로 채우긴 싫은데."], ["The table was just complete then: now it's spoilt.\"", "그때는 판매대가 딱 완벽했잖아. 이제는 망쳐 버렸어.\""]], [["\"I dare say she'd put them back if you asked her,\"", "\"네가 부탁하면 도로 갖다 놓아 줄 것 같은데.\""], ["suggested some one.", "누군가가 넌지시 말했다."]], [["\"How could I after all the fuss?\"", "\"그런 소동을 다 벌이고 나서 내가 어떻게 그래?\""], ["began May, but she did not finish, for Amy's voice came across the hall, saying pleasantly,--", "메이가 운을 떼다가 말을 끝맺지 못했으니, 에이미의 목소리가 홀을 가로질러 상냥하게 들려왔기 때문이다."]], [["\"You may have them, and welcome, without asking, if you want them. I was just thinking I'd offer to put them back, for they belong to your table rather than mine. Here they are; please take them, and forgive me if I was hasty in carrying them away last night.\"", "\"부탁할 것 없이 그냥 가져도 돼. 필요하다면 얼마든지. 마침 도로 갖다 놓겠다고 할까 생각하던 참이었거든. 내 판매대보다는 네 판매대에 더 어울리는 물건들이니까. 여기 있어. 부디 받아 줘. 그리고 어젯밤에 성급하게 들고 가 버린 거 용서해 줘.\""]], [["As she spoke, Amy returned her contribution, with a nod and a smile, and hurried away again, feeling that it was easier to do a friendly thing than it was to stay and be thanked for it.", "말을 하면서 에이미는 고갯짓과 미소와 함께 자기 출품작을 돌려주고는 다시 서둘러 자리를 떴다. 친절을 베푸는 것이, 거기 남아서 고맙다는 인사를 받는 것보다 차라리 쉽다고 느끼면서."]], [["\"Now, I call that lovely of her, don't you?\"", "\"이거야말로 정말 멋지지 않니?\""], ["cried one girl.", "한 여자애가 외쳤다."]], [["May's answer was inaudible; but another young lady, whose temper was evidently a little soured by making lemonade, added, with a disagreeable laugh, \"Very lovely; for she knew she wouldn't sell them at her own table.\"", "메이의 대답은 들리지 않았다. 그러나 레모네이드를 만드느라 심사가 좀 뒤틀린 게 분명한 또 다른 아가씨가 못마땅한 웃음을 지으며 덧붙였다. \"정말 멋지지. 어차피 자기 판매대에서는 못 팔 거란 걸 알았으니까.\""]], [["Now, that was hard; when we make little sacrifices we like to have them appreciated, at least; and for a minute Amy was sorry she had done it, feeling that virtue was not always its own reward. But it is,--as she presently discovered; for her spirits began to rise, and her table to blossom under her skilful hands; the girls were very kind, and that one little act seemed to have cleared the atmosphere amazingly.", "이건 좀 야박한 말이었다. 작은 희생을 치를 때면 우리는 적어도 알아주기라도 바라는 법이니까. 그래서 에이미는 잠시, 미덕이 늘 그 자체로 보상이 되는 건 아니라고 느끼며, 괜히 그랬다고 후회했다. 그러나 미덕은 분명 그 자체로 보상이 되었으니, 곧 에이미가 깨닫게 되었듯이 그녀의 기분이 차츰 살아나기 시작했고, 솜씨 좋은 손길 아래 판매대가 꽃처럼 피어났던 것이다. 여자애들은 무척 친절했고, 그 작은 행동 하나가 분위기를 놀라울 만큼 말끔하게 갈아 놓은 듯했다."]], [["It was a very long day, and a hard one to Amy, as she sat behind her table, often quite alone, for the little girls deserted very soon: few cared to buy flowers in summer, and her bouquets began to droop long before night.", "에이미에게는 무척 긴 하루, 또 고된 하루였다. 어린 여자애들이 금세 자리를 떠 버린 탓에 에이미는 판매대 뒤에 혼자 앉아 있을 때가 많았다. 여름철에 꽃을 사려는 사람은 드물었고, 그녀의 꽃다발은 밤이 되기 한참 전부터 시들기 시작했다."]], [["The art-table _was_ the most attractive in the room; there was a crowd about it all day long, and the tenders were constantly flying to and fro with important faces and rattling money-boxes.", "미술 판매대는 그 방에서 정말로 가장 인기가 많았다. 온종일 그 주위로 사람들이 북적였고, 판매를 맡은 이들은 자못 중요해 보이는 얼굴로 돈 상자를 절그럭거리며 쉴 새 없이 이리저리 분주히 오갔다."], ["Amy often looked wistfully across, longing to be there, where she felt at home and happy, instead of in a corner with nothing to do.", "에이미는 자기 마음이 편하고 행복하다고 느껴지는 그곳에 가 있고 싶어, 할 일 없이 구석에 처박혀 있는 대신 거기 있고 싶어, 종종 사무치는 눈길로 건너편을 바라보았다. 우리 가운데 누군가에게는 이것이 별로 대수롭지 않은 고생처럼 보일지도 모른다."], ["It might seem no hardship to some of us; but to a pretty, blithe young girl, it was not only tedious, but very trying; and the thought of being found there in the evening by her family, and Laurie and his friends, made it a real martyrdom.", "그러나 예쁘고 쾌활한 어린 아가씨에게는 지루할 뿐 아니라 무척 견디기 힘든 일이었다. 게다가 저녁에 가족과 로리와 그 친구들에게 이런 데 있는 모습을 들킬 거라는 생각은 그것을 진짜 순교나 다름없게 만들었다."]], [["She did not go home till night, and then she looked so pale and quiet that they knew the day had been a hard one, though she made no complaint, and did not even tell what she had done.", "에이미는 밤이 되어서야 집에 돌아왔는데, 얼굴이 어찌나 핼쑥하고 말이 없는지, 식구들은 불평 한마디 없고 자기가 한 일을 입 밖에 내지도 않았지만 그 하루가 고됐다는 걸 알 수 있었다."], ["Her mother gave her an extra cordial cup of tea, Beth helped her dress, and made a charming little wreath for her hair, while Jo astonished her family by getting herself up with unusual care, and hinting darkly that the tables were about to be turned.", "어머니는 그녀에게 평소보다 진하게 우려낸 차 한 잔을 따라 주었고, 베스는 옷 입는 것을 도우며 머리에 꽂을 어여쁜 작은 화관을 만들어 주었다. 한편 조는 평소답지 않게 공들여 차려입어 식구들을 놀라게 하고는, 곧 형세가 뒤집힐 거라고 은근히 의미심장하게 언질을 주었다."]], [["\"Don't do anything rude, pray, Jo.", "\"제발 무례한 짓은 하지 마, 조 언니."], ["I won't have any fuss made, so let it all pass, and behave yourself,\"", "난 소란 떠는 거 싫으니까, 다 그냥 넘어가고 얌전히 굴어.\""], ["begged Amy, as she departed early, hoping to find a reinforcement of flowers to refresh her poor little table.", "에이미가 가엾은 자기 판매대를 새롭게 꾸며 줄 꽃 지원군을 구할 수 있기를 바라며 일찍 나서면서 당부했다."]], [["\"I merely intend to make myself entrancingly agreeable to every one I know, and to keep them in your corner as long as possible.", "\"난 그저 내가 아는 모든 사람한테 정신을 못 차릴 만큼 상냥하게 굴어서, 그 사람들을 되도록 오래 네 구석에 붙잡아 둘 작정일 뿐이야."], ["Teddy and his boys will lend a hand, and we'll have a good time yet,\"", "테디하고 걔네 친구들도 거들어 줄 테니, 우리 아주 신나게 놀아 보자고.\""], ["returned Jo, leaning over the gate to watch for Laurie.", "조가 대문에 기대 로리를 기다리며 대답했다."], ["Presently the familiar tramp was heard in the dusk, and she ran out to meet him.", "이윽고 땅거미 속에서 귀에 익은 발소리가 들려오자, 조는 달려 나가 그를 맞이했다."]], [["\"Is that my boy?\"", "\"우리 도련님인가?\""]], [["\"As sure as this is my girl!\"", "\"여기 있는 게 우리 아가씨인 게 분명하듯이!\""], ["and Laurie tucked her hand under his arm, with the air of a man whose every wish was gratified.", "로리는 모든 소원이 다 이루어진 사내 같은 태도로 조의 손을 자기 팔짱 안에 끼웠다."]], [["\"O Teddy, such doings!\"", "\"아, 테디, 글쎄 이런 일이 있었어!\""], ["and Jo told Amy's wrongs with sisterly zeal.", "조는 누이다운 열의로 에이미가 당한 부당한 일을 들려주었다."]], [["\"A flock of our fellows are going to drive over by and by, and I'll be hanged if I don't make them buy every flower she's got, and camp down before her table afterward,\"", "\"우리 패거리가 좀 있다가 마차 몰고 우르르 갈 거야. 걔들을 시켜서 에이미가 가진 꽃을 한 송이도 빠짐없이 사게 하고, 그러고 나서 걔 판매대 앞에 진을 치고 앉지 않으면 내 손에 장을 지지지.\""], ["said Laurie, espousing her cause with warmth.", "로리가 열을 내며 조의 편을 들었다."]], [["\"The flowers are not at all nice, Amy says, and the fresh ones may not arrive in time.", "\"꽃이 영 신통치 않다고 에이미가 그러더라. 게다가 새 꽃은 제때 도착하지 않을지도 몰라."], ["I don't wish to be unjust or suspicious, but I shouldn't wonder if they never came at all.", "부당하게 굴거나 의심하고 싶진 않지만, 그 꽃이 아예 안 와도 난 놀라지 않을 것 같아."], ["When people do one mean thing they are very likely to do another,\"", "사람이 한 가지 못된 짓을 하면 또 한 가지를 하기 십상이거든.\""], ["observed Jo, in a disgusted tone.", "조가 진저리 난다는 투로 말했다."]], [["\"Didn't Hayes give you the best out of our gardens?", "\"헤이스가 우리 정원에서 제일 좋은 걸 골라 주지 않았어?"], ["I told him to.\"", "내가 그러라고 했는데.\""]], [["\"I didn't know that; he forgot, I suppose; and, as your grandpa was poorly, I didn't like to worry him by asking, though I did want some.\"", "\"그건 몰랐는걸. 그 사람이 깜빡한 모양이야. 그리고 너희 할아버지가 편찮으셔서, 꽃이 좀 필요하긴 했지만 여쭤봐서 성가시게 해 드리고 싶지가 않았어.\""]], [["\"Now, Jo, how could you think there was any need of asking!", "\"이런, 조, 어떻게 굳이 부탁할 필요가 있다고 생각할 수가 있어!"], ["They are just as much yours as mine.", "그건 내 것이나 마찬가지로 네 것이기도 한걸."], ["Don't we always go halves in everything?\"", "우리 늘 뭐든 반씩 나누지 않아?\""], ["began Laurie, in the tone that always made Jo turn thorny.", "로리가 늘 조를 가시 돋게 만드는 그 말투로 말문을 열었다."]], [["\"Gracious, I hope not!", "\"세상에, 제발 그러지 말아 줘!"], ["half of some of your things wouldn't suit me at all.", "네 물건 중에는 반쪽 가져도 나한테 영 안 어울리는 게 있단 말이야. 그나저나 여기서 이렇게 시시덕거리고 있을 때가 아니지."], ["But we mustn't stand philandering here; I've got to help Amy, so you go and make yourself splendid; and if you'll be so very kind as to let Hayes take a few nice flowers up to the Hall, I'll bless you forever.\"", "난 에이미를 도와야 하니까, 너는 가서 멋지게 차려입고 와. 그리고 헤이스 시켜서 회관에 좋은 꽃 몇 송이만 갖다 놓도록 그렇게 친절을 베풀어 주면, 내가 평생 너를 축복할게.\""]], [["\"Couldn't you do it now?\"", "\"지금 그러면 안 될까?\" 로리가 어찌나 의미심장하게 묻는지, 조는 야박할 만큼 다급히 그의 면전에서 대문을 닫아 버리고는 창살 사이로 외쳤다."], ["asked Laurie, so suggestively that Jo shut the gate in his face with inhospitable haste, and called through the bars, \"Go away, Teddy; I'm busy.\"", "\"저리 가, 테디. 나 바쁘거든.\""]], [["Thanks to the conspirators, the tables _were_ turned that night; for Hayes sent up a wilderness of flowers, with a lovely basket, arranged in his best manner, for a centre-piece; then the March family turned out _en masse_, and Jo exerted herself to some purpose, for people not only came, but stayed, laughing at her nonsense, admiring Amy's taste, and apparently enjoying themselves very much.", "음모자들 덕분에, 그날 밤 형세는 정말로 뒤집혔다. 헤이스가 꽃을 산더미처럼 보내왔는데, 한가운데 놓을 장식으로 솜씨를 한껏 부려 꾸민 어여쁜 바구니까지 곁들였던 것이다."], ["Laurie and his friends gallantly threw themselves into the breach, bought up the bouquets, encamped before the table, and made that corner the liveliest spot in the room.", "그러자 마치 집안 식구들이 총출동했고, 조가 톡톡히 한몫을 해낸 덕에 사람들이 모여들기만 한 게 아니라 머물러 있었으니, 그녀의 익살에 웃고 에이미의 안목에 감탄하면서 다들 무척 즐거워하는 눈치였다. 로리와 그 친구들은 의기롭게 그 빈틈에 뛰어들어 꽃다발을 죄다 사들이고는, 판매대 앞에 진을 친 채 그 구석을 방 안에서 가장 활기찬 자리로 만들어 놓았다."], ["Amy was in her element now, and, out of gratitude, if nothing more, was as sprightly and gracious as possible,--coming to the conclusion, about that time, that virtue _was_ its own reward, after all.", "이제 에이미는 물 만난 고기 같았고, 다른 건 몰라도 고마운 마음에서라도 더없이 발랄하고 우아하게 굴었다. 그러면서 그 무렵 미덕은 결국 그 자체로 보상이 되더라는 결론에 이르렀다."]], [["Jo behaved herself with exemplary propriety; and when Amy was happily surrounded by her guard of honor, Jo circulated about the hall, picking up various bits of gossip, which enlightened her upon the subject of the Chester change of base.", "조는 더없이 바르게 처신했다. 그리고 에이미가 명예 호위병들에게 흐뭇하게 둘러싸여 있는 동안, 조는 홀을 이리저리 돌아다니며 갖가지 소문 조각을 주워 모았는데, 그것들이 체스터 집안의 자리 변경 건에 관해 그녀를 깨우쳐 주었다."], ["She reproached herself for her share of the ill-feeling, and resolved to exonerate Amy as soon as possible; she also discovered what Amy had done about the things in the morning, and considered her a model of magnanimity.", "조는 그 반감에 자기가 한몫했다는 사실을 자책하며, 되도록 빨리 에이미의 누명을 벗겨 주리라 마음먹었다. 또한 에이미가 그날 아침 물건들을 두고 어떻게 처신했는지도 알게 되어, 동생을 너그러움의 본보기로 여기게 되었다."], ["As she passed the art-table, she glanced over it for her sister's things, but saw no signs of them.", "미술 판매대를 지나며 조는 동생의 물건이 있나 훑어보았지만, 그 흔적조차 보이지 않았다."], ["\"Tucked away out of sight, I dare say,\"", "'눈에 안 띄게 어디 치워 버렸나 보지.'"], ["thought Jo, who could forgive her own wrongs, but hotly resented any insult offered to her family.", "조는 그렇게 생각했다. 자기가 당한 부당함은 용서할 수 있어도 가족에게 가해진 모욕만큼은 발끈 분개하는 그녀였다."]], [["\"Good evening, Miss Jo.", "\"안녕하세요, 조 양."], ["How does Amy get on?\"", "에이미는 잘하고 있나요?\""], ["asked May, with a conciliatory air, for she wanted to show that she also could be generous.", "메이가 화해하려는 듯한 태도로 물었으니, 자기 역시 너그러울 수 있다는 걸 보여 주고 싶었던 것이다."]], [["\"She has sold everything she had that was worth selling, and now she is enjoying herself.", "\"팔 만한 건 죄다 팔아 치우고, 이제는 즐기는 중이에요."], ["The flower-table is always attractive, you know, 'especially to gentlemen.'\"", "알다시피 꽃 판매대는 언제나 인기가 많잖아요, '특히 신사분들한테' 말이죠.\""]], [["Jo _couldn't_ resist giving that little slap, but May took it so meekly she regretted it a minute after, and fell to praising the great vases, which still remained unsold.", "조는 그 작은 한 방을 날리고 싶은 마음을 참을 수가 없었다. 그러나 메이가 너무도 순순히 받아들이는 바람에 잠시 후 조는 그 말을 후회했고, 아직 팔리지 않은 채 남아 있는 큰 꽃병들을 칭찬하기 시작했다."]], [["\"Is Amy's illumination anywhere about?", "\"에이미가 채식한 책은 어디 없나요?"], ["I took a fancy to buy that for father,\"", "그건 아버지께 사다 드리고 싶은데.\""], ["said Jo, very anxious to learn the fate of her sister's work.", "조가 동생 작품의 운명을 몹시 궁금해하며 말했다."]], [["\"Everything of Amy's sold long ago; I took care that the right people saw them, and they made a nice little sum of money for us,\"", "\"에이미 물건은 진작에 다 팔렸어요. 제가 알맞은 사람들 눈에 띄게 신경을 좀 썼더니, 우리한테 짭짤한 돈을 벌어 주었지요.\""], ["returned May, who had overcome sundry small temptations, as well as Amy, that day.", "메이가 대답했는데, 그날 그녀 역시 에이미 못지않게 자잘한 유혹들을 여럿 이겨 낸 터였다."]], [["Much gratified, Jo rushed back to tell the good news; and Amy looked both touched and surprised by the report of May's words and manner.", "무척 흐뭇해진 조는 그 좋은 소식을 전하러 달려갔고, 에이미는 메이의 말과 태도에 관한 이야기를 듣고는 감동한 동시에 놀란 기색이었다."]], [["\"Now, gentlemen, I want you to go and do your duty by the other tables as generously as you have by mine--especially the art-table,\"", "\"자, 여러분, 제 판매대에 베풀어 주신 만큼 다른 판매대에도 너그럽게 의무를 다하러 가 주세요. 특히 미술 판매대로요.\""], ["she said, ordering out \"Teddy's Own,\" as the girls called the college friends.", "에이미가 여자애들이 \"테디 부대\"라고 부르는 대학생 친구들을 내보내며 말했다."]], [["\"'Charge, Chester, charge!'", "\"'돌격하라, 체스터, 돌격하라!'가 저 판매대의 표어로다."], ["is the motto for that table; but do your duty like men, and you'll get your money's worth of _art_ in every sense of the word,\"", "다만 사내답게 의무를 다하시라, 그러면 온갖 의미에서 '예술'에 걸맞은 본전을 뽑게 되리니.\" 헌신적인 방진(方陣)이 출정을 준비하는 가운데, 못 말리는 조가 말했다."], ["said the irrepressible Jo, as the devoted phalanx prepared to take the field.", "* 방진: 병사들을 네모꼴로 빽빽이 세운 진형. 옮긴이."]], [["\"To hear is to obey, but March is fairer far than May,\"", "\"명을 들으면 곧 따르리, 허나 마치는 메이보다 훨씬 어여뻐라.\""], ["said little Parker, making a frantic effort to be both witty and tender, and getting promptly quenched by Laurie, who said, \"Very well, my son, for a small boy!\"", "어린 파커가 재치 있는 동시에 다정하려고 필사적으로 애를 쓰며 말했다가, 곧장 로리한테 진압당하고 말았다. 로리가 \"어린 녀석치곤 제법이로구나, 얘야!\""], ["and walked him off, with a paternal pat on the head.", "하고는 아버지처럼 그 머리를 토닥이며 데리고 가 버린 것이다."]], [["\"Buy the vases,\"", "\"꽃병들을 사 줘.\""], ["whispered Amy to Laurie, as a final heaping of coals of fire on her enemy's head.", "에이미가 적의 머리에 마지막으로 숯불을 쌓아 올리는 셈치고 로리에게 속삭였다."]], [["To May's great delight, Mr. Laurence not only bought the vases, but pervaded the hall with one under each arm.", "메이가 크게 기뻐하게도, 로런스 씨는 그 꽃병들을 사 주었을 뿐 아니라 양쪽 겨드랑이에 하나씩 끼고 홀을 누비고 다녔다."], ["The other gentlemen speculated with equal rashness in all sorts of frail trifles, and wandered helplessly about afterward, burdened with wax flowers, painted fans, filigree portfolios, and other useful and appropriate purchases.", "다른 신사들도 그에 못지않게 무모하게 온갖 부서지기 쉬운 자질구레한 물건들에 투자했고, 그 뒤로는 밀랍 꽃이며 채색 부채며 가는 세공 무늬 문서철이며 그 밖의 쓸모 있고 적절한 구매품들을 잔뜩 짊어진 채 어쩔 줄 모르고 헤매고 다녔다."], ["Aunt Carrol was there, heard the story, looked pleased, and said something to Mrs.", "캐럴 고모할머니도 그 자리에 있다가 자초지종을 듣고는 흐뭇해하며, 한쪽 구석에서 마치 부인에게 뭐라고 말을 건넸다."], ["March in a corner, which made the latter lady beam with satisfaction, and watch Amy with a face full of mingled pride and anxiety, though she did not betray the cause of her pleasure till several days later.", "그 말에 마치 부인은 만족스레 얼굴이 환해져서는 자랑스러움과 걱정스러움이 뒤섞인 표정으로 에이미를 지켜보았으나, 그 기쁨의 까닭을 털어놓은 것은 며칠이 더 지나서였다."]], [["The fair was pronounced a success; and when May bade Amy good night, she did not \"gush\"", "바자회는 성공작으로 평가받았다."], ["as usual, but gave her an affectionate kiss, and a look which said, \"Forgive and forget.\"", "그리고 메이는 에이미에게 작별 인사를 할 때, 여느 때처럼 호들갑을 떠는 대신 다정하게 입을 맞추고는 \"용서하고 잊자\"라고 말하는 듯한 눈빛을 보냈다."], ["That satisfied Amy; and when she got home she found the vases paraded on the parlor chimney-piece, with a great bouquet in each.", "에이미는 그것으로 흡족했다. 집에 돌아오니 그 꽃병들이 응접실 벽난로 선반 위에 나란히 전시되어 있었고, 저마다 커다란 꽃다발이 꽂혀 있었다."], ["\"The reward of merit for a magnanimous March,\"", "\"너그러운 마치 양에게 내리는 미덕의 포상이라네.\""], ["as Laurie announced with a flourish.", "로리가 거창한 손짓과 함께 선언했다."]], [["\"You've a deal more principle and generosity and nobleness of character than I ever gave you credit for, Amy.", "\"넌 내가 인정해 준 것보다 훨씬 더 지조 있고 너그럽고 고결한 성품을 지녔구나, 에이미."], ["You've behaved sweetly, and I respect you with all my heart,\"", "정말 곱게 처신했어. 진심으로 널 존경한다.\""], ["said Jo warmly, as they brushed their hair together late that night.", "그날 밤늦게 함께 머리를 빗으며 조가 따뜻하게 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, we all do, and love her for being so ready to forgive.", "\"맞아, 우리 모두 그래. 그렇게 선뜻 용서해 줘서 더 사랑스럽고."], ["It must have been dreadfully hard, after working so long, and setting your heart on selling your own pretty things.", "그토록 오래 공들이고 네 예쁜 물건들을 꼭 팔겠다고 마음을 단단히 먹었는데, 얼마나 견디기 힘들었겠니."], ["I don't believe I could have done it as kindly as you did,\"", "나라면 너처럼 그렇게 다정하게 해내진 못했을 거야.\""], ["added Beth from her pillow.", "베스가 베개에 머리를 묻은 채 덧붙였다."]], [["\"Why, girls, you needn't praise me so; I only did as I'd be done by.", "\"아유, 언니들, 그렇게 칭찬할 거 없어. 난 그저 남한테 대접받고 싶은 대로 했을 뿐인걸."], ["You laugh at me when I say I want to be a lady, but I mean a true gentlewoman in mind and manners, and I try to do it as far as I know how.", "내가 숙녀가 되고 싶다고 하면 다들 비웃지만, 내 말은 마음씨와 몸가짐이 다 참된 숙녀가 되고 싶다는 거야. 그래서 내가 아는 한 그렇게 되려고 애쓰는 거고."], ["I can't explain exactly, but I want to be above the little meannesses and follies and faults that spoil so many women.", "정확히 설명은 못 하겠지만, 난 그토록 많은 여자들을 망쳐 놓는 그 자잘한 야비함이며 어리석음이며 결점들을 넘어서고 싶어."], ["I'm far from it now, but I do my best, and hope in time to be what mother is.\"", "지금이야 한참 멀었지만, 그래도 최선을 다하고 있고, 언젠가는 엄마 같은 사람이 되고 싶어.\""]], [["Amy spoke earnestly, and Jo said, with a cordial hug,--", "에이미가 진지하게 말하자, 조가 다정하게 끌어안으며 말했다."]], [["\"I understand now what you mean, and I'll never laugh at you again. You are getting on faster than you think, and I'll take lessons of you in true politeness, for you've learned the secret, I believe. Try away, deary; you'll get your reward some day, and no one will be more delighted than I shall.\"", "\"이제 네 말뜻을 알겠어. 다시는 널 비웃지 않을게. 넌 네 생각보다 빨리 자라고 있어. 참된 예의범절은 내가 너한테 배워야겠다. 넌 그 비결을 터득한 것 같으니까. 부지런히 애써 보렴, 얘야. 언젠가 그 보답을 받게 될 거야. 그러면 나보다 더 기뻐할 사람은 없을 거고.\""]], [["A week later Amy did get her reward, and poor Jo found it hard to be delighted. A letter came from Aunt Carrol, and Mrs.", "일주일 뒤 에이미는 정말로 그 보답을 받았는데, 가엾은 조는 그것을 기뻐하기가 여간 힘든 게 아니었다."], ["March's face was illuminated to such a degree, when she read it, that Jo and Beth, who were with her, demanded what the glad tidings were.", "캐럴 고모할머니에게서 편지가 한 통 왔는데, 그것을 읽는 마치 부인의 얼굴이 어찌나 환해지던지, 곁에 있던 조와 베스가 무슨 반가운 소식이냐고 다그쳐 물었다."]], [["\"Aunt Carrol is going abroad next month, and wants--\"", "\"캐럴 고모님이 다음 달에 외국에 나가시는데, 글쎄……\""]], [["\"Me to go with her!\"", "\"저더러 같이 가자고요!\""], ["burst in Jo, flying out of her chair in an uncontrollable rapture.", "조가 걷잡을 수 없는 환희에 의자에서 벌떡 일어나며 말을 가로챘다."]], [["\"No, dear, not you; it's Amy.\"", "\"아니란다, 얘야. 너 말고 에이미야.\""]], [["\"O mother! she's too young; it's my turn first. I've wanted it so long--it would do me so much good, and be so altogether splendid--I _must_ go.\"", "\"아, 엄마! 쟤는 너무 어려요. 순서로 따지면 제가 먼저잖아요. 전 정말 오랫동안 그걸 바랐어요. 저한테 얼마나 도움이 되겠어요. 그리고 더없이 멋진 일이 될 텐데. 저, 꼭 가야 해요.\""]], [["\"I'm afraid it's impossible, Jo.", "\"안타깝지만 그건 불가능하단다, 조."], ["Aunt says Amy, decidedly, and it is not for us to dictate when she offers such a favor.\"", "고모님께서 분명히 에이미라고 하셨고, 그런 호의를 베풀어 주시는데 우리가 이래라저래라 할 처지는 못 되잖니.\""]], [["\"It's always so.", "\"늘 이런 식이라니까."], ["Amy has all the fun and I have all the work.", "재미있는 건 죄다 에이미 차지고, 일은 죄다 제 몫이고요."], ["It isn't fair, oh, it isn't fair!\"", "불공평해요, 아, 정말 불공평하다고요!\""], ["cried Jo passionately.", "조가 격하게 소리쳤다."]], [["\"I'm afraid it is partly your own fault, dear.", "\"안타깝지만 그건 어느 정도 네 탓이기도 하단다, 얘야."], ["When Aunt spoke to me the other day, she regretted your blunt manners and too independent spirit; and here she writes, as if quoting something you had said,--'I planned at first to ask Jo; but as \"favors burden her,\"", "며칠 전 고모님이 나한테 말씀하실 때, 네 무뚝뚝한 태도와 지나치게 독립적인 기질을 아쉬워하시더구나. 여기 보렴, 네가 했던 말을 인용하시기라도 한 듯이 이렇게 쓰셨어."], ["and she \"hates French,\" I think I won't venture to invite her.", "'처음엔 조를 청할 생각이었지만, 그 애는 「호의를 부담스러워하고」"], ["Amy is more docile, will make a good companion for Flo, and receive gratefully any help the trip may give her.'\"", "「프랑스어라면 질색」이니, 선뜻 초대하지는 못하겠구나. 에이미가 더 고분고분하고, 플로에게 좋은 길동무가 되어 줄 테고, 이번 여행이 주는 도움이라면 무엇이든 고맙게 받아들이겠지.'\""]], [["\"Oh, my tongue, my abominable tongue!", "\"아, 내 혀, 이 끔찍한 내 혀!"], ["why can't I learn to keep it quiet?\"", "어째서 난 입을 다물 줄을 모를까?\""], ["groaned Jo, remembering words which had been her undoing.", "조가 자신을 망쳐 놓은 그 말들을 떠올리며 신음했다."], ["When she had heard the explanation of the quoted phrases, Mrs. March said sorrowfully,--", "인용된 구절들에 대한 설명을 듣고 나자, 마치 부인이 서글프게 말했다."]], [["\"I wish you could have gone, but there is no hope of it this time; so try to bear it cheerfully, and don't sadden Amy's pleasure by reproaches or regrets.\"", "\"네가 갈 수 있었으면 좋았을 텐데, 이번에는 가망이 없구나. 그러니 의젓하게 견뎌 보렴. 원망이나 후회로 에이미의 기쁨에 그늘을 드리우지 말고.\""]], [["\"I'll try,\"", "\"그래 볼게요.\""], ["said Jo, winking hard, as she knelt down to pick up the basket she had joyfully upset.", "조가 눈을 꾹꾹 깜박이며 말하고는, 좀 전에 기뻐서 엎어 버린 바구니를 주우려고 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["\"I'll take a leaf out of her book, and try not only to seem glad, but to be so, and not grudge her one minute of happiness; but it won't be easy, for it is a dreadful disappointment;\"", "\"쟤한테서 한 수 배워서, 기쁜 척만 하는 게 아니라 정말로 기뻐하고, 쟤 행복을 단 한순간도 시샘하지 않도록 애써 볼게요. 하지만 쉽진 않을 거예요."], ["and poor Jo bedewed the little fat pincushion she held with several very bitter tears.", "너무도 끔찍한 실망이니까요.\" 그러면서 가엾은 조는 손에 쥔 통통한 작은 바늘꽂이를, 무척이나 쓰라린 눈물 몇 방울로 적셨다."]], [["\"Jo, dear, I'm very selfish, but I couldn't spare you, and I'm glad you are not going quite yet,\"", "\"조 언니, 난 정말 이기적이지만, 난 언니를 보낼 수가 없어. 언니가 아직 안 가게 돼서 기뻐.\""], ["whispered Beth, embracing her, basket and all, with such a clinging touch and loving face, that Jo felt comforted in spite of the sharp regret that made her want to box her own ears, and humbly beg Aunt Carrol to burden her with this favor, and see how gratefully she would bear it.", "베스가 바구니까지 통째로 끌어안듯 매달리는 손길과 사랑스러운 얼굴로 속삭이자, 조는 날카로운 후회를 무릅쓰고도 마음이 누그러지는 것을 느꼈다. 그 후회 때문에 조는 자기 귀라도 한 대 쥐어박고 싶었고, 캐럴 고모할머니께 이 호의의 짐을 부디 자기에게 지워 주십사, 그러면 얼마나 고맙게 견뎌 내는지 보시라며 겸손히 빌고 싶었던 것이다."]], [["By the time Amy came in, Jo was able to take her part in the family jubilation; not quite as heartily as usual, perhaps, but without repinings at Amy's good fortune.", "에이미가 들어올 무렵에는, 조도 식구들의 환희에 한몫 거들 수 있게 되었다. 아마 평소만큼 진심에서 우러나진 못했겠지만, 그래도 에이미의 행운을 두고 투덜대지는 않았다."], ["The young lady herself received the news as tidings of great joy, went about in a solemn sort of rapture, and began to sort her colors and pack her pencils that evening, leaving such trifles as clothes, money, and passports to those less absorbed in visions of art than herself.", "정작 본인은 그 소식을 더없이 기쁜 낭보로 받아들여, 자못 엄숙한 환희에 젖어 돌아다녔다. 그러고는 그날 저녁부터 물감을 분류하고 연필을 챙기기 시작하면서, 옷이며 돈이며 여권 같은 자질구레한 것들은 자기처럼 예술의 환상에 푹 빠지지 않은 사람들에게 맡겨 버렸다."]], [["\"It isn't a mere pleasure trip to me, girls,\"", "\"이건 나한테 그저 놀러 가는 여행이 아니야, 언니들.\""], ["she said impressively, as she scraped her best palette.", "에이미가 가장 아끼는 팔레트를 긁어내며 의미심장하게 말했다."], ["\"It will decide my career; for if I have any genius, I shall find it out in Rome, and will do something to prove it.\"", "\"내 진로를 결정짓는 일이거든. 나한테 조금이라도 천재성이 있다면 로마에서 그걸 알아낼 테고, 그러면 그걸 증명할 뭔가를 해낼 테니까.\""]], [["\"Suppose you haven't?\"", "\"천재성이 없으면 어쩌고?\""], ["said Jo, sewing away, with red eyes, at the new collars which were to be handed over to Amy.", "조가 에이미에게 건네줄 새 칼라를 충혈된 눈으로 꿰매며 말했다."]], [["\"Then I shall come home and teach drawing for my living,\"", "\"그럼 집에 돌아와서 그림 가르치며 먹고살아야지.\""], ["replied the aspirant for fame, with philosophic composure; but she made a wry face at the prospect, and scratched away at her palette as if bent on vigorous measures before she gave up her hopes.", "명성을 꿈꾸는 그녀가 철학자다운 침착함으로 대답했다. 그러나 그 앞날을 떠올리자 얼굴을 찡그렸고, 희망을 접기 전에 한바탕 격렬한 수단이라도 강구하려는 듯 팔레트를 박박 긁어 댔다."]], [["\"No, you won't; you hate hard work, and you'll marry some rich man, and come home to sit in the lap of luxury all your days,\"", "\"아니, 넌 안 그럴걸. 넌 고된 일이라면 질색이니까, 어디 부자랑 결혼해서 평생 호강하며 살려고 집에 돌아올 거야.\""], ["said Jo.", "조가 말했다."]], [["\"Your predictions sometimes come to pass, but I don't believe that one will.", "\"언니 예언이 가끔 들어맞긴 하지만, 그 예언은 안 맞을 것 같아."], ["I'm sure I wish it would, for if I can't be an artist myself, I should like to be able to help those who are,\"", "그렇게 됐으면 좋겠다는 생각은 들지만 말이야. 내가 직접 예술가가 못 된다면, 예술가들을 도와줄 수 있는 사람이라도 됐으면 하거든.\""], ["said Amy, smiling, as if the part of Lady Bountiful would suit her better than that of a poor drawing-teacher.", "에이미가 미소 지으며 말했는데, 마치 가난한 그림 선생 노릇보다는 자선 부인 노릇이 자기에게 더 어울린다는 듯한 투였다."]], [["\"Hum!\" said Jo, with a sigh; \"if you wish it you'll have it, for your wishes are always granted--mine never.\"", "\"흥!\" 조가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"네가 바라면 다 이루어질 거야. 네 소원은 늘 들어주시니까. 내 건 한 번도 안 들어주시고.\""]], [["\"Would you like to go?\"", "\"언니도 가고 싶어?\""], ["asked Amy, thoughtfully patting her nose with her knife.", "에이미가 칼로 자기 코를 톡톡 두드리며 생각에 잠긴 듯 물었다."]], [["\"Rather!\"", "\"가고말고!\""]], [["\"Well, in a year or two I'll send for you, and we'll dig in the Forum for relics, and carry out all the plans we've made so many times.\"", "\"그럼, 일이 년 뒤에 내가 언니를 부를게. 우리 둘이 포룸에서 유물도 캐내고, 그동안 숱하게 세워 온 계획들을 죄다 실행에 옮기자.\""]], [["\"Thank you; I'll remind you of your promise when that joyful day comes, if it ever does,\"", "\"고마워. 그 즐거운 날이 오면, 만약 오기나 한다면, 네 약속을 일깨워 줄게.\""], ["returned Jo, accepting the vague but magnificent offer as gratefully as she could.", "조가 막연하지만 근사한 그 제안을 되도록 고맙게 받아들이며 대답했다."]], [["There was not much time for preparation, and the house was in a ferment till Amy was off.", "채비할 시간이 넉넉지 않아, 에이미가 떠날 때까지 집 안은 온통 들썩였다."], ["Jo bore up very well till the last flutter of blue ribbon vanished, when she retired to her refuge, the garret, and cried till she couldn't cry any more.", "조는 마지막으로 펄럭이던 파란 리본 자락이 사라질 때까지는 잘 버텼지만, 그 뒤로는 자신의 도피처인 다락방으로 물러가 더는 울 수 없을 때까지 울었다."], ["Amy likewise bore up stoutly till the steamer sailed; then, just as the gangway was about to be withdrawn, it suddenly came over her that a whole ocean was soon to roll between her and those who loved her best, and she clung to Laurie, the last lingerer, saying with a sob,--", "에이미도 증기선이 출항할 때까지는 꿋꿋이 버텼다. 그러다 막 트랩이 거두어지려는 순간, 자기와 자기를 가장 사랑하는 사람들 사이로 곧 대양 하나가 통째로 출렁이게 되리라는 사실이 불현듯 사무쳐 와, 마지막까지 머뭇거리던 로리에게 매달리며 흐느끼는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, take care of them for me; and if anything should happen--\"", "\"아, 나 대신 식구들을 잘 보살펴 줘. 그리고 혹시라도 무슨 일이 생기면……\""]], [["\"I will, dear, I will; and if anything happens, I'll come and comfort you,\"", "\"그럴게, 에이미, 그럴게. 무슨 일이 생기면 내가 가서 널 위로해 줄게.\""], ["whispered Laurie, little dreaming that he would be called upon to keep his word.", "로리가 속삭였다. 자기가 그 약속을 지켜야 할 날이 오리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못한 채."]], [["So Amy sailed away to find the old world, which is always new and beautiful to young eyes, while her father and friend watched her from the shore, fervently hoping that none but gentle fortunes would befall the happy-hearted girl, who waved her hand to them till they could see nothing but the summer sunshine dazzling on the sea.", "그리하여 에이미는 구세계를 찾아 항해를 떠났으니, 그곳은 젊은 눈에는 늘 새롭고 아름다운 법이다. 한편 그녀의 아버지와 친구는 물가에 서서 그녀를 지켜보며, 마음씨 고운 이 소녀에게 부디 다정한 운명 외에는 아무것도 닥치지 않기를 간절히 빌었다. 소녀는 바다 위에 눈부시게 반짝이는 여름 햇살 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않게 될 때까지 그들에게 손을 흔들었다. \"런던."]], [["\"Here I really sit at a front window of the Bath Hotel, Piccadilly.", "\"더없이 사랑하는 식구들에게,\n\"저는 지금 정말로 피커딜리에 있는 배스 호텔 앞쪽 창가에 앉아 있어요."], ["It's not a fashionable place, but uncle stopped here years ago, and won't go anywhere else; however, we don't mean to stay long, so it's no great matter.", "멋들어진 곳은 아니지만, 외삼촌이 몇 해 전에 여기 묵으신 뒤로 다른 데는 통 안 가려고 하시거든요. 그래도 오래 머물 생각은 아니니 별 상관없어요."], ["Oh, I can't begin to tell you how I enjoy it all!", "아, 이 모든 게 얼마나 즐거운지 도무지 말로 다 못 하겠어요."], ["I never can, so I'll only give you bits out of my note-book, for I've done nothing but sketch and scribble since I started.", "영영 못 할 것 같으니, 그냥 제 수첩에서 토막토막 골라 들려드릴게요. 출발한 뒤로 스케치하고 끄적이는 것 말고는 아무것도 안 했거든요."]], [["\"I sent a line from Halifax, when I felt pretty miserable, but after that I got on delightfully, seldom ill, on deck all day, with plenty of pleasant people to amuse me. Every one was very kind to me, especially the officers. Don't laugh, Jo; gentlemen really are very necessary aboard ship, to hold on to, or to wait upon one; and as they have nothing to do, it's a mercy to make them useful, otherwise they would smoke themselves to death, I'm afraid.", "\"핼리팩스에서 한 줄 부쳤을 땐 꽤 비참한 기분이었는데, 그 뒤로는 즐겁게 잘 지냈어요. 좀처럼 멀미도 안 하고, 온종일 갑판에 나와 있었고, 저를 즐겁게 해 줄 유쾌한 사람들도 많았거든요. 다들 저한테 무척 친절했는데, 특히 장교들이 그랬어요. 웃지 마, 조 언니. 배에서는 신사들이 정말 꼭 필요해. 붙잡고 의지할 사람으로든, 시중들어 줄 사람으로든 말이야. 게다가 그 사람들은 할 일도 없으니, 쓸모 있게 부려 먹는 게 자비를 베푸는 셈이지. 안 그러면 줄담배만 피우다 제 명에 못 죽을 게 분명하거든."]], [["\"Aunt and Flo were poorly all the way, and liked to be let alone, so when I had done what I could for them, I went and enjoyed myself.", "\"고모할머니랑 플로는 오는 내내 몸이 안 좋아서 가만히 내버려 두는 걸 좋아하셨어요. 그래서 두 분께 해 드릴 수 있는 걸 다 하고 나면, 저는 가서 마음껏 즐겼어요."], ["Such walks on deck, such sunsets, such splendid air and waves!", "갑판 위를 거니는 그 산책이며, 그 노을이며, 그 멋진 공기와 파도라니! 우리가 그토록 웅장하게 물살을 가르며 나아갈 때면, 빠른 말을 타는 것만큼이나 가슴 벅찼어요."], ["It was almost as exciting as riding a fast horse, when we went rushing on so grandly.", "베스 언니도 왔으면 좋았을 텐데. 언니한테 정말 좋았을 거야."], ["I wish Beth could have come, it would have done her so much good; as for Jo, she would have gone up and sat on the main-top jib, or whatever the high thing is called, made friends with the engineers, and tooted on the captain's speaking-trumpet, she'd have been in such a state of rapture.", "조 언니로 말하자면, 메인 톱 지브인지 그 높다란 게 뭐라 불리든 그 위에 기어올라 앉아서, 기관사들하고 친구가 되고, 선장의 메가폰을 빵빵 불어 댔겠지. 그렇게나 황홀해했을 거야."]], [["\"It was all heavenly, but I was glad to see the Irish coast, and found it very lovely, so green and sunny, with brown cabins here and there, ruins on some of the hills, and gentlemen's country-seats in the valleys, with deer feeding in the parks.", "\"다 천국 같았지만, 그래도 아일랜드 해안을 보니 반가웠고, 무척 아름답더군요. 어찌나 푸르고 햇살이 가득하던지, 군데군데 갈색 오두막이 있고, 언덕마다 폐허가 서 있고, 골짜기에는 신사들의 시골 저택이 있고, 사슴들이 사유지에서 풀을 뜯고 있었어요."], ["It was early in the morning, but I didn't regret getting up to see it, for the bay was full of little boats, the shore _so_ picturesque, and a rosy sky overhead.", "이른 아침이었지만 그걸 보려고 일어난 걸 후회하지 않았어요. 만에는 작은 배들이 가득했고, 해안은 어찌나 그림 같던지, 머리 위로는 장밋빛 하늘이 펼쳐져 있었거든요."], ["I never shall forget it.", "절대 못 잊을 거예요."]], [["\"At Queenstown one of my new acquaintances left us,--Mr.", "\"퀸스타운에서 새로 사귄 사람 중 하나가 우리 곁을 떠났어요."], ["Lennox,--and when I said something about the Lakes of Killarney, he sighed and sung, with a look at me,--", "레녹스 씨였는데, 제가 킬라니 호수 이야기를 좀 꺼냈더니, 절 바라보며 한숨을 쉬고는 이렇게 노래하더군요."]], [["'Oh, have you e'er heard of Kate Kearney?", "'아, 케이트 컨이를 들어 본 적 있나요?"], ["She lives on the banks of Killarney; From the glance of her eye, Shun danger and fly, For fatal's the glance of Kate Kearney.'", "킬라니 호숫가에 사는 그녀를. 그녀의 눈빛을 보거든 위험을 피해 달아나요, 케이트 컨이의 눈길은 치명적이니.'"]], [["Wasn't that nonsensical?", "그거 참 시답잖지 않아요?"]], [["\"We only stopped at Liverpool a few hours.", "\"리버풀에는 몇 시간밖에 안 머물렀어요."], ["It's a dirty, noisy place, and I was glad to leave it.", "지저분하고 시끄러운 곳이라 떠나게 돼서 다행이었죠."], ["Uncle rushed out and bought a pair of dog-skin gloves, some ugly, thick shoes, and an umbrella, and got shaved _à la_ mutton-chop, the first thing.", "외삼촌은 부리나케 나가서 제일 먼저 개가죽 장갑 한 켤레랑 보기 흉한 두툼한 신발이며 우산을 사고, 양고기 갈빗살 모양으로 구레나룻을 다듬었어요."], ["Then he flattered himself that he looked like a true Briton; but the first time he had the mud cleaned off his shoes, the little bootblack knew that an American stood in them, and said, with a grin, 'There yer har, sir.", "그러고는 자기가 진짜 영국 신사처럼 보인다고 흐뭇해하셨죠. 그런데 처음으로 신발에 묻은 진흙을 닦으니, 그 어린 구두닦이가 그 안에 미국인이 들어 있다는 걸 알아채고는 씩 웃으며 그러더래요."], ["I've give 'em the latest Yankee shine.'", "'다 됐습니다, 손님. 최신 양키식으로 광을 내 드렸지요.'"], ["It amused uncle immensely.", "외삼촌은 그게 어찌나 우스웠는지 몰라요."], ["Oh, I _must_ tell you what that absurd Lennox did!", "아, 그 엉뚱한 레녹스가 무슨 짓을 했는지 꼭 들려드려야겠어요!"], ["He got his friend Ward, who came on with us, to order a bouquet for me, and the first thing I saw in my room was a lovely one, with 'Robert Lennox's compliments,'", "우리랑 같이 온 친구 워드를 시켜서 저한테 꽃다발을 주문하게 한 거예요. 제 방에 들어가서 제일 먼저 본 게 어여쁜 꽃다발이었는데, 카드에 '로버트 레녹스 드림'이라고 적혀 있었어요."], ["on the card. Wasn't that fun, girls?", "재미있지 않니, 언니들?"], ["I like travelling.", "난 여행이 참 좋아."]], [["\"I never _shall_ get to London if I don't hurry.", "\"이러다가 서두르지 않으면 도무지 런던에 닿질 못하겠네요."], ["The trip was like riding through a long picture-gallery, full of lovely landscapes.", "그 여정은 사랑스러운 풍경으로 가득한 기다란 화랑을 지나는 것 같았어요."], ["The farmhouses were my delight; with thatched roofs, ivy up to the eaves, latticed windows, and stout women with rosy children at the doors.", "농가들이 제일 마음에 들었어요. 초가지붕에 처마까지 담쟁이가 타고 올랐고, 격자창이 달렸고, 문간에는 혈색 좋은 아이들을 데리고 선 튼실한 아낙들이 있었죠."], ["The very cattle looked more tranquil than ours, as they stood knee-deep in clover, and the hens had a contented cluck, as if they never got nervous, like Yankee biddies.", "토끼풀 속에 무릎까지 잠겨 선 가축들조차 우리 쪽 가축보다 더 평온해 보였고, 암탉들은 양키 닭들처럼 신경질을 부리는 법이 없는 듯, 만족스럽게 꼬꼬댁거렸어요."], ["Such perfect color I never saw,--the grass so green, sky so blue, grain so yellow, woods so dark,--I was in a rapture all the way.", "그렇게 완벽한 색은 난생처음 봤어요. 풀은 어찌나 푸르고, 하늘은 어찌나 파랗고, 곡식은 어찌나 노랗고, 숲은 어찌나 짙던지, 가는 내내 황홀했어요."], ["So was Flo; and we kept bouncing from one side to the other, trying to see everything while we were whisking along at the rate of sixty miles an hour.", "플로도 그랬고요. 우리는 시속 100킬로미터로 쌩쌩 달리는 와중에 뭐든 다 보려고 이쪽저쪽으로 통통 옮겨 다녔어요."], ["Aunt was tired and went to sleep, but uncle read his guide-book, and wouldn't be astonished at anything.", "고모할머니는 지치셔서 주무셨고, 외삼촌은 안내서를 읽으며 뭘 봐도 놀라는 법이 없었죠. 우리는 이런 식으로 갔어요."], ["This is the way we went on: Amy, flying up,--'Oh, that must be Kenilworth, that gray place among the trees!'", "에이미가 벌떡 일어나서 '아, 저게 케닐워스인가 봐, 나무들 사이에 저 회색 건물!' 플로가 제 창가로 쏜살같이 와서, '어쩜 이렇게 멋질까!"], ["Flo, darting to my window,--'How sweet!", "우리 언제 저기 가야 해요, 그렇죠, 아빠?'"], ["We must go there some time, won't we, papa?'", "외삼촌은 태연히 자기 신발에 감탄하며, '아니란다, 얘야."], ["Uncle, calmly admiring his boots,--'No, my dear, not unless you want beer; that's a brewery.'", "맥주가 마시고 싶다면 모를까. 저건 양조장이거든.'"]], [["\"A pause,--then Flo cried out, 'Bless me, there's a gallows and a man going up.'", "\"잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 플로가 외쳤어요. '어머나, 저기 교수대가 있고 사람이 올라가고 있어요.'"], ["'Where, where?'", "'어디, 어디?'"], ["shrieks Amy, staring out at two tall posts with a cross-beam and some dangling chains.", "에이미가 가로대가 달린 두 개의 높다란 기둥이며 거기 매달려 흔들리는 쇠사슬들을 멍하니 내다보며 비명을 질렀죠."], ["'A colliery,'", "'탄광이로구나.'"], ["remarks uncle, with a twinkle of the eye.", "외삼촌이 눈을 반짝이며 말했어요."], ["'Here's a lovely flock of lambs all lying down,'", "'여기 사랑스러운 양 떼가 죄다 누워 있어요.'"], ["says Amy. 'See, papa, aren't they pretty!'", "에이미가 말하자, '봐요, 아빠, 예쁘죠!'"], ["added Flo sentimentally.", "플로가 감상에 젖어 덧붙였어요."], ["'Geese, young ladies,' returns uncle, in a tone that keeps us quiet till Flo settles down to enjoy 'The Flirtations of Capt.", "'거위란다, 아가씨들.'"], ["Cavendish,' and I have the scenery all to myself.", "외삼촌이 우리를 잠잠하게 만드는 말투로 대꾸하자, 플로가 얌전히 「캐번디시 대위의 연애 행각」을 즐기는 데 정착했고, 저는 풍경을 온전히 혼자 차지하게 되었죠."]], [["\"Of course it rained when we got to London, and there was nothing to be seen but fog and umbrellas.", "\"런던에 닿으니 당연하다는 듯 비가 왔고, 안개와 우산 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았어요."], ["We rested, unpacked, and shopped a little between the showers.", "우리는 쉬고, 짐을 풀고, 비가 잠깐씩 멎는 사이에 쇼핑을 좀 했어요."], ["Aunt Mary got me some new things, for I came off in such a hurry I wasn't half ready.", "메리 고모할머니가 새 물건을 몇 가지 사 주셨어요. 워낙 급하게 떠나오느라 채비가 반도 안 됐었거든요."], ["A white hat and blue feather, a muslin dress to match, and the loveliest mantle you ever saw.", "하얀 모자에 파란 깃털, 거기 어울리는 모슬린 드레스, 그리고 여태 본 것 중 가장 어여쁜 망토예요."], ["Shopping in Regent Street is perfectly splendid; things seem so cheap--nice ribbons only sixpence a yard.", "리젠트 거리에서 쇼핑하는 건 정말 근사해요. 물건이 어찌나 싼지, 고운 리본이 1야드에 겨우 6펜스예요."], ["I laid in a stock, but shall get my gloves in Paris.", "잔뜩 사 두었지만, 장갑은 파리에서 살 거예요."], ["Doesn't that sound sort of elegant and rich?", "어쩐지 우아하고 부티 나는 소리지 않아요?"]], [["\"Flo and I, for the fun of it, ordered a hansom cab, while aunt and uncle were out, and went for a drive, though we learned afterward that it wasn't the thing for young ladies to ride in them alone.", "\"플로랑 저는 재미 삼아, 고모할머니랑 외삼촌이 나가신 사이에 핸섬 마차를 한 대 불러서 드라이브를 갔어요. 나중에야 젊은 아가씨들이 그런 마차를 단둘이 타는 건 점잖지 못한 일이라는 걸 알았지만요."], ["It was so droll!", "어찌나 우습던지!"], ["for when we were shut in by the wooden apron, the man drove so fast that Flo was frightened, and told me to stop him.", "나무 덮개에 갇혀 있는데 마부가 어찌나 빨리 모는지, 플로가 겁을 먹고 저더러 마부를 세우라고 했거든요."], ["But he was up outside behind somewhere, and I couldn't get at him.", "그런데 그 사람은 어딘가 바깥 뒤쪽 높은 데 올라타 있어서 도무지 닿을 수가 없었어요."], ["He didn't hear me call, nor see me flap my parasol in front, and there we were, quite helpless, rattling away, and whirling around corners at a break-neck pace.", "제가 불러도 못 듣고, 앞에서 양산을 펄럭여도 못 보고, 우리는 그저 속수무책으로 덜컹덜컹 내달리며 목이 부러질 듯한 속도로 모퉁이를 휙휙 돌았죠."], ["At last, in my despair, I saw a little door in the roof, and on poking it open, a red eye appeared, and a beery voice said,--", "마침내 절망에 빠진 저는 지붕에 난 작은 문 하나를 발견했고, 그걸 콕콕 찔러 열자 벌건 눈 하나가 나타나더니, 술 취한 목소리가 그러더군요."]], [["\"'Now then, mum?'", "\"'뭡니까, 아씨?'"]], [["\"I gave my order as soberly as I could, and slamming down the door, with an 'Aye, aye, mum,'", "\"저는 되도록 진지하게 분부를 내렸고, 그 사람은 '예이, 예이, 아씨'"], ["the man made his horse walk, as if going to a funeral.", "하며 문을 쾅 닫더니, 꼭 장례 행렬에라도 가듯 말을 걷게 했어요."], ["I poked again, and said, 'A little faster;'", "제가 다시 콕콕 찌르고 '좀 더 빨리요'"], ["then off he went, helter-skelter, as before, and we resigned ourselves to our fate.", "하자, 그제야 아까처럼 정신없이 내달렸고, 우리는 운명에 우리를 내맡겼죠."]], [["\"To-day was fair and we went to Hyde Park, close by, for we are more aristocratic than we look.", "\"오늘은 날이 개어서 바로 옆에 있는 하이드 파크에 갔어요."], ["The Duke of Devonshire lives near.", "우리가 겉보기보다 더 귀족적이거든요."], ["I often see his footmen lounging at the back gate; and the Duke of Wellington's house is not far off.", "데번셔 공작이 근처에 사는데, 그 댁 종복들이 뒷문에서 빈둥거리는 걸 자주 봐요. 웰링턴 공작 저택도 멀지 않고요."], ["Such sights as I saw, my dear!", "글쎄, 거기서 본 광경이라니!"], ["It was as good as Punch, for there were fat dowagers rolling about in their red and yellow coaches, with gorgeous Jeameses in silk stockings and velvet coats, up behind, and powdered coachmen in front.", "펀치 인형극 못지않았어요. 뚱뚱한 미망인 부인들이 빨갛고 노란 마차를 타고 굴러다녔는데, 뒤에는 비단 양말에 벨벳 외투를 걸친 화려한 하인들이 매달려 있고, 앞에는 분을 바른 마부들이 앉아 있었거든요."], ["Smart maids, with the rosiest children I ever saw; handsome girls, looking half asleep; dandies, in queer English hats and lavender kids, lounging about, and tall soldiers, in short red jackets and muffin caps stuck on one side, looking so funny I longed to sketch them.", "산뜻한 하녀들이 여태 본 것 중 가장 혈색 좋은 아이들을 데리고 있고, 어여쁜 아가씨들은 반쯤 졸린 듯한 얼굴이고, 멋쟁이들은 괴상한 영국식 모자에 연보랏빛 새끼염소 가죽 장갑을 끼고 어슬렁거리고, 키 큰 병사들은 짧은 빨간 재킷에 머핀처럼 생긴 모자를 한쪽으로 삐딱하게 얹은 채로 어찌나 우스워 보이던지, 그려 보고 싶어 안달이 났어요."]], [["\"Rotten Row means '_Route de Roi_,'", "\"로튼 로는 '왕의 길'이라는 뜻이래요. 그렇지만 지금은 뭐랄까 승마 학교에 더 가까워요."], ["or the king's way; but now it's more like a riding-school than anything else.", "말들은 훌륭하고, 남자들은, 특히 마부들은 말을 잘 타지만, 여자들은 뻣뻣하게 굴면서 통통 튀는데 그건 우리 식 규칙엔 안 맞아요. 저는 미국식으로 질주하는 멋진 모습을 보여 주고 싶어 안달이 났어요."], ["The horses are splendid, and the men, especially the grooms, ride well; but the women are stiff, and bounce, which isn't according to our rules.", "그 사람들은 짧은 승마복에 높은 모자를 쓰고 점잔을 빼며 위아래로 천천히 다녔는데, 꼭 장난감 노아의 방주에 나오는 여자들 같았거든요. 다들 말을 타요."], ["I longed to show them a tearing American gallop, for they trotted solemnly up and down, in their scant habits and high hats, looking like the women in a toy Noah's Ark.", "노인들도, 뚱뚱한 부인들도, 어린 아이들도요. 그리고 젊은이들은 여기서 한껏 시시덕거리고요."], ["Every one rides,--old men, stout ladies, little children,--and the young folks do a deal of flirting here; I saw a pair exchange rosebuds, for it's the thing to wear one in the button-hole, and I thought it rather a nice little idea.", "한 쌍이 장미 봉오리를 주고받는 걸 봤는데, 여기선 그걸 단춧구멍에 꽂는 게 유행이거든요. 제법 괜찮은 발상이다 싶었어요."]], [["\"In the P.M. to Westminster Abbey; but don't expect me to describe it, that's impossible--so I'll only say it was sublime! This evening we are going to see Fechter, which will be an appropriate end to the happiest day of my life.", "\"오후에는 웨스트민스터 사원에 갔어요. 그렇지만 그걸 묘사해 달라는 기대는 마세요. 그건 불가능하니까요. 그냥 숭고했다고만 할게요! 오늘 저녁엔 페히터를 보러 가요. 제 생애 가장 행복한 하루의 알맞은 마무리가 되겠죠. \"자정."]], [["\"It's very late, but I can't let my letter go in the morning without telling you what happened last evening.", "\"무척 늦었지만, 어젯밤에 무슨 일이 있었는지 들려드리지 않고서는 아침에 편지를 부칠 수가 없네요."], ["Who do you think came in, as we were at tea?", "우리가 차를 마시고 있는데 누가 들어왔게요?"], ["Laurie's English friends, Fred and Frank Vaughn!", "로리의 영국 친구들, 프레드랑 프랭크 본이었어요!"], ["I was _so_ surprised, for I shouldn't have known them but for the cards.", "어찌나 놀랐는지, 카드가 없었으면 못 알아봤을 거예요."], ["Both are tall fellows, with whiskers; Fred handsome in the English style, and Frank much better, for he only limps slightly, and uses no crutches.", "둘 다 구레나룻을 기른 키 큰 청년들인데, 프레드는 영국식으로 잘생겼고, 프랭크는 훨씬 더 나아요. 이젠 약간 절뚝거릴 뿐 목발도 안 짚거든요."], ["They had heard from Laurie where we were to be, and came to ask us to their house; but uncle won't go, so we shall return the call, and see them as we can.", "둘은 로리한테서 우리가 어디 있을지 듣고서, 자기네 집에 초대하려고 찾아왔어요. 그런데 외삼촌이 안 가려고 하셔서, 우리가 답방을 가서 되도록 자주 보기로 했어요."], ["They went to the theatre with us, and we did have _such_ a good time, for Frank devoted himself to Flo, and Fred and I talked over past, present, and future fun as if we had known each other all our days.", "둘이 우리랑 같이 극장에 갔는데, 정말 재밌게 놀았어요. 프랭크는 플로한테 푹 빠져 있었고, 프레드랑 저는 평생 알고 지낸 사이처럼 지난날, 오늘, 그리고 앞으로 누릴 재미에 관해 이야기를 나눴거든요."], ["Tell Beth Frank asked for her, and was sorry to hear of her ill health.", "베스 언니한테 프랭크가 안부를 묻더라고, 언니가 아프다는 말에 안타까워하더라고 전해 줘."], ["Fred laughed when I spoke of Jo, and sent his 'respectful compliments to the big hat.'", "프레드는 제가 조 언니 얘기를 꺼내자 웃으면서 '그 큰 모자한테 정중한 안부를 전한다'고 했어요."], ["Neither of them had forgotten Camp Laurence, or the fun we had there.", "둘 다 로런스 캠프며 거기서 누렸던 재미를 잊지 않고 있었어요."], ["What ages ago it seems, doesn't it?", "그게 얼마나 까마득한 옛날 같은지, 그렇지 않아요?"]], [["\"Aunt is tapping on the wall for the third time, so I _must_ stop.", "\"고모할머니가 벌써 세 번째 벽을 두드리시니, 이만 멈춰야겠어요."], ["I really feel like a dissipated London fine lady, writing here so late, with my room full of pretty things, and my head a jumble of parks, theatres, new gowns, and gallant creatures who say 'Ah!'", "이렇게 밤늦도록 예쁜 물건으로 가득한 방에 앉아 편지를 쓰고 있자니, 머릿속은 공원이며 극장이며 새 드레스며 '아!' 하고 감탄사를 내뱉으며 진짜 영국 귀족다운 거드름으로 금빛 콧수염을 배배 꼬는 멋쟁이들로 뒤죽박죽이고, 정말이지 방탕한 런던 귀부인이 된 기분이에요."], ["and twirl their blond mustaches with the true English lordliness.", "다들 너무 보고 싶어요."], ["I long to see you all, and in spite of my nonsense am, as ever, your loving AMY.\"", "이런 실없는 소리를 늘어놓아도, 저는 언제까지나 식구들을 사랑하는\n에이미.\""]], [["\"In my last I told you about our London visit,--how kind the Vaughns were, and what pleasant parties they made for us.", "\"사랑하는 언니들에게, \"파리. \"지난 편지에서 런던 방문 이야기를 들려드렸죠."], ["I enjoyed the trips to Hampton Court and the Kensington Museum more than anything else,--for at Hampton I saw Raphael's cartoons, and, at the Museum, rooms full of pictures by Turner, Lawrence, Reynolds, Hogarth, and the other great creatures.", "본 집안 사람들이 얼마나 친절했는지, 우리를 위해 얼마나 즐거운 모임들을 마련해 줬는지요. 그중에서도 햄프턴 코트랑 켄싱턴 박물관에 간 게 제일 즐거웠어요."], ["The day in Richmond Park was charming, for we had a regular English picnic, and I had more splendid oaks and groups of deer than I could copy; also heard a nightingale, and saw larks go up.", "햄프턴에서는 라파엘로의 밑그림을 봤고, 박물관에서는 터너, 로런스, 레이놀즈, 호가스, 그 밖의 위대한 거장들의 그림으로 가득한 방들을 봤거든요. 리치먼드 공원에서 보낸 하루는 매혹적이었어요."], ["We 'did'", "우리가 제대로 된 영국식 소풍을 즐겼는데, 도저히 다 옮겨 그릴 수 없을 만큼 멋진 떡갈나무며 사슴 무리가 있었거든요."], ["London to our hearts'", "나이팅게일 소리도 듣고, 종달새가 날아오르는 것도 봤어요. 프레드랑 프랭크 덕분에 우리는 런던을 마음껏 '구경'했고, 떠나기가 아쉬웠어요."], ["content, thanks to Fred and Frank, and were sorry to go away; for, though English people are slow to take you in, when they once make up their minds to do it they cannot be outdone in hospitality, _I_ think.", "영국 사람들은 마음을 여는 데 더디지만, 일단 받아들이기로 마음을 정하면 그 환대만큼은 누구한테도 뒤지지 않는다고 저는 생각해요. 본 집안 사람들은 다음 겨울에 로마에서 우리랑 만나기를 바라는데, 그러지 못하면 저는 끔찍하게 실망할 거예요."], ["The Vaughns hope to meet us in Rome next winter, and I shall be dreadfully disappointed if they don't, for Grace and I are great friends, and the boys very nice fellows,--especially Fred.", "그레이스랑 저는 둘도 없는 친구가 됐고, 그 남자애들도 아주 좋은 친구들이거든요. 특히 프레드가요."]], [["\"Well, we were hardly settled here, when he turned up again, saying he had come for a holiday, and was going to Switzerland.", "\"그런데 여기 자리를 잡기가 무섭게 그 애가 또 나타난 거예요. 휴가를 보내러 왔다면서, 스위스로 갈 거래요."], ["Aunt looked sober at first, but he was so cool about it she couldn't say a word; and now we get on nicely, and are very glad he came, for he speaks French like a native, and I don't know what we should do without him.", "고모할머니가 처음엔 떨떠름한 표정이셨지만, 그 애가 어찌나 태연한지 한마디도 못 하시더라고요. 이젠 우리도 잘 지내고, 그 애가 와 줘서 무척 다행이에요."], ["Uncle doesn't know ten words, and insists on talking English very loud, as if that would make people understand him.", "프레드는 프랑스어를 토박이처럼 하는데, 그 애가 없으면 어쩌나 싶거든요."], ["Aunt's pronunciation is old-fashioned, and Flo and I, though we flattered ourselves that we knew a good deal, find we don't, and are very grateful to have Fred do the '_parley vooing_,'", "외삼촌은 열 마디도 못 하면서, 그러면 사람들이 알아듣기라도 하는 양 영어로 아주 크게 말하겠다고 고집을 부리세요. 고모할머니 발음은 구식이고, 플로랑 저는 우리가 꽤 안다고 으스댔지만 알고 보니 모르는 터라, 외삼촌이 '파를리부'"], ["as uncle calls it.", "라고 부르는 그 프랑스어 노릇을 프레드가 해 주니 무척 고맙죠."]], [["\"Such delightful times as we are having!", "\"우리는 얼마나 즐거운 시간을 보내는지 몰라요!"], ["sight-seeing from morning till night, stopping for nice lunches in the gay _cafés_, and meeting with all sorts of droll adventures.", "아침부터 밤까지 구경을 다니고, 흥겨운 카페에서 멈춰 맛있는 점심을 먹고, 온갖 우스꽝스러운 모험을 만나기도 해요. 비 오는 날엔 루브르에서 그림에 흠뻑 빠져 지내요."], ["Rainy days I spend in the Louvre, revelling in pictures.", "조 언니라면 가장 빼어난 그림 몇 점을 보고도 그 못된 코를 치켜들 거예요. 예술을 느끼는 영혼이 없으니까요."], ["Jo would turn up her naughty nose at some of the finest, because she has no soul for art; but _I_ have, and I'm cultivating eye and taste as fast as I can.", "그렇지만 저는 있어서, 눈과 안목을 되도록 빨리 갈고닦는 중이에요. 언니는 차라리 위인들의 유물을 더 좋아할 거예요."], ["She would like the relics of great people better, for I've seen her Napoleon's cocked hat and gray coat, his baby's cradle and his old toothbrush; also Marie Antoinette's little shoe, the ring of Saint Denis, Charlemagne's sword, and many other interesting things.", "제가 나폴레옹의 삼각모랑 회색 외투, 그 아들의 요람과 낡은 칫솔을 봤거든요. 마리 앙투아네트의 작은 신발이며, 생드니의 반지, 샤를마뉴의 검, 그 밖에 흥미로운 것들도 많이 봤고요."], ["I'll talk for hours about them when I come, but haven't time to write.", "가서 몇 시간이고 그 이야기를 들려줄게요. 지금은 쓸 시간이 없어서요."]], [["\"The Palais Royale is a heavenly place,--so full of _bijouterie_ and lovely things that I'm nearly distracted because I can't buy them.", "\"팔레 루아얄은 천국 같은 곳이에요. 보석 세공품이며 사랑스러운 물건들로 가득한데, 살 수가 없으니 미칠 지경이에요."], ["Fred wanted to get me some, but of course I didn't allow it.", "프레드가 좀 사 주려고 했지만, 당연히 못 하게 했죠. 그다음에 불로뉴 숲이랑 샹젤리제는 정말이지 장엄해요."], ["Then the Bois and the Champs Elysées are _très magnifique_.", "황실 가족도 여러 번 봤어요. 황제는 못생기고 험상궂게 생긴 남자였고, 황후는 창백하고 예뻤지만 차림새가 영 촌스러웠어요."], ["I've seen the imperial family several times,--the emperor an ugly, hard-looking man, the empress pale and pretty, but dressed in bad taste, _I_ thought,--purple dress, green hat, and yellow gloves.", "보랏빛 드레스에 초록 모자, 노란 장갑이라니. 어린 나폴레옹은 잘생긴 소년인데, 가정교사랑 재잘재잘 이야기하며 앉아 있다가, 사두마차를 타고 지나가면서 사람들에게 손에 입을 맞춰 보내요."], ["Little Nap. is a handsome boy, who sits chatting to his tutor, and kisses his hand to the people as he passes in his four-horse barouche, with postilions in red satin jackets, and a mounted guard before and behind.", "마차에는 빨간 새틴 재킷을 입은 마부들이 타고, 앞뒤로는 기마 호위병들이 따르고요."]], [["\"We often walk in the Tuileries Gardens, for they are lovely, though the antique Luxembourg Gardens suit me better.", "\"우리는 종종 튀일리 정원을 거닐어요. 거기가 사랑스럽거든요."], ["Père la Chaise is very curious, for many of the tombs are like small rooms, and, looking in, one sees a table, with images or pictures of the dead, and chairs for the mourners to sit in when they come to lament.", "그래도 저는 고풍스러운 뤽상부르 정원이 더 마음에 들어요. 페르 라셰즈는 무척 신기해요."], ["That is so Frenchy.", "무덤 가운데 상당수가 작은 방 같아서, 들여다보면 탁자가 있고, 그 위에 고인의 조각상이나 초상화가 있고, 애도하러 온 사람들이 앉아서 슬퍼할 수 있게 의자도 있거든요. 그게 참 프랑스답죠."]], [["\"Our rooms are on the Rue de Rivoli, and, sitting in the balcony, we look up and down the long, brilliant street. It is so pleasant that we spend our evenings talking there, when too tired with our day's work to go out. Fred is very entertaining, and is altogether the most agreeable young man I ever knew,--except Laurie, whose manners are more charming. I wish Fred was dark, for I don't fancy light men; however, the Vaughns are very rich, and come of an excellent family, so I won't find fault with their yellow hair, as my own is yellower.", "\"우리 방은 리볼리 거리에 있어서, 발코니에 앉으면 길고 화려한 거리를 위아래로 내다볼 수 있어요. 어찌나 기분 좋은지, 낮 동안 너무 지쳐서 밖에 못 나갈 때면 저녁을 거기서 이야기를 나누며 보내요. 프레드는 무척 재미있는 사람이라, 제가 여태 알던 청년 중에 통틀어 가장 유쾌해요. 로리만 빼고요. 그 애 매너가 더 매력적이거든요. 프레드가 머리색이 짙었으면 좋았을 텐데. 저는 머리 밝은 남자는 별로거든요. 그래도 본 집안은 무척 부자고 훌륭한 가문 출신이니, 그 노란 머리를 탓하진 않을래요. 제 머리가 더 노라니까요."]], [["\"Next week we are off to Germany and Switzerland; and, as we shall travel fast, I shall only be able to give you hasty letters.", "\"다음 주에는 독일이랑 스위스로 떠나요. 빠르게 이동할 테니, 급하게 쓴 편지밖에 못 보내 드릴 거예요."], ["I keep my diary, and try to 'remember correctly and describe clearly all that I see and admire,'", "저는 일기를 쓰면서, 아버지가 일러 주신 대로 '내가 보고 감탄하는 모든 것을 정확히 기억하고 똑똑히 묘사'하려고 애쓰고 있어요. 저한테 좋은 연습이 되고, 스케치북이랑 같이 보면 이런 끄적임보다 제 여행을 훨씬 잘 그려 보실 수 있을 거예요."], ["as father advised.", "\"안녕히 계세요."], ["It is good practice for me, and, with my sketch-book, will give you a better idea of my tour than these scribbles.", "다정하게 안아 드려요. 당신들의 친구.\""]], [["\"Adieu; I embrace you tenderly. VOTRE AMIE.\"", "\"사랑하는 엄마에게, \"하이델베르크."]], [["\"Having a quiet hour before we leave for Berne, I'll try to tell you what has happened, for some of it is very important, as you will see.", "\"베른으로 떠나기 전에 조용한 시간이 좀 나서, 그동안 무슨 일이 있었는지 들려드릴게요. 보시면 아시겠지만, 그중엔 무척 중요한 일도 있거든요."]], [["\"The sail up the Rhine was perfect, and I just sat and enjoyed it with all my might.", "\"라인강을 거슬러 오르는 뱃길은 완벽해서, 저는 그저 앉아서 온 힘을 다해 즐겼어요."], ["Get father's old guide-books, and read about it; I haven't words beautiful enough to describe it.", "아버지의 낡은 안내서들을 꺼내서 그 강에 관해 읽어 보세요. 저는 그걸 묘사할 만큼 아름다운 말을 갖고 있지 못하거든요."], ["At Coblentz we had a lovely time, for some students from Bonn, with whom Fred got acquainted on the boat, gave us a serenade.", "코블렌츠에서는 즐거운 시간을 보냈는데, 프레드가 배에서 사귄 본 출신 대학생 몇 명이 우리한테 세레나데를 불러 줬거든요."], ["It was a moonlight night, and, about one o'clock, Flo and I were waked by the most delicious music under our windows.", "달밤이었는데, 새벽 한 시쯤 플로랑 저는 창문 아래서 들려오는 더없이 황홀한 음악에 잠을 깼어요."], ["We flew up, and hid behind the curtains; but sly peeps showed us Fred and the students singing away down below.", "우리는 벌떡 일어나 커튼 뒤에 숨었지만, 슬쩍 엿보니 프레드랑 대학생들이 저 아래서 노래하고 있더군요."], ["It was the most romantic thing I ever saw,--the river, the bridge of boats, the great fortress opposite, moonlight everywhere, and music fit to melt a heart of stone.", "여태 본 것 중 가장 낭만적인 광경이었어요. 강이며, 배로 이은 다리며, 맞은편의 거대한 요새며, 사방의 달빛이며, 돌 같은 마음도 녹일 만한 음악이라니."]], [["\"When they were done we threw down some flowers, and saw them scramble for them, kiss their hands to the invisible ladies, and go laughing away,--to smoke and drink beer, I suppose.", "\"그들이 노래를 마치자 우리는 꽃을 몇 송이 던져 주었고, 그들이 그걸 주우려고 다투다가, 보이지 않는 숙녀들에게 손에 입을 맞춰 보내고는 웃으며 사라지는 걸 봤어요. 담배 피우고 맥주 마시러 갔겠죠, 아마."], ["Next morning Fred showed me one of the crumpled flowers in his vest-pocket, and looked very sentimental.", "이튿날 아침 프레드가 조끼 주머니에 든 그 구겨진 꽃 한 송이를 보여 주며 무척 감상에 젖은 표정을 짓더군요."], ["I laughed at him, and said I didn't throw it, but Flo, which seemed to disgust him, for he tossed it out of the window, and turned sensible again.", "저는 그를 비웃으며 그건 제가 던진 게 아니라 플로가 던진 거라고 했어요. 그러자 그가 질색하는 눈치였는지 그걸 창밖으로 던져 버리고 다시 멀쩡해지더라고요."], ["I'm afraid I'm going to have trouble with that boy, it begins to look like it.", "아무래도 저 애 때문에 골치 좀 썩을 것 같아요. 그런 낌새가 보이기 시작했거든요."]], [["\"The baths at Nassau were very gay, so was Baden-Baden, where Fred lost some money, and I scolded him.", "\"나사우의 온천은 무척 흥겨웠고, 바덴바덴도 그랬어요. 거기서 프레드가 돈을 좀 잃기에 제가 야단을 쳤어요."], ["He needs some one to look after him when Frank is not with him.", "프랭크가 곁에 없을 땐 누군가 그 애를 돌봐 줘야 해요."], ["Kate said once she hoped he'd marry soon, and I quite agree with her that it would be well for him.", "케이트가 언젠가 그 애가 어서 결혼했으면 좋겠다고 했는데, 그게 그 애한테 좋을 거라는 말엔 저도 전적으로 동감이에요."], ["Frankfort was delightful; I saw Goethe's house, Schiller's statue, and Dannecker's famous 'Ariadne.'", "프랑크푸르트는 아주 즐거웠어요. 괴테의 집이랑 실러의 동상, 그리고 다네커의 유명한 '아리아드네'를 봤거든요."], ["It was very lovely, but I should have enjoyed it more if I had known the story better.", "무척 사랑스러웠지만, 그 이야기를 더 잘 알았더라면 훨씬 더 즐겼을 거예요."], ["I didn't like to ask, as every one knew it, or pretended they did.", "다들 알거나 아는 척하는 통에 물어보기가 싫었거든요."], ["I wish Jo would tell me all about it; I ought to have read more, for I find I don't know anything, and it mortifies me.", "조 언니가 그 이야기를 죄다 들려줬으면 좋겠어요. 책을 더 많이 읽었어야 했는데, 제가 아는 게 아무것도 없다는 걸 깨닫고 나니 창피해요."]], [["\"Now comes the serious part,--for it happened here, and Fred is just gone.", "\"이제 심각한 부분이 나와요. 그게 여기서 일어났고, 프레드는 방금 떠났거든요."], ["He has been so kind and jolly that we all got quite fond of him; I never thought of anything but a travelling friendship, till the serenade night.", "그 애가 어찌나 친절하고 유쾌한지 우리 모두 그 애를 무척 좋아하게 됐어요. 세레나데가 있던 밤 전까지만 해도, 저는 여행 중에 나누는 우정 말고는 아무 생각도 안 했거든요."], ["Since then I've begun to feel that the moonlight walks, balcony talks, and daily adventures were something more to him than fun.", "그런데 그 뒤로, 그 달빛 산책이며 발코니에서 나눈 이야기며 날마다 겪는 모험들이 그 애한테는 단순한 재미 이상이라는 걸 느끼기 시작했어요. 전 시시덕거리지 않았어요, 엄마, 정말이에요."], ["I haven't flirted, mother, truly, but remembered what you said to me, and have done my very best.", "엄마가 저한테 해 주신 말씀을 기억하면서 정말 최선을 다했거든요. 사람들이 절 좋아하는 걸 제가 어쩌겠어요."], ["I can't help it if people like me; I don't try to make them, and it worries me if I don't care for them, though Jo says I haven't got any heart.", "일부러 그러게 만들지도 않고, 제가 그 사람들한테 마음이 없으면 그게 또 마음에 걸리는걸요. 조 언니는 제가 마음이라곤 없다고 하지만요."], ["Now I know mother will shake her head, and the girls say, 'Oh, the mercenary little wretch!'", "자, 엄마가 고개를 저으실 거고, 언니들은 '아, 저 돈만 밝히는 못된 것!' 하고 말할 줄 알아요."], ["but I've made up my mind, and, if Fred asks me, I shall accept him, though I'm not madly in love.", "그래도 저는 마음을 정했어요. 프레드가 청혼하면 저는 받아들일 거예요."], ["I like him, and we get on comfortably together.", "미친 듯이 사랑하는 건 아니지만요."], ["He is handsome, young, clever enough, and very rich,--ever so much richer than the Laurences.", "저는 그 애가 좋고, 우리는 함께 있으면 편안하게 잘 지내거든요. 그 애는 잘생기고, 젊고, 머리도 제법 좋고, 무척 부자예요."], ["I don't think his family would object, and I should be very happy, for they are all kind, well-bred, generous people, and they like me.", "로런스 집안보다 훨씬 더 부유하죠. 그 애 가족도 반대하지 않을 것 같고, 저는 무척 행복할 거예요."], ["Fred, as the eldest twin, will have the estate, I suppose, and such a splendid one as it is!", "다들 친절하고 예의 바르고 너그러운 사람들이고, 절 좋아하니까요. 프레드가 쌍둥이 형이니까 영지를 물려받겠죠, 아마."], ["A city house in a fashionable street, not so showy as our big houses, but twice as comfortable, and full of solid luxury, such as English people believe in.", "그게 어찌나 훌륭한 영지인지! 멋들어진 거리에 있는 시내 저택은 우리네 큰 집들만큼 화려하진 않아도 두 배는 더 안락하고, 영국 사람들이 신봉하는 그런 든든한 호사로 가득해요."], ["I like it, for it's genuine.", "그게 가짜가 아니라 진짜라 저는 마음에 들어요."], ["I've seen the plate, the family jewels, the old servants, and pictures of the country place, with its park, great house, lovely grounds, and fine horses.", "은식기며 가문의 보석이며 늙은 하인들이며, 사유지와 그곳의 정원과 큰 저택, 사랑스러운 부지와 멋진 말들을 담은 그림도 봤어요."], ["Oh, it would be all I should ask!", "아, 그거면 제가 바랄 게 더 없겠어요!"], ["and I'd rather have it than any title such as girls snap up so readily, and find nothing behind.", "여자애들이 그토록 덥석 채 가지만 막상 뒤에는 아무것도 없는 그런 작위 따위보다 저는 그게 더 좋아요."], ["I may be mercenary, but I hate poverty, and don't mean to bear it a minute longer than I can help.", "제가 돈을 밝히는 건지도 모르지만, 저는 가난이 싫고, 어쩔 수 없는 경우가 아니라면 단 한순간도 더 견딜 마음이 없어요."], ["One of us _must_ marry well; Meg didn't, Jo won't, Beth can't yet, so I shall, and make everything cosey all round.", "우리 중 누군가는 잘 결혼해야 해요. 메그 언니는 그러지 못했고, 조 언니는 안 할 거고, 베스 언니는 아직 못 하니, 제가 해서 두루두루 다 편안하게 만들어야죠."], ["I wouldn't marry a man I hated or despised.", "제가 미워하거나 경멸하는 남자랑은 결혼하지 않을 거예요. 그건 확실해요."], ["You may be sure of that; and, though Fred is not my model hero, he does very well, and, in time, I should get fond enough of him if he was very fond of me, and let me do just as I liked.", "그리고 프레드가 제 이상형은 아니지만, 그 애 정도면 무척 괜찮아요. 그 애가 저를 무척 사랑해서 제가 하고 싶은 대로 다 하게 내버려 둔다면, 시간이 지나면서 저도 그 애를 충분히 좋아하게 될 거예요."], ["So I've been turning the matter over in my mind the last week, for it was impossible to help seeing that Fred liked me.", "그래서 지난 한 주 동안 이 일을 곰곰이 곱씹어 봤어요. 프레드가 절 좋아한다는 걸 모를 수가 없었거든요."], ["He said nothing, but little things showed it; he never goes with Flo, always gets on my side of the carriage, table, or promenade, looks sentimental when we are alone, and frowns at any one else who ventures to speak to me.", "말로는 아무 내색도 안 했지만, 자잘한 것들이 그걸 드러냈어요. 그 앤 절대 플로랑 같이 안 가고 늘 마차나 식탁이나 산책길에서 제 옆자리를 차지하고, 우리 둘만 있으면 감상에 젖은 표정을 짓고, 다른 누가 저한테 감히 말이라도 걸려고 하면 인상을 찡그려요."], ["Yesterday, at dinner, when an Austrian officer stared at us, and then said something to his friend,--a rakish-looking baron,--about '_ein wonderschönes Blöndchen_,'", "어제 저녁 식사 때, 어느 오스트리아 장교가 우리를 빤히 쳐다보더니 방탕해 보이는 남작인 자기 친구한테 '아인 분더쇠네스 블뢴트헨'"], ["Fred looked as fierce as a lion, and cut his meat so savagely, it nearly flew off his plate.", "어쩌고 하니까, 프레드가 사자처럼 사납게 노려보면서 고기를 어찌나 험악하게 써는지 접시에서 튀어나갈 뻔했어요."], ["He isn't one of the cool, stiff Englishmen, but is rather peppery, for he has Scotch blood in him, as one might guess from his bonnie blue eyes.", "그 앤 차갑고 뻣뻣한 영국 남자가 아니라, 오히려 다혈질이에요. 그 어여쁜 파란 눈만 봐도 짐작할 수 있듯이, 스코틀랜드 피가 흐르거든요."]], [["\"Well, last evening we went up to the castle about sunset,--at least all of us but Fred, who was to meet us there, after going to the Post Restante for letters. We had a charming time poking about the ruins, the vaults where the monster tun is, and the beautiful gardens made by the elector, long ago, for his English wife. I liked the great terrace best, for the view was divine; so, while the rest went to see the rooms inside, I sat there trying to sketch the gray stone lion's head on the wall, with scarlet woodbine sprays hanging round it. I felt as if I'd got into a romance, sitting there, watching the Neckar rolling through the valley, listening to the music of the Austrian band below, and waiting for my lover, like a real story-book girl. I had a feeling that something was going to happen, and I was ready for it. I didn't feel blushy or quakey, but quite cool, and only a little excited.", "* 아인 분더쇠네스 블뢴트헨: 독일어로 '몹시 아름다운 금발 아가씨'라는 뜻. 옮긴이. \"그래서 어젯밤 우리는 해 질 무렵 성에 올라갔어요. 적어도 프레드만 빼고 다요. 그 앤 우체국에 편지를 찾으러 갔다가 거기서 우리랑 만나기로 했거든요. 우리는 폐허며, 그 괴물 같은 거대한 술통이 있는 지하실이며, 오래전에 선제후가 영국인 아내를 위해 만든 아름다운 정원을 둘러보며 매혹적인 시간을 보냈어요. 저는 그 커다란 테라스가 제일 좋았어요. 경치가 황홀했거든요. 그래서 다들 안쪽 방들을 보러 간 사이, 저는 거기 앉아 벽에 새겨진 회색 돌 사자 머리를 그려 보려고 했어요. 그 둘레로는 진홍빛 인동덩굴 가지들이 늘어져 있었죠. 거기 앉아 골짜기를 굽이쳐 흐르는 네카어강을 바라보고, 아래쪽 오스트리아 악단의 음악을 들으며, 진짜 이야기책 속 소녀처럼 제 연인을 기다리고 있자니, 마치 소설 속에라도 들어온 기분이었어요. 뭔가 일어날 것 같은 예감이 들었고, 저는 그럴 채비가 되어 있었죠. 얼굴이 붉어지거나 떨리지도 않고, 아주 침착하게, 그저 조금 들떠 있을 뿐이었어요."]], [["\"By and by I heard Fred's voice, and then he came hurrying through the great arch to find me. He looked so troubled that I forgot all about myself, and asked what the matter was. He said he'd just got a letter begging him to come home, for Frank was very ill; so he was going at once, in the night train, and only had time to say good-by. I was very sorry for him, and disappointed for myself, but only for a minute, because he said, as he shook hands,--and said it in a way that I could not mistake,--'I shall soon come back; you won't forget me, Amy?'", "\"이윽고 프레드의 목소리가 들리더니, 그 애가 절 찾아 그 큰 아치를 지나 부랴부랴 다가왔어요. 어찌나 근심스러운 얼굴이던지, 저는 제 일 따위는 까맣게 잊고 무슨 일이냐고 물었어요. 방금 편지를 받았는데 어서 집으로 오라는 내용이래요. 프랭크가 위독하다고요. 그래서 그 앤 그 길로 밤 기차를 타고 떠나야 한다며, 작별 인사를 할 시간밖에 없었어요. 저는 그 애가 무척 안됐고, 저 스스로도 실망스러웠지만, 그것도 잠시뿐이었어요. 그 애가 제 손을 잡으며, 그것도 오해할 수 없는 어조로 이렇게 말했거든요. '곧 돌아올게요. 저를 잊지 않을 거죠, 에이미?'"]], [["\"I didn't promise, but I looked at him, and he seemed satisfied, and there was no time for anything but messages and good-byes, for he was off in an hour, and we all miss him very much. I know he wanted to speak, but I think, from something he once hinted, that he had promised his father not to do anything of the sort yet awhile, for he is a rash boy, and the old gentleman dreads a foreign daughter-in-law. We shall soon meet in Rome; and then, if I don't change my mind, I'll say 'Yes, thank you,' when he says 'Will you, please?'", "\"저는 약속하진 않았지만, 그 애를 바라보았고, 그 앤 흡족해 하는 눈치였어요. 그러고는 전갈을 남기고 작별 인사를 나누는 것 말곤 다른 걸 할 틈이 없었어요. 한 시간 만에 떠나 버렸거든요. 우리 모두 그 애를 무척 그리워해요. 그 애가 말을 하고 싶어 했다는 건 알지만, 언젠가 그 애가 넌지시 비친 말로 미루어 짐작건대, 한동안은 그런 일을 하지 않겠다고 아버지께 약속한 것 같아요. 그 애가 워낙 무모한 데다, 그 노신사가 외국인 며느리를 몹시 꺼리거든요. 우리는 곧 로마에서 만날 거예요. 그때 가서 제 마음이 변하지 않는다면, 그 애가 '제발 저랑 결혼해 줄래요?' 하고 물을 때 저는 '네, 고마워요' 하고 답할 거예요."]], [["\"Of course this is all _very private_, but I wished you to know what was going on.", "\"물론 이건 전부 극비예요. 그래도 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 엄마는 알고 계셨으면 했어요."], ["Don't be anxious about me; remember I am your 'prudent Amy,'", "저 때문에 걱정 마세요. 제가 엄마의 '신중한 에이미'라는 거 기억하시고, 절대 경솔하게 굴지 않을 테니 안심하세요."], ["and be sure I will do nothing rashly.", "충고는 얼마든지 보내 주세요."], ["Send me as much advice as you like; I'll use it if I can.", "할 수 있으면 따를게요."], ["I wish I could see you for a good talk, Marmee.", "엄마랑 마주 앉아 실컷 이야기 나눌 수 있다면 좋겠어요."], ["Love and trust me.", "절 사랑하고 믿어 주세요."]], [["\"Ever your AMY.\"", "\"언제까지나 엄마의 에이미.\""]], [["\"Jo, I'm anxious about Beth.\"", "\"조, 베스가 걱정이구나.\""]], [["\"Why, mother, she has seemed unusually well since the babies came.\"", "\"왜요, 엄마? 아기들이 태어난 뒤로 베스는 유난히 좋아 보이던데요.\""]], [["\"It's not her health that troubles me now; it's her spirits.", "\"지금 내가 걱정하는 건 그 애 건강이 아니라 마음이란다."], ["I'm sure there is something on her mind, and I want you to discover what it is.\"", "무언가 마음에 걸리는 게 있는 게 분명한데, 네가 그게 뭔지 알아내 줬으면 싶구나.\""]], [["\"What makes you think so, mother?\"", "\"왜 그렇게 생각하세요, 엄마?\""]], [["\"She sits alone a good deal, and doesn't talk to her father as much as she used.", "\"혼자 앉아 있을 때가 많고, 예전만큼 아버지하고 이야기도 안 하더구나."], ["I found her crying over the babies the other day.", "며칠 전엔 아기들을 보며 우는 걸 봤단다."], ["When she sings, the songs are always sad ones, and now and then I see a look in her face that I don't understand.", "노래를 부를 때면 늘 슬픈 곡들이고, 이따금 그 애 얼굴에서 도무지 알 수 없는 표정이 스치곤 해."], ["This isn't like Beth, and it worries me.\"", "이건 베스답지 않아서, 마음이 쓰인단다.\""]], [["\"Have you asked her about it?\"", "\"엄마가 그 애한테 물어보셨어요?\""]], [["\"I have tried once or twice; but she either evaded my questions, or looked so distressed that I stopped.", "\"한두 번 해 봤지. 그런데 내 물음을 슬쩍 피하거나, 너무 괴로운 표정을 짓기에 그만뒀단다."], ["I never force my children's confidence, and I seldom have to wait for it long.\"", "나는 내 아이들이 속내를 털어놓도록 억지로 다그치는 법이 없고, 그걸 오래 기다려야 했던 적도 드물거든.\""]], [["Mrs.", "마치 부인이 말하면서 조를 흘낏 보았지만, 맞은편 얼굴은 베스의 일 말고는 그 어떤 은밀한 근심도 알지 못하는 듯했다."], ["March glanced at Jo as she spoke, but the face opposite seemed quite unconscious of any secret disquietude but Beth's; and, after sewing thoughtfully for a minute, Jo said,--", "잠시 생각에 잠겨 바느질하던 조가 입을 열었다."]], [["\"I think she is growing up, and so begins to dream dreams, and have hopes and fears and fidgets, without knowing why, or being able to explain them.", "\"제 생각엔 그 애가 자라고 있어서, 그래서 까닭도 모르고 설명도 못 하면서 이런저런 꿈을 꾸고, 희망이며 두려움이며 안절부절못하는 마음을 품기 시작한 것 같아요."], ["Why, mother, Beth's eighteen, but we don't realize it, and treat her like a child, forgetting she's a woman.\"", "글쎄, 엄마, 베스가 열여덟이잖아요. 그런데 우린 그걸 실감하지 못하고 그 애가 어엿한 여자라는 걸 잊은 채 어린애처럼 대하고 있어요.\""]], [["\"So she is.", "\"정말 그렇구나."], ["Dear heart, how fast you do grow up,\"", "아이고, 너희가 어쩜 이리도 빨리 자라는지.\""], ["returned her mother, with a sigh and a smile.", "어머니가 한숨과 미소를 함께 지으며 대답했다."]], [["\"Can't be helped, Marmee, so you must resign yourself to all sorts of worries, and let your birds hop out of the nest, one by one.", "\"어쩔 수 없잖아요, 엄마. 그러니 온갖 걱정거리에 단단히 마음을 다잡으시고, 새끼 새들이 둥지 밖으로 한 마리씩 폴짝폴짝 날아가게 내버려 두셔야죠."], ["I promise never to hop very far, if that is any comfort to you.\"", "저는 절대 멀리는 안 날아가겠다고 약속할게요. 그게 조금이나마 위안이 되신다면요.\""]], [["\"It is a great comfort, Jo; I always feel strong when you are at home, now Meg is gone.", "\"큰 위안이 되고말고, 조. 메그가 떠난 지금, 네가 집에 있으면 나는 늘 든든하단다."], ["Beth is too feeble and Amy too young to depend upon; but when the tug comes, you are always ready.\"", "베스는 너무 약하고 에이미는 너무 어려서 기댈 수가 없는데, 막상 힘든 고비가 닥치면 너는 언제나 채비가 되어 있으니까.\""]], [["\"Why, you know I don't mind hard jobs much, and there must always be one scrub in a family.", "\"아, 제가 고된 일은 별로 마다하지 않잖아요. 게다가 집안에는 늘 궂은일 도맡을 사람이 하나쯤 있어야 하고요."], ["Amy is splendid in fine works, and I'm not; but I feel in my element when all the carpets are to be taken up, or half the family fall sick at once.", "에이미는 섬세한 일에는 빼어나지만 저는 아니에요. 그래도 온 집 안 양탄자를 다 걷어 내야 한다거나, 식구 절반이 한꺼번에 앓아눕거나 할 때면 저는 물 만난 기분이 들거든요."], ["Amy is distinguishing herself abroad; but if anything is amiss at home, I'm your man.\"", "에이미는 외국에서 두각을 나타내고 있지만, 집에 무슨 일이 생기면 제가 적임자죠.\""]], [["\"I leave Beth to your hands, then, for she will open her tender little heart to her Jo sooner than to any one else.", "\"그럼 베스는 네 손에 맡기마. 그 애는 다른 누구보다도 너 조한테 그 여린 마음을 더 빨리 열 테니까."], ["Be very kind, and don't let her think any one watches or talks about her.", "아주 다정하게 대해 주고, 누가 자기를 지켜보거나 자기 얘기를 한다는 생각이 들지 않게 해 주렴."], ["If she only would get quite strong and cheerful again, I shouldn't have a wish in the world.\"", "그 애가 다시 아주 튼튼하고 명랑해지기만 한다면, 나는 이 세상에 더 바랄 게 없겠다.\""]], [["\"Happy woman!", "\"행복한 분이시네요!"], ["I've got heaps.\"", "저는 바라는 게 수두룩한데.\""]], [["\"My dear, what are they?\"", "\"얘야, 그게 뭔데?\""]], [["\"I'll settle Bethy's troubles, and then I'll tell you mine.", "\"베스 일부터 해결하고 나서 제 걸 말씀드릴게요."], ["They are not very wearing, so they'll keep;\"", "그렇게 닳아 없어질 것들은 아니니까, 좀 묵혀 둬도 괜찮아요.\""], ["and Jo stitched away, with a wise nod which set her mother's heart at rest about her, for the present at least.", "조가 슬기로운 고갯짓과 함께 바느질을 계속하자, 어머니의 마음은 적어도 당분간은 조에 대한 걱정을 내려놓고 놓였다."]], [["While apparently absorbed in her own affairs, Jo watched Beth; and, after many conflicting conjectures, finally settled upon one which seemed to explain the change in her.", "겉으로는 제 일에 푹 빠진 듯하면서도, 조는 베스를 지켜보았다. 그리고 서로 엇갈리는 온갖 추측 끝에, 마침내 동생의 변화를 설명해 줄 듯한 한 가지에 생각이 닿았다."], ["A slight incident gave Jo the clue to the mystery, she thought, and lively fancy, loving heart did the rest.", "사소한 사건 하나가 조에게 그 수수께끼의 실마리를 던져 주었다고 그녀는 여겼고, 발랄한 상상력과 사랑 가득한 마음이 나머지를 채웠다."], ["She was affecting to write busily one Saturday afternoon, when she and Beth were alone together; yet as she scribbled, she kept her eye on her sister, who seemed unusually quiet.", "어느 토요일 오후, 조는 베스와 단둘이 있을 때 바삐 글을 쓰는 척하고 있었다. 그러나 끄적이는 와중에도 유난히 조용한 동생에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다."], ["Sitting at the window, Beth's work often dropped into her lap, and she leaned her head upon her hand, in a dejected attitude, while her eyes rested on the dull, autumnal landscape.", "창가에 앉은 베스는 일감을 자꾸 무릎에 떨어뜨리고는, 한 손에 머리를 괴고 풀 죽은 자세로, 칙칙한 가을 풍경에 눈길을 머문 채였다."], ["Suddenly some one passed below, whistling like an operatic blackbird, and a voice called out,--", "갑자기 누군가가 오페라에 나오는 검은지빠귀처럼 휘파람을 불며 아래쪽을 지나갔고, 한 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["\"All serene!", "\"별일 없지!"], ["Coming in to-night.\"", "오늘 밤에 들를게.\""]], [["Beth started, leaned forward, smiled and nodded, watched the passer-by till his quick tramp died away, then said softly, as if to herself,--", "베스는 깜짝 놀라 몸을 앞으로 기울이더니, 미소 지으며 고개를 끄덕이고는, 지나가는 그를 가만히 바라보았다. 그의 빠른 발소리가 멀어져 사라질 때까지 지켜보다가, 혼잣말처럼 나직이 말했다."]], [["\"How strong and well and happy that dear boy looks.\"", "\"저 사랑스러운 애는 어쩜 저렇게 튼튼하고 건강하고 행복해 보일까.\""]], [["\"Hum!\"", "\"흠!\""], ["said Jo, still intent upon her sister's face; for the bright color faded as quickly as it came, the smile vanished, and presently a tear lay shining on the window-ledge.", "조는 여전히 언니의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 말했다. 밝게 떠올랐던 홍조가 떠오를 때만큼이나 빠르게 사라지고, 미소도 가시더니, 이내 눈물 한 방울이 창틀 위에서 반짝이며 맺혔다."], ["Beth whisked it off, and glanced apprehensively at Jo; but she was scratching away at a tremendous rate, apparently engrossed in \"Olympia's Oath.\"", "베스는 얼른 그것을 훔쳐 내고는 불안한 듯 조를 흘끔 보았다. 하지만 조는 무서운 속도로 펜을 놀리며 「올림피아의 맹세」에 푹 빠져 있는 듯했다."], ["The instant Beth turned, Jo began her watch again, saw Beth's hand go quietly to her eyes more than once, and, in her half-averted face, read a tender sorrow that made her own eyes fill.", "베스가 고개를 돌리는 순간, 조는 다시 언니를 살피기 시작했고, 베스의 손이 한 번 이상 조용히 눈가로 올라가는 것을, 그리고 반쯤 돌린 얼굴에서 자신의 눈마저 젖게 만드는 애틋한 슬픔을 읽어 냈다."], ["Fearing to betray herself, she slipped away, murmuring something about needing more paper.", "속마음을 들킬까 두려워, 조는 종이가 더 필요하다는 둥 뭐라 중얼거리며 슬그머니 자리를 떴다."]], [["\"Mercy on me, Beth loves Laurie!\"", "\"세상에, 베스가 로리를 사랑하는 거야!\""], ["she said, sitting down in her own room, pale with the shock of the discovery which she believed she had just made.", "조는 자기 방으로 들어가 앉으며 말했다. 방금 알아냈다고 믿은 그 사실의 충격으로 얼굴이 새하얗게 질려 있었다."], ["\"I never dreamt of such a thing.", "\"그런 건 꿈에도 생각 못 했어."], ["What _will_ mother say?", "엄마는 뭐라고 하실까?"], ["I wonder if he--\"", "혹시 그 애도——\""], ["there Jo stopped, and turned scarlet with a sudden thought.", "거기서 조는 말을 멈추더니, 문득 떠오른 생각에 얼굴이 새빨개졌다."], ["\"If he shouldn't love back again, how dreadful it would be.", "\"만약 그 애가 같은 마음이 아니라면, 얼마나 끔찍할까. 그 애도 사랑해야 해."], ["He must; I'll make him!\"", "내가 그렇게 만들 거야!\""], ["and she shook her head threateningly at the picture of the mischievous-looking boy laughing at her from the wall.", "그러면서 조는 벽에서 자신을 보고 웃고 있는 장난기 가득한 소년의 사진을 향해 위협하듯 고개를 흔들었다. \"어휴, 우리 정말 무섭게도 자라 버렸네."], ["\"Oh dear, we _are_ growing up with a vengeance.", "메그는 결혼해서 엄마가 됐고, 에이미는 파리에서 잘나가고 있고, 베스는 사랑에 빠졌어."], ["Here's Meg married and a mamma, Amy flourishing away at Paris, and Beth in love.", "사고나 안 치고 정신 똑바로 차린 건 나뿐이잖아.\" 조는 사진에 눈을 고정한 채 잠시 골똘히 생각하더니, 찌푸렸던 이마를 펴고, 맞은편 얼굴을 향해 단호하게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["I'm the only one that has sense enough to keep out of mischief.\"", "\"아뇨, 됐어요, 선생님. 당신은 무척 매력적이지만, 바람개비만큼이나 줏대가 없거든요."], ["Jo thought intently for a minute, with her eyes fixed on the picture; then she smoothed out her wrinkled forehead, and said, with a decided nod at the face opposite, \"No, thank you, sir; you're very charming, but you've no more stability than a weathercock; so you needn't write touching notes, and smile in that insinuating way, for it won't do a bit of good, and I won't have it.\"", "그러니 가슴 뭉클한 쪽지를 써 보내거나, 그런 식으로 은근슬쩍 미소 지을 필요 없어요. 아무 소용 없을 테고, 난 그런 거 안 받을 테니까.\""]], [["Then she sighed, and fell into a reverie, from which she did not wake till the early twilight sent her down to take new observations, which only confirmed her suspicion.", "그러고 나서 조는 한숨을 쉬며 깊은 상념에 잠겼다. 이른 황혼이 그녀를 아래층으로 내려보내 새로운 관찰을 하게 만들 때까지 그 상념에서 깨어나지 못했고, 그 관찰은 그녀의 의심을 더욱 굳혀 줄 뿐이었다."], ["Though Laurie flirted with Amy and joked with Jo, his manner to Beth had always been peculiarly kind and gentle, but so was everybody's; therefore, no one thought of imagining that he cared more for her than for the others.", "로리가 에이미와 시시덕거리고 조와 농담을 주고받기는 했지만, 베스를 대하는 태도만큼은 늘 유난히 다정하고 부드러웠다. 그러나 사람들 모두가 그랬으므로, 그가 베스를 다른 자매들보다 더 마음에 두고 있으리라고는 아무도 상상하지 못했다."], ["Indeed, a general impression had prevailed in the family, of late, that \"our boy\"", "사실 요즘 들어 가족들 사이에는 \"우리 애\"가 조를 그 어느 때보다 좋아하고 있다는 인상이 널리 퍼져 있었다."], ["was getting fonder than ever of Jo, who, however, wouldn't hear a word upon the subject, and scolded violently if any one dared to suggest it.", "다만 조는 그 이야기를 한마디도 들으려 하지 않았고, 누가 감히 그런 말을 꺼내기라도 하면 사납게 쏘아붙였다."], ["If they had known the various tender passages of the past year, or rather attempts at tender passages which had been nipped in the bud, they would have had the immense satisfaction of saying, \"I told you so.\"", "만약 지난 한 해 동안 오갔던 갖가지 애틋한 장면들을, 아니, 싹도 트기 전에 잘려 나간 애틋한 장면들의 시도를 가족들이 알았더라면, \"그것 봐, 내가 뭐랬어\" 하고 말하는 어마어마한 만족감을 누렸을 것이다."], ["But Jo hated \"philandering,\" and wouldn't allow it, always having a joke or a smile ready at the least sign of impending danger.", "그러나 조는 \"연애질\"을 질색했고 그런 걸 용납하지 않았으며, 위험이 닥칠 기미가 조금이라도 보이면 늘 농담이나 미소를 준비해 두었다."]], [["When Laurie first went to college, he fell in love about once a month; but these small flames were as brief as ardent, did no damage, and much amused Jo, who took great interest in the alternations of hope, despair, and resignation, which were confided to her in their weekly conferences.", "로리는 대학에 처음 들어갔을 무렵 한 달에 한 번꼴로 사랑에 빠졌다. 하지만 이런 작은 불꽃들은 뜨거운 만큼이나 짧았고, 아무런 피해도 입히지 않았으며, 조를 무척 즐겁게 해 주었다."], ["But there came a time when Laurie ceased to worship at many shrines, hinted darkly at one all-absorbing passion, and indulged occasionally in Byronic fits of gloom.", "조는 매주 둘이 나누는 대화에서 그가 털어놓는 희망과 절망과 체념의 교차를 흥미진진하게 들었다. 그런데 어느 시점에 이르러 로리는 여러 신전에 경배하기를 그만두고, 무언가 온통 마음을 사로잡는 열정에 대해 어둡게 암시하기 시작하더니, 이따금 바이런풍의 우울에 빠져들곤 했다."], ["Then he avoided the tender subject altogether, wrote philosophical notes to Jo, turned studious, and gave out that he was going to \"dig,\"", "그러고는 애틋한 화제를 아예 피하고, 조에게 철학적인 쪽지를 써 보내고, 면학에 매달렸으며, 화려한 영광 속에 졸업할 작정으로 \"파고들\" 거라고 공언했다."], ["intending to graduate in a blaze of glory.", "이것은 황혼녘의 속삭임이나 다정하게 손을 맞잡는 일이나 그윽한 눈길보다 조에게 훨씬 잘 맞았다."], ["This suited the young lady better than twilight confidences, tender pressures of the hand, and eloquent glances of the eye; for with Jo, brain developed earlier than heart, and she preferred imaginary heroes to real ones, because, when tired of them, the former could be shut up in the tin-kitchen till called for, and the latter were less manageable.", "조는 가슴보다 머리가 먼저 발달한 데다, 진짜 영웅보다 상상 속 영웅을 더 좋아했기 때문이다. 상상 속 영웅이란 싫증이 나면 부를 때까지 양철 부엌에 가둬 둘 수 있는데, 진짜 영웅은 그렇게 다루기가 만만치 않았다."]], [["Things were in this state when the grand discovery was made, and Jo watched Laurie that night as she had never done before.", "상황이 이러할 때 그 대단한 발견이 이루어졌고, 조는 그날 밤 일찍이 본 적 없는 눈으로 로리를 지켜보았다."], ["If she had not got the new idea into her head, she would have seen nothing unusual in the fact that Beth was very quiet, and Laurie very kind to her.", "만약 그 새로운 생각이 머릿속에 박혀 있지 않았더라면, 베스가 무척 조용하고 로리가 베스에게 무척 친절하다는 사실에서 아무런 이상함도 느끼지 못했을 것이다."], ["But having given the rein to her lively fancy, it galloped away with her at a great pace; and common sense, being rather weakened by a long course of romance writing, did not come to the rescue.", "그러나 발랄한 공상에 일단 고삐를 풀어 주자, 그 공상은 엄청난 속도로 조를 태우고 내달렸다. 그리고 오랫동안 연애 소설을 써 온 탓에 다소 약해진 상식은 그녀를 구하러 와 주지 않았다."], ["As usual, Beth lay on the sofa, and Laurie sat in a low chair close by, amusing her with all sorts of gossip; for she depended on her weekly \"spin,\" and he never disappointed her.", "여느 때처럼 베스는 소파에 누워 있었고, 로리는 그 곁의 낮은 의자에 앉아 온갖 잡담으로 그녀를 즐겁게 해 주고 있었다."], ["But that evening, Jo fancied that Beth's eyes rested on the lively, dark face beside her with peculiar pleasure, and that she listened with intense interest to an account of some exciting cricket-match, though the phrases, \"caught off a tice,\"", "베스는 매주 그의 \"수다\"에 기대를 걸었고, 그는 한 번도 그녀를 실망시키지 않았다. 그런데 그날 저녁, 조는 베스의 눈길이 곁에 있는 발랄하고 거무스레한 얼굴에 유난한 기쁨으로 머물러 있는 것처럼, 그리고 베스가 어느 흥미진진한 크리켓 경기 이야기를 강렬한 관심을 가지고 듣고 있는 것처럼 느꼈다."], ["\"stumped off his ground,\" and \"the leg hit for three,\"", "\"타이스에 걸려 아웃\", \"공이 빠져 스텀프 아웃\", \"레그 사이드로 쳐서 3점\""], ["were as intelligible to her as Sanscrit.", "같은 말들은 베스에게 산스크리트어만큼이나 알아들을 수 없는 것이었는데도 말이다."], ["She also fancied, having set her heart upon seeing it, that she saw a certain increase of gentleness in Laurie's manner, that he dropped his voice now and then, laughed less than usual, was a little absent-minded, and settled the afghan over Beth's feet with an assiduity that was really almost tender.", "또한 조는 그것을 꼭 보고야 말겠다고 마음먹은 터라, 로리의 태도에서 어떤 다정함이 더해진 것을, 그가 이따금 목소리를 낮추고, 평소보다 덜 웃고, 약간 넋이 나가 있고, 거의 다정하다 할 만큼 정성스레 베스의 발 위에 무릎담요를 덮어 주는 것을 본 것 같았다."]], [["\"Who knows?", "\"누가 알겠어?"], ["stranger things have happened,\"", "더 별난 일도 일어나는걸.\""], ["thought Jo, as she fussed about the room.", "조는 방 안을 부산스레 오가며 생각했다."], ["\"She will make quite an angel of him, and he will make life delightfully easy and pleasant for the dear, if they only love each other.", "\"베스라면 저 애를 완전한 천사로 만들 거고, 저 애는 우리 사랑스러운 베스를 위해 인생을 즐겁고 편안하게 만들어 줄 거야. 둘이 서로 사랑하기만 한다면 말이지."], ["I don't see how he can help it; and I do believe he would if the rest of us were out of the way.\"", "저 애가 안 그럴 리가 없잖아. 우리 나머지가 방해만 안 된다면 분명 그럴 거야.\""]], [["As every one _was_ out of the way but herself, Jo began to feel that she ought to dispose of herself with all speed.", "자신을 빼고는 모두가 방해되지 않는 처지였으므로, 조는 자기 자신을 한시바삐 치워 버려야겠다고 느끼기 시작했다."], ["But where should she go?", "그런데 어디로 가야 하나?"], ["and burning to lay herself upon the shrine of sisterly devotion, she sat down to settle that point.", "자매애라는 신전에 자기 몸을 바치고 싶어 안달이 난 조는, 그 문제를 정하려고 자리에 앉았다."]], [["Now, the old sofa was a regular patriarch of a sofa,--long, broad, well-cushioned, and low; a trifle shabby, as well it might be, for the girls had slept and sprawled on it as babies, fished over the back, rode on the arms, and had menageries under it as children, and rested tired heads, dreamed dreams, and listened to tender talk on it as young women. They all loved it, for it was a family refuge, and one corner had always been Jo's favorite lounging-place. Among the many pillows that adorned the venerable couch was one, hard, round, covered with prickly horsehair, and furnished with a knobby button at each end; this repulsive pillow was her especial property, being used as a weapon of defence, a barricade, or a stern preventive of too much slumber.", "그런데 그 낡은 소파는 소파 중에서도 어엿한 가장(家長)이었다. 길고, 넓고, 푹신하고, 낮았다. 다소 낡기는 했지만, 그럴 만도 한 것이, 아이들은 갓난아기였을 때 그 위에서 잠자고 뒹굴었고, 어린아이였을 때는 등받이 너머로 낚시질을 하고 팔걸이에 올라타고 그 밑에 동물원을 차렸으며, 처녀가 되어서는 그 위에서 지친 머리를 쉬고 꿈을 꾸고 다정한 이야기에 귀를 기울였다. 자매들은 모두 그 소파를 사랑했다. 그것은 가족의 안식처였고, 한쪽 구석은 늘 조가 가장 좋아하는 자리였다. 이 유서 깊은 소파를 장식한 여러 베개 가운데, 딱딱하고 둥글며 까슬까슬한 말총으로 덮여 있고 양 끝에 울퉁불퉁한 단추가 달린 것이 하나 있었다. 이 흉측한 베개야말로 조의 특별한 소유물이었다. 방어용 무기로, 바리케이드로, 혹은 지나친 졸음을 막는 엄중한 예방책으로 쓰였다."]], [["Laurie knew this pillow well, and had cause to regard it with deep aversion, having been unmercifully pummelled with it in former days, when romping was allowed, and now frequently debarred by it from taking the seat he most coveted, next to Jo in the sofa corner.", "로리는 이 베개를 잘 알았고, 그것을 깊이 혐오할 만한 이유가 있었다. 한바탕 뒹구는 놀이가 허용되던 옛날에는 그 베개로 무자비하게 두들겨 맞았고, 지금은 소파 구석 조의 옆자리, 그가 가장 탐내는 그 자리에 앉지 못하도록 번번이 그 베개에 가로막혔기 때문이다."], ["If \"the sausage\" as they called it, stood on end, it was a sign that he might approach and repose; but if it lay flat across the sofa, woe to the man, woman, or child who dared disturb it!", "그들이 \"소시지\"라고 부르던 그 베개가 세워져 있으면 그가 다가와 쉬어도 좋다는 신호였지만, 소파를 가로질러 납작하게 누워 있으면 감히 그것을 건드리는 남자든 여자든 아이든 화를 면치 못했다!"], ["That evening Jo forgot to barricade her corner, and had not been in her seat five minutes, before a massive form appeared beside her, and, with both arms spread over the sofa-back, both long legs stretched out before him, Laurie exclaimed, with a sigh of satisfaction,--", "그날 저녁 조는 자기 구석에 바리케이드 치는 것을 깜빡 잊었고, 자리에 앉은 지 채 오 분도 되지 않아, 옆에 떡 벌어진 체구가 나타나더니, 두 팔을 소파 등받이에 쫙 펴고 두 다리를 앞으로 길게 뻗으며 로리가 만족스러운 한숨과 함께 외쳤다."]], [["\"Now, _this_ is filling at the price.\"", "\"자, 이거야말로 값진 호사로군.\""]], [["\"No slang,\"", "\"속어 쓰지 마.\""], ["snapped Jo, slamming down the pillow.", "조가 쏘아붙이며 베개를 탁 내리쳤다. 하지만 이미 늦었다."], ["But it was too late, there was no room for it; and, coasting on to the floor, it disappeared in a most mysterious manner.", "베개를 둘 자리가 없었던 것이다. 베개는 바닥으로 미끄러져 내려가더니 도무지 알 수 없는 방식으로 사라져 버렸다."]], [["\"Come, Jo, don't be thorny.", "\"왜 그래, 조, 그렇게 가시 돋칠 거 없잖아."], ["After studying himself to a skeleton all the week, a fellow deserves petting, and ought to get it.\"", "일주일 내내 공부하느라 뼈만 남은 녀석은 떠받들어 줄 자격이 있고, 마땅히 그래야 하는 거라고.\""]], [["\"Beth will pet you; I'm busy.\"", "\"베스가 떠받들어 줄 거야. 난 바빠.\""]], [["\"No, she's not to be bothered with me; but you like that sort of thing, unless you've suddenly lost your taste for it.", "\"아냐, 베스를 나 때문에 귀찮게 하면 안 되지. 그리고 넌 그런 거 좋아하잖아."], ["Have you?", "갑자기 취향이 바뀐 게 아니라면 말이야."], ["Do you hate your boy, and want to fire pillows at him?\"", "그래? 넌 네 녀석이 미워서 베개나 던지고 싶은 거야?\""]], [["Anything more wheedlesome than that touching appeal was seldom heard, but Jo quenched \"her boy\" by turning on him with the stern query,--", "그 가슴 뭉클한 호소보다 더 알랑거리는 말은 좀처럼 듣기 힘들었지만, 조는 엄격한 질문으로 돌아서며 \"자기 녀석\"의 기를 꺾어 버렸다."]], [["\"How many bouquets have you sent Miss Randal this week?\"", "\"이번 주에 랜들 양한테 꽃다발 몇 개나 보냈어?\""]], [["\"Not one, upon my word.", "\"맹세코 하나도 안 보냈어."], ["She's engaged.", "그 여자 약혼했거든."], ["Now then.\"", "자, 됐지.\""]], [["\"I'm glad of it; that's one of your foolish extravagances,--sending flowers and things to girls for whom you don't care two pins,\"", "\"잘됐네. 그게 바로 네 어리석은 사치 중 하나야."], ["continued Jo reprovingly.", "눈곱만큼도 마음에 없는 여자애들한테 꽃이며 뭐며 보내는 거.\" 조가 나무라듯 말을 이었다."]], [["\"Sensible girls, for whom I do care whole papers of pins, won't let me send them 'flowers and things,'", "\"마음에 쏙 드는 분별 있는 여자애들은 나한테 '꽃이며 뭐며' 보내게 해 주질 않으니, 내가 어쩌겠어?"], ["so what can I do? My feelings must have a _went_.\"", "내 감정도 배출구가 있어야 할 거 아냐.\""]], [["\"Mother doesn't approve of flirting, even in fun; and you do flirt desperately, Teddy.\"", "\"엄마는 장난이라도 시시덕거리는 걸 좋게 안 보셔. 그런데 넌 정말 필사적으로 시시덕거리잖아, 테디.\""]], [["\"I'd give anything if I could answer, 'So do you.'", "\"'너도 그러잖아'라고 받아칠 수만 있다면 뭐든 내놓겠어."], ["As I can't, I'll merely say that I don't see any harm in that pleasant little game, if all parties understand that it's only play.\"", "그럴 수 없으니, 그냥 이 말만 할게. 관련된 모든 사람이 그게 그냥 놀이일 뿐이라는 걸 안다면, 그 즐거운 작은 놀이에 무슨 해가 있는지 모르겠다고.\""]], [["\"Well, it does look pleasant, but I can't learn how it's done.", "\"글쎄, 즐거워 보이긴 하는데, 난 그걸 어떻게 하는 건지 못 배우겠더라."], ["I've tried, because one feels awkward in company, not to do as everybody else is doing; but I don't seem to get on,\"", "사람들 틈에서 다들 하는 걸 안 하면 어색하니까 해 보긴 했는데, 영 늘질 않는 거 있지.\""], ["said Jo, forgetting to play Mentor.", "조가 멘토 노릇 하는 걸 깜빡한 채 말했다."]], [["\"Take lessons of Amy; she has a regular talent for it.\"", "\"에이미한테 배워. 걔는 그 방면에 아주 타고난 재능이 있으니까.\""]], [["\"Yes, she does it very prettily, and never seems to go too far.", "\"맞아, 걔는 그걸 아주 예쁘게 하고, 절대 도를 넘는 법이 없지."], ["I suppose it's natural to some people to please without trying, and others to always say and do the wrong thing in the wrong place.\"", "어떤 사람들은 애쓰지 않아도 남을 즐겁게 하는 게 타고나고, 또 어떤 사람들은 늘 엉뚱한 자리에서 엉뚱한 말과 행동을 하는 게 타고나나 봐.\""]], [["\"I'm glad you can't flirt; it's really refreshing to see a sensible, straightforward girl, who can be jolly and kind without making a fool of herself.", "\"난 네가 시시덕거리지 못하는 게 좋아. 분별 있고 솔직한 여자애가 자기 망신살 안 뻗치면서도 유쾌하고 다정할 수 있는 걸 보면 정말 신선하다니까."], ["Between ourselves, Jo, some of the girls I know really do go on at such a rate I'm ashamed of them.", "우리끼리 얘긴데, 조, 내가 아는 여자애들 중엔 정말 어찌나 심하게 구는지 내가 다 부끄러울 정도야."], ["They don't mean any harm, I'm sure; but if they knew how we fellows talked about them afterward, they'd mend their ways, I fancy.\"", "나쁜 뜻이 없는 건 분명한데, 우리 남자들이 나중에 자기들 얘기를 어떻게 떠드는지 알면 행실을 고칠 거야, 아마.\""]], [["\"They do the same; and, as their tongues are the sharpest, you fellows get the worst of it, for you are as silly as they, every bit. If you behaved properly, they would; but, knowing you like their nonsense, they keep it up, and then you blame them.\"", "\"여자애들도 똑같아. 게다가 여자들 혀가 더 매서우니까, 너희 남자들이 더 호되게 당하는 거지. 너희도 여자애들 못지않게 어리석거든, 하나부터 열까지. 너희가 제대로 처신하면 여자애들도 그럴 거야. 그런데 너희가 자기들 허튼짓을 좋아하는 걸 아니까 계속하는 거고, 그래 놓고 너흰 여자애들을 탓하잖아.\""]], [["\"Much you know about it, ma'am,\"", "\"네가 그쪽 일을 잘도 알겠다, 아가씨.\""], ["said Laurie, in a superior tone.", "로리가 잘난 체하는 어투로 말했다."], ["\"We don't like romps and flirts, though we may act as if we did sometimes.", "\"우린 가끔 그런 척할 때도 있지만, 까불대고 시시덕거리는 여자애들을 좋아하진 않아."], ["The pretty, modest girls are never talked about, except respectfully, among gentlemen.", "예쁘고 얌전한 여자애들은 신사들 사이에서 정중하게가 아니면 입에 오르내리지도 않는다고."], ["Bless your innocent soul!", "그 순진한 마음에 복이 있기를!"], ["If you could be in my place for a month you'd see things that would astonish you a trifle.", "네가 한 달만 내 입장이 되어 보면 좀 놀랄 만한 것들을 보게 될걸."], ["Upon my word, when I see one of those harum-scarum girls, I always want to say with our friend Cock Robin,--", "정말이지, 그 덜렁대는 여자애들 중 하나를 볼 때마다, 난 늘 우리 친구 콕 로빈처럼 말하고 싶어진다니까——"]], [["\"'Out upon you, fie upon you, Bold-faced jig!'\"", "'썩 꺼져라, 망측해라, 뻔뻔스러운 깡충이야!'\""]], [["It was impossible to help laughing at the funny conflict between Laurie's chivalrous reluctance to speak ill of womankind, and his very natural dislike of the unfeminine folly of which fashionable society showed him many samples. Jo knew that \"young Laurence\"", "여자라는 족속을 험담하기 꺼리는 로리의 기사도적 망설임과, 사교계가 숱하게 보여 준 여자답지 못한 어리석음에 대한 그의 지극히 자연스러운 혐오, 그 우스꽝스러운 충돌에 웃지 않을 도리가 없었다."], ["was regarded as a most eligible _parti_ by worldly mammas, was much smiled upon by their daughters, and flattered enough by ladies of all ages to make a coxcomb of him; so she watched him rather jealously, fearing he would be spoilt, and rejoiced more than she confessed to find that he still believed in modest girls.", "조는 \"젊은 로런스\"가 세속적인 어머니들에게 더없이 좋은 신랑감으로 여겨지고, 그 딸들에게서 숱한 미소를 받으며, 온갖 나이대의 여성들에게서 멋쟁이 허풍선이로 만들 만큼 충분히 떠받들어진다는 걸 알고 있었다. 그래서 그가 버릇이 잘못 들까 두려워 다소 질투 어린 눈으로 그를 지켜보았고, 그가 여전히 얌전한 여자애들을 믿고 있다는 걸 알고는 입 밖으로 낸 것보다 훨씬 더 기뻤다."], ["Returning suddenly to her admonitory tone, she said, dropping her voice, \"If you _must_ have a 'went,'", "갑자기 타이르는 어조로 돌아가, 조는 목소리를 낮추며 말했다."], ["Teddy, go and devote yourself to one of the 'pretty, modest girls'", "\"테디, 정 '배출구'가 있어야겠다면, 네가 정말 존경하는 그 '예쁘고 얌전한 여자애'"], ["whom you do respect, and not waste your time with the silly ones.\"", "중 하나에게 가서 정성을 쏟아. 어리석은 애들한테 시간 낭비하지 말고.\""]], [["\"You really advise it?\"", "\"정말 그러라고 권하는 거야?\""], ["and Laurie looked at her with an odd mixture of anxiety and merriment in his face.", "로리는 얼굴에 불안과 즐거움이 묘하게 뒤섞인 표정으로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["\"Yes, I do; but you'd better wait till you are through college, on the whole, and be fitting yourself for the place meantime.", "\"응, 그래. 하지만 전체적으로 보면 대학을 마칠 때까지 기다리는 게 낫겠어."], ["You're not half good enough for--well, whoever the modest girl maybe,\"", "그동안 그 자리에 어울리는 사람이 되도록 갈고닦으면서 말이야. 넌 그——그러니까, 그 얌전한 여자애가 누구든 간에, 그 절반만큼도 못한 사람이거든.\""], ["and Jo looked a little queer likewise, for a name had almost escaped her.", "조 역시 좀 묘한 표정이 되었는데, 하마터면 이름 하나가 입 밖으로 새어 나올 뻔했기 때문이다."]], [["\"That I'm not!\"", "\"맞아, 난 그래!\""], ["acquiesced Laurie, with an expression of humility quite new to him, as he dropped his eyes, and absently wound Jo's apron-tassel round his finger.", "로리는 그에게는 영 낯선 겸허한 표정으로 수긍하며, 눈을 내리깔고는 무심히 조의 앞치마 술을 손가락에 감았다."]], [["\"Mercy on us, this will never do,\"", "\"세상에, 이러면 안 되는데.\""], ["thought Jo; adding aloud, \"Go and sing to me.", "조는 속으로 생각하며, 소리 내어 덧붙였다. \"가서 노래나 불러 줘."], ["I'm dying for some music, and always like yours.\"", "난 음악이 너무 듣고 싶고, 네 노래는 늘 좋으니까.\""]], [["\"I'd rather stay here, thank you.\"", "\"여기 있는 게 더 좋은데, 사양할게.\""]], [["\"Well, you can't; there isn't room.", "\"안 돼, 그럴 수 없어. 자리가 없잖아."], ["Go and make yourself useful, since you are too big to be ornamental.", "가서 쓸모 있는 일이나 해. 장식품으로 두기엔 넌 너무 크니까."], ["I thought you hated to be tied to a woman's apron-string?\"", "넌 여자 앞치마 끈에 매여 있는 거 싫어한다며?\""], ["retorted Jo, quoting certain rebellious words of his own.", "조가 예전에 그가 했던 반항적인 말을 인용하며 받아쳤다."]], [["\"Ah, that depends on who wears the apron!\"", "\"아, 그건 누가 앞치마를 두르고 있느냐에 달렸지!\""], ["and Laurie gave an audacious tweak at the tassel.", "로리가 대담하게 술을 홱 잡아당겼다."]], [["\"Are you going?\"", "\"갈 거야, 말 거야?\""], ["demanded Jo, diving for the pillow.", "조가 베개를 향해 몸을 던지며 다그쳤다."]], [["He fled at once, and the minute it was well \"Up with the bonnets of bonnie Dundee,\"", "그는 단숨에 달아났고, \"어여쁜 던디의 보닛을 높이 들어라\"가 한창 울려 퍼지는 사이에 조는 슬그머니 자리를 떴다."], ["she slipped away, to return no more till the young gentleman had departed in high dudgeon.", "그 젊은 신사가 잔뜩 골이 난 채 떠나 버릴 때까지 다시는 돌아오지 않았다."]], [["Jo lay long awake that night, and was just dropping off when the sound of a stifled sob made her fly to Beth's bedside, with the anxious inquiry, \"What is it, dear?\"", "조는 그날 밤 오랫동안 잠들지 못하고 깨어 있다가, 막 잠이 들려는 참에 억눌린 흐느낌 소리가 들려와 베스의 침대맡으로 날아갔다. \"왜 그래, 베스?\" 그녀는 걱정스레 물었다."]], [["\"I thought you were asleep,\"", "\"언니 자는 줄 알았어.\""], ["sobbed Beth.", "베스가 흐느꼈다."]], [["\"Is it the old pain, my precious?\"", "\"또 그 통증이니, 우리 귀한 동생?\""]], [["\"No; it's a new one; but I can bear it,\"", "\"아니, 다른 통증이야. 그래도 견딜 수 있어.\""], ["and Beth tried to check her tears.", "베스는 눈물을 참으려 애썼다."]], [["\"Tell me all about it, and let me cure it as I often did the other.\"", "\"전부 다 얘기해 봐. 예전 그 통증 자주 낫게 해 줬듯이, 내가 낫게 해 줄게.\""]], [["\"You can't; there is no cure.\"", "\"못 해. 이건 약이 없는 거야.\""], ["There Beth's voice gave way, and, clinging to her sister, she cried so despairingly that Jo was frightened.", "거기서 베스의 목소리가 무너졌고, 언니에게 매달려 어찌나 절망적으로 우는지 조는 겁이 났다."]], [["\"Where is it?", "\"어디가 아픈데?"], ["Shall I call mother?\"", "엄마 불러올까?\""]], [["Beth did not answer the first question; but in the dark one hand went involuntarily to her heart, as if the pain were there; with the other she held Jo fast, whispering eagerly, \"No, no, don't call her, don't tell her.", "베스는 첫 번째 질문에는 답하지 않았다. 하지만 어둠 속에서 한 손이 마치 거기에 통증이 있는 듯 저도 모르게 가슴으로 갔다."], ["I shall be better soon.", "다른 손으로는 조를 꼭 붙든 채 다급히 속삭였다. \"아니, 아니, 엄마 부르지 마, 엄마한텐 말하지 마."], ["Lie down here and 'poor'", "나 곧 괜찮아질 거야. 여기 누워서 내 머리 좀 '쓰다듬어'"], ["my head.", "줘."], ["I'll be quiet, and go to sleep; indeed I will.\"", "나 조용히 있다가 잘게, 정말이야.\""]], [["Jo obeyed; but as her hand went softly to and fro across Beth's hot forehead and wet eyelids, her heart was very full, and she longed to speak. But young as she was, Jo had learned that hearts, like flowers, cannot be rudely handled, but must open naturally; so, though she believed she knew the cause of Beth's new pain, she only said, in her tenderest tone, \"Does anything trouble you, deary?\"", "조는 그 말을 따랐다. 하지만 그녀의 손이 베스의 뜨거운 이마와 젖은 눈꺼풀 위를 부드럽게 오가는 동안, 그녀의 가슴은 벅차올랐고, 무어라 말하고 싶어 견딜 수 없었다. 그러나 조는 아직 어렸어도, 마음이란 꽃과 같아서 거칠게 다룰 수 없으며 저절로 피어나야 한다는 걸 배운 터였다. 그래서 베스의 새로운 통증의 원인을 안다고 믿으면서도, 더없이 다정한 어조로 이렇게만 말했다. \"뭔가 널 괴롭히는 게 있니, 동생아?\""]], [["\"Yes, Jo,\"", "\"응, 언니.\""], ["after a long pause.", "한참 뜸을 들인 뒤였다."]], [["\"Wouldn't it comfort you to tell me what it is?\"", "\"그게 뭔지 나한테 말하면 위로가 되지 않을까?\""]], [["\"Not now, not yet.\"", "\"지금은 아니야, 아직은.\""]], [["\"Then I won't ask; but remember, Bethy, that mother and Jo are always glad to hear and help you, if they can.\"", "\"그럼 안 물어볼게. 하지만 베스, 엄마랑 나는 할 수만 있다면 언제든 네 얘기를 들어 주고 도와주고 싶어 한다는 거, 잊지 마.\""]], [["\"I know it.", "\"알아."], ["I'll tell you by and by.\"", "조만간 말할게.\""]], [["\"Is the pain better now?\"", "\"이제 통증은 좀 나아졌어?\""]], [["\"Oh, yes, much better; you are so comfortable, Jo!\"", "\"응, 훨씬 나아. 언니가 옆에 있으니 너무 편안해, 조!\""]], [["\"Go to sleep, dear; I'll stay with you.\"", "\"자, 자거라, 동생아. 내가 곁에 있어 줄게.\""]], [["So cheek to cheek they fell asleep, and on the morrow Beth seemed quite herself again; for at eighteen, neither heads nor hearts ache long, and a loving word can medicine most ills.", "그렇게 둘은 뺨을 맞대고 잠이 들었고, 이튿날 베스는 완전히 제 모습으로 돌아온 듯했다. 열여덟 살에는 머리도 마음도 오래 아프지 않는 법이고, 사랑이 담긴 한마디면 웬만한 병은 다 나으니까."]], [["But Jo had made up her mind, and, after pondering over a project for some days, she confided it to her mother.", "하지만 조는 마음을 굳혔고, 며칠 동안 한 가지 계획을 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에 엄마에게 털어놓았다."]], [["\"You asked me the other day what my wishes were.", "\"엄마, 며칠 전에 제 바람이 뭐냐고 물으셨죠."], ["I'll tell you one of them, Marmee,\"", "그중 하나를 말씀드릴게요.\""], ["she began, as they sat alone together.", "둘이 단둘이 앉아 있을 때 조가 입을 열었다."], ["\"I want to go away somewhere this winter for a change.\"", "\"올겨울에 기분 전환 삼아 어디론가 떠나고 싶어요.\""]], [["\"Why, Jo?\"", "\"왜, 조?\""], ["and her mother looked up quickly, as if the words suggested a double meaning.", "엄마는 그 말이 두 가지 의미를 담고 있기라도 한 듯 재빨리 고개를 들었다."]], [["With her eyes on her work, Jo answered soberly, \"I want something new; I feel restless, and anxious to be seeing, doing, and learning more than I am. I brood too much over my own small affairs, and need stirring up, so, as I can be spared this winter, I'd like to hop a little way, and try my wings.\"", "조는 일감에 눈을 둔 채 차분히 대답했다. \"뭔가 새로운 게 필요해요. 안절부절못하겠고, 지금보다 더 많이 보고 행동하고 배우고 싶어 안달이 나요. 제 자잘한 일에 너무 골몰하니까, 좀 자극이 필요해요. 그러니까 올겨울엔 제가 없어도 괜찮으니, 잠깐 멀리 폴짝 뛰어나가 날개를 한번 시험해 보고 싶어요.\""]], [["\"Where will you hop?\"", "\"어디로 폴짝 뛰어나갈 건데?\""]], [["\"To New York.", "\"뉴욕이요."], ["I had a bright idea yesterday, and this is it.", "어제 좋은 생각이 떠올랐는데, 이거예요."], ["You know Mrs. Kirke wrote to you for some respectable young person to teach her children and sew.", "커크 부인이 엄마한테 편지로 자기 아이들 가르치고 바느질해 줄 점잖은 젊은 사람을 구한다고 했잖아요."], ["It's rather hard to find just the thing, but I think I should suit if I tried.\"", "딱 맞는 사람을 찾기가 꽤 어렵긴 한데, 제가 해 보면 적임일 것 같아요.\""]], [["\"My dear, go out to service in that great boarding-house!\"", "\"얘야, 그 큰 하숙집에 가서 일을 하겠다고!\""], ["and Mrs. March looked surprised, but not displeased.", "마치 부인은 놀란 표정이었지만, 못마땅해하지는 않았다."]], [["\"It's not exactly going out to service; for Mrs.", "\"꼭 일하러 가는 건 아니에요."], ["Kirke is your friend,--the kindest soul that ever lived,--and would make things pleasant for me, I know.", "커크 부인은 엄마 친구이고——세상에 둘도 없이 마음씨 고운 분이고——제가 편하게 지내도록 해 주실 거예요, 전 알아요. 그 가족은 다른 사람들이랑 따로 지내고, 거긴 절 아는 사람도 없어요."], ["Her family is separate from the rest, and no one knows me there.", "안다 해도 상관없고요. 떳떳한 일인데 부끄러울 게 뭐 있어요.\""], ["Don't care if they do; it's honest work, and I'm not ashamed of it.\"", "\"나도 부끄럽다고 생각 안 한다. 그런데 네 글쓰기는?\""]], [["\"Nor I; but your writing?\" \"All the better for the change.", "\"기분 전환하면 오히려 더 잘될 거예요."], ["I shall see and hear new things, get new ideas, and, even if I haven't much time there, I shall bring home quantities of material for my rubbish.\"", "새로운 걸 보고 듣고, 새로운 아이디어도 얻을 거고요. 거기서 시간이 많지 않더라도, 제 글거리가 될 재료를 잔뜩 가지고 돌아올 거예요.\""]], [["\"I have no doubt of it; but are these your only reasons for this sudden fancy?\"", "\"그건 의심하지 않는다. 그런데 이 갑작스러운 생각의 이유가 그게 다니?\""]], [["\"No, mother.\"", "\"아니에요, 엄마.\""]], [["\"May I know the others?\"", "\"다른 이유도 알 수 있을까?\""]], [["Jo looked up and Jo looked down, then said slowly, with sudden color in her cheeks, \"It may be vain and wrong to say it, but--I'm afraid--Laurie is getting too fond of me.\"", "조는 고개를 들었다가 다시 숙이더니, 두 뺨에 갑자기 홍조를 띤 채 천천히 말했다. \"이런 말 하는 게 잘난 척 같고 잘못된 건지도 모르겠지만——제 생각엔——로리가 절 너무 좋아하게 되는 것 같아서 겁이 나요.\""]], [["\"Then you don't care for him in the way it is evident he begins to care for you?\" and Mrs.", "\"그럼 넌, 그 애가 너에 대해 분명히 품기 시작한 그런 감정을 똑같이 그 애한테 품고 있진 않다는 거니?\""], ["March looked anxious as she put the question.", "마치 부인이 그 질문을 던지며 걱정스러운 표정을 지었다."]], [["\"Mercy, no!", "\"세상에, 아니에요!"], ["I love the dear boy, as I always have, and am immensely proud of him; but as for anything more, it's out of the question.\"", "그 사랑스러운 애를 늘 그랬듯이 아끼고, 그 애가 무척 자랑스럽긴 해요. 하지만 그 이상은, 어림도 없어요.\""]], [["\"I'm glad of that, Jo.\"", "\"그렇다니 다행이구나, 조.\""]], [["\"Why, please?\"", "\"왜요, 엄마?\""]], [["\"Because, dear, I don't think you suited to one another.", "\"얘야, 너희 둘은 서로 어울리지 않는다고 생각하거든."], ["As friends you are very happy, and your frequent quarrels soon blow over; but I fear you would both rebel if you were mated for life.", "친구로서는 너희가 무척 행복하고, 자주 다퉈도 금세 풀리지. 하지만 만약 평생을 짝으로 맺어진다면 둘 다 반발할 것 같아 걱정이야."], ["You are too much alike and too fond of freedom, not to mention hot tempers and strong wills, to get on happily together, in a relation which needs infinite patience and forbearance, as well as love.\"", "너희는 너무 닮았고 자유를 너무 좋아하는 데다, 다혈질에 고집까지 센데, 사랑은 물론이고 무한한 인내와 너그러움이 필요한 관계에서 행복하게 지내기란 어려운 일이거든.\""]], [["\"That's just the feeling I had, though I couldn't express it.", "\"제가 느낀 게 바로 그거예요, 표현은 못 했지만."], ["I'm glad you think he is only beginning to care for me.", "엄마가 그 애가 이제 막 절 좋아하기 시작한 거라고 생각하셔서 기뻐요."], ["It would trouble me sadly to make him unhappy; for I couldn't fall in love with the dear old fellow merely out of gratitude, could I?\"", "그 애를 불행하게 만든다면 너무 마음이 아플 거예요. 단지 고마워서 그 사랑스러운 옛 친구랑 사랑에 빠질 수는 없잖아요, 그렇죠?\""]], [["\"You are sure of his feeling for you?\"", "\"그 애가 너한테 품은 감정을 확신하니?\""]], [["The color deepened in Jo's cheeks, as she answered, with the look of mingled pleasure, pride, and pain which young girls wear when speaking of first lovers,--", "조의 두 뺨에 홍조가 짙어졌고, 첫사랑을 이야기하는 어린 처녀들이 짓는, 기쁨과 자긍심과 아픔이 뒤섞인 표정으로 대답했다."]], [["\"I'm afraid it is so, mother; he hasn't said anything, but he looks a great deal.", "\"그런 것 같아요, 엄마. 그 애가 뭐라고 말한 건 아니지만, 눈빛이 많은 걸 말하거든요."], ["I think I had better go away before it comes to anything.\"", "일이 어떻게 되기 전에 멀리 떠나는 게 나을 것 같아요.\""]], [["\"I agree with you, and if it can be managed you shall go.\"", "\"엄마도 같은 생각이다. 그러니 일이 잘 풀린다면 너는 떠나는 거다.\""]], [["Jo looked relieved, and, after a pause, said, smiling, \"How Mrs.", "조는 마음이 놓이는 표정이었고, 잠시 뒤 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["Moffat would wonder at your want of management, if she knew; and how she will rejoice that Annie still may hope.\"", "\"모펏 부인이 이 일을 알면 엄마의 처세가 서투르다고 얼마나 놀랄까요. 그리고 애니한테 아직 희망이 있다고 얼마나 기뻐할까요.\""]], [["\"Ah, Jo, mothers may differ in their management, but the hope is the same in all,--the desire to see their children happy.", "\"아, 조, 엄마들마다 처세는 다를지언정 바라는 건 다 똑같단다——제 자식이 행복한 걸 보고 싶은 마음이지."], ["Meg is so, and I am content with her success.", "메그는 행복하고, 엄마는 그 애가 잘된 걸로 만족한다."], ["You I leave to enjoy your liberty till you tire of it; for only then will you find that there is something sweeter.", "너는 자유를 실컷 누리도록 내버려 두마, 네가 그것에 싫증이 날 때까지. 그래야만 비로소 그보다 더 달콤한 게 있다는 걸 깨닫게 될 테니까."], ["Amy is my chief care now, but her good sense will help her.", "지금 엄마가 가장 마음 쓰는 건 에이미인데, 그 애는 분별이 있으니 스스로 잘 헤쳐 나갈 거야."], ["For Beth, I indulge no hopes except that she may be well.", "베스에 대해선, 그저 건강하기만을 바랄 뿐 다른 바람은 품지 않는다."], ["By the way, she seems brighter this last day or two.", "그러고 보니 그 애가 요 며칠 좀 밝아 보이더구나."], ["Have you spoken to her?\"", "네가 그 애랑 이야기를 나눴니?\""]], [["\"Yes; she owned she had a trouble, and promised to tell me by and by.", "\"네. 베스가 고민이 있다고 인정했고, 조만간 말해 주겠다고 약속했어요."], ["I said no more, for I think I know it;\"", "전 더 묻지 않았어요, 그게 뭔지 알 것 같아서요.\""], ["and Jo told her little story.", "그러고는 조가 자신의 짤막한 이야기를 들려주었다."]], [["Mrs. March shook her head, and did not take so romantic a view of the case, but looked grave, and repeated her opinion that, for Laurie's sake, Jo should go away for a time.", "마치 부인은 고개를 가로저으며 그 일을 그리 낭만적으로 보지 않았지만, 진지한 표정으로, 로리를 위해서라도 조가 한동안 떠나 있는 게 좋겠다는 의견을 거듭 밝혔다."]], [["\"Let us say nothing about it to him till the plan is settled; then I'll run away before he can collect his wits and be tragical.", "\"계획이 정해지기 전까진 그 애한테 아무 말도 하지 말기로 해요. 그런 다음 그 애가 정신 차리고 비극을 연출하기 전에 제가 달아나 버릴게요."], ["Beth must think I'm going to please myself, as I am, for I can't talk about Laurie to her; but she can pet and comfort him after I'm gone, and so cure him of this romantic notion.", "베스는 제가 그저 제 좋자고 가는 줄 알 거예요, 실제로도 그렇고요. 베스한테 로리 얘긴 할 수 없으니까요."], ["He's been through so many little trials of the sort, he's used to it, and will soon get over his love-lornity.\"", "하지만 제가 떠나고 나면 베스가 그 애를 다독이고 위로해 줄 수 있을 테고, 그렇게 해서 그 애의 이 낭만적인 생각을 고쳐 줄 수 있겠죠. 그 애는 이런 사소한 시련을 워낙 많이 겪어 봐서 익숙하니까, 금세 이 상사병을 떨쳐 낼 거예요.\""]], [["Jo spoke hopefully, but could not rid herself of the foreboding fear that this \"little trial\" would be harder than the others, and that Laurie would not get over his \"love-lornity\" as easily as heretofore.", "조는 희망 섞인 어조로 말했지만, 이 \"사소한 시련\"이 다른 시련들보다 더 힘겨우리라는, 그리고 로리가 예전처럼 쉽게 \"상사병\"을 떨쳐 내지는 못하리라는 불길한 예감을 떨쳐 버릴 수 없었다."]], [["The plan was talked over in a family council, and agreed upon; for Mrs.", "이 계획은 가족회의에서 논의되어 합의에 이르렀다. 커크 부인이 조를 기꺼이 받아들였고, 편안한 보금자리를 마련해 주겠다고 약속했기 때문이다."], ["Kirke gladly accepted Jo, and promised to make a pleasant home for her.", "가르치는 일은 조를 자립하게 해 줄 테고, 틈틈이 생기는 여가는 글쓰기로 알차게 쓸 수 있을 터였다."], ["The teaching would render her independent; and such leisure as she got might be made profitable by writing, while the new scenes and society would be both useful and agreeable.", "게다가 새로운 풍경과 사람들은 유익하면서도 즐거운 경험이 될 것이었다. 조는 이 전망이 마음에 들었고 어서 떠나고 싶어 안달이었다."], ["Jo liked the prospect and was eager to be gone, for the home-nest was growing too narrow for her restless nature and adventurous spirit.", "집이라는 둥지가 안절부절못하는 그녀의 천성과 모험심에는 너무 비좁아지고 있었던 것이다. 모든 게 정해지자, 조는 두려움에 떨며 로리에게 그 소식을 전했다."], ["When all was settled, with fear and trembling she told Laurie; but to her surprise he took it very quietly.", "그런데 뜻밖에도 그는 무척 담담하게 받아들였다. 그는 요즘 들어 평소보다 진지했지만 무척 유쾌했고, 새사람이 되려는 거냐고 농담 삼아 추궁하자 차분하게 대답했다."], ["He had been graver than usual of late, but very pleasant; and, when jokingly accused of turning over a new leaf, he answered soberly, \"So I am; and I mean this one shall stay turned.\"", "\"맞아. 그리고 이번엔 새사람이 된 채로 쭉 갈 작정이야.\""]], [["Jo was very much relieved that one of his virtuous fits should come on just then, and made her preparations with a lightened heart,--for Beth seemed more cheerful,--and hoped she was doing the best for all.", "조는 마침 그때 그의 갸륵한 마음이 발동한 것에 무척 안도하며 한결 가벼워진 마음으로 떠날 채비를 했다. 베스도 더 명랑해 보였고, 자신이 모두를 위해 최선을 다하고 있다고 믿어졌다."]], [["\"One thing I leave to your especial care,\"", "\"한 가지를 특별히 너한테 부탁하고 갈게.\""], ["she said, the night before she left.", "떠나기 전날 밤 조가 말했다."]], [["\"You mean your papers?\"", "\"언니 원고들 말이야?\""], ["asked Beth.", "베스가 물었다."]], [["\"No, my boy.", "\"아니, 내 녀석 말이야."], ["Be very good to him, won't you?\"", "그 애한테 잘해 줘, 알았지?\""]], [["\"Of course I will; but I can't fill your place, and he'll miss you sadly.\"", "\"당연히 그럴게. 하지만 내가 언니 자리를 대신할 순 없고, 그 앤 언니를 무척 그리워할 거야.\""]], [["\"It won't hurt him; so remember, I leave him in your charge, to plague, pet, and keep in order.\"", "\"그래도 그 앤 괜찮을 거야. 그러니까 기억해, 내가 그 애를 너한테 맡기는 거야. 골려 주고, 다독여 주고, 단속해 주라고.\""]], [["\"I'll do my best, for your sake,\"", "\"언니를 위해서 최선을 다할게.\""], ["promised Beth, wondering why Jo looked at her so queerly.", "베스가 약속하며, 조가 왜 자기를 그렇게 묘하게 쳐다보는지 의아해했다."]], [["When Laurie said \"Good-by,\"", "로리는 \"잘 가\"라고 말하면서, 의미심장하게 속삭였다."], ["he whispered significantly, \"It won't do a bit of good, Jo.", "\"아무 소용 없을걸, 조. 내 눈이 널 지켜보고 있으니까, 행동 조심해."], ["My eye is on you; so mind what you do, or I'll come and bring you home.\"", "안 그러면 내가 가서 널 집으로 데려올 거야.\" \"뉴욕, 11월."]], [["\"NEW YORK, November.", "사랑하는 엄마와 베스에게,"]], [["\"I'm going to write you a regular volume, for I've got heaps to tell, though I'm not a fine young lady travelling on the continent. When I lost sight of father's dear old face, I felt a trifle blue, and might have shed a briny drop or two, if an Irish lady with four small children, all crying more or less, hadn't diverted my mind; for I amused myself by dropping gingerbread nuts over the seat every time they opened their mouths to roar.", "제가 대륙을 여행하는 우아한 아가씨는 아니지만, 들려드릴 이야기가 산더미라 책 한 권은 너끈히 쓸 작정이에요. 아빠의 사랑스러운 옛 얼굴이 시야에서 사라졌을 때 좀 울적해져서 짭짤한 눈물 한두 방울 흘릴 뻔했어요. 그런데 어린아이 넷을 데리고 탄 어떤 아일랜드 부인이 제 마음을 딴 데로 돌려놓지 않았다면 정말 그랬을 거예요. 그 애들이 너나없이 울어 댔거든요. 그 애들이 울려고 입을 벌릴 때마다 좌석 너머로 생강과자를 떨어뜨려 주면서 혼자 즐거워했어요."]], [["\"Soon the sun came out, and taking it as a good omen, I cleared up likewise, and enjoyed my journey with all my heart.", "곧 해가 나왔는데, 좋은 징조로 여기고 저도 마음이 환해져서 여행을 진심으로 즐겼어요."]], [["\"Mrs. Kirke welcomed me so kindly I felt at home at once, even in that big house full of strangers.", "커크 부인이 어찌나 친절히 맞아 주시는지, 낯선 사람들로 가득한 그 큰 집에서도 단번에 마음이 편안해졌어요."], ["She gave me a funny little sky-parlor--all she had; but there is a stove in it, and a nice table in a sunny window, so I can sit here and write whenever I like.", "부인은 저한테 우습고 자그마한 다락방을 내주셨어요. 가진 게 그것뿐이라면서요."], ["A fine view and a church-tower opposite atone for the many stairs, and I took a fancy to my den on the spot.", "그래도 난로가 있고, 볕이 드는 창가에 멋진 탁자가 있어서, 마음 내킬 때마다 여기 앉아 글을 쓸 수 있어요."], ["The nursery, where I am to teach and sew, is a pleasant room next Mrs.", "전망도 좋고 맞은편엔 교회 탑도 있어서 그 많은 계단을 오르내리는 수고가 보상이 돼요. 전 단번에 제 방이 마음에 들었어요."], ["Kirke's private parlor, and the two little girls are pretty children,--rather spoilt, I fancy, but they took to me after telling them 'The Seven Bad Pigs;'", "제가 아이들을 가르치고 바느질할 육아실은 커크 부인의 개인 응접실 옆에 있는 쾌적한 방이고, 두 어린 여자아이는 예쁜 아이들이에요. 좀 버릇없긴 한 것 같지만, '못된 일곱 마리 돼지'"], ["and I've no doubt I shall make a model governess.", "이야기를 들려줬더니 저를 따르더라고요. 전 분명히 모범적인 가정교사가 될 거예요."]], [["\"I am to have my meals with the children, if I prefer it to the great table, and for the present I do, for I _am_ bashful, though no one will believe it.", "제가 큰 식탁보다 아이들과 함께 먹는 게 좋으면 그래도 된대요. 당분간은 그러려고요. 아무도 안 믿겠지만, 전 정말 수줍음을 타거든요."]], [["\"'Now, my dear, make yourself at home,' said Mrs.", "'자, 얘야, 편하게 지내렴.'"], ["K. in her motherly way; 'I'm on the drive from morning to night, as you may suppose with such a family; but a great anxiety will be off my mind if I know the children are safe with you.", "커크 부인이 어머니 같은 어투로 말씀하셨어요."], ["My rooms are always open to you, and your own shall be as comfortable as I can make it.", "'보다시피 이렇게 식구가 많으니 난 아침부터 밤까지 동분서주한단다. 그래도 아이들이 너와 함께 있어 안전하다는 걸 알면 큰 걱정 하나는 덜겠구나."], ["There are some pleasant people in the house if you feel sociable, and your evenings are always free.", "내 방은 언제든 너한테 열려 있고, 네 방도 내가 할 수 있는 한 편안하게 해 주마. 사람들과 어울리고 싶으면, 이 집에 좋은 사람들이 더러 있단다."], ["Come to me if anything goes wrong, and be as happy as you can.", "저녁 시간은 언제나 자유고. 뭔가 잘 안 풀리면 나한테 오렴, 그리고 되도록 즐겁게 지내거라."], ["There's the tea-bell; I must run and change my cap;'", "어머나, 차 마실 시간 종이 울리네. 어서 가서 모자를 바꿔 써야겠다.'"], ["and off she bustled, leaving me to settle myself in my new nest.", "그러고는 부산스레 가 버리셔서, 저는 새 둥지에 혼자 자리를 잡게 됐어요."]], [["\"As I went downstairs, soon after, I saw something I liked.", "조금 뒤에 아래층으로 내려가다가 마음에 드는 광경을 봤어요."], ["The flights are very long in this tall house, and as I stood waiting at the head of the third one for a little servant girl to lumber up, I saw a gentleman come along behind her, take the heavy hod of coal out of her hand, carry it all the way up, put it down at a door near by, and walk away, saying, with a kind nod and a foreign accent,--", "이 높다란 집은 계단참이 무척 긴데, 제가 세 번째 계단참 꼭대기에서 어린 하녀 아이가 무겁게 올라오기를 기다리고 서 있는데, 어떤 신사가 그 애 뒤로 따라오더니, 그 애 손에서 무거운 석탄 통을 받아 들고, 위까지 죽 날라다가, 가까운 어느 문 앞에 내려놓고는, 친절히 고개를 끄덕이며 외국식 억양으로 이렇게 말하고 걸어가는 거예요."]], [["\"'It goes better so.", "'이렇게 하는 게 낫지요."], ["The little back is too young to haf such heaviness.'", "어린 등이 그런 무거운 걸 들기엔 너무 어려요.'"]], [["\"Wasn't it good of him?", "참 갸륵하지 않아요?"], ["I like such things, for, as father says, trifles show character.", "전 그런 게 좋아요. 아빠 말씀처럼 사소한 일이 사람 됨됨이를 보여 주잖아요."], ["When I mentioned it to Mrs. K., that evening, she laughed, and said,--", "그날 저녁 커크 부인한테 그 얘기를 했더니 웃으면서 이러시더라고요."]], [["\"'That must have been Professor Bhaer; he's always doing things of that sort.'", "'분명 베어 교수일 거야. 그분은 늘 그런 일을 하거든.'"]], [["\"Mrs. K.", "커크 부인 말로는 그분은 베를린에서 왔고, 무척 박식하고 선량하지만, 교회 쥐만큼이나 가난해서, 자기 입에 풀칠하고 여기서 가르치고 있는 어린 고아 조카 둘을 교육시키느라 강습을 한대요."], ["told me he was from Berlin; very learned and good, but poor as a church-mouse, and gives lessons to support himself and two little orphan nephews whom he is educating here, according to the wishes of his sister, who married an American.", "미국 사람과 결혼한 누이의 뜻에 따라서 그 조카들을 맡았다나 봐요. 그리 낭만적인 이야기는 아니지만 저는 흥미로웠어요."], ["Not a very romantic story, but it interested me; and I was glad to hear that Mrs. K.", "그리고 커크 부인이 그분 학생 몇몇을 위해 응접실을 빌려준다는 얘길 듣고 반가웠어요."], ["lends him her parlor for some of his scholars.", "응접실과 육아실 사이에 유리문이 있거든요."], ["There is a glass door between it and the nursery, and I mean to peep at him, and then I'll tell you how he looks.", "그래서 그분을 슬쩍 엿보고, 어떻게 생긴 분인지 말씀드릴게요."], ["He's almost forty, so it's no harm, Marmee.", "그분은 마흔 가까이 되었으니, 흠 잡힐 일은 아니죠, 엄마."]], [["\"After tea and a go-to-bed romp with the little girls, I attacked the big work-basket, and had a quiet evening chatting with my new friend.", "차를 마시고 어린 여자아이들과 잠자리 들기 전 한바탕 뒹굴고 난 뒤, 저는 큰 반짇고리를 공략했고, 새 친구와 도란도란 이야기 나누며 조용한 저녁을 보냈어요. 일기 형식으로 편지를 써서 일주일에 한 번씩 부칠게요."], ["I shall keep a journal-letter, and send it once a week; so good-night, and more to-morrow.\"", "그럼 안녕히 주무세요. 내일 더 쓸게요.\""]], [["\"_Tuesday Eve._", "\"화요일 저녁."]], [["\"Had a lively time in my seminary, this morning, for the children acted like Sancho; and at one time I really thought I should shake them all round.", "오늘 아침엔 제 학당에서 한바탕 소동이 벌어졌어요. 아이들이 산초처럼 굴어서, 한순간엔 정말 전부 붙잡고 흔들어 줄까 싶더라니까요."], ["Some good angel inspired me to try gymnastics, and I kept it up till they were glad to sit down and keep still.", "어느 착한 천사가 영감을 줘서 체조를 시켜 봤는데, 아이들이 기진맥진해서 기꺼이 자리에 앉아 얌전히 있을 때까지 계속했어요."], ["After luncheon, the girl took them out for a walk, and I went to my needle-work, like little Mabel, 'with a willing mind.'", "점심을 먹고 나서 하녀 아이가 애들을 데리고 산책을 나갔고, 저는 작은 메이블처럼 '기꺼운 마음으로' 바느질에 매달렸어요."], ["I was thanking my stars that I'd learned to make nice button-holes, when the parlor-door opened and shut, and some one began to hum,--", "단춧구멍을 깔끔하게 만드는 법을 배워 둔 걸 다행으로 여기고 있는데, 응접실 문이 열렸다 닫히더니 누군가 콧노래를 흥얼거리기 시작하는 거예요——"]], [["'Kennst du das land,'", "'그대는 아는가, 저 나라를'"]], [["like a big bumble-bee. It was dreadfully improper, I know, but I couldn't resist the temptation; and lifting one end of the curtain before the glass door, I peeped in. Professor Bhaer was there; and while he arranged his books, I took a good look at him. A regular German,--rather stout, with brown hair tumbled all over his head, a bushy beard, good nose, the kindest eyes I ever saw, and a splendid big voice that does one's ears good, after our sharp or slipshod American gabble. His clothes were rusty, his hands were large, and he hadn't a really handsome feature in his face, except his beautiful teeth; yet I liked him, for he had a fine head; his linen was very nice, and he looked like a gentleman, though two buttons were off his coat, and there was a patch on one shoe. He looked sober in spite of his humming, till he went to the window to turn the hyacinth bulbs toward the sun, and stroke the cat, who received him like an old friend. Then he smiled; and when a tap came at the door, called out in a loud, brisk tone,--", "마치 커다란 호박벌처럼요. 무례한 짓인 줄 알지만, 유혹을 떨칠 수가 없었어요. 그래서 유리문 앞에 드리운 커튼 한쪽 끝을 들추고 안을 들여다봤죠. 베어 교수가 거기 있었어요. 그분이 책을 정리하는 동안 저는 그분을 찬찬히 뜯어봤어요. 영락없는 독일 사람이에요. 좀 통통하고, 갈색 머리는 온통 헝클어져 있고, 텁수룩한 턱수염에, 코는 잘생겼고, 제가 본 중에 가장 다정한 눈을 가졌고, 우리 미국 사람들의 새된 수다나 어설픈 말투 끝에 듣자니 귀가 다 시원해지는 멋지고 우렁찬 목소리를 지녔어요. 옷은 후줄근하고 손은 컸고, 얼굴엔 정말 잘생긴 데라곤 한 군데도 없었어요. 아름다운 치아만 빼고요. 그래도 저는 그분이 마음에 들었어요. 멋진 머리를 가졌거든요. 셔츠는 무척 깔끔했고, 코트 단추 두 개가 떨어지고 한쪽 신발엔 기운 자국이 있었지만, 신사처럼 보였어요. 흥얼거리는 와중에도 진지해 보이더니, 창가로 가서 히아신스 구근을 햇빛 쪽으로 돌려놓고 고양이를 쓰다듬는데, 그 고양이가 오랜 친구처럼 그분을 반기더라고요. 그제야 그분이 미소를 지었어요. 그리고 문을 똑똑 두드리는 소리가 나자, 우렁차고 활기찬 목소리로 외쳤어요——"]], [["\"'Herein!'", "'들어와요!'"]], [["\"I was just going to run, when I caught sight of a morsel of a child carrying a big book, and stopped to see what was going on.", "막 달아나려는데, 큰 책을 든 자그마한 아이가 눈에 들어와서, 무슨 일인가 보려고 멈춰 섰어요."]], [["\"'Me wants my Bhaer,'", "'베어 아저씨가 필요해요.'"], ["said the mite, slamming down her book, and running to meet him.", "그 꼬마가 책을 쾅 내려놓고 그분에게 달려가며 말했어요."]], [["\"'Thou shalt haf thy Bhaer; come, then, and take a goot hug from him, my Tina,'", "'네 베어 아저씨를 주마. 자, 이리 와서 아저씨한테 꼭 안기렴, 우리 티나.'"], ["said the Professor, catching her up, with a laugh, and holding her so high over his head that she had to stoop her little face to kiss him.", "교수가 웃으며 그 애를 번쩍 들어 올려 머리 위로 한껏 치켜드는 바람에, 아이는 입을 맞추려고 자그마한 얼굴을 숙여야 했어요."]], [["\"'Now me mus tuddy my lessin,'", "'이제 공부를 해야 해요.' 그 우스운 꼬마가 말을 이었어요."], ["went on the funny little thing; so he put her up at the table, opened the great dictionary she had brought, and gave her a paper and pencil, and she scribbled away, turning a leaf now and then, and passing her little fat finger down the page, as if finding a word, so soberly that I nearly betrayed myself by a laugh, while Mr.", "그러자 그분은 아이를 탁자 위에 앉히고, 아이가 가져온 큰 사전을 펼쳐 종이와 연필을 주었고, 아이는 이따금 책장을 넘기며 글씨를 끄적였어요. 마치 단어를 찾는 듯 통통한 작은 손가락으로 책장을 죽 짚어 내려가는데, 어찌나 진지한지 하마터면 웃음을 터뜨려 들킬 뻔했어요."], ["Bhaer stood stroking her pretty hair, with a fatherly look, that made me think she must be his own, though she looked more French than German.", "그동안 베어 씨는 그 아이의 예쁜 머리칼을 쓰다듬으며 아버지 같은 눈길로 바라보아서, 그 애가 독일 아이라기보다 프랑스 아이처럼 보였는데도 분명 그분 친자식일 거라는 생각이 들었어요."]], [["\"Another knock and the appearance of two young ladies sent me back to my work, and there I virtuously remained through all the noise and gabbling that went on next door.", "또 누군가 노크를 하고 젊은 아가씨 둘이 나타나는 바람에 저는 일감으로 돌아갔고, 옆방에서 벌어지는 온갖 시끄러운 소리와 떠드는 소리에도 꿋꿋이 자리를 지켰어요."], ["One of the girls kept laughing affectedly, and saying 'Now Professor,'", "한 아가씨는 계속 꾸민 듯이 웃으면서 교태 어린 어조로 '어머, 교수님'"], ["in a coquettish tone, and the other pronounced her German with an accent that must have made it hard for him to keep sober.", "하고 그랬고, 다른 아가씨는 독일어를 어찌나 이상한 억양으로 발음하는지 그분이 진지함을 유지하기 어려웠을 거예요."]], [["\"Both seemed to try his patience sorely; for more than once I heard him say emphatically, 'No, no, it is _not_ so; you haf not attend to what I say;' and once there was a loud rap, as if he struck the table with his book, followed by the despairing exclamation, 'Prut! it all goes bad this day.'", "둘 다 그분의 인내심을 호되게 시험하는 것 같았어요. 그분이 한 번 이상 단호하게 이렇게 말하는 게 들렸거든요. '아니요, 아니에요, 그게 아니지요. 내가 한 말을 안 들으셨군요.' 그리고 한 번은 책으로 탁자를 내리치기라도 한 듯 쾅 하는 소리가 나더니, 절망적인 탄식이 뒤따랐어요. '프루트! 오늘은 죄다 엉망진창이군.'"]], [["\"Poor man, I pitied him; and when the girls were gone, took just one more peep, to see if he survived it.", "가엾은 분, 정말 안쓰러웠어요. 그래서 아가씨들이 가고 난 뒤, 그분이 무사한지 보려고 딱 한 번 더 들여다봤어요."], ["He seemed to have thrown himself back in his chair, tired out, and sat there with his eyes shut till the clock struck two, when he jumped up, put his books in his pocket, as if ready for another lesson, and, taking little Tina, who had fallen asleep on the sofa, in his arms, he carried her quietly away.", "그분은 기진맥진해서 의자에 몸을 뒤로 던지듯 기댄 채, 눈을 감고 앉아 있다가, 시계가 두 시를 치자 벌떡 일어나 다음 강습이라도 준비하듯 책들을 주머니에 넣고는, 소파에서 잠든 어린 티나를 안아 들어 조용히 데리고 나갔어요."], ["I fancy he has a hard life of it.", "아무래도 그분은 고된 삶을 사는 것 같아요."]], [["\"Mrs. Kirke asked me if I wouldn't go down to the five o'clock dinner; and, feeling a little bit homesick, I thought I would, just to see what sort of people are under the same roof with me.", "커크 부인이 다섯 시 만찬에 내려오지 않겠냐고 물으셨어요."], ["So I made myself respectable, and tried to slip in behind Mrs.", "저는 좀 향수에 젖어 있던 터라, 저랑 한 지붕 아래 사는 사람들이 어떤 부류인지나 볼까 해서 그러겠다고 했어요."], ["Kirke; but as she is short, and I'm tall, my efforts at concealment were rather a failure.", "그래서 단정하게 차려입고 커크 부인 뒤에 슬쩍 묻어 들어가려 했는데, 부인은 키가 작고 저는 키가 커서, 몸을 숨기려는 노력이 영 실패로 돌아갔어요."], ["She gave me a seat by her, and after my face cooled off, I plucked up courage, and looked about me.", "부인이 자기 옆자리를 내주셨고, 저는 얼굴 화끈거리는 게 가라앉은 뒤에 용기를 내어 주위를 둘러봤어요."], ["The long table was full, and every one intent on getting their dinner,--the gentlemen especially, who seemed to be eating on time, for they _bolted_ in every sense of the word, vanishing as soon as they were done.", "긴 식탁은 사람들로 꽉 찼고, 다들 식사에 여념이 없었어요. 특히 신사들은 시간 맞춰 먹어야 하는 듯, 말 그대로 후딱 해치우고는 다 먹기가 무섭게 사라져 버렸어요."], ["There was the usual assortment of young men absorbed in themselves; young couples absorbed in each other; married ladies in their babies, and old gentlemen in politics.", "거기엔 으레 있는 부류가 다 모여 있었어요. 자기 자신에게 몰두한 젊은 남자들, 서로에게 몰두한 젊은 연인들, 아기에게 몰두한 결혼한 부인들, 정치에 몰두한 노신사들."], ["I don't think I shall care to have much to do with any of them, except one sweet-faced maiden lady, who looks as if she had something in her.", "한 사람만 빼고는 누구와도 깊이 엮이고 싶지 않을 것 같아요. 그 한 사람은 다정한 얼굴의 노처녀인데, 속에 뭔가 든 사람처럼 보이거든요."]], [["\"Cast away at the very bottom of the table was the Professor, shouting answers to the questions of a very inquisitive, deaf old gentleman on one side, and talking philosophy with a Frenchman on the other.", "식탁 맨 끝자리에 내쳐지듯 앉아 있는 분이 바로 그 교수였어요. 한쪽에선 무척 꼬치꼬치 캐묻는 귀먹은 노신사의 질문에 큰 소리로 답하고, 다른 한쪽에선 어느 프랑스 사람과 철학을 논하면서요."], ["If Amy had been here, she'd have turned her back on him forever, because, sad to relate, he had a great appetite, and shovelled in his dinner in a manner which would have horrified 'her ladyship.'", "만약 에이미가 여기 있었다면 그분에게서 영영 등을 돌렸을 거예요. 말하기 안됐지만, 그분은 식욕이 어마어마해서 '귀하신 그 아가씨'가 기겁할 만한 방식으로 음식을 퍼 넣었거든요."], ["I didn't mind, for I like 'to see folks eat with a relish,'", "저는 개의치 않았어요. 해나 말마따나 '사람이 맛나게 먹는 걸 보는'"], ["as Hannah says, and the poor man must have needed a deal of food after teaching idiots all day.", "게 좋고, 게다가 하루 종일 멍청이들을 가르친 가엾은 분이라면 음식이 무척 많이 필요했을 테니까요."]], [["\"As I went upstairs after dinner, two of the young men were settling their hats before the hall-mirror, and I heard one say low to the other, 'Who's the new party?'", "만찬을 마치고 위층으로 올라가는데, 젊은 남자 둘이 현관 거울 앞에서 모자를 매만지고 있었고, 한 사람이 다른 사람에게 나직이 하는 말이 들렸어요. '저 새로 온 여자는 누구야?'"]], [["\"'Governess, or something of that sort.'", "'가정교사나 뭐 그런 거겠지.'"]], [["\"'What the deuce is she at our table for?'", "'대체 왜 우리 식탁에 앉아 있는 거야?'"]], [["\"'Friend of the old lady's.'", "'노부인 친구래.'"]], [["\"'Handsome head, but no style.'", "'머리는 그럴듯한데, 세련된 맛이 없군.'"]], [["\"'Not a bit of it.", "'전혀 없지."], ["Give us a light and come on.'", "불 좀 빌려줘, 가자.'"]], [["\"I felt angry at first, and then I didn't care, for a governess is as good as a clerk, and I've got sense, if I haven't style, which is more than some people have, judging from the remarks of the elegant beings who clattered away, smoking like bad chimneys. I hate ordinary people!\"", "처음엔 화가 났는데, 그다음엔 상관없어졌어요. 가정교사도 점원만큼이나 떳떳한 일이고, 저는 세련된 맛은 없어도 분별은 있으니까요. 그건 어떤 사람들보다는 나은 거잖아요. 굴뚝처럼 담배 연기를 뿜으며 또각또각 걸어가던 그 우아하신 분들의 말투로 미루어 보면요. 저는 속물 같은 인간들이 정말 싫어요!\""]], [["\"_Thursday._", "\"목요일."]], [["\"Yesterday was a quiet day, spent in teaching, sewing, and writing in my little room, which is very cosey, with a light and fire.", "어제는 조용한 하루였어요. 불 켜고 난로 지핀, 무척 아늑한 제 작은 방에서 가르치고 바느질하고 글 쓰며 보냈죠."], ["I picked up a few bits of news, and was introduced to the Professor.", "자잘한 소식을 몇 가지 주워들었고, 교수에게 소개도 받았어요."], ["It seems that Tina is the child of the Frenchwoman who does the fine ironing in the laundry here.", "알고 보니 티나는 여기 세탁실에서 고급 다림질을 맡고 있는 프랑스 여자의 아이래요. 그 꼬마는 베어 씨에게 홀딱 반해서, 그분이 집에 있을 때면 강아지처럼 집 안 곳곳을 졸졸 따라다녀요."], ["The little thing has lost her heart to Mr.", "그분은 '총각'이지만 아이들을 무척 좋아해서 그걸 흐뭇해하시고요."], ["Bhaer, and follows him about the house like a dog whenever he is at home, which delights him, as he is very fond of children, though a 'bacheldore.'", "키티와 미니 커크도 그분을 무척 따르고, 그분이 만들어 내는 놀이며, 가져다주는 선물이며, 들려주는 멋진 이야기에 대해 온갖 얘기를 늘어놓아요."], ["Kitty and Minnie Kirke likewise regard him with affection, and tell all sorts of stories about the plays he invents, the presents he brings, and the splendid tales he tells.", "젊은 남자들은 그분을 놀려 대는 것 같아요. '늙은 프리츠', '라거 맥주', '큰곰자리'라고 부르면서 그분 이름을 가지고 온갖 농담을 한대요."], ["The young men quiz him, it seems, call him Old Fritz, Lager Beer, Ursa Major, and make all manner of jokes on his name. But he enjoys it like a boy, Mrs.", "그래도 그분은 소년처럼 그걸 즐기고, 어찌나 너그럽게 받아넘기는지, 그분의 외국식 행동거지에도 불구하고 다들 그분을 좋아한대요."], ["K. says, and takes it so good-naturedly that they all like him, in spite of his foreign ways.", "커크 부인이 그러시더라고요."]], [["\"The maiden lady is a Miss Norton,--rich, cultivated, and kind. She spoke to me at dinner to-day (for I went to table again, it's such fun to watch people), and asked me to come and see her at her room. She has fine books and pictures, knows interesting persons, and seems friendly; so I shall make myself agreeable, for I _do_ want to get into good society, only it isn't the same sort that Amy likes.", "그 노처녀는 노턴 양이라는 분이에요. 부유하고, 교양 있고, 친절하죠. 오늘 만찬에서 저한테 말을 거셨어요. (제가 또 식탁에 갔거든요. 사람 구경하는 게 어찌나 재밌는지요.) 그러고는 자기 방에 한번 놀러 오라고 하셨어요. 그분은 좋은 책과 그림을 가지고 있고, 흥미로운 사람들도 알고, 친근해 보여요. 그러니 저도 싹싹하게 굴어야겠어요. 정말이지 좋은 사람들과 어울리고 싶거든요. 다만 에이미가 좋아하는 부류와는 다른 부류지만요."]], [["\"I was in our parlor last evening, when Mr.", "어젯밤 우리 응접실에 있는데 베어 씨가 커크 부인께 드릴 신문 몇 부를 들고 들어오셨어요."], ["Bhaer came in with some newspapers for Mrs. Kirke.", "부인은 거기 없었지만, 작은 노부인 같은 미니가 저를 아주 예쁘게 소개했어요."], ["She wasn't there, but Minnie, who is a little old woman, introduced me very prettily: 'This is mamma's friend, Miss March.'", "'이분은 엄마 친구인 마치 양이에요.'"]], [["\"'Yes; and she's jolly and we like her lots,'", "'네, 그리고 유쾌하고, 우린 이 언니를 엄청 좋아해요.'"], ["added Kitty, who is an _enfant terrible_.", "천방지축인 키티가 덧붙였어요."]], [["\"We both bowed, and then we laughed, for the prim introduction and the blunt addition were rather a comical contrast.", "우리 둘은 인사를 나누고 나서 웃음을 터뜨렸어요. 깍듯한 소개와 무뚝뚝한 첨언이 꽤 우스꽝스러운 대조를 이뤘거든요."]], [["\"'Ah, yes, I hear these naughty ones go to vex you, Mees Marsch.", "'아, 그렇군요, 이 말썽꾸러기들이 마치 양을 성가시게 하는 모양이지요."], ["If so again, call at me and I come,'", "또 그러거든 나를 부르세요, 그럼 내가 가지요.'"], ["he said, with a threatening frown that delighted the little wretches.", "그분이 위협하듯 인상을 찌푸리며 말했고, 그 작은 악동들은 그 모습에 신이 났어요."]], [["\"I promised I would, and he departed; but it seems as if I was doomed to see a good deal of him, for to-day, as I passed his door on my way out, by accident I knocked against it with my umbrella. It flew open, and there he stood in his dressing gown, with a big blue sock on one hand, and a darning-needle in the other; he didn't seem at all ashamed of it, for when I explained and hurried on, he waved his hand, sock and all, saying in his loud, cheerful way,--", "저는 그러겠다고 했고, 그분은 가셨어요. 그런데 아무래도 저는 그분을 자주 마주칠 운명인가 봐요. 오늘 외출하다가 그분 방 앞을 지나는데, 그만 우산으로 문을 툭 치고 말았거든요. 문이 벌컥 열리더니, 그 안에 그분이 실내복 차림으로 서 있었어요. 한 손엔 커다란 파란 양말을 끼고, 다른 손엔 깁는 바늘을 들고서요. 그분은 전혀 부끄러워하는 기색이 없었어요. 제가 사정을 설명하고 서둘러 가려는데, 양말까지 낀 손을 흔들면서 특유의 우렁차고 명랑한 어투로 말씀하셨거든요——"]], [["\"'You haf a fine day to make your walk.", "'산책하기 좋은 날이군요."], ["_Bon voyage, mademoiselle._'", "잘 다녀오세요, 마드무아젤.'"]], [["\"I laughed all the way downstairs; but it was a little pathetic, also, to think of the poor man having to mend his own clothes.", "저는 아래층까지 내려가는 내내 웃었어요. 하지만 가엾은 분이 직접 옷을 기워야 한다고 생각하니 좀 애처롭기도 했어요."], ["The German gentlemen embroider, I know; but darning hose is another thing, and not so pretty.\"", "독일 신사들이 자수는 놓는다지만, 양말 깁기는 또 다른 얘기고, 그리 보기 좋은 일도 아니잖아요.\""]], [["\"_Saturday._", "\"토요일."]], [["\"Nothing has happened to write about, except a call on Miss Norton, who has a room full of lovely things, and who was very charming, for she showed me all her treasures, and asked me if I would sometimes go with her to lectures and concerts, as her escort,--if I enjoyed them.", "쓸 만한 일이라곤 노턴 양을 방문한 것밖에 없네요. 그분 방은 사랑스러운 물건들로 가득했고, 그분은 무척 매력적이었어요."], ["She put it as a favor, but I'm sure Mrs. Kirke has told her about us, and she does it out of kindness to me.", "자기 보물을 죄다 보여 주고는, 제가 강연이나 음악회를 좋아한다면 가끔 자기와 함께 동행자로 가지 않겠냐고 물으셨거든요."], ["I'm as proud as Lucifer, but such favors from such people don't burden me, and I accepted gratefully.", "호의처럼 말씀하셨지만, 분명 커크 부인이 우리 사정을 얘기해서 저를 배려해 주시려는 거예요. 저는 루시퍼처럼 자존심이 세지만, 그런 분들의 그런 호의는 부담스럽지 않아서, 감사히 받아들였어요."]], [["\"When I got back to the nursery there was such an uproar in the parlor that I looked in; and there was Mr.", "육아실로 돌아오니 응접실에서 어찌나 시끌벅적한지 들여다봤어요."], ["Bhaer down on his hands and knees, with Tina on his back, Kitty leading him with a jump-rope, and Minnie feeding two small boys with seed-cakes, as they roared and ramped in cages built of chairs.", "베어 씨가 손과 무릎을 바닥에 짚고 엎드려 있는데 티나가 그 등에 올라타 있고, 키티는 줄넘기 줄로 그분을 끌고 다니고, 미니는 의자로 만든 우리 안에서 으르렁대며 날뛰는 사내아이 둘에게 씨앗 케이크를 먹이고 있었어요."]], [["\"'We are playing _nargerie_,'", "'우리 동물원 놀이 하는 거예요.'"], ["explained Kitty.", "키티가 설명했어요."]], [["\"'Dis is mine effalunt!'", "'이건 내 코끼리예요!'"], ["added Tina, holding on by the Professor's hair.", "티나가 교수의 머리칼을 붙들고 덧붙였어요."]], [["\"'Mamma always allows us to do what we like Saturday afternoon, when Franz and Emil come, doesn't she, Mr.", "'엄마는 토요일 오후에 프란츠랑 에밀이 오면 우리가 하고 싶은 대로 하게 늘 허락해 주세요, 그렇죠, 베어 씨?'"], ["Bhaer?' said Minnie.", "미니가 말했어요."]], [["\"The 'effalunt' sat up, looking as much in earnest as any of them, and said soberly to me,--", "그 '코끼리'가 몸을 일으켜 앉더니, 누구 못지않게 진지한 표정으로 저한테 차분히 말했어요——"]], [["\"'I gif you my wort it is so.", "'정말입니다, 내 약속하지요."], ["If we make too large a noise you shall say \"Hush!\"", "우리가 너무 시끄럽게 굴거든 \"쉿!\""], ["to us, and we go more softly.'", "하고 말해 주세요, 그럼 우린 더 조용히 할 테니까요.'"]], [["\"I promised to do so, but left the door open, and enjoyed the fun as much as they did,--for a more glorious frolic I never witnessed.", "저는 그러겠다고 약속했지만, 문을 열어 둔 채 아이들 못지않게 그 놀이를 즐겼어요. 그렇게 신나는 한바탕 놀이는 본 적이 없거든요."], ["They played tag and soldiers, danced and sung, and when it began to grow dark they all piled on to the sofa about the Professor, while he told charming fairy stories of the storks on the chimney-tops, and the little 'kobolds,'", "술래잡기와 병정놀이를 하고, 춤추고 노래하다가, 날이 어둑해지자 모두 교수를 둘러싸고 소파에 우르르 올라앉았고, 그분은 굴뚝 꼭대기에 사는 황새들과, 눈송이가 떨어질 때 그걸 타고 내려오는 작은 '코볼트들'"], ["who ride the snow-flakes as they fall.", "이야기 같은 멋진 동화를 들려줬어요."], ["I wish Americans were as simple and natural as Germans, don't you?", "미국 사람들도 독일 사람들처럼 소박하고 꾸밈없으면 좋겠어요, 그렇지 않아요?"]], [["\"I'm so fond of writing, I should go spinning on forever if motives of economy didn't stop me, for though I've used thin paper and written fine, I tremble to think of the stamps this long letter will need.", "전 글쓰기를 워낙 좋아해서, 절약을 생각하지 않았다면 한도 끝도 없이 써 내려갔을 거예요. 얇은 종이에 잔글씨로 썼는데도, 이 긴 편지에 우표가 얼마나 들지 생각하면 아찔하거든요."], ["Pray forward Amy's as soon as you can spare them.", "에이미 편지는 여유 되는 대로 어서 부쳐 주세요."], ["My small news will sound very flat after her splendors, but you will like them, I know.", "제 자잘한 소식은 그 애의 화려한 소식 뒤에 들으면 무척 시시하겠지만, 두 분은 좋아하실 거 알아요."], ["Is Teddy studying so hard that he can't find time to write to his friends?", "테디는 친구들한테 편지 쓸 틈도 없을 만큼 그렇게 열심히 공부하는 거예요?"], ["Take good care of him for me, Beth, and tell me all about the babies, and give heaps of love to every one.", "저 대신 그 애 잘 챙겨 줘, 베스. 그리고 아기들 얘기도 다 들려주고, 모두에게 사랑을 듬뿍 전해 줘."]], [["\"From your faithful JO.", "언제나 변함없는 너희의 조가."]], [["\"P. S.", "추신."], ["On reading over my letter it strikes me as rather Bhaery; but I am always interested in odd people, and I really had nothing else to write about.", "편지를 다시 읽어 보니 좀 베어 교수 얘기투성이 같네요. 하지만 저는 별난 사람들한테 늘 관심이 가고, 정말 달리 쓸 거리가 없었거든요."], ["Bless you!\"", "모두 잘 지내요!\" \"12월."]], [["\"As this is to be a scribble-scrabble letter, I direct it to you, for it may amuse you, and give you some idea of my goings on; for, though quiet, they are rather amusing, for which, oh, be joyful! After what Amy would call Herculaneum efforts, in the way of mental and moral agriculture, my young ideas begin to shoot and my little twigs to bend as I could wish. They are not so interesting to me as Tina and the boys, but I do my duty by them, and they are fond of me. Franz and Emil are jolly little lads, quite after my own heart; for the mixture of German and American spirit in them produces a constant state of effervescence. Saturday afternoons are riotous times, whether spent in the house or out; for on pleasant days they all go to walk, like a seminary, with the Professor and myself to keep order; and then such fun!", "사랑하는 우리 베치에게,\n이건 갈겨쓰는 두서없는 편지가 될 테니까 너한테 보낸다. 네가 재밌어할 테고, 내가 어떻게 지내는지 짐작도 될 거야. 조용히 지내긴 해도 꽤 재밌게 지내거든. 그러니 자, 기뻐하렴! 에이미라면 '헤르쿨라네움 같은 노력'이라고 부를 만한 정신적·도덕적 농사를 지은 끝에, 내 어린 새싹들이 움트기 시작하고, 어린 잔가지들이 내가 바라는 대로 휘어지고 있어. 그 애들은 티나나 사내아이들만큼 흥미롭진 않지만, 난 그 애들한테 내 본분을 다하고, 그 애들도 날 좋아해. 프란츠랑 에밀은 유쾌한 꼬맹이들인데, 딱 내 마음에 들어. 그 애들 안에 독일과 미국의 기질이 뒤섞여 있어서 늘 부글부글 끓어오르는 상태거든. 토요일 오후는 집 안에서 보내든 밖에서 보내든 시끌벅적한 시간이야. 날이 좋으면 다들 학당처럼 줄지어 산책을 나가는데, 교수와 내가 질서를 잡는 역할을 맡아. 그러면 얼마나 재밌는지!"]], [["\"We are very good friends now, and I've begun to take lessons.", "우린 이제 무척 좋은 친구가 됐고, 난 강습도 받기 시작했어."], ["I really couldn't help it, and it all came about in such a droll way that I must tell you. To begin at the beginning, Mrs.", "정말 안 받을 수가 없었는데, 그 사연이 어찌나 우스꽝스러운지 꼭 들려줘야겠어."], ["Kirke called to me, one day, as I passed Mr. Bhaer's room, where she was rummaging.", "처음부터 얘기하자면, 어느 날 내가 베어 씨 방 앞을 지나는데 커크 부인이 그 안에서 뭔가를 뒤지다가 날 부르셨어."]], [["\"'Did you ever see such a den, my dear?", "'얘야, 이런 굴 본 적 있니?"], ["Just come and help me put these books to rights, for I've turned everything upside down, trying to discover what he has done with the six new handkerchiefs I gave him not long ago.'", "어서 와서 이 책들 정리하는 것 좀 도와주렴. 얼마 전에 내가 준 새 손수건 여섯 장을 이분이 어떻게 했는지 찾아내려고 죄다 뒤집어엎어 놨거든.'"]], [["\"I went in, and while we worked I looked about me, for it was 'a den,' to be sure.", "들어가서 정리하면서 둘러봤는데, 정말이지 '굴'이 따로 없더라."], ["Books and papers everywhere; a broken meerschaum, and an old flute over the mantel-piece as if done with; a ragged bird, without any tail, chirped on one window-seat, and a box of white mice adorned the other; half-finished boats and bits of string lay among the manuscripts; dirty little boots stood drying before the fire; and traces of the dearly beloved boys, for whom he makes a slave of himself, were to be seen all over the room.", "책이랑 종이가 사방에 널려 있고, 벽난로 선반 위엔 깨진 해포석 파이프랑 다 쓴 듯한 낡은 플루트가 놓여 있고, 꽁지 빠진 너덜너덜한 새 한 마리가 한쪽 창가에서 짹짹거리고, 다른 쪽 창가는 흰 쥐가 든 상자가 장식하고 있었어. 만들다 만 배들이랑 끈 토막이 원고들 사이에 널려 있고, 더러운 작은 장화가 난롯불 앞에서 마르고 있고, 그분이 종처럼 헌신하는 그 사랑스러운 사내아이들의 흔적이 온 방에 가득했어."], ["After a grand rummage three of the missing articles were found,--one over the bird-cage, one covered with ink, and a third burnt brown, having been used as a holder.", "한바탕 뒤진 끝에 잃어버린 손수건 중 세 장을 찾았는데, 하나는 새장 위에 있고, 하나는 잉크범벅이 됐고, 또 하나는 냄비 받침으로 쓰여서 갈색으로 그을려 있었어."]], [["\"'Such a man!'", "'사람도 참!'"], ["laughed good-natured Mrs. K., as she put the relics in the rag-bag.", "마음씨 좋은 커크 부인이 그 잔해들을 헝겊 자루에 넣으며 웃으셨어."], ["'I suppose the others are torn up to rig ships, bandage cut fingers, or make kite-tails.", "'나머지는 배 돛 만드느라, 베인 손가락 싸매느라, 아니면 연 꼬리 만드느라 찢어 버렸겠지."], ["It's dreadful, but I can't scold him: he's so absent-minded and good-natured, he lets those boys ride over him rough-shod.", "한심하긴 하지만 그분을 나무랄 수가 없어. 어찌나 정신이 없고 너그러운지, 저 사내애들이 자길 마구잡이로 부려 먹게 내버려 두거든."], ["I agreed to do his washing and mending, but he forgets to give out his things and I forget to look them over, so he comes to a sad pass sometimes.'", "내가 그분 빨래랑 수선을 맡아 주기로 했는데, 그분은 내놓을 걸 내놓는 걸 잊고, 나는 또 그걸 살피는 걸 잊어버려서, 그분이 가끔 딱한 처지가 된단다.'"]], [["\"'Let me mend them,'", "'제가 수선할게요.'"], ["said I.", "내가 말했어."], ["'I don't mind it, and he needn't know.", "'저는 괜찮고, 그분이 몰라도 되잖아요."], ["I'd like to,--he's so kind to me about bringing my letters and lending books.'", "저도 하고 싶어요. 제 편지 가져다주고 책 빌려주고, 그분이 저한테 어찌나 친절하신데요.'"]], [["\"So I have got his things in order, and knit heels into two pairs of the socks,--for they were boggled out of shape with his queer darns.", "그래서 그분 물건을 정리해 두고, 양말 두 켤레엔 뒤꿈치를 떠 넣었어. 그분의 별난 깁기 솜씨 탓에 모양이 다 뒤틀려 있었거든."], ["Nothing was said, and I hoped he wouldn't find it out, but one day last week he caught me at it.", "아무 말도 안 했고 그분이 알아채지 못하길 바랐는데, 지난주 어느 날 나를 딱 붙잡고 마셨지 뭐야."], ["Hearing the lessons he gives to others has interested and amused me so much that I took a fancy to learn; for Tina runs in and out, leaving the door open, and I can hear.", "그분이 남들한테 해 주는 강습을 듣다 보니 어찌나 흥미롭고 재밌던지 나도 배우고 싶어졌거든. 티나가 들락날락하면서 문을 열어 둬서 들을 수 있었어."], ["I had been sitting near this door, finishing off the last sock, and trying to understand what he said to a new scholar, who is as stupid as I am.", "나는 이 문 가까이 앉아 마지막 양말을 마무리하면서, 그분이 나만큼이나 아둔한 새 학생에게 하는 말을 알아들어 보려 애쓰고 있었어."], ["The girl had gone, and I thought he had also, it was so still, and I was busily gabbling over a verb, and rocking to and fro in a most absurd way, when a little crow made me look up, and there was Mr.", "그 여학생은 가고, 너무 조용해서 그분도 갔거니 했는데, 나는 동사 하나를 부지런히 중얼거리면서 우스꽝스럽기 짝이 없게 몸을 앞뒤로 흔들고 있었어."], ["Bhaer looking and laughing quietly, while he made signs to Tina not to betray him.", "그런데 작은 웃음소리에 고개를 들어 보니, 베어 씨가 나를 보며 조용히 웃고 있고, 티나한테는 자기를 들키지 말라고 손짓을 하고 있는 거야."]], [["\"'So!' he said, as I stopped and stared like a goose, 'you peep at me, I peep at you, and that is not bad; but see, I am not pleasanting when I say, haf you a wish for German?'", "'그것 보세요!' 내가 멈추고 거위처럼 멍하니 쳐다보자 그분이 말했어. '당신은 나를 엿보고, 나는 당신을 엿보고, 그것도 나쁘지 않지요. 하지만 보세요, 농담이 아니고 진지하게 묻는 겁니다. 독일어를 배우고 싶은 마음이 있나요?'"]], [["\"'Yes; but you are too busy.", "'네, 하지만 너무 바쁘시잖아요."], ["I am too stupid to learn,'", "저는 너무 아둔해서 못 배워요.'"], ["I blundered out, as red as a peony.", "나는 작약처럼 얼굴이 새빨개진 채 더듬더듬 말했어."]], [["\"'Prut!", "'프루트!"], ["we will make the time, and we fail not to find the sense.", "시간이야 만들면 되고, 우리는 뜻을 찾아내는 데 실패하지 않을 겁니다."], ["At efening I shall gif a little lesson with much gladness; for, look you, Mees Marsch, I haf this debt to pay,'", "저녁에 내 아주 기쁜 마음으로 짧은 강습을 해 드리지요. 그게 말입니다, 마치 양, 나한텐 갚아야 할 빚이 있으니까요.'"], ["and he pointed to my work.", "그러면서 내 일감을 가리켰어. '그래요, 그 친절한 부인들은 자기들끼리 이렇게 말하지요."], ["'Yes, they say to one another, these so kind ladies, \"he is a stupid old fellow; he will see not what we do; he will never opserve that his sock-heels go not in holes any more, he will think his buttons grow out new when they fall, and believe that strings make theirselves.\"", "\"저이는 아둔한 늙은이라, 우리가 뭘 하는지 못 볼 거야. 자기 양말 뒤꿈치에 더는 구멍이 안 난다는 걸 결코 알아채지 못할 거고, 떨어진 단추가 새로 돋아난 줄 알 거고, 끈이 저절로 생기는 줄 믿겠지.\""], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["but I haf an eye, and I see much.", "하지만 나한텐 눈이 있고, 보는 게 많아요."], ["I haf a heart, and I feel the thanks for this.", "나한텐 마음이 있고, 이에 대한 고마움을 느끼지요."], ["Come, a little lesson then and now, or no more good fairy works for me and mine.'", "자, 그러니 지금 당장 짧은 강습을 시작합시다. 안 그러면 나와 내 식구를 위한 착한 요정의 일도 더는 없을 테니까요.'"]], [["\"Of course I couldn't say anything after that, and as it really is a splendid opportunity, I made the bargain, and we began.", "그러니 그 뒤로 무슨 말을 할 수 있었겠어. 게다가 정말 멋진 기회라, 약속을 맺고 시작했어."], ["I took four lessons, and then I stuck fast in a grammatical bog.", "네 번 강습을 받았는데, 그러다 문법의 수렁에 단단히 빠지고 말았어."], ["The Professor was very patient with me, but it must have been torment to him, and now and then he'd look at me with such an expression of mild despair that it was a toss-up with me whether to laugh or cry.", "교수는 나한테 무척 인내심을 발휘했지만, 그분한텐 분명 고역이었을 거야. 이따금 나를 그렇게 온화한 절망의 표정으로 바라보는데, 웃어야 할지 울어야 할지 갈팡질팡할 정도였어."], ["I tried both ways; and when it came to a sniff of utter mortification and woe, he just threw the grammar on to the floor, and marched out of the room.", "둘 다 해 봤지. 그러다 도저히 못 참겠다는 굴욕과 비탄의 콧소리가 나오는 지경에 이르자, 그분은 문법책을 바닥에 휙 던져 버리고 방을 나가 버렸어."], ["I felt myself disgraced and deserted forever, but didn't blame him a particle, and was scrambling my papers together, meaning to rush upstairs and shake myself hard, when in he came, as brisk and beaming as if I'd covered myself with glory.", "나는 영영 망신당하고 버림받은 기분이 들었지만, 그분을 조금도 탓하진 않았어. 위층으로 달려가 나 자신을 호되게 흔들어 줄 작정으로 종이들을 주섬주섬 챙기고 있는데, 마치 내가 영광에 휩싸이기라도 한 듯 활기차고 환한 얼굴로 그분이 들어오는 거야."]], [["\"'Now we shall try a new way.", "'이제 새로운 방법을 써 봅시다."], ["You and I will read these pleasant little Märchen together, and dig no more in that dry book, that goes in the corner for making us trouble.'", "당신과 내가 이 즐겁고 짤막한 메르헨을 함께 읽기로 해요. 우리를 괴롭히는 저 메마른 책은 더 파지 말고, 구석으로 치워 버리는 겁니다.'"]], [["\"He spoke so kindly, and opened Hans Andersen's fairy tales so invitingly before me, that I was more ashamed than ever, and went at my lesson in a neck-or-nothing style that seemed to amuse him immensely.", "어찌나 다정하게 말씀하면서, 한스 안데르센의 동화책을 내 앞에 어찌나 솔깃하게 펼쳐 놓는지, 나는 어느 때보다 부끄러워져서, 죽기 살기 식으로 강습에 덤벼들었어. 그게 그분을 무척 즐겁게 한 것 같더라."], ["I forgot my bashfulness, and pegged away (no other word will express it) with all my might, tumbling over long words, pronouncing according to the inspiration of the minute, and doing my very best.", "나는 수줍음도 잊고 (이보다 더 적확한 표현은 없을 거야) 죽어라 매달려서, 긴 단어에서 자빠지고, 그때그때 영감 가는 대로 발음하면서, 정말 온 힘을 다했어."], ["When I finished reading my first page, and stopped for breath, he clapped his hands and cried out, in his hearty way, 'Das ist gute!", "첫 쪽을 다 읽고 숨을 돌리려 멈추자, 그분은 손뼉을 치며 특유의 진심 어린 어투로 외쳤어. '잘했어요!"], ["Now we go well!", "이제 잘되는군요!"], ["My turn.", "내 차례입니다."], ["I do him in German; gif me your ear.'", "독일어로 읽어 줄 테니, 귀를 기울여 보세요.'"], ["And away he went, rumbling out the words with his strong voice, and a relish which was good to see as well as hear.", "그러고는 우렁찬 목소리로 단어들을 우르릉우르릉 굴려 가며 읽어 내려갔는데, 듣기에도 보기에도 좋은 그 흥겨움이라니. 다행히 그 이야기가 '꿋꿋한 양철 병정'이었거든."], ["Fortunately the story was the 'Constant Tin Soldier,'", "너도 알다시피 우스운 이야기라 나도 웃을 수 있었어. 정말 웃었지."], ["which is droll, you know, so I could laugh,--and I did,--though I didn't understand half he read, for I couldn't help it, he was so earnest, I so excited, and the whole thing so comical.", "그분이 읽는 걸 절반도 못 알아들었지만, 어쩔 수가 없었어. 그분은 너무 진지하고, 나는 너무 들떴고, 모든 게 너무 우스꽝스러웠거든."]], [["\"After that we got on better, and now I read my lessons pretty well; for this way of studying suits me, and I can see that the grammar gets tucked into the tales and poetry as one gives pills in jelly.", "그 뒤로 우리는 더 잘 진도를 나갔고, 이제 난 강습 내용을 꽤 잘 읽어. 이런 공부 방식이 나한테 딱 맞거든."], ["I like it very much, and he doesn't seem tired of it yet,--which is very good of him, isn't it?", "보니까 문법이 이야기랑 시 속에 쏙쏙 박혀 들어오더라, 마치 알약을 젤리에 싸서 먹이듯이. 난 그게 무척 좋고, 그분은 아직 싫증을 안 내는 것 같아."], ["I mean to give him something on Christmas, for I dare not offer money.", "정말 갸륵하지 않니? 크리스마스 때 그분께 뭔가 드리고 싶어."], ["Tell me something nice, Marmee.", "돈은 차마 못 드리겠으니까. 뭐 좋은 게 없을까, 엄마, 좀 알려 줘요."]], [["\"I'm glad Laurie seems so happy and busy, that he has given up smoking, and lets his hair grow.", "로리가 그렇게 행복하고 바쁘게 지내고, 담배도 끊고, 머리도 기르고 있다니 기뻐."], ["You see Beth manages him better than I did.", "봐, 베스가 나보다 그 앨 더 잘 다루잖아."], ["I'm not jealous, dear; do your best, only don't make a saint of him.", "질투하는 거 아니야, 동생아. 네 최선을 다해, 다만 그 애를 성인군자로 만들진 마."], ["I'm afraid I couldn't like him without a spice of human naughtiness.", "사람다운 장난기가 좀 없으면 그 앨 좋아하기 어려울 것 같거든."], ["Read him bits of my letters.", "그 애한테 내 편지 몇 토막 읽어 줘."], ["I haven't time to write much, and that will do just as well.", "나는 길게 쓸 시간이 없으니, 그걸로도 충분할 거야."], ["Thank Heaven Beth continues so comfortable.\"", "베스가 계속 이렇게 편안하다니, 하느님 감사합니다.\" \"1월."]], [["\"A Happy New Year to you all, my dearest family, which of course includes Mr.", "새해 복 많이 받으세요, 사랑하는 우리 식구들 모두. 물론 여기엔 L."], ["L. and a young man by the name of Teddy.", "어르신과 테디라는 이름의 청년도 포함이에요."], ["I can't tell you how much I enjoyed your Christmas bundle, for I didn't get it till night, and had given up hoping.", "크리스마스 꾸러미가 얼마나 기뻤는지 이루 다 말할 수가 없어요. 밤이 돼서야 받아서, 이젠 틀렸구나 단념하고 있었거든요."], ["Your letter came in the morning, but you said nothing about a parcel, meaning it for a surprise; so I was disappointed, for I'd had a 'kind of a feeling'", "편지는 아침에 왔는데, 두 분이 소포 얘긴 일언반구도 안 하셔서요. 놀라게 해 주려고 그러셨겠지만, 그래서 좀 서운했어요."], ["that you wouldn't forget me.", "두 분이 절 잊지 않으리라는 '느낌 비슷한 게' 있었거든요."], ["I felt a little low in my mind, as I sat up in my room, after tea; and when the big, muddy, battered-looking bundle was brought to me, I just hugged it, and pranced.", "차를 마신 뒤 방에 앉아 있는데 마음이 좀 가라앉았어요. 그런데 크고, 진흙 묻고, 너덜너덜해진 그 꾸러미가 제게 전해지자, 저는 그냥 그걸 끌어안고 깡충깡충 뛰었어요."], ["It was so _homey_ and refreshing, that I sat down on the floor and read and looked and ate and laughed and cried, in my usual absurd way.", "어찌나 정겹고 기운이 나던지, 바닥에 주저앉아 읽고 들여다보고 먹고 웃고 울고, 늘 그러는 우스꽝스러운 식으로 그랬어요."], ["The things were just what I wanted, and all the better for being made instead of bought.", "물건들은 죄다 제가 원하던 거였고, 산 게 아니라 손수 만든 거라 더더욱 좋았어요."], ["Beth's new 'ink-bib' was capital; and Hannah's box of hard gingerbread will be a treasure.", "베스의 새 '잉크 받이'는 일품이고, 해나가 준 딱딱한 생강과자 상자는 보물이 될 거예요."], ["I'll be sure and wear the nice flannels you sent, Marmee, and read carefully the books father has marked.", "엄마가 보내 주신 멋진 플란넬 옷은 꼭 챙겨 입고, 아빠가 표시해 두신 책들은 꼼꼼히 읽을게요."], ["Thank you all, heaps and heaps!", "모두 정말정말 고마워요!"]], [["\"Speaking of books reminds me that I'm getting rich in that line for, on New Year's Day, Mr.", "책 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데, 그 방면으로 제가 부자가 되어 가고 있어요. 새해 첫날에 베어 씨가 멋진 셰익스피어 전집을 주셨거든요."], ["Bhaer gave me a fine Shakespeare.", "그분이 무척 아끼는 책이라, 저도 자주 감탄하며 봤어요. 그분의 독일어 성경, 플라톤, 호메로스, 밀턴과 함께 가장 귀한 자리에 모셔져 있던 책이거든요."], ["It is one he values much, and I've often admired it, set up in the place of honor, with his German Bible, Plato, Homer, and Milton; so you may imagine how I felt when he brought it down, without its cover, and showed me my name in it, 'from my friend Friedrich Bhaer.'", "그러니 그분이 그걸 내려서, 표지도 떼고, 제 이름이 적힌 걸 보여 줬을 때 제 심정이 어땠을지 상상이 가실 거예요. '내 벗 프리드리히 베어가.'"]], [["\"'You say often you wish a library: here I gif you one; for between these lids (he meant covers) is many books in one. Read him well, and he will help you much; for the study of character in this book will help you to read it in the world and paint it with your pen.'", "'당신은 서재를 갖고 싶다는 말을 자주 했지요. 자, 여기 하나 드립니다. 이 표지 사이에는 (그분은 책장을 뜻했어요) 여러 책이 한 권에 담겨 있으니까요. 잘 읽으세요, 그러면 당신에게 큰 도움이 될 겁니다. 이 책에서 사람 됨됨이를 공부하면, 세상 속에서 그것을 읽어 내고 당신의 펜으로 그려 내는 데 도움이 될 테니까요.'"]], [["\"I thanked him as well as I could, and talk now about 'my library,'", "저는 할 수 있는 한 정성껏 감사를 표했고, 이제 책을 백 권이나 가진 양 '제 서재'"], ["as if I had a hundred books.", "얘기를 하고 다녀요."], ["I never knew how much there was in Shakespeare before; but then I never had a Bhaer to explain it to me.", "셰익스피어 안에 그렇게 많은 게 들어 있는 줄 예전엔 미처 몰랐어요. 하긴 그걸 설명해 줄 베어 씨가 없었으니까요."], ["Now _don't_ laugh at his horrid name; it isn't pronounced either Bear or Beer, as people _will_ say it, but something between the two, as only Germans can give it.", "자, 그분 이름이 끔찍하다고 비웃지 마요. 그건 사람들이 자꾸 그러듯이 '베어(곰)'도 '비어(맥주)'도 아니고, 그 중간쯤 되는, 독일 사람만 낼 수 있는 발음이거든요."], ["I'm glad you both like what I tell you about him, and hope you will know him some day.", "두 분 다 제가 그분에 대해 들려드린 얘기를 마음에 들어 하셔서 기뻐요. 언젠가 그분을 알게 되시면 좋겠어요."], ["Mother would admire his warm heart, father his wise head.", "엄마는 그분의 따뜻한 마음을, 아빠는 그분의 지혜로운 머리를 존경하실 거예요."], ["I admire both, and feel rich in my new 'friend Friedrich Bhaer.'", "저는 둘 다 존경하고, 제 새로운 '벗 프리드리히 베어' 덕분에 부자가 된 기분이에요."]], [["\"Not having much money, or knowing what he'd like, I got several little things, and put them about the room, where he would find them unexpectedly. They were useful, pretty, or funny,--a new standish on his table, a little vase for his flower,--he always has one, or a bit of green in a glass, to keep him fresh, he says,--and a holder for his blower, so that he needn't burn up what Amy calls 'mouchoirs.' I made it like those Beth invented,--a big butterfly with a fat body, and black and yellow wings, worsted feelers, and bead eyes. It took his fancy immensely, and he put it on his mantel-piece as an article of _vertu_; so it was rather a failure after all. Poor as he is, he didn't forget a servant or a child in the house; and not a soul here, from the French laundry-woman to Miss Norton, forgot him. I was so glad of that.", "돈도 많지 않고 그분이 뭘 좋아할지도 몰라서, 자잘한 것들을 몇 개 마련해서 방 여기저기에 놓아 두었어요. 그분이 뜻밖에 발견하도록요. 쓸모 있거나, 예쁘거나, 우스운 것들이었어요. 탁자에 놓을 새 잉크 스탠드, 꽃을 꽂을 작은 꽃병——그분은 늘 꽃 한 송이를, 아니면 유리잔에 푸른 잎사귀 한 가지를 꽂아 둬요. 그래야 기운이 난다나요——그리고 입김기에 씌울 받침도요. 에이미가 '무슈아르'라고 부르는 것(손수건)을 태워 먹지 않도록요. 그건 베스가 고안한 것처럼 만들었어요. 통통한 몸통에 검고 노란 날개가 달리고, 털실 더듬이에 구슬 눈이 박힌 커다란 나비로요. 그분이 그걸 어찌나 마음에 들어 하던지, 골동품처럼 벽난로 선반에 올려놓으시는 거예요. 그러니 결국 좀 실패한 셈이죠. 그분은 그렇게 가난하면서도, 이 집의 하인이며 아이며 한 사람도 빠뜨리지 않았어요. 그리고 여기 있는 누구 하나, 프랑스 세탁부에서 노턴 양에 이르기까지, 그분을 잊지 않았고요. 저는 그게 무척 기뻤어요."]], [["\"They got up a masquerade, and had a gay time New Year's Eve.", "다들 가장무도회를 열어서 섣달그믐을 흥겹게 보냈어요."], ["I didn't mean to go down, having no dress; but at the last minute, Mrs. Kirke remembered some old brocades, and Miss Norton lent me lace and feathers; so I dressed up as Mrs.", "저는 입을 옷이 없어서 내려가지 않으려 했는데, 막판에 커크 부인이 낡은 양단 옷 몇 벌을 떠올리셨고, 노턴 양이 레이스와 깃털을 빌려줘서, 저는 맬러프롭 부인으로 차려입고 가면을 쓴 채 들어섰어요."], ["Malaprop, and sailed in with a mask on.", "목소리를 바꿔서 아무도 절 못 알아봤어요."], ["No one knew me, for I disguised my voice, and no one dreamed of the silent, haughty Miss March (for they think I am very stiff and cool, most of them; and so I am to whipper-snappers) could dance and dress, and burst out into a 'nice derangement of epitaphs, like an allegory on the banks of the Nile.'", "말없고 도도한 마치 양이 (대다수가 절 무척 뻣뻣하고 차갑다고 여기거든요. 사실 풋내기들한텐 제가 그렇긴 해요) 춤추고 차려입고 '나일 강둑의 우의(寓意)처럼 묘비명을 멋들어지게 헝클어뜨리며'"], ["I enjoyed it very much; and when we unmasked, it was fun to see them stare at me.", "신나게 떠들 줄은 아무도 꿈에도 생각 못 했죠. 정말 즐거웠어요."], ["I heard one of the young men tell another that he knew I'd been an actress; in fact, he thought he remembered seeing me at one of the minor theatres.", "가면을 벗었을 때 다들 절 빤히 쳐다보는 걸 보는 게 재밌었어요. 젊은 남자 하나가 다른 남자한테 제가 배우였던 게 틀림없다고 하는 걸 들었어요."], ["Meg will relish that joke. Mr.", "실은 어느 군소 극장에서 절 본 기억이 난다나요."], ["Bhaer was Nick Bottom, and Tina was Titania,--a perfect little fairy in his arms.", "메그가 그 농담을 좋아할 거예요. 베어 씨는 닉 보텀으로 분장했고, 티나는 티타니아였어요."], ["To see them dance was 'quite a landscape,'", "그분 품에 안긴 완벽한 작은 요정이었죠. 둘이 춤추는 걸 보고 있자니, 테디식 표현을 빌리자면 '한 폭의 풍경화'"], ["to use a Teddyism.", "같았어요."]], [["\"I had a very happy New Year, after all; and when I thought it over in my room, I felt as if I was getting on a little in spite of my many failures; for I'm cheerful all the time now, work with a will, and take more interest in other people than I used to, which is satisfactory.", "이러니저러니 해도 저는 무척 행복한 새해를 맞았어요. 방에서 곰곰이 돌이켜 보니, 숱한 실패에도 불구하고 제가 조금씩 나아가고 있는 것 같은 기분이 들었어요."], ["Bless you all!", "요즘은 줄곧 명랑하고, 의욕을 가지고 일하고, 예전보다 다른 사람들한테 더 관심을 갖게 됐거든요. 그게 흐뭇해요."], ["Ever your loving", "모두에게 축복이 있기를! 언제나 너희를 사랑하는\n조가.\""]], [["Though very happy in the social atmosphere about her, and very busy with the daily work that earned her bread, and made it sweeter for the effort, Jo still found time for literary labors.", "자기를 둘러싼 사교적인 분위기 속에서 무척 행복하고, 자기 밥벌이가 되어 주는, 그리고 그 수고 덕분에 더 달콤해지는 일과로 무척 바빴지만, 조는 그래도 문학 작업을 위한 시간을 냈다. 지금 그녀를 사로잡은 목적은 가난하고 야심 찬 처녀에게는 자연스러운 것이었다."], ["The purpose which now took possession of her was a natural one to a poor and ambitious girl; but the means she took to gain her end were not the best.", "다만 그 목적을 이루기 위해 택한 수단이 최선은 아니었다. 돈이 권력을 가져다준다는 것을 그녀는 알았다."], ["She saw that money conferred power: money and power, therefore, she resolved to have; not to be used for herself alone, but for those whom she loved more than self.", "그러니 돈과 권력, 그것을 손에 넣기로 그녀는 결심했다. 자기 혼자만을 위해서가 아니라, 자기 자신보다 더 사랑하는 이들을 위해서였다."]], [["The dream of filling home with comforts, giving Beth everything she wanted, from strawberries in winter to an organ in her bedroom; going abroad herself, and always having _more_ than enough, so that she might indulge in the luxury of charity, had been for years Jo's most cherished castle in the air.", "집을 안락함으로 채우고, 베스에게 그 애가 바라는 건 무엇이든, 겨울철 딸기부터 침실에 둘 오르간까지 다 마련해 주고, 자신도 외국에 나가 보고, 늘 차고 넘칠 만큼 가져서 자선이라는 호사를 마음껏 누리는 꿈. 그것은 수년간 조가 가장 소중히 품어 온 공중누각이었다."]], [["The prize-story experience had seemed to open a way which might, after long travelling and much up-hill work lead to this delightful _château en Espagne_.", "현상 공모 입선의 경험은, 오랜 여정과 숱한 고된 노력 끝에 이 즐거운 공중누각으로 이어질지도 모를 길 하나를 열어 준 것 같았다."], ["But the novel disaster quenched her courage for a time, for public opinion is a giant which has frightened stouter-hearted Jacks on bigger bean-stalks than hers.", "그러나 그 소설의 참사가 한동안 그녀의 용기를 꺾어 놓았다. 여론이란 그녀의 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 콩나무 줄기에 올라탄, 그녀보다 더 담대한 잭들마저 겁먹게 한 거인이니까."], ["Like that immortal hero, she reposed awhile after the first attempt, which resulted in a tumble, and the least lovely of the giant's treasures, if I remember rightly.", "그 불멸의 영웅처럼, 그녀도 굴러떨어지는 것으로, 그리고 내 기억이 맞다면 거인의 보물 중 가장 보잘것없는 것을 얻는 것으로 끝난 첫 번째 시도 뒤에 한동안 쉬었다. 하지만 \"다시 일어나 또 한 번\"이라는 정신은 잭에게나 조에게나 똑같이 강했다."], ["But the \"up again and take another\"", "그래서 그녀는 다시 기어 올라갔다."], ["spirit was as strong in Jo as in Jack; so she scrambled up, on the shady side this time, and got more booty, but nearly left behind her what was far more precious than the money-bags.", "이번엔 그늘진 쪽으로. 그리고 더 많은 노획물을 손에 넣었지만, 돈 자루보다 훨씬 더 소중한 것을 하마터면 뒤에 두고 올 뻔했다."]], [["She took to writing sensation stories; for in those dark ages, even all-perfect America read rubbish.", "조는 선정적인 이야기를 쓰기 시작했다. 그 어두운 시대에는 완벽하기 그지없다는 미국조차도 쓰레기 같은 글을 읽었으니까."], ["She told no one, but concocted a \"thrilling tale,\"", "그녀는 아무에게도 말하지 않고 \"오싹한 이야기\""], ["and boldly carried it herself to Mr. Dashwood, editor of the \"Weekly Volcano.\"", "하나를 지어내, 대담하게도 그것을 직접 「위클리 볼케이노」의 편집장인 대시우드 씨에게 들고 갔다."], ["She had never read \"Sartor Resartus,\" but she had a womanly instinct that clothes possess an influence more powerful over many than the worth of character or the magic of manners.", "그녀는 「의상 철학」을 읽어 본 적은 없었지만, 옷차림이 많은 사람들에게 인격의 가치나 매너의 마력보다 더 강력한 영향을 미친다는 여자다운 직감은 있었다."], ["So she dressed herself in her best, and, trying to persuade herself that she was neither excited nor nervous, bravely climbed two pairs of dark and dirty stairs to find herself in a disorderly room, a cloud of cigar-smoke, and the presence of three gentlemen, sitting with their heels rather higher than their hats, which articles of dress none of them took the trouble to remove on her appearance.", "그래서 가장 좋은 옷으로 차려입고, 흥분하지도 긴장하지도 않았다고 스스로를 설득하려 애쓰면서, 용감하게 어둡고 더러운 계단 두 칸을 올라갔다. 그러자 어수선한 방과, 자욱한 시가 연기와, 모자보다 발뒤꿈치를 한참 더 높이 올린 채 앉아 있는 신사 셋이 나타났다."], ["Somewhat daunted by this reception, Jo hesitated on the threshold, murmuring in much embarrassment,--", "그들 중 누구도 그녀가 나타났다고 해서 그 모자라는 의복을 굳이 벗으려 들지 않았다. 이런 환대에 다소 기가 죽어, 조는 문턱에서 머뭇거리며 무척 당황한 채 중얼거렸다."]], [["\"Excuse me, I was looking for the 'Weekly Volcano'", "\"실례합니다, 「위클리 볼케이노」 사무실을 찾고 있었는데요."], ["office; I wished to see Mr. Dashwood.\"", "대시우드 씨를 뵙고 싶어서요.\""]], [["Down went the highest pair of heels, up rose the smokiest gentleman, and, carefully cherishing his cigar between his fingers, he advanced, with a nod, and a countenance expressive of nothing but sleep.", "가장 높이 올라가 있던 발뒤꿈치가 내려가고, 가장 연기에 휩싸인 신사가 일어나더니, 시가를 손가락 사이에 조심스레 끼운 채, 고개를 까딱하며 졸음 말고는 아무것도 드러나지 않는 표정으로 다가왔다."], ["Feeling that she must get through the matter somehow, Jo produced her manuscript, and, blushing redder and redder with each sentence, blundered out fragments of the little speech carefully prepared for the occasion.", "어떻게든 이 일을 해치워야 한다고 느낀 조는 원고를 꺼냈고, 한 문장 한 문장 점점 더 빨갛게 달아오르면서, 이 자리를 위해 공들여 준비한 짤막한 말을 토막토막 더듬거렸다."]], [["\"A friend of mine desired me to offer--a story--just as an experiment--would like your opinion--be glad to write more if this suits.\"", "\"제 친구가 한 편 제출해 보라고 해서요——이야기 한 편이요——그냥 시험 삼아서요——의견을 듣고 싶다고요——이게 괜찮으면 더 쓰고 싶다고요.\""]], [["While she blushed and blundered, Mr. Dashwood had taken the manuscript, and was turning over the leaves with a pair of rather dirty fingers, and casting critical glances up and down the neat pages.", "그녀가 얼굴을 붉히며 더듬대는 동안, 대시우드 씨는 원고를 받아 들고, 다소 더러운 손가락으로 책장을 넘기면서, 깔끔한 지면을 위아래로 비평하듯 훑어보았다."]], [["\"Not a first attempt, I take it?\"", "\"첫 시도는 아닌 것 같은데요?\""], ["observing that the pages were numbered, covered only on one side, and not tied up with a ribbon,--sure sign of a novice.", "책장에 번호가 매겨져 있고, 한쪽 면에만 글이 적혀 있으며, 리본으로 묶여 있지 않은 것을 보고 한 말이었다. 그것은 풋내기가 아니라는 확실한 표시였으니까."]], [["\"No, sir; she has had some experience, and got a prize for a tale in the 'Blarneystone Banner.'\"", "\"네, 그렇습니다. 그 친구는 경험이 좀 있고, 「블라니스톤 배너」에 실린 이야기로 상도 탔어요.\""]], [["\"Oh, did she?\" and Mr.", "\"오, 그랬군요.\""], ["Dashwood gave Jo a quick look, which seemed to take note of everything she had on, from the bow in her bonnet to the buttons on her boots.", "대시우드 씨는 조를 재빨리 한 번 훑어보았다. 보닛에 단 리본부터 장화의 단추까지, 그녀가 걸친 모든 것을 눈여겨보는 듯한 눈길이었다."], ["\"Well, you can leave it, if you like.", "\"음, 원하시면 두고 가셔도 됩니다."], ["We've more of this sort of thing on hand than we know what to do with at present; but I'll run my eye over it, and give you an answer next week.\"", "이런 종류는 지금 우리가 감당 못 할 만큼 쌓여 있긴 한데, 그래도 한번 훑어보고 다음 주에 답을 드리지요.\""]], [["Now, Jo did _not_ like to leave it, for Mr.", "이제 조는 그것을 두고 가고 싶지가 않았다."], ["Dashwood didn't suit her at all; but, under the circumstances, there was nothing for her to do but bow and walk away, looking particularly tall and dignified, as she was apt to do when nettled or abashed.", "대시우드 씨가 영 마음에 들지 않았기 때문이다. 하지만 사정이 그러하니, 고개를 숙여 인사하고 걸어 나오는 것 말고는 달리 할 수 있는 게 없었다."], ["Just then she was both; for it was perfectly evident, from the knowing glances exchanged among the gentlemen, that her little fiction of \"my friend\"", "그녀는 발끈하거나 무안해질 때면 으레 그러듯, 유난히 키가 크고 위엄 있어 보이는 모습으로 그렇게 했다. 마침 그 순간 그녀는 발끈하기도 무안하기도 했다."], ["was considered a good joke; and a laugh, produced by some inaudible remark of the editor, as he closed the door, completed her discomfiture.", "신사들이 주고받는 그 알 만하다는 눈길로 보아, \"제 친구\"라는 그녀의 작은 허구가 멋진 농담거리로 여겨졌다는 게 분명했기 때문이다. 그리고 편집장이 들리지 않게 무어라 한 말에 터져 나온 웃음소리가,\n그가 문을 닫는 순간, 그녀의 낭패감은 절정에 달했다."], ["Half resolving never to return, she went home, and worked off her irritation by stitching pinafores vigorously; and in an hour or two was cool enough to laugh over the scene, and long for next week.", "조는 다시는 돌아오지 않겠다고 반쯤 다짐하며 집으로 돌아갔고, 앞치마를 힘차게 꿰매면서 짜증을 풀어 냈다. 그러다 한두 시간 뒤에는 마음이 충분히 가라앉아 그 장면을 떠올리며 웃을 수 있었고, 다음 주가 기다려지기까지 했다."]], [["When she went again, Mr. Dashwood was alone, whereat she rejoiced; Mr.", "다시 찾아갔을 때 대시우드 씨는 혼자였고, 조는 그게 반가웠다."], ["Dashwood was much wider awake than before, which was agreeable; and Mr.", "대시우드 씨는 전보다 훨씬 또렷하게 깨어 있었고, 그것도 기분 좋은 일이었다."], ["Dashwood was not too deeply absorbed in a cigar to remember his manners: so the second interview was much more comfortable than the first.", "게다가 대시우드 씨는 시가에 너무 깊이 빠져 예의를 잊을 만큼은 아니었다. 그래서 두 번째 만남은 첫 번째보다 훨씬 편안했다."]], [["\"We'll take this\"", "\"이걸 채택하겠습니다.\""], ["(editors never say I), \"if you don't object to a few alterations.", "(편집자는 결코 '제가'라고 말하지 않는다.) \"몇 군데 손보는 데 이의가 없으시다면요."], ["It's too long, but omitting the passages I've marked will make it just the right length,\"", "너무 길지만, 제가 표시한 부분을 빼면 딱 적당한 길이가 될 겁니다.\""], ["he said, in a business-like tone.", "그가 사무적인 어조로 말했다."]], [["Jo hardly knew her own MS.", "조는 자기 원고를 거의 알아보지 못할 지경이었다."], ["again, so crumpled and underscored were its pages and paragraphs; but, feeling as a tender parent might on being asked to cut off her baby's legs in order that it might fit into a new cradle, she looked at the marked passages, and was surprised to find that all the moral reflections--which she had carefully put in as ballast for much romance--had been stricken out.", "종이마다, 문단마다 어찌나 구겨지고 줄이 그어져 있던지. 그러나 새 요람에 맞추기 위해 아기의 다리를 잘라 내라는 요구를 받은 자상한 부모의 심정이 되어 표시된 부분들을 살펴보던 조는, 많은 낭만에 무게중심을 잡아 주려고 정성껏 끼워 넣었던 도덕적 성찰들이 죄다 삭제되어 있는 것을 발견하고 깜짝 놀랐다."]], [["\"But, sir, I thought every story should have some sort of a moral, so I took care to have a few of my sinners repent.\"", "\"하지만 선생님, 저는 모든 이야기에는 어떤 식으로든 교훈이 있어야 한다고 생각해서, 일부러 죄인들 몇몇을 뉘우치게 만들었는데요.\""]], [["Mr. Dashwood's editorial gravity relaxed into a smile, for Jo had forgotten her \"friend,\"", "대시우드 씨의 편집자다운 근엄함이 누그러지며 미소로 바뀌었다."], ["and spoken as only an author could.", "조가 자기 \"친구\" 이야기를 깜빡 잊고 오직 작가만이 할 법한 말을 했기 때문이다."]], [["\"People want to be amused, not preached at, you know.", "\"사람들은 즐거움을 원하지, 설교를 듣고 싶어 하지 않거든요."], ["Morals don't sell nowadays;\"", "요즘 교훈은 안 팔립니다.\""], ["which was not quite a correct statement, by the way.", "덧붙이자면, 이건 그다지 정확한 말은 아니었다."]], [["\"You think it would do with these alterations, then?\"", "\"그럼 이렇게 손보면 괜찮겠다는 말씀이신가요?\""]], [["\"Yes; it's a new plot, and pretty well worked up--language good, and so on,\"", "\"네, 새로운 줄거리에다 꽤 잘 다듬어졌습니다. 문장도 좋고요.\""], ["was Mr. Dashwood's affable reply.", "대시우드 씨의 상냥한 대답이었다."]], [["\"What do you--that is, what compensation--\"", "\"그러면 얼마나, 그러니까 보수는 얼마나—\""], ["began Jo, not exactly knowing how to express herself.", "조는 어떻게 말을 꺼내야 할지 정확히 알지 못한 채 입을 열었다."]], [["\"Oh, yes, well, we give from twenty-five to thirty for things of this sort.", "\"아, 네, 그렇죠. 이런 종류의 글에는 이십오 달러에서 삼십 달러까지 드립니다."], ["Pay when it comes out,\" returned Mr.", "게재될 때 지급하고요.\""], ["Dashwood, as if that point had escaped him; such trifles often do escape the editorial mind, it is said.", "마치 그 부분을 깜빡했다는 듯이 대시우드 씨가 대답했다. 그런 사소한 일은 편집자의 머릿속에서 곧잘 빠져나가곤 한다지 않는가."]], [["\"Very well; you can have it,\"", "\"좋아요, 이걸 가지셔도 돼요.\""], ["said Jo, handing back the story, with a satisfied air; for, after the dollar-a-column work, even twenty-five seemed good pay.", "조는 만족스러운 표정으로 원고를 다시 건네며 말했다. 한 단(段)에 일 달러짜리 일을 해 온 처지로서는 이십오 달러도 후한 보수처럼 느껴졌던 것이다."]], [["\"Shall I tell my friend you will take another if she has one better than this?\"", "\"제 친구가 이것보다 더 나은 글을 가지고 있으면, 또 받아 주시겠다고 전해도 될까요?\""], ["asked Jo, unconscious of her little slip of the tongue, and emboldened by her success.", "조는 자기도 모르게 말이 헛나온 것을 의식하지 못한 채, 성공에 용기를 얻어 물었다."]], [["\"Well, we'll look at it; can't promise to take it.", "\"글쎄요, 보긴 보겠습니다만 채택을 약속할 순 없습니다."], ["Tell her to make it short and spicy, and never mind the moral.", "짧고 자극적으로 쓰라고, 교훈은 신경 쓰지 말라고 전하세요."], ["What name would your friend like to put to it?\"", "친구분은 어떤 이름을 붙이고 싶어 하시나요?\""], ["in a careless tone.", "무심한 어조였다."]], [["\"None at all, if you please; she doesn't wish her name to appear, and has no _nom de plume_,\"", "\"이름은 아예 안 넣었으면 해요. 제 친구는 자기 이름이 드러나는 걸 원치 않고, 필명도 없거든요.\""], ["said Jo, blushing in spite of herself.", "조는 자기도 모르게 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."]], [["\"Just as she likes, of course.", "\"물론 본인 뜻대로 하셔야죠."], ["The tale will be out next week; will you call for the money, or shall I send it?\"", "이 이야기는 다음 주에 나올 겁니다. 돈은 찾으러 오시겠습니까, 아니면 제가 보내 드릴까요?\""], ["asked Mr.", "대시우드 씨가 물었다."], ["Dashwood, who felt a natural desire to know who his new contributor might be.", "새 기고자가 누구일지 알고 싶은 자연스러운 호기심이 일었던 것이다."]], [["\"I'll call.", "\"찾으러 오겠어요."], ["Good morning, sir.\"", "안녕히 계세요, 선생님.\""]], [["As she departed, Mr.", "조가 떠나자 대시우드 씨는 발을 책상에 올려놓으며 우아하게 한마디 했다."], ["Dashwood put up his feet, with the graceful remark, \"Poor and proud, as usual, but she'll do.\"", "\"여느 때처럼 가난하고 자존심 세군. 하지만 쓸 만하겠어.\""]], [["Following Mr. Dashwood's directions, and making Mrs.", "대시우드 씨의 지시를 따르고 노스베리 부인을 본보기 삼아, 조는 무모하게도 선정적인 문학의 거품 이는 바다로 풍덩 뛰어들었다."], ["Northbury her model, Jo rashly took a plunge into the frothy sea of sensational literature; but, thanks to the life-preserver thrown her by a friend, she came up again, not much the worse for her ducking.", "그러나 한 친구가 던져 준 구명대 덕분에 그녀는 다시 떠올랐고, 물에 빠진 것치고는 그리 큰 해를 입지 않았다."]], [["Like most young scribblers, she went abroad for her characters and scenery; and banditti, counts, gypsies, nuns, and duchesses appeared upon her stage, and played their parts with as much accuracy and spirit as could be expected.", "여느 젊은 글쟁이들처럼 조도 인물과 배경을 외국에서 가져왔다. 그리하여 산적과 백작, 집시와 수녀, 공작 부인이 그녀의 무대에 등장해, 기대할 수 있는 만큼의 정확함과 활기로 제 역할을 해냈다."], ["Her readers were not particular about such trifles as grammar, punctuation, and probability, and Mr.", "그녀의 독자들은 문법이나 구두점, 개연성 따위의 사소한 것에는 까다롭지 않았고, 대시우드 씨는 가장 낮은 값으로 자기 지면을 채우도록 너그러이 허락했다."], ["Dashwood graciously permitted her to fill his columns at the lowest prices, not thinking it necessary to tell her that the real cause of his hospitality was the fact that one of his hacks, on being offered higher wages, had basely left him in the lurch.", "그가 이렇게 후한 진짜 이유가, 글품팔이꾼 하나가 더 높은 보수를 제안받고 비열하게도 자기를 곤경에 빠뜨린 채 떠나 버렸기 때문이라는 사실은 굳이 그녀에게 말할 필요가 없다고 여겼다."]], [["She soon became interested in her work, for her emaciated purse grew stout, and the little hoard she was making to take Beth to the mountains next summer grew slowly but surely as the weeks passed.", "조는 곧 자기 일에 흥미를 붙였다. 비쩍 마른 지갑이 두둑해졌고, 다음 여름에 베스를 산으로 데려가려고 모으던 작은 돈도 몇 주가 지나면서 느리지만 확실하게 불어났기 때문이다."], ["One thing disturbed her satisfaction, and that was that she did not tell them at home.", "한 가지가 그녀의 만족에 그늘을 드리웠는데, 바로 집에 이 일을 말하지 않았다는 점이었다."], ["She had a feeling that father and mother would not approve, and preferred to have her own way first, and beg pardon afterward.", "아버지와 어머니가 찬성하지 않으리라는 느낌이 있어서, 일단 제멋대로 한 다음 나중에 용서를 빌고 싶었던 것이다."], ["It was easy to keep her secret, for no name appeared with her stories; Mr.", "비밀을 지키기는 쉬웠다. 글에는 이름이 실리지 않았으니까."], ["Dashwood had, of course, found it out very soon, but promised to be dumb; and, for a wonder, kept his word.", "대시우드 씨야 물론 곧 알아챘지만 입을 다물겠다고 약속했고, 놀랍게도 그 약속을 지켰다."]], [["She thought it would do her no harm, for she sincerely meant to write nothing of which she should be ashamed, and quieted all pricks of conscience by anticipations of the happy minute when she should show her earnings and laugh over her well-kept secret.", "조는 이 일이 자기에게 해가 되지 않으리라 생각했다. 부끄러워할 만한 글은 결코 쓰지 않을 작정이었으니까. 그리고 언젠가 자기가 번 돈을 보여 주며 잘 지켜 온 비밀을 두고 함께 웃을 행복한 순간을 떠올리는 것으로 양심의 가책을 모두 가라앉혔다."]], [["But Mr.", "그러나 대시우드 씨는 짜릿한 이야기 외에는 무엇도 받아 주지 않았다."], ["Dashwood rejected any but thrilling tales; and, as thrills could not be produced except by harrowing up the souls of the readers, history and romance, land and sea, science and art, police records and lunatic asylums, had to be ransacked for the purpose.", "짜릿함이란 독자의 영혼을 갈가리 헤집어 놓는 것 말고는 만들어 낼 수 없는 것이어서, 역사와 로맨스, 육지와 바다, 과학과 예술, 경찰 기록과 정신병원까지 그 목적을 위해 샅샅이 뒤져야 했다. 조는 곧 자신의 순진한 경험으로는 사회의 밑바닥에 깔린 비극적 세계를 그저 몇 자락밖에 들여다보지 못했음을 깨달았다."], ["Jo soon found that her innocent experience had given her but few glimpses of the tragic world which underlies society; so, regarding it in a business light, she set about supplying her deficiencies with characteristic energy.", "그래서 이를 사업적인 관점으로 바라보며, 특유의 활력으로 부족한 부분을 채우는 일에 착수했다. 이야기 소재를 찾는 데 열심이었고, 솜씨는 빼어나지 못해도 줄거리만은 독창적으로 만들겠다는 일념으로, 그녀는 신문에서 사고와 사건과 범죄를 뒤졌다."], ["Eager to find material for stories, and bent on making them original in plot, if not masterly in execution, she searched newspapers for accidents, incidents, and crimes; she excited the suspicions of public librarians by asking for works on poisons; she studied faces in the street, and characters, good, bad, and indifferent, all about her; she delved in the dust of ancient times for facts or fictions so old that they were as good as new, and introduced herself to folly, sin, and misery, as well as her limited opportunities allowed.", "독극물에 관한 책을 찾는 바람에 공공 도서관 사서들의 의심을 사기도 했다. 거리에서 사람들의 얼굴을 살피고, 주변의 선하고 악하고 그저 그런 온갖 인물들을 연구했다."], ["She thought she was prospering finely; but, unconsciously, she was beginning to desecrate some of the womanliest attributes of a woman's character.", "너무 오래되어 도리어 새것이나 다름없는 사실이나 허구를 찾아 옛 시대의 먼지를 파헤쳤고, 제한된 기회가 허락하는 한 어리석음과 죄악과 비참함에 스스로를 들이밀었다. 그녀는 자기가 잘나가고 있다고 생각했지만, 자기도 모르게 여자의 성품 가운데 가장 여성스러운 자질들을 더럽히기 시작하고 있었다."], ["She was living in bad society; and, imaginary though it was, its influence affected her, for she was feeding heart and fancy on dangerous and unsubstantial food, and was fast brushing the innocent bloom from her nature by a premature acquaintance with the darker side of life, which comes soon enough to all of us.", "그녀는 나쁜 사회 속에서 살고 있었고, 비록 상상 속의 것일망정 그 영향은 그녀에게 미쳤다. 위험하고 실체 없는 양식으로 마음과 공상을 살찌우고 있었으며, 누구에게나 머지않아 찾아오게 마련인 삶의 어두운 면을 너무 일찍 접한 탓에 천성의 순결한 빛을 빠르게 지워 가고 있었던 것이다."]], [["She was beginning to feel rather than see this, for much describing of other people's passions and feelings set her to studying and speculating about her own,--a morbid amusement, in which healthy young minds do not voluntarily indulge.", "조는 이것을 눈으로 보기보다는 차츰 느끼기 시작했다. 다른 사람들의 격정과 감정을 숱하게 묘사하다 보니, 자기 자신의 그것을 들여다보고 곱씹게 되었던 것이다."], ["Wrong-doing always brings its own punishment; and, when Jo most needed hers, she got it.", "건강한 젊은 정신이라면 자발적으로 빠져들지 않을 병적인 유희였다. 잘못은 언제나 제 나름의 벌을 데려오는 법이고, 조에게 그 벌이 가장 필요했을 때 그것은 어김없이 찾아왔다."]], [["I don't know whether the study of Shakespeare helped her to read character, or the natural instinct of a woman for what was honest, brave, and strong; but while endowing her imaginary heroes with every perfection under the sun, Jo was discovering a live hero, who interested her in spite of many human imperfections.", "셰익스피어 연구가 인물을 읽어 내는 데 도움을 주었는지, 아니면 정직하고 용감하고 강한 것을 알아보는 여자의 본능적 직감이 그랬는지 나로서는 모르겠다."], ["Mr.", "다만 상상 속 영웅들에게 세상의 온갖 완벽함을 갖춰 주는 동안, 조는 인간적인 결점이 많은데도 자기 마음을 끄는 살아 있는 영웅 하나를 발견하고 있었다."], ["Bhaer, in one of their conversations, had advised her to study simple, true, and lovely characters, wherever she found them, as good training for a writer.", "바에르 씨는 언젠가 대화 중에, 어디서 마주치든 소박하고 진실하며 사랑스러운 인물들을 연구하라고, 그것이 작가에게 좋은 훈련이 된다고 조에게 조언한 적이 있었다. 조는 그 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들였다."], ["Jo took him at his word, for she coolly turned round and studied him,--a proceeding which would have much surprised him, had he known it, for the worthy Professor was very humble in his own conceit.", "천연덕스럽게 돌아앉아 그를 연구했던 것이다. 그가 이 사실을 알았다면 무척 놀랐을 행동이었으니, 그 훌륭한 교수는 자기 자신에 대해 매우 겸손했기 때문이다."]], [["Why everybody liked him was what puzzled Jo, at first.", "처음에 조를 어리둥절하게 한 것은 어째서 모두가 그를 좋아하는가 하는 점이었다. 그는 부자도 거물도 아니었고, 젊지도 잘생기지도 않았다."], ["He was neither rich nor great, young nor handsome; in no respect what is called fascinating, imposing, or brilliant; and yet he was as attractive as a genial fire, and people seemed to gather about him as naturally as about a warm hearth.", "흔히 말하는 매혹적이거나 위풍당당하거나 화려한 구석이라곤 어느 모로도 없었다. 그런데도 그는 따뜻한 불처럼 사람을 끌어당겼고, 사람들은 마치 온기 도는 화롯가에 모이듯 자연스레 그의 곁에 모여드는 듯했다."], ["He was poor, yet always appeared to be giving something away; a stranger, yet every one was his friend; no longer young, but as happy-hearted as a boy; plain and peculiar, yet his face looked beautiful to many, and his oddities were freely forgiven for his sake.", "그는 가난했지만 늘 무언가를 베푸는 사람처럼 보였다. 이방인이었지만 누구나 그의 벗이었다."], ["Jo often watched him, trying to discover the charm, and, at last, decided that it was benevolence which worked the miracle.", "더는 젊지 않았으나 소년처럼 마음이 즐거웠다. 수수하고 별난 사람이었지만 많은 이들에게 그 얼굴은 아름다워 보였고, 그의 괴팍함은 그를 봐서 기꺼이 용서받았다."], ["If he had any sorrow, \"it sat with its head under its wing,\" and he turned only his sunny side to the world.", "조는 그 매력의 정체를 알아내려고 종종 그를 지켜보았고, 마침내 그 기적을 일으킨 것은 인자함이라고 결론 내렸다."], ["There were lines upon his forehead, but Time seemed to have touched him gently, remembering how kind he was to others.", "그에게 슬픔이 있다 한들 그것은 \"날개 아래 머리를 묻고 잠들어 있었\"고, 그는 오직 햇살 비치는 쪽만을 세상에 내보였다. 그의 이마에는 주름이 잡혀 있었지만, 세월마저도 그가 남들에게 얼마나 친절했는지를 기억하고 부드럽게 어루만지고 지나간 듯했다."], ["The pleasant curves about his mouth were the memorials of many friendly words and cheery laughs; his eyes were never cold or hard, and his big hand had a warm, strong grasp that was more expressive than words.", "입가의 정다운 곡선들은 숱한 다정한 말과 유쾌한 웃음의 흔적이었다. 그의 눈은 결코 차갑거나 매섭지 않았고, 그 커다란 손은 따뜻하고 힘찬 악수를 건넸으니, 그것은 말보다 더 많은 것을 전했다."]], [["His very clothes seemed to partake of the hospitable nature of the wearer.", "심지어 그의 옷마저 입은 사람의 후덕한 성품을 닮은 듯했다. 옷들은 편안해 보였고, 그를 편하게 해 주는 것을 좋아하는 것 같았다."], ["They looked as if they were at ease, and liked to make him comfortable; his capacious waistcoat was suggestive of a large heart underneath; his rusty coat had a social air, and the baggy pockets plainly proved that little hands often went in empty and came out full; his very boots were benevolent, and his collars never stiff and raspy like other people's.", "넉넉한 조끼는 그 안에 큰 마음이 들어 있음을 짐작게 했고, 빛바랜 코트에는 사람 좋은 분위기가 어려 있었으며, 불룩한 주머니들은 작은 손들이 종종 빈 채로 들어갔다가 가득 채워 나왔음을 분명히 보여 주었다. 그의 장화조차 인자해 보였고, 깃은 남들 것처럼 빳빳하고 까칠하지가 않았다."]], [["\"That's it!\"", "\"바로 그거야!\""], ["said Jo to herself, when she at length discovered that genuine good-will towards one's fellow-men could beautify and dignify even a stout German teacher, who shovelled in his dinner, darned his own socks, and was burdened with the name of Bhaer.", "마침내 조는 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. 동료 인간을 향한 진심 어린 선의가, 저녁밥을 퍼먹고 양말을 손수 꿰매고 바에르라는 이름까지 짊어진 뚱뚱한 독일인 선생마저 아름답고 기품 있게 만들 수 있다는 사실을 깨닫고서였다."]], [["Jo valued goodness highly, but she also possessed a most feminine respect for intellect, and a little discovery which she made about the Professor added much to her regard for him.", "조는 선함을 무척 귀하게 여겼지만, 지성에 대한 지극히 여성스러운 존경심도 지니고 있었다. 그리고 교수에 관해 알게 된 작은 사실 하나가 그에 대한 존경을 한층 더했다."], ["He never spoke of himself, and no one ever knew that in his native city he had been a man much honored and esteemed for learning and integrity, till a countryman came to see him, and, in a conversation with Miss Norton, divulged the pleasing fact.", "그는 결코 자기 이야기를 하지 않았고, 그가 고향 도시에서 학식과 지조로 크게 존경받고 추앙받던 인물이었다는 것을 아무도 몰랐다. 그러던 차에 한 동향 사람이 그를 찾아왔고, 노턴 양과 나눈 대화에서 그 흐뭇한 사실을 털어놓았다."], ["From her Jo learned it, and liked it all the better because Mr. Bhaer had never told it.", "조는 그녀에게서 그 이야기를 전해 듣고, 바에르 씨가 한 번도 입에 올리지 않았던 까닭에 더욱 좋게 여겼다."], ["She felt proud to know that he was an honored Professor in Berlin, though only a poor language-master in America; and his homely, hard-working life was much beautified by the spice of romance which this discovery gave it.", "비록 미국에서는 가난한 어학 선생에 불과하지만 베를린에서는 존경받는 교수였다는 사실을 알게 되자 조는 자랑스러웠다. 이 발견이 더해 준 한 줄기 낭만이, 그의 소박하고 고된 삶을 한결 아름답게 빛내 주었다."]], [["Another and a better gift than intellect was shown her in a most unexpected manner.", "지성보다 더 나은 또 다른 선물이 뜻밖의 방식으로 조에게 모습을 드러냈다."], ["Miss Norton had the _entrée_ into literary society, which Jo would have had no chance of seeing but for her.", "노턴 양은 문학계에 출입할 자격이 있었는데, 그녀가 아니었다면 조로서는 그 세계를 구경할 기회조차 없었을 터였다."], ["The solitary woman felt an interest in the ambitious girl, and kindly conferred many favors of this sort both on Jo and the Professor.", "외로운 그 여인은 야심 찬 소녀에게 관심을 가져, 조와 교수 두 사람 모두에게 이런 종류의 호의를 친절히 여러 차례 베풀어 주었다."], ["She took them with her, one night, to a select symposium, held in honor of several celebrities.", "어느 날 밤, 그녀는 몇몇 명사들을 기리는 자리로 마련된 특별한 모임에 두 사람을 데려갔다."]], [["Jo went prepared to bow down and adore the mighty ones whom she had worshipped with youthful enthusiasm afar off.", "조는 멀리서 젊은 열정으로 우러러보던 위대한 인물들 앞에 머리를 조아리고 흠모할 채비를 하고 갔다."], ["But her reverence for genius received a severe shock that night, and it took her some time to recover from the discovery that the great creatures were only men and women after all.", "그러나 천재를 향한 그녀의 경외심은 그날 밤 심한 충격을 받았고, 그 위대한 존재들도 결국은 한낱 남자와 여자에 지나지 않더라는 깨달음에서 헤어나기까지는 적잖은 시간이 걸렸다."], ["Imagine her dismay, on stealing a glance of timid admiration at the poet whose lines suggested an ethereal being fed on \"spirit, fire, and dew,\"", "\"정령과 불과 이슬\"을 먹고 사는 천상의 존재를 떠올리게 하는 시를 짓는 시인에게 수줍은 흠모의 눈길을 슬쩍 던졌다가, 그가 지적인 얼굴을 벌겋게 물들일 만큼 열렬한 기세로 저녁을 게걸스레 먹어 치우는 모습을 보았을 때 그녀가 느낀 낭패감을 상상해 보라."], ["to behold him devouring his supper with an ardor which flushed his intellectual countenance.", "우상이 무너지기라도 한 듯 그쪽에서 눈을 돌린 조는, 자신의 낭만적 환상을 순식간에 깨뜨리는 다른 사실들도 발견했다."], ["Turning as from a fallen idol, she made other discoveries which rapidly dispelled her romantic illusions.", "위대한 소설가는 추(錘)처럼 규칙적으로 두 개의 디캔터 사이를 오갔다. 유명한 신학자는 그 시대의 마담 드 스탈 가운데 하나와 대놓고 시시덕거리고 있었는데, 그 여인은 또 다른 코린을 노려보고 있었다."], ["The great novelist vibrated between two decanters with the regularity of a pendulum; the famous divine flirted openly with one of the Madame de Staëls of the age, who looked daggers at another Corinne, who was amiably satirizing her, after out-manœuvring her in efforts to absorb the profound philosopher, who imbibed tea Johnsonianly and appeared to slumber, the loquacity of the lady rendering speech impossible.", "그 코린은 상냥하게 그녀를 풍자하던 참이었으니, 심오한 철학자를 차지하려는 책략에서 그녀를 따돌린 직후였다. 정작 그 철학자는 존슨식으로 차를 들이켜며 조는 듯 보였는데, 부인의 수다 때문에 도무지 말을 끼워 넣을 수가 없었던 것이다."], ["The scientific celebrities, forgetting their mollusks and glacial periods, gossiped about art, while devoting themselves to oysters and ices with characteristic energy; the young musician, who was charming the city like a second Orpheus, talked horses; and the specimen of the British nobility present happened to be the most ordinary man of the party.", "과학계의 명사들은 자기네 연체동물과 빙하기는 잊은 채 예술을 두고 잡담을 나누면서, 특유의 활력으로 굴과 아이스크림에 몰두했다. 제2의 오르페우스처럼 온 도시를 매료시키던 젊은 음악가는 말[馬] 이야기를 늘어놓았고, 그 자리에 있던 영국 귀족의 표본은 공교롭게도 일행 가운데 가장 평범한 인물이었다."]], [["Before the evening was half over, Jo felt so completely _désillusionée_, that she sat down in a corner to recover herself. Mr.", "저녁 모임이 절반도 지나기 전에 조는 어찌나 완전히 환멸을 느꼈던지, 마음을 추스르려고 구석에 가 앉았다."], ["Bhaer soon joined her, looking rather out of his element, and presently several of the philosophers, each mounted on his hobby, came ambling up to hold an intellectual tournament in the recess.", "곧 바에르 씨가 자기도 영 어색한 듯한 얼굴로 그녀 곁에 와 앉았고, 이내 철학자 몇몇이 저마다 자기 신조라는 말[馬]에 올라타 그 후미진 자리로 어슬렁어슬렁 다가와 지적인 한판 시합을 벌였다. 대화는 조의 이해를 까마득히 넘어서는 것이었지만, 그녀는 그것을 즐겼다."], ["The conversation was miles beyond Jo's comprehension, but she enjoyed it, though Kant and Hegel were unknown gods, the Subjective and Objective unintelligible terms; and the only thing \"evolved from her inner consciousness,\"", "칸트와 헤겔이 알 수 없는 신들이고 주관이니 객관이니 하는 것이 알아들을 수 없는 용어이긴 했어도 말이다. 그 모든 것이 끝난 뒤에 \"그녀의 내적 의식에서 진화해 나온\""], ["was a bad headache after it was all over.", "유일한 것이라곤 지독한 두통뿐이었다."], ["It dawned upon her gradually that the world was being picked to pieces, and put together on new, and, according to the talkers, on infinitely better principles than before; that religion was in a fair way to be reasoned into nothingness, and intellect was to be the only God.", "세상이 갈가리 해체되었다가, 새롭게, 그리고 떠드는 자들 말로는 전보다 무한히 더 나은 원리에 따라 다시 짜 맞춰지고 있다는 것이 조에게 차츰 분명해졌다. 종교는 따져 묻기로 따져 묻다가 무(無)로 사그라들 판이었고, 지성만이 유일한 신이 될 참이었다."], ["Jo knew nothing about philosophy or metaphysics of any sort, but a curious excitement, half pleasurable, half painful, came over her, as she listened with a sense of being turned adrift into time and space, like a young balloon out on a holiday.", "조는 철학이니 형이상학이니 하는 것에 대해서는 아무것도 몰랐지만, 절반은 즐겁고 절반은 괴로운 묘한 흥분이 그녀를 사로잡았다. 마치 휴일을 맞아 떠오른 어린 풍선처럼, 시간과 공간 속으로 떠밀려 표류하는 듯한 기분으로 귀를 기울이면서였다."]], [["She looked round to see how the Professor liked it, and found him looking at her with the grimmest expression she had ever seen him wear.", "교수가 이 자리를 어떻게 여기는지 보려고 둘러보던 조는, 그가 일찍이 본 적 없는 가장 험상궂은 표정으로 자기를 바라보고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["He shook his head, and beckoned her to come away; but she was fascinated, just then, by the freedom of Speculative Philosophy, and kept her seat, trying to find out what the wise gentlemen intended to rely upon after they had annihilated all the old beliefs.", "그는 고개를 가로젓고 어서 나가자고 손짓했다. 하지만 그녀는 그때 사변 철학의 거침없음에 매료되어 자리를 지킨 채, 이 현명한 신사들이 옛 신념을 죄다 무너뜨린 다음에는 대체 무엇에 기대려는 것인지 알아내려 애썼다."]], [["Now, Mr.", "그런데 바에르 씨는 수줍은 사람이라 자기 의견을 좀처럼 꺼내 놓지 않았다."], ["Bhaer was a diffident man, and slow to offer his own opinions, not because they were unsettled, but too sincere and earnest to be lightly spoken.", "의견이 확고하지 않아서가 아니라, 너무 진실하고 진지해서 가벼이 입에 올릴 수 없었기 때문이다."], ["As he glanced from Jo to several other young people, attracted by the brilliancy of the philosophic pyrotechnics, he knit his brows, and longed to speak, fearing that some inflammable young soul would be led astray by the rockets, to find, when the display was over, that they had only an empty stick or a scorched hand.", "철학적 불꽃놀이의 현란함에 홀린 조와 다른 몇몇 젊은이를 번갈아 바라보던 그는 미간을 찌푸렸고, 말을 하고 싶어 안달이 났다. 불붙기 쉬운 어린 영혼 하나가 그 폭죽에 이끌려 길을 잃었다가, 막상 불꽃놀이가 끝나고 나면 손에 남은 거라곤 텅 빈 막대기나 그을린 손바닥뿐임을 알게 될까 봐 두려웠던 것이다."]], [["He bore it as long as he could; but when he was appealed to for an opinion, he blazed up with honest indignation, and defended religion with all the eloquence of truth,--an eloquence which made his broken English musical, and his plain face beautiful.", "그는 참을 수 있는 데까지 참았다. 그러나 의견을 묻는 말이 그에게 던져지자, 그는 정직한 분노로 활활 타올라 진리만이 지닌 온갖 웅변으로 종교를 변호했다."], ["He had a hard fight, for the wise men argued well; but he didn't know when he was beaten, and stood to his colors like a man.", "그 웅변은 그의 서툰 영어를 음악처럼 들리게 하고, 그 수수한 얼굴을 아름답게 만들었다. 현명한 자들이 논변에 능했으므로 그것은 힘겨운 싸움이었다."], ["Somehow, as he talked, the world got right again to Jo; the old beliefs, that had lasted so long, seemed better than the new; God was not a blind force, and immortality was not a pretty fable, but a blessed fact.", "하지만 그는 자기가 졌다는 것을 알지 못한 채 사내답게 제 깃발을 지켰다. 어찌 된 영문인지, 그가 말하는 동안 세상은 조에게 다시 제자리를 찾았다."], ["She felt as if she had solid ground under her feet again; and when Mr.", "그토록 오래 이어져 온 옛 신념들이 새것보다 나아 보였다. 신은 눈먼 힘이 아니었고, 불멸은 그럴듯한 우화가 아니라 복된 사실이었다."], ["Bhaer paused, out-talked, but not one whit convinced, Jo wanted to clap her hands and thank him.", "그녀는 발밑에 다시 단단한 땅이 디뎌지는 듯했다. 바에르 씨가 말문이 막혀 멈추었으나 조금도 설득당하지는 않은 채 말을 그치자, 조는 손뼉을 치며 그에게 고마워하고 싶었다."]], [["She did neither; but she remembered this scene, and gave the Professor her heartiest respect, for she knew it cost him an effort to speak out then and there, because his conscience would not let him be silent.", "그녀는 둘 다 하지 않았다. 그러나 이 장면을 마음에 새겼고, 교수에게 진심 어린 존경을 바쳤다."], ["She began to see that character is a better possession than money, rank, intellect, or beauty; and to feel that if greatness is what a wise man has defined it to be, \"truth, reverence, and good-will,\"", "그때 그 자리에서 소신을 밝히는 데 그가 적잖은 용기를 냈음을 알았기 때문이다. 양심이 그를 침묵하도록 내버려 두지 않았던 것이다."], ["then her friend Friedrich Bhaer was not only good, but great.", "그녀는 성품이 돈이나 지위, 지성, 미모보다 더 나은 재산임을 깨닫기 시작했다. 그리고 어느 현자가 정의한 대로 위대함이 \"진리와 경외와 선의\"라면, 자기 벗 프리드리히 바에르는 선할 뿐만 아니라 위대하기도 한 사람이라고 느끼게 되었다."]], [["This belief strengthened daily.", "이 믿음은 나날이 굳어졌다. 그녀는 그의 호감을 소중히 여기고, 그의 존경을 갈망하고, 그의 우정에 걸맞은 사람이 되고 싶었다."], ["She valued his esteem, she coveted his respect, she wanted to be worthy of his friendship; and, just when the wish was sincerest, she came near losing everything.", "그런데 그 바람이 가장 진실했던 바로 그때, 그녀는 모든 것을 잃을 뻔했다. 모든 일은 종이 모자 하나에서 비롯되었다."], ["It all grew out of a cocked hat; for one evening the Professor came in to give Jo her lesson, with a paper soldier-cap on his head, which Tina had put there, and he had forgotten to take off.", "어느 날 저녁 교수가 조에게 수업을 해 주러 왔는데, 머리에 종이로 만든 군인 모자를 쓰고 있었던 것이다. 티나가 씌워 준 것을 그가 벗는 것을 깜빡한 채였다."]], [["\"It's evident he doesn't look in his glass before coming down,\"", "'분명 내려오기 전에 거울을 안 보신 거야.' 그가 \"안녕하시오\""], ["thought Jo, with a smile, as he said \"Goot efening,\"", "하고 의젓하게 자리에 앉는 것을 보며 조는 미소 띤 채 생각했다."], ["and sat soberly down, quite unconscious of the ludicrous contrast between his subject and his head-gear, for he was going to read her the \"Death of Wallenstein.\"", "그는 자기가 읽으려는 주제와 머리에 쓴 것 사이의 우스꽝스러운 대비를 전혀 의식하지 못하고 있었다. 그가 그녀에게 읽어 줄 것은 바로 \"발렌슈타인의 죽음\"이었으니까."]], [["She said nothing at first, for she liked to hear him laugh out his big, hearty laugh, when anything funny happened, so she left him to discover it for himself, and presently forgot all about it; for to hear a German read Schiller is rather an absorbing occupation.", "조는 처음에는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 우스운 일이 생기면 터져 나오는 그의 크고 호탕한 웃음소리를 듣는 것을 좋아했던 터라, 스스로 알아채도록 내버려 두었던 것이다."], ["After the reading came the lesson, which was a lively one, for Jo was in a gay mood that night, and the cocked-hat kept her eyes dancing with merriment.", "그러다 이내 그 일을 까맣게 잊었다. 독일인이 실러를 낭독하는 것을 듣는 일이란 꽤나 사람을 몰입시키는 일이었으니까."], ["The Professor didn't know what to make of her, and stopped at last, to ask, with an air of mild surprise that was irresistible,--", "낭독이 끝나자 수업이 이어졌고, 그날따라 조의 기분이 들떠 있었던 데다 종이 모자 때문에 눈에 자꾸 장난기 어린 웃음이 어른거려, 수업은 활기가 넘쳤다. 교수는 그녀를 어떻게 이해해야 할지 몰라 결국 멈추고는, 도무지 거부할 수 없는 잔잔한 놀라움이 어린 표정으로 물었다."]], [["\"Mees Marsch, for what do you laugh in your master's face?", "\"마치 양, 어찌 선생 면전에서 그리 웃는 거요?"], ["Haf you no respect for me, that you go on so bad?\"", "그렇게 못되게 구는 걸 보니 나에 대한 존경심이 없는 게요?\""]], [["\"How can I be respectful, sir, when you forget to take your hat off?\"", "\"선생님이 모자 벗는 걸 깜빡하셨는데 어떻게 존경할 수 있겠어요?\""], ["said Jo.", "조가 말했다."]], [["Lifting his hand to his head, the absent-minded Professor gravely felt and removed the little cocked-hat, looked at it a minute, and then threw back his head, and laughed like a merry bass-viol.", "건망증 심한 교수는 손을 머리로 가져가 진지하게 더듬더니, 그 작은 종이 모자를 벗어 잠깐 들여다보고는, 고개를 뒤로 젖히고 흥겨운 베이스 비올처럼 껄껄 웃었다."]], [["\"Ah!", "\"아!"], ["I see him now; it is that imp Tina who makes me a fool with my cap.", "이제 알겠소. 그 말썽꾸러기 티나가 모자로 나를 바보로 만들었군."], ["Well, it is nothing; but see you, if this lesson goes not well, you too shall wear him.\"", "뭐, 별일 아니오. 하지만 두고 보시오, 이 수업이 잘 안 되면 양도 이걸 써야 할 게요.\""]], [["But the lesson did not go at all for a few minutes, because Mr.", "그러나 수업은 몇 분 동안 영 진행되지 못했다."], ["Bhaer caught sight of a picture on the hat, and, unfolding it, said, with an air of great disgust,--", "바에르 씨가 모자에 그려진 그림을 발견하고 그것을 펼치더니, 몹시 역겹다는 듯이 말했기 때문이다."]], [["\"I wish these papers did not come in the house; they are not for children to see, nor young people to read.", "\"이런 신문이 집 안에 들어오지 않으면 좋겠소. 아이들이 볼 것도, 젊은이들이 읽을 것도 아니오."], ["It is not well, and I haf no patience with those who make this harm.\"", "좋지 않아요. 이런 해악을 만드는 자들은 도무지 참아 줄 수가 없소.\""]], [["Jo glanced at the sheet, and saw a pleasing illustration composed of a lunatic, a corpse, a villain, and a viper.", "조가 그 종이를 흘끗 보니, 미치광이와 시체, 악당과 독사로 이루어진 보기 좋은 삽화가 그려져 있었다. 그녀는 그것이 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["She did not like it; but the impulse that made her turn it over was not one of displeasure, but fear, because, for a minute, she fancied the paper was the \"Volcano.\"", "하지만 그것을 뒤집게 만든 충동은 불쾌함이 아니라 두려움이었다. 한순간 그 신문이 \"볼케이노\"인 줄 알았던 것이다."], ["It was not, however, and her panic subsided as she remembered that, even if it had been, and one of her own tales in it, there would have been no name to betray her.", "그러나 그것은 아니었고, 설령 그렇다 한들, 그리고 거기에 자기 이야기가 실려 있다 한들, 정체를 드러낼 이름은 적혀 있지 않으리라는 데 생각이 미치자 놀란 마음은 가라앉았다."], ["She had betrayed herself, however, by a look and a blush; for, though an absent man, the Professor saw a good deal more than people fancied.", "그러나 그녀는 표정과 홍조로 이미 스스로 정체를 드러내고 만 셈이었다. 건망증 있는 사람이긴 해도, 교수는 사람들이 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 많은 것을 보고 있었으니까."], ["He knew that Jo wrote, and had met her down among the newspaper offices more than once; but as she never spoke of it, he asked no questions, in spite of a strong desire to see her work.", "그는 조가 글을 쓴다는 것을 알았고, 신문사들이 모여 있는 거리에서 그녀와 한두 번 마주친 적도 있었다. 하지만 그녀가 한 번도 그 이야기를 하지 않았으므로, 그녀의 작품을 보고 싶은 마음이 간절했어도 아무것도 묻지 않았다."], ["Now it occurred to him that she was doing what she was ashamed to own, and it troubled him.", "그런데 지금, 그녀가 스스로 인정하기 부끄러워하는 일을 하고 있다는 생각이 들자 그는 마음이 편치 않았다."], ["He did not say to himself, \"It is none of my business; I've no right to say anything,\"", "그는 많은 사람들이 그러듯 '내가 알 바 아니지, 내겐 뭐라 말할 권리가 없어'라고 자기에게 말하지 않았다."], ["as many people would have done; he only remembered that she was young and poor, a girl far away from mother's love and father's care; and he was moved to help her with an impulse as quick and natural as that which would prompt him to put out his hand to save a baby from a puddle.", "다만 그녀가 어머니의 사랑과 아버지의 보살핌에서 멀리 떨어진, 젊고 가난한 소녀임을 떠올렸을 뿐이다. 그리하여 그는, 웅덩이에 빠지려는 아기를 구하려고 손을 내밀 때만큼이나 빠르고 자연스러운 충동에 이끌려 그녀를 돕고 싶어졌다."], ["All this flashed through his mind in a minute, but not a trace of it appeared in his face; and by the time the paper was turned, and Jo's needle threaded, he was ready to say quite naturally, but very gravely,--", "이 모든 것이 순식간에 그의 머릿속을 스쳤지만, 그 흔적은 얼굴에 조금도 드러나지 않았다. 신문이 뒤집히고 조의 바늘에 실이 꿰일 무렵, 그는 아주 자연스럽게, 그러나 매우 진지하게 말할 채비가 되어 있었다."]], [["\"Yes, you are right to put it from you.", "\"그래요, 그걸 멀리하는 게 옳소."], ["I do not like to think that good young girls should see such things.", "착한 처녀들이 그런 걸 봐야 한다고 생각하면 영 마음이 안 좋소."], ["They are made pleasant to some, but I would more rather give my boys gunpowder to play with than this bad trash.\"", "그런 게 어떤 이들에게는 즐거움이 되겠지만, 나는 이런 못된 쓰레기를 주느니 차라리 내 아이들에게 화약을 쥐여 주고 놀게 하겠소.\""]], [["\"All may not be bad, only silly, you know; and if there is a demand for it, I don't see any harm in supplying it.", "\"다 나쁜 건 아니고, 그저 시시한 것뿐이에요, 아시겠지만. 게다가 수요가 있는데 그걸 공급하는 게 무슨 해가 되는지 모르겠어요."], ["Many very respectable people make an honest living out of what are called sensation stories,\"", "점잖은 사람들 중에도 이른바 선정 소설로 정직하게 먹고사는 이가 많거든요.\""], ["said Jo, scratching gathers so energetically that a row of little slits followed her pin.", "조는 어찌나 힘차게 주름을 잡는지, 핀이 지나간 자리마다 작은 구멍이 줄지어 났다."]], [["\"There is a demand for whiskey, but I think you and I do not care to sell it.", "\"위스키에도 수요가 있지만, 양이나 나나 그걸 팔고 싶지는 않잖소."], ["If the respectable people knew what harm they did, they would not feel that the living _was_ honest.", "점잖은 사람들이 자기가 끼치는 해악을 안다면, 그걸 정직한 벌이라 여기지 못할 거요."], ["They haf no right to put poison in the sugar-plum, and let the small ones eat it.", "사탕에 독을 넣어 어린것들이 먹게 할 권리는 없소. 안 되지."], ["No; they should think a little, and sweep mud in the street before they do this thing.\"", "그런 짓을 하느니 잠시 생각을 좀 하고, 차라리 길에서 진흙이나 쓸어야 하오.\""]], [["Mr. Bhaer spoke warmly, and walked to the fire, crumpling the paper in his hands.", "바에르 씨는 열을 띠고 말하며 신문을 손에 구겨 쥔 채 난로 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["Jo sat still, looking as if the fire had come to her; for her cheeks burned long after the cocked hat had turned to smoke, and gone harmlessly up the chimney.", "조는 가만히 앉아 있었는데, 마치 불이 자기에게 옮겨붙은 듯한 모습이었다. 종이 모자가 연기로 변해 굴뚝으로 무해하게 사라진 뒤에도 한참 동안 그녀의 두 뺨은 화끈거렸으니까."]], [["\"I should like much to send all the rest after him,\"", "\"나머지도 죄다 저놈을 따라 보내 버리면 좋겠소.\""], ["muttered the Professor, coming back with a relieved air.", "교수가 후련한 얼굴로 돌아오며 중얼거렸다."]], [["Jo thought what a blaze her pile of papers upstairs would make, and her hard-earned money lay rather heavily on her conscience at that minute.", "조는 위층에 쌓아 둔 자기 신문 더미가 얼마나 큰 불길을 일으킬까 생각했고, 그 순간 힘겹게 번 돈이 양심을 꽤 무겁게 짓눌렀다. 그러다 스스로를 위로하듯 생각했다."], ["Then she thought consolingly to herself, \"Mine are not like that; they are only silly, never bad, so I won't be worried;\"", "'내 건 저런 게 아냐. 그냥 시시할 뿐이지, 나쁘진 않으니까 걱정할 거 없어.'"], ["and taking up her book, she said, with a studious face,--", "그러고는 책을 집어 들고 열심히 공부하려는 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["\"Shall we go on, sir?", "\"계속할까요, 선생님?"], ["I'll be very good and proper now.\"", "이제 아주 얌전하고 바르게 굴게요.\""]], [["\"I shall hope so,\"", "\"그러길 바라오.\""], ["was all he said, but he meant more than she imagined; and the grave, kind look he gave her made her feel as if the words \"Weekly Volcano\"", "그가 한 말은 이것뿐이었지만, 그 속에는 그녀가 짐작하는 것보다 더 많은 뜻이 담겨 있었다."], ["were printed in large type on her forehead.", "그가 그녀에게 던진 진지하고 다정한 눈빛에, 그녀는 마치 자기 이마에 \"위클리 볼케이노\"라는 글자가 큼직하게 박혀 있는 듯한 기분이 들었다."]], [["As soon as she went to her room, she got out her papers, and carefully re-read every one of her stories.", "방으로 돌아오자마자 그녀는 자기 신문들을 꺼내, 자기가 쓴 이야기를 하나하나 꼼꼼히 다시 읽었다. 약간 근시였던 바에르 씨는 가끔 안경을 썼는데, 조는 한 번 그것을 써 보고는 자기 책의 작은 활자가 어찌나 크게 보이던지 웃은 적이 있었다."], ["Being a little short-sighted, Mr.", "그런데 지금은 교수의 정신적, 혹은 도덕적 안경까지 쓴 것만 같았다."], ["Bhaer sometimes used eye-glasses, and Jo had tried them once, smiling to see how they magnified the fine print of her book; now she seemed to have got on the Professor's mental or moral spectacles also; for the faults of these poor stories glared at her dreadfully, and filled her with dismay.", "이 한심한 이야기들의 결점이 끔찍하리만치 또렷이 눈에 들어와 그녀를 낭패감으로 가득 채웠던 것이다."]], [["\"They _are_ trash, and will soon be worse than trash if I go on; for each is more sensational than the last. I've gone blindly on, hurting myself and other people, for the sake of money; I know it's so, for I can't read this stuff in sober earnest without being horribly ashamed of it; and what _should_ I do if they were seen at home, or Mr. Bhaer got hold of them?\"", "'이건 정말 쓰레기야. 이대로 계속하다간 곧 쓰레기보다 못한 게 될 거야. 한 편 한 편이 갈수록 더 선정적이잖아. 나는 돈 때문에 눈먼 채로 달려와, 나 자신과 다른 사람들을 해치고 있었어. 그래, 그게 맞아. 이런 걸 멀쩡한 정신으로는 끔찍하게 부끄럽지 않고서는 못 읽겠으니까. 그런데 만약 이게 집에서 발각되거나, 바에르 씨 손에 들어가기라도 하면 난 대체 어떡하지?'"]], [["Jo turned hot at the bare idea, and stuffed the whole bundle into her stove, nearly setting the chimney afire with the blaze.", "생각만으로도 조는 얼굴이 화끈 달아올랐고, 그 뭉치를 통째로 난로에 쑤셔 넣었다. 불길이 어찌나 거센지 하마터면 굴뚝에 불이 붙을 뻔했다."]], [["\"Yes, that's the best place for such inflammable nonsense; I'd better burn the house down, I suppose, than let other people blow themselves up with my gunpowder,\"", "'그래, 이런 불붙기 쉬운 헛소리에는 저기가 제격이야. 다른 사람들이 내 화약으로 자폭하게 두느니, 차라리 집을 태워 버리는 게 낫겠지.'"], ["she thought, as she watched the \"Demon of the Jura\" whisk away, a little black cinder with fiery eyes.", "조는 \"쥐라산맥의 악마\"가 이글거리는 눈을 한 작은 검정 잿덩이가 되어 휙 사라지는 것을 지켜보며 생각했다."]], [["But when nothing remained of all her three months' work except a heap of ashes, and the money in her lap, Jo looked sober, as she sat on the floor, wondering what she ought to do about her wages.", "그러나 석 달간의 작업이 한 줌의 재 더미와 무릎 위에 놓인 돈 말고는 아무것도 남기지 않았을 때, 조는 바닥에 앉아 자기가 받은 보수를 어찌해야 할지 고민하며 심각한 표정을 지었다."]], [["\"I think I haven't done much harm _yet_, and may keep this to pay for my time,\"", "\"아직은 큰 해를 끼친 것 같진 않으니, 이건 내가 들인 시간값으로 받아 둬도 되겠지.\""], ["she said, after a long meditation, adding impatiently, \"I almost wish I hadn't any conscience, it's so inconvenient.", "한참을 골똘히 생각한 끝에 그녀는 말하고는, 짜증스럽게 덧붙였다. \"차라리 양심이란 게 없었으면 좋겠어."], ["If I didn't care about doing right, and didn't feel uncomfortable when doing wrong, I should get on capitally.", "어찌나 거추장스러운지. 옳은 일을 하는 데 마음 쓰지 않고, 그른 일을 할 때 불편하지만 않으면, 정말 잘나갈 텐데."], ["I can't help wishing sometimes, that father and mother hadn't been so particular about such things.\"", "가끔은 아버지하고 어머니가 그런 일에 그렇게까지 까다롭지 않으셨더라면 하는 바람을 떨칠 수가 없다니까.\""]], [["Ah, Jo, instead of wishing that, thank God that \"father and mother _were_ particular,\"", "아, 조야, 그런 바람을 품을 게 아니라 \"아버지와 어머니가 까다로우셨던\" 것을 하느님께 감사하렴."], ["and pity from your heart those who have no such guardians to hedge them round with principles which may seem like prison-walls to impatient youth, but which will prove sure foundations to build character upon in womanhood.", "그리고 너를 원칙으로 빙 둘러싸 줄 그런 보호자가 없는 이들을 진심으로 가엾이 여기렴. 그 원칙이 참을성 없는 젊은이에게는 감옥의 벽처럼 느껴질지 몰라도, 여인이 되었을 때 성품을 쌓아 올릴 든든한 주춧돌이 되어 줄 테니 말이다."]], [["Jo wrote no more sensational stories, deciding that the money did not pay for her share of the sensation; but, going to the other extreme, as is the way with people of her stamp, she took a course of Mrs.", "조는 더 이상 선정 소설을 쓰지 않았다. 그 돈이 자기가 떠안은 선정성의 몫을 보상해 주지 못한다는 결론을 내렸던 것이다."], ["Sherwood, Miss Edgeworth, and Hannah More; and then produced a tale which might have been more properly called an essay or a sermon, so intensely moral was it.", "그러나 그녀 같은 부류의 사람들이 으레 그렇듯 정반대 극단으로 치달아, 셔우드 부인과 에지워스 양, 해나 모어를 한차례 섭렵했다. 그러고는 차라리 수필이나 설교라고 부르는 편이 더 알맞을 법한 이야기를 한 편 써냈다."], ["She had her doubts about it from the beginning; for her lively fancy and girlish romance felt as ill at ease in the new style as she would have done masquerading in the stiff and cumbrous costume of the last century.", "그만큼 지독하게 교훈적이었다. 그녀는 처음부터 그것에 의구심을 품고 있었다."], ["She sent this didactic gem to several markets, but it found no purchaser; and she was inclined to agree with Mr.", "발랄한 공상과 소녀다운 낭만이 이 새로운 문체 속에서 영 불편해했던 것이다. 마치 그녀 자신이 지난 세기의 뻣뻣하고 거추장스러운 의상을 걸치고 가장무도회에 나선 듯한 기분이었다."], ["Dashwood, that morals didn't sell.", "그녀는 이 교훈적인 보석을 여러 곳에 보냈지만 사 줄 사람을 찾지 못했고, 교훈은 안 팔린다던 대시우드 씨의 말에 수긍하는 쪽으로 기울었다."]], [["Then she tried a child's story, which she could easily have disposed of if she had not been mercenary enough to demand filthy lucre for it.", "그다음으로 조는 아동물을 써 보았는데, 그 대가로 더러운 돈을 요구할 만큼 잇속을 따지지만 않았더라면 쉽게 팔 수 있었을 것이다."], ["The only person who offered enough to make it worth her while to try juvenile literature was a worthy gentleman who felt it his mission to convert all the world to his particular belief.", "아동 문학에 도전해 볼 만한 값을 제시한 유일한 사람은, 온 세상을 자기 특정 신앙으로 개종시키는 것을 사명으로 여기는 어느 점잖은 신사였다."], ["But much as she liked to write for children, Jo could not consent to depict all her naughty boys as being eaten by bears or tossed by mad bulls, because they did not go to a particular Sabbath-school, nor all the good infants, who did go, as rewarded by every kind of bliss, from gilded gingerbread to escorts of angels, when they departed this life with psalms or sermons on their lisping tongues.", "그러나 아무리 아이들을 위한 글을 쓰고 싶어도, 조는 자기 이야기 속 말썽꾸러기 소년들이 특정 주일학교에 다니지 않는다는 이유로 죄다 곰에게 잡아먹히거나 미친 황소에게 들이받히게 그리고, 거기에 다니는 착한 아이들은 죄다, 혀 짧은 입으로 찬송가나 설교를 읊으며 이 세상을 떠날 때 금박 입힌 생강 과자에서 천사의 호위에 이르기까지 온갖 복락으로 보상받는 식으로 묘사하는 데는 동의할 수 없었다."], ["So nothing came of these trials; and Jo corked up her inkstand, and said, in a fit of very wholesome humility,--", "그리하여 이 시도들에서도 아무 성과가 없었고, 조는 잉크병 마개를 닫고 아주 건전한 겸손에 사로잡혀 말했다."]], [["\"I don't know anything; I'll wait till I do before I try again, and, meantime, 'sweep mud in the street,'", "\"난 아무것도 몰라. 제대로 알게 될 때까지 기다렸다가 다시 시도하겠어."], ["if I can't do better; that's honest, at least;\"", "그동안에는 더 나은 걸 할 수 없다면 '길에서 진흙이나 쓸겠어.'"], ["which decision proved that her second tumble down the bean-stalk had done her some good.", "그건 적어도 정직한 일이니까.\" 이 결심은 콩나무에서 두 번째로 굴러떨어진 것이 그녀에게 어느 정도 약이 되었음을 보여 주었다."]], [["While these internal revolutions were going on, her external life had been as busy and uneventful as usual; and if she sometimes looked serious or a little sad no one observed it but Professor Bhaer. He did it so quietly that Jo never knew he was watching to see if she would accept and profit by his reproof; but she stood the test, and he was satisfied; for, though no words passed between them, he knew that she had given up writing. Not only did he guess it by the fact that the second finger of her right hand was no longer inky, but she spent her evenings downstairs now, was met no more among newspaper offices, and studied with a dogged patience, which assured him that she was bent on occupying her mind with something useful, if not pleasant.", "이런 내적인 변혁이 일어나는 동안, 그녀의 겉으로 드러난 삶은 여느 때처럼 바쁘면서도 별일 없이 흘러갔다. 가끔 그녀가 심각해 보이거나 조금 슬퍼 보일 때도, 그것을 알아챈 사람은 바에르 교수밖에 없었다. 그가 어찌나 조용히 살폈던지, 조는 그가 자기 충고를 받아들여 도움이 될지 지켜보고 있다는 것을 전혀 몰랐다. 하지만 그녀는 그 시험을 견뎌 냈고, 그는 만족했다. 두 사람 사이에 오간 말은 없었어도, 그녀가 글쓰기를 그만두었음을 그는 알았기 때문이다. 그녀의 오른손 둘째손가락에 더 이상 잉크가 묻어 있지 않다는 사실로도 짐작했지만, 그뿐이 아니었다. 그녀는 이제 저녁 시간을 아래층에서 보냈고, 신문사들 사이에서 더는 마주치지 않았으며, 끈질긴 인내로 공부에 매달렸다. 그것은 그녀가, 비록 즐겁지는 않더라도 무언가 유익한 일에 마음을 쏟기로 작정했음을 그에게 확신시켜 주었다."]], [["He helped her in many ways, proving himself a true friend, and Jo was happy; for, while her pen lay idle, she was learning other lessons beside German, and laying a foundation for the sensation story of her own life.", "그는 여러 모로 그녀를 도우며 자신이 참된 벗임을 증명했고, 조는 행복했다. 펜을 놀리지 않는 동안 그녀는 독일어 말고도 다른 가르침을 배우고 있었고, 자기 인생이라는 한 편의 선정 소설의 토대를 놓고 있었으니까."]], [["It was a pleasant winter and a long one, for she did not leave Mrs.", "즐겁고도 긴 겨울이었다. 그녀가 커크 부인의 집을 떠난 것은 6월이 되어서였다."], ["Kirke till June. Every one seemed sorry when the time came; the children were inconsolable, and Mr.", "떠날 때가 오자 모두가 서운해하는 듯했다."], ["Bhaer's hair stuck straight up all over his head, for he always rumpled it wildly when disturbed in mind.", "아이들은 위로받을 길이 없었고, 바에르 씨의 머리카락은 온통 곤두서 있었다. 그는 마음이 심란할 때면 늘 머리를 마구 헝클어뜨렸기 때문이다."]], [["\"Going home?", "\"집에 가신다고요?"], ["Ah, you are happy that you haf a home to go in,\"", "아, 양은 돌아갈 집이 있으니 행복한 사람이오.\""], ["he said, when she told him, and sat silently pulling his beard, in the corner, while she held a little levee on that last evening.", "그녀가 떠난다고 말하자 그가 한 말이었다. 그러고는 마지막 날 저녁 그녀가 작은 송별 모임을 여는 동안, 그는 구석에 말없이 앉아 수염만 잡아당겼다."]], [["She was going early, so she bade them all good-by over night; and when his turn came, she said warmly,--", "그녀는 아침 일찍 떠날 예정이라 밤사이에 모두에게 작별 인사를 했다. 그의 차례가 되자 그녀는 따뜻하게 말했다."]], [["\"Now, sir, you won't forget to come and see us, if you ever travel our way, will you?", "\"자, 선생님, 혹시라도 저희 동네 쪽으로 여행하시게 되면 꼭 들러 주실 거죠?"], ["I'll never forgive you if you do, for I want them all to know my friend.\"", "안 그러시면 절대 용서하지 않을 거예요. 우리 식구들 모두에게 제 친구를 소개하고 싶거든요.\""]], [["\"Do you?", "\"그러고 싶소?"], ["Shall I come?\"", "내가 가도 되겠소?\""], ["he asked, looking down at her with an eager expression which she did not see.", "그가 그녀로서는 미처 보지 못한 간절한 표정으로 그녀를 내려다보며 물었다."]], [["\"Yes, come next month; Laurie graduates then, and you'd enjoy Commencement as something new.\"", "\"네, 다음 달에 오세요. 그때 로리가 졸업하는데, 졸업식이 새로운 구경거리가 돼서 즐거우실 거예요.\""]], [["\"That is your best friend, of whom you speak?\"", "\"양이 말하는 그 사람이 가장 친한 친구요?\""], ["he said, in an altered tone.", "그가 달라진 어조로 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, my boy Teddy; I'm very proud of him, and should like you to see him.\"", "\"네, 제 친구 테디예요. 저는 그 애가 무척 자랑스럽고, 선생님께도 보여 드리고 싶어요.\""]], [["Jo looked up then, quite unconscious of anything but her own pleasure in the prospect of showing them to one another. Something in Mr.", "그제야 조는 고개를 들었는데, 두 사람을 서로에게 소개할 생각에 들뜬 것 말고는 아무것도 의식하지 못한 채였다."], ["Bhaer's face suddenly recalled the fact that she might find Laurie more than a \"best friend,\"", "그런데 바에르 씨의 얼굴에 어린 무언가가 문득, 자기가 로리를 한낱 \"가장 친한 친구\""], ["and, simply because she particularly wished not to look as if anything was the matter, she involuntarily began to blush; and the more she tried not to, the redder she grew.", "이상으로 여기게 될지도 모른다는 사실을 떠올리게 했다. 그러자 단지 무슨 일이라도 있는 것처럼 보이고 싶지 않다는 그 마음 때문에, 그녀는 자기도 모르게 얼굴을 붉히기 시작했다."], ["If it had not been for Tina on her knee, she didn't know what would have become of her.", "그리고 붉히지 않으려 애쓸수록 더욱 빨개졌다. 무릎 위에 티나가 없었더라면 어떻게 됐을지 그녀 자신도 알 수 없었다."], ["Fortunately, the child was moved to hug her; so she managed to hide her face an instant, hoping the Professor did not see it.", "다행히 아이가 그녀를 와락 끌어안았고, 덕분에 그녀는 잠깐 얼굴을 숨기며 교수가 못 봤기를 바랄 수 있었다."], ["But he did, and his own changed again from that momentary anxiety to its usual expression, as he said cordially,--", "하지만 그는 보았고, 그의 얼굴은 그 한순간의 불안에서 다시 평소의 표정으로 돌아왔다. 그러고는 다정하게 말했다."]], [["\"I fear I shall not make the time for that, but I wish the friend much success, and you all happiness.", "\"그때 시간을 낼 수 있을 것 같진 않소만, 그 친구가 크게 성공하고 양들 모두가 행복하기를 바라오."], ["Gott bless you!\"", "신의 가호가 있기를!\""], ["and with that, he shook hands warmly, shouldered Tina, and went away.", "그렇게 말하며 그는 따뜻하게 악수를 하고 티나를 어깨에 들쳐 업은 채 떠났다."]], [["But after the boys were abed, he sat long before his fire, with the tired look on his face, and the \"_heimweh_,\" or homesickness, lying heavy at his heart.", "그러나 아이들이 잠자리에 든 뒤, 그는 얼굴에 지친 빛을 띤 채 오래도록 난롯불 앞에 앉아 있었고, 가슴속에는 \"하임베\", 곧 향수(鄕愁)가 무겁게 자리하고 있었다."], ["Once, when he remembered Jo, as she sat with the little child in her lap and that new softness in her face, he leaned his head on his hands a minute, and then roamed about the room, as if in search of something that he could not find.", "한 번은 어린아이를 무릎에 앉히고 얼굴에 그 새로운 부드러움을 띠고 있던 조를 떠올리며 잠시 두 손에 머리를 묻었다가, 무언가 찾을 수 없는 것을 찾기라도 하듯 방 안을 서성거렸다."]], [["\"It is not for me; I must not hope it now,\"", "\"이건 내 것이 아니야. 지금은 바라서는 안 돼.\""], ["he said to himself, with a sigh that was almost a groan; then, as if reproaching himself for the longing that he could not repress, he went and kissed the two towzled heads upon the pillow, took down his seldom-used meerschaum, and opened his Plato.", "그는 거의 신음에 가까운 한숨을 내쉬며 혼잣말을 했다. 그러고는 억누를 수 없는 그 갈망을 자책이라도 하듯, 베개 위 헝클어진 두 머리에 입을 맞추고, 좀처럼 쓰지 않던 해포석 파이프를 내려 들고는 플라톤을 펼쳤다."]], [["He did his best, and did it manfully; but I don't think he found that a pair of rampant boys, a pipe, or even the divine Plato, were very satisfactory substitutes for wife and child and home.", "그는 최선을 다했고, 또 사내답게 해냈다. 하지만 펄펄 뛰는 두 사내아이와 파이프 한 대, 심지어 신성한 플라톤조차 아내와 자식과 가정을 대신해 줄 만족스러운 대용품이 되지는 못했으리라."]], [["Early as it was, he was at the station, next morning, to see Jo off; and, thanks to him, she began her solitary journey with the pleasant memory of a familiar face smiling its farewell, a bunch of violets to keep her company, and, best of all, the happy thought,--", "이른 시각이었지만 이튿날 아침 그는 조를 배웅하러 역에 나와 있었다. 그 덕분에 그녀는 작별의 미소를 띤 낯익은 얼굴의 흐뭇한 기억과, 길동무가 되어 줄 제비꽃 한 다발과, 무엇보다도 행복한 생각 하나를 품은 채 홀로 길을 떠날 수 있었다."]], [["\"Well, the winter's gone, and I've written no books, earned no fortune; but I've made a friend worth having, and I'll try to keep him all my life.\"", "\"그래, 겨울은 지나갔어. 책을 쓰지도, 재물을 벌지도 못했지. 하지만 사귈 만한 가치가 있는 벗을 하나 얻었으니, 평생 그를 잃지 않도록 애써야지.\" 35장"]], [["Whatever his motive might have been, Laurie studied to some purpose that year, for he graduated with honor, and gave the Latin oration with the grace of a Phillips and the eloquence of a Demosthenes, so his friends said.", "가슴앓이\n동기가 무엇이었든 간에, 로리는 그해 어느 정도 보람 있게 공부했다. 우등으로 졸업하면서 라틴어 연설을 했는데, 친구들 말로는 필립스 같은 우아함과 데모스테네스 같은 웅변을 갖추었다고 했다."], ["They were all there, his grandfather,--oh, so proud!--Mr.", "식구들 모두가 그 자리에 있었다."], ["and Mrs.", "할아버지는 — 아, 어찌나 자랑스러워하시던지!"], ["March, John and Meg, Jo and Beth, and all exulted over him with the sincere admiration which boys make light of at the time, but fail to win from the world by any after-triumphs.", "— 그리고 마치 부부, 존과 메그, 조와 베스가 다 함께 그를 진심으로 흠모하며 기뻐했다. 그것은 소년들이 그 당시에는 대수롭지 않게 여기지만, 훗날 어떤 승리로도 세상에서 얻어 내지 못하는 그런 흠모였다."]], [["\"I've got to stay for this confounded supper, but I shall be home early to-morrow; you'll come and meet me as usual, girls?\"", "\"이 빌어먹을 만찬 때문에 남아 있어야 하지만, 내일은 일찍 집에 갈 거야. 너희들, 여느 때처럼 마중 나와 줄 거지?\""], ["Laurie said, as he put the sisters into the carriage after the joys of the day were over.", "그날의 즐거움이 끝난 뒤 자매들을 마차에 태우며 로리가 말했다. 그는 \"너희들\"이라고 했지만, 사실은 조를 두고 한 말이었다."], ["He said \"girls,\"", "옛 관습을 지키는 사람은 조밖에 없었으니까."], ["but he meant Jo, for she was the only one who kept up the old custom; she had not the heart to refuse her splendid, successful boy anything, and answered warmly,--", "조는 자기의 멋지고 성공한 소년에게 무엇 하나 거절할 마음이 없어서, 따뜻하게 대답했다."]], [["\"I'll come, Teddy, rain or shine, and march before you, playing '_Hail the conquering hero comes_,'", "\"갈게, 테디. 비가 오나 해가 나나 갈 거야."], ["on a jews-harp.\"", "그리고 입금(口琴)으로 '보아라, 정복의 용사 오신다'를 연주하면서 네 앞에서 행진해 줄게.\""]], [["Laurie thanked her with a look that made her think, in a sudden panic, \"Oh, deary me!", "로리는 어떤 눈빛으로 그녀에게 고마움을 표했는데, 그 눈빛에 조는 문득 겁이 나서 이런 생각이 들었다."], ["I know he'll say something, and then what shall I do?\"", "'아, 어떡하지! 분명 무슨 말을 할 텐데, 그러면 난 어쩌지?'"]], [["Evening meditation and morning work somewhat allayed her fears, and having decided that she wouldn't be vain enough to think people were going to propose when she had given them every reason to know what her answer would be, she set forth at the appointed time, hoping Teddy wouldn't do anything to make her hurt his poor little feelings. A call at Meg's, and a refreshing sniff and sip at the Daisy and Demijohn, still further fortified her for the _tête-à-tête_, but when she saw a stalwart figure looming in the distance, she had a strong desire to turn about and run away.", "저녁의 사색과 아침의 일이 그녀의 두려움을 어느 정도 가라앉혀 주었다. 자기가 어떤 대답을 할지 충분히 짐작할 만한 빌미를 다 주어 놓고도 상대가 청혼할 거라고 여길 만큼 자만하지는 않겠다고 마음먹은 그녀는, 약속한 시간에 길을 나섰다. 테디가 그 가엾고 여린 마음에 상처를 줄 만한 짓을 하지 않기를 바라면서였다. 메그의 집에 들러 데이지와 데미존을 보며 기운을 차리고 한숨 돌리자, 그 단둘의 만남을 앞두고 한층 더 마음이 다잡아졌다. 하지만 저 멀리 건장한 형체가 불쑥 나타나는 것을 보자, 돌아서서 달아나고 싶은 강한 충동이 일었다."]], [["\"Where's the jews-harp, Jo?\"", "\"입금은 어디 있어, 조?\""], ["cried Laurie, as soon as he was within speaking distance.", "말소리가 닿을 만한 거리에 이르자마자 로리가 외쳤다."]], [["\"I forgot it;\"", "\"깜빡했어.\""], ["and Jo took heart again, for that salutation could not be called lover-like.", "조는 다시 마음을 놓았다. 그런 인사라면 연인다운 것이라고는 할 수 없었으니까."]], [["She always used to take his arm on these occasions; now she did not, and he made no complaint, which was a bad sign, but talked on rapidly about all sorts of far-away subjects, till they turned from the road into the little path that led homeward through the grove.", "평소 이럴 때면 그녀는 늘 그의 팔짱을 끼곤 했다. 그런데 이번에는 그러지 않았고, 그는 불평하지 않았다."], ["Then he walked more slowly, suddenly lost his fine flow of language, and, now and then, a dreadful pause occurred.", "그것이 나쁜 징조였다. 그는 집으로 향하는 작은 오솔길로 들어서서 작은 숲을 지날 때까지, 갖가지 동떨어진 화제를 빠르게 늘어놓기만 했다."], ["To rescue the conversation from one of the wells of silence into which it kept falling, Jo said hastily,--", "그러다 숲에 접어들자 그는 걸음이 느려지더니, 갑자기 그 유창하던 말솜씨를 잃어버렸고, 이따금 끔찍한 침묵이 흘렀다. 자꾸만 빠져드는 그 침묵의 우물 가운데 하나에서 대화를 건져 올리려고 조가 황급히 말했다."]], [["\"Now you must have a good long holiday!\"", "\"이제 너 푹 쉬는 긴 휴가를 보내야겠다!\""]], [["\"I intend to.\"", "\"그럴 생각이야.\""]], [["Something in his resolute tone made Jo look up quickly to find him looking down at her with an expression that assured her the dreaded moment had come, and made her put out her hand with an imploring,--", "그의 단호한 어조에서 무언가가 느껴져 조는 얼른 고개를 들었고, 그가 두려워하던 순간이 왔음을 알려 주는 표정으로 자기를 내려다보고 있는 것을 발견했다. 그녀는 애원하듯 손을 내밀었다."]], [["\"No, Teddy, please don't!\"", "\"안 돼, 테디, 제발 그러지 마!\""]], [["\"I will, and you _must_ hear me.", "\"할 거야, 너는 내 말을 들어야 해."], ["It's no use, Jo; we've got to have it out, and the sooner the better for both of us,\"", "소용없어, 조. 우린 이 문제를 매듭지어야 하고, 빠를수록 우리 둘 다에게 좋아.\""], ["he answered, getting flushed and excited all at once.", "그가 갑자기 얼굴이 붉어지고 흥분해서 대답했다."]], [["\"Say what you like, then; I'll listen,\"", "\"그럼 하고 싶은 말을 해. 들을게.\""], ["said Jo, with a desperate sort of patience.", "조가 자포자기한 듯한 인내심으로 말했다."]], [["Laurie was a young lover, but he was in earnest, and meant to \"have it out,\"", "로리는 젊은 연인이었지만 진심이었고, 그러다 죽는 한이 있어도 \"매듭짓겠다\"고 작정했다."], ["if he died in the attempt; so he plunged into the subject with characteristic impetuosity, saying in a voice that _would_ get choky now and then, in spite of manful efforts to keep it steady,--", "그래서 그는 특유의 격정으로 본론에 뛰어들어, 침착하게 유지하려는 사내다운 노력에도 불구하고 자꾸만 목이 메는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"I've loved you ever since I've known you, Jo; couldn't help it, you've been so good to me. I've tried to show it, but you wouldn't let me; now I'm going to make you hear, and give me an answer, for I _can't_ go on so any longer.\"", "\"너를 안 뒤로 줄곧 사랑해 왔어, 조. 어쩔 수가 없었어. 네가 나한테 너무 잘해 줬으니까. 그걸 보여 주려고 애썼지만 넌 받아 주지 않았지. 이제 난 너한테 기어이 듣게 하고, 대답을 받아 낼 거야. 더는 이렇게 못 견디겠으니까.\""]], [["\"I wanted to save you this; I thought you'd understand--\"", "\"난 너한테 이런 일을 겪게 하고 싶지 않았어. 네가 알아주리라 생각했는데—\""], ["began Jo, finding it a great deal harder than she expected.", "조가 입을 열었지만, 생각보다 훨씬 더 힘든 일이었다."]], [["\"I know you did; but girls are so queer you never know what they mean.", "\"알아, 그랬다는 거. 하지만 여자들은 어찌나 알 수 없는지 무슨 뜻인지 도무지 모르겠어."], ["They say No when they mean Yes, and drive a man out of his wits just for the fun of it,\"", "'아니'라고 해 놓고 '응'이라는 뜻이고, 그냥 재미로 남자를 미치게 하잖아.\""], ["returned Laurie, entrenching himself behind an undeniable fact.", "로리가 부정할 수 없는 사실 뒤에 몸을 숨기며 대꾸했다."]], [["\"_I_ don't.", "\"난 안 그래."], ["I never wanted to make you care for me so, and I went away to keep you from it if I could.\"", "난 네가 나를 그렇게 좋아하게 만들고 싶었던 적이 없어. 그래서 할 수 있다면 막아 보려고 멀리 떠났던 거야.\""]], [["\"I thought so; it was like you, but it was no use. I only loved you all the more, and I worked hard to please you, and I gave up billiards and everything you didn't like, and waited and never complained, for I hoped you'd love me, though I'm not half good enough--\" here there was a choke that couldn't be controlled, so he decapitated buttercups while he cleared his \"confounded throat.\"", "\"나도 그렇게 생각했어. 너다운 일이었지. 하지만 소용없었어. 난 오히려 너를 더 사랑하게 됐을 뿐이야. 네 마음에 들려고 무던히 애쓰고, 당구도 끊고, 네가 싫어하는 건 다 그만뒀어. 그리고 기다리면서 한 번도 불평하지 않았어. 내가 절반도 좋은 사람은 못 되지만, 그래도 네가 날 사랑해 주기를 바랐으니까—\" 여기서 도저히 억누를 수 없는 울먹임이 새어 나오자, 그는 \"빌어먹을 목\"을 가다듬는 척하며 미나리아재비 꽃 모가지를 똑똑 꺾었다."]], [["\"Yes, you are; you're a great deal too good for me, and I'm so grateful to you, and so proud and fond of you, I don't see why I can't love you as you want me to. I've tried, but I can't change the feeling, and it would be a lie to say I do when I don't.\"", "\"넌 충분히 좋은 사람이야. 오히려 나한테는 과분하지. 난 너한테 정말 고맙고, 또 네가 너무 자랑스럽고 좋아. 그런데 왜 네가 바라는 대로 너를 사랑할 수 없는지 모르겠어. 애써 봤지만, 이 감정을 바꿀 수가 없어. 사랑하지 않으면서 사랑한다고 말하는 건 거짓말일 거야.\""]], [["\"Really, truly, Jo?\"", "\"정말, 진심으로 그래, 조?\""]], [["He stopped short, and caught both her hands as he put his question with a look that she did not soon forget.", "그는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 그녀의 두 손을 붙잡고, 그녀가 좀처럼 잊지 못할 눈빛으로 물었다."]], [["\"Really, truly, dear.\"", "\"정말, 진심으로 그래.\""]], [["They were in the grove now, close by the stile; and when the last words fell reluctantly from Jo's lips, Laurie dropped her hands and turned as if to go on, but for once in his life that fence was too much for him; so he just laid his head down on the mossy post, and stood so still that Jo was frightened.", "이제 두 사람은 작은 숲속, 울타리 층계 바로 옆에 있었다. 마지막 말이 조의 입술에서 마지못해 떨어지자, 로리는 그녀의 손을 놓고 계속 걸어가려는 듯 돌아섰다. 하지만 평생 처음으로 그 울타리가 그에게는 너무 버거웠다. 그래서 그는 이끼 낀 기둥에 그저 머리를 기댄 채, 조가 겁이 날 만큼 꼼짝도 않고 서 있었다."]], [["\"O Teddy, I'm so sorry, so desperately sorry, I could kill myself if it would do any good!", "\"아, 테디, 정말 미안해. 너무너무 미안해서, 그게 도움이 된다면 차라리 나를 죽이고 싶을 정도야!"], ["I wish you wouldn't take it so hard.", "네가 이걸 그렇게 힘들어하지 않으면 좋겠어."], ["I can't help it; you know it's impossible for people to make themselves love other people if they don't,\"", "나도 어쩔 수가 없어. 사람이 사랑하지 않는 사람을 억지로 사랑하게 만드는 건 불가능하다는 거, 너도 알잖아.\""], ["cried Jo inelegantly but remorsefully, as she softly patted his shoulder, remembering the time when he had comforted her so long ago.", "조가 격식도 없이, 하지만 가책에 차서 외쳤다. 그러면서 오래전 그가 자기를 그토록 위로해 주던 때를 떠올리며 그의 어깨를 가만히 토닥였다."]], [["\"They do sometimes,\"", "\"가끔은 그렇게 되기도 해.\""], ["said a muffled voice from the post.", "기둥 쪽에서 먹먹한 목소리가 들려왔다."]], [["\"I don't believe it's the right sort of love, and I'd rather not try it,\"", "\"그건 올바른 종류의 사랑이 아닐 거고, 난 그런 건 시도하고 싶지 않아.\""], ["was the decided answer.", "단호한 대답이었다."]], [["There was a long pause, while a blackbird sung blithely on the willow by the river, and the tall grass rustled in the wind.", "긴 침묵이 흘렀다. 강가 버드나무 위에서 검은새 한 마리가 흥겹게 노래했고, 키 큰 풀이 바람에 사각거렸다."], ["Presently Jo said very soberly, as she sat down on the step of the stile,--", "이윽고 조가 울타리 층계에 앉으며 아주 차분하게 말했다."]], [["\"Laurie, I want to tell you something.\"", "\"로리, 너한테 할 말이 있어.\""]], [["He started as if he had been shot, threw up his head, and cried out, in a fierce tone--", "그는 총에라도 맞은 듯 흠칫하더니 고개를 번쩍 들고 사나운 어조로 외쳤다."]], [["\"_Don't_ tell me that, Jo; I can't bear it now!\"", "\"그 말은 하지 마, 조. 지금은 도저히 못 견뎌!\""]], [["\"Tell what?\"", "\"무슨 말?\""], ["she asked, wondering at his violence.", "그의 격렬함에 의아해하며 그녀가 물었다."]], [["\"That you love that old man.\"", "\"네가 그 늙은이를 사랑한다는 말.\""]], [["\"What old man?\"", "\"무슨 늙은이?\""], ["demanded Jo, thinking he must mean his grandfather.", "조가 따져 물었다. 그가 분명 자기 할아버지를 두고 하는 말이라고 생각하면서."]], [["\"That devilish Professor you were always writing about.", "\"네가 늘 편지에 쓰던 그 빌어먹을 교수 말이야."], ["If you say you love him, I know I shall do something desperate;\"", "네가 그자를 사랑한다고 하면, 난 분명 무슨 끔찍한 짓을 저지를 거야.\""], ["and he looked as if he would keep his word, as he clenched his hands, with a wrathful spark in his eyes.", "그가 두 손을 불끈 쥐고 눈에 노여운 불꽃을 튀기며, 정말 그 말대로 할 것 같은 표정을 지었다."]], [["Jo wanted to laugh, but restrained herself, and said warmly, for she, too, was getting excited with all this,--", "조는 웃음이 터질 뻔했지만 꾹 참고, 자기도 이 모든 일에 흥분이 일던 터라 따뜻하게 말했다."]], [["\"Don't swear, Teddy!", "\"욕하지 마, 테디!"], ["He isn't old, nor anything bad, but good and kind, and the best friend I've got, next to you.", "그분은 늙지도 않았고, 나쁜 구석이라곤 하나도 없어. 선하고 친절하시고, 너 다음으로 내가 가진 가장 좋은 친구야."], ["Pray, don't fly into a passion; I want to be kind, but I know I shall get angry if you abuse my Professor.", "제발, 이렇게 발끈하지 마. 난 너한테 다정하게 굴고 싶은데, 네가 우리 교수님을 모욕하면 분명 화가 날 것 같아."], ["I haven't the least idea of loving him or anybody else.\"", "난 그분이든 다른 누구든 사랑할 생각이 눈곱만큼도 없어.\""]], [["\"But you will after a while, and then what will become of me?\"", "\"하지만 언젠가는 그렇게 될 거고, 그럼 난 어떻게 되는 거야?\""]], [["\"You'll love some one else too, like a sensible boy, and forget all this trouble.\"", "\"넌 분별 있는 소년답게 다른 누군가를 사랑하게 될 거고, 이 모든 괴로움은 다 잊어버릴 거야.\""]], [["\"I _can't_ love any one else; and I'll never forget you, Jo, never!", "\"난 다른 누구도 사랑할 수 없어. 그리고 너를 절대 잊지 않을 거야, 조, 절대로!"], ["never!\"", "절대로!\""], ["with a stamp to emphasize his passionate words.", "그가 격정에 찬 말에 힘을 싣느라 발을 쾅 굴렀다."]], [["\"What _shall_ I do with him?\"", "'얘를 어쩌면 좋아?'"], ["sighed Jo, finding that emotions were more unmanageable than she expected.", "조는 감정이라는 게 생각보다 훨씬 다루기 힘든 것임을 깨달으며 한숨지었다."], ["\"You haven't heard what I wanted to tell you.", "\"넌 내가 하려던 말을 아직 안 들었잖아."], ["Sit down and listen; for indeed I want to do right and make you happy,\"", "앉아서 들어 봐. 난 정말로 옳은 일을 하고 너를 행복하게 해 주고 싶거든.\""], ["she said, hoping to soothe him with a little reason, which proved that she knew nothing about love.", "그녀는 약간의 이치로 그를 달래 보려 했지만, 그것은 그녀가 사랑에 대해 아무것도 모른다는 증거였다."]], [["Seeing a ray of hope in that last speech, Laurie threw himself down on the grass at her feet, leaned his arm on the lower step of the stile, and looked up at her with an expectant face.", "그 마지막 말에서 한 줄기 희망의 빛을 본 로리는 그녀의 발치 풀밭에 털썩 몸을 던지고, 울타리 아래 층계에 팔을 기댄 채 기대에 찬 얼굴로 그녀를 올려다보았다."], ["Now that arrangement was not conducive to calm speech or clear thought on Jo's part; for how _could_ she say hard things to her boy while he watched her with eyes full of love and longing, and lashes still wet with the bitter drop or two her hardness of heart had wrung from him?", "그런데 그런 자세는 조가 차분하게 말하거나 또렷하게 생각하는 데 전혀 도움이 되지 않았다. 사랑과 갈망으로 가득한 눈으로, 그녀의 모진 마음이 짜낸 쓰디쓴 눈물 한두 방울로 아직 속눈썹이 젖은 채 자기를 바라보는 소년에게, 어떻게 매정한 말을 할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["She gently turned his head away, saying, as she stroked the wavy hair which had been allowed to grow for her sake,--how touching that was, to be sure!--", "그녀는 그를 위해 기르도록 내버려 둔 그 물결치는 머리카락을 쓰다듬으며 — 그게 얼마나 가슴 뭉클한 일이었던가! — 그의 고개를 부드럽게 돌려놓고 말했다."]], [["\"I agree with mother that you and I are not suited to each other, because our quick tempers and strong wills would probably make us very miserable, if we were so foolish as to--\"", "\"엄마 말씀이 맞아. 너랑 나는 서로 어울리지 않아."], ["Jo paused a little over the last word, but Laurie uttered it with a rapturous expression,--", "우리 둘 다 성미가 급하고 고집이 세서, 만약 어리석게도 그런 짓을 했다간 아마 아주 비참해질 테니까—\" 조는 마지막 말 앞에서 잠시 머뭇거렸지만, 로리가 황홀한 표정으로 그 말을 내뱉었다."]], [["\"Marry,--no, we shouldn't!", "\"결혼 말이지. 아니, 우린 비참해지지 않을 거야!"], ["If you loved me, Jo, I should be a perfect saint, for you could make me anything you like.\"", "네가 날 사랑한다면, 난 완벽한 성자가 될 거야. 넌 날 네가 원하는 대로 뭐든 만들 수 있으니까.\""]], [["\"No, I can't.", "\"아니, 난 못 해."], ["I've tried it and failed, and I won't risk our happiness by such a serious experiment.", "해 봤지만 실패했어. 그런 중대한 실험으로 우리 행복을 걸 순 없어."], ["We don't agree and we never shall; so we'll be good friends all our lives, but we won't go and do anything rash.\"", "우린 서로 안 맞고, 앞으로도 절대 그럴 거야. 그러니 평생 좋은 친구로 지내되, 경솔한 짓은 하지 말자.\""]], [["\"Yes, we will if we get the chance,\"", "\"기회만 생기면 우린 할 거야.\""], ["muttered Laurie rebelliously.", "로리가 반항하듯 중얼거렸다."]], [["\"Now do be reasonable, and take a sensible view of the case,\"", "\"제발 이성적으로 생각하고, 이 일을 분별 있게 봐 줘.\""], ["implored Jo, almost at her wit's end.", "조가 거의 어찌할 바를 모르며 간청했다."]], [["\"I won't be reasonable; I don't want to take what you call 'a sensible view;'", "\"난 이성적으로 안 굴 거야. 네가 말하는 그 '분별 있는 관점'이란 것도 갖고 싶지 않아."], ["it won't help me, and it only makes you harder.", "그건 나한테 아무 도움도 안 되고, 너만 더 모질게 만들 뿐이야."], ["I don't believe you've got any heart.\"", "난 너한테 마음이란 게 있긴 한지 모르겠어.\""]], [["\"I wish I hadn't!\"", "\"나도 없었으면 좋겠어!\""]], [["There was a little quiver in Jo's voice, and, thinking it a good omen, Laurie turned round, bringing all his persuasive powers to bear as he said, in the wheedlesome tone that had never been so dangerously wheedlesome before,--", "조의 목소리가 살짝 떨렸고, 그것을 좋은 징조로 여긴 로리는 돌아앉아 온갖 설득력을 동원해, 일찍이 그토록 위험할 만큼 달콤했던 적 없는 달래는 어조로 말했다."]], [["\"Don't disappoint us, dear!", "\"우리를 실망시키지 마, 조!"], ["Every one expects it.", "다들 그렇게 되길 바라고 있어."], ["Grandpa has set his heart upon it, your people like it, and I can't get on without you.", "할아버지도 그 일에 마음을 두고 계시고, 네 식구들도 좋아하고, 난 너 없이는 살 수가 없어."], ["Say you will, and let's be happy.", "그러겠다고 말해 줘, 우리 행복해지자."], ["Do, do!\"", "제발, 제발!\""]], [["Not until months afterward did Jo understand how she had the strength of mind to hold fast to the resolution she had made when she decided that she did not love her boy, and never could.", "자기는 자기 소년을 사랑하지 않으며 앞으로도 결코 사랑할 수 없다고 결론 내렸을 때 다진 그 결심을, 어떻게 그토록 굳건히 지킬 정신력이 있었는지 조는 여러 달이 지나서야 비로소 이해할 수 있었다."], ["It was very hard to do, but she did it, knowing that delay was both useless and cruel.", "무척 힘든 일이었지만 그녀는 해냈다. 머뭇거려 봤자 소용도 없고 잔인하기만 하다는 것을 알았기 때문이다."]], [["\"I can't say 'Yes' truly, so I won't say it at all.", "\"난 진심으로 '응'이라고 말할 수 없으니까, 아예 말하지 않을래."], ["You'll see that I'm right, by and by, and thank me for it\"--she began solemnly.", "머잖아 내가 옳았다는 걸 알게 될 거고, 오히려 나한테 고마워하게 될 거야—\" 그녀가 엄숙하게 입을 열었다."]], [["\"I'll be hanged if I do!\"", "\"내가 그러면 목을 매달겠어!\""], ["and Laurie bounced up off the grass, burning with indignation at the bare idea.", "로리가 생각만으로도 분에 차 풀밭에서 벌떡 튀어 일어났다."]], [["\"Yes, you will!\" persisted Jo; \"you'll get over this after a while, and find some lovely, accomplished girl, who will adore you, and make a fine mistress for your fine house. I shouldn't. I'm homely and awkward and odd and old, and you'd be ashamed of me, and we should quarrel,--we can't help it even now, you see,--and I shouldn't like elegant society and you would, and you'd hate my scribbling, and I couldn't get on without it, and we should be unhappy, and wish we hadn't done it, and everything would be horrid!\"", "\"아니, 그렇게 될 거야!\" 조가 고집스레 말을 이었다. \"넌 머잖아 이걸 다 극복하고, 사랑스럽고 교양 있는 아가씨를 만날 거야. 너를 흠모하고, 네 멋진 집의 훌륭한 안주인이 되어 줄 그런 사람을. 난 그렇게 못 해. 난 못생기고 어설프고 별나고 나이 들었어. 너는 나를 부끄러워하게 될 거고, 우린 싸우게 될 거야. 봐, 지금도 어쩔 수 없잖아. 게다가 난 우아한 사교계를 싫어하지만 넌 좋아하고, 넌 내 끄적거리는 글을 질색할 텐데 난 그게 없으면 못 살아. 그러니 우린 불행해지고, 괜히 그런 짓을 했다고 후회하게 될 거고, 모든 게 끔찍해질 거야!\""]], [["\"Anything more?\"", "\"더 할 말 있어?\""], ["asked Laurie, finding it hard to listen patiently to this prophetic burst.", "로리가 이 예언 같은 쏟아냄을 참을성 있게 듣기 힘들어하며 물었다."]], [["\"Nothing more, except that I don't believe I shall ever marry.", "\"없어. 다만, 난 평생 결혼하지 않을 것 같아."], ["I'm happy as I am, and love my liberty too well to be in any hurry to give it up for any mortal man.\"", "난 지금 이대로 행복하고, 내 자유를 너무 사랑해서, 어떤 남자를 위해서든 그걸 서둘러 포기할 마음이 없거든.\""]], [["\"I know better!\"", "\"난 잘 알아!\""], ["broke in Laurie.", "로리가 끼어들었다."], ["\"You think so now; but there'll come a time when you _will_ care for somebody, and you'll love him tremendously, and live and die for him.", "\"지금은 그렇게 생각하지. 하지만 언젠가 네가 누군가를 정말로 마음에 두게 될 때가 올 거고, 그땐 그 사람을 엄청나게 사랑하고, 그 사람을 위해 살고 죽으려 할 거야."], ["I know you will, it's your way, and I shall have to stand by and see it;\"", "분명 그럴 거야, 그게 너다운 거니까. 그리고 난 옆에 서서 그걸 지켜봐야겠지.\""], ["and the despairing lover cast his hat upon the ground with a gesture that would have seemed comical, if his face had not been so tragical.", "절망에 빠진 연인은 모자를 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳤는데, 그 얼굴이 그토록 비극적이지만 않았다면 우스꽝스러워 보였을 몸짓이었다."]], [["\"Yes, I _will_ live and die for him, if he ever comes and makes me love him in spite of myself, and you must do the best you can!\" cried Jo, losing patience with poor Teddy. \"I've done my best, but you _won't_ be reasonable, and it's selfish of you to keep teasing for what I can't give. I shall always be fond of you, very fond indeed, as a friend, but I'll never marry you; and the sooner you believe it, the better for both of us,--so now!\"", "\"그래, 난 그 사람을 위해 살고 죽을 거야. 만약 그런 사람이 나타나서, 나 자신도 어쩔 수 없이 그를 사랑하게 만든다면 말이야. 그러니 넌 네가 할 수 있는 최선을 다해야 해!\" 조가 가엾은 테디에게 인내심을 잃고 외쳤다. \"난 최선을 다했는데도 넌 도무지 이성적으로 굴지를 않아. 내가 줄 수 없는 걸 자꾸 졸라 대는 건 이기적인 짓이야. 난 친구로서 너를 늘 좋아할 거야, 정말 무척이나. 하지만 너랑은 절대 결혼하지 않아. 그러니 빨리 그걸 믿을수록 우리 둘 다에게 좋아. 자, 됐지!\""]], [["That speech was like fire to gunpowder.", "그 말은 화약에 불을 댕긴 것과 같았다."], ["Laurie looked at her a minute as if he did not quite know what to do with himself, then turned sharply away, saying, in a desperate sort of tone,--", "로리는 잠시 어쩔 줄 모르겠다는 듯이 그녀를 바라보다가, 홱 돌아서며 자포자기한 듯한 어조로 말했다."]], [["\"You'll be sorry some day, Jo.\"", "\"언젠간 후회할 거야, 조.\""]], [["\"Oh, where are you going?\"", "\"아니, 어디 가는 거야?\""], ["she cried, for his face frightened her.", "그의 얼굴이 그녀를 겁먹게 했기에 그녀가 외쳤다."]], [["\"To the devil!\"", "\"악마한테나!\""], ["was the consoling answer.", "그것이 위로가 되는 대답이었다."]], [["For a minute Jo's heart stood still, as he swung himself down the bank, toward the river; but it takes much folly, sin, or misery to send a young man to a violent death, and Laurie was not one of the weak sort who are conquered by a single failure. He had no thought of a melodramatic plunge, but some blind instinct led him to fling hat and coat into his boat, and row away with all his might, making better time up the river than he had done in many a race. Jo drew a long breath and unclasped her hands as she watched the poor fellow trying to outstrip the trouble which he carried in his heart.", "순간 조의 심장이 멎는 듯했다. 그가 강 쪽으로 둑을 미끄러져 내려갔던 것이다. 하지만 젊은이를 끔찍한 죽음으로 몰아넣으려면 어지간한 어리석음이나 죄악, 혹은 비참함이 있어야 하는 법이고, 로리는 한 번의 실패에 무너질 만큼 나약한 부류가 아니었다. 그에게는 극적으로 강물에 뛰어들겠다는 생각 따위는 없었다. 다만 어떤 맹목적인 본능에 이끌려 모자와 코트를 자기 보트에 내던지고, 있는 힘껏 노를 저어 멀어져 갔다. 어느 시합 때보다도 빠른 속도로 강을 거슬러 올라가면서. 조는 긴 숨을 내쉬고 맞잡았던 두 손을 풀었다. 그 가엾은 친구가 가슴에 품은 괴로움을 떼어 놓으려 애쓰는 모습을 지켜보면서였다."]], [["\"That will do him good, and he'll come home in such a tender, penitent state of mind, that I sha'n't dare to see him,\"", "\"저렇게 하면 좀 나아질 거야. 그리고 집에 돌아올 때쯤이면 어찌나 다정하고 뉘우치는 마음일지, 난 차마 그 애를 못 볼 거야.\""], ["she said; adding, as she went slowly home, feeling as if she had murdered some innocent thing, and buried it under the leaves,--", "그녀는 그렇게 말하고는, 마치 죄 없는 무언가를 죽여 나뭇잎 아래 묻어 버린 듯한 기분으로 천천히 집을 향해 걸으며 덧붙였다."]], [["\"Now I must go and prepare Mr.", "\"이제 가서 로렌스 할아버지께, 우리 가엾은 소년한테 아주 다정하게 대해 달라고 미리 말씀드려야겠어."], ["Laurence to be very kind to my poor boy.", "할아버지가 베스를 사랑해 주시면 좋겠는데."], ["I wish he'd love Beth; perhaps he may, in time, but I begin to think I was mistaken about her.", "어쩌면 시간이 지나면 그러실지도 몰라. 하지만 내가 그 애에 대해 잘못 생각했던 것 같아."], ["Oh dear!", "아, 정말!"], ["how can girls like to have lovers and refuse them.", "여자애들은 어떻게 연인을 두고서 또 거절하기를 좋아할 수가 있을까."], ["I think it's dreadful.\"", "정말 끔찍한 일이야.\""]], [["Being sure that no one could do it so well as herself, she went straight to Mr.", "이 일은 자기만큼 잘해 낼 사람이 없으리라 확신한 조는 곧장 로렌스 씨에게 가서, 그 괴로운 이야기를 용감하게 끝까지 전했다."], ["Laurence, told the hard story bravely through, and then broke down, crying so dismally over her own insensibility that the kind old gentleman, though sorely disappointed, did not utter a reproach.", "그러고는 무너져 내려, 자신의 무정함을 두고 어찌나 서럽게 울던지, 그 인자한 노신사는 몹시 실망스러우면서도 책망 한마디 입에 올리지 않았다. 그는 어떤 처녀가 로리를 사랑하지 않을 수 있는지 도무지 이해할 수가 없었고, 그녀가 마음을 바꾸기를 바랐다."], ["He found it difficult to understand how any girl could help loving Laurie, and hoped she would change her mind, but he knew even better than Jo that love cannot be forced, so he shook his head sadly, and resolved to carry his boy out of harm's way; for Young Impetuosity's parting words to Jo disturbed him more than he would confess.", "하지만 사랑이란 억지로 되는 것이 아님을 그는 조보다도 더 잘 알았기에, 슬프게 고개를 가로저으며 자기 소년을 위험에서 멀리 데리고 가기로 결심했다. 그 혈기 왕성한 젊은이가 조에게 남긴 작별의 말이, 그가 인정하려는 것 이상으로 그를 불안하게 했던 것이다."]], [["When Laurie came home, dead tired, but quite composed, his grandfather met him as if he knew nothing, and kept up the delusion very successfully for an hour or two.", "로리가 녹초가 되었지만 꽤 차분해진 모습으로 집에 돌아오자, 할아버지는 아무것도 모르는 척 그를 맞이했고, 한두 시간 동안 그 시치미를 아주 그럴듯하게 유지했다."], ["But when they sat together in the twilight, the time they used to enjoy so much, it was hard work for the old man to ramble on as usual, and harder still for the young one to listen to praises of the last year's success, which to him now seemed love's labor lost.", "그러나 두 사람이 그토록 즐기곤 하던 황혼 녘에 함께 앉아 있자니, 늙은이로서는 여느 때처럼 이런저런 이야기를 늘어놓기가 힘들었고, 젊은이로서는 지난해의 성공에 대한 칭찬을 듣기가 더더욱 괴로웠다. 그에게 그것은 이제 사랑의 헛수고처럼 여겨졌으니까."], ["He bore it as long as he could, then went to his piano, and began to play.", "그는 참을 수 있는 데까지 참다가, 피아노로 가서 연주를 시작했다."], ["The windows were open; and Jo, walking in the garden with Beth, for once understood music better than her sister, for he played the \"Sonata Pathétique,\"", "창문은 열려 있었다. 베스와 함께 정원을 거닐던 조는 이번만은 동생보다 음악을 더 잘 이해했다."], ["and played it as he never did before.", "그가 \"비창 소나타\"를, 그것도 일찍이 친 적 없는 연주로 치고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["\"That's very fine, I dare say, but it's sad enough to make one cry; give us something gayer, lad,\"", "\"썩 훌륭하구나. 그런데 사람을 울리고도 남을 만큼 구슬프네."], ["said Mr.", "좀 더 흥겨운 걸 들려주렴, 얘야.\""], ["Laurence, whose kind old heart was full of sympathy, which he longed to show, but knew not how.", "로렌스 씨가 말했다. 그 인자한 노인의 마음은 보여 주고 싶지만 어떻게 표현해야 할지 모르는 연민으로 가득했다."]], [["Laurie dashed into a livelier strain, played stormily for several minutes, and would have got through bravely, if, in a momentary lull, Mrs.", "로리는 좀 더 활기찬 가락으로 옮겨 가, 몇 분간 거세게 연주했다."], ["March's voice had not been heard calling,--", "잠시 소리가 잦아드는 틈에 마치 부인의 목소리가 들려오지만 않았더라면, 그는 용케 끝까지 잘 쳐 냈을 것이다."]], [["\"Jo, dear, come in; I want you.\"", "\"조야, 들어오너라. 너한테 볼일이 있구나.\""]], [["Just what Laurie longed to say, with a different meaning!", "로리가 다른 의미로 그토록 하고 싶었던 바로 그 말이었다!"], ["As he listened, he lost his place; the music ended with a broken chord, and the musician sat silent in the dark.", "그 소리를 듣다가 그는 박자를 놓쳤다. 음악은 깨진 화음으로 끝났고, 연주자는 어둠 속에 말없이 앉아 있었다."]], [["\"I can't stand this,\"", "\"이건 못 견디겠구나.\""], ["muttered the old gentleman.", "노신사가 중얼거렸다."], ["Up he got, groped his way to the piano, laid a kind hand on either of the broad shoulders, and said, as gently as a woman,--", "그는 자리에서 일어나 더듬더듬 피아노로 다가가, 그 넓은 두 어깨에 다정한 손을 얹고는 여인처럼 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["\"I know, my boy, I know.\"", "\"안다, 얘야. 다 안다.\""]], [["No answer for an instant; then Laurie asked sharply,--", "한순간 아무 대답이 없었다. 그러다 로리가 날카롭게 물었다."]], [["\"Who told you?\"", "\"누가 그러셨어요?\""]], [["\"Jo herself.\"", "\"조가 직접 그러더구나.\""]], [["\"Then there's an end of it!\"", "\"그럼 다 끝난 거네요!\""], ["and he shook off his grandfather's hands with an impatient motion; for, though grateful for the sympathy, his man's pride could not bear a man's pity.", "그는 짜증스러운 몸짓으로 할아버지의 손을 뿌리쳤다. 그 연민이 고맙긴 했어도, 사내의 자존심으로는 사내의 동정을 견딜 수 없었던 것이다."]], [["\"Not quite; I want to say one thing, and then there shall be an end of it,\"", "\"아주 끝난 건 아니다."], ["returned Mr.", "한마디만 하마."], ["Laurence, with unusual mildness.", "그러고 나면 이걸로 끝내자꾸나.\""], ["\"You won't care to stay at home just now, perhaps?\"", "로렌스 씨가 보기 드물게 부드러운 어조로 대답했다. \"지금은 집에 있고 싶지 않을 테지, 아마?\""]], [["\"I don't intend to run away from a girl.", "\"전 계집애한테서 달아날 생각은 없어요."], ["Jo can't prevent my seeing her, and I shall stay and do it as long as I like,\"", "조가 제가 그 애를 만나는 걸 막을 순 없고, 전 여기 남아서 제가 원하는 만큼 그 애를 볼 거예요.\""], ["interrupted Laurie, in a defiant tone.", "로리가 반항적인 어조로 말을 가로막았다."]], [["\"Not if you are the gentleman I think you.", "\"네가 내가 생각하는 그런 신사라면 그러지 않겠지."], ["I'm disappointed, but the girl can't help it; and the only thing left for you to do is to go away for a time.", "나도 실망스럽다만, 그 애도 어쩔 수 없는 일이야. 네가 할 수 있는 유일한 일은 한동안 멀리 떠나는 것뿐이다."], ["Where will you go?\"", "어디로 가겠느냐?\""]], [["\"Anywhere.", "\"아무 데나요."], ["I don't care what becomes of me;\"", "제가 어떻게 되든 상관없어요.\""], ["and Laurie got up, with a reckless laugh, that grated on his grandfather's ear.", "로리가 자리에서 일어나며, 할아버지의 귀에 거슬리는 자포자기한 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["\"Take it like a man, and don't do anything rash, for God's sake.", "\"사내답게 받아들이고, 제발 경솔한 짓일랑 하지 마라."], ["Why not go abroad, as you planned, and forget it?\"", "계획했던 대로 외국에 나가서 잊어버리는 게 어떻겠느냐?\" \"그럴 수 없어요.\""]], [["\"I can't.\"", "\"하지만 넌 그렇게 가고 싶어 안달하지 않았느냐."], ["\"But you've been wild to go, and I promised you should when you got through college.\"", "대학을 마치면 보내 주겠다고 내가 약속도 했고.\""]], [["\"Ah, but I didn't mean to go alone!\"", "\"아, 하지만 전 혼자 갈 생각은 아니었어요!\""], ["and Laurie walked fast through the room, with an expression which it was well his grandfather did not see.", "로리는 방 안을 빠르게 서성거렸다. 할아버지가 못 본 것이 다행인 표정을 지은 채."]], [["\"I don't ask you to go alone; there's some one ready and glad to go with you, anywhere in the world.\"", "\"혼자 가라는 게 아니다. 너와 함께 세상 어디든 기꺼이 갈 채비가 된 사람이 하나 있단다.\""]], [["\"Who, sir?\"", "\"그게 누군데요?\""], ["stopping to listen. \"Myself.\"", "그가 걸음을 멈추고 귀를 기울였다."]], [["Laurie came back as quickly as he went, and put out his hand, saying huskily,--", "\"바로 나다.\" 로리는 나갈 때처럼 잽싸게 돌아와 손을 내밀며 잠긴 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"I'm a selfish brute; but--you know--grandfather--\"", "\"전 이기적인 짐승이에요. 하지만 — 아시잖아요 — 할아버지 —\""]], [["\"Lord help me, yes, I do know, for I've been through it all before, once in my own young days, and then with your father.", "\"오, 알다마다. 안다, 나도 다 겪어 봤으니까."], ["Now, my dear boy, just sit quietly down, and hear my plan.", "한 번은 내 젊은 시절에, 또 한 번은 네 아버지 일로 말이다. 자, 얘야, 그저 가만히 앉아서 내 계획을 들어 보렴."], ["It's all settled, and can be carried out at once,\" said Mr.", "다 정해진 일이고, 당장 실행에 옮길 수 있단다.\""], ["Laurence, keeping hold of the young man, as if fearful that he would break away, as his father had done before him.", "로렌스 씨는 젊은이를 붙잡은 채로 말했다. 마치 예전에 그 아버지가 그랬듯 그가 뿌리치고 달아날까 두려워하는 듯이."]], [["\"Well, sir, what is it?\"", "\"그래서, 그게 뭔데요?\""], ["and Laurie sat down, without a sign of interest in face or voice.", "로리는 얼굴에도 목소리에도 아무 관심을 내비치지 않은 채 자리에 앉았다."]], [["\"There is business in London that needs looking after; I meant you should attend to it; but I can do it better myself, and things here will get on very well with Brooke to manage them.", "\"런던에 살펴봐야 할 사업이 있다. 원래는 네가 처리하기를 바랐다만, 내가 직접 하는 편이 낫겠고, 이곳 일은 브룩이 맡아 관리하면 아주 잘 굴러갈 거야."], ["My partners do almost everything; I'm merely holding on till you take my place, and can be off at any time.\"", "동업자들이 거의 다 해 주고 있으니, 나야 네가 내 자리를 물려받을 때까지 그저 붙들고 있는 것뿐이라 언제든 떠날 수 있어.\""]], [["\"But you hate travelling, sir; I can't ask it of you at your age,\"", "\"하지만 할아버지는 여행을 싫어하시잖아요. 연세도 있으신데 그걸 부탁드릴 순 없어요.\""], ["began Laurie, who was grateful for the sacrifice, but much preferred to go alone, if he went at all.", "로리가 입을 열었다. 그 희생이 고맙긴 했지만, 정작 가게 된다면 혼자 가는 편이 훨씬 좋았던 것이다."]], [["The old gentleman knew that perfectly well, and particularly desired to prevent it; for the mood in which he found his grandson assured him that it would not be wise to leave him to his own devices.", "노신사는 그 점을 완벽히 알았고, 특히 그것을 막고 싶어 했다. 손자가 처한 그 심정으로 보아, 제멋대로 하도록 내버려 두는 것은 현명하지 못하다는 확신이 들었기 때문이다."], ["So, stifling a natural regret at the thought of the home comforts he would leave behind him, he said stoutly,--", "그래서 그는 뒤에 두고 갈 가정의 안락을 떠올리며 절로 솟는 아쉬움을 억누르고, 씩씩하게 말했다."]], [["\"Bless your soul, I'm not superannuated yet.", "\"걱정 마라, 난 아직 노망난 늙은이가 아니다. 그 생각을 하니 꽤 즐거워지는구나."], ["I quite enjoy the idea; it will do me good, and my old bones won't suffer, for travelling nowadays is almost as easy as sitting in a chair.\"", "내게도 좋은 일이 될 거고, 이 늙은 뼈도 끄떡없을 게다. 요즘 여행이란 의자에 앉아 있는 것만큼이나 편하니까 말이다.\""]], [["A restless movement from Laurie suggested that _his_ chair was not easy, or that he did not like the plan, and made the old man add hastily,--", "로리가 안절부절못하는 몸짓을 하는 것을 보니 그의 의자가 편치 않거나, 아니면 그 계획이 마음에 들지 않는다는 뜻이었다. 그래서 노인은 황급히 덧붙였다."]], [["\"I don't mean to be a marplot or a burden; I go because I think you'd feel happier than if I was left behind.", "\"훼방꾼이나 짐이 될 생각은 없다. 내가 가는 건, 내가 뒤에 남는 것보다 네가 더 마음 편할 것 같아서야."], ["I don't intend to gad about with you, but leave you free to go where you like, while I amuse myself in my own way.", "너와 졸졸 붙어 다닐 생각은 없고, 네가 가고 싶은 곳을 마음껏 다니게 두마. 나는 내 식대로 즐길 테니."], ["I've friends in London and Paris, and should like to visit them; meantime you can go to Italy, Germany, Switzerland, where you will, and enjoy pictures, music, scenery, and adventures to your heart's content.\"", "런던과 파리에 친구들이 있으니 그들을 찾아보고 싶구나. 그동안 너는 이탈리아든 독일이든 스위스든 가고 싶은 데로 가서, 그림이며 음악이며 풍경이며 모험을 실컷 즐기렴.\""]], [["Now, Laurie felt just then that his heart was entirely broken, and the world a howling wilderness; but at the sound of certain words which the old gentleman artfully introduced into his closing sentence, the broken heart gave an unexpected leap, and a green oasis or two suddenly appeared in the howling wilderness.", "그때 로리는 자기 마음이 완전히 부서졌고 세상이 울부짖는 황야 같다고 느끼고 있었다. 그러나 노신사가 마지막 문장에 슬쩍 끼워 넣은 어떤 말들이 귀에 들어오자, 부서진 마음이 뜻밖에 한 번 펄쩍 뛰었고, 울부짖는 황야 속에 푸른 오아시스 한두 개가 불쑥 나타났다."], ["He sighed, and then said, in a spiritless tone,--", "그는 한숨을 쉬고는 맥없는 어조로 말했다."]], [["\"Just as you like, sir; it doesn't matter where I go or what I do.\"", "\"좋으실 대로 하세요, 할아버지. 제가 어디로 가든 무얼 하든 상관없으니까요.\""]], [["\"It does to me, remember that, my lad; I give you entire liberty, but I trust you to make an honest use of it.", "\"내게는 상관있단다, 그걸 명심해라, 얘야. 너한테 완전한 자유를 주마."], ["Promise me that, Laurie.\"", "다만 그 자유를 정직하게 쓰리라 믿는다. 그러겠다고 약속하렴, 로리.\""]], [["\"Anything you like, sir.\"", "\"뭐든 원하시는 대로요, 할아버지.\""]], [["\"Good,\"", "'됐다.'"], ["thought the old gentleman.", "노신사는 생각했다."], ["\"You don't care now, but there'll come a time when that promise will keep you out of mischief, or I'm much mistaken.\"", "'지금은 아무래도 좋다지만, 언젠가 그 약속이 너를 말썽에서 지켜 줄 때가 올 게다. 내 생각이 크게 틀리지 않았다면 말이야.'"]], [["Being an energetic individual, Mr.", "기력이 넘치는 사람답게 로렌스 씨는 쇠가 달았을 때 두드렸다."], ["Laurence struck while the iron was hot; and before the blighted being recovered spirit enough to rebel, they were off.", "그리하여 풀이 죽은 그 사람이 반항할 만큼 기운을 차리기도 전에 두 사람은 떠났다. 출발 준비에 필요한 기간 동안, 로리는 이런 경우 젊은 신사들이 으레 그러는 식으로 처신했다."], ["During the time necessary for preparation, Laurie bore himself as young gentlemen usually do in such cases.", "그는 침울했다가, 짜증을 냈다가, 수심에 잠겼다가 했다. 입맛을 잃고 옷차림을 소홀히 했으며, 피아노를 격정적으로 치는 데 많은 시간을 쏟았다."], ["He was moody, irritable, and pensive by turns; lost his appetite, neglected his dress, and devoted much time to playing tempestuously on his piano; avoided Jo, but consoled himself by staring at her from his window, with a tragical face that haunted her dreams by night, and oppressed her with a heavy sense of guilt by day.", "조를 피하면서도, 창문에서 그녀를 빤히 바라보는 것으로 스스로를 위로했다. 그 비극적인 얼굴은 밤이면 그녀의 꿈자리를 어지럽혔고, 낮이면 그녀를 무거운 죄책감으로 짓눌렀다."], ["Unlike some sufferers, he never spoke of his unrequited passion, and would allow no one, not even Mrs.", "어떤 고통받는 이들과 달리, 그는 보답받지 못한 사랑을 결코 입에 올리지 않았고, 마치 부인을 비롯한 그 누구도 위로하거나 동정을 건네지 못하게 했다."], ["March, to attempt consolation or offer sympathy.", "어떤 면에서 이것은 친구들에게 다행이었다."], ["On some accounts, this was a relief to his friends; but the weeks before his departure were very uncomfortable, and every one rejoiced that the \"poor, dear fellow was going away to forget his trouble, and come home happy.\"", "하지만 그가 떠나기 전 몇 주는 무척 불편했고, 모두가 \"그 가엾고 다정한 친구가 멀리 떠나서 괴로움을 잊고 행복하게 돌아오기를\" 바라며 안도했다."], ["Of course, he smiled darkly at their delusion, but passed it by, with the sad superiority of one who knew that his fidelity, like his love, was unalterable.", "물론 그는 그들의 그런 헛된 기대에 어두운 미소를 지었지만, 자기의 신의가 자기의 사랑처럼 변치 않으리라는 것을 아는 자의 슬픈 우월감으로 그것을 흘려보냈다."]], [["When the parting came he affected high spirits, to conceal certain inconvenient emotions which seemed inclined to assert themselves. This gayety did not impose upon anybody, but they tried to look as if it did, for his sake, and he got on very well till Mrs. March kissed him, with a whisper full of motherly solicitude; then, feeling that he was going very fast, he hastily embraced them all round, not forgetting the afflicted Hannah, and ran downstairs as if for his life. Jo followed a minute after to wave her hand to him if he looked round. He did look round, came back, put his arms about her, as she stood on the step above him, and looked up at her with a face that made his short appeal both eloquent and pathetic.", "이별의 순간이 오자, 그는 자꾸만 고개를 들려는 거추장스러운 감정들을 감추려고 한껏 들뜬 척했다. 그 명랑함에 속는 사람은 아무도 없었지만, 모두 그를 위해 속는 척했다. 그리하여 그는 마치 부인이 어머니다운 염려가 가득 담긴 속삭임과 함께 입을 맞출 때까지는 꽤 잘 버텼다. 그러나 자기가 무너지는 게 빨라지는 것을 느끼자, 그는 황급히 모두를 한 바퀴 끌어안았다. 슬픔에 잠긴 해나도 빠뜨리지 않고서. 그러고는 목숨이라도 걸린 듯 아래층으로 뛰어 내려갔다. 잠시 후 조가 뒤따라 나갔다. 그가 돌아본다면 손을 흔들어 주려고. 그는 정말로 돌아보았고, 되돌아와 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안았다. 그녀가 그보다 한 단 위 계단에 서 있는 채로. 그러고는 그녀를 올려다보았는데, 그 얼굴이 그의 짧은 호소를 웅변적이면서도 애처롭게 만들었다."]], [["\"O Jo, can't you?\"", "\"아, 조, 정말 안 되겠니?\""]], [["\"Teddy, dear, I wish I could!\"", "\"테디, 나도 그럴 수 있으면 좋겠어!\""]], [["That was all, except a little pause; then Laurie straightened himself up, said \"It's all right, never mind,\" and went away without another word. Ah, but it wasn't all right, and Jo _did_ mind; for while the curly head lay on her arm a minute after her hard answer, she felt as if she had stabbed her dearest friend; and when he left her without a look behind him, she knew that the boy Laurie never would come again.", "그게 다였다. 잠깐의 침묵 말고는. 이윽고 로리는 몸을 똑바로 펴고 \"괜찮아, 신경 쓰지 마\"라고 말한 뒤, 한마디도 더 없이 떠나갔다. 아, 하지만 괜찮지 않았고, 조도 정말로 마음이 쓰였다. 그 매정한 대답 직후 그 곱슬머리가 잠시 자기 팔에 기대어 있는 동안, 그녀는 자기가 가장 소중한 벗을 칼로 찌른 듯한 기분이었다. 그리고 그가 한 번도 뒤돌아보지 않고 그녀를 떠났을 때, 그녀는 소년 로리가 결코 다시는 돌아오지 않으리라는 것을 알았다. 36장"]], [["# BETH'S SECRET.", "# BETH'S SECRET."]], [["When Jo came home that spring, she had been struck with the change in Beth. No one spoke of it or seemed aware of it, for it had come too gradually to startle those who saw her daily; but to eyes sharpened by absence, it was very plain; and a heavy weight fell on Jo's heart as she saw her sister's face. It was no paler and but little thinner than in the autumn; yet there was a strange, transparent look about it, as if the mortal was being slowly refined away, and the immortal shining through the frail flesh with an indescribably pathetic beauty. Jo saw and felt it, but said nothing at the time, and soon the first impression lost much of its power; for Beth seemed happy, no one appeared to doubt that she was better; and, presently, in other cares, Jo for a time forgot her fear.", "베스의 비밀\n그해 봄 집에 돌아왔을 때 조는 베스의 변화에 충격을 받았다. 아무도 그것을 입에 올리지 않았고, 알아채는 것 같지도 않았다. 변화가 너무 서서히 찾아온 탓에 날마다 보는 사람들은 놀라지 않았던 것이다. 하지만 떨어져 지내면서 예민해진 눈에는 그것이 너무도 또렷했고, 동생의 얼굴을 본 조의 가슴에는 무거운 짐이 내려앉았다. 베스의 얼굴은 가을에 비해 더 창백하지도, 별로 더 야위지도 않았다. 그런데도 묘하게 투명한 기운이 어려 있었다. 마치 죽어야 할 부분이 천천히 정제되어 깎여 나가고, 죽지 않는 부분이 그 연약한 살을 뚫고 말로 표현할 수 없이 애잔한 아름다움으로 빛나는 듯했다. 조는 그것을 보았고 느꼈지만, 그때는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그리고 곧 그 첫인상은 힘을 많이 잃었다. 베스가 행복해 보였고, 아무도 그녀가 나아지고 있다는 것을 의심하지 않는 듯했으며, 이내 다른 근심거리들 속에서 조는 한동안 그 두려움을 잊었다."]], [["But when Laurie was gone, and peace prevailed again, the vague anxiety returned and haunted her.", "그러나 로리가 떠나고 다시 평온이 깃들자, 그 막연한 불안이 되돌아와 그녀를 떠나지 않았다. 그녀는 자기 죄를 고백하고 용서받았다."], ["She had confessed her sins and been forgiven; but when she showed her savings and proposed the mountain trip, Beth had thanked her heartily, but begged not to go so far away from home.", "하지만 모아 둔 돈을 내보이며 산으로 가자고 제안했을 때, 베스는 진심으로 고마워하면서도 집에서 그렇게 멀리 떠나지는 말자고 청했다. 바닷가로 한 번 더 짧게 다녀오는 편이 자기에게 더 맞으리라는 것이었다."], ["Another little visit to the seashore would suit her better, and, as grandma could not be prevailed upon to leave the babies, Jo took Beth down to the quiet place, where she could live much in the open air, and let the fresh sea-breezes blow a little color into her pale cheeks.", "할머니는 아기들을 떼어 놓고 떠나도록 설득할 수가 없었으므로, 조는 베스를 데리고 그 조용한 곳으로 내려갔다. 거기서라면 동생이 바깥 공기를 마음껏 쐬며 지내고, 상쾌한 바닷바람이 그 창백한 두 뺨에 약간의 혈색을 불어넣어 줄 수 있을 터였다."]], [["It was not a fashionable place, but, even among the pleasant people there, the girls made few friends, preferring to live for one another. Beth was too shy to enjoy society, and Jo too wrapped up in her to care for any one else; so they were all in all to each other, and came and went, quite unconscious of the interest they excited in those about them, who watched with sympathetic eyes the strong sister and the feeble one, always together, as if they felt instinctively that a long separation was not far away.", "세련된 곳은 아니었지만, 그곳의 유쾌한 사람들 틈에서도 두 자매는 친구를 거의 사귀지 않았다. 서로만을 위해 지내는 편을 좋아했던 것이다. 베스는 너무 수줍어서 사교를 즐기지 못했고, 조는 동생에게 너무 푹 빠져 있어 다른 누구에게도 마음을 쓸 수 없었다. 그리하여 둘은 서로에게 전부였고, 주위 사람들이 자기들에게 품는 관심도 전혀 의식하지 못한 채 오가곤 했다. 사람들은 동정 어린 눈으로 그 강한 언니와 연약한 동생을 지켜보았다. 늘 함께 붙어 있는 두 사람을, 마치 머지않아 긴 이별이 다가오리라는 것을 본능적으로 느끼기라도 한 듯이."]], [["They did feel it, yet neither spoke of it; for often between ourselves and those nearest and dearest to us there exists a reserve which it is very hard to overcome. Jo felt as if a veil had fallen between her heart and Beth's; but when she put out her hand to lift it up, there seemed something sacred in the silence, and she waited for Beth to speak. She wondered, and was thankful also, that her parents did not seem to see what she saw; and, during the quiet weeks, when the shadow grew so plain to her, she said nothing of it to those at home, believing that it would tell itself when Beth came back no better. She wondered still more if her sister really guessed the hard truth, and what thoughts were passing through her mind during the long hours when she lay on the warm rocks, with her head in Jo's lap, while the winds blew healthfully over her, and the sea made music at her feet.", "두 사람도 그것을 느끼고 있었다. 그러나 누구도 그 이야기를 입에 올리지 않았다. 우리 자신과, 우리에게 가장 가깝고 소중한 이들 사이에는 좀처럼 넘어서기 힘든 어떤 조심스러움이 자리하는 법이니까. 조는 마치 자기 마음과 베스의 마음 사이에 베일이 드리워진 것 같았다. 하지만 그것을 걷어 올리려고 손을 내밀면, 그 침묵 속에 무언가 신성한 것이 깃든 듯해서 베스가 먼저 입을 열기를 기다렸다. 그녀는 부모님이 자기가 보는 것을 보지 못하는 듯한 게 의아하면서도 감사했다. 그리고 그 그늘이 그토록 또렷이 보이던 그 고요한 몇 주 동안, 집에 있는 식구들에게는 그 일에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 베스가 나아지지 않은 채 돌아가면 저절로 드러나게 되리라 여겼던 것이다. 그녀는 더더욱 의아했다. 동생이 정말로 그 가혹한 진실을 짐작하고 있는지, 그리고 따뜻한 바위에 누워 조의 무릎에 머리를 얹은 채, 건강을 부르는 바람이 위로 불어오고 발치에서 바다가 음악을 연주하는 그 긴 시간 동안 동생의 마음속에는 어떤 생각들이 스쳐 가는지."]], [["One day Beth told her.", "어느 날 베스가 그것을 털어놓았다."], ["Jo thought she was asleep, she lay so still; and, putting down her book, sat looking at her with wistful eyes, trying to see signs of hope in the faint color on Beth's cheeks.", "동생이 어찌나 미동도 없이 누워 있던지 조는 잠든 줄로만 알았다. 그래서 책을 내려놓고 동생을 애틋한 눈길로 바라보며, 베스의 두 뺨에 어린 희미한 혈색에서 희망의 징표를 찾아보려 했다."], ["But she could not find enough to satisfy her, for the cheeks were very thin, and the hands seemed too feeble to hold even the rosy little shells they had been gathering.", "하지만 그녀를 안심시킬 만큼은 찾을 수 없었다. 두 뺨은 너무 야위었고, 두 손은 그동안 주워 모으던 그 발그레한 작은 조개껍데기조차 쥐기 버거워 보일 만큼 가냘팠으니까."], ["It came to her then more bitterly than ever that Beth was slowly drifting away from her, and her arms instinctively tightened their hold upon the dearest treasure she possessed.", "그 순간, 베스가 자기에게서 천천히 멀어져 가고 있다는 사실이 그 어느 때보다도 쓰라리게 다가왔고, 그녀의 두 팔은 본능적으로 동생을 더 꼭 끌어안았다. 그녀가 지닌 가장 소중한 보물을 붙잡고 있던 손길이었다."], ["For a minute her eyes were too dim for seeing, and, when they cleared, Beth was looking up at her so tenderly that there was hardly any need for her to say,--", "잠시 두 눈이 흐려져 아무것도 보이지 않았고, 다시 맑아졌을 때 베스가 너무도 다정하게 그녀를 올려다보고 있어, 굳이 말을 보탤 필요도 없을 정도였다."]], [["\"Jo, dear, I'm glad you know it.", "\"조 언니, 언니가 알아서 다행이야."], ["I've tried to tell you, but I couldn't.\"", "말하려고 애써 봤는데, 도무지 못 하겠더라.\""]], [["There was no answer except her sister's cheek against her own, not even tears; for when most deeply moved, Jo did not cry.", "대답이라곤 자기 뺨에 와 닿은 동생의 뺨뿐, 눈물조차 없었다. 가장 깊이 마음이 흔들릴 때면 조는 울지 않았다."], ["She was the weaker, then, and Beth tried to comfort and sustain her, with her arms about her, and the soothing words she whispered in her ear.", "그럴 때 더 약한 쪽은 조였다. 베스가 두 팔로 언니를 감싸 안은 채 위로하고 다독이며 귓가에 부드러운 말을 속삭였다."]], [["\"I've known it for a good while, dear, and, now I'm used to it, it isn't hard to think of or to bear. Try to see it so, and don't be troubled about me, because it's best; indeed it is.\"", "\"꽤 오래전부터 알고 있었어, 언니. 이제 익숙해져서, 생각하거나 견디는 게 그렇게 힘들지 않아. 언니도 그렇게 받아들이려고 해 봐. 그리고 내 걱정은 하지 마. 이게 제일 좋은 거니까. 정말 그래.\""]], [["\"Is this what made you so unhappy in the autumn, Beth?", "\"그래서 가을에 그렇게 마음이 무거웠던 거니, 베스?"], ["You did not feel it then, and keep it to yourself so long, did you?\"", "그때 이미 느꼈으면서, 그렇게 오래 혼자만 묻어 두고 있었던 거야?\" 조가 물었다."], ["asked Jo, refusing to see or say that it _was_ best, but glad to know that Laurie had no part in Beth's trouble.", "그게 제일 좋은 일이라는 걸 보지도, 입에 담지도 않으려 했지만, 베스의 괴로움에 로리가 아무런 관련이 없다는 걸 알게 되어 마음이 놓였다."]], [["\"Yes, I gave up hoping then, but I didn't like to own it.", "\"응, 그때 희망을 접었어. 그래도 인정하기는 싫었거든."], ["I tried to think it was a sick fancy, and would not let it trouble any one.", "그냥 아파서 떠오른 헛생각일 거라고 여기려 했고, 누구한테도 짐이 되게 하고 싶지 않았어."], ["But when I saw you all so well and strong, and full of happy plans, it was hard to feel that I could never be like you, and then I was miserable, Jo.\"", "그런데 다들 그렇게 건강하고 기운 넘치고, 행복한 계획으로 가득 찬 모습을 보니까, 나는 절대 언니들처럼 될 수 없다는 게 너무 힘들더라. 그래서 비참했어, 언니.\""]], [["\"O Beth, and you didn't tell me, didn't let me comfort and help you!", "\"아, 베스, 그런데도 나한테 말 안 하고, 내가 위로하고 도와주지도 못하게 했단 말이야!"], ["How could you shut me out, and bear it all alone?\"", "어떻게 나를 밀어내고 그걸 혼자서 다 견딜 수 있었니?\""]], [["Jo's voice was full of tender reproach, and her heart ached to think of the solitary struggle that must have gone on while Beth learned to say good-by to health, love, and life, and take up her cross so cheerfully.", "조의 목소리에는 애틋한 책망이 가득했다. 베스가 건강과 사랑과 삶에게 작별을 고하는 법을 익히고, 그토록 밝게 자기 십자가를 짊어지는 동안 홀로 치렀을 그 싸움을 생각하니 가슴이 아렸다."]], [["\"Perhaps it was wrong, but I tried to do right; I wasn't sure, no one said anything, and I hoped I was mistaken. It would have been selfish to frighten you all when Marmee was so anxious about Meg, and Amy away, and you so happy with Laurie,--at least, I thought so then.\"", "\"잘못한 건지도 몰라. 그래도 옳게 하려고 애썼어. 확신도 없었고, 아무도 무슨 말을 해 주지 않았고, 내가 잘못 안 거였으면 좋겠다 싶었거든. 엄마는 메그 언니 일로 그렇게 마음 졸이고, 에이미는 멀리 가 있고, 언니는 로리랑 그렇게 행복한데, 다들 겁먹게 하는 건 이기적인 일이잖아. 적어도 그때는 그렇게 생각했어.\""]], [["\"And I thought that you loved him, Beth, and I went away because I couldn't,\"", "\"난 또 네가 그 애를 좋아하는 줄 알았어, 베스. 그래서 난 그럴 수가 없어서 떠나 버린 거고.\""], ["cried Jo, glad to say all the truth.", "조가 외쳤다. 마침내 진실을 다 털어놓을 수 있어 후련했다."]], [["Beth looked so amazed at the idea that Jo smiled in spite of her pain, and added softly,--", "베스가 그 말에 어찌나 놀란 표정을 짓던지, 조는 가슴 아픈 와중에도 웃음이 나서 부드럽게 덧붙였다."]], [["\"Then you didn't, deary?", "\"그럼 넌 아니었던 거니, 얘?"], ["I was afraid it was so, and imagined your poor little heart full of love-lornity all that while.\"", "난 그런 줄 알고 걱정했단다. 네 가엾고 작은 가슴이 그동안 내내 짝사랑으로 가득 차 있는 줄 알고 말이야.\""]], [["\"Why, Jo, how could I, when he was so fond of you?\"", "\"어머, 언니, 그 사람이 언니를 그렇게 좋아하는데 내가 어떻게 그래?\""], ["asked Beth, as innocently as a child.", "베스가 어린아이처럼 천진하게 물었다."], ["\"I do love him dearly; he is so good to me, how can I help it?", "\"난 그 사람을 정말 좋아해. 나한테 얼마나 잘해 주는데, 어떻게 안 좋아할 수가 있겠어?"], ["But he never could be anything to me but my brother.", "그래도 그 사람은 나한테 오빠 말고는 아무것도 될 수 없어."], ["I hope he truly will be, sometime.\"", "언젠가 정말 오빠가 되면 좋겠다.\""]], [["\"Not through me,\"", "\"나를 통해서는 아니야.\""], ["said Jo decidedly.", "조가 단호하게 말했다."], ["\"Amy is left for him, and they would suit excellently; but I have no heart for such things, now.", "\"그 애한테는 에이미가 남아 있잖아. 둘이 아주 잘 어울릴 거야."], ["I don't care what becomes of anybody but you, Beth.", "하지만 난 지금 그런 일에 마음 쓸 여유가 없어. 너 말고는 누가 어떻게 되든 신경 안 써, 베스."], ["You _must_ get well.\"", "넌 꼭 나아야 해.\""]], [["\"I want to, oh, so much!", "\"나도 그러고 싶어, 아, 얼마나 그러고 싶은데!"], ["I try, but every day I lose a little, and feel more sure that I shall never gain it back.", "애써 봐. 그런데 날마다 조금씩 잃어 가고, 다시는 되찾지 못하리란 게 점점 더 확실해져."], ["It's like the tide, Jo, when it turns, it goes slowly, but it can't be stopped.\"", "꼭 밀물 같아, 언니. 한번 방향이 바뀌면 천천히 가긴 해도, 멈출 수는 없는 거잖아.\""]], [["\"It _shall_ be stopped, your tide must not turn so soon, nineteen is too young.", "\"멈추게 할 거야. 네 물때는 그렇게 일찍 바뀌면 안 돼."], ["Beth, I can't let you go.", "열아홉은 너무 어려. 베스, 난 널 보낼 수 없어."], ["I'll work and pray and fight against it.", "일하고 기도하고, 그것과 맞서 싸울 거야."], ["I'll keep you in spite of everything; there must be ways, it can't be too late.", "무슨 일이 있어도 널 붙잡아 둘 거야. 분명 방법이 있을 거야."], ["God won't be so cruel as to take you from me,\"", "너무 늦었을 리 없어. 하느님이 너를 내게서 데려가실 만큼 잔인하실 리 없어.\""], ["cried poor Jo rebelliously, for her spirit was far less piously submissive than Beth's.", "가엾은 조가 반항하듯 외쳤다. 베스만큼 경건하게 순응하는 영혼과는 거리가 멀었기 때문이다."]], [["Simple, sincere people seldom speak much of their piety; it shows itself in acts, rather than in words, and has more influence than homilies or protestations.", "소박하고 진실한 사람들은 자신의 신앙을 좀처럼 입에 올리지 않는다. 신앙은 말보다 행동으로 드러나며, 설교나 다짐보다 더 큰 힘을 지닌다."], ["Beth could not reason upon or explain the faith that gave her courage and patience to give up life, and cheerfully wait for death.", "베스는 삶을 내려놓고 기꺼이 죽음을 기다릴 용기와 인내를 주는 그 믿음을 따져 묻거나 설명할 수 없었다."], ["Like a confiding child, she asked no questions, but left everything to God and nature, Father and mother of us all, feeling sure that they, and they only, could teach and strengthen heart and spirit for this life and the life to come.", "그저 모든 것을 믿고 의지하는 아이처럼, 아무것도 묻지 않고, 우리 모두의 아버지요 어머니인 하느님과 자연에 모든 것을 맡겼다. 그분들만이, 오직 그분들만이 이 삶과 다가올 삶을 위해 마음과 영혼을 가르치고 굳세게 해 주실 수 있다고 믿어 의심치 않았다."], ["She did not rebuke Jo with saintly speeches, only loved her better for her passionate affection, and clung more closely to the dear human love, from which our Father never means us to be weaned, but through which He draws us closer to Himself.", "베스는 성인 같은 말로 조를 나무라지 않았다. 다만 그 격렬한 애정 때문에 언니를 더욱 사랑했고, 우리 아버지께서 결코 우리를 떼어 놓으려 하지 않으시며 오히려 그것을 통해 우리를 당신 곁으로 더 가까이 이끄시는, 그 소중한 인간의 사랑에 더욱 매달렸다."], ["She could not say, \"I'm glad to go,\"", "\"기꺼이 가겠어\"라고는 말할 수 없었다."], ["for life was very sweet to her; she could only sob out, \"I try to be willing,\"", "베스에게 삶은 너무도 달콤했으니까. 그저 \"기꺼이 받아들이려고 애써\"라고 흐느낄 뿐이었다."], ["while she held fast to Jo, as the first bitter wave of this great sorrow broke over them together.", "그 커다란 슬픔의 첫 쓰디쓴 물결이 두 사람 위로 함께 부서져 내리는 동안, 베스는 조를 꼭 붙들었다."]], [["By and by Beth said, with recovered serenity,--", "이윽고 베스가 다시 평온을 되찾은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"You'll tell them this when we go home?\"", "\"우리 집에 돌아가면 언니가 식구들한테 이 얘기를 해 줄 거지?\""]], [["\"I think they will see it without words,\"", "\"말하지 않아도 다들 알 것 같은데.\""], ["sighed Jo; for now it seemed to her that Beth changed every day.", "조가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. 이제는 베스가 하루가 다르게 변해 가는 것만 같았다."]], [["\"Perhaps not; I've heard that the people who love best are often blindest to such things.", "\"아마 모를 거야. 가장 사랑하는 사람들이 이런 일에는 가장 눈멀어 있는 경우가 많다고 들었거든."], ["If they don't see it, you will tell them for me.", "식구들이 알아채지 못하면, 언니가 날 대신해서 말해 줘."], ["I don't want any secrets, and it's kinder to prepare them.", "난 비밀을 두고 싶지 않고, 미리 마음의 준비를 시켜 드리는 게 더 다정한 거잖아."], ["Meg has John and the babies to comfort her, but you must stand by father and mother, won't you, Jo?\"", "메그 언니한테는 존 형부랑 아기들이 위로가 되어 주겠지만, 언니는 아빠 엄마 곁을 지켜 줘야 해. 그렇게 해 줄 거지, 언니?\""]], [["\"If I can; but, Beth, I don't give up yet; I'm going to believe that it _is_ a sick fancy, and not let you think it's true,\"", "\"할 수 있다면 그럴게. 그래도, 베스, 난 아직 포기 안 해."], ["said Jo, trying to speak cheerfully.", "이게 아파서 떠오른 헛생각이라고 믿을 거고, 네가 그걸 사실이라고 여기게 두지 않을 거야.\" 조가 애써 밝게 말하려 했다."]], [["Beth lay a minute thinking, and then said in her quiet way,--", "베스는 잠시 누운 채 생각에 잠겼다가, 특유의 차분한 말투로 입을 열었다."]], [["\"I don't know how to express myself, and shouldn't try, to any one but you, because I can't speak out, except to my Jo.", "\"어떻게 말해야 할지 모르겠어. 언니가 아닌 다른 사람한테는 아예 해 보려고도 안 할 거야."], ["I only mean to say that I have a feeling that it never was intended I should live long.", "우리 조 언니가 아니면 속을 털어놓을 수가 없거든. 그냥 내가 하고 싶은 말은, 난 애초에 오래 살게 되어 있던 게 아니라는 느낌이 든다는 거야."], ["I'm not like the rest of you; I never made any plans about what I'd do when I grew up; I never thought of being married, as you all did.", "난 다른 식구들이랑은 달라. 자라서 뭘 할지 같은 계획을 세워 본 적도 없고, 언니들처럼 결혼을 생각해 본 적도 없어."], ["I couldn't seem to imagine myself anything but stupid little Beth, trotting about at home, of no use anywhere but there.", "난 도무지 나 자신을, 집 안에서 종종거리며 돌아다니고 거기 말고는 어디서도 쓸모없는 멍청하고 작은 베스 말고 다른 무엇으로 상상할 수가 없었어."], ["I never wanted to go away, and the hard part now is the leaving you all.", "어디로도 떠나고 싶었던 적이 없어. 지금 힘든 건 식구들을 두고 떠나는 거야."], ["I'm not afraid, but it seems as if I should be homesick for you even in heaven.\"", "무섭진 않은데, 천국에서도 식구들이 그리워서 향수병에 걸릴 것만 같아.\""]], [["Jo could not speak; and for several minutes there was no sound but the sigh of the wind and the lapping of the tide.", "조는 말을 잇지 못했다. 몇 분 동안 바람의 한숨 소리와 밀물이 찰싹거리는 소리 말고는 아무 소리도 없었다."], ["A white-winged gull flew by, with the flash of sunshine on its silvery breast; Beth watched it till it vanished, and her eyes were full of sadness.", "하얀 날개의 갈매기 한 마리가 은빛 가슴에 햇살을 반짝이며 날아갔다."], ["A little gray-coated sand-bird came tripping over the beach, \"peeping\"", "베스는 그것이 사라질 때까지 지켜보았고, 두 눈에는 슬픔이 가득했다."], ["softly to itself, as if enjoying the sun and sea; it came quite close to Beth, looked at her with a friendly eye, and sat upon a warm stone, dressing its wet feathers, quite at home.", "회색 옷을 입은 작은 물떼새 한 마리가 해변을 종종걸음으로 가로지르며, 햇살과 바다를 즐기듯 혼자서 가만가만 \"삐악거렸다.\" 새는 베스에게 아주 가까이 다가와 다정한 눈으로 그녀를 바라보더니, 따뜻한 돌 위에 앉아 젖은 깃털을 다듬으며 제집처럼 편안히 굴었다."], ["Beth smiled, and felt comforted, for the tiny thing seemed to offer its small friendship, and remind her that a pleasant world was still to be enjoyed.", "베스는 미소 지으며 위로를 받았다. 그 자그마한 것이 작은 우정을 건네고, 아직 즐길 만한 즐거운 세상이 남아 있음을 일깨워 주는 것 같았다."]], [["\"Dear little bird!", "\"귀여운 작은 새!"], ["See, Jo, how tame it is.", "언니, 봐, 얼마나 길이 잘 들었는지. 난 갈매기보다 물떼새가 더 좋아."], ["I like peeps better than the gulls: they are not so wild and handsome, but they seem happy, confiding little things.", "갈매기처럼 거칠고 멋지진 않지만, 행복하고 다정한 작은 것들 같거든. 작년 여름에 난 얘네를 내 새들이라고 불렀어."], ["I used to call them my birds, last summer; and mother said they reminded her of me,--busy, quaker-colored creatures, always near the shore, and always chirping that contented little song of theirs.", "엄마는 얘네를 보면 내가 생각난다고 했지. 부지런하고 퀘이커교도처럼 수수한 빛깔에, 늘 물가 가까이 머물면서, 늘 자기들만의 만족스러운 작은 노래를 지저귀는 것들이라고."], ["You are the gull, Jo, strong and wild, fond of the storm and the wind, flying far out to sea, and happy all alone.", "언니는 갈매기야, 강하고 거칠고, 폭풍과 바람을 좋아하고, 멀리 바다로 날아가서 혼자서도 행복한."], ["Meg is the turtle-dove, and Amy is like the lark she writes about, trying to get up among the clouds, but always dropping down into its nest again.", "메그 언니는 멧비둘기고, 에이미는 자기가 쓴 시 속의 종달새 같아. 구름 사이로 솟아오르려 애쓰지만, 늘 다시 둥지로 내려앉는."], ["Dear little girl!", "사랑스러운 애!"], ["she's so ambitious, but her heart is good and tender; and no matter how high she flies, she never will forget home.", "야망이 정말 크지만, 마음은 곱고 다정하잖아. 아무리 높이 날아도 집은 절대 잊지 않을 거야."], ["I hope I shall see her again, but she seems _so_ far away.\"", "그 애를 다시 볼 수 있으면 좋겠는데, 너무나 멀리 있는 것 같아.\""]], [["\"She is coming in the spring, and I mean that you shall be all ready to see and enjoy her.", "\"봄에 올 거야. 그때 네가 그 애를 맞이하고 함께 즐길 수 있게 다 준비시켜 둘 거야."], ["I'm going to have you well and rosy by that time,\"", "그때까지 널 건강하고 발그레하게 만들어 놓을 거야.\" 조가 입을 뗐다."], ["began Jo, feeling that of all the changes in Beth, the talking change was the greatest, for it seemed to cost no effort now, and she thought aloud in a way quite unlike bashful Beth.", "베스의 여러 변화 중에서도 말이 많아진 변화가 가장 크다는 생각이 들었다. 이제는 아무런 힘도 들이지 않는 듯했고, 수줍던 예전의 베스답지 않게 생각을 소리 내어 말했다."]], [["\"Jo, dear, don't hope any more; it won't do any good, I'm sure of that.", "\"조 언니, 더는 희망 갖지 마. 아무 소용없을 거야."], ["We won't be miserable, but enjoy being together while we wait.", "난 그걸 확실히 알아. 우리 비참해하지는 말고, 기다리는 동안 함께 있는 걸 즐기자."], ["We'll have happy times, for I don't suffer much, and I think the tide will go out easily, if you help me.\"", "행복한 시간을 보내자. 난 별로 고통스럽지 않고, 언니가 도와주면 물때가 쉽게 빠져나갈 것 같거든.\""]], [["Jo leaned down to kiss the tranquil face; and with that silent kiss, she dedicated herself soul and body to Beth.", "조는 몸을 숙여 그 평온한 얼굴에 입을 맞추었다. 그 말 없는 입맞춤으로, 조는 영혼과 몸을 다 바쳐 베스에게 헌신하기로 했다."]], [["She was right: there was no need of any words when they got home, for father and mother saw plainly, now, what they had prayed to be saved from seeing. Tired with her short journey, Beth went at once to bed, saying how glad she was to be at home; and when Jo went down, she found that she would be spared the hard task of telling Beth's secret. Her father stood leaning his head on the mantel-piece, and did not turn as she came in; but her mother stretched out her arms as if for help, and Jo went to comfort her without a word.", "베스의 말이 옳았다. 집에 돌아왔을 때 굳이 말이 필요 없었다. 아버지와 어머니는 이제, 보지 않게 해 달라고 기도해 온 그것을 또렷이 알아보았다. 짧은 여정에도 지친 베스는 집에 와서 얼마나 기쁜지 모르겠다며 곧장 잠자리에 들었다. 조가 아래층으로 내려갔을 때, 베스의 비밀을 털어놓아야 하는 힘든 일을 덜게 되었음을 알았다. 아버지는 벽난로 선반에 머리를 기댄 채 서 있었고, 그녀가 들어와도 돌아보지 않았다. 다만 어머니가 도움을 청하듯 두 팔을 벌렸고, 조는 말없이 다가가 어머니를 위로했다. 새로운 인상"]], [["# NEW IMPRESSIONS.", "# NEW IMPRESSIONS."]], [["At three o'clock in the afternoon, all the fashionable world at Nice may be seen on the Promenade des Anglais,--a charming place; for the wide walk, bordered with palms, flowers, and tropical shrubs, is bounded on one side by the sea, on the other by the grand drive, lined with hotels and villas, while beyond lie orange-orchards and the hills.", "오후 세 시면 니스의 멋쟁이들이 모두 프로므나드 데 장글레로 나온다. 매혹적인 곳이다."], ["Many nations are represented, many languages spoken, many costumes worn; and, on a sunny day, the spectacle is as gay and brilliant as a carnival.", "야자수와 꽃, 열대 관목이 늘어선 넓은 산책로는 한쪽으로는 바다와 맞닿아 있고, 다른 쪽으로는 호텔과 별장이 즐비한 대로변과 이어지며, 그 너머로는 오렌지 과수원과 언덕이 펼쳐져 있다. 온갖 나라 사람들이 모여 온갖 언어를 쓰고 온갖 옷차림을 하고 있어, 화창한 날이면 그 광경이 카니발만큼이나 흥겹고 화려하다."], ["Haughty English, lively French, sober Germans, handsome Spaniards, ugly Russians, meek Jews, free-and-easy Americans, all drive, sit, or saunter here, chatting over the news, and criticising the latest celebrity who has arrived,--Ristori or Dickens, Victor Emmanuel or the Queen of the Sandwich Islands.", "거만한 영국인, 활기찬 프랑스인, 진중한 독일인, 잘생긴 스페인인, 못생긴 러시아인, 온순한 유대인, 격식 없는 미국인이 모두 마차를 몰거나 앉아 있거나 거닐면서, 새 소식을 이야기하고, 최근 도착한 유명 인사를 입에 올린다. 리스토리든 디킨스든, 빅토르 에마누엘이든 샌드위치 제도의 여왕이든 말이다."], ["The equipages are as varied as the company, and attract as much attention, especially the low basket-barouches in which ladies drive themselves, with a pair of dashing ponies, gay nets to keep their voluminous flounces from overflowing the diminutive vehicles, and little grooms on the perch behind.", "마차도 사람들만큼이나 가지각색이라 못지않게 눈길을 끄는데, 특히 숙녀들이 직접 모는 낮은 바구니 모양의 사륜마차가 그렇다. 멋진 조랑말 한 쌍에, 풍성한 주름 장식이 자그마한 마차 밖으로 흘러넘치지 않도록 화려한 망을 두르고, 뒤편 발판에는 어린 마부들이 올라타 있다."]], [["Along this walk, on Christmas Day, a tall young man walked slowly, with his hands behind him, and a somewhat absent expression of countenance.", "크리스마스 날, 이 산책로를 따라 키 큰 청년 하나가 뒷짐을 진 채, 어딘가 멍한 표정으로 천천히 걷고 있었다."], ["He looked like an Italian, was dressed like an Englishman, and had the independent air of an American,--a combination which caused sundry pairs of feminine eyes to look approvingly after him, and sundry dandies in black velvet suits, with rose-colored neckties, buff gloves, and orange-flowers in their button-holes, to shrug their shoulders, and then envy him his inches.", "그는 이탈리아 사람처럼 보였고, 영국 사람처럼 차려입었으며, 미국 사람 특유의 거리낌 없는 분위기를 풍겼다. 그 조합 덕분에 여러 여인의 눈길이 흐뭇하게 그의 뒤를 좇았고, 검은 벨벳 정장에 장밋빛 넥타이를 매고 황갈색 장갑을 끼고 단춧구멍에 오렌지 꽃을 꽂은 멋쟁이들은 어깨를 으쓱하고는 그의 큰 키를 부러워했다."], ["There were plenty of pretty faces to admire, but the young man took little notice of them, except to glance, now and then, at some blonde girl, or lady in blue.", "감탄할 만한 예쁜 얼굴이 많았지만, 청년은 그들에게 별 관심을 두지 않았다. 그저 이따금 금발 아가씨나 파란 옷을 입은 숙녀에게 눈길을 던질 뿐이었다."], ["Presently he strolled out of the promenade, and stood a moment at the crossing, as if undecided whether to go and listen to the band in the Jardin Publique, or to wander along the beach toward Castle Hill.", "이윽고 그는 산책로를 벗어나 횡단로 어귀에서 잠시 멈춰 섰다. 자르댕 퓌블리크에서 악단의 연주를 들으러 갈지, 아니면 해변을 따라 캐슬 힐 쪽으로 거닐지 마음을 정하지 못한 듯했다."], ["The quick trot of ponies'", "조랑말의 빠른 발굽 소리에 그가 고개를 들었다."], ["feet made him look up, as one of the little carriages, containing a single lady, came rapidly down the street.", "숙녀 한 명만 탄 작은 마차 한 대가 거리를 빠르게 내려오고 있었다."], ["The lady was young, blonde, and dressed in blue.", "그 숙녀는 젊고 금발이었으며, 파란 옷을 입고 있었다."], ["He stared a minute, then his whole face woke up, and, waving his hat like a boy, he hurried forward to meet her.", "그는 잠시 멍하니 바라보다가 얼굴이 온통 환해지더니, 소년처럼 모자를 흔들며 서둘러 그녀를 맞으러 다가갔다."]], [["\"O Laurie, is it really you?", "\"어머, 로리, 정말 너야?"], ["I thought you'd never come!\"", "영영 안 올 줄 알았는데!\""], ["cried Amy, dropping the reins, and holding out both hands, to the great scandalization of a French mamma, who hastened her daughter's steps, lest she should be demoralized by beholding the free manners of these \"mad English.\"", "에이미가 고삐를 떨구고 두 손을 내밀며 소리쳤다. 그 바람에 곁에 있던 프랑스 부인이 크게 놀라, 이 \"정신 나간 영국 사람들\"의 격식 없는 태도를 보고 딸이 물들까 봐 걸음을 재촉했다."]], [["\"I was detained by the way, but I promised to spend Christmas with you, and here I am.\"", "\"오는 길에 일이 좀 있어서 늦었어. 그래도 너랑 크리스마스를 보내겠다고 약속했잖아. 그래서 이렇게 왔지.\""]], [["\"How is your grandfather?", "\"할아버지는 어떠셔?"], ["When did you come?", "언제 왔어?"], ["Where are you staying?\"", "어디 묵고 있어?\""]], [["\"Very well--last night--at the Chauvain.", "\"아주 잘 지내셔. 어젯밤에 왔고, 쇼뱅 호텔에 묵고 있어."], ["I called at your hotel, but you were all out.\"", "너희 호텔에 들렀는데, 다들 나가고 없더라.\""]], [["\"I have so much to say, I don't know where to begin!", "\"할 말이 너무 많아서 어디서부터 시작해야 할지 모르겠어! 어서 타."], ["Get in, and we can talk at our ease; I was going for a drive, and longing for company.", "편하게 얘기하자. 마차 산책을 나가려던 참이었는데, 마침 같이 갈 사람이 있었으면 했거든."], ["Flo's saving up for to-night.\"", "플로는 오늘 밤을 위해 기운을 아끼는 중이야.\""]], [["\"What happens then, a ball?\"", "\"오늘 밤에 무슨 일 있어? 무도회야?\""]], [["\"A Christmas party at our hotel.", "\"우리 호텔에서 크리스마스 파티를 열어."], ["There are many Americans there, and they give it in honor of the day.", "미국 사람들이 많이 묵고 있는데, 이날을 기념해서 여는 거야."], ["You'll go with us, of course?", "너도 우리랑 같이 갈 거지?"], ["Aunt will be charmed.\"", "이모님이 무척 좋아하실 거야.\""]], [["\"Thank you.", "\"고마워."], ["Where now?\"", "그럼 지금은 어디로 가?\""], ["asked Laurie, leaning back and folding his arms, a proceeding which suited Amy, who preferred to drive; for her parasol-whip and blue reins over the white ponies'", "로리가 등을 기대고 팔짱을 끼며 물었다. 직접 모는 걸 더 좋아하는 에이미에게는 잘된 일이었다."], ["backs, afforded her infinite satisfaction.", "양산 채찍과 흰 조랑말 등 위로 늘어뜨린 파란 고삐가 그녀에게 더없는 만족을 안겨 주었으니까."]], [["\"I'm going to the banker's first, for letters, and then to Castle Hill; the view is so lovely, and I like to feed the peacocks.", "\"먼저 은행에 가서 편지를 받고, 그다음에 캐슬 힐로 갈 거야. 경치가 정말 사랑스럽고, 난 공작새한테 먹이 주는 게 좋거든."], ["Have you ever been there?\"", "거기 가 본 적 있어?\""]], [["\"Often, years ago; but I don't mind having a look at it.\"", "\"여러 번, 몇 년 전에. 그래도 한 번 더 둘러보는 것도 괜찮지.\""]], [["\"Now tell me all about yourself.", "\"이제 네 얘기 좀 다 해 봐."], ["The last I heard of you, your grandfather wrote that he expected you from Berlin.\"", "네 소식을 마지막으로 들은 게, 할아버지가 네가 베를린에서 올 거라고 편지에 쓰셨을 때야.\""]], [["\"Yes, I spent a month there, and then joined him in Paris, where he has settled for the winter.", "\"응, 거기서 한 달 지내고, 파리에서 할아버지랑 합류했어. 겨울 동안 파리에 자리 잡으셨거든."], ["He has friends there, and finds plenty to amuse him; so I go and come, and we get on capitally.\"", "거기 친구분들이 계셔서 즐길 거리가 많으셔. 그래서 난 오가고, 우리 둘 다 잘 지내.\""]], [["\"That's a sociable arrangement,\"", "\"사이좋게 지내시는구나.\" 에이미가 말했다."], ["said Amy, missing something in Laurie's manner, though she couldn't tell what.", "로리의 태도에서 뭔가 빠진 듯한 느낌이 들었지만, 그게 뭔지는 알 수 없었다."]], [["\"Why, you see he hates to travel, and I hate to keep still; so we each suit ourselves, and there is no trouble.", "\"그게, 할아버지는 여행을 질색하시고, 난 가만있는 걸 질색하잖아. 그러니까 각자 좋을 대로 하면 아무 문제가 없어."], ["I am often with him, and he enjoys my adventures, while I like to feel that some one is glad to see me when I get back from my wanderings.", "자주 곁에 있어 드리고, 할아버지는 내 모험담을 즐기시지. 나도 떠돌다 돌아오면 누군가 날 반겨 준다는 게 좋고."], ["Dirty old hole, isn't it?\"", "지저분하고 낡은 동네지?\""], ["he added, with a look of disgust, as they drove along the boulevard to the Place Napoleon, in the old city.", "그가 옛 시가지의 나폴레옹 광장으로 가는 큰길을 달리며, 질색하는 표정으로 덧붙였다."]], [["\"The dirt is picturesque, so I don't mind.", "\"지저분한 게 그림 같아서 난 괜찮아."], ["The river and the hills are delicious, and these glimpses of the narrow cross-streets are my delight.", "강이랑 언덕은 정말 멋지고, 좁은 골목길이 언뜻언뜻 보이는 게 난 너무 좋거든. 자, 이제 저 행렬이 지나갈 때까지 기다려야겠다."], ["Now we shall have to wait for that procession to pass; it's going to the Church of St. John.\"", "생장 교회로 가는 행렬이야.\""]], [["While Laurie listlessly watched the procession of priests under their canopies, white-veiled nuns bearing lighted tapers, and some brotherhood in blue, chanting as they walked, Amy watched him, and felt a new sort of shyness steal over her; for he was changed, and she could not find the merry-faced boy she left in the moody-looking man beside her. He was handsomer than ever, and greatly improved, she thought; but now that the flush of pleasure at meeting her was over, he looked tired and spiritless,--not sick, nor exactly unhappy, but older and graver than a year or two of prosperous life should have made him. She couldn't understand it, and did not venture to ask questions; so she shook her head, and touched up her ponies, as the procession wound away across the arches of the Paglioni bridge, and vanished in the church.", "로리가 닫집 아래의 사제들과 불 켠 가는 초를 든 흰 너울의 수녀들, 그리고 걸으면서 성가를 부르는 파란 옷의 어느 수도회 사람들을 무심히 바라보는 동안, 에이미는 로리를 바라보았다. 그러자 새로운 종류의 수줍음이 그녀에게 스며들었다. 그가 변했기 때문이다. 자기가 두고 떠났던 명랑한 얼굴의 소년을, 곁에 있는 침울해 보이는 남자에게서 찾을 수가 없었다. 에이미가 보기에 그는 그 어느 때보다 잘생겼고, 훨씬 멋있어졌다. 하지만 그녀를 만난 기쁨의 홍조가 가시고 나니, 그는 지치고 풀이 죽어 보였다. 아프거나 딱히 불행해 보이지는 않았지만, 한두 해의 풍요로운 삶이 만들었을 법한 것보다 더 늙고 더 진중해 보였다. 에이미는 그 까닭을 알 수 없었고, 감히 캐묻지도 못했다. 그래서 행렬이 팔리오니 다리의 아치를 가로질러 굽이굽이 멀어지다가 교회로 사라지자, 고개를 한 번 젓고는 조랑말을 가볍게 채찍질했다."]], [["\"_Que pensez vous_?\"", "\"크 팡세 부?\""], ["she said, airing her French, which had improved in quantity, if not in quality, since she came abroad.", "그녀가 프랑스어를 뽐내며 말했다. 외국에 나온 뒤로 질은 몰라도 양은 늘어난 프랑스어였다."]], [["\"That mademoiselle has made good use of her time, and the result is charming,\"", "\"마드무아젤이 시간을 잘 보냈고, 그 결과가 매혹적이라는 거지.\""], ["replied Laurie, bowing, with his hand on his heart, and an admiring look.", "로리가 가슴에 손을 얹고 고개를 숙이며, 감탄 어린 눈빛으로 대답했다."]], [["She blushed with pleasure, but somehow the compliment did not satisfy her like the blunt praises he used to give her at home, when he promenaded round her on festival occasions, and told her she was \"altogether jolly,\"", "에이미는 기뻐서 얼굴을 붉혔다. 하지만 어쩐지 그 찬사는, 예전에 집에서 그가 해 주던 무뚝뚝한 칭찬만큼 마음에 차지 않았다."], ["with a hearty smile and an approving pat on the head.", "그때는 잔칫날이면 그녀 주위를 빙 돌면서, 진심 어린 미소로 머리를 흔쾌히 토닥이며 \"아주 근사하다\"고 말해 주곤 했었다."], ["She didn't like the new tone; for, though not _blasé_, it sounded indifferent in spite of the look.", "에이미는 이 새로운 말투가 마음에 들지 않았다. 권태에 찌든 건 아니었지만, 그 눈빛에도 불구하고 어딘가 무심하게 들렸다."]], [["\"If that's the way he's going to grow up, I wish he'd stay a boy,\"", "'저렇게 어른이 될 거라면, 차라리 소년인 채로 있는 게 낫겠어.'"], ["she thought, with a curious sense of disappointment and discomfort, trying meantime to seem quite easy and gay.", "에이미는 묘한 실망감과 불편함을 느끼며 그렇게 생각했다. 그러면서도 자기는 아주 편안하고 즐거워 보이려 애썼다."]], [["At Avigdor's she found the precious home-letters, and, giving the reins to Laurie, read them luxuriously as they wound up the shady road between green hedges, where tea-roses bloomed as freshly as in June.", "아비그도르 은행에서 에이미는 그토록 소중한 집에서 온 편지들을 받았고, 고삐를 로리에게 넘긴 채 그것을 한껏 즐기며 읽었다. 푸른 산울타리 사이로 그늘진 길을 굽이굽이 올라가는데, 거기엔 차나무 장미가 유월처럼 싱그럽게 피어 있었다."]], [["\"Beth is very poorly, mother says.", "\"엄마가 그러는데, 베스가 많이 안 좋대."], ["I often think I ought to go home, but they all say 'stay;'", "난 자꾸 집에 가야 하는 게 아닌가 싶은데, 다들 '그냥 있으라'고 해."], ["so I do, for I shall never have another chance like this,\"", "그래서 여기 있는 거야. 이런 기회는 다시는 없을 테니까.\""], ["said Amy, looking sober over one page.", "에이미가 한 장을 읽으며 침울한 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["\"I think you are right, there; you could do nothing at home, and it is a great comfort to them to know that you are well and happy, and enjoying so much, my dear.\"", "\"내 생각엔 네 말이 맞아. 집에 가도 네가 할 수 있는 일이 없을 거고, 네가 잘 지내고 행복하고 이렇게 많은 걸 누리고 있다는 걸 아는 게 식구들에게는 큰 위안이 될 거야.\""]], [["He drew a little nearer, and looked more like his old self, as he said that; and the fear that sometimes weighed on Amy's heart was lightened, for the look, the act, the brotherly \"my dear,\"", "그가 말하면서 조금 더 가까이 다가왔고, 한결 예전의 그다워 보였다. 그러자 이따금 에이미의 마음을 짓누르던 두려움이 가벼워졌다."], ["seemed to assure her that if any trouble did come, she would not be alone in a strange land.", "그 눈빛과 몸짓, 오빠 같은 \"이런\" 한마디가, 혹시라도 무슨 어려움이 닥치더라도 낯선 땅에서 혼자가 아니리라는 걸 그녀에게 확인시켜 주는 것 같았다."], ["Presently she laughed, and showed him a small sketch of Jo in her scribbling-suit, with the bow rampantly erect upon her cap, and issuing from her mouth the words, \"Genius burns!\"", "이윽고 그녀가 웃으며, 글을 끄적이는 차림새의 조를 그린 작은 그림을 보여 주었다. 모자 위에는 리본이 사납게 곤두서 있고, 입에서는 \"천재의 불꽃이 타오른다!\"라는 말이 흘러나오고 있었다."]], [["Laurie smiled, took it, put it in his vest-pocket, \"to keep it from blowing away,\"", "로리는 미소 지으며 그것을 받아, \"날아가 버리지 않게\""], ["and listened with interest to the lively letter Amy read him.", "조끼 주머니에 넣고는, 에이미가 읽어 주는 생기 넘치는 편지에 흥미롭게 귀를 기울였다."]], [["\"This will be a regularly merry Christmas to me, with presents in the morning, you and letters in the afternoon, and a party at night,\"", "\"이번엔 정말 즐거운 크리스마스가 되겠다. 아침엔 선물, 오후엔 너랑 편지, 밤엔 파티.\""], ["said Amy, as they alighted among the ruins of the old fort, and a flock of splendid peacocks came trooping about them, tamely waiting to be fed.", "에이미가 말했다. 두 사람은 옛 요새의 폐허 속에 내려섰고, 화려한 공작새 떼가 길든 모습으로 먹이를 기다리며 그들 주위로 우르르 몰려들었다."], ["While Amy stood laughing on the bank above him as she scattered crumbs to the brilliant birds, Laurie looked at her as she had looked at him, with a natural curiosity to see what changes time and absence had wrought.", "에이미가 그의 위쪽 둑에 서서 화려한 새들에게 빵 부스러기를 뿌리며 웃고 있는 동안, 로리는 그녀가 그를 보았던 것처럼 그녀를 바라보았다. 세월과 떨어져 있던 시간이 어떤 변화를 만들어 냈는지 보려는 자연스러운 호기심이었다."], ["He found nothing to perplex or disappoint, much to admire and approve; for, overlooking a few little affectations of speech and manner, she was as sprightly and graceful as ever, with the addition of that indescribable something in dress and bearing which we call elegance.", "그는 당혹스럽거나 실망스러운 점은 찾지 못했고, 감탄하고 흐뭇해할 점은 많이 찾아냈다. 말투와 태도에 밴 자그마한 가식 몇 가지만 눈감아 준다면, 그녀는 여전히 발랄하고 우아했으며, 거기에 우리가 기품이라 부르는, 옷차림과 몸가짐에 어린 그 무어라 형용할 수 없는 무언가까지 더해져 있었다."], ["Always mature for her age, she had gained a certain _aplomb_ in both carriage and conversation, which made her seem more of a woman of the world than she was; but her old petulance now and then showed itself, her strong will still held its own, and her native frankness was unspoiled by foreign polish.", "나이에 비해 늘 어른스러웠던 그녀는, 몸가짐과 대화 양쪽에서 일종의 침착함을 얻어, 실제보다 더 세상물정에 밝은 여인처럼 보였다. 하지만 옛날의 발끈하는 성미가 이따금 비쳤고, 강한 의지는 여전히 제 몫을 지켰으며, 타고난 솔직함은 외국식 세련됨에도 망가지지 않고 그대로였다."]], [["Laurie did not read all this while he watched her feed the peacocks, but he saw enough to satisfy and interest him, and carried away a pretty little picture of a bright-faced girl standing in the sunshine, which brought out the soft hue of her dress, the fresh color of her cheeks, the golden gloss of her hair, and made her a prominent figure in the pleasant scene.", "로리가 그녀의 공작새 먹이 주는 모습을 지켜보는 동안 이 모든 걸 다 읽어 낸 건 아니었다. 하지만 흐뭇하고 흥미를 느낄 만큼은 보았고, 햇살 속에 서 있는 환한 얼굴의 아가씨라는 어여쁜 작은 그림 하나를 마음에 담아 갔다. 햇살이 그녀의 옷의 부드러운 빛깔과 뺨의 싱그러운 혈색, 머리카락의 금빛 윤기를 도드라지게 했고, 그 즐거운 풍경 속에서 그녀를 단연 돋보이는 인물로 만들어 주었다."]], [["As they came up on to the stone plateau that crowns the hill, Amy waved her hand as if welcoming him to her favorite haunt, and said, pointing here and there,--", "언덕 꼭대기를 이루는 돌 고원에 올라서자, 에이미는 그를 자기가 좋아하는 곳으로 맞아들이듯 손을 흔들며, 여기저기를 가리키면서 말했다."]], [["\"Do you remember the Cathedral and the Corso, the fishermen dragging their nets in the bay, and the lovely road to Villa Franca, Schubert's Tower, just below, and, best of all, that speck far out to sea which they say is Corsica?\"", "\"대성당이랑 코르소 거리, 만에서 그물을 끌어올리는 어부들, 빌라프랑카로 가는 사랑스러운 길, 바로 저 아래 슈베르트 탑, 그리고 무엇보다, 저 멀리 바다에 코르시카라고들 하는 저 점, 기억나?\""]], [["\"I remember; it's not much changed,\"", "\"기억나. 별로 변한 게 없네.\""], ["he answered, without enthusiasm.", "그가 별다른 감흥 없이 대답했다."]], [["\"What Jo would give for a sight of that famous speck!\"", "\"조 언니가 저 유명한 점을 한 번 볼 수 있다면 뭐든 다 내놓을 텐데!\""], ["said Amy, feeling in good spirits, and anxious to see him so also.", "에이미가 말했다. 기분이 좋았고, 그도 그렇기를 바랐다."]], [["\"Yes,\"", "\"그래.\""], ["was all he said, but he turned and strained his eyes to see the island which a greater usurper than even Napoleon now made interesting in his sight.", "그가 한 말이라곤 그게 다였지만, 몸을 돌려 눈을 가늘게 뜨고 그 섬을 바라보았다. 이제는 나폴레옹보다 더 큰 찬탈자가 그의 눈에 그 섬을 흥미롭게 만들어 놓은 것이었다."]], [["\"Take a good look at it for her sake, and then come and tell me what you have been doing with yourself all this while,\"", "\"언니를 생각해서 잘 봐 둬. 그리고 와서 그동안 뭐 하고 지냈는지 다 얘기해 줘.\""], ["said Amy, seating herself, ready for a good talk.", "에이미가 진득하게 이야기할 채비를 하고 자리에 앉으며 말했다."]], [["But she did not get it; for, though he joined her, and answered all her questions freely, she could only learn that he had roved about the continent and been to Greece.", "하지만 그러지는 못했다. 그가 그녀 곁에 앉아 모든 질문에 거리낌 없이 답하긴 했어도, 알아낼 수 있는 거라곤 그가 대륙 여기저기를 떠돌았고 그리스에 다녀왔다는 것뿐이었다."], ["So, after idling away an hour, they drove home again; and, having paid his respects to Mrs.", "그렇게 한 시간을 빈둥거리다가, 두 사람은 다시 마차를 몰아 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["Carrol, Laurie left them, promising to return in the evening.", "로리는 캐롤 부인에게 인사를 드린 뒤, 저녁에 다시 오겠다고 약속하며 자리를 떴다."]], [["It must be recorded of Amy that she deliberately \"prinked\" that night.", "그날 밤 에이미가 작정하고 \"치장에 공을 들였다\"는 사실은 기록해 두어야겠다."], ["Time and absence had done its work on both the young people; she had seen her old friend in a new light, not as \"our boy,\"", "세월과 떨어져 있던 시간은 두 젊은이 모두에게 제 일을 해 놓았다. 그녀는 옛 친구를 새로운 눈으로 보게 되었다."], ["but as a handsome and agreeable man, and she was conscious of a very natural desire to find favor in his sight.", "\"우리 꼬마\"가 아니라, 잘생기고 호감 가는 한 남자로 말이다. 그리고 그의 눈에 들고 싶다는, 지극히 자연스러운 마음을 의식하고 있었다."], ["Amy knew her good points, and made the most of them, with the taste and skill which is a fortune to a poor and pretty woman.", "에이미는 자기 장점을 알았고, 가난하고 예쁜 여자에게 큰 재산이나 다름없는 안목과 솜씨로 그것을 한껏 살려 냈다."]], [["Tarlatan and tulle were cheap at Nice, so she enveloped herself in them on such occasions, and, following the sensible English fashion of simple dress for young girls, got up charming little toilettes with fresh flowers, a few trinkets, and all manner of dainty devices, which were both inexpensive and effective.", "니스에서는 탈라탕과 튈이 쌌으므로, 그녀는 그런 자리가 있을 때면 그 천으로 몸을 감쌌다. 그리고 어린 아가씨에게는 수수하게 입히는 분별 있는 영국식 유행을 따라, 싱싱한 꽃 몇 송이와 자그마한 장신구 몇 개, 온갖 앙증맞은 장치들로 매혹적인 작은 차림새를 꾸며 냈는데, 돈도 적게 들면서 효과는 그만이었다."], ["It must be confessed that the artist sometimes got possession of the woman, and indulged in antique _coiffures_, statuesque attitudes, and classic draperies.", "다만 고백하건대, 이따금 그녀 안의 예술가가 여자를 사로잡아, 고풍스러운 머리 모양과 조각상 같은 자세, 고전적인 드레이프 차림에 빠져들기도 했다."], ["But, dear heart, we all have our little weaknesses, and find it easy to pardon such in the young, who satisfy our eyes with their comeliness, and keep our hearts merry with their artless vanities.", "하지만 어쩌랴, 우리 모두에겐 저마다 작은 약점이 있는 법, 그리고 그런 약점은 젊은이들에게서라면 너그러이 봐주기 쉬운 법이다. 젊은이들은 그 아리따움으로 우리 눈을 즐겁게 하고, 그 천진한 허영심으로 우리 마음을 흥겹게 해 주니까."]], [["\"I do want him to think I look well, and tell them so at home,\"", "\"내가 예뻐 보인다고 그가 생각해 줬으면 좋겠어. 그래서 집에 그렇게 전해 줬으면 좋겠어.\""], ["said Amy to herself, as she put on Flo's old white silk ball-dress, and covered it with a cloud of fresh illusion, out of which her white shoulders and golden head emerged with a most artistic effect.", "에이미가 플로의 낡은 흰 실크 무도회 드레스를 입고, 그 위를 싱그러운 일루전 천의 구름으로 덮으면서 혼잣말을 했다. 그 사이로 하얀 어깨와 금빛 머리가 더없이 예술적인 효과를 내며 드러났다."], ["Her hair she had the sense to let alone, after gathering up the thick waves and curls into a Hebe-like knot at the back of her head.", "머리는 굵은 물결과 곱슬을 한데 모아 뒤통수에 헤베 여신처럼 매듭 지은 다음, 더 손대지 않을 만큼의 분별은 있었다."]], [["\"It's not the fashion, but it's becoming, and I can't afford to make a fright of myself,\"", "\"이게 유행은 아니지만 잘 어울려. 그리고 난 나 자신을 흉하게 만들 형편이 못 되거든.\""], ["she used to say, when advised to frizzle, puff, or braid, as the latest style commanded.", "그녀는 최신 유행에 따라 머리를 지지거나 부풀리거나 땋으라는 권유를 받을 때면 그렇게 말하곤 했다."]], [["Having no ornaments fine enough for this important occasion, Amy looped her fleecy skirts with rosy clusters of azalea, and framed the white shoulders in delicate green vines.", "이 중요한 자리에 걸맞은 고급 장신구가 없었던 에이미는, 가볍고 보드라운 치맛자락을 장밋빛 진달래 송이로 잡아 묶고, 하얀 어깨를 가녀린 초록 덩굴로 둘러 감쌌다."], ["Remembering the painted boots, she surveyed her white satin slippers with girlish satisfaction, and _chasséed_ down the room, admiring her aristocratic feet all by herself.", "그림이 그려진 부츠의 기억을 떠올리며, 흰 새틴 슬리퍼를 소녀다운 만족감으로 살펴보고는, 방 안에서 혼자 샤세 스텝을 밟으며 자기의 귀족적인 발을 흐뭇하게 감상했다."]], [["\"My new fan just matches my flowers, my gloves fit to a charm, and the real lace on aunt's _mouchoir_ gives an air to my whole dress.", "\"새 부채는 내 꽃이랑 딱 어울리고, 장갑은 신통하게도 꼭 맞고, 이모님 무슈아르에 달린 진짜 레이스는 드레스 전체에 운치를 더해 주네."], ["If I only had a classical nose and mouth I should be perfectly happy,\"", "코랑 입만 좀 고전적이면 더 바랄 게 없을 텐데.\""], ["she said, surveying herself with a critical eye, and a candle in each hand.", "그녀가 양손에 촛불을 하나씩 든 채, 비평하는 눈으로 자기 모습을 살피며 말했다."]], [["In spite of this affliction, she looked unusually gay and graceful as she glided away; she seldom ran,--it did not suit her style, she thought, for, being tall, the stately and Junoesque was more appropriate than the sportive or piquante. She walked up and down the long saloon while waiting for Laurie, and once arranged herself under the chandelier, which had a good effect upon her hair; then she thought better of it, and went away to the other end of the room, as if ashamed of the girlish desire to have the first view a propitious one. It so happened that she could not have done a better thing, for Laurie came in so quietly she did not hear him; and, as she stood at the distant window, with her head half turned, and one hand gathering up her dress, the slender, white figure against the red curtains was as effective as a well-placed statue.", "그런 고민에도 불구하고, 그녀는 미끄러지듯 걸어 나갈 때 유난히 흥겹고 우아해 보였다. 그녀는 좀처럼 뛰지 않았다. 자기 스타일에 어울리지 않는다고 여겼기 때문이다. 키가 큰 만큼, 까불거리거나 발랄한 것보다 위엄 있고 유노 여신 같은 분위기가 더 어울린다고 생각했다. 그녀는 로리를 기다리며 긴 응접실을 오갔고, 한번은 샹들리에 아래에 자리를 잡았는데, 그게 머리에 좋은 효과를 냈다. 그러다 다시 생각을 고쳐먹고는, 첫인상을 좋게 남기고 싶어 하는 소녀다운 마음이 부끄러운 듯, 방 반대편 끝으로 가 버렸다. 그런데 공교롭게도 그보다 더 잘한 일이 없었다. 로리가 어찌나 조용히 들어왔는지 그녀가 그 소리를 듣지 못한 것이다. 그녀가 멀찍한 창가에 고개를 반쯤 돌리고 한 손으로 드레스 자락을 그러쥔 채 서 있는 모습은, 붉은 커튼을 배경으로 한 그 가녀린 흰 자태가 자리를 잘 잡은 조각상만큼이나 인상적이었다."]], [["\"Good evening, Diana!\"", "\"안녕, 디아나!\""], ["said Laurie, with the look of satisfaction she liked to see in his eyes when they rested on her.", "로리가 말했다. 그가 그녀에게 눈길을 둘 때면 그녀가 보고 싶어 하던 바로 그 흐뭇한 표정이었다."]], [["\"Good evening, Apollo!\"", "\"안녕, 아폴로!\" 그녀도 미소로 화답했다."], ["she answered, smiling back at him, for he, too, looked unusually _debonnaire_, and the thought of entering the ball-room on the arm of such a personable man caused Amy to pity the four plain Misses Davis from the bottom of her heart.", "그 역시 유난히 멋스러워 보였다. 이렇게 풍채 좋은 남자의 팔짱을 끼고 무도회장에 들어선다는 생각에, 에이미는 그 수수한 데이비스 자매 네 사람이 진심으로 가엾게 느껴질 정도였다."]], [["\"Here are your flowers; I arranged them myself, remembering that you didn't like what Hannah calls a 'sot-bookay,'\"", "\"네 꽃 여기 있어. 너는 해나가 '꽃다발 잡탕'이라고 부르는 그런 걸 싫어하던 걸 기억하고 내가 직접 만들었어.\""], ["said Laurie, handing her a delicate nosegay, in a holder that she had long coveted as she daily passed it in Cardiglia's window.", "로리가 가녀린 꽃다발을 건네며 말했다. 그것을 꽂은 받침은 그녀가 카르딜리아 가게 진열장을 날마다 지나치면서 오랫동안 탐내던 것이었다."]], [["\"How kind you are!\"", "\"어쩜 이렇게 다정하니!\""], ["she exclaimed gratefully.", "그녀가 고마워하며 외쳤다."], ["\"If I'd known you were coming I'd have had something ready for you to-day, though not as pretty as this, I'm afraid.\"", "\"네가 오는 줄 알았으면 오늘 너한테 줄 걸 미리 준비해 뒀을 텐데. 이것만큼 예쁘진 못했겠지만.\""]], [["\"Thank you; it isn't what it should be, but you have improved it,\"", "\"고마워. 제대로 된 모양새는 아니지만, 네가 더 보기 좋게 만들었네.\""], ["he added, as she snapped the silver bracelet on her wrist.", "그녀가 손목에 은팔찌를 채우는 걸 보며 그가 덧붙였다."]], [["\"Please don't.\"", "\"제발 그러지 마.\""]], [["\"I thought you liked that sort of thing?\"", "\"넌 그런 말 좋아하는 줄 알았는데?\""]], [["\"Not from you; it doesn't sound natural, and I like your old bluntness better.\"", "\"너한테 듣는 건 아니야. 너답지가 않아서, 차라리 예전의 그 무뚝뚝함이 더 좋아.\""]], [["\"I'm glad of it,\"", "\"그렇다니 다행이네.\""], ["he answered, with a look of relief; then buttoned her gloves for her, and asked if his tie was straight, just as he used to do when they went to parties together, at home.", "그가 안도하는 표정으로 대답했다. 그러고는 예전에 함께 집에서 파티에 가던 때처럼, 그녀의 장갑 단추를 채워 주고 자기 넥타이가 똑바른지 물었다."]], [["The company assembled in the long _salle à manger_, that evening, was such as one sees nowhere but on the Continent.", "그날 밤 길쭉한 식당에 모인 사람들은, 유럽 대륙이 아니고서는 어디서도 볼 수 없는 부류였다."], ["The hospitable Americans had invited every acquaintance they had in Nice, and, having no prejudice against titles, secured a few to add lustre to their Christmas ball.", "손님 대접을 좋아하는 미국인들이 니스에서 아는 사람이란 사람은 죄다 초대했고, 작위에 대한 거부감이 없었던 터라, 크리스마스 무도회에 광채를 더하려고 작위 있는 인사 몇 명도 확보해 두었다."]], [["A Russian prince condescended to sit in a corner for an hour, and talk with a massive lady, dressed like Hamlet's mother, in black velvet, with a pearl bridle under her chin.", "러시아 공작 한 명이 황송하게도 한 시간 동안 구석에 앉아, 햄릿의 어머니처럼 검은 벨벳을 두르고 턱 밑에 진주 끈을 늘어뜨린 풍채 좋은 부인과 이야기를 나누었다."], ["A Polish count, aged eighteen, devoted himself to the ladies, who pronounced him \"a fascinating dear,\" and a German Serene Something, having come for the supper alone, roamed vaguely about, seeking what he might devour.", "열여덟 살 난 폴란드 백작은 숙녀들에게 헌신적으로 굴어, 숙녀들로부터 \"매력 넘치는 사람\"이라는 평을 받았고, 독일의 무슨 전하라는 인사는 오로지 저녁 식사 때문에 와서, 뭘 먹어 치울까 두리번거리며 정처 없이 돌아다녔다."], ["Baron Rothschild's private secretary, a large-nosed Jew, in tight boots, affably beamed upon the world, as if his master's name crowned him with a golden halo; a stout Frenchman, who knew the Emperor, came to indulge his mania for dancing, and Lady de Jones, a British matron, adorned the scene with her little family of eight.", "로스차일드 남작의 개인 비서인, 꽉 끼는 부츠를 신은 코 큰 유대인은, 마치 주인의 이름이 자기 머리에 황금빛 후광을 씌워 주기라도 한 양 세상을 향해 상냥하게 미소를 흘렸다. 황제와 안면이 있다는 뚱뚱한 프랑스인은 춤에 미친 자기 취미를 마음껏 풀러 왔고, 영국 부인인 더 존스 부인은 여덟 식구나 되는 단란한 가족으로 그 자리를 빛냈다."], ["Of course, there were many light-footed, shrill-voiced American girls, handsome, lifeless-looking English ditto, and a few plain but piquante French demoiselles; likewise the usual set of travelling young gentlemen, who disported themselves gayly, while mammas of all nations lined the walls, and smiled upon them benignly when they danced with their daughters.", "물론 발놀림 가볍고 목소리 새된 미국 아가씨들도 많았고, 잘생겼지만 생기 없어 보이는 영국 아가씨들도 그만큼 있었으며, 수수해도 톡톡 튀는 프랑스 아가씨 몇몇도 있었다. 또 여느 때처럼 여행 중인 젊은 신사들 무리가 흥겹게 즐겼고, 온갖 나라의 어머니들이 벽가에 늘어서서 제 딸들과 춤추는 청년들을 인자하게 바라보며 미소 지었다."]], [["Any young girl can imagine Amy's state of mind when she \"took the stage\" that night, leaning on Laurie's arm. She knew she looked well, she loved to dance, she felt that her foot was on her native heath in a ball-room, and enjoyed the delightful sense of power which comes when young girls first discover the new and lovely kingdom they are born to rule by virtue of beauty, youth, and womanhood. She did pity the Davis girls, who were awkward, plain, and destitute of escort, except a grim papa and three grimmer maiden aunts, and she bowed to them in her friendliest manner as she passed; which was good of her, as it permitted them to see her dress, and burn with curiosity to know who her distinguished-looking friend might be. With the first burst of the band, Amy's color rose, her eyes began to sparkle, and her feet to tap the floor impatiently; for she danced well, and wanted Laurie to know it: therefore the shock she received can better be imagined than described, when he said, in a perfectly tranquil tone,--", "그날 밤 로리의 팔에 기대어 \"무대에 오른\" 순간 에이미가 어떤 심정이었을지는, 어떤 젊은 아가씨든 능히 상상할 수 있으리라. 그녀는 자기가 예뻐 보인다는 걸 알았고, 춤추기를 좋아했으며, 무도회장에서는 제 세상에 있는 듯한 기분이 들었다. 그리고 젊은 아가씨가 미모와 젊음, 여성다움이라는 권능으로 다스리도록 태어난 그 새롭고 사랑스러운 왕국을 처음 발견했을 때 찾아오는, 그 짜릿한 권력의 감각을 만끽했다. 어색하고 수수한 데다, 무뚝뚝한 아버지와 그보다 더 무뚝뚝한 노처녀 이모 셋 말고는 함께해 줄 사람도 없는 데이비스네 아가씨들이 그녀는 정말 가여웠다. 그래서 지나치면서 더없이 다정한 태도로 그들에게 인사를 건넸다. 친절한 일이었다. 덕분에 그들은 그녀의 드레스를 구경할 수 있었고, 저 기품 있어 보이는 친구가 대체 누구인지 궁금해서 애가 탔으니까. 악단이 첫 곡을 터뜨리자 에이미의 얼굴에는 혈색이 돌고, 두 눈은 반짝이기 시작했으며, 두 발은 조바심을 내며 바닥을 톡톡 두드렸다. 그녀는 춤을 잘 추었고, 로리에게 그걸 알려 주고 싶었다. 그러니 그가 더없이 태평한 어조로 이렇게 물었을 때 그녀가 받은 충격은, 말로 설명하기보다 그저 상상하는 편이 나을 것이다."]], [["\"Do you care to dance?\"", "\"춤추고 싶어?\""]], [["\"One usually does at a ball.\"", "\"무도회에서야 보통 그렇지.\""]], [["Her amazed look and quick answer caused Laurie to repair his error as fast as possible.", "그녀의 놀란 표정과 톡 쏘는 대답에, 로리는 서둘러 자기 실수를 만회했다."]], [["\"I meant the first dance.", "\"첫 춤 말이야."], ["May I have the honor?\"", "영광을 베풀어 주겠어?\""]], [["\"I can give you one if I put off the Count.", "\"백작님과의 약속을 미루면 한 곡 줄 수는 있어."], ["He dances divinely; but he will excuse me, as you are an old friend,\"", "그분은 춤을 기막히게 잘 추거든. 그래도 네가 오랜 친구니까 양해해 주실 거야.\""], ["said Amy, hoping that the name would have a good effect, and show Laurie that she was not to be trifled with.", "에이미가 말했다. 그 이름이 좋은 효과를 내서, 자기를 함부로 대해선 안 된다는 걸 로리가 알아주기를 바라면서."]], [["\"Nice little boy, but rather a short Pole to support\n\"'A daughter of the gods, Divinely tall, and most divinely fair,'\"", "\"착한 꼬마긴 한데, '여신의 따님, 신처럼 키 크고, 더없이 신처럼 어여쁜' 이를 받쳐 주기엔 키가 좀 짧은 폴란드 사람이로군.\""]], [["was all the satisfaction she got, however.", "하지만 그녀가 얻은 만족이라곤 그게 다였다."]], [["The set in which they found themselves was composed of English, and Amy was compelled to walk decorously through a cotillon, feeling all the while as if she could dance the Tarantula with a relish.", "두 사람이 끼게 된 조는 영국 사람들로 짜여 있어, 에이미는 코티용을 점잖게 추며 걸어 나갈 수밖에 없었다. 그러면서도 내내, 신이 나서 타란텔라라도 출 수 있을 것 같은 기분이었다."], ["Laurie resigned her to the \"nice little boy,\"", "로리는 그녀를 \"착한 꼬마\"에게 넘기고, 앞으로의 즐거움을 위해 에이미를 미리 잡아 두지도 않은 채, 플로에 대한 의무를 다하러 가 버렸다. 이 괘씸하리만치 앞을 못 내다본 처사는 마땅한 벌을 받았다."], ["and went to do his duty to Flo, without securing Amy for the joys to come, which reprehensible want of forethought was properly punished, for she immediately engaged herself till supper, meaning to relent if he then gave any signs of penitence.", "에이미가 그 즉시 저녁 식사 때까지 춤 상대를 다 정해 버렸기 때문이다. 단, 그가 그때 가서 뉘우치는 기색이라도 보이면 마음을 누그러뜨릴 셈이었다."], ["She showed him her ball-book with demure satisfaction when he strolled, instead of rushing, up to claim her for the next, a glorious polka-redowa; but his polite regrets didn't impose upon her, and when she gallopaded away with the Count, she saw Laurie sit down by her aunt with an actual expression of relief.", "그가 다음 곡, 그 멋진 폴카 르도바를 추자며 달려오기는커녕 어슬렁어슬렁 다가왔을 때, 그녀는 새침한 만족감으로 자기 무도회 수첩을 보여 주었다. 하지만 그의 정중한 사과는 그녀를 속이지 못했고, 그녀가 백작과 함께 미끄러져 가는데, 로리가 실제로 안도하는 표정을 지으며 이모 곁에 앉는 게 보였다."]], [["That was unpardonable; and Amy took no more notice of him for a long while, except a word now and then, when she came to her chaperon, between the dances, for a necessary pin or a moment's rest.", "그건 용서할 수 없는 일이었다. 그래서 에이미는 한참 동안 그에게 더는 눈길을 주지 않았다."], ["Her anger had a good effect, however, for she hid it under a smiling face, and seemed unusually blithe and brilliant.", "다만 춤과 춤 사이에, 핀이 필요하거나 잠깐 쉬려고 보호자인 이모에게 올 때 이따금 한마디씩 건넬 뿐이었다. 그래도 그녀의 분노는 좋은 효과를 냈다."], ["Laurie's eyes followed her with pleasure, for she neither romped nor sauntered, but danced with spirit and grace, making the delightsome pastime what it should be.", "미소 띤 얼굴 뒤에 그것을 감추니, 유난히 명랑하고 눈부셔 보였던 것이다. 로리의 눈길이 흐뭇하게 그녀를 좇았다."], ["He very naturally fell to studying her from this new point of view; and, before the evening was half over, had decided that \"little Amy was going to make a very charming woman.\"", "그녀는 까불지도 어슬렁대지도 않고, 활기와 기품을 담아 춤추며, 그 즐거운 놀이를 본디 그래야 할 모습으로 만들어 냈다. 그는 아주 자연스럽게 이 새로운 시각에서 그녀를 살펴보기 시작했고, 저녁이 반도 지나기 전에 \"어린 에이미가 아주 매력적인 여인이 되겠다\"고 단정 짓고 말았다."]], [["It was a lively scene, for soon the spirit of the social season took possession of every one, and Christmas merriment made all faces shine, hearts happy, and heels light.", "활기 넘치는 광경이었다. 이내 사교철의 흥취가 모두를 사로잡았고, 크리스마스의 흥겨움이 모든 얼굴을 빛내고 마음을 즐겁게 하고 발걸음을 가볍게 했다."], ["The musicians fiddled, tooted, and banged as if they enjoyed it; everybody danced who could, and those who couldn't admired their neighbors with uncommon warmth.", "악사들은 바이올린을 켜고 나팔을 불고 북을 두드리며 신이 난 듯했다. 출 수 있는 사람은 다 춤을 췄고, 못 추는 사람은 보기 드문 열의로 옆 사람을 찬탄했다."], ["The air was dark with Davises, and many Joneses gambolled like a flock of young giraffes.", "데이비스 사람들로 공기가 어두컴컴할 지경이었고, 수많은 존스 사람들은 어린 기린 떼처럼 깡충거렸다."], ["The golden secretary darted through the room like a meteor, with a dashing Frenchwoman, who carpeted the floor with her pink satin train.", "금빛 비서는 분홍 새틴 자락으로 바닥을 쓸고 다니는 화려한 프랑스 여인과 함께 유성처럼 방 안을 누볐다."], ["The Serene Teuton found the supper-table, and was happy, eating steadily through the bill of fare, and dismayed the _garçons_ by the ravages he committed.", "점잖은 독일 사람은 만찬 식탁을 찾아내어 행복해했는데, 차림표를 처음부터 끝까지 꾸준히 먹어 치우며, 그 약탈상에 급사들을 아연실색하게 만들었다."], ["But the Emperor's friend covered himself with glory, for he danced everything, whether he knew it or not, and introduced impromptu pirouettes when the figures bewildered him.", "그러나 황제의 친구는 영광을 한 몸에 뒤집어썼다. 그는 아는 춤이든 모르는 춤이든 가리지 않고 죄다 췄고, 스텝이 헷갈릴 때면 즉흥적으로 피루엣을 끼워 넣었던 것이다."], ["The boyish abandon of that stout man was charming to behold; for, though he \"carried weight,\"", "그 뚱뚱한 남자의 소년 같은 거침없음은 보고 있기에 흐뭇했다. \"몸집은 묵직해도\""], ["he danced like an india-rubber ball.", "고무공처럼 춤을 췄으니까. 그는 달리고, 날고, 껑충거렸다."], ["He ran, he flew, he pranced; his face glowed, his bald head shone; his coat-tails waved wildly, his pumps actually twinkled in the air, and when the music stopped, he wiped the drops from his brow, and beamed upon his fellow-men like a French Pickwick without glasses.", "얼굴은 달아오르고 벗어진 머리는 번들거렸으며, 연미복 자락은 사납게 펄럭이고 무도화는 정말이지 공중에서 반짝거렸다. 음악이 멈추자 그는 이마의 땀방울을 훔치고는, 안경 없는 프랑스판 피크위크처럼 동료 인간들을 향해 환하게 웃었다."]], [["Amy and her Pole distinguished themselves by equal enthusiasm, but more graceful agility; and Laurie found himself involuntarily keeping time to the rhythmic rise and fall of the white slippers as they flew by as indefatigably as if winged.", "에이미와 그녀의 폴란드 사람은 똑같은 열의에, 더 우아한 날렵함으로 단연 돋보였다. 로리는 자기도 모르게, 날개라도 단 듯 지칠 줄 모르고 휙휙 스쳐 지나가는 흰 슬리퍼가 율동적으로 오르락내리락하는 데 맞춰 박자를 타고 있었다."], ["When little Vladimir finally relinquished her, with assurances that he was \"desolated to leave so early,\" she was ready to rest, and see how her recreant knight had borne his punishment.", "마침내 어린 블라디미르가 \"이렇게 일찍 떠나야 하다니 비통하다\"는 인사를 남기고 그녀를 놓아주자, 에이미는 쉬면서, 자기를 배신했던 기사가 벌을 어떻게 견뎌 냈는지 살펴볼 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["It had been successful; for, at three-and-twenty, blighted affections find a balm in friendly society, and young nerves will thrill, young blood dance, and healthy young spirits rise, when subjected to the enchantment of beauty, light, music, and motion.", "벌은 효과가 있었다. 스물세 살 나이에는 시든 애정도 다정한 사교 모임에서 위안을 얻는 법이고, 젊은 신경은 짜릿하게 떨리고 젊은 피는 춤추며 건강한 젊은 기운은 솟구치게 마련이니, 미모와 빛과 음악과 율동의 마법에 걸리면 더더욱 그렇다."], ["Laurie had a waked-up look as he rose to give her his seat; and when he hurried away to bring her some supper, she said to herself, with a satisfied smile,--", "로리는 자리를 내주려고 일어서는데 어딘가 활기를 되찾은 얼굴이었다. 그가 저녁거리를 가지러 서둘러 떠나자, 그녀는 만족스러운 미소를 지으며 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["\"Ah, I thought that would do him good!\"", "'아, 저게 도움이 될 줄 알았어!'"]], [["\"You look like Balzac's 'Femme peinte par elle-même,'\"", "\"넌 발자크의 '자화상을 그린 여인' 같아.\""], ["he said, as he fanned her with one hand, and held her coffee-cup in the other.", "그가 한 손으로는 그녀에게 부채질을 하고 다른 손으로는 그녀의 커피잔을 든 채 말했다."]], [["\"My rouge won't come off;\"", "\"내 볼연지는 안 지워져.\""], ["and Amy rubbed her brilliant cheek, and showed him her white glove with a sober simplicity that made him laugh outright.", "에이미가 발그레한 뺨을 문지르고는, 진지하고 천연덕스러운 태도로 흰 장갑을 그에게 보여 주었다. 그 바람에 그는 그만 소리 내어 웃고 말았다."]], [["\"What do you call this stuff?\"", "\"이 천은 뭐라고 부르는 거야?\""], ["he asked, touching a fold of her dress that had blown over his knee.", "그가 자기 무릎 위로 흘러내린 그녀의 드레스 주름을 만지며 물었다."]], [["\"Illusion.\"", "\"일루전.\""]], [["\"Good name for it; it's very pretty--new thing, isn't it?\"", "\"이름 한번 잘 지었네. 아주 예쁜데, 새로 나온 거야?\""]], [["\"It's as old as the hills; you have seen it on dozens of girls, and you never found out that it was pretty till now--_stupide_!\"", "\"산만큼 오래된 거야. 수많은 여자애들이 입은 걸 봤으면서, 이제껏 그게 예쁜지도 몰랐던 거잖아. 멍청이!\""]], [["\"I never saw it on you before, which accounts for the mistake, you see.\"", "\"네가 입은 건 처음 봤거든. 그래서 못 알아본 거지, 알겠어?\""]], [["\"None of that, it is forbidden; I'd rather take coffee than compliments just now.", "\"그런 말 마, 금지야. 지금은 칭찬보다 커피가 낫겠어."], ["No, don't lounge, it makes me nervous.\"", "아니, 그렇게 늘어져 있지 마, 신경 쓰이니까.\""]], [["Laurie sat bolt upright, and meekly took her empty plate, feeling an odd sort of pleasure in having \"little Amy\"", "로리는 등을 꼿꼿이 세우고 얌전히 그녀의 빈 접시를 받아 들었다. \"어린 에이미\"에게 이래라저래라 부림을 당하는 게 묘하게 즐거웠다."], ["order him about; for she had lost her shyness now, and felt an irresistible desire to trample on him, as girls have a delightful way of doing when lords of creation show any signs of subjection.", "그녀는 이제 수줍음을 떨쳐 냈고, 그를 짓밟고 싶은 거부할 수 없는 욕구를 느끼고 있었다. 만물의 주인이라는 남자들이 조금이라도 굴복하는 기색을 보이면, 여자들이 흐뭇하게 그렇게 굴게 마련인 것처럼."]], [["\"Where did you learn all this sort of thing?\"", "\"이런 건 다 어디서 배운 거야?\""], ["he asked, with a quizzical look.", "그가 짓궂은 표정으로 물었다."]], [["\"As 'this sort of thing' is rather a vague expression, would you kindly explain?\"", "\"'이런 거'라는 게 좀 애매한 표현인데, 친절하게 설명 좀 해 줄래?\""], ["returned Amy, knowing perfectly well what he meant, but wickedly leaving him to describe what is indescribable.", "에이미가 받아쳤다. 그가 무슨 뜻인지 뻔히 알면서도, 짓궂게도 형용할 수 없는 것을 그더러 설명해 보라고 떠넘긴 것이다."]], [["\"Well--the general air, the style, the self-possession, the--the--illusion--you know,\"", "\"그게, 전체적인 분위기랄까, 스타일이랄까, 침착함이랄까, 그, 그, 일루전 같은 거, 알잖아.\""], ["laughed Laurie, breaking down, and helping himself out of his quandary with the new word.", "로리가 더듬거리며 웃다가, 그 새 단어로 곤경에서 빠져나왔다."]], [["Amy was gratified, but, of course, didn't show it, and demurely answered, \"Foreign life polishes one in spite of one's self; I study as well as play; and as for this\"--with a little gesture toward her dress--\"why, tulle is cheap, posies to be had for nothing, and I am used to making the most of my poor little things.\"", "에이미는 흐뭇했지만, 당연히 그 내색은 하지 않고 새침하게 대꾸했다. \"외국 생활은 본인 뜻과 상관없이 사람을 세련되게 만들거든. 난 노는 만큼 공부도 해. 그리고 이건,\" 드레스 쪽으로 가볍게 손짓하며, \"글쎄, 튈은 싸고, 꽃은 거저 얻을 수 있고, 난 가진 게 변변찮으니 그걸 최대한 살리는 데 이골이 났거든.\""]], [["Amy rather regretted that last sentence, fearing it wasn't in good taste; but Laurie liked her the better for it, and found himself both admiring and respecting the brave patience that made the most of opportunity, and the cheerful spirit that covered poverty with flowers. Amy did not know why he looked at her so kindly, nor why he filled up her book with his own name, and devoted himself to her for the rest of the evening, in the most delightful manner; but the impulse that wrought this agreeable change was the result of one of the new impressions which both of them were unconsciously giving and receiving.", "에이미는 마지막 문장이 좀 후회스러웠다. 품위 없게 들렸을까 봐 걱정스러웠던 것이다. 하지만 로리는 그 말 때문에 그녀가 더 좋아졌다. 기회를 최대한 살려 내는 그 씩씩한 인내심과, 가난을 꽃으로 덮어 가리는 그 명랑한 기상에 감탄하는 동시에 존경하는 마음이 일었다. 에이미는 그가 왜 그렇게 다정하게 자기를 바라보는지, 왜 자기 무도회 수첩을 자기 이름으로 다 채우는지, 왜 그날 밤 남은 시간 동안 더없이 즐거운 태도로 자기에게만 매달리는지 알지 못했다. 하지만 이 유쾌한 변화를 빚어낸 충동은, 두 사람이 알지 못하는 사이에 서로 주고받고 있던 그 새로운 인상들 중 하나가 빚어낸 결과였다. 선반 위에 얹혀"]], [["# ON THE SHELF.", "# ON THE SHELF."]], [["In France the young girls have a dull time of it till they are married, when \"_Vive la liberté_\" becomes their motto.", "프랑스에서 젊은 아가씨들은 결혼하기 전까지 따분한 시절을 보내다가, 결혼하고 나서야 \"자유 만세!\"가 그들의 좌우명이 된다."], ["In America, as every one knows, girls early sign the declaration of independence, and enjoy their freedom with republican zest; but the young matrons usually abdicate with the first heir to the throne, and go into a seclusion almost as close as a French nunnery, though by no means as quiet.", "미국에서는, 누구나 알다시피, 아가씨들이 일찌감치 독립 선언서에 서명하고 공화주의자의 열의로 자유를 누린다. 하지만 젊은 새댁들은 대개 왕위를 이을 첫 후계자가 생기면 자리에서 물러나, 프랑스 수녀원만큼이나 폐쇄적인—물론 결코 그만큼 조용하지는 않은—은둔 생활로 들어간다."], ["Whether they like it or not, they are virtually put upon the shelf as soon as the wedding excitement is over, and most of them might exclaim, as did a very pretty woman the other day, \"I'm as handsome as ever, but no one takes any notice of me because I'm married.\"", "좋든 싫든 그들은 결혼식의 들뜬 기분이 가시기가 무섭게 사실상 선반 위에 얹히고 마는데, 그들 대부분은 얼마 전 어느 무척 예쁜 여인이 한 말처럼 외칠 만하다. \"난 여전히 예쁜데, 결혼했다고 아무도 날 거들떠보지 않아.\""]], [["Not being a belle or even a fashionable lady, Meg did not experience this affliction till her babies were a year old, for in her little world primitive customs prevailed, and she found herself more admired and beloved than ever.", "미인도, 멋쟁이 부인도 아니었던 메그는 아기들이 한 살이 될 때까지는 이런 시련을 겪지 않았다. 그녀의 작은 세상에서는 소박한 관습이 통했고, 그녀는 자기가 그 어느 때보다 더 칭송받고 사랑받는다는 걸 알게 되었기 때문이다."]], [["As she was a womanly little woman, the maternal instinct was very strong, and she was entirely absorbed in her children, to the utter exclusion of everything and everybody else.", "여성스러운 작은 여인이었던 메그는 모성 본능이 무척 강했고, 다른 모든 것과 모든 이를 완전히 제쳐 둔 채 아이들에게만 온통 빠져 있었다."], ["Day and night she brooded over them with tireless devotion and anxiety, leaving John to the tender mercies of the help, for an Irish lady now presided over the kitchen department.", "밤낮으로 지칠 줄 모르는 헌신과 노심초사로 아이들 위에 마음을 졸였고, 존은 도우미의 손길에 내맡겨 두었다. 이제 부엌은 아일랜드 여인이 맡아 꾸리고 있었으니 말이다."], ["Being a domestic man, John decidedly missed the wifely attentions he had been accustomed to receive; but, as he adored his babies, he cheerfully relinquished his comfort for a time, supposing, with masculine ignorance, that peace would soon be restored.", "가정적인 남자였던 존은 그동안 으레 받아 오던 아내의 보살핌이 못내 아쉬웠다. 하지만 아기들을 끔찍이 사랑했으므로, 머잖아 평온이 되돌아오리라는 남자다운 무지로, 한동안은 기꺼이 자기 안락을 양보했다."], ["But three months passed, and there was no return of repose; Meg looked worn and nervous, the babies absorbed every minute of her time, the house was neglected, and Kitty, the cook, who took life \"aisy,\"", "그러나 석 달이 지나도록 평온은 돌아오지 않았다. 메그는 지치고 신경이 곤두선 얼굴이었고, 아기들이 그녀의 일분일초를 다 앗아 갔으며, 집안은 방치되었고, \"느긋하게\""], ["kept him on short commons.", "사는 요리사 키티는 그를 변변찮은 식사로 굶주리게 했다."], ["When he went out in the morning he was bewildered by small commissions for the captive mamma; if he came gayly in at night, eager to embrace his family, he was quenched by a \"Hush!", "아침에 나갈 때면 그는 집에 갇힌 엄마가 시키는 자질구레한 심부름들로 어리둥절했고, 밤에 식구들을 안고 싶어 신이 나서 들어오면 \"쉿! 온종일 보채다가 이제 막 잠들었어요\"라는 말에 김이 샜다."], ["they are just asleep after worrying all day.\" If he proposed a little amusement at home, \"No, it would disturb the babies.\"", "집에서 가볍게 즐길 거리를 제안하면 \"안 돼요, 아기들이 깨요\"였고, 강연이나 음악회 얘기를 슬쩍 꺼내면 책망 어린 눈빛과 함께 \"재미 보겠다고 내 아이들을 두고 가라고요?"], ["If he hinted at a lecture or concert, he was answered with a reproachful look, and a decided \"Leave my children for pleasure, never!\"", "절대 안 돼요!\"라는 단호한 대답이 돌아왔다. 그의 잠은 아기들의 울음소리와, 밤의 정적 속에서 소리 없이 이리저리 거니는 유령 같은 형상의 환영에 깨지기 일쑤였다."], ["His sleep was broken by infant wails and visions of a phantom figure pacing noiselessly to and fro in the watches of the night; his meals were interrupted by the frequent flight of the presiding genius, who deserted him, half-helped, if a muffled chirp sounded from the nest above; and when he read his paper of an evening, Demi's colic got into the shipping-list, and Daisy's fall affected the price of stocks, for Mrs.", "식사는, 위층 둥지에서 희미한 칭얼거림이라도 들려오면 음식을 반쯤 덜다 말고 그를 버려두고 달려가는 살림의 여신 때문에 번번이 끊겼다. 저녁에 신문을 읽으려 들면, 데미의 배앓이가 선적 명세란에 끼어들고 데이지의 넘어진 일이 주가에 영향을 미쳤다."], ["Brooke was only interested in domestic news.", "브룩 부인의 관심사라곤 오로지 집안 소식뿐이었으니까. 가엾은 남자는 무척 불편했다."], ["The poor man was very uncomfortable, for the children had bereft him of his wife; home was merely a nursery, and the perpetual \"hushing\"", "아이들이 그에게서 아내를 앗아 가 버린 것이다. 집은 한낱 육아실이 되어 버렸고, 끊임없는 \"쉿\""], ["made him feel like a brutal intruder whenever he entered the sacred precincts of Babyland.", "소리에 그는 베이비랜드라는 그 신성한 영역에 발을 들일 때마다 자기가 무지막지한 침입자가 된 기분이었다."], ["He bore it very patiently for six months, and, when no signs of amendment appeared, he did what other paternal exiles do,--tried to get a little comfort elsewhere.", "여섯 달 동안 그는 무척이나 참을성 있게 견뎠다. 그러나 나아질 기미가 보이지 않자, 다른 아버지 유배자들이 하는 대로 했다."], ["Scott had married and gone to housekeeping not far off, and John fell into the way of running over for an hour or two of an evening, when his own parlor was empty, and his own wife singing lullabies that seemed to have no end.", "다른 데서나마 약간의 위안을 얻으려 한 것이다. 스콧이 결혼해서 멀지 않은 곳에 살림을 차렸던 터라, 존은 자기 집 거실이 텅 비고 아내가 끝도 없을 듯한 자장가나 부르고 있는 저녁이면, 한두 시간씩 그 집에 건너가 지내는 버릇이 들었다."], ["Mrs. Scott was a lively, pretty girl, with nothing to do but be agreeable, and she performed her mission most successfully.", "스콧 부인은 발랄하고 예쁜 여인으로, 상냥하게 구는 것 말고는 할 일이 없었고, 그 소임을 더없이 멋지게 해냈다."], ["The parlor was always bright and attractive, the chess-board ready, the piano in tune, plenty of gay gossip, and a nice little supper set forth in tempting style.", "거실은 늘 환하고 매력적이었으며, 체스판이 준비되어 있고, 피아노는 잘 조율되어 있었으며, 흥겨운 잡담이 넘쳐 났고, 군침 도는 차림새의 아담한 저녁 식사가 차려졌다."]], [["John would have preferred his own fireside if it had not been so lonely; but as it was, he gratefully took the next best thing, and enjoyed his neighbor's society.", "자기 집 난롯가가 그토록 외롭지만 않았다면 존은 그쪽을 더 좋아했을 것이다. 하지만 사정이 사정인지라, 그는 차선의 것을 고맙게 받아들이며 이웃의 사교를 즐겼다."]], [["Meg rather approved of the new arrangement at first, and found it a relief to know that John was having a good time instead of dozing in the parlor, or tramping about the house and waking the children.", "메그도 처음에는 이 새로운 형편을 제법 반겼다. 존이 거실에서 꾸벅꾸벅 졸거나, 집 안을 쿵쿵 돌아다니며 아이들을 깨우는 대신 즐거운 시간을 보낸다고 생각하니 마음이 놓였던 것이다."], ["But by and by, when the teething worry was over, and the idols went to sleep at proper hours, leaving mamma time to rest, she began to miss John, and find her work-basket dull company, when he was not sitting opposite in his old dressing-gown, comfortably scorching his slippers on the fender.", "그러나 이윽고 이앓이로 인한 걱정이 끝나고, 그 작은 우상들이 적당한 시간에 잠들어 엄마에게 쉴 틈이 생기자, 메그는 존이 그리워지기 시작했다. 그가 낡은 실내복 차림으로 맞은편에 앉아, 난로 망에 슬리퍼를 편안하게 그슬리고 있지 않으니, 바느질 바구니가 따분한 벗으로 느껴졌다."], ["She would not ask him to stay at home, but felt injured because he did not know that she wanted him without being told, entirely forgetting the many evenings he had waited for her in vain.", "그렇다고 그에게 집에 있어 달라고 청하지는 않았다. 다만 말하지 않아도 자기가 그를 원하는 걸 그가 알아주지 못하니 서운했다."], ["She was nervous and worn out with watching and worry, and in that unreasonable frame of mind which the best of mothers occasionally experience when domestic cares oppress them.", "그가 헛되이 자기를 기다린 그 숱한 저녁들은 까맣게 잊은 채로 말이다. 그녀는 아이를 지켜보고 노심초사하느라 신경이 곤두서고 진이 빠진 상태였고, 집안일에 짓눌릴 때면 더없이 훌륭한 어머니들조차 이따금 빠지는 그 불합리한 심정에 사로잡혀 있었다."], ["Want of exercise robs them of cheerfulness, and too much devotion to that idol of American women, the teapot, makes them feel as if they were all nerve and no muscle.", "운동 부족은 그들에게서 명랑함을 앗아 가고, 미국 여인들의 우상인 찻주전자에 지나치게 빠지다 보면 마치 온몸이 신경 덩어리일 뿐 근육이라곤 없는 것처럼 느껴지게 만든다."]], [["\"Yes,\" she would say, looking in the glass, \"I'm getting old and ugly; John doesn't find me interesting any longer, so he leaves his faded wife and goes to see his pretty neighbor, who has no incumbrances. Well, the babies love me; they don't care if I am thin and pale, and haven't time to crimp my hair; they are my comfort, and some day John will see what I've gladly sacrificed for them, won't he, my precious?\"", "\"그래.\" 그녀는 거울을 들여다보며 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"난 늙고 추해지고 있어. 존은 더 이상 날 흥미로워하지 않으니까, 빛바랜 아내를 두고 거치적거릴 게 없는 예쁜 이웃을 보러 가는 거야. 그래도 아기들은 날 사랑하지. 내가 마르고 창백하든, 머리 지질 틈이 없든 신경 안 쓰니까. 얘들은 내 위안이야. 그리고 언젠가는 존도 내가 얘들을 위해 무얼 기꺼이 희생했는지 알게 되겠지, 그렇지, 우리 아가?\""]], [["To which pathetic appeal Daisy would answer with a coo, or Demi with a crow, and Meg would put by her lamentations for a maternal revel, which soothed her solitude for the time being. But the pain increased as politics absorbed John, who was always running over to discuss interesting points with Scott, quite unconscious that Meg missed him. Not a word did she say, however, till her mother found her in tears one day, and insisted on knowing what the matter was, for Meg's drooping spirits had not escaped her observation.", "그 애처로운 하소연에 데이지는 옹알이로, 데미는 까르르 소리로 답해 주었고, 그러면 메그는 한탄을 접어 두고 모성의 향연에 빠져들었다. 그것이 그때만큼은 그녀의 외로움을 달래 주었다. 하지만 정치가 존을 사로잡으면서 고통은 깊어 갔다. 그는 흥미로운 화젯거리를 의논하러 늘 스콧네로 건너갔고, 메그가 자기를 그리워하는 줄은 전혀 알지 못했다. 그래도 그녀는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 어느 날 어머니가 그녀가 눈물짓는 걸 발견하고는, 대체 무슨 일이냐며 한사코 알려 들기 전까지는. 메그의 풀 죽은 기색이 어머니의 눈을 비껴가지는 못했던 것이다."]], [["\"I wouldn't tell any one except you, mother; but I really do need advice, for, if John goes on so much longer I might as well be widowed,\"", "\"엄마 말고는 아무한테도 말 안 할 거예요."], ["replied Mrs.", "하지만 정말 조언이 필요해서요."], ["Brooke, drying her tears on Daisy's bib, with an injured air.", "존이 이런 식으로 계속 나가다간, 저는 차라리 과부가 되는 편이 낫겠어요.\" 브룩 부인이 서러운 기색으로, 데이지의 턱받이로 눈물을 닦으며 대답했다."]], [["\"Goes on how, my dear?\"", "\"어떤 식으로 나가는데, 얘야?\""], ["asked her mother anxiously.", "어머니가 걱정스레 물었다."]], [["\"He's away all day, and at night, when I want to see him, he is continually going over to the Scotts'.", "\"온종일 나가 있고, 밤에 제가 보고 싶을 때는 끊임없이 스콧네로 건너가요."], ["It isn't fair that I should have the hardest work, and never any amusement.", "가장 힘든 일은 제가 다 떠맡고, 즐길 거리라곤 하나도 없다니 너무 불공평해요."], ["Men are very selfish, even the best of them.\"", "남자들은 정말 이기적이에요. 아무리 좋은 남자라도요.\""]], [["\"So are women; don't blame John till you see where you are wrong yourself.\"", "\"여자들도 마찬가지란다. 네가 어디서 잘못하고 있는지 살펴보기 전에는 존을 탓하지 마라.\""]], [["\"But it can't be right for him to neglect me.\"", "\"하지만 저를 소홀히 하는 건 옳을 리 없잖아요.\""]], [["\"Don't you neglect him?\"", "\"넌 그이를 소홀히 하지 않았고?\""]], [["\"Why, mother, I thought you'd take my part!\"", "\"어머, 엄마, 전 엄마가 제 편을 들어 주실 줄 알았어요!\""]], [["\"So I do, as far as sympathizing goes; but I think the fault is yours, Meg.\"", "\"그래, 마음으로 헤아려 주는 거라면 네 편이야. 하지만 잘못은 너한테 있는 것 같구나, 메그.\""]], [["\"I don't see how.\"", "\"어째서 그런지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["\"Let me show you.", "\"내가 짚어 주마."], ["Did John ever neglect you, as you call it, while you made it a point to give him your society of an evening, his only leisure time?\"", "네가 저녁마다—그이한테는 유일한 여가 시간이지—꼬박꼬박 곁을 지켜 주던 동안에는, 네 말마따나 존이 너를 소홀히 한 적이 있었니?\""]], [["\"No; but I can't do it now, with two babies to tend.\"", "\"아니요. 하지만 이제는 돌볼 아기가 둘이나 되는데 그럴 수가 없잖아요.\""]], [["\"I think you could, dear; and I think you ought.", "\"내 생각엔 할 수 있을 것 같구나, 얘야. 그리고 마땅히 그래야 한다고 생각해."], ["May I speak quite freely, and will you remember that it's mother who blames as well as mother who sympathizes?\"", "아주 솔직하게 말해도 되겠니? 그리고 마음으로 헤아려 주는 것도 엄마지만, 나무라는 것도 엄마라는 걸 기억해 주겠니?\""]], [["\"Indeed I will!", "\"그럼요!"], ["Speak to me as if I were little Meg again.", "제가 다시 어린 메그가 된 것처럼 말씀해 주세요."], ["I often feel as if I needed teaching more than ever since these babies look to me for everything.\"", "이 아기들이 모든 걸 저한테 기대게 되면서, 저는 그 어느 때보다 더 가르침이 필요한 것 같은 기분이 자주 들거든요.\""]], [["Meg drew her low chair beside her mother's, and, with a little interruption in either lap, the two women rocked and talked lovingly together, feeling that the tie of motherhood made them more one than ever.", "메그는 낮은 의자를 어머니 곁으로 끌어다 놓았고, 두 여인은 각자 무릎에 아기를 하나씩 안은 채 이따금 방해를 받아 가며, 다정하게 흔들고 이야기를 나누었다. 모성이라는 끈이 그 어느 때보다 둘을 하나로 묶어 주는 것을 느끼면서."]], [["\"You have only made the mistake that most young wives make,--forgotten your duty to your husband in your love for your children.", "\"넌 그저 젊은 아내들 대부분이 저지르는 실수를 했을 뿐이야. 아이들을 향한 사랑에 빠져 남편에 대한 도리를 잊은 거지."], ["A very natural and forgivable mistake, Meg, but one that had better be remedied before you take to different ways; for children should draw you nearer than ever, not separate you, as if they were all yours, and John had nothing to do but support them.", "아주 자연스럽고 용서받을 만한 실수란다, 메그. 하지만 네가 영영 다른 길로 들어서기 전에 바로잡는 편이 좋겠어."], ["I've seen it for some weeks, but have not spoken, feeling sure it would come right in time.\"", "아이들이란 마치 다 네 것이고 존은 그저 먹여 살리기만 하면 된다는 듯이 너희를 갈라놓는 게 아니라, 그 어느 때보다 너희를 가까이 끌어당겨 줘야 하는 거니까. 몇 주 전부터 지켜봤지만, 때가 되면 바로잡히려니 싶어 아무 말 안 했단다.\""]], [["\"I'm afraid it won't.", "\"바로잡힐 것 같지가 않아요."], ["If I ask him to stay, he'll think I'm jealous; and I wouldn't insult him by such an idea.", "제가 집에 있어 달라고 하면 그이는 제가 질투한다고 여길 거예요. 그런 생각으로 그이를 모욕하고 싶진 않아요."], ["He doesn't see that I want him, and I don't know how to tell him without words.\"", "그이는 제가 자길 원하는 걸 모르고, 저는 말하지 않고 어떻게 전해야 할지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["\"Make it so pleasant he won't want to go away.", "\"집을 너무 즐거운 곳으로 만들어서, 그이가 나가고 싶지 않게 하렴."], ["My dear, he's longing for his little home; but it isn't home without you, and you are always in the nursery.\"", "얘야, 그이는 자기의 작은 집을 그리워하고 있단다. 하지만 네가 없으면 집이 아니고, 너는 늘 육아실에만 있잖니.\""]], [["\"Oughtn't I to be there?\"", "\"제가 거기 있으면 안 되나요?\""]], [["\"Not all the time; too much confinement makes you nervous, and then you are unfitted for everything. Besides, you owe something to John as well as to the babies; don't neglect husband for children, don't shut him out of the nursery, but teach him how to help in it. His place is there as well as yours, and the children need him; let him feel that he has his part to do, and he will do it gladly and faithfully, and it will be better for you all.\"", "\"내내 있어선 안 되지. 너무 갇혀 지내면 신경이 곤두서고, 그러면 무엇 하나 제대로 할 수 없게 돼. 게다가 너는 아기들한테 못지않게 존한테도 갚아야 할 게 있단다. 아이들 때문에 남편을 소홀히 하지 말고, 그이를 육아실에서 밀어내지 말고, 거기서 거들 줄 알도록 가르치렴. 그이의 자리도 네 자리만큼이나 거기 있고, 아이들에게도 아빠가 필요해. 그이도 자기 몫이 있다는 걸 느끼게 해 줘. 그러면 기꺼이, 성실하게 해낼 거고, 너희 모두에게 더 나을 거야.\""]], [["\"You really think so, mother?\"", "\"정말 그렇게 생각하세요, 엄마?\""]], [["\"I know it, Meg, for I've tried it; and I seldom give advice unless I've proved its practicability.", "\"난 안단다, 메그. 직접 해 봤으니까."], ["When you and Jo were little, I went on just as you are, feeling as if I didn't do my duty unless I devoted myself wholly to you.", "그리고 엄마는 효과를 입증해 보지 않은 조언은 좀처럼 하지 않아. 너랑 조가 어렸을 때, 나도 꼭 지금 너처럼 했단다."], ["Poor father took to his books, after I had refused all offers of help, and left me to try my experiment alone.", "너희한테 온전히 매달리지 않으면 도리를 다하지 못하는 것 같았거든. 가엾은 네 아빠는, 내가 도와주겠다는 손길을 다 마다하자 책으로 파고들었고, 나는 혼자서 그 시도를 떠안게 됐지."], ["I struggled along as well as I could, but Jo was too much for me.", "나름대로 안간힘을 썼지만, 조는 내게 너무 벅찼어."], ["I nearly spoilt her by indulgence.", "응석을 받아 주다 그 애를 망칠 뻔했지."], ["You were poorly, and I worried about you till I fell sick myself.", "너는 몸이 약했고, 나는 너를 걱정하다 그만 내가 앓아눕고 말았어."], ["Then father came to the rescue, quietly managed everything, and made himself so helpful that I saw my mistake, and never have been able to get on without him since.", "그제야 네 아빠가 나서서, 모든 걸 조용히 꾸려 나가며 어찌나 큰 힘이 되어 주던지, 나는 내 잘못을 깨달았고, 그 후로는 그이 없이 살아갈 수 없게 되었단다."], ["That is the secret of our home happiness: he does not let business wean him from the little cares and duties that affect us all, and I try not to let domestic worries destroy my interest in his pursuits.", "그게 우리 집 행복의 비결이야. 그이는 일 때문에 우리 모두에게 영향을 미치는 자질구레한 보살핌과 의무에서 멀어지는 법이 없고, 나도 집안 걱정 때문에 그이의 일에 대한 관심을 저버리지 않으려 애쓰지."], ["Each do our part alone in many things, but at home we work together, always.\"", "많은 일들을 각자 따로 해내지만, 집에서는 늘 함께 일한단다.\""]], [["\"It is so, mother; and my great wish is to be to my husband and children what you have been to yours.", "\"맞아요, 엄마. 제 큰 소원은 엄마가 아빠랑 우리에게 그래 주신 것처럼, 저도 제 남편과 아이들에게 그런 사람이 되는 거예요."], ["Show me how; I'll do anything you say.\"", "어떻게 하면 되는지 알려 주세요. 엄마가 말씀하시는 건 뭐든 할게요.\""]], [["\"You always were my docile daughter.", "\"넌 늘 엄마 말 잘 듣는 딸이었지."], ["Well, dear, if I were you, I'd let John have more to do with the management of Demi, for the boy needs training, and it's none too soon to begin.", "그럼, 얘야, 내가 너라면, 존이 데미를 돌보는 일에 더 관여하게 하겠어. 그 애한텐 가르침이 필요한데, 지금 시작해도 결코 이른 게 아니거든."], ["Then I'd do what I have often proposed, let Hannah come and help you; she is a capital nurse, and you may trust the precious babies to her while you do more housework.", "그리고 내가 자주 권하던 대로, 해나를 불러 너를 돕게 하겠어. 해나는 일등 유모니까, 네가 집안일을 더 하는 동안 그 소중한 아기들을 믿고 맡길 수 있어."], ["You need the exercise, Hannah would enjoy the rest, and John would find his wife again.", "너한텐 운동이 필요하고, 해나는 그 쉬는 일을 즐거워할 거고, 존은 잃어버린 아내를 되찾게 되겠지. 바깥출입을 더 하렴."], ["Go out more; keep cheerful as well as busy, for you are the sunshine-maker of the family, and if you get dismal there is no fair weather.", "바쁘게 지내되 명랑하게도 지내야 해. 너는 이 집의 햇살을 만드는 사람이고, 네가 우울해지면 맑은 날이 없는 셈이니까."], ["Then I'd try to take an interest in whatever John likes,--talk with him, let him read to you, exchange ideas, and help each other in that way.", "그리고 존이 좋아하는 건 뭐든 관심을 가지려고 해 봐. 그이와 이야기를 나누고, 그이가 너한테 책을 읽어 주게 하고, 생각을 주고받고, 그렇게 서로 도우렴."], ["Don't shut yourself up in a bandbox because you are a woman, but understand what is going on, and educate yourself to take your part in the world's work, for it all affects you and yours.\"", "여자라는 이유로 작은 상자 속에 너를 가두지 말고, 세상이 어떻게 돌아가는지 알고, 세상일에서 네 몫을 해낼 수 있도록 너 자신을 갈고닦으렴. 그게 다 너와 네 식구에게 영향을 미치는 일이니까.\""]], [["\"John is so sensible, I'm afraid he will think I'm stupid if I ask questions about politics and things.\"", "\"존은 워낙 분별이 있어서, 제가 정치 같은 걸 물으면 절 멍청하다고 여길까 봐 걱정이에요.\""]], [["\"I don't believe he would; love covers a multitude of sins, and of whom could you ask more freely than of him?", "\"난 그러지 않을 거라고 믿어. 사랑은 숱한 허물을 덮어 주는 법이고, 그이만큼 네가 거리낌 없이 물을 수 있는 사람이 또 어디 있겠니?"], ["Try it, and see if he doesn't find your society far more agreeable than Mrs.", "한번 해 보렴."], ["Scott's suppers.\"", "그이가 스콧 부인의 저녁 식사보다 네 곁을 훨씬 더 좋아하게 되는지 어떤지 두고 보자.\""]], [["\"I will.", "\"그럴게요."], ["Poor John!", "가엾은 존!"], ["I'm afraid I _have_ neglected him sadly, but I thought I was right, and he never said anything.\"", "제가 그이를 너무 슬프게 소홀히 했나 봐요. 그래도 전 제가 옳은 줄 알았고, 그이는 한 번도 아무 말 안 했거든요.\""]], [["\"He tried not to be selfish, but he _has_ felt rather forlorn, I fancy. This is just the time, Meg, when young married people are apt to grow apart, and the very time when they ought to be most together; for the first tenderness soon wears off, unless care is taken to preserve it; and no time is so beautiful and precious to parents as the first years of the little lives given them to train. Don't let John be a stranger to the babies, for they will do more to keep him safe and happy in this world of trial and temptation than anything else, and through them you will learn to know and love one another as you should. Now, dear, good-by; think over mother's preachment, act upon it if it seems good, and God bless you all!\"", "\"그이는 이기적이지 않으려고 애썼지만, 꽤 쓸쓸했을 거야, 아마. 메그, 지금이 바로 그때란다. 젊은 부부가 서로 멀어지기 쉬운 때이자, 그래서 가장 함께해야 할 때이지. 첫 다정함은 정성껏 지켜 내지 않으면 금세 닳아 없어지고 마니까. 그리고 부모에게는 길러 내라고 주어진 그 작은 생명들의 첫 몇 해만큼 아름답고 소중한 때가 없단다. 존이 아기들한테 낯선 사람이 되게 하지 마. 이 시험과 유혹의 세상에서 아이들만큼 그이를 안전하고 행복하게 지켜 줄 건 또 없거든. 그리고 아이들을 통해 너희 둘은 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 서로를 알고 사랑하는 법을 배우게 될 거야. 자, 얘야, 이제 가 봐야겠다. 엄마의 잔소리를 잘 곱씹어 보고, 좋을 것 같으면 그대로 해 보렴. 그리고 너희 모두에게 하느님의 축복이 함께하기를!\""]], [["Meg did think it over, found it good, and acted upon it, though the first attempt was not made exactly as she planned to have it.", "메그는 그 말을 곱씹어 보았고, 좋은 조언이라 여겨 실행에 옮겼다. 비록 첫 시도가 그녀가 계획한 그대로 이루어지지는 않았지만 말이다."], ["Of course the children tyrannized over her, and ruled the house as soon as they found out that kicking and squalling brought them whatever they wanted.", "물론 아이들은 그녀에게 횡포를 부렸고, 발버둥 치고 악을 쓰면 원하는 건 뭐든 얻어 낼 수 있다는 걸 알게 되자마자 온 집안을 쥐락펴락했다."], ["Mamma was an abject slave to their caprices, but papa was not so easily subjugated, and occasionally afflicted his tender spouse by an attempt at paternal discipline with his obstreperous son.", "엄마는 그들의 변덕에 굽실거리는 노예였지만, 아빠는 그리 쉽게 굴복하지 않았다. 그래서 이따금 떼쓰는 아들을 아버지답게 다잡으려 들어, 다정한 아내를 괴롭게 했다."], ["For Demi inherited a trifle of his sire's firmness of character,--we won't call it obstinacy,--and when he made up his little mind to have or to do anything, all the king's horses and all the king's men could not change that pertinacious little mind.", "데미는 제 아비의 굳은 성품을—고집이라고는 부르지 말자—조금 물려받았던 터라, 일단 작은 마음을 정하고 무얼 가지거나 하겠다고 들면, 임금님의 말도, 임금님의 신하들도 그 옹고집의 작은 마음을 바꿀 수 없었다. 엄마는 우리 귀한 애가 편견을 이겨 내도록 가르치기엔 아직 너무 어리다고 여겼지만, 아빠는 순종을 배우는 데 이른 때란 결코 없다고 믿었다."], ["Mamma thought the dear too young to be taught to conquer his prejudices, but papa believed that it never was too soon to learn obedience; so Master Demi early discovered that when he undertook to \"wrastle\" with \"parpar,\"", "그리하여 데미 도련님은, \"아빠\"와 \"씨름\"을 하려 들 때면 늘 자기가 지고 만다는 걸 일찍이 깨달았다."], ["he always got the worst of it; yet, like the Englishman, Baby respected the man who conquered him, and loved the father whose grave \"No, no,\" was more impressive than all mamma's love-pats.", "그러면서도 영국 사람처럼, 우리 아기는 자기를 이긴 사람을 존경했고, 엄마의 그 어떤 사랑의 토닥임보다도 그 진중한 \"안 돼, 안 돼\"가 더 인상적인 아빠를 사랑했다."]], [["A few days after the talk with her mother, Meg resolved to try a social evening with John; so she ordered a nice supper, set the parlor in order, dressed herself prettily, and put the children to bed early, that nothing should interfere with her experiment. But, unfortunately, Demi's most unconquerable prejudice was against going to bed, and that night he decided to go on a rampage; so poor Meg sung and rocked, told stories and tried every sleep-provoking wile she could devise, but all in vain, the big eyes wouldn't shut; and long after Daisy had gone to byelow, like the chubby little bunch of good-nature she was, naughty Demi lay staring at the light, with the most discouragingly wide-awake expression of countenance.", "어머니와 이야기를 나눈 며칠 뒤, 메그는 존과 함께 단란한 저녁 한때를 보내 보기로 마음먹었다. 그래서 맛깔난 저녁을 차리고, 거실을 정돈하고, 예쁘게 차려입고, 아이들을 일찍 재워, 자기 시도에 아무것도 끼어들지 못하게 했다. 하지만 공교롭게도 데미의 가장 꺾기 힘든 고집은 잠자리에 드는 것에 대한 거부였고, 그날 밤 그 애는 한바탕 난리를 치기로 작정했다. 그래서 가엾은 메그는 노래를 부르고 흔들어 주고, 이야기를 들려주고, 잠을 부를 만한 온갖 꾀를 다 부려 보았지만, 죄다 허사였다. 그 큰 눈은 도무지 감기지 않았다. 그리고 천성이 좋은, 통통하고 자그마한 덩어리인 데이지가 곤히 잠든 지 한참이 지나도록, 짓궂은 데미는 더없이 맥 빠지게 말짱한 표정으로 불빛을 빤히 쳐다보며 누워 있었다."]], [["\"Will Demi lie still like a good boy, while mamma runs down and gives poor papa his tea?\"", "\"데미가 착한 아이답게 가만히 누워 있을래? 엄마가 잠깐 내려가서 가엾은 아빠한테 차를 좀 갖다 드리는 동안에 말이야.\""], ["asked Meg, as the hall-door softly closed, and the well-known step went tiptoeing into the dining-room.", "현관문이 살며시 닫히고, 귀에 익은 발소리가 발끝으로 식당으로 들어가자 메그가 물었다."]], [["\"Me has tea!\"", "\"나도 차!\""], ["said Demi, preparing to join in the revel.", "데미가 그 잔치에 끼어들 채비를 하며 말했다."]], [["\"No; but I'll save you some little cakies for breakfast, if you'll go bye-by like Daisy.", "\"안 돼. 그래도 데이지처럼 코 자면, 아침에 먹게 작은 과자 좀 남겨 둘게."], ["Will you, lovey?\"", "그렇게 할래, 우리 아가?\""]], [["\"Iss!\"", "\"응!\""], ["and Demi shut his eyes tight, as if to catch sleep and hurry the desired day.", "데미가 두 눈을 질끈 감았다. 마치 잠을 붙들어 그 바라는 아침을 어서 오게 하려는 듯이."]], [["Taking advantage of the propitious moment, Meg slipped away, and ran down to greet her husband with a smiling face, and the little blue bow in her hair which was his especial admiration.", "이 좋은 틈을 타, 메그는 살그머니 빠져나와 아래층으로 내려가, 미소 띤 얼굴로 남편을 맞았다. 머리에는 그가 유독 좋아하는 그 작은 파란 리본을 매고서."], ["He saw it at once, and said, with pleased surprise,--", "그는 그것을 단번에 알아보고, 반갑고 놀란 기색으로 말했다."]], [["\"Why, little mother, how gay we are to-night.", "\"아니, 우리 작은 엄마, 오늘 밤 우리 참 화사한데."], ["Do you expect company?\"", "손님이라도 오시나?\""]], [["\"Only you, dear.\"", "\"당신뿐이에요, 여보.\""]], [["\"Is it a birthday, anniversary, or anything?\"", "\"생일이야, 기념일이야, 아니면 무슨 날이야?\""]], [["\"No; I'm tired of being a dowdy, so I dressed up as a change.", "\"아니요. 칠칠치 못한 모습이 지겨워서, 기분 전환 삼아 차려입어 봤어요."], ["You always make yourself nice for table, no matter how tired you are; so why shouldn't I when I have the time?\"", "당신은 아무리 피곤해도 식사 자리에선 늘 단정하게 차려입잖아요. 그러니 시간 있을 때 저라고 그러면 안 될 것 없죠.\""]], [["\"I do it out of respect to you, my dear,\"", "\"난 당신에 대한 예의로 그러는 거요, 여보.\""], ["said old-fashioned John.", "구식인 존이 말했다."]], [["\"Ditto, ditto, Mr. Brooke,\"", "\"저도 마찬가지예요, 똑같아요, 브룩 씨.\""], ["laughed Meg, looking young and pretty again, as she nodded to him over the teapot.", "메그가 웃으며, 찻주전자 너머로 그에게 고개를 끄덕였다. 다시 젊고 예뻐 보였다."]], [["\"Well, it's altogether delightful, and like old times.", "\"음, 더없이 즐겁군. 꼭 옛날 같아."], ["This tastes right.", "이거 맛이 제대로네."], ["I drink your health, dear.\"", "당신을 위해 건배하리다, 여보.\" 그러고 존은 차분하고 황홀한 기색으로 차를 한 모금 들이켰다."], ["And John sipped his tea with an air of reposeful rapture, which was of very short duration, however; for, as he put down his cup, the door-handle rattled mysteriously, and a little voice was heard, saying impatiently,--", "하지만 그 황홀함은 무척 짧았다. 그가 잔을 내려놓는 순간, 문손잡이가 알 수 없이 덜그럭거리더니, 작은 목소리가 조바심을 내며 들려왔던 것이다."]], [["\"Opy doy; me's tummin!\"", "\"무이 여어, 내가 갈 꼬야!\""]], [["\"It's that naughty boy.", "\"저 짓궂은 녀석."], ["I told him to go to sleep alone, and here he is, downstairs, getting his death a-cold pattering over that canvas,\"", "혼자 자라고 일러뒀더니, 글쎄 저렇게 아래층까지 내려와서, 저 천 깔개 위를 발발거리다 감기로 죽을 작정인가 봐요.\""], ["said Meg, answering the call.", "메그가 그 부름에 답하며 말했다."]], [["\"Mornin' now,\"", "\"이제 아침이야.\""], ["announced Demi, in a joyful tone, as he entered, with his long night-gown gracefully festooned over his arm, and every curl bobbing gayly as he pranced about the table, eying the \"cakies\"", "데미가 즐거운 목소리로 알리며 들어왔다."], ["with loving glances.", "긴 잠옷을 팔에 우아하게 늘어뜨리고, 식탁 주위를 깡충거리며 곱슬머리가 죄다 흥겹게 까딱이는 채로, \"과자\"를 애정 어린 눈길로 곁눈질하면서."]], [["\"No, it isn't morning yet.", "\"아니야, 아직 아침 아니야."], ["You must go to bed, and not trouble poor mamma; then you can have the little cake with sugar on it.\"", "잠자리에 가야지, 가엾은 엄마 괴롭히지 말고. 그러면 위에 설탕 얹은 작은 케이크를 줄게.\""]], [["\"Me loves parpar,\"", "\"나 아빠 조아.\""], ["said the artful one, preparing to climb the paternal knee, and revel in forbidden joys.", "영악한 녀석이 아버지 무릎에 기어올라 금지된 즐거움에 빠질 채비를 하며 말했다."], ["But John shook his head, and said to Meg,--", "하지만 존은 고개를 저으며 메그에게 말했다."]], [["\"If you told him to stay up there, and go to sleep alone, make him do it, or he will never learn to mind you.\"", "\"당신이 저 애한테 거기 올라가서 혼자 자라고 일렀으면, 그렇게 하게 만들어요. 안 그러면 영영 당신 말을 듣는 법을 못 배울 테니까.\""]], [["\"Yes, of course.", "\"그래요, 맞아요."], ["Come, Demi;\"", "자, 데미.\""], ["and Meg led her son away, feeling a strong desire to spank the little marplot who hopped beside her, laboring under the delusion that the bribe was to be administered as soon as they reached the nursery.", "메그가 아들을 데리고 가는데, 곁에서 깡충거리는 그 훼방꾼 꼬마를 한 대 찰싹 때려 주고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았다. 데미는 육아실에 닿기만 하면 약속한 미끼를 받게 되리라는 착각에 부풀어 있었다."]], [["Nor was he disappointed; for that short-sighted woman actually gave him a lump of sugar, tucked him into his bed, and forbade any more promenades till morning.", "그리고 그 기대는 빗나가지 않았다. 그 앞 못 보는 여인이 정말로 그에게 설탕 한 덩이를 주고는, 이불을 덮어 재우며 아침까지는 더 이상 나돌아 다니지 말라고 일렀으니까."]], [["\"Iss!\"", "\"응!\""], ["said Demi the perjured, blissfully sucking his sugar, and regarding his first attempt as eminently successful.", "위증한 데미가 황홀하게 설탕을 빨며, 자기의 첫 시도를 대단한 성공으로 여기며 대답했다."]], [["Meg returned to her place, and supper was progressing pleasantly, when the little ghost walked again, and exposed the maternal delinquencies by boldly demanding,--", "메그는 제자리로 돌아왔고, 저녁 식사가 기분 좋게 이어지고 있었다. 그런데 그 작은 유령이 다시 나타나, 대담하게 요구하며 엄마의 잘못을 까발렸다."]], [["\"More sudar, marmar.\"", "\"엄마, 어얼탕 더.\""]], [["\"Now this won't do,\"", "\"이건 안 되겠어.\""], ["said John, hardening his heart against the engaging little sinner.", "존이 그 귀여운 작은 죄인에게 마음을 모질게 먹으며 말했다."], ["\"We shall never know any peace till that child learns to go to bed properly.", "\"저 애가 제대로 잠자리에 드는 법을 배우기 전엔 우리에게 평화란 없을 거요."], ["You have made a slave of yourself long enough; give him one lesson, and then there will be an end of it.", "당신은 충분히 오래 자기 자신을 노예로 만들었어. 한 번만 가르치면, 그걸로 끝날 거요."], ["Put him in his bed and leave him, Meg.\"", "침대에 눕히고 그냥 두고 와요, 메그.\""]], [["\"He won't stay there; he never does, unless I sit by him.\"", "\"저 애는 거기 안 있을 거예요. 제가 곁에 앉아 있지 않으면 절대 안 그러는걸요.\""]], [["\"I'll manage him.", "\"내가 다잡지."], ["Demi, go upstairs, and get into your bed, as mamma bids you.\"", "데미, 위층으로 올라가서, 엄마가 시키는 대로 침대에 들어가.\""]], [["\"S'ant!\"", "\"시여!\""], ["replied the young rebel, helping himself to the coveted \"cakie,\" and beginning to eat the same with calm audacity.", "어린 반항아가 그 탐내던 \"과자\"를 집어 들고는, 태연하고 뻔뻔하게 그것을 먹기 시작하며 대답했다."]], [["\"You must never say that to papa; I shall carry you if you don't go yourself.\"", "\"아빠한테는 절대 그렇게 말하면 안 돼. 네가 스스로 안 가면 내가 안고 가겠다.\""]], [["\"Go 'way; me don't love parpar;\"", "\"저리 가. 나 아빠 안 조아.\""], ["and Demi retired to his mother's skirts for protection.", "데미가 보호받으려고 엄마 치맛자락 뒤로 물러났다."]], [["But even that refuge proved unavailing, for he was delivered over to the enemy, with a \"Be gentle with him, John,\"", "하지만 그 피난처마저 소용없었다. \"살살 다뤄요, 존\"이라는 말과 함께 그가 적에게 넘겨졌으니까."], ["which struck the culprit with dismay; for when mamma deserted him, then the judgment-day was at hand.", "그 말은 어린 죄인을 경악에 빠뜨렸다. 엄마가 자기를 버린 마당이니, 이제 심판의 날이 코앞이었던 것이다."], ["Bereft of his cake, defrauded of his frolic, and borne away by a strong hand to that detested bed, poor Demi could not restrain his wrath, but openly defied papa, and kicked and screamed lustily all the way upstairs.", "케이크를 빼앗기고, 장난도 빼앗기고, 억센 손에 안겨 그 끔찍한 침대로 실려 가게 되자, 가엾은 데미는 분을 참지 못하고 드러내 놓고 아빠에게 대들며, 위층으로 올라가는 내내 발버둥 치고 악을 써 댔다."], ["The minute he was put into bed on one side, he rolled out on the other, and made for the door, only to be ignominiously caught up by the tail of his little toga, and put back again, which lively performance was kept up till the young man's strength gave out, when he devoted himself to roaring at the top of his voice.", "한쪽으로 침대에 눕혀지기가 무섭게 반대쪽으로 굴러 나와 문으로 내달았다가, 작은 잠옷 자락을 잡혀 보기 좋게 붙들려 다시 눕혀졌다."], ["This vocal exercise usually conquered Meg; but John sat as unmoved as the post which is popularly believed to be deaf.", "이 활기찬 공연은 도련님의 기운이 다할 때까지 이어졌고, 그러자 그는 목청껏 울부짖는 데 온 힘을 쏟았다."], ["No coaxing, no sugar, no lullaby, no story; even the light was put out, and only the red glow of the fire enlivened the \"big dark\"", "이 성악 연습은 으레 메그를 굴복시키곤 했다. 하지만 존은 흔히들 귀머거리라고 여기는 기둥처럼 꿈쩍도 하지 않고 앉아 있었다."], ["which Demi regarded with curiosity rather than fear.", "어르는 말도, 설탕도, 자장가도, 이야기도 없었다."], ["This new order of things disgusted him, and he howled dismally for \"marmar,\"", "심지어 불까지 꺼지고, 난롯불의 붉은 빛만이 그 \"커다란 어둠\"을 밝힐 뿐이었는데, 데미는 그것을 두려움보다는 호기심으로 바라보았다."], ["as his angry passions subsided, and recollections of his tender bondwoman returned to the captive autocrat.", "이 새로운 질서가 그는 못마땅해서, 분에 찬 격정이 가라앉고 자기의 그 다정한 시녀에 대한 기억이 붙들린 폭군에게 되돌아오자, \"엄마\"를 부르며 처량하게 울부짖었다."], ["The plaintive wail which succeeded the passionate roar went to Meg's heart, and she ran up to say beseechingly,--", "그 격렬한 울부짖음에 뒤이은 애처로운 흐느낌이 메그의 가슴을 찔렀고, 그녀는 위층으로 달려 올라가 사정하듯 말했다."]], [["\"Let me stay with him; he'll be good, now, John.\"", "\"저 애 곁에 있게 해 줘요. 이제 착하게 굴 거예요, 존.\""]], [["\"No, my dear, I've told him he must go to sleep, as you bid him; and he must, if I stay here all night.\"", "\"안 돼, 여보. 난 저 애한테 당신이 시키는 대로 자야 한다고 일렀고, 내가 밤새도록 여기 있는 한이 있어도 그렇게 해야 해.\""]], [["\"But he'll cry himself sick,\"", "\"하지만 울다가 병이라도 나면 어떡해요.\""], ["pleaded Meg, reproaching herself for deserting her boy.", "메그가 아들을 버려둔 자신을 책망하며 애원했다."]], [["\"No, he won't, he's so tired he will soon drop off, and then the matter is settled; for he will understand that he has got to mind. Don't interfere; I'll manage him.\"", "\"아니, 안 그래. 너무 지쳐서 곧 곯아떨어질 거고, 그러면 일이 매듭지어지는 거지. 자기가 말을 들어야 한다는 걸 깨닫게 될 테니까. 끼어들지 마요. 내가 다잡을 테니.\""]], [["\"He's my child, and I can't have his spirit broken by harshness.\"", "\"저 앤 제 아이고, 전 저 애의 기개가 모진 매질에 꺾이는 걸 두고 볼 수 없어요.\""]], [["\"He's my child, and I won't have his temper spoilt by indulgence.", "\"저 앤 내 아이고, 난 저 애의 성품이 응석으로 망가지는 걸 두고 보지 않겠소."], ["Go down, my dear, and leave the boy to me.\"", "내려가요, 여보. 저 앤 나한테 맡기고.\""]], [["When John spoke in that masterful tone, Meg always obeyed, and never regretted her docility.", "존이 그 위엄 있는 어조로 말할 때면 메그는 늘 따랐고, 자기가 고분고분했던 걸 후회한 적이 없었다."]], [["\"Please let me kiss him once, John?\"", "\"한 번만 입 맞추게 해 줘요, 존?\""]], [["\"Certainly.", "\"물론이지."], ["Demi, say 'good-night'", "데미, 엄마한테 '잘 자'"], ["to mamma, and let her go and rest, for she is very tired with taking care of you all day.\"", "인사하고, 가서 쉬게 해 드려. 엄마는 온종일 너희 보살피느라 무척 지쳤으니까.\""]], [["Meg always insisted upon it that the kiss won the victory; for after it was given, Demi sobbed more quietly, and lay quite still at the bottom of the bed, whither he had wriggled in his anguish of mind.", "메그는 늘, 그 입맞춤이 승리를 가져왔다고 우겼다. 입맞춤을 해 주고 나니, 데미가 한결 잠잠하게 흐느끼며, 마음의 괴로움에 몸부림치다 기어든 침대 발치에 가만히 누웠으니까."]], [["\"Poor little man, he's worn out with sleep and crying.", "'가엾은 녀석, 잠이랑 울음에 진이 다 빠졌군."], ["I'll cover him up, and then go and set Meg's heart at rest,\"", "이불 덮어 주고, 가서 메그 마음을 놓이게 해 줘야지.'"], ["thought John, creeping to the bedside, hoping to find his rebellious heir asleep.", "존은 그 반항하던 후계자가 잠들었기를 바라며, 살그머니 침대 곁으로 다가가며 생각했다."]], [["But he wasn't; for the moment his father peeped at him, Demi's eyes opened, his little chin began to quiver, and he put up his arms, saying, with a penitent hiccough, \"Me's dood, now.\"", "하지만 그는 자고 있지 않았다. 아버지가 들여다보는 순간 데미의 두 눈이 떠졌고, 작은 턱이 떨리기 시작하더니, 두 팔을 들어 올리며 뉘우치는 딸꾹질과 함께 말했다. \"나 이제 차캐.\""]], [["Sitting on the stairs, outside, Meg wondered at the long silence which followed the uproar; and, after imagining all sorts of impossible accidents, she slipped into the room, to set her fears at rest. Demi lay fast asleep; not in his usual spread-eagle attitude, but in a subdued bunch, cuddled close in the circle of his father's arm and holding his father's finger, as if he felt that justice was tempered with mercy, and had gone to sleep a sadder and a wiser baby. So held, John had waited with womanly patience till the little hand relaxed its hold; and, while waiting, had fallen asleep, more tired by that tussle with his son than with his whole day's work.", "밖에서 계단에 앉아 있던 메그는, 그 소란 뒤에 이어진 긴 침묵에 의아해했다. 그러다 온갖 있을 법하지 않은 사고들을 다 상상하고는, 두려움을 가라앉히려 방 안으로 살며시 들어갔다. 데미는 곤히 잠들어 있었다. 평소처럼 팔다리를 활짝 벌린 모양새가 아니라, 얌전히 웅크린 채, 아버지의 팔이 두른 원 안에 바싹 파고들어 아버지의 손가락을 꼭 쥐고 있었다. 마치 정의에 자비가 섞여 있음을 느끼고, 한결 슬프지만 한결 지혜로워진 아기가 되어 잠든 듯했다. 그렇게 손가락을 붙들린 채, 존은 그 작은 손이 쥔 힘을 풀 때까지 여인 같은 인내심으로 기다리다가, 기다리던 끝에 그만 잠들고 말았다. 아들과의 그 씨름이 온종일의 일보다 더 그를 지치게 한 것이었다."]], [["As Meg stood watching the two faces on the pillow, she smiled to herself, and then slipped away again, saying, in a satisfied tone,--", "베개 위의 두 얼굴을 지켜보며 서 있던 메그는 혼자 미소 지었다. 그러고는 만족스러운 어조로 이렇게 말하며 다시 살그머니 빠져나갔다."]], [["\"I never need fear that John will be too harsh with my babies: he _does_ know how to manage them, and will be a great help, for Demi _is_ getting too much for me.\"", "\"존이 우리 아기들한테 너무 모질까 봐 걱정할 일은 없겠어. 정말 다잡을 줄 아는구나. 큰 도움이 되겠어. 데미가 나한테는 너무 벅차지고 있으니까.\""]], [["When John came down at last, expecting to find a pensive or reproachful wife, he was agreeably surprised to find Meg placidly trimming a bonnet, and to be greeted with the request to read something about the election, if he was not too tired.", "마침내 존이 내려왔을 때, 그는 시무룩하거나 원망 어린 아내를 보게 되리라 짐작했지만, 메그가 차분하게 보닛을 손질하고 있는 모습을 보고 기분 좋게 놀랐다. 게다가 너무 피곤하지 않다면 선거에 관한 기사를 좀 읽어 달라는 부탁까지 받았다."], ["John saw in a minute that a revolution of some kind was going on, but wisely asked no questions, knowing that Meg was such a transparent little person, she couldn't keep a secret to save her life, and therefore the clew would soon appear.", "존은 어떤 종류의 혁명 같은 것이 일어나고 있음을 단번에 알아차렸지만, 메그가 워낙 속이 훤히 들여다보이는 사람이라 목숨이 걸려도 비밀을 못 지킨다는 걸 알았기에, 머지않아 단서가 드러나리라 여기고 현명하게 아무것도 묻지 않았다."], ["He read a long debate with the most amiable readiness, and then explained it in his most lucid manner, while Meg tried to look deeply interested, to ask intelligent questions, and keep her thoughts from wandering from the state of the nation to the state of her bonnet.", "그는 더없이 상냥하고 선뜻한 태도로 긴 토론 기사를 읽어 준 다음, 자기 딴에는 가장 명료한 방식으로 그것을 설명했다. 그러는 동안 메그는 깊은 관심이 있는 척하려고, 똑똑한 질문을 던지려고, 그리고 생각이 나라의 형편에서 자기 보닛의 형편으로 새어 나가지 않도록 애썼다."], ["In her secret soul, however, she decided that politics were as bad as mathematics, and that the mission of politicians seemed to be calling each other names; but she kept these feminine ideas to herself, and when John paused, shook her head, and said with what she thought diplomatic ambiguity,--", "하지만 마음속 깊은 곳에서는 정치가 수학만큼이나 고약하고, 정치인들의 사명이란 서로에게 욕을 퍼붓는 일인 듯하다고 결론지었다. 그래도 메그는 이런 여자다운 생각을 혼자만 간직했고, 존이 잠시 멈추자 고개를 저으며 스스로 외교적인 애매함이라 여긴 말투로 이렇게 말했다."]], [["\"Well, I really don't see what we are coming to.\"", "\"음, 정말이지 우리가 어떻게 되려는 건지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["John laughed, and watched her for a minute, as she poised a pretty little preparation of lace and flowers on her hand, and regarded it with the genuine interest which his harangue had failed to waken.", "존은 웃음을 터뜨리고는, 그녀가 레이스와 꽃으로 곱게 꾸민 작은 물건을 손 위에 올려놓고서 자기의 일장 연설로는 끝내 불러일으키지 못했던 진정한 관심을 담아 그것을 바라보는 모습을 잠시 지켜보았다."]], [["\"She is trying to like politics for my sake, so I'll try and like millinery for hers, that's only fair,\"", "\"이 사람은 나를 위해서 정치를 좋아해 보려고 애쓰는구나. 그러니 나도 이 사람을 위해서 모자 만드는 일을 좋아해 보려고 애써야지, 그게 공평하니까.\""], ["thought John the Just, adding aloud,--", "공정한 존은 이렇게 생각하고는 소리 내어 덧붙였다."]], [["\"That's very pretty; is it what you call a breakfast-cap?\"", "\"아주 예쁜데, 이게 당신이 말하는 아침 모자라는 거요?\""]], [["\"My dear man, it's a bonnet!", "\"여보, 이건 보닛이에요!"], ["My very best go-to-concert-and-theatre bonnet.\"", "음악회랑 극장 갈 때 쓰는 제일 좋은 보닛이라고요.\""]], [["\"I beg your pardon; it was so small, I naturally mistook it for one of the fly-away things you sometimes wear.", "\"미안하오. 워낙 작아서 당신이 가끔 쓰는 그 하늘하늘한 것들 가운데 하나인 줄 알았지."], ["How do you keep it on?\"", "이건 어떻게 쓰고 있는 거요?\""]], [["\"These bits of lace are fastened under the chin with a rosebud, so;\"", "\"이 레이스 자락을 턱 밑에서 장미 봉오리로 여미는 거예요, 이렇게요.\""], ["and Meg illustrated by putting on the bonnet, and regarding him with an air of calm satisfaction that was irresistible.", "그러면서 메그는 보닛을 직접 써 보이고는, 거역할 수 없을 만큼 차분히 만족스러운 표정으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["\"It's a love of a bonnet, but I prefer the face inside, for it looks young and happy again,\"", "\"사랑스러운 보닛이군. 하지만 난 그 안에 든 얼굴이 더 좋아."], ["and John kissed the smiling face, to the great detriment of the rosebud under the chin.", "다시 젊고 행복해 보이니까.\" 그러면서 존은 미소 짓는 그 얼굴에 입을 맞추었고, 그 바람에 턱 밑의 장미 봉오리는 큰 손상을 입고 말았다."]], [["\"I'm glad you like it, for I want you to take me to one of the new concerts some night; I really need some music to put me in tune.", "\"마음에 드신다니 기뻐요. 언제 밤에 새로 하는 음악회 가운데 하나에 저를 데려가 주셨으면 해서요."], ["Will you, please?\"", "정말이지 마음을 가다듬으려면 음악이 좀 필요하거든요. 그래 주실 거죠, 네?\""]], [["\"Of course I will, with all my heart, or anywhere else you like.", "\"물론이지, 기꺼이. 음악회든 당신이 가고 싶은 어디든."], ["You have been shut up so long, it will do you no end of good, and I shall enjoy it, of all things.", "너무 오래 집 안에만 갇혀 있었으니, 당신한테 더없이 좋을 거요. 나도 무엇보다 즐거울 테고."], ["What put it into your head, little mother?\"", "그런데 어쩌다 그런 생각을 하게 됐소, 작은 엄마?\""]], [["\"Well, I had a talk with Marmee the other day, and told her how nervous and cross and out of sorts I felt, and she said I needed change and less care; so Hannah is to help me with the children, and I'm to see to things about the house more, and now and then have a little fun, just to keep me from getting to be a fidgety, broken-down old woman before my time. It's only an experiment, John, and I want to try it for your sake as much as for mine, because I've neglected you shamefully lately, and I'm going to make home what it used to be, if I can. You don't object, I hope?\"", "\"음, 며칠 전에 엄마하고 이야기를 나눴어요. 제가 얼마나 신경이 곤두서고 짜증 나고 기분이 엉망인지 말씀드렸더니, 변화하고 부담을 좀 덜라고 하시더라고요. 그래서 해나가 아이들 돌보는 걸 도와주기로 했고, 저는 집안일을 좀 더 챙기고, 이따금 소소하게 즐기기도 하면서, 제 나이보다 일찍 안절부절못하고 시들어 버린 노파가 되지 않도록 하려는 거예요. 그저 시험 삼아 해 보는 거예요, 존. 제 자신을 위해서이기도 하지만 당신을 위해서이기도 해요. 요즘 제가 부끄러울 만큼 당신을 소홀히 했으니까요. 그래서 할 수만 있다면 우리 집을 예전의 그 모습으로 되돌려 놓을 작정이에요. 반대하지 않으시죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["Never mind what John said, or what a very narrow escape the little bonnet had from utter ruin; all that we have any business to know, is that John did _not_ appear to object, judging from the changes which gradually took place in the house and its inmates.", "존이 무어라 말했는지, 그 작은 보닛이 완전히 망가질 위기를 얼마나 아슬아슬하게 모면했는지는 굳이 따질 것 없다. 우리가 알아 둘 만한 것이라곤, 집과 그 식구들에게 차츰 일어난 변화로 미루어 볼 때 존이 반대하는 기색을 전혀 보이지 않았다는 사실뿐이다."], ["It was not all Paradise by any means, but every one was better for the division of labor system; the children throve under the paternal rule, for accurate, steadfast John brought order and obedience into Babydom, while Meg recovered her spirits and composed her nerves by plenty of wholesome exercise, a little pleasure, and much confidential conversation with her sensible husband.", "결코 모든 것이 낙원 같지는 않았지만, 분업 체계 덕분에 식구마다 한결 나아졌다. 정확하고 한결같은 존이 아기 나라에 질서와 순종을 가져온 덕에 아이들은 아버지의 다스림 아래 무럭무럭 자랐고, 메그는 건강에 좋은 운동을 충분히 하고 소소한 즐거움을 누리고 분별 있는 남편과 흉허물 없는 대화를 많이 나누면서 활기를 되찾고 신경을 가라앉혔다."], ["Home grew home-like again, and John had no wish to leave it, unless he took Meg with him.", "집은 다시 집다워졌고, 존은 메그를 데려가는 경우가 아니면 집을 떠나고 싶어 하지 않았다."], ["The Scotts came to the Brookes' now, and every one found the little house a cheerful place, full of happiness, content, and family love.", "이제 스콧 부부가 브룩 부부의 집을 찾아왔고, 누구나 이 작은 집을 행복과 만족과 가족애가 가득한 즐거운 곳으로 여겼다."], ["Even gay Sallie Moffatt liked to go there.", "명랑한 샐리 모팻조차 그곳에 가기를 좋아했다."], ["\"It is always so quiet and pleasant here; it does me good, Meg,\"", "\"여긴 늘 어쩌면 이렇게 조용하고 기분이 좋은지 몰라. 나한테 참 좋아, 메그.\""], ["she used to say, looking about her with wistful eyes, as if trying to discover the charm, that she might use it in her great house, full of splendid loneliness; for there were no riotous, sunny-faced babies there, and Ned lived in a world of his own, where there was no place for her.", "그녀는 마치 화려한 외로움으로 가득한 자기 큰 집에서도 써먹을 수 있도록 그 매력의 정체를 찾아내려는 듯, 동경 어린 눈으로 주위를 둘러보며 그렇게 말하곤 했다. 그녀의 집에는 떠들썩하게 뛰노는 햇살 같은 아기들이 없었고, 네드는 그녀가 들어설 자리 없는 자기만의 세계 속에서 살았기 때문이다."]], [["This household happiness did not come all at once, but John and Meg had found the key to it, and each year of married life taught them how to use it, unlocking the treasuries of real home-love and mutual helpfulness, which the poorest may possess, and the richest cannot buy.", "이 가정의 행복은 한꺼번에 찾아온 것이 아니었다. 다만 존과 메그는 그 열쇠를 찾아냈고, 결혼 생활의 한 해 한 해가 그것을 어떻게 쓰는지를 두 사람에게 가르쳐 주었다."], ["This is the sort of shelf on which young wives and mothers may consent to be laid, safe from the restless fret and fever of the world, finding loyal lovers in the little sons and daughters who cling to them, undaunted by sorrow, poverty, or age; walking side by side, through fair and stormy weather, with a faithful friend, who is, in the true sense of the good old Saxon word, the \"house-band,\"", "그리하여 가장 가난한 이도 가질 수 있고 가장 부유한 이도 살 수 없는, 참된 가정의 사랑과 서로 돕는 마음이라는 보물 창고를 열어 갔다. 이것이야말로 젊은 아내와 어머니들이 기꺼이 몸을 누일 만한 선반이다."], ["and learning, as Meg learned, that a woman's happiest kingdom is home, her highest honor the art of ruling it, not as a queen, but a wise wife and mother.", "세상의 끊임없는 근심과 열병으로부터 안전하고, 자기에게 매달리는 어린 아들딸 속에서 슬픔과 가난과 나이에도 굴하지 않는 충실한 연인을 찾으며, 맑은 날에도 폭풍우 치는 날에도 충직한 벗과 나란히 걸어가는 것이다. 그 벗이야말로 옛 색슨어의 참뜻 그대로 \"집을 묶어 주는 띠\"이니, 메그가 배웠듯 여자의 가장 행복한 왕국은 가정이며 그 가장 높은 명예는 그곳을 다스리는 기술임을, 여왕으로서가 아니라 지혜로운 아내이자 어머니로서 다스리는 기술임을 배워 가는 것이다."]], [["# LAZY LAURENCE.", "# LAZY LAURENCE."]], [["Laurie went to Nice intending to stay a week, and remained a month. He was tired of wandering about alone, and Amy's familiar presence seemed to give a home-like charm to the foreign scenes in which she bore a part.", "* 집을 묶어 주는 띠 : 영어 husband(남편)의 옛 어원은 '집(house)'과 '묶다(band)'에서 왔다는 뜻 : 옮긴이."], ["He rather missed the \"petting\"", "로리는 일주일만 머물 생각으로 니스에 갔다가 한 달을 머물렀다."], ["he used to receive, and enjoyed a taste of it again; for no attentions, however flattering, from strangers, were half so pleasant as the sisterly adoration of the girls at home.", "혼자 떠도는 데 지쳐 있던 그에게, 에이미라는 낯익은 존재는 그녀가 한몫을 맡고 있는 이국의 풍경에 집 같은 매력을 더해 주는 듯했다. 그는 예전에 받던 \"응석 받아 주기\"가 못내 그리웠고, 다시 그 맛을 보는 것이 즐거웠다."], ["Amy never would pet him like the others, but she was very glad to see him now, and quite clung to him, feeling that he was the representative of the dear family for whom she longed more than she would confess.", "아무리 우쭐하게 만드는 것이라 해도 낯선 사람들의 호의는, 집에 있는 소녀들의 누이다운 흠모에 견주면 절반도 즐겁지 않았기 때문이다. 에이미는 다른 자매들처럼 그를 떠받들어 주려 하지 않았지만, 지금은 그를 보게 되어 무척 기뻐했고, 그가 자기가 인정하는 것보다 더 그리워하던 그리운 가족의 대표라는 생각에 그에게 거의 매달리다시피 했다."], ["They naturally took comfort in each other's society, and were much together, riding, walking, dancing, or dawdling, for, at Nice, no one can be very industrious during the gay season.", "두 사람은 자연스럽게 서로의 벗으로 위안을 얻었고, 함께 말을 타고 걷고 춤추고 빈둥대며 많은 시간을 같이 보냈다. 니스에서는 화려한 사교철에 그 누구도 그리 부지런할 수 없는 법이니까."], ["But, while apparently amusing themselves in the most careless fashion, they were half-consciously making discoveries and forming opinions about each other.", "하지만 겉으로는 더없이 무심한 척 즐기는 동안에도, 두 사람은 반쯤은 의식하면서 서로에 대해 이런저런 사실을 발견하고 견해를 세워 가고 있었다."], ["Amy rose daily in the estimation of her friend, but he sunk in hers, and each felt the truth before a word was spoken.", "에이미는 날마다 친구의 평가 속에서 올라갔지만 그는 그녀의 평가 속에서 내려갔고, 두 사람 다 한마디도 나누기 전에 그 진실을 느끼고 있었다."], ["Amy tried to please, and succeeded, for she was grateful for the many pleasures he gave her, and repaid him with the little services to which womanly women know how to lend an indescribable charm.", "에이미는 그를 기쁘게 하려 애썼고 또 성공했다. 그가 안겨 준 수많은 즐거움이 고마웠기에, 여자다운 여자만이 형언할 수 없는 매력을 불어넣을 줄 아는 그 소소한 시중으로 보답한 것이다."], ["Laurie made no effort of any kind, but just let himself drift along as comfortably as possible, trying to forget, and feeling that all women owed him a kind word because one had been cold to him.", "로리는 어떤 종류의 노력도 하지 않았다. 그저 되도록 편안하게 흘러가는 대로 자신을 내맡긴 채, 잊으려 애쓰면서, 한 여자가 자기에게 냉랭했으니 모든 여자가 자기에게 다정한 말 한마디쯤은 빚지고 있다고 느꼈다."], ["It cost him no effort to be generous, and he would have given Amy all the trinkets in Nice if she would have taken them; but, at the same time, he felt that he could not change the opinion she was forming of him, and he rather dreaded the keen blue eyes that seemed to watch him with such half-sorrowful, half-scornful surprise.", "그에게 너그럽게 구는 것은 아무런 수고도 들지 않는 일이라, 에이미가 받기만 한다면 니스의 장신구를 죄다 사 줄 수도 있었으리라. 하지만 동시에 그는 그녀가 자기에 대해 세워 가는 평가를 바꿀 수 없음을 느꼈고, 반쯤은 슬픈, 반쯤은 비웃는 듯한 놀라움으로 자기를 지켜보는 듯한 그 날카로운 파란 눈이 다소 두렵기도 했다."]], [["\"All the rest have gone to Monaco for the day; I preferred to stay at home and write letters.", "\"다른 사람들은 다 모나코로 하루 나들이를 갔어요. 난 집에 남아서 편지나 쓰는 게 좋았고요."], ["They are done now, and I am going to Valrosa to sketch; will you come?\"", "이제 다 썼으니까 발로사에 그림 그리러 가려는데, 같이 갈래요?\""], ["said Amy, as she joined Laurie one lovely day when he lounged in as usual, about noon.", "어느 화창한 날, 평소처럼 정오 무렵 어슬렁어슬렁 들어선 로리에게 에이미가 다가가며 말했다."]], [["\"Well, yes; but isn't it rather warm for such a long walk?\"", "\"음, 그래요. 그런데 이렇게 긴 산책을 하기엔 좀 더운 거 아닌가?\""], ["he answered slowly, for the shaded _salon_ looked inviting, after the glare without.", "그는 느릿느릿 대답했다. 밖의 눈부신 햇살을 보고 나니 그늘진 응접실이 더 끌렸기 때문이다."]], [["\"I'm going to have the little carriage, and Baptiste can drive, so you'll have nothing to do but hold your umbrella and keep your gloves nice,\" returned Amy, with a sarcastic glance at the immaculate kids, which were a weak point with Laurie.", "\"작은 마차를 쓸 거예요. 바티스트가 몰아 줄 거고요. 그러니 당신은 양산이나 받쳐 들고 장갑이나 깨끗하게 간수하는 것 말고는 할 일이 없을 거예요.\" 에이미가 흠 하나 없는 새끼 양가죽 장갑을 비꼬듯 흘끗 보며 받아쳤다. 그 장갑은 로리의 약점이었다."]], [["\"Then I'll go with pleasure;\"", "\"그렇다면 기꺼이 가지요.\""], ["and he put out his hand for her sketch-book.", "그러면서 그는 그녀의 스케치북을 받으려고 손을 내밀었다."], ["But she tucked it under her arm with a sharp--", "하지만 그녀는 그것을 팔 밑에 잽싸게 끼워 넣으며 톡 쏘았다."]], [["\"Don't trouble yourself; it's no exertion to me, but _you_ don't look equal to it.\"", "\"수고하실 것 없어요. 저한테는 힘든 일도 아니고, 당신은 그럴 기운도 없어 보이는걸요.\""]], [["Laurie lifted his eyebrows, and followed at a leisurely pace as she ran downstairs; but when they got into the carriage he took the reins himself, and left little Baptiste nothing to do but fold his arms and fall asleep on his perch.", "로리는 눈썹을 치켜올린 채, 그녀가 계단을 뛰어 내려가자 느긋한 걸음으로 뒤따랐다. 하지만 마차에 올라타자 그는 직접 고삐를 쥐었고, 어린 바티스트는 팔짱을 끼고 마부석에서 잠드는 것 말고는 할 일이 없게 되었다."]], [["The two never quarrelled,--Amy was too well-bred, and just now Laurie was too lazy; so, in a minute he peeped under her hat-brim with an inquiring air; she answered with a smile, and they went on together in the most amicable manner.", "두 사람은 결코 다투지 않았다. 에이미는 너무나 교양 있었고, 지금의 로리는 너무나 게을렀으니까. 그래서 잠시 후 그가 살피는 표정으로 그녀의 모자챙 아래를 들여다보자, 그녀는 미소로 답했고, 두 사람은 더없이 다정한 분위기 속에 함께 길을 이어 갔다."]], [["It was a lovely drive, along winding roads rich in the picturesque scenes that delight beauty-loving eyes.", "아름다움을 사랑하는 눈을 즐겁게 하는 그림 같은 풍경이 가득한, 구불구불한 길을 따라가는 사랑스러운 마차 길이었다."], ["Here an ancient monastery, whence the solemn chanting of the monks came down to them.", "한쪽에는 오래된 수도원이 있어, 수도사들의 엄숙한 성가가 그들에게까지 흘러 내려왔다."], ["There a bare-legged shepherd, in wooden shoes, pointed hat, and rough jacket over one shoulder, sat piping on a stone, while his goats skipped among the rocks or lay at his feet.", "다른 한쪽에는 맨다리에 나막신을 신고 뾰족한 모자를 쓰고 한쪽 어깨에 거친 윗옷을 걸친 양치기가 돌 위에 앉아 피리를 불었고, 그의 염소들은 바위 사이를 폴짝거리거나 발치에 누워 있었다."], ["Meek, mouse-colored donkeys, laden with panniers of freshly-cut grass, passed by, with a pretty girl in a _capaline_ sitting between the green piles, or an old woman spinning with a distaff as she went.", "갓 베어 낸 풀을 광주리에 가득 실은 온순하고 쥐색을 띤 당나귀들이, 초록빛 풀더미 사이에 카팔린을 쓴 예쁜 소녀를 태우거나, 가면서 물레가락으로 실을 잣는 노파를 태운 채 지나갔다."], ["Brown, soft-eyed children ran out from the quaint stone hovels to offer nosegays, or bunches of oranges still on the bough.", "갈색 피부에 부드러운 눈을 한 아이들이 고풍스러운 돌 오두막에서 뛰어나와 꽃다발이나 아직 가지에 달린 오렌지 송이를 내밀었다."], ["Gnarled olive-trees covered the hills with their dusky foliage, fruit hung golden in the orchard, and great scarlet anemones fringed the roadside; while beyond green slopes and craggy heights, the Maritime Alps rose sharp and white against the blue Italian sky.", "옹이진 올리브나무들이 거무스름한 잎으로 언덕을 뒤덮었고, 과수원에는 황금빛 열매가 매달렸으며, 길가에는 새빨간 아네모네가 가장자리를 수놓았다. 그리고 푸른 비탈과 바위투성이 산봉우리 너머로, 마리팀 알프스가 이탈리아의 푸른 하늘을 배경으로 또렷하고 새하얗게 솟아 있었다."]], [["Valrosa well deserved its name, for, in that climate of perpetual summer, roses blossomed everywhere.", "발로사는 그 이름값을 톡톡히 했다. 영원한 여름이 이어지는 그 기후 속에서 장미가 어디에나 피어 있었기 때문이다."], ["They overhung the archway, thrust themselves between the bars of the great gate with a sweet welcome to passers-by, and lined the avenue, winding through lemon-trees and feathery palms up to the villa on the hill.", "장미는 아치 위로 늘어졌고, 큰 대문 창살 사이로 고개를 내밀어 지나가는 이들에게 향긋한 인사를 건넸으며, 레몬나무와 깃털 같은 야자수 사이로 구불구불 이어져 언덕 위 별장까지 닿는 길가를 따라 줄지어 있었다."], ["Every shadowy nook, where seats invited one to stop and rest, was a mass of bloom; every cool grotto had its marble nymph smiling from a veil of flowers, and every fountain reflected crimson, white, or pale pink roses, leaning down to smile at their own beauty.", "발길을 멈추고 쉬어 가라며 의자가 놓인 그늘진 구석마다 꽃이 무더기로 피었고, 서늘한 동굴마다 대리석 요정이 꽃의 너울 사이로 미소 짓고 있었으며, 분수마다 진홍빛, 흰빛, 옅은 분홍빛 장미가 비쳐, 제 아름다움에 미소 지으려는 듯 고개를 숙이고 있었다."], ["Roses covered the walls of the house, draped the cornices, climbed the pillars, and ran riot over the balustrade of the wide terrace, whence one looked down on the sunny Mediterranean, and the white-walled city on its shore.", "장미는 집의 벽을 뒤덮었고, 처마 장식을 휘감았고, 기둥을 타고 올랐으며, 넓은 테라스의 난간 위로 제멋대로 흐드러졌다. 그 테라스에서는 햇살 가득한 지중해와 그 기슭에 자리한 흰 성벽의 도시가 내려다보였다."]], [["\"This is a regular honeymoon Paradise, isn't it?", "\"여긴 영락없이 신혼여행 낙원이네요, 안 그래요?"], ["Did you ever see such roses?\"", "이런 장미를 본 적 있어요?\""], ["asked Amy, pausing on the terrace to enjoy the view, and a luxurious whiff of perfume that came wandering by.", "에이미가 테라스에 멈춰 서서 경치와, 흘러오는 풍성한 향기 한 줄기를 즐기며 물었다."]], [["\"No, nor felt such thorns,\"", "\"아니, 이런 가시도 느껴 본 적 없는걸.\""], ["returned Laurie, with his thumb in his mouth, after a vain attempt to capture a solitary scarlet flower that grew just beyond his reach.", "로리가 엄지손가락을 입에 문 채 대꾸했다. 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 핀 진홍빛 꽃 한 송이를 꺾으려다 헛수고를 한 뒤였다."]], [["\"Try lower down, and pick those that have no thorns,\"", "\"더 아래쪽으로 손을 뻗어서 가시 없는 걸 따요.\""], ["said Amy, gathering three of the tiny cream-colored ones that starred the wall behind her.", "에이미가 자기 뒤편 벽을 별처럼 수놓은 자그마한 크림색 장미 세 송이를 꺾으며 말했다. 그녀는 화해의 표시로 그것을 그의 단춧구멍에 꽂아 주었고, 그는 묘한 표정으로 잠시 그것을 내려다보며 서 있었다."], ["She put them in his button-hole, as a peace-offering, and he stood a minute looking down at them with a curious expression, for in the Italian part of his nature there was a touch of superstition, and he was just then in that state of half-sweet, half-bitter melancholy, when imaginative young men find significance in trifles, and food for romance everywhere.", "그의 이탈리아인다운 천성 속에는 미신적인 데가 조금 있었던 데다, 마침 그는 상상력이 풍부한 젊은이들이 하찮은 것에서 의미를 찾고 어디서나 낭만의 양식을 발견하게 되는, 반쯤은 달콤하고 반쯤은 쓰라린 우수에 잠겨 있었기 때문이다. 그는 가시 돋친 붉은 장미를 향해 손을 뻗으면서 조를 떠올렸다."], ["He had thought of Jo in reaching after the thorny red rose, for vivid flowers became her, and she had often worn ones like that from the greenhouse at home.", "선명한 꽃이 그녀에게 잘 어울렸고, 집의 온실에서 꺾은 그런 꽃을 그녀가 종종 달고 다녔으니까. 에이미가 준 옅은 빛깔의 장미는 이탈리아 사람들이 죽은 이의 손에 놓아 줄 뿐 신부의 화관에는 결코 쓰지 않는 종류였다."], ["The pale roses Amy gave him were the sort that the Italians lay in dead hands, never in bridal wreaths, and, for a moment, he wondered if the omen was for Jo or for himself; but the next instant his American common-sense got the better of sentimentality, and he laughed a heartier laugh than Amy had heard since he came.", "그래서 그는 한순간, 그 불길한 징조가 조를 향한 것인지 자기를 향한 것인지 의아해했다. 하지만 다음 순간 그의 미국인다운 상식이 감상을 누르고 이겨 냈고, 그는 그가 온 뒤로 에이미가 들어 보지 못한, 한층 더 활기찬 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["\"It's good advice; you'd better take it and save your fingers,\"", "\"좋은 충고예요. 그 말 듣고 손가락이나 아끼는 게 낫겠어요.\""], ["she said, thinking her speech amused him.", "에이미가 자기 말이 그를 웃긴 줄 알고 말했다."]], [["\"Thank you, I will,\"", "\"고마워요, 그럴게요.\""], ["he answered in jest, and a few months later he did it in earnest.", "그는 농담조로 대답했고, 몇 달 뒤 진심으로 그렇게 했다."]], [["\"Laurie, when are you going to your grandfather?\"", "\"로리, 할아버지께는 언제 갈 거예요?\""], ["she asked presently, as she settled herself on a rustic seat.", "에이미가 곧 시골풍 의자에 자리를 잡으며 물었다."]], [["\"Very soon.\"", "\"곧.\""]], [["\"You have said that a dozen times within the last three weeks.\"", "\"지난 삼 주 동안 그 말만 열두 번은 했어요.\""]], [["\"I dare say; short answers save trouble.\"", "\"그럴 만도 하지. 짧게 대답하면 성가신 일이 줄어드니까.\""]], [["\"He expects you, and you really ought to go.\"", "\"할아버지가 기다리시잖아요. 정말 가야 해요.\""]], [["\"Hospitable creature!", "\"손님 대접 좋아하시는 분이지."], ["I know it.\"", "나도 알아요.\""]], [["\"Then why don't you do it?\"", "\"그럼 왜 안 가요?\""]], [["\"Natural depravity, I suppose.\"", "\"타고난 못된 성미 때문이겠죠, 아마.\""]], [["\"Natural indolence, you mean.", "\"타고난 게으름이겠죠."], ["It's really dreadful!\"", "정말 너무하네요!\""], ["and Amy looked severe.", "에이미가 엄한 표정을 지었다."]], [["\"Not so bad as it seems, for I should only plague him if I went, so I might as well stay, and plague you a little longer, you can bear it better; in fact, I think it agrees with you excellently;\" and Laurie composed himself for a lounge on the broad ledge of the balustrade.", "\"보기만큼 나쁘진 않아요. 내가 가 봤자 할아버지를 성가시게 할 뿐이니, 차라리 여기 머물면서 당신을 좀 더 성가시게 하는 게 낫지. 당신은 그걸 더 잘 견디니까. 사실 당신한테는 아주 잘 맞는 것 같던데.\" 그러면서 로리는 난간의 넓은 턱에 비스듬히 기대 빈둥거릴 자세를 잡았다."]], [["Amy shook her head, and opened her sketch-book with an air of resignation; but she had made up her mind to lecture \"that boy,\"", "에이미는 고개를 저으며 체념한 듯한 표정으로 스케치북을 펼쳤다."], ["and in a minute she began again.", "하지만 그녀는 \"저 녀석\"에게 한바탕 훈계를 하기로 마음먹은 터라, 곧 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["\"What are you doing just now?\"", "\"지금 뭐 해요?\""]], [["\"Watching lizards.\"", "\"도마뱀 구경.\""]], [["\"No, no; I mean what do you intend and wish to do?\"", "\"아니, 아니요. 앞으로 뭘 할 작정이고 뭘 하고 싶으냐는 거예요.\""]], [["\"Smoke a cigarette, if you'll allow me.\"", "\"허락해 주면 담배나 한 대 피우고 싶군.\""]], [["\"How provoking you are!", "\"정말 사람 약 올리네요! 난 시가 같은 거 안 좋아해요."], ["I don't approve of cigars, and I will only allow it on condition that you let me put you into my sketch; I need a figure.\"", "하지만 당신을 내 그림에 넣게 해 준다면 봐줄게요. 인물이 하나 필요하거든요.\""]], [["\"With all the pleasure in life.", "\"세상에 더없이 기쁜 일이지. 어떻게 그릴 거요?"], ["How will you have me,--full-length or three-quarters, on my head or my heels?", "전신상으로, 아니면 사분의 삼만? 머리로 설지, 발로 설지?"], ["I should respectfully suggest a recumbent posture, then put yourself in also, and call it '_Dolce far niente._'\"", "정중하게 제안하건대, 비스듬히 누운 자세가 좋겠소. 그리고 당신도 함께 그려 넣어서 '돌체 파르 니엔테'라고 이름 붙이면 되겠군.\""]], [["\"Stay as you are, and go to sleep if you like.", "\"그 자세 그대로 있어요. 졸리면 자도 좋고요."], ["_I_ intend to work hard,\"", "나는 열심히 그릴 작정이니까.\""], ["said Amy, in her most energetic tone.", "에이미가 더없이 의욕에 찬 어조로 말했다."]], [["\"What delightful enthusiasm!\"", "\"이 무슨 즐거운 열정이람!\""], ["and he leaned against a tall urn with an air of entire satisfaction.", "그는 더없이 흡족한 표정으로 키 큰 항아리에 몸을 기댔다."]], [["\"What would Jo say if she saw you now?\"", "\"지금 당신 모습을 조가 본다면 뭐라고 할까요?\""], ["asked Amy impatiently, hoping to stir him up by the mention of her still more energetic sister's name.", "에이미가 안달하며 물었다. 자기보다 한층 더 의욕 넘치는 언니의 이름을 들먹여 그를 자극해 보려는 속셈이었다."]], [["\"As usual, 'Go away, Teddy, I'm busy!'\" He laughed as he spoke, but the laugh was not natural, and a shade passed over his face, for the utterance of the familiar name touched the wound that was not healed yet. Both tone and shadow struck Amy, for she had seen and heard them before, and now she looked up in time to catch a new expression on Laurie's face,--a hard, bitter look, full of pain, dissatisfaction, and regret. It was gone before she could study it, and the listless expression back again. She watched him for a moment with artistic pleasure, thinking how like an Italian he looked, as he lay basking in the sun with uncovered head, and eyes full of southern dreaminess; for he seemed to have forgotten her, and fallen into a reverie.", "\"늘 하던 대로 '저리 가, 테디, 나 바빠!' 하겠지.\" 그는 말하면서 웃었지만, 그 웃음은 자연스럽지 않았고 그의 얼굴 위로 그늘이 스쳤다. 그 익숙한 이름을 입에 올리자, 아직 아물지 않은 상처가 건드려졌기 때문이다. 그 어조와 그늘이 에이미의 마음을 쳤다. 전에도 보고 들은 적이 있었기 때문이다. 그리고 이번에는 마침 그녀가 고개를 들어, 로리의 얼굴에 떠오른 새로운 표정을 포착했다. 고통과 불만과 후회로 가득한, 단단하고 쓰라린 표정이었다. 그녀가 그것을 찬찬히 살피기도 전에 그 표정은 사라졌고, 다시 무기력한 표정이 돌아왔다. 그녀는 예술가다운 즐거움을 느끼며 잠시 그를 지켜보았다. 모자도 쓰지 않고 햇살을 쬐며 누운 채 남국의 몽상으로 가득한 눈을 한 그의 모습이 어쩌면 이리도 이탈리아 사람 같은지 생각하면서. 그는 그녀를 잊은 듯 깊은 몽상에 빠져 있었다."]], [["\"You look like the effigy of a young knight asleep on his tomb,\"", "\"꼭 무덤 위에 잠든 젊은 기사의 조각상 같아요.\""], ["she said, carefully tracing the well-cut profile defined against the dark stone.", "에이미가 어두운 돌을 배경으로 또렷이 드러난 그의 잘생긴 옆모습을 조심스레 따라 그리며 말했다."]], [["\"Wish I was!\" \"That's a foolish wish, unless you have spoilt your life.", "\"인생을 망쳐 버린 게 아니라면, 그건 어리석은 소망이에요."], ["You are so changed, I sometimes think--\"", "당신은 너무 변했어요. 가끔 이런 생각이 들어요—\""], ["there Amy stopped, with a half-timid, half-wistful look, more significant than her unfinished speech.", "거기서 에이미는 말을 멈추었다. 반쯤은 머뭇거리고 반쯤은 안타까워하는 그 눈빛이, 끝맺지 못한 말보다 더 많은 것을 담고 있었다."]], [["Laurie saw and understood the affectionate anxiety which she hesitated to express, and looking straight into her eyes, said, just as he used to say it to her mother,--", "로리는 그녀가 차마 입에 담지 못하는 애정 어린 걱정을 보았고 또 이해했다. 그래서 그녀의 어머니에게 그러던 그대로, 그녀의 눈을 똑바로 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["\"It's all right, ma'am.\"", "\"다 괜찮아요, 부인.\""]], [["That satisfied her and set at rest the doubts that had begun to worry her lately.", "그 말이 그녀를 안심시켰고, 요즘 그녀를 괴롭히기 시작한 의심을 잠재웠다."], ["It also touched her, and she showed that it did, by the cordial tone in which she said,--", "또한 그 말은 그녀의 마음을 건드렸고, 그녀는 다정한 어조로 그 마음을 드러냈다."]], [["\"I'm glad of that!", "\"그렇다니 다행이에요!"], ["I didn't think you'd been a very bad boy, but I fancied you might have wasted money at that wicked Baden-Baden, lost your heart to some charming Frenchwoman with a husband, or got into some of the scrapes that young men seem to consider a necessary part of a foreign tour.", "당신이 아주 못된 짓을 하고 다녔으리라곤 생각하지 않았지만, 혹시 그 못된 바덴바덴에서 돈을 탕진했거나, 남편 있는 매력적인 프랑스 여자한테 마음을 빼앗겼거나, 아니면 젊은 남자들이 외국 여행에 으레 따라붙는 거라고 여기는 그런 사고를 친 건 아닐까 싶었거든요."], ["Don't stay out there in the sun; come and lie on the grass here, and 'let us be friendly,'", "거기 햇볕 아래 그러고 있지 말고, 이리 와서 풀밭에 누워요."], ["as Jo used to say when we got in the sofa-corner and told secrets.\"", "그리고 조가 우리가 소파 구석에 모여 비밀 이야기를 나눌 때 하던 말처럼, '우리 사이좋게 지내자고요.'\""]], [["Laurie obediently threw himself down on the turf, and began to amuse himself by sticking daisies into the ribbons of Amy's hat, that lay there.", "로리는 순순히 잔디밭에 몸을 던지고는, 거기 놓여 있던 에이미의 모자 리본에 데이지를 꽂으며 장난을 치기 시작했다."]], [["\"I'm all ready for the secrets;\"", "\"비밀 이야기, 들을 준비 다 됐어요.\""], ["and he glanced up with a decided expression of interest in his eyes.", "그가 눈에 분명한 관심을 띠며 고개를 들었다."]], [["\"I've none to tell; you may begin.\"", "\"난 할 얘기 없어요. 당신부터 시작해요.\""]], [["\"Haven't one to bless myself with.", "\"나도 내세울 비밀 하나 없는걸."], ["I thought perhaps you'd had some news from home.\"", "혹시 집에서 무슨 소식이라도 들었나 했지.\""]], [["\"You have heard all that has come lately.", "\"최근에 온 소식은 당신도 다 들었잖아요."], ["Don't you hear often?", "자주 소식 못 받아요?"], ["I fancied Jo would send you volumes.\"", "난 조가 당신한테 책 몇 권 분량은 써 보낼 줄 알았는데.\""]], [["\"She's very busy; I'm roving about so, it's impossible to be regular, you know.", "\"걔는 무척 바쁘니까. 게다가 난 이렇게 떠돌아다니니 꼬박꼬박 받기가 불가능하지, 알잖아요."], ["When do you begin your great work of art, Raphaella?\"", "그런데 당신의 위대한 예술 작품은 언제 시작할 거요, 라파엘라 양?\""], ["he asked, changing the subject abruptly after another pause, in which he had been wondering if Amy knew his secret, and wanted to talk about it.", "그가 또 한 차례 침묵 끝에 불쑥 화제를 바꾸며 물었다. 그 침묵 동안 그는 에이미가 자기 비밀을 알고서 그 이야기를 하고 싶어 하는 것은 아닐까 생각하던 참이었다."]], [["\"Never,\"", "\"영영 안 해요.\""], ["she answered, with a despondent but decided air.", "에이미가 풀이 죽었으면서도 단호한 태도로 대답했다. \"로마가 내 허영심을 죄다 앗아 갔거든요."], ["\"Rome took all the vanity out of me; for after seeing the wonders there, I felt too insignificant to live, and gave up all my foolish hopes in despair.\"", "거기서 그 경이로운 것들을 보고 나니, 내가 너무 보잘것없어서 살아갈 가치도 없는 듯 느껴졌어요. 그래서 절망 속에 어리석은 희망을 다 버렸죠.\""]], [["\"Why should you, with so much energy and talent?\"", "\"그렇게 기운도 재능도 넘치는 사람이 왜 그래요?\""]], [["\"That's just why,--because talent isn't genius, and no amount of energy can make it so.", "\"바로 그래서예요. 재능은 천재성이 아니고, 아무리 기운이 넘쳐도 그걸 천재성으로 만들 순 없으니까요."], ["I want to be great, or nothing.", "난 위대해지거나, 아니면 아무것도 안 되거나 둘 중 하나예요."], ["I won't be a common-place dauber, so I don't intend to try any more.\"", "평범하게 물감이나 처바르는 사람은 되지 않을 거예요. 그러니 더는 시도할 생각이 없어요.\""]], [["\"And what are you going to do with yourself now, if I may ask?\"", "\"그럼 이제 자신을 어떻게 할 작정인지 물어봐도 될까요?\""]], [["\"Polish up my other talents, and be an ornament to society, if I get the chance.\"", "\"내 다른 재능들을 갈고닦아서, 기회가 닿으면 사교계의 꽃이 될 거예요.\""]], [["It was a characteristic speech, and sounded daring; but audacity becomes young people, and Amy's ambition had a good foundation.", "그것은 그녀다운 말이었고 당돌하게 들렸다. 하지만 대담함은 젊은이들에게 잘 어울리는 법이고, 에이미의 야망에는 탄탄한 근거가 있었다."], ["Laurie smiled, but he liked the spirit with which she took up a new purpose when a long-cherished one died, and spent no time lamenting.", "로리는 미소를 지었지만, 오래 품어 온 목표 하나가 죽자 한탄으로 시간을 허비하지 않고 새 목표를 받아드는 그 기개가 마음에 들었다."]], [["\"Good!", "\"좋아요!"], ["and here is where Fred Vaughn comes in, I fancy.\"", "그리고 여기서 프레드 본이 등장하는 거겠죠, 내 짐작에는.\""]], [["Amy preserved a discreet silence, but there was a conscious look in her downcast face, that made Laurie sit up and say gravely,--", "에이미는 신중하게 침묵을 지켰지만, 고개 숙인 그녀의 얼굴에는 무언가 의식하는 기색이 떠올라 있었다. 그 기색에 로리는 몸을 일으켜 앉으며 진지하게 말했다."]], [["\"Now I'm going to play brother, and ask questions.", "\"이제 내가 오빠 노릇을 하면서 질문 좀 할게요."], ["May I?\"", "그래도 될까요?\""]], [["\"I don't promise to answer.\"", "\"대답하겠다고 약속은 못 해요.\""]], [["\"Your face will, if your tongue won't.", "\"입은 말을 안 해도 얼굴이 말해 줄 거요."], ["You aren't woman of the world enough yet to hide your feelings, my dear.", "당신은 아직 감정을 숨길 만큼 세상 물정에 닳은 여자가 아니거든, 에이미."], ["I heard rumors about Fred and you last year, and it's my private opinion that, if he had not been called home so suddenly and detained so long, something would have come of it--hey?\"", "작년에 당신하고 프레드에 관한 소문을 들었는데, 내 개인적인 생각으로는 만약 그가 그렇게 갑작스레 집으로 불려 가서 그토록 오래 발이 묶이지 않았다면 무슨 일이 벌어졌을 것 같단 말이지. 어때요?\""]], [["\"That's not for me to say,\"", "\"그건 내가 할 말이 아니죠.\""], ["was Amy's prim reply; but her lips would smile, and there was a traitorous sparkle of the eye, which betrayed that she knew her power and enjoyed the knowledge.", "에이미는 새침하게 대답했다. 하지만 입가에는 자꾸 미소가 번졌고, 눈에는 배신처럼 반짝이는 빛이 어려, 자기 힘을 잘 알고 그 앎을 즐기고 있음을 드러냈다."]], [["\"You are not engaged, I hope?\"", "\"약혼한 건 아니죠, 설마?\""], ["and Laurie looked very elder-brotherly and grave all of a sudden.", "로리가 별안간 무척 오빠다운 진지한 표정을 지었다. \"아니요.\""]], [["\"But you will be, if he comes back and goes properly down upon his knees, won't you?\"", "\"하지만 그가 돌아와서 제대로 무릎을 꿇으면 약혼하겠죠, 안 그래요?\" \"그럴 가능성이 크죠.\""]], [["\"Very likely.\" \"Then you are fond of old Fred?\"", "\"그럼 그 프레드 녀석을 좋아하긴 하는 거요?\""]], [["\"I could be, if I tried.\"", "\"좋아하려고 마음먹으면 그럴 수 있어요.\""]], [["\"But you don't intend to try till the proper moment?", "\"하지만 적당한 때가 오기 전엔 마음먹을 생각이 없다는 거군?"], ["Bless my soul, what unearthly prudence!", "맙소사, 이 무슨 세상에 다시없을 신중함이람!"], ["He's a good fellow, Amy, but not the man I fancied you'd like.\"", "그는 좋은 친구예요, 에이미. 하지만 내가 생각하기에 당신이 좋아할 만한 사람은 아닌데.\""]], [["\"He is rich, a gentleman, and has delightful manners,\"", "\"그는 부자고, 신사고, 매너가 아주 훌륭해요.\""], ["began Amy, trying to be quite cool and dignified, but feeling a little ashamed of herself, in spite of the sincerity of her intentions.", "에이미는 자못 차분하고 위엄 있게 보이려 애쓰며 입을 뗐지만, 그 진심 어린 의도에도 불구하고 어쩐지 스스로가 조금 부끄러웠다."]], [["\"I understand; queens of society can't get on without money, so you mean to make a good match, and start in that way?", "\"알겠어요. 사교계의 여왕은 돈이 없으면 굴러갈 수 없으니, 당신은 좋은 혼처를 잡아서 그렇게 출발할 작정이라는 거군요."], ["Quite right and proper, as the world goes, but it sounds odd from the lips of one of your mother's girls.\"", "세상 돌아가는 이치로 보면 지극히 옳고 마땅한 일이지만, 당신 어머니 딸의 입에서 나오니 어쩐지 어색하게 들리는걸.\""]], [["\"True, nevertheless.\"", "\"그래도 사실인걸요.\""]], [["A short speech, but the quiet decision with which it was uttered contrasted curiously with the young speaker.", "짧은 말이었지만, 그것을 내뱉는 차분한 단호함이 그 젊은 화자와는 묘하게 어울리지 않았다."], ["Laurie felt this instinctively, and laid himself down again, with a sense of disappointment which he could not explain.", "로리는 본능적으로 그것을 느끼고는, 까닭을 알 수 없는 실망감과 함께 다시 몸을 뉘었다."], ["His look and silence, as well as a certain inward self-disapproval, ruffled Amy, and made her resolve to deliver her lecture without delay.", "그의 표정과 침묵이, 그리고 어떤 속마음의 자책감이 에이미를 언짢게 했고, 미루지 말고 당장 훈계를 하리라 마음먹게 했다."]], [["\"I wish you'd do me the favor to rouse yourself a little,\"", "\"제발 부탁이니 좀 분발해 줬으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["she said sharply.", "그녀가 톡 쏘듯 말했다."]], [["\"Do it for me, there's a dear girl.\"", "\"나 대신 분발해 줘요, 착한 아가씨.\""]], [["\"I could, if I tried;\"", "\"마음만 먹으면 할 수 있어요.\""], ["and she looked as if she would like doing it in the most summary style.", "그녀는 더없이 간단명료한 방식으로 그러고 싶다는 듯한 표정이었다."]], [["\"Try, then; I give you leave,\"", "\"그럼 해 봐요. 허락할 테니.\""], ["returned Laurie, who enjoyed having some one to tease, after his long abstinence from his favorite pastime.", "로리가 받아쳤다. 가장 좋아하는 소일거리를 오래 끊고 지낸 터라, 약 올릴 상대가 생긴 것이 즐거웠다."]], [["\"You'd be angry in five minutes.\"", "\"오 분도 안 돼서 화낼걸요.\""]], [["\"I'm never angry with you.", "\"난 당신한테는 절대 화 안 내요."], ["It takes two flints to make a fire: you are as cool and soft as snow.\"", "불을 일으키려면 부싯돌이 두 개 있어야 하는데, 당신은 눈처럼 차갑고 부드럽거든.\""]], [["\"You don't know what I can do; snow produces a glow and a tingle, if applied rightly.", "\"내가 무얼 할 수 있는지 모르는군요. 눈도 제대로 쓰면 화끈거리고 따끔거리는 법이에요."], ["Your indifference is half affectation, and a good stirring up would prove it.\"", "당신의 무관심은 절반은 꾸민 거고, 한번 제대로 휘저어 놓으면 그게 드러날걸요.\""]], [["\"Stir away; it won't hurt me and it may amuse you, as the big man said when his little wife beat him.", "\"실컷 휘저어 봐요. 난 아프지도 않을 거고, 당신은 재미있을지도 모르니."], ["Regard me in the light of a husband or a carpet, and beat till you are tired, if that sort of exercise agrees with you.\"", "덩치 큰 사내가 자그마한 아내한테 매를 맞으면서 한 말처럼 말이오. 날 남편이나 양탄자쯤으로 여기고, 그런 운동이 당신한테 맞는다면 지칠 때까지 두드려 봐요.\""]], [["Being decidedly nettled herself, and longing to see him shake off the apathy that so altered him, Amy sharpened both tongue and pencil, and began:--", "자기도 단단히 약이 올라, 그를 그토록 딴사람으로 만든 그 무기력을 그가 떨쳐 내는 모습을 보고 싶어 애가 탄 에이미는, 혀끝도 연필 끝도 날카롭게 벼리고는 입을 열었다."]], [["\"Flo and I have got a new name for you; it's 'Lazy Laurence.'", "\"플로하고 내가 당신한테 새 별명을 붙였어요. '게으른 로런스'예요."], ["How do you like it?\"", "어때요, 마음에 들어요?\""]], [["She thought it would annoy him; but he only folded his arms under his head, with an imperturbable \"That's not bad.", "그를 약 올릴 줄 알았는데, 그는 머리 밑에 팔을 괴며 태연하게 말할 뿐이었다. \"나쁘지 않은데."], ["Thank you, ladies.\"", "고마워요, 아가씨들.\""]], [["\"Do you want to know what I honestly think of you?\"", "\"내가 당신을 정말 어떻게 생각하는지 알고 싶어요?\""]], [["\"Pining to be told.\"", "\"못 들어서 안달이 났는걸.\""]], [["\"Well, I despise you.\"", "\"음, 난 당신을 경멸해요.\""]], [["If she had even said \"I hate you,\"", "만약 그녀가 토라진 척, 혹은 새침한 어조로 \"당신이 미워요\"라고만 했다면 그는 웃어넘기고 오히려 좋아했을 것이다."], ["in a petulant or coquettish tone, he would have laughed, and rather liked it; but the grave, almost sad, accent of her voice made him open his eyes, and ask quickly,--", "하지만 진지하다 못해 거의 슬픔에 잠긴 그 목소리에 그는 눈을 번쩍 뜨고 다급히 물었다."]], [["\"Why, if you please?\"", "\"왜요, 어디 말해 봐요?\""]], [["\"Because, with every chance for being good, useful, and happy, you are faulty, lazy, and miserable.\"", "\"착하고 쓸모 있고 행복할 기회가 얼마든지 있는데도, 당신은 결점투성이에 게으르고 비참하니까요.\""]], [["\"Strong language, mademoiselle.\"", "\"센 표현이군요, 아가씨.\""]], [["\"If you like it, I'll go on.\"", "\"마음에 들면 계속할게요.\""]], [["\"Pray, do; it's quite interesting.\"", "\"제발 그래 줘요. 아주 흥미진진하니까.\""]], [["\"I thought you'd find it so; selfish people always like to talk about themselves.\"", "\"그렇게 느낄 줄 알았어요. 이기적인 사람들은 늘 자기 얘기 하는 걸 좋아하거든요.\""]], [["\"Am _I_ selfish?\"", "\"내가 이기적이라고?\""], ["The question slipped out involuntarily and in a tone of surprise, for the one virtue on which he prided himself was generosity.", "그 질문은 본의 아니게, 놀란 어조로 불쑥 새어 나왔다. 그가 스스로 자부하던 단 하나의 미덕이 너그러움이었기 때문이다."]], [["\"Yes, very selfish,\"", "\"네, 아주 이기적이죠.\""], ["continued Amy, in a calm, cool voice, twice as effective, just then, as an angry one.", "에이미가 차분하고 냉정한 목소리로 말을 이었다. 그 목소리는 마침 화난 목소리보다 곱절은 더 효과적이었다."], ["\"I'll show you how, for I've studied you while we have been frolicking, and I'm not at all satisfied with you.", "\"어떻게 그런지 보여 줄게요. 우리가 신나게 노는 동안 내가 당신을 쭉 살펴봤는데, 영 마음에 안 들거든요."], ["Here you have been abroad nearly six months, and done nothing but waste time and money and disappoint your friends.\"", "외국에 나온 지 여섯 달이 다 돼 가는데, 당신이 한 일이라곤 시간과 돈을 허비하고 친구들을 실망시킨 것뿐이잖아요.\""]], [["\"Isn't a fellow to have any pleasure after a four-years grind?\"", "\"사 년이나 죽어라 공부했으면 좀 즐길 수도 있는 거 아닌가?\""]], [["\"You don't look as if you'd had much; at any rate, you are none the better for it, as far as I can see.", "\"그리 즐긴 것 같아 보이지도 않는걸요. 어쨌든 내가 보기엔 그걸로 조금도 나아진 게 없어요."], ["I said, when we first met, that you had improved.", "우리가 처음 만났을 때 난 당신이 한결 나아졌다고 했죠."], ["Now I take it all back, for I don't think you half so nice as when I left you at home.", "이제 그 말 다 취소할래요. 내가 집을 떠나올 때보다 절반도 괜찮아 보이지 않거든요."], ["You have grown abominably lazy; you like gossip, and waste time on frivolous things; you are contented to be petted and admired by silly people, instead of being loved and respected by wise ones.", "끔찍하게 게을러졌고, 남 얘기나 좋아하고, 하찮은 일에 시간을 허비하고, 지혜로운 사람들한테 사랑받고 존경받는 대신 어리석은 사람들한테 떠받들리고 떠받들어지는 데 만족하고 있잖아요."], ["With money, talent, position, health, and beauty,--ah, you like that, Old Vanity!", "돈에 재능에 지위에 건강에 외모까지—아, 그 말은 좋은가 보군요, 이 허영덩어리 양반!"], ["but it's the truth, so I can't help saying it,--with all these splendid things to use and enjoy, you can find nothing to do but dawdle; and, instead of being the man you might and ought to be, you are only--\"", "하지만 사실인걸요, 그러니 안 할 수가 없네요—이 멋진 것들을 다 쓰고 누릴 수 있으면서도, 당신은 빈둥대는 것 말고는 할 일을 못 찾고 있어요. 그리고 될 수도 있고 되어야 마땅한 사람이 되는 대신, 당신은 그저—\""], ["There she stopped, with a look that had both pain and pity in it.", "거기서 그녀는 말을 멈추었다. 그 눈빛에는 고통과 연민이 함께 담겨 있었다."]], [["\"Saint Laurence on a gridiron,\"", "\"석쇠 위에 놓인 성 라우렌티우스란 말이지.\" 로리가 부드럽게 그 문장을 마무리 지어 주었다."], ["added Laurie, blandly finishing the sentence.", "하지만 훈계는 효과를 내기 시작했다. 이제 그의 눈에는 또렷이 깨어난 빛이 반짝였고, 반쯤은 화나고 반쯤은 상처받은 표정이 아까의 무관심을 밀어내고 들어섰다."], ["But the lecture began to take effect, for there was a wide-awake sparkle in his eyes now, and a half-angry, half-injured expression replaced the former indifference.", "* 성 라우렌티우스 : 석쇠 위에서 화형당한 순교 성인. 에이미의 '게으른 로런스'라는 별명을 받아 자신을 순교자에 빗댄 말장난 : 옮긴이."]], [["\"I supposed you'd take it so.", "\"그렇게 받아들일 줄 알았어요."], ["You men tell us we are angels, and say we can make you what we will; but the instant we honestly try to do you good, you laugh at us, and won't listen, which proves how much your flattery is worth.\"", "당신네 남자들은 우리더러 천사라고, 우리가 마음먹은 대로 당신들을 만들 수 있다고 하죠. 하지만 우리가 진심으로 당신들을 위해 좋게 해 주려고 하는 순간, 비웃기나 하고 귀담아듣질 않잖아요."], ["Amy spoke bitterly, and turned her back on the exasperating martyr at her feet.", "그게 바로 당신들의 입에 발린 칭찬이 얼마나 값어치 없는지 증명하는 거예요.\" 에이미가 쓰라리게 내뱉고는, 발치의 그 약 올리는 순교자에게서 등을 돌렸다."]], [["In a minute a hand came down over the page, so that she could not draw, and Laurie's voice said, with a droll imitation of a penitent child,--", "잠시 후 손 하나가 그림 위로 내려와 그녀가 그림을 그릴 수 없게 했고, 로리의 목소리가 참회하는 어린아이를 우스꽝스럽게 흉내 내며 들려왔다."]], [["\"I will be good, oh, I will be good!\"", "\"착하게 굴게요, 아, 착하게 굴게요!\""]], [["But Amy did not laugh, for she was in earnest; and, tapping on the outspread hand with her pencil, said soberly,--", "하지만 에이미는 웃지 않았다. 진지했기 때문이다. 그녀는 펼쳐진 그 손을 연필로 톡톡 두드리며 차분히 말했다."]], [["\"Aren't you ashamed of a hand like that?", "\"이런 손이 부끄럽지도 않아요?"], ["It's as soft and white as a woman's, and looks as if it never did anything but wear Jouvin's best gloves, and pick flowers for ladies.", "여자 손처럼 보드랍고 하얀데, 평생 주뱅이 최고급 장갑이나 끼고 숙녀들한테 꽃이나 꺾어 주는 것 말고는 아무 일도 안 해 본 것 같잖아요."], ["You are not a dandy, thank Heaven!", "하늘에 감사하게도 당신이 멋이나 부리는 한량은 아니죠."], ["so I'm glad to see there are no diamonds or big seal-rings on it, only the little old one Jo gave you so long ago.", "그러니 여기 다이아몬드나 커다란 인장 반지 같은 게 없고, 오래전에 조가 준 그 작고 낡은 반지 하나만 끼워져 있는 게 다행이에요."], ["Dear soul, I wish she was here to help me!\"", "그 착한 애가 여기 있어서 날 도와줬으면 좋겠네요!\""]], [["\"So do I!\"", "\"나도 그래요!\""]], [["The hand vanished as suddenly as it came, and there was energy enough in the echo of her wish to suit even Amy.", "손은 나타날 때만큼이나 갑작스레 사라졌고, 그 소망에 맞장구치는 울림에는 에이미조차 흡족할 만큼 충분한 기운이 실려 있었다."], ["She glanced down at him with a new thought in her mind; but he was lying with his hat half over his face, as if for shade, and his mustache hid his mouth.", "그녀는 새로운 생각을 품은 채 그를 내려다보았다. 하지만 그는 햇볕을 가리려는 듯 모자로 얼굴을 반쯤 덮은 채 누워 있었고, 콧수염이 입을 가리고 있었다."], ["She only saw his chest rise and fall, with a long breath that might have been a sigh, and the hand that wore the ring nestled down into the grass, as if to hide something too precious or too tender to be spoken of.", "그녀에게 보이는 것이라곤 한숨이었을지도 모를 긴 숨에 가슴이 오르내리는 것과, 입에 담기엔 너무 소중하거나 너무 여린 무언가를 감추려는 듯 반지 낀 손이 풀밭 속으로 파고드는 모습뿐이었다."], ["All in a minute various hints and trifles assumed shape and significance in Amy's mind, and told her what her sister never had confided to her.", "순식간에 갖가지 암시와 사소한 일들이 에이미의 머릿속에서 형체와 의미를 갖추며, 언니가 결코 털어놓은 적 없는 사실을 그녀에게 일러 주었다."], ["She remembered that Laurie never spoke voluntarily of Jo; she recalled the shadow on his face just now, the change in his character, and the wearing of the little old ring, which was no ornament to a handsome hand.", "그녀는 로리가 한 번도 제 입으로 조 이야기를 꺼낸 적이 없음을 떠올렸고, 방금 그의 얼굴에 스친 그늘과, 그의 성격에 일어난 변화와, 잘생긴 손에 어울리는 장신구라곤 할 수 없는 그 작고 낡은 반지를 끼고 다니는 것을 곱씹어 보았다."], ["Girls are quick to read such signs and feel their eloquence.", "소녀들은 이런 징표를 재빨리 읽어 내고 그 웅변을 느끼는 법이다."], ["Amy had fancied that perhaps a love trouble was at the bottom of the alteration, and now she was sure of it.", "에이미는 어쩌면 사랑의 아픔이 그 변화의 밑바닥에 깔려 있는 게 아닐까 짐작해 왔는데, 이제 확신이 섰다."], ["Her keen eyes filled, and, when she spoke again, it was in a voice that could be beautifully soft and kind when she chose to make it so.", "그녀의 날카로운 눈에 물기가 차올랐고, 다시 입을 열었을 때 그 목소리는, 마음먹기에 따라 그토록 아름답게 부드럽고 다정해질 수 있는 그런 목소리였다."]], [["\"I know I have no right to talk so to you, Laurie; and if you weren't the sweetest-tempered fellow in the world, you'd be very angry with me.", "\"내가 당신한테 이렇게 말할 자격이 없다는 거 알아요, 로리. 만약 당신이 세상에서 제일 성품이 너그러운 사람이 아니었다면 나한테 무척 화를 냈겠죠."], ["But we are all so fond and proud of you, I couldn't bear to think they should be disappointed in you at home as I have been, though, perhaps, they would understand the change better than I do.\"", "하지만 우린 모두 당신을 무척 아끼고 자랑스러워하니까, 나처럼 집에서도 다들 당신한테 실망하게 된다고 생각하면 견딜 수가 없었어요. 어쩌면 그들은 나보다 이 변화를 더 잘 이해할지도 모르지만요.\""]], [["\"I think they would,\"", "\"그럴 거예요.\""], ["came from under the hat, in a grim tone, quite as touching as a broken one.", "모자 밑에서 무뚝뚝한 어조로 대답이 흘러나왔다. 그 무뚝뚝함은 떨리는 목소리만큼이나 가슴을 울렸다."]], [["\"They ought to have told me, and not let me go blundering and scolding, when I should have been more kind and patient than ever.", "\"진작 나한테 말해 줬어야죠. 그래서 내가 그렇게 더듬대며 잔소리나 늘어놓게 두지 말고요."], ["I never did like that Miss Randal, and now I hate her!\"", "난 그 어느 때보다 다정하고 인내심 있게 굴었어야 했는데. 난 그 랜들 양인지 뭔지 하는 여자가 처음부터 마음에 안 들었는데, 이제 그 여자가 미워요!\""], ["said artful Amy, wishing to be sure of her facts this time.", "교활한 에이미가 말했다. 이번에는 사실을 확실히 해 두고 싶었던 것이다."]], [["\"Hang Miss Randal!\"", "\"랜들 양이라니, 무슨!\""], ["and Laurie knocked the hat off his face with a look that left no doubt of his sentiments toward that young lady.", "그러면서 로리는 손으로 얼굴에서 모자를 쳐 냈다. 그 표정에는 그 젊은 아가씨에 대한 자기 감정이 어떠한지 의심의 여지가 없었다."]], [["\"I beg pardon; I thought--\"", "\"미안해요. 난 또—\""], ["and there she paused diplomatically.", "거기서 그녀는 능숙하게 말끝을 흐렸다."]], [["\"No, you didn't; you knew perfectly well I never cared for any one but Jo.\"", "\"아니, 그렇게 생각한 거 아니잖아요. 내가 조 말고는 그 누구한테도 마음을 둔 적 없다는 걸 당신은 똑똑히 알고 있으면서.\""], ["Laurie said that in his old, impetuous tone, and turned his face away as he spoke.", "로리는 예전의 그 격한 어조로 그렇게 말하고는, 말하는 동안 얼굴을 돌렸다."]], [["\"I did think so; but as they never said anything about it, and you came away, I supposed I was mistaken.", "\"그렇게 생각하긴 했어요. 하지만 다들 그 얘기는 한마디도 안 했고, 당신은 떠나 버렸으니, 내가 잘못 안 줄 알았죠."], ["And Jo wouldn't be kind to you?", "그래, 조가 당신한테 다정하게 굴지 않았다는 거예요?"], ["Why, I was sure she loved you dearly.\"", "어머, 난 조가 당신을 끔찍이 사랑하는 줄로만 알았는데.\""]], [["\"She _was_ kind, but not in the right way; and it's lucky for her she didn't love me, if I'm the good-for-nothing fellow you think me. It's her fault, though, and you may tell her so.\"", "\"다정하긴 했지. 하지만 옳은 방식은 아니었어요. 그리고 내가 당신 생각대로 그렇게 못난 녀석이라면, 걔가 날 사랑하지 않은 게 걔한텐 다행이지. 그래도 그건 걔 잘못이에요. 걔한테 그렇게 전해도 좋아요.\""]], [["The hard, bitter look came back again as he said that, and it troubled Amy, for she did not know what balm to apply.", "그 말을 하면서 단단하고 쓰라린 표정이 다시 돌아왔고, 그것이 에이미의 마음을 아프게 했다. 어떤 위로의 약을 발라 주어야 할지 알 수 없었기 때문이다."]], [["\"I was wrong, I didn't know.", "\"내가 잘못했어요, 몰랐던 거예요."], ["I'm very sorry I was so cross, but I can't help wishing you'd bear it better, Teddy, dear.\"", "그렇게 매몰차게 굴어서 정말 미안해요. 하지만 좀 더 의젓하게 견뎌 줬으면 하는 바람을 어쩔 수가 없네요, 테디.\""]], [["\"Don't, that's her name for me!\"", "\"하지 말아요, 그건 조가 날 부르던 이름이니까!\""], ["and Laurie put up his hand with a quick gesture to stop the words spoken in Jo's half-kind, half-reproachful tone.", "로리는 조의 반쯤은 다정하고 반쯤은 나무라는 듯한 그 어조로 나온 말을 가로막으려는 듯, 잽싼 몸짓으로 손을 들어 올렸다."], ["\"Wait till you've tried it yourself,\"", "\"당신도 직접 겪어 보고 나서 말해요.\""], ["he added, in a low voice, as he pulled up the grass by the handful.", "그는 한 움큼씩 풀을 쥐어뜯으며 낮은 목소리로 덧붙였다."]], [["\"I'd take it manfully, and be respected if I couldn't be loved,\"", "\"나라면 사내답게 견뎌 내겠어요. 사랑받지 못한다면 존경이라도 받겠어요.\""], ["said Amy, with the decision of one who knew nothing about it.", "에이미가 그 일에 대해 아무것도 모르는 사람 특유의 단호함으로 말했다."]], [["Now, Laurie flattered himself that he _had_ borne it remarkably well, making no moan, asking no sympathy, and taking his trouble away to live it down alone.", "그런데 로리는, 자기가 그것을 놀랄 만큼 잘 견뎌 왔다고, 신음 한 번 흘리지 않고 동정 한 번 구하지 않은 채 홀로 아픔을 이겨 내려고 짊어지고 다녔다고 자부하던 터였다."], ["Amy's lecture put the matter in a new light, and for the first time it did look weak and selfish to lose heart at the first failure, and shut himself up in moody indifference.", "에이미의 훈계는 그 일을 새로운 빛 아래 비추어 주었고, 처음으로 그는 첫 실패에 낙심해 음울한 무관심 속에 스스로를 가두는 것이 정말 나약하고 이기적으로 보였다."], ["He felt as if suddenly shaken out of a pensive dream, and found it impossible to go to sleep again.", "그는 마치 시름에 잠긴 꿈에서 갑작스레 흔들려 깨어난 듯했고, 다시 잠들기란 불가능하다는 것을 깨달았다."], ["Presently he sat up, and asked slowly,--", "이윽고 그는 몸을 일으켜 앉으며 천천히 물었다."]], [["\"Do you think Jo would despise me as you do?\"", "\"조도 당신처럼 날 경멸할까요?\""]], [["\"Yes, if she saw you now.", "\"네, 지금 당신을 본다면요."], ["She hates lazy people.", "그 애는 게으른 사람을 싫어해요."], ["Why don't you do something splendid, and _make_ her love you?\"", "뭔가 멋진 일을 해서 그 애가 당신을 사랑하게 만들면 되잖아요?\""]], [["\"I did my best, but it was no use.\"", "\"최선을 다했지만 소용없었어요.\""]], [["\"Graduating well, you mean?", "\"우수한 성적으로 졸업한 걸 말하는 거예요?"], ["That was no more than you ought to have done, for your grandfather's sake.", "그건 할아버지를 봐서라도 당연히 했어야 할 일 그 이상도 이하도 아니에요."], ["It would have been shameful to fail after spending so much time and money, when every one knew you _could_ do well.\"", "다들 당신이 잘할 수 있다는 걸 아는데, 그 많은 시간과 돈을 들이고도 실패했다면 부끄러운 일이었겠죠.\""]], [["\"I did fail, say what you will, for Jo wouldn't love me,\"", "\"뭐라 해도 난 실패한 거예요. 조가 날 사랑하지 않았으니까.\""], ["began Laurie, leaning his head on his hand in a despondent attitude.", "로리가 풀이 죽은 자세로 손에 머리를 괴며 입을 뗐다."]], [["\"No, you didn't, and you'll say so in the end, for it did you good, and proved that you could do something if you tried.", "\"아니요, 당신은 실패하지 않았어요. 결국엔 당신도 그렇게 말하게 될걸요."], ["If you'd only set about another task of some sort, you'd soon be your hearty, happy self again, and forget your trouble.\"", "그건 당신한테 좋은 일이었고, 당신이 마음만 먹으면 뭔가 해낼 수 있다는 걸 증명해 줬으니까요. 어떤 종류든 또 다른 일에 착수하기만 하면, 당신은 곧 예전처럼 활기차고 행복한 사람으로 돌아가서 아픔을 잊게 될 거예요.\""]], [["\"That's impossible.\"", "\"그건 불가능해요.\""]], [["\"Try it and see.", "\"해 보면 알아요. 어깨를 으쓱하면서 '저 애가 그런 걸 뭘 알아'"], ["You needn't shrug your shoulders, and think, 'Much she knows about such things.'", "하고 생각할 것 없어요. 내가 지혜로운 척하는 건 아니지만, 잘 살피긴 하거든요."], ["I don't pretend to be wise, but I _am_ observing, and I see a great deal more than you'd imagine.", "당신이 생각하는 것보다 훨씬 많은 걸 본다고요. 난 다른 사람들의 경험과 모순에 관심이 많아요."], ["I'm interested in other people's experiences and inconsistencies; and, though I can't explain, I remember and use them for my own benefit.", "그리고 설명은 못 해도, 그걸 기억해 두었다가 내게 도움이 되게 쓰죠. 원한다면 평생 조를 사랑해도 좋아요."], ["Love Jo all your days, if you choose, but don't let it spoil you, for it's wicked to throw away so many good gifts because you can't have the one you want.", "하지만 그것 때문에 자신을 망치진 말아요. 가질 수 없는 하나 때문에 그 많은 좋은 선물을 내던지는 건 못된 짓이니까요."], ["There, I won't lecture any more, for I know you'll wake up and be a man in spite of that hardhearted girl.\"", "자, 더는 잔소리 안 할게요. 그 모진 여자애가 어쨌든 간에, 당신이 정신을 차리고 어엿한 남자가 되리란 걸 아니까요.\""]], [["Neither spoke for several minutes.", "두 사람 다 한동안 말이 없었다."], ["Laurie sat turning the little ring on his finger, and Amy put the last touches to the hasty sketch she had been working at while she talked.", "로리는 손가락에 낀 작은 반지를 빙빙 돌리며 앉아 있었고, 에이미는 이야기하는 동안 그리던 즉석 스케치에 마지막 손질을 했다."], ["Presently she put it on his knee, merely saying,--", "이윽고 그녀는 그것을 그의 무릎에 올려놓으며 한마디만 했다."]], [["\"How do you like that?\"", "\"이거 어때요?\""]], [["He looked and then he smiled, as he could not well help doing, for it was capitally done,--the long, lazy figure on the grass, with listless face, half-shut eyes, and one hand holding a cigar, from which came the little wreath of smoke that encircled the dreamer's head.", "그는 그림을 보고는 미소 지을 수밖에 없었다. 솜씨가 빼어났기 때문이다. 풀밭 위에 길게 늘어진 게으른 형상, 무기력한 얼굴, 반쯤 감긴 눈, 그리고 한 손에 쥔 시가에서 피어오른 작은 연기 고리가 몽상가의 머리를 감싸고 있었다."]], [["\"How well you draw!\"", "\"정말 잘 그리는군!\""], ["he said, with genuine surprise and pleasure at her skill, adding, with a half-laugh,--", "그는 그녀의 솜씨에 진심으로 놀라고 기뻐하며 말하고는, 반쯤 웃으며 덧붙였다."]], [["\"Yes, that's me.\"", "\"그래요, 이게 나죠.\""]], [["\"As you are: this is as you were;\"", "\"지금의 당신이죠. 이건 예전의 당신이고요.\""], ["and Amy laid another sketch beside the one he held.", "그러면서 에이미는 그가 들고 있는 그림 옆에 또 다른 스케치 한 장을 내려놓았다."]], [["It was not nearly so well done, but there was a life and spirit in it which atoned for many faults, and it recalled the past so vividly that a sudden change swept over the young man's face as he looked.", "그것은 솜씨가 그만 못했지만, 여러 흠을 메워 줄 만큼 생기와 기백이 넘쳤고, 과거를 어찌나 생생하게 떠올려 주는지, 그것을 바라보는 젊은이의 얼굴 위로 별안간 변화가 스쳐 갔다. 그것은 로리가 말을 길들이는 모습을 거칠게 그린 스케치에 지나지 않았다."], ["Only a rough sketch of Laurie taming a horse; hat and coat were off, and every line of the active figure, resolute face, and commanding attitude, was full of energy and meaning.", "모자도 외투도 벗어 던졌고, 활동적인 형상과 결연한 얼굴과 위풍당당한 자세의 선 하나하나에 기운과 의미가 가득했다. 막 굴복당한 잘생긴 짐승은 팽팽히 당겨진 고삐 아래 목을 둥글게 휜 채, 한쪽 발로 안달하듯 땅을 차고, 자기를 제압한 그 목소리에 귀 기울이기라도 하듯 두 귀를 쫑긋 세우고 서 있었다."], ["The handsome brute, just subdued, stood arching his neck under the tightly drawn rein, with one foot impatiently pawing the ground, and ears pricked up as if listening for the voice that had mastered him.", "흐트러진 갈기와, 바람에 날리는 기수의 머리칼과 꼿꼿한 자세에는, 갑작스레 멈춰 선 동작과 힘과 용기와 젊음의 활기가 어려, '돌체 파르 니엔테' 스케치의 나른한 우아함과 날카롭게 대비되었다."], ["In the ruffled mane, the rider's breezy hair and erect attitude, there was a suggestion of suddenly arrested motion, of strength, courage, and youthful buoyancy, that contrasted sharply with the supine grace of the \"_Dolce far niente_\"", "로리는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 하지만 그의 눈이 한 그림에서 다른 그림으로 옮겨 가는 동안, 에이미는 그가 얼굴을 붉히며 입을 꾹 다무는 것을 보았다."], ["sketch. Laurie said nothing; but, as his eye went from one to the other, Amy saw him flush up and fold his lips together as if he read and accepted the little lesson she had given him.", "마치 그녀가 일러 준 그 작은 교훈을 읽고 받아들이기라도 하듯이."], ["That satisfied her; and, without waiting for him to speak, she said, in her sprightly way,--", "그것으로 그녀는 만족했고, 그가 입을 열기를 기다리지 않고 특유의 발랄한 어조로 말했다."]], [["\"Don't you remember the day you played Rarey with Puck, and we all looked on?", "\"당신이 퍽을 데리고 레어리 흉내를 내던 날 기억 안 나요? 우리가 다 같이 구경했잖아요."], ["Meg and Beth were frightened, but Jo clapped and pranced, and I sat on the fence and drew you.", "메그하고 베스는 무서워했지만, 조는 손뼉을 치며 깡충깡충 뛰었고, 난 울타리에 앉아서 당신을 그렸죠."], ["I found that sketch in my portfolio the other day, touched it up, and kept it to show you.\"", "며칠 전에 화첩에서 그 스케치를 발견하고는, 손을 좀 보고 당신한테 보여 주려고 간직해 뒀어요.\""]], [["\"Much obliged.", "* 레어리 : 당대에 유명했던 미국의 조련사 존 레어리."], ["You've improved immensely since then, and I congratulate you.", "말을 부드럽게 길들이는 기법으로 이름을 날렸다 : 옮긴이. \"고마워요."], ["May I venture to suggest in 'a honeymoon Paradise'", "그때 이후로 솜씨가 엄청나게 늘었군요. 축하해요."], ["that five o'clock is the dinner-hour at your hotel?\"", "그런데 '신혼여행 낙원'에서 외람되지만 한 말씀 드려도 될까요? 당신네 호텔 저녁 식사 시간이 다섯 시던데요.\""]], [["Laurie rose as he spoke, returned the pictures with a smile and a bow, and looked at his watch, as if to remind her that even moral lectures should have an end.", "로리는 말하면서 일어나, 미소와 함께 정중히 고개 숙여 그림들을 돌려주고는, 도덕적인 훈계에도 끝이 있어야 한다는 것을 일깨우려는 듯 시계를 들여다보았다."], ["He tried to resume his former easy, indifferent air, but it _was_ an affectation now, for the rousing had been more efficacious than he would confess.", "그는 예전의 편안하고 무심한 태도를 되찾으려 애썼지만, 이제 그것은 꾸민 태도에 불과했다. 그를 흔들어 깨운 것이 그가 인정하려는 것보다 훨씬 큰 효과를 냈기 때문이다."], ["Amy felt the shade of coldness in his manner, and said to herself,--", "에이미는 그의 태도에 드리운 차가운 그늘을 느끼고 속으로 중얼거렸다."]], [["\"Now I've offended him.", "\"이제 내가 저이 기분을 상하게 했네."], ["Well, if it does him good, I'm glad; if it makes him hate me, I'm sorry; but it's true, and I can't take back a word of it.\"", "음, 그게 저이한테 도움이 된다면 기쁘고, 저이가 날 미워하게 된다면 슬프지만, 사실인 건 사실이니 한마디도 도로 거둘 순 없어.\""]], [["They laughed and chatted all the way home; and little Baptiste, up behind, thought that monsieur and mademoiselle were in charming spirits.", "두 사람은 집으로 오는 내내 웃고 떠들었고, 뒤편에 앉은 어린 바티스트는 무슈와 마드무아젤이 기분이 아주 좋은 모양이라고 생각했다."], ["But both felt ill at ease; the friendly frankness was disturbed, the sunshine had a shadow over it, and despite their apparent gayety, there was a secret discontent in the heart of each.", "하지만 둘 다 마음이 편치 않았다. 다정한 솔직함이 흐트러졌고, 햇살 위에 그늘이 드리웠으며, 겉으로는 명랑해 보였어도 각자의 마음속에는 남모를 불만이 자리 잡고 있었다."]], [["\"Shall we see you this evening, _mon frère_?\"", "\"오늘 저녁에 만날까요, 몽 프레르?\""], ["asked Amy as they parted at her aunt's door.", "에이미가 숙모의 집 앞에서 헤어지며 물었다."]], [["\"Unfortunately I have an engagement.", "\"안타깝게도 선약이 있어요."], ["_Au revoir, mademoiselle_,\"", "오 르부아르, 마드무아젤.\""], ["and Laurie bent as if to kiss her hand, in the foreign fashion, which became him better than many men.", "그러면서 로리는 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추려는 듯, 다른 많은 남자들보다 그에게 더 잘 어울리는 그 이국적인 방식으로 몸을 숙였다."], ["Something in his face made Amy say quickly and warmly,--", "그의 얼굴에 어린 무언가가 에이미로 하여금 다급하고 다정하게 말하게 했다."]], [["\"No; be yourself with me, Laurie, and part in the good old way.", "\"안 돼요. 나한테는 본래의 당신으로 있어 줘요, 로리."], ["I'd rather have a hearty English hand-shake than all the sentimental salutations in France.\"", "그리고 그 좋은 옛날 방식으로 헤어지자고요. 난 프랑스식의 그 모든 감상적인 인사보다 영국식의 힘찬 악수가 더 좋아요.\""]], [["\"Good-by, dear,\"", "\"잘 있어요, 그럼.\""], ["and with these words, uttered in the tone she liked, Laurie left her, after a hand-shake almost painful in its heartiness.", "그녀가 좋아하는 그 어조로 이 말을 건네고는, 거의 아플 만큼 힘찬 악수를 끝으로 로리는 그녀 곁을 떠났다."]], [["Next morning, instead of the usual call, Amy received a note which made her smile at the beginning and sigh at the end:--", "이튿날 아침, 평소의 방문 대신 에이미는 한 통의 쪽지를 받았는데, 그 글은 시작 부분에서는 그녀를 미소 짓게 했고 끝부분에서는 한숨짓게 했다."]], [["\"Please make my adieux to your aunt, and exult within yourself, for 'Lazy Laurence'", "\"나의 멘토에게,\n숙모님께 작별 인사를 전해 주시고, 마음속으로 의기양양해하셔도 좋습니다."], ["has gone to his grandpa, like the best of boys.", "'게으른 로런스'가 더없이 착한 소년처럼 할아버지께로 갔으니까요."], ["A pleasant winter to you, and may the gods grant you a blissful honeymoon at Valrosa!", "즐거운 겨울 보내시길, 그리고 신들께서 발로사에서의 더없이 행복한 신혼여행을 당신께 허락하시기를!"], ["I think Fred would be benefited by a rouser.", "프레드에게는 한바탕 흔들어 깨우는 게 약이 될 것 같군요."], ["Tell him so, with my congratulations.", "내 축하의 말과 함께 그렇게 전해 주세요."]], [["\"Yours gratefully, TELEMACHUS.\"", "감사를 담아, 텔레마코스.\""]], [["\"Good boy!", "* 멘토·텔레마코스 : 호메로스의 서사시에서 멘토는 텔레마코스를 이끈 스승. 에이미가 자신을 가르쳤다는 뜻에서 로리가 스스로를 텔레마코스에, 에이미를 멘토에 빗댄 것이다 : 옮긴이."], ["I'm glad he's gone,\"", "\"착하기도 하지! 떠났다니 다행이야.\""], ["said Amy, with an approving smile; the next minute her face fell as she glanced about the empty room, adding, with an involuntary sigh,--", "에이미가 흐뭇한 미소를 지으며 말했다. 그러나 다음 순간, 텅 빈 방을 둘러보던 그녀의 얼굴은 어두워졌고, 그녀는 저도 모르게 한숨을 쉬며 덧붙였다."]], [["\"Yes, I _am_ glad, but how I shall miss him!\"", "\"그래, 다행이긴 한데, 그이가 얼마나 그리울까!\""]], [["# THE VALLEY OF THE SHADOW.", "# THE VALLEY OF THE SHADOW."]], [["When the first bitterness was over, the family accepted the inevitable, and tried to bear it cheerfully, helping one another by the increased affection which comes to bind households tenderly together in times of trouble.", "처음의 쓰라림이 가시자, 가족은 피할 수 없는 일을 받아들이고 그것을 의연하게 견디려 애썼다. 어려운 시절에 한 가정을 다정하게 묶어 주는, 한층 깊어진 애정으로 서로를 도우면서."], ["They put away their grief, and each did his or her part toward making that last year a happy one.", "그들은 슬픔을 한쪽으로 밀어 두고, 그 마지막 한 해를 행복한 한 해로 만들기 위해 저마다 제 몫을 다했다."]], [["The pleasantest room in the house was set apart for Beth, and in it was gathered everything that she most loved,--flowers, pictures, her piano, the little work-table, and the beloved pussies.", "집에서 가장 쾌적한 방을 베스에게 따로 마련해 주었고, 그 방에는 베스가 가장 사랑하는 모든 것을 모아 두었다. 꽃, 그림, 그녀의 피아노, 작은 반짇고리, 그리고 사랑하는 고양이들."], ["Father's best books found their way there, mother's easy-chair, Jo's desk, Amy's finest sketches; and every day Meg brought her babies on a loving pilgrimage, to make sunshine for Aunty Beth.", "아버지의 가장 아끼는 책들이 그리로 옮겨졌고, 어머니의 안락의자며, 조의 책상이며, 에이미의 가장 멋진 스케치들도 그리로 왔다. 그리고 날마다 메그는 베스 이모에게 햇살을 비춰 주려고 사랑스러운 순례 길에 아기들을 데려왔다."], ["John quietly set apart a little sum, that he might enjoy the pleasure of keeping the invalid supplied with the fruit she loved and longed for; old Hannah never wearied of concocting dainty dishes to tempt a capricious appetite, dropping tears as she worked; and from across the sea came little gifts and cheerful letters, seeming to bring breaths of warmth and fragrance from lands that know no winter.", "존은 그 환자에게 그녀가 좋아하고 그리워하는 과일을 끊이지 않게 대 주는 기쁨을 누리려고 조용히 약간의 돈을 따로 떼어 두었고, 늙은 해나는 변덕스러운 입맛을 돋우려 맛깔스러운 음식을 만드는 일에 도무지 지치는 법이 없었으니, 일하는 내내 눈물을 떨구었다. 그리고 바다 건너편에서는 자그마한 선물과 명랑한 편지들이 와서, 겨울을 모르는 땅에서 따스함과 향기의 숨결을 실어 오는 듯했다."]], [["Here, cherished like a household saint in its shrine, sat Beth, tranquil and busy as ever; for nothing could change the sweet, unselfish nature, and even while preparing to leave life, she tried to make it happier for those who should remain behind.", "여기, 신전 안에 모셔진 가정의 성인처럼, 베스는 여느 때나 다름없이 평온하고 부지런하게 앉아 있었다. 그 무엇도 그 곱고 헌신적인 천성을 바꾸지 못했으니, 삶을 떠날 채비를 하는 와중에도 그녀는 뒤에 남을 이들을 위해 삶을 더 행복하게 만들려 애썼다."], ["The feeble fingers were never idle, and one of her pleasures was to make little things for the school-children daily passing to and fro,--to drop a pair of mittens from her window for a pair of purple hands, a needle-book for some small mother of many dolls, pen-wipers for young penmen toiling through forests of pot-hooks, scrap-books for picture-loving eyes, and all manner of pleasant devices, till the reluctant climbers up the ladder of learning found their way strewn with flowers, as it were, and came to regard the gentle giver as a sort of fairy godmother, who sat above there, and showered down gifts miraculously suited to their tastes and needs.", "가냘픈 손가락은 한시도 놀지 않았고, 그녀의 즐거움 가운데 하나는 날마다 오가는 학교 아이들을 위해 자그마한 것들을 만드는 일이었다. 보랏빛으로 곱은 손을 위해 창문으로 벙어리장갑 한 켤레를 떨어뜨려 주고, 인형을 잔뜩 거느린 어린 엄마에게는 바늘집을, 갈고리 모양 글자의 숲을 헤쳐 나가는 어린 글공부꾼에게는 펜 닦개를, 그림 좋아하는 눈을 위해서는 스크랩북을, 그 밖에도 온갖 즐거운 물건을 만들어 주었다."], ["If Beth had wanted any reward, she found it in the bright little faces always turned up to her window, with nods and smiles, and the droll little letters which came to her, full of blots and gratitude.", "그리하여 배움의 사다리를 마지못해 오르던 아이들은 제 갈 길에 꽃이 뿌려진 것을 발견하게 되었고, 저 위에 앉아 자기들의 취향과 필요에 신통하게도 꼭 맞는 선물을 비처럼 뿌려 주는 그 다정한 분을, 일종의 요정 대모로 여기게 되었다. 베스가 어떤 보답을 바랐다면, 그녀는 늘 자기 창문을 올려다보며 고개를 끄덕이고 미소 짓는 그 환한 작은 얼굴들에서, 그리고 잉크 얼룩과 고마움으로 가득한 우스꽝스러운 작은 편지들 속에서 그 보답을 찾았다."]], [["The first few months were very happy ones, and Beth often used to look round, and say \"How beautiful this is!\" as they all sat together in her sunny room, the babies kicking and crowing on the floor, mother and sisters working near, and father reading, in his pleasant voice, from the wise old books which seemed rich in good and comfortable words, as applicable now as when written centuries ago; a little chapel, where a paternal priest taught his flock the hard lessons all must learn, trying to show them that hope can comfort love, and faith make resignation possible. Simple sermons, that went straight to the souls of those who listened; for the father's heart was in the minister's religion, and the frequent falter in the voice gave a double eloquence to the words he spoke or read.", "처음 몇 달은 무척 행복했다. 베스는 자주 주위를 둘러보며 \"정말 아름다워!\" 하고 말하곤 했다. 모두가 그녀의 볕 잘 드는 방에 함께 모여 앉아, 아기들이 바닥에서 발길질하며 옹알이하고, 어머니와 자매들이 곁에서 바느질하며, 아버지가 그 다정한 목소리로 지혜로운 옛 책들을 읽어 주던 때 말이다. 그 책들은 좋고 위로가 되는 말씀이 풍성하여, 수백 년 전에 쓰였을 때나 지금이나 한결같이 들어맞는 듯했다. 그곳은 작은 예배당이었고, 아버지라는 사제가 자기 양 떼에게 누구나 배워야 할 그 어려운 가르침을 일러 주며, 희망이 사랑을 위로할 수 있고 믿음이 체념을 가능하게 한다는 것을 보여 주려 애썼다. 듣는 이의 영혼에 곧장 가닿는 소박한 설교였다. 그 목사의 신앙 속에 아버지의 마음이 깃들어 있었기에, 자주 떨리던 그 목소리가 그가 말하거나 읽는 말씀에 곱절의 웅변을 더해 주었던 것이다."]], [["It was well for all that this peaceful time was given them as preparation for the sad hours to come; for, by and by, Beth said the needle was \"so heavy,\"", "다가올 슬픈 시간을 위한 준비로 이 평화로운 시간이 그들에게 주어진 것은 모두에게 다행한 일이었다. 머지않아 베스가 바늘이 \"너무 무겁다\"고 말하며 그것을 영영 내려놓았기 때문이다."], ["and put it down forever; talking wearied her, faces troubled her, pain claimed her for its own, and her tranquil spirit was sorrowfully perturbed by the ills that vexed her feeble flesh. Ah me!", "말하는 것이 그녀를 지치게 했고, 사람들의 얼굴이 그녀를 괴롭혔으며, 고통이 그녀를 제 것이라 주장했고, 그 평온한 영혼은 가냘픈 육신을 괴롭히는 병고에 슬프도록 흔들렸다."], ["such heavy days, such long, long nights, such aching hearts and imploring prayers, when those who loved her best were forced to see the thin hands stretched out to them beseechingly, to hear the bitter cry, \"Help me, help me!\"", "아, 그 얼마나 무거운 나날이었던가, 그 얼마나 길고 긴 밤이었던가, 그 얼마나 미어지는 가슴과 애원하는 기도였던가. 그녀를 가장 사랑하는 이들이, 그 야윈 손이 자기들을 향해 애원하듯 뻗쳐 오는 것을 보아야 했고, \"도와줘, 도와줘!\""], ["and to feel that there was no help.", "하는 쓰라린 울부짖음을 들어야 했으며, 그러면서도 도울 길이 없음을 느껴야 했으니. 평온하던 영혼의 슬픈 일식이요, 젊은 생명이 죽음과 벌이는 모진 싸움이었다."], ["A sad eclipse of the serene soul, a sharp struggle of the young life with death; but both were mercifully brief, and then, the natural rebellion over, the old peace returned more beautiful than ever.", "하지만 둘 다 자비롭게도 짧았고, 이윽고 그 자연스러운 저항이 끝나자 옛 평화가 그 어느 때보다 아름답게 되돌아왔다. 가녀린 육신이 무너져 가는 가운데, 베스의 영혼은 강해졌다."], ["With the wreck of her frail body, Beth's soul grew strong; and, though she said little, those about her felt that she was ready, saw that the first pilgrim called was likewise the fittest, and waited with her on the shore, trying to see the Shining Ones coming to receive her when she crossed the river.", "그녀는 말은 거의 없었지만, 곁에 있는 이들은 그녀가 채비를 마쳤음을 느꼈고, 가장 먼저 부름받은 순례자가 또한 가장 합당한 자임을 보았으며, 그녀와 함께 강기슭에서 기다렸다. 그녀가 강을 건널 때 그녀를 맞으러 오는 빛나는 이들을 보려 애쓰면서."]], [["Jo never left her for an hour since Beth had said, \"I feel stronger when you are here.\"", "조는 베스가 \"네가 곁에 있으면 더 힘이 나\"라고 말한 뒤로, 단 한 시간도 그녀 곁을 떠나지 않았다. 그녀는 방 안의 긴 의자에서 자다가 자주 깨어 불을 다시 지피고, 좀처럼 무엇도 청하는 법 없이 \"폐가 되지 않으려\""], ["She slept on a couch in the room, waking often to renew the fire, to feed, lift, or wait upon the patient creature who seldom asked for anything, and \"tried not to be a trouble.\"", "애쓰는 그 가여운 환자에게 먹을 것을 떠먹이고, 일으켜 앉히고, 시중을 들었다. 온종일 그녀는 그 방을 떠나지 못하고 다른 간병인이 끼어드는 것을 질투했으며, 그때 간병할 이로 뽑힌 것을, 제 인생이 가져다준 그 어떤 영예보다 더 자랑스러워했다."], ["All day she haunted the room, jealous of any other nurse, and prouder of being chosen then than of any honor her life ever brought her.", "조에게는 더없이 소중하고 보탬이 되는 시간이었다. 이제 그녀의 마음이 꼭 필요한 가르침을 받았으니까."], ["Precious and helpful hours to Jo, for now her heart received the teaching that it needed; lessons in patience were so sweetly taught her that she could not fail to learn them; charity for all, the lovely spirit that can forgive and truly forget unkindness, the loyalty to duty that makes the hardest easy, and the sincere faith that fears nothing, but trusts undoubtingly.", "인내의 교훈이 어찌나 곱게 가르쳐졌던지 그녀는 그것을 배우지 않을 수 없었다. 모두를 향한 자애, 모진 처사를 용서하고 참되이 잊을 줄 아는 그 사랑스러운 마음, 가장 힘든 일도 수월하게 만드는 의무에 대한 충실함, 그리고 아무것도 두려워하지 않고 의심 없이 믿는 진실한 신앙을."]], [["Often, when she woke, Jo found Beth reading in her well-worn little book, heard her singing softly, to beguile the sleepless night, or saw her lean her face upon her hands, while slow tears dropped through the transparent fingers; and Jo would lie watching her, with thoughts too deep for tears, feeling that Beth, in her simple, unselfish way, was trying to wean herself from the dear old life, and fit herself for the life to come, by sacred words of comfort, quiet prayers, and the music she loved so well.", "조는 잠에서 깰 때면 베스가 손때 묻은 작은 책을 읽고 있는 것을 자주 보았고, 잠 못 드는 밤을 달래려 가만히 노래 부르는 것을 듣기도 했으며, 투명한 손가락 사이로 느린 눈물이 떨어지는 동안 두 손에 얼굴을 묻는 것을 보기도 했다. 그러면 조는 누운 채로 그녀를 지켜보며, 눈물로도 다 담을 수 없을 만큼 깊은 생각에 잠기곤 했다. 베스가 그 소박하고 헌신적인 방식으로, 위로의 거룩한 말씀과 조용한 기도와 그토록 사랑하던 음악을 통해, 정든 옛 삶에서 자신을 떼어 내고 다가올 삶에 자신을 맞추려 애쓰고 있음을 느끼면서."]], [["Seeing this did more for Jo than the wisest sermons, the saintliest hymns, the most fervent prayers that any voice could utter; for, with eyes made clear by many tears, and a heart softened by the tenderest sorrow, she recognized the beauty of her sister's life,--uneventful, unambitious, yet full of the genuine virtues which \"smell sweet, and blossom in the dust,\" the self-forgetfulness that makes the humblest on earth remembered soonest in heaven, the true success which is possible to all.", "이것을 지켜보는 일은, 그 어떤 목소리로 읊어진 가장 지혜로운 설교나, 가장 성스러운 찬송가나, 가장 간절한 기도보다도 조에게 더 큰 것을 해 주었다. 숱한 눈물로 맑아진 눈과, 더없이 다정한 슬픔으로 보드라워진 마음으로, 그녀는 언니의 삶이 지닌 아름다움을 알아보게 되었으니까. 굵직한 사건도 없고 야망도 없었으나, \"흙먼지 속에서도 향기를 풍기며 피어나는\" 참된 미덕으로 가득했던 삶을. 땅 위에서 가장 비천한 이를 하늘에서 가장 먼저 기억하게 하는 그 자기 망각을, 누구에게나 가능한 그 진정한 성공을."]], [["One night, when Beth looked among the books upon her table, to find something to make her forget the mortal weariness that was almost as hard to bear as pain, as she turned the leaves of her old favorite Pilgrim's Progress, she found a little paper, scribbled over in Jo's hand.", "어느 밤, 베스가 고통만큼이나 견디기 힘든 그 죽을 것 같은 피로를 잊게 해 줄 무언가를 찾으려고 탁자 위의 책들을 뒤지다가, 가장 좋아하던 낡은 『천로역정』의 책장을 넘기던 중, 조의 필체로 휘갈겨 쓴 작은 종이 한 장을 발견했다."], ["The name caught her eye, and the blurred look of the lines made her sure that tears had fallen on it.", "제 이름이 눈에 들어왔고, 흐릿하게 번진 글줄들로 미루어 그 위에 눈물이 떨어졌었음을 확신했다."]], [["\"Poor Jo!", "\"가엾은 조! 곤히 잠들어 있으니 깨워서 허락을 구하진 않을래."], ["she's fast asleep, so I won't wake her to ask leave; she shows me all her things, and I don't think she'll mind if I look at this,\"", "언닌 나한테 뭐든 다 보여 주잖아. 그러니 이걸 봐도 마음 쓰지 않을 거야.\""], ["thought Beth, with a glance at her sister, who lay on the rug, with the tongs beside her, ready to wake up the minute the log fell apart.", "베스는 양탄자 위에 누운 언니를 흘끗 보며 생각했다. 조는 장작이 무너지는 순간 곧장 깰 채비로, 부젓가락을 곁에 둔 채 누워 있었다."]], [["\"Sitting patient in the shadow Till the blessed light shall come, A serene and saintly presence Sanctifies our troubled home.", "\"나의 베스에게. 축복의 빛이 찾아올 때까지 그늘 속에 묵묵히 앉아, 고요하고 성스러운 그 존재가 근심 어린 우리 집을 거룩하게 하네."], ["Earthly joys and hopes and sorrows Break like ripples on the strand Of the deep and solemn river Where her willing feet now stand.", "땅 위의 기쁨도 희망도 슬픔도 깊고 엄숙한 강의 기슭에서 잔물결처럼 부서지나니, 이제 그녀의 발길이 기꺼이 그곳에 서 있네."]], [["\"O my sister, passing from me, Out of human care and strife, Leave me, as a gift, those virtues Which have beautified your life.", "오 나의 언니, 내게서 떠나 인간의 근심과 다툼에서 벗어나는 이여, 당신의 삶을 아름답게 한 그 미덕들을 선물로 내게 남겨 주오."], ["Dear, bequeath me that great patience Which has power to sustain A cheerful, uncomplaining spirit In its prison-house of pain.", "사랑하는 언니, 그 위대한 인내를 물려주오, 고통의 감옥 속에서도 밝고 불평 없는 정신을 지탱할 힘을 지닌 그 인내를."]], [["\"Give me, for I need it sorely, Of that courage, wise and sweet, Which has made the path of duty Green beneath your willing feet.", "내게 주오, 절실히 필요하니, 지혜롭고 다정한 그 용기를, 당신의 기꺼운 발길 아래 의무의 길을 푸르게 만든 그 용기를."], ["Give me that unselfish nature, That with charity divine Can pardon wrong for love's dear sake-- Meek heart, forgive me mine!", "그 사심 없는 천성을 내게 주오, 거룩한 자애로 사랑을 위해 잘못을 용서할 줄 아는 그 마음을— 온유한 마음이여, 나의 잘못을 용서해 주오!"]], [["\"Thus our parting daily loseth Something of its bitter pain, And while learning this hard lesson, My great loss becomes my gain.", "그리하여 우리의 이별은 날마다 그 쓰라린 아픔을 조금씩 덜어 가고, 이 모진 가르침을 배우는 동안 나의 큰 상실은 나의 얻음이 되네."], ["For the touch of grief will render My wild nature more serene, Give to life new aspirations, A new trust in the unseen.", "슬픔의 손길이 나의 거친 천성을 한결 평온하게 하고, 삶에 새로운 열망을, 보이지 않는 것에 대한 새 믿음을 줄 테니."]], [["\"Henceforth, safe across the river, I shall see forevermore A beloved, household spirit Waiting for me on the shore.", "이제부터 강을 무사히 건너, 나는 영원히 보게 되리, 강기슭에서 나를 기다리는 사랑하는, 가정의 영혼을."], ["Hope and faith, born of my sorrow, Guardian angels shall become, And the sister gone before me By their hands shall lead me home.\"", "나의 슬픔에서 태어난 희망과 믿음이 수호천사가 되어, 나보다 먼저 떠난 언니가 그들의 손을 빌려 나를 집으로 이끌어 주리.\""]], [["Blurred and blotted, faulty and feeble, as the lines were, they brought a look of inexpressible comfort to Beth's face, for her one regret had been that she had done so little; and this seemed to assure her that her life had not been useless, that her death would not bring the despair she feared.", "글줄들은 흐릿하게 번지고 얼룩지고, 서툴고 힘이 없었지만, 베스의 얼굴에 이루 말할 수 없는 위안의 빛을 가져다주었다. 그녀의 한 가지 회한은 자기가 한 일이 너무 적었다는 것이었는데, 이 글이 그녀에게, 그녀의 삶이 헛되지 않았음을, 그녀의 죽음이 그녀가 두려워하던 절망을 불러오지 않으리란 것을 확신시켜 주는 듯했기 때문이다."], ["As she sat with the paper folded between her hands, the charred log fell asunder.", "그녀가 두 손 사이에 종이를 접어 쥔 채 앉아 있는데, 까맣게 탄 장작이 두 동강 났다."], ["Jo started up, revived the blaze, and crept to the bedside, hoping Beth slept.", "조가 벌떡 일어나 불길을 되살리고는 베스가 잠들었기를 바라며 침대 곁으로 살금살금 다가왔다."]], [["\"Not asleep, but so happy, dear.", "\"잠든 거 아냐, 오히려 무척 행복해, 조."], ["See, I found this and read it; I knew you wouldn't care.", "이것 봐, 내가 이걸 발견해서 읽었어. 언니가 괜찮아할 줄 알았거든."], ["Have I been all that to you, Jo?\"", "내가 너한테 정말 이런 사람이었니, 조?\""], ["she asked, with wistful, humble earnestness.", "베스가 그립고도 겸허한 진심을 담아 물었다."]], [["\"O Beth, so much, so much!\"", "\"오 베스, 정말 그래, 정말 그렇고말고!\""], ["and Jo's head went down upon the pillow, beside her sister's.", "그러면서 조의 머리가 언니 곁의 베개 위로 떨어졌다."]], [["\"Then I don't feel as if I'd wasted my life.", "\"그렇다면 내 인생을 허비한 게 아닌 것 같아."], ["I'm not so good as you make me, but I _have_ tried to do right; and now, when it's too late to begin even to do better, it's such a comfort to know that some one loves me so much, and feels as if I'd helped them.\"", "난 네가 그려 준 만큼 착하진 않지만, 옳게 살려고 애쓰긴 했거든. 그리고 이제 더 잘 살아 보기를 시작하기에도 너무 늦은 마당에, 누군가 날 이렇게나 사랑하고, 내가 그들에게 도움이 됐다고 느낀다는 걸 아는 게 이렇게 위안이 되네.\""]], [["\"More than any one in the world, Beth.", "\"세상 그 누구보다도 그래, 베스."], ["I used to think I couldn't let you go; but I'm learning to feel that I don't lose you; that you'll be more to me than ever, and death can't part us, though it seems to.\"", "난 언닐 보낼 수 없을 것만 같았어. 하지만 이젠 내가 언닐 잃는 게 아니라는 걸, 언니가 그 어느 때보다 내게 더 큰 존재가 되리란 걸, 죽음이 우릴 갈라놓는 것 같아도 결코 갈라놓을 수 없다는 걸 느끼는 법을 배워 가고 있어.\""]], [["\"I know it cannot, and I don't fear it any longer, for I'm sure I shall be your Beth still, to love and help you more than ever. You must take my place, Jo, and be everything to father and mother when I'm gone. They will turn to you, don't fail them; and if it's hard to work alone, remember that I don't forget you, and that you'll be happier in doing that than writing splendid books or seeing all the world; for love is the only thing that we can carry with us when we go, and it makes the end so easy.\"", "\"그럴 수 없다는 거, 나도 알아. 그래서 더는 두렵지 않아. 난 분명 여전히 너의 베스로 남아서, 그 어느 때보다 더 너를 사랑하고 돕게 될 테니까. 네가 내 자리를 대신해 줘야 해, 조. 내가 떠나고 나면 아버지 어머니께 모든 것이 되어 드려. 두 분은 너한테 기대실 테니, 두 분을 저버리지 마. 그리고 혼자 애쓰는 게 힘들거든, 내가 너를 잊지 않는다는 걸, 그리고 그렇게 하는 것이 멋진 책을 쓰거나 온 세상을 구경하는 것보다 너를 더 행복하게 하리란 걸 기억해. 사랑이야말로 우리가 떠날 때 함께 가져갈 수 있는 유일한 것이고, 그것이 마지막을 그토록 수월하게 해 주니까.\""]], [["\"I'll try, Beth;\"", "\"그렇게 할게, 베스.\""], ["and then and there Jo renounced her old ambition, pledged herself to a new and better one, acknowledging the poverty of other desires, and feeling the blessed solace of a belief in the immortality of love.", "그리고 바로 그때 그 자리에서, 조는 옛 야망을 버리고 더 새롭고 더 나은 야망에 자신을 바치기로 다짐했다. 다른 욕망들의 빈곤함을 인정하며, 사랑의 불멸에 대한 믿음이 주는 그 복된 위안을 느끼면서."]], [["So the spring days came and went, the sky grew clearer, the earth greener, the flowers were up fair and early, and the birds came back in time to say good-by to Beth, who, like a tired but trustful child, clung to the hands that had led her all her life, as father and mother guided her tenderly through the Valley of the Shadow, and gave her up to God.", "그렇게 봄날은 오고 갔고, 하늘은 더 맑아지고, 땅은 더 푸르러지고, 꽃들은 곱고 일찍 피어났으며, 새들은 베스에게 작별을 고하러 때맞춰 돌아왔다. 베스는 지치되 미더운 아이처럼, 평생 자기를 이끌어 준 그 손들을 꼭 붙들었고, 아버지와 어머니는 죽음의 골짜기를 지나는 그녀를 다정하게 인도하여 하느님께 그녀를 내드렸다."]], [["Seldom, except in books, do the dying utter memorable words, see visions, or depart with beatified countenances; and those who have sped many parting souls know that to most the end comes as naturally and simply as sleep.", "책 속에서가 아니고서는, 죽어 가는 이가 기억에 남을 말을 하거나, 환영을 보거나, 거룩한 빛이 어린 얼굴로 떠나는 일은 좀처럼 없다. 그리고 떠나는 영혼을 숱하게 배웅해 본 이들은, 대다수에게 끝이란 잠처럼 자연스럽고 단순하게 찾아온다는 것을 안다."], ["As Beth had hoped, the \"tide went out easily;\"", "베스가 바라던 대로 \"물결은 수월하게 빠져나갔다.\""], ["and in the dark hour before the dawn, on the bosom where she had drawn her first breath, she quietly drew her last, with no farewell but one loving look, one little sigh.", "그리고 동트기 전 어두운 시각, 처음 숨을 들이쉬던 그 품 안에서, 그녀는 단 한 번의 사랑스러운 눈길과 단 한 번의 작은 한숨 말고는 작별 인사도 없이, 조용히 마지막 숨을 거두었다."]], [["With tears and prayers and tender hands, mother and sisters made her ready for the long sleep that pain would never mar again, seeing with grateful eyes the beautiful serenity that soon replaced the pathetic patience that had wrung their hearts so long, and feeling, with reverent joy, that to their darling death was a benignant angel, not a phantom full of dread.", "눈물과 기도와 다정한 손길로, 어머니와 자매들은 고통이 다시는 그르칠 수 없는 그 긴 잠을 위해 그녀를 단장해 주었다. 그토록 오래 그들의 가슴을 미어지게 했던 가련한 인내가 이내 물러나고 그 자리에 깃든 아름다운 평온을 고마운 눈으로 바라보면서, 그리고 사랑하는 이에게 죽음이란 두려움 가득한 유령이 아니라 자애로운 천사임을 경건한 기쁨으로 느끼면서."]], [["When morning came, for the first time in many months the fire was out, Jo's place was empty, and the room was very still.", "아침이 밝았을 때, 여러 달 만에 처음으로 난롯불은 꺼져 있었고, 조의 자리는 비어 있었으며, 방은 무척 고요했다."], ["But a bird sang blithely on a budding bough, close by, the snow-drops blossomed freshly at the window, and the spring sunshine streamed in like a benediction over the placid face upon the pillow,--a face so full of painless peace that those who loved it best smiled through their tears, and thanked God that Beth was well at last.", "하지만 바로 곁의 움트는 나뭇가지 위에서 새 한 마리가 명랑하게 노래했고, 창가에는 스노드롭이 싱그럽게 피어났으며, 봄 햇살이 축복처럼 흘러들어 베개 위의 그 평온한 얼굴을 비추었다. 고통 없는 평화로 그토록 가득한 얼굴이라, 그녀를 가장 사랑하던 이들은 눈물 너머로 미소 지으며, 베스가 마침내 평안해졌음을 하느님께 감사드렸다."]], [["# LEARNING TO FORGET.", "# LEARNING TO FORGET."]], [["Amy's lecture did Laurie good, though, of course, he did not own it till long afterward; men seldom do, for when women are the advisers, the lords of creation don't take the advice till they have persuaded themselves that it is just what they intended to do; then they act upon it, and, if it succeeds, they give the weaker vessel half the credit of it; if it fails, they generously give her the whole. Laurie went back to his grandfather, and was so dutifully devoted for several weeks that the old gentleman declared the climate of Nice had improved him wonderfully, and he had better try it again. There was nothing the young gentleman would have liked better, but elephants could not have dragged him back after the scolding he had received; pride forbid, and whenever the longing grew very strong, he fortified his resolution by repeating the words that had made the deepest impression, \"I despise you;\" \"Go and do something splendid that will _make_ her love you.\"", "에이미의 훈계는 로리에게 도움이 되었다. 물론 그는 한참 뒤에야 그것을 인정했지만 말이다. 남자들이란 좀처럼 그러지 않는 법이다. 여자가 충고를 하면, 만물의 영장이라는 그들은 그것이 마침 자기가 하려던 바로 그것이라고 스스로를 설득하기 전까지는 충고를 받아들이지 않으니까. 그렇게 설득하고 나서야 그대로 행동하고, 만약 그것이 성공하면 그 공의 절반을 약한 그릇인 여자에게 돌리며, 만약 실패하면 너그럽게도 그 책임 전부를 여자에게 떠넘긴다. 로리는 할아버지에게로 돌아갔고, 몇 주 동안 어찌나 효성스럽게 헌신했던지, 노신사는 니스의 기후가 손자를 놀랍게 좋아지게 했으니 다시 한번 가 보는 게 좋겠다고 말했다. 이 젊은 신사로서야 그보다 더 반가운 일이 없었겠지만, 그렇게 한바탕 꾸중을 들은 마당에 코끼리로 끌어도 그를 다시 그리로 데려갈 수는 없었을 것이다. 자존심이 그것을 허락하지 않았으니, 그리움이 몹시 강해질 때마다 그는 가장 깊은 인상을 남긴 그 말들을 되뇌며 결심을 다졌다. \"난 당신을 경멸해요.\" \"멋진 일을 해서 그 애가 당신을 사랑하게 만들어요.\""]], [["Laurie turned the matter over in his mind so often that he soon brought himself to confess that he _had_ been selfish and lazy; but then when a man has a great sorrow, he should be indulged in all sorts of vagaries till he has lived it down.", "로리는 그 문제를 마음속으로 하도 여러 번 곱씹은 끝에, 자기가 이기적이고 게을렀음을 인정하기에 이르렀다. 하지만 한 남자에게 큰 슬픔이 닥쳤을 때는, 그것을 이겨 낼 때까지 온갖 변덕을 너그러이 봐줘야 마땅한 법이다."], ["He felt that his blighted affections were quite dead now; and, though he should never cease to be a faithful mourner, there was no occasion to wear his weeds ostentatiously.", "그는 이제 자기의 짓밟힌 애정이 완전히 죽었다고 느꼈다. 그리고 비록 자기가 영영 충실한 애도자이기를 그치지 않으리라 해도, 그 상복을 굳이 요란하게 떨치고 다닐 까닭은 없었다."], ["Jo _wouldn't_ love him, but he might _make_ her respect and admire him by doing something which should prove that a girl's \"No\"", "조는 그를 사랑하지 않겠지만, 소녀의 \"싫어요\" 한마디가 자기 인생을 망쳐 놓지 못했음을 증명할 무언가를 해냄으로써, 그녀가 자기를 존경하고 흠모하게 만들 수는 있으리라."], ["had not spoilt his life.", "그는 늘 무언가를 해낼 작정이었으니, 에이미의 충고는 사실 필요 없는 것이었다."], ["He had always meant to do something, and Amy's advice was quite unnecessary.", "그저 앞서 말한 그 짓밟힌 애정이 점잖게 묻힐 때까지 기다리고 있었을 뿐이었다."], ["He had only been waiting till the aforesaid blighted affections were decently interred; that being done, he felt that he was ready to \"hide his stricken heart, and still toil on.\"", "그 장례가 끝나자, 그는 이제 \"상처받은 가슴을 감추고 묵묵히 힘써 나갈\" 준비가 되었다고 느꼈다."]], [["As Goethe, when he had a joy or a grief, put it into a song, so Laurie resolved to embalm his love-sorrow in music, and compose a Requiem which should harrow up Jo's soul and melt the heart of every hearer.", "괴테가 기쁨이나 슬픔이 생기면 그것을 노래로 지었던 것처럼, 로리도 자기의 사랑의 슬픔을 음악 속에 방부 처리해 두기로 마음먹고, 조의 영혼을 뒤흔들고 듣는 이마다 가슴을 녹일 진혼곡을 작곡하기로 했다."], ["Therefore the next time the old gentleman found him getting restless and moody, and ordered him off, he went to Vienna, where he had musical friends, and fell to work with the firm determination to distinguish himself.", "그리하여 다음번에 노신사가 그가 다시 안절부절못하며 침울해하는 것을 보고 어디든 가라고 명하자, 그는 음악을 하는 친구들이 있는 빈으로 가서, 반드시 두각을 나타내고 말겠다는 굳은 결심으로 작업에 매달렸다."], ["But, whether the sorrow was too vast to be embodied in music, or music too ethereal to uplift a mortal woe, he soon discovered that the Requiem was beyond him, just at present.", "하지만 그 슬픔이 음악에 담기에 너무 거대했던 탓인지, 아니면 음악이 너무 영묘하여 인간의 비애를 떠받치기에는 역부족이었던 탓인지, 그는 적어도 지금으로서는 그 진혼곡이 제 능력 밖임을 곧 깨달았다."], ["It was evident that his mind was not in working order yet, and his ideas needed clarifying; for often in the middle of a plaintive strain, he would find himself humming a dancing tune that vividly recalled the Christmas ball at Nice, especially the stout Frenchman, and put an effectual stop to tragic composition for the time being.", "그의 정신이 아직 제대로 돌아갈 상태가 아니고, 그의 착상이 정리될 필요가 있다는 것이 분명했다. 처량한 가락을 짓는 도중에, 그는 어느새 자기가 니스의 크리스마스 무도회를, 특히 그 뚱뚱한 프랑스 남자를 생생히 떠올리게 하는 흥겨운 춤곡을 흥얼거리고 있음을 발견하기 일쑤였고, 그러면 비극적인 작곡은 그것으로 영락없이 중단되곤 했으니 말이다."]], [["Then he tried an Opera, for nothing seemed impossible in the beginning; but here, again, unforeseen difficulties beset him.", "그래서 그는 오페라를 시도해 보았다. 처음에는 무엇 하나 불가능해 보이지 않았으니까."], ["He wanted Jo for his heroine, and called upon his memory to supply him with tender recollections and romantic visions of his love.", "하지만 여기서도 또다시 뜻밖의 난관이 그를 가로막았다. 그는 조를 자기 작품의 여주인공으로 삼고 싶어, 그 사랑에 대한 다정한 추억과 낭만적인 환상을 떠올려 달라고 기억에 청했다."], ["But memory turned traitor; and, as if possessed by the perverse spirit of the girl, would only recall Jo's oddities, faults, and freaks, would only show her in the most unsentimental aspects,--beating mats with her head tied up in a bandanna, barricading herself with the sofa-pillow, or throwing cold water over his passion _à la_ Gummidge,--and an irresistible laugh spoilt the pensive picture he was endeavoring to paint.", "그런데 기억은 배신자로 돌변했다. 마치 그 소녀의 짓궂은 혼령에라도 씐 듯, 기억은 오로지 조의 별난 점과 결점과 변덕만을 떠올려 주었고, 그녀를 더없이 비낭만적인 모습으로만 보여 주었다."], ["Jo wouldn't be put into the Opera at any price, and he had to give her up with a \"Bless that girl, what a torment she is!\"", "반다나로 머리를 동여맨 채 깔개를 두드려 먼지를 털어 내거나, 소파 쿠션으로 자기를 둘러싸 바리케이드를 치거나, 거미지 식으로 그의 열정에 찬물을 끼얹는 모습 따위로 말이다. 그러면 도무지 참을 수 없는 웃음이 그가 그리려 애쓰던 시름 어린 그림을 망쳐 놓았다."], ["and a clutch at his hair, as became a distracted composer.", "조는 어떤 값을 치러도 오페라 속에 들어가려 하지 않았고, 그는 \"저 애 좀 보게, 정말 골칫덩어리라니까!\" 하면서, 심란한 작곡가답게 머리칼을 움켜쥔 채 그녀를 포기해야 했다."]], [["When he looked about him for another and a less intractable damsel to immortalize in melody, memory produced one with the most obliging readiness.", "* 거미지 : 찰스 디킨스의 소설 『데이비드 코퍼필드』에 나오는 인물로, 늘 신세를 한탄하며 우는소리를 하는 과부 : 옮긴이."], ["This phantom wore many faces, but it always had golden hair, was enveloped in a diaphanous cloud, and floated airily before his mind's eye in a pleasing chaos of roses, peacocks, white ponies, and blue ribbons.", "그래서 그가 가락 속에 영원히 남길, 좀 더 다루기 쉬운 다른 처녀를 찾아 주위를 둘러보자, 기억은 더없이 고분고분한 태도로 하나를 내놓았다. 이 환영은 여러 얼굴을 하고 있었지만, 언제나 금발이었고, 비치는 듯한 구름에 감싸여, 장미와 공작과 흰 조랑말과 푸른 리본이 어우러진 기분 좋은 혼돈 속에서 그의 마음의 눈앞에 사뿐히 떠다녔다."], ["He did not give the complacent wraith any name, but he took her for his heroine, and grew quite fond of her, as well he might; for he gifted her with every gift and grace under the sun, and escorted her, unscathed, through trials which would have annihilated any mortal woman.", "그는 이 흡족한 환영에 아무 이름도 붙이지 않았지만, 그녀를 자기 작품의 여주인공으로 삼고는 꽤 좋아하게 되었다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그는 그녀에게 하늘 아래 온갖 재능과 우아함을 부여하고, 어떤 인간 여자라도 산산조각 냈을 시련들을 그녀가 흠집 하나 없이 헤쳐 나가도록 호위해 주었으니까."]], [["Thanks to this inspiration, he got on swimmingly for a time, but gradually the work lost its charm, and he forgot to compose, while he sat musing, pen in hand, or roamed about the gay city to get new ideas and refresh his mind, which seemed to be in a somewhat unsettled state that winter.", "이 영감 덕분에 그는 한동안 일이 순조롭게 풀렸지만, 차츰 그 작업은 매력을 잃어 갔고, 펜을 손에 쥔 채 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있거나, 새 착상을 얻고 정신을 새롭게 하려고 그 화려한 도시를 이리저리 쏘다니는 동안 작곡하는 것을 잊어버렸다. 그해 겨울 그의 마음은 어쩐지 어수선한 상태인 듯했다."], ["He did not do much, but he thought a great deal and was conscious of a change of some sort going on in spite of himself.", "그는 한 일이 별로 없었지만 생각은 무척 많이 했고, 자기도 모르게 어떤 변화가 일어나고 있음을 의식했다."], ["\"It's genius simmering, perhaps.", "\"어쩌면 천재성이 무르익는 중인지도 몰라."], ["I'll let it simmer, and see what comes of it,\"", "푹 익도록 내버려 두고 무엇이 나오는지 봐야지.\""], ["he said, with a secret suspicion, all the while, that it wasn't genius, but something far more common.", "그는 그렇게 말했지만, 그 내내 마음 한구석에는 그것이 천재성이 아니라 훨씬 더 흔한 무언가라는 은밀한 의심이 자리하고 있었다."], ["Whatever it was, it simmered to some purpose, for he grew more and more discontented with his desultory life, began to long for some real and earnest work to go at, soul and body, and finally came to the wise conclusion that every one who loved music was not a composer.", "그것이 무엇이었든 간에 어떤 결실을 향해 무르익어 갔으니, 그는 그 종잡을 수 없는 생활에 점점 더 불만을 느끼게 되었고, 영혼과 육신을 다 바쳐 매달릴 진실하고 진지한 일을 갈망하기 시작했으며, 마침내 음악을 사랑하는 사람이라고 다 작곡가는 아니라는 현명한 결론에 이르렀다. 왕립 극장에서 훌륭하게 공연된 모차르트의 웅장한 오페라 한 편을 보고 돌아온 그는, 자기가 쓴 것을 훑어보고 가장 잘된 대목 몇을 연주해 본 다음, 멘델스존과 베토벤과 바흐의 흉상을 올려다보며 앉아 있었다."], ["Returning from one of Mozart's grand operas, splendidly performed at the Royal Theatre, he looked over his own, played a few of the best parts, sat staring up at the busts of Mendelssohn, Beethoven, and Bach, who stared benignly back again; then suddenly he tore up his music-sheets, one by one, and, as the last fluttered out of his hand, he said soberly to himself,--", "그들도 인자하게 그를 마주 내려다보았다. 그러다 그는 별안간 악보를 한 장 한 장 찢어 버렸고, 마지막 한 장이 손에서 팔랑팔랑 떨어져 나가자 차분히 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["\"She is right!", "\"에이미 말이 옳아!"], ["Talent isn't genius, and you can't make it so.", "재능은 천재성이 아니고, 그걸 천재성으로 만들 수도 없어."], ["That music has taken the vanity out of me as Rome took it out of her, and I won't be a humbug any longer.", "로마가 그 애한테서 허영심을 앗아 갔듯, 이 음악이 나한테서 허영심을 앗아 갔군. 더는 사기꾼 노릇 안 할 테야."], ["Now what shall I do?\"", "자, 이제 뭘 한다?\""]], [["That seemed a hard question to answer, and Laurie began to wish he had to work for his daily bread.", "그것은 답하기 어려운 질문인 듯했고, 로리는 자기가 날마다 먹을 빵을 벌어야 하는 처지였더라면 좋았겠다는 생각마저 들기 시작했다. 지금이야말로, 언젠가 그가 거칠게 표현한 대로 \"타락의 길로 빠지기\"에 더없이 알맞은 기회였다."], ["Now, if ever, occurred an eligible opportunity for \"going to the devil,\"", "돈은 넘쳐 나는데 할 일은 없었고, 사탄은 속담대로 손이 차고 한가한 자에게 일거리를 마련해 주기를 즐기는 법이니까."], ["as he once forcibly expressed it, for he had plenty of money and nothing to do, and Satan is proverbially fond of providing employment for full and idle hands.", "가엾은 친구에게는 밖에서도 안에서도 유혹이 차고 넘쳤지만, 그는 그것을 꽤나 잘 견뎌 냈다. 그가 자유를 소중히 여겼던 만큼이나, 아니 그 이상으로 신의와 신뢰를 소중히 여겼기 때문이다."], ["The poor fellow had temptations enough from without and from within, but he withstood them pretty well; for, much as he valued liberty, he valued good faith and confidence more, so his promise to his grandfather, and his desire to be able to look honestly into the eyes of the women who loved him, and say \"All's well,\" kept him safe and steady.", "그래서 할아버지에게 한 약속과, 자기를 사랑하는 여인들의 눈을 떳떳이 들여다보며 \"다 잘 지내고 있어요\"라고 말할 수 있기를 바라는 마음이 그를 안전하고 흔들림 없이 지켜 주었다."]], [["Very likely some Mrs.", "아마 어떤 그런디 부인은 이렇게 말하리라. \"난 그 말 못 믿겠어."], ["Grundy will observe, \"I don't believe it; boys will be boys, young men must sow their wild oats, and women must not expect miracles.\"", "사내아이는 사내아이일 뿐이고, 젊은 남자는 한 번쯤 방탕을 부려 봐야 하는 법이며, 여자들이 기적을 바라선 안 되지.\" 그래요, 그런디 부인, 당신이야 못 믿겠지요."], ["I dare say _you_ don't, Mrs.", "하지만 그래도 그것은 사실입니다."], ["Grundy, but it's true nevertheless.", "여자들은 숱한 기적을 일으키니까요. 그리고 나는, 여자들이 그런 말에 맞장구치기를 거부함으로써 남자다움의 기준을 끌어올리는 기적마저도 일으킬 수 있으리라 믿습니다."], ["Women work a good many miracles, and I have a persuasion that they may perform even that of raising the standard of manhood by refusing to echo such sayings.", "사내아이는 사내아이답게, 되도록 오래 그렇게 두어요. 젊은 남자가 굳이 방탕을 부려야겠다면 그러게 두고요."], ["Let the boys be boys, the longer the better, and let the young men sow their wild oats if they must; but mothers, sisters, and friends may help to make the crop a small one, and keep many tares from spoiling the harvest, by believing, and showing that they believe, in the possibility of loyalty to the virtues which make men manliest in good women's eyes.", "하지만 어머니와 누이와 벗들은, 선량한 여자들의 눈에 남자를 가장 남자답게 만드는 그 미덕에 대한 충실함이 가능함을 믿고 또 믿는다는 것을 보여 줌으로써, 그 수확을 적게 만들고 숱한 잡초가 추수를 망치지 못하게 도울 수 있습니다. 만약 그것이 여자들의 망상이라면, 망상일망정 누릴 수 있을 때 누리게 두어요."], ["If it _is_ a feminine delusion, leave us to enjoy it while we may, for without it half the beauty and the romance of life is lost, and sorrowful forebodings would embitter all our hopes of the brave, tender-hearted little lads, who still love their mothers better than themselves, and are not ashamed to own it.", "그것이 없다면 삶의 아름다움과 낭만의 절반은 사라질 테고, 슬픈 예감이 우리의 모든 희망을 쓰디쓰게 할 테니까요. 아직도 자기 자신보다 어머니를 더 사랑하고, 그것을 부끄러워하지 않고 인정하는, 용감하고 마음 여린 어린 사내아이들에 대한 희망 말입니다."]], [["Laurie thought that the task of forgetting his love for Jo would absorb all his powers for years; but, to his great surprise, he discovered it grew easier every day. He refused to believe it at first, got angry with himself, and couldn't understand it; but these hearts of ours are curious and contrary things, and time and nature work their will in spite of us. Laurie's heart _wouldn't_ ache; the wound persisted in healing with a rapidity that astonished him, and, instead of trying to forget, he found himself trying to remember. He had not foreseen this turn of affairs, and was not prepared for it. He was disgusted with himself, surprised at his own fickleness, and full of a queer mixture of disappointment and relief that he could recover from such a tremendous blow so soon. He carefully stirred up the embers of his lost love, but they refused to burst into a blaze: there was only a comfortable glow that warmed and did him good without putting him into a fever, and he was reluctantly obliged to confess that the boyish passion was slowly subsiding into a more tranquil sentiment, very tender, a little sad and resentful still, but that was sure to pass away in time, leaving a brotherly affection which would last unbroken to the end.", "로리는 조에 대한 사랑을 잊는 일이 앞으로 몇 년 동안 자기의 모든 기력을 빨아들이리라 여겼다. 하지만 그를 무척 놀라게 한 것은, 그 일이 날마다 점점 더 수월해진다는 사실이었다. 그는 처음에는 그것을 믿으려 하지 않았고, 스스로에게 화를 냈으며, 도무지 영문을 알 수 없었다. 하지만 우리네 마음이란 묘하고 어긋나는 물건이라, 시간과 자연은 우리야 어쨌든 제 뜻대로 일을 해치우는 법이다. 로리의 마음은 좀처럼 아파 주려 하지 않았다. 상처는 그를 어리둥절하게 할 만큼 빠르게 줄곧 아물어 갔고, 잊으려 애쓰는 대신 그는 어느새 자기가 기억해 내려 애쓰고 있음을 발견했다. 그는 일이 이렇게 돌아가리라곤 예상하지 못했기에 미처 마음의 준비가 되어 있지 않았다. 그는 자기 자신에게 정나미가 떨어졌고, 자기의 변덕스러움에 놀랐으며, 그토록 엄청난 타격에서 이렇게나 빨리 회복될 수 있다는 데에 실망과 안도가 묘하게 뒤섞인 감정으로 가득했다. 그는 꺼져 가는 사랑의 잿불을 조심스레 들쑤셔 보았지만, 그것은 좀처럼 불길로 타오르려 하지 않았다. 그저 아늑한 온기만이 남아 있을 뿐이었다. 그것은 그를 열에 들뜨게 하지 않으면서도 따뜻하게 데워 주고 기운을 북돋워 주는 무엇이었다. 그리하여 그는 마지못해 인정할 수밖에 없었다. 소년다운 격정이 서서히 가라앉으면서 한결 차분한 감정으로 바뀌어 가고 있다는 것을. 그 감정은 무척 부드럽고, 아직 조금은 슬프고 원망스럽기도 했지만, 시간이 지나면 틀림없이 사라지고, 끝까지 변치 않을 형제 같은 애정만 남을 터였다."]], [["As the word \"brotherly\" passed through his mind in one of these reveries, he smiled, and glanced up at the picture of Mozart that was before him:--", "그렇게 이런저런 몽상에 잠겨 있던 어느 순간, \"형제 같은\"이라는 말이 머릿속을 스치자 그는 빙긋 웃으며 눈앞에 걸린 모차르트의 초상화를 올려다보았다."]], [["\"Well, he was a great man; and when he couldn't have one sister he took the other, and was happy.\"", "\"그래, 그분은 위대한 사람이었지. 한 자매를 얻을 수 없게 되자 다른 자매를 택했고, 그래서 행복했으니까.\""]], [["Laurie did not utter the words, but he thought them; and the next instant kissed the little old ring, saying to himself,--", "로리는 그 말을 입 밖에 내지는 않았으나 마음속으로 그렇게 생각했다. 그러고는 이내 손가락에 낀 작고 낡은 반지에 입을 맞추며 스스로에게 말했다."]], [["\"No, I won't!", "\"아니야, 그러지 않을 거야!"], ["I haven't forgotten, I never can.", "난 잊지 않았어, 결코 잊을 수 없어."], ["I'll try again, and if that fails, why, then--\"", "다시 한번 부딪쳐 볼 거야. 그래도 안 된다면, 그땐, 그때는—\""]], [["Leaving his sentence unfinished, he seized pen and paper and wrote to Jo, telling her that he could not settle to anything while there was the least hope of her changing her mind.", "말을 끝맺지 못한 채 그는 펜과 종이를 집어 들고 조에게 편지를 썼다. 조가 마음을 바꿀 가능성이 조금이라도 남아 있는 한 자기는 무슨 일에도 도무지 마음을 붙일 수 없다고."], ["Couldn't she, wouldn't she, and let him come home and be happy?", "그러니 마음을 바꿔 줄 수는 없겠느냐고, 그래서 자기가 집으로 돌아가 행복해질 수 있게 해 줄 수는 없겠느냐고."], ["While waiting for an answer he did nothing, but he did it energetically, for he was in a fever of impatience.", "답장을 기다리는 동안 그는 아무 일도 하지 못했지만, 그 아무것도 하지 않는 일만큼은 열정적으로 해냈다. 조바심에 온몸이 달아올랐기 때문이다."], ["It came at last, and settled his mind effectually on one point, for Jo decidedly couldn't and wouldn't.", "마침내 답장이 왔고, 적어도 한 가지만은 그의 마음을 확실하게 정리해 주었다. 조는 단호하게 마음을 바꿀 수도, 바꾸려 하지도 않았던 것이다."], ["She was wrapped up in Beth, and never wished to hear the word \"love\" again.", "조는 베스에게 온통 마음을 쏟고 있었고, \"사랑\"이라는 말은 두 번 다시 듣고 싶지 않다고 했다."], ["Then she begged him to be happy with somebody else, but always to keep a little corner of his heart for his loving sister Jo.", "그러면서 부디 다른 사람과 행복해지라고, 다만 늘 자기를 사랑하는 누이 조를 위해 마음 한구석은 조금 비워 두라고 당부했다."], ["In a postscript she desired him not to tell Amy that Beth was worse; she was coming home in the spring, and there was no need of saddening the remainder of her stay.", "추신에서는 베스가 더 나빠졌다는 사실을 에이미에게는 알리지 말아 달라고 했다. 에이미는 봄에 집으로 돌아올 텐데, 남은 여행을 굳이 슬프게 만들 필요는 없으니까."], ["That would be time enough, please God, but Laurie must write to her often, and not let her feel lonely, homesick, or anxious.", "하느님이 허락하신다면 그때 알려도 늦지 않으리라고. 다만 로리가 자주 편지를 써서 에이미가 외롭거나, 향수에 젖거나, 불안해하지 않도록 해 주기를 바란다고 했다."]], [["\"So I will, at once.", "\"그래, 당장 써야지."], ["Poor little girl; it will be a sad going home for her, I'm afraid;\"", "가엾은 것, 집으로 돌아오는 길이 슬프기만 하겠구나.\""], ["and Laurie opened his desk, as if writing to Amy had been the proper conclusion of the sentence left unfinished some weeks before.", "그러고는 로리는 책상을 열었다. 마치 몇 주 전에 끝맺지 못한 그 문장의 마땅한 결말이 에이미에게 편지를 쓰는 일이라는 듯이."]], [["But he did not write the letter that day; for, as he rummaged out his best paper, he came across something which changed his purpose. Tumbling about in one part of the desk, among bills, passports, and business documents of various kinds, were several of Jo's letters, and in another compartment were three notes from Amy, carefully tied up with one of her blue ribbons, and sweetly suggestive of the little dead roses put away inside. With a half-repentant, half-amused expression, Laurie gathered up all Jo's letters, smoothed, folded, and put them neatly into a small drawer of the desk, stood a minute turning the ring thoughtfully on his finger, then slowly drew it off, laid it with the letters, locked the drawer, and went out to hear High Mass at Saint Stefan's, feeling as if there had been a funeral; and, though not overwhelmed with affliction, this seemed a more proper way to spend the rest of the day than in writing letters to charming young ladies.", "그러나 그날 편지를 쓰지는 못했다. 가장 좋은 편지지를 뒤지다가 마음을 바꿔 놓는 무언가와 마주쳤기 때문이다. 책상 한쪽, 청구서와 여권과 온갖 사무 서류들이 뒤엉킨 틈에 조의 편지 몇 통이 굴러다니고 있었다. 그리고 다른 칸에는 에이미가 보낸 쪽지 세 장이 그녀의 파란 리본 하나로 정성스레 묶여 있었는데, 그 안에 곱게 끼워 둔 빛바랜 장미 꽃잎이 은은하게 떠오를 만큼 향긋했다. 반쯤은 뉘우치고 반쯤은 재미있어하는 표정으로, 로리는 조의 편지를 모두 그러모아 반듯이 펴고 접어서 책상의 작은 서랍에 가지런히 넣었다. 그러고는 한참을 서서 손가락에 낀 반지를 생각에 잠겨 돌리다가, 천천히 빼서 편지와 함께 넣고 서랍을 잠갔다. 그런 다음 마치 장례를 치른 듯한 기분으로 성 슈테판 성당의 대미사를 들으러 나갔다. 슬픔에 짓눌린 것은 아니었지만, 사랑스러운 아가씨에게 편지를 쓰는 것보다는 그렇게 하루의 나머지를 보내는 편이 더 어울릴 것만 같았다."]], [["The letter went very soon, however, and was promptly answered, for Amy _was_ homesick, and confessed it in the most delightfully confiding manner.", "그렇지만 편지는 곧 부쳐졌고, 답장도 즉시 돌아왔다. 에이미는 정말로 향수병에 걸려 있었고, 더없이 사랑스럽고 솔직한 태도로 그것을 털어놓았기 때문이다."], ["The correspondence flourished famously, and letters flew to and fro, with unfailing regularity, all through the early spring.", "편지 왕래는 보란 듯이 무르익었고, 편지는 이른 봄 내내 어김없이 규칙적으로 오갔다. 로리는 자기가 만든 흉상들을 팔아 치우고, 오페라 악보는 불쏘시개로 만들어 버린 뒤 파리로 돌아갔다."], ["Laurie sold his busts, made allumettes of his opera, and went back to Paris, hoping somebody would arrive before long.", "머지않아 누군가 도착하기를 바라면서. 그는 니스로 가고 싶어 견딜 수가 없었지만, 청을 받기 전에는 가지 않을 작정이었다."], ["He wanted desperately to go to Nice, but would not till he was asked; and Amy would not ask him, for just then she was having little experiences of her own, which made her rather wish to avoid the quizzical eyes of \"our boy.\"", "그리고 에이미는 그에게 와 달라고 청하지 않았다. 그 무렵 그녀는 그녀 나름의 작은 일들을 겪고 있었고, 그래서 \"우리 애\"의 짓궂은 눈길을 되도록 피하고 싶었기 때문이다."]], [["Fred Vaughn had returned, and put the question to which she had once decided to answer \"Yes, thank you;\" but now she said, \"No, thank you,\" kindly but steadily; for, when the time came, her courage failed her, and she found that something more than money and position was needed to satisfy the new longing that filled her heart so full of tender hopes and fears. The words, \"Fred is a good fellow, but not at all the man I fancied you would ever like,\" and Laurie's face when he uttered them, kept returning to her as pertinaciously as her own did when she said in look, if not in words, \"I shall marry for money.\" It troubled her to remember that now, she wished she could take it back, it sounded so unwomanly. She didn't want Laurie to think her a heartless, worldly creature; she didn't care to be a queen of society now half so much as she did to be a lovable woman; she was so glad he didn't hate her for the dreadful things she said, but took them so beautifully, and was kinder than ever. His letters were such a comfort, for the home letters were very irregular, and were not half so satisfactory as his when they did come. It was not only a pleasure, but a duty to answer them, for the poor fellow was forlorn, and needed petting, since Jo persisted in being stony-hearted. She ought to have made an effort, and tried to love him; it couldn't be very hard, many people would be proud and glad to have such a dear boy care for them; but Jo never would act like other girls, so there was nothing to do but be very kind, and treat him like a brother.", "프레드 본이 돌아와, 한때 에이미가 \"네, 고마워요\" 하고 대답하기로 마음먹었던 바로 그 질문을 던졌다. 하지만 이제 그녀는 \"아니요, 고마워요\"라고, 다정하지만 단호하게 말했다. 정작 그때가 오자 용기가 나지 않았고, 부드러운 희망과 두려움으로 가득 차오른 가슴속의 새로운 갈망을 채우기에는 돈과 지위만으로는 부족하다는 것을 깨달았기 때문이다. \"프레드는 좋은 친구지만, 누나가 좋아할 만한 사람과는 전혀 거리가 멀어\"라던 말과, 그 말을 할 때 로리의 표정이 자꾸만 그녀에게 되돌아왔다. 마치 그녀 자신이 말로는 아니어도 눈빛으로 \"난 돈을 보고 결혼할 거야\"라고 했을 때의 그 표정이 끈질기게 되살아나듯이. 이제 그 일을 떠올리는 것이 그녀를 괴롭혔다. 도로 주워 담을 수 있었으면 싶었다. 그 말이 너무도 여자답지 못하게 들렸으니까. 그녀는 로리가 자기를 매정하고 속물적인 인간으로 여기는 게 싫었다. 이제는 사교계의 여왕이 되는 일보다 사랑스러운 여자가 되는 일이 곱절은 더 간절했다. 그녀가 했던 그 끔찍한 말들 때문에 로리가 자기를 미워하기는커녕 오히려 그것을 그토록 너그럽게 받아 주고 전보다 더 다정하게 굴어 주어 얼마나 다행인지 몰랐다. 그의 편지는 큰 위안이 되었다. 집에서 오는 편지는 들쭉날쭉한 데다, 어쩌다 와도 그의 편지만큼 흡족하지가 않았던 것이다. 그의 편지에 답장을 쓰는 것은 즐거움일 뿐 아니라 의무이기도 했다. 조가 한사코 매정하게 굴고 있으니, 그 가엾은 사람은 외로워하며 누군가의 다정함이 필요했으니까. 조도 좀 애를 써서 그를 사랑해 보려 했어야 했다. 그리 어려운 일도 아닐 텐데. 그렇게 다정한 청년이 마음을 써 준다면 많은 사람이 자랑스럽고 기쁠 텐데. 하지만 조는 결코 다른 여자들처럼 행동하지 않았다. 그러니 할 수 있는 일이라곤 그저 그에게 더없이 다정하게 굴면서 오빠 대하듯 하는 것뿐이었다."]], [["If all brothers were treated as well as Laurie was at this period, they would be a much happier race of beings than they are.", "만약 세상의 모든 오빠들이 이 무렵 로리가 받은 만큼 잘 대접받는다면, 그들은 지금보다 훨씬 더 행복한 족속이 되었으리라. 에이미는 더 이상 잔소리를 하지 않았다."], ["Amy never lectured now; she asked his opinion on all subjects; she was interested in everything he did, made charming little presents for him, and sent him two letters a week, full of lively gossip, sisterly confidences, and captivating sketches of the lovely scenes about her.", "무슨 일이든 그의 의견을 물었고, 그가 하는 모든 일에 관심을 보였으며, 사랑스러운 선물을 손수 만들어 주고, 일주일에 두 통씩 편지를 보냈다. 발랄한 이야기와 누이다운 속마음, 그리고 주위의 아름다운 풍경을 매혹적으로 그린 스케치로 가득한 편지를."], ["As few brothers are complimented by having their letters carried about in their sisters'", "누이의 주머니 속에 편지가 담겨 다니고, 부지런히 읽고 또 읽히고, 짧으면 그것이 아쉬워 눈물 짓고, 길면 거기에 입을 맞추며, 고이고이 간직되는 영광을 누리는 오빠는 흔치 않으니, 에이미가 그런 애틋하고도 어리석은 짓들을 했다고 굳이 귀띔하지는 않으련다."], ["pockets, read and reread diligently, cried over when short, kissed when long, and treasured carefully, we will not hint that Amy did any of these fond and foolish things.", "다만 그해 봄 그녀가 조금 창백해지고 수심에 잠긴 것만은 분명했다. 사교계의 흥취를 부쩍 잃고서, 혼자 스케치를 하러 나가는 일이 잦아졌다."], ["But she certainly did grow a little pale and pensive that spring, lost much of her relish for society, and went out sketching alone a good deal.", "집에 돌아와도 내보일 만한 것은 거의 없었지만, 아마 자연을 공부하고 있었으리라. 발로사 테라스에 두 손을 모은 채 몇 시간이고 앉아 있거나, 문득 떠오르는 무엇이든 무심히 그려 보면서."], ["She never had much to show when she came home, but was studying nature, I dare say, while she sat for hours, with her hands folded, on the terrace at Valrosa, or absently sketched any fancy that occurred to her,--a stalwart knight carved on a tomb, a young man asleep in the grass, with his hat over his eyes, or a curly-haired girl in gorgeous array, promenading down a ball-room on the arm of a tall gentleman, both faces being left a blur according to the last fashion in art, which was safe, but not altogether satisfactory.", "무덤에 새겨진 늠름한 기사라든가, 모자로 눈을 가린 채 풀밭에 잠든 청년이라든가, 화려하게 차려입은 곱슬머리 소녀가 키 큰 신사의 팔을 잡고 무도회장을 거니는 모습이라든가. 두 사람의 얼굴은 요즘 미술의 유행을 따라 흐릿하게 남겨 두었는데, 안전하기는 했지만 영 만족스럽지는 못했다."]], [["Her aunt thought that she regretted her answer to Fred; and, finding denials useless and explanations impossible, Amy left her to think what she liked, taking care that Laurie should know that Fred had gone to Egypt.", "이모는 에이미가 프레드에게 한 대답을 후회한다고 여겼다. 아니라고 해 봤자 소용없고 설명하기도 불가능했으므로, 에이미는 이모가 좋을 대로 생각하게 내버려 두었다."], ["That was all, but he understood it, and looked relieved, as he said to himself, with a venerable air,--", "다만 프레드가 이집트로 떠났다는 사실만큼은 로리가 알도록 신경 썼다. 그게 다였지만 로리는 그 뜻을 알아차렸고, 안도한 표정으로 짐짓 어른스러운 태도를 지으며 혼자 중얼거렸다."]], [["\"I was sure she would think better of it.", "\"역시 다시 생각할 줄 알았어."], ["Poor old fellow!", "가엾은 친구!"], ["I've been through it all, and I can sympathize.\"", "나도 다 겪어 본 일이니, 그 마음 알지.\""]], [["With that he heaved a great sigh, and then, as if he had discharged his duty to the past, put his feet up on the sofa, and enjoyed Amy's letter luxuriously.", "그러고는 크게 한숨을 내쉬더니, 마치 지난날에 대한 도리를 다했다는 듯 두 발을 소파에 올려놓고 에이미의 편지를 호사스럽게 음미했다."]], [["While these changes were going on abroad, trouble had come at home; but the letter telling that Beth was failing never reached Amy, and when the next found her, the grass was green above her sister. The sad news met her at Vevay, for the heat had driven them from Nice in May, and they had travelled slowly to Switzerland, by way of Genoa and the Italian lakes. She bore it very well, and quietly submitted to the family decree that she should not shorten her visit, for, since it was too late to say good-by to Beth, she had better stay, and let absence soften her sorrow. But her heart was very heavy; she longed to be at home, and every day looked wistfully across the lake, waiting for Laurie to come and comfort her.", "이렇게 외국에서 이런 변화들이 일어나는 동안, 집에는 불행이 닥쳐왔다. 그러나 베스가 위중하다는 편지는 끝내 에이미에게 닿지 못했고, 다음 편지가 그녀를 찾았을 때는 이미 동생의 무덤 위로 풀이 푸르게 돋아 있었다. 슬픈 소식은 브베에서 그녀를 맞았다. 5월의 더위에 쫓겨 니스를 떠난 일행은 제노바와 이탈리아 호수들을 거쳐 천천히 스위스로 옮겨 가 있던 참이었다. 에이미는 잘 견뎌 냈고, 여행을 앞당기지 말라는 가족의 결정에 조용히 따랐다. 이제 와 베스에게 작별 인사를 할 수도 없게 된 마당이니, 차라리 남아 있으면서 떨어져 지내는 동안 슬픔을 누그러뜨리는 편이 나았던 것이다. 그래도 그녀의 마음은 무겁기만 했다. 집에 가고 싶어 못 견뎌 하며, 날마다 호수 건너편을 애타게 바라보았다. 로리가 와서 자기를 위로해 주기를 기다리며."]], [["He did come very soon; for the same mail brought letters to them both, but he was in Germany, and it took some days to reach him.", "로리는 정말 곧 찾아왔다. 같은 우편으로 두 사람 모두에게 편지가 왔지만, 로리는 독일에 있었던 터라 닿기까지 며칠이 걸렸다."], ["The moment he read it, he packed his knapsack, bade adieu to his fellow-pedestrians, and was off to keep his promise, with a heart full of joy and sorrow, hope and suspense.", "편지를 읽은 그 순간 그는 배낭을 꾸리고 함께 걷던 도보 여행 동료들에게 작별을 고한 뒤, 약속을 지키러 길을 나섰다. 기쁨과 슬픔, 희망과 초조함으로 가슴이 가득한 채로."]], [["He knew Vevay well; and as soon as the boat touched the little quay, he hurried along the shore to La Tour, where the Carrols were living _en pension_.", "그는 브베를 잘 알았다. 배가 작은 부두에 닿자마자 호숫가를 따라 라투르로 서둘러 갔다."], ["The _garçon_ was in despair that the whole family had gone to take a promenade on the lake; but no, the blond mademoiselle might be in the chateau garden.", "캐럴 일가가 하숙하고 있는 곳이었다. 종업원은 절망스럽다는 듯, 온 식구가 호수로 산책을 나갔다고 했다."], ["If monsieur would give himself the pain of sitting down, a flash of time should present her.", "하지만, 아니, 금발의 아가씨라면 성 정원에 계실지도 모른다고. 무슈가 잠시 앉아 수고로움을 감내해 주신다면 눈 깜짝할 사이에 모셔 오겠노라고."], ["But monsieur could not wait even \"a flash of time,\" and, in the middle of the speech, departed to find mademoiselle himself.", "그러나 무슈는 \"눈 깜짝할 사이\"조차 기다릴 수가 없어, 그 말이 채 끝나기도 전에 직접 아가씨를 찾으러 자리를 떴다."]], [["A pleasant old garden on the borders of the lovely lake, with chestnuts rustling overhead, ivy climbing everywhere, and the black shadow of the tower falling far across the sunny water.", "아름다운 호숫가에 자리한 정겹고 오래된 정원이었다. 머리 위로는 밤나무가 바스락거리고, 사방에는 담쟁이가 기어오르고, 탑의 검은 그림자가 햇살 부서지는 물 위로 길게 드리워져 있었다."], ["At one corner of the wide, low wall was a seat, and here Amy often came to read or work, or console herself with the beauty all about her.", "나지막하고 너른 담장 한 모서리에 의자가 하나 놓여 있었는데, 에이미는 곧잘 이곳에 와서 책을 읽거나 바느질을 하거나, 주위의 아름다움에 마음을 달래곤 했다."], ["She was sitting here that day, leaning her head on her hand, with a homesick heart and heavy eyes, thinking of Beth, and wondering why Laurie did not come.", "그날도 그녀는 거기 앉아 한 손으로 머리를 괴고, 향수에 젖은 가슴과 무거운 눈으로 베스를 생각하며 로리가 왜 오지 않는지 궁금해하고 있었다. 그녀는 그가 저 너머 안뜰을 가로질러 오는 소리도 듣지 못했고, 지하 통로에서 정원으로 이어지는 아치문 앞에 그가 멈춰 서는 것도 보지 못했다."], ["She did not hear him cross the court-yard beyond, nor see him pause in the archway that led from the subterranean path into the garden.", "그는 잠시 그 자리에 서서, 새로운 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다. 일찍이 아무도 본 적 없는 것—에이미라는 사람의 부드러운 면을 보았다."], ["He stood a minute, looking at her with new eyes, seeing what no one had ever seen before,--the tender side of Amy's character.", "그녀를 둘러싼 모든 것이 말없이 사랑과 슬픔을 일깨웠다. 무릎 위의 눈물 자국 번진 편지, 머리를 묶은 검은 리본, 얼굴에 어린 여인다운 아픔과 인내."], ["Everything about her mutely suggested love and sorrow,--the blotted letters in her lap, the black ribbon that tied up her hair, the womanly pain and patience in her face; even the little ebony cross at her throat seemed pathetic to Laurie, for he had given it to her, and she wore it as her only ornament.", "목에 걸린 작은 흑단 십자가조차 로리에게는 애처롭게 보였다. 그가 준 것이었고, 그녀가 유일한 장신구로 걸고 다니는 것이었기 때문이다."], ["If he had any doubts about the reception she would give him, they were set at rest the minute she looked up and saw him; for, dropping everything, she ran to him, exclaiming, in a tone of unmistakable love and longing,--", "그녀가 자기를 어떻게 맞아 줄지 일말의 의심이라도 있었다면, 그녀가 고개를 들어 그를 보는 순간 그 의심은 말끔히 사라졌으리라. 그녀는 모든 것을 내던지고 그에게로 달려오며, 더없이 분명한 사랑과 그리움이 담긴 목소리로 외쳤으니까."]], [["\"O Laurie, Laurie, I knew you'd come to me!\"", "\"오, 로리, 로리, 와 줄 줄 알았어요!\""]], [["I think everything was said and settled then; for, as they stood together quite silent for a moment, with the dark head bent down protectingly over the light one, Amy felt that no one could comfort and sustain her so well as Laurie, and Laurie decided that Amy was the only woman in the world who could fill Jo's place, and make him happy. He did not tell her so; but she was not disappointed, for both felt the truth, were satisfied, and gladly left the rest to silence.", "그때 모든 말이 오갔고, 모든 것이 정해졌다고 나는 생각한다. 두 사람이 잠시 말없이 마주 선 채, 검은 머리가 환한 머리 위로 감싸듯 숙여졌을 때, 에이미는 로리만큼 자기를 잘 위로하고 지탱해 줄 사람은 없다는 것을 느꼈고, 로리는 조의 자리를 채워 자기를 행복하게 해 줄 여자는 이 세상에 에이미뿐이라고 마음을 정했다. 그는 그렇게 말로 옮기지는 않았다. 그래도 에이미는 실망하지 않았다. 두 사람 모두 그 진실을 느꼈고, 만족했으며, 나머지는 기꺼이 침묵에 맡겼으니까."]], [["In a minute Amy went back to her place; and, while she dried her tears, Laurie gathered up the scattered papers, finding in the sight of sundry well-worn letters and suggestive sketches good omens for the future.", "이윽고 에이미는 제자리로 돌아갔다. 그녀가 눈물을 닦는 동안 로리는 흩어진 종이들을 그러모았다."], ["As he sat down beside her, Amy felt shy again, and turned rosy red at the recollection of her impulsive greeting.", "닳고 닳은 편지 몇 장과 의미심장한 스케치들을 보면서 앞날에 대한 좋은 징조를 읽어 냈다. 그가 곁에 앉자, 에이미는 다시 수줍어져서, 조금 전 충동적으로 인사하던 일을 떠올리며 발그레해졌다."]], [["\"I couldn't help it; I felt so lonely and sad, and was so very glad to see you.", "\"어쩔 수가 없었어요. 너무 외롭고 슬펐는데, 당신을 보니 너무 반가워서."], ["It was such a surprise to look up and find you, just as I was beginning to fear you wouldn't come,\"", "이제 안 오시려나 보다 막 걱정하기 시작하던 참에 고개를 드니 당신이 있어서, 어찌나 놀랐는지.\""], ["she said, trying in vain to speak quite naturally.", "그녀는 애써 아무렇지 않게 말하려 했지만 잘되지 않았다."]], [["\"I came the minute I heard.", "\"소식을 듣자마자 왔어요."], ["I wish I could say something to comfort you for the loss of dear little Beth; but I can only feel, and--\"", "사랑스러운 베스를 잃은 슬픔을 위로할 무슨 말이라도 할 수 있으면 좋으련만. 그저 마음으로 느낄 수밖에 없네요, 그리고—\""], ["He could not get any further, for he, too, turned bashful all of a sudden, and did not quite know what to say.", "그도 더는 말을 잇지 못했다. 그 역시 별안간 부끄러워져서, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었던 것이다."], ["He longed to lay Amy's head down on his shoulder, and tell her to have a good cry, but he did not dare; so took her hand instead, and gave it a sympathetic squeeze that was better than words.", "그는 에이미의 머리를 자기 어깨에 기대게 하고 실컷 울라고 말해 주고 싶었지만, 차마 그럴 용기가 나지 않았다. 그래서 그 대신 그녀의 손을 잡고, 말보다 나은 위로의 손길로 꼭 쥐어 주었다."]], [["\"You needn't say anything; this comforts me,\"", "\"아무 말 안 해도 돼요. 이것만으로도 위로가 되니까요.\""], ["she said softly.", "그녀가 나직이 말했다. \"베스는 편안하고 행복할 테니, 돌아오라고 빌어선 안 되겠죠."], ["\"Beth is well and happy, and I mustn't wish her back; but I dread the going home, much as I long to see them all.", "그래도 집에 가는 게 두려워요. 다들 보고 싶어 미칠 것 같은데도요."], ["We won't talk about it now, for it makes me cry, and I want to enjoy you while you stay.", "지금은 그 얘기 그만해요. 자꾸 눈물이 나니까."], ["You needn't go right back, need you?\"", "당신이 머무는 동안만큼은 함께 있는 걸 누리고 싶어요. 바로 돌아가실 건 아니죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["\"Not if you want me, dear.\"", "\"네가 원한다면 안 가지.\""]], [["\"I do, so much.", "\"원해요, 무척이나."], ["Aunt and Flo are very kind; but you seem like one of the family, and it would be so comfortable to have you for a little while.\"", "이모도 플로도 정말 다정하지만, 당신은 꼭 우리 식구 같으니까요. 잠시라도 곁에 있어 주면 얼마나 든든할까요.\""]], [["Amy spoke and looked so like a homesick child, whose heart was full, that Laurie forgot his bashfulness all at once, and gave her just what she wanted,--the petting she was used to and the cheerful conversation she needed.", "에이미의 말과 표정이 마음 가득 향수에 젖은 어린아이 같아서, 로리는 단번에 수줍음을 잊고 그녀가 바라는 것을 그대로 주었다. 익숙한 다정함과, 그녀에게 필요한 명랑한 대화를."]], [["\"Poor little soul, you look as if you'd grieved yourself half-sick!", "\"가엾은 것, 슬픔에 잠겨 반쯤 병이라도 든 사람 같구나!"], ["I'm going to take care of you, so don't cry any more, but come and walk about with me; the wind is too chilly for you to sit still,\"", "이제 내가 돌볼 테니 그만 울고, 같이 좀 걷자. 바람이 차서 가만히 앉아 있으면 안 되겠어.\""], ["he said, in the half-caressing, half-commanding way that Amy liked, as he tied on her hat, drew her arm through his, and began to pace up and down the sunny walk, under the new-leaved chestnuts.", "그가 에이미가 좋아하는, 반쯤은 어르고 반쯤은 명령하는 투로 말하며 그녀의 모자를 매 주고, 자기 팔에 그녀의 팔을 끼우더니, 새잎 돋은 밤나무 아래 햇살 비치는 산책로를 천천히 거닐기 시작했다."], ["He felt more at ease upon his legs; and Amy found it very pleasant to have a strong arm to lean upon, a familiar face to smile at her, and a kind voice to talk delightfully for her alone.", "그는 두 발로 걸으니 한결 마음이 놓였다. 그리고 에이미는 기댈 든든한 팔과 마주 보고 웃어 줄 익숙한 얼굴, 오직 자기만을 위해 즐겁게 이야기해 주는 다정한 목소리가 있다는 것이 무척이나 좋았다."]], [["The quaint old garden had sheltered many pairs of lovers, and seemed expressly made for them, so sunny and secluded was it, with nothing but the tower to overlook them, and the wide lake to carry away the echo of their words, as it rippled by below.", "이 예스럽고 정겨운 정원은 수많은 연인을 품어 왔으며, 그들을 위해 일부러 만들어진 곳 같았다. 햇볕이 따스하고 호젓한 데다, 그들을 내려다보는 것이라곤 탑뿐이었고, 발치에서 잔물결을 일으키며 흘러가는 너른 호수가 그들의 말의 메아리를 멀리 실어 갔으니까."], ["For an hour this new pair walked and talked, or rested on the wall, enjoying the sweet influences which gave such a charm to time and place; and when an unromantic dinner-bell warned them away, Amy felt as if she left her burden of loneliness and sorrow behind her in the chateau garden.", "한 시간 동안 이 새로운 한 쌍은 걷고 이야기를 나누거나, 담장에 기대어 쉬면서, 그 시간과 장소에 그토록 매혹을 더해 주는 달콤한 기운을 만끽했다. 그리고 낭만이라곤 없는 저녁 식사 종이 그들을 불러들였을 때, 에이미는 외로움과 슬픔의 짐을 성 정원에 두고 오는 듯한 기분이었다."]], [["The moment Mrs.", "캐럴 부인은 달라진 에이미의 얼굴을 본 순간, 새로운 생각이 번뜩 떠올라 속으로 외쳤다."], ["Carrol saw the girl's altered face, she was illuminated with a new idea, and exclaimed to herself, \"Now I understand it all,--the child has been pining for young Laurence.", "\"이제 다 알겠구나. 이 아이가 그동안 젊은 로런스를 그리워하며 애를 태운 거야."], ["Bless my heart, I never thought of such a thing!\"", "세상에, 내가 그 생각을 못 했다니!\""]], [["With praiseworthy discretion, the good lady said nothing, and betrayed no sign of enlightenment; but cordially urged Laurie to stay, and begged Amy to enjoy his society, for it would do her more good than so much solitude. Amy was a model of docility; and, as her aunt was a good deal occupied with Flo, she was left to entertain her friend, and did it with more than her usual success.", "이 마음씨 좋은 부인은 칭찬받을 만큼 신중하게 아무 말도 하지 않고 눈치챈 기색조차 내비치지 않았다. 다만 로리에게 더 머물라고 진심으로 권하고, 에이미에게는 그와 함께 시간을 즐기라고 했다. 혼자 외롭게 지내는 것보다 그편이 훨씬 이로울 테니까. 에이미는 더없이 고분고분했다. 이모가 플로를 챙기느라 정신이 없어, 그녀는 친구를 상대하는 몫을 맡게 되었고, 그 일을 여느 때보다 훨씬 훌륭하게 해냈다."]], [["At Nice, Laurie had lounged and Amy had scolded; at Vevay, Laurie was never idle, but always walking, riding, boating, or studying, in the most energetic manner, while Amy admired everything he did, and followed his example as far and as fast as she could. He said the change was owing to the climate, and she did not contradict him, being glad of a like excuse for her own recovered health and spirits.", "니스에서는 로리가 빈둥거렸고 에이미가 잔소리를 했지만, 브베에서는 로리가 한시도 가만있지 않았다. 늘 걷고, 말을 타고, 배를 젓고, 공부를 했는데, 그것도 더없이 정력적으로였다. 그러는 동안 에이미는 그가 하는 모든 일에 감탄하며, 할 수 있는 한 멀리, 빠르게 그를 따라 했다. 그는 그 변화가 기후 덕분이라고 했고, 그녀는 굳이 반박하지 않았다. 되찾은 건강과 활기에 자기도 같은 핑계를 댈 수 있어 다행이었던 것이다."]], [["The invigorating air did them both good, and much exercise worked wholesome changes in minds as well as bodies. They seemed to get clearer views of life and duty up there among the everlasting hills; the fresh winds blew away desponding doubts, delusive fancies, and moody mists; the warm spring sunshine brought out all sorts of aspiring ideas, tender hopes, and happy thoughts; the lake seemed to wash away the troubles of the past, and the grand old mountains to look benignly down upon them, saying, \"Little children, love one another.\"", "기운을 북돋워 주는 공기는 두 사람 모두에게 이로웠고, 많은 운동은 몸뿐 아니라 마음에도 건강한 변화를 가져왔다. 영원히 변치 않는 산봉우리들 가운데서 두 사람은 삶과 의무를 한결 또렷하게 바라보게 된 듯했다. 신선한 바람은 낙심하게 하는 의심과 그릇된 환상, 우울한 안개를 날려 버렸다. 따스한 봄 햇살은 온갖 높은 뜻과 부드러운 희망, 행복한 생각을 끌어냈다. 호수는 지난날의 시름을 씻어 내는 듯했고, 웅장하고 오래된 산들은 자애로이 그들을 굽어보며 이렇게 말하는 것 같았다. \"어린아이들아, 서로 사랑하여라.\""]], [["In spite of the new sorrow, it was a very happy time, so happy that Laurie could not bear to disturb it by a word.", "새로운 슬픔에도 불구하고, 그것은 무척 행복한 시간이었다. 로리가 한마디 말로도 그 분위기를 깨뜨릴 수 없을 만큼 행복했다."], ["It took him a little while to recover from his surprise at the rapid cure of his first, and, as he had firmly believed, his last and only love.", "그가 자신의 첫사랑이자, 굳게 믿어 온 마지막이자 유일한 사랑이 그토록 빠르게 치유된 데 대한 놀라움에서 벗어나기까지는 얼마간 시간이 걸렸다."], ["He consoled himself for the seeming disloyalty by the thought that Jo's sister was almost the same as Jo's self, and the conviction that it would have been impossible to love any other woman but Amy so soon and so well.", "그는 조의 동생은 조 자신과 거의 다름없다는 생각과, 에이미가 아니고서는 어떤 여자도 이토록 빨리, 이토록 깊이 사랑하기란 불가능했으리라는 확신으로, 겉보기에 변심처럼 보이는 그 일에 스스로 위안을 얻었다."], ["His first wooing had been of the tempestuous order, and he looked back upon it as if through a long vista of years, with a feeling of compassion blended with regret.", "그의 첫사랑은 폭풍 같은 종류였고, 이제 그는 그것을 아득한 세월 너머의 일처럼 돌아보며 후회와 뒤섞인 연민을 느꼈다. 그는 그 사랑을 부끄러워하지 않았다."], ["He was not ashamed of it, but put it away as one of the bitter-sweet experiences of his life, for which he could be grateful when the pain was over.", "다만 아픔이 지나가고 나면 고마워할 수 있는, 삶의 쓰면서도 단 경험 가운데 하나로 마음 한구석에 갈무리해 두었다. 두 번째 구애만큼은 되도록 차분하고 단순하게 하리라 마음먹었다."], ["His second wooing he resolved should be as calm and simple as possible; there was no need of having a scene, hardly any need of telling Amy that he loved her; she knew it without words, and had given him his answer long ago.", "장면을 만들 필요도 없고, 에이미에게 사랑한다고 굳이 말할 필요조차 거의 없었다. 그녀는 말 없이도 그것을 알았고, 이미 오래전에 그에게 답을 주었으니까."], ["It all came about so naturally that no one could complain, and he knew that everybody would be pleased, even Jo.", "모든 것이 너무도 자연스럽게 이루어져 누구도 탓할 수 없었고, 그는 조차 포함해 모두가 기뻐하리라는 것을 알았다."], ["But when our first little passion has been crushed, we are apt to be wary and slow in making a second trial; so Laurie let the days pass, enjoying every hour, and leaving to chance the utterance of the word that would put an end to the first and sweetest part of his new romance.", "그러나 첫 풋사랑이 한번 짓밟히고 나면 두 번째 시도에는 조심스럽고 더디어지는 법이라, 로리는 매 순간을 누리며 날들을 흘려보냈고, 새로운 사랑 이야기의 가장 처음이자 가장 달콤한 부분에 종지부를 찍을 그 한마디를 언제 입에 올릴지는 우연에 맡겨 두었다."]], [["He had rather imagined that the _dénouement_ would take place in the chateau garden by moonlight, and in the most graceful and decorous manner; but it turned out exactly the reverse, for the matter was settled on the lake, at noonday, in a few blunt words.", "그는 결말이 달빛 비치는 성 정원에서, 더없이 우아하고 점잖은 방식으로 이루어지리라 막연히 상상했었다. 그러나 정작은 정반대로 흘러갔다."], ["They had been floating about all the morning, from gloomy St.", "그 일은 한낮의 호수 위에서, 무뚝뚝한 몇 마디로 정해졌으니까."], ["Gingolf to sunny Montreux, with the Alps of Savoy on one side, Mont St.", "그들은 오전 내내 배를 타고 떠다녔다. 음울한 생젱골프에서 햇살 가득한 몽트뢰까지, 한쪽으로는 사부아 알프스, 다른 쪽으로는 생베르나르 산과 당뒤미디를 두고서."], ["Bernard and the Dent du Midi on the other, pretty Vevay in the valley, and Lausanne upon the hill beyond, a cloudless blue sky overhead, and the bluer lake below, dotted with the picturesque boats that look like white-winged gulls.", "골짜기에는 어여쁜 브베가, 그 너머 언덕에는 로잔이 있었고, 머리 위에는 구름 한 점 없는 푸른 하늘이, 발아래에는 그보다 더 푸른 호수가 펼쳐졌다. 호수 위에는 흰 날개 갈매기 같은 그림 같은 배들이 점점이 떠 있었다."]], [["They had been talking of Bonnivard, as they glided past Chillon, and of Rousseau, as they looked up at Clarens, where he wrote his \"Héloise.\"", "그들은 시용을 미끄러지듯 지나며 보니바르 이야기를 했고, 루소가 「엘로이즈」를 쓴 클라랑을 올려다보며 루소 이야기를 했다."], ["Neither had read it, but they knew it was a love-story, and each privately wondered if it was half as interesting as their own.", "둘 다 그 작품을 읽어 본 적은 없었지만 사랑 이야기라는 것은 알았고, 저마다 속으로 그것이 자기들 이야기의 절반만큼이라도 흥미로울까 궁금해했다."], ["Amy had been dabbling her hand in the water during the little pause that fell between them, and, when she looked up, Laurie was leaning on his oars, with an expression in his eyes that made her say hastily, merely for the sake of saying something,--", "두 사람 사이에 잠시 침묵이 내려앉은 동안 에이미는 손을 물에 담그고 찰랑이고 있었는데, 고개를 들어 보니 로리가 노에 기댄 채 어떤 눈빛으로 그녀를 바라보고 있었다. 그 눈빛에 그녀는 그저 무슨 말이라도 해야겠다는 생각에 황급히 입을 열었다."]], [["\"You must be tired; rest a little, and let me row; it will do me good; for, since you came, I have been altogether lazy and luxurious.\"", "\"피곤하겠어요. 좀 쉬어요, 제가 저을게요. 저한테도 좋을 거예요. 당신이 온 뒤로 저는 줄곧 게으름만 피우며 호사를 누렸으니까요.\""]], [["\"I'm not tired; but you may take an oar, if you like.", "\"피곤하지 않아. 그래도 원하면 노 하나 잡아도 돼."], ["There's room enough, though I have to sit nearly in the middle, else the boat won't trim,\"", "자리는 넉넉하니까. 다만 균형이 안 맞아서 내가 거의 한가운데 앉아야겠지만.\""], ["returned Laurie, as if he rather liked the arrangement.", "로리는 그 자리 배치가 꽤 마음에 든다는 투로 대답했다."]], [["Feeling that she had not mended matters much, Amy took the offered third of a seat, shook her hair over her face, and accepted an oar.", "별로 사정을 나아지게 하지 못했다는 것을 느끼며, 에이미는 권하는 대로 자리 삼분의 일에 걸터앉아 머리를 얼굴 위로 늘어뜨리고 노를 받아 들었다."], ["She rowed as well as she did many other things; and, though she used both hands, and Laurie but one, the oars kept time, and the boat went smoothly through the water.", "그녀는 다른 여러 일을 잘하듯 노 젓는 것도 잘했다. 그녀는 두 손을 쓰고 로리는 한 손만 썼는데도 노는 박자를 맞추었고, 배는 물 위를 매끄럽게 나아갔다."]], [["\"How well we pull together, don't we?\"", "\"우리 정말 손발이 잘 맞죠?\""], ["said Amy, who objected to silence just then.", "그 순간만큼은 침묵이 싫었던 에이미가 말했다."]], [["\"So well that I wish we might always pull in the same boat.", "\"너무 잘 맞아서, 언제까지나 같은 배를 함께 저으면 좋겠어."], ["Will you, Amy?\"", "그래 줄래, 에이미?\""], ["very tenderly.", "무척 다정하게."]], [["\"Yes, Laurie,\"", "\"네, 로리.\""], ["very low.", "아주 나직하게."]], [["Then they both stopped rowing, and unconsciously added a pretty little _tableau_ of human love and happiness to the dissolving views reflected in the lake.", "그러고는 두 사람 다 노 젓기를 멈추었고, 자기들도 모르는 사이에 호수에 비쳐 어른거리는 풍경 위로, 인간의 사랑과 행복이 빚어내는 어여쁜 한 폭의 그림을 보태고 있었다."]], [["# ALL ALONE.", "# ALL ALONE."]], [["It was easy to promise self-abnegation when self was wrapped up in another, and heart and soul were purified by a sweet example; but when the helpful voice was silent, the daily lesson over, the beloved presence gone, and nothing remained but loneliness and grief, then Jo found her promise very hard to keep.", "자기 자신이 다른 사람에게 온통 쏠려 있고, 마음과 영혼이 어떤 다정한 본보기로 정화되어 있을 때는 자기를 버리겠노라 약속하기가 쉬웠다. 그러나 그 힘이 되어 주던 목소리가 잠잠해지고, 날마다의 가르침이 끝나고, 사랑하던 이의 존재가 사라지고, 남은 것이라곤 외로움과 슬픔뿐일 때, 조는 그 약속을 지키기가 무척이나 어렵다는 것을 깨달았다."], ["How could she \"comfort father and mother,\"", "동생에 대한 끝없는 그리움으로 제 가슴이 미어지는데 어떻게 \"아버지와 어머니를 위로\"할 수 있단 말인가."], ["when her own heart ached with a ceaseless longing for her sister; how could she \"make the house cheerful,\"", "베스가 옛집을 떠나 새집으로 가 버리면서 집 안의 빛과 온기와 아름다움이 죄다 떠나 버린 것만 같은데 어떻게 \"집을 명랑하게\""], ["when all its light and warmth and beauty seemed to have deserted it when Beth left the old home for the new; and where in all the world could she \"find some useful, happy work to do,\"", "만들 수 있단 말인가. 그리고 이 너른 세상 어디에서, 그 자체로 보답이던 사랑의 봉사를 대신할 \"쓸모 있고 행복한 일거리\"를 찾을 수 있단 말인가?"], ["that would take the place of the loving service which had been its own reward?", "그녀는 앞이 막막한 채로 절망스럽게 제 의무를 다하려 애썼다. 그러면서도 내내 속으로는 그 모든 것에 반항했다."], ["She tried in a blind, hopeless way to do her duty, secretly rebelling against it all the while, for it seemed unjust that her few joys should be lessened, her burdens made heavier, and life get harder and harder as she toiled along.", "제 몇 안 되는 기쁨은 줄어들고 짐은 더 무거워지고, 발버둥 칠수록 삶이 점점 더 고되어지는 것이 부당하게만 느껴졌으니까. 어떤 사람들은 온통 햇살만 받고 어떤 사람들은 온통 그늘만 받는 듯했다."], ["Some people seemed to get all sunshine, and some all shadow; it was not fair, for she tried more than Amy to be good, but never got any reward, only disappointment, trouble, and hard work.", "그것은 공평하지 않았다. 자기는 에이미보다 더 착하게 살려고 애썼는데도 보답은커녕 실망과 시름, 고된 일밖에 돌아오는 게 없었으니까."]], [["Poor Jo, these were dark days to her, for something like despair came over her when she thought of spending all her life in that quiet house, devoted to humdrum cares, a few small pleasures, and the duty that never seemed to grow any easier.", "가엾은 조, 그녀에게는 어두운 나날이었다. 이 조용한 집에서 평생토록 따분한 살림살이와 몇 가지 자잘한 즐거움, 그리고 도무지 쉬워질 줄 모르는 의무에 매여 살 생각을 하면 절망 비슷한 것이 밀려왔던 것이다."], ["\"I can't do it.", "\"난 못 해."], ["I wasn't meant for a life like this, and I know I shall break away and do something desperate if somebody don't come and help me,\"", "난 이런 삶에 맞는 사람이 아니야. 누군가 와서 날 도와주지 않으면 분명 뛰쳐나가 무슨 무모한 짓이라도 저지르고 말 거야.\""], ["she said to herself, when her first efforts failed, and she fell into the moody, miserable state of mind which often comes when strong wills have to yield to the inevitable.", "처음의 노력이 실패로 돌아가고, 강한 의지가 피할 수 없는 운명에 굴복해야 할 때 흔히 찾아오는 음울하고 비참한 심정에 빠져들었을 때, 그녀는 그렇게 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["But some one did come and help her, though Jo did not recognize her good angels at once, because they wore familiar shapes, and used the simple spells best fitted to poor humanity.", "그러나 누군가 와서 그녀를 도와주었다. 조는 자신의 수호천사들을 단번에 알아보지 못했다."], ["Often she started up at night, thinking Beth called her; and when the sight of the little empty bed made her cry with the bitter cry of an unsubmissive sorrow, \"O Beth, come back!", "그들이 낯익은 모습을 하고서, 가엾은 인간에게 가장 알맞은 소박한 주문을 썼기 때문이다. 그녀는 밤마다 베스가 부르는 것만 같아 화들짝 깨곤 했다."], ["come back!\"", "작고 텅 빈 침대를 보고 순순히 받아들이지 못한 슬픔의 쓰라린 울음을 토할 때면—\"오, 베스, 돌아와!"], ["she did not stretch out her yearning arms in vain; for, as quick to hear her sobbing as she had been to hear her sister's faintest whisper, her mother came to comfort her, not with words only, but the patient tenderness that soothes by a touch, tears that were mute reminders of a greater grief than Jo's, and broken whispers, more eloquent than prayers, because hopeful resignation went hand-in-hand with natural sorrow.", "돌아오라고!\"—그리워 뻗은 두 팔이 헛되지 않았다. 동생의 가장 희미한 속삭임에도 귀를 곤두세웠던 그 어머니가 이제는 조의 흐느낌을 누구보다 빨리 듣고 위로하러 왔으니까."], ["Sacred moments, when heart talked to heart in the silence of the night, turning affliction to a blessing, which chastened grief and strengthened love.", "말로써만이 아니라, 손길 하나로 달래 주는 끈기 있는 다정함과, 조의 것보다 더 큰 슬픔을 말없이 일깨우는 눈물과, 기도보다 더 절절한 끊긴 속삭임으로. 희망에 찬 체념이 자연스러운 슬픔과 손을 맞잡고 있었기에 그러했다."], ["Feeling this, Jo's burden seemed easier to bear, duty grew sweeter, and life looked more endurable, seen from the safe shelter of her mother's arms.", "밤의 정적 속에서 마음과 마음이 이야기를 나누며 고통을 축복으로 바꾸어, 슬픔을 다듬고 사랑을 굳건히 해 주던 거룩한 순간들. 이것을 느끼자 조의 짐은 한결 가벼워진 듯했고, 의무는 더 달콤해졌으며, 어머니의 품이라는 안전한 안식처에서 바라본 삶은 더 견딜 만해 보였다."]], [["When aching heart was a little comforted, troubled mind likewise found help; for one day she went to the study, and, leaning over the good gray head lifted to welcome her with a tranquil smile, she said, very humbly,--", "쓰라리던 마음이 조금 위로받자 어지럽던 머릿속에도 도움이 찾아왔다. 어느 날 그녀는 서재로 가서, 차분한 미소로 자기를 맞으려 들린 그 고운 잿빛 머리 위로 몸을 숙이며 무척 겸손하게 말했다."]], [["\"Father, talk to me as you did to Beth.", "\"아버지, 베스한테 하시던 것처럼 저한테도 말씀해 주세요."], ["I need it more than she did, for I'm all wrong.\"", "저한테는 그게 그 애보다 더 필요해요. 전 다 엉망진창이거든요.\""]], [["\"My dear, nothing can comfort me like this,\"", "\"얘야, 이보다 더 큰 위로는 없단다.\""], ["he answered, with a falter in his voice, and both arms round her, as if he, too, needed help, and did not fear to ask it.", "그가 떨리는 목소리로 대답하며 두 팔로 그녀를 감쌌다. 마치 그 역시 도움이 필요했고, 그것을 청하기를 두려워하지 않는다는 듯이."]], [["Then, sitting in Beth's little chair close beside him, Jo told her troubles,--the resentful sorrow for her loss, the fruitless efforts that discouraged her, the want of faith that made life look so dark, and all the sad bewilderment which we call despair.", "그리하여 베스가 쓰던 작은 의자에 아버지 곁에 바싹 붙어 앉아, 조는 제 시름을 털어놓았다. 동생을 잃은 원망스러운 슬픔, 자기를 낙담시키던 헛된 노력들, 삶을 그토록 어둡게만 보이게 한 믿음의 결핍, 그리고 우리가 절망이라 부르는 그 모든 서글픈 막막함을."], ["She gave him entire confidence, he gave her the help she needed, and both found consolation in the act; for the time had come when they could talk together not only as father and daughter, but as man and woman, able and glad to serve each other with mutual sympathy as well as mutual love.", "그녀가 온 마음을 다 털어놓자 아버지는 그녀에게 필요한 도움을 주었고, 그 일로 두 사람 다 위안을 얻었다. 이제 부녀로서만이 아니라 한 사람의 남자와 여자로서, 사랑뿐 아니라 서로에 대한 공감으로 기꺼이 서로를 도울 수 있는 때가 온 것이다."], ["Happy, thoughtful times there in the old study which Jo called \"the church of one member,\"", "조가 \"신도가 한 사람뿐인 교회\"라 부르던 그 오래된 서재에서 보낸 행복하고 사색에 잠긴 시간들."], ["and from which she came with fresh courage, recovered cheerfulness, and a more submissive spirit; for the parents who had taught one child to meet death without fear, were trying now to teach another to accept life without despondency or distrust, and to use its beautiful opportunities with gratitude and power.", "그녀는 그곳에서 새로운 용기와 되찾은 명랑함, 그리고 한결 순순해진 마음을 안고 나왔다. 한 아이에게 두려움 없이 죽음을 맞도록 가르쳤던 부모가, 이제 또 다른 아이에게 낙심이나 불신 없이 삶을 받아들이고, 삶이 주는 아름다운 기회들을 감사함과 힘으로 쓰도록 가르치려 애쓰고 있었으니까."]], [["Other helps had Jo,--humble, wholesome duties and delights that would not be denied their part in serving her, and which she slowly learned to see and value. Brooms and dishcloths never could be as distasteful as they once had been, for Beth had presided over both; and something of her housewifely spirit seemed to linger round the little mop and the old brush, that was never thrown away. As she used them, Jo found herself humming the songs Beth used to hum, imitating Beth's orderly ways, and giving the little touches here and there that kept everything fresh and cosey, which was the first step toward making home happy, though she didn't know it, till Hannah said with an approving squeeze of the hand,--", "조에게는 또 다른 도움들도 있었다. 그녀를 섬기는 제 몫을 외면당하지 않으려는, 소박하고 건강한 의무와 즐거움들. 조는 그것들을 보고 소중히 여기는 법을 차츰 배워 갔다. 빗자루와 행주는 더 이상 예전처럼 진저리 나는 물건일 수 없었다. 베스가 둘 다 맡아 쓰던 것이었으니까. 결코 버리지 않은 그 작은 걸레와 낡은 솔 언저리에는 동생의 살림꾼다운 정신이 어려 있는 듯했다. 그것들을 쓰면서 조는 어느새 베스가 흥얼거리던 노래를 흥얼거리고, 베스의 단정한 살림 솜씨를 흉내 내고, 여기저기 작은 손길을 더해 모든 것을 산뜻하고 아늑하게 가꾸고 있는 자신을 발견했다. 그것이 집을 행복하게 만드는 첫걸음이었지만, 그녀는 미처 알지 못했다. 해나가 손을 흐뭇하게 꼭 쥐며 이렇게 말하기 전까지는."]], [["\"You thoughtful creter, you're determined we sha'n't miss that dear lamb ef you can help it.", "\"참 속 깊은 아가씨야. 할 수만 있으면 그 사랑스러운 양을 우리가 그리워하지 않게 하려고 단단히 마음먹었구먼."], ["We don't say much, but we see it, and the Lord will bless you for't, see ef He don't.\"", "우리야 말은 많이 안 해도 다 보고 있다우. 하느님께서 그 일로 아가씨한테 복을 내리실 거예요, 안 그러시나 두고 보라고요.\""]], [["As they sat sewing together, Jo discovered how much improved her sister Meg was; how well she could talk, how much she knew about good, womanly impulses, thoughts, and feelings, how happy she was in husband and children, and how much they were all doing for each other.", "함께 앉아 바느질을 하다가, 조는 언니 메그가 얼마나 좋게 변했는지 알게 되었다. 얼마나 말을 조리 있게 하는지, 착하고 여자다운 마음과 생각과 감정에 대해 얼마나 많은 것을 아는지, 남편과 아이들 곁에서 얼마나 행복한지, 그리고 그들이 서로를 위해 얼마나 많은 것을 하고 있는지를."]], [["\"Marriage is an excellent thing, after all.", "\"결혼이란 게 결국 참 좋은 거구나."], ["I wonder if I should blossom out half as well as you have, if I tried it?\"", "나도 한번 해 보면 언니만큼이나 활짝 피어날 수 있을까?\""], ["said Jo, as she constructed a kite for Demi, in the topsy-turvy nursery.", "어수선한 아이 방에서 데미에게 줄 연을 만들며 조가 말했다."]], [["\"It's just what you need to bring out the tender, womanly half of your nature, Jo.", "\"바로 그게 네 안에 있는 부드럽고 여자다운 반쪽을 끌어내 줄 거야, 조."], ["You are like a chestnut-burr, prickly outside, but silky-soft within, and a sweet kernel, if one can only get at it.", "너는 밤송이 같은 사람이야. 겉은 까칠해도 속은 비단처럼 부드럽고, 일단 닿기만 하면 알맹이가 달콤하지."], ["Love will make you show your heart some day, and then the rough burr will fall off.\"", "언젠가 사랑이 네 마음을 열어 보이게 할 거고, 그럼 그 거친 껍질도 떨어져 나갈 거야.\""]], [["\"Frost opens chestnut-burrs, ma'am, and it takes a good shake to bring them down.", "\"밤송이는 서리가 내려야 벌어지는 법이고, 떨어뜨리려면 한참을 흔들어 대야 하잖아."], ["Boys go nutting, and I don't care to be bagged by them,\"", "사내애들이 밤 주우러 다니지만, 난 그 애들 자루에 담기긴 싫은걸.\""], ["returned Jo, pasting away at the kite which no wind that blows would ever carry up, for Daisy had tied herself on as a bob.", "조는 그렇게 받아치며 연에 풀칠을 했다. 데이지가 꼬리 추 노릇을 하겠다며 제 몸을 매달아 놓은 통에, 어떤 바람이 불어도 결코 하늘로 떠오를 리 없는 연이었다."]], [["Meg laughed, for she was glad to see a glimmer of Jo's old spirit, but she felt it her duty to enforce her opinion by every argument in her power; and the sisterly chats were not wasted, especially as two of Meg's most effective arguments were the babies, whom Jo loved tenderly. Grief is the best opener for some hearts, and Jo's was nearly ready for the bag: a little more sunshine to ripen the nut, then, not a boy's impatient shake, but a man's hand reached up to pick it gently from the burr, and find the kernel sound and sweet. If she had suspected this, she would have shut up tight, and been more prickly than ever; fortunately she wasn't thinking about herself, so, when the time came, down she dropped.", "메그는 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 조의 옛 기개가 살짝 비치는 것이 반가웠던 것이다. 그러면서도 자기가 할 수 있는 온갖 논리를 동원해 제 생각을 밀어붙이는 것이 도리라고 여겼다. 그리고 그 자매간의 수다는 헛되지 않았다. 특히 메그의 가장 효과적인 두 가지 논거가 바로 아기들이었으니, 조가 그 아이들을 끔찍이 사랑했기 때문이다. 어떤 마음에는 슬픔이 가장 좋은 여는 도구가 된다. 조의 마음도 거의 자루에 담길 채비가 되어 있었다. 밤알을 여물게 할 햇살이 조금만 더 있으면, 그다음엔 사내애의 조급한 흔듦이 아니라 어른의 손이 뻗어 와 부드럽게 껍질에서 그것을 따내어, 알맹이가 온전하고 달콤하다는 것을 알아보면 될 일이었다. 만약 조가 이를 눈치챘다면 단단히 입을 다물고 어느 때보다 까칠하게 굴었으리라. 다행히도 그녀는 자기 생각에 골몰해 있지 않았기에, 때가 오자 톡 떨어졌다."]], [["Now, if she had been the heroine of a moral story-book, she ought at this period of her life to have become quite saintly, renounced the world, and gone about doing good in a mortified bonnet, with tracts in her pocket. But, you see, Jo wasn't a heroine; she was only a struggling human girl, like hundreds of others, and she just acted out her nature, being sad, cross, listless, or energetic, as the mood suggested. It's highly virtuous to say we'll be good, but we can't do it all at once, and it takes a long pull, a strong pull, and a pull all together, before some of us even get our feet set in the right way. Jo had got so far, she was learning to do her duty, and to feel unhappy if she did not; but to do it cheerfully--ah, that was another thing! She had often said she wanted to do something splendid, no matter how hard; and now she had her wish, for what could be more beautiful than to devote her life to father and mother, trying to make home as happy to them as they had to her? And, if difficulties were necessary to increase the splendor of the effort, what could be harder for a restless, ambitious girl than to give up her own hopes, plans, and desires, and cheerfully live for others?", "자, 만약 조가 교훈적인 이야기책의 여주인공이었다면, 인생의 이 무렵에 이르러 마땅히 무척 성스러운 사람이 되어 세상을 등지고, 수수한 보닛을 쓴 채 주머니에 전도지를 넣고 다니며 선행을 베풀었어야 했으리라. 하지만 보시다시피 조는 여주인공이 아니었다. 그녀는 그저 수백 명의 다른 소녀들과 마찬가지로 발버둥 치는 한 인간 소녀였을 뿐이고, 그저 제 본성대로 행동했다. 기분에 따라 슬퍼하기도, 짜증을 내기도, 무기력해지기도, 활기에 차기도 하면서. 착하게 살겠다고 말하기야 더없이 갸륵하지만, 그것을 단번에 해낼 수는 없는 법이다. 우리 중 누군가가 두 발을 제대로 된 길에 디디기까지에도 오래 끌어당기고, 힘껏 끌어당기고, 다 함께 끌어당겨야 한다. 조는 거기까지는 와 있었다. 제 의무를 다하는 법을 배워 가고 있었고, 그러지 못하면 마음이 편치 않았다. 그러나 그것을 기꺼이 즐겁게 해내는 일—아, 그건 또 다른 문제였다! 그녀는 곧잘 무언가 멋진 일을, 아무리 힘들어도 좋으니 해 보고 싶다고 말하곤 했다. 그리고 이제 그 소원이 이루어진 셈이었다. 아버지와 어머니를 위해 제 삶을 바쳐, 그분들이 자기에게 그래 주었듯 그분들에게 집을 행복하게 만들어 드리는 것보다 더 아름다운 일이 어디 있겠는가? 그리고 그 노력의 빛이 더 환해지려면 어려움이 필요하다면, 들떠 있고 야심 찬 소녀에게 자기 자신의 희망과 계획과 욕망을 단념하고 기꺼이 남을 위해 사는 것보다 더 힘든 일이 어디 있겠는가?"]], [["Providence had taken her at her word; here was the task, not what she had expected, but better, because self had no part in it: now, could she do it? She decided that she would try; and, in her first attempt, she found the helps I have suggested. Still another was given her, and she took it, not as a reward, but as a comfort, as Christian took the refreshment afforded by the little arbor where he rested, as he climbed the hill called Difficulty.", "섭리는 그녀의 말 그대로를 받아들였다. 여기 그 과업이 있었다. 그녀가 기대했던 것은 아니었지만, 거기엔 자기 자신이 끼어들 자리가 없었으므로 오히려 더 나은 것이었다. 자, 그녀가 그것을 해낼 수 있을까? 그녀는 한번 해 보기로 마음먹었고, 그 첫 시도에서 앞서 이야기한 도움들을 만났다. 또 하나의 도움이 그녀에게 주어졌고, 그녀는 그것을 보상이 아니라 위안으로 받아들였다. \"곤경\"이라 불리는 언덕을 오르던 크리스천이 작은 정자에서 누렸던 쉼을 받아들이듯이."]], [["\"Why don't you write?", "\"넌 왜 글을 안 쓰니?"], ["That always used to make you happy,\"", "그게 늘 너를 행복하게 해 주었잖아.\""], ["said her mother, once, when the desponding fit overshadowed Jo.", "한번은 낙심한 기운이 조를 짓누를 때, 어머니가 그렇게 말했다."]], [["\"I've no heart to write, and if I had, nobody cares for my things.\"", "\"글 쓸 마음이 안 나요. 설령 쓴다 해도 제 글에 신경 쓰는 사람은 아무도 없어요.\""]], [["\"We do; write something for us, and never mind the rest of the world.", "\"우리가 신경 쓰잖니. 우리를 위해 뭐든 써 보렴, 세상 사람들이야 어떻든 신경 쓰지 말고."], ["Try it, dear; I'm sure it would do you good, and please us very much.\"", "한번 해 봐, 얘야. 분명 너한테도 좋을 거고, 우리도 무척 기쁠 거야.\""]], [["\"Don't believe I can;\"", "\"할 수 있을 것 같지 않아요.\""], ["but Jo got out her desk, and began to overhaul her half-finished manuscripts.", "그래도 조는 책상을 꺼내, 쓰다 만 원고들을 뒤적이기 시작했다."]], [["An hour afterward her mother peeped in, and there she was, scratching away, with her black pinafore on, and an absorbed expression, which caused Mrs.", "한 시간쯤 뒤 어머니가 살짝 들여다보니, 조는 검은 앞치마를 두른 채 무언가에 온통 빠져든 표정으로 사각사각 글을 써 내려가고 있었다."], ["March to smile, and slip away, well pleased with the success of her suggestion.", "그 모습에 마치 부인은 빙긋 웃으며 슬며시 물러났다. 자신의 제안이 성공한 것에 흐뭇해하면서."], ["Jo never knew how it happened, but something got into that story that went straight to the hearts of those who read it; for, when her family had laughed and cried over it, her father sent it, much against her will, to one of the popular magazines, and, to her utter surprise, it was not only paid for, but others requested.", "어찌 된 일인지 조 자신도 알 수 없었지만, 그 이야기에는 읽는 이의 마음을 곧장 파고드는 무언가가 깃들어 있었다. 식구들이 그 글을 읽고 웃다가 울고 나자, 아버지는 그녀가 한사코 마다하는데도 그것을 어느 인기 잡지사로 보냈는데, 놀랍게도 원고료를 받았을 뿐 아니라 다른 글도 청탁받았던 것이다."], ["Letters from several persons, whose praise was honor, followed the appearance of the little story, newspapers copied it, and strangers as well as friends admired it.", "그 짧은 이야기가 실린 뒤로, 그 칭찬이 곧 영예인 여러 사람의 편지가 잇따랐고, 신문들이 그것을 옮겨 실었으며, 친구뿐 아니라 낯선 이들까지 그 글에 감탄했다."], ["For a small thing it was a great success; and Jo was more astonished than when her novel was commended and condemned all at once.", "작은 것치고는 큰 성공이었다. 조는 자기 소설이 칭찬과 비난을 한꺼번에 받았을 때보다 더 어리둥절했다."]], [["\"I don't understand it.", "\"이해가 안 돼요."], ["What _can_ there be in a simple little story like that, to make people praise it so?\"", "그렇게 단순하고 자그마한 이야기에 도대체 무엇이 들었기에 사람들이 그토록 칭찬하는 걸까요?\""], ["she said, quite bewildered.", "그녀가 어리둥절해하며 말했다."]], [["\"There is truth in it, Jo, that's the secret; humor and pathos make it alive, and you have found your style at last. You wrote with no thought of fame or money, and put your heart into it, my daughter; you have had the bitter, now comes the sweet. Do your best, and grow as happy as we are in your success.\"", "\"거기엔 진실이 담겨 있단다, 조. 그게 비결이야. 해학과 애잔함이 그것을 살아 있게 하지. 너는 마침내 네 문체를 찾은 거란다. 너는 명성이나 돈은 생각지도 않고, 거기에 네 마음을 쏟아부었으니까, 얘야. 쓴 것을 맛보았으니, 이제 단 것이 올 차례구나. 최선을 다하렴. 그리고 네 성공을 보며 우리만큼 행복해지렴.\""]], [["\"If there _is_ anything good or true in what I write, it isn't mine; I owe it all to you and mother and to Beth,\"", "\"제 글에 무언가 좋거나 참된 것이 있다면, 그건 제 것이 아니에요. 다 아버지와 어머니, 그리고 베스 덕분이지요.\""], ["said Jo, more touched by her father's words than by any amount of praise from the world.", "조는 세상의 그 어떤 칭찬보다 아버지의 말에 더 깊이 감동하며 말했다."]], [["So, taught by love and sorrow, Jo wrote her little stories, and sent them away to make friends for themselves and her, finding it a very charitable world to such humble wanderers; for they were kindly welcomed, and sent home comfortable tokens to their mother, like dutiful children whom good fortune overtakes.", "그리하여 사랑과 슬픔으로 단련된 조는 자그마한 이야기들을 써서, 그 글들이 스스로와 그녀를 위해 친구를 사귀도록 세상으로 내보냈다. 그녀는 세상이 이런 보잘것없는 떠돌이들에게 무척 너그러운 곳임을 알게 되었다. 글들은 따뜻하게 환영받았고, 행운을 만난 효성스러운 자식들처럼 어머니에게 흐뭇한 선물을 보내왔으니까."]], [["When Amy and Laurie wrote of their engagement, Mrs.", "에이미와 로리가 약혼 소식을 전해 왔을 때, 마치 부인은 조가 그 일을 기뻐하기 어려워하지 않을까 걱정했다."], ["March feared that Jo would find it difficult to rejoice over it, but her fears were soon set at rest; for, though Jo looked grave at first, she took it very quietly, and was full of hopes and plans for \"the children\"", "그러나 그 걱정은 곧 사라졌다. 조는 처음엔 표정이 굳었지만 무척 차분하게 받아들였고, 편지를 두 번 채 읽기도 전에 \"그 아이들\"에 대한 희망과 계획으로 가득해졌으니까."], ["before she read the letter twice.", "그것은 일종의 글로 쓴 이중창 같았다."], ["It was a sort of written duet, wherein each glorified the other in lover-like fashion, very pleasant to read and satisfactory to think of, for no one had any objection to make.", "연인답게 서로를 한껏 추켜세우고 있어서, 읽기에도 즐겁고 떠올리기에도 흐뭇했다. 누구도 토를 달 데가 없었다."]], [["\"You like it, mother?\"", "\"마음에 드세요, 어머니?\""], ["said Jo, as they laid down the closely written sheets, and looked at one another.", "빽빽이 쓰인 편지지를 내려놓고 서로 바라보며 조가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, I hoped it would be so, ever since Amy wrote that she had refused Fred.", "\"그래. 에이미가 프레드를 거절했다고 써 보낸 뒤로 줄곧 이렇게 되기를 바랐단다."], ["I felt sure then that something better than what you call the 'mercenary spirit'", "그때 난 그 애한테 네가 말하던 그 '돈만 밝히는 마음'보다 더 나은 무언가가 찾아왔다고 확신했지."], ["had come over her, and a hint here and there in her letters made me suspect that love and Laurie would win the day.\"", "그리고 편지 여기저기에 비치는 기색을 보고, 사랑과 로리가 이기리라는 걸 짐작했어.\""]], [["\"How sharp you are, Marmee, and how silent!", "\"마미도 참 눈치가 빠르셔라, 그러고도 어쩜 그리 입이 무거우셨어요!"], ["You never said a word to me.\"", "저한테는 한마디도 안 하셨잖아요.\""]], [["\"Mothers have need of sharp eyes and discreet tongues when they have girls to manage.", "\"딸들을 건사하려면 어미는 눈이 밝고 입은 신중해야 하는 법이란다."], ["I was half afraid to put the idea into your head, lest you should write and congratulate them before the thing was settled.\"", "그 생각을 네 머릿속에 넣어 줄까 봐 적이 두려웠어. 일이 정해지기도 전에 네가 편지를 써서 그 애들을 축하해 버릴까 봐.\""]], [["\"I'm not the scatter-brain I was; you may trust me, I'm sober and sensible enough for any one's _confidante_ now.\"", "\"전 이제 예전처럼 덜렁이가 아니에요. 절 믿으셔도 돼요. 이젠 누구의 속내 친구가 되어도 좋을 만큼 진중하고 분별 있으니까요.\""]], [["\"So you are, dear, and I should have made you mine, only I fancied it might pain you to learn that your Teddy loved any one else.\"", "\"그렇고말고, 얘야. 진작 너를 내 속내 친구로 삼았어야 했는데, 다만 너의 테디가 다른 사람을 사랑하게 됐다는 걸 알면 네가 아파할까 봐 그랬단다.\""]], [["\"Now, mother, did you really think I could be so silly and selfish, after I'd refused his love, when it was freshest, if not best?\"", "\"어머니, 정말로 제가 그렇게 어리석고 이기적일 거라고 생각하셨어요? 그 사랑이 가장 좋을 때는 아니었어도 가장 싱싱할 때 제가 거절했는데도요?\""]], [["\"I knew you were sincere then, Jo, but lately I have thought that if he came back, and asked again, you might, perhaps, feel like giving another answer. Forgive me, dear, I can't help seeing that you are very lonely, and sometimes there is a hungry look in your eyes that goes to my heart; so I fancied that your boy might fill the empty place if he tried now.\"", "\"그땐 네가 진심이라는 걸 알았다, 조. 다만 요즘 들어 이런 생각이 들더구나. 만약 그 애가 돌아와 다시 청한다면, 어쩌면 너는 이번엔 다른 대답을 하고 싶어질지도 모르겠다고. 용서하렴, 얘야. 네가 무척 외로워하는 걸 어미가 모를 수 없잖니. 가끔 네 눈에 어린 굶주린 듯한 빛이 내 가슴을 찌른단다. 그래서 만약 너의 그 애가 이제라도 다시 부딪쳐 본다면, 그 빈자리를 채워 줄 수 있지 않을까 싶었어.\""]], [["\"No, mother, it is better as it is, and I'm glad Amy has learned to love him. But you are right in one thing: I _am_ lonely, and perhaps if Teddy had tried again, I might have said 'Yes,' not because I love him any more, but because I care more to be loved than when he went away.\"", "\"아니에요, 어머니. 지금 이대로가 나아요. 그리고 에이미가 그를 사랑하게 돼서 기뻐요. 하지만 한 가지는 어머니 말씀이 맞아요. 전 외로워요. 그러니 만약 테디가 다시 부딪쳐 왔다면, 어쩌면 '네'라고 했을지도 몰라요. 그를 더 사랑해서가 아니라, 그가 떠났을 때보다 지금이 사랑받는 일에 더 마음이 쓰이니까요.\""]], [["\"I'm glad of that, Jo, for it shows that you are getting on.", "\"그 말을 들으니 기쁘구나, 조. 네가 나아지고 있다는 뜻이니까."], ["There are plenty to love you, so try to be satisfied with father and mother, sisters and brothers, friends and babies, till the best lover of all comes to give you your reward.\"", "너를 사랑할 사람은 많으니, 가장 좋은 연인이 와서 네게 보답을 줄 때까지 아버지와 어머니, 언니와 동생들, 친구들과 아기들로 만족하려무나.\""]], [["\"Mothers are the _best_ lovers in the world; but I don't mind whispering to Marmee that I'd like to try all kinds. It's very curious, but the more I try to satisfy myself with all sorts of natural affections, the more I seem to want. I'd no idea hearts could take in so many; mine is so elastic, it never seems full now, and I used to be quite contented with my family. I don't understand it.\"", "\"세상에서 가장 좋은 연인은 어머니들인걸요. 그래도 마미한테는 살짝 귀띔할게요. 전 온갖 종류의 사랑을 다 해 보고 싶어요. 참 이상해요. 온갖 자연스러운 애정으로 제 자신을 채워 보려 할수록, 더 많은 걸 바라게 되는 것 같아요. 마음이 그렇게 많은 걸 담을 수 있을 줄은 몰랐어요. 제 마음은 어찌나 늘어나는지 이젠 도무지 가득 차는 법이 없어요. 예전엔 식구들만으로도 충분히 만족했는데. 이해가 안 가요.\""]], [["\"I do;\" and Mrs.", "\"난 이해한단다.\""], ["March smiled her wise smile, as Jo turned back the leaves to read what Amy said of Laurie.", "마치 부인은 지혜로운 미소를 지었고, 조는 에이미가 로리에 대해 쓴 대목을 다시 읽으려 편지를 뒤로 넘겼다."]], [["\"It is so beautiful to be loved as Laurie loves me; he isn't sentimental, doesn't say much about it, but I see and feel it in all he says and does, and it makes me so happy and so humble that I don't seem to be the same girl I was.", "\"로리가 날 사랑해 주는 만큼 사랑받는다는 건 너무도 아름다운 일이에요. 그이는 감상적이지 않고, 그 일에 대해 말을 많이 하지도 않아요."], ["I never knew how good and generous and tender he was till now, for he lets me read his heart, and I find it full of noble impulses and hopes and purposes, and am so proud to know it's mine.", "하지만 그이가 하는 모든 말과 행동에서 저는 그것을 보고 느껴요. 그래서 너무나 행복하고 또 너무나 겸허해져서, 제가 예전의 그 아이가 아닌 것만 같아요."], ["He says he feels as if he 'could make a prosperous voyage now with me aboard as mate, and lots of love for ballast.'", "저는 그이가 얼마나 착하고 너그럽고 다정한지 이제껏 몰랐어요. 그이가 제게 마음을 활짝 열어 보여 주는데, 그 안이 고귀한 충동과 희망과 다짐으로 가득해서, 그게 제 것이라는 게 너무 자랑스러워요."], ["I pray he may, and try to be all he believes me, for I love my gallant captain with all my heart and soul and might, and never will desert him, while God lets us be together.", "그이는 '이제 너를 항해사로 태우고, 사랑을 잔뜩 바닥짐으로 실으면 순조로운 항해를 할 수 있을 것 같다'고 말해요. 부디 그렇게 되기를 빌고, 그이가 믿어 주는 그런 사람이 되려고 애쓰고 있어요."], ["O mother, I never knew how much like heaven this world could be, when two people love and live for one another!\"", "저는 제 늠름한 선장을 온 마음과 영혼과 힘을 다해 사랑하고, 하느님이 우리를 함께 있게 해 주시는 한 결코 그이를 떠나지 않을 테니까요. 오, 어머니, 두 사람이 서로를 사랑하고 서로를 위해 살아갈 때, 이 세상이 천국과 이토록 비슷해질 수 있는지 전 미처 몰랐어요!\""]], [["\"And that's our cool, reserved, and worldly Amy!", "\"이게 우리의 그 차분하고 새침하고 속물적이던 에이미라니!"], ["Truly, love does work miracles.", "사랑이란 정말 기적을 일으키는구나."], ["How very, very happy they must be!\"", "그 애들이 얼마나, 얼마나 행복할까!\""], ["And Jo laid the rustling sheets together with a careful hand, as one might shut the covers of a lovely romance, which holds the reader fast till the end comes, and he finds himself alone in the work-a-day world again.", "그러고는 조는 사각거리는 편지지를 조심스러운 손길로 한데 모았다. 끝이 올 때까지 독자를 단단히 붙잡아 두다가, 마지막 장을 덮는 순간 다시 자기 혼자 분주한 일상의 세상에 남겨졌음을 깨닫게 하는, 어느 아름다운 사랑 이야기의 표지를 덮듯이."]], [["By and by Jo roamed away upstairs, for it was rainy, and she could not walk. A restless spirit possessed her, and the old feeling came again, not bitter as it once was, but a sorrowfully patient wonder why one sister should have all she asked, the other nothing. It was not true; she knew that, and tried to put it away, but the natural craving for affection was strong, and Amy's happiness woke the hungry longing for some one to \"love with heart and soul, and cling to while God let them be together.\"", "이윽고 조는 위층으로 어슬렁어슬렁 올라갔다. 비가 와서 산책을 할 수 없었던 것이다. 어딘가 들뜬 기운이 그녀를 사로잡았고, 옛 감정이 다시 찾아왔다. 한때처럼 쓰라리지는 않았지만, 어찌하여 한 자매는 바라는 것을 다 갖고 다른 자매는 아무것도 갖지 못하는가, 슬프지만 참을성 있게 묻는 그런 의문이었다. 그건 사실이 아니었다. 그녀도 알았기에 그 생각을 떨치려 애썼지만, 애정을 향한 자연스러운 갈망은 강했고, 에이미의 행복은 \"온 마음과 영혼으로 사랑하며, 하느님이 함께 있게 해 주시는 동안 매달릴\" 누군가를 향한 굶주린 그리움을 일깨웠다."]], [["Up in the garret, where Jo's unquiet wanderings ended, stood four little wooden chests in a row, each marked with its owner's name, and each filled with relics of the childhood and girlhood ended now for all.", "조의 불안한 발걸음이 멎은 다락방에는 작은 나무 궤짝 네 개가 나란히 놓여 있었다. 저마다 주인의 이름이 적혀 있었고, 이제는 모두에게 끝나 버린 어린 시절과 소녀 시절의 유물들로 가득했다."], ["Jo glanced into them, and when she came to her own, leaned her chin on the edge, and stared absently at the chaotic collection, till a bundle of old exercise-books caught her eye.", "조는 궤짝들을 들여다보다가 자기 것에 이르자 가장자리에 턱을 괴고, 뒤죽박죽 모여 있는 물건들을 멍하니 바라보았다. 그러다 낡은 연습장 한 묶음에 눈길이 갔다."], ["She drew them out, turned them over, and re-lived that pleasant winter at kind Mrs.", "그것을 꺼내 이리저리 뒤적이며, 그녀는 친절한 커크 부인 댁에서 보낸 그 즐거운 겨울을 다시 살았다. 처음엔 미소 짓다가, 이내 생각에 잠긴 표정이 되더니, 그다음엔 슬퍼졌다."], ["Kirke's.", "그리고 교수의 글씨로 적힌 짧은 글귀에 이르자, 입술이 떨리기 시작하고 책들이 무릎에서 미끄러져 내렸다."], ["She had smiled at first, then she looked thoughtful, next sad, and when she came to a little message written in the Professor's hand, her lips began to tremble, the books slid out of her lap, and she sat looking at the friendly words, as if they took a new meaning, and touched a tender spot in her heart.", "그녀는 그 다정한 글귀를 가만히 바라보았다. 마치 그것이 새로운 의미를 띠며 가슴속 어떤 여린 자리를 건드리기라도 한 듯이."]], [["\"Wait for me, my friend.", "\"날 기다려 주오, 나의 벗이여."], ["I may be a little late, but I shall surely come.\"", "조금 늦을지는 몰라도, 반드시 가리다.\""]], [["\"Oh, if he only would! So kind, so good, so patient with me always; my dear old Fritz, I didn't value him half enough when I had him, but now how I should love to see him, for every one seems going away from me, and I'm all alone.\"", "\"오, 정말 와 주신다면! 늘 그토록 다정하고, 그토록 착하고, 내게 그토록 참을성 있던 분. 사랑하는 나의 프리츠. 곁에 계실 땐 그분의 진가를 절반도 알아보지 못했는데, 이제는 어찌나 뵙고 싶은지. 다들 내게서 떠나가 버리고, 난 이렇게 혼자가 됐으니까.\""]], [["And holding the little paper fast, as if it were a promise yet to be fulfilled, Jo laid her head down on a comfortable rag-bag, and cried, as if in opposition to the rain pattering on the roof.", "그러고는 마치 아직 이루어지지 않은 약속이라도 되는 양 그 작은 종이를 꼭 쥔 채, 조는 푹신한 헝겊 자루에 머리를 묻고 울었다. 지붕을 두드리는 빗소리에 맞서기라도 하듯이."]], [["Was it all self-pity, loneliness, or low spirits?", "그것은 모두 자기연민이었을까, 외로움이었을까, 아니면 가라앉은 기분이었을까?"], ["or was it the waking up of a sentiment which had bided its time as patiently as its inspirer?", "그도 아니면, 그것을 일깨운 이가 그러했듯 끈기 있게 제때를 기다려 온 어떤 감정의 깨어남이었을까?"], ["Who shall say?", "그 누가 말할 수 있으랴?"]], [["Jo was alone in the twilight, lying on the old sofa, looking at the fire, and thinking.", "조는 황혼 무렵 혼자 낡은 소파에 누워, 난롯불을 바라보며 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 땅거미 지는 시간을 보내는 그녀의 가장 좋아하는 방식이었다."], ["It was her favorite way of spending the hour of dusk; no one disturbed her, and she used to lie there on Beth's little red pillow, planning stories, dreaming dreams, or thinking tender thoughts of the sister who never seemed far away.", "아무도 그녀를 방해하지 않았고, 그녀는 베스가 쓰던 작고 붉은 베개에 누워 이야기를 구상하고, 꿈을 꾸고, 결코 멀리 있는 것 같지 않은 동생을 향한 애틋한 생각에 잠기곤 했다. 그녀의 얼굴은 지치고, 무겁고, 다소 슬퍼 보였다."], ["Her face looked tired, grave, and rather sad; for to-morrow was her birthday, and she was thinking how fast the years went by, how old she was getting, and how little she seemed to have accomplished.", "내일이 그녀의 생일이었던 것이다. 세월이 얼마나 빠르게 흘러가는지, 자기가 얼마나 나이를 먹어 가는지, 그러고도 이뤄 놓은 것은 얼마나 보잘것없는지를 생각하고 있었다."], ["Almost twenty-five, and nothing to show for it.", "곧 스물다섯인데 내세울 것이 아무것도 없었다. 그 점에서 조는 잘못 생각하고 있었다."], ["Jo was mistaken in that; there was a good deal to show, and by and by she saw, and was grateful for it.", "내세울 것은 꽤 많았으니까. 머지않아 그녀도 그것을 알아보고 고마워하게 된다."]], [["\"An old maid, that's what I'm to be. A literary spinster, with a pen for a spouse, a family of stories for children, and twenty years hence a morsel of fame, perhaps; when, like poor Johnson, I'm old, and can't enjoy it, solitary, and can't share it, independent, and don't need it. Well, I needn't be a sour saint nor a selfish sinner; and, I dare say, old maids are very comfortable when they get used to it; but--\" and there Jo sighed, as if the prospect was not inviting.", "\"노처녀, 내가 될 건 그거지. 펜을 신랑 삼고, 이야기들을 자식 삼은 문학하는 노처녀. 그러다 한 이십 년 뒤엔 어쩌면 한 줌의 명성이라도 얻겠지. 그땐 가엾은 존슨처럼 늙어서 그걸 누리지도 못하고, 외로워서 함께 나누지도 못하고, 자립해서 그게 필요하지도 않을 때겠지만. 뭐, 그래도 시큰둥한 성자도, 이기적인 죄인도 될 건 없잖아. 노처녀도 익숙해지고 나면 꽤 편안하다잖아. 하지만—\" 그러고는 조는 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그 앞날이 그리 달갑지만은 않다는 듯이."]], [["It seldom is, at first, and thirty seems the end of all things to five-and-twenty; but it's not so bad as it looks, and one can get on quite happily if one has something in one's self to fall back upon. At twenty-five, girls begin to talk about being old maids, but secretly resolve that they never will be; at thirty they say nothing about it, but quietly accept the fact, and, if sensible, console themselves by remembering that they have twenty more useful, happy years, in which they may be learning to grow old gracefully. Don't laugh at the spinsters, dear girls, for often very tender, tragical romances are hidden away in the hearts that beat so quietly under the sober gowns, and many silent sacrifices of youth, health, ambition, love itself, make the faded faces beautiful in God's sight. Even the sad, sour sisters should be kindly dealt with, because they have missed the sweetest part of life, if for no other reason; and, looking at them with compassion, not contempt, girls in their bloom should remember that they too may miss the blossom time; that rosy cheeks don't last forever, that silver threads will come in the bonnie brown hair, and that, by and by, kindness and respect will be as sweet as love and admiration now. Gentlemen, which means boys, be courteous to the old maids, no matter how poor and plain and prim, for the only chivalry worth having is that which is the readiest to pay deference to the old, protect the feeble, and serve womankind, regardless of rank, age, or color. Just recollect the good aunts who have not only lectured and fussed, but nursed and petted, too often without thanks; the scrapes they have helped you out of, the \"tips\" they have given you from their small store, the stitches the patient old fingers have set for you, the steps the willing old feet have taken, and gratefully pay the dear old ladies the little attentions that women love to receive as long as they live. The bright-eyed girls are quick to see such traits, and will like you all the better for them; and if death, almost the only power that can part mother and son, should rob you of yours, you will be sure to find a tender welcome and maternal cherishing from some Aunt Priscilla, who has kept the warmest corner of her lonely old heart for the \"the best nevvy in the world.\"", "처음에는 좀처럼 달갑지 않은 법이고, 스물다섯에게 서른은 모든 것의 끝처럼 보인다. 그러나 보기만큼 나쁘지는 않아서, 스스로 기댈 무언가만 있다면 얼마든지 행복하게 살아갈 수 있다. 스물다섯에 소녀들은 노처녀가 된다는 이야기를 꺼내기 시작하지만, 속으로는 결코 그렇게 되지 않으리라 다짐한다. 서른이 되면 그 이야기는 입에 올리지 않은 채 조용히 그 사실을 받아들이고, 분별이 있다면 앞으로도 스무 해는 더 쓸모 있고 행복한 세월이 남아 그동안 우아하게 늙어 가는 법을 배울 수 있다고 떠올리며 스스로를 위로한다. 노처녀들을 비웃지 마라, 사랑스러운 소녀들이여. 점잖은 옷자락 아래 그토록 잔잔히 뛰는 가슴속에는 더없이 애틋하고 비극적인 사랑 이야기가 숨어 있는 일이 많고, 젊음과 건강과 야심, 그리고 사랑 자체를 말없이 바친 수많은 희생이 그 빛바랜 얼굴들을 하느님 보시기에 아름답게 만드니까. 슬프고 시큰둥한 자매들조차 따뜻하게 대해 주어야 한다. 다른 까닭이 없더라도, 그들이 인생에서 가장 달콤한 부분을 놓쳤다는 그 한 가지 이유만으로. 그들을 경멸이 아니라 연민으로 바라보면서, 한창 피어나는 소녀들은 자기들 역시 꽃 피는 시절을 놓칠 수 있다는 것을, 발그레한 뺨이 영원하지 않다는 것을, 고운 갈색 머리에도 은실이 섞일 것이며, 머지않아 친절과 존경이 지금의 사랑과 찬사만큼 달콤해지리라는 것을 기억해야 한다. 신사 여러분, 그러니까 소년들이여, 노처녀들에게 예의를 갖추어라. 아무리 가난하고 못나고 깐깐하더라도. 지닐 만한 가치가 있는 단 하나의 기사도란 신분이나 나이, 피부색을 가리지 않고 노인을 가장 기꺼이 받들고, 약한 이를 보호하며, 여인들을 섬기는 것이니까. 그저 떠올려 보라. 잔소리를 하고 부산을 떨 뿐 아니라 간호해 주고 다독여 주기도 한, 그러고도 너무 자주 고맙다는 말 한마디 듣지 못한 그 좋은 아주머니들을. 그분들이 너희를 끌어내 준 곤경들을, 그 빠듯한 살림에서 쥐여 준 \"용돈\"을, 참을성 있는 늙은 손가락이 너희를 위해 떠 준 바늘땀들을, 기꺼이 움직여 준 늙은 발걸음들을. 그러니 고마운 마음으로, 그 다정한 노부인들에게 여자들이 평생토록 받고 싶어 하는 그 작은 정성들을 갚아 드려라. 눈 맑은 소녀들은 그런 됨됨이를 금세 알아보고, 그 때문에 너희를 더욱 좋아할 것이다. 그리고 어머니와 아들을 갈라놓을 수 있는 거의 유일한 힘인 죽음이 너희에게서 어머니를 앗아 가더라도, 너희는 \"세상에서 둘도 없는 조카 녀석\"을 위해 외로운 늙은 가슴의 가장 따뜻한 한구석을 비워 둔 어느 프리실라 아주머니에게서 다정한 환대와 어머니 같은 보살핌을 분명 얻게 될 것이다."]], [["Jo must have fallen asleep (as I dare say my reader has during this little homily), for suddenly Laurie's ghost seemed to stand before her,--a substantial, lifelike ghost,--leaning over her, with the very look he used to wear when he felt a good deal and didn't like to show it. But, like Jenny in the ballad,--", "조는 (이 짤막한 설교를 듣는 동안 나의 독자가 그러했으리라 짐작되듯) 잠이 들었던 게 틀림없다. 별안간 로리의 유령이 그녀 앞에 서 있는 것만 같았으니까. 살아 있는 듯 생생한, 실체 있는 유령이었다. 그 유령은 그녀 위로 몸을 숙이고 있었는데, 무언가를 깊이 느끼면서도 내색하기 싫어할 때 짓던 바로 그 표정 그대로였다. 그러나 옛 노래 속의 제니처럼,"]], [["\"She could not think it he,\"", "\"그것이 그이라고는 차마 믿을 수 없어\""]], [["and lay staring up at him in startled silence, till he stooped and kissed her.", "그녀는 깜짝 놀라 말없이 그를 올려다보기만 했다. 그가 몸을 숙여 입을 맞출 때까지."], ["Then she knew him, and flew up, crying joyfully,--", "그제야 그를 알아본 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 기쁨에 차 외쳤다."]], [["\"O my Teddy!", "\"오, 내 테디!"], ["O my Teddy!\"", "오, 내 테디!\""]], [["\"Dear Jo, you are glad to see me, then?\"", "\"조 누나, 그래도 날 보고 반가운 거지?\""]], [["\"Glad!", "\"반갑다마다!"], ["My blessed boy, words can't express my gladness.", "내 귀한 녀석아, 이 반가움은 말로는 다 못 해."], ["Where's Amy?\"", "에이미는 어디 있어?\""]], [["\"Your mother has got her down at Meg's.", "\"어머니가 메그 누나네로 데려가셨어."], ["We stopped there by the way, and there was no getting my wife out of their clutches.\"", "오는 길에 거기 들렀는데, 다들 내 아내를 붙잡고 놓아주질 않잖아.\""]], [["\"Your what?\"", "\"네 뭐라고?\""], ["cried Jo, for Laurie uttered those two words with an unconscious pride and satisfaction which betrayed him.", "조가 소리쳤다. 로리가 그 두 마디를 자기도 모르게 우쭐대며 흐뭇하게 내뱉어 속내를 들켜 버렸기 때문이다."]], [["\"Oh, the dickens!", "\"이런, 제기랄!"], ["now I've done it;\"", "그만 일을 저질러 버렸네.\""], ["and he looked so guilty that Jo was down upon him like a flash.", "그가 어찌나 죄지은 표정을 짓던지, 조는 번개같이 그를 몰아세웠다."]], [["\"You've gone and got married!\"", "\"기어이 결혼을 해 버렸구나!\""]], [["\"Yes, please, but I never will again;\"", "\"응, 미안. 하지만 두 번 다시는 안 할게.\""], ["and he went down upon his knees, with a penitent clasping of hands, and a face full of mischief, mirth, and triumph.", "그러고는 그는 뉘우치듯 두 손을 모으고 무릎을 꿇었다. 짓궂음과 즐거움과 의기양양함이 가득한 얼굴로."]], [["\"Actually married?\"", "\"정말로 결혼한 거야?\""]], [["\"Very much so, thank you.\"", "\"아주 확실하게, 고마워.\""]], [["\"Mercy on us!", "\"세상에!"], ["What dreadful thing will you do next?\"", "다음엔 또 무슨 끔찍한 짓을 저지를 작정이니?\""], ["and Jo fell into her seat, with a gasp.", "그러고는 조는 숨을 헐떡이며 자리에 털썩 주저앉았다."]], [["\"A characteristic, but not exactly complimentary, congratulation,\"", "\"누나다운 축하긴 한데, 그리 듣기 좋은 축하는 아니네.\""], ["returned Laurie, still in an abject attitude, but beaming with satisfaction.", "로리가 여전히 비굴한 자세 그대로, 그러면서도 만족감에 환히 빛나며 대꾸했다."]], [["\"What can you expect, when you take one's breath away, creeping in like a burglar, and letting cats out of bags like that?", "\"도둑처럼 살금살금 기어들어 와서 사람 숨을 다 막아 놓고, 그렇게 비밀을 툭 흘려 놓고선 무슨 축하를 바라니?"], ["Get up, you ridiculous boy, and tell me all about it.\"", "일어나, 이 우스꽝스러운 녀석아. 그리고 자초지종을 다 얘기해.\""]], [["\"Not a word, unless you let me come in my old place, and promise not to barricade.\"", "\"한마디도 못 해. 내 옛 자리에 앉게 해 주고, 막아 세우지 않겠다고 약속하지 않으면.\""]], [["Jo laughed at that as she had not done for many a long day, and patted the sofa invitingly, as she said, in a cordial tone,--", "조는 그 말에 오랜만에 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그러고는 다정한 목소리로 소파를 토닥이며 권했다."]], [["\"The old pillow is up garret, and we don't need it now; so, come and 'fess, Teddy.\"", "\"그 베개는 다락방에 있어. 이제 필요도 없고. 그러니 와서 다 털어놔, 테디.\""]], [["\"How good it sounds to hear you say 'Teddy'!", "\"누나가 '테디'라고 불러 주는 게 어찌나 듣기 좋은지!"], ["No one ever calls me that but you;\"", "날 그렇게 부르는 사람은 누나밖에 없어.\""], ["and Laurie sat down, with an air of great content.", "그러고는 로리는 더없이 만족스러운 태도로 자리에 앉았다."]], [["\"What does Amy call you?\"", "\"에이미는 널 뭐라고 불러?\""]], [["\"My lord.\"", "\"내 주인님.\""]], [["\"That's like her.", "\"그 애답네."], ["Well, you look it;\"", "하긴, 너도 그래 보이긴 해.\""], ["and Jo's eyes plainly betrayed that she found her boy comelier than ever.", "조의 눈은 제 녀석이 어느 때보다 잘생겨 보인다고 분명하게 말하고 있었다."]], [["The pillow was gone, but there _was_ a barricade, nevertheless,--a natural one, raised by time, absence, and change of heart.", "베개는 사라졌지만, 그래도 막아선 무언가가 있긴 했다. 세월과 떨어져 지낸 시간, 그리고 변해 버린 마음이 세워 놓은 자연스러운 장벽이었다."], ["Both felt it, and for a minute looked at one another as if that invisible barrier cast a little shadow over them.", "두 사람 다 그것을 느꼈고, 잠시 서로를 바라보았다. 그 보이지 않는 장벽이 둘 위로 옅은 그늘을 드리우기라도 한 듯이."], ["It was gone directly, however, for Laurie said, with a vain attempt at dignity,--", "그러나 그 그늘은 곧 사라졌다. 로리가 짐짓 위엄을 부려 보려 헛되이 애쓰며 이렇게 말했으니까."]], [["\"Don't I look like a married man and the head of a family?\"", "\"내가 결혼한 남자에다 한 집안의 가장으로 안 보여?\""]], [["\"Not a bit, and you never will.", "\"눈곱만큼도. 그리고 앞으로도 영 안 그럴걸."], ["You've grown bigger and bonnier, but you are the same scapegrace as ever.\"", "덩치는 커지고 더 잘생겨졌지만, 넌 여전히 옛날 그대로 말썽꾸러기인걸.\""]], [["\"Now, really, Jo, you ought to treat me with more respect,\"", "\"이거 참, 조 누나, 날 좀 더 존중해 줘야 하는 거 아니야.\""], ["began Laurie, who enjoyed it all immensely.", "로리가 이 모든 게 더없이 즐겁다는 듯 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["\"How can I, when the mere idea of you, married and settled, is so irresistibly funny that I can't keep sober!\"", "\"어떻게 그래? 네가 결혼해서 자리 잡았다는 생각만 해도 우스워 죽겠는데 정색을 어떻게 하니!\""], ["answered Jo, smiling all over her face, so infectiously that they had another laugh, and then settled down for a good talk, quite in the pleasant old fashion.", "조가 온 얼굴에 웃음을 띠며 대꾸했는데, 어찌나 옮는지 두 사람은 또 한바탕 웃고 나서, 옛 정겨운 방식 그대로 도란도란 이야기를 시작했다."]], [["\"It's no use your going out in the cold to get Amy, for they are all coming up presently.", "\"에이미 데리러 추운 데 나갈 것 없어. 다들 곧 올라올 테니까."], ["I couldn't wait; I wanted to be the one to tell you the grand surprise, and have 'first skim,'", "난 도무지 못 기다리겠더라고. 그 깜짝 소식을 누나한테 맨 처음 전하는 사람이 되고 싶었거든."], ["as we used to say when we squabbled about the cream.\"", "옛날에 크림 가지고 다툴 때 말마따나 '첫 국물'을 떠먹고 싶었어.\""]], [["\"Of course you did, and spoilt your story by beginning at the wrong end.", "\"그래, 너답구나. 그러다 거꾸로 시작해서 이야기를 다 망쳐 버렸잖니."], ["Now, start right, and tell me how it all happened; I'm pining to know.\"", "자, 이번엔 제대로 처음부터, 어떻게 다 된 건지 얘기해 봐. 궁금해 미치겠으니까.\""]], [["\"Well, I did it to please Amy,\"", "\"음, 난 에이미를 기쁘게 해 주려고 한 거야.\""], ["began Laurie, with a twinkle that made Jo exclaim,--", "로리가 눈을 반짝이며 입을 떼자, 조가 외쳤다."]], [["\"Fib number one; Amy did it to please you.", "\"거짓말 첫 번째. 에이미가 널 기쁘게 해 주려고 한 거겠지."], ["Go on, and tell the truth, if you can, sir.\"", "자, 계속해 봐. 할 수 있으면 진실만 말해, 이 양반아.\""]], [["\"Now she's beginning to marm it; isn't it jolly to hear her?\"", "\"이제 슬슬 훈계 선생님 노릇을 하려 드는걸. 저 말투 듣는 게 어찌나 재미난지 몰라.\""], ["said Laurie to the fire, and the fire glowed and sparkled as if it quite agreed.", "로리가 난롯불을 향해 말하자, 난롯불도 전적으로 동의한다는 듯 환히 일렁이며 탁탁 튀었다."], ["\"It's all the same, you know, she and I being one.", "\"어차피 다 같은 얘기야. 그 애랑 나는 하나니까."], ["We planned to come home with the Carrols, a month or more ago, but they suddenly changed their minds, and decided to pass another winter in Paris.", "한 달 남짓 전에 우린 캐럴 댁 식구들과 함께 집으로 돌아오기로 했었어. 그런데 그분들이 갑자기 마음을 바꿔서 파리에서 겨울을 한 번 더 나기로 한 거야."], ["But grandpa wanted to come home; he went to please me, and I couldn't let him go alone, neither could I leave Amy; and Mrs.", "하지만 할아버지는 집에 오고 싶어 하셨어. 날 위해 가셨던 거니까, 혼자 보내 드릴 순 없었지."], ["Carrol had got English notions about chaperons and such nonsense, and wouldn't let Amy come with us.", "그렇다고 에이미를 두고 올 수도 없고. 게다가 캐럴 부인은 보호자니 뭐니 하는 영국식 관념이 머릿속에 박혀 있어서, 에이미가 우리랑 같이 오는 걸 허락하지 않으시는 거야."], ["So I just settled the difficulty by saying, 'Let's be married, and then we can do as we like.'\"", "그래서 내가 그냥 이렇게 말해서 문제를 정리해 버렸어. '우리 결혼하자, 그럼 마음대로 할 수 있잖아'라고.\""]], [["\"Of course you did; you always have things to suit you.\"", "\"그래, 너답네. 넌 늘 일을 네 좋을 대로 맞추는 녀석이지.\""]], [["\"Not always;\"", "\"늘 그런 건 아니야.\""], ["and something in Laurie's voice made Jo say hastily,--", "로리의 목소리에 어린 무언가에 조는 황급히 말을 돌렸다."]], [["\"How did you ever get aunt to agree?\"", "\"그래, 도대체 이모님은 어떻게 설득한 거야?\""]], [["\"It was hard work; but, between us, we talked her over, for we had heaps of good reasons on our side.", "\"쉽지 않았어. 그래도 둘이 같이 이모님을 구슬렸지."], ["There wasn't time to write and ask leave, but you all liked it, had consented to it by and by, and it was only 'taking Time by the fetlock,'", "우리 쪽에 그럴듯한 이유가 잔뜩 있었거든. 편지로 허락을 구할 시간이야 없었지만, 어차피 다들 좋아할 일이고 머지않아 동의했을 일이잖아."], ["as my wife says.\"", "그러니 그저 내 아내 말마따나 '시간의 발목을 잡아챈' 것뿐이었어.\""]], [["\"Aren't we proud of those two words, and don't we like to say them?\"", "\"'내 아내'라는 그 두 마디가 어지간히도 자랑스럽고, 입에 올리고 싶어 죽겠나 보지?\""], ["interrupted Jo, addressing the fire in her turn, and watching with delight the happy light it seemed to kindle in the eyes that had been so tragically gloomy when she saw them last.", "조가 이번엔 자기가 난롯불을 향해 끼어들며, 마지막으로 보았을 때 그토록 비극적으로 어둡던 그 눈에 이제 행복의 빛이 일렁이는 것을 흐뭇하게 지켜보며 말했다."]], [["\"A trifle, perhaps; she's such a captivating little woman I can't help being proud of her. Well, then, uncle and aunt were there to play propriety; we were so absorbed in one another we were of no mortal use apart, and that charming arrangement would make everything easy all round; so we did it.\"", "\"아주 조금은, 그럴지도. 그 앤 어찌나 매혹적인 여자인지 자랑스럽지 않을 수가 없는걸. 어쨌든, 그때 외삼촌과 이모님이 거기 계셔서 체면치레를 해 주셨고, 우린 서로한테 푹 빠져 있어서 떨어져 있으면 아무짝에도 쓸모가 없었어. 그러니 그 멋진 방법이면 모든 게 두루 수월해질 거였지. 그래서 그렇게 한 거야.\""]], [["\"When, where, how?\"", "\"언제, 어디서, 어떻게?\""], ["asked Jo, in a fever of feminine interest and curiosity, for she could not realize it a particle.", "조가 여자다운 관심과 호기심에 달떠 물었다. 도무지 한 톨도 실감이 나지 않았던 것이다."]], [["\"Six weeks ago, at the American consul's, in Paris; a very quiet wedding, of course, for even in our happiness we didn't forget dear little Beth.\"", "\"여섯 주 전에, 파리의 미국 영사관에서. 물론 아주 조용한 결혼식이었어. 그렇게 행복한 와중에도 사랑스러운 베스를 잊지는 않았으니까.\""]], [["Jo put her hand in his as he said that, and Laurie gently smoothed the little red pillow, which he remembered well.", "조가 그 말에 그의 손에 제 손을 포갰고, 로리는 자기가 잘 기억하는 그 작고 붉은 베개를 가만히 쓸어 주었다."]], [["\"Why didn't you let us know afterward?\"", "\"그런데 왜 나중에라도 우리한테 안 알린 거야?\""], ["asked Jo, in a quieter tone, when they had sat quite still a minute.", "두 사람이 잠시 가만히 앉아 있다가, 조가 한결 차분해진 목소리로 물었다."]], [["\"We wanted to surprise you; we thought we were coming directly home, at first; but the dear old gentleman, as soon as we were married, found he couldn't be ready under a month, at least, and sent us off to spend our honeymoon wherever we liked.", "\"누나를 놀래 주고 싶었거든. 처음엔 곧장 집으로 올 줄 알았어."], ["Amy had once called Valrosa a regular honeymoon home, so we went there, and were as happy as people are but once in their lives.", "그런데 그 다정하신 할아버지가 결혼하시자마자 적어도 한 달은 채비할 시간이 필요하다는 걸 아시고는, 우리더러 어디든 좋은 데서 신혼여행을 보내고 오라며 떠나보내셨지 뭐야. 에이미가 언젠가 발로사를 두고 그야말로 신혼여행을 보낼 만한 곳이라고 했었거든."], ["My faith!", "그래서 거기로 갔지. 사람이 평생에 단 한 번 누릴 만큼 행복했어."], ["wasn't it love among the roses!\"", "맙소사! 장미꽃 속의 사랑이라니, 그야말로!\""]], [["Laurie seemed to forget Jo for a minute, and Jo was glad of it; for the fact that he told her these things so freely and naturally assured her that he had quite forgiven and forgotten.", "로리는 잠시 조의 존재를 잊은 듯했고, 조는 그게 반가웠다. 그가 이런 이야기를 그토록 거리낌 없이 자연스럽게 한다는 것은, 그가 완전히 용서하고 잊었다는 증거였으니까."], ["She tried to draw away her hand; but, as if he guessed the thought that prompted the half-involuntary impulse, Laurie held it fast, and said, with a manly gravity she had never seen in him before,--", "그녀는 손을 빼려 했지만, 반쯤 무심결에 그런 충동이 인 까닭을 알아채기라도 한 듯 로리가 손을 꼭 붙들었다. 그러고는 일찍이 그에게서 본 적 없는 사내다운 진중함으로 말했다."]], [["\"Jo, dear, I want to say one thing, and then we'll put it by forever. As I told you in my letter, when I wrote that Amy had been so kind to me, I never shall stop loving you; but the love is altered, and I have learned to see that it is better as it is. Amy and you change places in my heart, that's all. I think it was meant to be so, and would have come about naturally, if I had waited, as you tried to make me; but I never could be patient, and so I got a heartache. I was a boy then, headstrong and violent; and it took a hard lesson to show me my mistake. For it _was_ one, Jo, as you said, and I found it out, after making a fool of myself. Upon my word, I was so tumbled up in my mind, at one time, that I didn't know which I loved best, you or Amy, and tried to love both alike; but I couldn't, and when I saw her in Switzerland, everything seemed to clear up all at once. You both got into your right places, and I felt sure that it was well off with the old love before it was on with the new; that I could honestly share my heart between sister Jo and wife Amy, and love them both dearly. Will you believe it, and go back to the happy old times when we first knew one another?\"", "\"조 누나, 한 가지만 말하고, 그다음엔 영영 덮어 두자. 편지에 썼던 것처럼, 에이미가 내게 그토록 다정하게 대해 줬다고 쓰면서 말했듯이, 난 결코 누나를 사랑하는 걸 멈추지 않을 거야. 하지만 그 사랑은 달라졌고, 지금 이대로가 더 낫다는 걸 알게 됐어. 그저 내 마음속에서 에이미와 누나가 자리를 바꾼 것뿐이야. 난 그게 본래 그렇게 될 일이었다고 생각해. 누나가 그러라고 했던 것처럼 내가 기다렸다면 자연스레 그렇게 됐을 거야. 그런데 난 도무지 참을성이 없어서 가슴앓이를 했던 거지. 그땐 난 소년이었어. 고집 세고 격하기만 한. 그래서 내 잘못을 깨닫는 데 호된 가르침이 필요했지. 그건 잘못이었으니까, 조 누나. 누나 말대로. 그리고 난 한참 바보짓을 하고 나서야 그걸 알았어. 정말이지 한때는 마음이 어찌나 뒤죽박죽이던지, 누나가 좋은지 에이미가 좋은지도 모르겠어서 둘을 똑같이 사랑하려고 해 봤어. 그런데 그게 안 되더라고. 그러다 스위스에서 그 애를 본 순간, 모든 게 단번에 또렷해졌어. 누나도 그 애도 제자리를 찾았고, 새 사랑을 시작하기 전에 옛 사랑을 잘 매듭지었다는 걸 확실히 느꼈어. 난 누나 조와 아내 에이미에게 내 마음을 떳떳하게 나누어, 둘 다 깊이 사랑할 수 있어. 누나도 그걸 믿고, 우리가 처음 알게 됐던 그 행복하던 옛 시절로 돌아가 주겠어?\""]], [["\"I'll believe it, with all my heart; but, Teddy, we never can be boy and girl again: the happy old times can't come back, and we mustn't expect it. We are man and woman now, with sober work to do, for playtime is over, and we must give up frolicking. I'm sure you feel this; I see the change in you, and you'll find it in me. I shall miss my boy, but I shall love the man as much, and admire him more, because he means to be what I hoped he would. We can't be little playmates any longer, but we will be brother and sister, to love and help one another all our lives, won't we, Laurie?\"", "\"진심으로 믿을게. 하지만 테디, 우린 두 번 다시 소년 소녀로 돌아갈 순 없어. 그 행복하던 옛 시절은 돌아오지 않고, 우리도 그걸 바라선 안 돼. 이제 우린 어른이 됐고, 진중한 할 일이 있잖아. 놀이 시간은 끝났으니, 까불며 노는 건 그만둬야지. 너도 분명 느낄 거야. 난 네게서 그 변화를 봐. 너도 내게서 보게 될 거고. 내 녀석이 그리울 테지만, 어른이 된 너도 그만큼 사랑할 거고, 더 우러러볼 거야. 네가 내가 바라던 사람이 되려고 하니까. 우린 더 이상 어린 놀이 동무가 될 순 없지만, 오누이가 되어 평생토록 서로를 사랑하고 도울 거야. 그렇지, 로리?\""]], [["He did not say a word, but took the hand she offered him, and laid his face down on it for a minute, feeling that out of the grave of a boyish passion, there had risen a beautiful, strong friendship to bless them both.", "그는 한마디도 하지 않고, 그녀가 내미는 손을 잡아 잠시 그 위에 얼굴을 묻었다. 소년다운 격정의 무덤에서 두 사람 모두를 축복할 아름답고 굳건한 우정이 피어났음을 느끼면서."], ["Presently Jo said cheerfully, for she didn't want the coming home to be a sad one,--", "이윽고 조가 명랑하게 말했다. 돌아온 이 만남을 슬프게 만들고 싶지 않았던 것이다."]], [["\"I can't make it true that you children are really married, and going to set up housekeeping.", "\"너희가 정말 결혼해서 살림을 차린다는 게 도무지 실감이 안 나."], ["Why, it seems only yesterday that I was buttoning Amy's pinafore, and pulling your hair when you teased.", "어제만 해도 에이미 앞치마를 채워 주고, 네가 놀리면 머리채를 잡아당기곤 했던 것 같은데."], ["Mercy me, how time does fly!\"", "어머나, 세월 참 빠르기도 하지!\""]], [["\"As one of the children is older than yourself, you needn't talk so like a grandma.", "\"그 아이들 중 하나는 누나보다 나이가 많은데, 무슨 할머니처럼 말씀하시네."], ["I flatter myself I'm a 'gentleman growed,'", "난 페거티가 데이비드를 두고 한 말마따나 '다 자란 신사'라고 자부하는걸."], ["as Peggotty said of David; and when you see Amy, you'll find her rather a precocious infant,\"", "그리고 에이미를 보면, 그 애가 꽤나 조숙한 갓난쟁이라는 걸 알게 될 거야.\" 로리가 그녀의 어머니다운 태도를 재미있어하며 말했다."], ["said Laurie, looking amused at her maternal air.", "* 페거티가 데이비드를 두고 한 말 : 찰스 디킨스의 소설 「데이비드 코퍼필드」에서 유모 페거티가 한 표현(옮긴이)."]], [["\"You may be a little older in years, but I'm ever so much older in feeling, Teddy.", "\"나이야 네가 조금 많을지 몰라도, 마음만큼은 내가 훨씬 늙었어, 테디."], ["Women always are; and this last year has been such a hard one that I feel forty.\"", "여자들은 늘 그렇거든. 게다가 지난 한 해가 어찌나 고됐던지, 난 마흔은 된 기분이야.\""]], [["\"Poor Jo!", "\"가엾은 조 누나!"], ["we left you to bear it alone, while we went pleasuring.", "우리가 놀러 다니는 동안 누나를 혼자 두고 그걸 다 견디게 했구나. 정말 나이가 들었네."], ["You _are_ older; here's a line, and there's another; unless you smile, your eyes look sad, and when I touched the cushion, just now, I found a tear on it.", "여기 주름 한 줄, 저기 또 한 줄. 누나는 웃지 않으면 눈이 슬퍼 보여."], ["You've had a great deal to bear, and had to bear it all alone.", "방금 쿠션을 만져 보니 눈물이 떨어져 있더라. 누나는 견뎌야 할 게 너무 많았는데, 그걸 다 혼자 견뎌야 했구나."], ["What a selfish beast I've been!\"", "난 얼마나 이기적인 짐승이었던 거야!\""], ["and Laurie pulled his own hair, with a remorseful look.", "로리는 뉘우치는 표정으로 제 머리카락을 쥐어뜯었다."]], [["But Jo only turned over the traitorous pillow, and answered, in a tone which she tried to make quite cheerful,--", "그러나 조는 그저 그 속내를 들켜 버린 베개를 뒤집어 놓고는, 애써 명랑하게 들리도록 한 목소리로 대답했다."]], [["\"No, I had father and mother to help me, the dear babies to comfort me, and the thought that you and Amy were safe and happy, to make the troubles here easier to bear.", "\"아니야. 난 도와주는 아버지와 어머니가 계셨고, 위로가 돼 주는 사랑스러운 아기들이 있었어."], ["I _am_ lonely, sometimes, but I dare say it's good for me, and--\"", "게다가 너랑 에이미가 무사히 행복하게 지낸다는 생각이 여기서의 시름을 더 견디기 쉽게 해 줬는걸. 가끔 외롭긴 하지만, 그것도 나한테 좋은 거겠지, 그리고—\""]], [["\"You never shall be again,\"", "\"이제 두 번 다시 외롭게 두지 않을게.\""], ["broke in Laurie, putting his arm about her, as if to fence out every human ill.", "로리가 마치 온갖 불행을 막아 주려는 듯 그녀를 팔로 감싸며 말을 가로챘다."], ["\"Amy and I can't get on without you, so you must come and teach 'the children'", "\"에이미랑 나는 누나 없인 못 살아."], ["to keep house, and go halves in everything, just as we used to do, and let us pet you, and all be blissfully happy and friendly together.\"", "그러니 누나가 와서 '그 아이들'한테 살림하는 법을 가르쳐 주고, 옛날처럼 뭐든 반씩 나누고, 우리한테 응석도 부려 줘. 다 같이 더없이 행복하고 다정하게 지내자.\""]], [["\"If I shouldn't be in the way, it would be very pleasant.", "\"내가 방해만 안 된다면, 그것도 참 즐겁겠다."], ["I begin to feel quite young already; for, somehow, all my troubles seemed to fly away when you came.", "벌써 한결 젊어지는 기분인걸. 어쩐 일인지 네가 오니까 내 시름이 죄다 날아가 버린 것 같아."], ["You always were a comfort, Teddy;\"", "넌 늘 위안이 됐어, 테디.\""], ["and Jo leaned her head on his shoulder, just as she did years ago, when Beth lay ill, and Laurie told her to hold on to him.", "그러고는 조는 그의 어깨에 머리를 기댔다. 몇 해 전 베스가 앓아누웠을 때 로리가 자기한테 기대라고 했던 그때처럼."]], [["He looked down at her, wondering if she remembered the time, but Jo was smiling to herself, as if, in truth, her troubles _had_ all vanished at his coming.", "그는 그녀가 그때를 기억하는지 궁금해하며 내려다보았지만, 조는 혼자 미소 짓고 있었다. 정말로 그가 와 준 것만으로 시름이 죄다 사라져 버리기라도 한 듯이."]], [["\"You are the same Jo still, dropping tears about one minute, and laughing the next.", "\"누나는 여전히 그대로네. 한순간 눈물을 뚝뚝 흘리다가 다음 순간 깔깔 웃으니."], ["You look a little wicked now; what is it, grandma?\"", "지금은 좀 짓궂은 표정인데, 무슨 일이야, 할머니?\""]], [["\"I was wondering how you and Amy get on together.\"", "\"너랑 에이미가 어떻게 지낼지 궁금해서.\""]], [["\"Like angels!\"", "\"천사들처럼!\""]], [["\"Yes, of course, at first; but which rules?\"", "\"그래, 그야 물론, 처음엔 그렇겠지. 그런데 누가 휘어잡는데?\""]], [["\"I don't mind telling you that she does, now; at least I let her think so,--it pleases her, you know. By and by we shall take turns, for marriage, they say, halves one's rights and doubles one's duties.\"", "\"지금은 그 애가 휘어잡는다고 솔직히 말해 줄게. 적어도 그 애가 그렇게 여기게 해 준다고나 할까. 그게 그 애를 기쁘게 하거든. 머지않아 번갈아 가게 될 거야. 결혼이란 게 권리는 반으로 줄이고 의무는 곱으로 늘린다잖아.\""]], [["\"You'll go on as you begin, and Amy will rule you all the days of your life.\"", "\"넌 시작한 대로 쭉 갈 거고, 에이미는 평생토록 널 휘어잡을걸.\""]], [["\"Well, she does it so imperceptibly that I don't think I shall mind much.", "\"뭐, 그 애가 어찌나 티 안 나게 하는지 난 별로 개의치 않을 것 같아. 그 앤 잘 다스릴 줄 아는 부류의 여자거든."], ["She is the sort of woman who knows how to rule well; in fact, I rather like it, for she winds one round her finger as softly and prettily as a skein of silk, and makes you feel as if she was doing you a favor all the while.\"", "솔직히 난 오히려 그게 좋아. 명주실 한 타래처럼 부드럽고 어여쁘게 사람을 제 손가락에 감아쥐면서도, 내내 마치 자기가 내게 호의를 베푸는 것처럼 느끼게 만드니까.\""]], [["\"That ever I should live to see you a henpecked husband and enjoying it!\"", "\"내가 살다 살다 네가 공처가가 돼서, 그걸 즐기는 꼴까지 보게 될 줄이야!\""], ["cried Jo, with uplifted hands.", "조가 두 손을 번쩍 치켜들며 외쳤다."]], [["It was good to see Laurie square his shoulders, and smile with masculine scorn at that insinuation, as he replied, with his \"high and mighty\"", "로리가 어깨를 펴고, 그런 빈정거림에 사내다운 경멸의 미소를 지어 보이는 것은 보기에 좋았다. 그는 \"도도하고 위풍당당한\""], ["air,--", "태도로 대꾸했다."]], [["\"Amy is too well-bred for that, and I am not the sort of man to submit to it.", "\"에이미는 그러기엔 너무 교양 있는 여자고, 나도 그런 데 굴복할 사내는 아니야."], ["My wife and I respect ourselves and one another too much ever to tyrannize or quarrel.\"", "내 아내와 나는 우리 자신과 서로를 너무 존중해서, 군림하거나 다투는 일은 없을 거야.\""]], [["Jo liked that, and thought the new dignity very becoming, but the boy seemed changing very fast into the man, and regret mingled with her pleasure.", "조는 그 말이 마음에 들었고, 그 새로운 위엄이 무척 어울린다고 생각했다. 그러나 소년은 어른으로 빠르게 변해 가는 듯했고, 그 기쁨에 아쉬움이 뒤섞였다."]], [["\"I am sure of that; Amy and you never did quarrel as we used to.", "\"분명 그럴 거야. 에이미랑 너는 우리처럼 다툰 적이 없었으니까."], ["She is the sun and I the wind, in the fable, and the sun managed the man best, you remember.\"", "우화에서처럼 그 애는 해이고 난 바람이지. 너도 기억하지, 사람을 다룬 건 해가 더 솜씨 있었잖아.\""]], [["\"She can blow him up as well as shine on him,\"", "\"그 애는 사람한테 햇볕만 쬐어 주는 게 아니라 한바탕 호되게 몰아붙일 줄도 알아.\""], ["laughed Laurie.", "로리가 웃었다."], ["\"Such a lecture as I got at Nice!", "\"니스에서 내가 받은 그 설교라니! 정말이지 누나의 어떤 잔소리보다 한참 더 매서웠어."], ["I give you my word it was a deal worse than any of your scoldings,--a regular rouser.", "그야말로 사람을 깨우는 호통이었지. 언젠가 다 얘기해 줄게."], ["I'll tell you all about it sometime,--_she_ never will, because, after telling me that she despised and was ashamed of me, she lost her heart to the despicable party and married the good-for-nothing.\"", "그 앤 절대 안 할 테니까. 날 경멸하고 부끄럽다고 해 놓고선, 정작 그 경멸하던 작자한테 마음을 빼앗겨 그 변변찮은 녀석이랑 결혼해 버렸으니 말이야.\""]], [["\"What baseness!", "\"이런 못된 것!"], ["Well, if she abuses you, come to me, and I'll defend you.\"", "흠, 그 애가 널 함부로 대하거든 나한테 와. 내가 편들어 줄 테니까.\""]], [["\"I look as if I needed it, don't I?\"", "\"내가 그런 게 필요해 보여, 안 그래?\""], ["said Laurie, getting up and striking an attitude which suddenly changed from the imposing to the rapturous, as Amy's voice was heard calling,--", "로리가 일어서며 한 자세를 취했는데, 에이미의 목소리가 들려오자 그 자세는 위풍당당하던 데서 별안간 황홀한 것으로 바뀌었다."]], [["\"Where is she?", "\"어디 있어요?"], ["Where's my dear old Jo?\"", "나의 사랑하는 조 언니 어디 있어요?\""]], [["In trooped the whole family, and every one was hugged and kissed all over again, and, after several vain attempts, the three wanderers were set down to be looked at and exulted over.", "온 식구가 우르르 몰려 들어왔고, 모두가 다시 한번 끌어안고 입을 맞추었다. 그리고 몇 번의 헛수고 끝에야 세 떠돌이를 자리에 앉혀 놓고 바라보며 한껏 기뻐할 수 있었다."], ["Mr. Laurence, hale and hearty as ever, was quite as much improved as the others by his foreign tour, for the crustiness seemed to be nearly gone, and the old-fashioned courtliness had received a polish which made it kindlier than ever.", "여느 때처럼 정정하고 기운찬 로런스 씨는 외국 여행으로 다른 이들 못지않게 좋아져 있었다."], ["It was good to see him beam at \"my children,\"", "무뚝뚝함은 거의 가시고, 예스러운 품위에는 윤기가 더해져 어느 때보다 따뜻해 보였다. 그가 \"내 아이들\"이라 부르는 젊은 부부를 향해 환히 웃는 모습은 보기에 좋았다."], ["as he called the young pair; it was better still to see Amy pay him the daughterly duty and affection which completely won his old heart; and best of all, to watch Laurie revolve about the two, as if never tired of enjoying the pretty picture they made.", "에이미가 그 노인의 마음을 송두리째 사로잡는 딸다운 도리와 애정을 그에게 바치는 모습은 더더욱 보기 좋았다. 그리고 무엇보다 좋은 것은, 로리가 두 사람이 빚어내는 어여쁜 그림을 아무리 보아도 질리지 않는다는 듯 그 둘 주위를 맴도는 모습이었다."]], [["The minute she put her eyes upon Amy, Meg became conscious that her own dress hadn't a Parisian air, that young Mrs. Moffat would be entirely eclipsed by young Mrs.", "에이미를 보는 순간, 메그는 제 옷차림에 파리풍이라곤 없다는 것을, 젊은 모팻 부인이 젊은 로런스 부인 앞에서는 완전히 빛을 잃으리라는 것을, 그리고 \"그 귀부인\"이 더없이 우아하고 기품 있는 여인이라는 것을 의식하게 되었다."], ["Laurence, and that \"her ladyship\"", "조는 그 한 쌍을 지켜보며 생각했다."], ["was altogether a most elegant and graceful woman.", "'둘이 정말 잘 어울리는구나!"], ["Jo thought, as she watched the pair, \"How well they look together!", "내가 옳았어."], ["I was right, and Laurie has found the beautiful, accomplished girl who will become his home better than clumsy old Jo, and be a pride, not a torment to him.\" Mrs.", "로리는 그 집을 투박한 옛 조보다 더 잘 가꿔 줄, 그래서 그에게 괴로움이 아니라 자랑이 될 아름답고 교양 있는 여자를 찾았어.'"], ["March and her husband smiled and nodded at each other with happy faces, for they saw that their youngest had done well, not only in worldly things, but the better wealth of love, confidence, and happiness.", "마치 부인과 그 남편은 행복한 얼굴로 서로에게 미소를 지으며 고개를 끄덕였다. 막내딸이 세속적인 면에서뿐 아니라, 사랑과 신뢰와 행복이라는 더 값진 재산에서도 잘해 냈음을 보았기 때문이다."]], [["For Amy's face was full of the soft brightness which betokens a peaceful heart, her voice had a new tenderness in it, and the cool, prim carriage was changed to a gentle dignity, both womanly and winning.", "에이미의 얼굴에는 평화로운 마음을 드러내는 부드러운 광채가 가득했고, 목소리에는 전에 없던 다정함이 깃들어 있었으며, 차갑고 새침하던 몸가짐은 여성스럽고도 사람을 끌어당기는 우아한 기품으로 바뀌어 있었다."], ["No little affectations marred it, and the cordial sweetness of her manner was more charming than the new beauty or the old grace, for it stamped her at once with the unmistakable sign of the true gentlewoman she had hoped to become.", "가식이라곤 조금도 그 모습을 해치지 않았고, 진심에서 우러난 다정한 태도는 새로 얻은 아름다움이나 예전의 우아함보다도 더 매력적이었다. 그것이야말로 에이미가 되고자 했던 진정한 숙녀임을 분명하게 새겨 주는 표시였기 때문이다."]], [["\"Love has done much for our little girl,\"", "\"사랑이 우리 어린 딸을 많이 변하게 했군요.\""], ["said her mother softly.", "어머니가 나직이 말했다."]], [["\"She has had a good example before her all her life, my dear,\"", "\"평생 좋은 본보기를 곁에 두고 자랐으니까요, 여보.\""], ["Mr. March whispered back, with a loving look at the worn face and gray head beside him.", "마치 씨가 옆에 앉은 늙고 지친 얼굴과 희끗한 머리를 애정 어린 눈길로 바라보며 속삭이듯 대답했다."]], [["Daisy found it impossible to keep her eyes off her \"pitty aunty,\" but attached herself like a lap-dog to the wonderful châtelaine full of delightful charms.", "데이지는 \"예쁜 이모\"에게서 도무지 눈을 뗄 수 없었지만, 무릎 강아지처럼 매달린 곳은 사랑스러운 장신구들이 잔뜩 달린 그 멋진 허리 장식이었다."], ["Demi paused to consider the new relationship before he compromised himself by the rash acceptance of a bribe, which took the tempting form of a family of wooden bears from Berne.", "데미는 베른에서 온 나무 곰 가족 인형이라는 솔깃한 형태의 뇌물을 덥석 받아들여 자신을 굽히기 전에, 잠시 멈추어 이 새로운 관계를 곰곰이 따져 보았다."], ["A flank movement produced an unconditional surrender, however, for Laurie knew where to have him.", "그러나 측면 공격이 무조건 항복을 이끌어 냈으니, 로리는 데미를 어떻게 다루어야 하는지 잘 알고 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"Young man, when I first had the honor of making your acquaintance you hit me in the face: now I demand the satisfaction of a gentleman;\"", "\"이봐, 젊은이, 내가 처음 자네와 인사를 나누는 영광을 누렸을 때 자네는 내 얼굴을 한 대 쳤지. 이제 신사로서 그 결투의 사죄를 요구하네.\""], ["and with that the tall uncle proceeded to toss and tousle the small nephew in a way that damaged his philosophical dignity as much as it delighted his boyish soul.", "그렇게 말하더니 키 큰 삼촌은 작은 조카를 번쩍 들어 올려 헝클어뜨리기 시작했고, 그 바람에 데미의 철학자 같은 위엄은 무너졌지만 사내아이다운 마음은 그만큼 신이 났다."]], [["\"Blest if she ain't in silk from head to foot? Ain't it a relishin'", "\"머리부터 발끝까지 비단을 두르고 있으니 이거 원, 정말 대단하지 않아?"], ["sight to see her settin' there as fine as a fiddle, and hear folks calling little Amy, Mis.", "저렇게 앉아 있는 모습이 바이올린처럼 곱고, 사람들이 우리 꼬맹이 에이미를 보고 로런스 부인이라고 부르는 걸 듣자니 어찌나 흐뭇한지!\""], ["Laurence?\" muttered old Hannah, who could not resist frequent \"peeks\"", "늙은 해나가 중얼거렸다."], ["through the slide as she set the table in a most decidedly promiscuous manner.", "그녀는 도무지 종잡을 수 없는 방식으로 식탁을 차리면서도 문틈으로 자꾸만 \"엿보고\" 싶은 마음을 참을 수가 없었다."]], [["Mercy on us, how they did talk!", "세상에, 어찌나 이야기들을 해 대던지!"], ["first one, then the other, then all burst out together, trying to tell the history of three years in half an hour.", "처음엔 한 사람이, 그다음엔 또 한 사람이, 그러다 결국 모두가 한꺼번에 터져 나오며 삼 년 세월을 반 시간 안에 풀어놓으려 했다."], ["It was fortunate that tea was at hand, to produce a lull and provide refreshment, for they would have been hoarse and faint if they had gone on much longer.", "마침 차 마실 시간이 다가온 게 다행이었으니, 덕분에 잠시 숨을 돌리고 기운을 차릴 수 있었다. 그렇게라도 멈추지 않았다면 다들 목이 쉬고 기진맥진했을 터였다."], ["Such a happy procession as filed away into the little dining-room! Mr.", "작은 식당으로 줄지어 들어가는 그 행렬은 어찌나 행복해 보이던지!"], ["March proudly escorted \"Mrs. Laurence;\"", "마치 씨는 자랑스럽게 \"로런스 부인\"을 모셨고, 마치 부인은 그 못지않게 자랑스럽게 \"내 아들\"의 팔에 기댔다."], ["Mrs. March as proudly leaned on the arm of \"my son;\"", "노신사는 조의 팔을 잡으며 \"이제 자네가 내 딸이 되어 주어야 하네\""], ["the old gentleman took Jo, with a whispered \"You must be my girl now,\"", "하고 속삭이고는, 난롯가의 텅 빈 구석을 흘끗 바라보았다."], ["and a glance at the empty corner by the fire, that made Jo whisper back, with trembling lips, \"I'll try to fill her place, sir.\"", "그 눈길에 조도 입술을 떨며 속삭이듯 대답했다. \"그 자리를 채우도록 애써 볼게요.\""]], [["The twins pranced behind, feeling that the millennium was at hand, for every one was so busy with the new-comers that they were left to revel at their own sweet will, and you may be sure they made the most of the opportunity.", "쌍둥이는 천년왕국이 코앞에 온 듯한 기분으로 뒤에서 깡충거렸다. 다들 새로 온 사람들에게 정신이 팔려 둘을 마음껏 뛰놀게 내버려 두었으니, 두 아이가 그 기회를 놓치지 않고 한껏 누렸으리라는 건 두말할 나위가 없다."], ["Didn't they steal sips of tea, stuff gingerbread _ab libitum_, get a hot biscuit apiece, and, as a crowning trespass, didn't they each whisk a captivating little tart into their tiny pockets, there to stick and crumble treacherously, teaching them that both human nature and pastry are frail?", "차를 몰래 홀짝거리고, 진저브레드를 마음껏 입에 쑤셔 넣고, 따끈한 비스킷을 하나씩 차지하지 않았겠는가. 게다가 가장 큰 죄로, 둘 다 먹음직스러운 작은 타르트를 하나씩 조그만 주머니에 슬쩍 집어넣어 그 안에서 음흉하게 들러붙고 부스러지게 만들지 않았겠는가."], ["Burdened with the guilty consciousness of the sequestered tarts, and fearing that Dodo's sharp eyes would pierce the thin disguise of cambric and merino which hid their booty, the little sinners attached themselves to \"Dranpa,\"", "인간 본성도 페이스트리도 다 약하기 짝이 없다는 사실을 그렇게 배운 셈이다. 슬쩍 빼돌린 타르트 때문에 죄책감을 짊어진 데다, 도도의 날카로운 눈이 노획물을 가린 얇은 옥양목과 메리노 천을 꿰뚫어 볼까 봐 겁이 난 두 어린 죄인은, 안경을 쓰지 않은 \"할아버지\"에게 찰싹 달라붙었다."], ["who hadn't his spectacles on. Amy, who was handed about like refreshments, returned to the parlor on Father Laurence's arm; the others paired off as before, and this arrangement left Jo companionless.", "다과처럼 이 사람 저 사람에게 떠밀려 다니던 에이미는 로런스 할아버지의 팔에 기대어 응접실로 돌아갔다."], ["She did not mind it at the minute, for she lingered to answer Hannah's eager inquiry,--", "나머지는 아까처럼 짝을 지었고, 그렇게 짝짓다 보니 조만 짝이 없게 되었다. 조는 그 순간엔 별로 개의치 않았으니, 해나의 다급한 물음에 대답하느라 뒤에 처져 있었기 때문이다."]], [["\"Will Miss Amy ride in her coop (_coupé_), and use all them lovely silver dishes that's stored away over yander?\"", "\"에이미 아가씨는 그 마차(쿠페)를 타고 다니고, 저쪽에 잔뜩 쟁여 둔 그 예쁜 은그릇들을 다 쓰게 되나요?\""]], [["\"Shouldn't wonder if she drove six white horses, ate off gold plate, and wore diamonds and point-lace every day.", "\"여섯 마리 백마가 끄는 마차를 몰고, 금 접시에 음식을 담아 먹고, 다이아몬드에 레이스 장식을 날마다 두르고 다닌대도 난 놀라지 않을 거예요."], ["Teddy thinks nothing too good for her,\"", "테디는 에이미한테는 아무리 좋은 것도 부족하다고 생각하니까요.\""], ["returned Jo with infinite satisfaction.", "조가 더없이 흡족한 얼굴로 대답했다."]], [["\"No more there is!", "\"암, 그렇고말고요!"], ["Will you have hash or fish-balls for breakfast?\"", "아침으로는 해시를 드릴까요, 생선완자를 드릴까요?\""], ["asked Hannah, who wisely mingled poetry and prose.", "시(詩)와 산문을 슬기롭게 섞어 쓰는 해나가 물었다."]], [["\"I don't care;\" and Jo shut the door, feeling that food was an uncongenial topic just then. She stood a minute looking at the party vanishing above, and, as Demi's short plaid legs toiled up the last stair, a sudden sense of loneliness came over her so strongly that she looked about her with dim eyes, as if to find something to lean upon, for even Teddy had deserted her. If she had known what birthday gift was coming every minute nearer and nearer, she would not have said to herself, \"I'll weep a little weep when I go to bed; it won't do to be dismal now.\" Then she drew her hand over her eyes,--for one of her boyish habits was never to know where her handkerchief was,--and had just managed to call up a smile when there came a knock at the porch-door.", "\"아무거나 상관없어요.\" 조는 그렇게 말하고 문을 닫았다. 지금 음식 얘기는 영 마음에 맞지 않는 화제라는 생각이 들었기 때문이다. 조는 잠시 서서 위층으로 사라지는 식구들을 바라보았다. 데미의 짧은 격자무늬 다리가 마지막 계단을 힘겹게 오르는 모습을 보고 있자니, 별안간 외로움이 어찌나 사무치게 밀려오던지, 조는 무언가 기댈 것을 찾으려는 듯 흐릿해진 눈으로 주위를 둘러보았다. 테디마저 자기를 버리고 가 버렸으니 말이다. 만약 어떤 생일 선물이 매 순간 점점 더 가까이 다가오고 있는지 알았더라면, 조는 속으로 \"잠자리에 들면 조금 울어야지. 지금 우울해 봤자 소용없으니까\" 하고 중얼거리지는 않았을 것이다. 그러고는 손등으로 눈을 훔쳤다. 사내아이 같은 버릇 중 하나로, 손수건이 어디 있는지 도무지 모르는 게 그녀였으니까. 그렇게 간신히 미소를 지어 올렸을 때, 현관문을 두드리는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["She opened it with hospitable haste, and started as if another ghost had come to surprise her; for there stood a tall, bearded gentleman, beaming on her from the darkness like a midnight sun.", "조는 손님을 맞이하듯 서둘러 문을 열었다가, 또 다른 유령이라도 나타나 자기를 놀래기라도 한 양 흠칫 놀랐다. 거기 키 크고 수염을 기른 신사가 서서, 어둠 속에서 한밤중의 태양처럼 그녀를 향해 환하게 미소 짓고 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"O Mr. Bhaer, I _am_ so glad to see you!\"", "\"오, 베어 선생님, 정말 정말 반가워요!\""], ["cried Jo, with a clutch, as if she feared the night would swallow him up before she could get him in.", "조는 그를 와락 붙잡으며 소리쳤다. 안으로 들이기도 전에 밤이 그를 삼켜 버릴까 두려워하기라도 하듯이."]], [["\"And I to see Miss Marsch,--but no, you haf a party--\"", "\"저도 마치 양을 뵈어서—아니, 손님이 와 계시는군요.\""], ["and the Professor paused as the sound of voices and the tap of dancing feet came down to them.", "안에서 두런거리는 목소리와 춤추는 발소리가 들려오자 베어 교수는 말을 멈추었다."]], [["\"No, we haven't, only the family.", "\"아니에요, 손님은 없어요."], ["My sister and friends have just come home, and we are all very happy.", "식구들뿐이에요. 언니랑 친구들이 막 돌아와서 다들 무척 기뻐하고 있어요."], ["Come in, and make one of us.\"", "들어와서 함께해요.\""]], [["Though a very social man, I think Mr.", "베어 씨는 무척 사교적인 사람이긴 했지만, 내 생각에는 점잖게 물러갔다가 다른 날 다시 찾아오려 했을 것이다."], ["Bhaer would have gone decorously away, and come again another day; but how could he, when Jo shut the door behind him, and bereft him of his hat?", "그러나 조가 그의 등 뒤로 문을 닫고 모자까지 빼앗아 버렸으니 어쩔 도리가 없지 않았겠는가? 어쩌면 조의 얼굴 때문이었는지도 모른다."], ["Perhaps her face had something to do with it, for she forgot to hide her joy at seeing him, and showed it with a frankness that proved irresistible to the solitary man, whose welcome far exceeded his boldest hopes.", "그를 보고 반가운 마음을 감추는 걸 깜빡 잊고, 거부할 수 없을 만큼 솔직하게 그 기쁨을 드러냈으니까. 그 환대는 외로운 그가 감히 품었던 가장 대담한 기대마저 훌쩍 뛰어넘는 것이었다."]], [["\"If I shall not be Monsieur de Trop, I will so gladly see them all.", "\"제가 거추장스러운 사람이 아니라면, 다들 기꺼이 뵙고 싶군요."], ["You haf been ill, my friend?\"", "그동안 편찮으셨소, 나의 친구?\""]], [["He put the question abruptly, for, as Jo hung up his coat, the light fell on her face, and he saw a change in it.", "그는 불쑥 그렇게 물었다. 조가 그의 외투를 걸어 두는 동안 불빛이 그녀의 얼굴에 비쳤고, 그는 거기서 어떤 변화를 보았던 것이다."]], [["\"Not ill, but tired and sorrowful.", "\"아픈 건 아니고요, 지치고 슬펐어요."], ["We have had trouble since I saw you last.\"", "지난번에 뵌 뒤로 힘든 일이 있었거든요.\""]], [["\"Ah, yes, I know.", "\"아, 그렇군요, 알고 있소."], ["My heart was sore for you when I heard that;\"", "그 소식을 들었을 때 제 마음도 아렸지요.\""], ["and he shook hands again, with such a sympathetic face that Jo felt as if no comfort could equal the look of the kind eyes, the grasp of the big, warm hand.", "그는 다시 손을 맞잡았는데, 그 얼굴이 어찌나 다정하던지, 조는 그 따뜻한 눈빛과 크고 따스한 손길만 한 위로가 세상에 또 있을까 싶었다."]], [["\"Father, mother, this is my friend, Professor Bhaer,\"", "\"아버지, 어머니, 이분이 제 친구 베어 교수님이세요.\""], ["she said, with a face and tone of such irrepressible pride and pleasure that she might as well have blown a trumpet and opened the door with a flourish.", "조는 도무지 억누를 수 없는 자부심과 기쁨이 어린 얼굴과 목소리로 말했다. 차라리 나팔을 불고 문을 활짝 열어젖히며 요란하게 소개했다 해도 그보다 더하지는 않았을 것이다."]], [["If the stranger had had any doubts about his reception, they were set at rest in a minute by the cordial welcome he received.", "이방인이 자기를 어떻게 맞이할지 조금이라도 의심을 품었다면, 그 따뜻한 환대에 단숨에 마음이 놓였을 것이다."], ["Every one greeted him kindly, for Jo's sake at first, but very soon they liked him for his own.", "다들 처음에는 조를 봐서 그를 다정히 맞았지만, 이내 그 자체로 그를 좋아하게 되었다."], ["They could not help it, for he carried the talisman that opens all hearts, and these simple people warmed to him at once, feeling even the more friendly because he was poor; for poverty enriches those who live above it, and is a sure passport to truly hospitable spirits.", "그럴 수밖에 없었으니, 그는 모든 마음의 문을 여는 부적을 지니고 있었던 것이다. 이 소박한 사람들은 단번에 그에게 마음이 따뜻해졌고, 그가 가난하다는 사실 때문에 오히려 더 친근하게 느껴졌다."], ["Mr. Bhaer sat looking about him with the air of a traveller who knocks at a strange door, and, when it opens, finds himself at home.", "가난은 그것을 넘어서 사는 이들을 풍요롭게 하고, 진정 후한 마음을 지닌 이들에게 가는 확실한 통행증이 되기 때문이다."], ["The children went to him like bees to a honey-pot; and, establishing themselves on each knee, proceeded to captivate him by rifling his pockets, pulling his beard, and investigating his watch, with juvenile audacity.", "베어 씨는 낯선 문을 두드렸다가 문이 열리자 제집에 온 듯한 기분이 든 나그네 같은 표정으로 주위를 둘러보며 앉아 있었다."], ["The women telegraphed their approval to one another, and Mr.", "아이들은 꿀단지에 모여드는 벌처럼 그에게로 달려갔다."], ["March, feeling that he had got a kindred spirit, opened his choicest stores for his guest's benefit, while silent John listened and enjoyed the talk, but said not a word, and Mr.", "양쪽 무릎에 자리를 잡고 앉더니, 어린아이다운 대담함으로 그의 주머니를 뒤지고, 수염을 잡아당기고, 회중시계를 살펴보며 그를 사로잡기 시작했다. 부인들은 흡족한 마음을 서로 눈짓으로 주고받았고, 마치 씨는 마음이 통하는 벗을 얻었다는 기분에 자신의 가장 귀한 이야기 곳간을 손님을 위해 열었다."], ["Laurence found it impossible to go to sleep.", "말없는 존은 그 대화를 듣고 즐기면서도 한마디도 하지 않았고, 로런스 씨는 도무지 잠들 수가 없었다."]], [["If Jo had not been otherwise engaged, Laurie's behavior would have amused her; for a faint twinge, not of jealousy, but something like suspicion, caused that gentleman to stand aloof at first, and observe the new-comer with brotherly circumspection.", "만약 조가 다른 일에 정신이 팔려 있지 않았더라면 로리의 태도가 그녀에게 꽤나 재미있었을 것이다. 질투까지는 아니어도 의심에 가까운 어떤 미묘한 거슬림 때문에, 이 신사는 처음엔 한발 물러나 형제다운 신중함으로 새로 온 사람을 살폈던 것이다."], ["But it did not last long.", "하지만 그건 오래가지 않았다."], ["He got interested in spite of himself, and, before he knew it, was drawn into the circle; for Mr.", "그는 자기도 모르게 흥미를 느꼈고, 어느새 그 무리 속으로 끌려들었다."], ["Bhaer talked well in this genial atmosphere, and did himself justice.", "베어 씨가 이 화기애애한 분위기 속에서 말솜씨를 잘 발휘하며 자기 진면목을 드러냈기 때문이다."], ["He seldom spoke to Laurie, but he looked at him often, and a shadow would pass across his face, as if regretting his own lost youth, as he watched the young man in his prime.", "그는 좀처럼 로리에게 직접 말을 걸지 않았지만, 자주 그를 바라보았고, 그럴 때마다 얼굴에 그늘이 스쳤다. 한창때의 젊은이를 지켜보면서, 마치 자기가 잃어버린 청춘을 아쉬워하는 듯이."], ["Then his eye would turn to Jo so wistfully that she would have surely answered the mute inquiry if she had seen it; but Jo had her own eyes to take care of, and, feeling that they could not be trusted, she prudently kept them on the little sock she was knitting, like a model maiden aunt.", "그러다 그의 눈길이 조에게로 향할 때면 어찌나 애틋하던지, 만약 조가 그 눈길을 보았더라면 그 말 없는 물음에 분명 대답했을 것이다. 하지만 조는 자기 눈을 단속하느라 바빴고, 그 눈을 믿을 수 없다고 느낀 터라, 본보기가 될 만한 노처녀 이모처럼 조심스레 시선을 자기가 뜨개질하던 작은 양말에 붙박아 두었다."]], [["A stealthy glance now and then refreshed her like sips of fresh water after a dusty walk, for the sidelong peeps showed her several propitious omens. Mr.", "이따금 슬쩍 던지는 눈길은 먼지투성이 길을 걷고 난 뒤 마시는 시원한 물 한 모금처럼 그녀를 새롭게 했다."], ["Bhaer's face had lost the absent-minded expression, and looked all alive with interest in the present moment, actually young and handsome, she thought, forgetting to compare him with Laurie, as she usually did strange men, to their great detriment.", "곁눈질로 살핀 그 모습에 여러 가지 좋은 조짐이 보였기 때문이다. 베어 씨의 얼굴에서는 멍한 표정이 사라지고, 지금 이 순간에 대한 관심으로 온통 생기가 돌았다."], ["Then he seemed quite inspired, though the burial customs of the ancients, to which the conversation had strayed, might not be considered an exhilarating topic.", "정말이지 젊고 잘생겨 보였다고 조는 생각했다. 평소 낯선 남자들을 로리와 견주어 한참 깎아내리던 버릇도 깜빡 잊은 채로."], ["Jo quite glowed with triumph when Teddy got quenched in an argument, and thought to herself, as she watched her father's absorbed face, \"How he would enjoy having such a man as my Professor to talk with every day!\"", "게다가 그는 꽤 영감에 차 보였는데, 대화가 옛사람들의 장례 풍습으로 흘러가 있었으니 그게 딱히 신나는 화제라고는 할 수 없는데도 그랬다. 테디가 논쟁에서 입을 다물게 되자 조는 승리의 기쁨에 환히 빛났고, 무언가에 몰두한 아버지의 얼굴을 바라보며 속으로 생각했다."], ["Lastly, Mr. Bhaer was dressed in a new suit of black, which made him look more like a gentleman than ever.", "\"우리 교수님 같은 분과 날마다 이야기를 나눌 수 있다면 아버지가 얼마나 좋아하실까!\""], ["His bushy hair had been cut and smoothly brushed, but didn't stay in order long, for, in exciting moments, he rumpled it up in the droll way he used to do; and Jo liked it rampantly erect better than flat, because she thought it gave his fine forehead a Jove-like aspect.", "마지막으로, 베어 씨는 새 검은색 정장을 차려입고 있어서 그 어느 때보다 신사다워 보였다. 텁수룩하던 머리는 잘라서 매끈하게 빗어 넘겼지만 오래 정돈된 채로 있지는 않았다."], ["Poor Jo, how she did glorify that plain man, as she sat knitting away so quietly, yet letting nothing escape her, not even the fact that Mr.", "흥분하는 순간이면 늘 하던 그 우스꽝스러운 버릇대로 머리카락을 헝클어뜨렸기 때문이다. 조는 그게 납작하게 붙어 있는 것보다 제멋대로 곤두선 모습이 더 좋았으니, 그래야 그의 멋진 이마가 주피터 같은 위풍을 띤다고 여겼기 때문이다."], ["Bhaer actually had gold sleeve-buttons in his immaculate wristbands!", "가엾은 조, 그 수수한 남자를 어찌나 떠받들던지. 그토록 조용히 앉아 뜨개질을 하면서도 무엇 하나 놓치지 않았으니, 베어 씨가 티 하나 없는 소맷부리에 금 단추를 달고 있다는 사실까지도 말이다!"]], [["\"Dear old fellow!", "\"다정한 양반 같으니!"], ["He couldn't have got himself up with more care if he'd been going a-wooing,\"", "청혼하러 가는 길이었대도 이보다 더 정성껏 차려입지는 못했을 거야.\""], ["said Jo to herself; and then a sudden thought, born of the words, made her blush so dreadfully that she had to drop her ball, and go down after it to hide her face.", "조는 속으로 그렇게 중얼거렸다. 그러다 그 말에서 비롯된 갑작스러운 생각에 얼굴이 어찌나 끔찍하게 달아오르던지, 실뭉치를 떨어뜨리고는 그걸 줍는다는 핑계로 몸을 숙여 얼굴을 감춰야 했다."]], [["The manœuvre did not succeed as well as she expected, however; for, though just in the act of setting fire to a funeral-pile, the Professor dropped his torch, metaphorically speaking, and made a dive after the little blue ball.", "하지만 그 작전은 그녀가 기대한 만큼 잘 먹히지 않았다. 비유하자면 막 화장용 장작더미에 불을 붙이려던 참이었던 교수가 횃불을 떨어뜨리고 그 작은 파란 실뭉치를 향해 쑥 몸을 숙였기 때문이다."], ["Of course they bumped their heads smartly together, saw stars, and both came up flushed and laughing, without the ball, to resume their seats, wishing they had not left them.", "당연히 둘은 머리를 세게 부딪쳤고, 눈앞에 별이 번쩍였으며, 둘 다 실뭉치는 못 집은 채 얼굴이 빨개져 웃으며 일어나서는 자리로 돌아갔다. 차라리 자리를 뜨지 말걸 하고 후회하면서."]], [["Nobody knew where the evening went to; for Hannah skilfully abstracted the babies at an early hour, nodding like two rosy poppies, and Mr.", "저녁이 어떻게 지나갔는지는 아무도 몰랐다. 해나가 발그레한 양귀비 두 송이처럼 꾸벅거리던 아기들을 이른 시각에 솜씨 좋게 빼돌렸고, 로런스 씨는 쉬러 집으로 돌아갔다."], ["Laurence went home to rest.", "나머지는 난롯가에 둘러앉아 시간 가는 줄도 모른 채 이야기를 나누었다."], ["The others sat round the fire, talking away, utterly regardless of the lapse of time, till Meg, whose maternal mind was impressed with a firm conviction that Daisy had tumbled out of bed, and Demi set his night-gown afire studying the structure of matches, made a move to go.", "그러다 어머니다운 마음에 데이지가 침대에서 굴러떨어졌고 데미가 성냥의 구조를 연구하다 잠옷에 불을 붙였으리라는 확고한 믿음에 사로잡힌 메그가 일어설 채비를 했다."]], [["\"We must have our sing, in the good old way, for we are all together again once more,\"", "\"옛날 그 좋던 방식대로 다 함께 노래를 불러야 해. 다시 한 번 모두 한자리에 모였으니까.\""], ["said Jo, feeling that a good shout would be a safe and pleasant vent for the jubilant emotions of her soul.", "조가 말했다. 한바탕 목청껏 노래하는 것이 가슴 가득 차오른 기쁨을 풀어놓을 안전하고 즐거운 출구라는 생각이 들었던 것이다."]], [["They were not _all_ there. But no one found the words thoughtless or untrue; for Beth still seemed among them, a peaceful presence, invisible, but dearer than ever, since death could not break the household league that love made indissoluble. The little chair stood in its old place; the tidy basket, with the bit of work she left unfinished when the needle grew \"so heavy,\" was still on its accustomed shelf; the beloved instrument, seldom touched now, had not been moved; and above it Beth's face, serene and smiling, as in the early days, looked down upon them, seeming to say, \"Be happy. I am here.\"", "모두가 그 자리에 있는 것은 아니었다. 하지만 누구도 그 말을 경솔하다거나 사실이 아니라고 여기지 않았다. 베스는 여전히 그들 가운데 있는 것 같았으니까. 평온한 존재로, 보이지는 않아도 그 어느 때보다 소중하게. 죽음도 사랑이 풀 수 없도록 단단히 맺어 놓은 그 가족의 결속을 끊지는 못했기 때문이다. 작은 의자는 제자리에 그대로 놓여 있었다. 바늘이 \"너무 무거워졌을\" 때 미처 끝내지 못하고 남긴 일감이 담긴 단정한 바구니도 늘 두던 선반 위에 그대로 있었다. 이제는 좀처럼 만지지 않는 그 사랑하던 악기도 옮기지 않은 채였고, 그 위에서는 옛날처럼 평온하게 미소 짓는 베스의 얼굴이 그들을 내려다보며 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"행복하세요. 저는 여기 있어요.\""]], [["\"Play something, Amy.", "\"뭐든 한번 쳐 봐, 에이미."], ["Let them hear how much you have improved,\"", "네가 얼마나 늘었는지 다들 들려주렴.\""], ["said Laurie, with pardonable pride in his promising pupil.", "로리가 장래가 촉망되는 제자를 향해 봐줄 만한 자부심을 담아 말했다."]], [["But Amy whispered, with full eyes, as she twirled the faded stool,--", "그러나 에이미는 색이 바랜 의자를 빙그르르 돌리며 눈에 눈물을 가득 머금고 속삭였다."]], [["\"Not to-night, dear.", "\"오늘 밤은 아니에요, 여보."], ["I can't show off to-night.\"", "오늘은 솜씨를 뽐낼 수가 없어요.\""]], [["But she did show something better than brilliancy or skill; for she sung Beth's songs with a tender music in her voice which the best master could not have taught, and touched the listeners'", "하지만 에이미는 화려함이나 기교보다 더 나은 무언가를 보여 주었다. 베스가 부르던 노래들을 부드러운 음색에 실어 불렀는데, 그건 제아무리 뛰어난 스승도 가르칠 수 없는 것이었고, 그 어떤 영감으로도 얻을 수 없었을 더 감미로운 힘으로 듣는 이들의 마음을 어루만졌다."], ["hearts with a sweeter power than any other inspiration could have given her. The room was very still, when the clear voice failed suddenly at the last line of Beth's favorite hymn.", "베스가 가장 좋아하던 찬송가의 마지막 구절에서 그 맑은 목소리가 별안간 끊겼을 때, 방 안은 아주 고요했다."], ["It was hard to say,--", "그 구절은 차마 부르기가 힘들었다."]], [["\"Earth hath no sorrow that heaven cannot heal;\"", "\"하늘이 고치지 못할 슬픔이란 이 땅에 없으리니.\""]], [["and Amy leaned against her husband, who stood behind her, feeling that her welcome home was not quite perfect without Beth's kiss.", "그러자 에이미는 뒤에 서 있던 남편에게 몸을 기댔다. 베스의 입맞춤 없는 귀향은 어쩐지 온전하지 못하다는 느낌이 들었던 것이다."]], [["\"Now, we must finish with Mignon's song; for Mr.", "\"자, 이제 미뇽의 노래로 마무리하자."], ["Bhaer sings that,\"", "그건 베어 선생님이 부르시니까.\""], ["said Jo, before the pause grew painful.", "침묵이 괴로워지기 전에 조가 말했다."], ["And Mr. Bhaer cleared his throat with a gratified \"Hem!\"", "그러자 베어 씨는 흡족한 듯 \"흠!\""], ["as he stepped into the corner where Jo stood, saying,--", "하고 헛기침을 하고는 조가 서 있는 구석으로 걸어가며 말했다."]], [["\"You will sing with me?", "\"저와 함께 부르시겠소?"], ["We go excellently well together.\"", "우리 둘이 아주 잘 어울리니까.\""]], [["A pleasing fiction, by the way; for Jo had no more idea of music than a grasshopper.", "그건 그런데 듣기 좋으라고 하는 말이었다. 조는 음악에 관해서라면 메뚜기만큼도 아는 게 없었으니까."], ["But she would have consented if he had proposed to sing a whole opera, and warbled away, blissfully regardless of time and tune.", "하지만 그가 오페라 한 편을 통째로 부르자고 했어도 그녀는 응했을 것이다. 그녀는 박자도 음정도 더없이 행복하게 무시한 채 흥얼거렸다."], ["It didn't much matter; for Mr.", "그래도 큰 문제는 없었다. 베어 씨가 진짜 독일 사람답게 진심을 담아 멋지게 불렀으니까."], ["Bhaer sang like a true German, heartily and well; and Jo soon subsided into a subdued hum, that she might listen to the mellow voice that seemed to sing for her alone.", "그리고 조는 이내 나직한 흥얼거림으로 잦아들었다. 오직 자기만을 위해 부르는 듯한 그 부드러운 목소리에 귀 기울이려고."]], [["\"Know'st thou the land where the citron blooms,\"", "\"레몬꽃 피어나는 그 땅을 그대 아는가\""], ["used to be the Professor's favorite line, for \"das land\"", "하는 구절은 교수가 늘 가장 좋아하던 대목이었다."], ["meant Germany to him; but now he seemed to dwell, with peculiar warmth and melody, upon the words,--", "그에게 \"그 땅\"이란 곧 독일을 뜻했으니까. 하지만 지금 그는 유난히 따뜻하고 선율 가득한 목소리로 이 구절에 머무는 듯했다."]], [["\"There, oh there, might I with thee, O my beloved, go!\"", "\"그곳, 오 그곳으로, 그대와 함께,\n오 내 사랑이여, 갈 수 있다면!\""]], [["and one listener was so thrilled by the tender invitation that she longed to say she did know the land, and would joyfully depart thither whenever he liked.", "그러자 듣고 있던 한 사람은 그 다정한 초대에 어찌나 가슴이 떨리던지, 자기는 그 땅을 안다고, 그가 원한다면 언제든 기꺼이 그곳으로 떠나겠다고 말하고 싶어졌다."]], [["The song was considered a great success, and the singer retired covered with laurels.", "그 노래는 대성공으로 여겨졌고, 가수는 월계관을 쓰고 물러났다."], ["But a few minutes afterward, he forgot his manners entirely, and stared at Amy putting on her bonnet; for she had been introduced simply as \"my sister,\"", "하지만 몇 분 뒤 그는 예의를 완전히 잊은 채, 보닛을 쓰는 에이미를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 에이미가 그저 \"제 여동생\"으로만 소개되었고, 그가 온 뒤로 아무도 그녀를 새 이름으로 부르지 않았던 것이다."], ["and no one had called her by her new name since he came. He forgot himself still further when Laurie said, in his most gracious manner, at parting,--", "헤어질 때 로리가 더없이 정중한 태도로 이렇게 말하자, 그는 더더욱 정신을 놓고 말았다."]], [["\"My wife and I are very glad to meet you, sir.", "\"제 아내와 저는 선생님을 뵙게 되어 무척 기쁩니다."], ["Please remember that there is always a welcome waiting for you over the way.\"", "길 건너 저희 집에는 언제든 환영이 기다리고 있다는 걸 기억해 주십시오.\""]], [["Then the Professor thanked him so heartily, and looked so suddenly illuminated with satisfaction, that Laurie thought him the most delightfully demonstrative old fellow he ever met.", "그러자 교수가 어찌나 진심으로 고마워하며 별안간 흡족함으로 환하게 빛나던지, 로리는 그를 여태껏 만나 본 사람 중 가장 유쾌하게 속내를 잘 드러내는 양반이라고 여겼다."]], [["\"I too shall go; but I shall gladly come again, if you will gif me leave, dear madame, for a little business in the city will keep me here some days.\"", "\"저도 그만 가 보아야겠지만, 허락해 주신다면 다시 기꺼이 찾아오겠소, 부인. 시내에 볼일이 좀 있어서 며칠은 여기 더 머물게 될 테니.\""]], [["He spoke to Mrs.", "그는 마치 부인에게 말했지만 시선은 조를 향하고 있었다."], ["March, but he looked at Jo; and the mother's voice gave as cordial an assent as did the daughter's eyes; for Mrs.", "그리고 어머니의 목소리는 딸의 눈빛 못지않게 다정하게 승낙의 뜻을 전했다."], ["March was not so blind to her children's interest as Mrs. Moffat supposed.", "마치 부인은 모팻 부인이 짐작한 것처럼 자기 아이들의 마음에 어두운 사람이 아니었으니까."]], [["\"I suspect that is a wise man,\" remarked Mr.", "\"내 보기에 저 사람은 현명한 사람이오.\""], ["March, with placid satisfaction, from the hearth-rug, after the last guest had gone.", "마지막 손님이 돌아간 뒤, 마치 씨가 난롯가 깔개 위에서 잔잔한 흡족함을 담아 말했다."]], [["\"I know he is a good one,\" added Mrs.", "\"좋은 사람인 건 분명해요.\""], ["March, with decided approval, as she wound up the clock.", "마치 부인이 시계태엽을 감으며 단호한 찬성의 뜻을 보탰다."]], [["\"I thought you'd like him,\"", "\"두 분이 그분을 좋아하실 줄 알았어요.\""], ["was all Jo said, as she slipped away to her bed.", "조는 그 말만 남기고 슬그머니 잠자리로 빠져나갔다."]], [["She wondered what the business was that brought Mr.", "조는 무슨 볼일이 베어 씨를 시내로 데려왔을까 궁금해하다가, 결국 그가 어딘가에서 큰 명예로운 자리에 임명되었는데 너무 겸손해서 그 사실을 입에 올리지 않은 거라고 결론지었다."], ["Bhaer to the city, and finally decided that he had been appointed to some great honor, somewhere, but had been too modest to mention the fact.", "만약 그가 제 방으로 돌아가 안전해지자 한 그림을 바라보던 모습을 조가 보았더라면, 어쩌면 이 문제에 약간의 빛이 비쳤을지도 모른다."], ["If she had seen his face when, safe in his own room, he looked at the picture of a severe and rigid young lady, with a good deal of hair, who appeared to be gazing darkly into futurity, it might have thrown some light upon the subject, especially when he turned off the gas, and kissed the picture in the dark.", "그 그림 속에는 숱 많은 머리를 한, 엄격하고 꼿꼿한 젊은 여인이 어두운 눈길로 미래를 응시하는 듯 그려져 있었다. 더욱이 그가 가스등을 끄고 어둠 속에서 그 그림에 입을 맞추는 모습을 보았더라면 말이다."]], [["# MY LORD AND LADY.", "# MY LORD AND LADY."]], [["\"Please, Madam Mother, could you lend me my wife for half an hour?", "\"어머니, 제 아내를 반 시간만 빌려주시겠어요?"], ["The luggage has come, and I've been making hay of Amy's Paris finery, trying to find some things I want,\"", "짐이 도착해서 제가 찾는 물건들을 찾느라 에이미의 파리 옷가지들을 죄다 뒤집어 놓는 중이거든요.\""], ["said Laurie, coming in the next day to find Mrs.", "이튿날 들어선 로리가 말했다."], ["Laurence sitting in her mother's lap, as if being made \"the baby\" again.", "로런스 부인이 어머니 무릎에 앉아 다시 \"아기\"가 된 듯이 있는 모습을 보고 한 말이었다."]], [["\"Certainly.", "\"그럼요. 가 보렴, 얘야."], ["Go, dear; I forget that you have any home but this,\" and Mrs.", "네게 이 집 말고 다른 집이 있다는 걸 깜빡했구나.\""], ["March pressed the white hand that wore the wedding-ring, as if asking pardon for her maternal covetousness.", "그렇게 말하며 마치 부인은 결혼반지를 낀 그 흰 손을 꼭 쥐었다. 어머니다운 욕심을 용서해 달라는 듯이."]], [["\"I shouldn't have come over if I could have helped it; but I can't get on without my little woman any more than a--\"", "\"제가 어떻게든 안 와도 됐으면 안 왔을 텐데, 우리 작은 아내가 없으면 도무지 안 되거든요. 마치—\""]], [["\"Weathercock can without wind,\"", "\"바람 없는 풍향계 신세지.\""], ["suggested Jo, as he paused for a simile; Jo had grown quite her own saucy self again since Teddy came home.", "로리가 비유할 말을 찾느라 멈칫하자 조가 거들었다. 테디가 돌아온 뒤로 조는 다시 예전의 발랄하고 당돌한 자기 모습으로 돌아와 있었다."]], [["\"Exactly; for Amy keeps me pointing due west most of the time, with only an occasional whiffle round to the south, and I haven't had an easterly spell since I was married; don't know anything about the north, but am altogether salubrious and balmy, hey, my lady?\"", "\"바로 그거야. 에이미는 나를 대부분 정서쪽으로만 향하게 하고, 가끔 남쪽으로 살짝 돌게 할 뿐이거든. 결혼한 뒤로 동풍이 분 적은 한 번도 없고, 북쪽은 어떤지 전혀 모르겠지만, 어쨌든 아주 쾌적하고 온화하지. 안 그래, 마님?\""]], [["\"Lovely weather so far; I don't know how long it will last, but I'm not afraid of storms, for I'm learning how to sail my ship.", "\"여태까진 날씨가 더없이 좋네요. 얼마나 갈지는 모르겠지만, 폭풍이 와도 무섭지 않아요."], ["Come home, dear, and I'll find your bootjack; I suppose that's what you are rummaging after among my things.", "제 배를 어떻게 몰아야 하는지 배우는 중이거든요. 집에 가요, 여보."], ["Men are _so_ helpless, mother,\"", "부츠 벗기는 걸 찾아 줄게요. 내 물건들을 뒤지는 게 아마 그것 때문이겠죠."], ["said Amy, with a matronly air, which delighted her husband.", "남자들은 어쩜 이렇게 손이 많이 가는지, 어머니.\" 에이미가 안주인다운 태도로 말하자 남편은 그 모습에 즐거워했다."]], [["\"What are you going to do with yourselves after you get settled?\"", "\"자리 잡고 나면 둘이 뭘 할 작정이니?\""], ["asked Jo, buttoning Amy's cloak as she used to button her pinafores.", "조가 에이미의 망토 단추를 채워 주며 물었다. 예전에 앞치마 단추를 채워 주던 것처럼."]], [["\"We have our plans; we don't mean to say much about them yet, because we are such very new brooms, but we don't intend to be idle.", "\"우리도 계획이 있어. 아직은 우리가 워낙 새내기다 보니 그 얘긴 별로 안 하려고."], ["I'm going into business with a devotion that shall delight grandfather, and prove to him that I'm not spoilt.", "그래도 빈둥거릴 생각은 없어. 난 할아버지가 기뻐하실 만큼 헌신적으로 사업에 뛰어들어서, 내가 버릇없이 망가지지 않았다는 걸 증명할 거야."], ["I need something of the sort to keep me steady.", "나를 다잡으려면 그런 게 좀 필요하거든."], ["I'm tired of dawdling, and mean to work like a man.\"", "빈둥대는 데도 이젠 질렸으니, 남자답게 일할 작정이야.\""]], [["\"And Amy, what is she going to do?\"", "\"그럼 에이미는 뭘 할 거니?\""], ["asked Mrs. March, well pleased at Laurie's decision, and the energy with which he spoke.", "로리의 결심과 그 말에 담긴 패기에 흡족해진 마치 부인이 물었다."]], [["\"After doing the civil all round, and airing our best bonnet, we shall astonish you by the elegant hospitalities of our mansion, the brilliant society we shall draw about us, and the beneficial influence we shall exert over the world at large.", "\"두루두루 예의를 차리고 우리의 가장 좋은 보닛을 한껏 뽐낸 다음에는, 우리 저택의 우아한 환대로, 우리가 끌어모을 화려한 사교계로, 그리고 우리가 온 세상에 끼칠 유익한 영향력으로 두 분을 놀라게 해 드릴 거예요."], ["That's about it, isn't it, Madame Récamier?\"", "대충 그런 거지, 안 그래, 레카미에 부인?\""], ["asked Laurie, with a quizzical look at Amy.", "로리가 에이미를 짓궂게 바라보며 물었다."]], [["\"Time will show.", "\"두고 보면 알겠죠."], ["Come away, Impertinence, and don't shock my family by calling me names before their faces,\"", "이리 와요, 버르장머리 없는 양반. 식구들 앞에서 별명으로 부르며 우리 집안을 놀라게 하지 말고요.\""], ["answered Amy, resolving that there should be a home with a good wife in it before she set up a _salon_ as a queen of society.", "에이미가 대답했다. 사교계의 여왕으로 살롱을 차리기 전에, 우선 좋은 아내가 있는 가정을 꾸리겠다고 마음먹으면서."]], [["\"How happy those children seem together!\" observed Mr.", "\"저 아이들, 함께 있는 모습이 어찌나 행복해 보이는지!\""], ["March, finding it difficult to become absorbed in his Aristotle after the young couple had gone.", "마치 씨가 말했다. 젊은 부부가 돌아간 뒤로 아리스토텔레스에 좀처럼 몰두하지 못하면서."]], [["\"Yes, and I think it will last,\" added Mrs.", "\"그래요, 그리고 그게 오래갈 것 같아요.\""], ["March, with the restful expression of a pilot who has brought a ship safely into port.", "마치 부인이 배를 무사히 항구로 데려온 도선사처럼 편안한 표정으로 덧붙였다."]], [["\"I know it will.", "\"분명히 그럴 거예요."], ["Happy Amy!\"", "행복한 에이미!\""], ["and Jo sighed, then smiled brightly as Professor Bhaer opened the gate with an impatient push.", "조는 한숨을 내쉬었다가, 베어 교수가 조바심을 내며 대문을 밀치고 들어서자 환하게 미소 지었다."]], [["Later in the evening, when his mind had been set at rest about the bootjack, Laurie said suddenly to his wife, who was flitting about, arranging her new art treasures,--", "그날 저녁 늦게, 부츠 벗기개 일로 마음이 놓인 로리가 새로 장만한 미술품들을 정리하느라 이리저리 분주히 다니는 아내에게 불쑥 말했다."]], [["\"Mrs. Laurence.\"", "\"로런스 부인.\""]], [["\"My lord!\"", "\"네, 나리!\""]], [["\"That man intends to marry our Jo!\"", "\"저 양반, 우리 조랑 결혼할 작정이야!\""]], [["\"I hope so; don't you, dear?\"", "\"그랬으면 좋겠어요. 당신도 그렇지 않아요, 여보?\""]], [["\"Well, my love, I consider him a trump, in the fullest sense of that expressive word, but I do wish he was a little younger and a good deal richer.\"", "\"음, 여보, 난 그 사람을 그 말의 가장 넓은 뜻에서 정말 멋진 사람이라고 생각해. 다만 좀 더 젊고 훨씬 더 부자였으면 좋겠다 싶긴 하지.\""]], [["\"Now, Laurie, don't be too fastidious and worldly-minded.", "\"이런, 로리, 너무 까다롭고 속물스럽게 굴지 말아요."], ["If they love one another it doesn't matter a particle how old they are nor how poor.", "두 사람이 서로 사랑한다면 나이가 몇이든 얼마나 가난하든 조금도 상관없어요."], ["Women _never_ should marry for money--\"", "여자는 절대로 돈 때문에 결혼하면 안—\""], ["Amy caught herself up short as the words escaped her, and looked at her husband, who replied, with malicious gravity,--", "에이미는 말이 입 밖으로 새어 나가자 화들짝 말을 끊고 남편을 바라보았고, 남편은 짓궂게 짐짓 진지한 얼굴로 대꾸했다."]], [["\"Certainly not, though you do hear charming girls say that they intend to do it sometimes.", "\"그럼, 절대 안 되고말고. 가끔 매력적인 아가씨들이 그럴 작정이라고 말하는 걸 듣긴 하지만 말이야."], ["If my memory serves me, you once thought it your duty to make a rich match; that accounts, perhaps, for your marrying a good-for-nothing like me.\"", "내 기억이 맞다면, 당신도 한때는 부유한 결혼을 하는 게 도리라고 여겼었지. 그러니 어쩌면 그래서 나 같은 쓸모없는 인간이랑 결혼한 건지도 모르겠군.\""]], [["\"O my dearest boy, don't, don't say that!", "\"오, 내 사랑, 제발, 제발 그런 말 말아요!"], ["I forgot you were rich when I said 'Yes.'", "'네'라고 대답했을 때 난 당신이 부자라는 걸 잊고 있었어요."], ["I'd have married you if you hadn't a penny, and I sometimes wish you _were_ poor that I might show how much I love you;\"", "당신한테 한 푼도 없었어도 결혼했을 거예요. 가끔은 차라리 당신이 가난했으면 좋겠어요."], ["and Amy, who was very dignified in public and very fond in private, gave convincing proofs of the truth of her words.", "내가 당신을 얼마나 사랑하는지 보여 줄 수 있게요.\" 사람들 앞에서는 무척 점잖고 둘만 있을 때는 무척 다정한 에이미는, 자기 말이 진심임을 분명히 증명해 보였다."]], [["\"You don't really think I am such a mercenary creature as I tried to be once, do you?", "\"내가 한때 되려고 애썼던 것처럼 그렇게 돈만 밝히는 인간이라고 정말로 생각하는 건 아니죠?"], ["It would break my heart if you didn't believe that I'd gladly pull in the same boat with you, even if you had to get your living by rowing on the lake.\"", "당신이 호수에서 노를 저어 먹고살아야 하는 처지였대도 기꺼이 당신과 한 배를 탔으리라는 걸 믿어 주지 않는다면, 내 가슴이 찢어질 거예요.\""]], [["\"Am I an idiot and a brute?", "\"내가 멍청이에 짐승이라도 되는 줄 알아? 어떻게 그렇게 생각하겠어."], ["How could I think so, when you refused a richer man for me, and won't let me give you half I want to now, when I have the right?", "당신은 나를 위해 더 부유한 남자를 마다했고, 이제 내가 줄 권리가 생겼는데도 주고 싶은 것의 절반도 받으려 하질 않는데. 가엾게도 여자들은 날마다 그렇게들 하고, 그게 자기들의 유일한 구원이라고 배우지."], ["Girls do it every day, poor things, and are taught to think it is their only salvation; but you had better lessons, and, though I trembled for you at one time, I was not disappointed, for the daughter was true to the mother's teaching.", "하지만 당신은 더 나은 가르침을 받았어. 한때는 당신 때문에 마음 졸이기도 했지만 실망하진 않았어."], ["I told mamma so yesterday, and she looked as glad and grateful as if I'd given her a check for a million, to be spent in charity.", "딸이 어머니의 가르침에 충실했으니까. 어제 장모님께도 그렇게 말씀드렸더니, 자선에 쓰라고 백만 달러짜리 수표라도 드린 것처럼 기뻐하고 고마워하셨어."], ["You are not listening to my moral remarks, Mrs.", "듣고 있지 않군, 로런스 부인. 내 도덕적인 이야기를.\""], ["Laurence;\"", "로리는 말을 멈추었다."], ["and Laurie paused, for Amy's eyes had an absent look, though fixed upon his face.", "에이미의 눈이 그의 얼굴에 붙박여 있으면서도 어딘가 딴 데 가 있는 듯했기 때문이다."]], [["\"Yes, I am, and admiring the dimple in your chin at the same time.", "\"듣고 있어요. 동시에 당신 턱의 보조개를 감탄하며 보고 있었고요."], ["I don't wish to make you vain, but I must confess that I'm prouder of my handsome husband than of all his money.", "당신을 우쭐하게 만들고 싶진 않지만, 솔직히 고백하자면 난 당신 돈 전부보다 잘생긴 내 남편이 더 자랑스러워요."], ["Don't laugh, but your nose is _such_ a comfort to me;\"", "웃지 마요, 당신 코는 정말이지 나한테 큰 위안이거든요.\""], ["and Amy softly caressed the well-cut feature with artistic satisfaction.", "그렇게 말하며 에이미는 그 잘 빠진 콧날을 예술가다운 흡족함으로 부드럽게 어루만졌다."]], [["Laurie had received many compliments in his life, but never one that suited him better, as he plainly showed, though he did laugh at his wife's peculiar taste, while she said slowly,--", "로리는 살면서 숱한 찬사를 들었지만, 이보다 더 마음에 드는 건 한 번도 없었다. 그건 표정에 고스란히 드러났다. 아내의 별난 취향에 웃기는 했지만 말이다. 그러는 동안 에이미가 천천히 말했다."]], [["\"May I ask you a question, dear?\"", "\"하나 물어봐도 돼요, 여보?\""]], [["\"Of course you may.\"", "\"물론이지.\""]], [["\"Shall you care if Jo does marry Mr. Bhaer?\"", "\"조가 정말 베어 씨랑 결혼하면 당신은 신경 쓰일까요?\""]], [["\"Oh, that's the trouble, is it?", "\"오, 그게 걱정이었군?"], ["I thought there was something in the dimple that didn't suit you.", "그 보조개 속에 당신 마음에 안 드는 뭔가가 있다 싶었어. 난 구유 위의 개* 같은 사람이 아니라 세상에서 가장 행복한 사내야."], ["Not being a dog in the manger, but the happiest fellow alive, I assure you I can dance at Jo's wedding with a heart as light as my heels.", "장담하는데, 난 발만큼이나 가벼운 마음으로 조의 결혼식에서 춤출 수 있어. 못 믿겠어, 내 사랑?\""], ["Do you doubt it, my darling?\"", "* 구유 위의 개: 자기는 못 먹으면서 남도 못 먹게 막는 심술쟁이를 뜻하는 옛 우화 : 옮긴이."]], [["Amy looked up at him, and was satisfied; her last little jealous fear vanished forever, and she thanked him, with a face full of love and confidence.", "에이미는 그를 올려다보고는 마음이 놓였다. 마지막 남은 작은 질투의 두려움이 영영 사라졌고, 그녀는 사랑과 신뢰가 가득한 얼굴로 그에게 고마움을 전했다."]], [["\"I wish we could do something for that capital old Professor.", "\"저 멋진 노교수를 위해 뭐든 해 드릴 수 있으면 좋겠어."], ["Couldn't we invent a rich relation, who shall obligingly die out there in Germany, and leave him a tidy little fortune?\"", "독일 어딘가에서 친절하게 돌아가시면서 그분께 제법 짭짤한 재산을 남겨 줄 부유한 친척이라도 하나 지어낼 수 없을까?\" 로리가 말했다."], ["said Laurie, when they began to pace up and down the long drawing-room, arm-in-arm, as they were fond of doing, in memory of the chateau garden.", "두 사람이 긴 응접실을 팔짱을 끼고 오르락내리락 거닐기 시작할 무렵이었다. 그건 성(城)의 정원을 추억하며 둘이 즐겨 하던 일이었다."]], [["\"Jo would find us out, and spoil it all; she is very proud of him, just as he is, and said yesterday that she thought poverty was a beautiful thing.\"", "\"조가 알아채고 다 망쳐 버릴 거예요. 조는 지금 그대로의 그분을 무척 자랑스러워하거든요. 어제는 가난이야말로 아름다운 거라고 생각한다고 했어요.\""]], [["\"Bless her dear heart!", "\"그 착한 마음씨 좀 봐!"], ["she won't think so when she has a literary husband, and a dozen little professors and professorins to support.", "글 쓰는 남편에다 부양해야 할 꼬마 교수 나리와 교수 아씨를 한 다스나 거느리게 되면 그렇게 생각하진 않을걸."], ["We won't interfere now, but watch our chance, and do them a good turn in spite of themselves.", "지금은 우리가 나서지 말고 기회를 엿보다가, 두 사람이 모르는 새에 좋은 일을 해 주자고."], ["I owe Jo for a part of my education, and she believes in people's paying their honest debts, so I'll get round her in that way.\"", "난 조한테 교육의 일부를 빚졌고, 조는 사람이 정직한 빚은 갚아야 한다고 믿는 사람이니, 그런 식으로 둘러서 조를 구슬려 보지 뭐.\""]], [["\"How delightful it is to be able to help others, isn't it?", "\"남을 도울 수 있다는 게 얼마나 기쁜 일인지 몰라요. 그렇죠?"], ["That was always one of my dreams, to have the power of giving freely; and, thanks to you, the dream has come true.\"", "거리낌 없이 베풀 수 있는 힘을 갖는 것, 그게 늘 제 꿈 중 하나였어요. 그런데 당신 덕분에 그 꿈이 이루어졌네요.\""]], [["\"Ah! we'll do quantities of good, won't we? There's one sort of poverty that I particularly like to help. Out-and-out beggars get taken care of, but poor gentlefolks fare badly, because they won't ask, and people don't dare to offer charity; yet there are a thousand ways of helping them, if one only knows how to do it so delicately that it does not offend. I must say, I like to serve a decayed gentleman better than a blarneying beggar; I suppose it's wrong, but I do, though it is harder.\"", "\"아! 우린 좋은 일을 잔뜩 할 거야. 안 그래? 내가 특히 돕고 싶은 가난이 한 종류 있어. 노골적인 거지들은 보살핌을 받지만, 가난한 양반들은 형편이 딱하거든. 손을 내밀 줄도 모르고, 사람들도 감히 자선을 베풀 엄두를 못 내니까. 그래도 그들을 돕는 방법은 천 가지나 있어. 기분 상하지 않게 아주 섬세하게 하는 법만 안다면 말이야. 솔직히 말해서, 난 입에 발린 소리나 늘어놓는 거지보다 몰락한 양반을 돕는 게 더 좋아. 그게 잘못된 건지도 모르지만, 정말 그래. 더 어려운 일인데도 말이야.\""]], [["\"Because it takes a gentleman to do it,\"", "\"그건 양반이라야 할 수 있는 일이니까요.\""], ["added the other member of the domestic admiration society.", "가정 내 상호 찬미회의 또 다른 회원이 거들었다."]], [["\"Thank you, I'm afraid I don't deserve that pretty compliment.", "\"고마워. 그 멋진 칭찬을 받을 자격이 내겐 없는 것 같지만 말이야."], ["But I was going to say that while I was dawdling about abroad, I saw a good many talented young fellows making all sorts of sacrifices, and enduring real hardships, that they might realize their dreams.", "그건 그렇고, 내가 외국에서 빈둥거리며 지낼 때, 꿈을 이루려고 온갖 희생을 치르고 진짜 고생을 견뎌 내는 재능 있는 젊은이들을 많이 봤어. 그중엔 정말 훌륭한 친구들도 있었지."], ["Splendid fellows, some of them, working like heroes, poor and friendless, but so full of courage, patience, and ambition, that I was ashamed of myself, and longed to give them a right good lift.", "영웅처럼 일하고, 가난하고 의지가지없으면서도 어찌나 용기와 인내와 야망이 넘치던지, 난 내 자신이 부끄러워졌고 그들을 제대로 끌어 주고 싶어 안달이 났어. 그런 사람들은 돕는 보람이 있어."], ["Those are people whom it's a satisfaction to help, for if they've got genius, it's an honor to be allowed to serve them, and not let it be lost or delayed for want of fuel to keep the pot boiling; if they haven't, it's a pleasure to comfort the poor souls, and keep them from despair when they find it out.\"", "재능을 타고났다면 그들을 섬길 수 있다는 게 영광이고, 솥을 계속 끓일 땔감이 없어서 그 재능이 사그라지거나 늦춰지지 않게 해 주는 거니까. 재능이 없다 해도, 그 가엾은 영혼들을 위로하고 그 사실을 깨달았을 때 절망하지 않게 막아 주는 건 기쁜 일이지.\""]], [["\"Yes, indeed; and there's another class who can't ask, and who suffer in silence.", "\"그래요, 정말로요. 그리고 손 내밀 줄 모르고 말없이 괴로워하는 또 다른 부류가 있어요."], ["I know something of it, for I belonged to it before you made a princess of me, as the king does the beggar-maid in the old story.", "저도 그게 어떤 건지 좀 알아요. 옛이야기에서 임금님이 거지 소녀를 공주로 만들어 주듯 당신이 나를 공주로 만들어 주기 전까지, 저도 그 부류에 속했으니까요."], ["Ambitious girls have a hard time, Laurie, and often have to see youth, health, and precious opportunities go by, just for want of a little help at the right minute.", "야망 있는 여자들은 힘든 시절을 보내요, 로리. 그리고 딱 알맞은 순간에 약간의 도움이 없어서 청춘과 건강과 소중한 기회를 그냥 흘려보내는 일이 많죠."], ["People have been very kind to me; and whenever I see girls struggling along, as we used to do, I want to put out my hand and help them, as I was helped.\"", "사람들은 제게 무척 친절했어요. 그러니 예전의 우리처럼 힘겹게 헤쳐 나가는 여자아이들을 볼 때마다, 제가 도움받았던 것처럼 손을 내밀어 그들을 돕고 싶어요.\""]], [["\"And so you shall, like an angel as you are!\"", "\"그래, 당신은 그렇게 할 거야. 천사 같은 당신이니까!\""], ["cried Laurie, resolving, with a glow of philanthropic zeal, to found and endow an institution for the express benefit of young women with artistic tendencies.", "로리가 외쳤다. 그러고는 박애심에 불타올라, 예술적 재능을 지닌 젊은 여성들을 위한 기관을 세우고 기금을 대기로 마음먹었다."], ["\"Rich people have no right to sit down and enjoy themselves, or let their money accumulate for others to waste.", "\"부유한 사람들은 가만히 앉아 자기들만 즐기거나, 남들이 탕진하라고 돈을 쌓아 둘 권리가 없어."], ["It's not half so sensible to leave legacies when one dies as it is to use the money wisely while alive, and enjoy making one's fellow-creatures happy with it.", "죽을 때 유산을 남기는 건, 살아 있는 동안 그 돈을 현명하게 쓰고 그것으로 이웃을 행복하게 만들며 즐거워하는 것에 비하면 절반도 분별 있는 일이 못 돼."], ["We'll have a good time ourselves, and add an extra relish to our own pleasure by giving other people a generous taste.", "우린 우리대로 즐겁게 지내면서, 남들에게도 후하게 맛보게 해 줌으로써 우리 즐거움에 특별한 풍미를 더할 거야. 당신은 작은 도르가*가 되어 줄래?"], ["Will you be a little Dorcas, going about emptying a big basket of comforts, and filling it up with good deeds?\"", "위안거리를 잔뜩 담은 큰 바구니를 비우고 다니면서 그 자리에 선행을 채워 넣는?\" * 도르가: 가난한 이들을 위해 옷을 지어 베푼 성경 속 자비로운 여인 : 옮긴이."]], [["\"With all my heart, if you will be a brave St.", "\"기꺼이요. 당신이 용감한 성 마르티노*가 되어 준다면요."], ["Martin, stopping, as you ride gallantly through the world, to share your cloak with the beggar.\"", "세상을 늠름하게 말 달려 지나가다 멈춰 서서, 거지에게 당신 망토를 나눠 주는 그런 사람이요.\""]], [["\"It's a bargain, and we shall get the best of it!\"", "* 성 마르티노: 추위에 떠는 거지에게 자기 망토를 잘라 나눠 준 일화로 유명한 성인 : 옮긴이."]], [["So the young pair shook hands upon it, and then paced happily on again, feeling that their pleasant home was more home-like because they hoped to brighten other homes, believing that their own feet would walk more uprightly along the flowery path before them, if they smoothed rough ways for other feet, and feeling that their hearts were more closely knit together by a love which could tenderly remember those less blest than they.", "\"그럼 약속한 거다. 그리고 그 약속에서 가장 큰 덕을 보는 건 우리겠지!\" 그렇게 젊은 부부는 그 약속의 표시로 손을 맞잡고, 다시 행복하게 거닐기 시작했다. 다른 가정들을 환하게 밝혀 주기를 바라기에 자기들의 정겨운 집이 한층 더 집답게 느껴졌고, 다른 발들이 디딜 거친 길을 평탄히 골라 준다면 눈앞에 펼쳐진 꽃길을 따라 자기들의 발걸음도 한결 더 바르게 나아가리라 믿었으며, 자기들보다 덜 축복받은 이들을 다정히 기억할 줄 아는 사랑으로 두 사람의 마음이 한층 더 단단히 맺어진 것을 느끼면서."]], [["# DAISY AND DEMI.", "# DAISY AND DEMI."]], [["I cannot feel that I have done my duty as humble historian of the March family, without devoting at least one chapter to the two most precious and important members of it.", "마치 집안의 보잘것없는 기록자로서 그 집안의 가장 소중하고 중요한 두 식구에게 적어도 한 장(章)을 바치지 않고서는, 내 도리를 다했다고 도무지 느낄 수가 없다. 데이지와 데미는 이제 분별이 생길 나이에 이르렀다."], ["Daisy and Demi had now arrived at years of discretion; for in this fast age babies of three or four assert their rights, and get them, too, which is more than many of their elders do.", "이 빠른 시대에는 서너 살짜리 아기들도 제 권리를 주장하고, 또 그걸 얻어 내기까지 하니, 어른들 가운데 많은 이들도 못 하는 일이다. 만약 떠받들림에 흠뻑 젖어 완전히 버릇이 망가질 위험에 처한 쌍둥이가 세상에 있었다면, 바로 이 재잘대는 브룩 집안 쌍둥이였을 것이다."], ["If there ever were a pair of twins in danger of being utterly spoilt by adoration, it was these prattling Brookes.", "물론 둘은 여태껏 태어난 아이들 중 가장 비범했으니, 여덟 달에 걸음마를 했고, 열두 달에 청산유수로 말을 했으며, 두 살에는 식탁에 제자리를 차지하고 앉아 보는 이들을 모두 매료시킬 만큼 의젓하게 굴었다는 사실만 말해도 알 만하다. 세 살에 데이지는 \"바늘\"을 달라고 졸라 정말로 네 땀을 떠서 주머니를 만들었고, 찬장에 살림을 차려 놓고 손톱만 한 요리용 화덕을 어찌나 솜씨 좋게 다루던지 해나의 눈에 자랑스러운 눈물이 맺힐 정도였다."], ["Of course they were the most remarkable children ever born, as will be shown when I mention that they walked at eight months, talked fluently at twelve months, and at two years they took their places at table, and behaved with a propriety which charmed all beholders.", "한편 데미는 할아버지에게서 글자를 배웠는데, 할아버지는 팔다리로 글자 모양을 만들어 알파벳을 가르치는 새로운 방법을 고안해 냈으니, 머리와 발의 체조를 한데 결합한 셈이었다. 사내아이는 일찌감치 기계에 대한 재능을 드러내어 아버지를 기쁘게 하고 어머니를 골치 아프게 했다."], ["At three, Daisy demanded a \"needler,\"", "보이는 기계마다 흉내 내려 들어 육아실을 온통 난장판으로 만들었던 것이다."], ["and actually made a bag with four stitches in it; she likewise set up housekeeping in the sideboard, and managed a microscopic cooking-stove with a skill that brought tears of pride to Hannah's eyes, while Demi learned his letters with his grandfather, who invented a new mode of teaching the alphabet by forming the letters with his arms and legs, thus uniting gymnastics for head and heels.", "그의 \"재봉틀\"은 끈과 의자와 빨래집게와 실패로 이루어진 알 수 없는 구조물로, 바퀴가 \"빙글빙글\" 돌게 만든 것이었다."], ["The boy early developed a mechanical genius which delighted his father and distracted his mother, for he tried to imitate every machine he saw, and kept the nursery in a chaotic condition, with his \"sewin-sheen,\"--a mysterious structure of string, chairs, clothes-pins, and spools, for wheels to go \"wound and wound;\"", "또 큰 의자 등받이에 바구니를 매달아 놓고는 너무 잘 믿는 누이를 거기 끌어올리려 헛되이 애썼는데, 누이는 여성스러운 헌신으로 작은 머리가 쿵쿵 부딪치도록 가만히 있다가 구조되곤 했다. 그러면 어린 발명가는 분개하여 이렇게 말하는 것이었다."], ["also a basket hung over the back of a big chair, in which he vainly tried to hoist his too confiding sister, who, with feminine devotion, allowed her little head to be bumped till rescued, when the young inventor indignantly remarked, \"Why, marmar, dat's my lellywaiter, and me's trying to pull her up.\"", "\"엄마, 이건 내 승강기란 말이야. 누나를 위로 끌어올리려는 거라고.\""]], [["Though utterly unlike in character, the twins got on remarkably well together, and seldom quarrelled more than thrice a day.", "성격은 영판 딴판이었지만 쌍둥이는 놀랄 만큼 사이좋게 지냈고, 하루에 세 번 넘게 다투는 일은 좀처럼 없었다."], ["Of course, Demi tyrannized over Daisy, and gallantly defended her from every other aggressor; while Daisy made a galley-slave of herself, and adored her brother as the one perfect being in the world.", "물론 데미는 데이지에게 군림하면서도 다른 어떤 침입자로부터든 늠름하게 누이를 지켜 주었고, 데이지는 갤리선 노예가 되어 오빠를 세상에서 둘도 없는 완벽한 존재로 떠받들었다."], ["A rosy, chubby, sunshiny little soul was Daisy, who found her way to everybody's heart, and nestled there.", "데이지는 발그레하고 통통하며 햇살처럼 밝은 작은 영혼이라, 누구의 마음에든 파고들어 그 안에 둥지를 틀었다."], ["One of the captivating children, who seem made to be kissed and cuddled, adorned and adored like little goddesses, and produced for general approval on all festive occasions.", "입맞춤을 받고 품에 안기고, 어린 여신처럼 꾸며지고 떠받들리며, 잔칫날마다 사람들의 칭찬을 받으려고 태어난 듯한, 그런 사랑스러운 아이들 가운데 하나였다."], ["Her small virtues were so sweet that she would have been quite angelic if a few small naughtinesses had not kept her delightfully human.", "자그마한 미덕들이 어찌나 사랑스럽던지, 자그마한 말썽이 몇 가지 끼어 사랑스럽게 인간다움을 지켜 주지 않았다면 거의 천사 같았을 것이다."], ["It was all fair weather in her world, and every morning she scrambled up to the window in her little night-gown to look out, and say, no matter whether it rained or shone, \"Oh, pitty day, oh, pitty day!\"", "그 아이의 세상은 늘 맑은 날씨여서, 매일 아침 작은 잠옷 차림으로 창가로 기어올라 밖을 내다보며, 비가 오든 해가 나든 상관없이 이렇게 말했다. \"오, 예쁜 날, 오, 예쁜 날!\""], ["Every one was a friend, and she offered kisses to a stranger so confidingly that the most inveterate bachelor relented, and baby-lovers became faithful worshippers.", "누구나 다 친구였고, 낯선 사람에게도 어찌나 미덥게 입맞춤을 건네던지, 아무리 골수 독신주의자라도 마음이 누그러졌고, 아기를 좋아하는 사람들은 충실한 숭배자가 되었다."]], [["\"Me loves evvybody,\"", "\"나는 모든 사람을 사랑해.\""], ["she once said, opening her arms, with her spoon in one hand, and her mug in the other, as if eager to embrace and nourish the whole world.", "언젠가 데이지는 한 손에 숟가락을, 다른 손에 머그잔을 든 채 두 팔을 활짝 벌리며 그렇게 말했다. 마치 온 세상을 끌어안고 먹여 주고 싶어 안달이 난 듯이."]], [["As she grew, her mother began to feel that the Dove-cote would be blest by the presence of an inmate as serene and loving as that which had helped to make the old house home, and to pray that she might be spared a loss like that which had lately taught them how long they had entertained an angel unawares.", "데이지가 자라면서, 어머니는 비둘기집*이 그 옛집을 집답게 만들어 주었던 그 존재만큼이나 평온하고 사랑 가득한 식구의 손길로 축복받게 되리라는 느낌이 들기 시작했다. 그리고 얼마나 오랫동안 자기들도 모르게 천사를 품어 왔는지를 최근에야 가르쳐 준 그런 상실을 데이지만은 면하게 해 달라고 기도하게 되었다."], ["Her grandfather often called her \"Beth,\" and her grandmother watched over her with untiring devotion, as if trying to atone for some past mistake, which no eye but her own could see.", "할아버지는 데이지를 자주 \"베스\"라고 불렀고, 할머니는 자기 눈에만 보이는 어떤 지난 잘못을 속죄하려는 듯이 지칠 줄 모르는 정성으로 그 아이를 지켜보았다."]], [["Demi, like a true Yankee, was of an inquiring turn, wanting to know everything, and often getting much disturbed because he could not get satisfactory answers to his perpetual \"What for?\"", "* 비둘기집: 메그와 존이 사는 작은 집의 별칭 : 옮긴이. 데미는 진짜 양키답게 캐묻기를 좋아하는 성미라 무엇이든 알고 싶어 했고, 끊임없는 \"왜요?\"에 시원한 답을 얻지 못해 자주 몹시 안달했다."]], [["He also possessed a philosophic bent, to the great delight of his grandfather, who used to hold Socratic conversations with him, in which the precocious pupil occasionally posed his teacher, to the undisguised satisfaction of the womenfolk.", "또한 그는 철학적인 기질도 지니고 있어서 할아버지를 크게 기쁘게 했다. 할아버지는 데미와 소크라테스식 대화를 나누곤 했는데, 그 조숙한 제자가 이따금 스승을 쩔쩔매게 만들어 집안 여인들을 대놓고 흡족하게 했다."]], [["\"What makes my legs go, dranpa?\"", "\"할아버지, 내 다리는 뭐 때문에 움직여요?\""], ["asked the young philosopher, surveying those active portions of his frame with a meditative air, while resting after a go-to-bed frolic one night.", "어린 철학자가 어느 날 밤 자기 전 한바탕 뛰놀고 나서 쉬던 중에, 그 부지런히 움직이는 신체 부위를 명상하듯 살피며 물었다."]], [["\"It's your little mind, Demi,\"", "\"그건 네 작은 마음이란다, 데미.\""], ["replied the sage, stroking the yellow head respectfully.", "현자가 그 노란 머리를 정중히 쓰다듬으며 대답했다."]], [["\"What is a little mine?\"", "\"작은 마음이 뭔데요?\""]], [["\"It is something which makes your body move, as the spring made the wheels go in my watch when I showed it to you.\"", "\"네 몸을 움직이게 하는 무언가지. 전에 내가 보여 준 회중시계에서 태엽이 바퀴를 돌게 하는 것처럼 말이다.\""]], [["\"Open me; I want to see it go wound.\"", "\"날 열어 봐요. 그게 빙글빙글 도는 걸 보고 싶어요.\""]], [["\"I can't do that any more than you could open the watch.", "\"그건 네가 시계를 열 수 없는 것만큼이나 나도 못 한단다."], ["God winds you up, and you go till He stops you.\"", "하느님이 너한테 태엽을 감아 주시면, 너는 그분이 멈추실 때까지 움직이는 거야.\""]], [["\"Does I?\"", "\"내가요?\""], ["and Demi's brown eyes grew big and bright as he took in the new thought.", "데미의 갈색 눈이 새로운 생각을 받아들이며 크고 반짝반짝 빛났다."], ["\"Is I wounded up like the watch?\"", "\"내가 시계처럼 태엽이 감겼어요?\""]], [["\"Yes; but I can't show you how; for it is done when we don't see.\"", "\"그렇단다. 하지만 어떻게 그러는지는 보여 줄 수가 없어. 우리가 보지 않을 때 이루어지는 일이거든.\""]], [["Demi felt of his back, as if expecting to find it like that of the watch, and then gravely remarked,--", "데미는 시계처럼 되어 있는지 알아보려는 듯 제 등을 만져 보더니, 진지하게 말했다."]], [["\"I dess Dod does it when I's asleep.\"", "\"내 생각엔 하느님이 내가 자고 있을 때 하시는 것 같아요.\""]], [["A careful explanation followed, to which he listened so attentively that his anxious grandmother said,--", "이어서 정성스러운 설명이 뒤따랐고, 데미가 어찌나 골똘히 듣던지 걱정스러워진 할머니가 말했다."]], [["\"My dear, do you think it wise to talk about such things to that baby?", "\"여보, 저 아기한테 그런 얘기를 하는 게 현명한 일일까요?"], ["He's getting great bumps over his eyes, and learning to ask the most unanswerable questions.\"", "눈 위로 혹이 점점 커지고, 도무지 답할 수 없는 질문들만 배우고 있잖아요.\""]], [["\"If he is old enough to ask the questions he is old enough to receive true answers.", "\"질문을 할 만큼 나이를 먹었으면 참된 답을 받을 만큼 나이를 먹은 거요."], ["I am not putting the thoughts into his head, but helping him unfold those already there.", "난 저 아이 머릿속에 생각을 집어넣는 게 아니라, 이미 거기 있는 생각을 펼치도록 돕는 거요."], ["These children are wiser than we are, and I have no doubt the boy understands every word I have said to him.", "이 아이들은 우리보다 더 지혜로워서, 저 녀석이 내가 한 말을 한마디도 빠짐없이 알아듣는다는 걸 난 의심치 않소."], ["Now, Demi, tell me where you keep your mind?\"", "자, 데미, 네 마음을 어디에 두고 있는지 말해 보겠니?\""]], [["If the boy had replied like Alcibiades, \"By the gods, Socrates, I cannot tell,\"", "만약 그 아이가 알키비아데스처럼 \"신들께 맹세코, 소크라테스여, 저는 모르겠습니다\"라고 대답했더라도 할아버지는 놀라지 않았을 것이다."], ["his grandfather would not have been surprised; but when, after standing a moment on one leg, like a meditative young stork, he answered, in a tone of calm conviction, \"In my little belly,\" the old gentleman could only join in grandma's laugh, and dismiss the class in metaphysics.", "하지만 데미가 명상에 잠긴 어린 황새처럼 한쪽 다리로 잠시 서 있다가 차분한 확신의 어조로 \"내 작은 배 속에요\"라고 대답하자, 노신사는 그저 할머니의 웃음에 함께 끼어들며 형이상학 수업을 마치는 수밖에 없었다."]], [["There might have been cause for maternal anxiety, if Demi had not given convincing proofs that he was a true boy, as well as a budding philosopher; for, often, after a discussion which caused Hannah to prophesy, with ominous nods, \"That child ain't long for this world,\"", "만약 데미가 새싹 같은 철학자일 뿐 아니라 진짜 사내아이라는 사실을 미덥게 증명해 보이지 않았더라면, 어머니로서 걱정할 만도 했을 것이다."], ["he would turn about and set her fears at rest by some of the pranks with which dear, dirty, naughty little rascals distract and delight their parents' souls.", "해나가 불길하게 고개를 끄덕이며 \"저 애는 이 세상 사람 같지가 않아\"라고 예언하게 만드는 토론을 벌이고 나면, 데미는 곧잘 돌변하여, 사랑스럽고 꾀죄죄하고 말썽꾸러기인 어린 개구쟁이들이 부모의 영혼을 애태우고 또 즐겁게 하는 그런 장난들로 그녀의 두려움을 잠재워 놓곤 했기 때문이다."]], [["Meg made many moral rules, and tried to keep them; but what mother was ever proof against the winning wiles, the ingenious evasions, or the tranquil audacity of the miniature men and women who so early show themselves accomplished Artful Dodgers?", "메그는 도덕 규칙을 많이 정해 놓고 그것들을 지키려 애썼다. 하지만 그토록 일찍부터 노련한 솜씨꾼 사기꾼*의 면모를 드러내는 이 꼬마 남녀의 사람 홀리는 꾀와 기발한 둘러대기, 혹은 천연덕스러운 대담함 앞에서 끄떡없었던 어머니가 세상에 어디 있었던가?"]], [["\"No more raisins, Demi, they'll make you sick,\"", "* 솜씨꾼 사기꾼: 찰스 디킨스 소설 「올리버 트위스트」에 나오는 소매치기 소년의 별명 : 옮긴이. \"건포도는 그만, 데미."], ["says mamma to the young person who offers his services in the kitchen with unfailing regularity on plum-pudding day.", "그러다 배탈 난다.\" 자두 푸딩 만드는 날이면 어김없이 부엌에 와서 거들겠다고 나서는 어린 양반에게 엄마가 말한다."]], [["\"Me likes to be sick.\"", "\"나는 배탈 나는 거 좋아.\""]], [["\"I don't want to have you, so run away and help Daisy make patty-cakes.\"", "\"엄마는 싫어. 그러니 저리 가서 데이지가 패티케이크 만드는 거나 도와주렴.\""]], [["He reluctantly departs, but his wrongs weigh upon his spirit; and, by and by, when an opportunity comes to redress them, he outwits mamma by a shrewd bargain.", "데미는 마지못해 물러나지만, 자기가 당한 부당함이 마음을 짓누른다. 그러다 이윽고 그것을 바로잡을 기회가 오자, 그는 약삭빠른 흥정으로 엄마를 한 수 위에서 따돌린다."]], [["\"Now you have been good children, and I'll play anything you like,\"", "\"자, 너희 착하게 굴었으니, 너희가 원하는 건 뭐든 놀아 줄게.\""], ["says Meg, as she leads her assistant cooks upstairs, when the pudding is safely bouncing in the pot.", "푸딩이 냄비 속에서 무사히 통통 끓고 있을 때, 메그가 조수 요리사들을 위층으로 데리고 가며 말한다."]], [["\"Truly, marmar?\"", "\"정말이지, 엄마?\""], ["asks Demi, with a brilliant idea in his well-powdered head.", "데미가 하얗게 분칠된 머릿속에 멋진 생각 하나를 떠올리며 묻는다."]], [["\"Yes, truly; anything you say,\"", "\"그럼, 정말이지. 네가 말하는 건 뭐든.\""], ["replies the short-sighted parent, preparing herself to sing \"The Three Little Kittens\"", "근시안적인 엄마가, 바람이 불든 다리가 빠지든 아랑곳없이 \"세 마리 아기 고양이\"를 대여섯 번이나 불러 주거나, \"1페니짜리 빵을 사러\""], ["half a dozen times over, or to take her family to \"Buy a penny bun,\"", "식구들을 데려갈 채비를 하며 대답한다."], ["regardless of wind or limb. But Demi corners her by the cool reply,--", "그러나 데미는 시치미를 뚝 떼고 이렇게 대답하여 엄마를 궁지로 몰아넣는다."]], [["\"Then we'll go and eat up all the raisins.\"", "\"그럼 우리 가서 건포도 다 먹어 버리자.\""]], [["Aunt Dodo was chief playmate and _confidante_ of both children, and the trio turned the little house topsy-turvy.", "도도 이모는 두 아이의 으뜸가는 놀이 동무이자 비밀 털어놓는 상대였고, 셋이서 작은 집을 발칵 뒤집어 놓았다."], ["Aunt Amy was as yet only a name to them, Aunt Beth soon faded into a pleasantly vague memory, but Aunt Dodo was a living reality, and they made the most of her, for which compliment she was deeply grateful.", "에이미 이모는 아직 이름뿐인 존재였고, 베스 이모는 이내 어렴풋하고 정겨운 기억으로 희미해졌지만, 도도 이모는 살아 있는 실재였다. 두 아이는 도도 이모를 한껏 활용했고, 그 점에 대해 도도는 깊이 고마워했다."], ["But when Mr. Bhaer came, Jo neglected her playfellows, and dismay and desolation fell upon their little souls.", "그런데 베어 씨가 오자 조는 놀이 동무들을 등한시했고, 두 어린 영혼에는 절망과 황량함이 내려앉았다."], ["Daisy, who was fond of going about peddling kisses, lost her best customer and became bankrupt; Demi, with infantile penetration, soon discovered that Dodo liked to play with \"the bear-man\"", "입맞춤을 팔러 다니기 좋아하던 데이지는 가장 큰 단골을 잃고 파산했다. 데미는 어린아이다운 통찰력으로, 도도가 자기보다 \"곰 아저씨\"와 노는 걸 더 좋아한다는 사실을 곧 알아차렸다."], ["better than she did with him; but, though hurt, he concealed his anguish, for he hadn't the heart to insult a rival who kept a mine of chocolate-drops in his waistcoat-pocket, and a watch that could be taken out of its case and freely shaken by ardent admirers.", "하지만 마음이 아프면서도 그 괴로움을 감추었으니, 조끼 주머니에 초콜릿 사탕 광맥을 품고 다니고, 케이스에서 꺼내 열렬한 숭배자들이 마음껏 흔들어 볼 수 있는 회중시계를 가진 연적을 차마 모욕할 마음이 들지 않았던 것이다."]], [["Some persons might have considered these pleasing liberties as bribes; but Demi didn't see it in that light, and continued to patronize the \"bear-man\"", "어떤 사람들은 이런 기분 좋은 호의를 뇌물로 여겼을지도 모른다. 하지만 데미는 그렇게 보지 않았고, 우수에 잠긴 듯 너그러운 태도로 \"곰 아저씨\"를 계속 후원했다."], ["with pensive affability, while Daisy bestowed her small affections upon him at the third call, and considered his shoulder her throne, his arm her refuge, his gifts treasures of surpassing worth.", "한편 데이지는 세 번째 부름 만에 그 작은 애정을 그에게 바쳤고, 그의 어깨를 자기 옥좌로, 그의 팔을 자기 피난처로, 그의 선물을 더없이 값진 보물로 여겼다. 신사들은 더러 자기가 마음을 둔 여인의 어린 친척들에게 별안간 발작적인 애정을 쏟기도 한다."], ["Gentlemen are sometimes seized with sudden fits of admiration for the young relatives of ladies whom they honor with their regard; but this counterfeit philoprogenitiveness sits uneasily upon them, and does not deceive anybody a particle.", "하지만 그런 거짓 자식 사랑은 그들에게 어색하게 들러붙어 있을 뿐, 조금도 누구를 속이지 못한다. 그러나 베어 씨의 애정은 진심이었고, 더욱이 효과적이었다."], ["Mr. Bhaer's devotion was sincere, however likewise effective,--for honesty is the best policy in love as in law; he was one of the men who are at home with children, and looked particularly well when little faces made a pleasant contrast with his manly one.", "사랑에서도 법에서와 마찬가지로 정직이 최선의 방책이니까."], ["His business, whatever it was, detained him from day to day, but evening seldom failed to bring him out to see--well, he always asked for Mr.", "그는 아이들과 편안하게 어울리는 부류의 사람이었고, 어린 얼굴들이 그의 사내다운 얼굴과 보기 좋은 대비를 이룰 때면 유난히 멋져 보였다. 그의 볼일은, 그게 무엇이든, 날이면 날마다 그를 붙들어 두었지만, 저녁이면 그는 어김없이 길을 나서 와서—음, 그는 늘 마치 씨를 찾았으니, 짐작건대 그분이 그를 끌어당기는 사람이었나 보다."], ["March, so I suppose _he_ was the attraction.", "인자한 아빠는 자기가 그 사람이라는 착각에 빠져, 마음이 통하는 벗과 긴 토론을 나누며 즐거워했다."], ["The excellent papa labored under the delusion that he was, and revelled in long discussions with the kindred spirit, till a chance remark of his more observing grandson suddenly enlightened him.", "그러던 어느 날, 좀 더 눈치 빠른 손자의 무심한 한마디가 별안간 그의 눈을 뜨게 해 주기 전까지는."]], [["Mr. Bhaer came in one evening to pause on the threshold of the study, astonished by the spectacle that met his eye.", "어느 날 저녁 베어 씨가 들어서다가 서재 문턱에서 멈춰 서서는, 눈앞에 펼쳐진 광경에 깜짝 놀랐다."], ["Prone upon the floor lay Mr. March, with his respectable legs in the air, and beside him, likewise prone, was Demi, trying to imitate the attitude with his own short, scarlet-stockinged legs, both grovellers so seriously absorbed that they were unconscious of spectators, till Mr.", "마치 씨가 그 점잖은 다리를 허공에 치켜든 채 바닥에 엎드려 있었고, 그 옆에는 역시나 엎드린 데미가 자기의 짧고 새빨간 양말을 신은 다리로 그 자세를 흉내 내려 애쓰고 있었다."], ["Bhaer laughed his sonorous laugh, and Jo cried out, with a scandalized face,--", "두 엎드린 자는 어찌나 진지하게 몰두해 있던지 구경꾼이 있는 줄도 몰랐는데, 베어 씨가 그 우렁찬 웃음을 터뜨리고 조가 어처구니없다는 얼굴로 소리치고 나서야 알아차렸다."]], [["\"Father, father, here's the Professor!\"", "\"아버지, 아버지, 교수님 오셨어요!\""]], [["Down went the black legs and up came the gray head, as the preceptor said, with undisturbed dignity,--", "검은 다리가 내려가고 희끗한 머리가 올라오는 동안, 스승은 흐트러짐 없는 위엄으로 말했다."]], [["\"Good evening, Mr. Bhaer.", "\"안녕하시오, 베어 선생."], ["Excuse me for a moment; we are just finishing our lesson.", "잠시만 실례하겠소. 지금 막 수업을 끝내는 참이라."], ["Now, Demi, make the letter and tell its name.\"", "자, 데미, 글자를 만들고 그 이름을 말해 보렴.\""]], [["\"I knows him!\"", "\"나 이거 알아요!\""], ["and, after a few convulsive efforts, the red legs took the shape of a pair of compasses, and the intelligent pupil triumphantly shouted, \"It's a We, dranpa, it's a We!\"", "그렇게 외치고는 몇 차례 안간힘을 쓴 끝에 빨간 다리가 컴퍼스 한 쌍 모양이 되더니, 영리한 제자가 의기양양하게 소리쳤다. \"이건 더블유예요, 할아버지, 더블유라고요!\""]], [["\"He's a born Weller,\"", "\"타고난 웰러로구나.\" 아버지가 몸을 추스르고 조카가 학교가 끝난 기쁨을 표현할 유일한 방법이라는 듯 물구나무서기를 시도하자, 조가 웃으며 말했다."], ["laughed Jo, as her parent gathered himself up, and her nephew tried to stand on his head, as the only mode of expressing his satisfaction that school was over.", "* 웰러: 「픽윅 클럽 여행기」의 등장인물 새뮤얼 웰러를 빗댄 말장난. 그는 자기 이름을 'V'로 발음했다 : 옮긴이."]], [["\"What have you been at to-day, bübchen?\"", "\"오늘은 무얼 했니, 꼬마야?\""], ["asked Mr. Bhaer, picking up the gymnast.", "베어 씨가 그 작은 체조 선수를 안아 올리며 물었다."]], [["\"Me went to see little Mary.\"", "\"꼬마 메리 보러 갔어요.\""]], [["\"And what did you there?\"", "\"가서는 무얼 했고?\""]], [["\"I kissed her,\"", "\"그 애한테 뽀뽀했어요.\""], ["began Demi, with artless frankness.", "데미가 천진하고 솔직하게 입을 떼었다."]], [["\"Prut!", "\"저런!"], ["thou beginnest early.", "일찌감치 시작하는구나."], ["What did the little Mary say to that?\" asked Mr.", "그 꼬마 메리는 거기에 뭐라고 하더냐?\""], ["Bhaer, continuing to confess the young sinner, who stood upon his knee, exploring the waistcoat-pocket.", "베어 씨가 물으며, 자기 무릎에 서서 조끼 주머니를 탐색하던 어린 죄인의 고백을 계속 들었다."]], [["\"Oh, she liked it, and she kissed me, and I liked it.", "\"아, 메리도 좋아했어요. 메리도 나한테 뽀뽀했고, 나도 좋았어요."], ["_Don't_ little boys like little girls?\"", "사내아이들은 여자아이들 좋아하잖아요?\""], ["added Demi, with his mouth full, and an air of bland satisfaction.", "데미가 입을 가득 채운 채, 흐뭇하고 부드러운 표정으로 덧붙였다."]], [["\"You precocious chick!", "\"이 조숙한 병아리 같으니!"], ["Who put that into your head?\"", "누가 그런 생각을 머릿속에 넣어 줬니?\""], ["said Jo, enjoying the innocent revelations as much as the Professor.", "조가 교수만큼이나 그 천진한 고백을 즐기며 물었다."]], [["\"'Tisn't in mine head; it's in mine mouf,\"", "\"그건 머릿속에 없어요. 내 입속에 있어요.\""], ["answered literal Demi, putting out his tongue, with a chocolate-drop on it, thinking she alluded to confectionery, not ideas.", "곧이곧대로 받아들이는 데미가, 그녀가 생각이 아니라 사탕을 말하는 줄 알고 초콜릿 사탕을 올려놓은 혀를 쑥 내밀며 대답했다."]], [["\"Thou shouldst save some for the little friend: sweets to the sweet, mannling;\"", "\"꼬마 친구한테 좀 남겨 줘야지. 달콤한 건 달콤한 이에게, 꼬마 양반.\""], ["and Mr. Bhaer offered Jo some, with a look that made her wonder if chocolate was not the nectar drunk by the gods.", "그렇게 말하며 베어 씨가 조에게도 좀 권했는데, 그 눈빛에 조는 초콜릿이야말로 신들이 마시던 신주(神酒)가 아닐까 싶을 정도였다."], ["Demi also saw the smile, was impressed by it, and artlessly inquired,--\n\"Do great boys like great girls, too, 'Fessor?\"", "데미도 그 미소를 보았고, 거기에 마음이 움직여 천연덕스럽게 물었다."]], [["Like young Washington, Mr. Bhaer \"couldn't tell a lie;\" so he gave the somewhat vague reply that he believed they did sometimes, in a tone that made Mr. March put down his clothes-brush, glance at Jo's retiring face, and then sink into his chair, looking as if the \"precocious chick\" had put an idea into _his_ head that was both sweet and sour. Why Dodo, when she caught him in the china-closet half an hour afterward, nearly squeezed the breath out of his little body with a tender embrace, instead of shaking him for being there, and why she followed up this novel performance by the unexpected gift of a big slice of bread and jelly, remained one of the problems over which Demi puzzled his small wits, and was forced to leave unsolved forever.", "\"교수님, 큰 남자아이들도 큰 여자아이들 좋아해요?\""]], [["# UNDER THE UMBRELLA.", "# UNDER THE UMBRELLA."]], [["While Laurie and Amy were taking conjugal strolls over velvet carpets, as they set their house in order, and planned a blissful future, Mr. Bhaer and Jo were enjoying promenades of a different sort, along muddy roads and sodden fields.", "어린 워싱턴처럼 베어 씨는 \"거짓말을 할 줄 몰랐다.\""], ["\"I always do take a walk toward evening, and I don't know why I should give it up, just because I often happen to meet the Professor on his way out,\" said Jo to herself, after two or three encounters; for, though there were two paths to Meg's, whichever one she took she was sure to meet him, either going or returning.", "그래서 가끔은 그러는 것 같다고 다소 모호하게 대답했는데, 그 어조 때문에 마치 씨는 옷솔을 내려놓고, 물러나는 조의 얼굴을 흘끗 보더니 의자에 푹 주저앉았다."], ["He was always walking rapidly, and never seemed to see her till quite close, when he would look as if his short-sighted eyes had failed to recognize the approaching lady till that moment. Then, if she was going to Meg's, he always had something for the babies; if her face was turned homeward, he had merely strolled down to see the river, and was just about returning, unless they were tired of his frequent calls.", "마치 \"조숙한 병아리\"가 달콤하면서도 시큼한 어떤 생각을 자기 머릿속에 집어넣기라도 한 듯한 표정으로."]], [["Under the circumstances, what could Jo do but greet him civilly, and invite him in? If she _was_ tired of his visits, she concealed her weariness with perfect skill, and took care that there should be coffee for supper, \"as Friedrich--I mean Mr.", "반 시간쯤 뒤에 도도가 도자기 찬장에서 데미를 붙잡았을 때, 거기 있었다고 흔들어 대는 대신 다정한 포옹으로 그 작은 몸의 숨이 거의 막힐 만큼 꼭 끌어안은 까닭은 무엇이었을까."], ["Bhaer--doesn't like tea.\" By the second week, every one knew perfectly well what was going on, yet every one tried to look as if they were stone-blind to the changes in Jo's face.", "또 그 색다른 행동에 이어 큼직한 잼 바른 빵 한 조각을 뜻밖에 선물한 까닭은 무엇이었을까."], ["They never asked why she sang about her work, did up her hair three times a day, and got so blooming with her evening exercise; and no one seemed to have the slightest suspicion that Professor Bhaer, while talking philosophy with the father, was giving the daughter lessons in love.", "그건 데미가 그 작은 머리를 굴리며 풀어 보려 애쓰다가, 끝내 영영 풀지 못한 채 남겨 둘 수밖에 없었던 수수께끼 가운데 하나로 남았다."]], [["Jo couldn't even lose her heart in a decorous manner, but sternly tried to quench her feelings; and, failing to do so, led a somewhat agitated life. She was mortally afraid of being laughed at for surrendering, after her many and vehement declarations of independence. Laurie was her especial dread; but, thanks to the new manager, he behaved with praiseworthy propriety, never called Mr. Bhaer \"a capital old fellow\" in public, never alluded, in the remotest manner, to Jo's improved appearance, or expressed the least surprise at seeing the Professor's hat on the Marches' hall-table nearly every evening. But he exulted in private and longed for the time to come when he could give Jo a piece of plate, with a bear and a ragged staff on it as an appropriate coat-of-arms. For a fortnight, the Professor came and went with lover-like regularity; then he stayed away for three whole days, and made no sign,--a proceeding which caused everybody to look sober, and Jo to become pensive, at first, and then--alas for romance!--very cross.", "로리와 에이미가 보드라운 양탄자 위를 부부답게 거닐며 집을 정돈하고 더없이 행복한 미래를 그려 가는 동안, 베어 씨와 조는 진흙길과 질척한 들판을 따라 그와는 사뭇 다른 산책을 즐기고 있었다."]], [["\"Disgusted, I dare say, and gone home as suddenly as he came.", "\"난 늘 저녁 무렵에 산책을 하는데, 마침 교수님이 나오시는 길에 자주 마주친다고 해서 그걸 그만둘 이유는 없잖아.\""], ["It's nothing to me, of course; but I _should_ think he would have come and bid us good-by, like a gentleman,\"", "두세 차례 마주친 뒤 조가 속으로 중얼거렸다. 메그네로 가는 길이 두 갈래 있었지만, 어느 쪽으로 가든 그녀는 오가는 길에 영락없이 그와 마주쳤던 것이다."], ["she said to herself, with a despairing look at the gate, as she put on her things for the customary walk, one dull afternoon.", "그는 늘 빠르게 걷다가, 아주 가까이 와서야 그녀를 알아보는 것 같았다."], ["\"You'd better take the little umbrella, dear; it looks like rain,\"", "그제야 근시인 눈이 다가오는 숙녀를 그 순간까지 못 알아본 듯한 표정을 짓는 것이었다."], ["said her mother, observing that she had on her new bonnet, but not alluding to the fact.", "그러고 나서 그녀가 메그네로 가는 길이면 그는 늘 아기들 줄 것을 가지고 있었고, 그녀가 집 쪽으로 가는 길이면 그는 그저 강 구경을 하러 내려왔다가 막 돌아가려던 참이라고 했다. 자기가 자주 들르는 게 식구들이 지겹지 않다면 말이다."]], [["\"Yes, Marmee; do you want anything in town? I've got to run in and get some paper,\"", "이런 상황에서 조가 그에게 정중히 인사하고 안으로 들이는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["returned Jo, pulling out the bow under her chin before the glass as an excuse for not looking at her mother. \"Yes; I want some twilled silesia, a paper of number nine needles, and two yards of narrow lavender ribbon.", "설령 그의 방문이 지겨웠다 해도 조는 그 싫증을 완벽한 솜씨로 감추었고, 저녁 식사에는 커피가 빠지지 않도록 신경 썼다."], ["Have you got your thick boots on, and something warm under your cloak?\"", "\"프리드리히가—아니, 베어 선생님이 차를 안 좋아하시니까.\""]], [["\"If you happen to meet Mr. Bhaer, bring him home to tea.", "둘째 주가 되자 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 다들 훤히 알게 되었지만, 그래도 다들 조의 얼굴에 일어나는 변화에 까막눈인 척하려 애썼다."], ["I quite long to see the dear man,\" added Mrs.", "조가 왜 일하면서 노래를 흥얼거리는지, 왜 하루에 세 번이나 머리를 매만지는지, 저녁 운동을 하고 와서 왜 그토록 발그레하게 피어나는지, 아무도 묻지 않았다."], ["March. Jo heard _that_, but made no answer, except to kiss her mother, and walk rapidly away, thinking with a glow of gratitude, in spite of her heartache,--", "그리고 베어 교수가 아버지와 철학을 논하는 사이에 딸에게는 사랑을 가르치고 있다는 걸 조금이라도 눈치챈 사람은 아무도 없는 듯했다."]], [["\"How good she is to me! What _do_ girls do who haven't any mothers to help them through their troubles?\"", "조는 점잖게 마음을 빼앗기지조차 못하고, 그 감정을 엄하게 억누르려 애쓰다가, 그러지 못한 끝에 다소 마음이 들뜬 나날을 보냈다."], ["The dry-goods stores were not down among the counting-houses, banks, and wholesale warerooms, where gentlemen most do congregate; but Jo found herself in that part of the city before she did a single errand, loitering along as if waiting for some one, examining engineering instruments in one window and samples of wool in another with most unfeminine interest; tumbling over barrels, being half-smothered by descending bales, and hustled unceremoniously by busy men who looked as if they wondered \"how the deuce she got there.\"", "그토록 여러 차례 열렬히 독립을 선언해 놓고 항복했다고 비웃음당할까 봐 죽도록 겁이 났던 것이다."], ["A drop of rain on her cheek recalled her thoughts from baffled hopes to ruined ribbons; for the drops continued to fall, and, being a woman as well as a lover, she felt that, though it was too late to save her heart, she might her bonnet.", "그중에서도 로리가 가장 두려웠다. 하지만 새 감독* 덕분에 로리는 칭찬받아 마땅할 만큼 점잖게 처신했다."], ["Now she remembered the little umbrella, which she had forgotten to take in her hurry to be off; but regret was unavailing, and nothing could be done but borrow one or submit to a drenching.", "사람들 앞에서 베어 씨를 \"멋진 노인장\"이라고 부르지 않았고, 조의 달라진 외모를 조금도 입에 올리지 않았으며, 거의 매일 저녁 마치 집안 현관 탁자에 놓인 교수의 모자를 보고도 털끝만큼의 놀라움도 내비치지 않았다."], ["She looked up at the lowering sky, down at the crimson bow already flecked with black, forward along the muddy street, then one long, lingering look behind, at a certain grimy warehouse, with \"Hoffmann, Swartz, & Co.\" over the door, and said to herself, with a sternly reproachful air,--", "다만 속으로는 의기양양해하며, 곰과 울퉁불퉁한 지팡이를 어울리는 문장(紋章)으로 새긴 은쟁반을 조에게 선물할 날이 오기를 고대했다."]], [["\"It serves me right! What business had I to put on all my best things and come philandering down here, hoping to see the Professor? Jo, I'm ashamed of you! No, you shall _not_ go there to borrow an umbrella, or find out where he is, from his friends. You shall trudge away, and do your errands in the rain; and if you catch your death and ruin your bonnet, it's no more than you deserve. Now then!\" With that she rushed across the street so impetuously that she narrowly escaped annihilation from a passing truck, and precipitated herself into the arms of a stately old gentleman, who said, \"I beg pardon, ma'am,\" and looked mortally offended. Somewhat daunted, Jo righted herself, spread her handkerchief over the devoted ribbons, and, putting temptation behind her, hurried on, with increasing dampness about the ankles, and much clashing of umbrellas overhead. The fact that a somewhat dilapidated blue one remained stationary above the unprotected bonnet, attracted her attention; and, looking up, she saw Mr. Bhaer looking down.", "* 새 감독: 로리의 아내가 된 에이미를 가리킴 : 옮긴이."]], [["\"I feel to know the strong-minded lady who goes so bravely under many horse-noses, and so fast through much mud.", "두 주 동안 교수는 연인다운 규칙성으로 오갔다. 그러다 사흘을 내리 발길을 끊고 아무런 기별도 없었다."], ["What do you down here, my friend?\" \"I'm shopping.\"", "그 일로 다들 침울한 표정이 되었고, 조는 처음엔 수심에 잠기더니, 그다음엔—아아, 가엾은 낭만이여!—몹시 심술궂어졌다."]], [["Mr. Bhaer smiled, as he glanced from the pickle-factory on one side, to the wholesale hide and leather concern on the other; but he only said politely,--\n\"You haf no umbrella.", "\"질려서 왔을 때처럼 갑자기 집으로 가 버린 거겠지."], ["May I go also, and take for you the bundles?\"", "물론 나야 아무 상관도 없지만, 그래도 신사답게 와서 작별 인사쯤은 하고 갈 줄 알았는데.\" 어느 음울한 오후, 조가 으레 하는 산책에 나서려고 옷을 차려입으며, 대문을 절망스럽게 바라보며 속으로 중얼거렸다."]], [["Jo's cheeks were as red as her ribbon, and she wondered what he thought of her; but she didn't care, for in a minute she found herself walking away arm-in-arm with her Professor, feeling as if the sun had suddenly burst out with uncommon brilliancy, that the world was all right again, and that one thoroughly happy woman was paddling through the wet that day.", "\"작은 우산을 가져가는 게 좋겠다, 얘야. 비가 올 것 같구나.\""], ["\"We thought you had gone,\" said Jo hastily, for she knew he was looking at her.", "어머니가 말했다."], ["Her bonnet wasn't big enough to hide her face, and she feared he might think the joy it betrayed unmaidenly.", "조가 새 보닛을 쓴 것을 알아챘지만 그 점은 입에 올리지 않은 채."]], [["\"Did you believe that I should go with no farewell to those who haf been so heavenly kind to me?\"", "\"네, 엄마. 시내에서 뭐 필요한 거 있어요?"], ["he asked so reproachfully that she felt as if she had insulted him by the suggestion, and answered heartily,--\n\"No, _I_ didn't; I knew you were busy about your own affairs, but we rather missed you,--father and mother especially.\"", "종이를 좀 사러 들어가야 하거든요.\" 조가 대답하며, 어머니를 보지 않으려는 핑계로 거울 앞에서 턱 밑의 리본을 잡아당겼다."]], [["\"I'm always glad to see you, sir.\"", "\"그래."], ["In her anxiety to keep her voice quite calm, Jo made it rather cool, and the frosty little monosyllable at the end seemed to chill the Professor, for his smile vanished, as he said gravely,--", "능직 무명천이랑, 9호 바늘 한 봉지랑, 가느다란 라벤더색 리본 2야드만 사다 주렴. 두꺼운 부츠는 신었고, 망토 안에 따뜻한 거 입었니?\""]], [["\"I haf no longer any business here; it is done.\"", "\"그런 것 같아요.\" 조가 건성으로 대답했다."]], [["\"Successfully, I hope?\" said Jo, for the bitterness of disappointment was in that short reply of his.", "\"혹시 베어 선생님을 만나거든, 차 마시러 집으로 모시고 오렴."], ["\"I ought to think so, for I haf a way opened to me by which I can make my bread and gif my Jünglings much help.\"", "그 다정한 양반이 정말 보고 싶구나.\" 마치 부인이 덧붙였다."]], [["\"Tell me, please! I like to know all about the--the boys,\" said Jo eagerly. \"That is so kind, I gladly tell you. My friends find for me a place in a college, where I teach as at home, and earn enough to make the way smooth for Franz and Emil. For this I should be grateful, should I not?\"", "조는 그 말을 들었지만 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고, 그저 어머니에게 입을 맞추고는 빠르게 걸어 나갔다. 가슴이 아픈 와중에도 고마움에 가슴이 뜨거워지며 이렇게 생각하면서."]], [["\"Indeed you should. How splendid it will be to have you doing what you like, and be able to see you often, and the boys!\" cried Jo, clinging to the lads as an excuse for the satisfaction she could not help betraying. \"Ah! but we shall not meet often, I fear; this place is at the West.\"", "\"엄마는 나한테 어쩌면 이렇게 잘해 주실까! 힘든 일을 겪을 때 곁에서 도와줄 엄마가 없는 여자아이들은 대체 어떻게 살아갈까?\""]], [["\"So far away!\" and Jo left her skirts to their fate, as if it didn't matter now what became of her clothes or herself.", "포목점들은 신사들이 가장 많이 모여드는 회계 사무소며 은행이며 도매 창고들 사이에 있지 않았다."], ["Mr. Bhaer could read several languages, but he had not learned to read women yet.", "그런데도 조는 심부름 한 가지 보기도 전에 시내의 바로 그 구역에 와 있었다."], ["He flattered himself that he knew Jo pretty well, and was, therefore, much amazed by the contradictions of voice, face, and manner, which she showed him in rapid succession that day, for she was in half a dozen different moods in the course of half an hour.", "누군가를 기다리기라도 하듯 어슬렁대면서, 한쪽 진열창에서는 측량 기구를, 다른 진열창에서는 양모 견본을 여자답지 않은 관심으로 들여다보았다. 통에 걸려 비틀거리고, 내려오는 짐짝에 깔려 숨이 막힐 뻔하고, \"도대체 어쩌다 여기 온 거야\""], ["When she met him she looked surprised, though it was impossible to help suspecting that she had come for that express purpose.", "하고 의아해하는 듯한 바쁜 남자들에게 거리낌 없이 떠밀리면서. 뺨에 떨어진 빗방울 한 줄기가 그녀의 생각을 어그러진 희망에서 망가진 리본으로 되돌려 놓았다."], ["When he offered her his arm, she took it with a look that filled him with delight; but when he asked if she missed him, she gave such a chilly, formal reply that despair fell upon him.", "빗방울이 계속 떨어졌고, 그녀는 사랑에 빠진 여자이기도 했으므로, 마음을 구하기엔 너무 늦었어도 보닛만은 구할 수 있을지 모른다고 느꼈던 것이다. 그제야 조는 작은 우산을 떠올렸다."], ["On learning his good fortune she almost clapped her hands: was the joy all for the boys?", "서둘러 나오느라 챙기는 걸 깜빡한 것이었다. 하지만 후회해 봐야 소용없었고, 우산을 빌리거나 흠뻑 젖는 걸 감수하는 수밖에 도리가 없었다."], ["Then, on hearing his destination, she said, \"So far away!\"", "그녀는 잔뜩 찌푸린 하늘을 올려다보고, 이미 검은 얼룩이 점점이 진 진홍색 리본을 내려다보고, 진창인 거리를 앞으로 내다보더니, 마지막으로 어떤 거뭇한 창고를 한참 동안 미련스레 뒤돌아보았다."], ["in a tone of despair that lifted him on to a pinnacle of hope; but the next minute she tumbled him down again by observing, like one entirely absorbed in the matter,--", "문 위에 \"호프만, 스워츠 상회\"라고 적힌 그 창고를. 그러고는 자기를 엄하게 나무라는 투로 중얼거렸다."]], [["\"Here's the place for my errands; will you come in? It won't take long.\"", "\"꼴좋다!"], ["Jo rather prided herself upon her shopping capabilities, and particularly wished to impress her escort with the neatness and despatch with which she would accomplish the business.", "가장 좋은 옷을 다 차려입고 교수님을 볼까 하는 마음에 여기까지 어슬렁대며 내려온 게 잘한 짓이람? 조, 너 정말 부끄러운 줄 알아!"], ["But, owing to the flutter she was in, everything went amiss; she upset the tray of needles, forgot the silesia was to be \"twilled\"", "안 돼, 우산을 빌리거나 그분 친구들한테서 그분이 어디 있는지 알아내려고 저기 들어가면 절대 안 돼."], ["till it was cut off, gave the wrong change, and covered herself with confusion by asking for lavender ribbon at the calico counter. Mr.", "빗속을 터덜터덜 걸어가서 심부름이나 하는 거야."], ["Bhaer stood by, watching her blush and blunder; and, as he watched, his own bewilderment seemed to subside, for he was beginning to see that on some occasions women, like dreams, go by contraries.", "그러다 죽을병에 걸리고 보닛을 망친대도, 그건 네가 당해도 싼 일이야. 자, 그럼!\""]], [["When they came out, he put the parcel under his arm with a more cheerful aspect, and splashed through the puddles as if he rather enjoyed it, on the whole. \"Should we not do a little what you call shopping for the babies, and haf a farewell feast to-night if I go for my last call at your so pleasant home?\" he asked, stopping before a window full of fruit and flowers.", "그렇게 말하고는 어찌나 거세게 길을 가로질러 내달렸던지, 지나가던 짐마차에 치여 박살 날 뻔한 걸 가까스로 피하면서 어느 위엄 있는 노신사의 품으로 곤두박질치고 말았다. 노신사는 \"실례하오, 부인\" 하고는 몹시 기분이 상한 표정을 지었다. 다소 기가 죽은 조는 몸을 바로 세우고, 그 가엾은 리본 위에 손수건을 펼쳐 덮고는, 유혹을 뒤로한 채 서둘러 걸음을 옮겼다. 발목께가 점점 더 축축해지고 머리 위로는 우산들이 요란하게 부딪히는 가운데. 보호받지 못하는 보닛 위로 어딘가 낡아빠진 파란 우산 하나가 가만히 머물러 있다는 사실이 그녀의 주의를 끌었다. 올려다보니 베어 씨가 내려다보고 있었다."]], [["\"What will we buy?\" said Jo, ignoring the latter part of his speech, and sniffing the mingled odors with an affectation of delight as they went in. \"May they haf oranges and figs?\" asked Mr. Bhaer, with a paternal air.", "\"이렇게 숱한 말 코앞을 용감히 헤치고, 이렇게 많은 진창을 빠르게 지나가는 심지 굳은 숙녀를 알아보겠소. 여기서 무얼 하시오, 나의 친구?\""]], [["\"Hamburg grapes; yes, we shall surely drink to the Fatherland in those?\" Jo frowned upon that piece of extravagance, and asked why he didn't buy a frail of dates, a cask of raisins, and a bag of almonds, and done with it? Whereat Mr. Bhaer confiscated her purse, produced his own, and finished the marketing by buying several pounds of grapes, a pot of rosy daisies, and a pretty jar of honey, to be regarded in the light of a demijohn. Then, distorting his pockets with the knobby bundles, and giving her the flowers to hold, he put up the old umbrella, and they travelled on again.", "\"쇼핑하는 중이에요.\""]], [["\"Miss Marsch, I haf a great favor to ask of you,\" began the Professor, after a moist promenade of half a block.", "베어 씨는 한쪽의 피클 공장에서 다른 쪽의 가죽 도매상으로 눈길을 옮기며 미소 지었지만, 그저 정중하게 말할 뿐이었다."]], [["\"Yes, sir;\" and Jo's heart began to beat so hard she was afraid he would hear it.", "\"우산이 없구려."], ["\"I am bold to say it in spite of the rain, because so short a time remains to me.\"", "저도 같이 가서 짐을 좀 들어드려도 되겠소?\""]], [["\"Yes, sir;\" and Jo nearly crushed the small flower-pot with the sudden squeeze she gave it. \"I wish to get a little dress for my Tina, and I am too stupid to go alone. Will you kindly gif me a word of taste and help?\"", "\"네, 고마워요.\""]], [["\"Yes, sir;\" and Jo felt as calm and cool, all of a sudden, as if she had stepped into a refrigerator.", "조의 뺨은 리본만큼이나 빨개졌고, 그가 자기를 어떻게 생각할까 궁금했다."], ["\"Perhaps also a shawl for Tina's mother, she is so poor and sick, and the husband is such a care.", "하지만 상관없었다. 다음 순간 그녀는 교수와 팔짱을 끼고 걷고 있었으니까."], ["Yes, yes, a thick, warm shawl would be a friendly thing to take the little mother.\"", "별안간 해가 유난히 눈부시게 터져 나와 세상이 다시 온전해진 듯했고, 더없이 행복한 여자 하나가 그날 빗속을 첨벙첨벙 걸어가고 있는 듯한 기분이 들었다."]], [["\"I'll do it with pleasure, Mr. Bhaer.", "\"우린 선생님이 가 버리신 줄 알았어요.\""], ["I'm going very fast and he's getting dearer every minute,\"", "조가 황급히 말했다."], ["added Jo to herself; then, with a mental shake, she entered into the business with an energy which was pleasant to behold. Mr.", "그가 자기를 보고 있다는 걸 알았기 때문이다."], ["Bhaer left it all to her, so she chose a pretty gown for Tina, and then ordered out the shawls. The clerk, being a married man, condescended to take an interest in the couple, who appeared to be shopping for their family.", "보닛이 얼굴을 가릴 만큼 크지 않아서, 거기 드러난 기쁨을 그가 처녀답지 못하다고 여길까 봐 두려웠다."]], [["\"Your lady may prefer this; it's a superior article, a most desirable color, quite chaste and genteel,\"", "\"그토록 천국같이 친절히 대해 준 분들께 작별 인사도 없이 떠나리라 여기셨소?\""], ["he said, shaking out a comfortable gray shawl, and throwing it over Jo's shoulders.", "그가 어찌나 나무라는 투로 묻던지, 조는 그런 말로 그를 모욕한 듯한 기분이 들어 진심으로 대답했다."]], [["\"Does this suit you, Mr.", "\"아니요, 그렇게 생각 안 했어요."], ["Bhaer?\"", "선생님이 선생님 일로 바쁘신 줄 알았어요."], ["she asked, turning her back to him, and feeling deeply grateful for the chance of hiding her face.", "그래도 우린 선생님이 좀 그리웠어요. 특히 아버지랑 어머니가요.\""]], [["\"Excellently well; we will haf it,\" answered the Professor, smiling to himself as he paid for it, while Jo continued to rummage the counters like a confirmed bargain-hunter. \"Now shall we go home?\" he asked, as if the words were very pleasant to him.", "\"그럼 그대는?\""]], [["\"Yes; it's late, and I'm _so_ tired.\" Jo's voice was more pathetic than she knew; for now the sun seemed to have gone in as suddenly as it came out, the world grew muddy and miserable again, and for the first time she discovered that her feet were cold, her head ached, and that her heart was colder than the former, fuller of pain than the latter. Mr. Bhaer was going away; he only cared for her as a friend; it was all a mistake, and the sooner it was over the better. With this idea in her head, she hailed an approaching omnibus with such a hasty gesture that the daisies flew out of the pot and were badly damaged. \"This is not our omniboos,\" said the Professor, waving the loaded vehicle away, and stopping to pick up the poor little flowers.", "\"전 늘 선생님을 뵈면 반가워요.\""]], [["\"I beg your pardon, I didn't see the name distinctly. Never mind, I can walk. I'm used to plodding in the mud,\" returned Jo, winking hard, because she would have died rather than openly wipe her eyes. Mr. Bhaer saw the drops on her cheeks, though she turned her head away; the sight seemed to touch him very much, for, suddenly stooping down, he asked in a tone that meant a great deal,--", "목소리를 아주 침착하게 유지하려고 애쓰다 보니, 조의 말투는 오히려 다소 쌀쌀맞아졌고, 끝에 붙은 그 차가운 짧은 한마디가 교수의 마음을 얼어붙게 한 듯했다. 그의 미소가 사라졌고, 그는 진지하게 말했다."]], [["\"Heart's dearest, why do you cry?\"", "\"고맙소."], ["Now, if Jo had not been new to this sort of thing she would have said she wasn't crying, had a cold in her head, or told any other feminine fib proper to the occasion; instead of which that undignified creature answered, with an irrepressible sob,--", "떠나기 전에 한 번 더 들르겠소.\""]], [["\"Because you are going away.\"", "\"그럼 정말 가시는 거예요?\""]], [["\"Ach, mein Gott, that is _so_ good!\" cried Mr. Bhaer, managing to clasp his hands in spite of the umbrella and the bundles. \"Jo, I haf nothing but much love to gif you; I came to see if you could care for it, and I waited to be sure that I was something more than a friend. Am I? Can you make a little place in your heart for old Fritz?\" he added, all in one breath. \"Oh, yes!\" said Jo; and he was quite satisfied, for she folded both hands over his arm, and looked up at him with an expression that plainly showed how happy she would be to walk through life beside him, even though she had no better shelter than the old umbrella, if he carried it.", "\"여기엔 이제 볼일이 없소. 다 끝났으니.\""]], [["It was certainly proposing under difficulties, for, even if he had desired to do so, Mr. Bhaer could not go down upon his knees, on account of the mud; neither could he offer Jo his hand, except figuratively, for both were full; much less could he indulge in tender demonstrations in the open street, though he was near it: so the only way in which he could express his rapture was to look at her, with an expression which glorified his face to such a degree that there actually seemed to be little rainbows in the drops that sparkled on his beard. If he had not loved Jo very much, I don't think he could have done it _then_, for she looked far from lovely, with her skirts in a deplorable state, her rubber boots splashed to the ankle, and her bonnet a ruin. Fortunately, Mr. Bhaer considered her the most beautiful woman living, and she found him more \"Jove-like\" than ever, though his hat-brim was quite limp with the little rills trickling thence upon his shoulders (for he held the umbrella all over Jo), and every finger of his gloves needed mending. Passers-by probably thought them a pair of harmless lunatics, for they entirely forgot to hail a 'bus, and strolled leisurely along, oblivious of deepening dusk and fog. Little they cared what anybody thought, for they were enjoying the happy hour that seldom comes but once in any life, the magical moment which bestows youth on the old, beauty on the plain, wealth on the poor, and gives human hearts a foretaste of heaven. The Professor looked as if he had conquered a kingdom, and the world had nothing more to offer him in the way of bliss; while Jo trudged beside him, feeling as if her place had always been there, and wondering how she ever could have chosen any other lot. Of course, she was the first to speak--intelligibly, I mean, for the emotional remarks which followed her impetuous \"Oh, yes!\" were not of a coherent or reportable character.", "\"잘되셨기를 바라요.\" 조가 말했다. 그의 그 짧은 대답에 실망의 씁쓸함이 배어 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"Friedrich, why didn't you--\"", "\"그래야겠지요."], ["\"Ah, heaven, she gifs me the name that no one speaks since Minna died!\"", "빵벌이를 하며 내 어린 학생들에게 큰 도움을 줄 수 있는 길이 열렸으니."], ["cried the Professor, pausing in a puddle to regard her with grateful delight.", "이 일을 감사히 여겨야겠지요, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["\"I always call you so to myself--I forgot; but I won't, unless you like it.\"", "\"그럼요, 그러셔야죠. 선생님이 좋아하시는 일을 하시게 되다니 얼마나 멋진 일이에요."], ["\"Like it? it is more sweet to me than I can tell.", "그리고 선생님과 그 아이들을 자주 볼 수 있을 테고요!\""], ["Say 'thou,'", "조가 외쳤다."], ["also, and I shall say your language is almost as beautiful as mine.\"", "도무지 감출 수 없는 기쁨을 그 아이들을 핑계 삼아 둘러대면서."]], [["\"Isn't 'thou' a little sentimental?\"", "\"아!"], ["asked Jo, privately thinking it a lovely monosyllable.", "하지만 우린 자주 못 만날 것 같소. 그곳이 서부라서.\""]], [["\"Sentimental? Yes. Thank Gott, we Germans believe in sentiment, and keep ourselves young mit it. Your English 'you' is so cold, say 'thou,' heart's dearest, it means so much to me,\" pleaded Mr. Bhaer, more like a romantic student than a grave professor.", "\"그렇게 멀리요!\" 조는 이제 옷이든 자기 몸이든 어찌 되든 상관없다는 듯, 치맛자락을 운명에 내맡겨 버렸다."]], [["\"Well, then, why didn't thou tell me all this sooner?\"", "베어 씨는 몇 개 국어를 읽을 줄 알았지만, 아직 여자를 읽는 법은 익히지 못했다. 그는 자기가 조를 꽤 잘 안다고 자부했고, 그래서 그날 조가 잇따라 보여 준 목소리와 표정과 태도의 모순에 무척이나 어리둥절했다."], ["asked Jo bashfully.", "반 시간 사이에 조의 기분이 대여섯 번이나 바뀌었던 것이다."], ["\"Now I shall haf to show thee all my heart, and I so gladly will, because thou must take care of it hereafter.", "그를 만났을 때 조는 놀란 표정이었지만, 바로 그 목적으로 거기 왔으리라는 의심을 떨치기란 불가능했다. 그가 팔을 내밀자 조는 그를 기쁨으로 가득 채우는 눈빛으로 그 팔을 잡았다."], ["See, then, my Jo,--ah, the dear, funny little name!--I had a wish to tell something the day I said good-by, in New York; but I thought the handsome friend was betrothed to thee, and so I spoke not.", "하지만 자기가 그리웠느냐고 묻자 어찌나 쌀쌀맞고 격식 차린 대답을 하던지 그는 절망에 빠졌다. 그의 행운을 듣고는 손뼉이라도 칠 듯했지만—그 기쁨이 다 아이들 때문이었을까?"], ["Wouldst thou have said 'Yes,'", "그러다 그의 행선지를 듣고는 절망스러운 어조로 \"그렇게 멀리요!\"라고 말해 그를 희망의 꼭대기로 들어 올렸다."], ["then, if I _had_ spoken?\"", "하지만 다음 순간 마치 그 일에만 온통 몰두한 사람처럼 이렇게 말해 그를 다시 굴러떨어뜨렸다."]], [["\"I don't know; I'm afraid not, for I didn't have any heart just then.\"", "\"여기가 제 심부름할 가게예요. 들어가실래요? 오래 안 걸릴 거예요.\""]], [["\"Prut! that I do not believe.", "조는 자기 쇼핑 솜씨를 제법 자랑스러워했고, 특히 깔끔하고 신속하게 일을 해치우는 모습으로 동행에게 깊은 인상을 주고 싶었다."], ["It was asleep till the fairy prince came through the wood, and waked it up. Ah, well, 'Die erste Liebe ist die beste;'", "하지만 마음이 들떠 있던 탓에 모든 게 어긋났다."], ["but that I should not expect.\" \"Yes, the first love _is_ the best; so be contented, for I never had another.", "바늘 쟁반을 엎질렀고, 무명천을 잘라 낸 다음에야 \"능직\"으로 해야 했다는 걸 깜빡 떠올렸고, 거스름돈을 잘못 내주었으며, 옥양목 판매대에서 라벤더색 리본을 달라고 해서 스스로를 당황스럽게 만들었다."], ["Teddy was only a boy, and soon got over his little fancy,\"", "베어 씨는 곁에 서서 그녀가 얼굴을 붉히고 실수하는 모습을 지켜보았다. 그렇게 지켜보는 사이, 그의 당혹감도 가라앉는 듯했다."], ["said Jo, anxious to correct the Professor's mistake.", "여자란 어떤 경우에는 꿈과 마찬가지로 거꾸로 풀이된다는 걸 알아차리기 시작했던 것이다."]], [["\"Good! then I shall rest happy, and be sure that thou givest me all. I haf waited so long, I am grown selfish, as thou wilt find, Professorin.\" \"I like that,\" cried Jo, delighted with her new name. \"Now tell me what brought you, at last, just when I most wanted you?\"", "가게를 나서며 그는 한결 밝아진 표정으로 꾸러미를 옆구리에 끼었고, 대체로 보아 오히려 즐거워하는 듯 웅덩이를 첨벙첨벙 밟으며 걸었다."]], [["\"This;\" and Mr.", "\"이른바 쇼핑이라는 걸 아기들을 위해 좀 하고, 내가 그토록 즐거운 그대의 집에 마지막으로 들르게 된다면, 오늘 밤 작별 잔치를 벌이는 게 어떻겠소?\""], ["Bhaer took a little worn paper out of his waistcoat-pocket. Jo unfolded it, and looked much abashed, for it was one of her own contributions to a paper that paid for poetry, which accounted for her sending it an occasional attempt.", "그가 과일과 꽃이 가득한 진열창 앞에 멈춰 서며 물었다."]], [["\"How could that bring you?\" she asked, wondering what he meant.", "\"뭘 살까요?\""], ["\"I found it by chance; I knew it by the names and the initials, and in it there was one little verse that seemed to call me. Read and find him; I will see that you go not in the wet.\"", "조가 그의 말 뒷부분은 못 들은 척하며, 가게 안으로 들어서면서 짐짓 즐거운 듯이 뒤섞인 향기를 들이마시며 물었다."]], [["Jo obeyed, and hastily skimmed through the lines which she had christened--", "\"오렌지랑 무화과 먹어도 되겠소?\" 베어 씨가 아버지다운 태도로 물었다."]], [["\"Four little chests all in a row, Dim with dust, and worn by time, All fashioned and filled, long ago, By children now in their prime. Four little keys hung side by side, With faded ribbons, brave and gay When fastened there, with childish pride, Long ago, on a rainy day. Four little names, one on each lid, Carved out by a boyish hand, And underneath there lieth hid Histories of the happy band Once playing here, and pausing oft To hear the sweet refrain, That came and went on the roof aloft, In the falling summer rain. \"'Meg' on the first lid, smooth and fair. I look in with loving eyes, For folded here, with well-known care, A goodly gathering lies, The record of a peaceful life,-- Gifts to gentle child and girl, A bridal gown, lines to a wife, A tiny shoe, a baby curl. No toys in this first chest remain, For all are carried away, In their old age, to join again In another small Meg's play. Ah, happy mother! well I know You hear, like a sweet refrain, Lullabies ever soft and low In the falling summer rain.", "\"생기면 잘 먹어요.\""]], [["\"'Jo' on the next lid, scratched and worn, And within a motley store Of headless dolls, of school-books torn, Birds and beasts that speak no more; Spoils brought home from the fairy ground Only trod by youthful feet, Dreams of a future never found, Memories of a past still sweet; Half-writ poems, stories wild, April letters, warm and cold, Diaries of a wilful child, Hints of a woman early old; A woman in a lonely home, Hearing, like a sad refrain,-- 'Be worthy love, and love will come,' In the falling summer rain. \"My Beth! the dust is always swept From the lid that bears your name, As if by loving eyes that wept, By careful hands that often came. Death canonized for us one saint, Ever less human than divine, And still we lay, with tender plaint, Relics in this household shrine.-- The silver bell, so seldom rung, The little cap which last she wore, The fair, dead Catherine that hung By angels borne above her door; The songs she sang, without lament, In her prison-house of pain, Forever are they sweetly blent With the falling summer rain.", "\"견과류는 좋아하오?\""]], [["\"Upon the last lid's polished field-- Legend now both fair and true-- A gallant knight bears on his shield, 'Amy,' in letters gold and blue. Within lie snoods that bound her hair, Slippers that have danced their last, Faded flowers laid by with care, Fans whose airy toils are past; Gay valentines, all ardent flames, Trifles that have borne their part In girlish hopes and fears and shames,-- The record of a maiden heart Now learning fairer, truer spells, Hearing, like a blithe refrain, The silver sound of bridal bells In the falling summer rain. \"Four little chests all in a row, Dim with dust, and worn by time, Four women, taught by weal and woe To love and labor in their prime. Four sisters, parted for an hour, None lost, one only gone before, Made by love's immortal power, Nearest and dearest evermore. Oh, when these hidden stores of ours Lie open to the Father's sight, May they be rich in golden hours, Deeds that show fairer for the light, Lives whose brave music long shall ring, Like a spirit-stirring strain, Souls that shall gladly soar and sing In the long sunshine after rain.", "\"다람쥐처럼요.\""]], [["\"It's very bad poetry, but I felt it when I wrote it, one day when I was very lonely, and had a good cry on a rag-bag. I never thought it would go where it could tell tales,\" said Jo, tearing up the verses the Professor had treasured so long. \"Let it go, it has done its duty, and I will haf a fresh one when I read all the brown book in which she keeps her little secrets,\" said Mr. Bhaer, with a smile, as he watched the fragments fly away on the wind. \"Yes,\" he added earnestly, \"I read that, and I think to myself, 'She has a sorrow, she is lonely, she would find comfort in true love. I haf a heart full, full for her; shall I not go and say, 'If this is not too poor a thing to gif for what I shall hope to receive, take it in Gott's name?'\"", "\"함부르크 포도는, 그렇지, 이걸로 꼭 조국을 위해 건배해야겠소?\""]], [["\"And so you came to find that it was not too poor, but the one precious thing I needed,\" whispered Jo. \"I had no courage to think that at first, heavenly kind as was your welcome to me. But soon I began to hope, and then I said, 'I will haf her if I die for it,' and so I will!\" cried Mr. Bhaer, with a defiant nod, as if the walls of mist closing round them were barriers which he was to surmount or valiantly knock down.", "조는 그 사치스러움에 눈살을 찌푸리며, 차라리 대추야자 한 바구니에 건포도 한 통, 아몬드 한 자루를 사서 끝내 버리지 그러냐고 물었다. 그러자 베어 씨는 그녀의 지갑을 압수하고 자기 것을 꺼내더니, 포도 몇 파운드와 발그레한 데이지 화분 하나, 그리고 큰 술병처럼 여길 만한 예쁜 꿀단지 하나를 사며 장보기를 끝냈다. 그러고는 울퉁불퉁한 꾸러미들로 주머니를 잔뜩 불룩하게 만들고, 그녀에게는 꽃을 들게 한 다음, 낡은 우산을 펼쳐 들었고, 둘은 다시 길을 걸었다."]], [["Jo thought that was splendid, and resolved to be worthy of her knight, though he did not come prancing on a charger in gorgeous array.", "\"마치 양, 한 가지 큰 부탁이 있소.\""], ["\"What made you stay away so long?\" she asked presently, finding it so pleasant to ask confidential questions and get delightful answers that she could not keep silent.", "반 블록쯤 축축한 산책을 한 끝에 교수가 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["\"It was not easy, but I could not find the heart to take you from that so happy home until I could haf a prospect of one to give you, after much time, perhaps, and hard work. How could I ask you to gif up so much for a poor old fellow, who has no fortune but a little learning?\" \"I'm glad you _are_ poor; I couldn't bear a rich husband,\" said Jo decidedly, adding, in a softer tone, \"Don't fear poverty; I've known it long enough to lose my dread, and be happy working for those I love; and don't call yourself old,--forty is the prime of life. I couldn't help loving you if you were seventy!\"", "\"네, 말씀하세요.\" 조의 심장이 어찌나 세차게 뛰던지, 그가 그 소리를 들을까 봐 겁이 났다."]], [["The Professor found that so touching that he would have been glad of his handkerchief, if he could have got at it; as he couldn't, Jo wiped his eyes for him, and said, laughing, as she took away a bundle or two,--\n\"I may be strong-minded, but no one can say I'm out of my sphere now, for woman's special mission is supposed to be drying tears and bearing burdens. I'm to carry my share, Friedrich, and help to earn the home. Make up your mind to that, or I'll never go,\" she added resolutely, as he tried to reclaim his load.", "\"비가 오는데도 감히 말하는 건, 내게 남은 시간이 너무 짧아서요.\""]], [["\"We shall see. Haf you patience to wait a long time, Jo? I must go away and do my work alone. I must help my boys first, because, even for you, I may not break my word to Minna. Can you forgif that, and be happy while we hope and wait?\" \"Yes, I know I can; for we love one another, and that makes all the rest easy to bear. I have my duty, also, and my work. I couldn't enjoy myself if I neglected them even for you, so there's no need of hurry or impatience. You can do your part out West, I can do mine here, and both be happy hoping for the best, and leaving the future to be as God wills.\"", "\"네, 말씀하세요.\" 조는 별안간 손에 힘이 들어가 그 작은 화분을 으스러뜨릴 뻔했다."]], [["\"Ah! thou gifest me such hope and courage, and I haf nothing to gif back but a full heart and these empty hands,\"", "\"내 티나에게 줄 작은 옷을 한 벌 사고 싶은데, 나 혼자 가기엔 너무 어리석어서 말이오."], ["cried the Professor, quite overcome.", "안목 있는 한마디와 도움을 좀 베풀어 주시겠소?\""]], [["# HARVEST TIME.", "# HARVEST TIME."]], [["Jo never, never would learn to be proper; for when he said that as they stood upon the steps, she just put both hands into his, whispering tenderly, \"Not empty now;\" and, stooping down, kissed her Friedrich under the umbrella. It was dreadful, but she would have done it if the flock of draggle-tailed sparrows on the hedge had been human beings, for she was very far gone indeed, and quite regardless of everything but her own happiness. Though it came in such a very simple guise, that was the crowning moment of both their lives, when, turning from the night and storm and loneliness to the household light and warmth and peace waiting to receive them, with a glad \"Welcome home!\" Jo led her lover in, and shut the door. For a year Jo and her Professor worked and waited, hoped and loved, met occasionally, and wrote such voluminous letters that the rise in the price of paper was accounted for, Laurie said. The second year began rather soberly, for their prospects did not brighten, and Aunt March died suddenly. But when their first sorrow was over,--for they loved the old lady in spite of her sharp tongue,--they found they had cause for rejoicing, for she had left Plumfield to Jo, which made all sorts of joyful things possible.", "\"네, 그러죠.\" 조는 별안간 냉장고 속으로 발을 들여놓기라도 한 듯 차분하고 싸늘해진 기분이 들었다."]], [["\"It's a fine old place, and will bring a handsome sum; for of course you intend to sell it,\"", "\"어쩌면 티나 어머니에게 줄 숄도 하나."], ["said Laurie, as they were all talking the matter over, some weeks later. \"No, I don't,\"", "그 부인이 워낙 가난하고 병약한 데다 남편 때문에 얼마나 고생을 하는지."], ["was Jo's decided answer, as she petted the fat poodle, whom she had adopted, out of respect to his former mistress.", "그래요, 그래, 두툼하고 따뜻한 숄이라면 그 가엾은 어머니에게 가져다주기에 정다운 선물이 되겠지.\""]], [["\"Yes, I do.\"", "\"기꺼이 도와드릴게요, 베어 선생님."], ["\"But, my dear girl, it's an immense house, and will take a power of money to keep it in order.", "난 아주 빠르게 가고 있는데, 그분은 매 순간 점점 더 사랑스러워지고 있어.\" 조가 속으로 덧붙였다."], ["The garden and orchard alone need two or three men, and farming isn't in Bhaer's line, I take it.\"", "그러고는 마음을 한 번 다잡고서, 보기 좋을 만큼의 활기를 띠고 그 일에 뛰어들었다."]], [["\"And you expect to live on the produce of the place? Well, that sounds paradisiacal, but you'll find it desperate hard work.\"", "베어 씨가 모든 걸 그녀에게 맡겼으므로, 조는 티나에게 줄 예쁜 옷을 고르고 나서 숄을 가져오라고 했다."], ["\"The crop we are going to raise is a profitable one;\" and Jo laughed.", "결혼한 몸인 점원은 가족을 위해 쇼핑하는 듯 보이는 이 두 사람에게 짐짓 관심을 보이며 거들었다."]], [["\"Of what is this fine crop to consist, ma'am?\" \"Boys.", "\"부인께서는 이걸 더 마음에 들어 하실 겁니다."], ["I want to open a school for little lads,--a good, happy, homelike school, with me to take care of them, and Fritz to teach them.\"", "고급품인 데다 더없이 좋은 색이고, 아주 정숙하고 품위 있지요.\" 그는 그렇게 말하며 포근한 회색 숄을 펼쳐 조의 어깨에 둘러 주었다."]], [["\"There's a truly Joian plan for you!", "\"이게 마음에 드세요, 베어 선생님?\""], ["Isn't that just like her?\"", "조가 그에게 등을 돌리며 물었다."], ["cried Laurie, appealing to the family, who looked as much surprised as he.", "얼굴을 감출 기회가 생긴 것에 깊이 감사하면서."]], [["\"So do I,\"", "\"아주 마음에 드오."], ["added her husband, who welcomed the thought of a chance for trying the Socratic method of education on modern youth.", "이걸로 하지요.\" 교수가 대답하며 속으로 미소 지은 채 값을 치렀고, 조는 영락없는 알뜰 흥정꾼처럼 계속 판매대를 뒤졌다."]], [["\"It will be an immense care for Jo,\"", "\"이제 집에 갈까요?\""], ["said Meg, stroking the head of her one all-absorbing son.", "그가 그 말이 무척이나 기쁘다는 듯 물었다."]], [["\"Jo can do it, and be happy in it. It's a splendid idea.", "\"네, 늦었고, 나 정말 너무 지쳤어요.\""], ["Tell us all about it,\" cried Mr.", "조의 목소리는 자기가 아는 것보다 더 애처로웠다."], ["Laurence, who had been longing to lend the lovers a hand, but knew that they would refuse his help. \"I knew you'd stand by me, sir.", "이제 해가 떠올랐을 때만큼이나 갑작스레 다시 숨어 버린 듯했고, 세상은 다시 진창에다 비참해졌으며, 그녀는 처음으로 자기 발이 시리고 머리가 아프며, 가슴이 발보다 더 차갑고 머리보다 더 고통으로 가득하다는 걸 깨달았다."], ["Amy does too--I see it in her eyes, though she prudently waits to turn it over in her mind before she speaks.", "베어 씨는 떠나간다. 그는 그저 친구로서 자기를 아낄 뿐이다."], ["Now, my dear people,\" continued Jo earnestly, \"just understand that this isn't a new idea of mine, but a long-cherished plan.", "다 착각이었으니, 빨리 끝날수록 좋다."], ["Before my Fritz came, I used to think how, when I'd made my fortune, and no one needed me at home, I'd hire a big house, and pick up some poor, forlorn little lads, who hadn't any mothers, and take care of them, and make life jolly for them before it was too late. I see so many going to ruin, for want of help at the right minute; I love so to do anything for them; I seem to feel their wants, and sympathize with their troubles, and, oh, I should _so_ like to be a mother to them!\"", "그런 생각에 사로잡혀, 조는 다가오는 합승마차를 어찌나 황급한 손짓으로 불러 세웠던지 화분에서 데이지꽃들이 튀어나와 잔뜩 망가지고 말았다."]], [["Mrs. March held out her hand to Jo, who took it, smiling, with tears in her eyes, and went on in the old enthusiastic way, which they had not seen for a long while. \"I told my plan to Fritz once, and he said it was just what he would like, and agreed to try it when we got rich. Bless his dear heart, he's been doing it all his life,--helping poor boys, I mean, not getting rich; that he'll never be; money doesn't stay in his pocket long enough to lay up any. But now, thanks to my good old aunt, who loved me better than I ever deserved, _I'm_ rich, at least I feel so, and we can live at Plumfield perfectly well, if we have a flourishing school. It's just the place for boys, the house is big, and the furniture strong and plain. There's plenty of room for dozens inside, and splendid grounds outside. They could help in the garden and orchard: such work is healthy, isn't it, sir? Then Fritz can train and teach in his own way, and father will help him. I can feed and nurse and pet and scold them; and mother will be my stand-by. I've always longed for lots of boys, and never had enough; now I can fill the house full, and revel in the little dears to my heart's content. Think what luxury,--Plumfield my own, and a wilderness of boys to enjoy it with me!\"", "\"이건 우리 마차가 아니오.\" 교수가 짐을 가득 실은 그 마차를 손짓으로 보내고는, 그 가엾은 작은 꽃들을 주우려고 멈춰 서며 말했다."]], [["As Jo waved her hands, and gave a sigh of rapture, the family went off into a gale of merriment, and Mr. Laurence laughed till they thought he'd have an apoplectic fit.", "\"죄송해요. 이름을 똑똑히 못 봤어요. 괜찮아요, 걸어가면 돼요. 진창을 터덜터덜 걷는 데는 이골이 났거든요.\" 조가 대답하며 눈을 질끈질끈 깜빡였다. 차라리 죽을지언정 대놓고 눈물을 닦지는 않으려 했던 것이다."]], [["\"I don't see anything funny,\" she said gravely, when she could be heard.", "바에르 선생은 조가 고개를 돌렸는데도 그녀의 뺨에 맺힌 물방울을 보았다."], ["\"Nothing could be more natural or proper than for my Professor to open a school, and for me to prefer to reside on my own estate.\"", "그 모습이 그의 마음을 몹시 흔들었는지, 그는 갑자기 몸을 숙이며 깊은 뜻이 담긴 목소리로 물었다. \"내 마음속 가장 소중한 사람, 왜 우는 거요?\""]], [["\"She is putting on airs already,\" said Laurie, who regarded the idea in the light of a capital joke. \"But may I inquire how you intend to support the establishment? If all the pupils are little ragamuffins, I'm afraid your crop won't be profitable in a worldly sense, Mrs. Bhaer.\"", "이런 일에 익숙한 사람이었다면 조는 우는 게 아니라고, 감기에 걸렸다고, 아니면 이런 상황에 어울리는 여자다운 거짓말이라도 했을 것이다. 그런데 이 체면머리 없는 사람은 참을 수 없는 흐느낌과 함께 이렇게 대답했다."]], [["\"Now don't be a wet-blanket, Teddy. Of course I shall have rich pupils, also,--perhaps begin with such altogether; then, when I've got a start, I can take a ragamuffin or two, just for a relish. Rich people's children often need care and comfort, as well as poor. I've seen unfortunate little creatures left to servants, or backward ones pushed forward, when it's real cruelty. Some are naughty through mismanagement or neglect, and some lose their mothers. Besides, the best have to get through the hobbledehoy age, and that's the very time they need most patience and kindness. People laugh at them, and hustle them about, try to keep them out of sight, and expect them to turn, all at once, from pretty children into fine young men. They don't complain much,--plucky little souls,--but they feel it. I've been through something of it, and I know all about it. I've a special interest in such young bears, and like to show them that I see the warm, honest, well-meaning boys' hearts, in spite of the clumsy arms and legs and the topsy-turvy heads. I've had experience, too, for haven't I brought up one boy to be a pride and honor to his family?\" \"I'll testify that you tried to do it,\" said Laurie, with a grateful look.", "\"당신이 떠나니까요.\""]], [["\"And I've succeeded beyond my hopes; for here you are, a steady, sensible business man, doing heaps of good with your money, and laying up the blessings of the poor, instead of dollars. But you are not merely a business man: you love good and beautiful things, enjoy them yourself, and let others go halves, as you always did in the old times. I _am_ proud of you, Teddy, for you get better every year, and every one feels it, though you won't let them say so. Yes, and when I have my flock, I'll just point to you, and say, 'There's your model, my lads.'\"", "\"아, 마인 고트, 그것 참 다행이오!\" 바에르 선생이 외쳤다. 우산과 보따리를 든 채로도 어떻게든 두 손을 마주 잡으려 애쓰면서. \"조, 내가 줄 수 있는 건 가득한 사랑뿐이오. 당신이 그것을 받아 줄 수 있을지 보려고 왔소. 그리고 내가 친구 이상의 무언가가 맞는지 확인하려고 기다렸소. 내가 그렇소? 이 늙은 프리츠를 위해 당신 마음속에 자그마한 자리 하나 마련해 줄 수 있겠소?\" 그는 단숨에 덧붙였다."]], [["Poor Laurie didn't know where to look; for, man though he was, something of the old bashfulness came over him as this burst of praise made all faces turn approvingly upon him.", "\"오, 그럼요!\" 조가 말했다. 그는 더없이 만족스러웠다. 조가 두 손을 그의 팔에 포개고는, 그가 우산을 들어 주기만 한다면 이 낡은 우산밖에 비를 피할 데가 없다 해도 그의 곁에서 평생을 걸어가는 일이 얼마나 행복할지 환히 드러내는 표정으로 그를 올려다보았기 때문이다."]], [["\"I say, Jo, that's rather too much,\" he began, just in his old boyish way.", "그것은 분명 여러 가지로 곤란한 처지에서 하는 청혼이었다."], ["\"You have all done more for me than I can ever thank you for, except by doing my best not to disappoint you.", "마음이야 굴뚝같았겠지만 바에르 선생은 진창 때문에 무릎을 꿇을 수도 없었다."], ["You have rather cast me off lately, Jo, but I've had the best of help, nevertheless; so, if I've got on at all, you may thank these two for it;\"", "두 손이 다 차 있으니 비유적으로가 아니라면 조에게 손을 내밀 수도 없었다. 더구나 곁에 한길이 있는데 길거리에서 다정한 표현을 마음껏 할 수도 없는 노릇이었다."], ["and he laid one hand gently on his grandfather's white head, the other on Amy's golden one, for the three were never far apart. \"I do think that families are the most beautiful things in all the world!\"", "그래서 그가 환희를 표현할 수 있는 유일한 방법은 그녀를 바라보는 것뿐이었는데, 그 표정이 그의 얼굴을 어찌나 환하게 빛나게 했던지 수염에 반짝이는 물방울 속에 정말로 작은 무지개가 떠 있는 것만 같았다."], ["burst out Jo, who was in an unusually uplifted frame of mind just then.", "만약 그가 조를 무척 사랑하지 않았더라면 그때 그렇게 할 수는 없었으리라."], ["\"When I have one of my own, I hope it will be as happy as the three I know and love the best. If John and my Fritz were only here, it would be quite a little heaven on earth,\"", "치맛자락은 한심한 꼴이었고, 고무장화는 발목까지 흙탕물이 튀었으며, 보닛은 엉망이 되어, 조의 모습은 사랑스러움과는 거리가 멀었으니까."], ["she added more quietly. And that night, when she went to her room, after a blissful evening of family counsels, hopes, and plans, her heart was so full of happiness that she could only calm it by kneeling beside the empty bed always near her own, and thinking tender thoughts of Beth.", "다행히도 바에르 선생은 그녀를 세상에서 가장 아름다운 여인이라 여겼고, 모자챙이 흘러내리는 물줄기에 축 늘어져 그의 어깨로 빗물이 떨어지고(우산을 온통 조에게 씌워 주고 있었으니) 장갑 손가락마다 죄다 기워야 할 형편인데도, 조는 그가 어느 때보다 더 \"주피터 같다\"고 느꼈다."]], [["It was a very astonishing year altogether, for things seemed to happen in an unusually rapid and delightful manner.", "지나가는 사람들은 아마 두 사람을 별 해 없는 미치광이 한 쌍으로 여겼을 것이다."], ["Almost before she knew where she was, Jo found herself married and settled at Plumfield.", "둘은 마차를 불러 세우는 것조차 까맣게 잊은 채, 짙어 가는 땅거미와 안개도 아랑곳없이 한가로이 거닐었으니까. 누가 어떻게 생각하든 조금도 개의치 않았다."], ["Then a family of six or seven boys sprung up like mushrooms, and flourished surprisingly, poor boys as well as rich; for Mr.", "두 사람은 평생에 좀처럼 한 번밖에 오지 않는 행복한 시간을, 늙은이에게 젊음을, 평범한 이에게 아름다움을, 가난한 이에게 부를 안겨 주고, 인간의 마음에 천국을 미리 맛보게 해 주는 그 마법 같은 순간을 누리고 있었다. 교수는 마치 왕국 하나를 정복한 듯, 세상이 더는 그에게 베풀 행복이 없다는 듯한 표정이었다."], ["Laurence was continually finding some touching case of destitution, and begging the Bhaers to take pity on the child, and he would gladly pay a trifle for its support.", "한편 조는 그의 곁을 터벅터벅 걸으며, 자기 자리가 늘 거기였던 것만 같은 기분에 잠겨, 어떻게 다른 인생을 택할 수도 있었을까 의아해했다. 물론 먼저 입을 연 건 조였다."], ["In this way the sly old gentleman got round proud Jo, and furnished her with the style of boy in which she most delighted.", "알아들을 만한 말로 입을 연 건 말이다. 그녀의 격정적인 \"오, 그럼요!\"에 뒤이은 감정 어린 말들은 조리 있지도, 옮겨 적을 만하지도 않았으니까."]], [["Of course it was up-hill work at first, and Jo made queer mistakes; but the wise Professor steered her safely into calmer waters, and the most rampant ragamuffin was conquered in the end. How Jo did enjoy her \"wilderness of boys,\" and how poor, dear Aunt March would have lamented had she been there to see the sacred precincts of prim, well-ordered Plumfield overrun with Toms, Dicks, and Harrys! There was a sort of poetic justice about it, after all, for the old lady had been the terror of the boys for miles round; and now the exiles feasted freely on forbidden plums, kicked up the gravel with profane boots unreproved, and played cricket in the big field where the irritable \"cow with a crumpled horn\" used to invite rash youths to come and be tossed. It became a sort of boys' paradise, and Laurie suggested that it should be called the \"Bhaer-garten,\" as a compliment to its master and appropriate to its inhabitants. It never was a fashionable school, and the Professor did not lay up a fortune; but it _was_ just what Jo intended it to be,--\"a happy, homelike place for boys, who needed teaching, care, and kindness.\" Every room in the big house was soon full; every little plot in the garden soon had its owner; a regular menagerie appeared in barn and shed, for pet animals were allowed; and, three times a day, Jo smiled at her Fritz from the head of a long table lined on either side with rows of happy young faces, which all turned to her with affectionate eyes, confiding words, and grateful hearts, full of love for \"Mother Bhaer.\" She had boys enough now, and did not tire of them, though they were not angels, by any means, and some of them caused both Professor and Professorin much trouble and anxiety. But her faith in the good spot which exists in the heart of the naughtiest, sauciest, most tantalizing little ragamuffin gave her patience, skill, and, in time, success; for no mortal boy could hold out long with Father Bhaer shining on him as benevolently as the sun, and Mother Bhaer forgiving him seventy times seven. Very precious to Jo was the friendship of the lads; their penitent sniffs and whispers after wrong-doing; their droll or touching little confidences; their pleasant enthusiasms, hopes, and plans; even their misfortunes, for they only endeared them to her all the more. There were slow boys and bashful boys; feeble boys and riotous boys; boys that lisped and boys that stuttered; one or two lame ones; and a merry little quadroon, who could not be taken in elsewhere, but who was welcome to the \"Bhaer-garten,\" though some people predicted that his admission would ruin the school.", "\"프리드리히, 왜 당신은……\""]], [["Yes; Jo was a very happy woman there, in spite of hard work, much anxiety, and a perpetual racket. She enjoyed it heartily, and found the applause of her boys more satisfying than any praise of the world; for now she told no stories except to her flock of enthusiastic believers and admirers. As the years went on, two little lads of her own came to increase her happiness,--Rob, named for grandpa, and Teddy, a happy-go-lucky baby, who seemed to have inherited his papa's sunshiny temper as well as his mother's lively spirit. How they ever grew up alive in that whirlpool of boys was a mystery to their grandma and aunts; but they flourished like dandelions in spring, and their rough nurses loved and served them well. There were a great many holidays at Plumfield, and one of the most delightful was the yearly apple-picking; for then the Marches, Laurences, Brookes, and Bhaers turned out in full force, and made a day of it. Five years after Jo's wedding, one of these fruitful festivals occurred,--a mellow October day, when the air was full of an exhilarating freshness which made the spirits rise, and the blood dance healthily in the veins. The old orchard wore its holiday attire; golden-rod and asters fringed the mossy walls; grasshoppers skipped briskly in the sere grass, and crickets chirped like fairy pipers at a feast; squirrels were busy with their small harvesting; birds twittered their adieux from the alders in the lane; and every tree stood ready to send down its shower of red or yellow apples at the first shake. Everybody was there; everybody laughed and sang, climbed up and tumbled down; everybody declared that there never had been such a perfect day or such a jolly set to enjoy it; and every one gave themselves up to the simple pleasures of the hour as freely as if there were no such things as care or sorrow in the world.", "\"아, 맙소사, 미나가 죽은 뒤로 아무도 부르지 않던 이름을 이 사람이 불러 주는구려!\" 교수가 외치며 물웅덩이 한가운데 멈춰 서서 고마움 어린 기쁨으로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["Mr. March strolled placidly about, quoting Tusser, Cowley, and Columella to Mr.", "\"속으로는 늘 그렇게 불렀어요……"], ["Laurence, while enjoying--", "깜박했네요. 하지만 당신이 싫다면 안 부를게요.\""]], [["\"The gentle apple's winey juice.\"", "\"싫다니?"], ["The Professor charged up and down the green aisles like a stout Teutonic knight, with a pole for a lance, leading on the boys, who made a hook and ladder company of themselves, and performed wonders in the way of ground and lofty tumbling.", "말로 다 못 할 만큼 달콤한걸."], ["Laurie devoted himself to the little ones, rode his small daughter in a bushel-basket, took Daisy up among the birds' nests, and kept adventurous Rob from breaking his neck.", "'그대'라고도 불러 주오."], ["Mrs. March and Meg sat among the apple piles like a pair of Pomonas, sorting the contributions that kept pouring in; while Amy, with a beautiful motherly expression in her face, sketched the various groups, and watched over one pale lad, who sat adoring her with his little crutch beside him.", "그러면 당신 나라 말이 우리 말만큼이나 아름답다고 말하겠소.\""]], [["Jo was in her element that day, and rushed about, with her gown pinned up, her hat anywhere but on her head, and her baby tucked under her arm, ready for any lively adventure which might turn up. Little Teddy bore a charmed life, for nothing ever happened to him, and Jo never felt any anxiety when he was whisked up into a tree by one lad, galloped off on the back of another, or supplied with sour russets by his indulgent papa, who labored under the Germanic delusion that babies could digest anything, from pickled cabbage to buttons, nails, and their own small shoes. She knew that little Ted would turn up again in time, safe and rosy, dirty and serene, and she always received him back with a hearty welcome, for Jo loved her babies tenderly. At four o'clock a lull took place, and baskets remained empty, while the apple-pickers rested, and compared rents and bruises. Then Jo and Meg, with a detachment of the bigger boys, set forth the supper on the grass, for an out-of-door tea was always the crowning joy of the day. The land literally flowed with milk and honey on such occasions, for the lads were not required to sit at table, but allowed to partake of refreshment as they liked,--freedom being the sauce best beloved by the boyish soul. They availed themselves of the rare privilege to the fullest extent, for some tried the pleasing experiment of drinking milk while standing on their heads, others lent a charm to leap-frog by eating pie in the pauses of the game, cookies were sown broadcast over the field, and apple-turnovers roosted in the trees like a new style of bird. The little girls had a private tea-party, and Ted roved among the edibles at his own sweet will.", "\"'그대'는 좀 감상적이지 않아요?\" 조가 물었다. 속으로는 사랑스러운 한 음절이라고 생각하면서."]], [["When no one could eat any more, the Professor proposed the first regular toast, which was always drunk at such times,--\"Aunt March, God bless her!\" A toast heartily given by the good man, who never forgot how much he owed her, and quietly drunk by the boys, who had been taught to keep her memory green.", "\"감상적이라고? 그렇소. 고트께 감사하게도, 우리 독일 사람들은 감상을 믿고, 그걸로 젊음을 지킨다오. 당신네 영어의 '유'는 너무 차갑소. '그대'라고 해 주오, 내 마음속 가장 소중한 사람, 나에게는 정말 큰 의미라오.\" 바에르 선생이 근엄한 교수라기보다 낭만적인 학생처럼 졸랐다."]], [["\"Now, grandma's sixtieth birthday!", "\"그럼, 왜 그대는 이 모든 걸 진작 말하지 않았어요?\""], ["Long life to her, with three times three!\"", "조가 수줍게 물었다."]], [["That was given with a will, as you may well believe; and the cheering once begun, it was hard to stop it. Everybody's health was proposed, from Mr.", "\"이제 그대에게 내 마음을 모두 보여 줘야겠구려."], ["Laurence, who was considered their special patron, to the astonished guinea-pig, who had strayed from its proper sphere in search of its young master.", "기꺼이 그러겠소."], ["Demi, as the oldest grandchild, then presented the queen of the day with various gifts, so numerous that they were transported to the festive scene in a wheelbarrow.", "앞으로 그대가 그 마음을 돌봐 주어야 하니까."], ["Funny presents, some of them, but what would have been defects to other eyes were ornaments to grandma's,--for the children's gifts were all their own. Every stitch Daisy's patient little fingers had put into the handkerchiefs she hemmed was better than embroidery to Mrs.", "자, 그럼 나의 조여 — 아, 사랑스럽고 우스운 그 작은 이름!"], ["March; Demi's shoe-box was a miracle of mechanical skill, though the cover wouldn't shut; Rob's footstool had a wiggle in its uneven legs, that she declared was very soothing; and no page of the costly book Amy's child gave her was so fair as that on which appeared, in tipsy capitals, the words,--\"To dear Grandma, from her little Beth.\" During this ceremony the boys had mysteriously disappeared; and, when Mrs.", "— 뉴욕에서 작별 인사를 하던 날, 나는 무언가 말하고 싶었소."], ["March had tried to thank her children, and broken down, while Teddy wiped her eyes on his pinafore, the Professor suddenly began to sing. Then, from above him, voice after voice took up the words, and from tree to tree echoed the music of the unseen choir, as the boys sung, with all their hearts, the little song Jo had written, Laurie set to music, and the Professor trained his lads to give with the best effect.", "하지만 그 잘생긴 친구가 그대와 약혼한 줄로 알았기에 입을 다물었소."], ["This was something altogether new, and it proved a grand success; for Mrs. March couldn't get over her surprise, and insisted on shaking hands with every one of the featherless birds, from tall Franz and Emil to the little quadroon, who had the sweetest voice of all.", "만약 그때 내가 말했더라면, 그대는 '네'라고 했겠소?\""]], [["After this, the boys dispersed for a final lark, leaving Mrs.", "\"모르겠어요. 그러지 않았을 것 같아요."], ["March and her daughters under the festival tree.", "그때는 마음이라는 게 없었거든요.\""]], [["\"I don't think I ever ought to call myself 'Unlucky Jo'", "\"풋! 그건 못 믿겠소."], ["again, when my greatest wish has been so beautifully gratified,\" said Mrs.", "그건 그저 잠들어 있었을 뿐, 요정 왕자가 숲을 지나 와서 깨워 줄 때까지 말이오."], ["Bhaer, taking Teddy's little fist out of the milk-pitcher, in which he was rapturously churning.", "아, 뭐, '첫사랑이 가장 좋은 사랑'이라지만, 그것까진 바라지 않겠소.\""]], [["\"And yet your life is very different from the one you pictured so long ago.", "\"맞아요, 첫사랑이 가장 좋은 사랑이에요. 그러니 만족하세요."], ["Do you remember our castles in the air?\"", "나에겐 다른 사랑이 없었으니까요."], ["asked Amy, smiling as she watched Laurie and John playing cricket with the boys.", "테디는 그저 소년이었고, 잠깐의 변덕은 금세 떨쳐 버렸어요.\" 교수의 오해를 바로잡고 싶은 마음에 조가 말했다."]], [["\"Dear fellows! It does my heart good to see them forget business, and frolic for a day,\" answered Jo, who now spoke in a maternal way of all mankind. \"Yes, I remember; but the life I wanted then seems selfish, lonely, and cold to me now. I haven't given up the hope that I may write a good book yet, but I can wait, and I'm sure it will be all the better for such experiences and illustrations as these;\" and Jo pointed from the lively lads in the distance to her father, leaning on the Professor's arm, as they walked to and fro in the sunshine, deep in one of the conversations which both enjoyed so much, and then to her mother, sitting enthroned among her daughters, with their children in her lap and at her feet, as if all found help and happiness in the face which never could grow old to them. \"My castle was the most nearly realized of all. I asked for splendid things, to be sure, but in my heart I knew I should be satisfied, if I had a little home, and John, and some dear children like these. I've got them all, thank God, and am the happiest woman in the world;\" and Meg laid her hand on her tall boy's head, with a face full of tender and devout content.", "\"잘됐소! 그럼 나는 마음 놓고 행복할 수 있겠구려. 그대가 모든 걸 나에게 준다고 확신하면서 말이오. 너무 오래 기다린 탓에 나는 이기적인 사람이 됐다오, 당신도 곧 알게 되겠지만, 나의 교수 부인.\""]], [["\"My castle is very different from what I planned, but I would not alter it, though, like Jo, I don't relinquish all my artistic hopes, or confine myself to helping others fulfil their dreams of beauty.", "\"그거 마음에 들어요.\""], ["I've begun to model a figure of baby, and Laurie says it is the best thing I've ever done. I think so myself, and mean to do it in marble, so that, whatever happens, I may at least keep the image of my little angel.\"", "조가 새 호칭에 기뻐하며 외쳤다."], ["As Amy spoke, a great tear dropped on the golden hair of the sleeping child in her arms; for her one well-beloved daughter was a frail little creature and the dread of losing her was the shadow over Amy's sunshine.", "\"이제 말해 봐요."], ["This cross was doing much for both father and mother, for one love and sorrow bound them closely together. Amy's nature was growing sweeter, deeper, and more tender; Laurie was growing more serious, strong, and firm; and both were learning that beauty, youth, good fortune, even love itself, cannot keep care and pain, loss and sorrow, from the most blest; for--", "무엇 때문에 내가 가장 당신을 필요로 한 바로 그때 마침내 찾아온 거예요?\""]], [["\"Into each life some rain must fall, Some days must be dark and sad and dreary.\"", "\"이것 때문이오.\" 바에르 선생이 조끼 주머니에서 낡고 작은 종잇조각을 꺼냈다."]], [["\"She is growing better, I am sure of it, my dear.", "조는 그것을 펼쳐 보고는 몹시 무안해했다."], ["Don't despond, but hope and keep happy,\" said Mrs.", "그것은 시에 원고료를 주는 어느 신문에 자기가 보낸 글 가운데 하나였기 때문이다."], ["March, as tender-hearted Daisy stooped from her knee, to lay her rosy cheek against her little cousin's pale one.", "그래서 그녀가 가끔 한 편씩 시도해 보낸 것이었다."]], [["\"I never ought to, while I have you to cheer me up, Marmee, and Laurie to take more than half of every burden,\" replied Amy warmly. \"He never lets me see his anxiety, but is so sweet and patient with me, so devoted to Beth, and such a stay and comfort to me always, that I can't love him enough. So, in spite of my one cross, I can say with Meg, 'Thank God, I'm a happy woman.'\" \"There's no need for me to say it, for every one can see that I'm far happier than I deserve,\" added Jo, glancing from her good husband to her chubby children, tumbling on the grass beside her. \"Fritz is getting gray and stout; I'm growing as thin as a shadow, and am thirty; we never shall be rich, and Plumfield may burn up any night, for that incorrigible Tommy Bangs _will_ smoke sweet-fern cigars under the bed-clothes, though he's set himself afire three times already. But in spite of these unromantic facts, I have nothing to complain of, and never was so jolly in my life. Excuse the remark, but living among boys, I can't help using their expressions now and then.\"", "\"이게 어떻게 당신을 오게 했다는 거예요?\" 무슨 말인지 몰라 조가 물었다."]], [["\"Yes, Jo, I think your harvest will be a good one,\"", "\"우연히 발견했소. 이름과 머리글자를 보고 알아봤지."], ["began Mrs. March, frightening away a big black cricket that was staring Teddy out of countenance.", "그 안에 나를 부르는 듯한 작은 시 한 구절이 있었소."], ["\"Not half so good as yours, mother.", "읽어 보고 그를 찾아보오."], ["Here it is, and we never can thank you enough for the patient sowing and reaping you have done,\" cried Jo, with the loving impetuosity which she never could outgrow.", "당신이 빗물에 젖지 않게 내가 우산을 봐 주리다.\""]], [["\"I hope there will be more wheat and fewer tares every year,\"", "조는 시키는 대로, 자기가 이렇게 제목 붙인 시구를 서둘러 훑어보았다."], ["said Amy softly.", "\"다락방에서."]], [["\"A large sheaf, but I know there's room in your heart for it, Marmee dear,\" added Meg's tender voice.", "작은 궤짝 넷이 나란히 한 줄로, 먼지에 흐려지고 세월에 닳았네, 오래전에 모두 짜이고 채워졌지, 이제는 한창때를 맞은 아이들 손에."]], [["Touched to the heart, Mrs. March could only stretch out her arms, as if to gather children and grandchildren to herself, and say, with face and voice full of motherly love, gratitude, and humility,--", "작은 열쇠 넷이 나란히 걸려 있네, 빛바랜 리본 달고, 씩씩하고 화사했지 어린아이의 자랑으로 거기 매어 두던 그때, 오래전, 어느 비 오던 날에."]], [["\"O, my girls, however long you may live, I never can wish you a greater happiness than this!\"", "작은 이름 넷, 뚜껑마다 하나씩, 소년다운 손길로 새겨 두었고, 그 아래엔 숨어 있네, 행복한 무리의 이야기들이."]], [["# THE LITTLE WOMEN SERIES.", "# THE LITTLE WOMEN SERIES."]], [["Louisa M. Alcott's Writings\n=Little Women=; or Meg, Jo, Beth, and Amy. Illustrated. 16mo. $1.50.", "한때 여기서 놀다가, 곧잘 멈추어 달콤한 후렴에 귀 기울이던, 저 높은 지붕 위로 오갔던 그 가락, 여름 비 내리던 그 소리에."]], [["=Little Men.= Life at Plumfield with Jo's Boys. Illustrated. 16mo. $1.50. =Jo's Boys and How They Turned Out.= A Sequel to \"Little Men.\" Portrait of Author. 16mo. $1.50.", "'메그' 첫 번째 뚜껑에, 매끈하고 고운. 나는 사랑 어린 눈으로 들여다보네, 여기 낯익은 정성으로 고이 접혀, 어여쁜 것들이 한가득 모여 있으니, 평온한 한 생애의 기록이라네 — 상냥한 아이와 소녀에게 준 선물들, 웨딩드레스, 아내에게 바친 시구, 조그마한 신발 하나, 아기 머리카락 한 올."]], [["=Eight Cousins=; or, The Aunt-Hill. Illustrated. 16mo. $1.50. =Rose in Bloom.= A Sequel to \"Eight Cousins.\" Illustrated. 16mo. $1.50.", "이 첫 궤짝엔 장난감이 남지 않았네, 모두 어딘가로 옮겨 갔으니, 세월이 흘러, 또 다른 작은 메그의 놀이에 다시 함께하려고."]], [["=Jack and Jill.= A Village Story. Illustrated. 16mo. $1.50. The above eight volumes, uniformly bound in cloth, gilt, in box, $12.00.", "아, 행복한 어머니! 잘 알고 있네 그대가 달콤한 후렴처럼 듣고 있음을, 한없이 부드럽고 나직한 자장가를, 여름 비 내리는 그 소리 속에서."]], [["# THE SPINNING-WHEEL SERIES", "# THE SPINNING-WHEEL SERIES"]], [["THE LITTLE WOMEN SERIES. _New Illustrated Edition._ Printed from new plates with new cover designs, and illustrated with 84 full-page plates from drawings especially made for this edition by Reginald B. Birch, Alice Barber Stephens, Jessie Willcox Smith, and Harriet Roosevelt Richards. 8 vols. Crown 8vo. Decorated cloth, gilt, in box. $16.00. Separately, $2.00. =Spinning-Wheel Stories.= =Silver Pitchers.= =Proverb Stories.= =A Garland for Girls.=", "'조' 다음 뚜껑에, 긁히고 닳은, 그 안엔 잡동사니가 가득하네, 머리 없는 인형들, 찢어진 교과서, 이제는 말 없는 새들과 짐승들, 젊은 발길만이 밟던 요정 나라에서 가져온 전리품들, 끝내 찾지 못한 미래의 꿈들, 여전히 달콤한 지난날의 추억들, 쓰다 만 시들, 거친 이야기들, 사월의 편지들, 따뜻하고 차가운, 고집 센 아이의 일기장, 일찍 늙어 버린 여인의 자취들, 외로운 집 안의 한 여인, 구슬픈 후렴처럼 듣고 있네 — '사랑받을 만하면, 사랑은 찾아오리라.' 여름 비 내리는 그 소리 속에서."]], [["# AUNT JO'S SCRAP-BAG", "# AUNT JO'S SCRAP-BAG"]], [["THE SPINNING-WHEEL SERIES. _New Illustrated Edition._ Uniform in size with the Illustrated Edition of the Little Women Series. With 36 full-page plates by well-known artists. 4 vols. Crown 8vo. Decorated cloth. In box, $6.00. Separately, $1.50. =My Boys.= =Shawl-Straps.= =Cupid and Chow-Chow.= =My Girls.= =Jimmy's Cruise in the Pinafore.= =An Old-Fashioned Thanksgiving.=", "나의 베스! 네 이름이 적힌 뚜껑에는 먼지가 늘 말끔히 닦여 있네, 눈물 흘리던 사랑의 눈길이, 자주 찾던 정성스러운 손길이 그러했듯. 죽음이 우리에게 성인 하나를 거룩히 했으니, 인간이라기보다 늘 신성에 가까웠던, 그래도 우리는 다정한 탄식과 함께 이 가정의 성소에 유물을 놓아두네 — 좀처럼 울리지 않던 은방울, 그 애가 마지막으로 쓰던 작은 모자, 천사들이 받쳐 들어 문 위에 걸어 두었던 곱고 죽어 버린 캐서린, 고통의 감옥 안에서도 한탄 없이 부르던 그 애의 노래들, 영원토록 그것들은 달콤하게 어우러지네, 여름 비 내리는 그 소리와 함께."]], [["# NOVELS, ETC.", "# NOVELS, ETC."]], [["3 vols. Each, $1.00.", "마지막 뚜껑의 윤기 흐르는 면 위에 — 이제는 곱고도 참된 전설이라 — 한 늠름한 기사가 방패에 새겨 지녔네, '에이미'라고, 금빛과 푸른빛 글자로."], ["The set uniformly bound in cloth, gilt, in box, $3.00. =Hospital Sketches.= =Work.= =Comic Tragedies.= =Moods.= =A Modern Mephistopheles.= =Life of Louisa May Alcott.=", "그 안엔 그녀의 머리를 묶던 댕기, 마지막 춤을 끝낸 무도화, 정성스레 놓아둔 시든 꽃들, 가벼운 수고를 다한 부채들, 화사한 밸런타인 카드들, 온통 뜨거운 불꽃들, 소녀다운 희망과 두려움과 부끄러움에서 한몫을 했던 자질구레한 것들 — 처녀의 마음이 남긴 기록이라네, 이제는 더 곱고 참된 주문을 배우며, 경쾌한 후렴처럼 듣고 있네, 혼례의 은빛 종소리를, 여름 비 내리는 그 소리 속에서."]], [["A simple story of the home life of four girls.", "작은 궤짝 넷이 나란히 한 줄로, 먼지에 흐려지고 세월에 닳았네, 네 여인이 기쁨과 슬픔에 배워 한창때에 사랑하고 수고하였네."], ["A portrayal of child life, natural, wholesome, and inspiring. One of the best and most popular children's books ever written.", "네 자매, 한때 잠시 헤어졌으나, 하나도 잃지 않고, 한 사람만 먼저 떠났을 뿐, 사랑의 불멸한 힘으로 맺어져 영원토록 가장 가깝고 가장 소중하리."], ["2. LITTLE MEN: Life at Plumfield with Jo's Boys", "오, 우리의 이 숨겨 둔 곳간이 아버지의 눈앞에 활짝 펼쳐질 때, 황금 같은 시간들로, 빛 속에서 더 고와지는 행실들로 풍성하기를, 씩씩한 가락이 오래도록 울려 퍼질, 영혼을 일깨우는 선율 같은 삶들이기를, 비 갠 뒤 길게 비치는 햇살 속에서 기꺼이 솟아올라 노래할 영혼들이기를."]], [["Illustrated. 16mo. $1.50. Gives delightful pictures of boy life at old Plumfield, and is brimful of activity, merriment, health, and happiness.", "제이 엠.\""]], [["Illustrated.", "\"형편없는 시예요."], ["16mo. $1.50.", "하지만 쓸 때는 정말 그렇게 느꼈어요."], ["This sequel to \"Little Men\"", "무척 외로웠던 어느 날, 헝겊 자루에 기대 실컷 울고 나서요."], ["takes up the story and carries Jo's boys through the home struggles and adventures in the outside world until they are fairly launched on the sea of manhood.", "이게 비밀을 까발릴 수 있는 데까지 가게 될 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐어요.\" 조가 교수가 그토록 오래 간직해 온 시구를 북북 찢으며 말했다."]], [["Illustrated. 16mo. $1.50. The heroine of this book is shown as a possible improvement upon the girl of the period, who seems sadly ignorant or ashamed of the good old fashions which made women truly beautiful and honored.", "\"그냥 보내 주오. 제 할 일은 다 했으니. 그리고 그녀가 작은 비밀들을 적어 둔 갈색 책을 다 읽고 나면 새것을 하나 얻으면 되지.\" 바에르 선생이 미소 지으며 말했다. 조각들이 바람에 날아가는 것을 지켜보면서. \"그렇소.\" 그가 진지하게 덧붙였다. \"나는 그것을 읽고 속으로 생각했소. '이 사람은 슬픔을 안고 있구나, 외롭구나, 참된 사랑에서 위안을 얻을 수 있겠구나. 내겐 그녀를 향한 마음이 가득, 가득 차 있으니, 가서 이렇게 말하면 어떨까. 내가 받기를 바라는 것에 비해 이것이 너무 보잘것없는 게 아니라면, 신의 이름으로 받아 주오, 라고.'\""]], [["5. EIGHT COUSINS; or, the Aunt-Hill\nIllustrated.", "\"그래서 와 보니 그게 보잘것없기는커녕 내게 꼭 필요한 단 하나의 소중한 것이었군요.\""], ["16mo. $1.50.", "조가 속삭였다."]], [["The story of a pretty-faced and sunny-tempered little girl, obliged by the death of her parents to live with her uncle and her aunts, thereby coming in contact with seven cousins--all boys.", "\"처음엔 그렇게 생각할 용기가 없었소. 당신이 천사처럼 다정하게 나를 맞아 주었는데도 말이오."], ["Illustrated.", "하지만 곧 희망을 품기 시작했고, 그러고는 말했소. '죽는 한이 있어도 이 사람을 얻으리라.'"], ["16mo.", "그리고 그렇게 할 것이오!\""], ["$1.50.", "바에르 선생이 단호히 고개를 끄덕이며 외쳤다. 마치 그들을 에워싸며 닫혀 오는 안개의 벽이 자기가 넘거나 용감히 무너뜨려야 할 장벽이라는 듯이."]], [["This sequel to \"Eight Cousins\" carries on the story of Rose and the cousins, and is full of vivacity, fresh and stirring incident, and brilliant character painting. Illustrated. 16mo. $1.50.", "조는 그 모습이 멋지다고 생각했고, 화려한 차림으로 군마를 타고 달려오지는 않았어도, 자기 기사에게 어울리는 사람이 되겠다고 다짐했다."]], [["Ben and his dog Sancho run away from a circus and find a home with Bob and Betty in the old house under the lilacs.", "\"왜 그렇게 오래 안 왔어요?\" 조가 곧 물었다."], ["Told in Miss Alcott's best style.", "은밀한 질문을 던지고 기쁜 대답을 듣는 일이 어찌나 즐거운지 도무지 잠자코 있을 수가 없었다."]], [["Illustrated. 16mo.", "\"쉽지 않았소."], ["$1.50. A vivid yet natural portrayal of home and school life in a New England village, full of the sympathetic quality which lends such a charm to Miss Alcott's writings.", "하지만 당신에게 줄 보금자리를 마련할 가망이 생기기 전까지는, 그 행복한 집에서 당신을 데려올 엄두가 나지 않았소."], ["It is a lively and jolly narrative.", "아마 오랜 시간과 고된 일을 거쳐야 했겠지. 가진 거라곤 약간의 학식뿐인 가난하고 늙은 사내를 위해, 어떻게 당신더러 그토록 많은 걸 포기하라고 할 수 있었겠소?\""]], [["# COMIC TRAGEDIES", "# COMIC TRAGEDIES"]], [["_Uniform with \"The Little Women Series.\"_\nWritten by \"Jo\"", "\"당신이 가난한 게 오히려 기뻐요."], ["and \"Meg,\"", "부유한 남편은 견디지 못했을 거예요.\""], ["and acted by the \"Little Women,\"", "조가 단호하게 말하더니, 한결 부드러운 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"가난을 두려워하지 마세요."], ["with a Foreword by \"Meg.\"", "나는 가난을 충분히 겪어서 더는 무섭지 않고, 사랑하는 사람들을 위해 일하는 게 행복하답니다."], ["Portraits, etc.", "그리고 자신을 늙었다고 하지 마세요. 마흔은 인생의 한창때예요."], ["16mo. $1.50.", "당신이 일흔이라 해도 나는 사랑하지 않을 수 없었을 거예요!\""]], [["# LOUISA MAY ALCOTT", "# LOUISA MAY ALCOTT"]], [["Her Life, Letters, and Journals. Edited by Ednah D. Cheney. With photogravure portraits, etc. 16mo. $1.50. Other Stories by LOUISA M. ALCOTT", "교수는 그 말이 어찌나 가슴에 사무쳤던지 손수건을 꺼낼 수만 있었다면 좋아했을 텐데, 꺼낼 수가 없으니 조가 대신 그의 눈물을 닦아 주었다. 그러고는 보따리 한두 개를 받아 들며 웃으면서 말했다."]], [["# SPINNING-WHEEL STORIES", "# SPINNING-WHEEL STORIES"]], [["Four volumes of healthy and hearty short stories so told as to fascinate the young people, while inculcating sturdy courage and kindness to the weak in the boys, and in the girls those virtues which fit them for filling a woman's place in the home.", "\"내가 주관이 세긴 해도, 이젠 누구도 내가 분수를 벗어났다곤 못할걸요. 여자의 특별한 사명이란 눈물을 닦아 주고 짐을 나눠 지는 거라고들 하니까요."], ["1. SPINNING-WHEEL STORIES With twelve initial illustrations.", "내 몫은 내가 지겠어요, 프리드리히, 그리고 보금자리 마련하는 걸 도울 거예요."], ["16mo.", "그렇게 마음먹지 않으면 나는 안 갈래요.\""], ["$1.25.", "그가 짐을 도로 가져가려 하자 조가 단호하게 덧붙였다."]], [["# AUNT JO'S SCRAP BAG", "# AUNT JO'S SCRAP BAG"]], [["4. A GARLAND FOR GIRLS With illustrations.", "\"두고 봅시다."], ["16mo. $1.25.", "오래 기다릴 인내심이 있겠소, 조?"], ["The above four volumes, uniformly bound in cloth, gilt, in box, $5.00.", "나는 떠나서 혼자 내 일을 해야 하오. 내 아이들부터 도와야 하니까."], ["Six books of jolly, readable stories told in Miss Alcott's best style and sure to please young people.", "당신을 위해서라도 미나와의 약속을 저버릴 순 없기 때문이오. 그것을 용서하고, 우리가 희망을 품고 기다리는 동안 행복해 줄 수 있겠소?\""]], [["2. SHAWL-STRAPS Illustrated.", "\"네, 그럴 수 있어요."], ["Story of a voyage abroad.", "우리는 서로 사랑하니까요. 그러면 나머지는 다 견디기 쉬워져요."], ["16mo. $1.00\n3.", "나에게도 내 의무가 있고, 내 일이 있어요."], ["CUPID AND CHOW-CHOW Illustrated.", "당신을 위해서라도 그것들을 소홀히 하면 나는 즐거울 수 없을 거예요. 그러니 서두르거나 조급해할 필요가 없어요."], ["16mo. $1.00.", "당신은 서부에서 당신 몫을 하고, 나는 여기서 내 몫을 하고, 둘 다 가장 좋은 결과를 바라며 행복하게, 미래는 하느님 뜻에 맡기면 돼요.\""]], [["5. JIMMY'S CRUISE IN THE PINAFORE, ETC. Illustrated. 16mo. $1.00. 6. AN OLD-FASHIONED THANKSGIVING Illustrated. 16mo. $1.00.", "\"아! 당신은 나에게 이토록 큰 희망과 용기를 주는데, 나는 가득한 마음과 이 빈손밖에 돌려줄 게 없구려.\" 교수가 완전히 감격해 외쳤다."]], [["# LULU'S LIBRARY", "# LULU'S LIBRARY"]], [["The above six volumes, uniformly bound in cloth, gilt, in box, $6.00. Delightful short stories, many of them founded on incidents from Miss Alcott's life. Told so as to attract children, and all showing the spirit of cheerful accomplishment in the face of discouragements.", "조는 끝끝내 얌전해지는 법을 배우지 못할 사람이었다. 그가 계단에 서서 그렇게 말하자, 조는 두 손을 그의 손에 포개 넣으며 다정하게 속삭였다. \"이젠 빈손이 아니에요.\" 그러고는 몸을 숙여 우산 아래에서 자기 프리드리히에게 입을 맞췄다. 끔찍한 짓이었지만, 울타리에 늘어앉은 꼬질꼬질한 참새 떼가 사람이었다 해도 조는 그렇게 했을 것이다. 정말이지 깊이 빠져 버려, 자기 행복 말고는 아무것도 아랑곳하지 않았으니까. 비록 그토록 소박한 모습으로 찾아왔지만, 그 순간은 두 사람 인생의 절정이었다. 밤과 폭풍과 외로움에서 돌아서서, 그들을 맞으려 기다리는 가정의 불빛과 온기와 평화를 향해, \"잘 왔어요!\" 하는 반가운 인사와 함께 조가 연인을 안으로 이끌고 문을 닫던 그 순간이."]], [["Three volumes. Each, $1.00. The set, uniformly bound in cloth, gilt, in box, $3.00.", "추수기"]], [["# MISS ALCOTT'S NOVELS", "# MISS ALCOTT'S NOVELS"]], [["HOSPITAL SKETCHES and Camp and Fireside Stories. With illustrations.", "조와 그녀의 교수는 일 년 동안 일하며 기다렸고, 희망을 품으며 사랑했고, 이따금 만났으며, 어찌나 방대한 편지를 주고받았던지 종잇값이 오른 게 다 그 탓이라고 로리가 말할 정도였다."], ["16mo. $1.50.", "둘째 해는 다소 우울하게 시작됐다."], ["WORK A Story of Experience.", "형편은 좀처럼 나아지지 않았고, 마치 대고모님이 갑자기 세상을 떠났기 때문이다."], ["Illustrated by Sol Eytinge.", "하지만 첫 슬픔이 가시고 나자 — 입은 매서웠어도 다들 그 노부인을 사랑했으니까 — 그들에게는 기뻐할 까닭이 생겼다."], ["16mo. $1.50.", "대고모님이 플럼필드를 조에게 남겼고, 그것이 온갖 즐거운 일을 가능케 했던 것이다."]], [["MOODS A Novel. 16mo.", "\"근사한 옛집이라 꽤 괜찮은 값을 받을 거야."], ["$1.50. A MODERN MEPHISTOPHELES and a Whisper in the Dark.", "물론 팔 생각이겠지.\""], ["16mo. $1.50.", "몇 주 뒤 다들 그 일을 의논하던 자리에서 로리가 말했다."]], [["Other Stories by LOUISA M.", "\"아니, 안 팔아.\""], ["ALCOTT\nA HOLE IN THE WALL. Illustrated.", "조의 단호한 대답이었다."], ["12mo. 50 cents.", "옛 주인을 생각해 거둬들인 살찐 푸들을 쓰다듬으면서."]], [["An account of a poor boy's admiration for a beautiful garden to which he is invited by a little girl friend. (\"How They Camped Out\" in same volume.)\nMARJORIE'S THREE GIFTS. Illustrated. 12mo. 50 cents.", "\"거기서 살겠다는 건 아니지?\""]], [["A fairy tale told Marjorie comes true, and there enter into her life three good fairies: Industry, Cheerfulness, and Love. (\"Roses and Forget-me-nots\" in same volume.)\nMAY FLOWERS. Illustrated. 12mo. 50 cents.", "\"맞아, 살 거야.\""]], [["The experiences of six earnest young girls who try to make the sad lives about them happier.", "\"하지만 조, 그 집은 어마어마하게 커서 관리하는 데만도 큰돈이 들어."], ["Full of sensible hints as to wisest methods of charity.", "정원과 과수원만 해도 사람 두세 명은 있어야 하고, 농사는 바에르 선생 분야가 아닐 텐데.\""]], [["A CHRISTMAS DREAM. Illustrated. 12mo. 50 cents.", "\"내가 권하면 그이도 거기서 한번 해 볼 거야.\""]], [["A rather spoiled child gets her first real enjoyment of Christmas by making others happy. (\"Baa!", "\"그래서 그 땅에서 나는 걸로 먹고살겠다고?"], ["Baa!\" in same volume.)", "글쎄, 듣기엔 무릉도원 같지만, 막상 해 보면 죽도록 힘든 일이라는 걸 알게 될걸.\""]], [["THE CANDY COUNTRY. Illustrated.", "\"우리가 거둘 작물은 수지맞는 거야.\""], ["12mo. 50 cents.", "조가 웃었다."]], [["A quaint little fable in which the young heroine visits Candy-land and is finally contented to return to Bread-land. (\"How They Ran Away\" in same volume.)\nLITTLE BUTTON ROSE. Illustrated. 12mo. 50 cents.", "\"그 근사한 작물이라는 게 대체 뭡니까, 부인?\""]], [["A bright, vivacious child visits her maiden aunts.", "\"사내아이들이지. 어린 사내아이들을 위한 학교를 열고 싶어."], ["Her influence on the somewhat narrow characters about her is delightfully described.", "좋고, 행복하고, 가정 같은 학교를. 내가 그 애들을 돌보고, 프리츠가 가르치는 거야.\""]], [["Two girls travel through Europe.", "\"이거야말로 진짜 조다운 계획이네!"], ["The frivolous Ethel learns the advantages of culture and simple dignity from her companion.", "딱 조답지 않아?\" 로리가 식구들에게 호소하듯 외쳤지만, 식구들도 그만큼이나 놀란 표정이었다."]], [["MOUNTAIN-LAUREL AND MAIDENHAIR. Illustrated.", "\"난 마음에 드는구나.\""], ["12mo. 50 cents.", "마치 부인이 단호하게 말했다."]], [["The story of a New Hampshire farmer's daughter who is fond of writing verses. PANSIES AND WATER-LILIES.", "\"나도 그렇다.\""], ["Illustrated.", "남편이 덧붙였다."], ["12mo. 50 cents.", "그는 현대의 젊은이들에게 소크라테스식 교육법을 시험해 볼 기회가 생긴다는 생각을 반겼다."]], [["\"Pansies\" is a story of a girls'", "\"조한테는 엄청난 짐이 될 거예요.\""], ["discussion of books; \"Water-Lilies\" is a romance by the sea-shore.", "메그가 자기의 온 마음을 사로잡은 외아들의 머리를 쓰다듬으며 말했다."]], [["A droll account of the travels of two dolls.", "\"조라면 해낼 수 있고, 그 안에서 행복할 거야."], ["(\"Shadow-Children\"", "멋진 생각이야."], ["and \"The Moss People\"", "어디 자세히 말해 보렴.\""], ["in same volume.)\nMORNING GLORIES AND QUEEN ASTOR. Illustrated.", "로런스 씨가 외쳤다."], ["12mo. 50 cents.", "그는 두 연인에게 손을 보태 주고 싶어 안달이었지만, 그들이 자기 도움을 마다하리라는 걸 알고 있었다."]], [["# THE LITTLE MEN PLAY. THE LITTLE WOMEN PLAY.", "# THE LITTLE MEN PLAY. THE LITTLE WOMEN PLAY."]], [["Aunt Wee changes Daisy from a petulant to a cheerful girl by interesting her in the wonderful world of nature which Daisy has never before learned to appreciate justly.", "\"어르신은 제 편이 되어 주실 줄 알았어요. 에이미도 그렇고요 — 눈을 보면 알아요."], ["Adapted from Miss Alcott's famous stories, \"Little Men\"", "신중해서 입을 떼기 전에 곰곰이 생각하느라 기다리고 있지만요. 자, 사랑하는 여러분.\""], ["and \"Little Women,\"", "조가 진지하게 말을 이었다. \"이게 갑자기 떠오른 생각이 아니라 오래 품어 온 계획이라는 걸 알아주세요."], ["by Elizabeth L.", "프리츠가 오기 전부터 저는 늘 생각했어요."], ["Gould. Illustrated.", "돈을 모으고 집에서 더는 나를 필요로 하지 않게 되면, 큰 집을 빌려서 어머니도 없는 가엾고 쓸쓸한 사내아이들을 거둬들여 돌보고, 너무 늦기 전에 그 애들의 삶을 즐겁게 해 주겠다고요."], ["12mo.", "제때 도움을 받지 못해 망가져 가는 아이들이 정말 많아요."], ["Price 50 cents each.", "저는 그 애들을 위해 뭐든 해 주는 게 너무 좋고, 그 애들이 무엇을 원하는지 느껴지고, 그 애들의 고민에 공감이 가요. 아, 저는 정말이지 그 애들의 어머니가 되고 싶어요!\""]], [["Two forty-five minute plays of two acts each, for eight or ten little people.", "마치 부인이 조에게 손을 내밀자, 조는 눈물 어린 눈으로 미소 지으며 그 손을 잡았다."], ["They will prove a source of limitless delight. LITTLE, BROWN, & COMPANY, Publishers", "그러고는 오래도록 보지 못했던 옛날의 그 열정적인 투로 말을 이었다."]], [["Project Gutenberg has two versions of Little Women by Louisa May Alcott. Original Edition Illustrated Edition\nWelcome to the Project Gutenberg Illustrated Edition of Little Women by Louisa May Alcott, published by Little, Brown, and Company. Some versions of the book, such as this one, use the full title of the book from the title page, _Little Women; Or Meg, Jo, Beth, and Amy_.", "\"언젠가 프리츠에게 제 계획을 얘기했더니, 그이도 꼭 그러고 싶었다면서 우리가 부자가 되면 한번 해 보자고 했어요. 그이의 다정한 마음에 축복이 있기를. 그이는 평생 그 일을 해 왔어요 — 가난한 아이들을 돕는 일 말이에요, 부자가 되는 게 아니라요. 부자는 절대 못 될 거예요. 돈이 주머니에 모일 만큼 오래 머무는 법이 없으니까요. 하지만 이제, 제가 받을 자격도 없을 만큼 저를 아껴 주신 좋은 대고모님 덕분에 저는 부자예요. 적어도 그런 기분이에요. 학교만 잘되면 우리는 플럼필드에서 아주 잘 살 수 있어요. 사내아이들에겐 더없이 맞춤한 곳이에요. 집은 크고, 가구는 튼튼하고 수수해요. 안에는 수십 명이 들어갈 자리가 넉넉하고, 바깥에는 멋진 마당이 있어요. 그 애들이 정원과 과수원 일을 거들 수 있어요. 그런 일이 건강에 좋잖아요, 어르신? 그러면 프리츠는 자기 방식대로 아이들을 길들이고 가르치고, 아버지가 그이를 도와주실 거예요. 저는 그 애들을 먹이고 보살피고 어르고 야단치고, 어머니가 제 든든한 버팀목이 되어 주실 거예요. 저는 늘 사내아이들이 많았으면 했는데, 한 번도 충분한 적이 없었어요. 이제 집을 가득 채우고, 그 작은 녀석들과 마음껏 즐길 수 있어요. 얼마나 호사스러운지 생각해 보세요 — 플럼필드가 제 것이고, 그 안을 함께 누빌 사내아이들이 우글우글하다니!\""]], [["We used the version of the book from Little, Brown, and Company: copyright 1896, for this transcription. A scanned copy of this book is available through the internet archive, courtesy of the New York Public Library.", "조가 두 손을 흔들며 황홀한 한숨을 내쉬자, 식구들은 까르르 웃음보를 터뜨렸고, 로런스 씨는 졸도라도 할 줄 알 만큼 웃어 댔다."]], [["A copy of the first version of the novel, published in 1869, was consulted for emendations, the proper rendering of words hyphenated and split between two lines for spacing, and other issues in transcribing the novel.", "\"뭐가 우습다는 건지 모르겠네요.\" 다시 말소리가 들릴 만해지자 조가 진지하게 말했다."], ["We are not trying to change this version of the novel back to the 1869 novel, but correct the errors that were made in re-transcribing and updating the text. Passages in italics were indicated by _underscores_.", "\"제 교수님이 학교를 여는 것, 그리고 제가 제 소유의 땅에서 사는 걸 더 좋아하는 것보다 더 자연스럽고 마땅한 일이 어디 있어요.\""]], [["Throughout the dialogues, there were words used to mimic accents of the speakers.", "\"벌써 거드름을 피우는데.\" 이 생각을 그저 기막힌 농담으로 여기는 로리가 말했다."], ["Those words were retained as-is.", "\"그런데 그 학교를 어떻게 꾸려 갈 생각인지 여쭤봐도 될까?"], ["Errors in punctuations and hyphenation were not corrected unless otherwise noted below.", "학생이 죄다 꼬마 부랑아라면, 세속적인 의미에서는 작물이 수지가 안 맞을까 걱정인데요, 바에르 부인.\""]], [["A note about spacing of illustrations: If there are four lines above the illustration, then the illustration was at the top of a new chapter. If there are four lines below the illustration, then the illustration was at the end of a chapter. If there is two lines between a chapter heading and the illustration, then the illustration was an inline illustration (usually a drop-cap illustration). On page vii, in the Table of Contents, change page 7 to 1 for the beginning of Chapter One.", "\"찬물 끼얹지 마, 테디. 물론 부유한 학생도 받을 거야 — 어쩌면 그런 애들로만 시작할지도 모르고. 그러다 자리가 잡히면 별미 삼아 부랑아 한둘쯤 받는 거지. 부잣집 아이들도 가난한 아이들 못지않게 보살핌과 위안이 필요할 때가 많아. 하인들 손에 내맡겨진 불쌍한 어린것들이나, 정말 잔인하게도 뒤처진다고 억지로 떠밀리는 아이들을 봤거든. 어떤 애들은 잘못된 양육이나 방치 탓에 못된 아이가 되고, 어떤 애들은 어머니를 잃지. 게다가 제일 착한 애들도 어설픈 사춘기를 거쳐야 하는데, 바로 그때야말로 가장 큰 인내와 다정함이 필요한 시기야. 사람들은 그 애들을 비웃고, 떠밀고, 눈에 안 띄게 두려 하면서, 예쁜 아이가 어느 날 갑자기 의젓한 청년으로 변하기를 바라지. 그 애들은 별로 불평도 안 해 — 야무진 어린 영혼들이거든 — 하지만 다 느낀단 말이야. 나도 비슷한 걸 겪어 봐서 잘 알아. 나는 그런 어린 곰 같은 녀석들에게 각별한 관심이 가고, 서툰 팔다리와 뒤죽박죽인 머릿속에도 불구하고 그 따뜻하고 정직하고 선량한 소년의 마음이 내 눈엔 보인다는 걸 알려 주고 싶어. 게다가 나는 경험도 있어. 한 소년을 길러서 집안의 자랑이자 영예가 되게 했잖아?\""]], [["In the List of Illustrations, for the illustration on page 147, \"postmistress\"", "\"네가 그러려고 애쓴 건 내가 증언하지.\""], ["was replaced with \"post-mistress\".", "로리가 고마운 눈길로 말했다."]], [["In the List of Illustrations, for the illustration on page 235, \"tea-pot\"", "\"그리고 나는 기대 이상으로 성공했어. 봐, 너는 이렇게 듬직하고 분별 있는 사업가가 됐고, 돈으로 숱한 좋은 일을 하면서, 달러 대신 가난한 이들의 축복을 쌓고 있잖아."], ["was replaced with \"teapot\".", "하지만 너는 단순한 사업가가 아니야."], ["On page 30, the punctuation after 'stained they are'", "너는 선하고 아름다운 것을 사랑하고, 그것을 스스로 즐기면서도 옛날처럼 늘 남들과 절반씩 나누지. 나는 네가 정말 자랑스러워, 테디."], ["may be a colon, but on page 41 of the 1869 book, it is a semicolon.", "너는 해마다 더 나아지고, 누구나 그걸 느껴, 네가 입 밖에 내지 못하게 하는데도 말이야. 그래, 내 아이들을 거두게 되면 나는 너를 가리키며 이렇게 말할 거야."], ["We used the semi-colon.", "'얘들아, 저게 너희가 본받을 본보기란다.'\""]], [["On page 34, transcribe red-headed with the hyphen. See page 44 of the 1869 novel.", "가엾은 로리는 시선을 둘 데를 몰랐다."], ["On page 40, a period was added after \"room where old Mr\". See page 50 of the 1869 novel.", "어엿한 어른이 됐는데도, 이렇게 쏟아진 칭찬에 모든 얼굴이 흐뭇하게 그를 향하자 옛날의 수줍음이 어느새 되살아났던 것이다."]], [["On page 41, the semicolon after \"Laurie went on the box\"", "\"이봐, 조, 그건 좀 과한데.\" 그가 옛날 소년 시절 그대로의 투로 입을 열었다."], ["was replaced with a comma.", "\"너희는 내가 평생 다 갚을 수 없을 만큼 많은 걸 해 줬어."], ["See page 52 of the 1869 novel.", "너희를 실망시키지 않으려고 최선을 다하는 것 말고는 갚을 길이 없을 정도야."], ["On page 62, mantel-piece was transcribed with the hyphen.", "요즘 들어 너는 나를 좀 멀리했지만, 그래도 나는 더없이 좋은 도움을 받았어. 그러니 내가 조금이라도 잘됐다면, 그건 이 두 사람 덕으로 돌리렴.\""], ["See Page 75 of the 1869 novel.", "그러고는 한 손을 할아버지의 흰머리에, 다른 손을 에이미의 금빛 머리에 다정하게 얹었다. 세 사람은 좀처럼 멀리 떨어지는 법이 없었다."]], [["On page 63, checked the clause \"and I've been trying to do it this ever so long.\"", "\"나는 정말이지 가족이야말로 이 세상에서 가장 아름다운 거라고 생각해!\" 그때 유난히 마음이 벅차오른 조가 불쑥 외쳤다."], ["It is written the same way on page 77 of the 1869 book.", "\"언젠가 나도 내 가정을 갖게 되면, 내가 알고 가장 사랑하는 이 세 가정처럼 행복하길 바라. 존이랑 우리 프리츠까지 여기 있다면, 정말이지 이 땅의 작은 천국일 텐데.\""], ["No change was made.", "그녀가 한결 차분하게 덧붙였다."], ["On page 64, add period after \"red and shining with merriment.\" See page 79 of the 1869 book.", "그리고 그날 밤, 가족과 의논하고 희망하고 계획하던 더없이 행복한 저녁을 보낸 뒤 자기 방으로 올라간 조는, 마음이 행복으로 가득 차 도무지 가라앉질 않아, 늘 자기 침대 곁에 놓인 빈 침대 옆에 무릎 꿇고 베스를 다정하게 떠올리고 나서야 겨우 마음을 가라앉힐 수 있었다."]], [["On page 68, changed weet to sweet in 'the damp weet air.'", "그해는 모든 면에서 참으로 놀라운 한 해였다. 일들이 유난히 빠르고 기쁘게 일어나는 것만 같았으니까."], ["See page 84 of the 1869 novel.", "조는 정신을 차릴 새도 없이 어느새 결혼해 플럼필드에 자리를 잡았다. 그러자 예닐곱 명의 사내아이들이 버섯처럼 쑥쑥 솟아나 놀랍도록 무성하게 자랐다."], ["On page 79, add comma after I remain in the letter.", "부잣집 아이도, 가난한 집 아이도 함께였다. 로런스 씨가 끊임없이 가슴 아픈 빈곤 사례를 찾아내, 바에르 부부에게 그 아이를 가엾이 여겨 거둬 달라며, 양육비로 약간이나마 기꺼이 내겠다고 부탁했기 때문이다."], ["See page 95 of the 1869 novel.", "이런 식으로 그 능청스러운 노신사는 자존심 센 조를 구슬려, 그녀가 가장 좋아하는 유형의 사내아이들을 대 주었다."]], [["On page 84, ferrule was an adjustment from the 1869 book, which only used one r in spelling the word (see page 101).", "물론 처음엔 고된 오르막길이었고, 조는 엉뚱한 실수도 저질렀다. 하지만 현명한 교수가 그녀를 잔잔한 물길로 무사히 이끌어 주었고, 아무리 막무가내인 부랑아도 끝내는 손에 길들여졌다."], ["Webster's dictionary spells ferrule with two rs.", "조는 그 \"우글거리는 사내아이들\"을 얼마나 즐거워했던가. 그리고 가엾고 사랑스러운 마치 대고모님이 살아서, 단정하고 질서 정연하던 플럼필드의 신성한 영역이 톰이며 딕이며 해리 같은 녀석들로 우글거리는 걸 봤다면 얼마나 한탄했을까!"], ["On page 109, a period was added after \"and the old man quite dotes on them\".", "그래도 따지고 보면 일종의 인과응보 같은 데가 있었다. 그 노부인은 한때 몇 마일 안의 사내아이들에게 공포의 대상이었으니까."], ["See page 130 of the 1869 novel.", "이제 그 추방당했던 녀석들이 금지된 자두를 마음껏 따 먹고, 신성모독 같은 신발로 자갈을 야단도 안 맞고 걷어차며, 성마른 \"뿔 굽은 암소\"가 겁 없는 젊은이더러 들이받혀 보라며 부르곤 하던 그 너른 들판에서 크리켓을 했다. 그곳은 일종의 사내아이들의 낙원이 되었고, 로리는 그곳 주인에게 바치는 찬사이자 그곳 주민들에게도 어울리게, \"바에르 정원\"이라고 부르자고 제안했다."]], [["On page 109, a period was added after \"asked another voice\".", "그곳은 결코 유행하는 학교가 아니었고, 교수는 재산을 모으지도 못했다. 하지만 그곳은 조가 의도한 바로 그것이었다 — \"가르침과 보살핌과 다정함이 필요한 사내아이들을 위한, 행복하고 가정 같은 곳.\""], ["See page 131 of the 1869 novel.", "그 큰 집의 방마다 이내 가득 찼고, 정원의 작은 텃밭마다 이내 임자가 생겼다. 애완동물을 기르게 허락했기에 헛간과 광에는 어엿한 동물원이 생겨났다."], ["On page 112, change colon to semicolon after \"if you don't;\"--see page 134 of the 1869 novel.", "그리고 하루 세 번, 조는 양옆으로 행복한 어린 얼굴들이 죽 늘어앉은 긴 식탁의 상석에서 자기 프리츠에게 미소를 보냈다. 그 얼굴들은 하나같이 다정한 눈길과 믿음 어린 말과 고마워하는 마음으로, \"바에르 어머니\"를 향한 사랑을 가득 담아 그녀를 돌아보았다."]], [["On page 113, transcribe ear-rings with the hyphen.", "이제 조에게는 사내아이들이 넘쳐 났지만, 결코 싫증 나지 않았다. 그 애들이 천사는 어림도 없었고, 더러는 교수와 교수 부인에게 적잖은 골칫거리와 근심을 안겨 주었는데도 말이다."], ["See page 135 of the 1869 novel. On Page 123, \"One thing remember, my girls:\"", "하지만 아무리 못되고 건방지고 약 올리는 부랑아라도 그 마음 한구석에 있는 선한 자리를 향한 그녀의 믿음이 그녀에게 인내와 솜씨를, 그리고 마침내 성공을 안겨 주었다."], ["is written as it appears in the 1896 novel.", "햇살처럼 자애롭게 비춰 주는 바에르 아버지와, 일흔 번씩 일곱 번이라도 용서해 주는 바에르 어머니 앞에서, 오래 버틸 수 있는 사내아이란 세상에 없었으니까. 조에게는 그 녀석들과의 우정이 무척이나 소중했다."], ["A comma instead of a colon was used after my girls in the 1869 novel (see page 146).", "잘못을 저지른 뒤의 뉘우치는 훌쩍임과 속삭임, 우습거나 가슴 뭉클한 자그마한 속내, 흐뭇한 열정과 희망과 계획, 심지어 그 애들의 불운까지도. 그런 것들은 그저 그 애들을 더욱더 사랑스럽게 만들 뿐이었다."], ["\"One thing =to= remember,\"", "굼뜬 아이도 있고 수줍은 아이도 있었다. 허약한 아이도 있고 시끌벅적한 아이도 있었다."], ["may work better today, or even \"Remember one thing,\"", "혀짤배기 아이도 있고 말 더듬는 아이도 있었다. 다리 저는 아이도 한둘 있었다."], ["but we left this as Ms.", "그리고 다른 데서는 받아 주지 않았지만 \"바에르 정원\"에서는 환영받은, 명랑한 어린 혼혈아도 있었다."], ["Alcott wrote it.", "몇몇 사람은 그 아이를 받으면 학교가 망할 거라고 점쳤지만 말이다."]], [["On Page 124, the P. C.", "그렇다, 조는 그곳에서 무척 행복한 여인이었다."], ["is the Pickwick Club from a novel by Charles Dickens. Samuel Pickwick, Tracy Tupman, Augustus Snodgrass, and Nathaniel Winkle were introdued by Charles Dickens in the first chapter of The Pickwick Papers.", "고된 일과 숱한 근심, 끊이지 않는 소동에도 불구하고."], ["Samuel Weller makes his first appearance in Chapter Ten of that novel.", "그녀는 그것을 진심으로 즐겼고, 사내아이들의 갈채가 세상의 그 어떤 칭찬보다 더 흡족하다는 걸 알았다."], ["On page 128, in the Pickwick Portfolio, there is no period after \"it is nearly school time\" in N.", "이제 그녀는 오로지 자기를 열렬히 믿고 따르는 무리에게만 이야기를 들려주었으니까."], ["Winkle's letter. This period was also missing on page 151 of the 1869 novel.", "세월이 흐르면서 자기 자식인 어린 사내아이 둘이 생겨나 그녀의 행복을 더해 주었다 — 할아버지의 이름을 딴 롭, 그리고 천하태평한 아기 테디였는데, 어머니의 발랄한 기질뿐 아니라 아빠의 햇살 같은 성정까지 물려받은 듯했다."], ["The missing period was intentional.", "사내아이들이 소용돌이치는 그 한복판에서 어떻게 두 아이가 무사히 자랐는지는 할머니와 이모들에게 수수께끼였지만, 두 아이는 봄날의 민들레처럼 무럭무럭 자랐고, 그 거친 보모들은 두 아이를 사랑하며 알뜰히 보살폈다."]], [["On page 135, the ambiguous punctuation after \"Oh, dear, no!\"", "플럼필드에는 휴일이 무척 많았는데, 그중에서도 가장 즐거운 것 하나가 해마다 열리는 사과 따기였다. 그때면 마치네, 로런스네, 브룩네, 바에르네 식구가 총출동해 하루를 즐겼던 것이다."], ["is an exclamation point.", "조가 결혼한 지 오 년째 되던 해, 이 풍요로운 잔치 가운데 하나가 열렸다."], ["See page 160 of the 1869 novel.", "무르익은 시월의 어느 날로, 공기에는 기운을 북돋우는 상쾌함이 가득해 마음이 들뜨고 혈관 속 피가 건강하게 춤추는 것 같았다."], ["On page 146, change buttonholes to button-holes.", "오래된 과수원은 잔칫날 차림새를 하고 있었다. 미역취와 과꽃이 이끼 낀 돌담을 수놓았고, 메뚜기가 시든 풀밭에서 활기차게 뛰어다녔으며, 귀뚜라미가 잔칫상의 요정 피리 부는 악사처럼 울어 댔다."], ["On page 173 of the 1869 novel, this word was hyphenated and split between two lines for spacing.", "다람쥐들은 저들의 자그마한 추수에 분주했고, 새들은 골목길 오리나무 위에서 작별 인사를 지저귀었으며, 나무마다 처음 한 번 흔들기만 하면 빨갛고 노란 사과를 우수수 떨어뜨릴 채비를 하고 있었다. 모두가 거기 모였다."], ["There were seven other occurrences of button-hole or button-holes in the novel.", "모두가 웃고 노래하며, 기어 올라갔다가 굴러떨어졌다. 모두가 이렇게 완벽한 날도, 그날을 즐기는 이렇게 유쾌한 무리도 일찍이 없었다고 입을 모았다."], ["We therefore used the hyphen.", "그리고 저마다, 마치 이 세상에 근심이나 슬픔 따위는 존재하지도 않는다는 듯, 그 시간의 소박한 즐거움에 거리낌 없이 자신을 내맡겼다."]], [["On page 150, \"Betty\" was replaced with \"Bethy\".", "마치 씨는 평온하게 거닐며 로런스 씨에게 터서와 카울리와 콜루멜라를 인용해 주었다."], ["This error was also made in the 1869 novel--see page 177. The character addressed is Beth.", "그러면서—"]], [["On page 158, a period was added after \"still kneeling\". See page 187 of the 1869 novel. On page 160, \"hard a lee\" is spelled the same way in the 1869 novel (see page 189) and this novel. We left this as is.", "\"순한 사과의 포도주 같은 즙\""]], [["On page 166, a comma was added after \"Meg\" in \"Meg obediently following\". See page 195 of the 1869 book. On page 185, \"receptable\" was replaced with \"receptacle\". See page 217 of the 1869 novel.", "을 음미했다."]], [["On page 185, transcribe door-way with the hyphen.", "교수는 창 대신 장대를 들고 푸른 통로를 오르내리며, 마치 건장한 게르만 기사처럼 사내아이들을 이끌었다."], ["See page 217 of the 1869 novel.", "아이들은 자기들끼리 갈고리 사다리 소방대를 꾸려, 땅에서고 공중에서고 온갖 재주넘기로 놀라운 묘기를 펼쳤다."], ["Also, change doorway to door-way a few lines down the same page.", "로리는 어린 꼬마들에게 헌신했다. 어린 딸을 한 말들이 광주리에 태워 주고, 데이지를 새 둥지가 있는 데까지 들어 올려 주고, 모험심 많은 롭이 목이 부러지지 않게 지켰다."], ["See page 218 of the 1869 novel. On page 189, the comma after \"published every day\"", "마치 부인과 메그는 한 쌍의 포모나처럼 사과 더미 사이에 앉아, 끊임없이 쏟아져 들어오는 수확물을 골라냈다."], ["was replaced with a period. See page 225 of 1869 book.", "한편 에이미는 얼굴에 아름다운 어머니다운 표정을 띠고서, 여러 무리를 스케치하며, 곁에 작은 목발을 둔 채 그녀를 흠모하듯 바라보고 앉은 한 창백한 사내아이를 지켜보았다."]], [["On page 198, the clause: \"Beth, go and ask Mr.", "그날 조는 물 만난 고기 같았다. 치맛자락을 걷어 핀으로 꽂고, 모자는 머리 아닌 아무 데나 걸쳐 둔 채, 아기를 옆구리에 낀 채로 이리저리 뛰어다니며, 어떤 신나는 모험이 벌어지든 뛰어들 채비를 하고 있었다."], ["Laurence for a couple of bottles of old wine:\"", "어린 테디는 마치 마법에 걸린 듯 무탈했다."], ["was written as it appeared in the 1896 novel. The clause ended in with a semi-colon in the 1869 book (see page 234).", "도무지 무슨 일도 생기는 법이 없어서, 한 아이가 그 애를 번쩍 들어 나무 위로 올려놓든, 다른 아이가 등에 업고 달리든, 아기란 절인 양배추부터 단추, 못, 제 신발까지 뭐든 소화한다는 게르만식 미신에 빠진 너그러운 아빠가 시큼한 사과를 먹이든, 조는 조금도 걱정하지 않았다."], ["On page 200, change needlework to needle-work.", "어린 테드가 때가 되면 다시 나타나리라는 걸, 무사하고 발그레하게, 꼬질꼬질하면서도 태평하게 돌아오리라는 걸 알았으니까."], ["See page 236 of the 1869 novel.", "그리고 그녀는 늘 진심 어린 환영으로 그 애를 다시 맞았다. 조는 자기 아기들을 더없이 다정하게 사랑했던 것이다."]], [["On page 209, \"turnovers\"", "네 시가 되자 잠시 한가한 틈이 찾아왔고, 광주리는 빈 채로 놓여 있었다."], ["was replaced with \"turn-overs\".", "사과 따던 이들이 쉬면서 옷이 찢긴 데와 멍든 데를 견주어 보는 동안에. 그러고 나서 조와 메그는 큰 사내아이들 한 무리를 데리고 풀밭에 저녁상을 차렸다."], ["See page 246 of the 1869 novel.", "야외에서 마시는 차야말로 늘 그날의 절정의 기쁨이었으니까. 그런 날이면 땅에는 그야말로 젖과 꿀이 흘러넘쳤다."], ["On page 214, the single quotation mark before \"Head Nurse of Ward\"", "사내아이들에게 식탁에 앉으라고 요구하지 않고, 좋을 대로 음식을 들게 허락했던 것이다. 자유야말로 소년의 영혼이 가장 사랑하는 양념이니까."], ["was replaced with a double quotation mark.", "아이들은 이 드문 특권을 마음껏 누렸다. 누구는 물구나무를 선 채 우유 마시기라는 흥미로운 실험을 해 보았고, 누구는 등 짚고 넘기 놀이 중간중간 파이를 먹어 가며 그 놀이에 멋을 더했으며, 쿠키는 들판에 온통 흩뿌려졌고, 사과 턴오버는 새로운 종류의 새처럼 나무에 올라앉았다."], ["See page 252 of the 1869 novel.", "어린 여자아이들은 자기들끼리 따로 다과회를 열었고, 테드는 제 마음 내키는 대로 먹을거리 사이를 누볐다."]], [["On page 218, \"Year's ago\" was replaced with \"Years ago\". See page 257 of the 1869 novel. On page 219, \"ask him so help\" was replaced with \"ask him to help\". See page 257 of the 1869 novel.", "아무도 더는 먹을 수 없게 되자, 교수가 첫 번째 정식 건배를 제의했는데, 이런 자리에서면 늘 빠지지 않던 것이었다 — \"마치 대고모님, 하느님의 축복이 함께하기를!\" 그녀에게 얼마나 큰 신세를 졌는지 결코 잊지 않는 그 선한 사내가 진심으로 외친 건배였고, 그녀를 기억하도록 배워 온 사내아이들이 조용히 잔을 비웠다."]], [["On page 219, add period after \"give it to her.\" See page 258 of the 1869 novel. On page 230, \"two, A.M.\" is spelled the same way, with the comma, in this book and in the 1869 novel (on page 272). The comma was retained.", "\"이제 할머니의 예순 번째 생신을 위하여! 만수무강을 빌며, 만세 삼창 세 번!\""]], [["On page 244, \"postscrips\"", "그것은 능히 짐작할 수 있듯이 우렁차게 외쳐졌고, 한번 시작된 환호는 좀처럼 멈추기 어려웠다."], ["was replaced with \"postscripts\".", "그들의 특별한 후원자로 여겨지던 로런스 씨부터, 어린 주인을 찾아 제 본분의 영역을 벗어나 헤매던 어리둥절한 기니피그에 이르기까지, 모두의 건강을 위한 건배가 제의됐다."], ["See page 287 of the 1869 novel.", "그러고 나서 맏손주인 데미가 그날의 여왕에게 갖가지 선물을 바쳤는데, 어찌나 많은지 잔치 자리까지 손수레로 실어 날라야 할 정도였다. 더러는 우스꽝스러운 선물들이었지만, 다른 사람의 눈에는 흠으로 보일 것들이 할머니의 눈에는 장식이었다 — 아이들의 선물은 전부 손수 만든 것이었으니까."], ["On page 279, place exclamation point after won't in 'No, I won't!'", "데이지의 참을성 있는 작은 손가락이 손수건에 떠 넣은 한 땀 한 땀이, 마치 부인에게는 자수보다 더 나았다. 데미의 신발 상자는 뚜껑이 닫히지 않았어도 기계 솜씨의 기적이었다."], ["See page 329 in the 1869 novel.", "롭의 발받침은 비뚤배뚤한 다리가 흔들거렸는데, 부인은 그게 아주 마음을 편안하게 해 준다고 했다. 그리고 에이미의 아이가 드린 값비싼 책의 그 어떤 면도, 비뚤배뚤한 대문자로 \"사랑하는 할머니께, 어린 베스가\"라고 적힌 그 면만큼 곱지 않았다."]], [["On page 286, \"actingly\"", "이 의식이 치러지는 동안 사내아이들은 슬그머니 사라지고 없었다."], ["was replaced with \"acting\". See page 337 of the 1869 novel.", "그리고 마치 부인이 아이들에게 고마움을 전하려다 그만 목이 메어 버리고, 테디가 자기 앞치마로 부인의 눈물을 닦아 주던 그때, 교수가 불쑥 노래를 부르기 시작했다."], ["On page 288, add comma after mankind in the clause \"who felt at peace with all mankind even his mischievous pupil.\"", "그러자 그의 머리 위에서 한 목소리 또 한 목소리가 그 노랫말을 이어받았고, 나무에서 나무로 보이지 않는 합창단의 가락이 메아리쳤다. 사내아이들이 온 마음을 다해, 조가 짓고 로리가 곡을 붙이고 교수가 가장 멋지게 부르도록 가르친 그 작은 노래를 부른 것이다."], ["See page 339 of the 1869 novel.", "이것은 완전히 새로운 것이었고, 대성공이었다. 마치 부인은 놀라움을 가라앉히지 못하고, 키 큰 프란츠와 에밀부터 모두 중에 가장 고운 목소리를 지닌 그 어린 혼혈아에 이르기까지, 깃털 없는 새들 한 마리 한 마리와 일일이 악수를 나누겠다고 고집했다."]], [["On page 294, transcribe gray-headed with the hyphen. See page 5 of the 1869 novel. On page 295, add a comma after salary in the phrase \"with an honestly-earned salary.\" See page 7 of the 1869 book.", "이 일이 끝나고 사내아이들은 마지막으로 한바탕 놀려고 흩어졌고, 마치 부인과 딸들만 잔치의 나무 아래 남았다."]], [["Checked the clause \"But once get used to these slight blemishes\" on page 297.", "\"이렇게 내 가장 큰 소원이 이토록 곱게 이루어졌으니, 이제 다시는 나 자신을 '운 없는 조'라고 불러서는 안 되겠어요.\""], ["The sentence appears the same way on page 10 of the 1869 novel. Checked the clause \"People who hire all these things done for them never know what they lose\"", "바에르 부인이 어린 테디의 작은 주먹을 우유 단지에서 꺼내며 말했다."], ["on page 298. The sentence has a comma after them, but is otherwise written the same way on page 11 of the 1869 novel.", "아이가 그 안을 신나게 휘젓고 있던 참이었다."]], [["On page 299, transcribe door-handles with the hyphen. See page 13 of the 1869 novel.", "\"그런데도 언니 인생은 언니가 옛날에 그리던 거랑은 아주 다르네."], ["On page 339, \"shortcomings\"", "우리의 공중누각 기억나?\""], ["was replaced with \"short-comings\". See page 62 of the 1869 novel.", "에이미가 사내아이들과 크리켓을 하는 로리와 존을 지켜보며 미소 띤 얼굴로 물었다."]], [["On page 345, \"furbelows and notions\"", "\"착한 사람들! 저렇게 일을 잊고 하루 신나게 노는 걸 보니 내 마음이 다 흐뭇하구나.\""], ["was written \"furbelows and quinny-dingles\"", "조가 대답했다. 그녀는 이제 온 인류를 두고 어머니 같은 투로 말했다."], ["in the 1869 novel.", "\"그래, 기억나. 하지만 그때 내가 바라던 인생은 지금의 내겐 이기적이고 외롭고 차갑게만 보여."], ["See page 59 of the 1869 novel.", "아직 좋은 책을 한 권 쓸 수 있으리란 희망은 버리지 않았지만, 기다릴 수 있어."], ["We made no change, and only point this out because quinny-dingles is such a memorable word that those intimate with the novel may notice the change.", "그리고 이런 경험과 본보기 덕분에 분명 더 나은 책이 될 거라고 확신해.\" 그러면서 조는 저 멀리 활기찬 사내아이들에게서, 교수의 팔에 기댄 채 햇살 속을 오가며 둘 다 그토록 좋아하는 대화에 푹 빠진 자기 아버지에게로, 그리고 다시 어머니에게로 손짓했다."], ["On page 353, change snowbank to snow-bank. See page 79 of the 1869 novel.", "어머니는 딸들 한가운데 보좌에 앉은 듯, 손주들을 무릎에 올리고 발치에 둔 채로 있었는데, 그 얼굴은 그들에게 결코 늙는 법이 없었고, 다들 그 얼굴에서 위로와 행복을 얻는 것 같았다."]], [["On page 363, a double quotation mark was added before \"Cross-patch, draw the latch\".", "\"내 누각이 모두 중에서 가장 가깝게 이루어졌어. 물론 나는 근사한 것들을 바라긴 했지만, 마음속으로는 알고 있었어."], ["See page 91 of the 1869 book.", "작은 집과 존, 그리고 이런 사랑스러운 아이들만 몇 있으면 만족하리라는 걸."], ["On page 379, change period after Jo to a comma in the clause \"as for Jo.", "나는 그걸 다 가졌고, 하느님께 감사하게도 세상에서 가장 행복한 여자야.\""], ["she would have gone up\".", "메그가 자기의 키 큰 사내아이 머리에 손을 얹으며 말했다."], ["See page 109 of the 1869 book.", "다정하고 경건한 만족이 가득한 얼굴로."]], [["On page 380, a comma was added after \"all lying down\".", "\"내 누각은 내가 계획했던 거랑은 아주 다르지만, 그렇다고 바꾸진 않을래. 비록 언니처럼 나도 예술가의 꿈을 다 접거나, 남들이 아름다움의 꿈을 이루도록 돕는 일에만 나를 가두진 않을 거지만."], ["See page 111 of the 1869 book.", "나는 아기 조각상을 만들기 시작했는데, 로리는 그게 내가 만든 것 중에 최고래. 나도 그렇게 생각하고, 대리석으로 만들 작정이야."], ["On page 393, the punctuation after 'but so was everybody's'", "무슨 일이 생기든 적어도 내 어린 천사의 모습만은 간직할 수 있게.\" 에이미가 말하는 사이, 그녀의 품에서 잠든 아이의 금빛 머리 위로 굵은 눈물 한 방울이 떨어졌다."], ["is difficult to read.", "그녀가 너무도 사랑하는 하나뿐인 딸은 연약한 어린것이었고, 그 아이를 잃을지 모른다는 두려움이 에이미의 햇살에 드리운 그늘이었던 것이다. 이 십자가는 아버지에게도 어머니에게도 많은 것을 해 주고 있었다."], ["It could be a colon or semicolon.", "하나의 사랑과 하나의 슬픔이 두 사람을 가깝게 묶어 주었으니까. 에이미의 천성은 더 다정하고 더 깊고 더 부드럽게 자라 갔다."], ["In the 1869 novel, the mark is a semi-colon (see page 126).", "로리는 더 진중하고 강하고 굳건해져 갔다. 그리고 두 사람은 배워 가고 있었다."], ["We used the semi-colon.", "아름다움도, 젊음도, 행운도, 심지어 사랑 그 자체도, 가장 복받은 사람에게서조차 근심과 고통, 상실과 슬픔을 막아 주지는 못한다는 것을. 왜냐하면—"]], [["On page 396, the second line of the verse beginning with \"'Out upon you,\" is indented. In the 1869 version, the capital B of \"Bold-faced jig!'\" is lined-up under Out. We aligned the verse as the 1869 version of the novel--see page 131. On page 404, add period after heaviness. See page 140 of the 1869 novel.", "\"누구의 삶에든 얼마쯤 비는 내리는 법, 어떤 날은 어둡고 슬프고 음울할 수밖에.\""]], [["On page 405, transcribe needle-work with the hyphen.", "\"저 애는 점점 나아지고 있어, 나는 확신한단다, 얘야."], ["See page 141 of the 1869 book.", "낙심하지 말고, 희망을 품고 행복하게 지내렴.\""], ["On Page 411, a letter is curiously addressed to Betsey, both here and on page 148 of the 1869 book.", "마치 부인이 말했다. 마음씨 고운 데이지가 할머니 무릎에서 몸을 숙여, 발그레한 뺨을 어린 사촌의 창백한 뺨에 갖다 댈 때였다."]], [["On Page 413, removed double quotes around Yes in \"Yes,\" they say to one another, these so kind ladies.", "\"마미가 절 북돋워 주시고, 로리가 모든 짐의 절반 넘게 짊어져 주는데, 제가 낙심해선 안 되죠.\""], ["Instead, place a single quote in front of Yes, because Bhaer is resuming his quote.", "에이미가 따뜻하게 대답했다."], ["The resumed quote concludes with a single quote after me and mine. See page 151 of the 1869 novel.", "\"그이는 절대 제게 자기 근심을 내비치는 법이 없고, 제게 너무도 다정하고 참을성 있고, 베스에게 그토록 헌신적이고, 언제나 제게 든든한 버팀목이자 위안이 되어 주어서, 저는 아무리 사랑해도 모자라요."], ["The double quote before 'he is a stupid old fellow' is actually a triple-nested quote, ending in make themselves.", "그러니 제 십자가 하나에도 불구하고, 저도 메그 언니처럼 말할 수 있어요."], ["On page 417, transcribe Teddy-ism as Teddyism. See page 155 of the 1869 novel.", "'하느님께 감사하게도, 나는 행복한 여자야.'\""]], [["On page 451, a period was added after \"I can't let you go\".", "\"나야 그런 말을 할 필요도 없지. 내가 누릴 자격보다 훨씬 더 행복하다는 건 누가 봐도 뻔하니까.\""], ["See page 196 of the 1869 novel.", "조가 덧붙였다. 착한 남편에게서, 곁의 풀밭에서 뒹구는 토실토실한 자기 아이들에게로 눈길을 옮기면서."], ["On page 463, \"Tarantula\"", "\"프리츠는 점점 머리가 세고 살이 붙어 가고, 나는 그림자처럼 비쩍 말라 가는 데다 서른이야."], ["was used as the name of a dance, but the author might have meant \"Tarantella,\"", "우리는 결코 부자가 못 될 테고, 그 못 말리는 토미 뱅스가 벌써 세 번이나 제 몸에 불을 냈는데도 기어이 이불 속에서 양치류 시가를 피워 대는 통에, 플럼필드는 어느 날 밤에 홀랑 타 버릴지도 몰라."], ["which is the name of an Italian dance about tarantulas.", "하지만 이런 낭만 없는 사실들에도 불구하고, 나는 불평할 게 하나도 없고, 평생 이렇게 즐거운 적이 없었어. 이런 말 써서 미안하지만, 사내아이들 틈에서 살다 보니 가끔 그 애들 말투를 안 쓸 수가 없네.\""]], [["On page 468, transcribe chess-board with the hyphen. See page 218 of the 1869 novel. On page 512, a period was added after \"she said softly\". If you see page 272 of the 1869 novel, you will also notice a comma in 'she said, softly.' We added the period but not the comma.", "\"그래, 조, 네 추수는 풍성할 거다.\" 마치 부인이 말을 꺼내며, 테디를 빤히 노려보아 무안하게 만들던 커다란 검은 귀뚜라미 한 마리를 쫓아 주었다."]], [["On page 514, the 1869 novel did not have a comma after oar in the sentence: \"I'm not tired; but you may take an oar, if you like.", "\"엄마 것의 절반에도 못 미칠걸요. 자, 여기 있잖아요, 엄마가 그토록 참을성 있게 씨 뿌리고 거두신 걸 우리는 아무리 감사해도 모자라요.\""], ["See page 525 of the 1869 novel.", "조가 외쳤다."], ["We did not remove the comma.", "끝내 못 버린 그 사랑 넘치는 격정으로."]], [["On page 527, \"the 'the best nevvy\"", "\"해마다 밀은 더 많아지고 가라지는 더 적어지면 좋겠어요.\""], ["was replaced with \"the best nevvy\".", "에이미가 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["On page 527, change he to the in 'like Jenny and he ballad'.", "\"큰 볏단이지만, 마미의 마음속엔 그걸 담을 자리가 있다는 걸 알아요.\""], ["See page 291 of the 1869 novel.", "메그의 다정한 목소리가 덧붙였다."]], [["On page 531, David and Peggotty refer to two characters from the novel \"David Copperfield\"", "마음 깊이 감동한 마치 부인은 그저 두 팔을 활짝 벌릴 뿐이었다. 마치 자식들과 손주들을 죄다 품에 끌어안기라도 하려는 듯이."], ["by Charles Dickens.", "그러고는 어머니다운 사랑과 감사와 겸손이 가득한 얼굴과 목소리로 말했다."]], [["On page 534, change of to off in the clause: Daisy found it impossible to keep her eyes of her \"pitty aunty,\" ... see page 300 of the 1869 novel.", "\"오, 내 딸들아, 너희가 아무리 오래 산다 해도, 나는 결코 이보다 더 큰 행복을 빌어 줄 수가 없겠구나!\""]]];
